// GENERATED by `node tools/grim.js build` — do not edit. Sources: content/**/*.grim, locales/**/*.lang
window.LUMENHOLD_SOURCES = [{"path":"content/core/balance.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Numbers (design §8.4). Tier words in effects (`mood +medium`,\n#  `rel npc -small`) take their amount from `sizes`; challenge\n#  difficulty tiers shift the DC from the expected skill at that\n#  point of the run (`curve`, one value per turn, fitted by\n#  `node tools/grim.js curve`). All values are tunable.\n# ============================================================\n\n@balance\ndesc: Size tiers are tiny, small, medium, large and huge; skills use the xp row.\nsizes: health 3 5 10 16 25 | mood 4 8 13 20 30 | gold 3 8 15 30 60 | renown 2 4 8 13 20 | mana 3 6 10 16 25\n  | alignment 3 6 10 16 25 | xp 2 3 6 10 16 | lore 1 1 3 5 8 | rel 1 2 4 6 9 | spark 4 8 13 20 30\ndifficulty: trivial -15 easy -7 medium 0 hard 8 heroic 16\n# Skill XP (design §8.5): the next point costs a + b × level. Fitted so a balanced player averages about 55-60 at the\n# end of a four-year run (L: with the spell bonus); a focused skill reaches ~65-75 by month 24 (design ~85: an alarm).\nxp_cost: 1 0.04\n# Rank XP needed for each promotion (Member → Officer, Officer → Captain), plus performance ≥ 70.\nrank_xp: 10 25\nweights: health 1 mood 1 gold 0.5 renown 0.8 mana 0.4 alignment 0 xp 1.5 rel 0.6 spark 0.4\n# The combat stub (playtest 2, M39, M5): until the minigame, a fight reads arcana, vigor and guile (weighted), plus\n# condition × (the mean of health, mood and mana − 50) and spells × total spell levels (at most +15). Entering costs mana.\ncombat: arcana 1 vigor 1 guile 0.5 condition 0.2 spells 0.75\ncombat_cost: small\n# How far one skill point moves a check's odds (M14): `wild` checks swing whatever your skill; `steady` ones follow it.\nswing: normal 2.5 wild 1.2 steady 4\n# A shift's pay (M10): `wage` in a job's cards pays the job's `pay:` at your rank × this share.\nwage: short 0.5 full 1 overtime 2\n# Coursework (feedback 2026-10-15): the skill XP an enrolled course gives once a term month, at course level I, II, III.\ncoursework: small small small\n# The range a check's odds are clamped to (L-3): a 5% forced fail and a 5% forced success. Milestone checks (a\n# `class: milestone` card, an exam) have no forced fail.\nodds: min 5 max 95\n# The check ladder (docs/authoring/checks.md, design §8.6): checks written with a rung, odds by nature and a luck word.\n# A rung is the DC: who or what you're up against.\ndc_tiers: novice- 0 novice 4 novice+ 8 apprentice- 12 apprentice 16 apprentice+ 20 journeyman- 24 journeyman 28\n  journeyman+ 32 adept- 36 adept 40 adept+ 44 expert- 48 expert 52 expert+ 56 master- 60 master 64 master+ 68\n  grandmaster- 72 grandmaster 76 grandmaster+ 80 archmage- 84 archmage 88 archmage+ 92\n# Odds by nature: the chance (%) when your skill matches the DC; a circumstance step (`+1 if …`) moves it by odds_step (z).\nlikelihood: certain 97 sure 90 likely 70 even 50 unlikely 30 slim 15 long_shot 5 freak 1\nodds_step: 0.5\n# Luck: the bell's σ. Odds = floor + (ceil − floor) × Φ(z(odds) + (skill − DC) / σ); milestones run 0–100.\nsigma: steady 12 normal 20 wild 32 luck 80\nbell: floor 2 ceil 98\n# Drift: how far the DC is pulled toward the expected skill (`curve`). Defaults: story none; milestone, crisis low; else high.\ndrift: none 0 low 0.15 mid 0.3 high 0.5\n# A course's standard rung at levels I, II, III (a track's `standard:` overrides), and the rungs a course card rises per\n# course level above its own `level:`.\ncourse_standard: apprentice+ adept+ master+\ncourse_step: 5\ncourse_drift: low\n# Exam preparation (L-3): a course card adds `attend` by itself (options add or cost more); each term month's\n# coursework adds `monthly` (feedback 2026-10-15b: it replaced the silent per-card 2, so preparation shows); at most `cap` counts.\nstudy: attend 0 cap 15 monthly 2\n# A course exam's difficulty at course level I, II and III: the course standard ± rungs, with low drift (checks.md).\n# Level III sits two rungs up, because only the stronger players reach it (skill-check survey, 2026-10-15).\nexam_tiers: standard+1 standard+1 standard+1\n# An exam's grades (feedback 2026-10-15): A from `a` over the bar, B from `b`, a pass from `pass`, each on the bell (no\n# hard A gate). Going for top marks (`sit_exam wild`) lowers the A mark by `ambitious` and is sat at `ambitious_sigma`.\nexam_bands: a 15 b 5 pass -5 ambitious 8 ambitious_sigma 20\n# Peers grow with the player (feedback 2026-10-15): a character with one of these tags (classmates, and the pen pal at\n# Thornwick) has every skill stat scaled up with E(turn), so that by the last month their best skill is `share` of the\n# final E(turn). Faculty and villagers keep theirs.\nnpc_growth: share 0.9 tags peer thornwick\n# A known spell adds `per` to checks of its discipline's skill, at most `cap` (design §9.2; L-3).\nspell_check: per 2 cap 6\n# Learning a spell, or a level of one, gives skill XP in its discipline (L-6): one tier per spell level.\nspell_bonus: medium large huge\n# Spark follows friendship (M37): a friendship gain adds spark × (spark / per), at most cap× (partner× for a partner);\n# spark crossing `cooldown` asks whether you want it (@game spark_cooldown).\nspark_follow: per 25 cap 2 partner 3 cooldown 30\ncurve: 12 13 14 16 18 19 19 21 23 24 26 26 27 28 29 31 33 33 34 35 36 37 39 40 40 41 42 43 46 46 47 48 49 50 52 53 53 54 54 55 57 57 58 58 59 60 61 62\n\n# ---------------- Presets (design §8.4): named groups of effects ----------------\n\n@preset grind\nparams: skill\ndesc: Hard work on one skill.\ndo:\n  $skill +medium\n  mood -small\n  health -tiny\n\n@preset rest\ndesc: A real rest.\ndo:\n  mood +medium\n  health +small\n\n@preset social\nparams: who\ndesc: Time with someone.\ndo:\n  rel $who +small\n  mood +small\n  gold -tiny\n\n@preset work\ndesc: Paid work.\ndo:\n  gold +medium\n  mood -small\n  health -tiny\n\n@preset splurge\ndesc: Spending to feel better.\ndo:\n  mood +large\n  gold -medium\n\n@preset risk\nparams: res\ndesc: A gamble: a large gain or a medium loss.\ndo:\n  chance even:\n    $res +large\n  else:\n    $res -medium\n\n# ---------------- Origins (docs/origin-design.md, 2026-10-16) ----------------\n# Starting packages, applied before the first term's charges. Skills are XP grants through the ordinary XP cost\n# (xp_cost: 1 0.04), calibrated to land on the design's levels from the base values in rules.grim: each grant is\n# the sum of 1 + 0.04 × level from the base to the target (test/origins.test.js recomputes them). Purses are added\n# to the base 30 crowns.\n\n@preset origin_noble_start\ndesc: Charm 25, Lore 12; 90 crowns in all.\ndo:\n  charm += 27.88\n  lore += 5.52\n  gold +huge\n\n@preset origin_scholar_start\ndesc: Lore 28, Arcana 12; 60 crowns in all.\ndo:\n  lore += 34\n  arcana += 5.52\n  gold +large\n\n@preset origin_hedge_start\ndesc: Alchemy 30, Arcana 15, Lore 6; 45 crowns in all.\ndo:\n  alchemy += 40.8\n  arcana += 10.08\n  lore -= 2\n  gold +medium\n\n@preset origin_street_start\ndesc: Guile 30, Vigor 18; 38 crowns in all.\ndo:\n  guile += 40.8\n  vigor += 15\n  gold +small\n\n@preset origin_merchant_start\ndesc: Charm 25, Guile 15; 150 crowns in all.\ndo:\n  charm += 27.88\n  guile += 12.6\n  gold +huge\n  gold +huge\n\n# Support before each later term (terms 2–8), paid by the origin's funding card in the last break month. Cut by\n# about a third from the design's amounts (2026-10-16: supported origins ended runs with several hundred crowns).\n@preset noble_allowance\ndesc: The family's allowance, 38 crowns, and the expectations that come with it.\ndo:\n  gold +large\n  gold +small\n  mood -small\n\n@preset noble_gift\ndesc: A family gift on top, 11 crowns (30% of terms, rolled when the letter comes).\ndo:\n  gold +small\n  gold +tiny\n\n@preset scholarship_stipend\ndesc: By the latest completed term's GPA: 3.5 or higher, 46 crowns (with an 8-crown merit bonus); 3.0 or higher, 38; below, nothing.\ndo:\n  if term_gpa >= 3.5:\n    gold +large\n    gold +small\n    gold +small\n  elif term_gpa >= 3.0:\n    gold +large\n    gold +small\n\n@preset trade_returns\ndesc: The company's share after a good trading period, 78 crowns (70% of terms, rolled when the report comes).\ndo:\n  gold +huge\n  gold +medium\n  gold +tiny\n"},{"path":"content/core/rules.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Lumenhold Academy — core rules\n#  Resources, skills, calendar, global hooks.\n#  See docs/grimoire-script.md for the language.\n# ============================================================\n\n@game\ntitle: Lumenhold Academy\nsubtitle: Four years. Forty-eight moons. One apprentice.\n# The content format version (design §12). Frozen: v1 may only grow; see test/golden/schema-v1.json.\nschema: 1\n# Four years (design §4.1). The academic year starts in autumn; the run ends in Starfall of year 4 (turn 48).\n# Year 4's last break is the wrap-up: no plans or duties, board on the house; its last month only resolves the run.\nyears: 4\nlast_month: 12\nwrapup: 11-12\nmonths: Harvestmoon, Emberwane, Mistveil, Frostcall, Deepwinter, Thawmoon, Stormbloom, Greenrise, Blossomtide, Highsun, Goldenhaze, Starfall\ncalendar: term=1-4 break=5-6 term=7-10 break=11-12\nseasons: autumn=1-3 winter=4-6 spring=7-9 summer=10-12\nsummer_months: 5, 6, 11, 12\n# Each month is one day (design §4.2). The two visits are picked on the map.\nday_term: morning=courses noon=social:1-2 afternoon=visit evening=visit night=world:1-2 midnight=special:0-1\nday_break: morning=plan afternoon=visit evening=visit night=world:1 midnight=special:0\nday_last: morning=special:0\n# Social and world cards are dealt across the whole day (playtest 2, M43), not only at noon and night; `slot:` requires a\n# slot and `time:` favours one.\nspread: social world\n# The origin is picked on its own screen before the game (its options are the origins).\norigins: choose_origin\n# Exams are cards (playtest 2, M4): this one is pushed for each enrolled course in a term's last month.\nexam: course_exam\n# A failed course pushes this card in the course's context (playtest 2, N4): a won appeal turns the F into a C.\nappeal: grade_appeal\nspark_cooldown: spark_cooldown\n# A job is a fixed number of shifts a term (playtest 2, I1): this card is pushed on the first visit after the last one.\noff_shift: job_off_shift\n# An option that charges crowns up front is locked when you can't pay (review fixes), unless every option would be.\nafford: gold\n# The term-time map; a break plan's `map_id:` replaces it (home, or the internship host's).\nmap_id: campus\ncards_per_month: 3\nmax_cards_per_month: 7\nsocial_per_month: 1\nmin_random_per_month: 1\nintro: The sky-barge lurches. Below, the lake glitters; above, the seven towers of Lumenhold Academy pierce the clouds. You have a trunk, a borrowed wand and a letter of acceptance with your name spelled wrong. Four years begin now.\n\n# ---------------- Resources ----------------\n# The top bars (design §8.1) are Health · Mood · Mana · Renown · Alignment, in this order;\n# `display: panel` resources are shown in the You panel instead; `display: number` (Crowns, Renown) sits in the top row\n# as a plain number, after the meters, with what next month's start will take or give under it.\n\n@resource health\nname: Health\nicon: heart\nmin: 0\nmax: 100\nstart: 70\ncolor: #d9606a\ndesc: Your body. At zero you collapse.\nwarn_below: 20\non_zero:\n  queue crisis_collapse now\n\n@resource mood\nname: Mood\nicon: sun\nmin: 0\nmax: 100\nstart: 60\ncolor: #e6b84a\ndesc: Your spirits. At zero, despair takes hold.\nwarn_below: 20\non_zero:\n  queue crisis_despair now\n\n@resource mana\nname: Mana\nicon: sparkle\nmin: 0\nmax: 40 + arcana / 2\nstart: 30\ncolor: #5fa8e8\ndesc: The magic you can spend on spells. Refills each month, faster the stronger your Arcana.\nwarn_below: 8\n\n@resource renown\nname: Renown\nicon: star\nmin: 0\nmax: 100\nstart: 5\ncolor: #b58ae0\n# A plain number beside Crowns (feedback 2026-10-15), with next month's fading shown under it.\ndisplay: number\ndesc: How much the academy talks about you — for better or worse. Fades a little every month.\n\n@resource alignment\nname: Alignment\nicon: moon\nmin: -100\nmax: 100\nstart: 0\ncolor: #c9b8ff\ndrag: -50 0.5\ndesc: Lumen or Hollow: the light you serve, or the dark that has noticed you. Deep in the Hollow, the way back is slow.\n\n@resource gold\nname: Crowns\nicon: coin\nmin: 0\nmax: 99999\nstart: 30\ncolor: #d7a948\n# Shown in the middle of the top row as a plain number (playtest 2, step 5), not in the You panel.\ndisplay: number\nwarn_below: 6\ndesc: Crowns in your purse. Rent is due every month.\non_zero:\n  flag broke\n\n# ---------------- Skills ----------------\n\n@skill arcana\nname: Arcana\nicon: wand\nstart: 8\nmax: 100\ndesc: Raw spellcraft — power, control, nerve.\n\n@skill lore\nname: Lore\nicon: book\nstart: 8\nmax: 100\ndesc: Scholarship, theory, history, languages of the old world.\n\n@skill alchemy\nname: Alchemy\nicon: potion\nstart: 6\nmax: 100\ndesc: Potions, herbs, reagents and the patience to stir clockwise.\n\n@skill charm\nname: Charm\nicon: mask\nstart: 8\nmax: 100\ndesc: Wit, warmth, persuasion. Making people want to say yes.\n\n@skill vigor\nname: Vigor\nicon: sword\nstart: 8\nmax: 100\ndesc: Stamina, reflexes, dueling and broom-flight.\n\n@skill guile\nname: Guile\nicon: eye\nstart: 6\nmax: 100\ndesc: Sneaking, lying, noticing what others miss.\n\n# ---------------- Global hooks ----------------\n\n# Unpaid board (design §8.2): a hungry month; twice in a row, Grisk settles it for you (grisk_board_debt).\n@hook rent\non: month_start\nlabel: Dormitory & board\nwhen: turn > 1 and term\ndo:\n  if gold >= 8:\n    gold -8\n    board_missed = 0\n  else:\n    gold = 0\n    gain hungry\n    board_missed += 1\n    if board_missed >= 2 and not has(in_debt) and not flag(grisk_done):\n      board_missed = 0\n      queue grisk_board_debt\n  say: {board_missed == 0: {~The bursar takes the board money with a nod and a tick in a very large book.|Board paid: a bed, three meals and the right to complain about both.|You pay the board and count what's left twice.}|{~The board went unpaid. The refectory still feeds you, a little less each day.|No board money this month. Lunch is a pocketed roll.|Board unpaid again. Your name in the bursar's very large book is underlined now.}}\n\n@hook break_rent\non: month_start\nlabel: Board at the castle\nwhen: break and (has(plan_castle) or has(plan_work))\ndo:\n  gold -8\n  say: {~The castle in the holidays still wants its board.|A bed in an empty dormitory costs the same as a full one.}\n\n# Tuition each term (design §8.2): everyone pays it (origin design 2026-10-16). A family's or the Crown's support\n# arrives as cash before the term, on the origin's funding card (content/events/origin_funding.grim).\n@hook tuition\non: term_start\nlabel: Tuition\ndo:\n  gold -16\n  say: {~Term fees, paid at a window with a very small grille.|The bursar's quill scratches. Another term is paid for.}\n\n# Course fees (review fixes): charged with the term, like tuition, not by the enrolment card.\n@hook course_fees\non: term_start\nwhen: term_index <= 6\nlabel: Course fees\ndo:\n  gold -small\n  say: {~Course fees, paid at the registrar's window with the tuition.|The registrar takes the course fees with a sigh, as if it pained her too.}\n\n@hook gpa_probation\non: term_end\nlabel: Academic probation\nwhen: gpa > 0 and (year >= 2 or month >= 10)\ndo:\n  if gpa < 1.5 and not has(probation):\n    gain probation\n  if gpa < 1.0:\n    gpa_low += 1\n  else:\n    gpa_low = 0\n  if gpa_low >= 2:\n    gpa_low = 0\n    queue crisis_probation\n\n# Coursework (feedback 2026-10-15): each enrolled course trains its main skill once a term month through its cards,\n# like a job's wage (engine: courseworkFor, `@balance coursework:`), so there are no coursework hooks.\n\n@hook term_stress\non: month_start\nlabel: Term-time pressure\nwhen: turn > 1 and term\ndo:\n  mood -3\n  if month == 4 or month == 10:\n    mood -6\n  say: {month == 4 or month == 10: {~Exams are coming, and everybody's quill moves faster.|The library stays open late and is full until it closes.|You dream of an exam you haven't revised for, which is most of them.}|{~Essays stack up on your desk like weather coming in.|Lectures, reading, more reading. Term has a weight of its own.|Everyone is behind. Everyone says so in the corridors.}}\n\n@hook exhaustion_drain\non: month_start\nlabel: Running on empty\nwhen: health < 25\ndo:\n  mood -6\n  say: {~You are so tired that the stairs look personal.|Your body has opinions about this month, and none of them are kind.|You fall asleep in a lecture and wake up in a different mood.}\n\n@hook gloom_drain\non: month_start\nlabel: Gloom\nwhen: mood < 20\ndo:\n  health -5\n  say: {~A grey month. You eat little and sleep badly.|Low spirits sit in your chest like a cold.|The gloom gets into your bones; even the stairs feel steeper.}\n\n@hook natural_recovery\non: month_start\nlabel: Rest\nwhen: health < 70 and not has(sick) and not has(injured)\ndo:\n  health +10\n  say: {~A few early nights and a warm meal put some colour back in your face.|You rest, and your body thanks you slowly.|Sleep, soup and a quiet week. You feel more like yourself.}\n\n@hook fame_fades\non: month_start\nlabel: Fading gossip\nwhen: turn > 1 and renown >= 5\ndo:\n  renown -= floor(renown * 0.2)\n  say: {~The corridors find something else to talk about.|Yesterday's gossip goes the way of yesterday's bread.|Fewer heads turn when you pass. It's restful.}\n\n@hook mana_regen\non: month_start\nlabel: Mana returns\nwhen: turn > 1\ndo:\n  mana += 10 + arcana / 10\n  say: {~The magic seeps back into you overnight, like warmth into cold hands.|Your fingertips tingle again by morning.|Whatever you spent, the castle quietly gives some back.}\n\n# Ambient drains (design §8.2): exam nerves in the finals months, so mana matters even without casting.\n@hook exam_nerves\non: month_start\nlabel: Exam nerves\nwhen: month == 4 or month == 10\ndo:\n  mana -= 15\n  say: {~Exam nerves eat at your magic: your wand hand won't stay still.|Revision drinks your mana before you can cast a thing.}\n\n# ---------------- Game start ----------------\n\n@hook start\non: game_start\ndo:\n  queue intro_arrival now\n  # Picked on the origin screen: the mirror only reflects it. Otherwise (tools, old callers) the mirror asks.\n  if has(origin_noble) or has(origin_scholar) or has(origin_hedge) or has(origin_street) or has(origin_merchant):\n    queue sorting_mirror now\n  else:\n    queue choose_origin now\n"},{"path":"content/endings.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Endings. `kind: early` endings are only reached via `end <id>`\n#  and stay exclusive (game over).\n#  Graduation builds a yearbook (design §10.5) from `slot:`s, each\n#  checked in descending `priority`:\n#    headline (default)  1: career / academic; `ending_graduate`\n#                           (no `when`) is the fallback\n#    mystery             0-1: the Hollow Star outcome\n#    relationships       0-3: bonds\n#    personal            0-2: alignment, renown\n#  `excludes:` drops other endings once this one is picked.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- Early endings ----------------\n\n@ending ending_sent_home\nkind: early\ntitle: Sent Home\nart: cross\ntext: The infirmary writes to your family. A carriage comes for you on a grey morning and you watch the towers shrink in the rear window. Your body gave out before your dreams did. Perhaps, one day, you will come back.\n\n@ending ending_quiet_departure\nkind: early\ntitle: The Quiet Departure\nart: rain\ntext: You take the dawn barge without telling anyone. The academy does not notice right away; then, one by one, people do. Some of them write. You keep the letters in a box you don't open for years. When you finally do, you are someone gentler, and you find you are not alone.\n\n@ending ending_expelled\nkind: early\ntitle: Expelled\nart: scales\ntext: The Board's decision is final. Your wand is not taken — Lumenhold is not cruel — but your name is struck from the register in ink that cannot be unwritten. You walk out of the gates with a trunk and a lesson, and the long road ahead is entirely your own.\n\n@ending ending_hollow_claimed\nkind: early\ntitle: Claimed by the Hollow Star\nart: moon\ntext: You open the door beneath the lake. The Hollow Star looks back. For a moment you understand everything, and then there is no \"you\" left to understand it. On clear nights students swear they see a new star, black against black, right above the seventh tower.\n\n@ending ending_runaway\nkind: early\ntitle: Gone With the Wind\nart: map\ntext: You and Corvin leave Lumenhold on a stolen sky-barge at midnight, laughing so hard you can barely steer. The academy will tell the story for years — usually wrong. You never graduate. You don't regret it, mostly.\n\n# ---------------- Final endings (graduation) ----------------\n\n@ending ending_hollow_keeper\nslot: mystery\npriority: 100\ntitle: Keeper of the Hollow Star\nart: moon\nwhen: flag(hollow_bargain)\ntext: You graduate with a black star on your palm and a voice in the back of your mind that is not entirely yours. The Hollow Star gave you power; it will want something back. For now, the towers bow a little when you pass, and even the headmistress looks away first.\n\n@ending ending_hollow_freed\nslot: mystery\npriority: 98\ntitle: Herald of the Hollow Star\nart: moon\nwhen: flag(hollow_freed)\ntext: The spring the lake froze, the Star went free, and you held the door. It did not swallow you; it remembered who let it out. The Northern families write to you as one of their own now, and wherever you walk, candles burn a little lower, as if bowing. Lumenhold still hands you a diploma. Nobody at the ceremony looks you in the eye.\n\n@ending ending_hollow_failed\nslot: mystery\npriority: 90\ntitle: The Night Was Lost\nart: door\nwhen: flag(hollow_failed)\ntext: The spring the lake froze, you went down beneath it, and the door would not close for you. The headmistress closed it in your place and paid for it with years she didn't have to spare. The Wardens don't blame you. Every spring, when the lake thaws, you do, a little, and then you get up and keep the old doors anyway.\n\n@ending ending_hollow_hero\nslot: mystery\npriority: 95\ntitle: Warden of Lumenhold\nart: shield\nwhen: flag(hollow_sealed)\ntext: The academy will never fully know what you did beneath the lake. The headmistress does. At graduation she gives you a silver key without a lock and a seat among the Wardens, the quiet few who keep the old doors closed. Kael stands in the crowd and, for once, he is smiling.\n\n@ending ending_archmage\npriority: 80\ntitle: The Archmage's Path\nart: wand\nwhen: arcana >= 80 and gpa >= 2.5\ntext: Graduating with highest honours and a power that makes the candles lean toward you, you are offered an apprenticeship with the Conclave of the Seven Towers. Professor Thorne shakes your hand and says, gruffly, \"Don't waste it.\" You won't.\n\n@ending ending_scholar\npriority: 75\ntitle: Scholar of the Spire\nart: book\nwhen: (lore >= 98 or (flag(thesis_distinction) and lore >= 85)) and gpa >= 2.5\ntext: Your thesis is shelved in the Spire Library, in the section students whisper about. The faculty offers you a junior fellowship. You will spend your life among books that bite and secrets that wait — and you could not be happier.\n\n@ending ending_beloved\nslot: relationships\npriority: 70\ntitle: Hand in Hand\nart: heart\nwhen: bonds(partner) > 0 and mood >= 60\ntext: Graduation is a blur of robes and cheers, but what you remember is a hand finding yours as the hats go up. Whatever comes next, you won't face it alone. Lumenhold taught you magic; it also taught you this.\n\n# A partner ending names them (playtest 2, B31); each replaces the generic one above.\n\n@ending ending_beloved_lyra\nslot: relationships\npriority: 72\ntitle: The Second Flame\nart: heart\nwhen: bond(lyra) == \"partner\" and mood >= 60\nexcludes: ending_beloved\ntext: When the hats go up, {lyra} catches yours before it lands and hands it back without a word. Later, in front of her whole family, she takes your hand and doesn't let go. For House Vaneshade, that is a declaration. For her it is simpler: she has finally found someone she doesn't need to beat.\n\n@ending ending_beloved_tobias\nslot: relationships\npriority: 72\ntitle: Home, Wherever\nart: heart\nwhen: bond(tobias) == \"partner\" and mood >= 60\nexcludes: ending_beloved\ntext: {tobias} cries through the whole ceremony and blames the pollen. Afterwards he lifts you off your feet in front of everyone, then shows you a drawing of a farmhouse with two chairs on the porch. \"No pressure,\" he says. \"The goats have already voted.\"\n\n@ending ending_beloved_seraphine\nslot: relationships\npriority: 72\ntitle: Already Foreseen\nart: heart\nwhen: bond(seraphine) == \"partner\" and mood >= 60\nexcludes: ending_beloved\ntext: \"I saw this,\" {seraphine} says as the hats go up. \"Years ago. You were holding my hand.\" You are. For once she smiles at the present, as if it has finally caught up with her, and asks whether you'd like to know what happens next. You tell her you'd rather find out together.\n\n@ending ending_beloved_corvin\nslot: relationships\npriority: 72\ntitle: No More Jokes\nart: heart\nwhen: bond(corvin) == \"partner\" and mood >= 60\nexcludes: ending_beloved\ntext: {corvin} slips a key into your palm during the speeches. It opens nothing at Lumenhold; he has checked. \"It's for wherever we end up,\" he says, and for once he doesn't make a joke of it. Then he does, of course. But his hand stays in yours.\n\n@ending ending_beloved_mir\nslot: relationships\npriority: 72\ntitle: Built to Last\nart: heart\nwhen: bond(mir) == \"partner\" and mood >= 60\nexcludes: ending_beloved\ntext: {mir} has rigged the hats to fly back down. Most of them do. In the cheerful chaos they press a small brass ring into your hand; it ticks faintly, like a second heart. \"It'll run for fifty years,\" they say. \"Probably. We should check.\"\n\n@ending ending_beloved_kael\nslot: relationships\npriority: 72\ntitle: Thaw\nart: heart\nwhen: bond(kael) == \"partner\" and mood >= 60\nexcludes: ending_beloved\ntext: {kael} doesn't throw his hat. He stands beside you in the crowd, and when the cheering is loudest he says, very quietly, \"Come north with me. Or I stay. Either.\" It is the longest sentence about feelings he has ever spoken. His hand, in yours, is warm.\n\n@ending ending_beloved_nessa\nslot: relationships\npriority: 72\ntitle: Front Page\nart: heart\nwhen: bond(nessa) == \"partner\" and mood >= 60\nexcludes: ending_beloved\ntext: The Ledger's graduation issue runs a small notice at the bottom of page six, set in {nessa}'s own type: two names, one line, no comment. All of Lumenhold reads it before breakfast. {nessa} pretends not to watch them read it, and holds your hand under the table the whole time.\n\n@ending ending_alchemist\npriority: 65\ntitle: Master of the Crucible\nart: cauldron\nwhen: alchemy >= 72\ntext: Your potions are already sold under the counter in three cities. On graduation day you sign the lease on a crooked little shop by the harbour. The sign reads your name, and underneath: NO REFUNDS FOR TRANSFORMATIONS.\n\n@ending ending_celebrity\nslot: personal\npriority: 60\ntitle: The Name on Every Lip\nart: star\nwhen: renown >= 45\ntext: The Lumen Ledger runs a special edition about you. First-years queue for your autograph. You graduate as the most talked-about student in a generation, and the world beyond the gates is already waiting to hear what you'll do next.\n\n@ending ending_merchant\npriority: 55\ntitle: Gold in the Vaults\nart: coin\nwhen: gold >= 180\ntext: You leave Lumenhold with a diploma and a fortune. Magic, you've decided, is good, but compound interest is better. Within five years your trading house has offices in every port — and an enchanted ledger that never loses a coin.\n\n@ending ending_shadow\npriority: 50\ntitle: The Quiet Hand\nart: mask\nwhen: guile >= 75\ntext: No one quite knows how you graduated with those grades. No one quite remembers the favours you called in. A discreet letter offers you a position with the Crown's Office of Unseen Affairs. You accept, of course. You'd already read it before it was sent.\n\n@ending ending_beloved_friends\nslot: relationships\npriority: 45\ntitle: The Ones Who Stayed\nart: group\nwhen: bonds(friend) + bonds(close) >= 3\ntext: You may not leave as the brightest or the richest, but you leave surrounded. Your friends throw their hats higher than anyone's, and the photograph from that day — all of you squinting into the sun — will hang in every home you ever have.\n\n@ending ending_barely\npriority: 40\ntitle: By the Skin of Your Teeth\nart: hourglass\nwhen: (turn > 4 and gpa < 2.0) or flag(thesis_resubmit)\ntext: Your name is called last, after a long pause and some whispering between examiners. But it is called. You graduate. You are a mage of Lumenhold, and nobody ever has to know the details.\n\n@ending ending_bystander\nslot: mystery\npriority: 20\ntitle: Someone Else's Lantern\nart: lantern\nwhen: not (flag(hollow_sealed) or flag(hollow_bargain) or flag(hollow_freed) or flag(hollow_failed))\ntext: There was a spring frost, the year you graduated, and a night when the bell under the lake would not stop ringing. You were somewhere else: asleep, or studying, or holding a door shut for the first-years. Someone else went down with the lantern. You never learn their whole story, but every spring, when the lake thaws, you find yourself grateful to them.\n\n@ending ending_graduate\npriority: 0\ntitle: A Mage of Lumenhold\nart: tower\ntext: Robes, hats, a speech that runs long. You walk out of the gates of Lumenhold a qualified mage — not legendary, not disgraced, simply yourself, with four years of stories and a whole world to spend them in.\n\n@ending ending_teacher\npriority: 72\ntitle: The Teacher's Desk\nart: feather\nwhen: flag(career_teaching) and flag(career_accepted)\ntext: In the autumn you return to Lumenhold — not on the student barge, but on the faculty one. Your classroom has a window over the lake and a desk with somebody else's initials carved in it. On the first day a terrified first-year asks a question you once asked. You know exactly what to say.\n\n@ending ending_wanderer\npriority: 58\ntitle: The Road of Wonders\nart: tent\nwhen: flag(career_circus) and flag(career_accepted)\ntext: You join the Travelling Circus of Wonders as its youngest illusionist. For years you cross the kingdoms in a painted wagon, making dragons out of moonlight for children who have never seen magic. Every winter, a letter arrives from Lumenhold. You always write back.\n\n# ---------------- Relationship lines: Ilse Dumont's chain ----------------\n\n@ending ending_tamsin_friends\nslot: relationships\npriority: 40\ntitle: A Friend Across the Hills\nart: letter\nwhen: flag(tamsin_friends) and rel(tamsin) >= 15\ntext: Your yearbook entry has a green seal pressed into the corner, which is not allowed. Tamsin Reed of Thornwick sent it, with a note: \"Four years of rivalry, four hundred letters, one friend. Insufferable to the end.\" Every spring, at a town halfway between, there are biscuits.\n\n@ending ending_dumonts_reunited\nslot: relationships\npriority: 50\ntitle: Two Dumonts in the Photograph\nart: snow\nwhen: flag(dumont_reunited)\ntext: In the graduation photograph, at the edge of the crowd, two pale heads lean together: Kael, and the sister who was raised to open the door he refused. Neither of them is smiling at the camera. They are smiling at each other, which is rarer, and which you helped to happen.\n\n@ending ending_ilse_taken\nslot: relationships\npriority: 45\ntitle: A Chair Left Empty\nart: rain\nwhen: flag(ilse_taken) and not flag(dumont_reunited)\ntext: The man in grey took Ilse north, and no letter has come. At graduation Kael keeps the seat beside him empty without saying why. You know why. Some evenings, when the lake goes very still, you still look for a girl in white furs on the far shore.\n\n# ---------------- Personal lines (yearbook) ----------------\n\n@ending ending_thesis_hollowstar\nslot: personal\npriority: 35\ntitle: The Thesis They Locked Away\nart: book\nwhen: flag(thesis_hollowstar)\ntext: Your thesis on the founders' seal earned a distinction and then disappeared into the headmistress's tower. Its catalogue card in the Spire Library reads only \"Restricted\", in her handwriting. Three people in the world have read it. One of them still writes to you with questions.\n\n@ending ending_lumen\nslot: personal\npriority: 30\ntitle: A Lantern for Others\nart: lantern\nwhen: alignment >= 60\ntext: In the yearbook, under your portrait, someone has written: \"The one you went to when it mattered.\" First-years you barely remember will name their own lanterns after you. Lumenhold has a word for people like that. It is the same word as its name.\n\n@ending ending_hollow_touched\nslot: personal\npriority: 30\ntitle: The Shadow at Your Heel\nart: mask\nwhen: alignment <= -60\ntext: Your portrait in the yearbook is the only one where the candles behind you seem to have gone out. People step aside when you pass, not quite knowing why. The Hollow has learned your name, and you find, more and more often, that you answer to it.\n"},{"path":"content/events/alignment.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Alignment (design §8.2): Lumen (+) ↔ Hollow (−).\n#  Thresholds ±30 and ±60 open these events; characters tagged\n#  `virtuous` or `ruthless` (content/world/cast.grim) react to\n#  where you stand. Deep in the Hollow the way back is slow\n#  (the resource's drag), so the way-back events cost real effort.\n#  Hollow Star plot scenes that read alignment live in\n#  chain_hollowstar.grim (hs_grey_invitation, hs_grey_tests,\n#  hs_wardens_notice).\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- Hollow (alignment <= -30) ----------------\n\n@event align_cold_candles\ntitle: The Candles Lean Away\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nwhen: alignment <= -30\nrepeat: 12\nweight: 10\nart: candle\ntags: hollow\nscene: A long stone corridor at dusk, every candle flame bending away from one student walking down the middle of it.\ntext: {You notice it on the east stair. As you pass, the candle flames bend away from you, all of them, like grass from a wind. A first-year on the landing notices too.|The candles lean away again. You have started to time it. It is getting faster.}\n> left: Let them lean\n  guile +small\n  alignment -small\n  renown -tiny\n  say: You walk on without looking. Behind you the first-year says nothing at all, which is its own kind of saying.\n> right: Stop and relight one by hand\n  mood -small\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: You cup a flame back to life with your palm. It flickers, considers you, and stays. It takes longer than it should.\n\n@event align_whispered_answer\ntitle: A Voice Over Your Shoulder\nclass: routine\nslot: night\ntone: eerie\nwhen: alignment <= -30 and term\nrepeat: 12\nweight: 13\nart: eye\ntags: hollow study\nscene: A student bent over a desk by candlelight, revision notes everywhere, and a cold patch of frost forming on the inkwell.\ntext: Revising late, you get stuck on the same question for the third time. Then the answer is simply there, in a voice that is almost yours, from just behind your left ear. The inkwell has frosted over.\n> left: Write it down\n  lore +small\n  alignment -small\n  say: It is right. Of course it is right. You write until dawn and never once get stuck, and in the morning you cannot remember learning any of it.\n> right: Close the book\n  mood -small\n  alignment +small\n  say: You close the book and sit with your hands flat on the cover until the frost on the inkwell melts. The question stays unanswered. It's yours, at least.\n\n@event align_maud_way_back\ntitle: Weeding, Dearie\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nactor: maud\ntrigger: social\nwhen: alignment <= -30 and met(maud)\nrepeat: 8\nweight: 14\nart: leaf\ntags: hollow nature\nscene: An old woman in a straw hat kneeling in a vegetable bed, handing a trowel up to a student who hasn't asked for one.\ntext: Old Maud looks at you for a long moment over her spectacles, the way she looks at a plant that's been kept in the wrong window. \"You've a cold look about you, dearie,\" she says, and holds out a trowel. \"Weeds don't pull themselves. Neither does the rest.\"\n> left: Kneel and start pulling\n  health -small\n  alignment +medium\n  rel maud +small\n  say: You weed until your knees ache and your hands smell of earth instead of frost. Maud hums the whole time. Nothing's fixed. Something has started.\n> right: \"I'm fine.\"\n  rel maud -small\n  mood +tiny\n  say: \"Course you are,\" Maud says, and goes back to her beans. The trowel stays in the soil beside her, handle towards you, all afternoon.\n\n@event align_reproach\ntitle: \"I Don't Know You Lately\"\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ncast:\n  friend: any where tagged(friend, virtuous) and rel(friend) >= 6\nper: friend\ntrigger: social\nwhen: alignment <= -30\nrepeat: 12\nweight: 12\nart: heartbreak\ntags: social hollow\nscene: Two friends on a cold bench in the cloister, one looking straight ahead, the other looking at the first.\ntext: {friend} finds you in the cloister and sits without saying hello. \"People talk,\" {they} say{s} at last. \"I keep telling them they're wrong about you. I'd like to keep being right.\"\n> left: \"You are. I'll do better.\"\n  alignment +small\n  mood -small\n  rel friend +small\n  say: {friend} nods, slowly, and bumps {their} shoulder against yours. It is an apology and a warning at once.\n> right: \"Mind your own business.\"\n  rel friend -medium\n  alignment -tiny\n  guile +tiny\n  say: {friend} gets up and goes. {They} don't look back. That's what stays with you: {they} always used to look back.\n\n@event align_ruthless_nod\ntitle: Finally, Someone Who Gets It\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ncast:\n  other: any where tagged(other, ruthless)\nper: other\ntrigger: social\nwhen: alignment <= -30\nrepeat: 12\nweight: 10\nart: mask\ntags: social hollow\nscene: A hard-faced student leaning on a pillar, giving another a slow, approving nod across a busy corridor.\ntext: {rel(other) < 10: {other} has always looked straight through you. Today {they} nod{s} as you pass|{other} catches your eye in the corridor and nod{s} again}, slow and approving, the way people nod at someone who has finally learned the rules of a game.\n> left: Nod back\n  rel other +medium\n  alignment -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: The nod comes back to you. It feels like being let into a room. You can't tell whether the room is warm.\n> right: Keep walking\n  rel other -small\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: You keep walking. {other} snorts, as if you'd failed a test you didn't know you were sitting.\n\n@event align_northern_parcel\ntitle: A Parcel for the North\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: alignment <= -30\nrepeat: 12\nweight: 13\nart: pouch\ntags: hollow money\nscene: A hooded courier in a rain-dark alley behind the village, holding out a small parcel wrapped in black oilcloth and a purse.\ntext: {A hooded courier stops you behind the bakery.|The hooded courier is waiting again, same alley, same black oilcloth.} \"For the boathouse, after dark,\" {they say, and hold out the parcel and a purse.|they say. \"The families remember who is reliable.\"} The oilcloth is cold enough to ache.\n> left: Carry it\n  gold +medium\n  alignment -medium\n  health -tiny\n  say: You leave it on the boathouse step at midnight and don't look back. In the morning there's frost on the step in the shape of a hand, and the purse is heavier than it looked.\n> right: Walk away\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: You leave the courier holding it. \"Pity,\" they say, without any pity at all, and the alley is empty when you look back.\n\n@event align_cruel_advantage\ntitle: Something You Could Use\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ncast:\n  mark: any where tagged(mark, student) and rel(mark) < 12\nper: mark\nwhen: alignment <= -30\nrepeat: 12\nweight: 13\nart: scroll\ntags: social hollow\nscene: A crumpled letter on a library floor, a student's name on the envelope, another student bending to pick it up.\ntext: {mark} has dropped a letter in the library: something private, something {they} would hate the corridor to hear. You could hand it back. You could also keep it, and {they} would owe you, quietly, for a long time.\n> left: Keep it\n  guile +small\n  alignment -small\n  rel mark -small\n  say: You fold it into your book. When you mention it, very lightly, {mark} goes pale and agrees to everything. It works. It keeps working.\n> right: Hand it back\n  rel mark +small\n  mood -tiny\n  alignment +small\n  say: You hand it back unread. {mark} stares at you, then at the letter, then says \"thank you\" as if it costs something. It does, for both of you.\n\n@event align_frost_in_the_spell\ntitle: The Cold That Helps\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nwhen: alignment <= -30 and term\nrepeat: 12\nweight: 13\nart: snow\ntags: hollow study\nscene: A student in an empty practice room, a spell flaring unnaturally bright in one hand, frost creeping up the sleeve to the elbow.\ntext: Practising alone, you notice that your spells come easier when you let the cold in, the cold that has been following you lately. It climbs your sleeve like ivy. The candle beside you leans away.\n> left: Let the cold in\n  arcana +small\n  alignment -small\n  health -tiny\n  say: The spell flares bright and clean, brighter than anything you've managed in daylight. It takes an hour for the feeling to come back into your fingers. You don't mind as much as you should.\n> right: Practise the slow way\n  arcana +tiny\n  mood -small\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: You shake the cold out of your hands and do it the slow way, badly, again and again. It is your own bad spell, at least.\n\n# ---------------- Lumen (alignment >= 30) ----------------\n\n@event align_asked_for_help\ntitle: \"You're the One People Ask\"\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: alignment >= 30\nrepeat: 12\nweight: 10\nart: lantern\ntags: social lumen\nscene: A nervous first-year with an armful of torn notes hovering at the door of a common room, looking at one particular older student.\ntext: {~A first-year hovers by your table with a torn essay and a face like a wet weekend.|A first-year you've never met asks if you're the one who helps people. Apparently you are.|Someone has written your name on the common-room board under \"ask them\".} \"Somebody said you'd know what to do,\" {~they say, as if that settles it.|they say, and then wait.|they say, pointing back at the board.}\n> left: Sit them down and help\n  health -small\n  renown +small\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: It takes the whole evening and half your sleep. They leave lighter than they came. So, oddly, do you.\n> right: Point them to a tutor\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You give them a name and a smile and get back to your own work. They look disappointed for a moment, then grateful anyway.\n\n@event align_warm_regard\ntitle: A Good Word\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ncast:\n  friend: any where tagged(friend, virtuous) and rel(friend) >= 3\nper: friend\ntrigger: social\nwhen: alignment >= 30\nrepeat: 12\nweight: 10\nart: heart\ntags: social lumen\nscene: Two students sharing a pot of tea in a sunny window seat, one pushing the last biscuit towards the other.\ntext: {friend} pushes the last biscuit across to you. \"People talk, you know. About the kind things you do when you think nobody's looking,\" {they} say{s}. \"Don't make a face. It's good. Someone should say so.\"\n> left: Accept the biscuit, and the praise\n  rel friend +small\n  mood +small\n  gold -tiny\n  say: You eat the biscuit. {friend} looks very pleased. You buy the next pot.\n> right: Say anyone would have done the same\n  rel friend +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: \"Anyone would,\" you say. \"Nobody did,\" says {friend}, and lets you change the subject, but the look {they} give{s} you says it has only been postponed.\n\n@event align_saint_jibe\ntitle: \"Saint\"\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ncast:\n  other: any where tagged(other, ruthless) and tagged(other, peer)\nper: other\ntrigger: social\nwhen: alignment >= 30\nrepeat: 12\nweight: 10\nart: scales\ntags: social lumen\nscene: A smirking student blocking a doorway with one arm, another student standing in front of them with an armful of books.\ntext: {other} blocks the doorway with one arm. \"Here comes the saint,\" {they} say{s}, loud enough for the corridor. \"Tell me, does it get heavy, being better than everyone?\"\n> left: Let it go\n  mood -small\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: You wait. Eventually {other} gets bored and drops the arm. Two first-years behind you look at you like you've done something brave. You're not sure you have.\n> right: Give as good as you get\n  rel other -small\n  mood +small\n  guile +tiny\n  say: Your answer is quick and much funnier than theirs. The corridor laughs. {other} goes red. It feels wonderful for about an hour.\n"},{"path":"content/events/calendar_2b.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  The four-year loop's fixed beats (design §4, §7; phase 2b).\n#  These replace the old calendar menus and exams, which are\n#  retired by tools/migrations/0001-retire-v02-calendar.js.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- Enrolment (design §7.3): two courses a term ----------------\n\n@event enrolment\ntitle: Enrolment\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: term and (month == 1 or month == 7)\nrepeat: 6\npriority: 80\ntype: menu\noffers: courses\npick: 2\nclass: story\ntone: light\nart: scroll\ntags: class study\nscene: The registrar's hall at the start of term, students queueing at long tables with course lists that rewrite themselves.\ntext: {term_index == 1: Your first term. The registrar slides a list across the desk: four courses open to first-years.|A new term, and a new list. Courses you've passed are gone from it; new levels have appeared.} {member(thesis): Choose one; the thesis takes your other slot.|Choose two.} Course fees are eight crowns.\n> none: There is nothing left for you to take\n  when: offered(courses) == 0\n  say: The registrar checks the list twice. Nothing on it is open to you this term. \"Enjoy the quiet,\" she says, without much conviction.\n\n# ---------------- Club Fair (design §7.4): one club at a time ----------------\n\n@event club_fair_v3\ntitle: The Club Fair\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: month == 2\nrepeat: 12\npriority: 60\ntype: menu\noffers: clubs\nclass: story\ntone: light\nart: group\ntags: club festival\nscene: The courtyard full of recruiting tables, banners snapping, a duelling demonstration drawing a crowd.\ntext: The Club Fair fills the courtyard. Every table wants you; every captain swears theirs is the one that matters. {rel(lyra) >= 6: Lyra is at the Duelling Circle's table, pretending not to watch which way you walk.|}\n> none: {member(club): Stay with your club|Not this year}\n  hint: {member(club): Another year with the same table.|You can join at next year's fair.}\n  say: {member(club): {~You stop by your own club's table to help hand out leaflets. Someone asks you if it's worth joining. You say yes and mean it.|You walk past every other table with the ease of someone already spoken for.}|You collect three leaflets and a free biscuit, and sign nothing.}\n> quit: Resign from your club\n  when: member(club)\n  hint: You can join again at next year's fair, as a new member.\n  leave club\n  say: {~You hand back your club pin at the table where you got it. It feels smaller than it did.|You resign. Your evenings are yours again, and nobody will expect you anywhere.}\n\n# ---------------- The Job Board (design §7.4): visiting it is the change event ----------------\n\n@event job_board_visit\ntitle: The Job Board\nplace: job_board\nrepeat: 1\nweight: 30\ntype: menu\noffers: jobs\nclass: story\ntone: light\nart: paper\ntags: job money\nscene: A cork board crowded with notices, a student running a finger down a column of shift times.\ntext: Notices for work, three layers deep: the library, the Gilded Newt, Pell & Daughters, the courier loft. Each wants someone reliable. Each says so twice.\n> look: Just looking\n  say: You read every notice twice and take none of them down.\n> quit: Hand in your notice\n  when: member(job)\n  hint: You can find work again later.\n  leave job\n  mood +small\n  say: You hand in your notice. Your evenings are yours again; so is your empty purse.\n\n# ---------------- Break plans (design §4.3) ----------------\n\n@event break_plan\ntitle: The Break\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: break and (month == 5 or month == 11) and not wrapup\nrepeat: 6\npriority: 75\ntype: menu\nclass: story\ntone: warm\nart: sun\ntags: home\nscene: The academy gates thrown open, barges loading trunks, a few students lingering on the steps with nowhere they'd rather be.\ntext: {month == 5: The winter break.|The summer break.} Two months without lectures. The castle empties, mostly.\n> castle: Stay at the castle\n  hint: Quiet corridors and secrets. Board 8 crowns a month.\n  gain plan_castle\n  say: The castle in the holidays is a different building: louder echoes, kinder staff, doors you've never seen open.\n> home: Go home\n  hint: Family, old friends, free board.\n  gain plan_home\n  say: The barge, the coach, the last mile on foot. Home is smaller than you remember, and warmer.\n> work: Work on campus\n  hint: A holiday gig that pays well. Board 8 crowns a month.\n  gain plan_work\n  queue holiday_gigs now\n  say: You trade the holidays for wages. Your feet will remember it.\n> intern: Start your internship\n  when: member(internship) and month == 11\n  hint: A stipend, a supervisor and a project.\n  gain plan_internship\n  say: You pack a trunk of notebooks and set off {member(intern_expedition): up the long road to the high plateau|{member(intern_field_station): across the moors to the field station|{member(intern_thornwick): on the coach to Thornwick, of all places|on the long coach to the capital}}}.\n\n# ---------------- Track notices (pushed by the engine at term end) ----------------\n\n@event course_exam\ntitle: Exam Day\ntrigger: queued\nclass: story\ntone: tense\nart: scroll\ntags: study class\nscene: A silent exam hall in late light, rows of bent heads, an hourglass on the invigilator's desk.\ntext: Exam day for {track}. The hall is silent except for quills and somebody's knee bouncing under a desk. {~The paper is long.|The first question is one you revised.|The first question is one you didn't.|The invigilator turns the hourglass with a click you feel in your teeth.}\n> left: Answer what you know, carefully\n  sit_exam steady\n  say: {~You write slowly: everything you're sure of, and nothing you aren't.|You check every answer twice and hand in at the bell.|The first question is one you didn't revise, so you leave it and bank the rest.|You pace yourself by the hourglass and finish with sand to spare.}\n> right: Go for top marks\n  sit_exam wild\n  say: {~You take the hardest question first and gamble the essay on an idea you had at breakfast.|You write the answer you'd want to read, not the safe one.|The first question is one you didn't revise. You answer it anyway, boldly, from first principles.|You race the hourglass and write until the last grain, and past it.}\n\n@event track_warning\ntitle: A Word About Your Attendance\ntrigger: queued\nclass: incident\ntone: tense\nart: paper\ntags: job club\nscene: A folded note on official paper, the words \"performance\" and \"concern\" underlined twice.\ntext: A note about {track}: your attendance and your efforts have slipped, and it has been noticed. One more term like this and there will be consequences.\n> left: Promise to do better\n  mood -small\n  say: You write back a promise in your neatest hand. Now you'll have to keep it.\n> right: Let it slide\n  perform track -small\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You put the note in a drawer and skip the next session to prove a point. The note doesn't stop existing in the drawer.\n\n# Every job at term end (playtest 2, M33): stay on for another term, or resign.\n@event job_term_review\ntitle: Another Term?\ntrigger: queued\nclass: milestone\ntone: light\nart: coin\ntags: job money\nscene: A staff rota pinned to a door, last term's names crossed off, a blank column waiting for next term's.\ntext: The term is over, and so is the rota for {track}. A fresh one goes up for next term, and there's a gap where your name usually goes. {~Nobody would blame you either way.|Your feet have an opinion. So does your purse.|Someone has already pencilled a question mark next to it.}\n> left: Stay on next term\n  say: {~You write your name in the gap. Another term of shifts, another term of wages.|Your name goes back on the rota, in ink this time.|You rub out the question mark and write your name over it.}\n> right: Resign\n  leave track\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {~You hand in your notice, politely. The evenings are yours again; the purse is lighter for it.|You leave the gap empty. You can always find work again, if you need to.|You leave the question mark where it is. Someone else can answer it.}\n\n@event track_promotion\ntitle: A Step Up\ntrigger: queued\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nart: trophy\ntags: job club\nscene: A small ceremony in a cramped room, someone pinning a badge to a student's collar while the others applaud.\ntext: {track} wants to promote you. More responsibility, more respect, and in the right places, more pay.\n> left: Accept\n  promote track\n  renown +small\n  mood +small\n  say: The badge is a little crooked. You don't straighten it.\n> right: Not yet\n  mood +tiny\n  say: \"When you're ready, then.\" They mean it. The offer will come again.\n\n# ---------------- Research internship (design §7.5; the Royal Library is the slice host) ----------------\n\n@event intern_application\ntitle: Applications Open\ntrigger: auto\nrepeat: 2\npriority: 50\ntype: menu\npick: 6\nwhen: month == 7 and year >= 2 and year <= 3\nclass: story\ntone: light\nart: letter\ntags: job study\nscene: A cluster of notices with wax seals pinned above all the others: summer research posts, each with its own demands.\ntext: Summer research posts are open. Six notices, six seals, six lists of requirements. Apply to as many as you like: the first letter is an evening's work, and every one after it costs another evening and the postage.\non_show:\n  intern_letters = 0\n> royal: The Royal Library\n  when: level(astromancy) >= 2 and not member(intern_royal_library)\n  locked: {member(intern_royal_library): They've already asked you back.|Needs Astromancy II.}\n  hint: GPA 2.5, an interview on lore, a love of indexes.\n  apply intern_royal_library\n  intern_letters += 1\n  if intern_letters >= 2 and gold >= 8:\n    gold -small\n    mood -tiny\n    say: {~Another letter, another evening, another stamp. Your hand aches in a very particular place.|You write it once, cross out half, write it again. The postage comes out of your own purse.}\n  elif intern_letters < 2:\n    charm +tiny\n    say: You write the letter three times and send the best one. Probably the best one.\n  else:\n    leave intern_royal_library\n    mood -tiny\n    say: You write the letter, and then find you can't pay the postage. It stays on your desk.\n> conclave: The Conclave Laboratory\n  when: level(evocation) >= 2 and not member(intern_conclave)\n  locked: {member(intern_conclave): They've already asked you back.|Needs Evocation II.}\n  hint: GPA 3.0, an interview on arcana, and a word from Thorne helps.\n  apply intern_conclave\n  intern_letters += 1\n  if intern_letters >= 2 and gold >= 8:\n    gold -small\n    mood -tiny\n    say: {~Another letter, another evening, another stamp. Your hand aches in a very particular place.|You write it once, cross out half, write it again. The postage comes out of your own purse.}\n  elif intern_letters < 2:\n    charm +tiny\n    say: You write the letter three times and send the best one. Probably the best one.\n  else:\n    leave intern_conclave\n    mood -tiny\n    say: You write the letter, and then find you can't pay the postage. It stays on your desk.\n> alchemists: The Guild of Alchemists' Works\n  when: level(alchemy) >= 2 and not member(intern_alchemists)\n  locked: {member(intern_alchemists): They've already asked you back.|Needs Alchemy II.}\n  hint: GPA 2.0, an interview on alchemy, and a strong stomach.\n  apply intern_alchemists\n  intern_letters += 1\n  if intern_letters >= 2 and gold >= 8:\n    gold -small\n    mood -tiny\n    say: {~Another letter, another evening, another stamp. Your hand aches in a very particular place.|You write it once, cross out half, write it again. The postage comes out of your own purse.}\n  elif intern_letters < 2:\n    charm +tiny\n    say: You write the letter three times and send the best one. Probably the best one.\n  else:\n    leave intern_alchemists\n    mood -tiny\n    say: You write the letter, and then find you can't pay the postage. It stays on your desk.\n> expedition: The Observatory Expedition\n  when: level(astromancy) >= 2 and not member(intern_expedition)\n  locked: {member(intern_expedition): They've already asked you back.|Needs Astromancy II.}\n  hint: An interview on vigor, and the legs for a plateau.\n  apply intern_expedition\n  intern_letters += 1\n  if intern_letters >= 2 and gold >= 8:\n    gold -small\n    mood -tiny\n    say: {~Another letter, another evening, another stamp. Your hand aches in a very particular place.|You write it once, cross out half, write it again. The postage comes out of your own purse.}\n  elif intern_letters < 2:\n    charm +tiny\n    say: You write the letter three times and send the best one. Probably the best one.\n  else:\n    leave intern_expedition\n    mood -tiny\n    say: You write the letter, and then find you can't pay the postage. It stays on your desk.\n> field: The Beastcraft Field Station\n  when: level(beastcraft) >= 2 and not member(intern_field_station)\n  locked: {member(intern_field_station): They've already asked you back.|Needs Beastcraft II.}\n  hint: An interview on vigor. Boots provided, mostly.\n  apply intern_field_station\n  intern_letters += 1\n  if intern_letters >= 2 and gold >= 8:\n    gold -small\n    mood -tiny\n    say: {~Another letter, another evening, another stamp. Your hand aches in a very particular place.|You write it once, cross out half, write it again. The postage comes out of your own purse.}\n  elif intern_letters < 2:\n    charm +tiny\n    say: You write the letter three times and send the best one. Probably the best one.\n  else:\n    leave intern_field_station\n    mood -tiny\n    say: You write the letter, and then find you can't pay the postage. It stays on your desk.\n> thornwick: The Thornwick Exchange\n  when: level(illusion) >= 2 and not member(intern_thornwick)\n  locked: {member(intern_thornwick): They've already asked you back.|Needs Illusion II.}\n  hint: GPA 2.5, an interview on charm. Bring a thick skin.\n  apply intern_thornwick\n  intern_letters += 1\n  if intern_letters >= 2 and gold >= 8:\n    gold -small\n    mood -tiny\n    say: {~Another letter, another evening, another stamp. Your hand aches in a very particular place.|You write it once, cross out half, write it again. The postage comes out of your own purse.}\n  elif intern_letters < 2:\n    charm +tiny\n    say: You write the letter three times and send the best one. Probably the best one.\n  else:\n    leave intern_thornwick\n    mood -tiny\n    say: You write the letter, and then find you can't pay the postage. It stays on your desk.\n> none: Not this year\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You let the notices flap. There's always next year, until there isn't.\n\n@event intern_offer\ntitle: Letters With Seals\ntrigger: auto\ntype: menu\nwhen: month == 9 and year >= 2 and year <= 3 and (applied(internship) or (guile >= 35 and rel(isolde) >= 6 and not member(intern_unseen_office)))\nrepeat: 12\npriority: 55\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nart: letter\ntags: study\nscene: Heavy envelopes with wax seals on a dormitory desk, one of them plain grey with no seal at all.\ntext: The spring post brings answers. {applied(internship): There are sealed letters on your desk. Which do you open first?|There are no more sealed letters.} {guile >= 35 and rel(isolde) >= 6 and not member(intern_unseen_office): There is also a plain grey envelope you never asked for.|}\n> royal: Letter from the Royal Library\n  when: applied(intern_royal_library)\n  hint: They asked for a GPA of 2.5. The interview tests lore.\n  if gpa >= 2.5:\n    challenge research adept-:\n      win:\n        join intern_royal_library\n        renown +small\n        leave intern_conclave\n        leave intern_alchemists\n        leave intern_expedition\n        leave intern_field_station\n        leave intern_thornwick\n        say: \"We are pleased.\" You read the letter until the seal cracks. A summer in the oldest archives in the realm.\n      lose:\n        leave intern_royal_library\n        say: \"We regret.\" The interview went well, they write, and not quite well enough.\n        if applied(internship):\n          queue intern_offer now\n  else:\n    leave intern_royal_library\n    say: \"We regret.\" They wanted a GPA of 2.5, and they say so twice.\n    if applied(internship):\n      queue intern_offer now\n> conclave: Letter from the Conclave\n  when: applied(intern_conclave)\n  hint: They asked for a GPA of 3.0. The interview tests arcana. A word from Thorne would help.\n  if gpa >= 3.0:\n    if rel(thorne) >= 6:\n      challenge research using arcana adept likely:\n        win:\n          join intern_conclave\n          renown +small\n          leave intern_royal_library\n          leave intern_alchemists\n          leave intern_expedition\n          leave intern_field_station\n          leave intern_thornwick\n          say: \"We are pleased.\" You read the letter until the seal cracks. A summer in spell research under the Seven Towers.\n        lose:\n          leave intern_conclave\n          say: \"We regret.\" The interview went well, they write, and not quite well enough.\n          if applied(internship):\n            queue intern_offer now\n    else:\n      challenge research using arcana adept unlikely:\n        win:\n          join intern_conclave\n          renown +small\n          leave intern_royal_library\n          leave intern_alchemists\n          leave intern_expedition\n          leave intern_field_station\n          leave intern_thornwick\n          say: \"We are pleased.\" You read the letter until the seal cracks. A summer in spell research under the Seven Towers.\n        lose:\n          leave intern_conclave\n          say: \"We regret.\" The interview went well, they write, and not quite well enough.\n          if applied(internship):\n            queue intern_offer now\n  else:\n    leave intern_conclave\n    say: \"We regret.\" They wanted a GPA of 3.0, and they say so twice.\n    if applied(internship):\n      queue intern_offer now\n> alchemists: Letter from the Alchemists' Works\n  when: applied(intern_alchemists)\n  hint: They asked for a GPA of 2.0. The interview tests alchemy.\n  if gpa >= 2.0:\n    challenge research using alchemy journeyman+:\n      win:\n        join intern_alchemists\n        renown +small\n        leave intern_royal_library\n        leave intern_conclave\n        leave intern_expedition\n        leave intern_field_station\n        leave intern_thornwick\n        say: \"We are pleased.\" You read the letter until the seal cracks. A summer in the great vats of the Guild.\n      lose:\n        leave intern_alchemists\n        say: \"We regret.\" The interview went well, they write, and not quite well enough.\n        if applied(internship):\n          queue intern_offer now\n  else:\n    leave intern_alchemists\n    say: \"We regret.\" They wanted a GPA of 2.0, and they say so twice.\n    if applied(internship):\n      queue intern_offer now\n> expedition: Letter from the Expedition\n  when: applied(intern_expedition)\n  hint: The interview tests vigor.\n  challenge research using vigor journeyman+:\n    win:\n      join intern_expedition\n      renown +small\n      leave intern_royal_library\n      leave intern_conclave\n      leave intern_alchemists\n      leave intern_field_station\n      leave intern_thornwick\n      say: \"We are pleased.\" You read the letter until the seal cracks. A summer of sky charts on the high plateau.\n    lose:\n      leave intern_expedition\n      say: \"We regret.\" The interview went well, they write, and not quite well enough.\n      if applied(internship):\n        queue intern_offer now\n> field: Letter from the Field Station\n  when: applied(intern_field_station)\n  hint: The interview tests vigor.\n  challenge research using vigor journeyman+:\n    win:\n      join intern_field_station\n      renown +small\n      leave intern_royal_library\n      leave intern_conclave\n      leave intern_alchemists\n      leave intern_expedition\n      leave intern_thornwick\n      say: \"We are pleased.\" You read the letter until the seal cracks. A summer in the moors, the heather and the creatures in them.\n    lose:\n      leave intern_field_station\n      say: \"We regret.\" The interview went well, they write, and not quite well enough.\n      if applied(internship):\n        queue intern_offer now\n> thornwick: Letter from Thornwick\n  when: applied(intern_thornwick)\n  hint: They asked for a GPA of 2.5. The interview tests charm.\n  if gpa >= 2.5:\n    challenge research using charm adept-:\n      win:\n        join intern_thornwick\n        renown +small\n        leave intern_royal_library\n        leave intern_conclave\n        leave intern_alchemists\n        leave intern_expedition\n        leave intern_field_station\n        say: \"We are pleased.\" You read the letter until the seal cracks. A summer in the rival academy's illusion laboratory.\n      lose:\n        leave intern_thornwick\n        say: \"We regret.\" The interview went well, they write, and not quite well enough.\n        if applied(internship):\n          queue intern_offer now\n  else:\n    leave intern_thornwick\n    say: \"We regret.\" They wanted a GPA of 2.5, and they say so twice.\n    if applied(internship):\n      queue intern_offer now\n> unseen: Open the plain envelope\n  when: guile >= 35 and rel(isolde) >= 6 and not member(intern_unseen_office)\n  join intern_unseen_office\n  guile +small\n  leave intern_royal_library\n  leave intern_conclave\n  leave intern_alchemists\n  leave intern_expedition\n  leave intern_field_station\n  leave intern_thornwick\n  say: Inside: a time, an address and no signature. The paper is blank again by morning. You go anyway.\n> decline: Fold the letters away\n  leave intern_royal_library\n  leave intern_conclave\n  leave intern_alchemists\n  leave intern_expedition\n  leave intern_field_station\n  leave intern_thornwick\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You fold them carefully. Next year, perhaps.\n\n# The end of a placement (feedback 2026-10-15b): the supervisor's report, by performance. A strong one (80+) brings an\n# offer to come back (M16); otherwise the post ends.\n@event intern_assessment\ntitle: The Supervisor's Report\ntrigger: queued\npriority: 90\nclass: milestone\ntone: tense\nart: paper\ntags: study job\nscene: A supervisor's desk on the last day of a placement, a written report face down under a pen.\ntext: Your last day at {member(intern_royal_library): the Royal Library Archives|{member(intern_conclave): the Conclave Laboratory|{member(intern_alchemists): the Guild of Alchemists' Works|{member(intern_expedition): the Observatory Expedition|{member(intern_field_station): the Beastcraft Field Station|{member(intern_thornwick): the Thornwick Exchange|the office with no name}}}}}}. Your supervisor turns the report over. {performance(internship) >= 80: \"Exceptional,\" it says at the top, underlined, and the rest is better.|{performance(internship) >= 60: \"Reliable and capable.\" A few notes in the margin, all of them fair.|{performance(internship) >= 40: \"Adequate.\" The word sits on the page by itself.|\"Did not meet expectations.\" Your supervisor doesn't quite look at you.}}}\n> left: Ask for a letter of reference\n  if performance(internship) >= 60:\n    renown +small\n    say: The letter comes a week later, sealed, and the registrar files a copy with a look you haven't had from her before.\n  else:\n    mood -small\n    say: The letter comes a month later: two lines, your name spelled wrong in one of them. You put it at the bottom of a drawer.\n  if performance(internship) >= 80:\n    queue intern_asked_back\n  else:\n    leave internship\n> right: Ask what you could have done better\n  mood -tiny\n  if member(intern_royal_library) or member(intern_expedition):\n    lore +small\n  elif member(intern_conclave):\n    arcana +small\n  elif member(intern_alchemists):\n    alchemy +small\n  elif member(intern_field_station):\n    vigor +small\n  elif member(intern_thornwick):\n    charm +small\n  else:\n    guile +small\n  say: Your supervisor talks for twenty minutes without notes. Some of it stings. You write all of it down on the coach home, and read it twice.\n  if performance(internship) >= 80:\n    queue intern_asked_back\n  else:\n    leave internship\n\n# An internship lasts one summer (M16). The host asks you back only after a strong one.\n@event intern_asked_back\ntitle: Asked Back\ntrigger: queued\nwhen: year <= 3\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nart: letter\ntags: study job\nscene: A letter on good paper with a familiar seal, the words \"next summer\" underlined once, neatly.\ntext: A letter from {member(intern_royal_library): the Royal Library Archives|{member(intern_conclave): the Conclave Laboratory|{member(intern_alchemists): the Guild of Alchemists' Works|{member(intern_expedition): the Observatory Expedition|{member(intern_field_station): the Beastcraft Field Station|{member(intern_thornwick): the Thornwick Exchange|the office with no name}}}}}}. They were pleased with your summer, and the post is yours again next summer if you want it.\n> left: Write back yes\n  renown +tiny\n  say: {~You write back the same evening. Next summer is spoken for, and you find you're glad.|\"Yes,\" you write, and then three more lines to make it sound less eager.}\n> right: Thank them, and decline\n  leave internship\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {~You thank them warmly and say no. Next summer is yours to spend, or to apply for something new.|A polite no. You keep the letter anyway; it's good to be asked.}\n\n# ---------------- Thesis (design §7.6) ----------------\n\n@event thesis_setup\ntitle: Choosing a Thesis\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: year == 4 and month == 1 and not member(thesis)\nrepeat: 12\npriority: 85\ntype: menu\nclass: story\ntone: tense\nart: book\ntags: study\nscene: A professor's study, three topic proposals laid out on the desk like cards.\ntext: The final year opens with a thesis. Before you choose this term's courses, Professor Thorne lays out three topics, at three levels of ambition.\n> safe: A careful survey\n  hint: Easier, and it will read as easier.\n  join thesis\n  flag thesis_safe\n  say: A sensible topic. Thorne says \"Sensible,\" the way other people say \"Disappointing.\"\n> solid: An original study\n  hint: The usual path.\n  join thesis\n  flag thesis_solid\n  say: Thorne nods. \"Good. Bring me something I don't already know.\"\n> ambitious: Something no one has done\n  hint: Harder, and worth more.\n  join thesis\n  flag thesis_ambitious\n  say: Thorne is quiet for a long time. \"Hm,\" he says, and for once it sounds like hope.\n\n@event thesis_crunch\ntitle: The Crunch\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: final_year and month == 9 and member(thesis) and progress(thesis) < 60\nrepeat: 12\npriority: 40\nclass: incident\ntone: tense\nart: hourglass\ntags: study\nscene: A thesis carrel at dawn, a candle burned to a stub, drafts on the floor and a student asleep on the keyboard of a typing-quill.\ntext: The defence is next month and your thesis is not ready. Your advisor's note is three words long: \"Well? Final draft.\"\n> left: Work through the nights\n  progress thesis +large\n  health -medium\n  mood -medium\n  say: You stop seeing daylight. The draft gets better and you get worse, which seems to be how this works.\n> right: Hand in what you have\n  mood +tiny\n  progress thesis +small\n  say: You tidy what's there and accept the gaps. Your advisor sighs, which is not the same as no.\n\n@event thesis_defence\ntitle: The Defence\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: final_year and month == 10 and member(thesis)\nrepeat: 12\npriority: 65\nclass: milestone\ntone: tense\nart: scroll\ntags: study exam\nscene: A semicircular table with three examiners, a student standing at a lectern with a stack of notes.\ntext: Three examiners, one lectern, and a year of work. {progress(thesis) >= 60: You know this material better than anyone in the room.|There are gaps, and you know where they are.} First, you present. Then they ask.\n> left: Defend it\n  hint: A safe topic is easier to defend and earns less; an ambitious one is harder and earns more. A year of thesis work counts.\n  def_score = 0\n  if not (flag(thesis_safe) or flag(thesis_ambitious)):\n    challenge negotiate using lore expert:\n      win:\n        def_score = 2\n        say: Your presentation runs clean from first slide to last. One examiner has stopped taking notes and is just listening.\n      partial:\n        def_score = 1\n        say: A solid presentation with one wobble in the middle. You recover. They noticed the wobble.\n      lose:\n        mood -small\n        say: You lose your place twice and your nerve once. The examiners exchange a look you'll remember for years.\n  elif flag(thesis_safe):\n    challenge negotiate using lore expert likely:\n      win:\n        def_score = 2\n        say: A careful survey, carefully presented. Nobody could fault a word of it. Nobody looks thrilled, either.\n      partial:\n        def_score = 1\n        say: Careful, competent and a little dull, and you can see one examiner fighting a yawn.\n      lose:\n        mood -small\n        say: Even a safe topic has its gaps, and your presentation walks straight into one of them.\n  else:\n    challenge negotiate using lore expert unlikely:\n      win:\n        def_score = 2\n        if clue(seven_locks) + clue(star_key) + clue(ilse_truth) >= 3:\n          flag thesis_hollowstar\n        say: Something no one has done, laid out so clearly they can't look away. One examiner has put down their pen.\n      partial:\n        def_score = 1\n        say: They follow you most of the way, arguing among themselves about whether it's brilliant or mad. Possibly both.\n      lose:\n        mood -small\n        say: You reach too far in the first ten minutes, and spend the rest of the hour trying to get back. They let you.\n  if progress(thesis) >= 70:\n    def_score += 1\n    say: And under the presentation, a year of work they can feel. Every page they turn has been turned before.\n  elif progress(thesis) < 30:\n    def_score -= 1\n    say: Under the presentation, the thin chapters show. One examiner starts counting pages.\n  queue thesis_defence_question now\n> right: Open with your weakest chapter\n  hint: Name the gaps before they find them. Safe, and modest.\n  def_score = 1\n  mood -tiny\n  queue thesis_defence_question now\n  say: You start with what you couldn't prove, and why, and what you'd do next. It takes the wind out of their sails, and a little out of yours.\n  if progress(thesis) >= 70:\n    def_score += 1\n    say: The rest of it stands on a year of real work, and they can tell.\n  elif progress(thesis) < 30:\n    def_score -= 1\n    say: The trouble is how many chapters are weak. They notice that too.\n\n@event thesis_defence_question\ntitle: The Question\ntrigger: queued\nrepeat: 12\nclass: milestone\ntone: tense\nart: scroll\ntags: study exam\nscene: An examiner leaning over a semicircular table, pointing at one line in a thick bound thesis, the student at the lectern very still.\ntext: The examiner who hasn't spoken yet opens your thesis to a page near the end, puts a finger on one line and asks the question you hoped no one would ask.\n> left: Answer it head-on\n  challenge research expert:\n    win:\n      def_score += 1\n      say: You answer it, and then the question behind it, and then the one behind that. The examiner closes the thesis, slowly.\n    lose:\n      def_score -= 1\n      mood -small\n      say: Your answer starts well and ends somewhere you didn't mean to go. The examiner lets the silence do the rest.\n  queue thesis_defence_verdict now\n> right: Admit what you don't know\n  mood -tiny\n  if def_score >= 2:\n    def_score += 1\n    say: \"I don't know,\" you say, \"and here is how I'd find out.\" From someone who has just presented so well, it sounds like mastery. Two examiners nod.\n  else:\n    say: \"I don't know,\" you say, \"and here is how I'd find out.\" It stings a little, and earns you nothing. The examiner moves on.\n  queue thesis_defence_verdict now\n\n@event thesis_defence_verdict\ntitle: The Verdict\ntrigger: queued\nrepeat: 12\nclass: milestone\ntone: tense\nart: scroll\ntags: study exam\nscene: A student waiting alone on a bench in a long stone corridor outside a closed oak door, hands clasped, a clock on the wall.\ntext: They send you out. Twenty minutes on a bench in the corridor, with the clock and your heartbeat. Then the door opens, and they call you back in.\n> ok: Go back in\n  if def_score >= 3:\n    flag thesis_distinction\n    if flag(thesis_ambitious):\n      renown +huge\n    elif flag(thesis_safe):\n      renown +medium\n    else:\n      renown +large\n    say: They stop asking questions and start asking for copies. Distinction. One of them shakes your hand twice, as if the first didn't count.\n  elif def_score >= 1:\n    flag thesis_passed\n    if flag(thesis_ambitious):\n      renown +medium\n    else:\n      renown +small\n    say: A pass, with one examiner's handwriting all over your conclusion. \"Good work,\" says the chair, and means it, mostly.\n  else:\n    flag thesis_resubmit\n    mood -large\n    if flag(thesis_ambitious):\n      renown -small\n    say: They send you away to resubmit. It isn't the end. It feels like it. The idea was good, says the chair. The proof wasn't there yet.\n  # The defence closes the thesis: its lab events stop (B30).\n  leave thesis\n\n# ---------------- Festivals that take a visit (design §4.4) ----------------\n\n@event lantern_night_v3\ntitle: Lantern Night\nplace: lakeshore\nreplaces: visit2\nwhen: month == 2\nrepeat: 12\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nart: lantern\ntags: festival\nscene: The Mirror Lake at night, hundreds of paper lanterns drifting over the water, students crowding the shore.\ntext: Lantern Night. The whole academy gathers on the lakeshore with paper lanterns, each carrying a wish out over the water. {month == 2 and year == 1: Your first one.|Another year, another wish.}\n> left: Wish for something selfish\n  mood +medium\n  say: Your lantern goes out over the water and keeps its secret. You feel lighter for having asked.\n> right: Wish for someone else\n  mood +medium\n  say: You don't tell anyone who. The lantern seems to know; it flies straight.\n\n# ---------------- Calendar beats ----------------\n\n@event year_four\ntitle: The Final Year\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: turn == 37\npriority: 90\nclass: story\ntone: warm\nart: tower\ntags: story\nscene: The seven towers at dawn from the barge, the same view as the first day, but the student watching it has changed.\ntext: The barge again, the towers again, and a thesis waiting. The first-years on the deck look at you the way you once looked at the fourth-years. At the opening feast the headmistress says, \"Fourth years, this is your last year in these halls. What you become here, you will be for a long time.\" She looks, for a moment, directly at you.\n> ok: Here we go\n  mood +small\n  say: You know which steps creak and which doors stick. The thesis will want evenings of its own each term; something else may have to give, a club or a job.\n\n# ---------------- Appeals (playtest 2, N4): pushed after a failed course, in its context (@game appeal) ----------------\n\n@event grade_appeal\ntitle: The Appeal\ntrigger: queued\nclass: story\ntone: tense\nart: scales\ntags: study class\nscene: A long table of grey-robed examiners in a panelled room, a single student standing before them holding an exam paper.\ntext: The examiners' list is pinned up, and beside {track} there is an F. The clerk mentions, without looking up, that a student may appeal a grade in person, once, before the board. Most don't. Some of those who do come out with a C.\n> left: Appeal before the board\n  challenge negotiate expert unlikely drift low:\n    win:\n      regrade track C\n      say: You talk for ten minutes about the paper, the question and the answer they marked wrong. The board confers in whispers. The F becomes a C, in red ink, with a sigh.\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      renown -tiny\n      say: The board hears you out, then reads your paper aloud. It's worse aloud. The F stands, and the queue outside the door heard every word.\n> right: Accept it, and read the comments\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: The examiner's comments are fair, which is the worst thing about them. You copy out every one. Next time you'll know exactly where it went wrong.\n\n# ---------------- Lead-ins (playtest 2, N1): what's coming, before it comes ----------------\n\n@event registrar_list\ntitle: The Registrar's List\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: turn == 1\npriority: 85\nclass: story\ntone: light\nart: scroll\ntags: study\nscene: A crowd of new students around a long parchment list pinned in an entrance hall, one of them tracing a line with a finger.\ntext: Before enrolment, the registrar's list goes up in the entrance hall: each course, what it tests, which spells it teaches, and which open only next term. A second-year taps one. \"That one's hard,\" she says, then taps another. \"So's that.\"\n> ok: Read it twice\n  say: Two courses a term, a fee each time, and an exam at the end of each. Pass a level and you learn its spells.\n\n@event club_fair_flyer\ntitle: Flyers Under the Door\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: turn == 1\nslot: night\nclass: story\ntone: light\nart: paper\ntags: club\nscene: A dormitory floor scattered with colourful club flyers, more sliding under the door as a student watches.\ntext: One flyer slides under your door, then another, then nine. The Club Fair is next month in the courtyard: the Duelling Circle, the Lumen Ledger, the Verdant Circle. One club at a time, says the small print, and each wants a couple of evenings a term.\n> ok: Keep the flyers\n  say: You pin them over your bed and argue with them until you fall asleep.\n\n@event job_board_notice\ntitle: Hands Wanted\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: turn == 2 and not member(job)\nslot: noon\nclass: story\ntone: light\nart: paper\ntags: job money\nscene: A refectory door with a notice pinned to it, three different handwritings added beneath the printed part.\ntext: Pinned to the refectory door: \"Hands wanted. See the Job Board, entrance hall.\" Under it, in pencil: \"The Newt pays best. The library is warmest.\" Under that, in a third hand: \"Pell counts.\"\n> ok: Make a note of it\n  say: One job at a time, a few shifts a term, paid by the shift. The board is up from morning until evening.\n"},{"path":"content/events/calendar.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Calendar: the academy year's fixed beats.\n#  Most are `trigger: auto` — forced into the month's deck\n#  whenever their `when` is true (and `repeat` allows).\n#  Month 1 Harvestmoon ... 12 Starfall. 11–12 are summer recess.\n# ============================================================\n\n@event intro_arrival\ntitle: The Sky-Barge\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: queued\nart: tower\ntags: story\nscene: A wooden barge with patched sails drifting between clouds toward seven impossibly tall towers above a mirror-still lake.\ntext: The barge drops through the clouds and there it is: Lumenhold, seven towers stitched together with bridges of light. A first-year beside you is quietly being sick over the side. A prefect with a ledger counts heads and frowns at yours.\n> ok: Step onto the landing\n  say: Your boots touch stone that hums faintly underfoot. Four years. Forty-eight moons.\n\n@event choose_origin\ntitle: Who Were You Before?\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: queued\ntype: menu\nart: mirror\ntags: story\nscene: A tall enchanted mirror in the entrance hall, showing a different version of the viewer in each pane.\ntext: The Sorting Mirror in the entrance hall does not sort you into anything. It simply asks, in a voice like cold water, who you were before you came here. It waits.\n> noble: A younger child of minor nobility\n  hint: Charm, a known name, and a family allowance with strings attached.\n  gain origin_noble\n  say: The mirror shows you a crest you have tried to outgrow. It approves, faintly.\n> scholar: A provincial scholarship student\n  hint: Lore above all. The stipend depends on last term's grades.\n  gain origin_scholar\n  say: The mirror shows you a candle burning very late in a very small room.\n> hedge: Raised by a marsh hedge-witch\n  hint: Alchemy, some Arcana, good spirits. No money from home.\n  gain origin_hedge\n  say: The mirror smells, briefly, of wet moss and woodsmoke. You feel at home.\n> street: Raised on the streets of Port Calder\n  hint: Guile and grit. Little gold, and no one to send more.\n  gain origin_street\n  say: The mirror shows a pickpocket's quick hands. You check your pockets out of habit.\n> merchant: The heir to a trading company\n  hint: The biggest purse and a nose for bargains. The company pays when trade is good.\n  gain origin_merchant\n  say: The mirror shows ledgers, ships and your mother's disappointed face. Still: gold.\n\n@event sorting_mirror\ntitle: The Sorting Mirror\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: queued\nart: mirror\ntags: story\nscene: A tall enchanted mirror in the entrance hall, showing a different version of the viewer in each pane.\ntext: The Sorting Mirror in the entrance hall does not sort you into anything. It simply asks, in a voice like cold water, who you were before you came here. You tell it.\n> ok: Look into the mirror\n  say: {has(origin_noble): The mirror shows you a crest you have tried to outgrow. It approves, faintly.|{has(origin_scholar): The mirror shows you a candle burning very late in a very small room.|{has(origin_hedge): The mirror smells, briefly, of wet moss and woodsmoke. You feel at home.|{has(origin_street): The mirror shows a pickpocket's quick hands. You check your pockets out of habit.|The mirror shows ledgers, ships and your mother's disappointed face. Still: gold.}}}}\n\n@event wrapup_begins\ntitle: The Last Weeks\nclass: story\ntone: warm\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: wrapup and month == 11\npriority: 70\nart: tower\ntags: story\nscene: An emptying castle in late summer light, trunks stacked in a corridor, a notice pinned to the great hall door.\ntext: A notice on the great hall door: classes are over, duties are done, and final-years may stay on until graduation, board on the house. The castle is yours for one last summer.\n> ok: Make the most of it\n  mood +small\n  say: You walk the long way to breakfast, past every door you ever got lost behind.\n\n@event choose_elective\n# retired in phase 2b (was \"month == 1 or month == 6\"); replaced by enrolment (offers: courses, pick: 2)\ntitle: Choosing an Elective\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: false\nrepeat: 5\npriority: 45\ntype: menu\nart: scroll\ntags: study class retired\nscene: A long noticeboard covered in fluttering enchanted parchment, each sheet trying to shoulder the others aside.\ntext: The registrar's noticeboard flaps with sign-up sheets for this term's electives. Core lectures are compulsory; your elective is yours to choose. The sheets jostle for your attention.\n> pyro: Pyromancy\n  hint: +Arcana each month, small burns. Taught by Professor Thorne.\n  gain class_pyromancy\n> tide: Tidecalling\n  hint: +Arcana, +Vigor, lakeside calm.\n  gain class_tidecalling\n> illusion: Illusion & Glamour\n  hint: +Guile, +Charm.\n  gain class_illusion\n> alchemy: Practical Alchemy\n  hint: +Alchemy.\n  gain class_alchemy\n> divination: Divination\n  hint: +Lore, occasional gloomy omens.\n  gain class_divination\n> artifice: Artifice\n  hint: +Alchemy, +Lore.\n  gain class_artifice\n> beast: Beastcraft\n  hint: +Vigor, +Mood. Smells of hay.\n  gain class_beastcraft\n> astro: Astromancy\n  hint: +Lore, late nights.\n  gain class_astromancy\n> healing: Healing Arts\n  hint: +Alchemy, +Lore, a little health.\n  gain class_healing\n> umbral: Umbral Theory (restricted)\n  hint: Requires Lore 20 or second year. +Arcana, dark moods.\n  when: lore >= 20 or year >= 2\n  locked: Needs Lore 20, or your second year.\n  gain class_umbral\n  flag took_umbral\n\n@event club_fair\n# retired in phase 2b (was \"month == 2\"); replaced by club_fair_v3 (offers: clubs)\ntitle: The Club Fair\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: false\nrepeat: 12\npriority: 40\ntype: menu\nart: group\ntags: social club retired\nscene: The great courtyard filled with banners and stalls: a duelist juggling sparks, a choir floating a foot off the ground, a girl handing out newspapers that shout headlines.\ntext: The courtyard is a riot of banners. Every club wants you, or at least your membership fee. A broom whizzes overhead trailing a SKYRACE TRYOUTS banner; somewhere a choir is harmonising with a cat.\n> duel: Dueling Circle\n  hint: +Vigor +Arcana, bruises. Home of the Spring Tournament.\n  gain club_dueling\n  meet lyra\n> choir: Choir of the Spheres\n  hint: +Charm, +Mood.\n  gain club_choir\n> sky: Skyrace Squad\n  hint: +Vigor, crowd glory, crashes.\n  gain club_skyrace\n> ledger: The Lumen Ledger\n  hint: +Guile +Charm. Nessa Quill's student paper.\n  gain club_ledger\n  meet nessa\n> garden: Verdant Circle\n  hint: +Alchemy +Mood. Greenhouses and gossip.\n  gain club_garden\n  meet tobias\n> stars: Stargazers\n  hint: +Lore +Mood.\n  gain club_stargazers\n> none: Keep your evenings free\n  hint: No club (leaves your current club, if any).\n  lose club_dueling\n  lose club_choir\n  lose club_skyrace\n  lose club_ledger\n  lose club_garden\n  lose club_stargazers\n  mood +tiny\n\n@event job_board\n# retired in phase 2b (was \"month == 3 or month == 9\"); replaced by job_board_visit (the Job Board place)\ntitle: The Job Board\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: false\nrepeat: 5\npriority: 35\ntype: menu\nart: coin\ntags: job money retired\nscene: A cork board outside the bursar's office, papered with notices; one is on fire, one is wriggling.\ntext: Rent is ten crowns a month and your purse is a fragile thing. The board outside the bursar's office offers honest work, and some that is merely work.\n> library: Library Assistant — 10 gold/month\n  hint: +Lore, a little dull.\n  gain job_library\n> tavern: The Gilded Newt — 16 gold/month\n  hint: +Charm, tiring evenings in the village.\n  gain job_tavern\n> apothecary: Apothecary Clerk — 12 gold/month\n  hint: Requires Alchemy 12. +Alchemy.\n  when: alchemy >= 12\n  locked: Needs Alchemy 12.\n  gain job_apothecary\n> stables: Familiar Stables — 11 gold/month\n  hint: +Vigor. Things bite.\n  gain job_stables\n> tutor: First-Year Tutor — 12 gold/month\n  hint: Requires Lore 18. +Standing.\n  when: lore >= 18\n  locked: Needs Lore 18.\n  gain job_tutor\n> courier: Broom Courier — 18 gold/month\n  hint: Requires Vigor 14. Dangerous.\n  when: vigor >= 14\n  locked: Needs Vigor 14.\n  gain job_courier\n> none: No job (quit any current one)\n  lose job_library\n  lose job_tavern\n  lose job_apothecary\n  lose job_stables\n  lose job_tutor\n  lose job_courier\n\n@event lantern_night\n# retired in phase 2b (was \"month == 2\"); replaced by lantern_night_v3 (a festival at the lakeshore)\ntitle: Lantern Night\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: false\nrepeat: 12\nart: lantern\ntags: festival social retired\nscene: Hundreds of paper lanterns rising off the lake at dusk, each with a wish written on it in glowing ink.\ntext: On Lantern Night every student writes a wish on a paper lantern and lets it go over the lake. Rumour says the lanterns that sink were wishes the lake wanted for itself.\n> left: Wish for something selfish\n  chance even:\n    gold +large\n    say: Your lantern rises high. The next morning you find coins in your shoe. The lake is generous, and a little creepy.\n  else:\n    mood -small\n    say: Your lantern sinks with a hiss. Something under the water now knows what you want.\n    flag lake_knows\n    alignment -small\n> right: Wish for someone else\n  alignment +tiny\n  mood +medium\n  renown +small\n  say: Your lantern rises and catches the wind. Across the shore, someone you have never met laughs out loud for no reason.\n\n@event kael_arrives\ntitle: The Boy From the North\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: turn == 3\npriority: 30\nactor: kael\nart: snow\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: A pale young man in a fur-lined coat standing at the door of the dining hall, frost spreading from his boots across the floor.\ntext: Mid-term, the dining hall doors open and frost crawls across the floor. A boy in a fur coat walks in, takes the empty seat beside you, and says nothing at all. The headmistress introduces him as Kael Dumont, a transfer. She does not say from where.\n> left: Leave him be\n  meet kael\n  rel kael +tiny\n  say: You eat in silence. When you leave, your cup of tea has frozen solid.\n> right: Offer him the bread\n  meet kael\n  rel kael +medium\n  say: He looks at the bread as if no one has offered him anything in a long time. \"...Thank you.\" His voice sounds like wind over snow.\n  flag kael_kindness\n\n@event winter_trials\n# retired in phase 2b (was \"month == 4\"); replaced by term-end exams (engine, design §7.3)\ntitle: The Winter Trials\nclass: story\ntone: tense\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: false\nrepeat: 12\npriority: 40\nart: hourglass\ntags: study exam retired\nscene: A vast examination hall where floating quills hover over a hundred desks and the windows are thick with frost.\ntext: The Winter Trials. A hundred desks, a hundred floating quills, one very old proctor who may be asleep. The first question asks you to explain the Third Law of Correspondence. The second asks you to demonstrate it.\n> left: Lean on theory\n  challenge research journeyman:\n    win:\n      study +huge\n      mood +small\n      say: Your answer is so thorough the quill gets a cramp. Top marks.\n    lose:\n      study -large\n      mood -small\n      say: You write three pages and realise halfway that you have been answering a different question.\n> right: Lean on practice\n  challenge spell journeyman:\n    win:\n      study +huge\n      renown +small\n      say: Your demonstration is clean and bright. The proctor wakes up and applauds.\n    lose:\n      study -large\n      health -small\n      say: The demonstration backfires. Politely, but with smoke.\n\n@event midwinter_ball\nslot: evening\ntitle: The Midwinter Ball\nclass: story\ntone: warm\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: month == 4\nrepeat: 12\npriority: 40\ntype: menu\nart: music\ntags: festival romance social\nscene: The great hall transformed: an ice-crystal chandelier, couples waltzing a foot above the floor, snow falling indoors without ever landing.\ntext: The great hall has been turned to ice and starlight for the Midwinter Ball. Couples float above the floor. Who will you go with? You can only ask someone you know well.\n> lyra: Ask Lyra\n  when: met(lyra) and rel(lyra) >= 9\n  hint: {&She has had six invitations. She hasn't said yes to any.|She has had invitations again. She is pretending not to count them.}\n  challenge negotiate vs lyra.charm +1 if spark(lyra) >= 25:\n    win:\n      spark lyra +medium\n      rel lyra +medium\n      mood +medium\n      queue ball_last_dance now with lyra\n      say: {bond(lyra) == \"partner\": \"Obviously,\" she says, before you finish asking. She leads, of course.|{flag(ball_lyra): \"Again? Fine. You've improved.\" She dances like a duel, and this year you nearly keep up.|\"Finally, someone who doesn't stammer.\" She dances like a duel — and wins.}}\n      flag ball_lyra\n    lose:\n      rel lyra -small\n      mood -small\n      say: \"I'm going alone. It's more efficient.\" You watch her dance alone all night, perfectly.\n> tobias: Ask Tobias\n  when: met(tobias) and rel(tobias) >= 9\n  hint: {flag(ball_tobias): He has been practising with a broom again. The broom has improved.|He has been practising with a broom.}\n  spark tobias +medium\n  rel tobias +medium\n  mood +medium\n  queue ball_last_dance now with tobias\n  say: {flag(ball_tobias): He only steps on your feet twice this year, and is so proud of it that he tells everyone. It's the best night of the term.|He steps on your feet nine times and apologises ten. It's the best night of the term.}\n  flag ball_tobias\n> seraphine: Ask Seraphine\n  when: met(seraphine) and rel(seraphine) >= 9\n  hint: She may already know you'll ask.\n  spark seraphine +medium\n  rel seraphine +medium\n  mood +medium\n  queue ball_last_dance now with seraphine\n  say: {flag(ball_seraphine): \"Same as last time,\" Seraphine says, \"but you'll wear blue.\" You hadn't planned to. You do.|\"I said yes three weeks ago,\" she says, \"you just hadn't asked yet.\" She dances a beat ahead of the music.}\n  flag ball_seraphine\n> corvin: Ask Corvin\n  when: met(corvin) and rel(corvin) >= 9\n  hint: Odds he shows up: unclear.\n  chance likely:\n    spark corvin +medium\n    mood +medium\n    queue ball_last_dance now with corvin\n    say: {flag(ball_corvin): He arrives on time, which alarms everyone, in a jacket he swears is borrowed. He is still the best dancer in the room.|He arrives late, in a stolen jacket, and is the best dancer in the room.}\n    flag ball_corvin\n  else:\n    mood -medium\n    say: {flag(ball_corvin_arrested): He never shows. A note arrives at midnight: \"Not arrested this time. Worse. Explain later.\" He never does.|He never shows. A note arrives at midnight: \"Got arrested. Long story. Save me a dance next year.\"}\n    flag ball_corvin_arrested\n> mir: Ask Mir\n  when: met(mir) and rel(mir) >= 9\n  hint: {flag(ball_mir): They promise the violin has been fixed.|They're bringing a self-playing violin.}\n  spark mir +medium\n  rel mir +small\n  mood +medium\n  say: {flag(ball_mir): The violin behaves all night. Mir is almost disappointed, until it plays the last waltz entirely on its own and bows.|Mir's violin plays itself, then plays the chandelier, then plays the windows. You are asked to leave early. Worth it.}\n  flag ball_mir\n> kael: Ask Kael\n  when: met(kael) and rel(kael) >= 12\n  hint: He doesn't seem to feel the cold.\n  spark kael +medium\n  rel kael +medium\n  mood +medium\n  queue ball_last_dance now with kael\n  say: {flag(ball_kael): He takes your hand without asking this time, as if it were already decided. The frost-ferns on the glass grow into a whole winter garden.|He holds your hand like it is something fragile. Where your fingers touch, frost-ferns bloom on the glass.}\n  flag ball_kael\n> nessa: Ask Nessa\n  when: met(nessa) and rel(nessa) >= 9\n  hint: She'll be working. She's always working.\n  spark nessa +medium\n  rel nessa +medium\n  renown +small\n  queue ball_last_dance now with nessa\n  say: {flag(ball_nessa): She leaves her notebook in her bag all night. \"The column can make something up,\" she says. It does.|She takes notes between dances. \"For the society column.\" Then she stops taking notes.}\n  flag ball_nessa\n> friends: Go with friends\n  mood +medium\n  charm +tiny\n  say: {flag(ball_friends): You go as a pack again, and this year someone has taught everyone the same dance, badly. It is still perfect.|You go as a pack and dance badly together and it is perfect.}\n  flag ball_friends\n> skip: Stay in and study\n  lore +small\n  study +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: The music drifts up through the floor while you read. Somewhere, people are happy. You are productive.\n\n@event spring_tournament\ntitle: The Spring Tournament\nclass: story\ntone: tense\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: month == 8 and not member(duelling)\nrepeat: 12\npriority: 40\nart: duel\ntags: festival rival\nchain: tournament\nscene: A ring of white stone on the lawn, crowds in the stands, two duelists bowing as their wands spark.\ntext: The Spring Tournament: dueling on the great lawn, the whole academy watching. Entry is open to anyone foolish enough.\n> left: Watch from the stands\n  mood +small\n  say: You eat candied chestnuts and scream for strangers.\n> right: Enter the tournament\n  challenge combat journeyman+ drift mid:\n    win:\n      renown +medium\n      say: First round: you win, barely, and {flag(tournament_champion): the crowd remembers your name from last year and chants it.|the crowd learns your name.}\n      queue tournament_semifinal now\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      renown +tiny\n      say: First round: you are knocked out of the ring in eleven seconds. A record, apparently.\n\n@event spring_tournament_circle\ntitle: The Spring Tournament\nclass: story\ntone: tense\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: month == 8 and member(duelling)\nrepeat: 12\npriority: 40\nchain: tournament\nart: duel\ntags: rival\nscene: The great lawn ringed with banners, a chalk duelling circle at its centre and the Duelling Circle's names on a board.\ntext: The Spring Tournament: duelling on the great lawn, the whole academy watching. The Duelling Circle's names went up on the board a week ago, yours among them. {rank(duelling) >= 3: You are the captain, and the Circle expects its captain to win something.|The captain wants results, and says so.}\n> left: Fight to make a good showing\n  challenge combat journeyman drift mid:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      perform duelling +small\n      say: You win your first bout cleanly and lose the second honourably. {rank(duelling) >= 3: Your second-years cheer their captain louder than anyone in the stands.|The captain ticks your name, which from her is a medal.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      say: You go out in the first round, on your feet at least. {rank(duelling) >= 3: Your second-years are kind about it, which is worse.|The captain says you fought like someone who expected to lose.}\n> right: Go for the title\n  challenge combat journeyman+ drift mid:\n    win:\n      renown +medium\n      say: First round: you win, barely, and the crowd learns your name.\n      queue tournament_semifinal now\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      renown +tiny\n      perform duelling -tiny\n      say: First round: you are knocked out of the ring in eleven seconds. A record, apparently, and not one the Circle wanted.\n\n@event tournament_semifinal\ntitle: Semifinal\nclass: story\ntone: tense\ntrigger: queued\nart: duel\ntags: rival\nchain: tournament\nscene: The same ring at sunset, scorch marks on the stone. Your opponent is grinning.\ntext: The semifinal. Your opponent is a fourth-year who has never lost. The crowd is louder now; you can hear your own name in it.\n> left: Fight clean\n  challenge combat expert- drift mid:\n    win:\n      renown +medium\n      say: You trade spells like questions and answers — and get the last word.\n      queue tournament_final now\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      renown +small\n      say: A good fight. Not good enough. The crowd applauds you off the lawn.\n> right: Fight dirty\n  alignment -small\n  challenge deceive adept drift mid:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      say: A feint, a trip, a jinx to the shoelaces. Legal. Mostly.{met(bram):  On the prefects' bench Bram watches the whole thing with his jaw set, and doesn't clap.|}\n      queue tournament_final now\n      if met(bram):\n        rel bram -small\n    lose:\n      renown -small\n      say: The referee sees the trick. Disqualified, and booed.\n\n@event tournament_final\ntitle: The Final\nclass: story\ntone: tense\ntrigger: queued\nart: trophy\ntags: rival festival\nchain: tournament\nscene: The dueling ring under floating lanterns, a silver cup on a plinth, and across from you, a familiar face.\ntext: The final. Across the ring stands {lyra}, wand raised, eyes blazing. \"I was hoping it would be you,\" she says, and she is not being friendly.\n> left: Give everything you have\n  meet lyra\n  challenge combat vs lyra.arcana +3:\n    win:\n      renown +huge\n      mood +large\n      flag tournament_champion\n      gain famous\n      rel lyra -medium\n      if bond(lyra) == \"none\":\n        bond lyra rival\n      say: Your final spell lands like a bell. Silence — then the lawn erupts. Lyra stares at you as if seeing you for the first time, then walks off the lawn without shaking your hand.\n    lose:\n      renown +medium\n      rel lyra +small\n      say: She wins, but only just. Afterwards, she offers her hand. \"Next year,\" she says. It is a promise and a threat.\n> right: Yield with a bow\n  meet lyra\n  rel lyra +large\n  renown +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: The crowd boos. Lyra does not cheer her victory. Later she finds you: \"Why?\" You don't have a good answer, and she seems to like that.\n\n@event grand_exams\n# retired in phase 2b (was \"month == 10\"); replaced by term-end exams (engine, design §7.3)\ntitle: Grand Examinations\nclass: story\ntone: tense\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: false\nrepeat: 12\npriority: 40\nart: scroll\ntags: study exam retired\nscene: A circular examination chamber with a domed ceiling of moving constellations; examiners in grey robes sit behind a long table.\ntext: Year-end examinations. Three days, five examiners and a practical in front of a panel that includes the headmistress. Your standing for the year rides on this.\n> left: Prepare a dazzling practical\n  challenge spell adept+:\n    win:\n      study +huge\n      renown +small\n      say: You finish your practical and the examiners actually lean forward. Distinction.\n    lose:\n      study -huge\n      mood -medium\n      say: The practical sputters. An examiner writes something very short.\n> right: Write a meticulous thesis\n  challenge research adept+:\n    win:\n      study +huge\n      mood +small\n      say: Your thesis is cited by one examiner in an argument with another. Distinction.\n    lose:\n      study -huge\n      mood -medium\n      say: Your thesis is described, aloud, as \"ambitious\". It is not a compliment.\n\n@event summer_plans\n# retired in phase 2b (was \"month == 11\"); replaced by break_plan\ntitle: Summer Recess\nclass: story\ntone: warm\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: false\nrepeat: 12\npriority: 40\ntype: menu\nart: sun\ntags: home retired\nscene: The academy gates thrown open to green hills; students with trunks waving from departing sky-barges.\ntext: The towers empty for the summer. Two months of freedom. How will you spend them?\n> home: Go home\n  hint: Rest and recover. No rent while away.\n  gain summer_away\n  health +large\n  mood +large\n  if has(origin_noble):\n    say: Your family hosts three dinners in your honour and asks about your marriage prospects at all of them.\n  elif has(origin_street):\n    say: The old crowd in Port Calder treats you like a returning hero, and steals your trunk only once.\n  else:\n    say: Home is smaller than you remember, and warmer. Someone made your favourite pie.\n> work: Take summer work in the village\n  hint: About 50 gold over the summer. Tiring.\n  gain summer_work\n  vigor +small\n  say: You haul crates, serve tables and polish brass until your hands know the shape of every coin.\n> study: Stay and study in the empty library\n  hint: +Lore, +Arcana, +Standing. Lonely.\n  lore +medium\n  arcana +medium\n  study +medium\n  mood -small\n  say: Just you, the books and the dust motes. You learn things the term never had time for.\n> travel: Travel to the coast\n  hint: Costs 40 gold. Great for mood.\n  when: gold >= 40\n  locked: Needs 40 crowns.\n  gold -large\n  gain summer_away\n  mood +huge\n  charm +small\n  say: Salt air, shell markets, a sea serpent at sunset. You come back tanned and ten years younger.\n> expedition: Join Old Maud's herb expedition\n  hint: Requires knowing Maud. Adventure.\n  when: met(maud)\n  gain summer_away\n  alchemy +medium\n  vigor +small\n  rel maud +large\n  health +small\n  say: Two months in the Whisperwood with Maud and her goat. You learn which mushrooms sing and which ones lie.\n\n@event summer_letter\ntitle: A Letter From Home\nclass: story\ntone: warm\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: month == 12 and not has(summer_away) and not wrapup\nrepeat: 12\nart: letter\ntags: home\nscene: A letter sealed with candle wax lying on a sunlit windowsill.\ntext: A letter arrives in the last month of summer, the handwriting so familiar it hurts. They want to know how you are doing. Really doing.\n> left: Write back honestly\n  mood +small\n  if (turn > 4 and gpa < 2.0):\n    mood -medium\n    say: You tell them about your grades. Their reply is kind, which is worse.\n  else:\n    say: You write six pages. It feels like setting down a heavy bag.\n> right: Write back that everything is perfect\n  mood -tiny\n  guile +tiny\n  alignment -tiny\n  chance even:\n    gold +medium\n    say: Proud of you, they write, and enclose a little money{has(origin_noble) or has(origin_merchant): , as if a purse were the right answer to a letter.|  you suspect they couldn't spare.}\n  else:\n    say: They reply with exclamation marks. The lie sits in your stomach all week.\n\n@event starfall\nslot: night\ntitle: Starfall\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: month == 12 and year < 4 and not off_campus\nrepeat: 12\npriority: 30\ntype: menu\nart: star\ntags: festival\nscene: Students lying on the observatory roof under a sky streaked with falling stars.\ntext: {year == 1: The last night of the academic year. Everyone climbs onto the observatory roof, and the rest spill out onto the lawns with blankets, to watch the stars fall. They say if you catch one in a jar, you get a year of luck.|Starfall again. The observatory roof, the lawns full of blankets, the whole academy lying on its back.} {starfall_jar >= 1: Your jar is ready, cork in hand.|} {~The first star falls before anyone is ready.|Somebody has brought a lute, unwisely.|The lake is full of reflected stars.}\n> left: Lie back and just watch\n  mood +medium\n  health +small\n  starfall_jar = 0\n  say: You count forty falling stars and then stop counting. It's enough.\n> right: Try to catch a star\n  hint: Nobody has caught one in years. A good jar from the observatory improves the odds.\n  if starfall_jar >= 1:\n    challenge feat apprentice unlikely luck:\n      win:\n        renown +small\n        mood +small\n        gain inspired\n        queue starfall_caught\n        say: It lands in your jar with a hiss and glows for weeks. Students come to see it.\n      lose:\n        health -small\n        say: You lunge, miss, and nearly roll off the roof. A prefect catches you by the collar.\n  else:\n    challenge feat apprentice slim luck:\n      win:\n        renown +small\n        mood +small\n        gain inspired\n        queue starfall_caught\n        say: It lands in your jar with a hiss and glows for weeks. Students come to see it.\n      lose:\n        health -small\n        say: You lunge, miss, and nearly roll off the roof. A prefect catches you by the collar.\n  starfall_jar = 0\n> together: Find your sweetheart on the lawn\n  when: bonds_around(partner) > 0\n  starfall_jar = 0\n  queue starfall_together now\n  say: You leave the roof and go looking. You know exactly where to look.\n> wish: Make the Starfall wish\n  starfall_jar = 0\n  queue starfall_wish now\n  say: You climb to the very top of the roof, where the old custom says the wishes go.\n\n@event year_two\ntitle: Second Year\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: turn == 13\npriority: 55\nart: tower\ntags: story\nscene: The seven towers in autumn colours, the first-year barge arriving below, tiny and nervous.\ntext: The barge brings new first-years, and you watch them gawp at the towers. Was that really you a year ago? You know which staircases move now, and which professors you can charm. Second-years, a prefect announces, may apply for summer research posts in Stormbloom.{chosen(break_plan, home):  Your trunk still smells of home, and there's a cake in it you were told to share.|}\n> ok: Begin the second year\n  mood +small\n  renown +small\n  say: You unpack faster than last year, and in the right order. Someone in your year already has a list of the research posts, and the castle's midnight doors, side by side.\n\n@event term_begins\ntitle: The Spring Term\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: term and month == 7\nrepeat: 4\npriority: 85\nart: tower\ntags: story\nscene: The castle steps after the winter break, trunks stacked by the doors and the first lamps lit in the towers.\ntext: {chosen(break_plan, home): You come back from home with a tin of something your family insists you'll need, and the castle feels enormous after a winter of small rooms.|{chosen(break_plan, work): Your holiday wages are in your purse and your feet still ache from earning them. The castle fills up around you, trunk by trunk.|After a winter of empty corridors, the castle fills up around you again, and the noise of it is almost too much.}} The spring term begins, and the registrar's hall is already queueing.\n> left: Get a head start on the reading\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You find a window seat in the library before the term has properly started. By the time the others arrive, you have a list of questions and a crick in your neck.\n> right: Catch up with everyone first\n  mood +small\n  gold -tiny\n  say: You spend the first evening in the common room, then at the Newt, hearing everyone's winter. The reading can wait a day. It waits three.\n\n@event year_three\ntitle: Third Year\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: turn == 25\npriority: 55\nart: crown\ntags: story\nscene: The headmistress addressing a hall of third-years, every candle in the room leaning toward her.\ntext: \"Third years,\" the headmistress says, \"you are past the middle of your time here. Next year will ask a great deal of you; spend this one finding out what you want from it.\" She looks, for a moment, directly at you.{chosen(break_plan, home):  You came straight from home, and the summer's quiet is still on you.|}\n> ok: Begin the third year\n  mood +tiny\n  study +tiny\n  say: You know the castle well enough now to get lost in it on purpose. This is the last year for a summer research post, and the holiday notices by the gates pay better every year.\n\n@event graduation_eve\nslot: night\ntitle: The Eve of Graduation\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: turn == max_turns\npriority: 60\nart: crown\ntags: story\nscene: Graduation robes laid out on a dormitory bed, a single candle burning, the towers lit gold outside the window.\ntext: Tomorrow you graduate. Your robes are laid out on the bed, slightly too long. Outside, the towers are lit gold. Four years have gone like a single held breath.\n> left: Spend the night with friends\n  mood +medium\n  say: {bonds(friend) + bonds(close) + bonds(partner) > 0: Laughter until dawn. Promises to write. Some of them, you'll keep.|You knock on a few doors. Nobody who answers is a close friend, not yet, but they pour you a cup and keep you up laughing till dawn. You trade addresses anyway.}\n> right: Walk the halls alone one last time\n  lore +small\n  mood +small\n  say: Every corridor holds a memory. At the end of the last one, the castle sighs, as if saying goodbye.\n"},{"path":"content/events/chain_hollowstar_acts.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  THE HOLLOW STAR: the acts as arc beats (arc: hollowstar)\n#\n#  Rumours, leads, clues (playtest 2, X11): the beats fire for every\n#  player and leave leads (@clue kind: lead, content/world/clues.grim);\n#  clues are found only by following a lead to its place or person.\n#  Act I (omens, year 1)\n#    beat 1  hs_lake_breathes -> leads: the lake, the archive, Maud\n#    beat 2  hs_grey_stranger (chain_hollowstar.grim) -> lead: the man in grey\n#  Act II (investigation, year 2): the Wardens stir, Ilse arrives.\n#    beat 4  hs_silver_rings, hs_seraphine_towers, hs_ilse_arrives\n#    beat 5  hs_frost_ring\n#  Act III (escalation, year 3): a tower goes dark, sides are taken.\n#    beat 7  hs_dark_tower (may advance the clock to 8), hs_maud_locks\n#    beat 8  hs_two_shadows -> hs_choosing_sides -> sided_wardens | sided_dumonts | sided_self\n#  Act IV (convergence, year 4): preparation before the summons.\n#    beat 10 hs_last_winter (who stands with you beneath the lake), hs_ilse_door\n#  Preparation, and how the tunnel and the threshold went, set the\n#  tier of the seal in hs_beneath; the seal can fail (hollow_failed).\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- Act I: omens ----------------\n\n@event hs_lake_breathes\ntitle: The Lake Breathes\narc: hollowstar\nbeat: 1\nslot: evening\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nart: snow\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: The Great Hall at supper, every head turned to the tall windows, the lake beyond them gone white with sudden frost.\ntext: At supper the lake outside the tall windows turns white, all at once, and stays white for one breath. Then it thaws. Far under the water a bell tolls once. The headmistress stands. \"A cold current,\" she says pleasantly. \"Eat your soup.\" Nobody does.\n> left: Go down to the shore after supper\n  meet maud\n  find lead_lake_breathed\n  find lead_maud_friend\n  health -tiny\n  say: The shore is soaked and freezing. Old Maud is already there with a lantern, watching the water. \"Last time it did that, I lost a friend,\" she says, not quite to you. Then she sends you to bed, and you go, shivering.\n> right: Watch the high table instead\n  find lead_founders_archive\n  lore +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: The headmistress finishes her soup. Professor Thorne doesn't touch his, and after supper he goes straight to the library archive. You lie awake wondering what he went to read.\n\n# ---------------- Act II: the Wardens stir ----------------\n\n@event hs_silver_rings\ntitle: Silver at Supper\narc: hollowstar\nbeat: 4\nslot: night\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nart: ring\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: The Great Hall at supper, candlelight catching three identical silver rings on three very different hands at the high table.\ntext: At supper you notice three identical silver rings at the high table: on the headmistress, on Professor Thorne and on Old Maud's earthy hand. After curfew, from your window, you see all three walking the lakeshore with lanterns, stopping at the foot of each tower in turn.\n> left: Follow them down\n  challenge deceive vs thorne.guile:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      say: You shadow them from tower to tower. At each one they lay a palm on the stone and wait. At the last, Maud takes her hand away and blows on her fingers as if the wall were ice.\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      rel thorne -small\n      say: A lantern swings toward you. \"Hm,\" says Professor Thorne. \"Bed.\" He doesn't report you, which somehow feels worse.\n> right: Draw the curtains\n  health +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You sleep well, mostly. In the morning the rings are still there at breakfast, and nobody at the high table looks as if they slept at all.\n\n@event hs_seraphine_towers\ntitle: Seven, Then Six\narc: hollowstar\nbeat: 4\nslot: night\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nactor: seraphine\nart: tower\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: A seer on the observatory balcony at night, counting the academy's towers on her fingers, one finger held back.\ntext: Seraphine is on the observatory balcony, counting towers on her fingers. \"Seven,\" she says. \"Seven. Six, and then… no. Seven, for now.\" She looks at you as if you had arrived a little early. \"What month is it? I lose track around the… you know.\"\n> left: \"Which one goes out?\"\n  lore +small\n  mood -small\n  find lead_seventh_tower\n  say: She points, then frowns at her own hand. \"That one. Or the one next to it. Next year, I think. Not the seventh; nobody's lit the seventh for a hundred years.\" She doesn't remember saying it the next morning, but you do.\n> right: \"It's {month}. Evening.\"\n  rel seraphine +medium\n  mood +tiny\n  say: She lets out a long breath and sits down on the cold stone. \"Thank you. I was halfway into a winter.\" You stay until she is sure which one she's in.\n\n@event hs_ilse_arrives\ntitle: Kael Goes Pale\narc: hollowstar\nbeat: 4\nwhen: met(kael)\nclass: story\ntone: tense\nactor: kael\nart: snow\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: A breakfast table in the refectory, a pale northern student staring at an unsealed letter, frost creeping across his teacup.\ntext: At breakfast a village boy brings Kael a note, and Kael reads it and goes whiter than usual, which is a feat. Frost creeps across his tea. \"My sister,\" he says. \"She's taken a room above the bakery.\" He folds the note very small. \"She isn't here to visit.\"\n> left: Go to the bakery yourself\n  flag ilse_in_village\n  find lead_white_furs\n  queue ilse_village in 1\n  rel kael -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: \"Don't,\" he says, too quickly. \"She'd only… don't.\" He doesn't eat. You decide you'll go anyway, soon, and he can see you decide it, and looks away.\n> right: Leave him to it\n  flag ilse_in_village\n  find lead_white_furs\n  rel kael -tiny\n  say: You let him be. He sits with the note until the hall empties. That afternoon the whole village is talking about a girl in white furs whose coins freeze to the counter.\n\n@event hs_frost_ring\ntitle: A Ring of Frost\narc: hollowstar\nbeat: 5\nslot: night\nclass: story\ntone: tense\nart: snow\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: The Mirror Lake on a warm spring night, a perfect white ring of frost on the water, a tall woman standing at the waterline with a lantern.\ntext: A warm night, and a ring of frost has formed in the middle of the lake: perfectly round, perfectly white, as wide as the Great Hall. Far below it, very faintly, a bell tolls once. The headmistress is standing alone at the waterline.\n> left: Watch what she does\n  challenge deceive master- likely:\n    win:\n      find lake_bell\n      lore +small\n      say: She kneels and speaks to the water, slow and patient, the way you would speak to a sleeping animal. The ring shrinks, cracks, and is gone. She stays kneeling a long time afterwards.\n    lose:\n      rel isolde +tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: \"Good evening,\" she says, without turning round. \"You are the third student this week. The other two asked questions.\" She tells you nothing, and remembers your name.\n> right: Stay inside\n  mood -small\n  health +tiny\n  say: You stay inside with the shutters closed. By morning the lake is blue and glittering, and the first-years are already arguing about whether anyone saw anything at all.\n\n# ---------------- Act III: escalation ----------------\n\n@event hs_dark_tower\ntitle: The Tower That Went Dark\narc: hollowstar\nbeat: 7\nclass: story\ntone: grim\nart: candle\ntags: mystery hollowstar danger\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: An academy tower standing black against the night sky among its lit neighbours, and below it a lake half-covered in autumn ice.\ntext: Mid-lecture, every lamp in the Lecture Tower goes out at once, and none of them will light again. Nobody is hurt. By morning, weeks before any frost, half the lake has frozen: a sheet of ice that ends in a clean edge, as if cut. The prefects are already telling everyone to keep off it.\n> left: Walk out onto the ice\n  challenge research expert:\n    win:\n      advance hollowstar +1\n      lore +medium\n      health -small\n      say: Under your boots the ice is clear as glass. Far down, something round and dark sits on the lakebed, and a faint glow runs along its rim. Then the glow turns, slowly, as if to look up.\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      say: The ice groans and a crack races past your feet. You scramble back to the shore soaked to the knee, and a prefect writes your name down very slowly.\n> right: Carry lamps for the prefects\n  renown +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: You spend the night carrying lamps up the Lecture Tower stairs. Every one goes out at the doorway. From her window across the courtyard, the headmistress watches you try.\n\n@event hs_maud_locks\ntitle: Maud Has Seen Ice Before\narc: hollowstar\nbeat: 7\nwhen: seen(hs_dark_tower) > 0\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nactor: maud\nart: cup\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: An old woman wrapped in a shawl on her cottage step, two mugs steaming beside her, looking down at a half-frozen lake.\ntext: Maud is on her cottage step with two mugs of tea, as if she expected you. \"The lake doesn't freeze this time of year, dearie,\" she says. \"Last time it froze through like this, I lost a friend to it.\" She pushes a mug toward you. \"Sit. Or ask. You can't really do both.\"\n> left: Sit with her\n  rel maud +medium\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You drink your tea and watch the ice. She hums something old. When the mugs are empty she squeezes your hand. \"That was the right one,\" she says, and you'll only understand why much later.\n> right: Ask what's holding it shut\n  find seven_locks\n  find lead_seventh_tower\n  rel maud +small\n  mood -small\n  say: \"Seven towers, seven locks, one in each,\" she says. \"When a tower goes quiet, so does its lock.\" She looks at the dark tower. \"Locks can be mended. They need hands, and they need a key.\"\n\n@event hs_two_shadows\ntitle: Two Shadows on the Ice\narc: hollowstar\nbeat: 8\nclass: story\ntone: tense\nart: lantern\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: Dusk on a half-frozen lake, two dark figures meeting in the middle of the ice, one holding a lantern, the dark tower behind them.\ntext: At dusk, two figures walk out onto the frozen half of the lake from opposite shores: the headmistress with her lantern, and Kael, alone. They meet in the middle and talk for a long time, and part without shaking hands. At breakfast, both of them look at you a moment too long.\n> left: Ask Kael what they said\n  rel kael +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: \"She wants the door shut her way. I want it shut mine.\" He stirs his tea until it freezes. \"We both asked about you. Don't make that face. You'll be asked.\"\n> right: Ask the headmistress\n  meet isolde\n  rel isolde +small\n  rel kael -tiny\n  say: \"We disagree about method,\" she says, \"and agree about the door.\" She studies you over her spectacles. \"Expect an invitation. Possibly two. Choose with your eyes open.\" Kael, across the hall, sees you leave her tower.\n\n@event hs_choosing_sides\ntitle: Two Invitations\narc: hollowstar\nbeat: 8\nwhen: seen(hs_two_shadows) > 0\ntype: menu\nclass: story\ntone: tense\nart: letter\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: A student holding two folded notes, one sealed in silver wax and one in frost, standing between a tower and a boathouse.\ntext: Two invitations in one day. A note in the headmistress's neat hand: \"My study, after supper. Come alone. There will be chess.\" And Kael, waiting at the boathouse: \"She wants you in her circle. I know the door from the other side.\" He looks at the dark tower. \"Come with us. If you want.\"\n> wardens: Join the headmistress's circle\n  hint: The Wardens: old rules, old wards.\n  flag sided_wardens\n  rel isolde +large\n  rel kael -small\n  lore +small\n  say: She beats you at chess in eleven moves, then tells you about the seal while she resets the board. \"Rings are for people who have stood on the ice,\" she says. \"Until then, you have my door.\"\n> dumonts: Stand with the Dumonts\n  hint: Kael's way, against his own family.\n  flag sided_dumonts\n  rel kael +large\n  rel isolde -small\n  if met(ilse):\n    rel ilse +small\n  arcana +small\n  say: \"Not my family,\" Kael says. \"Us.\" He teaches you to hold a ward in a wind that wants your fingers. His hands are cold. They are steadier than yours.\n> self: Go my own way\n  hint: Nobody's circle. Nobody's help.\n  flag sided_self\n  guile +small\n  lore +small\n  mood -small\n  say: You thank them both and keep your own notes in your own cipher. It's lonelier than you expected. Both of them, you notice, keep an eye on you anyway.\n> none: Stay out of it\n  hint: It isn't your door.\n  mood +small\n  rel kael -tiny\n  say: You turn both invitations down. Kael nods as if he expected it. The headmistress's note simply says, the next morning, \"Understood. Mind the ice.\"\n\n# ---------------- Act IV: convergence ----------------\n\n@event hs_last_winter\ntitle: The Last Winter Before\narc: hollowstar\nbeat: 10\nwhen: clues(hollowstar) >= 2 or flag(sided_wardens) or flag(sided_dumonts) or flag(sided_self) or flag(warden_trial)\ntype: menu\nclass: story\ntone: tense\nart: lantern\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: Lantern light in a frost-rimed window, the frozen edge of the lake beyond it, a student's desk covered in maps of seven towers.\ntext: The Lecture Tower has been dark for a year, and the lake freezes overnight in circles now. In spring the seal will weaken, and how you spend this winter decides what you carry beneath the lake. You have one winter and not enough evenings.\n> kael: Train with Kael on the ice\n  hint: He'll fight beside you in the tunnel.\n  flag winter_kael\n  rel kael +large\n  health -small\n  say: He drills you on the ice until you can hold a ward with numb hands. \"Again,\" he says, and then, quietly, \"Good.\" You will remember that he said it.\n> maud: Mend wards with Maud\n  hint: She'll have taught you the wards that open stone.\n  flag winter_maud\n  rel maud +large\n  mood -small\n  say: You walk the towers with her, tracing old wards back into the stone. \"Your hands are steadier than mine were,\" she says, and doesn't say whose hands she means.\n> isolde: Study with the headmistress\n  hint: You'll know the founders' words by heart.\n  flag winter_isolde\n  rel isolde +large\n  lore +small\n  mood -small\n  say: You lose at chess every evening and learn the founders' words between moves. Once you nearly win. Her short, delighted laugh is the warmest sound you hear all winter.\n> ilse: Spar with Ilse at the shore\n  hint: She'll talk her family's servant down for you.\n  when: met(ilse) and not flag(ilse_taken) and not flag(ilse_went_north)\n  flag winter_ilse\n  rel ilse +large\n  health -small\n  say: She knocks you into the snow eleven times. The twelfth time you don't fall, and she looks at you as if you had done a trick she has never learned the counter to.\n> alone: Rest while you still can\n  hint: A quiet winter. No new training, but rested.\n  rest\n  say: You sleep, you eat, you read novels with no doors in them. It's the kindest winter you've had in years, and you can feel the lake getting colder anyway.\n\n@event hs_ilse_door\ntitle: What Ilse Was Taught\narc: hollowstar\nbeat: 10\nwhen: met(ilse) and not clue(ilse_truth) and not flag(ilse_taken) and not flag(ilse_went_north)\nactor: ilse\nclass: story\ntone: grim\nart: door\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: A young woman in white furs drawing a round door in the snow with the tip of a wand, a frozen lake behind her.\ntext: Ilse draws a circle in the snow with her wand, then a smaller one inside it. \"This is the door,\" she says. \"I have drawn it every day since I was six. I have never seen it.\" She doesn't look up. \"Ask me what they taught me to do. Or don't. I don't care which.\"\n> left: \"What did they teach you?\"\n  find ilse_truth\n  rel ilse +small\n  mood -small\n  say: She tells you, flatly, like a lesson recited: how to stand, what to say, where to put her hands. \"They raised me to open it,\" she says. \"Nobody asked me if I wanted to.\" Then, sharply: \"Now you know. Don't look at me like that.\"\n> right: \"You don't have to tell me.\"\n  rel ilse +medium\n  mood -tiny\n  say: She rubs the circle out with her boot. \"Kindness,\" she says, as if naming a trick. \"I haven't learned the counter to that one yet.\" She doesn't leave, though.\n"},{"path":"content/events/chain_hollowstar.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  MAIN CHAIN: The Hollow Star\n#\n#  Something sleeps beneath the lake, sealed by Lumenhold's\n#  founders. Kael Dumont's family serve it. Progress follows the\n#  arc (content/world/arcs.grim): the clock advances on schedule,\n#  beats leave leads, and the clues you followed them to are\n#  counted by clues(hollowstar) (X11; the beats are in\n#  chain_hollowstar_acts.grim, the clues in world/clues.grim).\n#    leads          the archive or the Restricted Section, the boathouse,\n#                   Maud, Kael, Seraphine, the seventh tower\n#    clues >= 2     grey visitors\n#    clues >= 3     the headmistress\n#  Alignment (Lumen + / Hollow -) is the moral axis: the Star's\n#  gifts pull toward the Hollow; refusing them, toward the Lumen.\n#  Finale (final year, Blossomtide): hs_the_night -> hs_tunnel ->\n#  hs_threshold -> hs_beneath (menu); the seal can fail (hollow_failed),\n#  for those who engaged (or whom the Star has claimed, alignment <= -60).\n#  Outcomes fill the yearbook's mystery line: sealed / bargained /\n#  freed (alignment <= -60) / failed / claimed (game over) / bystander.\n# ============================================================\n\n@event hs_dreams_begin\ntime: morning\ntitle: The Black Star Dream\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: turn >= 5 and clock(hollowstar) >= 1 and not has(hollow_dreams)\nweight: 14\nart: moon\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: A dreamscape: a frozen lake seen from beneath, a round door glowing at the bottom, and above it a star that is a hole in the sky.\ntext: You dream of the lake from underneath. There is a round door at the bottom, and above the water a star that is not a light but an absence. A voice says your name like it is tasting it. You wake with frost on your pillow.\n> left: Write the dream down\n  lore +small\n  find lead_black_star_dream\n  gain hollow_dreams\n  say: Your notes fill four pages. In the morning you don't remember writing the last line: \"It is not asleep. It is waiting.\"\n> right: Ask Seraphine what it means\n  meet seraphine\n  rel seraphine +small\n  find lead_black_star_dream\n  gain hollow_dreams\n  say: Seraphine listens, then hands you a sprig of lavender. \"It won't stop the dreams,\" she says. \"But it'll make them smell nicer.\"\n\n@event hs_grey_stranger\ntitle: A Man in Grey\narc: hollowstar\nbeat: 2\nslot: night\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: met(kael)\nart: snow\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: A frozen fountain in a moonlit courtyard; a tall man in a grey coat facing a younger man, frost spreading from both.\ntext: Late at night in the courtyard, Kael is arguing with a tall man in a grey coat. The fountain between them has frozen mid-splash. \"The door under the lake will be opened,\" the man says calmly. \"By you, or by your sister. Your mother doesn't mind which.\"\n> left: Try to slip away unseen\n  guile +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  find lead_man_in_grey\n  say: You back into the shadows. The man in grey turns his head and looks straight at where you're hiding, then smiles and walks away. You don't sleep much.\n> right: Step out and interrupt\n  rel kael +large\n  health -tiny\n  find lead_man_in_grey\n  flag confronted_grey\n  say: \"Everything all right?\" The man in grey studies you, nods politely and leaves; the cold he leaves behind bites through your sleeves. Kael's hands are shaking. \"You shouldn't have done that,\" he says. \"...Thank you.\"\n\n@event hs_founders_records\ntitle: The Founders' Ledger\nplace: library\ntime: morning afternoon\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: clue(lead_founders_archive) and not clue(founders_ledger)\nweight: 14\nart: book\ntags: mystery hollowstar study\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: A dusty archive vault, a giant ledger open on a lectern, one page carefully razored out.\ntext: In the archive vault you find the Founders' Ledger, three hundred years old. The entry for the academy's founding year has been cut out with a razor. But the page beneath still holds the impression of the pen.\n> left: Leave it — this feels dangerous\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You close the ledger. The dust settles. Some doors are better left alone. Probably.\n> right: Rub charcoal over the page\n  challenge research apprentice+:\n    win:\n      flag knows_founders_seal\n      find knows_founders_seal\n      find founders_ledger\n      lore +small\n      say: Ghostly letters emerge: \"Seven towers, seven locks. The Star sleeps beneath the water. The key is a star that holds no light.\" The founders built the academy as a seal.\n    lose:\n      lore +tiny\n      say: The impression is too faint. You make out only three words: \"...beneath the water...\"\n\n@event hs_restricted_founders\ntitle: The Chained Drafts\nplace: restricted_section\ntime: midnight\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: clue(lead_founders_archive) and not clue(founders_ledger)\nweight: 16\nart: lock\ntags: mystery hollowstar study\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: A dark aisle of the Restricted Section at midnight, a bundle of yellowed drafts chained to a shelf, the chain faintly glowing.\ntext: The archive's ledger has its founding page cut out. The Restricted Section keeps the founders' own drafts, chained to a shelf at the very back, and the chain is warm to the touch. Somewhere behind you, a book turns its own page.\n> left: Work the chain loose\n  challenge deceive journeyman:\n    win:\n      flag knows_founders_seal\n      find founders_ledger\n      find knows_founders_seal\n      health -tiny\n      say: The links give one by one, and burn your fingers doing it. The drafts say it plainly, in three hands: \"Seven towers, seven locks. The Star sleeps beneath the water. The key is a star that holds no light.\" Lumenhold is a seal.\n    lose:\n      gain detention\n      mood -small\n      say: The chain shrieks like a ward. You're out of the window before the prefect arrives, but not before he sees your face. Detention, and a long look from the librarian.\n> right: Copy the shelf-list instead\n  lore +small\n  mana -tiny\n  find lead_seventh_tower\n  say: The drafts' shelf-list is less guarded and nearly as strange. One entry reads only \"Key: the seventh tower, top room. Do not lend.\" You copy it out by the light of a reading charm you have to keep alive for an hour, and it leaves you drained.\n\n@event hs_boathouse_bell\ntitle: The Hatch in the Boathouse\nplace: boathouse\ntime: evening night midnight\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: clue(lead_lake_breathed) and not clue(lake_bell)\nweight: 16\nart: bell\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: The inside of an old wooden boathouse at night, a square hatch open in the floorboards, stone steps leading down into dark water.\ntext: Under a coil of rotten rope in the boathouse there's a hatch, and under the hatch, stone steps going down into the lake. The air coming up smells of cold bronze. From somewhere below, very faint, a bell is tolling under the water.\n> left: Go down the steps\n  challenge feat apprentice:\n    win:\n      flag found_lake_tunnel\n      find lake_bell\n      health -tiny\n      say: The steps become a tunnel under the lakebed, dry and ice-cold, running toward the sound. You follow it until you can see the bell's green rim in the dark ahead. Then you turn back, frozen to the bone, and remember every turning.\n    lose:\n      find lake_bell\n      health -small\n      say: The third step is ice. You go into the water to the chest and come up gasping, but you've seen it: a tunnel under the lake, running toward the bell. You walk home soaked.\n> right: Listen at the waterline\n  find lake_bell\n  mood -small\n  say: You lie on the boards with your ear to the gap. The bell tolls seven times, slow, and between the strokes you hear a draught moving under the lake, the way air moves in a corridor. Something down there is a door. You don't sleep well.\n\n@event hs_seraphine_shadow\ntitle: One Star Too Many\nplace: observatory\nslot: evening\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nactor: seraphine\nwhen: clue(lead_black_star_dream) and met(seraphine) and not clue(second_shadow)\nweight: 16\nart: star\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: An observatory at night, the great telescope tilted down toward the window and the lake below, a seer pointing at the reflection of the stars in the water.\ntext: Seraphine has turned the great telescope down, not at the sky but at the lake, where the stars are reflected. \"Look,\" she says. \"In the water. There's one star too many, and it's dark.\" She counts under her breath. \"It wasn't there in my dream. It was in yours.\"\n> left: Chart it with her\n  meet seraphine\n  find second_shadow\n  lore +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: You chart it till dawn on the cold floor: a small dark shape in the reflection that has no star above it, sitting exactly over the middle of the lake. Your knees never quite forgive you.\n> right: Tell her about your dream\n  meet seraphine\n  find second_shadow\n  rel seraphine +medium\n  mood -small\n  say: She finishes your sentences, three of them, before you've started them. Then she shows you the dark star in the water, right over the middle of the lake. You both stop talking for a long time.\n\n@event hs_kael_truth\ntitle: What Kael Is Running From\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: (clue(lead_man_in_grey) or clues(hollowstar) >= 1) and rel(kael) >= 15 and not flag(kael_told)\nactor: kael\nweight: 16\nart: snow\ntags: mystery hollowstar social\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: Two students sitting on the edge of the frozen boathouse jetty at night, one with frost in his hair, both looking down at the black water.\ntext: Kael sits with you on the boathouse jetty. \"My family serve something under this lake,\" he says at last. \"They sent me here to open its door. I came to stop them. Some nights I'm not sure which I'll actually do.\"\n> left: \"Then we stop them together.\"\n  flag kael_told kael_ally\n  rel kael +large\n  spark kael +tiny\n  alignment +small\n  find kael_family\n  if bond(kael) == \"none\":\n    bond kael friend\n  say: He looks at you for a long moment, and the frost on the jetty melts a little. \"Together,\" he repeats, as if trying out a word from another language.\n> right: Tell the headmistress\n  flag kael_told reported_kael\n  rel kael -huge\n  rel isolde +medium\n  alignment +tiny\n  find kael_family\n  say: The headmistress thanks you gravely. The next day Kael is moved to a room in the headmistress's tower. He doesn't look at you anymore.\n\n@event hs_star_mark\ntitle: The Cold Coin Burns\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: (flag(star_coin) or flag(bell_fragment)) and not has(star_mark) and not flag(threw_star)\nweight: 12\nart: star\ntags: mystery hollowstar magic\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: A student's open palm, a pale star-shaped mark glowing on it, a blackened coin smoking on the floor beside them.\ntext: The black-starred relic you've been carrying burns suddenly, so cold it feels hot. When you drop it, there's a pale star-shaped mark on your palm. It hums when you're near the lake.\n> left: Throw the relic into the lake\n  flag threw_star\n  unflag star_coin bell_fragment\n  alignment +medium\n  mood +tiny\n  say: It sinks without a ripple. The mark on your hand fades — almost entirely. Something under the water sighs.\n> right: Keep it. Feel the power.\n  gain star_mark\n  arcana +medium\n  alignment -medium\n  say: The mark pulses with every heartbeat. Spells come easier now. Much easier. That should probably worry you more than it does.\n\n@event hs_grey_offer\ntitle: An Offer in Grey\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: turn >= 18 and (clues(hollowstar) >= 2 or alignment <= -30) and not flag(grey_offer_seen)\nweight: 12\nart: mask\ntags: mystery hollowstar danger\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: A grey-coated man sitting uninvited at a library table, a black velvet box open in front of him, the candles around him burning blue.\ntext: The man in grey is waiting at your library table. \"You've been asking questions about the Star,\" he says pleasantly. \"It's noticed you. It could make you extraordinary. All it wants is for someone to open a door.\"\n> left: \"Get out.\"\n  alignment +small\n  flag grey_offer_seen\n  mood +tiny\n  say: He inclines his head and leaves. The candles return to yellow. You realise your hands are trembling.\n> right: \"...What would it give me?\"\n  flag grey_offer_seen hollow_tempted\n  arcana +medium\n  alignment -medium\n  say: He shows you, just for a moment. Power, endless and cold. When he's gone, you can still taste it.\n\n@event hs_grey_invitation\ntime: evening night\ntitle: A Seat at the Northern Table\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: alignment <= -60 and turn >= 12 and not flag(hollow_courted)\nweight: 16\nart: letter\ntags: mystery hollowstar danger\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: A black-edged letter on a dormitory pillow, its wax seal pressed with a star that has no light in it.\ntext: There is a letter on your pillow, sealed with a black star. The handwriting is beautiful. \"The families have watched you with great interest,\" it says. \"There is a seat for you at our table, when the door is opened. Come to the boathouse at midnight, or burn this. Either is an answer.\"\n> left: Go to the boathouse\n  flag hollow_courted hollow_tempted\n  alignment -small\n  guile +small\n  health -small\n  say: The man in grey is waiting with two cups of something hot. He talks to you as an equal for an hour. You come back with frost in your hair and the feeling of having been measured, and found to fit.\n> right: Burn it\n  alignment +medium\n  mood -small\n  say: The paper burns blue and slow, and smells of the lake. It takes a long time to go out. Climbing back from where you've been is slower still, but this is a step.\n\n@event hs_grey_tests\ntitle: The Good Argument\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: alignment >= 30 and turn >= 14 and not flag(grey_tested)\nweight: 12\nart: mask\ntags: mystery hollowstar danger\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: A grey-coated man standing beside a student at the infirmary window, both looking down at a first-year with a bandaged arm.\ntext: The man in grey falls into step beside you outside the infirmary. \"You help people,\" he says pleasantly. \"I admire it. But think how many more you could help with real power. The Star doesn't care what it's used for. Only that someone uses it.\"\n> left: \"No.\"\n  flag grey_tested\n  alignment +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: \"No,\" you say. He smiles as if you'd said something charming, and leaves. The first-year in the window waves at you. You wave back, and your hand is shaking.\n> right: \"...How many more?\"\n  flag grey_tested hollow_tempted\n  alignment -medium\n  lore +small\n  say: He tells you. The numbers are very large and very cold. That night, you dream of the lake from underneath, and you are not frightened in the dream.\n\n@event hs_wardens_notice\ntitle: The Headmistress Takes Note\nclass: story\ntone: warm\nactor: isolde\nwhen: alignment >= 60 and turn >= 12 and not flag(wardens_noticed)\nweight: 16\nart: crown\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: A tall woman in dark robes pausing on a stair to look down at one student among many, a silver ring glinting on her hand.\ntext: On the grand stair the headmistress stops beside you. \"Several people have told me about you,\" she says, \"and not one of them was complaining. That is rare.\" Her silver ring catches the light. \"I keep a list of students I would trust with a door. Don't let it go to your head.\"\n> left: \"I'd be honoured.\"\n  meet isolde\n  flag wardens_noticed\n  rel isolde +medium\n  mood -tiny\n  say: \"Good,\" she says. \"Honour is a heavy thing to carry. Practise.\" She walks on. You stand on the stair for some time.\n> right: \"What kind of door?\"\n  meet isolde\n  flag wardens_noticed\n  rel isolde +small\n  lore +small\n  say: Her short, delighted laugh. \"The kind that ought to stay shut,\" she says. \"Keep asking questions like that. Just not on the stairs.\"\n\n@event hs_seventh_tower\ntitle: The Seventh Tower\nplace: seventh_tower\nslot: evening\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: clue(lead_seventh_tower) and not flag(star_key) and not flag(gave_star_key)\nweight: 16\nart: tower\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: The top of a narrow, abandoned tower, cobwebbed and moonlit, with a wall bearing a star-shaped recess.\ntext: The seventh tower has been locked for a century. You find a way in. At the very top, a single room: dust, moonlight, and a star-shaped hollow in the wall, holding something that does not reflect any light.\n> left: Take it quietly\n  challenge deceive journeyman:\n    win:\n      flag star_key\n      find star_key\n      mood -small\n      queue hs_key_breakfast in 1\n      say: A key shaped like a star, black as a hole in the world. As you lift it, every candle in the academy goes out for one breath. You walk back in the dark with it cold against your ribs, and you don't sleep.\n    lose:\n      gain detention\n      mood -small\n      say: A ward shrieks. A prefect finds you on the stairs. You get detention and a very long look from the headmistress.\n> right: Take it boldly\n  challenge spell journeyman:\n    win:\n      flag star_key\n      find star_key\n      mana -small\n      queue hs_key_breakfast in 1\n      say: You tear through the ward with brute force, and it drains you to the bone. The key comes free. Every candle in the academy goes out for one breath, and somewhere below, a door hears it.\n    lose:\n      health -large\n      say: The ward throws you down two flights of stairs.\n\n@event hs_key_breakfast\ntitle: Something of Mine\nclass: story\ntone: tense\ntrigger: queued\ndefer: off_campus\nactor: isolde\nart: key\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: The high table at breakfast, the headmistress setting down her cup and looking along the hall at one student, the candles behind her newly lit.\ntext: At breakfast the headmistress sets down her cup and looks straight at you along the whole length of the hall. Afterwards she is waiting at the door. \"Every candle in my academy went out,\" she says pleasantly, \"and you have something of mine.\"\n> left: Give her the key\n  meet isolde\n  unflag star_key\n  flag gave_star_key wardens_noticed\n  rel isolde +medium\n  alignment +small\n  say: She takes it in a gloved hand without looking at it. \"Thank you. It will be where it is needed, on the night. So, I hope, will you.\" You feel lighter, and a little robbed.\n> right: Keep it\n  meet isolde\n  flag kept_star_key\n  rel isolde -medium\n  say: A long pause. \"Then keep it close,\" she says, \"and never let it near the water before its time. People will come asking for it. Not all of them will ask.\" From then on, the man in grey always seems to know where you are.\n\n@event hs_isolde_summons\ntitle: The Headmistress Knows\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: final_year and month >= 3 and month <= 7 and (clues(hollowstar) >= 3 or flag(kael_ally) or flag(wardens_noticed) or flag(sided_wardens) or flag(sided_dumonts) or flag(sided_self))\npriority: 45\nactor: isolde\nart: crown\ntags: mystery hollowstar story\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: A circular study at the top of a tower, walls lined with sleeping portraits, the headmistress standing by a window that looks down on the lake.\ntext: The headmistress summons you to her tower. \"You have been following the same thread I have,\" she says. \"On the night of the Blossom moon, the seal will weaken. I need people I can trust beneath the lake. Or you can walk away. No one would blame you.\"\n> left: Walk away\n  meet isolde\n  flag hollow_out\n  mood +small\n  say: \"Wise,\" she says, and you can't tell if she means it. \"Then stay above, and keep the first-years indoors.\"\n> right: \"I'll be there.\"\n  alignment +small\n  meet isolde\n  flag warden_trial\n  find star_key\n  rel isolde +large\n  arcana +small\n  mood -small\n  say: She presses a silver ward-ring into your hand. \"{flag(star_key): The founders' key is with you. Keep it close.|{flag(gave_star_key): The founders' key is in my desk.|The founders' key is kept at the top of the seventh tower.}} On the night, it comes down with us. Learn quickly. You have until Blossomtide.\" You lose a little sleep over that every night.\n\n@event hs_the_night\ntitle: The Night the Lake Froze\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: final_year and month == 9 and (not flag(hollow_out) or flag(hollow_tempted) or flag(hollow_courted)) and (clues(hollowstar) >= 3 or flag(warden_trial) or flag(kael_ally) or flag(sided_wardens) or flag(sided_dumonts) or flag(sided_self) or flag(wardens_noticed) or flag(hollow_courted) or alignment <= -60)\npriority: 70\nart: snow\ntags: mystery hollowstar danger\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: The whole lake frozen white in the middle of spring, cracks glowing from beneath, students crowding the towers' windows.\ntext: In the middle of spring, the lake freezes in a single breath. Cracks glow beneath the ice. The bell under the water is tolling, and every candle in the academy leans toward the shore.\n> left: Go down beneath the lake\n  queue hs_tunnel now\n  say: You run for the boathouse. Behind you, someone is shouting your name. You don't stop.\n> right: Stay above and protect the students\n  alignment +small\n  health -large\n  renown +large\n  rel isolde +small\n  flag hollow_held_line\n  say: You hold the doors of the first-year tower all night against things made of frost and whispers. By dawn the lake thaws. Something below is still asleep — for now.\n\n@event hs_tunnel\ntitle: The Man in the Tunnel\nclass: story\ntone: tense\ntrigger: queued\ntype: menu\npriority: 95\nart: mask\ntags: mystery hollowstar danger\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: A slick stone tunnel under the ice, lit blue from above, a tall man in a grey coat standing in the middle of it with his hands folded.\ntext: The tunnel from the boathouse runs down under the ice, slick and glowing blue. Halfway down, the man in grey is waiting{=flag(ilse_taken) and \", and Ilse stands beside him with her eyes on nothing\" or \", alone, as if he has all night\"}. \"Go back up,\" he says pleasantly. \"This isn't your door.\"\n> fight: Break through him\n  hint: Combat. Easier with Kael at your side.\n  queue hs_threshold now\n  if flag(kael_ally) or flag(winter_kael):\n    challenge combat master-:\n      win:\n        flag hs_tunnel_clear\n        health -small\n        say: Kael's frost and your spell hit him together. He slides back along the ice, bows, and is simply not there any more. You go on with bruised ribs and a clear road.\n      lose:\n        health -large\n        say: He swats the two of you aside like moths, then steps back to let you pass, smiling. \"Go on, then.\" You go down bleeding, and late.\n  else:\n    challenge combat master- unlikely:\n      win:\n        flag hs_tunnel_clear\n        health -small\n        say: Your spell catches him square, and he slides back along the ice, surprised for the first time. He bows and is gone. You go on with bruised ribs and a clear road.\n      lose:\n        health -large\n        say: He swats your spell aside, then steps back to let you pass, smiling. \"Go on, then.\" You go down bleeding, and late.\n> talk: Talk your way past\n  hint: Charm. Easier if Ilse is safe, or you know what she was raised for.\n  queue hs_threshold now\n  if flag(ilse_saved) or flag(winter_ilse) or clue(ilse_truth):\n    challenge negotiate master-:\n      win:\n        flag hs_tunnel_clear\n        mood -small\n        say: You tell him the Dumont children have chosen, both of them, and that you know exactly what they were raised for. For a moment he looks old. Then he steps aside. It costs you something to see that look.\n      lose:\n        mood -medium\n        say: He listens courteously, agrees with every word, and lets you pass only once you've heard every word of his. You arrive shaken, and late.\n  else:\n    challenge negotiate master- unlikely:\n      win:\n        flag hs_tunnel_clear\n        mood -small\n        say: You talk fast and well, and somewhere in it he decides you're more trouble than you're worth. He steps aside with a little bow. Your hands don't stop shaking for a while.\n      lose:\n        mood -medium\n        say: He listens courteously, agrees with every word, and lets you pass only once you've heard every word of his. You arrive shaken, and late.\n> slip: Find another way down\n  hint: Guile. Easier if you know the old tunnels.\n  queue hs_threshold now\n  if flag(found_lake_tunnel) or clue(lake_bell):\n    challenge deceive master- likely:\n      win:\n        flag hs_tunnel_clear\n        health -small\n        say: You know a side passage he doesn't, the one the bell's rope once ran through. You come out below him, breathless and spent, and he never sees you go.\n      lose:\n        health -medium\n        say: The side passage is half flooded. You come out soaked and freezing, below him, but late, and he calls a polite good evening after you.\n  else:\n    challenge deceive master- unlikely:\n      win:\n        flag hs_tunnel_clear\n        mana -small\n        say: You fold yourself into the dark and go round him along the wall, a breath at a time. It drains you, but he never turns his head.\n      lose:\n        health -medium\n        say: He turns his head the moment you move. \"Good evening.\" He lets you pass anyway, and the cold he leaves in your bones comes with you.\n\n@event hs_threshold\ntitle: The Founders' Words\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: queued\ntype: menu\npriority: 95\nart: book\ntags: mystery hollowstar danger\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: A wall of black stone at the end of a tunnel, carved with lines of script that drift when not looked at, a faint bell-tone hanging in the air.\ntext: The tunnel ends at a wall of black stone carved with the founders' words, in a script that drifts when you look away. Behind it the bell is tolling. The wall will open for someone who can read it, or someone who can make it.\n> read: Read the founders' words\n  hint: Lore. The founders' seal and the seven locks make it easier.\n  queue hs_beneath now\n  if clue(knows_founders_seal) and clue(seven_locks):\n    challenge research master- likely:\n      win:\n        flag hs_threshold_read\n        mood -tiny\n        say: You've read these words before, in pieces, and you know where the locks fall. Said whole, they open the wall like a door, and the strain of it sits behind your eyes.\n      lose:\n        health -small\n        mood -small\n        say: You stumble on the last line, and the wall opens only a crack. You squeeze through it; it scrapes skin off your shoulder on the way.\n  elif clue(knows_founders_seal) or clue(seven_locks) or flag(winter_isolde):\n    challenge research master-:\n      win:\n        flag hs_threshold_read\n        mood -tiny\n        say: You know enough to guess the rest. The wall listens, and slides aside, and the strain of it sits behind your eyes.\n      lose:\n        health -small\n        mood -small\n        say: You stumble on the last line, and the wall opens only a crack. You squeeze through it; it scrapes skin off your shoulder on the way.\n  else:\n    challenge research master- unlikely:\n      win:\n        flag hs_threshold_read\n        mood -tiny\n        say: You don't know the words, but you know how words like these are built. Line by line, the wall gives way, and the strain of it sits behind your eyes.\n      lose:\n        health -small\n        mood -small\n        say: The script slides away from you. In the end the wall opens a crack on its own, as if amused. You squeeze through; it takes skin with it.\n> force: Force the stone open\n  hint: Arcana, and it hurts. Easier if Maud taught you her wards.\n  queue hs_beneath now\n  if flag(winter_maud):\n    challenge spell master-:\n      win:\n        flag hs_threshold_read\n        health -small\n        say: You trace Maud's wards backwards into the stone, the way she showed you. The wall cracks down the middle and opens, and your hands ache to the elbow.\n      lose:\n        health -medium\n        say: The stone fights back. You get through in the end, but your hands are raw and shaking.\n  else:\n    challenge spell master- unlikely:\n      win:\n        flag hs_threshold_read\n        health -small\n        say: You put everything you have into the stone, and it cracks down the middle. Your hands ache to the elbow.\n      lose:\n        health -medium\n        say: The stone fights back. You get through in the end, but your hands are raw and shaking.\n\n@event hs_beneath\ntitle: The Door Beneath the Lake\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: queued\ntype: menu\npriority: 95\nart: door\ntags: mystery hollowstar danger\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: A vast domed cavern under the lake, a round door of black stone standing open a crack, and through it a star that is pure absence.\ntext: Beyond the wall, the round door. It stands open a crack. Through it, the Hollow Star looks at you — and it is beautiful, and it is hungry, and it knows your name.{=flag(kael_ally) and \" Kael is beside you, wand raised, frost spreading from his feet.\" or \"\"}\n> seal: Seal the door\n  hint: Needs the star key, the founders' secret or a ward-ring. Arcana; preparation and the way down make it easier. It can fail.\n  when: flag(star_key) or flag(knows_founders_seal) or flag(warden_trial) or flag(gave_star_key)\n  alignment +small\n  hs_prep = clue(seven_locks) + clue(star_key) + clue(ilse_truth) + (rel(kael) >= 18) + (rel(ilse) >= 18) + (rel(maud) >= 18) + (rel(isolde) >= 18) + 2 * (flag(sided_wardens) or flag(sided_dumonts)) + flag(sided_self) + (alignment >= 30) + (alignment >= 60) + (spell_level(starlight) >= 1) + (spell_level(ward) >= 1) + flag(wardens_noticed)\n  hs_seal = hs_prep + 2 * flag(hs_tunnel_clear) + 2 * flag(hs_threshold_read)\n  if hs_seal >= 13:\n    challenge spell master sure:\n      win:\n        flag hollow_sealed\n        renown +huge\n        mood +large\n        lose star_mark\n        lose hollow_dreams\n        queue hs_aftermath in 1\n        say: {flag(knows_founders_seal): You speak the founders' words|You set the seal with everything you have}, and the door grinds shut. The Star screams without sound. Above, the ice cracks and the lake breathes again.\n      lose:\n        flag hollow_failed\n        health -huge\n        lose hollow_dreams\n        queue hs_aftermath_lost in 1\n        if flag(kael_ally) or flag(winter_kael):\n          rel kael +large\n          say: Your seal falters. Kael throws every ounce of frost in him into it, and it still isn't enough: the door stays ajar. The last thing you see is the headmistress's lantern coming down the tunnel.\n        else:\n          say: Your seal falters and breaks, and takes most of you with it. The door stays ajar. The last thing you see is the headmistress's lantern coming down the tunnel.\n  elif hs_seal >= 9:\n    challenge spell master likely:\n      win:\n        flag hollow_sealed\n        renown +huge\n        mood +large\n        lose star_mark\n        lose hollow_dreams\n        queue hs_aftermath in 1\n        say: {flag(knows_founders_seal): You speak the founders' words|You set the seal with everything you have}, and the door grinds shut. The Star screams without sound. Above, the ice cracks and the lake breathes again.\n      lose:\n        flag hollow_failed\n        health -huge\n        lose hollow_dreams\n        queue hs_aftermath_lost in 1\n        if flag(kael_ally) or flag(winter_kael):\n          rel kael +large\n          say: Your seal falters. Kael throws every ounce of frost in him into it, and it still isn't enough: the door stays ajar. The last thing you see is the headmistress's lantern coming down the tunnel.\n        else:\n          say: Your seal falters and breaks, and takes most of you with it. The door stays ajar. The last thing you see is the headmistress's lantern coming down the tunnel.\n  elif hs_seal >= 5:\n    challenge spell master:\n      win:\n        flag hollow_sealed\n        renown +huge\n        mood +large\n        lose star_mark\n        lose hollow_dreams\n        queue hs_aftermath in 1\n        say: {flag(knows_founders_seal): You speak the founders' words|You set the seal with everything you have}, and the door grinds shut. The Star screams without sound. Above, the ice cracks and the lake breathes again.\n      lose:\n        flag hollow_failed\n        health -huge\n        lose hollow_dreams\n        queue hs_aftermath_lost in 1\n        if flag(kael_ally) or flag(winter_kael):\n          rel kael +large\n          say: Your seal falters. Kael throws every ounce of frost in him into it, and it still isn't enough: the door stays ajar. The last thing you see is the headmistress's lantern coming down the tunnel.\n        else:\n          say: Your seal falters and breaks, and takes most of you with it. The door stays ajar. The last thing you see is the headmistress's lantern coming down the tunnel.\n  elif hs_seal >= 2:\n    challenge spell master unlikely:\n      win:\n        flag hollow_sealed\n        renown +huge\n        mood +large\n        lose star_mark\n        lose hollow_dreams\n        queue hs_aftermath in 1\n        say: {flag(knows_founders_seal): You speak the founders' words|You set the seal with everything you have}, and the door grinds shut. The Star screams without sound. Above, the ice cracks and the lake breathes again.\n      lose:\n        flag hollow_failed\n        health -huge\n        lose hollow_dreams\n        queue hs_aftermath_lost in 1\n        if flag(kael_ally) or flag(winter_kael):\n          rel kael +large\n          say: Your seal falters. Kael throws every ounce of frost in him into it, and it still isn't enough: the door stays ajar. The last thing you see is the headmistress's lantern coming down the tunnel.\n        else:\n          say: Your seal falters and breaks, and takes most of you with it. The door stays ajar. The last thing you see is the headmistress's lantern coming down the tunnel.\n  else:\n    challenge spell master slim:\n      win:\n        flag hollow_sealed\n        renown +huge\n        mood +large\n        lose star_mark\n        lose hollow_dreams\n        queue hs_aftermath in 1\n        say: {flag(knows_founders_seal): You speak the founders' words|You set the seal with everything you have}, and the door grinds shut. The Star screams without sound. Above, the ice cracks and the lake breathes again.\n      lose:\n        flag hollow_failed\n        health -huge\n        lose hollow_dreams\n        queue hs_aftermath_lost in 1\n        if flag(kael_ally) or flag(winter_kael):\n          rel kael +large\n          say: Your seal falters. Kael throws every ounce of frost in him into it, and it still isn't enough: the door stays ajar. The last thing you see is the headmistress's lantern coming down the tunnel.\n        else:\n          say: Your seal falters and breaks, and takes most of you with it. The door stays ajar. The last thing you see is the headmistress's lantern coming down the tunnel.\n> bargain: Accept the Star's bargain\n  hint: Power beyond measure. A voice in your head forever. Only for those it has already tempted.\n  when: alignment <= -30 or flag(hollow_tempted)\n  flag hollow_bargain\n  alignment -large\n  arcana +huge\n  gain star_mark\n  health -large\n  lose hollow_dreams\n  if alignment <= -30:\n    mood +medium\n    say: You take what it offers, and it feels like coming home. The door closes on its own, for now. The Star settles into the back of your mind like a cat finding a warm lap. The price comes out of your body: you climb the tunnel grey-lipped, and for days every stair leaves you breathless.\n  else:\n    mood -large\n    say: You take what it offers. The door closes on its own, for now. The Star settles into the back of your mind like a cat finding a warm lap. You climb the tunnel grey-lipped, and for a week you lie awake, too weak for stairs.\n> open: Open the door\n  hint: It wants out. Only one already deep in the Hollow could walk through beside it.\n  when: alignment <= -60 or flag(hollow_tempted)\n  if alignment <= -60:\n    flag hollow_freed\n    alignment -large\n    arcana +large\n    gain star_mark\n    lose hollow_dreams\n    renown -medium\n    say: You set your palm on the black stone and push. The door swings wide. The Star rises past you like a breath held for three hundred years, and on its way out it touches your shoulder, the way a lord touches a sword. Above, every candle in Lumenhold goes out, and does not come back on until dawn.\n  else:\n    end ending_hollow_claimed\n> flee: Run\n  health -large\n  mood -large\n  flag hollow_fled\n  say: You run until the tunnel spits you out onto the shore. Behind you, the door is still ajar. In the morning, the headmistress's hair has gone white.\n\n@event hs_aftermath\ntitle: After the Thaw\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: queued\nart: sun\ntags: hollowstar story\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: The lake glittering in spring sunshine, students skimming stones, a bandaged figure sitting on the jetty.\ntext: The lake glitters as if nothing happened. Most of the academy believes there was a strange spring frost. The headmistress knows better. So does Kael, who finds you on the jetty with two cups of tea.\n> left: Sit with him in silence\n  rel kael +large\n  mood +medium\n  say: You watch the water. It's just water, now. Kael's tea, for once, doesn't freeze.\n> right: Tell him how you feel\n  if spark(kael) >= 25:\n    spark kael +large\n    bond kael partner\n    say: He kisses you, and for the first time since you've known him, his hands are warm.\n  else:\n    rel kael +large\n    if bond(kael) == \"none\":\n      bond kael close\n    say: \"I owe you everything,\" he says. \"I'm not good at owing people. I'll learn.\"\n\n@event hs_aftermath_lost\ntitle: The Headmistress Closed It\nclass: story\ntone: grim\ntrigger: queued\nactor: isolde\nart: door\ntags: hollowstar story\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: An infirmary bed by a window, the lake thawed outside, a tall woman with newly white hair sitting beside it without her silver ring.\ntext: You wake in the infirmary. The lake has thawed, and the door is shut: the headmistress came down after you and closed it with her own hands. She is sitting by your bed. Her hair has gone white overnight, and her silver ring is gone.\n> left: \"I'm sorry.\"\n  meet isolde\n  rel isolde +medium\n  mood -medium\n  say: \"You went down,\" she says. \"Most don't. The door is shut, and I am old, and that was always the price for someone.\" She pats your hand once, like a chess move. You can't stop thinking about it for weeks.\n> right: \"What did it cost you?\"\n  meet isolde\n  lore +small\n  mood -large\n  say: She tells you, plainly, and you wish she hadn't. The seal will hold for another lifetime. She won't. She asks you not to tell the others, and you don't, and it weighs on you.\n\n@event hs_kael_sister\ntitle: The Sister\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: flag(kael_ally) and turn >= 22 and not flag(met_sister)\nweight: 10\nart: snow\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: A girl in white furs standing at the academy gates in a snowstorm that is falling only on her.\ntext: A girl in white furs is waiting at the gates in a private snowstorm. Kael's younger sister, Ilse. \"If my brother won't open it,\" she says, \"I will. Unless you can give me a reason not to.\"\n> left: Convince her\n  flag met_sister\n  find lead_white_furs\n  find ilse_truth\n  challenge negotiate journeyman:\n    win:\n      rel kael +large\n      say: You talk for an hour. Then she's crying, and Kael is holding her, and the snow stops. She stays in the village to be near him.\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: She listens politely and leaves. \"We'll see each other again,\" she says. \"Beneath the water.\"\n> right: Warn the headmistress\n  flag met_sister\n  find lead_white_furs\n  find ilse_truth\n  rel isolde +small\n  rel kael -small\n  say: Ilse is escorted from the grounds. Kael understands. He says he understands. He's quiet for a long time.\n"},{"path":"content/events/chain_ilse.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  CHAIN: Ilse Dumont (expansion)\n#  Kael's younger sister, raised by the Hollow cult. She takes a room\n#  above the bakery in year 2 (hs_ilse_arrives, X11); you meet her\n#  there if you follow the lead, or at the gates (hs_kael_sister).\n#    ilse_village -> ilse_frost_lessons -> ilse_grey_returns\n#      -> (ilse_saved) ilse_kael_reunion / ilse_northern_letter / ilse_farewell\n#      -> (ilse_taken) she is gone north\n# ============================================================\n\n@event ilse_village\ntitle: The Girl at the Bakery\nplace: village\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nactor: ilse\nwhen: not met(ilse) and (flag(ilse_in_village) or flag(met_sister))\nweight: 20\nart: snow\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nchain: ilse\nscene: A village bakery counter, a pale girl in white furs pushing a coin that is visibly frosted across the wood, a baffled baker.\ntext: In the village bakery a girl in white furs is trying to pay for a bun with a coin so cold it has frozen to the counter. The baker is losing patience. You recognise those eyes: they're Kael's. \"Ilse,\" she says, before you ask. \"Don't.\"\n> left: Pay for her bun\n  meet ilse\n  gold -tiny\n  rel ilse +medium\n  say: She stares at the bun, then at you, as if kindness is a trick she hasn't learned the counter to yet. \"Fine,\" she says. \"Thank you. Fine.\"\n> right: Leave her to it\n  meet ilse\n  rel ilse -tiny\n  say: You leave. Through the window you see the coin crack, and the baker's breath freeze in the air. Ilse walks out with the bun. Nobody stops her.\n\n@event ilse_frost_lessons\ntitle: A Trade in Winter\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: social\nactor: ilse\nwhen: rel(ilse) >= 3 and not flag(ilse_taken) and not flag(ilse_went_north)\nweight: 14\nart: snow\ntags: magic hollowstar\nchain: ilse\nscene: A frozen pond at the edge of the woods, a girl in white furs drawing a perfect hexagon of frost in the air, a student copying her clumsily.\ntext: Ilse finds you by the frozen pond. \"My brother won't talk to me. You will. Tell me how he really is, and I'll teach you Northern frost-craft. It's better than anything your professors know.\"\n> left: Tell her the truth about Kael\n  rel ilse +large\n  arcana +medium\n  say: You tell her about the cold hands, {seen(astro_kael_roof): the roof at three in the morning|the nights he doesn't sleep}, the bread. She listens without moving. Then she teaches you to freeze a falling leaf mid-air.\n> right: Protect Kael's privacy\n  rel ilse -small\n  rel kael +small\n  arcana +small\n  say: \"He can tell you himself,\" you say. Ilse's eyes go flat. But the next day, she's at the pond again. \"Fine. I'll teach you anyway. You'll need it.\" She teaches you badly on purpose for an hour, and then, despite herself, well.\n\n@event ilse_grey_returns\ntime: night\ntitle: The Man in Grey Comes for Ilse\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: social\nactor: ilse\nwhen: rel(ilse) >= 7 and turn >= 20 and not flag(ilse_saved) and not flag(ilse_taken)\nweight: 18\nart: mask\ntags: danger hollowstar\nchain: ilse\nscene: A moonlit village lane, a man in a grey coat with one hand on a girl's shoulder, frost and shadow coiling around them both.\ntext: Late at night, a lane behind the bakery. The man in grey has one hand on Ilse's shoulder. \"Home,\" he is saying softly. \"Your family is waiting. The door is waiting.\" Ilse sees you. She doesn't call out. She just looks.\n> left: Step between them\n  challenge combat journeyman likely:\n    win:\n      flag ilse_saved\n      rel ilse +large\n      renown +small\n      alignment +small\n      find ilse_truth\n      say: Your spell breaks his grip. He looks at his frosted hand with mild surprise, bows, and walks into the dark. Ilse is shaking. \"Nobody's ever — \" she says, and stops.\n    lose:\n      flag ilse_taken\n      health -large\n      alignment +small\n      find ilse_truth\n      say: He swats your spell aside like a moth. When you get up, the lane is empty except for a single white glove in the snow.\n> right: Run for Kael\n  if met(kael):\n    flag ilse_saved\n    rel kael +large\n    rel ilse +medium\n    find ilse_truth\n    say: Kael arrives like a blizzard. The man in grey takes one look at him and leaves without a word. Brother and sister stand in the snow, not touching, not speaking.\n  else:\n    flag ilse_taken\n    mood -medium\n    find ilse_truth\n    say: By the time you're back with help, the lane is empty. There's frost on the cobbles in the shape of a closed door.\n\n@event ilse_kael_reunion\ntitle: Brother and Sister\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: social\nactor: ilse\nwhen: flag(ilse_saved) and met(kael) and not flag(dumont_reunited) and not flag(ilse_went_north)\nweight: 16\nart: snow\ntags: social hollowstar\nchain: ilse\nscene: Two pale siblings on opposite ends of a frozen jetty, a third person between them holding three cups of tea.\ntext: Kael and Ilse have been in the same village for weeks and have spoken eleven words to each other. You have three cups of tea, a frozen jetty and a very bad idea.\n> left: Sit them both down\n  challenge negotiate apprentice+:\n    win:\n      flag dumont_reunited\n      rel kael +large\n      rel ilse +large\n      say: It takes an hour and two cups of tea are frozen solid. Then Ilse says \"I was scared,\" and Kael says \"So was I,\" and you quietly leave them to it.\n    lose:\n      rel kael +small\n      rel ilse +small\n      say: They argue in a language you don't speak, at a temperature you can't endure. But they argue — which, you suspect, is a start.\n> right: Let them find their own way\n  flag dumont_reunited\n  rel kael +small\n  rel ilse +small\n  say: You leave the tea on the jetty. A week later you see them walking by the lake together, a careful arm's length apart. Then less.\n\n@event ilse_northern_letter\ntitle: A Summons From the North\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: social\nactor: ilse\nwhen: flag(ilse_saved) and final_year and not flag(ilse_decided)\nweight: 14\nart: letter\ntags: hollowstar\nchain: ilse\nscene: A girl holding a letter sealed with a black star, standing at a window that looks north toward far white mountains.\ntext: A letter with a black-star seal. The Dumont family summons Ilse home. \"If I don't go, they'll send someone worse than the man in grey,\" she says. \"If I do go, I could tell them to their faces that it's over. Or they could keep me.\"\n> left: \"Go. Tell them it's over.\"\n  flag ilse_decided ilse_went_north\n  rel ilse +medium\n  say: She leaves on the dawn barge, chin up. Before she goes, she presses her forehead to yours, a Northern farewell. \"If I'm not back by graduation,\" she says, \"come and get me.\"\n> right: \"Stay. Let them come.\"\n  flag ilse_decided\n  rel ilse +large\n  gain frost_ward\n  say: She stays. She sews a sliver of winter into your collar \"in case they do come\". Nobody does. Not yet.\n\n@event ilse_farewell\ntitle: Snow in Blossomtide\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: ilse\nwhen: flag(ilse_saved) and final_year and month >= 7 and month <= 9 and not flag(ilse_goodbye) and not flag(ilse_went_north)\nweight: 14\nart: snow\ntags: social\nchain: ilse\nscene: A single snowflake falling in a spring orchard full of blossom, a girl in white furs catching it on her glove and holding it out.\ntext: It's spring, the orchard is in full blossom, and it is snowing — gently, over one tree. Ilse is underneath it. \"Graduation's soon,\" she says. \"Everyone's leaving. I wanted to give you something before you forgot me.\"\n> left: \"I won't forget you.\"\n  flag ilse_goodbye\n  rel ilse +large\n  gain frost_ward\n  mood +medium\n  say: She doesn't believe you, but she smiles anyway — the first real smile you've seen on her. She gives you a snowflake that never melts.\n> right: Invite her to the graduation\n  flag ilse_goodbye\n  rel ilse +large\n  rel kael +small\n  say: \"Me? At a graduation?\" She looks horrified, and then, slowly, delighted. On the day, she sits next to Kael in the second row and cries more than anyone.\n"},{"path":"content/events/chain_thornwick.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  The Thornwick rivalry (playtest 2, X9): four years of a rival\n#  school and one friend in it, apart from the Hollow Star.\n#    the pen pal: life_pen_pal (y1) -> Tamsin's letters (social,\n#      actor tamsin) -> asked for secrets (y2) / offered theirs\n#      (y3) -> what they were sent to do (tamsin_confession)\n#    the fixture: life_rival_academy (y1, they visit) ->\n#      rival_return_visit (y2) -> rival_contest (y3, combat) ->\n#      rival_last_match (y4) -> tamsin_farewell\n#  Ties: the Thornwick internship, Lyra's opinion, a yearbook line.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- The letters ----------------\n\n@event tamsin_second_letter\ntitle: Insufferable AND Rude\ntrigger: queued\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: letter\ntags: social\nscene: A second letter with a green Thornwick seal on a dormitory pillow, the word \"NOTED\" underlined three times.\ntext: A second letter from Thornwick, in the same quick hand. \"Insufferable AND rude. Noted. I'm giving you one more chance, because I'm a generous person and because my roommate bet me you'd never answer. Prove her wrong. T. Reed.\"\n> left: Write back, finally\n  meet tamsin\n  rel tamsin +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You write back, grudgingly at first and then not at all grudgingly. Four pages. The answer comes within a week, with a postscript: \"My roommate owes me a pie. Thank you.\"\n> right: Let it lie\n  say: You leave it on the pillow. Somewhere across the hills, a roommate wins a pie. You'll never know what you missed, which is how it should be, probably.\n\n@event tamsin_letter_gossip\ntitle: News From Thornwick\nactor: tamsin\ntrigger: letter\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 6\nweight: 12\nart: letter\ntags: social letter\nscene: A letter with a green seal open on a desk, its margins full of tiny unflattering sketches of Thornwick professors.\ntext: {Tamsin has written again, six pages of Thornwick gossip with sketches of the professors in the margins.|Another letter from Tamsin, signed \"your rival, nemesis and only friend west of the hills\".|Tamsin's letter is mostly a drawing of their illusion professor as a goat.} {Their head of house has banned whistling.|Someone at Thornwick set the library on fire, a little.|They want to know if Lumenhold's food is really as bad as everyone says.} Answering properly would take an evening.\n> left: Write back at length\n  rel tamsin +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You write till the candle gutters, with sketches of your own professors. Tamsin's reply calls your drawing of Thorne \"libellous and correct\".|Five pages back, with the castle's gossip and your worst week. They answer the worst week first.|You answer the goat with your own worst professor as a hen, and four pages on the refectory food. Tamsin replies that you've undersold the food.}\n> right: A few lines and a sketch\n  rel tamsin +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {A short note and a drawing of a gargoyle. Tamsin writes back that it's \"the most Lumenhold thing ever sent through the post\".|Three lines. Tamsin replies with three pages, offended and delighted.|A few lines about the food and a goat of your own in the margin. Tamsin replies with a whole menagerie.}\n\n@event tamsin_letter_questions\ntitle: Twenty Questions\nactor: tamsin\ntrigger: letter\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: letter\ntags: social letter\nscene: A letter numbered down the margin from one to twenty, each line a question, a student chewing a quill over the answers.\ntext: {Tamsin's letter is a numbered list of twenty questions about life at Lumenhold, from \"Is the lake haunted?\" to \"What do you eat for breakfast?\"|Another list of questions. Number seven is \"Who is the most annoying person at your school?\"|Questions again. \"Be honest,\" says number one. \"Number twenty can be a lie.\"} {Number twelve asks about your exams.|Number fifteen asks about the lake.|Number three asks about the food.} Number nineteen just says \"Are you happy?\"\n> left: Answer every one honestly\n  rel tamsin +small\n  lore +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {Twenty honest answers, even number nineteen. It's harder than it looks. Their reply begins, \"Thank you for nineteen.\"|You answer the lot. Writing it down, you learn a few things about yourself.|You answer all twenty honestly, even number three, which is about the food and unkind. Number twenty you leave as a lie, as permitted.}\n> right: Make the answers outrageous\n  charm +tiny\n  rel tamsin +tiny\n  say: {The lake is haunted by a choir of geese. Breakfast is boiled bricks. Tamsin's reply is two pages of laughter and a request for \"the true answers, eventually\".|You invent a Lumenhold of dragons and duels before dawn. Tamsin believes none of it and asks for more.|Every answer is outrageous except number twenty, which is the true one. Tamsin works out which, and writes back only \"I knew it.\"}\n\n@event tamsin_letter_trouble\ntitle: A Short Letter\nactor: tamsin\ntrigger: letter\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: rel(tamsin) >= 4\nweight: 12\nart: letter\ntags: social letter\nscene: A single short page with a green seal, the handwriting cramped and unsteady, a blot where a sentence was crossed out hard.\ntext: Tamsin's letter is one page, which has never happened. They failed their illusion examination, the one thing they were good at, and their grandmother's theatre has been sold. There's a sentence crossed out so hard the nib went through the paper.\n> left: Write back that night\n  rel tamsin +medium\n  mood -small\n  health -tiny\n  say: You write until two in the morning: the time you failed, what helped, a list of everything they're good at that isn't illusion. Their next letter is six pages again, and the first line is \"You idiot, you made me cry in the refectory.\"\n> right: Send them something instead\n  gold -small\n  rel tamsin +small\n  say: You send a parcel: Mabel's best biscuits, a book of plays, a drawing of a gargoyle holding a sign that says \"RETAKE\". Tamsin writes back that the gargoyle is on their wall now, and that they passed.\n\n@event tamsin_letter_asks_secret\ntitle: A Small Favour\nactor: tamsin\ntrigger: letter\nclass: story\ntone: tense\nwhen: year >= 2 and rel(tamsin) >= 6\nweight: 12\nart: letter\ntags: social letter rival\nscene: A letter half-read on a desk, a single paragraph underlined twice, a student staring out of a window at the hills.\ntext: Tamsin's letter is cheerful until the last paragraph. \"Small favour. Thornwick's duelling captain wants to know Lumenhold's tournament order. Just the order. Everyone does it, honestly. I'd owe you.\" The underline is very neat, as if they did it twice.\n> left: Send the real order\n  alignment -small\n  rel tamsin +small\n  flag told_thornwick\n  say: You write it out: who fights first, who last. It feels like nothing at all until you post it, and then it feels like a stone. Tamsin's reply thanks you, too warmly.\n> right: Send a false one\n  guile +small\n  alignment -tiny\n  rel tamsin -tiny\n  say: You invent an order, plausible and wrong. At the tournament, Thornwick's captain looks baffled for the whole first round. Tamsin's next letter is short and doesn't mention it, which is how you know they know.\n> refuse: Say no, and say why\n  rel tamsin -small\n  alignment +small\n  say: \"Ask me anything else,\" you write. Their reply is a week late and very polite. Then, a fortnight after: \"You were right. Sorry. Still friends?\" You write back yes before you've finished reading.\n\n@event tamsin_letter_offers_secret\ntitle: Their Turn\nactor: tamsin\ntrigger: letter\nclass: story\ntone: tense\nwhen: year == 3 and month <= 7 and seen(rival_contest) == 0 and rel(tamsin) >= 8 and not flag(thornwick_secrets)\nweight: 12\nart: letter\ntags: social letter rival\nscene: A folded paper inside a letter, covered in diagrams of duelling circles and arrows, a student holding it at arm's length as if it were hot.\ntext: Folded inside Tamsin's letter is a second paper: Thornwick's whole plan for this year's contest, every pairing, every trick. \"Fair's fair,\" they've written. \"Burn it or use it. I won't ask which.\"\n> left: Use it\n  flag thornwick_secrets\n  alignment -small\n  rel tamsin +small\n  say: You keep the plan and study it until you know it better than Thornwick does. It will help. You can't quite look at your own reflection while you read it.\n> right: Burn it unread\n  alignment +small\n  rel tamsin +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You burn it in the candle without unfolding it, and write back that you did. Their reply has one line: \"I knew you would. That's why I sent it.\" You're still not sure if it was a test.\n\n@event tamsin_confession\ntitle: What I Was Sent to Do\nactor: tamsin\ntrigger: letter\nclass: milestone\ntone: tense\nwhen: year >= 2 and rel(tamsin) >= 10\nramp: rel(tamsin) 18..30\nweight: 14\nart: letter\ntags: social letter rival\nscene: A long letter in a shaky hand spread across a desk, a green seal broken beside it, a student reading with one hand over their mouth.\ntext: Tamsin's letter is long and the hand is unsteady. \"I have to tell you. The first letter wasn't random. Thornwick set some of us to write to Lumenhold students and report back. I reported for two letters. Then I liked you, and I stopped, and I've been ashamed ever since. You can stop writing. I'd understand.\"\n> left: Forgive them\n  rel tamsin +large\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You write back one line: \"Third letter onwards, then. That's when I started liking you too.\" Their reply is the longest yet, and the first not signed with an insult.\n> right: Ask what they reported\n  rel tamsin +small\n  lore +tiny\n  say: They tell you, exactly: the food, the weather, your opinion of Professor Thorne. Nothing that mattered. \"I made it boring on purpose,\" they write. You laugh, and then you forgive them anyway.\n\n@event tamsin_letter_after\ntitle: After Graduation\nactor: tamsin\ntrigger: letter\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: final_year and month >= 7 and rel(tamsin) >= 6\nweight: 14\nart: letter\ntags: social letter\nscene: A letter with a green seal and a pressed flower, a map of the realm sketched on the back with two towns circled.\ntext: Tamsin's letter has a map on the back with two towns circled: Lumenhold and Thornwick, and a line between them. \"After graduation, there'll be no school to be rivals for. What are we, then?\" Under it, smaller: \"Don't answer that. Answer it.\"\n> left: \"Friends. Obviously.\"\n  rel tamsin +medium\n  mood +small\n  say: You write it in letters an inch high. Their reply has the map again, with a third town circled halfway between: \"Meeting point. Every spring. Bring biscuits.\"\n> right: \"Still rivals. Obviously.\"\n  rel tamsin +small\n  charm +tiny\n  say: \"Excellent,\" they write back. \"I was worried you'd go soft.\" Under it, very small: \"Friends too, though. Obviously.\"\n\n# ---------------- The yearly fixture ----------------\n\n@event rival_return_visit\ntitle: To Thornwick\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: year == 2 and month == 8 and not off_campus\ntype: menu\nclass: story\ntone: light\nart: tower\ntags: rival social\nscene: A barge full of Lumenhold students arriving at a rival academy of green-roofed towers, a crowd of green-robed students waiting on the jetty.\ntext: This year it's Lumenhold's turn to visit. A barge leaves for Thornwick at dawn with a delegation of students who have been told to behave. {met(tamsin): Tamsin has written three letters about it. The last one just says \"COME\".|Thornwick has, by all accounts, better plumbing.}\n> go: Join the delegation\n  hint: Two days across the hills. The barge fare is yours.\n  gold -small\n  mood +small\n  if met(tamsin):\n    rel tamsin +medium\n    say: Tamsin is on the jetty, smaller than their letters and louder, holding a sign with your name spelled wrong on purpose. They show you every corner of Thornwick and argue that each one beats Lumenhold. They're mostly wrong.\n  else:\n    charm +tiny\n    say: Thornwick's towers are green-roofed and smug, and their plumbing really is better. A student in green follows you around the library, asking about the lake.\n> stay: Stay at the castle\n  hint: A quiet weekend with half the school gone.\n  rest\n  if met(tamsin):\n    rel tamsin -small\n    say: You stay. The castle is wonderfully quiet. Tamsin's next letter is very short, and sulks, and then forgives you on the last line.\n  else:\n    say: The castle is half empty and wholly peaceful. You read in the window seat all weekend and don't miss Thornwick at all.\n\n@event rival_contest\ntitle: The Inter-School Contest\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: year == 3 and month == 8 and not off_campus\nclass: story\ntone: tense\nart: trophy\ntags: rival duel\nscene: A packed duelling arena under banners of blue and green, two duellists facing each other in the chalk ring, a crowd on its feet.\ntext: The inter-school contest, at Lumenhold this year, before both schools and half the village. You've made the team. {met(tamsin): In the final, the name opposite yours is Tamsin Reed. They wave from across the ring, apologetically, and then stop smiling.|In the final, a Thornwick illusionist faces you, smiling far too much.}{flag(thornwick_secrets): You know every trick they've planned.|}\n> left: Fight to win\n  if flag(thornwick_secrets):\n    challenge combat adept likely:\n      win:\n        flag thornwick_contest_won\n        renown +large\n        health -small\n        say: You counter every planned trick before it's cast. Lumenhold wins the contest, and the castle roars. {met(tamsin): Tamsin shakes your hand afterwards and holds on too long. \"You read the plan,\" they say quietly. \"Good.\" You can't tell if they mean it.|}\n      lose:\n        renown -tiny\n        health -medium\n        say: Knowing the plan isn't enough: they change it in the ring. Thornwick takes the cup. The plan, in your pocket, feels heavier than ever.\n  else:\n    challenge combat adept:\n      win:\n        flag thornwick_contest_won\n        renown +large\n        health -small\n        say: An honest win, hard and close. Lumenhold takes the cup and carries you round the arena. {met(tamsin): Tamsin is on the ground laughing. \"Next year,\" they gasp. \"Next year I'll flatten you.\"|}\n      lose:\n        renown -tiny\n        health -medium\n        say: You lose by a breath. Thornwick sings all the way back to their barge. {met(tamsin): Tamsin helps you up and doesn't gloat, not once, which you'll never forget.|}\n> right: Fight clean and generous\n  renown +small\n  alignment +tiny\n  if met(tamsin):\n    rel tamsin +small\n  say: You fight well and fairly, and when you have them down you let them stand before the next exchange. You lose on points. The crowd stands for both of you anyway, and Lyra, in the stands, actually claps.\n\n@event rival_last_match\ntitle: The Last Match\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: year == 4 and month == 8 and not off_campus\nclass: story\ntone: warm\nart: trophy\ntags: rival duel\nscene: An evening exhibition match on the lawn between two academies, lanterns strung between the trees, final-year students in blue and green laughing in the ring.\ntext: The last match of your year against Thornwick's: an exhibition on the lawn, lanterns in the trees, nothing at stake but four years of grudges. {met(tamsin): Tamsin has asked to be your opponent. \"For the record,\" they say. \"Ours.\"|Thornwick's final-years have brought cake, which feels like a trick.}\n> left: Give it everything\n  challenge combat adept+:\n    win:\n      renown +medium\n      queue tamsin_farewell in 1\n      say: You win the last exchange of your schooling against Thornwick, and the lawn erupts. {met(tamsin): Tamsin, flat on the grass, laughs up at the lanterns. \"Fine,\" they say. \"Fine. Record's yours.\"|}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      queue tamsin_farewell in 1\n      say: You lose, gloriously, and lie on the grass looking at the lanterns. {met(tamsin): Tamsin lies down next to you. \"Record's mine,\" they say, and then, quieter, \"I'll miss this.\"|}\n> right: Turn it into a show\n  charm +small\n  mood +small\n  queue tamsin_farewell in 1\n  say: You turn the match into a performance, illusions and flourishes and a duel that never lands a blow. {met(tamsin): Tamsin catches on at once and plays along. The two schools applaud the same thing for the first time in a century.|Both schools applaud the same thing for the first time in a century.}\n\n@event tamsin_farewell\ntitle: Across the Hills\ntrigger: queued\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nwhen: met(tamsin)\nart: letter\ntags: social\nscene: Two students at a barge landing at dusk, one in Lumenhold blue and one in Thornwick green, exchanging small wrapped parcels.\ntext: At the barge landing, before Thornwick's delegation leaves for the last time, Tamsin gives you a small parcel. Inside: every letter you ever sent them, tied with string. \"So you can read how insufferable you were,\" they say. \"I kept copies. Obviously.\"\n> left: Give them yours\n  rel tamsin +medium\n  mood +small\n  flag tamsin_friends\n  say: You hand over the box you've kept under your bed for four years: every letter they sent, every insult, every postscript. They read the first one on the barge steps and laugh, and the barge leaves with them still laughing.\n> right: \"Write to me. Same as ever.\"\n  rel tamsin +small\n  flag tamsin_friends\n  say: \"Every week,\" they promise. \"Insufferably.\" The barge pulls away. They're still waving when it rounds the bend, and so are you.\n\n# ---------------- Ties ----------------\n\n@event intern_thornwick_tamsin\ntitle: A Familiar Hand\ntrack: intern_thornwick\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: met(tamsin)\nweight: 16\nart: mask\ntags: study social\nscene: A laboratory at a rival academy, a student in Thornwick green looking up from a glowing illusion on a bench, mid-grin, at a visitor in the doorway.\ntext: At the Thornwick lab, the student at the next bench, back from a week away, looks up from a glowing illusion and grins. It's Tamsin. \"They said a Lumenhold intern had come,\" they say. \"I told them it had better be you, or I'd sabotage everything.\"\n> left: Work beside them from now on\n  wage\n  perform intern_thornwick +tiny\n  rel tamsin +medium\n  say: The rest of the summer at the next bench. They show you every trick in Thornwick's book, and you show them three Lumenhold ones they swear aren't legal. Your supervisor calls you \"the inseparables\", not kindly.\n> right: Keep it professional\n  wage\n  perform intern_thornwick +medium\n  rel tamsin -tiny\n  say: You work at your own bench and keep your mind on the work. The lab notices; Tamsin notices more. \"Fine,\" they say at the end. \"You were right. But I missed you, at the next bench.\"\n\n@event lyra_on_thornwick\ntitle: Lyra on Thornwick\nactor: lyra\ntrigger: social\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: seen(rival_contest) > 0 and not off_campus and around(lyra)\nweight: 12\nart: duel\ntags: social rival\nscene: A proud red-haired student sitting on a bench by the duelling hall, a newspaper folded to the contest results across her knee.\ntext: Lyra has the Ledger folded to the contest results. \"Thornwick,\" she says, as if it were a mild disease. {met(tamsin): \"And you write letters to one of them. Every week. I've seen the green seals.\"|\"I hope you enjoyed humiliating them. Or being humiliated. I was too angry to watch closely.\"}\n> left: Defend Thornwick\n  rel lyra -small\n  charm +tiny\n  say: You tell her Thornwick's people are people. She listens with enormous scepticism. A month later she asks, very casually, whether {met(tamsin): your Thornwick friend|any of Thornwick's duellists} would ever want a practice duel. \"For research,\" she says.\n> right: Agree they're insufferable\n  rel lyra +small\n  say: \"Insufferable,\" you agree. She looks almost happy. \"Exactly,\" she says. \"Thank you.\" {met(tamsin): You don't tell Tamsin, who would agree with her.|You keep to yourself that the Thornwick illusionist was rather good.}\n"},{"path":"content/events/chain_timeloop.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  The time loop (playtest 2, X8 with L21): the cards each loop\n#  can lead to. The start, the hub and the fix are in\n#  expansion_life.grim (life_timeloop_1/2/3). Every card here ends\n#  by looping again (loop_n += 1), and the ninth loop breaks it.\n#  Nothing done in an experiment lasts; what you exploit does,\n#  and costs more each time (loop_exploit).\n# ============================================================\n\n@event timeloop_exploit\ntitle: Every Bet, Every Answer\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: queued\nrepeat: 8\nart: coin\ntags: mystery money\nchain: timeloop\nscene: A student at the centre of a crowd in the courtyard collecting coins from groaning classmates, a quiz paper marked full marks in their other hand.\ntext: {loop_exploit == 0: You know which way every coin will fall, and what's on this afternoon's quiz.|{loop_exploit == 1: The bets again. Your classmates are starting to look at you oddly when you win.|The bets, the quiz, the same faces losing. Their groans have stopped being funny.}} {~The quiz is on the founders.|A fourth-year bets you can't name every tower.|Someone bets on which way the prefect will fall.}\n> left: Take everything you can\n  gold +medium\n  study +small\n  alignment -small\n  loop_exploit += 1\n  if loop_exploit >= 3:\n    mood -medium\n    health -small\n    queue timeloop_noticed now\n    say: You win it all again, and the winning has a taste like tin. The loop tightens: the morning is shorter now, the edges closer. And someone is watching you across the courtyard as if they know.\n  else:\n    mood -small\n    loop_n += 1\n    if loop_n >= 9:\n      queue timeloop_cap now\n    else:\n      queue life_timeloop_2 now\n    say: {~The coins are real when you wake, and the marks stay in the ledger. So does the hollow feeling, a little deeper each time.|You win again. The crowns carry over into the next Tuesday. So does the weight of them.|You win the bet on the prefect, who falls exactly where you knew. The coins carry over; so does the look on the prefect's face.}\n> right: Just the quiz, then stop\n  study +small\n  alignment -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  loop_n += 1\n  if loop_n >= 9:\n    queue timeloop_cap now\n  else:\n    queue life_timeloop_2 now\n  say: {~Full marks, and nothing else. It's a small cheat, and it still sits badly at supper.|You ace the quiz and leave the bets alone. A small mercy, to yourself mostly.|You take the quiz and leave the bet on the prefect alone. You don't even watch the fall.}\n\n@event timeloop_noticed\ntitle: \"You've Done This Before\"\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: queued\nactor: seraphine\nrepeat: 2\nart: eye\ntags: mystery social\nchain: timeloop\nscene: A seer catching a student's sleeve in a crowded corridor, her eyes very clear, the other students blurred around them as if moving too fast.\ntext: Seraphine catches your sleeve in the corridor. Her eyes are very clear. \"You've done this before,\" she says. \"Today. Many times. I can see the creases in it.\" She doesn't let go. \"Whatever you're taking from it, it's taking from you too.\"\n> left: Tell her everything\n  meet seraphine\n  rel seraphine +medium\n  loop_clues += 1\n  loop_n += 1\n  if loop_n >= 9:\n    queue timeloop_cap now\n  else:\n    queue life_timeloop_2 now\n  say: You tell her, all of it, in a window seat. She listens and then says, \"Ask the clocks in the astronomy tower.\" Tomorrow she won't remember. Today, she holds your hand like she will.\n> right: Pull away\n  meet seraphine\n  mood -small\n  loop_n += 1\n  if loop_n >= 9:\n    queue timeloop_cap now\n  else:\n    queue life_timeloop_2 now\n  say: You pull free and walk fast. She doesn't follow. \"I'll forget this,\" she calls after you. \"You won't.\" She's right, and that's the worst of it.\n\n@event timeloop_dare_confess\ntitle: Things You'd Never Say\nclass: story\ntone: warm\ntrigger: queued\nrepeat: 3\nart: heart\ntags: mystery romance\nchain: timeloop\nscene: Two students in an empty corridor at dusk, one mid-sentence with their heart on their sleeve, the other's face caught between surprise and joy.\ntext: {Nothing today will last. So you find the person you've never told, and you tell them.|Another Tuesday, another confession nobody will remember.|You've confessed to them three times now, three different ways.} {They look at you as if you've handed them something breakable.|They laugh, kindly, then don't.|They take a long time to answer.}\n> left: Say it all, properly\n  mood +small\n  loop_n += 1\n  if loop_n >= 9:\n    queue timeloop_cap now\n  else:\n    queue life_timeloop_2 now\n  say: {You say all of it. They say something back that you'll keep for the rest of your life, and they'll have forgotten by breakfast. It's the most bittersweet thing you've ever done.|They kiss you, or hug you, or cry; it's different each time. By morning it never happened. Only you know.|You say it a fourth way. They take a long time to answer, and when they do it's the best answer yet. Gone by morning.}\n> right: Say it badly, on purpose\n  mood +tiny\n  charm +tiny\n  loop_n += 1\n  if loop_n >= 9:\n    queue timeloop_cap now\n  else:\n    queue life_timeloop_2 now\n  say: {You make a terrible speech, as a rehearsal. They're baffled. You learn exactly which words you'd use if this were real, and file them away.|You stumble through it, and practise, and stumble less. One Tuesday you'll say it on a Wednesday.|You say it badly on purpose, and they take a long time to answer anyway, as if they heard what you meant.}\n\n@event timeloop_dare_fall\ntitle: Off the Observatory Roof\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: queued\nrepeat: 3\nart: feather\ntags: mystery\nchain: timeloop\nscene: A student standing on the observatory parapet with arms wide, the lake far below, the morning sky enormous.\ntext: {If nothing lasts, then nothing can hurt for long. You climb the observatory parapet with the whole lake below you.|The parapet again. You know exactly how the wind feels at the top.|You've done this before. You still can't quite believe you do it.} {The lake is very blue.|Gulls wheel below you.|The bell in the tower is about to ring.}\n> left: Jump\n  mood +small\n  health -tiny\n  loop_n += 1\n  if loop_n >= 9:\n    queue timeloop_cap now\n  else:\n    queue life_timeloop_2 now\n  say: {You fall for a long, bright moment, and the lake rushes up, and you wake in your bed to the smell of eggs. Your heart is still pounding. It was glorious, and you'll never do it again. You'll do it again.|You hit the water like a bell. Then: eggs, sunlight, your own bed. You laugh out loud at breakfast and the prefect stares.|You jump as the bell rings, and fall in time with it. Eggs, sunlight, bed, and the bell still in your ears.}\n> right: Just look\n  mood +tiny\n  lore +tiny\n  loop_n += 1\n  if loop_n >= 9:\n    queue timeloop_cap now\n  else:\n    queue life_timeloop_2 now\n  say: {You sit on the parapet and watch the whole valley wake, the same as yesterday, the same as tomorrow. From up here the loop looks almost peaceful.|You don't jump. You count every roof in the village instead, and find one more than there should be.|The bell rings while you're sitting on the edge. You count the strokes, the same as yesterday, and climb down.}\n\n@event timeloop_dare_kindness\ntitle: One Perfect Day for a Stranger\nclass: story\ntone: warm\ntrigger: queued\nrepeat: 3\nart: sun\ntags: mystery social\nchain: timeloop\nscene: A shy first-year in the refectory looking astonished at a slice of cake, a note and a flower appearing one after another in front of them.\ntext: {There's a shy first-year who eats alone every Tuesday. Today you know everything that will go wrong for them, and you can stop all of it.|The same first-year, the same table. You know their day by heart now.|{flag(loop_perfect_day): You've made this stranger's Tuesday perfect before. You do it again anyway.|You've sat with this stranger before. Today you could do more.}} {They get the juice in the face at ten.|They fail the quiz by one mark.|Someone laughs at their hat.}\n> left: Make it perfect\n  flag loop_perfect_day\n  mood +small\n  gold -tiny\n  loop_n += 1\n  if loop_n >= 9:\n    queue timeloop_cap now\n  else:\n    queue life_timeloop_2 now\n  say: {You move the juice, whisper the quiz answer, compliment the hat and buy them a slice of cake at tea. By evening they're laughing with three new friends. Tomorrow none of it will have happened, except in you.|A perfect day, built carefully, down to the cake you paid for, gone by morning. You don't mind as much as you thought you would.|You get to the hat before the laughter does, and by lunch it's the fashion. By evening three people are wearing one. None of them will remember.}\n> right: Just sit with them\n  mood +tiny\n  charm +tiny\n  loop_n += 1\n  if loop_n >= 9:\n    queue timeloop_cap now\n  else:\n    queue life_timeloop_2 now\n  say: {You sit with them at lunch and talk about nothing. They smile once. That one, somehow, you're sure they keep.|An hour of company for someone lonely. It doesn't last. It isn't nothing.|Someone laughs at their hat, and you laugh too, kindly, with them. They decide to keep it on.}\n\n@event timeloop_clue_bells\ntitle: The Bells Are Wrong\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: queued\nrepeat: 2\nart: bell\ntags: mystery study\nchain: timeloop\nscene: A student counting chimes in a castle stairwell, one hand raised, the great clock face above them stopped between two hours.\ntext: You spend this Tuesday listening. Every bell in the castle strikes on time, except the astronomy tower's, which strikes a beat late, and then a beat early, as if it's arguing with itself.\n> left: Follow the sound up the tower\n  lore +small\n  loop_clues += 1\n  health -tiny\n  loop_n += 1\n  if loop_n >= 9:\n    queue timeloop_cap now\n  else:\n    queue life_timeloop_2 now\n  say: Four hundred steps. At the top the door is locked and humming. Through the keyhole, gears turning the wrong way. The morning folds before you can open it.\n> right: Ask the clockmaster\n  lore +tiny\n  loop_clues += 1\n  mood -tiny\n  loop_n += 1\n  if loop_n >= 9:\n    queue timeloop_cap now\n  else:\n    queue life_timeloop_2 now\n  say: The clockmaster swears the tower clock is perfect. \"The big chronometer up there? Not wound for fifty years.\" He looks at you oddly. \"Unless someone's been at it.\"\n\n@event timeloop_clue_light\ntitle: A Light in the Astronomy Tower\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: queued\nrepeat: 2\nart: lantern\ntags: mystery study\nchain: timeloop\nscene: A dark astronomy tower at dawn, a single lamp burning in its highest window, a student watching from a courtyard far below.\ntext: You wake before the eggs this Tuesday and look out of the window. One lamp is burning in the astronomy tower, the only light in the castle. It goes out at the exact moment the morning begins.\n> left: Be there when it goes out\n  loop_clues += 1\n  health -small\n  loop_n += 1\n  if loop_n >= 9:\n    queue timeloop_cap now\n  else:\n    queue life_timeloop_2 now\n  say: You run the stairs in the dark and reach the top as the lamp dies. Someone small is curled by a great brass clock, asleep. Then the morning folds, and you're in bed again, breathless.\n> right: Watch the window all night\n  loop_clues += 1\n  mood -small\n  loop_n += 1\n  if loop_n >= 9:\n    queue timeloop_cap now\n  else:\n    queue life_timeloop_2 now\n  say: You don't sleep. At three a shadow passes the window: someone small, with a wand. At four the lamp goes out, and so do you.\n\n@event timeloop_clue_firstyear\ntitle: The Boy Who Dreads Wednesday\nclass: story\ntone: warm\ntrigger: queued\nrepeat: 4\nart: book\ntags: mystery social\nchain: timeloop\nscene: A small first-year hunched over a textbook at breakfast, not eating, a note in the margin reading \"one more day\".\ntext: {There's a first-year who doesn't eat breakfast on Tuesdays. He reads the same page of a textbook over and over. In the margin he's written \"one more day\".|The boy again, the page again. He's in your dreams now.|You sit down opposite the boy before he can leave.} Tomorrow is his first exam.\n> left: Ask him about the exam\n  loop_clues += 1\n  charm +tiny\n  loop_n += 1\n  if loop_n >= 9:\n    queue timeloop_cap now\n  else:\n    queue life_timeloop_2 now\n  say: He's terrified of it: if he fails, he goes home. \"I wished for one more day,\" he says, and then looks frightened. \"I mean, I just wished.\" He glances at the astronomy tower without meaning to.\n> right: Help him study instead\n  loop_clues += 1\n  lore +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  loop_n += 1\n  if loop_n >= 9:\n    queue timeloop_cap now\n  else:\n    queue life_timeloop_2 now\n  say: You teach him the chapter he's been failing to read. He nearly gets it. He mentions, offhand, the big clock in the astronomy tower, \"the one that grants wishes, people say\".\n\n@event timeloop_cap\ntitle: Wednesday, Whether You Like It or Not\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: queued\nart: hourglass\ntags: mystery magic\nchain: timeloop\nscene: A shattered brass chronometer in a tower room at dawn, springs and gears scattered across the floor, a first-year and a student staring at the wreck.\ntext: The loop pulls tight one last time and snaps. Somewhere above, a great clock bursts its springs, and the morning comes in like a wave. It's Wednesday. You feel as if you've run every Tuesday at once.\n> left: Climb the tower to see\n  flag timeloop_done\n  health -small\n  mood -small\n  queue timeloop_after in 1\n  say: The astronomy tower's chronometer lies in pieces, and a first-year sits among them, white-faced. \"I just wanted one more day,\" he whispers. You sit with him until the clockmaster comes. Your bones ache for a week.\n> right: Go back to bed\n  flag timeloop_done\n  mood -medium\n  queue timeloop_after in 1\n  say: You sleep through Wednesday's breakfast, and it is the best sleep of your life. When you wake, nobody remembers anything, and you remember all of it.\n\n@event timeloop_after\ntitle: Only You Remember\nclass: story\ntone: warm\ntrigger: queued\nart: hourglass\ntags: mystery\nchain: timeloop\nscene: A student alone at a window seat on an ordinary morning, a cup of tea going cold, a clock on the wall ticking forward at last.\ntext: Weeks later, the Tuesdays still visit you in dreams. {loop_exploit >= 2: People remember your winning streak, though not why, and some of them have stopped betting with you.|Nobody else remembers anything.} You remember the eggs, the bells, the same morning over and over. You could tell someone, if you wanted.\n> left: Tell one person the truth\n  mood +small\n  if loop_exploit >= 2:\n    renown -tiny\n    say: You tell a friend everything over tea. They don't believe a word and believe all of it. At the end they ask, gently, whether you gave back the winnings. You didn't. It weighs, a little.\n  else:\n    say: You tell a friend everything over tea. They don't believe a word and believe all of it. {seen(timeloop_dare_kindness) > 0: At the end they say, \"So you were kind to a stranger, again and again, and nobody knew.\" You nod. \"Sounds like you,\" they say.|At the end they say, \"So you lived one day over and over, and nobody knew.\" You nod. \"Sounds exhausting,\" they say.}\n> right: Keep it to yourself\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You keep it. Some mornings you catch a phrase you once said in a loop, and the person you say it to looks at you as if they almost remember. They never quite do.\n"},{"path":"content/events/chains_minor.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Minor chains: short cause-and-effect stories.\n#    forbidden  — the Restricted Section key (from umbral_forbidden_notes)\n#    curse      — curing the Umbral Curse\n#    debt       — Grisk the moneylender (see state in_debt)\n#    familiar   — Ember the fox-spirit\n#    ghost      — the haunted dormitory\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- The Restricted Section ----------------\n\n@event forbidden_section_1\ntitle: The Silver Key\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: queued\nslot: night\ndefer: off_campus\nart: key\ntags: mystery\nchain: forbidden\nscene: A heavy iron door at the end of a library aisle, a silver key turning in its lock, a candle held low.\ntext: Late at night. The silver key fits the Restricted Section door perfectly. Beyond it, shelves of black-bound books breathe softly in the dark. Somewhere, a prefect's footsteps.\n> left: Turn back\n  mood -tiny\n  flag forbidden_abandoned\n  say: You lock the door again and hang the key on a nail in your wardrobe. Some nights you hear it rattling.\n> right: Slip inside\n  challenge deceive apprentice+:\n    win:\n      lore +medium\n      queue forbidden_section_2 in 2\n      say: You spend two hours reading by candle-stub. You leave with a head full of things no student should know.\n    lose:\n      gain detention\n      gain watched\n      rel bram -medium\n      say: Bram Hollis is waiting by the door when you come out. He is smiling. It's the worst smile you've ever seen.\n\n@event forbidden_section_2\ntitle: The Codex of Hollow Names\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: queued\nslot: night\ndefer: off_campus\nart: book\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nchain: forbidden\nscene: A book bound in something that is not leather, open on a reading stand, its letters slowly rearranging themselves.\ntext: You keep going back. One night, a book finds you. It falls from a high shelf and lands open at your feet: the Codex of Hollow Names. The first page is a list of names. The last one is still being written. It's yours.\n> left: Put it back\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You shelve it. When you look back, it has fallen again, open at the same page.\n> right: Read it\n  lore +medium\n  arcana +medium\n  alignment -tiny\n  find codex_names\n  queue forbidden_section_3 in 2\n  chance even:\n    gain umbral_curse\n    say: You read until dawn. You learn about the thing under the lake. When you finally close the book, your fingertips are grey.\n  else:\n    say: You read until dawn and close the book with shaking hands. Some things you can't unread.\n\n@event forbidden_section_3\ntitle: Caught\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: queued\nslot: night\ndefer: off_campus\nactor: isolde\nart: lock\ntags: mystery\nchain: forbidden\nscene: A tall silhouette in the doorway of the Restricted Section, holding a lantern that casts long shadows over a startled student.\ntext: A lantern blazes in the doorway. Not a prefect this time: the headmistress herself. She looks at the book in your hands, then at you, for a very long time. \"Most students who find that book,\" she says, \"don't put it down.\"\n> left: Apologise and accept punishment\n  alignment +small\n  meet isolde\n  study -medium\n  gain detention\n  rel isolde +small\n  unflag forbidden_key\n  say: She takes the key. \"Detention, every evening this month; your coursework can wait. And come to my tower on Thursdays. If you're going to learn dangerous things, you'll learn them properly.\"\n> right: Ask her what it means\n  meet isolde\n  rel isolde +medium\n  lore +medium\n  find codex_names\n  unflag forbidden_key\n  say: A long pause. \"It means something is waking, and it is looking for help. Make sure it doesn't find yours.\" She takes the key, leaves you the candle, and forgets, or chooses to forget, the detention.\n\n# ---------------- The Umbral Curse ----------------\n\n@event curse_cure_1\ntitle: A Cold That Won't Leave\nclass: story\ntone: grim\nwhen: has(umbral_curse) and not flag(moonpetal_sought)\nrepeat: 6\nweight: 30\nart: skull\ntags: crisis mystery\nchain: curse\nscene: A student wrapped in three blankets beside a roaring fire, breath still fogging the air, fingertips grey.\ntext: {&The cold under your skin is getting worse.|The cold is back, worse than before.} Sister Amaranth's tonics do nothing. She looks troubled. \"This isn't an illness. It's a hunger.\" She lowers her voice. \"Old Maud might know something. Don't tell anyone I said so.\"\n> left: Endure it\n  mood -tiny\n  arcana +tiny\n  say: You pile on blankets and keep going. The cold whispers sometimes. You pretend not to hear. Maud, you suppose, will still be there if it gets worse.\n> right: Go to Old Maud\n  meet maud\n  rel maud +small\n  flag moonpetal_sought\n  find lead_moonpetal\n  say: Maud takes one look at your fingertips and puts the kettle on. \"Moonpetal,\" she says. \"Grows in the Whisperwood. Only blooms after dark. You'll fetch it yourself — the cure has to come from the cursed.\"\n\n@event curse_cure_2\ntitle: Moonpetal\nclass: story\ntone: tense\nplace: whisperwood\nslot: evening\nwhen: has(umbral_curse) and flag(moonpetal_sought)\nrepeat: 6\nweight: 60\nart: flower\ntags: nature danger\nchain: curse\nscene: A dark forest at nightfall, a single silver flower glowing in a clearing, eyes watching from between the trees.\ntext: The Whisperwood after dark. Things move between the trees. In a clearing, a single silver flower is opening, and between you and it stands a stag made of shadow.\n> left: Sneak around it\n  challenge deceive journeyman:\n    win:\n      lose umbral_curse\n      rel maud +medium\n      say: You creep through the bracken and pluck the moonpetal. Maud's brew tastes like moonlight and burnt sugar. The cold is gone.\n    lose:\n      health -large\n      say: The stag sees you. You run. No moonpetal tonight; you'll have to come back.\n> right: Face the stag\n  challenge combat adept-:\n    win:\n      lose umbral_curse\n      vigor +small\n      rel maud +medium\n      say: You stand your ground. The stag lowers its head — and steps aside. Maud's brew drives the cold out of your bones.\n    lose:\n      health -large\n      say: The stag charges. You wake up in the bracken at dawn. Maud bandages you. \"Another night,\" she says.\n\n# ---------------- Grisk the Moneylender ----------------\n\n@event grisk_offer\ntitle: A Helpful Goblin\nclass: story\ntone: tense\nwhen: gold < 15 and turn >= 3 and not has(in_debt) and not flag(grisk_done)\nweight: 30\nart: pouch\ntags: money danger\nchain: debt\nscene: A small goblin in a velvet waistcoat and pince-nez, sitting on a crate in an alley, a ledger in one hand and a quill behind his ear.\ntext: A goblin in a velvet waistcoat falls into step beside you. \"Grisk,\" he says, \"friend to the temporarily embarrassed. Sixty crowns, today. Twelve a month for six months. Very reasonable.\"\n> left: No thank you\n  meet grisk\n  mood -tiny\n  say: \"My door is always open,\" Grisk says. You suspect it is actually always locked.\n> right: Take the loan\n  meet grisk\n  gold +huge\n  gain in_debt\n  debt_paid = 0\n  debt_missed = 0\n  flag grisk_done\n  say: He counts out sixty crowns, writes your name in the ledger and smiles with a great many teeth.\n\n@event grisk_board_debt\ntitle: Settled, With Interest\nclass: incident\ntone: tense\ntrigger: queued\npriority: 70\nart: pouch\ntags: money danger\nchain: debt\nscene: A goblin in a velvet waistcoat waiting outside the bursar's office, waving a receipt like a small flag.\ntext: The bursar's office has stopped sending reminders. Instead, Grisk is waiting outside it with a receipt. \"Two months' board, paid in full,\" he beams. \"By me. A loan, naturally, with a little extra for your pocket. Twelve a month for six months. You can sign here.\"\n> left: Sign his ledger\n  meet grisk\n  gold +large\n  gold +medium\n  gain in_debt\n  debt_paid = 0\n  debt_missed = 0\n  flag grisk_done\n  say: You sign. He has already paid the board out of it; he counts the rest into your palm and writes your name in the ledger with a flourish that has teeth in it.\n> right: Beg the bursar for time\n  meet grisk\n  flag grisk_done\n  mood -small\n  renown -tiny\n  say: You spend an afternoon on a hard bench outside the bursar's office and leave with a month's grace and the feeling of having been looked at over spectacles for a very long time.\n\n@event grisk_favour\ntitle: Grisk's Proposition\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: has(in_debt)\nweight: 14\nart: chain\ntags: money mystery\nchain: debt\nscene: The goblin moneylender leaning close in a library alcove, pushing a slip of paper across a table.\ntext: Grisk finds you in the library. \"I could cancel your debt,\" he murmurs. \"A small favour. There's a book in the archive — the Founders' Ledger. A client wants a page. Just one page.\"\n> left: Refuse\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: \"Pity.\" He makes a note in his ledger. It looks expensive.\n> right: Do the favour\n  alignment -small\n  challenge deceive apprentice+:\n    win:\n      lose in_debt\n      flag grisk_favour\n      say: You razor a page from the oldest ledger in the archive. Grisk tears up your debt. You notice the page had a black star on it.\n    lose:\n      gain detention\n      study -medium\n      say: The archivist catches you with a razor in your hand. Detention. Grisk is nowhere to be found, and the debt stands.\n\n@event grisk_smuggling\ntime: afternoon evening\ntitle: One More Job\nclass: story\ntone: tense\nwhen: flag(grisk_favour) and not has(in_debt)\nweight: 10\nart: pouch\ntags: money danger\nchain: debt\nscene: A crate of clinking bottles hidden under a tarp on a dark boathouse dock, a small goblin silhouetted against the lake.\ntext: Grisk again. \"You were so good last time. I have parcels that need carrying: collected at the boathouse at midnight, delivered to the village by day. Thirty crowns a parcel. Steady work, for steady hands.\"\n> left: You're done with him\n  unflag grisk_favour\n  flag grisk_gone\n  mood +small\n  say: \"Everyone's done with me eventually,\" Grisk says, without rancour. You never see him again.\n> right: Hear him out\n  flag grisk_contact\n  find lead_grisk_jetty\n  alignment -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: He explains the arrangement: the jetty, midnight, oilcloth, a cobbler's back door. \"Any month you like,\" he says. \"I'm very flexible.\" You walk away with the arrangement in your head, and it won't leave.\n\n# ---------------- Ember the Fox-Spirit ----------------\n\n@event fox_injured\ntitle: Smoke in the Woods\nclass: story\ntone: light\nwhen: turn >= 4 and not flag(fox_met)\nweight: 6 + flag(familiar_affinity) * 14\nart: fox\ntags: nature\nchain: familiar\nscene: A small red fox lying in the snow at the forest edge, its tail smouldering like a dying campfire, one paw caught in a rusted snare.\ntext: At the edge of the forest, a thin plume of smoke. A small fox is caught in a rusted snare, its tail smouldering like an ember. It snarls at you, then whimpers.\n> left: Leave it — it's wild\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You walk away. That night you dream of smoke.\n> right: Free it and bandage the paw\n  flag fox_met\n  health -tiny\n  alignment +tiny\n  queue fox_returns in 2\n  say: It bites you, of course. But it lets you wrap the paw. Then it limps into the trees, glancing back once with eyes like embers.\n\n@event fox_returns\ntitle: A Visitor at the Window\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: queued\nart: fox\ntags: nature\nchain: familiar\nscene: A fox with a softly glowing tail sitting on a stone windowsill at dawn, a ruby glinting at its feet.\ntext: Two months later, a fox with a glowing tail is sitting on your windowsill. Its paw has healed. At its feet: a small, dusty ruby.\n> left: Shoo it away\n  gold +medium\n  say: It leaves the ruby and vanishes. You sell the ruby. Somehow you feel you've failed a test.\n> right: Open the window\n  gold +medium\n  mood +small\n  queue fox_bond in 1\n  say: It hops in, inspects your room thoroughly and falls asleep on your pillow. Your pillow is now slightly scorched. You don't mind.\n\n@event fox_bond\ntitle: Ember\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: queued\nart: fox\ntags: nature magic\nchain: familiar\nscene: A student and a small fire-tailed fox nose to nose, a soft golden light connecting them.\ntext: The fox has decided. {term: It follows you to class|It follows you through the half-empty corridors}, sleeps on your feet and growls at Bram. Students aren't allowed unregistered familiars. Registering one costs fifteen crowns.\n> left: Hide her\n  challenge deceive apprentice:\n    win:\n      gain familiar_fox\n      say: You name her Ember. Nobody notices a fox that doesn't want to be noticed.\n    lose:\n      gain familiar_fox\n      gold -medium\n      say: A professor spots her at once. You pay what you can of the fee — and Ember is yours, officially.\n> right: Register her properly\n  gold -medium\n  gain familiar_fox\n  rel isolde +tiny\n  say: The registrar stamps a form. \"Name?\" \"Ember,\" you say. The fox looks smug.\n\n@event fox_helps\ntitle: Ember Finds Something\nclass: story\ntone: light\nwhen: has(familiar_fox)\nweight: 10\nrepeat: 8\nart: fox\ntags: nature\nchain: familiar\nscene: A fox nosing through dry leaves under a hedge and emerging with a purse in its jaws.\ntext: {~Ember drags you, by the sleeve, through three corridors and a hedge. She's found something. She's very proud of it.|Ember sits on your book until you look at her. She has found something, and she has buried it, and now she wants you to dig.|Ember comes back from the lake with her tail steaming and something in her jaws. She drops it at your feet like a gift.}\n> left: Let her keep it\n  mood +small\n  say: {~It's a sock. Ember is thrilled. You are thrilled for her.|It's a glove that smells of the stables. Ember sleeps on it for a week.|It's a pine cone, slightly charred. She guards it from Bram personally.}\n> right: See what it is\n  chance even:\n    gold +medium\n    say: A lost purse. You return it to its owner, who insists you keep the reward.\n  else:\n    lore +small\n    say: A lost page from a professor's lecture notes. Enlightening.\n\n# ---------------- The Haunted Dormitory ----------------\n\n@event ghost_socks\ntime: morning\ntitle: Rearranged Socks\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: turn >= 3 and not flag(ghost_met)\nweight: 8\nart: ghost\ntags: mystery\nchain: ghost\nscene: A dormitory drawer open in candlelight, socks arranged in a careful spiral, a faint shimmer in the air above them.\ntext: Every morning, your socks have been rearranged. By colour. Then alphabetically by colour. Last night they spelled out \"HELP\".\n> left: Ignore it\n  gain haunted\n  say: You buy new socks. The next morning they're arranged by mood.\n> right: Stay up and watch\n  flag ghost_met\n  gain haunted\n  queue ghost_reveal now\n  say: At three in the morning, the drawer slides open by itself.\n\n@event ghost_reveal\ntitle: Prudence\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: queued\nart: ghost\ntags: mystery\nchain: ghost\nscene: A translucent girl in old-fashioned school robes, hovering by a window, holding a faded letter.\ntext: A girl in old-fashioned robes, translucent and very polite. \"Prudence Ashworth, class of 1793. Terribly sorry about the socks. I never finished my letter. Could you deliver it? The village. The bakery. Please.\"\n> left: Exorcise her\n  lose haunted\n  alignment -tiny\n  arcana +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You speak the banishing rite. She fades with a small, hurt look. Your socks stay put after that. You feel a bit rotten.\n> right: Promise to deliver it\n  queue ghost_letter in 1\n  say: She claps her translucent hands. \"Oh, thank you! It's only taken two hundred years.\"\n\n@event ghost_letter\ntitle: A Letter Two Hundred Years Late\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: queued\nart: letter\ntags: mystery social\nscene: A cosy village bakery, an old baker reading a yellowed letter with tears on her cheeks, a ghostly figure smiling in the doorway.\ntext: The bakery has been in the same family for two hundred years. The old baker reads Prudence's letter at the counter. \"My great-great-great-grandmother waited for this,\" she says. \"She never stopped.\"\n> left: Stay and listen to the story\n  lose haunted\n  mood +large\n  lore +small\n  say: Prudence was in love with the baker's daughter. When you return, the ghost is waiting, glowing like a sunrise. Then she's gone, at peace.\n> right: Leave quietly\n  lose haunted\n  mood +medium\n  renown +small\n  say: The baker presses a free loaf into your hands. That night, your socks are arranged into a heart. Then never again.\n"},{"path":"content/events/classes.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Elective events — only drawn while the matching class state\n#  is active (see states/states.grim, group: elective).\n#  Convention: weight 16 so your class shows up most months.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- Pyromancy ----------------\n\n@event pyro_candle\ntitle: Light the Candle\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: evocation\nweight: 16\nart: candle\ntags: class magic\nscene: A dark classroom, a single candle on a stool at the far end, Professor Thorne's silhouette with folded arms.\ntext: Professor Thorne places a candle at the far end of the hall. \"Light it. From there.\" The class holds its breath. Last week a boy lit his own sleeve.\n> left: A careful spark\n  challenge spell standard steady:\n    win:\n      arcana +tiny\n      rel thorne +small\n      say: The wick catches with a polite little flame. Thorne grunts. From him, that is applause.\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      say: Nothing. Then a wisp of smoke from your fingertips. A few people giggle.\n> right: Show them real fire\n  challenge spell standard+2:\n    win:\n      arcana +small\n      renown +small\n      rel thorne +tiny\n      say: The candle erupts in a pillar of white flame and then settles, obedient. The room goes quiet.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      study evocation -small\n      rel thorne -tiny\n      say: The candle lights. So do the curtains. And, briefly, your eyebrows.\n\n@event pyro_ember_heart\ntitle: The Ember Within\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: evocation\nweight: 16\nart: flame\ntags: class magic\nscene: Students sitting cross-legged in a stone circle, each with a faint glow behind their ribs.\ntext: Today's exercise: hold a spark inside your chest and let it grow. \"Fire is fed by will,\" says Thorne. \"And by you, if you are careless.\" It burns. It is supposed to burn.\n> left: Stop when it hurts\n  health -tiny\n  arcana +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You let the spark gutter out. Thorne says nothing. The silence is a grade.\n> right: Hold it longer than anyone\n  arcana +medium\n  health -medium\n  say: Your vision goes white at the edges. When you open your eyes, your breath is steaming and the stones around you are warm.\n\n@event pyro_lyra_spar\ntitle: A Challenge in Class\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: met(lyra)\ntrack: evocation\nactor: lyra\nweight: 14\nart: flame\ntags: class rival\nscene: Two students facing each other across a scorched practice ring; one is a tall girl with crimson hair, flames curling around her wrists.\ntext: \"Professor, I'd like a demonstration partner.\" Lyra Vaneshade points her wand at you without looking. \"That one.\"\n> left: Politely decline\n  rel lyra -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: \"As expected,\" Lyra says, and turns away. It stings worse than a burn would.\n> right: Step into the ring\n  challenge combat vs lyra.arcana:\n    win:\n      rel lyra +medium\n      renown +small\n      if bond(lyra) == \"none\":\n        bond lyra rival\n      say: Your fire meets hers and holds. When Thorne calls time, Lyra is breathing hard. \"Again. Next week,\" she says.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      rel lyra +small\n      say: She wins in under a minute — but she looks at you afterwards like she's filing you away for later.\n\n# ---------------- Tidecalling ----------------\n\n@event tide_lake_lesson\ntitle: The Lake Answers\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrack: evocation\nweight: 16\nart: wave\ntags: class magic nature\nscene: Students standing waist-deep in a silver lake at dawn, ripples spiralling out from their outstretched hands.\ntext: Waist-deep in the freezing lake at dawn, Professor Marisol tells you to ask the water to rise. \"Ask,\" she says. \"Never demand. The lake remembers rudeness.\"\n> left: Command it anyway\n  challenge spell apprentice+ unlikely:\n    win:\n      arcana +small\n      say: The water rises in a shivering column. Marisol raises an eyebrow. \"Effective. Rude.\"\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      say: The lake declines, and expresses its displeasure by knocking you flat.\n> right: Listen first\n  lore +small\n  mood +small\n  say: You stand still until the cold stops hurting. The lake murmurs — and for a moment you could swear it said your name.\n  chance unlikely:\n    flag lake_knows\n\n@event tide_storm_drill\ntitle: Storm Drill\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: evocation\nweight: 16\nart: rain\ntags: class nature\nscene: A rocky shore under black clouds, a few figures in soaked robes holding back a curtain of rain with raised hands.\ntext: A storm rolls over the lake and Marisol is delighted. \"Perfect conditions! Hold back the rain for as long as you can.\" Most of the class has already fled.\n> left: Shelter with the sensible ones\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You watch from the boathouse, dry and slightly ashamed.\n> right: Stay out in it\n  vigor +small\n  arcana +tiny\n  chance unlikely -1 if health >= 60:\n    gain sick\n    say: You hold the rain back for almost a minute. You also catch Lake Fever.\n  else:\n    say: For a full minute, the rain parts around you. Marisol whoops like a child.\n\n@event tide_drowned_bell\ntitle: The Drowned Bell\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: not flag(bell_fragment) and seen(hs_lake_breathes)\ntrack: evocation\nweight: 12\nart: bell\ntags: class mystery hollowstar\nscene: Murky green water, a shaft of light catching the bronze curve of an enormous bell half-buried in silt.\ntext: Ever since the lake held its breath in Mistveil, you listen for it. During a breathing-charm lesson you hear it: a bell, deep under the lake, tolling slow and soft. Nobody else seems to notice.\n> left: Mention it to Professor Marisol\n  lore +tiny\n  say: Her face goes very still. \"There is no bell,\" she says. \"There hasn't been a bell for three hundred years.\"\n  flag heard_bell\n  find lead_lake_breathed\n> right: Dive down to find it\n  challenge feat adept- wild:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      flag bell_fragment\n      find lead_lake_breathed\n      say: Deep in the silt you find a broken piece of bronze, warm to the touch, engraved with a black star.\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      say: The water closes over you, dark and cold. You surface gasping, empty-handed, with the bell still ringing in your teeth.\n\n# ---------------- Illusion ----------------\n\n@event illusion_mirror_self\ntitle: A Second You\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: illusion\nweight: 16\nart: mirror\ntags: class magic\nscene: A student facing a shimmering duplicate of themselves; the duplicate's smile is a little too wide.\ntext: Assignment: conjure a convincing illusion of yourself. Professor Vey circles the room. \"Most students make themselves taller. Interesting, what people choose.\"\n> left: Make it flattering\n  charm +small\n  mood +small\n  say: Your double is radiant, confident, slightly better-looking. Vey writes something down and smiles thinly.\n> right: Make it accurate\n  guile +tiny\n  lore +tiny\n  say: Your double slouches the way you slouch and chews its lip the way you do. It is deeply unsettling. Vey awards top marks.\n  study illusion +tiny\n\n@event illusion_professor_prank\ntitle: The Professor's Face\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: illusion\nweight: 14\nart: mask\ntags: class social\nscene: A student wearing a shimmering glamour of a stern old professor, classmates stifling laughter behind a door.\ntext: Your classmates dare you: wear a glamour of Professor Thorne and cancel tomorrow's Arcana lecture. \"Nobody will know,\" says someone who will definitely tell.\n> left: Absolutely not\n  study illusion +tiny\n  say: Wise. Someone else tries it the next day and spends a week scrubbing gargoyles.\n> right: Do it\n  alignment -tiny\n  challenge deceive using charm journeyman+ wild:\n    win:\n      renown +medium\n      mood +small\n      say: \"Class is cancelled,\" you announce in Thorne's gravel voice. The cheering is audible from three towers away.\n    lose:\n      gain detention\n      study illusion -small\n      rel thorne -small\n      say: Halfway through, the glamour flickers. The real Thorne is standing in the doorway. He is not laughing.\n\n@event illusion_dragon\ntitle: The Courtyard Dragon\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: arcana >= 12\ntrack: illusion\nweight: 12\nart: dragon\ntags: class magic\nscene: A vast translucent dragon coiled around the academy's central fountain, students frozen mid-step beneath it.\ntext: For your term project you must create an illusion that fools a crowd. You could make something small and flawless. Or you could put a dragon in the courtyard at lunchtime.\n> left: Small and flawless\n  study illusion +medium\n  say: A single butterfly that nobody can tell is fake. Vey taps it with her wand and it bursts into light. \"Perfect.\"\n> right: Dragon. Obviously.\n  challenge spell using charm standard+1 wild:\n    win:\n      renown +medium\n      study illusion +small\n      say: The dragon uncurls around the fountain and yawns. Forty students scream. Vey laughs until she cries.\n    lose:\n      renown +small\n      study illusion -small\n      rel isolde -tiny\n      say: The dragon is magnificent for four seconds and then sneezes into a cloud of glitter. The headmistress was eating lunch below it.\n\n# ---------------- Practical Alchemy ----------------\n\n@event alch_first_brew\ntitle: Draught of Mild Courage\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: alchemy\nweight: 16\nart: cauldron\ntags: class\nscene: Rows of bubbling cauldrons in a low stone room, each giving off a different coloured steam.\ntext: The recipe is on the board: Draught of Mild Courage. Seven ingredients, three stirs clockwise, one counter-clockwise, and never — never — add the newt eye early.\n> left: Follow the recipe exactly\n  alchemy +tiny\n  study alchemy +tiny\n  say: A clear, honey-coloured brew. The professor sniffs it and nods. Safe, solid, unremarkable.\n> right: Improvise a little\n  challenge brew standard wild:\n    win:\n      alchemy +medium\n      gold +small\n      say: Your variant tastes like cinnamon and makes people brave enough to ask for things. A fourth-year buys the whole flask.\n    lose:\n      gain frog_voice\n      say: You taste-test. Mistake. For the next month, everything you say comes out as a deep, wet croak.\n\n@event alch_unstable_cauldron\ntitle: Unstable Cauldron\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrack: alchemy\nweight: 14\nart: potion\ntags: class danger\nscene: A cauldron shuddering on its iron legs, violet bubbles swelling over the rim, a student frozen beside it.\ntext: The cauldron next to yours starts to shudder. Its owner, a terrified first-year, has added the newt eye early. It is going to blow.\n> left: Dive for cover\n  say: The explosion paints the ceiling violet. Nobody is hurt. Everybody is purple.\n> right: Try to neutralise it\n  challenge brew standard+1:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      alchemy +small\n      say: A pinch of salt, a drop of moonwater, a quick counter-stir. The bubbling dies. The first-year bursts into grateful tears.\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      say: The cauldron goes off in your face. You spend a week with violet eyebrows and a ringing in your ears.\n\n@event alch_corvin_market\ntitle: A Side Business\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: met(corvin)\ntrack: alchemy\nactor: corvin\nweight: 12\nart: bottle\ntags: class money\nscene: A young man leaning on a cauldron table, holding up a vial against the light with a merchant's grin.\ntext: Corvin turns your leftover potions over in his hands. \"These would sell for real crowns in the village. I know a buyer. I take a third. Nobody needs to know they came from a classroom.\"\n> left: Decline\n  rel corvin -tiny\n  say: \"Your loss, saint.\" He tosses the vial back. You fumble it.\n> right: Shake on it\n  alignment -tiny\n  gold +large\n  rel corvin +small\n  chance unlikely:\n    gain detention\n    study alchemy -small\n    say: It goes well until a vial turns up in a professor's tea. You and Corvin share a detention and an alibi.\n  else:\n    say: Thirty crowns, no questions asked. Corvin winks. \"Pleasure doing crime with you.\"\n\n# ---------------- Divination ----------------\n\n@event div_tea_leaves\ntitle: Reading the Leaves\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\ntrack: astromancy\nweight: 16\nart: cup\ntags: class mystery\nscene: A delicate teacup tipped on its saucer, the dregs forming a shape like a skull — or possibly a mushroom.\ntext: You read your partner's tea leaves. The shape at the bottom of the cup is unmistakably a skull. Your partner is looking at you hopefully.\n> left: Tell the truth\n  lore +small\n  chance even:\n    say: \"A skull?\" They laugh. \"Professor says a skull means change.\" They're relieved. You aren't sure you should be.\n  else:\n    mood -tiny\n    say: They go pale and leave early. The next day they trip down the stairs. Nothing serious. Still.\n> right: Lie kindly\n  mood -tiny\n  charm +small\n  say: \"A mushroom,\" you say. \"Means good fortune.\" They beam. The professor, passing behind you, says nothing at all.\n\n@event div_next_tuesday\ntitle: The Mirror of Next Tuesday\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\ntrack: astromancy\nweight: 14\nart: mirror\ntags: class mystery\nscene: An ornate standing mirror whose reflection shows the room on a different day — the light at a different angle, a different person sitting in your chair.\ntext: The Mirror of Next Tuesday shows exactly what it promises. Students take turns looking. Some come away smiling. One comes away and goes straight to the infirmary.\n> left: Take a look\n  chance even:\n    study astromancy +medium\n    alignment -tiny\n    say: Next Tuesday you are sitting an Astromancy quiz. You memorise the questions. Is that cheating? It's at least ambiguous.\n  else:\n    mood -medium\n    say: Next Tuesday you are crying in the rain outside the observatory. You don't know why. You spend the week dreading it.\n> right: Decline\n  mood +tiny\n  say: \"I'd rather be surprised.\" The professor smiles as if you have passed a different test entirely.\n\n@event div_seraphine_vision\ntitle: She Grabs Your Hand\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrack: astromancy\nactor: seraphine\nweight: 14\nart: eye\ntags: class mystery hollowstar\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: A dreamy-eyed girl gripping a classmate's wrist across a table of crystal balls, her pupils wide and silver.\ntext: In the middle of crystal-gazing, Seraphine Hale seizes your wrist. Her eyes have gone silver. \"The door under the lake,\" she whispers. \"Don't let him open it. Or do. I can't see which.\"\n> left: Pull away\n  meet seraphine\n  rel seraphine -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: She blinks and lets go. \"Sorry. That happens.\" She goes back to her crystal as if nothing occurred.\n> right: Ask who she means\n  meet seraphine\n  rel seraphine +medium\n  lore +tiny\n  flag heard_prophecy\n  say: \"Someone cold,\" she says. \"Someone kind.\" Then she shakes her head, smiles vaguely, and asks if you'd like a biscuit.\n\n# ---------------- Artifice ----------------\n\n@event art_clockwork_beetle\ntitle: The Clockwork Beetle\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: artifice\nweight: 16\nart: gear\ntags: class\nscene: A brass beetle the size of a fist on a workbench, tiny gears visible through its translucent wing-cases.\ntext: Your project: a brass beetle that walks. The gears are in. The spring is wound. You could make it efficient, or you could give it a little personality.\n> left: Make it efficient\n  study artifice +small\n  lore +tiny\n  say: It walks in a perfect straight line until it hits a wall. Full marks.\n> right: Give it personality\n  mood +small\n  chance unlikely:\n    renown +small\n    say: It escapes during the presentation and lives in the walls for a month, occasionally stealing biscuits. It becomes a minor legend.\n  else:\n    say: It refuses to walk unless you compliment it. The professor is torn between marking you down and asking how you did it.\n\n@event art_mir_collab\ntitle: A Joint Project\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: artifice\nactor: mir\nweight: 14\nart: gear\ntags: class social\nscene: A cluttered workbench covered in tools, sketches and one smoking teapot; a grinning student with goggles pushed up into wild hair.\ntext: Mir Ten drops onto the bench beside you with a sketch of something that is either a self-stirring cauldron or a small war machine. \"Partners? Your hands, my brain. Or the other way round. Both, actually.\"\n> left: Work alone\n  meet mir\n  study artifice +tiny\n  say: \"Fair,\" says Mir, with no hard feelings, and is immediately on fire. Not badly.\n> right: Partner with Mir\n  meet mir\n  challenge brew standard wild:\n    win:\n      study artifice +medium\n      rel mir +medium\n      say: The cauldron stirs itself, hums a tune and only threatens the professor once. Mir hugs you with oily hands.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      rel mir +small\n      say: The prototype explodes. Mir laughs so hard they cry. \"Again! We were so close!\" You find you're laughing too.\n\n@event art_enchanted_quill\ntitle: The Self-Writing Quill\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: artifice\nweight: 14\nart: feather\ntags: class\nscene: A quill writing busily on its own over a sheaf of paper while its owner sleeps on folded arms.\ntext: You've enchanted a quill to take dictation. It works beautifully. A thought occurs: what if it wrote your essays, too?\n> left: Just use it for notes\n  lore +small\n  say: Your notes are flawless. Even the professor's jokes are transcribed, with a small marginal comment: \"(not funny).\"\n> right: Let it write your essays\n  alignment -tiny\n  chance even:\n    study artifice +medium\n    mood +small\n    say: Your essays arrive on time, in impeccable penmanship. A little too impeccable. Nobody notices. Yet.\n  else:\n    study artifice -medium\n    gain detention\n    say: The quill develops opinions and writes a scathing review of the professor. It signs your name.\n\n# ---------------- Beastcraft ----------------\n\n@event beast_griffon_chick\ntitle: Hatching Day\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: beastcraft\nweight: 16\nart: feather\ntags: class nature\nscene: A straw nest under a heat lamp, a cracked golden egg, and a damp fluffy griffon chick blinking at the world.\ntext: The griffon egg is cracking. Professor Brask whispers that the first face a chick sees, it trusts forever — or attacks forever. Hard to predict.\n> left: Hang back\n  lore +tiny\n  say: The chick sees Brask first and bites her. She seems delighted.\n> right: Offer your hand\n  challenge feat apprentice+ luck:\n    win:\n      mood +medium\n      vigor +tiny\n      say: The chick peeps, nuzzles your palm and falls asleep. You are, apparently, its mother now.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      say: It bites. Hard. Griffon beaks are sharper than they look, and they look extremely sharp.\n\n@event beast_manticore\ntitle: The Manticore Is Loose\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrack: beastcraft\nweight: 12\nart: paw\ntags: class danger\nscene: A shadowy lion-shape with a scorpion's tail prowling between the greenhouse benches, students pressed against the glass.\ntext: Someone left the manticore pen open. It's in the greenhouse, tail twitching, between you and the only exit.\n> left: Back away slowly\n  say: You edge out. Professor Brask arrives with a bucket of meat and a lullaby and the crisis is over in minutes.\n> right: Try to calm it\n  challenge negotiate adept wild:\n    win:\n      renown +medium\n      vigor +tiny\n      say: You hum the lullaby Brask taught you. The manticore yawns, sits, and lets you scratch behind its ear. The whole class saw.\n    lose:\n      health -large\n      gain injured\n      say: The manticore does not like your humming. The tail is quicker than you are.\n\n@event beast_familiar_call\ntitle: Calling a Familiar\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: not has(familiar_fox)\ntrack: beastcraft\nweight: 12\nart: fox\ntags: class magic\nscene: A circle of salt and candles in a forest clearing at dusk, shapes of small animals watching from the edge of the trees.\ntext: Brask teaches the summoning circle. \"It doesn't summon,\" she says. \"It invites. Most of the time, nothing comes. That's fine. Familiars choose you.\"\n> left: Just watch the others\n  lore +tiny\n  say: A first-year is chosen by a very large toad. The toad looks smug about it.\n> right: Sit in the circle\n  mood +small\n  flag familiar_affinity\n  say: Nothing comes. But for a long moment, something at the edge of the trees watches you with eyes like embers. Then it is gone.\n\n# ---------------- Astromancy ----------------\n\n@event astro_comet\ntitle: A New Comet\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: astromancy\nweight: 16\nart: star\ntags: class\nscene: An observatory dome open to the night, a long-tailed comet smeared across the sky, students crowded at the telescopes.\ntext: A comet appears that isn't in any of the charts. The observatory is in uproar. Everyone wants to be the one to name it.\n> left: Sketch it carefully\n  lore +small\n  say: Your drawing is lovely. Someone else's name goes on the comet. You keep the drawing.\n> right: Calculate its path\n  challenge research standard+1:\n    win:\n      renown +medium\n      lore +small\n      say: Your math is right. The comet is named after you, or at least after a slight misspelling of you.\n    lose:\n      study astromancy -tiny\n      say: Your calculation suggests the comet will hit the academy on Thursday. It does not. You are teased about it for weeks.\n\n@event astro_kael_roof\ntitle: Up All Night\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: met(kael)\ntrack: astromancy\nactor: kael\nweight: 14\nart: moon\ntags: class mystery hollowstar\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: A young man in a fur coat sitting alone on the observatory roof, frost on the tiles around him, staring at one spot in the stars.\ntext: On your way to the morning lecture you find Kael alone on the observatory roof. He has been there all night. Frost has spread across the tiles around him. He is staring at a patch of sky where there are no stars at all.\n> left: Leave him be\n  say: You go down to class. By evening there's a perfect circle of frost on the roof where he sat, and no Kael.\n> right: Sit beside him\n  rel kael +medium\n  lore +tiny\n  flag saw_hollow_star\n  say: \"There,\" he says, pointing at the dark. \"Can you see it? A star that eats light.\" You look for a long time. You think you can.\n\n@event astro_sleepless\ntitle: Sleepless Weeks\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: astromancy\nweight: 14\nart: bed\ntags: class\nscene: A student asleep on an open star atlas at a library desk, a guttering candle beside them, dawn light in the window.\ntext: Midnight lectures, dawn homework, morning classes. You have started seeing constellations in your porridge.\n> left: Skip a few lectures\n  study track -tiny\n  gain slacking\n  health +small\n  say: You sleep for fourteen hours and wake up feeling like a person.\n> right: Power through\n  health -small\n  lore +small\n  say: You learn the names of forty stars and forget the names of two friends. Temporarily.\n\n# ---------------- Healing Arts ----------------\n\n@event heal_first_patient\ntitle: Your First Patient\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: alchemy\nweight: 16\nart: cross\ntags: class\nscene: A pale first-year sitting on an infirmary cot holding a wrist at a strange angle, a nervous student healer kneeling beside them.\ntext: A first-year stumbles into the practice ward with a wrist that bends the wrong way. Sister Amaranth is busy. The first-year looks at you with enormous, trusting eyes.\n> left: Fetch Sister Amaranth\n  study alchemy +tiny\n  say: Amaranth fixes it in seconds and nods at you. \"Knowing your limits is the first lesson.\"\n> right: Heal it yourself\n  challenge brew standard+1:\n    win:\n      alchemy +small\n      renown +small\n      say: The bone slides back with a soft click and a glow of warm light. The first-year tells everyone you're a genius.\n    lose:\n      study alchemy -small\n      say: The wrist is now straight. The fingers, however, are pointing backwards. Amaranth sighs and fixes both.\n\n@event heal_fever_ward\ntitle: Fever in the Village\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: alchemy\nweight: 12\nart: cross\ntags: class nature\nscene: A crowded village hall lined with cots, lanterns hanging from the rafters, student healers moving between patients.\ntext: Lake Fever is spreading through the village below the academy. Sister Amaranth asks for student volunteers. It's hard, unpaid, and you might catch it.\n> left: Decline\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You watch the volunteers board the barge in the morning. You do your homework. It feels like the wrong homework.\n> right: Volunteer\n  renown +tiny\n  alchemy +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  alignment +tiny\n  chance unlikely -1 if health >= 60:\n    gain sick\n    say: You save lives, and catch the fever yourself. The village sends a basket of honey cakes to your sickbed.\n  else:\n    say: A week of long nights and cool cloths. When you leave, the baker's daughter gives you her best loaf.\n\n@event heal_tobias_sting\ntitle: Stung\nclass: windfall\ntone: light\nwhen: met(tobias)\ntrack: alchemy\nactor: tobias\nweight: 12\nart: flower\ntags: class social\nscene: A broad-shouldered boy sitting on a greenhouse bench, grimacing, holding up a hand swollen to twice its size.\ntext: Tobias wanders into the practice ward holding up a hand swollen like a pumpkin. \"Mandrake,\" he says. \"She didn't like my singing.\" He looks hopefully at you.\n> left: Send him to the infirmary\n  rel tobias +tiny\n  say: \"Right. Proper healer. Good idea.\" He goes, a little disappointed.\n> right: Treat him yourself\n  alchemy +tiny\n  rel tobias +medium\n  spark tobias +tiny\n  say: Your hands are steady. The swelling goes down. Tobias stares at his hand, then at you. \"You're really good at this,\" he says softly.\n\n# ---------------- Umbral Theory ----------------\n\n@event umbral_whisper\ntitle: The Shadow Speaks\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\ntrack: umbral\nweight: 16\nart: skull\ntags: class mystery danger\nscene: A student at a desk in a dim lecture hall, their shadow on the wall behind them turned the wrong way, leaning close to whisper.\ntext: In the middle of a lecture on the Nature of Absence, your shadow leans away from the lamp, toward your ear, and says your name.\n> left: Banish it\n  arcana +tiny\n  say: You speak the warding phrase. Your shadow snaps back into place, sulking. It does not speak again. You think.\n> right: Answer it\n  lore +medium\n  chance unlikely:\n    gain umbral_curse\n    say: It tells you something about death you can't unlearn. The cold starts the next morning.\n  else:\n    say: It tells you where a lost book is shelved in the library. It is right. You decide not to wonder how it knew.\n\n@event umbral_bone_mouse\ntitle: The Bone Mouse\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: umbral\nweight: 14\nart: skull\ntags: class magic\nscene: A tiny mouse skeleton on a silver tray, one bony paw twitching, students leaning away from it.\ntext: Practical exercise: animate the skeleton of a field mouse. \"Purely educational,\" the professor says, with a smile nobody trusts.\n> left: Refuse on principle\n  study umbral -tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You write a short essay on why instead. The professor reads it with surprising attention.\n> right: Do the exercise\n  challenge spell standard:\n    win:\n      arcana +medium\n      study umbral +small\n      say: The little skeleton stands up, washes its non-existent whiskers, and lies down again. Perfect control.\n    lose:\n      gain haunted\n      say: It gets up. It does not lie down again. It follows you back to your dormitory and sits on your pillow every night.\n\n@event umbral_forbidden_notes\ntitle: Forbidden Notes\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrack: umbral\nweight: 12\nart: book\ntags: class mystery\nchain: forbidden\nscene: A battered notebook with a cracked leather cover being slid across a library table, a silver key tucked into its spine.\ntext: A fourth-year slides a battered notebook across the table. \"My notes on the Restricted Section. I'm graduating. You look like you'd use them.\" A silver key is tucked in the spine.\n> left: Decline\n  say: The fourth-year shrugs and slides the notebook to the next student. You watch it go.\n> right: Take the notebook\n  alignment -tiny\n  lore +medium\n  flag forbidden_key\n  queue forbidden_section_1 in 1\n  say: The notes are brilliant, half-mad and annotated in three colours of ink. The key is cold in your pocket.\n"},{"path":"content/events/clubs.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Club events — drawn while the club state is active.\n#  Clubs are joined at the Club Fair (calendar.grim) except the\n#  Midnight Cartographers, who find you.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- Dueling Circle ----------------\n\n@event duel_practice_bout\ntitle: Practice Bout\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: duelling\nweight: 14\nart: duel\ntags: club\nscene: A chalk circle on a stone floor under high windows, two students mid-duel, a ring of members cheering.\ntext: Wednesday practice. The captain pairs you with a fourth-year who has a reputation for ending bouts quickly. \"Defence or attack,\" the captain says. \"Pick one and commit.\"\n> left: Hold your ground\n  challenge combat apprentice+ steady:\n    win:\n      vigor +small\n      renown +small\n      say: You weather everything she throws until she runs out of breath. The captain nods slowly.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      say: You hold your ground very bravely, right up until you're on the floor.\n> right: Go on the attack\n  challenge combat apprentice+ wild:\n    win:\n      arcana +small\n      renown +small\n      say: Your opening hex catches her off guard. Point to you. The circle whoops.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      say: She sidesteps and you hex the wall. The wall wins.\n\n@event duel_bram_hazing\ntitle: Initiation, Prefect-Style\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: rank(duelling) == 1\ntrack: duelling\nactor: bram\nweight: 12\nart: shield\ntags: club rival\nscene: A burly student in a prefect's sash blocking a corridor, wand twirling, a crowd of members watching nervously.\ntext: Bram Hollis, prefect and the Circle's loudest member, has decided new members need \"toughening up\". He's made you hold a shield-charm against him for an hour. The hour is not up.\n> left: Endure it\n  meet bram\n  vigor +small\n  mood -small\n  rel bram +small\n  say: Your arms shake. You don't drop the charm. Bram grunts, almost approvingly, and finds someone else to torment.\n> right: Drop the shield and duel him\n  meet bram\n  challenge combat vs bram.vigor:\n    win:\n      renown +medium\n      rel bram -large\n      bond bram rival\n      say: You disarm him in front of everyone. His wand clatters across the floor. The silence is delicious. He will remember this.\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      rel bram -small\n      say: Bram flattens you with a single bored flick. \"Toughening up,\" he reminds the room.\n\n@event duel_lyra_training\ntitle: Training Partners\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: met(lyra) and month == 6 and rel(lyra) >= 0\ntrack: duelling\nactor: lyra\nweight: 14\nart: duel\ntags: club rival social\nscene: A misty lawn at dawn, two figures practising wand forms side by side, frost on the grass.\ntext: Lyra finds you after practice. \"The tournament is in two months. You're the only person in this club who doesn't bore me. Train with me. Dawn. Every day. Don't be late.\"\n> left: Decline — you need sleep\n  rel lyra -tiny\n  say: \"Sleep,\" she repeats, as if it's a foreign word. \"Fine.\"\n> right: Agree\n  health -tiny\n  gain training\n  rel lyra +medium\n  say: She's a merciless coach. Somewhere around the third week, you notice she's started bringing two cups of tea.\n\n# ---------------- Choir of the Spheres ----------------\n\n@event choir_solo\ntitle: Solo Auditions\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nplace: great_hall\nweight: 14\nart: music\ntags: club\nscene: A circular choir loft under a painted dome, a single student standing in a pool of light, the rest of the choir floating gently above their seats.\ntext: Auditions for the Midwinter solo. The choirmaster's eyebrows are famously hard to impress. Your throat is dry. Your hands are wet.\n> left: Stay in the back row\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You harmonise from safety, and it's lovely. Somebody else gets the solo and cries with relief.\n> right: Audition\n  challenge negotiate apprentice+:\n    win:\n      renown +medium\n      mood +small\n      say: You hit the high note and the candles in the loft all flare at once. The choirmaster's eyebrows rise one millimetre. You got it.\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: Your voice cracks on the high note and a candle goes out in protest.\n\n@event choir_levitation\ntitle: Off-Key Altitude\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nplace: great_hall\nweight: 14\nart: music\ntags: club magic\nscene: A choir hovering a few feet off the floor, all in harmony — except one singer, who has dropped back to the ground with a thump.\ntext: The Choir of the Spheres literally floats when it harmonises. You keep falling out of the air on the third verse. People are starting to notice the thumps.\n> left: Mime the hard bits\n  guile +tiny\n  say: Nobody notices. You float serenely, mouthing words. It's a little bit like fraud.\n> right: Practise every night\n  charm +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: A week of late nights and you float through the third verse like a feather. The choirmaster gives you a tiny nod.\n\n@event choir_concert\ntitle: The Winter Concert\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: (month == 4 or month == 5)\nplace: great_hall\nweight: 16\nart: bell\ntags: club festival\nscene: The great hall at night, a floating choir lit by candles, the audience below with faces tilted up.\ntext: The winter concert. The great hall is packed. Somewhere in the audience, the Ledger's critic is sharpening her quill.\n> left: Sing for the audience\n  renown +small\n  mood +small\n  say: You sing and the hall sways. The review calls the choir \"unearthly\". You cut it out and keep it.\n> right: Sing for yourself\n  mood +medium\n  say: You close your eyes and forget anyone is listening. When you open them, you are higher than everyone else.\n\n# ---------------- Skyrace Squad ----------------\n\n@event sky_tryouts\ntitle: Tryouts\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: not flag(sky_team)\nplace: flight_field\nweight: 18\nart: broom\ntags: club\nscene: Brooms streaking around the towers, hoops of light hanging in the sky, a captain with a whistle on the battlements.\ntext: Skyrace tryouts: three laps around the towers, through the hoops, without dying. The captain has a stopwatch and no visible sense of humour.\n> left: Fly clean and steady\n  challenge feat apprentice+ steady:\n    win:\n      flag sky_team\n      find lead_sky_championship\n      renown +small\n      say: You're not the fastest, but you hit every hoop. \"Reserve,\" says the captain. Then: \"...no. Team.\"\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      say: You miss two hoops and nearly a gargoyle. \"Reserves,\" says the captain. \"Keep practising.\"\n> right: Go full speed\n  challenge feat apprentice+ wild:\n    win:\n      flag sky_team\n      find lead_sky_championship\n      renown +medium\n      say: You scream through the hoops so fast the stopwatch has to be checked. You're on the team. Obviously.\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      say: You go full speed into the north tower. The tower is fine.\n\n@event sky_crash\ntitle: A Bad Landing\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nplace: flight_field\nweight: 12\nart: broom\ntags: club danger\nscene: A broom snapped in two on the grass, a student sitting up dazed, teammates landing around them.\ntext: Practice. A gust catches you mid-turn, and the ground arrives rather sooner than expected. Your ankle is making an interesting shape.\n> left: Walk it off\n  vigor +tiny\n  chance even:\n    gain injured\n    say: You do not walk it off. You limp it off, for a month.\n  else:\n    say: Tough as old boots. The team cheers when you get back on your broom.\n> right: Straight to the infirmary\n  gold -tiny\n  health +small\n  say: Sister Amaranth sets it in a minute and charges you three crowns for \"carelessness\". Fair.\n\n@event sky_championship\ntitle: Lumenhold vs. Thornwick\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: flag(sky_team) and month >= 6\nplace: flight_field\nweight: 16\nart: trophy\ntags: club festival\nscene: Two teams of broom-riders in blue and green above a packed stadium, the golden finishing hoop glowing at the top of the highest tower.\ntext: The championship against Thornwick Academy. Final lap. You're neck and neck with their captain, and the golden hoop is at the top of the tallest tower.\n> left: Play it safe\n  renown +small\n  say: Thornwick takes the hoop by a whisker. Still: second place in the region. The team carries you off the field anyway.\n> right: The suicide dive\n  challenge feat adept- wild:\n    win:\n      renown +medium\n      gain famous\n      mood +large\n      say: You dive straight down the tower face and pull up at the last second, through the hoop. The stadium explodes.\n    lose:\n      health -large\n      gain injured\n      renown +small\n      say: You dive. You miss. The crowd gasps, then cheers you anyway when you stagger up waving.\n\n# ---------------- The Lumen Ledger ----------------\n\n@event ledger_pie_scoop\ntitle: The Case of the Missing Pies\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: lumen_ledger\nactor: nessa\nweight: 14\nart: paper\ntags: club social\nscene: A cramped newspaper office in a tower attic, a printing press clanking, a girl with an ink-stained grin pointing at a map pinned with string.\ntext: Nessa slams a file on your desk. \"Pies. Forty-two of them, gone from the kitchens in a single night. I want the culprit, I want a photo and I want it by Friday.\"\n> left: Write a light-hearted piece\n  charm +tiny\n  say: \"Pie-rates Strike Again.\" Nessa groans at the headline and prints it anyway.\n> right: Actually investigate\n  challenge deceive journeyman-:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      rel nessa +medium\n      say: The culprit: a family of brownies living in the chimney. Your story runs on the front page. Nessa frames it.\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      rel nessa +tiny\n      say: You stake out the kitchens for three nights and catch nothing but a cold. Nessa pats your head.\n\n@event ledger_bram_story\ntitle: The Prefect's Bribes\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: met(bram)\ntrack: lumen_ledger\nactor: nessa\nweight: 12\nart: scales\ntags: club rival\nscene: An anonymous note spread on a desk under a lamp, next to a sketch of a prefect taking a coin purse from a first-year.\ntext: An anonymous tip: Bram Hollis has been taking bribes from first-years to skip their demerits. You have a witness. Nessa wants your byline on it. \"This is real journalism,\" she says, eyes shining.\n> left: Kill the story\n  alignment -tiny\n  rel nessa -small\n  rel bram +small\n  say: Nessa is disappointed in you. Somehow, Bram finds out you buried it. He nods at you in the corridor. You feel slightly dirty.\n> right: Publish it\n  alignment +tiny\n  renown +medium\n  rel nessa +medium\n  rel bram -large\n  gain watched\n  if rel(bram) < -18:\n    bond bram nemesis\n  say: The story runs. Bram is stripped of his sash for a month. He gets it back. And now he knows exactly who you are.\n\n@event ledger_deadline\ntitle: Deadline Night\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: lumen_ledger\nactor: nessa\nweight: 12\nart: candle\ntags: club\nscene: The newspaper office at 3am, crumpled drafts on the floor, two students slumped over typesetting trays.\ntext: The press runs at dawn and half the paper is blank. Nessa is chewing her quill. \"I need you tonight. All night. I'll owe you.\"\n> left: Say you have revision to do\n  study +tiny\n  rel nessa -tiny\n  say: You sleep. The paper comes out with a large drawing of a duck on page four. Nessa doesn't speak to you for a day.\n> right: Pull an all-nighter\n  health -small\n  study -tiny\n  rel nessa +medium\n  say: You write three articles and a horoscope. At dawn Nessa falls asleep on your shoulder, ink on her nose.\n\n# ---------------- Verdant Circle ----------------\n\n@event garden_mandrakes\ntitle: The Mandrake Choir\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: verdant\nweight: 14\nart: leaf\ntags: club nature\nscene: Rows of potted mandrakes in a steamy greenhouse, their little leafy heads opened in song.\ntext: The greenhouse mandrakes have started singing again. Off-key and enthusiastic. The earmuffs are on the hook by the door. Tobias isn't wearing his; he's harmonising.\n> left: Earmuffs on\n  alchemy +tiny\n  say: You repot six mandrakes in blissful silence. Good honest work.\n> right: Sing along\n  mood +small\n  chance unlikely:\n    health -tiny\n    say: You sing along and promptly faint. Mandrake song is strong stuff. You wake up very relaxed.\n  else:\n    say: The mandrakes adore you. The greenhouse sounds like a pub at closing time.\n\n@event garden_moonflower\ntitle: The Moonflower\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: met(tobias)\ntrack: verdant\nactor: tobias\nweight: 12\nart: flower\ntags: club romance nature\nscene: A single silver flower opening in the dark of a glass greenhouse, two faces lit by its glow.\ntext: \"The moonflower's going to bloom tonight,\" Tobias says, a little breathless. \"Once every eleven years. I'm staying up to see it. You... could stay too. If you want.\"\n> left: You need sleep\n  rel tobias -tiny\n  say: \"Course. Yeah.\" He tells you about it at breakfast, in detail, with gestures.\n> right: Stay up with him\n  health -tiny\n  rel tobias +medium\n  spark tobias +small\n  mood +small\n  say: At 2am the flower opens and fills the greenhouse with silver light. Tobias doesn't look at the flower. He looks at you.\n\n@event garden_maud_secret\ntitle: The Secret Garden\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: member(verdant) or (month >= 7 and month <= 9)\nactor: maud\nweight: 5\nart: mushroom\ntags: club nature\nscene: An old woman in a battered straw hat holding open a hidden door in an ivy-covered wall; beyond, impossible flowers glow.\ntext: The old groundskeeper beckons you behind the potting shed, to a door in the wall you've walked past a hundred times without seeing. \"You've got the hands for it,\" Old Maud says. \"Want to see what grows where nobody's looking?\"\n> left: Thank her and decline\n  meet maud\n  rel maud +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: \"Suit yourself.\" The door is gone when you look back. You make your class with a minute to spare, oddly pleased with yourself.\n> right: Follow her in\n  meet maud\n  rel maud +medium\n  alchemy +small\n  gain blessed\n  study -tiny\n  say: Inside: glowing ferns, silver bees, a pear tree that hums. Maud tucks a sprig of rosemary into your pocket. \"For luck. Don't lose it.\" You lose the whole afternoon in there, and a class with it.\n\n# ---------------- Stargazers ----------------\n\n@event stars_cocoa_night\ntime: evening night\ntitle: Cocoa on the Roof\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nplace: observatory\nweight: 14\nart: cup\ntags: club social\nscene: Students wrapped in blankets on a flat roof, steaming mugs in hand, telescopes pointed at a velvet sky.\ntext: Stargazers meet on the roof with blankets, telescopes and an illegal quantity of cocoa. Tonight is cloudy. Nobody cares.\n> left: Talk until dawn\n  mood +medium\n  charm +tiny\n  say: You talk about home, about the future, about whether stars are lonely. Someone falls asleep on your shoulder.\n> right: Do your homework by starlight\n  lore +small\n  study +tiny\n  say: You finish your essay by the light of the cocoa-warmer charm. Productive. A little lonely.\n\n@event stars_meteor_bet\ntime: evening night\ntitle: The Meteor Wager\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nplace: observatory\nweight: 12\nart: dice\ntags: club money\nscene: A chalkboard on an observatory wall covered in tally marks and names, a pile of coins on a stool beneath it.\ntext: The club is running a betting pool on tonight's meteor count. The current favourite is 40. The charts suggest something else. Or do they?\n> left: Bet on the charts\n  when: gold >= 8\n  challenge research journeyman-:\n    win:\n      gold +medium\n      say: Fifty-three meteors. You called fifty-two. Close enough to win the pot.\n    lose:\n      gold -small\n      say: The charts were wrong, or you read them wrong. Eight crowns gone.\n> right: Stay out of it\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You watch the meteors for their own sake. Turns out that's the best seat in the house.\n\n@event stars_constellation\ntitle: An Unnamed Shape\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nplace: observatory\nweight: 12\nart: star\ntags: club mystery\nscene: A star chart spread on a table, a student's finger tracing a new pattern between unmarked stars.\ntext: Plotting the autumn sky, you notice seven faint stars that form a ring around nothing. The charts call that patch of sky \"empty\". It doesn't look empty.\n> left: Ignore it\n  say: You plot the usual constellations. Your chart gets top marks.\n  study astromancy +tiny\n> right: Investigate\n  lore +medium\n  flag saw_hollow_star\n  say: You spend a week in the archives. Three hundred years ago, the patch was called the Hollow. Then someone scraped the name off every chart.\n\n# ---------------- Midnight Cartographers ----------------\n\n@event carto_invite\ntime: evening night\ntitle: An Invitation at Midnight\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nwhen: met(corvin) and rel(corvin) >= 6 and not member(cartographers) and turn >= 4\nactor: corvin\nweight: 20\nart: map\ntags: club mystery\nscene: A slip of black paper pushed under a dormitory door, a silver ink map on it that rearranges itself as you watch.\ntext: A black card under your door. On it, a map in silver ink that redraws itself while you watch. Corvin's handwriting: \"Midnight. Under the third gargoyle. Bring a candle. Tell no one.\"\n> left: Ignore it\n  rel corvin -tiny\n  say: The card blanks itself the next morning. Corvin doesn't mention it, but he's a little cooler for a week.\n> right: Go\n  health -tiny\n  join cartographers\n  rel corvin +medium\n  guile +tiny\n  say: Under the gargoyle: six students, a stack of maps and a passage you'd never seen. \"Welcome,\" says Corvin, \"to the club that doesn't exist.\"\n\n@event carto_thursday_stairs\ntitle: The Thursday Staircase\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nslot: midnight\ntrack: cartographers\nweight: 14\nart: map\ntags: club mystery\nscene: A spiral staircase emerging from a blank wall in moonlight, its steps unfolding one by one.\ntext: The Cartographers have heard of a staircase that only exists on Thursdays between midnight and one. Tonight is Thursday. It is 11:58.\n> left: Map it carefully\n  health -tiny\n  lore +small\n  guile +tiny\n  say: You chart forty-one steps and a landing with a window onto a sky that isn't this one. The map is the club's pride.\n> right: Climb it to the top\n  challenge deceive journeyman wild:\n    win:\n      gold +large\n      say: At the top, a forgotten storeroom. Inside: a chest of old academy coins. The club splits it. Corvin cries a little.\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: At one o'clock the staircase stops existing. You were on it. You land in a laundry chute.\n\n@event carto_lake_tunnel\ntitle: The Tunnel Under the Lake\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: not flag(found_lake_tunnel)\ntrack: cartographers\nweight: 12\nart: door\ntags: club mystery hollowstar\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: A narrow brick tunnel dripping with water, lit by a single candle, ending at a round door carved with a black star.\ntext: A loose stone in the boathouse cellar opens on a tunnel running out under the lake. The bricks drip. At the far end, a round door carved with a black star. It is warm to the touch.\n> left: Mark it and leave\n  flag found_lake_tunnel\n  lore +tiny\n  say: You add the tunnel to the club map with a skull beside it. Corvin adds a second skull, to be safe.\n> right: Press your palm to the star\n  flag found_lake_tunnel\n  alignment -small\n  arcana +small\n  mood -tiny\n  gain hollow_dreams\n  say: The star drinks the warmth out of your hand. Behind the door, something very large shifts in its sleep.\n"},{"path":"content/events/crisis.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Crises: queued by resources hitting zero (see core/rules.grim)\n#  and by debt. These are the \"game over\" gates — each gives the\n#  player one way out, at a price, before ending the run.\n# ============================================================\n\n@event crisis_collapse\ntitle: Collapse\nclass: crisis\ntone: grim\ntrigger: queued\npriority: 90\nart: cross\ntags: crisis\nscene: A narrow infirmary bed under a high arched window, a healer's lamp floating beside it.\ntext: The world tilts. When it rights itself you are in the infirmary, and Sister Amaranth is looking at you over her spectacles. \"You have been running your body like a borrowed broom,\" she says.\n> left: Pay for the full cure (30 gold)\n  gold -large\n  health +huge\n  say: Three foul tonics and two days' sleep later, you feel almost human.\n> right: Ask to rest instead\n  if collapses >= 1:\n    end ending_sent_home\n  else:\n    health +huge\n    study -large\n    mood -small\n    collapses += 1\n    lose sick\n    say: A long, dull fortnight in bed. Sister Amaranth warns you: \"Next time, I write to your family.\"\n\n@event crisis_despair\ntitle: The Grey\nclass: crisis\ntone: grim\ntrigger: queued\npriority: 90\nart: rain\ntags: crisis\nscene: A student sitting on the edge of a dormitory bed, rain on the window, a packed trunk by the door.\ntext: Everything has gone grey. You have packed your trunk twice this week and unpacked it once. It would be so easy to take the next barge and simply stop.\n> left: Reach out to someone\n  if bonds(friend) + bonds(close) + bonds(partner) > 0:\n    mood +huge\n    rest\n    say: You knock on a friend's door. They don't ask questions. They just make tea, and stay.\n  elif met(maud):\n    mood +huge\n    rel maud +medium\n    say: Old Maud finds you in the gardens and puts a trowel in your hand. \"Dig,\" she says. You dig. It helps more than it should.\n  elif despairs >= 1:\n    end ending_quiet_departure\n  else:\n    mood +large\n    despairs += 1\n    say: You talk to the night porter, of all people. He tells you about his own first year. It's enough, for now.\n> right: Take the next barge\n  if despairs >= 1:\n    end ending_quiet_departure\n  else:\n    mood +large\n    despairs += 1\n    say: You get as far as the landing. The barge is late. By the time it arrives, you have decided to stay one more month.\n\n@event crisis_probation\ntitle: The Disciplinary Board\nclass: crisis\ntone: grim\ntrigger: queued\npriority: 90\nart: scales\ntags: crisis\nscene: Three professors behind a high bench in a dim chamber, the headmistress in the centre, hands folded.\ntext: Two terms of failing marks. You are summoned before the Disciplinary Board: three professors and the headmistress, who looks at you with an expression of deep, patient disappointment. {flag(board_reprieve): They have seen you here before.|}\n> left: Plead your case\n  if flag(board_reprieve):\n    end ending_expelled\n  else:\n    challenge negotiate journeyman likely:\n      win:\n        flag board_reprieve\n        study +huge\n        gain probation\n        say: You speak honestly and well. They grant you one reprieve, and a study plan that fills every evening. \"There will not be a second,\" says the headmistress, pleasantly.\n      lose:\n        end ending_expelled\n> right: Promise to work harder\n  if flag(board_reprieve):\n    end ending_expelled\n  else:\n    flag board_reprieve\n    study +huge\n    gain probation\n    mood -medium\n    say: \"Promises are cheap,\" says Professor Thorne. \"We'll take this one. Only this one.\" A reprieve, a study plan in his own hand, and a very long term ahead.\n\n# The first missed payment (M22): a reminder, with interest.\n@event grisk_reminder\ntitle: A Friendly Reminder\nclass: incident\ntone: tense\ntrigger: queued\npriority: 70\nart: letter\ntags: money\nchain: debt\nscene: A small, very neat note pinned to a dormitory door with a brass tack, the handwriting tiny and precise.\ntext: A note on your door, in a tidy goblin hand: \"A payment was missed. Mister Grisk is a patient businessman. Mister Grisk charges interest on his patience.\" Underneath, smaller: \"Pay something, or another month will be added.\"\n> left: Scrape together what you can\n  if gold > 0:\n    gold = 0\n    debt_missed = 0\n    mood -small\n    say: You empty your purse into an envelope and slide it under his door. It isn't twelve crowns. It is, says the note in reply, \"a gesture. Noted.\"\n  else:\n    debt_paid -= 1\n    mood -small\n    say: There is nothing to put in the envelope. You write \"next month, I promise\" on a scrap and slide that under his door instead. The reply is a single line, \"Promises are added to the ledger,\" and another month is.\n> right: Put it out of your mind\n  debt_paid -= 1\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You put the note in a drawer. Another month goes on the ledger, and next month the knock on your door will be louder.\n\n@event grisk_collectors\ntitle: Grisk's Collectors\nclass: crisis\ntone: grim\ntrigger: queued\npriority: 80\nart: chain\ntags: crisis money\nchain: debt\nscene: Two large trolls in ill-fitting waistcoats looming in a dormitory doorway, one holding a ledger.\ntext: Two trolls in waistcoats are waiting outside your dormitory. \"Mister Grisk,\" says the larger one, \"is disappointed.\" The smaller one cracks his knuckles, which sounds like a rockslide.\n> left: Hand over everything you have\n  gold = 0\n  debt_missed = 0\n  mood -medium\n  if debt_paid >= 3:\n    lose in_debt\n    say: They count your purse twice and declare the debt settled. \"Pleasure,\" says the large one, meaning it.\n  else:\n    debt_paid += 1\n    renown -small\n    say: They take every coin and your good boots, in front of half the corridor. \"Partial,\" they say, and leave.\n> right: Run\n  challenge feat apprentice+ wild:\n    win:\n      debt_missed = 1\n      mood -tiny\n      say: You escape across the rooftops. They'll be back, but not this month.\n    lose:\n      health -huge\n      debt_missed = 0\n      say: You do not escape. The trolls are surprisingly gentle about breaking only one rib.\n      gain injured\n"},{"path":"content/events/expansion_classes.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Expansion — one more event per elective, plus a core-class\n#  event everyone can get.\n# ============================================================\n\n@event core_flying_lesson\nslot: morning\ntitle: Compulsory Broom Flight\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: year == 1 and month <= 6 and term\nweight: 12\nart: broom\ntags: class danger\nscene: A row of nervous first-years standing beside brooms on the lawn, one broom already rising on its own, an instructor with a whistle.\ntext: Compulsory first-year broom flight. The instructor blows her whistle. Your broom rises, hesitates, and then — clearly having taken a dislike to you — begins drifting toward the lake.\n> left: Hold on and steer it back\n  challenge feat apprentice wild:\n    win:\n      vigor +small\n      renown +small\n      say: You wrestle it round and land, wobbling, exactly on the mark. The instructor nods. The broom grudgingly accepts you.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      say: The broom wins. You land in the shallows. Everyone sees. Everyone remembers.\n> right: Jump off while it's still low\n  mood -tiny\n  vigor +tiny\n  say: You drop four feet onto the grass. Undignified, but dry. The broom carries on to the lake alone and sinks with a sulky gurgle.\n\n@event pyro_dragon_breath\ntitle: A Sample of Dragonfire\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: evocation\nweight: 12\nart: dragon\ntags: class magic\nscene: A glass jar containing a single writhing flame of deep blue, held in tongs by a stern professor, students leaning back from it.\ntext: Thorne holds up a sealed jar containing one flame of genuine dragonfire, blue-white and furious. \"Who would like to try holding it?\" he asks. Nobody moves. He looks at you.\n> left: Hold it\n  challenge spell standard+1:\n    win:\n      arcana +medium\n      renown +small\n      rel thorne +small\n      say: It sears and struggles and then, astonishingly, settles in your palm like a small warm animal. The class stares. Thorne almost smiles.\n    lose:\n      health -large\n      say: The dragonfire disagrees. You spend a week with bandaged hands and a new respect for dragons.\n> right: Watch someone else\n  lore +tiny\n  say: A fourth-year volunteers and loses a sleeve. You take careful notes on what not to do.\n\n@event tide_water_spirit_race\ntitle: The Water Spirit Race\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: evocation\nweight: 12\nart: wave\ntags: class nature\nscene: Students swimming across a lake at dawn, translucent water-spirits racing alongside them like silver dolphins.\ntext: Professor Marisol's final challenge: swim the lake while the water spirits race you. \"They cheat,\" she says cheerfully. \"Ask them nicely not to.\"\n> left: Race them flat out\n  challenge feat journeyman wild:\n    win:\n      vigor +medium\n      renown +small\n      say: You touch the far shore a hand's length ahead of the fastest spirit. It sulks, then laughs like a fountain.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      say: The spirits tow you in circles. You arrive last, but very clean.\n> right: Ask them nicely\n  challenge negotiate apprentice+:\n    win:\n      mood +medium\n      arcana +tiny\n      say: You ask. They carry you across on their backs like a raft. Marisol says that counts. Marisol says it's the whole point.\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      say: They pretend not to hear. Water spirits are terrible at manners.\n\n@event illusion_mirror_maze\ntitle: The Mirror Maze Exam\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: illusion\nweight: 12\nart: mirror\ntags: class magic\nscene: A maze of tall mirrors in a dark hall, each reflecting a different version of the same student, one reflection grinning.\ntext: Professor Vey's exam: a maze of enchanted mirrors. Find the exit. Some reflections lie. One of them is — she promises — the real you.\n> left: Trust your own eyes\n  challenge deceive standard:\n    win:\n      guile +medium\n      study illusion +small\n      say: You spot the reflection that blinks late, and follow it out. Vey awards top marks and says, \"Most people trust the one that looks happiest.\"\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: You follow the happiest reflection for an hour. It leads you in a circle and waves goodbye.\n> right: Shatter the illusion\n  challenge spell standard:\n    win:\n      arcana +small\n      study illusion +tiny\n      say: Every mirror goes dark at once. Exit found. Vey writes, \"Effective. Inelegant. Pass.\"\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      say: You shatter one mirror. It was real. Seven years of bad luck, the caretaker says, or three crowns for the glass.\n      gold -tiny\n\n@event alch_love_potion\ntitle: The Love Potion Assignment\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ntrack: alchemy\nweight: 12\nart: potion\ntags: class romance\nscene: A pink, glittering potion in a heart-shaped flask on a workbench, a student holding it up to the light with a doubtful expression.\ntext: This week's assignment: brew a love potion — \"for analytical purposes only\". Your flask glows a suspicious pink. Someone at the next bench asks if they can buy it. Several people ask.\n> left: Brew it perfectly, then pour it away\n  alchemy +medium\n  study alchemy +small\n  say: Perfect colour, perfect scent. You pour it down the sink. The pipes are affectionate for a week.\n> right: Sell it\n  alignment -small\n  gold +medium\n  chance unlikely:\n    gain detention\n    say: A prefect falls in love with the headmistress's portrait. It's traced back to you. Worth it, almost.\n  else:\n    say: Fifteen crowns. You never ask what it was used for. You see a lot of unusual couples at dinner.\n\n@event div_prophecy_exam\ntitle: The Prophecy Exam\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\ntrack: astromancy\nweight: 12\nart: eye\ntags: class mystery\nscene: A dim divination classroom, a single student staring into a crystal ball, the examiner's quill hovering expectantly over a blank page.\ntext: The mock Divination exam: gaze into the crystal and make one prophecy about the coming month. The examiner will check. \"Vague prophecies,\" she warns, \"fail.\"\n> left: Make a bold, specific prophecy\n  chance even:\n    study astromancy +large\n    renown +small\n    say: \"On the eighth, the kitchen cat will have kittens in the headmistress's hat.\" On the eighth, she does. Top marks in the mock.\n  else:\n    study astromancy -small\n    say: \"On the eighth, it will snow indoors.\" It does not snow indoors. The examiner writes \"ambitious\".\n> right: Make a careful, safe prophecy\n  study astromancy +small\n  lore +tiny\n  say: \"Someone will be late to breakfast.\" Someone is. The examiner sighs and gives you a bare pass: it was vague, but it was checkable.\n\n@event art_golem_helper\ntitle: The Little Golem\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: artifice\nweight: 12\nart: gear\ntags: class magic\nscene: A knee-high clay golem carrying a teetering stack of books across a classroom, a student watching anxiously as it nears a staircase.\ntext: Your term project: a knee-high clay golem that carries books. It works! It works so well that it has started carrying everyone's books, whether they want it to or not.\n> left: Add a \"stop\" command\n  challenge research standard-2:\n    win:\n      study artifice +medium\n      lore +small\n      say: The golem stops. It also waits politely. The professor asks if she can borrow it for her office.\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      say: Now it stops randomly, mid-staircase, holding forty books. Nobody can get past it. It looks very pleased.\n> right: Let it roam free\n  chance likely:\n    renown +small\n    mood +small\n    say: The golem becomes a beloved castle fixture, delivering books to whoever looks sad. It signs every delivery with your initials, in clay.\n  else:\n    renown -tiny\n    say: It also delivers a Restricted Section book to a first-year. The librarian follows the clay smudges straight to your door.\n\n@event beast_hippocampus\ntitle: The Hippocampus Foal\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: beastcraft\nweight: 12\nart: wave\ntags: class nature\nscene: A tiny sea-horse foal with a fish's tail struggling in a rock pool on the lake shore, a student kneeling beside the water.\ntext: A hippocampus foal — half pony, half fish — has been stranded in a shrinking rock pool on the lakeshore. Professor Brask is on the other side of the grounds. The foal is panicking.\n> left: Carry it to the lake\n  alignment +tiny\n  challenge feat apprentice:\n    win:\n      mood +medium\n      vigor +tiny\n      say: It thrashes, then trusts you. You wade out and let it go. It circles you three times before diving deep.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood +small\n      say: It kicks you in the stomach with surprising force — but you get it to the water. Brask says that's how they say thank you.\n> right: Run for Professor Brask\n  lore +small\n  say: Brask arrives with a bucket and a lullaby. She makes you hold the bucket. You learn more in ten minutes than in a whole term.\n\n@event astro_eclipse\ntitle: The Eclipse\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrack: astromancy\nweight: 12\nart: moon\ntags: class mystery\nscene: A black disc sliding across the sun above the observatory dome, students on the roof with smoked-glass lenses, a halo of light around the darkness.\ntext: An eclipse, the first in forty years. The astromancy class is on the roof with smoked-glass lenses. At the moment of totality, you notice something the charts don't show: a second shadow, smaller, moving the wrong way.\n> left: Record it precisely\n  lore +medium\n  find second_shadow\n  say: You chart the second shadow's path exactly. It crosses the sky and stops — directly above the lake.\n> right: Just watch the eclipse\n  mood +medium\n  say: For four minutes the world holds its breath. You'll remember the cold, and the silence, and the strange birdsong, for the rest of your life.\n\n@event heal_triage_drill\ntitle: Triage\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrack: alchemy\nweight: 12\nart: cross\ntags: class danger\nscene: A practice ward full of students acting injured, groaning theatrically on cots, a single student healer in the middle with a clipboard.\ntext: Sister Amaranth's triage drill: twelve \"injured\" students, one healer — you — and ten minutes. Some are faking worse than others. One, you realise, is genuinely hurt.\n> left: Treat the genuine injury first\n  challenge brew standard-1:\n    win:\n      alchemy +medium\n      study alchemy +small\n      say: You spot the real injury in the first minute. Amaranth nods, once, which is the highest honour in the infirmary.\n    lose:\n      study alchemy +tiny\n      say: You find the real injury in minute nine. Late, but not too late. Amaranth's lips thin. \"Again tomorrow.\"\n> right: Work systematically down the line\n  study alchemy +tiny\n  lore +tiny\n  say: Methodical, thorough and slow. The real patient waits seven minutes. Amaranth makes you write an essay on why.\n\n@event umbral_last_lecture\ntitle: The Last Lecture on Absence\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: year >= 2\ntrack: umbral\nweight: 12\nart: skull\ntags: class mystery hollowstar\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: An Umbral Theory lecture hall lit only by a black candle, the professor drawing a star with a hollow centre on the board.\ntext: The Umbral professor draws a star with a hollow centre on the board. \"This is not on the syllabus,\" she says. \"Three hundred years ago this academy imprisoned an absence that thinks. I believe you should know that before you graduate.\"\n> left: Take careful notes\n  lore +medium\n  flag knows_founders_seal\n  find knows_founders_seal\n  find seven_locks\n  say: She speaks for an hour about seals and locks and the founders' fear. At the end she wipes the board clean and says, \"This lecture never happened.\"\n> right: Ask why she's telling you\n  rel isolde +tiny\n  say: \"Because something is stirring,\" she says, \"and the headmistress asked me to find out which of you would ask that question.\" She writes your name down.\n"},{"path":"content/events/expansion_faculty.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Expansion — short arcs for Professor Thorne, Old Maud and\n#  Headmistress Marrow. All `trigger: social` (need met + rel).\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- Professor Thorne ----------------\n\n@event thorne_old_rival\ntitle: An Old Rival\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: thorne\nwhen: rel(thorne) >= 9 and year >= 2\nweight: 12\nart: duel\ntags: rival study\nscene: Two elderly mages facing each other across a dueling ring at dusk, one stern and grey, one flamboyant in purple, a nervous student holding a towel at the side.\ntext: A flamboyant old wizard in purple has arrived to settle \"a forty-year-old matter\" with Professor Thorne. A formal duel needs a second. Thorne looks around the courtyard and, to everyone's astonishment, points at you.\n> left: Stand as his second\n  mood -tiny\n  rel thorne +medium\n  renown +small\n  arcana +tiny\n  say: The duel lasts four seconds, the longest of your life. Thorne wins. Then both old men sit on the grass and laugh until they wheeze. You've never seen him laugh. Neither has anyone else.\n> right: Politely decline\n  rel thorne -tiny\n  say: \"Hm,\" says Thorne, and asks the caretaker instead. He wins anyway. He does not mention it again, which is somehow worse.\n\n@event thorne_roses\ntime: morning\ntitle: The Professor's Roses\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: thorne\nwhen: rel(thorne) >= 6 and month >= 7 and month <= 10 and met(maud)\nweight: 10\nart: flower\ntags: nature\nscene: A severe old professor kneeling in a hidden rose garden at dawn, sleeves rolled up, murmuring encouragement to a single struggling rosebush.\ntext: At dawn, behind the observatory, you find Professor Thorne on his knees in a tiny walled rose garden, murmuring encouragement to a sickly bush. He sees you. Neither of you breathes.\n> left: Back away and never speak of it\n  rel thorne +medium\n  say: You leave. The next day in class he calls on you for the hardest question — and then, very quietly, gives you full marks for a so-so answer.\n> right: Offer to help with the sick bush\n  challenge brew apprentice+:\n    win:\n      rel thorne +large\n      alchemy +small\n      say: You know the blight; Maud taught you. Within a month the bush is covered in red roses. Thorne leaves one, without comment, on your desk.\n    lose:\n      rel thorne +small\n      say: Your remedy does nothing. But Thorne lets you kneel beside him for an hour, saying nothing, which you suspect is a great honour.\n\n@event thorne_shaking_hands\ntime: noon afternoon\ntitle: Thorne's Hands\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: thorne\nwhen: rel(thorne) >= 15 and year >= 2\nweight: 10\nart: hand\ntags: social\nscene: An old professor gripping the edge of a lectern with both hands, a dropped wand on the floor, a single student in the doorway.\ntext: After class you see Thorne's wand slip from fingers that won't stop trembling. He picks it up, slowly. \"Age,\" he says, not looking at you. \"It comes for every storm eventually. You'll say nothing.\"\n> left: Say nothing, as he asks\n  mood -tiny\n  rel thorne +medium\n  say: You never mention it. But you start arriving early to set up his demonstrations. He notices. He lets you.\n> right: Tell Sister Amaranth\n  rel thorne -medium\n  say: Amaranth's tonic steadies his hands. Thorne is furious with you for a week. Then he stops being furious, and starts, grudgingly, teaching you his hardest spells — \"before I can't.\"\n\n# ---------------- Old Maud ----------------\n\n@event maud_goat\ntitle: Bartholomew in the Library\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: maud\nwhen: rel(maud) >= 3\nweight: 12\nart: book\ntags: nature\nscene: A shaggy goat standing on a library table chewing a book, an apoplectic librarian below, an old woman in a straw hat hurrying through the stacks.\ntext: Maud's goat, Bartholomew, has got into the library and is eating the Restricted Section. The librarian is apoplectic. Maud, hurrying in, grabs your arm. \"He likes you. Get him out before he eats anything with teeth.\"\n> left: Lure him out with an apple\n  challenge negotiate apprentice- wild:\n    win:\n      rel maud +medium\n      say: Bartholomew follows the apple, and you, out into the sunlight. He has eaten one-third of \"Curses of the Northern Wastes\". He seems fine. Mostly.\n    lose:\n      rel maud +small\n      health -tiny\n      say: Bartholomew eats the apple, then your sleeve, then headbutts you into a shelf. Maud gets him out in the end, laughing.\n> right: Pick him up and carry him out\n  health -tiny\n  vigor +small\n  rel maud +medium\n  say: He is heavier than he looks and struggles like a sack of angry cats. You carry him all the way to the paddock. The librarian applauds.\n\n@event maud_recipe\ntitle: Maud's Soup\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: maud\nwhen: rel(maud) >= 12\nweight: 10\nart: cauldron\ntags: nature home\nscene: A small cottage kitchen, a black iron pot over the fire, an old woman guiding a student's hand as they stir.\ntext: \"I'm going to teach you my soup,\" says Maud, rolling up her sleeves. \"Cures colds, broken hearts and most curses. Don't write it down. You'll remember it if you need it.\"\n> left: Learn it properly\n  alchemy +small\n  rel maud +medium\n  say: It takes all afternoon. Some of the ingredients are herbs; some, as far as you can tell, are just stories. You remember every step.\n> right: Mostly eat it\n  health +tiny\n  rel maud +tiny\n  say: You learn about half of it and eat three bowls. Maud declares that the most important part of the recipe anyway.\n\n@event maud_old_wand\ntitle: A Wand From Sixty Years Ago\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: social\nactor: maud\nwhen: rel(maud) >= 18 and flag(knows_founders_seal)\nweight: 14\nart: wand\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: An old woman unwrapping a slender silver wand from a faded silk scarf, a photograph of three young students on the table beside it.\ntext: Maud unwraps something from a faded scarf: a slender silver wand. \"Edmund's,\" she says. \"He was the one who didn't come back. He'd have wanted it to go beneath the lake again, with someone who meant to close the door.\"\n> left: Accept it\n  arcana +medium\n  flag edmunds_wand\n  rel maud +medium\n  say: It fits your hand like it was waiting. When you hold it near the lake, it hums the same note as the drowned bell.\n> right: Tell her to keep it\n  rel maud +large\n  mood +small\n  say: \"Keep it for him,\" you say. She holds it for a long time. \"Sixty years,\" she says, \"and nobody ever said that.\" She makes tea, and you sit with her until dark.\n\n# ---------------- Headmistress Marrow ----------------\n\n@event isolde_chess\ntitle: A Game of Tower Chess\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: isolde\nwhen: rel(isolde) >= 6\nweight: 10\nart: crown\ntags: study\nscene: A chessboard of carved crystal towers on a small table by a tower window, two players, one candle, the lake silver below.\ntext: The headmistress keeps a board of crystal towers by her window. \"Do you play?\" she asks. It is clearly not a question. The pieces are already moving into position by themselves.\n> left: Play to win\n  challenge research vs isolde.lore wild:\n    win:\n      rel isolde +medium\n      lore +small\n      say: You win. She looks at the board for a long moment, then laughs — a short, delighted sound. \"Again,\" she says. \"Thursday.\"\n    lose:\n      rel isolde +small\n      lore +tiny\n      say: She wins in nine moves and spends the next hour showing you exactly why. It is the most useful lesson you've had all year.\n> right: Play to learn how she thinks\n  mood -tiny\n  rel isolde +medium\n  guile +small\n  say: You lose, slowly, watching. She sacrifices pieces she seems to love. By the end, you understand a great deal about how the academy is run.\n\n@event isolde_portrait\ntitle: The Young Headmistress\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: social\nactor: isolde\nwhen: rel(isolde) >= 3\nweight: 8\nart: mirror\ntags: mystery\nscene: A portrait in a forgotten corridor of a laughing young woman in student robes with a wand behind her ear, frame dusty, eyes unmistakably the headmistress's.\ntext: In a forgotten corridor you find a portrait of a laughing girl with a wand stuck behind her ear and ink on her nose. The plaque has been scratched off. The eyes are unmistakably the headmistress's. The portrait winks.\n> left: Ask the portrait about her\n  chance likely:\n    guile +tiny\n    lore +tiny\n    say: \"Detention record of the decade,\" whispers the portrait. \"Set the observatory on fire twice. Best student this castle ever had.\" It winks again.\n  else:\n    mood -tiny\n    say: The portrait pretends to be asleep, loudly. By the time you give up, the corridor has rearranged itself, and it takes you half the evening to find your way out.\n> right: Tell the headmistress you found it\n  rel isolde +small\n  say: She is quiet for a moment. \"I thought they'd burned it.\" She almost smiles. \"Leave it where it is. Someone should remember her.\"\n\n@event isolde_judgement\ntitle: A Question of Mercy\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: isolde\nwhen: rel(isolde) >= 12 and year >= 2\nweight: 10\nart: scales\ntags: story\nscene: The headmistress at her desk with a single file open, a student seated opposite, rain on the window behind them.\ntext: The headmistress slides a file across her desk. A second-year stole from the apothecary to pay for a sick mother's medicine. The rules say expulsion. \"You're a student,\" she says. \"Tell me what a student thinks is just.\"\n> left: Argue for mercy\n  rel isolde +medium\n  charm +tiny\n  say: You argue for an hour. She listens to all of it. The second-year is given a job in the infirmary and a stern lecture. The mother recovers.\n> right: The rules exist for a reason\n  rel isolde +small\n  lore +tiny\n  say: \"Yes,\" she says, \"they do.\" She closes the file. You never learn what she decided, but the second-year is still in class the next week, looking grateful at nobody in particular.\n"},{"path":"content/events/expansion_life.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Expansion — more academy life, plus a short chain:\n#    timeloop: life_timeloop_1 -> life_timeloop_2 -> life_timeloop_3\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- The Tuesday That Wouldn't End ----------------\n\n# The time loop (playtest 2, X8 with L21): one Tuesday, lived over and over in a single day of the game. The hub\n# (`life_timeloop_2`) counts the loops in `loop_n`; each loop you exploit it, experiment or investigate, and each\n# card ends by looping again. Clues (`loop_clues`) open the fix (`life_timeloop_3`), which gets easier every loop;\n# at the ninth loop it breaks by itself (`timeloop_cap`), so it can never soft-lock. More cards: chain_timeloop.grim.\n@event life_timeloop_1\nslot: morning\ntitle: Haven't We Done This?\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: turn >= 5 and not flag(timeloop_started)\nweight: 6\nart: hourglass\ntags: mystery magic\nchain: timeloop\nscene: A student at breakfast staring at a plate of eggs with intense déjà vu, a clock on the wall with its hands spinning backward.\ntext: Breakfast. Eggs. Thorne trips on the third step. A first-year spills juice on a prefect. You know all of this is about to happen, because it already happened. Yesterday. Which was also Tuesday.\n> left: Ignore it, you're probably just tired\n  flag timeloop_started\n  loop_n = 1\n  loop_clues = 0\n  loop_exploit = 0\n  mood -tiny\n  queue life_timeloop_2 now\n  say: You eat the eggs. They taste exactly as they did yesterday. Thorne trips on the third step. You go to bed early, uneasy, and wake to eggs.\n> right: Warn Thorne about the step\n  flag timeloop_started\n  loop_n = 1\n  loop_clues = 0\n  loop_exploit = 0\n  rel thorne +tiny\n  queue life_timeloop_2 now\n  say: \"The third step, Professor.\" He looks at you strangely and steps over it. Somewhere, a clock skips. You go to bed, and wake, and it's still Tuesday.\n\n@event life_timeloop_2\ntitle: Tuesday, Again\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: queued\ntype: menu\nrepeat: 10\nart: hourglass\ntags: mystery magic\nchain: timeloop\nscene: The same breakfast scene repeated, but the student is now standing on the table, holding up a sign that reads \"IT'S STILL TUESDAY\".\ntext: {loop_n <= 1: It's Tuesday again. Eggs, the third step, the juice, the prefect. Nobody else notices anything.|{loop_n <= 3: Tuesday. You could recite breakfast. You could recite the prefect's yelp.|{loop_n <= 5: Tuesday, again. The days are starting to feel thin, like a page read too often.|{loop_n <= 7: Tuesday. The eggs taste of nothing now. The clock in the hall ticks a beat late.|Tuesday. The edges of the morning are fraying, like a loop pulled too tight. It can't hold much longer.}}}} {loop_clues >= 3: You know where it comes from now.|{loop_clues >= 1: You have a thread to pull.|You have no idea why.}}\n> exploit: Use it: win every bet, ace the quiz\n  hint: Crowns and marks carry over. It wears on you, and someone may notice.\n  queue timeloop_exploit now\n  say: You know every answer and every coin-toss. Why not?\n> experiment: Do something you'd never dare\n  hint: Nothing you do today will last. Almost nothing.\n  chance unlikely:\n    queue timeloop_dare_confess now\n  else:\n    chance even:\n      queue timeloop_dare_fall now\n    else:\n      queue timeloop_dare_kindness now\n  say: Nothing today can stick. You might as well find out who you are when nothing sticks.\n> investigate: Look for the cause\n  hint: Each loop, a clue. Clues make the fix possible.\n  if loop_clues == 0:\n    queue timeloop_clue_bells now\n  elif loop_clues == 1:\n    queue timeloop_clue_light now\n  else:\n    queue timeloop_clue_firstyear now\n  say: Something is doing this. Clocks don't stop on their own.\n> fix: Try to break the loop\n  hint: A spell against the clock. Hard at first; easier every loop, and with what you've found.\n  when: loop_clues >= 1\n  queue life_timeloop_3 now\n  say: You go up the astronomy tower before breakfast, while the morning is still new.\n\n@event life_timeloop_3\ntitle: The Broken Chronometer\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: queued\nrepeat: 8\nart: gear\ntags: mystery magic\nchain: timeloop\nscene: An astronomy tower at night, a giant brass chronometer with its gears spinning in reverse, a sleeping first-year slumped beside it with a wand still in hand.\ntext: In the astronomy tower, a first-year is asleep beside an enormous brass chronometer, wand still stuck in its winding key. \"I just wanted one more day before my exam,\" says the note on his chest. {loop_clues >= 3: You know exactly which gear, and why.|You know roughly where to start.}\n> left: Wrench the clock forward\n  loop_tier = loop_n + 2 * (loop_clues >= 3)\n  if loop_tier >= 7:\n    challenge spell adept likely:\n      win:\n        flag timeloop_done\n        arcana +small\n        queue timeloop_after in 1\n        say: The gears groan, catch and turn forward. Somewhere a clock strikes, properly, for the first time in {loop_n >= 6: what feels like a month|days}. It's Wednesday. The first-year in the corner wakes up and starts to cry with relief.\n      lose:\n        health -small\n        loop_n += 1\n        if loop_n >= 9:\n          queue timeloop_cap now\n        else:\n          queue life_timeloop_2 now\n        say: The chronometer bites, and the morning folds up like a letter. You wake to eggs, and the third step, and the juice. It's Tuesday. You know a little more about the clock.\n  elif loop_tier >= 5:\n    challenge spell adept:\n      win:\n        flag timeloop_done\n        arcana +small\n        queue timeloop_after in 1\n        say: The gears groan, catch and turn forward. Somewhere a clock strikes, properly, for the first time in {loop_n >= 6: what feels like a month|days}. It's Wednesday. The first-year in the corner wakes up and starts to cry with relief.\n      lose:\n        health -small\n        loop_n += 1\n        if loop_n >= 9:\n          queue timeloop_cap now\n        else:\n          queue life_timeloop_2 now\n        say: The chronometer bites, and the morning folds up like a letter. You wake to eggs, and the third step, and the juice. It's Tuesday. You know a little more about the clock.\n  elif loop_tier >= 3:\n    challenge spell adept unlikely:\n      win:\n        flag timeloop_done\n        arcana +small\n        queue timeloop_after in 1\n        say: The gears groan, catch and turn forward. Somewhere a clock strikes, properly, for the first time in {loop_n >= 6: what feels like a month|days}. It's Wednesday. The first-year in the corner wakes up and starts to cry with relief.\n      lose:\n        health -small\n        loop_n += 1\n        if loop_n >= 9:\n          queue timeloop_cap now\n        else:\n          queue life_timeloop_2 now\n        say: The chronometer bites, and the morning folds up like a letter. You wake to eggs, and the third step, and the juice. It's Tuesday. You know a little more about the clock.\n  else:\n    challenge spell adept slim:\n      win:\n        flag timeloop_done\n        arcana +small\n        queue timeloop_after in 1\n        say: The gears groan, catch and turn forward. Somewhere a clock strikes, properly, for the first time in {loop_n >= 6: what feels like a month|days}. It's Wednesday. The first-year in the corner wakes up and starts to cry with relief.\n      lose:\n        health -small\n        loop_n += 1\n        if loop_n >= 9:\n          queue timeloop_cap now\n        else:\n          queue life_timeloop_2 now\n        say: The chronometer bites, and the morning folds up like a letter. You wake to eggs, and the third step, and the juice. It's Tuesday. You know a little more about the clock.\n> right: Wake the first-year and fix it together\n  charm +small\n  mood -tiny\n  if loop_clues >= 2:\n    flag timeloop_done\n    queue timeloop_after in 1\n    say: You shake him awake. He's horrified, then helpful. Between your clues and his guilt you find the gear together, and the clock strikes Wednesday. He passes his exam. You never tell him how many Tuesdays it was.\n  else:\n    loop_n += 1\n    if loop_n >= 9:\n      queue timeloop_cap now\n    else:\n      queue life_timeloop_2 now\n    say: You shake him awake, and he panics, and the wand snaps in the key. The morning folds up. You wake to eggs. It's Tuesday, and you know a little more about the boy.\n\n# ---------------- Around the castle ----------------\n\n@event life_portrait_escape\ntitle: The Escaped Portrait\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nweight: 10\nart: mirror\ntags: magic mystery\nscene: An empty gilded picture frame on a gallery wall, and further down the corridor a painted duchess hiding inside a landscape of a picnic.\ntext: An empty frame in the long gallery. The Duchess of Ambry has escaped her portrait and is hiding in a landscape three corridors away, refusing to come back. \"That frame,\" she declares, \"is hideous.\"\n> left: Persuade her back\n  challenge negotiate apprentice+ wild:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      say: You promise to have her frame regilded. She returns, mollified. The caretaker regilds it. You've acquired a duchess who now considers you a personal friend.\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      say: She calls you a peasant and moves to a painting of a shipwreck. She's still there. She says she likes the sea air.\n> right: Leave her in the picnic\n  mood +tiny\n  say: She seems happier among the sandwiches. So does the picnic. Some arrangements simply work.\n\n@event life_library_cat\ntitle: The Library Cat\nwhen: term and not wrapup\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nweight: 10\nart: cat\ntags: home\nscene: An enormous fluffy cat asleep on the exact book a student needs, on a high library shelf, one eye half-open.\ntext: The library cat — a vast, ancient beast called Footnote — is asleep on the only copy of the book you need. You have an essay due tomorrow. Footnote has no deadlines.\n> left: Move the cat\n  challenge feat apprentice wild:\n    win:\n      study +small\n      say: You lift Footnote like a sack of warm flour. He allows it, with contempt. You get the book. He gets your chair.\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      say: Footnote does not consent to being moved. You now have three long scratches and still no book.\n> right: Bribe the cat\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: One sardine. Footnote rises like a sultan, stretches, and moves exactly one book to the left. It's enough.\n\n@event life_invent_spell\ntime: night\ntitle: A Spell of Your Own\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: arcana >= 18\nweight: 10\nart: sparkle\ntags: magic study\nscene: A student at a desk covered in crossed-out incantations, a tiny spiral of light hovering over the page, an expression of dawning triumph.\ntext: Late at night you realise there's a gap — a spell nobody has written yet, just a small one, to make ink dry instantly. You could try to invent it. Inventing spells, Thorne says, is \"how most of my former students lost their eyebrows\".\n> left: Invent it carefully\n  challenge research journeyman steady:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      learn_spell mend\n      say: Three weeks of theory and one perfect evening. The ink dries, and so does a paper cut on your thumb: what you've written is a cousin of Mend. Thorne grunts and puts your name on next year's lecture slide.\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      say: Your spell dries ink instantly. It also dries the paper, the desk and your eyes. You shelve it for now.\n> right: Just try things until something works\n  chance unlikely:\n    arcana +medium\n    renown +small\n    say: Somehow — through chaos and luck — it works. You couldn't explain why under oath. It still counts.\n  else:\n    health -small\n    say: Your eyebrows survive. Your desk does not.\n\n@event life_cursed_umbrella\ntitle: The Cursed Umbrella\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: month >= 3 and month <= 7\nweight: 8\nart: rain\ntags: magic\nscene: A student holding a black umbrella under which a tiny private rainstorm is pouring, while the sky outside is perfectly clear.\ntext: You borrowed an umbrella from lost property. It's black, elegant and cursed: underneath it, it is always raining. The rest of the world is sunny. You are very wet.\n> left: Break the curse\n  challenge spell apprentice:\n    win:\n      arcana +tiny\n      gold +tiny\n      alignment -tiny\n      say: The curse lifts with a soft hiss. The umbrella is now just a very nice umbrella. It isn't yours, but you sell it to Corvin anyway; he wants it \"for drama\".\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      say: Now it rains sideways.\n> right: Return it to lost property\n  alignment +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: The caretaker takes it back, sighs, and hangs it on a hook labelled \"DO NOT\". There are six other things on that hook.\n\n@event life_exam_dream\ntime: night\ntitle: The Exam Dream\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: month == 3 or month == 4 or month == 9 or month == 10\nweight: 10\nart: bed\ntags: home\nscene: A dream-image: a student in an exam hall in their pyjamas, the exam paper written in a language that keeps changing, a clock with no hands.\ntext: You dream you're in the exam hall in your pyjamas. The paper is in a language that keeps changing. The proctor is Bartholomew the goat. You wake at 4am, heart pounding.\n> left: Get up and revise\n  study +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: You revise until breakfast. The real exam paper is written in a normal language. You're absurdly relieved.\n> right: Go back to sleep\n  health +tiny\n  say: You dream again. This time the goat gives you full marks. You choose to take this as an omen.\n\n@event life_garden_plot\ntitle: A Patch of Earth\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: not has(garden_plot) and turn >= 4\nweight: 5\nart: leaf\ntags: nature\nscene: A small square of freshly turned earth behind the greenhouses, a wooden stake with a student's name painted on it, a watering can beside it.\ntext: Old Maud is giving out the abandoned plots behind the greenhouses. There's one left: small, weedy and full of rocks. \"It's yours if you want it,\" she says. \"Gardens are a lot of work. Most of the work is noticing.\"\n> left: Take the plot\n  gain garden_plot\n  mood +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: You spend a whole weekend clearing rocks and earn a back that complains for a week. By spring, you have lettuces, three violent mandrakes and a strange purple flower nobody can name.\n> right: Leave it for someone else\n  mood +tiny\n  say: A shy first-year takes it and grows giant pumpkins. You feel oddly proud of them.\n\n# The pen pal's first letter (playtest 2, X9): Tamsin Reed, the start of chain_thornwick.grim.\n@event life_pen_pal\ntitle: A Letter From Thornwick\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: year == 1 and month == 2 and not met(tamsin) and not off_campus\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: letter\ntags: social\nscene: A letter with a green Thornwick seal open on a desk, beside a reply half-written in Lumenhold blue ink.\ntext: A letter arrives from a stranger at Thornwick Academy, the rival school. \"Dear Lumenhold student chosen at random,\" it begins. \"I'm told you're all insufferable. Prove me wrong? T. Reed (illusion, first year, not insufferable).\"\n> left: Write back\n  meet tamsin\n  rel tamsin +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: Your reply is four pages long and only slightly insufferable, and it takes the whole evening. The answer arrives within a week, signed \"Tamsin\". You've made a friend in enemy territory.\n> right: Ignore it\n  mood +tiny\n  queue tamsin_second_letter in 3\n  say: You ignore it, and enjoy the feeling of being unbothered for about a day. Some letters, you suspect, don't take no for an answer.\n\n@event life_three_headed_puppy\ntitle: A Very Lost Puppy\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nweight: 8\nart: paw\ntags: nature danger\nscene: A puppy with three heads sitting in a corridor, one head chewing a shoe, one licking a wall, one howling mournfully.\ntext: There is a puppy in the dormitory corridor. It has three heads. One is eating a shoe, one is licking the wall, one is howling. It is very lost and, all three heads agree, very hungry.\n> left: Feed it and find its owner\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +medium\n  say: You buy it a string of sausages from the kitchens, then follow its owner's frantic shouting to Professor Brask's office. She weeps with relief. The puppy's three heads each lick you once, in strict order.\n> right: Call Professor Brask\n  lore +tiny\n  say: Brask arrives at a run with a lead and three biscuits, and explains to you, at length, how to tell which head is in charge. \"Bad boys,\" she says fondly. All three heads look deeply ashamed.\n\n@event life_bell_tower\ntime: night\ntitle: The Midnight Bell\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: turn >= 4\nweight: 8\nart: bell\ntags: danger social\nscene: A dark bell tower interior, ropes hanging down, a group of students at the bottom daring one of their number to climb.\ntext: Tradition: once in your time at Lumenhold, you must ring the old bell at midnight without getting caught. Your dorm has decided tonight is your night.\n> left: Climb and ring it\n  challenge deceive apprentice+:\n    win:\n      renown +medium\n      mood +small\n      say: BONG. The whole castle wakes. By the time the prefects reach the tower you're back in bed, innocent and smug.\n    lose:\n      gain detention\n      renown +small\n      say: BONG — and Bram is at the bottom of the stairs when you come down. Detention. But you rang it, and everybody heard.\n> right: Decline the dare\n  mood -tiny\n  say: Someone else rings it instead and becomes a legend. You'll always wonder.\n\n@event life_fog_maze\ntitle: The Fog Maze\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nwhen: month == 3 or month == 4\nweight: 8\nart: compass\ntags: nature mystery\nscene: The academy grounds swallowed by thick white fog, hedges looming like walls, a lantern glowing faintly ahead.\ntext: Fog has come up off the lake so thick you can't see your own feet. The path to the greenhouses has become a maze. Somewhere ahead, someone is calling for help, faintly.\n> left: Follow the voice\n  alignment +tiny\n  challenge feat apprentice wild:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      mood +small\n      say: You find a first-year sitting on a bench, completely lost, sixty feet from the door. You walk them in. They tell everyone you saved their life.\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      health -tiny\n      say: You walk in circles for an hour and end up exactly where you started. The voice was a very clever crow.\n> right: Cast a light and wait\n  arcana +tiny\n  say: You raise a floating light and wait. One by one, three lost students find their way to it. You feel like a lighthouse.\n\n@event life_wishing_well\ntitle: The Wishing Well\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nweight: 8\nart: coin\ntags: magic money\nscene: An old stone well in the corner of a cloister, coins glinting at the bottom, a carved inscription half-worn away.\ntext: In the old cloister there's a wishing well. The inscription says: \"ONE COIN, ONE WISH, ONE PRICE.\" The bottom glitters with three hundred years of coins.\n> left: Throw in a coin and wish\n  gold -tiny\n  chance even:\n    mood +medium\n    say: You wish for a good month. It is, mostly. Coincidence, probably.\n  else:\n    mood -tiny\n    gold +tiny\n    say: You wish. The well hiccups and spits your coin back out. Apparently, you can't afford that wish.\n> right: Fish out a few old coins\n  alignment -tiny\n  challenge deceive journeyman wild:\n    win:\n      gold +medium\n      say: Fifteen crowns' worth of antique coins. The well is silent. Disapprovingly silent.\n    lose:\n      gold -tiny\n      mood -tiny\n      say: The well bites. You lose your own purse in it. ONE PRICE indeed.\n\n@event life_borrowed_broom\ntitle: The Borrowed Broom\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nweight: 8\nart: broom\ntags: danger\nscene: A student clinging to a bucking broom high above the castle, its bristles on fire, a smaller student on the ground waving frantically.\ntext: A second-year lends you their broom for an errand. Midway across the lake, you discover why they were so keen to lend it: it is extremely ticklish, and it hates you.\n> left: Wrestle it home\n  challenge feat apprentice+ wild:\n    win:\n      vigor +small\n      say: You land in a heap on the lawn, triumphant and windswept. The broom sulks. The second-year owes you a favour.\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      say: You land in the lake. The broom flies home without you, very pleased with itself.\n> right: Soothe it\n  challenge negotiate apprentice+ wild:\n    win:\n      charm +tiny\n      mood +small\n      say: You talk to it gently all the way across. It purrs. Brooms shouldn't purr. Now it follows you around.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      say: It is not soothed. It is, if anything, more ticklish, and it tips you into the shallows on the way home.\n\n@event life_double_trouble\ntitle: Your Double Is Loose\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: (enrolled(illusion) or passed(illusion, 1)) or seen(illusion_mirror_self) > 0\nweight: 6\nart: mask\ntags: magic danger\nscene: A student staring in horror across a crowded corridor at a perfect copy of themselves, cheerfully chatting up a professor.\ntext: An illusion of you — one you thought you'd dismissed — is still wandering the castle. It has been to three of your classes, flirted with a prefect, and volunteered you for the choir.\n> left: Catch it\n  challenge deceive apprentice+:\n    win:\n      guile +small\n      say: You corner it in a broom cupboard. It looks at you, shrugs and pops like a bubble. The prefect, however, still expects a date.\n    lose:\n      renown +small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: It escapes, and spends a week as the most charming version of you. People are disappointed when you come back.\n> right: Let it run its course\n  renown +small\n  study +tiny\n  say: It attends your lectures and takes better notes than you do. When it fades after a week, you're almost sorry.\n\n@event life_auction\ntitle: The Lost-Property Auction\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: gold >= 10\nweight: 8\nart: dice\ntags: money\nscene: A crowded hall, a caretaker with a gavel at a lectern, tables piled with lost scarves, wands, boots and one suspiciously humming box.\ntext: Once a year the caretaker auctions off unclaimed lost property. There's a box nobody wants that hums faintly. The bidding starts at eight crowns. Nobody's bidding.\n> left: Bid on the humming box\n  gold -small\n  chance even:\n    gain inspired\n    say: Inside: a music box that plays a different song every time you open it. Each one makes you think of something new.\n  else:\n    gain hiccup_hex\n    say: Inside: a very old, very annoyed hiccup curse, which immediately transfers itself to you.\n> right: Just watch the bidding\n  mood +tiny\n  say: Nobody bids on the box. A first-year buys a single left boot for a crown and looks thrilled. You leave with nothing and a good story.\n\n@event life_village_busking\ntitle: Busking in the Village\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: visits_to(great_hall) >= 3 or charm >= 16\nweight: 8\nart: music\ntags: money social\nscene: A cobbled village square, a student singing beside a fountain with an upturned hat at their feet, villagers pausing to listen.\ntext: You're short on crowns, and the village square has a fountain, an empty hat and a crowd of shoppers. You do know a few songs.\n> left: Sing your heart out\n  challenge negotiate apprentice wild:\n    win:\n      gold +medium\n      renown +small\n      say: A small crowd gathers. Someone requests an encore. The hat fills with copper and one silver coin from a weeping old man.\n    lose:\n      gold +tiny\n      mood -tiny\n      say: Three crowns and a sympathetic look from a dog. Busking is harder than it looks.\n> right: Play the street-magic act instead\n  challenge spell apprentice wild:\n    win:\n      gold +medium\n      mood +small\n      say: You make doves out of the fountain spray. Children scream with joy. The hat overflows.\n    lose:\n      gold +tiny\n      say: Your doves come out as damp sparrows. People are kind about it.\n\n@event life_moths\ntitle: A Storm of Moths\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nwhen: month >= 7 and month <= 10\nweight: 5\nart: moon\ntags: nature mystery\nscene: A dormitory room filled with thousands of pale moths circling a single candle, a student standing in the doorway, astonished.\ntext: You open your door to find your room full of moths — thousands of them, pale as moonlight, circling your candle in silent spirals. It's eerie. It's also, you can't help noticing, beautiful.\n> left: Open the window and let them go\n  mood +small\n  say: They pour out into the night in a silver river. One stays behind, on your pillow, for a week. You name it Gregory.\n> right: Study them\n  lore +small\n  alchemy +tiny\n  say: They're luna moths, migrating — and drawn to something in your room. You find it: a crumb of moonpetal on your sleeve. Mystery solved. Mostly.\n"},{"path":"content/events/expansion_people.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Expansion — more character events (social slot).\n#  Three each for Lyra, Tobias, Seraphine, Corvin, Mir, Nessa,\n#  Kael; two for Bram.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- Lyra ----------------\n\n@event lyra_toast\ntitle: Lyra Cannot Cook\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: lyra\nwhen: rel(lyra) >= 9\nweight: 10\nart: flame\ntags: social\nscene: A dormitory kitchenette full of smoke, a crimson-haired girl holding a blackened slice of toast at arm's length, a fire-charm still sparking in her other hand.\ntext: Smoke pours from the kitchenette. Lyra Vaneshade, pyromancy prodigy, is holding a piece of bread that is now mostly charcoal. \"I can incinerate a target at forty paces,\" she says. \"I cannot make toast. Don't. Say. Anything.\"\n> left: Say absolutely nothing\n  rel lyra +small\n  say: You open a window, take the bread, and make two perfect slices with a toasting fork. She eats hers in dignified silence. Then she asks for another.\n> right: Teach her — gently\n  mood -tiny\n  rel lyra +medium\n  spark lyra +tiny\n  charm +tiny\n  say: \"Less fire,\" you say. \"No, less. Less than that.\" It takes eleven slices. On the twelfth, she makes perfect golden toast and looks prouder than after any duel.\n\n@event lyra_mother_visit\ntitle: Lady Vaneshade\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: lyra\nwhen: rel(lyra) >= 15 and year >= 2\nweight: 12\nart: crown\ntags: social rival\nscene: A tall woman in crimson and black walking through the courtyard like a queen inspecting troops, Lyra two steps behind her, very straight.\ntext: Lady Vaneshade has come to inspect the academy, and her daughter. She stops in front of you. \"You must be the one my daughter mentions,\" she says. \"Constantly. I'd like to know why.\"\n> left: Answer with perfect manners\n  challenge negotiate journeyman +1 if has(origin_noble):\n    win:\n      rel lyra +large\n      renown +small\n      say: You are polite, precise and unimpressed. Lady Vaneshade studies you, then says, \"Hm. Adequate.\" Lyra looks at you like you've slain a dragon.\n    lose:\n      rel lyra +small\n      mood -small\n      say: You stumble over a title. Lady Vaneshade's eyebrow says everything. Later, Lyra finds you. \"She does that to everyone,\" she says. \"You lasted longer than most.\"\n> right: Answer honestly\n  mood -tiny\n  rel lyra +medium\n  if spark(lyra) >= 15:\n    spark lyra +small\n  say: \"Because she's brilliant,\" you say, \"and nobody tells her.\" Lady Vaneshade is silent. Lyra is redder than her hair. The visit ends early.\n\n@event lyra_rival_respect\ntitle: Respect, Grudgingly\nclass: milestone\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: lyra\nwhen: bond(lyra) == \"rival\" and rel(lyra) >= 9\nweight: 16\nart: flame\ntags: rival social\nscene: Two rivals sitting on the edge of a scorched practice ring, wands laid down between them, sharing a flask of water.\ntext: After another draw, Lyra drops onto the edge of the ring beside you. \"You're the only person here who makes me better,\" she says, as if confessing a crime. \"I hate it. I don't want it to stop.\"\n> left: \"Then let's keep it a rivalry.\"\n  rel lyra +medium\n  say: \"Good,\" she says, relieved. \"Rivalry I understand.\" She shoves you, lightly. You shove her back. The rivalry continues, warmer.\n> right: \"Or we could just be friends.\"\n  bond lyra friend\n  rel lyra +large\n  say: She considers the word like an unfamiliar spell. \"Friends,\" she says. \"Fine. But I'm still going to beat you.\" You'd be disappointed if she didn't.\n\n# ---------------- Tobias ----------------\n\n@event tobias_scarecrow\ntitle: The Scarecrow Project\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: tobias\nwhen: rel(tobias) >= 6\nweight: 10\nart: leaf\ntags: social nature\nscene: A scarecrow in a straw hat waving its arms at crows in a vegetable patch, a big farm boy and a student watching it proudly.\ntext: Tobias has built a scarecrow for the greenhouse plots and wants to enchant it to scare crows. \"Just a little life,\" he says. \"Just enough to wave. Nothing creepy.\"\n> left: Help enchant it\n  challenge spell apprentice:\n    win:\n      rel tobias +medium\n      say: The scarecrow waves. It also learns to wave hello, and to tip its hat to the headmistress. The crows are terrified. The first-years adore it.\n    lose:\n      rel tobias +small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: The scarecrow comes to life, looks at you both, and walks off into the forest. You see it sometimes, at dusk, waving.\n> right: Suggest a cat instead\n  rel tobias +small\n  say: You borrow the greenhouse cat. The crows leave immediately. Tobias is a little sad about the scarecrow, so you help him make it a hat.\n\n@event tobias_dance_lessons\ntitle: Two Left Feet\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: tobias\nwhen: rel(tobias) >= 9 and (month == 3 or month == 4)\nweight: 12\nart: music\ntags: social romance\nscene: An empty classroom with desks pushed to the walls, a big boy counting steps aloud, a broom propped against the wall as his former partner.\ntext: Tobias has pushed the desks against the walls of an empty classroom. \"The Ball's {month == 4: tonight|next month},\" he says, mortified. \"I've been practising with a broom. The broom has complained.\"\n> left: Teach him to waltz\n  health -tiny\n  rel tobias +medium\n  spark tobias +tiny\n  charm +tiny\n  say: One-two-three, one-two-three. He steps on you exactly fifty times. On the fifty-first bar, something clicks, and for a moment he's graceful. He looks astonished.\n> right: Tell him nobody cares about footwork\n  rel tobias +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: \"Just enjoy it,\" you say. He thinks about this, nods, and dances like a delighted bear. It's terrible. It's wonderful.\n\n@event tobias_storm_greenhouse\ntime: night\ntitle: Storm Over the Greenhouses\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: tobias\nwhen: rel(tobias) >= 12 and month >= 6 and month <= 8\nweight: 12\nart: bolt\ntags: nature danger\nscene: Rain lashing a glass greenhouse at night, panes cracking, a big boy holding a door shut against the wind while plants rattle inside.\ntext: A storm hits at midnight. Glass is cracking in the greenhouses. Tobias is out there in his nightshirt, trying to hold a door shut against the wind with his whole body. \"The moonflowers!\" he shouts. \"Eleven years!\"\n> left: Run out and help\n  health -small\n  alignment +tiny\n  rel tobias +large\n  spark tobias +tiny\n  say: You hold the panes together with every shielding charm you know until dawn. Every moonflower survives. Tobias hugs you, soaked, laughing, half crying.\n> right: Fetch Old Maud\n  health -tiny\n  rel tobias +small\n  rel maud +small\n  say: Maud arrives in her dressing gown, mutters three words at the sky, and the wind politely goes elsewhere. Tobias stares at her. \"Teach me that,\" he says.\n\n# ---------------- Seraphine ----------------\n\n@event seraphine_lost_day\ntitle: Seraphine Is Tomorrow\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: social\nactor: seraphine\nwhen: rel(seraphine) >= 6\nweight: 10\nart: hourglass\ntags: mystery social\nscene: A girl sitting at the wrong desk in an empty classroom, a teacup in her hands, the calendar on the wall showing the next day's date.\ntext: Seraphine is sitting in an empty classroom, waiting for a lesson that's happening tomorrow. \"I've slipped,\" she says calmly. \"I'm a day ahead. It happens. I need someone to remind me what today is.\"\n> left: Talk her back into today\n  rel seraphine +medium\n  lore +tiny\n  say: You tell her what you had for breakfast, what the weather's like, what Thorne said this morning. Slowly her eyes stop looking through you. \"Oh,\" she says. \"Hello. Today.\"\n> right: Ask her what tomorrow holds\n  chance even:\n    study +small\n    say: \"There's a surprise quiz in every class,\" she says. \"They planned it together. Question three is always a trick.\" It is, every time. You get them all right.\n  else:\n    mood -tiny\n    say: \"It rains,\" she says. \"You get wet. You'll be fine.\" You are not fine. You get very wet.\n\n@event seraphine_painting\ntitle: A Painting of Somewhere Else\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: social\nactor: seraphine\nwhen: rel(seraphine) >= 12\nweight: 10\nart: eye\ntags: social mystery\nscene: A small canvas on an easel showing a stranger's cottage by the sea, a girl with paint on her fingers watching someone look at it.\ntext: Seraphine has painted a small cottage by the sea, with a crooked chimney and a blue door. \"It's yours,\" she says. \"In twelve years. I thought you should see it now. In case you want a different door.\"\n> left: Keep the painting\n  chance likely:\n    mood +small\n    rel seraphine +medium\n    say: You hang it over your bed. Some nights you swear the smoke from the chimney moves. You decide, privately, that you like the blue door.\n  else:\n    rel seraphine +small\n    mood -tiny\n    say: You hang it over your bed. Some mornings the door is a different colour, and you lie there wondering which one you chose.\n> right: Ask her to paint her own future\n  rel seraphine +medium\n  spark seraphine +tiny\n  say: She looks at the blank canvas for a long time. \"I never can,\" she says. \"Seers can't see themselves.\" Then, quietly: \"Unless someone's standing next to us.\"\n\n@event seraphine_mother\ntitle: The Oracle's Summons\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: seraphine\nwhen: rel(seraphine) >= 15 and year >= 2\nweight: 12\nart: letter\ntags: social\nscene: A girl holding a letter that glows faintly silver, standing at the top of a tower stair, looking down as if about to fall or fly.\ntext: Seraphine's mother, the Oracle of Vell, wants her home to take up the family seat. \"I'd spend my life telling kings what they want to hear,\" Seraphine says. \"But she's already seen me say yes. She always sees me say yes.\"\n> left: \"Then surprise her.\"\n  mood -tiny\n  rel seraphine +large\n  say: Seraphine writes back: no. For a whole day she's terrified, and so are you. Then she's giddy. \"I didn't see that coming,\" she keeps saying. \"I didn't see it coming!\"\n> right: \"Maybe she's right.\"\n  rel seraphine -medium\n  say: She nods slowly. She doesn't go — not yet. But for a long while she stops telling you her visions, and you miss them more than you expected.\n\n# ---------------- Corvin ----------------\n\n@event corvin_confiscation_vault\ntitle: The Confiscation Vault\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: corvin\nwhen: rel(corvin) >= 12\nweight: 12\nart: lock\ntags: social danger\nscene: A dark vault crammed with confiscated student contraband — fanged frisbees, cursed dice, a goblet of endless soup — two figures with a lantern at the door.\ntext: \"The confiscation vault,\" Corvin breathes. \"Three hundred years of everything the faculty ever took off a student. My grandfather's lucky deck is in there. I just want it back. Lookout?\"\n> left: Keep lookout\n  alignment -tiny\n  challenge deceive adept-:\n    win:\n      rel corvin +large\n      gold +small\n      say: He comes out with the deck and a handful of confiscated coins \"for your trouble\". The deck is, it turns out, genuinely lucky. He wins at cards for a month.\n    lose:\n      gain detention\n      rel corvin +medium\n      say: The vault alarm is a very loud goblin. You both spend a week cataloguing contraband — which Corvin considers a victory.\n> right: Talk him out of it\n  rel corvin -tiny\n  challenge negotiate vs corvin.guile:\n    win:\n      rel corvin +medium\n      say: You convince him to just ask Thorne. Thorne, astonishingly, gives it back. \"Your grandfather cheated me with it in '41,\" he says. \"Tell him I said hello.\"\n    lose:\n      say: He goes anyway, alone. He comes back with the deck and a black eye from the goblin. Worth it, he says.\n\n@event corvin_brother\ntitle: Corvin's Brother\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: corvin\nwhen: rel(corvin) >= 15 and year >= 2\nweight: 12\nart: mask\ntags: social money\nscene: A rough-looking young man leaning on the academy gate, cap pulled low, Corvin facing him with his hands in his pockets.\ntext: There's a rough-looking man at the gates, and Corvin has gone very still. \"My brother,\" he says. \"He wants money. He always wants money. If I don't pay, he tells the headmistress where I learned to pick locks.\"\n> left: Help Corvin face him down\n  alignment +small\n  challenge negotiate journeyman:\n    win:\n      rel corvin +large\n      say: You stand beside Corvin while he says no — the first time, he tells you later, that he's ever said it. His brother leaves. Corvin sits down on the steps and laughs and shakes.\n    lose:\n      rel corvin +medium\n      mood -small\n      say: It turns ugly. In the end you empty your purse into his hand, just to make him leave. Corvin presses every coin back into yours that evening, with a strange new seriousness.\n> right: Stay out of family business\n  rel corvin -small\n  say: Corvin pays. You see him counting out every coin he has. He's quiet for a long while after.\n\n@event corvin_cards\ntitle: How to Cheat at Cards\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: corvin\nwhen: rel(corvin) >= 6\nweight: 10\nart: cards\ntags: social money\nscene: A candlelit table in a corner of the common room, a boy fanning a deck of cards with a flourish, a student watching his hands very closely.\ntext: \"Rule one: never cheat,\" says Corvin, shuffling so fast the cards blur. \"Rule two: if you're going to cheat, never get caught. Rule three: I'm about to teach you how they cheat, so you never lose to them.\"\n> left: Learn the tricks\n  guile +small\n  rel corvin +small\n  say: By midnight you can spot a marked card, a bottom deal and a mirror charm. You will never be fleeced at Wizard's Hand again.\n> right: Learn to play honestly — and well\n  when: gold >= 3\n  chance likely:\n    lore +tiny\n    rel corvin +small\n    gold +small\n    say: He teaches you odds and nerve instead. That weekend you win eight crowns at the common-room game without cheating. Corvin is weirdly proud.\n  else:\n    gold -tiny\n    lore +tiny\n    rel corvin +small\n    say: He teaches you odds and nerve instead. That weekend the odds take three crowns off you. \"Also a lesson,\" says Corvin.\n\n# ---------------- Mir ----------------\n\n@event mir_ornithopter\ntitle: The Test Flight\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: mir\nwhen: rel(mir) >= 9\nweight: 12\nart: gear\ntags: social danger\nscene: A rickety winged contraption balanced on the edge of a tower roof, a goggled artificer strapping into it, a friend holding a rope.\ntext: Mir has built a flying machine with canvas wings and seven propellers. It is balanced on the edge of the north tower. \"Brooms are so last century,\" they say, strapping in. \"I need someone on the rope. Probably. Possibly.\"\n> left: Hold the rope\n  challenge feat apprentice wild:\n    win:\n      rel mir +large\n      renown +small\n      say: The machine flies — wobbly, loud, glorious — twice around the tower before landing in the lake. Mir surfaces whooping. You're both legends by dinner.\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      rel mir +medium\n      say: The rope wins. You are dragged eight feet along the roof before letting go. The machine lands in a tree. Mir waves from it, delighted.\n> right: Insist on a safety net\n  rel mir +small\n  alchemy +tiny\n  say: You spend a day building a net. The machine flies four feet and drops into it. \"Data!\" says Mir happily. Nobody dies. It's a good day.\n\n@event mir_lab_fire\ntitle: Fire in the Workshop\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: mir\nwhen: rel(mir) >= 12\nweight: 10\nart: flame\ntags: danger\nscene: A workshop full of smoke and orange light, notebooks scattered across a burning bench, a figure in goggles coughing in the doorway.\ntext: The workshop is on fire. Properly this time. Mir is in the doorway, coughing — and trying to go back in. \"My notebooks! Three years of notes!\"\n> left: Grab the notebooks\n  alignment +tiny\n  challenge feat apprentice+ wild:\n    win:\n      rel mir +large\n      health -small\n      say: You come out singed, smoking, with an armful of notebooks. Mir stares at you, then at the notebooks, then bursts into tears and hugs you, both of you coughing.\n    lose:\n      health -large\n      rel mir +medium\n      say: You get halfway before the smoke drives you back. You save one notebook. Mir says it's the most important one. You both know it isn't. They mean it anyway.\n> right: Drag Mir away\n  alignment +tiny\n  rel mir +medium\n  health -tiny\n  say: You haul Mir back from the door. The notebooks burn. Mir is silent for a week. Then they start a new notebook. The first page just says: \"Thank you.\"\n\n@event mir_name_day\ntitle: Mir's Name Day\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: mir\nwhen: rel(mir) >= 15\nweight: 10\nart: gem\ntags: social\nscene: A cluttered workshop decorated with paper chains and one small cake with a single gear-shaped candle, an artificer looking embarrassed and pleased.\ntext: It's Mir's name day. They mention it casually, as if it doesn't matter. It clearly matters. Nobody else seems to know.\n> left: Throw a surprise party\n  gold -small\n  rel mir +large\n  mood +small\n  say: Six friends, one cake and a banner you painted badly. Mir walks in, stops, and has to sit down. \"Nobody's ever...\" they start, and can't finish.\n> right: Give them a handmade gift\n  rel mir +medium\n  spark mir +tiny\n  say: A little brass key that fits nothing. \"For whatever you build next,\" you say. Mir wears it on a chain, every day, for years.\n\n# ---------------- Nessa ----------------\n\n@event nessa_headmistress_interview\ntitle: The Impossible Interview\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: nessa\nwhen: rel(nessa) >= 9 and (met(isolde) and rel(isolde) >= 6)\nweight: 12\nart: paper\ntags: social\nscene: A newspaper editor and a student waiting outside the headmistress's tower door, the editor clutching a list of questions three pages long.\ntext: \"Nobody has interviewed the headmistress in twenty years,\" Nessa says. \"But she likes you. Probably. Get me in the door and I'll name my firstborn after you.\"\n> left: Ask the headmistress\n  rel isolde -tiny\n  challenge negotiate journeyman +1 if renown >= 25:\n    win:\n      rel nessa +large\n      renown +medium\n      say: The headmistress agrees to ten minutes. She gives Nessa forty, and gives you a look that says you have spent a favour. The interview runs on the front page and sells out three printings.\n    lose:\n      rel nessa +small\n      say: \"No,\" says the headmistress, pleasantly. \"But tell Miss Quill her piece on the kitchens was excellent.\" Nessa frames the rejection. The headmistress is a shade cooler with you in the corridors after that.\n> right: Suggest someone overlooked instead\n  rel nessa +medium\n  charm +tiny\n  say: Nessa interviews the night porter instead. It's the most-read piece of the year. Half the academy cries over breakfast.\n\n@event nessa_typo\ntime: morning\ntitle: A Catastrophic Typo\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: nessa\nwhen: rel(nessa) >= 6 and met(thorne)\nweight: 10\nart: paper\ntags: social\nscene: A newspaper with a single word circled in red ink, an editor with her head in her hands, a stack of papers still warm from the press.\ntext: The Ledger has gone to print. In the profile of Professor Thorne, a single wrong letter has turned \"Thorne: Still Lively\" into \"Thorne: Still Lovely\". By breakfast the whole academy will be calling the most frightening man in the castle lovely. Five hundred copies.\n> left: Help her fix every copy overnight\n  health -tiny\n  rel nessa +medium\n  say: You correct five hundred copies with a scraping charm and a lot of tea. At dawn, Nessa falls asleep on the press. Thorne never knows.\n> right: Tell her to run a correction and own it\n  mood -tiny\n  rel nessa +small\n  rel thorne +small\n  say: The correction is gracious. Thorne, astonishingly, cuts it out and pins it to his door. \"Precision,\" he says in class, \"is a virtue. So is apology.\"\n\n@event nessa_burnout\ntime: evening night\ntitle: The Editor Sleeps\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: nessa\nwhen: rel(nessa) >= 12 and year >= 2\nweight: 10\nart: candle\ntags: social\nscene: A girl asleep at a desk in a newspaper office, her self-writing quill still scratching on its own, the candle burned down to a stub.\ntext: Nessa hasn't slept properly in a week. She's asleep at her desk now, and her quill — unsupervised — is still writing next week's paper. It has started inventing news.\n> left: Put her to bed and run the paper yourself\n  health -tiny\n  rel nessa +large\n  study -small\n  say: You edit, typeset and print one issue, and miss a day of classes doing it. It's adequate. When Nessa wakes she reads it, winces at your headlines, and hugs you for a long time.\n> right: Wake her and make her rest\n  mood -tiny\n  rel nessa +medium\n  say: She argues for ten minutes, then sleeps for fourteen hours. The quill's invented news about a dragon in the kitchens turns out to be true.\n\n# ---------------- Kael ----------------\n\n@event kael_northern_lights\ntime: night\ntitle: The Lights of the North\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: kael\nwhen: rel(kael) >= 12 and (month <= 5 or month >= 12)\nweight: 12\nart: star\ntags: social romance\nscene: Two figures on a frozen hillside under ribbons of green and violet light, one pointing upward, frost forming a circle around them.\ntext: Kael wakes you at midnight. \"Come,\" he says. On the hill above the lake, the sky is moving — green and violet ribbons, the lights of his home, here, for him. \"I called them,\" he says. \"I wanted you to see.\"\n> left: Watch in silence\n  health -tiny\n  rel kael +small\n  mood +medium\n  say: You watch until the lights fade. Neither of you speaks. On the walk down, he takes your hand without seeming to notice he's done it.\n> right: Ask him about home\n  health -tiny\n  rel kael +medium\n  lore +small\n  say: He tells you about ice palaces and singing glaciers and a mother who never once said she was proud. Then he says: \"This place is more home now.\" He means the hill. He means you.\n  spark kael +tiny\n\n@event kael_fever\ntitle: Kael Burns\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: kael\nwhen: rel(kael) >= 9\nweight: 10\nart: rain\ntags: danger social\nscene: A pale boy in a narrow bed, sweating, frost forming and melting on the pillow around his head in a constant cycle.\ntext: Kael has a fever — and for someone made of frost, a fever is dangerous. The infirmary's warming charms are making it worse. Sister Amaranth looks frightened. \"He needs cold,\" she says. \"Real cold. I don't know where to get it.\"\n> left: Bring him snow from the mountain\n  alignment +tiny\n  challenge feat journeyman+:\n    win:\n      rel kael +large\n      health -medium\n      say: You fly to the peaks on a borrowed broom and come back with a sack of ancient snow. By morning his fever breaks. He doesn't let go of your sleeve all day.\n    lose:\n      health -large\n      rel kael +medium\n      say: The mountain wins. You come back half-frozen and empty-handed. Kael recovers anyway, slowly — and visits you in the next bed.\n> right: Send word to Ilse\n  if met(ilse) and not flag(ilse_taken) and not flag(ilse_went_north):\n    rel kael +medium\n    rel ilse +medium\n    say: Ilse arrives in a whirl of snow, puts her hand on his forehead and says something sharp in Northern. The fever breaks. Kael stares at his sister for a long time.\n  else:\n    rel kael +small\n    say: You can't find her. Kael recovers slowly, on his own, the way he's always done everything.\n\n@event kael_letter_home\ntitle: A Letter He Won't Send\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: kael\nwhen: rel(kael) >= 15 and flag(kael_told)\nweight: 10\nart: letter\ntags: social\nscene: A boy at a library desk, a letter half-written in sharp Northern script, a candle beside it, ink frozen in the pen.\ntext: Kael is writing a letter. He's rewritten the first line eleven times. \"To my mother,\" he says. \"I'm telling her I won't come home. I won't open the door. I'm telling her I've found...\" He stops.\n> left: \"Found what?\"\n  rel kael +medium\n  spark kael +tiny\n  say: He looks at you for a long time. Then he writes the last word of the sentence without showing you, seals it, and sends it. He's lighter for days.\n> right: Help him find the words\n  health -tiny\n  rel kael +medium\n  charm +tiny\n  say: You sit together until the candle's gone. The letter, when it's finished, is short. It doesn't apologise once. He sends it at dawn.\n\n# ---------------- Bram ----------------\n\n@event bram_father_visit\ntitle: Bram's Father\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: bram\nwhen: flag(bram_softened) and rel(bram) >= 9\nweight: 14\nart: shield\ntags: social\nscene: A towering grey-haired man in an old head-boy's sash speaking down to a red-faced prefect in the entrance hall, students hurrying past pretending not to look.\ntext: A tall man in an old head-boy's sash is speaking to Bram in the entrance hall, quietly and terribly. Bram has shrunk two sizes. \"...never amount to anything,\" the man is saying.\n> left: Interrupt — say something good\n  challenge negotiate apprentice+:\n    win:\n      rel bram +large\n      say: You tell his father {seen(bram_turns): about the night at the boathouse|what Bram is like when nobody's keeping score}. The man falls silent. Bram looks at you like you've handed him a sword.\n    lose:\n      rel bram +medium\n      say: His father looks through you as if you're furniture. But afterwards, Bram finds you and says, roughly, \"Nobody's ever stood there before. Thanks.\"\n> right: Find Bram afterwards\n  mood -tiny\n  rel bram +medium\n  say: He's behind the stables, again. You sit on the bucket next to his. You don't talk. After a while he says, \"Same time next year, probably,\" and almost laughs.\n\n@event bram_cheating\ntitle: Bram Cheats\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: bram\nwhen: met(bram) and (month == 4 or month == 10)\nweight: 10\nart: scroll\ntags: rival study\nscene: A prefect in an exam hall slipping a tiny scroll out of his sleeve, a student two desks away watching.\ntext: In the exam hall, two desks away, you see Bram Hollis — prefect, enforcer of rules — slip a tiny scroll out of his sleeve. He sees you seeing him. His face goes white.\n> left: Report him\n  rel bram -huge\n  rel isolde +small\n  renown +small\n  alignment +small\n  say: Bram loses his sash for the rest of the year. Some students cheer. You don't feel like cheering. Neither, later, does he.\n> right: Say nothing — and let him know you saw\n  alignment -tiny\n  rel bram +medium\n  guile +small\n  say: After the exam you just look at him. He nods, once. You never mention it. But his demerits stop, for you and for everyone you know.\n"},{"path":"content/events/expansion_social.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Expansion — more generic relationship events (actor: any).\n# ============================================================\n\n@event soc_lost_notes\ntitle: The Borrowed Notes\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ncast:\n  npc: any where (tagged(npc, student) or tagged(npc, prefect)) and rel(npc) >= 3\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nrepeat: 8\nart: scroll\ntags: social study\nscene: A student holding a single soggy page of notes, the rest of the stack floating away down a stream behind them.\ntext: {npc} borrowed all your lecture notes last week. {They} {are} now standing in front of you holding a single soggy page. \"There was a stream,\" {they} say{s}. \"And a duck. Mostly a duck.\"\n> left: Laugh it off\n  rel npc +medium\n  study -tiny\n  say: You rewrite your notes from memory. {npc} brings you a cake every day for a week, by way of apology. The cakes are better than the notes were.\n> right: Make {them} rewrite them\n  rel npc -tiny\n  study +tiny\n  say: {npc} copies out forty pages from a friend's notes, grumbling. The new notes are neater than yours ever were.\n\n@event soc_birthday\ntitle: A Birthday\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ncast:\n  npc: any where tagged(npc, peer) and rel(npc) >= 9\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nrepeat: 12\nart: gem\ntags: social festival\nscene: A small cake with a single candle in a dark common room, a group of students crouched behind a sofa, a surprised face in the doorway.\ntext: {&You find out — by accident — that it's {npc}'s birthday. {They} haven't told anyone. {They} {are} spending it in the library, alone, on purpose.|It's {npc}'s birthday again, and again {they} haven't told anyone. {They} {are} in the library, alone, as if you might have forgotten.}\n> left: Organise a surprise\n  gold -small\n  rel npc +large\n  mood +small\n  say: {&Seven people jump out from behind a sofa. {npc} screams, then laughs, then has to sit down. \"How did you know?\" You'll never tell.|This year it's a cake shaped, badly, like the library. {npc} pretends to be annoyed for nearly a minute.}\n> right: Join {them} in the library\n  rel npc +medium\n  lore +tiny\n  say: {&You sit down opposite {them} with your own book.|You take the same seat as last year, with your own book.} Neither of you says happy birthday. At midnight {they} slide{s} a biscuit across the table, and you both grin.\n\n@event soc_bad_news\ntitle: Bad News From Home\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ncast:\n  npc: any where (tagged(npc, student) or tagged(npc, prefect)) and rel(npc) >= 12\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nrepeat: 12\nart: letter\ntags: social\nscene: A student sitting on a stone staircase with a crumpled letter, head bowed, a friend sitting down beside them.\ntext: {npc} is sitting on the stairs with a letter in {their} lap, not moving. {Their} grandmother is ill. The barge home takes two days. \"I don't know whether to go,\" {they} say{s}.\n> left: \"Go. We'll cover your classes.\"\n  rel npc +large\n  study -tiny\n  say: {npc} catches the next barge. Every evening you copy out the day's notes for {them} from {their} classmates. When {npc} comes back, {their} grandmother is better, and {they} hug{s} you on the landing.\n> right: Just sit with {them}\n  rel npc +medium\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You sit there until the stairs move under you. You don't fix anything. That's not what {they} needed.\n\n@event soc_stargazing\ntitle: Counting Stars\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ncast:\n  npc: any where romanceable(npc) and rel(npc) >= 12 and bond(npc) != \"partner\"\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nrepeat: 8\nart: star\ntags: social romance\nscene: Two students lying on their backs on a flat roof, close but not quite touching, a sky full of stars above.\ntext: {&{npc} has dragged a blanket up to the roof.|{npc} is on the roof again with the blanket, and a flask this time.|{npc} has found a better spot on the roof, out of the wind, and a better blanket.} \"There's a meteor shower,\" {they} say{s}. \"Supposedly.\" There's room on the blanket for two, just about.\n> left: Count meteors\n  health -tiny\n  rel npc +medium\n  mood +small\n  say: You count thirty-one. {npc} counts thirty-four and accuses you of not looking properly. You weren't, entirely. You stay until the cold drives you in at dawn.\n> right: Talk about everything else\n  health -tiny\n  spark npc +small\n  rel npc +small\n  say: You don't see a single meteor. You talk about home and fear and what you'd do if you could do anything. When you look up, the sky's gone grey with dawn.\n\n@event soc_jealous_friend\ntitle: Jealousy\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ncast:\n  npc: any where tagged(npc, peer) and (bond(npc) == \"friend\" or bond(npc) == \"close\")\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nwhen: known() >= 4\nrepeat: 10\nart: mask\ntags: social\nscene: A student standing alone at the edge of a laughing group, arms folded, watching someone else take what used to be their seat.\ntext: {~{npc} has been odd all week. Finally it comes out.|{npc} has been short with you at every meal. Finally, on the stairs, it comes out.|{npc} cancels on you twice and then turns up at your door. It comes out in a rush.} \"You've got all these new friends now. I just — I don't know where I fit anymore.\"\n> left: Reassure {them}\n  rel npc +medium\n  charm +tiny\n  say: You tell {them} exactly where {they} fit: right here, first. {npc} rolls {their} eyes, but {they}'re smiling. The oddness is gone by dinner.\n> right: Tell {them} friendship isn't a contest\n  rel npc -tiny\n  lore +tiny\n  say: You're right, technically. {npc} knows it. It still lands badly. It takes a few weeks for things to feel easy again. You spend the evenings you'd have spent soothing {them} in the library instead.\n\n@event soc_teach_spell\ntitle: A Trick Worth Learning\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ncast:\n  npc: any where tagged(npc, peer) and rel(npc) >= 7\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nrepeat: 8\nart: wand\ntags: social magic\nscene: Two students in an empty classroom, one demonstrating a wand movement, the other copying it with a tiny spark of light.\ntext: {npc} has a spell {they} invented — a little thing, half-useful, half-ridiculous — and wants to teach it to someone. {&\"It makes your tea exactly the right temperature,\" {they} say{s}. \"Forever.\"|\"It finds the page you were reading,\" {they} say{s}. \"Any book, anywhere.\"|\"It keeps your socks dry,\" {they} say{s}. \"Lake, rain, puddles. Dry.\"}\n> left: Learn it properly\n  chance likely:\n    arcana +small\n    rel npc +small\n    say: It takes an hour. {&Your tea is now perfect, always.|You will never lose your page again.|Your socks are dry. Your socks will always be dry.} You'll use this spell more than anything you learn in class.\n  else:\n    health -tiny\n    rel npc +small\n    say: {&The first attempt freezes your tea solid, and your eyebrows with it. The second works. Your eyebrows take a day to thaw.|The first attempt finds every page in the library at once. The second works. The librarian is not amused.|The first attempt dries your socks, your shoes and most of your feet. The second works. Your skin flakes for a day.}\n> right: Teach {them} one of yours in return\n  rel npc +medium\n  charm +tiny\n  say: You trade spells like secrets. By midnight you've both got a new trick and an inside joke that will last three years.\n\n@event soc_secret_admirer\ntitle: A Secret Admirer\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ncast:\n  npc: any where romanceable(npc) and spark(npc) >= 15 and bond(npc) != \"partner\"\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nart: letter\ntags: romance\nscene: A folded note tucked into a library book with a pressed flower, unsigned, the handwriting careful and slightly shaky.\ntext: Folded notes keep appearing in your library books. Unsigned. They're funny, and a little nervous, and they know things about you that only someone paying attention would know. The handwriting looks familiar.\n> left: Leave a note back\n  spark npc +small\n  say: You write back. The notes get longer. One day the last line just says: \"It's {npc}. You knew, didn't you?\" You did.\n> right: Stake out the library\n  challenge deceive apprentice:\n    win:\n      spark npc +medium\n      rel npc +small\n      say: You catch {npc} red-handed, mid-tuck. {They} turn{s} the colour of a pyromancy exam. You both laugh until the librarian throws you out.\n    lose:\n      spark npc +tiny\n      say: You wait three nights and catch nobody. On the fourth morning the note says: \"Nice try.\"\n\n@event soc_injured_friend\ntitle: Carried\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ncast:\n  npc: any where tagged(npc, peer) and rel(npc) >= 6\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nrepeat: 10\nart: cross\ntags: social danger\nscene: A student carrying another piggyback across a muddy lawn, the injured one's ankle wrapped in a scarf.\ntext: {npc} has twisted an ankle on the far side of the grounds, a long way from the infirmary, and it's getting dark. {They} {are} trying very hard to pretend it doesn't hurt.\n> left: Carry {them} on your back\n  vigor +small\n  rel npc +medium\n  health -tiny\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: It's a long way. {npc} narrates the whole journey like a horse race. By the time you reach the infirmary you're both laughing too hard to breathe.\n> right: Run for help\n  rel npc +small\n  say: You run for Sister Amaranth, who arrives with a floating stretcher and a lot of opinions. {npc} is fine. {They} send{s} you a thank-you sweet.\n\n@event soc_rival_decency\ntitle: An Unexpected Kindness\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ncast:\n  npc: any where tagged(npc, peer) and (bond(npc) == \"rival\" or bond(npc) == \"nemesis\" or rel(npc) <= -9)\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nrepeat: 10\nart: hand\ntags: rival social\nscene: A student picking up a rival's scattered books from a wet floor, the rival frozen mid-step, not sure what to say.\ntext: {&You drop your books in the rain, and the first person to stoop and help pick them up is {npc}.|You slip on the wet stairs, and the hand that catches your elbow is {npc}'s.|The wind off the lake takes your scarf, and {npc} is the one who runs after it.} {They} hand{s} it back without a word, looking as surprised as you are.\n> left: Thank {them} properly\n  rel npc +large\n  say: \"Don't make it a thing,\" {npc} mutters. But the next day {they} nod{s} to you in the corridor. {&Something has thawed.|It's getting harder to stay enemies.|You're not sure what you are to each other anymore.}\n> right: Suspect a trick\n  rel npc -tiny\n  guile +tiny\n  say: You check for hexes. There aren't any. {npc} saw you checking. You'll both remember that.\n\n@event soc_partner_anniversary\ntitle: An Anniversary\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ncast:\n  npc: any where bond(npc) == \"partner\" and spark(npc) >= 40\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nrepeat: 12\nart: ring\ntags: romance\nscene: Two people on a bench by the lake at sunset, one holding out a small wrapped box, the other's hands over their mouth.\ntext: It's been a while now — long enough that {npc} has started calling things \"ours\". Today {they} hand{s} you a small wrapped box and won't meet your eyes.\n> left: Open it now\n  spark npc +small\n  mood +medium\n  say: Inside: a pebble from the lakeshore, polished to a shine, from the day you first really talked. \"It's stupid,\" {npc} says. It isn't. You carry it everywhere, and spend the next day hunting for something half as good to give back.\n> right: Give {them} yours first\n  gold -small\n  spark npc +medium\n  mood +medium\n  say: You'd had the same idea, and spent most of your purse on it. You both open your boxes at once. They're nearly the same gift. You laugh until the sun goes down.\n"},{"path":"content/events/expansion_summer.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Expansion — Summer recess (months 11–12).\n#  `has(summer_away)` = went home / travelled; otherwise you're\n#  at the near-empty academy or working in the village.\n# ============================================================\n\n@event sum_empty_castle\ntitle: The Empty Castle\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nwhen: month >= 11\nplan: castle\nweight: 16\nart: tower\ntags: mystery home\nscene: A vast empty dining hall in summer, chairs upturned on the tables, a single student eating alone in a shaft of dusty sunlight.\ntext: In summer the castle breathes differently. Corridors you've walked a thousand times are silent; doors that are always locked stand slightly open. A portrait yawns. Nobody is watching.\n> left: Explore the open doors\n  challenge deceive apprentice:\n    win:\n      lore +medium\n      gold +small\n      alignment -tiny\n      say: Behind one door: a forgotten trophy room. Behind another: a professor's stash of very old toffee and a purse of lost-property coins nobody will miss. You pocket it anyway.\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      say: Behind the first door: the caretaker, in a dressing gown, very surprised. You both agree this never happened.\n> right: Enjoy the quiet\n  health +small\n  say: You read in window seats, nap on the great lawn and eat breakfast at noon. For two weeks the most famous academy in the kingdom is just a big, peaceful house.\n\n@event sum_home_chores\ntitle: Help Around the House\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: month >= 11 and not has(origin_noble)\nplan: home\nweight: 14\nart: house\ntags: home\nscene: A farmhouse kitchen in high summer, a basket of laundry, a list of chores pinned to the door, a textbook hidden under a tea towel.\ntext: Home is lovely for about three days. Then the list appears on the kitchen door: the roof, the goats, the fence, the cousin's wedding. Your summer reading list is hidden under a tea towel.\n> left: Do the chores — with magic\n  challenge spell apprentice-:\n    win:\n      mood +small\n      renown +small\n      say: The fence mends itself, the goats are briefly levitated, the roof is better than new. The whole street comes to watch. Your mother pretends not to be proud.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      say: The goats are briefly levitated. Unfortunately so is the fence. Your family is very patient about it.\n> right: Do them the slow way, and talk\n  health -tiny\n  mood +small\n  vigor +small\n  say: You fix the fence by hand with your father, saying very little and then, suddenly, a lot. It's the best conversation you've had all year.\n\n@event sum_old_friend\ntitle: An Old Friend\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: month >= 11\nplan: home\nweight: 12\nart: group\ntags: social home\nscene: Two young people sitting on a stone wall above a village, one in academy robes, one in work clothes, a little space between them.\ntext: Your oldest friend from home is waiting at the crossroads, like always. But something's different. \"So,\" they say, not quite looking at you, \"I suppose you're too grand for us now.\"\n> left: Prove you're not\n  mood +medium\n  charm +tiny\n  say: You spend the summer exactly as you used to — orchard raids, river swims, terrible jokes. By Starfall, the space between you on the wall is gone.\n> right: Admit you've changed\n  lore +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: \"I have,\" you say. \"So have you.\" They laugh, surprised. You talk all night about who you're both becoming. It's different from before. It might be better.\n\n@event sum_midsummer_fair\ntime: afternoon\ntitle: The Midsummer Fair\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: (month == 11 or month == 12) and gold >= 3\nweight: 12\nart: tent\ntags: festival money\nscene: A village green full of striped tents, a carousel of enchanted wooden animals, lanterns strung between trees, a strongman lifting a cow.\ntext: The village Midsummer Fair: enchanted carousel, ring-toss, a strongman, and a booth promising \"GUESS YOUR WEIGHT IN CHEESE\". You have a few crowns in your pocket and a whole afternoon.\n> left: Win something at the ring-toss\n  challenge feat apprentice+ wild:\n    win:\n      gold +medium\n      mood +small\n      say: Three rings, three hits. You win a stuffed griffon the size of a sheep and sell it to a crying child's father for fifteen crowns. Everyone wins.\n    lose:\n      gold -tiny\n      mood +tiny\n      say: Every ring bounces off. The ring-toss is definitely enchanted. You don't care; you're eating candied apples.\n> right: Ride the carousel\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +medium\n  say: Your wooden hippogriff takes off, very slightly, at the top of every turn. You ride it nine times.\n\n@event sum_letter_from_friend\ntitle: A Summer Letter\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: letter\ncast:\n  npc: any where (tagged(npc, student) or tagged(npc, prefect)) and rel(npc) >= 12\nper: npc\nwhen: month >= 11\nart: letter\ntags: social\nscene: A thick letter with a doodle on the envelope arriving by owl at a summer window.\ntext: An owl arrives with a thick, slightly crumpled letter from {npc}. Six pages of summer news, two doodles, one pressed flower, and at the end, in smaller writing: \"I miss the castle. I miss you, actually.\"\n> left: Write a short reply\n  rel npc +small\n  say: You write a page. It's warm, if brief. {npc} keeps it anyway.\n> right: Write back for hours\n  health -tiny\n  rel npc +medium\n  mood +small\n  say: You write until the candle gutters. Twelve pages. You can't remember the last time you wrote that much that wasn't an essay.\n\n@event sum_rain_week\ntitle: A Week of Rain\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: month >= 11\nweight: 10\nart: rain\ntags: home\nscene: Rain streaming down a tall window, a student curled in an armchair with a fat novel, a teapot steaming on the sill.\ntext: It rains for a solid week. Not dramatic rain: just steady, grey, endless rain. Every plan you made for the summer is quietly drowning.\n> left: Read a very long novel\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +small\n  lore +tiny\n  say: You buy an eight-hundred-page novel about a family of sea-witches, read every word and cry at the end. It's the best week of the summer.\n> right: Practise spells indoors\n  arcana +small\n  say: You practise until you can light every candle in the room with a flick. You also set fire to one curtain. It's raining; it goes out quickly.\n\n@event sum_sea_serpent\ntitle: Fishing With an Uncle\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: month >= 11\nplan: home\nweight: 8\nart: wave\ntags: nature danger\nscene: A small fishing boat on a grey-green sea, a fishing line drawn taut, something very long and scaled rising beside the hull.\ntext: Your uncle takes you fishing. Something takes the bait. Something very large. The line sings. The boat tilts. A long scaled neck rises out of the water and looks at you with polite interest.\n> left: Cut the line\n  mood +tiny\n  say: The serpent sinks back with a sigh like a disappointed whale. Your uncle tells the story for the rest of his life, and it gets bigger every time.\n> right: Try to talk to it\n  challenge negotiate adept- wild:\n    win:\n      lore +medium\n      renown +small\n      say: You greet it in the old courtesies {met(brask): Professor Brask taught you|from a book you once read and never expected to need}. It bows its great head, leaves a pearl on the deck and swims away. Your uncle faints.\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      say: The serpent is not interested in conversation. It is interested in the boat. You swim home.\n\n@event sum_reading_list\ntitle: The Summer Reading List\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: month == 11 and not wrapup\nweight: 12\nart: scroll\ntags: study\nscene: A long scroll unrolling off a table and across the floor, a list of titles in tiny handwriting, a student staring at it in despair.\ntext: The summer reading list arrives by owl. It is a scroll. It unrolls across the floor, out of the door and partway down the stairs. Thorne has added a note at the bottom: \"Optional. (It is not optional.)\"\n> left: Read all of it\n  lore +medium\n  lore +small\n  mood -small\n  say: You read everything. When term starts, Thorne asks a question about the last book and you're the only one who can answer. His eyebrow rises half a millimetre.\n> right: Read the first and last chapters of each\n  guile +tiny\n  lore +tiny\n  say: A time-honoured strategy. You can talk confidently about the beginning and end of forty books. The middle remains a mystery, like most of life.\n\n@event sum_night_market\nslot: night\ntitle: The Night Market\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nwhen: month >= 11 and gold >= 15\nweight: 10\nart: lantern\ntags: money mystery\nscene: A market that only appears after midnight, stalls lit with coloured flames, hooded sellers, a bottle that seems to contain a small thundercloud.\ntext: A night market appears in a field outside the village — it wasn't there yesterday and won't be tomorrow. A hooded stall-keeper offers you a stoppered bottle with a tiny thundercloud inside. \"Fifteen crowns. What it does is a surprise.\"\n> left: Buy the bottle\n  gold -medium\n  chance even:\n    gain inspired\n    arcana +small\n    say: You uncork it in your room. The little storm rains ideas on you for a fortnight. Your notebooks overflow.\n  else:\n    mood -tiny\n    gain sick\n    say: You uncork it in your room. The little storm rains on you. Just rain. For a fortnight. You catch a cold.\n> right: Just look around\n  health -tiny\n  mood +small\n  lore +tiny\n  say: You wander until dawn, buying nothing, watching everything. When the sun rises the field is empty except for a single glowing feather. You keep it.\n\n@event sum_end_of_summer\ntitle: The Last Week of Summer\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: month == 12\nweight: 5\nart: sun\ntags: home\nscene: A student packing a trunk in golden late-summer light, a half-finished jar of jam, a pressed flower and a new textbook on the bed.\ntext: The last week of summer has that particular light — golden, heavy, already nostalgic. Your trunk is half-packed. Part of you is desperate to get back. Part of you wants this week to last forever.\n> left: Squeeze every drop out of it\n  mood +medium\n  health +tiny\n  say: Late swims, long dinners, one final sunset from the hill. You arrive at the barge sunburnt and happy and only slightly late.\n> right: Get ready for the new year\n  gain inspired\n  lore +tiny\n  say: You re-read your notes, sharpen your quills and polish your wand. When term starts you are, for once, ahead. It's an unfamiliar feeling.\n"},{"path":"content/events/expansion_y3.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Expansion — Final year: careers, last times, farewells.\n#  career_* flags (set by the Career Fair) + career_accepted\n#  feed two endings: ending_teacher, ending_wanderer.\n# ============================================================\n\n@event y3_career_fair\ntitle: The Career Fair\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: final_year and month == 2\npriority: 42\ntype: menu\nart: scroll\ntags: study story\nchain: careers\nscene: The great hall lined with recruiters' booths — a Conclave mage in silver, a librarian behind a wall of books, a circus ringmaster juggling fire.\ntext: Every guild, order and institution in the kingdom has sent someone to the great hall. They are all very polite. They are all, very clearly, shopping. Where will you leave your name?\n> conclave: The Conclave of the Seven Towers\n  hint: Apprentice archmages. The interview tests your Arcana.\n  flag career_conclave\n  queue y3_interview now\n  say: You leave your name with the mage in silver, who writes it down without looking up. The interview is set.\n> library: The Royal Library\n  hint: Keepers of every book ever written. The interview tests your Lore.\n  flag career_library\n  queue y3_interview now\n  say: The librarian takes your name and asks what you're reading at the moment. You answer. The interview is set.\n> guild: The Guild of Alchemists\n  hint: Potions, profit, explosions. The interview tests your Alchemy.\n  flag career_guild\n  queue y3_interview now\n  say: The alchemist at the guild's booth shakes your hand and checks it for burns. The interview is set.\n> unseen: The Crown's Office of Unseen Affairs\n  hint: They found you, actually. The interview tests your Guile.\n  flag career_unseen\n  queue y3_interview now\n  say: A woman nobody else seems to notice takes your name and tells you where to be. The interview is set.\n> circus: The Travelling Circus of Wonders\n  hint: Wagons, crowds, illusions. The interview tests your Charm.\n  flag career_circus\n  queue y3_interview now\n  say: The ringmaster juggles your name into the air, catches it and writes it down. The interview is set.\n> teaching: Stay at Lumenhold as a junior teacher\n  hint: Needs a faculty member who trusts you: Thorne, Maud or the Headmistress.\n  when: rel(thorne) >= 15 or rel(maud) >= 15 or rel(isolde) >= 15\n  locked: Nobody on the faculty knows you well enough to vouch for you.\n  flag career_teaching\n  queue y3_interview now\n  say: Your old professor puts your name forward to the faculty, with a few words you don't hear. The interview is set.\n> none: Not yet — you'd rather not decide\n  mood +tiny\n  if month < 4:\n    queue y3_career_fair in 1\n  say: You collect a tote bag, three pamphlets and a free quill. {month < 4: The future can wait one more month; the fair is back next month.|The fair packs up for good. The future will have to come and find you.}\n\n@event y3_interview\ntitle: The Interview\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: queued\nart: scales\ntags: study story\nchain: careers\nscene: A small panelled room, a long table, three interviewers and one empty chair facing them.\ntext: Three people behind a long table. One takes notes; one smiles too much; one says nothing at all, which is clearly the one who decides. \"Tell us,\" says the smiling one, \"why you.\"\n> left: Let your record speak\n  iv_score = 0\n  challenge research adept:\n    win:\n      iv_score += 1\n      queue y3_interview_practical now\n      say: You talk about what you've learned and, more importantly, what you got wrong and fixed. The silent one writes a single word. You suspect it's \"yes\".\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      queue y3_interview_practical now\n      say: You recite your achievements like a shopping list. The silent one looks at the clock. \"Now,\" says the smiling one, \"a small practical.\"\n> right: Tell them a story\n  iv_score = 0\n  challenge negotiate adept wild:\n    win:\n      iv_score += 1\n      queue y3_interview_practical now\n      say: You tell them about the night the lake froze, or the night you caught a star, or the night you were wrong. The silent one laughs. Out loud.\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      queue y3_interview_practical now\n      say: The story goes on too long and ends in the wrong place. Somewhere in the middle you lost them, and yourself. \"Now,\" says the smiling one, \"a small practical.\"\n\n@event y3_interview_practical\ntitle: The Practical\nclass: story\ntone: tense\ntrigger: queued\nart: scales\ntags: study story\nchain: careers\nscene: A plain table in a panelled room set with a single task, three interviewers watching a student roll up their sleeves.\ntext: The note-taker sets a task on the table. {flag(career_conclave): A ward with a flaw in it, somewhere.|{flag(career_library): A crumbling manuscript in three hands and no catalogue.|{flag(career_guild): A cauldron, a recipe with one ingredient missing, and a clock.|{flag(career_unseen): A sealed letter, and the request to read it without breaking the seal.|{flag(career_circus): An empty stage, a sceptical crowd of three, and five minutes.|A class of imaginary first-years, and a lesson to give them.}}}}} \"In your own time,\" says the smiling one, and turns over an hourglass.\n> left: Do it properly\n  if flag(career_conclave):\n    challenge spell expert drift low:\n      win:\n        iv_score += 1\n        say: You find the flaw, name it and close it, and the ward rings like a struck glass. The silent one leans forward for the first time.\n      lose:\n        mood -small\n        say: The flaw finds you first. The ward fizzes out in your hands, and the note-taker writes for a long time.\n  elif flag(career_library):\n    challenge research expert drift low:\n      win:\n        iv_score += 1\n        say: Three hands, three centuries, one lost chapter. You date them all before the sand runs out. The silent one nods, once.\n      lose:\n        mood -small\n        say: You date the second hand a century wrong, and know it the moment you say it. The note-taker writes for a long time.\n  elif flag(career_guild):\n    challenge brew expert drift low:\n      win:\n        iv_score += 1\n        say: You find the missing ingredient by its absence and the cauldron settles to a perfect simmer. Someone behind the table sniffs, approvingly.\n      lose:\n        mood -small\n        say: The cauldron spits and turns a colour no recipe asks for. The note-taker writes for a long time.\n  elif flag(career_unseen):\n    challenge deceive expert drift low:\n      win:\n        iv_score += 1\n        say: Steam, a thin blade, a little patience. You read it, reseal it and hand it back. The silent one smiles, which is somehow worse.\n      lose:\n        mood -small\n        say: The seal cracks with a sound like a shot. The silent one does not look surprised. The note-taker writes for a long time.\n  elif flag(career_circus):\n    challenge negotiate expert drift low:\n      win:\n        iv_score += 1\n        say: Five minutes of nonsense and wonder, and the crowd of three are on their feet. One of them is the silent one.\n      lose:\n        mood -small\n        say: The crowd of three stays seated, arms folded, for all five minutes. The note-taker writes for a long time.\n  else:\n    challenge negotiate using lore expert drift low:\n      win:\n        iv_score += 1\n        say: You teach the empty chairs the thing you most wish someone had taught you, and forget for a while that they're empty. The silent one nods.\n      lose:\n        mood -small\n        say: You lose the imaginary class in the first minute, which you hadn't known was possible. The note-taker writes for a long time.\n  queue y3_interview_question now\n> right: Ask questions before you start\n  mood -tiny\n  iv_score += 1\n  if iv_score >= 2:\n    say: You ask three good questions, then do the task carefully and well. It's slower than they'd like. The silent one doesn't seem to mind.\n  else:\n    iv_score -= 1\n    say: You ask three good questions and run out of sand before you've finished. \"Careful,\" says the smiling one, in a tone that isn't quite praise.\n  queue y3_interview_question now\n\n@event y3_interview_question\ntitle: The Silent One's Question\nclass: story\ntone: tense\ntrigger: queued\nart: scales\ntags: study story\nchain: careers\nscene: A small panelled room, an interviewer who hasn't spoken leaning forward over folded hands, a student on the other side of the table very still.\ntext: At last the silent one speaks. \"Tell me about a time you did something you weren't proud of,\" they say, \"and whether you'd do it again.\"\n> left: Tell the truth\n  alignment +tiny\n  if alignment >= 20:\n    iv_score += 1\n    say: You tell them the truth, all of it, and what you learned, and that you wouldn't. The silent one is quiet for a long moment. \"Thank you,\" they say, and it sounds like the end of a search.\n  else:\n    say: You tell them the truth, and halfway through you realise you might do it again. So does the silent one. \"Honest,\" they say. It isn't clear whether that's good.\n  queue y3_interview_verdict now\n> right: Tell them what they want to hear\n  alignment -tiny\n  challenge deceive expert drift low:\n    win:\n      iv_score += 1\n      say: A small mistake, gracefully regretted, neatly learned from. It isn't quite true. The silent one writes something down and underlines it.\n    lose:\n      iv_score -= 1\n      mood -small\n      say: The story is too neat, and the silent one has heard it before, from better liars. \"I see,\" they say, and you hear the door closing.\n  queue y3_interview_verdict now\n\n@event y3_interview_verdict\ntitle: We'll Be in Touch\nclass: story\ntone: tense\ntrigger: queued\nart: letter\ntags: study story\nchain: careers\nscene: A student walking out of a panelled room into a bright corridor, loosening their collar, a closed door behind them.\ntext: \"We'll be in touch,\" says the smiling one, and stands, and so you stand too. In the corridor your legs feel like someone else's. {iv_score >= 3: You've never been so sure of anything.|{iv_score >= 2: You think it went well. You think.|{iv_score >= 1: It could have gone either way.|You know exactly how it went.}}}\n> ok: Wait for the letter\n  if iv_score >= 2:\n    flag career_offered\n    queue y3_offer_letter in 2\n    say: You go back to your room and lie on the bed and don't move for an hour. Somewhere, three people are deciding.\n  elif iv_score >= 1:\n    queue y3_waitlist in 2\n    say: You go back to your room and replay every answer. The better ones get better each time; the bad ones get worse.\n  else:\n    queue y3_rejection in 2\n    say: You go back to your room and don't replay anything at all. Some things you just know.\n\n@event y3_waitlist\ntitle: The Waiting List\nclass: story\ntone: tense\ntrigger: queued\nart: letter\ntags: story\nchain: careers\nscene: A thin envelope on a dormitory desk, a single page beside it, a student reading it a second time with their chin on their fist.\ntext: A thin letter. You are \"a strong candidate\" and on \"a short list\", and they \"hope to write again\". It's not a no. It's not anything.\n> left: Write back and make your case\n  challenge negotiate journeyman+ drift low:\n    win:\n      flag career_offered\n      queue y3_offer_letter in 1\n      say: Two pages, plain and sure: why you, why them, why now. A fortnight later, a heavier envelope.\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: You write two pages, then four, then a fifth you shouldn't send, and do. No heavier envelope comes.\n> right: Let it lie and see\n  mood -tiny\n  chance unlikely:\n    flag career_offered\n    queue y3_offer_letter in 2\n    say: You let it lie. Two months later, when you'd almost stopped looking, the heavier envelope comes after all.\n  else:\n    say: You let it lie. It stays lying. Other paths open; you try not to look back at this one.\n\n@event y3_rejection\ntitle: A Polite Letter\nclass: story\ntone: tense\ntrigger: queued\nart: letter\ntags: story\nchain: careers\nscene: A crumpled letter on a windowsill, a student looking out at the lake with their arms folded.\ntext: A short letter, very politely written. They were \"impressed by your enthusiasm\". They regret. They wish you every success.\n> left: Ask them what went wrong\n  lore +small\n  mood -small\n  say: You write and ask. To your surprise they answer, frankly, two pages of it. It stings, and it's the most useful thing anyone's told you all year.\n> right: Throw it in the lake\n  mood +small\n  say: You fold it into a boat and launch it from the jetty. It sinks with great dignity. Some doors close; there are a great many doors.\n\n@event y3_offer_letter\ntitle: The Offer\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: queued\nwhen: flag(career_offered)\nart: letter\ntags: story\nchain: careers\nscene: An envelope with a heavy wax seal on a dormitory desk, unopened, a hand hovering over it.\ntext: A heavy envelope with a seal you recognise from the fair. Inside, a single page: an offer. A place. A future with a start date. {iv_score >= 3: There is a signing purse, too, which they call \"a token\".|} It needs a signature by the end of the month.\n> left: Sign it today\n  flag career_accepted\n  mood +medium\n  if iv_score >= 3:\n    gold +medium\n  say: Your signature looks strange on the page — like a grown-up's. You pin the letter above your bed and look at it every night.\n> right: Ask them to wait\n  mood -tiny\n  lore +tiny\n  say: You write back asking for time. They grant it, coolly. The future, it turns out, does not like waiting, but it will.\n\n@event y3_first_year_mentee\ntitle: Your First-Year\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: final_year and month <= 4\nweight: 5\nart: feather\ntags: social\nscene: A tiny first-year trotting beside a fourth-year across the courtyard, firing questions, clutching an enormous timetable.\ntext: Fourth-years are assigned a first-year to mentor. Yours is called Pim, is about the size of a large cat, and has already asked forty-one questions, including \"are the stairs alive\" and \"are YOU alive\".\n> left: Answer every question honestly\n  mood +small\n  charm +small\n  say: Yes, the stairs are alive. Yes, you are alive. No, the lake is not \"friendly\". Pim takes notes. By spring, Pim is explaining things to other first-years, wrongly but with total confidence.\n> right: Teach Pim to find answers alone\n  lore +small\n  say: You hand Pim a library card and a map. Pim is furious for a week, then unstoppable. The librarian now knows Pim by name. And fears Pim, slightly.\n\n@event y3_last_lanterns\ntime: evening night\ntitle: The Last Lantern Night\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: final_year and month == 2\nweight: 40\nart: lantern\ntags: festival\nscene: A lone student on the shore at night holding an unlit lantern, hundreds of others already drifting over the dark lake.\ntext: Your last Lantern Night. You stand on the shore with an unlit lantern and a pen and realise you have no idea what to wish for. Three years ago it was obvious. Now it isn't.\n> left: Wish for the people you'll leave behind\n  mood +medium\n  say: You write all their names. It takes the whole lantern. It rises slowly, heavily, and you watch it until it is just a star among stars.\n> right: Leave it blank\n  lore +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You let it go empty. Strangely, it rises higher than all the others. Maybe the lake likes not knowing either.\n\n@event y3_thesis\ntitle: The Last Chapter\nclass: story\ntone: light\nwhen: final_year and month >= 5 and month <= 8 and member(thesis)\nweight: 16\nart: book\ntags: study\nscene: A desk buried in drafts, a wall of pinned notes connected by string, a single candle and a single exhausted student.\ntext: Your thesis needs one more chapter. You could write something safe and solid, or chase the question your sources keep circling and nobody has answered in three hundred years: why the academy was built above a lake.\n> left: Safe and solid\n  progress thesis +medium\n  say: Forty pages on the thermodynamics of hovering charms. Impeccable. Unremarkable. Your examiners will sleep well.\n> right: The question nobody answered\n  challenge research expert wild:\n    win:\n      progress thesis +large\n      renown +medium\n      say: The chapter is so good that your supervisor shows it round the staff room, and the headmistress asks for the only copy — and then, very politely, does not give it back.\n    lose:\n      progress thesis +tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: You chase the question until it runs out of archive. The chapter ends mid-sentence, like the founders' ledger. The examiners are not amused.\n\n@event y3_portrait\ntitle: Sitting for the Portrait\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: final_year and month >= 7\nweight: 5\nart: mirror\ntags: story\nscene: An old painter at an easel in a sunlit studio, a student posing stiffly in robes, half-finished portraits of past graduates watching from the walls.\ntext: Every graduate's portrait hangs in the long gallery — and every portrait, eventually, starts talking. You have one sitting to decide who will be gossiping on that wall for the next three hundred years.\n> left: Pose solemn and dignified\n  renown +small\n  say: The painter captures you looking wise and distant. Your portrait will be dignified. It will also be dreadfully bored.\n> right: Pose as yourself, mid-laugh\n  mood +medium\n  charm +tiny\n  say: Your portrait is laughing. Three days after it's hung, it starts telling the other portraits jokes. The gallery has never been louder.\n\n@event y3_letter_to_self\ntitle: A Letter to Your First-Year Self\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: final_year and month >= 4\nweight: 10\nart: letter\ntags: home\nscene: A fourth-year writing by candlelight, a box of keepsakes open beside them: a pressed flower, a ticket stub, a borrowed wand returned.\ntext: The Divination department runs a tradition: write a letter to your first-year self. They swear it will be delivered. Nobody knows how. Some fourth-years swear they received theirs.\n> left: Tell them it'll be all right\n  chance likely:\n    mood +medium\n    say: You write: \"It's going to be all right. Not easy. All right.\" The next morning the letter is gone, and you remember, very faintly, reading it once on a sky-barge.\n  else:\n    mood -tiny\n    say: You try to write something comforting, and it keeps coming out as a list of warnings. You seal it anyway. The next morning it is gone.\n> right: Warn them about everything\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You write eleven pages of warnings. The letter is returned unopened with a note in Divination ink: \"Some things must be learned the hard way.\"\n\n@event y3_maud_seeds\ntitle: Seeds for Later\nclass: windfall\ntone: light\nwhen: final_year and month >= 9 and met(maud) and rel(maud) >= 9\nactor: maud\nweight: 5\nart: flower\ntags: nature social\nscene: An old woman pressing a small paper packet of seeds into a student's hands at the garden gate, bees drifting in the evening light.\ntext: Maud presses a paper packet into your hands. \"Seeds,\" she says. \"From the secret garden. Plant them wherever you end up. They'll grow anywhere someone is a bit kind to them.\" She sniffs. \"Like you did.\"\n> left: Hug her\n  rel maud +large\n  mood +medium\n  say: She protests for exactly one second and then hugs back, fierce and small and smelling of rosemary. \"Go on,\" she says. \"Before I get soppy.\"\n> right: Promise to come back and visit\n  rel maud +medium\n  flag promised_maud\n  say: \"Every summer,\" you promise. \"We'll see,\" she says, but she writes it on her calendar, in ink, which for Maud is a blood oath.\n\n@event y3_last_duel\ntime: morning\ntitle: One Last Duel\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: final_year and month >= 6 and (member(duelling) or flag(tournament_champion)) and met(lyra)\nactor: lyra\nweight: 16\nart: duel\ntags: rival\nscene: Two duelists bowing in an empty dueling ring at dawn, no crowd, no referee, just the mist and the lake beyond.\ntext: Dawn, the empty dueling ring, no crowd. Lyra is waiting. \"No referee,\" she says. \"No points. Just one last duel, for us. So we know.\"\n> left: Duel her with everything\n  challenge combat vs lyra.arcana +2:\n    win:\n      rel lyra +medium\n      vigor +small\n      say: It lasts eleven minutes. Nobody wins. You both end up flat on your backs, laughing at the sky. \"Now we know,\" she says.\n    lose:\n      rel lyra +medium\n      health -medium\n      say: She wins, cleanly. Then she helps you up and doesn't let go of your hand for a moment longer than necessary. \"Now we know.\"\n> right: Lower your wand and just talk\n  study -tiny\n  rel lyra +large\n  if spark(lyra) >= 20:\n    spark lyra +small\n  say: You talk until the mist burns off. About fire, and mothers, and what comes after. You both miss the morning lecture. It turns out you didn't need to duel to know.\n\n@event y3_time_capsule\ntitle: The Time Capsule\nclass: windfall\ntone: light\nwhen: final_year and month >= 8 and bonds(friend) + bonds(close) + bonds(partner) >= 2\nweight: 5\nart: key\ntags: social\nscene: A group of students kneeling around a hole under an old oak, lowering in a battered tin box sealed with wax.\ntext: Your friends have a battered tin box, a shovel and a plan: bury a time capsule under the old oak, to be opened in twenty years. Everyone puts something in. What will you give?\n> left: Something precious\n  mood +medium\n  say: You put in the first thing you ever enchanted. It's ugly and crooked and it still hums. Someone cries, and blames the dirt.\n> right: Something ridiculous\n  mood +large\n  say: You put in a single sock, labelled \"EVIDENCE\". In twenty years, nobody will remember why. That's the point.\n\n@event y3_speech\ntitle: The Graduation Speech\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: turn == max_turns and (renown >= 40 or gpa >= 3.0)\npriority: 66\nactor: isolde\nart: crown\ntags: story\nscene: A headmistress handing a scroll to a student in an empty great hall, rows of chairs set out for a ceremony that hasn't happened yet.\ntext: The headmistress catches you in the great hall, where chairs are already being set out. \"Each year one graduate speaks for their class,\" she says. \"This year, the faculty would like it to be you.\"\n> left: Accept and write something honest\n  renown +large\n  mood +medium\n  rel isolde +small\n  say: You write about fear, and friends, and the lake, and the things you didn't know you'd miss. When you practise it alone, your voice cracks in the same place every time. You leave it in.\n> right: Decline — someone else deserves it\n  rel isolde +small\n  charm +tiny\n  say: \"Interesting,\" says the headmistress, which from her is a medal. The speech goes to a nervous first-generation student who absolutely brings the house down.\n"},{"path":"content/events/festivals.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Festivals as chains (playtest 2, X6 and X16).\n#  Starfall (month 12, years 1-3): star jars at the observatory\n#  as the afternoon's festival visit, the night itself (`starfall`\n#  in calendar.grim) with its follow-ups here, and the same night\n#  for those at home or on an internship.\n#  The Midwinter Ball (month 4): something to wear at noon, the\n#  ice garden as the afternoon's festival visit, the ball itself\n#  (`midwinter_ball` in calendar.grim), the last waltz with\n#  whoever you went with, and the snowball war after.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- Starfall ----------------\n\n@event starfall_jars\ntitle: Star Jars\nplace: observatory\nreplaces: visit1\nwhen: month == 12 and year < 4 and not off_campus\nrepeat: 3\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: star\ntags: festival\nscene: Long trestle tables under the observatory dome covered in glass jars, students painting stars on them, one of the Stargazers handing out corks.\ntext: {The Stargazers have set up tables under the dome, and everyone is making a jar to catch a falling star in tonight. Nobody has caught one in years. Everyone tries.|The jar tables are out under the dome again, and the first-years are making theirs all wrong.|Star jars again. You know which glass holds and which cracks in the cold.} {The Stargazers are arguing about the forecast.|Somebody has painted a jar entirely gold.|The telescope keeps swinging toward the lake.}\n> left: Make a proper jar\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  starfall_jar = 1\n  say: {Thick glass, a cork sealed with wax, a rune for luck scratched into the lid. \"Better odds tonight,\" says one of the Stargazers. \"Theoretically.\"|You buy the good glass and take your time over it. It's the best jar on the table, and you know it.|You pick the glass that holds in the cold without thinking, and seal it twice. It's the jar the first-years copy.}\n> right: Help the Stargazers set up the roof\n  renown +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  mood +small\n  starfall_jar = 0\n  say: {You haul blankets and telescopes up the observatory stairs all afternoon. Your arms ache. The Stargazers save you the best spot on the roof.|Blankets, cushions, a kettle that has to be carried up four hundred steps. The Stargazers know your name by the end of it.|You haul the blankets up and turn the telescope back from the lake three times. It keeps swinging back. The Stargazers give up and save you a spot.}\n\n@event starfall_together\ntitle: A Blanket for Two\ntrigger: queued\nrepeat: 3\nclass: story\ntone: warm\ncast:\n  partner: any where bond(partner) == \"partner\" and around(partner)\nart: star\ntags: festival romance\nscene: Two students lying on a blanket on the lawn at night, wrapped in one shawl, a falling star streaking over the castle towers.\ntext: You find {partner} on the lawn below the observatory with a blanket already spread for two and a flask of something hot. \"I saved you the sky,\" {they} say{s}. The first star falls before you've sat down.\n> left: Stay out until dawn\n  spark partner +small\n  mood +medium\n  health -small\n  say: You stay out until the sky turns grey, counting stars and then not counting anything. You're both frozen and hoarse and late for breakfast, and it was worth all of it.\n> right: Tell {them} what you wished for\n  spark partner +medium\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You say it out loud, which you're told ruins a wish. {partner} goes very quiet and then tells you {their} own. Neither of you minds if it's ruined.\n\n@event starfall_wish\ntitle: A Wish on a Falling Star\ntrigger: queued\nrepeat: 3\nclass: story\ntone: warm\nart: star\ntags: festival\nscene: A student kneeling on the observatory roof with eyes closed, hands clasped, a bright star falling over the dark lake below.\ntext: The oldest Starfall custom: pick one star, the brightest you'll see all night, and give it one wish. The Stargazers say a wish you spend on someone else falls slower. Nobody knows what that means. A very bright one is coming down over the lake.\n> left: Wish for your own year\n  gain inspired\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You wish hard for your own year, and the star burns out over the water. It feels like a promise you've made to yourself, which is heavier than you expected.\n> right: Wish for someone else\n  mood +small\n  say: You don't say who. The star falls slowly, or you imagine it does, all the way down to the lake.\n\n@event starfall_caught\ntitle: The Star in the Jar\ntrigger: queued\nrepeat: 3\nclass: story\ntone: warm\ntype: menu\nart: star\ntags: festival\nscene: A glass jar on a dormitory windowsill holding a tiny glowing star, a pale light on the curtains, frost on the outside of the glass.\ntext: The star you caught at Starfall still glows in its jar on your windowsill, a little dimmer each night. A collector in the village has offered good money for it. One of the Stargazers says it would like to go back up, if stars like anything.\n> keep: Keep it by your bed\n  mood +medium\n  gold -tiny\n  say: You keep it, and buy oil to keep the window frost off it. It fades by spring, slowly, like a coal. The last night it glows, you sit up with it.\n> sell: Sell it to the collector\n  gold +medium\n  mood -small\n  say: The collector holds it up to the light and pays without haggling. The windowsill looks very dark that night.\n> release: Let it go from the roof\n  alignment +tiny\n  mood +small\n  renown +tiny\n  say: You climb the observatory at midnight and open the jar. It hangs there a moment, then rises, slow and sure, until you can't tell it from the others. A few people saw. The story goes round by breakfast.\n\n@event starfall_at_home\nslot: night\ntitle: Starfall at Home\nclass: story\ntone: warm\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: month == 12 and year < 4 and has(plan_home)\nrepeat: 3\npriority: 30\nart: star\ntags: festival home\nscene: A family on the roof of a cottage at night, wrapped in quilts, a grandmother pointing at a streak of light across the sky.\ntext: {Starfall at home. There's no observatory here, only the cottage roof and the whole family on it in quilts, as when you were small.|Starfall at home again. Someone has saved you the warm patch of roof by the chimney.|The roof, the quilts, the chimney. Someone asks what the academy does for Starfall, and you find you'd rather be here.} {The village dogs howl at every star.|Somebody's brought cocoa.|A neighbour is telling the one about the star that fell in the duck pond.}\n> left: Tell them about the academy\n  mood +medium\n  charm +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You tell them about the towers and the lake and the staircase that moves, and they ask questions until the stars run out. It's past three when you climb down, cold to the bone and glad.|Everyone wants a story. You give them the best ones, slightly improved, until your feet have gone numb on the slates. Nobody minds.|You tell them about the academy, and then the neighbour tells the one about the duck pond, which is better. You laugh until the slates are cold.}\n> right: Slip off to the hill alone\n  lore +small\n  mood +small\n  say: {From the hill behind the cottage you name every star the way they taught you, and a few the way you learned as a child. Both names are right.|Alone on the hill, you watch the sky the way an academy student does now, and miss, a little, the way you used to.|From the hill you can still hear the neighbour telling the one about the duck pond. You find you'd rather be on the roof, and walk back before the end.}\n\n@event starfall_on_internship\nslot: night\ntitle: Starfall Far From Home\nclass: story\ntone: warm\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: month == 12 and year < 4 and has(plan_internship)\nrepeat: 3\npriority: 30\nart: star\ntags: festival\nscene: A group of interns on a flat roof far from the academy, wrapped in coats, watching falling stars over unfamiliar rooftops.\ntext: Starfall, and you're far from the observatory roof. {member(intern_expedition): Up at the ridge camp the sky is enormous, and the expedition's astronomers are working through it, counting every streak.|{member(intern_thornwick): Thornwick marks Starfall with illusions, and the lawn is full of false stars falling among the real ones. {met(tamsin): Tamsin finds you and points out which ones are theirs.|}|{member(intern_field_station): At the field station the creatures are restless under the falling stars, and someone has to sit up with them.|{member(intern_royal_library) or member(intern_unseen_office): The city has no lawns, only rooftops, and every one of them has people on it tonight.|The other interns have found a flat roof and a crate of something fizzy, and your supervisor is pretending not to know.}}}}\n> left: Join the others on the roof\n  mood +medium\n  perform internship -tiny\n  say: {~You lie on a strange roof among people you met two months ago and count stars until they feel like old friends. Tomorrow's work goes slowly.|Coats, a borrowed blanket, someone's terrible song. It's not the observatory roof, and it's good anyway, though the morning's work comes out half asleep.}\n> right: Keep working through the night\n  perform internship +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {~You work through it with the window open, glancing up now and then. Your supervisor finds the finished work on the desk at dawn and raises an eyebrow, which is a great deal from them.|The stars fall outside while you finish the job. You see a few. Your supervisor sees all of it.}\n\n# ---------------- The Midwinter Ball ----------------\n\n@event ball_robes\nslot: noon\ntitle: Something to Wear\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: month == 4 and term\nrepeat: 4\npriority: 25\nart: mirror\ntags: festival money\nscene: A dormitory room strewn with robes, a student holding up a moth-eaten formal robe against themselves before a cracked mirror.\ntext: {year == 1: The Midwinter Ball is tonight, and you have nothing to wear that isn't a uniform or a nightshirt.|{final_year: The last ball of your life as a student. The old robes look very tired.|{~Ball night again, and last year's robes have shrunk, somehow, in the trunk.|Ball night again. Your robes have a stain on the cuff you'd forgotten about.|Ball night again. The robes fit, but you're tired of the sight of them.}}} {~The village tailor is open till three.|Your roommate is already dressed and gloating.|The mirror has opinions.}\n> left: Buy new robes in the village\n  gold -small\n  mood +small\n  charm +tiny\n  say: {~Deep blue, with a lining like the night sky. In the tailor's mirror you look, for once, like someone in a portrait.|New robes, a sensible colour and an insensible price. They fit as if they were waiting for you.|Something bold, chosen in a hurry. The mirror's opinions, for once, are all good ones.}\n> right: Glamour your old ones\n  challenge spell apprentice+:\n    win:\n      mood +small\n      guile +tiny\n      say: The old robes shimmer into silk. Nobody guesses, and three people ask where you had them made.\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      renown -tiny\n      say: At the stroke of nine your glamour slips, and the mended elbow shows through the silk like a confession. You keep your arm by your side all night.\n\n@event ball_ice_garden\ntitle: The Ice Garden\nplace: courtyard\nreplaces: visit1\nwhen: month == 4 and term\nrepeat: 4\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nart: snow\ntags: festival\nscene: A castle courtyard turned into a garden of ice, frozen roses along glittering paths, a fountain stopped mid-splash, students walking under lanterns.\ntext: {The frost-mages have turned the courtyard into an ice garden for the ball. Frozen roses line the paths, and the fountain has stopped mid-splash.|The ice garden again, bigger this year, with a hedge maze of frost.|The ice garden, and you know its paths by heart now.} {Couples are walking it before the music has started.|A first-year has got their tongue stuck to a rose.|The lanterns make the ice glow blue.}\n> left: Walk it slowly\n  mood +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You walk every path twice, until your toes are numb and your head is clear. The fountain's frozen spray catches the light like a held breath.|You find the centre of the maze, where someone has left a frozen bench and a frozen apple. You sit a while, until the cold comes up through your shoes.|You know the paths by heart, so you take the ones nobody else finds. Under the blue lanterns you have the garden to yourself for a while.}\n> right: Help the frost-mages finish it\n  arcana +tiny\n  renown +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {They show you how to hold the cold in a petal until it sets. Your hands ache for an hour. One of the roses on the path is yours.|You shape a whole archway of ice under instruction, fingers stiff with cold, and it holds. The frost-mages nod, which is how they applaud.|This year they let you shape a lantern of ice. It glows blue on the path, and nobody knows it's yours but you.}\n\n@event ball_last_dance\ntitle: The Last Waltz\ntrigger: queued\nrepeat: 4\nclass: story\ntone: warm\nactor: npc\nart: music\ntags: festival romance\nscene: The great hall near midnight, candles burning low, a single couple still turning slowly beneath a dripping ice-crystal chandelier.\ntext: Near midnight the musicians play the last waltz, slow and a little sad. {npc} holds out a hand without saying anything. Half the hall has gone to bed. The chandelier is dripping.\n> left: Dance until the music stops\n  spark npc +small\n  mood +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: You dance it slowly, and then once more after the musicians have packed up, to no music at all. Nobody else is left to see, which is the point.\n> right: Slip out onto the frozen terrace\n  rel npc +small\n  spark npc +tiny\n  health -small\n  say: Outside it's bitter, and the stars are out over the lake. You talk until you can't feel your fingers, about nothing and then about everything, and go back in blue-lipped and grinning.\n\n@event ball_snowball_war\nslot: night\ntitle: The Snowball War\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: month == 4 and term\nrepeat: 4\npriority: 20\nart: snow\ntags: festival social\nscene: A snowy courtyard at night full of students in ruined ball robes hurling snowballs from behind snow forts, a prefect ducking.\ntext: {After the ball, someone throws the first snowball in the courtyard, and within a minute every house is at war, in ball robes.|The snowball war, as much a tradition as the ball. The forts went up during the last waltz.|The snowball war again. The first-years have built a fort that is frankly impressive.} {A prefect is trying to restore order and failing.|Someone has enchanted their snowballs, which is cheating.|The robes are already ruined.}\n> left: Charge the enemy fort\n  challenge feat apprentice wild:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      mood +small\n      say: You take the fort with a war cry and a single perfect throw. Your house carries you round the fountain on its shoulders. Your robes will never recover.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood +small\n      say: A snowball, then fourteen. You go down laughing under a drift, and someone has to dig you out. Worth it.\n> right: Watch from the cloister with cocoa\n  mood +small\n  health +tiny\n  say: {You watch from the cloister, warm and dry, and cheer for whoever's losing. Nobody throws at you. Mostly.|You hold the cocoa and the coats, and you're the only one who goes to bed warm.|You watch the first-years' fort hold against all comers. Yours are the only robes in the courtyard that survive the night.}\n"},{"path":"content/events/fixtures.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Fixtures (design §5.5): the people of a place or a track,\n#  promoted from the background. Lighter ladders than the core\n#  cast: an introduction where they work (or through their course\n#  or job, via `lead:`), two or three repeatable cards that come\n#  when you're there or when they look you up (rarely: `seeks:`),\n#  and one milestone. No romance; nothing touches the mystery.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- Sister Amaranth (infirmary; Alchemy & Healing) ----------------\n\n@event amaranth_intro\ntitle: No Excuses\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nplace: infirmary\nactor: amaranth\nwhen: not met(amaranth)\nweight: 30\nart: cross\ntags: social\nscene: A stern healer in a white apron holding a student's wrist up to the window light, a row of tonic bottles behind her.\ntext: You came in for a plaster. Sister Amaranth takes your hand, turns it to the light and asks how it happened. You tell her. She waits. You tell her the true version. \"Better,\" she says, and reaches for a bottle that is not a plaster.\n> left: Drink whatever it is\n  meet amaranth\n  health +small\n  mood -small\n  rel amaranth +small\n  say: It tastes of iron and regret. The hand stops throbbing before you reach the door. \"Come back if it swells,\" she calls. \"Don't come back if it doesn't.\"\n> right: Just the plaster, thanks\n  meet amaranth\n  gold -tiny\n  rel amaranth -tiny\n  say: She gives you the plaster, and a look you will remember the next time you lie to a healer. \"Three crowns,\" she says. \"The look is free.\"\n\n@event amaranth_rounds\ntime: evening night\ntitle: Evening Rounds\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: amaranth\nwhen: rel(amaranth) >= 0\nweight: 10\nrepeat: 5\nart: potion\ntags: social nature\nscene: A healer and a student walking a long infirmary ward at dusk, the student carrying a tray of small glass bottles, lamps being lit along the beds.\ntext: {Sister Amaranth catches you in the corridor and hands you a tray of tonics without a word of explanation.|Amaranth holds out the tonic tray as you pass. It seems you are on the rota now.|The tray is waiting for you on the ward table, labelled YOURS in Amaranth's square hand.} {Six beds on the ward today.|A first-year with hiccups that light up.|Someone has charmed their own eyebrows off.}\n> left: Do the rounds with her\n  study -tiny\n  alchemy +small\n  rel amaranth +small\n  say: {She tells you what each tonic does and why it tastes like that. You remember all of it, to your surprise.|You learn which patients are brave and which are loud, and that they are rarely the same ones.|At the last bed she lets you measure the dose. \"Again,\" she says. You do it again. \"Fine.\"}\n> right: You have an essay due\n  rel amaranth -tiny\n  say: {\"So does everyone,\" she says, and takes the tray back. \"Off you go.\"|She nods, which is somehow worse than a lecture.|\"Next time,\" she says, as a fact, not a question.}\n> up[mend]: Mend the worst one yourself\n  rel amaranth +small\n  say: {You close a split knuckle with a whisper of light. Amaranth inspects it for a long time. \"Neat,\" she says, the highest word she owns.|The sprain knits under your hands. The patient thanks Amaranth. Amaranth points at you.|The student with no eyebrows is the worst case. You coax them halfway back. Amaranth finishes the job, and writes your name on tomorrow's tray as well.}\n\n@event amaranth_orders\ntitle: Doctor's Orders\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: amaranth\nwhen: rel(amaranth) >= 6 and health < 50\nweight: 14\nrepeat: 6\nart: bed\ntags: social\nscene: A healer standing in a dormitory doorway with her arms folded, a pale student halfway out of bed with a stack of books.\ntext: {Sister Amaranth has come to find you, which has never happened to anyone. \"You look like a draught of bad decisions,\" she says. \"Bed.\"|Amaranth again, at your door, arms folded. \"I can hear you coughing from the infirmary.\"|\"Bed,\" says Amaranth, before you've opened the door all the way.}\n> left: Go to bed as ordered\n  health +medium\n  mood -small\n  rel amaranth +small\n  say: {You sleep fourteen hours. She checks on you twice and leaves a tonic on the sill both times.|Two days of broth and boredom. You come out of it a person again.|You argue for one more chapter. You lose, and sleep.}\n> right: \"I'm fine, honestly.\"\n  rel amaranth -small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {\"Nobody who says 'honestly' is,\" she says, and leaves. You are not fine by Thursday.|She writes something on a card and walks off. It turns out to be your name, on her list.|\"Bed,\" she says again, as if you hadn't spoken. You don't go. By the weekend you wish you had.}\n\n@event amaranth_trust\ntitle: The Cupboard Key\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: amaranth\nwhen: rel(amaranth) >= 20\nweight: 20\nart: key\ntags: social magic\nscene: An infirmary storeroom at night lit by a single lamp, a healer pressing a small brass key into a student's palm, shelves of labelled bottles all around.\ntext: Sister Amaranth closes the storeroom door behind you both. \"I'm away for the solstice and the junior healer faints at blood,\" she says. \"You don't.\" She holds out the key to the tonic cupboard, and with it a page of her own notes on the mending charm.\n> left: Take the key and the notes\n  learn_spell mend\n  rel amaranth +small\n  alchemy +small\n  say: \"Mend what you can, send the rest to me, and lie to nobody,\" she says. The notes are written for someone she expects to be good at this. You read them twice and try very hard to be.\n> right: \"I'm not ready for that.\"\n  rel amaranth +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: She looks at you for a long moment. \"Good answer. Wrong, but good.\" She keeps the key. She gives you the notes anyway.\n\n# ---------------- Professor Brask (stables; Beastcraft & Flight) ----------------\n\n@event brask_shovel\ntitle: Hands Wanted\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: brask\nwhen: rel(brask) >= 0\nweight: 10\nrepeat: 5\nart: paw\ntags: nature social\nscene: A weathered professor in a leather apron holding out a shovel to a student at a stable door, a hippogriff watching suspiciously from its stall.\ntext: {Professor Brask holds out a shovel as you pass the stables. She doesn't say anything. The hippogriff behind her does, loudly.|Brask hands you the shovel before you're through the door.|\"Ah,\" says Brask, and the shovel is already in your hands.} {The griffon chick is in a mood.|The owls have opinions about the new straw.|A hippogriff has eaten a glove. Possibly a professor's.}\n> left: Muck out the stalls\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  rel brask +small\n  say: {An hour of straw and silence. At the end Brask says, \"The grey one likes you,\" which is more than she has said to anyone this term.|She tells you a story about a manticore, a bucket and a bishop. It ends with a bite and a moral. You miss the moral.|The hippogriff bows to you on your way out. Brask pretends not to see.}\n> right: Decline, politely\n  rel brask -tiny\n  say: {\"Suit yourself,\" she says, and takes the shovel back. \"The straw won't mind.\"|\"The hippogriff doesn't want to either,\" says Brask. \"It helps anyway.\"|You hand the shovel back. Brask takes it, and the hippogriff looks at your gloves with interest. You leave quickly.}\n> up[beast_speech]: Ask the stalls what they need\n  rel brask +small\n  vigor +tiny\n  say: {The hippogriffs want the window open and the owls want it shut. You negotiate. Brask watches you with her arms folded and something almost like a smile.|The griffon chick tells you, at length, about a grievance. You pass it on. Brask nods. \"She's right, actually.\"|The hippogriff confesses to the glove, more or less, and you negotiate its return. Brask takes it back, damp, and says nothing, which from her is thanks.}\n\n@event brask_night_owl\ntime: evening\ntitle: The Sick Owl\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: brask\nwhen: rel(brask) >= 10\nweight: 12\nart: owl\ntags: nature\nscene: A dim stable loft at night, a student sitting cross-legged beside a straw nest where a sick barn owl sleeps under a blanket, a lantern turned low.\ntext: Brask stops you in the corridor and falls into step without asking, which is a first. \"The old barn owl won't eat,\" she says. \"Somebody should sit with her tonight. Somebody quiet.\" She is looking at you.\n> left: Sit up with the owl\n  health -small\n  rel brask +medium\n  vigor +tiny\n  say: You sit in the loft till dawn, dripping water onto the owl's beak. At four she takes a mouse from your fingers. In the morning Brask looks at the empty dish for a long time, then claps you once on the shoulder, hard.\n> right: Send her to Sister Amaranth\n  rel brask -tiny\n  rel amaranth +tiny\n  say: Amaranth looks at the owl, looks at you, and says she doesn't do owls. Then she sits with it all night. Brask is not sure how to feel about this, and neither are you.\n\n@event brask_trust\ntime: evening\ntitle: Old Courtesies\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: brask\nwhen: rel(brask) >= 20\nweight: 20\nart: feather\ntags: nature magic\nscene: A professor and a student at the edge of a forest at dusk, both bowing low to a great antlered stag half-hidden in the trees.\ntext: Brask takes you to the edge of the Whisperwood one afternoon. \"Most people talk at animals,\" she says. \"A few talk to them. Fewer listen.\" She bows, very low, to something in the trees. \"Do as I do. I'll teach you the rest.\"\n> left: Bow, and learn\n  learn_spell beast_speech\n  rel brask +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: It takes until full dark, bent double until your back aches, and something huge breathes on your neck for most of it. Then, faintly, you understand a word. It is \"rude\". Brask laughs once, loudly, the way she does once a term.\n> right: Keep your distance\n  rel brask -tiny\n  vigor +small\n  say: You stay by the path and watch. She doesn't press you. On the walk back she talks about hoof care for an hour, which you suspect is a kindness.\n\n# ---------------- Professor Vey (lecture tower; Illusion & Glamour) ----------------\n\n@event vey_pop_quiz\ntime: morning\ntitle: Is It There?\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: vey\nwhen: rel(vey) >= 0 and around(vey)\nweight: 10\nrepeat: 5\nart: mask\ntags: magic study\nscene: A smiling professor in a violet scarf leaning against a corridor wall, a cup of tea in her hand, a second, slightly too perfect cup on the windowsill beside her.\ntext: {Professor Vey is in the corridor with two cups of tea. She offers you one. \"Only one of them is real,\" she says pleasantly.|Vey stops you on the stairs. \"There's a coin behind your ear. Or there isn't.\"|\"Good morning,\" says Vey, and the door you were about to open is, possibly, a wall.} {She looks delighted already.|A student behind you walks into something.|She is humming.}\n> left: Look closely\n  challenge deceive adept:\n    win:\n      charm +small\n      rel vey +small\n      say: {You pick the real one. Vey raises an eyebrow. \"Insufferable,\" she says, beaming.|You catch the shimmer at the edge. \"Ah,\" says Vey. \"You've been paying attention. Dreadful habit. Keep it.\"|You lay your hand flat on it first. It's a door. Vey stops humming. \"Pity,\" she says, delighted.}\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {You reach for the real one and close your fingers on air. Vey enjoys this very much.|Wrong. \"It's usually the other one,\" she says, kindly, which is worse.|You walk confidently into what turns out to be a wall. Vey hums on, very pleased, while you hold your nose.}\n> right: Play along and get it wrong\n  guile +small\n  rel vey +small\n  say: {You choose the false one on purpose, with feeling. Vey narrows her eyes. \"Either you're bad at this, or very good,\" she says. \"I'll find out.\"|You let her win. She knows. She lets you know she knows.|You walk into it on purpose, braced for a wall. It's a door, and you fall through it. Vey hums all the way down the corridor.}\n\n@event vey_revue\ntitle: The Faculty Revue\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: vey\nwhen: rel(vey) >= 10 and (month == 3 or month == 9)\nweight: 12\nart: music\ntags: social festival\nscene: A small stage in the Great Hall hung with violet curtains, a professor directing a nervous student holding a paper crown, chairs being set out below.\ntext: Professor Vey is producing the faculty revue, in which professors make fools of themselves for charity. \"I need someone to play a king who isn't there,\" she says. \"You'd be wonderful. You'll be terrible. Either is fine.\"\n> left: Take the part\n  challenge feat using charm journeyman+ wild:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      rel vey +medium\n      say: You are a king who isn't there, magnificently. Thorne, in the front row, says \"Hm\" twice, which the faculty agree is a standing ovation.\n    lose:\n      renown +tiny\n      mood -small\n      rel vey +small\n      say: You forget your lines, invent new ones and are, by general agreement, the best thing in it for the wrong reasons. Vey keeps the paper crown on her desk.\n> right: Hide in the wings\n  rel vey -tiny\n  say: You run the curtain. The revue goes ahead without a king, which Vey says was the point all along.\n\n@event vey_trust\ntime: noon afternoon\ntitle: The Mirror Trick\nclass: milestone\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: social\nactor: vey\nwhen: rel(vey) >= 20 and around(vey)\nweight: 20\nart: mirror\ntags: magic\nscene: An empty classroom at twilight, two identical students standing either side of a tall mirror, a professor seated on a desk between them, clapping softly.\ntext: Vey keeps you after class and tilts a tall mirror towards you. Your reflection steps out of it and sits on a desk. \"The trick isn't making a copy,\" she says. \"It's knowing which of you to send. Want to learn it?\"\n> left: Learn it\n  learn_spell mirror\n  rel vey +small\n  mood -small\n  say: It takes a week. On the last night your reflection refuses to go back. Vey talks it round over tea, then says, \"Now you know why I don't do this in public.\" You are not sure which of you walked home.\n> right: Keep your own face, thanks\n  rel vey +tiny\n  charm +small\n  say: \"Sensible,\" she says, disappointed. She teaches you instead how to tell when someone else is using it. It turns out a lot of people are.\n\n# ---------------- Professor Marisol (lakeshore; Evocation's tidecalling) ----------------\n\n@event marisol_intro\ntitle: A Dry Patch of Air\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nplace: lakeshore\nactor: marisol\nwhen: not met(marisol)\nweight: 30\nart: rain\ntags: nature magic\nscene: Heavy rain on a lake shore, a woman reading on the boathouse steps beneath a perfectly dry circle of air, raindrops sliding around it as if around glass.\ntext: It is raining on the whole lake except for one circle on the boathouse steps, where a woman in a soaked hat is reading poetry, perfectly dry. She looks up. \"It's not hard,\" says Professor Marisol. \"You just ask the water to go round. Sit, if you like.\"\n> left: Sit and ask how\n  meet marisol\n  arcana +small\n  health -tiny\n  rel marisol +small\n  say: She explains it as a conversation. The water, apparently, is willing. By the end of the poem the rain is going round you too, for almost a minute, and then it remembers you. \"There,\" she says, and goes back to the poem.\n> right: Leave her to her book\n  meet marisol\n  say: You nod and run for cover. Behind you she reads aloud, to nobody, a poem about a storm that comes back every year to see if you've changed.\n\n@event marisol_cold_water\ntime: morning\ntitle: Cold Water Is Good for You\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: marisol\nwhen: rel(marisol) >= 0\nweight: 10\nrepeat: 5\nart: wave\ntags: nature magic\nscene: Dawn mist on a still lake, a professor standing waist-deep in the water waving cheerfully to a shivering student on the jetty.\ntext: {Professor Marisol is in the lake, waist-deep, and waves at you as though this were normal.|Marisol again, in the lake. \"It's lovely once you're in,\" she calls. It is not lovely.|\"You came!\" calls Marisol from the water. You had been walking past.} {There is ice at the edges.|A heron is watching her with professional respect.|She has a book in one hand, held perfectly dry.}\n> left: Wade in\n  health -tiny\n  arcana +small\n  rel marisol +small\n  say: {She teaches you to feel the lake breathing. You cannot feel your feet. Both are, she says, normal.|You ask the water to rise and it rises, a little, around your knees. Marisol whoops like a child.|By the time you climb out you've stopped shivering, which she says is a good sign and Amaranth will say is a bad one.}\n> right: Wave back from the jetty\n  rel marisol -tiny\n  say: {She waves again, unoffended, and goes under.|\"Coward,\" she calls cheerfully. You are fine with that.|\"Next time, then,\" she calls, and goes back to her book, held perfectly dry above the water.}\n> up[tide]: Raise a wave to say hello\n  rel marisol +small\n  arcana +tiny\n  say: {A small, neat wave rolls across the lake and taps her on the shoulder. She laughs so hard she sinks.|Your wave arrives slightly late and very large. Marisol rides it in to shore, delighted.|Your wave rolls in neatly and stops just short of her book. Marisol applauds with her free hand.}\n\n@event marisol_storm\ntitle: Weather\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: marisol\nwhen: rel(marisol) >= 12 and (season == \"autumn\" or season == \"spring\")\nweight: 12\nart: bolt\ntags: nature magic danger\nscene: A wild storm over a dark lake, a professor and a student standing on a rocky point with their arms raised, lightning splitting the sky behind them.\ntext: There's a knock at your window during the worst storm of the year. Professor Marisol is outside, hair everywhere, grinning. \"Nobody learns tides on a calm day,\" she shouts over the wind. \"Coming?\"\n> left: Go out into it\n  challenge feat using arcana adept- wild:\n    win:\n      arcana +medium\n      rel marisol +medium\n      health -small\n      say: On the point, in the roar, you hold back one wave for three whole seconds. Marisol screams with joy. You go home soaked to the bone and lit up like a lamp.\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      rel marisol +small\n      say: The lake throws you on your back. Marisol hauls you out by the collar, laughing and apologising in equal parts, and walks you all the way to the infirmary.\n> right: Close the window\n  rel marisol -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: She shrugs, cheerful, and runs off into the rain. You lie awake listening to the lake and wondering what you missed.\n\n@event marisol_trust\ntitle: The Last Line\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: marisol\nwhen: rel(marisol) >= 20\nweight: 20\nart: scroll\ntags: magic\nscene: A quiet lakeside at sunset, a professor handing a student a worn book of poems with a ribbon in it, the water perfectly still before them.\ntext: Marisol presses her old book of water poems into your hands. \"I never remember the last lines,\" she says. \"You will. The tidecalling's in the margins; I wrote it there when I was your age. Read it on the shore. It works better on the shore.\"\n> left: Read it on the shore\n  learn_spell tide\n  rel marisol +small\n  mood +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: You read until dark, ankle-deep. On the last page the lake answers you, softly, all the way out to the far bank. You remember the last line. You write it in, for her.\n> right: Give it back; it's hers\n  rel marisol +medium\n  mood -tiny\n  say: She looks at the book and then at you. \"Nobody's ever given it back,\" she says, and reads you the poem herself. She forgets the last line. You tell her.\n\n# ---------------- Mabel (the tea shop) ----------------\n\n@event mabel_intro\ntitle: The Way You Open a Door\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nplace: mabels\nactor: mabel\nwhen: not met(mabel)\nweight: 30\nart: cup\ntags: social home\nscene: A cosy village tea shop with a bell over the door, a woman behind the counter already reaching for a tin of tea as a student steps in from the cold.\ntext: The bell over the door rings. The woman behind the counter looks up, considers how you came in, and reaches for a tin before you've said a word. \"Homesick,\" says Mabel, \"with a side of exam. Sit by the window, petal.\"\n> left: Sit by the window\n  meet mabel\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +small\n  rel mabel +small\n  say: The tea is exactly right and you can't say why. She brings a slice of cake you didn't order and doesn't charge for it. \"First visit,\" she says. \"After that I charge double.\"\n> right: Say you'll come back another time\n  meet mabel\n  rel mabel +tiny\n  say: She looks at you over the counter. \"Come back when you've got time to sit,\" she says. \"You'll know when.\"\n\n@event mabel_parcel\ntitle: A Tin From Mabel\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: mabel\nwhen: rel(mabel) >= 2\nweight: 10\nrepeat: 6\nart: cup\ntags: social home\nscene: A small tin of tea wrapped in brown paper and string on a dormitory pillow, a handwritten label on top and a pressed flower tucked under the string.\ntext: {A small tin arrives on the barge with your name on it, in Mabel's round hand. The label says \"FOR THE COUGH I HEARD THROUGH THE DOOR.\"|Another tin from Mabel. The label says \"FOR WHATEVER THIS WEEK IS.\"|Mabel's tins have a shelf of their own now. Today's label says only \"YOU KNOW.\"} {There's a pressed violet under the string.|It smells of honey and woodsmoke.|There's a biscuit taped to the lid.}\n> left: Bring her something back\n  gold -tiny\n  health +small\n  rel mabel +small\n  say: {You bring her a jar of honey from the market. She calls it bribery and puts it on the shelf behind the counter.|You bring her flowers from the village stall. \"Don't,\" she says. \"Drink it.\" You do. It works.|You bring her a tin of your own, labelled \"YOU KNOW\". She laughs until she has to sit down, and keeps it.}\n> right: Send it back with a note\n  rel mabel -tiny\n  say: {She sends it back again with a longer note. You give up and drink it.|The tin comes back on the next barge. The label now says \"STUBBORN.\"|You send it back with a note that says \"I KNOW\". It comes back with two biscuits taped to the lid.}\n\n@event mabel_cup\ntitle: Your Cup on the Hook\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: mabel\nwhen: rel(mabel) >= 15\nweight: 18\nart: cup\ntags: social home\nscene: A row of mismatched cups hanging on hooks behind a tea-shop counter, a woman hanging a new one with a student's initial painted on it.\ntext: Behind Mabel's counter is a row of hooks with cups on them, each painted with a letter. You've never asked whose they are. Today, while you watch, Mabel hangs a new one with your initial on it. \"Regulars,\" she says, as if that settled it.\n> left: Ask whose the others are\n  rel mabel +small\n  mood +medium\n  say: She tells you, cup by cup: a fisherman, a dead headmaster, a girl who became a duchess and still writes. \"And you,\" she says. Something in your chest unknots.\n> right: Say thank you and sit down\n  mood +small\n  health +small\n  say: You sit. She brings your tea in your cup. You don't need to say anything else, and she doesn't either.\n\n# ---------------- Mr Grimsby (the bookshop) ----------------\n\n@event grimsby_intro\ntitle: Not That One\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nplace: grimsbys_books\nactor: grimsby\nwhen: not met(grimsby)\nweight: 30\nart: book\ntags: study money\nscene: A cramped bookshop with leaning shelves, an elderly bookseller on a ladder holding out a book to a student below, the student holding a list.\ntext: You ask for a textbook. An elderly man on a ladder says \"Ah!\" with enormous enthusiasm and hands you a history of lighthouses. \"Not what you asked for,\" says Mr Grimsby. \"What you'll need. You'll see.\"\n> left: Buy the lighthouse book\n  meet grimsby\n  gold -small\n  lore +small\n  rel grimsby +small\n  say: You read it on the barge home. Chapter four has the exact proof your essay was missing. When you go back to say so, he only nods. \"They always come back,\" he says. \"Lighthouses.\"\n> right: Say you'll think about it\n  meet grimsby\n  rel grimsby -tiny\n  say: He puts the lighthouses back on the shelf, sadly, like a doctor whose prescription has been refused. \"It'll keep,\" he says. \"So will you.\"\n\n@event grimsby_parcel\ntitle: The Wrong Book, Posted\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: grimsby\nwhen: rel(grimsby) >= 2\nweight: 10\nrepeat: 6\nart: book\ntags: study\nscene: A brown-paper parcel tied with string on a library desk, a student unwrapping an odd old book while a cat watches from the next chair.\ntext: {A parcel arrives from Grimsby's. Inside is a book you didn't order and a note that says \"Thought of you. Pay when convenient. A. G.\"|Another parcel from Mr Grimsby. This one is about eels.|Grimsby's parcels come monthly now, like weather.} {It smells of rain and pipe smoke.|There's a bus ticket from another century inside the cover.|The note is written on the back of a recipe.}\n> left: Keep it and pay him\n  gold -small\n  lore +small\n  rel grimsby +small\n  say: {It's the wrong book, exactly as promised, and somewhere in it is the right page. It always is.|You read it out of politeness and finish it out of something else.|You pay him, and try the recipe on the back of the note. The book and the cake are both strange, and both good.}\n> right: Send it back with the carrier\n  rel grimsby -tiny\n  say: {The carrier takes it back for nothing, grumbling. Grimsby sends a cheerful note that says \"Next time, then.\"|He sends it straight back with a second, wronger book. You keep both.|You send it back. Next month's parcel arrives anyway, as if nothing had happened, with a new recipe on the back of the note.}\n\n@event grimsby_mind_the_shop\ntitle: Mind the Shop\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: grimsby\nwhen: rel(grimsby) >= 15\nweight: 18\nart: key\ntags: study money\nscene: A student alone behind the counter of a crowded old bookshop at dusk, a ring of keys on the counter and a customer peering in through the dusty window.\ntext: Mr Grimsby has a ladder accident (a mild one; he lands on the poetry) and needs someone to mind the shop for a week. \"You know where everything is,\" he says, which is untrue. \"Better than me,\" he adds, which is.\n> left: Mind the shop\n  lore +medium\n  gold +small\n  mood -small\n  rel grimsby +medium\n  say: You sell eleven books, all of them the wrong ones, and every customer comes back to thank you. Grimsby, on crutches, says you have the gift. He means it as a warning.\n> right: \"I can't, I'm sorry.\"\n  rel grimsby -small\n  say: He closes for the week instead, with a sign that says GONE TO LIGHTHOUSE. Nobody knows which one.\n\n# ---------------- Old Pell (Pell & Daughters; the apothecary job) ----------------\n\n@event pell_intro\ntitle: A Thousand Drawers\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nplace: pell_apothecary\nactor: pell\nwhen: not met(pell)\nweight: 30\nart: bottle\ntags: job money\nscene: An old apothecary on a library ladder in front of a wall of tiny labelled drawers, peering down over his spectacles at a student at the counter.\ntext: You ask for something for a headache. An old man on a ladder stops, counts the drawers under his breath and comes down with exactly one twist of paper. \"Willow and feverfew,\" says Old Pell. \"Three pinches. Not four. Four is for people who don't listen.\"\n> left: Ask why not four\n  meet pell\n  alchemy +small\n  rel pell +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: He tells you, at length, with diagrams drawn in the counter dust. He's so pleased someone asked that he forgets to charge you. You leave with the cure, a headache from the lecture and the dosage for nine other things.\n> right: Pay and go\n  meet pell\n  gold -tiny\n  health +tiny\n  say: He nods at the door as you leave, as if adding you to a ledger of people who will be back. The headache is gone before the quay, and you are back within a month.\n\n@event pell_count_drops\ntitle: Count Them Aloud\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: pell\nwhen: rel(pell) >= 0 and member(apothecary_clerk)\nweight: 10\nrepeat: 5\nart: bottle\ntags: job study\nscene: An old apothecary at a wooden counter holding a glass dropper over a vial, a student beside him counting on their fingers, a wall of tiny labelled drawers behind them.\ntext: {Old Pell sets a dropper in your hand. \"Forty. Aloud.\" He watches your lips, not the vial.|\"Forty,\" says Pell, and stands behind you. You can hear him counting along under his breath.|Pell has started leaving you the hard tinctures. That is how he says he trusts you.} {A daughter rolls her eyes at the counter.|The drawers rattle when a cart goes by.|Something in drawer three hundred is humming.}\n> left: Count, aloud, every drop\n  alchemy +small\n  mood -tiny\n  rel pell +small\n  say: {Forty, exactly. Pell says nothing for a full minute, then \"Again.\" You are fairly sure that was praise.|You lose count at thirty-one and start over without being told. Pell grunts approvingly.|Forty, of a tincture that fights back. Pell holds the vial to the light and puts it in drawer three hundred. The humming stops.}\n> right: Eyeball it; it's close enough\n  challenge brew apprentice wild:\n    win:\n      alchemy +tiny\n      say: {It's right. Pell checks it three times and is visibly annoyed that it's right.|Close enough is, today, exactly right. \"Luck,\" says Pell. \"Don't.\"|The hard tincture forgives you, today. Pell sniffs it and puts it away without comment, which is worse than comment.}\n    lose:\n      rel pell -small\n      perform apothecary_clerk -small\n      say: {Forty-three. Pell pours it out without a word and hands you the dropper again. The silence lasts all shift.|Too many. A daughter fixes it before he sees. He sees.|Too many, of the hard one. Drawer three hundred hums louder, and Pell keeps the dropper for the rest of the week.}\n\n@event pell_daughters\ntitle: Pell & Daughters\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: pell\nwhen: rel(pell) >= 20\nweight: 18\nart: scales\ntags: job home\nscene: An old apothecary and his two grown daughters arguing across a counter over a new painted shop sign, a student standing awkwardly between them.\ntext: Pell's daughters want a new sign, brighter labels and a counter that faces the door. Pell wants it to be fifty years ago. All three of them turn to you, the only person in the shop who isn't family. \"Well?\" says Pell.\n> left: Side with the daughters\n  rel pell -small\n  mood +small\n  say: Pell stamps into the back room. A week later the new sign goes up. Underneath it, in small old letters, it still says PELL. He shows you, gruffly, as though you'd asked.\n> right: \"The drawers have never been wrong.\"\n  rel pell +medium\n  if member(apothecary_clerk):\n    gold +small\n    say: Pell glows. The daughters glare. Pell gives you a bonus and the daughters give you all the worst stocktakes. Everyone feels this is fair.\n  else:\n    health +small\n    say: Pell glows. The daughters glare. Pell presses a bottle of his best tonic on you, and the daughters give you a look that lasts a month.\n\n# ---------------- Grisk (the moneylender) ----------------\n\n@event grisk_pleasantries\ntitle: Pleasantries\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: grisk\nwhen: rel(grisk) >= -10 and not flag(grisk_gone)\nweight: 8\nrepeat: 6\nart: pouch\ntags: money social\nscene: A goblin in a velvet waistcoat sitting at a corner table in a low-beamed tavern, raising a small glass to a student, a fat ledger at his elbow.\ntext: {Grisk raises a small glass from the corner table of the Gilded Newt. \"My friend,\" he says. \"Do sit. I'm not working.\"|Grisk, at the corner table again. \"How is the purse?\" he asks, with what sounds like real concern.|{has(in_debt) or flag(grisk_done): \"Ah, my favourite client,\" purrs Grisk.|\"Ah, my favourite non-client,\" purrs Grisk.}} {His ledger stays closed, which feels deliberate.|He is eating a single olive very slowly.|The landlady gives you a warning look.}\n> left: Sit for one drink\n  renown -tiny\n  guile +small\n  rel grisk +small\n  say: {He talks about money the way other people talk about weather, and you learn more in an hour than in a term of Umbral Theory.|He tells you which of the village shops are in trouble and why. It feels like gossip. It is a lesson.|He pays for the drinks and writes nothing down, which is the most unsettling thing he has ever done.}\n> right: Keep walking\n  rel grisk -tiny\n  say: {\"Another time,\" he calls after you. \"I'm very patient.\"|He raises the glass anyway. You feel it between your shoulder blades all the way out.|You keep walking. The landlady nods to you on your way out, very slightly.}\n\n@event grisk_tip\ntitle: A Tip, Between Friends\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: grisk\nwhen: rel(grisk) >= 4 and not has(in_debt) and not flag(grisk_gone)\nweight: 10\nart: coin\ntags: money\nscene: A goblin leaning across a tavern table to murmur into a student's ear, sliding a single gold coin across the wood with one finger.\ntext: Grisk slides a coin across the table. \"Between friends,\" he murmurs. \"The barge company is about to buy a second barge. The ferryman's cousin will sell his share this week, cheap, because he doesn't know. You could know.\"\n> left: Buy the share\n  hint: The share costs a fair few crowns.\n  alignment -tiny\n  gold -medium\n  chance likely:\n    gold +large\n    gold +medium\n    rel grisk +small\n    say: The second barge arrives. The share triples. Grisk lifts his glass across the Newt. You feel rich and slightly unwell.\n  else:\n    say: The second barge sinks on its first crossing. Grisk is sympathetic. \"Risk,\" he says. \"Delicious, and sometimes poison.\"\n> right: Leave the cousin his share\n  rel grisk -tiny\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: Grisk pockets the coin. \"Honest,\" he says, as if diagnosing something. \"It'll pass. Or it won't, and you'll be poor and admired.\"\n"},{"path":"content/events/incidents.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Incidents and money sinks (design §8.2).\n#  A) forced sinks: setbacks that cost crowns or health, always\n#     with a way to soften them. Gated on gold so they drain\n#     savings rather than break poor players.\n#  B) voluntary sinks: patronage and donations for the well-off.\n#  C) year three: a term of Thornwick exchange students.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- A) Forced sinks ----------------\n\n@event inc_fever_bill\ntime: morning\ntitle: A Fever and a Bill\nclass: incident\ntone: tense\nwhen: turn >= 3 and gold >= 30 and seen(inc_fever_bill) < 2 and since(inc_fever_bill) >= 12\nweight: 6\nrepeat: 2\nart: potion\ntags: life money\nscene: A pale student in an infirmary bed, a stern nurse holding out a small blue bottle and a folded bill.\ntext: You wake in the infirmary with a fever that makes the ceiling breathe. Sister Amaranth holds up a small blue bottle. \"This clears it by morning. It is also not free,\" she says. \"Or you can sweat it out, like your ancestors.\"\n> left: Buy the tonic\n  gold -large\n  health -small\n  say: {~It tastes of tin and regret.|It tastes like a pond that has given up.} By morning the ceiling is a ceiling again, and your purse is noticeably lighter.\n> right: Sweat it out\n  health -medium\n  study -small\n  say: Three days of damp sheets and strange dreams about stairs. You miss a week of notes, and come back feeling like a wrung-out dishcloth.\n\n@event inc_broken_wand\ntitle: Snapped on the Stairs\nclass: incident\ntone: tense\nwhen: turn >= 3 and gold >= 30 and seen(inc_broken_wand) == 0\nweight: 6\nrepeat: 1\nart: wand\ntags: magic money\nscene: A student kneeling on a stone staircase, holding the two halves of a wand that still fizzes faintly at the break.\ntext: The moving stairs shift without warning. You catch yourself; your wand doesn't. It lies in two pieces on the landing, still fizzing at the break like a sulking firework.\n> left: Have it properly rebound\n  gold -large\n  say: The repairer in the village tuts, splints it with silver wire and charges you for every tut. It works better than before, which is somehow worse.\n> right: Bind it with string and hope\n  challenge spell apprentice wild:\n    win:\n      mood -tiny\n      say: It holds. It spits a little when you cast, like a cat that forgives you but wants it noted.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      study -small\n      say: It holds for three days, then sneezes a spell backwards. Your eyebrows grow back within the week; your marks take longer.\n\n@event inc_library_fine\ntitle: The Overdue Notice\nclass: incident\ntone: light\nwhen: turn >= 3 and gold >= 30 and seen(inc_library_fine) < 2 and since(inc_library_fine) >= 12\nweight: 6\nrepeat: 2\nart: paper\ntags: study money\nscene: A prefect in a red sash holding out a long paper notice, a book with teeth marks on the desk between them.\ntext: A prefect in a red sash slides a notice across your desk. The library book you borrowed has come back late, damp and, somehow, with teeth marks. \"Damages,\" she says. \"Or you can argue. People do. It never goes well.\"\n> left: Pay the fine\n  gold -medium\n  mood -tiny\n  say: She stamps your receipt with great satisfaction. The book is reshelved in a cage, which feels like the right outcome for everyone.\n> right: Argue that the book bit first\n  challenge negotiate apprentice+ unlikely:\n    win:\n      mood +tiny\n      say: You produce the bite on your thumb as evidence. The prefect inspects it, then the book, and waives the fine. \"Fair,\" she says. \"It has a history.\"\n    lose:\n      gold -large\n      study -tiny\n      say: The prefect writes down every word you say, then doubles the fine for \"contempt of librarian\". You pay it, and lose an evening's revision to the demerit hearing besides.\n\n@event inc_letter_home\ntitle: Bad Weather at Home\nclass: incident\ntone: grim\nwhen: turn >= 3 and gold >= 30 and seen(inc_letter_home) == 0\nweight: 6\nrepeat: 1\nart: letter\ntags: home money\nscene: A student at a window reading a rain-spotted letter, a small pile of coins on the sill beside them.\ntext: A letter from home, written in a hurry. A storm took half the roof, and the repairs will cost more than anyone has. Nobody asks you for anything. That's how you know it's bad.\n> left: Send crowns home\n  gold -large\n  alignment +small\n  mood -small\n  say: You send everything you can spare and a little you can't. The reply comes a month later: a drawing of a roof, finished, and a pressed flower.\n> right: Write back with love, not money\n  mood -medium\n  say: You write four pages and keep your purse. The reply is kind. It doesn't mention the roof. That night you lie awake and listen to the rain.\n\n@event inc_stolen_purse\ntitle: Market Day Fingers\nclass: incident\ntone: tense\nwhen: turn >= 3 and gold >= 30 and seen(inc_stolen_purse) < 2 and since(inc_stolen_purse) >= 12\nweight: 6\nrepeat: 2\nart: pouch\ntags: money danger\nscene: A crowded village square on market day, a figure in a grey hood slipping away between the stalls with a small purse.\ntext: Somewhere between the cheese stall and the man selling enchanted socks, your purse stops being yours. You catch a grey hood slipping between the stalls, already three stalls ahead.\n> left: Run after the hood\n  challenge feat apprentice+:\n    win:\n      gold -tiny\n      health -tiny\n      say: You corner the thief behind the bakery. The purse comes back a little lighter; they'd already spent some on a bun. You let them keep the bun.\n    lose:\n      gold -large\n      health -small\n      mood -small\n      say: You trip over a goose, then a second goose. When you get up, the hood is gone, and so are your crowns.\n> right: Let it go\n  gold -large\n  mood -small\n  say: You tell yourself it's only money. You tell yourself that all the way back up the hill, and it never quite takes.\n\n@event inc_hiccuping_robe\ntime: morning\ntitle: The Robe That Hiccups\nclass: incident\ntone: light\nwhen: turn >= 3 and gold >= 20 and seen(inc_hiccuping_robe) == 0\nweight: 6\nrepeat: 1\nart: cauldron\ntags: magic money\nscene: A student in potion-stained robes jolting mid-step as their robe gives a visible hiccup, classmates hiding smiles.\ntext: A cauldron two benches down boils over, and a whole batch of Draught of Unending Hiccups soaks your best robe. The stain fades by supper. The hiccups don't. Every few minutes, the robe jumps.\n> left: Buy a new robe\n  gold -medium\n  mood -tiny\n  say: The tailor in the village fits you in an afternoon and charges you for a week. The old robe now hiccups quietly in the bottom of your trunk.\n> right: Wear it with dignity\n  renown -small\n  mood -small\n  say: {~It hiccups through a lecture on silence.|It hiccups through the headmistress's announcements.} Someone starts a tally on the common-room wall. You are briefly famous for the wrong reasons.\n\n# ---------------- B) Voluntary sinks: patronage ----------------\n\n@event patron_tournament_purse\ntitle: A Patron's Purse\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: (month == 7 or month == 8) and gold >= 80\nweight: 10\nrepeat: 12\nart: trophy\ntags: festival money\nscene: A festival committee table on a lawn, a thin purse on a velvet cushion and a list of patrons with plenty of space left.\ntext: The Spring Tournament committee has a velvet cushion, a very thin prize purse and a list of patrons with a lot of room left on it. Patrons are read out before the first bout, in a loud voice, to the whole academy.\n> left: Fund the champion's purse\n  gold -large\n  renown +medium\n  say: {~Your name is read out first, and mispronounced only slightly.|Your name rings across the lawn before the first bout. Two first-years bow to you, unsure why.} The champion thanks you from the podium.\n> right: Cheer from the stands instead\n  mood +tiny\n  say: Other names are read out, among them a professor's cat and \"a well-wisher\". You cheer for all of them, and loudest for the champion.\n\n@event patron_ledger_print\ntitle: Paper for the Press\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: turn >= 6 and gold >= 80\nweight: 6\nrepeat: 12\nart: paper\ntags: social money\nscene: A cramped office under a staircase, a groaning printing press, and a young editor holding out an empty paper tray.\ntext: Nessa Quill corners you by the library stairs, holding an empty paper tray like evidence. \"Special edition. Biggest story of the term. No paper. You,\" she says, pointing, \"look solvent. Patrons get their name on the masthead.\"\n> left: Pay for the whole print run\n  gold -large\n  rel nessa +medium\n  renown +small\n  say: \"Headline: generous student saves free press,\" Nessa says, already writing it. The masthead lists you in tiny capitals, which she says is the most honour anyone gets.\n> right: Help her fold the edition instead\n  rel nessa +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: You'd rather give her your time than your crowns. Nessa scrounges paper from the Ledger's scrap bin, and you fold until midnight, fingers grey with ink. Nessa credits you on the back page as \"the folding department\".\n\n# ---------------- B) Voluntary sinks: donations ----------------\n\n@event donate_greenhouse_glass\ntime: morning\ntitle: The Broken Panes\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: turn >= 3 and gold >= 80 and not discovered(greenhouses)\nweight: 8\nrepeat: 3\nart: leaf\ntags: nature money\nscene: A collection tin on a garden gate, broken glass glittering in the grass behind it and steam escaping from a damaged greenhouse.\ntext: Last night's hail went through the greenhouse glass like a hammer through toffee. A collection tin hangs on the gate with a hand-lettered sign: every pane paid for keeps the mandrakes from catching cold.\n> left: Pay for a whole pane\n  gold -large\n  mood +small\n  discover greenhouses\n  say: A gardener with mud to the elbows insists on showing you your pane. Inside it is warm, green and loud with opinions. You are welcome, she says, any morning.\n> right: Walk on\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You walk past the tin. Behind the gate, something small and leafy sneezes. You feel, obscurely, that you've been judged.\n\n@event donate_duel_floor\ntitle: The Scorched Floor\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: turn >= 3 and gold >= 80 and not discovered(duel_hall)\nweight: 8\nrepeat: 3\nart: sword\ntags: club money\nscene: A sweaty duellist with a collection box at the foot of a stair, scorched floorboards stacked behind them.\ntext: A duellist with a bandaged hand rattles a box at the foot of the east stair. \"The hall floor,\" he says. \"Someone set it on fire again. Every board you buy, we carve your initials on the underside. Honour, and splinters.\"\n> left: Buy a row of floorboards\n  gold -large\n  renown +small\n  discover duel_hall\n  say: He drags you upstairs to see. The hall smells of smoke and wax, and the practice dummies glare at you. Your initials go on the underside of the board by the door.\n> right: Wish him luck with the bandage\n  mood -tiny\n  say: He thanks you with exaggerated courtesy and goes back to rattling. Somewhere above, steel rings on steel, and nobody invites you up.\n\n# ---------------- C) Year three: the Thornwick exchange ----------------\n\n@event xchg_arrival\ntitle: Guests in Green\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: year == 3 and month <= 2 and seen(xchg_arrival) == 0\nweight: 12\nart: mask\ntags: social rival\nscene: Four students in green Thornwick robes standing in a grand entrance hall, the portraits on the walls all turned to stare at them.\ntext: Four students in Thornwick green arrive for a term, with trunks and very polite smiles. Within an hour every portrait in the entrance hall is winking at the wrong people. A prefect looks around for someone, anyone, to show them the castle.\n> left: Offer to show them round\n  challenge negotiate apprentice+:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      charm +small\n      say: You give the full tour, moving stairs included. By the end the boy with the sketchbook admits Lumenhold's towers are \"not as crooked as reported\".\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      renown -tiny\n      say: Halfway round, they convince you a wall is a door. It isn't. They are very sorry, and laughing too hard to say so.\n> right: Watch them from a distance\n  guile +small\n  say: You watch them all week. They watch back. You learn that the quiet one counts doorways, and that they know you're watching.\n\n@event xchg_ink_cuffs\ntitle: The Ink-Stained Cuffs\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: year == 3 and month <= 4 and seen(xchg_arrival) > 0 and seen(xchg_farewell) == 0\nweight: 12\nart: book\ntags: study rival\nscene: A library at dusk, a student in green robes with ink-stained cuffs copying from a book chained to a desk.\ntext: Late in the library, you find the Thornwick girl with the ink-stained cuffs copying from a chained book she shouldn't have reached. She sees you see her. \"It's only history,\" she says. \"Unless you want it to be a scandal.\"\n> left: Report her to the librarian\n  renown +small\n  mood -small\n  say: The librarian thanks you, coldly. The Thornwick girl is confined to the guest wing for a week. She nods to you at dinner afterwards, as if you had both played well.\n> right: Keep quiet, for a favour\n  alignment -tiny\n  guile +small\n  say: She tears out the page she copied and hands you the other half of her notes instead. \"We're even,\" she says. You're fairly sure you aren't.\n\n@event xchg_farewell\ntitle: A Thornwick Farewell\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nwhen: year == 3 and month <= 4 and seen(xchg_arrival) > 0 and since(xchg_arrival) >= 2 and seen(xchg_farewell) == 0\nweight: 12\nart: group\ntags: social rival\nscene: Students in green and blue robes on a jetty at dawn, a barge waiting, one of them holding out a striped scarf.\ntext: The Thornwick students leave on today's barge. The boy with the sketchbook finds you on the jetty and holds out his scarf. \"It's a custom,\" he says. \"Probably. I've just decided.\" Behind him his friends pretend not to listen.\n> left: Swap scarves\n  mood +medium\n  renown -tiny\n  say: You trade scarves on the jetty. Someone from your own house mutters \"traitor\" at breakfast. You wear it anyway, and get a letter from across the hills by the end of the month.\n> right: Challenge him to one last duel\n  challenge combat adept- wild:\n    win:\n      renown +medium\n      say: You win, narrowly, on the wet boards. He laughs, bows, and sketches you mid-spell on the barge as it pulls away. The sketch arrives by post, flattering.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood +small\n      say: He wins with an illusion of a second you. You both end up in the lake. It is, somehow, the best goodbye you've had.\n"},{"path":"content/events/jobs.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Job events — drawn while a job state is active.\n#  Jobs are taken at the Job Board (calendar.grim).\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- Library Assistant ----------------\n\n@event lib_biting_book\ntitle: The Book That Bites\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: library_assistant\nweight: 14\nart: book\ntags: job\nscene: A thick leather book with tiny teeth along its pages snapping at a hand reaching for it on a high shelf.\ntext: Reshelving in the zoology section, a fat leather book clamps onto your finger and refuses to let go. The head librarian, without looking up, says: \"Stroke the spine.\"\n> left: Muzzle it with a belt\n  wage\n  health -tiny\n  vigor +small\n  say: You wrestle it into submission and shelve it. It growls at you every time you pass for the rest of the term.\n> right: Stroke the spine and read it\n  wage\n  lore +medium\n  health -tiny\n  perform track -tiny\n  say: It purrs, opens and turns out to be a fascinating treatise on carnivorous plants. It only bites twice more. The reshelving waits until tomorrow.\n\n@event lib_overdue_professor\ntitle: Overdue\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: met(thorne)\ntrack: library_assistant\nweight: 12\nart: hourglass\ntags: job\nscene: A severe professor towering over a library desk, a stack of books under one arm, a very small student assistant on the other side holding a fines ledger.\ntext: Professor Thorne returns a book that is eleven years overdue. The fine, by library rules, is forty crowns. He looks at you. You look at the ledger.\n> left: Waive the fine\n  wage\n  rel thorne +tiny\n  say: \"Hm,\" says Thorne. You can't tell if you've earned his respect or lost it.\n> right: Enforce the rules\n  wage\n  rel thorne +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: A long silence. Then he pays, in exact change. \"Good,\" he says, and you could swear he almost smiles.\n\n@event lib_restricted_cart\ntitle: The Restricted Cart\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\ntrack: library_assistant\nweight: 12\nart: lock\ntags: job mystery\nscene: A wheeled library cart chained shut, piled with black-bound books, parked alone in a candlelit aisle.\ntext: You are asked to wheel the Restricted Section returns to the vault. The cart's chain has come loose. One of the books has fallen open on a page that is writing itself.\n> left: Close it without looking\n  wage\n  perform track +tiny\n  say: The book snaps shut with a sound like a disappointed sigh. The vault door clangs. The head librarian notes the time, approvingly.\n> right: Read a few lines\n  wage\n  lore +medium\n  chance unlikely:\n    gain umbral_curse\n    say: The words crawl off the page and up your sleeve. That night, you can't get warm.\n  else:\n    say: A spell for walking through mirrors. You will never try it. Probably.\n\n# ---------------- The Gilded Newt ----------------\n\n@event tavern_brawl\ntitle: Brawl at the Newt\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrack: tavern\nweight: 14\nart: cup\ntags: job danger\nscene: A low-beamed tavern mid-brawl: a chair in mid-air, a dwarf on a table, cider sloshing everywhere.\ntext: A dwarf and a troll disagree about the price of turnips, and the Gilded Newt erupts. A chair flies past your head. The owner is hiding behind the bar, gesturing at you to do something.\n> left: Duck behind the bar too\n  wage\n  say: You crouch beside the owner and share a bag of crisps while the room destroys itself.\n> right: Break it up\n  wage\n  challenge combat apprentice+ wild:\n    win:\n      gold +medium\n      renown +small\n      say: You slam a tray on the bar and bellow. Silence. The troll apologises. The dwarf tips you fifteen crowns.\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      say: You step in. Someone's elbow steps into your face.\n\n@event tavern_star_coin\ntitle: A Generous Stranger\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrack: tavern\nweight: 12\nart: coin\ntags: job mystery hollowstar\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: A hooded figure at a corner table sliding a black coin across the wood, its surface stamped with a dark star.\ntext: A hooded stranger nurses a cider all evening and leaves a tip: a black coin stamped with a hollow star. \"For the student,\" they say. \"Tell Kael Dumont his family sends regards.\"\n> left: Refuse the coin\n  wage\n  say: The stranger shrugs and pockets it. When you look again, the corner table is empty and the cider is frozen solid.\n> right: Take it\n  wage\n  gold +tiny\n  flag star_coin\n  alignment -small\n  say: The coin is cold as ice and heavier than it should be. You find yourself turning it over in your pocket for days.\n\n@event tavern_extra_shifts\ntitle: More Shifts\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: tavern\nweight: 10\nart: coin\ntags: job money\nscene: A ruddy tavern owner leaning on the bar, wiping a mug, a rota pinned behind him covered in crossed-out names.\ntext: The owner is short-handed. \"Double shifts for a month,\" he says. \"Double pay. I know you've got studies, but you've also got rent.\"\n> left: Decline\n  wage\n  study +tiny\n  say: \"Fair enough,\" he says, and asks again next week.\n> right: Take the extra shifts\n  wage overtime\n  health -medium\n  study -small\n  say: A month of late nights and early lectures. Your purse is heavy. So are your eyelids.\n\n# Hazards of the job (L-1): minor incidents that show why the Newt's pay is higher.\n\n@event tavern_broken_glass\ntitle: Broken Glass\nclass: incident\ntone: tense\ntrack: tavern\nrepeat: 6\nweight: 8\nart: cup\ntags: job danger\nscene: A tavern floor glittering with broken tankards, a server crouching with a cloth, a dog padding closer.\ntext: {A tray of tankards meets a regular's elbow, and the floor is suddenly all glass.|Another tray, another floor of glass. The Newt goes through tankards the way other places go through napkins.|Glass on the floor again, and the same regular's elbow to blame.} {The regular is very sorry, loudly.|Everyone lifts their feet.|The dog is already heading for it.}\n> left: Clear it by hand, fast\n  wage\n  health -small\n  perform track +tiny\n  say: {You have it cleared before the dog gets there, with a cut across your palm to show for it. The landlady binds it with a bar towel.|Fast and thorough, apart from the sliver you find in your thumb at midnight.|You beat the dog to it by a whisker and a nicked finger. The landlady gives the dog a sausage for its trouble.}\n> right: Fetch the broom and do it properly\n  wage\n  perform track -tiny\n  say: {By the time you're back with the broom, three customers have left with their cider unfinished. The landlady counts them.|You sweep slowly and safely while the bar goes dry. The landlady taps the clock.|You go for the broom, and the landlady has to hold the dog back by the collar while the customers' cider goes warm.}\n\n@event tavern_after_closing\ntitle: After Closing\nclass: incident\ntone: tense\ntrack: tavern\nrepeat: 6\nweight: 8\nart: lantern\ntags: job danger\nscene: A lantern-lit shore path at night, a tired server walking alone beside black water, the castle's towers far off.\ntext: {Your shift tonight runs until the Newt closes, long after the last barge.|Another late shift. By closing time the lake will be dark.|Late shift again. You've stopped expecting to see the last barge.} {season == \"winter\": These nights the shore path is ice under snow.|{Fog comes off the water most nights now.|The shore path has no lamps.|Somebody always sings, badly, on the way home.}} It's a long walk round the lake to the castle.\n> left: Walk the shore path\n  wage\n  health -small\n  say: {Two hours round the lake, and every noise in the reeds is something. You reach the gates with wet boots and a turned ankle.|You walk home in the dark and slip twice. You fall into bed as the first bell rings.|You walk home with the other late workers, singing badly to keep the dark off. You're hoarse by the gates, and late to bed.}\n> right: Sleep by the Newt's fire\n  wage\n  study -tiny\n  say: {The landlady lends you a blanket and the settle by the fire. You wake at dawn smelling of woodsmoke and miss the first lecture.|You sleep on the settle under somebody's coat and catch the morning barge, too late for your first class.|You hear the others singing their way home along the shore and choose the fire. The settle is lumpy, and you miss the first lecture anyway.}\n\n# ---------------- Apothecary Clerk ----------------\n\n@event apoth_wrong_label\ntitle: A Mislabelled Jar\nclass: windfall\ntone: light\ntrack: apothecary_clerk\nweight: 14\nart: bottle\ntags: job\nscene: Shelves of glass jars in a narrow shop, one jar labelled SLEEPING DRAUGHT glowing an ominous green.\ntext: The jar labelled \"Sleeping Draught\" is green. Sleeping Draught is blue. You're fairly sure the green stuff is Draught of Unending Hiccups. Old Pell is very proud of his labelling.\n> left: Quietly fix the label\n  wage\n  alchemy +tiny\n  say: Nobody ever knows. Somewhere, a customer sleeps soundly instead of hiccupping for a week.\n> right: Point it out to Pell\n  wage\n  gold +tiny\n  alchemy +tiny\n  say: Pell goes red, then thanks you gruffly and slips you three crowns \"for your eyes\".\n\n@event apoth_poor_mother\ntitle: Can't Afford It\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: apothecary_clerk\nweight: 12\nart: potion\ntags: job\nscene: A tired woman at a shop counter counting copper coins, a small child coughing beside her.\ntext: A woman counts out coppers for fever tonic. She's fifteen crowns short. Her son coughs into her coat. Pell is in the back room.\n> left: Charge the full price\n  wage\n  if gold >= 15:\n    alignment -small\n  mood -small\n  say: She leaves without it. The bell on the door rings for a very long time.\n> right: Pay the difference yourself\n  wage\n  alignment +small\n  gold -medium\n  mood +medium\n  renown +small\n  say: You slip the tonic into her basket. Next week a pie appears on the counter with your name on it.\n\n@event apoth_nightshade_order\ntitle: The Nightshade Order\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\ntrack: apothecary_clerk\nweight: 10\nart: skull\ntags: job mystery\nscene: An order slip on a counter listing a dozen deadly reagents, signed with an elegant, illegible flourish.\ntext: A note arrives with a sealed purse: a large order of nightshade, grave-moss and powdered moonstone. Legal, technically. Ingredients for something very old and very forbidden, also technically.\n> left: Fill the order\n  wage\n  gold +medium\n  flag filled_dark_order\n  say: The courier who collects it never shows his face. The purse is very generous.\n> right: Refuse and report it\n  wage\n  rel isolde +small\n  renown +small\n  say: The headmistress listens to your report in silence. \"Thank you,\" she says. \"Tell no one else.\" She looks worried.\n\n# ---------------- Familiar Stables ----------------\n\n@event stables_hippogriff\ntitle: Bow to the Hippogriff\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nplace: stables\nweight: 14\nart: feather\ntags: job nature\nscene: A proud hippogriff in a straw-strewn stall, head lowered, regarding a stable hand who is bowing very deeply.\ntext: The new hippogriff won't let anyone near the feed trough. The stablemaster says to bow, keep eye contact and not blink. \"Whatever you do, don't blink.\"\n> left: Leave the feed and run\n  say: You toss the feed bucket and flee. The hippogriff eats it, and the bucket.\n> right: Bow and hold its gaze\n  challenge feat apprentice:\n    win:\n      vigor +small\n      mood +small\n      say: It bows back. After that it follows you around the stables like a large, feathered dog.\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      say: You blinked. It knocks you flat against the trough, and you spend the evening in the infirmary with your arm in a sling.\n\n@event stables_dragon_egg\ntitle: An Egg in the Hay\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nwhen: not flag(dragon_egg)\nplace: stables\nweight: 10\nart: dragon\ntags: job mystery\nscene: A speckled egg the size of a melon nestled in hay, steam rising from it, a stable hand's lantern held over it.\ntext: Mucking out the far stall, you find an egg the size of a melon, nestled in the hay and steaming gently. It's very warm. It's very definitely not a chicken's.\n> left: Report it to Professor Brask\n  renown +small\n  rel brask +tiny\n  say: Brask nearly faints. \"Smugglers! A firedrake egg!\" It's taken to the menagerie. You get a commendation.\n> right: Keep it\n  alignment -tiny\n  flag dragon_egg\n  queue egg_hatches in 3\n  say: You smuggle it back to your dormitory wrapped in a scarf. It purrs. Eggs shouldn't purr.\n\n@event egg_hatches\ntitle: It Hatches\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: queued\nart: dragon\ntags: danger mystery\nscene: A tiny red dragon perched on a scorched dormitory pillow, a puff of smoke rising from its nostrils, a horrified roommate in the doorway.\ntext: At 3am your pillow catches fire. The egg has hatched: a firedrake the size of a kitten, delighted with itself and setting fire to everything it loves. It loves you very much.\n> left: Hand it over to the menagerie\n  mood -tiny\n  renown +small\n  say: Brask takes it gently. \"You'll be able to visit.\" You do, every week. It always tries to set you on fire, affectionately.\n> right: Hide it as long as you can\n  challenge deceive apprentice wild:\n    win:\n      mood +medium\n      gold -medium\n      say: You keep it secret for a month — buying fireproof sheets, raw meat, an asbestos sock — before releasing it in the mountains. It circles once, roaring.\n    lose:\n      gain detention\n      study -medium\n      health -small\n      say: The smoke alarm charm goes off in three towers at once. Discovered, singed, and in very deep trouble.\n\n@event stables_night_shift\ntitle: The Sick Pegasus\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nplace: stables\nweight: 12\nart: moon\ntags: job nature\nscene: A white pegasus lying in a moonlit stall, wings folded, a student sitting beside it with a lantern and a blanket.\ntext: The oldest pegasus in the stables is sick. Someone has to sit with her overnight, keep her warm, and make sure she drinks. It's unpaid. Old Maud says she'll do it if no one else will. Maud is eighty.\n> left: Let Maud do it\n  health +tiny\n  alignment -tiny\n  say: You sleep well. Maud is a little slower the next day.\n> right: Take the night shift\n  health -tiny\n  alignment +tiny\n  mood +small\n  rel maud +small\n  say: She drinks. You talk to her all night about home. At dawn she stands, shakes her wings, and nuzzles your hair.\n\n# ---------------- First-Year Tutor ----------------\n\n@event tutor_hopeless\ntitle: The Hopeless Case\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nplace: courtyard\nweight: 14\nart: wand\ntags: job\nscene: A tiny first-year in oversized robes pointing a wand at a feather, which is stubbornly not moving, while a tutor watches with infinite patience.\ntext: Your tutee has been trying to levitate a feather for three weeks. The feather has not moved. The tutee is starting to cry. You have one more session before their exam.\n> left: Drill the basics, again\n  arcana +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: Wrist, word, will. Wrist, word, will. On the exam, the feather twitches. It's enough to pass. Your own levitation has never been cleaner.\n> right: Try something unorthodox\n  challenge negotiate apprentice wild:\n    win:\n      mood +medium\n      renown +small\n      say: You tell them to imagine the feather is in love with the ceiling. It shoots upward and sticks there. They pass with distinction.\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      say: You tell them to imagine the feather is in love with the ceiling. They are confused. So is the feather.\n\n@event tutor_bribe\ntitle: An Indecent Proposal\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nplace: courtyard\nweight: 12\nart: pouch\ntags: job money\nscene: A rich student in tailored robes sliding a heavy coin purse across a study table, smiling smugly.\ntext: A rich second-year doesn't want tutoring. He wants an essay. \"Thirty crowns,\" he says, dropping a purse on the table. \"Nobody needs to know.\"\n> left: Refuse\n  study +tiny\n  say: He sneers and goes elsewhere. You spend the evening on your own essays. Later you see him in detention, scrubbing a cauldron.\n> right: Write the essay\n  alignment -small\n  gold +large\n  chance unlikely:\n    study -large\n    gain detention\n    say: His professor recognises your style. You both end up in detention. He doesn't speak to you. Fine.\n  else:\n    guile +tiny\n    say: He gets a decent mark. You get thirty crowns and a slightly sour feeling.\n\n@event tutor_prodigy\ntitle: Surpassed\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nplace: courtyard\nweight: 10\nart: sparkle\ntags: job\nscene: A small first-year casting a dazzling spell that fills a classroom with light, their tutor watching with complicated feelings.\ntext: Your newest tutee, a first-year barely a month in, casts in a week what took you a year. Then she asks you a question you can't answer.\n> left: Bluff an answer\n  guile +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  alignment -tiny\n  say: She nods politely. You both know.\n> right: Admit you don't know and find out together\n  lore +small\n  mood +small\n  say: You spend an evening in the library together. She thanks you as if you've taught her something. Maybe you have.\n\n# ---------------- Broom Courier ----------------\n\n@event courier_storm\ntitle: Delivery in a Storm\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrack: broom_courier\nweight: 14\nart: bolt\ntags: job danger\nscene: A courier on a broom bent low against a lightning-lit sky, a parcel strapped to their back, rain sheeting sideways.\ntext: An urgent parcel needs to cross the lake tonight. There's a storm. The dispatcher offers double pay to whoever's fool enough. Everyone looks at you.\n> left: Wait for the storm to pass\n  wage\n  gold -tiny\n  say: The parcel arrives late. The client deducts it from your wages. Rude, but survivable.\n> right: Fly through it\n  challenge feat adept- wild:\n    win:\n      wage overtime\n      vigor +small\n      say: You thread the lightning like a needle and land soaked, laughing, with the parcel dry.\n    lose:\n      health -large\n      gain injured\n      say: Lightning strikes close enough to stand your hair on end. You lose the broom, the parcel and the pay, and gain a limp.\n\n@event courier_whispering_parcel\ntitle: The Whispering Parcel\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrack: broom_courier\nweight: 10\nart: letter\ntags: job mystery\nscene: A brown paper parcel tied with black string on a courier's satchel, frost forming on its corners, faint whispers rising from it.\ntext: Your parcel is addressed to \"K. Dumont, Stillwater House\". It is ice cold and it whispers when you aren't looking at it.\n> left: Deliver it unopened\n  wage\n  rel kael +small\n  meet kael\n  say: Kael takes it without a word. His hands shake. The next day, he looks like he hasn't slept.\n> right: Peek inside\n  wage\n  alignment -tiny\n  challenge deceive apprentice+:\n    win:\n      flag read_kael_parcel\n      lore +small\n      say: Inside: a letter in a cold, beautiful hand. \"The Star wakes. Come home, or open the door. Your family.\" You reseal it perfectly.\n    lose:\n      rel kael -medium\n      meet kael\n      say: The seal hisses as it breaks. Kael is waiting at the door when you arrive. He saw. He says nothing, which is worse.\n\n@event courier_sky_pirates\ntitle: Sky Pirates\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrack: broom_courier\nweight: 10\nart: broom\ntags: job danger\nscene: Three ragged figures on patched brooms surrounding a courier above the clouds, cutlasses glinting.\ntext: Three figures on patched brooms drop out of a cloud and box you in. \"Toll,\" says their leader, a woman with a parrot that is also on a tiny broom. \"Eight crowns or the parcel.\"\n> left: Pay the toll\n  wage\n  gold -small\n  say: They let you pass with a salute. The parrot salutes too.\n> right: Outfly them\n  wage\n  challenge feat journeyman+:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      gold +tiny\n      say: You dive through a cloudbank and lose them over the lake. The client tips extra for the excitement.\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      gold -small\n      say: They catch you, take the toll, the parcel and your hat. The parrot wears it.\n\n# Hazards of the job (L-1): minor incidents that show why the courier's pay is higher.\n\n@event broom_courier_bad_landing\ntitle: A Bad Landing\nclass: incident\ntone: tense\ntrack: broom_courier\nrepeat: 6\nweight: 8\nart: broom\ntags: job danger\nscene: A courier sprawled on wet flagstones beside a broom, parcels scattered across a rain-slick courtyard.\ntext: {The courtyard flagstones are wet, and your broom has opinions about wet flagstones.|Another wet landing. Your knees remember the last one.|Wet flagstones again. You've learned to dread this courtyard.} {You come in too fast.|A gust takes the tail as you drop.|A pigeon chooses this moment to exist.} You hit the ground rolling, and your ankle says no.\n> left: Finish the round anyway\n  wage\n  health -small\n  perform track +tiny\n  say: {You limp the last three parcels to their doors. The dispatcher notes your time and, later, your ankle.|You finish the round on one good leg and a great deal of stubbornness. The bruise is spectacular by evening.|You finish the round with a limp and a grudge against pigeons. The dispatcher writes down both.}\n> right: Hand your sack to another courier\n  wage short\n  health -tiny\n  say: {Another courier takes your sack with a sympathetic wince. Half a shift's pay, and an evening with your foot up.|You sit on the loft steps with a cold cloth on your ankle while someone else flies your round. They keep half the pay.|You hand over your sack and spend the evening on the loft steps, foot up, glaring at pigeons.}\n\n@event broom_courier_lost_parcel\ntitle: One Parcel Short\nclass: incident\ntone: tense\ntrack: broom_courier\nrepeat: 6\nweight: 8\nart: letter\ntags: job danger\nscene: A courier on a windy tower top turning an empty mail sack inside out, the lake far below.\ntext: {Somewhere between the loft and the lecture tower, a parcel has left your sack.|One parcel short, again. You've started counting at every landing.|Another parcel gone. You count at every landing, and still you lose one.} {It was marked \"Fragile\".|It was marked \"Urgent\", in red, twice.|It was addressed to a professor.}\n> left: Retrace your route\n  challenge research using guile apprentice:\n    win:\n      wage\n      health -tiny\n      say: {You find it in a hedge behind the greenhouses, whole and slightly damp. You deliver it late, with a story nobody believes.|It's on the boathouse roof. You climb up for it and deliver it with moss on your knees.|You find it in a gutter of the lecture tower and deliver it to the professor, who signs for it without looking up.}\n    lose:\n      wage short\n      perform track -small\n      say: {You search until dark. The loft takes the parcel's worth from your pay, and the dispatcher stops calling you reliable.|Nothing. The client complains twice, in writing, and the loss comes out of your wages.|The professor's parcel never turns up. The professor does, at the loft, and the loss comes out of your wages.}\n> right: Report it lost at once\n  wage short\n  say: {The dispatcher writes it in the ledger and the loss in your pay. \"Happens,\" they say, in a voice that means it shouldn't.|You own up straight away. The loft takes it out of your wages, but the dispatcher seems to think better of you for it.|You report it at once. The dispatcher winces at the address, a professor's. It comes out of your pay, with an apology to write.}\n\n# ---------------- Off shift (playtest 2, I1) ----------------\n\n# `@game off_shift`: pushed on the first visit to a job's place after the term's last shift, once a run for every\n# job together. A hint not to work overtime; the visit then draws the place's ordinary cards.\n@event job_off_shift\ntitle: Not Your Shift\ntrigger: queued\nclass: incident\ntone: light\nart: hourglass\ntags: job\nscene: A duty roster pinned by a staff door, every one of a student's shifts for the term already ticked off.\ntext: You turn up for work out of habit. The roster by the door says your shifts this term are done, and whoever runs the place says so too. \"Nobody pays you to be here today. Go and have a life.\"\n> left: Take the time for yourself\n  mood +small\n  say: {~You leave before anyone can change their mind, and spend the hours on nothing in particular. It's wonderful.|You take the advice. The place gets on without you, and so, it turns out, do you.}\n> right: Stay and help anyway\n  renown +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~You stay, unpaid. Someone notices. Nobody thanks you, and you're tired the next morning.|You lend a hand for an hour. It's not a shift and it doesn't pay, but people remember who stayed.}\n"},{"path":"content/events/last_day.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  The last day (playtest 2, X10; fills M21's stub). Month 48 is\n#  Starfall of the final year and holds only pushed cards, in\n#  priority order: the morning, the results, the career, the\n#  lake, the people, the speech (expansion_y3.grim), the last\n#  Starfall, and the eve of graduation (calendar.grim). Each card\n#  looks back on what the run was; the endings follow.\n# ============================================================\n\n@event last_morning\ntitle: The Last Morning\nclass: story\ntone: warm\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: turn == max_turns\npriority: 95\nart: tower\ntags: story\nscene: A dormitory room in early light, trunks packed and strapped, graduation robes hanging on the wardrobe door, the towers gold through the window.\ntext: You wake before the bell, out of habit, and there's no bell. Your trunk is packed and strapped. The robes hang on the wardrobe door. It's Starfall, the last day of the year, and tomorrow you graduate. The castle is very quiet, as if it's listening.\n> ok: Get up and begin the last day\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {has(origin_noble): You dress with care, the way you were raised to.|{has(origin_street): You check your pockets out of habit, then laugh at yourself.|You dress slowly, wanting the morning to last.}} The staircase that moves waits for you at the top, for once, as if it knows.\n\n@event last_results\ntitle: The Final Lists\nclass: story\ntone: tense\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: turn == max_turns\npriority: 90\nart: scroll\ntags: story study\nscene: A crowd of final-year students around a great oak door in the entrance hall, long lists of names pinned to it, one student reaching up to touch a line.\ntext: The final lists are pinned to the great hall door, and the whole year is crowded in front of them. {gpa >= 3.5: Your name is near the top, and someone has drawn a small star beside it.|{gpa >= 2.5: Your name sits in the middle of the list, solid and unremarkable, exactly where you hoped.|{gpa >= 2.0: Your name is near the bottom, but it's there.|Your name is on the list, just. Someone has pencilled \"phew\" next to it, possibly you.}}} {flag(thesis_distinction): In the thesis column, one word in gold ink, Distinction.|{flag(thesis_passed): In the thesis column, a plain pass.|{flag(thesis_resubmit): In the thesis column, in red, To be resubmitted.|}}}\n> ok: Touch your name, for luck\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {gpa >= 3.0: People you barely know shake your hand on the way out. You let them.|{gpa >= 2.0: You run a finger along the line. Four years, one line. It's enough.|You stand there a while after the crowd has gone. It's on the list. It counts.}}\n\n@event last_career\ntitle: What Comes Next\nclass: story\ntone: warm\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: turn == max_turns\npriority: 85\ntype: menu\nart: letter\ntags: story\nchain: careers\nscene: A letter with a heavy seal open on a packed trunk, a student sitting on the trunk lid reading it by the window.\ntext: {flag(career_accepted): A letter about your start date. {flag(career_conclave): The Conclave expects you at the Seven Towers in autumn, in grey.|{flag(career_library): The Royal Library has a desk for you, and a key to a room nobody else may enter.|{flag(career_guild): The Guild of Alchemists sends a list of the protective clothing you must buy yourself.|{flag(career_unseen): The Office of Unseen Affairs sends no letter at all, which is how you know you're expected.|{flag(career_circus): The circus wagons will pass the village in a week, and they'll slow down, but they won't stop.|The faculty barge leaves in autumn, and you'll be on it, on the wrong side of the lectern.}}}}}|{flag(career_offered): The offer is still on your desk, unsigned. Today is the last day it stands.|No letter, no start date. Everyone else seems to know where they're going, or says so.}}\n> sign: Sign the offer after all\n  when: flag(career_offered) and not flag(career_accepted)\n  flag career_accepted\n  mood +small\n  say: You sign it on the trunk lid and run it to the owl tower before you can change your mind. {iv_score >= 3: The signing purse lapsed with their deadline; the place, it turns out, did not. |} Your handwriting looks like a grown-up's.\n> reply: Write back that you'll be there\n  when: flag(career_accepted)\n  mood +small\n  say: You write it in your best hand and seal it with the academy's wax for the last time. Then you sit for a while with your hands in your lap, getting used to being expected somewhere.\n> road: Leave it all open\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {flag(career_accepted): The letter can wait a day. Today is still yours, the last one that is.|You decide not to decide, not today. The world is very large, and it's been waiting four years. It can wait one more day.}\n> home: Write home about what's next\n  mood +small\n  say: You write home, and tell them the truth about what comes next, or what doesn't yet. It's the longest letter you've ever sent, and the easiest.\n\n@event last_lake\ntitle: The Lake, One Last Time\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: turn == max_turns\npriority: 80\nart: wave\ntags: story mystery\nscene: A student standing alone at the end of a wooden jetty at dawn, the Mirror Lake perfectly still, the towers reflected upside down in it.\ntext: You walk down to the lake before breakfast, the way you did your first morning. {flag(hollow_sealed): It's clearer than you've ever seen it, clear all the way down, and nothing looks back. You did that. Almost nobody knows.|{flag(hollow_freed): It has been different since the spring it froze, warmer somehow, and the candles along the shore lean toward you as you pass. You did that too.|{flag(hollow_bargain): The water is still. The mark on your palm is not. It hums as you come near, like something greeting something.|{flag(hollow_fled): The water looks ordinary. It isn't. Somewhere under it a door stands ajar, because you ran, and the headmistress's hair has been white since that night.|{flag(hollow_failed): It's calm this morning. You know what lies beneath it, and who paid to keep it closed. You stand on the jetty a long time.|{clues(hollowstar) >= 1: There were stories about this lake, and you followed some of them a little way. You never learned what they meant, or whether they meant anything. The lake isn't telling.|It's flat and blue and ordinary, and the towers stand upside down in it. Four years, and it never once did anything strange. That you saw.}}}}}}\n> ok: Say goodbye to the lake\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {~You skim one stone. It skips seven times, which is your record, and nobody saw.|You crouch and put your hand in the water. It's cold, the way it always was.} Then you walk back up to the castle without looking round.\n\n@event last_friends\ntitle: The Last Ordinary Morning\nclass: story\ntone: warm\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: turn == max_turns\npriority: 75\ncast:\n  friend: any where rel(friend) >= 15 and not tagged(friend, outsider)\nart: cup\ntags: story social\nscene: Two students sitting on the rim of a courtyard fountain with mugs of tea, packed trunks stacked by the doors behind them.\ntext: {friend} finds you in the courtyard with two mugs of tea and no particular reason. \"Last ordinary morning,\" {they} say{s}. \"I thought it should be spent properly.\" You sit on the rim of the fountain and don't talk about tomorrow.\n> left: Promise to visit, and mean it\n  rel friend +small\n  mood +small\n  say: You set a date, a real one, with a place and a time, and write it in both your notebooks. {friend} underlines it twice. You both know which of you will be late.\n> right: Give {them} something of yours\n  rel friend +medium\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You give {friend} the thing on your desk you'd most miss: a cracked mug, a dog-eared book, the stone from the lake. {They} turn{s} it over and over and say{s} nothing, which says enough.\n\n@event last_partner\ntitle: After Tomorrow\nclass: story\ntone: warm\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: turn == max_turns\npriority: 72\ncast:\n  partner: any where bond(partner) == \"partner\"\nart: heart\ntags: story romance\nscene: Two students sitting on a castle battlement at noon, legs dangling over the edge, a map of the realm spread between them, weighted with an apple.\ntext: {partner} has a map, an apple and the whole afternoon. \"Tomorrow we're not students,\" {they} say{s}. \"So I thought we'd better have a plan. Or decide not to.\" The map is spread on the battlement between you, weighted at one corner with the apple.\n> left: Plan the first year together\n  spark partner +small\n  mood +small\n  say: You plan it badly and in detail: a town on the map, a room above a shop, whose turn it is to cook. {partner} draws a circle round the town in ink, so it can't be rubbed out.\n> right: Fold the map, and just have today\n  spark partner +medium\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You fold the map and put it away. \"Whatever comes,\" you say, and {partner} takes your hand, and for an afternoon there's nothing on the other side of tomorrow at all.\n\n@event last_rival\ntitle: One Last Word\nclass: story\ntone: tense\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: turn == max_turns\npriority: 70\ncast:\n  rival: any where bond(rival) == \"rival\" or bond(rival) == \"nemesis\"\nart: wand\ntags: story social\nscene: Two students facing each other in an empty duelling hall, wands holstered, packed trunks visible through the open door behind them.\ntext: {rival} is waiting in the empty duelling hall, as if by appointment. Four years of grudges, and tomorrow there won't be a school to have them in. \"Well,\" {they} say{s}. \"That's that, then.\"\n> left: Offer your hand\n  rel rival +medium\n  mood +small\n  say: {rival} looks at your hand for a long moment, then takes it, hard. \"You were worth beating,\" {they} say{s}, and then, almost too quietly to hear, \"and worth losing to.\"\n> right: \"Rematch, in ten years.\"\n  rel rival +small\n  renown +tiny\n  say: \"Ten years,\" says {rival}, \"and I'll still win.\" You shake on it in front of the last of the Circle, who spread it round the year by supper. It's the closest either of you will come to saying anything kind.\n\n@event last_starfall\ntitle: The Last Starfall\nclass: story\ntone: warm\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: turn == max_turns\npriority: 62\nart: star\ntags: festival story\nscene: Final-year students in a row on the observatory roof under a sky full of falling stars, graduation robes thrown over their shoulders like blankets.\ntext: Tonight is Starfall, and by tradition the final-years have the observatory roof to themselves. The first-years are down on the lawns with their jars. Up here, everyone is pretending not to be sad.\n> left: Catch one for the road\n  challenge feat apprentice slim luck:\n    win:\n      mood +small\n      renown +tiny\n      say: It drops into your hands with a hiss, the last thing Lumenhold gives you. The whole roof cheers. You'll keep it in its jar on a windowsill somewhere you haven't seen yet.\n    lose:\n      mood +tiny\n      say: You lunge, miss, and sit down hard, and the whole roof laughs, and you laugh too. Some things you're supposed to leave behind.\n> right: Lie back with everyone\n  mood +medium\n  say: You lie in a row with your year, shoulder to shoulder, and count the stars aloud until the counting falls apart. Somebody starts to cry, and then everybody is laughing.\n"},{"path":"content/events/life.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Academy life — the random pool. Small moments that fill the\n#  month: weather, food, money, mischief, mishaps.\n#  Seasonal ones use `month` (1 Harvestmoon ... 12 Starfall).\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- Around the castle ----------------\n\n@event life_staircase\ntitle: The Staircase Moves\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: tower\ntags: magic\nwhen: term and not wrapup\nscene: A grand spiral staircase in mid-rotation, a student clinging to the banister as the steps swing toward a different floor.\ntext: Halfway up the west staircase, it decides to be somewhere else. You're now heading toward a floor you've never seen, at speed, and you're late for class.\n> left: Hold on and see where it goes\n  lore +tiny\n  chance even:\n    gold +small\n    say: It deposits you in a dusty forgotten classroom. In a desk: eight old crowns and a love letter from 1802.\n  else:\n    study -tiny\n    say: It takes you to the roof. Then the cellar. Then the roof again. You miss your morning lectures entirely.\n> right: Jump for the landing\n  challenge feat novice+ wild:\n    win:\n      vigor +tiny\n      say: You leap and land neatly. A passing portrait applauds.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      say: You leap and land, but not neatly. A passing portrait winces.\n\n@event life_mystery_stew\ntime: evening\ntitle: Mystery Stew\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nart: meal\ntags: home\nscene: A huge cauldron of grey-green stew in the dining hall, a ladle standing upright in it unaided.\ntext: Tonight's dinner is described on the board only as \"Stew\". It is grey-green. The ladle is standing up by itself. The cook is watching you with hope.\n> left: Eat it bravely\n  chance likely:\n    health +small\n    mood +small\n    say: It's... delicious? Rich, peppery, faintly glowing. You go back for seconds. The cook beams.\n  else:\n    health -medium\n    say: It's not delicious. It's not anything a stomach should know about. You spend the night regretting it.\n> right: Toast and jam again\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You eat toast. The cook looks hurt. You feel guilty all evening.\n\n@event life_lost_wand\ntitle: Where Is Your Wand?\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: wand\ntags: magic\nwhen: term and not wrapup\nscene: A dormitory turned upside down, clothes everywhere, a student on hands and knees peering under a bed.\ntext: Your wand is gone. It was on your desk. Now it isn't. Classes start in twenty minutes.\n> left: Tear the room apart\n  chance likely:\n    say: It's in your boot. Why is it in your boot? You make it to class with seconds to spare.\n  else:\n    study -small\n    mood -tiny\n    say: You find it at lunch, inside the pillowcase. You've missed a whole morning.\n> right: Try a summoning charm\n  challenge spell novice+:\n    win:\n      arcana +tiny\n      say: It flies into your hand from inside the wardrobe, offended at having been lost.\n    lose:\n      mana -tiny\n      say: Every pencil in the dormitory flies at your face. The wand stays hidden out of spite.\n\n@event life_snoring_roommate\ntitle: The Snorer\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nart: bed\ntags: home\nscene: A dark dormitory, one bed shaking with snores so loud the curtains flutter, another occupant glaring with a pillow over their head.\ntext: Someone in your dormitory snores like a dragon with a head cold, and nobody will admit to it. You haven't slept properly in a week.\n> left: Brew a silencing draught\n  challenge brew apprentice-:\n    win:\n      health +small\n      alchemy +tiny\n      alignment -tiny\n      say: Perfect silence. Too perfect. The culprit, the quiet one by the window, is now silent even when awake. It wears off by Tuesday. You never quite ask their permission.\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      say: The draught finds the wrong bed. Now two people snore, in harmony.\n> right: Move to the common room sofa\n  health -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  charm +tiny\n  say: You sleep on the sofa and meet everyone who comes down at 3am. Some of them are very interesting.\n\n@event life_portrait_gossip\ntitle: The Portraits Talk\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nart: mirror\ntags: mystery\nscene: A long gallery of gilded portraits, painted figures leaning out of their frames toward each other, whispering behind painted hands.\ntext: Two portraits in the east gallery are gossiping about a student. Then one of them says your name and they both go suspiciously quiet.\n> left: Bribe them with flattery\n  challenge negotiate novice+:\n    win:\n      guile +tiny\n      lore +tiny\n      say: Ten minutes of compliments on their brushwork, and they tell you everything. Gossip about half the faculty, too.\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      say: \"Flattery,\" sniffs the duchess, \"is so common.\" They turn their backs. Literally.\n> right: Pretend you didn't hear\n  say: You walk on. Behind you, the whispering resumes, louder.\n\n@event life_pixies\ntitle: Pixie Infestation\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nart: sparkle\ntags: danger\nscene: A dormitory full of tiny blue winged creatures swinging from curtains, stealing socks and pulling hair.\ntext: Someone left a window open and the dormitory is now full of pixies. They're stealing socks, unscrewing bedposts and laughing in very high voices.\n> left: Fight them off\n  challenge combat apprentice- wild:\n    win:\n      vigor +tiny\n      renown +small\n      say: You catch nine with a laundry bag. The dorm cheers. The pixies swear at you, colourfully.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      say: You lose. The pixies hang you from the chandelier by your robe. It takes an hour to get down.\n> right: Bribe them with sugar\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: You lay a trail of sugar cubes out of the window. The pixies follow it, munching, and leave one sock as a tip.\n\n@event life_gargoyle\ntitle: A Talkative Gargoyle\nclass: windfall\ntone: light\nweight: 5\nart: tower\ntags: magic\nscene: A weathered stone gargoyle on a parapet, one eye open, mouth moving, a student sitting beside it with a sandwich.\ntext: The gargoyle above the east door clears its throat as you pass. \"You,\" it rumbles. \"You look like someone who'd listen. Nobody listens to gargoyles.\"\n> left: Hurry along\n  say: \"Typical,\" says the gargoyle. It sulks until spring.\n> right: Sit and listen\n  lore +small\n  mood +tiny\n  say: It tells you three hundred years of academy gossip, including where the founders hid the good biscuits. You find the biscuits.\n\n@event life_brownies\ntime: morning\ntitle: Help in the Night\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nart: house\ntags: magic home\nscene: Tiny bearded figures in aprons tidying a cluttered student desk by moonlight, one darning a sock.\ntext: You wake to find your desk tidied, your socks darned and your essay proofread. Brownies. They expect something in return. Traditionally: cream.\n> left: Leave out a bowl of cream\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +small\n  study +tiny\n  say: Your room stays spotless all term. Your essays have suspiciously good spelling.\n> right: Leave them nothing\n  alignment -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: The next morning, every one of your socks is tied in knots. Brownies keep score.\n\n@event life_midnight_snack\ntime: night\ntitle: Midnight Hunger\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nart: meal\ntags: home\nscene: A student in pyjamas tiptoeing past a sleeping prefect toward a door marked KITCHENS, a candle cupped in their hand.\ntext: It's midnight and you're starving. The kitchens are three floors down, past a prefect's post.\n> left: Go back to bed hungry\n  health -tiny\n  say: You dream of sandwiches. Long, detailed, beautiful sandwiches.\n> right: Sneak down\n  challenge deceive apprentice:\n    win:\n      health +small\n      mood +small\n      say: Cold pie, a pot of custard, a heel of bread. Feast of kings.\n    lose:\n      gain detention\n      say: The prefect is awake. You spend a week washing the pots you were trying to raid.\n\n@event life_insomnia\ntime: night\ntitle: The Ceiling at 3am\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nart: moon\ntags: home\nscene: A student lying awake staring at a ceiling where faint painted stars have started slowly moving.\ntext: You can't sleep. Your brain has decided to replay every embarrassing thing you've said since you were six.\n> left: Get up and study\n  lore +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: By dawn you've read three chapters and remembered one of them.\n> right: Chamomile, and count the stars\n  gold -tiny\n  health +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: Halfway through counting, the stars start counting back. Then you're asleep.\n\n@event life_morning_run\ntime: morning\ntitle: The Dawn Run\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: sun\ntags: nature\nwhen: month <= 3 or month >= 7\nweight: 5\nscene: The lakeshore path at dawn, mist on the water, a lone runner's breath steaming, the towers catching the first gold light.\ntext: A group of students runs the lakeshore every dawn. They've invited you. It's very early. It's very cold.\n> left: Sleep in\n  mood +small\n  say: You hear them come back, glowing and smug. You are warm and unrepentant.\n> right: Run with them\n  vigor +small\n  health +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You nearly die at the first mile and feel immortal by the third. The lake shines like a coin. You feel every step of it all through your lectures.\n\n@event life_homework_mountain\ntitle: The Pile\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: scroll\ntags: study\nwhen: term and not wrapup\nscene: A desk buried under a precarious tower of scrolls and books, a student peeking hopelessly over the top.\ntext: Three essays, a potion log, a star chart and a reading list the length of your arm. All due this week. The pile has achieved its own weather.\n> left: Grind through it\n  study +small\n  health -small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You finish at 4am on the last day. Every piece is handed in. You sleep through breakfast.\n> right: Pick what matters, skip the rest\n  study -tiny\n  gain slacking\n  mood +small\n  say: You hand in two of five and go for a walk. The professors are not pleased. You feel great, temporarily.\n\n@event life_stolen_answers\ntitle: The Answers\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nart: scroll\ntags: study danger\nwhen: month == 3 or month == 4 or month == 9 or month == 10\nscene: A folded paper being passed under a library table, the faint glow of an examiner's seal on it.\ntext: A third-year is selling copies of next week's exam papers. Eight crowns for the set. \"Guaranteed real,\" he whispers. \"Straight from the examiner's desk.\"\n> left: Walk away\n  alignment +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: Two weeks later, five students who bought copies are suspended. The answers were fake.\n> right: Buy a copy\n  alignment -small\n  gold -small\n  chance unlikely:\n    study +large\n    guile +tiny\n    say: They're real. You know every question before you sit down. You also can't quite look the examiner in the eye.\n  else:\n    study -medium\n    gain detention\n    say: They're fake, and planted. You're caught with a copy. Detention, and a very cold letter home.\n\n@event life_backfire\ntitle: Spell Backfire\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nart: sparkle\ntags: magic danger\nscene: A student mid-hiccup, a small firework bursting from their mouth, classmates ducking.\ntext: You mispronounce a minor cheering charm in the corridor. Something goes wrong. Every time you hiccup, a tiny firework comes out.\n> left: Go to the infirmary\n  gold -tiny\n  say: Sister Amaranth cures it in a minute and charges you three crowns. \"For the ceiling,\" she says.\n> right: Live with it\n  gain hiccup_hex\n  renown +small\n  say: You become briefly famous as \"the sparkler\". It's not the fame you wanted.\n\n@event life_lake_swim\ntitle: A Swim in the Lake\nclass: story\ntone: light\nart: wave\ntags: nature\nwhen: month >= 9 or month <= 1\nscene: Students leaping off a wooden jetty into a sparkling lake on a hot afternoon, something large and dark moving far beneath the surface.\ntext: It's hot and the whole year has gone down to the jetty. Something large is rumoured to live in the deep part. People are swimming anyway.\n> left: Stay on the jetty\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You dangle your feet and eat cherries. Something brushes your toe. You keep your feet up after that.\n> right: Dive in\n  health +small\n  mood +medium\n  chance slim:\n    flag lake_knows\n    say: In the deep part, you open your eyes underwater and see a round door, far below, glowing. Then you need air.\n  else:\n    say: The water's perfect. Something large and friendly swims under you, and you decide not to panic.\n\n@event life_stray_cat\ntitle: A Cat Moves In\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nart: cat\ntags: home\nscene: A scruffy black cat sitting on a dormitory pillow as if it has always lived there, one torn ear, very green eyes.\ntext: A black cat with one torn ear is sitting on your pillow. It looks at you as though you are the one trespassing.\n> left: Shoo it out\n  say: It leaves with enormous dignity and moves in with your neighbour, who spoils it rotten.\n> right: Let it stay\n  mood +small\n  study -tiny\n  say: It's called Professor now. It sleeps on your homework. You've never been happier.\n\n@event life_storm_night\ntitle: The Lights Go Out\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nart: bolt\ntags: mystery\nwhen: month >= 6 and month <= 8\nscene: A dark common room lit only by lightning, students huddled under blankets, one telling a ghost story.\ntext: A storm knocks out every light-crystal in the dormitory. The common room fills with students, blankets and somebody's terrible ghost story.\n> left: Tell an even scarier story\n  challenge negotiate apprentice-:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      mood +small\n      say: Your story is so good that three people sleep with the lights on for a week. You're a legend.\n    lose:\n      mood +tiny\n      say: Your story is not scary. It is, however, accidentally hilarious.\n> right: Light the room with a spell\n  challenge spell novice+:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      arcana +tiny\n      say: You fill the room with soft floating lights. Someone starts singing. It turns into the best night of term.\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      say: You light the sofa. Briefly. The storm seems less frightening after that.\n\n@event life_mirror_talks\ntime: morning\ntitle: The Honest Mirror\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nweight: 5\nart: mirror\ntags: magic\nscene: A bathroom mirror with a stern painted face in its silvering, frowning at a student mid-toothbrush.\ntext: The bathroom mirror has started commenting on your appearance. \"Hair,\" it says this morning. Just: \"Hair.\"\n> left: Ask for its advice\n  charm +small\n  say: It turns out to be an excellent stylist. You look great. You hate that it's right.\n> right: Cover it with a towel\n  mood +tiny\n  say: It sulks under the towel. Muffled: \"Hair.\"\n\n@event life_prank_war\ntitle: Prank War\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: mask\ntags: social\nscene: A corridor ankle-deep in enchanted soap bubbles, two dormitories' worth of students hurling them at each other.\ntext: The dormitory across the corridor turned your door into a goat. The goat is fine. Honour, however, demands retaliation.\n> left: Rise above it\n  study -tiny\n  say: You take the high road. The goat eats your homework. You let it.\n> right: Retaliate\n  challenge deceive apprentice:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      mood +small\n      say: You fill their dorm with a thousand singing bubbles. They concede by dawn.\n    lose:\n      gain detention\n      renown +tiny\n      say: You're caught carrying a crate of bubbles. Detention, but the goat seems proud of you.\n> up[glamour]: Glamour their door into a goat\n  renown +small\n  mood +small\n  say: You return the compliment with a glamour: their door becomes a goat, an extremely convincing one, which bleats when knocked. It lasts three days. Nobody can prove a thing.\n\n@event life_mail_owl\ntitle: The Wrong Letter\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nweight: 5\nart: letter\ntags: mystery\nscene: A ruffled delivery owl dropping a scented pink envelope onto a breakfast plate, a student looking up in confusion.\ntext: A delivery owl drops a scented pink letter in your porridge. It's addressed to someone else. It's a love letter. It's very, very passionate.\n> left: Deliver it to its owner\n  charm +tiny\n  renown +tiny\n  say: The recipient goes crimson and thanks you. Two weeks later, they're holding hands with the sender in the dining hall.\n> right: Read it first\n  alignment -tiny\n  guile +tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: You read it. You will never be able to look the potions professor in the eye again.\n\n# ---------------- Money ----------------\n\n@event life_money_from_home\ntitle: A Parcel From Home\nclass: windfall\ntone: warm\nart: pouch\ntags: home money\nwhen: turn >= 2\nweight: 5\nscene: A brown paper parcel tied with string on a dormitory bed, a knitted scarf, a jar of jam and a small coin purse spilling out.\ntext: A parcel from home: a knitted scarf, a jar of jam, a note telling you to eat vegetables, and a small purse of coins.\n> left: Save the money\n  gold +medium\n  mood +small\n  say: You tuck it away. The jam lasts six days.\n> right: Spend it on a treat for your friends\n  gold +tiny\n  mood +medium\n  charm +tiny\n  say: Cake from the village for everyone. You're popular for an entire afternoon.\n\n@event life_found_purse\ntitle: A Purse in the Grass\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: pouch\ntags: money\nscene: A velvet purse lying in the long grass beside a path, coins glinting at its open mouth, initials embroidered on it.\ntext: A fat velvet purse lies in the grass by the path. Thirty crowns at least. Embroidered initials: A.T.\n> left: Return it to its owner\n  alignment +small\n  rel thorne +small\n  say: A.T. turns out to be Aldric Thorne. He takes the purse and stares at you a long moment. \"Honest,\" he says. It sounds like a diagnosis.\n> right: Keep it\n  alignment -small\n  gold +large\n  guile +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: Thirty crowns richer. A few days later, Thorne mentions in class that he lost something. He looks at no one in particular.\n\n@event life_peddler\ntitle: The Travelling Peddler\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: lantern\ntags: money magic\nscene: A gaudy painted wagon at the academy gates, hung with lanterns and trinkets, a peddler with a gold tooth grinning.\ntext: A peddler's wagon has parked by the gates. \"Lucky charms! Genuine article! Thirty crowns, and fortune follows you for a season!\"\n> left: Buy a lucky charm\n  hint: 30 gold\n  gold -large\n  chance even:\n    gain inspired\n    mood +small\n    say: It's a real one. Things go right all month. You start talking to it.\n  else:\n    mood -tiny\n    say: It turns your neck green. Fortune does not follow you. The peddler is long gone.\n> right: Just browse\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You buy nothing and enjoy a sugared apple and the peddler's outrageous stories.\n\n@event life_bookshop\ntitle: Sale at Grimsby's\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: book\ntags: money study\nscene: A crooked bookshop with books stacked to the ceiling, a hand-painted sign in the window reading HALF OFF, MOSTLY.\ntext: Grimsby's Books is having a sale. An old copy of \"Advanced Arcane Theory\" for fifteen crowns. Normally forty. It's annotated, apparently, by a famous wizard.\n> left: Buy it\n  hint: 15 gold\n  gold -medium\n  lore +medium\n  arcana +small\n  say: The annotations are brilliant, rude and occasionally about the author's divorce. You learn a great deal.\n> right: Leave it\n  say: You walk away. You think about it for weeks.\n\n@event life_new_robes\ntitle: Shabby Robes\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: crown\ntags: money social\nwhen: renown >= 10\nscene: A student in a hall mirror holding up a fraying sleeve, a tailor's flyer in their other hand.\ntext: Your robes are fraying at every hem. Someone at dinner called them \"vintage\". It was not a compliment.\n> left: Patch them yourself\n  charm +tiny\n  say: Your stitching is crooked but cheerful. Some people start copying it.\n> right: Buy new robes (30 gold)\n  when: gold >= 30\n  hint: 30 gold\n  gold -large\n  renown +small\n  charm +small\n  say: Midnight-blue wool, silver trim. People actually turn to look. You walk differently all week.\n\n@event life_card_game\ntime: night\ntitle: A Game of Wizard's Hand\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: cards\ntags: money social\nscene: A smoky corner of the common room, students around a low table with cards and coins, one card floating and changing face.\ntext: A late-night game of Wizard's Hand. The cards change faces when no one's looking. The pot is thirty crowns. The upper-years look hungry.\n> left: Play\n  when: gold >= 15\n  challenge deceive apprentice+ wild:\n    win:\n      gold +large\n      say: You read every bluff and keep a straight face. Thirty crowns and a reputation.\n    lose:\n      gold -medium\n      say: You lose fifteen crowns and the upper-years' respect, in that order.\n> right: Watch and learn\n  guile +tiny\n  say: You learn more watching than you would have playing. Mostly about who cheats.\n\n@event life_essay_prize\ntitle: The Essay Prize\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: feather\ntags: study money\nscene: A brass plaque on a noticeboard announcing THE LUMEN ESSAY PRIZE — 30 CROWNS, a quill pinned beneath it.\ntext: The Lumen Essay Prize: thirty crowns for the best essay on \"Magic and Duty\". The deadline is Friday. You have an idea already.\n> left: Enter\n  study -tiny\n  challenge research adept-:\n    win:\n      gold +large\n      renown +medium\n      say: The essay eats your week, and wins. Your essay is read aloud at dinner. You don't know where to look.\n    lose:\n      lore +tiny\n      say: The essay eats your week, and doesn't win, but the judges' notes are glowing. Next year, maybe.\n> right: Skip it\n  mood +tiny\n  say: The winner reads their essay aloud at dinner. Yours would have been better. Probably. You had a very pleasant week instead.\n\n@event life_charity\ntitle: The Winter Fund\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: coin\ntags: money social\nwhen: month >= 3 and month <= 5\nscene: A collection jar shaped like a lantern on the dining hall table, half full of coins, a hand-lettered sign: FOR THE VILLAGE WINTER FUND.\ntext: The village winter fund is collecting for families who can't afford fuel this winter. The jar is half full.\n> left: Donate fifteen crowns\n  hint: 15 gold\n  alignment +small\n  gold -medium\n  renown +small\n  mood +small\n  say: Your name goes on a little list by the jar. You feel warm, which is the point.\n> right: Walk past\n  if gold >= 15:\n    alignment -tiny\n  say: {gold >= 15: You walk past. The jar fills anyway. You think about it on cold nights.|You have nothing to give this year. The jar fills anyway. You think about it on cold nights.}\n\n@event life_shady_offer\ntime: afternoon evening\ntitle: A Quiet Job\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nart: mask\ntags: money danger\nscene: A hooded figure in an alley behind the village tavern holding out a sealed envelope, a purse visible in their other hand.\ntext: A hooded figure behind the tavern: \"Deliver this envelope to the boathouse tonight. Don't open it. Thirty crowns.\" The envelope is warm. Under the hood, a glint of pince-nez, and teeth: this is one of Grisk's errands.\n> left: Refuse\n  alignment +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: The figure melts into the dark. The next morning, someone's boat is missing, and you wonder what was in the envelope, and whether you could have stopped it.\n> right: Do the job\n  gold +large\n  alignment -small\n  flag grisk_contact\n  find lead_grisk_jetty\n  chance unlikely:\n    gain watched\n    say: You make the drop. Bram is watching from the boathouse window and writes something down. On the jetty, a note in a neat hand: \"Good work. Midnight, any month. G.\"\n  else:\n    guile +tiny\n    say: You make the drop. Nobody sees. Thirty crowns, a cold feeling in your stomach, and a note on the jetty: \"Good work. Midnight, any month. G.\"\n\n# ---------------- Body & mind ----------------\n\n@event life_food_poisoning\ntime: evening night\ntitle: The Fish Was Off\nclass: incident\ntone: grim\nart: meal\ntags: crisis\nscene: A student curled on a dormitory bed clutching their stomach, a half-eaten fish pie on the bedside table.\ntext: The fish pie at dinner was a mistake. Your stomach is staging a revolution.\n> left: Ride it out\n  health -medium\n  mood -tiny\n  say: A miserable night. You never look at fish pie again.\n> right: Get a tonic from the infirmary\n  gold -tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: The tonic tastes like old pennies, but it works.\n\n@event life_self_doubt\ntitle: Imposter\nclass: windfall\ntone: warm\nweight: 5\nart: mirror\ntags: home\nscene: A student sitting alone on the edge of a fountain at night, staring at their reflection broken up by ripples.\ntext: Everyone around you seems to belong here more than you do. They cast faster, learn quicker, laugh easier. Maybe the letter was a mistake.\n> left: Write down everything you've learned\n  mood +medium\n  lore +tiny\n  say: The list is longer than you expected. Much longer. You pin it above your bed.\n> right: Talk to someone about it\n  if bonds(friend) + bonds(close) + bonds(partner) > 0:\n    mood +large\n    say: Your friend laughs — not at you. \"You? Everyone thinks you're the one who belongs.\" It helps more than you can say.\n  else:\n    mood +small\n    say: You talk to the night porter. He tells you everyone feels this way. You decide to believe him.\n\n@event life_sleepwalking\ntime: night\ntitle: Sleepwalking\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nart: moon\ntags: mystery\nscene: A student in pyjamas standing on a moonlit battlement, eyes closed, one foot raised over the edge, an owl watching.\ntext: You wake up on the battlements in your pyjamas, at the very edge, with no memory of how you got there. Your feet are wet with lake water.\n> left: Tell Sister Amaranth\n  gold -tiny\n  health +tiny\n  say: A sleeping draught and a stern warning. It doesn't happen again.\n> right: Keep it to yourself\n  mood -tiny\n  chance unlikely:\n    flag lake_knows\n    say: It happens twice more. Each time you're facing the lake. Each time, you're a little closer to the water.\n  else:\n    say: It doesn't happen again. You start locking your door from the inside, just in case.\n\n@event life_quiet_afternoon\nslot: afternoon\ntitle: A Quiet Afternoon\nclass: windfall\ntone: light\nweight: 5\nart: leaf\ntags: nature\nscene: A student lying in the long grass under an oak tree, a book open on their chest, clouds drifting above the towers.\ntext: For once, nothing is due, nobody needs you, and the sun is out. A whole afternoon, unclaimed.\n> left: Nap under the oak tree\n  health +medium\n  mood +small\n  say: You wake to find a bird sitting on your book. It seems content. So are you.\n> right: Practise spells in the meadow\n  arcana +small\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You make dandelion seeds dance in spirals for an hour. Nobody sees. It's perfect.\n\n@event life_injury_stairs\ntitle: The Trick Step\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nart: cross\ntags: danger\nscene: A stone staircase with one step glowing faintly, a student mid-fall, books flying everywhere.\ntext: The ninth step on the north stair is a trick step. Everyone knows. You forgot. Your leg sinks to the knee and the stair holds on.\n> left: Wait for someone to help\n  study -tiny\n  say: Forty minutes later, a first-year pulls you out. You're late for everything.\n> right: Wrench yourself free\n  challenge feat apprentice-:\n    win:\n      vigor +tiny\n      say: You pull free with a pop. Your shoe stays in the stair forever.\n    lose:\n      gain injured\n      health -small\n      say: Something in your knee goes click. Not a good click.\n\n@event life_lake_fever\ntitle: Lake Fever Season\nclass: routine\ntone: grim\nart: rain\ntags: crisis\nwhen: month >= 3 and month <= 6\nweight: 5\nscene: A dormitory where half the beds hold sniffling students with faintly blue-glowing noses, tissues everywhere.\ntext: Half your dormitory has Lake Fever. Noses are glowing blue. You feel a tickle in your throat.\n> left: Dose up on Maud's tea\n  if met(maud):\n    health +small\n    say: Maud's lemon-and-thunder tea keeps you upright through the whole outbreak.\n  else:\n    chance even:\n      gain sick\n      say: The tea from the kitchens isn't the same. Your nose starts glowing on Thursday.\n    else:\n      say: You dodge it. Somehow.\n> right: Keep going as normal\n  chance likely:\n    gain sick\n    say: You keep going as normal for exactly two days.\n  else:\n    vigor +tiny\n    say: Iron constitution. You sail through. Everyone hates you a little.\n\n# ---------------- Seasons ----------------\n\n@event life_first_snow\ntitle: First Snow\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: snow\ntags: nature festival\nwhen: month == 4 or month == 5\nscene: The academy courtyard blanketed in fresh snow, students mid-snowball fight, a snowman wearing a professor's hat.\ntext: The first snow of winter falls overnight and the whole academy wakes up giddy. Classes are optional, officially. Unofficially, they're cancelled by snowball.\n> left: Join the snowball war\n  mood +medium\n  vigor +tiny\n  chance unlikely:\n    health -tiny\n    say: You take a snowball to the face from the headmistress herself. Her aim is terrifying.\n  else:\n    say: Your side wins. You build a snow-fort that lasts until Thawmoon.\n> right: Hot chocolate by the fire\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +small\n  health +tiny\n  say: You watch from the window, warm and smug, while the snowballs fly.\n\n@event life_spring_cleaning\ntitle: Spring Cleaning\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nart: broom\ntags: home\nwhen: month == 7 or month == 8\nscene: A dormitory with every window thrown open, brooms sweeping by themselves, dust swirling in sunbeams.\ntext: Spring cleaning. The brooms are enchanted, but the enchantment needs supervising. Your dorm's brooms have formed a union.\n> left: Negotiate with the brooms\n  challenge negotiate novice+ wild:\n    win:\n      mood +small\n      say: You agree to one day off a week and a polish. The dorm sparkles.\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      say: The brooms do not accept your terms. They sweep you into a cupboard.\n> right: Clean by hand\n  vigor +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: An afternoon of scrubbing. It's oddly satisfying. You find three lost quills and a sandwich from Thawmoon.\n\n@event life_summer_heat\ntitle: The Heat\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: sun\ntags: nature\nwhen: month == 10 or month == 11 or month == 12\nscene: Students lying flat on the cool stone floor of a shaded colloquy, fanning themselves with exam papers.\ntext: The heat is unbearable. Even the gargoyles are sweating. The lake is crowded, the library stifling, the dormitories ovens.\n> left: Cast a cooling charm on your room\n  challenge spell apprentice-:\n    win:\n      mood +small\n      renown +small\n      say: Your room becomes an icebox. People pay to visit. You let them for free. Mostly.\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      say: You make it colder. Briefly. Then hotter. Then it rains inside.\n> right: Sleep on the roof\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You sleep under the stars with half the dormitory. It's the best idea anyone's had all year.\n\n@event life_harvest_festival\nslot: evening\ntitle: The Harvest Feast\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: auto\nrepeat: 12\nart: meal\ntags: festival\nwhen: month == 1 and turn > 1\nscene: Long tables groaning under pies and roast vegetables, candles floating above, a giant pumpkin carved like the headmistress.\ntext: The Harvest Feast: pies the size of cartwheels, cider, and {&a pumpkin carved to look like the headmistress, which she pretends not to notice.|a pumpkin carved to look like Professor Thorne this year, which he pretends not to notice, badly.|a turnip carved to look like the headmistress, by popular demand. She is seen smiling at it.}\n> left: Eat until you can't move\n  health +small\n  mood +medium\n  say: {&You eat three helpings of everything. You're carried back to the dorm by friends. Worth it.|You pace yourself this year, and still lose to the pies. Worth it.|You win the cider-tasting by not falling over. Worth it.}\n> right: Help in the kitchens for extra pay\n  gold +medium\n  charm +tiny\n  say: {&You serve pie to the entire academy. The cooks slip you coins and a whole pie of your own.|The cooks remember you and put you on gravy, a promotion. Coins, and a pie of your own.|You carve the pumpkin yourself this year. The cooks pay you double in pie and the usual in coins.}\n\n# The rivalry's first fixture (playtest 2, X9): Thornwick visits in year 1's Greenrise.\n@event life_rival_academy\ntitle: Visitors From Thornwick\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: year == 1 and month == 8 and not off_campus\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nart: shield\ntags: rival social\nscene: A delegation of students in green robes marching through the gates, noses raised, a Lumenhold crowd watching suspiciously.\ntext: A delegation from Thornwick Academy is visiting. Their students are smug, polished and very loudly unimpressed with Lumenhold's plumbing. {met(tamsin): One of them is scanning the crowd with a letter in their hand, and you know the handwriting.|}\n> left: Be a gracious host\n  mood -tiny\n  charm +small\n  renown +tiny\n  if met(tamsin):\n    rel tamsin +small\n    say: Tamsin finds you before you find them, and is exactly like their letters, only faster. You show them the observatory. They admit, grudgingly, that it's better than theirs.\n  else:\n    say: You show them the observatory. One of them admits, grudgingly, that it's better than theirs.\n> right: Defend Lumenhold's honour\n  challenge combat apprentice+:\n    win:\n      renown +medium\n      say: You challenge their best to a friendly duel and win. The Thornwick students are quieter at dinner.\n    lose:\n      renown -small\n      health -small\n      say: You challenge their best to a friendly duel and lose, quite unfriendly. Thornwick will be insufferable.\n\n@event life_lost_firstyear\ntitle: A Lost First-Year\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: compass\ntags: social\nwhen: month <= 3\nweight: 5\nscene: A tiny first-year in enormous robes standing in the middle of a junction of five identical corridors, clutching a timetable upside down.\ntext: A tiny first-year is standing in the middle of the corridor, clutching an upside-down timetable and trying very hard not to cry.\n> left: Point the way and hurry on\n  say: You point. They go the wrong way anyway. You hope they're all right.\n> right: Walk them to class\n  alignment +tiny\n  mood +small\n  study -tiny\n  charm +tiny\n  say: You walk them all the way and they talk the entire time. You're late, but they've decided you're their hero.\n\n@event life_frog_escape\ntitle: The Frogs Are Out\nclass: routine\ntrack: alchemy\ntone: tense\nweight: 5\nart: frog\ntags: danger\nscene: A potions classroom overrun by dozens of hopping green frogs, one sitting smugly on the professor's desk.\ntext: Someone knocked over the frog crate in Potions. Thirty frogs are loose. One has climbed into the professor's hat. He has not noticed yet.\n> left: Help round them up\n  vigor +tiny\n  study alchemy +tiny\n  say: You catch eleven, a personal best. The professor finds the hat frog at dinner. You were not involved.\n> right: Quietly let a few escape out the window\n  mood +small\n  guile +tiny\n  say: Five frogs hop to freedom in the lake. You feel like a liberator.\n\n@event life_fortune_teller\ntitle: The Village Fortune-Teller\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nart: eye\ntags: mystery\nscene: A beaded doorway into a tiny dim tent, an old woman with a crystal ball and too many rings beckoning.\ntext: In the village, an old fortune-teller beckons you into her tent. \"Eight crowns for a good future, dearie. The bad ones I tell for free.\"\n> left: Decline politely\n  chance even:\n    mood +tiny\n    say: \"Suit yourself,\" she calls after you. \"You will meet a tall stranger.\" You already have. Several. It's an academy.\n  else:\n    mood -tiny\n    say: \"Beware of water,\" she calls after you, free of charge. She won't say more. You think about the lake a lot.\n> right: Pay eight crowns\n  gold -small\n  mood +small\n  say: Your future is wonderful. She's very specific. You are fairly sure she's making it up, and you don't care.\n\n@event life_volunteer\ntitle: Clean-Up Duty\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nweight: 5\nart: broom\ntags: home\nscene: Students in rolled-up sleeves raking leaves on the great lawn, a groundskeeper directing them with a pitchfork.\ntext: After the storm, the grounds are a wreck. Volunteers needed to help clear branches and rebuild the hen-house. No pay. Lots of mud.\n> left: Volunteer\n  vigor +tiny\n  mood +small\n  rel maud +small\n  say: A long day of mud and laughter. An old woman in a straw hat brings everyone cocoa and remembers your name.\n> right: Stay in and study\n  study +tiny\n  say: You study through the shouting and hammering outside. The hen-house is very nice when it's done.\n"},{"path":"content/events/night.grim","text":"# The midnight turn (playtest 2, M41; stub — X13 fills it): after a lead-in in year 1 Stormbloom, a night card each\n# on-campus month, first in the midnight slot (after every night card), so going out leads straight to the pick. \"Go out\" opens the midnight pick (`visit midnight`); six places are open then. Going out in term\n# queues `night_suspicion` at 33%, and risky outcomes queue it at their own odds (I4); two copies bring at most one\n# card the next morning (or the first month back on campus), the only way to a lockout. The cards never say whether\n# you were seen.\n\n@event night_lead_in\ntitle: An Unsigned Note\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: year == 1 and month == 7 and not off_campus\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nart: map\ntags: mystery\nscene: A folded note slipped under a dormitory door at night, unsigned, a tiny silver map drawn in one corner.\ntext: A note under your door, unsigned: \"The castle has a second timetable. It starts at midnight. Curfew is a suggestion made by people who go to bed early.\" In the corner, a map no bigger than a thumbnail, showing six doors that are open when they shouldn't be.\n> left: Burn it\n  flag night_unlocked\n  find lead_second_timetable\n  say: You hold it to the candle. The map in the corner is the last thing to burn, and you could swear it rearranged itself first.\n> right: Keep it\n  flag night_unlocked\n  find lead_second_timetable\n  guile +tiny\n  say: You fold it into your book. Some nights you take it out and look at the six doors.\n\n@event night_out\ntitle: After Curfew\ntrigger: auto\nslot: midnight\nwhen: flag(night_unlocked) and not off_campus and not has(locked_out) and turn < max_turns\nrepeat: 1\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nart: moon\ntags: mystery\nscene: A dormitory window at midnight, the castle dark beyond it, one lantern moving along a far battlement.\ntext: {month == 12: Starfall is close, and the sky over the towers is already restless.|{month == 4 or month == 10: Exams soon. Half the windows on your corridor are still lit.|{season == \"winter\": {~Frost on the window, and the corridors are colder than the snow.|Snow on the sills; the tower creaks like a ship.|Ice in the water jug, and the tower silent with snow.}|{season == \"summer\": {~The night is warm and the castle's windows are all open.|Moths at the window, and somebody laughing on the lawn.|The night is short and never quite dark.}|{~The castle settles for the night, beam by beam.|Rain on the leads, and a gargoyle gargling it.|The wind finds every keyhole in the tower.}}}}} {has(watched): Bram does his rounds earlier these nights. You've learned the sound of his boots.|{health < 30: Every bone you own would like to stay in bed.|{mood < 25: You can't sleep anyway.|{gold < 10: Your purse is light, and some doors after midnight pay.|{bonds(partner) > 0: {~A note under your door, in a hand you know: \"Roof?\"|You could be on the observatory roof with someone right now.|Somewhere across the castle, someone you love is also awake.}|{final_year: {~Your last year. The castle at night won't be yours much longer.|The doors you know by heart will open for someone else next year.|Next year someone else will lie awake in this bed.}|{~Somewhere a door that should be locked isn't.|Down in the courtyard, a lantern goes out on purpose.|Someone has left the servants' stair unbolted again.}}}}}}}\n> left: Sleep\n  say: {~You blow out the candle and let the castle keep its secrets for one more night.|Sleep. The night can wait. It always does.|You pull the blanket up and listen to the castle until you're asleep.}\n> right: Go out\n  mood -small\n  health -small\n  visit midnight\n  if term:\n    queue night_suspicion chance unlikely\n  say: {~You wait for the last footsteps to fade, then ease the door open.|Boots in hand, you take the servants' stair.|You slip out while the castle is still settling, and nobody hears.}\n\n# The morning after you were noticed (queued by the night's cards, I4): the only source of lockouts.\n@event night_suspicion\ntitle: A Word in the Morning\ntrigger: queued\nslot: morning\n# Caught in a term's last month and off home or to an internship (I6): it waits until you're back on campus.\ndefer: off_campus\nrepeat: 1\nclass: incident\ntone: tense\nart: eye\ntags: crisis\nscene: A prefect waiting at the foot of a dormitory stair at breakfast time, arms folded, a small notebook open.\ntext: {has(watched): Bram is waiting at the foot of the stair, and this time he has a notebook open. \"Twice,\" he says. \"I did warn you.\"|Bram is waiting at the foot of the stair. \"Someone was out last night,\" he says, looking at nobody in particular, which is to say at you.}\n> left: Deny everything\n  guile +tiny\n  if has(watched):\n    lose watched\n    gain locked_out\n    gain detention 3\n    mood -medium\n    say: Three months of detention and a door that is checked at midnight. Bram writes it all down, neatly.\n  else:\n    gain watched\n    say: \"I'll be keeping an eye on you.\" He means it literally.\n> right: Own up\n  alignment +tiny\n  if has(watched):\n    lose watched\n    gain locked_out\n    gain detention 3\n    mood -medium\n    say: Three months of detention and a door that is checked at midnight. Bram writes it all down, neatly.\n  else:\n    gain watched\n    say: \"I'll be keeping an eye on you.\" He means it literally.\n\n# ---------------- One night at each open place (X13 adds more) ----------------\n\n@event midnight_newt_cards\ntitle: Upstairs at the Newt\nplace: gilded_newt\nslot: midnight\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 2\nweight: 10\nart: cup\ntags: money danger\nscene: A smoky upstairs room at a tavern after hours, a card table under one lamp, coins stacked high.\ntext: Upstairs at the Gilded Newt, after hours, the real game starts. The stakes are higher than anything downstairs, and nobody asks your name.\n> left: Play a few hands\n  alignment -tiny\n  gold -medium\n  challenge deceive adept- unlikely luck:\n    win:\n      gold +large\n      gold +medium\n      say: {~You leave with more than you came with and a few new enemies who smile too much.|Luck, nerve and one well-timed sigh. The pot is yours.}\n    lose:\n      queue night_suspicion chance even\n      say: {~The cards go cold, and so does your purse. On the stair down, a face from the castle turns away a little too fast.|You lose your stake and walk back in the dark, poorer, wiser and not sure you went unseen.}\n> right: Watch, then leave\n  say: {~You watch one hand, see someone lose a familiar on a bluff, and leave before you're tempted.|You stay long enough to learn the rules, and leave before they learn you.}\n\n@event midnight_boathouse_ring\ntitle: The Ring Under the Boathouse\nplace: boathouse\nslot: midnight\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 2\nweight: 10\nart: sword\ntags: danger money\nscene: A cellar under a boathouse, lanterns hung from the beams, students in a ring around two duellists, coins changing hands.\ntext: Under the boathouse, by lantern light, someone has chalked a ring on the flagstones. Purses go to whoever is still standing. \"Next,\" says the student holding the money, looking at you.\n> left: Step into the ring\n  challenge combat adept-:\n    win:\n      gold +medium\n      renown +tiny\n      say: {~You win the purse and the ring's respect, which is worth less and lasts longer.|Your opponent yields. Somebody whistles. The purse is heavier than you expected.}\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      queue night_suspicion chance even\n      say: {~You lose, and limp home past a window where a light is still burning.|A bruising loss, and a lantern somewhere on the path home. Maybe it saw you; maybe not.}\n> right: Bet on someone else\n  gold -small\n  chance even:\n    gold +medium\n    say: {~Your fighter wins. You collect, quietly.|A good bet. You leave before anyone remembers your face.}\n  else:\n    say: {~Your fighter goes down in the first exchange. So does your stake.|You picked badly. The bookmaker smiles.}\n\n@event midnight_kitchen_raid\ntitle: The Kitchens at Midnight\nplace: refectory\nslot: midnight\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 2\nweight: 10\nart: meal\ntags: danger\nscene: A dark castle kitchen at midnight, a student with a candle reaching into a larder, a cat watching from the hearth.\ntext: The refectory kitchens at midnight: banked fires, a larder that isn't locked, and the smell of tomorrow's bread already rising.\n> left: Fill your pockets\n  alignment -tiny\n  lose hungry\n  health +small\n  queue night_suspicion chance even\n  say: {~Bread, cheese and half a pie. Nobody counts the pies, you hope, and the scullery door creaks as you leave.|You eat by the banked fire with the kitchen cat. On your way out, a kitchen boy is up early. Did he see you?}\n> right: Just warm yourself by the fire\n  mood +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~You sit by the embers until your hands stop hurting, and take nothing. The walk back is cold.|The kitchen cat sits with you. You leave the larder alone, feel strangely proud of it, and shiver all the way back to bed.}\n\n@event midnight_restricted_stacks\ntitle: The Restricted Stacks\nplace: restricted_section\nslot: midnight\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 2\nweight: 10\nart: book\ntags: mystery danger\nscene: A chained library section at midnight, a student reading by a stolen candle, shelves leaning in like listeners.\ntext: The Restricted Section at midnight. The chains on the shelves clink when nobody touches them. One book has been left open, as if for you.\n> left: Read the open book\n  challenge research apprentice+:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      say: {~Three pages before the book closes itself. You remember every word, which is worrying.|The book shows you a diagram you're not supposed to understand. You understand it.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      queue night_suspicion chance even\n      say: {~The book slams shut and a lamp comes on two floors down. You're gone before it reaches the stair.|The pages scream, briefly. You leave quickly, and hope nobody heard.}\n> right: Leave it open and go\n  say: {~You back away from the book. It seems disappointed.|You leave it open for whoever it was waiting for. It wasn't you, you decide.}\n\n@event midnight_observatory_stars\ntitle: Stars at Midnight\nplace: observatory\nslot: midnight\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 2\nweight: 10\nart: moon\ntags: nature\nscene: The observatory roof at midnight, a student lying on the leads, the sky thick with stars.\ntext: The observatory roof at midnight is the quietest place in the castle. {bonds(partner) > 0: You've brought someone.|The stars are very close tonight.} Nobody comes up here after curfew, mostly.\n> left: Stay until the sky turns\n  mood +medium\n  health -small\n  say: {~You count stars until you lose count, and then until the sky goes grey. You'll be tired. It was worth it.|A meteor, then another. You stay until the first bell.}\n> right: An hour, then bed\n  mood +small\n  queue night_suspicion chance slim\n  say: {~An hour of stars, and you're back in bed before anyone stirs, you think.|You slip back down the stair, the sky still in your eyes. A lantern turns a far corner, and you don't wait to see whose.}\n\n@event midnight_passage_mapping\ntitle: The Passage After Dark\nplace: gargoyle_passage\nslot: midnight\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 2\nweight: 10\nart: map\ntags: club mystery\nscene: A secret passage behind a gargoyle at midnight, chalk marks on the walls, a candle guttering in a draught from nowhere.\ntext: Behind the third gargoyle the passage is busier at midnight than at noon. {met(corvin): Corvin's chalk marks are fresh.|Someone's chalk marks are fresh.} A draught comes from a direction that has no wall.\n> left: Follow the draught\n  challenge research apprentice+:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      guile +tiny\n      say: {~The draught leads you to a door you've never seen, and then, politely, closes it.|A new turning, a new stair. You chalk it for the map.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      queue night_suspicion chance even\n      say: {~After an hour of cold stone, the passage lets you out somewhere unexpected: the prefects' corridor, empty, you hope.|You get lost for an hour and come out in the wrong tower, cold and dusty, and not alone on the stair.}\n> right: Mark it and go back\n  say: {~You chalk an arrow at the turning and leave the rest for another night.|You mark it on your copy of the map and go back to bed, a little sorry.}\n"},{"path":"content/events/origin_funding.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Origin funding (docs/origin-design.md, 2026-10-16): the money a family, the Crown or a company sends before each\n#  later term (terms 2–8), as one card in the last break month (turns 6, 12 … 42), wherever you spend the break.\n#  The amounts and odds are presets in content/core/balance.grim. A roll happens once, when the card is drawn\n#  (`on_show`), so the text matches what arrives and a reload doesn't change it. Payment needs no choice.\n# ============================================================\n\n@event noble_letter\ntitle: A Letter from Home\nclass: story\ntone: warm\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: has(origin_noble) and (month == 6 or month == 12) and term_index <= 7\nrepeat: 6\npriority: 60\nart: letter\ntags: home money\nscene: A heavy envelope with a wax family seal on a writing desk, a bank draft half out of it.\non_show:\n  unflag family_gift\n  chance unlikely:\n    flag family_gift\ntext: {month == 6: Before the spring term|Before the new year} a letter comes with your family's seal and a draft on the family bank. {flag(family_gift): {&Folded inside is a smaller note from your grandmother, and a little more money \"for something nice\".|There's an extra sum this time, marked \"a gift\", which in your family means it will be mentioned at dinner for a year.|Your father has added something on top, with a line about how proud he would be of better marks.}|{&The allowance, to the crown, and three pages about cousins who are doing very well.|The usual sum, and a reminder that the family name goes wherever you go.|The allowance, and a postscript asking whether you have met anyone suitable.}}\n> ok: Fold the letter away\n  noble_allowance\n  if flag(family_gift):\n    noble_gift\n  say: {&You read it twice: once for the money, once for what it wants from you.|You put the draft in your purse and the letter in a drawer, and the letter is heavier.|You write back the same night: thank you, yes, of course, you'll try.}\n\n@event scholarship_award\ntitle: The Bursary List\nclass: story\ntone: tense\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: has(origin_scholar) and (month == 6 or month == 12) and term_index <= 7\nrepeat: 6\npriority: 60\nart: scroll\ntags: study money\nscene: A list of names pinned outside the registrar's office, a clerk's red ink beside some of them.\ntext: The bursary list {month == 6: for the spring term|for the new year} goes up outside the registrar's office. {term_gpa >= 3.5: {&Your name is near the top with a small star beside it: the full stipend, and a merit bonus.|The clerk hands you the envelope herself. The full stipend, and a little extra \"for distinction\".|Your name, the full sum, and the word \"Merit\" in red ink, which the clerk says she rarely gets to use.}|{term_gpa >= 3.0: {&Your name is there: the stipend is renewed for another term.|\"Renewed,\" says the list, in the clerk's tidy hand. You breathe out.|Your stipend stands for another term. The marks held, if only just.}|{term_gpa > 0: {&Your name is there with a line through the amount. Last term's marks fell below the 3.0 the Crown pays for, and the stipend waits until they come back up.|The clerk is apologetic: the Crown pays for marks of 3.0 and above, and last term yours were not. Next term, perhaps.|No stipend this term. Your marks dipped below what the Crown pays for, and the list says so in plain ink.}|{&Your name isn't on the list. No marks were recorded for you last term, and the Crown pays for marks.|The clerk checks twice. No grades last term means no stipend this one.|An empty line where your amount should be: the Crown pays for marks, and last term you had none.}}}}\n> ok: Read your line again\n  scholarship_stipend\n  say: {term_gpa >= 3.0: {&You fold the envelope into your book and walk a little taller to breakfast.|Tuition, board and something left over. You allow yourself one good dinner.|You write home that the stipend held. Your family will read the letter aloud to the neighbours.}|{&You do the sums on the back of a lecture note, and they come out short. Work, then, or thinner meals.|You walk away from the list without looking back at it. Next term the marks will be better; they have to be.|You go straight to the library, which is free, and stay until it closes.}}\n\n@event trading_report\ntitle: The Company's Report\nclass: story\ntone: tense\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: has(origin_merchant) and (month == 6 or month == 12) and term_index <= 7\nrepeat: 6\npriority: 60\nart: scales\ntags: home money\nscene: A merchant company's ledger report on thick paper, columns of figures, a bank draft clipped to the first page or not.\non_show:\n  unflag trade_good\n  chance likely:\n    flag trade_good\ntext: {month == 6: The company's half-year report|The company's year-end report} arrives with a clerk's note on top. {flag(trade_good): {&The spice fleet came in early and whole. Your share is paid in full.|A good season: the northern warehouses sold out by midsummer. Your share comes with your mother's compliments.|Profit on every route but one. Your share is enclosed, and so is a list of what it cost.}|{&A ship lost off the southern coast, and a cargo of silk ruined by rain. There is no share this term.|The markets turned. The report is long and the envelope is empty: no share until trade recovers.|Pirates, tariffs and a warehouse fire, in that order. Your mother writes that the company cannot spare anything this term.}}\n> ok: Fold the report away\n  if flag(trade_good):\n    trade_returns\n  say: {flag(trade_good): {&You count it twice and lock it away. Next term's tuition is safe.|You read the figures like a story with a happy ending, and put the draft somewhere safe.|You think about what the good season cost someone else, and then about tuition, and put the draft away.}|{&You count what's in your purse instead. It will have to do.|You read the figures twice, as if they might change. They don't.|You write back that you understand, and then go and look at the job board.}}\n"},{"path":"content/events/people_others.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Character events — Kael, Bram, faculty and staff.\n#  (Kael's main arc lives in chain_hollowstar.grim.)\n# ============================================================\n\n# ================= KAEL DUMONT =================\n\n@event kael_snowball\ntitle: Snow in Thawmoon\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: kael\nwhen: rel(kael) >= 6 and month >= 5 and month <= 7\nweight: 10\nart: snow\ntags: social\nscene: A courtyard where it is snowing in one small circle only, a boy in a fur coat standing at its centre holding a perfect snowball.\ntext: It's snowing in the courtyard. Only in a small circle, around Kael, who is holding a snowball and looking at it as though he's never been allowed to throw one.\n> left: Leave him to it\n  say: When you pass again, the circle of snow is gone, and there's a small, lopsided snowman in its place.\n> right: Throw the first one\n  health -tiny\n  rel kael +small\n  mood +small\n  vigor +tiny\n  say: Your snowball hits him in the ear. He stares. Then he laughs — a real laugh, startled out of him — and the snow triples.\n\n@event kael_cold_hands\ntime: evening night\ntitle: Cold Hands\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: kael\nwhen: rel(kael) >= 12 and spark(kael) < 35\nweight: 10\nart: heart\ntags: social romance\nscene: Two students sitting close in a library alcove, one holding the other's frost-white hand between both of theirs.\ntext: Kael's hands are always cold. In the library tonight they're so cold the ink in his pen has frozen. He's trying to thaw it with his breath, embarrassed.\n> left: Lend him your gloves\n  health -tiny\n  rel kael +small\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: He wears them for the rest of the term. They're far too small. He doesn't care. Your own fingers go numb on the walk back.\n> right: Warm his hands in yours\n  rel kael +medium\n  spark kael +small\n  say: He goes very still. Then, slowly, the frost on his fingertips melts. \"Nobody's ever been warm enough,\" he says quietly. \"Before.\"\n\n@event kael_confession\nramp: spark(kael) 25..40\ntitle: Thaw\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: kael\nwhen: spark(kael) >= 25 and rel(kael) >= 18 and bond(kael) != \"partner\"\nweight: 30\nart: snow\ntags: romance\nscene: Frost-ferns spreading across a dark window behind a pale boy who is, for once, looking directly at someone.\ntext: \"I was taught not to want things,\" Kael says. \"Wanting things is how they control you.\" Frost blooms across the window behind him. \"I want you. I'm sorry. I don't know what to do with it.\"\n> left: Gently say no\n  spark kael -large\n  rel kael +tiny\n  say: He nods, as if he expected it. \"Thank you for being honest. Nobody is.\" The frost on the window fades slowly.\n> right: Show him what to do with it\n  bond kael partner\n  spark kael +large\n  mood +large\n  say: You kiss him. Every window in the corridor frosts over at once, and then, very slowly, melts.\n\n# ================= BRAM HOLLIS =================\n\n@event bram_intro\ntitle: Running in the Halls\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nactor: bram\nwhen: not met(bram) and turn >= 2\nweight: 30\nart: scroll\ntags: rival\nscene: A burly prefect with a red sash blocking a corridor, a demerit ledger open in one hand and a quill poised.\ntext: \"Running in the halls.\" A burly prefect with a red sash steps into your path, ledger already open. \"Name?\" You weren't running. You were walking briskly.\n> left: Accept the demerit\n  meet bram\n  mood -tiny\n  say: He writes your name very slowly, enjoying it. Bram Hollis. You will learn to recognise his footsteps.\n> right: Argue\n  meet bram\n  rel bram -medium\n  renown +small\n  say: \"Walking briskly,\" you insist. Two demerits: one for running, one for \"lip\". Some students nearby clap quietly.\n\n@event bram_hall_tax\ntitle: The Hall Tax\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: bram\nwhen: rel(bram) < 6 and not (flag(bram_sash_lost) and since(corvin_framed) < 10)\nweight: 12\nart: coin\ntags: rival money\nscene: A prefect leaning against a stone archway, palm out, a line of nervous first-years shuffling past him.\ntext: Bram has invented a \"hall tax\" for using the east staircase. Eight crowns. \"Maintenance,\" he says. The first-years are paying. He looks at you expectantly.\n> left: Pay it\n  gold -small\n  rel bram +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: He pockets the coins. \"Pleasure.\" You take the west staircase forever after.\n> right: Refuse loudly\n  alignment +tiny\n  rel bram -medium\n  renown +small\n  chance even:\n    gain watched\n    say: The first-years stop paying too. Bram is furious. He'll find a way to make you pay later. He always does.\n  else:\n    say: Your refusal spreads. Within a week, nobody pays the hall tax. Bram drops it, scowling.\n\n@event bram_sabotage\ntitle: Missing Notes\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: bram\nwhen: rel(bram) <= -12 and (month == 4 or month == 10)\nweight: 14\nart: scroll\ntags: rival study\nscene: An empty desk drawer, a few scattered pages on the floor, a red prefect's sash glimpsed disappearing around a corner.\ntext: Two days before exams, your revision notes vanish from your desk. All of them. You saw a red sash in the corridor.\n> left: Rewrite them from memory\n  challenge research apprentice+:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      say: You rewrite everything in one furious night. Your new notes are better than the old ones. Take that, Bram.\n    lose:\n      study -medium\n      health -tiny\n      say: You rewrite what you can. It isn't enough. Exams go worse than they should.\n> right: Search his room\n  challenge deceive vs bram.guile:\n    win:\n      rel bram -medium\n      guile +tiny\n      say: You find your notes under his mattress, and also a teddy bear. You take your notes and leave the bear exactly where it was, slightly straightened. He will know.\n    lose:\n      gain detention\n      rel bram -small\n      say: He catches you in his room. That's two weeks of detention, and he gets to supervise.\n\n@event bram_crying\ntitle: Behind the Stables\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: bram\nwhen: met(bram) and turn >= 8 and not flag(bram_softened)\nweight: 8\nart: letter\ntags: social\nscene: A big prefect sitting on an upturned bucket behind the stables, a crumpled letter in his fist, shoulders shaking.\ntext: Behind the stables, you find Bram Hollis sitting on a bucket, crying into a crumpled letter. He sees you and freezes. \"If you tell anyone,\" he begins, but his voice cracks.\n> left: Walk away and never mention it\n  rel bram +medium\n  say: You leave. You never tell anyone. Somehow, he knows you didn't. His demerits become fewer.\n> right: Sit down and ask what happened\n  mood -tiny\n  flag bram_softened\n  rel bram +huge\n  say: His father. A letter saying he's a disappointment, again. \"He was a prefect too,\" Bram says. \"Head boy. I'm just... loud.\" He wipes his face. \"Don't be nice to me. I don't know what to do with it.\"\n\n@event bram_turns\nramp: rel(bram) 6..15\ntitle: An Unexpected Ally\nclass: milestone\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: bram\nwhen: flag(bram_softened) and rel(bram) >= 6 and bond(bram) != \"friend\"\nweight: 12\nart: shield\ntags: social\nscene: A prefect stepping between a cornered student and a pair of older bullies, arms crossed.\ntext: Two fourth-years corner you by the boathouse, looking for trouble. Then a red sash steps between you. \"Problem?\" says Bram, cracking his knuckles. They leave fast.\n> left: Thank him awkwardly\n  rel bram +medium\n  say: \"Don't make it weird,\" he says, and walks off. He's smiling, slightly.\n> right: Offer your hand\n  rel bram +large\n  bond bram friend\n  say: He stares at your hand. Then he shakes it, hard. \"Fine. Friends. But I'm still giving you demerits.\" He does. Fewer.\n\n@event bram_duel\ntitle: A Formal Challenge\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: bram\nwhen: bond(bram) == \"rival\" or bond(bram) == \"nemesis\"\nweight: 12\nart: duel\ntags: rival danger\nscene: A white glove lying on a dormitory floor, a red sash folded beside it, a crowd peering through the doorway.\ntext: Bram throws a glove at your feet in front of half the dormitory. \"Formal challenge. The dueling green, right now. Unless you're a coward.\" Everyone is watching.\n> left: Decline\n  renown -small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: \"Coward,\" he says, delighted. It sticks for a while. Then people forget. Mostly.\n> right: Accept\n  challenge combat vs bram.vigor:\n    win:\n      renown +large\n      rel bram +small\n      say: You disarm him in three moves. The crowd goes wild. Bram, on the grass, starts laughing. \"Fine,\" he says. \"Fine.\"\n    lose:\n      health -large\n      renown +small\n      say: He wins. But it takes him a full ten minutes, and he's limping too. He nods to you after. Grudging respect.\n\n# ================= PROFESSOR THORNE =================\n\n@event thorne_intro\ntitle: The First Question\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nactor: thorne\nwhen: not met(thorne) and enrolled(evocation)\nweight: 16\nart: wand\ntags: study\nscene: A steep lecture theatre, a stern grey-bearded professor pointing a long finger at one student in the middle row.\ntext: First Evocation lecture. Professor Thorne surveys the room like a hawk over a field of mice. His finger lands on you. \"You. What is the first law of the Arcane?\"\n> left: Admit you don't know\n  meet thorne\n  rel thorne +small\n  say: \"Correct answer,\" he says. \"The first law is: know what you don't know. The rest of you, write that down.\"\n> right: Take a guess\n  meet thorne\n  challenge research apprentice wild:\n    win:\n      rel thorne +small\n      study evocation +tiny\n      say: \"Energy follows intention.\" His eyebrows twitch. \"Acceptable.\" The room exhales.\n    lose:\n      rel thorne -tiny\n      mood -tiny\n      say: Your guess is so wrong the student next to you gasps. Thorne simply says, \"No,\" and moves on.\n\n@event thorne_office_hours\ntime: noon afternoon\ntitle: Office Hours\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: thorne\nwhen: rel(thorne) >= 6 and arcana >= 14\nweight: 10\nart: candle\ntags: study\nscene: A cramped office full of scorched books and a single lit candle, a stern professor leaning back in his chair, fingers steepled.\ntext: Thorne stops you after class. \"Your casting is sloppy and your instincts are good. That's an annoying combination. Thursdays. Seven o'clock. My office. Don't be late.\"\n> left: Politely decline — you're stretched thin\n  rel thorne -tiny\n  say: \"Hm.\" He seems neither surprised nor pleased. Thorne is rarely either.\n> right: Be there at seven\n  arcana +small\n  rel thorne +medium\n  health -tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: Two hours of brutal drills. At the end, he hands you a book from his own shelf. \"Read it. Return it unburnt.\"\n\n@event thorne_storm_story\ntitle: The Storm Duel\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: thorne\nwhen: rel(thorne) >= 12\nweight: 8\nart: bolt\ntags: study\nscene: An old professor standing at a rain-lashed window, lightning illuminating a long scar along his jaw.\ntext: During a thunderstorm, Thorne stops mid-lecture and watches the lightning. \"They say I out-dueled a storm once,\" he says, to nobody in particular. \"Would you like to know what actually happened?\"\n> left: \"Yes, sir.\"\n  gain inspired\n  rel thorne +small\n  study evocation -tiny\n  say: He lost, he says. Badly. He spent a year learning why. \"Losing well,\" he says, \"is the only thing worth teaching.\" The class is silent. The lecture never resumes.\n> right: Stay quiet\n  rel thorne +tiny\n  study evocation +tiny\n  say: He shakes his head and returns to the lecture, which is a good one. You'll always wonder.\n\n@event thorne_recommendation\ntitle: A Letter of Recommendation\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: thorne\nwhen: rel(thorne) >= 18 and final_year\nweight: 16\nart: letter\ntags: study\nscene: A wax-sealed envelope on a professor's desk with a student's name written on it in sharp, angular handwriting.\ntext: Thorne calls you to his office and slides an envelope across the desk. \"The Conclave asked for my recommendations. I wrote one. I don't write many.\" He pauses. \"Don't make me regret it.\"\n> left: Thank him sincerely\n  flag thorne_letter\n  rel thorne +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: \"Go away,\" he says gruffly. \"I have marking.\" You see him smile at the window as you leave.\n> right: Ask why you\n  mood -tiny\n  flag thorne_letter\n  rel thorne +medium\n  say: \"Because you ask questions like that,\" he says. \"Most students don't. Go.\" You leave unsure whether you were praised or dismissed.\n\n@event thorne_disappointed\ntitle: A Word in Private\nclass: routine\ntone: grim\ntrigger: social\nactor: thorne\nwhen: (turn > 4 and gpa < 2.0)\nweight: 14\nart: scales\ntags: study crisis\nscene: A professor standing by his desk with a sheaf of red-marked papers, a student standing awkwardly before him.\ntext: Thorne holds up your last three Evocation essays. All red. \"You're better than this,\" he says. \"That's not a compliment. It's an accusation.\"\n> left: Make excuses\n  rel thorne -small\n  mood -tiny\n  guile +tiny\n  alignment -tiny\n  say: He listens to them all and says nothing. The silence is the worst grade yet.\n> right: Ask for help\n  mood -small\n  rel thorne +medium\n  study evocation +medium\n  lore +tiny\n  say: He sighs and pulls out a chair. \"Sit. We'll start with the first essay, and we'll stay until it's right.\" You stay until midnight.\n\n# ================= OLD MAUD =================\n\n@event maud_chickens\ntitle: The Great Chicken Escape\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nactor: maud\nwhen: not met(maud) and turn >= 2\nweight: 12\nart: feather\ntags: nature social\nscene: Chickens fleeing across a lawn in every direction, an old woman in a straw hat waving a broom, students diving after birds.\ntext: Forty chickens are loose on the main lawn, pursued by an old woman in a straw hat. \"Don't just stand there, dearie!\" she shouts at you. \"Grab the speckled one, she's the ringleader!\"\n> left: Keep walking\n  say: You hear a crash, a squawk and a triumphant cackle behind you. The chickens, it seems, are caught.\n> right: Grab the speckled one\n  meet maud\n  challenge feat apprentice wild:\n    win:\n      rel maud +medium\n      say: You tackle the ringleader. The rest give up. \"Good hands,\" says Old Maud, pressing a warm egg into your palm. \"Come for tea.\"\n    lose:\n      rel maud +small\n      health -tiny\n      say: The ringleader escapes and pecks you, but Maud is impressed by your enthusiasm. \"Come for tea. Bring plasters.\"\n\n@event maud_honey\ntitle: Honey for a Cold\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: maud\nwhen: rel(maud) >= 3 and (has(sick) or health < 40)\nweight: 18\nart: flower\ntags: nature\nscene: A small cottage kitchen with bundles of herbs hanging from the beams, an old woman pouring golden honey into a steaming mug.\ntext: Maud spots you sniffling across the lawn and drags you into her cottage. \"Sit,\" she orders, pouring honey into something steaming that smells of lemon and thunder.\n> left: Drink it all\n  health +medium\n  lose sick\n  rel maud +small\n  say: It tastes like summer and lightning. You sneeze sparks for the rest of the day. By morning you're cured and slightly glowing.\n> right: Drink it and help with chores\n  health +small\n  lose sick\n  rel maud +small\n  alchemy +tiny\n  say: You sip and sort herbs while she tells you the names of every plant in the room. You remember most of them.\n\n@event maud_old_student\ntitle: Maud Was a Student Once\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: social\nactor: maud\nwhen: (rel(maud) >= 9 or (clue(lead_maud_friend) and rel(maud) >= 4)) and clock(hollowstar) >= 1\nweight: 14\nart: moon\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: An old woman sitting on a bench by the lake at dusk, looking at the water as if it might look back.\ntext: \"I was a student here, you know,\" Maud says, watching the lake. \"Sixty years ago. The lake froze one spring. Three of my friends went down to the boathouse that night. Only two came back.\" She doesn't look at you. \"Ask me what I know.\"\n> left: \"You don't have to tell me.\"\n  rel maud +medium\n  queue maud_tea_tells in 1\n  say: She pats your hand. \"Kind. But time's short, dearie.\" She tells you anyway, a little at a time, over many cups of tea.\n> right: \"What do you know?\"\n  rel maud +small\n  mood -small\n  flag knows_founders_seal\n  find knows_founders_seal\n  find lead_seventh_tower\n  say: \"The founders built this place as a lid. Seven towers, and the key kept in the one nobody climbs.\" She finally looks at you. \"The thing below loves a lonely heart. Don't be lonely.\" It's a long walk back in the dark.\n\n@event maud_bees\ntitle: The Silver Bees\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: maud\nwhen: rel(maud) >= 6 and month >= 7 and month <= 10\nweight: 10\nart: flower\ntags: nature\nscene: Wooden hives in a wildflower meadow, an old woman in a veiled hat lifting a frame dripping with silver honey, bees glinting in the sun.\ntext: \"The silver bees need moving to the summer meadow,\" Maud says, handing you a veiled hat. \"They're friendly. Mostly. Don't make any sudden thoughts.\"\n> left: Help with the hives\n  challenge feat apprentice- wild:\n    win:\n      health +small\n      rel maud +medium\n      gold +small\n      say: The bees hum around you like a blessing. Maud gives you a jar of silver honey that sells for eight crowns. You eat half first.\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      rel maud +small\n      say: You have a sudden thought. The bees know. You spend the evening with vinegar on your stings.\n> right: Watch from a safe distance\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You watch Maud move hives barehanded, singing to them. It's the calmest you've felt all month.\n\n# Review fixes (G): the kind answer to maud_old_student; she tells you anyway, over tea.\n@event maud_tea_tells\ntitle: Over Many Cups of Tea\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: queued\nactor: maud\nart: cup\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: A cluttered cottage kitchen at dusk, two teacups and a pot between an old woman and a student, the lake grey in the window.\ntext: Maud keeps her word, a little at a time. Over a month of afternoons and more tea than is good for anyone, the story of that spring comes out in pieces: the boathouse, the cold, the friend who didn't come back.\n> left: Listen to the end\n  rel maud +small\n  mood -small\n  flag knows_founders_seal\n  find knows_founders_seal\n  find lead_seventh_tower\n  say: \"The founders built this place as a lid,\" she says on the last afternoon. \"Seven towers, and the key kept in the one nobody climbs. The thing below loves a lonely heart. Don't be lonely.\" She pours another cup.\n> right: Tell her it can wait\n  rel maud +medium\n  queue maud_tea_tells in 2\n  say: \"It can't, dearie,\" she says, \"but I like you for saying so.\" She tells you about her chickens instead. You'll come back for the rest.\n\n@event maud_ill\ntitle: Maud Is Unwell\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: maud\nwhen: rel(maud) >= 12 and year >= 2 and not flag(maud_ill_done)\nweight: 12\nart: house\ntags: social\nscene: A small cottage bedroom with a patchwork quilt, an old woman propped up on pillows, a student reading aloud by candlelight.\ntext: Maud's chickens are running wild, unfed. You find her in bed, grey-faced and coughing. \"Just a chill,\" she wheezes. \"I'm eighty-one. I'm allowed.\"\n> left: Fetch Sister Amaranth\n  flag maud_ill_done\n  rel maud +medium\n  say: Amaranth brings tonics and a stern lecture. Maud is back on her feet in a week, grumbling about fuss.\n> right: Look after her yourself for a week\n  health -tiny\n  alignment +tiny\n  flag maud_ill_done\n  rel maud +large\n  study -small\n  gain blessed\n  say: You feed chickens, brew teas, read her the newspaper. When she's well, she gives you a sprig of rosemary. \"You'll do,\" she says. \"You'll do.\"\n\n# ================= HEADMISTRESS ISOLDE MARROW =================\n\n@event isolde_intro\ntime: evening\ntitle: She Knows Your Name\nclass: windfall\ntone: light\nactor: isolde\nwhen: not met(isolde) and turn >= 3 and year == 1 and term\nweight: 5\nart: crown\ntags: story\nscene: A tall pale woman in dark robes pausing behind a student's chair in a candlelit dining hall, a hand resting lightly on the chair back.\ntext: At dinner, a cool hand rests on the back of your chair. The headmistress. \"{player},\" she says, though you've never met. \"Your marks in Arcana are interesting. Your marks in punctuality, less so.\" She moves on.\n> left: Stammer an apology\n  meet isolde\n  rel isolde +tiny\n  say: She's already three tables away. Every student between you sits a little straighter.\n> right: \"Interesting how, Headmistress?\"\n  meet isolde\n  rel isolde +small\n  renown +small\n  say: She pauses. Turns. Almost smiles. \"Ask me again at the end of your first year,\" she says. The table stares at you.\n\n@event isolde_tea\ntitle: Tea in the Tower\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: isolde\nwhen: renown >= 25 and rel(isolde) >= 0\nweight: 10\nart: cup\ntags: social\nscene: A round tower study at twilight, two delicate teacups on a small table, a headmistress pouring tea with a perfectly steady hand.\ntext: An invitation to tea with the headmistress. Students talk about these the way soldiers talk about battles. She pours. \"Tell me, {player}: what do you want to become?\"\n> left: Give an ambitious answer\n  challenge negotiate apprentice+:\n    win:\n      rel isolde +medium\n      renown +small\n      say: She listens without interrupting, then says, \"Good. Ambition without arrogance. Rarer than you'd think.\"\n    lose:\n      rel isolde -tiny\n      say: She nods politely. \"Ambition is a fine servant,\" she says, \"and a poor master.\" You're dismissed shortly after.\n> right: Admit you don't know yet\n  rel isolde +medium\n  lore +tiny\n  say: \"Neither did I, at your age,\" she says. \"I still don't, some days. That's the point of an academy.\" She refills your cup.\n\n@event isolde_warning\ntitle: A Quiet Warning\nclass: routine\ntone: grim\ntrigger: social\nactor: isolde\nwhen: has(detention) or has(watched) or has(probation)\nweight: 10\nart: scales\ntags: crisis\nscene: A headmistress walking alongside a student in a long colonnade, hands clasped behind her back, speaking without looking at them.\ntext: The headmistress falls into step beside you in the colonnade. \"I hear your name more often than I'd like,\" she says pleasantly. \"In the wrong rooms. I'd hate to hear it in mine.\"\n> left: Promise to do better\n  rel isolde +small\n  mood -tiny\n  lose watched\n  say: \"Good.\" She stops walking. You keep going. When you look back, she's gone.\n> right: Defend yourself\n  challenge negotiate journeyman +1 if renown >= 25:\n    win:\n      rel isolde +small\n      lose watched\n      say: You lay it out plainly. She listens, then nods once. \"Fair. I'll have a word with certain prefects.\"\n    lose:\n      rel isolde -small\n      say: \"Hm,\" she says. \"Let's hope you're right.\" It doesn't feel like hope.\n\n# ---------------- Track leads met on a card of their own (playtest 2, N1): queued by enrolling or joining ----------------\n\n@event vey_intro\ntitle: Tea That Isn't There\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nactor: vey\nwhen: not met(vey) and enrolled(illusion)\nweight: 30\nart: mask\ntags: study magic\nscene: A lecture room hung with velvet curtains, a woman in a violet coat pouring from an empty teapot into a student's empty cup.\ntext: First Illusion lecture. Professor Vey pours you a cup of tea from a pot you would swear is empty, and waits. \"Milk?\" she asks. The whole row turns to see what you'll do with a cup of nothing.\n> left: Drink it, and thank her\n  meet vey\n  challenge deceive using charm apprentice+:\n    win:\n      charm +small\n      rel vey +small\n      say: You sip, sigh and ask where she buys it. Vey's mouth twitches. \"Lapsang. There was never any tea, of course. Sit down; you'll do.\"\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      say: You raise the cup with a flourish and tip it a little too far. Nothing pours out, and the whole row sees nothing pour out. Vey writes something down, and smiles at it.\n> right: Say the cup is empty\n  meet vey\n  lore +small\n  say: \"It's empty,\" you say. \"So it is,\" says Vey, pleased and disappointed at once. \"Honest eyes. We shall have to do something about those.\"\n\n@event brask_intro\ntitle: Shovel. There. Go.\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nactor: brask\nwhen: not met(brask) and enrolled(beastcraft)\nweight: 30\nart: paw\ntags: study nature\nscene: A long stable in morning light, a grey-haired professor talking earnestly to a hippogriff while a student stands holding a shovel.\ntext: First Beastcraft lesson, at the stables. Professor Brask is explaining to a hippogriff, in full and courteous sentences, that the new students will be clumsy and she is to be patient with them. Then she turns to you. \"Shovel. There. Go.\"\n> left: Muck out without a word\n  meet brask\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  rel brask +small\n  say: You shovel until your arms shake. Brask watches, then tells the hippogriff, loud enough for you to hear, \"This one might do.\" To you she says nothing, which you later learn is praise.\n> right: Ask what today's lesson is\n  meet brask\n  lore +small\n  rel brask -tiny\n  say: Brask tells the hippogriff a story about a student who asked questions before shovelling. It ends with a bite. \"Moral: shovel.\" You shovel, a little behind the others.\n\n@event pell_first_shift\ntitle: Count Aloud\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nactor: pell\nwhen: not met(pell) and member(apothecary_clerk)\nweight: 30\nart: bottle\ntags: job\nscene: A narrow apothecary lined with tiny labelled drawers, an old man on a ladder holding out a glass dropper to a nervous new clerk.\ntext: Your first shift at Pell & Daughters. Old Pell climbs down from his ladder, hands you a dropper and a bottle of tincture, and points at a waiting customer. \"Twelve drops,\" he says. \"Aloud. Everyone counts aloud in this shop. The drawers like it.\"\n> left: Count every drop aloud\n  meet pell\n  alchemy +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: \"One. Two. Three.\" Your voice fills the shop; the customer joins in at nine. Pell nods once. \"Twelve. Not eleven. Not thirteen. You'll count like that until they bury you.\"\n> right: Count in your head, quickly\n  meet pell\n  challenge brew apprentice:\n    win:\n      rel pell -tiny\n      renown +tiny\n      say: Twelve drops, done in a breath; the customer tells the whole queue how quick the new clerk is. Pell counts the level against the light. \"Correct. Next time, aloud. I like to hear a thing being right.\"\n    lose:\n      rel pell -small\n      say: You lose count at eight and guess. Pell tips the bottle out and starts it again, aloud, without a word to you. The customer leaves with the right dose and a look at you.\n"},{"path":"content/events/people_students.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Character events — fellow students.\n#  Intros are `trigger: random` (they introduce the NPC).\n#  Everything else is `trigger: social`: drawn from the monthly\n#  social slot, only once the NPC has been met.\n#  Romance arc per character: spark (generic) -> love >= 35 ->\n#  confession -> partner events. See social.grim for generic ones.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ================= LYRA VANESHADE =================\n\n@event lyra_intro\ntitle: The Last Copy\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nactor: lyra\nwhen: not met(lyra) and turn >= 2\nweight: 30\nart: book\ntags: social rival\nscene: Two hands reaching for the same red-bound book on a library shelf; one hand wears a signet ring with a flame crest.\ntext: You reach for the last copy of \"Foundations of Flame\" and a ringed hand gets there first. \"I need it more,\" says a tall girl with crimson hair, not looking at you. \"You'll only underline the wrong bits.\"\n> left: Let her have it\n  meet lyra\n  rel lyra +tiny\n  say: \"At least you know your place,\" she says, which is somehow worse than rudeness. Lyra Vaneshade. Everyone knows the name.\n> right: Argue for it\n  meet lyra\n  challenge negotiate vs lyra.charm:\n    win:\n      rel lyra +medium\n      say: You quote chapter three at her. She raises an eyebrow, then hands you the book. \"Fine. Return it underlined correctly.\"\n    lose:\n      rel lyra -small\n      say: She wins the argument in four sentences and leaves with the book. You think of the perfect comeback that night.\n\n@event lyra_letter\ntitle: A Letter With a Black Seal\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: lyra\nwhen: rel(lyra) >= 6 and (year > 1 or month > 4)\nweight: 12\nart: letter\ntags: social\nscene: A girl sitting on a window seat in an empty corridor, crushing a letter with a black wax seal in her fist, face turned away.\ntext: You find Lyra on a window seat with a crumpled letter. Black seal. Her family's. She's not crying; Vaneshades don't. But she's not quite not crying either.\n> left: Pretend you didn't see\n  say: You walk past. Later, she's flawless again, as if nothing happened. Maybe nothing did.\n> right: Sit down beside her\n  mood -tiny\n  rel lyra +large\n  spark lyra +tiny\n  flag lyra_vulnerable\n  say: \"Second place,\" she says eventually. \"I came second in the Winter Trials. My mother wants to know who beat me.\" She almost laughs. \"It's always someone.\"\n\n@event lyra_study_rivalry\ntime: morning noon\ntitle: Top of the Class\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: lyra\nwhen: rel(lyra) > -24 and term and not wrapup\nweight: 10\nrepeat: 4\nart: scroll\ntags: social study rival\nscene: Two students at opposite ends of a long library table, surrounded by towers of books, glaring over the tops of them.\ntext: The professors post this week's rankings, with an open paper this afternoon for anyone who wants to improve their place. Lyra is first{&. You're close enough to see the back of her head.|, again. You're close enough to read her handwriting.|. Of course she is. You're one name below her, which is somehow worse than three.} She notices you noticing.\n> left: Suggest studying together\n  mood -tiny\n  lore +small\n  rel lyra +small\n  say: {&\"Together?\" She considers this like a foreign custom. Then she pulls out a chair. She's an excellent, merciless study partner.|She already has two cups of tea and your chair pulled out. \"You're late,\" she says. You aren't.|\"Fine. But I'm quizzing you first.\" She is merciless again, and you're better for it.}\n> right: Beat her outright\n  challenge research vs lyra.lore:\n    win:\n      study +medium\n      rel lyra +small\n      renown +small\n      say: The afternoon paper puts you first. Lyra stares at the list for a long time. Then she finds you and says, \"Good.\" She means it.\n    lose:\n      study +tiny\n      rel lyra -tiny\n      say: {&You cram through lunch and come third. Lyra is still first. She doesn't gloat. That's worse.|Second, by one mark. Lyra leaves a note on your desk: \"Close.\" You keep it.|You come fourth. Lyra looks almost disappointed on your behalf, which is unbearable.}\n\n@event lyra_family_gala\ntitle: The Vaneshade Winter Gala\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: lyra\nwhen: rel(lyra) >= 18 and month >= 3 and month <= 6\nweight: 12\nart: crown\ntags: social romance\nscene: A glittering ballroom in a noble house, candles floating in mid-air, a tall woman in crimson watching the crowd like a hawk.\ntext: \"My family's winter gala,\" Lyra says, too casually. \"I need a guest who won't embarrass me. You'll do. Probably. You'll need proper clothes.\"\n> left: Decline\n  rel lyra -tiny\n  say: \"Of course.\" A pause. \"It doesn't matter.\" It clearly does.\n> right: Go with her (15 gold for clothes)\n  gold -medium\n  charm +tiny\n  renown +small\n  spark lyra +small\n  rel lyra +small\n  chance unlikely -1 if has(origin_noble):\n    mood -small\n    say: Lyra's mother asks who your parents are, and then asks no further questions at all. Lyra squeezes your hand under the table, hard.\n  else:\n    mood +medium\n    say: You dance, you charm a duke, you don't spill anything. In the carriage home, Lyra falls asleep on your shoulder.\n\n@event lyra_confession\nramp: spark(lyra) 25..40\ntitle: Lyra Doesn't Lose\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: lyra\nwhen: spark(lyra) >= 25 and rel(lyra) >= 18 and bond(lyra) != \"partner\"\nweight: 30\nart: flame\ntags: romance\nscene: A girl with crimson hair standing very close in a practice ring at dusk, sparks still drifting from her wand, cheeks flushed.\ntext: After practice, Lyra doesn't leave. \"I don't lose,\" she says, staring at the scorch marks. \"I don't. But I think — \" She stops. Starts again. \"I think I've lost to you. Say something before I set something on fire.\"\n> left: Let her down gently\n  spark lyra -large\n  rel lyra +tiny\n  say: She nods, sharp and quick. \"Understood.\" She practises alone for a month. Then one day she nods to you in the corridor, and it's almost all right.\n> right: Kiss her\n  bond lyra partner\n  spark lyra +large\n  mood +large\n  say: Nothing catches fire. Well. Something does, but it's only the practice dummy, and neither of you notices for a while.\n\n@event lyra_family_ultimatum\ntitle: The Ultimatum\nclass: routine\ntone: grim\ntrigger: social\nactor: lyra\nwhen: bond(lyra) == \"partner\" and turn >= 14\nweight: 18\nart: crown\ntags: romance crisis\nscene: Lyra holding a letter with a black seal, her other hand clenched around a ring she has just pulled off.\ntext: Another black-sealed letter. Lyra reads it twice and goes white. \"My mother has found out about us. End it, she says, or she ends my inheritance.\" She looks at you. \"Tell me what to do.\"\n> left: \"Choose yourself, not them.\"\n  if spark(lyra) >= 50:\n    challenge negotiate vs lyra.charm likely:\n      win:\n        rel lyra +large\n        spark lyra +medium\n        flag lyra_free\n        say: She tears the letter in half. Then in quarters. Then she sets the quarters on fire and laughs like she hasn't in years.\n      lose:\n        rel lyra -small\n        spark lyra -medium\n        mood -medium\n        say: \"Easy for you to say,\" she snaps. She doesn't mean it. She also doesn't take it back.\n  else:\n    challenge negotiate vs lyra.charm:\n      win:\n        rel lyra +large\n        spark lyra +medium\n        flag lyra_free\n        say: She tears the letter in half. Then in quarters. Then she sets the quarters on fire and laughs like she hasn't in years.\n      lose:\n        rel lyra -small\n        spark lyra -medium\n        mood -medium\n        say: \"Easy for you to say,\" she snaps. She doesn't mean it. She also doesn't take it back.\n> right: Step back so she doesn't lose everything\n  bond lyra ex\n  gain heartbroken\n  rel lyra +small\n  say: You end it before she has to. She doesn't argue. That's how you know how much it costs her.\n\n@event lyra_rival_sabotage\ntitle: Sabotage in Class\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: lyra\nwhen: bond(lyra) == \"rival\" and rel(lyra) <= -4\nweight: 12\nart: potion\ntags: rival\nscene: A cauldron spitting black smoke, a crimson-haired student walking away with a tiny vial hidden in her sleeve.\ntext: Your perfect potion turns black and starts screaming. Across the room, Lyra is very carefully not looking at you.\n> left: Retaliate\n  challenge deceive vs lyra.guile:\n    win:\n      rel lyra -small\n      renown +small\n      say: Next lesson, her wand produces only bubbles. Pink ones. She knows. You both know. It's war. It's sort of fun.\n    lose:\n      gain detention\n      rel lyra -medium\n      say: You're caught slipping something into her bag. She looks genuinely delighted.\n> right: Confront her directly\n  mood -tiny\n  rel lyra +medium\n  say: \"That was beneath you.\" She flushes. \"...Yes. It was.\" The next day there's a perfect replacement potion on your desk.\n\n@event lyra_burnout\ntime: morning\ntitle: Lyra Falls\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: lyra\nwhen: rel(lyra) >= 12 and year >= 2\nweight: 10\nart: cross\ntags: social\nscene: A girl collapsed across a library desk among scattered notes, quill still in hand, candle burned down to nothing.\ntext: You find Lyra face-down on a library desk at dawn, not asleep — out cold. She's been studying for three days straight. \"Don't tell anyone,\" she mumbles when she comes round. \"Mother can't know.\"\n> left: Take her to the infirmary anyway\n  rel lyra -tiny\n  spark lyra +tiny\n  say: She's furious. Then she sleeps for nineteen hours. When she wakes up, she says, quietly, \"Thank you.\"\n> right: Cover for her and make her rest\n  health -tiny\n  alignment +tiny\n  rel lyra +medium\n  study -tiny\n  say: You tell her professors she has a cold and sit by her door with your homework. She sleeps. You keep watch.\n\n# ================= TOBIAS FENNICK =================\n\n@event tobias_intro\ntitle: The Lost Boy\nclass: windfall\ntone: light\nactor: tobias\nwhen: not met(tobias) and turn >= 2\nweight: 30\nart: compass\ntags: social\nscene: A large, gentle-faced boy leading a crying first-year by the hand down the wrong corridor, both very lost.\ntext: A big, broad-shouldered boy is leading a crying first-year through the corridors. \"Don't worry, I know exactly where the infirmary is,\" he says, and walks confidently into a broom cupboard.\n> left: Point them the right way\n  meet tobias\n  rel tobias +small\n  say: \"Ah. Left, you say. Right. I mean, correct.\" He grins, enormous and sheepish. \"Tobias. Turnip scholarship. Thanks.\"\n> right: Walk with them\n  meet tobias\n  rel tobias +medium\n  mood +small\n  say: You end up taking the scenic route, and the first-year stops crying because Tobias is telling a very long story about a goat.\n\n@event tobias_homesick\ntitle: Homesick\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: tobias\nwhen: rel(tobias) >= 6\nweight: 12\nart: leaf\ntags: social nature\nscene: A dormitory windowsill crowded with clay pots of seedlings, a big boy watering them with a tiny can.\ntext: Tobias has gone quiet. He's planted seedlings in every cup in the common room. \"Harvest at home,\" he says. \"First one I've missed. Ma's doing it alone.\"\n> left: Cheer him up with a joke\n  rel tobias +small\n  say: Your joke is terrible. He laughs anyway, the big rumbling laugh, and that seems to help.\n> right: Help him build a window garden\n  gold -tiny\n  rel tobias +small\n  alchemy +tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: You buy seeds and a stack of pots. By the end of the week the common room smells of tomato leaves. Tobias whistles while he waters them.\n\n@event tobias_bad_harvest\ntitle: The Harvest Failed\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: tobias\nwhen: rel(tobias) >= 15 and turn >= 8 and not flag(tobias_harvest_done)\nweight: 14\nart: rain\ntags: social money\nscene: A boy packing a battered trunk in an empty dormitory, a letter with smudged handwriting open on the bed.\ntext: Tobias is packing. Blight took the harvest. His family needs money, and hands. \"Thirty crowns would get them through winter,\" he says, not asking. \"But I haven't got thirty crowns. I've got two arms.\"\n> left: Persuade him to stay and find work here\n  flag tobias_harvest_done\n  challenge negotiate vs tobias.charm:\n    win:\n      rel tobias +medium\n      say: You walk him to the job board. He signs up for double shifts at the stables and sends every crown home. He stays.\n    lose:\n      rel tobias +tiny\n      mood -small\n      flag tobias_went_home\n      say: He listens, nods, and takes the barge in the morning. He's back in two months, thinner and quieter.\n> right: Give him thirty crowns\n  when: gold >= 30\n  flag tobias_harvest_done\n  gold -large\n  alignment +tiny\n  rel tobias +huge\n  spark tobias +tiny\n  say: He tries to refuse three times. Then he hugs you so hard your spine cracks. \"I'll pay you back. Every crown. Every one.\"\n  queue tobias_repays in 6\n\n@event tobias_repays\ntitle: Every Crown\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: queued\nactor: tobias\nart: pouch\ntags: social money\nscene: A clumsy hand-stitched pouch of coins and a jar of honey left on a dormitory pillow.\ntext: A lumpy, hand-stitched pouch on your pillow: thirty crowns exactly, in small coins. And a jar of honey from home. A note in big, careful letters: \"TOLD YOU. — T.\"\n> ok: Smile\n  gold +large\n  rel tobias +small\n  mood +small\n  say: The honey tastes of a farm you have never seen and somehow already miss.\n\n@event tobias_godparent\ntitle: The Mandrake Baby\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: tobias\nwhen: rel(tobias) >= 9\nweight: 10\nart: leaf\ntags: social nature\nscene: A tiny mandrake in a pot wearing a knitted hat, a beaming boy presenting it like a newborn.\ntext: Tobias presents a flowerpot containing a very small mandrake in a knitted hat. \"This is Gerald,\" he says. \"I'd like you to be his godparent.\" Gerald screams softly.\n> left: Politely decline\n  rel tobias -tiny\n  say: \"No pressure,\" says Tobias, visibly pressured. Gerald glares at you from under his hat.\n> right: Accept with honour\n  health -tiny\n  rel tobias +small\n  mood +small\n  alchemy +tiny\n  say: You are now Gerald's godparent. Gerald bites you. Tobias says this means Gerald loves you.\n\n@event tobias_bullied\ntitle: Tobias and the Prefect\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: tobias\nwhen: met(bram) and rel(tobias) >= 3\nweight: 10\nart: shield\ntags: social rival\nscene: A burly prefect mocking a big farm boy in a crowded corridor, students sniggering, the boy's ears bright red.\ntext: Bram is doing an impression of Tobias's accent in the corridor. People are laughing. Tobias is pretending not to hear, ears burning red.\n> left: Stay out of it\n  rel tobias -small\n  say: Tobias doesn't mention it. He doesn't sit with you at dinner, either.\n> right: Step in\n  alignment +small\n  rel tobias +large\n  rel bram -medium\n  challenge negotiate vs bram.charm wild:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      say: \"Funny,\" you say. \"Do your father next.\" The laughter turns on Bram. He stalks off. Tobias stares at you in awe.\n    lose:\n      gain watched\n      say: Bram turns his attention to you instead, with interest. Tobias brings you a pie later. A whole pie.\n\n@event tobias_confession\nramp: spark(tobias) 25..40\ntitle: A Confession, With Vegetables\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: tobias\nwhen: spark(tobias) >= 25 and rel(tobias) >= 18 and bond(tobias) != \"partner\"\nweight: 30\nart: heart\ntags: romance\nscene: A big boy holding out a perfect pumpkin like an offering in a greenhouse, cheeks scarlet.\ntext: Tobias corners you in the greenhouse holding a very large pumpkin. \"I grew this,\" he says. \"For you. It's the best one. I didn't know how else to — I like you. Like that. I've practised this on the pumpkin. Sorry.\"\n> left: Let him down gently\n  spark tobias -large\n  rel tobias +small\n  say: He nods a lot. \"That's all right. That's fine. You can still have the pumpkin.\" You keep it until it rots, out of loyalty.\n> right: Say yes\n  bond tobias partner\n  spark tobias +large\n  mood +large\n  say: He drops the pumpkin. It's fine; pumpkins are sturdy. So, it turns out, are his hugs.\n\n@event tobias_picnic\ntitle: A Picnic in the Orchard\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: tobias\nwhen: bond(tobias) == \"partner\" and month >= 7 and month <= 10\nweight: 14\nrepeat: 10\nart: tree\ntags: romance nature\nscene: A checked blanket under blossoming apple trees, a basket of bread and cheese, two students lying in the dappled light.\ntext: Tobias has packed a picnic: {~bread he baked, cheese he begged from the kitchens, apples from the orchard he has befriended|a pie with your initials in the crust, slightly burnt, and a jar of honey from someone he helped|plums, hard cheese and something he calls a salad, which is mostly flowers}. \"No {~exams|essays|professors} for six hours,\" he announces. \"I checked.\"\n> left: Bring your books anyway\n  lore +small\n  spark tobias -tiny\n  say: You study on the blanket. He eyes the books, sighs a very small sigh, and falls asleep with his head in your lap. It's still lovely.\n> right: Leave everything behind\n  mood +medium\n  health +small\n  spark tobias +small\n  say: The afternoon goes golden and slow. You can't remember the last time you didn't think about grades.\n\n# ================= SERAPHINE HALE =================\n\n@event seraphine_intro\ntitle: Your Left Shoe\nclass: windfall\ntone: eerie\nactor: seraphine\nwhen: not met(seraphine) and turn >= 2\nweight: 30\nart: eye\ntags: social mystery\nscene: A slight girl with silver-grey eyes and a scarf of stars passing in a corridor, pointing absently at someone's feet.\ntext: A girl drifts past you in the corridor and says, without stopping, \"You're going to lose your left shoe today. Don't worry, it's under the stairs in the east wing.\"\n> left: Ignore her\n  meet seraphine\n  say: That afternoon a staircase moves and eats your left shoe. It is, eventually, under the stairs in the east wing.\n> right: Ask how she knows\n  meet seraphine\n  rel seraphine +medium\n  say: \"I don't know things,\" she says. \"Things know me.\" Then: \"Seraphine. You'll want the shoe back by four.\" You get it back at 3:58.\n\n@event seraphine_tarot\ntitle: The Cards\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: social\nactor: seraphine\nwhen: rel(seraphine) >= 3\nweight: 12\nart: cards\ntags: social mystery\nscene: A tarot spread on a velvet cloth in candlelight, a girl's pale fingers turning over a card showing a lightning-struck tower.\ntext: Seraphine shuffles a battered tarot deck. \"One card,\" she says. \"For you. Only if you want it. They're quite rude sometimes.\"\n> left: Draw a card\n  chance even:\n    mood +small\n    say: The Star. \"Hope,\" she says, delighted. \"And someone's going to tell you a secret. Oh — it's me. I like you.\" She blushes.\n    rel seraphine +small\n    spark seraphine +small\n  else:\n    mood -tiny\n    say: The Tower. She looks at it a long time. \"It's fine,\" she says. \"Towers fall. That's what they're for.\"\n    rel seraphine +small\n> right: Decline\n  rel seraphine +tiny\n  say: \"Good,\" she says, putting the deck away. \"Knowing is overrated.\"\n\n@event seraphine_lake_vision\ntitle: She Walks Into the Lake\nclass: story\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: seraphine\nwhen: rel(seraphine) >= 6 and month >= 3 and month <= 9\nweight: 10\nart: wave\ntags: social danger\nscene: A girl in a nightgown wading into a misty lake at night, eyes glowing silver, arms outstretched toward something under the water.\ntext: From your window you see Seraphine walking into the lake in her nightgown, eyes shining silver, already up to her waist.\n> left: Wake a prefect\n  rel seraphine +small\n  say: The prefect drags her out, coughing. She doesn't remember anything. She looks at you strangely for days.\n> right: Run down and pull her out\n  alignment +small\n  challenge feat apprentice likely:\n    win:\n      rel seraphine +large\n      spark seraphine +small\n      flag seraphine_lake_told\n      say: You reach her just as she goes under. On the shore she wakes and whispers, \"It was calling me. It called you too.\" Then she holds on very tight.\n    lose:\n      health -large\n      gain sick\n      rel seraphine +large\n      say: You both nearly drown. You both come down with Lake Fever. She brings you soup that tastes faintly of the future.\n\n@event seraphine_future\ntitle: I Saw You Old\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: social\nactor: seraphine\nwhen: rel(seraphine) >= 15\nweight: 10\nart: hourglass\ntags: social mystery\nscene: Two students sitting on the rim of a dry fountain, one looking up at the stars, the other looking at the first with a soft smile.\ntext: \"I saw you old,\" Seraphine says suddenly. \"Last night. You were happy. Really happy. There was someone with you.\" She pauses. \"Do you want to know who?\"\n> left: Yes, tell me\n  chance even:\n    spark seraphine +small\n    say: She looks at you for a long time. \"I'll tell you when you're old,\" she says, and goes pink.\n  else:\n    mood +small\n    say: \"Someone kind,\" she says. \"That's all I could see. Their hands were kind.\"\n> right: No, let it be a surprise\n  mood -tiny\n  rel seraphine +medium\n  say: She smiles like you've given her a present. \"That's the right answer. Nobody ever gives the right answer.\" Not knowing nags at you for days.\n\n@event seraphine_overwhelmed\ntitle: Too Many Tomorrows\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: seraphine\nwhen: rel(seraphine) >= 9 and year >= 2 and term\nweight: 10\nart: eye\ntags: social\nscene: A girl curled in a dark corner of an observatory with her hands over her eyes, a teacup gone cold beside her.\ntext: Seraphine hasn't come to class in a week. You find her in a dark storeroom with her hands over her eyes. \"It's too loud,\" she whispers. \"Everyone's futures. All at once.\"\n> left: Fetch Sister Amaranth\n  rel seraphine +small\n  say: Amaranth gives her a sleeping draught and a dark room. She recovers. She doesn't say much about it.\n> right: Sit with her in the dark\n  rel seraphine +large\n  spark seraphine +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You don't speak. After an hour she takes her hands from her eyes. \"Yours is quiet,\" she says. \"Your future. It's very quiet. I like it.\"\n\n@event seraphine_confession\nramp: spark(seraphine) 25..40\ntitle: She Already Knew\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: seraphine\nwhen: spark(seraphine) >= 25 and rel(seraphine) >= 18 and bond(seraphine) != \"partner\"\nweight: 30\nart: star\ntags: romance\nscene: A girl with silver eyes holding out a single falling-star seed in her palm on a dark observatory balcony.\ntext: \"I've seen this conversation forty-one times,\" Seraphine says. \"In twenty-nine of them, you say yes. I've been very nervous about the other twelve.\" She holds out her hand. \"I like you. That part was always the same.\"\n> left: Gently say no\n  spark seraphine -large\n  rel seraphine +tiny\n  say: She nods. \"Twelve,\" she says softly. \"I knew it might be twelve.\" She's kind to you afterwards. Somehow that hurts more.\n> right: Take her hand\n  bond seraphine partner\n  spark seraphine +large\n  mood +large\n  say: \"Twenty-nine,\" she whispers, and laughs, and for once she's entirely in the present.\n\n# ================= CORVIN ASHBY =================\n\n@event corvin_intro\ntitle: Light Fingers\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nactor: corvin\nwhen: not met(corvin) and turn >= 2\nweight: 30\nart: mask\ntags: social\nscene: A dark-haired student with a crooked grin holding up a purse between two fingers, mid-bow.\ntext: Someone bumps you in the crowd. A moment later a dark-haired boy is bowing and holding up your purse. \"You dropped this,\" he says. \"Into my pocket. Terrible habit of yours. Corvin Ashby.\"\n> left: Snatch it back and report him\n  meet corvin\n  rel corvin -small\n  say: He's gone before the prefect arrives. Your purse is all there, plus an IOU you don't remember receiving.\n> right: Laugh\n  gold -tiny\n  meet corvin\n  rel corvin +medium\n  guile +tiny\n  say: \"Oh, I like you,\" he says, and teaches you the trick on the spot. You practise it on him. He lets you win. Probably. Later, you are three crowns short. Probably a coincidence.\n\n@event corvin_kitchen_raid\ntime: night\ntitle: The Kitchen Raid\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: corvin\nwhen: rel(corvin) >= 3\nweight: 12\nart: meal\ntags: social\nscene: Two students creeping through a cavernous castle kitchen at night, a mountain of tarts on a table, a sleeping cook snoring by the hearth.\ntext: \"Midnight,\" Corvin whispers. \"The cooks have made forty treacle tarts for the faculty dinner. Forty. Nobody needs forty.\"\n> left: Not tonight\n  rel corvin -tiny\n  say: He goes alone. The next morning there's a treacle tart on your pillow. You decide not to ask.\n> right: Lead the raid\n  alignment -tiny\n  challenge deceive apprentice:\n    win:\n      mood +medium\n      rel corvin +medium\n      say: You get out with nine tarts and a bottle of elderflower cordial. You eat them on the roof until sunrise.\n    lose:\n      gain detention\n      rel corvin +medium\n      say: The cook wakes up. You both spend a week scrubbing pans, laughing too hard to work.\n\n@event corvin_debt\ntitle: Corvin's Little Problem\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: corvin\nwhen: rel(corvin) >= 12 and not flag(corvin_owes) and not flag(corvin_debt_refused)\nweight: 12\nart: chain\ntags: social money\nscene: A boy with a black eye sitting on the dormitory stairs, trying very hard to look casual.\ntext: Corvin has a black eye and a very casual expression. \"Small misunderstanding with some trolls,\" he says. \"Thirty crowns would clear it right up. I'll pay you back. Probably with interest. Possibly with a goat.\"\n> left: Refuse\n  flag corvin_debt_refused\n  rel corvin -medium\n  say: \"Course. No problem.\" He has another black eye the next week. He's still friendly. Mostly.\n> right: Give him thirty crowns\n  when: gold >= 30\n  gold -large\n  alignment +tiny\n  flag corvin_owes\n  rel corvin +large\n  queue corvin_repays in 4\n  say: He stares at the coins, then at you. \"No one's ever just... done that.\" He's quiet for a whole minute. A record.\n\n@event corvin_repays\ntitle: Interest\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: queued\nactor: corvin\nart: coin\ntags: social money\nscene: A small brass key and a fat purse left on a desk with a playing card, the jack of spades, tucked under them.\ntext: There's a fat purse on your desk and a jack of spades tucked under it. It's heavy, and it clinks.\n> left: Count it\n  chance likely:\n    gold +huge\n    rel corvin +medium\n    say: Sixty crowns. A note: \"Interest. Don't ask. — C.\" You don't ask.\n  else:\n    gold +tiny\n    rel corvin +small\n    say: Three crowns and a very convincing IOU for the other twenty-seven, with a doodle of a goat.\n> right: Find him and ask where it came from\n  rel corvin +large\n  gold +large\n  say: \"I won it,\" he says. \"Fairly. Mostly.\" He pushes the exact thirty into your hand. \"The rest's mine. I've got another debt to pay.\"\n\n@event corvin_rooftop\nslot: night\ntitle: Up on the Roof\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: corvin\nwhen: rel(corvin) >= 18\nweight: 10\nart: moon\ntags: social\nscene: Two students sitting on a steep slate roof under a full moon, legs dangling over a dizzying drop, sharing a bottle of something fizzy.\ntext: On the roof at 2am, Corvin goes quiet. \"I'm on my last warning,\" he says. \"One more detention and they send me home. And home is... not a good place.\" He laughs, badly.\n> left: Tell him to keep his head down\n  rel corvin +small\n  say: \"Head down. Right. I'll try anything once.\" He keeps his head down for almost a month.\n> right: Promise you'll cover for him\n  rel corvin +large\n  spark corvin +tiny\n  flag covering_corvin\n  say: He looks at you like nobody has ever promised him anything. \"You're an idiot,\" he says. \"Thank you.\"\n\n@event corvin_framed\ntitle: Corvin Is Framed\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: corvin\nwhen: rel(corvin) >= 9 and met(bram) and turn >= 10\nweight: 10\nart: scales\ntags: social rival\nscene: A boy standing before a disciplinary desk, a stolen silver goblet on it, a smug prefect at his shoulder.\ntext: The headmistress's silver goblet has been found in Corvin's trunk. He swears he didn't take it. Bram, who found it, looks very pleased. Corvin is one detention from expulsion.\n> left: Stay out of it\n  rel corvin -large\n  chance even:\n    say: Corvin talks his way out, barely. He doesn't look at you for a month.\n  else:\n    rel corvin -large\n    say: Corvin is suspended for a month. When he comes back, he's different. Quieter.\n> right: Find proof he was framed\n  challenge deceive vs bram.guile:\n    win:\n      rel corvin +large\n      rel bram -large\n      renown +small\n      flag bram_sash_lost\n      say: You find the bill of sale: Bram bought a fake. Bram is stripped of his sash for the rest of the year. Corvin buys you a goat. An actual goat.\n    lose:\n      rel corvin +medium\n      gain watched\n      say: You can't prove it. But you stood up for him in front of everyone, and he'll never forget it. Neither will Bram.\n\n@event corvin_confession\nramp: spark(corvin) 25..40\ntitle: Corvin, Serious for Once\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: corvin\nwhen: spark(corvin) >= 25 and rel(corvin) >= 18 and bond(corvin) != \"partner\"\nweight: 30\nart: cards\ntags: romance\nscene: A boy holding out a playing card — the queen of hearts — with an unsteady hand, no grin for once.\ntext: Corvin holds out a playing card. The queen of hearts. His hand isn't quite steady. \"No trick,\" he says. \"No angle. I just — I like you more than I like anything, and I'm terrified, and I wanted you to have this.\"\n> left: Gently decline\n  spark corvin -large\n  rel corvin +tiny\n  say: He laughs, too quickly. \"Worth a shot.\" He keeps the card. You see it sometimes, tucked in his sleeve.\n> right: Take the card\n  bond corvin partner\n  spark corvin +large\n  mood +large\n  say: For once, Corvin Ashby has nothing to say. He just kisses you, and then grins, and then can't stop grinning.\n\n@event corvin_runaway\nslot: night\ntitle: Let's Leave Tonight\nclass: story\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: corvin\nwhen: bond(corvin) == \"partner\" and final_year\nweight: 16\nart: map\ntags: romance danger\nscene: A small sky-barge tied to the academy dock at midnight, lanterns lit, a boy at the tiller holding out his hand.\ntext: Corvin's at your door at midnight, bag over his shoulder. \"There's a sky-barge at the dock and nobody guarding it. Let's go. Tonight. The world's huge and we're wasting it in classrooms.\"\n> left: Stay and finish what you started\n  rel corvin -small\n  spark corvin +tiny\n  say: He looks at you for a long time. Then he drops the bag. \"Fine. But after graduation, we steal a boat. A big one.\"\n> right: Take his hand\n  end ending_runaway\n\n# ================= MIR TEN =================\n\n@event mir_intro\ntitle: An Explosion in the West Wing\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nactor: mir\nwhen: not met(mir) and turn >= 2\nweight: 30\nart: gear\ntags: social\nscene: A cloud of purple smoke billowing from a workshop door, a soot-covered student emerging with goggles and a thumbs-up.\ntext: The west wing explodes. Not badly. A figure in goggles stumbles out of the purple smoke, coughing, and gives you a thumbs-up. \"Don't worry! That was intentional! Mostly! Can you hold this?\" They hand you a ticking teapot.\n> left: Put the teapot down and back away\n  meet mir\n  rel mir +tiny\n  say: The teapot stops ticking and starts humming the academy anthem. \"Mir,\" they say. \"They/them. Thanks for not throwing it.\"\n> right: Hold the teapot steady\n  meet mir\n  challenge feat apprentice- wild:\n    win:\n      rel mir +medium\n      say: You keep it level while they twist a valve. It stops ticking. \"You've got good hands,\" Mir says. \"I'm going to need those.\"\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      rel mir +medium\n      say: The teapot goes off. Your eyebrows survive, mostly. Mir is apologising and laughing at the same time.\n\n@event mir_owl_gift\ntitle: A Present With Gears\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: mir\nwhen: rel(mir) >= 18 and not has(familiar_owl)\nweight: 14\nart: owl\ntags: social magic\nscene: A small brass owl with glowing amber eyes perched on a student's wrist, its clockwork feathers fluffing.\ntext: Mir presents a brass owl with amber glass eyes. It blinks, hoots the hour and says \"HOMEWORK\" in a tinny voice. \"Made it for you. It remembers deadlines. And birthdays. And grudges, slightly.\"\n> left: It's too much\n  rel mir +small\n  say: Mir looks crushed, then rallies. \"I'll give it to Thorne. He needs reminding to smile.\" The owl, you hear, refuses to work for him.\n> right: Accept it gratefully\n  mood -tiny\n  gain familiar_owl\n  rel mir +medium\n  spark mir +tiny\n  say: The owl settles on your shoulder and says, \"WELCOME.\" Mir pretends to have something in their eye. At six the next morning, the owl says \"HOMEWORK.\" It says it every morning for the rest of the year.\n\n@event mir_fair\ntitle: The Artificers' Fair\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: mir\nwhen: rel(mir) >= 9 and month >= 6 and month <= 9\nweight: 12\nart: gear\ntags: social festival\nscene: A hall crammed with bizarre inventions — walking teapots, singing clocks — and one enormous brass machine draped in a sheet.\ntext: The Artificers' Fair. Mir's entry is under a sheet and occasionally growls. \"I need an assistant to stand next to it and look confident,\" they say. \"And possibly run.\"\n> left: Watch from a safe distance\n  mood +tiny\n  rel mir +tiny\n  say: The machine turns out to be a mechanical bear that bakes bread. It wins second place. Mir is ecstatic and flour-covered.\n> right: Be the confident assistant\n  challenge negotiate apprentice wild:\n    win:\n      renown +medium\n      rel mir +medium\n      say: You present it like a showman. The bear bakes a perfect loaf and bows. First prize. Mir kisses the trophy, then, in a fluster, your cheek.\n      spark mir +tiny\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      renown +small\n      rel mir +small\n      say: The bear eats your sleeve and swats you into the flour bins on its way to the judges. The crowd loves it. Third prize and a very good story.\n\n@event mir_workshop_closed\ntitle: The Workshop Is Shut\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: mir\nwhen: rel(mir) >= 12 and year >= 2\nweight: 10\nart: lock\ntags: social\nscene: A workshop door chained shut with a faculty notice nailed to it, a forlorn student sitting on a toolbox outside.\ntext: After the third fire this term, the faculty has padlocked Mir's workshop. Mir is sitting on a toolbox outside it, looking like someone took their heart and filed it under \"hazards\".\n> left: Plead their case to Professor Thorne\n  challenge negotiate master-:\n    win:\n      rel mir +large\n      rel thorne +tiny\n      say: Thorne agrees to reopen it — with fire buckets and a weekly inspection. Mir cries on you. Oil and all.\n    lose:\n      rel mir +small\n      rel thorne -tiny\n      flag mir_workshop_shut\n      say: Thorne refuses. But Mir heard you try. \"Nobody's ever argued for me before,\" they say.\n> right: Help them build a secret workshop\n  challenge deceive adept-:\n    win:\n      rel mir +large\n      guile +tiny\n      flag mir_workshop_shut\n      say: An abandoned classroom behind a tapestry. You bring candles; Mir brings everything else. It's perfect. It's almost entirely fireproof.\n    lose:\n      rel mir +medium\n      gain detention\n      flag mir_workshop_shut\n      say: Bram finds the secret workshop on day two. Worth it, says Mir, scrubbing cauldrons beside you.\n\n@event mir_dance_machine\ntitle: The Dancing Machine\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: mir\nwhen: rel(mir) >= 6\nweight: 10\nart: music\ntags: social\nscene: A spindly brass contraption with too many legs attempting a waltz in an empty classroom, a student dancing with it.\ntext: Mir has built a machine that teaches dancing. It has six legs and opinions. \"It needs a test subject,\" they say. \"It's mostly safe. It only kicked me twice.\"\n> left: Make Mir test it\n  rel mir -tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: You watch Mir and the machine waltz, badly, across the room. It kicks them twice; Mir gives you a look. It's the funniest thing you've ever seen.\n> right: Dance with the machine\n  charm +small\n  health -tiny\n  rel mir +medium\n  say: It kicks you twice too. But by the end you can waltz, and Mir is clapping with oily hands.\n\n@event mir_confession\nramp: spark(mir) 25..40\ntitle: Blueprint\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: mir\nwhen: spark(mir) >= 25 and rel(mir) >= 18 and bond(mir) != \"partner\"\nweight: 30\nart: gear\ntags: romance\nscene: A rolled-out blueprint on a workbench showing a heart-shaped mechanism labelled with tiny careful notes, a nervous artificer beside it.\ntext: Mir unrolls a blueprint. It's a mechanical heart, labelled in tiny careful handwriting: \"Does not work without the other part.\" They clear their throat. \"I'm bad at saying things. So I drew it. You're the other part. If you want.\"\n> left: Kindly say no\n  spark mir -large\n  rel mir +small\n  say: Mir rolls up the blueprint carefully. \"Good to know. Data is data.\" They're fine. They're mostly fine.\n> right: \"I want.\"\n  bond mir partner\n  spark mir +large\n  mood +large\n  say: The heart doesn't need to work. But Mir builds it anyway, and it ticks on your windowsill, in time with your own.\n\n@event mir_family_business\ntitle: The Family Clockworks\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: mir\nwhen: rel(mir) >= 15 and year >= 2 and not flag(mir_family_done)\nweight: 10\nart: gear\ntags: social\nscene: An artificer holding a stiff letter embossed with a gear crest, a train ticket tucked inside, staring at it blankly.\ntext: A letter from Mir's parents: the family clockworks needs them home. A ticket is enclosed. \"They think this is a hobby,\" Mir says. \"This. Is. Not. A. Hobby.\"\n> left: Tell them family comes first\n  flag mir_family_done\n  rel mir +small\n  mood +tiny\n  say: Mir goes home for a month and comes back with a new scar and a clearer jaw. \"They know now,\" they say. \"It's not a hobby. You were right to send me.\"\n> right: Help them write back\n  flag mir_family_done mir_family_wrote\n  rel mir +large\n  charm +tiny\n  study -small\n  say: Together you spend three evenings, and most of your coursework, on a letter so persuasive it should be framed. Mir's mother replies: \"Fine. Win a prize.\" Mir intends to win all of them.\n\n# ================= NESSA QUILL =================\n\n@event nessa_intro\ntitle: First Impressions\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nactor: nessa\nwhen: not met(nessa) and turn >= 2\nweight: 30\nart: paper\ntags: social\nscene: A girl with ink-stained fingers and a self-writing quill floating at her shoulder, holding a notebook out expectantly.\ntext: A girl with a quill floating at her shoulder plants herself in front of you. \"Nessa Quill, Lumen Ledger. First impressions of Lumenhold, go. Be interesting, please. The last six were boring.\"\n> left: Give a safe answer\n  meet nessa\n  rel nessa +tiny\n  say: The quill writes \"SAFE\" in the margin and underlines it twice. Nessa sighs and moves on.\n> right: Say something outrageous\n  meet nessa\n  challenge negotiate apprentice wild:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      rel nessa +medium\n      say: You're quoted on the front page. Half the academy now thinks you're hilarious. The other half thinks you're dangerous. Nessa thinks both.\n    lose:\n      renown +small\n      rel nessa +tiny\n      mood -tiny\n      say: You are quoted. Out of context. Extremely out of context.\n\n@event nessa_gossip_column\ntitle: You're in the Gossip Column\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: nessa\nwhen: renown >= 15\nweight: 12\nart: paper\ntags: social\nscene: A copy of the Lumen Ledger pinned to a noticeboard, a gossip column circled in red ink, students gathered round giggling.\ntext: This week's Ledger gossip column features you, prominently, under the headline \"WHO IS THE MYSTERY STUDENT ALWAYS IN THE LIBRARY AT MIDNIGHT?\" Nessa swears she didn't write it.\n> left: Confront Nessa\n  rel nessa -tiny\n  challenge negotiate vs nessa.charm:\n    win:\n      say: She prints a correction. Sort of. It's a much more flattering paragraph about you.\n      renown +small\n    lose:\n      say: \"Freedom of the press,\" says Nessa, sweetly. You both know she wrote it.\n> right: Lean into the mystery\n  chance likely:\n    renown +small\n    mood +small\n    rel nessa +small\n    say: You start leaving cryptic notes in the library. The column runs for six more weeks. Nessa is delighted.\n  else:\n    renown +tiny\n    mood -small\n    say: The column runs for six more weeks. By the fourth it has decided you are haunting the library, and people move tables when you sit down.\n\n@event nessa_source\ntitle: The Source\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: nessa\nwhen: rel(nessa) >= 12 and (member(library_assistant) or rel(thorne) >= 9)\nweight: 10\nart: feather\ntags: social\nscene: Two students in a dark corner of the library, one whispering, the other writing furiously in a notebook.\ntext: Nessa leans in. \"You've got faculty access. The professors are planning to change the exam format and they're hiding it. I just need a quote. Anonymous. Please?\"\n> left: Refuse — that's a breach of trust\n  rel nessa -small\n  mood +tiny\n  say: Nessa sulks for a day. Then she respects you for it, which she hates. You sleep well.\n> right: Give her the scoop\n  rel nessa +large\n  chance unlikely:\n    rel thorne -large\n    say: The story runs. Thorne knows exactly who talked. His glare could curdle milk.\n  else:\n    say: The story runs. Students are grateful. Nobody knows it was you. Nessa buys you cake.\n\n@event nessa_hollow_story\ntitle: Nessa Smells a Story\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: social\nactor: nessa\nwhen: clues(hollowstar) >= 1 and rel(nessa) >= 6\nweight: 12\nart: paper\ntags: social mystery hollowstar\nscene: A wall in the newspaper office covered in pinned clippings and red string connecting a frozen lake, a black star and a fur-coated boy.\ntext: Nessa's office wall is covered in red string. In the middle: a sketch of a black star. \"Frozen fountains. Missing pages. A transfer from the North. Something's going on and you know about it. Spill.\"\n> left: Warn her off — it's dangerous\n  rel nessa -tiny\n  say: \"That's what people say right before the best stories.\" But she takes the string down. Mostly.\n> right: Share what you know\n  rel nessa +medium\n  chance unlikely:\n    gain watched\n    say: Nessa's article runs with half the facts. The man in grey reads it. So does Bram, who starts following you around.\n  else:\n    say: Nessa's notes are scarily thorough. She's found a record of three students who vanished in 1723. All on Blossom moons.\n\n@event nessa_paper_threatened\ntitle: Stop the Presses\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: nessa\nwhen: rel(nessa) >= 9 and year >= 2\nweight: 10\nart: paper\ntags: social\nscene: A printing press with a faculty seal stamped across it, a defiant student standing in front of it with arms crossed.\ntext: The headmistress has threatened to shut down the Ledger after an article about faculty salaries. Nessa is organising a protest. \"I need people on the steps. Visible people. You.\"\n> left: Stay out of it\n  rel nessa -small\n  say: The protest is small. The Ledger survives, barely. Nessa notices who wasn't there.\n> right: Stand on the steps\n  rel nessa +large\n  rel isolde -small\n  renown +medium\n  say: Forty students on the steps, chanting. The headmistress comes out and, astonishingly, listens, looking straight at you the whole time, and not warmly. The Ledger stays.\n\n@event nessa_confession\nramp: spark(nessa) 25..40\ntitle: Off the Record\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: nessa\nwhen: spark(nessa) >= 25 and rel(nessa) >= 18 and bond(nessa) != \"partner\"\nweight: 30\nart: feather\ntags: romance\nscene: A girl with an inkpot in one hand, her self-writing quill hovering above a blank page that she has commanded to stay blank.\ntext: Nessa puts down her quill. Then she tells it to stop writing. It's the first time you've seen it still. \"This is off the record,\" she says. \"Completely. I like you. I've drafted this eleven times. None of the drafts were good enough.\"\n> left: Gently say no\n  spark nessa -large\n  rel nessa +tiny\n  say: \"Noted,\" she says crisply. She keeps her quill still a moment longer. Then it starts writing again, and so does she.\n> right: Kiss her\n  bond nessa partner\n  spark nessa +large\n  mood +large\n  say: Her quill, unattended, writes a single word in the margin: \"FINALLY.\"\n"},{"path":"content/events/pools/castle_halls.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Baseline pools (phase 3, step 1b): castle_halls.\n#  Repeatable routine events for map visits to the castle's\n#  public halls: courtyard, library, refectory, lecture tower,\n#  great hall, infirmary, alchemy laboratory and job board.\n#  Spec: docs/authoring/pools.md.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- The Courtyard ----------------\n\n@event courtyard_sundial_bench\ntime: afternoon\ntitle: The Slow Sundial\nplace: courtyard\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: sun\ntags: study\nscene: A stone bench beside a weathered sundial in a flagstone courtyard, a student reading with their back to the dial.\ntext: {There's a bench by the old sundial, which runs eleven minutes slow.|Someone has scratched a tiny \"+11\" by the dial's noon. A fellow sufferer.|The sundial bench is yours by custom. A first-year asked if you came with it.} {season == \"winter\": Frost fills the hour lines, and the shadow skates across them.|{Pigeons are holding a meeting on the gnomon.|A first-year checks a watch against it, horrified.|Someone has chalked a face where noon should be.}}\n> left: Read until the shadow moves\n  lore +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {season == \"winter\": {Your fingers go numb by page two. The chapter is worth it.|You read in gloves, turning pages with your teeth.|You read in your own bench's frost-free corner. A first-year brings you a hot chestnut, as a tribute to the bench.}|{You finish a chapter in the minutes the sundial owes you, squinting into the glare.|A pigeon reads over your shoulder, unimpressed, and your neck has a crick in it by the end.|The shadow crawls; you outpace it by three pages and a stiff back.}}\n> right: Close your eyes a while\n  mood +small\n  renown -tiny\n  say: {You wake with a chalk moustache and no suspects.|You wake to a prefect clearing his throat an inch from your ear.|You wake rested and late, eleven minutes late by the dial.}\n\n@event courtyard_saved_seat\ntime: afternoon\ntitle: A Seat on the Rim\nplace: courtyard\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: group\ntags: social\ncast:\n  here: present(place) where tagged(here, peer) and rel(here) >= 0\nscene: Two students sitting on the dry edge of a courtyard fountain, bags between them, pigeons circling overhead.\ntext: {{here} has claimed the dry stretch of the fountain rim and waves you over.|{here} is on your favourite stretch of the fountain rim, the one the sun reaches first, and waves you over.|Your stretch of the fountain rim is known now. {here} is sitting on it and shifts over without being asked.} {Two towers' bells are arguing about the hour.|Pigeons circle like creditors.|{season == \"winter\": Your breath hangs between you.|Someone's lunch smells better than yours.}}\n> left: Sit down and catch up\n  social here\n  if npc_is(corvin):\n    say: {Corvin tells you three stories, one of them true, and calls you \"saint\" when you don't ask which.|Corvin spots a prefect, palms something off the rim, and goes on talking as if nothing happened.|Corvin shifts over and hands you half of something he shouldn't have. \"Your seat, saint. I kept it warm. Illegally.\"}\n  elif npc_is(nessa):\n    say: {Nessa interviews you about the weather, the soup and your love life, in that order, and writes down all three.|Nessa reads you tomorrow's headline and asks if it's too cruel. It is. She keeps it.|Nessa shifts over without looking up from her notes. \"You're in tomorrow's column,\" she says. \"Nicely, for once.\"}\n  elif npc_is(bram):\n    say: {Bram lists every rule broken in the courtyard while you sit. Then, gruffly, he asks how your exams went.|Bram writes a demerit for a passing first-year, then tears it up when he thinks you aren't looking.|Bram shifts over with great dignity and informs you the seat is technically unassigned. Then he guards it while you fetch a cider.}\n  else:\n    say: {You trade the castle's news for an hour. {here} does most of the trading.|You talk until the shadows cross the fountain. Neither of you can remember about what.|You sit on your stretch of the rim and catch up. {here} already knows half your news and makes you tell it anyway.}\n  say: The cider from the cart by the gate is on you.\n> right: Wave, and keep walking\n  lore +small\n  rel here -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You promise next time and take your book to the library instead. {here} looks as if {they} {are} counting.|You spend the hour with a book instead. It is a good book; it is not much company.|You wave and keep walking. {here} stays on your stretch of the rim, which feels like a small theft.}\n\n@event courtyard_seasonal_game\ntitle: The Courtyard Game\nplace: courtyard\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: group\ntags: social\nscene: Students playing a rowdy game across a flagstone courtyard while others crowd the edges shouting bets.\ntext: {season == \"winter\": A snowball war between Emberhall and Stillwater has reached the treaty stage.|{season == \"spring\": A charmed ball is loose, dodging everyone who kicks at it.|{season == \"summer\": It's too hot for sport, so it's catch with a charmed apple that bites.|It's conker season. The champion conker is said to be older than the fountain.}}} {Someone shouts for one more player.|They shout for you by name.|Both sides shout for you, and start bidding.}\n> left: Join in\n  challenge duel using vigor apprentice wild:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      mood +small\n      health -tiny\n      say: {Your side wins, and the result is disputed until supper.|You score the deciding point and are carried three whole steps before they drop you.|You join the side that bid more. They win, and the other side says you were bought, which you were.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood +tiny\n      say: {You are flattened, politely, by both sides at once.|You spend the rest of the game as the thing the other side aims at.|Both sides think you're on theirs. Both sides find out, at once, in the middle of the courtyard.}\n> right: Hold the coats and the bets\n  chance even:\n    gold +small\n    say: {The favourite loses. You are the only person in the courtyard who is pleased about it.|The underdog wins by a whisker, and the coats you are holding are suddenly lighter on your conscience.|You hold the coats and both sides' bids. The underdog wins. You are, briefly, the richest person in the courtyard.}\n  else:\n    if gold > 0:\n      gold -small\n      say: {The favourite wins, and so does everyone who backed them, out of your pocket.|Everyone bet the same way, and everyone was right. You pay out in a hail of small coins.|The favourite wins, and both sides had bet on it through you. You pay out twice, which shouldn't be possible.}\n    else:\n      mood -small\n      say: The favourite wins. With nothing in your pocket to pay out, you're chased twice round the fountain and lose a coat button to the crowd.\n\n# ---------------- The Library ----------------\n\n@event library_wandering_book\ntitle: The Book That Moved\nplace: library\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: book\ntags: study\nscene: Tall library shelves leaning very slightly, a gap where one book should be, a student on a ladder peering into it.\ntext: {Yesterday your book was on the third floor. Today the third floor has never heard of it.|Your book has moved again, leaving a bookmark in the gap, like a note.|You keep a map of where the shelves hide your books. They've started hiding the map.} {In its place is a shelf of cookery spells.|In its place are bound weather reports.|In its place is one slim volume, face out, waiting.} {final_year: Soon they'll play these games with someone else.|}\n> left: Hunt it down\n  lore +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {You find it on the sixth floor, filed under \"regret\".|You find it in the stairwell, halfway between floors, as if it had been trying to leave.|You find it at once. Either you are learning the shelves, or they are getting lazy.}\n> right: Read what's in front of you\n  mood +small\n  say: {You read the wrong book with great conviction, and quote it at supper.|You learn a great deal about something nobody will ever ask you about.|The wrong book turns out to be a very good novel. You read all of it, and none of it will be on any exam.}\n\n@event library_shared_table\ntitle: The Last Free Chair\nplace: library\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: candle\ntags: social study\ncast:\n  here: present(place) where tagged(here, peer) and rel(here) >= 0\nscene: A crowded library reading room, every table full but one, a single empty chair facing a student absorbed in a book.\ntext: {The library is full. The only free chair is opposite {here}.|The only free chair is opposite {here}. A shelf has leaned over it, as if keeping it for you.|The library knows your habits now. The free chair, opposite {here}, has a book you wanted waiting on it.|The chair opposite {here} is so reliably free that you suspect the library of arranging it.} {Rain ticks on the tall windows.|A shelf nearby is quietly rearranging itself.|Footnote is asleep in the returns basket.|{season == \"winter\": The radiators clank like a ghost with a grudge.|Dust turns gold in the light.}}\n> left: Take the chair and work\n  rel here +small\n  lore +small\n  if npc_is(kael):\n    say: {Kael doesn't look up. By the end of the hour there is frost on your inkwell and a tiny, careful correction in your margin.|Kael pushes a page of his notes across to you, then looks away as if it had moved on its own.|Kael moves his inkwell to make room for yours. By the end, there's frost on both, and a note in his hand: \"Your third line.\"|Kael says one sentence in the whole hour. It's the answer to the question you were stuck on. He goes back to reading.}\n  elif npc_is(lyra):\n    say: {Lyra slides the candle an inch toward your side without looking up. Neither of you mentions it.|Lyra finishes before you, and waits, and says she wasn't waiting.|Lyra corrects your notation without asking and is right. You correct hers and are also right. Neither of you mentions it.|Lyra works at your pace for once, and you finish together. She says it was coincidence, twice.}\n  elif npc_is(seraphine):\n    say: {Seraphine writes a word in your notes that you won't understand until next week.|Seraphine reads the same page for an hour, smiling at something that isn't on it.|Seraphine slides you a book you hadn't asked for. You'll need it on Thursday, it turns out.|Seraphine works in silence, then says your name once, softly, as if checking it was still there.}\n  elif npc_is(bram):\n    say: {Bram informs you that you breathe too loudly. Then he lends you his ruler.|Bram underlines every rule in his book twice. Once, he asks your opinion of one.|Bram shares his lamp and lectures you on its proper use. The lecture is long. The lamp is very good.|Bram reads your essay upside down from across the table and finds the mistake. He's insufferable about it, and right.}\n  else:\n    say: {You work in silence for an hour, which turns out to be a kind of company.|Neither of you speaks. Both of you get further than you would have alone.|You work opposite {here} as if you'd planned to. By the end you've both got further, and nodded once.|You share the table for the hour and the cat for the last ten minutes. It's a good arrangement.}\n> right: Whisper a question\n  rel here +small\n  mood +small\n  renown -tiny\n  say: {The conversation is worth it. The look from the front desk has a long memory.|You get three sentences in before a shush arrives from two floors up.|You whisper your question to {here} and get a whispered answer. The front desk lets it pass, this once.|Your whisper wakes the cat in the returns basket, who complains loudly. You are shushed for the cat's noise.}\n\n@event library_overdue\ntitle: Overdue\nplace: library\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: book\ntags: study money\nscene: A student holding a book behind their back near a wicker returns basket, a cat on the front desk watching them.\ntext: {The book you borrowed in a hurry is overdue, and the library knows it. The spine has started to sigh.|Another overdue book. The front desk has begun keeping a little card with your name on it.|Your overdue books sigh in chorus now, and the card at the front desk has a second side.} {Footnote watches you approach the returns basket with professional interest.|The front desk is, for once, unattended.|A queue of first-years is paying fines in small, sad coins.}\n> left: Own up and pay the fine\n  alignment +tiny\n  lore +small\n  if gold > 0:\n    gold -small\n    say: {The fine is paid; the desk, mollified, lends you the sequel.|You pay, and are lent another book, which you suspect is a trap.|The desk sighs, takes your coins and hands you a book it says you'll like. It is always right, which is the annoying part.}\n  else:\n    mood -small\n    say: You own up with empty pockets. The desk lets you work the fine off reshelving, which takes all evening, and lends you the sequel anyway.\n> right: Slip it into the basket\n  alignment -tiny\n  challenge deceive apprentice:\n    win:\n      guile +small\n      mood +tiny\n      say: {The book goes in without a sound. It stops sighing at once, which feels like forgiveness.|You drop it in under cover of a sneeze. Footnote saw. Footnote will keep it to himself, for a price.|You slip it in while the first-years pay their fines. Nobody notices one more sad sound.}\n    lose:\n      renown -tiny\n      mood -tiny\n      if gold > 0:\n        gold -medium\n        say: {The book announces itself with a sigh like a dying organ. Everyone turns. You pay double.|It lands with a groan. The front desk adds a mark to your card, and the fine, doubled.|The book sighs in harmony with your other overdue books. The desk adds a third side to your card.}\n      else:\n        say: The book announces itself with a sigh like a dying organ. Everyone turns. With nothing to pay, you're put on reshelving until the fine is worked off twice over.\n\n# ---------------- The Refectory ----------------\n\n@event refectory_late_supper\nslot: evening\ntitle: Late Supper\nplace: refectory\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: meal\ntags: home\nscene: The far end of a long refectory table at night, most benches empty, a single student with a bowl and a half-written letter.\ntext: {You come in late. The benches are empty and the cooks are wiping the far tables.|The cook leaves a lamp lit at the end of the bench now, where the draught can't find you.|Your late bowl is waiting under a plate, with a spoon laid beside it. Nobody admits to it.} {year == 1: Home feels very far away tonight.|{final_year: You count the suppers you have left, and stop.|{The candles are down to stubs.|Someone has left a half-done puzzle on the next table.|The cook is humming in the kitchen behind the hatch.}}}\n> left: Write a letter home\n  mood +small\n  gold -tiny\n  say: {You write two pages and post them with the last of your coppers.|You write about the food. You do not write about anything else, but they'll know.|You write a letter that is mostly questions. You post it before you can make it braver.}\n> right: Revise over the bowl\n  lore +small\n  say: {You learn three dates and a spill of gravy across all of them.|The cook reads over your shoulder and corrects one of your answers. She is right.|You revise over your bowl. The cook takes the plate away when you've finished and leaves a second helping.}\n\n@event refectory_house_tables\ntime: evening\ntitle: Where to Sit\nplace: refectory\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: group\ntags: social\nscene: A crowded refectory at suppertime, a student standing with a tray between a loud house table and a quieter one of strangers.\ntext: {Supper, and the question of where to sit.|Your house table has started saving a gap on the bench, roughly your width.|The gap is a named seat now, and the quiet table nods at you too.} Your house table is {loud with a story about a goat|in the middle of a pudding treaty|singing, badly}. At the far end, {season == \"winter\": some second-years from Rootward huddle round a warming charm.|some second-years from Duskgate are arguing quietly about a map.}\n> left: Squeeze in with your house\n  mood +small\n  renown +tiny\n  say: {You eat with an elbow in your ribs and laugh the whole way through.|Somebody's story gets better every time you hear it. So does the pudding.|You squeeze into your gap and join the singing, badly, to fit in. It works.}\n> right: Sit with the strangers\n  charm +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {They are polite, then curious, then actually rather good company.|It is awkward for exactly one course. By pudding, they have told you their secret map route.|The quiet table makes room as if you'd always sat there. They ask about your house's singing, with sympathy.}\n\n@event refectory_pudding_tax\ntime: evening\ntitle: The Pudding Tax\nplace: refectory\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: cup\ntags: social\ncast:\n  friend: any where rel(friend) >= 6 and (tagged(friend, student) or tagged(friend, prefect)) and around(friend)\nscene: Two friends at a refectory table, one guarding a bowl of treacle pudding, the other holding a spoon at the ready.\ntext: {{friend} sits down opposite you and looks at your pudding the way a cat looks at a fish.|Your pudding has a reputation now. {friend} sits down opposite you with a spoon already in hand.|Word of your pudding tax has got round the refectory. Tonight {friend} comes to collect, with a little receipt.} {season == \"winter\": Tonight it's treacle sponge, steaming.|{Tonight it's apple crumble.|Tonight it's a custard that wobbles with intent.|Tonight it's jam roly-poly, much defended.}}\n> left: Pay the pudding tax\n  rel friend +small\n  mood -small\n  if npc_is(tobias):\n    say: {Tobias apologises four times, eats it all, then tells you a long story about a goat who did the same. It helps, somehow.|Tobias eats half, then pushes the bowl back with a sprig of mint from his pocket on top, as an apology.|Tobias pays you back with a receipt of his own: half a jar of honey from home. You call it even.}\n  elif npc_is(lyra):\n    say: {\"Efficient,\" Lyra says, finishing it. The next day a whole pudding appears on your tray. She doesn't mention it.|Lyra eats it in four exact bites and critiques the custard. She does not give it back.|Lyra presents the receipt, takes exactly the tax and not a crumb more. She is, you have to admit, scrupulous.}\n  elif npc_is(mir):\n    say: {Mir eats it while explaining a pudding-defence device that would probably work. Probably.|Mir eats it with a spoon they built themselves, which hums. They say the humming is probably normal.|Mir hands you a receipt they've engraved on brass, collects, and promises a refund mechanism by spring.}\n  else:\n    say: {{friend} eats exactly half and leaves you the burnt corner.|{friend} eats it slowly, savouring your defeat.|{friend} collects the tax and writes \"paid\" on the receipt. You're starting to suspect the receipts are the point.}\n> right: Defend your pudding\n  challenge deceive vs friend.guile:\n    win:\n      mood +small\n      rel friend -tiny\n      say: {You hide it under a napkin and point at the window. It works. {friend} will remember this.|You claim it's been sneezed on. {friend} retreats in disgust, and you eat in peace.|You refuse to recognise the receipt. {friend} appeals to the table, which rules in your favour, narrowly.}\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      rel friend +tiny\n      say: {{friend} sees straight through you and takes the whole thing as a penalty. It is hard to stay cross.|{friend} distracts you with a story and the bowl is empty before it ends.|You dispute the receipt. {friend} produces a second receipt for the dispute, and takes the pudding as costs.}\n\n# ---------------- The Lecture Tower ----------------\n\n@event lecture_tower_old_board\ntitle: Someone Else's Lecture\nplace: lecture_tower\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: scroll\ntags: study\nscene: An empty spiral lecture hall, a blackboard still dense with chalk diagrams from a class that has long gone.\ntext: {term: {The fourth-floor hall is empty, but someone else's lecture is still on the board.|In the empty hall, a stub of chalk waits on the ledge, as if for you.|The board has a corner fenced off in chalk, labelled \"answers here\".}|In the break the boards are wiped, except one nobody dares touch.} {Today it's a ward diagram, labelled \"wrong, but how?\"|Today it's half a proof, ending in a thumbprint.|Today it's a list headed \"on the exam\", crossed out.}\n> left: Copy it all down\n  arcana +small\n  say: {Your hand cramps. Your head, for once, does not.|You copy it all and understand most of it. The rest you'll grow into.|You copy the board, fenced corner and all. Your answer from last time is still there, with a tick beside it.}\n> right: Add your own answer\n  chance likely:\n    renown +small\n    say: {Next time, someone has added \"yes!\" under your line.|Someone has answered your answer. The board has become a correspondence.|By the afternoon someone has drawn a box around your line, to keep it safe.}\n  else:\n    renown -tiny\n    mood -tiny\n    say: {The professor whose board it was finds you holding the chalk. \"Hm,\" says the look.|Your answer is wiped by the time you reach the door, by someone who knew better.|You write in the \"answers here\" corner. The next morning there's a line through it, and \"nearly\", underlined.}\n\n@event lecture_tower_stair_question\ntitle: One Question a Flight\nplace: lecture_tower\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: tower\ntags: study\ncast:\n  prof: thorne\nscene: A narrow spiral stair inside a stone tower, a severe professor climbing ahead with a stack of papers, a student a step behind.\ntext: {You meet Professor Thorne on the spiral stair. He allows one question per flight, and climbs fast.|Professor Thorne on the stair. He has slowed down by about one step in seven, which is how you know he expects you.|Thorne hands you half his stack of papers at the foot of the stair, without a word, and starts climbing.} {term: |The tower is half empty for the break; your footsteps echo twice.}{final_year: There are not many of these climbs left.|}\n> left: Ask a hard question\n  challenge research using arcana adept-:\n    win:\n      arcana +small\n      rel prof +tiny\n      say: {He answers in four words, and the four words are enough.|\"Hm,\" he says, then answers it properly, all the way to the seventh floor.|You carry half his papers and ask your question on the fourth flight. He answers on the seventh, and it was worth the wait.}\n    lose:\n      arcana +tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: {He answers with a harder question and leaves you on the landing.|\"Wrong question,\" he says. \"Find the right one by Thursday.\"|He takes his papers back on the landing. \"Better question next time,\" he says, which means he'll be expecting one.}\n> right: Climb in silence beside him\n  rel prof +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {He climbs faster than you. At the top he says, \"Adequate,\" and you are fairly sure he means the climbing.|Neither of you says a word for seven flights. It is oddly restful, apart from your legs.|You carry his papers in silence. At the top he takes them back and says, \"Thursday,\" which is an invitation.}\n\n@event lecture_tower_scorched_room\ntitle: The Scorched Room\nplace: lecture_tower\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: flame\ntags: magic danger\nscene: A practice room on a lower floor of a stone tower, walls streaked with scorch marks, a sign by the door forbidding unsupervised casting.\ntext: {The practice room on the Evocation floor is empty. A sign forbids unsupervised casting.|The scorched room is empty. Since your last visit, someone has underlined the sign.|The scorched room. The sign is underlined three times now, and signed by the headmistress.} {A fresh scorch mark is shaped like a surprised face.|Someone has left a bucket of sand by the door.|{season == \"winter\": The walls are the only warm thing here.|The air smells faintly of singed eyebrow.}}\n> left: Practise anyway\n  mana -small\n  chance likely:\n    arcana +small\n    say: {Your spell lands clean in the centre of the oldest scorch mark. You leave no evidence.|You practise until the flame does what you mean, not what you say.|You practise under the headmistress's signature. Your spell is very good. You leave before it's tempted to be better.}\n  else:\n    health -small\n    renown -tiny\n    say: {A new scorch mark joins the wall, shaped very like you.|The spell comes back at you. You leave with your eyebrows, mostly.|Your scorch mark lands right under the headmistress's signature. You'd swear the signature looks disappointed.}\n> right: Work through the theory\n  lore +small\n  say: {You draw the spell on paper fifty times. Paper is very forgiving.|You learn exactly why the walls look like that, and resolve not to add to them.|You work the theory under the thrice-underlined sign. You'll cast it properly, supervised, and it will be perfect.}\n\n# ---------------- The Great Hall ----------------\n\n@event great_hall_tomorrow_sky\ntitle: Tomorrow's Weather\nplace: great_hall\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: candle\ntags: magic\nscene: An empty great hall in the evening, floating candles drifting under a vaulted ceiling that shows a different sky from the windows.\ntext: {The great hall is empty but for the candles. The ceiling shows tomorrow.|One candle drifts over to you and hovers at reading height.|Your candle meets you at the door now and leads you to your bench.} {season == \"winter\": Tomorrow, it says, it will snow.|{season == \"spring\": Tomorrow, a storm with bad manners.|{season == \"summer\": Tomorrow is cloudless, stars and all.|Tomorrow is fog, rolling across the vaults.}}} {final_year: There are fewer tomorrows here now.|}\n> left: Lie on a bench and watch\n  mood +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You watch tomorrow until your neck stiffens. It is worth it.|A candle drifts down to see if you are all right, and stays.|You fall half asleep and dream tomorrow a little early.}\n> right: Read by the floating candles\n  lore +small\n  say: {A candle drips wax onto the best paragraph. You read it anyway, through the wax.|The candles follow your page like curious moths. It is the best light in the castle.|Your candle sits on the bench beside you like a friend. The light is steady, and you read further than you meant to.}\n\n@event great_hall_choir_rehearsal\ntime: evening\ntitle: The Choir Rehearses\nplace: great_hall\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: music\ntags: social\nscene: A choir rehearsing on the stage of a candlelit great hall, a few singers floating a foot off the boards, the benches mostly empty.\ntext: {The choir is rehearsing on the stage, a foot off the floor, and one voice short.|Still a voice short. The conductor has left a spare songbook on your bench.|The spare songbook has your name pencilled in it, and the alto line marked in red.} {season == \"winter\": They are learning the Midwinter music.|{Tonight it's a round that nobody can find the end of.|Tonight it's a lament, sung far too cheerfully.|Tonight it's a song for the lake, sung very softly.}}\n> left: Listen from the back\n  mood +small\n  gold -tiny\n  say: {The collection hat comes round. It is worth every coin.|You listen until the candles gutter. The hat comes round twice.|You listen from the back with your name pencilled into a book you're not singing from. It's the best of both.}\n> right: Fill the missing voice\n  challenge negotiate apprentice:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      charm +tiny\n      say: {You hit every note but one. The conductor says it was the best one.|You float a little off the floor on the last chord. Nobody mentions it, which is how you know.|You sing your marked alto line and the choir is whole. The conductor doesn't say anything, and doesn't need to.}\n    lose:\n      renown -tiny\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {You come in confidently, in the wrong key, in the wrong song. The choir is very kind about it, which is worse.|You sing the round in a straight line and finish alone. The conductor thanks you for your enthusiasm.|You sing the red line, which is the soprano line, it turns out. The choir carries you, out of kindness.}\n\n@event great_hall_high_table_dare\ntitle: The High Table Dare\nplace: great_hall\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: crown\ntags: social danger\nscene: An empty high table on a dais at the end of a dark great hall, a carved chair at its centre, students daring each other from the doorway.\ntext: {Someone dares you to sit in the headmistress's chair at the empty high table.|The dare comes round again, and someone has started keeping a tally in chalk on the doorframe.|The tally on the doorframe has your column and everyone else's. First-years dare each other to dare you.} {The chair is still warm.|A cushion embroidered with an owl sits on it.|Her teacup has been left behind, still steaming, which is worse.}\n> left: Take the dare\n  challenge deceive apprentice wild:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      mood +small\n      say: {You sit, cross your legs, and sip the air. The doorway erupts.|You sit for a full minute. The chair is surprisingly comfortable, and very slightly warm.|You take the dare, and lift the steaming teacup, and drink. Nobody in the doorway breathes. It's very good tea.}\n    lose:\n      renown -small\n      say: {As you sit, a quiet, delighted laugh comes from the dark beyond the dais. You leave very fast.|A portrait above the table clears its throat. You have never moved so fast in your life.|You reach for the steaming teacup and a voice behind you says, \"That's mine.\" You don't look round. You simply leave.}\n> right: Tell them you value your life\n  renown -tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {They call you a coward, fondly. You keep your dignity, and your place at the academy.|\"Coward,\" they chant. You bow. It seems the safest thing to do in that hall.|You decline, and point out the steaming cup. The first-years go very quiet, and so does the dare.}\n\n# ---------------- The Infirmary ----------------\n\n@event infirmary_spare_bed\ntitle: The Spare Bed\nplace: infirmary\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: bed\ntags: home\nscene: A row of white infirmary beds under a high arched window, one turned down, a stern healer pointing at it.\ntext: {You only came in for a plaster.|Your name is on the slate by the end bed, in pencil.|Your name on the slate is in ink now.} {health < 40: Sister Amaranth takes one look at you and points at the spare bed.|Sister Amaranth decides, over her spectacles, that you'll live.} {It smells of lavender and singed wool.|Two beds down, someone hiccups in a minor key.|{season == \"winter\": A warming charm hums under every blanket.|The curtains breathe.}}\n> left: Lie down for an hour\n  mood +small\n  health +small\n  gold -tiny\n  say: {You sleep the deep, dreamless sleep of the infirmary. The tonic afterwards costs a few crowns and tastes of regret.|You wake under a blanket you didn't pull up. There is a tonic on the side, and a bill under it.|Your name is on the slate in ink, so you take the end bed without asking. The tonic is waiting, and so is the bill.}\n> right: Help roll bandages\n  alchemy +small\n  say: {Sister Amaranth teaches you the knot she uses. You will never tie your boots any other way.|You roll bandages and listen to her opinion of duelling, which is long and specific.|You roll bandages as if you work here. Sister Amaranth says that if you're going to be here this often you may as well.}\n\n@event infirmary_visiting_hour\ntitle: Visiting Hour\nplace: infirmary\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: cross\ntags: social\ncast:\n  friend: any where rel(friend) >= 6 and (tagged(friend, student) or tagged(friend, prefect)) and around(friend)\nscene: A friend propped up in an infirmary bed by a window, a visitor on a stool beside them with a paper bag of grapes.\ntext: {Visiting hour. {friend} is in the bed by the window,|Visiting hour. Sister Amaranth waves you to the stool you used last time. Today it's {friend},|Visiting hour. The stool by the window has worn to your shape. Today it's {friend},} {nursing a duelling bruise the colour of plums.|recovering from a draught of hiccups that hiccups back.|wrapped in three blankets against a lake chill.} {They} brighten{s} when you come in.\n> left: Sit and keep {them} company\n  social friend\n  say: {You bring grapes and eat most of them yourself. {friend} doesn't mind.|You stay until Sister Amaranth sweeps you out. {friend} waves from the pillows.|You sit on your worn stool and tell {friend} everything they've missed. {They} fall{s} asleep halfway, smiling.}\n> right: Smuggle in a pastry\n  chance likely:\n    rel friend +small\n    mood +tiny\n    say: {The pastry makes it past the desk. {friend} eats it under the blanket like a crime.|You hide the pastry in a hollowed-out book. {friend} is delighted by both.|You smuggle in a hot pastry under your coat. {friend} eats it wrapped in all three blankets and declares you a hero.}\n  else:\n    rel friend +tiny\n    renown -tiny\n    say: {Sister Amaranth confiscates the pastry and eats it herself, slowly, while looking at you.|Sister Amaranth smells the pastry from across the ward and holds out her hand without a word.|Sister Amaranth takes the pastry, cuts it in three, and gives you, {friend} and herself a piece each. Then she lectures you both.}\n\n@event infirmary_tonic_cupboard\ntitle: The Tonic Cupboard\nplace: infirmary\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: potion\ntags: danger\nscene: A tall glass-fronted cupboard of coloured tonics in an empty infirmary, its door ajar, a teacup steaming on the healer's desk.\ntext: {The tonic cupboard is open, and Sister Amaranth is in the back having her tea.|The tonic cupboard is open at teatime. A new label on the door reads \"counted daily\".|At teatime the cupboard is open, and the label now reads \"counted daily, by name\". The bright blue bottles have moved to the front.} {mana < 20: Your head aches with the dry ache of spent magic.|{The bottles clink in the draught.|A label reads, in her handwriting, \"absolutely not\".|The bright blue bottles have a new lock.}}\n> left: Help yourself to a tonic\n  alignment -tiny\n  challenge deceive apprentice+:\n    win:\n      mana +small\n      health +tiny\n      guile +tiny\n      say: {You drink it in the corridor. It tastes of peppermint and getting away with things.|You pocket the bottle, rinse it later, and put it back full of water. You feel slightly worse about that.|You take a blue one from the front, since that's where they've been put. You suspect that was the point.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      renown -tiny\n      say: {\"That one is for goats,\" says Sister Amaranth from the doorway. She makes you drink the right one, which is worse.|Sister Amaranth's teacup appears at your elbow. \"Put it back,\" she says, \"and sit down.\"|The blue bottles were put at the front to see who'd take one. Sister Amaranth writes your name in the \"counted daily\" book.}\n> right: Ask for one properly\n  health +small\n  mana +tiny\n  if gold > 0:\n    gold -small\n    say: {She charges you, lectures you, and gives you the good one.|She charges you, lectures you a little less, and gives you the good one.|\"You again,\" she says, and hands it over before you ask.}\n  else:\n    mood -small\n    say: She looks at your empty purse, gives you the good one anyway, and lectures you twice as long.\n\n# ---------------- The Alchemy Laboratory ----------------\n\n@event alchemy_lab_spare_cauldron\ntitle: A Spare Cauldron\nplace: alchemy_lab\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: cauldron\ntags: study magic\nscene: A long laboratory bench in the afternoon light, a small cauldron free among larger ones, jars of ingredients lined up beside it.\ntext: {There is a spare cauldron on the second bench, unclaimed.|The spare cauldron has a chalk mark on its rim like your initial.|Your cauldron, everyone calls it. Someone has scratched your initial into the handle.} {Someone has left a ladle standing upright in something.|The bench smells of mint and burnt sugar.|{season == \"summer\": The heat makes every brew twice as eager.|The windows have steamed over with other people's experiments.}}\n> left: Brew a practice batch\n  challenge brew apprentice:\n    win:\n      alchemy +small\n      mood +tiny\n      say: {The brew turns the right colour at the right moment. It feels like being trusted.|It comes out clear and smells of nothing, which is exactly right.|You brew in your cauldron, by your initial. It knows your hand by now and doesn't argue.}\n    lose:\n      alchemy +tiny\n      health -tiny\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {The brew turns a colour you have never seen. So do your eyebrows.|It foams over and sets, firmly, around your sleeve.|Even your own cauldron turns on you today. The brew sets, and your initial is the only part of the handle you can see.}\n> right: Scrub the cauldrons for crowns\n  gold +tiny\n  alchemy +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: {You learn more about failed potions from their residue than from any lecture.|You scrub until the bench is clean. Then you scratch your own initials into it, small, under the rim.|You scrub every cauldron and yours last, with care. It shines. Someone else uses it the next morning, and you mind.}\n\n@event alchemy_lab_third_floor\ntitle: The Third-Floor Fumes\nplace: alchemy_lab\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: bottle\ntags: social magic\nscene: A crowded upper floor of an alchemy laboratory, coloured vapour hanging under the ceiling, students working with handkerchiefs tied over their faces.\ntext: {On the third floor the ventilation charm has given up, and the fumes have taken charge.|Someone has hung a sign by the stairs giving the fumes' mood of the day.|The fumes' sign has a column for forecasts now, in several hands.} {Everyone is speaking in rhyme and trying to hide it.|Everyone is laughing at nothing and cannot stop.|Everyone is being unusually honest about everyone else's hair.} {season == \"winter\": The windows are frozen shut.|}\n> left: Stay and work in the fug\n  alchemy +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You get a great deal done and agree with everyone about everything.|You leave with good notes and a headache that hums.|You work through the honesty fumes and hear what everyone thinks of your hair. Your notes are excellent. Your hair is fine.}\n> right: Force the windows open\n  vigor +tiny\n  renown +tiny\n  say: {season == \"winter\": {The ice cracks. Half the floor cheers; half complains of the cold.|The frozen latch gives, and so does your thumbnail.|The window cracks open and the cold rushes in. Everyone stops being honest at once, with relief.}|{The windows give. The fumes leave, sulking, and the floor applauds.|You wedge the window with a textbook. The fumes drift out, and nearly the textbook too.|You open the window and the honest fumes leave. The silence that follows is extremely polite.}}\n\n@event alchemy_lab_unlabelled_jar\ntitle: The Unlabelled Jar\nplace: alchemy_lab\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: potion\ntags: magic danger\nscene: A dusty shelf of ingredient jars in an alchemy laboratory, one jar with no label glowing faintly, a student reaching for it.\ntext: {Someone has left an unlabelled jar on the shared shelf. It glows, faintly.|Another unlabelled jar on the shared shelf, beside the cautious label someone wrote for the last one.|The shared shelf now has a row of careful labels, and one new jar at the end of it, waiting its turn.} {It smells of thunder.|Something inside it turns over when you look.|It is warm to the touch.} {gpa >= 3: You could probably identify it, given time.|You have no idea what it is.}\n> left: Add a pinch to your brew\n  mana -tiny\n  chance even:\n    mana +medium\n    alchemy +tiny\n    say: {The brew turns gold and so do you, briefly. You feel enormous.|The brew sings a single note and leaves you humming with power.|A pinch of the warm stuff and your brew purrs like a cat. You don't know what it did, but it's better.}\n  else:\n    health -small\n    say: {The brew ignites, politely, and then impolitely.|Your cauldron sneezes. So, for the rest of the afternoon, do you.|The warm stuff makes your brew too warm, and then your bench, and then your sleeve.}\n> right: Label it, carefully\n  lore +small\n  say: {An hour of tests later, you write a cautious label. Someone adds a skull to it.|You work out what it is. Then you wash your hands three times.|You label it and add it to the row. Yours is the most careful label on the shelf, and the most honest: \"Warm. Unsure.\"}\n\n# ---------------- The Job Board ----------------\n\n@event job_board_odd_jobs\ntitle: Odd Jobs\nplace: job_board\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: paper\ntags: job money\nscene: A cork board in an entrance hall, a small notice pinned at an angle under the official job postings, a student reading it with a frown.\ntext: {Under the proper postings, someone has pinned an odd job.|Under the proper postings is an odd job, and a second pin someone has left beside it, for answers.|The odd-jobs corner has a card addressed to you by name, in a hand you're starting to recognise.} {Someone wants a homesick portrait read to.|Someone wants a ladder held, no questions asked.|Someone wants a reluctant gargoyle walked.} {term: |In the break the board is half bare, and the odd jobs pay a little better.}\n> left: Take the job\n  gold +small\n  mood -tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {It takes all afternoon and pays exactly what it said, which is rarer than it should be.|It is stranger than the notice suggested, and pays slightly less.|You take the gargoyle for its walk. It doesn't want to go, and then it doesn't want to come back. The pay is fair.}\n> right: Haggle first\n  challenge negotiate apprentice:\n    win:\n      gold +small\n      gold +tiny\n      charm +tiny\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {You haggle the fee up by a third. Your employer seems to respect you more for it.|You agree a price and a biscuit. The biscuit turns out to be the better half of the deal.|You haggle with the hand you recognise. They pay what you ask, and leave a note: \"Worth it.\"}\n    lose:\n      renown -tiny\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {Your employer takes the notice down and pins up a new one, asking for \"someone less difficult\".|The job goes to a first-year who asked for nothing. They seem very happy with it.|You haggle, and the next card in that hand is addressed to someone else. You've been dropped, politely.}\n\n@event job_board_lost_familiars\ntitle: Lost and Found\nplace: job_board\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: cat\ntags: social\nscene: The corner of a cork board crowded with hand-drawn posters of lost familiars and a ransom note for a sock.\ntext: {The corner of the board for lost things is busier than the jobs.|An old poster in the lost-things corner now reads \"found, thanks to a student\".|The lost-things corner has thank-you notes now. One draws the rescuer with a halo.} {A toad called \"Sir\" is missing, last seen being brave.|A ransom note demands three biscuits for one sock.|A small owl is lost, answers to nothing, and bites.} {season == \"winter\": Half the familiars have simply gone somewhere warm.|}\n> left: Go looking for it\n  chance even:\n    gold +small\n    mood +small\n    say: {You find it behind a radiator in the east tower, deeply unrepentant. The reward is small and grateful.|You find it, and the owner cries, and the reward is mostly biscuits.|You find the owl in the rafters of the owlery, pretending to belong. It bites you. The owner pays double, for the bite.}\n  else:\n    health -tiny\n    mood -tiny\n    say: {You search for an hour and find only a different, much angrier creature.|You don't find it. The next day the poster says \"Found! Thanks anyway.\"|You find a small owl. It is not the small owl. It also bites.}\n> right: Read every notice twice\n  guile +small\n  say: {The board is the castle's diary. You learn who lost what, and who owes whom.|You learn three secrets from the small print, and who has terrible handwriting.|You read every notice, including the thank-you notes. One of them is about you. You read that one three times.}\n\n# Job board stories (playtest 2, N13): small, everyday, plot-safe.\n\n@event job_board_yellow_notice\ntitle: The Oldest Notice\nplace: job_board\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nweight: 10\nart: paper\ntags: job social\nscene: A corner of a cork board where one notice, yellowed and curling, is pinned beneath years of newer ones.\ntext: In the bottom corner of the board, under years of newer notices, is one gone yellow and soft: \"Wanted, Thursdays: someone to read the newssheet aloud to the portrait on the west stair. He likes the sport and the obituaries. Small pay.\" Someone has renewed the pin.\n> left: Answer it, this Thursday\n  gold +tiny\n  mood +small\n  study -tiny\n  say: The portrait is a bald man in an old-fashioned collar who hasn't had the news read to him in years. He cheers the duelling results and sighs at the obituaries. You lose your Thursday evening, and he asks you to come back.\n> right: Ask the porter who pins it\n  lore +small\n  say: The porter says the notice was there when he started, and that a few crowns appear on the stair every Thursday whether anyone reads or not. He has never asked who leaves it. He suggests you don't either.\n\n@event job_board_torn_notice\ntitle: Half a Notice\nplace: job_board\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nweight: 10\nart: paper\ntags: job money\nscene: Two students tugging at either end of a single job notice in front of a crowded cork board, the paper starting to tear.\ntext: A well-paid notice (\"one evening's portering, generous rates\") has drawn two students, who have both got hold of it. It tears down the middle as you arrive. One holds the half with the pay; the other holds the half with the address. Both look at you.\n> left: Settle it between them\n  charm +small\n  say: You suggest they share the evening and split the fee. They both hate the idea, then both agree to it, then both thank you. You get nothing but a reputation for being reasonable.\n> right: Read the address and go yourself\n  gold +small\n  alignment -tiny\n  renown -tiny\n  say: You read the address over a shoulder and get there first. The job pays well. By supper both of them have told everyone what you did.\n\n@event job_board_monthly_letter\ntitle: A Letter a Month\nplace: job_board\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nweight: 10\nart: letter\ntags: job social\nscene: A small handwritten notice pinned beside a sealed letter on a cork board, a student reaching to unpin both.\ntext: A small notice in a careful, shaky hand: \"Wanted: a student to post a letter for me in the village, first of each month. A few crowns. I can't manage the hill any more.\" The letter is pinned beside it, sealed and addressed to someone in the village.\n> left: Take the letter down the hill\n  gold +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: You post it, then the next, then the next. In the third month the letter-writer leaves a note: the replies have started coming. You never learn who either of them is. You keep the crowns in a jar.\n> right: Leave it for someone else\n  say: You leave it on the board. The next morning it's gone, and someone has drawn a small heart beside the notice in pencil.\n\n# Odd gigs (feedback 2026-10-15): the Job Board shows its job menu on every visit and one of these after it, so a\n# visit always has something small to take or leave. Everyday and plot-safe; each repeats every few months.\n\n@event job_board_copying\ntitle: Fair Copies Wanted\nplace: job_board\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 10\nart: scroll\ntags: job money\nscene: A cork board with a notice in a sick student's shaky hand, a stack of borrowed lecture notes tied with string beneath it.\ntext: {A notice in a shaky hand. A student down with fever needs a fortnight of lecture notes copied out fair, and will pay for neatness.|Another fever, another fortnight of notes to copy fair. The notice says \"neat hand preferred\", and someone has written your name beside it.|The copying notice again. By now you can copy a page without reading a word of it, if you want to.}\n> left: Copy them for the pay\n  gold +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {Two evenings of careful letters. The pay arrives with a note that says your sevens are beautiful.|You copy every page, blot nothing and are paid exactly. Your wrist aches until the weekend.|You copy without reading, as fast as the ink allows. The pay is fair and the evening is gone.}\n> right: Copy them slowly, and learn them too\n  gold +tiny\n  lore +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {You read as you copy. Half the pay goes on candles, and you learn a course you aren't even taking.|You copy slowly and read every line. The notes are better than your own, and you keep a few of the ideas.|You read it all, slowly. The student pays a little less for the delay, and you know a little more for it.}\n\n@event job_board_message_run\ntitle: Messages Across the Castle\nplace: job_board\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: letter\ntags: job money\nscene: A student with a satchel of folded notes running across a castle bridge between towers, a staircase swinging away behind them.\ntext: {A professor wants a runner for the afternoon, with notes for every tower before the bell. A small fee, and no reading them.|The runner's notice is back, with a longer list of towers.|The runner's notice, and a list you could walk in your sleep. The staircases have learned your face.}\n> left: Run the round yourself\n  gold +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You reach the last tower as the bell goes. Your legs are jelly and the fee is honest.|Seven towers, two moving staircases and one argument with a portrait. Paid in full.|You take the shortcuts the staircases allow you now. Paid, and back in time for supper.}\n> right: Send the owls instead\n  gold +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {You pay the owlery out of the fee and keep the rest, and the afternoon. The professor never knows.|The owls do it faster than you would have. You pocket what's left and feel only slightly guilty.|The owls know the round now as well as you. You keep a little of the fee and all of your afternoon.}\n\n@event job_board_market_stall\ntitle: Mind the Stall\nplace: job_board\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: coin\ntags: job money\nscene: A village market stall heaped with jars and woollens, a student behind it with a cash tin, the owner hobbling away towards a chair in the sun.\ntext: {A villager with a bad knee wants someone to mind her market stall on Saturday morning, for a share of the takings.|The stall again: jars, woollens, the bad knee and the share of the takings.|The stall owner pins the notice up with your name already on it, and a question mark, to be polite.}\n> left: Mind it as she asked\n  gold +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You sell three jars and a scarf and count the tin twice. She pays you a fair share and a jar.|A slow morning, honestly counted. She is pleased with the tin and gives you your share.|You know the prices by heart now. She hands you your share without counting it, which is a kind of compliment.}\n> right: Talk up the wares\n  gold +tiny\n  charm +small\n  say: {You sell like a pedlar and the takings are up. Most of the extra goes back to her, as it should, and you've learned a patter.|You talk a farmer into two scarves he didn't know he needed. Your share is small and your voice is gone.|You could sell her jars to anyone now. She jokes about leaving you the stall.}\n\n@event job_board_lost_property\ntitle: The Lost-Property Box\nplace: job_board\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: key\ntags: job money\nscene: The porter's lodge, a vast wooden box of odd gloves, scarves, books and one boot, a student sorting them into piles.\ntext: {The porter wants the lost-property box sorted before it starts to smell, and pays by the hour.|The lost-property box is full again, the porter says, mostly of the same gloves.|The porter slides the box across without a word. You know where the odd boot goes now.}\n> left: Sort it quickly for the pay\n  gold +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {Gloves, scarves, books and one boot, in piles by dusk. The porter pays and keeps the boot for himself.|You sort it in an hour. The porter pays for two, out of habit.|You sort it fast. The porter pays, and then tries the odd boot on again, hopefully.}\n> right: Read what people lose\n  gold +tiny\n  guile +small\n  say: {You learn who carries love letters and who never returns library books. The porter pays you less for taking so long.|You read the names in the books and the notes in the pockets, and put everything back exactly. A slow hour, and a useful one.|You know the castle by what it drops. The porter pays a little less, and you put the gossip away for later.}\n\n@event job_board_sick_familiar\ntitle: A Sick Familiar\nplace: job_board\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: cat\ntags: job money\nscene: A student sitting up by lamplight beside a basket where a small sick familiar is curled in a blanket, a bottle of tonic on the table.\ntext: {A student going home for a funeral needs someone to sit up with their sick familiar for two nights. They will pay.|Another notice for a night sitter, for a sick toad this time, and the pay is better.|The familiars' night-sitter: that's you, apparently. A cat this time, and a worried owner.}\n> left: Sit up with it, both nights\n  gold +small\n  health -small\n  say: {Two nights of tonic every hour and very little sleep. It recovers. So, eventually, do you.|The toad recovers and you nearly don't. The owner pays double and calls you a saint.|The cat is ungrateful and lives. You're paid, and spend the next day asleep in lectures.}\n> right: Sit up one night, and find a second sitter\n  gold +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {You take the first night and talk a friend into the second. The pay splits; the sleep doesn't.|One night each, you and a sleepy first-year. The familiar is fine. Half the pay is still pay.|You know who will sit up for a little money now. You split the nights and the pay, and sleep.}\n\n@event job_board_porters_crates\ntitle: Crates for the Porter\nplace: job_board\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 10\nart: house\ntags: job money\nscene: A cart of wooden crates at the foot of a long castle stair, a student lifting one while the porter points upward.\ntext: {The porter needs crates carried up from the barge, a great many of them, up a great many stairs.|Crates again, the porter says, pointing at the barge. More of them than last time.|You hear the barge bell and the porter waves before you're even down the stairs. Crates.}\n> left: Carry them all\n  gold +small\n  health -small\n  say: {Forty crates up seventy stairs. The porter pays you and a little extra for not dropping the one that clinked.|Your back will remember every crate. The pay is good.|You carry the lot and don't count them any more. Paid, and sore.}\n> right: Carry what you can, and train with it\n  gold +tiny\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You take them slowly, two steps at a time, and count it as exercise. The porter pays for the crates you managed.|Half the crates, carried properly. Your legs are stronger and your purse a little lighter than it could be.|You carry what you can and stop before your back does. Paid for half, and stronger for it.}\n\n@event job_board_tutor_hour\ntitle: An Hour of Tutoring\nplace: job_board\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: book\ntags: job money\nscene: An empty classroom, a nervous first-year with an essay full of crossings-out and an older student pointing at a line.\ntext: {A first-year's notice, written with great care, asks for an hour's help with an essay. They can pay a little.|The same first-year's careful handwriting: another essay, another hour.|The first-year has stopped writing notices. They just pin up the essay, with a coin taped to the corner.}\n> left: Take the hour, and the coin\n  gold +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {An hour of commas and confusion. The essay improves; so does your purse, slightly.|You take them through it line by line. They pay, and thank you twice.|You know their mistakes before they make them now. The hour goes quickly, and they pay.}\n> right: Help, and leave the coin pinned\n  mood +small\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: {You leave the coin taped to the board. The essay comes back with a good mark, and the first-year tells everyone.|You help for nothing. They try to pay you anyway, and you won't let them.|You leave the coin where it is, as always. Somebody has drawn a small sun beside the notice.}\n\n@event job_board_greenhouse_pots\ntitle: Pots for Old Maud\nplace: job_board\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: leaf\ntags: job money\nscene: A greenhouse doorway stacked with muddy clay pots, an old gardener on a stool directing a student with a scrubbing brush.\ntext: {Old Maud's notice asks for clay pots scrubbed before planting, for a small wage and a cup of tea.|Maud's notice again. More pots, the same tea.|Maud has given up pinning notices. The pots are just waiting at the greenhouse door, with your brush on top.}\n> left: Scrub them for the wage\n  gold +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {A hundred pots, scrubbed and stacked. Maud pays you and says you're slower than her and twice as thorough.|You scrub them all and drink the tea. The wage is small and honestly earned.|You scrub in silence, and so does she. She pays you and lets you take a cutting home.}\n> right: Scrub them and ask about the plants\n  gold +tiny\n  alchemy +small\n  say: {Maud talks while you scrub, about which leaf cures what and which leaf kills. She pays a little less, because of the talking.|You learn three remedies and a poison you should never brew. The wage is smaller; the lesson isn't.|Maud tests you on last month's lesson while you scrub. You pass, mostly. The wage is small and the tea is good.}\n"},{"path":"content/events/pools/castle_towers.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Baseline pools: the castle towers (phase 3, step 1b).\n#  Repeatable routine events for the observatory, Mir's workshop,\n#  the Ledger office, the headmistress's tower, the Restricted\n#  Section, the third gargoyle and the seventh tower, plus the\n#  hidden-course hints for artifice, starcalling and hollow studies.\n#  No clues, no arc moves: these places stay mysterious.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- The Observatory ----------------\n\n@event observatory_night_log\ntime: evening night\ntitle: The Night Log\nplace: observatory\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: book\ntags: study magic\nscene: A brass observatory dome at night, one student writing in a huge logbook by lamplight while the great telescope swings round on its own.\ntext: {The observatory keeps a night log, and anyone may write in it.|{flag(wrote_night_log): Since your last entry|Since you were last here}, someone has drawn a moustache on the moon.|You know the log's hands by now: the careful one, the frantic one, the one who only ever writes \"cloudy\".} {season == \"winter\": Frost feathers the inside of the dome.|{The telescope turns, unasked, towards the north.|A draught turns the pages for you.|The lamp is low on oil, as always.}}\n> left: Log what the telescope finds\n  flag wrote_night_log\n  lore +small\n  health -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You chart a star that wasn't there last month. The log already has it, in a hand forty years older.|Three hours, two pages and one sneeze that smudges a constellation.|The telescope settles on a quiet blue star and waits until you've written it down properly.}\n> right: Read the old entries instead\n  mood +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {A winter entry from long ago says \"Saw nothing. Stayed anyway. Worth it.\"|A love letter written across a page of moon sightings. Very bad. Very sweet.|{final_year and flag(wrote_night_log): You find your own first entry and barely know the hand.|A student from your year logged your star a week before you did, with a small drawn smile.}}\n\n@event observatory_one_star_each\ntime: evening night\ntitle: One Star Each\nplace: observatory\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: star\ntags: social magic\ncast:\n  here: present(place) where tagged(here, peer)\nscene: Two students at the eyepiece of a great brass telescope, one stepping aside to let the other look.\ntext: {The rule chalked on the observatory door says one star each, then pass it on. {here} has been at the eyepiece for some time.|Someone has added \"two if they're small\" under the chalk rule. {here} is at the eyepiece.|Someone is always at the telescope when you arrive; tonight it's {here}.} {season == \"summer\": The nights are short and the queue is impatient.|{The dome is cold enough to see your breath.|The telescope creaks round to follow something.|Below, the bridges of light are going out one by one.}}\n> left: Share the eyepiece\n  rel here +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {cast_as(here, seraphine): {\"That one,\" Seraphine says, \"is going to matter in, oh.\" She doesn't finish.|\"Left of it,\" Seraphine says. \"Next spring's left.\" You look anyway.|\"There,\" Seraphine says, and moves your hand an inch. \"That's the one you'll want. Don't ask me when.\"}|{cast_as(here, kael): {Kael steps aside and points. When your hands get cold, he stands closer.|Kael says the star's name in a Northern word, once, and doesn't explain.|Kael shares the eyepiece without being asked. Below, the bridges go out one by one, and neither of you counts them.}|You take turns until neither of you remembers whose star it was.}}\n> right: Wait your turn properly\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {cast_as(here, seraphine): {Seraphine leaves mid-sentence. \"You'll want it,\" she says. \"Later. Now, I mean.\"|\"Yours is the next one,\" Seraphine says, and it is.|Seraphine steps back as you arrive, as if she'd been holding your place. \"Right on time,\" she says, puzzling you.}|{cast_as(here, kael): {Kael yields with a formal nod and goes out onto the roof. Frost follows.|Kael waits behind you. When you turn round, he's gone.|Kael gives up the eyepiece the moment you arrive and stays to watch the bridges go out with you.}|By your turn the star has set. The next one is nearly as good.}}\n\n@event observatory_roof_ledge\ntitle: The Roof Ledge\nplace: observatory\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: moon\ntags: life danger\nscene: A student sitting on a narrow ledge outside a brass dome, legs dangling over the castle roofs, the lake far below.\ntext: {A window in the dome opens onto the roof, and onto a ledge that everyone swears they have never sat on.|Someone else who never sits there has oiled the latch.|The ledge. You know where the loose tile is now.} {season == \"winter\": The tiles are white with frost.|{The lake below is a second sky.|A warm wind comes up from the village.|Someone's scarf is snagged on the gutter.}}\n> left: Climb out onto the ledge\n  challenge feat apprentice:\n    win:\n      mood +small\n      lore +tiny\n      say: {You sit with the whole sky to yourself until the stars begin to wheel.|The castle looks small from here, and kind, and very old.|You step over the loose tile without looking. Someone's scarf is still on the gutter; you leave it, for whoever comes next.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {The loose tile is loose in a new place. You slide, grab, and hang on, and climb back in with skinned palms.|A gust takes your balance and most of your dignity. The gutter keeps the rest.|You reach for the snagged scarf and the gutter reaches for you. You get back in with the scarf and a bruise.}\n> right: Watch through the glass\n  lore +tiny\n  say: {You see half the sky and all of the smudges.|Through the glass the stars look like they're behind a shop window. You can look; you can't have.|You watch through the glass. On the ledge outside, someone else is sitting where you'd have sat, and they wave.}\n\n@event observatory_scraped_word\ntime: evening night\ntitle: The Scraped-Out Word\nplace: observatory\nwhen: not enrolled(starcalling) and not passed(starcalling, 1)\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 8\nweight: 6\nart: star\ntags: study mystery\nscene: An old observatory logbook open by lamplight, the same word scraped out of line after line.\ntext: {In the night log's oldest volume, every entry by a student who had finished Advanced Astromancy uses the same word, and every time it has been scraped out.|You've worked out that the missing word was something you did to a star, not something you saw.|You know these pages now. The word is gone; the shape of the work around it is not.} {In one margin, \"Ask. Be told.\"|A pressed flower falls out.|The lamp gutters as you turn the page.}\n> left: Trace the gaps by lamplight\n  find heard_of_starcalling\n  lore +small\n  mana -small\n  say: You feed a thread of witchlight into the gaps, and it drinks more than you meant to give. {The words around the gaps say \"called\", \"named\", \"down\". Whoever taught it taught it aloud, to people who had finished Advanced Astromancy.|\"...and the light came down when I said its name...\" Then the gap. You will need someone to tell you the word.|The shape around the gap is a verb. Whatever it was, it was done to a star, and it was taught, not written.}\n> right: Ask the telescope, half joking\n  lore +tiny\n  say: {The telescope swings to one bright star and stays there, patient as a teacher who knows you aren't ready.|It turns to a named star and holds it. You get the feeling you have to finish Advanced Astromancy before it explains.|The telescope turns, very slowly, to the same star as always. You're beginning to think it's a reminder, not an answer.}\n\n# ---------------- Mir's Workshop ----------------\n\n@event workshop_spare_bench\ntitle: The Spare Bench\nplace: workshop\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: gear\ntags: magic life\nscene: A cluttered workshop bench under a hand-painted sign, a music box in pieces beside a jar of springs, fire buckets by the door.\ntext: {Mir keeps a spare bench for visitors, under a sign that says TOUCH THINGS (CAREFULLY).|The sign over the spare bench has grown a second line, (MORE CAREFULLY).|Your bench, as Mir has started calling it, has a jar of springs waiting.} {Mir waves a spanner at you without looking up.|Something on a high shelf is ticking.|{season == \"winter\": The forge-lamp makes it the warmest room in the west wing.|The window is open to let the smoke out.}}\n> left: Mend a jammed music box\n  alchemy +small\n  mood -tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: A loose spring bites your thumb on the way in. {It plays again, a little too fast, like it's late for something.|You fix the tune and break the lid. Mir says that's probably progress.|It plays a waltz nobody has heard in years. You leave it on the sill to finish.}\n> right: Just watch Mir work\n  mood +small\n  rel mir +tiny\n  say: {Mir explains a gear train for an hour. You follow about a third, which they say is probably a record.|You sit by the fire buckets and watch the sparks. Your eyes sting. It's a good afternoon.|Mir hands you things to hold and forgets you're holding them. It's oddly restful.}\n\n@event workshop_volunteer\ntitle: A Volunteer With Hands\nplace: workshop\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: gear\ntags: social magic\ncast:\n  maker: mir\nscene: An artificer in soot-streaked goggles holding out a strange brass contraption, a fire bucket waiting hopefully on the floor.\ntext: {\"Perfect timing! I need a volunteer and you have hands!\" {maker} holds up {a pair of boots that walk on their own|a hat that remembers where you left things|goggles that see round corners}.|{maker} has a new invention and a familiar look. Today: {boots that walk on their own|a hat with a memory|goggles for corners}.|You don't ask anymore. You just look at what {maker} is holding, then at the fire bucket.}\n> left: Try it on\n  chance likely:\n    alchemy +small\n    rel maker +tiny\n    mood +tiny\n    say: {It works! For eleven glorious minutes, it works, and {maker} writes down every one of them.|It does exactly what it should. {maker} looks almost suspicious and walks round it three times.|It works. You still don't know what it was for, and {maker} is too delighted to tell you.}\n  else:\n    health -small\n    rel maker +small\n    say: {It works in the wrong direction. You spend a while upside down. {maker} apologises at length, with diagrams.|There's a bang, a smell of singed eyebrow, and {maker} saying \"Interesting!\" in a voice that means sorry.|It goes off with a pop and a smell of hot brass. {maker} writes \"promising\" and hands you the bucket.}\n> right: Hold the fire bucket\n  rel maker +tiny\n  say: {You hold the bucket. You don't need the bucket. {maker} looks faintly disappointed.|You need the bucket. Briefly, splendidly, you need the bucket.|You hold the bucket. You need it twice, which {maker} calls an improvement.}\n\n@event workshop_stirring_cauldron\ntitle: The Self-Stirring Cauldron\nplace: workshop\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: cauldron\ntags: magic danger\nscene: A small cauldron in a workshop corner stirring itself with a wooden spoon, a stern paper note pinned to its rim.\ntext: {The self-stirring cauldron in the corner wears a note. DO NOT ADJUST. IT KNOWS WHAT IT DID.|Someone has underlined the cauldron's note twice and added the word STILL.|The cauldron's spoon pauses when you come in, as if it remembers you.} {It's going anticlockwise, which it isn't supposed to.|It smells of toffee and lightning.|{season == \"spring\": Someone has put flowers in it. It is stirring them.|It's stirring soup for nobody.}}\n> left: Adjust the enchantment\n  challenge enchant apprentice+:\n    win:\n      alchemy +small\n      mood +tiny\n      say: {It settles into a slow, contented clockwise. Mir, passing, says \"Oh!\" and then \"Huh,\" and then \"Teach me.\"|The spoon taps the rim twice, like a thank-you, and gets on with it.|You adjust it, gently, the way it seems to want. It stirs the soup for nobody a little more cheerfully.}\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: {It stirs your sleeve, then you, then your sleeve again.|It speeds up instead. The ceiling now has toffee on it, and so do you.|It recognises you, and holds a grudge. The spoon flicks soup at you until you back away.}\n> right: Take notes from a safe distance\n  lore +tiny\n  say: {You write down everything it's doing wrong. The list is long and slightly envious.|You sketch the rune on its base. The cauldron splashes your page, on purpose you think.|You take notes from the doorway. The spoon pauses, as if waiting for you to finish the sentence.}\n\n@event workshop_glass_cabinet\ntitle: The Glass Cabinet\nplace: workshop\nwhen: not enrolled(artifice) and not passed(artifice, 1)\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 8\nweight: 6\nart: gear\ntags: study magic\nscene: A tall glass cabinet at the back of a workshop, clockwork animals inside it, a faded card propped against the glass.\ntext: {At the back of the workshop is a glass cabinet that isn't Mir's, full of faculty machines, very still.|A clockwork mouse in the glass cabinet has moved since your last visit, and now faces the door.|The cabinet. Mir says the machines in it think, probably, and are waiting for the right student.} A card in old ink: \"Artifice. By invitation, to those who have passed Alchemy II and Evocation II.\"\n> left: Study the machines through the glass\n  alchemy +small\n  say: {Nothing moves while you watch. The moment you look away, something ticks.|You count the gears in a brass beetle and lose count. Somebody made this who could make things and make them burn.|The machines don't move, but you're sure they're thinking. One of them, very slightly, nods.}\n> right: Try the cabinet lock\n  alignment -tiny\n  challenge deceive journeyman-:\n    win:\n      guile +small\n      mood +tiny\n      say: {The lock gives. The mouse winds itself up, bows, and goes back to sleep. You close the door very quietly.|The door swings open an inch, and every machine inside turns its head to look at you. You close it again, politely.|The lock opens at a touch, as if it had been waiting. The machines look at you, and then, satisfied, at each other.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      renown -tiny\n      say: {The cabinet shrieks like a kettle. Mir is delighted. The corridor is not.|The lock bites your pick and keeps it. Mir says it does that, probably, to people it likes.|The lock doesn't bite this time. It simply refuses, the way a cat refuses, and Mir says that's progress.}\n\n# ---------------- The Lumen Ledger Office ----------------\n\n@event ledger_office_unprintable\ntitle: Letters We Will Not Print\nplace: ledger_office\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: letter\ntags: life\nscene: A wicker basket overflowing with letters by the door of a cramped newspaper office, front pages papering the walls.\ntext: {By the Ledger's door sits a basket marked LETTERS WE WILL NOT PRINT. Visitors may read them.|The unprintable basket has overflowed into a second basket, which is also full.|You are a connoisseur of the unprintable basket now.} {On top is a complaint about the soup, in verse.|One is just a drawing of a prefect, unflattering and accurate.|{season == \"winter\": Someone demands that snow be cancelled.|Someone asks who keeps moving the stairs.}}\n> left: Read the whole basket\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {The best one is an apology to a goat.|One is from a first-year who misses home. You put it back gently.|{final_year and flag(ledger_letter_sent): A letter in your own first-year hand. You don't remember sending it. You were right.|Three letters about the same broken window, all blaming different people.}}\n> right: Write one of your own\n  flag ledger_letter_sent\n  chance unlikely:\n    renown +small\n    mood +tiny\n    say: {They print it. Two people quote it at you at breakfast; one of them disagrees with it loudly.|It runs on the back page, under your initials. You feel oddly famous for a day.|They print it, with a footnote from the editor disagreeing. It's the most-read thing in the issue.}\n  else:\n    mood -tiny\n    say: {It goes in the basket. By next week, someone is reading it aloud.|It's rejected with a note: \"Too sensible.\" You aren't sure that's a criticism.|It goes in the basket. You find it there next week, with someone else's reply pinned to it.}\n\n@event ledger_office_deadline_night\ntitle: Deadline Night\nplace: ledger_office\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: paper\ntags: social\ncast:\n  here: present(place) where tagged(here, peer)\nscene: A cramped office lit by one lamp, a groaning printing press, two students folding newspapers into tall stacks.\ntext: {The press is groaning like a dragon on deadline, and {here} eyes you like a captain eyes a spare oar.|{here} hands you a stack before you've said hello, and a rag for your fingers.|You take a stack and start folding. {here} doesn't look up, which is a compliment.} {The ink on the front page is still wet.|A headline has a spelling mistake nobody will admit to.|{final_year and flag(ledger_folded): You've folded this paper for years now.|The press shudders as if it's thinking of stopping.}}\n> left: Fold until the press stops\n  flag ledger_folded\n  rel here +small\n  health -small\n  say: {cast_as(here, nessa): {\"Good. Fast. Again,\" says Nessa. From her, that's a sonnet.|Nessa folds twice as fast as you and reads every page as she goes.|Nessa doesn't say anything. She just slides the next stack to you, which is the highest honour on deadline night.}|{By dawn your fingers are grey with ink, and you know tomorrow's news before anyone.|You fold until the stacks are taller than you. Then you fold the ones that fell over.|You fold in rhythm with the press. When it stops, you keep going for a moment, out of habit.}}\n> right: Make your excuses\n  mood +small\n  rel here -small\n  renown -tiny\n  say: {cast_as(here, nessa): {\"Deserter,\" Nessa calls. \"Noted. Quoted. Forgiven by Thursday.\"|Nessa doesn't look up. \"Coward,\" she says, almost fondly, and writes something down.|Nessa looks up for once. \"Fine. Go. You owe me a Thursday.\" You do, and she collects.}|{You slip away before the second stack. The press groans after you, reproachfully.|You plead an essay. Nobody believes you. Tomorrow's paper mentions a certain absent volunteer.|You leave, and the press shudders to a stop as you go. Somebody blames you, and it goes in the paper.}}\n\n@event ledger_office_drying_line\ntitle: The Drying Line\nplace: ledger_office\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: paper\ntags: life\nscene: Freshly printed pages pegged on a line across a cramped office to dry, one page hanging slightly crooked.\ntext: {Tomorrow's gossip column is pegged on a line to dry, and nobody is watching it.|The proofs hang on the line, and the peg on the gossip column is a new red one.|The drying line. You know which peg holds the gossip column.} {One item names your house.|One item begins \"Which second-year was seen...\"|{final_year: One item is about the graduating year. Possibly you.|A whole paragraph is about a broken window.}}\n> left: Read ahead\n  challenge deceive apprentice+:\n    win:\n      guile +small\n      mood +small\n      say: {You read it all and hang it back exactly as crooked as it was.|You know tomorrow's scandal tonight. It is much smaller than it will look in print.|You read the item under the red peg. It's about you, and it's true, and it's very short. You leave it exactly as it was.}\n    lose:\n      renown -small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {Caught. The next column has an item beginning \"Which nosy visitor...\"|A peg snaps. The page lands face-down in the ink tray, and everyone turns round.|You lift the red peg and the whole line comes down. Tomorrow's column has a new item, and it's about you.}\n> right: Leave the line alone\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You spend the night wondering. That's the price of manners.|You find out with everyone else, at breakfast. It was about you anyway.|You leave it. You know which peg it is, and that's almost the same as knowing.}\n\n# ---------------- The Headmistress's Tower ----------------\n\n@event headmistress_tower_stone_owl\ntime: evening\ntitle: The Stone Owl\nplace: headmistress_tower\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: owl\ntags: magic\nscene: A stone owl on the newel post of a spiral stair, one eye open, a student standing very still before it.\ntext: {The stone owl on the stair opens one eye. \"A question first,\" it says, in a voice like a door closing.|The owl's other eye is half open tonight. It has been waiting all day for someone to ask.|The owl opens both eyes for you now, which may or may not be an honour.} {Today's question is about the founders.|Today's question is a riddle with no right answer.|{season == \"winter\": There is snow on its head, and it is not amused.|A moth has landed on its beak. It is pretending not to notice.}}\n> left: Answer the question\n  challenge research journeyman wild:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      rel isolde +tiny\n      say: {The owl closes its eyes and the stair is yours. Upstairs, someone laughs, short and pleased.|\"Adequate,\" says the owl, which from a stone owl is a standing ovation.|You answer before it finishes asking. The owl looks at you with both eyes for a long time, then closes them.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: {\"No,\" says the owl, and closes both eyes. You climb anyway, feeling marked down.|The owl repeats your answer back to you, slowly. It sounds worse.|Your answer is right and the owl says \"No\" anyway, to see what you'll do. You climb on. It seems satisfied.}\n> right: Climb past without a word\n  guile +small\n  rel isolde -tiny\n  say: {The owl says nothing. At the top, the door is shut. You have the strong feeling you've been noted.|You get past. The owl's eye follows you all the way up, and all the way down again.|You climb past and nod to it, as you'd nod to a porter. It nods back, very slightly.}\n\n@event headmistress_tower_tea\ntitle: Two Cups Poured\nplace: headmistress_tower\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: cup\ntags: social\ncast:\n  host: isolde\nscene: A tower study full of books, a tall pale woman pouring two cups of tea beside a board of crystal towers by the window.\ntext: {The headmistress pours two cups unasked. \"Sit. Tell me how your term is going, and then how it is really going.\"|Two cups, already poured. \"Well,\" says {host}, \"how is it really going?\"|You sit before she asks. She looks pleased, and doesn't say so.} {The crystal towers on her board have moved.|Rain ticks on the high windows.|{final_year: \"Your last year,\" she says. \"I remember your first.\"|Footnote, the library cat, sleeps on her papers.}}\n> left: Tell her the truth\n  rel host +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {She listens without interrupting, which is worse than any lecture and better than most comfort.|She hears all of it and says only, \"Yes. I thought so.\" Somehow that helps, later.|You tell her before she asks. She hears it all, then tells you something true about her own first years. It doesn't help, and then it does.}\n> right: Give her the polished version\n  challenge deceive vs isolde.guile -2:\n    win:\n      charm +small\n      mood +tiny\n      say: {She laughs, short and delighted, and lets you get away with it. You aren't sure that's a good sign.|\"Very good,\" she says. \"Next time, the true one.\" She means both halves.|She lets the polished version stand and pours more tea. \"You've got better at it,\" she says. You can't tell if that's praise.}\n    lose:\n      rel host -tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: {\"Mm,\" she says, and pours you more tea, and waits. You end up telling her anyway.|She lets the silence go on until it says everything you didn't.|She hears the polished version, then repeats the one sentence of it that was true. You talk for an hour after that.}\n\n@event headmistress_tower_window_seat\ntitle: The Window Seat\nplace: headmistress_tower\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: book\ntags: study\nscene: A deep window seat high in a tower, a student reading with the lake far below, a headmistress writing letters at a desk behind.\ntext: {The headmistress waves you to the window seat without looking up from her letters. \"Read anything. Break nothing.\"|A book has been left on the window seat, open, as if for you.|Your window seat, the headmistress calls it now, as though it always was.} {Below, the lake is perfectly still.|Her quill scratches; the clock does not tick.|{season == \"autumn\": Leaves blow past at the height of the clouds.|The bridges of light come on one by one below.}}\n> left: Read what she left out\n  lore +small\n  say: {A history of the seven towers, annotated in her hand. One note just says \"No.\"|A book of old law, with a dried rose marking a page about second chances.|A treatise on wards so dull it must be important. You get through it. She notices.}\n> right: Doze on the window seat\n  mood +small\n  health +tiny\n  rel isolde -tiny\n  say: {You wake to find a blanket over you and no comment at all, which is its own kind of comment.|You sleep through most of the visit. When you wake, she is still writing, and gives you a look over her spectacles that says she had hoped you would read.|You doze in your window seat. You wake to find she's moved her desk lamp so it doesn't shine in your eyes.}\n\n# ---------------- The Restricted Section ----------------\n\n@event restricted_section_breathing_shelves\ntitle: The Breathing Shelves\nplace: restricted_section\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: book\ntags: study mystery\nscene: Black-bound books on tall shelves in the dark, a single candle on a reading table, the spines faintly rising and falling.\ntext: {The black-bound books breathe in the dark, slow as sleepers. You find a chair and light a candle.|You've learned to breathe with the shelves; it helps.|Your chair is where you left it. The books shift, as if making room.} {One spine is warm to the touch.|Somewhere a page turns by itself.|{season == \"winter\": Your breath fogs. Theirs doesn't.|The candle burns low and blue.}}\n> left: Read until the candle gutters\n  lore +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {You read about wards that were old when the towers were new. Afterwards you can't sleep, and don't quite want to.|You read a whole chapter before you notice the book has been reading along, a line ahead.|You read in your chair by a low blue candle. The shelves breathe with you, and the chapter goes in like a held breath.}\n> right: Read only the spines\n  lore +tiny\n  mana -tiny\n  say: {Even the titles take something out of you. One of them is just your house's name.|You walk the shelves by candlelight. The spines lean towards you, and then politely away.|You walk the spines, and they make room for you as you pass. One spine you haven't seen before has no title at all.}\n\n@event restricted_section_lantern\ntitle: Holding the Lantern\nplace: restricted_section\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: lantern\ntags: social mystery\ncast:\n  friend: any where rel(friend) >= 6 and tagged(friend, student)\nscene: Two students in a dark library aisle, one holding a lantern high, the other reading a black book, shadows stretching behind them.\ntext: {{friend} came with you, for courage, and is holding the lantern in both hands.|You bring a second candle this time; {friend} takes the lantern without being asked.|You know which aisles creak now. {friend} holds the lantern where you point.} {\"Did that book just sigh?\" {they} whisper{s}.|A shelf creaks behind you both.|{final_year: \"I'll miss this,\" {they} say{s}, not sounding sure.|The lantern makes the shadows walk.}}\n> left: Read while they keep watch\n  lore +small\n  rel friend -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You read; {friend} keeps watch and keeps count of every noise, out loud.|You lose an hour in a book. {friend} loses an hour staring at a shelf that keeps staring back.|You read; {friend} holds the light where you point, and the shadows walk where {they} hold{s} it. Neither of you says much on the way back.}\n> right: Take the lantern for a while\n  rel friend +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You hold the light so {friend} can read. Something in the dark rustles the whole time, and you don't let it show.|{friend} reads aloud in a whisper. The books seem to lean in to listen.|You take the lantern and {friend} reads to you, a page you'll both remember. The shadows stop walking to listen.}\n\n@event restricted_section_chained_book\ntime: evening\ntitle: The Chained Book\nplace: restricted_section\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: chain\ntags: mystery danger\nscene: A heavy book chained to a library shelf, lying open on its own under a dim candle, the chain hanging slack.\ntext: {One book here is chained to its shelf, and tonight it has opened itself to a page.|The chained book lies open at a different page, and the chain is a link longer.|The chained book has been expecting you. That's the worst part.} {The page is in a script you almost know.|The page shows a lock with no keyhole.|The page is blank until you look away.}\n> left: Read the open page\n  challenge research adept-:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      mana +small\n      say: A thread of stored power runs up your arm from the page. {The words make sense for exactly as long as you're reading them. Afterwards you only remember that they did.|You read it, and the book turns its own page, satisfied, as if you'd passed something.|The page stays blank while you look and fills when you blink. You read it in blinks. It takes all night.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      health -tiny\n      say: {The words read you back. For a day afterwards you keep feeling underlined.|The candle goes out mid-line. In the dark, the page keeps going.|You look away and the page fills; you look back and it's blank, and smug. Your eyes ache for a day.}\n> right: Close it and step back\n  mood -tiny\n  mana +tiny\n  say: As the cover shuts, something that had been quietly drawing on your magic lets go. {The chain rattles as the cover shuts, like a sigh. You stand with your back to it until your hands stop shaking.|You close it gently, the way you'd close a door on someone sleeping.|You close it before it can finish expecting you. The chain lies still, a link shorter.}\n\n@event restricted_section_handleless_door\ntime: evening\ntitle: The Door Without a Handle\nplace: restricted_section\nwhen: not enrolled(hollow_studies) and not passed(hollow_studies, 1)\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 8\nweight: 6\nart: door\ntags: study mystery\nscene: A plain door with no handle at the back of a dark library, chalk writing on it that has not smudged in years.\ntext: {At the very back is a door with no handle and no window. On it, in chalk that never smudges, HOLLOW STUDIES, BY LEAVE OF THE HEADMISTRESS.|Tonight the door without a handle is warm, like the outer door.|You've read its chalk so often you could recite it.} Underneath, smaller: \"Umbral Theory II. Astromancy II. And know what the founders made, and why.\"\n> left: Knock\n  rel isolde +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: {Nobody answers. Then, very far away, a pleasant voice says, \"Not yet.\"|Your knock doesn't echo. It just stops, as if something on the other side caught it. Next day the headmistress nods to you in the corridor, as if she heard.|You knock, as you always do. This time, from far away, someone knocks back, once, as if to say they've noted it.}\n> right: Copy the chalk into your notes\n  lore +small\n  mana -small\n  say: Copying the chalk draws on your magic like a cold draught. {The chalk makes your pencil cold. Two courses and a question, and the question is the hard part.|You copy it word for word. Back in your room, your notes have the same words, and one more underline than you made.|You copy it from memory and check it against the door. The chalk agrees with you, which feels like being agreed with.}\n\n# ---------------- The Third Gargoyle ----------------\n\n@event gargoyle_passage_old_marks\ntitle: Marks on the Wall\nplace: gargoyle_passage\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: map\ntags: life mystery\nscene: A narrow stone passage behind a gargoyle, its walls covered in scratched initials, dates and tiny arrows, lit by a stub of candle.\ntext: {The passage walls are covered in initials, dates and tiny arrows, scratched by generations who were never here.|Among the marks on the wall is a new one, still sharp.|You know these walls now: the arrows that lie, the dates that can't be right, the initials that keep coming back.} {The wind sings through the gargoyle's teeth.|Below, a door closes that shouldn't be there.|{season == \"winter\": Frost picks out every letter.|Moths sleep in the deeper scratches.}}\n> left: Add your own mark\n  renown +tiny\n  guile +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You scratch your initials small and low, with a tiny arrow pointing nowhere. It feels like signing something.|{final_year and flag(left_wall_mark): You add the year beside your old mark. It looks so much younger than you.|A knuckle pays for it. The mark is crooked and entirely yours.}|You add your mark beside the initials that keep coming back. Next time, there's a tiny arrow pointing to yours.}\n  flag left_wall_mark\n> right: Follow an old arrow\n  lore +small\n  say: {The arrow leads to another arrow, which leads to a wall, which has a joke scratched on it.|The arrow leads to a slit window with a view of the lake no plan admits to. Your knees disagree with the route.|You follow the arrow you know lies, to see how it lies. It lies beautifully, all the way to a view of the stars.}\n\n@event gargoyle_passage_offering_dish\ntime: evening\ntitle: The Gargoyle's Dish\nplace: gargoyle_passage\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: coin\ntags: magic life\nscene: A weathered gargoyle on a moonlit parapet with a dish worn into its stone paw, holding a button, a biscuit and a bent key.\ntext: {The third gargoyle has a dish worn into its paw, and in the dish are offerings, a button, a biscuit, a bent key.|Someone has left the gargoyle a single grape, peeled.|The gargoyle looks at you the way a landlord looks at the first of the month.} {Tonight it looks hungry.|Tonight it looks smug.|{season == \"summer\": The stone is still warm from the day.|Rain runs out of its mouth in a steady complaint.}}\n> left: Leave a few crowns in the dish\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: {By morning the crowns are gone, and so is the biscuit. Nobody admits to either.|The crowns clink in. The gargoyle's expression doesn't change, but the wind drops.|You pay the gargoyle its crowns, like rent. The night is calm, and the stair doesn't move once on your way back.}\n> right: Borrow the biscuit\n  alignment -tiny\n  chance even:\n    mood +small\n    health +tiny\n    say: {It's a very good biscuit. Somebody's mother made it. You feel a little bad, and then less.|Shortbread, still crisp. The gargoyle allows it, this once.|You borrow the peeled grape. The gargoyle's look follows you down the stair, but the grape is excellent.}\n  else:\n    mood -small\n    say: {It's made of stone. The gargoyle's joke, and it's been waiting a long time to tell it.|The wind gets up the moment you take it, and stays up all night, rattling your window.|You take the grape, and the gargoyle collects. Your boots are full of rainwater by the bottom of the stair.}\n\n@event gargoyle_passage_parapet_cards\ntitle: Cards on the Parapet\nplace: gargoyle_passage\nslot: midnight\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: cards\ntags: social\ncast:\n  here: present(place) where tagged(here, peer)\nscene: Two students playing cards by lantern-light on a high castle parapet, a pot of buttons between them and the lake far below.\ntext: {On the parapet, by lantern-light, {here} is dealing cards for buttons, and crowns for anyone who dares.|{here} has a new deck, and the pot of buttons has grown.|{here} deals you in before you've sat down.} {The wind keeps trying to take the jack.|A pigeon watches the game with too much interest.|{final_year: \"One more for the road,\" {they} say{s}.|The lake below holds the lantern perfectly still.}}\n> left: Play a hand\n  when: gold >= 8\n  challenge deceive apprentice+ wild:\n    win:\n      gold +tiny\n      mood +small\n      say: {cast_as(here, corvin): {\"Well, look at you,\" Corvin says, paying out. \"Who taught you that? Don't say me.\"|\"Remind me never to owe you money,\" Corvin tells the pigeon, and pays you in buttons and, grudgingly, crowns.|Corvin deals you in, loses, and laughs. \"You're getting good. I'll have to start cheating properly.\"}|{You win the pot, buttons and a few actual crowns.|You bluff with nothing and the wind turns your card over at the right moment. You take the buttons, and the crowns under them, with dignity.|You're dealt in before you sit and win before you've warmed up. The pot is mostly buttons, and some of it is crowns.}}\n    lose:\n      gold -small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {cast_as(here, corvin): {Corvin scoops the pot and winces. \"Saint, you're the worst liar I love playing.\" He gives you your buttons back, at least.|\"Never bluff a man who owes three people money,\" Corvin says, kindly.|Corvin deals from the bottom and you know it and can't prove it. You lose with a smile, which annoys him.}|{You lose your buttons, then some crowns, then the will to bluff.|The wind takes your best card over the parapet. It was the only one you had.|You lose the hand to a card the pigeon was standing on. The pigeon will not be questioned.}}\n> right: Watch the lake instead\n  mood +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  rel here +tiny\n  say: {cast_as(here, corvin): {\"Saint,\" Corvin says, not looking up, \"you'll never be rich.\" He passes you his scarf.|Corvin deals you out without a word and tosses you his scarf when the wind picks up.|Corvin deals you out without asking and hands you his scarf before the wind does. \"Lake's nice tonight,\" he says. It is.}|{You sit with your back to the stone and watch the dark water while the cards slap behind you. It's cold, and peaceful.|You count the lights in the village until the game's over.|You watch the lake hold the lantern still. Behind you, someone wins, and someone else blames the pigeon.}}\n\n# ---------------- The Seventh Tower ----------------\n\n@event seventh_tower_stair_count\ntitle: The Stair That Changes\nplace: seventh_tower\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: tower\ntags: mystery\nscene: A narrow spiral stair inside a dusty stone tower, a single candle climbing it, moonlight falling through slit windows.\ntext: {The seventh tower's stair never has the same number of steps twice. Scholars count it to learn how the castle bends; everyone else says it lets you up faster if you don't look.|Back on the stair that won't keep its count. You've started chalking each total on the wall; no two agree.|You've stopped expecting the stair to agree with you. You count anyway; it's a kind of manners.} {Dust lies thick on every step but yours.|The moon follows you from window to window.|{season == \"winter\": The cold comes up through your shoes.|Your candle leans towards the top, like a dog on a lead.}}\n> left: Count every step\n  lore +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {You lose count twice and get two answers, both certain, both wrong.|Up, one number; down, one more. You don't stay to find the extra step.|{final_year and flag(counted_seventh_stair): The count matches your very first climb. You can't say why that frightens you.|You count a step that isn't there, and your foot believes you.}}\n  flag counted_seventh_stair\n> right: Climb without counting\n  mana +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You climb by feel, in the dark between windows. The stair seems to like that better.|You stop counting and the climb is suddenly short, as if the tower was in a hurry too.|You climb without counting, candle leaning ahead. You're at the top before you've thought about it, and it feels allowed.}\n\n@event seventh_tower_dust_room\ntime: evening\ntitle: Dust and Moonlight\nplace: seventh_tower\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: moon\ntags: mystery\nscene: A bare round room at the top of a tower, thick dust on the floor marked by footprints, moonlight slanting through one window onto a star-shaped hollow in the wall.\ntext: {At the top is one room of dust and moonlight, with a star-shaped hollow in the wall. The footprints in the dust aren't yours.|More footprints in the dust; some are yours now.|The room keeps your footprints like a guest book.} {The moon sits in the window as if it lives here.|The hollow in the wall is darker than the night.|A moth beats at the glass from outside, trying to get in.}\n> left: Sit in the moonlight\n  mana +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {The light is cold and kind. You sit until your shadow forgets which way to fall.|You sit where the moon is brightest. It feels like being looked at by someone very old and very tired.|You sit with the moth tapping at the glass and the moon in the window. Nothing happens, very deeply, for an hour.}\n> right: Sweep away your footprints\n  guile +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You sweep your way out backwards with your sleeve. The dust settles behind you, and then, you'd swear, rearranges itself.|You cough your way to the door. The other footprints you leave alone. They seem to want to stay.|You sweep your prints away and leave the others. By the next visit, yours are back, in the same places.}\n\n@event seventh_tower_lake_window\ntitle: The Window Over the Lake\nplace: seventh_tower\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: wave\ntags: mystery danger\nscene: A small arched window high in a tower, its latch half open, the Mirror Lake lying still and silver far below.\ntext: {The tower's one window looks down on the Mirror Lake, and the latch isn't locked. They say if you open it, you can hear the lake.|The latch moves more easily than last time, as if someone else has been practising.|The window. You've stood here often enough that your hand finds the latch before you decide anything.} {The lake is so still it looks painted.|There's a light on the water that isn't the moon's.|{season == \"winter\": The lake is grey and hard.|Mist lies on the water.}}\n> left: Open it and listen\n  challenge research using arcana journeyman- wild:\n    win:\n      mana +small\n      lore +tiny\n      say: {You hear water, and under it a sound too low to be wind. You decide it was the pipes, and don't quite believe yourself.|Nothing but wind and water. You stay until you're sure, and you're never quite sure.|You open it without deciding to. The lake is quiet, and then not, and then quiet again, as if it changed its mind about you.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      health -tiny\n      say: {The cold comes in and won't leave. For days you hear dripping in dry rooms.|The lake is silent, entirely, the way a room goes silent when you walk in on a conversation.|The window opens on its own before your hand gets there. You shut it fast, and the latch is cold for a week.}\n> right: Leave it latched\n  mood -tiny\n  health +tiny\n  say: {You leave the latch alone and go down to bed, and lie awake wondering what you didn't hear.|You put your palm flat on the cold glass instead. The lake does nothing. It's very good at that.|You leave it latched and rest your hand on the latch a while. It feels like keeping a promise to someone.}\n"},{"path":"content/events/pools/clubs_jobs.grim","text":"# Baseline pools (phase 3, step 1b): club practice and job shifts.\n# Clubs: duelling, lumen_ledger, verdant, cartographers. Jobs: library_assistant, tavern, apothecary_clerk, broom_courier.\n# Each track: three repeatable routines (two at rank 1, one at rank 2) and one rank-2 milestone.\n\n# ---------------- Duelling Circle (track: duelling) ----------------\n\n@event duelling_ward_line\ntitle: The Ward Line\ntrack: duelling\nrank: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: shield\ntags: club duel\nscene: A row of students holding shimmering shields across a scorched hall while seniors lob sparks at them.\ntext: {Ward drill. Members hold shields in a line while the seniors lob stinging hexes down the hall.|You know by now which senior aims low, and which one apologises.|You could hold a shield in your sleep, and one night you did.} {A dummy at the end of the line has a shield of its own, out of pity.|Someone has chalked a tally of scorched floorboards. It is long.|A first-year holds the shield upside down and does surprisingly well.}\n> left: Take the front of the line\n  challenge combat apprentice+ steady:\n    win:\n      perform duelling +small\n      vigor +small\n      say: {Your shield takes everything and hums about it afterwards.|Hexes splash off your ward like rain off a good hat.|You hold until the bell. {around(lyra): Lyra|The senior on the whistle} writes something down, which is either praise or a list of your faults.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      perform duelling +tiny\n      say: {A hex finds the gap by your elbow. Your arm fizzes until supper.|Your ward folds like wet paper. The senior apologises, mostly to the floor.|You hold the front half asleep, as usual, and a hex wakes you. Your arm fizzes until supper, and the first-year beside you holds.}\n> right: Hold the back row tonight\n  arcana +small\n  perform duelling -tiny\n  say: {From the back you watch how the good shields bend instead of break. Nobody watches you.|You spend the drill studying the front row's mistakes, which feels useful and looks like hiding.|Safe at the back, you learn a great deal about the ceiling.}\n> up[ward]: Hold the line with a real ward\n  perform duelling +small\n  arcana +small\n  say: {You raise a proper ward, the one from Umbral Theory, and the seniors' hexes slide off it like rain off glass. {around(lyra): Lyra stops the drill to look.|The seniors stop the drill to look.}|Your ward holds the whole line's flank. Your arm aches from the mana, not the hexes.|You raise the real ward without thinking, the way you hold a shield in your sleep. The seniors give up hexing your end of the line.}\n\n@event duelling_dummy_mending\ntitle: Mending the Dummies\ntrack: duelling\nrank: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: duel\ntags: club\nscene: A student alone in a duelling hall at night, stitching straw back into a battered practice dummy.\ntext: {After practice someone has to mend the dummies, and tonight the rota says it's you.|Mending night again, and the rota has your name on it.|You know every dummy here by its scars. They look at you with something like trust.} {The tall dummy is missing an arm. Someone always takes the arm.|One dummy is now mostly scorch mark.|Someone has painted a new name on a dummy's chest, and a grudge beneath it.}\n> left: Stay late and stitch them up\n  mood -small\n  perform duelling +small\n  rank_xp duelling +tiny\n  say: {By midnight every dummy is whole and you have straw in places straw should not be.|You sew one dummy's arm on slightly backwards. It looks more dangerous for it.|The hall is quiet, the needle is steady, and for an hour nobody is shouting about footwork.}\n> right: Leave them for the morning\n  mood +tiny\n  perform duelling -tiny\n  say: {In the morning the dummies are still in pieces, and so is the seniors' patience.|You slip out early. Someone finds the dummies and puts your name on next week's rota, underlined twice.|You leave them. In the morning the dummy with the new name has been mended by someone else, with your name painted under the grudge.}\n\n@event duelling_refereeing_lyra\ntitle: Refereeing Lyra\ntrack: duelling\nrank: 2\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nactor: lyra\nwhen: around(lyra)\nart: duel\ntags: club duel\nscene: A student with a whistle watching a noble duellist in a chalk circle, her opponent's shield just failing.\ntext: {Officers referee, so tonight you hold the whistle while Lyra duels a second-year.|You know the circle's chalk by heart now, and Lyra's footwork better.|{rel(lyra) >= 12: Lyra no longer asks who is refereeing. She looks for you.|The whistle has a dent in it from some earlier, unrecorded argument.}} {Her last spell landed a breath after the bell.|She stepped out of the circle by a boot's width, and knows it.|Her last hex went in after her opponent had called the yield.} {final_year: One of the last bouts you will call for her.|}\n> left: Call it against her\n  rel lyra -small\n  alignment +tiny\n  perform duelling +small\n  rank_xp duelling +small\n  say: {\"Noted,\" Lyra says, in a voice that could chill wine. Next day her feet are perfect.|Lyra stares at you, then shakes her opponent's hand. Later a cup of tea appears on your bench.|The hall goes silent. Lyra bows to the second-year, very correctly, and to you, slightly less.}\n> right: Let it stand\n  rel lyra +tiny\n  alignment -tiny\n  perform duelling -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {Lyra wins. The second-year looks at you, and you find something interesting about your whistle.|Nobody objects. Lyra does not thank you; if anything, she looks a little disappointed in you.|The bout goes in the book as hers. It sits there all week like a stone in your shoe.}\n\n@event duelling_running_practice\ntitle: Running Practice\ntrack: duelling\nrank: 2\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nweight: 20\nart: duel\ntags: club duel\nscene: A note pinned to a duelling hall door, a crowd of students waiting inside in chalk circles.\ntext: A note is pinned to the hall door in Lyra's sharp hand: \"Called home. You are running practice. Footwork first. Do not let anyone set the ceiling on fire again.\" The whole Circle turns to see what you will say.\n> left: Run it exactly her way\n  perform duelling +small\n  rank_xp duelling +medium\n  rel lyra +small\n  say: Footwork, then shields, then footwork again. Nobody sets the ceiling on fire. Lyra reads your practice log on her return and says \"adequate\", then reads it again when she thinks you've gone.\n> right: Run it your own way\n  rank_xp duelling +medium\n  renown +small\n  mood +small\n  say: You turn the footwork drill into a game of tag with stinging hexes. The ceiling only smoulders. A week later Lyra runs the same game and calls it her idea, which is the highest praise she has.\n\n# ---------------- The Lumen Ledger (track: lumen_ledger) ----------------\n\n@event lumen_ledger_letters_box\ntitle: The Letters Box\ntrack: lumen_ledger\nrank: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: letter\ntags: club\nscene: A battered wooden box under a library staircase, overflowing with folded letters and one sock.\ntext: {The letters box is full. Stringers sort it, Nessa says, having no dignity yet to lose.|Sorting day. A regular correspondent has started numbering their letters.|Under the stairs the box has burst its lid. You know the regulars by their handwriting now.} {Porridge complaints, a poem about the lake and a sock.|A furious letter about the crossword, in a professor's hand.|An anonymous tip in lemon juice, noticed only because it smells nice.}\n> left: Print the angriest letter\n  perform lumen_ledger +small\n  renown +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {It runs on page four. By breakfast several people are angry about the angry letter, which Nessa calls \"engagement\".|The letter runs. Its author glares at you over the porridge, as if you had written it.|You print the lemon-juice tip, warmed over a candle. Half the castle holds the page to the fire. Nessa calls it the best-selling issue of term.}\n> right: Answer them all by hand\n  charm +small\n  mood -small\n  perform lumen_ledger +tiny\n  say: {You write back to every one of them, even the ones that weren't really letters. Your hand cramps; somewhere in the castle a lonely first-year gets a reply.|A pile of replies, all polite. Nessa calls it \"a waste of a perfectly good grudge\", then borrows your phrasing.|You write back to every regular, by number. The numbering correspondent replies with a number of their own, and a thank-you.}\n\n@event lumen_ledger_courtyard_quotes\ntitle: A Word from the Courtyard\ntrack: lumen_ledger\nrank: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: paper\ntags: club social\nscene: A student with a notebook stopping passers-by in a busy courtyard.\ntext: {Every issue needs a word from the courtyard. One question, many opinions.|Word has got round the courtyard that the Ledger is asking questions.|Half the courtyard now takes the long way round your notebook.} This week: {is the soup getting thinner?|who will win the Spring Tournament?|is the lake louder lately?} {season == \"winter\": Everyone is hurrying indoors.|{Some second-years pretend not to see you.|A passing professor looks very quotable.|A prefect makes a detour to avoid you, which is a quote of a sort.}}\n> left: Stop strangers and ask\n  challenge negotiate apprentice:\n    win:\n      perform lumen_ledger +small\n      charm +small\n      say: {A gardener, a prefect and a very small first-year give you the best quotes of the term. The first-year's is unprintable, and perfect.|You get a quote so good Nessa moves it to the front page and pretends it was her idea to ask.|You ask the question to the people who take the long way round. They answer, to get rid of you, and the answers are the best yet.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      perform lumen_ledger +tiny\n      say: {Everyone is busy, cold or suddenly deaf. You come back with \"no comment\" in five different tones of voice.|A fourth-year answers at length, entirely about himself.|Everyone takes the long way round you. You get one quote, from the prefect, and it is \"No.\"}\n> right: Just quote your friends\n  mood +tiny\n  perform lumen_ledger -tiny\n  say: {Every opinion comes from someone you had lunch with. Nessa reads them and says, \"Interesting. Your friends are very consistent.\"|It's quick. It's also the courtyard as represented by one person in several scarves.|You quote your friends again. Nessa runs it under a headline about the same four people, and it's somehow popular.}\n\n@event lumen_ledger_red_ink\ntitle: Red Ink\ntrack: lumen_ledger\nrank: 2\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nactor: nessa\nart: scroll\ntags: club\nscene: A cramped office papered with old front pages, an editor slashing a manuscript with a red quill.\ntext: {Nessa edits your story with a red quill and no mercy.|Nessa has your draft, and this time the red quill hesitates once before it moves.|Edit night. You have learned to bring two copies, one for Nessa to kill.} \"{Kill your darlings. Bury them. Page six.|Lead's buried. Dig it up. Headline it.|Good. Too long. Shorter. Shorter than that.}\" She has cut your favourite paragraph.\n> left: Fight for the paragraph\n  challenge negotiate vs nessa.charm:\n    win:\n      perform lumen_ledger +small\n      rank_xp lumen_ledger +small\n      say: {Nessa reads it again, out loud, and puts it back. \"Fine. Stays. Don't get used to it.\"|You win the paragraph. Nessa makes you cut a different one, as a tax.|You fight for it, and it gets shorter, and shorter than that, and it stays. Nessa calls it \"a paragraph now\".}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      rel nessa -tiny\n      say: {\"No,\" says Nessa, and that is the whole argument.|She listens to your case with great interest, then cuts the paragraph anyway, plus the one you defended it with.|You fight with your second copy ready. Nessa cuts the paragraph from both.}\n> right: Let her cut it\n  perform lumen_ledger +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {It reads better without it. You will never forgive her.|The story runs, tighter and less yours. Nessa scribbles \"good\" in the margin, a word Nessa hands out like gold.|You let her cut it, and slip it into next week's story instead. She notices, and lets it stand.}\n\n@event lumen_ledger_front_page\ntitle: Front Page\ntrack: lumen_ledger\nrank: 2\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nweight: 20\nart: paper\ntags: club\nscene: A creaking printing press at dawn spitting out newspapers, a student holding the first copy up to the light.\ntext: The press groans like a dragon and spits out the dawn edition. Your story is on the front page, under your own name. Nessa hands you a tack without looking up. \"Wall,\" she says. \"That's where front pages go.\"\n> left: Pin it to the wall yourself\n  rank_xp lumen_ledger +medium\n  perform lumen_ledger +small\n  rel nessa +small\n  say: You find a gap between a scandal from before you were born and a recipe someone printed by mistake. It looks small up there, and permanent. Nessa pretends not to watch you step back and admire it.\n> right: Send the first copy home\n  rank_xp lumen_ledger +medium\n  mood +medium\n  gold -tiny\n  say: You post it home with a note that says nothing about how long it took. The reply comes within the week: they have framed it, slightly crooked, over the kitchen fire.\n\n# ---------------- Verdant Circle (track: verdant) ----------------\n\n@event verdant_watering_rota\ntitle: The Watering Rota\ntrack: verdant\nrank: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: leaf\ntags: club nature\nscene: A student with a watering can and a huge chart in a steaming greenhouse, ferns leaning in hungrily.\ntext: {You're on the watering rota, a chart the size of a door.|The ferns have learned your footsteps and lean in.|You no longer need the chart, luckily, since a fern ate it.} {season == \"winter\": Frost outside the glass, drips down your collar inside.|{season == \"summer\": It's so hot the cacti look smug.|{The biting ferns are in a mood.|Something in the far bed grew overnight.|The moonflowers do nothing, beautifully, as they will for years.}}}\n> left: Follow the chart exactly\n  perform verdant +small\n  alchemy +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: {Warm rainwater for the ferns, cold tea for the moss, nothing at all for the cactus that bites. It takes all afternoon.|By the chart, to the drop. {around(tobias): Tobias says the plants look \"right pleased\", and they do, in a leafy way.|The plants look pleased, in a leafy way.}|You follow the chart from memory, since the fern ate it. You're right to the drop, and the fern looks unrepentant.}\n> right: Water by feel\n  challenge research using alchemy apprentice:\n    win:\n      alchemy +small\n      perform verdant +small\n      say: {You read the leaves instead of the chart. Everything perks up, and one fern makes a sound suspiciously like purring.|You guess right on every bed. {around(tobias): Tobias asks how you knew. You honestly don't.|You couldn't say how you knew.}|You water by feel and talk to the moonflowers. They still do nothing, beautifully, and the rest of the house thrives.}\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      perform verdant -tiny\n      say: {You drown a pot of sundew, and a biting fern takes a polite nip of your thumb.|The moss wanted tea, not water. It sulks visibly, and the cactus you reach past bites.|You water the moonflowers by feel and drown them by feel. They do nothing, as usual, but wetly.}\n\n@event verdant_repotting_day\ntitle: Repotting Day\ntrack: verdant\nrank: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: flower\ntags: club nature\nscene: Students in earmuffs wrestling squalling mandrakes into bigger pots on a crowded greenhouse bench.\ntext: {Repotting day. The mandrakes have outgrown their pots and have opinions about it.|Someone always takes the good earmuffs, so you bring your own now.|You know which mandrakes kick, and which only pretend to.} {Tobias's own mandrake watches from a shelf in a knitted hat, superior.|A seed tray of something blue has started to hum.|{season == \"spring\": Everything is trying to grow at once.|The glass is fogged and the benches crowded.}}\n> left: Take on the mandrakes\n  health -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  perform verdant +small\n  rank_xp verdant +tiny\n  say: {One mandrake has rooted into the bench itself. It takes a trowel, a lullaby and both hands to talk it out.|Mandrake after mandrake, each into a bigger pot. Your ears ring all the way to supper.|The biggest one goes in headfirst and comes out, somehow, the right way up. You are not sure who won.}\n> right: Take the quiet seed trays\n  alchemy +small\n  health -tiny\n  perform verdant +tiny\n  say: {Hundreds of seedlings, pricked out one by one. Your back will remember this tomorrow.|The seed trays are peaceful and endless. By dusk you have names for most of them.|You do the seed trays with your own earmuffs on, for the peace. One tray hums, and you hum back.}\n\n@event verdant_tobias_cuttings\ntitle: The Cuttings Question\ntrack: verdant\nrank: 2\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nactor: tobias\nwhen: around(tobias)\nart: leaf\ntags: club nature\nscene: A big gentle student cradling a tray of leggy cuttings, eyeing a full compost heap with dread.\ntext: {The benches are full and someone must choose which cuttings stay. Tobias can't. \"It's like choosing between goats,\" he says.|Bench day. Tobias hovers over a tray of cuttings, apologising to them in advance and glancing at you.|Tobias hands you the tray before he can change his mind. He is grateful in a way that looks like grief.} {season == \"autumn\": The frost will take the weakest soon anyway.|}\n> left: Choose, and compost the rest\n  perform verdant +small\n  rank_xp verdant +small\n  rel tobias -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You keep the strongest and compost the rest. Tobias says \"fair's fair\", then goes very quiet over the heap.|The benches breathe again. Tobias thanks you, then tells you a long story about a goat he couldn't sell. You understand it is about the cuttings.|You choose quickly, so Tobias doesn't have to watch. He takes the compost bucket himself, and you let him.}\n> right: Find room for every one\n  rel tobias +small\n  perform verdant -tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {You find room on windowsills, under benches and in a teacup. Nothing grows well, but everything grows. Tobias laughs from the belly.|\"Ma always said a crowded field still feeds you,\" Tobias says, beaming. The benches groan. So does Old Maud, when she sees.|You find room again, on the last windowsill nobody had thought of. Tobias names that one after you.}\n\n@event verdant_greenhouse_keys\ntitle: The Greenhouse Keys\ntrack: verdant\nrank: 2\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nweight: 20\nactor: tobias\nwhen: around(tobias)\nart: key\ntags: club nature\nscene: A heavy ring of old iron keys handed over in a steaming glasshouse at dusk.\ntext: At dusk Tobias holds out the iron key ring, warm from his pocket. \"Ma says you give the keys to whoever shuts the gate without being asked,\" he says. \"You always shut the gate.\" From now on, opening up and locking up are yours.\n> left: Open up at dawn\n  rank_xp verdant +medium\n  perform verdant +small\n  health -tiny\n  rel tobias +small\n  say: Dawn, the glass silver with dew, every plant turning to the light you let in. You had never noticed they all turn at once. For a moment you are the only person in the world.\n> right: Walk the night round with a lamp\n  rank_xp verdant +medium\n  alchemy +small\n  mood +small\n  say: You walk the benches with a lamp, checking latches and saying goodnight, because Tobias does and it feels rude not to. Something in the far bed says it back. You decide not to mention it.\n\n# ---------------- Midnight Cartographers (track: cartographers) ----------------\n\n@event cartographers_survey_run\ntitle: A Survey Run\ntrack: cartographers\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: map\ntags: club mystery\nscene: A student pacing a dark stone passage by candlelight, chalking marks on the wall.\ntext: {Initiates do the pacing. Corvin hands you chalk and a passage to measure before the castle changes its mind.|Your chalk is a stub now, so you mark the turns with notches.|Your stride is a unit of measurement on the master map now, and the castle seems to know it.} {The passage smells of oranges, which nobody can explain.|Behind the wall, a clock ticks that no one has ever found.|Your candle leans, very slightly, toward the left-hand turning.}\n> left: Pace it properly\n  challenge research using guile apprentice:\n    win:\n      guile +small\n      say: {Every turning chalked and counted. The passage is exactly where it was last month, which on the master map counts as a scandal.|The passage is two strides longer from this end than from the other. Corvin is delighted.|You pace it with the stride the map uses. The left turning, where your candle leans, wasn't there last week. Corvin inks it in.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: {Your chalk marks lead neatly into a wall that wasn't there when you started.|You lose count somewhere in the dark and have to admit it, which in this club is rarer than a map.|You follow the leaning candle into the left-hand turning and come out a floor below, with no stairs in between.}\n> right: Copy last month's figures\n  alignment -tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {You copy the old figures and sit in an alcove eating toffee. The castle, as if offended, moves the passage a foot.|The figures match last month's exactly. Corvin looks at them, then at you. \"Forger,\" he says, approvingly.|You copy the figures with your own stride as the unit. The castle, offended, changes your stride.}\n\n@event cartographers_lantern_patrol\ntitle: The Prefect's Lantern\ntrack: cartographers\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: lantern\ntags: club mystery\nscene: Students pressed into a stone alcove as a lantern's glow sweeps along a dark corridor.\ntext: {Every expedition has the moment a lantern comes round the corner. Tonight's has a red sash behind it.|A lantern, a red sash, footsteps. You know this part.|You can tell the prefects on patrol apart by their footsteps now.} {The alcove beside you is just big enough for one.|A loose flagstone ahead clanks if you step on it.|The tapestry here already has feet showing under it, and they aren't yours.}\n> left: Freeze in the shadows\n  challenge deceive apprentice+:\n    win:\n      guile +tiny\n      say: {The light slides over you like water off a duck. The footsteps go on.|You don't breathe for a very long time. The prefect sneezes, apologises to nobody and walks on.|You freeze beside the tapestry with the feet. The prefect sees the feet, not yours, and someone else spends Saturday scrubbing pans.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: {The lantern stops on your face. You spend Saturday scrubbing pans and inventing a reason you were inside a wall.|You talk your way out with a story about a lost cat. It costs you the night's mapping and most of your dignity.|The feet under the tapestry turn out to be the prefect's own. You both have some explaining to do.}\n> right: Go back the long way\n  guile +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {The long way is four flights of freezing stairs. You learn every one of them by heart.|You double back through the kitchens and arrive late, cold and unseen. Corvin raises a mug of milk he certainly didn't pay for.|You know these footsteps. You take the long way at exactly the prefect's pace, one corridor behind.}\n\n@event cartographers_inking_night\ntitle: Inking Night\ntrack: cartographers\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nactor: corvin\nart: map\ntags: club\nscene: Two students bent over a huge map drawn in silver ink that shifts as they watch, in a hidden candlelit room.\ntext: {Mappers ink the master map. Corvin unrolls it, silver lines shifting like breath, and passes you the pen.|Inking night. The map has redrawn a stair since your last visit, out of spite.|The map has started leaving a blank wherever you hesitate.} {\"Leave the one behind the kitchens off,\" he says. \"Not for long. For me.\"|\"The stair under the clock. Don't draw it. Don't ask why.\"|He taps a corridor. \"That one stays off the map. Humour me.\"}\n> left: Ink it anyway\n  rel corvin -small\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: {You draw it, line by honest line. Corvin watches, then laughs, a little too late. \"Saint,\" he says, without the usual smile.|It goes on the map. Corvin is quiet for the rest of the night, which on him is very loud.|You ink it, and the map leaves a blank there anyway, as if it agreed with Corvin. He looks at you and says nothing.}\n> right: Leave it off for him\n  rel corvin +small\n  say: {You leave a careful blank. Corvin squeezes your shoulder and doesn't make a joke, which is how you know it matters.|The map gets a gap and Corvin keeps his secret. You wonder, later, what it's for.|You leave the corridor off. The map closes over the gap so smoothly you'd never know. Neither would anyone else.}\n\n@event cartographers_silver_pen\ntitle: A Pen of Your Own\ntrack: cartographers\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nweight: 20\nactor: corvin\nart: map\ntags: club\nscene: A silver-nibbed pen laid on a folded map in a candlelit stone alcove.\ntext: Corvin presses a pen into your hand: silver nib, black barrel, warm from his pocket. \"Mappers carry their own,\" he says. \"And the first passage you find, you name. Club rule. I made it up, but it's a rule.\" Tonight you found one.\n> left: Name it after yourself\n  guile +small\n  mood +small\n  say: Your name goes on the master map in your own silver hand, at the mouth of a passage that smells faintly of rain. The ink shivers, then settles, as if the castle had agreed.\n> right: Name it for a club joke\n  rel corvin +small\n  say: You name it after the night's worst joke. Corvin laughs so hard a prefect two floors down stops to listen. \"Keeper material,\" he whispers, and for once he doesn't laugh after.\n\n# ---------------- Library Assistant (track: library_assistant) ----------------\n\n@event library_assistant_wandering_shelves\ntitle: The Wandering Section\ntrack: library_assistant\nrank: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: book\ntags: job study\nscene: A library aisle with an empty gap where a whole bookcase should be, a student holding a catalogue.\ntext: {The shelves rearranged themselves overnight. History is missing, and a queue is forming for it.|The shelves have wandered, this time the {poetry|herbals|atlases}. You have brought string to mark the way.|The head librarian simply hands you the catalogue now and points upward.} {Footnote sits where the section used to be.|A first-year swears she saw it go upstairs.|There is a faint trail in the dust, as if something heavy had tiptoed.}\n> left: Hunt it down by catalogue\n  wage\n  challenge research apprentice+ wild:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      perform library_assistant +small\n      say: {You find it on the fifth floor, wedged between the cookery books, looking innocent.|You track it by its shelfmarks to a landing that wasn't there yesterday. The queue cheers, quietly, because it's a library.|You follow the tiptoe trail to the atlases, three floors up. They are pretending to be geography.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      perform library_assistant -tiny\n      say: {You search until closing. In the morning the section is back where it started, faintly smug.|You find the wrong section, twice. The queue disperses with sighs you will hear in your sleep.|You follow the trail in the dust to a blank wall. The trail continues on the other side, presumably.}\n> right: Put up a sign and wait\n  wage\n  mood +tiny\n  perform library_assistant -tiny\n  say: {\"Back shortly,\" says your sign. The section returns in the afternoon. The queue does not.|You sit at the desk and wait. A student asks whether the sign means you or the books.|You put up the sign and the catalogue, open at the right page. The section comes back to see what you wrote about it.}\n\n@event library_assistant_hush_round\ntitle: The Hush Round\ntrack: library_assistant\nrank: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: candle\ntags: job\nscene: A student assistant with a lamp walking between study tables in a dim library, one finger to her lips.\ntext: {Hush round. One lap of the reading room, a lamp, a finger to your lips.|The reading room has learned the sound of your lamp. Whispers stop a table ahead of you.|The regulars hear your step and straighten up before you reach them.} {A table of second-years is giggling.|An old scholar snores on his notes, in a scholarly key.|Somebody's quill is scratching so loudly it echoes.} {final_year: Soon this will be someone else's lap.|}\n> left: Hush them firmly\n  wage\n  mood -tiny\n  perform library_assistant +small\n  rank_xp library_assistant +tiny\n  say: {One look and the room falls silent. Somewhere a bookcase seems to approve.|You hush them. They glare. In the morning one of them leaves a note on the desk: \"sorry\", in very small writing.|You hush the scratching quill. Its owner hears your step before you arrive and is already writing quietly.}\n> right: Let them be\n  wage\n  mood +tiny\n  perform library_assistant -tiny\n  say: {You let it go. The noise rises, the head librarian appears from nowhere, and looks at you, not at them.|You leave them to it. For a while the murmur is the nicest sound in the building.|You let them be. The regulars straighten up anyway when you pass, out of habit, and the noise sorts itself out.}\n\n@event library_assistant_reference_desk\ntitle: The Reference Desk\ntrack: library_assistant\nrank: 2\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: scroll\ntags: job study\nscene: A student behind a reference desk facing a line of readers, one of them holding up a green ribbon.\ntext: {Clerks take the reference desk alone. The first question arrives before you sit down.|The desk has a worn patch now, exactly where your elbows go.|You keep a list of the strangest questions now, and it is long.} {A professor wants \"the green book, about this thick, I read it in my youth\".|A first-year wants the book their professor \"definitely mentioned\".|A visiting scholar wants the one book that explains why bees hum in thirds, by tonight.}\n> left: Dig until you find it\n  wage\n  challenge research journeyman+:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      perform library_assistant +small\n      rank_xp library_assistant +tiny\n      say: {You find it in the end, in the wrong section, under a cat. The reader looks at you as if you had worked a miracle, and you have.|Three ladders, two catalogues and an educated guess. It is the right book.|There's no such book. You find three that explain it between them, by tonight. The scholar adds your name to the acknowledgements.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      health -tiny\n      say: {Hours later you admit defeat. The book, it turns out, was on the desk all along, propping up the inkpot.|You never find it. You dream of card catalogues all night.|The bees remain unexplained. You add the question to your list, at the top.}\n> right: Send them off with a good guess\n  wage\n  mood +tiny\n  perform library_assistant -tiny\n  say: {You point confidently at a staircase. They come back an hour later with a different book, which they say they liked better.|\"Try the fourth floor.\" They do. You hope the fourth floor helps.|You send them to the apiary. They come back with a bee and no book, delighted.}\n\n@event library_assistant_stack_keys\ntitle: The Stack Keys\ntrack: library_assistant\nrank: 2\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nweight: 20\nart: key\ntags: job study\nscene: An old librarian handing a brass ring of keys to a student among towering shelves at closing time.\ntext: At closing the head librarian presses a brass ring of keys into your hand: the upper stacks, the map drawers, the ladder chains. Not the Restricted Section. \"Lock up. The shelves settle after dark. Let them.\"\n> left: Do the closing round alone\n  wage\n  rank_xp library_assistant +medium\n  lore +small\n  mood +small\n  say: You walk every floor with the keys chiming. The shelves shift and creak and settle, like an old house going to sleep, and this time you are the one who gets to hear it.\n> right: Let Footnote come along\n  wage\n  rank_xp library_assistant +medium\n  mood +medium\n  say: Footnote pads ahead of you up every stair, checking each lock before you do. At the top he sits in the window and lets you scratch his ears, once. You suspect that was the real promotion.\n\n# ---------------- Server at the Gilded Newt (track: tavern) ----------------\n\n@event tavern_cider_rush\ntitle: The Cider Rush\ntrack: tavern\nrank: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: cup\ntags: job money\nscene: A crowded low-beamed tavern, a server balancing a tray of steaming mugs through the press of bodies.\ntext: {The rush at the Gilded Newt. A dozen orders shouted at once, and a tray that holds fewer.|The door won't stay shut. You have started remembering faces by their drink, which works until two people want spiced cider.|The regulars just point now, and you know what the points mean.} {A barge crew has come in soaked and thirsty.|The card table is arguing about the rules again.|{season == \"winter\": Snow is melting off every coat in the room.|Half the village is on the step.}}\n> left: Keep every order in your head\n  wage\n  challenge research apprentice:\n    win:\n      gold +tiny\n      perform tavern +small\n      say: {Every mug to the right hand. The whole room tips like it's payday.|You don't miss once. The landlady, who remembers every tab to the penny, gives you a look of professional respect.|The regulars point, and you deliver. The door won't stay shut, and nobody waits more than a minute.}\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      perform tavern -tiny\n      say: {The spiced cider goes to the wrong table. The right table has opinions. The wrong table drinks it anyway.|You mix up two orders and three tempers.|Half the village points at once. You misread two points and a wave.}\n> right: Write every order down\n  wage\n  perform tavern +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {Slow but sure. Nobody gets the wrong drink; nobody tips the slow server either.|Your order pad fills with a shorthand only you can read. By closing, you can't either.|You write every order, even the points. By closing you have a full record of the village's habits.}\n\n@event tavern_regulars_corner\ntitle: The Regulars' Corner\ntrack: tavern\nrank: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: group\ntags: job social\nscene: An old barge-man, a herb-wife from the lane and a quiet traveller at a fireside table, beckoning a server over.\ntext: {The regulars' corner by the fire wants you to sit for a minute. The fire is warm, and the minute is tempting.|The regulars' corner has saved you a stool now, with a cushion on it.|The regulars wave you over by name. They want to know how your exams went, all of them, in order.} {The old barge-man is telling the eel story again.|The herb-wife from the lane wants her pie warmed \"exactly as before\".|A quiet traveller is listening to everything.}\n> left: Sit with them a while\n  wage\n  charm +small\n  gold +tiny\n  perform tavern -tiny\n  say: {You hear the latest instalment of everyone's story. They tip you for listening, which is a better rate than serving.|You sit for one minute and stay for ten. The landlady clears her throat across the room like a falling tree.|You tell them about your exams, all of them, in order. The quiet traveller listens hardest and tips best.}\n> right: Keep the trays moving\n  wage\n  perform tavern +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You wave and keep going. The corner toasts your back. The eel story will keep.|You work the room like weather. The corner calls you \"the brisk one\" now, which is not a compliment.|You keep moving. The quiet traveller watches you all evening, and leaves without a word, and a large tip.}\n\n@event tavern_tab_book\ntitle: The Tab Book\ntrack: tavern\nrank: 2\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\ncast:\n  regular: present(place) where not tagged(regular, faculty)\nart: coin\ntags: job money\nscene: A ledger open on a tavern bar, a long column of figures, a hopeful customer at the far end.\ntext: {Barkeeps keep the tab book, and the tab book says {regular} owes the Newt rather a lot.|The tab book has grown a second volume, and {regular} has a page in it.|{regular} is at the end of the bar, and so, in the book, is a long column of figures.} {{They} smile{s} at you hopefully.|{They} {are} pretending to be fascinated by the fire.|{They} {are} counting coins that clearly won't be enough.}\n> left: Call the tab in\n  wage\n  gold +tiny\n  perform tavern +small\n  rel regular -small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {{regular} pays, slowly, coin by coin, as if each one were a friend leaving. The landlady slides you a few crowns for the collecting.|You call it in. {regular} pays half and an apology. The landlady counts both, and gives you a few crowns of it for your trouble.|You call it in. {regular} pays in coins and a promise, and the landlady accepts the coins. You get a share of those.}\n> right: Let it ride another week\n  wage\n  rel regular +small\n  perform tavern -tiny\n  say: {{regular} looks at you as if you had hung the moon. The landlady looks at you as if you had lost it.|Another week. The column grows, and so, oddly, does {regular}'s loyalty.|You let it ride and add a line to the long column. {regular} buys you a drink with money {they} clearly owe the Newt.}\n\n@event tavern_behind_the_bar\ntitle: Behind the Bar\ntrack: tavern\nrank: 2\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nweight: 20\nart: cup\ntags: job\nscene: A landlady setting a heavy cellar key on a polished tavern bar, the hearth fire glowing behind her.\ntext: The landlady sets the cellar key on the bar between you. \"You pour from tonight,\" she says. \"Barkeeps feed the fire, too. It hasn't gone out in my lifetime, nor my mother's. Don't be the one.\" The hearth crackles, as if listening.\n> left: Bank the fire at closing\n  wage\n  rank_xp tavern +medium\n  perform tavern +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: You stay after the last song, banking the coals the way she showed you, slow and careful as tucking in a child. In the morning it is still burning. You check twice anyway.\n> right: Pour the staff a round\n  wage\n  rank_xp tavern +medium\n  mood +small\n  gold -tiny\n  say: You pour your first round for the cooks and the pot-boy. The landlady takes hers without a word, then taps the bar twice, which apparently means \"well poured\" in a language older than the Newt.\n\n# ---------------- Apothecary Clerk (track: apothecary_clerk) ----------------\n\n@event apothecary_clerk_counting_drops\ntitle: Counting Drops\ntrack: apothecary_clerk\nrank: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: bottle\ntags: job\nscene: A student squinting at a glass dropper over a tiny vial at an apothecary counter, a wall of labelled drawers behind.\ntext: {Old Pell's first rule is to count every drop aloud. Today the counter is lined with tinctures waiting to be measured.|Drop-counting. Your tongue knows the numbers before your eyes do.|Counting drops. Old Pell says you count like a metronome now, which from him is a sonnet.} {A customer is waiting, tapping a coin on the glass.|One of the daughters is humming, which throws off your count.|Today's tincture is the colour of a bruise and smells of violets.}\n> left: Count aloud, every drop\n  wage\n  perform apothecary_clerk +small\n  alchemy +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: {Every vial exact. Anyone in the shop has now heard you count higher than anyone should.|Aloud, drop by drop. Old Pell, passing, nods once without breaking stride.|You count the violet one aloud in perfect time. Pell, passing, nods in time too.}\n> right: Count by eye, faster\n  wage\n  challenge brew apprentice wild:\n    win:\n      perform apothecary_clerk +small\n      gold +tiny\n      say: {Quick and true. You clear the counter before the tea goes cold, and a satisfied customer leaves a coin.|By eye, and spot on. One of the daughters raises an eyebrow and says nothing to her father.|You count by eye in your metronome rhythm. Pell checks one vial, finds it perfect, and stops checking.}\n    lose:\n      perform apothecary_clerk -small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {One drop over. Old Pell can tell from across the shop. He tips the vial out and makes you count the next one aloud, twice.|You overshoot. The tincture turns from violet to a colour with no name, and Pell sighs like a closing door.|You go by eye and the violet one goes the colour of a worse bruise. Pell makes you count the rest aloud, as punishment and as lesson.}\n\n@event apothecary_clerk_drawer_check\ntitle: The Thousand Drawers\ntrack: apothecary_clerk\nrank: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: potion\ntags: job mystery\nscene: A wall of tiny labelled wooden drawers, one of them rattling on its own.\ntext: {Each shift a clerk checks a row of drawers against the stock book. Today's row starts with one that is stuck.|A different row today, and the same feeling that something in it is awake.|Your row for the week. You have learned that the wrong drawer is never just the wrong drawer.} {One is labelled in a hand that isn't Pell's.|One is rattling, very gently, as if politely.|One smells of snow, in {season == \"winter\": a shop this warm|the wrong season entirely}.}\n> left: Open the odd drawer\n  wage\n  challenge research using alchemy apprentice wild:\n    win:\n      alchemy +small\n      perform apothecary_clerk +small\n      say: {Inside, a nest of dried moonpetals that belong on the top row. You move them, and the drawer stops sulking.|You coax it open and find the missing powdered newt. Pell crosses something off a list he has kept for a year.|Inside is a little snow that hasn't melted, and a note in Pell's hand: \"Don't.\" You close it. Pell thanks you, oddly sincerely.}\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {The drawer opens with a sneeze of pepperwort. You sneeze until closing.|Something small and grey bolts out of the drawer and into another, and you never find out which.|The snow-smelling drawer is full of cold. It gets out, and the shop is frosted until noon.}\n> right: Sign it off unchecked\n  wage\n  alignment -tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  perform apothecary_clerk -tiny\n  say: {You tick the row and move on. Behind you a drawer rattles, as if to say it noticed.|\"Checked,\" you write. Pell reads it, opens one drawer at random, and closes it again with a look.|You sign off the row, cold drawer and all. Pell checks that one himself, as if he'd expected you to.}\n\n@event apothecary_clerk_counter_advice\ntitle: Asking the Dispenser\ntrack: apothecary_clerk\nrank: 2\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: potion\ntags: job\nscene: A worried customer leaning over an apothecary counter, a student behind it consulting a heavy recipe book.\ntext: {Dispensers advise, and today a customer asks you instead of Pell.|Pell has moved your stool to the end of the counter, where the customers come.|You know the regular coughs by sound now, before anyone opens the door.} {A farmer has a cough that sounds like a gate.|A nervous student wants something for courage before exams.|A barge-hand has a back that hasn't straightened since autumn.}\n> left: Prescribe it yourself\n  wage\n  challenge brew journeyman:\n    win:\n      perform apothecary_clerk +small\n      rank_xp apothecary_clerk +small\n      say: {You mix it, label it and explain the dose twice. A week later they come back just to say it worked.|Your remedy, your writing on the bottle. Pell reads the label, reaches for his pen, and leaves it as it is.|You know the barge-hand's back from the sound of the door. The liniment is ready before they reach the counter.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      perform apothecary_clerk -tiny\n      say: {It helps a little, then not at all. They come back, and this time they ask for Pell.|Pell takes the bottle from your hand, adds one thing and gives it back. He doesn't say a word, which is worse.|You recommend the liniment for the barge-hand's back. It's the wrong one, and the barge-hand smells of it for a week.}\n> right: Fetch one of the daughters\n  wage\n  alchemy +small\n  perform apothecary_clerk -tiny\n  say: {The elder daughter takes over and talks the customer down like a nervous horse. You watch and write it all down.|The younger daughter mixes it in half the time you would have. She lets you hold the funnel.|Both daughters come, and argue the case across you. You learn twice as much and get the cure from neither.}\n\n@event apothecary_clerk_labelling_pen\ntitle: The Labelling Pen\ntrack: apothecary_clerk\nrank: 2\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nweight: 20\nart: feather\ntags: job\nscene: An old apothecary handing over a well-worn pen beside a stack of fresh blank labels.\ntext: Old Pell is very proud of his labelling. So it means something when he puts his own pen in your hand, its nib worn to his slant, and slides a stack of blank labels across the counter. \"New stock,\" he says. \"Your hand from now on. Neat, mind.\"\n> left: Label the new drawers yourself\n  wage\n  rank_xp apothecary_clerk +medium\n  alchemy +small\n  perform apothecary_clerk +small\n  say: You write every label slowly, in your best hand. Pell inspects them through his spectacles one by one and changes not a single letter. His daughters notice before you do.\n> right: Ask him to watch the first one\n  wage\n  rank_xp apothecary_clerk +medium\n  mood +medium\n  say: He watches. Your first label wobbles at the end. Pell takes the pen, adds a tiny flourish to your wobble so that it looks deliberate, and returns the pen for good.\n\n# ---------------- Broom Courier (track: broom_courier) ----------------\n\n@event broom_courier_morning_crossing\ntitle: The Lake Crossing\ntrack: broom_courier\nrank: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: broom\ntags: job\nscene: A courier on a broom skimming low over a mirror-still lake at dawn, a mail sack over one shoulder.\ntext: {The dispatch bell rings. The village sack is ready, and the quickest way is over the lake.|You have started timing yourself against the gulls.|The lake knows your shadow by now.} {season == \"winter\": Ice fog sits on the water like a lid.|{season == \"summer\": The water is so still you fly over your own reflection.|{A headwind is coming off the hills.|Gulls are holding a meeting on your route.|The far shore is lost in mist.}}}\n> left: Fly straight across\n  wage\n  challenge feat apprentice wild:\n    win:\n      perform broom_courier +small\n      gold +tiny\n      say: {Straight as a thrown stone. You're early, and the village postmistress tips early couriers.|You skim the water so low your boots leave a wake. Nobody sees, which is a shame.|You fly straight into the mist, by memory. The far shore appears exactly where you expected it.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      perform broom_courier -tiny\n      say: {A gust drops you into the shallows. The sack stays dry; you do not.|The far shore vanishes and you fly a slow circle round a buoy. You arrive late and very damp.|The mist takes the far shore and your sense of direction with it. You arrive at the right village from the wrong side.}\n> right: Take the long way round\n  wage\n  perform track -tiny\n  say: {Round the shore, safe and slow. The dispatcher checks the clock and sighs.|The long way. You wave to a fisherman, who does not wave back.|The long way, round the mist. The fisherman waves, for the first time. You're so surprised you nearly fall off.}\n\n@event broom_courier_broom_scramble\ntitle: The Broom Scramble\ntrack: broom_courier\nrank: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: broom\ntags: job\nscene: A draughty loft rack of battered brooms, couriers jostling to grab the least battered one.\ntext: {Only one loft broom is in a good state, and every courier on shift wants it.|You have started arriving early for the brooms, and so has everyone else.|You could pick the good broom blindfold, by the smell of its varnish.} {What's left is one that pulls left.|What's left sheds twigs when nervous.|What's left only flies if you're polite to it.} {season == \"winter\": Frost has got into all their bristles.|}\n> left: Shoulder in for the good broom\n  wage\n  perform broom_courier +small\n  alignment -tiny\n  say: {You get there first, by a whisker and an elbow. The second-year you elbowed will remember.|You shoulder a second-year aside and the good broom is yours. It flies like a dream; theirs flies like a complaint.|You pick out the good broom by its varnish while the others are still arguing. You're over the lake before they notice.}\n> right: Take the difficult one\n  wage\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  perform broom_courier +tiny\n  say: {It fights you the whole way across the lake. Your arms will remember this, and so will you.|It takes the whole route to come to an understanding with it. You win, narrowly.|You're polite to the difficult one, very polite. It flies well, and everyone else is too embarrassed to try it.}\n\n@event broom_courier_dead_letters\ntitle: The Dead-Letter Shelf\ntrack: broom_courier\nrank: 2\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: letter\ntags: job mystery\nscene: A dusty shelf of undeliverable parcels in a courier loft, one tied with a faded ribbon.\ntext: {Couriers sort the dead letters, post with no proper address, waiting on a dusty shelf.|The dead-letter shelf has grown a second row.|You know which parcels have waited longest now, and which have stopped expecting anyone.} Today, a parcel addressed only to {\"the tall one in the red scarf, third tower\"|\"whoever plays the flute at dawn\"|\"my brother, the one who never writes\"}.\n> left: Track down the recipient\n  wage\n  challenge research apprentice+:\n    win:\n      perform broom_courier +small\n      rank_xp broom_courier +tiny\n      say: {It takes three towers and a dozen questions, but the parcel reaches the right hands. They open it at once and cry a little, in a good way.|Found them. The look on their face is worth every stair.|You find the brother. He's a porter in the next village and he does write; the letters just never arrive. Now one has.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: {You knock on every door you can think of. The parcel goes back on the shelf, a little more crumpled, a little more sad.|Wrong door. Wrong tower. Wrong week.|There are a great many brothers who never write. None of them is this one.}\n> right: Leave it on the shelf\n  wage\n  mood +tiny\n  perform broom_courier -tiny\n  say: {It goes back with the others. Some of them have been waiting longer than you have been alive.|You leave it. That night you dream of a parcel on a high tower, waiting.|You leave it, but you dust the shelf. The parcels that have stopped expecting anyone look a little less forgotten.}\n\n@event broom_courier_own_pigeonhole\ntitle: Your Own Pigeonhole\ntrack: broom_courier\nrank: 2\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nweight: 20\nart: bell\ntags: job\nscene: A courier loft wall of pigeonholes, one freshly painted with a student's name, a brass bell above.\ntext: The dispatcher has painted your name over a pigeonhole, in letters that will outlast the loft's roof. \"Your route from now on,\" the dispatcher says. \"Village, barge landing, the high towers. When the bell rings for you, you answer.\" The dispatch bell rings. It's for you.\n> left: Fly the route at once\n  wage\n  rank_xp broom_courier +medium\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: You fly your own route for the first time, every turning yours. At the barge landing someone calls you by name to hand over a letter. The wind is cold, and you don't mind at all.\n> right: Stand the runners a round\n  wage\n  rank_xp broom_courier +medium\n  mood +small\n  gold -tiny\n  say: The runners toast your pigeonhole with mugs of loft tea. Somebody chalks a little crown above your name. It is still there at the end of the year, smudged, the most permanent thing in the loft.\n"},{"path":"content/events/pools/courses_a.grim","text":"# Baseline course-morning pools, group courses_a (docs/authoring/pools.md):\n# Evocation, Astromancy & Divination, Alchemy & Healing. Repeatable routine events, three at level I and two each at II and III.\n\n# ---------------- Evocation (track: evocation, skill arcana) ----------------\n\n@event evo_snuffing_drill\ntitle: The Snuffing Drill\ntrack: evocation\nlevel: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: candle\ntags: class magic\nscene: A row of iron braziers burning in a scorched lecture hall, students standing before them with their hands raised as if to calm a horse.\ntext: {Evocation I opens with a lesson nobody expected. \"Anyone can start a fire,\" says the lecturer. \"Stopping one is the course.\"|The braziers are lit before you arrive. You know which one smokes.|Your brazier goes out when you walk in now, out of habit. The lecturer relights it, unamused.} {The front row smells of singed eyebrow.|Someone's brazier has gone out from fright.|The lecturer is timing you with an egg-timer.}\n> left: Call it back slowly\n  arcana +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {The flames shrink into embers, then into a warm ache in your palm.|It takes the whole hour. Your brazier goes out like a sigh.|You call it back while the egg-timer runs. It goes out with a second to spare, and the lecturer turns the timer over again.} {season == \"winter\": The hall is colder afterwards, and it's your fault.|The lecturer nods once.}\n> right: Snuff it in one breath\n  challenge exam standard-1:\n    win:\n      arcana +small\n      study evocation +tiny\n      say: {The fire folds itself into your hand all at once.|One breath, and the brazier is dark.|You snuff it before the egg-timer has turned.} The lecturer writes something down without looking surprised, which is the compliment.\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {The fire goes out. It goes out up your sleeve first.|The brazier flares, then sulks, then goes out on its own terms.|You rush it for the timer, and the fire rushes back.} You spend the rest of the hour holding your wrist in a bucket.\n\n@event evo_bucket_duty\ntitle: Bucket Duty\ntrack: evocation\nlevel: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: wave\ntags: class study\nscene: A student standing at the edge of a busy lecture hall beside a bucket of sand and a bucket of water, watching sparks fly.\ntext: Every Evocation I lesson needs someone on the buckets: sand for the pyromancers, water for everyone else. {&Today it's your name on the board.|It's your turn again. The rota has a sense of humour.|Your name is on the board. You suspect the rota is personal.} {&The water bucket has a frog in it.|Someone has labelled the sand bucket \"Hope\".|The water bucket is half empty, because the tidecallers borrowed it.}\n> left: Mind the buckets, watch closely\n  study evocation +small\n  say: {&You put out four sleeves and a textbook, and learn more from their mistakes than from your own.|Watching from the side, you finally see what the lecturer means by \"breathe first\".|You keep the buckets full and borrowed back from the tidecallers, and watch the whole lesson from the best seat in the hall.} {month == 4 or month == 10: It's the best revision you've done all term.|Nobody thanks the bucket monitor.}\n> right: Swap for a turn up front\n  arcana +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {&You trade your duty for a turn at the practice line. Nobody is minding the buckets when your spark jumps the desk.|A first-year takes your buckets for a promise of help with their essay. Your practice goes well; your thumb goes pink.|You swap, and the tidecallers have the water bucket. Your spark finds the one dry curtain in the hall.}\n\n@event evo_steady_cup\ntitle: The Steady Cup\ntrack: evocation\nlevel: 1\nlesson: mirror_lake\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: cup\ntags: class magic\nscene: A row of students on a stone terrace holding brimming cups at arm's length while a professor reads aloud from a slim book of poems.\ntext: {Professor Marisol's Tidecalling mornings begin with a full cup held at arm's length. Keep the water level by will alone while she reads poetry aloud.|Marisol has brought a new book of poems, thicker than the last.|You can hold the cup half asleep now, and often are.} {season == \"winter\": The water has a skin of ice, which she says is cheating.|{The poem is very long.|The poem is about the sea, and so are your sleeves.|A gull is watching your cup with intent.}}\n> left: Hold it to the last line\n  arcana +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {Your arm burns by the third stanza. The water doesn't move.|By the end your arm is shaking and the water isn't.|The gull lands on your wrist in the last verse. The water doesn't notice, and neither, quite, do you.} The poem goes on for ever, and so, it feels, do you. Marisol closes the book at last. \"There. The lake would approve.\"\n> right: Spill, and study her hands\n  study evocation +small\n  say: {Your cup tips over in the second verse. You spend the rest watching how Marisol turns her wrist, and write it down.|You give up on the cup and watch Marisol instead. She holds a whole pond steady while turning pages.|The gull takes your cup the moment you spill it. You watch Marisol hold hers steady through the gull's attempt on hers too.} {final_year: You'll miss her terrible poems.|Your shoes are wet.}\n\n@event evo_shaping_hour\ntitle: Shaping Hour\ntrack: evocation\nlevel: 2\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: flame\ntags: class magic\nscene: A lecture hall full of small fires shaped like birds, keys and letters, one of them visibly trying to escape through a window.\ntext: Evocation II is shaping: flame made into a sphere, a key, a bird. The lecturer keeps a ledger of every scorch mark in the hall and makes you sign for yours. {flag(scorched_ledger): Your page in the ledger has a few signatures on it already.|Your page in the ledger is still blank.} {&Today's shape is a bird.|Today's shape is a key that turns.|Today's shape is your own initial, which is harder than it sounds.}\n> left: A hundred plain spheres\n  arcana +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {&Sphere after sphere, until your hands do it without asking you.|It's dull, honest work. Your spheres come out round, every one.|Spheres until your fingers know the curve. Your initial would come easier after this, you suspect.} {season == \"summer\": The hall is an oven by noon.|The lecturer says nothing, which is approval.}\n> right: Try the hard shape\n  challenge exam standard+1:\n    win:\n      arcana +small\n      study evocation +small\n      say: {&Your bird flaps twice, circles the lectern and goes out on a perch.|Your key turns in the air, with a click you feel more than hear.|Your initial hangs in the air, crisp at every edge.} The lecturer turns the ledger round to show it hasn't moved.\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: {&Your bird has opinions, and leaves through the window.|Your key comes out as a fork, and a hot one.|Your initial comes out as somebody else's, and singes your cuff.} You sign the ledger. The lecturer blots it for you, almost kindly.\n      flag scorched_ledger\n> up[flame]: Shape it at a whisper\n  study evocation +small\n  say: {&You call the flame softly and it comes, small and obedient as a candle. The lecturer leans in to watch.|The shape comes out right first time, at a whisper. Your page in the ledger stays clean.|The flame stays the size you asked for, which is the whole lesson.}\n\n@event evo_kettle_pairs\ntitle: Fire Meets Water\ntrack: evocation\nlevel: 2\nlesson: mirror_lake\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: cauldron\ntags: class study\nscene: Pairs of students on either side of copper kettles, one with a palm of flame and one with a palm of water, steam curling between them.\ntext: {This is why Pyromancy and Tidecalling are taught together. One of you heats the kettle, one cools it, and it must simmer and never boil.|The kettles come out of the cupboard, every dent a story.|You know every kettle by its dents now. Two dents are yours.} {Your partner is a nervous second-year from Stillwater.|Your partner is very confident, which is worrying.|Your partner yawns hugely.}\n> left: Take the fire side\n  challenge exam standard:\n    win:\n      arcana +small\n      study evocation +small\n      say: {You and your partner find the rhythm together, and the kettle hums all hour.|Push, give, push. The kettle simmers like a contented cat.|Your partner yawns through the whole hour, and the kettle simmers anyway. You've been doing both halves.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {The kettle boils, then whistles, then lifts its lid at you. You both get a faceful of steam.|You push too hard. Your partner panics and freezes the spout, and the trapped steam bursts out of the lid at you.|Your partner falls asleep mid-lesson and the kettle boils. You both wake up very suddenly.}\n> right: Take the water side\n  study evocation +small\n  mana -small\n  say: {Cooling is the patient half. You hold the kettle back all hour and your partner gets the credit.|You lend the kettle your cold for an hour and feel drained by noon.|You hold the water side alone while your partner yawns. The lecturer notices who did the work.} {final_year: The kettle has been here longer than you, and will stay after you've gone.|Your fingertips stay damp until lunch.}\n\n@event evo_deep_call\ntitle: Calling From the Deep\ntrack: evocation\nlevel: 3\nlesson: mirror_lake\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: wave\ntags: class danger\nscene: Advanced students on a jetty with their arms raised, a column of dark, old lake water rising toward them out of the depths.\ntext: {Advanced Evocation calls water from the lake bed, where it is cold and old and in no hurry.|The jetty is slick at this hour.|The jetty boards know your boots by now.} The lecturer paces behind the line. The water comes up carrying things. {Today, a bottle with no message in it.|Today, a single boot, laced.|Today, nothing at all, which is somehow worse.} {season == \"winter\": Ice grinds at the edges of the column.|Mist sits on the water.}\n> left: Reach deeper than the rest\n  challenge exam standard+1 wild:\n    win:\n      arcana +medium\n      say: {You call water up from somewhere it has been still for a century. It rises like a held breath and comes down gently.|The water obeys, then lingers, as if it wanted to talk.|You call from deeper than anyone, and the water brings nothing up at all. It feels like being refused.} The lecturer stops pacing.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mana -small\n      say: {The lake pulls back. You're on your knees on the jetty, cold to the bone.|The water rises, considers you, and drops onto your head.|You reach for the deep water and something reaches back, gently, and lets go. You sit down on the jetty for a while.} The lecturer pulls you up with no comment, which is a comment.\n> right: Keep to the shallows\n  study evocation +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {You keep to safer water and log what the others bring up. It's useful and it's dull.|You hold your column at a sensible depth and watch someone else go white with the cold.|You keep to the shallows. The others bring up nothing today, and look at the water a long time.} {gpa >= 3: The lecturer says you have nothing to prove. You'd like to.|You'll go deeper next time, perhaps.}\n\n@event evo_signature_flame\ntitle: A Fire of Your Own\ntrack: evocation\nlevel: 3\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: flame\ntags: class magic\nscene: A student alone at a stone bench in a dim hall, coaxing a flame of an unusual colour above their palm.\ntext: Advanced Evocation asks every student for a signature flame: its own colour, its own heat, its own temper. {Yours is a sulky orange.|Yours has turned a gold that you're quietly proud of.|Yours knows you now. It leans toward you when you come in.} {Somebody's flame across the hall is green and smells of pine.|Somebody's flame is silver and hums.|The lecturer's own flame is lit on the lectern, blue and tiny, never discussed.}\n> left: Push it somewhere new\n  challenge exam standard+1 wild:\n    win:\n      arcana +medium\n      say: {Your flame slides into a colour nobody has a name for. The lecturer asks you to do it again, slowly.|For a moment your flame burns without smoke and without heat, just light.|Your flame leans toward the lecturer's blue one and takes a little of its colour. The lecturer covers hers.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -small\n      say: {Your flame flares white, takes a bite of your hand and goes back to orange as if nothing happened.|The new colour lasts a heartbeat. The blister lasts a week.|Your flame reaches for something new and finds your sleeve. It knows you, apparently, and still bites.}\n> right: Refine what it already is\n  study evocation +small\n  say: {You spend the morning making it steadier, not stranger. It's a better flame by noon, and a less exciting one.|You tune the heat by a hair, then another hair.|You refine it until it leans toward you on its own. That's all it does, and it's enough.} {final_year: You'll carry this fire out of Lumenhold with you.|It's yours, at least.}\n\n# ---------------- Astromancy & Divination (track: astromancy, skill lore) ----------------\n\n@event astro_tea_rota\ntitle: Another Pot of Tea\ntrack: astromancy\nlevel: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: cup\ntags: class study\nscene: A round classroom under a brass dome, every desk crowded with teacups, a huge brown teapot steaming on the lectern.\ntext: Astromancy I has a tea-leaf morning every week, and tea leaves require tea. {The class gets through three pots before ten.|The tea rota has your name on it again.|The teapot greets you like an old colleague.} {Your cup shows something that is either a ship or a boot.|Your cup shows a shape the textbook calls the ladder. It looks like a spoon.|Your cup shows rain. Your cup always shows rain.}\n> left: Drink the pot and read\n  lore +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {By the fourth cup your hands are shaking and the leaves are making more sense.|You read six cups in a row and see the pattern underneath the shapes.|Your cup shows rain again. It rains. You're starting to think that's the reading.} {season == \"winter\": At least the tea is warm.|You will not sleep tonight.}\n> right: Brew the next pot\n  study astromancy +small\n  say: {You take the rota and brew for the room. Watching leaves settle in a whole pot teaches you more than any one cup.|You brew it strong, the way the lecturer likes it, and learn the shapes as they sink.|You brew the pot as the teapot likes it, which is the lecturer's way plus a pinch. The room reads better for it.} {season == \"summer\": The classroom is a steam bath by ten.|Nobody thanks the brewer, but everyone notices weak tea.}\n\n@event astro_star_names\ntitle: Naming the Stars\ntrack: astromancy\nlevel: 1\nlesson: observatory\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: star\ntags: class study\nscene: Students craning their necks under a painted dome of constellations while a lecturer points upward with a very long pole.\ntext: {Astromancy I begins with names. Three hundred stars painted on the dome, to be recited on request.|The long pole comes out. The lecturer points; you name.|You dream the star names some nights now, and wake reciting.} {Today it's the winter constellations.|Today it's the faint stars nobody can see without squinting.|Today the lecturer points at a patch of bare plaster and waits to see who invents a name.}\n> left: Recite them until perfect\n  study astromancy +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {You chant star names under your breath all morning, like a prayer you don't quite believe in.|By noon you can name the whole southern sky backwards.|You recite them in your sleep, apparently, and again at breakfast. Your roommate can name the southern sky now too.} {month == 4 or month == 10: The exam is close, and the names are finally sticking.|Your neck aches from looking up.}\n> right: Volunteer to go first\n  challenge exam standard-1:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      renown +tiny\n      say: {You name every star the pole touches, including the bare plaster, which you correctly call \"nothing\".|The lecturer runs out of stars before you run out of names.|The lecturer points at bare plaster. You invent a name so plausible that two people write it down.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      lore +tiny\n      say: {You call a bright star by the name of a lesser one. The lesser one's champion in the back row is offended.|You blank on the fourth star, and a whole constellation falls out of your head.|The lecturer points at bare plaster and you name it confidently, with a star that doesn't exist. It's in three people's notes by lunch.}\n\n@event astro_cold_dome\ntitle: The Open Dome\ntrack: astromancy\nlevel: 1\nlesson: observatory\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: moon\ntags: class study\nscene: The observatory's brass dome cranked open to a pale dawn sky, one student at the great telescope and the rest huddled round an iron stove.\ntext: {Astromancy mornings start before the sun, under a dome cranked open to the sky.|The dome creaks open. You've learned which side of the stove is warmest.|The telescope's eyepiece has the shape of your eye by now.} {season == \"winter\": It is bitterly cold, and the stove is the most popular student in the class.|{season == \"summer\": The air is soft and the sky is already going pale.|There's dew on the telescope and on you.}} Somebody has to take the telescope.\n> left: Take the telescope turn\n  lore +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You track a planet across the eyepiece until {season == \"winter\": your eyelashes stick to it|your eyes water and your neck gives up}.|The great telescope wanders, as it does. You coax it back to the sky you were assigned.|The eyepiece fits your eye. You find the planet before the lecturer has finished saying where it is.} {final_year: You've looked through it more times than you can count, and you'll still miss it.|{season == \"winter\": Your fingers take an hour to thaw.|You yawn through the whole of breakfast.}}\n> right: Stay by the stove with charts\n  study astromancy +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {The stove students form a little society of the warm and the guilty. You copy charts and hear every complaint.|You annotate the charts while the others shiver. The lecturer notices who is warm.|You keep the stove and the charts. Someone else takes the telescope and complains it's shaped wrong.}\n\n@event astro_foresight_drill\ntitle: Catch the Chalk\ntrack: astromancy\nlevel: 2\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: hourglass\ntags: class magic\nscene: A lecturer letting a stick of chalk fall from an outstretched hand while a student lunges to catch it a moment before it drops.\ntext: {Foresight at Astromancy II is practised on chalk. The lecturer drops a stick without warning; you catch it before it falls, by seeing it a heartbeat early.|The lecturer has opened a new box of chalk, which is a threat.|The lecturer's drops have got sneakier, and so have your hands.} {The floor is white with failures.|A student from Stillwater has caught four in a row and is unbearable about it.|The lecturer is dropping two at once today.}\n> left: Catch it before it falls\n  challenge exam standard wild:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      study astromancy +small\n      say: {Your hand is there before the chalk is. The lecturer looks at it, then at you, then drops another.|You catch it a breath before the drop.|Two sticks at once. You catch one in each hand, and the lecturer drops a third out of spite.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: {You see it falling, clearly, half a second after it lands.|You catch a stick of chalk that wasn't dropped. The real one hits your shoe.|Two sticks at once, and you catch the one in the past.}\n> right: Time the drops instead\n  study astromancy +small\n  mana -small\n  say: {You keep a tally of every drop and every catch and start to see the lecturer's rhythm.|You work out that the lecturer always blinks first. It's not foresight, but it helps.|Your tally shows the lecturer drops the second stick a beat after the first, always. It's not foresight. It works.} Holding a little seeing-charm open the whole hour to check your tally leaves your head aching. {gpa >= 3: The lecturer asks to see your tally.|You'd rather have caught one.}\n> up[foresight]: Let foresight do it\n  lore +small\n  say: {You stop trying and let the spell look ahead for you. Your hand opens, and the chalk drops into it like a tame bird.|It's a two-chalk day. The spell shows you both, and you catch both.|The lecturer stops mid-drop and just looks at you. You catch it anyway.}\n\n@event astro_starlight_jars\ntitle: Bottling Starlight\ntrack: astromancy\nlevel: 2\nlesson: observatory\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: bottle\ntags: class magic\nscene: Students on the observatory roof before dawn, holding glass jars up to the sky, each jar glowing faintly with captured light.\ntext: {Starlight is gathered before dawn, one star at a time, in glass jars on the observatory roof.|The roof is slippery with frost or dew, depending.|The starlight jars. Yours has a chip in the rim you've grown fond of.} {Most of the class is aiming for the brightest stars.|The lecturer says faint light keeps longer.|There's a slip of cloud coming in from the lake.}\n> left: Wait for the faint stars\n  challenge research standard:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      study astromancy +small\n      say: {Your jar fills slowly with a cool, pale light that lasts all day in your pocket.|The faint stars come one at a time.|You wait out the cloud with your chipped jar. The faint stars come back for you, one by one.} The lecturer holds your jar up and says, \"That will keep.\"\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: {The cloud comes in before your jar is half full, and you're cold for nothing.|You wait so long the sun rises on you and burns the jar to plain glass.|The cloud from the lake settles in for the morning. Your chipped jar stays empty, and you stay cold.}\n> right: Take the bright, easy ones\n  study astromancy +small\n  say: {Your jar is full in ten minutes and glows like a lamp. The lecturer calls it a lamp.|Easy light, quickly gathered and gone by noon.|You fill your chipped jar with the bright ones before the cloud arrives. It's gone by lunch, as usual.} {season == \"summer\": The short nights are no help.|You're back in bed before the bell.}\n\n@event astro_moving_charts\ntitle: The Charts Move\ntrack: astromancy\nlevel: 3\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: map\ntags: class study\nscene: A long table covered in star charts whose inked constellations are visibly sliding across the parchment, students pinning corners down.\ntext: {Advanced Astromancy uses charts that move. The inked stars drift to where they will be, not where they are.|The charts are livelier this morning, and one has crept off the table.|You've stopped flinching when the stars move. You still check twice.} {Your chart keeps rearranging itself into a shape like a doorway.|Your chart is sulking in one corner.|Your chart is going faster than the others.} Pin it and read it, or read it as it goes.\n> left: Pin it and trace the drift\n  challenge research standard+1:\n    win:\n      lore +medium\n      say: {You pin each star and trace its path, and for a moment you can see the whole season ahead laid out in ink.|The drift makes sense all at once.|You pin the fast chart and trace a season so quickly it makes you dizzy. The lecturer asks to keep the tracing.}\n    lose:\n      mana -small\n      mood -small\n      say: {You pour magic into the pins to hold it, and the chart slides out from under them anyway, leaving ink on your sleeves.|You hold the chart down with a binding until your magic runs thin, then trace the drift for an hour. It goes back to where it started, laughing at you in its own way.|The fast chart drags the pins with it across the table and onto the floor. You chase it on your knees.}\n> right: Sketch every shape it makes\n  study astromancy +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {You fill four pages with the shapes the chart passes through. Most are nothing. One is almost something.|You draw it as it moves, like a portrait of someone who won't sit still.|You sketch the fast chart at its own pace. Your drawing hand is exhausted. The shapes are beautiful.} {final_year: Soon the stars will move without you watching.|The lecturer collects your pages without a word.}\n\n@event astro_far_glimpse\ntitle: Looking Too Far\ntrack: astromancy\nlevel: 3\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: eye\ntags: class magic\nscene: A student staring into a dark mirror at the centre of a curtained room, a lecturer standing close with an hourglass.\ntext: {At this level Foresight reaches a season ahead. The lecturer allows one glimpse each, timed by an hourglass. People who look longer come back strange.|The dark mirror waits behind its curtain.|The hourglass is turned before you sit down. The lecturer knows your pace.} {final_year: This is your last year of looking ahead from this room, which makes the mirror harder to look at.|{The curtains are drawn against the morning.|Someone before you came out very quiet.|The mirror is very clean today, which is somehow worse.}}\n> left: Look a season ahead\n  challenge exam standard+1 wild:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      study astromancy +small\n      say: {You see a lit window and a cup going cold, and you know exactly where and when it will be. You keep it to yourself.|You see something small and true. You'll remember it when it happens.|You see a season ahead before the hourglass is half gone. You stop yourself, which the lecturer notes.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -small\n      say: {The mirror shows you too much at once. You come out with a nosebleed and a headache that lasts till dinner.|You see nothing, and then you see everything.|You look until the sand runs out, and a little after. The lecturer pulls you back by the shoulder.}\n> right: Look only as far as tomorrow\n  study astromancy +small\n  mana -small\n  say: {Tomorrow, says the mirror, it will rain at noon. It does. It's not much, but it's right.|You look only one day ahead. Lunch will be soup.|You look only to tomorrow, in your timed turn. It will be cold. It is.} {gpa >= 3: The lecturer says caution is a talent too.|It's safe, and it feels small.}\n\n# ---------------- Alchemy & Healing (track: alchemy, skill alchemy) ----------------\n\n@event alch_stir_count\ntitle: Clockwise\ntrack: alchemy\nlevel: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: cauldron\ntags: class study\nscene: A laboratory of identical cauldrons, a whole class stirring in unison while the lecturer counts aloud from the front.\ntext: {Alchemy I is mostly stirring, and the stirring is counted aloud by the whole class, in unison, like a choir with spoons.|The chorus is getting good. Someone has started harmonising.|You count stirs in your sleep now, and sometimes on the stairs.} {Today's draught is a simple tonic.|Today's draught is meant to turn blue at forty.|Today the lecturer keeps changing the count to see who's listening.}\n> left: Count every stir aloud\n  study alchemy +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {By the hundredth stir your voice is gone and your tonic is perfect.|Your count is right and your draught is dull. Both are what was asked.|The lecturer changes the count; you change with it, aloud. Your tonic is the only one that's right.} {month == 4 or month == 10: The exam recipes come back to you in rhythm.|Your wrist aches.}\n> right: Stir by the colour instead\n  challenge brew standard-1:\n    win:\n      alchemy +small\n      say: {You stop counting and watch the colour. It turns blue exactly when you expect it to.|You lose the count and keep the draught. The lecturer can tell, and allows it.|The count changes and you don't care, because you're watching the colour. The lecturer asks how you knew.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      health -tiny\n      say: {You watch the colour too closely and miss it changing. The fumes make your eyes water.|Your draught turns a colour the lecturer has never seen. It's not a good sign.|You watch the colour and miss the count changing. Your draught is right for a different recipe.}\n\n@event alch_chopping_board\ntitle: Prep Work\ntrack: alchemy\nlevel: 1\nlesson: pell_and_daughters\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: leaf\ntags: class study\nscene: A long scrubbed bench heaped with roots, berries and dried beetles, students bent over chopping boards with small sharp knives.\ntext: {&Half of alchemy, says the lecturer, is cutting things up correctly. The ingredients come from Pell & Daughters in the village, packed in straw, every one labelled in a careful hand.|The crate from the village is heavier today.|Your knife knows the way, and your thumb knows where not to be.} {&Today it's roots, to be sliced paper-thin.|Today it's beetles, to be crushed and not thought about.|Today it's berries, which stain everything.}\n> left: Prep for the whole bench\n  alchemy +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {&You cut enough roots for six. Your bench-mates bring you tea, which is fair.|You crush beetles for an hour and think firmly about other things.|You prepare enough berries for six, and your hands stay purple till dinner.} The crate is empty when you're done.\n> right: Prep yours, read ahead\n  study alchemy +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: You rush your share and get to the reading, plus a small cut on your thumb you'll be explaining all day.\n\n@event alch_bandage_round\ntitle: Bandage Round\ntrack: alchemy\nlevel: 1\nlesson: infirmary\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: cross\ntags: class study\nscene: A sunny infirmary ward where students roll bandages at a long table while a nun in a starched apron checks their work.\ntext: {&The healing half of Alchemy I is taught in the infirmary, where Sister Amaranth has the class roll bandages and watch her work.|Sister Amaranth inspects every roll, and unrolls one of yours.|Sister Amaranth hands you a roll without looking up.} {&A third-year is in with a burnt hand from Evocation.|A first-year is in with a scraped knee and a great deal to say about it.|A second-year is in with a sprained wrist from the flight field.}\n> left: Roll bandages and watch\n  study alchemy +small\n  say: {&You roll, and watch Sister Amaranth clean a cut in about three movements.|Your bandages pass inspection.|You roll, and watch Sister Amaranth strap a wrist so it can't move and doesn't hurt. You try it on your own wrist later.} {season == \"winter\": The ward fills with sniffling students all morning.|She says nothing, which is high praise.}\n> right: Offer to treat a patient\n  challenge exam standard:\n    win:\n      alchemy +small\n      say: {&You treat the burn properly. Sister Amaranth says, \"Hm.\" From her, that is a speech.|You clean and bind the scraped knee neatly. The patient doesn't even wince. Sister Amaranth adjusts one knot.|You strap the wrist firmly and neatly. The patient flexes it and looks impressed.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: {&You bandage the wrong place entirely. The patient is too polite to say so; Sister Amaranth is not.|Your bandage unrolls itself and the patient has to wait while you start again.|You strap the wrist so tightly the patient's fingers go white. Sister Amaranth loosens it, and looks at you.}\n\n@event alch_purify_samples\ntitle: Lake Water Samples\ntrack: alchemy\nlevel: 2\nlesson: mirror_lake\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: bottle\ntags: class magic\nscene: A bench lined with jars of murky lake water, a lecturer at the end raising one now-clear jar to drink it.\ntext: {Alchemy II hands out jars of murky lake water, to be made fit to drink. The lecturer has a habit of drinking the results.|The lecturer looks thirsty, which is never a good sign.|The lecturer has brought a favourite cup for this, chipped at the rim.} {Your jar has a small weed in it.|Your jar is suspiciously green.|Your jar has a tiny snail in it, which you set free.}\n> left: Purify it thoroughly\n  challenge brew standard:\n    win:\n      alchemy +small\n      study alchemy +small\n      say: {Your water comes out clear and sweet. The lecturer drinks it, pauses and nods.|Clean as rainwater.|Your water goes into the lecturer's chipped cup, and the lecturer drinks it to the bottom.} {flag(purified_well): The lecturer drinks a second glass, and pretends not to.|The lecturer drinks a second glass, which nobody has seen before.}\n      flag purified_well\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: {You test it first, just a sip, as the lecturer always does. You spend the afternoon close to the washroom.|Your water clears, then clouds again. You taste it anyway, to be sure, and regret it all afternoon.|You purify it so hard the snail you set free comes back to complain. The water tastes of snail anyway.}\n> right: Filter it the slow way\n  study alchemy +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {Cloth, sand and charcoal, the old way. It takes the whole lesson.|You filter it by hand and write every step down. The lecturer drinks it and says, \"Patient.\"|You filter it slowly and the snail goes back to the lake in a cup of its own. The lecturer approves of both.} {final_year: You'll be filtering water like this for the rest of your life, probably.|Your hands are grey with charcoal.}\n\n@event alch_long_simmer\ntitle: The Long Simmer\ntrack: alchemy\nlevel: 2\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: hourglass\ntags: class study\nscene: A bubbling cauldron in an empty laboratory, a single student slumped on a stool beside it with an hourglass and a book.\ntext: {Some potions simmer for hours and must be watched. Whoever is on watch when the colour turns gets the credit, or the blame.|The class takes turns at the cauldron, and the rota has come round.|Your turn at the simmer. You've brought a cushion this time.} {The rest of the class has gone to lunch.|The potion is making a noise like a contented cat.|The lecturer has left a note that says \"Do not leave it.\"}\n> left: Sit the whole watch\n  alchemy +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {Three hours, one cauldron, one hourglass. You see it turn and catch it at the right moment.|You read the same paragraph forty times and stir at every bubble.|You sit the watch on your cushion, as the note says. The potion turns, and you're there.} {season == \"summer\": The laboratory is stifling.|By the end, you can tell its mood from the bubbles.}\n> right: Set a charm to watch it\n  challenge enchant standard:\n    win:\n      study alchemy +small\n      mood +tiny\n      say: {Your charm rings like a bell when the colour turns. You come back from lunch in time to take it off the fire.|The charm works and you get to eat.|You set the charm and leave a note of your own under the lecturer's: \"I didn't, technically.\" It works.}\n    lose:\n      alchemy +tiny\n      health -small\n      say: {Your charm falls asleep. The potion boils over and you breathe in a lot of steam getting it off the fire.|The charm rings for no reason, three times, and the fourth time you ignore it. That was the real one: you get a faceful of steam pulling the cauldron off the fire.|The charm watches it perfectly until the lecturer's note blows onto it. Then it watches the note.}\n\n@event alch_volatile_reagents\ntitle: Volatile Reagents\ntrack: alchemy\nlevel: 3\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: potion\ntags: class danger\nscene: A hushed laboratory, a student holding a glass vial of glowing liquid at arm's length, everyone else very still behind a screen.\ntext: {Advanced Alchemy handles reagents that react to being looked at the wrong way. The lecturer has one rule. One person speaks at a time, and that person is the one holding the vial.|Nobody is speaking. Somebody is breathing too loudly.|You've stopped holding your breath, mostly.} {Today's vial is pale yellow and fizzing.|Today's vial is dark red and perfectly still, which is worse.|Today's vial is labelled in the lecturer's hand: \"Gently.\"}\n> left: Handle it yourself\n  challenge brew standard+1 steady:\n    win:\n      alchemy +medium\n      say: {You measure it drop by drop, speaking only the numbers. It settles exactly as it should.|Your hands don't shake.|You handle it gently, as labelled, and it behaves for you. The lecturer takes the label off and keeps it.} The lecturer lets out a breath.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {The vial pops like a cork. Your eyebrows survive; your gloves do not.|A drop too many. The flask hisses and coughs out blue smoke, and you spend the hour in the infirmary.|You handle it gently, which turns out not to be gently enough. The label lands in your hair.}\n> right: Pass it to the steadiest hand\n  study alchemy +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {You hand the vial to someone with steadier hands and take careful notes. It stings a little.|You watch someone else do it right. You'll know how next time.|You pass the \"Gently\" vial on. The steady hand drops it, very gently, into the sink. Everyone breathes again.} {gpa >= 3: The lecturer says you're allowed to be cautious.|You note every step.}\n\n@event alch_original_remedy\ntitle: A Remedy of Your Own\ntrack: alchemy\nlevel: 3\nlesson: infirmary\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: potion\ntags: class study\nscene: A student at a cluttered bench writing a recipe in a notebook, a small flask of their own remedy steaming beside it.\ntext: {Advanced Alchemy asks for an original remedy, invented, written up, and tested under Sister Amaranth's eye.|Another revision. The notebook has more crossings-out than words.|Your remedy is nearly finished. It has been nearly finished for weeks.} {This version is for headaches.|This version is for the common cold, which has resisted remedies for centuries.|This version is for exam nerves, which you need.}\n> left: Test it on yourself\n  challenge brew standard+1 wild:\n    win:\n      alchemy +medium\n      say: {It works. Your headache lifts and your mood with it.|It works better than you expected.|It works. Your hands stop shaking, and for an afternoon so does your dread of exams.} {flag(remedy_in_book): Sister Amaranth adds a note to your page in her book.|Sister Amaranth writes your recipe in her own book.}\n      flag remedy_in_book\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -small\n      say: {It does something, but not what you wanted. Your ears are warm for the rest of the day.|It cures the sniffles and replaces them with hiccups.|It calms your nerves so thoroughly that you fall asleep at the bench.}\n> right: Ask Sister Amaranth to review it\n  study alchemy +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {She crosses out three ingredients and one adjective.|She reads it twice, then hands it back. \"Sound,\" she says. \"Dull.\"|She reads it and takes one dose herself, before the exam she's invigilating. \"Adequate,\" she says, calmly.} {final_year: You'll keep this recipe long after you leave Lumenhold.|It's better already, and you hate it a little.}\n> up[purify]: Purify the base first\n  alchemy +small\n  say: {You draw every impurity out of the base before you start. The remedy comes out clear and gentle.|The base comes clean in one pass. Sister Amaranth smells it and says nothing, which is approval.|With a pure base, the fault in your recipe finally shows. You fix it.}\n"},{"path":"content/events/pools/courses_b.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Baseline pools (phase 3, step 1b): course mornings for\n#  Illusion & Glamour, Beastcraft & Flight and Umbral Theory.\n#  Repeatable routine events, drawn once per enrolled course a day.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- Illusion & Glamour (track: illusion) ----------------\n\n@event illu_candle_drill\ntitle: The Candle Drill\ntrack: illusion\nlevel: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: candle\ntags: class magic\nscene: A row of unlit candles on a lecture bench, each crowned with a flame that casts no shadow, a professor holding her palm over one.\ntext: {Illusion I opens with a row of cold candles. Make them burn, Professor Vey says, without burning.|Vey's drawer of candles is open. Every one is older than you.|The candle drill. Some mornings you could do it asleep.} {Vey passes her hand through each flame and says \"cold\" in the same flat voice.|Someone's flame leans in a draught only it can feel.|The student beside you has made a lovely candle and forgotten the flame.}\n> left: Hold the flame until it's right\n  study illusion +small\n  mana -small\n  say: {Your flame steadies, then flickers in time with your breathing, which Vey says is the point.|Vey's palm hovers over your flame a moment longer than the others. \"Almost warm,\" she says. From her, that's a sonnet.|By the bell your flame has a proper blue heart. You feel oddly proud of something that isn't there.}\n> right: Make it gutter and smoke\n  challenge deceive using charm standard:\n    win:\n      charm +small\n      mood +tiny\n      say: {You add a thread of smoke and a little gutter. The student behind you reaches to pinch it out.|Your candle drips wax that isn't there onto a desk that is. Vey watches it fall and says nothing, which means yes.|Your candle gutters and smokes so well that the student beside you lends it their flame. Vey writes \"two for one\".}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: {The smoke goes up and the flame goes out, as though embarrassed by its own ambition.|Vey waves a hand through the whole performance. \"A candle doesn't try this hard,\" she says.|You add so much smoke the drill becomes a fog. Vey opens a window that isn't there, and the fog obligingly leaves.}\n\n@event illu_one_small_change\ntitle: One Small Change\ntrack: illusion\nlevel: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: mirror\ntags: class magic\nscene: Students bent over little hand mirrors, one changing the colour of her own eyes, another squinting at a nose that isn't quite his.\ntext: {Hand mirrors all round. Glamour your own face, Vey says; change one small thing and hold it.|Out come the mirrors. A glamour you can't hold on yourself, Vey says, won't hold on anyone.|Mirror practice. You know your own face too well by now.} {Vey says people always change their eyes first.|A second-year from Rootward is giving himself a better chin, slowly.|The mirrors fog, as they do {season == \"winter\": in the cold|when the room warms}.}\n> left: Change something trivial\n  study illusion +small\n  say: {You move a freckle half an inch. It takes the whole hour. Vey initials your page without looking up.|You darken one eyebrow and keep it dark. Dull, careful work, and the glamour holds.|A small scar vanishes and comes back, vanishes and comes back. By the end it stays gone until you let it.}\n> right: Change what you'd really change\n  charm +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {It holds beautifully. You look at it longer than the exercise requires, and let it go more slowly than you'd like.|Vey glances over your shoulder. \"Ah,\" she says, kindly enough. \"That one. Everybody has one.\"|You hold it all hour. Afterwards your real face seems slightly unfamiliar, like a coat you've lent out.}\n\n@event illu_spot_the_coin\ntitle: Spot the Coin\ntrack: illusion\nlevel: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: coin\ntags: class social\ncast:\n  partner: any where tagged(partner, student)\nscene: Two students at a bench with three coins between them, one coin shimmering very slightly at the edge.\ntext: {Partner work. {partner} lays out three crowns, and one of them is an illusion. Say which.|Three crowns from {partner}. One isn't there, and {they} look{s} pleased about it.|You and {partner} have started keeping score.} {Vey walks the room flicking real coins, to confuse everyone.|The windows are bright and the illusions show it.|The next bench is arguing whether a coin can be \"partly fake\".}\n> left: Study the coins honestly\n  study illusion +small\n  rel partner +tiny\n  say: {It's the one with no tarnish. Illusions always come out cleaner than money does.|You stare until your eyes water and pick the middle one. Wrong. {partner} pockets the real two, laughing.|You get it on the third try, by the shine that doesn't move when the light does.}\n> right: Watch their face, not the coins\n  challenge deceive using charm standard:\n    win:\n      charm +small\n      rel partner +tiny\n      say: {{partner} glances at the left coin a fraction too fondly. You point. {They} groan{s}.|You keep smiling until {partner} gives it away by smiling back. Vey notes that this isn't the exercise, and that it worked.|You know {partner}'s face by now. {They} know{s} you know, and pick{s} a coin with a straight face to beat you. It doesn't work.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: {{partner} has a better face than you'd thought. Your wrong guess buys {them} a cup of tea.|You watch {partner} so hard you miss the coin blinking out of existence entirely.|{partner} has been practising a blank face in the mirror. It shows. You lose a point and buy the tea.}\n\n@event illu_bread_smell\ntitle: The Smell of Bread\ntrack: illusion\nlevel: 2\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: meal\ntags: class magic\nscene: A lecture bench with an illusory loaf of bread on a board, steam rising from it, and several students sniffing it doubtfully.\ntext: {Illusion II adds the other senses. Today, a loaf that looks, smells and sounds fresh when broken.|More bread. Sight is easy, Vey says; smell is where liars are caught.|The bread lesson. The floor smells of loaves that never existed.} {Somebody's loaf smells strongly of wet dog.|One loaf crackles beautifully and smells of nothing.|{gpa >= 3: Vey asks you to go first.|Vey asks you to go last, which feels pointed.}}\n> left: Get the smell right\n  study illusion +small\n  say: {You spend an hour on the smell of a crust. By the end it's nearly convincing and you're desperately hungry.|You think of a bakery on a cold street and the smell arrives with it. Vey sniffs, and nods once.|The smell is right. The sound of breaking is wrong. Vey says that's the usual order.}\n> right: Add the warmth of the oven\n  challenge enchant using charm standard:\n    win:\n      charm +small\n      renown +tiny\n      say: {Vey picks your loaf up and puts it down hastily, as if it had burnt her. The class actually applauds.|The bench beside you swears the room got warmer. It didn't. That's the point.|You add the warmth of the oven and the smell of the morning after. Vey takes your loaf home, she claims, by mistake.}\n    lose:\n      mana -small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {The warmth arrives and the loaf leaves. You're left holding a hot, empty board.|Your bread steams, then smokes, then smells of a cupboard. Vey writes something short.|You add warmth, and the smell goes with it. The loaf is perfect and hot and smells of absolutely nothing.}\n\n@event illu_find_vey\ntitle: Find the Professor\ntrack: illusion\nlevel: 2\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: mask\ntags: class magic\nscene: A lecture hall where the professor's chair is empty and everyone is looking suspiciously at everyone else, including a pigeon on the sill.\ntext: {Professor Vey is rarely at the lectern in Illusion II. She is somewhere in the room, wearing someone else, and the first to name her wins.|Vey's chair is empty. Everyone eyes the porter with the mop.|The lectern is empty. You trust nothing in this room.} {There's a pigeon on the sill.|A student nobody remembers has taken the back row.|One of the portraits seems unusually attentive.}\n> left: Watch everyone, all lesson\n  study illusion +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {You don't find her, but you notice who blinks late. Vey turns out to have been the pigeon.|At the end she steps out of the porter's coat. You'd suspected the mop. The mop was real.|You learn three classmates' nervous habits and nothing about Vey, who was the attentive portrait all along.}\n> right: Name her early\n  challenge deceive using charm standard+2:\n    win:\n      charm +small\n      mood +tiny\n      say: {You name her before the bell has finished ringing. She says, \"Hm,\" which from Vey means a great deal.|\"Professor,\" you say to the new student in the back row. The glamour falls away like a dropped shawl. Vey looks almost pleased.|You name the attentive portrait. The paint slides off Vey's face like a curtain. \"Hm,\" she says.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: {You accuse a perfectly ordinary classmate. He will remember it. So will Vey, who was standing behind you.|You accuse the quiet student in the back row, who turns out to be a quiet student in the back row. Vey was the mop.|You point at the portrait. The portrait points back. Vey, at the lectern after all, makes a note.}\n\n@event illu_courtyard_hour\ntitle: Holding the Courtyard\ntrack: illusion\nlevel: 3\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: sparkle\ntags: class magic danger\nscene: A student standing very still in a busy courtyard, sweat on their brow, while a flock of illusory swallows circles above the crowd.\ntext: {Advanced Illusion leaves the classroom. Hold an illusion over the busy courtyard for an hour, and don't let it fray.|Another courtyard hour. The porters step round you now.|The courtyard. {final_year: One of the last times you'll hold this sky.|Your hands know the weight of it.}} {A flock of swallows today.|Rain that doesn't wet anyone.|{season == \"winter\": Snow that never lands.|{season == \"spring\": Blossom that never lands.|{season == \"autumn\": Leaves that never land.|Thistledown that never lands.}}}}\n> left: Hold it the full hour\n  charm +small\n  health -small\n  say: {By the end your head rings like a struck bell. Nobody looked up once, which is how you know it worked.|A first-year holds out a hand to catch something you made. You keep it falling for her until the bell.|You hold it, then sit on the fountain's edge for longer than you'd like anyone to see.}\n> right: Keep it small and steady\n  study illusion +small\n  say: {You keep only a corner of it, perfect, for an hour. Vey says ambition is cheap and an hour is not.|You shrink it to a patch of weather over one bench. It never frays, and nobody notices, which is the point and the pity.|You keep it small: a few flakes over one bench, falling for the hour. A first-year sits under it and doesn't know why she's happy.}\n\n@event illu_original_work\ntitle: An Illusion of Your Own\ntrack: illusion\nlevel: 3\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: eye\ntags: class magic\nscene: A darkened studio room, a student alone at the centre, and around them the half-formed image of a lit window at dusk.\ntext: {Original work, an illusion nobody taught you. Vey asks one question of each piece. \"What does it make people feel?\"|More original work. Vey's question hasn't changed; your answers get longer.|Studio morning. {final_year: Next year, Vey says, she'll ask someone else.|Vey goes round the room asking her question.}} {A classmate has made a door that sighs.|Someone has made the smell of rain on hot stone.|Someone's illusion is humming.}\n> left: Make something true\n  study illusion +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {You make the window of a house you grew up near, at dusk. Vey stands in front of it a long time and writes nothing down.|You make a corridor that remembers who walked down it. It's sadder than you meant. Vey says that's usually the good sign.|You make the hum of a kitchen at night, kettle cooling. Vey sits down in front of it, which she never does.}\n> right: Make something dazzling\n  challenge enchant using charm standard+1 steady:\n    win:\n      charm +small\n      renown +small\n      say: {A staircase of light climbs into the ceiling and keeps going. The class goes quiet. \"Yes,\" says Vey. \"But what does it feel like?\"|Your piece fills the room with lanterns, one for each person watching. Vey counts them, and finds one for herself.|Your piece hums and blazes and then, at the end, goes very quiet and small. Vey writes \"that last part\" in her notes.}\n    lose:\n      mana -small\n      mood -small\n      say: {It's dazzling for a breath and then empty, like a fair after the fair has gone.|Vey watches it all, politely. \"Very bright,\" she says. You'd have preferred anything else.|It hums, and blazes, and Vey waits politely for it to stop. \"And when it's over?\" she asks. You don't have an answer.}\n\n# ---------------- Beastcraft & Flight (track: beastcraft) ----------------\n\n@event beast_mucking_out\ntitle: Mucking Out\ntrack: beastcraft\nlevel: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: paw\ntags: class nature\nscene: A student with a pitchfork in a straw-strewn stable, a hippogriff watching from its stall with a look of deep judgement.\ntext: {Beastcraft I begins in the stables, with a pitchfork. Professor Brask says you don't touch a creature until you've cleaned up after it.|Pitchforks. Brask still hasn't said when that rule ends.|The stables, early. You know which stall to start with.} {The hippogriff in the end stall is watching you.|Owls shift in the rafters, unimpressed.|{season == \"summer\": The heat doubles the smell.|A cold wind finds every gap.}}\n> left: Muck out properly\n  vigor +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {Two stalls, a barrow and a blister. Brask inspects your work and calls the straw \"nearly decent\".|You finish before anyone else. The hippogriff treads straight through your clean straw, deliberately.|Brask says nothing, which you've learned is the best review she gives humans.}\n> right: Bow to the hippogriff first\n  challenge feat standard:\n    win:\n      study beastcraft +small\n      mood +tiny\n      say: {You bow low and hold it. The hippogriff considers, then bows back. Brask writes your name on the board, without comment.|The hippogriff lets you near enough to see where its feathers turn to fur. You'll remember the exact line.|You bow to the hippogriff in the end stall, as you always do now. It bows back before you've finished.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      say: {You bow too fast. The hippogriff takes it as a challenge and accepts.|The hippogriff looks at your bow, then at your hat, and shoves you into the barrow to get at it. It eats the hat.|You bow and the hippogriff ignores you entirely, which is worse than the hat. You muck out under its scorn.}\n\n@event beast_hover_line\ntitle: The Hover Line\ntrack: beastcraft\nlevel: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: broom\ntags: class danger\nscene: A line of students hovering a foot above a lawn on brooms, one broom drifting out of line, a stern professor counting slowly.\ntext: {Flight is half the course. Brask lines you up on the lawn to hover a foot up and hold still while she counts.|The hover line. Brask counts slowly on purpose, you're sure.|Hover line. You and your broom have an understanding, of sorts.} {Somebody's broom keeps edging toward the lake.|An owl has landed on the end of a broom, and its rider is pretending not to notice.|Brask says, as always, that brooms are harder to reason with than griffons.}\n> left: Hold still until the count\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You hold it until forty. Your legs remember it for a day.|The broom bucks once at thirty-one. You hold on. Brask gets to fifty and nods.|Steady all the way. Brask says it's nice to see a broom that knows who's in charge, and she means the broom.}\n> right: Study how the others fall off\n  study beastcraft +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You stand at the end and watch. You learn a great deal about balance, and nobody lets you forget you didn't fly.|You take notes on every wobble. Brask reads them and says you should consider a career in inquests.|You watch the owl on the broom-end win the hover line. Brask writes down the owl's name.}\n\n@event beast_feeding_round\ntitle: Feeding Round\ntrack: beastcraft\nlevel: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: feather\ntags: class nature\ncast:\n  partner: any where tagged(partner, student)\nscene: Two students carrying buckets between stalls, a sulky griffon chick in a nest of straw, owls leaning down from the rafters.\ntext: {Feeding round, in pairs. You and {partner} have two buckets and a list, and the griffon chick is first on it.|Feeding round with {partner}. The griffon chick has heard the buckets.|You and {partner} could do the round blindfolded now. The chick would still sulk.} {Brask has written \"DO NOT HURRY\" at the top of the list.|The owls lean down from the rafters to watch.|{partner} is humming to the buckets.}\n> left: Take the griffon chick\n  challenge feat standard wild:\n    win:\n      vigor +small\n      rel partner +tiny\n      say: {The chick eats from the bucket, then from your hand, then tries your sleeve. {partner} applauds quietly.|It sulks, then eats, then sulks about having eaten. Brask says that's progress for a griffon.|You and {partner} feed the chick in turns, humming. It sulks, but eats in rhythm.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      say: {The chick bites the bucket, then the hand holding it. {partner} finishes the round alone, kindly.|It knocks the bucket over, and you with it, and sits in it, glaring. You spend the hour, bruised and soaked, fishing out its breakfast.|{partner}'s humming puts the chick in a mood. It takes the mood out on your hand.}\n> right: Take the owls instead\n  study beastcraft +small\n  rel partner +tiny\n  say: {You learn the owls' names from the tags on their perches, and what a pellet is, in detail.|Up and down the ladder for an hour. {partner} survives the chick and is grateful in a quiet way.|The owls take their mice with great ceremony. One of them seems to be counting yours.}\n\n@event beast_hare_paddock\ntitle: Running with Hares\ntrack: beastcraft\nlevel: 2\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: bolt\ntags: class magic\nscene: Students sprinting across a paddock alongside a scattering of brown hares, one student's feet suddenly much faster than the rest of them.\ntext: {Beastcraft II teaches haste the old way. Run with the hares until their speed rubs off. \"Borrow it,\" Brask says. \"Don't steal it.\"|The hare paddock. The hares remember you.|Hares. You dream in sudden right angles now.} {The grass is wet and the hares don't care.|Brask times you with a crooked hourglass.|{season == \"summer\": It's hot and the hares are lazy.|The paddock smells of rain.}}\n> left: Run until it catches\n  vigor +small\n  health -small\n  say: {For three strides you're faster than you've any right to be. Then you're in the hedge.|You catch it on the fourth lap, a lightness behind the knees. You pay for it with a nap you can't refuse.|The hares let you keep up for a moment, out of curiosity. It's the best moment of your week.}\n> right: Watch the hares and take notes\n  study beastcraft +small\n  say: {You sketch the way a hare turns, everything at once, no warning. Brask says your sketch is better than your running.|You note how the hares rest between bursts. Brask reads it and says, \"Yes. That's the half everyone forgets.\"|You sketch the hares in sudden right angles. Brask pins your page up by the paddock gate.}\n\n@event beast_complaints\ntitle: The Complaints Hour\ntrack: beastcraft\nlevel: 2\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: owl\ntags: class nature\nscene: A student sitting on an upturned bucket in a stable, surrounded by animals who all seem to be talking at once.\ntext: {Beast-speech practice. You sit on a bucket in the stables and listen. Every creature has been waiting to complain, and the owls start with the draught.|Another hour on the bucket. The owls open, as always, with the draught.|The bucket, the owls, the draught. Brask calls it fieldwork.} {The hippogriff wants different hay. It is very specific.|The griffon chick has a list.|{gpa >= 3: Brask leaves you alone with them, which feels like trust.|Brask stays near the door, which feels like supervision.}}\n> left: Hear every complaint out\n  study beastcraft +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {The owls talk for forty minutes about the draught. You understand every word now, which is the trouble.|The hippogriff explains its views on hay at length. You come away fluent and exhausted.|A mouse you didn't know was there wants to be on the record. You put it on the record.}\n> right: Fix the draught yourself\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You climb into the rafters with a hammer and a plank. The owls supervise. The draught stops, and so do the complaints, briefly.|You patch the gap with straw and a board. The owls say thank you, then mention the window.|You fix the draught. The owls thank you, then move on to the window, the hay and the mouse, in order.}\n\n@event beast_lake_flight\ntitle: Over the Lake\ntrack: beastcraft\nlevel: 3\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nlesson: mirror_lake\nart: feather\ntags: class danger\nscene: A student on a hippogriff's back skimming low over a mirror-still lake, their reflection racing along beneath them.\ntext: {Advanced Beastcraft. A hippogriff each, over the lake, no saddle unless you ask. Brask won't think less of you for asking. She'll just remember.|The lake flight. Your hippogriff knows the way.|Over the lake. {final_year: You catch yourself memorising the view.|Your reflection keeps pace.}} {The water is perfectly still.|A barge below, its passengers waving.|{season == \"summer\": The air is full of midges.|The wind off the water stings.}}\n> left: Ride bareback, low over the water\n  challenge feat standard+1:\n    win:\n      vigor +small\n      mood +small\n      say: {You skim so low a wingtip draws a line across the lake. For one breath you and your reflection touch hands.|The hippogriff banks and you go with it, as if you'd always known how. Brask says nothing at all, then grins.|You fly low enough for your reflection to keep pace, wingtip to wingtip. You don't want to land.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {The hippogriff dips and you don't. The lake is colder than it looks. It always is.|You slide off on the turn and hang on by a fistful of feathers, all the way back to shore.|You race your reflection and your reflection wins. You surface laughing, and very cold.}\n> right: Take the saddle and the high circuit\n  study beastcraft +small\n  say: {Saddled, high and steady. You see all seven towers at once, and nobody sees you.|The safe circuit, twice. Brask remembers, as promised, and nods at you all the same.|You take the saddle and the high circuit, and look down at the lake as if memorising it. Brask lets you take a second lap.}\n\n@event beast_manticore_rounds\ntitle: Manticore Rounds\ntrack: beastcraft\nlevel: 3\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: paw\ntags: class danger\nscene: A manticore lying in straw with its eyes half-closed, one student gently holding its scorpion tail while another hums nearby.\ntext: {Advanced students check the manticore once a month. Someone holds the tail; someone hums the lullaby. Brask lets you choose.|Manticore rounds. It knows the lullaby better than you do.|The manticore yawns at you, which is either a welcome or a threat.} {Its tail twitches in its sleep.|It has been off its food, Brask says, and is in a mood.|{final_year: You wonder who'll hold the tail after you.|Brask has brought a bucket of meat and a smaller one of patience.}}\n> left: Hold the tail\n  challenge feat standard+1 wild:\n    win:\n      vigor +small\n      renown +tiny\n      say: {You hold the tail still while Brask checks the sting. It's warm and heavy and does not, at any point, stab you.|The manticore lets you hold its tail the way a cat lets you hold a paw. Under protest, briefly, as a favour.|You hold the tail while Brask feeds it from the bucket. It eats from her hand and watches you the whole time.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      say: {The tail flicks. You let go just in time, mostly. Sister Amaranth asks no questions; she's seen this course before.|It rolls over, tail and all, onto you. Nothing breaks, but you're bruised in the exact shape of a manticore.|It's in a mood, and the tail knows. You let go a moment too late and spend an hour with Sister Amaranth.}\n> right: Hum the lullaby\n  study beastcraft +small\n  say: {You hum the whole lullaby twice, off-key on the high bit. The manticore sleeps through its own check-up.|You hum. A classmate holds the tail. Brask says the humming is the harder job, and nobody believes her.|You hum while Brask brings the meat. The manticore eats, slowly, and for once the mood lifts.}\n\n# ---------------- Umbral Theory (track: umbral) ----------------\n\n@event umbral_dim_lamps\ntitle: Reading by Shadow\ntrack: umbral\nlevel: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: lantern\ntags: class mystery\nscene: A dim lecture room at the top of a tower, objects hidden under cloths on a table, their shadows thrown long on the wall behind.\ntext: {Umbral Theory I, top of the tower, lamps low on purpose. Name what's under each cloth by its shadow alone.|The cloths are back. The lamps are as low as ever.|Shadow reading. The dark no longer seems strange, which the professor says is the first lesson.} {One shadow is slightly larger than its object.|The professor moves the lamp an inch, and every answer changes.|{season == \"summer\": It's warm outside, never up here.|Rain on the windows makes the shadows shiver.}}\n> left: Read them patiently\n  study umbral +small\n  say: {A teapot, a skull, a folded letter addressed to you. You open it later. It's blank.|You name every shadow but one. Under that cloth there is nothing at all. The professor doesn't explain.|Slowly, you get them all. The shadows told you, the professor says; you only had to stop interrupting.}\n> right: Slip in a false shadow\n  challenge deceive standard:\n    win:\n      guile +small\n      say: {You add the shadow of a cat to the wall. Three classmates write \"cat\". The professor writes your name, and underlines it.|Your false shadow passes all morning. The professor only catches it when it doesn't move with the lamp.|Your false shadow shivers with the rain like the real ones. Nobody finds it. The professor shakes your hand, which is cold.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: {Your false shadow points the wrong way. The professor turns it round gently, like straightening a picture.|Your shadow-cat sits up and leaves. It wasn't meant to do that. The professor doesn't seem surprised.|The rain makes every shadow shiver except yours. The professor notices the one that keeps still.}\n\n@event umbral_chalk_ward\ntitle: Pale Shells\ntrack: umbral\nlevel: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: shield\ntags: class magic\nscene: Students behind faint, pale bubbles of light as a professor flicks pieces of chalk at them from the front of a dim room.\ntext: {Ward drill, in pairs. One of you raises a pale shell over the other, and the professor lets the lamp-shadows loose along the floor to find the gap.|Pairs again. The professor's shadows have learned new tricks since last time.|The paired drill. You know how the shadows like to come: low, and from behind.} {Your partner's shell keeps flickering.|Your partner is a first-year who has never held a ward for more than a minute.|{gpa >= 3: The professor pairs you with the weakest student, on purpose.|The professor pairs you with someone as nervous as you are.}}\n> left: Hold the shell over both of you\n  study umbral +small\n  mana -small\n  say: {You stretch your shell over your partner as well. The shadows nose along its edge for an hour and find nothing. You feel every touch in your teeth.|Two people under one ward is twice the weight. Yours holds until the bell, just.|The shadows give up on your shell and go after the next pair. The professor nods at you, once.}\n> right: Coach your partner through theirs\n  guile +small\n  say: {You talk your partner through it, breath by breath. Their shell holds for nine minutes, then a shadow slips under it and makes you both jump. \"Teaching,\" says the professor, \"is also a ward.\"|You keep your own shell down and watch for the gaps in theirs. By the end, they've stopped flickering. You've learned where shadows like to get in.|You coach your nervous partner, and you're nervous too. The shadows come low and from behind; you both turn in time.}\n\n@event umbral_quiet_minutes\ntitle: The Quiet Minutes\ntrack: umbral\nlevel: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: candle\ntags: class mystery\nscene: A circle of students sitting perfectly still in near darkness, a single candle in the middle, everyone listening very hard.\ntext: {Each lesson opens with ten minutes of silence. \"Listen to what's hidden,\" the professor says, and puts out every lamp but one.|The quiet minutes. You can hear the tower settle, stone by stone.|Ten minutes of silence. You look forward to it, and also not.} {Someone's stomach rumbles at the worst moment.|The candle leans toward the door, though nothing comes in.|{season == \"summer\": A bee is lost in the room somewhere.|The wind hums in the stairwell.}}\n> left: Listen properly\n  study umbral +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {You hear the tower breathing, which it shouldn't, and a sigh on the stair that nobody makes.|You hear the lake, which is impossible from up here. The professor says that's normal for this room. It is not reassuring.|In the silence you hear someone you miss, very faintly. When the lamps come up, you don't mention it.}\n> right: Quietly finish other homework\n  challenge deceive adept-:\n    win:\n      guile +small\n      say: {You finish a whole essay by feel in the dark. The professor never notices, or never says.|Not a scratch of the quill. When the lamps come up, your essay is done and your face is innocent.|You write in the dark with the wind humming the rhythm. The essay is done when the lamps come up.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: {When the lamps come up the professor is behind you, reading your essay. \"Your third paragraph is weak,\" she says.|Your quill squeaks. In that silence it sounds like a scream. Everyone turns.|A gust slams the stairwell door and you drop your quill. It rolls, loudly, to the professor's feet.}\n\n@event umbral_short_distances\ntitle: Short Distances\ntrack: umbral\nlevel: 2\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: ghost\ntags: class magic danger\nscene: A student stepping into the shadow under one desk and halfway out of the shadow under another, the room's lamps burning low.\ntext: {Umbral Theory II brings the shadowstep, desk to desk. \"The shadows in between are not friendly,\" the professor says, \"so don't linger.\"|Shadowstep drill. Three desks this time, then four.|The desks. You know the way between them, and the cold that lives there.} {Someone comes out under the wrong desk.|The lamps are lower than usual.|{gpa >= 3: The professor sets you the long way round.|The professor sets you the short way, and watches.}}\n> left: Step across\n  challenge deceive standard:\n    win:\n      guile +small\n      say: {In, through, out. It takes no time and lasts forever. You come out under the far desk with frost on your sleeves.|Four desks, clean. Something in between brushes your ankle, curious, and lets you pass.|You take your set route and come out exactly where the professor pointed. The cold in between nods at you, almost.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {You come out one desk short and somewhat upside down. The cold stays in your fingers until lunch.|You linger, just for a moment. It's enough. You come back pale, and the professor sits you by the one warm lamp.|The professor's route is longer than you'd like. You come out at the end, but so does the cold, with you.}\n> right: Map the route on paper first\n  study umbral +small\n  say: {You draw every shadow in the room, and where it goes at each hour. The professor keeps your map.|You work out the route in ink and walk it once, slowly, in the light. It's dull, and correct.|You map the set route desk by desk. The professor checks your map and adds one shadow you'd missed.}\n\n@event umbral_hidden_object\ntitle: Present but Unseen\ntrack: umbral\nlevel: 2\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: key\ntags: class mystery\nscene: A dim classroom being searched by several students at once while one of them sits calmly at a desk with a small brass key in plain sight.\ntext: {Hide a small key in this room, present but unseen. \"Hiding is not removal,\" the professor says. \"Removal is for amateurs.\"|The key, and somewhere to hide it. Your classmates are better at finding things.|The key exercise. Half the class pats their pockets out of habit.} {The key is brass today.|The key is silver and very cold.|Last time a key hid in a shadow for two days.}\n> left: Hide it, and stay calm\n  challenge deceive standard:\n    win:\n      guile +small\n      mood +tiny\n      say: {You leave the key on the desk in plain sight, in your own shadow. Nobody sees it. The professor smiles, which is rarer than a key.|You hide it in the dark behind your own ear. They search the room twice and never once look at you.|You hide the key in a shadow and leave it there for two days, like the last one. Nobody finds it. The professor does, and approves.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: {A first-rate hiding place, found in under a minute by someone who simply asked the shadow.|You hide it so well you can't find it yourself. The professor retrieves it from the wall with a sigh.|You hide it in a shadow, and the shadow moves with the afternoon. By the bell the key is in the corridor.}\n> right: Search for everyone else's\n  study umbral +small\n  say: {You find four keys, one in a hat, one in a hinge, one in a shadow that didn't want to let go.|You find one key and a pencil nobody has missed for years. The professor counts both.|You find the key that hid for two days. It's still in its shadow, and you have to ask it nicely to come out.}\n\n@event umbral_stair_wards\ntitle: The Old Stair Wards\ntrack: umbral\nlevel: 3\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: shield\ntags: class mystery\nscene: A student kneeling on an ancient spiral stair, hands pressed to a faint ring of light set into the stone.\ntext: {Advanced students renew the small wards on the tower's old stair. Some are newer than the stone. Some, the professor says, are much older.|The stair wards. You know which step hums.|The stair. {final_year: Someone else will kneel here next year.|Your knees know every step.}} {One ward is warm, as if someone's just left.|Something beyond a ward leans back when you touch it.|The stair lamps are mostly a suggestion.}\n> left: Renew them carefully\n  study umbral +small\n  mana -small\n  say: {Step by step, ring by ring. Each ward takes a little of you and hums.|You renew eleven and pause at the twelfth. Leave that one, the professor says, and doesn't say whose it is.|At the top you're light-headed and the wards are bright. Something on the other side has gone quiet.}\n> right: Study the oldest ward instead\n  guile +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {You trace the oldest ring with one finger. The pattern folds in on itself like a knot nobody was meant to untie, and you feel watched for noticing even that.|The old ward is cold, careful work, made by someone who was afraid. You can feel the fear still in the stone.|You study the oldest ward by your hands alone, the lamps being no help. It's older than the stair. You're sure of that much.}\n\n@event umbral_errand\ntitle: The Shadow's Errand\ntrack: umbral\nlevel: 3\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: moon\ntags: class magic mystery\nscene: A student standing in lamplight with no shadow at all, while across the room their shadow lifts a book from a shelf.\ntext: {Original work. Send your own shadow on an errand across the room, the professor says, and have it come back. \"It will want to wander. They all do.\"|Shadow errands. Yours picks its own route now.|Errand day. {final_year: Does your shadow know you're leaving soon?|Your shadow seems eager, which worries you.}} {Someone's shadow is still under a desk from last week.|The professor's own shadow is missing today.|The lamps are low and the shadows long.}\n> left: Send it, and trust it\n  challenge deceive standard+1:\n    win:\n      guile +small\n      mood +tiny\n      say: {Your shadow crosses the room, lifts a book, and brings it back open at a page you needed. You didn't ask it to.|It comes back with a feather from the rafters, which wasn't the errand. The professor counts it as initiative.|Your shadow goes out long and comes back short, carrying a stub of candle. You don't know where it got it.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      health -tiny\n      say: {It comes back an hour late and a little the wrong shape. For a day it lags behind you on stairs, sulking, and you can't get warm.|It doesn't come back until dusk. When it does, it smells of the lake. The professor makes you sit with the lamp until your hands stop shaking with cold.|It stretches out under the low lamps and doesn't want to come back. When it does, it's longer than you for a day.}\n> right: Keep it on a short leash\n  study umbral +small\n  mana -small\n  say: {Your shadow goes as far as the window and no further. It looks out a long time before it comes back.|Two yards and home. Dull, safe and precise. The professor says precision is how shadow-workers get old.|You keep it close under the low lamps. It strains toward the long shadows and comes back to you, reluctantly.}\n"},{"path":"content/events/pools/courses_hidden.grim","text":"# Baseline course-morning pools, group courses_hidden (docs/authoring/pools.md):\n# the three hidden courses, Artifice, Starcalling and Hollow Studies. Repeatable routine events, two each at level I.\n\n# ---------------- Artifice (track: artifice, skills alchemy arcana; Mir's Workshop) ----------------\n\n@event art_escapement_drill\ntitle: The Ticking Hour\ntrack: artifice\nlevel: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: gear\ntags: class study\nscene: A cluttered workshop bench covered in tiny brass gears, files and loupes, a row of half-built clockwork hearts ticking out of time with one another.\ntext: {Artifice begins with a heart, a fist of clockwork that must tick evenly before it is allowed to think.|Your clockwork heart is waiting where you left it, sulking at the wrong speed.|You can hear your own heart on the bench from the doorway now. Its tick is almost yours.} {The bench beside you ticks like hail.|One heart near the door is running backwards, proudly.|The lecturer listens to each bench with eyes closed.} {season == \"winter\": The brass is cold enough to bite.|The workshop smells of solder.}\n> left: File every tooth by hand\n  alchemy +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {Tooth by tooth, under a loupe, until your eyes water. The tick evens out by the bell.|You file all morning and burn one thumb on the solder. The heart ticks steady and slightly smug.|It's slow, fiddly work, and your hands learn it faster than your head does.}\n> right: Charm the spring to keep time\n  challenge enchant standard:\n    win:\n      arcana +small\n      study artifice +tiny\n      say: {The charm settles into the spring like a cat into a basket. Your heart ticks like a metronome.|You whisper the rhythm into the coil and it keeps it. The lecturer opens one eye, then closes it again.|The charm takes your own pulse as its pattern. The lecturer listens with eyes closed and nods: your heart ticks in time with you.}\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {The spring takes the charm too literally and unwinds all at once, across the room.|Your heart ticks perfectly for a minute, then very fast, then not at all. Someone hands you a fire bucket just in case.|The charm copies your pulse a little too well, and when you get nervous, so does the heart. The lecturer listens, eyes closed, and winces.}\n\n@event art_stirring_cauldron\ntitle: The Cauldron That Stirs\ntrack: artifice\nlevel: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: cauldron\ntags: class magic\nscene: A copper cauldron on iron legs stirring itself with a long brass arm while students crowd round with notebooks and a wary distance.\ntext: {The class project is a cauldron that stirs itself. It does, mostly, in whatever direction it pleases.|The cauldron has been rebuilt again. It stirs with a new brass arm and the same old temper.|The cauldron recognises you, or behaves as if it does.} {Today it stirs clockwise, then argues.|Today it stirs so fast the porridge in it is climbing out.|Today the arm keeps tapping the rim like an impatient guest.} {final_year: You'll miss its moods, a little.|The fire buckets by the door are full, for once.}\n> left: Recut the runes on the arm\n  study artifice +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You spend the morning with a graver and a magnifier, recutting the stirring runes one stroke at a time. Your fingers are nicked; the arm moves sweetly.|Fine work in brass, the whole lesson long. The arm stirs evenly, and you have solder burns in a neat little row.|The arm taps the rim the whole time you work, as if hurrying you. When the runes are recut it stops tapping and stirs, which may be thanks.}\n> right: Test it on a live brew\n  challenge brew standard:\n    win:\n      alchemy +small\n      study artifice +tiny\n      say: {The cauldron stirs your brew exactly as the recipe asks, and you take the credit for both.|The brew turns the right colour at the right minute. The cauldron seems to know it did well.|The arm stops tapping the moment your brew goes in, and stirs it perfectly. It seems to like you.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: {The arm speeds up, the brew climbs the walls, and the whole class steps back as one.|The cauldron stirs your brew into something with the texture of an argument. You scrub it out until the bell.|The arm taps the rim, impatient, then stirs your brew in a temper. You clean the ceiling until the bell.}\n\n# ---------------- Starcalling (track: starcalling, skills lore arcana; the Observatory, at night) ----------------\n\n@event star_names_in_the_dark\ntitle: Names in the Dark\ntrack: starcalling\nlevel: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: star\ntags: class study\nscene: A dark brass dome open to a sky full of stars, a student at the eyepiece of a great telescope while another recites from memory, breath steaming.\ntext: {Starcalling is taught by appointment, at night, and this morning's note gives you tonight's hour. It begins with names. \"You cannot call what you cannot name.\"|Another note at breakfast, another night appointment under the dome. The list of names is longer than it was.|Tonight's appointment under the dome again. You know where the draught comes in.} {The note warns that the great telescope has been swinging round on its own.|Meteors are expected tonight.|The note is written on the back of a star chart.} {season == \"winter\": Tonight your breath will freeze on the eyepiece.|Tonight the air will smell of cut grass from the lawns below.}\n> left: Recite the names until perfect\n  lore +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {You say them over and over into the cold until they stop being words and start being places.|Name after name, in order and then backwards. By the end you could find them blindfold, and nearly have to.|You stumble on the same name four times. The fifth time it comes out right, and you feel it land somewhere.}\n> right: Let the telescope choose a star\n  challenge research standard-1:\n    win:\n      study starcalling +small\n      say: {The telescope settles on a star you've never heard of. You find its name in the old charts before the lecturer can tell you.|Wherever it points, you follow, and the charts agree with it. That's a whole lesson learned the other way round.|You let the telescope choose and check it against the chart on the back of the note. It's right, and so are you.}\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {The telescope picks a star and then changes its mind every ten minutes. You chase it round the dome until midnight.|You spend the night at the eyepiece and come down with a stiff neck and one name you aren't sure of.|The telescope chooses a star that isn't on the chart on the back of the note. You spend the night in the draught, trying to prove one of them wrong.}\n\n@event star_thread_of_light\ntitle: A Thread of Light\ntrack: starcalling\nlevel: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: sparkle\ntags: class magic\nscene: A student in a dark observatory with hands cupped upward, a single silver thread of starlight running down through the open dome into their palms.\ntext: {Tonight's lesson, says the morning note, is to call down a single thread of a named star's light, no thicker than a hair.|Tonight's lesson is the same as ever: one thread, one star, held as long as you can bear it.|You've called down threads before. The morning note says tonight the lecturer wants it held longer.} {The morning sky is clear, and the night promises to be.|Thin cloud is forecast to cross the star you've chosen.|At breakfast the others are already practising the hand positions.}\n> left: Call the thread down yourself\n  study starcalling +small\n  mana -small\n  say: {The thread comes down cold and bright and pours into your palms. You hold it until your hands shake.|It takes three tries. On the third, the light drops into your hands like water from a great height.|A hair of light, the length of the whole tower. When it fades, your fingers glow for an hour.}\n> right: Keep the night log instead\n  arcana +small\n  say: {You write down every thread the others call, with the star, the minute and how long it held. It's dull, cold work, and you start to see the pattern.|You hold the lamp and the log while the others call. Watching from outside, you see exactly where their hands go wrong.|Tonight the others hold their threads longer, and you note to the second when each one breaks. The pattern is in the breaking.} {season == \"winter\": Your ink keeps freezing.|The lecturer reads your log twice.}\n> up[starlight]: Hold your own starlight up to it\n  study starcalling +small\n  say: {You light your own starlight and the thread finds it, like calling to like. It holds twice as long.|Your starlight steadies the thread, and the lecturer has you show the others how.|The thread drops straight into the light you're already holding. For a moment the dome is bright as noon.}\n\n# ---------------- Hollow Studies (track: hollow_studies, skills guile lore; the headmistress, a room without windows) ----------------\n\n@event hollow_sealed_boxes\ntitle: How It Was Sealed\ntrack: hollow_studies\nlevel: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: lock\ntags: class mystery\nscene: A windowless room lined with black-bound books, a single lamp on a table, a tall calm woman setting a small locked box in front of a student.\ntext: {Each lesson, the headmistress sets a sealed thing in front of you. \"Tell me how it was closed,\" she says. \"Not what is inside. How.\"|Another sealed thing on the table. Today it is {a letter with a wax seal|a small lacquered box|a jar with its lid charmed shut}.|The headmistress places today's sealed thing with a little smile. She has started choosing harder ones.} {The lamp hisses.|The books on the shelves breathe slowly in the dark.|She sits with her hands folded, unhurried.}\n> left: Study the seal for the hour\n  lore +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {You trace every line of it without touching. At the end you can name each step of the sealing, in order. She nods once.|It takes the whole hour and a headache, but you work it out backwards, layer by layer.|You get two layers right and one wrong. She tells you which, kindly, and you'll remember.}\n> right: Open it without leaving a trace\n  challenge deceive standard:\n    win:\n      guile +small\n      study hollow_studies +tiny\n      say: {You open it and close it again, and nothing shows. She looks at it, then at you, and laughs, short and delighted.|Open, closed, not a mark. \"Good,\" she says. \"Now never do that to a door.\"|It's a harder one, and it opens anyway, and closes, and shows nothing. She unfolds her hands for the first time all lesson.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: {The seal cracks with a tiny, final sound. The headmistress writes something in her book. She remembers every mistake, and now there is one more.|It opens, and it shows. \"Everyone breaks one,\" she says pleasantly. \"Rarely so loudly.\"|The harder seals are harder for a reason. It opens with a mark that won't go, and she lets you sit with it, unhurried.}\n\n@event hollow_reading_the_blank\ntitle: Reading the Blank Page\ntrack: hollow_studies\nlevel: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: book\ntags: class mystery\nscene: A student bent over an apparently blank page by lamplight, faint lines of ink surfacing under their fingertips, black-bound books leaning in from the shelves.\ntext: {\"Most things that are hidden are not gone,\" the headmistress says, and gives you a blank page. \"Find what was written. Gently.\"|A new blank page today. It feels warmer than paper should.|You know the weight of hidden writing in a page by now, like a pocket with something in it.} {The books on the nearest shelf seem to lean closer.|The lamp burns low and blue.|Somewhere in the stacks, a book turns its own page.} {final_year: She asks where you'll go after graduation, and listens.|She is reading while you work, and doesn't look up.}\n> left: Coax the ink up slowly\n  study hollow_studies +small\n  mana -small\n  say: {You warm the page with a thread of power, a breath at a time. The writing surfaces slowly, safe and dull. It's someone's laundry list, very carefully hidden.|Line by line, the ink comes up. It's an old timetable. The lesson was in the finding.|It takes most of your strength. The page gives up a single sentence about the weather, decades ago.}\n> right: Read it bare-handed\n  guile +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {The ink comes up fast under your fingers, and so does a chill that stays in your hand until morning.|You read it quickly and well. Your fingertips ache for an hour, and the headmistress hands you a cup of tea without comment.|You find the writing the way you'd find a coin in a pocket, and read it bare-handed. Somewhere in the stacks a page turns, and your fingers are cold until supper.}\n> up[ward]: Let your ward do the reading\n  lore +small\n  say: {Your ward settles over the page like a held breath, and the writing rises into it. The headmistress raises an eyebrow, pleased.|You read through the ward as if through glass. It costs you, but not your hands.|The hidden lines surface inside your shell, one by one, in no hurry.}\n"},{"path":"content/events/pools/courses_upper.grim","text":"# Course-morning pools, group courses_upper (docs/authoring/pools.md):\n# two more events each at levels II and III for the six core courses. Repeatable routine events.\n\n# ---------------- Evocation (track: evocation, skill arcana) ----------------\n\n@event evo_rain_barrel\ntitle: Rain on Demand\ntrack: evocation\nlevel: 2\nlesson: mirror_lake\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: rain\ntags: class magic\nscene: A row of students on a lakeside terrace, each with a small grey cloud above a wooden barrel, one cloud raining sideways onto a neighbour.\ntext: {Tidecalling at Evocation II. Pull a cloud off the lake, park it over a barrel, and fill the barrel to the chalk line. Not over, not under.|The barrels are out on the terrace again, each with a chalk line.|You know your barrel now, the one with the iron band that's slipped.} {Professor Marisol is reading on the steps, under a perfectly dry patch of air.|The wind keeps trying to borrow the clouds.|{season == \"winter\": The clouds come off the lake as sleet.|A heron watches the barrels with professional interest.}}\n> left: Wring it out to the line\n  challenge spell standard steady:\n    win:\n      arcana +small\n      study evocation +tiny\n      say: {The last drop lands on the chalk. Marisol looks up from her book and back down, which counts.|You stop the rain a breath early and let the drip finish the job. Exactly to the line.|You know how much your barrel leaks at the slipped band, and you rain that much extra. Exactly to the line.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      health -tiny\n      say: {Your cloud overshoots, fills the barrel, and keeps going down your collar.|The cloud drifts left and rains on the next barrel, which is not yours, and on its owner, who is very not yours. The splashback finds your collar anyway.|The heron moves at the wrong moment and so does your cloud. It empties itself over the heron, who leaves offended.}\n> right: Drizzle it in, slowly\n  arcana +small\n  mana -small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {It takes the whole lesson. Your cloud is the size of a pillow by the end, and very tired.|You fill it a thimble at a time. The others have gone in for lunch. The line is met.|You drizzle it in while your barrel leaks it out at the band. It's a long lesson, but you win in the end.}\n\n@event evo_ember_stair\ntitle: Carrying the Ember\ntrack: evocation\nlevel: 2\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: flame\ntags: class magic danger\nscene: A student climbing a dim spiral stair, a single glowing ember resting in their bare open palm.\ntext: {Pyromancy at Evocation II. Carry a live ember in your bare palm from the ground floor to the top of the Lecture Tower. Keep it alight. Keep your hand.|The ember drill again. The stair hasn't got any shorter.|Ember day. You've counted the stair by now, and you know where the draught is.} {A door on the third floor opens just as you pass.|Someone ahead of you is climbing very fast and swearing quietly.|The lamps on the stair are out, and your ember is the only light.}\n> left: Feed it, and climb fast\n  challenge feat using arcana standard:\n    win:\n      arcana +small\n      say: {You keep the ember bright with small breaths of heat and take the stairs two at a time. It arrives fat and orange.|You reach the top with the ember glowing and your palm only warm. The lecturer tips it into a brazier without a word.|Your ember lights the dark stair all the way up. People wait on the landings to follow you.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      say: {The ember flares on the landing and you learn exactly how hot a palm can get. You arrive with it alight, and with a blister.|You feed it too much at the draughty window. It bites. You carry it up the last flight in your other hand.|You feed it in the dark and misjudge it. It bites, and you finish the climb by its light and your own swearing.}\n> right: Shield it, and climb slow\n  study evocation +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {You climb at a crawl with the ember cupped against the draughts. It is alight at the top, just. So are your nerves.|Step by step, a held breath at each window. Last up, but the ember's still glowing.|You shield it from the draught you know is coming. It reaches the top small and steady, and so do you.}\n\n@event evo_firebreak\ntitle: The Reed Burn\ntrack: evocation\nlevel: 3\nlesson: mirror_lake\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: flame\ntags: class magic danger\nscene: Advanced students along a lake shore in a line, half of them calling a low wall of fire through dry reeds, half of them holding back a curtain of lake water behind it.\ntext: {Advanced Evocation does real work, the controlled burn of the dead reeds along the lake shore. Pyromancers lead the fire, tidecallers hold the line behind it.|Burn day. The shore smells of smoke for a week afterwards.|The reed burn. You can tell a fire's mood from its smoke now.} {The wind is steady from the lake.|The wind can't decide.|The reeds are drier than anyone would like.} {season == \"summer\": The air is hot already.|Your breath steams.}\n> left: Walk at the fire's head\n  challenge spell standard+1:\n    win:\n      arcana +medium\n      say: {The fire follows your hands down the shore like a dog on a lead. The lecturer lets you take it all the way to the boathouse.|You lead the fire low and even, and it goes out where you tell it. Behind you there's nothing but clean black ground.|You lead the fire through the dry reeds slowly and keep it on a short lead. Not a spark gets away.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -small\n      say: {A gust lifts the fire over your head. The tidecallers drop it in a wall of lake water, and you with it.|The fire jumps the line and runs. It's caught in a minute, but your eyebrows weren't.|The dry reeds go up faster than you can lead them. The tidecallers save the boathouse; you save some of your sleeve.}\n> right: Hold the water line\n  study evocation +small\n  mana -small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You stand in the shallows holding a curtain of lake water for an hour. Nothing gets past you. Nobody notices, which is the point.|Your arms ache from keeping the water up. It's the least glamorous job on the shore and the one the lecturer checks first.|You hold the water line through the worst of it. When the dry reeds flare, your curtain is the one that holds.}\n\n@event evo_drowned_flame\ntitle: Fire Under Water\ntrack: evocation\nlevel: 3\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: candle\ntags: class magic\nscene: A glass tank of water on a scorched bench, a small blue flame burning steadily at the bottom of it.\ntext: {Original work in Advanced Evocation, a fire that burns underwater. Every student gets a tank. Most tanks get a new pane every week.|Your tank is waiting, with a fresh crack along one pane.|The tank again. You talk to the water now, and to the fire, separately.} {Someone's tank has boiled dry and they're staring at it.|A flame at the next bench burns green beneath the water, quite calm.|The lecturer is standing well back today.}\n> left: Light it at the bottom\n  challenge spell standard+1 wild:\n    win:\n      arcana +small\n      mood +tiny\n      say: {A small blue flame takes hold under the water and stays. The bubbles go up in a thin silver line.|It catches, gutters, and settles. The water around it doesn't even warm. You don't know how you did it.|You light it while the lecturer stands well back. It burns, small and stubborn, and the lecturer comes a step closer.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      say: {The water boils in a heartbeat and the pane splits. You wear the steam on your face for the rest of the day.|The flame goes up instead of in. The tank cracks, and your sleeve is wet and scorched at once.|You learn why the lecturer is standing back. The pane goes, and so does your eyebrow.}\n> right: Wrap it in a bubble first\n  arcana +small\n  mana -small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You hold a skin of air around the flame and sink them both. It's half a cheat, and the lecturer says so. It burns all hour.|Your flame sits in a bubble at the bottom like a moth in a jar. Not the assignment, but not nothing.|You wrap it in a bubble, as you always do now. The lecturer says it's time to try without, and doesn't make you.}\n\n# ---------------- Astromancy & Divination (track: astromancy, skill lore) ----------------\n\n@event astro_sealed_forecast\ntitle: Tomorrow's Weather\ntrack: astromancy\nlevel: 2\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: rain\ntags: class study\nscene: A row of sealed envelopes pinned to a corkboard in the observatory, each labelled with a student's name and tomorrow's date.\ntext: {Foresight at Astromancy II. Write down tomorrow's weather to the hour, seal it, pin it to the board. The lecturer opens the envelopes a day later, in public.|The envelopes come out again.|You've written so many forecasts you dream in drizzle.} {Outside the dome the sky is giving nothing away.|Last week's best forecast just said \"Wet\", and was right.|{season == \"winter\": Frost on the dome, and snow somewhere in the future.|Clouds over the lake, moving with intent.}}\n> left: Commit to the hour\n  challenge research standard wild:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      study astromancy +tiny\n      say: {\"Rain at the third bell, stopping at the fourth.\" It does. The lecturer reads it out twice.|You forecast a fog to the hour. When the envelope is opened, the fog is pressing on the dome.|You write \"Sleet at the second bell, from the lake.\" It comes off the lake at the second bell. The lecturer stops reading the others.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: {\"Clear by noon.\" It rains till evening. Your envelope is read out to great enjoyment.|You predicted wind. Tomorrow turns out so still that a feather on the sill doesn't move.|You forecast with great confidence. Tomorrow is the opposite of everything you wrote, in order.}\n> right: Hedge, and learn from it\n  study astromancy +small\n  say: {\"Some weather, at some point.\" The lecturer reads it out flatly and moves on. But you spent the hour watching how the good forecasters work.|You write a careful forecast with three possibilities. It's right, technically. The lecturer calls it \"an umbrella of a prophecy\".|You hedge, and read the others' envelopes after. You can tell the good ones by how short they are.}\n\n@event astro_stubborn_telescope\ntitle: The Stubborn Telescope\ntrack: astromancy\nlevel: 2\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: planet\ntags: class study\nscene: A student tugging at a huge brass telescope in a dome, while it points stubbornly at a different patch of sky.\ntext: {Astromancy II needs exact positions, and that means a night on the great telescope. At the morning lecture your name goes up on tonight's rota. The great telescope, everyone warns you, points wherever it likes.|Your name is on tonight's telescope rota again. The lecturer warns you it is already pointing at something else.|Your night on the telescope again. The lecturer only raises an eyebrow at you as the rota goes up.} {This week it wants the moon.|This week it's fixed on the lake, which is not in the sky.|This week it keeps drifting east, slowly, like a sigh.}\n> left: Argue it round\n  challenge research standard:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      say: {You coax it, inch by inch, and it settles on your star with a creak like a shrug.|You find the right thing to say to an old brass instrument. It points where you ask. You write the position fast.|You coax it off its eastward drift with a gentle hand and a sharp word. It sighs back to your star.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      health -tiny\n      say: {It spends the night pointing where it likes. So do you, in the end, cold and annoyed.|You lean on it. It leans back. You go to bed at the fourth bell with nothing written down.|It drifts east all night, slowly, and takes you with it. You write down a very good position for the wrong star.}\n> right: Work it out from tables\n  lore +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {You leave the telescope to its moods and work the positions out by candlelight from the old tables. It takes until dawn.|Pages of sums by candle. The telescope watches over your shoulder, pointedly.|You use the tables and let the telescope drift where it likes. Your positions are good, and you're in bed by midnight.}\n\n@event astro_long_watch\ntitle: Keeping the Light\ntrack: astromancy\nlevel: 3\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: lantern\ntags: class magic\nscene: A student on the observatory roof before dawn, a glass lantern in their lap holding a single cold point of starlight.\ntext: {Advanced Starlight. At the morning lecture you're given tonight's task. Hold one star's light in a lantern through a whole night on the roof. It will want to go home before dawn. Your job is to keep it.|The night watch on the roof comes round again. After the morning lecture you collect a lantern and a blanket.|Your lantern, your star, your corner of the roof, booked for tonight.} {The star you've drawn is pale and blue.|The star you've drawn is small and stubborn.|The star you've drawn is very old and very faint.} {season == \"winter\": There will be frost on the slates.|The night will be warm and full of moths.}\n> left: Keep it all night\n  lore +small\n  study astromancy +small\n  health -small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {At dawn your star is still in the lantern, blinking at the sun it was never meant to meet. You are blinking too.|You keep it till the lecturer comes up at sunrise. Your eyes are full of sand and your lantern is full of light.|Your old faint star almost goes out at the fourth bell. You talk to it until dawn, and it stays.}\n> right: Let it go at midnight\n  lore +tiny\n  say: {You open the lantern and the light goes up like a kite let loose. You watch it all the way home and then go to bed.|You let it go early. The lecturer's note just says \"Released at midnight.\" You suspect it's not a compliment.|You let the old star go at midnight. It goes slowly, as if it isn't sure of the way. You watch it until you can't.}\n> up[starlight]: Feed it your own starlight\n  lore +small\n  say: {You pour a little of your own starlight into the lantern. The star brightens as if it had been lonely.|Your light and the star's together are enough to read by. You read until dawn.|You give the old star some of your light. It burns brighter than it has in a long time, and you feel it go out of you.}\n\n@event astro_two_futures\ntitle: Both Futures\ntrack: astromancy\nlevel: 3\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: dice\ntags: class magic\nscene: A coin balanced on edge on a dark table in the observatory, with two faint ghostly outcomes hovering above it.\ntext: {Advanced Foresight. The lecturer balances a coin on its edge. Read both futures at once, the one where it falls heads and the one where it falls tails, and hold them both.|The coin on its edge again. You know how it feels to be two places at once now.|The coin. People come out of this lesson pressing their temples.} {Someone in the front row says \"heads\" in two voices.|The lecturer is drinking tea and looking at nobody.|The candles burn straight up, very still.}\n> left: Hold both to the end\n  challenge exam standard+1:\n    win:\n      lore +medium\n      say: {For a long moment you live both futures, side by side, and neither is frightening. Then the coin falls, and one of them goes out.|You hold both until the lecturer flicks the coin. One future folds away, neat as a letter.|You hold both in the still candlelight until the lecturer stops the coin with a finger. Both futures are still there. \"Interesting,\" the lecturer says.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -small\n      say: {The futures tangle and you get a headache that lasts till supper. For an hour you keep reaching for the wrong door handle.|You lose track of which future is which, and of which way is down. The lecturer sits you by an open window.|You hold both too long. For the rest of the day you hear things a moment before they happen, and you hate it.}\n> right: Read one, then the other\n  study astromancy +small\n  mana -small\n  say: {You read heads, then tails, carefully, one after the other. It's not the exercise, but you get both right.|One future at a time. Slower, safer, and the lecturer says you'll get there.|One future, then the other, and you know both now before you read them. The lecturer says that's the start of holding them.}\n\n# ---------------- Alchemy & Healing (track: alchemy, skill alchemy) ----------------\n\n@event alch_unlabelled_vial\ntitle: The Unlabelled Vial\ntrack: alchemy\nlevel: 2\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: bottle\ntags: class study\nscene: A vial of clear liquid on a lab bench beside a strip of paper turning slowly purple, a student squinting at both.\ntext: {Alchemy II hands out an unlabelled vial each, and an hour to say what's in it. Paper strips, a candle, a nose and a notebook are allowed.|Another unlabelled vial. The lecturer enjoys these.|The vial is waiting on your bench. You've started recognising the corks.} {Yours is perfectly clear and smells faintly of pears.|Yours is cloudy and keeps settling into layers.|Yours is warm to the touch.} {The ventilation charm is wheezing again.|The lab is quiet except for scratching quills.|The lecturer is humming, which means the vials are harder this week.}\n> left: Test it the long way\n  alchemy +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {Strip, flame, cold, filter. By the end of the hour you've a name and three reasons for it.|Every test, in order, written up neatly. Your notes are dull and your answer is right.|The warm one takes the whole hour, test by test. It's something you've never met, and you name it anyway, correctly.}\n> right: Trust your nose\n  challenge brew standard wild:\n    win:\n      alchemy +small\n      study alchemy +tiny\n      say: {One sniff and you know it. You write the name and use the spare hour to check yourself.|You tip the vial under your nose and the answer arrives before the smell does. The lecturer raises an eyebrow and a tick.|It's warm, and that tells you most of it. You sniff to confirm. The lecturer stops humming.}\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: {One sniff and your eyes stream for the rest of the morning. You guessed wrong, too.|It's not what you thought, and it is very much up your nose. You spend the hour at the window.|You sniff the warm one. It sniffs back, in a way, and you spend the hour with a damp cloth on your face.}\n\n@event alch_cracked_tray\ntitle: The Cracked Tray\ntrack: alchemy\nlevel: 2\nlesson: infirmary\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: heart\ntags: class magic\nscene: A tray in the infirmary laid with a cracked teacup, a cracked hand mirror and a cracked bone from a teaching skeleton, a student's hands hovering over them.\ntext: {Mend at Alchemy II. Sister Amaranth sets out a tray of cracked things, cup, mirror and bone. \"Mend those,\" she says, \"and then we'll see about people.\"|The tray again. Sister Amaranth has found new things to crack.|Sister Amaranth puts the tray down and nods at it. You know the drill.} {A second-year is waiting on a cot with a sprained wrist from broom practice.|A third-year with a scalded hand from Evocation is waiting.|A first-year is in with a split lip and a long story about a door.}\n> left: Mend the tray, perfectly\n  challenge enchant standard:\n    win:\n      alchemy +small\n      say: {The cracks close like seams being sewn shut. Sister Amaranth runs a thumbnail along the cup and finds nothing.|Cup, mirror, bone, whole. Sister Amaranth says \"Fine,\" which is her highest word.|You mend the tray in half the time and Sister Amaranth sends you to the first-year with the split lip. It mends cleanly.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: {The cup mends with a ridge in it, the mirror with a ripple. Sister Amaranth sets them on the shelf of shame.|You mend the bone a little crooked. Sister Amaranth says that's how you learn and puts it back in the skeleton anyway.|The mirror mends with your face in it, slightly wrong. Sister Amaranth turns it to the wall.}\n> right: Mend the patient instead\n  study alchemy +small\n  mana -small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You skip the tray and take the patient, with Sister Amaranth's hand on your shoulder. It goes slowly. It works.|Sister Amaranth lets you try on the patient while she watches. You're shaky and slow, and you'll never mend a cup the same way again.|You mend the first-year's lip and hear the whole story about the door. It wasn't a door. You keep that to yourself.}\n\n@event alch_lake_fever\ntitle: Lake Fever Season\ntrack: alchemy\nlevel: 3\nlesson: infirmary\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: potion\ntags: class magic\nscene: A row of infirmary cots, students under blankets looking flushed and sorry for themselves, an advanced student drawing a faint grey haze out of one patient's brow.\ntext: {Advanced Alchemy treats real patients. The infirmary has Lake Fever cases, from swimming where they shouldn't. Sister Amaranth wants the fever drawn out, not just cooled.|Lake Fever again. Students never learn, Sister Amaranth says, and neither do lakes.|Fever duty. Sister Amaranth hands you a list without looking up.} {Your patient says they only went in up to the knees.|Your patient is a prefect, very embarrassed.|Your patient insists they were pushed.}\n> left: Draw the fever out by hand\n  challenge enchant standard+1:\n    win:\n      alchemy +small\n      study alchemy +small\n      say: {The fever comes out like a grey thread pulled from wool. Your patient sleeps. You'd like to.|You draw it out slowly and the flush leaves their face. Sister Amaranth checks your work and doesn't check twice.|You draw out the fever and the story with it. They were pushed. Sister Amaranth writes down the name.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {You draw out half of it and the half you drew comes with you. You spend the evening shivering in the next cot.|The cure has to come from somewhere. You wake up two hours later on the floor, with a blanket on you.|The fever comes out angry and goes into you. You spend the night in the next cot, insisting you weren't pushed.}\n> right: Brew the slow tonic\n  alchemy +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {Three hours of stirring willow bark and lake moss. The tonic works by morning, which is slower and safer.|You brew the old remedy by the book. Sister Amaranth tastes it, grimaces, and approves.|You brew the tonic without the book now. Sister Amaranth tastes it and doesn't grimace, which worries you more.}\n> up[purify]: Purify it in one pass\n  study alchemy +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {Your spell pulls the fever clean in a single pass. It leaves you yawning.|One pass of Purify and the patient sits up asking for soup. The tiredness lands on you an hour later.|You purify it in one pass, and your patient sits up and says who pushed them. You're asleep before Sister Amaranth asks.}\n\n@event alch_third_floor_still\ntitle: The Still Cracks\ntrack: alchemy\nlevel: 3\nclass: incident\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: cauldron\ntags: class danger\nscene: A copper still on a long bench, hissing from a fresh crack, a thin green vapour creeping across the laboratory floor.\ntext: {Advanced Alchemy distils essences that would rather be gas. The ventilation charm gave up on the third floor years ago. Today your still cracks.|Distilling day, and a hiss from your still that you know.|Your still has cracked again. You're getting fast at noticing.} {The vapour is green and smells of mint.|The vapour is pale and smells of nothing, which is worse.|The vapour is curling toward the next bench.}\n> left: Mend the crack, still hot\n  challenge enchant standard+1:\n    win:\n      alchemy +small\n      say: {You close the copper under your palms while it's still singing. The hiss stops. The class breathes out.|The crack seals with a sound like a sigh, and the batch is saved. The lecturer opens a window, for form's sake.|You seal it before the vapour reaches the next bench. Its owner never knows how close it came.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -small\n      say: {The crack closes, then reopens with a pop. You breathe in a mouthful and spend the afternoon seeing double.|You burn your fingertips on the copper and the vapour gets out anyway. The lab is cleared for an hour.|The vapour reaches the next bench before you do. Two of you spend the afternoon seeing double.}\n> right: Shout, and clear the bench\n  study alchemy -tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: {You call the warning and clear the bench. The batch is lost, nobody is hurt, and you start again next week.|Everyone is out in the corridor in a minute flat. The lecturer goes back in, a scarf over the mouth, and comes out carrying your ruined batch.|You clear the bench and the one next to it. The lecturer notes that you thought of the neighbours, and the batch is still lost.}\n\n# ---------------- Illusion & Glamour (track: illusion, skill charm) ----------------\n\n@event illu_walking_double\ntitle: The Walking Double\ntrack: illusion\nlevel: 2\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: mirror\ntags: class magic\nscene: Two identical students walking side by side across a lecture hall, one of them leaving no footprints in the chalk dust.\ntext: {Mirror at Illusion II. Make a double of yourself and walk it across the room. \"Standing doubles are furniture,\" says Professor Vey. \"Make it walk.\"|Doubles day. Vey watches feet, not faces.|Your double's walk is improving. It has your slouch now.} {The floor has been dusted with chalk, to show footprints.|Someone's double is walking backwards.|Vey is sitting in the front row, which is unsettling.}\n> left: Walk it right across\n  challenge enchant using charm standard:\n    win:\n      charm +small\n      say: {Your double crosses the room at your pace and even scuffs the chalk. Vey watches its feet the whole way and says nothing, which is high praise.|It walks, it turns, it nearly sits down. Vey says, \"Better.\"|Your double slouches across the room, Vey in the front row watching every step. \"That's you,\" Vey says. It isn't a compliment, quite.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: {Halfway across, your double's legs forget to move and it glides. Vey says it's a ghost, not a person, and moves on.|Your double walks into the wall and keeps walking. The class watches it vanish into the plaster.|Your double sees Vey in the front row and freezes, exactly as you would. It's very lifelike, and it doesn't walk.}\n> right: Keep it still and perfect\n  study illusion +small\n  mana -small\n  say: {Your double stands at your elbow, perfect to the last button. It doesn't move. Vey calls it a very good wardrobe.|You make it flawless and keep it standing. Vey taps its nose, and frowns when it holds.|Your double stands perfectly still in front of Vey, slouch and all. Vey reaches out and adjusts its collar, which holds.}\n\n@event illu_good_news\ntitle: Good News, Read Aloud\ntrack: illusion\nlevel: 2\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: mask\ntags: class magic\nscene: A student standing at a lectern reading from a dull notice while the listening class smiles as though hearing wonderful news.\ntext: {Glamour at Illusion II, and today it's the voice. Vey hands you a dull notice and asks you to read it so the class hears good news.|Another notice from Vey's drawer of very dull notices.|Vey has saved the dullest notice for you.} {Today's is about the drains.|Today's is about a change to the laundry rota.|Today's is a list of overdue library books, in full.}\n> left: Glamour every word\n  challenge deceive using charm standard:\n    win:\n      charm +small\n      say: {By the second line the room is beaming. Someone claps. Vey waits for the clapping to stop and asks what the notice said. Nobody knows.|You read it warm and low, and the class leans in as if you were reading their letters from home.|You read out the overdue books like a list of prize-winners. Three students go to the library that afternoon.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: {Your glamour slips on a long word and the room hears exactly what it is, a notice. Someone yawns on purpose.|You overdo it. The class looks alarmed, as if the notice were about to propose.|The glamour holds for twenty titles and drops on the twenty-first. The class hears the rest exactly as they are.}\n> right: Read it plain, watch others\n  study illusion +small\n  say: {You read it straight and sit down. Then you watch the others, and notice the best of them barely change their voices at all.|Plain reading, polite applause. You spend the hour writing down where the good ones breathe.|You read the list plain. It takes a long time. You learn more watching Vey's face than you would have by trying.}\n> up[glamour]: Glamour the notice itself\n  charm +small\n  renown +tiny\n  say: {You don't touch your voice; you glamour the paper. Every student who reads it over your shoulder sees good news in their own hand. Vey applauds, twice, slowly.|You glamour the ink so it shimmers like a wedding invitation. The class fights over the notice afterwards. It is still, underneath, a very dull notice.|You glamour the list into an honours roll. Half the class goes to look for their names, and finds their overdue books.}\n\n@event illu_false_room\ntitle: The Room That Isn't\ntrack: illusion\nlevel: 3\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: door\ntags: class magic danger\nscene: A student walking confidently into a room that is partly painted light, a real stone wall standing where the illusion shows an open doorway.\ntext: {Advanced Illusion asks for a double of a whole room. Hang it over the real one and walk a classmate through it. Vey's rule is simple. If anyone walks into a wall, it's your fault.|The room exercise. Somebody always walks into something.|You hang your room. You've learned where the real walls are.} {Vey's volunteer today is a porter with a tray of cups.|Vey's volunteer today is Vey.|The real room has been rearranged overnight.}\n> left: Hang the whole room\n  challenge enchant using charm standard+1:\n    win:\n      charm +medium\n      say: {Your walker crosses the false room and sits down in a false chair, which you hold up for them. Vey writes for a long time.|The room holds. Nobody touches a real wall. Vey walks it twice to be sure.|You hang your room over the rearranged one and nobody notices which is which, including, briefly, Vey.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      health -tiny\n      say: {Your walker steps through a door you painted onto solid stone. You catch them as they fall, take the edge of the stone on your own elbow, and walk them to Sister Amaranth with an apology.|Halfway across the room flickers, and the walker freezes. So do you, and you walk hip-first into a real chair.|You hang your room over where the walls were yesterday. The walker finds today's walls with their nose.}\n> right: Chalk the real walls first\n  study illusion +small\n  mana -small\n  say: {You hang half a room, with the real walls marked out in chalk so nobody could miss them. Vey calls it \"a room with a safety rope\".|Safe and visible, chalk lines everywhere. Vey sighs, and then copies down how you did the window.|You chalk the new walls first, all of them. Vey says that's the only correct answer to a rearranged room.}\n\n@event illu_peeling_layers\ntitle: Seeing Through\ntrack: illusion\nlevel: 3\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: eye\ntags: class magic\nscene: A lecture hall in layers like peeled paper: one layer with velvet curtains, one bare stone, one darker one beneath, and a student standing at its centre.\ntext: {Advanced Illusion. To make illusions you must see through them. Vey layers the hall in false rooms, one on another, and you peel them back one at a time.|Layer day. The hall looks normal. That's never a good sign.|You count the layers when you come in. You're usually wrong.} {The top layer has velvet curtains.|The top layer is a summer garden.|The top layer is the hall itself, just slightly too tidy.}\n> left: Peel down to the stone\n  challenge exam standard+1:\n    win:\n      charm +small\n      study illusion +tiny\n      say: {Seven layers, then bare stone. Vey asks how many. You say seven. She smiles and says eight, and points at herself.|You reach the stone and stay there, sure of it. Vey nods slowly. \"Hold on to that,\" she says.|The too-tidy hall comes off last, and under it is the real hall, untidy and wonderful. Vey says you're the first today.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      health -tiny\n      say: {You peel one too many and the stone underneath looks false. For the rest of the day you knock on walls, with a headache behind your eyes.|After the fourth layer you stop being sure which way is up. Vey walks you to a chair, which is real.|You peel the tidy hall and find another tidy hall. And another. Vey stops you at the sixth, gently.}\n> right: Stop at the third layer\n  charm +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {Three layers and you stop, dizzy. Vey says knowing when to stop is the other half of the skill.|You get through three and sit down. The hall still looks too tidy, and you decide not to ask.|You stop at three, and you're right to: the third was the real one. Vey says so, with some surprise.}\n\n# ---------------- Beastcraft & Flight (track: beastcraft, skill vigor) ----------------\n\n@event beast_hedge_run\ntitle: The Hedge Run\ntrack: beastcraft\nlevel: 2\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: broom\ntags: class magic danger\nscene: Students on brooms skimming low over a line of hedges in a field, one broom bucking as its rider leans forward.\ntext: {Beastcraft II puts haste on a broom, low over the hedges, as fast as you dare. Professor Brask says brooms are harder to reason with than hippogriffs, and harder to apologise to.|The hedge run. Your broom remembers last time.|Hedges. You've learned to read a broom's sulk through the handle.} {Brask is timing with a crooked hourglass.|The hedges have been trimmed, unevenly.|{season == \"autumn\": Leaves come up behind every broom.|{season == \"spring\": The hedges are full of nesting birds who object.|The ground is hard and unforgiving.}}}\n> left: Haste at the last hedge\n  challenge feat standard wild:\n    win:\n      vigor +small\n      study beastcraft +tiny\n      say: {You borrow a hawk's speed for the last hedge and clear it by a hand. Brask stops the hourglass and nods.|The broom flinches, then commits. You come in fastest of the morning, with hedge in your hair.|You feel the broom's sulk through the handle and haste it anyway, kindly. It clears the last hedge and forgives you.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      say: {The broom bucks at the haste and you go over the hedge without it.|You're fast. The broom is faster, briefly, and then upside down. You land in a bramble.|The broom has been sulking all morning and saves it for the last hedge. You go over; it stays.}\n> right: Fly it clean and steady\n  vigor +small\n  say: {No haste, just a clean low line over every hedge. Brask says it's the best boring flying she's seen all week.|You fly it slow and tidy. The broom approves. Brask is less impressed.|You fly it steady and let it sulk. Brask says the broom will remember you were patient, and so will she.}\n\n@event beast_owl_post\ntitle: Teaching the Owls\ntrack: beastcraft\nlevel: 2\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: owl\ntags: class nature\nscene: A student in a stable loft with a young owl on their arm, pointing out of a high window toward a distant tower.\ntext: {Beast-speech at Beastcraft II. The young owls must learn the post routes. Brask hands you one and says, \"Explain the castle to it.\"|A new young owl, and the same castle to explain.|Another owl. You've got a speech now, and a map scratched on a beam.} {Your owl wants to know why there are so many towers.|Your owl has strong opinions about the library.|Your owl keeps falling asleep, standing up.}\n> left: Explain it in owl\n  challenge negotiate using vigor standard:\n    win:\n      vigor +small\n      study beastcraft +tiny\n      say: {You describe the castle in winds, perches and mice. The owl blinks, and goes and comes back with Brask's letter.|It takes a while to get \"left\" across to a bird. When it does, the owl does the route twice to show off.|You give your speech, map and all. The sleepy owl wakes for the important part and does the route without a mistake.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: {The owl listens closely, then flies to the kitchens. Every time.|The owl makes you explain \"tower\" seven times and then goes to sleep on your head.|Your owl sleeps through the whole speech, then wakes and flies to the kitchens. You're starting to take it personally.}\n> right: Walk the route with it\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You walk the whole post route with the owl on your arm, up every stair. Your legs ache and the owl has learned it.|Up and down the castle with an owl on your wrist. People stare. The owl stares back.|You walk the route with the owl asleep on your arm. It learns it anyway, somehow. Brask says they do that.}\n\n@event beast_hooves\ntitle: Hoof Day\ntrack: beastcraft\nlevel: 3\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: paw\ntags: class nature danger\nscene: A student crouched beside a hippogriff in a stall, a hoof trimming knife in hand, the hippogriff looking down with one sceptical eye.\ntext: {Advanced Beastcraft does the work nobody else wants. Today it's hooves. A hippogriff's need trimming each month, and the hippogriff disagrees.|Hoof day. The stall is the same; the hippogriff's opinion is too.|Hooves. You go in with a bucket of apples and a plan.} {Today's hippogriff is grey and patient.|Today's is young and very sure of itself.|Today's is old and wants to tell you about it.}\n> left: Talk it into lifting a foot\n  challenge negotiate using vigor standard+1 wild:\n    win:\n      vigor +small\n      study beastcraft +small\n      say: {It listens to your case and lifts a hoof with enormous dignity. Brask watches from the door and says nothing.|You talk it through every hoof. It sighs at you between each one, like a patient uncle.|The old hippogriff tells you about its youth for every hoof. You listen to all of it, and it lifts each foot when the story ends.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {It lets you get all the way to the last hoof before stepping on your foot.|It hears you out, then sits on you. Brask lifts it off with one hand and a word.|The old one talks and talks and doesn't lift a foot. You've been had, and Brask is laughing at the door.}\n> right: Rope, and Brask holding\n  vigor +small\n  health -small\n  say: {The old way, then. A rope, a strong arm, Brask at its head. It's sweaty work and nobody enjoys it, including the hippogriff.|With Brask holding the head and you holding on, you finish every hoof. Your shoulders will remember it.|Apples first, then rope, then Brask. The plan works, and the hippogriff eats the plan.}\n\n@event beast_night_stable\ntitle: Sitting Up With the Sick\ntrack: beastcraft\nlevel: 3\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: lantern\ntags: class nature\nscene: A student sitting in straw by lantern light, a sick creature curled beside them, the rest of the stables asleep.\ntext: {Advanced students take turns sitting up in the stables when a creature is ill. At the morning lecture Brask hands you a lantern, a blanket and a list for tonight.|Your name is on Brask's night-watch list again, and the list is shorter.|Night watch again tonight. Brask hands over the lantern without a word; the owls will only mutter.} {This time it's the griffon chick, off its food.|This time it's an old owl with a cough.|This time it's a hippogriff who ate something it shouldn't.} {season == \"winter\": The stable will be cold enough to see your breath.|At least it's warm in the straw.}\n> left: Sit up all night\n  vigor +small\n  study beastcraft +small\n  health -small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You don't sleep, and by dawn it's breathing easier. Brask finds you asleep in the straw and leaves you there.|You watch it all night, changing the water and the straw. In the morning it eats, and so do you, enormously.|You sit up with the hippogriff while it groans about its mistake. By dawn it's eating again, and you're asleep against its side.}\n> right: Ask it where it hurts\n  challenge negotiate using vigor standard+1:\n    win:\n      vigor +small\n      say: {It tells you, in its own way, exactly where it hurts. You write it on Brask's list and both of you sleep.|It points with its whole body. You bring the right tonic and it drops off before the lantern does.|It tells you exactly what it ate, with some pride. You fetch the purge and it forgives you by morning.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: {It tells you a great deal, about its neighbours, the hay and the moon, and nothing about where it hurts.|It won't talk to you. It talks to the lantern. You stay up anyway, which is the point.|It won't say what it ate, out of embarrassment. You guess, and Brask corrects the guess in the morning.}\n> up[beast_speech]: Speak with it properly\n  study beastcraft +small\n  say: {With the spell, it's easy. It explains the pain, the food, and what it thinks of the night. You fetch the right remedy.|You speak properly, and it answers properly, slowly, like someone who's been waiting. You both feel better.|You ask it properly and it tells you, ashamed, what it ate. The remedy is simple. The lecture you give it is not.}\n\n# ---------------- Umbral Theory (track: umbral, skill guile) ----------------\n\n@event umbral_ward_candle\ntitle: Ward the Candle\ntrack: umbral\nlevel: 2\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: candle\ntags: class magic social\ncast:\n  partner: any where tagged(partner, student)\nscene: Two students at a dim bench under low lamps, a candle between them inside a faint pale shell, one student trying to blow it out.\ntext: {Ward at Umbral Theory II. Ward a candle while your partner tries to put it out. Today that's {partner}.|Candle wards again, and the lamps are lower. {partner} is across the bench.|The candle exercise. You're paired with {partner}, who rolls up {their} sleeves.} {The professor walks between the benches with a hand over the flames.|The room smells of wax and burnt thread.|Someone's ward has shrunk to the size of a thimble.}\n> left: A thick, honest shell\n  guile +small\n  mana -small\n  rel partner +tiny\n  say: {Your ward is plain and solid. {partner} blows and blows and the candle doesn't even lean.|Strong and dull. {partner} gives up and the two of you watch the flame burn down.|Your shell is thick and calm. {partner}'s sleeves are rolled for nothing; the flame doesn't even notice {them}.}\n> right: A ward that turns them aside\n  challenge deceive standard:\n    win:\n      guile +small\n      study umbral +tiny\n      say: {Your ward turns {partner}'s breath round, and it blows {their} own hair into {their} eyes. The professor stops and says, \"Ah.\"|{partner} reaches for your candle and finds {their} hand on the wrong side of the bench. The professor approves.|Your ward turns {partner} aside so gently that {they} try{s} three times before working out what happened.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      rel partner -tiny\n      say: {Your clever ward turns aside the wrong thing, and the candle goes out with a smug little puff. {partner} smirks.|The ward bends {partner}'s breath straight into your face. You sneeze and the candle goes out.|Your ward shrinks to a thimble, like your neighbour's, and {partner} pinches the flame out with two fingers.}\n\n@event umbral_dark_roll\ntitle: Who Moved\ntrack: umbral\nlevel: 2\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: moon\ntags: class magic\nscene: A pitch-dark classroom at the top of a tower, a few vague shapes of students sitting perfectly still in the gloom.\ntext: {Umbral Theory II. The professor puts out every lamp, and in the dark one student moves. Say who, and where to.|Lamps out. You've learned to hear a sleeve.|The dark. You know the room's sounds, and which floorboard is a liar.} {Rain on the tower roof makes it harder.|It's so quiet you can hear the stair breathing.|Somebody's stomach rumbles, which helps no one.}\n> left: Listen, and name them\n  guile +small\n  say: {\"Third bench, to the window.\" Right. The professor relights a lamp and moves on.|You get it on the second try, by the creak of a bench and a faint smell of soap.|\"Second bench, toward the door, stepping over the liar.\" The professor laughs in the dark, once.}\n> right: Be the one who moves\n  challenge deceive standard:\n    win:\n      guile +small\n      study umbral +tiny\n      say: {You cross the whole room in the dark and nobody names you. When the lamps come up you're behind the professor.|Nobody hears you. The professor says, \"Someone moved,\" and seems pleased not to know who.|You step over the floorboard that lies and cross the room in silence. Even the professor guesses wrong.}\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: {You catch a desk with your shin. \"Window, limping,\" says everyone at once.|Your shoe squeaks on the second step, you turn your ankle stopping, and the whole class names you, with feeling.|You avoid the liar and find a new one. The whole room names you before you've reached the wall.}\n\n@event umbral_corridor_step\ntitle: Across the Corridor\ntrack: umbral\nlevel: 3\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: ghost\ntags: class magic danger\nscene: A dim stone corridor at the top of a tower, a student half stepping out of the shadow of one doorway while their own shadow lingers in another across the hall.\ntext: {Advanced Umbral Theory. Shadowstep across the corridor, door to door, the farthest the professor allows. \"Short distances only,\" the professor says. \"This is as short as long gets.\"|The corridor. The cold in between is colder than last time.|You stand at the door with your heels on the line. You know the cold that's coming.} {The lamps are out on the landing.|A draught is moving along the floor.|Someone came back from it with frost on one shoulder.}\n> left: Step across\n  challenge deceive standard+1 wild:\n    win:\n      guile +small\n      mood +tiny\n      say: {One shadow, cold and long, and then the other. You come out on the far side shaking and grinning.|You step in by one door and out by the other, with a breath of frost on your collar. The professor writes your name, and a time.|You step into the cold you knew was coming and out the far door with nothing on your shoulder. The professor writes a shorter time.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -small\n      say: {The cold in between is longer than you thought. You come out in the right doorway and can't feel your hands for an hour.|You step and come out in the same doorway you went in by, with the feeling that something looked at you on the way.|You come back with frost on one shoulder, like the others. It takes until supper to melt, and it isn't only cold.}\n> right: Walk it, counting shadows\n  study umbral +small\n  say: {You walk the corridor and count every shadow and every lamp. The professor calls this \"mapping your road\".|You pace it out and write down each shadow's depth. Next time you'll step it. Probably.|You walk it and count the shadows, again. They're the same as last time, which you now realise is the lesson.}\n> up[shadowstep]: Step with a held breath\n  guile +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You step without thinking about it, the way the spell knows. The cold barely touches you.|The spell takes you over quick and clean. Your fingers are cold for a minute.|You hold your breath and step. No frost, no cold, no time at all. The professor stops writing.}\n\n@event umbral_unpick_ward\ntitle: Unpicking the Box\ntrack: umbral\nlevel: 3\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: lock\ntags: class magic\nscene: A small iron-bound box on a bench under a dim lamp, faint threads of a pale ward wrapped around it, a student leaning close with a needle.\ntext: {Advanced Umbral Theory. The professor wards a small box and you unpick the ward without it noticing. \"A ward that notices you,\" the professor says, \"tells someone.\"|Box day. The professor has a new box, and a new ward on it.|The box. You've got a favourite needle for this now.} {This ward is fine as a cobweb.|This ward is thick and patient.|This ward hums when you breathe on it.}\n> left: Thread by thread\n  guile +small\n  mana -small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {An hour with a needle and a steady breath, and the box opens. The professor checks the ward and it has no idea.|You undo it strand by strand. It takes the whole lesson, and you don't look up once.|Your favourite needle and a slow breath. The humming ward goes quiet one strand at a time, and never notices it's gone.}\n> right: Cut it in one stroke\n  challenge deceive standard+1:\n    win:\n      guile +small\n      study umbral +small\n      say: {You find the knot and cut it clean. The ward never knows. The professor raises a brow and opens the box.|One cut and the threads fall away. The professor spends a moment looking at the box and then at you.|You cut it between hums. The ward stops mid-note and never starts again. The professor writes something down.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      health -tiny\n      say: {The ward notices, snaps across your knuckles and rings like a little bell. The professor says, \"And now someone knows.\" Everyone looks at you.|The ward snaps back at your hand and stings. The box stays shut.|You cut it mid-hum. It shrieks instead, and the professor has to open the window to let the sound out.}\n"},{"path":"content/events/pools/dating.grim","text":"# Dating (playtest 2, X4): more time together once you're a couple. Generic cards for any partner (`cast: partner`,\n# `per: partner`), then one date per romanceable character that only they would plan. They come to you as social\n# cards; a partner's warmth decides how often (design §5.5).\n\n# ---------------- Any partner ----------------\n\n@event date_market_day\ntitle: Market Day\ntrigger: social\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ncast:\n  partner: any where bond(partner) == \"partner\" and around(partner)\nper: partner\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 12\nart: coin\ntags: romance\nscene: Two students wandering a village market arm in arm, stalls of ribbons and pies, one holding up a ridiculous hat.\ntext: {Market day in the village, and {partner} has decided you're going together.|Market day again. {partner} knows which stall has the good pies now.|{partner} has a list for the market, and you're on it.} {A stall sells hats nobody should wear.|There's a juggler with one ball too many.|{season == \"winter\": Roasted chestnuts, paper cones, cold fingers.|Strawberries, a whole cart of them.}}\n> left: Buy {them} something silly\n  gold -small\n  spark partner +small\n  say: {You buy {partner} the worst hat at the market. {They} wear{s} it all day, with enormous dignity.|A tin ring from a pedlar, too big for any finger. {partner} threads it on a string and doesn't take it off.|Halfway down {partner}'s list, between flour and string, you find \"something silly\" in your own handwriting. A paper crown. {They} wear{s} it home.}\n> right: Just walk and talk\n  rel partner +small\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {You walk the whole market twice and buy nothing at all. You talk the entire time and remember none of it, only the voice.|An afternoon of nothing in particular, which is exactly what you both needed.|You ignore the list together and just walk. {partner} crosses things off anyway, one by one, as if you'd bought them.}\n\n@event date_study_together\ntitle: Studying Together\ntrigger: social\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ncast:\n  partner: any where bond(partner) == \"partner\" and around(partner)\nper: partner\nwhen: term\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 12\nart: book\ntags: romance study\nscene: Two students at a library table, books piled between them, one pretending to read while watching the other.\ntext: {{partner} suggests studying together. In theory, this is efficient.|Another study evening with {partner}. The theory remains a theory.|{partner} has saved you a seat in the library and stacked your books for you.} {The exam is in a fortnight.|The library is very quiet.|Your knees keep touching under the table.}\n> left: Actually study\n  study +small\n  spark partner -tiny\n  say: {You study, properly, in companionable silence. {partner} sighs once, theatrically. You both pass the next quiz.|Two hours of real work. {partner} quizzes you on the walk back, a little wistful that it was only studying, and you get them all right.|You keep your knees to yourself and your eyes on the page, mostly. {partner} stacked the books in the right order, and you get through all of them.}\n> right: Don't study at all\n  spark partner +small\n  study -tiny\n  say: {You pass notes, then drawings, then you're both thrown out of the library for laughing. Worth it.|Not a page is turned. The librarian moves you apart, which only makes it worse.|Your knees keep touching and neither of you moves. The books stay stacked exactly as {partner} left them.}\n\n@event date_first_quarrel\ntitle: A Quarrel\ntrigger: social\nclass: incident\ntone: tense\ncast:\n  partner: any where bond(partner) == \"partner\" and spark(partner) >= 30 and around(partner)\nper: partner\nweight: 10\nart: heart\ntags: romance\nscene: Two students on opposite ends of a cold courtyard bench, not looking at each other, a dropped scarf between them.\ntext: Your first real quarrel with {partner}. It started about something small, a missed supper, and became about something large before either of you noticed. Now you're on opposite ends of a bench and neither of you will speak first.\n> left: Speak first\n  rel partner +small\n  mood -small\n  say: \"I'm sorry,\" you say, and it costs you. {partner} looks at you, then shuffles along the bench. \"Me too.\" You talk it through until the bell. It's better after, and you both know how it's done now.\n> right: Wait for {them}\n  spark partner -small\n  rel partner -tiny\n  say: You wait. So does {partner}. You leave separately. The next day you both pretend it didn't happen, and it lies between you like the scarf on the bench.\n\n@event date_meet_friends\ntitle: Meeting the Friends\ntrigger: social\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ncast:\n  partner: any where bond(partner) == \"partner\" and around(partner)\n  friend: any where rel(friend) >= 12 and bond(friend) != \"partner\" and tagged(friend, student) and not tagged(friend, outsider) and around(friend)\nper: partner\nweight: 10\nart: group\ntags: romance social\nscene: Three students around a refectory table, two of them sizing each other up over the bread basket, one in the middle smiling too hard.\ntext: {friend} and {partner} have heard a great deal about each other, all from you, and now they're at the same supper table. {friend} is being very polite. {partner} is being very polite back. You are dying.\n> left: Steer the talk to something they share\n  challenge negotiate apprentice+:\n    win:\n      rel friend +small\n      rel partner +small\n      say: You find it on the third try: a shared loathing of Thursday's soup. By pudding they're ganging up on you, which is exactly what you wanted.\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: Every subject dies. They're kind about it, both of them, and the silence is very long. \"Well,\" says {friend}, afterwards. \"They're nice.\" It's not quite a compliment.\n> right: Let them find their own way\n  rel friend +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You eat your supper and let them circle each other. By the end they've agreed on nothing except that you talk in your sleep. You never find out how {partner} knew that and {friend} did.\n\n@event date_rainy_afternoon\ntitle: Rained In\ntrigger: social\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ncast:\n  partner: any where bond(partner) == \"partner\" and around(partner)\nper: partner\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: candle\ntags: romance\nscene: Rain streaming down a tall window seat, two students under one blanket with a board game between them, cups of tea steaming.\ntext: {Rain all afternoon, and {partner} has found the window seat in the west tower, a blanket and an old board game with half the pieces.|Rain again, and {partner} has claimed the window seat for the two of you.|Rain, a blanket, and a game whose missing pieces someone has replaced with ones carved from soap.} {The rain is loud on the leads.|The tea is too hot.|The game's rules are mostly invented.}\n> left: Play to win\n  spark partner +tiny\n  guile +tiny\n  say: {You win, ruthlessly. {partner} demands a rematch and wins that. Neither of you will ever forget the score.|You cheat; {partner} cheats better. The game ends in a pillow fight and a broken teacup.|You invent a rule; {partner} invents a better one. A soap piece wins in the end, and you both agree it earned it.}\n> right: Forget the game\n  spark partner +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {The game is abandoned by the second move. You talk about your families until the rain stops and the room is dark and you're late for supper.|You fall asleep under the blanket and wake up cold and late and very happy.|You carve another soap piece together while the rain goes on. Nobody can say what it was meant to be, and {partner} keeps it anyway.}\n\n@event date_forgotten_day\ntitle: The Day You Forgot\ntrigger: social\nclass: incident\ntone: tense\ncast:\n  partner: any where bond(partner) == \"partner\" and around(partner)\nper: partner\nweight: 9\nart: heart\ntags: romance\nscene: A student holding a small wrapped present out to another student, whose hands are empty and whose face has just fallen.\ntext: {partner} hands you a small parcel, wrapped carefully. \"It's our day,\" {they} say{s}. Apparently there is a day. You didn't know. You have nothing. {partner} sees you realise it.\n> left: Confess and make it up\n  gold -small\n  spark partner +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: \"I forgot,\" you say. {They} laugh{s}, a little hurt. That evening you turn up with flowers from the village and a terrible poem, and the poem does it.\n> right: Pretend your gift is \"coming\"\n  alignment -tiny\n  chance even:\n    gold -medium\n    say: You run to the village before the shops shut and spend more than you meant on something you hope looks planned. {partner} believes you, or kindly pretends to.\n  else:\n    spark partner -small\n    say: The shops are shut. Your gift \"is delayed\" for a week. {partner} doesn't ask about it again, and that is worse than asking.\n\n@event date_dance_lesson\ntitle: Learning to Dance\ntrigger: social\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ncast:\n  partner: any where bond(partner) == \"partner\" and around(partner)\nwhen: month == 4 or month == 12\nper: partner\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 12\nart: music\ntags: romance\nscene: Two students practising dance steps in an empty classroom, chairs pushed back, one counting aloud and the other looking at their feet.\ntext: With the {month == 4: Midwinter Ball|Starfall dance} coming, {partner} has decided you're going to learn properly, in an empty classroom, with the chairs pushed back. {~You start on the dance with the spin.|\"Again,\" says {partner}. \"From the turn.\"|Somebody is watching from the door.|Your feet have opinions.|There's no music, only counting.}\n> left: Practise until it's right\n  vigor +tiny\n  spark partner +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~By the end of the hour you can do the turn without looking. {partner} says \"There,\" very softly, and doesn't let go straight away.|Your feet ache, and you can dance. Nearly.|Whoever is watching from the door claps when you finally get the turn. {partner} bows to them, and then to you.|Your feet stop arguing about halfway through the hour. By the end you can do the turn, and {partner} is insufferably pleased.|You count until the counting turns into a tune, and the turn comes out right on the last beat.}\n> right: Make it a joke\n  mood +small\n  rel partner +small\n  say: {~You invent a new dance, mostly flailing. {partner} laughs so hard {they} ha{s} to sit down, then joins in.|You never learn the turn. You learn that {partner} snorts when {they} laugh{s}, which is better.|You play it up for whoever is watching at the door. They laugh, {partner} laughs, and nobody learns the turn.|You let your feet do what they like. {partner} copies them exactly, and it becomes a dance of its own.|You count in the wrong order on purpose. {partner} loses count laughing, and that's the end of the lesson.}\n\n@event date_after_graduation\ntitle: After\ntrigger: social\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ncast:\n  partner: any where bond(partner) == \"partner\" and spark(partner) >= 60 and around(partner)\nwhen: final_year\nper: partner\nweight: 16\nart: heart\ntags: romance\nscene: Two students sitting on the castle wall at sunset, legs dangling, the whole valley below them gold.\ntext: On the castle wall, with the light going long over the valley, {partner} asks the question neither of you has said out loud: \"After graduation. What happens to us?\" The valley below is gold. {They} {are} not looking at you, very deliberately.\n> left: \"We go together.\"\n  spark partner +medium\n  flag after_together\n  mood +small\n  say: {They} let{s} out a breath {they}'d been holding for months. You plan it there and then, badly: a city, a cottage, a list of rules for the kitchen. It doesn't matter that it's badly. It's yours.\n> right: \"Let's not decide yet.\"\n  rel partner +small\n  spark partner -tiny\n  say: \"Not yet,\" {they} agree{s}, too quickly, and takes your hand. You watch the gold go grey. It's honest, and it's lonelier than the other answer.\n\n# ---------------- One date only they would plan ----------------\n\n@event lyra_date_dawn_duel\ntitle: A Duel for Two\nactor: lyra\ntrigger: social\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: bond(lyra) == \"partner\" and around(lyra)\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 12\nart: duel\ntags: romance duel\nscene: Two duellists bowing in a misty courtyard at dawn, wands lowered, both trying not to smile.\ntext: {Lyra's idea of a romantic afternoon is a duel in the empty courtyard, \"to keep you sharp\".|Another courtyard duel. Lyra has started calling them \"our afternoons\".|Lyra is already in the courtyard, wand out, sleeves rolled.} {She's brought coffee for after.|She's in a terrible mood, which means she slept well.|\"No holding back,\" she says. \"I'll know.\"}\n> left: Duel her properly\n  challenge duel vs lyra.arcana:\n    win:\n      spark lyra +small\n      arcana +tiny\n      say: {You win. Lyra stares at you as if you'd proposed. \"Again,\" she says, breathless, and then, \"Tomorrow.\" It's the best thing anyone has ever said to you.|A clean win. She shakes your hand, then doesn't let go of it.|No holding back, so you don't, and you win. She grins as if she'd won herself, and demands you show her how.}\n    lose:\n      spark lyra +tiny\n      health -small\n      say: {She wins, because she always does, and then sits beside you on the cold steps with the coffee and explains your mistake, very gently, for her.|You lose. She bandages your knuckle herself and pretends it's an inspection.|No holding back on either side. She wins, just, and admits on the steps that it was closer than she'll ever tell anyone else.}\n> right: Let her win, and tell her so\n  rel lyra -tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: {She knows the moment you drop your guard. \"Don't you dare,\" she says, and then kisses you, furious, and then makes you do it again properly.|She's outraged. She's also, for the rest of the day, unusually soft.|\"I said I'd know,\" she says, the moment you drop your guard. She makes you start again, and this time you don't dare.}\n\n@event tobias_date_greenhouse_supper\ntitle: Lunch Among the Tomatoes\nactor: tobias\ntrigger: social\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: bond(tobias) == \"partner\" and around(tobias)\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 12\nart: flower\ntags: romance nature\nscene: A candlelit table set up between rows of tomato plants in a warm greenhouse at night, a big student serving soup from a pot.\ntext: {Tobias has cooked lunch in the greenhouse, at a table between the tomato rows, with everything he grew himself.|Lunch in the greenhouse again. The tomatoes have names now.|Tobias has added a second candle and a jar of wildflowers.} {The soup is his grandmother's recipe.|A tomato plant is leaning in to listen.|There is far too much bread.}\n> left: Eat everything, and ask for seconds\n  spark tobias +small\n  health +tiny\n  say: {You eat three bowls and he beams like the sun. He tells you about the farm, the goats, the year the river froze. You could listen for ever, and nearly do.|Seconds, thirds, a slice of pie. Tobias watches you eat with an expression you want to keep in a jar.|You eat until the bread is gone, which takes some doing. Tobias lights the second candle off the first and says he'll bake less next time. He won't.}\n> right: Help him in the beds after\n  rel tobias +small\n  vigor +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You weed the beds in the afternoon sun, side by side. Your back aches; your hands smell of tomato leaves. He hums the whole time.|An hour of planting. He shows you how to tell a seedling it's doing well. You feel ridiculous, and do it anyway, until your knees ache from the kneeling.|You work off the bread in the beds after. He picks you a wildflower for the jar and pretends it was a weed.}\n\n@event seraphine_date_a_future\ntitle: A Future She Saw\nactor: seraphine\ntrigger: social\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: bond(seraphine) == \"partner\" and around(seraphine)\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 12\nart: eye\ntags: romance magic\nscene: A seer and a student lying on the observatory floor beneath the open dome, a scatter of tarot cards between them.\ntext: {Seraphine says she's seen a future with the two of you in it, and wants to tell you about it on the observatory floor, under the shuttered dome.|Another future, another hour on the observatory floor.|\"I saw us again,\" Seraphine says, lying down beside you. \"Older.\"} {She's shuffling the cards without looking.|The dome's painted stars have faded to grey.|She is, for once, entirely in the present.}\n> left: Ask her to tell you\n  spark seraphine +small\n  lore +tiny\n  say: {She tells you. A kitchen, a cat, a terrible argument about curtains, a garden. It's so ordinary you could cry. She says it's her favourite one.|A future with snow in it, and an old friend who comes to stay and never leaves. She stops halfway and holds your hand.|She tells you about the older two of you: slower, quieter, still arguing about curtains. She laughs at her own future and holds your hand all the way through it.}\n> right: Ask her not to\n  rel seraphine +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {\"Let it surprise us,\" you say. She looks at you as if no one has ever asked her that. Then she puts the cards away, all of them.|She's quiet a while, then laughs. \"You're the only person I can't read,\" she says. \"It's very restful.\"|\"Then don't tell me,\" you say. She doesn't. For a whole hour she's only here, with you, and that's the best future yet.}\n\n@event corvin_date_rooftop_supper\ntitle: Supper on the Roof\nactor: corvin\ntrigger: social\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: bond(corvin) == \"partner\" and around(corvin)\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 12\nart: map\ntags: romance\nscene: A tablecloth spread on a castle rooftop between chimneys, a candle in a jar, two students eating pastries above the sleeping towers.\ntext: {Corvin leads you through three passages and a window to a rooftop between chimneys, where a tablecloth and supper are waiting.|Another rooftop, another tablecloth. You don't ask where he finds them.|\"New roof,\" says Corvin. \"Better view. Mostly legal.\"} {There are pastries you don't recognise.|There's a candle in a jam jar.|He's brought cushions, which is unlike him.}\n> left: Ask where the supper came from\n  guile +tiny\n  spark corvin +tiny\n  alignment -tiny\n  say: {\"Borrowed,\" he says. From the headmistress's kitchen. You eat it anyway, and it tastes of getting away with something. He grins all evening.|\"Don't ask, saint.\" You don't. The pastries are perfect.|\"Mostly legal,\" he repeats, when you ask. The supper is from the prefects' pantry. You eat it on his cushions and laugh until a window opens below.}\n> right: Just enjoy it\n  spark corvin +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You eat supper on the roof until the candle gutters and the cold comes up through the slates. He doesn't make a single joke for a whole hour, and when you notice, he kisses you to stop you saying so.|The chimneys are warm, the air is cold, and you climb down at midnight scraped and happy.|You lean back on the cushions and watch the towers go dark one by one. He says nothing clever all evening, and you like him better for it.}\n\n@event mir_date_invention\ntitle: They Built You Something\nactor: mir\ntrigger: social\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: bond(mir) == \"partner\" and around(mir)\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 12\nart: gear\ntags: romance\nscene: A cluttered workshop bench, a small brass contraption with wings sitting on it, a grinning inventor and a nervous student leaning away.\ntext: {Mir has built you a present, a brass bird that sings your name, mostly.|Another present from Mir's bench, ticking ominously.|Mir presents their newest invention with a flourish and a fire bucket.} {It's warm to the touch.|It has seven moving parts and one they don't explain.|\"It's probably safe,\" they say.}\n> left: Wind it up at once\n  chance likely:\n    spark mir +small\n    mood +small\n    say: {It sings your name, slightly wrong, all the way round the workshop, and lands in your hand. Mir bursts into happy tears and pretends it's soot.|It works. It works! They kiss you on the cheek and immediately start on version two.|It's probably safe, and for once it is. It hums a tune you both know and settles on your shoulder. Mir puts the fire bucket down, astonished.}\n  else:\n    spark mir +tiny\n    health -small\n    say: {It sings for three seconds and then explodes, gently, into springs. Mir is mortified. You laugh until they do too, and help them pick up every piece.|It sets your sleeve on fire. Mir has the bucket ready. \"Version two,\" they say grimly.|It's probably not safe after all. The fire bucket earns its keep, and Mir chalks \"probably\" on the bench as a warning to themself.}\n> right: Ask them how it works first\n  rel mir +small\n  alchemy +tiny\n  say: {They explain for an hour, with diagrams. You understand a third of it and love all of it. You wind it up together, very carefully, and it works.|Two hours of explanation and three cups of tea. The bird sings at the end. You've never been so proud of a thing you didn't make.|They explain why it's probably safe, at length, with the fire bucket close by. It is. You wind it up together, and it works.}\n\n@event kael_date_snow\ntitle: Snow, Just for You\nactor: kael\ntrigger: social\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: bond(kael) == \"partner\" and around(kael)\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 12\nart: snow\ntags: romance magic\nscene: Snow falling over one small patch of a spring lawn, two students standing in it, one holding out his hand to catch the flakes.\ntext: {Kael leads you to a corner of the lawn and makes it snow, only there, only on the two of you.|Another private snowfall. He's getting better at the shapes.|Kael has made it snow again. This time the flakes are stars.} {season == \"summer\": It's a hot day; the snow steams as it falls.|{The flakes are as big as coins.|Somewhere a bird is very confused.|It's the most he's said with his hands in weeks.}}\n> left: Catch the flakes\n  spark kael +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You catch them on your tongue until your teeth ache with cold, and laugh, and Kael, astonishingly, laughs too, out loud. The snow falls harder when he's happy, it turns out.|You're both soaked and frozen. He warms your hands in his, which have warmed, against all habit.|You catch a star-shaped flake on your glove and it doesn't melt for a whole minute. Kael watches it more than you do, and smiles when it goes.}\n> right: Ask him what it costs\n  rel kael +small\n  lore +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {\"Nothing,\" he says. Then, honestly, \"A little cold in the chest, for a day.\" You make him stop. He looks at you as if no one had ever asked him to.|He tells you about Northern frost-craft and what it takes. Then he makes one last snowflake, small, and lets you keep it.|\"Less, now,\" he says, which isn't an answer. You make him stop anyway, and he lets the last star land in your hair.}\n\n@event nessa_date_off_record\ntitle: Off the Record\nactor: nessa\ntrigger: social\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: bond(nessa) == \"partner\" and around(nessa)\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 12\nart: paper\ntags: romance\nscene: A newspaper office late at night, an enchanted quill lying still on a desk for once, two students sharing a pot of tea among the proofs.\ntext: {Nessa locks the Ledger office, puts her quill in a drawer and says, \"Off the record. For as long as you like.\"|Another hour off the record. The quill sulks in its drawer.|Nessa has started saying \"off the record\" when she means \"I love you\". You've noticed.} {There's a pot of tea and a plate of toast.|The proofs are stacked and, for once, ignored.|It's raining on the skylight.}\n> left: Ask her about herself\n  spark nessa +small\n  say: {She talks about her childhood for an hour, which she never does. A printing shop, a father who read the news aloud. You don't take a single note. She notices.|Nessa, off the record, is quieter and funnier. You stay until the lamp runs out.|You ask about her, and she says \"off the record\" first, and then, quietly, a great deal. The rain on the skylight keeps it between the two of you.}\n> right: Tell her a secret of your own\n  rel nessa +small\n  say: {You tell her something nobody knows. She holds it like it's the most valuable story she's ever had, and never writes it down.|She listens, and then tells you one back. Off the record. It feels like a promise.|You tell her your secret. She says \"off the record\" back, and you both know what she means.}\n"},{"path":"content/events/pools/grounds.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Baseline pools: the grounds (phase 3, step 1b).\n#  Repeatable routine events for map visits to the lakeshore,\n#  duelling hall, greenhouses, flight field, stables, boathouse,\n#  orchard, Maud's cottage, the secret garden, the courier loft\n#  and the Whisperwood. Spec: docs/authoring/pools.md.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- The Lakeshore ----------------\n\n@event lakeshore_shore_walk\ntitle: The Long Way Round\nplace: lakeshore\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: moon\ntags: romance nature\ncast:\n  date: any where romanceable(date) and rel(date) >= 12 and around(date)\nscene: Two figures walking a lakeside path at dusk, the towers reflected upside down in the still water beside them.\ntext: {You came out to walk alone. {date} finds you on the shore path and falls into step without asking.|The shore path is where you go to think. Today {date} has the same idea.|You know every stone of the shore path by now. Today {date} walks it with you.} {season == \"winter\": The lake is frozen at the edges and your breath walks ahead of you.|{season == \"summer\": The water is warm and full of sky.|{Mist lies on the water like a sheet someone forgot to fold.|The towers stand upside down in the lake, lit window by lit window.|The water is so still that a dropped leaf makes rings for a minute.}}}\n> left: Walk the long way round\n  spark date +small\n  rel date +tiny\n  health -small\n  say: {final_year: Halfway round, {date} says \"next year\" and stops, because there won't be one here. You walk the rest closer together.|{season == \"winter\": {You walk until your toes go numb. {date} lends you {their} scarf and pretends not to be cold.|The ice sings under your boots. {date} takes your arm on the slippery stretch and forgets to let go.|You walk the frozen edge in step. {date} counts the herons out loud, and you let {them} lose count.}|{You walk until the far lamps go out. Neither of you mentions the time.|{date} points out a lit tower window and tells you who lives behind it. Half of it is invented. You don't mind which half.|You stand at the water's edge in silence, and the silence is the good kind.}}}\n> right: Talk about anything but us\n  rel date +small\n  spark date -tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You talk about exams, the cook and the weather. It's easy. It's also not what either of you came for.|You argue cheerfully about whether the lake has a bottom. It's a good walk. It's only a walk.|You talk about the lake, the cook and everything except the thing. It gets easier to leave it unsaid, which is not a good sign.}\n\n@event lakeshore_cold_swim\ntitle: The Cold Swim\nplace: lakeshore\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: wave\ntags: nature life\nscene: Students in towels on a lakeshore, one wading in up to the knees and shrieking, the towers reflected behind them.\ntext: {season == \"winter\": Students in towels are daring each other into the lake through a hole in the ice.|{season == \"summer\": Half the academy is in the lake; the other half pretends not to want to be.|A few hardy students are swimming out to the flat rock, shrieking.}} {The water looks inviting. It always does.|You know how cold the Mirror Lake really is. You're tempted anyway.|You and the lake have an understanding. It doesn't help.}\n> left: Brave the water\n  challenge feat apprentice:\n    win:\n      vigor +small\n      mood +small\n      health -tiny\n      say: {season == \"winter\": You go under, come up roaring, and are hauled out a hero.|{You slap the flat rock, whoop, and swim back to applause from people with dry hair.|You reach the rock first. The cold reaches you second, but you were already winning.|You swim out to the rock and back without thinking about it. That's the trick, it turns out.}}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {You come back blue to the lips and sneezing. Sister Amaranth sighs before you've even sat down.|Halfway out, the lake reminds you who is in charge. You turn back with what dignity you have left.|The lake wins again. Sister Amaranth has a blanket ready before you've reached the shore.}\n> right: Hold the towels and heckle\n  charm +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You announce every dive like a herald. You're dry, popular and a tiny bit jealous.|You keep the towels warm and the commentary warmer. Someone throws a wet sock at you, which is fair.|You keep the towels and the score. The swimmers argue with your score, which means they trust it.}\n\n# ---------------- The Duelling Hall ----------------\n\n@event duel_hall_dummies\ntitle: The Practice Dummies\nplace: duel_hall\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: shield\ntags: magic\nscene: An empty duelling hall with scorched floorboards and a row of battered straw practice dummies, one with a painted moustache.\ntext: {The near end of the hall belongs to the practice dummies, who watch you come in. The one with the painted moustache looks especially unforgiving.|The dummy with the moustache is still here, with a new scorch mark and, you'd swear, a new grudge.|The moustached dummy greets you with its usual silence. Someone has given it an eyepatch.} {The floorboards are still warm from the last session.|A singed glove lies abandoned on the rack.|Rain drums on the high windows.}\n> left: Drill your hexes on it\n  arcana +small\n  mana -small\n  say: {Hex after hex until your wrists ache. The dummy loses a sleeve and none of its contempt.|You land a clean one at last. A wisp of straw floats down, slowly, like applause.|You knock the eyepatch clean off. The dummy looks more menacing without it, which you didn't plan for.}\n> right: Restuff the battered ones\n  mood +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You haul a bale of straw over from the stables and restuff three dummies. They look almost grateful, which is unsettling.|You restuff the moustached one with special care. It still looks like it's planning something.|You restuff the moustached one and straighten its eyepatch. It's the best-dressed thing in the hall.}\n\n@event duel_hall_gallery\ntitle: From the Gallery\nplace: duel_hall\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: duel\ntags: magic social\ncast:\n  here: present(place) where tagged(here, peer)\nscene: A narrow wooden gallery above a duelling floor, one student watching another drill spells at a target below.\ntext: {From the gallery above the duelling floor you can watch whoever is practising. Today it's {here}.|You've learned to read duels from up here. Today {here} is drilling.|The gallery rail has a groove where you lean. Below, {here} is drilling.} {Sparks drift up to the gallery rail.|Somebody's familiar is asleep on the scoreboard.|The ceiling has acquired a new scorch mark.}\n> left: Offer to spar\n  challenge combat vs here.arcana:\n    win:\n      rel here +small\n      arcana +tiny\n      health -tiny\n      say: {You take a round off {here}. {They} stare{s} at you for a long second, then ask{s} for another.|You win with a feint you stole from watching {here}. {They} notice{s}, and look{s} annoyed and pleased at once.|You spar with {here} as if you'd done it for years, because from the gallery, you have. {They} win{s} narrowly and look{s} at you differently.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      rel here +tiny\n      say: {{here} puts you on the floorboards, politely. From down there the ceiling's scorch marks make a sort of map.|{here} disarms you so neatly that the dummies seem to wince. Your wand skids under the gallery stairs.|{here} lands a spell that singes the ceiling and you with it. You add your own mark to the map.}\n> right: Watch and take notes\n  lore +small\n  say: {You fill a page with what {here} does with {their} feet. You'll never get to try it, and you know it.|You sketch {here}'s guard from above. On paper it has a gap. On the floor it doesn't.|You take notes from your groove at the rail. Your notes on {here} could fill a book now, and might.}\n\n@event duel_hall_humming_circle\ntime: evening\ntitle: The Humming Circle\nplace: duel_hall\nwhen: slot == \"evening\"\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: rune\ntags: magic\nscene: A dark duelling hall lit only by a ring of faintly glowing runes on the floor.\ntext: {The evening bouts are over. At the near end of the hall the warding circle is still humming.|The warding circle is humming. Whoever's drilling at the far end isn't looking your way.|The circle hums. You know its tune by now.} {final_year: Soon someone else will be sneaking in here after hours.|{A practice wand lies on the rack, still warm.|The steward's broom leans by the door, watching.|Tonight the ceiling stain looks like a dragon.}}\n> left: Step in and try a hard one\n  challenge spell adept-:\n    win:\n      arcana +small\n      mana -small\n      say: {The spell comes out clean and the circle drinks it. Somewhere a dummy loses its head and nobody ever finds out how.|You try the thing you saw a fourth-year do. It works. You tell no one, and walk back grinning.|You cast the hard one and the circle hums the note back to you, a little higher. You think that's approval.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mana -small\n      say: {The circle hands your spell straight back to you. Your eyebrows will regrow.|The circle flares, the dummies rattle, and you leave very quickly before the steward arrives.|The circle swallows half your spell and spits out the other half in your direction. The steward's broom falls over, laughing.}\n> right: Sweep up for the steward\n  gold +tiny\n  say: {You sweep up a winter's worth of ash. The steward pays you a few crowns and a grunt that might be thanks.|You sweep. The circle hums at you the whole time, like a cat that wants to be let out.|You sweep in time with the hum. The steward says you're the only one who does it properly, and pays you for the compliment.}\n\n# ---------------- The Greenhouses ----------------\n\n@event greenhouses_warm_glass\ntitle: The Warm Glass\nplace: greenhouses\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: leaf\ntags: nature\nscene: A student reading on an upturned pot among tall ferns inside a steamy glass greenhouse, snow on the panes outside.\ntext: {season == \"winter\": Outside it's snowing. Inside, the glass runs with warm water and everything smells of earth.|{season == \"summer\": The greenhouses are sweltering, and everyone has retreated to the far end where the vents are.|The greenhouses steam gently. Someone is singing to the mandrakes at the far end.}} {You find a dry corner by the ferns, out of everyone's way.|Someone has left a cushion in your corner by the ferns.|The ferns lean toward you when you come in, as they do for you now.}\n> left: Do your reading in the warm\n  lore +small\n  say: {You get through a whole chapter. The pages curl in the damp and you come out smelling of moss.|You read until the steam makes the ink run. What's left of the chapter sticks.|You read with the ferns leaning in to listen. The chapter goes in easily, and the moss smell stays a day.}\n> right: Nap on the potting bench\n  mood +small\n  health +tiny\n  renown -tiny\n  say: {You wake with a seed tray for a pillow and a first-year staring at you. By supper it's a story.|You wake to a mandrake humming a lullaby. You choose not to tell anyone. Someone tells everyone.|You nap on your cushion among the ferns. Someone has put a sign by your feet. It says \"Do not water\".}\n\n@event greenhouses_repotting\ntitle: Repotting Day\nplace: greenhouses\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: flower\ntags: nature social\ncast:\n  here: present(place)\nscene: A student elbow-deep in compost at a greenhouse bench, handing a trowel to someone arriving.\ntext: {here} is up to the elbows in compost, repotting {season == \"spring\": seedlings that have outgrown everything|{season == \"autumn\": bulbs for the winter|something with more teeth than leaves}}. {\"Hands?\" {they} ask{s}.|{They} hand{s} you a trowel before you've said hello.|There's a trowel on the hook that has come to be yours. {here} nods at it.}\n> left: Get your hands dirty\n  rel here +small\n  alchemy +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You repot a whole row. Something nips your thumb in thanks.|You're filthy, nipped and oddly happy. {here} says you have good hands for roots.|You take your trowel off its hook and repot the difficult ones. Nothing bites you, which {here} says is a first.}\n> right: Ask questions, stay clean\n  lore +small\n  rel here -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You learn a great deal about soil. {here} learns that you're afraid of it.|You take notes. {here} gives you a look over the trowel that says notes are not hands.|You ask your questions with your trowel in your hand, which {here} counts as progress.}\n\n@event greenhouses_cuttings\ntitle: The Cuttings Bench\nplace: greenhouses\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: potion\ntags: nature\nscene: A greenhouse bench of labelled cuttings drying in rows, a row of snapdragons with actual teeth watching from their pots.\ntext: {A row of silver-leaf cuttings is drying on the bench|A tray of night-orchid cuttings sits by the door|A bundle of moonpetal cuttings is wrapped in damp cloth}, and Pell & Daughters pays well for that sort of thing. {Nobody is watching. The snapdragons are.|The snapdragons have seen you here before. They remember.|The snapdragons snap at you on principle now.}\n> left: Pocket a cutting\n  challenge deceive apprentice:\n    win:\n      gold +small\n      alignment -tiny\n      say: {Pell's counts the leaves twice and pays without a question. You don't look at the snapdragons on your next visit.|It sells for more than you expected, which somehow makes it worse.|Moonpetal sells for twice the others. Pell's doesn't ask where it came from, which you notice.}\n    lose:\n      renown -small\n      alignment -tiny\n      say: {A snapdragon shrieks. Three Verdant students turn around at once. You put it back with a cough that convinces no one.|Your pocket rustles at the wrong moment. You spend the rest of the visit apologising to a fern.|The snapdragons have been waiting for this. They raise the alarm before your hand reaches the cloth.}\n> right: Feed the snapdragons\n  alchemy +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {They take the food, then a little of your finger. You learn which end to hold the tongs by.|Fed, the snapdragons purr like kettles. One of them still bites you, out of habit.|You feed the snapdragons and they snap anyway, gently, out of principle. You've come to find it friendly.}\n\n# ---------------- The Flight Field ----------------\n\n@event flight_field_cheating_wind\ntitle: The Cheating Wind\nplace: flight_field\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: broom\ntags: life\nscene: A long green field with tall hoops at either end, one student wobbling on a school broom against a gusting wind.\ntext: {season == \"winter\": The flight field is white, the hoops wearing caps of snow.|{season == \"spring\": The field is soft with new grass and the wind is playful.|{The field is empty but for the wind, practising on its own.|A lone first-year is flying very careful circles.|A pair of fourth-years are racing low over the grass.}}} {The school brooms in the shed are, at best, opinionated.|Your usual broom is in the shed, the one that pulls left.|The broom that pulls left now pulls right, out of spite.}\n> left: Practise your landings\n  vigor +small\n  health -small\n  say: {A dozen landings. A few of them on purpose. The grass keeps a careful record of the rest.|The wind waits until you're nearly down, then shoves. You land in a hoop instead. Technically, a goal.|Your broom pulls right; you lean left. The landings come out straight, for once.}\n> right: Hire a decent broom\n  gold -small\n  mood +small\n  vigor +tiny\n  say: {The good broom goes where you look, not where it feels like. For an hour, flying is easy and the field is yours.|You fly the length of the field and back with the wind on your side for once. It costs a week's sweets.|You hire the good broom and leave the spiteful one in the shed. It will be waiting next time, and it will remember.}\n\n@event flight_field_pickup_race\ntitle: The Pick-Up Race\nplace: flight_field\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: trophy\ntags: social life\nscene: Three students on brooms jostling at a crooked painted start line, a fourth holding up a scarf as a starting flag.\ntext: {A pick-up race is forming at the far hoops. Three brooms, two rules, no referee.|The pick-up racers are back. The rules have grown to four.|The pick-up race is a fixture now. Someone has painted a finish line, crookedly.} {A second-year from Emberhall is taking bets.|A Brasswick student has bolted a lantern to their broom-tail.|A Rootward student is flying barefoot, for grip.}\n> left: Borrow a broom and race\n  challenge duel using vigor journeyman+ wild:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      mood +small\n      health -tiny\n      say: {You win by a broom's length and a lot of shouting. The second-year taking bets has to pay out and never forgives you.|You take the last hoop sideways and cross first. Someone adds a new rule, about you.|You cross the crooked finish line first, barefoot Rootward student close behind. Your name goes up on the post in chalk.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {You finish last, and partly in a hedge.|The wind takes your side, then changes its mind. You walk the broom back to the start.|You lose to someone flying barefoot. You consider the lesson in this and decide against it.}\n> right: Bet a few crowns on the outsider\n  gold -tiny\n  chance unlikely:\n    gold +small\n    mood +tiny\n    say: {The outsider wins on a gust nobody else saw coming. You are suddenly very good at betting.|The outsider comes in first by a nose. You toast the nose.|You bet on the barefoot flyer. Grip, it turns out, is everything.}\n  else:\n    mood -tiny\n    say: {The outsider stops halfway to admire the view. Your crowns go with them.|Your flyer is disqualified for a rule invented on the spot. You don't argue. It's a good rule.|Your outsider's broom throws them at the first hoop. The crowns stay where they landed, which is not with you.}\n\n@event flight_field_long_grass\ntitle: The Long Grass\nplace: flight_field\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: pouch\ntags: life\nscene: The long grass at the edge of a flight field, scattered with things dropped from brooms, a student crouching to look.\ntext: {The long grass at the edge of the flight field catches everything that falls off a broom.|Someone has planted a little flag in the long grass, to mark where things land.|You've come to think of the long grass as the academy's real lost-and-found.|Somebody has started a ledger of the long grass, pinned to the fence. Today's entry is yours to write.} Today it's {a velvet glove|a letter with no envelope|a single boot, laced|someone's notes for an exam}. {season == \"autumn\": The leaves are trying to bury it.|{season == \"winter\": It's half under frost.|A grasshopper is sitting on it.}}\n> left: Keep what you find\n  guile +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  alignment -tiny\n  chance unlikely:\n    renown -small\n    say: {The owner lands right beside you while you're still holding it. There's a short, very long silence.|A second-year marches over the grass shouting \"Thief!\" at the sky in general and you in particular.|A boy hops across the field on one foot, shouting. He wants his boot back, and he wants to know why you're holding it.|The owner of the notes finds you reading them. Their exam is tomorrow. It turns out yours is too.}\n  else:\n    say: {Finders, keepers. The grass doesn't argue, and neither does your conscience, much.|Nobody comes looking. You tell yourself that settles it.|It's a good boot. You'll need another one, but it's a start.|The notes are better than yours. You tell yourself that settles it.}\n> right: Carry it to the porter's lodge\n  alignment +small\n  renown +tiny\n  say: {It's a long walk round the field. The porter writes it in a book and gives you a look of mild surprise.|The porter thanks you with a peppermint from a jar that has clearly been open since the founders.|The porter puts the boot on a shelf of single boots, all left feet. This one is a right. He looks almost moved.|The porter reads the first page of the notes, says \"Hm,\" and files them under urgent.}\n\n# ---------------- The Familiar Stables ----------------\n\n@event stables_owl_loft\ntitle: The Owl Loft\nplace: stables\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: owl\ntags: nature\nscene: A hay loft in old stables, rows of sleepy owls on the rafters above a student reading by a lantern.\ntext: {Up the ladder, above the hippogriffs, is a hay loft full of owls and quiet.|The owls open one eye each, decide you're furniture, and go back to sleep.|The owls have stopped opening their eyes for you. You've been accepted.} {season == \"winter\": The warmth of the animals rises through the boards.|{Rain ticks on the roof.|A stray letter has fallen into the hay.|The griffon chick is complaining somewhere below.}}\n> left: Read up among the owls\n  lore +small\n  say: {You read until the light goes. The hay gets in everywhere, and the chapter gets in too.|An owl reads over your shoulder for a whole page, then hoots, pointedly, at a footnote.|You read until the griffon below stops complaining. The owls don't stir. You belong up here now.}\n> right: Help sort the owl post\n  charm +small\n  say: {You sort a dropped bundle of post by hand. One owl seems to know which letters you're going to misfile.|You deliver three soggy letters to their owners on the way back. One of them hugs you, which you weren't ready for.|You sort the owl post by the light of the hatch. An owl drops a letter in your lap; it's for you.}\n\n@event stables_brask_rounds\ntitle: Brask's Rounds\nplace: stables\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: paw\ntags: nature\nscene: A burly professor stroking a hippogriff's beak in a stable aisle, ignoring a student holding two feed buckets.\ntext: {Professor Brask is on her rounds, telling each hippogriff she's a very good girl. She tells you to mind the bucket.|Brask is on her rounds. She nods at you, which is more than most get.|Brask greets you by name now, somewhere after the owls.} {season == \"spring\": There are new hippogriff foals, all legs and beak.|{A hippogriff is sulking about its feathers.|The feed is late and everyone is cross.|The stable cat is asleep in the oats.}}\n> left: Ask Brask a question\n  lore +small\n  say: {She answers it at length, to the hippogriff. You take notes anyway.|\"Good question,\" she says, to the owl. You decide that counts.|She answers you directly, which has never happened. The stable cat wakes up to see what's going on.}\n> right: Carry the feed buckets\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {Every stall, two buckets each. Brask says nothing, which is her way of saying thank you.|A hippogriff bows to you over its bucket. Brask looks almost jealous.|You carry the buckets and work around the cat in the oats. Brask hands you the last bucket without being asked.}\n\n@event stables_griffon_sulk\ntitle: The Sulking Griffon\nplace: stables\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: feather\ntags: nature\nscene: A griffon chick with puffed-up feathers glaring from the corner of a stall, a student holding out a morsel at arm's length.\ntext: {The griffon chick is sulking in the corner stall, feathers puffed, refusing breakfast.|The griffon chick is sulking again. It's bigger this time, and so is the sulk.|The griffon is hardly a chick any more, and still sulks like one. It watches you come in.} {year == 1: The sign on the stall says not to hand-feed it, underlined twice.|{The sign on the stall now has a drawing of a bitten hand.|The sign on the stall has been underlined until the paper tore.|Someone has added \"I mean it\" to the sign, in Brask's hand.}}\n> left: Offer it breakfast by hand\n  challenge negotiate apprentice wild:\n    win:\n      charm +small\n      mood +small\n      say: {It considers you for a long time, then takes the morsel with great delicacy. You feel chosen.|It eats from your hand and then, briefly, leans on you. You don't move for ten minutes.|It takes the morsel and then nudges your hand for another. Brask, passing, crosses out \"I mean it\".}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {It takes the morsel, a corner of your sleeve and a nip of your thumb. The sign was right.|It turns its back on you so pointedly that a passing owl looks embarrassed, and its tail catches you hard across the shins on the way round.|It takes the morsel and your glove, and then the sign agrees with it. Brask adds a second underline.}\n> right: Leave it to its sulk\n  lore +tiny\n  say: {You read the notice about griffon moods instead. It is mostly a list of things that will not help.|You leave it alone. It looks, as you go, a little disappointed you gave up.|You leave it to sulk. It follows you along the bars, sulking at a closer range.}\n\n# ---------------- The Boathouse ----------------\n\n@event boathouse_jetty_end\ntitle: The End of the Jetty\nplace: boathouse\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: lantern\ntags: romance\ncast:\n  date: any where romanceable(date) and rel(date) >= 12 and around(date)\nscene: Two people sitting side by side at the end of a wooden jetty, boots over dark water, a lantern between them.\ntext: {{date} is at the end of the jetty, boots over the water. {They} move{s} over without a word.|The end of the jetty has become your place for saying things. Today {date} is already there.|You know which plank creaks. {date} doesn't, and finds it.} {season == \"winter\": The lake has frozen round the posts and creaks like an old house.|{The rowing boats knock gently together.|A heron fishes in the shallows, pretending not to listen.|The village lamps blink on across the water.}}\n> left: Hire a boat and row out\n  gold -small\n  spark date +small\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {season == \"winter\": You can't row on ice, so you sit in a beached boat and pretend. {date} calls it a fine voyage.|{You row badly, {date} laughs, and then neither of you is rowing at all.|Out in the middle the shore goes quiet. {date} says your name like the end of a sentence.|You row out until the village lamps are behind you. {date} trails a hand in the water and doesn't say anything for a long time.}}\n> right: Stay on the jetty and talk\n  rel date +small\n  mood +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {final_year: You talk about where you'll both be next year. Neither of you says \"together\", and both of you hear it.|{You talk until the lantern gutters. The jetty is cold and you don't notice until you stand.|You say one of the things you couldn't say indoors. {date} listens all the way to the end.|You stay on the jetty, on the plank that creaks. {date} says it sounds like you, and won't explain.}}\n\n@event boathouse_green_boat\ntitle: The Leaky Green Boat\nplace: boathouse\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: wave\ntags: life\nscene: Inside an old wooden boathouse, an upturned green rowing boat on trestles, initials carved into every beam above.\ntext: {The boathouse smells of tar and old rope. Its rowing boats are mostly leaks held together by paint, the green one worst of all.|Someone has patched the green boat since your last visit, badly, with what looks like a cake tin.|The boathouse knows you. The green boat still leaks.} {Initials are carved into every beam, some so old they've gone soft.|Rain patters on the roof.|A spider has strung a web across the oars, and seems proud of it.}\n> left: Patch the green boat\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You spend an hour with tar and a mallet. The green boat floats, briefly, with an air of surprise.|Your patch holds for a whole circuit of the boathouse. You have tar in your eyebrows and a certain pride.|You patch the green boat again. It leaks again. You're starting to think that's what it's for.}\n> right: Carve your initials in a beam\n  hint: The boatman charges a few crowns a beam.\n  mood +small\n  gold -tiny\n  say: {final_year: You pay the boatman and carve them next to a pair from sixty years ago. Somebody, one day, will run a thumb over yours and wonder.|{You pay the boatman, and your penknife snaps on the last letter. The initials stay, slightly wrong, which feels right.|You pay the boatman, find a carving that could almost be yours from long ago, and add your own beneath it.|You pay the boatman and carve your initials under the spider's web. The spider doesn't object.}}\n\n@event boathouse_overheard\ntitle: Things Said Outside\nplace: boathouse\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: eye\ntags: social\nscene: Two silhouettes talking quietly on a jetty at night, seen through the slats of a boathouse wall.\ntext: {Two voices out on the jetty, low and urgent. People come to the boathouse to say what they can't say indoors.|You've found the gap in the boathouse planks where every word on the jetty comes through.|Another quiet conversation on the jetty. You're starting to know the academy by its secrets.} {It sounds like a break-up.|It sounds like an apology that isn't going well.|It sounds like someone confessing to a pudding.}\n> left: Listen a little longer\n  guile +small\n  alignment -tiny\n  chance unlikely:\n    renown -small\n    say: {A plank creaks under you. The voices stop. Two very red faces come round the door, and now they know your face too.|An oar slides off its peg with a clatter. Footsteps; a hiss of \"Who's there?\"; you are, unfortunately.|You lean on the plank you know creaks. The pudding confession stops mid-sentence, and two faces you know appear at the door.}\n  else:\n    say: {You learn more than you wanted to. You'll carry it carefully, and never tell.|It ends well, as it happens. You slip away before anyone has to know you were there.|It's about a pudding, honestly. You leave knowing more than any academy should about one pudding.}\n> right: Cough loudly and leave\n  alignment +tiny\n  charm +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {They wait for you to pass, hushed and grateful. You'll never know how it ended, and that's the price.|You make a great show of looking for an oar. By the time you've found one, they've gone.|You cough and leave. On the path you meet the pudding's owner, and decide not to say anything.}\n\n# ---------------- The Orchard ----------------\n\n@event orchard_under_the_trees\ntitle: Under the Old Trees\nplace: orchard\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: tree\ntags: romance nature\ncast:\n  date: any where romanceable(date) and rel(date) >= 12 and around(date)\nscene: Two people beneath an ancient apple tree, blossom drifting down around them like snow.\ntext: {season == \"spring\": Blossom is coming down like snow, and {date} has some in {their} hair.|{season == \"autumn\": The trees are heavy with apples and wasps, and {date} is reaching for one.|{season == \"winter\": The orchard is bare and silver, and {date} is walking between the trees.|{date} has claimed the best of the orchard shade.}}} {{They} wave{s} you over.|{They} saw you coming before you saw {them}.|The old trees seem to expect you by now. {date} only half does.}\n> left: Stay until the light goes\n  spark date +small\n  health -small\n  say: {season == \"spring\": {You stay until the blossom buries your shoes. You miss supper, and you'd do it again.|{date} picks blossom out of your hair, one petal at a time, and takes far too long about it.|You lie in the blossom until you're both freckled with it. {date} says it suits you, and keeps a petal.}|{season == \"winter\": {Your fingers go white, and {date} takes your hands to warm them, and doesn't give them back.|You lean against the same cold trunk until the stars come out over the bare branches.|You walk the bare rows until the frost comes up. {date} breathes on your hands and says it's for practical reasons.}|{You stay until the shadows outgrow the trees. You miss supper. Neither of you mentions it.|The light goes gold, then blue. {date} says something quiet you'll turn over for days.|You stay until the first star. {date} names it after something you said, and won't tell you which thing.}}}\n> right: Help in the orchard together\n  rel date +small\n  vigor +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {season == \"autumn\": {You fill two baskets and earn one wasp sting each. The cook gives you a pie, which you share on the steps.|{date} holds the ladder and says nothing useful, and you don't want {them} to.|You pick from the top branches while {date} sorts the basket below. Every good apple goes to you, which you notice.}|{season == \"winter\": {You prune dead wood side by side until your hands ache. {date} stacks it with great seriousness.|You wrap the young trees against the frost. {date} ties every knot twice.|You mend the orchard fence together, one post each. {date}'s post is straighter, and {they} won't stop mentioning it.}|{season == \"spring\": {You carry hives along the rows for the blossom. One bee follows {date} all afternoon.|You clear the grass under the blossom. {date} keeps stopping to look up.|You graft a branch onto an old tree. {date} says you'll both have to come back to see if it takes.}|{You water the young trees from a bucket chain of two. {date} is soaked by the end, and so are you.|You prop up a branch bowed with early apples. {date} holds it, and holds still, and you notice.|You rake the cut grass into rows. {date} lies down in the first row, and the work goes no further.}}}}\n\n@event orchard_windfalls\ntitle: Windfalls\nplace: orchard\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: pouch\ntags: nature life\nscene: A student gathering fallen fruit into a basket under gnarled orchard trees, older than the towers behind them.\ntext: {The orchard trees are older than the towers, and never tidy.|The oldest tree has dropped its fruit in a ring, like a table laid for guests.|You know which tree drops what by now, and which one throws.} {season == \"autumn\": Pears and apples lie bruised in the grass, attended by wasps.|{season == \"winter\": Last year's apples lie frozen, hard as stones.|{season == \"spring\": It's mostly blossom, and the odd stubborn pear.|Early apples lie in the shade, small and sharp.}}}\n> left: Gather windfalls to sell\n  gold +small\n  health -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {The cook buys your basket for the cider vats and finds {season == \"winter\": a frozen beetle|a wasp} in it. You both pretend that's normal.|A full basket, {season == \"winter\": numb fingers|two stings} and a crick in your back. The crowns feel earned.|You go straight to the trees that drop the good ones. The cook pays for a full basket and says you have an eye.}\n> right: Eat one under the oldest tree\n  mood +small\n  chance unlikely:\n    health -small\n    say: {{season == \"winter\": It's frozen solid. Your teeth will remember this.|There was a wasp in it. There was, briefly, a wasp in you.}|It's sour enough to turn your face inside out, and your stomach complains about it until supper. A passing first-year laughs, and so, eventually, do you.|The one that throws gets you on the shoulder. You eat its apple out of spite, and it's {season == \"winter\": frozen through|full of wasp}, and so is your mood.}\n  else:\n    health +tiny\n    say: {It's the best thing you've eaten all month. The old tree creaks, as if pleased.|You eat it slowly. Above you, the oldest tree drops one more, gently, beside your hand.|You eat it under the oldest tree, as you always do now. It drops a leaf on your knee, and you keep it.}\n\n@event orchard_cider_shed\ntitle: The Cider Shed\nplace: orchard\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: cup\ntags: social life\nscene: A tumbledown orchard shed with a wooden cider press inside, three students turning the screw and a fourth keeping watch at the door.\ntext: {In the orchard shed, a few students are running an unofficial cider press.|The lookout at the cider shed has started nodding you in.|The cider shed is a fixture now. Even the lookout seems bored.} {season == \"autumn\": The press groans with this year's apples.|The shed smells of last autumn and wet straw.} {A goat is watching from the hedge, very carefully saying nothing.|Someone has carved a menu into the door.|A prefect's scarf hangs on a nail, unexplained.}\n> left: Buy a cup\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +small\n  chance unlikely:\n    renown -tiny\n    say: {A prefect turns up as you raise the cup. Everyone is suddenly very interested in apples.|The lookout whistles too late. A prefect takes your name, then, after some thought, your cup.|The prefect whose scarf it is comes back for it. Everyone looks at the scarf, then at you, holding a cup.}\n  else:\n    say: {It's sharp, cold and a little bit fizzy. The day goes golden at the edges.|You drink it on an upturned bucket while someone tells a story about a goose. Excellent cider. Better goose.|You drink it under the prefect's scarf. Nobody asks, and the cider is the best yet.}\n> right: Help turn the press, for a share\n  alchemy +small\n  mood -tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You turn the screw until your shoulders burn. The second-year in charge teaches you why some apples are better bruised.|You're sticky to the elbows and you've learned more about fermentation than in a term of class.|You turn the press with the regulars now. The second-year gives you a jar of your own to take home.}\n\n# ---------------- Old Maud's Cottage ----------------\n\n@event maud_cottage_kettle\ntitle: Tea at Maud's\nplace: maud_cottage\nwhen: slot == \"evening\"\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: cup\ntags: home\nscene: A low cottage kitchen, a kettle on the hob, an old woman pouring tea into a chipped cup, a goat's head poking through the open door.\ntext: {Old Maud's kettle is nearly boiling, as it always is. \"Sit down, dearie,\" she says. \"You look like a puddle with a worry.\"|The kettle is nearly boiling. Maud has already put out your cup, the chipped one.|Your chipped cup is waiting. Maud says it's been asking after you.|Maud pours before you've sat down. \"Same as ever, dearie,\" she says, and it is.} {Bartholomew is eating a tea towel.|Rain drums on the thatch.|A hen is asleep on the windowsill.|Bees drone in the eaves.}\n> left: Stay for a second cup\n  mood +small\n  rel maud +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {Maud tells you a sad story the way other people give a recipe. You walk home late and cold, thinking about it.|\"Never trust a dry spring, dearie,\" she says. You won't understand it for a fortnight.|The second cup becomes a third. It's dark when you leave, and you've only half noticed.|You stay for a second cup and the bees in the eaves put Maud to sleep mid-story. You wash the cups and let yourself out.}\n> right: Ask her for a remedy\n  health +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {She brews you something that tastes of ditches. \"Down in one, dearie.\" You feel better at once, and resent it.|\"For what ails you,\" she says, and doesn't say what she thinks that is. It tastes of nettles and of being right.|She hands you a remedy before you've said what's wrong. It works, which is the annoying part.|\"Honey and bee-balm, dearie, and stop worrying.\" It tastes of summer. You stop worrying, briefly.}\n\n@event maud_cottage_bartholomew\ntitle: Bartholomew Is Out\nplace: maud_cottage\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: house\ntags: home\nscene: A goat standing triumphantly on the roof of a henhouse, chickens scattering, a student approaching with an apple held out.\ntext: {Bartholomew the goat has got out.|Bartholomew is out again, and has learned to lift the gate latch with his teeth.|Bartholomew has escaped so often that the hens no longer scatter.} {slot == \"evening\": Maud doesn't even look up from her knitting.|Maud's note on the door says she's at the greenhouses, and to mind the goat.} {He has eaten half a washing line.|He is standing on the roof of the henhouse, looking pleased.|He is in the cabbages, conducting a slow and thorough war.}\n> left: Chase him down\n  challenge feat apprentice wild:\n    win:\n      rel maud +small\n      vigor +tiny\n      say: {You corner him by the water butt. He gives up with the air of someone who allowed it. {slot == \"evening\": \"Ta, dearie,\" calls Maud.|You tie him to the gate and leave Maud a note.}|You catch him on the second lap. He chews your sleeve all the way back, as a souvenir.|You chase him out of the cabbages with the ease of long practice. The hens don't even look up.}\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: {He leads you twice round the orchard and once through the hen run, then trots home on his own.|You end up in the cabbages. Bartholomew watches you from the roof, chewing, unhurried.|He leads you through the cabbages and leaves you there. The war continues without you.}\n> right: Bribe him with your lunch\n  rel maud +tiny\n  mood +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {He takes your sandwich and follows you home like a gentleman. You go hungry until supper.|Your lunch disappears in three bites. Bartholomew looks at you as if you might be worth knowing.|He takes your lunch and walks home ahead of you. He knows the way better than you do by now.}\n\n@event maud_cottage_top_shelf\ntitle: The Top Shelf\nplace: maud_cottage\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: bottle\ntags: magic home\nscene: A high cottage shelf crowded with stoppered jars and handwritten labels, one jar glowing faintly blue.\ntext: {{slot == \"evening\": Maud has stepped out to the hens.|Maud is up at the greenhouses.} The top shelf of jars is right there, every label in her spidery hand.|Maud has stepped out, and the top shelf has gained a jar since you were last here.|You know the top shelf by heart, except for whichever jar Maud added last.} One label reads {\"not for you, dearie\"|\"only in emergencies\"|\"do not shake\"}, and the jar is {glowing faintly|humming|warm to the touch}.\n> left: Open it, just a little\n  alignment -tiny\n  chance even:\n    mana +small\n    mood +small\n    say: {Something sweet and blue drifts out and settles in your chest. You feel, briefly, enormous.|A breath of summer rain escapes. You put the lid back on smiling.|It's warm, and so is the air that comes out. You stand in it like a cat in a sunbeam until you hear Maud's step.}\n  else:\n    health -small\n    mood -tiny\n    say: {Your eyebrows turn the colour of the label. When Maud sees them, she says nothing whatsoever.|It sneezes. Then you sneeze, for an hour. \"Told you, dearie,\" says Maud later, not unkindly.|You shook it, a little. The label was right. Your hair stands on end until supper.}\n> right: Dust the shelf instead\n  alchemy +small\n  say: {You dust every jar and read every label. Maud's handwriting is a whole education, if a strange one.|Maud finds a clean shelf. \"Somebody's been learning,\" she says, which isn't quite thanks.|You dust the new jar first and read its label twice. Maud finds you there and gives you a look that's almost proud.}\n\n# ---------------- The Secret Garden ----------------\n\n@event secret_garden_humming_pear\ntitle: The Humming Pear\nplace: secret_garden\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: tree\ntags: nature magic\nscene: A student lying in the grass under a pear tree in a hidden walled garden, ferns glowing softly around them.\ntext: {The pear tree in Maud's secret garden is humming to itself.|The pear tree has changed its tune since you were last here, as if it's been practising.|The pear tree hums when you come in now. You'd like to think it's for you.} {season == \"winter\": Its tune is low and slow, like something asleep.|{season == \"spring\": Its tune has gone quick and bright with blossom.|{Its tune is one you almost know.|Its tune has a sad turn in the middle.|Its tune is one you've heard Maud whistle.}}}\n> left: Hum along with it\n  mana +small\n  say: {You find the harmony on the third try. The tree corrects you twice, kindly.|The two of you hum until the ferns glow brighter. Afterwards you can't get the tune out of your head for days.|You hum along without thinking now. The pear tree drops a pear in your lap at the end, which feels like applause.}\n> right: Nap in its shade\n  mood +small\n  health +small\n  if gold > 0:\n    gold -tiny\n    say: {You wake rested, a few crowns lighter, with a fresh root curled over the spot where they fell.|You sleep like a seed. A few crowns slip out of your pocket and the roots close over them. Rent, perhaps.|You sleep in your usual hollow between the roots. A few crowns are gone when you wake, and the tree looks very innocent.}\n  else:\n    say: You sleep like a seed and wake with your empty pocket turned out, as if the roots had checked.\n\n@event secret_garden_silver_bees\ntitle: The Silver Bees\nplace: secret_garden\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: sparkle\ntags: nature magic\nscene: Bees made of silver light hovering around a hive in a hollow stump, a student reaching carefully towards a dripping comb.\ntext: {The silver bees are working the ferns; their hive in the old stump drips honey the colour of moonlight.|The silver bees have built a second comb, lopsided, as if in a hurry.|The silver bees know you now, more or less.} {season == \"winter\": Even in winter they fly, slowly, like snow going the wrong way.|{The comb is fuller than you've ever seen it.|One of them lands on your sleeve and stays.|They're dancing a direction you can't read.}}\n> left: Take a little comb\n  alchemy +small\n  chance unlikely:\n    health -small\n    say: {A silver sting feels like a cold spark. Your hand glows for an hour and aches for two.|The bees file a complaint, in the form of your thumb.|They know your hand by now, and sting it anyway, more out of habit than anger.}\n  else:\n    say: {You take a thumb's width of comb. It tastes like starlight and burns a little going down.|The bees let you take a little, and watch to make sure it's only a little.|They let you take a little and dance something at you afterwards. You'd give a lot to know what it meant.}\n> right: Follow a bee home\n  lore +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {The bee leads you through brambles to a fern you've never seen, and then leaves you there, scratched and curious.|You follow it all the way round the garden and back to the stump. It seems to find this funny.|You follow a bee that's dancing the strange direction. It leads you to the garden wall, and over it, and you can't follow.}\n\n@event secret_garden_glowing_ferns\ntitle: The Glowing Ferns\nplace: secret_garden\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: leaf\ntags: nature magic\nscene: A hidden garden at dusk, waist-high ferns glowing pale green, one student crouched with fingers near a frond.\ntext: {The ferns in the secret garden glow when the light goes. One frond would light your dormitory for a week. Maud said not to pick them.|Since your last visit a new fern has unfurled by the gate, already glowing.|The ferns glow. You know exactly what a frond is worth, and what Maud said.} {One has grown over the path, right at your hand.|A broken frond lies on the ground already, still glowing.|They're brighter than you've ever seen them.}\n> left: Pick a single frond\n  mood +small\n  alignment -tiny\n  chance unlikely:\n    mood -small\n    say: {The frond goes dark before you reach the door. The garden feels a little colder to you the next time.|The frond curls up in your hand like a woodlouse and goes out. The silver bees follow you to the gate, accusingly.|You pick the brightest one. It goes out in your hand like a sigh, and the rest dim a little as you pass.}\n  else:\n    say: {It lights your dormitory green for a week. You read by it, and feel very slightly watched.|You keep it in a jar by your bed. It glows softer every night, like something being polite.|It's the brightest frond you've ever seen, and it lights your room for a fortnight. You don't tell Maud.}\n> right: Leave them glowing\n  lore +small\n  say: {You sit and watch the glow instead, and learn the way it moves with your breath. Maud would approve, if she knew.|You leave them be. On the way out, the ferns lean after you, like a dog that wanted to come.|You sit among them at their brightest. They glow brighter while you stay, and that's enough.}\n\n# ---------------- The Courier Loft ----------------\n\n@event courier_loft_letter_home\ntitle: A Letter Home\nplace: courier_loft\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: letter\ntags: home\nscene: A draughty loft full of pigeonholes, a student writing a letter on a parcel by the light of a high window.\ntext: {The courier loft will take a letter home for next to nothing. You've meant to write for weeks.|This time the letter has been half-written in your head since breakfast.|Writing home is a habit now, and the loft knows your handwriting.} {final_year: There aren't many letters left to send from here.|{season == \"winter\": Frost on the window; the pigeonholes stuffed with cards.|{The dispatch bell rings twice while you write.|A broken broom leans against your elbow.|A courier is reading over your shoulder, and apologises.}}}\n> left: Write it all down and send it\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: {You write three pages and post them before you can cross anything out. You feel lighter, and a little homesick.|You tell them about your classes, and not about the hard parts. Then, in a postscript, you tell them about the hard parts.|You write the letter you've written before, with this month's news in it. The courier says your family must look forward to them.}\n> right: Write it, then don't send it\n  lore +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You read it over and fold it into your pocket instead. Some letters are only for writing.|You keep this one. The courier asks if you're sure. You aren't.|You put this one with the others you didn't send. It's getting to be quite a stack.}\n\n@event courier_loft_dispatch_bell\ntitle: The Dispatch Bell\nplace: courier_loft\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: bell\ntags: life\nscene: A chaotic loft as a dispatch bell clangs, couriers grabbing brooms and parcels, one holding a parcel out to a visitor.\ntext: {The dispatch bell clangs and the loft explodes into brooms. A courier shoves a parcel at you. \"You've got legs. Lecture tower!\"|The couriers have started eyeing the ladder whenever someone climbs it.|The bell. A parcel. A look. You know the drill.} {The parcel is ticking.|The parcel is addressed to \"Whoever Is On Duty\".|The parcel is very cold.}\n> left: Run the parcel across campus\n  gold +tiny\n  vigor +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You get it there out of breath and in time. The tip is a few crowns and a very strange look.|You run it up four flights of stairs. The recipient signs for it without looking up, as if you were the post.|You run the cold parcel across campus with your hands going numb. The tip is generous, and given quickly.}\n> right: Hand it back, politely\n  mood +tiny\n  renown -tiny\n  say: {\"Fine, fine,\" says the courier, already on a broom. You hear about it, loudly, from the rafters.|The courier takes it back with a sigh that follows you down the ladder.|You hand the cold parcel back. The courier holds it at arm's length and doesn't argue.}\n\n@event courier_loft_broom_rack\ntitle: The Fast Broom\nplace: courier_loft\nwhen: not member(broom_courier)\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: broom\ntags: life\nscene: A rack of courier brooms in a loft, one sleek black broom set apart from the others with a red tag on its handle.\ntext: {At the end of the rack is the courier loft's fast broom, tagged in red. Couriers only. Nobody's watching.|The fast broom is back on the rack, red tag and all.|The red-tagged broom. You've thought about it every time you've come up here.} {season == \"winter\": The loft doors are open on a sky full of snow.|{The loft doors stand open on a blue sky.|Someone has polished it recently.|The red tag has a fresh scorch mark.}}\n> left: Take it for one lap\n  alignment -tiny\n  challenge feat journeyman+ wild:\n    win:\n      mood +small\n      renown +tiny\n      say: {One lap of the towers, fast enough to make your ears sing. You put it back with the bristles barely warm.|You fly it under the bridges of light and come back laughing. Nobody saw, except, possibly, everybody.|You take the fast broom for one lap at last. It flies like it's been waiting for you. You put it back and can't stop smiling.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -small\n      say: {It knows you aren't a courier. It shows you the courtyard, very close up.|It bucks you off into a haystack outside the stables. The stable hand laughs about it for a week.|The broom with the scorched tag has a scorched temper. It puts you through the stable roof.}\n> right: Tighten its bristles instead\n  lore +small\n  say: {You spend an hour learning how a good broom is bound. It's more satisfying than you'd admit.|You retie the bristles properly. Some courier will fly a little faster tomorrow and never know why.|You rebind the bristles and the scorched tag. The head courier notices and says nothing, which is promising.}\n\n# ---------------- The Whisperwood ----------------\n\n@event whisperwood_sleeptalk\ntitle: The Forest Talks\nplace: whisperwood\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: tree\ntags: nature mystery\nscene: A student standing very still in a misty old forest, head tilted, as if listening to the trees.\ntext: {The Whisperwood is talking in its sleep. You stand very still to make out the words.|You've started to catch whole words in the forest's mumbling.|You've learned to stand still and let the forest talk.} Today it's {listing trees that were felled|arguing about the rain|saying a name, over and over, that isn't yours}. {season == \"winter\": Snow muffles half of it.|{season == \"autumn\": Leaves fall with every word.|Fog moves between the trunks.}}\n> left: Listen closely\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You catch a whole sentence. It's about a winter long ago, and it's sad, and you walk home with it.|It notices you listening and goes quiet, the way a sleeper does. You leave feeling rude.|You listen to the name until you could say it in your sleep. You don't know whose it is. You'll find out, one day.}\n> right: Walk on, humming\n  vigor +small\n  say: {You walk the long loop humming loudly. The forest grumbles and turns over. You come back with muddy boots and a clear head.|You hum all the way round. Twice, the forest hums back.|You hum over the name it keeps saying. The forest falls quiet, as if it had been waiting for someone to answer.}\n\n@event whisperwood_foragers\ntitle: Foraging Season\nplace: whisperwood\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: mushroom\ntags: nature social\nscene: A handful of students with baskets crouching in a forest clearing, one holding up a mushroom for the others to inspect.\ntext: {season == \"autumn\": The Whisperwood is full of mushrooms and students with baskets.|{season == \"winter\": A few hardy foragers are scraping lichen off the oaks.|{season == \"spring\": The clearings are thick with wild garlic and foragers.|The brambles are heavy with berries, and so are the foragers.}}} {A second-year from Rootward waves you over.|The Rootward forager is back, and saves you a spot.|The foragers treat you as one of their own now.}\n> left: Forage for the apothecary\n  gold +small\n  health -small\n  say: {Pell & Daughters buys your basket and throws out a third of it. You've still got nettle rash.|A good basket, a fair price, and brambles in places you'd rather not discuss.|The foragers show you their own patch. Pell & Daughters keeps the whole basket, which has never happened.}\n> right: Learn the plants from the expert\n  alchemy +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {The second-year names everything, twice, with its uses and its poisons. You come away full of names and short of patience.|You learn which mushroom is supper and which is a funeral. It takes until supper, and you'll need it.|You teach a first-year what the Rootward student taught you, and get two of the names wrong. They're corrected for you, kindly.}\n\n@event whisperwood_off_path\ntitle: The Path That Wasn't\nplace: whisperwood\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: compass\ntags: nature mystery\nscene: A narrow path branching off into fog between old trees, a student pausing at the fork with a hand on a marked trunk.\ntext: {A path opens to your left that wasn't there last time.|Another path that wasn't there before. The Whisperwood does this.|The wood has offered you a new path again. You're starting to think it likes you.} {It's lined with pale stones.|It smells of woodsmoke where there's no fire.|It goes downhill, which is wrong, because you're at the bottom of the hill.}\n> left: Follow it a little way\n  challenge research journeyman wild:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      mana +small\n      health -tiny\n      say: {It leads to a clearing you'll never find again, full of moonpetal buds. You come back with a pocketful of quiet.|It loops you round to an old stone with words on it. You copy them down. They mean nothing yet.|You follow it downhill, though there's no down left. It brings you out above the lake, which is impossible, and lovely.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {You walk in circles until dusk and come out at the lake, with no idea how.|The path ends at a wall of fog. Turning back takes much longer than going in did.|You follow it downhill for an hour and come out exactly where you started. The wood seems pleased with itself.}\n> right: Mark a tree and turn back\n  guile +tiny\n  say: {You carve an arrow in the bark. The next time you come, the arrow points somewhere else.|You turn back. The path watches you go, and quietly closes behind you.|You mark a tree. The next morning there's a mark on the tree beside it, in the same hand.}\n"},{"path":"content/events/pools/internships.grim","text":"# Internships (playtest 2, X12; with M16): a shared backbone every placement draws (`plan: internship`; its\n# performance through `perform internship`, whichever host you are with), and for each host one card that changes\n# something beyond the summer. The everyday cards per host are in pools/plans_thesis.grim.\n\n# ---------------- The backbone ----------------\n\n@event intern_midsummer_review\ntitle: The Midsummer Review\nplan: internship\nclass: milestone\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 4\nwhen: month == 12\nweight: 14\nart: scroll\ntags: study job\nscene: A supervisor's desk with a single sheet of notes face down, a student sitting opposite with hands folded too tightly.\ntext: {Halfway through the placement, your supervisor asks to see you. There's a single sheet of notes on the desk, face down.|The midsummer review again: the desk, the sheet, the tea nobody drinks.|You know the review's shape by now. You still don't know what's on the sheet.} {performance(internship) >= 60: Your supervisor is almost smiling.|{performance(internship) < 40: Your supervisor is not smiling.|Your supervisor's face gives nothing away.}}\n> left: Ask for honest criticism\n  perform internship +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {You get it, three things you do badly, in order. You fix two of them by the end of the summer. Your supervisor notices both.|\"Most interns ask for praise,\" your supervisor says, and turns the sheet over. It's a long list. You're grateful for every line, eventually.|This time you ask to see the sheet itself. It's shorter than you feared, and every line is fair.}\n> right: Ask what you've done well\n  mood +small\n  perform internship -tiny\n  say: {Your supervisor reads the good half aloud. You leave glowing, and never hear the other half.|\"Your handwriting,\" your supervisor says, after a pause, \"is excellent.\" You decide to take it.|Your supervisor reads you the top of the sheet and folds the rest away. You'll wonder what's under the fold.}\n\n@event intern_credit_taken\ntitle: Someone Else's Name\nplan: internship\nclass: incident\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 10\nart: paper\ntags: study job\nscene: A report pinned on a wall, a student staring at the name at the bottom of it, which is not their own.\ntext: {The report you spent a week on is pinned up in the corridor, praised by everyone, with another intern's name at the bottom.|Another piece of your work, another name on it.|It's happened again, and this time the other intern doesn't even look away.} {Your supervisor is reading it with approval.|The other intern is being congratulated.|Your notes are still on your desk, in your hand.}\n> left: Take it to your supervisor\n  challenge negotiate journeyman+:\n    win:\n      perform internship +medium\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {You bring your notes, your drafts, your dates. Your supervisor reads them in silence and changes the name in red ink. The corridor goes very quiet around you.|You make your case calmly. The name is changed. The other intern doesn't speak to you again, which is fine.|You put your notes on your supervisor's desk, in your hand and dated. The name is changed, and this time the other intern looks away.}\n    lose:\n      perform internship -small\n      mood -small\n      say: {Your supervisor hears you out and says it's \"a matter for the two of you\". It isn't. You go back to your desk with your face burning.|Without proof enough, you sound petty, and you know it. The name stays.|Your notes are in your hand, but so, it turns out, is a draft in theirs. Your supervisor can't tell which came first, and the name stays.}\n> right: Let it go and work faster\n  perform internship +tiny\n  mood -medium\n  say: {You say nothing and do the next one twice as well, in ink that's hard to copy. It doesn't feel like justice. It feels like work.|You let it go. You write your name on every page from then on, small, in the corner.|You take your notes home. The next piece of work never leaves your sight until it's on the supervisor's desk.}\n\n@event intern_city_evening\ntitle: An Evening Off\nplan: internship\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: lantern\ntags: social\nscene: A lamplit street far from the academy, interns spilling out of a busy inn, one looking back at the lit window of the workplace.\ntext: {{member(intern_expedition) or member(intern_field_station): The other interns are going out tonight, three miles to a village dance, with a fiddler and a supper laid on at a few coins a head.|The other interns are going out tonight, to an inn with music and a stall that sells fried bread at midnight.}|Another evening off, and the others are already at the door.|\"Coming?\" says the intern with the biscuits. \"Last time you said next time.\"} {member(intern_expedition) or member(intern_field_station): {The track is dark, but somebody has a lantern.|It's the only dance for thirty miles.|Someone has brought a fiddle of their own.}|{The streets are bright.|There's a fair on the river.|Somebody knows a band.}} Your work for tomorrow isn't done.\n> left: Go out with them\n  mood +medium\n  perform internship -tiny\n  gold -tiny\n  say: {You pay your share of the supper, dance badly, laugh well and walk home at two in the morning, arm in arm with people you met last month. Tomorrow's work is a little worse for it.|You go, and stand your round. You learn more about the place from the other interns in one evening than from a month of briefings, and remember half of it.|You keep last time's promise and go. The intern with the biscuits shares them on the walk home, and tomorrow's work is a little worse for it.}\n> right: Finish tomorrow's work\n  perform internship +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {You finish the work by lamplight. You hear them come home, singing. In the morning your work is the best on the table.|You stay in. The work is good. The evening is long.|\"Next time,\" you say again, and mean it less. Your work is excellent, and the biscuits are gone by morning.}\n\n@event intern_letter_from_campus\ntitle: A Letter From the Castle\nplan: internship\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: letter\ntags: social\ncast:\n  friend: any where rel(friend) >= 8 and tagged(friend, student)\nscene: A letter open on a desk far from the academy, its margins full of little drawings of the castle, a student smiling at it.\ntext: {A letter from {friend}, forwarded twice, with the castle drawn in the margins.|Another letter from campus, this one from {friend}.|{friend}'s handwriting on the envelope. You open it before your coat is off.} {The letter misses you, in a roundabout way.|{They} {are} bored without you, {they} say{s}.|There's gossip, and a pressed flower.} Answering it properly would take the evening.\n> left: Write back at length\n  rel friend +small\n  perform internship -tiny\n  say: {Six pages, with drawings of your own. You post it at dawn and are late for the briefing, and don't mind.|You write back everything: the work, the place, the interns. It's like talking to {them}.|You answer the gossip with gossip of your own, and press a flower from here to send back. It's like being in two places.}\n> right: Send a quick note\n  perform internship +tiny\n  rel friend -tiny\n  say: {\"Busy. Miss you. More soon.\" It's true, and it's thin, and you know it.|Three lines and a promise. You mean to write more. The summer is very full.|A quick note, and the pressed flower kept in your notebook. You'll write properly when the summer's less full.}\n\n# ---------------- One card per host that reaches past the summer ----------------\n\n@event intern_conclave_your_ward\ntitle: A Ward of Your Own\ntrack: intern_conclave\nclass: milestone\ntone: tense\nweight: 18\nart: shield\ntags: study magic\nscene: A circular test chamber, a student at the centre holding up a shimmering ward against a bolt of light, researchers behind thick glass on their feet.\ntext: The Conclave is testing a new ward, and the researcher who designed it is ill. \"You've read every note,\" says your supervisor. \"Cast it. If it holds, it's yours to keep, and your name goes in the paper.\" Behind the glass, half the Conclave has come to watch.\n> left: Cast it\n  wage\n  challenge spell expert:\n    win:\n      perform intern_conclave +large\n      learn_spell ward\n      renown +small\n      say: The bolt hits the ward and stops, humming, an inch from your nose. Behind the glass, someone starts clapping. Your name goes in the paper, third of four, and the ward is yours for good.\n    lose:\n      perform intern_conclave -small\n      health -medium\n      say: The ward holds for half a heartbeat and then doesn't. You wake on the floor with your hair standing up and a researcher fanning you with your own notes.\n> right: Ask them to wait for the designer\n  wage\n  perform intern_conclave -tiny\n  lore +small\n  say: \"Sensible,\" says your supervisor, in a voice that means \"disappointing\". The designer recovers, casts it, and thanks you in the paper's footnotes for the notes you kept.\n\n@event intern_alchemists_safety\ntitle: The Cracked Vat\ntrack: intern_alchemists\nclass: milestone\ntone: grim\nweight: 18\nart: cauldron\ntags: job danger\nscene: A huge iron vat in an alchemical works, a hairline crack weeping green steam, workers walking past it without looking.\ntext: The seventh vat at the Guild works has a crack in it. Everyone knows. The foreman says it'll last the season, and the season's quota depends on it. Workers walk past it without looking. You've seen what the steam does to brass.\n> left: Report it to the Guild masters\n  wage\n  alignment +small\n  perform intern_alchemists -small\n  mood -small\n  say: The masters close the seventh vat for a week and the foreman doesn't speak to you again. In the third week the replacement vat arrives, and an old worker shakes your hand without a word.\n> right: Mend it yourself, quietly\n  wage\n  challenge brew expert:\n    win:\n      perform intern_alchemists +medium\n      alchemy +medium\n      say: You seal it from the inside in the night, with a sealant of your own. It holds through the season. The foreman never knows. The old worker does, somehow, and leaves an apple on your bench every morning.\n    lose:\n      health -large\n      perform intern_alchemists -small\n      say: The steam gets you as the seal gives. You wake in the works infirmary with blistered hands, and the vat is closed anyway.\n\n@event intern_expedition_new_star\ntitle: A Star Nobody Charted\ntrack: intern_expedition\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nweight: 18\nart: star\ntags: study magic\nscene: A telescope on a high plateau at night, a student with an eye to the eyepiece and a chart on their knee, a single new star circled in red.\ntext: The lead gives you tonight's watch at the eyepiece, alone. At three in the morning there's a star in it that isn't on any chart: small, steady, blue. The expedition lead is asleep. By custom, whoever records a new star first may name it.\n> left: Wake the lead and share it\n  wage\n  perform intern_expedition +medium\n  lore +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: The lead comes in a nightshirt and stares until dawn. The star goes in the record under the expedition's name and yours, and the whole camp toasts it at breakfast.\n> right: Record it in your own name\n  wage\n  renown +medium\n  perform intern_expedition +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You write it into the log: the time, the place, a name you choose. It's yours for as long as there are charts. At breakfast the lead reads the log twice, and is polite to you all week, which is worse.\n\n@event intern_field_station_foal\ntitle: The Orphaned Foal\ntrack: intern_field_station\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nweight: 18\nart: paw\ntags: nature\nscene: A newborn hippogriff foal shivering in the heather on the moor, a student kneeling with a blanket, the field station lamps far off.\ntext: On the moor, a hippogriff foal, newborn and alone. The station's rule is plain: you count wild creatures, you don't raise them. The station chief says it won't last the night. It looks at you as if you were the only warm thing on the moor, which you are.\n> left: Take it back and raise it\n  wage\n  vigor +small\n  health -small\n  alignment +small\n  perform intern_field_station -small\n  rel brask +small\n  say: You feed it every two hours for a month and break the rule every one of them. By summer's end it flies. Back at Lumenhold, Professor Brask hears the story and invites you to supper. \"Rules,\" she says, \"are for people who've never held a foal.\"\n> right: Count it and leave it\n  wage\n  perform intern_field_station +small\n  mood -medium\n  say: You write it down: one foal, alone, the date. You don't sleep. In the morning the chief tells you its mother came back in the night. You never find out if that's true.\n\n@event intern_thornwick_offer\ntitle: An Offer From Thornwick\ntrack: intern_thornwick\nclass: milestone\ntone: tense\nweight: 18\nart: letter\nwhen: month == 12\ntags: study\nscene: A lacquered office at a rival academy, a smiling dean sliding a sealed offer across a desk, a window full of unfamiliar towers.\ntext: Thornwick's dean asks to see you on your last week. \"Transfer to us,\" she says, sliding a sealed offer across the desk. \"Full scholarship, your own lab bench, a seat in our illusion faculty in time. Lumenhold never valued glamour. We do.\" She's sincere, and that's the danger.\n> left: Decline, warmly\n  wage\n  perform intern_thornwick +small\n  rel vey +small\n  say: \"Lumenhold is home,\" you say. She smiles as if she expected it. \"The offer stands.\" Back at the castle, Professor Vey hears about it somehow and gives you an approving look that lasts all term.\n> right: Keep the offer, unopened\n  wage\n  gold +medium\n  renown +small\n  flag thornwick_offer\n  mood -small\n  say: You keep it, sealed, in the bottom of your trunk. Thornwick sends a stipend \"as a courtesy\". Knowing there's a door out makes some evenings easier, and some harder.\n\n@event intern_unseen_office_file\ntitle: The File With Your Name\ntrack: intern_unseen_office\nclass: milestone\ntone: eerie\nweight: 18\nart: eye\ntags: mystery\nscene: A windowless office lined with drawers, a student holding open a thin file with their own name on the label, a clerk's empty chair behind them.\ntext: Sorting files for the Office, you find one with your name on it. It's thin. The clerk has gone to lunch. The last page is about Lumenhold, and the lake, and a tower nobody has opened in a century.\n> left: Read it all\n  wage\n  guile +small\n  find lead_seventh_tower\n  perform intern_unseen_office -small\n  say: They know where you sleep and what you eat, which is unsettling. The last page is mostly blacked out. What's left says the seventh tower \"is to remain closed\", and nothing about why. When the clerk comes back you are filing something else. The clerk looks at you a little too long.\n> right: Put it back unread\n  wage\n  perform intern_unseen_office +medium\n  mood -small\n  say: You put it back. The clerk returns, checks the drawer and smiles for the first time all summer. \"The Office notices who doesn't look,\" the clerk says. You didn't look. You'll wonder for years.\n"},{"path":"content/events/pools/letters.grim","text":"# Letters (playtest 2, M38, M19; stub — N14 fills them): off campus a letter comes each night, and anyone who\n# reaches you while one of you is away writes instead of calling. `trigger: letter` keeps them out of every other\n# pool. Repeatable routine events; see docs/authoring/pools.md.\n\n@event letter_from_a_friend\ntitle: A Letter From {friend}\ntrigger: letter\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ncast:\n  friend: any where met(friend) and rel(friend) >= 6\nper: friend\nrepeat: 6\nweight: 20\nart: letter\ntags: social letter\nscene: A folded letter on a windowsill, the handwriting crowded to the very edge of the page, a pressed leaf inside.\ntext: {friend} has written. {~Three pages, mostly about nothing, which is the best kind.|Half of it is crossed out and the other half underlined.|There's a drawing in the margin that is probably meant to be you.} {off_campus: The castle, they say, is too quiet without you.|They're away, and they say the quiet there is the wrong kind.}\n> left: Write back at length\n  rel friend +small\n  gold -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You write until the candle gutters and spend the postage you meant for something else. It's worth it.|Four pages back, and a pressed flower of your own. You pay the extra stamp.|You write back pages and draw them in the margin, just as badly. The postage costs more than you meant it to.}\n> right: Send a short note\n  rel friend +tiny\n  chance likely:\n    say: {~A few lines, warm enough. They'll know you read it.|\"Missing you. More soon.\" It says enough.|A few lines, and a drawing of them in return. They'll laugh.}\n  else:\n    rel friend -tiny\n    say: {~Your note is so short it reads as cross. You meant it kindly.|Two lines. You can picture their face reading it, and it isn't pleased.|A short note, and no drawing. They'll notice the missing drawing more than the words.}\n\n@event letter_castle_news\ntitle: News From the Castle\ntrigger: letter\nwhen: has(plan_home)\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: paper\ntags: letter\nscene: A copy of the academy newssheet, forwarded in an envelope, its corners soft from travelling.\ntext: Someone has forwarded the Lumen Ledger to you at home. {~A professor's hat has gone missing.|The lake froze, or thawed, depending on which column you believe.|There's a letter to the editor about the refectory soup, and it is furious.} It's strange to read about the castle from your old bedroom.\n> left: Read it cover to cover\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You read every word, even the notices. By the end you're homesick for a place you're not at.|The whole paper, twice. Home feels very far from the castle tonight, and the castle very far from home.|You read every word, down to the furious letter about the soup, and find you miss even the soup.}\n> right: Just the gossip page\n  chance likely:\n    mood +small\n    say: {~The gossip is excellent. You laugh out loud and have to explain it to everyone at the table.|Someone you know is in it, for something ridiculous. Perfect.|The gossip is good, but the furious letter about the soup is better. You read it aloud at the table.}\n  else:\n    mood -tiny\n    say: {~The gossip is about someone you like, and it's unkind.|There's nothing in it you didn't already know. Somehow that's worse.|The gossip is all about a quarrel you'd rather not hear of. Even the letter about the soup can't save it.}\n\n@event letter_writing_home\ntitle: Writing Home\ntrigger: letter\nwhen: has(plan_internship)\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: letter\ntags: letter home\nscene: A borrowed desk in a strange room, a half-written letter under a lamp, a map of somewhere else pinned on the wall.\ntext: The placement keeps you busy, and home feels further away than the miles. {~There's ink, paper and a quiet hour.|The lamp in your lodgings is good for writing.|You owe home a letter, and you know it.}\n> left: Write a proper letter home\n  mood +small\n  gold -tiny\n  say: {~Four pages about the work, the food and the people. The postage is dear from here. You send it anyway.|You tell them everything, and in telling it you find out how much you like it here.|You owe them a letter, so you write a long one, and the debt feels paid with interest. The postage hurts.}\n> right: A few lines and back to work\n  chance likely:\n    mood +tiny\n    say: {~Short and cheerful. It'll do, and they'll be glad of it.|\"All well, more soon.\" True, mostly.|A few lines to settle what you owe. They'll be glad of it, and you'll owe them a longer one.}\n  else:\n    mood -tiny\n    say: {~You write three lines and feel guilty for a week.|The letter is so brief it reads like a receipt.|You pay what you owe with three lines. It doesn't feel paid.}\n\n# ---------------- More letters (playtest 2, N14): general ones, then two for each romanceable character ----------------\n\n@event letter_reading_list\ntitle: A Reading List\ntrigger: letter\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 2\nweight: 10\nart: book\ntags: letter study\nscene: A long handwritten list of book titles on academy paper, a small inkblot at the bottom where the writer paused.\ntext: A professor has written over the break, as some of them do, with a reading list for next term: eleven titles, three of them underlined, one with \"(if you dare)\" beside it. {~The handwriting slopes like a staircase.|There's a coffee ring on the corner.}\n> left: Start on the list now\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You read the first two by candlelight while everyone else is resting. The third is the one marked \"if you dare\". You don't, yet.|You make real progress and lose two afternoons of holiday to it. Next term will be easier for it.}\n> right: Put it under the bed until term\n  mood +small\n  say: {~The list goes under the bed with your winter boots. The holiday stays a holiday.|You don't read a word, and you don't feel a bit sorry.}\n\n@event letter_parcel_from_home\ntitle: A Parcel From Home\ntrigger: letter\nwhen: not has(plan_home)\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 2\nweight: 10\nart: pouch\ntags: letter home\nscene: A brown paper parcel tied with string, opened to show a squashed cake, a pair of knitted socks and a short letter.\ntext: A parcel from home, squashed by the post: {~a cake that has become a different shape of cake|a tin of biscuits and a jar of jam|knitted socks and a fruit loaf}, and a letter that says, in four different hands, that everyone is well and the cat is not.\n> left: Share it with everyone around you\n  charm +small\n  say: {~You pass it round and it vanishes in minutes. People you barely know thank you by name the next day.|You share it out to the last crumb. Someone gives you a biscuit back, out of pity.|You cut the fruit loaf into as many slices as there are hands. The socks you keep.}\n> right: Keep it for yourself\n  health +small\n  say: {~You eat it slowly, over a week, a slice a night. It tastes like your kitchen table.|You hide it under your bed and ration it. It lasts, and so does the feeling of home.|You keep the loaf for the cold nights and wear the socks to bed. Both last till the end of the month.}\n\n@event letter_wrong_address\ntitle: Not For You\ntrigger: letter\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nweight: 10\nart: letter\ntags: letter\nscene: A sealed envelope addressed in an unfamiliar hand to a name that isn't the reader's, lying on a windowsill beside a stamp.\ntext: A letter arrives that isn't yours: the right house, the wrong name, a perfumed envelope sealed with green wax. It's addressed to someone you've never heard of. The seal is very thin.\n> left: Forward it unopened\n  gold -tiny\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: You write \"not known here, please try again\" on the back, add a stamp of your own and send it on its way. You'll never know what it said, which is how it should be.\n> right: Open it, just to see\n  guile +small\n  alignment -tiny\n  say: It's a love letter, and a bad one, full of rhymes on \"moon\". You reseal it carefully and send it on, feeling worse than you expected.\n\n@event letter_bookshop_bill\ntitle: A Bill You Don't Remember\ntrigger: letter\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nweight: 10\nart: coin\ntags: letter money\nscene: A crisp bill from a bookshop, an itemised list with one title circled, held up beside a student's puzzled face.\ntext: A bill from the village bookshop for a book called \"On Small Explosions\", which you have never seen, never borrowed and never wanted. The bookseller writes that you signed for it. The signature looks a little like yours.\n> left: Pay it and be done\n  gold -small\n  say: You pay it. A week later the book arrives, and it's actually quite good. You still don't know who signed for it.\n> right: Write back and dispute it\n  challenge negotiate apprentice:\n    win:\n      charm +small\n      say: You write a polite, firm letter. The bookseller writes back: wrong student, same name, profuse apologies, and a bookmark by way of sorry.\n    lose:\n      if gold > 0:\n        gold -small\n        mood -tiny\n        say: The bookseller replies with a copy of the signature and a note that she has \"dealt with your sort before\". You pay, and add a stiff note of your own.\n      else:\n        mood -small\n        health -tiny\n        say: The bookseller replies with a copy of the signature and a note that she has \"dealt with your sort before\". Your purse is empty, so you work the bill off, shelving her stock for a long, dusty afternoon.\n\n@event letter_old_friend\ntitle: From Before\ntrigger: letter\nwhen: not has(plan_home)\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nweight: 10\nart: letter\ntags: letter home\nscene: A letter in a familiar childhood hand, with a small drawing of a village street in the margin.\ntext: A letter from someone you grew up with, from before Lumenhold. {~They're apprenticed to a cooper now.|They're getting married in the spring.|They've moved to the city and hate it.} The letter asks what magic is like. It's a good question, and you don't know how to answer it.\n> left: Write back pages and pages\n  mood +small\n  gold -tiny\n  say: You try to explain magic, and the castle, and what it's like to fail an exam in front of a ghost. It takes four pages and an extra stamp. It's the best letter you've written.\n> right: Send a postcard of the towers\n  say: A postcard of the seven towers, and \"It's hard to explain. Come and see?\" You both know they won't. It's a kind postcard all the same.\n\n@event letter_ledger_competition\ntitle: The Holiday Essay Prize\ntrigger: letter\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nweight: 10\nart: paper\ntags: letter study\nscene: A folded copy of a student newspaper with a competition notice circled in red ink.\ntext: The Lumen Ledger's holiday issue has found you: an essay prize, \"What I Did Not Do This Holiday\", five hundred words, the winner printed on the front page and paid in crowns.\n> left: Write an entry\n  challenge research journeyman+ slim:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      gold +small\n      say: Your essay about the lake you didn't swim in wins. It's printed on the front page, a little cut, under your name spelled right for once.\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      say: You write a good essay that doesn't win. The winner wrote about a goat. Everyone agrees the goat was the right choice.\n> right: Let someone else win\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You don't write a word of it, and spend the afternoon doing one of the things you didn't do.\n\n@event letter_from_a_firstyear\ntitle: Advice, Please\ntrigger: letter\nwhen: year >= 2\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nweight: 10\nart: letter\ntags: letter social\nscene: A short anxious letter on cheap paper, several words crossed out, a small blot where the writer pressed too hard.\ntext: A first-year you once showed the way to the library has written to you over the break. They're thinking of leaving. The letter is short, crossed out in places, and ends: \"Did you ever feel like this?\"\n> left: Write back honestly, at length\n  charm +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You tell them about your own worst month, which isn't easy to write. Next term they find you at breakfast and say they stayed because of it.\n> right: Send a few kind lines\n  mood +tiny\n  say: \"Yes. It passes. Come back.\" It's short, and it's true. You hope it's enough. At the start of term you see them in the queue for the barge.\n\n@event letter_registrar_forms\ntitle: Forms in Triplicate\ntrigger: letter\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nweight: 10\nart: scroll\ntags: letter study\nscene: A thick envelope of forms with the academy's seal, three identical copies of everything fanned out on a table.\ntext: The registrar's office has sent forms, in triplicate, about next term: your courses, your board, your \"intentions regarding extracurricular activities\". There is a box marked \"Other (explain)\". It is very small.\n> left: Fill them in properly now\n  lore +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: An afternoon of copying your own name three times. At the start of term, your enrolment takes four minutes while everyone else queues. Smug is a feeling too.\n> right: Leave them until the barge\n  mood +small\n  say: You fill them in on the barge, on your knee, in the wind. One copy goes over the side. The registrar sighs at you all the way through enrolment.\n\n# Two letters each from the romanceable characters: one friendly, one when there's a spark.\n\n@event letter_lyra_drills\ntitle: A List of Drills\ntrigger: letter\nactor: lyra\nwhen: rel(lyra) >= 10\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nweight: 14\nart: letter\ntags: letter social duel\nscene: A letter of numbered duelling drills in crisp handwriting, a scratched-out postscript at the bottom.\ntext: Lyra has written. It's a list of drills, numbered, with a note at the bottom: \"Do these. You're too slow. Everyone is.\" There's a postscript under that, scratched out and rewritten three times and then scratched out for good.\n> left: Do every drill, and send your times\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  rel lyra +tiny\n  say: You do them in the cold every morning and post her your times. Her reply is one word: \"Adequate.\" It's underlined, which from Lyra is a bouquet.\n> right: Ask what the postscript said\n  chance even +1 if rel(lyra) >= 30:\n    rel lyra +small\n    say: Two weeks later, one line: \"It said: write back.\" Then, smaller: \"You did. Good.\"\n  else:\n    rel lyra -tiny\n    say: She ignores the question entirely and sends a second list, longer, with no postscript at all.\n\n@event letter_lyra_book\ntitle: Foundations of Flame\ntrigger: letter\nactor: lyra\nwhen: spark(lyra) >= 20\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nweight: 14\nart: book\ntags: letter romance\nscene: A worn copy of a spellbook unwrapped from brown paper, every margin full of small, fierce annotations in red ink.\ntext: A parcel from Lyra: her own copy of \"Foundations of Flame\", every margin crowded with notes in red ink. On the flyleaf: \"You'll only underline the wrong bits. So I've done it for you.\" The handwriting goes a little uneven at the end.\n> left: Read every note in the margins\n  lore +small\n  spark lyra +tiny\n  say: Her notes are sharp, funny and occasionally rude about the author. On page ninety there's a small flame drawn beside a paragraph, and your initials.\n> right: Annotate it back and return it\n  spark lyra +small\n  gold -tiny\n  say: You add your own notes in green and post it back at your own expense. Her reply says you're wrong about chapter four. She keeps the book, and your notes, and doesn't cross them out.\n\n@event letter_tobias_goats\ntitle: Four Pages of Goats\ntrigger: letter\nactor: tobias\nwhen: rel(tobias) >= 10\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nweight: 14\nart: letter\ntags: letter social\nscene: A long letter in a big, careful hand with a drawing of a goat in the corner and a recipe card tucked into the fold.\ntext: Tobias has written four pages, mostly about goats: one has escaped, one has eaten a hat, and one is, he writes, \"going through something\". Tucked in the fold is a recipe for his gran's barley soup, \"for when the castle food gets you down\".\n> left: Make the soup\n  health +small\n  gold -tiny\n  say: You buy the barley and borrow a pot. The soup is thick and plain and exactly what you needed. You write and tell him so; he writes back that his gran says you're welcome.\n> right: Write back with a story of your own\n  rel tobias +small\n  say: You send him the story of the ghost in the bathtub, at length. His reply says he read it to the goats, and that the one going through something seemed to feel better.\n\n@event letter_tobias_seeds\ntitle: Seeds for a Windowsill\ntrigger: letter\nactor: tobias\nwhen: spark(tobias) >= 20\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nweight: 14\nart: flower\ntags: letter romance nature\nscene: A small paper packet of seeds tied with garden twine, a note in big careful handwriting beside it.\ntext: A packet of seeds from Tobias, tied with garden twine. \"For your windowsill. They'll keep you company. Water them Tuesdays.\" Then, squashed in at the bottom: \"Sorry if that's soppy.\"\n> left: Plant them straight away\n  mood +small\n  spark tobias +tiny\n  say: Three shoots by the end of the month, leaning toward the light. You water them on Tuesdays and think of him on the other days too.\n> right: Write back that it isn't soppy\n  spark tobias +small\n  say: You write that it isn't soppy, and then a whole page about why. His reply is two lines long and you can tell he went red writing it.\n\n@event letter_seraphine_card\ntitle: Half a Sentence\ntrigger: letter\nactor: seraphine\nwhen: rel(seraphine) >= 10\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nweight: 14\nart: cards\ntags: letter social\nscene: A single tarot card in an envelope, with half a sentence written across the back in silver ink.\ntext: An envelope from Seraphine with a single tarot card inside, the Wheel, and half a sentence on the back in silver ink: \"Not this week but the one after, don't take the\"\n> left: Try to work out the rest\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You spend an evening with the card and every book on tarot you can find, and spend the following week avoiding stairs, carts and soup. Nothing happens. Probably.\n> right: Write and ask her to finish it\n  rel seraphine +small\n  say: Her reply comes ten days later, and finishes the sentence: \"…long way round. Or do. It's lovely either way.\" It is, when you take it.\n\n@event letter_seraphine_painting\ntitle: A Painting of You\ntrigger: letter\nactor: seraphine\nwhen: spark(seraphine) >= 20\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nweight: 14\nart: rose\ntags: letter romance\nscene: A small watercolour of a student reading under a tree in blossom, unwrapped from tissue paper on a desk.\ntext: Seraphine has sent a small watercolour: you, reading under a tree in blossom, lit from one side. It is very good. You have never sat under that tree, and it isn't spring.\n> left: Hang it by your bed\n  mood +small\n  spark seraphine +tiny\n  say: It's the first thing you see each morning. It makes you look kinder than you feel, and you start, a little, to feel it.\n> right: Write and ask when she saw this\n  spark seraphine +small\n  say: She writes back, \"Next spring,\" and nothing else, and then a second letter the same day: \"I hope that's all right.\"\n\n@event letter_corvin_bakery\ntitle: The Best Bakery in the Kingdom\ntrigger: letter\nactor: corvin\nwhen: rel(corvin) >= 10\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nweight: 14\nart: map\ntags: letter social\nscene: A hand-drawn map of an unfamiliar town with a cross in ink over one corner, a postscript crammed into the margin.\ntext: A letter from Corvin, with a map of a town you've never heard of and a cross on one corner. \"Found the best bakery in the kingdom. Can't afford it. Thought you should know where it is, saint, in case you're ever richer than me. Low bar.\"\n> left: Send him a few crowns for a pastry\n  gold -small\n  rel corvin +small\n  say: You fold a few crowns into the reply. His next letter is mostly about the pastry, in loving detail, and then, at the very end and without a joke: \"Thank you.\"\n> right: Write back a map of your own\n  guile +small\n  say: You draw him a map of every place you've found that he hasn't, with crosses and a key. He writes back that two of them are wrong and one of them is brilliant.\n\n@event letter_corvin_no_jokes\ntitle: A Letter With No Jokes\ntrigger: letter\nactor: corvin\nwhen: spark(corvin) >= 20\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nweight: 14\nart: letter\ntags: letter romance\nscene: A single short page in quick handwriting, with no doodles and no crossings-out, folded very small.\ntext: Corvin's letter has no jokes in it. You read it twice to be sure. It says the place is too quiet, that he keeps turning to say something to you, and that he isn't used to that. It isn't signed \"your favourite thief\". It's just signed.\n> left: Write back just as plainly\n  spark corvin +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You write back with no jokes either, and it's harder than you thought. His reply comes by return of post, and the first line is a joke, and the second line isn't.\n> right: Send a joke to make it easier\n  rel corvin +small\n  spark corvin -tiny\n  say: You send him the worst joke you know. He replies with a worse one, and you're both on safer ground. You wonder, later, if you missed something.\n\n@event letter_mir_blueprint\ntitle: A Letter That Folds Itself\ntrigger: letter\nactor: mir\nwhen: rel(mir) >= 10\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nweight: 14\nart: gear\ntags: letter social magic\nscene: A letter folding itself back up on a desk like a paper bird, a blueprint with arrows and exclamation marks spread out underneath.\ntext: Mir's letter starts folding itself back up while you're still reading it. Inside is a blueprint for a self-folding letter (\"PROBABLY works!!\") and a page of excited tangents about hinges.\n> left: Build one yourself\n  arcana +small\n  gold -tiny\n  say: Wire, wax paper and one small enchantment. Your letter folds itself into a perfect swan and then, unprompted, into a slightly worse frog. Mir's reply is all exclamation marks.\n> right: Write back with improvements\n  rel mir +small\n  say: You suggest a stiffer hinge and a slower fold. Mir writes back that you are a genius and that they've already tried it and it exploded, but in a promising way.\n\n@event letter_mir_ticking\ntitle: Something That Ticks\ntrigger: letter\nactor: mir\nwhen: spark(mir) >= 20\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nweight: 14\nart: gear\ntags: letter romance\nscene: A tiny brass mechanism the size of a walnut ticking softly on a pillow, a note in hurried handwriting beside it.\ntext: A small brass thing the size of a walnut, ticking softly, with a note in Mir's hurried hand: \"It's a metronome. Probably. It's set to a resting heart rate. Mine, actually, it took ages. Anyway!!\"\n> left: Keep it on your pillow\n  mood +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: It ticks you to sleep every night, slightly faster than a resting heart rate. You don't mention that. Some nights it keeps you awake.\n> right: Write back how much you like it\n  spark mir +small\n  say: You write that you like it very much. Mir's reply is three pages about escapements, and then at the very end, very small: \"good. i'm glad. good.\"\n\n@event letter_kael_three_lines\ntitle: Three Lines\ntrigger: letter\nactor: kael\nwhen: rel(kael) >= 10\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nweight: 14\nart: snow\ntags: letter social\nscene: A sheet of good paper with only three short lines on it in careful handwriting, a great deal of white space below.\ntext: A letter from Kael. Three lines, in careful handwriting, with a great deal of white paper below them: the weather, a star he saw, and \"I hope you are well. That is the whole letter.\"\n> left: Write back three lines\n  rel kael +small\n  say: You write back three lines: your weather, a star of your own, and that you're well. His reply comes promptly. It has four lines. You count them twice.\n> right: Write back three pages\n  charm +small\n  rel kael +tiny\n  gold -tiny\n  say: You write three pages and pay the extra postage. His reply takes a long time and says only: \"Thank you. I read it four times. I do not know how to answer so much.\" He keeps it, though.\n\n@event letter_kael_star_chart\ntitle: The Sky on Your Birthday\ntrigger: letter\nactor: kael\nwhen: spark(kael) >= 20\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nweight: 14\nart: star\ntags: letter romance\nscene: A hand-inked star chart unfolded on a bed, one small cluster of stars circled in pencil, a short note pinned to the corner.\ntext: Kael has sent a star chart, inked by hand, with one cluster circled in pencil. The note says: \"The sky on the night you were born, from here. I checked the tables twice. It is a good sky.\"\n> left: Find those stars yourself tonight\n  lore +small\n  health -tiny\n  spark kael +tiny\n  say: You stand outside in the cold until you find them, low over the roofs. Your feet go numb. It is a good sky; he was right.\n> right: Thank him plainly\n  spark kael +small\n  say: You write that it's the kindest thing anyone has sent you. His reply says \"You are welcome.\" Below that, smaller, as if added later: \"Very.\"\n\n@event letter_nessa_deadline\ntitle: URGENT\ntrigger: letter\nactor: nessa\nwhen: rel(nessa) >= 10\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nweight: 14\nart: paper\ntags: letter social\nscene: A letter with URGENT written across the top in capitals and underlined three times, a deadline circled at the bottom.\ntext: A letter from Nessa, URGENT underlined three times: the Ledger needs three hundred words on holidays for the first issue of term, \"interesting ones please, the last six were about weather\". Deadline circled. Twice.\n> left: Write the piece\n  renown +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You spend an evening on three hundred words and post them. Nessa cuts eighty, prints the rest on page four and writes back \"Good. Rewrite the ending next time.\"\n> right: Write back off the record\n  rel nessa +small\n  say: You write back with no piece at all, just how she is. Her reply has no capitals in it. It's the longest thing she's ever written that she isn't going to print.\n\n@event letter_nessa_off_record\ntitle: Off the Record\ntrigger: letter\nactor: nessa\nwhen: spark(nessa) >= 20\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nweight: 14\nart: letter\ntags: letter romance\nscene: A letter with OFF THE RECORD written at the top and then, for once, a page of short, unhurried sentences.\ntext: Nessa's letter has OFF THE RECORD at the top, and then, for once, short sentences with nothing to sell. She's tired. The holidays are long. She keeps writing headlines about you in her head and crossing them out.\n> left: Reply off the record too\n  spark nessa +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You write back slowly and honestly, which costs you more than you expected. Her answer is posted the day your letter must have reached her, which means she wrote it the moment she read yours.\n> right: Ask to see the headlines\n  charm +small\n  spark nessa +tiny\n  say: She sends three. One is unprintable, one is very sweet and one is \"LOCAL STUDENT STILL NOT REPLYING\", which is fair. You reply at once.\n"},{"path":"content/events/pools/money.grim","text":"# Money outside jobs (playtest 2, X14; D4/D7): financial aid, the bursar's loan, favours for the faculty and\n# upper-years, and the pawnbroker. Hinted at low gold, and sooner for those with no family money; never obligations.\n# The illicit routes (company, the ring, the high table, Grisk's parcels) are the night's (pools/night.grim).\n\n@event aid_notice\ntitle: The Hardship Notice\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: term and turn > 2 and not has(hardship_grant) and not has(bursar_loan) and since(aid_interview) > 10 and (gold < 6 or (gold < 15 and (has(origin_hedge) or has(origin_street) or has(origin_scholar))))\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 14\nart: scales\ntags: money\nscene: A notice pinned beside the bursar's office door, half covered by a newer notice about lost owls, a queue of three students pretending not to queue.\ntext: {A small notice by the bursar's door reads \"Hardship fund. Applications in writing. All circumstances considered.\"|The hardship notice again, under a new one about lost owls.|Someone has underlined \"all circumstances\" on the hardship notice.} {Three students are pretending not to queue.|The ink on the forms is still wet.|The bursar's door is ajar.}\n> left: Fill in the forms\n  mood -tiny\n  queue aid_interview in 1\n  say: {Four pages. One asks you to list everything you own. It doesn't take long, and that is the hard part.|You fill in the forms at a library table with your arm around them so nobody can see.|The bursar's door is open, so you hand the forms in yourself. \"All circumstances,\" the bursar says, reading. \"Yes.\"}\n> right: Not yet\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {You walk past. Your pride is intact, and so is your empty purse.|Another month, you tell yourself. You have a button left.|You read \"all circumstances\" twice and walk past the open door. Not yet.}\n\n@event aid_interview\ntitle: The Bursar Will See You\ntrigger: queued\ndefer: off_campus\nclass: incident\ntone: tense\nart: scales\ntags: money\nscene: A small cluttered office, a grey-haired bursar behind a ledger as large as a tombstone, a student sitting very upright on a hard chair.\ntext: The bursar has your forms open under her hand and a ledger the size of a tombstone at her elbow. \"The fund is small,\" she says, \"and the hands are many. Tell me why it should be yours. Or, if you'd rather not beg, I can lend you sixty crowns against your future, at no interest.\"\n> left: Make your case\n  challenge negotiate journeyman:\n    win:\n      gain hardship_grant\n      mood -tiny\n      say: You tell her plainly, and she listens without pity, which helps. \"One term,\" she says, and stamps it. \"Come back if it's still hard.\" Walking out, you feel both lighter and smaller.\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: She hears you out, and then reads you the list of people ahead of you. Some of their circumstances are worse. \"Next year,\" she says, kindly. You almost wish she hadn't been kind.\n> right: Take the loan\n  gold +huge\n  gain bursar_loan\n  loan_paid = 0\n  say: Sixty crowns, counted out in old coins, and your name in the tombstone ledger. \"Eight a month, when you can, and a little less at the end,\" she says. \"I'm a patient woman. The ledger is more patient still.\" It sits on your shoulder from then on.\n\n@event favour_vey_letter\ntitle: A Discreet Errand\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nactor: vey\nwhen: met(vey) and gold < 25 and term\nweight: 10\nart: letter\ntags: money social\nscene: A professor in a violet coat holding out a sealed letter and a silver coin between two gloved fingers, in an empty theatre corridor.\ntext: Professor Vey stops you in an empty corridor with a sealed letter and a silver coin. \"A letter for the village. The house with the blue shutters. Into the hand of whoever opens the door, and not a word.\" Her smile is magnificent and gives nothing away.\n> left: Deliver it, unread\n  meet vey\n  gold +small\n  rel vey +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: The house with the blue shutters is at the far end of the village, in the rain. An old man opens the door, reads the name on the letter and starts to cry. You walk back soaked and never ask.\n> right: Hold it up to the light first\n  meet vey\n  gold +small\n  guile +tiny\n  alignment -tiny\n  rel vey -small\n  say: You make out one line: \"...I have never stopped...\" Then you deliver it. The next day Vey says, without looking at you, \"The light in that corridor is very good, isn't it?\"\n\n@event favour_thorne_marking\ntitle: Forty Essays\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nactor: thorne\nwhen: met(thorne) and gold < 25 and year >= 2 and term\nweight: 10\nart: book\ntags: money study\nscene: A stack of forty first-year essays on a desk under a lamp, a red pen, a student with an expression of growing despair.\ntext: Professor Thorne puts forty first-year essays on your desk. \"Mark them. A crown for every five. Be honest. Honesty is a kindness they will not thank you for.\" He leaves. The first essay begins, \"Fire is hot.\"\n> left: Mark them honestly\n  meet thorne\n  gold +small\n  lore +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: Forty essays, eleven of them good, one brilliant and the rest about how fire is hot. Thorne reads your marks and says \"Hm.\" You've learned that \"Hm\" is pay too.\n> right: Mark them kindly\n  meet thorne\n  gold +small\n  mood +tiny\n  rel thorne -small\n  say: You give every first-year a word of encouragement. Thorne raises your marks on three and lowers them on twenty. \"Kindness,\" he says, \"is not the same as a pass.\" He pays you anyway.\n\n@event favour_amaranth_shift\ntitle: The Night Shift\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nactor: amaranth\nwhen: met(amaranth) and gold < 25 and term\nweight: 10\nart: cross\ntags: money\nscene: A dim infirmary ward at night, a student in an apron carrying a basin between rows of beds, a stern healer writing by a single lamp.\ntext: Sister Amaranth needs a pair of hands for the infirmary's night shift. \"Basins, bandages, and sitting with whoever can't sleep. Eight crowns. Don't faint. If you faint, you're a patient, and patients don't get paid.\"\n> left: Take the shift\n  meet amaranth\n  gold +small\n  alchemy +tiny\n  health -small\n  say: A first-year with a fever, a prefect with a broken wrist, a boy who just wants someone awake. By dawn you're grey with tiredness. Amaranth says, \"Keep doing that,\" and pays you.\n> right: Offer to roll bandages by day instead\n  meet amaranth\n  gold +tiny\n  rel amaranth +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: She looks at you over her spectacles and hands you a mountain of linen. \"Bandages it is. Less pay, all of the tedium.\" By the end you could roll them in your sleep.\n\n@event upper_year_errands\ntitle: Errands for Upper-Years\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: term and year <= 2 and gold < 30\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 8\nart: pouch\ntags: money social\nscene: A crowded common room, a lounging upper-year holding out a coin and a list, a younger student taking the list with a sigh.\ntext: {Upper-years pay for errands, a book fetched or a queue stood in or a letter carried to the owlery.|An upper-year waves a list and a coin at you. Word has got round.|\"You again,\" says the upper-year, pleased. \"Good. The usual, plus my laundry.\"} {The list is long.|The coin is small.|One item on the list just says \"find my cat\".}\n> left: Run the errands\n  gold +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {You run up and down the towers all evening and are paid in coins and condescension.|You fetch, carry and queue. Your legs are tired; your purse is less light.|The usual, plus the laundry, plus the cat, which is on the owlery roof. You're paid for two of the three.}\n> right: Name your price\n  challenge negotiate apprentice+:\n    win:\n      gold +medium\n      say: {\"More, or find your own cat.\" They pay. You find the cat anyway; it was in the laundry.|You haggle like a merchant's child. The upper-year pays with grudging respect.|\"The laundry's extra,\" you say. They pay it, and add the cat for free. You find it in the library.}\n    lose:\n      renown -tiny\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {\"Never mind,\" says the upper-year, and gives the list to a first-year behind you. Word gets round of that too.|They laugh, and find someone cheaper. You keep your dignity and nothing else.|\"The laundry's extra,\" you say. The upper-year gives the list to someone who doesn't think so.}\n\n@event pawnbroker_window\ntitle: The Pawnbroker's Window\nplace: village\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: gold < 10\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 12\nart: scales\ntags: money\nscene: A dusty village shop window crowded with pawned treasures, wands, rings and a stuffed owl, a student looking in with their hands in empty pockets.\ntext: {The pawnbroker's window is full of other people's treasures, wands and rings and a stuffed owl. The sign says \"Fair prices. Redemption within the year.\"|{flag(pawned_something): The pawnbroker's window again. Something of yours is in it now.|The pawnbroker's window again, the same treasures in new places.}|The pawnbroker nods at you through the glass like an acquaintance.} {A first-year's silver locket is in the front row.|There's a familiar's collar, empty.|The stuffed owl seems to judge you.}\n> left: Pawn something you'd miss\n  flag pawned_something\n  gold +medium\n  mood -small\n  say: {Your good boots, or your grandmother's ring, or the wand-case with your name burned in it. The money is fair; the ticket says \"within the year\". You put the ticket somewhere safe and try not to look at it.|You hand over something you love, and take the money, and walk home faster than usual.|The pawnbroker greets you by name and writes the ticket without asking what it is. The owl watches the whole transaction.}\n> right: Walk on\n  say: {You walk on with your hands in your pockets. They're empty, but they're yours.|Not today. The owl watches you go.|The pawnbroker nods through the glass; you nod back, and walk on. The owl looks unconvinced.}\n"},{"path":"content/events/pools/night.grim","text":"# The midnight pools (playtest 2, X13): what the six open places hold after curfew, and who is about. Most of the\n# castle is sealed; what is open feels shady, and nights are riskier than days: a risky outcome queues\n# `night_suspicion` at its own odds (M41). Illicit money (the ring, the high table, the stores, Grisk's parcels,\n# stolen pages) is a third way to pay and pulls alignment toward the Hollow; hosting upstairs is legal, only risky. Harmless nights exist too.\n\n# ---------------- The Gilded Newt, upstairs ----------------\n\n@event newt_company\ntitle: Company for the Evening\nplace: gilded_newt\nslot: midnight\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: cup\ntags: money danger\nscene: An upstairs room at a tavern after hours, card tables under low lamps, well-dressed guests laughing, a student in borrowed finery refilling a glass.\ntext: {The woman who runs the upstairs room looks you over. \"The guests like good company. Laugh at the jokes, keep the glasses full, lose gracefully at cards. Nothing more. Guests who want more are shown the stair.\"|The upstairs room again: velvet, lamplight, guests from the capital who tip in silver.|You know the room now, and which guests to sit beside.} {Tonight it's a merchant's birthday.|Tonight, a card party for a visiting magistrate.|Tonight the guests are masked, for no reason anyone gives.}\n> left: Work the evening\n  challenge negotiate journeyman:\n    win:\n      gold +medium\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {You laugh in all the right places and lose at cards exactly as much as you're meant to. The tips are heavy, and so is your head on the way home.|A guest tells you the story of their life. You listen as if it mattered. Perhaps it did. The tip says it did.|Behind the masks the guests are easier, and so are you. You sit beside the right ones and come home with heavy pockets and no names.}\n    lose:\n      gold +tiny\n      mood -small\n      queue night_suspicion chance unlikely\n      say: {A guest's hand wanders and doesn't take the hint. You leave by the back stair with a few coins and a cold feeling that lasts the week.|You say the wrong thing to the wrong magistrate, and are paid off early and shown the stair. Someone from the castle is drinking at the bar below.|A masked guest won't take no for an answer, and nobody can see who it is. You leave by the back stair with a few coins and a very bad feeling.}\n  if bonds(partner) > 0:\n    queue night_partner_asks chance unlikely\n> right: Not tonight\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You turn the work down and walk home past the lit windows, a little poorer and quite sure of yourself.|\"Another night, then,\" says the woman, writing something in a small book. You wonder what.|You turn it down. The woman nods as if she expected it, and puts a small mark by your name in her book.}\n\n@event newt_high_table\ntitle: The High Table\nplace: gilded_newt\nslot: midnight\ntype: menu\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 4\nwhen: not flag(grisk_gone)\nweight: 9\nart: cards\ntags: money danger\nscene: A round table in a back room under a single green lamp, piles of coins, a goblin in a velvet waistcoat keeping the bank.\ntext: {Behind the upstairs room there's a back room, and in it the high table. Grisk keeps the bank. Nobody here plays for crowns alone.|The high table again. Grisk nods to you as if you were a regular. You are becoming one.|Grisk has kept your seat warm, he says. It is not a comforting thought.} {A first-year's familiar sits in a cage beside the pot.|Someone has staked a silver wand and is sweating about it.|Somebody's signed promise is in the pot, folded small.}\n> purse: Stake your purse\n  hint: Guile against the table.\n  meet grisk\n  alignment -tiny\n  gold -medium\n  challenge deceive adept unlikely wild:\n    win:\n      gold +large\n      gold +medium\n      mood +small\n      say: {You read the table like a book you've already finished. The pot slides over, and Grisk applauds politely with two fingers.|Your nerve holds and theirs doesn't. You walk home heavy with silver and hollow with nerves.|Somebody's folded promise is in the pot. You win it, and give it back to the owner unread. Grisk thinks that's hilarious.}\n    lose:\n      queue night_suspicion chance even\n      say: {The cards turn on you, then the room does. You leave lighter, and a prefect's lamp is going round the courtyard.|You lose the pot and your temper with it. Grisk sympathises, with interest.|You lose, and Grisk offers to take a signed promise instead of the coin you're short. You pay in coin, all of it.}\n> favour: Stake a favour to Grisk\n  hint: Win big, or owe him a job.\n  when: not flag(owes_grisk)\n  meet grisk\n  alignment -small\n  challenge deceive adept- unlikely wild:\n    win:\n      gold +large\n      say: You stake \"one favour, to be named\" and win. Grisk smiles, tears up the promise, and keeps the pieces. \"Next time,\" he says.\n    lose:\n      flag owes_grisk\n      mood -small\n      queue grisk_names_favour in 1\n      say: You lose, and Grisk folds your promise into his waistcoat pocket like a love letter. \"I'll be in touch,\" he says. \"Soon.\"\n> health: Play the bone-dice\n  hint: The old northern game. Win or bleed.\n  alignment -tiny\n  challenge combat adept- wild:\n    win:\n      gold +medium\n      renown +tiny\n      say: The bone-dice game is played with a knife between the fingers. You keep all ten and win the pot. Grisk calls you \"dear heart\" for the rest of the night.\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      gold +small\n      say: You win the pot and lose a fair amount of skin from your left hand. Amaranth, in the morning, asks no questions, very loudly.\n> leave: Watch one hand, then go\n  say: {You watch a student lose a familiar on a bluff, and go home and hug yours, or your pillow.|You watch, learn and leave. Grisk raises his glass to your back.|You watch a signed promise change hands across the table, and decide never to sign anything in this room.}\n\n@event night_partner_asks\ntitle: Where Were You?\ntrigger: queued\ndefer: off_campus\nclass: incident\ntone: tense\nart: heart\ntags: romance\ncast:\n  partner: any where bond(partner) == \"partner\" and around(partner)\nscene: Two students on a cold stone stair in the morning, one holding a tavern's silver token found in the other's coat.\ntext: {partner} is waiting on the stair with a silver token from the Gilded Newt in {their} palm. It fell out of your coat. \"The upstairs room,\" {they} say{s}. \"People talk. I'd rather hear it from you.\"\n> left: Tell {them} everything\n  spark partner -tiny\n  rel partner +small\n  mood -small\n  say: You tell the truth: the glasses, the cards, the guests, the stair. {partner} is quiet a long time. \"Thank you for telling me,\" {they} say{s} at last, and means it, and it still costs you both.\n> right: \"It's nothing. A card game.\"\n  spark partner -small\n  alignment -tiny\n  say: {partner} looks at the token, then at you, and puts it in your hand. \"All right,\" {they} say{s}. It isn't. For a week {they} sit{s} a little further away at supper.\n\n@event grisk_names_favour\ntitle: The Favour, Named\ntrigger: queued\ndefer: off_campus\nwhen: not flag(grisk_gone)\nclass: incident\ntone: tense\nactor: grisk\nart: chain\ntags: money danger\nscene: A goblin in a velvet waistcoat waiting in a library alcove, a folded promise held up between two fingers.\ntext: Grisk is waiting in the library alcove with your promise between two fingers. \"The favour,\" he says. \"A small one. A crate goes from the boathouse to the village. Tonight. Or, if you prefer, the price of the crate.\" He smiles. \"I always offer a choice.\"\n> left: Move the crate\n  unflag owes_grisk\n  flag grisk_contact\n  find lead_grisk_jetty\n  alignment -small\n  challenge deceive vs bram.guile +1:\n    win:\n      say: The crate clinks and, faintly, sings. You don't look inside. Grisk tears up your promise. \"You're good at this,\" he says, which is the worst thing he could say.\n    lose:\n      gain watched\n      mood -small\n      say: Bram stops you on the jetty and lifts the tarp. Charm-wine. The crate is gone, the favour counts as paid, and Bram has your name.\n> right: Pay him off\n  unflag owes_grisk\n  gold -large\n  mood -small\n  say: You count out the crowns and Grisk gives you back the promise with a small bow. It feels like buying your own name back. It is, more or less.\n\n# ---------------- The boathouse ----------------\n\n@event ring_on_yourself\ntitle: Bet on Yourself\nplace: boathouse\nslot: midnight\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 9\nart: sword\ntags: danger money\nscene: A lantern-lit cellar under a boathouse, a chalk ring on wet flagstones, a bookmaker holding up a slate of odds, a crowd in scarves.\ntext: {The ring under the boathouse has a bookmaker now, with a slate. \"Fancy yourself?\" he says. \"Put your money where your fists are.\"|The bookmaker knows your name and your odds, and they've shortened.|Your name is on the slate tonight before you've said you'll fight.} {The water slaps under the floor.|Somebody is collecting teeth in a jar, as a joke, probably.|The crowd is bigger than last time.}\n> left: Bet your purse on yourself\n  alignment -tiny\n  gold -medium\n  challenge combat adept-:\n    win:\n      gold +large\n      gold +medium\n      renown +tiny\n      health -small\n      say: {You win, bloodied and grinning, and the bookmaker pays out through his teeth.|The crowd roars. You collect your bet and your opponent's grudging handshake, and limp home rich.|Your name was on the slate before you agreed, and the crowd is bigger for it. You win, and the bookmaker pays you in front of all of them.}\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      queue night_suspicion chance even\n      say: {You go down in the third exchange, and your purse goes with you. On the way home a light is on in the prefects' corridor.|A bad loss: the bet, a tooth's worth of pride and most of your skin.|The bigger crowd came to see you lose, it turns out. You oblige, and your purse goes with your pride.}\n  if met(lyra):\n    queue lyra_ring_word chance unlikely\n> right: Fight for the purse only\n  challenge combat adept-:\n    win:\n      gold +small\n      health -small\n      say: {A clean win for a small purse. Nobody's rich, nobody's hurt much.|You win, bow to your opponent and leave before the bookmaker can talk you into more.|You fight for the purse alone and win it in front of the biggest crowd yet. The bookmaker sighs at the money he didn't make on you.}\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      say: {You lose, bow, and spend the walk home finding new bruises.|A loss, and the loser's purse, which is nothing at all.|You lose in front of the biggest crowd yet. At least it was only the purse.}\n\n@event lyra_ring_word\ntitle: Lyra Has Heard\ntrigger: queued\ndefer: off_campus or not around(lyra)\nclass: incident\ntone: tense\nactor: lyra\nart: sword\ntags: social\nscene: A proud red-haired student blocking a corridor with folded arms, a duelling glove tucked in her belt.\ntext: Lyra blocks the corridor. \"The ring under the boathouse.\" She says it as if it were a smell. \"Brawling for coins in a cellar, in front of drunks. You can actually duel. I've seen you. This is beneath you.\" A pause. \"I'd hate to lose to someone who does it.\"\n> left: \"Then spar with me instead.\"\n  rel lyra +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: She stares, then almost smiles. \"Tomorrow, the Duelling Hall, six sharp.\" She beats you, and then shows you why, which from Lyra is close to an apology for the corridor.\n> right: \"It pays. You wouldn't know.\"\n  rel lyra -medium\n  gold +tiny\n  say: Her face shuts like a door. \"No,\" she says. \"I wouldn't.\" She walks off. Later the Newt's landlady hands back the deposit you left on your bar tab: Lyra has paid it off. That is somehow worse.\n\n@event grisk_pickup\ntitle: A Parcel at the Boathouse\nplace: boathouse\nslot: midnight\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 12\nactor: grisk\nwhen: flag(grisk_contact) and not flag(grisk_parcel) and not flag(grisk_gone)\nart: pouch\ntags: money danger\nscene: A dark boathouse jetty at midnight, a small goblin holding out a parcel wrapped in oilcloth, the lake black behind him.\ntext: {Grisk is waiting on the jetty, a parcel in oilcloth under his arm. \"Into the village by tomorrow afternoon. A cobbler's back door. Don't open it, don't drop it, don't be seen.\"|The jetty, the oilcloth, Grisk's teeth in the dark. \"Same arrangement,\" he says.|Grisk again, and a heavier parcel than last time.} {It ticks faintly.|It's cold enough to frost your gloves.|It smells of oranges and something under the oranges.}\n> left: Take the parcel\n  meet grisk\n  alignment -tiny\n  flag grisk_parcel\n  queue grisk_delivery in 1\n  say: {You take it. It is heavier than it looks, and the walk back to your room is very long.|You hide it under your bed. You don't sleep well with it there.|You take the heavier parcel. It smells of oranges, and under the oranges of something you decide not to name.}\n> right: Not this month\n  meet grisk\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {\"Next month, then,\" says Grisk, as if it were already arranged. He tucks the parcel away. You feel watched all the way back up the lawn.|Grisk shrugs. \"There's always someone.\" You don't like how true it sounds.|You turn down the heavier parcel. Grisk weighs it in his hand, then in his mind. \"Your loss,\" he says. \"Or not.\"}\n\n@event grisk_delivery\ntitle: The Cobbler's Back Door\ntrigger: queued\nslot: afternoon\ndefer: off_campus\nclass: incident\ntone: tense\nart: pouch\ntags: money danger\nscene: A narrow village lane in daylight, a student with an oilcloth parcel under one arm walking past a prefect who is buying apples.\ntext: {~You carry Grisk's parcel down to the village in broad daylight.|Grisk's parcel, the village lane, and too many people about.|The parcel is in your satchel and the satchel is suddenly the heaviest thing you own.} Bram is at the apple stall. The cobbler's back door is past him.\n> left: Walk past as if you belong\n  unflag grisk_parcel\n  challenge deceive vs bram.guile +1:\n    win:\n      gold +large\n      alignment -tiny\n      say: {~You nod to Bram, buy an apple, and knock twice at the cobbler's door. A hand takes the parcel; another gives you a purse. Your heart takes an hour to slow down.|The door opens before you knock. Coins, a nod, done. You eat the apple on the way home and taste none of it.|The satchel feels heavier with every step past Bram. He waves. You wave. The cobbler pays, and your hands shake until supper.}\n    lose:\n      gain watched\n      flag grisk_angry\n      mood -small\n      queue grisk_cross in 1\n      say: {~Bram calls your name. You drop the parcel in a horse trough and walk on, very calmly. Bram follows you halfway home.|You panic at the corner and leave the parcel behind a water butt. When you go back, it's gone, and Bram is writing in his notebook.|Bram asks to see inside your satchel, kindly. You show him your books, and he never looks in the other pocket. You walk home sick.}\n> right: Leave it at the door and run\n  unflag grisk_parcel\n  gold +small\n  say: {~You go the long way round, drop it on the cobbler's step and run. The cobbler pays you, short, for the lack of manners.|You don't knock, and you don't wait. Short pay, slipped under your door that night.|You drop the satchel's weight on the cobbler's step and run. He pays you short, and you're glad to be rid of it.}\n\n@event grisk_cross\ntitle: Grisk Is Not Pleased\ntrigger: queued\nclass: incident\ntone: grim\nactor: grisk\nart: chain\ntags: money danger\nscene: A goblin in a velvet waistcoat sitting on a student's bed uninvited, a ledger open on his knee, the dormitory window open behind him.\ntext: Grisk is sitting on your bed. You didn't leave the window open. \"A parcel was lost,\" he says pleasantly, turning a page in his ledger. \"Lost things have a price. I've written it down. Would you like to see?\"\n> left: Put it on your account\n  meet grisk\n  unflag grisk_angry\n  if has(in_debt):\n    debt_paid = debt_paid - 2\n    mood -small\n    say: \"Two more months,\" he says, and writes it neatly. \"Such a good customer.\" He leaves by the window. You don't sleep with it open again.\n  else:\n    gain in_debt\n    debt_paid = 0\n    debt_missed = 0\n    mood -small\n    say: He writes your name in the ledger under a new line: one parcel, six payments. \"Twelve a month,\" he says, \"as usual.\" He leaves by the window. You don't sleep with it open again.\n> right: Tell him you're done\n  meet grisk\n  unflag grisk_angry grisk_contact\n  flag grisk_gone\n  health -small\n  renown -tiny\n  say: \"Everyone is, eventually,\" Grisk says. On your way to breakfast, the stair you always take collapses three steps under you. Nobody saw. Everybody heard. You never see him again.\n\n# ---------------- The refectory kitchens ----------------\n\n@event kitchen_stores\ntitle: The Stores\nplace: refectory\nslot: midnight\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 9\nart: meal\ntags: money danger\nscene: A dark storeroom behind a castle kitchen, sacks of sugar and crates of oranges stacked high, a student with an empty satchel.\ntext: {Behind the kitchens is the storeroom, full of sugar, spices and oranges from the south. The village pays well for oranges in winter, and nobody counts the crates.|The storeroom again. You know which crate is at the back and never counted.|The stores have a new padlock, which is mostly decorative.} {A mouse watches you without judgement.|The kitchen cat does judge you.|Somewhere a pot is still dripping.}\n> left: Fill a satchel to sell\n  alignment -small\n  gold +small\n  queue night_suspicion chance even\n  say: {Oranges, a cone of sugar, a jar of saffron. The village grocer asks no questions and pays in coppers. Somebody in the castle will be short of saffron.|A full satchel and an empty conscience, or nearly. On the way out, the scullery door bangs behind you.|The new padlock opens with a hairpin. You fill the satchel, and the grocer pays, and you wonder who'll be blamed for the lock.}\n> right: Take one orange and go\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {You eat it in the dark by the banked fire. It's the best orange you have ever eaten, and you know exactly why.|One orange. You leave the peel in the fire so nobody finds it.|You take one orange past the new padlock and feel worse about it than about all the crates.}\n\n@event kitchen_night_baker\ntitle: The Night Baker\nplace: refectory\nslot: midnight\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 9\nart: meal\ntags: nature\nscene: A warm castle bakehouse at midnight, a flour-dusted old baker kneading dough by the light of an open oven, a student leaning in the doorway.\ntext: {The bakehouse behind the kitchens is lit. The night baker, flour to the elbows, looks up. \"Hungry or helpful?\" she says. \"Both is allowed.\"|The night baker waves you in without looking up. \"Dough's waiting.\"|The bakehouse smells of tomorrow's bread. The baker has saved you a heel of yesterday's.} {The oven sighs.|A tray of rolls is cooling by the door.|She is humming something old.}\n> left: Knead until the oven's hot\n  lose hungry\n  health -tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: {You knead until your arms shake. She gives you the first roll out of the oven and no lecture at all.|An hour of flour and warmth, and a hot roll for your trouble. You go back to bed with your hands smelling of bread, and sleep like a stone.|You knead while she hums something old. When the loaves go in, she toasts yesterday's heel for you, and you eat it on the step.}\n> right: Just sit by the oven\n  lose hungry\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {You sit by the open oven with your knees pulled up. She works, talks to the dough, and lets you be.|You sit by the oven with a roll from the cooling tray while she works. She doesn't ask why you're up. You don't ask why she is.|You eat the heel of bread and warm your feet. Upstairs, the castle is cold; down here, it never is.}\n\n# ---------------- The Restricted Section ----------------\n\n@event restricted_page_buyer\ntitle: A Buyer for Old Pages\nplace: restricted_section\nslot: midnight\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 8\nwhen: met(grisk)\nart: book\ntags: money danger mystery\nscene: A dim Restricted Section aisle at midnight, a student holding a razor over an illuminated page, a black book watching from the shelf above.\ntext: {Grisk mentioned, once, that a collector pays for illuminated pages \"of a certain age\". The Restricted Section is full of them.|{flag(sold_page): There's a gap on the shelf where the last page you sold came from.|The illuminated book you read last time is back on its shelf, untouched.}|The illuminated books lean away from you as you pass.} {A gold-leaf dragon glints on one page.|One margin is full of tiny stars, all black.|A page here is worth a month's board.}\n> left: Cut a page\n  flag sold_page\n  alignment -small\n  gold +medium\n  queue night_suspicion chance even\n  say: {One clean cut. The book doesn't cry out, which is somehow worse. The collector pays well, and never asks where.|You sell the page and keep the guilt, which no one will buy.|The books lean away, and you cut a page anyway. A month's board, in one stroke. You don't come back to this aisle for a long time.}\n> right: Read it instead\n  lore +small\n  mana -tiny\n  say: {You read the illuminated page by moonlight. It's a recipe for honey cakes, in exquisite gold leaf, and the gilding draws at your mana the whole time you read. You leave it where it is.|The page is a list of stars, by name, and each name pulls a little magic out of you as you read it. You learn three, and put the book back.|The books lean away from you, then back, as you read instead of cut. The page is a map of the castle as it was. It costs you a little magic to look at it.}\n\n# ---------------- The observatory roof ----------------\n\n@event midnight_roof_partner\ntitle: The Roof, Together\nplace: observatory\nslot: midnight\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\ncast:\n  partner: any where bond(partner) == \"partner\" and around(partner)\nart: star\ntags: romance nature\nscene: Two students lying side by side on an observatory roof at midnight under a sky full of stars, one blanket between them.\ntext: {You've brought {partner} up to the observatory roof, with a stolen blanket and two cups of cocoa going cold.|{partner} knows the way up now, and goes first.|{partner} has brought the blanket this time.} {The stars are very close.|A meteor, then another.|The castle below is all asleep.}\n> left: Stay until the sky turns\n  spark partner +small\n  health -small\n  queue night_suspicion chance unlikely\n  say: {You name the stars between you, wrong, and argue about it, and stay until the sky goes grey. The stair down is lit by a prefect's lamp you just avoid.|You fall asleep up there and wake to the first bell, frozen and happy.|{partner} wraps you both in the blanket {they} brought and talks you to sleep. The castle wakes up around you.}\n> right: An hour, then back\n  rel partner +small\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {One hour, one blanket, back in bed before anyone stirs. You can still feel where {partner} was leaning on you.|You go down early. {partner} squeezes your hand on the stair, which is better than any star.|One hour under {partner}'s blanket. {They} fold{s} it and give{s} it to you at the bottom of the stair: \"For next time.\"}\n\n@event kael_midnight_roof\ntitle: Kael at Midnight\nplace: observatory\nactor: kael\nslot: midnight\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 12\nart: snow\ntags: social\nscene: A northern student sitting on the observatory parapet at midnight with frost spreading from his hands, the lake far below, the sky thick with stars.\ntext: {Kael is on the observatory parapet, which he shouldn't be, with frost spreading from his fingers into the stone. He moves over to make room.|Kael again, on the parapet, the night's cold not touching him.|Kael has left room on the parapet before you arrive.} {\"I can't sleep south of the mountains,\" he says. \"Too warm.\"|He is watching the lake, not the stars.|\"Northern stars,\" he says, pointing at nothing you can see.}\n> left: Sit with him\n  meet kael\n  rel kael +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You sit until your fingers ache from the cold. He names northern constellations in a language that sounds like snow falling. It's the most he's ever said.|He doesn't talk. Neither do you. When you go, he says \"Goodnight,\" as if it were difficult, and it was.|He's left room, so you take it. He points out a northern star you can't see, and you pretend you can. He knows, and is pleased.}\n> right: Ask what he's watching for\n  meet kael\n  lore +tiny\n  rel kael -tiny\n  say: {\"For it to wake,\" he says, and then shuts up like a book. You've pressed too hard. But you know which way he faces now.|\"Nothing,\" he says, too fast. He's watching the middle of the lake. You leave him to it, colder than when you came.|\"Home,\" he says, about the northern stars, and nothing more. It's the most honest thing he's told you, and he looks away after.}\n\n# ---------------- The gargoyle passage ----------------\n\n@event corvin_note_was_mine\ntitle: Second Timetable\nplace: gargoyle_passage\nactor: corvin\nslot: midnight\nclass: milestone\ntone: light\nwhen: seen(night_lead_in) > 0 and rel(corvin) >= 6\nart: map\ntags: social mystery\nscene: A secret passage behind a gargoyle at midnight, a grinning dark-haired student holding up a thumbnail-sized silver map, chalk marks on the wall behind him.\ntext: In the gargoyle passage, Corvin holds up a map no bigger than a thumbnail. \"Recognise it? The castle has a second timetable. It starts at midnight.\" He grins. \"Who did you think writes notes like that? Prefects?\"\n> left: \"Why me?\"\n  meet corvin\n  rel corvin +medium\n  mood -tiny\n  say: \"Because you looked at that door at the back of the library the way I do.\" He shrugs, and doesn't joke. \"And because it's lonely, knowing all the doors.\" You don't sleep much, after.\n> right: \"You could have signed it.\"\n  meet corvin\n  rel corvin +small\n  guile +tiny\n  say: \"And spoil it?\" He tucks the map into your pocket. \"Keep it. The doors move. The map knows.\" You check it in the morning, and one of the six doors has moved.\n\n@event corvin_midnight_watch\ntitle: Corvin Keeps Watch\nplace: gargoyle_passage\nactor: corvin\nslot: midnight\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: lantern\ntags: social\nscene: A dark corridor at midnight, a dark-haired student leaning out of a niche to wave someone past, a prefect's lantern turning a far corner.\ntext: {Corvin leans out of a niche as you pass. \"Bram's on the east stair. Take the west. You owe me nothing, I'm a saint.\"|Corvin's in his niche again with an apple and a list of where every prefect is.|\"West stair,\" says Corvin, before you ask.} {He looks tired.|He has a new scratch on his cheek.|He's whistling, which he shouldn't.}\n> left: Keep him company\n  meet corvin\n  rel corvin +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You share his apple and his niche. He tells you which tapestry hides a cupboard full of confiscated wands. You're up till the bells.|You keep watch with him for an hour. He's funnier at midnight, and sadder, and pretends to be neither.|He whistles until you shush him, then whistles quieter. You keep watch till the bell and learn three new routes.}\n> right: Take the west stair\n  meet corvin\n  guile +tiny\n  say: {The west stair is dark and empty, as promised. Corvin's map is never wrong. You wonder who taught him to be so careful.|You slip past Bram's lamp with a corridor to spare.|West stair, before you've asked. Corvin's whistling follows you down it, far too loud, and nobody catches either of you.}\n\n# ---------------- The lakeshore ----------------\n\n@event midnight_swim\ntitle: The Lake at Midnight\nplace: lakeshore\nslot: midnight\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: wave\ntags: nature\nscene: A moonlit lakeshore at midnight, clothes folded on a rock, a swimmer's head and a ring of ripples in the silver water.\ntext: {season == \"winter\": The lake at midnight has a skin of ice at the edges and stars in the middle.|{~The lake at midnight is black and silver and perfectly still.|Nobody swims after curfew, which is why you do.|The water is warm from the day and the air is not.}} {clock(hollowstar) >= 5: Far out, a ring of frost forms and fades.|{~An owl crosses the moon.|Somewhere on the far shore, a lantern.|A fish jumps, very loudly.}}\n> left: Swim out to the middle\n  mood +medium\n  health -small\n  say: {~You swim until the castle is a toy and the stars are all around you. The cold gets into your bones and stays till breakfast.|You float on your back in the middle of the lake. Something far below hums, once. You swim back faster than you swam out.|You swim out and turn on your back. The stars and the water are the same colour, and you lose track of which is which. You swim back slowly.}\n> right: Paddle, then get dry\n  mood +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~Knee-deep, then waist-deep, then out, gasping and laughing at yourself. Your teeth chatter all the way up the lawn.|A quick, cold paddle, and back to bed glowing.|You paddle until the fish jumps right beside you. You're out of the water before you've decided to be.}\n\n@event grey_on_the_shore\ntitle: Someone Else Who Can't Sleep\nplace: lakeshore\nslot: midnight\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: clock(hollowstar) >= 2 and seen(hs_grey_stranger) > 0 and not flag(grey_shore_seen)\nweight: 14\nart: mask\ntags: mystery hollowstar danger\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: A moonlit lakeshore at midnight, a tall man in a grey coat sitting on an upturned boat, frost spreading from his boots across the pebbles.\ntext: The man in grey is sitting on an upturned boat at the water's edge, as if he'd been waiting for a ferry. \"You can't sleep either,\" he says pleasantly. \"Nobody sleeps well near this lake. Sit. I won't keep you long. I only want to tell you what the headmistress won't.\"\n> left: Sit and listen\n  flag grey_shore_seen\n  alignment -tiny\n  lore +small\n  mood -small\n  say: He tells you, kindly, that the founders were frightened people who buried what they couldn't understand, and that it has been lonely down there for three hundred years. It sounds true. You walk back with it, and can't put it down.\n> right: Walk away\n  flag grey_shore_seen\n  alignment +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: You turn your back and walk, and the cold follows you all the way up the lawn like a dog. At the door you look back. The boat is empty, and frosted white.\n"},{"path":"content/events/pools/placeholders.grim","text":"# Placeholders (playtest 2, I7): places whose ordinary visits could come up empty get one quiet card each, so a\n# visit always shows something. Low weight, so real cards win when they're available. Tagged `placeholder`: the\n# budget lint lists them instead of judging them, and X replaces them with real content.\n\n@event courier_loft_quiet_hour\ntitle: A Quiet Hour at the Loft\nplace: courier_loft\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 1\nweight: 2\nart: letter\ntags: placeholder job\nscene: An empty courier loft in slanting light, parcels stacked neatly, a single broom asleep in its rack.\ntext: The Courier Loft is quiet today. The dispatch bell hangs still, the pigeonholes are full and nobody needs anything flown anywhere. It's a nice place when it isn't in a hurry.\n> left: Sit by the window a while\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {~You watch the brooms doze in their rack and the dust turn in the light. Nothing happens, pleasantly.|An hour among the parcels with nobody shouting your name. You leave in a better mood.}\n> right: Head off\n  say: {~You leave the loft to its quiet.|Nothing to fly today. You go and find something else to do.}\n\n@event headmistress_tower_quiet_hour\ntitle: An Empty Study\nplace: headmistress_tower\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 1\nweight: 2\nart: book\ntags: placeholder\nscene: A tower study with nobody in it, tea going cold on a tray, a board of crystal towers catching the afternoon sun.\ntext: The study at the top of the tower is empty. The headmistress is elsewhere, the tea has gone cold and the crystal board by the window throws little rainbows on the books. It's a nice room when nobody is deciding your future in it.\n> left: Enjoy the view a moment\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {~You stand at the window and look down at the whole castle, small and busy. Then you go, before anyone asks why you're here.|The rainbows move slowly across the shelves. It's very peaceful, and a little like trespassing.}\n> right: Leave before anyone comes\n  say: {~You close the door softly behind you.|Nobody here today. You take the long stair down.}\n\n@event ledger_office_quiet_hour\ntitle: A Slow News Day\nplace: ledger_office\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 1\nweight: 2\nart: paper\ntags: placeholder club\nscene: A cramped newspaper office under a staircase, the press silent, old front pages curling on the walls.\ntext: It's a slow news day at the Lumen Ledger. The press is cold, the editor's chair is empty and the only sound is the library above, creaking. It's a nice room when nobody is on deadline.\n> left: Read the old front pages\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {~You read headlines from before you were born. Half of them are about the refectory soup.|An hour with the old front pages. The castle has always been like this, it turns out.}\n> right: Come back on deadline night\n  say: {~Nothing to write today. You leave the press to its nap.|You tidy a stack of proofs and go.}\n\n@event flight_field_quiet_hour\ntitle: An Empty Field\nplace: flight_field\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 1\nweight: 2\nart: leaf\ntags: placeholder\nscene: A long green flying field with nobody on it, hoops at either end, grass flattened by the wind.\ntext: Nobody is flying today. The Flight Field is all grass and wind, the hoops at either end swinging a little on their own. It's a nice place when nobody is crashing into it.\n> left: Lie in the grass a while\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {~You watch the clouds race the hoops. The clouds win.|The wind does the flying today. You just watch it.}\n> right: Walk on\n  say: {~You cut across the empty field and go on your way.|No lessons, no races. You leave the field to the wind.}\n"},{"path":"content/events/pools/plans_gigs.grim","text":"# Holiday gigs (playtest 2, M6; X15): working on campus over a break, you take one of two gigs open to anyone\n# (the grounds, the kitchens), or, at a term job's top rank, its holiday version (`gig_library`, `gig_newt`,\n# `gig_pell`, `gig_post`), which pays a little better. A gig pays through its cards; board is still paid.\n# Repeatable routine events; see docs/authoring/pools.md.\n\n@event holiday_gigs\ntitle: Holiday Work\ntrigger: queued\ntype: menu\nclass: story\ntone: warm\nart: coin\ntags: job money\nscene: A noticeboard by the castle gates in the emptying holidays, two handwritten notices pinned side by side.\ntext: Two notices are still pinned by the gates. {season == \"winter\": The snow has got under one of them.|The sun has curled the corners of both.} Old Maud wants hands for the grounds. The Refectory kitchens want someone who can peel fast and not complain about it. {rank(library_assistant) >= 3 or rank(tavern) >= 3 or rank(apothecary_clerk) >= 3 or rank(broom_courier) >= 3: A third notice has your name on it.|}\n> grounds: Work the grounds for Old Maud\n  hint: Outdoor work, heavy and honest. Good pay; hard on the body.\n  gain gig_grounds\n  say: Maud looks you up and down, hands you a pair of gloves two sizes too big, and says, \"You'll grow into them.\"\n> kitchens: Work the Refectory kitchens\n  hint: Long hours by the ovens. Good pay; hard on the temper.\n  gain gig_kitchens\n  say: The head cook gives you an apron, a knife and a sack of onions, and doesn't ask your name until the sack is empty.\n> library: Run the library's holiday stocktake\n  when: rank(library_assistant) >= 3\n  hint: For senior clerks. Better pay; long days in the stacks.\n  gain gig_library\n  say: The head librarian hands you the stocktake ledger, a lamp and a pair of gauntlets. \"Count everything,\" she says. \"Twice, if it moves.\"\n> newt: Run the Newt through the holiday rush\n  when: rank(tavern) >= 3\n  hint: For the Newt's managers. The best tips of the year; the longest nights.\n  gain gig_newt\n  say: The landlady gives you the keys and goes to visit her sister. \"Don't let the fire go out,\" she says, \"or the village.\"\n> pell: Mix the season's remedies at Pell's\n  when: rank(apothecary_clerk) >= 3\n  hint: For assistant apothecaries. Good pay; the village depends on you.\n  gain gig_pell\n  say: Old Pell ties an apron on you himself. \"Half the valley will be coughing by next week,\" he says, delighted. \"We'll be rich.\"\n> post: Fly the holiday post\n  when: rank(broom_courier) >= 3\n  hint: For chief couriers. Every parcel in the valley; the weather against you.\n  gain gig_post\n  say: The loft is stacked to the rafters with parcels in brown paper. Your name is chalked on the board above the busiest route.\n\n# The notices go up in a term's last month, so a player can plan the break (X15).\n@event holiday_notices\ntitle: Notices by the Gate\ntrigger: auto\nslot: afternoon\nwhen: term and (month == 4 or month == 10) and not (final_year and month == 10)\nrepeat: 6\npriority: 30\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: paper\ntags: job money\nscene: A noticeboard by the castle gates near the end of term, fresh notices for holiday work pinned over the old ones, students stopping to read.\ntext: {Holiday work notices are going up by the gates, for the grounds and the kitchens, both paying better than any term job.|The holiday notices again, the same two, in fresher ink.|The notices by the gate. You know them by heart.} {rank(library_assistant) >= 3 or rank(tavern) >= 3 or rank(apothecary_clerk) >= 3 or rank(broom_courier) >= 3: One is addressed to you by name, from your own job.|{Someone has written \"and board!\" under one.|A first-year is copying them out.|The wind is trying to take them.}}\n> left: Pin them up for the cook\n  gold +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {The head cook pays you a coin to pin the new notices straight. Working the break would mean no lie-ins, and real money.|You pin them up, get a coin for it, and read the hours. It's tempting, and tiring just to read.|You pin them up against the wind for the head cook, who pays you a coin and asks, again, whether you'll be staying.}\n> right: Walk past\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {The break is for sleeping, you decide, and walk on. The notices flap behind you.|Not this time. You'll choose when the break comes.|You walk past. You know them by heart, and the break is still for sleeping.}\n\n# ---------------- The grounds (gig_grounds) ----------------\n\n@event gig_grounds_hard_ground\ntitle: Hard Ground\nplan: work\nwhen: has(gig_grounds)\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 30\nart: leaf\ntags: job nature money\nscene: A student and an old woman working a frozen garden bed at dawn, breath steaming, a wheelbarrow between them.\ntext: {season == \"winter\": The ground is frozen two fingers deep and Maud wants the beds turned anyway.|The ground is baked hard and Maud wants the beds turned anyway.} \"The soil doesn't take holidays,\" she says. {~She hands you the heavier spade.|The bees watch you from their hives with professional interest.|She is already on her third bed.}\n> left: Dig until it's done\n  gold +large\n  health -small\n  vigor +small\n  say: {~By noon your hands have blisters on their blisters. Maud counts your wages into them anyway.|You turn every bed. Maud inspects them, says \"Hm,\" and pays you a little over.|You dig until Maud's third bed is matched by your third. She pays you, and gives you the lighter spade next time.}\n> right: Work at Maud's pace\n  gold +medium\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~Maud works slowly and never stops, and by evening you understand why. Less done, a smaller purse, an easier back.|You keep to her pace and her stories. She pays for the beds you turned, not the ones she did.|You work at her pace, and she still finishes three beds to your one. She pays for yours, and laughs about it.}\n\n@event gig_grounds_storm_damage\ntitle: After the Storm\nplan: work\nwhen: has(gig_grounds)\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 30\nart: tree\ntags: job nature money\nscene: A fallen branch across an orchard path after a storm, a student with a saw, an old woman pointing at something higher up.\ntext: {season == \"winter\": Last night's storm brought down half a pear tree.|A summer storm in the night has stripped the orchard.} Maud wants it cleared before the stay-behinds go walking. A cracked limb still hangs over the path.\n> left: Climb up and cut it down\n  gold +large\n  challenge feat apprentice+:\n    win:\n      say: {~The limb comes down where you meant it to. Maud pays you extra for not falling.|You lower it on a rope like a professional. Maud pretends not to be impressed.}\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      say: {~The limb comes down where it wanted to. So do you, more or less. Maud pays you and sends you to the infirmary.|You get the limb and the limb gets you.}\n> right: Rope it off and clear the rest\n  gold +medium\n  say: {~You clear the path and rope off the danger. Maud nods: she'll ask someone with a ladder. The pay is for the path.|Safe and sensible, and a smaller purse for it.}\n\n@event gig_grounds_hive_check\ntitle: Checking the Hives\nplan: work\nwhen: has(gig_grounds)\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 30\nart: leaf\ntags: job nature\nscene: A student in a veiled hat leaning over an open beehive beside an old woman, who isn't wearing one.\ntext: Maud's hives sit at the end of the orchard and she checks them {season == \"winter\": by ear in winter, knocking and listening|by hand in summer, slow and bare-armed}. Today she wants you to do it. \"Tell them who you are,\" she says. \"They like manners.\"\n> left: Introduce yourself properly\n  gold +medium\n  mood +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~You tell the bees your name and your house. Only one stings you, on principle. Maud pays you and calls you a natural.|You feel foolish for about a minute, and then you don't. The hive hums back, and one bee makes a point of it.}\n> right: Get it done quickly\n  gold +large\n  chance even:\n    say: {~Quick and quiet. The bees let it pass this once.|You're done before the bees notice. Maud notices.}\n  else:\n    health -small\n    say: {~The bees take your hurry personally. Maud pays you and pulls out the stings with no sympathy at all.|Three stings and a lesson about manners.}\n\n# ---------------- The Refectory kitchens (gig_kitchens) ----------------\n\n@event gig_kitchens_onions\ntitle: The Onion Sack\nplan: work\nwhen: has(gig_kitchens)\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 30\nart: meal\ntags: job money\nscene: A student at a scrubbed kitchen table behind a mountain of onions, eyes streaming, a huge cook stirring a cauldron behind them.\ntext: {The holiday kitchens feed fewer mouths and work no fewer hours.|Back at the long table, back to the sack.|The head cook has started saving the worst sack for you, as a compliment.} {season == \"winter\": The Midwinter stock needs a hundred onions.|The summer stew needs a hundred onions.} Nobody has ever needed fewer.\n> left: Peel the whole sack\n  gold +large\n  mood -small\n  say: {A hundred onions and not one tear left in you. The head cook pays you and adds a heel of fresh bread.|You finish the sack. The head cook counts the onions, then your wages, both correctly.|You peel the worst sack in the kitchen and don't cry once. The head cook calls it a personal best, and pays it.}\n> right: Peel, then help at the stove\n  gold +medium\n  alchemy +tiny\n  say: {Half a sack and an hour at the stove. Less pay, but now you know why the soup tastes like home.|The head cook lets you stir. Somehow the stew tastes of your grandmother's. You get paid for the onions.|You peel half the worst sack and stir the rest of the morning. The head cook pays for the onions and teaches you a stew.}\n\n@event gig_kitchens_burnt_pan\ntitle: The Burnt Pan\nplan: work\nwhen: has(gig_kitchens)\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 30\nart: meal\ntags: job money\nscene: A smoking pan on a range, a student holding it at arm's length, a cook turning round with a ladle raised.\ntext: You turn away for a moment and the porridge for the stay-behinds catches. The smell is already halfway to the dining hall. The head cook hasn't noticed. Yet.\n> left: Own up and start again\n  gold +medium\n  mood -small\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: {~The head cook sighs the sigh of thirty years in kitchens and docks you for the oats. You make it again, properly.|\"Everyone burns one,\" says the head cook. \"Once.\" You are paid, minus the oats.}\n> right: Scrape it and serve it\n  gold +large\n  alignment -tiny\n  chance likely:\n    say: {~Nobody notices. The stay-behinds eat it and ask for more. You don't feel great about it.|A little smoky. \"Rustic,\" says someone. You get the full day's pay.}\n  else:\n    mood -medium\n    say: {~A professor sends it back with a note. The head cook reads it aloud, slowly, to the whole kitchen.|The smell gives you away. You're paid in full and in disapproval.}\n\n@event gig_kitchens_feast_night\ntitle: Feast Night\nplan: work\nwhen: has(gig_kitchens)\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 30\nart: cup\ntags: job money social\nscene: A steaming kitchen on a feast night, trays going out in a stream, a student in an apron grinning through the chaos.\ntext: {season == \"winter\": Tonight the castle keeps a small Midwinter table for those who stayed, and the kitchens will cook as if for the whole school.|Tonight the stay-behinds hold a summer supper in the courtyard, and the kitchens will cook as if for the whole school.} At breakfast the head cook chalks up the rota: somebody carries trays, somebody plates.\n> left: Volunteer to carry trays\n  gold +large\n  health -small\n  say: {~You carry until your arms forget being arms. The head cook pays you double for the night, in coins and leftovers.|Up and down the stairs until midnight. Somebody toasts the kitchens and you hear it through the door.}\n> right: Ask to plate in the back\n  gold +medium\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~You plate by the ovens and listen to the feast through the wall. Smaller pay, a scorched wrist, and the best seat in the house.|Quiet hands in a hot room. The head cook gives you the last slice of pie, which is its own wage.}\n\n@event gig_grounds_goat\ntitle: Bartholomew Escapes\nplan: work\nwhen: has(gig_grounds)\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 26\nart: paw\ntags: job nature money\nscene: A goat standing triumphantly on the roof of a garden shed, a student with a rope below, an old woman laughing behind them.\ntext: {Bartholomew the goat has got out again and is standing on the shed roof, eating the thatch.|Bartholomew is on the shed roof. He has made this a habit.|The goat is on the roof. You don't even ask how any more.} Maud is laughing too hard to help. {The thatch is going fast.|He has a cabbage in his mouth.|He looks at you like a duke.}\n> left: Climb up after him\n  gold +large\n  health -small\n  vigor +tiny\n  say: {You get him down after an hour and a fall. Maud pays you double for the entertainment, she says, and a little for the bruises.|Up the ladder, along the ridge, one hand on his horn. He comes down when he's ready, which is later than you are.|You climb up and bow to him. He considers it, then lets himself be led down like a duke leaving a ball. Maud pays you for the bow.}\n> right: Bribe him down with an apple\n  gold +medium\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {One apple, held very still. He considers it for a long time, like a merchant, and comes down. Maud pays for the hour you saved.|He takes the apple and stays where he is. Then, at his own pace, he comes down. Maud pays you for patience.|He takes the apple like tribute and descends. Maud pays you, and says he'll expect two next time.}\n\n@event gig_kitchens_staff_supper\ntitle: The Staff Supper\nplan: work\nwhen: has(gig_kitchens)\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 26\nart: meal\ntags: job money\nscene: Cooks and kitchen hands crowded round a scrubbed table late at night, eating the day's leftovers, one student serving.\ntext: {At the morning briefing the head cook announces a staff supper tonight, after the last service. The whole kitchen eats at the scrubbed table, on leftovers, bread and the head cook's opinions.|Staff supper again tonight. They're saving you a place now.|The head cook bangs a ladle at breakfast. \"Staff supper tonight. Sit, or serve.\"} {Somebody is already promising a story about a swan.|The pot-boy is asleep on his feet.|There's a cake cooling that nobody will admit to making.}\n> left: Serve, then wash up\n  gold +large\n  health -small\n  say: {You finish the washing-up at midnight. The head cook pays you extra and calls you \"the useful one\".|You serve, wash up, and wake the pot-boy at midnight to send him to bed. The head cook pays you for both jobs.|You serve forty bowls, wash forty bowls, and are paid for the extra hour in coins and a slice of the mystery cake.}\n> right: Sit and eat with them\n  gold +medium\n  mood +small\n  say: {You eat, and laugh at the swan story, and leave earlier with a smaller purse and a full stomach.|You sit. The cooks tell you which professors send back their soup. It's worth more than the wages.|You sit. Nobody admits to the cake, so everyone eats it. You leave full and a little poorer.}\n\n# ---------------- The holiday versions of term jobs (X15), at their top rank ----------------\n\n@event gig_library_stocktake\ntitle: The Stocktake\nplan: work\nwhen: has(gig_library)\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 30\nart: book\ntags: job study money\nscene: A deserted library in the holidays, a student on a tall ladder with a ledger, books stacked on every table, dust in the lamplight.\ntext: {The library in the holidays is all yours and all to be counted.|The stocktake ledger is half full; the shelves are not half counted.|You've stopped counting books and started counting floors.} {A shelf of atlases has migrated to the poetry section.|One book is not on any list and seems pleased about it.|The lamp in the east wing keeps going out, politely.}\n> left: Count the restricted shelves too\n  gold +large\n  lore +small\n  health -small\n  say: {The restricted shelves count back. You finish with the right number and a bite on the thumb. The librarian dresses the bite and writes it in the ledger, under \"damages\".|You count every chained volume. One of them counts you. The pay is better than term-time's by a good margin.|You count the restricted floor by the lamp that keeps going out. The books count you in the dark. The pay is the best of the break.}\n> right: Keep to the open stacks\n  gold +large\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {Open stacks only, every book counted, the ledger perfect. The librarian pays you in full and says nothing about the shelves you left.|A long, dusty, honest day. You go to bed counting.|You count the open floors and leave the east wing to its lamp. The librarian pays in full and asks no questions.}\n\n@event gig_library_lost_shelf\ntitle: The Shelf That Wasn't There\nplan: work\nwhen: has(gig_library)\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 24\nart: book\ntags: job mystery money\nscene: A narrow library aisle that seems to go on further than the building, a student holding a lamp up to an unfamiliar shelf.\ntext: {The stocktake finds a shelf that isn't in any ledger.|The extra shelf again. It has grown a book since yesterday.|The shelf is longer than the room it's in, now.} {The books on it have no titles.|One is warm.|One is labelled with the date of tomorrow.}\n> left: Catalogue it\n  gold +large\n  lore +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {You list every book. The librarian reads your list, goes pale and pays you to forget it. You don't.|You catalogue the shelf by lamplight. In the morning, it's gone, and your list is the only proof.|You catalogue the book labelled with tomorrow's date. Tomorrow, the label has changed to the day after. The librarian pays you and takes your list.}\n> right: Leave it off the ledger\n  gold +medium\n  guile +tiny\n  say: {You don't write it down. Some shelves don't want to be counted. The librarian, passing, nods as if you'd passed a test.|You skip it. The ledger balances. The shelf, you'd swear, looks relieved.|You don't write down the book with tomorrow's date. Tomorrow, it isn't there. The librarian thanks you, oddly sincerely.}\n\n@event gig_library_last_day\ntitle: The Ledger Balances\nplan: work\nwhen: has(gig_library)\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 20\nart: scroll\ntags: job money\nscene: A head librarian signing a thick stocktake ledger at a desk under a window, a tired student waiting with ink on every finger.\ntext: {The last page of the stocktake. The librarian reads it slowly, all of it.|Another stocktake done, another ledger signed.|The librarian signs, and blots, and signs again for luck.} {\"Not one missing,\" she says. \"That has never happened.\"|\"Three missing, and one extra. Normal.\"|She closes the ledger with both hands.}\n> left: Ask for a reference\n  gold +large\n  renown +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {She writes one on the spot, three lines, and the third makes you blush. The pay is good and the reference is better.|She writes you a reference in a hand like engraving. It's worth more than the wages, someone tells you later.|She signs the ledger and then, without being asked, a reference. \"You'll want it,\" she says. You will.}\n> right: Ask for the afternoon off\n  gold +medium\n  mood +small\n  say: {She gives you the afternoon and a little less pay. You spend it asleep in the reading room, which is the best use of it.|An afternoon off, and a library to yourself. You read for pleasure for the first time in months.|She closes the ledger and gives you the afternoon before you ask. You spend it in the window seat, doing nothing whatsoever.}\n\n@event gig_newt_rush\ntitle: The Holiday Rush\nplan: work\nwhen: has(gig_newt)\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 30\nart: cup\ntags: job money\nscene: A packed village tavern at the holidays, garlands over the bar, every table full, a student manager behind the bar with three jugs in hand.\ntext: {The landlady hands you the keys over breakfast. Tonight, she warns, the Newt will be full to the rafters with farmers, barge crews and families home for the season.|Another holiday night ahead, and the Newt is already promised to a full house.|By morning, half the valley has said it's coming to the Newt tonight.} {A wedding party has sent word.|The fiddler has promised a song with forty verses.|The firewood is stacked to the ceiling.}\n> left: Keep the bar open till the last song\n  gold +large\n  charm +small\n  health -small\n  say: {You pour till the song ends, then pour for the encore. The till is the heaviest you've ever counted, and your feet will never forgive you.|Closing at three, the till heavy, the floor sticky. The landlady's note says \"Well done\" in two places.|Half the valley comes, and you keep pouring until the firewood runs low. The till is heavy, and so is your sleep.}\n> right: Close at the proper hour\n  gold +large\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You call time at midnight, firmly and politely. Some grumble. The till is lighter, and you sleep.|Last orders on the stroke. The till loses some takings; you keep your temper.|You close on the stroke, half the valley or not. They go home singing, which counts as a good night.}\n\n@event gig_newt_brawl\ntitle: A Fight Over a Song\nplan: work\nwhen: has(gig_newt)\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 24\nart: cup\ntags: job danger money\nscene: Two barge crews squaring up in the middle of a crowded tavern, a student manager stepping between them with a tray.\ntext: {Two barge crews disagree about the last verse of a song, loudly.|The barge crews again, and the song again, and the same verse.|This time it's a farmer and a fisherman, and the song is a different one.} {A chair is already in the air.|Somebody has taken their coat off.|The fiddler has stopped playing.}\n> left: Step between them\n  challenge combat apprentice+:\n    win:\n      gold +large\n      renown +tiny\n      say: {You stop the first punch with a tray and the second with a look. Both crews buy a round to make up. You take the tips.|You walk between them and stay there. Eventually somebody laughs, and the song goes on.|The fiddler stops; you start the next verse yourself, badly. Both sides correct you, together, and forget to fight.}\n    lose:\n      gold +medium\n      health -medium\n      say: {The tray takes one punch and your jaw takes the other. They pay for the chair, at least.|You get a black eye and a free drink, which you can't drink, being on duty.|The fiddler stops, and so does the fight, just as you step in. Everyone laughs at your timing. You take the bruise for nothing.}\n> right: Bribe them with a free round\n  gold +medium\n  charm +tiny\n  say: {One round on the house, and they're singing together by the second verse. The house is a little poorer.|A free round fixes most things. The landlady's ledger will notice.|A free round, and the fiddler starts again. The farmer and the fisherman sing a duet, and the landlady's ledger winces.}\n\n@event gig_newt_fire\ntitle: The Fire Must Not Go Out\nplan: work\nwhen: has(gig_newt)\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 20\nart: candle\ntags: job money\nscene: The great hearth of a village tavern late at night, embers banked low, a student kneeling with tongs and a bucket of ash.\ntext: {The landlady's fire has never gone out in her lifetime or her mother's. She's away tonight, and this morning the wind is already in the chimney.|The landlady is away again tonight, and the fire is already low. The wind is from the north.|The fire flickered last night as you locked up, as if to test you. Tonight it's yours again.} {The wood is damp.|There's frost on the inside of the windows.|The cat is sitting on the warmest stone, unhelpfully.}\n> left: Sit up with it all night\n  gold +large\n  health -small\n  say: {You feed it one stick at a time until dawn. When the landlady comes back, she puts a hand on the stones and nods at you without a word.|You sleep in the chair with the tongs in your lap. The fire lives. So, nearly, do you.|You sit up with it, cat on the warmest stone. At dawn it's still burning, and the cat has not moved once.}\n> right: Bank it and trust it\n  gold +large\n  mood -small\n  say: {You bank it the way she showed you and go to bed. You dream of ash. In the morning, it's alive, and you're shaking.|You trust it. It holds. You don't sleep a wink anyway.|You bank it and move the cat. The cat moves back. In the morning the fire's alive, and the cat is very warm.}\n\n@event gig_pell_remedy_rush\ntitle: Cough Season\nplan: work\nwhen: has(gig_pell)\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 30\nart: bottle\ntags: job alchemy money\nscene: A crowded village apothecary in winter, a queue of coughing villagers, a student and an old man filling bottles as fast as they can.\ntext: {The whole valley is coughing, and Pell's queue is out of the door.|Day three of the cough. Pell is humming. You are not.|The queue at Pell's has its own gossip by now.} {A carter wants the cherry one.|A farmer wants something for his cow.|A professor in disguise wants something \"for a friend\".}\n> left: Mix to order, carefully\n  gold +large\n  alchemy +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {Every bottle mixed for the person in front of you. It's slow, and Pell pays you for every one of them.|You learn sixty coughs by ear. Pell says you're wasted on students.|You mix the disguised professor's remedy \"for a friend\" with great discretion. Pell charges double for the discretion and gives you half.}\n> right: Mix in big batches\n  gold +large\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {One cauldron, one recipe, three hundred bottles. It works for nearly everyone. Pell pays you and looks at the cauldron for a long time.|Fast and efficient. A farmer comes back the next day about the cow.|The professor in disguise takes a bottle from the batch and looks offended to be treated like everyone else.}\n\n@event gig_pell_night_knock\ntitle: A Knock After Closing\nplan: work\nwhen: has(gig_pell)\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 24\nart: bottle\ntags: job alchemy money\nscene: An apothecary's door at night, a lantern held up to a frightened villager in the doorway, a student in a nightcap behind the counter.\ntext: {Pell asks you over breakfast to sleep above the shop tonight. There's a fever about, and the knocks come after closing.|Another night above the shop, Pell asks. The knocks keep coming after dark.|You're sleeping above the shop again tonight. You've stopped bothering to unlace your boots.} {Pell sleeps like a stone.|The fire goes out at ten.|The ledger says not to open after dark.}\n> left: Mix any remedy yourself\n  challenge brew adept-:\n    win:\n      gold +large\n      alchemy +small\n      say: {The knock comes at midnight. A shepherd, his wife burning with fever. You mix the remedy by candlelight from memory. She's better by morning; he pays in eggs and coins, and Pell pays you for the night.|The remedy is right. Pell, in the morning, checks your work and says nothing, which from him is a medal.|You sleep in your boots and mix the remedy before you're properly awake. It's right. Pell finds the mortar warm in the morning, and nods.}\n    lose:\n      gold +medium\n      mood -small\n      say: {The first batch is wrong. You wake Pell, who fixes it in a minute and pays you anyway, for trying.|You get it right on the third try, at dawn, and you shake all the next day.|You mix it half asleep and half right. The knock comes back an hour later, and Pell comes down.}\n> right: Wake Pell for anything\n  gold +medium\n  say: {When the knock comes, Pell comes down grumbling, mixes it in two minutes and goes back to bed. You learn more watching than mixing.|You wake him. He's not cross. \"That's what I'm for,\" he says.|You wake Pell, who's already pulling his boots on. He's started sleeping in them too.}\n\n@event gig_pell_ledger\ntitle: Pell's Books\nplan: work\nwhen: has(gig_pell)\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 20\nart: scroll\ntags: job money\nscene: A cluttered apothecary back room, an old man and a student bent over a ledger full of crossings-out, jars all around them.\ntext: {Pell's account books have not been balanced since his daughters left home.|The account books again. Pell has found another drawer of receipts.|You're nearly at the bottom of the receipts. Pell is nearly at the bottom of his patience.} {Half the debts are owed to him.|Half the debts are owed by him.|Some debts are in a currency that no longer exists.}\n> left: Balance them properly\n  gold +large\n  lore +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: {Three days of receipts. At the end, Pell is owed more than he thought. He pays you out of it, generously.|You balance every page. Pell signs it as if it were a treaty.|You convert the vanished currency at an old rate you find in a drawer. Pell is owed a goat, a few crowns and an apology. He pays you from the crowns.}\n> right: Forgive the small debts\n  gold +medium\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: {You strike out every small debt owed to Pell, everything under a crown. Pell reads the list and nods. \"They'd never have paid,\" he says. \"Now they'll come back.\"|Small debts forgiven, the book is simpler and the village is kinder to Pell. The pay is a little less.|You forgive the debts in the vanished currency, since nobody could pay them anyway. Pell laughs for the first time in a week.}\n\n@event gig_post_storm_run\ntitle: The Storm Run\nplan: work\nwhen: has(gig_post)\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 30\nart: feather\ntags: job danger money\nscene: A courier on a broom flying low over snowy fields in a storm, a sack of parcels strapped behind them, a farmhouse light ahead.\ntext: {The holiday post doesn't wait for weather. Tonight's route crosses the ridge in a storm.|The ridge again, and the wind again.|You've flown this route so often the farm dogs know your broom.} {The sack is heavier than the broom likes.|One parcel is labelled \"fragile\" in four languages.|The light at the last farm is always on for you.}\n> left: Fly straight over the ridge\n  challenge feat adept wild:\n    win:\n      gold +large\n      vigor +small\n      say: {Over the ridge and down to the farms in half the time. The bonus for early delivery is very good.|The wind does its worst and you do better. Every parcel delivered, dry.|You fly the ridge with the farm dogs barking you home. The light at the last farm is on, and the bonus is too.}\n    lose:\n      gold +medium\n      health -medium\n      say: {The wind takes you into a hedge. The parcels survive. You are less sure about yourself.|You deliver everything, late and frozen, with a sprained wrist.|The storm hides the light at the last farm. You find it by the dogs, an hour late and very wet.}\n> right: Take the valley road\n  gold +large\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {The long way round, low and slow. Late, but every parcel's in one piece, and so are you.|The valley road adds two hours and no danger. The pay is the pay.|You take the valley road. The light at the last farm is still on when you get there, late. Somebody waited.}\n\n@event gig_post_wrong_parcel\ntitle: The Wrong Parcel\nplan: work\nwhen: has(gig_post)\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 24\nart: letter\ntags: job money\nscene: A farmhouse doorway in the snow, an old woman holding up a parcel that is clearly ticking, a courier on the step looking worried.\ntext: {A parcel you delivered yesterday has come back. The old woman says it isn't hers, and it's ticking.|Another wrong address. The holiday post is chaos.|The labels are getting worse. One just says \"Aunt\".} {The ticking is getting faster.|It smells of oranges.|It's addressed to someone who died years ago.}\n> left: Find the right house tonight\n  gold +large\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You fly the valley till midnight and find the right house. The ticking was a clock, a present for a grandfather. He gives you a slice of cake and a coin.|You find the owner three farms over. The bonus for \"lost and found\" is surprisingly good.|The person it's addressed to died years ago. You find their granddaughter instead, who opens it in the doorway and laughs and cries at once. The bonus is good.}\n> right: Return it to the loft\n  gold +medium\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You take it back to the loft. Someone else will fly it tomorrow, and get the bonus.|The loft-master sighs and puts it on the shelf of the lost. It ticks there all week.|You take it back. The loft-master reads the name, goes quiet, and says he'll deliver this one himself.}\n\n@event gig_post_last_parcel\ntitle: The Last Parcel\nplan: work\nwhen: has(gig_post)\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 20\nart: letter\ntags: job money\nscene: An empty courier loft at the end of a holiday shift, one small parcel left on the shelf, addressed in shaky handwriting.\ntext: {The last parcel of the day is small, and addressed in a shaky hand to a cottage on the far moor.|One parcel left again. Always one.|The last parcel, and the last daylight.} {It's light as a feather.|It's warm.|It rattles like a box of buttons.}\n> left: Fly it out now\n  gold +large\n  health -small\n  say: {The cottage is an hour into the dark. An old man opens the door and holds the parcel as if it were alive. \"From my son,\" he says. The pay feels like the least of it.|You deliver it at dusk to a woman who cries. You don't ask why. You fly home slowly.|It rattles all the way across the moor. Buttons, the woman says at the door, from her mother's coat. She gives you one.}\n> right: Leave it for the morning\n  gold +large\n  mood -small\n  say: {It waits on the shelf all night and you think about it all night. You fly it at dawn.|In the morning you deliver it, a day late. Nobody minds but you.|You leave it till morning, last light being gone. It rattles on the shelf all night, as if impatient.}\n"},{"path":"content/events/pools/plans_more.grim","text":"# More break-plan pool events (castle, home, work) and the \"regular\" milestones.\n# Repeatable routine events; see docs/authoring/pools.md.\n\n# ---------------- Break: at the castle (plan: castle) ----------------\n\n@event break_castle_holiday_repairs\ntitle: Holiday Repairs\nplan: castle\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: house\ntags: home\nscene: A caretaker with a long list on a clipboard in an empty castle corridor, a student trying to look busy nearby.\ntext: {The caretaker has a list of jobs that can only be done when the castle is empty, and an eye for idle students.|The caretaker's list is out again. Your name is pencilled in at the bottom, provisionally.|The caretaker hands you the list and a mug of tea, in that order.} {season == \"winter\": Today it's snow on the battlements, heavy enough to worry the roof leads.|Today it's the dormitory mattresses, which must be carried out and beaten in the sun.}\n> left: Lend a hand\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {season == \"winter\": {You shovel snow off the battlements until you can't feel your ears. The view is worth one of them.|You and the caretaker clear a whole parapet in silence. At the end you're handed a biscuit, which is how the caretaker says thank you.|You drink the tea, then shovel the battlements till noon. The caretaker ticks you off the list, which feels like a medal.}|{You beat forty mattresses. Two of them contained socks, one a love letter, and one a very surprised mouse.|You carry mattresses down six flights of moving stairs. One flight moves halfway through, with you and the mattress on it.|Tea first, then mattresses until the sun goes down. The caretaker ticks you off the list with a flourish.}}\n> right: Make myself scarce\n  challenge deceive apprentice:\n    win:\n      mood +small\n      say: {You remember an urgent appointment with a book and vanish round a corner. The caretaker's sigh follows you a long way.|You hide in a window seat until the list has moved on to someone else.|You drink the tea, hand back the list and disappear before the mug is cold.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      vigor +tiny\n      say: {The caretaker finds you behind a tapestry and hands you a shovel without comment.|You get as far as the stairs, which choose that moment to move you straight back to the caretaker.|You finish the tea and try to slip off. The caretaker taps the list, and you work down it until supper.}\n\n@event break_castle_flight_field\ntitle: The Field to Yourself\nplan: castle\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: broom\ntags: home\nscene: An empty flight field in the holidays, hoops at either end, a single student on a broom high above the grass.\ntext: {With the flying lessons over for the break, the flight field is empty and nobody has locked the broom shed.|The broom shed is unlocked again. You suspect it's on purpose now.|The flight field in the break. You know which broom in the shed pulls left.} {season == \"winter\": The hoops are rimed with frost and the wind has teeth.|The air shimmers with heat, and the wind is lazy for once.} {A crow is using the far hoop as a perch.|Somebody's lost scarf is snagged on the near hoop.|The grass is printed with a fox's tracks.}\n> left: Fly the hoops, fast\n  challenge feat apprentice wild:\n    win:\n      vigor +small\n      mood +small\n      say: {You thread every hoop and pull up so late that the grass brushes your boots. Nobody saw. That's almost a shame.|You lap the field until the hoops blur. Landing, you find you're grinning like a first-year.|You take the broom that pulls left and fly the hoops leaning right. Below you, the fox's tracks blur into one long line.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {The wind cheats, as it always does, and you meet the far hoop with your shoulder.|You land in the long grass, which has caught a great many students before you. It does not catch gently.|You forget which broom pulls left until the far hoop reminds you. You land in the fox's tracks, hard.}\n> right: Practise slow, careful turns\n  vigor +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {Left, right, left, at walking pace, for an hour. It is deeply dull and, annoyingly, it works.|You practise the turn that always throws you until it doesn't. Your arms ache all evening.|You turn against the broom that pulls left until it gives up and flies straight. You land beside the fox's tracks, pleased with you both.}\n\n@event break_castle_bored_stairs\ntitle: The Bored Staircases\nplan: castle\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: tower\ntags: home\nscene: A grand castle stairwell with several staircases swinging slowly through the air, a lone student waiting on a landing.\ntext: {With nobody to carry up and down, the castle's staircases have grown restless.|The staircases are bored again, and they've noticed you.|The stairs know you're one of the few left. One swings over the moment you reach the landing.} {season == \"winter\": The stairwell is cold enough to see your breath.|Warm air drifts up the stairwell with the smell of cut grass.} {A flight is drifting past with a lost shoe on its top step.|Two staircases are sulking against the wall.|One flight keeps nudging the landing, like a dog with a stick.}\n> left: Ride whichever one comes\n  chance likely:\n    guile +small\n    mood +small\n    say: {It carries you to a corridor you've never seen, full of portraits who are thrilled to have a visitor.|It takes you the long way, through three towers, and sets you down exactly where you wanted to be.|The eager flight carries you off like a dog with a stick, the long way round, and brings you back very pleased with itself. It was a good ride.}\n  else:\n    health -tiny\n    mood -small\n    say: {It drops you on a landing in the east wing and refuses to come back. You walk for an hour.|It swings out over the stairwell and stops. You wait there, cramped and cold, with nothing to read, until it gets bored again.|The eager flight carries you off like a dog with a stick and drops you somewhere it thinks you'll like. You don't. It takes an hour to walk back.}\n> right: Take the long way, on foot\n  vigor +small\n  mood -small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You take the servants' stair, all two hundred steps of it. The staircases watch you go, disappointed.|You walk round by the bridges. It takes all morning, and your legs will remember it.|You walk past the eager flight. It follows you the length of the stairwell, nudging the banister, and you don't give in.}\n\n@event break_castle_night_rounds\ntitle: The Lone Prefect\nplan: castle\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: lantern\ntags: home\nscene: A single lantern bobbing along a bridge between dark towers at night, a student watching it from a window.\ntext: {Only one prefect has stayed for the break, and at breakfast they sigh that seven towers are a lot to patrol tonight.|The lone prefect is pinning tonight's rounds to the noticeboard again.|You know the lone prefect's rounds by now, to the quarter-hour, and tonight's are on the noticeboard.} {season == \"winter\": The corridors are cold enough to frost the portraits.|The nights are warm, and every window in the castle stands open after dark.} {A staircase shifts somewhere below with a long stone sigh.|An owl has got into the east wing.|Somewhere a door bangs in a draught.}\n> left: Wander the towers tonight\n  challenge deceive apprentice+:\n    win:\n      guile +small\n      mood +small\n      say: {You have the castle to yourself for an hour. Moonlit bridges, sleeping portraits, the whole lake laid out below.|You slip past the lantern twice and watch the lake from a window nobody uses. It feels like owning the place.|You know the rounds to the quarter-hour, and you're never where the lantern is. The banging door covers your footsteps all the way to the roof.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      guile +tiny\n      say: {The lantern swings round and finds you. The prefect writes your name in the demerit ledger with visible relief at having something to write.|You walk straight into the prefect on a dark landing. You both scream, and then you get a demerit.|The prefect changed the rounds after pinning them up. The lantern is exactly where you are, and a moment later so is your name, in the ledger.}\n> right: Offer to walk the rounds\n  charm +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {The prefect is glad of the company and tells you which portraits snore. You go to bed very late.|You carry the lantern while the prefect checks doors. Somewhere in the fourth tower you become friends, a little.|You walk the rounds you already know by heart. The prefect is impressed, then suspicious, and you shut the banging door together.}\n\n# ---------------- Break: at home (plan: home) ----------------\n\n@event break_home_family_recipe\ntitle: The Family Recipe\nplan: home\nslot: morning\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: cauldron\ntags: home\nscene: A warm family kitchen, an older relative guiding a student's hands over a steaming pot, jars and bowls everywhere.\ntext: {Someone at home has decided you're old enough for the family recipe, and ties an apron on you before breakfast.|The recipe lessons resume. You're trusted with the knife now, if not the salt.|You know the first half of the recipe by heart. The second half is still guarded like a treaty.} {season == \"winter\": It's the stew for the cold months, which takes all day.|It's the summer preserve, and every fruit in the house is involved.} {Nobody measures anything.|A pinch turns out to mean a handful.|The pot is older than you are.}\n> left: Write it all down\n  alchemy +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {You take notes like it's a potions practical. You're corrected on every line, and then on the corrections.|Your notes are thorough, exact and, you're told, missing the point.|You write down the first half. When the second half begins, your notebook is gently confiscated.}\n> right: Learn it by hand, the old way\n  mood +small\n  alchemy +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You burn a finger and get a nod. In this kitchen, that's a diploma.|You get it wrong in a way that turns out to be how your grandmother made it. Everyone goes quiet, and then they eat it all.|Your hands learn a step of the second half before anyone means to teach it. Nobody says a word, but you're the one left stirring the old pot.}\n\n@event break_home_own_bed\ntitle: Your Own Bed\nplan: home\nslot: morning\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: bed\ntags: home\nscene: A narrow bed under a cottage window in early light, a student buried in blankets while the house bustles below.\ntext: {Your own bed, after a term in the dormitory. You wake at the academy's hour anyway.|The house wakes at dawn. It always has; you'd forgotten.|You know which floorboard creaks on the way down, and that everyone else learned it first.} {season == \"winter\": There's frost on the window and the blankets weigh a ton.|Sunlight is coming through the curtains, and someone is already whistling in the yard.} Downstairs, {the kettle is going.|someone is calling your name.|the door bangs for the third time.}\n> left: Sleep until noon\n  health +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You sleep like a stone. At lunch you're called \"the scholar\" with a new and pointed emphasis.|You wake at noon, rested and ashamed. The whole house has done a morning's work, and mentions it.|You sleep through the door banging a third time. When you finally come down, you miss the creaky floorboard, and nobody hears how late you are.}\n> right: Get up with everyone\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {season == \"winter\": {You break the ice on the water butt and carry wood in before breakfast. Your hands sting all morning.|You're out in the dark with a lantern before the cockerel. The cockerel resents the competition.|You step over the creaking floorboard and are at the woodpile before anyone knows you're up. Your fingers sting till noon.}|{You're in the yard by sunrise, hauling and fetching. Breakfast, when it comes, is the best of the year.|You work the cool hours with everyone else and see why they swear by them.|You step over the creaking floorboard and are in the yard before the door bangs again. By breakfast the work is half done.}}\n\n@event break_home_mending_chair\ntitle: The Mending Chair\nplan: home\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: wand\ntags: home\nscene: A chair by a cottage door piled with broken household things, a student in academy robes eyeing them with a wand.\ntext: {Word goes round the house that the scholar can fix things.|The things that need fixing have been waiting for you on a chair by the door.|The mending chair fills up between breaks, like a well.} {season == \"winter\": Today it's a stove door that won't stay shut.|Today it's a window that sticks in the heat.} {Also a clock that strikes thirteen.|Also a doll with a missing eye.|Also a teapot nobody admits to breaking.}\n> left: Mend it with a spell\n  challenge spell apprentice:\n    win:\n      arcana +small\n      mood +small\n      say: {It mends with a click and a little puff of light. The family applauds, then brings out three more things.|You fix it so well that it works better than new. Now everyone wants their things to work better than new.|The teapot mends without a crack showing. Somebody looks very relieved, and the chair fills up again by supper.}\n    lose:\n      mana -small\n      mood -small\n      say: {It's mended, technically. It also hums now, and nobody can make it stop.|The spell fixes it and then, for good measure, everything else on the chair. Most of it did not need fixing that way.|The teapot mends, and now pours only for whoever broke it. Nobody will pick it up.}\n> right: Mend it by hand\n  vigor +small\n  say: {Glue, string, a borrowed hammer and the whole morning. It holds. You don't trust it, but it holds.|You fix it the way your family always has: slowly, with a lot of opinions from the doorway.|You glue the teapot together crack by crack. Nobody admits to anything, but someone brings you tea in it.}\n\n@event break_home_the_trunk\ntitle: The Trunk in the Corner\nplan: home\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: house\ntags: home\nscene: An open travelling trunk in the corner of a small bedroom, overflowing with jars, socks and parcels in cloth.\ntext: {Your trunk stands open in the corner, and the family keeps putting things in it.|The trunk is open again, and it's filling up again.|The trunk is a family project now.} {season == \"winter\": Woollen socks, a jar of something pickled, a cake wrapped in three cloths.|Dried fruit, a straw hat nobody asked for, a cake wrapped in three cloths.} {final_year: Someone says \"for next term\", then stops.|{The lid won't close.|Someone sits on the lid.|A note is tucked into a sock, not to be read till later.}}\n> left: Take everything\n  health +small\n  gold -tiny\n  say: {The bargeman charges you double for the weight, but you eat well for a month.|You pay the barge extra for the weight and haul it all back across the lake. Half the corridor smells of pickles until spring.|You take everything. The bargeman charges for the weight, and somewhere in the third cloth you find a note from home.}\n> right: Sneak half of it back\n  guile +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You smuggle the jars back into the pantry at night. In the morning they're in the trunk again, wrapped tighter.|You leave the cake on a shelf by the door. You feel worse about it than you expected.|You manage to leave one jar behind. In its place, someone has tucked a note.}\n\n# ---------------- Break: working full-time (plan: work) ----------------\n\n@event break_work_stocktake\ntitle: Stocktaking\nplan: work\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: scales\ntags: job\nscene: A student with a ledger and pencil counting rows of stock in a quiet workplace, the owner reading over their shoulder.\ntext: {The quiet days of the break are, says whoever is in charge, the time to count everything.|Stocktaking again. You have opinions about how to count things now.|Stocktaking. You count faster than your boss, and slower than you'd like.} {has(gig_newt): At the Gilded Newt that means every tankard, keg and spoon.|{has(gig_pell): At Pell & Daughters that means a thousand drawers.|{has(gig_post): At the loft that means every parcel, pigeonhole and broom.|{has(gig_library): In the library that means books that won't hold still to be counted.|{has(gig_kitchens): In the kitchens that means every ladle, pot and sack of flour.|{has(gig_grounds): In the sheds that means every trowel, rake and seed packet.|That means everything, twice.}}}}}} The tally comes out one short.\n> left: Track down the missing one\n  challenge research using guile apprentice:\n    win:\n      guile +small\n      gold +tiny\n      say: {You find it wedged behind the counter, where it has been since the last stocktake. You're tipped for your thoroughness.|You find the mistake in the ledger itself: someone counted a box as a crate. You're paid a little extra, and nothing else is said.|You count faster than your boss and find it first, under a sack nobody has moved in years. You're tipped, a little grudgingly.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      health -tiny\n      say: {You search until dusk, on your knees in the dust. It stays missing, and your boss looks at you as if you might have eaten it.|You count everything again and get a different number. Then a third.|You count faster than your boss, and wrong. The missing one stays missing, and now so does another.}\n> right: Write it off and move on\n  mood +small\n  gold -tiny\n  say: {\"Breakage,\" you write. A coin is docked from your pay, on principle.|You write it off, and your boss sighs and takes it from your wages.|You write it off before your boss has finished counting. It comes out of your wages all the same.}\n\n@event break_work_complaint\ntitle: The Complaint\nplan: work\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: hand\ntags: job\nscene: An irate customer leaning over a counter, finger raised, while a young worker stands very still behind it.\ntext: {Someone has come back with a complaint and a voice built for it.|Another complaint, another raised voice. You've learned to breathe out first.|This one has been saving the complaint all week. You can tell.} {has(gig_newt): The cider was too warm, or too cold, or too cider.|{has(gig_pell): The tonic did nothing, which is worse, they say, than doing something.|{has(gig_post): The parcel arrived wet, late and somehow smug.|{has(gig_library): The book they want is out, and they want it in.|{has(gig_kitchens): The stew had a button in it, and they want to know whose.|{has(gig_grounds): A visitor's boots are ruined, and the mud on the path is apparently your mud.|The complaint has several parts, all of them loud.}}}}}} Whoever's in charge is out.\n> left: Talk them down\n  challenge negotiate apprentice+:\n    win:\n      charm +small\n      gold +tiny\n      say: {You agree with everything until there's nothing left to agree with. They leave calm, and they leave a tip.|You listen, nod and offer them a chair. By the end they're telling you about their sister.|You let them say a week's worth of complaint without interrupting once. By the end they're almost apologetic, and they tip.}\n    lose:\n      mood -medium\n      say: {It gets louder, and then it leaves, still loud, to tell everyone in the valley about you.|They demand your boss, then you, then your boss again, and you spend the rest of the day hearing about it.|A week's worth of complaint, and you interrupt it once. That's once too many.}\n> right: Give them what they want\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {You hand over a refund out of your wages. They leave. The quiet afterwards is worth a coin.|A free one, on you. They take it without thanks, and the next one is lovely.|You give in before they're halfway through a week's worth of complaint. They seem almost disappointed.}\n\n@event break_work_half_hour\ntitle: Half an Hour Off\nplan: work\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: cup\ntags: job\nscene: A tired student in a work apron standing in a back doorway, looking out at the weather with a cup in hand.\ntext: {Half an hour off in the middle of a long holiday shift.|Your half hour again. You know exactly how far it goes now.|Half an hour. You spend the first minute deciding.} {season == \"winter\": Outside, the snow is coming down sideways.|Outside, the sun is bright enough to hurt.} {The back room has one chair and one draught.|A colleague has left half a pie, possibly for sharing.|A clock somewhere strikes the wrong hour.}\n> left: Walk out into the weather\n  mood +small\n  health -tiny\n  gold -tiny\n  say: {season == \"winter\": {You buy roast chestnuts and eat them walking, snow in your collar. You come back soaked and much happier.|You buy a hot pie for the walk to the lake's edge and back. Your nose will not forgive you.|You spend a minute deciding and the rest in the snow, to the end of the lane and back. A clock strikes the wrong hour as you come in.}|{You buy a cold drink and sit in the sun until your neck burns. Worth it.|You buy a lemonade and walk to the water and stand there. Half an hour is longer outside.|You spend a minute deciding and the rest in the sun. A clock strikes the wrong hour, and you nearly believe it.}}\n> right: Nap in the back room\n  health +small\n  say: {You sleep sitting up and wake with the shape of a crate on your cheek.|You doze off and dream of work, which seems unfair. The bell wakes you mid-shift in the dream.|You spend a minute deciding, and wake to a clock striking the wrong hour. You're late, or early. Nobody can say.}\n\n@event break_work_short_wages\ntitle: A Coin Short\nplan: work\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: coin\ntags: job money\nscene: A student counting a small pile of coins on a counter for the second time, frowning, an employer pretending not to watch.\ntext: {Wages, counted out into your hand. You count them again.|Wage day. You count your coins at the counter, in front of everyone, on principle.|Wage day. Your boss counts slowly, and so do you.} {It's short by one.|It's short, and there's a button where a coin should be.|It's short by just enough to notice.} {has(gig_newt): The landlady remembers every tab to the penny, and has somehow forgotten this one.|{has(gig_pell): Old Pell counted it himself, drop by drop.|{has(gig_post): The dispatch ledger says otherwise, in smudged ink.|{has(gig_library): The pay envelope says otherwise, in very small writing.|{has(gig_kitchens): The head cook's tally is written in gravy.|{has(gig_grounds): Maud's pay book says otherwise, in pencil.|Someone's sums say otherwise.}}}}}}\n> left: Point it out\n  challenge negotiate apprentice:\n    win:\n      gold +small\n      charm +tiny\n      say: {You show your sums. Your boss checks them, coughs, and pays you the difference with a little extra for the embarrassment.|You point it out politely and firmly. The missing coin appears, with an apology and a pastry.|You count as slowly as your boss did, aloud, coin by coin. The difference is paid before you reach the end.}\n    lose:\n      gold +tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: {Your boss recounts, gets the same wrong answer, and looks hurt that you asked.|You're told it's for the breakage. You don't remember any breakage. Nobody else does either.|You count aloud, slowly. Your boss counts back, slower, and reaches a different number. You give up before supper.}\n> right: Let it go this once\n  gold +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  charm +tiny\n  say: {You pocket it without a word. Your boss sees you notice, and you're given the easy shift next week.|One coin. You decide you can afford to be the bigger person, just about.|Short by just enough to notice, and you notice, and say nothing. Your boss counts faster next time.}\n\n# ---------------- Regulars (one-time milestones at public places) ----------------\n\n@event refectory_cooks_taster\ntitle: The Cook's Taster\nplace: refectory\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nweight: 30\nwhen: visits_to(refectory) >= 6\nart: meal\ntags: life\nscene: A refectory cook holding out a small spoon over the counter to a student, a new pot steaming behind her.\ntext: The cook stops you before you reach the bench and holds out a spoon. \"You're in here more than the cats,\" she says. \"So you can taste the new pot first. Regulars do.\" Behind her a dozen steaming pots wait their turn, and the other students in the queue look on with naked envy.\n> left: Give an honest verdict\n  health +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You say it wants salt and less of the grey thing. The cook frowns, tastes, and nods. From then on your bowl comes from the bottom of the pot, where the good bits are.\n> right: Praise it to the rafters\n  mood +small\n  say: You declare it the best soup in the kingdom. The cook doesn't believe a word of it, and gives you a second spoonful anyway.\n\n@event library_footnote_bag\ntitle: Footnote's Approval\nplace: library\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nweight: 30\nwhen: visits_to(library) >= 6\nart: cat\ntags: life\nscene: A huge old library cat curled asleep on a student's satchel under a reading desk, a student standing over it, uncertain.\ntext: You come back from the shelves to find Footnote asleep on your bag, curled tight, one paw over the strap. Footnote sleeps on the reference desk, as a rule, and almost never on anyone's belongings; everybody knows that. A passing librarian stops, looks from the cat to you, and nods, as if something official has happened.\n> left: Wait for him to wake\n  mood +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: You read standing up until the lamps are lit. Footnote wakes, stretches, and walks off without a glance. Your bag smells of warm cat for a week, and you find you don't mind.\n> right: Ease the bag out, inch by inch\n  guile +small\n  say: You slide it free a fraction at a time. Footnote opens one eye, judges you, and allows it. The librarian says she's never seen him allow anything.\n\n@event mabels_your_usual\ntitle: Your Usual\nplace: mabels\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nweight: 30\nwhen: visits_to(mabels) >= 6\nart: cup\ntags: life\nscene: A small tea shop counter with a cup already poured and a slice of cake on a plate, the shopkeeper smiling at a student in the doorway.\ntext: You haven't even reached the counter. Mabel already has your cup out, the right tea steeping and the cake you always end up choosing on a plate. \"Your usual,\" she says, and it isn't a question. On the slate behind the till, among the regulars, there's a new line in chalk.\n> left: Have the usual\n  mood +small\n  gold -tiny\n  say: It's exactly right, as it always is. Mabel watches you take the first sip and turns back to the shelves, satisfied.\n> right: Ask for something new\n  charm +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: Mabel's eyebrows rise. Then she smiles like someone handed a puzzle and reaches for a tin from the very top shelf. It's odd and smoky, and you don't like it, and you're glad you asked.\n\n@event bakery_bun_set_aside\ntitle: The Bun Set Aside\nplace: bakery\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nweight: 30\nwhen: visits_to(bakery) >= 6\nart: meal\ntags: life\nscene: A baker's son handing a paper-wrapped bun across a flour-dusted counter, a letter written on the paper in flour.\ntext: The baker's son reaches under the counter before you've said a word and brings out a bun wrapped in paper, with your initial on it in flour. \"Saved from the first batch,\" he says. \"Regulars get the corner ones.\" Behind him, by the ovens, the oldest baker nods as if she arranged it all herself.\n> left: Insist on paying for it\n  gold -tiny\n  charm +small\n  say: He refuses twice and accepts the third time. The coin goes in a jar by the till marked, in the oldest baker's hand, \"for the post\".\n> right: Thank him and eat it warm\n  mood +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: It's still warm and far too big, and you eat it on the step in four bites and burn your mouth. You'd do it again tomorrow.\n\n@event grimsbys_right_book\ntitle: The Right Book, First Time\nplace: grimsbys_books\nclass: milestone\ntone: light\nweight: 30\nwhen: visits_to(grimsbys_books) >= 6\nart: book\ntags: life\nscene: A cramped bookshop of leaning shelves, an elderly bookseller holding out a book to a student who has only just stepped inside.\ntext: You step into Grimsby's and open your mouth, and Mr Grimsby is already holding out a book. It is exactly the one you came for. He looks as astonished as you are. \"Must be getting to know you,\" he says, and then, worried, \"Don't tell anyone.\"\n> left: Buy it before it changes\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: You pay at once, in case it turns into a book on beekeeping. It doesn't. You carry it all the way back to the castle, checking.\n> right: Ask what else he'd pick for me\n  charm +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: Restored to form, Mr Grimsby brings you a history of eels and a book of riddles, and lets you read the riddles in the window seat until closing. They're very good, and no help at all.\n\n@event courtyard_in_the_know\ntitle: In the Know\nplace: courtyard\nclass: milestone\ntone: light\nweight: 30\nwhen: visits_to(courtyard) >= 6\nart: group\ntags: social\nscene: A knot of students gathered on the rim of a courtyard fountain, leaning in, one waving a newcomer over.\ntext: The knot of students by the fountain goes quiet as you pass, and then one of them waves you over. \"You're out here every day,\" says a second-year from Duskgate. \"So. What have you heard?\" It seems you've been counted among the courtyard's regulars, and regulars are expected to bring news.\n> left: Share something harmless\n  charm +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You offer up a rumour about the refectory pudding. It's received with grave interest and is all over the castle by supper, much improved.\n> right: Say I've heard nothing\n  guile +small\n  say: They take your silence as proof that you know everything. By the end of the week, people are asking you for the news.\n"},{"path":"content/events/pools/plans_thesis.grim","text":"# Baseline pools (phase 3, step 1b): break plans, thesis and internship hosts.\n# Repeatable routine events; see docs/authoring/pools.md.\n\n# ---------------- Break: at the castle (plan: castle) ----------------\n\n@event break_castle_one_fire\ntitle: The One Lit Fire\nplan: castle\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: flame\ntags: home\nscene: A cavernous common room in the holidays, a handful of students in blankets crowded around {season == \"winter\": one roaring hearth|one open window}.\ntext: {With the houses empty, the stewards light one common room and let the rest go dark.|Someone has brought a kettle, and now there's a rota for it.|The break common room. You know everyone's blanket by now.} {season == \"winter\": The best armchair is right by the fire.|The best armchair is by the only breeze.} {A first-year from Stillwater is asleep in it.|Footnote is in it. Footnote moves for no one.|The steward's dog is in it, and the steward is on the dog's side.}\n> left: Talk my way into the armchair\n  challenge deceive apprentice:\n    win:\n      mood +small\n      guile +tiny\n      say: {You mention that the kitchens are giving out free toffee. The chair empties. You feel only slightly bad.|You announce, with great authority, that the armchair is due to be re-stuffed. It is, now, by you.|You offer the dog your toast. It's a fair trade, and the chair is still warm.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: {Nobody moves. Someone says, \"Nice try,\" without looking up from their book.|The whole room watches you fail to dislodge the armchair's occupant. It's the most entertainment anyone has had all break.|The dog growls, the steward sides with the dog, and you take the floor.}\n> right: Take the draughty corner\n  lore +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {season == \"winter\": {The corner is cold enough to see your breath, but you read three chapters.|You read in gloves. Turning pages becomes a skill.|You find a blanket nobody claims and read until your nose goes numb.}|{It smells of old ink and warm dust. You read three chapters.|A wasp shares your corner. You reach an understanding.|The breeze turns your pages for you, mostly the right way. You read three chapters anyway.}}\n\n@event break_castle_kitchen_rota\ntitle: The Holiday Kitchen\nplan: castle\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: meal\ntags: home\nscene: A steaming castle kitchen with half its ovens cold, the cook handing aprons to two reluctant students.\ntext: {Half the kitchen staff have gone home for the break, and the cook has started drafting whoever wanders past.|The cook spots you in the corridor and holds up an apron.|The cook holds out an apron without a word. It has your name stitched on it now.} {season == \"winter\": There are puddings to steam for the few who stayed, and a goose that refuses to fit the oven.|The orchard has dropped everything at once, and every pan in the kitchen is full of jam.}\n> left: Roll up your sleeves\n  alchemy +small\n  health -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {season == \"winter\": {You stir puddings till your arm aches. \"Just like potion work,\" says the cook. It isn't.|You baste the goose. The goose wins, narrowly.|The goose fits at last. The cook says it was her idea.}|{A morning of jam teaches you more about setting points than a term of alchemy.|You label forty jars. The cook re-spells \"gooseberry\" on all of them.|You stone plums all morning. Your fingers are purple until Tuesday.}}\n> right: Discover urgent business elsewhere\n  guile +tiny\n  mood +small\n  gold -tiny\n  say: {You escape to the village and buy a pie you could have had for free. The cook sees you come back with the crumbs.|You hide in the village bakery until lunch is over and buy what you could have eaten for free. The cook gives you a meaningful look all evening.|You claim a library emergency. The cook, who knows the library is shut, says nothing and saves you nothing.}\n\n@event break_castle_empty_library\ntitle: The Library on Holiday\nplan: castle\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: book\ntags: home study\nscene: A great library with no one at the desks, a cat asleep on the reference desk and a single student choosing a book.\ntext: {The librarians have gone home, and the library has been left to you and the cat.|Footnote has moved from the reference desk to your chair, and opens one eye.|Your break-morning library. One window seat is yours now.} {season == \"winter\": Frost has drawn ferns on the tall windows.|Dust turns slowly in the long summer light.} {Every desk is free.|Someone has left half an essay on the theory of hiccups.|The reshelving cart is full, and nobody is coming for it.}\n> left: Read whatever I like\n  mood +small\n  lore +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You read a book about the history of spoons and forget to eat lunch. It was a very good book about spoons.|You read until the light goes, then a little after. Your eyes will mention it tomorrow.|You finish the book you started last break. The bookmark was still where you left it.}\n> right: Get ahead on next term\n  lore +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {final_year: You make notes for a term you'll only half get to use. It still feels like the right thing to do.|{You make a neat set of notes for next term. Future you will be grateful. Present you is bored stiff.|You read ahead three chapters and find the professor's favourite mistake waiting in the fourth.|You copy out next term's reading list and get through half of it. The other half looks smug.}}\n\n@event break_castle_post_day\ntitle: Post for the Stay-Behinds\nplan: castle\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: letter\ntags: home\nscene: A porter sorting a thin pile of letters on a long empty table, a few students waiting hopefully.\ntext: {Post day. The porter lays the letters out on one table in the Great Hall.|The porter now sorts the stay-behinds' letters into a pile of their own.|The porter nods to you. He keeps your letters on top now.} {season == \"winter\": {Most of it is Midwinter cards for people who went home.|One parcel is exactly the shape of a scarf.|One envelope has your name on it, in handwriting you know.}|{Most of it is postcards from people's travels.|A postcard says \"Wish you were here\", and nobody knows whose it is.|One envelope has your name on it and smells of the sea.}}\n> left: Write a long letter home\n  mood +small\n  gold -tiny\n  say: {final_year: You write that this is your last break at the castle, and then look at the sentence for a long time.|{You write four pages and pay for the heavy stamp. It's the closest thing to a conversation you've had all week.|You describe the empty castle so well that you feel lonelier writing it than living it.|You write about the porter, the biscuit and the empty hall. It reads like a good life, which it is.}}\n> right: Help the porter sort it all\n  charm +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {The porter tells you who writes to whom, in strict confidence. You know far too much about the second-years now.|You sort the post into houses. There's nothing for you this time. The porter gives you a biscuit, which is nearly as good.|You sort the pile in half the time it took you once. The porter calls you his deputy, and nobody argues.}\n\n# ---------------- Break: at home (plan: home) ----------------\n\n@event break_home_kitchen_table\ntitle: Homework at the Kitchen Table\nplan: home\nslot: morning\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: house\ntags: home study\nscene: A family kitchen in the morning, a student's books spread among the breakfast plates, someone reaching across them for the butter.\ntext: {You spread your books across the kitchen table, where you did your sums as a child.|Your books have been moved for the bread, and one is being used as a trivet.|The family works around your books now. Mostly.} {season == \"winter\": The stove is the only warm place, so everyone is in the kitchen.|The door is open, and everyone passing asks what the book is about.} {A cousin has drawn a dragon in your margin.|The kettle boils every quarter-hour, on principle.|Someone has put a book of yours under a wobbly table leg, and it works.}\n> left: Study through the noise\n  lore +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {You read the same paragraph nine times and learn it perfectly, along with the price of eggs.|You get through a chapter. Your notes now have a jam stain, a crumb and the shopping list on them.|You learn to read with one hand and pass the salt with the other. A whole chapter goes in.}\n> right: Give up and help with lunch\n  mood +small\n  say: {You're sent to the shop for the one thing everyone forgot, and you buy sweets with the change.|You peel, chop and get told about everyone in the village. Your book stays shut, and it's the best morning of the break.|You're on potatoes again. You're told you peel like a scholar, which isn't a compliment.}\n\n@event break_home_the_box\ntitle: The Box Under the Bed\nplan: home\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: key\ntags: home\nscene: A narrow childhood bedroom with a bed far too short for its occupant, an old box of keepsakes pulled out onto the floor.\ntext: {Your old room is smaller than you remember. Under the bed is the box where you kept everything that mattered when you were nine.|Someone has dusted the box since last time and pretended not to look inside.|You've almost reached the bottom of the box.} {This time it's a wand you made from a stick and a lot of string.|This time it's the letter you wrote to the academy at age ten, asking to be let in early.|This time it's a drawing of seven towers, each with a little flag.}\n> left: Pack it in your trunk\n  mood +small\n  say: {It comes back to Lumenhold with you. The trunk is heavier, and so, somehow, are you.|You pack it between your robes. Every time you open the trunk this term, it will be the first thing you see.|You pack it carefully. There's not much left in the box now, and that feels like something ending.}\n> right: Give it to your cousin\n  charm +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {Your cousin, who is applying to the academy next autumn, takes it with enormous seriousness. You feel very old.|Your cousin promises to keep it safe. By supper it has been traded to a neighbour for a jar of honey.|Your cousin says thank you and puts it in a box of their own, under their own bed. You feel very old and quite happy.}\n\n@event break_home_family_debate\ntitle: The Family Debate\nplan: home\nslot: morning\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: meal\ntags: home\nscene: A breakfast table mid-argument, several relatives pointing at a student as if they were the deciding vote.\ntext: {At breakfast the family is arguing, and someone says, \"Ask the scholar.\"|The argument has started without you. Everyone turns as you come down the stairs.|\"The scholar is up,\" someone announces. They've saved today's argument for you.} {season == \"winter\": {Is it colder than the year of the big snow?|Can you freeze a pond by thinking at it?|Can a wizard make the hens lay in winter?}|{Do swallows sleep at the bottom of rivers?|Will it rain on the fair, and can you stop it?|Is the old well really haunted, and should it be?}}\n> left: Settle it with what I've learned\n  chance likely:\n    lore +small\n    mood +small\n    say: {You explain it so clearly that the argument ends. There is a short, respectful silence, and then a new argument starts.|You settle it. An uncle demands to see it in writing. You write it on a napkin, and he keeps the napkin.|You settle it with a diagram on the back of the almanac. It's still pinned by the stove the next time you're home.}\n  else:\n    lore +tiny\n    mood -small\n    say: {You explain it perfectly, and they all decide the academy has taught you nonsense.|Halfway through you realise you're wrong. So does everyone else.|You're right, and you prove it, and nobody believes you, because the almanac says otherwise.}\n> right: Refuse to take sides\n  charm +small\n  say: {You butter your toast with great care and say, \"Hm.\" It does not stop anyone, but it does save you.|You change the subject to the neighbour's fence. It works, but now there are two arguments.|You say both sides have a point. Both sides turn on you. It's the most united the family has been all year.}\n\n@event break_home_village_children\ntitle: The Wizard Is Home\nplan: home\nslot: morning\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: sparkle\ntags: home\nscene: A gaggle of villagers at a cottage gate in the morning, staring hopefully at a student in academy robes.\ntext: {Word has spread that you're home from the wizard school. Half the village has found a reason to pass the gate.|The neighbours are back at the gate, with a cousin from the next village to show you off to.|The gate committee is in session. They've drawn up a list.} {season == \"winter\": They want more snow for the sledging.|They want it to stop being so hot.} {The baker has brought a jar, in case you make something worth keeping.|The miller's wife asks if you've ever met a dragon.|The blacksmith has a stick that might be a wand.}\n> left: Show them some sparks\n  mana -small\n  mood +small\n  arcana +tiny\n  say: {You make a little burst of light over the gate. The neighbours cheer and demand it again, and again.|You make a spark dance across the gatepost. The baker tries to catch it in her apron, or thinks she does, which is the same thing.|You work through the committee's list of sparks, in order. They tick each one off and ask for the list again tomorrow.}\n> right: Teach them something real\n  charm +small\n  say: {You explain how the light gets into a spell. They listen politely and then ask for sparks.|You show them how to sit still and listen to a candle. The old miller really hears it. You can tell.|You make the blacksmith's stick glow for a minute. It isn't a wand, but he'll never believe that now.}\n\n# ---------------- Break: working full-time (plan: work) ----------------\n\n@event break_work_holiday_rush\ntitle: The Holiday Rush\nplan: work\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: coin\ntags: job money\nscene: A holiday rush at a busy workplace, a student in the thick of it wearing the fixed smile of someone on their fourth hour of the same question.\ntext: {The holidays bring the crowds.|This time you've learned to eat breakfast before the rush, not during it.|You can feel the rush coming an hour before it arrives.} {has(gig_newt): The Gilded Newt is packed to the beams.|{has(gig_pell): The queue at Pell & Daughters is out of the door.|{has(gig_post): The loft's shelves are stacked to the rafters with parcels.|{has(gig_library): The library is full of last-minute readers.|{has(gig_kitchens): The Refectory is feeding everyone who stayed, and all their visiting families.|{has(gig_grounds): The grounds are full of visitors walking off their holiday dinners, and every one of them has a question.|Everyone wants something, and now.}}}}}} {season == \"winter\": {Everyone wants it before Midwinter.|Wet boots, short tempers.|Everyone is in a hurry to be somewhere warm.}|{Everyone is sunburnt.|Travellers keep asking the way.|The heat slows everyone down except the queue.}}\n> left: Smile through it\n  charm +small\n  gold +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: {You smile until your face aches. Somebody tips you a coin and a peppermint.|You smile through the fourth complaint about the weather, as if it were your fault. Somehow the tips are better than ever.|You have a smile for every complaint now, sorted by type. The tips follow.}\n> right: Take the heavy jobs instead\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You haul the heaviest loads all morning and talk to no one. Your arms complain, and nobody else does.|You take the dull, heavy work nobody wants. It's tiring and blissfully quiet.|You know where every heavy thing goes by now. You're done by noon, and nobody has spoken to you. Perfect.}\n\n@event break_work_opening_up\ntitle: Opening Up\nplan: work\nwhen: has(gig_newt) or has(gig_pell)\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: key\ntags: job\nscene: A student unlocking a shop door in the half-light of early morning, breath steaming, the street empty behind them.\ntext: {In the holidays you're trusted with the keys. You open up at dawn, alone.|You've learned to lift the door as you turn the key.|You could open up in your sleep, and once you nearly did.} {season == \"winter\": It's still dark, and the stove has gone out.|The sun is up and the street smells of warm stone.} {There's a note under the door from someone who wanted to come in at midnight.|A cat waits on the step as if it has an appointment.|The place is yours for half an hour.}\n> left: Do it all by the book\n  gold +small\n  mood -tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {Swept, stocked and opened to the minute. The owner notices and slips you a little extra.|Everything is ready when the first customer arrives. Nobody notices but you. Still, the coin is good.|You open up to the minute, as always. The owner has stopped checking.}\n> right: Steal a quiet half hour\n  chance likely:\n    mood +small\n    say: {You drink tea by the window and watch the street wake up. It's the best part of the job.|You read a chapter with your feet up. Nobody ever knows.|There's a chair by the window that's yours now. Half an hour of nothing, and nobody the wiser.}\n  else:\n    mood -tiny\n    gold -small\n    say: {The owner arrives early and finds you with your feet up. The half hour comes out of your wages.|The first customer arrives early and finds the door locked. They leave a note. So does the owner.|The owner has taken to arriving early just to check, and today it pays off. The half hour comes out of your wages.}\n\n@event break_work_regular\ntitle: The Holiday Regular\nplan: work\nwhen: has(gig_newt) or has(gig_pell)\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: cup\ntags: job\nscene: An elderly customer settling in for a long chat at a counter, a student behind it glancing at the queue.\ntext: {Every holiday has its regular. This one comes every morning and wants to talk.|The regular is back. They wave to you as if you were old friends.|The regular has started calling you by your first name.} {Today it's about their late husband's beard.|Today it's about a dragon they swear they saw as a girl.|Today it's about the year the lake froze, which may or may not have happened.} The queue behind them is getting longer.\n> left: Let them finish the story\n  charm +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You hear the whole story, standing, while the queue thins out to somewhere else. Your feet ache by the end. The regular leaves happy, and so, oddly, are you.|The story has an ending you didn't expect, and you'll think about it all day. You stood through all of it, and your back says so.|You know how their stories end by now, and you let them tell it anyway. The queue waits. Your feet don't forgive you.}\n> right: Hurry them gently along\n  gold +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: {You serve the queue and the regular leaves, mid-sentence, with a little wave. It shouldn't bother you as much as it does.|The queue is grateful. The regular says, \"Another time, then,\" and you both know they mean it.|You keep the queue moving and promise the regular the rest tomorrow. They hold you to it, every time.}\n\n@event break_work_new_hire\ntitle: The Holiday Hire\nplan: work\nwhen: not has(gig_grounds) and not has(gig_post)\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: hand\ntags: job\nscene: A nervous new worker holding a tray at the wrong angle, a student leaning over to correct them.\ntext: {The boss has taken on a holiday hire and put them next to you.|Another holiday hire, another nervous face. You're the old hand now.|This break's new hire looks at you the way you once looked at everyone.} {They've already dropped something.|They ask whether the job is always like this. You don't answer.|They're very keen and very fast, and very nearly right.}\n> left: Show them the ropes\n  charm +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {By noon they know where everything is and that the boss is all bark. You've lost a morning and gained a friend.|You teach them the trick with the till. They teach you a song. You both get told off for the song.|You show them everything you wish someone had shown you. By the end of the day they're showing the next one.}\n> right: Let them learn the hard way\n  mood +tiny\n  gold -tiny\n  say: {They learn. Unfortunately, they learn by breaking a jar that the boss puts on your account.|You let them flounder. It takes them all day to find the stockroom, and you end up covering their shift anyway. The boss takes the stock they spoiled out of your pay.|They learn fast, mostly from your mistakes, which they found in the ledger. The boss bills you for the latest one.}\n\n# ---------------- Break: internship (plan: internship) ----------------\n\n@event break_internship_lodgings\ntitle: Breakfast at the Lodgings\nplan: internship\nslot: morning\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: bed\ntags: home\nscene: A borrowed bed at dawn, boots by the door, a bowl of breakfast waiting and a notebook open on the blanket.\ntext: {{member(intern_expedition): Your lodging for the placement is a tent that flaps all night.|{member(intern_field_station): Your lodging is a bunk in the station cabin, under a shelf of jars.|Your lodging is one small room and a breakfast you pay for.}}|By now you can make any lodging home in an evening, a book on the sill and your boots by the door.|You've slept in enough borrowed beds to know breakfast matters more than the mattress.} {season == \"winter\": {Your breath steams.|The water jug has frozen.|Ice on the inside of the window.}|{It's warm before sunrise.|A bird is shouting outside.|The window is open, and someone's breakfast is frying.}} {Breakfast is porridge.|Breakfast is porridge with opinions.|Breakfast is unnamed.}\n> left: Eat a proper breakfast\n  mood +small\n  gold -tiny\n  say: {You eat slowly and listen to everyone's gossip. You arrive late enough to be noticed.|Fed, warm and five minutes late. Your supervisor looks at the clock and says nothing at all.|You eat everything and ask for the recipe. You're late, and your supervisor has started to expect it.}\n> right: Skip it and go in early\n  lore +small\n  health -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You're the first one in. You get through a pile of work before anyone else arrives, and your stomach growls through every meeting after.|You read the day's notes on the way. By lunchtime you're faint and well informed.|You go in early with an apple in your pocket. It's gone by the first meeting, and the hunger stays until supper.}\n\n@event break_internship_briefing\ntitle: The Morning Briefing\nplan: internship\nslot: morning\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: scroll\ntags: study\nscene: A supervisor reading a list of tasks aloud to a row of interns, one of whom has stopped writing.\ntext: {Your supervisor reads the day's tasks aloud, once, very fast.|You bring two pens now, and a spare for the intern beside you.|You've learned to read the briefing from the way your supervisor holds the list.} {One task makes no sense at all.|One task seems to mean the opposite of what it says.|The last task is mumbled into a cup of tea.} {final_year: It's your last placement, and you want it to count.|The others all write as if they understood.}\n> left: Ask what it means\n  charm +tiny\n  lore +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {Your supervisor explains it again, slowly, while the other interns smirk. Two of them come to you at lunch to ask what it meant.|\"Good question,\" says your supervisor, in a tone that suggests it wasn't. You get the task right, anyway.|You ask before anyone else can pretend. Your supervisor answers, and three pens around you start writing.}\n> right: Guess and get on with it\n  chance even:\n    lore +small\n    mood +tiny\n    say: {You guess right. Your supervisor nods as if they never doubted you.|It turns out the odd task was \"take the rest of the morning off\". Lucky. You spend it reading in the archive.|You guess, and you've guessed this one before. The task is done before anyone else has finished asking.}\n  else:\n    mood -small\n    say: {You guess wrong and spend the morning carefully doing the opposite of what was wanted.|You did the right task to the wrong thing. The wrong thing will never be the same again.|Your guess is confident, thorough and wrong. Your supervisor reads the result twice and asks you, kindly, to ask next time.}\n\n@event break_internship_other_interns\ntitle: The Other Interns\nplan: internship\nslot: evening\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: group\ntags: study\nscene: A small group of interns eating supper together, one sitting slightly apart with a notebook.\ntext: {There are other interns here, and they've already formed a supper table.|You've learned to find the supper table on the first day, and which seat isn't anyone's.|By now you can tell within a morning which intern is the one to sit beside.} {One has a cousin on the Conclave and mentions it hourly.|One is better than all of you, and very nice about it.|One has been here before and knows where the biscuits are.} {season == \"winter\": Supper is eaten in coats.|Supper is eaten in the last of the sun.}\n> left: Join them for supper\n  mood +small\n  charm +tiny\n  gold -tiny\n  say: {You chip in for the shared food and find out which of the supervisors are kind. Useful, and fun.|You talk shop until your supper is cold. It turns out everyone else is as lost as you are, which helps.|You sit beside the one who knows where the biscuits are. By pudding you know where everything else is too.}\n> right: Eat alone with your notes\n  lore +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {You finish the reading for tomorrow. You hear laughter from the table, and you don't look up.|You get ahead of everyone. Nobody saves you a biscuit.|You eat with your notes again. Someone leaves a biscuit by your elbow without a word.}\n\n@event break_internship_stipend\ntitle: Stipend Day\nplan: internship\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: pouch\ntags: money\nscene: A small envelope of coins on a desk, a student looking at it with the expression of someone doing sums.\ntext: {Stipend day. A small envelope, your name spelled almost right.|You've learned to budget the envelope before you open it.|Stipend day. You know what it's for before it arrives, and it still feels like a gift.} {member(intern_expedition) or member(intern_field_station): {The supply cart has come, with a pedlar riding on top.|A pedlar has walked a very long way to sell you socks.|The pedlar is back, and has heard about you.}|{season == \"winter\": {There's a warm scarf in a shop window you pass.|A bookshop nearby has a sale on almanacs.|The baker on the corner has started putting one aside on stipend day.}|{A cart is selling lemon ices on the corner.|A bookshop nearby has put its sale out in the sun.|The baker on the corner sells the last of the cherry buns at noon.}}}\n> left: Treat yourself\n  mood +small\n  gold -small\n  say: {member(intern_expedition) or member(intern_field_station): {You buy the pedlar's thickest socks. Your feet have never been so grateful.|You buy a jar of honey from the pedlar and eat it with a spoon, in triumph.|You buy the pedlar's whole stock of liquorice. He says you're his best customer, and you probably are.}|{season == \"winter\": {You buy it. It's quite unnecessary, and the warmest you've felt all week.|You spend the lot before lunch and regret none of it.|You spend it on something warm and something sweet, and keep a single coin back for luck.}|{You spend it before the corner and don't regret a crown.|You take your purchase to a bench in the sun and stay until your nose burns.|You buy the last cherry bun and a book you don't need, and eat one while reading the other.}}}\n> right: Save it and borrow the book\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You put the envelope away and borrow the book instead. It's the dullest thing you've ever read, and the most useful. You copy out half of it the first night.|The envelope goes under your mattress. You read the borrowed book, and the next time your supervisor mentions it you nod in the right places.|The envelope joins the others under the mattress. The pile is getting interesting, and so is the book.}\n\n# ---------------- Thesis (track: thesis) ----------------\n\n@event thesis_margin_notes\ntitle: Notes in the Margin\ntrack: thesis\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: scroll\ntags: study\nscene: A thesis draft returned with a professor's spiky handwriting crawling up every margin.\ntext: {Professor Thorne returns your chapter. In the margin of page one he has written \"No.\"|Another chapter back. The margins say \"Why?\", \"Source?\" and, once, \"Hm.\"|Thorne's notes are getting shorter. You can't decide whether that's good.} {Page six has a single word underlined three times.|One note just says \"See me.\"|There's a tick on page nine. You stare at it for a while.} {progress(thesis) >= 60: The argument is nearly there, and he knows it.|There's a long way to go.}\n> left: Rewrite to his notes\n  progress thesis +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {You rewrite every page he marked. The chapter is shorter and better, and you hate that he was right.|You take every note. When he reads it again he crosses out only one thing, and you could cry.|You rewrite to his notes without arguing now. It saves a week, and he's usually right, which still annoys you.}\n> right: Defend my version\n  challenge negotiate using lore vs thorne.lore -2:\n    win:\n      progress thesis +small\n      rel thorne +tiny\n      say: {You make your case at his office door. He listens, says \"Hm,\" and crosses out his own note.|You argue for your paragraph and he lets you keep it. \"Then make it say so,\" he says, which is almost praise.|You defend the paragraph with a source he hasn't read. He reads it overnight and gives you the paragraph.}\n    lose:\n      progress thesis -tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: {You argue. Thorne waits until you've finished, then points at the sentence that proves him right.|He hears you out, then writes a new note in the margin. It says \"No.\" again, in smaller letters.|You argue. He agrees with every point and still writes \"No.\" You've begun to find this comforting.}\n\n@event thesis_carrel_neighbour\ntitle: The Next Carrel\ntrack: thesis\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: candle\ntags: study\nscene: Two thesis carrels side by side, the partition between them covered in pinned notes and a shared candle.\ntext: {Your carrel neighbour, a final-year from Rootward, reads aloud under their breath.|Your carrel neighbour now reads your chapter titles aloud too.|You and your carrel neighbour now speak a language of sighs.} {They're stuck on chapter three.|They've found a source that disagrees with you both.|They pass you half a sandwich over the partition.} {season == \"winter\": The thesis lab is cold enough for gloves.|Someone's notes are escaping through the window.}\n> left: Swap drafts for an hour\n  chance likely:\n    progress thesis +small\n    charm +tiny\n    mood -tiny\n    say: {They find the hole in your argument in ten minutes. You find three in theirs. Everyone feels better and worse.|They read your chapter and say, \"Oh, I see.\" You're not sure you did, until now.|You swap drafts over the partition, sandwich crumbs and all. Both chapters come back better.}\n  else:\n    mood -small\n    say: {They love your chapter, which helps nobody. You spend the hour fixing their commas.|They ask so many questions that you start doubting your title.|Their notes on your chapter are longer than your chapter. You lose the afternoon reading them.}\n> right: Hunt a source in the stacks\n  lore +small\n  progress thesis +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You find it on the bottom shelf, under a dictionary and a sleeping cat. It says exactly what you hoped, in a footnote.|Three hours of climbing ladders for one paragraph. It's a very good paragraph.|You find the source and a better one beside it. You bring both back and share the second.}\n\n@event thesis_is_it_good\ntitle: Is It Any Good?\ntrack: thesis\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: hourglass\ntags: study\nscene: A student at a carrel in early light, holding a thick draft at arm's length as if it might bite.\ntext: {Halfway through rereading your thesis, you can't tell if it's brilliant or obvious.|The doubt has learned your timetable. It comes the morning after good work.|You know this doubt now. It reaches your carrel first.} {progress(thesis) >= 60: Most of it is written, which almost makes it worse.|So much of it is still unwritten.} {final_year and season == \"spring\": After this term there are no more terms.|{First-years laugh in the courtyard below.|Last night's candle is a puddle.|Someone two carrels down sighs the same sigh.}}\n> left: Push on regardless\n  progress thesis +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {You write a page you don't believe in. By lunch you believe in about half of it, which is a start.|You keep going. The doubt keeps you company, and gets bored by evening.|You write through it, as you have before. By evening the doubt has gone quiet, and the page is good.}\n> right: Walk by the lake instead\n  mood +small\n  health +tiny\n  progress thesis -tiny\n  say: {You walk around the lake and come back with no new pages and one good idea. You'll find out tomorrow if it's good.|The lake is mirror-still and has no opinion on your thesis. It's a relief.|You walk the long way round the lake. The doubt can't keep up, and you come back with a sentence that works.}\n\n# ---------------- The Thesis Laboratory (place: thesis_lab) ----------------\n\n@event thesis_lab_spare_carrel\ntitle: The Spare Carrel\nplace: thesis_lab\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: candle\ntags: study\nscene: A long room of wooden carrels, one at the end empty but for a candle stub and a stray page of footnotes.\ntext: {There's an empty carrel at the end of the row, with a candle stub and nobody's name on it.|The spare carrel is still unclaimed. You've started thinking of its candle stub as yours.|You know the carrels by their scratches now. The spare one has a new scratch, and you suspect it's yours.} {season == \"winter\": Everyone writes in fingerless gloves.|The windows are open, and a footnote blows off someone's desk.} {Somewhere a quill scratches without stopping.|Someone is whispering \"however\" over and over.|A candle gutters out three carrels down, and someone groans.}\n> left: Work until the candle dies\n  lore +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {You write until the stub drowns in its own wax. Your notes are good, and your neck will remember them.|You read one book and half of another. The room's silence does half the work for you.|You work until the stub gives out and finish the paragraph by feel. It's better than the ones you could see.}\n> right: Just sit in the quiet\n  chance likely:\n    mood +small\n    say: {You sit with your hands in your lap and listen to everyone else think. It's oddly restful.|You doze off with your cheek on someone's old footnotes. Nobody minds.|You sit in the quiet and the argument rearranges itself while you're not looking. You write it down before it changes its mind.}\n  else:\n    mood -tiny\n    say: {An advisor looks into the carrel and asks what you're working on. \"Thinking,\" you say. He writes that down.|A final-year asks if you'd mind the corner. Their own candle has given out.|An advisor finds you sitting still and lectures you on idleness for exactly as long as you'd have sat.}\n\n@event thesis_lab_footnote_war\ntitle: The Footnote War\nplace: thesis_lab\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: scroll\ntags: study\nscene: Two advisors arguing across a row of carrels, final-year students peering over the partitions to watch.\ntext: {Two advisors are arguing about a footnote across three carrels, and every final-year in the room has stopped pretending to work.|The footnote war has broken out again. Someone has drawn up sides on a scrap of paper.|By now the footnote war has rules, a referee and a running score chalked on the door.} {Today it's about whether an old founders' letter counts as a source.|Today it's about a comma.|Today it's about a footnote to a footnote.}\n> left: Weigh in with a source\n  challenge research expert-:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      renown +tiny\n      say: {You produce the source, open at the right page. Both advisors fall silent, and one of them asks for your name.|Your citation settles it. The loser shakes your hand and then rereads the page for an hour.|Your source wins the round. The referee chalks a point on the door under a new column, which has your name on it.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      renown -tiny\n      say: {Your source turns out to be the one they were both arguing against. They unite, briefly, to explain why.|You cite the wrong edition. The room winces as one.|Your source is ruled out of order by the referee, on a technicality you'll be studying for weeks.}\n> right: Keep my head down and listen\n  lore +small\n  say: {You learn more about sources from ten minutes of the war than from a term of lectures, and you never have to say a word.|You take notes on both sides. Neither of them would like how fair your notes are.|You keep your head down and your notebook open. The war is the best seminar in the building, and it's free.}\n\n@event thesis_lab_borrowed_candle\ntime: evening\ntitle: Borrowing a Candle\nplace: thesis_lab\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: candle\ntags: study\nscene: A dark carrel lit by one dying candle, a full box of candles on the empty desk next door.\ntext: {The candles here give out at the worst moment. Yours just has, and the carrel next door has a full box and nobody in it.|Your candle gives out again. Next door, the usual box of spares and nobody in the chair.|The lab's candles have outlasted no one's patience tonight, least of all yours. There's a box next door.} {A draft lies open beside the box, face up.|A note on the box says \"Help yourself\" in one hand and \"Don't\" in another.|Someone has drawn a small eye on the box.}\n> left: Borrow one and keep working\n  alignment -tiny\n  chance likely:\n    lore +small\n    mood -tiny\n    say: {You light it and work until the bell. You leave a note that says \"IOU one candle\", unsigned.|One candle, two good hours. You put the stub back, which is either honest or worse.|You borrow one and replace it the next morning with two. The box owner has started leaving the lid open.}\n  else:\n    mood -small\n    renown -tiny\n    say: {The owner comes back for a forgotten scarf and finds you lit by their candle. They say nothing, which is worse.|By morning the whole lab knows who borrowed the candle. Someone leaves a box on your desk, with a ribbon.|The box is counted now, and the count is short. A note appears asking the borrower to come forward. You don't.}\n> right: Go back in the dark\n  mood +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You walk back across the bridges in the dark, your argument going round and round. It is better by the time you reach your bed.|You stub your toe twice on the way out. The idea you had on the stairs was worth both.|You know the way back in the dark by now. The argument walks with you and arrives a step ahead.}\n\n# ---------------- Internship: the Royal Library Archives ----------------\n\n@event intern_royal_library_slips\ntitle: Request Slips\ntrack: intern_royal_library\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: paper\ntags: study\nscene: A brass chute dropping paper slips into a basket at the top of a deep stairwell lined with shelves.\ntext: {Scholars upstairs send their requests down a brass chute. Your job is to fetch them from the stacks, which go down for miles.|You've worn a path in the dust of the third landing.|You can read the chute's rattle now. A long rattle means someone wants something from the very bottom.} {One slip is in a hand so old the ink has gone brown.|One slip asks for \"the blue one\".|One slip is a love letter, sent down by mistake.}\n> left: Take the deep requests\n  wage\n  perform intern_royal_library +small\n  lore +small\n  health -small\n  say: {Four hundred steps down, the air is cold enough to keep books for ever. You come back up with the book and a lot of opinions about stairs.|You find the volume wedged behind a pipe. The scholar who asked for it nearly weeps.|You take the bottom slip without looking at it. Down there, you know the shelves better than the catalogue does.}\n> right: Take the near shelves\n  wage\n  perform intern_royal_library -tiny\n  lore +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {You fetch the easy ones and leave the deep slips for someone else. Someone else notices.|You're fast and pleasant, and everyone knows you've never been below the third landing.|You clear the near shelves twice over. The long rattles go to someone else, who looks at you as they pass.}\n\n@event intern_royal_library_mending\ntitle: The Mending Table\ntrack: intern_royal_library\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: book\ntags: study\nscene: A bright worktable under a lamp, a crumbling manuscript laid out in pieces and a student holding fine tweezers.\ntext: {Today you're at the mending table, where crumbling records are put back together one flake at a time.|The binder hands you the tweezers without a word, which counts as a greeting.|The binder trusts you with the older pages now, which are the worst.} {The page is a royal decree on the correct length of goose necks.|The page is a recipe, half eaten by something.|The page is a list of names, one crossed out very hard.}\n> left: Mend it myself\n  wage\n  challenge research journeyman+ steady:\n    win:\n      perform intern_royal_library +small\n      lore +small\n      say: {Every flake goes back where it belongs. The binder holds it up to the lamp and says, \"Acceptable,\" which here is a medal.|You save the page and read it while the glue dries. It's better than you expected.|You put the list back together, crossed-out name and all. The binder reads the name and says nothing for a while.}\n    lose:\n      perform intern_royal_library -small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {A sneeze. The binder looks at the scattered flakes and then at you. You both get the tweezers.|One flake ends up in the wrong century. Nobody can prove it was you, but everybody knows.|The oldest flake crumbles at your touch. The binder writes \"lost in repair\" in the ledger, in your hand.}\n> right: Watch the binder work\n  wage\n  lore +small\n  perform intern_royal_library -tiny\n  say: {You watch for an hour and learn more than a book could tell you. The binder says you're slow. You were.|The binder works without speaking and you learn by watching. It's very peaceful and very little gets done.|You watch the binder mend the page you'd have ruined. Next time, you'll know how. That's the idea.}\n\n# ---------------- Internship: the Conclave Laboratory ----------------\n\n@event intern_conclave_test_chamber\ntitle: The Test Chamber\ntrack: intern_conclave\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: wand\ntags: study magic\nscene: A circular chamber with scorch-marked walls, a student at the centre with a wand raised and researchers watching through thick glass.\ntext: {The Conclave's researchers need someone to cast their newest spell for the first time. The walls are thick for a reason.|Someone has chalked a new circle on the floor. The old one is scorched.|You know every scorch mark in the chamber by now, and who made each one.} {Today's spell is meant to make a candle burn backwards.|Today's spell is meant to tidy a room. It has opinions about what tidy means.|Today's spell is unnamed. Nobody will say why.}\n> left: Step into the chamber\n  wage\n  challenge enchant using arcana adept:\n    win:\n      perform intern_conclave +small\n      arcana +small\n      say: {The spell works on the first try. Behind the glass, someone writes your name in the margin of their notes.|It works, more or less, for one glorious minute before the spell remembers itself.|The spell works, and nobody behind the glass will say what it was for. They thank you very politely.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      perform intern_conclave -tiny\n      say: {The spell goes off sideways. Your eyebrows are fine. Your fringe has views of its own now.|The spell goes off sideways, and your robes will never fold the same way again.|The spell goes off sideways and adds a new scorch mark to the wall. Someone labels it with your name.}\n> right: Take readings behind the glass\n  wage\n  arcana +tiny\n  perform intern_conclave +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: {You write down numbers for three hours while someone else gets to be brave. They're very good numbers.|You note every flicker. A senior researcher reads your page and asks for your notes next time.|You take the readings for a spell nobody will name. The numbers are strange, and you're asked to forget them.}\n\n@event intern_conclave_minutes\ntitle: Taking the Minutes\ntrack: intern_conclave\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: scroll\ntags: study magic\nscene: A long table of elderly mages in heavy robes, all talking at once, a young intern writing furiously at the end.\ntext: {You've been asked to take the minutes of a Conclave research committee. There are nine members, and none of them ever agrees.|The committee is still arguing about last time's question, and now about the minutes too.|You could take these minutes in your sleep, and some of the committee do attend them that way.} {Two of them are not speaking to each other, and want it recorded.|One of them asks what you think.|One of them falls asleep, and wakes up to vote.}\n> left: Offer an opinion when asked\n  wage\n  chance even:\n    perform intern_conclave +small\n    renown +tiny\n    mood -tiny\n    say: {The table goes silent. Then the oldest mage says, \"The intern is right,\" and the rest pretend they agreed all along.|Your opinion settles the argument. It also starts two new ones.|You give your opinion and the sleeper wakes up to agree with it. It carries, seven to two.}\n  else:\n    perform intern_conclave -tiny\n    mood -small\n    say: {Nine heads turn towards you, slowly. You say something about wands. It isn't minuted.|You give your opinion and it is recorded in full, with the note \"(intern)\".|Your opinion is noted, discussed, voted down and then adopted next meeting without credit.}\n> right: Keep my head down and write\n  wage\n  perform intern_conclave +small\n  lore +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: {Your minutes are perfect and so dull that nobody reads them, which in a committee is the highest praise.|You record every word of the argument. Your hand aches, and you understand the Conclave much better than you wanted to.|You write it all down, including the vote of the member who slept through it. The minutes are accurate, which nobody wanted.}\n\n# ---------------- Internship: the Guild of Alchemists' Works ----------------\n\n@event intern_alchemists_vat_watch\ntitle: Vat Watch\ntrack: intern_alchemists\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: cauldron\ntags: study\nscene: A huge copper vat in a steaming hall, a student on a gantry staring into it with a colour chart in hand.\ntext: {Your task at the works is to watch one great vat and call the foreman if it changes colour.|You've brought your own colour chart now, with notes in the margins.|You and the vat understand each other now. It's an unsettling feeling.} {The amber is drifting, very slowly.|There's a bubble that keeps coming back, like a thought.|The vat smells faintly of cinnamon, which it never has before.} The foreman is all the way at the other end of the hall.\n> left: Adjust it myself\n  wage\n  challenge brew adept-:\n    win:\n      perform intern_alchemists +small\n      alchemy +small\n      say: {A pinch of salt and a slow stir, and the vat settles back to its proper self. The foreman never knew, but the vat did.|You correct it before it goes wrong. When the foreman passes, he gives the vat a satisfied look as if he did it.|You trace the cinnamon to a cracked spice valve and close it. The vat settles, and the foreman finds the valve next week.}\n    lose:\n      perform intern_alchemists -small\n      health -tiny\n      say: {The vat turns a brilliant violet and hisses. The foreman arrives at a run, and you'll be hearing about it for weeks.|You add too much salt. The vat sulks a muddy brown until the foreman coaxes it back, at length, with you watching.|You make it worse. A lot worse. The whole hall smells of cinnamon until the end of the week.}\n> right: Run for the foreman\n  wage\n  perform intern_alchemists +tiny\n  alchemy +tiny\n  mood -small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You run the length of the hall. The foreman fixes it in a second and explains how, at length, as you catch your breath.|By the time you're back with the foreman the colour has corrected itself. He stares at you, then at the vat.|You run for the foreman, who sniffs the air, says \"Cinnamon,\" and goes very pale. He fixes it, and doesn't explain.}\n\n@event intern_alchemists_no_flame\ntitle: The Rule About Flames\ntrack: intern_alchemists\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: cup\ntags: study\nscene: A break room in an alchemical works, workers gathered around a kettle sitting on a glowing stone that is not, technically, on fire.\ntext: {There's a strict rule at the works against naked flames, and against any heating reaction the foreman hasn't approved. The break room has spent forty years looking for one he will.|Break time. The kettle sits on its warm stone, and the workers look at you hopefully.|By now the break room asks the academy intern first, whatever the problem.} {Today the warm stone has gone cold.|Today someone wants toast, which is a much bigger question.|Today a new inspector is touring the works.}\n> left: Help brew the flameless tea\n  wage\n  chance likely:\n    alchemy +small\n    mood +small\n    say: {You heat the kettle with a reaction you learned in your first year. The break room calls it \"the academy method\" and adopts it on the spot.|It works, though everything boiled on it comes out a little purple.|You brew the tea with the new inspector in the room, and explain the method so confidently that he takes notes.}\n  else:\n    perform intern_alchemists -small\n    mood -tiny\n    say: {Your method works so well that someone in charge notices the steam from a reaction nobody approved. There's a meeting about it, with you in it.|The kettle boils, and so, briefly, does the table.|The inspector walks in mid-reaction. There's a form for it, and you fill it in.}\n> right: Stick to the rules\n  wage\n  perform intern_alchemists +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: {You drink cold water while everyone else drinks tea brewed on an unapproved reaction. The foreman notes your conduct, and the break room notes it too.|You remind everyone of the rule. You are absolutely right and nobody offers you a biscuit for the rest of the week.|You keep to the rules with the inspector watching. He commends you by name, and the break room never forgives it.}\n\n# ---------------- Internship: the Observatory Expedition ----------------\n\n@event intern_expedition_thin_air\ntitle: Up the Ridge\ntrack: intern_expedition\nslot: evening\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: compass\ntags: study\nscene: A line of expedition members climbing a rocky ridge at dusk, carrying brass instruments wrapped in blankets.\ntext: {The instruments have to go up to the ridge before dark, and the air on the plateau is very thin.|Your legs know the path now. Your lungs are still negotiating.|You've carried things up this ridge so often that the goats have stopped looking at you.} {season == \"winter\": {Snow has drifted against the path.|The wind cuts through every layer you own.|Ice has glazed the steps cut into the rock.}|{The rocks are still hot from the afternoon.|The sky is clear all the way to the top of the world.|The evening wind smells of thyme and stone.}}\n> left: Carry the great lens\n  wage\n  perform intern_expedition +small\n  vigor +tiny\n  health -small\n  say: {You carry the great lens to the top without dropping it, then sit very still for a long time. The leader nods at you, once.|Your arms are numb by the top, and the lens is perfect. It's worth it when the first star shows in it.|You carry the lens up without thinking about it. Nobody watches you any more, which is the compliment.}\n> right: Do the sums in the tent\n  wage\n  lore +small\n  perform intern_expedition -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You work the night's sums by lamplight, warm and quiet. The lamp gutters twice. Your sums don't.|You have the charts ready when they come down. Nobody thanks you, because the charts are always ready.|You do the sums in the tent, as usual. The numbers are good, and so is the stove.}\n\n@event intern_expedition_chart_dispute\ntitle: A Star Out of Place\ntrack: intern_expedition\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: star\ntags: study\nscene: Two expedition members bent over a star chart by lamplight, one pointing firmly at a dot.\ntext: {Your chart and the senior astronomer's disagree about where one faint star is.|The senior astronomer's charts and yours have a running disagreement now, kept in red ink.|The senior astronomer now checks your charts first. A compliment, possibly.} {Probably your hand shook.|Probably their eyesight.|Probably the thin air.} {season == \"winter\": The nights are long, with time to recheck everything.|The nights are short, and every hour counts.}\n> left: Recheck it all night\n  wage\n  lore +small\n  perform intern_expedition +tiny\n  health -small\n  say: {You recheck it until dawn. You were right by a hair, and nobody will ever care but you.|You were wrong by a hair. You fix it, and know the sky better than you did.|You recheck by lamplight and find the air was bending the star. You note it, and the astronomer copies your note.}\n> right: Raise it with the astronomer\n  wage\n  chance even:\n    perform intern_expedition +small\n    charm +tiny\n    say: {The astronomer checks, grumbles and corrects their chart. Then they buy you a cup of the camp's terrible coffee.|\"Well spotted,\" says the astronomer, through their teeth.|You raise it, and the astronomer has already checked it. \"You first, this time,\" they say.}\n  else:\n    perform intern_expedition -small\n    mood -tiny\n    say: {The astronomer checks and shows you, in front of everyone, exactly where you went wrong.|They don't check. They just look at you over their spectacles until you go away.|The astronomer blames the thin air and refuses to discuss it further. The star stays where they put it.}\n\n# ---------------- Internship: the Beastcraft Field Station ----------------\n\n@event intern_field_station_count\ntitle: The Dawn Count\ntrack: intern_field_station\nslot: morning\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: feather\ntags: study nature\nscene: A student lying flat in the heather at dawn, a tally board in hand, watching nesting towers on the moor.\ntext: {Every morning someone has to lie in the heather and count what's living on the moor. Today it's you.|You've learned which tussock is the least wet, and you guard it.|You're the station's best counter now, mostly because you've stopped minding the damp.} {season == \"winter\": {Snow hares are out, white on white.|Frost has made the heather crackle under you.|A fox is counting the same hares.}|{The skylarks are going up one after another.|The midges have found you.|The heather is warm, and so is your tussock.}}\n> left: Count every last one\n  wage\n  perform intern_field_station +small\n  vigor +tiny\n  health -small\n  say: {You count until your fingers are numb and the tally is perfect. The warden reads it twice and pins it on the wall.|You stay out until the very last nest is counted. You come back soaked and very proud of the number.|You count them all from your tussock. The warden copies your tally into the station book without checking it.}\n> right: Make a sensible estimate\n  wage\n  chance likely:\n    lore +small\n    mood +tiny\n    say: {You mark it \"estimated\" in the margin, the way the station counts the far nests anyway. The warden accepts it, and you spend the extra hour by the stove with a cup of tea.|The warden checks your estimate against his own count and nods. He does the same on wet mornings.|Your estimate is close enough that the warden uses it for the far nests too. You get the stove first.}\n  else:\n    perform intern_field_station -small\n    mood -tiny\n    say: {The warden counted too. His number is not your number. He makes a note.|Your estimate includes a flock that left last week. The warden is very nice about it, which is worse.|Your estimate misses a new colony on the far slope. The warden found it yesterday and was waiting to see if you would.}\n\n@event intern_field_station_patient\ntitle: The Patient in Paddock Three\ntrack: intern_field_station\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: paw\ntags: study nature\nscene: A muddy paddock with a splinted creature glaring from a straw nest, a student holding out a bucket at arm's length.\ntext: {The station's latest patient is a moor-drake with a splinted wing and no gratitude at all.|Feeding time in paddock three. The patient sees you coming and lowers its head.|The drake in paddock three recognises you now. This hasn't made it any nicer.} {It has eaten a glove.|It is pretending to sleep, which it does very badly.|It has learned to open the bucket by itself.} {season == \"winter\": Its breath steams in the frosty air.|It is lying in the one patch of shade.}\n> left: Feed it by hand\n  wage\n  chance likely:\n    perform intern_field_station +small\n    vigor +small\n    say: {It takes the food from your hand, very gently. Then it bites the bucket, to make a point.|It lets you check the splint, grumbling the whole time like an old man at the barber's.|It eats from your hand and then opens the bucket to check you haven't kept any back.}\n  else:\n    health -small\n    vigor +tiny\n    say: {It takes the food, a bit of your sleeve and a bruise's worth of your arm.|It butts you into the mud and then looks pleased with itself.|It opens the bucket itself and eats the lot, then bites you for being slow.}\n> right: Use the long tongs\n  wage\n  perform intern_field_station +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: {You feed it from a safe distance. It eats, and looks at you with deep contempt.|The tongs work perfectly. You feel you've let the patient down, somehow.|It takes the tongs off you and drops them in the mud. You feed it by hand after all.}\n\n# ---------------- Internship: the Thornwick Exchange Lab ----------------\n\n@event intern_thornwick_pastries\ntitle: Thornwick Hospitality\ntrack: intern_thornwick\nslot: morning\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: mask\ntags: study\nscene: A polished laboratory table at a rival academy, a plate of perfect pastries set out for a visiting student.\ntext: {Each morning your Thornwick hosts leave pastries on your desk, with a polite note.|The notes have grown longer and politer, which is worrying.|The pastries are a game now, and both sides know it.} {They look perfect, which in an illusion lab is a warning.|One is steaming slightly, which it shouldn't be.|Today there's one plain biscuit, which is somehow worse.} {season == \"winter\": The pastries look very warm.|The icing hasn't melted in the heat.}\n> left: Eat one and smile\n  wage\n  chance likely:\n    mood +small\n    charm +tiny\n    say: {It's real, and very good. The Thornwick interns look almost disappointed.|It's real. You eat it slowly, in front of everyone, and thank them warmly.|You eat the plain biscuit with every sign of delight. Nobody in the lab can tell if it was real, including you.}\n  else:\n    mood -small\n    charm +tiny\n    say: {It's a glamour over something dry and joyless. You eat the whole thing with a smile, and the lab respects that.|It tastes of nothing at all. You compliment the baker, and someone in the corner laughs.|The biscuit turns to air in your mouth. You keep chewing anyway, and the lab gives you a small round of applause.}\n> right: Bring my own breakfast\n  wage\n  guile +small\n  perform intern_thornwick -tiny\n  say: {You eat your own bread, very pointedly. The Thornwick students write something down.|You leave the plate untouched. Your supervisor here calls it \"a very Lumenhold choice\", and doesn't mean it nicely.|You eat your own bread and offer them some. They check it for glamours, which is the friendliest thing they've done.}\n\n@event intern_thornwick_rivalry\ntitle: The Old Grudge\ntrack: intern_thornwick\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: duel\ntags: study\nscene: A group of rival students around a lab bench, one of them leaning in with a pointed smile towards a visitor in different robes.\ntext: {The Thornwick students have questions about Lumenhold. Most of them are about the last time the two schools met on the duelling field.|The Thornwick students have found a new angle on the old grudge.|You and the Thornwick students have a routine now. It's almost friendly.} {Today they want to know if Lumenhold's towers still lean.|Today they ask, sweetly, if Lumenhold teaches illusion yet.|Today one of them pretends to be you, very well.}\n> left: Out-charm them\n  wage\n  challenge negotiate adept-:\n    win:\n      perform intern_thornwick +small\n      charm +small\n      say: {You answer every barb with a better one. By lunch they're laughing with you rather than at you.|You make the room laugh at their joke, and then at them. Their supervisor smiles behind her notes.|You do a better impression of them than they did of you. The lab agrees, and so, grudgingly, do they.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      perform intern_thornwick -tiny\n      say: {You try to be witty. It comes out as a speech about the founders. They let you finish, kindly.|You lose the exchange so badly that one of them takes a bow on your behalf.|Their impression of you is so good that you forget your answer. They finish it for you, in your voice.}\n> right: Let it slide\n  wage\n  perform intern_thornwick +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: {You smile and go back to work. Your supervisor here notices, and approves, and you feel like you lost anyway.|You say nothing. It takes them all morning to realise you've won.|You let it go, as usual. They seem almost disappointed, and one of them saves you a seat at lunch.}\n\n# ---------------- Internship: the Office of Unseen Affairs ----------------\n\n@event intern_unseen_office_coffee\ntitle: The Coffee Machine\ntrack: intern_unseen_office\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: cup\ntags: study mystery\nscene: A plain room with no windows and a gleaming coffee machine, quiet people in plain coats standing near it, not quite talking.\ntext: {Nobody at the Office talks about anything, except the coffee machine. It's very good.|A queue forms around the machine each evening, though nobody seems to join it.|The coffee queue, you've learned, is where the Office's real work gets done.} {Two people beside you discuss the weather in a way that isn't about the weather.|A cup has a name on it that was there yesterday, under another name.|The machine hisses, and everyone falls silent until it stops.}\n> left: Linger at the machine\n  wage\n  chance likely:\n    guile +small\n    perform intern_unseen_office +tiny\n    say: {You learn three things nobody told you, and one thing nobody knows you know.|You drink your coffee slowly and listen. By the second cup you understand what nobody is saying.|You stand in the queue long enough to be part of it. Someone tells you something, and you understand it was meant for you.}\n  else:\n    mood -small\n    say: {Someone notices you listening and asks, very kindly, how your day is going. You don't sleep that night.|You overhear something you very much wish you hadn't. It can't be unheard.|The machine hisses mid-sentence and the room goes silent. When it stops, everyone is looking at you.}\n> right: Take my cup back to my desk\n  wage\n  perform intern_unseen_office +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {You work through the evening with your coffee. The work is dull and your supervisor approves, which is a little chilling.|Your desk is very quiet. It's the most anyone at the Office will say about you, and here that's high praise.|You take your cup to your desk. When you come back for the next one, the queue has saved your place.}\n\n@event intern_unseen_office_wrong_file\ntitle: The Wrong File\ntrack: intern_unseen_office\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: eye\ntags: study mystery\nscene: A plain folder with no label lying on an empty desk under a single lamp.\ntext: {A file has been left on your desk. It isn't yours, and it isn't labelled.|A second unlabelled file. You're starting to think it's a test.|The unlabelled files keep arriving. You've stopped asking where they come from.} This one is tied shut with a red ribbon. {It's very thin.|It's very thick, with a coffee ring on the cover.|The knot is one you don't recognise.}\n> left: Read it before returning it\n  wage\n  alignment -tiny\n  challenge deceive adept-:\n    win:\n      guile +small\n      perform intern_unseen_office +small\n      say: {You read it, retie the ribbon perfectly and hand it in. Your supervisor checks the knot and smiles, very slightly.|You read every page. It's about a man who sells hats. Nothing has ever made you more nervous.|You read it, retie the unfamiliar knot from memory and hand it in. Your supervisor checks it twice and then teaches you a new one.}\n    lose:\n      perform intern_unseen_office -small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {Your supervisor takes the file back and glances at the ribbon. \"Interesting knot,\" she says.|You put the pages back in the wrong order. Somehow, everyone knows by morning.|You can't retie the knot. You hand it in with an ordinary bow, and your supervisor looks at it for a long time.}\n> right: Return it unopened\n  wage\n  alignment +tiny\n  perform intern_unseen_office +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: {You hand it back without looking. Your supervisor says nothing, which here is approval. You'll wonder about it for weeks.|You return it with the ribbon untouched. The next day there's a slightly better coffee waiting on your desk.|You return it unopened, as always. The files stop coming for a week, and then there are two.}\n"},{"path":"content/events/pools/renown.grim","text":"# Renown (playtest 2, X3; L22: renown was hard to raise): twice a year a chance to be noticed is pushed\n# (`renown_chance`), and a handful of cards in the pools stake renown on a hard challenge: much to gain, a real\n# price to fail. Some open only once you're known.\n\n@event renown_chance\ntitle: A Chance to Be Noticed\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: term and (month == 2 or month == 8) and not final_year\nrepeat: 6\npriority: 25\ntype: menu\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nart: trophy\ntags: renown\nscene: A noticeboard in the entrance hall crowded with announcements, one gold-bordered notice pinned dead centre, students jostling to read it.\ntext: {A gold-bordered notice in the entrance hall says the academy wants volunteers.|Another gold notice, and the crowd around it is bigger this time.|The gold notice again. People glance at you as they read it now.} {Whoever stands up will be seen by the whole school.|The village is invited.|There's a prize, and there's the shame of losing in public.}\n> demonstrate: Cast a spell before the school\n  hint: Arcana, hard. Fame or embarrassment.\n  queue renown_demonstration now\n  say: You write your name on the list before you can think better of it.\n> debate: Enter the great debate\n  hint: Lore, hard. The faculty will be listening.\n  queue renown_debate now\n  say: You sign up for the debate. Your hands are shaking already.\n> fair: Perform at the village fair\n  hint: Charm and a spell. The village pays and remembers.\n  queue renown_village_fair now\n  say: You put your name down for the village fair. Somebody will have to teach you to bow.\n> pass: Keep your head down\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {You read the notice and walk on. Fame can find someone else this term.|Not this time. You've got enough to do without an audience.|People glance at you as you walk past the notice. Let them. You'd rather not lose in public this term.}\n\n@event renown_demonstration\ntitle: Before the Whole School\ntrigger: queued\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nart: wand\ntags: renown magic\nscene: The Great Hall packed to the rafters, every house at its table, a single student standing in the middle of the floor with a wand raised.\ntext: The whole school is in the Great Hall, and so is the faculty, and so, at the end, is the headmistress. You're in the middle of the floor. Your spell is the most ambitious thing you know. There's a long silence while everyone waits for you to begin.\n> left: Cast the hardest thing you know\n  challenge spell adept drift mid:\n    win:\n      renown +large\n      arcana +small\n      say: It works, beautifully, better than ever before. The hall is silent and then very loud. For a month, first-years point at you in corridors.\n    lose:\n      renown -small\n      mood -medium\n      say: It fizzles in front of everyone, with a noise like a sad goose. The hall is very kind about it, which is unbearable. You'll be \"the goose\" until someone else fails.\n> right: Cast something safe, perfectly\n  renown +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: Something simple, done flawlessly. Polite applause. Nobody will remember it, and nobody will laugh at you either.\n\n@event renown_debate\ntitle: The Great Debate\ntrigger: queued\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nart: scroll\ntags: renown study\nscene: Two lecterns on a stage under banners, a student at one pointing emphatically, an older student at the other frowning, a packed hall of faces in the dark.\ntext: The great debate: \"That magic should be taught to anyone who asks.\" You've drawn the side you disagree with, against a fourth-year who has never lost. The faculty are in the front row. Thorne is taking notes.\n> left: Argue your side brilliantly anyway\n  challenge research expert drift mid:\n    win:\n      renown +large\n      lore +small\n      rel thorne +small\n      say: You argue the side you don't believe so well that you half-believe it. The fourth-year concedes. Thorne, afterwards, says \"Hm,\" twice, which has never happened.\n    lose:\n      renown -small\n      mood -small\n      say: The fourth-year takes you apart, politely and completely. The hall applauds you for trying, which is the cruellest kind of applause.\n> right: Argue what you actually believe\n  renown +tiny\n  charm +small\n  mood -small\n  say: You throw away the brief and argue your own side, which is against the rules. You lose on points and win the hall. Thorne writes something down, and you'll never know what.\n\n@event renown_village_fair\ntitle: Magic at the Fair\ntrigger: queued\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: sparkle\ntags: renown money\nscene: A small stage at a village fair under bunting, a student conjuring coloured lights over a crowd of apprentices and farmers.\ntext: The village fair has a stage, bunting and a crowd of farmers who have seen every trick there is. The village apprentices sit at the front, cross-legged, unimpressed in advance.\n> left: Put on a real show\n  challenge negotiate journeyman:\n    win:\n      renown +medium\n      gold +small\n      say: Lights, a dancing hat, a dove that turns into a shower of petals. The apprentices whoop. The farmers pay for the hat trick twice. The village talks about it until harvest.\n    lose:\n      renown -tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: The dove refuses to turn into anything and sits on your head. The apprentices love it; the farmers don't pay.\n> right: Do card tricks for the front row\n  renown +small\n  mood +small\n  say: You sit on the edge of the stage and do card tricks for the apprentices until your voice gives out. No fame to speak of. Half of them walk you to the gate.\n\n@event renown_ice_rescue\ntitle: A First-Year on the Ice\nclass: incident\ntone: tense\nwhen: season == \"winter\" and term\nweight: 5\nart: snow\ntags: renown danger\nscene: A small figure clinging to the edge of a broken patch of ice in the middle of a frozen lake, students shouting on the shore, a student running out across the ice.\ntext: A first-year has gone through the ice on the lake. A crowd is shouting on the shore, and nobody is moving. You're nearest.\n> left: Go out on the ice\n  alignment +tiny\n  challenge feat adept:\n    win:\n      renown +large\n      health -small\n      say: You crawl out flat on the ice and pull them free. By supper the whole school knows your name, and the first-year's mother writes you a letter you keep for ever.\n    lose:\n      health -large\n      renown +small\n      say: The ice gives under you too. The prefects pull you both out with a ladder. You spend a week in the infirmary, a little famous, mostly frozen.\n> right: Run for the prefects\n  renown +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: You run for help, and the prefects get there with ladders in time. Someone else is the hero. You were right, and you feel wrong about it all winter.\n\n@event renown_portrait\ntitle: A Portrait for the Hall\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: renown >= 30\nweight: 8\nart: mirror\ntags: renown money\nscene: A painter's easel in a sunlit corridor, a half-finished portrait of a student in formal robes, the sitter trying very hard not to scratch their nose.\ntext: The academy hangs a portrait of its most talked-about students in the long gallery each year, and someone has suggested you. The painter's fee is yours to pay, as is the custom. The other portraits are already watching you.\n> left: Sit for it\n  gold -medium\n  renown +medium\n  mood -tiny\n  say: Six sittings, an itchy collar and a painter who hums. The portrait is good, if a little more heroic than you are. Passing it, first-years straighten their backs.\n> right: Decline politely\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You decline. Your purse is grateful. The portrait that goes up instead is of someone who looks very pleased with themselves, and you find you don't mind at all.\n\n@event renown_rumour\ntitle: A Rumour About You\nclass: incident\ntone: tense\nwhen: renown >= 40\nweight: 5\nart: mask\ntags: renown social\nscene: Two students whispering behind their hands in a corridor, a third walking past them with a stiff back, a crumpled gossip sheet on the floor.\ntext: Being known has a price: there's a rumour about you going round the castle, untrue and a little bit funny, and it's in Nessa's gossip column by Friday. People stop talking when you walk in.\n> left: Answer it in public\n  challenge negotiate journeyman unlikely wild:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      say: You laugh it off at supper, loudly and well, with a better story of your own. By Monday everyone's repeating yours instead.\n    lose:\n      renown -small\n      mood -small\n      say: You deny it too hard, and it sounds true. The rumour grows a second chapter. It'll pass. It takes a month.\n> right: Ignore it\n  renown -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You say nothing. It burns for a fortnight and then dies of boredom. You learn who repeated it, and remember.\n\n@event renown_patron_dinner\ntitle: Dinner With a Patron\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: renown >= 25 and term\nweight: 8\nart: meal\ntags: renown money\nscene: A long candlelit dining table in a grand townhouse, a student in borrowed formal robes seated between a wealthy patron and a portrait of the patron.\ntext: A wealthy patron of the academy has asked to meet \"the students everyone's talking about\", and you're one of them. Dinner is nine courses. The patron likes clever talk and has been known to fund a promising student's whole final year.\n> left: Be dazzling\n  challenge negotiate expert:\n    win:\n      renown +medium\n      gold +medium\n      say: You're witty through eight courses and wise on the ninth. The patron sends you home with a purse \"for books\" and an invitation to come back next year.\n    lose:\n      renown -small\n      mood -small\n      say: You use the wrong fork, then the wrong joke. The patron is kind and bored. You're not asked back.\n> right: Be honest about your struggles\n  renown +small\n  gold +small\n  say: You talk plainly about money and work and exams. The patron listens, the only one at the table who does. A small purse arrives the next week with no letter, just a crest.\n"},{"path":"content/events/pools/spell_lessons.grim","text":"# Spell lessons (playtest 2, N3): once a spell is learned, the next term month shows where and how it's really taught,\n# and by whom. One card per spell, once a run. Written for the current twelve spells; reworked if the minigames change them.\n\n@event lesson_flame\ntitle: Putting Fire Out\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: spell_level(flame) >= 1 and term\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nactor: thorne\nart: flame\ntags: magic study\nscene: A stone practice room with scorched walls, a stern grey-bearded professor beside a roaring brazier, a bucket of water at his feet.\ntext: Flame is yours now. Professor Thorne gathers the year's new Flame casters in the stone practice room and lights a brazier. \"Any fool can start a fire,\" he says. \"You are here to learn to stop one.\" There is a bucket of water at his feet, and he forbids you to touch it.\n> left: Pull the fire back into your hand\n  meet thorne\n  arcana +small\n  mana -small\n  say: You call the flame back like a sleeve pulled over a wrist. It stings, and the brazier flares and sinks. \"Again,\" says Thorne. He says it eleven times.\n> right: Ask what the bucket is for\n  meet thorne\n  lore +small\n  rel thorne +small\n  say: \"For the student who doesn't ask,\" says Thorne, and empties it over the brazier of the boy beside you. Then he explains, for an hour, how every great fire he has known began.\n\n@event lesson_tide\ntitle: Waist-Deep at Dawn\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: spell_level(tide) >= 1 and term\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nactor: marisol\nart: wave\ntags: magic study\nscene: A misty lake at dawn, a professor standing waist-deep in the water with arms raised, students hesitating on the pebbled shore.\ntext: Professor Marisol teaches Tide where it lives: waist-deep in the lake at dawn. \"Cold water,\" she says happily, \"is good for everyone.\" She recites a poem about waves and stops before the last line, frowning. The lake waits for you.\n> left: Wade in and call a wave\n  meet marisol\n  arcana +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: The water is so cold it feels like an opinion. Your wave rises knee-high and slaps Marisol full in the chest, and she whoops. \"The last line!\" she shouts. \"It was about this!\"\n> right: Call it from the shore\n  meet marisol\n  lore +small\n  rel marisol -tiny\n  say: You work the spell from the dry pebbles and raise a neat little swell. Marisol looks at your dry boots with real sorrow, as if at a poem with no ending.\n\n@event lesson_foresight\ntitle: The Bottom of the Cup\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: spell_level(foresight) >= 1 and term\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nart: eye\ntags: magic study\nscene: An observatory room with tea cups on every desk, a lecturer in three scarves peering into a student's cup with a magnifying glass.\ntext: The astromancy lecturer, who wears three scarves in every season, sits the class at the observatory with cups of tea and no instructions. Foresight, it turns out, begins at the bottom of the cup: what you think you see, and what you want to.\n> left: Read your own cup\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: The leaves say a hard week is coming. It does, from Tuesday. The lecturer says that's the trouble with reading your own: you start making it true.\n> right: Read your neighbour's\n  charm +small\n  say: You tell your neighbour a letter is on its way. A letter arrives at supper. Now half the dormitory wants their leaves read, and you have to learn to say \"unclear\" kindly.\n\n@event lesson_starlight\ntitle: A Jar on the Roof\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: spell_level(starlight) >= 1 and term\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nslot: night\nart: star\ntags: magic study\nscene: A flat observatory roof at night, a student holding up a glass jar toward a sky crowded with stars, breath steaming.\ntext: Starlight is taught after midnight on the observatory roof, where the astromancy lecturer hands you a glass jar and tells you to hold it up \"until something falls in\". The stars are very far away. The roof is very cold.\n> left: Hold the jar until it fills\n  lore +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: At three in the morning a thread of light settles in the jar like milk in tea. You carry it down the stairs in both hands. You can't feel your fingers until breakfast.\n> right: Lie back and watch the sky\n  mood +small\n  say: You lie on the roof and name the constellations with whoever is beside you. Your jar stays empty. The lecturer, passing, says that's the first lesson too, and the one most people skip.\n\n@event lesson_mend\ntitle: Teacups First\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: spell_level(mend) >= 1 and term\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nactor: amaranth\nart: cup\ntags: magic study\nscene: An infirmary storeroom, a stern healer dropping a teacup to the stone floor while a student kneels over the pieces with a wand.\ntext: Sister Amaranth teaches Mend with teacups. She drops one on the infirmary floor without looking. \"Mend it.\" When it's whole she drops another. \"Bones are harder,\" she says, rolling bandages. \"Cups first.\"\n> left: Mend cup after cup\n  meet amaranth\n  alchemy +small\n  mana -small\n  say: By the twentieth cup you can hear the crack close before you see it. Amaranth says \"Keep doing that,\" and drops a teapot.\n> right: Ask her about the bones\n  meet amaranth\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: She tells you, still rolling bandages, about the worst break she ever set. It was a student's; that student is a professor now. You go a little pale, and listen to every word.\n\n@event lesson_purify\ntitle: Make It Water\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: spell_level(purify) >= 1 and term\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nactor: amaranth\nart: cauldron\ntags: magic study\nscene: An alchemy lab bench with a cauldron of thick green liquid, a healer standing over a student who holds a wand above it.\ntext: Purify is taught over a cauldron of something green that Sister Amaranth will not name. \"Make it water,\" she says, and goes back to her own work. That is the whole of the instruction. The smell is a kind of instruction too.\n> left: Purify it layer by layer\n  meet amaranth\n  alchemy +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: Slowly, layer by layer, the green turns grey, then cloudy, then clear. You breathe the fumes for two hours and get a headache for one. Amaranth tastes the result, nods and pours it into the kettle.\n> right: Ask what it was\n  meet amaranth\n  rel amaranth +small\n  say: \"Last term's soup,\" she says. You decide not to believe her, then decide you do. She shows you the trick of it while you laugh, and you purify half a cup, badly.\n\n@event lesson_glamour\ntitle: Pick a Card\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: spell_level(glamour) >= 1 and term\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nactor: vey\nart: cards\ntags: magic study\nscene: A velvet-curtained classroom, a professor in a violet coat fanning out a hand of playing cards toward a row of students.\ntext: Professor Vey's glamour lesson is a card game. Each student glamours a plain card to look like the queen of stars, and Vey picks out the true queen from the pile. Nobody, she says, has ever made her pick wrong. \"Fool me, and you may skip the essay.\"\n> left: Glamour your card\n  meet vey\n  challenge deceive master- slim:\n    win:\n      charm +small\n      rel vey +small\n      say: Vey picks your card, turns it over and finds a plain two. She laughs out loud, a real laugh, and the class goes silent. \"Well,\" she says. \"Well, well. No essay for you.\"\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      say: Vey passes over your card without a glance. \"The queen of stars,\" she says kindly, \"does not blush.\"\n> right: Watch how she catches them\n  meet vey\n  guile +small\n  say: You watch her eyes instead of the cards. She never looks at the faces, only at the hands holding them. You write that down, and she sees you write it, and smiles.\n\n@event lesson_mirror\ntitle: Gently\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: spell_level(mirror) >= 1 and term\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nactor: vey\nart: mirror\ntags: magic study\nscene: A dim classroom with the curtains drawn, a hand mirror on every desk, a professor standing well to one side of the glass.\ntext: Professor Vey teaches Mirror in an empty classroom with the curtains drawn, which is not like her. \"A mirror charm shows people themselves,\" she says. \"Do it gently. Most people have not asked to see.\" She stands to one side of every glass in the room.\n> left: Practise on yourself first\n  meet vey\n  charm +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: The charm shows you yourself a moment too honestly. You practise until you can look at it without flinching. Mostly.\n> right: Ask why she avoids the glass\n  meet vey\n  rel vey +small\n  say: \"Vanity,\" says Vey, with a magnificent smile that answers nothing at all. But she lets you stay after the others leave, and shows you how to soften the charm's edges.\n\n@event lesson_haste\ntitle: Race the Hippogriff\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: spell_level(haste) >= 1 and term\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nactor: brask\nart: feather\ntags: magic study\nscene: A windy flight field marked with flags, a grey-haired professor holding a stopwatch, a student sprinting beside a hippogriff.\ntext: Professor Brask teaches Haste on the flight field, with a stopwatch and a hippogriff that is faster than the spell. \"Beat her,\" she says to you. To the hippogriff she explains, at length and politely, that she is not to bite you if you win.\n> left: Race the hippogriff\n  meet brask\n  challenge feat adept-:\n    win:\n      vigor +small\n      rel brask +tiny\n      say: You cross the flag a beak's length ahead. The hippogriff looks at Brask; Brask looks at the stopwatch, then at you. \"Hm,\" she says. From her, it's a medal.\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      say: The hippogriff wins by a length and takes your hat as her prize. Brask tells a story about a student who lost to a goose. It ends with a bite. \"Moral: faster.\"\n> right: Time the others first\n  meet brask\n  lore +small\n  say: You hold the stopwatch and watch the fast ones. They aren't running harder; they start the spell a step before they need it. Brask sees you notice, and hands you the next race.\n\n@event lesson_beast_speech\ntitle: The Mule Has Views\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: spell_level(beast_speech) >= 1 and term\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nactor: brask\nart: paw\ntags: magic study\nscene: A warm stall with straw on the floor, a student sitting cross-legged opposite a grey old mule who stares back without blinking.\ntext: Beast-speech, says Professor Brask, is mostly listening. She sits you in a stall beside an old mule who has, she says, \"views\". You are to hear them. The mule looks at you as if you're the one being taught.\n> left: Sit with him all afternoon\n  meet brask\n  vigor +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: In the fourth hour the mule's thoughts come through: mostly apples, and one long grievance about a farrier. Brask says \"Good,\" to the mule, not to you.\n> right: Try the spell straight away\n  meet brask\n  arcana +small\n  mana -small\n  say: You cast before you've listened and get one word back, very clearly: \"Rude.\" Brask laughs once, loudly. It's the laugh of the term, and everyone in the stables heard it.\n\n@event lesson_ward\ntitle: Chalk\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: spell_level(ward) >= 1 and term\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: shield\ntags: magic study\nscene: A dim room at the top of a tower, low lamps, a lecturer mid-throw with a piece of chalk while students hold up shimmering wards.\ntext: At the top of the lecture tower, where the lamps burn dim on purpose, the umbral lecturer teaches Ward by throwing chalk. Hard, and without warning. \"A ward that only works when you're ready,\" says the lecturer, \"is a decoration.\"\n> left: Hold the ward all hour\n  guile +small\n  mana -small\n  say: The first chalk hits your forehead. The fifth bounces off the ward with a sound like a bell. By the end of the hour your ward is up before you know you've raised it.\n> right: Watch the lecturer's wrist\n  lore +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: You let the ward drop and watch how the throws begin. Three pieces of chalk find your forehead while you do. But you see it now: the shoulder moves first.\n\n@event lesson_shadowstep\ntitle: Where the Voice Was\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: spell_level(shadowstep) >= 1 and term\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntime: night\nart: moon\ntags: magic study\nscene: A pitch-dark tower room with one dim lamp guttering out, the outline of a student stepping between deep shadows.\ntext: Shadowstep is taught in the dark. The umbral lecturer puts out the last dim lamp and calls your name from somewhere else in the room. You have to arrive where the voice was before it moves. Somewhere, a chair is waiting for your shins.\n> left: Step into the dark\n  guile +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: You step, and the dark folds, and you're across the room with a bruise from the chair. The voice is right beside you. \"Better,\" it says, and moves away again.\n> right: Wait for your eyes to adjust\n  lore +small\n  say: You stand still until the dark has edges. The lecturer, somewhere, says that's cheating, and that cheating is half of umbral magic. You step only once, but you step exactly.\n"},{"path":"content/events/pools/statuses.grim","text":"# Statuses in play (playtest 2, N2, N5, N8): cards that show a state you're in, so it is more than a number at month end.\n\n# ---------------- Hungry ----------------\n\n@event hungry_leftovers\ntitle: The Refectory's Leftovers\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nslot: night\nwhen: has(hungry) and term\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 20\nart: meal\ntags: food money\nscene: A refectory kitchen door at closing time, a cook scraping the last of a stew into a bowl for a student waiting in the doorway.\ntext: {The refectory is closing, and the kitchens are scraping the pots. A cook sees you hovering in the doorway and holds up a ladle, eyebrows raised.|The same cook, the same ladle. She doesn't ask any more; she just raises her eyebrows.|You know the kitchens' closing time to the minute now.} {A prefect is still at the end table, finishing an essay.|Two first-years are sweeping, and watching.|The cook is humming, which means there's plenty.}\n> left: Take the bowl\n  health +small\n  renown -tiny\n  say: {It's the burnt bit at the bottom of the stew and it is the best thing you've ever eaten. The prefect looks up from her essay and remembers your face.|The cook fills the bowl to the brim. The first-years see, and by breakfast half your year knows why.|You know the closing time to the minute, and so does the cook. She fills the bowl before you're through the door, humming.}\n> right: Shake your head and leave\n  health -tiny\n  say: {\"Not hungry,\" you say, to nobody in particular, and go upstairs on an empty stomach that disagrees.|You walk out with your pride and nothing else. Your stomach complains all night.|You know the closing time, and leave a minute before it. The humming follows you up the stairs, and so does the smell.}\n\n@event hungry_pocketed_rolls\ntitle: Rolls for Later\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: has(hungry) and term\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 20\nart: meal\ntags: food money\nscene: A breakfast table heaped with bread rolls, a student's hand hovering near a pocket while a prefect patrols the aisle.\ntext: {The refectory's bread rolls are piled high. Board-payers only, says the sign; it doesn't say how many.|The rolls again, and the sign again, and your empty pockets.|Another meal. You've got good at looking as if you've paid.} {The prefect on duty is reading a letter.|The prefect on duty is looking straight at the bread.|The prefect on duty has gone for more tea.}\n> left: Pocket a few for later\n  health +small\n  alignment -tiny\n  say: {Three rolls in your robe, still warm. They last a day, and so does the guilt.|You fill your pockets and walk out at an even pace, crumbs and all.|Two rolls and an apple. Nobody stops you. You eat them on the stairs, a little ashamed.}\n> right: Eat what's on your plate\n  say: {One roll, eaten slowly, and nothing taken that isn't yours. By the next meal you're light-headed and honest.|You eat what you're given, and the hunger keeps you company for hours.|One roll, slowly, with the look of someone who has paid. The hunger knows better.}\n\n@event hungry_maud_soup\ntitle: Soup at the Cottage\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nactor: maud\nwhen: has(hungry) and met(maud) and term\nrepeat: 2\nweight: 20\nart: cauldron\ntags: food social\nscene: A crooked cottage kitchen full of hanging herbs, an old woman ladling soup from a black pot into a chipped bowl.\ntext: {Old Maud catches you on the path by her cottage. \"You've a hungry look, dearie. Come and eat.\" She has a pot on already, as if she knew.|Maud's pot is on again. She doesn't ask, and you don't explain.} {The soup is leek and something she won't name.|A bee sits on the windowsill, waiting for its share.}\n> left: Stay for soup and a story\n  health +small\n  mood +small\n  rel maud +tiny\n  study -tiny\n  say: {Two bowls, bread, and a sad story about her sister told like a recipe. Your reading waits until morning, and it'll be late.|She talks about the winter the lake froze twice while you eat. The essay you meant to write tonight stays unwritten.}\n> right: Take a jar back to your room\n  health +small\n  say: {She fills a jar and wraps it in a cloth. \"Bring the jar back,\" she says. \"That's the price.\" You eat at your desk, studying.|You carry the warm jar up the hill and eat it over your books. Maud waves from the door, a little disappointed.}\n\n# ---------------- Detention ----------------\n\n@event detention_cauldrons\ntitle: Cauldron Duty\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: has(detention)\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 20\nart: cauldron\ntags: study\nscene: A dim potions storeroom, a student up to the elbows in a crusted cauldron while a prefect reads by a lamp at the door.\ntext: {Detention, and a row of crusted cauldrons and a prefect by the door with a book.|Detention again. The cauldrons are the same; the crust is worse.|You know these cauldrons by name now. One of them, you suspect, knows you.} {Something in the third one is still bubbling.|The prefect is pretending not to be bored.|The prefect has brought a second book this time.}\n> left: Scrub them properly\n  alchemy +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You scrub until your knuckles are raw, and learn which residues bite back and which only sulk.|By the last cauldron you can tell a week-old draught from a month-old one by the smell alone. Your hands sting for days.|You scrub your old acquaintances one by one. The one that knows you gives up its crust almost gently.}\n> right: Talk your way to an early night\n  challenge negotiate apprentice+:\n    win:\n      mood +small\n      say: {You talk about the prefect's book until the prefect is talking about it too, and at nine they wave you off. \"Same time next week. Possibly.\"|You find out you're both from the same county. The cauldrons can wait for someone less charming.|The prefect is on the second book and glad of the interruption. You talk until nine, and the cauldrons stay crusted another week.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      health -tiny\n      say: {\"Nice try,\" says the prefect, and hands you the worst cauldron in the row.|The prefect hears you out, then points at the third cauldron, the bubbling one. \"For the speech.\"|The prefect doesn't look up from the second book. \"The one that knows you,\" they say. \"Start there.\"}\n\n@event detention_lines\ntitle: Lines, With Company\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ncast:\n  other: any where met(other) and tagged(other, student)\nwhen: has(detention)\nrepeat: 2\nweight: 16\nart: book\ntags: study social\nscene: An empty classroom at dusk, two students at separate desks writing the same sentence over and over, a quill scratching on its own at the front.\ntext: Detention means lines, a hundred of them, and {other} is at the next desk doing the same. {~The sentence is \"I will not bring livestock into the library.\"|The sentence is \"Curfew is not a suggestion.\"|The sentence is \"Stairs are for walking.\"} An enchanted quill at the front of the room writes down everything anyone says.\n> left: Do your homework under the desk\n  challenge deceive apprentice likely:\n    win:\n      study +small\n      say: {~A hundred lines and an evening's revision for every course. The quill at the front records nothing but scratching.|You alternate a line and a page of notes. Nobody checks. Your whole week's reading is the better for it.|You write \"Stairs are for walking\" with one hand and a week's notes with the other. The quill at the front records only the stairs.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      study -tiny\n      say: {~The prefect lifts your lines, finds your notes underneath and confiscates them until the end of term.|Your notes fall on the floor at exactly the wrong moment. The prefect keeps them, and makes you start the lines again.|Between the stairs and the notes you lose track. The prefect finds forty lines of notes among the stairs, and you start again.}\n> right: Talk to {other}\n  rel other +small\n  say: {~By the fortieth line you know why {other} is here, and it's a better story than yours. The quill at the front writes it all down, word for word.|You compare crimes. {other} laughs so hard the quill at the front writes \"LAUGHTER\" in capitals.|{other} tells you which staircase is to blame. You laugh until the quill at the front writes \"STAIRS?\" in the margin.}\n\n# ---------------- Probation ----------------\n\n@event probation_advisor\ntitle: A Word With Your Advisor\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: has(probation) and term\nrepeat: 2\nweight: 20\nart: scales\ntags: study\nscene: A cluttered faculty office with a ticking clock, an advisor and a student either side of a desk with a marks list between them.\ntext: {A summons to your advisor's office. Your marks lie on the desk between you, upside down for you, right way up for them.|Your advisor again, and the same marks, a little better or a little worse.} \"Two terms under the line and the Disciplinary Board sits,\" they say. \"Shall we make sure it doesn't?\"\n> left: Make a study plan together\n  study +medium\n  mood -small\n  say: {An hour later you have a timetable that colours every evening grey, and you start on the first evening of it that night. It is a very good timetable. You hate it.|The plan has a column for sleep, and it is narrow. You work through the first week of it with your advisor checking in.}\n> right: Talk about what's going wrong\n  mood +small\n  say: {You say aloud the thing you hadn't said to anyone. Your advisor listens and makes no plan at all. You leave lighter, and no better prepared.|It turns out your advisor failed a year, once. You talk about that instead of your marks.}\n\n@event probation_letter\ntitle: The Warning Letter\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: has(probation) and term\nweight: 20\nart: letter\ntags: study\nscene: A stiff envelope with the academy's seal lying open on a desk, a student holding the letter at arm's length.\ntext: A letter from the registrar, in the stiff envelope everyone dreads: Academic Probation, your name spelled correctly for once, and a line about \"consequences\" underlined twice. A copy, it says, has gone to your family.\n> left: Pin it above your desk\n  study +small\n  mood -small\n  say: The letter glares at you every time you look up. It works, in the way a headache works: you study, and you don't enjoy a minute of it.\n> right: Put it in a drawer\n  say: Out of sight, mostly out of mind. Mostly. Some nights you open the drawer, look at it and close the drawer again.\n\n# ---------------- Sick or injured ----------------\n\n@event sick_in_the_infirmary\ntitle: A Bed by the Window\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: has(sick) and term\nrepeat: 2\nweight: 20\nart: bed\ntags: health\nscene: A row of white infirmary beds, a student wrapped in blankets with a faintly glowing blue nose and a steaming mug beside them.\ntext: {Lake fever. Your nose glows faintly blue, and Sister Amaranth says \"Bed,\" in the voice that means it.|The fever again, and the same bed by the window.} {Lectures are going on without you.|Someone has left a stack of notes on the chair.}\n> left: Stay in bed all week\n  health +medium\n  study -small\n  say: {You sleep, drink something bitter and sleep again. When you go back, the class is two chapters ahead.|A week of blankets and broth. You feel much better and know much less.}\n> right: Go to lectures in a scarf\n  study +tiny\n  health -small\n  say: {You sniffle through Arcana in three scarves. The notes are good; the coughing fit in the middle is not.|You make every lecture, and every lecture makes you worse. Sister Amaranth sees you on the stairs and says nothing, very loudly.}\n\n@event injured_on_the_stairs\ntitle: The Moving Stairs, Limping\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: has(injured) and term\nrepeat: 2\nweight: 20\nart: door\ntags: health\nscene: A grand staircase swinging slowly between landings, a student with a bandaged ankle clinging to the banister as it moves.\ntext: {Your ankle is bandaged and the stairs are moving, as stairs here do. The lecture is three landings up and starts in five minutes.|The stairs again, the ankle again. The lecture, as ever, is at the top.}\n> left: Take the long way, slowly\n  health +small\n  study -tiny\n  say: {You limp round by the back corridor and arrive as everyone is packing up. The ankle thanks you; your notes don't.|Slow, careful and very late. The ankle mends a little faster for it.}\n> right: Hop the gap as it swings\n  challenge feat apprentice wild:\n    win:\n      renown +tiny\n      say: {You time it perfectly and land on one foot. Somebody on the landing applauds.|One hop, one wobble, one bow. You're on time and briefly famous.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      say: {You land on the wrong foot, the bad one. The stairs swing on without you, and you sit on the step for a while.|The gap is wider than it looked. You arrive on time and limping twice as badly.}\n\n# ---------------- An empty purse (N5: a hint, never an obligation) ----------------\n\n@event purse_and_the_board\ntitle: A Light Purse\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: term and gold < 10 and not member(job) and turn > 2\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 16\nart: pouch\ntags: money\nscene: A student emptying a coin purse on a dormitory bed, three coins and a button rolling out across the blanket.\ntext: {You tip out your purse on the bed. Three coins and a button.|The purse again. The button is still there; the coins are fewer.|The purse, the button, and fewer coins than ever. You've stopped counting the button.} Board is due at the start of every month. {On the way to breakfast you passed the Job Board in the entrance hall, and someone was taking down a notice.|Someone in your year says the Newt is hiring again.|There's a hardship notice by the bursar's door, someone says, and professors who pay for errands.}\n> left: Sell your second-best cloak\n  gold +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {A second-hand stall in the village gives you a fair price and an unfair look. The castle is draughty without it.|You sell the cloak. It was your grandmother's. You're nearly sure she'd understand.|The second-hand stall knows you by now. The price is fair, and the look is kinder than last time.}\n> right: Look at the Job Board later\n  say: {You make a note. The Job Board, entrance hall, morning to evening. One job at a time, paid by the shift.|You'll go and read the notices. Probably. Board is due either way.|You'll look at the Job Board, and at the hardship notice by the bursar's door. Board is due either way.}\n\n# ---------------- Familiars (N8): cards instead of a monthly tick ----------------\n\n@event ember_on_the_essay\ntitle: A Fox on the Essay\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: has(familiar_fox) and term\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 12\nart: fox\ntags: nature study\nscene: A fox-spirit curled asleep on a student's half-written essay, her tail smouldering gently at one corner of the page.\ntext: {Ember has chosen your week's coursework to sleep on, and her tail is smouldering, as it does when she dreams.|Ember is asleep on your notes again. The corner of every page is already brown.|Ember has a new favourite sleeping place, and all of it is due on Monday.}\n> left: Rescue the essay\n  study +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You slide the pile out from under her. She opens one eye, says something rude in fox and sulks under the bed until supper.|You rescue the pages and lose a fox: she spends the evening on your pillow with her back to you.|You lift her off Monday's work in one piece. She lets you, which is worse: she's planning something.}\n> right: Let her sleep and write around her\n  mood +small\n  study -tiny\n  say: {You write the conclusion in the margin, round a sleeping fox. Your essays come back scorched at one corner and marked down for it.|She's warm, she's purring in her own way, and your notes have a hole in them now. It's worth it.|You write Monday's work round her, a paragraph at a time, while her tail smoulders in the margin. It smells of smoke, and it's marked down for it, and it's in.}\n\n@event ember_night_hunt\ntitle: Ember Goes Hunting\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nslot: night\nwhen: has(familiar_fox)\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 12\nart: fox\ntags: nature mystery\nscene: A dark castle corridor lit only by the glow of a fox's tail, a student in a nightgown following at a distance.\ntext: {Ember tugs at your blanket. Then at your sleeve. Then at your ear. She wants you to come.|Ember is at the door again, tail glowing, waiting.} {Somewhere below, something is scratching.|The corridors are cold and very quiet.}\n> left: Follow her through the castle\n  guile +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {She leads you down three staircases to a loose stone, and sits by it, very pleased. Behind it, nothing but a draught. You're back at dawn, frozen.|A mouse, two corridors and a door you'd never noticed. You get back to bed cold and much better at walking quietly.}\n> right: Pull the blanket over your head\n  health +tiny\n  say: {You sleep. Ember goes alone and comes back at dawn smelling of the kitchens, enormously smug.|She sighs at you, a fox's sigh, and goes without you. You sleep like a stone.}\n\n@event ember_and_the_sandwich\ntitle: The Prefect's Sandwich\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: has(familiar_fox) and term\nrepeat: 2\nweight: 12\nart: fox\ntags: nature social\nscene: A crowded refectory, a small fire-tailed fox trotting off with a sandwich while a prefect stares at an empty plate.\ntext: Ember trots through the refectory with a prefect's sandwich in her jaws, past forty witnesses. The prefect looks at the empty plate, then at the fox, then at the room. \"Whose,\" the prefect says, \"is that?\"\n> left: Own up and buy a new one\n  gold -tiny\n  renown -tiny\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: You raise your hand. The room laughs; you pay for a sandwich at the counter and hand it over. The prefect accepts it with dignity. Ember eats the first one under your chair.\n> right: Look as puzzled as everyone else\n  guile +small\n  alignment -tiny\n  say: You look around the room, as baffled as anybody. Ember sits on your foot under the table, chewing. The prefect never finds out, and Ember never lets you forget it.\n\n@event owl_six_oclock\ntitle: \"HOMEWORK\"\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nslot: night\nwhen: has(familiar_owl) and term\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 12\nart: owl\ntags: study\nscene: A brass clockwork owl on a bedpost at dawn, its amber eyes glowing, a student groaning under a pillow.\ntext: {Bedtime. \"HOMEWORK,\" says the clockwork owl, and then, because you haven't moved, \"HOMEWORK. DUE. TOMORROW.\"|\"HOMEWORK,\" says the owl, at bedtime, as ever. This time it's right. A week's exercises are due tomorrow, in every course, and you'd forgotten.|The owl's bedtime hoot has a new word in it: \"LATE.\"}\n> left: Stay up and write it now\n  study +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You write it all by candlelight with your eyes half shut. It's not your best work, but it's in. The owl says \"GOOD,\" which you didn't know it could.|Two hours of frantic quill-work by the last of the candle. You hand it in on time and nod off in the next lecture.|You write until the owl stops saying \"LATE\" and says nothing at all. It goes in on time, just.}\n> right: Hand it in late\n  health +tiny\n  study -tiny\n  say: {You go to sleep. The owl says \"DISAPPOINTED\" twice and goes quiet. Your work goes in a day late and is marked accordingly.|You sleep until eight. The owl doesn't speak to you until lunch, and then only to say \"LATE.\"|\"LATE,\" says the owl, and it is. You hand the work in a day after, and the owl says it again at breakfast.}\n\n@event owl_birthday\ntitle: \"BIRTHDAY\"\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ncast:\n  friend: any where met(friend) and rel(friend) >= 10 and tagged(friend, student) and around(friend)\nwhen: has(familiar_owl)\nrepeat: 2\nweight: 12\nart: owl\ntags: social\nscene: A clockwork owl fluttering in front of a student, its beak open mid-hoot, a calendar on the wall behind.\ntext: \"BIRTHDAY,\" says the owl, flapping in front of your face, and then, louder, \"BIRTHDAY.\" It turns its head toward the window, toward the dormitory where {friend} lives. Mir built it to remember birthdays. You had not.\n> left: Buy a proper present\n  gold -small\n  rel friend +small\n  say: You run down to the village before the shops close and come back with something {friend} actually wanted. {They} look{s} at you as if you've always remembered.\n> right: Write a card, quickly\n  rel friend +tiny\n  say: A card, a drawing of the owl and \"Happy birthday\" in your best hand. {friend} pins it up anyway, and asks how you knew.\n\n@event owl_wound_down\ntitle: The Owl Winds Down\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: has(familiar_owl) and met(mir)\nweight: 12\nart: owl\ntags: magic social\nscene: A brass owl slumped on a desk with one wing drooping, a student peering into a small open panel in its chest with a magnifying glass.\ntext: The clockwork owl has started saying \"HOME… WORK…\" very slowly, like a music box running down. One wing droops. There is a tiny panel in its chest, and a tinier key, and absolutely no instructions.\n> left: Take it back to Mir\n  rel mir +small\n  study -tiny\n  say: Mir lights up and spends the whole evening inside the owl with tweezers, explaining every spring. You miss your reading entirely. The owl comes back saying \"HOMEWORK\" faster than ever.\n> right: Fix it yourself\n  challenge enchant apprentice+:\n    win:\n      arcana +small\n      say: A wound spring, a drop of oil and a charm to keep it turning. The owl shakes itself and says \"THANK YOU,\" which it has never said before.\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      say: You fix it. Now it hoots on the half hour instead of the hour, all night, and says \"HOMEWORK\" in a voice like a kettle.\n"},{"path":"content/events/pools/upper_ranks.grim","text":"# Top-rank pools (playtest 2, X2): at a club's or job's highest rank (`rank: 3`), its cards carry more risk and more\n# reward instead of a quiet stat bump. They join the lower ranks' cards (pools.md); two per track.\n\n# ---------------- Duelling Circle: Captain ----------------\n\n@event duelling_captain_challenge\ntitle: The Captain's Challenge\ntrack: duelling\nrank: 3\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: duel\ntags: club duel\nscene: A captain of a duelling circle in a chalk ring facing a visiting champion, the whole hall packed to the rafters.\ntext: {A visiting champion has challenged the Circle's captain, by name, in front of everyone. The captain is you.|Another challenger at the door, asking for the captain.|They come for the captain now, from three valleys away.} {The hall is packed to the rafters.|{around(lyra): Lyra is in the front row with her arms folded.|The old captains are in the front row with their arms folded.}|Somebody is taking bets in the corridor.}\n> left: Accept in front of everyone\n  challenge combat expert drift mid:\n    win:\n      perform duelling +medium\n      renown +medium\n      health -small\n      say: {You win the third exchange and the hall comes apart. The champion bows, properly. The Circle will talk about it for a year.|A long, hard bout, and yours at the end. Your name goes on the board in gold paint.|You accept, with bets being taken in the corridor. You win, and somebody in the corridor wins a great deal more.}\n    lose:\n      perform duelling -small\n      renown -small\n      health -small\n      say: {You lose, in front of everyone, by a breath. The Circle carries you to the infirmary anyway, which is its own verdict.|Beaten, cleanly. The champion is gracious. That's the worst part.|You lose to someone from three valleys away, and the corridor pays out. The Circle buys you a drink with the winnings it didn't make.}\n> right: Send your best officer instead\n  perform duelling +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {Your officer wins, narrowly, and the hall cheers for the Circle. A few people look at you and wonder why you didn't step up.|You coach from the edge. The officer loses but fights well. It was the right call, you think, mostly.|You send your officer. They win, and the corridor's bookmaker grumbles that the captain was the better bet.}\n\n@event duelling_captain_injury\ntitle: The Captain's Call\ntrack: duelling\nrank: 3\nclass: routine\ntone: grim\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 9\nart: duel\ntags: club duel danger\nscene: A duellist kneeling in a chalk circle clutching a wrist, a captain crouching beside them, a referee waiting with a whistle.\ntext: {A first-year in the Circle has twisted a wrist in the final of the house cup, and insists on finishing.|Another final, another stubborn first-year with an injury.|The house cup final again, and again someone hurt who won't stop.} {The house's honour is riding on it.|Their parents are in the gallery.|The referee looks at you: the captain decides.}\n> left: Let them finish\n  chance even:\n    perform duelling +medium\n    renown +small\n    say: {They finish, and win, with one hand. The gallery stands. You stood beside them, and you'll remember the fear more than the win.|They finish and win. Amaranth bandages them afterwards and gives you a look that could strip paint.|The referee looks at you, and you nod. They finish, and win, and you hold your breath through every exchange.}\n  else:\n    perform duelling -medium\n    mood -small\n    say: {They finish and lose, and the wrist is worse. Sister Amaranth sends for you specifically, to explain.|The bout ends with a second injury. You let it happen. You don't sleep that night.|The referee looks at you, you nod, and you're wrong. The wrist goes. You walk them to the infirmary yourself.}\n> right: Stop the bout\n  alignment +tiny\n  perform duelling -small\n  say: {You call it. The house loses the cup and the first-year cries and calls you a coward. A week later, in the corridor, they thank you.|You stop it. The gallery boos. {around(lyra): Lyra, in the front row, nods once.|The referee, pocketing the whistle, nods once.}|The referee looks at you, and you stop it. The first-year doesn't speak to you for a week, then asks you to coach them.}\n\n# ---------------- The Lumen Ledger: Editor ----------------\n\n@event lumen_ledger_editor_scoop\ntitle: The Story That Could Close Us\ntrack: lumen_ledger\nrank: 3\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 10\nart: paper\ntags: club\nscene: A cramped newspaper office at night, a student editor reading a draft under a lamp, a stack of evidence beside it and the clock showing late.\ntext: {A reporter brings you a story about a governor of the academy, apparently true and well sourced, and dangerous.|Another story with teeth, on your desk with a dawn deadline.|Nessa calls it \"the kind of story that closes papers\". She says it admiringly.} {The evidence is a stack of letters.|The source wants to stay unnamed.|The printer needs the pages by dawn.}\n> left: Run it on the front page\n  challenge research expert:\n    win:\n      perform lumen_ledger +large\n      renown +medium\n      alignment +tiny\n      say: {Every fact holds. The Ledger sells out by breakfast; the governor resigns by supper. Nessa frames the front page.|It runs, it holds, and the castle talks of nothing else for a week.|You run it with the printer waiting, and every fact holds. Nessa says it's the story that closes papers, and it closes someone else's career instead.}\n    lose:\n      perform lumen_ledger -medium\n      renown -small\n      gain detention\n      say: {One fact was wrong, and it's the one they sue over. The Ledger prints a retraction; you write it. Detention, too, for good measure.|It runs, with a mistake in it. The governor's lawyers are very polite, and very thorough.|You run it by dawn, and the dawn is too soon. One fact needed another day. The retraction is yours to write.}\n> right: Hold it until it's airtight\n  perform lumen_ledger +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: {You hold it. Another paper in the village runs a thinner version first. Nessa doesn't speak to you for two days, then admits you were right.|You hold it for a month and check every line. When it finally runs, it's bulletproof, and old news.|You hold the presses until it's airtight. The printer charges you for the dawn, and Nessa pays it, grumbling.}\n\n@event lumen_ledger_editor_threat\ntitle: A Letter to the Editor\ntrack: lumen_ledger\nrank: 3\nclass: incident\ntone: grim\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 8\nart: letter\ntags: club danger\nscene: A black-edged letter open on a newspaper desk, a student editor reading it with a cold face, the window behind them dark.\ntext: {A letter to the editor, unsigned, about a story you ran. It knows where you sleep.|Another unsigned letter, the same careful hand.|The letters have stopped being polite.} {It smells of expensive ink.|The seal has been scraped off.|It's been slipped under the office door, not posted.}\n> left: Print it, with a reply\n  renown +small\n  perform lumen_ledger +small\n  mood -medium\n  say: {You print it in full on page two, with three lines of your own beneath. The castle loves it. You check your door twice every night for a month.|The letter goes in the paper. The next one takes a month to come. The waiting for it is worse.|You print the impolite one, impolitely answered. The letters stop. You don't know whether that's good.}\n> right: Take it to the headmistress\n  meet isolde\n  rel isolde +small\n  perform lumen_ledger -tiny\n  say: {She reads it once and puts it in a drawer. \"Leave it with me.\" For a long while, you don't get another. Nessa says that's censorship. It's also sleep.|The headmistress handles it. You don't ask how. The Ledger is quieter for a while.|The headmistress reads the one slipped under the door and asks who has a key to the office. The locks are changed by evening.}\n\n# ---------------- Verdant Circle: Head Gardener ----------------\n\n@event verdant_head_gardener_blight\ntitle: The Blight\ntrack: verdant\nrank: 3\nclass: routine\ntone: grim\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 10\nart: leaf\ntags: club nature\nscene: A greenhouse full of wilting plants, black spots on every leaf, a head gardener holding a sickly seedling up to the light.\ntext: {Blight in the greenhouses, black spots spreading by the hour. The Head Gardener decides what to burn.|The blight is back, in a different house.|Black spots again. You know the smell of it now.} {{around(tobias): Tobias is close to tears.|The Circle's youngest member is close to tears.}|The rarest plant in the collection is at the edge of it.|Maud has come up from the cottage to look.}\n> left: Try an untested cure on everything\n  challenge brew expert unlikely:\n    win:\n      perform verdant +large\n      alchemy +small\n      say: {The cure works, overnight. Every plant lives, even the rare one. Maud says she's never seen it done, and asks for the recipe.|By morning the spots are fading. The Circle sings, badly, in the greenhouse.|Maud watches you try the cure and says nothing until it works. Then she asks for the recipe, and you give it.}\n    lose:\n      perform verdant -medium\n      mood -medium\n      say: {The cure kills what the blight hadn't. You burn the whole house. {around(tobias): Tobias helps|The Circle helps}, and nobody blames you, which is worse.|It fails. You lose half the collection, and all of your confidence for a week.|Maud watches the cure fail and helps you burn what's left. \"Everyone loses a house once,\" she says. \"Once.\"}\n> right: Burn the sick plants now\n  perform verdant +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {You burn a third of the house and save the rest. It's the right call and it smells of grief.|You burn the sick ones before it spreads. The rare plant is among them.|Maud nods at what you burn and points at one plant you missed. You burn that too. She was right.}\n\n@event verdant_head_gardener_show\ntitle: The Valley Fair\ntrack: verdant\nrank: 3\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 9\nart: flower\ntags: club nature\nscene: A crowded show tent in a village field, prize vegetables on trestle tables, a student head gardener carrying an enormous potted plant toward the judges.\ntext: {The valley's flower show, and the Verdant Circle's entry is yours to choose.|The flower show again; the judges remember you.|The show tent smells of crushed grass and rivalry.} {A farmer's pumpkin is the size of a cart.|Thornwick has sent a team.|The judges are three old women who have seen everything.}\n> left: Enter the night-blooming lily\n  challenge brew adept- unlikely wild:\n    win:\n      perform verdant +medium\n      renown +small\n      gold +small\n      say: {It opens at dusk in front of the judges, as if on cue. First prize, and a purse, and a drawing in the village paper.|The lily wins, narrowly, over a cabbage. Nobody will forget the cabbage's grower's face.|The three old judges watch the lily open and say nothing. Then all three write \"first\" at once.}\n    lose:\n      perform verdant -tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: {It refuses to open. The cabbage wins. The lily blooms at midnight, for nobody, in your arms.|Second place, to Thornwick. {around(tobias): Tobias says|The Circle says} second is good. It isn't.|The three old judges have seen night-blooming lilies before. They give it a polite second and the cabbage a ribbon.}\n> right: Enter the reliable roses\n  perform verdant +small\n  say: {Third prize for the roses. Solid, respectable, forgettable.|The roses place. The Circle is pleased; the Circle is also bored.|The three old judges approve of roses. Second prize, and a long talk about pruning you'll actually use.}\n\n# ---------------- Library Assistant: Senior Clerk ----------------\n\n@event library_senior_banned_book\ntitle: A Request for a Banned Book\ntrack: library_assistant\nrank: 3\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 10\nart: book\ntags: job study\nscene: A library desk at closing time, a nervous first-year holding a request slip for a chained book, a senior clerk with the keys looking down at it.\ntext: {A first-year brings you a request slip for a chained book. Senior clerks hold the keys, and the rules say no.|Another request for a chained book, from a student who looks desperate.|They've heard you hold the keys. The slips keep coming.} {The first-year's essay depends on it.|The slip is signed by a professor, perhaps.|The book in question bites.}\n> left: Fetch it, against the rules\n  challenge deceive adept-:\n    win:\n      wage\n      gold +small\n      alignment -tiny\n      say: {You fetch it, watch them read it, and chain it back before closing. Nobody knows. The first-year passes with honours and leaves you a cake with crowns folded under it.|The book goes out and comes back, with a grateful envelope. The rules bent, and didn't break.|You fetch it in gloves. It bites the glove and not the reader. The essay is excellent, and the glove is a loss.}\n    lose:\n      wage short\n      perform library_assistant -medium\n      say: {The head librarian finds the chain unlocked. Half your pay docked and a long, quiet talk.|Caught, with the key in your hand, and docked for it. You're still Senior Clerk; nobody quite knows how.|It bites the reader, and the head librarian sees the bandage. Your pay is docked, and the reader's thanks are muffled.}\n> right: Refuse\n  wage\n  perform library_assistant +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {You say no. The first-year leaves in tears. The head librarian, who heard, nods.|The rules hold. So does your conscience, more or less.|You say no, and show them the book from a safe distance. They see it snap at the air and stop asking.}\n\n@event library_senior_night_flood\ntitle: Water in the Stacks\ntrack: library_assistant\nrank: 3\nclass: incident\ntone: grim\nrepeat: 2\nweight: 8\nart: wave\ntags: job danger\nscene: A flooded lower library level at night, water lapping at the bottom shelves, a senior clerk wading with an armful of books.\ntext: {A pipe bursts in the lower stacks at the end of your shift, and the senior clerk on duty is you.|Water in the stacks again. The pipes are older than the books.|The lower level floods every spring now.} {The rarest books are on the bottom shelf.|The water is rising.|Someone is shouting for help two floors up.}\n> left: Save the rare books first\n  wage overtime\n  perform library_assistant +medium\n  health -medium\n  say: {You carry armful after armful up the stair in freezing water until long after closing. Every rare book saved. Your boots are not.|You save the rarest and lose the common ones, and the head librarian, arriving at last, hugs you, which nobody has ever seen.|You save the rare books and then go up for the shouting. It's a first-year stuck on a ladder. You save them too.}\n> right: Run for the head librarian\n  wage\n  perform library_assistant -tiny\n  say: {By the time she comes, a shelf is lost. \"You did right to fetch me,\" she says, and looks at the shelf.|She takes charge. You carry what she points at.|You run for the head librarian, past the shouting two floors up. She sends you back for the shouter first. She was right.}\n\n# ---------------- The Gilded Newt: Manager ----------------\n\n@event tavern_manager_inspector\ntitle: The Inspector Calls\ntrack: tavern\nrank: 3\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 10\nart: cup\ntags: job money\nscene: A village tavern in the morning, a stern inspector in a long coat running a finger along a bar, a student manager following with a ledger.\ntext: {The licensing inspector is here, unannounced, and the landlady is away. The manager is you.|The inspector again, with the same long coat and longer list.|The inspector nods at you like an old enemy.} {The cellar has a leak.|The ledger has a page you'd rather they didn't read.|There's a goblin asleep in the back booth.}\n> left: Show everything, honestly\n  wage\n  challenge negotiate adept:\n    win:\n      perform tavern +large\n      gold +small\n      say: {The inspector finds the leak, notes it and passes the Newt with a warning. The landlady, back, gives you a bonus and her late mother's tankard.|A clean bill, almost. The inspector shakes your hand.|The inspector finds the goblin in the back booth, who turns out to be licensed. A clean bill, and a very long afternoon.}\n    lose:\n      perform tavern -medium\n      gold -small\n      say: {The fine is steep, the landlady takes a share out of your wages, and her face is steeper.|Three infractions. The till covers most of the fine. Your own wages cover the rest.|The inspector wakes the goblin in the back booth, who has opinions about inspectors. The fine is for the opinions.}\n> right: Steer them away from the cellar\n  wage\n  alignment -tiny\n  challenge deceive adept:\n    win:\n      perform tavern +medium\n      say: {The inspector never goes downstairs. You fix the leak that night with your own hands, guiltily.|A clean bill, by omission. You don't tell the landlady how.|You steer the inspector past the back booth and the goblin sleeps on. A clean bill, by the narrowest of margins.}\n    lose:\n      perform tavern -medium\n      gain watched\n      say: {The inspector goes straight to the cellar. The fine doubles for \"obstruction\". Bram hears of it somehow.|Caught steering. The inspector writes your name down in a separate book.|The goblin in the back booth wakes and gives the inspector a tour. Of the cellar.}\n\n@event tavern_manager_grisk_offer\ntitle: An Investor\ntrack: tavern\nrank: 3\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 2\nweight: 8\nwhen: met(grisk) and not flag(grisk_gone)\nart: pouch\ntags: job money danger\nscene: A goblin in a velvet waistcoat sitting at a tavern's best table, a bag of coin and a contract in front of him, a student manager standing opposite.\ntext: {Grisk would like to invest in the Newt, with new chairs and a second fire, \"and a little room upstairs for my associates\".|Grisk again, with a bigger bag of coin.|\"My offer stands,\" says Grisk, \"and grows.\"} {The chairs really are falling apart.|The landlady is away.|The bag of coin is very heavy.}\n> left: Take his money for the chairs\n  wage\n  gold +medium\n  alignment -small\n  perform tavern +small\n  flag grisk_contact\n  find lead_grisk_jetty\n  say: {New chairs, a second fire, a fat \"manager's commission\" in your pocket, and Grisk's associates upstairs on Thursdays. Trade is up. So is the noise, at midnight.|Grisk pays you a finder's cut in a sealed envelope. The Newt has never looked better. The upstairs room has never been busier, and you don't go up there.|You take the heavy bag. Grisk's offer grows into the upstairs, then the cellar, then your evenings. The commission is excellent.}\n> right: Turn him down\n  wage\n  perform tavern -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {\"Your loss,\" says Grisk, and means it as a threat. The chairs stay wobbly.|You say no. Grisk tips his hat and drinks somewhere else for a month, which hurts trade.|You turn the heavier bag down too. Grisk laughs, and says he likes a manager with standards. He'll be back.}\n\n# ---------------- Apothecary Clerk: Assistant Apothecary ----------------\n\n@event apothecary_assistant_poison\ntitle: A Dangerous Prescription\ntrack: apothecary_clerk\nrank: 3\nclass: routine\ntone: grim\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 10\nart: bottle\ntags: job alchemy danger\nscene: An apothecary counter, a prescription slip with a dose underlined in red, a young assistant alone behind the counter frowning at it.\ntext: {A prescription comes in from a village healer with a dose ten times too strong. Pell is out. Assistant apothecaries decide.|Another slip with a strange dose. You've learned to read the healer's handwriting, which doesn't help.|A dose that would fell a horse, for an old shepherd.} {The patient's grown son is waiting.|The healer is a day's ride away.|It might be a slip of the pen. It might not.}\n> left: Mix it at a safe dose\n  wage\n  challenge brew adept:\n    win:\n      perform apothecary_clerk +large\n      alchemy +small\n      say: {You work out what the healer meant and mix that. The patient recovers. Pell, back, reads the slip and goes white, then pats your shoulder for a long time.|Right dose, right remedy. The healer writes later to thank you, embarrassed.|You mix the shepherd a dose that wouldn't fell a sparrow. It works. The healer, told, sends Pell a very apologetic letter.}\n    lose:\n      perform apothecary_clerk -medium\n      mood -medium\n      say: {Your safe dose is too weak. The patient recovers slowly, and the son tells the whole village.|You get it nearly right. Nearly is a word you stop using for a while.|You cut the dose so far that it does nothing. The shepherd comes back, unwell, and Pell has to sort it out.}\n> right: Refuse until the healer confirms\n  wage short\n  perform apothecary_clerk +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: {A day's wait. The patient's son shouts at you. The healer confirms the slip was wrong. You were right, and it was a long day.|You send a rider. The answer comes at midnight. You stay up for it.|It was a slip of the pen. The healer confirms by midnight, and you mix the right dose by one.}\n\n@event apothecary_assistant_rival_shop\ntitle: A Shop Across the Square\ntrack: apothecary_clerk\nrank: 3\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 2\nweight: 8\nactor: pell\nart: bottle\ntags: job money\nscene: A gleaming new apothecary shop across a village square, its window full of bright bottles, an old apothecary peering at it from his own doorway.\ntext: {A new apothecary has opened across the square, all brass and bright bottles and half Pell's prices.|The shop across the square has a queue now.|Pell has started calling the new shop \"that brass insult\".} {Their remedies are mostly coloured water.|They've hired a singer for the doorway.|Pell's daughters have written to ask what's wrong.}\n> left: Expose their coloured water\n  wage\n  challenge research adept-:\n    win:\n      perform apothecary_clerk +medium\n      renown +small\n      say: {You buy a bottle of everything and test them in Pell's back room. The results go on a board in the window. The brass shop closes by the season's end.|Your tests are fair and public. The village comes back to Pell, a little sheepish.|You write to Pell's daughters with your test results. They come home for a week and help you nail the board to the window.}\n    lose:\n      perform apothecary_clerk -small\n      mood -small\n      say: {Their remedies turn out to work, annoyingly. You look petty. Pell says, \"Well, now we know.\"|Inconclusive, and everyone saw you try.|Pell's daughters write back that the brass shop's remedies are fine and that you should worry about Pell's. You do.}\n> right: Lower Pell's prices\n  wage short\n  perform apothecary_clerk +small\n  say: {Cheaper remedies, longer hours, a full shop. Pell grumbles and is secretly delighted.|You match them on price and beat them on care. It's slow, and it works.|You lower the prices and write to the daughters that all is well. It is, nearly. Pell reads your letter over your shoulder and doesn't object.}\n\n# ---------------- Broom Courier: Chief Courier ----------------\n\n@event courier_chief_urgent_crossing\ntitle: The Urgent Crossing\ntrack: broom_courier\nrank: 3\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 10\nart: feather\ntags: job danger\nscene: A courier on a broom flying through a mountain pass in a gale, a red-sealed package clutched to their chest, lightning on the peaks.\ntext: {A red-sealed package for the far side of the mountains, by tomorrow, and only the chief courier flies red seals.|Another red seal, another gale over the pass.|The red seals come to you by name now.} {The pass is in cloud.|A storm is coming in from the west.|The package is humming.}\n> left: Fly the pass tonight\n  wage overtime\n  challenge feat expert wild:\n    win:\n      perform broom_courier +large\n      renown +small\n      health -small\n      say: {Through the pass in the dark, lightning on both sides. You deliver at dawn to a pale woman who breaks the seal and weeps with relief. The bonus is the biggest of your life.|You make it, frozen, at dawn. The recipient signs with shaking hands.|The humming package hums louder in the pass and the wind stays off you. You deliver at dawn and don't ask what it was.}\n    lose:\n      perform broom_courier -medium\n      health -large\n      say: {The gale takes you into a snowfield. You walk the last mile with the package and a broken wrist, and arrive late.|You crash short of the pass and are found by a goatherd. The package arrives a day late, via goat.|The humming package hums a note the lightning likes. You land hard and walk it in, late, with the hair on your arms standing up.}\n> right: Wait for the storm to pass\n  wage\n  perform broom_courier -small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You wait out the storm and fly at dawn. A day late. The recipient is gracious, and the loft-master isn't.|Late but whole. You'll always wonder what was in it.|You wait out the storm with the humming package on the table. It hums all night. You don't sleep.}\n\n@event courier_chief_new_runner\ntitle: The New Runner\ntrack: broom_courier\nrank: 3\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 9\nart: feather\ntags: job\nscene: A courier loft at dawn, a nervous young runner holding a broom the wrong way round, a chief courier showing them the grip.\ntext: {The loft has a new runner, younger than you were, who holds the broom like a mop.|Another new runner, another bad grip.|The new runners all ask for you now.} {Their first route is across the lake.|They've never flown in rain.|Their parents are watching from the gate.}\n> left: Fly their first route with them\n  wage short\n  perform broom_courier +small\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {You fly beside them across the lake, calling the wind. They land shaking and grinning. They'll name a broom after you, they say.|A slow route, flown twice. They'll be good, one day.|You fly their first route with their parents watching from the gate. They land perfectly and pretend it was nothing.}\n> right: Let them learn on their own\n  wage\n  perform broom_courier +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {They come back soaked and late, with every parcel. You were the same.|They learn the hard way. So did you.|You let them go alone with their parents watching. They wobble, recover, and wave to the gate. You breathe again.}\n"},{"path":"content/events/pools/village_home.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Baseline pool: the village below the castle and the family\n#  home (break plan \"home\"). Repeatable routine events.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- The Village ----------------\n\n@event village_barge_crossing\ntitle: The Barge Across\nplace: village\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: wave\ntags: life\nscene: A flat-bottomed barge crossing a mirror-still lake, the seven towers shrinking behind it.\ntext: {The village barge is slower than walking, if you could walk on water.|The bargeman has started nodding at you like a regular.|The bargeman saves you the dry end of the bench.} {season == \"winter\": Ice ticks against the hull.|{season == \"summer\": Dragonflies ride the bow for free.|{A first-year is being sick, very politely.|Two prefects are pretending not to hold hands.|Someone is reading aloud to a crate of chickens.}}}\n> left: Read on the crossing\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You finish a chapter and miss the towers turning gold behind you.|You read the same paragraph four times; the lake keeps interrupting.|You look up only when the hull bumps the quay, and feel you've skipped something.}\n> right: Chat with the bargeman\n  charm +small\n  gold -tiny\n  say: {He tells you the lake was rougher in his father's day, and expects a tip for the history.|He has opinions about every tower and charges for none of them, except in tips.|He asks after your exams as if they were a sick relative. You tip him for the sympathy.}\n\n@event village_square_errand\ntitle: One Thing in the Square\nplace: village\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: coin\ntags: social\ncast:\n  friend: any where rel(friend) >= 6 and tagged(friend, student) and around(friend)\nscene: A crooked village market square, two students weaving between stalls, one already carrying too many parcels.\ntext: {friend} has come down to the village with you \"for one thing\", which turns out to be several. {First, a hat.|You've learned that \"one thing\" means at least three.|You've stopped asking what the one thing is.} {A stallholder is selling candied chestnuts in paper twists.|A tinker is mending a cauldron with a great deal of swearing.|The market cat has chosen {friend} and will not be moved.}\n> left: Carry the parcels\n  rel friend +small\n  health -tiny\n  queue village_errand_parcel in 2 with friend chance unlikely\n  say: {You come back up the hill with an armful of string-tied parcels and sore wrists.|{friend} buys you a chestnut as payment. It is not enough payment.|One parcel clinks suspiciously. You decide not to ask.}\n> right: Talk {them} out of it\n  charm +small\n  rel friend -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  chance likely:\n    queue village_errand_third_thing in 1 with friend chance unlikely\n    say: {You talk {friend} out of the third thing. {They} sulk{s} the whole way back to the barge.|You win. {friend} says you'd make a terrible shopkeeper and a worse friend, then takes your arm anyway.|You talk {friend} out of the third thing. The market cat follows {them} home anyway, which is a sort of purchase.}\n  else:\n    say: Your argument is excellent. {friend} buys the thing anyway, and now you've been wrong in public.\n\n@event village_last_barge\ntitle: The Last Barge\nplace: village\nslot: evening\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: lantern\ntags: life\nscene: A lamplit village quay at dusk, the last barge pulling at its rope, a student hesitating at the edge of the square.\ntext: {The last barge back leaves at the bell, and the bell is ringing.|The last-barge bell, and you're nowhere near the quay. The bargeman knows your running by now.|The bell. Of course the bell.} {season == \"winter\": Snow is coming down thick over the square.|{The inn has started a song with too many verses.|The lamps are only half lit.|The lake has gone the colour of pewter.}} {final_year: Not many evenings like this are left.|You could run. You could stay.}\n> left: Run for it\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You make it with a leap and one wet boot.|You make it; your scarf does not.|The bargeman holds the rope a heartbeat longer than he should. You owe him.}\n> right: Stay a little longer\n  mood +small\n  chance even:\n    if gold > 0:\n      gold -small\n      say: {A fisherman rows you back for a ruinous fare and a story you didn't ask for.|You pay the night ferryman far too much. He rows like he has somewhere better to be.|The fisherman who rows you back knows you by now and charges you the regular's rate, which is still a great deal.}\n    else:\n      health -small\n      say: You have no fare for the night ferryman, so you sleep on a bench under the Newt's eaves and wake with a cat on your feet.\n  else:\n    health -small\n    say: {You sleep on a bench under the Newt's eaves and wake up with a cat on your feet.|The first barge of morning finds you yawning on the quay.|You sleep under the Newt's eaves again. The cat has started saving you the warm end of the bench.}\n\n# ---------------- The Gilded Newt ----------------\n\n@event gilded_newt_fireside\ntitle: By the Newt's Fire\nplace: gilded_newt\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: flame\ntags: life\nscene: A low-beamed tavern with a roaring hearth, a student tucked into the corner seat beside it with a steaming cup.\ntext: {The Gilded Newt's fire has never gone out, and the landlady says it never will while she lives.|The landlady chalks your name on her slate before you've ordered.|Your corner by the fire is free. The regulars leave it for you now.} {season == \"winter\": Snow hisses off the boots of everyone who comes in.|{A dog is asleep with its paws in the hearth.|Someone has hung wet socks on the fire-irons.|A farmer is explaining turnips to nobody.}}\n> left: Order a cider and stay\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: {The cider is warm and spiced and costs exactly what the landlady says it does.|You stay until your cheeks are red and your worries are smaller.|The landlady brings the cider without being asked, and charges for it all the same.}\n> right: Work by the firelight\n  lore +small\n  say: {You get two pages done between three toasts and a fiddle.|The noise is terrible, and somehow you concentrate better than in the library.|A regular reads over your shoulder and corrects your spelling of \"wyvern\".}\n\n@event gilded_newt_card_game\ntime: evening night\ntitle: Cards at the Newt\nplace: gilded_newt\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: cards\ntags: money\nscene: A smoky back room in a tavern, a card game around a scarred table and one empty chair.\ntext: {There's a card game in the back room of the Gilded Newt, and an empty chair.|The empty chair in the back room has a cushion on it now, as if it's been waiting.|{flag(newt_cards_played): The card players greet you by the name of your worst hand.|The card players nod you towards the chair as if you were a regular.}} {Tonight it's a tinker, a widow and a man who shuffles like a conjuror.|Tonight the stakes are copper and pride.|Tonight a second-year from Duskgate is losing very cheerfully.}\n> left: Take the empty chair\n  hint: The stake is a few crowns a hand.\n  gold -small\n  flag newt_cards_played\n  challenge deceive apprentice+ wild:\n    win:\n      gold +medium\n      mood +tiny\n      say: {You win a handful of crowns and a reputation for an honest face.|You win, and the widow asks where you learned to bluff like that.|The Duskgate second-year loses to you as cheerfully as to everyone. You win a few crowns and an invitation to lose them back.}\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {You lose your crowns slowly, and then all at once.|The tinker takes your last coin and buys you a drink with it, which somehow makes it worse.|The Duskgate second-year wins for once, delighted. You lose your stake and keep your good humour, mostly.}\n> right: Watch for the tells\n  guile +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You spend the evening watching. The man who shuffles like a conjuror scratches his ear when he lies.|You learn a great deal about bluffing and nothing about having fun.|Someone asks if you're playing or haunting. You leave before you have to answer.}\n\n@event gilded_newt_corner_table\ntime: evening night\ntitle: The Corner Table\nplace: gilded_newt\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: cup\ntags: social\ncast:\n  here: present(place) where met(here) and tagged(here, student)\nscene: A tavern corner table with a half-empty jug of cider, someone familiar waving a student over.\ntext: {here} is at the corner table with {a jug of cider and no plans to leave.|the house jug, which the landlady refills for whoever sits there.|the table's jug. The landlady nods at you as you come in, as if you're expected.} \"{Sit down, you're blocking the fire.|You look like you need a drink. I look like I need company.|Don't tell anyone you saw me here.}\" {season == \"winter\": Snow is piling up against the window.|The window is open to the night.}\n> left: Sit down for one drink\n  rel here +small\n  gold -tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {One drink becomes three stories and a long walk to the barge.|{here} tells you something true halfway down the jug, then pretends not to have.|You stay later than you meant to. It's worth the morning.}\n> right: Wave and keep walking\n  lore +small\n  rel here -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You wave and get back to your book. {here} toasts your back, a little hurt.|{term: You plead an early lecture. It's even true, and you read for it before bed.|You plead an early start, and read in your room instead.}|You wave. {here} raises the jug at you as if you'll be back. You will.}\n\n# ---------------- Mabel's Tea Shop ----------------\n\n@event mabels_the_door\ntitle: Mabel Reads the Door\nplace: mabels\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: cup\ntags: life\nscene: A tiny tea shop crammed with tins, the shop-bell over the door still swinging, a shrewd woman behind the counter already reaching for a tin.\ntext: {Mabel looks up as you open the door and is already reaching for a tin.|Mabel watches you open the door the way a doctor watches a cough.|You've tried opening Mabel's door every way you can think of. She still knows.} {The bell over the door rings a little flat today.|There's a queue of two, which for Mabel's is a crowd.|A cake is cooling on the counter, out of reach and smug about it.}\n> left: Let Mabel choose your tea\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: {She brings you something smoky and says, \"For the week you've had.\" You hadn't told her about the week.|She brings you something with rose in it and won't explain why. It helps.|It's mint. It's always mint when you've been lying to yourself, apparently.}\n> right: Fake a cheerful entrance\n  challenge deceive journeyman:\n    win:\n      mood +small\n      say: {She brings you the cheerful tea and waves your coins away, \"for the performance\". It's so good you actually cheer up, which may have been her plan all along.|Mabel looks fooled, and doesn't charge you. Then she adds a biscuit, which means she wasn't.|You've practised the door. Mabel gives you the cheerful tea and a long look, and charges you for neither.}\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {She brings you a pot labelled, in her own hand, \"for people pretending\". She doesn't charge for it, which is somehow worse.|\"Nice try, dear,\" says Mabel, and brings the strong one, on the house.|Mabel watches your cheerful entrance the way a doctor watches a cough, and brings the strong one, free.}\n\n@event mabels_window_seat\ntitle: The Window Seat\nplace: mabels\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: book\ntags: study\nscene: A window seat in a tea shop looking across a still lake to seven distant towers, a teapot and open notes on the sill.\ntext: {Mabel's window seat looks out over the lake to the castle, and it's free.|Mabel has stopped asking if you're waiting for someone at the window seat.|Your window seat. Mabel puts a cushion on it when she sees you on the quay.} {season == \"winter\": Frost has drawn ferns on the glass.|{season == \"spring\": Blossom keeps landing on the sill.|{The towers look small from here, almost friendly.|Rain streaks the glass and blurs the towers.|Below, the barges come and go, slow as thoughts.}}}\n> left: Make one pot last all day\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You get through a whole chapter on the one free pot Mabel gives window-seat readers, under her increasingly pointed looks.|By the third hour the tea is cold and Mabel is dusting very close to your table.|You finish your notes. Mabel says nothing, which is worse.}\n> right: Order cake with it\n  mood +small\n  gold -small\n  say: {flag(mabel_lemon): {Mabel has started cutting the lemon cake before you sit down.|Mabel brings the lemon without asking. You eat it slowly while the barges come and go.|The lemon cake, the window, the lake. The work can wait.}|Mabel has three cakes. You pick the lemon, and it's worth every crown.}\n  flag mabel_lemon\n\n@event mabels_forty_teas\ntitle: Forty Kinds of Tea\nplace: mabels\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: cup\ntags: social\ncast:\n  friend: any where rel(friend) >= 6 and tagged(friend, student) and around(friend)\nscene: Two students at a small tea-shop table crowded with mismatched teapots, one writing in a notebook.\ntext: {friend} wants to try one of Mabel's forty teas, and Mabel insists on choosing. {Today she starts a chart of who has tried what.|Mabel's chart of your friends' teas is pinned behind the counter now; {friend}'s column gets a tick.|Mabel's chart runs to a second sheet. {friend}'s column is the shortest, and Mabel looks at it pointedly.} {Mabel pretends not to be interested.|Mabel has opinions about the order.|Mabel insists on the order, and on the chart.}\n> left: Pay for both pots\n  gold -small\n  rel friend +small\n  say: {{friend} pronounces today's tea \"bracing\", which means awful. You drink yours anyway.|You pay. Mabel marks you on the chart as a patron of the arts.|{friend} declares it the best tea in the village, then sniffs yours to be sure.}\n> right: Let {friend} pay this time\n  mood +tiny\n  rel friend -tiny\n  say: {{friend} pays, and calls you unadventurous for ordering your usual. Your tea is perfect, thank you.|\"Coward,\" says {friend} fondly, paying for your usual and their something purple.|{friend} pays, grumbling, and drinks something that smells of bonfires and looks betrayed by it.}\n\n# ---------------- Grimsby's Books ----------------\n\n@event grimsbys_wrong_book\ntitle: Not the Book You Wanted\nplace: grimsbys_books\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: book\ntags: study\nscene: A bookshop so crowded the shelves lean together, an elderly bookseller on a ladder triumphantly holding up the wrong book.\ntext: {You ask Mr Grimsby for a textbook. He vanishes into the shelves and comes back with something else entirely.|You ask Mr Grimsby for one thing. He brings you another, with great confidence.|Mr Grimsby sees you coming and is already halfway up a ladder, looking for the wrong book.} {Today's is a cookery book with a whole chapter on eels.|Today's is a history of a town that may not exist.|Today's is a guide to the moods of weather.}\n> left: Buy what he found\n  gold -small\n  lore +small\n  say: {It's useless and wonderful. You read half of it on the barge.|You learn more than you meant to about something you never meant to learn.|There's a pressed flower in it, and a name you don't know on the flyleaf.}\n> right: Search the shelves yourself\n  challenge research apprentice wild:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      say: {You find it behind a stack of almanacs, exactly where he said it wasn't, and read the chapter you need at the counter.|You find it. Mr Grimsby looks wounded and lets you read it at the counter for free, since you found it yourself.|You find it yourself, above the weather guides. Mr Grimsby insists it's a weather guide too, in a way.}\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: {A shelf leans on you for support. You hold it up for a long time.|An afternoon, a sneezing fit, and no book.|You search and find only more guides to the moods of weather. The weather today is a sulk.}\n\n@event grimsbys_damp_barrow\ntitle: The Damp Barrow\nplace: grimsbys_books\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: scroll\ntags: study\nscene: A wooden barrow of damp secondhand books outside a crooked bookshop, a student reading one in the drizzle.\ntext: {Outside Grimsby's there's a barrow of secondhand books, a coin apiece, slightly damp.|The barrow has a new sign: \"NO READING WITHOUT BUYING\". Nobody obeys it.|The barrow. You know its smell now: rain, glue and other people's thoughts.} {season == \"winter\": Someone has thrown a sack over it against the sleet.|{The top book is about knots.|A cat is asleep on the poetry.|A card in the corner says \"NOT RUDE, JUST OLD\".}}\n> left: Buy a damp book\n  gold -tiny\n  lore +small\n  say: {It dries out wavy and full of someone else's underlinings.|The margins are better than the book. Someone argued with every page.|The last page is a shopping list in faded ink. You find it oddly moving.}\n> right: Read it standing in the rain\n  mood +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You read until your feet are cold and Mr Grimsby taps on the window.|You read a whole chapter in the drizzle and don't buy it. You feel like a thief and a scholar at once.|A previous owner has written \"Don't believe this bit\" in the margin. You don't.}\n\n@event grimsbys_last_copy\ntitle: The Last Copy\nplace: grimsbys_books\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: book\ntags: social\ncast:\n  friend: any where rel(friend) >= 6 and tagged(friend, student) and around(friend)\nscene: Two students in a cramped bookshop, both holding the same book, neither letting go.\ntext: There's one copy left of the book you need, and {friend} has {their} hand on it too. {You both laugh. Neither of you lets go.|This keeps happening to you at Grimsby's. You're starting to suspect the shop.|Mr Grimsby swears he ordered two. It never arrives as two.} {\"I saw it first,\" {they} say{s}.|\"We could share,\" {they} offer{s}, unconvincingly.|Mr Grimsby says there might be another in the cellar. There won't.}\n> left: Let {them} have it\n  rel friend +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {{friend} promises to lend it to you \"very soon\". You know what that means.|You let go. {friend} buys you a bun on the way back, which nearly makes up for it.|{friend} reads it on the barge and tells you all the best parts, in the wrong order.}\n> right: Haggle for it\n  gold -tiny\n  challenge negotiate vs friend.charm:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      rel friend -tiny\n      say: {Mr Grimsby takes your side for reasons of his own. {friend} won't speak to you until the quay.|You win it fair and square, and {friend} says so in a voice that means the opposite.|You haggle, and win. Mr Grimsby promises {friend} the second copy he ordered. You both know it won't come.}\n    lose:\n      gold +tiny\n      rel friend -tiny\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {{friend} outbids you, and Mr Grimsby hands your crowns back with a sympathetic look.|You argue so long that a third-year buys it while you're both talking. Mr Grimsby gives your crowns back.|{friend} wins, and Mr Grimsby promises you the second copy. It never arrives as two.}\n\n# ---------------- Pell & Daughters ----------------\n\n@event pell_apothecary_drawers\ntitle: A Thousand Drawers\nplace: pell_apothecary\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: bottle\ntags: study\nscene: An apothecary wall of a thousand tiny labelled drawers, brass handles gleaming, a student reading the labels on tiptoe.\ntext: {Pell & Daughters has a thousand drawers, each labelled in a different hand.|You find a drawer you'd swear wasn't there last time.|You've read half the labels in the shop. The other half seem to be reading you.} Today it's {\"Moonwort. Do not hum near.\"|\"Dried courage, low quality.\"|\"Owl pellets (sorted).\"} {season == \"winter\": The shop smells of camphor and wet wool.|The shop smells of mint and something burnt.}\n> left: Buy a pinch of it\n  gold -tiny\n  alchemy +small\n  say: {One of the daughters weighs it on scales finer than an eyelash and tells you what it's really for.|It smells of the shop all the way back across the lake.|Old Pell counts the grains himself and warns you about three things you hadn't planned to do.}\n> right: Just read the labels\n  lore +small\n  say: {Old Pell watches you over his spectacles the whole time, as if labels could be stolen.|You copy a dozen labels into your notebook. A daughter asks if you're planning to open a rival shop.|You learn a great deal. Old Pell sighs every time you touch a handle.}\n\n@event pell_apothecary_queue\ntitle: Symptoms in the Queue\nplace: pell_apothecary\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: cross\ntags: social\nscene: A queue of villagers at an apothecary counter, all turning to a student in academy robes.\ntext: {The queue at Pell & Daughters notices your academy robes. A woman shows you her rash.|Word has got round the queue at Pell's that you're \"the young doctor\".|The queue parts for you as if you were a physician. You are not a physician.} {A farmer has a cough like a door hinge.|A boy has a frog stuck to his hand, and the frog seems content.|An old man wants something for \"the usual\", and won't say what the usual is.}\n> left: Offer some advice\n  challenge brew apprentice:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      mood +tiny\n      say: {Your advice is sound. One of the daughters nods, which in this shop is a medal.|The farmer tries your remedy and comes back to thank you. Old Pell grumbles about lost trade.|You guess the old man's \"usual\" from the way he walks. Old Pell grunts, which is agreement.}\n    lose:\n      renown -tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: {Old Pell corrects you in front of the whole queue, kindly, which is worse.|Your advice would have worked on a horse. A daughter says so.|You guess the old man's \"usual\" wrong, out loud. He tells the queue what it actually is. Everyone wishes he hadn't.}\n> right: Study a drawer intently\n  guile +small\n  say: {You pretend to be fascinated by a drawer of buttons until the queue moves on.|You say \"Hm\" like Professor Thorne and leave quickly.|You become very interested in the floor. The woman with the rash tells her friend you were rude.}\n\n@event pell_apothecary_tonic\ntitle: The Daughters' Tonic\nplace: pell_apothecary\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: potion\ntags: life\nscene: Two apothecary's daughters leaning over a counter, offering a student a small bottle of something that fizzes.\ntext: {One of Pell's daughters has a new tonic and needs someone to try it. Her father must not know.|The daughters have another tonic, and a notebook with your name at the top of a column.|The daughters see you and reach under the counter before you've said hello.} {This one is green and fizzes.|This one smells of pears and thunder.|This one is clear, which they say is the worrying kind.}\n> left: Drink it down\n  chance likely:\n    health +small\n    mood +tiny\n    say: {You feel wonderful, and briefly taller. The daughters take notes.|Your cold vanishes. So, for an hour, does your sense of smell.|The clear one does nothing at all, then everything at once. You feel marvellous. The daughters look nervous.}\n  else:\n    health -small\n    say: {You hiccup bubbles all the way back to the barge.|Your ears go warm and stay warm. The daughters take very interested notes.|The clear one turns your tongue blue. The daughters write \"clear: not clear\" in the notebook.}\n> right: Buy a proper remedy\n  gold -small\n  health +small\n  say: {Old Pell sells you something brown and reliable. The daughters look disappointed in you.|You buy the dull, tested thing. It works, dully.|A daughter wraps it and murmurs that you'll be sorry you missed the green one.}\n\n# ---------------- The Bakery ----------------\n\n@event bakery_bun_queue\ntitle: The Bun Queue\nplace: bakery\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: meal\ntags: life\nscene: A queue of villagers and students outside a little bakery at dawn, steam and the smell of cinnamon pouring out of the door.\ntext: {There's a queue outside the bakery for the afternoon batch, and it smells like a promise.|The baker's son counts heads in the bun queue and knows you by your scarf.|The bun queue. You know everyone in it by their order.} {season == \"winter\": Breath steams in the cold.|{season == \"autumn\": Leaves skitter round everyone's ankles.|{The baker's son is singing off-key.|A goat has joined the queue and nobody has the heart to send it away.|The queue is swapping recipes it will never use.}}}\n> left: Buy a bag for your corridor\n  gold -small\n  renown +small\n  say: {You come back with a warm bag and are, for one evening, the most popular person in your house.|The buns are gone before you've taken your coat off. Someone leaves you a thank-you note on a napkin.|A prefect takes one \"to check it for contraband\". It passes.}\n> right: Take a broken bun from the free tray\n  mood +small\n  say: {The baker's son gives you one that came out lopsided. You eat it on the quay while it's still too hot. Worth every burnt finger.|A broken bun, free: butter, cinnamon and the lake in the afternoon. Nothing has ever been better.|The free tray has one left. You eat it slowly and think about nothing at all.}\n\n@event bakery_oldest_baker\ntitle: The Oldest Baker\nplace: bakery\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: letter\ntags: social\nscene: A very old woman in a flour-dusted apron sitting by warm bread ovens, looking up hopefully as the shop door opens.\ntext: The oldest of the bakers sits by the ovens, too old to knead and too stubborn to leave. {She asks, as she asks every student, whether the post at the castle is reliable these days.|She asks about the castle post. Her daughter gives you a look that says: just answer.|\"Is the post reliable?\" she asks. You've learned the answer she likes.} {The ovens tick as they heat.|Flour hangs in the light.|The bell over the door rings, and she looks up, every time.}\n> left: Sit with her a while\n  lore +small\n  say: {She tells you about her grandmother's grandmother, who waited for a letter. She doesn't say how it ended.|She tells you the family has baked here for two hundred years. She makes it sound like a promise kept.|She falls asleep mid-story. Her daughter tucks a bun into your pocket and says thank you.}\n> right: Help knead the dough\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {The dough fights back. Your arms ache by the time the trays go in.|The baker shows you the turn of the wrist. You don't have it yet.|You knead until your shoulders burn. The first loaf out has your thumbprint on it.}\n\n@event bakery_cooling_rack\ntitle: The Cooling Rack\nplace: bakery\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: door\ntags: life\nscene: A tray of steaming buns cooling on the sill of an open bakery window, a crow and a student both eyeing it.\ntext: {Behind the bakery a window stands open, and on the sill a tray of buns is cooling, unwatched.|The cooling tray is back on the sill, nearer the edge this time. The bakers either trust everyone or are testing you.|The tray, the sill, the open window. It's practically an invitation.} {A crow is eyeing it too.|Two first-years are pretending not to look at it.|Steam curls off the tray into the cold air.}\n> left: Lift one off the sill\n  alignment -tiny\n  challenge deceive apprentice:\n    win:\n      mood +small\n      say: {You get away with it. It tastes slightly of guilt and mostly of cinnamon.|Clean away. The crow watches you with professional respect.|You lift one through the steam. Nobody sees except the crow, who takes the next.}\n    lose:\n      renown -tiny\n      if gold > 0:\n        gold -small\n        say: {A floury hand appears from the window, palm up. You pay double.|Caught by the baker's son, who makes you pay double and tells everyone in the queue.|The steam gives you away. A floury hand takes your coins, twice over.}\n      else:\n        mood -small\n        say: A floury hand appears from the window, palm up. Your pockets are empty, so you spend an hour scrubbing trays instead.\n> right: Ask for yesterday's\n  charm +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {The baker gives you a bag of yesterday's for nothing. They're as hard as roof slates.|You get the broken ones free. You eat one and lose a little faith in bread.|\"For the student who asks,\" says the baker, and hands you a stale one with a wink.}\n\n# ---------------- Home: the family cottage ----------------\n\n@event home_cottage_old_room\ntitle: Your Old Room\nplace: home_cottage\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: bed\ntags: home\nscene: A small childhood bedroom under the eaves, a too-short bed, a window looking out over fields.\ntext: {Your old room is exactly as you left it, except smaller.|Someone has been storing apples in your old room.|Your old room has started to feel like a guest room, and you like it less for that.} {season == \"winter\": There's frost on the inside of the window, as always.|The window won't shut properly in the heat, as always.} Downstairs, the house is busy without you.\n> left: Nap the afternoon away\n  health +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You sleep like a stone. Downstairs, someone has done all your chores, loudly.|You wake to the smell of supper and a pointed silence.|You sleep through the afternoon. You feel human, and slightly ashamed.}\n> right: Go down and help\n  mood +small\n  say: {final_year: {You go down and realise there won't be many more afternoons like this.|You memorise the kitchen without meaning to.|You go down and help, and try to learn how every sound in the house goes, for later.}|{You help with the fire. Nobody says much. Nobody needs to.|The creaky stair announces you. Someone hands you a cup before you've asked.|You go down and are handed the job you always had. Your hands know it before you do.}}\n\n@event home_cottage_list\ntitle: The List on the Table\nplace: home_cottage\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: paper\ntags: home\nscene: A kitchen table with a long handwritten list of chores weighted down by a teapot, a student in academy robes reading it with dismay.\ntext: {&Home has remembered that you know where everything is.|A day home, and already a list.|The list. Your name, underlined.} {has(origin_noble): {&The steward wants the ledgers checked.|The steward wants the tenants' letters answered.|The steward wants you to sit in on a dispute about a fence.}|{has(origin_merchant): {&The shop's stock needs counting.|The shop sign needs repainting.|The shop's ledger has a column nobody can explain.}|{has(origin_hedge): {&Your grandmother wants herbs hung to dry.|Your grandmother wants every jar labelled.|Your grandmother wants the bees told the news.}|{&The woodpile needs splitting.|The hen house door argues.|The well rope has frayed.}}}}\n> left: Work through the whole list\n  if has(origin_noble):\n    lore +tiny\n  else:\n    vigor +tiny\n  mood +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {&By supper the list is done, and someone you love says \"Well.\" It's the highest praise in the house.|It takes all day. You sleep better than you have all term.|You finish, and the list grows a second page in a different hand.}\n> right: Do it with magic\n  challenge enchant using arcana apprentice wild:\n    win:\n      arcana +small\n      mood +tiny\n      say: {&The work does itself while you supervise from a chair. The family is impressed and slightly unnerved.|It works. Your mother says it's cheating, then asks if you can do the gutters.|It works so well that a neighbour asks to borrow you.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      health -tiny\n      say: {&The spell goes slightly wrong. Something is now upside down that wasn't before.|Soot. Everywhere. Someone laughs until they cry, then hands you a broom.|The spell does the list twice, including the things that were already done.}\n\n@event home_cottage_after_supper\ntitle: The Same Question\nplace: home_cottage\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: candle\ntags: home\nscene: A family kitchen after supper by candlelight, dishes drying, everyone turned towards one student.\ntext: After supper, when the dishes are done, someone always asks it. {\"So what will you do, after?\"|\"Still don't know what you'll do after?\" An aunt, this time.|By now you could ask it yourself, in any of their voices, and someone does.} {final_year: This time the answer is close enough to touch.|{The fire pops.|Nobody pretends not to be listening.|Someone pours more tea to give you time.}}\n> left: Tell them the truth\n  charm +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {You say you don't know. It's quiet for a moment, then someone says that's allowed.|You tell them what you're afraid of. It sounds smaller said aloud at this table.|The truth makes your mother go quiet, and then hug you, which you weren't ready for.}\n> right: Give the polished answer\n  guile +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You give the answer you'd give a professor. They're proud. It sits oddly in your chest all night.|You make it sound like a plan. Everyone relaxes except you.|\"Very sensible,\" says an aunt. You feel as if you've told her a story.}\n\n# ---------------- Home: the fields ----------------\n\n@event home_fields_long_rows\ntitle: The Long Rows\nplace: home_fields\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: leaf\ntags: home nature\nscene: A student walking alone between long furrowed rows towards a low wall at the edge of the fields, the horizon wide and empty.\ntext: {You walk the long rows to the field's end, where you used to imagine towers.|You've seen the towers now. The horizon at the end of the rows still pulls.|The walk to the field's end is a habit now. Your feet know it by heart.} {season == \"winter\": The furrows crunch with frost.|The wheat is high and loud with insects.} {A hare bolts.|A crow follows you at a respectful distance.|Smoke rises from a chimney you can't see.}\n> left: Walk the whole boundary\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You come back muddy to the knees and hungry as a first-year.|You walk until your legs ache and your head is finally quiet.|You sit on the low wall where you always sat, and the wind does the thinking.}\n> right: Practise where no one watches\n  arcana +small\n  mana -small\n  say: {You set a scarecrow's hat spinning. Nobody sees. It's glorious.|You practise until your hands tingle and the crows give you a wide berth.|You try the spell you keep failing at school. Out here, with nobody watching, it nearly works.}\n\n@event home_fields_neighbours\ntitle: Neighbours' Work\nplace: home_fields\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: group\ntags: home social\nscene: Neighbours working together at the edge of a field, one waving a student in academy robes over to help.\ntext: {season == \"winter\": The neighbours are hauling wood before the next snow.|The neighbours are bringing in the hay before the weather turns.} {They wave you over without asking if you're busy.|This time somebody has saved you a pair of gloves.|They've stopped waving. They just hand you the gloves.} {The neighbour's boy wants to be turned into a frog.|A farmer calls you \"the scholar\", mostly kindly.|Someone has brought a jug of mostly cider.}\n> left: Lend a hand\n  vigor +small\n  health -small\n  say: {Blisters by noon, and a supper invitation from three households.|You work till dusk. Nobody mentions the academy once, which is restful.|Your back reminds you that books are lighter than anything out here.}\n> right: Bring them all a drink\n  gold -tiny\n  charm +small\n  say: {You come back from the market with a basket and become the most popular person in the field.|They drink to the academy, then to you, then to the weather.|The neighbour's boy asks for a frog again. You give him a biscuit; it's a compromise.}\n\n@event home_fields_pond\ntitle: The Old Pond\nplace: home_fields\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: wave\ntags: home nature\nscene: A pond at the bottom of a field under an old willow, local youngsters daring each other at its edge.\ntext: {season == \"winter\": The pond at the bottom of the fields has frozen over, and the young farmhands are daring each other onto the ice.|The pond at the bottom of the fields is warm and green, and the young farmhands are daring each other off the willow.} {You used to be one of them.|This time they dare you too.|They've started daring you first.} {A dog barks at the whole business.|The blacksmith's apprentice is keeping score.|Your old initials are still carved in the willow.}\n> left: Take the dare\n  chance likely:\n    mood +small\n    vigor +tiny\n    say: {season == \"winter\": {You skate across and back to roaring applause from three farmhands and a dog.|You slide the whole length on your back. It counts.|You skate past your old initials frozen into the willow's roots. The apprentice gives you full marks for nostalgia.}|{You drop off the willow into the green. It's colder than memory, and better.|A perfect splash. The scorekeeper gives it top marks.|You drop off the branch beside your old initials. The score is one you'll be proud of for a week.}}\n  else:\n    health -small\n    mood -tiny\n    say: {season == \"winter\": {You go through the ice up to your knees and limp home to a lecture and a fire.|The ice cracks under you and you scramble back, soaked and laughed at.|You stop to look at your initials, and the ice stops holding you.}|{You belly-flop. The sound carries across three fields. Everyone heard.|You slip off the willow early and land in the reeds, muddy to the ears.|You climb to your old initials and slip on them. The splash is not dignified.}}\n> right: Hold the coats\n  charm +small\n  say: {You hold the coats and referee the arguments. Someone has to be the grown-up now.|You keep score and are accused of bias by every side.|Holding the coats, you feel very old and quite content.}\n\n# ---------------- Home: the market town ----------------\n\n@event home_market_inn_crowd\ntitle: Outside the Inn\nplace: home_market\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: cup\ntags: home social\nscene: A knot of young locals outside a country inn, raising their cups as a student in academy robes walks up.\ntext: {The old crowd is outside the inn, exactly where you left them. \"Look who it is,\" someone says. \"The wizard.\"|The old crowd have been rehearsing the wizard joke. It has a second part now.|The old crowd outside the inn. Fewer of them every break: somebody married, somebody moved away.} {season == \"winter\": They're stamping their feet against the cold.|They're sitting on the inn steps in the long light.}\n> left: Buy a round\n  gold -small\n  mood +small\n  charm +tiny\n  say: {They drink your health and ask if you can make the beer stronger. You can't. You're asked again.|For an hour it's as if you never left.|Someone tells the story of the time you fell in the duck pond. It has grown since you left.}\n> right: Stay for the gossip only\n  guile +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You learn who's courting, who's feuding and whose goat ate whose washing. Somebody mutters that academy folk are tight with a coin.|You keep your crowns in your pocket and get very well informed.|They notice you didn't buy a round. They notice everything.}\n\n@event home_market_charm_seller\ntitle: The Charm Seller\nplace: home_market\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: gem\ntags: home money\nscene: A market stall hung with ribbons and trinkets under a hand-painted sign about academy blessings, a grandmother reaching for her purse.\ntext: {A stall in the market is selling \"genuine Lumenhold charms, blessed in the seven towers\".|The charm seller has new charms and a bigger sign. The claims have grown to match.|The charm seller sees your robes and starts packing up, then thinks better of it.} {has(origin_merchant): You know that markup. You were raised on it.|{A grandmother is buying one for luck.|The charms are bent nails tied with ribbon.|One of them is, worryingly, glowing.}}\n> left: Call out the fakes\n  alignment +tiny\n  challenge negotiate apprentice+:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {The crowd turns on him. A grandmother gets her coin back and gives you a cake. The seller gives you a look.|He folds, and offers you a share of the trade. You decline. He seems genuinely surprised.|You point out the glowing one. The seller goes pale and hands it to you to take away, free. The crowd cheers you anyway.}\n    lose:\n      renown -tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: {The crowd takes his side. \"Academy snob,\" someone says, buying two.|He out-talks you in front of everyone. You'd admire it if it were happening to someone else.|You call out the glowing one, and it glows brighter, as if insulted. The crowd decides it's genuine and buys the lot.}\n> right: Buy one for an aunt\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: {Your aunt will love it. It's a bent nail. She will love it anyway.|You buy one. It's useless, and the old woman beside you beams at you with approval.|You buy one that glows a little. You decide not to think about why.}\n\n@event home_market_post_office\ntitle: Letters to the Castle\nplace: home_market\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: letter\ntags: home\nscene: A post office the size of a cupboard, a clerk ceremoniously weighing a stack of letters addressed to a castle on a lake.\ntext: {The town's post office is the size of a cupboard, and its clerk has never posted anything to a magic academy.|The clerk has started weighing your letters with ceremony.|The clerk reaches for the castle stamp before you speak.} {season == \"winter\": The window is fogged; it smells of sealing wax.|Flies wander in to read the notices.} {final_year: Soon you'll write to the castle from elsewhere.|You think about who at the castle you miss.}\n> left: Write to everyone\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: {You write until your hand cramps. The clerk sighs at the stack.|You draw the cottage for someone who has never seen one.|You start one letter with \"I miss\" and decide to leave it that way.}\n> right: Write the essay you owe\n  lore +small\n  say: {You write the essay on the clerk's counter, between parcels. It's not bad.|You post an essay to a professor. The clerk asks if it'll get you into trouble. Probably.|You finish it and post it. It's the dullest thing the clerk has ever weighed.}\n\n# Follow-ups to the errand (playtest 2, N10), queued by its options with the same friend.\n\n@event village_errand_parcel\ntitle: The Parcel That Clinked\ntrigger: queued\ndefer: off_campus or not around(npc)\nactor: npc\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nart: pouch\ntags: social\nscene: A student unwrapping a string-tied parcel on a dormitory bed, a small glass jar of honey and a note inside.\ntext: {npc} turns up at your door with one of the parcels you carried up the hill: the one that clinked. \"This one was for you,\" {they} say{s}. \"I needed someone to carry it without guessing.\" Inside is a jar of village honey and a note: For the porter.\n> left: Open the honey now and share it\n  rel npc +small\n  study -tiny\n  say: You eat half the jar with {npc} on the dormitory floor, with spoons borrowed from the kitchens, and talk until your reading is a lost cause.\n> right: Save it for exam month\n  health +small\n  say: You put it on the shelf, labelled. In exam month, a spoonful at midnight gets you through two chapters. {npc} asks, later, if you liked it. You did.\n\n@event village_errand_third_thing\ntitle: The Third Thing, After All\ntrigger: queued\ndefer: off_campus or not around(npc)\nactor: npc\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: coin\ntags: social\nscene: A village stall with a single extravagant hat on a stand, two students looking at it, one with a purse already open.\ntext: {npc} has gone back down to the village for the third thing, the one you talked {them} out of, and has come back with it. \"You were right,\" {they} say{s}. \"I bought it anyway. Now I need you to say it suits me.\"\n> left: Say it suits {them}\n  rel npc +small\n  say: \"It suits you,\" you say. It doesn't, at all. {npc} wears it to breakfast every day for a week, beaming.\n> right: Tell {them} the truth, kindly\n  charm +small\n  rel npc -tiny\n  say: You say it's a brave choice. {npc} hears what you mean, laughs and gives it to the porter, who wears it all winter with great dignity.\n"},{"path":"content/events/slice_2a.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Phase 2a vertical slice (design §13): ten events that use every\n#  construct of the new event format — classes and tones, size\n#  tiers and presets, challenges with partial results and `vs`,\n#  spells and the swipe-up option, inline text variation, facts,\n#  memories and relations between characters — and teach two\n#  spells, Ward and Flame.\n# ============================================================\n\n@event slice_ward_lesson\ntitle: The First Ward\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nactor: thorne\nwhen: met(thorne) and turn >= 3 and spell_level(ward) == 0\nweight: 24\nart: shield\ntags: class study magic\nscene: An office lit by one lamp, an old professor holding a pale shell of light between his palms like a soap bubble.\ntext: Professor Thorne holds up a hand and the lamplight bends around it into a pale shell. \"A ward,\" he says. \"{~It stops one blow.|One blow. That's all it ever stops.} Learn it properly or not at all.\"\n> left: Practise until it holds\n  grind arcana\n  learn_spell ward\n  rel thorne +small\n  memory: confided thorne\n  say: By midnight your shell holds for a full breath. Thorne says \"Hm,\" which from him is a standing ovation.\n> right: Ask about the theory first\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  rel thorne +tiny\n  say: He talks for an hour about lattices and intent. You understand perhaps a third of it. \"Come back when the third is a half,\" he says.\n\n@event slice_candle_flame\ntitle: Borrowed Fire\nclass: milestone\ntone: light\nactor: lyra\nwhen: met(lyra) and turn >= 4 and spell_level(flame) == 0\nweight: 24\nart: flame\ntags: class magic rival\nscene: A crimson-haired student balancing a flame on one fingertip while another student squints at a sputtering candle.\ntext: Lyra watches you fail to light a candle for the third time. \"You're asking it,\" she says. \"Fire doesn't want to be asked. {rel(lyra) >= 12: Here — watch my hand.|Watch, if you can keep up.}\"\n> left: Watch her hand\n  learn_spell flame\n  rel lyra +small\n  spark lyra +tiny\n  learn lyra.prodigy\n  say: She doesn't explain. She just does it, slowly, twice. On the third try your candle catches, and so, very briefly, does your sleeve.\n> right: Challenge her to teach you properly\n  challenge duel vs lyra.arcana:\n    win:\n      learn_spell flame\n      rel lyra +medium\n      say: You hold your own long enough that she stops and actually explains. \"Fine,\" she says. \"You'll do.\"\n    partial:\n      learn_spell flame\n      health -small\n      say: She wins, and you hit the floor hard enough to feel it tomorrow, but she shows you the trick on the way out, as if it were nothing.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -small\n      say: You end up on the floor with singed eyebrows. Lyra steps over you. \"Ask better,\" she says.\n\n@event slice_stray_cold\ntitle: The Drift\nclass: incident\ntone: eerie\nwhen: turn >= 5\nweight: 16\nart: snow\ntags: mystery danger\nscene: A corridor at night where frost spreads across the flagstones toward a student holding a candle.\ntext: Cold drifts down the corridor like a tide coming in, and the torches lean away from it. {spell_level(ward) > 0: Your ward prickles at your fingertips.|You have nothing to hold it back.}\n> left: Run for the stairs\n  health -small\n  mood -small\n  say: You make the stairs with frost in your hair. Behind you, the corridor sighs and is just a corridor again.\n> right: Stand and study it\n  challenge research adept- wild:\n    win:\n      lore +medium\n      say: It isn't weather. It feels like being looked at, from somewhere far below. You write down everything before your hands warm up, and none of it makes sense yet.\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      say: You learn exactly one thing, which is how cold cold can be. Sister Amaranth wraps you in four blankets.\n> up[ward]: Raise a ward\n  mood +small\n  say: Your ward flares, pale and steady. The cold breaks against it and slides away, the way a wave gives up on a rock.\n\n@event slice_late_lamps\ntime: evening night\ntitle: The Last Lamp\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: turn >= 2\nweight: 14\nrepeat: 6\nart: candle\ntags: study\nscene: The library at closing time, one desk still lit, a cat asleep on an open book.\ntext: {~The library is emptying.|Rain on the high windows, and the library is emptying early.|Exam fever has filled the library all day; now, finally, it is emptying.} {~Footnote, the library cat, is asleep on the book you need|The book you need is on the top shelf, and the ladder squeaks|Someone has left a half-finished essay on your table, and a biscuit}, and the last lamp is yours if you want it.\n> left: Keep the lamp\n  grind lore\n  say: {~You read until Footnote leaves in protest.|You read until the words swim.|You read until the night porter coughs pointedly.}\n> right: Go to bed\n  health +small\n  say: {~You sleep like a stone. The book will still be there. So, probably, will Footnote.|You fall asleep before your head finds the pillow. You wake up knowing one thing you didn't, somehow.|You sleep well and dream of shelves. The lamp goes to someone who needed it more.}\n\n@event slice_quarrel\ntitle: A Quarrel in the Courtyard\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nactor: kael\nwhen: met(kael) and met(lyra) and turn >= 6 and around(lyra) and around(kael)\nweight: 16\nart: snow\ntags: social rival\nscene: Two students facing each other in a snowy courtyard, one breathing frost, the other with sparks at her fingertips.\ntext: Kael and Lyra are facing each other in the courtyard, and the {season == \"winter\": snow|grass} between them is half {season == \"winter\": melted|scorched}, half frozen. \"Say it again,\" Lyra says. Kael doesn't. He just looks at you.\n> left: Step between them\n  alignment +tiny\n  npc_rel lyra kael +small\n  rel kael +small\n  health -tiny\n  memory: protected kael\n  say: Lyra's sparks catch your sleeve, Kael's frost puts it out. They both stare at you. The fight, somehow, is over.\n> right: Walk past\n  npc_rel lyra kael -medium\n  rel kael -small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: Behind you, something cracks: ice, or temper. At dinner they sit at opposite ends of the hall.\n\n@event slice_burned_letter\ntitle: Ashes in the Grate\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nactor: lyra\nwhen: rel(lyra) >= 6 and not knows(lyra.mother)\nweight: 18\nart: letter\ntags: social\nscene: A fireplace where the corner of a letter with a black seal is curling into flame.\ntext: Lyra is feeding a letter into the common-room fire, a corner at a time. Black seal. She doesn't look round. \"It says 'well done',\" she says. \"It doesn't. I'm checking whether it burns better if I pretend.\"\n> left: Sit with her\n  learn lyra.mother\n  rel lyra +medium\n  mood -small\n  memory: confided lyra\n  say: You watch it burn together. \"She's never said it,\" Lyra says, to the fire. \"Not once.\" You don't say anything, and that seems to be right.\n> right: Make a joke\n  rel lyra -small\n  mood +tiny\n  say: She almost smiles. Then she puts the rest of the letter in at once and leaves you with the smoke.\n\n@event slice_hollow_whisper\ntitle: A Voice in the Water\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: turn >= 7\nweight: 12\nart: wave\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: A student kneeling at the edge of a still lake at night, their reflection smiling when they are not.\ntext: At the lakeshore your reflection speaks before you do. \"You're running low,\" it says, kindly. \"{mana < 15: I could fill you up.|I could make you stronger.} Just say yes. Saying yes costs nothing.\"\n> left: Say yes\n  mana +large\n  alignment -medium\n  say: Cold pours into you, generous and endless. When you stand, your reflection is a little slower to stand with you.\n> right: Walk away\n  alignment +small\n  mood -small\n  say: The reflection's smile stays on the water after you've gone. You don't look back. You can feel it not minding.\n\n@event slice_market_haggle\ntitle: The Spice Stall\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: turn >= 3\nweight: 12\nrepeat: 8\nart: coin\ntags: money\nscene: A crowded village market stall heaped with jars of glowing spices and a stall-holder with an enormous moustache.\ntext: The stall-holder in the village market wants thirty crowns for {~a jar of starpepper|a twist of dragon-chilli|a pot of cliff-bee honey}. Mabel swears by it for colds. The stall-holder swears by his prices.\n> left: Haggle\n  when: gold >= 30\n  challenge negotiate journeyman-:\n    win:\n      gold -small\n      health +small\n      say: Eight crowns and a compliment on your persistence. Mabel says you've been robbed, but only slightly.\n    partial:\n      gold -medium\n      health +small\n      say: Fifteen crowns. He throws in a free sneeze's worth of pepper, which you did not want.\n    lose:\n      gold -large\n      mood -tiny\n      say: You pay full price and somehow promise to come back for more. He waves you off like a nephew.\n> right: Leave it\n  health -tiny\n  say: You walk on. You catch a cold the next week, out of spite.\n\n@event slice_wager\ntitle: The Tower Wager\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: renown >= 3 and turn >= 4\nweight: 5\nart: dice\ntags: social\nscene: A knot of students around a table where coins and a pocket watch are being stacked as bets.\ntext: Half the common room is betting on whether you can climb the clock tower before the bell. Corvin is holding the purse, which is either reassuring or not.\n> left: Climb\n  vigor +tiny\n  chance even:\n    renown +large\n    say: {~You make it with a heartbeat to spare.|The bell rings as your hand touches the top.} They carry you round the common room on a chair.\n  else:\n    renown -medium\n    say: The bell rings when you're two-thirds up, and the caretaker has to talk you down with a ladder. They'll tell it for years.\n> right: Decline politely\n  renown -tiny\n  say: The crowd groans. Corvin pockets the purse thoughtfully and you decide not to ask.\n\n@event slice_frost_duel\ntitle: Frost Against Fire\nclass: milestone\ntone: tense\nactor: kael\nwhen: met(kael) and rel(kael) >= 9 and turn >= 8\nweight: 20\nart: duel\ntags: duel social\nscene: A duelling ring at dawn, frost spreading from one side and a faint warmth glowing from the other.\ntext: Kael asks for a practice duel, which for him is an astonishing number of words. \"Not to win,\" he adds. \"I want to see what you do when it's cold.\"\n> left: Duel him straight\n  memory: fought kael\n  challenge combat vs kael.arcana +1:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      rel kael +medium\n      spark kael +small\n      say: You win by a whisker, breathing steam. Kael bows, and his hands, for once, are warm when he shakes yours. Half the duelling club, it turns out, was watching from the doorway.\n    partial:\n      rel kael +small\n      health -small\n      say: A draw, of sorts: you're both on the ground. He laughs, a short surprised sound, and helps you up.\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      rel kael +small\n      say: He wins, gently. \"You stopped when it got cold,\" he says. \"Don't.\"\n> right: Decline; offer him tea instead\n  rel kael +small\n  mood +tiny\n  say: He thinks about this for a long time. \"Tea,\" he says. It freezes in his cup. You make him another.\n> up[flame]: Answer with fire\n  rel kael +medium\n  spark kael +small\n  renown +small\n  memory: fought kael\n  say: Your flame meets his frost in a column of steam that fills the ring and brings half the corridor running. When it clears, Kael is smiling at you, properly, for the first time.\n"},{"path":"content/events/slice_2b.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Phase 2b vertical slice (design §13): the world loop in the new\n#  format. The Evocation course (3 levels), the Duelling Circle, the\n#  Library Assistant job, five campus places, break plans, the\n#  Royal Library internship, the thesis, and a four-event arc\n#  (The Bell Under the Water). Uses cast roles, say[npc],\n#  present(place), queue within … and unqueue.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- Evocation (track: evocation) ----------------\n\n@event evo_first_spark\ntitle: The First Spark\ntrack: evocation\nlevel: 1\nlesson: lumenhold\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: flame\ntags: class magic\nscene: A lecture hall with scorch marks on every desk, students holding their palms up as if testing for rain.\ntext: Evocation I begins with a single instruction from the lectern: \"Make heat. Not fire. Heat.\" Half the class makes nothing. One student makes a small, apologetic fire.\n> left: Aim for heat, carefully\n  arcana +small\n  say: Your palm warms like a stone left in the sun. The lecturer nods, which is better than most get.\n> right: Aim for fire, boldly\n  challenge exam standard-1 wild:\n    win:\n      arcana +medium\n      renown +tiny\n      say: A neat little flame, exactly where you meant it. The lecturer writes your name down. Approvingly, you hope.\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      arcana +tiny\n      say: Your sleeve learns more about fire than you do. The lecture continues around the smell.\n\n@event evo_puddle_drill\ntitle: Puddle Drill\ntrack: evocation\nlevel: 1\nlesson: mirror_lake\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: wave\ntags: class magic\nscene: Students crouched over puddles in a stone courtyard, trying to make the water stand up.\ntext: Tidecalling half of the course: a puddle each, and the task of making it stand up and stay up. Yours trembles, considers it, and lies back down.\n> left: Practise until it stands\n  grind arcana\n  say: By the end of the hour your puddle stands like a sulky child. It counts.\n> right: Watch the student next to you\n  arcana +tiny\n  guile +tiny\n  say: You learn their trick, and also that they hum while they cast. You hum too. It works, inexplicably.\n\n@event evo_misfire\ntitle: Misfire\ntrack: evocation\nlevel: 1\nclass: incident\ntone: tense\nart: bolt\ntags: class danger\nscene: A lecture hall filling with steam after a spell meets a bucket of water at the wrong moment.\ntext: Someone's tidecalling meets someone else's flame in the middle of the room. The hall fills with scalding steam and twenty students who all have opinions.\n> left: Shield the front row\n  health -small\n  alignment +tiny\n  renown +small\n  say: You take the worst of it on your sleeves. The front row looks at you the way people look at lifeboats.\n> right: Get out and open the windows\n  mood -tiny\n  say: The steam pours out into the courtyard and becomes, briefly, a very small cloud with a grudge.\n\n@event evo_tide_wall\ntitle: The Tide Wall\ntrack: evocation\nlevel: 2\nlesson: mirror_lake\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nart: wave\ntags: class magic\nscene: A student holding back a wall of lake water on the shore while classmates watch from behind them.\ntext: Evocation II takes the class to the lakeshore. The exercise: raise a wall of water and hold it while a classmate walks past behind it. Your classmate looks nervous. So do you.\n> left: Hold it steady\n  challenge exam standard steady:\n    win:\n      arcana +medium\n      say: The wall holds. Your classmate walks past dry and amazed. You let it down gently, which is the hard part.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -small\n      say: The wall holds until it doesn't, and comes down on both of you. Your classmate is very wet and very gracious about it. You shiver until dinner.\n> right: Ask to go second\n  arcana +small\n  say: You watch three people do it first and learn from all of their mistakes.\n\n@event evo_lattice\ntitle: The Flame Lattice\ntrack: evocation\nlevel: 2\nlesson: founders_seal\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: flame\ntags: class study\nscene: Interlocking threads of fire hanging in the air of a lecture hall like a glowing net.\ntext: Fire woven into a lattice, each strand holding the others up. The lecturer says the founders built wards like this. The lecturer also says you have twenty minutes.\n> left: Build it slowly\n  lore +small\n  arcana +small\n  mood -small\n  say: Your lattice is small and ugly and holds perfectly. The lecturer says \"Founder-ish,\" which you choose to take as praise.\n> right: Build it big\n  chance even:\n    arcana +large\n    say: {~It's magnificent for nine seconds.|It holds just long enough for everyone to see it.} Then the strands find each other and the whole thing sighs into sparks, and you understand exactly why.\n  else:\n    mood -small\n    health -tiny\n    say: The strands find each other in the first second, and the whole thing goes up in your face. You spend the rest of the lesson picking sparks out of your hair.\n\n@event evo_storm_pairing\ntitle: Storm Pairing\ntrack: evocation\nlevel: 3\nclass: milestone\ntone: tense\nart: bolt\ntags: class duel\ncast:\n  partner: any where (tagged(partner, student) or tagged(partner, prefect))\nscene: Two students on a windswept tower top, one calling rain, the other calling lightning, the sky between them churning.\ntext: Advanced Evocation pairs students for a storm: one calls the rain, the other the lightning, and neither is allowed to let go first. You're paired with {partner}.\n> left: Call the lightning\n  challenge exam standard+1 wild:\n    win:\n      arcana +large\n      rel partner +small\n      say: The storm answers both of you at once. {partner} laughs into the wind, soaked and astonished.\n      say[lyra]: The storm answers both of you at once. Lyra looks at you as though you've finally said something worth hearing.\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      rel partner +tiny\n      say: The lightning goes where it likes. You both spend an afternoon in the infirmary, comparing burns.\n> right: Call the rain, and let them lead\n  arcana +medium\n  rel partner +small\n  say: You hold the rain steady while {partner} draws the lightning down. Afterwards {they} thank{s} you for not trying to steal it.\n\n@event evo_final_practical\ntitle: The Final Practical\ntrack: evocation\nlevel: 3\nwhen: month == 4 or month == 10\nclass: story\ntone: tense\nart: flame\ntags: class exam\nscene: An examination hall cleared of desks, a single student facing three examiners and a target dummy made of ice.\ntext: The last practical of Advanced Evocation: melt an ice dummy without touching the floor around it. Professor Thorne is on the panel, and he is not blinking.\n> left: Fire, precisely\n  challenge exam standard+1 steady:\n    win:\n      arcana +large\n      study evocation +medium\n      rel thorne +small\n      say: The ice runs off the dummy and not a drop touches the floor. Thorne says \"Hm,\" and writes something underlined.\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: The dummy melts, and so does a fair part of the floor. Thorne writes something with a question mark.\n> right: Water, patiently\n  arcana +medium\n  lore +small\n  say: You draw the cold out of the ice with water instead of fire. It takes twice as long. The panel watches every minute.\n\n# ---------------- Duelling Circle (track: duelling) ----------------\n\n@event duel_first_practice\ntitle: Footwork\ntrack: duelling\nrank: 1\nwhen: around(lyra)\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: duel\ntags: club duel\nscene: A duelling hall with chalk circles on the floor and practice dummies with names painted on their chests.\ntext: Lyra runs practice like a general. \"Footwork,\" she says. \"If your feet are wrong, your spell is a rumour.\" She pairs you with a dummy called Gerald the Second.\n> left: Drill footwork\n  vigor +small\n  perform track +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: Two hours of stepping in circles. Gerald the Second wins every round. Your feet, however, are now correct.\n> right: Spar for real\n  challenge combat vs lyra.vigor -1:\n    win:\n      vigor +medium\n      rel lyra +small\n      say: You land a clean disarm on your partner. Across the hall, Lyra raises an eyebrow, which is her version of a standing ovation.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      say: You spend the evening relearning which way is up. Lyra says that's the first lesson anyway.\n\n@event duel_footwork_feud\ntitle: The Footwork Feud\ntrack: duelling\nwhen: around(lyra) and around(bram)\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nart: duel\ntags: club rival\nscene: Two students arguing in the middle of a duelling circle while the rest of the club pretends not to listen.\ntext: Bram says the Circle's footwork is \"Emberhall nonsense\" and Lyra says Bram's footwork is \"a hazard to shipping\". Both of them look at you to settle it.\n> left: Side with Lyra\n  rel lyra +small\n  rel bram -small\n  npc_rel lyra bram -tiny\n  say: Lyra nods as if this were obvious. Bram takes a demerit book out of his pocket, looks at it, and puts it back. Progress.\n> right: Side with Bram\n  rel bram +small\n  rel lyra -small\n  say: Bram looks startled to be agreed with. Lyra looks at you like a puzzle she will solve later, with fire.\n\n@event duel_fixture\ntitle: Fixture Against Thornwick\ntrack: duelling\nrank: 2\nclass: milestone\ntone: tense\nart: trophy\ntags: club duel festival\nscene: A duelling green with rival banners, students from both academies packed onto benches.\ntext: Thornwick Academy's duelling team has crossed the hills for a fixture. The Circle needs one more duellist in the line-up, and the captain is looking at you.\n> left: Duel for the Circle\n  challenge combat adept-:\n    win:\n      renown +medium\n      perform track +medium\n      say: You win your bout, and the Circle wins the fixture by a whisker. Someone carries you a few steps before remembering they don't like you.\n    partial:\n      renown +small\n      perform track +small\n      say: A draw. The Circle loses by one bout, but nobody blames you, and {around(lyra): Lyra|the captain} says \"acceptable\" twice.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -small\n      say: The Thornwick duellist is very good and very polite about it, and every bruise you collect is a polite one. You hate both.\n> right: Offer to be the reserve\n  perform track +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You hold coats and bring water. It turns out reserves see everything, including how Thornwick cheats.\n\n@event duel_captain_call\ntitle: The Captain's Question\ntrack: duelling\nrank: 2\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nart: duel\ntags: club\nscene: An empty duelling hall at night, two students sitting on the edge of a chalk circle.\ntext: After practice, the captain sits beside you. \"When I graduate, someone has to run this place,\" she says. \"Someone who turns up. You turn up.\"\n> left: Say you'll think about it\n  perform track +small\n  rank_xp track +medium\n  say: \"Think fast,\" she says. \"Feet first, then spells.\" You're fairly sure that's a yes.\n> right: Say you're not the type\n  mood +tiny\n  say: \"Nobody's the type,\" she says. \"That's why it has to be someone who turns up.\"\n\n# ---------------- Library Assistant (track: library_assistant) ----------------\n\n@event lib_shelving_rush\ntitle: The Shelving Rush\ntrack: library_assistant\nwhen: month == 4 or month == 10\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: book\ntags: job study\nscene: A library cart overflowing with returned books, a student pushing it at a run between towering shelves.\ntext: Exam week, and every book in the library has been borrowed, read and returned at once. The cart is taller than you are. Footnote is sitting on top of it.\n> left: Shelve properly\n  wage\n  lore +small\n  perform track +small\n  mood -small\n  say: Every book in its place by closing. Footnote has moved to the top shelf to supervise.\n> right: Shelve quickly\n  wage\n  perform track -tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: The books are on shelves. Most of them are even on the right shelves. Someone will find out in a decade.\n\n@event lib_overdue_mage\ntitle: The Overdue Mage\ntrack: library_assistant\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: hourglass\ntags: job\nscene: An elderly mage at the library desk clutching a book that is sixty years overdue.\ntext: An old mage returns a book sixty years overdue and asks, with great dignity, what the fine is. The ledger says the fine is more than the library.\n> left: Waive it\n  wage\n  perform track -tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: The old mage thanks you and leaves a sweet on the desk. Later you find out he taught Professor Thorne.\n> right: Charge it, by the book\n  wage\n  gold +small\n  perform track +small\n  say: The mage pays in coins so old the bank has to look them up. The head librarian is delighted, and gives you the collector's share of the fine, as is tradition.\n\n@event lib_footnote_strike\ntitle: Footnote Goes on Strike\ntrack: library_assistant\nclass: incident\ntone: light\nart: cat\ntags: job\nscene: A large library cat lying across a reference desk, refusing to move, while students queue in front of it.\ntext: Footnote has lain down across the reference desk and will not move. Students are queueing. The cat has made its demands clear: none.\n> left: Bribe him with fish\n  wage\n  gold -tiny\n  perform track +small\n  say: Footnote accepts the fish, the apology and the chair you bring him. The queue resumes.\n> right: Work around him\n  wage\n  perform track -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You stamp books on the far end of the desk for an hour. Footnote watches with the calm of a cat who has won.\n\n# ---------------- Places ----------------\n\n@event court_fountain_freeze\ntitle: The Frozen Fountain\nplace: courtyard\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nart: snow\ntags: mystery\nscene: A courtyard fountain frozen mid-splash, the water hanging in the air like glass.\ntext: The courtyard fountain has frozen in the middle of a splash. The water hangs in the air like glass. A first-year is trying to lick it.\n> left: Stop the first-year\n  health -tiny\n  alignment +small\n  say: You pull the first-year away just as the ice cracks. For a moment you'd swear you saw a black star reflected in it.\n> right: Study the ice\n  lore +small\n  say: The ice is colder than ice should be. Your notes say \"colder than ice should be\" three times.\n\n@event court_rumour_mill\ntitle: The Rumour Mill\nplace: courtyard\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: group\ntags: social\ncast:\n  gossip: present(place) where tagged(gossip, peer)\nscene: Students huddled around the courtyard fountain trading whispers, one of them clearly enjoying it most.\ntext: {gossip} has news, which is to say gossip, which is to say three different stories about the same broken window. \"You'll want to hear this,\" {they} say{s}.\n> left: Listen to all of it\n  rel gossip +small\n  mood +small\n  say: You hear all three stories. Two are true. One is about you.\n  say[nessa]: You hear all three stories. Nessa is already writing the fourth.\n> right: Make up a better one\n  guile +small\n  rel gossip +tiny\n  renown +tiny\n  alignment -tiny\n  say: Your story is better than all three. By dinner it's the official version.\n\n@event lake_skimming\ntitle: Skimming Stones\nplace: lakeshore\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nart: wave\ntags: nature\ncast:\n  friend: any where tagged(friend, peer) and rel(friend) >= 6\nscene: Two students skimming stones across a perfectly still lake at dusk.\ntext: {friend} is skimming stones on the Mirror Lake. The water is so still each stone leaves rings that go on forever. \"Seven,\" {they} say{s}. \"Beat that.\"\n> left: Try to beat it\n  challenge duel apprentice wild:\n    win:\n      rel friend +small\n      mood +small\n      say: Nine skips. {friend} demands a rematch and gets one. The afternoon disappears into stones.\n    lose:\n      rel friend +small\n      mood +tiny\n      say: Your stone sinks with great dignity. {friend} laughs until {they} have to sit down.\n> right: Just watch the rings\n  mood +small\n  rel friend +tiny\n  say: You sit and watch the rings spread until the light goes. Neither of you says much. It's one of the good afternoons.\n\n@event lake_reflection_bet\ntitle: The Reflection Bet\nplace: lakeshore\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nart: mirror\ntags: mystery\nscene: A student staring into a still lake where their reflection appears to be holding a coin.\ntext: Your reflection in the Mirror Lake is holding a coin you don't have. It flips it. It catches it. It offers it to you.\n> left: Take the coin\n  gold +small\n  alignment -tiny\n  say: The coin is real, and cold, and you can't remember putting it in your pocket.\n> right: Shake your head\n  alignment +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: Your reflection shrugs, pockets the coin and walks out of the frame before you do.\n\n@event ref_food_fight\ntitle: Pudding Diplomacy\nplace: refectory\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: meal\ntags: social\nscene: A refectory mid-food-fight, puddings in the air, a prefect rising slowly from behind a table.\ntext: A pudding sails across the refectory and lands in Bram's lap. The hall goes silent. Every eye turns to the table it came from, which is yours.\n> left: Own up\n  alignment +tiny\n  rel bram +small\n  renown +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: Bram writes you a demerit, then pauses. \"Good throw,\" he says, almost without wanting to.\n> right: Throw another\n  renown +small\n  rel bram -small\n  mood +small\n  say: The second pudding starts a war that the refectory will talk about for years. Historians will argue about who won. You know.\n\n@event ref_cook_secret\ntitle: The Cook's Secret\nplace: refectory\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nart: cup\ntags: home\nscene: A huge kitchen behind the refectory, an enormous cook stirring a pot with a spoon the size of an oar.\ntext: The cook catches you lingering by the kitchen door. \"You look like someone who misses proper soup,\" she says, and hands you a bowl before you can deny it.\n> left: Eat it slowly\n  health +small\n  mood +small\n  say: It tastes like the kitchen at home. You don't say so. She can tell anyway.\n> right: Ask for the recipe\n  alchemy +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: \"Ha,\" says the cook, which means no, which means you'll have to keep coming back.\n\n@event obs_comet_watch\nslot: evening\ntitle: The Comet Watch\nplace: observatory\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nart: planet\ntags: magic\nscene: The brass dome of an observatory open to the night, a student at the eyepiece, another beside them with a notebook.\ntext: A comet is due tonight and the observatory is full of students pretending they came for the science. The telescope has a queue. The roof has a view.\n> left: Queue for the telescope\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: Your turn at the eyepiece lasts thirty seconds. The comet's tail is green. You'll remember it longer than most lectures.\n> right: Watch from the roof\n  mood +small\n  rel kael +tiny\n  say: The comet crosses the sky slowly, like it's looking for something. Kael is already on the roof. He moves over to make room.\n\n@event obs_star_chart\ntitle: The Missing Star\nplace: observatory\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nart: star\ntags: mystery magic\nscene: An old star chart spread on a desk, one constellation with a careful empty circle drawn where a star should be.\ntext: An old star chart in the observatory archive has a circle drawn where a star should be, with a note in faded ink: \"It eats the light. Do not chart it.\"\n> left: Copy the chart\n  lore +small\n  say: You copy it carefully. That night you dream of a circle with nothing inside it, waiting.\n> right: Put it back\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You return it to the drawer. The drawer feels colder when you close it.\n\n@event green_mandrake_choir\ntitle: The Mandrake Choir\nplace: greenhouses\nwhen: around(tobias)\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: leaf\ntags: nature\nscene: A greenhouse bench of potted mandrakes wearing tiny earmuffs, a student conducting them with a pencil.\ntext: Tobias has taught the greenhouse mandrakes to scream in harmony. He would like your opinion. The mandrakes would like an audience.\n> left: Conduct a verse\n  rel tobias +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: You conduct one verse. Your ears ring for a day. Tobias says it's the best performance they've given.\n> right: Wear earmuffs and listen\n  rel tobias +tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: Through the earmuffs it sounds almost beautiful. Tobias is so proud he cries a little.\n\n@event green_moonflower_sprout\ntitle: A Moonflower Seedling\nplace: greenhouses\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nart: flower\ntags: nature\nscene: A tiny silver-leaved seedling in a clay pot, glowing faintly in a dark greenhouse.\ntext: In a forgotten corner of the greenhouse, a moonflower seed has sprouted on its own, eleven years early. It glows faintly when you lean over it.\n> left: Pot it and look after it\n  alchemy +small\n  mood +small\n  say: You give it a pot, a label and a name. It glows a little brighter when you visit.\n> right: Tell Tobias\n  when: around(tobias)\n  rel tobias +medium\n  say: Tobias is speechless, then very loud. The seedling gets a sign, a fence and a rota.\n\n# ---------------- Break days ----------------\n\n@event castle_empty_halls\ntitle: Empty Halls\nplan: castle\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nart: tower\ntags: home\nscene: A long castle corridor in the holidays, dust in sunbeams, one student walking alone.\ntext: The castle in the holidays is enormous and quiet. Your footsteps echo down corridors you've never been alone in. A door you've never noticed is standing open.\n> left: Go through the door\n  guile +small\n  mood +tiny\n  discover seventh_tower\n  say: It leads to a stair that leads to a locked door that leads, you're almost sure, to the seventh tower.\n> right: Read in the window seat\n  lore +small\n  mood +small\n  say: You read all day in a window seat with the whole lake below you. The best kind of nothing.\n\n@event castle_skeleton_crew\ntitle: The Skeleton Crew\nplan: castle\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: meal\ntags: home\nscene: A handful of staff and students sharing a long table in a nearly empty great hall.\ntext: Supper in the holidays is one table in the Great Hall: you, three other students, the cook, two prefects and Old Maud, who has brought her goat.\n> left: Sit next to Maud\n  rel maud +small\n  mood +small\n  say: Maud tells a story about the frozen spring that she's never told the term-time students. Bartholomew eats your napkin.\n> right: Play cards with the prefects\n  gold +small\n  guile +tiny\n  say: The prefects play cards like they enforce rules: badly and with great confidence. You win dessert and most of their pocket money.\n\n@event home_family_supper\ntitle: Supper at Home\nplace: home_cottage\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nart: meal\ntags: home\nscene: A crowded kitchen table, a family passing dishes, a student back from the academy being asked too many questions.\ntext: Supper at home. Everyone wants to know what you've learned. You try to explain evocation to an aunt who thinks it's a kind of cheese.\n> left: Show them a spell\n  mood +small\n  arcana +tiny\n  say: You light every candle in the room at once. Your aunt applauds and asks if you can do it with cheese.\n> right: Just listen\n  mood +medium\n  health +tiny\n  say: You let them talk. You'd forgotten how loud and how good it is.\n\n@event home_old_friend\ntitle: An Old Friend\nplace: home_fields\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nart: leaf\ntags: home\nscene: Two old friends sitting on a field wall at sunset, one in academy robes, the other in work clothes.\ntext: Your oldest friend is sitting on the wall at the edge of the fields, exactly where you left them. \"So,\" they say. \"Are you a wizard now, or what?\"\n> left: Tell them everything\n  mood +medium\n  charm +tiny\n  say: You talk until the stars come out. They want to hear about the lake most of all.\n> right: Ask about their life instead\n  mood +small\n  charm +small\n  say: They've been busy too: a farm, a fiancé, a goat that bites. Your life isn't the only one that grew.\n\n@event home_market_day\ntitle: Market Day\nplace: home_market\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: coin\ntags: home money\nscene: A small country market, a student in academy robes being stared at by stallholders.\ntext: Market day in town. A stallholder recognises your academy robes and asks if you can do anything about his leaky roof. For a price.\n> left: Fix it with magic\n  gold +small\n  arcana +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: The roof is dry, the stallholder is thrilled and you are slightly scorched. He pays in apples and coins.\n> right: Just buy sweets\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: The sweet shop still sells your favourites. You eat them on the bridge like you used to.\n\n@event work_double_shift\ntitle: The Double Shift\nplan: work\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nart: coin\ntags: job money\nscene: A student asleep on their feet behind a counter, a clock on the wall showing a very late hour.\ntext: Holiday shifts pay double and feel triple. It's your second shift today and the evening rush hasn't started.\n> left: Push through\n  gold +medium\n  health -small\n  mood -small\n  say: You push through. The coins are heavy in your pocket and your feet are heavy everywhere else.\n> right: Swap the shift\n  mood +small\n  say: A colleague takes it for a favour you'll owe later. You sleep for eleven hours.\n\n# ---------------- The Royal Library internship (plan: internship) ----------------\n\n@event intern_first_assignment\ntitle: The First Assignment\ntrack: intern_royal_library\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: book\ntags: study\nscene: A vast underground archive, a supervisor handing a student a list longer than their arm.\ntext: Your supervisor at the Royal Library hands you a list of forty uncatalogued boxes. \"Find out what's in them,\" she says. \"Carefully. One of them is probably alive.\"\n> left: Work through the boxes\n  wage\n  lore +medium\n  mood -small\n  say: Box thirty-one is alive. It's a very small, very old dictionary that bites. You catalogue it.\n> right: Ask the other interns\n  wage\n  charm +small\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: The other interns have a system, a rota and a song. You learn all three.\n\n@event intern_index_maze\ntitle: The Index Maze\ntrack: intern_royal_library\nclass: milestone\ntone: tense\nart: map\ntags: study mystery\nscene: A student lost between endless shelves underground, following an index card trail by lamplight.\ntext: A cross-reference in the archive leads to another, and another, and another, deeper under the city. The last card is about the founders of Lumenhold.\n> left: Follow it to the end\n  wage\n  challenge research adept:\n    win:\n      lore +large\n      say: At the end is a letter from a founder about \"the seventh lock\". You copy it with shaking hands.\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: The trail ends at a missing card. Somebody took it. Recently.\n> right: Report it to your supervisor\n  wage\n  lore +small\n  perform track +small\n  say: Your supervisor reads the card, goes pale, thanks you for your care, and writes a letter to the headmistress of Lumenhold.\n\n# ---------------- Thesis (track: thesis) ----------------\n\n@event thesis_proposal\ntitle: The Proposal\ntrack: thesis\nwhen: progress(thesis) < 30\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nart: scroll\ntags: study\nscene: A desk covered in drafts, one page with a single sentence circled many times.\ntext: Your thesis proposal is due. Professor Thorne wants one sentence that says what you'll find out. You have eleven sentences and none of them is it.\n> left: Rewrite it all night\n  progress track +medium\n  health -small\n  say: At dawn you have one sentence. Thorne reads it twice. \"Yes,\" he says. \"That.\"\n> right: Hand in what you have\n  progress track +small\n  rel thorne -tiny\n  say: Thorne reads all eleven sentences and circles the fourth. \"That one,\" he says. \"Next time, only that one.\"\n\n@event thesis_lab_night\ntitle: Late in the Lab\ntrack: thesis\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nart: candle\ntags: study\nscene: A thesis laboratory at midnight, one carrel lit, a student surrounded by open books.\ntext: The thesis lab, long after everyone else has gone home: and your argument has a hole in it the size of the seventh tower.\n> left: Find the hole\n  progress track +medium\n  mood -small\n  say: You find it, fill it and find two more. That's progress, apparently.\n> right: Go to bed\n  rest\n  progress track -small\n  say: You sleep. In the morning the hole is still there, but you can see the edges of it.\n\n# ---------------- The Bell Under the Water (arc: the_bell) ----------------\n\n@event bell_first_ring\ntitle: A Bell Under the Water\narc: the_bell\nbeat: 1\nslot: night\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nart: bell\ntags: mystery\nscene: A dark lakeshore at night, ripples spreading from the middle of the lake though nothing has fallen in.\ntext: In the middle of the night you hear a bell, low and slow, from the middle of the lake. Nobody else in your dormitory wakes up.\n> left: Go down to the shore\n  find bell_heard\n  health -tiny\n  say: The water is perfectly still. Then, once, a ring spreads out from the centre, as if the lake had been struck from below.\n> right: Pull the blanket over your head\n  find bell_heard\n  mood -tiny\n  say: It rings seven times. You count every one. In the morning you are the only one who remembers.\n\n@event bell_maud_rope\ntitle: Maud's Rope\narc: the_bell\nbeat: 2\nslot: night\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nart: bell\ntags: mystery\ncast:\n  keeper: maud\nscene: An old woman in a garden shed holding up a frayed, waterlogged bell-rope.\ntext: {keeper} shows you a rope, frayed and black with lake water, that she's kept in her shed for sixty years. \"From the bell,\" she says. \"It came up the night the lake froze. Someone cut it.\"\n> left: Ask who cut it\n  find bell_rope\n  rel keeper +small\n  queue bell_answer within 1..3 with keeper\n  say: \"That,\" says {keeper}, \"is the question I've been asking for sixty years.\" She promises to tell you what she knows, when she's ready.\n> right: Leave her to her memories\n  find bell_rope\n  rel keeper +tiny\n  say: You leave her with the rope in her lap. She doesn't notice you go.\n\n@event bell_answer\ntitle: What Maud Remembers\ntrigger: queued\ndefer: off_campus\nactor: npc\nclass: story\ntone: warm\nart: lantern\ntags: mystery\nscene: An old woman and a student sitting by a cottage fire, a waterlogged rope coiled between them.\ntext: {npc} finds you by the boathouse. \"The rope,\" she says. \"Edmund cut it. To stop the bell ringing the thing awake. It went on ringing anyway.\"\n> ok: Listen\n  rel npc +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: She talks until the light goes. When she's finished, she seems lighter, and you seem to be carrying something.\n\n@event bell_drowned\ntitle: The Diving Bell\narc: the_bell\nbeat: 3\nslot: night\nclass: story\ntone: tense\nart: wave\ntags: mystery danger\nscene: A student in a borrowed diving helmet standing at the end of a jetty over black water.\ntext: The bell has rung every night for a week. The boathouse has an old diving helmet and a rope. The lake is very dark and very still.\n> left: Dive\n  challenge research adept- wild:\n    win:\n      find bell_name\n      lore +medium\n      say: Far down, the bell lies on its side, and around its rim, letters: a name you don't know. You surface gasping, and you keep the name.\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      say: The cold drives you back up before you see anything. Old Maud is waiting on the jetty with a blanket and a look.\n> right: Wait for the bell to stop\n  mood -small\n  lore +small\n  say: You wait for a week, listening from the shore, until you know its rhythm by heart. It doesn't stop. Something in you decides it will have to be you, next time.\n\n@event bell_silence\ntitle: The Bell Falls Silent\narc: the_bell\nbeat: 4\nslot: night\nwhen: clue(bell_name) or clue(bell_rope)\nclass: story\ntone: warm\nart: bell\ntags: mystery\nscene: The Mirror Lake at dawn, perfectly still, a student standing at the water's edge with a lantern.\ntext: {clue(bell_name): You say the name from the bell's rim out loud, over the water.|You stand at the shore with Maud's rope in your hands.} The bell rings one last time, and then the lake is just a lake.\n> ok: Let it rest\n  mood +medium\n  alignment +small\n  say: For the first time in sixty years, the lake is quiet. Somewhere in her cottage, you think, Maud sleeps through the night.\n"},{"path":"content/events/social.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Generic social events — `actor: any`.\n#  The engine binds `npc` to one met character for whom `when`\n#  is true. Use rel(npc), love(npc), bond(npc), romanceable(npc),\n#  tagged(npc, faculty) in conditions and {npc}, {they}, {them},\n#  {their}, {They} in text. These drive the relationship system:\n#  friendships form, romances spark, rivalries flare, bonds decay.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- Friendship ----------------\n\n@event soc_study_session\ntitle: Study Session\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ncast:\n  npc: any where (tagged(npc, student) or tagged(npc, prefect)) and rel(npc) >= 0\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nwhen: term and not wrapup\nrepeat: 4\nart: book\ntags: social study\nscene: Two students at a candlelit table piled with books, one explaining something with wild hand gestures.\ntext: {~{npc} drops into the seat across from you with an armful of books. \"Exams are coming for us all. Misery loves company. Study with me?\"|{npc} slides a stack of notes onto your table. \"I've read this chapter four times and it's still in a foreign language. Help?\"|{npc} finds you in the library and sits down without asking. \"If I study alone I'll just reorganise my quills. Keep me honest?\"}\n> left: You work better alone\n  lore +tiny\n  rel npc -tiny\n  say: You get a lot done. {~{npc} sits at the next table and sighs loudly every few minutes.|{npc} finds someone else, and their laughter carries across the library all evening.|{npc} leaves you a biscuit on the way out, which somehow makes it worse.}\n> right: Pull up a chair\n  mood -tiny\n  lore +small\n  study +tiny\n  rel npc +small\n  say: You get half as much done and remember twice as much of it. {~{npc} explains the Third Law with a biscuit diagram.|You quiz each other until the candle gutters.|{npc} makes up a rude rhyme for every date, and you will never forget a single one.}\n\n@event soc_borrow_money\ntitle: A Small Loan\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ncast:\n  npc: any where tagged(npc, peer) and rel(npc) >= 3\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nwhen: gold >= 20\nrepeat: 6\nart: pouch\ntags: social money\nscene: A student holding out an empty purse upside down with a sheepish grin.\ntext: {npc} turns out an empty purse. \"I'm fifteen crowns short {~for rent|for the term's ink and parchment|for the wand-mender, and it's my only wand}. I'll pay you back. I swear on my wand.\"\n> left: Sorry, you're tight too\n  rel npc -small\n  say: \"No, of course, I understand.\" {They} {are} quiet at dinner.\n> right: Lend fifteen crowns\n  gold -medium\n  alignment +tiny\n  rel npc +medium\n  chance likely:\n    queue soc_loan_repaid in 2 with npc\n    say: {npc} hugs you with the empty purse still in {their} hand.\n  else:\n    say: {npc} hugs you. You suspect you will never see those fifteen crowns again.\n\n@event soc_loan_repaid\ntitle: Paid Back\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: queued\nactor: npc\nart: coin\ntags: social money\nscene: A small stack of coins and a sweet bun left on a desk with a thank-you note.\ntext: {~{npc} finds you after class and presses a little stack of coins into your hand, plus a sticky bun. \"Fifteen crowns. And interest.\" The interest is the bun.|{npc} turns up at breakfast with a small purse and a very straight face. \"Fifteen crowns. I said I would.\"|{npc} slides fifteen crowns across the library table, one coin at a time. The last one has a note under it: \"Thank you. Really.\"}\n> left: Count it\n  gold +medium\n  rel npc +small\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You count it, because {they}'d be offended if you didn't. Fifteen exactly. {npc} nods, satisfied, as if a debt of honour has been settled.\n> right: Pocket it without counting\n  gold +medium\n  rel npc +small\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You put it away uncounted. {npc} looks quietly pleased to be trusted, which was worth more than the crowns.\n\n@event soc_become_friends\nramp: rel(npc) 18..30\ntitle: Something Like Friendship\nclass: milestone\ntone: light\ncast:\n  npc: any where tagged(npc, peer) and bond(npc) == \"none\" and rel(npc) >= 18\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nweight: 20\nart: group\ntags: social\nscene: Two students walking across the lawn at sunset, laughing at the same joke, shadows long behind them.\ntext: {npc} saves you a seat without asking now, and you save {them} one. You trade notes, complaints and the last biscuit without keeping score. Somewhere along the way, this became a friendship.\n> left: Keep a little distance\n  rel npc +tiny\n  say: You stay friendly. Just not too close. Some walls are load-bearing.\n> right: Call it what it is\n  bond npc friend\n  rel npc +medium\n  mood +small\n  say: \"So we're friends now,\" {npc} says. \"Officially.\" You shake on it, solemnly, then ruin it by laughing.\n\n@event soc_become_close\nramp: rel(npc) 36..50\nslot: night\ntitle: The Kind of Friend You Call at 3am\nclass: milestone\ntone: light\ncast:\n  npc: any where tagged(npc, peer) and bond(npc) == \"friend\" and rel(npc) >= 36\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nweight: 18\nart: heart\ntags: social\nscene: Two friends sitting on a dormitory floor at 3am wrapped in one blanket, empty mugs between them.\ntext: It's 3am. You can't sleep, and you've walked to {npc}'s door before you've even decided to. {They} open{s} it before you knock.\n> left: Apologise and go back to bed\n  rel npc +small\n  say: \"Don't be daft,\" {npc} says, but lets you go. In the morning there's tea outside your door.\n> right: Talk until sunrise\n  bond npc close\n  rel npc +medium\n  mood +medium\n  say: You tell {them} things you haven't told anyone. {They} tell you things back. The sun comes up and neither of you notices.\n\n@event soc_secret\ntitle: A Secret\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ncast:\n  npc: any where tagged(npc, peer) and rel(npc) >= 15\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nrepeat: 8\nart: lock\ntags: social\nscene: Two students huddled under a staircase, one whispering into the other's ear, a finger to their lips.\ntext: {~{npc} pulls you under the stairs. \"I need to tell someone or I'll burst. Swear you won't tell anyone. Not even the portraits.\"|{npc} closes the dormitory door, checks it, and checks it again. \"I have to tell someone. Swear you'll keep it.\"|{npc} walks you to the far end of the lake path, where nobody goes. \"Can you keep something? Really keep it?\"}\n> left: \"I'd rather not carry it.\"\n  rel npc -tiny\n  say: {npc} looks hurt, then relieved. \"Maybe that's better.\" You'll never know.\n> right: \"I swear.\"\n  mood -tiny\n  rel npc +medium\n  guile +tiny\n  say: {~It's not a big secret. It's the kind that's only big to the person holding it. You hold it with {them}.|It's about a letter from home, and it's heavier than {they} let on. You carry half of it now.|It's ridiculous, and {they} know it's ridiculous, and {they} needed someone else to know anyway. You do.}\n\n@event soc_argument\ntitle: Words Exchanged\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ncast:\n  npc: any where tagged(npc, peer) and rel(npc) >= 6 and bond(npc) != \"partner\"\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nrepeat: 6\nweight: 8\nart: bolt\ntags: social\nscene: Two students facing off in an empty corridor, one with arms crossed, the other mid-gesture, both flushed.\ntext: It starts with something small — {~a borrowed book, a joke that landed wrong|whose turn it was to fetch the tea|a seat someone saved and someone else took} — and suddenly you and {npc} are arguing {~in the corridor|on the stairs|in the middle of the refectory}, louder than either of you intended.\n> left: Apologise first\n  rel npc +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~\"I'm sorry.\" {npc} deflates. \"...Me too.\" It's over. It feels better than winning would have.|You say sorry first. {npc} looks almost disappointed not to get to say it. You share a bun about it later.|\"I'm sorry.\" {npc} blinks, then laughs. \"We were both being absurd.\" You were.}\n> right: Stand your ground\n  mood -tiny\n  chance even:\n    rel npc -medium\n    say: {npc} storms off. You were right. It doesn't feel as good as you'd hoped.\n  else:\n    rel npc +medium\n    charm +small\n    say: You argue it out until you're both laughing at how ridiculous it got. Somehow you're closer after.\n\n@event soc_village_trip\ntitle: A Day in the Village\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ncast:\n  npc: any where tagged(npc, peer) and rel(npc) >= 6\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nwhen: gold >= 5\nrepeat: 5\nart: house\ntags: social\nscene: A cobbled village street with crooked shopfronts, two students sharing a paper cone of roasted nuts.\ntext: \"Barge to the village? Tea at Mabel's, a wander round the bookshops, back before curfew?\" {npc} is already wearing {their} coat.\n> left: Not this week\n  rel npc -tiny\n  say: {~{They} bring{s} you back a cinnamon bun anyway.|{They} bring{s} you back a postcard of the village, which is three streets long.|{They} come{s} back with a story about a goat and tell{s} it badly.}\n> right: Grab your coat\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +medium\n  rel npc +small\n  say: {~Tea, buns, a bookshop cat that sits on {npc}'s feet. You almost miss the last barge, laughing.|Mabel's, then the market, then a long argument about which bun is best. You get the last barge home with sticky fingers.|Rain, so you spend the whole afternoon in the bookshop. {npc} buys a book about knots and explains every one on the barge home.}\n\n@event soc_friend_sick\ntitle: Sick Friend\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ncast:\n  npc: any where tagged(npc, peer) and (bond(npc) == \"friend\" or bond(npc) == \"close\" or bond(npc) == \"partner\")\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nrepeat: 8\nart: rain\ntags: social\nscene: A student tucked into an infirmary bed under a mountain of blankets, a friend sitting beside them with a bowl of soup.\ntext: {npc} has Lake Fever and is miserable in the infirmary. Sister Amaranth says visitors are allowed, but she won't be responsible.\n> left: Send a card\n  rel npc +tiny\n  say: You send a card with a drawing of Sister Amaranth as a dragon. {npc} keeps it on {their} bedside table.\n> right: Sit with them every evening\n  rel npc +medium\n  alignment +tiny\n  chance unlikely:\n    gain sick\n    say: You keep {them} company for a week. Then you're in the next bed. You both find this hilarious.\n  else:\n    say: {~You read aloud, badly, doing voices. {npc} recovers and claims it was the voices.|You teach {them} every card game you know and lose all of them. {npc} recovers, insufferably.|You bring soup from the kitchens every evening and tell {them} all the gossip. {npc} recovers mostly out of curiosity.}\n\n@event soc_gift_given\ntitle: A Small Gift\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ncast:\n  npc: any where tagged(npc, peer) and rel(npc) >= 9\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nrepeat: 7\nart: gem\ntags: social\nscene: A small wrapped parcel tied with ribbon left on a dormitory pillow, a pressed flower tucked into the bow.\ntext: There's a small parcel on your pillow. A note in {npc}'s handwriting: \"Saw this and thought of you. Don't make it weird.\"\n> left: Thank {them} later\n  mood +tiny\n  rel npc +tiny\n  say: {~It's a tiny brass compass that always points to the nearest teapot. You use it constantly.|It's a bookmark that hums when you stop reading. Annoying. You love it.|It's a pair of gloves that stay warm all winter. You wear them until they're threadbare.}\n> right: Get {them} something back (8 gold)\n  gold -small\n  mood +small\n  rel npc +medium\n  say: You find {them} {~a book {they}'d wanted for months|a scarf in exactly {their} colour|a sketchbook with good paper}. {npc}'s face when {they} open{s} it is worth twice the price.\n\n@event soc_drift\ntitle: Drifting Apart\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ncast:\n  npc: any where tagged(npc, peer) and rel(npc) >= 6 and rel(npc) < 24 and apart(npc) >= 2\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nwhen: since(soc_drift) > 3\nweight: 6\nrepeat: 6\nart: hourglass\ntags: social\nscene: A student glancing back across a busy courtyard at another who hasn't seen them, the space between them full of strangers.\ntext: You realise you haven't really talked to {npc} in weeks. You see {them} {~across the courtyard|at the far end of the refectory|on the library steps}, laughing with people you don't know.\n> left: Let it be\n  rel npc -small\n  say: People drift. It's nobody's fault. You still smile when you pass in the halls.\n> right: Go over and say hello\n  chance likely:\n    rel npc +medium\n    mood +tiny\n    say: {~\"There you are!\" {npc} says, as if you'd been the one missing. Maybe you had.|{npc} introduces you to everyone as \"the one I told you about\". You wonder what {they} told them. It seems to have been good.|{npc} shifts over to make room on the bench without a word, as if your seat had been waiting.}\n  else:\n    rel npc +tiny\n    mood -tiny\n    say: You go over. It is friendly, and short, and you both say \"we should catch up\" in a way that means you won't.\n\n@event soc_rumour\ntitle: Rumours About {npc}\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ncast:\n  npc: any where rel(npc) >= 9 and not tagged(npc, villager)\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nrepeat: 8\nart: paper\ntags: social\nscene: A cluster of students whispering behind their hands in a corridor, glancing toward someone just out of view.\ntext: A nasty rumour about {npc} is going round {~the common room|the refectory|the library steps}: {~that {they} cheated on an exam|that {they} {are} leaving at the end of term in disgrace|that {they} said something cruel about a friend}. It's not true. You know it's not true. People are laughing.\n> left: Let it blow over\n  mood -tiny\n  say: It blows over. {npc} never finds out you heard it. You find out you don't like yourself for it, a little.\n> right: Shut it down\n  alignment +tiny\n  challenge negotiate apprentice:\n    win:\n      rel npc +medium\n      renown +small\n      say: You make the rumour-monger look so foolish that the story dies on the spot. {npc} hears about it later and just squeezes your arm.\n    lose:\n      rel npc +small\n      renown -tiny\n      say: You make a scene and now there are rumours about you too. {npc} thinks you're an idiot. A loyal idiot.\n\n@event soc_homework_help\ntitle: Desperate Homework\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ncast:\n  npc: any where (tagged(npc, student) or tagged(npc, prefect)) and rel(npc) >= 0\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nwhen: term and not wrapup\nrepeat: 5\nart: feather\ntags: social study\nscene: A student with ink on their face clutching a half-finished essay, another student leaning over to read it.\ntext: {npc} waves a half-finished {~essay|lab report|star chart} at you. \"It's due in an hour and I've written the title twice. Help.\"\n> left: Explain the concept\n  challenge research apprentice:\n    win:\n      rel npc +medium\n      lore +tiny\n      say: You explain it so clearly that {npc} finishes the essay with time to spare. {They} insist{s} you're a genius.\n    lose:\n      rel npc +tiny\n      say: You explain it so confusingly that you both end up more lost. You hand it in together, two confused people against the world.\n> right: \"Copy mine.\"\n  alignment -tiny\n  rel npc +small\n  chance unlikely:\n    study -small\n    rel npc -tiny\n    say: The professor notices two identical pieces of work. You both get detention, a lecture, and a note on your record that every one of your professors reads.\n    gain detention\n  else:\n    say: {npc} hands it in and gets a better mark than you did. Unbelievable.\n\n@event soc_faculty_advice\ntitle: A Word of Advice\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ncast:\n  npc: any where tagged(npc, teacher) and rel(npc) >= 6\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nwhen: term and not wrapup\nrepeat: 6\nart: scroll\ntags: study\nscene: A professor walking with a student along a covered walkway, pointing at something in an open book.\ntext: {npc} catches you after a lecture. \"You've been working hard. Or hardly working. I can never tell with your year. Some advice, if you'll take it?\"\n> left: Politely decline\n  rel npc -tiny\n  say: \"Suit yourself.\" {They} walk{s} off. Faculty have long memories.\n> right: Listen carefully\n  mood -tiny\n  rel npc +small\n  study +small\n  lore +tiny\n  say: It's good advice about how to study, {~annoyingly good|delivered at great length|illustrated with a story from {their} own disastrous first year}. Every piece of work you hand in that month is better for it.\n\n# ---------------- Romance ----------------\n\n@event soc_spark\ntitle: A Spark\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ncast:\n  npc: any where romanceable(npc) and rel(npc) >= 9 and spark(npc) < 12 and bond(npc) != \"partner\"\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nwhen: bonds(partner) == 0\nweight: 12\nrepeat: 6\nart: sparkle\ntags: social romance\nscene: Two hands brushing as they reach for the same quill, both students freezing for a heartbeat.\ntext: You and {npc} reach for {&the same quill|the last biscuit on the plate|the same library book} at the same moment. Your hands touch. Neither of you lets go immediately. {&It's a perfectly ordinary quill.|It's a perfectly ordinary biscuit.|It's a perfectly ordinary book.}\n> left: Laugh it off\n  rel npc +tiny\n  say: \"All yours,\" you say. {npc} laughs. The moment passes. Mostly.\n> right: Hold on a moment longer\n  chance likely:\n    spark npc +medium\n    rel npc +tiny\n    say: {npc} looks up. Something in {their} face shifts. Then {they} let{s} go, a little pink, and {&the quill rolls under the desk|the biscuit breaks neatly in half|the book falls open on the floor between you}.\n  else:\n    rel npc +tiny\n    mood -tiny\n    say: {npc} pulls back as if burned, and the rest of the {&lesson|meal|afternoon} is very, very awkward.\n\n@event soc_date\ntitle: An Invitation\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ncast:\n  npc: any where romanceable(npc) and spark(npc) >= 12 and spark(npc) < 40 and bond(npc) != \"partner\" and rel(npc) >= 9\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nweight: 12\nrepeat: 4\nart: heart\ntags: social romance\nscene: A student nervously holding out a hand-drawn ticket to the village's floating lantern show.\ntext: You could ask {npc} to {&the lantern show in the village|the travelling players at the Newt|the music on the quay} this weekend. As friends. Or, you know. Not as friends.\n> left: Ask as friends\n  gold -tiny\n  rel npc +small\n  mood +small\n  say: You pay for both tickets and the hot cider. It's a lovely evening. You're both very careful about elbows.\n> right: Ask as more than friends\n  if spark(npc) >= 25:\n    challenge negotiate apprentice likely wild:\n      win:\n        spark npc +medium\n        mood +medium\n        say: {npc} says yes before you finish the sentence. You hold hands through the whole {&show and miss every lantern|play and miss every line|evening and miss every song}.\n      lose:\n        spark npc -tiny\n        mood -small\n        say: {npc} hesitates, then says {they}'d rather go as friends. It's a nice evening. It's a long evening.\n  else:\n    challenge negotiate apprentice wild:\n      win:\n        spark npc +medium\n        mood +medium\n        say: {npc} says yes before you finish the sentence. You hold hands through the whole {&show and miss every lantern|play and miss every line|evening and miss every song}.\n      lose:\n        spark npc -tiny\n        mood -small\n        say: {npc} hesitates, then says {they}'d rather go as friends. It's a nice evening. It's a long evening.\n\n@event soc_confess_generic\nramp: spark(npc) 30..45\ntitle: Feelings, Spoken\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ncast:\n  npc: any where romanceable(npc) and spark(npc) >= 30 and rel(npc) >= 18 and bond(npc) != \"partner\" and bond(npc) != \"ex\"\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nweight: 10\nart: heart\ntags: romance\nscene: Two students standing very close on a windswept bridge between towers, one with a hand over their heart.\ntext: On the bridge between the towers, the wind snatching at your robes, you realise you can't keep it in anymore. {npc} is looking at you like {they} know what you're about to say.\n> left: Say nothing\n  spark npc -small\n  say: The moment passes. You walk back together, a careful inch apart.\n> right: Tell {them}\n  challenge negotiate apprentice likely +1 if spark(npc) >= 60:\n    win:\n      bond npc partner\n      spark npc +medium\n      mood +large\n      say: \"Finally,\" {npc} says, and kisses you, and the wind steals your breath anyway.\n    lose:\n      spark npc -large\n      mood -medium\n      say: {npc} takes a long breath. \"I care about you,\" {they} say{s}. \"Just not like that. I'm sorry.\" The wind is very cold.\n\n@event soc_partner_date\ntitle: Date Night\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ncast:\n  npc: any where bond(npc) == \"partner\"\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 14\nart: candle\ntags: romance\nscene: Two people at a tiny candlelit table in a window alcove of a village tea shop, snow falling outside.\ntext: {npc} has booked the window table at Mabel's. Candles. {season == \"winter\": Snow outside.|The lake going gold outside.} \"We're splitting the bill,\" {they} say{s}. \"Don't argue.\"\n> left: Talk about the future\n  gold -tiny\n  spark npc +small\n  mood +small\n  say: {&You talk about where you'll be in ten years. In every version, you're both there.|You talk about after graduation, carefully, then not carefully at all.|You make a list of places you'll go together. It is already too long.}\n> right: Talk about absolutely nothing\n  spark npc +tiny\n  mood +medium\n  say: You argue for an hour about {&whether soup is a drink|whether owls are smart or just look it|which professor would win in a duel}, and {npc} pays the bill while you're making your closing argument. {&It is the best date of your life.|It might be better than the last one.|You lose the argument and don't mind.}\n\n@event soc_partner_neglect\ntitle: \"You're Always Busy\"\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ncast:\n  npc: any where bond(npc) == \"partner\" and apart(npc) >= 3\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nweight: 14\nrepeat: 5\nart: hourglass\ntags: romance\nscene: A person waiting alone at a table set for two, a candle burned low, the other chair empty.\ntext: {npc} is waiting outside your door. \"I feel like I only see you in corridors,\" {they} say{s}. \"Do you even want this?\"\n> left: \"I'm sorry. I'll make time.\"\n  spark npc +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  study -tiny\n  say: You skip a study session for a long walk by the lake. It's the right call.\n> right: \"You know how busy I am.\"\n  spark npc -medium\n  rel npc -medium\n  say: {npc} nods slowly. Something between you cools a degree.\n\n@event soc_breakup\ntitle: The End of Something\nclass: routine\ntone: grim\ncast:\n  npc: any where bond(npc) == \"partner\" and spark(npc) < 25\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nweight: 30\nart: heartbreak\ntags: romance crisis\nscene: Two people sitting on opposite ends of a bench in a grey garden, not looking at each other, rain beginning.\ntext: {npc} sits down at the far end of the bench. \"I think we both know,\" {they} say{s} quietly. \"This isn't working anymore.\"\n> left: Fight for it\n  challenge negotiate journeyman:\n    win:\n      spark npc +large\n      say: You talk for hours. It's raw and honest and it hurts. And at the end, {npc} takes your hand again.\n    lose:\n      bond npc ex\n      gain heartbroken\n      rel npc -large\n      say: You try. It only makes it harder. In the end you're both crying, and it's over anyway.\n> right: Let {them} go\n  bond npc ex\n  gain heartbroken\n  rel npc +small\n  say: You agree. It's terrible and gentle and right. {npc} kisses your forehead once before {they} leave{s}.\n\n@event soc_two_timing\ntitle: Found Out\nclass: incident\ntone: grim\ncast:\n  npc: any where bond(npc) == \"partner\"\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nwhen: bonds(partner) >= 2\nweight: 40\nart: heartbreak\ntags: romance crisis\nscene: A person holding up two crumpled love letters in a crowded corridor, the crowd parting around them.\ntext: {npc} is holding two letters. One is to {them}. The other isn't. \"I found it in your bag,\" {they} say{s}, very quietly. Half the corridor has stopped to watch.\n> left: Beg forgiveness\n  challenge negotiate journeyman- unlikely:\n    win:\n      spark npc -large\n      rel npc -large\n      say: {npc} doesn't forgive you. But {they} don't leave. Not yet. You're on borrowed time.\n    lose:\n      bond npc ex\n      rel npc -huge\n      renown -medium\n      gain heartbroken\n      say: {npc} tears both letters in half and walks away. Everyone knows by dinner.\n> right: Say nothing\n  bond npc ex\n  rel npc -huge\n  renown -medium\n  say: Your silence is an answer. {npc} drops the letters at your feet and leaves. The story is all over the academy.\n\n@event soc_ex_encounter\ntitle: Awkward\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ncast:\n  npc: any where bond(npc) == \"ex\"\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nrepeat: 6\nart: mirror\ntags: romance\nscene: Two people coming face to face in a narrow library aisle, both holding books, neither moving.\ntext: {~You turn into the library aisle and there's {npc}. Your ex. Holding the same book you came for.|The only free seat in the refectory is opposite {npc}. Your ex. {They} look{s} up at the same moment you do.|You open the door to the owlery and there's {npc}. Your ex. Holding a letter, halfway to an owl.} Neither of you moves.\n> left: Turn around and leave\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You find a different book in a different aisle and read the same paragraph six times.\n> right: Say hello like a person\n  rel npc +medium\n  challenge negotiate apprentice-:\n    win:\n      bond npc friend\n      say: It's awkward for one minute, then it isn't. You end up laughing about the worst of the dates you went on. Friends, maybe. Really.\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      say: \"Hello.\" \"Hello.\" You both stand there. Then you both leave. It's a start.\n\n# ---------------- Rivalry ----------------\n\n@event soc_rival_taunt\ntitle: A Public Taunt\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ncast:\n  npc: any where tagged(npc, peer) and (bond(npc) == \"rival\" or bond(npc) == \"nemesis\" or rel(npc) <= -12)\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nrepeat: 4\nart: mask\ntags: rival\nscene: A smirking student surrounded by a small audience, pointing at someone across a crowded dining hall.\ntext: In the middle of a crowded {~meal|common room|library queue}, {npc} loudly recounts {~your most embarrassing moment of the term|the time you walked into a door that was a painting|your worst spell in front of the whole class}. People laugh. {They} look{s} very pleased.\n> left: Ignore {them}\n  mood -small\n  say: The laughter dies down. {~You carry on with as much dignity as you can find.|You wait it out, and stare at a fixed point on the wall.|Someone nearby passes you a biscuit in silent solidarity.}\n> right: Fire back\n  challenge negotiate vs npc.charm:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      rel npc -small\n      say: {~Your comeback is so good that someone at the far table applauds.|Your comeback lands so well that people repeat it for a week.|Your comeback is quiet, exact and devastating.} {npc} goes scarlet.\n    lose:\n      renown -small\n      rel npc -tiny\n      say: Your comeback comes out as a squeak. The laughter doubles.\n\n@event soc_make_peace\nramp: rel(npc) 3..15\ntime: noon afternoon\ntitle: Burying the Hatchet\nclass: milestone\ntone: tense\ncast:\n  npc: any where tagged(npc, peer) and (bond(npc) == \"rival\" or bond(npc) == \"nemesis\") and rel(npc) >= 3\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nweight: 18\nart: hand\ntags: rival social\nscene: Two former rivals shaking hands on a dueling green at sunset, wands tucked away.\ntext: {npc} finds you after class, scuffing {their} shoes. \"This whole rivalry thing. It's getting tiring. Truce?\"\n> left: Keep the edge\n  rel npc -small\n  say: \"Thought so,\" {npc} says, half-smiling. The game goes on.\n> right: Shake on it\n  bond npc friend\n  rel npc +large\n  mood +small\n  say: You shake. It's strange — you know {them} better than most of your friends. Maybe that's the point.\n\n@event soc_rival_bet\ntitle: The Wager\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ncast:\n  npc: any where (tagged(npc, student) or tagged(npc, prefect)) and bond(npc) == \"rival\"\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nwhen: term and not wrapup\nrepeat: 6\nart: dice\ntags: rival\nscene: Two students slapping coins onto a table between them, a crowd gathering, a chalked tally on the wall.\ntext: \"Eight crowns says I {~outscore you in the next practical|light more candles in one breath than you|hold a ward longer than you},\" {npc} says, slapping coins on the table. A crowd forms.\n> left: Decline the bet\n  rel npc -tiny\n  say: \"Thought so.\" {npc} pockets the coins. You decide not to care. You care.\n> right: Take the bet\n  when: gold >= 8\n  challenge spell vs npc.arcana wild:\n    win:\n      gold +small\n      renown +small\n      rel npc +small\n      say: You win by a hair. {npc} pays up with a theatrical bow. \"Next time.\"\n    lose:\n      gold -small\n      rel npc +tiny\n      say: You lose by a hair. \"Next time,\" you say. {npc} grins. \"Next time.\"\n\n# Spark crossing @balance spark_follow's cooldown mark (playtest 2, M37): the engine asks once whether you want it.\n@event spark_cooldown\ntitle: Something Between You\ntrigger: queued\nactor: npc\ndefer: off_campus or not around(npc)\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nart: rose\ntags: romance social\nscene: Two students on a bench a little closer than friends usually sit, both pretending to read the same page.\ntext: Something has shifted between you and {npc}. {~They hold your gaze a moment too long.|They've started saving you a seat, and blushing about it.|Your names keep turning up next to each other in other people's gossip.} You could let it grow, or gently let it cool.\n> left: Let it grow\n  say: {~You don't say anything. You don't move away either.|You hold their gaze back, for exactly as long.|Let them gossip. You sit beside them anyway, in front of everyone.}\n> right: Let it cool, kindly\n  cool npc\n  rel npc +tiny\n  say: {~You find a gentle way to say you value them as a friend. They look relieved, or almost.|You keep it light and friendly, and the moment passes. The friendship stays.|You give the gossip nothing to work with, gently, and in a week it moves on. The friendship doesn't.}\n\n# ---------------- Jealousy (playtest 2, N7; design §8.2): two people with a spark for you, neither your partner yet ----------------\n\n@event jealousy_two_invitations\ntitle: Two Invitations\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\ncast:\n  suitor: any where romanceable(suitor) and spark(suitor) >= 30 and bond(suitor) != \"partner\" and around(suitor)\n  other: any where romanceable(other) and spark(other) >= 30 and bond(other) != \"partner\" and around(other)\nper: suitor\nrepeat: 2\nweight: 20\nart: heart\ntags: romance social\nscene: A student holding two folded notes, one in each hand, in a corridor where two people wait at opposite ends.\ntext: Two notes in one morning. {suitor} asks if you're free on Friday evening, in neat handwriting, crossed out once. {other} asks the same, for the same evening, in a hurry. They both know the other asked. The whole corridor seems to know.\n> left: Say yes to {suitor}\n  spark suitor +small\n  spark other -small\n  rel other -tiny\n  say: Friday with {suitor} is everything the note promised. On Saturday {other} passes you on the stairs with a smile that stops a little short of the eyes.\n> right: Say yes to {other}\n  spark other +small\n  spark suitor -small\n  rel suitor -tiny\n  say: Friday with {other} runs late and warm. On Saturday {suitor} is very polite to you at breakfast, which is worse than if they'd been rude.\n\n@event jealousy_seen_together\ntitle: \"Who Was That?\"\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\ncast:\n  suitor: any where romanceable(suitor) and spark(suitor) >= 30 and bond(suitor) != \"partner\" and around(suitor)\n  other: any where romanceable(other) and spark(other) >= 30 and bond(other) != \"partner\" and around(other)\nper: suitor\nrepeat: 2\nweight: 20\nart: heartbreak\ntags: romance social\nscene: Two students talking by a window in a crowded common room, one of them asking a question too casually while looking at someone across the room.\ntext: {suitor} saw you laughing with {other} in the courtyard yesterday, and is now asking about it too casually, in the voice people use when they've rehearsed. \"You two looked… close. Not that it's any of my business.\"\n> left: Tell the truth: you like {other}\n  spark suitor -small\n  rel suitor +tiny\n  spark other +tiny\n  say: It lands like a dropped plate. {suitor} nods, and swallows, and thanks you for not pretending. It's the kindest thing you've done all week, and it doesn't feel kind at all.\n> right: \"Oh, we're just friends\"\n  chance likely:\n    spark suitor +small\n    say: {suitor}'s shoulders come down an inch. You spend the rest of the evening together, and nobody mentions the courtyard again.\n  else:\n    spark suitor +tiny\n    spark other -small\n    say: It goes well, until word gets back to {other}, who hears \"just friends\" and decides to believe it.\n\n@event jealousy_cold_supper\ntitle: A Quiet Supper\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\ncast:\n  suitor: any where romanceable(suitor) and spark(suitor) >= 30 and bond(suitor) != \"partner\" and around(suitor)\n  other: any where romanceable(other) and spark(other) >= 30 and bond(other) != \"partner\" and around(other)\nper: suitor\nrepeat: 2\nweight: 20\nart: meal\ntags: romance social\nscene: A long refectory table at supper, one student eating in pointed silence beside another, a third person waving from the far end of the hall.\ntext: {suitor} has gone quiet at supper, and answers in single words. From the far end of the hall, {other} is waving you over to an empty seat. You know exactly why {suitor} is quiet. So, by the look of it, does {other}.\n> left: Stay and talk it through\n  spark suitor +small\n  spark other -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: A long walk after supper and a careful, clumsy conversation. {suitor} laughs at the end of it, which is something. At the far end of the hall, the empty seat stays empty.\n> right: Take the seat {other} saved\n  spark other +small\n  spark suitor -small\n  say: The seat is warm and so is {other}. Behind you, {suitor} finishes supper alone and leaves without the pudding, which everyone notices.\n"},{"path":"content/states/states.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Persistent states. A state is something that sticks to the\n#  player across months: an origin, a class, a club, a job, an\n#  illness, a curse. States can:\n#    tick:      run every month start while active\n#    duration:  default length in months (omit = until removed)\n#    group:     only one state of the same group at a time\n#    on_add / on_expire / on_remove: effects\n#  Events check them with has(state_id) and left(state_id).\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- Origins (chosen at the start) ----------------\n\n@state origin_noble\nname: Minor Nobility\nkind: origin\ngroup: origin\nart: crown\ndesc: A family name that opens doors, and letters from home that bring money and expectations in the same envelope.\nmoney: Comfortable: 90 crowns to start, and 38 from the family before each later term, now and then with a gift. Every letter weighs on you a little. Tuition is yours to pay.\non_add:\n  origin_noble_start\n  renown +6\n\n@state origin_scholar\nname: Scholarship Student\nkind: origin\ngroup: origin\nart: scroll\ndesc: The Crown pays for clever students, for as long as they stay clever.\nmoney: Tight: 60 crowns to start. Before each later term, 38 crowns if last term's GPA was 3.0 or better, 46 at 3.5 or better, and nothing below. Tuition is yours to pay.\non_add:\n  origin_scholar_start\n\n@state origin_hedge\nname: Hedge-Witch's Grandchild\nkind: origin\ngroup: origin\nart: mushroom\ndesc: Raised among drying herbs and muttered charms: a practical hand with potions, and not much patience for theory.\nmoney: Poor: 45 crowns and no money from home. You will need a job from the first term.\ntags: poor\non_add:\n  origin_hedge_start\n  mood +6\n\n@state origin_street\nname: Street-Raised\nkind: origin\ngroup: origin\nart: mask\ndesc: You learned to read crowds before you learned to read runes.\nmoney: Poor: 38 crowns and no one to send more. You will need a job from the first term.\ntags: poor\non_add:\n  origin_street_start\n\n@state origin_merchant\nname: Merchant's Child\nkind: origin\ngroup: origin\nart: scales\ndesc: Everything has a price, and you were taught to haggle it down.\nmoney: Rich but unsteady: 150 crowns to start, and 78 from the company before each later term when trade goes well, about two terms in three; nothing when it doesn't. Tuition is yours to pay.\non_add:\n  origin_merchant_start\n\n# ---------------- Electives (one per term) ----------------\n\n@state class_pyromancy\nname: Elective: Pyromancy\nkind: class\ngroup: elective\nart: flame\ntags: class\ndesc: Fire answers to will, not wishes. Singed eyebrows are considered a rite of passage.\ntick:\n  arcana +2\n  health -1\n\n@state class_tidecalling\nname: Elective: Tidecalling\nkind: class\ngroup: elective\nart: wave\ntags: class\ndesc: Lessons on the lake shore, bending water and patience in equal measure.\ntick:\n  arcana +1\n  vigor +1\n  mood +1\n\n@state class_illusion\nname: Elective: Illusion & Glamour\nkind: class\ngroup: elective\nart: mask\ntags: class\ndesc: The art of making people see what you want. Professor Vey insists it is not lying.\ntick:\n  guile +1\n  charm +1\n\n@state class_alchemy\nname: Elective: Practical Alchemy\nkind: class\ngroup: elective\nart: cauldron\ntags: class\ndesc: Brewing, distilling and the occasional controlled explosion.\ntick:\n  alchemy +2\n\n@state class_divination\nname: Elective: Divination\nkind: class\ngroup: elective\nart: eye\ntags: class\ndesc: Tea leaves, star charts and the Mirror of Next Tuesday.\ntick:\n  lore +1\n  if rand(1, 3) == 1:\n    mood -2\n\n@state class_artifice\nname: Elective: Artifice\nkind: class\ngroup: elective\nart: gear\ntags: class\ndesc: Enchanting objects to do what they were never meant to do.\ntick:\n  alchemy +1\n  lore +1\n\n@state class_beastcraft\nname: Elective: Beastcraft\nkind: class\ngroup: elective\nart: paw\ntags: class\ndesc: Familiars, griffon husbandry and dodging the manticore pen.\ntick:\n  vigor +1\n  mood +2\n\n@state class_astromancy\nname: Elective: Astromancy\nkind: class\ngroup: elective\nart: planet\ntags: class\ndesc: Midnight lectures on the observatory roof. You will learn the stars; you will lose sleep.\ntick:\n  lore +2\n  health -1\n\n@state class_healing\nname: Elective: Healing Arts\nkind: class\ngroup: elective\nart: cross\ntags: class\ndesc: Mending flesh with light and needle. Sister Amaranth expects perfection.\ntick:\n  alchemy +1\n  lore +1\n  health +1\n\n@state class_umbral\nname: Elective: Umbral Theory\nkind: class\ngroup: elective\nart: skull\ntags: class\ndesc: Restricted. The study of shadow, death and what lies behind both. Strictly theoretical. Mostly.\ntick:\n  arcana +2\n  lore +1\n  mood -2\n\n# ---------------- Clubs (one at a time) ----------------\n\n@state club_dueling\nname: Club: Dueling Circle\nkind: club\ngroup: club\nart: duel\ndesc: Bruises, glory and the Spring Tournament.\ntick:\n  vigor +1\n  arcana +1\n  health -1\n\n@state club_choir\nname: Club: Choir of the Spheres\nkind: club\ngroup: club\nart: music\ndesc: Harmonies that make the candles flicker in time.\ntick:\n  charm +1\n  mood +2\n\n@state club_skyrace\nname: Club: Skyrace Squad\nkind: club\ngroup: club\nart: broom\ndesc: Brooms, hoops and screaming crowds.\ntick:\n  vigor +2\n  health -1\n\n@state club_ledger\nname: Club: The Lumen Ledger\nkind: club\ngroup: club\nart: paper\ndesc: The student paper. Deadlines, scoops and enemies.\ntick:\n  guile +1\n  charm +1\n\n@state club_garden\nname: Club: Verdant Circle\nkind: club\ngroup: club\nart: leaf\ndesc: Greenhouse afternoons with mandrakes that sing off-key.\ntick:\n  alchemy +1\n  mood +1\n\n@state club_stargazers\nname: Club: Stargazers\nkind: club\ngroup: club\nart: star\ndesc: Telescopes, cocoa and constellations nobody else can name.\ntick:\n  lore +1\n  mood +1\n\n@state club_cartographers\nname: Club: Midnight Cartographers\nkind: club\ngroup: club\nart: map\ndesc: A club that officially does not exist, mapping corridors that officially do not either.\ntick:\n  guile +1\n  lore +1\n\n# ---------------- Jobs (one at a time) ----------------\n\n@state job_library\nname: Job: Library Assistant\nkind: job\ngroup: job\nart: book\ndesc: Reshelving books that bite. Quiet hours, modest pay.\ntick:\n  gold +10\n  lore +1\n  mood -1\n\n@state job_tavern\nname: Job: The Gilded Newt\nkind: job\ngroup: job\nart: cup\ndesc: Serving cider in the village tavern. Good tips, sore feet.\ntick:\n  gold +16\n  charm +1\n  health -2\n\n@state job_apothecary\nname: Job: Apothecary Clerk\nkind: job\ngroup: job\nart: bottle\ndesc: Labelling jars at Pell & Daughters. Do not lick anything.\ntick:\n  gold +12\n  alchemy +1\n\n@state job_stables\nname: Job: Familiar Stables\nkind: job\ngroup: job\nart: paw\ndesc: Feeding hippogriffs and mucking out things that breathe smoke.\ntick:\n  gold +11\n  vigor +1\n  health -1\n\n@state job_tutor\nname: Job: First-Year Tutor\nkind: job\ngroup: job\nart: feather\ndesc: Explaining the difference between a hex and a jinx, again.\ntick:\n  gold +12\n  study +tiny\n  mood -1\n\n@state job_courier\nname: Job: Broom Courier\nkind: job\ngroup: job\nart: letter\ndesc: Parcels across the lake at full speed. Pays well. Hurts often.\ntick:\n  gold +18\n  vigor +1\n  health -3\n\n# ---------------- Conditions ----------------\n\n@state hungry\nname: Hungry\nkind: debuff\nart: meal\nduration: 1\ndesc: The board went unpaid this month. Bread rolls pocketed from the refectory only go so far.\ntick:\n  health -3\n  mood -4\n  say: {~Your stomach growls through every lecture.|Bread rolls in your pockets, and not enough of them.|You learn exactly how long a bowl of porridge lasts.}\n\n@state sick\nname: Lake Fever\nkind: debuff\nart: rain\nduration: 2\ndesc: Shivers, sniffles and a nose that glows faintly blue.\ntick:\n  health -4\n  mood -2\n  say: {~You sniffle through the month, nose glowing faintly blue.|Shivers, tea and far too many handkerchiefs.}\n\n@state injured\nname: Injured\nkind: debuff\nart: cross\nduration: 2\ndesc: Something is bruised, sprained or bent the wrong way.\ntick:\n  health -2\n  vigor -1\n  say: {~Every staircase reminds you where it hurts.|You limp, and the moving stairs show no sympathy.}\n\n@state exhausted\nname: Exhausted\nkind: debuff\nart: bed\nduration: 1\ndesc: You could sleep standing up. You might have.\ntick:\n  health -3\n  mood -3\n  say: {~You yawn through a whole lecture without ever closing your mouth.|You could sleep standing up, and once you do.}\n\n@state inspired\nname: Inspired\nkind: buff\nart: sparkle\nduration: 2\ndesc: Ideas arrive faster than you can write them down.\ntick:\n  lore +1\n  arcana +1\n  mood +2\n  say: {~Ideas arrive faster than you can write them down.|You fill a notebook and start another.}\n\n@state heartbroken\nname: Heartbroken\nkind: debuff\nart: heartbreak\nduration: 3\ndesc: Songs are suddenly about you. All of them.\ntick:\n  mood -4\n  say: {~Every song in the common room is about you.|You walk the long way round, to miss a certain corridor.}\n\n@state slacking\nname: Skipping Classes\nkind: debuff\nart: bed\nduration: 2\ndesc: Freedom! Also: falling behind.\ntick:\n  mood +2\n  say: {~A free morning, a lie-in, a twinge of guilt.|You skip a lecture, and nobody seems to mind yet.}\n\n@state detention\nname: Detention\nkind: debuff\nart: lock\nduration: 1\ndesc: Scrubbing cauldrons under the eye of a prefect.\ntick:\n  mood -3\n  say: {~Cauldrons scrubbed under a prefect's eye.|Evenings in detention, smelling of old potion.}\n\n@state probation\nname: Academic Probation\nkind: debuff\nart: scales\ndesc: Your marks are below the line. Two terms under a 1.0 and the Disciplinary Board sits. A GPA of 2.0 clears it.\ntick:\n  mood -2\n  if gpa >= 2.0:\n    lose probation\n  say: {~Probation sits on your shoulder through every lecture.|Your name is on a list you never wanted to be on.}\n\n# Financial aid (playtest 2, X14): a term's grant from the bursar's hardship fund, or the bursar's own loan, which is\n# gentler than Grisk's (no interest, no collectors; a missed month costs only mood and waits).\n@state hardship_grant\nname: Hardship Grant\nkind: buff\nart: scales\nduration: 4\ndesc: The bursar's hardship fund pays 8 crowns a month for a term. The paperwork asked how poor you were, in detail.\ntick:\n  gold +8\n  say: {~The hardship grant arrives in a brown envelope with your name spelled wrong.|Eight crowns from the fund, and a form to say you received them.}\n\n@state bursar_loan\nname: Bursar's Loan\nkind: debuff\nart: scales\ndesc: The academy lent you sixty crowns. It takes 8 a month when you can pay, seven times, then a last 4, with no interest and no collectors: only a very patient ledger.\ntick:\n  if loan_paid >= 7 and gold >= 4:\n    gold -4\n    loan_paid += 1\n  elif loan_paid < 7 and gold >= 8:\n    gold -8\n    loan_paid += 1\n  else:\n    mood -1\n    say: {~No loan payment this month. The bursar notes it without comment, which is its own comment.|The loan waits. The ledger is patient; you are less so.}\n  if loan_paid >= 8:\n    lose bursar_loan\n    say: The bursar's loan is paid off. She stamps the ledger with something like a smile.\n\n@state in_debt\nname: Debt to Grisk\nkind: debuff\nart: chain\ndesc: The goblin moneylender takes 12 crowns a month until the debt is cleared. Ignore a missed payment and he adds another.\n# Unpaid, the debt escalates (playtest 2, M22): the first miss brings a reminder (ignore it and a month is added),\n# the next the collectors.\ntick:\n  if gold >= 12:\n    gold -12\n    debt_paid += 1\n  else:\n    mood -4\n    debt_missed += 1\n    if debt_missed == 1:\n      queue grisk_reminder now\n  if debt_paid >= 6:\n    lose in_debt\n  if debt_missed >= 2:\n    queue grisk_collectors now\n\n@state famous\nname: Talk of the Academy\nkind: buff\nart: star\nduration: 3\ndesc: Strangers know your name. Some of them even say it nicely.\ntick:\n  renown +2\n  mood +1\n\n@state hiccup_hex\nname: Hiccup Hex\nkind: debuff\nart: sparkle\nduration: 2\ndesc: Every hiccup releases a small, harmless, extremely embarrassing firework.\ntick:\n  mood -2\n  renown +1\n\n@state blessed\nname: Maud's Blessing\nkind: buff\nart: flower\nduration: 3\ndesc: A sprig of rosemary in your pocket that never wilts.\ntick:\n  health +3\n\n@state familiar_fox\nname: Familiar: Ember\nkind: buff\nart: fox\ndesc: A fox-spirit with a smouldering tail who has decided you are hers.\n\n@state familiar_owl\nname: Familiar: Clockwork Owl\nkind: buff\nart: owl\ndesc: Mir's gift. It hoots the hour, mostly correctly, and remembers your homework.\n\n@state training\nname: Tournament Training\nkind: buff\nart: duel\nduration: 2\ndesc: Dawn drills on the dueling green.\ntick:\n  vigor +2\n  arcana +1\n  health -1\n\n@state wakeful\nname: Draught of Wakefulness\nkind: buff\nart: potion\nduration: 1\ndesc: You haven't blinked in eleven hours. Your notes are immaculate.\ntick:\n  study +small\n  health -3\n\n@state haunted\nname: Haunted Dormitory\nkind: debuff\nart: ghost\nduration: 2\ndesc: Something rearranges your socks at night.\ntick:\n  mood -2\n\n@state watched\nname: Under Bram's Eye\nkind: debuff\nart: eye\nduration: 3\ndesc: The prefect is watching you. Specifically, and with relish.\ntick:\n  mood -1\n\n@state umbral_curse\nname: Umbral Curse\nkind: debuff\nart: skull\ndesc: A cold that lives under your skin. Something in the restricted section followed you home.\ntick:\n  health -3\n  mood -1\n  arcana +1\n\n@state hollow_dreams\nname: Hollow Dreams\nkind: debuff\nart: moon\ndesc: Every night: a black star, a door beneath the lake, a voice that knows your name.\ntick:\n  mood -1\n  lore +1\n\n@state star_mark\nname: The Star-Mark\nkind: buff\nart: star\ndesc: A pale, star-shaped mark on your palm that hums near old magic.\ntick:\n  arcana +1\n\n@state summer_away\nname: Summer Away\nkind: neutral\nart: house\nduration: 2\ndesc: Away from the academy for the summer recess. No rent, no classes.\n\n@state summer_work\nname: Summer Work\nkind: job\nart: coin\nduration: 2\ndesc: A summer of honest (and occasionally dishonest) labour.\ntick:\n  gold +25\n  health -2\n\n@state frog_voice\nname: Croaking\nkind: debuff\nart: frog\nduration: 1\ndesc: A botched transfiguration left you with the voice of a bullfrog.\ntick:\n  charm -1\n  mood -2\n\n@state garden_plot\nname: Garden Plot\nkind: buff\nart: leaf\ndesc: A little square of earth behind the greenhouses that is yours. Things grow; so do you.\ntick:\n  alchemy +1\n  if rand(1, 3) == 1:\n    mood +2\n\n@state pen_pal\nname: Pen Pal in Thornwick\nkind: buff\nart: letter\ndesc: A student at the rival academy who writes you long, funny letters full of terrible gossip.\n# Legacy (playtest 2, X9): the pen pal is now Tamsin (@npc tamsin) and their letters are cards; nothing grants this.\n\n@state frost_ward\nname: Ilse's Frost-Ward\nkind: buff\nart: snow\ndesc: A sliver of Northern winter that Ilse sewed into your collar. Cold things think twice about you.\ntick:\n  arcana +1\n  health +1\n\n# ---------------- Break plans (design §4.3; phase 2b) ----------------\n# Chosen at the start of each break (calendar_2b.grim: break_plan). A plan lasts the break.\n\n@state plan_castle\nname: Break: at the castle\nkind: plan\ngroup: plan\nduration: 2\nart: tower\ndesc: A skeleton crew, empty corridors and secrets. Board costs 6 crowns a month.\n\n@state plan_home\nname: Break: at home\nkind: plan\ngroup: plan\nduration: 2\nart: house\ntags: away\nmap_id: home\ndesc: Family, old friends and free board. The days are slower here.\non_add:\n  gain summer_away 2\n\n@state gig_grounds\nname: Holiday gig: the grounds\ngroup: gig\nduration: 2\nart: leaf\ndesc: Working the grounds for Old Maud this break. Good pay, sore hands.\n\n@state gig_kitchens\nname: Holiday gig: the kitchens\ngroup: gig\nduration: 2\nart: meal\ndesc: Working the Refectory kitchens this break. Good pay, short temper.\n\n# The holiday version of a term job (X15): for those at its top rank; its own pay, through its cards.\n@state gig_library\nname: Holiday gig: the stocktake\ngroup: gig\nduration: 2\nart: book\ndesc: The library's holiday stocktake, for its senior clerks. Every book counted, every bite noted. Better pay than the baseline gigs.\n\n@state gig_newt\nname: Holiday gig: the Newt's holiday rush\ngroup: gig\nduration: 2\nart: cup\ndesc: Running the Gilded Newt through the holiday rush, for its managers. The best tips of the year, and the longest nights.\n\n@state gig_pell\nname: Holiday gig: the remedy rush\ngroup: gig\nduration: 2\nart: bottle\ndesc: Mixing the season's remedies at Pell & Daughters, for its assistant apothecaries. Good pay, and the village depends on it.\n\n@state gig_post\nname: Holiday gig: the holiday post\ngroup: gig\nduration: 2\nart: letter\ndesc: Flying the holiday post for the courier loft, for its chief couriers. Every parcel in the valley, and the weather against you.\n\n@state plan_work\nname: Break: working full-time\nkind: plan\ngroup: plan\nduration: 2\nart: coin\ndesc: Working on campus through the break: a holiday gig, and board as usual. Tiring.\n\n@state plan_internship\nname: Break: internship\nkind: plan\ngroup: plan\nduration: 2\nart: book\ntags: away\nmap_id: internship\ndesc: A research placement away from the castle, with a stipend and a supervisor.\ntick: gold +6\n# One summer each (M16): the supervisor's report (feedback 2026-10-15b) comes first, and a strong one brings an offer\n# to come back; otherwise the post ends.\non_expire:\n  queue intern_assessment\n\n# ---------------- The midnight turn (playtest 2, M41) ----------------\n\n@state locked_out\nname: Confined After Dark\nkind: debuff\nduration: 3\nart: lock\ndesc: Caught once too often. Your door is checked at midnight, and the night is closed to you.\n"},{"path":"content/world/archetypes.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Archetypes (design §10.4): templates for side characters spawned\n#  in phase 4. Names come from per-language pools\n#  (locales/<lang>/names.lang). Every character is an adult.\n# ============================================================\n\n@archetype student\nnames: pool students\nage: 18..22\ntraits: pick 2\npronouns: any\nhouse: any\nrole: student\ndesc: A fellow student: a face in the corridor who might become a friend, a rival or a story.\n\n@archetype professor\nnames: pool professors\nage: 35..72\ntraits: pick 2\npronouns: any\nhouse: Faculty\nrole: professor\ndesc: A visiting or junior member of the faculty: an advisor, an examiner or an internship supervisor.\n\n@archetype villager\nnames: pool villagers\nage: 18..80\ntraits: pick 1\npronouns: any\nhouse: none\nrole: outsider\ndesc: Someone from the village across the lake: a shopkeeper, a bargeman, a customer at the Gilded Newt.\n\n@archetype northerner\nnames: pool northerners\nage: 18..40\ntraits: pick 2\npronouns: any\nhouse: none\nrole: outsider\ndesc: A traveller from the frozen North, with frost in their eyelashes and loyalties that are hard to read.\n"},{"path":"content/world/arcs.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Arcs (design §10.6): the main plot and character arcs as data.\n#  acts: name@year · clock: range · beats: clock@yNmM (the clock\n#  advances on schedule whatever the player does) · clues: what can\n#  be found · key_clues: needed for the finale, each with ≥ 2\n#  sources. Mechanics arrive in phase 2b; the structure in phase 3.\n# ============================================================\n\n@arc hollowstar\nname: The Hollow Star\ncanon: hard\nacts: omens@y1 investigation@y2 escalation@y3 convergence@y4\nact_names: Omens, Investigation, Escalation, Convergence\nclock: 0..12\nbeats: 1@y1m3 2@y1m9 4@y2m2 5@y2m9 7@y3m2 8@y3m7 10@y4m3 12@y4m9\nclues: founders_ledger lake_bell second_shadow seven_locks star_key kael_family ilse_truth knows_founders_seal codex_names\nkey_clues: seven_locks star_key ilse_truth\ncast: kael ilse isolde maud seraphine\ndesc: Something that eats light sleeps beneath the Mirror Lake, behind a door the founders sealed with seven locks. Someone wants it open.\n\n@arc lyra_and_her_mother\nname: Second Place\ncanon: hard\nacts: rivals@y1 cracks@y2 choice@y3\nact_names: Rivals, Cracks, Choice\nclock: 0..6\nclues: black_seal_letters\ncast: lyra\ndesc: Lyra Vaneshade has never once heard \"well done\". Her rivalry with you can become respect, friendship or more, and her mother will have something to say about all three.\n\n@arc kael_thaw\nname: Thaw\ncanon: hard\nacts: frost@y1 trust@y2 thaw@y3\nact_names: Frost, Trust, Thaw\nclock: 0..6\nclues: kael_family\ncast: kael\ndesc: Kael Dumont was sent to open a door and came to stop it. Whether he manages depends on whether anyone is warm enough.\n\n@arc ilse_dumont\nname: The Girl in White Furs\ncanon: hard\nacts: arrival@y2 the_man_in_grey@y3 north@y4\nact_names: Arrival, The Man in Grey, North\nclock: 0..6\nclues: ilse_truth\ncast: ilse kael\ndesc: Kael's sister was raised by the Hollow cult to do what he wouldn't. The man in grey wants her home. She might let him.\n\n@arc the_bell\nname: The Bell Under the Water\ncanon: soft\nacts: echo@y1 rope@y2\nact_names: Echo, Rope\nclock: 0..4\nbeats: 1@y1m2 2@y1m8 3@y2m3 4@y2m9\nclues: bell_heard bell_rope bell_name\ncast: maud\ndesc: The drowned bell under the Mirror Lake rings when no one is near it. Someone wants it found.\n"},{"path":"content/world/cast.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  The cast. Each NPC has a relationship record:\n#    rel  (-100..100)  how they feel about you\n#    love (0..100)     romantic interest (romance: true only)\n#    bond             none | friend | close | rival | nemesis | partner | ex\n#  Characters are introduced with `meet <id>` (or any rel/love/bond change).\n# ============================================================\n\n@npc lyra\nname: Lyra Vaneshade\ntitle: Heir of House Vaneshade, pyromancy prodigy\npronouns: she/her\nhouse: Emberhall\ncolor: #b0383c\nromance: true\nlook: hair:7 skin:5 style:1\ntags: noble ambitious fire peer\nage: 19\nrole: student\nyear: 1\ntraits: proud ambitious lonely stubborn\ninterests: duelling fire books\nstats: arcana 34 lore 22 alchemy 8 charm 20 vigor 28 guile 12 wealth 5\nschedule: duel_hall@afternoon,evening refectory@noon library@night:60\n# Breaks at the Vaneshade estate (X7): reachable only by letter.\nschedule_break: away\nrelations: kael:-10 bram:5 nessa:-5\nvoice: Clipped and formal; sarcastic when hurt. Never says \"please\" first, never admits to being tired, apologises by doing something useful.\ndesc: Brilliant, impatient and used to winning. She treats everyone as either a rival or furniture — and is quietly lonely about it.\n\n@npc tobias\nname: Tobias Fennick\ntitle: Farmer's son on a turnip scholarship\npronouns: he/him\nhouse: Rootward\ncolor: #4f8a3c\nromance: true\nlook: hair:2 skin:0 style:2\ntags: commoner kind herbs virtuous peer\nage: 19\nrole: student\nyear: 1\ntraits: kind gentle homesick loyal\ninterests: plants animals cooking dancing\nstats: arcana 12 lore 14 alchemy 26 charm 18 vigor 30 guile 4 wealth 1\nschedule: greenhouses@morning,afternoon refectory@noon,evening stables@evening:60\ngone_when: flag(tobias_went_home) and since(tobias_bad_harvest) < 2\n# Breaks on the family farm (X7): reachable only by letter.\nschedule_break: away\nrelations: maud:20 bram:-15 mir:10\nvoice: Warm, slow and earnest, with farm sayings and long stories about goats. Apologises too much; laughs from the belly.\ndesc: Big hands, bigger heart. Talks to plants and, worryingly, the plants seem to answer.\n\n@npc seraphine\nname: Seraphine Hale\ntitle: Seer who never quite looks at the present\npronouns: she/her\nhouse: Stillwater\ncolor: #6a4aa8\nromance: true\nlook: hair:4 skin:3 style:4\ntags: seer quiet mystery peer\nage: 19\nrole: student\nyear: 1\ntraits: dreamy distant perceptive kind\ninterests: prophecy painting tarot stars\nstats: arcana 20 lore 32 alchemy 10 charm 16 vigor 8 guile 14 wealth 3\nschedule: observatory@evening,night lakeshore@afternoon library@morning:50\nschedule_break: observatory@evening,night lakeshore@afternoon\nrelations: kael:10 lyra:5\nvoice: Half-sentences, finished in another week. Calm, vague, then suddenly exact about something that hasn't happened yet.\ndesc: Speaks in half-sentences, as if finishing the other half in a different week. Often right. Unsettlingly often.\n\n@npc corvin\nname: Corvin Ashby\ntitle: Charming liability\npronouns: he/him\nhouse: Duskgate\ncolor: #3a3a4a\nromance: true\nlook: hair:0 skin:1 style:3\ntags: rogue guile trouble peer\nage: 19\nrole: student\nyear: 1\ntraits: charming reckless loyal secretive\ninterests: maps locks cards money\nstats: arcana 16 lore 12 alchemy 14 charm 30 vigor 18 guile 38 wealth 1\nschedule: courtyard@noon,afternoon gargoyle_passage@night gargoyle_passage@midnight:40 gilded_newt@evening\n# Nowhere he'd go back to (X7): the Newt and the passages over breaks.\nschedule_break: gilded_newt@afternoon,evening gargoyle_passage@night gargoyle_passage@midnight:50\nrelations: bram:-30 nessa:10\nvoice: Quick, teasing, deflecting with jokes; calls you \"saint\" when you're being good. When he means something, the jokes stop and so does he.\ndesc: Knows every locked door in the academy and has opinions about all of them. Owes money to at least three people.\n\n# Renamed on 2026-10-15: this character was \"Mira\" (id `mira`, Chinese 米拉) and is now Mir (id `mir`, 米尔), they/them.\n# Every id was renamed with it (`mir_*`, `letter_mir_*`, `art_mir_collab`, `ending_beloved_mir`, `mir.*` facts).\n# Older records (tools/migrations/, past reviews) still use the old name; never reintroduce it in content.\n@npc mir\nname: Mir Ten\ntitle: Artificer of questionable inventions\npronouns: they/them\nhouse: Brasswick\ncolor: #2a8a8a\nromance: true\nlook: hair:5 skin:2 style:0\ntags: artificer chaotic peer\nage: 19\nrole: student\nyear: 1\ntraits: chaotic curious stubborn funny\ninterests: machines clocks flying\nstats: arcana 24 lore 20 alchemy 30 charm 14 vigor 10 guile 12 wealth 3\nschedule: workshop@afternoon,evening,night refectory@noon:50 lecture_tower@morning\nschedule_break: workshop@afternoon,evening,night\nrelations: tobias:10\nvoice: Fast, enthusiastic, full of technical tangents and exclamation marks. Talks to machines more gently than to people. Says \"probably\" a lot.\ndesc: Smells permanently of solder and ozone. Has built a clockwork owl, a self-stirring cauldron and at least one small fire.\n\n@npc kael\nname: Kael Dumont\ntitle: Transfer student from the frozen North\npronouns: he/him\nhouse: Stillwater\ncolor: #3a5a7a\nromance: true\nlook: hair:4 skin:0 style:0\ntags: mystery north hollowstar peer\nage: 20\nrole: student\nyear: 1\ntraits: guarded cold lonely protective\ninterests: stars snow books\nstats: arcana 36 lore 26 alchemy 8 charm 10 vigor 22 guile 18 wealth 2\nschedule: observatory@night observatory@midnight:30 library@afternoon:60 boathouse@evening\n# Can't go home (X7): the lake and the observatory all break long.\nschedule_break: lakeshore@afternoon boathouse@evening observatory@night observatory@midnight:40\nrelations: ilse:30 lyra:-10 seraphine:5\nvoice: Few words, quiet, as if each one costs something. Northern formality; literal-minded about feelings, startled by kindness.\ndesc: Arrived mid-term with no luggage and frost in his eyelashes. Doesn't talk about where he came from. Watches the sky at night.\n\n@npc nessa\nname: Nessa Quill\ntitle: Editor of the Lumen Ledger\npronouns: she/her\nhouse: Duskgate\ncolor: #a86a2a\nromance: true\nlook: hair:0 skin:4 style:3\ntags: gossip journalist virtuous peer\nage: 19\nrole: student\nyear: 1\ntraits: nosy idealistic overworked bold\ninterests: writing gossip justice\nstats: arcana 14 lore 26 alchemy 8 charm 30 vigor 12 guile 24 wealth 2\nschedule: ledger_office@afternoon,evening,night courtyard@noon gilded_newt@evening:25\nschedule_break: ledger_office@afternoon village@evening:50\nrelations: bram:-20 corvin:10 lyra:-5\nvoice: Rapid, bossy, headline-shaped. Treats every conversation as an interview; goes very quiet when it's off the record.\ndesc: Runs the student paper with a quill that writes on its own. Everyone is a story; some are just not finished yet.\n\n@npc bram\nname: Bram Hollis\ntitle: Prefect, enforcer, menace\npronouns: he/him\nhouse: Emberhall\ncolor: #7a4a20\nromance: false\nstart_rel: -10\nlook: hair:3 skin:1 style:2\ntags: prefect bully rival ruthless peer\nage: 19\nrole: prefect\nyear: 1\ntraits: bullying insecure proud loyal\ninterests: rules duelling\nstats: arcana 26 lore 18 alchemy 10 charm 14 vigor 32 guile 20 wealth 4\nschedule: courtyard@morning,noon duel_hall@evening library@afternoon:50\n# A prefect who stays on campus through every break (X7).\nschedule_break: courtyard@afternoon duel_hall@evening\nrelations: tobias:-15 corvin:-30 nessa:-20 lyra:5\nvoice: Loud, officious, rule-quoting; demerits as punctuation. Under it, his father's voice. When he's honest, he's gruff and brief.\ndesc: A prefect of your own year, with a ledger of demerits and a fondness for writing your name in it.\n\n@npc thorne\nname: Professor Aldric Thorne\nshort: Professor Thorne\ntitle: Master of Arcana\npronouns: he/him\nhouse: Faculty\ncolor: #444466\nromance: false\nlook: hair:4 skin:0 style:0\ntags: faculty mentor strict teacher\nage: 68\nrole: professor\nseeks: rarely\ntraits: severe exacting dry warm\ninterests: duelling roses teaching chess\nstats: arcana 88 lore 74 alchemy 30 charm 22 vigor 40 guile 36 wealth 4\nschedule: lecture_tower@morning,afternoon great_hall@noon library@evening:50\nschedule_break: library@afternoon,evening\nrelations: isolde:30 maud:20\nvoice: Terse, precise, allergic to flattery. \"Hm.\" is a full sentence. Praise arrives disguised as criticism, and very rarely.\ndesc: Severe, exacting, allergic to flattery. Rumoured to have once out-dueled a storm.\n\n@npc maud\nname: Old Maud\nshort: Old Maud\ntitle: Groundskeeper and hedge-witch\npronouns: she/her\nhouse: Faculty\ncolor: #6a7a3a\nromance: false\nlook: hair:4 skin:2 style:4\ntags: faculty kind herbs virtuous\nage: 81\nrole: staff\nseeks: sometimes\ntraits: kind eccentric patient warm\ninterests: plants bees soup animals history\nstats: arcana 46 lore 58 alchemy 72 charm 30 vigor 20 guile 34 wealth 2\nschedule: maud_cottage@morning,evening greenhouses@afternoon orchard@noon\nschedule_break: maud_cottage@morning,evening greenhouses@afternoon\nrelations: thorne:20 isolde:25 tobias:20\nvoice: Folksy and sharp; calls everyone \"dearie\". Gives advice you only understand two weeks later; tells sad stories over tea as if they were recipes.\ndesc: Keeps bees, chickens and a rather large secret garden. Gives advice you only understand two weeks later.\n\n@npc isolde\nname: Headmistress Isolde Marrow\nshort: Headmistress Marrow\ntitle: Headmistress of Lumenhold\npronouns: she/her\nhouse: Faculty\ncolor: #5a2a4a\nromance: false\nlook: hair:7 skin:5 style:1\ntags: faculty authority\nage: 57\nrole: headmistress\nseeks: rarely\ntraits: calm watchful formidable perceptive\ninterests: chess history justice\nstats: arcana 84 lore 90 alchemy 50 charm 60 vigor 30 guile 70 wealth 4\nschedule: headmistress_tower@morning,afternoon,evening great_hall@noon\nschedule_break: headmistress_tower@afternoon,evening\nrelations: thorne:30 maud:25\nvoice: Pleasant, unhurried, every word placed like a chess piece. Never raises her voice; never needs to. A short, delighted laugh when surprised.\ndesc: Tall, pale and impossibly calm. She remembers every student's name — and every student's mistakes.\n\n@npc ilse\nname: Ilse Dumont\ntitle: Kael's younger sister, a storm in white furs\npronouns: she/her\nhouse: none\ncolor: #8aa8c8\nromance: false\nlook: hair:4 skin:5 style:1\ntags: north hollowstar ruthless peer\nage: 18\nrole: outsider\ntraits: fierce lonely proud wary\ninterests: snow duelling\nstats: arcana 40 lore 18 alchemy 6 charm 12 vigor 26 guile 24 wealth 2\nschedule: village@afternoon,evening lakeshore@night\ngone_when: flag(ilse_taken) or flag(ilse_went_north)\nrelations: kael:20\nvoice: Sharp, curt, older than she is; Northern words when upset. Treats kindness like a trick she hasn't learned the counter to.\ndesc: Raised by the Hollow cult to open a door she has never seen. Sharp as an icicle, lonely as a frozen lake, and much younger than she pretends to be.\n\n# The pen pal at Thornwick (playtest 2, X9): never on campus but for the yearly fixture; their cards are letters.\n@npc tamsin\nname: Tamsin Reed\nshort: Tamsin\ntitle: Illusionist at Thornwick, your pen pal\npronouns: they/them\nhouse: none\ncolor: #3f7a4f\nromance: false\nlook: hair:3 skin:3 style:2\ntags: thornwick student\nage: 19\nrole: outsider\nseeks: sometimes\ntraits: funny curious ambitious loyal\ninterests: painting gossip theatre cards\nstats: arcana 26 lore 20 alchemy 10 charm 30 vigor 12 guile 28 wealth 2\nschedule_break: away\n# A pen pal (feedback 2026-10-15): reached only by letter, and never cast in other people's cards.\ndistant: true\nvoice: Writes the way they talk: fast, sly, all asides and crossings-out. Signs every letter with a new insult. Means every kind word, and hides them in postscripts.\ndesc: A Thornwick illusionist who answered a letter to \"a Lumenhold student chosen at random\" and kept writing. Insufferable, by their own account, and the best correspondent you have.\n\n# ---------------- Fixtures (design §5.5): people of a place or a track, with lighter ladders ----------------\n# They come looking for you rarely, and only once you've been coming to them (`seeks:`). No romance.\n\n@npc amaranth\nname: Sister Amaranth\nshort: Sister Amaranth\ntitle: Healer of the infirmary\npronouns: she/her\nhouse: Faculty\ncolor: #9a3b4a\nromance: false\nlook: hair:2 skin:3 style:0\ntags: faculty healer teacher virtuous\nage: 54\nrole: staff\nseeks: rarely\ntraits: severe honest patient warm\ninterests: plants soup history\nstats: arcana 40 lore 52 alchemy 80 charm 24 vigor 34 guile 30 wealth 2\nschedule: alchemy_lab@morning infirmary@afternoon,evening,night great_hall@noon\nschedule_break: infirmary@afternoon,evening,night\nrelations: maud:25 thorne:10 brask:15\nvoice: Short sentences, delivered while doing something else. Believes no excuse, forgives most things anyway. Praise comes as an instruction (\"Keep doing that.\").\ndesc: Runs the infirmary with warming charms, tonics and no patience for excuses. Teaches the healing half of Alchemy & Healing.\n\n@npc brask\nname: Professor Hedda Brask\nshort: Professor Brask\ntitle: Professor of Beastcraft & Flight\npronouns: she/her\nhouse: Faculty\ncolor: #7a5a2a\nromance: false\nlook: hair:3 skin:4 style:4\ntags: faculty teacher animals\nage: 61\nrole: professor\nseeks: rarely\ntraits: dry gentle stubborn brave\ninterests: animals flying history\nstats: arcana 52 lore 48 alchemy 30 charm 16 vigor 70 guile 28 wealth 2\nschedule: stables@morning,afternoon,evening great_hall@noon\nschedule_break: stables@morning,afternoon,evening\nrelations: amaranth:15 maud:20 thorne:5\nvoice: Speaks to creatures in full sentences and to students in half ones. Every story ends with a bite and a moral. Laughs once a term, loudly.\ndesc: Teaches Beastcraft & Flight and is kinder to animals than to students, though the gap closes for anyone who mucks out without being asked.\n\n@npc vey\nname: Professor Ottilie Vey\nshort: Professor Vey\ntitle: Professor of Illusion & Glamour\npronouns: she/her\nhouse: Faculty\ncolor: #6a4a8a\nromance: false\nlook: hair:6 skin:1 style:1\ntags: faculty teacher theatre\nage: 46\nrole: professor\nseeks: rarely\ntraits: charming perceptive sly funny\ninterests: theatre painting cards\nstats: arcana 60 lore 50 alchemy 20 charm 78 guile 70 vigor 22 wealth 3\nschedule: lecture_tower@morning,afternoon great_hall@noon\n# Breaks with the theatre season in the capital (X7).\nschedule_break: away\nrelations: thorne:5 marisol:20\nvoice: Amused, theatrical, a little too observant. Asks questions she already knows the answer to, to see what you'll do. Delighted by anyone who fools her.\ndesc: Teaches Illusion & Glamour and grades on whether you fooled her. She is very hard to fool, and very generous to the few who manage it.\n\n@npc marisol\nname: Professor Ines Marisol\nshort: Professor Marisol\ntitle: Professor of Tidecalling\npronouns: she/her\nhouse: Faculty\ncolor: #2a6a8a\nromance: false\nlook: hair:1 skin:3 style:4\ntags: faculty teacher tides\nage: 39\nrole: professor\nseeks: rarely\ntraits: dreamy warm reckless curious\ninterests: tides music writing\nstats: arcana 70 lore 56 alchemy 18 charm 40 guile 20 vigor 52 wealth 2\nschedule: lakeshore@morning,evening lecture_tower@afternoon\nschedule_break: lakeshore@morning,evening\nrelations: vey:20 thorne:15\nvoice: Soft and unhurried until there's weather; then she whoops. Quotes poems about water and forgets the last line. Believes cold water is good for everyone.\ndesc: Teaches the tidecalling half of Evocation, waist-deep in the lake at dawn. Keeps the lake calm on exam days and reads poetry under a dry patch of air when it rains.\n\n@npc mabel\nname: Mabel Thistlewood\nshort: Mabel\ntitle: Keeper of the tea shop\npronouns: she/her\nhouse: none\ncolor: #a0703a\nromance: false\nlook: hair:4 skin:2 style:4\ntags: village kind\nage: 63\nrole: villager\nseeks: rarely\ntraits: perceptive warm nosy patient\ninterests: cooking gossip music\nstats: arcana 14 lore 30 alchemy 36 charm 58 vigor 20 guile 40 wealth 3\nschedule: mabels@morning,noon,afternoon village@evening\nrelations: grimsby:20 pell:15\nvoice: Warm and brisk; finishes your order for you. Reads moods like weather and says so. Calls students \"petal\" and means something different every time.\ndesc: Forty kinds of tea, three kinds of cake, and a talent for reading your mood from the way you open her door.\n\n@npc grimsby\nname: Mr Alder Grimsby\nshort: Mr Grimsby\ntitle: Bookseller\npronouns: he/him\nhouse: none\ncolor: #5a4a2a\nromance: false\nlook: hair:4 skin:1 style:0\ntags: village books\nage: 72\nrole: villager\nseeks: rarely\ntraits: eccentric kind distant curious\ninterests: books history maps\nstats: arcana 20 lore 74 alchemy 12 charm 30 vigor 10 guile 22 wealth 2\nschedule: grimsbys_books@noon,afternoon village@evening\nrelations: mabel:20\nvoice: Vague, courteous, enthusiastic about the wrong thing. Recommends books by their smell. Remembers what you read three years ago and nothing about what you asked for today.\ndesc: Keeps a bookshop so crowded the shelves lean on each other. He will find anything except what you came in for, which is often what you needed.\n\n@npc pell\nname: Old Pell\nshort: Old Pell\ntitle: Apothecary of Pell & Daughters\npronouns: he/him\nhouse: none\ncolor: #3a5a3a\nromance: false\nlook: hair:4 skin:2 style:0\ntags: village\nage: 77\nrole: villager\nseeks: rarely\ntraits: exacting proud loyal stubborn\ninterests: plants rules money\nstats: arcana 22 lore 46 alchemy 76 charm 14 vigor 16 guile 30 wealth 3\nschedule: pell_apothecary@morning,afternoon village@evening\nrelations: mabel:15 grisk:-20\nvoice: Gruff, precise, counts aloud. Never apologises; corrects a label instead. Trusts people who measure twice, and says so once a decade.\ndesc: Taught three generations of the village to count drops. His daughters run the counter now; he runs the drawers, and the drawers are never wrong.\n\n@npc grisk\nname: Grisk\nshort: Grisk\ntitle: Moneylender, friend to the temporarily embarrassed\npronouns: he/him\nhouse: none\ncolor: #4a6a3a\nromance: false\nlook: hair:0 skin:0 style:1 body:1\ntags: village goblin money\nage: 58\nrole: villager\nseeks: sometimes\ntraits: sly charming patient guarded\ninterests: money cards gossip\nstats: arcana 18 lore 40 alchemy 20 charm 46 vigor 14 guile 82 wealth 4\nschedule: gilded_newt@evening village@afternoon\ngone_when: flag(grisk_gone)\nrelations: pell:-10\nvoice: Courtly, purring, every sentence an invoice. Calls debts \"friendships\". Never threatens; describes, pleasantly, what will happen.\ndesc: A goblin in a velvet waistcoat who lends to students: sixty crowns today, twelve a month for six. He keeps a ledger, and sometimes asks for favours instead of coin.\n"},{"path":"content/world/clues.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Clues and leads (playtest 2, X11) as the Journal shows them.\n#  kind: clue is one of its arc's clues: and counts in clues(arc);\n#  kind: lead points somewhere to look, never counts, and is\n#  struck through once its followed: holds. Only hard-canon story\n#  cards find clues; a lead may come from any story beat.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- The Hollow Star: leads ----------------\n\n@clue lead_lake_breathed\narc: hollowstar\nkind: lead\nname: The lake held its breath\ntext: In Mistveil the lake froze for one breath and the drowned bell tolled. Whoever keeps the boathouse has heard that shore for years.\nfollowed: clue(lake_bell)\n\n@clue lead_founders_archive\narc: hollowstar\nkind: lead\nname: What the founders wrote\ntext: The headmistress told the hall it was nothing. The founders' records in the library archive may disagree, and the Restricted Section keeps what the archive won't.\nfollowed: clue(founders_ledger)\n\n@clue lead_maud_friend\narc: hollowstar\nkind: lead\nname: Maud lost a friend\ntext: Old Maud was a student the last time the lake froze, and lost a friend to it. She might tell you more, if she trusts you.\nfollowed: clue(knows_founders_seal)\n\n@clue lead_man_in_grey\narc: hollowstar\nkind: lead\nname: A man in grey\ntext: A man in a grey coat told Kael to open something, or his sister would. Kael knows what. He'll tell someone he trusts.\nfollowed: clue(kael_family)\n\n@clue lead_black_star_dream\narc: hollowstar\nkind: lead\nname: A dream of a black star\ntext: You dreamt of the lake from underneath. Seraphine dreams too, and the observatory sees further than any dream.\nfollowed: clue(second_shadow)\n\n@clue lead_seventh_tower\narc: hollowstar\nkind: lead\nname: The seventh tower\ntext: Seven towers, seven locks. One tower has been locked for a century, and something is kept at the top of it.\nfollowed: clue(star_key)\n\n@clue lead_white_furs\narc: hollowstar\nkind: lead\nname: A girl in white furs\ntext: A Northern girl has taken a room above the village bakery. Kael went pale when he heard.\nfollowed: met(ilse)\n\n@clue heard_of_starcalling\narc: hollowstar\nkind: lead\nname: A scraped-out word\ntext: One word has been scraped out of the observatory's oldest logs. Students who finished Advanced Astromancy were taught it aloud.\nfollowed: enrolled(starcalling) or passed(starcalling, 1)\n\n# ---------------- The Hollow Star: clues ----------------\n\n@clue founders_ledger\narc: hollowstar\nname: The razored page\ntext: The founding year was cut out of the Founders' Ledger with a razor, but the pen's impression survived on the page beneath.\n\n@clue knows_founders_seal\narc: hollowstar\nname: The founders' seal\ntext: Lumenhold was built as a seal: seven towers, seven locks, and a key shaped like a star. Something sleeps beneath the water.\n\n@clue lake_bell\narc: hollowstar\nname: The bell under the water\ntext: A drowned bell lies under the lake and tolls when the thing below stirs. A tunnel runs down from the boathouse toward it.\n\n@clue second_shadow\narc: hollowstar\nname: A second shadow\ntext: Something small and dark crossed the sky the wrong way and stopped directly above the lake.\n\n@clue seven_locks\narc: hollowstar\nname: Seven locks\ntext: One lock in each tower; when a tower goes dark, so does its lock. Locks can be mended. They need hands, and a key.\n\n@clue star_key\narc: hollowstar\nname: The star key\ntext: A key shaped like a star that holds no light, kept at the top of the seventh tower. The seal can't be renewed without it, or without the founders' words.\n\n@clue kael_family\narc: hollowstar\nname: The Dumonts\ntext: Kael's family serve the thing under the lake. They sent him to open its door. He came to stop them.\n\n@clue ilse_truth\narc: hollowstar\nname: What Ilse was raised for\ntext: The Dumonts raised Kael's sister to open the door if he wouldn't. Nobody ever asked her if she wanted to.\n\n@clue codex_names\narc: hollowstar\nname: The Codex of Hollow Names\ntext: A book in the Restricted Section lists everyone the Star has noticed. The last name is still being written, and it is yours.\n\n# ---------------- The Bell Under the Water ----------------\n\n@clue bell_heard\narc: the_bell\nname: A bell where none is\ntext: The drowned bell rang in the night with no one near it, and nobody else woke.\n\n@clue bell_rope\narc: the_bell\nname: The cut rope\ntext: Maud has kept the bell's rope for sixty years. It came up the night the lake froze, cut clean.\n\n@clue bell_name\narc: the_bell\nname: The name on the rim\ntext: Letters run around the bell's rim on the lakebed: a name you don't know, and now keep.\n\n# ---------------- Second Place ----------------\n\n@clue black_seal_letters\narc: lyra_and_her_mother\nname: Letters under a black seal\ntext: House Vaneshade writes to Lyra under its black seal. Her mother asks who beat her, never how she is.\n\n# ---------------- Side threads (feedback 2026-10-15): hinted now, to be followed up later ----------------\n# A side thread's lead names no arc; the Clues panel groups it under its thread:. They never touch the mystery.\n\n@clue lead_moonpetal\nthread: The cold that won't leave\nkind: lead\nname: Moonpetal\ntext: Old Maud says the cure is a moonpetal from the Whisperwood, which blooms only after dark, and that you have to fetch it yourself.\nfollowed: not has(umbral_curse)\n\n@clue lead_second_timetable\nthread: The unsigned note\nkind: lead\nname: A second timetable\ntext: Someone slipped a note under your door: the castle has a second timetable, and it starts at midnight. Whoever wrote it knows the passages.\nfollowed: seen(corvin_note_was_mine) > 0\n\n@clue lead_grisk_jetty\nthread: Grisk's parcels\nkind: lead\nname: The jetty at midnight\ntext: Grisk's arrangement: a parcel collected at the boathouse jetty at midnight, carried to a cobbler's back door in the village by day.\nfollowed: seen(grisk_pickup) > 0 or flag(grisk_gone)\n\n@clue lead_sky_championship\nthread: The Skyrace team\nkind: lead\nname: The championship\ntext: You made the team. The championship against Thornwick is flown at the flight field later in the year.\nfollowed: seen(sky_championship) > 0\n"},{"path":"content/world/factions.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Factions (design §3.2): who wants what. alignment: lean on the\n#  Lumen (+) ↔ Hollow (−) axis · stance: toward the player at the\n#  start of a run.\n# ============================================================\n\n@faction wardens\nname: The Wardens\nmembers: isolde thorne maud\nalignment: 60\nstance: neutral\ndesc: The headmistress's quiet circle, sworn to keep the founders' seal. They recruit students only when the seal grows thin.\n\n@faction hollow_cult\nname: The Hollow Cult\nmembers: ilse\nalignment: -80\nstance: hostile\ndesc: Northern families, the Dumonts first among them, who serve the star beneath the lake. Their messenger is the man in grey.\n\n@faction midnight_cartographers\nname: The Midnight Cartographers\nmembers: corvin\nalignment: 0\nstance: neutral\ndesc: The club that doesn't exist: students who map the castle's secret passages and share them with almost no one.\n\n@faction faculty\nname: The Faculty\nmembers: isolde thorne maud\nalignment: 30\nstance: neutral\ndesc: The professors and staff of Lumenhold: strict, eccentric, and mostly on your side whether you like it or not.\n\n@faction conclave\nname: The Conclave of the Seven Towers\nalignment: 20\nstance: neutral\ndesc: The governing body of magic in the realm, headquartered in the capital. Hands out research posts, licences and very long letters.\n\n@faction thornwick\nname: Thornwick Academy\nalignment: 0\nstance: hostile\ndesc: The rival school across the hills. Famous for illusionists, tournament grudges and spies who think they're subtle.\n"},{"path":"content/world/facts/amaranth.grim","text":"# Facts about Sister Amaranth.\n\n@fact amaranth.healer\ntier: surface\ntext: Runs the infirmary and teaches the healing half of Alchemy & Healing. Believes no excuse.\n\n@fact amaranth.truth\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(amaranth_intro) > 0\ntext: She treats the true version of an injury faster than the story you tell her, and she always knows which one you told.\n\n@fact amaranth.rounds\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(amaranth_rounds) > 0\ntext: She does evening rounds of every bed herself, and has never once missed one.\n\n@fact amaranth.key\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(amaranth_trust) > 0\ntext: Only a handful of students have ever been trusted with the key to her tonic cupboard.\n"},{"path":"content/world/facts/bram.grim","text":"# Facts about Bram Hollis.\n\n@fact bram.prefect\ntier: surface\ntext: A prefect of your own year, with a red sash, a ledger of demerits and a fondness for your name.\n\n@fact bram.father\ntier: secret\nunlock: seen(bram_crying) > 0 or seen(bram_father_visit) > 0\ntext: His father was head boy, and has told Bram all his life that he will never amount to anything.\n\n@fact bram.hall_tax\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(bram_hall_tax) > 0\ntext: He once invented a \"hall tax\" for the east staircase.\n\n@fact bram.cheating\ntier: secret\nunlock: seen(bram_cheating) > 0\ntext: You saw him cheat in an exam. He knows you saw.\n\n@fact bram.ally\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(bram_turns) > 0\ntext: Under the sash, he will step between you and trouble. Then he tells you not to make it weird.\n"},{"path":"content/world/facts/brask.grim","text":"# Facts about Professor Brask.\n\n@fact brask.beasts\ntier: surface\ntext: Teaches Beastcraft & Flight from the stables, and talks to every creature in them more kindly than to students.\n\n@fact brask.stories\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(brask_shovel) > 0\ntext: Every one of her stories ends with a bite and a moral. She laughs out loud about once a term.\n\n@fact brask.owl\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(brask_night_owl) > 0\ntext: The old barn owl in the stable loft has outlived three of her colleagues. She would not say which loss hurt most.\n\n@fact brask.courtesies\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(brask_trust) > 0\ntext: She bows to the things that live in the Whisperwood, and believes it's why they have never bitten her. Much.\n"},{"path":"content/world/facts/corvin.grim","text":"# Facts about Corvin Ashby.\n\n@fact corvin.locks\ntier: surface\ntext: Knows every locked door in the academy and owes money to at least three people.\n\n@fact corvin.last_warning\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(corvin_rooftop) > 0\ntext: He is on his last warning. One more detention and they send him home, and home is not a good place.\n\n@fact corvin.brother\ntier: secret\nunlock: seen(corvin_brother) > 0\ntext: His brother turns up for money and threatens to tell the headmistress where Corvin learned to pick locks.\n\n@fact corvin.grandfather\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(corvin_confiscation_vault) > 0\ntext: His grandfather's lucky deck sat in the confiscation vault for decades. It really is lucky.\n\n@fact corvin.cartographers\ntier: secret\nunlock: member(cartographers)\ntext: He runs the Midnight Cartographers, the club that doesn't exist.\n\n@fact corvin.trolls\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(corvin_debt) > 0\ntext: He once owed thirty crowns to trolls and offered to pay you back with a goat.\n"},{"path":"content/world/facts/grimsby.grim","text":"# Facts about Mr Grimsby.\n\n@fact grimsby.books\ntier: surface\ntext: Keeps the village bookshop. He will find anything except what you came in for.\n\n@fact grimsby.wrong\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(grimsby_intro) > 0\ntext: The book he gives you instead is, more often than not, the one you needed.\n\n@fact grimsby.parcels\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(grimsby_parcel) > 0\ntext: He posts books to students he thinks need them, on credit, and has never once chased a debt.\n\n@fact grimsby.lighthouses\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(grimsby_mind_the_shop) > 0\ntext: He has a weakness for lighthouses, and when he closes the shop, it is usually to go and look at one.\n"},{"path":"content/world/facts/grisk.grim","text":"# Facts about Grisk.\n\n@fact grisk.lender\ntier: surface\ntext: A goblin moneylender in a velvet waistcoat: sixty crowns today, twelve a month for six months.\n\n@fact grisk.ledger\ntier: personal\nunlock: met(grisk) and seen(grisk_pleasantries) > 0\ntext: He calls debts \"friendships\", and keeps all of them in one ledger he never lets out of reach.\n\n@fact grisk.favours\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(grisk_favour) > 0\ntext: He sometimes asks for favours instead of coin, and the favours are for clients he never names.\n"},{"path":"content/world/facts/ilse.grim","text":"# Facts about Ilse Dumont.\n\n@fact ilse.furs\ntier: surface\ntext: Kael's younger sister: white furs, a private snowstorm and coins that freeze to counters.\n\n@fact ilse.raised\ntier: personal\nunlock: rel(ilse) >= 12\ntext: The Hollow cult raised her to open a door she has never seen.\n\n@fact ilse.frost_craft\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(ilse_frost_lessons) > 0\ntext: She knows Northern frost-craft better than anything the professors teach.\n\n@fact ilse.grey\ntier: secret\nunlock: seen(ilse_grey_returns) > 0\ntext: The man in grey came to take her home. She didn't call for help; she only looked.\n\n@fact ilse.scared\ntier: secret\nunlock: seen(ilse_kael_reunion) > 0\ntext: She admits, to her brother, that she was scared. So was he.\n"},{"path":"content/world/facts/isolde.grim","text":"# Facts about Headmistress Isolde Marrow.\n\n@fact isolde.names\ntier: surface\ntext: She remembers every student's name, and every student's mistakes.\n\n@fact isolde.portrait\ntier: secret\nunlock: seen(isolde_portrait) > 0\ntext: As a student she set the observatory on fire twice. Her portrait from those days is hidden in a forgotten corridor.\n\n@fact isolde.chess\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(isolde_chess) > 0\ntext: She plays tower chess by her window and laughs, short and delighted, when she loses.\n\n@fact isolde.thread\ntier: secret\nunlock: flag(warden_trial) or seen(hs_isolde_summons) > 0\ntext: She has been following the same thread you have: the seal beneath the lake is weakening.\n\n@fact isolde.mercy\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(isolde_judgement) > 0\ntext: She asks students what they think is just, and sometimes she listens.\n"},{"path":"content/world/facts/kael.grim","text":"# Facts about Kael Dumont.\n\n@fact kael.north\ntier: surface\ntext: A transfer from the frozen North who arrived mid-term with frost in his eyelashes.\n\n@fact kael.cold_hands\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(kael_cold_hands) > 0\ntext: His hands are always cold. Nobody has ever been warm enough before.\n\n@fact kael.sky\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(astro_kael_roof) > 0\ntext: He watches a patch of sky with no stars in it: a star that eats light.\n\n@fact kael.family\ntier: secret\nunlock: flag(kael_told)\ntext: His family serve something under the lake. They sent him to open its door; he came to stop them.\n\n@fact kael.sister\ntier: secret\nunlock: flag(met_sister) or seen(ilse_village) > 0\ntext: He has a younger sister, Ilse, raised to do what he wouldn't.\n\n@fact kael.mother\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(kael_northern_lights) > 0\ntext: His mother never once said she was proud of him.\n\n@fact kael.wanting\ntier: secret\nunlock: seen(kael_confession) > 0\ntext: He was taught not to want things, because wanting is how they control you.\n"},{"path":"content/world/facts/lyra.grim","text":"# Facts about Lyra Vaneshade (design §10.1). Tiers: surface (known on meeting),\n# personal (closeness), secret (specific events). Shown in the People panel in phase 4.\n\n@fact lyra.prodigy\ntier: surface\ntext: The pyromancy prodigy of House Vaneshade. Everyone knows the name, and she makes sure of it.\n\n@fact lyra.toast\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(lyra_toast) > 0\ntext: She can incinerate a target at forty paces. She cannot make toast.\n\n@fact lyra.mother\ntier: personal\nunlock: rel(lyra) >= 18\ntext: Her mother has never once told her \"well done\".\n\n@fact lyra.second_place\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(lyra_letter) > 0\ntext: She came second in the Winter Trials once. Her mother wrote to ask who beat her.\n\n@fact lyra.burnout\ntier: secret\nunlock: seen(lyra_burnout) > 0\ntext: She studies until she collapses, and begs you not to let her mother find out.\n\n@fact lyra.rival\ntier: personal\nunlock: bond(lyra) == \"rival\"\ntext: You are the only person here who makes her better. She hates it. She doesn't want it to stop.\n\n@fact lyra.inheritance\ntier: secret\nunlock: flag(lyra_free)\ntext: Her mother threatened her inheritance over you. She set the letter on fire.\n"},{"path":"content/world/facts/mabel.grim","text":"# Facts about Mabel.\n\n@fact mabel.teashop\ntier: surface\ntext: Keeps the tea shop in the village: forty kinds of tea, three kinds of cake.\n\n@fact mabel.door\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(mabel_intro) > 0\ntext: She reads your mood from the way you open her door, and is rarely wrong.\n\n@fact mabel.tins\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(mabel_parcel) > 0\ntext: She sends tins of tea up to the castle on the barge for students she's worried about, and refuses payment for them.\n\n@fact mabel.cups\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(mabel_cup) > 0\ntext: The cups on the hooks behind her counter belong to her regulars, living and dead. She remembers every one of them.\n"},{"path":"content/world/facts/marisol.grim","text":"# Facts about Professor Marisol.\n\n@fact marisol.tides\ntier: surface\ntext: Teaches the tidecalling half of Evocation, waist-deep in the lake at dawn, and keeps the lake calm on exam days.\n\n@fact marisol.poems\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(marisol_intro) > 0\ntext: She reads poems about water in the rain, under a dry patch of air, and never remembers their last lines.\n\n@fact marisol.storms\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(marisol_storm) > 0\ntext: She goes out into the worst storms of the year on purpose. She says nobody learns tides on a calm day.\n\n@fact marisol.margins\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(marisol_trust) > 0\ntext: She wrote her own tidecalling into the margins of a book of poems when she was a student, and still teaches from it.\n"},{"path":"content/world/facts/maud.grim","text":"# Facts about Old Maud.\n\n@fact maud.groundskeeper\ntier: surface\ntext: Groundskeeper and hedge-witch, keeper of bees, chickens and a goat called Bartholomew.\n\n@fact maud.student\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(maud_old_student) > 0\ntext: She was a student here sixty years ago, the spring the lake froze.\n\n@fact maud.edmund\ntier: secret\nunlock: seen(maud_old_wand) > 0\ntext: Edmund went down beneath the lake that night and didn't come back. She kept his silver wand.\n\n@fact maud.soup\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(maud_recipe) > 0\ntext: Her soup cures colds, broken hearts and most curses. You're not allowed to write it down.\n\n@fact maud.garden\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(garden_maud_secret) > 0\ntext: Behind the potting shed there is a door to a garden that only exists for people she shows it to.\n\n@fact maud.age\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(maud_ill) > 0\ntext: She is eighty-one, and says she's allowed the occasional chill.\n"},{"path":"content/world/facts/mir.grim","text":"# Facts about Mir Ten (they/them).\n# Formerly \"Mira\" (id `mira`); renamed 2026-10-15. See the note on `@npc mir` in cast.grim.\n\n@fact mir.artificer\ntier: surface\ntext: An artificer who smells of solder and ozone and has started at least one small fire.\n\n@fact mir.owl\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(mir_owl_gift) > 0\ntext: Built a clockwork owl that remembers deadlines, birthdays and, slightly, grudges.\n\n@fact mir.notebooks\ntier: personal\nunlock: rel(mir) >= 12\ntext: Three years of notebooks are the most precious things they own.\n\n@fact mir.family\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(mir_family_business) > 0\ntext: Their family runs a clockworks and thinks invention is a hobby. It is not a hobby.\n\n@fact mir.name_day\ntier: secret\nunlock: seen(mir_name_day) > 0\ntext: Nobody ever remembered their name day before.\n\n@fact mir.workshop\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(mir_workshop_closed) > 0\ntext: After the third fire in a term, the faculty padlocked their workshop.\n"},{"path":"content/world/facts/nessa.grim","text":"# Facts about Nessa Quill.\n\n@fact nessa.editor\ntier: surface\ntext: Editor of the Lumen Ledger, with a quill that writes on its own.\n\n@fact nessa.quill\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(nessa_burnout) > 0\ntext: Left unsupervised, her quill starts inventing the news.\n\n@fact nessa.interview\ntier: personal\nunlock: rel(nessa) >= 12\ntext: Nobody has interviewed the headmistress in twenty years. She means to be the first.\n\n@fact nessa.string\ntier: secret\nunlock: seen(nessa_hollow_story) > 0\ntext: Her office wall is covered in red string around a sketch of a black star.\n\n@fact nessa.press\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(nessa_paper_threatened) > 0\ntext: She would chain herself to the printing press before letting anyone shut the Ledger.\n\n@fact nessa.off_record\ntier: secret\nunlock: seen(nessa_confession) > 0\ntext: The only time her quill stops is when she tells it the conversation is off the record.\n"},{"path":"content/world/facts/pell.grim","text":"# Facts about Old Pell.\n\n@fact pell.apothecary\ntier: surface\ntext: Runs the drawers at Pell & Daughters. He taught three generations of the village to count drops.\n\n@fact pell.counting\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(pell_count_drops) > 0\ntext: He makes every new clerk count aloud. He trusts people who measure twice.\n\n@fact pell.daughters\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(pell_daughters) > 0\ntext: His daughters run the counter and want the shop to change; he wants it to be fifty years ago. They're all secretly proud of each other.\n"},{"path":"content/world/facts/seraphine.grim","text":"# Facts about Seraphine Hale.\n\n@fact seraphine.seer\ntier: surface\ntext: A seer who speaks in half-sentences and is unsettlingly often right.\n\n@fact seraphine.slips\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(seraphine_lost_day) > 0\ntext: Sometimes she slips a day ahead and needs someone to tell her what today is.\n\n@fact seraphine.oracle\ntier: personal\nunlock: rel(seraphine) >= 15\ntext: Her mother is the Oracle of Vell, who wants her home to tell kings what they want to hear.\n\n@fact seraphine.self\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(seraphine_painting) > 0\ntext: Seers can't see themselves, unless someone is standing next to them.\n\n@fact seraphine.lake\ntier: secret\nunlock: flag(seraphine_lake_told)\ntext: The lake calls to her at night. She says it calls to you too.\n\n@fact seraphine.too_loud\ntier: secret\nunlock: seen(seraphine_overwhelmed) > 0\ntext: When everyone's futures get too loud she hides in the dark. Yours, she says, is quiet.\n"},{"path":"content/world/facts/tamsin.grim","text":"# Facts about Tamsin Reed (playtest 2, X9).\n\n@fact tamsin.thornwick\ntier: surface\ntext: An illusion student at Thornwick, in your year, who writes to you.\n\n@fact tamsin.theatre\ntier: personal\nunlock: rel(tamsin) >= 8\ntext: Their grandmother runs a travelling theatre; they learned illusion painting her backdrops.\n\n@fact tamsin.assigned\ntier: secret\nunlock: seen(tamsin_confession) > 0\ntext: Thornwick told them to write to a Lumenhold student and report back. They stopped reporting by the third letter.\n"},{"path":"content/world/facts/thorne.grim","text":"# Facts about Professor Aldric Thorne.\n\n@fact thorne.storm\ntier: surface\ntext: Rumoured to have once out-duelled a storm.\n\n@fact thorne.lost\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(thorne_storm_story) > 0\ntext: He lost to the storm, badly. Losing well, he says, is the only thing worth teaching.\n\n@fact thorne.roses\ntier: secret\nunlock: seen(thorne_roses) > 0\ntext: He tends a tiny walled rose garden behind the observatory at dawn and talks to the roses.\n\n@fact thorne.hands\ntier: secret\nunlock: seen(thorne_shaking_hands) > 0\ntext: His hands have started to shake. Age comes for every storm eventually.\n\n@fact thorne.rival\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(thorne_old_rival) > 0\ntext: A flamboyant old wizard in purple has wanted a rematch with him for forty years.\n\n@fact thorne.letters\ntier: personal\nunlock: rel(thorne) >= 18\ntext: He writes very few letters of recommendation, and the Conclave reads every one.\n"},{"path":"content/world/facts/tobias.grim","text":"# Facts about Tobias Fennick.\n\n@fact tobias.scholarship\ntier: surface\ntext: A farmer's son on a turnip scholarship, built like a barn door and gentle as a lamb.\n\n@fact tobias.plants\ntier: surface\ntext: He talks to plants. Worryingly, they seem to answer.\n\n@fact tobias.ma\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(tobias_homesick) > 0\ntext: His mother runs the farm alone at harvest. It's the first harvest he has ever missed.\n\n@fact tobias.gerald\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(tobias_godparent) > 0\ntext: He is raising a mandrake called Gerald, who wears a knitted hat and screams softly.\n\n@fact tobias.moonflowers\ntier: personal\nunlock: rel(tobias) >= 12\ntext: The greenhouse moonflowers bloom once every eleven years. He would stand in a storm for them.\n\n@fact tobias.blight\ntier: secret\nunlock: seen(tobias_bad_harvest) > 0\ntext: Blight took his family's harvest. Thirty crowns stood between them and a hungry winter.\n\n@fact tobias.dancing\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(tobias_dance_lessons) > 0\ntext: He practised waltzing with a broom before the Midwinter Ball. The broom complained.\n"},{"path":"content/world/facts/vey.grim","text":"# Facts about Professor Vey.\n\n@fact vey.illusion\ntier: surface\ntext: Teaches Illusion & Glamour and grades on whether you fooled her.\n\n@fact vey.tests\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(vey_pop_quiz) > 0\ntext: She tests students in corridors, unannounced, with tea that isn't there. She keeps score.\n\n@fact vey.revue\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(vey_revue) > 0\ntext: She produces the faculty revue every year, and has never once let Professor Thorne refuse a part.\n\n@fact vey.mirror\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(vey_trust) > 0\ntext: She doesn't use the mirror charm in public. Once, her reflection didn't come back for a week.\n"},{"path":"content/world/lore/soft/academy.grim","text":"# Lore drafts (canon: soft): the academy and its customs.\n\n@lore lumenhold\nkeys: Lumenhold, academy, seven towers, bridges of light\ncanon: soft\ntext: Lumenhold Academy is seven towers joined by bridges of light, standing above a mirror-still lake. It has trained mages for three hundred years and has been a seal for exactly as long.\n\n@lore mirror_lake\nkeys: Mirror Lake, lake\ncanon: soft\ntext: The lake is so still it shows the towers upside down. Students swim in it in summer and catch Lake Fever when they shouldn't. Some nights it calls to seers.\n\n@lore houses\nkeys: houses, Emberhall, Rootward, Stillwater, Duskgate, Brasswick\ncanon: soft\ntext: Students belong to five houses: Emberhall (fire and ambition), Rootward (growing things), Stillwater (quiet and the far-sighted), Duskgate (shadows and secrets) and Brasswick (makers and tinkerers). Faculty belong to none.\n\n@lore crowns\nkeys: crowns, money, currency\ncanon: soft\ntext: The currency is the crown. Rent and board at the academy cost about ten crowns a month; a good meal in the village, one; a registered familiar, twenty.\n\n@lore calendar\nkeys: Harvestmoon, Emberwane, Mistveil, Frostcall, Deepwinter, Thawmoon, Stormbloom, Greenrise, Blossomtide, Highsun, Goldenhaze, Starfall\ncanon: soft\ntext: The academic year starts in autumn with Harvestmoon, then Emberwane, Mistveil, Frostcall, Deepwinter, Thawmoon, Stormbloom, Greenrise, Blossomtide, Highsun, Goldenhaze and Starfall.\n\n@lore festivals\nkeys: Harvest Feast, Lantern Night, Midwinter Ball, Spring Tournament, Starfall festival\ncanon: soft\ntext: The Harvest Feast opens the year; on Lantern Night lanterns carry wishes over the lake; the Midwinter Ball fills the Great Hall; the Spring Tournament crowns a duelling champion; and at Starfall the whole academy sleeps on the lawns to watch the meteors.\n\n@lore exams\nkeys: Winter Trials, Grand Examinations, exams\ncanon: soft\ntext: The Winter Trials test the term's work in Frostcall. The Grand Examinations close the year: three days, five examiners and a practical before a panel that includes the headmistress.\n\n@lore restricted_section\nkeys: Restricted Section, silver key\ncanon: soft\ntext: A locked section at the back of the library, opened by a silver key, where black-bound books breathe softly. Bartholomew the goat once ate a third of \"Curses of the Northern Wastes\" there.\n\n@lore confiscation_vault\nkeys: confiscation vault, contraband\ncanon: soft\ntext: Three hundred years of everything the faculty ever took off a student. Its alarm is a very loud goblin.\n\n@lore gargoyles\nkeys: gargoyle, third gargoyle, passages\ncanon: soft\ntext: The castle is riddled with passages that appear on no official plan. The way into the Midnight Cartographers' world is behind the third gargoyle on the north parapet.\n\n@lore moving_stairs\nkeys: staircases, moving stairs, east wing\ncanon: soft\ntext: Lumenhold's staircases move when they feel like it and occasionally eat shoes, which turn up under the stairs in the east wing.\n\n@lore demerits\nkeys: demerits, prefects, detention, sash\ncanon: soft\ntext: Prefects wear red sashes and keep ledgers of demerits. Detention is usually scrubbing pans or cataloguing contraband. Being stripped of a sash is the worst thing that can happen to a prefect short of expulsion.\n\n@lore familiars\nkeys: familiars, registration, Ember\ncanon: soft\ntext: Students may keep a familiar if it is registered: twenty crowns and a stamp from the registrar. Unregistered familiars are tolerated if nobody notices them, and some don't want to be noticed.\n\n@lore lumen_ledger\nkeys: Lumen Ledger, newspaper, press\ncanon: soft\ntext: The student newspaper, printed at dawn on a press that groans like a dragon. Its gossip column is feared; its editor, Nessa Quill, has a quill that writes on its own.\n\n@lore infirmary\nkeys: infirmary, Sister Amaranth, Lake Fever\ncanon: soft\ntext: Sister Amaranth runs the infirmary with warming charms, tonics and no patience for excuses. Lake Fever comes from swimming where you shouldn't; curse-cold comes from worse.\n\n@lore graduation\nkeys: graduation, speech, yearbook\ncanon: soft\ntext: Each year one graduate speaks for their class in the Great Hall, chosen by the faculty. Graduates plant something, keep something, or leave something behind.\n\n@lore fairs\nkeys: Club Fair, Job Board, Artificers' Fair, Midsummer Fair\ncanon: soft\ntext: In Emberwane the Club Fair fills the courtyard with recruiting tables; the Job Board in the entrance hall posts term-time work. The Artificers' Fair shows off students' machines, and in summer the village holds its Midsummer Fair with an enchanted carousel.\n\n@lore disciplinary_board\nkeys: Disciplinary Board, probation, expulsion\ncanon: soft\ntext: When a student's standing collapses, the Disciplinary Board meets: three professors, a long table and very little mercy. Probation comes first; expulsion second.\n\n@lore lumen_essay_prize\nkeys: Lumen Essay Prize, essay prize\ncanon: soft\ntext: An annual prize of thirty crowns for the best student essay on a set theme, judged by the faculty. Past themes include \"Magic and Duty\".\n\n@lore sorting_mirror\nkeys: Sorting Mirror, entrance hall\ncanon: soft\ntext: A tall enchanted mirror in the entrance hall. It does not sort anyone into anything; it asks, in a voice like cold water, who you were before you came.\n\n@lore spire_library\nkeys: Spire Library, the Spire\ncanon: soft\ntext: The academy's research library at the top of the tallest tower, where theses of distinction are shelved in a section students whisper about.\n"},{"path":"content/world/lore/soft/hollowstar.grim","text":"# Lore drafts (canon: soft), distilled from the existing events in phase 1.\n# Curate: review, correct, then move entries to lore/hard/ with canon: hard (design §3.6).\n# The Hollow Star and the founders' seal.\n\n@lore hollow_star\nkeys: Hollow Star, star, light-eating star, the thing under the lake\ncanon: soft\ntext: A star that is not a light but an absence: it eats light. It sleeps beneath the Mirror Lake, behind a round door. It is not asleep so much as waiting, and it learns the names of those who look for it.\n\n@lore founders_seal\nkeys: founders, seal, seven locks, seven towers\ncanon: soft\ntext: The founders built Lumenhold as a seal over the Hollow Star: seven towers, one lock in each, and a key shaped like a star that holds no light. The seal weakens over generations and must be renewed.\n\n@lore star_key\nkeys: star key, key, seventh tower\ncanon: soft\ntext: The key to the founders' seal is shaped like a star and black as a hole in the world. It was kept in a star-shaped hollow at the top of the seventh tower, behind wards that shriek.\n\n@lore seventh_tower\nkeys: seventh tower, locked stair\ncanon: soft\ntext: One of the seven towers has been locked for a century. At its top is a single room of dust and moonlight. When the star key is lifted, every candle in the academy goes out at once.\n\n@lore round_door\nkeys: door, round door, beneath the lake, tunnel\ncanon: soft\ntext: A tunnel from the boathouse runs down to a round door at the bottom of the lake. On the night the seal weakens, it stands open a crack, and the Star looks through.\n\n@lore drowned_bell\nkeys: drowned bell, bell, lake bell\ncanon: soft\ntext: A bell lies under the Mirror Lake. It tolls when the Star stirs, and once on the night the lake freezes. Edmund's silver wand hums the same note.\n\n@lore lake_freezes\nkeys: frozen lake, the Night, Blossom moon, freeze\ncanon: soft\ntext: When the seal is at its weakest, on the night of the Blossom moon, the lake freezes in a single breath in the middle of spring. Cracks glow beneath the ice and things made of frost and whispers come ashore.\n\n@lore founders_ledger\nkeys: Founders' Ledger, ledger, archive vault\ncanon: soft\ntext: The Founders' Ledger in the archive vault is three hundred years old. The entry for the founding year was cut out with a razor, but the impression of the pen remains on the page beneath. A client of Grisk's once paid to have another page removed.\n\n@lore codex_hollow_names\nkeys: Codex of Hollow Names, codex, Restricted Section\ncanon: soft\ntext: A book in the Restricted Section that finds its readers. Its first page is a list of names; the last is still being written. Reading it turns the fingertips grey.\n\n@lore star_mark\nkeys: star mark, mark, cold coin\ncanon: soft\ntext: Relics of the Star burn so cold they feel hot and can leave a pale star-shaped mark on the palm. The mark hums near the lake and makes spells come easier, which should worry its bearer more than it does.\n\n@lore hollow_dreams\nkeys: dreams, black star dream\ncanon: soft\ntext: Those the Star notices dream of the lake from underneath and wake with frost on their pillows. Lavender doesn't stop the dreams, but it makes them smell nicer.\n\n@lore man_in_grey\nkeys: man in grey, grey coat\ncanon: soft\ntext: A tall, polite man in a grey coat who serves the Star on behalf of the Northern families. Fountains freeze when he talks. He offers power to those who ask questions, and he has come for both Dumont children.\n\n@lore hollow_cult\nkeys: Hollow cult, cult, Northern families\ncanon: soft\ntext: Northern families who serve the Star beneath the lake and send their children south to open its door. Their letters carry a black-star seal.\n\n@lore dumont_family\nkeys: Dumont, Dumonts, Kael, Ilse\ncanon: soft\ntext: The Dumonts are the foremost of the Hollow families. They sent Kael to open the door; when he refused, they raised his younger sister Ilse to do it instead. Their mother has never told either child she was proud.\n\n@lore wardens\nkeys: Wardens, ward-ring, headmistress\ncanon: soft\ntext: The headmistress keeps a quiet circle sworn to the founders' seal. When the seal grows thin she recruits students she trusts and gives them silver ward-rings.\n\n@lore edmund\nkeys: Edmund, silver wand, frozen spring\ncanon: soft\ntext: Sixty years ago, the spring the lake froze, Maud and two friends went down to the boathouse. Edmund was the one who didn't come back. His slender silver wand was meant to go beneath the lake again, with someone who means to close the door.\n\n@lore starcalling\nkeys: Starcalling, named star, starlight\ncanon: soft\ntext: An old discipline of calling down the light of a named star. Light that the Hollow Star cannot swallow is one of the few weapons the founders left.\n\n@lore lumen_and_hollow\nkeys: Lumen, Hollow, light and dark, alignment\ncanon: soft\ntext: Lumenhold talks about people the way it talks about candles: some give light (Lumen), some swallow it (the Hollow). It is not a house or a club, only what people notice. Those who lean toward the Hollow find the Star noticing them back; the way out of it is slow, and usually starts with someone handing you something to do.\n\n@lore star_herald\nkeys: herald, Herald of the Hollow Star, freed\ncanon: soft\ntext: If the round door is opened by one the Star already counts as its own, it goes free instead of swallowing them, and remembers who let it out. The Northern families call such a person its herald.\n"},{"path":"content/world/lore/soft/people.grim","text":"# Lore drafts (canon: soft): minor figures and families.\n\n@lore vaneshade\nkeys: Vaneshade, Lady Vaneshade, black seal, winter gala\ncanon: soft\ntext: House Vaneshade is old pyromancer nobility. Its letters carry a black seal; its winter gala is an inspection disguised as a party. Lady Vaneshade's highest praise is \"Adequate.\"\n\n@lore oracle_of_vell\nkeys: Oracle of Vell, Vell, family seat\ncanon: soft\ntext: The Oracle of Vell, Seraphine's mother, tells kings what they want to hear and has already seen her daughter say yes to taking the family seat.\n\n@lore fennick_farm\nkeys: Fennick, farm, harvest, blight\ncanon: soft\ntext: The Fennick farm grows turnips, tomatoes and stubborn children. Tobias's mother runs it alone at harvest; one year blight nearly ruined them.\n\n@lore ten_clockworks\nkeys: Ten family, clockworks\ncanon: soft\ntext: Mir's family runs a clockworks and expects them home. After a persuasive letter, Mir's mother agreed: \"Fine. Win a prize.\"\n\n@lore ashby_family\nkeys: Ashby, Corvin's brother, Corvin's grandfather\ncanon: soft\ntext: Corvin's grandfather owned a genuinely lucky deck of cards; his brother turns up at the gates wanting money. Home, Corvin says, is not a good place.\n\n@lore hollis_family\nkeys: Hollis, Bram's father, head boy\ncanon: soft\ntext: Bram's father was a prefect and head boy at Lumenhold, and has never stopped telling his son he will not amount to anything.\n\n@lore grisk\nkeys: Grisk, goblin, moneylender, debt\ncanon: soft\ntext: A goblin in a velvet waistcoat, friend to the temporarily embarrassed: sixty crowns today, twelve a month for six months. He keeps a ledger and occasionally asks for favours instead of coin.\n\n@lore prudence_ashworth\nkeys: Prudence Ashworth, ghost, socks\ncanon: soft\ntext: A polite ghost from two hundred years ago who rearranges socks to get attention. She was in love with the baker's daughter and never delivered her letter.\n\n@lore footnote\nkeys: Footnote, library cat\ncanon: soft\ntext: The library cat, who sleeps on the reference desk and decides which books are worth reading.\n\n@lore bartholomew\nkeys: Bartholomew, goat\ncanon: soft\ntext: Maud's goat, who has eaten parts of the Restricted Section and most of a sleeve. He likes some students more than others.\n\n@lore gerald\nkeys: Gerald, mandrake\ncanon: soft\ntext: Tobias's mandrake, who wears a knitted hat, screams softly and bites the people he loves.\n\n@lore ember_fox\nkeys: Ember, fox, ember tail\ncanon: soft\ntext: A small fox with a tail that smoulders like an ember. If you free her from a snare she may come back with a ruby, or stay.\n\n@lore pim\nkeys: Pim, first-year, mentee\ncanon: soft\ntext: A nervous first-year who attaches to final-year students in need of a mentor.\n\n@lore duchess_of_ambry\nkeys: Duchess of Ambry, portrait\ncanon: soft\ntext: An escaped portrait who wanders the castle's frames, gossiping and refusing to return to her own.\n\n@lore professors\nkeys: Professor Marisol, Professor Vey, Professor Brask\ncanon: soft\ntext: Professor Marisol teaches Tidecalling and keeps the lake calm on exam days. Professor Vey teaches Illusion and grades on whether you fooled her. Professor Brask teaches Beastcraft and is kinder to animals than to students.\n\n@lore clockwork_owl\nkeys: clockwork owl, brass owl\ncanon: soft\ntext: Mir's brass owl with amber eyes: it hoots the hour, remembers deadlines and birthdays, and refuses to work for Professor Thorne.\n\n@lore thornes_rival\nkeys: wizard in purple, rematch\ncanon: soft\ntext: A flamboyant old wizard in purple who has wanted to settle a forty-year-old matter with Thorne. The rematch lasts four seconds; afterwards both old men laugh until they wheeze.\n\n@lore isolde_youth\nkeys: young headmistress, portrait, observatory fire\ncanon: soft\ntext: Before she was headmistress, Isolde Marrow was the student with the detention record of the decade, who set the observatory on fire twice.\n\n@lore tamsin_reed\nkeys: Tamsin Reed, Tamsin, pen pal\ncanon: soft\ntext: A Thornwick illusionist in the player's year, a pen pal since their first term. Raised in a travelling theatre; sly in letters, loyal in person. Thornwick set them to spy on Lumenhold through their letters, and they gave it up for the friendship.\n"},{"path":"content/world/lore/soft/places.grim","text":"# Lore drafts (canon: soft): places in and beyond the academy.\n\n@lore the_village\nkeys: village, barge, market square\ncanon: soft\ntext: The village lies across the lake, reached by barge. Its shopkeepers have seen every kind of student: the Gilded Newt, Mabel's tea shop, Grimsby's Books, Pell & Daughters and the bakery.\n\n@lore gilded_newt\nkeys: Gilded Newt, tavern\ncanon: soft\ntext: The village tavern, with low beams and a fire that never goes out. Students work shifts there and card games run late.\n\n@lore mabels\nkeys: Mabel's, tea shop\ncanon: soft\ntext: Forty kinds of tea and three kinds of cake. Mabel reads your mood from the way you open the door.\n\n@lore grimsbys\nkeys: Grimsby's Books, bookshop\ncanon: soft\ntext: A bookshop so crowded the shelves lean on each other. Mr Grimsby will find anything except what you came in for.\n\n@lore pell_and_daughters\nkeys: Pell & Daughters, apothecary, Old Pell\ncanon: soft\ntext: The village apothecary, with a thousand labelled drawers. Old Pell taught three generations to count drops.\n\n@lore bakery\nkeys: bakery, baker's daughter\ncanon: soft\ntext: The same family has baked in the village for two hundred years. A great-great-great-grandmother waited all her life for a letter from a student who died.\n\n@lore whisperwood\nkeys: Whisperwood, forest, moonpetal, shadow stag\ncanon: soft\ntext: An old forest west of the lake that talks in its sleep. Moonpetal blooms in its clearings only at midnight; a stag made of shadow guards them and steps aside only for the brave.\n\n@lore secret_garden\nkeys: secret garden, silver bees, humming pear tree\ncanon: soft\ntext: Behind the potting shed, a door in the wall leads to Maud's secret garden: glowing ferns, silver bees and a pear tree that hums. It only exists for people Maud has shown it to.\n\n@lore greenhouses\nkeys: greenhouses, moonflower\ncanon: soft\ntext: Glass houses that steam even in winter. The moonflowers there bloom once every eleven years and fill the greenhouse with silver light.\n\n@lore observatory\nkeys: observatory, telescope, east tower\ncanon: soft\ntext: A brass dome atop the east tower. The great telescope points wherever it likes. On cold nights Kael sits on the roof outside it, and frost spreads around him.\n\n@lore boathouse\nkeys: boathouse, jetty\ncanon: soft\ntext: An old boathouse with a jetty over the water, where people say the things they can't say indoors. The tunnel to the round door starts here.\n\n@lore thornwick\nkeys: Thornwick Academy, rival school\ncanon: soft\ntext: The rival academy across the hills, famous for illusionists and tournament grudges. Exchange students come and go with suspicious politeness.\n\n@lore the_north\nkeys: the North, Northern, ice palaces, singing glaciers, northern lights\ncanon: soft\ntext: The frozen North of ice palaces, singing glaciers and green-and-violet lights in the sky. Northerners touch foreheads in farewell and practise a frost-craft older than anything taught at Lumenhold.\n\n@lore port_calder\nkeys: Port Calder, coast, street origin\ncanon: soft\ntext: A rough harbour town on the coast, where students of the street origin learned their trade and where trunks get stolen only once per visit.\n\n@lore conclave\nkeys: Conclave, Conclave of the Seven Towers\ncanon: soft\ntext: The governing body of magic, in the capital. It asks professors for recommendations and runs laboratories where new spells are tested behind thick walls.\n\n@lore capital_institutions\nkeys: Royal Library, Guild of Alchemists, Office of Unseen Affairs, Travelling Circus of Wonders\ncanon: soft\ntext: Beyond the academy lie the Royal Library's archives, the Guild of Alchemists' works, the Crown's Office of Unseen Affairs, which has no sign and no address, and the Travelling Circus of Wonders, which recruits graduates who want to wander.\n"},{"path":"content/world/places.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Places (design §6.1). Positions are on the campus map\n#  (960 × 680, see docs/map-concept.svg): the castle stands on the\n#  north shore of the Mirror Lake, the village to the south-east,\n#  the Whisperwood to the west. Data only until phase 2b.\n#\n#  open:      the slots a place can be visited in\n#  discover:  when it becomes visible (omit for places open from the start)\n#  hint:      teaser shown while hinted (hinted_when) but not yet discovered\n#  draws:     tags of older events (no place: yet) that may happen here (compat, phase 2b)\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- Public from the start ----------------\n\n@place courtyard\nname: The Courtyard\nregion: castle\nmap: campus 585,278\nkind: public\nopen: morning noon afternoon evening night\nart: tower\ndraws: social festival rival story\ndesc: Flagstones worn smooth by three centuries of students, a fountain that freezes when it shouldn't, and the best gossip in the castle.\n\n@place library\nname: The Library\nregion: castle\nmap: campus 380,275\nkind: public\ntrack: library_assistant\nopen: morning noon afternoon evening night\nart: book\ndraws: study\ndesc: Seven floors of shelves that rearrange themselves when nobody is looking. Footnote the cat keeps order.\n\n@place refectory\nname: The Refectory\nregion: castle\nmap: campus 470,278\nkind: public\nopen: morning noon afternoon evening midnight\nart: meal\ndraws: social home money\ndesc: Long tables, longer benches, and soup that tastes faintly of whatever you're homesick for.\n\n@place lakeshore\nname: The Lakeshore\nregion: grounds\nmap: campus 480,505\nkind: public\nopen: morning noon afternoon evening night midnight\nart: wave\ndraws: nature mystery romance\ndesc: The Mirror Lake lies so still it shows the towers upside down. On some nights it shows other things.\n\n@place village\nname: The Village\nregion: village\nmap: campus 720,520\nkind: public\nopen: afternoon evening\nart: house\ndraws: money home\ndesc: A barge ride across the lake: crooked roofs, a market square and shopkeepers who have seen every kind of student.\n\n# ---------------- Castle ----------------\n\n@place lecture_tower\nname: The Lecture Tower\nregion: castle\nmap: campus 420,180\nkind: public\nopen: morning noon afternoon evening\nart: scroll\ndraws: class study exam\ndesc: Spiral lecture halls stacked one above another. Evocation on the lower floors, where scorch marks are expected; Umbral Theory at the top, where the lamps burn dim.\n\n@place observatory\nname: The Observatory\nregion: castle\nmap: campus 600,175\nkind: public\nopen: afternoon evening night midnight\nart: planet\ndraws: magic mystery\ndesc: A brass dome at the top of the east tower. The great telescope points wherever it likes; usually that is the right place.\n\n@place great_hall\nname: The Great Hall\nregion: castle\nmap: campus 455,215\nkind: public\nopen: noon afternoon evening\nart: crown\ndraws: festival social\ndesc: Floating candles, a vaulted ceiling that shows tomorrow's weather, and the stage where the choir rehearses and the Midwinter Ball is held.\n\n@place infirmary\nname: The Infirmary\nregion: castle\nmap: campus 530,200\nkind: public\nopen: morning noon afternoon evening night midnight\nart: potion\ndraws: crisis danger\ndesc: Clean white beds, a cupboard of tonics and Sister Amaranth, who has seen every kind of injury and believes none of the excuses.\n\n@place alchemy_lab\nname: The Alchemy Laboratory\nregion: castle\nmap: campus 555,240\nkind: public\nopen: morning afternoon evening\nart: cauldron\ndraws: class magic\ndesc: Long benches, cauldrons in every size, and a ventilation charm that has given up on the third floor.\n\n@place workshop\nname: Mir's Workshop\nregion: castle\nmap: campus 615,235\nkind: public\nopen: afternoon evening night\nclosed_when: flag(mir_workshop_shut) and since(mir_workshop_closed) < 3\ndiscover: met(mir)\nhint: Something clanks and hums behind the west-wing tapestry.\nhinted_when: turn >= 2\nart: gear\ndraws: magic\ndesc: A former classroom full of springs, gears, half-finished machines and the smell of solder. Fire buckets by the door, on the faculty's orders.\n\n@place job_board\nname: The Job Board\nregion: castle\nmap: campus 520,262\nkind: public\nopen: morning noon afternoon evening\nart: paper\ndraws: job money\n# The job menu first on every visit, then one odd gig (feedback 2026-10-15).\nalways: job_board_visit\ndesc: A cork board in the entrance hall layered with notices: jobs, lost familiars, club recruitment and the occasional ransom note for a sock.\n\n@place ledger_office\nname: The Lumen Ledger Office\nregion: castle\nmap: campus 395,230\nkind: club\ntrack: lumen_ledger\nopen: afternoon evening night\ndiscover: member(lumen_ledger) or met(nessa)\nart: paper\ndraws: club\ndesc: A cramped room under the library stairs, walls papered with old front pages. The printing press groans like a dragon on deadline night.\n\n@place headmistress_tower\nname: The Headmistress's Tower\nregion: castle\nmap: campus 500,130\nkind: public\nopen: afternoon evening\ndiscover: rel(isolde) >= 12 or flag(warden_trial)\nhint: A spiral stair guarded by a stone owl that only opens its eyes for some students.\nhinted_when: met(isolde)\nart: crown\ndraws: story\ndesc: Books, tea and a board of crystal towers by the window. You are only here because she wants you here.\n\n@place restricted_section\nname: The Restricted Section\nregion: castle\nmap: campus 352,245\nkind: public\nopen: afternoon evening midnight\ndiscover: seen(forbidden_section_1) > 0\nhint: A door at the back of the library that is always locked, and always slightly warm.\nhinted_when: turn >= 2\nart: lock\ndraws: mystery danger\ndesc: Black-bound books that breathe in the dark. Some of them read you back.\n\n@place gargoyle_passage\nname: The Third Gargoyle\nregion: castle\nmap: campus 418,135\nkind: society\ntrack: cartographers\nopen: afternoon evening midnight\ndiscover: member(cartographers)\nhint: Corvin keeps glancing at a particular gargoyle.\nhinted_when: met(corvin)\nart: map\ndraws: club mystery\ndesc: Behind the third gargoyle on the north parapet, a passage that isn't on any map. Except theirs.\n\n@place seventh_tower\nname: The Seventh Tower\nregion: castle\nmap: campus 500,72\nkind: public\nopen: afternoon evening\ndiscover: clue(lead_seventh_tower) or clues(hollowstar) >= 4\nhint: A locked stair that leads up into a tower nobody admits is there.\nhinted_when: clock(hollowstar) >= 1\nart: key\ndraws: mystery story\ndesc: Locked for a century. At the very top, one room of dust and moonlight, and a star-shaped hollow in the wall.\n\n@place thesis_lab\nname: The Thesis Laboratory\nregion: castle\nmap: campus 565,160\nkind: thesis\ntrack: thesis\nopen: morning afternoon evening\ndiscover: year >= 4\nart: book\ndraws: study\ndesc: Rows of carrels where final-year students and their advisors argue about footnotes until the candles give out.\n\n# ---------------- Grounds ----------------\n\n@place duel_hall\nname: The Duelling Hall\nregion: grounds\nmap: campus 660,150\nkind: club\ntrack: duelling\nopen: afternoon evening\ndiscover: member(duelling) or rel(lyra) >= 9\nhint: Steel rings somewhere above the east stair.\nhinted_when: met(lyra)\nart: duel\ndraws: rival\ndesc: Scorched floorboards and a ceiling that has seen things. The practice dummies have names, and grudges.\n\n@place greenhouses\nname: The Greenhouses\nregion: grounds\nmap: campus 740,235\nkind: club\ntrack: verdant\nopen: morning afternoon evening\ndiscover: member(verdant) or met(tobias) or met(maud)\nart: leaf\ndraws: nature\ndesc: Glass houses that steam even in winter. The moonflowers bloom once every eleven years; the mandrakes have opinions every day.\n\n@place flight_field\nname: The Flight Field\nregion: grounds\nmap: campus 850,300\nkind: public\nopen: morning afternoon evening\nart: broom\ndraws: club\ndesc: A long green field with hoops at either end and a wind that cheats. Broom lessons, races and spectacular landings.\n\n@place stables\nname: The Familiar Stables\nregion: grounds\nmap: campus 845,385\nkind: public\nopen: morning afternoon evening\nart: paw\ndraws: nature\ndesc: Hippogriffs, a sulky griffon chick, owls in the rafters and Professor Brask, who talks to all of them more kindly than to students.\n\n@place boathouse\nname: The Boathouse\nregion: grounds\nmap: campus 610,440\nkind: public\nopen: afternoon evening night midnight\nart: wave\ndraws: social mystery romance\ndesc: An old wooden boathouse and a jetty out over the water. People come here to say the things they can't say indoors.\n\n@place orchard\nname: The Orchard\nregion: grounds\nmap: campus 790,165\nkind: public\nopen: morning noon afternoon evening\nart: tree\ndraws: nature romance\ndesc: Apple and pear trees older than the towers. In spring the blossom falls like snow; once, it was snow.\n\n@place maud_cottage\nname: Old Maud's Cottage\nregion: grounds\nmap: campus 700,330\nkind: public\nopen: morning noon afternoon evening\ndiscover: met(maud)\nart: house\ndraws: nature home\ndesc: A thatched cottage with chickens on the doorstep, bees in the eaves, a goat called Bartholomew and a kettle that is always nearly boiling.\n\n@place secret_garden\nname: The Secret Garden\nregion: grounds\nmap: campus 735,355\nkind: public\nopen: morning afternoon evening\ndiscover: seen(garden_maud_secret) > 0\nhint: A door in the wall behind the potting shed that you keep walking past.\nhinted_when: met(maud)\nart: flower\ndraws: nature magic\ndesc: Glowing ferns, silver bees and a pear tree that hums. It only exists for people Maud has shown it to.\n\n@place courier_loft\nname: The Courier Loft\nregion: grounds\nmap: campus 880,230\nkind: job\ntrack: broom_courier\nopen: morning afternoon evening\ndiscover: turn >= 3\nart: letter\ndraws: job\ndesc: A draughty loft full of parcels, pigeonholes and brooms in various states of repair. The dispatch bell rings at all hours.\n\n# ---------------- Village ----------------\n\n@place gilded_newt\nname: The Gilded Newt\nregion: village\nmap: campus 800,480\nkind: job\ntrack: tavern\nopen: afternoon evening night midnight\nart: cup\ndraws: job money social\ndesc: The village tavern: low beams, a fire that never goes out, and a landlady who remembers every student's tab to the penny.\n\n@place mabels\nname: Mabel's Tea Shop\nregion: village\nmap: campus 725,565\nkind: public\nopen: morning noon afternoon evening\nart: cup\ndraws: social home\ndesc: Forty kinds of tea, three kinds of cake and Mabel, who reads your mood from the way you open the door.\n\n@place grimsbys_books\nname: Grimsby's Books\nregion: village\nmap: campus 680,600\nkind: public\nopen: noon afternoon evening\nart: book\ndraws: study money\ndesc: A bookshop so crowded the shelves lean on each other for support. Mr Grimsby will find anything except what you came in for.\n\n@place pell_apothecary\nname: Pell & Daughters\nregion: village\nmap: campus 770,600\nkind: job\ntrack: apothecary_clerk\nopen: morning afternoon evening\nart: bottle\ndraws: job money\ndesc: An apothecary of a thousand labelled drawers. Old Pell taught three generations to count drops; his daughters taught him to smile at customers.\n\n@place bakery\nname: The Bakery\nregion: village\nmap: campus 640,555\nkind: public\nopen: morning noon afternoon\nart: meal\ndraws: home money\ndesc: The same family has baked here for two hundred years. The buns are famous; so, in certain circles, is a letter that arrived very late.\n\n# ---------------- Wilds ----------------\n\n@place whisperwood\nname: The Whisperwood\nregion: wilds\nmap: campus 185,340\nkind: public\nopen: morning afternoon evening night midnight\ndiscover: met(maud) or flag(fox_met)\nhint: Fog sits on the western woods even at noon.\nhinted_when: turn >= 2\nart: tree\ndraws: nature danger mystery\ndesc: An old forest that talks in its sleep. Moonpetal grows in its clearings; so do things that are better left there.\n\n# ---------------- Internship hosts (one map each; design §7.5) ----------------\n\n@place royal_library_archives\nname: The Royal Library Archives\nregion: internship:royal_library\nmap: royal_library 480,300\nkind: internship\ntrack: intern_royal_library\nopen: morning afternoon\nart: book\ndesc: Miles of stacks beneath the capital, where the oldest records of the realm are kept in temperature-controlled silence.\n\n@place conclave_laboratory\nname: The Conclave Laboratory\nregion: internship:conclave\nmap: conclave 480,300\nkind: internship\ntrack: intern_conclave\nopen: morning afternoon evening\nart: wand\ndesc: The research laboratory of the Conclave of the Seven Towers, where new spells are tested behind very thick walls.\n\n@place alchemists_works\nname: The Guild Works\nregion: internship:alchemists\nmap: alchemists 480,300\nkind: internship\ntrack: intern_alchemists\nopen: morning afternoon\nart: cauldron\ndesc: The Guild of Alchemists' great works by the river: vats, chimneys and a strict rule about naked flames.\n\n@place observatory_camp\nname: The Expedition Camp\nregion: internship:expedition\nmap: expedition 480,300\nkind: internship\ntrack: intern_expedition\nopen: afternoon evening night\nart: planet\ndesc: Tents on a high plateau where the air is thin and the stars are close enough to argue with.\n\n@place field_station\nname: The Beastcraft Field Station\nregion: internship:field_station\nmap: field_station 480,300\nkind: internship\ntrack: intern_field_station\nopen: morning afternoon evening\nart: paw\ndesc: A cabin on the moors, ringed with paddocks and nesting towers, where injured creatures are mended and wild ones are counted.\n\n@place thornwick_lab\nname: The Thornwick Exchange Lab\nregion: internship:thornwick\nmap: thornwick 480,300\nkind: internship\ntrack: intern_thornwick\nopen: morning afternoon evening\nart: mask\ndesc: A guest laboratory at Thornwick Academy, the rival school, where the illusionists are excellent and the hospitality is suspicious.\n\n@place unseen_office\nname: The Office of Unseen Affairs\nregion: internship:unseen_office\nmap: unseen_office 480,300\nkind: internship\ntrack: intern_unseen_office\nopen: evening night\nart: eye\ndesc: The Crown's Office of Unseen Affairs. It has no sign, no address, and a very good coffee machine.\n\n# ---------------- Home (the \"go home\" break plan; map: home) ----------------\n\n@place home_cottage\nname: The Family Cottage\nregion: home\nmap: home 485,320\nkind: plan\nopen: morning noon afternoon evening night\nart: house\ndraws: home\ndesc: The kitchen table, the creaky stair, the smell of whatever is in the oven. Everyone asks how school is.\n\n@place home_fields\nname: The Fields\nregion: home\nmap: home 310,320\nkind: plan\nopen: morning afternoon evening\nart: leaf\ndraws: nature home\ndesc: Long rows, a low wall to sit on, and the same view you used to stare at when you dreamed of towers.\n\n@place home_market\nname: The Market Town\nregion: home\nmap: home 770,240\nkind: plan\nopen: noon afternoon evening\nart: coin\ndraws: money social\ndesc: Market stalls, the old crowd outside the inn, and the one shop that still sells your favourite sweets.\n"},{"path":"content/world/spells.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Spells (design §9.1): 2 per discipline, 3 levels each.\n#  cost: mana per level · power: strength per level (tunable).\n#  Data only until phase 2a adds the swipe-up spell option.\n# ============================================================\n\n@spell flame\nname: Flame\ndiscipline: evocation\ncost: 10, 9, 7\npower: 1, 2, 3\nart: flame\ndesc: Fire from the fingertips: a candle's worth at first, a bonfire's later. Burns through wards that only expect cleverness.\n\n@spell tide\nname: Tide\ndiscipline: evocation\ncost: 14, 10, 9\npower: 1, 2, 3\nart: wave\ndesc: Water called up and moved: a wave, a wall, a lake politely asked to step aside.\n\n@spell foresight\nname: Foresight\ndiscipline: astromancy\ncost: 10, 9, 7\npower: 1, 2, 3\nart: eye\ndesc: A glimpse of the next few moments. Enough to know which way the dice will fall, sometimes.\n\n@spell starlight\nname: Starlight\ndiscipline: astromancy\ncost: 18, 14, 10\npower: 1, 2, 3\nart: star\ndesc: The light of a named star, held in the hand. Darkness that eats light finds it hard to swallow.\n\n@spell mend\nname: Mend\ndiscipline: alchemy\ncost: 10, 9, 7\npower: 1, 2, 3\nart: heart\ndesc: Knits cuts, cracks and broken teacups. Hearts take longer.\n\n@spell purify\nname: Purify\ndiscipline: alchemy\ncost: 14, 10, 9\npower: 1, 2, 3\nart: potion\ndesc: Draws out poison, curse-cold and bad ingredients. The cure has to come from somewhere; it leaves you tired.\n\n@spell glamour\nname: Glamour\ndiscipline: illusion\ncost: 9, 7, 5\npower: 1, 2, 3\nart: mask\ndesc: A little more charm than you really have. People remember liking you and not quite why.\n\n@spell mirror\nname: Mirror\ndiscipline: illusion\ncost: 14, 10, 9\npower: 1, 2, 3\nart: mirror\ndesc: A double of yourself, or of anything you can see. It doesn't talk, which is its only flaw.\n\n@spell haste\nname: Haste\ndiscipline: beastcraft\ncost: 10, 9, 7\npower: 1, 2, 3\nart: bolt\ndesc: Speed borrowed from hares and hawks. You act first; you pay for it with a long nap.\n\n@spell beast_speech\nname: Beast-speech\ndiscipline: beastcraft\ncost: 10, 9, 7\npower: 1, 2, 3\nart: paw\ndesc: Talk with animals, briefly. Most of them have been waiting to complain.\n\n@spell ward\nname: Ward\ndiscipline: umbral\ncost: 14, 10, 9\npower: 1, 2, 3\nart: shield\ndesc: A pale shell that turns aside the next harm.\n\n@spell shadowstep\nname: Shadowstep\ndiscipline: umbral\ncost: 18, 14, 10\npower: 1, 2, 3\nart: ghost\ndesc: Step into one shadow and out of another. Short distances only; the shadows in between are not friendly.\n"},{"path":"content/world/timeline.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Timeline (design §3.2, §4.4). `history` entries are dated canon\n#  (years before the player's first year, as negative numbers; the\n#  first year is 0). `beat` entries are the in-run calendar of fixed\n#  beats (yNmM; m1 = Harvestmoon); in phase 2b they drive the dated\n#  `auto` events. Context for authors; not shown in the game.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- History ----------------\n\n@timeline founding\ndate: -300\nkind: history\ntext: The founders raise seven towers above the Mirror Lake as a seal over the Hollow Star, with one lock in each tower and a star-shaped key. The entry for the founding year in the Founders' Ledger is later cut out with a razor.\n\n@timeline prudence_ashworth\ndate: -200\nkind: history\ntext: Prudence Ashworth, a student, dies before she can deliver a letter to the baker's daughter in the village. Her ghost stays in the dormitories, rearranging socks.\n\n@timeline seventh_tower_locked\ndate: -100\nkind: history\ntext: The seventh tower is locked. Nobody living remembers why, and the faculty prefer it that way.\n\n@timeline the_frozen_spring\ndate: -60\nkind: history\ntext: The lake freezes one spring night. Three students go down to the boathouse; only two come back. Maud is one of them; Edmund is the one who doesn't return. His silver wand is never buried.\n\n@timeline thorne_and_the_storm\ndate: -40\nkind: history\ntext: Aldric Thorne duels a storm, loses badly, and spends a year learning why. The rumour says he won. A rival from those years still wants a rematch.\n\n@timeline isolde_appointed\ndate: -20\nkind: history\ntext: Isolde Marrow, once the student who set the observatory on fire twice, becomes headmistress. Her student portrait is hidden in a forgotten corridor.\n\n@timeline ledger_founded\ndate: -20\nkind: history\ntext: The Lumen Ledger prints its first issue. Nobody has interviewed the headmistress since she took office.\n\n@timeline kael_arrives\ndate: y1m3\nkind: beat\nevent: kael_arrives\ntext: Kael Dumont arrives mid-term from the North, with frost on the dining-hall floor behind him.\n\n# ---------------- The year's fixed beats (today's calendar; re-paced in phase 3) ----------------\n\n@timeline harvest_feast\ndate: y1m1\nkind: beat\nevent: choose_elective\ntext: The year begins: electives are chosen and the Harvest Feast is held.\n\n@timeline club_fair\ndate: y1m2\nkind: beat\nevent: club_fair\ntext: The Club Fair, and Lantern Night over the lake.\n\n@timeline lantern_night\ndate: y1m2\nkind: beat\nevent: lantern_night\ntext: Lantern Night: lanterns released over the Mirror Lake, each carrying a wish.\n\n@timeline job_board\ndate: y1m3\nkind: beat\nevent: job_board\ntext: The Job Board fills with term-time work.\n\n@timeline winter_trials\ndate: y1m4\nkind: beat\nevent: winter_trials\ntext: The Winter Trials, the first exams of the year.\n\n@timeline midwinter_ball\ndate: y1m5\nkind: beat\nevent: midwinter_ball\ntext: The Midwinter Ball in the Great Hall.\n\n@timeline spring_tournament\ndate: y1m8\nkind: beat\nevent: spring_tournament\ntext: The Spring Tournament on the duelling green.\n\n@timeline grand_examinations\ndate: y1m10\nkind: beat\nevent: grand_exams\ntext: The Grand Examinations close the academic year.\n\n@timeline summer_recess\ndate: y1m11\nkind: beat\nevent: summer_plans\ntext: Summer recess: the towers empty.\n\n@timeline starfall\ndate: y1m12\nkind: beat\nevent: starfall\ntext: Starfall, when the meteor showers come and the whole academy sleeps on the lawns.\n\n@timeline the_night\ndate: y3m9\nkind: beat\nevent: hs_the_night\ntext: The night the lake freezes. Moves to year 4 in the v0.3 calendar.\n"},{"path":"content/world/tracks.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Tracks (design §7): courses, clubs, jobs, internships and the\n#  thesis as one progression system. The reduced list of §7.1\n#  (decision D4). Data only until phase 2b; today's electives,\n#  clubs and jobs are still the `class_*`, `club_*` and `job_*`\n#  states in content/states/states.grim.\n#  legacy: the old states a track stands for (phase 2b compat: joining\n#  grants them, so their events keep firing) · opens: the first term a\n#  course is offered (default 1).\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- Courses: 6 disciplines × 3 levels ----------------\n\n@track evocation\nkind: course\nname: Evocation\nskill: arcana\nlevels: 3\nlevel_names: Evocation I, Evocation II, Advanced Evocation\nplace: lecture_tower\nteaches: flame@1 flame@2 tide@2 flame@3 tide@3\nlead: thorne\nintro: thorne_intro\ndesc: Fire and water, called up and held. Pyromancy and Tidecalling, taught together because they keep putting each other out.\npicked: You sign up for Evocation. The reading list comes with a note about singed eyebrows.\n\n@track astromancy\nkind: course\nname: Astromancy & Divination\nskill: lore\nlevels: 3\nlevel_names: Astromancy I, Astromancy II, Advanced Astromancy\nplace: observatory\nteaches: foresight@1 foresight@2 starlight@2 foresight@3 starlight@3\ndesc: Reading the sky and the time that hasn't happened yet. Tea leaves in the first year; star charts that move in the third.\npicked: You sign up for Astromancy. Someone hands you a teacup and tells you not to drink from it.\n\n@track alchemy\nkind: course\nname: Alchemy & Healing\nskill: alchemy\nlevels: 3\nlevel_names: Alchemy I, Alchemy II, Advanced Alchemy\nplace: alchemy_lab\nteaches: mend@1 mend@2 purify@2 mend@3 purify@3\nlead: amaranth\nintro: amaranth_intro\ndesc: Potions, remedies and the patience to stir clockwise. Half of it happens in the laboratory and half in the infirmary.\npicked: You sign up for Alchemy. Your first lesson is how to stir clockwise, and why.\n\n@track illusion\nkind: course\nname: Illusion & Glamour\nskill: charm\nlevels: 3\nlevel_names: Illusion I, Illusion II, Advanced Illusion\nplace: lecture_tower\nteaches: glamour@1 glamour@2 mirror@2 glamour@3 mirror@3\nlead: vey\nintro: vey_intro\ndesc: Making people see what you choose. Professor Vey grades on whether you fooled her, and she is very hard to fool.\npicked: You sign up for Illusion. Professor Vey looks at you as if you might already be one.\n\n@track beastcraft\nkind: course\nname: Beastcraft & Flight\nskill: vigor\nlevels: 3\nlevel_names: Beastcraft I, Beastcraft II, Advanced Beastcraft\nplace: stables\nteaches: haste@1 haste@2 beast_speech@2 haste@3 beast_speech@3\nopens: 2\nlead: brask\nintro: brask_intro\ndesc: Creatures, familiars and brooms. Professor Brask believes the first two are easier to reason with than the third.\npicked: You sign up for Beastcraft. Your name goes on the list beside a broom and a hippogriff.\n\n@track umbral\nkind: course\nname: Umbral Theory\nskill: guile\nlevels: 3\nlevel_names: Umbral Theory I, Umbral Theory II, Advanced Umbral Theory\nplace: lecture_tower\nteaches: ward@1 ward@2 shadowstep@2 ward@3 shadowstep@3\nopens: 2\ndesc: Shadow, wards and the magic of what is hidden. Taught at the top of the tower, where the lamps burn dim on purpose.\npicked: You sign up for Umbral Theory. The lamps at the top of the tower are dim on purpose.\n\n# ---------------- Hidden courses ----------------\n\n@track artifice\nkind: course\nname: Artifice\nskill: alchemy arcana\nlevels: 1\n# taken late, after level II–III elsewhere: its classwork is pitched at expert (docs/authoring/checks.md)\nstandard: expert\nlevel_names: Artifice\nplace: workshop\nhidden: true\nprereq: passed(alchemy, 2) and passed(evocation, 2)\ndesc: Enchanted machinery: clockwork that thinks, cauldrons that stir themselves. Offered to students who already know how to make things and how to make them burn.\npicked: You take the place in Artifice. Someone shows you where the fire buckets are before anything else.\n\n@track starcalling\nkind: course\nname: Starcalling\nskill: lore arcana\nlevels: 1\n# taken late, after level II–III elsewhere: its classwork is pitched at expert (docs/authoring/checks.md)\nstandard: expert\nlevel_names: Starcalling\nplace: observatory\nhidden: true\nprereq: passed(astromancy, 3) and clue(heard_of_starcalling)\ndesc: Calling down the light of a named star. Not on any syllabus; taught by appointment, at night.\npicked: You accept the appointment for Starcalling. The first one is tonight.\n\n@track hollow_studies\nkind: course\nname: Hollow Studies\nskill: guile lore\nlevels: 1\n# taken late, after level II–III elsewhere: its classwork is pitched at expert (docs/authoring/checks.md)\nstandard: expert\nlevel_names: Hollow Studies\nplace: restricted_section\nhidden: true\nprereq: passed(umbral, 2) and passed(astromancy, 2) and clue(knows_founders_seal)\ndesc: What the founders sealed, and how. The headmistress teaches it herself, to very few students, in a room without windows.\npicked: You accept a seat in Hollow Studies. The headmistress writes your name in a book with a black cover.\n\n# ---------------- Clubs (one at a time) ----------------\n\n@track duelling\nkind: club\nname: Duelling Circle\nskill: arcana vigor\nplace: duel_hall\nranks: Member, Officer, Captain\ndue: 2\nlead: lyra\nintro: lyra_intro\ndesc: Practice bouts, the Spring Tournament and a great deal of shouting about footwork.\npicked: You join the Duelling Circle. Someone is already explaining footwork to you, loudly.\n\n@track lumen_ledger\nkind: club\nname: The Lumen Ledger\nskill: charm lore\nplace: ledger_office\nranks: Stringer, Reporter, Editor\ndue: 2\nlead: nessa\nintro: nessa_intro\ndesc: The student newspaper. Scoops, deadlines, gossip columns and the occasional duty to the truth.\npicked: You join the Lumen Ledger. Nessa hands you a notebook and your first deadline.\n\n@track verdant\nkind: club\nname: Verdant Circle\nskill: alchemy\nplace: greenhouses\nranks: Member, Gardener, Head Gardener\ndue: 2\nlead: tobias\nintro: tobias_intro\ndesc: The greenhouse club: moonflowers, mandrakes and long talks with plants that occasionally answer.\npicked: You join the Verdant Circle. Tobias introduces you to the plants, one by one.\n\n# A society (playtest 2, M40): a hidden flavour group with no dues, performance or ranks, beside your club.\n@track cartographers\nkind: society\nname: Midnight Cartographers\nskill: guile\nplace: gargoyle_passage\nhidden: true\nlead: corvin\nintro: corvin_intro\ndesc: The club that doesn't exist, mapping the passages the castle would prefer to keep. Never offered at the Club Fair; you are invited, or you aren't.\n\n# ---------------- Jobs (one at a time) ----------------\n# Pay per full shift by rank (L-1): one base, 15 / 21 / 27, with the hazardous jobs (the Newt's nights, the courier's\n# flights) a quarter above it and the quiet ones (the library, the apothecary's counter) a fifth below.\n\n@track library_assistant\nkind: job\nname: Library Assistant\nskill: lore\nplace: library\nranks: Apprentice, Clerk, Senior Clerk\npay: 12, 17, 22\ndue: 3\ndesc: Shelving books that don't want to be shelved, and keeping Footnote off the reference desk.\npicked: You sign on at the library. Your first shift is next week, and Footnote is already watching you.\n\n@track tavern\nkind: job\nname: Server at the Gilded Newt\nskill: charm\nplace: gilded_newt\nranks: Server, Barkeep, Manager\npay: 19, 26, 34\ndue: 3\ndesc: Evening shifts behind the bar. Tips depend on charm; survival depends on remembering who ordered the spiced cider.\npicked: You sign on at the Gilded Newt. The landlady shows you the cellar and the back door.\n\n@track apothecary_clerk\nkind: job\nname: Apothecary Clerk\nskill: alchemy\nplace: pell_apothecary\nranks: Clerk, Dispenser, Assistant Apothecary\npay: 12, 17, 22\ndue: 3\nlead: pell\nintro: pell_first_shift\ndesc: Counting drops and reading labels at Pell & Daughters. The wrong drawer is never just the wrong drawer.\npicked: You sign on at Pell & Daughters. Pell shows you which drawers never to open.\n\n@track broom_courier\nkind: job\nname: Broom Courier\nskill: vigor\nplace: courier_loft\nranks: Runner, Courier, Chief Courier\npay: 19, 26, 34\ndue: 3\ndesc: Parcels, letters and the occasional live delivery, flown across the lake in all weathers.\npicked: You sign on at the courier loft. They give you a satchel and a broom that pulls left.\n\n# ---------------- Research internships (by application; design §7.5) ----------------\n\n@track intern_royal_library\nkind: internship\nname: Royal Library Archives\nskill: lore\npay: 18\nplace: royal_library_archives\ndesc: Cataloguing and research in the oldest archives of the realm.\n\n@track intern_conclave\nkind: internship\nname: Conclave Laboratory\nskill: arcana\npay: 18\nplace: conclave_laboratory\ndesc: Spell research with the Conclave of the Seven Towers. Prestigious; a faculty recommendation is expected.\n\n@track intern_alchemists\nkind: internship\nname: Guild of Alchemists' Works\nskill: alchemy\npay: 18\nplace: alchemists_works\ndesc: Industrial alchemy at the Guild's great works.\n\n@track intern_expedition\nkind: internship\nname: Observatory Expedition\nskill: lore\npay: 18\nplace: observatory_camp\ndesc: A summer of sky charts on a high plateau.\n\n@track intern_field_station\nkind: internship\nname: Beastcraft Field Station\nskill: vigor\npay: 18\nplace: field_station\ndesc: Tracking, tending and counting creatures on the moors.\n\n@track intern_thornwick\nkind: internship\nname: Thornwick Exchange Lab\nskill: charm\npay: 18\nplace: thornwick_lab\ndesc: An exchange placement at the rival academy's illusion laboratory.\n\n@track intern_unseen_office\nkind: internship\nname: Office of Unseen Affairs\nskill: guile\npay: 18\nplace: unseen_office\nhidden: true\ndesc: By invitation only. Nobody applies to the Office of Unseen Affairs.\n\n# ---------------- Thesis (year 4; design §7.6) ----------------\n\n@track thesis\nkind: thesis\nname: Thesis\nplace: thesis_lab\ndue: 2\ndesc: A year-long research project with a faculty advisor, ending in a defence before a committee of three.\n"},{"path":"content/world/vocabulary.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Controlled vocabularies (design §3.1). Character traits and\n#  interests, roles, tones and memory kinds must come from these\n#  lists; lint rejects anything else. Add a value here first.\n# ============================================================\n\n@vocab traits\ndesc: Personality traits for characters and archetypes.\nvalues: proud ambitious lonely kind gentle homesick dreamy distant perceptive charming reckless loyal secretive chaotic curious stubborn guarded cold protective bold nosy idealistic overworked insecure bullying severe exacting dry warm eccentric patient calm watchful formidable fierce wary funny anxious honest sly brave shy\n\n@vocab interests\ndesc: Things characters care about; used by casting and briefs.\nvalues: duelling fire tides plants animals cooking prophecy painting tarot maps locks cards machines clocks flying stars snow writing gossip justice rules chess roses bees soup history teaching books music dancing money theatre\n\n@vocab roles\ndesc: A character's place in the academy.\nvalues: student prefect professor staff headmistress outsider villager\n\n@vocab seeks\ndesc: How readily a character comes looking for you (design §5.5): often (the default; friends and rivals), sometimes, rarely (only once you've sought them out), never.\nvalues: often sometimes rarely never\n\n@vocab tones\ndesc: The mood of an event (design §5.2 variety, §12 `tone:`).\nvalues: light warm tense grim eerie\n\n@vocab memory_kinds\ndesc: What an option can leave behind in a character's memory (design §10.2).\nvalues: helped hurt laughed fought confided kissed betrayed rescued protected abandoned\n\n@vocab regions\ndesc: Where a place is (design §6.1). Internship hosts use internship:<host>.\nvalues: castle grounds village wilds home travel internship\n\n@vocab slots\ndesc: The parts of a day (design §4.2).\nvalues: morning noon afternoon evening night midnight\n\n@vocab houses\ndesc: Student houses.\nvalues: Emberhall Rootward Stillwater Duskgate Brasswick Faculty\n\n@vocab canon_allowlist\ndesc: Proper nouns that canon lint accepts without a worldbook entry: book and essay titles, potion names, headlines and other small props. Anything that matters to the world belongs in @lore instead.\nvalues: Advanced Arcane Theory, Arcane, Curses of the Northern Wastes, Draught of Mild Courage, Draught of Unending Hiccups, Sleeping Draught, Magic and Duty, Nature of Absence, Potions, Pie-rates, Strike Again, Third Law of Correspondence, Brilliant Lectures, Brilliant Lecturers, Foundations of Flame, F, On Small Explosions\n"}];
window.LUMENHOLD_LOCALES = {"zh-CN":[{"path":"locales/zh-CN/core/rules.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/core/rules.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@game\n# en: Lumenhold Academy\ntitle [fceae1] = 流明堡学院\n# en: Four years. Forty-eight moons. One apprentice.\nsubtitle [e1da8c] = 四年。四十八轮月。一名学徒。\n# en: The sky-barge lurches. Below, the lake glitters; above, the seven towers of Lumenhold Academy pierce the clouds. You have a trunk, a borrowed wand and a letter of acceptance with your name spelled wrong. Four years begin now.\nintro [2dd4dc] = 天空驳船猛地一晃。脚下，湖面波光粼粼；头顶，流明堡学院的七座高塔刺破云层。你有一只行李箱、一根借来的魔杖，还有一封把你名字拼错的录取信。四年，就从此刻开始。\n# en: Harvestmoon\nmonth.1 [39637f] = 丰收月\n# en: Emberwane\nmonth.2 [293c65] = 烬落月\n# en: Mistveil\nmonth.3 [82c3ac] = 雾纱月\n# en: Frostcall\nmonth.4 [dbae91] = 霜唤月\n# en: Deepwinter\nmonth.5 [668c02] = 深冬月\n# en: Thawmoon\nmonth.6 [0fe880] = 融雪月\n# en: Stormbloom\nmonth.7 [58f0f1] = 风暴月\n# en: Greenrise\nmonth.8 [d3ee01] = 青苏月\n# en: Blossomtide\nmonth.9 [dab8ca] = 花潮月\n# en: Highsun\nmonth.10 [c6ab59] = 盛阳月\n# en: Goldenhaze\nmonth.11 [886cae] = 金霭月\n# en: Starfall\nmonth.12 [ab87b4] = 星坠月\n\n@resource health\n# en: Health\nname [6145af] = 健康\n# en: Your body. At zero you collapse.\ndesc [a87509] = 你的身体。归零时，你会倒下。\n\n@resource mood\n# en: Mood\nname [64551e] = 心情\n# en: Your spirits. At zero, despair takes hold.\ndesc [ca3154] = 你的精神。归零时，绝望会将你吞没。\n\n@resource mana\n# en: Mana\nname [78ad2a] = 法力\n# en: The magic you can spend on spells. Refills each month, faster the stronger your Arcana.\ndesc [738cd1] = 你能用来施咒的魔法。每月恢复，奥术越强恢复越快。\n\n@resource renown\n# en: Renown\nname [996fb2] = 声望\n# en: How much the academy talks about you — for better or worse. Fades a little every month.\ndesc [e64a8d] = 学院里有多少人在谈论你——无论好坏。每个月都会淡去一点。\n\n@resource alignment\n# en: Alignment\nname [6abafe] = 立场\n# en: Lumen or Hollow: the light you serve, or the dark that has noticed you. Deep in the Hollow, the way back is slow.\ndesc [6e8e70] = 光明或空洞：你所侍奉的光，或已经注意到你的黑暗。在空洞中陷得越深，回来的路就越慢。\n\n@resource gold\n# en: Crowns\nname [a78479] = 金币\n# en: Crowns in your purse. Rent is due every month.\ndesc [cf79f6] = 钱袋里的克朗。每个月都要交房租。\n\n@skill arcana\n# en: Arcana\nname [6f1c33] = 奥术\n# en: Raw spellcraft — power, control, nerve.\ndesc [0428ad] = 纯粹的施法功力——力量、控制与胆量。\n\n@skill lore\n# en: Lore\nname [d907ad] = 学识\n# en: Scholarship, theory, history, languages of the old world.\ndesc [5aadaa] = 学问、理论、历史，以及古老世界的语言。\n\n@skill alchemy\n# en: Alchemy\nname [5d01fc] = 炼金\n# en: Potions, herbs, reagents and the patience to stir clockwise.\ndesc [dbde44] = 药剂、草药、试剂，以及顺时针搅拌的耐心。\n\n@skill charm\n# en: Charm\nname [df43f0] = 魅力\n# en: Wit, warmth, persuasion. Making people want to say yes.\ndesc [e1c3b8] = 机智、温暖与说服力。让别人心甘情愿地点头。\n\n@skill vigor\n# en: Vigor\nname [6c0ee4] = 体魄\n# en: Stamina, reflexes, dueling and broom-flight.\ndesc [9f1acb] = 耐力、反应、决斗和飞帚。\n\n@skill guile\n# en: Guile\nname [987273] = 诡计\n# en: Sneaking, lying, noticing what others miss.\ndesc [fe3ade] = 潜行、撒谎，以及注意到别人忽略的东西。\n\n@hook rent\n# en: Dormitory & board\nlabel [547aba] = 宿舍与膳食\n# en: {board_missed == 0: {~The bursar takes the board money with a nod and a tick in a very large book.|Board paid: a bed, three meals and the right to complain about both.|You pay the board and count what's left twice.}|{~The board went unpaid. The refectory still feeds you, a little less each day.|No board money this month. Lunch is a pocketed roll.|Board unpaid again. Your name in the bursar's very large book is underlined now.}}\ndo.say.1 [4a967f] = {board_missed == 0: {~财务官收下食宿费，点一点头，在一本非常厚的账簿上打了个勾。|食宿费付清了：一张床、一日三餐，外加抱怨这两样的权利。|你付了食宿费，把剩下的钱数了两遍。}|{~食宿费没交上。膳堂照样给你饭吃，只是一天比一天少。|这个月交不起食宿费。午饭是一个揣在兜里的面包。|食宿费又没交。财务主管那本大得出奇的账簿上，你的名字底下画了线。}}\n\n@hook break_rent\n# en: Board at the castle\nlabel [0b2949] = 城堡食宿\n# en: {~The castle in the holidays still wants its board.|A bed in an empty dormitory costs the same as a full one.}\ndo.say.1 [5ac98d] = {~假期里的城堡，照样要收食宿费。|空荡荡的宿舍里一张床，和住满时一个价。}\n\n@hook tuition\n# en: Tuition\nlabel [7ee27b] = 学费\n# en: {~Term fees, paid at a window with a very small grille.|The bursar's quill scratches. Another term is paid for.}\ndo.say.1 [4595be] = {~学期费交在一个铁栅格小得可怜的窗口。|财务官的羽毛笔沙沙作响。又一个学期付清了。}\n\n@hook course_fees\n# en: Course fees\nlabel [f462c1] = 课程费\n# en: {~Course fees, paid at the registrar's window with the tuition.|The registrar takes the course fees with a sigh, as if it pained her too.}\ndo.say.1 [f8b592] = {~课程费和学费一起，在教务处的窗口交清。|教务员收下课程费时叹了口气，仿佛她也心疼。}\n\n@hook gpa_probation\n# en: Academic probation\nlabel [5ecefe] = 学业察看\n\n@hook term_stress\n# en: Term-time pressure\nlabel [0e2de2] = 学期压力\n# en: {month == 4 or month == 10: {~Exams are coming, and everybody's quill moves faster.|The library stays open late and is full until it closes.|You dream of an exam you haven't revised for, which is most of them.}|{~Essays stack up on your desk like weather coming in.|Lectures, reading, more reading. Term has a weight of its own.|Everyone is behind. Everyone says so in the corridors.}}\ndo.say.1 [a08f53] = {month == 4 or month == 10: {~考试要来了，人人的羽毛笔都写得更快。|图书馆开到很晚，一直坐满到关门。|你梦见一场没复习过的考试，而没复习过的考试占了大多数。}|{~论文在你桌上越堆越高，像远处压过来的天气。|上课、阅读、更多的阅读。学期自有它的分量。|人人都落后了。走廊里人人都这么说。}}\n\n@hook exhaustion_drain\n# en: Running on empty\nlabel [069bc4] = 筋疲力尽\n# en: {~You are so tired that the stairs look personal.|Your body has opinions about this month, and none of them are kind.|You fall asleep in a lecture and wake up in a different mood.}\ndo.say.1 [76f080] = {~你累得连楼梯都像在跟你作对。|你的身体对这个月颇有意见，而且没一条是好话。|你在课上睡着了，醒来时换了一副心情。}\n\n@hook gloom_drain\n# en: Gloom\nlabel [0e658b] = 阴郁\n# en: {~A grey month. You eat little and sleep badly.|Low spirits sit in your chest like a cold.|The gloom gets into your bones; even the stairs feel steeper.}\ndo.say.1 [f3af29] = {~灰蒙蒙的一个月。你吃得少，睡得差。|低落的情绪像一场感冒，压在你胸口。|阴郁钻进了骨头里，连楼梯都显得更陡了。}\n\n@hook natural_recovery\n# en: Rest\nlabel [e989b7] = 休养\n# en: {~A few early nights and a warm meal put some colour back in your face.|You rest, and your body thanks you slowly.|Sleep, soup and a quiet week. You feel more like yourself.}\ndo.say.1 [91824c] = {~早睡几晚，吃几顿热饭，你的脸上又有了血色。|你好好休息，身体慢慢地道了谢。|睡觉、喝汤、清静的一周。你觉得自己又像自己了。}\n\n@hook fame_fades\n# en: Fading gossip\nlabel [06d4e9] = 流言消散\n# en: {~The corridors find something else to talk about.|Yesterday's gossip goes the way of yesterday's bread.|Fewer heads turn when you pass. It's restful.}\ndo.say.1 [5662c0] = {~走廊里找到了别的话题。|昨天的闲话，和昨天的面包一样，没人要了。|你经过时回头的人少了。挺清静的。}\n\n@hook mana_regen\n# en: Mana returns\nlabel [d61671] = 法力恢复\n# en: {~The magic seeps back into you overnight, like warmth into cold hands.|Your fingertips tingle again by morning.|Whatever you spent, the castle quietly gives some back.}\ndo.say.1 [27eb51] = {~魔力在夜里一点点渗回你身上，像暖意回到冻僵的手。|到了早上，你的指尖又开始发麻了。|你花掉的，城堡悄悄还回来一些。}\n\n@hook exam_nerves\n# en: Exam nerves\nlabel [8b0cdb] = 考前焦虑\n# en: {~Exam nerves eat at your magic: your wand hand won't stay still.|Revision drinks your mana before you can cast a thing.}\ndo.say.1 [90208e] = {~考前的紧张啃食着你的魔力：拿魔杖的手怎么也稳不住。|还没施一个咒，复习就先把你的法力喝光了。}\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/endings.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/endings.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@ending ending_sent_home\n# en: Sent Home\ntitle [a6af26] = 被送回家\n# en: The infirmary writes to your family. A carriage comes for you on a grey morning and you watch the towers shrink in the rear window. Your body gave out before your dreams did. Perhaps, one day, you will come back.\ntext [9741e7] = 医务室给你家里写了信。一个灰蒙蒙的早晨，马车来接你，你从后窗看着高塔渐渐缩小。你的身体先于你的梦想垮掉了。也许有一天，你还会回来。\n\n@ending ending_quiet_departure\n# en: The Quiet Departure\ntitle [b17cb0] = 悄然离去\n# en: You take the dawn barge without telling anyone. The academy does not notice right away; then, one by one, people do. Some of them write. You keep the letters in a box you don't open for years. When you finally do, you are someone gentler, and you find you are not alone.\ntext [e6ed08] = 你没告诉任何人，搭上了黎明的驳船。学院起初并没察觉；后来，人们一个接一个地发现了。有些人给你写了信。你把信收进一只盒子，好多年都没打开。等你终于打开时，你已变得更温柔，而且发现自己并不孤单。\n\n@ending ending_expelled\n# en: Expelled\ntitle [a01536] = 开除学籍\n# en: The Board's decision is final. Your wand is not taken — Lumenhold is not cruel — but your name is struck from the register in ink that cannot be unwritten. You walk out of the gates with a trunk and a lesson, and the long road ahead is entirely your own.\ntext [27607d] = 委员会的决定无法更改。你的魔杖没有被收走——流明堡并不残忍——但你的名字被一种无法抹去的墨水从名册上划掉了。你提着行李箱、带着一个教训走出校门，前方的漫漫长路，完全属于你自己。\n\n@ending ending_hollow_claimed\n# en: Claimed by the Hollow Star\ntitle [83514d] = 被空洞之星吞噬\n# en: You open the door beneath the lake. The Hollow Star looks back. For a moment you understand everything, and then there is no \"you\" left to understand it. On clear nights students swear they see a new star, black against black, right above the seventh tower.\ntext [205ca2] = 你打开了湖底之门。空洞之星回望着你。有那么一瞬间，你明白了一切；随后，再也没有一个“你”能去明白什么了。晴朗的夜里，学生们发誓看见了一颗新星——黑上之黑——就在第七塔的正上方。\n\n@ending ending_runaway\n# en: Gone With the Wind\ntitle [d9dde5] = 随风而去\n# en: You and Corvin leave Lumenhold on a stolen sky-barge at midnight, laughing so hard you can barely steer. The academy will tell the story for years — usually wrong. You never graduate. You don't regret it, mostly.\ntext [c5d68b] = 午夜时分，你和科尔文驾着一艘偷来的天空驳船离开了流明堡，笑得几乎掌不稳舵。学院会把这个故事讲上好多年——而且通常讲错。你始终没有毕业。你并不后悔，大体上。\n\n@ending ending_hollow_keeper\n# en: Keeper of the Hollow Star\ntitle [3ac5e6] = 空洞之星的守护者\n# en: You graduate with a black star on your palm and a voice in the back of your mind that is not entirely yours. The Hollow Star gave you power; it will want something back. For now, the towers bow a little when you pass, and even the headmistress looks away first.\ntext [8bdf91] = 你毕业时，掌心带着一颗黑色的星，脑海深处还有一个不完全属于你的声音。空洞之星给了你力量；它总有一天会要回些什么。至于现在，你经过时高塔都会微微低头，连院长都会先移开目光。\n\n@ending ending_hollow_freed\n# en: Herald of the Hollow Star\ntitle [5e0dc6] = 空洞之星的使者\n# en: The spring the lake froze, the Star went free, and you held the door. It did not swallow you; it remembered who let it out. The Northern families write to you as one of their own now, and wherever you walk, candles burn a little lower, as if bowing. Lumenhold still hands you a diploma. Nobody at the ceremony looks you in the eye.\ntext [0ea418] = 湖面冰封的那个春天，星挣脱而去，而为它扶住门的是你。它没有吞掉你；它记得是谁放它出来的。如今北境的世家把你当作自家人来写信，你走到哪里，烛火便低下去一点，像在鞠躬。流明堡照样发给你毕业证书。典礼上没有一个人敢直视你的眼睛。\n\n@ending ending_hollow_failed\n# en: The Night Was Lost\ntitle [3fa69b] = 失落之夜\n# en: The spring the lake froze, you went down beneath it, and the door would not close for you. The headmistress closed it in your place and paid for it with years she didn't have to spare. The Wardens don't blame you. Every spring, when the lake thaws, you do, a little, and then you get up and keep the old doors anyway.\ntext [41dc15] = 湖面封冻的那个春天，你下到了湖底，而那扇门不肯为你合上。校长替你关上了它，付出的是她本已所剩无多的岁月。守望者们不怪你。每年春天湖水解冻时，你会怪自己一点点，然后起身，照旧守着那些古老的门。\n\n@ending ending_hollow_hero\n# en: Warden of Lumenhold\ntitle [1eb489] = 流明堡的守望者\n# en: The academy will never fully know what you did beneath the lake. The headmistress does. At graduation she gives you a silver key without a lock and a seat among the Wardens, the quiet few who keep the old doors closed. Kael stands in the crowd and, for once, he is smiling.\ntext [fff990] = 学院永远不会完全知道你在湖底做了什么。院长知道。毕业典礼上，她交给你一把没有锁的银钥匙，以及守望者中的一席之地——那是守护古老之门、默默无闻的少数人。凯尔站在人群里，难得地，他在微笑。\n\n@ending ending_archmage\n# en: The Archmage's Path\ntitle [fea2fb] = 大法师之路\n# en: Graduating with highest honours and a power that makes the candles lean toward you, you are offered an apprenticeship with the Conclave of the Seven Towers. Professor Thorne shakes your hand and says, gruffly, \"Don't waste it.\" You won't.\ntext [e7e723] = 你以最高荣誉毕业，力量强到烛火都会向你倾斜。七塔议会向你发出了学徒邀约。索恩教授握着你的手，粗声说：“别浪费了。”你不会的。\n\n@ending ending_scholar\n# en: Scholar of the Spire\ntitle [02e34a] = 尖塔学者\n# en: Your thesis is shelved in the Spire Library, in the section students whisper about. The faculty offers you a junior fellowship. You will spend your life among books that bite and secrets that wait — and you could not be happier.\ntext [ea44fe] = 你的论文被收进了尖塔图书馆，就在学生们窃窃私语的那个区域。学院为你提供了初级研究员的职位。你将在会咬人的书和静静等待的秘密之间度过一生——再幸福不过了。\n\n@ending ending_beloved\n# en: Hand in Hand\ntitle [b32cc8] = 携手同行\n# en: Graduation is a blur of robes and cheers, but what you remember is a hand finding yours as the hats go up. Whatever comes next, you won't face it alone. Lumenhold taught you magic; it also taught you this.\ntext [ad5713] = 毕业典礼是长袍与欢呼交织的一片模糊，但你记得的，是帽子抛向空中时，一只手找到了你的手。无论接下来是什么，你都不会独自面对。流明堡教会了你魔法；也教会了你这个。\n\n@ending ending_beloved_lyra\n# en: The Second Flame\ntitle [c90153] = 第二簇火焰\n# en: When the hats go up, {lyra} catches yours before it lands and hands it back without a word. Later, in front of her whole family, she takes your hand and doesn't let go. For House Vaneshade, that is a declaration. For her it is simpler: she has finally found someone she doesn't need to beat.\ntext [1b430a] = 帽子抛向天空时，{lyra} 在你的帽子落地前接住了它，一言不发地递还给你。之后，当着她全家人的面，她握住你的手，再也没有松开。对凡妮希德家族来说，这是一份宣言；对她来说，事情更简单：她终于找到了一个不必去赢的人。\n\n@ending ending_beloved_tobias\n# en: Home, Wherever\ntitle [4a14ae] = 无论何处皆是家\n# en: {tobias} cries through the whole ceremony and blames the pollen. Afterwards he lifts you off your feet in front of everyone, then shows you a drawing of a farmhouse with two chairs on the porch. \"No pressure,\" he says. \"The goats have already voted.\"\ntext [f06768] = {tobias} 哭完了整场典礼，坚称是花粉过敏。之后他当着所有人的面把你抱离地面，又给你看一张画：一座农舍，门廊上摆着两把椅子。“不着急，”他说，“山羊们已经投过票了。”\n\n@ending ending_beloved_seraphine\n# en: Already Foreseen\ntitle [350c84] = 早已预见\n# en: \"I saw this,\" {seraphine} says as the hats go up. \"Years ago. You were holding my hand.\" You are. For once she smiles at the present, as if it has finally caught up with her, and asks whether you'd like to know what happens next. You tell her you'd rather find out together.\ntext [c966c4] = “我见过这一幕，”帽子抛起时 {seraphine} 说，“好几年前。你握着我的手。”你确实握着。她难得地对着此刻微笑，仿佛现在终于追上了她，然后问你想不想知道接下来会发生什么。你说你宁愿和她一起去发现。\n\n@ending ending_beloved_corvin\n# en: No More Jokes\ntitle [ffc267] = 不再说笑\n# en: {corvin} slips a key into your palm during the speeches. It opens nothing at Lumenhold; he has checked. \"It's for wherever we end up,\" he says, and for once he doesn't make a joke of it. Then he does, of course. But his hand stays in yours.\ntext [83042c] = 致辞的时候，{corvin} 把一把钥匙塞进你掌心。它在流明堡打不开任何一扇门——他检查过了。“这是给我们将来落脚的地方用的，”他说，难得没有拿它开玩笑。当然，过了一会儿他还是开了个玩笑。但他的手一直握着你的。\n\n@ending ending_beloved_mir\n# en: Built to Last\ntitle [66dc88] = 经久耐用\n# en: {mir} has rigged the hats to fly back down. Most of them do. In the cheerful chaos they press a small brass ring into your hand; it ticks faintly, like a second heart. \"It'll run for fifty years,\" they say. \"Probably. We should check.\"\ntext [36f87a] = {mir} 给帽子装了机关，让它们自己飞回来。大部分确实飞回来了。在一片欢乐的混乱里，TA 把一枚小小的黄铜戒指按进你手心：它轻轻地滴答作响，像第二颗心脏。“能走五十年，”TA 说，“大概吧。我们得一起验证一下。”\n\n@ending ending_beloved_kael\n# en: Thaw\ntitle [448405] = 解冻\n# en: {kael} doesn't throw his hat. He stands beside you in the crowd, and when the cheering is loudest he says, very quietly, \"Come north with me. Or I stay. Either.\" It is the longest sentence about feelings he has ever spoken. His hand, in yours, is warm.\ntext [edf40c] = {kael} 没有抛帽子。他站在人群里你的身边，在欢呼声最响的时候，非常轻地说：“跟我去北方吧。或者我留下。都行。”这是他说过的最长的一句关于感情的话。他握在你手里的手，是温暖的。\n\n@ending ending_beloved_nessa\n# en: Front Page\ntitle [c50df3] = 头版\n# en: The Ledger's graduation issue runs a small notice at the bottom of page six, set in {nessa}'s own type: two names, one line, no comment. All of Lumenhold reads it before breakfast. {nessa} pretends not to watch them read it, and holds your hand under the table the whole time.\ntext [ed9fec] = 《流明纪事报》的毕业特刊在第六版底部登了一则小启事，用的是 {nessa} 自己的铅字：两个名字，一行字，不加评论。全流明堡的人都在早饭前读到了它。{nessa} 假装没在看大家读它，却在桌子底下一直握着你的手。\n\n@ending ending_alchemist\n# en: Master of the Crucible\ntitle [cd1134] = 坩埚大师\n# en: Your potions are already sold under the counter in three cities. On graduation day you sign the lease on a crooked little shop by the harbour. The sign reads your name, and underneath: NO REFUNDS FOR TRANSFORMATIONS.\ntext [59bc7c] = 你的药剂早已在三座城市里私下热销。毕业那天，你在港口边签下了一间歪歪扭扭的小铺子的租约。招牌上写着你的名字，下面一行小字：变形类商品，概不退款。\n\n@ending ending_celebrity\n# en: The Name on Every Lip\ntitle [264e3e] = 人人口中的名字\n# en: The Lumen Ledger runs a special edition about you. First-years queue for your autograph. You graduate as the most talked-about student in a generation, and the world beyond the gates is already waiting to hear what you'll do next.\ntext [208c47] = 《流明纪事报》为你出了一期特刊。一年级新生排队找你签名。你作为一代人中最受热议的学生毕业，而校门外的世界，早已等着听你接下来要做什么。\n\n@ending ending_merchant\n# en: Gold in the Vaults\ntitle [2332ee] = 金库满盈\n# en: You leave Lumenhold with a diploma and a fortune. Magic, you've decided, is good, but compound interest is better. Within five years your trading house has offices in every port — and an enchanted ledger that never loses a coin.\ntext [729e03] = 你带着一纸文凭和一笔财富离开了流明堡。你认定：魔法固然好，复利更好。不出五年，你的商行在每个港口都设了分号——还有一本永远不会少一枚硬币的魔法账簿。\n\n@ending ending_shadow\n# en: The Quiet Hand\ntitle [9eb5a5] = 无声之手\n# en: No one quite knows how you graduated with those grades. No one quite remembers the favours you called in. A discreet letter offers you a position with the Crown's Office of Unseen Affairs. You accept, of course. You'd already read it before it was sent.\ntext [0900ac] = 没人说得清你是怎么凭那样的成绩毕业的。也没人记得你讨回了哪些人情。一封措辞谨慎的信，邀请你加入王室隐秘事务署。你当然接受了。信还没寄出，你就已经读过了。\n\n@ending ending_beloved_friends\n# en: The Ones Who Stayed\ntitle [5337c3] = 留下来的人\n# en: You may not leave as the brightest or the richest, but you leave surrounded. Your friends throw their hats higher than anyone's, and the photograph from that day — all of you squinting into the sun — will hang in every home you ever have.\ntext [8d3051] = 你离开时也许不是最聪明的，也不是最富有的，但你被大家簇拥着。你的朋友们把帽子抛得比谁都高；那天的合影——大家都在阳光里眯着眼——会挂在你今后住过的每一个家里。\n\n@ending ending_barely\n# en: By the Skin of Your Teeth\ntitle [434e96] = 惊险过关\n# en: Your name is called last, after a long pause and some whispering between examiners. But it is called. You graduate. You are a mage of Lumenhold, and nobody ever has to know the details.\ntext [7c93a5] = 在一段长长的停顿和考官之间的窃窃私语之后，你的名字最后一个被念到。但终究被念到了。你毕业了。你是流明堡的法师，没人需要知道其中的细节。\n\n@ending ending_bystander\n# en: Someone Else's Lantern\ntitle [93af7a] = 别人的灯笼\n# en: There was a spring frost, the year you graduated, and a night when the bell under the lake would not stop ringing. You were somewhere else: asleep, or studying, or holding a door shut for the first-years. Someone else went down with the lantern. You never learn their whole story, but every spring, when the lake thaws, you find yourself grateful to them.\ntext [737b03] = 你毕业那年下过一场春霜，还有一个夜晚，湖底的钟响个不停。那时你在别处：在睡觉，在读书，或是替一年级生死死顶住一扇门。提着灯笼下去的是别人。你始终没能知道他们的全部故事，但每年春天湖水解冻时，你总会发现自己在心里默默感谢他们。\n\n@ending ending_graduate\n# en: A Mage of Lumenhold\ntitle [691b6b] = 流明堡的法师\n# en: Robes, hats, a speech that runs long. You walk out of the gates of Lumenhold a qualified mage — not legendary, not disgraced, simply yourself, with four years of stories and a whole world to spend them in.\ntext [7b1e39] = 长袍、学位帽，一场拖得太长的致辞。你作为一名合格的法师走出流明堡的大门——不算传奇，也不丢人，只是你自己，带着四年的故事，和一整个可以挥霍它们的世界。\n\n@ending ending_teacher\n# en: The Teacher's Desk\ntitle [b21115] = 讲台之后\n# en: In the autumn you return to Lumenhold — not on the student barge, but on the faculty one. Your classroom has a window over the lake and a desk with somebody else's initials carved in it. On the first day a terrified first-year asks a question you once asked. You know exactly what to say.\ntext [dce2bc] = 秋天，你回到了流明堡——不是坐学生的驳船，而是坐教职员的那一艘。你的教室有一扇面朝湖水的窗，讲桌上刻着别人的名字缩写。开学第一天，一个吓坏了的一年级生问了一个你当年也问过的问题。你完全知道该怎么回答。\n\n@ending ending_wanderer\n# en: The Road of Wonders\ntitle [055eab] = 奇迹之路\n# en: You join the Travelling Circus of Wonders as its youngest illusionist. For years you cross the kingdoms in a painted wagon, making dragons out of moonlight for children who have never seen magic. Every winter, a letter arrives from Lumenhold. You always write back.\ntext [31dcb2] = 你加入了奇迹巡回马戏团，成了团里最年轻的幻术师。多年来，你坐着彩绘马车穿越一个又一个王国，用月光为从未见过魔法的孩子们变出巨龙。每年冬天，都会有一封来自流明堡的信。你每一封都回。\n\n@ending ending_tamsin_friends\n# en: A Friend Across the Hills\ntitle [c7176e] = 山那边的朋友\n# en: Your yearbook entry has a green seal pressed into the corner, which is not allowed. Tamsin Reed of Thornwick sent it, with a note: \"Four years of rivalry, four hundred letters, one friend. Insufferable to the end.\" Every spring, at a town halfway between, there are biscuits.\ntext [fb273b] = 你的毕业纪念册角落里压着一枚绿色的封蜡，这是不允许的。是荆木学院的塔姆辛·里德寄来的，附了张字条：“四年的较劲，四百封信，一个朋友。到最后都让人受不了。”每年春天，在两校中间的一座小镇上，都有饼干。\n\n@ending ending_dumonts_reunited\n# en: Two Dumonts in the Photograph\ntitle [51d393] = 合影里的两个杜蒙\n# en: In the graduation photograph, at the edge of the crowd, two pale heads lean together: Kael, and the sister who was raised to open the door he refused. Neither of them is smiling at the camera. They are smiling at each other, which is rarer, and which you helped to happen.\ntext [d79ff1] = 毕业合影里，人群边上，两颗浅色的脑袋靠在一起：凯尔，和那个被养大来打开他拒绝打开的那扇门的妹妹。他们谁都没有对着镜头笑。他们是在对彼此笑——这更难得，而你帮了这个忙。\n\n@ending ending_ilse_taken\n# en: A Chair Left Empty\ntitle [c33d8b] = 一把空着的椅子\n# en: The man in grey took Ilse north, and no letter has come. At graduation Kael keeps the seat beside him empty without saying why. You know why. Some evenings, when the lake goes very still, you still look for a girl in white furs on the far shore.\ntext [9df500] = 灰衣人把伊尔泽带去了北境，至今没有一封信。毕业典礼上，凯尔身旁的座位一直空着，他没说为什么。你知道为什么。有些傍晚，湖面静得出奇的时候，你仍会在对岸寻找一个穿白裘的女孩。\n\n@ending ending_thesis_hollowstar\n# en: The Thesis They Locked Away\ntitle [dad732] = 被锁起来的论文\n# en: Your thesis on the founders' seal earned a distinction and then disappeared into the headmistress's tower. Its catalogue card in the Spire Library reads only \"Restricted\", in her handwriting. Three people in the world have read it. One of them still writes to you with questions.\ntext [b15104] = 你关于创始者封印的毕业论文拿到了优等，随后便消失在院长塔里。尖塔图书馆里它的目录卡上只写着\"禁阅\"二字，是她的笔迹。世上读过它的只有三个人，其中一个至今还写信来向你请教。\n\n@ending ending_lumen\n# en: A Lantern for Others\ntitle [217ba6] = 为他人提灯的人\n# en: In the yearbook, under your portrait, someone has written: \"The one you went to when it mattered.\" First-years you barely remember will name their own lanterns after you. Lumenhold has a word for people like that. It is the same word as its name.\ntext [2de9cf] = 年鉴上，你的肖像下面有人写了一句：“要紧的时候，大家去找的那个人。”你几乎记不得的一年级生，日后会用你的名字给自己的灯笼命名。流明堡对这样的人有个说法——就是它自己的名字。\n\n@ending ending_hollow_touched\n# en: The Shadow at Your Heel\ntitle [b9eb7a] = 跟在脚边的影子\n# en: Your portrait in the yearbook is the only one where the candles behind you seem to have gone out. People step aside when you pass, not quite knowing why. The Hollow has learned your name, and you find, more and more often, that you answer to it.\ntext [7dd587] = 整本年鉴里，只有你的肖像身后的烛火像是熄了。人们在你经过时让开路，却说不清为什么。空洞已经知道了你的名字，而你越来越常发现，自己会应声。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/alignment.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/alignment.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event align_cold_candles\n# en: The Candles Lean Away\ntitle [1845e0] = 烛火避让\n# en: {You notice it on the east stair. As you pass, the candle flames bend away from you, all of them, like grass from a wind. A first-year on the landing notices too.|The candles lean away again. You have started to time it. It is getting faster.}\ntext [8f1ab2] = {你是在东楼梯上注意到的。你经过时，烛焰全都朝远离你的方向弯去，像风吹过草地。楼梯平台上的一个一年级生也看见了。|烛火又避开了。你开始给它计时。它越来越快了。}\n# en: A long stone corridor at dusk, every candle flame bending away from one student walking down the middle of it.\nscene [48ce03] = 黄昏时分一条长长的石廊，每一簇烛焰都朝远离走在中间那名学生的方向弯去。\n# en: Let them lean\nopt.left.label [131e40] = 由它们去吧\n# en: You walk on without looking. Behind you the first-year says nothing at all, which is its own kind of saying.\nopt.left.say.1 [93806d] = 你目不斜视地走过去。身后那个一年级生一句话也没说——这本身就是一种表态。\n# en: Stop and relight one by hand\nopt.right.label [87e0d1] = 停下，亲手点亮一盏\n# en: You cup a flame back to life with your palm. It flickers, considers you, and stays. It takes longer than it should.\nopt.right.say.1 [4fde72] = 你用掌心护着一簇火苗让它重新燃起。它摇曳着，打量着你，然后留了下来。这比本该花的时间要久。\n\n@event align_whispered_answer\n# en: A Voice Over Your Shoulder\ntitle [be6019] = 肩后的声音\n# en: Revising late, you get stuck on the same question for the third time. Then the answer is simply there, in a voice that is almost yours, from just behind your left ear. The inkwell has frosted over.\ntext [b8b636] = 深夜复习时，你第三次卡在同一道题上。然后答案就那样出现了——一个几乎是你自己的声音，从你左耳后方传来。墨水瓶上结了一层霜。\n# en: A student bent over a desk by candlelight, revision notes everywhere, and a cold patch of frost forming on the inkwell.\nscene [6d785b] = 烛光下伏案的学生，复习笔记摊得到处都是，墨水瓶上正凝出一片冰霜。\n# en: Write it down\nopt.left.label [f20ae7] = 把它写下来\n# en: It is right. Of course it is right. You write until dawn and never once get stuck, and in the morning you cannot remember learning any of it.\nopt.left.say.1 [d6cb97] = 它是对的。当然是对的。你一直写到天亮，一次也没卡住；可到了早上，你完全不记得自己学过这些。\n# en: Close the book\nopt.right.label [7a6dbd] = 合上书\n# en: You close the book and sit with your hands flat on the cover until the frost on the inkwell melts. The question stays unanswered. It's yours, at least.\nopt.right.say.1 [88c4b2] = 你合上书，双手平放在封面上，一直坐到墨水瓶上的霜化掉。那道题仍然没有答案。但至少它是你自己的。\n\n@event align_maud_way_back\n# en: Weeding, Dearie\ntitle [b9db32] = 来拔草吧，孩子\n# en: Old Maud looks at you for a long moment over her spectacles, the way she looks at a plant that's been kept in the wrong window. \"You've a cold look about you, dearie,\" she says, and holds out a trowel. \"Weeds don't pull themselves. Neither does the rest.\"\ntext [602ce6] = 老莫德从眼镜上方久久地看着你，就像在看一株被放错了窗台的植物。“你身上有股冷气，孩子，”她说着，递过来一把小铲子，“杂草不会自己拔掉。别的事也一样。”\n# en: An old woman in a straw hat kneeling in a vegetable bed, handing a trowel up to a student who hasn't asked for one.\nscene [557087] = 一位戴草帽的老妇人跪在菜畦里，把小铲子递给一个并没有开口要的学生。\n# en: Kneel and start pulling\nopt.left.label [2be082] = 跪下来开始拔\n# en: You weed until your knees ache and your hands smell of earth instead of frost. Maud hums the whole time. Nothing's fixed. Something has started.\nopt.left.say.1 [8ff3f1] = 你拔草拔到膝盖发酸，双手闻起来是泥土味而不是霜味。莫德一直在哼歌。什么都还没好起来。但有些事已经开始了。\n# en: \"I'm fine.\"\nopt.right.label [bdda04] = “我没事。”\n# en: \"Course you are,\" Maud says, and goes back to her beans. The trowel stays in the soil beside her, handle towards you, all afternoon.\nopt.right.say.1 [6bc997] = “当然没事，”莫德说着，回头去照料她的豆子。那把小铲子整个下午都插在她身边的土里，把手朝着你。\n\n@event align_reproach\n# en: \"I Don't Know You Lately\"\ntitle [5baaf9] = “我最近不认识你了”\n# en: {friend} finds you in the cloister and sits without saying hello. \"People talk,\" {they} say{s} at last. \"I keep telling them they're wrong about you. I'd like to keep being right.\"\ntext [8427e0] = {friend}在回廊里找到你，连招呼都没打就坐下了。“大家在议论，”{they}终于开口，“我一直告诉他们，他们看错你了。我希望我能一直是对的。”\n# en: Two friends on a cold bench in the cloister, one looking straight ahead, the other looking at the first.\nscene [1d2371] = 回廊里一张冰冷的长凳上坐着两个朋友，一个直视前方，另一个看着前者。\n# en: \"You are. I'll do better.\"\nopt.left.label [c698dd] = “你是对的。我会改。”\n# en: {friend} nods, slowly, and bumps {their} shoulder against yours. It is an apology and a warning at once.\nopt.left.say.1 [0320a5] = {friend}慢慢点了点头，用肩膀撞了撞你的肩。这既是和好，也是警告。\n# en: \"Mind your own business.\"\nopt.right.label [53fb82] = “少管闲事。”\n# en: {friend} gets up and goes. {They} don't look back. That's what stays with you: {they} always used to look back.\nopt.right.say.1 [d0c677] = {friend}起身走了，没有回头。让你一直记着的正是这一点：{they}以前总会回头的。\n\n@event align_ruthless_nod\n# en: Finally, Someone Who Gets It\ntitle [b51f5a] = 总算有人懂了\n# en: {rel(other) < 10: {other} has always looked straight through you. Today {they} nod{s} as you pass|{other} catches your eye in the corridor and nod{s} again}, slow and approving, the way people nod at someone who has finally learned the rules of a game.\ntext [6a6df3] = {rel(other) < 10: {other}向来对你视而不见。今天你经过时，{they}却朝你点了点头|{other}在走廊里和你对上目光，又一次朝你点了点头}——缓慢而赞许，就像人们朝一个终于学会游戏规则的人点头那样。\n# en: A hard-faced student leaning on a pillar, giving another a slow, approving nod across a busy corridor.\nscene [8d42a3] = 一个神情冷硬的学生倚着柱子，隔着熙攘的走廊朝另一人缓缓点头示意。\n# en: Nod back\nopt.left.label [ae3c6d] = 点头回应\n# en: The nod comes back to you. It feels like being let into a room. You can't tell whether the room is warm.\nopt.left.say.1 [375363] = 点头被回敬了过来。那感觉像是被请进了一间屋子。你说不清那屋子是否温暖。\n# en: Keep walking\nopt.right.label [6ca967] = 继续往前走\n# en: You keep walking. {other} snorts, as if you'd failed a test you didn't know you were sitting.\nopt.right.say.1 [1c408e] = 你继续往前走。{other}嗤了一声，好像你没通过一场你都不知道自己在考的考试。\n\n@event align_northern_parcel\n# en: A Parcel for the North\ntitle [917cb6] = 寄往北境的包裹\n# en: {A hooded courier stops you behind the bakery.|The hooded courier is waiting again, same alley, same black oilcloth.} \"For the boathouse, after dark,\" {they say, and hold out the parcel and a purse.|they say. \"The families remember who is reliable.\"} The oilcloth is cold enough to ache.\ntext [e57cd7] = {一个戴兜帽的信使在面包房后面拦住了你。|那个戴兜帽的信使又在等你了，同一条小巷，同一块黑油布。}“天黑后送到船屋，”{对方说着，递来包裹和一只钱袋。|对方说，“世家记得谁靠得住。”}那块油布冷得发疼。\n# en: A hooded courier in a rain-dark alley behind the village, holding out a small parcel wrapped in black oilcloth and a purse.\nscene [ab51fb] = 村子后面一条被雨淋暗的小巷里，一个戴兜帽的信使递出一个裹着黑油布的小包裹和一只钱袋。\n# en: Carry it\nopt.left.label [f4d75d] = 替他们送去\n# en: You leave it on the boathouse step at midnight and don't look back. In the morning there's frost on the step in the shape of a hand, and the purse is heavier than it looked.\nopt.left.say.1 [f86ac1] = 你在午夜把它放在船屋的台阶上，没有回头。早上，台阶上结着一只手形的霜，而那只钱袋比看上去要沉。\n# en: Walk away\nopt.right.label [96da1a] = 转身离开\n# en: You leave the courier holding it. \"Pity,\" they say, without any pity at all, and the alley is empty when you look back.\nopt.right.say.1 [371c1f] = 你让信使自己拿着它。“可惜，”对方说，语气里没有半点可惜。你回头时，小巷已经空了。\n\n@event align_cruel_advantage\n# en: Something You Could Use\ntitle [e223de] = 可以利用的东西\n# en: {mark} has dropped a letter in the library: something private, something {they} would hate the corridor to hear. You could hand it back. You could also keep it, and {they} would owe you, quietly, for a long time.\ntext [306670] = {mark}在图书馆掉了一封信：私密的事，{they}最不愿意让整条走廊听到的事。你可以把它还回去。你也可以留着它——那样{they}就会悄悄地欠你很久。\n# en: A crumpled letter on a library floor, a student's name on the envelope, another student bending to pick it up.\nscene [8a21c3] = 图书馆地板上一封揉皱的信，信封上写着一个学生的名字，另一个学生正弯腰去捡。\n# en: Keep it\nopt.left.label [87c2f5] = 留着它\n# en: You fold it into your book. When you mention it, very lightly, {mark} goes pale and agrees to everything. It works. It keeps working.\nopt.left.say.1 [18005f] = 你把它夹进书里。当你轻描淡写地提起它时，{mark}脸色发白，什么都答应了。这招管用，而且一直管用。\n# en: Hand it back\nopt.right.label [cb62a0] = 还给对方\n# en: You hand it back unread. {mark} stares at you, then at the letter, then says \"thank you\" as if it costs something. It does, for both of you.\nopt.right.say.1 [414642] = 你没拆开就把信还了回去。{mark}盯着你，又盯着那封信，然后说了声“谢谢”，好像这要付出什么代价。确实要——对你们俩都是。\n\n@event align_frost_in_the_spell\n# en: The Cold That Helps\ntitle [7dac5f] = 有用的寒意\n# en: Practising alone, you notice that your spells come easier when you let the cold in, the cold that has been following you lately. It climbs your sleeve like ivy. The candle beside you leans away.\ntext [a3ee9f] = 独自练习时，你发现只要放那股寒意进来——最近一直跟着你的那股寒意——咒语就来得更容易。它像常春藤一样爬上你的袖子。身旁的蜡烛避开了你。\n# en: A student in an empty practice room, a spell flaring unnaturally bright in one hand, frost creeping up the sleeve to the elbow.\nscene [2b39b1] = 空荡荡的练习室里，一名学生手中的咒语亮得反常，冰霜正沿着袖子爬到手肘。\n# en: Let the cold in\nopt.left.label [13fba8] = 放寒意进来\n# en: The spell flares bright and clean, brighter than anything you've managed in daylight. It takes an hour for the feeling to come back into your fingers. You don't mind as much as you should.\nopt.left.say.1 [ddd5a7] = 咒语亮得干净利落，比你在白天施展过的任何一次都亮。手指过了一个小时才恢复知觉。你没有本该有的那么在意。\n# en: Practise the slow way\nopt.right.label [210853] = 用笨办法练\n# en: You shake the cold out of your hands and do it the slow way, badly, again and again. It is your own bad spell, at least.\nopt.right.say.1 [21c347] = 你甩掉手上的寒意，用笨办法练，练得很差，一遍又一遍。至少那是你自己的蹩脚咒语。\n\n@event align_asked_for_help\n# en: \"You're the One People Ask\"\ntitle [8a706f] = “大家都来找你”\n# en: {~A first-year hovers by your table with a torn essay and a face like a wet weekend.|A first-year you've never met asks if you're the one who helps people. Apparently you are.|Someone has written your name on the common-room board under \"ask them\".} \"Somebody said you'd know what to do,\" {~they say, as if that settles it.|they say, and then wait.|they say, pointing back at the board.}\ntext [775c60] = {~一个一年级生拿着撕破的论文在你桌边徘徊，脸色像个阴雨的周末。|一个你从没见过的一年级生问你是不是那个会帮人的人。看来你是。|有人在公共休息室的告示板上“找他们问”一栏下写了你的名字。}“有人说你会知道该怎么办，”{~对方说，好像这就说明了一切。|对方说完，便等着。|对方说着，回头指了指那块板。}\n# en: A nervous first-year with an armful of torn notes hovering at the door of a common room, looking at one particular older student.\nscene [dd1c6d] = 一个紧张的一年级生抱着一摞撕破的笔记，在公共休息室门口徘徊，眼睛望着某一位高年级学生。\n# en: Sit them down and help\nopt.left.label [d2700f] = 让对方坐下，帮忙\n# en: It takes the whole evening and half your sleep. They leave lighter than they came. So, oddly, do you.\nopt.left.say.1 [c8dac7] = 这花了整整一个晚上，还搭上了你一半的睡眠。对方走的时候比来时轻松了。说来奇怪，你也是。\n# en: Point them to a tutor\nopt.right.label [0b7f21] = 介绍给辅导员\n# en: You give them a name and a smile and get back to your own work. They look disappointed for a moment, then grateful anyway.\nopt.right.say.1 [9dd1bd] = 你给了对方一个名字和一个微笑，然后回去做自己的功课。对方失望了片刻，但还是很感激。\n\n@event align_warm_regard\n# en: A Good Word\ntitle [352a57] = 一句好话\n# en: {friend} pushes the last biscuit across to you. \"People talk, you know. About the kind things you do when you think nobody's looking,\" {they} say{s}. \"Don't make a face. It's good. Someone should say so.\"\ntext [6b8c11] = {friend}把最后一块饼干推到你面前。“你知道吗，大家都在说。说你以为没人看见时做的那些好事，”{they}说，“别摆那副表情。那是好事。总得有人说出来。”\n# en: Two students sharing a pot of tea in a sunny window seat, one pushing the last biscuit towards the other.\nscene [7e8fe3] = 两个学生坐在阳光照进来的窗边座位上分一壶茶，其中一人把最后一块饼干推给另一人。\n# en: Accept the biscuit, and the praise\nopt.left.label [80690f] = 收下饼干和夸奖\n# en: You eat the biscuit. {friend} looks very pleased. You buy the next pot.\nopt.left.say.1 [909198] = 你吃掉了饼干。{friend}看起来非常得意。下一壶茶你请。\n# en: Say anyone would have done the same\nopt.right.label [c1a72c] = 说换了谁都会这么做\n# en: \"Anyone would,\" you say. \"Nobody did,\" says {friend}, and lets you change the subject, but the look {they} give{s} you says it has only been postponed.\nopt.right.say.1 [af9984] = “换了谁都会的，”你说。“可没人做，”{friend}说，然后由着你换了话题，但{they}看你的眼神说明，这个话题只是暂时搁置。\n\n@event align_saint_jibe\n# en: \"Saint\"\ntitle [136318] = “圣人”\n# en: {other} blocks the doorway with one arm. \"Here comes the saint,\" {they} say{s}, loud enough for the corridor. \"Tell me, does it get heavy, being better than everyone?\"\ntext [58f004] = {other}用一只胳膊挡住门口。“圣人来了，”{they}说，声音大得整条走廊都能听见，“告诉我，比所有人都好，累不累啊？”\n# en: A smirking student blocking a doorway with one arm, another student standing in front of them with an armful of books.\nscene [1e2bb0] = 一个冷笑着的学生用一只胳膊挡住门口，另一个学生抱着一摞书站在面前。\n# en: Let it go\nopt.left.label [7a5d2f] = 算了\n# en: You wait. Eventually {other} gets bored and drops the arm. Two first-years behind you look at you like you've done something brave. You're not sure you have.\nopt.left.say.1 [657e06] = 你等着。{other}终于觉得没意思，放下了胳膊。你身后两个一年级生看着你，好像你做了什么勇敢的事。你自己可不确定。\n# en: Give as good as you get\nopt.right.label [6cf248] = 以牙还牙\n# en: Your answer is quick and much funnier than theirs. The corridor laughs. {other} goes red. It feels wonderful for about an hour.\nopt.right.say.1 [28c5ab] = 你的回嘴又快又比对方的好笑得多。走廊里一片哄笑，{other}涨红了脸。这感觉美妙了大约一个小时。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/calendar_2b.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/calendar_2b.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event enrolment\n# en: Enrolment\ntitle [cbadef] = 选课\n# en: {term_index == 1: Your first term. The registrar slides a list across the desk: four courses open to first-years.|A new term, and a new list. Courses you've passed are gone from it; new levels have appeared.} {member(thesis): Choose one; the thesis takes your other slot.|Choose two.} Course fees are eight crowns.\ntext [3119c3] = {term_index == 1: 你的第一个学期。教务员把一张清单推过桌面：四门对一年级开放的课。|新学期，新清单。你已通过的课不见了，新的级别冒了出来。}{member(thesis): 选一门；毕业论文占了你另一个名额。|选两门。}课程费八克朗。\n# en: The registrar's hall at the start of term, students queueing at long tables with course lists that rewrite themselves.\nscene [6d30e9] = 开学时的教务大厅，学生们在长桌前排队，手里的课程表会自己改写。\n# en: There is nothing left for you to take\nopt.none.label [b606eb] = 没有你能选的课了\n# en: The registrar checks the list twice. Nothing on it is open to you this term. \"Enjoy the quiet,\" she says, without much conviction.\nopt.none.say.1 [3ba6f1] = 教务员把名单核对了两遍。这学期上面没有一门课对你开放。“享受清静吧。”她说，语气不太确定。\n\n@event club_fair_v3\n# en: The Club Fair\ntitle [ce4fee] = 社团招新会\n# en: The Club Fair fills the courtyard. Every table wants you; every captain swears theirs is the one that matters. {rel(lyra) >= 6: Lyra is at the Duelling Circle's table, pretending not to watch which way you walk.|}\ntext [fa6309] = 社团招新会挤满了庭院。每张桌子都想要你，每位社长都发誓自家才是最要紧的。{rel(lyra) >= 6: 莱拉站在决斗社的桌边，假装没在看你往哪儿走。|}\n# en: The courtyard full of recruiting tables, banners snapping, a duelling demonstration drawing a crowd.\nscene [72616b] = 庭院里摆满招新桌，旗帜猎猎作响，一场决斗示范引来一圈围观者。\n# en: {member(club): Stay with your club|Not this year}\nopt.none.label [9e77c4] = {member(club): 留在你的社团|今年算了}\n# en: {member(club): Another year with the same table.|You can join at next year's fair.}\nopt.none.hint [5c873f] = {member(club): 和同一张桌子再过一年。|明年的招新会还能加入。}\n# en: {member(club): {~You stop by your own club's table to help hand out leaflets. Someone asks you if it's worth joining. You say yes and mean it.|You walk past every other table with the ease of someone already spoken for.}|You collect three leaflets and a free biscuit, and sign nothing.}\nopt.none.say.1 [b61f4a] = {member(club): {~你在自己社团的桌边帮忙发传单。有人问你值不值得加入。你说值得，而且是真心的。|你从别的桌子前走过，带着一种名花有主的从容。}|你领了三张传单和一块免费饼干，什么也没签。}\n# en: Resign from your club\nopt.quit.label [990542] = 退出你的社团\n# en: You can join again at next year's fair, as a new member.\nopt.quit.hint [5bb00e] = 明年的招新会还能以新成员身份重新加入。\n# en: {~You hand back your club pin at the table where you got it. It feels smaller than it did.|You resign. Your evenings are yours again, and nobody will expect you anywhere.}\nopt.quit.say.1 [0a0a06] = {~你在当初领徽章的那张桌子前把社团徽章交了回去。它看起来比当时小了。|你退出了。晚上又归你自己了，也没人会在哪儿等你。}\n\n@event job_board_visit\n# en: The Job Board\ntitle [377c1b] = 招工告示板\n# en: Notices for work, three layers deep: the library, the Gilded Newt, Pell & Daughters, the courier loft. Each wants someone reliable. Each says so twice.\ntext [358cb2] = 招工启事叠了三层：图书馆、镀金蝾螈酒馆、佩尔父女药铺、信使阁楼。每家都要个靠得住的人，每家都说了两遍。\n# en: A cork board crowded with notices, a student running a finger down a column of shift times.\nscene [62cc7b] = 一块挤满告示的软木板，一名学生用手指顺着一列排班时间往下划。\n# en: Just looking\nopt.look.label [4af612] = 只是看看\n# en: You read every notice twice and take none of them down.\nopt.look.say.1 [5234e4] = 你把每张告示都读了两遍，一张也没揭下来。\n# en: Hand in your notice\nopt.quit.label [e9edc6] = 递交辞呈\n# en: You can find work again later.\nopt.quit.hint [974ede] = 以后还能再找工作。\n# en: You hand in your notice. Your evenings are yours again; so is your empty purse.\nopt.quit.say.1 [3dd0ab] = 你递交了辞呈。晚上又归你自己了，空荡荡的钱袋也是。\n\n@event break_plan\n# en: The Break\ntitle [e3e693] = 假期\n# en: {month == 5: The winter break.|The summer break.} Two months without lectures. The castle empties, mostly.\ntext [78e780] = {month == 5: 冬假。|暑假。}两个月没有课。城堡几乎空了。\n# en: The academy gates thrown open, barges loading trunks, a few students lingering on the steps with nowhere they'd rather be.\nscene [003006] = 学院大门敞开，驳船装着行李箱，几个学生在台阶上逗留，哪儿也不想去。\n# en: Stay at the castle\nopt.castle.label [04365a] = 留在城堡\n# en: Quiet corridors and secrets. Board 8 crowns a month.\nopt.castle.hint [6c24c3] = 安静的走廊和秘密。每月食宿 8 克朗。\n# en: The castle in the holidays is a different building: louder echoes, kinder staff, doors you've never seen open.\nopt.castle.say.1 [f9e1bd] = 假期里的城堡是另一座建筑：回声更响，职员更和气，还有你从没见开过的门。\n# en: Go home\nopt.home.label [c93952] = 回家\n# en: Family, old friends, free board.\nopt.home.hint [1bbb35] = 家人、老朋友、免费食宿。\n# en: The barge, the coach, the last mile on foot. Home is smaller than you remember, and warmer.\nopt.home.say.1 [0b9342] = 驳船、马车，最后一里路靠双脚。家比记忆中小，也比记忆中暖。\n# en: Work on campus\nopt.work.label [2ebfbe] = 留校打工\n# en: A holiday gig that pays well. Board 8 crowns a month.\nopt.work.hint [6b010a] = 一份工钱不错的假期零工。食宿每月 8 克朗。\n# en: You trade the holidays for wages. Your feet will remember it.\nopt.work.say.1 [e054a1] = 你用假期换了工钱。你的脚会记住这一切。\n# en: Start your internship\nopt.intern.label [744abc] = 开始实习\n# en: A stipend, a supervisor and a project.\nopt.intern.hint [abd74b] = 一份津贴、一位导师和一个课题。\n# en: You pack a trunk of notebooks and set off {member(intern_expedition): up the long road to the high plateau|{member(intern_field_station): across the moors to the field station|{member(intern_thornwick): on the coach to Thornwick, of all places|on the long coach to the capital}}}.\nopt.intern.say.1 [db63d2] = 你装了一箱笔记本，{member(intern_expedition): 踏上去高原的漫长山路|{member(intern_field_station): 穿过荒原前往野外站|{member(intern_thornwick): 坐上去荆木学院的马车，偏偏是那儿|坐上去王都的长途马车}}}。\n\n@event course_exam\n# en: Exam Day\ntitle [482384] = 考试日\n# en: Exam day for {track}. The hall is silent except for quills and somebody's knee bouncing under a desk. {~The paper is long.|The first question is one you revised.|The first question is one you didn't.|The invigilator turns the hourglass with a click you feel in your teeth.}\ntext [6e3a75] = {track}考试日。考场里鸦雀无声，只有羽毛笔的沙沙声，还有谁的膝盖在桌下抖个不停。{~试卷很长。|第一道题正好是你复习过的。|第一道题恰好是你没复习的。|监考老师把沙漏一翻，那一声咔哒你连牙齿都感觉得到。}\n# en: A silent exam hall in late light, rows of bent heads, an hourglass on the invigilator's desk.\nscene [43a564] = 傍晚光线里寂静的考场，一排排低着的头，监考桌上放着一只沙漏。\n# en: Answer what you know, carefully\nopt.left.label [7dfdbe] = 稳扎稳打，写你会的\n# en: {~You write slowly: everything you're sure of, and nothing you aren't.|You check every answer twice and hand in at the bell.|The first question is one you didn't revise, so you leave it and bank the rest.|You pace yourself by the hourglass and finish with sand to spare.}\nopt.left.say.1 [0fc20f] = {~你写得很慢：有把握的都写上，没把握的一个字也不写。|你每道题都检查两遍，铃响才交卷。|第一题你没复习，你就跳过它，把剩下的稳稳拿下。|你照着沙漏控制节奏，答完时沙子还剩不少。}\n# en: Go for top marks\nopt.right.label [4d6661] = 冲击高分\n# en: {~You take the hardest question first and gamble the essay on an idea you had at breakfast.|You write the answer you'd want to read, not the safe one.|The first question is one you didn't revise. You answer it anyway, boldly, from first principles.|You race the hourglass and write until the last grain, and past it.}\nopt.right.say.1 [ab3ae7] = {~你先做最难的题，把整篇论述押在早饭时冒出的一个想法上。|你写下自己想读的答案，而不是稳妥的那个。|第一题你没复习。你还是答了，大胆地从基本原理推起。|你和沙漏赛跑，一直写到最后一粒沙落下，还多写了几笔。}\n\n@event track_warning\n# en: A Word About Your Attendance\ntitle [6bd5a3] = 关于你的出勤\n# en: A note about {track}: your attendance and your efforts have slipped, and it has been noticed. One more term like this and there will be consequences.\ntext [2da6ae] = 一张关于{track}的便条：你的出勤和努力都在下滑，有人注意到了。再这样一个学期，就要有后果了。\n# en: A folded note on official paper, the words \"performance\" and \"concern\" underlined twice.\nscene [7cac89] = 一张折起来的公文纸，“表现”和“关切”两个词下面各划了两道线。\n# en: Promise to do better\nopt.left.label [cba502] = 保证会改进\n# en: You write back a promise in your neatest hand. Now you'll have to keep it.\nopt.left.say.1 [5983e6] = 你用最工整的字回了一份保证。现在你得兑现它了。\n# en: Let it slide\nopt.right.label [344baa] = 随它去\n# en: You put the note in a drawer and skip the next session to prove a point. The note doesn't stop existing in the drawer.\nopt.right.say.1 [4ea3c0] = 你把便条塞进抽屉，还故意翘掉了下一次，以示态度。便条在抽屉里也照样存在。\n\n@event job_term_review\n# en: Another Term?\ntitle [9e843b] = 再干一学期？\n# en: The term is over, and so is the rota for {track}. A fresh one goes up for next term, and there's a gap where your name usually goes. {~Nobody would blame you either way.|Your feet have an opinion. So does your purse.|Someone has already pencilled a question mark next to it.}\ntext [7bad94] = 学期结束了，{track}的排班表也到头了。下学期的新表会贴出来，你平常写名字的地方空着一格。{~怎么选都没人会怪你。|你的脚有意见。你的钱袋也有。|已经有人在旁边用铅笔画了个问号。}\n# en: A staff rota pinned to a door, last term's names crossed off, a blank column waiting for next term's.\nscene [2d74da] = 门上钉着一张员工排班表，上学期的名字都划掉了，空出一栏等着下学期。\n# en: Stay on next term\nopt.left.label [7e4424] = 下学期继续干\n# en: {~You write your name in the gap. Another term of shifts, another term of wages.|Your name goes back on the rota, in ink this time.|You rub out the question mark and write your name over it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [007bb9] = {~你在空格里写上名字。又一学期的班，又一学期的工钱。|你的名字回到了排班表上，这次用的是墨水。|你擦掉问号，在上面写上了自己的名字。}\n# en: Resign\nopt.right.label [b07941] = 辞职\n# en: {~You hand in your notice, politely. The evenings are yours again; the purse is lighter for it.|You leave the gap empty. You can always find work again, if you need to.|You leave the question mark where it is. Someone else can answer it.}\nopt.right.say.1 [41ee3c] = {~你客客气气地递了辞呈。晚上又归你了；钱袋也因此轻了。|你让那一格空着。真需要的话，总还能再找活干。|你让那个问号留在原处。让别人去回答吧。}\n\n@event track_promotion\n# en: A Step Up\ntitle [39f03f] = 更上一层\n# en: {track} wants to promote you. More responsibility, more respect, and in the right places, more pay.\ntext [a932cb] = {track}想提拔你。更多责任，更多尊重，在某些地方，还有更多薪水。\n# en: A small ceremony in a cramped room, someone pinning a badge to a student's collar while the others applaud.\nscene [c03b55] = 狭小房间里的一场小仪式，有人把徽章别在一名学生的领口上，其他人鼓掌。\n# en: Accept\nopt.left.label [b26149] = 接受\n# en: The badge is a little crooked. You don't straighten it.\nopt.left.say.1 [7c4086] = 徽章有点歪。你没去扶正它。\n# en: Not yet\nopt.right.label [8dd27c] = 还不是时候\n# en: \"When you're ready, then.\" They mean it. The offer will come again.\nopt.right.say.1 [ffa0ad] = “那就等你准备好。”他们是认真的。这个机会还会再来。\n\n@event intern_application\n# en: Applications Open\ntitle [e7df06] = 开始申请\n# en: Summer research posts are open. Six notices, six seals, six lists of requirements. Apply to as many as you like: the first letter is an evening's work, and every one after it costs another evening and the postage.\ntext [c4837d] = 夏季研究岗位开放申请了。六张告示，六枚火漆，六份要求清单。想申请几个都行：第一封信花你一个晚上，之后每多一封，就再搭上一个晚上和邮费。\n# en: A cluster of notices with wax seals pinned above all the others: summer research posts, each with its own demands.\nscene [a45b3c] = 一簇盖着火漆的告示钉在所有告示上方：夏季研究岗位，各有各的要求。\n# en: The Royal Library\nopt.royal.label [64f580] = 皇家图书馆\n# en: GPA 2.5, an interview on lore, a love of indexes.\nopt.royal.hint [fbd651] = GPA 2.5，学识面试，以及对索引的热爱。\n# en: {member(intern_royal_library): They've already asked you back.|Needs Astromancy II.}\nopt.royal.locked [c8e0b8] = {member(intern_royal_library): 他们已经请你回去了。|需要星象与占卜 II。}\n# en: {~Another letter, another evening, another stamp. Your hand aches in a very particular place.|You write it once, cross out half, write it again. The postage comes out of your own purse.}\nopt.royal.say.1 [69905a] = {~又一封信，又一个晚上，又一张邮票。你的手在一个非常特定的地方发酸。|你写了一遍，划掉一半，又写一遍。邮费从你自己的钱袋里出。}\n# en: You write the letter three times and send the best one. Probably the best one.\nopt.royal.say.2 [b67a70] = 信你写了三遍，寄出了最好的一封。大概是最好的一封。\n# en: You write the letter, and then find you can't pay the postage. It stays on your desk.\nopt.royal.say.3 [ccb58d] = 你写好了信，却发现付不起邮费。它只能留在你桌上。\n# en: The Conclave Laboratory\nopt.conclave.label [858e84] = 七塔议会实验室\n# en: GPA 3.0, an interview on arcana, and a word from Thorne helps.\nopt.conclave.hint [fd28c9] = GPA 3.0，奥术面试，有索恩的一句推荐会更好。\n# en: {member(intern_conclave): They've already asked you back.|Needs Evocation II.}\nopt.conclave.locked [fb2c57] = {member(intern_conclave): 他们已经请你回去了。|需要塑能术 II。}\n# en: {~Another letter, another evening, another stamp. Your hand aches in a very particular place.|You write it once, cross out half, write it again. The postage comes out of your own purse.}\nopt.conclave.say.1 [69905a] = {~又一封信，又一个晚上，又一张邮票。你的手在一个非常特定的地方发酸。|你写了一遍，划掉一半，又写一遍。邮费从你自己的钱袋里出。}\n# en: You write the letter three times and send the best one. Probably the best one.\nopt.conclave.say.2 [b67a70] = 信你写了三遍，寄出了最好的一封。大概是最好的一封。\n# en: You write the letter, and then find you can't pay the postage. It stays on your desk.\nopt.conclave.say.3 [ccb58d] = 你写好了信，却发现付不起邮费。它只能留在你桌上。\n# en: The Guild of Alchemists' Works\nopt.alchemists.label [80cc20] = 炼金师公会工坊\n# en: GPA 2.0, an interview on alchemy, and a strong stomach.\nopt.alchemists.hint [2d4395] = GPA 2.0，炼金面试，还得有个好胃口。\n# en: {member(intern_alchemists): They've already asked you back.|Needs Alchemy II.}\nopt.alchemists.locked [e156fa] = {member(intern_alchemists): 他们已经请你回去了。|需要炼金与治愈 II。}\n# en: {~Another letter, another evening, another stamp. Your hand aches in a very particular place.|You write it once, cross out half, write it again. The postage comes out of your own purse.}\nopt.alchemists.say.1 [69905a] = {~又一封信，又一个晚上，又一张邮票。你的手在一个非常特定的地方发酸。|你写了一遍，划掉一半，又写一遍。邮费从你自己的钱袋里出。}\n# en: You write the letter three times and send the best one. Probably the best one.\nopt.alchemists.say.2 [b67a70] = 信你写了三遍，寄出了最好的一封。大概是最好的一封。\n# en: You write the letter, and then find you can't pay the postage. It stays on your desk.\nopt.alchemists.say.3 [ccb58d] = 你写好了信，却发现付不起邮费。它只能留在你桌上。\n# en: The Observatory Expedition\nopt.expedition.label [a8e59b] = 天文台远征队\n# en: An interview on vigor, and the legs for a plateau.\nopt.expedition.hint [6f139e] = 体魄面试，外加能爬上高原的双腿。\n# en: {member(intern_expedition): They've already asked you back.|Needs Astromancy II.}\nopt.expedition.locked [f35bc6] = {member(intern_expedition): 他们已经请你回去了。|需要星象与占卜 II。}\n# en: {~Another letter, another evening, another stamp. Your hand aches in a very particular place.|You write it once, cross out half, write it again. The postage comes out of your own purse.}\nopt.expedition.say.1 [69905a] = {~又一封信，又一个晚上，又一张邮票。你的手在一个非常特定的地方发酸。|你写了一遍，划掉一半，又写一遍。邮费从你自己的钱袋里出。}\n# en: You write the letter three times and send the best one. Probably the best one.\nopt.expedition.say.2 [b67a70] = 信你写了三遍，寄出了最好的一封。大概是最好的一封。\n# en: You write the letter, and then find you can't pay the postage. It stays on your desk.\nopt.expedition.say.3 [ccb58d] = 你写好了信，却发现付不起邮费。它只能留在你桌上。\n# en: The Beastcraft Field Station\nopt.field.label [93e08b] = 驭兽野外站\n# en: An interview on vigor. Boots provided, mostly.\nopt.field.hint [c9d453] = 体魄面试。靴子基本上会发。\n# en: {member(intern_field_station): They've already asked you back.|Needs Beastcraft II.}\nopt.field.locked [5a1cba] = {member(intern_field_station): 他们已经请你回去了。|需要驭兽与飞行 II。}\n# en: {~Another letter, another evening, another stamp. Your hand aches in a very particular place.|You write it once, cross out half, write it again. The postage comes out of your own purse.}\nopt.field.say.1 [69905a] = {~又一封信，又一个晚上，又一张邮票。你的手在一个非常特定的地方发酸。|你写了一遍，划掉一半，又写一遍。邮费从你自己的钱袋里出。}\n# en: You write the letter three times and send the best one. Probably the best one.\nopt.field.say.2 [b67a70] = 信你写了三遍，寄出了最好的一封。大概是最好的一封。\n# en: You write the letter, and then find you can't pay the postage. It stays on your desk.\nopt.field.say.3 [ccb58d] = 你写好了信，却发现付不起邮费。它只能留在你桌上。\n# en: The Thornwick Exchange\nopt.thornwick.label [eb19f0] = 荆木学院交换项目\n# en: GPA 2.5, an interview on charm. Bring a thick skin.\nopt.thornwick.hint [5c642d] = GPA 2.5，魅力面试。脸皮得厚一点。\n# en: {member(intern_thornwick): They've already asked you back.|Needs Illusion II.}\nopt.thornwick.locked [9eb17a] = {member(intern_thornwick): 他们已经请你回去了。|需要幻术与魅影 II。}\n# en: {~Another letter, another evening, another stamp. Your hand aches in a very particular place.|You write it once, cross out half, write it again. The postage comes out of your own purse.}\nopt.thornwick.say.1 [69905a] = {~又一封信，又一个晚上，又一张邮票。你的手在一个非常特定的地方发酸。|你写了一遍，划掉一半，又写一遍。邮费从你自己的钱袋里出。}\n# en: You write the letter three times and send the best one. Probably the best one.\nopt.thornwick.say.2 [b67a70] = 信你写了三遍，寄出了最好的一封。大概是最好的一封。\n# en: You write the letter, and then find you can't pay the postage. It stays on your desk.\nopt.thornwick.say.3 [ccb58d] = 你写好了信，却发现付不起邮费。它只能留在你桌上。\n# en: Not this year\nopt.none.label [594ecf] = 今年算了\n# en: You let the notices flap. There's always next year, until there isn't.\nopt.none.say.1 [cd2444] = 你任由告示在风里拍打。总还有明年——直到没有为止。\n\n@event intern_offer\n# en: Letters With Seals\ntitle [51ad68] = 盖着火漆的信\n# en: The spring post brings answers. {applied(internship): There are sealed letters on your desk. Which do you open first?|There are no more sealed letters.} {guile >= 35 and rel(isolde) >= 6 and not member(intern_unseen_office): There is also a plain grey envelope you never asked for.|}\ntext [7b4927] = 春天的邮件带来了回音。{applied(internship): 你的书桌上放着几封封了火漆的信。先拆哪一封？|再没有封着火漆的信了。}{guile >= 35 and rel(isolde) >= 6 and not member(intern_unseen_office): 还有一只你从没申请过的素灰色信封。|}\n# en: Heavy envelopes with wax seals on a dormitory desk, one of them plain grey with no seal at all.\nscene [28d6f5] = 宿舍书桌上放着几只封着火漆的厚信封，其中一只是素灰色的，根本没有封漆。\n# en: Letter from the Royal Library\nopt.royal.label [04415c] = 皇家图书馆的来信\n# en: They asked for a GPA of 2.5. The interview tests lore.\nopt.royal.hint [693412] = 他们要求 GPA 2.5。面试考学识。\n# en: \"We are pleased.\" You read the letter until the seal cracks. A summer in the oldest archives in the realm.\nopt.royal.say.1 [d43827] = “我们很高兴。”你把信读了一遍又一遍，直到火漆裂开。一整个夏天，待在王国最古老的档案馆里。\n# en: \"We regret.\" The interview went well, they write, and not quite well enough.\nopt.royal.say.2 [4db135] = “我们很遗憾。”信上说，你的面试表现不错，只是还差一点。\n# en: \"We regret.\" They wanted a GPA of 2.5, and they say so twice.\nopt.royal.say.3 [ee2e12] = “我们很遗憾。”他们要的是 GPA 2.5，而且说了两遍。\n# en: Letter from the Conclave\nopt.conclave.label [187867] = 七塔议会的来信\n# en: They asked for a GPA of 3.0. The interview tests arcana. A word from Thorne would help.\nopt.conclave.hint [e87947] = 他们要求 GPA 3.0。面试考奥术。有索恩的一句推荐会更好。\n# en: \"We are pleased.\" You read the letter until the seal cracks. A summer in spell research under the Seven Towers.\nopt.conclave.say.1 [b0dd08] = “我们很高兴。”你把信读了一遍又一遍，直到火漆裂开。一整个夏天，在七塔之下研究法术。\n# en: \"We regret.\" The interview went well, they write, and not quite well enough.\nopt.conclave.say.2 [4db135] = “我们很遗憾。”信上说，你的面试表现不错，只是还差一点。\n# en: \"We are pleased.\" You read the letter until the seal cracks. A summer in spell research under the Seven Towers.\nopt.conclave.say.3 [b0dd08] = “我们很高兴。”你把信读了一遍又一遍，直到火漆裂开。一整个夏天，在七塔之下研究法术。\n# en: \"We regret.\" The interview went well, they write, and not quite well enough.\nopt.conclave.say.4 [4db135] = “我们很遗憾。”信上说，你的面试表现不错，只是还差一点。\n# en: \"We regret.\" They wanted a GPA of 3.0, and they say so twice.\nopt.conclave.say.5 [fa762a] = “我们很遗憾。”他们要的是 GPA 3.0，而且说了两遍。\n# en: Letter from the Alchemists' Works\nopt.alchemists.label [3ad0f0] = 炼金师工坊的来信\n# en: They asked for a GPA of 2.0. The interview tests alchemy.\nopt.alchemists.hint [0b2080] = 他们要求 GPA 2.0。面试考炼金。\n# en: \"We are pleased.\" You read the letter until the seal cracks. A summer in the great vats of the Guild.\nopt.alchemists.say.1 [d15c64] = “我们很高兴。”你把信读了一遍又一遍，直到火漆裂开。一整个夏天，守着公会的大缸。\n# en: \"We regret.\" The interview went well, they write, and not quite well enough.\nopt.alchemists.say.2 [4db135] = “我们很遗憾。”信上说，你的面试表现不错，只是还差一点。\n# en: \"We regret.\" They wanted a GPA of 2.0, and they say so twice.\nopt.alchemists.say.3 [1f5965] = “我们很遗憾。”他们要的是 GPA 2.0，而且说了两遍。\n# en: Letter from the Expedition\nopt.expedition.label [ca58f7] = 远征队的来信\n# en: The interview tests vigor.\nopt.expedition.hint [0bd21d] = 面试考体魄。\n# en: \"We are pleased.\" You read the letter until the seal cracks. A summer of sky charts on the high plateau.\nopt.expedition.say.1 [9d271b] = “我们很高兴。”你把信读了一遍又一遍，直到火漆裂开。一整个夏天，在高原上绘制星图。\n# en: \"We regret.\" The interview went well, they write, and not quite well enough.\nopt.expedition.say.2 [4db135] = “我们很遗憾。”信上说，你的面试表现不错，只是还差一点。\n# en: Letter from the Field Station\nopt.field.label [b25fe8] = 野外站的来信\n# en: The interview tests vigor.\nopt.field.hint [0bd21d] = 面试考体魄。\n# en: \"We are pleased.\" You read the letter until the seal cracks. A summer in the moors, the heather and the creatures in them.\nopt.field.say.1 [27ceb5] = “我们很高兴。”你把信读了一遍又一遍，直到火漆裂开。一整个夏天，在荒原和石楠丛里，与那里的生灵为伴。\n# en: \"We regret.\" The interview went well, they write, and not quite well enough.\nopt.field.say.2 [4db135] = “我们很遗憾。”信上说，你的面试表现不错，只是还差一点。\n# en: Letter from Thornwick\nopt.thornwick.label [c344dc] = 荆木学院的来信\n# en: They asked for a GPA of 2.5. The interview tests charm.\nopt.thornwick.hint [5c32c3] = 他们要求 GPA 2.5。面试考魅力。\n# en: \"We are pleased.\" You read the letter until the seal cracks. A summer in the rival academy's illusion laboratory.\nopt.thornwick.say.1 [64ec58] = “我们很高兴。”你把信读了一遍又一遍，直到火漆裂开。一整个夏天，待在对手学院的幻术实验室里。\n# en: \"We regret.\" The interview went well, they write, and not quite well enough.\nopt.thornwick.say.2 [4db135] = “我们很遗憾。”信上说，你的面试表现不错，只是还差一点。\n# en: \"We regret.\" They wanted a GPA of 2.5, and they say so twice.\nopt.thornwick.say.3 [ee2e12] = “我们很遗憾。”他们要的是 GPA 2.5，而且说了两遍。\n# en: Open the plain envelope\nopt.unseen.label [ca484a] = 拆开那只素信封\n# en: Inside: a time, an address and no signature. The paper is blank again by morning. You go anyway.\nopt.unseen.say.1 [702952] = 里面写着一个时间、一个地址，没有署名。到第二天早上，纸又变成了白纸。你还是去了。\n# en: Fold the letters away\nopt.decline.label [15c522] = 把信都收起来\n# en: You fold them carefully. Next year, perhaps.\nopt.decline.say.1 [b0ac8d] = 你把信仔细折好。也许明年吧。\n\n@event intern_assessment\n# en: The Supervisor's Report\ntitle [d9d7b2] = 导师的评语\n# en: Your last day at {member(intern_royal_library): the Royal Library Archives|{member(intern_conclave): the Conclave Laboratory|{member(intern_alchemists): the Guild of Alchemists' Works|{member(intern_expedition): the Observatory Expedition|{member(intern_field_station): the Beastcraft Field Station|{member(intern_thornwick): the Thornwick Exchange|the office with no name}}}}}}. Your supervisor turns the report over. {performance(internship) >= 80: \"Exceptional,\" it says at the top, underlined, and the rest is better.|{performance(internship) >= 60: \"Reliable and capable.\" A few notes in the margin, all of them fair.|{performance(internship) >= 40: \"Adequate.\" The word sits on the page by itself.|\"Did not meet expectations.\" Your supervisor doesn't quite look at you.}}}\ntext [b597e5] = 这是你在{member(intern_royal_library): 皇家图书馆档案馆|{member(intern_conclave): 七塔议会实验室|{member(intern_alchemists): 炼金师公会工坊|{member(intern_expedition): 观星远征队|{member(intern_field_station): 驭兽术野外站|{member(intern_thornwick): 荆木学院交换项目|那间没有名字的办公室}}}}}}的最后一天。导师把评语翻了过来。{performance(internship) >= 80: 最上面写着“出类拔萃”，还画了一道线，下面写得更好。|{performance(internship) >= 60: “可靠，能干。”页边有几条批注，条条中肯。|{performance(internship) >= 40: “尚可。”纸上孤零零就这两个字。|“未达预期。”导师的目光有点躲着你。}}}\n# en: A supervisor's desk on the last day of a placement, a written report face down under a pen.\nscene [8b3d61] = 实习最后一天，导师的书桌上，一份手写评语扣在桌面，上面压着一支笔。\n# en: Ask for a letter of reference\nopt.left.label [333892] = 请导师写一封推荐信\n# en: The letter comes a week later, sealed, and the registrar files a copy with a look you haven't had from her before.\nopt.left.say.1 [dc6c0f] = 一周后信到了，封得严严实实。教务员把副本归档时，看你的眼神是你从没见过的。\n# en: The letter comes a month later: two lines, your name spelled wrong in one of them. You put it at the bottom of a drawer.\nopt.left.say.2 [cc9272] = 一个月后信才到：短短两行，其中一行还把你的名字拼错了。你把它塞进了抽屉最底下。\n# en: Ask what you could have done better\nopt.right.label [af80e6] = 问问自己哪里还能做得更好\n# en: Your supervisor talks for twenty minutes without notes. Some of it stings. You write all of it down on the coach home, and read it twice.\nopt.right.say.1 [20ff3d] = 导师不看笔记，一口气讲了二十分钟。有些话很刺耳。回程的马车上你把它们全都记了下来，又读了两遍。\n\n@event intern_asked_back\n# en: Asked Back\ntitle [8aa9d6] = 再次相邀\n# en: A letter from {member(intern_royal_library): the Royal Library Archives|{member(intern_conclave): the Conclave Laboratory|{member(intern_alchemists): the Guild of Alchemists' Works|{member(intern_expedition): the Observatory Expedition|{member(intern_field_station): the Beastcraft Field Station|{member(intern_thornwick): the Thornwick Exchange|the office with no name}}}}}}. They were pleased with your summer, and the post is yours again next summer if you want it.\ntext [92bde8] = 一封来自{member(intern_royal_library): 皇家图书馆档案馆|{member(intern_conclave): 七塔议会实验室|{member(intern_alchemists): 炼金师公会工坊|{member(intern_expedition): 观星远征队|{member(intern_field_station): 驭兽术野外站|{member(intern_thornwick): 荆木学院交换项目|那间没有名字的办公室}}}}}}的信。他们对你这个夏天很满意，只要你愿意，明年夏天这个岗位还是你的。\n# en: A letter on good paper with a familiar seal, the words \"next summer\" underlined once, neatly.\nscene [522edb] = 一封用上好纸张写的信，盖着熟悉的火漆，“明年夏天”几个字下面工整地划了一道线。\n# en: Write back yes\nopt.left.label [622f58] = 回信答应\n# en: {~You write back the same evening. Next summer is spoken for, and you find you're glad.|\"Yes,\" you write, and then three more lines to make it sound less eager.}\nopt.left.say.1 [71669f] = {~你当晚就回了信。明年夏天有着落了，你发现自己挺高兴。|你写下“好的”，又添了三行，好让自己显得没那么迫不及待。}\n# en: Thank them, and decline\nopt.right.label [1b8626] = 致谢，婉拒\n# en: {~You thank them warmly and say no. Next summer is yours to spend, or to apply for something new.|A polite no. You keep the letter anyway; it's good to be asked.}\nopt.right.say.1 [a75a4d] = {~你热情地道谢，然后说不。明年夏天由你自己安排，或者去申请些新的。|一句礼貌的拒绝。信你还是留着了；被人邀请总是好事。}\n\n@event thesis_setup\n# en: Choosing a Thesis\ntitle [ed9c10] = 选定论文\n# en: The final year opens with a thesis. Before you choose this term's courses, Professor Thorne lays out three topics, at three levels of ambition.\ntext [c76290] = 最后一年以毕业论文开场。在你选定本学期的课程之前，索恩教授摆出了三个题目，野心各不相同。\n# en: A professor's study, three topic proposals laid out on the desk like cards.\nscene [82a455] = 教授的书房，三份选题提案像纸牌一样摊在桌上。\n# en: A careful survey\nopt.safe.label [d4d296] = 一篇稳妥的综述\n# en: Easier, and it will read as easier.\nopt.safe.hint [8d81c3] = 更容易，读起来也会显得更容易。\n# en: A sensible topic. Thorne says \"Sensible,\" the way other people say \"Disappointing.\"\nopt.safe.say.1 [5a3672] = 一个明智的题目。索恩说“明智”的语气，就像别人说“失望”。\n# en: An original study\nopt.solid.label [0d9f34] = 一项原创研究\n# en: The usual path.\nopt.solid.hint [8adec9] = 寻常的路。\n# en: Thorne nods. \"Good. Bring me something I don't already know.\"\nopt.solid.say.1 [9feb76] = 索恩点点头。“好。给我带来点我还不知道的东西。”\n# en: Something no one has done\nopt.ambitious.label [326da2] = 前无古人的课题\n# en: Harder, and worth more.\nopt.ambitious.hint [83c12d] = 更难，也更有分量。\n# en: Thorne is quiet for a long time. \"Hm,\" he says, and for once it sounds like hope.\nopt.ambitious.say.1 [18b887] = 索恩沉默了很久。“嗯，”他说，这一次听起来竟像是希望。\n\n@event thesis_crunch\n# en: The Crunch\ntitle [5ad669] = 最后冲刺\n# en: The defence is next month and your thesis is not ready. Your advisor's note is three words long: \"Well? Final draft.\"\ntext [db90b3] = 答辩就在下个月，你的论文还没写完。导师的便条只有短短一句：\"如何？交终稿。\"\n# en: A thesis carrel at dawn, a candle burned to a stub, drafts on the floor and a student asleep on the keyboard of a typing-quill.\nscene [d2cdf9] = 黎明时分的论文隔间，蜡烛燃成了残根，草稿散落一地，一名学生趴在打字羽毛笔的键盘上睡着了。\n# en: Work through the nights\nopt.left.label [ec1e9d] = 通宵赶工\n# en: You stop seeing daylight. The draft gets better and you get worse, which seems to be how this works.\nopt.left.say.1 [2f566c] = 你再也见不到日光。稿子越改越好，你却越来越糟，事情大概就是这样。\n# en: Hand in what you have\nopt.right.label [648c9a] = 有多少交多少\n# en: You tidy what's there and accept the gaps. Your advisor sighs, which is not the same as no.\nopt.right.say.1 [5cc846] = 你把现有的整理好，接受那些空缺。导师叹了口气——叹气不等于拒绝。\n\n@event thesis_defence\n# en: The Defence\ntitle [3a2f48] = 答辩\n# en: Three examiners, one lectern, and a year of work. {progress(thesis) >= 60: You know this material better than anyone in the room.|There are gaps, and you know where they are.} First, you present. Then they ask.\ntext [9ab052] = 三位考官，一座讲台，一年的心血。{progress(thesis) >= 60: 这些内容你比屋里任何人都熟。|有漏洞，而且你知道它们在哪儿。} 先是你陈述。然后他们提问。\n# en: A semicircular table with three examiners, a student standing at a lectern with a stack of notes.\nscene [f8606f] = 半圆形的长桌后坐着三位考官，一名学生站在讲台前，手里一摞笔记。\n# en: Defend it\nopt.left.label [0249ce] = 为它辩护\n# en: A safe topic is easier to defend and earns less; an ambitious one is harder and earns more. A year of thesis work counts.\nopt.left.hint [fa3c91] = 稳妥的题目更好答辩，回报也更少；大胆的题目更难，回报也更多。一年的论文功夫也会算数。\n# en: Your presentation runs clean from first slide to last. One examiner has stopped taking notes and is just listening.\nopt.left.say.1 [f074e0] = 你的陈述从头到尾干净利落。一位考官已经不再记笔记，只是在听。\n# en: A solid presentation with one wobble in the middle. You recover. They noticed the wobble.\nopt.left.say.2 [c4010c] = 一次扎实的陈述，中间晃了一下。你稳住了。他们注意到了那一下。\n# en: You lose your place twice and your nerve once. The examiners exchange a look you'll remember for years.\nopt.left.say.3 [6f66d2] = 你两次讲丢了位置，一次丢了胆子。考官们交换了一个你会记很多年的眼神。\n# en: A careful survey, carefully presented. Nobody could fault a word of it. Nobody looks thrilled, either.\nopt.left.say.4 [867002] = 一份严谨的综述，严谨地陈述。一个字都挑不出错。也没人显得激动。\n# en: Careful, competent and a little dull, and you can see one examiner fighting a yawn.\nopt.left.say.5 [02b389] = 严谨、称职，还有点乏味，你能看见一位考官在忍住哈欠。\n# en: Even a safe topic has its gaps, and your presentation walks straight into one of them.\nopt.left.say.6 [b10848] = 稳妥的题目也有漏洞，而你的陈述径直走进了其中一个。\n# en: Something no one has done, laid out so clearly they can't look away. One examiner has put down their pen.\nopt.left.say.7 [33edc7] = 前人从未做过的事，讲得清清楚楚，让他们移不开眼睛。一位考官放下了笔。\n# en: They follow you most of the way, arguing among themselves about whether it's brilliant or mad. Possibly both.\nopt.left.say.8 [c3424d] = 他们大半程都跟上了你，彼此争论这究竟是天才还是疯狂。也许两者都是。\n# en: You reach too far in the first ten minutes, and spend the rest of the hour trying to get back. They let you.\nopt.left.say.9 [8a9009] = 你在头十分钟就走得太远，剩下的一个小时都在设法往回走。他们由着你。\n# en: And under the presentation, a year of work they can feel. Every page they turn has been turned before.\nopt.left.say.10 [90a675] = 陈述之下，是他们能感觉到的一整年功夫。他们翻过的每一页，你都早已翻过。\n# en: Under the presentation, the thin chapters show. One examiner starts counting pages.\nopt.left.say.11 [cbd512] = 陈述之下，单薄的章节露了馅。一位考官开始数页数。\n# en: Open with your weakest chapter\nopt.right.label [dd91ef] = 从最薄弱的一章讲起\n# en: Name the gaps before they find them. Safe, and modest.\nopt.right.hint [047c2c] = 在他们发现之前先说出漏洞。稳妥，也平淡。\n# en: You start with what you couldn't prove, and why, and what you'd do next. It takes the wind out of their sails, and a little out of yours.\nopt.right.say.1 [e57cc7] = 你从无法证明的部分讲起，讲为什么，讲下一步会怎么做。这让他们泄了气，也让你泄了一点气。\n# en: The rest of it stands on a year of real work, and they can tell.\nopt.right.say.2 [f4fa42] = 其余部分立在一整年扎实的功夫上，他们看得出来。\n# en: The trouble is how many chapters are weak. They notice that too.\nopt.right.say.3 [3088c9] = 麻烦在于薄弱的章节太多了。这一点他们也注意到了。\n\n@event thesis_defence_question\n# en: The Question\ntitle [b2a4e8] = 那个问题\n# en: The examiner who hasn't spoken yet opens your thesis to a page near the end, puts a finger on one line and asks the question you hoped no one would ask.\ntext [f3f712] = 那位一直没开口的考官把你的论文翻到靠后的一页，手指按在一行字上，问出了你最希望没人问的那个问题。\n# en: An examiner leaning over a semicircular table, pointing at one line in a thick bound thesis, the student at the lectern very still.\nscene [24c9e8] = 一位考官俯身在半圆形的桌子上，指着一本厚厚的装订论文中的一行，讲台旁的学生一动不动。\n# en: Answer it head-on\nopt.left.label [f11e30] = 正面回答\n# en: You answer it, and then the question behind it, and then the one behind that. The examiner closes the thesis, slowly.\nopt.left.say.1 [298ca3] = 你回答了它，然后回答了它背后的问题，然后是再背后的那个。考官慢慢合上了论文。\n# en: Your answer starts well and ends somewhere you didn't mean to go. The examiner lets the silence do the rest.\nopt.left.say.2 [90a716] = 你的回答开头很好，结尾却去了一个你没打算去的地方。考官让沉默替自己说完了剩下的话。\n# en: Admit what you don't know\nopt.right.label [cb596c] = 承认你不知道的\n# en: \"I don't know,\" you say, \"and here is how I'd find out.\" From someone who has just presented so well, it sounds like mastery. Two examiners nod.\nopt.right.say.1 [ce3f4f] = “我不知道，”你说，“但我会这样去弄清楚。”出自一个刚刚陈述得如此出色的人之口，这听起来像是驾轻就熟。两位考官点了点头。\n# en: \"I don't know,\" you say, \"and here is how I'd find out.\" It stings a little, and earns you nothing. The examiner moves on.\nopt.right.say.2 [875e12] = “我不知道，”你说，“但我会这样去弄清楚。”这让你有点难受，也没给你加分。考官继续往下问了。\n\n@event thesis_defence_verdict\n# en: The Verdict\ntitle [5a8455] = 结果\n# en: They send you out. Twenty minutes on a bench in the corridor, with the clock and your heartbeat. Then the door opens, and they call you back in.\ntext [1f091a] = 他们请你出去。在走廊的长椅上等了二十分钟，只有钟声和你的心跳。然后门开了，他们叫你回去。\n# en: A student waiting alone on a bench in a long stone corridor outside a closed oak door, hands clasped, a clock on the wall.\nscene [bef3d0] = 一个学生独自坐在长长的石头走廊里的长椅上，紧闭的橡木门外，双手紧握，墙上挂着一只钟。\n# en: Go back in\nopt.ok.label [b89225] = 回去\n# en: They stop asking questions and start asking for copies. Distinction. One of them shakes your hand twice, as if the first didn't count.\nopt.ok.say.1 [9fca70] = 他们不再提问，开始讨要副本。优等。其中一位和你握了两次手，好像第一次不算数。\n# en: A pass, with one examiner's handwriting all over your conclusion. \"Good work,\" says the chair, and means it, mostly.\nopt.ok.say.2 [efa14a] = 通过了，只是结论部分写满了某位考官的批注。“干得好，”主席说，大体上是真心的。\n# en: They send you away to resubmit. It isn't the end. It feels like it. The idea was good, says the chair. The proof wasn't there yet.\nopt.ok.say.3 [c92bcd] = 他们让你回去重交。这不是终点。只是感觉像。主席说，想法是好的。只是证明还没到位。\n\n@event lantern_night_v3\n# en: Lantern Night\ntitle [0b1f19] = 灯笼夜\n# en: Lantern Night. The whole academy gathers on the lakeshore with paper lanterns, each carrying a wish out over the water. {month == 2 and year == 1: Your first one.|Another year, another wish.}\ntext [b3014a] = 灯笼夜。全院的人带着纸灯笼聚在湖岸，每盏灯都载着一个愿望飘向水面。{month == 2 and year == 1: 这是你的第一次。|又一年，又一个愿望。}\n# en: The Mirror Lake at night, hundreds of paper lanterns drifting over the water, students crowding the shore.\nscene [cd9b4c] = 夜晚的镜湖，数百盏纸灯笼在水面上漂，学生们挤满湖岸。\n# en: Wish for something selfish\nopt.left.label [7e0393] = 许一个自私的愿望\n# en: Your lantern goes out over the water and keeps its secret. You feel lighter for having asked.\nopt.left.say.1 [0210ff] = 你的灯笼飘向湖面，守着它的秘密。问出口之后，你觉得轻松了些。\n# en: Wish for someone else\nopt.right.label [bc6686] = 为别人许愿\n# en: You don't tell anyone who. The lantern seems to know; it flies straight.\nopt.right.say.1 [00b286] = 你没告诉任何人是谁。灯笼似乎知道，它飞得笔直。\n\n@event year_four\n# en: The Final Year\ntitle [a30775] = 最后一年\n# en: The barge again, the towers again, and a thesis waiting. The first-years on the deck look at you the way you once looked at the fourth-years. At the opening feast the headmistress says, \"Fourth years, this is your last year in these halls. What you become here, you will be for a long time.\" She looks, for a moment, directly at you.\ntext [cb5447] = 又是驳船，又是高塔，还有一篇论文在等着。甲板上的一年级生看你的眼神，就像你当年看四年级生那样。开学宴上，院长说：“四年级的同学们，这是你们在这些厅堂里的最后一年。你们在这里成为什么样的人，就会长久地是那样的人。”有那么一刻，她直直地看着你。\n# en: The seven towers at dawn from the barge, the same view as the first day, but the student watching it has changed.\nscene [542188] = 从驳船上望见的黎明七塔，和第一天的景色一样，但看风景的学生已经变了。\n# en: Here we go\nopt.ok.label [e1aa95] = 走吧\n# en: You know which steps creak and which doors stick. The thesis will want evenings of its own each term; something else may have to give, a club or a job.\nopt.ok.say.1 [e66c41] = 你知道哪级台阶会响，哪扇门会卡。毕业论文每学期都要占去好些晚上；总得放下点什么，一个社团，或者一份工作。\n\n@event grade_appeal\n# en: The Appeal\ntitle [83680b] = 申诉\n# en: The examiners' list is pinned up, and beside {track} there is an F. The clerk mentions, without looking up, that a student may appeal a grade in person, once, before the board. Most don't. Some of those who do come out with a C.\ntext [dc09dc] = 考官名单贴出来了，{track}旁边是一个 F。办事员头也不抬地提了一句：学生可以亲自向委员会申诉一次成绩。大多数人不去。去了的人里，有些拿回了一个 C。\n# en: A long table of grey-robed examiners in a panelled room, a single student standing before them holding an exam paper.\nscene [ba2029] = 镶着护墙板的房间里，一长排身穿灰袍的考官坐在桌后，一个学生拿着考卷独自站在他们面前。\n# en: Appeal before the board\nopt.left.label [fecb7e] = 向委员会申诉\n# en: You talk for ten minutes about the paper, the question and the answer they marked wrong. The board confers in whispers. The F becomes a C, in red ink, with a sigh.\nopt.left.say.1 [20d3bd] = 你就那张考卷、那道题和那个被判错的答案讲了十分钟。委员会低声商议。F 变成了 C，红墨水写的，附带一声叹气。\n# en: The board hears you out, then reads your paper aloud. It's worse aloud. The F stands, and the queue outside the door heard every word.\nopt.left.say.2 [63e52e] = 委员会听完你的话，然后把你的考卷念了一遍。念出来更糟。F 维持原判，门外排队的人一字不落全听见了。\n# en: Accept it, and read the comments\nopt.right.label [b758f4] = 接受成绩，读读评语\n# en: The examiner's comments are fair, which is the worst thing about them. You copy out every one. Next time you'll know exactly where it went wrong.\nopt.right.say.1 [1fbf52] = 考官的评语很公道，这正是最糟的地方。你一条一条抄下来。下次你会清楚地知道自己错在哪儿。\n\n@event registrar_list\n# en: The Registrar's List\ntitle [0ca2e7] = 教务处的课程表\n# en: Before enrolment, the registrar's list goes up in the entrance hall: each course, what it tests, which spells it teaches, and which open only next term. A second-year taps one. \"That one's hard,\" she says, then taps another. \"So's that.\"\ntext [f5996f] = 选课前，教务处的课程表贴在了门厅：每门课考什么，教哪些咒语，哪些下学期才开。一个二年级生点了点其中一门。“这门难，”她说，又点了另一门，“这门也难。”\n# en: A crowd of new students around a long parchment list pinned in an entrance hall, one of them tracing a line with a finger.\nscene [ddd05b] = 门厅里一群新生围着一张长长的羊皮纸名单，其中一个用手指顺着一行往下划。\n# en: Read it twice\nopt.ok.label [7de276] = 看两遍\n# en: Two courses a term, a fee each time, and an exam at the end of each. Pass a level and you learn its spells.\nopt.ok.say.1 [fb0418] = 每学期两门课，每次都要交课程费，每学期末各有一场考试。通过一个级别，就能学会它的咒语。\n\n@event club_fair_flyer\n# en: Flyers Under the Door\ntitle [fb904d] = 门缝下的传单\n# en: One flyer slides under your door, then another, then nine. The Club Fair is next month in the courtyard: the Duelling Circle, the Lumen Ledger, the Verdant Circle. One club at a time, says the small print, and each wants a couple of evenings a term.\ntext [f8867e] = 一张传单从门缝下塞进来，又一张，然后是九张。社团招新会下个月在庭院举行：决斗社、《流明纪事报》社、青翠社。小字写着：一次只能加入一个社团，每个社团每学期要占你几个晚上。\n# en: A dormitory floor scattered with colourful club flyers, more sliding under the door as a student watches.\nscene [f482c7] = 宿舍地板上散落着五颜六色的社团传单，还有更多正从门缝下塞进来，一个学生在一旁看着。\n# en: Keep the flyers\nopt.ok.label [e3ac1e] = 把传单留着\n# en: You pin them over your bed and argue with them until you fall asleep.\nopt.ok.say.1 [ce52b4] = 你把它们钉在床头，和它们争论到睡着。\n\n@event job_board_notice\n# en: Hands Wanted\ntitle [82086c] = 招人\n# en: Pinned to the refectory door: \"Hands wanted. See the Job Board, entrance hall.\" Under it, in pencil: \"The Newt pays best. The library is warmest.\" Under that, in a third hand: \"Pell counts.\"\ntext [eb646b] = 膳堂门上钉着：“招人。详见门厅招工告示板。”下面有人用铅笔写：“酒馆给钱最多。图书馆最暖和。”再下面是第三种笔迹：“佩尔要数数。”\n# en: A refectory door with a notice pinned to it, three different handwritings added beneath the printed part.\nscene [ee5aec] = 膳堂门上钉着一张告示，印刷字下面添了三种不同的笔迹。\n# en: Make a note of it\nopt.ok.label [70f916] = 记下来\n# en: One job at a time, a few shifts a term, paid by the shift. The board is up from morning until evening.\nopt.ok.say.1 [611dbe] = 一次只能做一份工，每学期几个班次，按班次付钱。告示板从早上一直开到傍晚。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/calendar.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/calendar.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event intro_arrival\n# en: The Sky-Barge\ntitle [59d00d] = 天空驳船\n# en: The barge drops through the clouds and there it is: Lumenhold, seven towers stitched together with bridges of light. A first-year beside you is quietly being sick over the side. A prefect with a ledger counts heads and frowns at yours.\ntext [402ca7] = 驳船穿过云层下降，它就在那里：流明堡，七座高塔由光之桥缝合在一起。你身旁一个一年级新生正悄悄趴在船舷边呕吐。一位拿着名册的级长在清点人头，数到你时皱了皱眉。\n# en: A wooden barge with patched sails drifting between clouds toward seven impossibly tall towers above a mirror-still lake.\nscene [45ee12] = 一艘打着补丁的木帆驳船在云间漂浮，驶向矗立在镜面般湖泊之上、高得不可思议的七座塔楼。\n# en: Step onto the landing\nopt.ok.label [2c37cd] = 踏上码头\n# en: Your boots touch stone that hums faintly underfoot. Four years. Forty-eight moons.\nopt.ok.say.1 [c9a4d2] = 你的靴子踩上了石板，脚下微微嗡鸣。四年。四十八轮月。\n\n@event choose_origin\n# en: Who Were You Before?\ntitle [3aa3fb] = 来这里之前，你是谁？\n# en: The Sorting Mirror in the entrance hall does not sort you into anything. It simply asks, in a voice like cold water, who you were before you came here. It waits.\ntext [408653] = 门厅里的“分院镜”并不会把你分到哪里去。它只是用冷水般的声音问你：来这里之前，你是谁？然后，它静静等待。\n# en: A tall enchanted mirror in the entrance hall, showing a different version of the viewer in each pane.\nscene [c49f90] = 门厅里一面高大的魔法镜，每一格镜面都映出观者不同的模样。\n# en: A younger child of minor nobility\nopt.noble.label [354ff7] = 小贵族家的小儿女\n# en: Charm, a known name, and a family allowance with strings attached.\nopt.noble.hint [b3c659] = 魅力、响亮的姓氏，还有附带条件的家里零花钱。\n# en: The mirror shows you a crest you have tried to outgrow. It approves, faintly.\nopt.noble.say.1 [5615e9] = 镜中出现一枚你一直想摆脱的家徽。它微微表示认可。\n# en: A provincial scholarship student\nopt.scholar.label [096b86] = 外省的奖学金学生\n# en: Lore above all. The stipend depends on last term's grades.\nopt.scholar.hint [183a1b] = 学识为先。津贴取决于上学期的成绩。\n# en: The mirror shows you a candle burning very late in a very small room.\nopt.scholar.say.1 [e182c1] = 镜中出现一间很小的屋子，一支蜡烛燃到很晚很晚。\n# en: Raised by a marsh hedge-witch\nopt.hedge.label [700715] = 由沼泽乡野女巫养大\n# en: Alchemy, some Arcana, good spirits. No money from home.\nopt.hedge.hint [cb60d3] = 炼金、一点奥术、好心情。家里不寄钱。\n# en: The mirror smells, briefly, of wet moss and woodsmoke. You feel at home.\nopt.hedge.say.1 [734ca9] = 镜子里一瞬间飘出湿苔藓和柴火烟的气味。你觉得像回到了家。\n# en: Raised on the streets of Port Calder\nopt.street.label [5f489c] = 在考尔德港的街头长大\n# en: Guile and grit. Little gold, and no one to send more.\nopt.street.hint [15f42a] = 诡计与韧劲。金币很少，也没人会再寄。\n# en: The mirror shows a pickpocket's quick hands. You check your pockets out of habit.\nopt.street.say.1 [dd71c2] = 镜中出现一双扒手的快手。你习惯性地摸了摸口袋。\n# en: The heir to a trading company\nopt.merchant.label [2fc5e4] = 一家商行的继承人\n# en: The biggest purse and a nose for bargains. The company pays when trade is good.\nopt.merchant.hint [3dffa5] = 最鼓的钱袋，对买卖嗅觉灵敏。生意好时商行会寄钱。\n# en: The mirror shows ledgers, ships and your mother's disappointed face. Still: gold.\nopt.merchant.say.1 [604ea4] = 镜中出现账簿、货船，还有你母亲失望的脸。不过：钱是真的多。\n\n@event sorting_mirror\n# en: The Sorting Mirror\ntitle [6d3a7b] = 分院镜\n# en: The Sorting Mirror in the entrance hall does not sort you into anything. It simply asks, in a voice like cold water, who you were before you came here. You tell it.\ntext [43f613] = 门厅里的“分院镜”并不会把你分到哪里去。它只是用冷水般的声音问你：来这里之前，你是谁？你告诉了它。\n# en: A tall enchanted mirror in the entrance hall, showing a different version of the viewer in each pane.\nscene [c49f90] = 门厅里一面高大的魔法镜，每一格镜面都映出观者不同的模样。\n# en: Look into the mirror\nopt.ok.label [bb5682] = 望向镜中\n# en: {has(origin_noble): The mirror shows you a crest you have tried to outgrow. It approves, faintly.|{has(origin_scholar): The mirror shows you a candle burning very late in a very small room.|{has(origin_hedge): The mirror smells, briefly, of wet moss and woodsmoke. You feel at home.|{has(origin_street): The mirror shows a pickpocket's quick hands. You check your pockets out of habit.|The mirror shows ledgers, ships and your mother's disappointed face. Still: gold.}}}}\nopt.ok.say.1 [8c73e5] = {has(origin_noble): 镜中出现一枚你一直想摆脱的家徽。它微微表示认可。|{has(origin_scholar): 镜中出现一间很小的屋子，一支蜡烛燃到很晚很晚。|{has(origin_hedge): 镜子里一瞬间飘出湿苔藓和柴火烟的气味。你觉得像回到了家。|{has(origin_street): 镜中出现一双扒手的快手。你习惯性地摸了摸口袋。|镜中出现账簿、货船，还有你母亲失望的脸。不过：钱是真的多。}}}}\n\n@event wrapup_begins\n# en: The Last Weeks\ntitle [0438f9] = 最后的几周\n# en: A notice on the great hall door: classes are over, duties are done, and final-years may stay on until graduation, board on the house. The castle is yours for one last summer.\ntext [172a68] = 大礼堂门上贴着一张告示：课程结束，职责已了，毕业生可以留到毕业典礼，食宿全免。最后一个夏天，城堡属于你。\n# en: An emptying castle in late summer light, trunks stacked in a corridor, a notice pinned to the great hall door.\nscene [1cbaa4] = 夏末的阳光里，城堡渐渐空了，走廊里堆着行李箱，大礼堂门上钉着一张告示。\n# en: Make the most of it\nopt.ok.label [793c29] = 好好享受\n# en: You walk the long way to breakfast, past every door you ever got lost behind.\nopt.ok.say.1 [725c27] = 你绕远路去吃早饭，经过每一扇你曾在后面迷过路的门。\n\n@event choose_elective\n# en: Choosing an Elective\ntitle [03e0dd] = 选择选修课\n# en: The registrar's noticeboard flaps with sign-up sheets for this term's electives. Core lectures are compulsory; your elective is yours to choose. The sheets jostle for your attention.\ntext [6ed4b3] = 教务处的告示板上，本学期选修课的报名表哗哗作响。核心课程是必修的；选修课由你自己挑。报名表们推推搡搡，争着吸引你的注意。\n# en: A long noticeboard covered in fluttering enchanted parchment, each sheet trying to shoulder the others aside.\nscene [803e43] = 一块长长的告示板，贴满了飘动的魔法羊皮纸，每一张都想用肩膀把别的挤开。\n# en: Pyromancy\nopt.pyro.label [22dcef] = 火焰术\n# en: +Arcana each month, small burns. Taught by Professor Thorne.\nopt.pyro.hint [cea401] = 每月 +奥术，偶有小烧伤。由索恩教授执教。\n# en: Tidecalling\nopt.tide.label [43908b] = 唤潮术\n# en: +Arcana, +Vigor, lakeside calm.\nopt.tide.hint [a0ea17] = +奥术，+体魄，湖畔的宁静。\n# en: Illusion & Glamour\nopt.illusion.label [5cdf61] = 幻术与魅影\n# en: +Guile, +Charm.\nopt.illusion.hint [21f570] = +诡计，+魅力。\n# en: Practical Alchemy\nopt.alchemy.label [990d4f] = 实用炼金\n# en: +Alchemy.\nopt.alchemy.hint [f7922d] = +炼金。\n# en: Divination\nopt.divination.label [61fc6c] = 占卜学\n# en: +Lore, occasional gloomy omens.\nopt.divination.hint [a946bb] = +学识，偶尔会有阴郁的预兆。\n# en: Artifice\nopt.artifice.label [de3328] = 奇械术\n# en: +Alchemy, +Lore.\nopt.artifice.hint [5dba10] = +炼金，+学识。\n# en: Beastcraft\nopt.beast.label [573a56] = 驭兽学\n# en: +Vigor, +Mood. Smells of hay.\nopt.beast.hint [2aa6d4] = +体魄，+心情。一股干草味。\n# en: Astromancy\nopt.astro.label [65a198] = 星象学\n# en: +Lore, late nights.\nopt.astro.hint [dc29a9] = +学识，经常熬夜。\n# en: Healing Arts\nopt.healing.label [e8c0bd] = 治愈术\n# en: +Alchemy, +Lore, a little health.\nopt.healing.hint [eab2fb] = +炼金，+学识，少量健康。\n# en: Umbral Theory (restricted)\nopt.umbral.label [430c05] = 暗影理论（受限）\n# en: Requires Lore 20 or second year. +Arcana, dark moods.\nopt.umbral.hint [6e81e4] = 需要学识 20 或二年级。+奥术，心情阴沉。\n# en: Needs Lore 20, or your second year.\nopt.umbral.locked [cd9acd] = 需要学识 20，或进入第二年。\n\n@event club_fair\n# en: The Club Fair\ntitle [ce4fee] = 社团招新会\n# en: The courtyard is a riot of banners. Every club wants you, or at least your membership fee. A broom whizzes overhead trailing a SKYRACE TRYOUTS banner; somewhere a choir is harmonising with a cat.\ntext [2193b8] = 庭院里旗帜招展，热闹非凡。每个社团都想要你——至少想要你的会费。一把飞帚从头顶呼啸而过，拖着“天空竞速队招新”的横幅；不知哪里，一支合唱团正在和一只猫合唱。\n# en: The great courtyard filled with banners and stalls: a duelist juggling sparks, a choir floating a foot off the ground, a girl handing out newspapers that shout headlines.\nscene [60c086] = 宏伟的庭院里满是旗帜和摊位：一名决斗者在抛接火花，一支合唱团悬浮在离地一尺的空中，一个女孩在分发会喊出头条的报纸。\n# en: Dueling Circle\nopt.duel.label [db424f] = 决斗社\n# en: +Vigor +Arcana, bruises. Home of the Spring Tournament.\nopt.duel.hint [72c44c] = +体魄 +奥术，满身淤青。春季锦标赛的大本营。\n# en: Choir of the Spheres\nopt.choir.label [41a5f0] = 天球合唱团\n# en: +Charm, +Mood.\nopt.choir.hint [ffc919] = +魅力，+心情。\n# en: Skyrace Squad\nopt.sky.label [b4b285] = 天空竞速队\n# en: +Vigor, crowd glory, crashes.\nopt.sky.hint [c4c7f0] = +体魄，观众的欢呼，以及坠机。\n# en: The Lumen Ledger\nopt.ledger.label [2606ae] = 《流明纪事报》社\n# en: +Guile +Charm. Nessa Quill's student paper.\nopt.ledger.hint [d5542a] = +诡计 +魅力。内莎·奎尔的学生报。\n# en: Verdant Circle\nopt.garden.label [7ac3fd] = 青翠社\n# en: +Alchemy +Mood. Greenhouses and gossip.\nopt.garden.hint [8c86f5] = +炼金 +心情。温室与八卦。\n# en: Stargazers\nopt.stars.label [44dc13] = 观星社\n# en: +Lore +Mood.\nopt.stars.hint [60f956] = +学识 +心情。\n# en: Keep your evenings free\nopt.none.label [418ad6] = 晚上留给自己\n# en: No club (leaves your current club, if any).\nopt.none.hint [6ad50e] = 不参加社团（若已有社团则退出）。\n\n@event job_board\n# en: The Job Board\ntitle [377c1b] = 招工告示板\n# en: Rent is ten crowns a month and your purse is a fragile thing. The board outside the bursar's office offers honest work, and some that is merely work.\ntext [185fc4] = 房租每月十克朗，而你的钱袋是个脆弱的东西。财务处门外的告示板上，有正经的工作，也有仅仅算是工作的工作。\n# en: A cork board outside the bursar's office, papered with notices; one is on fire, one is wriggling.\nscene [bbe568] = 财务处门外的一块软木告示板，贴满了启事；其中一张着了火，另一张在扭动。\n# en: Library Assistant — 10 gold/month\nopt.library.label [dece88] = 图书馆助理——每月 10 金币\n# en: +Lore, a little dull.\nopt.library.hint [1434fc] = +学识，有点无聊。\n# en: The Gilded Newt — 16 gold/month\nopt.tavern.label [13979f] = 镀金蝾螈酒馆——每月 16 金币\n# en: +Charm, tiring evenings in the village.\nopt.tavern.hint [8090e3] = +魅力，镇上累人的夜班。\n# en: Apothecary Clerk — 12 gold/month\nopt.apothecary.label [f748e6] = 药铺店员——每月 12 金币\n# en: Requires Alchemy 12. +Alchemy.\nopt.apothecary.hint [0e1e4f] = 需要炼金 12。+炼金。\n# en: Needs Alchemy 12.\nopt.apothecary.locked [784af2] = 需要炼金 12。\n# en: Familiar Stables — 11 gold/month\nopt.stables.label [8ccd8f] = 魔宠马厩——每月 11 金币\n# en: +Vigor. Things bite.\nopt.stables.hint [cf864a] = +体魄。会被咬。\n# en: First-Year Tutor — 12 gold/month\nopt.tutor.label [7403eb] = 一年级辅导员——每月 12 金币\n# en: Requires Lore 18. +Standing.\nopt.tutor.hint [9594b7] = 需要学识 18。+学业。\n# en: Needs Lore 18.\nopt.tutor.locked [ce2e73] = 需要学识 18。\n# en: Broom Courier — 18 gold/month\nopt.courier.label [142261] = 飞帚信使——每月 18 金币\n# en: Requires Vigor 14. Dangerous.\nopt.courier.hint [981b47] = 需要体魄 14。很危险。\n# en: Needs Vigor 14.\nopt.courier.locked [1f61e8] = 需要体魄 14。\n# en: No job (quit any current one)\nopt.none.label [7a08f4] = 不打工（辞去现有工作）\n\n@event lantern_night\n# en: Lantern Night\ntitle [0b1f19] = 灯笼夜\n# en: On Lantern Night every student writes a wish on a paper lantern and lets it go over the lake. Rumour says the lanterns that sink were wishes the lake wanted for itself.\ntext [608e29] = 灯笼夜里，每个学生都会在纸灯笼上写下一个愿望，让它飘过湖面。传说那些沉下去的灯笼，是湖想要据为己有的愿望。\n# en: Hundreds of paper lanterns rising off the lake at dusk, each with a wish written on it in glowing ink.\nscene [3a7795] = 黄昏时分，数百只纸灯笼从湖面升起，每一只上都用发光的墨水写着一个愿望。\n# en: Wish for something selfish\nopt.left.label [7e0393] = 许一个自私的愿望\n# en: Your lantern rises high. The next morning you find coins in your shoe. The lake is generous, and a little creepy.\nopt.left.say.1 [2f05e5] = 你的灯笼升得很高。第二天早上，你在鞋子里发现了硬币。湖很大方，也有点吓人。\n# en: Your lantern sinks with a hiss. Something under the water now knows what you want.\nopt.left.say.2 [f9d757] = 你的灯笼嘶的一声沉了下去。水下有什么东西，现在知道你想要什么了。\n# en: Wish for someone else\nopt.right.label [bc6686] = 为别人许愿\n# en: Your lantern rises and catches the wind. Across the shore, someone you have never met laughs out loud for no reason.\nopt.right.say.1 [002a2f] = 你的灯笼升起来，乘上了风。湖岸对面，一个你从未见过的人无缘无故地放声大笑。\n\n@event kael_arrives\n# en: The Boy From the North\ntitle [c3bb2e] = 来自北方的少年\n# en: Mid-term, the dining hall doors open and frost crawls across the floor. A boy in a fur coat walks in, takes the empty seat beside you, and says nothing at all. The headmistress introduces him as Kael Dumont, a transfer. She does not say from where.\ntext [fde128] = 学期过半，餐厅的大门打开，寒霜爬过地板。一个穿毛皮大衣的少年走进来，坐在你身旁的空位上，一言不发。院长介绍说他叫凯尔·杜蒙，是转学生。她没说他从哪里来。\n# en: A pale young man in a fur-lined coat standing at the door of the dining hall, frost spreading from his boots across the floor.\nscene [f60e23] = 一个面色苍白的年轻人身穿毛皮衬里的大衣站在餐厅门口，霜花从他的靴底向地板蔓延。\n# en: Leave him be\nopt.left.label [ce4663] = 别打扰他\n# en: You eat in silence. When you leave, your cup of tea has frozen solid.\nopt.left.say.1 [930b83] = 你们沉默地吃完了饭。你离开时，你那杯茶已经冻成了冰坨。\n# en: Offer him the bread\nopt.right.label [494eb6] = 把面包递给他\n# en: He looks at the bread as if no one has offered him anything in a long time. \"...Thank you.\" His voice sounds like wind over snow.\nopt.right.say.1 [6aa70f] = 他看着面包，仿佛很久没人给过他任何东西。“……谢谢。”他的声音像掠过雪原的风。\n\n@event winter_trials\n# en: The Winter Trials\ntitle [01bc52] = 冬季考核\n# en: The Winter Trials. A hundred desks, a hundred floating quills, one very old proctor who may be asleep. The first question asks you to explain the Third Law of Correspondence. The second asks you to demonstrate it.\ntext [f596dd] = 冬季考核。一百张课桌，一百支悬浮的羽毛笔，一位很老很老、可能已经睡着的监考官。第一题要你解释“第三对应定律”。第二题要你当场演示。\n# en: A vast examination hall where floating quills hover over a hundred desks and the windows are thick with frost.\nscene [f46856] = 宽阔的考场里，悬浮的羽毛笔停在一百张课桌上方，窗户上结着厚厚的霜。\n# en: Lean on theory\nopt.left.label [f71caf] = 靠理论取胜\n# en: Your answer is so thorough the quill gets a cramp. Top marks.\nopt.left.say.1 [eb0c7e] = 你的答案详尽到羽毛笔都抽筋了。满分。\n# en: You write three pages and realise halfway that you have been answering a different question.\nopt.left.say.2 [6df893] = 你写了三页，写到一半才发现自己答的是另一道题。\n# en: Lean on practice\nopt.right.label [e24f03] = 靠实践取胜\n# en: Your demonstration is clean and bright. The proctor wakes up and applauds.\nopt.right.say.1 [5963aa] = 你的演示干净利落、光彩夺目。监考官醒过来，鼓起了掌。\n# en: The demonstration backfires. Politely, but with smoke.\nopt.right.say.2 [5c503b] = 演示失败了。很有礼貌地失败了，但冒了烟。\n\n@event midwinter_ball\n# en: The Midwinter Ball\ntitle [81d1d0] = 仲冬舞会\n# en: The great hall has been turned to ice and starlight for the Midwinter Ball. Couples float above the floor. Who will you go with? You can only ask someone you know well.\ntext [e4d78e] = 为了仲冬舞会，大礼堂化作了冰雪与星光的世界。一对对舞伴悬浮在地板上方起舞。你要和谁一起去？只能邀请你熟悉的人。\n# en: The great hall transformed: an ice-crystal chandelier, couples waltzing a foot above the floor, snow falling indoors without ever landing.\nscene [84cb48] = 焕然一新的大礼堂：冰晶吊灯，离地一尺旋转华尔兹的舞伴，室内飘着永不落地的雪。\n# en: Ask Lyra\nopt.lyra.label [b5812c] = 邀请莱拉\n# en: {&She has had six invitations. She hasn't said yes to any.|She has had invitations again. She is pretending not to count them.}\nopt.lyra.hint [31cae8] = {&她收到了六份邀请，一份也没答应。|她又收到了一堆邀请，正假装没在数。}\n# en: {bond(lyra) == \"partner\": \"Obviously,\" she says, before you finish asking. She leads, of course.|{flag(ball_lyra): \"Again? Fine. You've improved.\" She dances like a duel, and this year you nearly keep up.|\"Finally, someone who doesn't stammer.\" She dances like a duel — and wins.}}\nopt.lyra.say.1 [053333] = {bond(lyra) == \"partner\": “那还用说，”你还没问完，她就答应了。当然是她领舞。|{flag(ball_lyra): “又来？行吧。你有进步。”她跳舞还是像决斗，今年你差点就跟上了。|“总算来了个不结巴的。”她跳舞像决斗一样——而且赢了。}}\n# en: \"I'm going alone. It's more efficient.\" You watch her dance alone all night, perfectly.\nopt.lyra.say.2 [ba4ab4] = “我一个人去。效率更高。”你看着她独自跳了一整晚，完美无缺。\n# en: Ask Tobias\nopt.tobias.label [81d042] = 邀请托比亚斯\n# en: {flag(ball_tobias): He has been practising with a broom again. The broom has improved.|He has been practising with a broom.}\nopt.tobias.hint [7aa1e2] = {flag(ball_tobias): 他又拿扫帚练了一年。扫帚的舞技进步了。|他一直在拿扫帚当舞伴练习。}\n# en: {flag(ball_tobias): He only steps on your feet twice this year, and is so proud of it that he tells everyone. It's the best night of the term.|He steps on your feet nine times and apologises ten. It's the best night of the term.}\nopt.tobias.say.1 [dd7f73] = {flag(ball_tobias): 今年他只踩了你两次，得意得逢人就说。这是本学期最美好的一晚。|他踩了你的脚九次，道歉了十次。这是本学期最美好的一晚。}\n# en: Ask Seraphine\nopt.seraphine.label [4c88b3] = 邀请瑟拉芬\n# en: She may already know you'll ask.\nopt.seraphine.hint [da4e86] = 她可能早就知道你会邀请她。\n# en: {flag(ball_seraphine): \"Same as last time,\" Seraphine says, \"but you'll wear blue.\" You hadn't planned to. You do.|\"I said yes three weeks ago,\" she says, \"you just hadn't asked yet.\" She dances a beat ahead of the music.}\nopt.seraphine.say.1 [8d2828] = {flag(ball_seraphine): “和上回一样，”瑟拉芬说，“不过你会穿蓝色。”你本来没打算穿。结果你穿了。|“我三周前就答应了，”她说，“只是你那时还没开口。”她的舞步总比音乐快半拍。}\n# en: Ask Corvin\nopt.corvin.label [cbffdf] = 邀请科尔文\n# en: Odds he shows up: unclear.\nopt.corvin.hint [bd223d] = 他会不会出现：不好说。\n# en: {flag(ball_corvin): He arrives on time, which alarms everyone, in a jacket he swears is borrowed. He is still the best dancer in the room.|He arrives late, in a stolen jacket, and is the best dancer in the room.}\nopt.corvin.say.1 [48f270] = {flag(ball_corvin): 他准时到了，把所有人都吓了一跳，身上那件外套他发誓是借的。他依然是全场跳得最好的人。|他迟到了，穿着一件偷来的外套，却是全场跳得最好的人。}\n# en: {flag(ball_corvin_arrested): He never shows. A note arrives at midnight: \"Not arrested this time. Worse. Explain later.\" He never does.|He never shows. A note arrives at midnight: \"Got arrested. Long story. Save me a dance next year.\"}\nopt.corvin.say.2 [0ac25d] = {flag(ball_corvin_arrested): 他始终没来。午夜时分送来一张字条：“这次没被捕。更糟。回头解释。”他始终没解释。|他始终没来。午夜时分送来一张字条：“被捕了。说来话长。明年给我留支舞。”}\n# en: Ask Mir\nopt.mir.label [c272fe] = 邀请米尔\n# en: {flag(ball_mir): They promise the violin has been fixed.|They're bringing a self-playing violin.}\nopt.mir.hint [ff16f8] = {flag(ball_mir): TA 保证小提琴已经修好了。|TA 要带一把会自己演奏的小提琴。}\n# en: {flag(ball_mir): The violin behaves all night. Mir is almost disappointed, until it plays the last waltz entirely on its own and bows.|Mir's violin plays itself, then plays the chandelier, then plays the windows. You are asked to leave early. Worth it.}\nopt.mir.say.1 [dddb91] = {flag(ball_mir): 小提琴整晚都很规矩。米尔几乎有点失望，直到最后一支华尔兹它完全自己拉完，还鞠了一躬。|米尔的小提琴自己拉了起来，然后拉起了吊灯，然后拉起了窗户。你们被请提前离场。值了。}\n# en: Ask Kael\nopt.kael.label [bf9bdb] = 邀请凯尔\n# en: He doesn't seem to feel the cold.\nopt.kael.hint [3c4856] = 他似乎感觉不到寒冷。\n# en: {flag(ball_kael): He takes your hand without asking this time, as if it were already decided. The frost-ferns on the glass grow into a whole winter garden.|He holds your hand like it is something fragile. Where your fingers touch, frost-ferns bloom on the glass.}\nopt.kael.say.1 [a16e50] = {flag(ball_kael): 这一次他没问就牵起了你的手，仿佛早已说定。玻璃上的霜蕨长成了一整座冬日花园。|他握着你的手，像握着什么易碎的东西。你们指尖相触之处，玻璃上绽开了霜蕨。}\n# en: Ask Nessa\nopt.nessa.label [6cfd84] = 邀请内莎\n# en: She'll be working. She's always working.\nopt.nessa.hint [698b3a] = 她会边跳边工作。她永远在工作。\n# en: {flag(ball_nessa): She leaves her notebook in her bag all night. \"The column can make something up,\" she says. It does.|She takes notes between dances. \"For the society column.\" Then she stops taking notes.}\nopt.nessa.say.1 [56ee97] = {flag(ball_nessa): 她整晚都把笔记本留在包里。“专栏可以随便编点什么，”她说。专栏真的编了。|她在舞曲间隙做笔记。“写社交专栏用的。”后来，她不再做笔记了。}\n# en: Go with friends\nopt.friends.label [596790] = 和朋友们一起去\n# en: {flag(ball_friends): You go as a pack again, and this year someone has taught everyone the same dance, badly. It is still perfect.|You go as a pack and dance badly together and it is perfect.}\nopt.friends.say.1 [7848f2] = {flag(ball_friends): 你们又成群结队地去了，今年有人教了大家同一支舞，大家都跳得很糟。依然完美。|你们成群结队地去，一起跳得乱七八糟，完美极了。}\n# en: Stay in and study\nopt.skip.label [6d8b57] = 留在宿舍学习\n# en: The music drifts up through the floor while you read. Somewhere, people are happy. You are productive.\nopt.skip.say.1 [c55696] = 你看书时，音乐透过地板飘上来。某个地方，人们很快乐。而你，很高效。\n\n@event spring_tournament\n# en: The Spring Tournament\ntitle [e40010] = 春季锦标赛\n# en: The Spring Tournament: dueling on the great lawn, the whole academy watching. Entry is open to anyone foolish enough.\ntext [75ecd9] = 春季锦标赛：在大草坪上决斗，全院围观。任何够傻的人都可以报名。\n# en: A ring of white stone on the lawn, crowds in the stands, two duelists bowing as their wands spark.\nscene [903c03] = 草坪上一圈白石决斗场，看台上挤满人群，两名决斗者行礼致意，魔杖迸出火花。\n# en: Watch from the stands\nopt.left.label [8d4dec] = 在看台上观战\n# en: You eat candied chestnuts and scream for strangers.\nopt.left.say.1 [8ad597] = 你吃着糖炒栗子，为素不相识的人尖叫助威。\n# en: Enter the tournament\nopt.right.label [dd70a1] = 报名参赛\n# en: First round: you win, barely, and {flag(tournament_champion): the crowd remembers your name from last year and chants it.|the crowd learns your name.}\nopt.right.say.1 [a286bf] = 第一轮：你赢了，险胜，{flag(tournament_champion): 观众还记得你去年的名字，齐声喊了起来。|观众记住了你的名字。}\n# en: First round: you are knocked out of the ring in eleven seconds. A record, apparently.\nopt.right.say.2 [39b48e] = 第一轮：你在十一秒内被打出场外。据说是新纪录。\n\n@event spring_tournament_circle\n# en: The Spring Tournament\ntitle [e40010] = 春季锦标赛\n# en: The Spring Tournament: duelling on the great lawn, the whole academy watching. The Duelling Circle's names went up on the board a week ago, yours among them. {rank(duelling) >= 3: You are the captain, and the Circle expects its captain to win something.|The captain wants results, and says so.}\ntext [baac89] = 春季锦标赛：在大草坪上决斗，全院围观。一周前，决斗社的名单就贴上了公告板，你的名字也在其中。{rank(duelling) >= 3: 你就是社长，决斗社指望社长拿点什么回来。|社长要的是成绩，而且直说了。}\n# en: The great lawn ringed with banners, a chalk duelling circle at its centre and the Duelling Circle's names on a board.\nscene [0ee357] = 挂满旗帜的大草坪，中央用粉笔画着决斗圈，一块板上写着决斗社成员的名字。\n# en: Fight to make a good showing\nopt.left.label [ad260b] = 打出一场像样的比赛\n# en: You win your first bout cleanly and lose the second honourably. {rank(duelling) >= 3: Your second-years cheer their captain louder than anyone in the stands.|The captain ticks your name, which from her is a medal.}\nopt.left.say.1 [84d823] = 你干净利落地赢下第一场，又体面地输掉第二场。{rank(duelling) >= 3: 你的二年级社员为社长欢呼，比看台上任何人都响。|社长在你的名字旁打了个勾——从她那儿来说，这就是一枚奖章。}\n# en: You go out in the first round, on your feet at least. {rank(duelling) >= 3: Your second-years are kind about it, which is worse.|The captain says you fought like someone who expected to lose.}\nopt.left.say.2 [9fdebe] = 你在第一轮就出局了，好歹是站着出去的。{rank(duelling) >= 3: 你的二年级社员对此很体贴，这更糟。|社长说你打得像个早就料到自己会输的人。}\n# en: Go for the title\nopt.right.label [a6e251] = 冲击冠军\n# en: First round: you win, barely, and the crowd learns your name.\nopt.right.say.1 [5dba97] = 第一轮：你赢了，赢得很险，全场都记住了你的名字。\n# en: First round: you are knocked out of the ring in eleven seconds. A record, apparently, and not one the Circle wanted.\nopt.right.say.2 [0831c2] = 第一轮：你在十一秒内被打出了场地。据说是个纪录，可惜不是决斗社想要的那种。\n\n@event tournament_semifinal\n# en: Semifinal\ntitle [4ddbc9] = 半决赛\n# en: The semifinal. Your opponent is a fourth-year who has never lost. The crowd is louder now; you can hear your own name in it.\ntext [69650a] = 半决赛。你的对手是一位从未输过的四年级生。人群更吵了；你能在喧闹中听见自己的名字。\n# en: The same ring at sunset, scorch marks on the stone. Your opponent is grinning.\nscene [54c532] = 夕阳下的同一座决斗场，石面上满是焦痕。你的对手咧嘴笑着。\n# en: Fight clean\nopt.left.label [e5ec00] = 堂堂正正地战斗\n# en: You trade spells like questions and answers — and get the last word.\nopt.left.say.1 [a411dd] = 你们你来我往地交换咒语，像一问一答——最后一句是你说的。\n# en: A good fight. Not good enough. The crowd applauds you off the lawn.\nopt.left.say.2 [2ec6f6] = 打得很好。但还不够好。人群鼓掌送你离开草坪。\n# en: Fight dirty\nopt.right.label [2323b9] = 耍点小手段\n# en: A feint, a trip, a jinx to the shoelaces. Legal. Mostly.{met(bram):  On the prefects' bench Bram watches the whole thing with his jaw set, and doesn't clap.|}\nopt.right.say.1 [e37fae] = 一个虚招，一个绊子，一个冲着鞋带的小咒。合规。大体上。{met(bram): 级长席上，布拉姆紧绷着下巴看完了全程，没有鼓掌。|}\n# en: The referee sees the trick. Disqualified, and booed.\nopt.right.say.2 [06fbf6] = 裁判看穿了把戏。你被取消资格，还挨了嘘声。\n\n@event tournament_final\n# en: The Final\ntitle [5a826c] = 决赛\n# en: The final. Across the ring stands {lyra}, wand raised, eyes blazing. \"I was hoping it would be you,\" she says, and she is not being friendly.\ntext [9091dd] = 决赛。决斗场对面站着{lyra}，魔杖高举，双眼燃着火光。“我就希望是你，”她说，语气可一点也不友好。\n# en: The dueling ring under floating lanterns, a silver cup on a plinth, and across from you, a familiar face.\nscene [2b21ea] = 悬浮灯笼下的决斗场，底座上放着一只银杯，对面是一张熟悉的面孔。\n# en: Give everything you have\nopt.left.label [eef34c] = 拼尽全力\n# en: Your final spell lands like a bell. Silence — then the lawn erupts. Lyra stares at you as if seeing you for the first time, then walks off the lawn without shaking your hand.\nopt.left.say.1 [e71e19] = 你最后一道咒语像钟声一样落下。一片寂静——随后草坪沸腾了。莱拉看着你，好像第一次真正看见你，然后没和你握手就离开了草坪。\n# en: She wins, but only just. Afterwards, she offers her hand. \"Next year,\" she says. It is a promise and a threat.\nopt.left.say.2 [6d4f46] = 她赢了，但只是险胜。赛后，她向你伸出手。“明年。”她说。这既是承诺，也是威胁。\n# en: Yield with a bow\nopt.right.label [7ad851] = 鞠躬认输\n# en: The crowd boos. Lyra does not cheer her victory. Later she finds you: \"Why?\" You don't have a good answer, and she seems to like that.\nopt.right.say.1 [957fd4] = 人群发出嘘声。莱拉没有为自己的胜利欢呼。后来她找到你：“为什么？”你答不上来，而她似乎正喜欢这一点。\n\n@event grand_exams\n# en: Grand Examinations\ntitle [9cf559] = 年度大考\n# en: Year-end examinations. Three days, five examiners and a practical in front of a panel that includes the headmistress. Your standing for the year rides on this.\ntext [148501] = 学年末考试。三天，五位考官，还要在包括院长在内的评审团面前完成一项实践考核。你这一年的学业，全押在这上面了。\n# en: A circular examination chamber with a domed ceiling of moving constellations; examiners in grey robes sit behind a long table.\nscene [a8e8ed] = 一间圆形考场，穹顶上的星座缓缓移动；身着灰袍的考官们坐在长桌后。\n# en: Prepare a dazzling practical\nopt.left.label [6b5ebd] = 准备一场惊艳的实践考核\n# en: You finish your practical and the examiners actually lean forward. Distinction.\nopt.left.say.1 [1ca3b3] = 你完成实践考核时，考官们居然真的向前倾了倾身子。优秀。\n# en: The practical sputters. An examiner writes something very short.\nopt.left.say.2 [1a07cb] = 实践考核噼啪作响地熄火了。一位考官写下了很短的一句话。\n# en: Write a meticulous thesis\nopt.right.label [018303] = 写一篇严谨的论文\n# en: Your thesis is cited by one examiner in an argument with another. Distinction.\nopt.right.say.1 [dbc14d] = 一位考官在和另一位争论时引用了你的论文。优秀。\n# en: Your thesis is described, aloud, as \"ambitious\". It is not a compliment.\nopt.right.say.2 [067468] = 你的论文被当众评价为“很有野心”。这可不是夸奖。\n\n@event summer_plans\n# en: Summer Recess\ntitle [84178b] = 暑假\n# en: The towers empty for the summer. Two months of freedom. How will you spend them?\ntext [28317e] = 塔楼在夏天空了下来。两个月的自由。你打算怎么过？\n# en: The academy gates thrown open to green hills; students with trunks waving from departing sky-barges.\nscene [35fcaa] = 学院大门向青翠的山丘敞开；拖着行李箱的学生们在离港的天空驳船上挥手。\n# en: Go home\nopt.home.label [c93952] = 回家\n# en: Rest and recover. No rent while away.\nopt.home.hint [735a7b] = 休息与恢复。离校期间不交房租。\n# en: Your family hosts three dinners in your honour and asks about your marriage prospects at all of them.\nopt.home.say.1 [47c092] = 家里为你办了三场晚宴，每一场都有人问起你的婚事。\n# en: The old crowd in Port Calder treats you like a returning hero, and steals your trunk only once.\nopt.home.say.2 [c552ac] = 考尔德港的老伙计们把你当成荣归故里的英雄，只偷了你的行李箱一次。\n# en: Home is smaller than you remember, and warmer. Someone made your favourite pie.\nopt.home.say.3 [c3fe6f] = 家比你记忆中小，也更温暖。有人做了你最爱吃的馅饼。\n# en: Take summer work in the village\nopt.work.label [3f8e3c] = 在镇上打暑期工\n# en: About 50 gold over the summer. Tiring.\nopt.work.hint [6173e0] = 整个夏天约 50 金币。很累。\n# en: You haul crates, serve tables and polish brass until your hands know the shape of every coin.\nopt.work.say.1 [55acbe] = 你搬箱子、端盘子、擦铜器，直到双手熟悉每一枚硬币的形状。\n# en: Stay and study in the empty library\nopt.study.label [968f6e] = 留在空荡的图书馆学习\n# en: +Lore, +Arcana, +Standing. Lonely.\nopt.study.hint [8c75a3] = +学识，+奥术，+学业。很孤单。\n# en: Just you, the books and the dust motes. You learn things the term never had time for.\nopt.study.say.1 [fe7d5b] = 只有你、书本和浮尘。你学到了学期里永远没时间学的东西。\n# en: Travel to the coast\nopt.travel.label [78a0b3] = 去海边旅行\n# en: Costs 40 gold. Great for mood.\nopt.travel.hint [b46376] = 花费 40 金币。心情大好。\n# en: Needs 40 crowns.\nopt.travel.locked [4deed8] = 需要 40 克朗。\n# en: Salt air, shell markets, a sea serpent at sunset. You come back tanned and ten years younger.\nopt.travel.say.1 [1db6b9] = 咸咸的海风，贝壳集市，夕阳下的海蛇。你回来时晒黑了，年轻了十岁。\n# en: Join Old Maud's herb expedition\nopt.expedition.label [49633f] = 参加老莫德的采药远征\n# en: Requires knowing Maud. Adventure.\nopt.expedition.hint [362c6b] = 需要认识老莫德。冒险。\n# en: Two months in the Whisperwood with Maud and her goat. You learn which mushrooms sing and which ones lie.\nopt.expedition.say.1 [929c02] = 和莫德还有她的山羊在低语森林里待了两个月。你学会了分辨哪些蘑菇会唱歌，哪些会撒谎。\n\n@event summer_letter\n# en: A Letter From Home\ntitle [418673] = 一封家书\n# en: A letter arrives in the last month of summer, the handwriting so familiar it hurts. They want to know how you are doing. Really doing.\ntext [67658a] = 夏天的最后一个月，一封信寄到了，字迹熟悉得让人心疼。他们想知道你过得怎么样。真正过得怎么样。\n# en: A letter sealed with candle wax lying on a sunlit windowsill.\nscene [ac2d7b] = 一封用烛蜡封口的信，躺在洒满阳光的窗台上。\n# en: Write back honestly\nopt.left.label [24b451] = 如实回信\n# en: You tell them about your grades. Their reply is kind, which is worse.\nopt.left.say.1 [5e0fac] = 你告诉了他们你的成绩。他们的回信很温柔，这反而更难受。\n# en: You write six pages. It feels like setting down a heavy bag.\nopt.left.say.2 [f6372c] = 你写了六页。感觉像放下了一个沉重的包袱。\n# en: Write back that everything is perfect\nopt.right.label [979dc2] = 回信说一切都很完美\n# en: Proud of you, they write, and enclose a little money{has(origin_noble) or has(origin_merchant): , as if a purse were the right answer to a letter.|  you suspect they couldn't spare.}\nopt.right.say.1 [b6e0ca] = 他们回信说为你骄傲，还附上了一点钱{has(origin_noble) or has(origin_merchant): ，好像一袋钱就是回信的正确答案。|——你猜他们其实拿不出来。}\n# en: They reply with exclamation marks. The lie sits in your stomach all week.\nopt.right.say.2 [75bc50] = 他们的回信满是感叹号。这个谎言在你胃里堵了一整个星期。\n\n@event starfall\n# en: Starfall\ntitle [ab87b4] = 星坠节\n# en: {year == 1: The last night of the academic year. Everyone climbs onto the observatory roof, and the rest spill out onto the lawns with blankets, to watch the stars fall. They say if you catch one in a jar, you get a year of luck.|Starfall again. The observatory roof, the lawns full of blankets, the whole academy lying on its back.} {starfall_jar >= 1: Your jar is ready, cork in hand.|} {~The first star falls before anyone is ready.|Somebody has brought a lute, unwisely.|The lake is full of reflected stars.}\ntext [56614b] = {year == 1: 学年的最后一夜。大家都爬上观星台屋顶，其余的人带着毯子涌到草坪上，看星星坠落。据说，如果你能用罐子接住一颗，就能交一整年的好运。|又是星坠节。观星台的屋顶，铺满毯子的草坪，整个学院都仰面躺着。} {starfall_jar >= 1: 你的罐子准备好了，软木塞就在手里。|} {~第一颗星星在大家还没准备好时就落下了。|有人带了一把鲁特琴，不太明智。|湖里满是倒映的星星。}\n# en: Students lying on the observatory roof under a sky streaked with falling stars.\nscene [7b9deb] = 学生们躺在天文台屋顶上，头顶的夜空划满了流星。\n# en: Lie back and just watch\nopt.left.label [df7032] = 躺下来，只是看着\n# en: You count forty falling stars and then stop counting. It's enough.\nopt.left.say.1 [d2a569] = 你数了四十颗流星，然后不再数了。这就够了。\n# en: Try to catch a star\nopt.right.label [734781] = 试着接住一颗星\n# en: Nobody has caught one in years. A good jar from the observatory improves the odds.\nopt.right.hint [26a72f] = 好些年没人接住过一颗了。观星台做的好罐子能提高几分胜算。\n# en: It lands in your jar with a hiss and glows for weeks. Students come to see it.\nopt.right.say.1 [f06369] = 它嘶的一声落进你的罐子，发光发了好几个星期。同学们都跑来看。\n# en: You lunge, miss, and nearly roll off the roof. A prefect catches you by the collar.\nopt.right.say.2 [dd228d] = 你扑了过去，没接到，差点滚下屋顶。一位级长揪住了你的衣领。\n# en: It lands in your jar with a hiss and glows for weeks. Students come to see it.\nopt.right.say.3 [f06369] = 它嘶的一声落进你的罐子，发了好几个星期的光。同学们都来看它。\n# en: You lunge, miss, and nearly roll off the roof. A prefect catches you by the collar.\nopt.right.say.4 [dd228d] = 你扑过去，没接住，差点从屋顶滚下去。一位级长揪住了你的领子。\n# en: Find your sweetheart on the lawn\nopt.together.label [6cf90b] = 去草坪上找你的心上人\n# en: You leave the roof and go looking. You know exactly where to look.\nopt.together.say.1 [8e8e09] = 你离开屋顶去找。你很清楚该去哪儿找。\n# en: Make the Starfall wish\nopt.wish.label [a6bf48] = 许下星坠节的愿望\n# en: You climb to the very top of the roof, where the old custom says the wishes go.\nopt.wish.say.1 [c0d3b2] = 你爬到屋顶的最高处，老习俗说愿望都从那里升上去。\n\n@event year_two\n# en: Second Year\ntitle [c51ea2] = 第二学年\n# en: The barge brings new first-years, and you watch them gawp at the towers. Was that really you a year ago? You know which staircases move now, and which professors you can charm. Second-years, a prefect announces, may apply for summer research posts in Stormbloom.{chosen(break_plan, home):  Your trunk still smells of home, and there's a cake in it you were told to share.|}\ntext [b83f07] = 驳船载来了新的一年级生，你看着他们仰望七塔，目瞪口呆。一年前那真的是你吗？如今你知道哪些楼梯会动，哪些教授能哄得住。一位级长宣布：二年级生可以在风暴月申请暑期研究职位。{chosen(break_plan, home): 你的箱子里还带着家的味道，里面还有一块蛋糕，家里人叮嘱你一定要分给大家。|}\n# en: The seven towers in autumn colours, the first-year barge arriving below, tiny and nervous.\nscene [aa7c3d] = 秋色中的七座高塔，下方新生的驳船正在靠岸，渺小而紧张。\n# en: Begin the second year\nopt.ok.label [338fd8] = 开始第二学年\n# en: You unpack faster than last year, and in the right order. Someone in your year already has a list of the research posts, and the castle's midnight doors, side by side.\nopt.ok.say.1 [08f4a0] = 你收拾行李比去年快，顺序也对了。你们年级已经有人列好了研究职位的清单，旁边并排列着城堡午夜开放的门。\n\n@event term_begins\n# en: The Spring Term\ntitle [c548b1] = 春季学期\n# en: {chosen(break_plan, home): You come back from home with a tin of something your family insists you'll need, and the castle feels enormous after a winter of small rooms.|{chosen(break_plan, work): Your holiday wages are in your purse and your feet still ache from earning them. The castle fills up around you, trunk by trunk.|After a winter of empty corridors, the castle fills up around you again, and the noise of it is almost too much.}} The spring term begins, and the registrar's hall is already queueing.\ntext [f78fe0] = {chosen(break_plan, home): 你从家里回来，带着一罐家人坚持说你用得上的东西。过了一个冬天的小屋子，城堡显得大得出奇。|{chosen(break_plan, work): 假期挣的工钱揣在钱袋里，挣钱时磨疼的脚还没缓过来。城堡里一只只箱子搬进来，又热闹起来了。|空荡荡的走廊过了一个冬天，城堡又热闹起来，吵得你几乎有点受不了。}}春季学期开始了，教务大厅已经排起了长队。\n# en: The castle steps after the winter break, trunks stacked by the doors and the first lamps lit in the towers.\nscene [6e70cd] = 寒假过后的城堡台阶，门边堆着箱子，塔楼里亮起了第一批灯。\n# en: Get a head start on the reading\nopt.left.label [62ecd0] = 先把书读起来\n# en: You find a window seat in the library before the term has properly started. By the time the others arrive, you have a list of questions and a crick in your neck.\nopt.left.say.1 [1998c7] = 学期还没正式开始，你就在图书馆占了个靠窗的位子。等别人到了，你已经攒了一串问题，脖子也落了枕。\n# en: Catch up with everyone first\nopt.right.label [55ea0a] = 先和大家叙叙旧\n# en: You spend the first evening in the common room, then at the Newt, hearing everyone's winter. The reading can wait a day. It waits three.\nopt.right.say.1 [ceec42] = 第一个晚上你先泡在公共休息室，后来又去了镀金蝾螈酒馆，听每个人讲自己的冬天。书可以晚一天再读。结果晚了三天。\n\n@event year_three\n# en: Third Year\ntitle [c19627] = 第三年\n# en: \"Third years,\" the headmistress says, \"you are past the middle of your time here. Next year will ask a great deal of you; spend this one finding out what you want from it.\" She looks, for a moment, directly at you.{chosen(break_plan, home):  You came straight from home, and the summer's quiet is still on you.|}\ntext [320bae] = “三年级的同学们，”院长说，“你们在这里的时光已经过半。明年会对你们提出很多要求；今年就用来弄明白，你们想从中得到什么。”有那么一刻，她直直地看着你。{chosen(break_plan, home): 你是从家里直接回来的，夏天的那份安静还留在身上。|}\n# en: The headmistress addressing a hall of third-years, every candle in the room leaning toward her.\nscene [3c61bc] = 院长向满堂的三年级生讲话，屋里的每一支蜡烛都向她倾斜。\n# en: Begin the third year\nopt.ok.label [85c8b5] = 开始第三年\n# en: You know the castle well enough now to get lost in it on purpose. This is the last year for a summer research post, and the holiday notices by the gates pay better every year.\nopt.ok.say.1 [9f3f57] = 你对城堡已经熟到可以故意在里面迷路了。今年是申请暑期研究职位的最后一年，而校门边的假期招工告示，一年比一年给得多。\n\n@event graduation_eve\n# en: The Eve of Graduation\ntitle [16a29d] = 毕业前夜\n# en: Tomorrow you graduate. Your robes are laid out on the bed, slightly too long. Outside, the towers are lit gold. Four years have gone like a single held breath.\ntext [feebe6] = 明天你就要毕业了。学士袍摊在床上，稍微长了一点。窗外，高塔被点亮成金色。四年时光，像屏住的一口气，倏忽而过。\n# en: Graduation robes laid out on a dormitory bed, a single candle burning, the towers lit gold outside the window.\nscene [47f9c3] = 宿舍床上摊着毕业袍，一支蜡烛独自燃烧，窗外的塔楼灯火金黄。\n# en: Spend the night with friends\nopt.left.label [476937] = 和朋友们共度今夜\n# en: {bonds(friend) + bonds(close) + bonds(partner) > 0: Laughter until dawn. Promises to write. Some of them, you'll keep.|You knock on a few doors. Nobody who answers is a close friend, not yet, but they pour you a cup and keep you up laughing till dawn. You trade addresses anyway.}\nopt.left.say.1 [1265bf] = {bonds(friend) + bonds(close) + bonds(partner) > 0: 笑到天亮。约好要写信。其中一些约定，你会遵守。|你敲了几扇门。来开门的人都还算不上挚友，但他们给你倒了一杯，陪你笑到天亮。你们还是交换了地址。}\n# en: Walk the halls alone one last time\nopt.right.label [9cc549] = 最后一次独自走遍厅廊\n# en: Every corridor holds a memory. At the end of the last one, the castle sighs, as if saying goodbye.\nopt.right.say.1 [fce683] = 每一条走廊都藏着回忆。走到最后一条的尽头时，城堡轻轻叹了口气，像是在道别。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/chain_hollowstar_acts.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/chain_hollowstar_acts.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event hs_lake_breathes\n# en: The Lake Breathes\ntitle [6ab0a7] = 湖在呼吸\n# en: At supper the lake outside the tall windows turns white, all at once, and stays white for one breath. Then it thaws. Far under the water a bell tolls once. The headmistress stands. \"A cold current,\" she says pleasantly. \"Eat your soup.\" Nobody does.\ntext [707666] = 晚餐时，高窗外的湖面一下子全白了，白了一口气的工夫，然后解冻了。水下远处，一口钟响了一声。校长站起身。“一股冷流而已，”她愉快地说，“喝你们的汤吧。”没有人动勺子。\n# en: The Great Hall at supper, every head turned to the tall windows, the lake beyond them gone white with sudden frost.\nscene [96af25] = 晚餐时的大礼堂，所有人都转头望向高窗，窗外的湖面骤然覆上一层白霜。\n# en: Go down to the shore after supper\nopt.left.label [84cab9] = 晚饭后去湖岸看看\n# en: The shore is soaked and freezing. Old Maud is already there with a lantern, watching the water. \"Last time it did that, I lost a friend,\" she says, not quite to you. Then she sends you to bed, and you go, shivering.\nopt.left.say.1 [0b9df6] = 湖岸湿透了，冷得刺骨。老莫德已经提着灯站在那儿，望着水面。“上次它这样的时候，我失去了一个朋友，”她说，不太像是对你说的。然后她打发你去睡觉，你哆嗦着走了。\n# en: Watch the high table instead\nopt.right.label [f3ff9f] = 改为留意高桌\n# en: The headmistress finishes her soup. Professor Thorne doesn't touch his, and after supper he goes straight to the library archive. You lie awake wondering what he went to read.\nopt.right.say.1 [3796d0] = 校长把汤喝完了。索恩教授一口没碰，晚饭后径直去了图书馆档案库。你躺在床上睡不着，猜他去读了什么。\n\n@event hs_silver_rings\n# en: Silver at Supper\ntitle [65d7d8] = 晚餐桌上的银戒\n# en: At supper you notice three identical silver rings at the high table: on the headmistress, on Professor Thorne and on Old Maud's earthy hand. After curfew, from your window, you see all three walking the lakeshore with lanterns, stopping at the foot of each tower in turn.\ntext [0e82e1] = 晚餐时，你注意到主宾席上有三枚一模一样的银戒指：一枚戴在院长手上，一枚在索恩教授手上，还有一枚在老莫德那双沾着泥土的手上。宵禁之后，你从窗口望见他们三人提着灯笼沿湖岸走，在每座塔脚下依次停步。\n# en: The Great Hall at supper, candlelight catching three identical silver rings on three very different hands at the high table.\nscene [7eebf1] = 晚餐时的大礼堂，烛光照亮主宾席上三只截然不同的手上三枚一模一样的银戒指。\n# en: Follow them down\nopt.left.label [088fbe] = 跟下去看看\n# en: You shadow them from tower to tower. At each one they lay a palm on the stone and wait. At the last, Maud takes her hand away and blows on her fingers as if the wall were ice.\nopt.left.say.1 [ec6d76] = 你从一座塔跟到另一座塔。每到一处，他们都把手掌贴在石墙上，静静等着。到了最后一座，莫德把手收回来，对着指尖呵气，仿佛那面墙是冰做的。\n# en: A lantern swings toward you. \"Hm,\" says Professor Thorne. \"Bed.\" He doesn't report you, which somehow feels worse.\nopt.left.say.2 [c0b820] = 一盏灯笼朝你转过来。“嗯。”索恩教授说，“睡觉去。”他没有上报你，不知为何这反而更难受。\n# en: Draw the curtains\nopt.right.label [899463] = 拉上窗帘\n# en: You sleep well, mostly. In the morning the rings are still there at breakfast, and nobody at the high table looks as if they slept at all.\nopt.right.say.1 [b6139e] = 你睡得还算不错。第二天早餐时，那几枚戒指还在，而主宾席上没有一个人看起来睡过觉。\n\n@event hs_seraphine_towers\n# en: Seven, Then Six\ntitle [257255] = 七座，然后六座\n# en: Seraphine is on the observatory balcony, counting towers on her fingers. \"Seven,\" she says. \"Seven. Six, and then… no. Seven, for now.\" She looks at you as if you had arrived a little early. \"What month is it? I lose track around the… you know.\"\ntext [a96e33] = 瑟拉芬站在观星台的阳台上，掰着手指数塔。“七座，”她说，“七座。六座，然后……不。七座，暂时。”她看着你，仿佛你来得稍微早了一点。“现在是几月？我总是在……你知道的那个地方，弄丢日子。”\n# en: A seer on the observatory balcony at night, counting the academy's towers on her fingers, one finger held back.\nscene [34ef65] = 夜里观星台的阳台上，一位预言者掰着手指数学院的塔，有一根手指迟迟没有伸出来。\n# en: \"Which one goes out?\"\nopt.left.label [a243d5] = “哪一座会熄灭？”\n# en: She points, then frowns at her own hand. \"That one. Or the one next to it. Next year, I think. Not the seventh; nobody's lit the seventh for a hundred years.\" She doesn't remember saying it the next morning, but you do.\nopt.left.say.1 [f52252] = 她指了一下，又对着自己的手皱起眉。“那一座。或者旁边那座。明年吧，我想。不是第七座；第七座已经一百年没人点灯了。”第二天早上她已经不记得说过这话，但你记得。\n# en: \"It's {month}. Evening.\"\nopt.right.label [1c7b30] = “现在是{month}，傍晚。”\n# en: She lets out a long breath and sits down on the cold stone. \"Thank you. I was halfway into a winter.\" You stay until she is sure which one she's in.\nopt.right.say.1 [1f863d] = 她长长地呼出一口气，在冰凉的石头上坐了下来。“谢谢。我已经半只脚踏进冬天了。”你一直陪着她，直到她确定自己身在哪个季节。\n\n@event hs_ilse_arrives\n# en: Kael Goes Pale\ntitle [1e244a] = 凯尔脸色发白\n# en: At breakfast a village boy brings Kael a note, and Kael reads it and goes whiter than usual, which is a feat. Frost creeps across his tea. \"My sister,\" he says. \"She's taken a room above the bakery.\" He folds the note very small. \"She isn't here to visit.\"\ntext [336751] = 早餐时，一个村里的男孩给凯尔送来一张字条，凯尔读完脸色比平时还白，这可不容易。霜爬上了他的茶杯。“我妹妹，”他说，“她在面包房楼上租了间屋子。”他把字条折得很小很小。“她不是来探亲的。”\n# en: A breakfast table in the refectory, a pale northern student staring at an unsealed letter, frost creeping across his teacup.\nscene [a3db11] = 膳堂里的早餐桌，一个苍白的北方学生盯着一封拆开的信，霜爬上了他的茶杯。\n# en: Go to the bakery yourself\nopt.left.label [675e2a] = 自己去面包房看看\n# en: \"Don't,\" he says, too quickly. \"She'd only… don't.\" He doesn't eat. You decide you'll go anyway, soon, and he can see you decide it, and looks away.\nopt.left.say.1 [119d01] = “别去，”他说得太快了，“她只会……别去。”他什么都没吃。你决定过些天还是要去，他看得出你已经拿定了主意，便移开了目光。\n# en: Leave him to it\nopt.right.label [b84f2c] = 让他自己处理\n# en: You let him be. He sits with the note until the hall empties. That afternoon the whole village is talking about a girl in white furs whose coins freeze to the counter.\nopt.right.say.1 [c13634] = 你没去打扰他。他拿着那张字条一直坐到大厅里的人走光。那天下午，整个村子都在议论一个穿白裘的姑娘，她的硬币会冻在柜台上。\n\n@event hs_frost_ring\n# en: A Ring of Frost\ntitle [6db639] = 一圈白霜\n# en: A warm night, and a ring of frost has formed in the middle of the lake: perfectly round, perfectly white, as wide as the Great Hall. Far below it, very faintly, a bell tolls once. The headmistress is standing alone at the waterline.\ntext [110bde] = 一个温暖的夜晚，湖心却结出了一圈白霜：浑圆，雪白，和大礼堂一样宽。在它下方很深的地方，隐约传来一声钟响。院长独自站在水边。\n# en: The Mirror Lake on a warm spring night, a perfect white ring of frost on the water, a tall woman standing at the waterline with a lantern.\nscene [34c053] = 温暖春夜里的镜湖，水面上一圈完美的白霜，一个高个子女人提着灯笼站在水边。\n# en: Watch what she does\nopt.left.label [29f82d] = 看她要做什么\n# en: She kneels and speaks to the water, slow and patient, the way you would speak to a sleeping animal. The ring shrinks, cracks, and is gone. She stays kneeling a long time afterwards.\nopt.left.say.1 [fe979a] = 她跪下来，对着湖水说话，缓慢而耐心，就像对一头熟睡的野兽说话。那圈白霜缩小、碎裂，然后消失了。她在那里跪了很久才起身。\n# en: \"Good evening,\" she says, without turning round. \"You are the third student this week. The other two asked questions.\" She tells you nothing, and remembers your name.\nopt.left.say.2 [8fce40] = “晚上好。”她头也不回地说，“你是这周第三个来的学生。另外两个至少提了问题。”她什么也没告诉你，只是记住了你的名字。\n# en: Stay inside\nopt.right.label [b9dac4] = 待在屋里\n# en: You stay inside with the shutters closed. By morning the lake is blue and glittering, and the first-years are already arguing about whether anyone saw anything at all.\nopt.right.say.1 [29cbf0] = 你关紧百叶窗待在屋里。到了早上，湖面湛蓝、波光粼粼，一年级生已经在争论到底有没有人看见过什么。\n\n@event hs_dark_tower\n# en: The Tower That Went Dark\ntitle [eb9204] = 熄灭的塔\n# en: Mid-lecture, every lamp in the Lecture Tower goes out at once, and none of them will light again. Nobody is hurt. By morning, weeks before any frost, half the lake has frozen: a sheet of ice that ends in a clean edge, as if cut. The prefects are already telling everyone to keep off it.\ntext [f6dbbf] = 讲课讲到一半，讲堂塔里所有的灯同时熄灭，再也点不亮了。没有人受伤。到了早上，离第一场霜还有好几周，半个湖却已经冻住：冰面的边缘齐整得像是刀切出来的。级长们已经在挨个叮嘱大家别上冰。\n# en: An academy tower standing black against the night sky among its lit neighbours, and below it a lake half-covered in autumn ice.\nscene [1361bd] = 一座学院塔楼在灯火通明的邻塔之间漆黑一片，塔下的湖面有一半覆着秋天的冰。\n# en: Walk out onto the ice\nopt.left.label [fe1ac8] = 走上冰面\n# en: Under your boots the ice is clear as glass. Far down, something round and dark sits on the lakebed, and a faint glow runs along its rim. Then the glow turns, slowly, as if to look up.\nopt.left.say.1 [b23a9e] = 脚下的冰清澈如玻璃。在很深的湖底，有个圆形的黑色东西，边缘流淌着微光。然后那道光缓缓转动，像是要抬头往上看。\n# en: The ice groans and a crack races past your feet. You scramble back to the shore soaked to the knee, and a prefect writes your name down very slowly.\nopt.left.say.2 [b53a93] = 冰面一声呻吟，一道裂缝从你脚边飞窜而过。你连滚带爬地退回岸上，膝盖以下全湿透了，一位级长慢条斯理地记下了你的名字。\n# en: Carry lamps for the prefects\nopt.right.label [6083a9] = 帮级长们搬灯\n# en: You spend the night carrying lamps up the Lecture Tower stairs. Every one goes out at the doorway. From her window across the courtyard, the headmistress watches you try.\nopt.right.say.1 [b28e54] = 你整夜提着灯爬讲堂塔的楼梯。每一盏灯一到门口就熄灭。庭院对面，院长站在她的窗前，看着你一次又一次地尝试。\n\n@event hs_maud_locks\n# en: Maud Has Seen Ice Before\ntitle [b78fd5] = 莫德见过这种冰\n# en: Maud is on her cottage step with two mugs of tea, as if she expected you. \"The lake doesn't freeze this time of year, dearie,\" she says. \"Last time it froze through like this, I lost a friend to it.\" She pushes a mug toward you. \"Sit. Or ask. You can't really do both.\"\ntext [a9ef2f] = 莫德坐在小屋的台阶上，身边放着两杯茶，像是早料到你会来。“这个时节湖是不会结冰的，亲爱的。”她说，“上一次它像这样冻透，我失去了一个朋友。”她把一杯茶推到你面前。“坐下。或者提问。两样可没法一起做。”\n# en: An old woman wrapped in a shawl on her cottage step, two mugs steaming beside her, looking down at a half-frozen lake.\nscene [f49e09] = 一位裹着披肩的老妇人坐在小屋台阶上，身边两杯茶冒着热气，俯瞰着半冻的湖面。\n# en: Sit with her\nopt.left.label [654e0a] = 陪她坐着\n# en: You drink your tea and watch the ice. She hums something old. When the mugs are empty she squeezes your hand. \"That was the right one,\" she says, and you'll only understand why much later.\nopt.left.say.1 [7b5291] = 你喝着茶，看着冰面。她哼着一支老调子。杯子空了，她捏了捏你的手。“选对了。”她说。你要过很久才明白为什么。\n# en: Ask what's holding it shut\nopt.right.label [8d8004] = 问是什么把门锁住\n# en: \"Seven towers, seven locks, one in each,\" she says. \"When a tower goes quiet, so does its lock.\" She looks at the dark tower. \"Locks can be mended. They need hands, and they need a key.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [0813e9] = “七座塔，七道锁，每座一道。”她说，“一座塔沉寂了，它的锁也就沉寂了。”她望着那座熄灭的塔。“锁是可以修的。要有人手，还要有一把钥匙。”\n\n@event hs_two_shadows\n# en: Two Shadows on the Ice\ntitle [beb4d9] = 冰上的两道影子\n# en: At dusk, two figures walk out onto the frozen half of the lake from opposite shores: the headmistress with her lantern, and Kael, alone. They meet in the middle and talk for a long time, and part without shaking hands. At breakfast, both of them look at you a moment too long.\ntext [151c09] = 黄昏时，两个身影从相对的两岸走上湖面已冻住的那一半：提着灯的校长，和独自一人的凯尔。他们在湖中央碰头，谈了很久，分开时没有握手。第二天早餐时，两人看你的目光都多停了一会儿。\n# en: Dusk on a half-frozen lake, two dark figures meeting in the middle of the ice, one holding a lantern, the dark tower behind them.\nscene [2506e5] = 黄昏时半冻的湖面，两个黑影在冰面中央相会，其中一人提着灯，身后是那座熄了灯的塔。\n# en: Ask Kael what they said\nopt.left.label [91a8f0] = 问凯尔他们说了什么\n# en: \"She wants the door shut her way. I want it shut mine.\" He stirs his tea until it freezes. \"We both asked about you. Don't make that face. You'll be asked.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [3a9a75] = “她想按她的方式把门关上。我想按我的方式。”他搅着茶，直到茶冻住。“我们都问起了你。别做那种表情。你会被邀请的。”\n# en: Ask the headmistress\nopt.right.label [733de3] = 去问校长\n# en: \"We disagree about method,\" she says, \"and agree about the door.\" She studies you over her spectacles. \"Expect an invitation. Possibly two. Choose with your eyes open.\" Kael, across the hall, sees you leave her tower.\nopt.right.say.1 [330924] = “我们在方法上有分歧，”她说，“在门的问题上意见一致。”她从眼镜上方打量着你。“等着收邀请吧。可能是两份。睁大眼睛再选。”大厅另一头，凯尔看见你从她的塔里出来。\n\n@event hs_choosing_sides\n# en: Two Invitations\ntitle [b97cfd] = 两份邀请\n# en: Two invitations in one day. A note in the headmistress's neat hand: \"My study, after supper. Come alone. There will be chess.\" And Kael, waiting at the boathouse: \"She wants you in her circle. I know the door from the other side.\" He looks at the dark tower. \"Come with us. If you want.\"\ntext [441bf4] = 一天之内收到两份邀请。一张字条上是院长工整的字迹：“晚饭后来我书房。独自来。会有棋局。”而凯尔在船屋等你：“她想让你加入她的圈子。我从门的另一边认识那扇门。”他望向那座熄灭的塔。“跟我们来吧。如果你愿意。”\n# en: A student holding two folded notes, one sealed in silver wax and one in frost, standing between a tower and a boathouse.\nscene [0fa513] = 一个学生手里拿着两张折好的字条，一张封着银色火漆，一张封着白霜，站在一座塔和一间船屋之间。\n# en: Join the headmistress's circle\nopt.wardens.label [d0d300] = 加入院长的圈子\n# en: The Wardens: old rules, old wards.\nopt.wardens.hint [8082aa] = 守望者：古老的规矩，古老的护界。\n# en: She beats you at chess in eleven moves, then tells you about the seal while she resets the board. \"Rings are for people who have stood on the ice,\" she says. \"Until then, you have my door.\"\nopt.wardens.say.1 [1f494e] = 她用十一步棋赢了你，一边重新摆棋盘，一边给你讲封印的事。“戒指是给站上过冰面的人的。”她说，“在那之前，我的门为你开着。”\n# en: Stand with the Dumonts\nopt.dumonts.label [1cf610] = 站在杜蒙兄妹一边\n# en: Kael's way, against his own family.\nopt.dumonts.hint [3d8ab6] = 凯尔的路，与他自己的家族为敌。\n# en: \"Not my family,\" Kael says. \"Us.\" He teaches you to hold a ward in a wind that wants your fingers. His hands are cold. They are steadier than yours.\nopt.dumonts.say.1 [b5a244] = “不是我的家族，”凯尔说，“是我们。”他教你在一阵想要夺走你手指的寒风里撑住护界。他的手很冷，却比你的稳。\n# en: Go my own way\nopt.self.label [90c2bc] = 走我自己的路\n# en: Nobody's circle. Nobody's help.\nopt.self.hint [06bcd0] = 不入任何圈子，也没人帮忙。\n# en: You thank them both and keep your own notes in your own cipher. It's lonelier than you expected. Both of them, you notice, keep an eye on you anyway.\nopt.self.say.1 [559438] = 你谢过他们两人，用自己的密码记自己的笔记。这比你想象的更孤单。你发现，他们俩还是都在暗中照看着你。\n# en: Stay out of it\nopt.none.label [03d24a] = 置身事外\n# en: It isn't your door.\nopt.none.hint [4c3c5a] = 那不是你的门。\n# en: You turn both invitations down. Kael nods as if he expected it. The headmistress's note simply says, the next morning, \"Understood. Mind the ice.\"\nopt.none.say.1 [80761e] = 你婉拒了两份邀请。凯尔点点头，像是早就料到。第二天早上，院长的字条上只写着：“明白了。当心冰面。”\n\n@event hs_last_winter\n# en: The Last Winter Before\ntitle [77b708] = 最后一个冬天之前\n# en: The Lecture Tower has been dark for a year, and the lake freezes overnight in circles now. In spring the seal will weaken, and how you spend this winter decides what you carry beneath the lake. You have one winter and not enough evenings.\ntext [b55ff8] = 讲堂塔已经黑了一年，如今湖面会在夜里冻出一个个圆圈。到了春天封印就会变弱，而你怎样度过这个冬天，决定了你会带着什么下到湖底。你只有一个冬天，傍晚却不够用。\n# en: Lantern light in a frost-rimed window, the frozen edge of the lake beyond it, a student's desk covered in maps of seven towers.\nscene [e5b843] = 结霜的窗户里透出灯笼的光，窗外是湖面冰冻的边缘，学生的书桌上铺满了七座塔的地图。\n# en: Train with Kael on the ice\nopt.kael.label [f6da05] = 和凯尔在冰上训练\n# en: He'll fight beside you in the tunnel.\nopt.kael.hint [d4a7b2] = 他会在隧道里与你并肩作战。\n# en: He drills you on the ice until you can hold a ward with numb hands. \"Again,\" he says, and then, quietly, \"Good.\" You will remember that he said it.\nopt.kael.say.1 [e69c38] = 他在冰上一遍遍操练你，直到你能用冻僵的手撑住护界。“再来。”他说，然后轻声补了一句：“很好。”你会记得他说过这句话。\n# en: Mend wards with Maud\nopt.maud.label [7044a1] = 和莫德修补护界\n# en: She'll have taught you the wards that open stone.\nopt.maud.hint [575164] = 她会教会你打开石头的护界。\n# en: You walk the towers with her, tracing old wards back into the stone. \"Your hands are steadier than mine were,\" she says, and doesn't say whose hands she means.\nopt.maud.say.1 [9b7f55] = 你陪她走遍七塔，把古老的护界一笔一笔描回石头里。“你的手比我当年的稳。”她说，却没说她指的是谁的手。\n# en: Study with the headmistress\nopt.isolde.label [d9f337] = 跟院长学习\n# en: You'll know the founders' words by heart.\nopt.isolde.hint [81ea3e] = 你会把创始者的咒文背得滚瓜烂熟。\n# en: You lose at chess every evening and learn the founders' words between moves. Once you nearly win. Her short, delighted laugh is the warmest sound you hear all winter.\nopt.isolde.say.1 [627308] = 每个傍晚你都输棋，在落子之间学会了创始者的咒文。有一次你差点赢了。她那短促而欣喜的笑声，是你整个冬天听过最温暖的声音。\n# en: Spar with Ilse at the shore\nopt.ilse.label [b13627] = 和伊尔泽在湖边对练\n# en: She'll talk her family's servant down for you.\nopt.ilse.hint [6b85d0] = 她会替你劝退她家族的那个仆从。\n# en: She knocks you into the snow eleven times. The twelfth time you don't fall, and she looks at you as if you had done a trick she has never learned the counter to.\nopt.ilse.say.1 [86c9c1] = 她把你打进雪里十一次。第十二次你没有倒下，她看着你，仿佛你使出了一招她从没学过怎么破解的戏法。\n# en: Rest while you still can\nopt.alone.label [639301] = 趁还能休息就休息\n# en: A quiet winter. No new training, but rested.\nopt.alone.hint [ead4d4] = 一个安静的冬天。没有新的训练，但精力充沛。\n# en: You sleep, you eat, you read novels with no doors in them. It's the kindest winter you've had in years, and you can feel the lake getting colder anyway.\nopt.alone.say.1 [7e93f5] = 你睡觉，吃饭，读那些里面没有门的小说。这是你多年来最温柔的一个冬天，可你还是感觉得到湖水在一天天变冷。\n\n@event hs_ilse_door\n# en: What Ilse Was Taught\ntitle [8a9ef8] = 伊尔泽学过的东西\n# en: Ilse draws a circle in the snow with her wand, then a smaller one inside it. \"This is the door,\" she says. \"I have drawn it every day since I was six. I have never seen it.\" She doesn't look up. \"Ask me what they taught me to do. Or don't. I don't care which.\"\ntext [f6aa80] = 伊尔泽用魔杖在雪地上画了一个圆，又在里面画了一个小圆。“这就是那扇门。”她说，“我从六岁起每天都画它。我从没见过它。”她没有抬头。“问我他们教了我做什么。或者别问。我无所谓。”\n# en: A young woman in white furs drawing a round door in the snow with the tip of a wand, a frozen lake behind her.\nscene [1adcfd] = 一个身披白裘的年轻女子用魔杖尖在雪地上画一扇圆门，身后是冰封的湖。\n# en: \"What did they teach you?\"\nopt.left.label [ee8d4b] = “他们教了你什么？”\n# en: She tells you, flatly, like a lesson recited: how to stand, what to say, where to put her hands. \"They raised me to open it,\" she says. \"Nobody asked me if I wanted to.\" Then, sharply: \"Now you know. Don't look at me like that.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [e300d4] = 她平板地讲给你听，像在背诵一堂课：怎么站，说什么，手放在哪里。“他们把我养大，就是为了让我打开它。”她说，“从没人问过我想不想。”然后她语气一冷：“现在你知道了。别用那种眼神看我。”\n# en: \"You don't have to tell me.\"\nopt.right.label [194cf6] = “你不必告诉我。”\n# en: She rubs the circle out with her boot. \"Kindness,\" she says, as if naming a trick. \"I haven't learned the counter to that one yet.\" She doesn't leave, though.\nopt.right.say.1 [8dd039] = 她用靴子把那个圆抹掉。“善意。”她说，像在叫出一个戏法的名字，“这一招，我还没学会怎么破解。”不过，她没有走。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/chain_hollowstar.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/chain_hollowstar.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event hs_dreams_begin\n# en: The Black Star Dream\ntitle [c0e94a] = 黑星之梦\n# en: You dream of the lake from underneath. There is a round door at the bottom, and above the water a star that is not a light but an absence. A voice says your name like it is tasting it. You wake with frost on your pillow.\ntext [aadef3] = 你梦见从水下仰望湖面。湖底有一扇圆门，水面之上有一颗星——它不是光，而是一种缺失。一个声音念出你的名字，像在品尝它。你醒来时，枕头上结着霜。\n# en: A dreamscape: a frozen lake seen from beneath, a round door glowing at the bottom, and above it a star that is a hole in the sky.\nscene [ba146d] = 梦境：从水下仰望结冰的湖面，湖底一扇发光的圆门，上方一颗如同天空破洞的星。\n# en: Write the dream down\nopt.left.label [f34e00] = 把梦记下来\n# en: Your notes fill four pages. In the morning you don't remember writing the last line: \"It is not asleep. It is waiting.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [5620a5] = 你的笔记写满了四页。早上，你不记得自己写过最后一行：“它没有沉睡。它在等待。”\n# en: Ask Seraphine what it means\nopt.right.label [37112a] = 问瑟拉芬这是什么意思\n# en: Seraphine listens, then hands you a sprig of lavender. \"It won't stop the dreams,\" she says. \"But it'll make them smell nicer.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [2b221e] = 瑟拉芬听完，递给你一枝薰衣草。“它止不住那些梦，”她说，“但能让梦闻起来香一点。”\n\n@event hs_grey_stranger\n# en: A Man in Grey\ntitle [52d66e] = 灰衣人\n# en: Late at night in the courtyard, Kael is arguing with a tall man in a grey coat. The fountain between them has frozen mid-splash. \"The door under the lake will be opened,\" the man says calmly. \"By you, or by your sister. Your mother doesn't mind which.\"\ntext [4465b8] = 深夜的庭院里，凯尔正和一个穿灰色大衣的高个子男人争执。他们之间的喷泉在水花溅起的一刻冻住了。“湖底那扇门终会被打开，”那人平静地说，“由你，或者由你妹妹。你母亲不在乎是谁。”\n# en: A frozen fountain in a moonlit courtyard; a tall man in a grey coat facing a younger man, frost spreading from both.\nscene [27f9c8] = 月光下庭院里一座冻结的喷泉；一个穿灰大衣的高个男人与一个年轻人对峙，霜从两人脚下蔓延。\n# en: Try to slip away unseen\nopt.left.label [fb2449] = 试着悄悄溜走\n# en: You back into the shadows. The man in grey turns his head and looks straight at where you're hiding, then smiles and walks away. You don't sleep much.\nopt.left.say.1 [e83ac5] = 你退回阴影里。灰衣人转过头，直直地看向你藏身的地方，然后笑了笑，走开了。你一夜没怎么睡。\n# en: Step out and interrupt\nopt.right.label [ec8045] = 走出去打断他们\n# en: \"Everything all right?\" The man in grey studies you, nods politely and leaves; the cold he leaves behind bites through your sleeves. Kael's hands are shaking. \"You shouldn't have done that,\" he says. \"...Thank you.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [36ccb7] = “没事吧？”灰衣人打量了你一番，礼貌地点点头，走了；他留下的寒意透过袖子直往里钻。凯尔的手在发抖。“你不该这么做的，”他说，“……谢谢。”\n\n@event hs_founders_records\n# en: The Founders' Ledger\ntitle [92688a] = 创始者账簿\n# en: In the archive vault you find the Founders' Ledger, three hundred years old. The entry for the academy's founding year has been cut out with a razor. But the page beneath still holds the impression of the pen.\ntext [8885d9] = 你在档案库里找到了三百年前的《创始者账簿》。记载学院创立那一年的条目被人用刀片割掉了。但下面那一页上，仍留着笔尖的压痕。\n# en: A dusty archive vault, a giant ledger open on a lectern, one page carefully razored out.\nscene [f6e330] = 积满灰尘的档案库，讲台上摊开一本巨大的账簿，其中一页被小心地割掉了。\n# en: Leave it — this feels dangerous\nopt.left.label [7ed54a] = 算了——这感觉很危险\n# en: You close the ledger. The dust settles. Some doors are better left alone. Probably.\nopt.left.say.1 [cce53b] = 你合上账簿。灰尘落定。有些门最好别去碰。大概吧。\n# en: Rub charcoal over the page\nopt.right.label [3eeead] = 用炭笔拓印那一页\n# en: Ghostly letters emerge: \"Seven towers, seven locks. The Star sleeps beneath the water. The key is a star that holds no light.\" The founders built the academy as a seal.\nopt.right.say.1 [8ceb50] = 幽灵般的字迹浮现出来：“七塔七锁。星眠于水下。钥匙是一颗不盛光的星。”创始者建起这座学院，正是为了作一道封印。\n# en: The impression is too faint. You make out only three words: \"...beneath the water...\"\nopt.right.say.2 [bee5f6] = 压痕太浅了。你只辨认出四个字：“……于水下……”\n\n@event hs_restricted_founders\n# en: The Chained Drafts\ntitle [872dc4] = 锁链里的草稿\n# en: The archive's ledger has its founding page cut out. The Restricted Section keeps the founders' own drafts, chained to a shelf at the very back, and the chain is warm to the touch. Somewhere behind you, a book turns its own page.\ntext [c28920] = 档案库那本账簿里建校的那一页被割掉了。禁书区里藏着创始者们亲笔的草稿，用锁链锁在最里面的书架上，那锁链摸上去是温的。你身后某处，一本书自己翻了一页。\n# en: A dark aisle of the Restricted Section at midnight, a bundle of yellowed drafts chained to a shelf, the chain faintly glowing.\nscene [1a2ec0] = 午夜禁书区一条昏暗的过道，一捆发黄的草稿被锁链锁在书架上，锁链隐隐发光。\n# en: Work the chain loose\nopt.left.label [aa3f60] = 把锁链弄松\n# en: The links give one by one, and burn your fingers doing it. The drafts say it plainly, in three hands: \"Seven towers, seven locks. The Star sleeps beneath the water. The key is a star that holds no light.\" Lumenhold is a seal.\nopt.left.say.1 [9baf12] = 链环一节一节松开，烫伤了你的手指。草稿上用三种笔迹写得明明白白：“七座塔，七把锁。星沉睡于水下。钥匙是一颗不含光的星。”流明堡就是一道封印。\n# en: The chain shrieks like a ward. You're out of the window before the prefect arrives, but not before he sees your face. Detention, and a long look from the librarian.\nopt.left.say.2 [c540d6] = 锁链像护界一样尖叫起来。你在级长赶到前翻出了窗，但他还是看见了你的脸。禁闭，外加图书管理员长长的一眼。\n# en: Copy the shelf-list instead\nopt.right.label [e5a618] = 改抄书目清单\n# en: The drafts' shelf-list is less guarded and nearly as strange. One entry reads only \"Key: the seventh tower, top room. Do not lend.\" You copy it out by the light of a reading charm you have to keep alive for an hour, and it leaves you drained.\nopt.right.say.1 [ba1ef3] = 草稿的书目清单看守得没那么严，却几乎一样古怪。其中一条只写着：“钥匙：第七塔，顶层。不外借。”你借着一道得撑上一个钟头的阅读咒的光把它抄下来，抄完人都虚脱了。\n\n@event hs_boathouse_bell\n# en: The Hatch in the Boathouse\ntitle [333304] = 船屋里的活板门\n# en: Under a coil of rotten rope in the boathouse there's a hatch, and under the hatch, stone steps going down into the lake. The air coming up smells of cold bronze. From somewhere below, very faint, a bell is tolling under the water.\ntext [d8dbb2] = 船屋里一卷烂绳子底下有一扇活板门，门下是通往湖里的石阶。往上涌的空气带着冰冷的青铜味。下面某处，非常微弱，一口钟正在水底鸣响。\n# en: The inside of an old wooden boathouse at night, a square hatch open in the floorboards, stone steps leading down into dark water.\nscene [947bde] = 夜里一座旧木船屋的内部，地板上一扇方形活板门敞开着，石阶通向漆黑的水里。\n# en: Go down the steps\nopt.left.label [26f409] = 走下台阶\n# en: The steps become a tunnel under the lakebed, dry and ice-cold, running toward the sound. You follow it until you can see the bell's green rim in the dark ahead. Then you turn back, frozen to the bone, and remember every turning.\nopt.left.say.1 [1ef535] = 台阶变成了湖床下的一条隧道，干燥而冰冷，朝着钟声延伸。你一直走到能看见前方黑暗中那口钟青绿的边沿。然后你转身往回走，冻得透骨，每一个拐弯都记在心里。\n# en: The third step is ice. You go into the water to the chest and come up gasping, but you've seen it: a tunnel under the lake, running toward the bell. You walk home soaked.\nopt.left.say.2 [c6b05c] = 第三级台阶结了冰。你一下子掉进齐胸深的水里，喘着气爬出来，但你看见了：湖底有条隧道，通向那口钟。你湿淋淋地走回了家。\n# en: Listen at the waterline\nopt.right.label [8f90c9] = 在水边倾听\n# en: You lie on the boards with your ear to the gap. The bell tolls seven times, slow, and between the strokes you hear a draught moving under the lake, the way air moves in a corridor. Something down there is a door. You don't sleep well.\nopt.right.say.1 [0754e3] = 你趴在木板上，耳朵贴着缝隙。钟慢慢地敲了七下，在两响之间，你听见湖底有气流在移动，就像空气在走廊里流动那样。下面有一扇门。你一夜没睡好。\n\n@event hs_seraphine_shadow\n# en: One Star Too Many\ntitle [bd51fe] = 多出来的一颗星\n# en: Seraphine has turned the great telescope down, not at the sky but at the lake, where the stars are reflected. \"Look,\" she says. \"In the water. There's one star too many, and it's dark.\" She counts under her breath. \"It wasn't there in my dream. It was in yours.\"\ntext [f656d5] = 瑟拉芬把那架大望远镜往下转，没对着天空，而是对着倒映着星星的湖面。“看，”她说，“水里。多了一颗星，而且是暗的。”她低声数着。“我的梦里没有它。是你的梦里有。”\n# en: An observatory at night, the great telescope tilted down toward the window and the lake below, a seer pointing at the reflection of the stars in the water.\nscene [93fed0] = 夜里的观星台，大望远镜朝下对着窗外的湖，一位预言者指着水中星星的倒影。\n# en: Chart it with her\nopt.left.label [c4492b] = 和她一起绘出它的位置\n# en: You chart it till dawn on the cold floor: a small dark shape in the reflection that has no star above it, sitting exactly over the middle of the lake. Your knees never quite forgive you.\nopt.left.say.1 [ff1233] = 你们在冰冷的地板上一直画到天亮：倒影里有一个小小的暗影，上方的天空里却没有对应的星，它正好停在湖的正中央。你的膝盖再也没完全原谅你。\n# en: Tell her about your dream\nopt.right.label [cc1d2c] = 告诉她你的梦\n# en: She finishes your sentences, three of them, before you've started them. Then she shows you the dark star in the water, right over the middle of the lake. You both stop talking for a long time.\nopt.right.say.1 [167dc3] = 你还没开口，她就替你说完了三句话。然后她指给你看水中那颗暗星，正在湖心上方。你们俩很久都没再说话。\n\n@event hs_kael_truth\n# en: What Kael Is Running From\ntitle [5fe24d] = 凯尔在逃避什么\n# en: Kael sits with you on the boathouse jetty. \"My family serve something under this lake,\" he says at last. \"They sent me here to open its door. I came to stop them. Some nights I'm not sure which I'll actually do.\"\ntext [01cf3e] = 凯尔和你一起坐在船屋的栈桥上。“我的家族侍奉着这湖底的某样东西，”他终于开口，“他们派我来打开它的门。我来是为了阻止他们。有些夜里，我也不确定自己最后会做哪一件。”\n# en: Two students sitting on the edge of the frozen boathouse jetty at night, one with frost in his hair, both looking down at the black water.\nscene [ede058] = 夜里两个学生坐在结冰的船屋栈桥边缘，其中一人发间挂着霜，两人都低头看着漆黑的湖水。\n# en: \"Then we stop them together.\"\nopt.left.label [72f262] = “那我们一起阻止他们。”\n# en: He looks at you for a long moment, and the frost on the jetty melts a little. \"Together,\" he repeats, as if trying out a word from another language.\nopt.left.say.1 [734531] = 他久久地看着你，栈桥上的霜融化了一点。“一起。”他重复道，像在尝试一个外语单词。\n# en: Tell the headmistress\nopt.right.label [bcf093] = 告诉院长\n# en: The headmistress thanks you gravely. The next day Kael is moved to a room in the headmistress's tower. He doesn't look at you anymore.\nopt.right.say.1 [07e3f1] = 院长郑重地感谢了你。第二天，凯尔被搬进了院长塔楼里的一个房间。他再也不看你了。\n\n@event hs_star_mark\n# en: The Cold Coin Burns\ntitle [2ace55] = 冰冷的硬币在灼烧\n# en: The black-starred relic you've been carrying burns suddenly, so cold it feels hot. When you drop it, there's a pale star-shaped mark on your palm. It hums when you're near the lake.\ntext [32d3ff] = 你一直带在身上的那件黑星遗物突然灼烧起来，冷到像是滚烫。你把它扔下时，掌心多了一枚苍白的星形印记。靠近湖边时，它会嗡嗡作响。\n# en: A student's open palm, a pale star-shaped mark glowing on it, a blackened coin smoking on the floor beside them.\nscene [be9e03] = 一个学生摊开的掌心，上面闪着苍白的星形印记，一枚发黑的硬币在旁边的地上冒着烟。\n# en: Throw the relic into the lake\nopt.left.label [9e07e3] = 把遗物扔进湖里\n# en: It sinks without a ripple. The mark on your hand fades — almost entirely. Something under the water sighs.\nopt.left.say.1 [f57ba3] = 它沉了下去，没起一丝涟漪。你手上的印记褪去了——几乎完全褪去。水下有什么东西叹了口气。\n# en: Keep it. Feel the power.\nopt.right.label [9c18f9] = 留着它。感受这份力量。\n# en: The mark pulses with every heartbeat. Spells come easier now. Much easier. That should probably worry you more than it does.\nopt.right.say.1 [49d379] = 印记随着每一次心跳搏动。现在施法容易多了。容易太多了。这本该比现在更让你担心才对。\n\n@event hs_grey_offer\n# en: An Offer in Grey\ntitle [72fb9a] = 灰色的提议\n# en: The man in grey is waiting at your library table. \"You've been asking questions about the Star,\" he says pleasantly. \"It's noticed you. It could make you extraordinary. All it wants is for someone to open a door.\"\ntext [8e2f80] = 灰衣人正等在你的图书馆座位上。“你一直在打听那颗星，”他和颜悦色地说，“它注意到你了。它能让你非同凡响。它想要的，只是有人去打开一扇门。”\n# en: A grey-coated man sitting uninvited at a library table, a black velvet box open in front of him, the candles around him burning blue.\nscene [252bba] = 一个穿灰大衣的男人不请自来地坐在图书馆桌旁，面前打开着一只黑丝绒盒子，周围的蜡烛燃着蓝色火焰。\n# en: \"Get out.\"\nopt.left.label [f967c3] = “滚出去。”\n# en: He inclines his head and leaves. The candles return to yellow. You realise your hands are trembling.\nopt.left.say.1 [0a3723] = 他欠了欠身，离开了。蜡烛恢复了黄色。你这才发现自己的手在发抖。\n# en: \"...What would it give me?\"\nopt.right.label [d466af] = “……它能给我什么？”\n# en: He shows you, just for a moment. Power, endless and cold. When he's gone, you can still taste it.\nopt.right.say.1 [5c20e2] = 他给你看了，只有一瞬间。力量，无穷无尽，冰冷彻骨。他走后，你仍能尝到那股滋味。\n\n@event hs_grey_invitation\n# en: A Seat at the Northern Table\ntitle [c52641] = 北境之席\n# en: There is a letter on your pillow, sealed with a black star. The handwriting is beautiful. \"The families have watched you with great interest,\" it says. \"There is a seat for you at our table, when the door is opened. Come to the boathouse at midnight, or burn this. Either is an answer.\"\ntext [13f2ac] = 你的枕头上放着一封信，用一枚黑色的星封着蜡。字迹很漂亮。“世家怀着极大的兴趣关注着你，”信上写道，“门打开之时，我们的席上会有你一个座位。午夜到船屋来，或者烧了这封信。两者都是答复。”\n# en: A black-edged letter on a dormitory pillow, its wax seal pressed with a star that has no light in it.\nscene [f56301] = 宿舍枕头上一封镶黑边的信，封蜡上压着一颗不发光的星。\n# en: Go to the boathouse\nopt.left.label [526aa1] = 去船屋\n# en: The man in grey is waiting with two cups of something hot. He talks to you as an equal for an hour. You come back with frost in your hair and the feeling of having been measured, and found to fit.\nopt.left.say.1 [c84e12] = 灰衣人端着两杯热饮在等你。他像对待平辈一样和你谈了一个小时。你回来时头发上沾着霜，觉得自己被丈量过，而且尺寸正合适。\n# en: Burn it\nopt.right.label [162285] = 烧掉它\n# en: The paper burns blue and slow, and smells of the lake. It takes a long time to go out. Climbing back from where you've been is slower still, but this is a step.\nopt.right.say.1 [dfcc08] = 信纸燃起蓝色的火，烧得很慢，散发着湖水的气味。它很久才烧尽。从你走到的地方爬回来还要更慢，但这是一步。\n\n@event hs_grey_tests\n# en: The Good Argument\ntitle [e22d78] = 好听的道理\n# en: The man in grey falls into step beside you outside the infirmary. \"You help people,\" he says pleasantly. \"I admire it. But think how many more you could help with real power. The Star doesn't care what it's used for. Only that someone uses it.\"\ntext [9adfef] = 灰衣人在医务室外与你并肩而行。“你帮助别人，”他和气地说，“我很欣赏。可想想看，若有真正的力量，你能多帮多少人。星不在乎它被用来做什么，只在乎有人用它。”\n# en: A grey-coated man standing beside a student at the infirmary window, both looking down at a first-year with a bandaged arm.\nscene [86ebbc] = 一个穿灰外套的男人和一名学生并肩站在医务室窗前，一起低头看着一个手臂缠着绷带的一年级生。\n# en: \"No.\"\nopt.left.label [371a7a] = “不。”\n# en: \"No,\" you say. He smiles as if you'd said something charming, and leaves. The first-year in the window waves at you. You wave back, and your hand is shaking.\nopt.left.say.1 [ce8c7e] = “不，”你说。他笑了笑，仿佛你说了句讨人喜欢的话，然后离开了。窗边的一年级生朝你挥手。你也挥手，手在发抖。\n# en: \"...How many more?\"\nopt.right.label [faa864] = “……能多帮多少？”\n# en: He tells you. The numbers are very large and very cold. That night, you dream of the lake from underneath, and you are not frightened in the dream.\nopt.right.say.1 [3239c8] = 他告诉了你。那些数字非常大，也非常冷。那天夜里，你梦见从湖底往上看，而梦里的你并不害怕。\n\n@event hs_wardens_notice\n# en: The Headmistress Takes Note\ntitle [f7baa8] = 院长记下了你\n# en: On the grand stair the headmistress stops beside you. \"Several people have told me about you,\" she says, \"and not one of them was complaining. That is rare.\" Her silver ring catches the light. \"I keep a list of students I would trust with a door. Don't let it go to your head.\"\ntext [aef554] = 在大楼梯上，院长在你身旁停下。“好几个人跟我提起过你，”她说，“没有一个是来告状的。这很少见。”她的银戒指映着光。“我有一份名单，上面是我愿意托付一扇门的学生。别因此得意忘形。”\n# en: A tall woman in dark robes pausing on a stair to look down at one student among many, a silver ring glinting on her hand.\nscene [25a5bf] = 一位身着深色长袍的高个女人在楼梯上停步，低头看着众多学生中的一个，手上的银戒指闪着光。\n# en: \"I'd be honoured.\"\nopt.left.label [bec864] = “我深感荣幸。”\n# en: \"Good,\" she says. \"Honour is a heavy thing to carry. Practise.\" She walks on. You stand on the stair for some time.\nopt.left.say.1 [e74b53] = “很好，”她说，“荣誉是件沉重的东西。多练练。”她继续往前走。你在楼梯上站了好一会儿。\n# en: \"What kind of door?\"\nopt.right.label [ee5679] = “什么样的门？”\n# en: Her short, delighted laugh. \"The kind that ought to stay shut,\" she says. \"Keep asking questions like that. Just not on the stairs.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [c568f6] = 她发出那声短促而愉快的笑。“应该一直关着的那种，”她说，“继续问这样的问题吧。只是别在楼梯上问。”\n\n@event hs_seventh_tower\n# en: The Seventh Tower\ntitle [44b8dc] = 第七塔\n# en: The seventh tower has been locked for a century. You find a way in. At the very top, a single room: dust, moonlight, and a star-shaped hollow in the wall, holding something that does not reflect any light.\ntext [9081b5] = 第七塔已经锁了一个世纪。你找到了进去的路。塔顶只有一个房间：灰尘、月光，墙上一个星形的凹槽，里面放着某样不反射任何光的东西。\n# en: The top of a narrow, abandoned tower, cobwebbed and moonlit, with a wall bearing a star-shaped recess.\nscene [c775ba] = 一座狭窄废塔的顶层，蛛网密布，月光洒入，墙上有一个星形凹槽。\n# en: Take it quietly\nopt.left.label [2a0b10] = 悄悄拿走\n# en: A key shaped like a star, black as a hole in the world. As you lift it, every candle in the academy goes out for one breath. You walk back in the dark with it cold against your ribs, and you don't sleep.\nopt.left.say.1 [f9b295] = 一把星形的钥匙，黑得像世界上的一个洞。你拿起它的一刻，学院里每一支蜡烛都熄灭了一口气的工夫。你揣着它在黑暗中走回去，它冰冷地贴着你的肋骨，你一夜没睡。\n# en: A ward shrieks. A prefect finds you on the stairs. You get detention and a very long look from the headmistress.\nopt.left.say.2 [ca0973] = 防护咒尖叫起来。一位级长在楼梯上逮住了你。你被关了禁闭，还挨了院长很长很长的一眼。\n# en: Take it boldly\nopt.right.label [f86c9d] = 强行拿走\n# en: You tear through the ward with brute force, and it drains you to the bone. The key comes free. Every candle in the academy goes out for one breath, and somewhere below, a door hears it.\nopt.right.say.1 [777369] = 你凭蛮力撕开了护界，这把你耗得精疲力尽。钥匙脱落下来。学院里每一支蜡烛都熄灭了一口气的工夫，而在下面某处，一扇门听见了。\n# en: The ward throws you down two flights of stairs.\nopt.right.say.2 [1014e6] = 防护咒把你甩下了两层楼梯。\n\n@event hs_key_breakfast\n# en: Something of Mine\ntitle [f9fe6f] = 我的东西\n# en: At breakfast the headmistress sets down her cup and looks straight at you along the whole length of the hall. Afterwards she is waiting at the door. \"Every candle in my academy went out,\" she says pleasantly, \"and you have something of mine.\"\ntext [e0137d] = 早餐时，校长放下杯子，隔着整个大厅直直地看着你。之后她在门口等你。“我学院里的每一支蜡烛都灭了，”她愉快地说，“而你拿着我的一样东西。”\n# en: The high table at breakfast, the headmistress setting down her cup and looking along the hall at one student, the candles behind her newly lit.\nscene [fdadd9] = 早餐时的高桌，校长放下杯子，沿着大厅望向一名学生，她身后的蜡烛刚刚重新点亮。\n# en: Give her the key\nopt.left.label [28b877] = 把钥匙交给她\n# en: She takes it in a gloved hand without looking at it. \"Thank you. It will be where it is needed, on the night. So, I hope, will you.\" You feel lighter, and a little robbed.\nopt.left.say.1 [86a9c7] = 她用戴手套的手接过去，看都没看一眼。“谢谢。那一夜，它会在需要它的地方。我希望你也会。”你觉得轻松了些，又有点像被抢了。\n# en: Keep it\nopt.right.label [87c2f5] = 留着它\n# en: A long pause. \"Then keep it close,\" she says, \"and never let it near the water before its time. People will come asking for it. Not all of them will ask.\" From then on, the man in grey always seems to know where you are.\nopt.right.say.1 [d35dc1] = 长长的停顿。“那就贴身收好，”她说，“时候未到，绝不要让它靠近湖水。会有人来讨要它。不是每个人都会开口讨。”从那以后，灰衣人似乎总知道你在哪儿。\n\n@event hs_isolde_summons\n# en: The Headmistress Knows\ntitle [214bdc] = 院长知道了\n# en: The headmistress summons you to her tower. \"You have been following the same thread I have,\" she says. \"On the night of the Blossom moon, the seal will weaken. I need people I can trust beneath the lake. Or you can walk away. No one would blame you.\"\ntext [aa0d92] = 院长把你召进她的塔楼。“你一直在追寻和我同一条线索，”她说，“花潮之月那一夜，封印会减弱。我需要可以信任的人到湖底去。你也可以选择离开。没有人会怪你。”\n# en: A circular study at the top of a tower, walls lined with sleeping portraits, the headmistress standing by a window that looks down on the lake.\nscene [8d69ed] = 塔顶的圆形书房，墙上挂满沉睡的肖像，院长站在俯瞰湖面的窗前。\n# en: Walk away\nopt.left.label [96da1a] = 离开\n# en: \"Wise,\" she says, and you can't tell if she means it. \"Then stay above, and keep the first-years indoors.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [00df9c] = “明智。”她说，你分不清她是不是真心的。“那就留在上面，让一年级生待在屋里。”\n# en: \"I'll be there.\"\nopt.right.label [d06338] = “我会去的。”\n# en: She presses a silver ward-ring into your hand. \"{flag(star_key): The founders' key is with you. Keep it close.|{flag(gave_star_key): The founders' key is in my desk.|The founders' key is kept at the top of the seventh tower.}} On the night, it comes down with us. Learn quickly. You have until Blossomtide.\" You lose a little sleep over that every night.\nopt.right.say.1 [76415e] = 她把一枚银色的守护戒指按进你手心。“{flag(star_key): 创始者的钥匙在你身上。收好。|{flag(gave_star_key): 创始者的钥匙在我的书桌里。|创始者的钥匙藏在第七塔塔顶。}}那一夜，它会跟我们一起下去。学快点。你只有到花潮月为止的时间。”每天夜里，你都为这事少睡一会儿。\n\n@event hs_the_night\n# en: The Night the Lake Froze\ntitle [6f7fcc] = 湖面冰封之夜\n# en: In the middle of spring, the lake freezes in a single breath. Cracks glow beneath the ice. The bell under the water is tolling, and every candle in the academy leans toward the shore.\ntext [9423a3] = 正值春天，湖面却在一口气之间冻结了。冰下的裂缝发着光。湖底的钟在敲响，学院里每一支蜡烛都朝着湖岸倾斜。\n# en: The whole lake frozen white in the middle of spring, cracks glowing from beneath, students crowding the towers' windows.\nscene [b58de9] = 春日里整个湖面冻得雪白，冰下裂缝透出光芒，学生们挤在塔楼的窗前。\n# en: Go down beneath the lake\nopt.left.label [983634] = 潜入湖底\n# en: You run for the boathouse. Behind you, someone is shouting your name. You don't stop.\nopt.left.say.1 [28f348] = 你朝船屋奔去。身后有人在喊你的名字。你没有停下。\n# en: Stay above and protect the students\nopt.right.label [3d4500] = 留在上面保护学生\n# en: You hold the doors of the first-year tower all night against things made of frost and whispers. By dawn the lake thaws. Something below is still asleep — for now.\nopt.right.say.1 [cee4e5] = 你整夜守着一年级塔楼的大门，抵挡由冰霜与低语构成的东西。黎明时湖面解冻了。下面的那个东西仍在沉睡——暂时。\n\n@event hs_tunnel\n# en: The Man in the Tunnel\ntitle [809d66] = 隧道里的人\n# en: The tunnel from the boathouse runs down under the ice, slick and glowing blue. Halfway down, the man in grey is waiting{=flag(ilse_taken) and \", and Ilse stands beside him with her eyes on nothing\" or \", alone, as if he has all night\"}. \"Go back up,\" he says pleasantly. \"This isn't your door.\"\ntext [0bcdbe] = 从船屋出发的隧道一路向下，钻到冰层底下，湿滑而泛着蓝光。走到一半，灰衣人正等在那里{=flag(ilse_taken) and \"，伊尔泽站在他身边，目光空洞\" or \"，独自一人，仿佛他有整夜的时间\"}。“回上面去吧，”他愉快地说，“这不是你的门。”\n# en: A slick stone tunnel under the ice, lit blue from above, a tall man in a grey coat standing in the middle of it with his hands folded.\nscene [07762c] = 冰层下一条湿滑的石隧道，上方透下蓝光，一个穿灰大衣的高个男人双手交握，站在隧道中间。\n# en: Break through him\nopt.fight.label [07b59f] = 从他身上闯过去\n# en: Combat. Easier with Kael at your side.\nopt.fight.hint [fe2cd0] = 战斗。有凯尔在身边会更容易。\n# en: Kael's frost and your spell hit him together. He slides back along the ice, bows, and is simply not there any more. You go on with bruised ribs and a clear road.\nopt.fight.say.1 [ffdeff] = 凯尔的寒霜和你的咒语同时击中了他。他沿着冰面滑了出去，鞠了一躬，然后就不在那儿了。你带着青肿的肋骨走上畅通的路。\n# en: He swats the two of you aside like moths, then steps back to let you pass, smiling. \"Go on, then.\" You go down bleeding, and late.\nopt.fight.say.2 [7290b7] = 他像拍飞蛾一样把你们俩扫到一边，然后退开让你们过去，微笑着说：“那就去吧。”你流着血下去，晚了一步。\n# en: Your spell catches him square, and he slides back along the ice, surprised for the first time. He bows and is gone. You go on with bruised ribs and a clear road.\nopt.fight.say.3 [d1e5ad] = 你的咒语正中他胸口，他沿着冰面滑了出去，第一次露出了意外的神情。他鞠了一躬，消失了。你带着青肿的肋骨走上畅通的路。\n# en: He swats your spell aside, then steps back to let you pass, smiling. \"Go on, then.\" You go down bleeding, and late.\nopt.fight.say.4 [4f4f17] = 他把你的咒语拍到一边，然后退开让你过去，微笑着说：“那就去吧。”你流着血下去，晚了一步。\n# en: Talk your way past\nopt.talk.label [f5bc3b] = 说服他让路\n# en: Charm. Easier if Ilse is safe, or you know what she was raised for.\nopt.talk.hint [ac568b] = 魅力。如果伊尔泽平安，或者你知道她被养大的目的，会更容易。\n# en: You tell him the Dumont children have chosen, both of them, and that you know exactly what they were raised for. For a moment he looks old. Then he steps aside. It costs you something to see that look.\nopt.talk.say.1 [086c71] = 你告诉他，杜蒙家的两个孩子都已经做了选择，而你清清楚楚地知道他们被养大是为了什么。有那么一刻，他显得苍老了。然后他让开了。看见那副神情，你心里也付出了点什么。\n# en: He listens courteously, agrees with every word, and lets you pass only once you've heard every word of his. You arrive shaken, and late.\nopt.talk.say.2 [631e3a] = 他彬彬有礼地听着，句句都表示赞同，直到你把他要说的每一句都听完，才放你过去。你到达时心神不宁，也晚了。\n# en: You talk fast and well, and somewhere in it he decides you're more trouble than you're worth. He steps aside with a little bow. Your hands don't stop shaking for a while.\nopt.talk.say.3 [30715a] = 你说得又快又好，说着说着他认定你麻烦得不值得。他微微鞠躬让开了。你的手抖了好一阵才停。\n# en: He listens courteously, agrees with every word, and lets you pass only once you've heard every word of his. You arrive shaken, and late.\nopt.talk.say.4 [631e3a] = 他彬彬有礼地听着，句句都表示赞同，直到你把他要说的每一句都听完，才放你过去。你到达时心神不宁，也晚了。\n# en: Find another way down\nopt.slip.label [1e4f9e] = 另找一条路下去\n# en: Guile. Easier if you know the old tunnels.\nopt.slip.hint [e07b2c] = 诡计。如果你熟悉那些旧隧道，会更容易。\n# en: You know a side passage he doesn't, the one the bell's rope once ran through. You come out below him, breathless and spent, and he never sees you go.\nopt.slip.say.1 [c75c91] = 你知道一条他不知道的岔道，就是当年钟绳穿过的那条。你从他下方钻了出来，气喘吁吁、精疲力尽，而他根本没看见你走过。\n# en: The side passage is half flooded. You come out soaked and freezing, below him, but late, and he calls a polite good evening after you.\nopt.slip.say.2 [705c25] = 岔道一半被水淹了。你从他下方钻出来，浑身湿透、冻得发抖，但晚了一步，他还在你身后客气地道了声晚安。\n# en: You fold yourself into the dark and go round him along the wall, a breath at a time. It drains you, but he never turns his head.\nopt.slip.say.3 [70ef4d] = 你把自己折进黑暗里，贴着墙一口气一口气地绕过他。这让你精疲力尽，但他始终没有转头。\n# en: He turns his head the moment you move. \"Good evening.\" He lets you pass anyway, and the cold he leaves in your bones comes with you.\nopt.slip.say.4 [e287c0] = 你一动，他就转过了头。“晚上好。”他还是放你过去了，而他留在你骨头里的寒意也跟着你下去了。\n\n@event hs_threshold\n# en: The Founders' Words\ntitle [5a0a8c] = 创始者的咒文\n# en: The tunnel ends at a wall of black stone carved with the founders' words, in a script that drifts when you look away. Behind it the bell is tolling. The wall will open for someone who can read it, or someone who can make it.\ntext [bc4d6a] = 隧道尽头是一面黑石墙，上面刻着创始者的咒文，字迹在你移开目光时会悄悄漂移。墙后，钟声在响。这面墙会为能读懂它的人打开，或者为能逼它打开的人打开。\n# en: A wall of black stone at the end of a tunnel, carved with lines of script that drift when not looked at, a faint bell-tone hanging in the air.\nscene [f06531] = 隧道尽头的一面黑石墙，刻着一行行不被注视时就会漂移的字，空气中悬着一丝微弱的钟声。\n# en: Read the founders' words\nopt.read.label [78313a] = 念出创始者的咒文\n# en: Lore. The founders' seal and the seven locks make it easier.\nopt.read.hint [4b99a5] = 学识。知道创始者的封印和七把锁会更容易。\n# en: You've read these words before, in pieces, and you know where the locks fall. Said whole, they open the wall like a door, and the strain of it sits behind your eyes.\nopt.read.say.1 [66592f] = 这些字你以前零零碎碎读到过，也知道那几把锁落在哪里。一口气念完，墙像门一样打开了，那股吃力的劲儿压在你眼睛后面。\n# en: You stumble on the last line, and the wall opens only a crack. You squeeze through it; it scrapes skin off your shoulder on the way.\nopt.read.say.2 [9a802e] = 你在最后一行卡住了，墙只开了一道缝。你从缝里挤过去，肩膀上被刮掉了一层皮。\n# en: You know enough to guess the rest. The wall listens, and slides aside, and the strain of it sits behind your eyes.\nopt.read.say.3 [d417b9] = 你知道的足够猜出其余部分。墙听着，滑向一边，那股吃力的劲儿压在你眼睛后面。\n# en: You stumble on the last line, and the wall opens only a crack. You squeeze through it; it scrapes skin off your shoulder on the way.\nopt.read.say.4 [9a802e] = 你在最后一行卡住了，墙只开了一道缝。你从缝里挤过去，肩膀上被刮掉了一层皮。\n# en: You don't know the words, but you know how words like these are built. Line by line, the wall gives way, and the strain of it sits behind your eyes.\nopt.read.say.5 [8d1233] = 你不认识这些字，但你知道这样的字是怎么构成的。一行一行地，墙让开了，那股吃力的劲儿压在你眼睛后面。\n# en: The script slides away from you. In the end the wall opens a crack on its own, as if amused. You squeeze through; it takes skin with it.\nopt.read.say.6 [b9d435] = 那些字从你眼前溜走。最后墙自己开了一道缝，像是觉得好笑。你挤了过去，它带走了你一层皮。\n# en: Force the stone open\nopt.force.label [da75b6] = 强行打开石墙\n# en: Arcana, and it hurts. Easier if Maud taught you her wards.\nopt.force.hint [4dd1c8] = 奥术，而且会疼。如果莫德教过你她的护界，会更容易。\n# en: You trace Maud's wards backwards into the stone, the way she showed you. The wall cracks down the middle and opens, and your hands ache to the elbow.\nopt.force.say.1 [8407a2] = 你照莫德教你的那样，把她的护界反着描进石头里。墙从中间裂开，打开了，你的双手一直疼到手肘。\n# en: The stone fights back. You get through in the end, but your hands are raw and shaking.\nopt.force.say.2 [2e5d18] = 石头在反抗。你最后还是过去了，但双手血肉模糊，抖个不停。\n# en: You put everything you have into the stone, and it cracks down the middle. Your hands ache to the elbow.\nopt.force.say.3 [8e17f7] = 你把全部力量都压进石头里，它从中间裂开了。你的双手一直疼到手肘。\n# en: The stone fights back. You get through in the end, but your hands are raw and shaking.\nopt.force.say.4 [2e5d18] = 石头在反抗。你最后还是过去了，但双手血肉模糊，抖个不停。\n\n@event hs_beneath\n# en: The Door Beneath the Lake\ntitle [76c2d3] = 湖底之门\n# en: Beyond the wall, the round door. It stands open a crack. Through it, the Hollow Star looks at you — and it is beautiful, and it is hungry, and it knows your name.{=flag(kael_ally) and \" Kael is beside you, wand raised, frost spreading from his feet.\" or \"\"}\ntext [c4b228] = 墙后就是那扇圆门。它开着一道缝。透过门缝，空洞之星在看着你——它很美，它很饥饿，它知道你的名字。{=flag(kael_ally) and \"凯尔就在你身旁，魔杖高举，冰霜从他脚下蔓延开来。\" or \"\"}\n# en: A vast domed cavern under the lake, a round door of black stone standing open a crack, and through it a star that is pure absence.\nscene [d3244b] = 湖底一座巨大的穹形洞窟，一扇黑石圆门开着一道缝，门后是一颗纯粹由“缺失”构成的星。\n# en: Seal the door\nopt.seal.label [b5b84d] = 封印这扇门\n# en: Needs the star key, the founders' secret or a ward-ring. Arcana; preparation and the way down make it easier. It can fail.\nopt.seal.hint [1129af] = 需要星钥、创始者的秘密或一枚守护戒指。奥术；准备和下来的路走得越顺越容易。可能失败。\n# en: {flag(knows_founders_seal): You speak the founders' words|You set the seal with everything you have}, and the door grinds shut. The Star screams without sound. Above, the ice cracks and the lake breathes again.\nopt.seal.say.1 [894b56] = {flag(knows_founders_seal): 你念出创始者的咒文|你倾尽所有设下封印}，门轰隆隆地合上了。星无声地尖叫。上方，冰层碎裂，湖重新开始呼吸。\n# en: Your seal falters. Kael throws every ounce of frost in him into it, and it still isn't enough: the door stays ajar. The last thing you see is the headmistress's lantern coming down the tunnel.\nopt.seal.say.2 [69a006] = 你的封印动摇了。凯尔把体内每一分寒冰都押了上去，仍然不够：门依旧半开着。你看见的最后一样东西，是校长的提灯沿着隧道下来。\n# en: Your seal falters and breaks, and takes most of you with it. The door stays ajar. The last thing you see is the headmistress's lantern coming down the tunnel.\nopt.seal.say.3 [c2675b] = 你的封印动摇、碎裂，几乎带走了你的全部。门依旧半开着。你看见的最后一样东西，是校长的提灯沿着隧道下来。\n# en: {flag(knows_founders_seal): You speak the founders' words|You set the seal with everything you have}, and the door grinds shut. The Star screams without sound. Above, the ice cracks and the lake breathes again.\nopt.seal.say.4 [894b56] = {flag(knows_founders_seal): 你念出创始者的咒文|你倾尽所有设下封印}，门轰隆隆地合上了。星无声地尖叫。上方，冰层碎裂，湖重新开始呼吸。\n# en: Your seal falters. Kael throws every ounce of frost in him into it, and it still isn't enough: the door stays ajar. The last thing you see is the headmistress's lantern coming down the tunnel.\nopt.seal.say.5 [69a006] = 你的封印动摇了。凯尔把体内每一分寒冰都押了上去，仍然不够：门依旧半开着。你看见的最后一样东西，是校长的提灯沿着隧道下来。\n# en: Your seal falters and breaks, and takes most of you with it. The door stays ajar. The last thing you see is the headmistress's lantern coming down the tunnel.\nopt.seal.say.6 [c2675b] = 你的封印动摇、碎裂，几乎带走了你的全部。门依旧半开着。你看见的最后一样东西，是校长的提灯沿着隧道下来。\n# en: {flag(knows_founders_seal): You speak the founders' words|You set the seal with everything you have}, and the door grinds shut. The Star screams without sound. Above, the ice cracks and the lake breathes again.\nopt.seal.say.7 [894b56] = {flag(knows_founders_seal): 你念出创始者的咒文|你倾尽所有设下封印}，门轰隆隆地合上了。星无声地尖叫。上方，冰层碎裂，湖重新开始呼吸。\n# en: Your seal falters. Kael throws every ounce of frost in him into it, and it still isn't enough: the door stays ajar. The last thing you see is the headmistress's lantern coming down the tunnel.\nopt.seal.say.8 [69a006] = 你的封印动摇了。凯尔把体内每一分寒冰都押了上去，仍然不够：门依旧半开着。你看见的最后一样东西，是校长的提灯沿着隧道下来。\n# en: Your seal falters and breaks, and takes most of you with it. The door stays ajar. The last thing you see is the headmistress's lantern coming down the tunnel.\nopt.seal.say.9 [c2675b] = 你的封印动摇、碎裂，几乎带走了你的全部。门依旧半开着。你看见的最后一样东西，是校长的提灯沿着隧道下来。\n# en: {flag(knows_founders_seal): You speak the founders' words|You set the seal with everything you have}, and the door grinds shut. The Star screams without sound. Above, the ice cracks and the lake breathes again.\nopt.seal.say.10 [894b56] = {flag(knows_founders_seal): 你念出创始者的咒文|你倾尽所有设下封印}，门轰然合上。空洞之星无声地尖叫。上方，冰层开裂，湖水重新开始呼吸。\n# en: Your seal falters. Kael throws every ounce of frost in him into it, and it still isn't enough: the door stays ajar. The last thing you see is the headmistress's lantern coming down the tunnel.\nopt.seal.say.11 [69a006] = 你的封印动摇了。凯尔把体内每一分寒冰都押了上去，仍然不够：门依旧半开着。你看见的最后一样东西，是校长的提灯沿着隧道下来。\n# en: Your seal falters and breaks, and takes most of you with it. The door stays ajar. The last thing you see is the headmistress's lantern coming down the tunnel.\nopt.seal.say.12 [c2675b] = 你的封印动摇、碎裂，几乎带走了你的全部。门依旧半开着。你看见的最后一样东西，是校长的提灯沿着隧道下来。\n# en: {flag(knows_founders_seal): You speak the founders' words|You set the seal with everything you have}, and the door grinds shut. The Star screams without sound. Above, the ice cracks and the lake breathes again.\nopt.seal.say.13 [894b56] = {flag(knows_founders_seal): 你念出创始者的咒文|你倾尽所有设下封印}，门轰然合上。空洞之星无声地尖叫。上方，冰层开裂，湖水重新开始呼吸。\n# en: Your seal falters. Kael throws every ounce of frost in him into it, and it still isn't enough: the door stays ajar. The last thing you see is the headmistress's lantern coming down the tunnel.\nopt.seal.say.14 [69a006] = 你的封印动摇了。凯尔把体内每一分寒冰都押了上去，仍然不够：门依旧半开着。你看见的最后一样东西，是校长的提灯沿着隧道下来。\n# en: Your seal falters and breaks, and takes most of you with it. The door stays ajar. The last thing you see is the headmistress's lantern coming down the tunnel.\nopt.seal.say.15 [c2675b] = 你的封印动摇、碎裂，几乎带走了你的全部。门依旧半开着。你看见的最后一样东西，是校长的提灯沿着隧道下来。\n# en: Accept the Star's bargain\nopt.bargain.label [3a1372] = 接受星的交易\n# en: Power beyond measure. A voice in your head forever. Only for those it has already tempted.\nopt.bargain.hint [8c7575] = 无法估量的力量。脑海里永远多一个声音。只给已经被它诱惑过的人。\n# en: You take what it offers, and it feels like coming home. The door closes on its own, for now. The Star settles into the back of your mind like a cat finding a warm lap. The price comes out of your body: you climb the tunnel grey-lipped, and for days every stair leaves you breathless.\nopt.bargain.say.1 [e0ea71] = 你收下了它给的东西，那感觉像是回到了家。门自己关上了——暂时。星在你脑海深处安顿下来，像一只猫找到了温暖的膝头。代价从你的身体里支付：你嘴唇发灰地爬出隧道，接连好几天，每上一段楼梯都喘不过气。\n# en: You take what it offers. The door closes on its own, for now. The Star settles into the back of your mind like a cat finding a warm lap. You climb the tunnel grey-lipped, and for a week you lie awake, too weak for stairs.\nopt.bargain.say.2 [fd18f9] = 你收下了它给的东西。门自己关上了——暂时。星在你脑海深处安顿下来，像一只猫找到了温暖的膝头。你嘴唇发灰地爬出隧道，一连一周都睡不着，虚弱得爬不动楼梯。\n# en: Open the door\nopt.open.label [6f1706] = 打开这扇门\n# en: It wants out. Only one already deep in the Hollow could walk through beside it.\nopt.open.hint [55783a] = 它想出来。只有早已深陷空洞的人，才能与它并肩走过这扇门。\n# en: You set your palm on the black stone and push. The door swings wide. The Star rises past you like a breath held for three hundred years, and on its way out it touches your shoulder, the way a lord touches a sword. Above, every candle in Lumenhold goes out, and does not come back on until dawn.\nopt.open.say.1 [35b189] = 你把手掌按在黑石上，用力一推。门豁然洞开。星从你身边升起，像一口憋了三百年的气；它离开时碰了碰你的肩，如同领主以剑轻触骑士。在上方，流明堡的每一支蜡烛都熄灭了，直到天亮才重新亮起。\n# en: Run\nopt.flee.label [57e66a] = 逃跑\n# en: You run until the tunnel spits you out onto the shore. Behind you, the door is still ajar. In the morning, the headmistress's hair has gone white.\nopt.flee.say.1 [3778d7] = 你一路狂奔，直到隧道把你吐在岸边。身后，那扇门仍半开着。第二天早上，院长的头发全白了。\n\n@event hs_aftermath\n# en: After the Thaw\ntitle [a5fec6] = 解冻之后\n# en: The lake glitters as if nothing happened. Most of the academy believes there was a strange spring frost. The headmistress knows better. So does Kael, who finds you on the jetty with two cups of tea.\ntext [983d15] = 湖面波光粼粼，仿佛什么都没发生过。学院里大多数人以为那只是一场奇怪的春霜。院长知道真相。凯尔也知道——他端着两杯茶，在栈桥上找到了你。\n# en: The lake glittering in spring sunshine, students skimming stones, a bandaged figure sitting on the jetty.\nscene [c9174b] = 春日阳光下波光粼粼的湖面，学生们在打水漂，一个缠着绷带的身影坐在栈桥上。\n# en: Sit with him in silence\nopt.left.label [ed0b65] = 和他静静坐着\n# en: You watch the water. It's just water, now. Kael's tea, for once, doesn't freeze.\nopt.left.say.1 [c6b7cf] = 你们看着湖水。现在，它只是水而已。凯尔的茶，难得地没有结冰。\n# en: Tell him how you feel\nopt.right.label [cb477f] = 告诉他你的心意\n# en: He kisses you, and for the first time since you've known him, his hands are warm.\nopt.right.say.1 [b9bd38] = 他吻了你。认识他以来第一次，他的手是温暖的。\n# en: \"I owe you everything,\" he says. \"I'm not good at owing people. I'll learn.\"\nopt.right.say.2 [f06ead] = “我欠你一切，”他说，“我不擅长欠人情。我会学的。”\n\n@event hs_aftermath_lost\n# en: The Headmistress Closed It\ntitle [1bb5f9] = 校长关上了它\n# en: You wake in the infirmary. The lake has thawed, and the door is shut: the headmistress came down after you and closed it with her own hands. She is sitting by your bed. Her hair has gone white overnight, and her silver ring is gone.\ntext [c6d32b] = 你在医务室里醒来。湖水已经解冻，门也关上了：校长跟在你后面下去，亲手把它关上了。她就坐在你床边。她的头发一夜之间全白了，那枚银戒指也不见了。\n# en: An infirmary bed by a window, the lake thawed outside, a tall woman with newly white hair sitting beside it without her silver ring.\nscene [e20e7d] = 窗边的一张医务室病床，窗外湖水已解冻，一个刚刚白了头的高个女人坐在床边，手上没有了银戒指。\n# en: \"I'm sorry.\"\nopt.left.label [c7fcad] = “对不起。”\n# en: \"You went down,\" she says. \"Most don't. The door is shut, and I am old, and that was always the price for someone.\" She pats your hand once, like a chess move. You can't stop thinking about it for weeks.\nopt.left.say.1 [a621a0] = “你下去了，”她说，“大多数人不会。门关上了，我老了，这本来就总得有人付出代价。”她拍了一下你的手，像下了一步棋。好几个星期，你都没法不去想这件事。\n# en: \"What did it cost you?\"\nopt.right.label [05910a] = “这让你付出了什么？”\n# en: She tells you, plainly, and you wish she hadn't. The seal will hold for another lifetime. She won't. She asks you not to tell the others, and you don't, and it weighs on you.\nopt.right.say.1 [edb9ba] = 她平静地告诉了你，你却希望她没说。封印能再撑一辈子。她撑不了。她请你别告诉别人，你没说，这件事一直压在你心上。\n\n@event hs_kael_sister\n# en: The Sister\ntitle [e9e230] = 妹妹\n# en: A girl in white furs is waiting at the gates in a private snowstorm. Kael's younger sister, Ilse. \"If my brother won't open it,\" she says, \"I will. Unless you can give me a reason not to.\"\ntext [f83d6e] = 一个身穿白色毛皮的女孩等在校门口，一场只属于她的暴风雪围着她下。凯尔的妹妹，伊尔泽。“如果我哥哥不肯打开它，”她说，“我来。除非你能给我一个不这么做的理由。”\n# en: A girl in white furs standing at the academy gates in a snowstorm that is falling only on her.\nscene [695730] = 一个身穿白色毛皮的女孩站在学院大门前，一场只落在她身上的暴风雪。\n# en: Convince her\nopt.left.label [089b27] = 说服她\n# en: You talk for an hour. Then she's crying, and Kael is holding her, and the snow stops. She stays in the village to be near him.\nopt.left.say.1 [43c937] = 你们谈了一个小时。然后她哭了，凯尔抱住了她，雪停了。她留在镇上，好离哥哥近一些。\n# en: She listens politely and leaves. \"We'll see each other again,\" she says. \"Beneath the water.\"\nopt.left.say.2 [905bac] = 她礼貌地听完，走了。“我们还会再见的，”她说，“在水下。”\n# en: Warn the headmistress\nopt.right.label [084aca] = 警告院长\n# en: Ilse is escorted from the grounds. Kael understands. He says he understands. He's quiet for a long time.\nopt.right.say.1 [1fbc19] = 伊尔泽被护送出了校园。凯尔理解。他说他理解。他沉默了很久。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/chain_ilse.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/chain_ilse.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event ilse_village\n# en: The Girl at the Bakery\ntitle [a7ff00] = 面包店里的女孩\n# en: In the village bakery a girl in white furs is trying to pay for a bun with a coin so cold it has frozen to the counter. The baker is losing patience. You recognise those eyes: they're Kael's. \"Ilse,\" she says, before you ask. \"Don't.\"\ntext [7c5df9] = 村里的面包店里，一个穿白色毛皮的女孩正想用一枚硬币买个小面包——那硬币冷得已经冻在了柜台上。面包师快没耐心了。你认得那双眼睛：是凯尔的。“伊尔泽，”不等你开口她就说，“别问。”\n# en: A village bakery counter, a pale girl in white furs pushing a coin that is visibly frosted across the wood, a baffled baker.\nscene [1cd5c0] = 村里面包店的柜台前，一个穿白色毛皮的苍白女孩把一枚明显结了霜的硬币推过木台，面包师一脸困惑。\n# en: Pay for her bun\nopt.left.label [31d334] = 替她付面包钱\n# en: She stares at the bun, then at you, as if kindness is a trick she hasn't learned the counter to yet. \"Fine,\" she says. \"Thank you. Fine.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [7b1308] = 她盯着面包，又盯着你，好像善意是一种她还没学会破解的把戏。“好吧，”她说，“谢谢。好吧。”\n# en: Leave her to it\nopt.right.label [d94459] = 随她去\n# en: You leave. Through the window you see the coin crack, and the baker's breath freeze in the air. Ilse walks out with the bun. Nobody stops her.\nopt.right.say.1 [1e970f] = 你走了。透过窗户，你看见那枚硬币裂开，面包师呼出的气在空中结成了冰。伊尔泽拿着面包走了出来。没人拦她。\n\n@event ilse_frost_lessons\n# en: A Trade in Winter\ntitle [9ac35e] = 冬日的交易\n# en: Ilse finds you by the frozen pond. \"My brother won't talk to me. You will. Tell me how he really is, and I'll teach you Northern frost-craft. It's better than anything your professors know.\"\ntext [5847bc] = 伊尔泽在结冰的池塘边找到你。“我哥哥不跟我说话。你会。告诉我他现在到底过得怎么样，我就教你北境霜术。比你那些教授懂的都强。”\n# en: A frozen pond at the edge of the woods, a girl in white furs drawing a perfect hexagon of frost in the air, a student copying her clumsily.\nscene [4361bc] = 树林边结冰的池塘，一个穿白色毛皮的女孩在空中画出一个完美的六角霜花，一名学生笨拙地跟着模仿。\n# en: Tell her the truth about Kael\nopt.left.label [63ce17] = 把凯尔的实情告诉她\n# en: You tell her about the cold hands, {seen(astro_kael_roof): the roof at three in the morning|the nights he doesn't sleep}, the bread. She listens without moving. Then she teaches you to freeze a falling leaf mid-air.\nopt.left.say.1 [9069e5] = 你告诉她那双冰冷的手、{seen(astro_kael_roof): 凌晨三点的屋顶|他睡不着的那些夜晚}、那些面包。她一动不动地听着。然后她教你让一片飘落的叶子在半空中冻住。\n# en: Protect Kael's privacy\nopt.right.label [4b5f67] = 守住凯尔的隐私\n# en: \"He can tell you himself,\" you say. Ilse's eyes go flat. But the next day, she's at the pond again. \"Fine. I'll teach you anyway. You'll need it.\" She teaches you badly on purpose for an hour, and then, despite herself, well.\nopt.right.say.1 [976a4c] = “他可以自己告诉你，”你说。伊尔泽的眼神冷了下来。但第二天，她又出现在池塘边。“行吧。我还是教你。你会用得上的。”她故意教得很糟，教了一个钟头，然后不由自主地教得很好。\n\n@event ilse_grey_returns\n# en: The Man in Grey Comes for Ilse\ntitle [4eacc9] = 灰衣人来带走伊尔泽\n# en: Late at night, a lane behind the bakery. The man in grey has one hand on Ilse's shoulder. \"Home,\" he is saying softly. \"Your family is waiting. The door is waiting.\" Ilse sees you. She doesn't call out. She just looks.\ntext [4c620a] = 深夜，面包店后面的小巷。灰衣人一只手搭在伊尔泽肩上。“回家吧，”他轻声说，“你的家人在等你。那扇门在等你。”伊尔泽看见了你。她没有呼救，只是看着你。\n# en: A moonlit village lane, a man in a grey coat with one hand on a girl's shoulder, frost and shadow coiling around them both.\nscene [fb6082] = 月光下的村中小巷，一个穿灰色外套的男人一只手搭在女孩肩上，霜与阴影缠绕在两人周围。\n# en: Step between them\nopt.left.label [f242e3] = 挡在他们之间\n# en: Your spell breaks his grip. He looks at his frosted hand with mild surprise, bows, and walks into the dark. Ilse is shaking. \"Nobody's ever — \" she says, and stops.\nopt.left.say.1 [f2b4a9] = 你的咒语打断了他的手。他略带惊讶地看着自己结霜的手，鞠了一躬，走进了黑暗。伊尔泽在发抖。“从来没有人——”她说，然后停住了。\n# en: He swats your spell aside like a moth. When you get up, the lane is empty except for a single white glove in the snow.\nopt.left.say.2 [eaa8cd] = 他像拍飞蛾一样拍开了你的咒语。等你爬起来，巷子里已空无一人，只有雪地上的一只白手套。\n# en: Run for Kael\nopt.right.label [839eee] = 跑去找凯尔\n# en: Kael arrives like a blizzard. The man in grey takes one look at him and leaves without a word. Brother and sister stand in the snow, not touching, not speaking.\nopt.right.say.1 [69e63e] = 凯尔像一场暴风雪般赶到。灰衣人只看了他一眼，便一言不发地离开了。兄妹俩站在雪里，没有碰触，没有说话。\n# en: By the time you're back with help, the lane is empty. There's frost on the cobbles in the shape of a closed door.\nopt.right.say.2 [8c5d56] = 等你带着帮手回来，巷子已经空了。鹅卵石上的霜结成了一扇紧闭的门的形状。\n\n@event ilse_kael_reunion\n# en: Brother and Sister\ntitle [2c92a4] = 兄与妹\n# en: Kael and Ilse have been in the same village for weeks and have spoken eleven words to each other. You have three cups of tea, a frozen jetty and a very bad idea.\ntext [210c93] = 凯尔和伊尔泽在同一个村子里待了好几个星期，一共只对彼此说了十一个字。你有三杯茶、一座结冰的栈桥，和一个非常糟糕的主意。\n# en: Two pale siblings on opposite ends of a frozen jetty, a third person between them holding three cups of tea.\nscene [d059c4] = 结冰栈桥的两端各坐着一个苍白的兄妹，中间一个人端着三杯茶。\n# en: Sit them both down\nopt.left.label [05e582] = 让他俩坐下来谈\n# en: It takes an hour and two cups of tea are frozen solid. Then Ilse says \"I was scared,\" and Kael says \"So was I,\" and you quietly leave them to it.\nopt.left.say.1 [75d491] = 花了一个钟头，有两杯茶冻成了冰坨。然后伊尔泽说：“我很害怕。”凯尔说：“我也是。”你悄悄离开，把时间留给他们。\n# en: They argue in a language you don't speak, at a temperature you can't endure. But they argue — which, you suspect, is a start.\nopt.left.say.2 [299f28] = 他们用一种你听不懂的语言、在一种你受不了的温度里吵架。但他们在吵——你猜，这算是个开始。\n# en: Let them find their own way\nopt.right.label [a54eb8] = 让他们自己找到路\n# en: You leave the tea on the jetty. A week later you see them walking by the lake together, a careful arm's length apart. Then less.\nopt.right.say.1 [79a710] = 你把茶留在栈桥上。一星期后，你看见他们一起沿着湖边散步，小心地隔着一臂远。后来就没那么远了。\n\n@event ilse_northern_letter\n# en: A Summons From the North\ntitle [2572c4] = 来自北方的召唤\n# en: A letter with a black-star seal. The Dumont family summons Ilse home. \"If I don't go, they'll send someone worse than the man in grey,\" she says. \"If I do go, I could tell them to their faces that it's over. Or they could keep me.\"\ntext [97b031] = 一封盖着黑星封印的信。杜蒙家族召伊尔泽回家。“如果我不去，他们会派一个比灰衣人更糟的人来，”她说，“如果我去了，我可以当面告诉他们一切都结束了。或者，他们会把我留下。”\n# en: A girl holding a letter sealed with a black star, standing at a window that looks north toward far white mountains.\nscene [4e2efd] = 一个女孩手握盖着黑星封印的信，站在一扇朝北的窗前，远处是白茫茫的群山。\n# en: \"Go. Tell them it's over.\"\nopt.left.label [6759bb] = “去吧，告诉他们结束了。”\n# en: She leaves on the dawn barge, chin up. Before she goes, she presses her forehead to yours, a Northern farewell. \"If I'm not back by graduation,\" she says, \"come and get me.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [fae690] = 她坐黎明的驳船走了，下巴抬得高高的。走之前，她把额头贴在你额头上——北境的告别礼。“如果毕业前我没回来，”她说，“就来接我。”\n# en: \"Stay. Let them come.\"\nopt.right.label [ef10a1] = “留下，让他们来。”\n# en: She stays. She sews a sliver of winter into your collar \"in case they do come\". Nobody does. Not yet.\nopt.right.say.1 [3114b6] = 她留了下来。她把一缕冬天缝进你的衣领，“万一他们真的来了”。没有人来。至少现在还没有。\n\n@event ilse_farewell\n# en: Snow in Blossomtide\ntitle [224b90] = 花潮月的雪\n# en: It's spring, the orchard is in full blossom, and it is snowing — gently, over one tree. Ilse is underneath it. \"Graduation's soon,\" she says. \"Everyone's leaving. I wanted to give you something before you forgot me.\"\ntext [ccd97c] = 春天，果园里繁花盛开，却下起了雪——轻轻地，只落在一棵树上。伊尔泽就站在树下。“快毕业了，”她说，“大家都要走了。我想在你忘记我之前送你点东西。”\n# en: A single snowflake falling in a spring orchard full of blossom, a girl in white furs catching it on her glove and holding it out.\nscene [63dad1] = 繁花盛开的春日果园里飘落一片雪花，一个穿白色毛皮的女孩用手套接住它，递了过来。\n# en: \"I won't forget you.\"\nopt.left.label [6d1b23] = “我不会忘记你的。”\n# en: She doesn't believe you, but she smiles anyway — the first real smile you've seen on her. She gives you a snowflake that never melts.\nopt.left.say.1 [48bf7c] = 她不相信你，但还是笑了——你第一次在她脸上看到真正的笑容。她送给你一片永不融化的雪花。\n# en: Invite her to the graduation\nopt.right.label [d72a91] = 邀请她来参加毕业典礼\n# en: \"Me? At a graduation?\" She looks horrified, and then, slowly, delighted. On the day, she sits next to Kael in the second row and cries more than anyone.\nopt.right.say.1 [55ef3a] = “我？去毕业典礼？”她一脸惊恐，然后，慢慢地，变成了欣喜。那天，她坐在第二排凯尔身边，哭得比谁都厉害。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/chain_thornwick.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/chain_thornwick.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event tamsin_second_letter\n# en: Insufferable AND Rude\ntitle [1faa8e] = 让人受不了，而且没礼貌\n# en: A second letter from Thornwick, in the same quick hand. \"Insufferable AND rude. Noted. I'm giving you one more chance, because I'm a generous person and because my roommate bet me you'd never answer. Prove her wrong. T. Reed.\"\ntext [77fe54] = 荆木学院的第二封信，同样潦草的笔迹。“让人受不了，而且没礼貌。记下了。我再给你一次机会，因为我是个大方的人，也因为我室友跟我打赌说你永远不会回信。证明她错了。T. 里德。”\n# en: A second letter with a green Thornwick seal on a dormitory pillow, the word \"NOTED\" underlined three times.\nscene [85442f] = 宿舍枕头上放着第二封信，盖着荆木学院的绿色封蜡，“记下了”三个字下面划了三道线。\n# en: Write back, finally\nopt.left.label [ebbeda] = 终于回信\n# en: You write back, grudgingly at first and then not at all grudgingly. Four pages. The answer comes within a week, with a postscript: \"My roommate owes me a pie. Thank you.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [b76498] = 你回了信，一开始很不情愿，后来一点也不。四页。不到一星期回信就到了，附言写着：“我室友欠我一个派。谢谢你。”\n# en: Let it lie\nopt.right.label [9e1bb5] = 随它去\n# en: You leave it on the pillow. Somewhere across the hills, a roommate wins a pie. You'll never know what you missed, which is how it should be, probably.\nopt.right.say.1 [2d2a7b] = 你把信留在枕头上。山那边的某处，一个室友赢了一个派。你永远不会知道自己错过了什么，大概本该如此。\n\n@event tamsin_letter_gossip\n# en: News From Thornwick\ntitle [ab4189] = 荆木来信\n# en: {Tamsin has written again, six pages of Thornwick gossip with sketches of the professors in the margins.|Another letter from Tamsin, signed \"your rival, nemesis and only friend west of the hills\".|Tamsin's letter is mostly a drawing of their illusion professor as a goat.} {Their head of house has banned whistling.|Someone at Thornwick set the library on fire, a little.|They want to know if Lumenhold's food is really as bad as everyone says.} Answering properly would take an evening.\ntext [b4f018] = {塔姆辛又来信了，六页荆木学院的八卦，页边画满了教授们的速写。|塔姆辛又来信了，署名“你的对手、宿敌，以及山这边唯一的朋友”。|塔姆辛的信大半是一幅画，画的是 TA 的幻术教授变成了一只山羊。} {TA 的学院院长禁止了吹口哨。|荆木学院有人把图书馆点着了，一点点。|TA 想知道流明堡的伙食是不是真像大家说的那么糟。} 认真回信得花一个晚上。\n# en: A letter with a green seal open on a desk, its margins full of tiny unflattering sketches of Thornwick professors.\nscene [23ad0a] = 桌上摊开一封盖着绿色封蜡的信，页边画满了荆木学院教授们小小的、毫不留情的速写。\n# en: Write back at length\nopt.left.label [7bb28c] = 写一封长信回去\n# en: {You write till the candle gutters, with sketches of your own professors. Tamsin's reply calls your drawing of Thorne \"libellous and correct\".|Five pages back, with the castle's gossip and your worst week. They answer the worst week first.|You answer the goat with your own worst professor as a hen, and four pages on the refectory food. Tamsin replies that you've undersold the food.}\nopt.left.say.1 [1db4f8] = {你写到蜡烛燃尽，附上你自己画的教授速写。塔姆辛的回信说你画的索恩教授“诽谤，但准确”。|回了五页，有城堡的八卦，也有你最糟的一周。TA 先回了那最糟的一周。|你回了一幅把你最头疼的教授画成母鸡的画，外加四页膳堂伙食。塔姆辛回信说你把伙食说得太好了。}\n# en: A few lines and a sketch\nopt.right.label [79d945] = 几行字和一幅速写\n# en: {A short note and a drawing of a gargoyle. Tamsin writes back that it's \"the most Lumenhold thing ever sent through the post\".|Three lines. Tamsin replies with three pages, offended and delighted.|A few lines about the food and a goat of your own in the margin. Tamsin replies with a whole menagerie.}\nopt.right.say.1 [7e1d88] = {一张短笺和一幅石像鬼的画。塔姆辛回信说，这是“邮路上寄过的最流明堡的东西”。|三行字。塔姆辛回了三页，又气又乐。|几行关于伙食的话，页边再画一只你自己的山羊。塔姆辛回了一整个动物园。}\n\n@event tamsin_letter_questions\n# en: Twenty Questions\ntitle [87b695] = 二十个问题\n# en: {Tamsin's letter is a numbered list of twenty questions about life at Lumenhold, from \"Is the lake haunted?\" to \"What do you eat for breakfast?\"|Another list of questions. Number seven is \"Who is the most annoying person at your school?\"|Questions again. \"Be honest,\" says number one. \"Number twenty can be a lie.\"} {Number twelve asks about your exams.|Number fifteen asks about the lake.|Number three asks about the food.} Number nineteen just says \"Are you happy?\"\ntext [e5eb74] = {塔姆辛的信是一份编了号的清单，二十个关于流明堡生活的问题，从“湖里闹鬼吗？”到“你早餐吃什么？”。|又是一张问题清单。第七个是“你们学校最烦人的是谁？”|又是问题。“说实话，”第一个写道。“第二十个可以撒谎。”} {第十二个问你的考试。|第十五个问那座湖。|第三个问伙食。}第十九个只写着“你快乐吗？”\n# en: A letter numbered down the margin from one to twenty, each line a question, a student chewing a quill over the answers.\nscene [06c1b8] = 一封信的页边从一编号到二十，每一行都是一个问题，一个学生咬着羽毛笔思考答案。\n# en: Answer every one honestly\nopt.left.label [68babc] = 每一个都老实回答\n# en: {Twenty honest answers, even number nineteen. It's harder than it looks. Their reply begins, \"Thank you for nineteen.\"|You answer the lot. Writing it down, you learn a few things about yourself.|You answer all twenty honestly, even number three, which is about the food and unkind. Number twenty you leave as a lie, as permitted.}\nopt.left.say.1 [5040a5] = {二十个老实的回答，连第十九个也是。这比看起来难。TA 的回信开头是：“谢谢你回答了第十九个。”|你全答了。写下来的时候，你对自己多了几分了解。|二十个你都老实回答了，连第三个也是，那个关于伙食，答案不太客气。第二十个你按规定撒了个谎。}\n# en: Make the answers outrageous\nopt.right.label [2a7503] = 把答案编得离谱些\n# en: {The lake is haunted by a choir of geese. Breakfast is boiled bricks. Tamsin's reply is two pages of laughter and a request for \"the true answers, eventually\".|You invent a Lumenhold of dragons and duels before dawn. Tamsin believes none of it and asks for more.|Every answer is outrageous except number twenty, which is the true one. Tamsin works out which, and writes back only \"I knew it.\"}\nopt.right.say.1 [ef7243] = {湖里闹的是一支鹅组成的合唱团。早餐是水煮砖头。塔姆辛回了两页笑声，并要求“以后告诉我真正的答案”。|你编出了一个有龙、黎明前决斗的流明堡。塔姆辛一个字也不信，还要你再多写点。|每个回答都荒唐透顶，只有第二十个是真的。塔姆辛看出来了，回信只写了一句：“我就知道。”}\n\n@event tamsin_letter_trouble\n# en: A Short Letter\ntitle [fc5c66] = 一封短信\n# en: Tamsin's letter is one page, which has never happened. They failed their illusion examination, the one thing they were good at, and their grandmother's theatre has been sold. There's a sentence crossed out so hard the nib went through the paper.\ntext [b7b79a] = 塔姆辛的信只有一页，这从来没有过。TA 的幻术考试挂了，那是 TA 唯一擅长的东西，祖母的剧团也卖掉了。有一句话被划得太狠，笔尖戳穿了纸。\n# en: A single short page with a green seal, the handwriting cramped and unsteady, a blot where a sentence was crossed out hard.\nscene [c5d9d6] = 一页短短的信盖着绿色封蜡，字迹局促而颤抖，一处墨渍是被狠狠划掉的一句话。\n# en: Write back that night\nopt.left.label [bb6a52] = 当晚就回信\n# en: You write until two in the morning: the time you failed, what helped, a list of everything they're good at that isn't illusion. Their next letter is six pages again, and the first line is \"You idiot, you made me cry in the refectory.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [8541b5] = 你一直写到凌晨两点：你挂科的那次，什么帮了你，还有一张清单，列出 TA 除了幻术之外擅长的所有事。TA 的下一封信又是六页，第一行是：“你这个笨蛋，害我在膳堂里哭了。”\n# en: Send them something instead\nopt.right.label [75de79] = 改寄点东西过去\n# en: You send a parcel: Mabel's best biscuits, a book of plays, a drawing of a gargoyle holding a sign that says \"RETAKE\". Tamsin writes back that the gargoyle is on their wall now, and that they passed.\nopt.right.say.1 [866768] = 你寄了个包裹：梅布尔茶馆最好的饼干、一本剧本集、一幅石像鬼举着“补考”牌子的画。塔姆辛回信说那只石像鬼现在挂在 TA 墙上，而且 TA 补考过了。\n\n@event tamsin_letter_asks_secret\n# en: A Small Favour\ntitle [24d010] = 一个小忙\n# en: Tamsin's letter is cheerful until the last paragraph. \"Small favour. Thornwick's duelling captain wants to know Lumenhold's tournament order. Just the order. Everyone does it, honestly. I'd owe you.\" The underline is very neat, as if they did it twice.\ntext [fdb7c9] = 塔姆辛的信一直很开心，直到最后一段。“帮个小忙。荆木的决斗队长想知道流明堡的比赛出场顺序。只要顺序。大家都这么干，真的。我会欠你一个人情。”那道下划线非常整齐，像是划了两遍。\n# en: A letter half-read on a desk, a single paragraph underlined twice, a student staring out of a window at the hills.\nscene [114522] = 一封读了一半的信放在桌上，有一段话下面划了两道线，一个学生望着窗外的群山发呆。\n# en: Send the real order\nopt.left.label [21dd30] = 寄出真实的顺序\n# en: You write it out: who fights first, who last. It feels like nothing at all until you post it, and then it feels like a stone. Tamsin's reply thanks you, too warmly.\nopt.left.say.1 [817ae0] = 你写了出来：谁先上，谁最后。在寄出之前，这感觉什么也不是；寄出之后，它像一块石头。塔姆辛回信道谢，热情得过了头。\n# en: Send a false one\nopt.right.label [bdb397] = 寄一份假的\n# en: You invent an order, plausible and wrong. At the tournament, Thornwick's captain looks baffled for the whole first round. Tamsin's next letter is short and doesn't mention it, which is how you know they know.\nopt.right.say.1 [9998b8] = 你编了一个顺序，合理，但全错。比赛上，荆木的队长整个第一轮都一脸茫然。塔姆辛的下一封信很短，只字未提，你就是这样知道 TA 知道了。\n# en: Say no, and say why\nopt.refuse.label [f281a1] = 拒绝，并说明理由\n# en: \"Ask me anything else,\" you write. Their reply is a week late and very polite. Then, a fortnight after: \"You were right. Sorry. Still friends?\" You write back yes before you've finished reading.\nopt.refuse.say.1 [15d176] = “问我别的什么都行，”你写道。TA 的回信晚了一个星期，非常客气。又过了两个星期：“你是对的。对不起。还是朋友吗？”你还没读完就回了“是”。\n\n@event tamsin_letter_offers_secret\n# en: Their Turn\ntitle [9873fa] = 轮到 TA 了\n# en: Folded inside Tamsin's letter is a second paper: Thornwick's whole plan for this year's contest, every pairing, every trick. \"Fair's fair,\" they've written. \"Burn it or use it. I won't ask which.\"\ntext [570c1c] = 塔姆辛的信里夹着另一张纸：荆木学院今年比赛的全盘计划，每一组对阵，每一个花招。“公平起见，”TA 写道，“烧了它，或者用它。我不会问你选了哪个。”\n# en: A folded paper inside a letter, covered in diagrams of duelling circles and arrows, a student holding it at arm's length as if it were hot.\nscene [cbfb50] = 一封信里夹着一张折起的纸，画满了决斗圈和箭头的图示，一个学生伸直胳膊拿着它，仿佛它烫手。\n# en: Use it\nopt.left.label [77da81] = 用它\n# en: You keep the plan and study it until you know it better than Thornwick does. It will help. You can't quite look at your own reflection while you read it.\nopt.left.say.1 [300161] = 你留下那份计划，研究到比荆木的人还熟。它会有用的。读它的时候，你有点不敢看镜子里的自己。\n# en: Burn it unread\nopt.right.label [f39f04] = 看都不看就烧掉\n# en: You burn it in the candle without unfolding it, and write back that you did. Their reply has one line: \"I knew you would. That's why I sent it.\" You're still not sure if it was a test.\nopt.right.say.1 [672e02] = 你没展开就在烛火上烧了它，并回信告诉 TA。TA 的回信只有一行：“我就知道你会。所以我才寄给你。”你到现在也不确定那是不是一场考验。\n\n@event tamsin_confession\n# en: What I Was Sent to Do\ntitle [f7c453] = 我被派来做的事\n# en: Tamsin's letter is long and the hand is unsteady. \"I have to tell you. The first letter wasn't random. Thornwick set some of us to write to Lumenhold students and report back. I reported for two letters. Then I liked you, and I stopped, and I've been ashamed ever since. You can stop writing. I'd understand.\"\ntext [d82e6f] = 塔姆辛的信很长，字迹颤抖。“我必须告诉你。第一封信不是随机的。荆木让我们中的一些人写信给流明堡的学生，然后回去汇报。我汇报了两封信的内容。后来我喜欢上了你，就停了，从那以后一直觉得羞愧。你可以不再写信了。我能理解。”\n# en: A long letter in a shaky hand spread across a desk, a green seal broken beside it, a student reading with one hand over their mouth.\nscene [71b1c0] = 一封长信摊在桌上，字迹颤抖，旁边是一枚碎开的绿色封蜡，一个学生一手捂着嘴读着。\n# en: Forgive them\nopt.left.label [6c4629] = 原谅 TA\n# en: You write back one line: \"Third letter onwards, then. That's when I started liking you too.\" Their reply is the longest yet, and the first not signed with an insult.\nopt.left.say.1 [b80866] = 你只回了一行：“那就从第三封信算起吧。我也是从那时开始喜欢你的。”TA 的回信是有史以来最长的一封，也是第一封署名里没有一句损人话的。\n# en: Ask what they reported\nopt.right.label [0e7b6d] = 问 TA 汇报了什么\n# en: They tell you, exactly: the food, the weather, your opinion of Professor Thorne. Nothing that mattered. \"I made it boring on purpose,\" they write. You laugh, and then you forgive them anyway.\nopt.right.say.1 [232dfa] = TA 一五一十地告诉了你：伙食、天气、你对索恩教授的看法。没有一件要紧的事。“我故意写得很无聊，”TA 写道。你笑了，然后还是原谅了 TA。\n\n@event tamsin_letter_after\n# en: After Graduation\ntitle [f78127] = 毕业之后\n# en: Tamsin's letter has a map on the back with two towns circled: Lumenhold and Thornwick, and a line between them. \"After graduation, there'll be no school to be rivals for. What are we, then?\" Under it, smaller: \"Don't answer that. Answer it.\"\ntext [275f1a] = 塔姆辛的信背面画着一张地图，圈出了两座城：流明堡和荆木，中间连着一条线。“毕业以后，就没有学校让我们较劲了。那我们算什么？”下面一行小字：“别回答。回答我。”\n# en: A letter with a green seal and a pressed flower, a map of the realm sketched on the back with two towns circled.\nscene [4c98cd] = 一封盖着绿色封蜡、夹着压花的信，背面草草画着王国的地图，圈出了两座城。\n# en: \"Friends. Obviously.\"\nopt.left.label [28c81e] = “朋友。显然。”\n# en: You write it in letters an inch high. Their reply has the map again, with a third town circled halfway between: \"Meeting point. Every spring. Bring biscuits.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [2b1665] = 你用一寸高的字写了下来。TA 的回信又附上了那张地图，在两城正中间圈了第三座小镇：“碰头地点。每年春天。带饼干。”\n# en: \"Still rivals. Obviously.\"\nopt.right.label [292e36] = “还是对手。显然。”\n# en: \"Excellent,\" they write back. \"I was worried you'd go soft.\" Under it, very small: \"Friends too, though. Obviously.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [8691d3] = “太好了，”TA 回信说，“我还担心你会变软呢。”下面是很小的字：“不过也是朋友。显然。”\n\n@event rival_return_visit\n# en: To Thornwick\ntitle [7f07af] = 前往荆木\n# en: This year it's Lumenhold's turn to visit. A barge leaves for Thornwick at dawn with a delegation of students who have been told to behave. {met(tamsin): Tamsin has written three letters about it. The last one just says \"COME\".|Thornwick has, by all accounts, better plumbing.}\ntext [88f77d] = 今年轮到流明堡回访了。一艘驳船将在黎明时分载着一支被叮嘱要守规矩的学生代表团驶向荆木学院。{met(tamsin): 塔姆辛为这事写了三封信。最后一封只写着“来”。|据大家说，荆木学院的水管比这儿好。}\n# en: A barge full of Lumenhold students arriving at a rival academy of green-roofed towers, a crowd of green-robed students waiting on the jetty.\nscene [88b117] = 一艘满载流明堡学生的驳船抵达一座绿顶塔楼林立的对手学院，码头上等着一群穿绿袍的学生。\n# en: Join the delegation\nopt.go.label [81e864] = 加入代表团\n# en: Two days across the hills. The barge fare is yours.\nopt.go.hint [6f7ab8] = 翻山越岭两天。船费你自己出。\n# en: Tamsin is on the jetty, smaller than their letters and louder, holding a sign with your name spelled wrong on purpose. They show you every corner of Thornwick and argue that each one beats Lumenhold. They're mostly wrong.\nopt.go.say.1 [e034ec] = 塔姆辛就在码头上，比信里矮，也比信里吵，举着一块故意把你名字拼错的牌子。TA 带你看遍了荆木的每个角落，坚称每一处都比流明堡强。大多数时候 TA 是错的。\n# en: Thornwick's towers are green-roofed and smug, and their plumbing really is better. A student in green follows you around the library, asking about the lake.\nopt.go.say.2 [d86a94] = 荆木学院的塔楼顶着绿瓦，一副得意洋洋的样子，他们的水管确实更好。一个穿绿袍的学生在图书馆里一直跟着你，打听那座湖的事。\n# en: Stay at the castle\nopt.stay.label [04365a] = 留在城堡\n# en: A quiet weekend with half the school gone.\nopt.stay.hint [2234e2] = 半个学校都走了，一个安静的周末。\n# en: You stay. The castle is wonderfully quiet. Tamsin's next letter is very short, and sulks, and then forgives you on the last line.\nopt.stay.say.1 [4ce199] = 你留下了。城堡安静得美妙。塔姆辛的下一封信非常短，在赌气，然后在最后一行原谅了你。\n# en: The castle is half empty and wholly peaceful. You read in the window seat all weekend and don't miss Thornwick at all.\nopt.stay.say.2 [638ff5] = 城堡空了一半，也彻底安宁了。你整个周末都窝在窗边座位上看书，一点也不想念荆木。\n\n@event rival_contest\n# en: The Inter-School Contest\ntitle [451f5d] = 校际比赛\n# en: The inter-school contest, at Lumenhold this year, before both schools and half the village. You've made the team. {met(tamsin): In the final, the name opposite yours is Tamsin Reed. They wave from across the ring, apologetically, and then stop smiling.|In the final, a Thornwick illusionist faces you, smiling far too much.}{flag(thornwick_secrets): You know every trick they've planned.|}\ntext [01c439] = 校际比赛今年在流明堡举行，两校师生和半个村子都来了。你入选了队伍。{met(tamsin): 决赛里，你对面的名字是塔姆辛·里德。TA 隔着赛场冲你挥手，带着歉意，然后收起了笑容。|决赛里，一个荆木的幻术师站在你对面，笑得过分灿烂。}{flag(thornwick_secrets): 他们计划的每一个花招你都知道。|}\n# en: A packed duelling arena under banners of blue and green, two duellists facing each other in the chalk ring, a crowd on its feet.\nscene [1dd393] = 座无虚席的决斗场，蓝绿两色旗帜高悬，两名决斗者在粉笔圈里对峙，观众全都站了起来。\n# en: Fight to win\nopt.left.label [0f68e4] = 全力求胜\n# en: You counter every planned trick before it's cast. Lumenhold wins the contest, and the castle roars. {met(tamsin): Tamsin shakes your hand afterwards and holds on too long. \"You read the plan,\" they say quietly. \"Good.\" You can't tell if they mean it.|}\nopt.left.say.1 [053f35] = 每一个预先计划的花招，你都在对方出手前化解了。流明堡赢下了比赛，整座城堡都在欢呼。{met(tamsin): 赛后塔姆辛和你握手，握得太久了。“你看了那份计划，”TA 轻声说。“很好。”你分辨不出 TA 是不是真心的。|}\n# en: Knowing the plan isn't enough: they change it in the ring. Thornwick takes the cup. The plan, in your pocket, feels heavier than ever.\nopt.left.say.2 [7a8a18] = 光知道计划是不够的：他们在场上改了。荆木捧走了奖杯。口袋里的那份计划，从没这么沉过。\n# en: An honest win, hard and close. Lumenhold takes the cup and carries you round the arena. {met(tamsin): Tamsin is on the ground laughing. \"Next year,\" they gasp. \"Next year I'll flatten you.\"|}\nopt.left.say.3 [d0c2e2] = 一场光明正大的胜利，艰难而胶着。流明堡捧起奖杯，抬着你绕场一周。{met(tamsin): 塔姆辛躺在地上笑。“明年，”TA 喘着气说，“明年我要把你打趴下。”|}\n# en: You lose by a breath. Thornwick sings all the way back to their barge. {met(tamsin): Tamsin helps you up and doesn't gloat, not once, which you'll never forget.|}\nopt.left.say.4 [668fb9] = 你以毫厘之差输了。荆木的人一路唱着歌回到驳船上。{met(tamsin): 塔姆辛把你拉起来，一次也没有炫耀，这一点你永远不会忘记。|}\n# en: Fight clean and generous\nopt.right.label [928af5] = 打得干净又大度\n# en: You fight well and fairly, and when you have them down you let them stand before the next exchange. You lose on points. The crowd stands for both of you anyway, and Lyra, in the stands, actually claps.\nopt.right.say.1 [fbea80] = 你打得漂亮而公平，把对手击倒后，还让 TA 站起来再进行下一回合。你按积分输了。观众依然为你们两个起立鼓掌，看台上的莱拉居然也拍了手。\n\n@event rival_last_match\n# en: The Last Match\ntitle [bf66e5] = 最后一场比赛\n# en: The last match of your year against Thornwick's: an exhibition on the lawn, lanterns in the trees, nothing at stake but four years of grudges. {met(tamsin): Tamsin has asked to be your opponent. \"For the record,\" they say. \"Ours.\"|Thornwick's final-years have brought cake, which feels like a trick.}\ntext [8a2019] = 你们这一届和荆木同届的最后一场比赛：草坪上的表演赛，树上挂着灯笼，赌注只有四年的积怨。{met(tamsin): 塔姆辛申请当你的对手。“为了记录，”TA 说。“我们俩的。”|荆木的毕业生带来了蛋糕，感觉像个圈套。}\n# en: An evening exhibition match on the lawn between two academies, lanterns strung between the trees, final-year students in blue and green laughing in the ring.\nscene [d7aa4a] = 两所学院在草坪上的傍晚表演赛，灯笼挂在树间，穿蓝袍和绿袍的毕业生在场中大笑。\n# en: Give it everything\nopt.left.label [607b0e] = 全力以赴\n# en: You win the last exchange of your schooling against Thornwick, and the lawn erupts. {met(tamsin): Tamsin, flat on the grass, laughs up at the lanterns. \"Fine,\" they say. \"Fine. Record's yours.\"|}\nopt.left.say.1 [163d65] = 你赢下了学生时代对荆木的最后一次交锋，草坪上一片沸腾。{met(tamsin): 塔姆辛平躺在草地上，冲着灯笼大笑。“好吧，”TA 说。“好吧。纪录归你了。”|}\n# en: You lose, gloriously, and lie on the grass looking at the lanterns. {met(tamsin): Tamsin lies down next to you. \"Record's mine,\" they say, and then, quieter, \"I'll miss this.\"|}\nopt.left.say.2 [9110fe] = 你输了，输得很漂亮，躺在草地上看着灯笼。{met(tamsin): 塔姆辛在你旁边躺下。“纪录归我了，”TA 说，然后更轻地说：“我会想念这个的。”|}\n# en: Turn it into a show\nopt.right.label [f31225] = 把它变成一场表演\n# en: You turn the match into a performance, illusions and flourishes and a duel that never lands a blow. {met(tamsin): Tamsin catches on at once and plays along. The two schools applaud the same thing for the first time in a century.|Both schools applaud the same thing for the first time in a century.}\nopt.right.say.1 [cf1fab] = 你把比赛变成了一场演出，幻术、花哨的动作，一场从没真正命中过的决斗。{met(tamsin): 塔姆辛立刻会意，配合着演了下去。一百年来，两所学校第一次为同一件事鼓掌。|一百年来，两所学校第一次为同一件事鼓掌。}\n\n@event tamsin_farewell\n# en: Across the Hills\ntitle [d009b3] = 山那边\n# en: At the barge landing, before Thornwick's delegation leaves for the last time, Tamsin gives you a small parcel. Inside: every letter you ever sent them, tied with string. \"So you can read how insufferable you were,\" they say. \"I kept copies. Obviously.\"\ntext [bedb5b] = 在驳船码头，荆木的代表团最后一次离开之前，塔姆辛递给你一个小包裹。里面是你寄给 TA 的每一封信，用绳子捆着。“好让你读读自己当年有多让人受不了，”TA 说。“我留了副本。显然。”\n# en: Two students at a barge landing at dusk, one in Lumenhold blue and one in Thornwick green, exchanging small wrapped parcels.\nscene [ab4aec] = 黄昏时的驳船码头，两个学生，一个穿流明堡蓝，一个穿荆木绿，互相交换着小小的包裹。\n# en: Give them yours\nopt.left.label [e830e2] = 把你的那份给 TA\n# en: You hand over the box you've kept under your bed for four years: every letter they sent, every insult, every postscript. They read the first one on the barge steps and laugh, and the barge leaves with them still laughing.\nopt.left.say.1 [9b6365] = 你递过去那个在床底下放了四年的盒子：TA 寄来的每一封信，每一句损人话，每一则附言。TA 在驳船台阶上读了第一封就笑了起来，驳船载着还在笑的 TA 离开了。\n# en: \"Write to me. Same as ever.\"\nopt.right.label [4bc311] = “给我写信。和以前一样。”\n# en: \"Every week,\" they promise. \"Insufferably.\" The barge pulls away. They're still waving when it rounds the bend, and so are you.\nopt.right.say.1 [056d39] = “每周一封，”TA 保证，“保证让人受不了。”驳船驶离。直到它转过河湾，TA 还在挥手，你也是。\n\n@event intern_thornwick_tamsin\n# en: A Familiar Hand\ntitle [3d7a98] = 熟悉的笔迹\n# en: At the Thornwick lab, the student at the next bench, back from a week away, looks up from a glowing illusion and grins. It's Tamsin. \"They said a Lumenhold intern had come,\" they say. \"I told them it had better be you, or I'd sabotage everything.\"\ntext [60fe67] = 在荆木实验室，隔壁工作台那个出门一周刚回来的学生从一个发光的幻象上抬起头，咧嘴一笑。是塔姆辛。“他们说来了个流明堡的实习生，”TA 说。“我告诉他们最好是你，不然我就把一切都搞砸。”\n# en: A laboratory at a rival academy, a student in Thornwick green looking up from a glowing illusion on a bench, mid-grin, at a visitor in the doorway.\nscene [1fa6b7] = 对手学院的一间实验室里，一个穿荆木绿的学生从工作台上发光的幻象抬起头，正咧着嘴笑向门口的访客。\n# en: Work beside them from now on\nopt.left.label [66b3d1] = 从此在 TA 旁边干活\n# en: The rest of the summer at the next bench. They show you every trick in Thornwick's book, and you show them three Lumenhold ones they swear aren't legal. Your supervisor calls you \"the inseparables\", not kindly.\nopt.left.say.1 [e74fe4] = 夏天剩下的日子都在隔壁工作台。TA 把荆木的每个诀窍都教给了你，你教了 TA 三个流明堡的，TA 发誓那些不合法。你的导师管你们叫“连体婴”，并不是夸奖。\n# en: Keep it professional\nopt.right.label [a540e8] = 保持公事公办\n# en: You work at your own bench and keep your mind on the work. The lab notices; Tamsin notices more. \"Fine,\" they say at the end. \"You were right. But I missed you, at the next bench.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [134fe0] = 你在自己的工作台干活，心思全放在工作上。实验室注意到了；塔姆辛注意到的更多。“好吧，”最后 TA 说。“你是对的。但我想念隔壁工作台上的你。”\n\n@event lyra_on_thornwick\n# en: Lyra on Thornwick\ntitle [06748b] = 莱拉谈荆木\n# en: Lyra has the Ledger folded to the contest results. \"Thornwick,\" she says, as if it were a mild disease. {met(tamsin): \"And you write letters to one of them. Every week. I've seen the green seals.\"|\"I hope you enjoyed humiliating them. Or being humiliated. I was too angry to watch closely.\"}\ntext [4b0bc5] = 莱拉把《流明纪事报》折到比赛结果那一页。“荆木，”她说，像在说一种轻微的疾病。{met(tamsin): “而你每周都给他们中的一个写信。我看见那些绿色封蜡了。”|“希望你羞辱他们羞辱得愉快。或者被羞辱得愉快。我气得没法仔细看。”}\n# en: A proud red-haired student sitting on a bench by the duelling hall, a newspaper folded to the contest results across her knee.\nscene [e50666] = 一个骄傲的红发学生坐在决斗厅旁的长椅上，膝上横着一份折到比赛结果的报纸。\n# en: Defend Thornwick\nopt.left.label [3f76fa] = 为荆木说话\n# en: You tell her Thornwick's people are people. She listens with enormous scepticism. A month later she asks, very casually, whether {met(tamsin): your Thornwick friend|any of Thornwick's duellists} would ever want a practice duel. \"For research,\" she says.\nopt.left.say.1 [c188c8] = 你告诉她，荆木的人也是人。她带着极大的怀疑听着。一个月后，她非常随意地问你，{met(tamsin): 你那位荆木的朋友|荆木的哪位决斗手}愿不愿意来一场练习决斗。“为了研究，”她说。\n# en: Agree they're insufferable\nopt.right.label [8f826f] = 同意他们让人受不了\n# en: \"Insufferable,\" you agree. She looks almost happy. \"Exactly,\" she says. \"Thank you.\" {met(tamsin): You don't tell Tamsin, who would agree with her.|You keep to yourself that the Thornwick illusionist was rather good.}\nopt.right.say.1 [b1271a] = “让人受不了，”你附和道。她看起来几乎是高兴的。“没错，”她说。“谢谢你。”{met(tamsin): 你没告诉塔姆辛，TA 会同意她的。|你没说出口的是，那位荆木幻术师其实相当厉害。}\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/chain_timeloop.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/chain_timeloop.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event timeloop_exploit\n# en: Every Bet, Every Answer\ntitle [082f70] = 每一场赌，每一道题\n# en: {loop_exploit == 0: You know which way every coin will fall, and what's on this afternoon's quiz.|{loop_exploit == 1: The bets again. Your classmates are starting to look at you oddly when you win.|The bets, the quiz, the same faces losing. Their groans have stopped being funny.}} {~The quiz is on the founders.|A fourth-year bets you can't name every tower.|Someone bets on which way the prefect will fall.}\ntext [0a1a07] = {loop_exploit == 0: 每一枚硬币会落向哪面，今天下午的小测考什么，你都知道。|{loop_exploit == 1: 又是那些赌局。你赢的时候，同学们开始用奇怪的眼神看你了。|赌局、小测、同样输掉的面孔。他们的哀叹已经不好笑了。}} {~小测考的是创始者。|一个四年级生跟你打赌，说你叫不全每一座塔的名字。|有人赌级长会往哪边摔。}\n# en: A student at the centre of a crowd in the courtyard collecting coins from groaning classmates, a quiz paper marked full marks in their other hand.\nscene [a57222] = 庭院里，一个学生被人群围在中间，从哀叹的同学手里收着硬币，另一只手拿着一张满分的小测卷。\n# en: Take everything you can\nopt.left.label [0c2b42] = 能拿的全拿\n# en: You win it all again, and the winning has a taste like tin. The loop tightens: the morning is shorter now, the edges closer. And someone is watching you across the courtyard as if they know.\nopt.left.say.1 [4bf76c] = 你又赢了个精光，胜利的滋味像锡。循环收紧了：早晨更短了，边缘更近了。而庭院对面有人正看着你，好像知道些什么。\n# en: {~The coins are real when you wake, and the marks stay in the ledger. So does the hollow feeling, a little deeper each time.|You win again. The crowns carry over into the next Tuesday. So does the weight of them.|You win the bet on the prefect, who falls exactly where you knew. The coins carry over; so does the look on the prefect's face.}\nopt.left.say.2 [7f5156] = {~醒来时硬币是真的，分数也留在成绩册上。那种空落落的感觉也留着，每次都更深一点。|你又赢了。金币带进了下一个星期二。它们的分量也是。|你赌赢了级长那一局，级长正好摔在你料到的地方。硬币带进了下一天；级长脸上的表情也是。}\n# en: Just the quiz, then stop\nopt.right.label [938eb6] = 只做小测，然后收手\n# en: {~Full marks, and nothing else. It's a small cheat, and it still sits badly at supper.|You ace the quiz and leave the bets alone. A small mercy, to yourself mostly.|You take the quiz and leave the bet on the prefect alone. You don't even watch the fall.}\nopt.right.say.1 [3f4c96] = {~满分，别的什么都不要。这只是个小小的作弊，晚饭时还是让你不舒服。|你考了满分，没碰那些赌局。一点小小的仁慈，主要是对你自己。|你参加了小测，没碰级长那一局。你连级长摔倒都没看。}\n\n@event timeloop_noticed\n# en: \"You've Done This Before\"\ntitle [54cfff] = “你以前做过这个”\n# en: Seraphine catches your sleeve in the corridor. Her eyes are very clear. \"You've done this before,\" she says. \"Today. Many times. I can see the creases in it.\" She doesn't let go. \"Whatever you're taking from it, it's taking from you too.\"\ntext [1b521a] = 瑟拉芬在走廊里拉住你的袖子。她的眼睛非常清澈。“你以前做过这个，”她说。“今天。很多次。我能看见它上面的折痕。”她没有松手。“不管你从里面拿走了什么，它也在从你身上拿走东西。”\n# en: A seer catching a student's sleeve in a crowded corridor, her eyes very clear, the other students blurred around them as if moving too fast.\nscene [9ea92b] = 一位预言者在拥挤的走廊里拉住一个学生的袖子，她的眼睛非常清澈，周围的学生模糊成一片，仿佛走得太快。\n# en: Tell her everything\nopt.left.label [92d56a] = 全都告诉她\n# en: You tell her, all of it, in a window seat. She listens and then says, \"Ask the clocks in the astronomy tower.\" Tomorrow she won't remember. Today, she holds your hand like she will.\nopt.left.say.1 [a61455] = 你在一个窗边座位上把一切都告诉了她。她听完说：“去问天文塔里的钟。”明天她就不会记得了。今天，她握着你的手，就像她会记得一样。\n# en: Pull away\nopt.right.label [1414c0] = 挣开\n# en: You pull free and walk fast. She doesn't follow. \"I'll forget this,\" she calls after you. \"You won't.\" She's right, and that's the worst of it.\nopt.right.say.1 [8fd623] = 你挣脱开，快步走开。她没有追上来。“我会忘了这件事，”她在你身后喊。“你不会。”她说得对，这才是最糟的。\n\n@event timeloop_dare_confess\n# en: Things You'd Never Say\ntitle [06d735] = 永远说不出口的话\n# en: {Nothing today will last. So you find the person you've never told, and you tell them.|Another Tuesday, another confession nobody will remember.|You've confessed to them three times now, three different ways.} {They look at you as if you've handed them something breakable.|They laugh, kindly, then don't.|They take a long time to answer.}\ntext [b12772] = {今天的一切都不会留下。于是你找到那个你从没告诉过的人，告诉了 TA。|又一个星期二，又一次没人会记得的告白。|你已经向 TA 告白了三次，三种不同的说法。} {TA 看着你，仿佛你递给了 TA 一件易碎的东西。|TA 笑了，很温柔，然后不笑了。|TA 过了很久才回答。}\n# en: Two students in an empty corridor at dusk, one mid-sentence with their heart on their sleeve, the other's face caught between surprise and joy.\nscene [76e15b] = 黄昏空荡的走廊里，两个学生，一个正把心里话说到一半，另一个的脸定格在惊讶与喜悦之间。\n# en: Say it all, properly\nopt.left.label [7d3c43] = 好好地，全说出来\n# en: {You say all of it. They say something back that you'll keep for the rest of your life, and they'll have forgotten by breakfast. It's the most bittersweet thing you've ever done.|They kiss you, or hug you, or cry; it's different each time. By morning it never happened. Only you know.|You say it a fourth way. They take a long time to answer, and when they do it's the best answer yet. Gone by morning.}\nopt.left.say.1 [e98d7f] = {你全说了。TA 回了你一句你会记一辈子的话，而到早餐时 TA 就会忘掉。这是你做过的最苦乐参半的事。|TA 吻了你，或者抱了你，或者哭了；每次都不一样。到了早上，什么都没发生过。只有你知道。|你换了第四种说法。TA 过了很久才回答，回答的是迄今为止最好的话。到早上就没了。}\n# en: Say it badly, on purpose\nopt.right.label [a92326] = 故意说得很糟\n# en: {You make a terrible speech, as a rehearsal. They're baffled. You learn exactly which words you'd use if this were real, and file them away.|You stumble through it, and practise, and stumble less. One Tuesday you'll say it on a Wednesday.|You say it badly on purpose, and they take a long time to answer anyway, as if they heard what you meant.}\nopt.right.say.1 [0d926e] = {你发表了一通糟糕透顶的演讲，当作彩排。TA 一头雾水。你弄清了如果这是真的，你会用哪些词，把它们收好。|你磕磕绊绊地说完，练习，再说得少磕绊一点。总有一个星期二，你会在星期三把它说出来。|你故意说得很糟，TA 还是过了很久才回答，好像听懂了你真正的意思。}\n\n@event timeloop_dare_fall\n# en: Off the Observatory Roof\ntitle [44cb45] = 从观星台屋顶跳下\n# en: {If nothing lasts, then nothing can hurt for long. You climb the observatory parapet with the whole lake below you.|The parapet again. You know exactly how the wind feels at the top.|You've done this before. You still can't quite believe you do it.} {The lake is very blue.|Gulls wheel below you.|The bell in the tower is about to ring.}\ntext [e67a8a] = {如果什么都留不下，那就没有什么能疼很久。你爬上观星台的护墙，整座湖就在你脚下。|又是护墙。你清楚地知道顶上的风是什么感觉。|你以前做过这个。你仍然不太相信自己会做。} {湖水很蓝。|海鸥在你脚下盘旋。|塔里的钟就要敲响了。}\n# en: A student standing on the observatory parapet with arms wide, the lake far below, the morning sky enormous.\nscene [6e1128] = 一个学生张开双臂站在观星台的护墙上，湖在很远的下方，清晨的天空无比辽阔。\n# en: Jump\nopt.left.label [1ef32d] = 跳\n# en: {You fall for a long, bright moment, and the lake rushes up, and you wake in your bed to the smell of eggs. Your heart is still pounding. It was glorious, and you'll never do it again. You'll do it again.|You hit the water like a bell. Then: eggs, sunlight, your own bed. You laugh out loud at breakfast and the prefect stares.|You jump as the bell rings, and fall in time with it. Eggs, sunlight, bed, and the bell still in your ears.}\nopt.left.say.1 [eeebdb] = {你下坠了一个漫长而明亮的瞬间，湖面扑面而来，然后你在床上醒来，闻到鸡蛋的味道。心还在狂跳。这太壮丽了，你再也不会这么做了。你还会再做的。|你像一口钟一样撞进水里。然后：鸡蛋、阳光、你自己的床。你在早餐时放声大笑，级长瞪着你。|钟响的那一刻你跳了下去，随着钟声下坠。鸡蛋、阳光、床，耳朵里还回荡着钟声。}\n# en: Just look\nopt.right.label [eee4be] = 只是看看\n# en: {You sit on the parapet and watch the whole valley wake, the same as yesterday, the same as tomorrow. From up here the loop looks almost peaceful.|You don't jump. You count every roof in the village instead, and find one more than there should be.|The bell rings while you're sitting on the edge. You count the strokes, the same as yesterday, and climb down.}\nopt.right.say.1 [d0fe61] = {你坐在护墙上，看着整个山谷醒来，和昨天一样，和明天一样。从这上面看，循环几乎是安宁的。|你没跳。你数起了村子里的每一个屋顶，发现比应有的多了一个。|你坐在边上时钟响了。你数着钟声，和昨天一样，然后爬了下来。}\n\n@event timeloop_dare_kindness\n# en: One Perfect Day for a Stranger\ntitle [7ec626] = 给陌生人的完美一天\n# en: {There's a shy first-year who eats alone every Tuesday. Today you know everything that will go wrong for them, and you can stop all of it.|The same first-year, the same table. You know their day by heart now.|{flag(loop_perfect_day): You've made this stranger's Tuesday perfect before. You do it again anyway.|You've sat with this stranger before. Today you could do more.}} {They get the juice in the face at ten.|They fail the quiz by one mark.|Someone laughs at their hat.}\ntext [89afd9] = {有个害羞的一年级生每个星期二都独自吃饭。今天你知道 TA 会遇到的每一件倒霉事，你可以全都阻止。|同一个一年级生，同一张桌子。你现在能背出 TA 的一天了。|{flag(loop_perfect_day): 你已经给这个陌生人过过完美的星期二了。你还是又做了一遍。|你以前陪这个陌生人坐过。今天你可以做得更多。}} {十点钟 TA 会被泼一脸果汁。|TA 的小测差一分不及格。|有人会嘲笑 TA 的帽子。}\n# en: A shy first-year in the refectory looking astonished at a slice of cake, a note and a flower appearing one after another in front of them.\nscene [62e470] = 膳堂里，一个害羞的一年级生惊讶地看着面前的一块蛋糕，一张字条和一朵花接连出现在 TA 面前。\n# en: Make it perfect\nopt.left.label [26d75d] = 让它完美\n# en: {You move the juice, whisper the quiz answer, compliment the hat and buy them a slice of cake at tea. By evening they're laughing with three new friends. Tomorrow none of it will have happened, except in you.|A perfect day, built carefully, down to the cake you paid for, gone by morning. You don't mind as much as you thought you would.|You get to the hat before the laughter does, and by lunch it's the fashion. By evening three people are wearing one. None of them will remember.}\nopt.left.say.1 [b68e8f] = {你挪开果汁，悄悄告诉 TA 小测答案，夸了那顶帽子，下午茶时还给 TA 买了块蛋糕。到傍晚，TA 和三个新朋友笑成一团。明天这一切都不会发生过，除了在你心里。|精心搭建的完美一天，连你掏钱买的蛋糕都算在内，到早上就没了。你没有想象中那么在意。|你赶在笑声之前替那顶帽子说了话，到午饭时它成了时髦。到傍晚，有三个人戴上了同样的帽子。他们谁都不会记得。}\n# en: Just sit with them\nopt.right.label [0e1419] = 只是陪 TA 坐坐\n# en: {You sit with them at lunch and talk about nothing. They smile once. That one, somehow, you're sure they keep.|An hour of company for someone lonely. It doesn't last. It isn't nothing.|Someone laughs at their hat, and you laugh too, kindly, with them. They decide to keep it on.}\nopt.right.say.1 [673eaa] = {午饭时你和 TA 坐在一起，聊些无关紧要的事。TA 笑了一次。不知怎么，你确信 TA 留住了那一次。|陪一个孤单的人待了一个小时。它不会持久。但它不是什么都没有。|有人笑那顶帽子，你也笑了，善意地，和 TA 一起笑。TA 决定继续戴着。}\n\n@event timeloop_clue_bells\n# en: The Bells Are Wrong\ntitle [9032a5] = 钟声不对\n# en: You spend this Tuesday listening. Every bell in the castle strikes on time, except the astronomy tower's, which strikes a beat late, and then a beat early, as if it's arguing with itself.\ntext [e09433] = 这个星期二你一直在听。城堡里的每一口钟都准时敲响，只有天文塔的那口，晚了一拍，然后又早了一拍，像在跟自己吵架。\n# en: A student counting chimes in a castle stairwell, one hand raised, the great clock face above them stopped between two hours.\nscene [bab28b] = 一个学生在城堡楼梯间里数着钟声，一只手举起，头顶巨大的钟面停在两个钟点之间。\n# en: Follow the sound up the tower\nopt.left.label [d80a5b] = 循着声音爬上塔\n# en: Four hundred steps. At the top the door is locked and humming. Through the keyhole, gears turning the wrong way. The morning folds before you can open it.\nopt.left.say.1 [350d2d] = 四百级台阶。顶上的门锁着，嗡嗡作响。透过钥匙孔，齿轮正反着转。你还没打开门，早晨就折了起来。\n# en: Ask the clockmaster\nopt.right.label [2333c1] = 去问钟表师\n# en: The clockmaster swears the tower clock is perfect. \"The big chronometer up there? Not wound for fifty years.\" He looks at you oddly. \"Unless someone's been at it.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [e028ed] = 钟表师发誓塔钟分毫不差。“上面那台大计时仪？五十年没上过发条了。”他古怪地看了你一眼。“除非有人动过它。”\n\n@event timeloop_clue_light\n# en: A Light in the Astronomy Tower\ntitle [762a1b] = 天文塔里的一盏灯\n# en: You wake before the eggs this Tuesday and look out of the window. One lamp is burning in the astronomy tower, the only light in the castle. It goes out at the exact moment the morning begins.\ntext [a928c7] = 这个星期二，你在鸡蛋之前醒来，往窗外看。天文塔里亮着一盏灯，是整座城堡唯一的光。在早晨开始的那一刻，它熄灭了。\n# en: A dark astronomy tower at dawn, a single lamp burning in its highest window, a student watching from a courtyard far below.\nscene [dcbcd8] = 黎明时分黑暗的天文塔，最高的窗户里亮着一盏孤灯，一个学生在远远的下方庭院里望着。\n# en: Be there when it goes out\nopt.left.label [84a88b] = 在它熄灭时赶到\n# en: You run the stairs in the dark and reach the top as the lamp dies. Someone small is curled by a great brass clock, asleep. Then the morning folds, and you're in bed again, breathless.\nopt.left.say.1 [344f2b] = 你在黑暗中冲上楼梯，赶到顶层时灯正好熄灭。一个小小的人蜷在一台巨大的黄铜钟旁边，睡着了。然后早晨折了起来，你又回到了床上，上气不接下气。\n# en: Watch the window all night\nopt.right.label [b3086c] = 整夜盯着那扇窗\n# en: You don't sleep. At three a shadow passes the window: someone small, with a wand. At four the lamp goes out, and so do you.\nopt.right.say.1 [e222fc] = 你没睡。三点，一个影子经过窗前：一个小个子，拿着魔杖。四点，灯灭了，你也睡着了。\n\n@event timeloop_clue_firstyear\n# en: The Boy Who Dreads Wednesday\ntitle [8239a7] = 害怕星期三的男孩\n# en: {There's a first-year who doesn't eat breakfast on Tuesdays. He reads the same page of a textbook over and over. In the margin he's written \"one more day\".|The boy again, the page again. He's in your dreams now.|You sit down opposite the boy before he can leave.} Tomorrow is his first exam.\ntext [8e8bda] = {有个一年级生星期二从不吃早饭。他一遍又一遍地读课本的同一页。页边写着“再多一天”。|又是那个男孩，又是那一页。他现在都出现在你梦里了。|你抢在男孩离开之前，坐到了他对面。} 明天是他的第一场考试。\n# en: A small first-year hunched over a textbook at breakfast, not eating, a note in the margin reading \"one more day\".\nscene [8ec428] = 早餐桌旁一个瘦小的一年级生弓着背对着课本，一口没吃，页边的字写着“再多一天”。\n# en: Ask him about the exam\nopt.left.label [68b305] = 问他考试的事\n# en: He's terrified of it: if he fails, he goes home. \"I wished for one more day,\" he says, and then looks frightened. \"I mean, I just wished.\" He glances at the astronomy tower without meaning to.\nopt.left.say.1 [290d4d] = 他怕极了：如果挂科，他就得回家。“我许愿再多要一天，”他说，然后显得很害怕。“我是说，我只是许了个愿。”他不由自主地瞟了一眼天文塔。\n# en: Help him study instead\nopt.right.label [99b23b] = 改为帮他复习\n# en: You teach him the chapter he's been failing to read. He nearly gets it. He mentions, offhand, the big clock in the astronomy tower, \"the one that grants wishes, people say\".\nopt.right.say.1 [5721a5] = 你给他讲了那一章，他一直读不进去的那一章。他差不多懂了。他随口提到天文塔里的那座大钟，“人们说，能实现愿望的那座”。\n\n@event timeloop_cap\n# en: Wednesday, Whether You Like It or Not\ntitle [a038bd] = 不管你喜不喜欢，星期三来了\n# en: The loop pulls tight one last time and snaps. Somewhere above, a great clock bursts its springs, and the morning comes in like a wave. It's Wednesday. You feel as if you've run every Tuesday at once.\ntext [0ed344] = 循环最后一次收紧，然后崩断了。头顶某处，一座大钟的发条迸裂，早晨像潮水一样涌了进来。是星期三了。你觉得自己像是一口气跑完了所有的星期二。\n# en: A shattered brass chronometer in a tower room at dawn, springs and gears scattered across the floor, a first-year and a student staring at the wreck.\nscene [9c34ea] = 黎明时塔楼房间里一台摔碎的黄铜计时仪，发条和齿轮散落一地，一个一年级生和一个学生呆呆地看着残骸。\n# en: Climb the tower to see\nopt.left.label [834daa] = 爬上塔去看看\n# en: The astronomy tower's chronometer lies in pieces, and a first-year sits among them, white-faced. \"I just wanted one more day,\" he whispers. You sit with him until the clockmaster comes. Your bones ache for a week.\nopt.left.say.1 [f85055] = 天文塔的计时仪碎成了一地，一个一年级生坐在碎片中间，脸色煞白。“我只是想再多要一天，”他低声说。你陪他坐着，直到钟表师赶来。你的骨头疼了一个星期。\n# en: Go back to bed\nopt.right.label [ef6924] = 回去睡觉\n# en: You sleep through Wednesday's breakfast, and it is the best sleep of your life. When you wake, nobody remembers anything, and you remember all of it.\nopt.right.say.1 [684fad] = 你睡过了星期三的早餐，这是你这辈子睡得最好的一觉。醒来时，谁都不记得任何事，而你全都记得。\n\n@event timeloop_after\n# en: Only You Remember\ntitle [bfd163] = 只有你记得\n# en: Weeks later, the Tuesdays still visit you in dreams. {loop_exploit >= 2: People remember your winning streak, though not why, and some of them have stopped betting with you.|Nobody else remembers anything.} You remember the eggs, the bells, the same morning over and over. You could tell someone, if you wanted.\ntext [623b3d] = 几个星期后，那些星期二还会在梦里来找你。{loop_exploit >= 2: 人们记得你的连胜，虽然不记得原因，有些人已经不跟你打赌了。|别人什么都不记得。} 你记得那些鸡蛋、那些钟声，同一个早晨一遍又一遍。如果你愿意，可以告诉某个人。\n# en: A student alone at a window seat on an ordinary morning, a cup of tea going cold, a clock on the wall ticking forward at last.\nscene [236d1b] = 一个寻常的早晨，一个学生独自坐在窗边座位上，一杯茶渐渐凉了，墙上的钟终于向前走着。\n# en: Tell one person the truth\nopt.left.label [f50d5d] = 把真相告诉一个人\n# en: You tell a friend everything over tea. They don't believe a word and believe all of it. At the end they ask, gently, whether you gave back the winnings. You didn't. It weighs, a little.\nopt.left.say.1 [476b55] = 你在喝茶时把一切都告诉了一个朋友。对方一个字都不信，又全都信了。最后，对方温和地问你有没有把赢来的钱还回去。你没有。这让你心里有点沉。\n# en: You tell a friend everything over tea. They don't believe a word and believe all of it. {seen(timeloop_dare_kindness) > 0: At the end they say, \"So you were kind to a stranger, again and again, and nobody knew.\" You nod. \"Sounds like you,\" they say.|At the end they say, \"So you lived one day over and over, and nobody knew.\" You nod. \"Sounds exhausting,\" they say.}\nopt.left.say.2 [a330bb] = 你在喝茶时把一切都告诉了一个朋友。对方一个字都不信，又全都信了。{seen(timeloop_dare_kindness) > 0: 最后对方说：“所以你一次又一次地对一个陌生人好，而没人知道。”你点点头。“听起来像你，”对方说。|最后对方说：“所以你把同一天过了一遍又一遍，而没人知道。”你点点头。“听起来就累，”对方说。}\n# en: Keep it to yourself\nopt.right.label [09de43] = 埋在心里\n# en: You keep it. Some mornings you catch a phrase you once said in a loop, and the person you say it to looks at you as if they almost remember. They never quite do.\nopt.right.say.1 [0e7b0c] = 你留着它。有些早上，你会脱口说出某句曾在循环里说过的话，听的人看着你，像是差一点就想起来了。但他们从来没有真正想起。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/chains_minor.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/chains_minor.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event forbidden_section_1\n# en: The Silver Key\ntitle [f6e05e] = 银钥匙\n# en: Late at night. The silver key fits the Restricted Section door perfectly. Beyond it, shelves of black-bound books breathe softly in the dark. Somewhere, a prefect's footsteps.\ntext [58977f] = 夜深了。银钥匙严丝合缝地插进了禁书区的门锁。门后，一排排黑皮书在黑暗中轻轻呼吸。不知哪里，传来级长的脚步声。\n# en: A heavy iron door at the end of a library aisle, a silver key turning in its lock, a candle held low.\nscene [f9b84e] = 图书馆过道尽头一扇沉重的铁门，一把银钥匙在锁孔里转动，一支蜡烛压得很低。\n# en: Turn back\nopt.left.label [2f9f2d] = 回头\n# en: You lock the door again and hang the key on a nail in your wardrobe. Some nights you hear it rattling.\nopt.left.say.1 [9742c9] = 你把门重新锁好，把钥匙挂在衣柜里的钉子上。有些夜里，你能听见它在哗啦作响。\n# en: Slip inside\nopt.right.label [316541] = 溜进去\n# en: You spend two hours reading by candle-stub. You leave with a head full of things no student should know.\nopt.right.say.1 [b2abde] = 你借着蜡烛头读了两个小时。离开时，脑子里装满了任何学生都不该知道的东西。\n# en: Bram Hollis is waiting by the door when you come out. He is smiling. It's the worst smile you've ever seen.\nopt.right.say.2 [84c17d] = 你出来时，布拉姆·霍利斯正等在门边。他在笑。那是你见过最糟糕的笑容。\n\n@event forbidden_section_2\n# en: The Codex of Hollow Names\ntitle [b71797] = 《空洞名录》\n# en: You keep going back. One night, a book finds you. It falls from a high shelf and lands open at your feet: the Codex of Hollow Names. The first page is a list of names. The last one is still being written. It's yours.\ntext [a441b0] = 你一次又一次地回去。某天夜里，一本书找上了你。它从高高的书架上掉下来，摊开落在你脚边：《空洞名录》。第一页是一串名字。最后一个名字还在书写中。是你的名字。\n# en: A book bound in something that is not leather, open on a reading stand, its letters slowly rearranging themselves.\nscene [a07d02] = 一本用某种不是皮革的东西装订的书摊在阅读架上，字母正缓缓重新排列。\n# en: Put it back\nopt.left.label [862ed2] = 把它放回去\n# en: You shelve it. When you look back, it has fallen again, open at the same page.\nopt.left.say.1 [5dc723] = 你把它放回书架。回头一看，它又掉了下来，摊开在同一页。\n# en: Read it\nopt.right.label [fb8482] = 读它\n# en: You read until dawn. You learn about the thing under the lake. When you finally close the book, your fingertips are grey.\nopt.right.say.1 [f6835c] = 你一直读到天亮。你了解了湖底的那个东西。终于合上书时，你的指尖变成了灰色。\n# en: You read until dawn and close the book with shaking hands. Some things you can't unread.\nopt.right.say.2 [c47a6e] = 你一直读到天亮，用颤抖的手合上了书。有些东西，读过就再也忘不掉。\n\n@event forbidden_section_3\n# en: Caught\ntitle [63a445] = 被抓了\n# en: A lantern blazes in the doorway. Not a prefect this time: the headmistress herself. She looks at the book in your hands, then at you, for a very long time. \"Most students who find that book,\" she says, \"don't put it down.\"\ntext [cff27d] = 门口亮起一盏灯笼。这次不是级长：是院长本人。她看了看你手里的书，又看了看你，看了很久很久。“大多数找到那本书的学生，”她说，“都放不下它。”\n# en: A tall silhouette in the doorway of the Restricted Section, holding a lantern that casts long shadows over a startled student.\nscene [21af3f] = 禁书区门口一个高大的剪影，手中提灯在受惊的学生身上投下长长的影子。\n# en: Apologise and accept punishment\nopt.left.label [17d414] = 道歉，接受惩罚\n# en: She takes the key. \"Detention, every evening this month; your coursework can wait. And come to my tower on Thursdays. If you're going to learn dangerous things, you'll learn them properly.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [19d763] = 她收走了钥匙。“禁闭，这个月每天晚上；你的功课只能先放一放。还有，每周四到我的塔楼来。既然你要学危险的东西，就该学得像样。”\n# en: Ask her what it means\nopt.right.label [a565a0] = 问她这意味着什么\n# en: A long pause. \"It means something is waking, and it is looking for help. Make sure it doesn't find yours.\" She takes the key, leaves you the candle, and forgets, or chooses to forget, the detention.\nopt.right.say.1 [c0f71e] = 长久的沉默。“意味着有东西在苏醒，而且它在寻找帮手。别让它找到你的帮助。”她拿走了钥匙，把蜡烛留给了你，也忘了——或者说故意忘了——禁闭的事。\n\n@event curse_cure_1\n# en: A Cold That Won't Leave\ntitle [a74f1f] = 驱不散的寒意\n# en: {&The cold under your skin is getting worse.|The cold is back, worse than before.} Sister Amaranth's tonics do nothing. She looks troubled. \"This isn't an illness. It's a hunger.\" She lowers her voice. \"Old Maud might know something. Don't tell anyone I said so.\"\ntext [fd21ec] = {&皮肤下的寒意越来越重了。|寒意又回来了，比上次更重。}阿玛兰斯修女的药剂毫无作用。她面露忧色。“这不是病。是一种饥饿。”她压低声音，“老莫德也许知道些什么。别说是我说的。”\n# en: A student wrapped in three blankets beside a roaring fire, breath still fogging the air, fingertips grey.\nscene [ecf450] = 一个学生裹着三层毯子坐在熊熊炉火旁，呼出的气仍凝成白雾，指尖发灰。\n# en: Endure it\nopt.left.label [9e8205] = 忍着\n# en: You pile on blankets and keep going. The cold whispers sometimes. You pretend not to hear. Maud, you suppose, will still be there if it gets worse.\nopt.left.say.1 [10575f] = 你堆上毯子，继续过日子。寒意有时会低语。你假装没听见。你想，要是更糟了，莫德总还在那儿。\n# en: Go to Old Maud\nopt.right.label [fb88bc] = 去找老莫德\n# en: Maud takes one look at your fingertips and puts the kettle on. \"Moonpetal,\" she says. \"Grows in the Whisperwood. Only blooms after dark. You'll fetch it yourself — the cure has to come from the cursed.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [aa1b58] = 莫德看了一眼你的指尖，就把水壶放上了炉子。“月瓣花，”她说，“长在低语森林里。只在天黑后开。你得自己去采——解药必须来自被诅咒的人。”\n\n@event curse_cure_2\n# en: Moonpetal\ntitle [676f04] = 月瓣花\n# en: The Whisperwood after dark. Things move between the trees. In a clearing, a single silver flower is opening, and between you and it stands a stag made of shadow.\ntext [b47cd6] = 天黑后的低语森林。树林间有东西在移动。一片空地上，一朵银色的花正在绽放，而你和它之间，站着一头由阴影构成的雄鹿。\n# en: A dark forest at nightfall, a single silver flower glowing in a clearing, eyes watching from between the trees.\nscene [4d6ecf] = 暮色中漆黑的森林，空地上一朵银花发着光，树影间有眼睛在窥视。\n# en: Sneak around it\nopt.left.label [efbf19] = 绕过它\n# en: You creep through the bracken and pluck the moonpetal. Maud's brew tastes like moonlight and burnt sugar. The cold is gone.\nopt.left.say.1 [1fb52a] = 你悄悄穿过蕨丛，摘下了月瓣花。莫德熬的药尝起来像月光和焦糖。寒意消失了。\n# en: The stag sees you. You run. No moonpetal tonight; you'll have to come back.\nopt.left.say.2 [f03c88] = 雄鹿看见了你。你拔腿就跑。今晚没有月瓣花了；你得再来一趟。\n# en: Face the stag\nopt.right.label [c629ba] = 直面雄鹿\n# en: You stand your ground. The stag lowers its head — and steps aside. Maud's brew drives the cold out of your bones.\nopt.right.say.1 [eecd0a] = 你站定不动。雄鹿低下头——然后让开了路。莫德的药把寒意从你骨子里驱了出去。\n# en: The stag charges. You wake up in the bracken at dawn. Maud bandages you. \"Another night,\" she says.\nopt.right.say.2 [056977] = 雄鹿冲了过来。黎明时你在蕨丛里醒来。莫德给你包扎。“改天晚上再来。”她说。\n\n@event grisk_offer\n# en: A Helpful Goblin\ntitle [6c54bd] = 乐于助人的地精\n# en: A goblin in a velvet waistcoat falls into step beside you. \"Grisk,\" he says, \"friend to the temporarily embarrassed. Sixty crowns, today. Twelve a month for six months. Very reasonable.\"\ntext [266d6d] = 一个穿天鹅绒马甲的地精走到你身边，和你并肩同行。“格里斯克，”他说，“暂时手头紧的人的朋友。今天给你六十克朗。每月还十二，还六个月。非常合理。”\n# en: A small goblin in a velvet waistcoat and pince-nez, sitting on a crate in an alley, a ledger in one hand and a quill behind his ear.\nscene [a36b2a] = 一个穿天鹅绒马甲、戴夹鼻眼镜的小地精坐在小巷的木箱上，一手拿着账簿，耳后夹着羽毛笔。\n# en: No thank you\nopt.left.label [6c52d1] = 不用了，谢谢\n# en: \"My door is always open,\" Grisk says. You suspect it is actually always locked.\nopt.left.say.1 [1a3e29] = “我的门永远为你敞开。”格里斯克说。你怀疑它其实永远锁着。\n# en: Take the loan\nopt.right.label [e30491] = 接受贷款\n# en: He counts out sixty crowns, writes your name in the ledger and smiles with a great many teeth.\nopt.right.say.1 [35d10d] = 他数出六十克朗，把你的名字写进账簿，笑着露出了非常多的牙齿。\n\n@event grisk_board_debt\n# en: Settled, With Interest\ntitle [3033c4] = 已结清，外加利息\n# en: The bursar's office has stopped sending reminders. Instead, Grisk is waiting outside it with a receipt. \"Two months' board, paid in full,\" he beams. \"By me. A loan, naturally, with a little extra for your pocket. Twelve a month for six months. You can sign here.\"\ntext [72d609] = 财务处已经不再寄催缴单了。取而代之的是，格里斯克拿着一张收据等在门外。“两个月的伙食费，全额付清，”他笑容满面，“我付的。当然是借款，外加一点零花钱。每月十二，还六个月。在这儿签字就行。”\n# en: A goblin in a velvet waistcoat waiting outside the bursar's office, waving a receipt like a small flag.\nscene [c90a78] = 一个穿天鹅绒马甲的地精等在财务处门外，像挥小旗一样挥着一张收据。\n# en: Sign his ledger\nopt.left.label [d542c5] = 在他的账本上签字\n# en: You sign. He has already paid the board out of it; he counts the rest into your palm and writes your name in the ledger with a flourish that has teeth in it.\nopt.left.say.1 [28c617] = 你签了字。伙食费他已经从里头付掉了；剩下的他数进你的掌心，在账本上龙飞凤舞地写下你的名字——那一笔带着獠牙。\n# en: Beg the bursar for time\nopt.right.label [ba6715] = 去求财务处宽限\n# en: You spend an afternoon on a hard bench outside the bursar's office and leave with a month's grace and the feeling of having been looked at over spectacles for a very long time.\nopt.right.say.1 [1267b3] = 你在财务处门外的硬板凳上坐了一下午，最后换来一个月的宽限，以及被人从眼镜上方久久打量的感觉。\n\n@event grisk_favour\n# en: Grisk's Proposition\ntitle [5cdd5b] = 格里斯克的提议\n# en: Grisk finds you in the library. \"I could cancel your debt,\" he murmurs. \"A small favour. There's a book in the archive — the Founders' Ledger. A client wants a page. Just one page.\"\ntext [816cbf] = 格里斯克在图书馆找到了你。“我可以免了你的债，”他低声说，“帮个小忙就行。档案库里有本书——《创始者账簿》。有位客户想要其中一页。就一页。”\n# en: The goblin moneylender leaning close in a library alcove, pushing a slip of paper across a table.\nscene [64ffac] = 地精放贷人在图书馆的壁龛里凑近，把一张纸条推过桌面。\n# en: Refuse\nopt.left.label [cada35] = 拒绝\n# en: \"Pity.\" He makes a note in his ledger. It looks expensive.\nopt.left.say.1 [84fdf0] = “可惜。”他在账簿上记了一笔。看起来代价不小。\n# en: Do the favour\nopt.right.label [c95c1c] = 帮这个忙\n# en: You razor a page from the oldest ledger in the archive. Grisk tears up your debt. You notice the page had a black star on it.\nopt.right.say.1 [3798eb] = 你从档案库最古老的账簿上割下了一页。格里斯克撕掉了你的欠条。你注意到那一页上有一颗黑星。\n# en: The archivist catches you with a razor in your hand. Detention. Grisk is nowhere to be found, and the debt stands.\nopt.right.say.2 [17b625] = 档案管理员撞见你手里拿着刀片。禁闭。格里斯克不见踪影，债务照旧。\n\n@event grisk_smuggling\n# en: One More Job\ntitle [5823eb] = 再干一票\n# en: Grisk again. \"You were so good last time. I have parcels that need carrying: collected at the boathouse at midnight, delivered to the village by day. Thirty crowns a parcel. Steady work, for steady hands.\"\ntext [e0fb8b] = 又是格里斯克。“上次你干得真漂亮。我有些包裹需要人送：午夜在船屋取货，白天送到村里。一个包裹三十克朗。稳定的活儿，给手稳的人。”\n# en: A crate of clinking bottles hidden under a tarp on a dark boathouse dock, a small goblin silhouetted against the lake.\nscene [539e69] = 漆黑的船屋码头上，一只装满叮当作响的瓶子的箱子藏在油布下，湖面映出一个小地精的剪影。\n# en: You're done with him\nopt.left.label [71da7e] = 你和他到此为止了\n# en: \"Everyone's done with me eventually,\" Grisk says, without rancour. You never see him again.\nopt.left.say.1 [288fa5] = “大家最后都会跟我到此为止的。”格里斯克毫无怨气地说。你再也没见过他。\n# en: Hear him out\nopt.right.label [b1225b] = 听他说下去\n# en: He explains the arrangement: the jetty, midnight, oilcloth, a cobbler's back door. \"Any month you like,\" he says. \"I'm very flexible.\" You walk away with the arrangement in your head, and it won't leave.\nopt.right.say.1 [69c60d] = 他把安排讲给你听：码头，午夜，油布包，鞋匠铺的后门。“哪个月都行，”他说，“我很好商量。”你走开时，这套安排一直在你脑子里转，赶也赶不走。\n\n@event fox_injured\n# en: Smoke in the Woods\ntitle [bf8cb4] = 林间青烟\n# en: At the edge of the forest, a thin plume of smoke. A small fox is caught in a rusted snare, its tail smouldering like an ember. It snarls at you, then whimpers.\ntext [b3132b] = 森林边缘，升起一缕细细的烟。一只小狐狸被生锈的捕兽夹夹住了，尾巴像余烬一样冒着火星。它冲你龇牙，然后呜咽起来。\n# en: A small red fox lying in the snow at the forest edge, its tail smouldering like a dying campfire, one paw caught in a rusted snare.\nscene [b579d6] = 森林边缘的雪地里躺着一只小红狐，尾巴像将熄的篝火般闷燃，一只爪子卡在生锈的捕兽夹里。\n# en: Leave it — it's wild\nopt.left.label [d9cfe4] = 别管——它是野的\n# en: You walk away. That night you dream of smoke.\nopt.left.say.1 [6bc432] = 你走开了。那天夜里，你梦见了烟。\n# en: Free it and bandage the paw\nopt.right.label [8a0394] = 放了它，包扎爪子\n# en: It bites you, of course. But it lets you wrap the paw. Then it limps into the trees, glancing back once with eyes like embers.\nopt.right.say.1 [113ea7] = 它当然咬了你一口。但它让你把爪子包扎好了。然后它一瘸一拐地走进树林，回头看了你一眼，眼睛像余烬。\n\n@event fox_returns\n# en: A Visitor at the Window\ntitle [8da400] = 窗边的访客\n# en: Two months later, a fox with a glowing tail is sitting on your windowsill. Its paw has healed. At its feet: a small, dusty ruby.\ntext [635017] = 两个月后，一只尾巴发光的狐狸坐在你的窗台上。它的爪子已经好了。它脚边放着一颗小小的、蒙尘的红宝石。\n# en: A fox with a softly glowing tail sitting on a stone windowsill at dawn, a ruby glinting at its feet.\nscene [41ab76] = 黎明时分，一只尾巴柔柔发光的狐狸坐在石砌窗台上，脚边闪着一颗红宝石。\n# en: Shoo it away\nopt.left.label [01018d] = 把它赶走\n# en: It leaves the ruby and vanishes. You sell the ruby. Somehow you feel you've failed a test.\nopt.left.say.1 [4959d7] = 它留下红宝石就消失了。你卖掉了红宝石。不知为何，你觉得自己没通过某种考验。\n# en: Open the window\nopt.right.label [15dfe6] = 打开窗户\n# en: It hops in, inspects your room thoroughly and falls asleep on your pillow. Your pillow is now slightly scorched. You don't mind.\nopt.right.say.1 [d3c253] = 它跳进来，把你的房间仔仔细细检查了一遍，然后在你的枕头上睡着了。你的枕头现在有点焦了。你不介意。\n\n@event fox_bond\n# en: Ember\ntitle [c95de4] = 余烬\n# en: The fox has decided. {term: It follows you to class|It follows you through the half-empty corridors}, sleeps on your feet and growls at Bram. Students aren't allowed unregistered familiars. Registering one costs fifteen crowns.\ntext [ccfb40] = 狐狸已经做了决定。{term: 它跟着你去上课|它跟着你穿过半空的走廊}，睡在你脚上，还冲布拉姆低吼。学生不允许拥有未登记的魔宠。登记一只要十五克朗。\n# en: A student and a small fire-tailed fox nose to nose, a soft golden light connecting them.\nscene [f6fbd2] = 一个学生和一只火尾小狐狸鼻尖相对，一道柔和的金光把他们连在一起。\n# en: Hide her\nopt.left.label [ce99de] = 把她藏起来\n# en: You name her Ember. Nobody notices a fox that doesn't want to be noticed.\nopt.left.say.1 [9f2aee] = 你给她取名“余烬”。一只不想被注意的狐狸，没人会注意到。\n# en: A professor spots her at once. You pay what you can of the fee — and Ember is yours, officially.\nopt.left.say.2 [8afb09] = 一位教授一眼就发现了她。你尽力交了能交的费用——余烬正式成了你的魔宠。\n# en: Register her properly\nopt.right.label [9506c8] = 正式登记\n# en: The registrar stamps a form. \"Name?\" \"Ember,\" you say. The fox looks smug.\nopt.right.say.1 [c579b1] = 登记员在表格上盖了章。“名字？”“余烬。”你说。狐狸一脸得意。\n\n@event fox_helps\n# en: Ember Finds Something\ntitle [020478] = 余烬找到了东西\n# en: {~Ember drags you, by the sleeve, through three corridors and a hedge. She's found something. She's very proud of it.|Ember sits on your book until you look at her. She has found something, and she has buried it, and now she wants you to dig.|Ember comes back from the lake with her tail steaming and something in her jaws. She drops it at your feet like a gift.}\ntext [1b03f8] = {~余烬叼着你的袖子，拖你穿过三条走廊和一道树篱。她找到了东西。她非常骄傲。|余烬坐在你的书上，直到你看向她。她找到了东西，还把它埋了起来，现在要你去挖。|余烬从湖边回来，尾巴冒着热气，嘴里叼着什么。她把它放在你脚边，像献上一份礼物。}\n# en: A fox nosing through dry leaves under a hedge and emerging with a purse in its jaws.\nscene [fa286c] = 一只狐狸在树篱下的枯叶里拱来拱去，叼出一只钱袋。\n# en: Let her keep it\nopt.left.label [dacce7] = 让她留着吧\n# en: {~It's a sock. Ember is thrilled. You are thrilled for her.|It's a glove that smells of the stables. Ember sleeps on it for a week.|It's a pine cone, slightly charred. She guards it from Bram personally.}\nopt.left.say.1 [0fa900] = {~是一只袜子。余烬高兴坏了。你也替她高兴。|是一只带着马厩味的手套。余烬在上面睡了一个星期。|是一颗烧焦了一点的松果。她亲自看着它，不让布拉姆靠近。}\n# en: See what it is\nopt.right.label [c3d611] = 看看是什么\n# en: A lost purse. You return it to its owner, who insists you keep the reward.\nopt.right.say.1 [8c1c68] = 一只丢失的钱袋。你把它还给了失主，失主坚持要你收下酬谢。\n# en: A lost page from a professor's lecture notes. Enlightening.\nopt.right.say.2 [3f4db6] = 一页遗失的教授讲义。很有启发。\n\n@event ghost_socks\n# en: Rearranged Socks\ntitle [c1194d] = 被整理的袜子\n# en: Every morning, your socks have been rearranged. By colour. Then alphabetically by colour. Last night they spelled out \"HELP\".\ntext [79fec3] = 每天早上，你的袜子都被重新整理过。按颜色。然后按颜色的首字母排序。昨晚，它们拼出了“救命”。\n# en: A dormitory drawer open in candlelight, socks arranged in a careful spiral, a faint shimmer in the air above them.\nscene [c7a0d1] = 烛光中一只打开的宿舍抽屉，袜子被整齐地排成螺旋，上方的空气里有一丝微光。\n# en: Ignore it\nopt.left.label [e2ae0c] = 不去管它\n# en: You buy new socks. The next morning they're arranged by mood.\nopt.left.say.1 [57e0f1] = 你买了新袜子。第二天早上，它们按心情排好了。\n# en: Stay up and watch\nopt.right.label [03406b] = 熬夜守着\n# en: At three in the morning, the drawer slides open by itself.\nopt.right.say.1 [69a0e4] = 凌晨三点，抽屉自己滑开了。\n\n@event ghost_reveal\n# en: Prudence\ntitle [a6d175] = 普鲁登丝\n# en: A girl in old-fashioned robes, translucent and very polite. \"Prudence Ashworth, class of 1793. Terribly sorry about the socks. I never finished my letter. Could you deliver it? The village. The bakery. Please.\"\ntext [fd675e] = 一个穿着老式长袍的女孩，半透明，非常有礼貌。“普鲁登丝·阿什沃斯，1793 届。袜子的事实在抱歉。我的信一直没写完。你能帮我送去吗？镇上。面包店。拜托了。”\n# en: A translucent girl in old-fashioned school robes, hovering by a window, holding a faded letter.\nscene [2de1ba] = 一个身穿老式校袍的半透明女孩悬浮在窗边，手里拿着一封褪色的信。\n# en: Exorcise her\nopt.left.label [eb2bc2] = 驱散她\n# en: You speak the banishing rite. She fades with a small, hurt look. Your socks stay put after that. You feel a bit rotten.\nopt.left.say.1 [940567] = 你念出驱逐仪式的咒文。她带着一点受伤的神情消散了。从那以后你的袜子再没动过。你觉得自己有点混蛋。\n# en: Promise to deliver it\nopt.right.label [32f8b3] = 答应帮她送信\n# en: She claps her translucent hands. \"Oh, thank you! It's only taken two hundred years.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [e468ed] = 她拍着半透明的双手。“噢，谢谢你！才等了两百年而已。”\n\n@event ghost_letter\n# en: A Letter Two Hundred Years Late\ntitle [4de394] = 迟到两百年的信\n# en: The bakery has been in the same family for two hundred years. The old baker reads Prudence's letter at the counter. \"My great-great-great-grandmother waited for this,\" she says. \"She never stopped.\"\ntext [07ef08] = 这家面包店两百年来一直由同一个家族经营。老面包师在柜台前读着普鲁登丝的信。“我的高高高祖母一直在等这封信，”她说，“她从没停止过等待。”\n# en: A cosy village bakery, an old baker reading a yellowed letter with tears on her cheeks, a ghostly figure smiling in the doorway.\nscene [481d5e] = 温馨的小镇面包店，一位老面包师读着一封泛黄的信，泪流满面，门口一个幽灵般的身影在微笑。\n# en: Stay and listen to the story\nopt.left.label [7f246b] = 留下来听这个故事\n# en: Prudence was in love with the baker's daughter. When you return, the ghost is waiting, glowing like a sunrise. Then she's gone, at peace.\nopt.left.say.1 [623354] = 原来普鲁登丝爱着面包师的女儿。你回去时，幽灵正等着你，像日出一样发着光。然后她走了，安详地。\n# en: Leave quietly\nopt.right.label [69ae5f] = 悄悄离开\n# en: The baker presses a free loaf into your hands. That night, your socks are arranged into a heart. Then never again.\nopt.right.say.1 [104d89] = 面包师把一条面包塞进你手里，不收钱。那天夜里，你的袜子被摆成了一颗心。之后再也没有过。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/classes.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/classes.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event pyro_candle\n# en: Light the Candle\ntitle [b66d59] = 点燃蜡烛\n# en: Professor Thorne places a candle at the far end of the hall. \"Light it. From there.\" The class holds its breath. Last week a boy lit his own sleeve.\ntext [249ae3] = 索恩教授把一支蜡烛放在大厅的尽头。“点燃它。就站在那儿。”全班屏住了呼吸。上周有个男生点着了自己的袖子。\n# en: A dark classroom, a single candle on a stool at the far end, Professor Thorne's silhouette with folded arms.\nscene [b8447a] = 昏暗的教室，远处凳子上立着一支蜡烛，索恩教授抱着双臂的剪影。\n# en: A careful spark\nopt.left.label [1455cd] = 一点小心的火花\n# en: The wick catches with a polite little flame. Thorne grunts. From him, that is applause.\nopt.left.say.1 [e34a27] = 烛芯燃起一朵彬彬有礼的小火苗。索恩哼了一声。从他嘴里出来，这就算掌声了。\n# en: Nothing. Then a wisp of smoke from your fingertips. A few people giggle.\nopt.left.say.2 [266366] = 什么也没有。然后你的指尖冒出一缕青烟。几个人咯咯笑了起来。\n# en: Show them real fire\nopt.right.label [c2dc32] = 让他们见识真正的火\n# en: The candle erupts in a pillar of white flame and then settles, obedient. The room goes quiet.\nopt.right.say.1 [d02a9d] = 蜡烛喷出一道白色火柱，随即乖乖地平息下来。教室里鸦雀无声。\n# en: The candle lights. So do the curtains. And, briefly, your eyebrows.\nopt.right.say.2 [ed4c08] = 蜡烛点着了。窗帘也点着了。还有——短暂地——你的眉毛。\n\n@event pyro_ember_heart\n# en: The Ember Within\ntitle [819842] = 心中余烬\n# en: Today's exercise: hold a spark inside your chest and let it grow. \"Fire is fed by will,\" says Thorne. \"And by you, if you are careless.\" It burns. It is supposed to burn.\ntext [9171f7] = 今天的练习：把一点火星含在胸中，让它慢慢长大。“火以意志为食，”索恩说，“如果你不小心，也以你为食。”很烫。本来就该烫。\n# en: Students sitting cross-legged in a stone circle, each with a faint glow behind their ribs.\nscene [c54174] = 学生们盘腿坐在石圈里，每个人的肋骨后都透出微弱的光。\n# en: Stop when it hurts\nopt.left.label [d5afc2] = 痛了就停\n# en: You let the spark gutter out. Thorne says nothing. The silence is a grade.\nopt.left.say.1 [189b77] = 你让火星熄灭了。索恩一言不发。这份沉默就是分数。\n# en: Hold it longer than anyone\nopt.right.label [a7c8d5] = 坚持得比谁都久\n# en: Your vision goes white at the edges. When you open your eyes, your breath is steaming and the stones around you are warm.\nopt.right.say.1 [4859e1] = 你的视野边缘泛起一片白。睁开眼时，你呼出的气在冒白烟，周围的石头都是温热的。\n\n@event pyro_lyra_spar\n# en: A Challenge in Class\ntitle [68aa64] = 课堂上的挑战\n# en: \"Professor, I'd like a demonstration partner.\" Lyra Vaneshade points her wand at you without looking. \"That one.\"\ntext [c4c320] = “教授，我想要一个演示搭档。”莱拉·凡妮希德看都没看，就把魔杖指向了你。“就那个。”\n# en: Two students facing each other across a scorched practice ring; one is a tall girl with crimson hair, flames curling around her wrists.\nscene [377b0a] = 两名学生隔着烧焦的练习圈对峙；其中一个是高挑的红发女孩，火焰缠绕在她的手腕上。\n# en: Politely decline\nopt.left.label [8a601d] = 礼貌地拒绝\n# en: \"As expected,\" Lyra says, and turns away. It stings worse than a burn would.\nopt.left.say.1 [6071b1] = “果然。”莱拉说完便转过身去。这比被火烫还疼。\n# en: Step into the ring\nopt.right.label [bf566a] = 走进圈内\n# en: Your fire meets hers and holds. When Thorne calls time, Lyra is breathing hard. \"Again. Next week,\" she says.\nopt.right.say.1 [4d681f] = 你的火焰迎上她的，稳稳顶住。索恩喊停时，莱拉气喘吁吁。“再来。下周。”她说。\n# en: She wins in under a minute — but she looks at you afterwards like she's filing you away for later.\nopt.right.say.2 [ab3e00] = 她不到一分钟就赢了——但她事后看你的眼神，像是把你归档留待日后处理。\n\n@event tide_lake_lesson\n# en: The Lake Answers\ntitle [146cec] = 湖水的回应\n# en: Waist-deep in the freezing lake at dawn, Professor Marisol tells you to ask the water to rise. \"Ask,\" she says. \"Never demand. The lake remembers rudeness.\"\ntext [781568] = 黎明时分，你站在齐腰深的冰冷湖水里，玛丽索尔教授让你请求湖水升起。“是请求，”她说，“永远别命令。湖会记仇。”\n# en: Students standing waist-deep in a silver lake at dawn, ripples spiralling out from their outstretched hands.\nscene [398563] = 黎明时分，学生们站在齐腰深的银色湖水中，涟漪从他们伸出的手掌间旋转着荡开。\n# en: Command it anyway\nopt.left.label [400388] = 偏要命令它\n# en: The water rises in a shivering column. Marisol raises an eyebrow. \"Effective. Rude.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [4cfa1b] = 湖水升起一道颤抖的水柱。玛丽索尔挑了挑眉。“有效。无礼。”\n# en: The lake declines, and expresses its displeasure by knocking you flat.\nopt.left.say.2 [412991] = 湖拒绝了，并用一个浪头把你拍倒来表达不满。\n# en: Listen first\nopt.right.label [c35e18] = 先倾听\n# en: You stand still until the cold stops hurting. The lake murmurs — and for a moment you could swear it said your name.\nopt.right.say.1 [17e74d] = 你一动不动，直到寒冷不再刺痛。湖水低声絮语——有一瞬间，你敢发誓它叫了你的名字。\n\n@event tide_storm_drill\n# en: Storm Drill\ntitle [cb32cb] = 风暴演练\n# en: A storm rolls over the lake and Marisol is delighted. \"Perfect conditions! Hold back the rain for as long as you can.\" Most of the class has already fled.\ntext [1b19ce] = 一场风暴卷过湖面，玛丽索尔高兴坏了。“完美的条件！尽可能久地挡住雨！”班上大部分人早已逃之夭夭。\n# en: A rocky shore under black clouds, a few figures in soaked robes holding back a curtain of rain with raised hands.\nscene [04bfa1] = 乌云下的岩石湖岸，几个浑身湿透的身影举起双手，挡住一道雨幕。\n# en: Shelter with the sensible ones\nopt.left.label [897439] = 和明智的人一起躲雨\n# en: You watch from the boathouse, dry and slightly ashamed.\nopt.left.say.1 [061ff3] = 你在船屋里观看，身上干爽，心里略感惭愧。\n# en: Stay out in it\nopt.right.label [f1db6c] = 留在雨里\n# en: You hold the rain back for almost a minute. You also catch Lake Fever.\nopt.right.say.1 [28d2b9] = 你挡住雨将近一分钟。你也染上了湖热病。\n# en: For a full minute, the rain parts around you. Marisol whoops like a child.\nopt.right.say.2 [814a26] = 整整一分钟，雨水在你周围分开。玛丽索尔像个孩子一样欢呼。\n\n@event tide_drowned_bell\n# en: The Drowned Bell\ntitle [5e6210] = 沉没的钟\n# en: Ever since the lake held its breath in Mistveil, you listen for it. During a breathing-charm lesson you hear it: a bell, deep under the lake, tolling slow and soft. Nobody else seems to notice.\ntext [c76f61] = 自从迷雾月湖水屏住呼吸之后，你就一直在留意。在一堂水下呼吸咒的课上，你听见了：湖底深处有一口钟，缓慢而轻柔地敲响。似乎没有别人注意到。\n# en: Murky green water, a shaft of light catching the bronze curve of an enormous bell half-buried in silt.\nscene [7fdf05] = 浑浊的绿色湖水中，一束光照亮了一口半埋在淤泥里的巨钟的青铜弧面。\n# en: Mention it to Professor Marisol\nopt.left.label [dfb6fd] = 告诉玛丽索尔教授\n# en: Her face goes very still. \"There is no bell,\" she says. \"There hasn't been a bell for three hundred years.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [084e01] = 她的表情一下子僵住了。“没有什么钟，”她说，“三百年来，那里都没有钟。”\n# en: Dive down to find it\nopt.right.label [86c496] = 潜下去找它\n# en: Deep in the silt you find a broken piece of bronze, warm to the touch, engraved with a black star.\nopt.right.say.1 [6a378c] = 在深深的淤泥里，你找到一块破碎的青铜，摸上去是温的，上面刻着一颗黑色的星。\n# en: The water closes over you, dark and cold. You surface gasping, empty-handed, with the bell still ringing in your teeth.\nopt.right.say.2 [d6f863] = 湖水在你头顶合拢，黑暗而冰冷。你两手空空地浮上水面，大口喘气，那钟声还在你牙齿间回荡。\n\n@event illusion_mirror_self\n# en: A Second You\ntitle [77533b] = 另一个你\n# en: Assignment: conjure a convincing illusion of yourself. Professor Vey circles the room. \"Most students make themselves taller. Interesting, what people choose.\"\ntext [a41437] = 作业：变出一个以假乱真的自己。维伊教授在教室里踱步。“大多数学生会把自己变高一点。人们的选择，真有意思。”\n# en: A student facing a shimmering duplicate of themselves; the duplicate's smile is a little too wide.\nscene [a36cd8] = 一个学生面对着自己闪闪发光的分身；分身的笑容咧得有点太开了。\n# en: Make it flattering\nopt.left.label [90461e] = 美化一下\n# en: Your double is radiant, confident, slightly better-looking. Vey writes something down and smiles thinly.\nopt.left.say.1 [07e402] = 你的分身容光焕发、自信满满，长得还比你好看一点。维伊记下了什么，淡淡一笑。\n# en: Make it accurate\nopt.right.label [a663ce] = 如实呈现\n# en: Your double slouches the way you slouch and chews its lip the way you do. It is deeply unsettling. Vey awards top marks.\nopt.right.say.1 [6dfcc6] = 你的分身和你一样驼着背，和你一样咬着嘴唇。这让人非常不安。维伊给了满分。\n\n@event illusion_professor_prank\n# en: The Professor's Face\ntitle [795234] = 教授的脸\n# en: Your classmates dare you: wear a glamour of Professor Thorne and cancel tomorrow's Arcana lecture. \"Nobody will know,\" says someone who will definitely tell.\ntext [f7081e] = 同学们怂恿你：用幻术变成索恩教授的模样，取消明天的奥术课。“没人会发现的。”说这话的人一定会去告密。\n# en: A student wearing a shimmering glamour of a stern old professor, classmates stifling laughter behind a door.\nscene [6c7f98] = 一个学生顶着严厉老教授的闪烁幻象，同学们躲在门后憋着笑。\n# en: Absolutely not\nopt.left.label [c1af76] = 绝对不行\n# en: Wise. Someone else tries it the next day and spends a week scrubbing gargoyles.\nopt.left.say.1 [2654ad] = 明智。第二天另一个人试了，结果刷了一个星期的石像鬼。\n# en: Do it\nopt.right.label [f29ea1] = 干了\n# en: \"Class is cancelled,\" you announce in Thorne's gravel voice. The cheering is audible from three towers away.\nopt.right.say.1 [52cb62] = “今天停课。”你用索恩那砂砾般的嗓音宣布。欢呼声在三座塔外都听得见。\n# en: Halfway through, the glamour flickers. The real Thorne is standing in the doorway. He is not laughing.\nopt.right.say.2 [c3574a] = 演到一半，幻术闪了一下。真正的索恩就站在门口。他没有笑。\n\n@event illusion_dragon\n# en: The Courtyard Dragon\ntitle [e608ce] = 庭院里的龙\n# en: For your term project you must create an illusion that fools a crowd. You could make something small and flawless. Or you could put a dragon in the courtyard at lunchtime.\ntext [47084a] = 你的学期作业是制造一个能骗过一群人的幻象。你可以做个小而完美的东西。或者，你可以在午饭时间往庭院里放一条龙。\n# en: A vast translucent dragon coiled around the academy's central fountain, students frozen mid-step beneath it.\nscene [56332c] = 一条巨大的半透明的龙盘绕在学院中央喷泉上，下方的学生们僵在原地。\n# en: Small and flawless\nopt.left.label [1a8234] = 小而完美\n# en: A single butterfly that nobody can tell is fake. Vey taps it with her wand and it bursts into light. \"Perfect.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [b8332b] = 一只谁也看不出是假的蝴蝶。维伊用魔杖轻轻一点，它化作一团光。“完美。”\n# en: Dragon. Obviously.\nopt.right.label [602404] = 龙。当然是龙。\n# en: The dragon uncurls around the fountain and yawns. Forty students scream. Vey laughs until she cries.\nopt.right.say.1 [617c57] = 龙绕着喷泉舒展身体，打了个哈欠。四十个学生尖叫起来。维伊笑得眼泪都出来了。\n# en: The dragon is magnificent for four seconds and then sneezes into a cloud of glitter. The headmistress was eating lunch below it.\nopt.right.say.2 [f1e819] = 那条龙壮观了四秒钟，然后打了个喷嚏，化作一团亮片。院长当时正在下面吃午饭。\n\n@event alch_first_brew\n# en: Draught of Mild Courage\ntitle [da711f] = 微勇药剂\n# en: The recipe is on the board: Draught of Mild Courage. Seven ingredients, three stirs clockwise, one counter-clockwise, and never — never — add the newt eye early.\ntext [74f236] = 配方写在黑板上：微勇药剂。七种原料，顺时针搅三下，逆时针一下，而且绝不——绝不——能提前放蝾螈眼。\n# en: Rows of bubbling cauldrons in a low stone room, each giving off a different coloured steam.\nscene [88ce92] = 低矮的石室里一排排冒泡的坩埚，每一口都升起不同颜色的蒸汽。\n# en: Follow the recipe exactly\nopt.left.label [ae651f] = 严格按配方来\n# en: A clear, honey-coloured brew. The professor sniffs it and nods. Safe, solid, unremarkable.\nopt.left.say.1 [6cc5ac] = 一锅清澈的蜂蜜色药剂。教授闻了闻，点点头。安全、扎实、平平无奇。\n# en: Improvise a little\nopt.right.label [99aea4] = 稍微即兴发挥\n# en: Your variant tastes like cinnamon and makes people brave enough to ask for things. A fourth-year buys the whole flask.\nopt.right.say.1 [228a65] = 你的改良版尝起来有肉桂味，能让人鼓起勇气开口要东西。一个四年级生把整瓶都买走了。\n# en: You taste-test. Mistake. For the next month, everything you say comes out as a deep, wet croak.\nopt.right.say.2 [875ffe] = 你亲口尝了一下。失误。接下来一个月，你说的每句话都变成了低沉潮湿的呱呱声。\n\n@event alch_unstable_cauldron\n# en: Unstable Cauldron\ntitle [69d2ed] = 不稳定的坩埚\n# en: The cauldron next to yours starts to shudder. Its owner, a terrified first-year, has added the newt eye early. It is going to blow.\ntext [e609c0] = 你旁边的坩埚开始颤抖。它的主人，一个吓坏了的一年级生，提前放了蝾螈眼。它要炸了。\n# en: A cauldron shuddering on its iron legs, violet bubbles swelling over the rim, a student frozen beside it.\nscene [444a20] = 一口坩埚在铁脚上颤动，紫色气泡从锅沿鼓出，旁边的学生僵住了。\n# en: Dive for cover\nopt.left.label [5f718b] = 扑倒找掩护\n# en: The explosion paints the ceiling violet. Nobody is hurt. Everybody is purple.\nopt.left.say.1 [c25dd0] = 爆炸把天花板染成了紫色。没人受伤。所有人都变紫了。\n# en: Try to neutralise it\nopt.right.label [cd26e0] = 试着中和它\n# en: A pinch of salt, a drop of moonwater, a quick counter-stir. The bubbling dies. The first-year bursts into grateful tears.\nopt.right.say.1 [21799b] = 一撮盐，一滴月光水，飞快地反向搅动。冒泡停了。那个一年级生感激得哭了出来。\n# en: The cauldron goes off in your face. You spend a week with violet eyebrows and a ringing in your ears.\nopt.right.say.2 [2f0478] = 坩埚在你脸上炸开了。你顶着紫色眉毛、耳朵嗡嗡响地过了一个星期。\n\n@event alch_corvin_market\n# en: A Side Business\ntitle [5b5ae3] = 副业\n# en: Corvin turns your leftover potions over in his hands. \"These would sell for real crowns in the village. I know a buyer. I take a third. Nobody needs to know they came from a classroom.\"\ntext [179554] = 科尔文把你剩下的药剂在手里翻来覆去地看。“这些在镇上能卖真金白银。我认识个买家。我抽三成。没人需要知道它们是从教室里出来的。”\n# en: A young man leaning on a cauldron table, holding up a vial against the light with a merchant's grin.\nscene [95a8de] = 一个年轻人倚着坩埚桌，把一只小瓶举到光下，脸上挂着商人的笑。\n# en: Decline\nopt.left.label [eaef39] = 拒绝\n# en: \"Your loss, saint.\" He tosses the vial back. You fumble it.\nopt.left.say.1 [160723] = “你的损失，圣人。”他把小瓶扔回来。你手忙脚乱地接住。\n# en: Shake on it\nopt.right.label [982713] = 一言为定\n# en: It goes well until a vial turns up in a professor's tea. You and Corvin share a detention and an alibi.\nopt.right.say.1 [f97a4d] = 一切顺利，直到一只小瓶出现在某位教授的茶里。你和科尔文共享了一次禁闭和一份不在场证明。\n# en: Thirty crowns, no questions asked. Corvin winks. \"Pleasure doing crime with you.\"\nopt.right.say.2 [d740ff] = 三十克朗，不问来路。科尔文眨眨眼。“和你一起犯罪真愉快。”\n\n@event div_tea_leaves\n# en: Reading the Leaves\ntitle [0b5484] = 解读茶叶\n# en: You read your partner's tea leaves. The shape at the bottom of the cup is unmistakably a skull. Your partner is looking at you hopefully.\ntext [724f94] = 你为搭档解读茶叶。杯底的形状明明白白是一个骷髅。你的搭档满怀希望地看着你。\n# en: A delicate teacup tipped on its saucer, the dregs forming a shape like a skull — or possibly a mushroom.\nscene [006d77] = 一只精致的茶杯倾倒在茶碟上，杯底的残渣形如骷髅——也可能是蘑菇。\n# en: Tell the truth\nopt.left.label [d8bf00] = 说实话\n# en: \"A skull?\" They laugh. \"Professor says a skull means change.\" They're relieved. You aren't sure you should be.\nopt.left.say.1 [fd9f44] = “骷髅？”对方笑了，“教授说骷髅代表改变。”对方松了口气。你却不确定该不该松这口气。\n# en: They go pale and leave early. The next day they trip down the stairs. Nothing serious. Still.\nopt.left.say.2 [e9a649] = 对方脸色煞白，提前离开了。第二天对方从楼梯上摔了一跤。不严重。但还是。\n# en: Lie kindly\nopt.right.label [542b7a] = 善意地撒谎\n# en: \"A mushroom,\" you say. \"Means good fortune.\" They beam. The professor, passing behind you, says nothing at all.\nopt.right.say.1 [739452] = “是蘑菇，”你说，“代表好运。”对方笑开了花。从你身后走过的教授什么也没说。\n\n@event div_next_tuesday\n# en: The Mirror of Next Tuesday\ntitle [765386] = 下周二之镜\n# en: The Mirror of Next Tuesday shows exactly what it promises. Students take turns looking. Some come away smiling. One comes away and goes straight to the infirmary.\ntext [3591e5] = “下周二之镜”展示的正如其名。学生们轮流去看。有人笑着离开。有一个人看完直接去了医务室。\n# en: An ornate standing mirror whose reflection shows the room on a different day — the light at a different angle, a different person sitting in your chair.\nscene [abfe7b] = 一面华丽的落地镜，镜中映出的是另一天的这间屋子——光线角度不同，你的椅子上坐着别人。\n# en: Take a look\nopt.left.label [a3cf08] = 看一眼\n# en: Next Tuesday you are sitting an Astromancy quiz. You memorise the questions. Is that cheating? It's at least ambiguous.\nopt.left.say.1 [0a0d2f] = 下周二你在参加星象学小测。你记下了题目。这算作弊吗？至少说不清。\n# en: Next Tuesday you are crying in the rain outside the observatory. You don't know why. You spend the week dreading it.\nopt.left.say.2 [004b37] = 下周二你在天文台外的雨里哭泣。你不知道为什么。接下来一整周你都提心吊胆。\n# en: Decline\nopt.right.label [eaef39] = 不看了\n# en: \"I'd rather be surprised.\" The professor smiles as if you have passed a different test entirely.\nopt.right.say.1 [18dfd0] = “我宁愿到时候再惊喜。”教授笑了，好像你通过了另一场完全不同的考试。\n\n@event div_seraphine_vision\n# en: She Grabs Your Hand\ntitle [a002e6] = 她抓住了你的手\n# en: In the middle of crystal-gazing, Seraphine Hale seizes your wrist. Her eyes have gone silver. \"The door under the lake,\" she whispers. \"Don't let him open it. Or do. I can't see which.\"\ntext [52e443] = 正凝视水晶球时，瑟拉芬·海尔一把抓住你的手腕。她的眼睛变成了银色。“湖下的门，”她低语，“别让他打开它。或者让他打开。我看不清是哪个。”\n# en: A dreamy-eyed girl gripping a classmate's wrist across a table of crystal balls, her pupils wide and silver.\nscene [bda6a7] = 一个眼神迷离的女孩隔着摆满水晶球的桌子抓住同学的手腕，瞳孔放大，泛着银光。\n# en: Pull away\nopt.left.label [1414c0] = 抽回手\n# en: She blinks and lets go. \"Sorry. That happens.\" She goes back to her crystal as if nothing occurred.\nopt.left.say.1 [d082da] = 她眨了眨眼，松开了手。“抱歉。偶尔会这样。”她若无其事地继续看她的水晶球。\n# en: Ask who she means\nopt.right.label [301f4e] = 问她说的是谁\n# en: \"Someone cold,\" she says. \"Someone kind.\" Then she shakes her head, smiles vaguely, and asks if you'd like a biscuit.\nopt.right.say.1 [c004e1] = “一个冰冷的人，”她说，“一个温柔的人。”然后她摇摇头，迷迷糊糊地笑了笑，问你要不要来块饼干。\n\n@event art_clockwork_beetle\n# en: The Clockwork Beetle\ntitle [8b7d56] = 发条甲虫\n# en: Your project: a brass beetle that walks. The gears are in. The spring is wound. You could make it efficient, or you could give it a little personality.\ntext [982bd2] = 你的作业：一只会走路的黄铜甲虫。齿轮装好了。发条上紧了。你可以让它高效，也可以给它一点个性。\n# en: A brass beetle the size of a fist on a workbench, tiny gears visible through its translucent wing-cases.\nscene [3cbbcc] = 工作台上一只拳头大小的黄铜甲虫，透过半透明的鞘翅能看见细小的齿轮。\n# en: Make it efficient\nopt.left.label [b934bb] = 让它高效\n# en: It walks in a perfect straight line until it hits a wall. Full marks.\nopt.left.say.1 [2f4098] = 它沿着一条完美的直线前进，直到撞上墙。满分。\n# en: Give it personality\nopt.right.label [40ed9d] = 给它点个性\n# en: It escapes during the presentation and lives in the walls for a month, occasionally stealing biscuits. It becomes a minor legend.\nopt.right.say.1 [a6886f] = 它在展示时逃跑了，在墙里住了一个月，时不时偷块饼干。它成了一个小小的传说。\n# en: It refuses to walk unless you compliment it. The professor is torn between marking you down and asking how you did it.\nopt.right.say.2 [550353] = 除非你夸它，不然它就不肯走。教授在扣你分和问你怎么做到的之间左右为难。\n\n@event art_mir_collab\n# en: A Joint Project\ntitle [7a3675] = 合作项目\n# en: Mir Ten drops onto the bench beside you with a sketch of something that is either a self-stirring cauldron or a small war machine. \"Partners? Your hands, my brain. Or the other way round. Both, actually.\"\ntext [687dbc] = 米尔·滕一屁股坐到你旁边的长凳上，拿着一张草图，上面画的东西要么是自动搅拌坩埚，要么是一台小型战争机器。“搭档吗？你的手，我的脑子。或者反过来。其实两样都要。”\n# en: A cluttered workbench covered in tools, sketches and one smoking teapot; a grinning student with goggles pushed up into wild hair.\nscene [455820] = 堆满工具和草图的工作台上还有一只冒烟的茶壶；一个咧嘴笑的学生把护目镜推到了蓬乱的头发上。\n# en: Work alone\nopt.left.label [e54a87] = 自己干\n# en: \"Fair,\" says Mir, with no hard feelings, and is immediately on fire. Not badly.\nopt.left.say.1 [461fb1] = “公平。”米尔毫无芥蒂地说，随即就着了火。不算严重。\n# en: Partner with Mir\nopt.right.label [89e907] = 和米尔搭档\n# en: The cauldron stirs itself, hums a tune and only threatens the professor once. Mir hugs you with oily hands.\nopt.right.say.1 [51d7a9] = 坩埚自己搅拌，还哼着小曲，只威胁了教授一次。米尔用满是油污的手抱住了你。\n# en: The prototype explodes. Mir laughs so hard they cry. \"Again! We were so close!\" You find you're laughing too.\nopt.right.say.2 [48c576] = 原型机爆炸了。米尔笑得眼泪直流。“再来！我们差一点就成了！”你发现自己也在笑。\n\n@event art_enchanted_quill\n# en: The Self-Writing Quill\ntitle [0d291a] = 自动羽毛笔\n# en: You've enchanted a quill to take dictation. It works beautifully. A thought occurs: what if it wrote your essays, too?\ntext [8d3e40] = 你给一支羽毛笔施了魔法，让它听写。效果好极了。你冒出一个念头：要是让它连论文也写了呢？\n# en: A quill writing busily on its own over a sheaf of paper while its owner sleeps on folded arms.\nscene [789717] = 一支羽毛笔在一沓纸上自己奋笔疾书，它的主人趴在胳膊上睡着了。\n# en: Just use it for notes\nopt.left.label [1cd0cf] = 只用它记笔记\n# en: Your notes are flawless. Even the professor's jokes are transcribed, with a small marginal comment: \"(not funny).\"\nopt.left.say.1 [04dda1] = 你的笔记无懈可击。连教授的笑话都记下来了，旁边还有一行小小的批注：“（不好笑）”。\n# en: Let it write your essays\nopt.right.label [904901] = 让它替你写论文\n# en: Your essays arrive on time, in impeccable penmanship. A little too impeccable. Nobody notices. Yet.\nopt.right.say.1 [fd9908] = 你的论文按时交上，字迹工整无比。工整得有点过分了。没人发现。暂时。\n# en: The quill develops opinions and writes a scathing review of the professor. It signs your name.\nopt.right.say.2 [7b012f] = 羽毛笔有了自己的主见，写了一篇痛批教授的檄文。署的是你的名字。\n\n@event beast_griffon_chick\n# en: Hatching Day\ntitle [e94ec5] = 破壳日\n# en: The griffon egg is cracking. Professor Brask whispers that the first face a chick sees, it trusts forever — or attacks forever. Hard to predict.\ntext [713b1c] = 狮鹫蛋正在裂开。布拉斯克教授低声说，雏鸟看见的第一张脸，它会永远信任——或者永远攻击。很难预料。\n# en: A straw nest under a heat lamp, a cracked golden egg, and a damp fluffy griffon chick blinking at the world.\nscene [561fcb] = 保温灯下的草窝里，一枚裂开的金蛋，一只湿漉漉、毛茸茸的狮鹫雏鸟正眨着眼打量世界。\n# en: Hang back\nopt.left.label [7bd914] = 往后站\n# en: The chick sees Brask first and bites her. She seems delighted.\nopt.left.say.1 [72d465] = 雏鸟第一眼看见了布拉斯克，咬了她一口。她看起来很开心。\n# en: Offer your hand\nopt.right.label [1f34dd] = 伸出手\n# en: The chick peeps, nuzzles your palm and falls asleep. You are, apparently, its mother now.\nopt.right.say.1 [dd4810] = 雏鸟啾啾叫着，蹭了蹭你的掌心，睡着了。看来，你现在是它妈妈了。\n# en: It bites. Hard. Griffon beaks are sharper than they look, and they look extremely sharp.\nopt.right.say.2 [96c90d] = 它咬了你。狠狠地。狮鹫的喙比看上去锋利，而它看上去就极其锋利。\n\n@event beast_manticore\n# en: The Manticore Is Loose\ntitle [c92a9e] = 蝎尾狮跑出来了\n# en: Someone left the manticore pen open. It's in the greenhouse, tail twitching, between you and the only exit.\ntext [1d8358] = 有人没关蝎尾狮的围栏。它在温室里，尾巴抽动着，正挡在你和唯一的出口之间。\n# en: A shadowy lion-shape with a scorpion's tail prowling between the greenhouse benches, students pressed against the glass.\nscene [5076e9] = 一个带着蝎尾的狮形黑影在温室长凳间徘徊，学生们紧贴着玻璃墙。\n# en: Back away slowly\nopt.left.label [b7e862] = 慢慢后退\n# en: You edge out. Professor Brask arrives with a bucket of meat and a lullaby and the crisis is over in minutes.\nopt.left.say.1 [92deb4] = 你一点点挪了出去。布拉斯克教授带着一桶肉和一首摇篮曲赶来，几分钟就平息了危机。\n# en: Try to calm it\nopt.right.label [469057] = 试着安抚它\n# en: You hum the lullaby Brask taught you. The manticore yawns, sits, and lets you scratch behind its ear. The whole class saw.\nopt.right.say.1 [3bf240] = 你哼起布拉斯克教的摇篮曲。蝎尾狮打了个哈欠，坐下来，让你挠它的耳后。全班都看见了。\n# en: The manticore does not like your humming. The tail is quicker than you are.\nopt.right.say.2 [422c18] = 蝎尾狮不喜欢你的哼唱。它的尾巴比你快。\n\n@event beast_familiar_call\n# en: Calling a Familiar\ntitle [b91c9f] = 召唤魔宠\n# en: Brask teaches the summoning circle. \"It doesn't summon,\" she says. \"It invites. Most of the time, nothing comes. That's fine. Familiars choose you.\"\ntext [3a2457] = 布拉斯克教授教你们召唤法阵。“它并不召唤，”她说，“它只是邀请。大多数时候什么也不会来。没关系。魔宠会自己选择你。”\n# en: A circle of salt and candles in a forest clearing at dusk, shapes of small animals watching from the edge of the trees.\nscene [e641a7] = 黄昏时林间空地上一圈盐和蜡烛，林边有小动物的身影在注视。\n# en: Just watch the others\nopt.left.label [4c0d66] = 只看别人做\n# en: A first-year is chosen by a very large toad. The toad looks smug about it.\nopt.left.say.1 [4d8722] = 一个一年级生被一只非常大的蟾蜍选中了。蟾蜍看起来很得意。\n# en: Sit in the circle\nopt.right.label [69d42b] = 坐进法阵\n# en: Nothing comes. But for a long moment, something at the edge of the trees watches you with eyes like embers. Then it is gone.\nopt.right.say.1 [9c9f2a] = 什么也没来。但有很长一段时间，林边有什么东西用余烬般的眼睛注视着你。然后它消失了。\n\n@event astro_comet\n# en: A New Comet\ntitle [6445f6] = 新彗星\n# en: A comet appears that isn't in any of the charts. The observatory is in uproar. Everyone wants to be the one to name it.\ntext [b8052f] = 一颗不在任何星图上的彗星出现了。天文台炸开了锅。人人都想当给它命名的那个人。\n# en: An observatory dome open to the night, a long-tailed comet smeared across the sky, students crowded at the telescopes.\nscene [9a815d] = 向夜空敞开的天文台穹顶，一颗长尾彗星划过天际，学生们挤在望远镜前。\n# en: Sketch it carefully\nopt.left.label [5702fd] = 仔细画下它\n# en: Your drawing is lovely. Someone else's name goes on the comet. You keep the drawing.\nopt.left.say.1 [605f74] = 你画得很美。彗星用了别人的名字。你留下了那幅画。\n# en: Calculate its path\nopt.right.label [e158e4] = 计算它的轨道\n# en: Your math is right. The comet is named after you, or at least after a slight misspelling of you.\nopt.right.say.1 [1b11c9] = 你算对了。彗星以你的名字命名——至少是你名字的一个小小的错误拼写。\n# en: Your calculation suggests the comet will hit the academy on Thursday. It does not. You are teased about it for weeks.\nopt.right.say.2 [0e494e] = 你的计算显示彗星将在周四撞上学院。它没有。你被取笑了好几个星期。\n\n@event astro_kael_roof\n# en: Up All Night\ntitle [f8c2d5] = 彻夜未眠\n# en: On your way to the morning lecture you find Kael alone on the observatory roof. He has been there all night. Frost has spread across the tiles around him. He is staring at a patch of sky where there are no stars at all.\ntext [d9607d] = 去上早课的路上，你发现凯尔独自坐在天文台屋顶上。他在那儿待了一整夜。他周围的瓦片上结满了霜。他盯着天上一块一颗星星也没有的地方。\n# en: A young man in a fur coat sitting alone on the observatory roof, frost on the tiles around him, staring at one spot in the stars.\nscene [4d08d9] = 一个穿毛皮大衣的年轻人独自坐在天文台屋顶，身旁瓦片结霜，凝视着星空中的某一点。\n# en: Leave him be\nopt.left.label [ce4663] = 别打扰他\n# en: You go down to class. By evening there's a perfect circle of frost on the roof where he sat, and no Kael.\nopt.left.say.1 [8f95a8] = 你下楼去上课了。到了傍晚，屋顶上他坐过的地方留下了一个完美的霜圈，凯尔却不见了。\n# en: Sit beside him\nopt.right.label [746969] = 坐到他身边\n# en: \"There,\" he says, pointing at the dark. \"Can you see it? A star that eats light.\" You look for a long time. You think you can.\nopt.right.say.1 [28b9f2] = “那里，”他指着黑暗说，“你看见了吗？一颗吞噬光的星。”你看了很久。你觉得你看见了。\n\n@event astro_sleepless\n# en: Sleepless Weeks\ntitle [848700] = 无眠的几周\n# en: Midnight lectures, dawn homework, morning classes. You have started seeing constellations in your porridge.\ntext [5695ef] = 午夜讲座，黎明作业，上午上课。你已经开始在粥里看见星座了。\n# en: A student asleep on an open star atlas at a library desk, a guttering candle beside them, dawn light in the window.\nscene [e3c887] = 一个学生趴在图书馆书桌上摊开的星图集上睡着了，蜡烛将熄，窗外透进晨光。\n# en: Skip a few lectures\nopt.left.label [659dd6] = 翘几节讲座\n# en: You sleep for fourteen hours and wake up feeling like a person.\nopt.left.say.1 [c5fbc8] = 你睡了十四个小时，醒来时终于觉得自己像个人了。\n# en: Power through\nopt.right.label [8bebcb] = 硬撑过去\n# en: You learn the names of forty stars and forget the names of two friends. Temporarily.\nopt.right.say.1 [a83f16] = 你记住了四十颗星星的名字，忘了两个朋友的名字。暂时的。\n\n@event heal_first_patient\n# en: Your First Patient\ntitle [aad265] = 你的第一位病人\n# en: A first-year stumbles into the practice ward with a wrist that bends the wrong way. Sister Amaranth is busy. The first-year looks at you with enormous, trusting eyes.\ntext [2614f1] = 一个一年级生跌跌撞撞地走进实习病房，手腕弯向了错误的方向。阿玛兰斯修女正忙着。那个一年级生用一双大大的、充满信任的眼睛看着你。\n# en: A pale first-year sitting on an infirmary cot holding a wrist at a strange angle, a nervous student healer kneeling beside them.\nscene [0b1ccb] = 一个脸色苍白的一年级生坐在医务室病床上，托着一只角度怪异的手腕，一位紧张的学生治疗师跪在旁边。\n# en: Fetch Sister Amaranth\nopt.left.label [0ebdc9] = 去叫阿玛兰斯修女\n# en: Amaranth fixes it in seconds and nods at you. \"Knowing your limits is the first lesson.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [386b03] = 阿玛兰斯几秒钟就治好了，对你点点头。“知道自己的极限，是第一课。”\n# en: Heal it yourself\nopt.right.label [6b0949] = 自己来治\n# en: The bone slides back with a soft click and a glow of warm light. The first-year tells everyone you're a genius.\nopt.right.say.1 [b682dc] = 骨头轻轻咔哒一声归位，泛起一阵温暖的光。那个一年级生逢人便说你是天才。\n# en: The wrist is now straight. The fingers, however, are pointing backwards. Amaranth sighs and fixes both.\nopt.right.say.2 [2d4421] = 手腕是直了。可手指却朝后指着。阿玛兰斯叹了口气，两样都修好了。\n\n@event heal_fever_ward\n# en: Fever in the Village\ntitle [3e73df] = 小镇里的热病\n# en: Lake Fever is spreading through the village below the academy. Sister Amaranth asks for student volunteers. It's hard, unpaid, and you might catch it.\ntext [ea106a] = 湖热病正在学院山下的小镇蔓延。阿玛兰斯修女在征集学生志愿者。辛苦、没报酬，你还可能被传染。\n# en: A crowded village hall lined with cots, lanterns hanging from the rafters, student healers moving between patients.\nscene [945032] = 一间摆满病床的拥挤镇公所，椽子上挂着灯笼，学生治疗师们在病人之间穿梭。\n# en: Decline\nopt.left.label [eaef39] = 拒绝\n# en: You watch the volunteers board the barge in the morning. You do your homework. It feels like the wrong homework.\nopt.left.say.1 [70c215] = 早上，你看着志愿者们登上驳船。你做着你的作业。感觉像是在做错的作业。\n# en: Volunteer\nopt.right.label [39a371] = 报名志愿\n# en: You save lives, and catch the fever yourself. The village sends a basket of honey cakes to your sickbed.\nopt.right.say.1 [d1e957] = 你救了人，自己也染上了热病。镇上送了一篮蜂蜜蛋糕到你的病床边。\n# en: A week of long nights and cool cloths. When you leave, the baker's daughter gives you her best loaf.\nopt.right.say.2 [61b0a3] = 一个星期的漫漫长夜和凉毛巾。你离开时，面包师的女儿送了你她烤得最好的一条面包。\n\n@event heal_tobias_sting\n# en: Stung\ntitle [1eecd2] = 被蛰了\n# en: Tobias wanders into the practice ward holding up a hand swollen like a pumpkin. \"Mandrake,\" he says. \"She didn't like my singing.\" He looks hopefully at you.\ntext [91e6f0] = 托比亚斯晃进实习病房，举着一只肿得像南瓜的手。“曼德拉草，”他说，“她不喜欢我唱歌。”他满怀希望地看着你。\n# en: A broad-shouldered boy sitting on a greenhouse bench, grimacing, holding up a hand swollen to twice its size.\nscene [37ac94] = 一个宽肩膀的男生坐在温室长凳上，龇牙咧嘴，举着一只肿成两倍大的手。\n# en: Send him to the infirmary\nopt.left.label [d6e106] = 让他去医务室\n# en: \"Right. Proper healer. Good idea.\" He goes, a little disappointed.\nopt.left.say.1 [d77544] = “对。找正经治疗师。好主意。”他走了，有点失望。\n# en: Treat him yourself\nopt.right.label [2aa3de] = 亲自为他治疗\n# en: Your hands are steady. The swelling goes down. Tobias stares at his hand, then at you. \"You're really good at this,\" he says softly.\nopt.right.say.1 [eb2831] = 你的手很稳。肿消了下去。托比亚斯盯着自己的手，又盯着你。“你真的很擅长这个。”他轻声说。\n\n@event umbral_whisper\n# en: The Shadow Speaks\ntitle [041067] = 影子开口了\n# en: In the middle of a lecture on the Nature of Absence, your shadow leans away from the lamp, toward your ear, and says your name.\ntext [a8a6b1] = 在一堂关于“缺席之本质”的讲座中途，你的影子背离了灯光，凑到你耳边，说出了你的名字。\n# en: A student at a desk in a dim lecture hall, their shadow on the wall behind them turned the wrong way, leaning close to whisper.\nscene [51c4bd] = 昏暗讲堂里的一个学生，身后墙上的影子朝向不对，正凑近低语。\n# en: Banish it\nopt.left.label [78332f] = 驱散它\n# en: You speak the warding phrase. Your shadow snaps back into place, sulking. It does not speak again. You think.\nopt.left.say.1 [9451ed] = 你念出防护咒语。影子啪地弹回原位，生着闷气。它没有再说话。你是这么想的。\n# en: Answer it\nopt.right.label [b623c8] = 回应它\n# en: It tells you something about death you can't unlearn. The cold starts the next morning.\nopt.right.say.1 [f3763b] = 它告诉了你一件关于死亡的事，你再也无法忘记。第二天早上，寒意开始了。\n# en: It tells you where a lost book is shelved in the library. It is right. You decide not to wonder how it knew.\nopt.right.say.2 [55a529] = 它告诉你图书馆里一本遗失的书放在哪里。它说对了。你决定不去想它是怎么知道的。\n\n@event umbral_bone_mouse\n# en: The Bone Mouse\ntitle [ae9175] = 骨鼠\n# en: Practical exercise: animate the skeleton of a field mouse. \"Purely educational,\" the professor says, with a smile nobody trusts.\ntext [c20b3b] = 实践练习：让一具田鼠骨架活动起来。“纯属教学目的。”教授说，露出一个没人信得过的微笑。\n# en: A tiny mouse skeleton on a silver tray, one bony paw twitching, students leaning away from it.\nscene [e2a825] = 银托盘上一具小小的老鼠骨架，一只骨爪在抽动，学生们纷纷向后躲。\n# en: Refuse on principle\nopt.left.label [d08672] = 出于原则拒绝\n# en: You write a short essay on why instead. The professor reads it with surprising attention.\nopt.left.say.1 [ceeff8] = 你改写了一篇短文说明理由。教授读得出奇地认真。\n# en: Do the exercise\nopt.right.label [eb5633] = 完成练习\n# en: The little skeleton stands up, washes its non-existent whiskers, and lies down again. Perfect control.\nopt.right.say.1 [6bb00e] = 小骨架站起来，洗了洗并不存在的胡须，又躺了回去。控制得完美无缺。\n# en: It gets up. It does not lie down again. It follows you back to your dormitory and sits on your pillow every night.\nopt.right.say.2 [6046b9] = 它站了起来。然后再也没躺回去。它跟着你回了宿舍，每晚都坐在你的枕头上。\n\n@event umbral_forbidden_notes\n# en: Forbidden Notes\ntitle [b392f3] = 禁忌笔记\n# en: A fourth-year slides a battered notebook across the table. \"My notes on the Restricted Section. I'm graduating. You look like you'd use them.\" A silver key is tucked in the spine.\ntext [9f2387] = 一个四年级生把一本破旧的笔记本推过桌面。“我关于禁书区的笔记。我要毕业了。你看起来用得上。”书脊里夹着一把银钥匙。\n# en: A battered notebook with a cracked leather cover being slid across a library table, a silver key tucked into its spine.\nscene [9bca00] = 一本皮面开裂的破旧笔记本被推过图书馆的桌面，书脊里夹着一把银钥匙。\n# en: Decline\nopt.left.label [eaef39] = 拒绝\n# en: The fourth-year shrugs and slides the notebook to the next student. You watch it go.\nopt.left.say.1 [d26ee6] = 四年级生耸耸肩，把笔记本推给了下一个学生。你看着它被推走。\n# en: Take the notebook\nopt.right.label [beda2e] = 收下笔记本\n# en: The notes are brilliant, half-mad and annotated in three colours of ink. The key is cold in your pocket.\nopt.right.say.1 [7a1f6e] = 笔记精彩绝伦、半疯半癫，用三种颜色的墨水做了批注。那把钥匙在你口袋里冰凉。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/clubs.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/clubs.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event duel_practice_bout\n# en: Practice Bout\ntitle [39d05a] = 练习对决\n# en: Wednesday practice. The captain pairs you with a fourth-year who has a reputation for ending bouts quickly. \"Defence or attack,\" the captain says. \"Pick one and commit.\"\ntext [4d124e] = 周三的练习。社长把你和一个以速战速决出名的四年级生分在一组。“防守还是进攻，”社长说，“选一个，然后坚持到底。”\n# en: A chalk circle on a stone floor under high windows, two students mid-duel, a ring of members cheering.\nscene [d4da5e] = 高窗下石地板上的粉笔圈，两名学生正在对决，一圈社员在欢呼。\n# en: Hold your ground\nopt.left.label [52ce0a] = 坚守阵地\n# en: You weather everything she throws until she runs out of breath. The captain nods slowly.\nopt.left.say.1 [209cbc] = 你扛住了她的每一招，直到她气喘吁吁。社长缓缓点头。\n# en: You hold your ground very bravely, right up until you're on the floor.\nopt.left.say.2 [774fd6] = 你非常勇敢地坚守着阵地，一直坚守到躺在地上为止。\n# en: Go on the attack\nopt.right.label [0fc83d] = 主动进攻\n# en: Your opening hex catches her off guard. Point to you. The circle whoops.\nopt.right.say.1 [25eae8] = 你的开局恶咒打了她个措手不及。你得一分。社员们一片欢呼。\n# en: She sidesteps and you hex the wall. The wall wins.\nopt.right.say.2 [1d6bcd] = 她一个侧身，你的恶咒打在了墙上。墙赢了。\n\n@event duel_bram_hazing\n# en: Initiation, Prefect-Style\ntitle [d6f56a] = 级长式入社仪式\n# en: Bram Hollis, prefect and the Circle's loudest member, has decided new members need \"toughening up\". He's made you hold a shield-charm against him for an hour. The hour is not up.\ntext [1ad653] = 布拉姆·霍利斯——级长，也是决斗社里嗓门最大的一个——认定新人需要“磨练磨练”。他让你顶着他的攻击维持护盾咒一小时。一小时还没到。\n# en: A burly student in a prefect's sash blocking a corridor, wand twirling, a crowd of members watching nervously.\nscene [c5bf3b] = 一个身披级长绶带的魁梧学生堵在走廊里转着魔杖，一群社员紧张地围观。\n# en: Endure it\nopt.left.label [9e8205] = 忍下去\n# en: Your arms shake. You don't drop the charm. Bram grunts, almost approvingly, and finds someone else to torment.\nopt.left.say.1 [cda0fd] = 你的手臂在发抖。你没有放下护盾。布拉姆哼了一声，几乎带着赞许，转身去折磨别人了。\n# en: Drop the shield and duel him\nopt.right.label [df3e2f] = 撤掉护盾，和他决斗\n# en: You disarm him in front of everyone. His wand clatters across the floor. The silence is delicious. He will remember this.\nopt.right.say.1 [df1203] = 你当众缴了他的械。他的魔杖哐啷滚过地板。那份寂静美妙极了。他会记住这一刻的。\n# en: Bram flattens you with a single bored flick. \"Toughening up,\" he reminds the room.\nopt.right.say.2 [675677] = 布拉姆百无聊赖地一挥杖就把你拍扁了。“磨练磨练。”他提醒全场。\n\n@event duel_lyra_training\n# en: Training Partners\ntitle [6e568e] = 训练搭档\n# en: Lyra finds you after practice. \"The tournament is in two months. You're the only person in this club who doesn't bore me. Train with me. Dawn. Every day. Don't be late.\"\ntext [7cf72d] = 练习结束后莱拉找到你。“锦标赛还有两个月。你是这个社里唯一不让我觉得无聊的人。跟我一起练。黎明。每天。别迟到。”\n# en: A misty lawn at dawn, two figures practising wand forms side by side, frost on the grass.\nscene [bd3dcc] = 黎明时雾气笼罩的草坪，两个身影并肩练习杖法，草上结着霜。\n# en: Decline — you need sleep\nopt.left.label [e4f9c1] = 拒绝——你需要睡觉\n# en: \"Sleep,\" she repeats, as if it's a foreign word. \"Fine.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [ceaa69] = “睡觉。”她重复了一遍，好像那是个外语词。“随你。”\n# en: Agree\nopt.right.label [362be3] = 答应\n# en: She's a merciless coach. Somewhere around the third week, you notice she's started bringing two cups of tea.\nopt.right.say.1 [28ecad] = 她是个毫不留情的教练。大约第三周，你发现她开始带两杯茶来了。\n\n@event choir_solo\n# en: Solo Auditions\ntitle [c9c57e] = 独唱选拔\n# en: Auditions for the Midwinter solo. The choirmaster's eyebrows are famously hard to impress. Your throat is dry. Your hands are wet.\ntext [d9b167] = 仲冬独唱的选拔。合唱指挥的眉毛出了名的难以打动。你的喉咙发干。你的手心冒汗。\n# en: A circular choir loft under a painted dome, a single student standing in a pool of light, the rest of the choir floating gently above their seats.\nscene [8e9373] = 彩绘穹顶下的圆形唱诗台，一名学生独自站在一束光里，其余团员轻轻悬浮在座位上方。\n# en: Stay in the back row\nopt.left.label [1db21b] = 待在后排\n# en: You harmonise from safety, and it's lovely. Somebody else gets the solo and cries with relief.\nopt.left.say.1 [7b529b] = 你在安全的位置和声，很动听。别人拿到了独唱，如释重负地哭了。\n# en: Audition\nopt.right.label [41aa8e] = 参加选拔\n# en: You hit the high note and the candles in the loft all flare at once. The choirmaster's eyebrows rise one millimetre. You got it.\nopt.right.say.1 [93eca5] = 你唱上了高音，唱诗台上所有的蜡烛同时一亮。指挥的眉毛抬高了一毫米。你选上了。\n# en: Your voice cracks on the high note and a candle goes out in protest.\nopt.right.say.2 [dae132] = 你的高音破了，一支蜡烛抗议般地熄灭了。\n\n@event choir_levitation\n# en: Off-Key Altitude\ntitle [8a0afa] = 跑调的海拔\n# en: The Choir of the Spheres literally floats when it harmonises. You keep falling out of the air on the third verse. People are starting to notice the thumps.\ntext [8ceebd] = 天球合唱团唱和声时真的会飘起来。可你每唱到第三段就会从空中掉下来。大家开始注意到那一声声“咚”了。\n# en: A choir hovering a few feet off the floor, all in harmony — except one singer, who has dropped back to the ground with a thump.\nscene [282038] = 一支悬浮在离地几尺高处齐声合唱的合唱团——除了一名团员，他“咚”地掉回了地上。\n# en: Mime the hard bits\nopt.left.label [583519] = 难的地方假唱\n# en: Nobody notices. You float serenely, mouthing words. It's a little bit like fraud.\nopt.left.say.1 [018b6b] = 没人发现。你安详地飘着，只动嘴不出声。有点像诈骗。\n# en: Practise every night\nopt.right.label [c7a2c5] = 每晚加练\n# en: A week of late nights and you float through the third verse like a feather. The choirmaster gives you a tiny nod.\nopt.right.say.1 [56e1a4] = 熬了一周夜，你像羽毛一样飘过了第三段。指挥朝你极轻地点了点头。\n\n@event choir_concert\n# en: The Winter Concert\ntitle [428813] = 冬季音乐会\n# en: The winter concert. The great hall is packed. Somewhere in the audience, the Ledger's critic is sharpening her quill.\ntext [ba472a] = 冬季音乐会。大礼堂座无虚席。观众席某处，《纪事报》的乐评人正在削她的羽毛笔。\n# en: The great hall at night, a floating choir lit by candles, the audience below with faces tilted up.\nscene [229006] = 夜晚的大礼堂，一支烛光映照的悬浮合唱团，下方的观众仰着脸。\n# en: Sing for the audience\nopt.left.label [44bf70] = 为观众而唱\n# en: You sing and the hall sways. The review calls the choir \"unearthly\". You cut it out and keep it.\nopt.left.say.1 [0a05a2] = 你一开口，整个礼堂都随之摇曳。乐评称合唱团“不似人间”。你把它剪下来收好了。\n# en: Sing for yourself\nopt.right.label [bcd2d2] = 为自己而唱\n# en: You close your eyes and forget anyone is listening. When you open them, you are higher than everyone else.\nopt.right.say.1 [e5902a] = 你闭上眼，忘了有人在听。再睁开时，你飘得比所有人都高。\n\n@event sky_tryouts\n# en: Tryouts\ntitle [066773] = 选拔赛\n# en: Skyrace tryouts: three laps around the towers, through the hoops, without dying. The captain has a stopwatch and no visible sense of humour.\ntext [ad510d] = 天空竞速队选拔：绕塔三圈，穿过所有光环，别摔死。队长手里拿着秒表，脸上看不出任何幽默感。\n# en: Brooms streaking around the towers, hoops of light hanging in the sky, a captain with a whistle on the battlements.\nscene [465a53] = 飞帚绕着高塔疾驰，光环悬在空中，城垛上站着一个吹哨子的队长。\n# en: Fly clean and steady\nopt.left.label [596f30] = 飞得干净稳当\n# en: You're not the fastest, but you hit every hoop. \"Reserve,\" says the captain. Then: \"...no. Team.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [a95632] = 你不是最快的，但每个光环都穿过了。“替补。”队长说。随即又改口：“……不。正式队员。”\n# en: You miss two hoops and nearly a gargoyle. \"Reserves,\" says the captain. \"Keep practising.\"\nopt.left.say.2 [35b3d5] = 你漏了两个光环，还差点撞上一个石像鬼。“替补。”队长说，“继续练。”\n# en: Go full speed\nopt.right.label [58ac43] = 全速冲刺\n# en: You scream through the hoops so fast the stopwatch has to be checked. You're on the team. Obviously.\nopt.right.say.1 [c5ef95] = 你尖叫着冲过光环，快到队长得核对一下秒表。你入选了。毫无悬念。\n# en: You go full speed into the north tower. The tower is fine.\nopt.right.say.2 [c3625d] = 你全速冲进了北塔。塔没事。\n\n@event sky_crash\n# en: A Bad Landing\ntitle [cc3b0c] = 糟糕的着陆\n# en: Practice. A gust catches you mid-turn, and the ground arrives rather sooner than expected. Your ankle is making an interesting shape.\ntext [ff2751] = 训练中。一阵风在你转弯时把你掀翻，地面来得比预想的要早一些。你的脚踝正呈现出一种有趣的形状。\n# en: A broom snapped in two on the grass, a student sitting up dazed, teammates landing around them.\nscene [6a9e37] = 草地上一把折成两截的飞帚，一个学生晕乎乎地坐起来，队友们在周围降落。\n# en: Walk it off\nopt.left.label [0e45b4] = 走两步就好了\n# en: You do not walk it off. You limp it off, for a month.\nopt.left.say.1 [4fdb76] = 并没有好。你一瘸一拐了一个月。\n# en: Tough as old boots. The team cheers when you get back on your broom.\nopt.left.say.2 [f9f523] = 皮实得很。你重新骑上飞帚时，全队都在欢呼。\n# en: Straight to the infirmary\nopt.right.label [510ab8] = 直奔医务室\n# en: Sister Amaranth sets it in a minute and charges you three crowns for \"carelessness\". Fair.\nopt.right.say.1 [7345b6] = 阿玛兰斯修女一分钟就把它接好了，还收了你三克朗的“粗心费”。公道。\n\n@event sky_championship\n# en: Lumenhold vs. Thornwick\ntitle [da4be7] = 流明堡对荆木学院\n# en: The championship against Thornwick Academy. Final lap. You're neck and neck with their captain, and the golden hoop is at the top of the tallest tower.\ntext [bd74b2] = 与荆木学院的冠军赛。最后一圈。你和对方队长并驾齐驱，金色光环就在最高塔的塔顶。\n# en: Two teams of broom-riders in blue and green above a packed stadium, the golden finishing hoop glowing at the top of the highest tower.\nscene [d82217] = 座无虚席的赛场上空，蓝绿两队飞帚手交错，金色终点光环在最高塔顶闪耀。\n# en: Play it safe\nopt.left.label [882711] = 稳妥起见\n# en: Thornwick takes the hoop by a whisker. Still: second place in the region. The team carries you off the field anyway.\nopt.left.say.1 [68b920] = 荆木以毫厘之差夺得光环。但不管怎样：地区亚军。全队还是把你扛出了场。\n# en: The suicide dive\nopt.right.label [494b9a] = 玩命俯冲\n# en: You dive straight down the tower face and pull up at the last second, through the hoop. The stadium explodes.\nopt.right.say.1 [742576] = 你沿着塔壁垂直俯冲，在最后一秒拉起，穿过光环。全场沸腾了。\n# en: You dive. You miss. The crowd gasps, then cheers you anyway when you stagger up waving.\nopt.right.say.2 [c58127] = 你俯冲了。你没穿中。观众倒吸一口凉气，等你踉跄着爬起来挥手时，又为你欢呼起来。\n\n@event ledger_pie_scoop\n# en: The Case of the Missing Pies\ntitle [269d69] = 馅饼失踪案\n# en: Nessa slams a file on your desk. \"Pies. Forty-two of them, gone from the kitchens in a single night. I want the culprit, I want a photo and I want it by Friday.\"\ntext [bcd7f3] = 内莎把一份卷宗拍在你桌上。“馅饼。四十二个，一夜之间从厨房消失。我要找出犯人，要照片，周五之前要。”\n# en: A cramped newspaper office in a tower attic, a printing press clanking, a girl with an ink-stained grin pointing at a map pinned with string.\nscene [6fc23f] = 塔楼阁楼里拥挤的报社，印刷机哐当作响，一个满脸墨迹、咧嘴笑的女孩指着一张钉满细绳的地图。\n# en: Write a light-hearted piece\nopt.left.label [ffe459] = 写篇轻松的小文\n# en: \"Pie-rates Strike Again.\" Nessa groans at the headline and prints it anyway.\nopt.left.say.1 [0a7002] = 《馅饼大盗再度出手》。内莎对着标题哀叹了一声，还是印了。\n# en: Actually investigate\nopt.right.label [42eb33] = 认真调查\n# en: The culprit: a family of brownies living in the chimney. Your story runs on the front page. Nessa frames it.\nopt.right.say.1 [5dd8ed] = 犯人：住在烟囱里的一窝棕仙。你的报道登上了头版。内莎把它裱了起来。\n# en: You stake out the kitchens for three nights and catch nothing but a cold. Nessa pats your head.\nopt.right.say.2 [6e3684] = 你在厨房蹲守了三晚，除了感冒什么也没逮到。内莎拍了拍你的头。\n\n@event ledger_bram_story\n# en: The Prefect's Bribes\ntitle [609034] = 级长的贿赂\n# en: An anonymous tip: Bram Hollis has been taking bribes from first-years to skip their demerits. You have a witness. Nessa wants your byline on it. \"This is real journalism,\" she says, eyes shining.\ntext [78f450] = 一条匿名线报：布拉姆·霍利斯一直在收一年级生的贿赂，帮他们免掉记过。你有目击证人。内莎想让这篇报道署你的名字。“这才是真正的新闻，”她说，眼睛闪闪发亮。\n# en: An anonymous note spread on a desk under a lamp, next to a sketch of a prefect taking a coin purse from a first-year.\nscene [b9aaf0] = 台灯下摊开的一张匿名字条，旁边是一幅速写：一名级长正从一年级生手里接过钱袋。\n# en: Kill the story\nopt.left.label [50cd1f] = 压下这篇报道\n# en: Nessa is disappointed in you. Somehow, Bram finds out you buried it. He nods at you in the corridor. You feel slightly dirty.\nopt.left.say.1 [307410] = 内莎对你很失望。不知怎么的，布拉姆知道是你压下了它。他在走廊里朝你点点头。你觉得自己有点脏。\n# en: Publish it\nopt.right.label [d859b5] = 发表它\n# en: The story runs. Bram is stripped of his sash for a month. He gets it back. And now he knows exactly who you are.\nopt.right.say.1 [017011] = 报道刊出了。布拉姆被摘掉绶带一个月。后来他又拿回去了。而现在，他非常清楚你是谁。\n\n@event ledger_deadline\n# en: Deadline Night\ntitle [d06cb7] = 截稿之夜\n# en: The press runs at dawn and half the paper is blank. Nessa is chewing her quill. \"I need you tonight. All night. I'll owe you.\"\ntext [75d368] = 印刷机天亮开印，报纸还空着一半。内莎咬着羽毛笔。“今晚我需要你。整晚。我欠你一个人情。”\n# en: The newspaper office at 3am, crumpled drafts on the floor, two students slumped over typesetting trays.\nscene [4bbe74] = 凌晨三点的报社，地上满是揉皱的草稿，两个学生趴在排字盘上。\n# en: Say you have revision to do\nopt.left.label [12b3be] = 说你还要复习\n# en: You sleep. The paper comes out with a large drawing of a duck on page four. Nessa doesn't speak to you for a day.\nopt.left.say.1 [f578ae] = 你去睡了。报纸第四版上印了一幅巨大的鸭子画。内莎一整天没理你。\n# en: Pull an all-nighter\nopt.right.label [d42cd2] = 熬个通宵\n# en: You write three articles and a horoscope. At dawn Nessa falls asleep on your shoulder, ink on her nose.\nopt.right.say.1 [99e236] = 你写了三篇文章和一份星座运势。天亮时，内莎靠在你肩上睡着了，鼻尖上沾着墨水。\n\n@event garden_mandrakes\n# en: The Mandrake Choir\ntitle [a16982] = 曼德拉草合唱团\n# en: The greenhouse mandrakes have started singing again. Off-key and enthusiastic. The earmuffs are on the hook by the door. Tobias isn't wearing his; he's harmonising.\ntext [df724d] = 温室里的曼德拉草又开始唱歌了。跑调，但热情洋溢。耳罩挂在门边的钩子上。托比亚斯没戴他的；他在跟着和声。\n# en: Rows of potted mandrakes in a steamy greenhouse, their little leafy heads opened in song.\nscene [65b35a] = 雾气蒸腾的温室里一排排盆栽曼德拉草，它们长着叶子的小脑袋张嘴高歌。\n# en: Earmuffs on\nopt.left.label [1586df] = 戴上耳罩\n# en: You repot six mandrakes in blissful silence. Good honest work.\nopt.left.say.1 [652505] = 你在幸福的寂静中给六株曼德拉草换了盆。踏踏实实的好活儿。\n# en: Sing along\nopt.right.label [91a84f] = 跟着唱\n# en: You sing along and promptly faint. Mandrake song is strong stuff. You wake up very relaxed.\nopt.right.say.1 [5d48fe] = 你跟着唱，然后立刻晕了过去。曼德拉草的歌声劲儿很大。你醒来时非常放松。\n# en: The mandrakes adore you. The greenhouse sounds like a pub at closing time.\nopt.right.say.2 [acd59a] = 曼德拉草们爱死你了。温室里听起来像打烊时的酒馆。\n\n@event garden_moonflower\n# en: The Moonflower\ntitle [924066] = 月光花\n# en: \"The moonflower's going to bloom tonight,\" Tobias says, a little breathless. \"Once every eleven years. I'm staying up to see it. You... could stay too. If you want.\"\ntext [1d0b11] = “今晚月光花要开了，”托比亚斯有点喘不过气地说，“十一年才开一次。我要熬夜看。你……也可以留下。如果你想的话。”\n# en: A single silver flower opening in the dark of a glass greenhouse, two faces lit by its glow.\nscene [ada882] = 漆黑的玻璃温室里一朵银色的花正在绽放，两张脸被它的光芒照亮。\n# en: You need sleep\nopt.left.label [145c0b] = 你得睡觉了\n# en: \"Course. Yeah.\" He tells you about it at breakfast, in detail, with gestures.\nopt.left.say.1 [a1e34d] = “当然。嗯。”早饭时他给你讲了一遍，事无巨细，手舞足蹈。\n# en: Stay up with him\nopt.right.label [4a8ffd] = 陪他熬夜\n# en: At 2am the flower opens and fills the greenhouse with silver light. Tobias doesn't look at the flower. He looks at you.\nopt.right.say.1 [7c8d48] = 凌晨两点，花开了，银光洒满温室。托比亚斯没有看花。他在看你。\n\n@event garden_maud_secret\n# en: The Secret Garden\ntitle [2193f1] = 秘密花园\n# en: The old groundskeeper beckons you behind the potting shed, to a door in the wall you've walked past a hundred times without seeing. \"You've got the hands for it,\" Old Maud says. \"Want to see what grows where nobody's looking?\"\ntext [e9e7ee] = 老园丁招手让你到盆栽棚后面，那里墙上有一扇门，你路过了上百次都没看见。“你有这双手，”老莫德说，“想看看在没人注意的地方长着什么吗？”\n# en: An old woman in a battered straw hat holding open a hidden door in an ivy-covered wall; beyond, impossible flowers glow.\nscene [044583] = 一位戴破草帽的老妇人推开爬满常春藤的墙上一扇隐秘的门；门后，不可思议的花朵闪着光。\n# en: Thank her and decline\nopt.left.label [4d277b] = 谢过她，婉拒了\n# en: \"Suit yourself.\" The door is gone when you look back. You make your class with a minute to spare, oddly pleased with yourself.\nopt.left.say.1 [68610a] = “随你。”你回头时，那扇门已经不见了。你赶在上课前一分钟进了教室，莫名地有点得意。\n# en: Follow her in\nopt.right.label [499306] = 跟她进去\n# en: Inside: glowing ferns, silver bees, a pear tree that hums. Maud tucks a sprig of rosemary into your pocket. \"For luck. Don't lose it.\" You lose the whole afternoon in there, and a class with it.\nopt.right.say.1 [9b2aee] = 里面有发光的蕨类、银色的蜜蜂，还有一棵会哼歌的梨树。莫德把一枝迷迭香塞进你口袋。“保佑你的。别弄丢了。”你在里面待掉了整个下午，还错过了一节课。\n\n@event stars_cocoa_night\n# en: Cocoa on the Roof\ntitle [a1a0e0] = 屋顶上的热可可\n# en: Stargazers meet on the roof with blankets, telescopes and an illegal quantity of cocoa. Tonight is cloudy. Nobody cares.\ntext [9a36b4] = 观星社在屋顶聚会，带着毯子、望远镜和数量不太合法的热可可。今晚多云。没人在乎。\n# en: Students wrapped in blankets on a flat roof, steaming mugs in hand, telescopes pointed at a velvet sky.\nscene [c74695] = 学生们裹着毯子坐在平坦的屋顶上，手捧冒热气的杯子，望远镜对着天鹅绒般的夜空。\n# en: Talk until dawn\nopt.left.label [2f64c7] = 聊到天亮\n# en: You talk about home, about the future, about whether stars are lonely. Someone falls asleep on your shoulder.\nopt.left.say.1 [6965e5] = 你们聊家乡，聊未来，聊星星会不会孤单。有人靠在你肩上睡着了。\n# en: Do your homework by starlight\nopt.right.label [5cf04c] = 借着星光写作业\n# en: You finish your essay by the light of the cocoa-warmer charm. Productive. A little lonely.\nopt.right.say.1 [6dcd21] = 你借着暖可可咒的光写完了论文。很高效。有点孤单。\n\n@event stars_meteor_bet\n# en: The Meteor Wager\ntitle [262c9e] = 流星赌局\n# en: The club is running a betting pool on tonight's meteor count. The current favourite is 40. The charts suggest something else. Or do they?\ntext [25d6ed] = 社团在为今晚的流星数量开赌盘。目前热门是 40 颗。星图暗示的数字却不一样。真的吗？\n# en: A chalkboard on an observatory wall covered in tally marks and names, a pile of coins on a stool beneath it.\nscene [e0c74a] = 天文台墙上的黑板写满计数和名字，下面凳子上堆着一堆硬币。\n# en: Bet on the charts\nopt.left.label [b8316b] = 押星图的数字\n# en: Fifty-three meteors. You called fifty-two. Close enough to win the pot.\nopt.left.say.1 [0a5d53] = 五十三颗流星。你押的是五十二。足够接近，赢下了奖池。\n# en: The charts were wrong, or you read them wrong. Eight crowns gone.\nopt.left.say.2 [bd7c8a] = 星图错了，或者是你看错了。八克朗没了。\n# en: Stay out of it\nopt.right.label [03d24a] = 不参与\n# en: You watch the meteors for their own sake. Turns out that's the best seat in the house.\nopt.right.say.1 [8c78f1] = 你只是单纯地看流星。原来这才是最好的位置。\n\n@event stars_constellation\n# en: An Unnamed Shape\ntitle [42283b] = 无名之形\n# en: Plotting the autumn sky, you notice seven faint stars that form a ring around nothing. The charts call that patch of sky \"empty\". It doesn't look empty.\ntext [541d68] = 绘制秋季星空时，你注意到七颗暗淡的星星围成一个环，环中空无一物。星图把那片天空标为“空白”。可它看起来并不空。\n# en: A star chart spread on a table, a student's finger tracing a new pattern between unmarked stars.\nscene [cb6cc5] = 桌上摊开的星图，一名学生的手指在未标注的星星间描出新的图案。\n# en: Ignore it\nopt.left.label [e2ae0c] = 不去管它\n# en: You plot the usual constellations. Your chart gets top marks.\nopt.left.say.1 [9e48fd] = 你画了常规的星座。你的星图得了最高分。\n# en: Investigate\nopt.right.label [c3e054] = 调查一下\n# en: You spend a week in the archives. Three hundred years ago, the patch was called the Hollow. Then someone scraped the name off every chart.\nopt.right.say.1 [81c647] = 你在档案馆泡了一周。三百年前，那片天空叫作“空洞”。后来有人把这个名字从每一张星图上刮掉了。\n\n@event carto_invite\n# en: An Invitation at Midnight\ntitle [324f7e] = 午夜邀请\n# en: A black card under your door. On it, a map in silver ink that redraws itself while you watch. Corvin's handwriting: \"Midnight. Under the third gargoyle. Bring a candle. Tell no one.\"\ntext [afdfc2] = 门缝下塞进一张黑色卡片。上面是一张银墨地图，在你眼前不断重绘。科尔文的笔迹：“午夜。第三尊石像鬼下面。带上蜡烛。别告诉任何人。”\n# en: A slip of black paper pushed under a dormitory door, a silver ink map on it that rearranges itself as you watch.\nscene [f40da5] = 一张从宿舍门缝塞进来的黑纸条，上面的银墨地图在你注视下自行重组。\n# en: Ignore it\nopt.left.label [e2ae0c] = 不理它\n# en: The card blanks itself the next morning. Corvin doesn't mention it, but he's a little cooler for a week.\nopt.left.say.1 [e32163] = 第二天早上卡片变成了空白。科尔文没提这事，但接下来一周对你冷淡了些。\n# en: Go\nopt.right.label [d0c56b] = 去\n# en: Under the gargoyle: six students, a stack of maps and a passage you'd never seen. \"Welcome,\" says Corvin, \"to the club that doesn't exist.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [de031b] = 石像鬼下面：六个学生、一摞地图，还有一条你从未见过的通道。“欢迎，”科尔文说，“来到这个不存在的社团。”\n\n@event carto_thursday_stairs\n# en: The Thursday Staircase\ntitle [571623] = 周四楼梯\n# en: The Cartographers have heard of a staircase that only exists on Thursdays between midnight and one. Tonight is Thursday. It is 11:58.\ntext [95afb9] = 制图社听说有一道楼梯，只在周四午夜到一点之间存在。今晚是周四。现在是 11:58。\n# en: A spiral staircase emerging from a blank wall in moonlight, its steps unfolding one by one.\nscene [54fe61] = 月光下一道螺旋楼梯从空白的墙面里冒出来，台阶一级一级地展开。\n# en: Map it carefully\nopt.left.label [150195] = 仔细绘制它\n# en: You chart forty-one steps and a landing with a window onto a sky that isn't this one. The map is the club's pride.\nopt.left.say.1 [0210dc] = 你画下了四十一级台阶，和一个平台——平台的窗外是另一片天空。这张地图成了社团的骄傲。\n# en: Climb it to the top\nopt.right.label [fb1da8] = 一直爬到顶\n# en: At the top, a forgotten storeroom. Inside: a chest of old academy coins. The club splits it. Corvin cries a little.\nopt.right.say.1 [13aaa2] = 顶上是一间被遗忘的储藏室。里面有一箱古老的学院硬币。社团把它分了。科尔文哭了一小会儿。\n# en: At one o'clock the staircase stops existing. You were on it. You land in a laundry chute.\nopt.right.say.2 [714828] = 一点整，楼梯不再存在。而你正站在上面。你掉进了洗衣房的滑道。\n\n@event carto_lake_tunnel\n# en: The Tunnel Under the Lake\ntitle [7b054e] = 湖底隧道\n# en: A loose stone in the boathouse cellar opens on a tunnel running out under the lake. The bricks drip. At the far end, a round door carved with a black star. It is warm to the touch.\ntext [ea3eb1] = 船屋地窖里一块松动的石头后面，是一条延伸到湖底的隧道。砖墙滴着水。尽头是一扇刻着黑色星星的圆门。摸上去是温的。\n# en: A narrow brick tunnel dripping with water, lit by a single candle, ending at a round door carved with a black star.\nscene [1d60a2] = 一条滴水的狭窄砖砌隧道，只有一支蜡烛照明，尽头是一扇刻着黑星的圆门。\n# en: Mark it and leave\nopt.left.label [98295f] = 做个标记就离开\n# en: You add the tunnel to the club map with a skull beside it. Corvin adds a second skull, to be safe.\nopt.left.say.1 [837f6b] = 你把隧道添到社团地图上，旁边画了个骷髅。科尔文又加了一个骷髅，以防万一。\n# en: Press your palm to the star\nopt.right.label [f4d16b] = 把手掌按在星上\n# en: The star drinks the warmth out of your hand. Behind the door, something very large shifts in its sleep.\nopt.right.say.1 [d08b1b] = 星星吸走了你掌心的温度。门后，有什么非常庞大的东西在睡梦中翻了个身。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/crisis.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/crisis.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event crisis_collapse\n# en: Collapse\ntitle [59ccfe] = 倒下\n# en: The world tilts. When it rights itself you are in the infirmary, and Sister Amaranth is looking at you over her spectacles. \"You have been running your body like a borrowed broom,\" she says.\ntext [f87609] = 世界倾斜了。等它恢复正常时，你已经躺在医务室里，阿玛兰斯修女正从眼镜上方看着你。“你对待自己的身体，就像对待一把借来的飞帚。”她说。\n# en: A narrow infirmary bed under a high arched window, a healer's lamp floating beside it.\nscene [27d146] = 高高的拱窗下一张窄窄的病床，旁边悬浮着一盏治疗师的灯。\n# en: Pay for the full cure (30 gold)\nopt.left.label [97a3e9] = 付钱彻底治好（30 金币）\n# en: Three foul tonics and two days' sleep later, you feel almost human.\nopt.left.say.1 [ecd52f] = 三剂难喝的药和两天的睡眠之后，你感觉差不多像个人了。\n# en: Ask to rest instead\nopt.right.label [8eabcd] = 请求静养\n# en: A long, dull fortnight in bed. Sister Amaranth warns you: \"Next time, I write to your family.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [b20134] = 在床上躺了漫长而无聊的两周。阿玛兰斯修女警告你：“下次，我就写信给你家里。”\n\n@event crisis_despair\n# en: The Grey\ntitle [9c424f] = 灰色\n# en: Everything has gone grey. You have packed your trunk twice this week and unpacked it once. It would be so easy to take the next barge and simply stop.\ntext [02373b] = 一切都变成了灰色。这周你收拾了两次行李，又拆开了一次。坐上下一班驳船，就此停下，是多么容易。\n# en: A student sitting on the edge of a dormitory bed, rain on the window, a packed trunk by the door.\nscene [dc74cf] = 一个学生坐在宿舍床沿，窗上有雨，门边放着收拾好的行李箱。\n# en: Reach out to someone\nopt.left.label [e2c5e9] = 向别人伸出手\n# en: You knock on a friend's door. They don't ask questions. They just make tea, and stay.\nopt.left.say.1 [5f5838] = 你敲响了朋友的门。对方什么也没问。只是泡了茶，陪着你。\n# en: Old Maud finds you in the gardens and puts a trowel in your hand. \"Dig,\" she says. You dig. It helps more than it should.\nopt.left.say.2 [a27142] = 老莫德在花园里找到你，把一把小铲子塞进你手里。“挖。”她说。你挖了。这比想象中更有用。\n# en: You talk to the night porter, of all people. He tells you about his own first year. It's enough, for now.\nopt.left.say.3 [26f9c5] = 你竟然和守夜门房聊了起来。他给你讲了他自己的一年级。暂时，这就够了。\n# en: Take the next barge\nopt.right.label [c52bdf] = 坐下一班驳船\n# en: You get as far as the landing. The barge is late. By the time it arrives, you have decided to stay one more month.\nopt.right.say.1 [d9e779] = 你走到了码头。驳船晚点了。等它到的时候，你决定再留一个月。\n\n@event crisis_probation\n# en: The Disciplinary Board\ntitle [f764c3] = 纪律委员会\n# en: Two terms of failing marks. You are summoned before the Disciplinary Board: three professors and the headmistress, who looks at you with an expression of deep, patient disappointment. {flag(board_reprieve): They have seen you here before.|}\ntext [db6aa2] = 连续两个学期挂科。你被传唤到纪律委员会面前：三位教授和院长，院长看着你的表情里满是深沉而耐心的失望。{flag(board_reprieve): 他们以前在这里见过你。|}\n# en: Three professors behind a high bench in a dim chamber, the headmistress in the centre, hands folded.\nscene [e88ce4] = 昏暗的厅堂里，三位教授坐在高高的长桌后，院长居中，双手交握。\n# en: Plead your case\nopt.left.label [bb8fcc] = 为自己辩护\n# en: You speak honestly and well. They grant you one reprieve, and a study plan that fills every evening. \"There will not be a second,\" says the headmistress, pleasantly.\nopt.left.say.1 [03505f] = 你说得诚恳而得体。他们给了你一次宽限，还有一份把每个晚上都排满的学习计划。“不会有第二次了，”院长和气地说。\n# en: Promise to work harder\nopt.right.label [a78172] = 保证会更努力\n# en: \"Promises are cheap,\" says Professor Thorne. \"We'll take this one. Only this one.\" A reprieve, a study plan in his own hand, and a very long term ahead.\nopt.right.say.1 [8df15e] = “承诺不值钱，”索恩教授说，“这一次我们收下。只此一次。”一次宽限，一份他亲笔写的学习计划，以及一个漫长的学期。\n\n@event grisk_reminder\n# en: A Friendly Reminder\ntitle [6aaed3] = 友情提醒\n# en: A note on your door, in a tidy goblin hand: \"A payment was missed. Mister Grisk is a patient businessman. Mister Grisk charges interest on his patience.\" Underneath, smaller: \"Pay something, or another month will be added.\"\ntext [67613b] = 你门上钉着一张字条，是工整的地精笔迹：“有一笔还款逾期了。格里斯克先生是位有耐心的生意人。格里斯克先生的耐心是要收利息的。”下面一行小字：“多少还一点，否则再加一个月。”\n# en: A small, very neat note pinned to a dormitory door with a brass tack, the handwriting tiny and precise.\nscene [309f11] = 一张小小的、非常整洁的字条用黄铜图钉钉在宿舍门上，字迹细小而一丝不苟。\n# en: Scrape together what you can\nopt.left.label [d226e0] = 能凑多少凑多少\n# en: You empty your purse into an envelope and slide it under his door. It isn't twelve crowns. It is, says the note in reply, \"a gesture. Noted.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [acdacd] = 你把钱袋倒进一个信封，塞到他门下。不到十二克朗。回信的字条上写着：“一份心意。已记录。”\n# en: There is nothing to put in the envelope. You write \"next month, I promise\" on a scrap and slide that under his door instead. The reply is a single line, \"Promises are added to the ledger,\" and another month is.\nopt.left.say.2 [b418a0] = 信封里没什么可放的。你在一张纸片上写下“下个月，我保证”，从他门缝底下塞了进去。回信只有一行：“承诺记入账簿。”账簿上又多了一个月。\n# en: Put it out of your mind\nopt.right.label [68419d] = 别去想它\n# en: You put the note in a drawer. Another month goes on the ledger, and next month the knock on your door will be louder.\nopt.right.say.1 [2bdb82] = 你把字条塞进抽屉。账本上又多了一个月，下个月的敲门声会更响。\n\n@event grisk_collectors\n# en: Grisk's Collectors\ntitle [eb1401] = 格里斯克的讨债人\n# en: Two trolls in waistcoats are waiting outside your dormitory. \"Mister Grisk,\" says the larger one, \"is disappointed.\" The smaller one cracks his knuckles, which sounds like a rockslide.\ntext [6dfc11] = 两个穿马甲的巨魔等在你的宿舍外。“格里斯克先生，”大个子说，“很失望。”小个子把指关节捏得咔咔响，听起来像山崩。\n# en: Two large trolls in ill-fitting waistcoats looming in a dormitory doorway, one holding a ledger.\nscene [8592b2] = 两个穿着不合身马甲的高大巨魔堵在宿舍门口，其中一个拿着账簿。\n# en: Hand over everything you have\nopt.left.label [eec2ea] = 交出所有的钱\n# en: They count your purse twice and declare the debt settled. \"Pleasure,\" says the large one, meaning it.\nopt.left.say.1 [377b97] = 他们把你的钱袋数了两遍，宣布债务两清。“荣幸。”大个子说，是真心的。\n# en: They take every coin and your good boots, in front of half the corridor. \"Partial,\" they say, and leave.\nopt.left.say.2 [8315e9] = 他们当着半条走廊的人，拿走了每一枚硬币和你最好的靴子。“部分还款。”他们说完就走了。\n# en: Run\nopt.right.label [57e66a] = 逃跑\n# en: You escape across the rooftops. They'll be back, but not this month.\nopt.right.say.1 [24257a] = 你从屋顶上逃走了。他们还会回来，但不是这个月。\n# en: You do not escape. The trolls are surprisingly gentle about breaking only one rib.\nopt.right.say.2 [0374eb] = 你没逃掉。巨魔们出人意料地温柔，只断了你一根肋骨。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/expansion_classes.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/expansion_classes.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event core_flying_lesson\n# en: Compulsory Broom Flight\ntitle [014809] = 必修飞帚课\n# en: Compulsory first-year broom flight. The instructor blows her whistle. Your broom rises, hesitates, and then — clearly having taken a dislike to you — begins drifting toward the lake.\ntext [d69dcf] = 一年级必修的飞帚课。教练吹响了哨子。你的扫帚升起来，犹豫了一下，然后——显然是不太喜欢你——开始往湖那边飘。\n# en: A row of nervous first-years standing beside brooms on the lawn, one broom already rising on its own, an instructor with a whistle.\nscene [40aef9] = 草坪上一排紧张的一年级生站在扫帚旁，其中一把已经自己飞了起来，教练手里拿着哨子。\n# en: Hold on and steer it back\nopt.left.label [231b0d] = 抓紧，把它拽回来\n# en: You wrestle it round and land, wobbling, exactly on the mark. The instructor nods. The broom grudgingly accepts you.\nopt.left.say.1 [9f0eb8] = 你硬是把它扳了回来，摇摇晃晃地正好落在标记上。教练点了点头。扫帚勉强认了你。\n# en: The broom wins. You land in the shallows. Everyone sees. Everyone remembers.\nopt.left.say.2 [ccfc57] = 扫帚赢了。你落进了浅水里。所有人都看见了。所有人都记住了。\n# en: Jump off while it's still low\nopt.right.label [c7b2ec] = 趁它还飞得低跳下来\n# en: You drop four feet onto the grass. Undignified, but dry. The broom carries on to the lake alone and sinks with a sulky gurgle.\nopt.right.say.1 [ce5e6e] = 你从四英尺高的地方跳到草地上。不太体面，但没湿。扫帚独自飞到湖上，咕嘟一声闷闷不乐地沉了下去。\n\n@event pyro_dragon_breath\n# en: A Sample of Dragonfire\ntitle [8d15de] = 一缕龙焰样本\n# en: Thorne holds up a sealed jar containing one flame of genuine dragonfire, blue-white and furious. \"Who would like to try holding it?\" he asks. Nobody moves. He looks at you.\ntext [d3969c] = 索恩举起一个密封的罐子，里面是一团货真价实的龙焰，蓝白色，怒气冲冲。“谁想试着捧一下？”他问。没人动。他看向了你。\n# en: A glass jar containing a single writhing flame of deep blue, held in tongs by a stern professor, students leaning back from it.\nscene [5714c4] = 一个玻璃罐里扭动着一团深蓝色的火焰，被一位严厉的教授用钳子夹着，学生们纷纷往后仰。\n# en: Hold it\nopt.left.label [bf9ba5] = 捧住它\n# en: It sears and struggles and then, astonishingly, settles in your palm like a small warm animal. The class stares. Thorne almost smiles.\nopt.left.say.1 [1f13d1] = 它灼烧着、挣扎着，然后——令人震惊地——像一只温暖的小动物一样在你掌心安顿下来。全班都看呆了。索恩差点笑了。\n# en: The dragonfire disagrees. You spend a week with bandaged hands and a new respect for dragons.\nopt.left.say.2 [77f691] = 龙焰不同意。你缠着绷带过了一个星期，对龙有了全新的敬意。\n# en: Watch someone else\nopt.right.label [1e1e35] = 看别人来\n# en: A fourth-year volunteers and loses a sleeve. You take careful notes on what not to do.\nopt.right.say.1 [4fe5a6] = 一个四年级生自告奋勇，结果烧掉了一只袖子。你仔细记下了哪些事不该做。\n\n@event tide_water_spirit_race\n# en: The Water Spirit Race\ntitle [da1049] = 水灵竞速\n# en: Professor Marisol's final challenge: swim the lake while the water spirits race you. \"They cheat,\" she says cheerfully. \"Ask them nicely not to.\"\ntext [2365f2] = 玛丽索尔教授的期末挑战：游过湖去，同时有水灵和你比赛。“它们会作弊，”她兴高采烈地说，“好好请它们别作弊。”\n# en: Students swimming across a lake at dawn, translucent water-spirits racing alongside them like silver dolphins.\nscene [561442] = 黎明时分，学生们游过湖面，半透明的水灵像银色海豚一样在他们身旁竞速。\n# en: Race them flat out\nopt.left.label [2cf84d] = 全力和它们比\n# en: You touch the far shore a hand's length ahead of the fastest spirit. It sulks, then laughs like a fountain.\nopt.left.say.1 [5f4a18] = 你比最快的那个水灵早一掌的距离摸到对岸。它生了会儿闷气，然后像喷泉一样笑了起来。\n# en: The spirits tow you in circles. You arrive last, but very clean.\nopt.left.say.2 [320621] = 水灵拖着你转圈圈。你最后一个到，不过洗得非常干净。\n# en: Ask them nicely\nopt.right.label [a2f34a] = 好好请求它们\n# en: You ask. They carry you across on their backs like a raft. Marisol says that counts. Marisol says it's the whole point.\nopt.right.say.1 [c7d0ee] = 你开口请求。它们像木筏一样把你驮了过去。玛丽索尔说这算数。玛丽索尔说这才是重点。\n# en: They pretend not to hear. Water spirits are terrible at manners.\nopt.right.say.2 [eac302] = 它们假装没听见。水灵最不讲礼貌了。\n\n@event illusion_mirror_maze\n# en: The Mirror Maze Exam\ntitle [55c841] = 镜之迷宫考试\n# en: Professor Vey's exam: a maze of enchanted mirrors. Find the exit. Some reflections lie. One of them is — she promises — the real you.\ntext [0aec9f] = 维伊教授的考试：一座魔法镜子组成的迷宫。找到出口。有些倒影会说谎。其中一个——她保证——是真正的你。\n# en: A maze of tall mirrors in a dark hall, each reflecting a different version of the same student, one reflection grinning.\nscene [b9068c] = 黑暗大厅里的高镜迷宫，每面镜子都映出同一个学生的不同模样，其中一个倒影在咧嘴笑。\n# en: Trust your own eyes\nopt.left.label [ccacee] = 相信自己的眼睛\n# en: You spot the reflection that blinks late, and follow it out. Vey awards top marks and says, \"Most people trust the one that looks happiest.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [f97c79] = 你发现了那个眨眼慢半拍的倒影，跟着它走了出去。维伊给了你最高分，说：“大多数人都相信看起来最开心的那个。”\n# en: You follow the happiest reflection for an hour. It leads you in a circle and waves goodbye.\nopt.left.say.2 [21a1ac] = 你跟着最开心的那个倒影走了一个钟头。它领你绕了一圈，然后挥手道别。\n# en: Shatter the illusion\nopt.right.label [aa2560] = 击碎幻象\n# en: Every mirror goes dark at once. Exit found. Vey writes, \"Effective. Inelegant. Pass.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [137f22] = 所有镜子同时暗了下去。出口找到了。维伊写道：“有效。不优雅。及格。”\n# en: You shatter one mirror. It was real. Seven years of bad luck, the caretaker says, or three crowns for the glass.\nopt.right.say.2 [a46c96] = 你击碎了一面镜子。它是真的。管理员说，要么倒霉七年，要么赔三克朗玻璃钱。\n\n@event alch_love_potion\n# en: The Love Potion Assignment\ntitle [d13e1c] = 爱情药水作业\n# en: This week's assignment: brew a love potion — \"for analytical purposes only\". Your flask glows a suspicious pink. Someone at the next bench asks if they can buy it. Several people ask.\ntext [3c39e1] = 本周作业：调制一剂爱情药水——“仅供分析之用”。你的烧瓶泛着可疑的粉光。隔壁桌有人问能不能买下来。好几个人都问了。\n# en: A pink, glittering potion in a heart-shaped flask on a workbench, a student holding it up to the light with a doubtful expression.\nscene [1a4005] = 工作台上一个心形烧瓶里装着闪亮的粉色药水，一名学生把它举到光下，满脸怀疑。\n# en: Brew it perfectly, then pour it away\nopt.left.label [8fa0a2] = 完美调好，然后倒掉\n# en: Perfect colour, perfect scent. You pour it down the sink. The pipes are affectionate for a week.\nopt.left.say.1 [d421ab] = 完美的颜色，完美的香气。你把它倒进了水槽。水管一整个星期都含情脉脉的。\n# en: Sell it\nopt.right.label [4ac442] = 卖掉它\n# en: A prefect falls in love with the headmistress's portrait. It's traced back to you. Worth it, almost.\nopt.right.say.1 [1f1132] = 一位级长爱上了院长的画像。追查下来找到了你。差不多值了。\n# en: Fifteen crowns. You never ask what it was used for. You see a lot of unusual couples at dinner.\nopt.right.say.2 [9fdb68] = 十五克朗。你从没问过它被用来干什么。你在晚餐时看到了很多不寻常的情侣。\n\n@event div_prophecy_exam\n# en: The Prophecy Exam\ntitle [fa5039] = 预言考试\n# en: The mock Divination exam: gaze into the crystal and make one prophecy about the coming month. The examiner will check. \"Vague prophecies,\" she warns, \"fail.\"\ntext [a8f59f] = 占卜学模拟考：凝视水晶球，对下个月做出一条预言。考官会核实。“含糊的预言，”她警告道，“不及格。”\n# en: A dim divination classroom, a single student staring into a crystal ball, the examiner's quill hovering expectantly over a blank page.\nscene [91fdd6] = 昏暗的占卜教室，一名学生独自凝视着水晶球，考官的羽毛笔悬在空白的纸上，满怀期待。\n# en: Make a bold, specific prophecy\nopt.left.label [d1a2b7] = 做一个大胆而具体的预言\n# en: \"On the eighth, the kitchen cat will have kittens in the headmistress's hat.\" On the eighth, she does. Top marks in the mock.\nopt.left.say.1 [8b2bab] = “八号那天，厨房的猫会在院长的帽子里生小猫。”八号那天，它真的生了。模拟考满分。\n# en: \"On the eighth, it will snow indoors.\" It does not snow indoors. The examiner writes \"ambitious\".\nopt.left.say.2 [fc7167] = “八号那天，室内会下雪。”室内并没有下雪。考官写下“有野心”。\n# en: Make a careful, safe prophecy\nopt.right.label [e30df1] = 做一个谨慎稳妥的预言\n# en: \"Someone will be late to breakfast.\" Someone is. The examiner sighs and gives you a bare pass: it was vague, but it was checkable.\nopt.right.say.1 [ab1063] = “有人早饭会迟到。”确实有人迟到了。考官叹了口气，给你打了个勉强及格：说得含糊，但好歹能核实。\n\n@event art_golem_helper\n# en: The Little Golem\ntitle [6f3f24] = 小魔像\n# en: Your term project: a knee-high clay golem that carries books. It works! It works so well that it has started carrying everyone's books, whether they want it to or not.\ntext [677080] = 你的学期作业：一个及膝高的黏土魔像，专门搬书。它成功了！成功得过了头，开始搬所有人的书，不管人家愿不愿意。\n# en: A knee-high clay golem carrying a teetering stack of books across a classroom, a student watching anxiously as it nears a staircase.\nscene [4ac493] = 一个及膝高的黏土魔像抱着一摞摇摇欲坠的书穿过教室，一名学生紧张地看着它走近楼梯。\n# en: Add a \"stop\" command\nopt.left.label [3c6ffc] = 加一个“停下”指令\n# en: The golem stops. It also waits politely. The professor asks if she can borrow it for her office.\nopt.left.say.1 [6ad2e4] = 魔像停下了，还会礼貌地等待。教授问能不能借去她的办公室用。\n# en: Now it stops randomly, mid-staircase, holding forty books. Nobody can get past it. It looks very pleased.\nopt.left.say.2 [d28e81] = 现在它会随机停下——在楼梯中间，抱着四十本书。谁也过不去。它看起来非常得意。\n# en: Let it roam free\nopt.right.label [d4c542] = 让它自由游荡\n# en: The golem becomes a beloved castle fixture, delivering books to whoever looks sad. It signs every delivery with your initials, in clay.\nopt.right.say.1 [4659c3] = 魔像成了城堡里人见人爱的一景，专给看起来难过的人送书。每次送书，它都会用黏土签上你名字的缩写。\n# en: It also delivers a Restricted Section book to a first-year. The librarian follows the clay smudges straight to your door.\nopt.right.say.2 [94ea9a] = 它还把一本禁书区的书送到了一个一年级生手上。图书管理员顺着泥印一路找到了你的门口。\n\n@event beast_hippocampus\n# en: The Hippocampus Foal\ntitle [21cc8d] = 小海马驹\n# en: A hippocampus foal — half pony, half fish — has been stranded in a shrinking rock pool on the lakeshore. Professor Brask is on the other side of the grounds. The foal is panicking.\ntext [4890ea] = 一只海马驹——半是小马，半是鱼——被困在湖边一个不断缩小的石潭里。布拉斯克教授在校园另一头。小马驹惊慌失措。\n# en: A tiny sea-horse foal with a fish's tail struggling in a rock pool on the lake shore, a student kneeling beside the water.\nscene [205918] = 湖边石潭里，一只长着鱼尾的小海马驹在挣扎，一名学生跪在水边。\n# en: Carry it to the lake\nopt.left.label [d9060c] = 把它抱回湖里\n# en: It thrashes, then trusts you. You wade out and let it go. It circles you three times before diving deep.\nopt.left.say.1 [4d71d5] = 它扑腾了一阵，然后信任了你。你蹚进水里放开它。它绕着你转了三圈，然后潜入深处。\n# en: It kicks you in the stomach with surprising force — but you get it to the water. Brask says that's how they say thank you.\nopt.left.say.2 [7f1482] = 它力气大得惊人地踢中了你的肚子——但你还是把它送回了水里。布拉斯克说，它们就是这样道谢的。\n# en: Run for Professor Brask\nopt.right.label [cb87c9] = 跑去找布拉斯克教授\n# en: Brask arrives with a bucket and a lullaby. She makes you hold the bucket. You learn more in ten minutes than in a whole term.\nopt.right.say.1 [07b643] = 布拉斯克带着一只水桶和一首摇篮曲赶来了。她让你拎着水桶。你在十分钟里学到的比一整个学期还多。\n\n@event astro_eclipse\n# en: The Eclipse\ntitle [deb525] = 日食\n# en: An eclipse, the first in forty years. The astromancy class is on the roof with smoked-glass lenses. At the moment of totality, you notice something the charts don't show: a second shadow, smaller, moving the wrong way.\ntext [1ca60e] = 一场日食，四十年来的第一次。星象学课的学生们拿着烟熏玻璃镜片站在屋顶上。全食的那一刻，你注意到了星图上没有的东西：第二道阴影，更小，朝着相反的方向移动。\n# en: A black disc sliding across the sun above the observatory dome, students on the roof with smoked-glass lenses, a halo of light around the darkness.\nscene [5b724a] = 天文台穹顶上方，一个黑色圆盘滑过太阳，屋顶上的学生们举着烟熏玻璃镜片，黑暗周围一圈光晕。\n# en: Record it precisely\nopt.left.label [ee9627] = 精确记录下来\n# en: You chart the second shadow's path exactly. It crosses the sky and stops — directly above the lake.\nopt.left.say.1 [07b204] = 你精确地画出了第二道阴影的轨迹。它划过天空，停了下来——正好停在湖的上方。\n# en: Just watch the eclipse\nopt.right.label [b5b48a] = 只是看日食\n# en: For four minutes the world holds its breath. You'll remember the cold, and the silence, and the strange birdsong, for the rest of your life.\nopt.right.say.1 [3ab28b] = 有四分钟，整个世界屏住了呼吸。你这辈子都会记得那份寒冷、那份寂静，还有那奇怪的鸟鸣。\n\n@event heal_triage_drill\n# en: Triage\ntitle [090621] = 伤员分诊\n# en: Sister Amaranth's triage drill: twelve \"injured\" students, one healer — you — and ten minutes. Some are faking worse than others. One, you realise, is genuinely hurt.\ntext [c92712] = 阿玛兰斯修女的分诊演练：十二个“受伤”的学生，一个治疗师——就是你——还有十分钟。有些人装得比别人更夸张。而有一个，你意识到，是真的受伤了。\n# en: A practice ward full of students acting injured, groaning theatrically on cots, a single student healer in the middle with a clipboard.\nscene [5bb9f0] = 一间练习病房里满是假装受伤的学生，在病床上夸张地呻吟，一名学生治疗师拿着记录板站在中间。\n# en: Treat the genuine injury first\nopt.left.label [ab25ba] = 先处理真正的伤\n# en: You spot the real injury in the first minute. Amaranth nods, once, which is the highest honour in the infirmary.\nopt.left.say.1 [ac5815] = 你在第一分钟就发现了真正的伤员。阿玛兰斯点了一下头——这是医务室里的最高荣誉。\n# en: You find the real injury in minute nine. Late, but not too late. Amaranth's lips thin. \"Again tomorrow.\"\nopt.left.say.2 [da92d2] = 你在第九分钟才找到真正的伤员。晚了，但还不算太晚。阿玛兰斯抿紧了嘴唇。“明天再来。”\n# en: Work systematically down the line\nopt.right.label [7c7311] = 按顺序一个个处理\n# en: Methodical, thorough and slow. The real patient waits seven minutes. Amaranth makes you write an essay on why.\nopt.right.say.1 [503f17] = 有条不紊、细致而缓慢。真正的病人等了七分钟。阿玛兰斯让你写一篇文章解释为什么。\n\n@event umbral_last_lecture\n# en: The Last Lecture on Absence\ntitle [9b0f64] = 关于虚无的最后一课\n# en: The Umbral professor draws a star with a hollow centre on the board. \"This is not on the syllabus,\" she says. \"Three hundred years ago this academy imprisoned an absence that thinks. I believe you should know that before you graduate.\"\ntext [3f5b86] = 暗影理论教授在黑板上画了一颗中心空洞的星星。“这不在教学大纲里，”她说，“三百年前，这所学院囚禁了一个会思考的虚无。我认为你们在毕业前应该知道这件事。”\n# en: An Umbral Theory lecture hall lit only by a black candle, the professor drawing a star with a hollow centre on the board.\nscene [59edcc] = 暗影理论的讲堂只点着一支黑色蜡烛，教授在黑板上画着一颗中心空洞的星星。\n# en: Take careful notes\nopt.left.label [4d95af] = 认真记笔记\n# en: She speaks for an hour about seals and locks and the founders' fear. At the end she wipes the board clean and says, \"This lecture never happened.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [4517c0] = 她讲了一个钟头，关于封印、锁和创始者的恐惧。最后她把黑板擦干净，说：“这堂课从没发生过。”\n# en: Ask why she's telling you\nopt.right.label [16a97a] = 问她为什么告诉你们\n# en: \"Because something is stirring,\" she says, \"and the headmistress asked me to find out which of you would ask that question.\" She writes your name down.\nopt.right.say.1 [9e5f65] = “因为有什么东西正在苏醒，”她说，“院长让我看看你们当中谁会问这个问题。”她写下了你的名字。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/expansion_faculty.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/expansion_faculty.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event thorne_old_rival\n# en: An Old Rival\ntitle [357013] = 宿敌老友\n# en: A flamboyant old wizard in purple has arrived to settle \"a forty-year-old matter\" with Professor Thorne. A formal duel needs a second. Thorne looks around the courtyard and, to everyone's astonishment, points at you.\ntext [067b32] = 一位身着紫袍、浮夸的老巫师来了，要和索恩教授了结“一桩四十年前的旧事”。正式决斗需要一名副手。索恩环顾庭院，然后——令所有人震惊地——指向了你。\n# en: Two elderly mages facing each other across a dueling ring at dusk, one stern and grey, one flamboyant in purple, a nervous student holding a towel at the side.\nscene [40cb18] = 黄昏，两位年迈的法师在决斗场两端对峙，一位严肃灰白，一位紫袍浮夸，一名紧张的学生拿着毛巾站在一旁。\n# en: Stand as his second\nopt.left.label [e6354b] = 当他的副手\n# en: The duel lasts four seconds, the longest of your life. Thorne wins. Then both old men sit on the grass and laugh until they wheeze. You've never seen him laugh. Neither has anyone else.\nopt.left.say.1 [8b9a7a] = 决斗只持续了四秒，却是你一生中最漫长的四秒。索恩赢了。然后两个老头坐在草地上，一直笑到喘不过气。你从没见过他笑。别人也没见过。\n# en: Politely decline\nopt.right.label [8a601d] = 礼貌地拒绝\n# en: \"Hm,\" says Thorne, and asks the caretaker instead. He wins anyway. He does not mention it again, which is somehow worse.\nopt.right.say.1 [ddf880] = “嗯，”索恩说，转而请了管理员。他照样赢了。他再也没提起这件事，这不知怎么反而更糟。\n\n@event thorne_roses\n# en: The Professor's Roses\ntitle [ce1627] = 教授的玫瑰\n# en: At dawn, behind the observatory, you find Professor Thorne on his knees in a tiny walled rose garden, murmuring encouragement to a sickly bush. He sees you. Neither of you breathes.\ntext [163ce7] = 黎明时分，在天文台后面，你发现索恩教授跪在一个围墙环绕的小玫瑰园里，低声鼓励着一株病恹恹的玫瑰。他看见了你。你们俩都屏住了呼吸。\n# en: A severe old professor kneeling in a hidden rose garden at dawn, sleeves rolled up, murmuring encouragement to a single struggling rosebush.\nscene [0c6758] = 黎明，一位严厉的老教授跪在隐秘的玫瑰园里，袖子卷起，低声鼓励着一株挣扎求生的玫瑰。\n# en: Back away and never speak of it\nopt.left.label [225e23] = 悄悄退开，永不提起\n# en: You leave. The next day in class he calls on you for the hardest question — and then, very quietly, gives you full marks for a so-so answer.\nopt.left.say.1 [f662e1] = 你离开了。第二天上课时，他点你回答最难的问题——然后，非常小声地，给你那个马马虎虎的答案打了满分。\n# en: Offer to help with the sick bush\nopt.right.label [aa5c3c] = 主动帮忙救那株玫瑰\n# en: You know the blight; Maud taught you. Within a month the bush is covered in red roses. Thorne leaves one, without comment, on your desk.\nopt.right.say.1 [4837e1] = 你认得这种枯萎病，是莫德教你的。一个月内，那株玫瑰开满了红花。索恩在你桌上留下一朵，什么也没说。\n# en: Your remedy does nothing. But Thorne lets you kneel beside him for an hour, saying nothing, which you suspect is a great honour.\nopt.right.say.2 [629b27] = 你的药方毫无效果。但索恩让你在他身边跪了一个钟头，一言不发——你猜这是莫大的荣幸。\n\n@event thorne_shaking_hands\n# en: Thorne's Hands\ntitle [aad4f1] = 索恩的手\n# en: After class you see Thorne's wand slip from fingers that won't stop trembling. He picks it up, slowly. \"Age,\" he says, not looking at you. \"It comes for every storm eventually. You'll say nothing.\"\ntext [b146e2] = 下课后，你看见索恩的魔杖从一双抖个不停的手指间滑落。他慢慢地把它捡起来。“年纪，”他说，没有看你，“每一场风暴终有平息之时。你什么也不会说。”\n# en: An old professor gripping the edge of a lectern with both hands, a dropped wand on the floor, a single student in the doorway.\nscene [d913fc] = 一位老教授双手紧抓讲台边缘，一根魔杖掉在地上，门口站着一名学生。\n# en: Say nothing, as he asks\nopt.left.label [08876a] = 照他说的，什么也不说\n# en: You never mention it. But you start arriving early to set up his demonstrations. He notices. He lets you.\nopt.left.say.1 [5d89d2] = 你从没提起这件事。但你开始提早到教室帮他布置演示。他注意到了。他默许了。\n# en: Tell Sister Amaranth\nopt.right.label [3f4d80] = 告诉阿玛兰斯修女\n# en: Amaranth's tonic steadies his hands. Thorne is furious with you for a week. Then he stops being furious, and starts, grudgingly, teaching you his hardest spells — \"before I can't.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [f813ac] = 阿玛兰斯的药剂让他的手稳了下来。索恩对你大发雷霆了一个星期。然后他不再生气，开始勉为其难地教你他最难的咒语——“趁我还能教。”\n\n@event maud_goat\n# en: Bartholomew in the Library\ntitle [07ab64] = 图书馆里的巴塞洛缪\n# en: Maud's goat, Bartholomew, has got into the library and is eating the Restricted Section. The librarian is apoplectic. Maud, hurrying in, grabs your arm. \"He likes you. Get him out before he eats anything with teeth.\"\ntext [ff1025] = 莫德的山羊巴塞洛缪闯进了图书馆，正在吃禁书区。图书管理员气得快中风了。莫德匆匆赶来，一把抓住你的胳膊。“它喜欢你。在它吃下什么长牙的书之前，把它弄出去。”\n# en: A shaggy goat standing on a library table chewing a book, an apoplectic librarian below, an old woman in a straw hat hurrying through the stacks.\nscene [579e73] = 一只毛蓬蓬的山羊站在图书馆的桌子上嚼书，下面是气急败坏的图书管理员，一位戴草帽的老妇人匆匆穿过书架。\n# en: Lure him out with an apple\nopt.left.label [89957a] = 用苹果把它引出来\n# en: Bartholomew follows the apple, and you, out into the sunlight. He has eaten one-third of \"Curses of the Northern Wastes\". He seems fine. Mostly.\nopt.left.say.1 [2ec444] = 巴塞洛缪跟着苹果——也跟着你——走进了阳光里。它吃掉了三分之一本《北方荒原诅咒大全》。它看起来没事。大体上没事。\n# en: Bartholomew eats the apple, then your sleeve, then headbutts you into a shelf. Maud gets him out in the end, laughing.\nopt.left.say.2 [cef45a] = 巴塞洛缪吃了苹果，然后吃了你的袖子，然后一头把你顶进了书架。最后是莫德笑着把它弄出去的。\n# en: Pick him up and carry him out\nopt.right.label [5273c3] = 把它抱起来扛出去\n# en: He is heavier than he looks and struggles like a sack of angry cats. You carry him all the way to the paddock. The librarian applauds.\nopt.right.say.1 [d608cc] = 它比看上去重，挣扎起来像一麻袋愤怒的猫。你一路把它扛到了围场。图书管理员鼓起了掌。\n\n@event maud_recipe\n# en: Maud's Soup\ntitle [913763] = 莫德的汤\n# en: \"I'm going to teach you my soup,\" says Maud, rolling up her sleeves. \"Cures colds, broken hearts and most curses. Don't write it down. You'll remember it if you need it.\"\ntext [dcac02] = “我要教你我的汤，”莫德卷起袖子说，“治感冒、治心碎、治大多数诅咒。别写下来。需要的时候你自然会记得。”\n# en: A small cottage kitchen, a black iron pot over the fire, an old woman guiding a student's hand as they stir.\nscene [a5474e] = 一间小小的农舍厨房，炉火上架着一口黑铁锅，一位老妇人握着学生的手教他搅拌。\n# en: Learn it properly\nopt.left.label [9c42bb] = 认真学会它\n# en: It takes all afternoon. Some of the ingredients are herbs; some, as far as you can tell, are just stories. You remember every step.\nopt.left.say.1 [d6eaaf] = 花了一整个下午。有些材料是草药；有些，在你看来，只是故事。你记住了每一步。\n# en: Mostly eat it\nopt.right.label [390278] = 主要负责吃\n# en: You learn about half of it and eat three bowls. Maud declares that the most important part of the recipe anyway.\nopt.right.say.1 [51d561] = 你学会了大约一半，喝了三碗。莫德宣布，这反正是这道菜谱里最重要的部分。\n\n@event maud_old_wand\n# en: A Wand From Sixty Years Ago\ntitle [017dae] = 六十年前的魔杖\n# en: Maud unwraps something from a faded scarf: a slender silver wand. \"Edmund's,\" she says. \"He was the one who didn't come back. He'd have wanted it to go beneath the lake again, with someone who meant to close the door.\"\ntext [8bc08b] = 莫德从一条褪色的围巾里解开一样东西：一根纤细的银魔杖。“埃德蒙的，”她说，“他就是那个没回来的人。他会希望它再到湖底去一次，跟着一个真心想关上那扇门的人。”\n# en: An old woman unwrapping a slender silver wand from a faded silk scarf, a photograph of three young students on the table beside it.\nscene [b81801] = 一位老妇人从一条褪色的丝巾里解出一根纤细的银魔杖，旁边桌上放着一张三个年轻学生的照片。\n# en: Accept it\nopt.left.label [5003f6] = 接受它\n# en: It fits your hand like it was waiting. When you hold it near the lake, it hums the same note as the drowned bell.\nopt.left.say.1 [fe42ab] = 它在你手里服帖得好像一直在等你。你把它举到湖边时，它和那口沉没的钟发出同一个音。\n# en: Tell her to keep it\nopt.right.label [680674] = 请她留着\n# en: \"Keep it for him,\" you say. She holds it for a long time. \"Sixty years,\" she says, \"and nobody ever said that.\" She makes tea, and you sit with her until dark.\nopt.right.say.1 [da80fd] = “替他留着吧，”你说。她握着它很久很久。“六十年了，”她说，“从来没有人这么说过。”她泡了茶，你陪她坐到天黑。\n\n@event isolde_chess\n# en: A Game of Tower Chess\ntitle [b9eeea] = 一局高塔棋\n# en: The headmistress keeps a board of crystal towers by her window. \"Do you play?\" she asks. It is clearly not a question. The pieces are already moving into position by themselves.\ntext [c9c818] = 院长在窗边放着一副水晶塔棋盘。“你下棋吗？”她问。这显然不是一个问题。棋子已经自己走到了位置上。\n# en: A chessboard of carved crystal towers on a small table by a tower window, two players, one candle, the lake silver below.\nscene [8c661f] = 塔楼窗边的小桌上放着一副雕刻水晶塔的棋盘，两位棋手，一支蜡烛，窗下的湖泛着银光。\n# en: Play to win\nopt.left.label [6c2256] = 为赢而下\n# en: You win. She looks at the board for a long moment, then laughs — a short, delighted sound. \"Again,\" she says. \"Thursday.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [ad5261] = 你赢了。她盯着棋盘看了很久，然后笑了——短促而开心的一声。“再来，”她说，“星期四。”\n# en: She wins in nine moves and spends the next hour showing you exactly why. It is the most useful lesson you've had all year.\nopt.left.say.2 [a26db6] = 她九步就赢了，然后花了一个钟头告诉你究竟为什么。这是你一整年里最有用的一课。\n# en: Play to learn how she thinks\nopt.right.label [51a7ef] = 为了解她的想法而下\n# en: You lose, slowly, watching. She sacrifices pieces she seems to love. By the end, you understand a great deal about how the academy is run.\nopt.right.say.1 [21753e] = 你输了，输得很慢，一边输一边看。她会牺牲那些她似乎很珍爱的棋子。到最后，你对这所学院是怎么运转的明白了许多。\n\n@event isolde_portrait\n# en: The Young Headmistress\ntitle [9f225c] = 年轻的院长\n# en: In a forgotten corridor you find a portrait of a laughing girl with a wand stuck behind her ear and ink on her nose. The plaque has been scratched off. The eyes are unmistakably the headmistress's. The portrait winks.\ntext [2b1741] = 在一条被遗忘的走廊里，你发现了一幅画像：一个笑着的女孩，耳后别着魔杖，鼻尖沾着墨水。铭牌被刮掉了。那双眼睛无疑是院长的。画像眨了眨眼。\n# en: A portrait in a forgotten corridor of a laughing young woman in student robes with a wand behind her ear, frame dusty, eyes unmistakably the headmistress's.\nscene [b07fba] = 被遗忘的走廊里一幅画像：一位身穿学生长袍、耳后别着魔杖、笑着的年轻女子，画框积满灰尘，眼睛无疑是院长的。\n# en: Ask the portrait about her\nopt.left.label [38d936] = 向画像打听她\n# en: \"Detention record of the decade,\" whispers the portrait. \"Set the observatory on fire twice. Best student this castle ever had.\" It winks again.\nopt.left.say.1 [a6720c] = “十年来禁闭记录最多的人，”画像低声说，“把天文台烧了两次。这座城堡有过的最好的学生。”它又眨了眨眼。\n# en: The portrait pretends to be asleep, loudly. By the time you give up, the corridor has rearranged itself, and it takes you half the evening to find your way out.\nopt.left.say.2 [b05711] = 肖像大声地装睡。等你放弃时，走廊已经自己换了样子，你花了半个晚上才找到出去的路。\n# en: Tell the headmistress you found it\nopt.right.label [966699] = 告诉院长你发现了它\n# en: She is quiet for a moment. \"I thought they'd burned it.\" She almost smiles. \"Leave it where it is. Someone should remember her.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [1bb647] = 她沉默了片刻。“我还以为他们把它烧了。”她几乎笑了。“就让它留在那儿吧。总该有人记得她。”\n\n@event isolde_judgement\n# en: A Question of Mercy\ntitle [a10d01] = 关于仁慈的问题\n# en: The headmistress slides a file across her desk. A second-year stole from the apothecary to pay for a sick mother's medicine. The rules say expulsion. \"You're a student,\" she says. \"Tell me what a student thinks is just.\"\ntext [050856] = 院长把一份卷宗推过桌面。一个二年级生为了给生病的母亲买药，从药铺偷了东西。按校规应当开除。“你是学生，”她说，“告诉我，学生认为什么才算公正。”\n# en: The headmistress at her desk with a single file open, a student seated opposite, rain on the window behind them.\nscene [d36bff] = 院长坐在书桌后，面前摊开一份卷宗，一名学生坐在对面，身后的窗上落着雨。\n# en: Argue for mercy\nopt.left.label [a3a062] = 为仁慈辩护\n# en: You argue for an hour. She listens to all of it. The second-year is given a job in the infirmary and a stern lecture. The mother recovers.\nopt.left.say.1 [eb948e] = 你辩了一个钟头。她全都听完了。那个二年级生被安排到医务室打工，外加一顿严厉的训诫。那位母亲康复了。\n# en: The rules exist for a reason\nopt.right.label [194310] = 校规自有其道理\n# en: \"Yes,\" she says, \"they do.\" She closes the file. You never learn what she decided, but the second-year is still in class the next week, looking grateful at nobody in particular.\nopt.right.say.1 [2c1752] = “是的，”她说，“它有。”她合上卷宗。你始终不知道她最后怎么决定的，但下个星期那个二年级生还在上课，对着不特定的某个人一脸感激。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/expansion_life.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/expansion_life.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event life_timeloop_1\n# en: Haven't We Done This?\ntitle [f7db95] = 这事是不是发生过？\n# en: Breakfast. Eggs. Thorne trips on the third step. A first-year spills juice on a prefect. You know all of this is about to happen, because it already happened. Yesterday. Which was also Tuesday.\ntext [8f145a] = 早餐。鸡蛋。索恩在第三级台阶上绊了一下。一个一年级生把果汁洒在了级长身上。你知道这一切马上就要发生，因为它已经发生过了。昨天。昨天也是星期二。\n# en: A student at breakfast staring at a plate of eggs with intense déjà vu, a clock on the wall with its hands spinning backward.\nscene [f64ed4] = 一名学生在早餐桌前盯着一盘鸡蛋，强烈的既视感涌上心头，墙上的钟指针在倒转。\n# en: Ignore it, you're probably just tired\nopt.left.label [f68ce0] = 别管它，大概只是累了\n# en: You eat the eggs. They taste exactly as they did yesterday. Thorne trips on the third step. You go to bed early, uneasy, and wake to eggs.\nopt.left.say.1 [1ccd6d] = 你吃了鸡蛋。味道和昨天一模一样。索恩教授在第三级台阶上绊了一下。你早早上床，心神不宁，醒来又是鸡蛋。\n# en: Warn Thorne about the step\nopt.right.label [eb6211] = 提醒索恩小心台阶\n# en: \"The third step, Professor.\" He looks at you strangely and steps over it. Somewhere, a clock skips. You go to bed, and wake, and it's still Tuesday.\nopt.right.say.1 [fc4336] = “第三级台阶，教授。”他奇怪地看了你一眼，跨了过去。某处，一座钟跳了一格。你上床，醒来，还是星期二。\n\n@event life_timeloop_2\n# en: Tuesday, Again\ntitle [d04f00] = 又是星期二\n# en: {loop_n <= 1: It's Tuesday again. Eggs, the third step, the juice, the prefect. Nobody else notices anything.|{loop_n <= 3: Tuesday. You could recite breakfast. You could recite the prefect's yelp.|{loop_n <= 5: Tuesday, again. The days are starting to feel thin, like a page read too often.|{loop_n <= 7: Tuesday. The eggs taste of nothing now. The clock in the hall ticks a beat late.|Tuesday. The edges of the morning are fraying, like a loop pulled too tight. It can't hold much longer.}}}} {loop_clues >= 3: You know where it comes from now.|{loop_clues >= 1: You have a thread to pull.|You have no idea why.}}\ntext [c737ca] = {loop_n <= 1: 又是星期二。鸡蛋、第三级台阶、果汁、级长。没有别人注意到任何事。|{loop_n <= 3: 星期二。你能背出早餐，能背出级长的那声惨叫。|{loop_n <= 5: 又是星期二。日子开始变薄了，像一页被翻了太多遍的纸。|{loop_n <= 7: 星期二。鸡蛋已经没有味道了。大厅里的钟晚了一拍。|星期二。早晨的边缘正在磨损，像一个被拉得太紧的圈。它撑不了多久了。}}}} {loop_clues >= 3: 现在你知道它从哪儿来了。|{loop_clues >= 1: 你手里有一根可以拉的线头。|你完全不知道为什么。}}\n# en: The same breakfast scene repeated, but the student is now standing on the table, holding up a sign that reads \"IT'S STILL TUESDAY\".\nscene [443eec] = 同样的早餐场景再次上演，但这一次学生站在了桌子上，举着一块牌子：“现在还是星期二”。\n# en: Use it: win every bet, ace the quiz\nopt.exploit.label [42a58e] = 利用它：每赌必赢，小测满分\n# en: Crowns and marks carry over. It wears on you, and someone may notice.\nopt.exploit.hint [522272] = 金币和分数都会留下。它会消磨你，也可能有人察觉。\n# en: You know every answer and every coin-toss. Why not?\nopt.exploit.say.1 [ceb5c5] = 每一道答案、每一次抛硬币你都知道。为什么不呢？\n# en: Do something you'd never dare\nopt.experiment.label [d06730] = 做一件你永远不敢做的事\n# en: Nothing you do today will last. Almost nothing.\nopt.experiment.hint [36f434] = 今天你做的一切都不会留下。几乎一切。\n# en: Nothing today can stick. You might as well find out who you are when nothing sticks.\nopt.experiment.say.1 [3cf8fb] = 今天什么都留不住。那不如看看，什么都留不住的时候，你是个怎样的人。\n# en: Look for the cause\nopt.investigate.label [c6ff51] = 寻找原因\n# en: Each loop, a clue. Clues make the fix possible.\nopt.investigate.hint [c9830b] = 每一轮，一条线索。线索让解法成为可能。\n# en: Something is doing this. Clocks don't stop on their own.\nopt.investigate.say.1 [21f23e] = 有什么东西在这么做。钟不会自己停下。\n# en: Try to break the loop\nopt.fix.label [e608ff] = 试着打破循环\n# en: A spell against the clock. Hard at first; easier every loop, and with what you've found.\nopt.fix.hint [7437c3] = 一道对抗时间的咒语。一开始很难；每一轮都会更容易，你找到的线索也有帮助。\n# en: You go up the astronomy tower before breakfast, while the morning is still new.\nopt.fix.say.1 [455845] = 趁早晨还新鲜，你在早餐前爬上了天文塔。\n\n@event life_timeloop_3\n# en: The Broken Chronometer\ntitle [d82d49] = 坏掉的计时仪\n# en: In the astronomy tower, a first-year is asleep beside an enormous brass chronometer, wand still stuck in its winding key. \"I just wanted one more day before my exam,\" says the note on his chest. {loop_clues >= 3: You know exactly which gear, and why.|You know roughly where to start.}\ntext [a07ba5] = 天文塔里，一个一年级生睡在一台巨大的黄铜计时仪旁边，魔杖还卡在发条钥匙里。他胸前的字条上写着：“我只是想在考试前多要一天。”{loop_clues >= 3: 你清楚地知道是哪个齿轮，也知道为什么。|你大概知道从哪儿下手。}\n# en: An astronomy tower at night, a giant brass chronometer with its gears spinning in reverse, a sleeping first-year slumped beside it with a wand still in hand.\nscene [4ad62e] = 夜晚的天文塔，一台巨型黄铜计时仪的齿轮在倒转，一个睡着的一年级生瘫在旁边，手里还握着魔杖。\n# en: Wrench the clock forward\nopt.left.label [86cabc] = 把钟硬拧向前\n# en: The gears groan, catch and turn forward. Somewhere a clock strikes, properly, for the first time in {loop_n >= 6: what feels like a month|days}. It's Wednesday. The first-year in the corner wakes up and starts to cry with relief.\nopt.left.say.1 [aa5594] = 齿轮呻吟着，卡住，然后向前转动。某处，一座钟正常地敲响了，这是{loop_n >= 6: 仿佛一个月|好几天}以来的第一次。是星期三了。角落里的一年级生醒过来，如释重负地哭了。\n# en: The chronometer bites, and the morning folds up like a letter. You wake to eggs, and the third step, and the juice. It's Tuesday. You know a little more about the clock.\nopt.left.say.2 [e63c45] = 计时仪咬了你一口，早晨像一封信一样折了起来。你醒来，鸡蛋、第三级台阶、果汁。是星期二。你对那座钟又多了解了一点。\n# en: The gears groan, catch and turn forward. Somewhere a clock strikes, properly, for the first time in {loop_n >= 6: what feels like a month|days}. It's Wednesday. The first-year in the corner wakes up and starts to cry with relief.\nopt.left.say.3 [aa5594] = 齿轮呻吟着，卡住，然后向前转动。某处，一座钟正常地敲响了，这是{loop_n >= 6: 仿佛一个月|好几天}以来的第一次。是星期三了。角落里的一年级生醒过来，如释重负地哭了。\n# en: The chronometer bites, and the morning folds up like a letter. You wake to eggs, and the third step, and the juice. It's Tuesday. You know a little more about the clock.\nopt.left.say.4 [e63c45] = 计时仪咬了你一口，早晨像一封信一样折了起来。你醒来，鸡蛋、第三级台阶、果汁。是星期二。你对那座钟又多了解了一点。\n# en: The gears groan, catch and turn forward. Somewhere a clock strikes, properly, for the first time in {loop_n >= 6: what feels like a month|days}. It's Wednesday. The first-year in the corner wakes up and starts to cry with relief.\nopt.left.say.5 [aa5594] = 齿轮呻吟着，卡住，然后向前转动。某处，一座钟正常地敲响了，这是{loop_n >= 6: 仿佛一个月|好几天}以来的第一次。是星期三了。角落里的一年级生醒过来，如释重负地哭了。\n# en: The chronometer bites, and the morning folds up like a letter. You wake to eggs, and the third step, and the juice. It's Tuesday. You know a little more about the clock.\nopt.left.say.6 [e63c45] = 计时仪咬了你一口，早晨像一封信一样折了起来。你醒来，鸡蛋、第三级台阶、果汁。是星期二。你对那座钟又多了解了一点。\n# en: The gears groan, catch and turn forward. Somewhere a clock strikes, properly, for the first time in {loop_n >= 6: what feels like a month|days}. It's Wednesday. The first-year in the corner wakes up and starts to cry with relief.\nopt.left.say.7 [aa5594] = 齿轮呻吟着，卡住，然后向前转动。某处，一座钟正常地敲响了，这是{loop_n >= 6: 仿佛一个月|好几天}以来的第一次。是星期三了。角落里的一年级生醒过来，如释重负地哭了。\n# en: The chronometer bites, and the morning folds up like a letter. You wake to eggs, and the third step, and the juice. It's Tuesday. You know a little more about the clock.\nopt.left.say.8 [e63c45] = 计时仪咬了你一口，早晨像一封信一样折了起来。你醒来，鸡蛋、第三级台阶、果汁。是星期二。你对那座钟又多了解了一点。\n# en: Wake the first-year and fix it together\nopt.right.label [64c5c1] = 叫醒一年级生，一起修好它\n# en: You shake him awake. He's horrified, then helpful. Between your clues and his guilt you find the gear together, and the clock strikes Wednesday. He passes his exam. You never tell him how many Tuesdays it was.\nopt.right.say.1 [3f81c1] = 你把他摇醒。他先是吓坏了，然后帮了大忙。靠你的线索和他的愧疚，你们一起找到了那个齿轮，钟敲响了星期三。他考试及格了。你从没告诉他到底过了多少个星期二。\n# en: You shake him awake, and he panics, and the wand snaps in the key. The morning folds up. You wake to eggs. It's Tuesday, and you know a little more about the boy.\nopt.right.say.2 [a2a494] = 你把他摇醒，他慌了，魔杖断在了钥匙里。早晨折了起来。你醒来，又是鸡蛋。是星期二，你对那个男孩又多了解了一点。\n\n@event life_portrait_escape\n# en: The Escaped Portrait\ntitle [2a676c] = 出逃的画像\n# en: An empty frame in the long gallery. The Duchess of Ambry has escaped her portrait and is hiding in a landscape three corridors away, refusing to come back. \"That frame,\" she declares, \"is hideous.\"\ntext [a18812] = 长廊里一个空画框。安布里公爵夫人逃出了她的画像，躲进了三条走廊外的一幅风景画里，拒绝回来。“那个画框，”她宣布，“丑死了。”\n# en: An empty gilded picture frame on a gallery wall, and further down the corridor a painted duchess hiding inside a landscape of a picnic.\nscene [805579] = 长廊墙上一个空空的镀金画框，走廊尽头，一位画中的公爵夫人正躲在一幅野餐风景画里。\n# en: Persuade her back\nopt.left.label [fd187a] = 劝她回来\n# en: You promise to have her frame regilded. She returns, mollified. The caretaker regilds it. You've acquired a duchess who now considers you a personal friend.\nopt.left.say.1 [af9e67] = 你答应给她的画框重新镀金。她回来了，气消了。管理员重新镀了金。你收获了一位公爵夫人，她现在把你当作私人朋友。\n# en: She calls you a peasant and moves to a painting of a shipwreck. She's still there. She says she likes the sea air.\nopt.left.say.2 [7c07eb] = 她骂你是个乡巴佬，搬进了一幅沉船图里。她至今还在那儿。她说她喜欢海风。\n# en: Leave her in the picnic\nopt.right.label [f13545] = 让她留在野餐里\n# en: She seems happier among the sandwiches. So does the picnic. Some arrangements simply work.\nopt.right.say.1 [09beab] = 她在三明治之间似乎更快乐。野餐也是。有些安排就是刚刚好。\n\n@event life_library_cat\n# en: The Library Cat\ntitle [d3cfff] = 图书馆的猫\n# en: The library cat — a vast, ancient beast called Footnote — is asleep on the only copy of the book you need. You have an essay due tomorrow. Footnote has no deadlines.\ntext [968aad] = 图书馆的猫——一只名叫“脚注”的巨大古老生物——正睡在你需要的那本书上，而那是唯一的一本。你的论文明天就要交。脚注没有截止日期。\n# en: An enormous fluffy cat asleep on the exact book a student needs, on a high library shelf, one eye half-open.\nscene [8ac48f] = 一只巨大的蓬松猫咪睡在图书馆高处书架上，身下正是学生需要的那本书，一只眼睛半睁着。\n# en: Move the cat\nopt.left.label [967e4f] = 把猫挪开\n# en: You lift Footnote like a sack of warm flour. He allows it, with contempt. You get the book. He gets your chair.\nopt.left.say.1 [52e940] = 你像抱一袋温热的面粉一样抱起脚注。它允许了，带着鄙夷。你拿到了书。它拿到了你的椅子。\n# en: Footnote does not consent to being moved. You now have three long scratches and still no book.\nopt.left.say.2 [6230a8] = 脚注不同意被挪动。你现在多了三道长长的抓痕，书还是没拿到。\n# en: Bribe the cat\nopt.right.label [011f22] = 贿赂这只猫\n# en: One sardine. Footnote rises like a sultan, stretches, and moves exactly one book to the left. It's enough.\nopt.right.say.1 [b290a1] = 一条沙丁鱼。脚注像苏丹一样起身，伸了个懒腰，正好往左挪了一本书的距离。够了。\n\n@event life_invent_spell\n# en: A Spell of Your Own\ntitle [9d35aa] = 属于你的咒语\n# en: Late at night you realise there's a gap — a spell nobody has written yet, just a small one, to make ink dry instantly. You could try to invent it. Inventing spells, Thorne says, is \"how most of my former students lost their eyebrows\".\ntext [40816d] = 深夜，你意识到有个空白——一个还没人写过的咒语，只是个小咒语，能让墨水瞬间变干。你可以试着发明它。索恩说，发明咒语是“我大多数前学生失去眉毛的方式”。\n# en: A student at a desk covered in crossed-out incantations, a tiny spiral of light hovering over the page, an expression of dawning triumph.\nscene [c075ca] = 一名学生坐在写满划掉的咒文的书桌前，一小团螺旋状的光悬浮在纸上，脸上渐渐露出胜利的神情。\n# en: Invent it carefully\nopt.left.label [6adb19] = 小心地发明它\n# en: Three weeks of theory and one perfect evening. The ink dries, and so does a paper cut on your thumb: what you've written is a cousin of Mend. Thorne grunts and puts your name on next year's lecture slide.\nopt.left.say.1 [b2a1fc] = 三周的理论，加一个完美的夜晚。墨水干了，你拇指上被纸划的口子也愈合了：你写出来的是愈合咒的一个远亲。索恩教授哼了一声，把你的名字写进了明年的讲义。\n# en: Your spell dries ink instantly. It also dries the paper, the desk and your eyes. You shelve it for now.\nopt.left.say.2 [20cce9] = 你的咒语能瞬间弄干墨水。也能弄干纸、书桌和你的眼睛。你暂时把它搁置了。\n# en: Just try things until something works\nopt.right.label [847e81] = 乱试一通，直到有效\n# en: Somehow — through chaos and luck — it works. You couldn't explain why under oath. It still counts.\nopt.right.say.1 [c2baba] = 不知怎么的——靠着混乱和运气——它成功了。就算发誓你也解释不了为什么。但它照样算数。\n# en: Your eyebrows survive. Your desk does not.\nopt.right.say.2 [182ef8] = 你的眉毛幸存了。你的书桌没有。\n\n@event life_cursed_umbrella\n# en: The Cursed Umbrella\ntitle [8c3b3e] = 被诅咒的伞\n# en: You borrowed an umbrella from lost property. It's black, elegant and cursed: underneath it, it is always raining. The rest of the world is sunny. You are very wet.\ntext [b42225] = 你从失物招领处借了一把伞。它是黑色的，很优雅，还被诅咒了：伞底下永远在下雨。外面整个世界阳光明媚。你浑身湿透。\n# en: A student holding a black umbrella under which a tiny private rainstorm is pouring, while the sky outside is perfectly clear.\nscene [3ed21b] = 一名学生撑着一把黑伞，伞下正下着一场私人小暴雨，而外面的天空晴朗无云。\n# en: Break the curse\nopt.left.label [bbf151] = 解除诅咒\n# en: The curse lifts with a soft hiss. The umbrella is now just a very nice umbrella. It isn't yours, but you sell it to Corvin anyway; he wants it \"for drama\".\nopt.left.say.1 [5d8f53] = 诅咒轻轻嘶的一声解除了。现在它只是一把非常好看的伞。它不是你的，但你还是把它卖给了科尔文，他说要拿来“营造戏剧效果”。\n# en: Now it rains sideways.\nopt.left.say.2 [1a72a4] = 现在雨是横着下的。\n# en: Return it to lost property\nopt.right.label [5ea06a] = 还给失物招领处\n# en: The caretaker takes it back, sighs, and hangs it on a hook labelled \"DO NOT\". There are six other things on that hook.\nopt.right.say.1 [c90b55] = 管理员收了回去，叹了口气，把它挂在一个标着“别碰”的钩子上。那个钩子上已经挂了六样别的东西。\n\n@event life_exam_dream\n# en: The Exam Dream\ntitle [e7738c] = 考试噩梦\n# en: You dream you're in the exam hall in your pyjamas. The paper is in a language that keeps changing. The proctor is Bartholomew the goat. You wake at 4am, heart pounding.\ntext [4ad83e] = 你梦见自己穿着睡衣坐在考场里。试卷上的语言不停地变来变去。监考官是山羊巴塞洛缪。凌晨四点你惊醒了，心怦怦直跳。\n# en: A dream-image: a student in an exam hall in their pyjamas, the exam paper written in a language that keeps changing, a clock with no hands.\nscene [e45f4e] = 梦境画面：一名学生穿着睡衣坐在考场里，试卷上的文字不停变换，一座没有指针的钟。\n# en: Get up and revise\nopt.left.label [02956b] = 起床复习\n# en: You revise until breakfast. The real exam paper is written in a normal language. You're absurdly relieved.\nopt.left.say.1 [ba0aba] = 你一直复习到早餐。真正的试卷是用正常语言写的。你如释重负得有点荒唐。\n# en: Go back to sleep\nopt.right.label [097b30] = 接着睡\n# en: You dream again. This time the goat gives you full marks. You choose to take this as an omen.\nopt.right.say.1 [c9ff1e] = 你又做梦了。这次山羊给了你满分。你决定把这当作一个好兆头。\n\n@event life_garden_plot\n# en: A Patch of Earth\ntitle [675687] = 一方泥土\n# en: Old Maud is giving out the abandoned plots behind the greenhouses. There's one left: small, weedy and full of rocks. \"It's yours if you want it,\" she says. \"Gardens are a lot of work. Most of the work is noticing.\"\ntext [492379] = 老莫德正在分发温室后面那些废弃的小块地。还剩一块：小小的，杂草丛生，满是石头。“想要的话就归你，”她说，“种园子很费功夫。大部分功夫是用心去看。”\n# en: A small square of freshly turned earth behind the greenhouses, a wooden stake with a student's name painted on it, a watering can beside it.\nscene [ae502d] = 温室后面一小块刚翻过的泥土，插着一根写有学生名字的木桩，旁边放着一只水壶。\n# en: Take the plot\nopt.left.label [a249fc] = 接下这块地\n# en: You spend a whole weekend clearing rocks and earn a back that complains for a week. By spring, you have lettuces, three violent mandrakes and a strange purple flower nobody can name.\nopt.left.say.1 [dcd8a6] = 你花了整整一个周末清理石头，换来一副抱怨了一星期的腰背。到了春天，你有了生菜、三棵暴躁的曼德拉草，和一朵谁也叫不出名字的奇怪紫花。\n# en: Leave it for someone else\nopt.right.label [43df3f] = 留给别人\n# en: A shy first-year takes it and grows giant pumpkins. You feel oddly proud of them.\nopt.right.say.1 [a9e348] = 一个害羞的一年级生接手了它，种出了巨大的南瓜。你莫名为它们感到骄傲。\n\n@event life_pen_pal\n# en: A Letter From Thornwick\ntitle [64e8f7] = 荆木来信\n# en: A letter arrives from a stranger at Thornwick Academy, the rival school. \"Dear Lumenhold student chosen at random,\" it begins. \"I'm told you're all insufferable. Prove me wrong? T. Reed (illusion, first year, not insufferable).\"\ntext [36cb26] = 一封信来自对手学校荆木学院的一个陌生人。“亲爱的、随机抽中的流明堡学生，”信的开头写道，“听说你们全都让人受不了。能证明我错了吗？T. 里德（幻术，一年级，并非让人受不了）。”\n# en: A letter with a green Thornwick seal open on a desk, beside a reply half-written in Lumenhold blue ink.\nscene [72beee] = 书桌上摊开一封盖着荆木绿色封印的信，旁边是一封用流明堡蓝墨水写了一半的回信。\n# en: Write back\nopt.left.label [ae4ebf] = 回信\n# en: Your reply is four pages long and only slightly insufferable, and it takes the whole evening. The answer arrives within a week, signed \"Tamsin\". You've made a friend in enemy territory.\nopt.left.say.1 [b1f05a] = 你的回信写了四页，只是稍微有点让人受不了，花了一整个晚上。不到一星期回信就到了，署名“塔姆辛”。你在敌方领土交到了一个朋友。\n# en: Ignore it\nopt.right.label [e2ae0c] = 不理它\n# en: You ignore it, and enjoy the feeling of being unbothered for about a day. Some letters, you suspect, don't take no for an answer.\nopt.right.say.1 [a29868] = 你没理它，享受了大约一天的无所谓。你怀疑，有些信是不接受拒绝的。\n\n@event life_three_headed_puppy\n# en: A Very Lost Puppy\ntitle [b1e6d0] = 一只迷路的小狗\n# en: There is a puppy in the dormitory corridor. It has three heads. One is eating a shoe, one is licking the wall, one is howling. It is very lost and, all three heads agree, very hungry.\ntext [01f554] = 宿舍走廊里有一只小狗。它有三个头。一个在啃鞋，一个在舔墙，一个在嚎叫。它彻底迷路了，而且——三个头一致认为——非常饿。\n# en: A puppy with three heads sitting in a corridor, one head chewing a shoe, one licking a wall, one howling mournfully.\nscene [9614f5] = 一只三头小狗坐在走廊里，一个头在嚼鞋子，一个在舔墙，一个在悲伤地嚎叫。\n# en: Feed it and find its owner\nopt.left.label [f395df] = 喂它，然后找它的主人\n# en: You buy it a string of sausages from the kitchens, then follow its owner's frantic shouting to Professor Brask's office. She weeps with relief. The puppy's three heads each lick you once, in strict order.\nopt.left.say.1 [658f91] = 你从厨房给它买了一串香肠，然后循着主人焦急的呼喊找到了布拉斯克教授的办公室。她松了一口气，哭了出来。小狗的三个头严格按顺序各舔了你一下。\n# en: Call Professor Brask\nopt.right.label [a54d9d] = 叫布拉斯克教授来\n# en: Brask arrives at a run with a lead and three biscuits, and explains to you, at length, how to tell which head is in charge. \"Bad boys,\" she says fondly. All three heads look deeply ashamed.\nopt.right.say.1 [8c0fca] = 布拉斯克拿着牵绳和三块饼干一路跑来，还拉着你讲了好半天怎么分辨哪个头说了算。“坏孩子们，”她宠溺地说。三个头看起来都羞愧极了。\n\n@event life_bell_tower\n# en: The Midnight Bell\ntitle [2cdb21] = 午夜钟声\n# en: Tradition: once in your time at Lumenhold, you must ring the old bell at midnight without getting caught. Your dorm has decided tonight is your night.\ntext [97f4cd] = 传统：在流明堡的日子里，你必须有一次在午夜敲响古钟而不被抓到。你的宿舍认定今晚就是你的夜晚。\n# en: A dark bell tower interior, ropes hanging down, a group of students at the bottom daring one of their number to climb.\nscene [33ec20] = 漆黑的钟楼内部，钟绳垂落，一群学生站在底下怂恿其中一人爬上去。\n# en: Climb and ring it\nopt.left.label [5bb0b4] = 爬上去敲钟\n# en: BONG. The whole castle wakes. By the time the prefects reach the tower you're back in bed, innocent and smug.\nopt.left.say.1 [37e43a] = 当——整座城堡都醒了。等级长们赶到钟楼时，你已经回到床上，一脸无辜又得意。\n# en: BONG — and Bram is at the bottom of the stairs when you come down. Detention. But you rang it, and everybody heard.\nopt.left.say.2 [0df3f9] = 当——而你下来时，布拉姆就站在楼梯底下。禁闭。但你敲响了它，所有人都听见了。\n# en: Decline the dare\nopt.right.label [78dc24] = 拒绝这个挑战\n# en: Someone else rings it instead and becomes a legend. You'll always wonder.\nopt.right.say.1 [a936f5] = 别人替你敲响了钟，成了传奇。你会一直好奇的。\n\n@event life_fog_maze\n# en: The Fog Maze\ntitle [a0d483] = 雾中迷宫\n# en: Fog has come up off the lake so thick you can't see your own feet. The path to the greenhouses has become a maze. Somewhere ahead, someone is calling for help, faintly.\ntext [ee15c0] = 湖上升起的雾浓得你连自己的脚都看不见。通往温室的小路变成了一座迷宫。前方某处，有人在微弱地呼救。\n# en: The academy grounds swallowed by thick white fog, hedges looming like walls, a lantern glowing faintly ahead.\nscene [666c24] = 学院的庭院被浓密的白雾吞没，树篱像墙一样隐约耸立，前方一盏灯笼发着微光。\n# en: Follow the voice\nopt.left.label [f27ba5] = 循着声音走\n# en: You find a first-year sitting on a bench, completely lost, sixty feet from the door. You walk them in. They tell everyone you saved their life.\nopt.left.say.1 [2c5755] = 你找到一个坐在长椅上、彻底迷路的一年级生，离门只有六十英尺。你领着对方进去。对方逢人便说你救了自己一命。\n# en: You walk in circles for an hour and end up exactly where you started. The voice was a very clever crow.\nopt.left.say.2 [fc0168] = 你绕了一个钟头的圈子，最后回到了起点。那声音是一只非常聪明的乌鸦。\n# en: Cast a light and wait\nopt.right.label [988921] = 点亮光芒，原地等待\n# en: You raise a floating light and wait. One by one, three lost students find their way to it. You feel like a lighthouse.\nopt.right.say.1 [380b7f] = 你升起一团漂浮的光，静静等待。三个迷路的学生一个接一个地找到了它。你觉得自己像一座灯塔。\n\n@event life_wishing_well\n# en: The Wishing Well\ntitle [8cab57] = 许愿井\n# en: In the old cloister there's a wishing well. The inscription says: \"ONE COIN, ONE WISH, ONE PRICE.\" The bottom glitters with three hundred years of coins.\ntext [0ae5ea] = 古老的回廊里有一口许愿井。铭文写着：“一枚硬币，一个愿望，一个代价。”井底闪烁着三百年来的硬币。\n# en: An old stone well in the corner of a cloister, coins glinting at the bottom, a carved inscription half-worn away.\nscene [9ea5b9] = 回廊角落里一口古老的石井，井底硬币闪闪发光，一段刻字已经磨损了一半。\n# en: Throw in a coin and wish\nopt.left.label [ce57aa] = 投一枚硬币许愿\n# en: You wish for a good month. It is, mostly. Coincidence, probably.\nopt.left.say.1 [4c845e] = 你许愿这个月过得顺利。大体上确实如此。大概是巧合。\n# en: You wish. The well hiccups and spits your coin back out. Apparently, you can't afford that wish.\nopt.left.say.2 [3fc324] = 你许了愿。井打了个嗝，把你的硬币吐了出来。看来这个愿望你付不起。\n# en: Fish out a few old coins\nopt.right.label [dbef4d] = 捞几枚古币出来\n# en: Fifteen crowns' worth of antique coins. The well is silent. Disapprovingly silent.\nopt.right.say.1 [f67aeb] = 价值十五克朗的古董硬币。井沉默着。不以为然地沉默着。\n# en: The well bites. You lose your own purse in it. ONE PRICE indeed.\nopt.right.say.2 [708beb] = 井咬人。你把自己的钱袋掉了进去。果然是“一个代价”。\n\n@event life_borrowed_broom\n# en: The Borrowed Broom\ntitle [c73e99] = 借来的扫帚\n# en: A second-year lends you their broom for an errand. Midway across the lake, you discover why they were so keen to lend it: it is extremely ticklish, and it hates you.\ntext [ca31e2] = 一个二年级生把扫帚借给你去跑腿。飞到湖中央时，你才发现对方为什么那么乐意借给你：它极其怕痒，而且讨厌你。\n# en: A student clinging to a bucking broom high above the castle, its bristles on fire, a smaller student on the ground waving frantically.\nscene [c7a1ed] = 一名学生紧紧抓着一把在城堡上空乱蹦的扫帚，帚尾着了火，地上一个小个子学生在拼命挥手。\n# en: Wrestle it home\nopt.left.label [745cb7] = 硬把它拽回去\n# en: You land in a heap on the lawn, triumphant and windswept. The broom sulks. The second-year owes you a favour.\nopt.left.say.1 [90315b] = 你一头栽在草坪上，得意洋洋、头发凌乱。扫帚生着闷气。那个二年级生欠你一个人情。\n# en: You land in the lake. The broom flies home without you, very pleased with itself.\nopt.left.say.2 [a5f5be] = 你掉进了湖里。扫帚自己飞回了家，非常得意。\n# en: Soothe it\nopt.right.label [52ec4c] = 安抚它\n# en: You talk to it gently all the way across. It purrs. Brooms shouldn't purr. Now it follows you around.\nopt.right.say.1 [542704] = 你一路温柔地跟它说话。它发出了呼噜声。扫帚不该会呼噜的。现在它到哪儿都跟着你。\n# en: It is not soothed. It is, if anything, more ticklish, and it tips you into the shallows on the way home.\nopt.right.say.2 [69c8e2] = 它没有被安抚。要说有什么变化，那就是更怕痒了，回去的路上还把你甩进了浅水里。\n\n@event life_double_trouble\n# en: Your Double Is Loose\ntitle [0b749f] = 你的分身跑了\n# en: An illusion of you — one you thought you'd dismissed — is still wandering the castle. It has been to three of your classes, flirted with a prefect, and volunteered you for the choir.\ntext [e2deca] = 一个你以为早就解除了的分身幻象，还在城堡里到处游荡。它去上了你的三节课，和一位级长调情，还替你报名了合唱团。\n# en: A student staring in horror across a crowded corridor at a perfect copy of themselves, cheerfully chatting up a professor.\nscene [f7b09f] = 一名学生惊恐地隔着拥挤的走廊望着一个和自己一模一样的复制品，那复制品正兴高采烈地和一位教授攀谈。\n# en: Catch it\nopt.left.label [6ffc79] = 抓住它\n# en: You corner it in a broom cupboard. It looks at you, shrugs and pops like a bubble. The prefect, however, still expects a date.\nopt.left.say.1 [75bb7a] = 你把它堵在扫帚间里。它看着你，耸了耸肩，像泡泡一样“啪”地消失了。不过，那位级长仍然在等着约会。\n# en: It escapes, and spends a week as the most charming version of you. People are disappointed when you come back.\nopt.left.say.2 [4bfacb] = 它溜走了，当了一个星期最有魅力的你。你回来的时候，大家都有点失望。\n# en: Let it run its course\nopt.right.label [0407c9] = 随它去吧\n# en: It attends your lectures and takes better notes than you do. When it fades after a week, you're almost sorry.\nopt.right.say.1 [c344b5] = 它替你上课，笔记记得比你还好。一星期后它消散时，你几乎有点舍不得。\n\n@event life_auction\n# en: The Lost-Property Auction\ntitle [a95e5d] = 失物拍卖会\n# en: Once a year the caretaker auctions off unclaimed lost property. There's a box nobody wants that hums faintly. The bidding starts at eight crowns. Nobody's bidding.\ntext [8cfccd] = 每年一次，管理员会拍卖无人认领的失物。有一个没人要的盒子在微微嗡鸣。起拍价八克朗。没人出价。\n# en: A crowded hall, a caretaker with a gavel at a lectern, tables piled with lost scarves, wands, boots and one suspiciously humming box.\nscene [c3957c] = 拥挤的大厅，管理员手持木槌站在讲台后，桌上堆满了丢失的围巾、魔杖、靴子，还有一个可疑地嗡嗡作响的盒子。\n# en: Bid on the humming box\nopt.left.label [fb0360] = 竞拍那个嗡鸣的盒子\n# en: Inside: a music box that plays a different song every time you open it. Each one makes you think of something new.\nopt.left.say.1 [2acaf5] = 里面是一个音乐盒，每次打开都会放一首不同的曲子。每一首都让你想到新的东西。\n# en: Inside: a very old, very annoyed hiccup curse, which immediately transfers itself to you.\nopt.left.say.2 [04b453] = 里面是一个非常古老、非常恼火的打嗝诅咒，它立刻转移到了你身上。\n# en: Just watch the bidding\nopt.right.label [dc6130] = 只看看拍卖\n# en: Nobody bids on the box. A first-year buys a single left boot for a crown and looks thrilled. You leave with nothing and a good story.\nopt.right.say.1 [f4e0bc] = 没人给那个盒子出价。一个一年级生花一克朗买了一只左脚靴子，看上去高兴极了。你两手空空地离开，带走了一个好故事。\n\n@event life_village_busking\n# en: Busking in the Village\ntitle [2797e6] = 小镇卖艺\n# en: You're short on crowns, and the village square has a fountain, an empty hat and a crowd of shoppers. You do know a few songs.\ntext [f6178e] = 你手头缺克朗，而小镇广场上有一座喷泉、一顶空帽子和一群购物的人。你确实会几首歌。\n# en: A cobbled village square, a student singing beside a fountain with an upturned hat at their feet, villagers pausing to listen.\nscene [96e354] = 铺着鹅卵石的小镇广场，一名学生在喷泉边唱歌，脚边放着一顶翻过来的帽子，镇民们驻足聆听。\n# en: Sing your heart out\nopt.left.label [ebf8e1] = 尽情放声歌唱\n# en: A small crowd gathers. Someone requests an encore. The hat fills with copper and one silver coin from a weeping old man.\nopt.left.say.1 [f60a68] = 一小群人围了过来。有人要求返场。帽子里装满了铜币，还有一位泪流满面的老人投下的一枚银币。\n# en: Three crowns and a sympathetic look from a dog. Busking is harder than it looks.\nopt.left.say.2 [06183f] = 三克朗，外加一条狗同情的眼神。卖艺比看起来难多了。\n# en: Play the street-magic act instead\nopt.right.label [1f6f47] = 改演街头魔术\n# en: You make doves out of the fountain spray. Children scream with joy. The hat overflows.\nopt.right.say.1 [747727] = 你用喷泉的水雾变出鸽子。孩子们开心得尖叫。帽子都装不下了。\n# en: Your doves come out as damp sparrows. People are kind about it.\nopt.right.say.2 [def817] = 你变出来的鸽子成了湿漉漉的麻雀。大家都很包容。\n\n@event life_moths\n# en: A Storm of Moths\ntitle [34c527] = 飞蛾风暴\n# en: You open your door to find your room full of moths — thousands of them, pale as moonlight, circling your candle in silent spirals. It's eerie. It's also, you can't help noticing, beautiful.\ntext [b3079c] = 你推开门，发现房间里满是飞蛾——成千上万只，白得像月光，绕着你的蜡烛无声地盘旋。很诡异。你也不得不承认，很美。\n# en: A dormitory room filled with thousands of pale moths circling a single candle, a student standing in the doorway, astonished.\nscene [ba25b5] = 宿舍房间里成千上万只苍白的飞蛾绕着一支蜡烛盘旋，一名学生站在门口，惊呆了。\n# en: Open the window and let them go\nopt.left.label [09f522] = 打开窗放它们走\n# en: They pour out into the night in a silver river. One stays behind, on your pillow, for a week. You name it Gregory.\nopt.left.say.1 [582ae8] = 它们像一条银色的河流涌入夜色。有一只留了下来，在你的枕头上待了一个星期。你给它起名叫格雷戈里。\n# en: Study them\nopt.right.label [3a81ec] = 研究它们\n# en: They're luna moths, migrating — and drawn to something in your room. You find it: a crumb of moonpetal on your sleeve. Mystery solved. Mostly.\nopt.right.say.1 [eb5366] = 它们是迁徙中的月形天蚕蛾——被你房间里的什么东西吸引了过来。你找到了：袖子上一点月瓣花的碎屑。谜团解开了。大体上。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/expansion_people.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/expansion_people.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event lyra_toast\n# en: Lyra Cannot Cook\ntitle [96d00a] = 莱拉不会做饭\n# en: Smoke pours from the kitchenette. Lyra Vaneshade, pyromancy prodigy, is holding a piece of bread that is now mostly charcoal. \"I can incinerate a target at forty paces,\" she says. \"I cannot make toast. Don't. Say. Anything.\"\ntext [0b5742] = 浓烟从小厨房里涌出来。莱拉·凡妮希德，火焰术天才，手里拿着一片如今基本是木炭的面包。“我能在四十步外把目标烧成灰，”她说，“我不会烤面包。别。说。话。”\n# en: A dormitory kitchenette full of smoke, a crimson-haired girl holding a blackened slice of toast at arm's length, a fire-charm still sparking in her other hand.\nscene [60f547] = 宿舍小厨房里满是浓烟，一个红发女孩把一片烤焦的面包举得远远的，另一只手上的火焰咒还在噼啪作响。\n# en: Say absolutely nothing\nopt.left.label [274efb] = 一个字也不说\n# en: You open a window, take the bread, and make two perfect slices with a toasting fork. She eats hers in dignified silence. Then she asks for another.\nopt.left.say.1 [daffed] = 你打开窗，接过面包，用烤叉烤出两片完美的吐司。她庄严地默默吃完了自己那片。然后又要了一片。\n# en: Teach her — gently\nopt.right.label [13a212] = 教她——温柔地\n# en: \"Less fire,\" you say. \"No, less. Less than that.\" It takes eleven slices. On the twelfth, she makes perfect golden toast and looks prouder than after any duel.\nopt.right.say.1 [84dd3f] = “火小一点，”你说，“不，再小。比那还小。”烤了十一片。到第十二片，她烤出了完美的金黄吐司，看起来比赢下任何一场决斗都骄傲。\n\n@event lyra_mother_visit\n# en: Lady Vaneshade\ntitle [6393ec] = 凡妮希德夫人\n# en: Lady Vaneshade has come to inspect the academy, and her daughter. She stops in front of you. \"You must be the one my daughter mentions,\" she says. \"Constantly. I'd like to know why.\"\ntext [b4cf90] = 凡妮希德夫人来视察学院，也来视察她的女儿。她在你面前停下。“你一定就是我女儿提起的那个人，”她说，“一直提。我想知道为什么。”\n# en: A tall woman in crimson and black walking through the courtyard like a queen inspecting troops, Lyra two steps behind her, very straight.\nscene [cce72a] = 一位身着红黑长裙的高个女人像女王检阅军队一样走过庭院，莱拉跟在她身后两步远，站得笔直。\n# en: Answer with perfect manners\nopt.left.label [d8a5a2] = 礼数周全地回答\n# en: You are polite, precise and unimpressed. Lady Vaneshade studies you, then says, \"Hm. Adequate.\" Lyra looks at you like you've slain a dragon.\nopt.left.say.1 [304a6e] = 你礼貌、精准，而且不卑不亢。凡妮希德夫人打量了你一番，说：“嗯。还行。”莱拉看你的眼神就像你屠了一条龙。\n# en: You stumble over a title. Lady Vaneshade's eyebrow says everything. Later, Lyra finds you. \"She does that to everyone,\" she says. \"You lasted longer than most.\"\nopt.left.say.2 [04ac64] = 你把一个头衔说结巴了。凡妮希德夫人的眉毛说明了一切。后来莱拉找到你。“她对谁都这样，”她说，“你撑得比大多数人久。”\n# en: Answer honestly\nopt.right.label [d41011] = 老实回答\n# en: \"Because she's brilliant,\" you say, \"and nobody tells her.\" Lady Vaneshade is silent. Lyra is redder than her hair. The visit ends early.\nopt.right.say.1 [756ede] = “因为她很出色，”你说，“而从没有人告诉她。”凡妮希德夫人沉默了。莱拉的脸比她的头发还红。视察提前结束了。\n\n@event lyra_rival_respect\n# en: Respect, Grudgingly\ntitle [7c61e5] = 不情愿的敬意\n# en: After another draw, Lyra drops onto the edge of the ring beside you. \"You're the only person here who makes me better,\" she says, as if confessing a crime. \"I hate it. I don't want it to stop.\"\ntext [f4816d] = 又一次平局之后，莱拉在你身边的场边坐下。“你是这里唯一能让我变得更强的人，”她说，像在坦白罪行，“我讨厌这样。我又不想它停下。”\n# en: Two rivals sitting on the edge of a scorched practice ring, wands laid down between them, sharing a flask of water.\nscene [da0f66] = 两个对手坐在一片焦黑的练习场边上，魔杖放在两人之间，分享着一壶水。\n# en: \"Then let's keep it a rivalry.\"\nopt.left.label [d4b161] = “那就继续当对手。”\n# en: \"Good,\" she says, relieved. \"Rivalry I understand.\" She shoves you, lightly. You shove her back. The rivalry continues, warmer.\nopt.left.say.1 [ceccda] = “好，”她松了一口气，“对手我懂。”她轻轻推了你一把。你推了回去。竞争还在继续，只是更暖了。\n# en: \"Or we could just be friends.\"\nopt.right.label [f2a2ab] = “或者我们可以只是朋友。”\n# en: She considers the word like an unfamiliar spell. \"Friends,\" she says. \"Fine. But I'm still going to beat you.\" You'd be disappointed if she didn't.\nopt.right.say.1 [988725] = 她像琢磨一个陌生咒语一样琢磨着这个词。“朋友，”她说，“行。但我还是要打败你。”她要是不这么说，你反而会失望。\n\n@event tobias_scarecrow\n# en: The Scarecrow Project\ntitle [2e1da2] = 稻草人计划\n# en: Tobias has built a scarecrow for the greenhouse plots and wants to enchant it to scare crows. \"Just a little life,\" he says. \"Just enough to wave. Nothing creepy.\"\ntext [3fc6d7] = 托比亚斯给温室的菜地做了一个稻草人，想给它施魔法来吓乌鸦。“就一点点生命，”他说，“刚好够它挥手。一点都不吓人。”\n# en: A scarecrow in a straw hat waving its arms at crows in a vegetable patch, a big farm boy and a student watching it proudly.\nscene [9ba2d5] = 菜地里一个戴草帽的稻草人朝乌鸦挥舞手臂，一个高大的农家男孩和一名学生自豪地看着它。\n# en: Help enchant it\nopt.left.label [d67ea8] = 帮忙施法\n# en: The scarecrow waves. It also learns to wave hello, and to tip its hat to the headmistress. The crows are terrified. The first-years adore it.\nopt.left.say.1 [0e53a1] = 稻草人挥手了。它还学会了挥手打招呼，以及向院长脱帽致意。乌鸦们吓坏了。一年级生们爱死它了。\n# en: The scarecrow comes to life, looks at you both, and walks off into the forest. You see it sometimes, at dusk, waving.\nopt.left.say.2 [96ecab] = 稻草人活了过来，看了看你们俩，然后走进了森林。你有时会在黄昏看见它，在挥手。\n# en: Suggest a cat instead\nopt.right.label [0efd5c] = 建议改用一只猫\n# en: You borrow the greenhouse cat. The crows leave immediately. Tobias is a little sad about the scarecrow, so you help him make it a hat.\nopt.right.say.1 [481aac] = 你借来了温室的猫。乌鸦立刻飞走了。托比亚斯为稻草人有点难过，于是你帮他给它做了一顶帽子。\n\n@event tobias_dance_lessons\n# en: Two Left Feet\ntitle [ed37de] = 两只左脚\n# en: Tobias has pushed the desks against the walls of an empty classroom. \"The Ball's {month == 4: tonight|next month},\" he says, mortified. \"I've been practising with a broom. The broom has complained.\"\ntext [71b9e3] = 托比亚斯把一间空教室的桌子都推到了墙边。“舞会{month == 4: 就在今晚|就在下个月}，”他窘得要命，“我一直在和一把扫帚练习。扫帚投诉了。”\n# en: An empty classroom with desks pushed to the walls, a big boy counting steps aloud, a broom propped against the wall as his former partner.\nscene [592891] = 一间空教室，桌子推到墙边，一个高大的男孩大声数着舞步，一把扫帚靠在墙边——他的前舞伴。\n# en: Teach him to waltz\nopt.left.label [97e7a3] = 教他跳华尔兹\n# en: One-two-three, one-two-three. He steps on you exactly fifty times. On the fifty-first bar, something clicks, and for a moment he's graceful. He looks astonished.\nopt.left.say.1 [4f58a0] = 一二三，一二三。他不多不少踩了你五十脚。到第五十一个小节，突然开了窍，有那么一会儿，他跳得很优雅。他自己都惊呆了。\n# en: Tell him nobody cares about footwork\nopt.right.label [22afe9] = 告诉他没人在乎舞步\n# en: \"Just enjoy it,\" you say. He thinks about this, nods, and dances like a delighted bear. It's terrible. It's wonderful.\nopt.right.say.1 [4eca0a] = “开心就好，”你说。他想了想，点点头，然后像一头快乐的熊一样跳了起来。跳得糟透了。也美妙极了。\n\n@event tobias_storm_greenhouse\n# en: Storm Over the Greenhouses\ntitle [3fabc5] = 温室上空的风暴\n# en: A storm hits at midnight. Glass is cracking in the greenhouses. Tobias is out there in his nightshirt, trying to hold a door shut against the wind with his whole body. \"The moonflowers!\" he shouts. \"Eleven years!\"\ntext [fb15f6] = 午夜，暴风雨来袭。温室的玻璃在开裂。托比亚斯穿着睡衣站在外面，用整个身体顶着门不让风吹开。“月光花！”他大喊，“十一年了！”\n# en: Rain lashing a glass greenhouse at night, panes cracking, a big boy holding a door shut against the wind while plants rattle inside.\nscene [f9b9b9] = 夜里，暴雨抽打着玻璃温室，窗格开裂，一个高大的男孩顶着狂风抵住门，里面的植物哗哗作响。\n# en: Run out and help\nopt.left.label [007212] = 冲出去帮忙\n# en: You hold the panes together with every shielding charm you know until dawn. Every moonflower survives. Tobias hugs you, soaked, laughing, half crying.\nopt.left.say.1 [d599ab] = 你用你会的每一个护盾咒把玻璃撑住，一直撑到黎明。每一朵月光花都活了下来。托比亚斯浑身湿透地抱住你，又笑又像要哭。\n# en: Fetch Old Maud\nopt.right.label [263929] = 去叫老莫德\n# en: Maud arrives in her dressing gown, mutters three words at the sky, and the wind politely goes elsewhere. Tobias stares at her. \"Teach me that,\" he says.\nopt.right.say.1 [efe395] = 莫德穿着睡袍赶来，对着天空嘟囔了三个词，风就礼貌地去了别处。托比亚斯盯着她。“教我这个，”他说。\n\n@event seraphine_lost_day\n# en: Seraphine Is Tomorrow\ntitle [d55aad] = 瑟拉芬活在明天\n# en: Seraphine is sitting in an empty classroom, waiting for a lesson that's happening tomorrow. \"I've slipped,\" she says calmly. \"I'm a day ahead. It happens. I need someone to remind me what today is.\"\ntext [2839c8] = 瑟拉芬坐在一间空教室里，等一堂明天才上的课。“我滑过头了，”她平静地说，“我提前了一天。这种事会发生。我需要有人提醒我今天是哪天。”\n# en: A girl sitting at the wrong desk in an empty classroom, a teacup in her hands, the calendar on the wall showing the next day's date.\nscene [5de877] = 一个女孩坐在空教室里不该坐的座位上，手捧茶杯，墙上的日历显示着第二天的日期。\n# en: Talk her back into today\nopt.left.label [cd1172] = 把她拉回今天\n# en: You tell her what you had for breakfast, what the weather's like, what Thorne said this morning. Slowly her eyes stop looking through you. \"Oh,\" she says. \"Hello. Today.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [de7259] = 你告诉她你早饭吃了什么、天气怎么样、索恩今早说了什么。她的目光慢慢不再穿过你。“哦，”她说，“你好。今天。”\n# en: Ask her what tomorrow holds\nopt.right.label [b4a8a2] = 问她明天会发生什么\n# en: \"There's a surprise quiz in every class,\" she says. \"They planned it together. Question three is always a trick.\" It is, every time. You get them all right.\nopt.right.say.1 [117549] = “每门课都会有突击小测，”她说，“他们串通好的。第三题永远是陷阱。”每次都是。你全答对了。\n# en: \"It rains,\" she says. \"You get wet. You'll be fine.\" You are not fine. You get very wet.\nopt.right.say.2 [111bc0] = “会下雨，”她说，“你会淋湿。你会没事的。”你并没事。你淋得透湿。\n\n@event seraphine_painting\n# en: A Painting of Somewhere Else\ntitle [e371b7] = 别处的风景\n# en: Seraphine has painted a small cottage by the sea, with a crooked chimney and a blue door. \"It's yours,\" she says. \"In twelve years. I thought you should see it now. In case you want a different door.\"\ntext [63e289] = 瑟拉芬画了一座海边的小屋，烟囱歪歪的，门是蓝色的。“这是你的，”她说，“十二年后。我觉得你应该现在就看看。万一你想要一扇不一样的门。”\n# en: A small canvas on an easel showing a stranger's cottage by the sea, a girl with paint on her fingers watching someone look at it.\nscene [88e8e6] = 画架上一幅小画布，画着一座陌生的海边小屋，一个手指沾着颜料的女孩看着别人端详它。\n# en: Keep the painting\nopt.left.label [93b1f9] = 收下这幅画\n# en: You hang it over your bed. Some nights you swear the smoke from the chimney moves. You decide, privately, that you like the blue door.\nopt.left.say.1 [c384bc] = 你把它挂在床头。有些夜晚，你发誓烟囱里的烟在动。你暗自决定，你喜欢那扇蓝色的门。\n# en: You hang it over your bed. Some mornings the door is a different colour, and you lie there wondering which one you chose.\nopt.left.say.2 [7a0c78] = 你把它挂在床头。有些早晨，那扇门换了颜色，你躺在床上琢磨自己选的是哪一扇。\n# en: Ask her to paint her own future\nopt.right.label [ab2144] = 请她画画自己的未来\n# en: She looks at the blank canvas for a long time. \"I never can,\" she says. \"Seers can't see themselves.\" Then, quietly: \"Unless someone's standing next to us.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [685acd] = 她对着空白的画布看了很久。“我画不出来，”她说，“先知看不见自己。”然后，轻声地：“除非有人站在我们身边。”\n\n@event seraphine_mother\n# en: The Oracle's Summons\ntitle [ef26c8] = 神谕的召唤\n# en: Seraphine's mother, the Oracle of Vell, wants her home to take up the family seat. \"I'd spend my life telling kings what they want to hear,\" Seraphine says. \"But she's already seen me say yes. She always sees me say yes.\"\ntext [bdd88c] = 瑟拉芬的母亲——维尔神谕——要她回家继承家族的席位。“我这辈子都得对国王们说他们想听的话，”瑟拉芬说，“可她已经看见我答应了。她总是看见我答应。”\n# en: A girl holding a letter that glows faintly silver, standing at the top of a tower stair, looking down as if about to fall or fly.\nscene [02abdd] = 一个女孩站在塔楼楼梯顶端，手握一封泛着微弱银光的信，俯视下方，仿佛就要坠落或飞起。\n# en: \"Then surprise her.\"\nopt.left.label [7766b6] = “那就给她个惊喜。”\n# en: Seraphine writes back: no. For a whole day she's terrified, and so are you. Then she's giddy. \"I didn't see that coming,\" she keeps saying. \"I didn't see it coming!\"\nopt.left.say.1 [913a47] = 瑟拉芬回信：不。整整一天她都吓坏了，你也一样。然后她兴奋得晕乎乎的。“我没预见到，”她一遍遍地说，“我没预见到！”\n# en: \"Maybe she's right.\"\nopt.right.label [0cb3e9] = “也许她是对的。”\n# en: She nods slowly. She doesn't go — not yet. But for a long while she stops telling you her visions, and you miss them more than you expected.\nopt.right.say.1 [7f4282] = 她慢慢点了点头。她没有回去——暂时还没有。但有很长一段时间，她不再告诉你她的预见了，而你比自己想象的更想念那些预见。\n\n@event corvin_confiscation_vault\n# en: The Confiscation Vault\ntitle [404008] = 没收品库\n# en: \"The confiscation vault,\" Corvin breathes. \"Three hundred years of everything the faculty ever took off a student. My grandfather's lucky deck is in there. I just want it back. Lookout?\"\ntext [724ca5] = “没收品库，”科尔文屏息道，“三百年来教职员从学生手里没收的所有东西。我祖父的幸运纸牌就在里面。我只想把它拿回来。帮我望风？”\n# en: A dark vault crammed with confiscated student contraband — fanged frisbees, cursed dice, a goblet of endless soup — two figures with a lantern at the door.\nscene [efcf9c] = 一间塞满学生违禁品的黑暗库房——长着獠牙的飞盘、被诅咒的骰子、一只永远盛满汤的高脚杯——门口两个提着灯笼的身影。\n# en: Keep lookout\nopt.left.label [89becd] = 帮他望风\n# en: He comes out with the deck and a handful of confiscated coins \"for your trouble\". The deck is, it turns out, genuinely lucky. He wins at cards for a month.\nopt.left.say.1 [2b5759] = 他拿着纸牌出来了，还有一把没收来的硬币，“算你的辛苦费”。结果那副牌真的很幸运。他打牌赢了一个月。\n# en: The vault alarm is a very loud goblin. You both spend a week cataloguing contraband — which Corvin considers a victory.\nopt.left.say.2 [4c5953] = 库房的警报是一只嗓门非常大的地精。你们俩花了一星期给违禁品编目——科尔文认为这算胜利。\n# en: Talk him out of it\nopt.right.label [3af407] = 劝他打消念头\n# en: You convince him to just ask Thorne. Thorne, astonishingly, gives it back. \"Your grandfather cheated me with it in '41,\" he says. \"Tell him I said hello.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [df43d8] = 你说服他直接去问索恩。令人震惊的是，索恩把牌还给了他。“你祖父四一年用它出老千坑过我，”他说，“替我向他问好。”\n# en: He goes anyway, alone. He comes back with the deck and a black eye from the goblin. Worth it, he says.\nopt.right.say.2 [8846c7] = 他还是去了，一个人。他带着纸牌和被地精打出的一只黑眼圈回来了。他说值了。\n\n@event corvin_brother\n# en: Corvin's Brother\ntitle [b626d0] = 科尔文的哥哥\n# en: There's a rough-looking man at the gates, and Corvin has gone very still. \"My brother,\" he says. \"He wants money. He always wants money. If I don't pay, he tells the headmistress where I learned to pick locks.\"\ntext [45c115] = 校门口站着一个看起来很粗野的男人，科尔文整个人僵住了。“我哥哥，”他说，“他要钱。他永远都要钱。我要是不给，他就去告诉院长我是在哪儿学会撬锁的。”\n# en: A rough-looking young man leaning on the academy gate, cap pulled low, Corvin facing him with his hands in his pockets.\nscene [a1863b] = 一个看起来粗野的年轻人倚在学院大门上，帽子压得很低，科尔文双手插兜面对着他。\n# en: Help Corvin face him down\nopt.left.label [cb6fb2] = 帮科尔文硬顶回去\n# en: You stand beside Corvin while he says no — the first time, he tells you later, that he's ever said it. His brother leaves. Corvin sits down on the steps and laughs and shakes.\nopt.left.say.1 [8f14e6] = 你站在科尔文身边，听他说“不”——他后来告诉你，这是他第一次说出这个字。他哥哥走了。科尔文在台阶上坐下，笑着，浑身发抖。\n# en: It turns ugly. In the end you empty your purse into his hand, just to make him leave. Corvin presses every coin back into yours that evening, with a strange new seriousness.\nopt.left.say.2 [27a527] = 场面变得很难看。最后你把钱袋倒空塞给他，只为让他走人。当天晚上，科尔文把每一枚硬币都塞回了你手里，带着一种陌生的郑重。\n# en: Stay out of family business\nopt.right.label [fef823] = 不掺和别人的家事\n# en: Corvin pays. You see him counting out every coin he has. He's quiet for a long while after.\nopt.right.say.1 [9f72cb] = 科尔文付了钱。你看见他把身上每一枚硬币都数了出来。之后他沉默了很久。\n\n@event corvin_cards\n# en: How to Cheat at Cards\ntitle [1de29d] = 怎样打牌出老千\n# en: \"Rule one: never cheat,\" says Corvin, shuffling so fast the cards blur. \"Rule two: if you're going to cheat, never get caught. Rule three: I'm about to teach you how they cheat, so you never lose to them.\"\ntext [e191e5] = “规则一：永远不要出千，”科尔文说着洗牌，快得纸牌都成了虚影，“规则二：如果要出千，永远别被抓到。规则三：我现在要教你他们是怎么出千的，这样你就永远不会输给他们。”\n# en: A candlelit table in a corner of the common room, a boy fanning a deck of cards with a flourish, a student watching his hands very closely.\nscene [373538] = 公共休息室角落一张烛光摇曳的桌子，一个男孩花哨地把一副牌摊成扇形，一名学生紧盯着他的手。\n# en: Learn the tricks\nopt.left.label [f47312] = 学会这些把戏\n# en: By midnight you can spot a marked card, a bottom deal and a mirror charm. You will never be fleeced at Wizard's Hand again.\nopt.left.say.1 [4eb4c5] = 到午夜，你已经能识破记号牌、底牌发牌和镜像咒。你再也不会在“巫师之手”牌局上被人宰了。\n# en: Learn to play honestly — and well\nopt.right.label [e8fdeb] = 学会诚实地打——还要打得好\n# en: He teaches you odds and nerve instead. That weekend you win eight crowns at the common-room game without cheating. Corvin is weirdly proud.\nopt.right.say.1 [ff72c2] = 他转而教你算概率和稳住心态。那个周末，你在休息室牌局上一把千都没出就赢了八克朗。科尔文骄傲得有点莫名其妙。\n# en: He teaches you odds and nerve instead. That weekend the odds take three crowns off you. \"Also a lesson,\" says Corvin.\nopt.right.say.2 [27b9a8] = 他改教你概率和胆量。那个周末，概率从你身上拿走了三克朗。“这也是一课，”科尔文说。\n\n@event mir_ornithopter\n# en: The Test Flight\ntitle [5ac4c6] = 试飞\n# en: Mir has built a flying machine with canvas wings and seven propellers. It is balanced on the edge of the north tower. \"Brooms are so last century,\" they say, strapping in. \"I need someone on the rope. Probably. Possibly.\"\ntext [800a69] = 米尔造了一台飞行器，有帆布翅膀和七个螺旋桨，正架在北塔的边缘。“扫帚太上个世纪了，”TA一边系安全带一边说，“我需要有人拉绳子。大概吧。也许。”\n# en: A rickety winged contraption balanced on the edge of a tower roof, a goggled artificer strapping into it, a friend holding a rope.\nscene [846151] = 一台摇摇晃晃的带翼装置架在塔顶边缘，一个戴护目镜的奇械师正把自己绑进去，一个朋友抓着一根绳子。\n# en: Hold the rope\nopt.left.label [710f01] = 拉住绳子\n# en: The machine flies — wobbly, loud, glorious — twice around the tower before landing in the lake. Mir surfaces whooping. You're both legends by dinner.\nopt.left.say.1 [15a88f] = 机器飞起来了——摇摇晃晃、吵吵闹闹、辉煌灿烂——绕塔飞了两圈，然后落进了湖里。米尔欢呼着冒出水面。到晚饭时，你们俩都成了传奇。\n# en: The rope wins. You are dragged eight feet along the roof before letting go. The machine lands in a tree. Mir waves from it, delighted.\nopt.left.say.2 [a4ec4a] = 绳子赢了。你被拖着在屋顶上滑了八英尺才松手。机器落在了一棵树上。米尔在树上开心地挥手。\n# en: Insist on a safety net\nopt.right.label [31e7de] = 坚持要装安全网\n# en: You spend a day building a net. The machine flies four feet and drops into it. \"Data!\" says Mir happily. Nobody dies. It's a good day.\nopt.right.say.1 [8acffd] = 你花了一天搭网。机器飞了四英尺，掉进了网里。“数据！”米尔开心地说。没人丧命。是美好的一天。\n\n@event mir_lab_fire\n# en: Fire in the Workshop\ntitle [d8f498] = 工坊失火\n# en: The workshop is on fire. Properly this time. Mir is in the doorway, coughing — and trying to go back in. \"My notebooks! Three years of notes!\"\ntext [b4fc7c] = 工坊着火了。这次是真的着火了。米尔站在门口咳嗽着——还想往里冲。“我的笔记本！三年的笔记！”\n# en: A workshop full of smoke and orange light, notebooks scattered across a burning bench, a figure in goggles coughing in the doorway.\nscene [1abf1a] = 满是浓烟和橙色火光的工坊，笔记本散落在燃烧的工作台上，一个戴护目镜的身影在门口咳嗽。\n# en: Grab the notebooks\nopt.left.label [6162da] = 去抢笔记本\n# en: You come out singed, smoking, with an armful of notebooks. Mir stares at you, then at the notebooks, then bursts into tears and hugs you, both of you coughing.\nopt.left.say.1 [5d9fd7] = 你出来时衣服烧焦、浑身冒烟，怀里抱着一摞笔记本。米尔盯着你，又盯着笔记本，然后哭着抱住了你，你们俩都在咳嗽。\n# en: You get halfway before the smoke drives you back. You save one notebook. Mir says it's the most important one. You both know it isn't. They mean it anyway.\nopt.left.say.2 [338a3e] = 你冲到一半就被浓烟逼了回来。你救出了一本。米尔说那是最重要的一本。你们都知道不是。但TA是真心的。\n# en: Drag Mir away\nopt.right.label [9bf39b] = 把米尔拖走\n# en: You haul Mir back from the door. The notebooks burn. Mir is silent for a week. Then they start a new notebook. The first page just says: \"Thank you.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [1a7747] = 你把米尔从门口拽了回来。笔记本烧光了。米尔沉默了一个星期。然后TA开始了一本新笔记。第一页只写着：“谢谢你。”\n\n@event mir_name_day\n# en: Mir's Name Day\ntitle [fc2a16] = 米尔的命名日\n# en: It's Mir's name day. They mention it casually, as if it doesn't matter. It clearly matters. Nobody else seems to know.\ntext [e4d87b] = 今天是米尔的命名日。TA随口提了一句，好像无所谓似的。显然很有所谓。其他人似乎都不知道。\n# en: A cluttered workshop decorated with paper chains and one small cake with a single gear-shaped candle, an artificer looking embarrassed and pleased.\nscene [7e06f4] = 一间杂乱的工坊挂着纸链装饰，一个小蛋糕上插着一根齿轮形状的蜡烛，奇械师看起来又难为情又开心。\n# en: Throw a surprise party\nopt.left.label [7bc69f] = 办一场惊喜派对\n# en: Six friends, one cake and a banner you painted badly. Mir walks in, stops, and has to sit down. \"Nobody's ever...\" they start, and can't finish.\nopt.left.say.1 [a115e8] = 六个朋友、一个蛋糕，还有一条你画得很烂的横幅。米尔走进来，愣住了，不得不坐下。“从来没有人……”TA开口，却说不下去。\n# en: Give them a handmade gift\nopt.right.label [36112f] = 送TA一件手工礼物\n# en: A little brass key that fits nothing. \"For whatever you build next,\" you say. Mir wears it on a chain, every day, for years.\nopt.right.say.1 [400f81] = 一把什么锁也开不了的小铜钥匙。“送给你接下来要造的东西，”你说。米尔把它挂在链子上，天天戴着，戴了好多年。\n\n@event nessa_headmistress_interview\n# en: The Impossible Interview\ntitle [4748ca] = 不可能的采访\n# en: \"Nobody has interviewed the headmistress in twenty years,\" Nessa says. \"But she likes you. Probably. Get me in the door and I'll name my firstborn after you.\"\ntext [f4a9d2] = “二十年来没人采访过院长，”内莎说，“但她喜欢你。大概吧。帮我进那扇门，我就用你的名字给我的头一个孩子取名。”\n# en: A newspaper editor and a student waiting outside the headmistress's tower door, the editor clutching a list of questions three pages long.\nscene [af2317] = 一位报社编辑和一名学生等在院长塔楼门外，编辑手里攥着一张三页长的问题清单。\n# en: Ask the headmistress\nopt.left.label [733de3] = 去问院长\n# en: The headmistress agrees to ten minutes. She gives Nessa forty, and gives you a look that says you have spent a favour. The interview runs on the front page and sells out three printings.\nopt.left.say.1 [825648] = 院长答应给十分钟。结果给了内莎四十分钟，还给了你一个眼神，意思是你已经用掉了一次人情。采访登上头版，加印了三次都卖光了。\n# en: \"No,\" says the headmistress, pleasantly. \"But tell Miss Quill her piece on the kitchens was excellent.\" Nessa frames the rejection. The headmistress is a shade cooler with you in the corridors after that.\nopt.left.say.2 [0b6722] = “不行，”院长和颜悦色地说，“不过请转告奎尔小姐，她写厨房的那篇非常出色。”内莎把这次拒绝裱了起来。从那以后，院长在走廊里对你冷淡了几分。\n# en: Suggest someone overlooked instead\nopt.right.label [d4c0f5] = 建议改采访一位被忽视的人\n# en: Nessa interviews the night porter instead. It's the most-read piece of the year. Half the academy cries over breakfast.\nopt.right.say.1 [c59390] = 内莎改为采访了守夜的门房。那成了全年阅读量最高的文章。半个学院的人在早餐时哭了。\n\n@event nessa_typo\n# en: A Catastrophic Typo\ntitle [f9820b] = 灾难性的错字\n# en: The Ledger has gone to print. In the profile of Professor Thorne, a single wrong letter has turned \"Thorne: Still Lively\" into \"Thorne: Still Lovely\". By breakfast the whole academy will be calling the most frightening man in the castle lovely. Five hundred copies.\ntext [8e82c5] = 《流明纪事报》已经付印。在索恩教授的人物专访里，一个错字把“索恩：依然矍铄”变成了“索恩：依然可爱”。到早饭时，全学院都会管城堡里最吓人的那个人叫“可爱”了。五百份。\n# en: A newspaper with a single word circled in red ink, an editor with her head in her hands, a stack of papers still warm from the press.\nscene [224cf5] = 一份报纸上有一个词被红墨水圈了出来，编辑双手抱头，一摞刚印好的报纸还带着余温。\n# en: Help her fix every copy overnight\nopt.left.label [5d1986] = 帮她连夜改好每一份\n# en: You correct five hundred copies with a scraping charm and a lot of tea. At dawn, Nessa falls asleep on the press. Thorne never knows.\nopt.left.say.1 [63580e] = 你用刮除咒和大量的茶改好了五百份。黎明时，内莎趴在印刷机上睡着了。索恩永远不会知道。\n# en: Tell her to run a correction and own it\nopt.right.label [f52406] = 让她登更正启事，坦然认错\n# en: The correction is gracious. Thorne, astonishingly, cuts it out and pins it to his door. \"Precision,\" he says in class, \"is a virtue. So is apology.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [02e473] = 更正启事写得很得体。令人震惊的是，索恩把它剪下来钉在了自己门上。“精确，”他在课上说，“是一种美德。道歉也是。”\n\n@event nessa_burnout\n# en: The Editor Sleeps\ntitle [7126ab] = 编辑睡着了\n# en: Nessa hasn't slept properly in a week. She's asleep at her desk now, and her quill — unsupervised — is still writing next week's paper. It has started inventing news.\ntext [b075ee] = 内莎已经一个星期没好好睡觉了。此刻她趴在桌上睡着了，而她的羽毛笔——无人看管——还在写下周的报纸。它已经开始编造新闻了。\n# en: A girl asleep at a desk in a newspaper office, her self-writing quill still scratching on its own, the candle burned down to a stub.\nscene [6ef002] = 报社办公室里一个女孩趴在桌上睡着了，她的自动羽毛笔还在自己沙沙书写，蜡烛已经烧成了一小截。\n# en: Put her to bed and run the paper yourself\nopt.left.label [9b9579] = 送她上床，你来办报\n# en: You edit, typeset and print one issue, and miss a day of classes doing it. It's adequate. When Nessa wakes she reads it, winces at your headlines, and hugs you for a long time.\nopt.left.say.1 [784bde] = 你编辑、排版、印出了一期，为此缺了一天的课。还过得去。内莎醒来读了，对你起的标题皱了皱眉，然后抱了你很久。\n# en: Wake her and make her rest\nopt.right.label [c44266] = 叫醒她，逼她休息\n# en: She argues for ten minutes, then sleeps for fourteen hours. The quill's invented news about a dragon in the kitchens turns out to be true.\nopt.right.say.1 [46fb0e] = 她争了十分钟，然后睡了十四个小时。羽毛笔编造的“厨房里有条龙”的新闻，结果是真的。\n\n@event kael_northern_lights\n# en: The Lights of the North\ntitle [8eda52] = 北方之光\n# en: Kael wakes you at midnight. \"Come,\" he says. On the hill above the lake, the sky is moving — green and violet ribbons, the lights of his home, here, for him. \"I called them,\" he says. \"I wanted you to see.\"\ntext [874ab7] = 凯尔在午夜叫醒了你。“来，”他说。湖上方的山坡上，天空在流动——绿色与紫色的光带，那是他家乡的极光，在这里，为他而来。“是我召来的，”他说，“我想让你看看。”\n# en: Two figures on a frozen hillside under ribbons of green and violet light, one pointing upward, frost forming a circle around them.\nscene [e2a410] = 两个身影站在结冰的山坡上，头顶是绿紫色的光带，其中一人指向天空，霜在他们周围结成一个圆。\n# en: Watch in silence\nopt.left.label [3bfadc] = 静静地看\n# en: You watch until the lights fade. Neither of you speaks. On the walk down, he takes your hand without seeming to notice he's done it.\nopt.left.say.1 [f93df4] = 你们一直看到极光消散。谁也没说话。下山时，他牵起了你的手，似乎自己都没察觉。\n# en: Ask him about home\nopt.right.label [946458] = 问他关于家乡的事\n# en: He tells you about ice palaces and singing glaciers and a mother who never once said she was proud. Then he says: \"This place is more home now.\" He means the hill. He means you.\nopt.right.say.1 [d63dfe] = 他给你讲冰之宫殿、会唱歌的冰川，还有一位从没说过一次为他骄傲的母亲。然后他说：“现在这里更像家了。”他说的是这座山坡。他说的是你。\n\n@event kael_fever\n# en: Kael Burns\ntitle [19ecba] = 凯尔在发烧\n# en: Kael has a fever — and for someone made of frost, a fever is dangerous. The infirmary's warming charms are making it worse. Sister Amaranth looks frightened. \"He needs cold,\" she says. \"Real cold. I don't know where to get it.\"\ntext [0e9394] = 凯尔发烧了——对一个由霜构成的人来说，发烧很危险。医务室的温暖咒只会让情况更糟。阿玛兰斯修女看起来很害怕。“他需要寒冷，”她说，“真正的寒冷。我不知道去哪儿弄。”\n# en: A pale boy in a narrow bed, sweating, frost forming and melting on the pillow around his head in a constant cycle.\nscene [44bb31] = 一张窄床上躺着一个苍白的男孩，满身是汗，他头边枕头上的霜不断凝结又融化。\n# en: Bring him snow from the mountain\nopt.left.label [395e96] = 去山上给他取雪\n# en: You fly to the peaks on a borrowed broom and come back with a sack of ancient snow. By morning his fever breaks. He doesn't let go of your sleeve all day.\nopt.left.say.1 [9f1fbf] = 你骑着借来的扫帚飞上山巅，带回一麻袋远古积雪。到早上，他的烧退了。他一整天都没松开你的袖子。\n# en: The mountain wins. You come back half-frozen and empty-handed. Kael recovers anyway, slowly — and visits you in the next bed.\nopt.left.say.2 [c8178a] = 大山赢了。你冻得半死、两手空空地回来。凯尔还是慢慢好了起来——还去隔壁病床探望了你。\n# en: Send word to Ilse\nopt.right.label [18fc37] = 给伊尔泽捎信\n# en: Ilse arrives in a whirl of snow, puts her hand on his forehead and says something sharp in Northern. The fever breaks. Kael stares at his sister for a long time.\nopt.right.say.1 [8cf203] = 伊尔泽裹着一阵飞雪赶到，把手放在他额头上，用北境语厉声说了句什么。烧退了。凯尔盯着他的妹妹看了很久。\n# en: You can't find her. Kael recovers slowly, on his own, the way he's always done everything.\nopt.right.say.2 [efbddf] = 你找不到她。凯尔靠自己慢慢好了起来，就像他一向独自扛下所有事那样。\n\n@event kael_letter_home\n# en: A Letter He Won't Send\ntitle [ff94b6] = 一封他不会寄出的信\n# en: Kael is writing a letter. He's rewritten the first line eleven times. \"To my mother,\" he says. \"I'm telling her I won't come home. I won't open the door. I'm telling her I've found...\" He stops.\ntext [71254e] = 凯尔在写信。第一行他已经重写了十一遍。“写给我母亲，”他说，“告诉她我不会回家了。我不会打开那扇门。告诉她我找到了……”他停住了。\n# en: A boy at a library desk, a letter half-written in sharp Northern script, a candle beside it, ink frozen in the pen.\nscene [fc0e7c] = 一个男孩坐在图书馆的书桌前，一封用凌厉的北境字体写了一半的信，旁边一支蜡烛，笔里的墨水冻住了。\n# en: \"Found what?\"\nopt.left.label [10295e] = “找到了什么？”\n# en: He looks at you for a long time. Then he writes the last word of the sentence without showing you, seals it, and sends it. He's lighter for days.\nopt.left.say.1 [cdad8b] = 他看了你很久。然后他没让你看，写下了那句话的最后一个词，封好信，寄了出去。之后好几天他都轻松了许多。\n# en: Help him find the words\nopt.right.label [a0a115] = 帮他找到合适的话\n# en: You sit together until the candle's gone. The letter, when it's finished, is short. It doesn't apologise once. He sends it at dawn.\nopt.right.say.1 [c25f80] = 你们一起坐到蜡烛燃尽。写完的信很短，一次也没道歉。他在黎明时寄了出去。\n\n@event bram_father_visit\n# en: Bram's Father\ntitle [26834d] = 布拉姆的父亲\n# en: A tall man in an old head-boy's sash is speaking to Bram in the entrance hall, quietly and terribly. Bram has shrunk two sizes. \"...never amount to anything,\" the man is saying.\ntext [1a56a1] = 一个披着旧式男生代表绶带的高个男人在门厅里和布拉姆说话，声音很轻，却很可怕。布拉姆缩小了两圈。“……永远不会有出息，”那人说。\n# en: A towering grey-haired man in an old head-boy's sash speaking down to a red-faced prefect in the entrance hall, students hurrying past pretending not to look.\nscene [d8766d] = 门厅里，一个身材高大、头发灰白、披着旧式男生代表绶带的男人居高临下地对一个满脸通红的级长说话，学生们匆匆走过，假装没在看。\n# en: Interrupt — say something good\nopt.left.label [720c7c] = 插话——说些好话\n# en: You tell his father {seen(bram_turns): about the night at the boathouse|what Bram is like when nobody's keeping score}. The man falls silent. Bram looks at you like you've handed him a sword.\nopt.left.say.1 [f142e1] = 你跟他父亲讲了{seen(bram_turns): 船屋那晚的事|没人记分的时候布拉姆是什么样的人}。那人沉默了。布拉姆看你的眼神，就像你递给了他一把剑。\n# en: His father looks through you as if you're furniture. But afterwards, Bram finds you and says, roughly, \"Nobody's ever stood there before. Thanks.\"\nopt.left.say.2 [bb9dcb] = 他父亲看你就像看一件家具。但事后布拉姆找到你，粗声粗气地说：“以前从没有人站在那儿过。谢了。”\n# en: Find Bram afterwards\nopt.right.label [46a529] = 事后去找布拉姆\n# en: He's behind the stables, again. You sit on the bucket next to his. You don't talk. After a while he says, \"Same time next year, probably,\" and almost laughs.\nopt.right.say.1 [7e1535] = 他又在马厩后面。你在他旁边的水桶上坐下。你们不说话。过了一会儿他说：“明年大概还是这个时候，”然后差点笑了出来。\n\n@event bram_cheating\n# en: Bram Cheats\ntitle [cf8d51] = 布拉姆作弊\n# en: In the exam hall, two desks away, you see Bram Hollis — prefect, enforcer of rules — slip a tiny scroll out of his sleeve. He sees you seeing him. His face goes white.\ntext [5bf337] = 考场上，隔着两张桌子，你看见布拉姆·霍利斯——级长、校规的执行者——从袖子里抽出一卷小纸条。他看见你看见了他。他的脸一下子白了。\n# en: A prefect in an exam hall slipping a tiny scroll out of his sleeve, a student two desks away watching.\nscene [6230cf] = 考场里一位级长正从袖子里抽出一卷小纸条，隔两张桌子的一名学生看在眼里。\n# en: Report him\nopt.left.label [794001] = 举报他\n# en: Bram loses his sash for the rest of the year. Some students cheer. You don't feel like cheering. Neither, later, does he.\nopt.left.say.1 [bf4a08] = 布拉姆被摘掉了绶带，直到学年结束。有些学生欢呼。你并不想欢呼。后来，他也一样。\n# en: Say nothing — and let him know you saw\nopt.right.label [5c554c] = 什么也不说——但让他知道你看见了\n# en: After the exam you just look at him. He nods, once. You never mention it. But his demerits stop, for you and for everyone you know.\nopt.right.say.1 [88ddd1] = 考完后你只是看着他。他点了一下头。你从没提起这件事。但他的记过停了——对你，也对你认识的每一个人。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/expansion_social.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/expansion_social.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event soc_lost_notes\n# en: The Borrowed Notes\ntitle [f46501] = 借走的笔记\n# en: {npc} borrowed all your lecture notes last week. {They} {are} now standing in front of you holding a single soggy page. \"There was a stream,\" {they} say{s}. \"And a duck. Mostly a duck.\"\ntext [093f86] = {npc}上星期借走了你所有的课堂笔记。现在{they}站在你面前，手里只剩一页湿透的纸。“有条小溪，”{they}说，“还有一只鸭子。主要是鸭子。”\n# en: A student holding a single soggy page of notes, the rest of the stack floating away down a stream behind them.\nscene [daf951] = 一名学生手里攥着一页湿透的笔记，身后剩下的那叠正顺着小溪漂走。\n# en: Laugh it off\nopt.left.label [29c02a] = 一笑了之\n# en: You rewrite your notes from memory. {npc} brings you a cake every day for a week, by way of apology. The cakes are better than the notes were.\nopt.left.say.1 [37846c] = 你凭记忆重写了笔记。{npc}一连一星期每天给你带一块蛋糕赔罪。蛋糕比笔记还好。\n# en: Make {them} rewrite them\nopt.right.label [53cd0f] = 让{them}重抄一份\n# en: {npc} copies out forty pages from a friend's notes, grumbling. The new notes are neater than yours ever were.\nopt.right.say.1 [a8c805] = {npc}一边抱怨一边从朋友那儿抄了四十页。新笔记比你自己写的还工整。\n\n@event soc_birthday\n# en: A Birthday\ntitle [492d55] = 生日\n# en: {&You find out — by accident — that it's {npc}'s birthday. {They} haven't told anyone. {They} {are} spending it in the library, alone, on purpose.|It's {npc}'s birthday again, and again {they} haven't told anyone. {They} {are} in the library, alone, as if you might have forgotten.}\ntext [8423a8] = {&你偶然发现今天是{npc}的生日。{they}谁也没告诉，正一个人待在图书馆里——故意的。|又到了{npc}的生日，{they}又是谁也没告诉。{they}一个人待在图书馆里，好像你会忘记似的。}\n# en: A small cake with a single candle in a dark common room, a group of students crouched behind a sofa, a surprised face in the doorway.\nscene [85796d] = 漆黑的公共休息室里一个插着一根蜡烛的小蛋糕，一群学生蹲在沙发后面，门口一张惊讶的脸。\n# en: Organise a surprise\nopt.left.label [c1b395] = 策划一场惊喜\n# en: {&Seven people jump out from behind a sofa. {npc} screams, then laughs, then has to sit down. \"How did you know?\" You'll never tell.|This year it's a cake shaped, badly, like the library. {npc} pretends to be annoyed for nearly a minute.}\nopt.left.say.1 [8021f6] = {&七个人从沙发后面跳出来。{npc}尖叫，然后大笑，然后不得不坐下。“你们怎么知道的？”你永远不会说。|今年是一个做成图书馆形状的蛋糕，做得很糟。{npc}假装生气了将近一分钟。}\n# en: Join {them} in the library\nopt.right.label [d7c810] = 去图书馆陪{them}\n# en: {&You sit down opposite {them} with your own book.|You take the same seat as last year, with your own book.} Neither of you says happy birthday. At midnight {they} slide{s} a biscuit across the table, and you both grin.\nopt.right.say.1 [fa64ac] = {&你带着自己的书在{them}对面坐下。|你带着自己的书，坐在和去年一样的位置。}谁也没说生日快乐。午夜时，{they}把一块饼干推过桌面，你们相视一笑。\n\n@event soc_bad_news\n# en: Bad News From Home\ntitle [9ab55c] = 家里来的坏消息\n# en: {npc} is sitting on the stairs with a letter in {their} lap, not moving. {Their} grandmother is ill. The barge home takes two days. \"I don't know whether to go,\" {they} say{s}.\ntext [c5229c] = {npc}坐在楼梯上，膝上放着一封信，一动不动。{their}祖母病了。坐驳船回家要两天。“我不知道该不该回去，”{they}说。\n# en: A student sitting on a stone staircase with a crumpled letter, head bowed, a friend sitting down beside them.\nscene [e888fc] = 一名学生坐在石阶上，手里攥着一封揉皱的信，低着头，一个朋友在旁边坐下。\n# en: \"Go. We'll cover your classes.\"\nopt.left.label [6868e0] = “去吧，课我们帮你顶着。”\n# en: {npc} catches the next barge. Every evening you copy out the day's notes for {them} from {their} classmates. When {npc} comes back, {their} grandmother is better, and {they} hug{s} you on the landing.\nopt.left.say.1 [564714] = {npc}赶上了下一班驳船。每天晚上，你都从{their}同学那里替{them}抄好当天的笔记。{npc}回来时，{their}祖母好些了，{they}在楼梯平台上抱住了你。\n# en: Just sit with {them}\nopt.right.label [75d0fd] = 只是陪{them}坐着\n# en: You sit there until the stairs move under you. You don't fix anything. That's not what {they} needed.\nopt.right.say.1 [439080] = 你们一直坐到楼梯在身下挪动。你什么也没解决。那本来就不是{they}需要的。\n\n@event soc_stargazing\n# en: Counting Stars\ntitle [559b85] = 数星星\n# en: {&{npc} has dragged a blanket up to the roof.|{npc} is on the roof again with the blanket, and a flask this time.|{npc} has found a better spot on the roof, out of the wind, and a better blanket.} \"There's a meteor shower,\" {they} say{s}. \"Supposedly.\" There's room on the blanket for two, just about.\ntext [e2e98e] = {&{npc}把一条毯子拖上了屋顶。|{npc}又带着毯子上了屋顶，这回还带了一只水壶。|{npc}在屋顶上找到了一个更避风的地方，还换了一条更好的毯子。}“有流星雨，”{they}说，“据说。”毯子上刚好够两个人躺。\n# en: Two students lying on their backs on a flat roof, close but not quite touching, a sky full of stars above.\nscene [6f5d93] = 两名学生仰面躺在平屋顶上，挨得很近却又没碰到，头顶是满天繁星。\n# en: Count meteors\nopt.left.label [b22e25] = 数流星\n# en: You count thirty-one. {npc} counts thirty-four and accuses you of not looking properly. You weren't, entirely. You stay until the cold drives you in at dawn.\nopt.left.say.1 [352d0b] = 你数到三十一颗。{npc}数到三十四颗，指责你没认真看。你确实没完全在看。你们一直待到黎明，直到寒气把你们赶回屋里。\n# en: Talk about everything else\nopt.right.label [a3c084] = 聊些别的一切\n# en: You don't see a single meteor. You talk about home and fear and what you'd do if you could do anything. When you look up, the sky's gone grey with dawn.\nopt.right.say.1 [88b087] = 你一颗流星也没看到。你们聊家乡、聊恐惧、聊如果什么都能做会去做什么。等你抬起头，天已经泛起了黎明的灰白。\n\n@event soc_jealous_friend\n# en: Jealousy\ntitle [bace8d] = 嫉妒\n# en: {~{npc} has been odd all week. Finally it comes out.|{npc} has been short with you at every meal. Finally, on the stairs, it comes out.|{npc} cancels on you twice and then turns up at your door. It comes out in a rush.} \"You've got all these new friends now. I just — I don't know where I fit anymore.\"\ntext [56bc6b] = {~{npc}一整个星期都怪怪的。终于说出来了。|{npc}每顿饭都对你爱答不理。终于，在楼梯上，说出来了。|{npc}放了你两次鸽子，然后又出现在你门口。话一股脑儿地涌了出来。}“你现在有那么多新朋友了。我就是——不知道自己还算什么了。”\n# en: A student standing alone at the edge of a laughing group, arms folded, watching someone else take what used to be their seat.\nscene [560bd3] = 一名学生抱着胳膊独自站在一群欢笑的人边上，看着别人坐上了曾经属于自己的位置。\n# en: Reassure {them}\nopt.left.label [c0cad1] = 让{them}安心\n# en: You tell {them} exactly where {they} fit: right here, first. {npc} rolls {their} eyes, but {they}'re smiling. The oddness is gone by dinner.\nopt.left.say.1 [944299] = 你明明白白地告诉{them}：{they}的位置就在这里，排第一。{npc}翻了个白眼，但在笑。晚饭前那股别扭劲就没了。\n# en: Tell {them} friendship isn't a contest\nopt.right.label [147a3c] = 告诉{them}友情不是比赛\n# en: You're right, technically. {npc} knows it. It still lands badly. It takes a few weeks for things to feel easy again. You spend the evenings you'd have spent soothing {them} in the library instead.\nopt.right.say.1 [80b7ce] = 严格来说你是对的。{npc}也知道。但这话还是说得不太好听。过了好几个星期，你们之间才重新自在起来。那些本该用来哄{them}的晚上，你都待在了图书馆。\n\n@event soc_teach_spell\n# en: A Trick Worth Learning\ntitle [8a5e67] = 值得一学的小把戏\n# en: {npc} has a spell {they} invented — a little thing, half-useful, half-ridiculous — and wants to teach it to someone. {&\"It makes your tea exactly the right temperature,\" {they} say{s}. \"Forever.\"|\"It finds the page you were reading,\" {they} say{s}. \"Any book, anywhere.\"|\"It keeps your socks dry,\" {they} say{s}. \"Lake, rain, puddles. Dry.\"}\ntext [be669b] = {npc}有一个自己发明的咒语——一个小东西，一半有用，一半荒唐——想教给别人。{&“它能让你的茶永远保持刚刚好的温度，”{they}说，“永远。”|“它能找到你读到的那一页，”{they}说，“任何书，任何地方。”|“它能让你的袜子保持干燥，”{they}说，“湖水、雨水、水洼，都不怕。”}\n# en: Two students in an empty classroom, one demonstrating a wand movement, the other copying it with a tiny spark of light.\nscene [bfffc4] = 空教室里两名学生，一个在示范魔杖的动作，另一个跟着模仿，杖尖冒出一点小火花。\n# en: Learn it properly\nopt.left.label [9c42bb] = 认真学会\n# en: It takes an hour. {&Your tea is now perfect, always.|You will never lose your page again.|Your socks are dry. Your socks will always be dry.} You'll use this spell more than anything you learn in class.\nopt.left.say.1 [75f1a5] = 花了一个钟头。{&你的茶现在永远是完美的。|你再也不会找不到读到的那一页了。|你的袜子是干的。你的袜子永远都会是干的。}这个咒语你用得会比课上学的任何东西都多。\n# en: {&The first attempt freezes your tea solid, and your eyebrows with it. The second works. Your eyebrows take a day to thaw.|The first attempt finds every page in the library at once. The second works. The librarian is not amused.|The first attempt dries your socks, your shoes and most of your feet. The second works. Your skin flakes for a day.}\nopt.left.say.2 [b46561] = {&第一次尝试把你的茶冻成了冰坨，连眉毛也一起冻上了。第二次成功了。你的眉毛花了一天才解冻。|第一次尝试同时找出了图书馆里的每一页。第二次成功了。图书管理员一点也不觉得好笑。|第一次尝试把你的袜子、鞋子和大半双脚都烘干了。第二次成功了。你的皮肤脱了一天的皮。}\n# en: Teach {them} one of yours in return\nopt.right.label [a24082] = 也教{them}一个你的\n# en: You trade spells like secrets. By midnight you've both got a new trick and an inside joke that will last three years.\nopt.right.say.1 [42f01a] = 你们像交换秘密一样交换咒语。到午夜时，你们都多了一个新把戏，还有一个能笑上三年的内部笑话。\n\n@event soc_secret_admirer\n# en: A Secret Admirer\ntitle [6041b8] = 神秘的仰慕者\n# en: Folded notes keep appearing in your library books. Unsigned. They're funny, and a little nervous, and they know things about you that only someone paying attention would know. The handwriting looks familiar.\ntext [a4cea9] = 你的图书馆借书里不断出现折好的字条。没有署名。它们很有趣，有点紧张，而且知道一些只有一直留意你的人才会知道的事。那笔迹看着有点眼熟。\n# en: A folded note tucked into a library book with a pressed flower, unsigned, the handwriting careful and slightly shaky.\nscene [469c2b] = 一张夹在图书馆书里的折叠字条，附着一朵压花，没有署名，字迹小心又微微发抖。\n# en: Leave a note back\nopt.left.label [ad88dc] = 回一张字条\n# en: You write back. The notes get longer. One day the last line just says: \"It's {npc}. You knew, didn't you?\" You did.\nopt.left.say.1 [5ee7cc] = 你回了信。字条越写越长。有一天，最后一行只写着：“是{npc}。你早就知道了，对吧？”你确实知道。\n# en: Stake out the library\nopt.right.label [010099] = 在图书馆蹲守\n# en: You catch {npc} red-handed, mid-tuck. {They} turn{s} the colour of a pyromancy exam. You both laugh until the librarian throws you out.\nopt.right.say.1 [a59ad0] = 你当场抓住了正往书里塞字条的{npc}。{they}的脸红得像火焰术考试。你们俩笑到被图书管理员赶了出去。\n# en: You wait three nights and catch nobody. On the fourth morning the note says: \"Nice try.\"\nopt.right.say.2 [ec1e48] = 你守了三个晚上，谁也没抓到。第四天早上，字条上写着：“想得美。”\n\n@event soc_injured_friend\n# en: Carried\ntitle [c0fda1] = 背回来\n# en: {npc} has twisted an ankle on the far side of the grounds, a long way from the infirmary, and it's getting dark. {They} {are} trying very hard to pretend it doesn't hurt.\ntext [f03529] = {npc}在校园的另一头扭伤了脚踝，离医务室很远，天也快黑了。{they}正非常努力地假装一点也不疼。\n# en: A student carrying another piggyback across a muddy lawn, the injured one's ankle wrapped in a scarf.\nscene [042d6a] = 一名学生背着另一名学生穿过泥泞的草坪，受伤那位的脚踝用围巾裹着。\n# en: Carry {them} on your back\nopt.left.label [374549] = 把{them}背回去\n# en: It's a long way. {npc} narrates the whole journey like a horse race. By the time you reach the infirmary you're both laughing too hard to breathe.\nopt.left.say.1 [6e8e08] = 路很长。{npc}一路像解说赛马一样解说着。等你们到医务室时，两个人都笑得喘不上气。\n# en: Run for help\nopt.right.label [689bdc] = 跑去找人帮忙\n# en: You run for Sister Amaranth, who arrives with a floating stretcher and a lot of opinions. {npc} is fine. {They} send{s} you a thank-you sweet.\nopt.right.say.1 [b2b6c2] = 你跑去找阿玛兰斯修女，她带着一副漂浮担架和一肚子意见赶来了。{npc}没事。{they}送了你一块谢礼糖。\n\n@event soc_rival_decency\n# en: An Unexpected Kindness\ntitle [10ec52] = 意外的善意\n# en: {&You drop your books in the rain, and the first person to stoop and help pick them up is {npc}.|You slip on the wet stairs, and the hand that catches your elbow is {npc}'s.|The wind off the lake takes your scarf, and {npc} is the one who runs after it.} {They} hand{s} it back without a word, looking as surprised as you are.\ntext [157405] = {&你的书在雨里掉了一地，第一个弯腰帮你捡的人竟是{npc}。|你在湿滑的楼梯上一滑，扶住你胳膊肘的那只手是{npc}的。|湖上吹来的风卷走了你的围巾，追上去的人是{npc}。}{they}一言不发地把东西递还给你，看上去和你一样惊讶。\n# en: A student picking up a rival's scattered books from a wet floor, the rival frozen mid-step, not sure what to say.\nscene [b9f110] = 一名学生从湿漉漉的地上捡起对手散落的书，对手僵在半步之中，不知该说什么。\n# en: Thank {them} properly\nopt.left.label [023f60] = 好好谢谢{them}\n# en: \"Don't make it a thing,\" {npc} mutters. But the next day {they} nod{s} to you in the corridor. {&Something has thawed.|It's getting harder to stay enemies.|You're not sure what you are to each other anymore.}\nopt.left.say.1 [6849d6] = “别弄得像回事似的，”{npc}嘟囔道。但第二天，{they}在走廊里冲你点了点头。{&有什么东西解冻了。|要继续当敌人越来越难了。|你也说不清你们现在算什么了。}\n# en: Suspect a trick\nopt.right.label [5792ea] = 怀疑这是个圈套\n# en: You check for hexes. There aren't any. {npc} saw you checking. You'll both remember that.\nopt.right.say.1 [1b0327] = 你检查有没有恶咒。没有。{npc}看见你在检查。你们俩都会记住这件事。\n\n@event soc_partner_anniversary\n# en: An Anniversary\ntitle [a63670] = 纪念日\n# en: It's been a while now — long enough that {npc} has started calling things \"ours\". Today {they} hand{s} you a small wrapped box and won't meet your eyes.\ntext [7539ef] = 已经有一阵子了——久到{npc}开始把东西叫作“我们的”。今天{they}递给你一个包好的小盒子，不敢看你的眼睛。\n# en: Two people on a bench by the lake at sunset, one holding out a small wrapped box, the other's hands over their mouth.\nscene [3c4e6d] = 日落时分，两个人坐在湖边的长椅上，一个递出一个包好的小盒子，另一个双手捂着嘴。\n# en: Open it now\nopt.left.label [a90872] = 现在就打开\n# en: Inside: a pebble from the lakeshore, polished to a shine, from the day you first really talked. \"It's stupid,\" {npc} says. It isn't. You carry it everywhere, and spend the next day hunting for something half as good to give back.\nopt.left.say.1 [dc0d28] = 里面是一颗湖边的鹅卵石，被打磨得闪闪发亮，是你们第一次真正谈心那天捡的。“这很傻，”{npc}说。一点也不傻。你走到哪儿都带着它，第二天又跑遍各处，想找一件哪怕有它一半好的回礼。\n# en: Give {them} yours first\nopt.right.label [64f5f8] = 先把你的送给{them}\n# en: You'd had the same idea, and spent most of your purse on it. You both open your boxes at once. They're nearly the same gift. You laugh until the sun goes down.\nopt.right.say.1 [f20b70] = 你们想到一块儿去了，你为此花掉了大半个钱袋。你们同时打开盒子。两份礼物几乎一模一样。你们一直笑到太阳落山。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/expansion_summer.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/expansion_summer.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event sum_empty_castle\n# en: The Empty Castle\ntitle [38b303] = 空荡荡的城堡\n# en: In summer the castle breathes differently. Corridors you've walked a thousand times are silent; doors that are always locked stand slightly open. A portrait yawns. Nobody is watching.\ntext [b8e5ca] = 夏天，城堡的呼吸都不一样了。你走过上千次的走廊寂静无声；那些永远上锁的门微微敞开着。一幅画像打了个哈欠。没有人在看。\n# en: A vast empty dining hall in summer, chairs upturned on the tables, a single student eating alone in a shaft of dusty sunlight.\nscene [06e080] = 夏日里一间空旷的大餐厅，椅子倒扣在桌上，一名学生独自坐在满是浮尘的阳光里吃饭。\n# en: Explore the open doors\nopt.left.label [476a88] = 探索那些敞开的门\n# en: Behind one door: a forgotten trophy room. Behind another: a professor's stash of very old toffee and a purse of lost-property coins nobody will miss. You pocket it anyway.\nopt.left.say.1 [08ba74] = 一扇门后：一间被遗忘的奖杯室。另一扇门后：某位教授私藏的陈年太妃糖，还有一袋没人会惦记的失物招领硬币。你还是把它揣进了兜里。\n# en: Behind the first door: the caretaker, in a dressing gown, very surprised. You both agree this never happened.\nopt.left.say.2 [eeeeef] = 第一扇门后：穿着睡袍、大吃一惊的管理员。你们俩一致同意，这事从没发生过。\n# en: Enjoy the quiet\nopt.right.label [293287] = 享受这份安静\n# en: You read in window seats, nap on the great lawn and eat breakfast at noon. For two weeks the most famous academy in the kingdom is just a big, peaceful house.\nopt.right.say.1 [08d11a] = 你在窗台上看书，在大草坪上打盹，中午才吃早饭。整整两个星期，王国最有名的学院只是一座又大又安宁的房子。\n\n@event sum_home_chores\n# en: Help Around the House\ntitle [da7efc] = 帮家里干活\n# en: Home is lovely for about three days. Then the list appears on the kitchen door: the roof, the goats, the fence, the cousin's wedding. Your summer reading list is hidden under a tea towel.\ntext [e1f4e8] = 家的美好大概能维持三天。然后清单就贴上了厨房门：屋顶、山羊、篱笆、表亲的婚礼。你的暑期书单被藏在一块茶巾底下。\n# en: A farmhouse kitchen in high summer, a basket of laundry, a list of chores pinned to the door, a textbook hidden under a tea towel.\nscene [c81350] = 盛夏的农舍厨房，一篮待洗的衣物，门上钉着家务清单，一本课本藏在茶巾底下。\n# en: Do the chores — with magic\nopt.left.label [2b5166] = 干家务——用魔法\n# en: The fence mends itself, the goats are briefly levitated, the roof is better than new. The whole street comes to watch. Your mother pretends not to be proud.\nopt.left.say.1 [6cae25] = 篱笆自己补好了，山羊短暂地浮了起来，屋顶比新的还好。整条街的人都跑来看。你妈妈假装一点也不骄傲。\n# en: The goats are briefly levitated. Unfortunately so is the fence. Your family is very patient about it.\nopt.left.say.2 [4bdcaf] = 山羊短暂地浮了起来。不幸的是，篱笆也浮起来了。你的家人对此非常有耐心。\n# en: Do them the slow way, and talk\nopt.right.label [b2c300] = 慢慢干，顺便聊聊\n# en: You fix the fence by hand with your father, saying very little and then, suddenly, a lot. It's the best conversation you've had all year.\nopt.right.say.1 [775d96] = 你和爸爸一起亲手修篱笆，一开始几乎不说话，后来突然说了很多。这是你一整年里最好的一次谈话。\n\n@event sum_old_friend\n# en: An Old Friend\ntitle [8809c5] = 老朋友\n# en: Your oldest friend from home is waiting at the crossroads, like always. But something's different. \"So,\" they say, not quite looking at you, \"I suppose you're too grand for us now.\"\ntext [66198b] = 你在家乡最老的朋友照例等在十字路口。但有些东西不一样了。“所以，”对方说，眼睛没怎么看你，“我猜你现在看不上我们了吧。”\n# en: Two young people sitting on a stone wall above a village, one in academy robes, one in work clothes, a little space between them.\nscene [b48b9c] = 两个年轻人坐在村子上方的石墙上，一个穿着学院长袍，一个穿着干活的衣服，中间隔着一点距离。\n# en: Prove you're not\nopt.left.label [4cb06b] = 证明你没有\n# en: You spend the summer exactly as you used to — orchard raids, river swims, terrible jokes. By Starfall, the space between you on the wall is gone.\nopt.left.say.1 [8019a4] = 你像从前一样度过这个夏天——偷果园、下河游泳、讲烂笑话。到了星坠月，石墙上你们之间的那点距离不见了。\n# en: Admit you've changed\nopt.right.label [11cd37] = 承认你变了\n# en: \"I have,\" you say. \"So have you.\" They laugh, surprised. You talk all night about who you're both becoming. It's different from before. It might be better.\nopt.right.say.1 [84ee4c] = “我是变了，”你说，“你也变了。”对方惊讶地笑了。你们聊了一整夜，聊你们各自正在变成什么样的人。和从前不一样了。也许会更好。\n\n@event sum_midsummer_fair\n# en: The Midsummer Fair\ntitle [fa85d7] = 仲夏集市\n# en: The village Midsummer Fair: enchanted carousel, ring-toss, a strongman, and a booth promising \"GUESS YOUR WEIGHT IN CHEESE\". You have a few crowns in your pocket and a whole afternoon.\ntext [d583e2] = 村里的仲夏集市：魔法旋转木马、套圈、大力士，还有一个摊位承诺“猜猜你的体重等于多少奶酪”。你兜里有几个克朗，还有一整个下午。\n# en: A village green full of striped tents, a carousel of enchanted wooden animals, lanterns strung between trees, a strongman lifting a cow.\nscene [b2cfbf] = 村子草地上满是条纹帐篷，一座由施了魔法的木头动物组成的旋转木马，树间挂着灯笼，一个大力士正举起一头牛。\n# en: Win something at the ring-toss\nopt.left.label [1e7b9b] = 套圈赢个奖\n# en: Three rings, three hits. You win a stuffed griffon the size of a sheep and sell it to a crying child's father for fifteen crowns. Everyone wins.\nopt.left.say.1 [b0aa3b] = 三个圈，三个全中。你赢了一只绵羊那么大的狮鹫玩偶，又以十五克朗卖给了一个哭闹孩子的爸爸。皆大欢喜。\n# en: Every ring bounces off. The ring-toss is definitely enchanted. You don't care; you're eating candied apples.\nopt.left.say.2 [09da43] = 每个圈都弹开了。这套圈摊绝对施了魔法。你不在乎，你正在吃糖苹果。\n# en: Ride the carousel\nopt.right.label [a83496] = 坐旋转木马\n# en: Your wooden hippogriff takes off, very slightly, at the top of every turn. You ride it nine times.\nopt.right.say.1 [8ddedf] = 你的木头鹰马每转到最高处都会轻轻地飞起来一点点。你坐了九次。\n\n@event sum_letter_from_friend\n# en: A Summer Letter\ntitle [947c1f] = 夏日来信\n# en: An owl arrives with a thick, slightly crumpled letter from {npc}. Six pages of summer news, two doodles, one pressed flower, and at the end, in smaller writing: \"I miss the castle. I miss you, actually.\"\ntext [915df1] = 一只猫头鹰送来了{npc}一封厚厚的、有点皱的信。六页夏日见闻，两幅涂鸦，一朵压花，最后用小一号的字写着：“我想念城堡。其实，我想你。”\n# en: A thick letter with a doodle on the envelope arriving by owl at a summer window.\nscene [c7230f] = 一封信封上画着涂鸦的厚信，由猫头鹰送到夏日的窗前。\n# en: Write a short reply\nopt.left.label [2e26d1] = 写一封简短的回信\n# en: You write a page. It's warm, if brief. {npc} keeps it anyway.\nopt.left.say.1 [ae145d] = 你写了一页。温暖，虽然简短。{npc}还是把它收了起来。\n# en: Write back for hours\nopt.right.label [c44657] = 回信写上几个钟头\n# en: You write until the candle gutters. Twelve pages. You can't remember the last time you wrote that much that wasn't an essay.\nopt.right.say.1 [d776a8] = 你一直写到蜡烛快灭。十二页。你都不记得上次写这么多不是论文的东西是什么时候了。\n\n@event sum_rain_week\n# en: A Week of Rain\ntitle [fa6851] = 一星期的雨\n# en: It rains for a solid week. Not dramatic rain: just steady, grey, endless rain. Every plan you made for the summer is quietly drowning.\ntext [8b734b] = 雨整整下了一个星期。不是戏剧性的雨，只是稳定、灰暗、没完没了的雨。你为这个夏天做的每一个计划都在悄悄被淹没。\n# en: Rain streaming down a tall window, a student curled in an armchair with a fat novel, a teapot steaming on the sill.\nscene [ea0fc1] = 雨水顺着高窗流下，一名学生蜷在扶手椅里捧着一本厚小说，窗台上的茶壶冒着热气。\n# en: Read a very long novel\nopt.left.label [fc9cb2] = 读一本很长的小说\n# en: You buy an eight-hundred-page novel about a family of sea-witches, read every word and cry at the end. It's the best week of the summer.\nopt.left.say.1 [f8199f] = 你买了一本八百页的小说，讲一个海巫家族的故事，一字不落地读完，读到结尾哭了。这是整个夏天最好的一周。\n# en: Practise spells indoors\nopt.right.label [8f2ff7] = 在室内练咒语\n# en: You practise until you can light every candle in the room with a flick. You also set fire to one curtain. It's raining; it goes out quickly.\nopt.right.say.1 [f19727] = 你一直练到手指一弹就能点亮房间里每一支蜡烛。你还烧着了一块窗帘。外面在下雨，火很快就灭了。\n\n@event sum_sea_serpent\n# en: Fishing With an Uncle\ntitle [340021] = 和叔叔去钓鱼\n# en: Your uncle takes you fishing. Something takes the bait. Something very large. The line sings. The boat tilts. A long scaled neck rises out of the water and looks at you with polite interest.\ntext [fbf017] = 叔叔带你去钓鱼。有东西咬钩了。非常大的东西。鱼线嗡嗡作响，船身倾斜。一条长满鳞片的长脖子从水里升起，带着礼貌的好奇看着你。\n# en: A small fishing boat on a grey-green sea, a fishing line drawn taut, something very long and scaled rising beside the hull.\nscene [035dc5] = 灰绿色海面上的一条小渔船，鱼线绷得紧紧的，一个很长、长满鳞片的东西正从船边升起。\n# en: Cut the line\nopt.left.label [c4df32] = 剪断鱼线\n# en: The serpent sinks back with a sigh like a disappointed whale. Your uncle tells the story for the rest of his life, and it gets bigger every time.\nopt.left.say.1 [f3a3c3] = 海蛇叹了口气沉了回去，像一头失望的鲸鱼。你叔叔后半辈子都在讲这个故事，而且每讲一次就更大一点。\n# en: Try to talk to it\nopt.right.label [2c2211] = 试着和它说话\n# en: You greet it in the old courtesies {met(brask): Professor Brask taught you|from a book you once read and never expected to need}. It bows its great head, leaves a pearl on the deck and swims away. Your uncle faints.\nopt.right.say.1 [299c43] = 你用{met(brask): 布拉斯克教授教过的|从一本书上读来、从没想过会用上的}古老礼节向它问好。它低下巨大的头，在甲板上留下一颗珍珠，然后游走了。你叔叔晕了过去。\n# en: The serpent is not interested in conversation. It is interested in the boat. You swim home.\nopt.right.say.2 [c01ca7] = 海蛇对谈话没兴趣。它对船有兴趣。你是游回家的。\n\n@event sum_reading_list\n# en: The Summer Reading List\ntitle [2a9b43] = 暑期书单\n# en: The summer reading list arrives by owl. It is a scroll. It unrolls across the floor, out of the door and partway down the stairs. Thorne has added a note at the bottom: \"Optional. (It is not optional.)\"\ntext [56544a] = 暑期书单由猫头鹰送达。是一卷羊皮纸。它在地板上展开，滚出门外，一直滚到楼梯中间。索恩在最下面加了一句：“选读。（并不是选读。）”\n# en: A long scroll unrolling off a table and across the floor, a list of titles in tiny handwriting, a student staring at it in despair.\nscene [593aae] = 一卷长长的羊皮纸从桌上滚落、铺满地板，上面用极小的字写满书名，一名学生绝望地盯着它。\n# en: Read all of it\nopt.left.label [281ec8] = 全部读完\n# en: You read everything. When term starts, Thorne asks a question about the last book and you're the only one who can answer. His eyebrow rises half a millimetre.\nopt.left.say.1 [0c1e02] = 你全读完了。开学时，索恩问了一个关于最后一本书的问题，只有你答得上来。他的眉毛抬高了半毫米。\n# en: Read the first and last chapters of each\nopt.right.label [2423ad] = 每本只读第一章和最后一章\n# en: A time-honoured strategy. You can talk confidently about the beginning and end of forty books. The middle remains a mystery, like most of life.\nopt.right.say.1 [bca605] = 一个历史悠久的策略。你能自信地谈论四十本书的开头和结尾。中间部分仍然是个谜，就像人生的大部分一样。\n\n@event sum_night_market\n# en: The Night Market\ntitle [8fbd8e] = 夜市\n# en: A night market appears in a field outside the village — it wasn't there yesterday and won't be tomorrow. A hooded stall-keeper offers you a stoppered bottle with a tiny thundercloud inside. \"Fifteen crowns. What it does is a surprise.\"\ntext [8af6b0] = 村外的田野里出现了一个夜市——昨天还没有，明天也不会在。一个戴兜帽的摊主递给你一个塞着瓶塞的瓶子，里面有一小朵雷雨云。“十五克朗。它有什么用？那是个惊喜。”\n# en: A market that only appears after midnight, stalls lit with coloured flames, hooded sellers, a bottle that seems to contain a small thundercloud.\nscene [ad8492] = 一个只在午夜后出现的集市，摊位亮着彩色火焰，戴兜帽的卖家，一个瓶子里似乎装着一小朵雷雨云。\n# en: Buy the bottle\nopt.left.label [19c560] = 买下瓶子\n# en: You uncork it in your room. The little storm rains ideas on you for a fortnight. Your notebooks overflow.\nopt.left.say.1 [3066ff] = 你在房间里拔开瓶塞。那场小风暴往你身上下了两星期的灵感雨。你的笔记本都写满了。\n# en: You uncork it in your room. The little storm rains on you. Just rain. For a fortnight. You catch a cold.\nopt.left.say.2 [07314d] = 你在房间里拔开瓶塞。那场小风暴往你身上下雨。就是雨。下了两星期。你感冒了。\n# en: Just look around\nopt.right.label [1c076f] = 只是逛逛\n# en: You wander until dawn, buying nothing, watching everything. When the sun rises the field is empty except for a single glowing feather. You keep it.\nopt.right.say.1 [a046d1] = 你一直逛到黎明，什么也没买，什么都看了。太阳升起时，田野里空空荡荡，只剩一根发光的羽毛。你把它留了下来。\n\n@event sum_end_of_summer\n# en: The Last Week of Summer\ntitle [64767a] = 夏天的最后一周\n# en: The last week of summer has that particular light — golden, heavy, already nostalgic. Your trunk is half-packed. Part of you is desperate to get back. Part of you wants this week to last forever.\ntext [fa47ef] = 夏天的最后一周有一种特别的光——金黄、沉甸甸的，已经带着怀念。你的箱子收拾了一半。一部分的你迫不及待想回去，一部分的你希望这一周永远不要结束。\n# en: A student packing a trunk in golden late-summer light, a half-finished jar of jam, a pressed flower and a new textbook on the bed.\nscene [79a65d] = 一名学生在夏末的金色光线里收拾箱子，床上放着半罐果酱、一朵压花和一本新课本。\n# en: Squeeze every drop out of it\nopt.left.label [660c83] = 榨干它的每一滴\n# en: Late swims, long dinners, one final sunset from the hill. You arrive at the barge sunburnt and happy and only slightly late.\nopt.left.say.1 [3d2155] = 很晚的游泳、很长的晚餐、山顶上最后一次日落。你到达驳船时晒伤了、很开心，只迟到了一点点。\n# en: Get ready for the new year\nopt.right.label [50fe4f] = 为新学年做准备\n# en: You re-read your notes, sharpen your quills and polish your wand. When term starts you are, for once, ahead. It's an unfamiliar feeling.\nopt.right.say.1 [fbc642] = 你重读笔记，削好羽毛笔，擦亮魔杖。开学时你难得地领先了一步。这是一种陌生的感觉。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/expansion_y3.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/expansion_y3.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event y3_career_fair\n# en: The Career Fair\ntitle [a6ba30] = 职业招聘会\n# en: Every guild, order and institution in the kingdom has sent someone to the great hall. They are all very polite. They are all, very clearly, shopping. Where will you leave your name?\ntext [5b87fc] = 王国里每个公会、教团和机构都派了人到大礼堂来。他们都非常客气。他们也都非常明显地在挑人。你要把名字留在哪里？\n# en: The great hall lined with recruiters' booths — a Conclave mage in silver, a librarian behind a wall of books, a circus ringmaster juggling fire.\nscene [7e3280] = 大礼堂里摆满了招聘摊位——一位身着银袍的议会法师、一位坐在书墙后的图书管理员、一位抛着火把的马戏团团长。\n# en: The Conclave of the Seven Towers\nopt.conclave.label [c832a4] = 七塔议会\n# en: Apprentice archmages. The interview tests your Arcana.\nopt.conclave.hint [04186b] = 大法师学徒。面试考你的奥术。\n# en: You leave your name with the mage in silver, who writes it down without looking up. The interview is set.\nopt.conclave.say.1 [fcffbb] = 你把名字留给了那位银袍法师，他头也没抬就记下了。面试定下来了。\n# en: The Royal Library\nopt.library.label [64f580] = 皇家图书馆\n# en: Keepers of every book ever written. The interview tests your Lore.\nopt.library.hint [e1ce52] = 世上每一本书的守护者。面试考你的学识。\n# en: The librarian takes your name and asks what you're reading at the moment. You answer. The interview is set.\nopt.library.say.1 [84b5cd] = 图书馆员记下你的名字，问你最近在读什么。你回答了。面试定下来了。\n# en: The Guild of Alchemists\nopt.guild.label [8a8f99] = 炼金师公会\n# en: Potions, profit, explosions. The interview tests your Alchemy.\nopt.guild.hint [acaaa6] = 药水、利润、爆炸。面试考你的炼金。\n# en: The alchemist at the guild's booth shakes your hand and checks it for burns. The interview is set.\nopt.guild.say.1 [342128] = 炼金师公会摊位上的人和你握了握手，顺便检查了一下有没有烫伤。面试定下来了。\n# en: The Crown's Office of Unseen Affairs\nopt.unseen.label [a4101a] = 王室隐秘事务署\n# en: They found you, actually. The interview tests your Guile.\nopt.unseen.hint [606647] = 其实是他们先找到你的。面试考你的诡计。\n# en: A woman nobody else seems to notice takes your name and tells you where to be. The interview is set.\nopt.unseen.say.1 [7f6715] = 一个似乎谁都没注意到的女人记下你的名字，告诉你该去哪儿。面试定下来了。\n# en: The Travelling Circus of Wonders\nopt.circus.label [f3ec0c] = 奇迹巡回马戏团\n# en: Wagons, crowds, illusions. The interview tests your Charm.\nopt.circus.hint [6df8a7] = 马车、人群、幻术。面试考你的魅力。\n# en: The ringmaster juggles your name into the air, catches it and writes it down. The interview is set.\nopt.circus.say.1 [fca380] = 马戏团团长把你的名字抛上空中，接住，然后记了下来。面试定下来了。\n# en: Stay at Lumenhold as a junior teacher\nopt.teaching.label [27030b] = 留在流明堡当见习教师\n# en: Needs a faculty member who trusts you: Thorne, Maud or the Headmistress.\nopt.teaching.hint [e8bcda] = 需要一位信任你的教职员：索恩教授、老莫德或院长。\n# en: Nobody on the faculty knows you well enough to vouch for you.\nopt.teaching.locked [bbf1f9] = 教职员里没有谁足够了解你，能为你担保。\n# en: Your old professor puts your name forward to the faculty, with a few words you don't hear. The interview is set.\nopt.teaching.say.1 [4a845e] = 你从前的教授把你的名字推荐给了教职员会，还附了几句你没听见的话。面试定下来了。\n# en: Not yet — you'd rather not decide\nopt.none.label [0a96fd] = 还不急——你暂时不想决定\n# en: You collect a tote bag, three pamphlets and a free quill. {month < 4: The future can wait one more month; the fair is back next month.|The fair packs up for good. The future will have to come and find you.}\nopt.none.say.1 [0f9734] = 你领了一个帆布袋、三本小册子和一支免费的羽毛笔。{month < 4: 未来可以再等一个月；招聘会下个月还会来。|招聘会彻底收摊了。未来只能自己来找你了。}\n\n@event y3_interview\n# en: The Interview\ntitle [7d775f] = 面试\n# en: Three people behind a long table. One takes notes; one smiles too much; one says nothing at all, which is clearly the one who decides. \"Tell us,\" says the smiling one, \"why you.\"\ntext [06648e] = 长桌后坐着三个人。一个做笔记；一个笑得太多；一个一言不发——显然，做决定的就是这一位。“说说看，”爱笑的那位开口，“为什么是你？”\n# en: A small panelled room, a long table, three interviewers and one empty chair facing them.\nscene [05e47c] = 一间镶着护墙板的小房间，一张长桌，三位面试官，对面一把空椅子。\n# en: Let your record speak\nopt.left.label [1ea228] = 让履历说话\n# en: You talk about what you've learned and, more importantly, what you got wrong and fixed. The silent one writes a single word. You suspect it's \"yes\".\nopt.left.say.1 [c8179c] = 你讲你学到了什么，更重要的是，你搞错过什么、又是怎么改正的。沉默的那位只写下一个词。你猜那是“行”。\n# en: You recite your achievements like a shopping list. The silent one looks at the clock. \"Now,\" says the smiling one, \"a small practical.\"\nopt.left.say.2 [2da86c] = 你像念购物清单一样背诵自己的成就。沉默的那位看了一眼钟。“现在，”爱笑的那位说，“来个小小的实操。”\n# en: Tell them a story\nopt.right.label [a082be] = 给他们讲个故事\n# en: You tell them about the night the lake froze, or the night you caught a star, or the night you were wrong. The silent one laughs. Out loud.\nopt.right.say.1 [1b6366] = 你讲起湖面结冰的那一夜，或者你接住一颗星星的那一夜，或者你犯错的那一夜。沉默的那位笑了。出声地笑了。\n# en: The story goes on too long and ends in the wrong place. Somewhere in the middle you lost them, and yourself. \"Now,\" says the smiling one, \"a small practical.\"\nopt.right.say.2 [4096b3] = 故事讲得太长，结尾也落错了地方。讲到一半，你就把他们弄丢了，也把自己弄丢了。“现在，”爱笑的那位说，“来个小小的实操。”\n\n@event y3_interview_practical\n# en: The Practical\ntitle [eaf727] = 实操\n# en: The note-taker sets a task on the table. {flag(career_conclave): A ward with a flaw in it, somewhere.|{flag(career_library): A crumbling manuscript in three hands and no catalogue.|{flag(career_guild): A cauldron, a recipe with one ingredient missing, and a clock.|{flag(career_unseen): A sealed letter, and the request to read it without breaking the seal.|{flag(career_circus): An empty stage, a sceptical crowd of three, and five minutes.|A class of imaginary first-years, and a lesson to give them.}}}}} \"In your own time,\" says the smiling one, and turns over an hourglass.\ntext [2862ee] = 记录的那位在桌上摆出一项任务。{flag(career_conclave): 一道结界，某处有个破绽。|{flag(career_library): 一份出自三人之手、没有目录、正在碎裂的手稿。|{flag(career_guild): 一口坩埚，一张缺了一味材料的配方，还有一只钟。|{flag(career_unseen): 一封封好的信，要求你不拆封蜡读出内容。|{flag(career_circus): 一个空舞台，三个将信将疑的观众，还有五分钟。|一群想象中的一年级生，和一堂要给他们上的课。}}}}} “不急，”爱笑的那位说着，把一只沙漏翻了过来。\n# en: A plain table in a panelled room set with a single task, three interviewers watching a student roll up their sleeves.\nscene [9735fb] = 镶板房间里一张朴素的桌子上摆着一项任务，三位面试官看着一个学生卷起袖子。\n# en: Do it properly\nopt.left.label [859632] = 认真完成\n# en: You find the flaw, name it and close it, and the ward rings like a struck glass. The silent one leans forward for the first time.\nopt.left.say.1 [19a282] = 你找到了破绽，说出它，然后补上，结界像被敲响的玻璃一样鸣响。沉默的那位第一次身体前倾。\n# en: The flaw finds you first. The ward fizzes out in your hands, and the note-taker writes for a long time.\nopt.left.say.2 [4a3fa4] = 破绽先找上了你。结界在你手里嘶嘶地熄灭了，记录的那位写了很久。\n# en: Three hands, three centuries, one lost chapter. You date them all before the sand runs out. The silent one nods, once.\nopt.left.say.3 [7bd96d] = 三种笔迹，三个世纪，一个遗失的章节。沙子漏完之前，你把它们的年代全断了出来。沉默的那位点了一下头。\n# en: You date the second hand a century wrong, and know it the moment you say it. The note-taker writes for a long time.\nopt.left.say.4 [c75925] = 你把第二种笔迹的年代断错了一个世纪，话一出口就知道了。记录的那位写了很久。\n# en: You find the missing ingredient by its absence and the cauldron settles to a perfect simmer. Someone behind the table sniffs, approvingly.\nopt.left.say.5 [220baa] = 你凭着缺失察觉出那味少了的材料，坩埚安定下来，完美地文火慢炖。桌子后面有人嗅了嗅，表示赞许。\n# en: The cauldron spits and turns a colour no recipe asks for. The note-taker writes for a long time.\nopt.left.say.6 [796d95] = 坩埚噼啪作响，变成了一种没有配方会要求的颜色。记录的那位写了很久。\n# en: Steam, a thin blade, a little patience. You read it, reseal it and hand it back. The silent one smiles, which is somehow worse.\nopt.left.say.7 [a79e9e] = 蒸汽、一片薄刃、一点耐心。你读完，重新封好，递了回去。沉默的那位笑了，不知怎么这更吓人。\n# en: The seal cracks with a sound like a shot. The silent one does not look surprised. The note-taker writes for a long time.\nopt.left.say.8 [e395b2] = 封蜡裂开，声音像一声枪响。沉默的那位毫不意外。记录的那位写了很久。\n# en: Five minutes of nonsense and wonder, and the crowd of three are on their feet. One of them is the silent one.\nopt.left.say.9 [c96f52] = 五分钟的胡闹与奇迹，三个观众站了起来。其中一个就是沉默的那位。\n# en: The crowd of three stays seated, arms folded, for all five minutes. The note-taker writes for a long time.\nopt.left.say.10 [e66680] = 整整五分钟，三个观众抱着胳膊坐着没动。记录的那位写了很久。\n# en: You teach the empty chairs the thing you most wish someone had taught you, and forget for a while that they're empty. The silent one nods.\nopt.left.say.11 [efb9c1] = 你把自己最希望当年有人教给你的东西教给了那些空椅子，有一阵子忘了它们是空的。沉默的那位点了点头。\n# en: You lose the imaginary class in the first minute, which you hadn't known was possible. The note-taker writes for a long time.\nopt.left.say.12 [a516ac] = 你在第一分钟就镇不住那群想象中的学生了，你都不知道这还有可能。记录的那位写了很久。\n# en: Ask questions before you start\nopt.right.label [00676d] = 开始前先提问\n# en: You ask three good questions, then do the task carefully and well. It's slower than they'd like. The silent one doesn't seem to mind.\nopt.right.say.1 [97b7a6] = 你提了三个好问题，然后仔细、出色地完成了任务。比他们希望的慢一些。沉默的那位似乎并不介意。\n# en: You ask three good questions and run out of sand before you've finished. \"Careful,\" says the smiling one, in a tone that isn't quite praise.\nopt.right.say.2 [50d652] = 你提了三个好问题，结果沙子漏完了还没做完。“仔细点，”爱笑的那位说，那语气算不上夸奖。\n\n@event y3_interview_question\n# en: The Silent One's Question\ntitle [740fdd] = 沉默者的问题\n# en: At last the silent one speaks. \"Tell me about a time you did something you weren't proud of,\" they say, \"and whether you'd do it again.\"\ntext [d81e5e] = 沉默的那位终于开口了。“说说你做过的一件不光彩的事，”对方说，“以及你会不会再做一次。”\n# en: A small panelled room, an interviewer who hasn't spoken leaning forward over folded hands, a student on the other side of the table very still.\nscene [5eab0f] = 一间小小的镶板房间，一位始终没开口的面试官双手交握、身体前倾，桌子另一边的学生一动不动。\n# en: Tell the truth\nopt.left.label [d8bf00] = 说实话\n# en: You tell them the truth, all of it, and what you learned, and that you wouldn't. The silent one is quiet for a long moment. \"Thank you,\" they say, and it sounds like the end of a search.\nopt.left.say.1 [8a3687] = 你说了实话，全部，还有你学到了什么，以及你不会再做。沉默的那位安静了很久。“谢谢你，”对方说，听起来像一场寻找终于结束了。\n# en: You tell them the truth, and halfway through you realise you might do it again. So does the silent one. \"Honest,\" they say. It isn't clear whether that's good.\nopt.left.say.2 [694938] = 你说了实话，说到一半你意识到自己可能还会再做。沉默的那位也意识到了。“很诚实，”对方说。不清楚这是好是坏。\n# en: Tell them what they want to hear\nopt.right.label [2318d9] = 说他们想听的\n# en: A small mistake, gracefully regretted, neatly learned from. It isn't quite true. The silent one writes something down and underlines it.\nopt.right.say.1 [450ed2] = 一个小小的过失，体面地后悔，干净地吸取了教训。这不完全是真的。沉默的那位写下了什么，还划了线。\n# en: The story is too neat, and the silent one has heard it before, from better liars. \"I see,\" they say, and you hear the door closing.\nopt.right.say.2 [ae64c0] = 这个故事太完美了，沉默的那位从更会撒谎的人那里听过。“我明白了，”对方说，你听见了门关上的声音。\n\n@event y3_interview_verdict\n# en: We'll Be in Touch\ntitle [8502a1] = 我们会再联系你\n# en: \"We'll be in touch,\" says the smiling one, and stands, and so you stand too. In the corridor your legs feel like someone else's. {iv_score >= 3: You've never been so sure of anything.|{iv_score >= 2: You think it went well. You think.|{iv_score >= 1: It could have gone either way.|You know exactly how it went.}}}\ntext [0b6c26] = “我们会再联系你的，”爱笑的那位说着站了起来，于是你也站了起来。到了走廊里，你的腿像是别人的。{iv_score >= 3: 你从没对一件事这么有把握过。|{iv_score >= 2: 你觉得还不错。你觉得。|{iv_score >= 1: 结果可能是好是坏。|你清楚地知道结果如何。}}}\n# en: A student walking out of a panelled room into a bright corridor, loosening their collar, a closed door behind them.\nscene [6f60cb] = 一个学生走出镶板房间，来到明亮的走廊里，松了松领口，身后的门关着。\n# en: Wait for the letter\nopt.ok.label [b2e924] = 等那封信\n# en: You go back to your room and lie on the bed and don't move for an hour. Somewhere, three people are deciding.\nopt.ok.say.1 [bddf2b] = 你回到房间，躺在床上，一个小时一动不动。某个地方，三个人正在做决定。\n# en: You go back to your room and replay every answer. The better ones get better each time; the bad ones get worse.\nopt.ok.say.2 [7f2983] = 你回到房间，把每个回答都回放了一遍。好的那些每回放一次都更好，糟的那些更糟。\n# en: You go back to your room and don't replay anything at all. Some things you just know.\nopt.ok.say.3 [964087] = 你回到房间，什么都没有回放。有些事你就是知道。\n\n@event y3_waitlist\n# en: The Waiting List\ntitle [b22c0d] = 候补名单\n# en: A thin letter. You are \"a strong candidate\" and on \"a short list\", and they \"hope to write again\". It's not a no. It's not anything.\ntext [c3aabb] = 一封薄薄的信。你是“一位很有实力的候选人”，在“一份短名单”上，他们“希望能再次来信”。这不是拒绝。这什么也不是。\n# en: A thin envelope on a dormitory desk, a single page beside it, a student reading it a second time with their chin on their fist.\nscene [1828d6] = 薄薄的信封放在宿舍书桌上，旁边一页信纸，一个学生用拳头托着下巴，正读第二遍。\n# en: Write back and make your case\nopt.left.label [625a25] = 回信为自己争取\n# en: Two pages, plain and sure: why you, why them, why now. A fortnight later, a heavier envelope.\nopt.left.say.1 [7ac0fb] = 两页，朴实而笃定：为什么是你，为什么是他们，为什么是现在。两个星期后，来了一个更厚的信封。\n# en: You write two pages, then four, then a fifth you shouldn't send, and do. No heavier envelope comes.\nopt.left.say.2 [987fe8] = 你写了两页，然后四页，然后写了不该寄的第五页，还是寄了。更厚的信封一直没来。\n# en: Let it lie and see\nopt.right.label [73a78b] = 先放着，看看再说\n# en: You let it lie. Two months later, when you'd almost stopped looking, the heavier envelope comes after all.\nopt.right.say.1 [b8c566] = 你把它放着。两个月后，就在你快要不再留意的时候，那个更厚的信封终究还是来了。\n# en: You let it lie. It stays lying. Other paths open; you try not to look back at this one.\nopt.right.say.2 [080470] = 你把它放着。它就一直放着。别的路打开了；你尽量不回头看这一条。\n\n@event y3_rejection\n# en: A Polite Letter\ntitle [d7fd3b] = 一封客气的信\n# en: A short letter, very politely written. They were \"impressed by your enthusiasm\". They regret. They wish you every success.\ntext [a0b886] = 一封短信，写得非常客气。他们“对你的热情印象深刻”。他们很遗憾。他们祝你一切顺利。\n# en: A crumpled letter on a windowsill, a student looking out at the lake with their arms folded.\nscene [c16037] = 一封揉皱的信放在窗台上，一个学生抱着胳膊望向湖面。\n# en: Ask them what went wrong\nopt.left.label [7fa9d5] = 问他们哪里出了问题\n# en: You write and ask. To your surprise they answer, frankly, two pages of it. It stings, and it's the most useful thing anyone's told you all year.\nopt.left.say.1 [0a0ada] = 你写信去问。出乎意料，他们坦率地回答了，足足两页。这很刺痛，也是今年别人告诉你的最有用的东西。\n# en: Throw it in the lake\nopt.right.label [0e9dff] = 扔进湖里\n# en: You fold it into a boat and launch it from the jetty. It sinks with great dignity. Some doors close; there are a great many doors.\nopt.right.say.1 [dda54f] = 你把它折成一只小船，从码头放下水。它极有尊严地沉了下去。有些门关上了；门多的是。\n\n@event y3_offer_letter\n# en: The Offer\ntitle [d66d52] = 录用通知\n# en: A heavy envelope with a seal you recognise from the fair. Inside, a single page: an offer. A place. A future with a start date. {iv_score >= 3: There is a signing purse, too, which they call \"a token\".|} It needs a signature by the end of the month.\ntext [656442] = 一个厚重的信封，封蜡上的纹章你在招聘会上见过。里面只有一页纸：一份录用。一个位置。一个有开始日期的未来。{iv_score >= 3: 还附了一笔签约金，他们称之为“一点心意”。|} 月底前需要签字。\n# en: An envelope with a heavy wax seal on a dormitory desk, unopened, a hand hovering over it.\nscene [fc8326] = 宿舍书桌上一个盖着厚重火漆的信封，还没拆开，一只手悬在上面。\n# en: Sign it today\nopt.left.label [f7ce3c] = 今天就签\n# en: Your signature looks strange on the page — like a grown-up's. You pin the letter above your bed and look at it every night.\nopt.left.say.1 [e65e25] = 你的签名在纸上看起来很陌生——像个大人的签名。你把信钉在床头，每晚都要看一眼。\n# en: Ask them to wait\nopt.right.label [3eda22] = 请他们再等等\n# en: You write back asking for time. They grant it, coolly. The future, it turns out, does not like waiting, but it will.\nopt.right.say.1 [1ecd4d] = 你回信请求多给些时间。对方同意了，语气冷淡。原来未来不喜欢等待——但它会等的。\n\n@event y3_first_year_mentee\n# en: Your First-Year\ntitle [891c46] = 你的一年级生\n# en: Fourth-years are assigned a first-year to mentor. Yours is called Pim, is about the size of a large cat, and has already asked forty-one questions, including \"are the stairs alive\" and \"are YOU alive\".\ntext [0665b1] = 四年级生都会分配到一个一年级生来带。你的那个叫皮姆，个头和一只大猫差不多，已经问了四十一个问题，包括“楼梯是活的吗”和“你是活的吗”。\n# en: A tiny first-year trotting beside a fourth-year across the courtyard, firing questions, clutching an enormous timetable.\nscene [7ece82] = 一个小小的一年级生小跑着跟在四年级生身边穿过庭院，连珠炮似的发问，怀里紧抱着一张巨大的课表。\n# en: Answer every question honestly\nopt.left.label [54cf3c] = 老老实实回答每个问题\n# en: Yes, the stairs are alive. Yes, you are alive. No, the lake is not \"friendly\". Pim takes notes. By spring, Pim is explaining things to other first-years, wrongly but with total confidence.\nopt.left.say.1 [e5db2e] = 是的，楼梯是活的。是的，你是活的。不，湖并不“友好”。皮姆一一记下。到了春天，皮姆已经在给别的一年级生讲解了——讲错了，但无比自信。\n# en: Teach Pim to find answers alone\nopt.right.label [9c42d4] = 教皮姆自己找答案\n# en: You hand Pim a library card and a map. Pim is furious for a week, then unstoppable. The librarian now knows Pim by name. And fears Pim, slightly.\nopt.right.say.1 [515043] = 你递给皮姆一张借书证和一张地图。皮姆气了一个星期，然后就势不可挡了。图书管理员现在叫得出皮姆的名字。而且有一点怕皮姆。\n\n@event y3_last_lanterns\n# en: The Last Lantern Night\ntitle [781522] = 最后一个灯笼夜\n# en: Your last Lantern Night. You stand on the shore with an unlit lantern and a pen and realise you have no idea what to wish for. Three years ago it was obvious. Now it isn't.\ntext [a78390] = 你的最后一个灯笼夜。你站在湖岸上，手里是一盏没点的灯笼和一支笔，忽然发现自己完全不知道该许什么愿。三年前答案再明显不过。现在不是了。\n# en: A lone student on the shore at night holding an unlit lantern, hundreds of others already drifting over the dark lake.\nscene [2d5995] = 夜里，一名学生独自站在岸边，手捧一盏未点亮的灯笼，数百盏灯笼已经飘向漆黑的湖面。\n# en: Wish for the people you'll leave behind\nopt.left.label [af62f4] = 为要留下的人们许愿\n# en: You write all their names. It takes the whole lantern. It rises slowly, heavily, and you watch it until it is just a star among stars.\nopt.left.say.1 [a49d3f] = 你写下了他们所有人的名字，写满了整盏灯笼。它缓缓地、沉甸甸地升起，你一直望着它，直到它变成群星中的一颗。\n# en: Leave it blank\nopt.right.label [54fbdb] = 留白\n# en: You let it go empty. Strangely, it rises higher than all the others. Maybe the lake likes not knowing either.\nopt.right.say.1 [154e37] = 你放走了一盏空灯笼。奇怪的是，它比其他所有灯笼都升得更高。也许湖也喜欢不知道答案。\n\n@event y3_thesis\n# en: The Last Chapter\ntitle [87e9c9] = 最后一章\n# en: Your thesis needs one more chapter. You could write something safe and solid, or chase the question your sources keep circling and nobody has answered in three hundred years: why the academy was built above a lake.\ntext [5724af] = 你的论文还差最后一章。你可以写点稳妥扎实的东西，也可以去追那个你的资料总在打转、三百年来无人解答的问题：学院为什么要建在湖上？\n# en: A desk buried in drafts, a wall of pinned notes connected by string, a single candle and a single exhausted student.\nscene [df58af] = 一张被草稿淹没的书桌，一面钉满笔记、用细绳相连的墙，一支蜡烛，一个筋疲力尽的学生。\n# en: Safe and solid\nopt.left.label [f81e8e] = 稳妥扎实\n# en: Forty pages on the thermodynamics of hovering charms. Impeccable. Unremarkable. Your examiners will sleep well.\nopt.left.say.1 [fe97ef] = 四十页论悬浮咒的热力学。无可挑剔，平平无奇。你的考官们会睡得很香。\n# en: The question nobody answered\nopt.right.label [e94f06] = 那个无人解答的问题\n# en: The chapter is so good that your supervisor shows it round the staff room, and the headmistress asks for the only copy — and then, very politely, does not give it back.\nopt.right.say.1 [e0a83d] = 这一章好到导师拿着它在教员休息室里到处给人看，院长还亲自索要了唯一的一份——然后，非常客气地，没有还回来。\n# en: You chase the question until it runs out of archive. The chapter ends mid-sentence, like the founders' ledger. The examiners are not amused.\nopt.right.say.2 [044bc6] = 你追着这个问题一直追到档案的尽头。这一章在一句话中间戛然而止，就像创始者的账簿。考官们一点也不觉得有趣。\n\n@event y3_portrait\n# en: Sitting for the Portrait\ntitle [a99086] = 坐着画像\n# en: Every graduate's portrait hangs in the long gallery — and every portrait, eventually, starts talking. You have one sitting to decide who will be gossiping on that wall for the next three hundred years.\ntext [d9377f] = 每位毕业生的画像都会挂在长廊里——而每幅画像迟早都会开口说话。你只有一次坐下来的机会，来决定接下来三百年在那面墙上说闲话的会是什么样的人。\n# en: An old painter at an easel in a sunlit studio, a student posing stiffly in robes, half-finished portraits of past graduates watching from the walls.\nscene [2a1426] = 阳光充足的画室里，一位老画家站在画架前，一名身着长袍的学生僵硬地摆着姿势，往届毕业生未完成的画像从墙上注视着他们。\n# en: Pose solemn and dignified\nopt.left.label [f39072] = 庄重得体地摆姿势\n# en: The painter captures you looking wise and distant. Your portrait will be dignified. It will also be dreadfully bored.\nopt.left.say.1 [af471d] = 画家画出了你睿智而疏远的神情。你的画像会很体面。它也会无聊得要命。\n# en: Pose as yourself, mid-laugh\nopt.right.label [e9bd51] = 做你自己，笑到一半\n# en: Your portrait is laughing. Three days after it's hung, it starts telling the other portraits jokes. The gallery has never been louder.\nopt.right.say.1 [c325e4] = 你的画像在笑。挂上去三天后，它开始给其他画像讲笑话。长廊从来没有这么吵过。\n\n@event y3_letter_to_self\n# en: A Letter to Your First-Year Self\ntitle [64655e] = 写给一年级的自己\n# en: The Divination department runs a tradition: write a letter to your first-year self. They swear it will be delivered. Nobody knows how. Some fourth-years swear they received theirs.\ntext [eb06b6] = 占卜系有个传统：给一年级的自己写一封信。他们发誓信一定会送到，没人知道是怎么送的。有些四年级生发誓自己收到过。\n# en: A fourth-year writing by candlelight, a box of keepsakes open beside them: a pressed flower, a ticket stub, a borrowed wand returned.\nscene [61521a] = 一个四年级生在烛光下写信，身旁打开着一个纪念品盒子：一朵压花、一张票根、一根借来又还掉的魔杖。\n# en: Tell them it'll be all right\nopt.left.label [c88d16] = 告诉自己一切都会好的\n# en: You write: \"It's going to be all right. Not easy. All right.\" The next morning the letter is gone, and you remember, very faintly, reading it once on a sky-barge.\nopt.left.say.1 [1f6e42] = 你写道：“会好起来的。不会容易，但会好起来。”第二天早上信不见了，而你隐约记起，自己曾在一艘天空驳船上读过它。\n# en: You try to write something comforting, and it keeps coming out as a list of warnings. You seal it anyway. The next morning it is gone.\nopt.left.say.2 [8efc83] = 你想写些安慰的话，可写出来总像一张警告清单。你还是把信封好了。第二天早上，它不见了。\n# en: Warn them about everything\nopt.right.label [065681] = 把所有事都警告一遍\n# en: You write eleven pages of warnings. The letter is returned unopened with a note in Divination ink: \"Some things must be learned the hard way.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [4ed2e4] = 你写了十一页的警告。信原封未动地退了回来，附着一张用占卜墨水写的字条：“有些事必须吃过苦头才学得会。”\n\n@event y3_maud_seeds\n# en: Seeds for Later\ntitle [6b0198] = 留给以后的种子\n# en: Maud presses a paper packet into your hands. \"Seeds,\" she says. \"From the secret garden. Plant them wherever you end up. They'll grow anywhere someone is a bit kind to them.\" She sniffs. \"Like you did.\"\ntext [59075b] = 莫德把一个纸包塞进你手里。“种子，”她说，“秘密花园里的。不管你最后去了哪儿，都种下去。只要有人对它们好一点，它们在哪儿都能长。”她吸了吸鼻子。“就像你一样。”\n# en: An old woman pressing a small paper packet of seeds into a student's hands at the garden gate, bees drifting in the evening light.\nscene [f66df9] = 花园门口，一位老妇人把一小包种子按进学生手里，暮色中蜜蜂缓缓飞舞。\n# en: Hug her\nopt.left.label [042130] = 拥抱她\n# en: She protests for exactly one second and then hugs back, fierce and small and smelling of rosemary. \"Go on,\" she says. \"Before I get soppy.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [51b153] = 她抗议了整整一秒钟，然后用力回抱你，又小又结实，带着迷迭香的味道。“去吧，”她说，“趁我还没变得肉麻。”\n# en: Promise to come back and visit\nopt.right.label [17c08e] = 答应回来看她\n# en: \"Every summer,\" you promise. \"We'll see,\" she says, but she writes it on her calendar, in ink, which for Maud is a blood oath.\nopt.right.say.1 [96e47e] = “每年夏天都来，”你保证。“走着瞧，”她说，却把这件事写在了日历上——用墨水写的，对莫德来说这等于血誓。\n\n@event y3_last_duel\n# en: One Last Duel\ntitle [15df69] = 最后一场决斗\n# en: Dawn, the empty dueling ring, no crowd. Lyra is waiting. \"No referee,\" she says. \"No points. Just one last duel, for us. So we know.\"\ntext [5b0db9] = 黎明，空荡荡的决斗场，没有观众。莱拉在等你。“没有裁判，”她说，“不计分。就最后一场决斗，为了我们自己。这样我们就知道了。”\n# en: Two duelists bowing in an empty dueling ring at dawn, no crowd, no referee, just the mist and the lake beyond.\nscene [b6c6d5] = 黎明时分，两名决斗者在空无一人的决斗场里互相鞠躬，没有观众，没有裁判，只有薄雾和远处的湖。\n# en: Duel her with everything\nopt.left.label [9ed8a1] = 全力以赴和她决斗\n# en: It lasts eleven minutes. Nobody wins. You both end up flat on your backs, laughing at the sky. \"Now we know,\" she says.\nopt.left.say.1 [e4826f] = 打了十一分钟。没有人赢。最后你们俩都仰面躺在地上，对着天空大笑。“现在我们知道了，”她说。\n# en: She wins, cleanly. Then she helps you up and doesn't let go of your hand for a moment longer than necessary. \"Now we know.\"\nopt.left.say.2 [cc0906] = 她干净利落地赢了。然后她把你拉起来，握着你的手比必要的多停了一会儿。“现在我们知道了。”\n# en: Lower your wand and just talk\nopt.right.label [075cd4] = 放下魔杖，只是聊聊\n# en: You talk until the mist burns off. About fire, and mothers, and what comes after. You both miss the morning lecture. It turns out you didn't need to duel to know.\nopt.right.say.1 [e438e7] = 你们一直聊到薄雾散尽。聊火焰，聊母亲，聊以后的事。你们俩都错过了早课。原来不需要决斗也能知道。\n\n@event y3_time_capsule\n# en: The Time Capsule\ntitle [f63962] = 时间胶囊\n# en: Your friends have a battered tin box, a shovel and a plan: bury a time capsule under the old oak, to be opened in twenty years. Everyone puts something in. What will you give?\ntext [950958] = 你的朋友们带来了一个破旧的铁皮盒、一把铲子和一个计划：在老橡树下埋一个时间胶囊，二十年后再打开。每个人都要放点东西进去。你放什么？\n# en: A group of students kneeling around a hole under an old oak, lowering in a battered tin box sealed with wax.\nscene [cd24b8] = 一群学生跪在老橡树下的坑边，把一个用蜡封好的破铁皮盒放进去。\n# en: Something precious\nopt.left.label [3a8593] = 放一件珍贵的东西\n# en: You put in the first thing you ever enchanted. It's ugly and crooked and it still hums. Someone cries, and blames the dirt.\nopt.left.say.1 [1fe988] = 你放进了你施过魔法的第一件东西。它又丑又歪，至今还在嗡嗡作响。有人哭了，然后怪是土进了眼睛。\n# en: Something ridiculous\nopt.right.label [957c3e] = 放一件荒唐的东西\n# en: You put in a single sock, labelled \"EVIDENCE\". In twenty years, nobody will remember why. That's the point.\nopt.right.say.1 [fa92c3] = 你放进了一只袜子，上面贴着标签：“证物”。二十年后，没人会记得为什么。这正是意义所在。\n\n@event y3_speech\n# en: The Graduation Speech\ntitle [ffc856] = 毕业致辞\n# en: The headmistress catches you in the great hall, where chairs are already being set out. \"Each year one graduate speaks for their class,\" she says. \"This year, the faculty would like it to be you.\"\ntext [eebc6f] = 院长在大礼堂里叫住了你，那里已经开始摆椅子了。“每年都由一位毕业生代表全班致辞，”她说，“今年，教职员们希望是你。”\n# en: A headmistress handing a scroll to a student in an empty great hall, rows of chairs set out for a ceremony that hasn't happened yet.\nscene [9ef184] = 空荡荡的大礼堂里，一位院长把一卷纸递给一名学生，一排排椅子已为尚未举行的典礼摆好。\n# en: Accept and write something honest\nopt.left.label [d1eed2] = 接受，写些真心话\n# en: You write about fear, and friends, and the lake, and the things you didn't know you'd miss. When you practise it alone, your voice cracks in the same place every time. You leave it in.\nopt.left.say.1 [35d90b] = 你写恐惧，写朋友，写湖，写那些你从不知道自己会怀念的东西。独自练习时，你的声音每次都在同一个地方哽住。你把它留在了稿子里。\n# en: Decline — someone else deserves it\nopt.right.label [121bee] = 婉拒——值得的另有其人\n# en: \"Interesting,\" says the headmistress, which from her is a medal. The speech goes to a nervous first-generation student who absolutely brings the house down.\nopt.right.say.1 [9ddc3d] = “有意思，”院长说——从她嘴里说出来，这就是一枚勋章。致辞的机会给了一位紧张的、家里第一代上学的学生，那位学生把全场都点燃了。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/festivals.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/festivals.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event starfall_jars\n# en: Star Jars\ntitle [b671d5] = 星罐\n# en: {The Stargazers have set up tables under the dome, and everyone is making a jar to catch a falling star in tonight. Nobody has caught one in years. Everyone tries.|The jar tables are out under the dome again, and the first-years are making theirs all wrong.|Star jars again. You know which glass holds and which cracks in the cold.} {The Stargazers are arguing about the forecast.|Somebody has painted a jar entirely gold.|The telescope keeps swinging toward the lake.}\ntext [495f07] = {观星社在穹顶下摆好了桌子，每个人都在做一只罐子，好在今晚接住一颗坠落的星星。好些年没人接住过了。人人都还是要试。|罐子桌又摆到了穹顶下，一年级生们的罐子全做错了。|又是星罐。哪种玻璃经得住寒气、哪种会裂，你已经心里有数了。} {观星社的人在争论天气预报。|有人把一只罐子整个涂成了金色。|望远镜总是往湖的方向转。}\n# en: Long trestle tables under the observatory dome covered in glass jars, students painting stars on them, one of the Stargazers handing out corks.\nscene [04d125] = 观星台穹顶下的长条桌上摆满了玻璃罐，学生们在上面画星星，一位观星社的社员在分发软木塞。\n# en: Make a proper jar\nopt.left.label [2c03ab] = 做一只像样的罐子\n# en: {Thick glass, a cork sealed with wax, a rune for luck scratched into the lid. \"Better odds tonight,\" says one of the Stargazers. \"Theoretically.\"|You buy the good glass and take your time over it. It's the best jar on the table, and you know it.|You pick the glass that holds in the cold without thinking, and seal it twice. It's the jar the first-years copy.}\nopt.left.say.1 [752062] = {厚玻璃，用蜡封的软木塞，盖子上刻一道祈求好运的符文。“今晚的胜算更大了，”一位观星社的社员说。“理论上。”|你买了好玻璃，慢慢地做。这是桌上最好的一只罐子，你自己也知道。|你想都没想就挑了耐冻的玻璃，封了两道。一年级生们照着做的就是你这只。}\n# en: Help the Stargazers set up the roof\nopt.right.label [7691a1] = 帮观星社布置屋顶\n# en: {You haul blankets and telescopes up the observatory stairs all afternoon. Your arms ache. The Stargazers save you the best spot on the roof.|Blankets, cushions, a kettle that has to be carried up four hundred steps. The Stargazers know your name by the end of it.|You haul the blankets up and turn the telescope back from the lake three times. It keeps swinging back. The Stargazers give up and save you a spot.}\nopt.right.say.1 [73b06c] = {你一下午都在把毯子和望远镜往观星台的楼梯上搬。胳膊酸疼。观星社给你留了屋顶上最好的位置。|毯子、坐垫，还有一只得扛着爬四百级台阶的水壶。到最后，观星社的人都记住了你的名字。|你把毯子搬上去，三次把望远镜从湖那边转回来。它总是又转回去。观星社的人放弃了，给你留了个位置。}\n\n@event starfall_together\n# en: A Blanket for Two\ntitle [7474ae] = 两人一条毯子\n# en: You find {partner} on the lawn below the observatory with a blanket already spread for two and a flask of something hot. \"I saved you the sky,\" {they} say{s}. The first star falls before you've sat down.\ntext [894c38] = 你在观星台下的草坪上找到了 {partner}，毯子已经为两个人铺好了，还带着一壶热乎乎的东西。“我给你留了一片天空，”{they} 说。你还没坐下，第一颗星星就落了下来。\n# en: Two students lying on a blanket on the lawn at night, wrapped in one shawl, a falling star streaking over the castle towers.\nscene [cd2222] = 夜晚的草坪上，两个学生裹着同一条披肩躺在毯子上，一颗流星划过城堡的塔楼。\n# en: Stay out until dawn\nopt.left.label [b0f836] = 在外面待到天亮\n# en: You stay out until the sky turns grey, counting stars and then not counting anything. You're both frozen and hoarse and late for breakfast, and it was worth all of it.\nopt.left.say.1 [ce070c] = 你们一直待到天色发灰，先是数星星，后来什么也不数了。你们俩都冻僵了，嗓子也哑了，早饭也迟到了，而这一切都值得。\n# en: Tell {them} what you wished for\nopt.right.label [28c1f2] = 告诉 {them} 你许了什么愿\n# en: You say it out loud, which you're told ruins a wish. {partner} goes very quiet and then tells you {their} own. Neither of you minds if it's ruined.\nopt.right.say.1 [069e3e] = 你把它说出了口，据说这样愿望就不灵了。{partner} 安静了很久，然后也告诉了你 {their}愿望。你们俩都不在乎它灵不灵了。\n\n@event starfall_wish\n# en: A Wish on a Falling Star\ntitle [a426d8] = 向流星许愿\n# en: The oldest Starfall custom: pick one star, the brightest you'll see all night, and give it one wish. The Stargazers say a wish you spend on someone else falls slower. Nobody knows what that means. A very bright one is coming down over the lake.\ntext [c2e706] = 星坠节最古老的习俗：挑一颗星，这一夜你能看到的最亮的那颗，把一个愿望交给它。观星社的人说，为别人许的愿，星星会落得慢一些。没人知道这是什么意思。一颗非常亮的星正朝湖面落下。\n# en: A student kneeling on the observatory roof with eyes closed, hands clasped, a bright star falling over the dark lake below.\nscene [ba4b65] = 一个学生跪在观星台屋顶上，闭着眼睛，双手合十，一颗明亮的星星正落向下方漆黑的湖面。\n# en: Wish for your own year\nopt.left.label [e770b9] = 为自己的这一年许愿\n# en: You wish hard for your own year, and the star burns out over the water. It feels like a promise you've made to yourself, which is heavier than you expected.\nopt.left.say.1 [3035ed] = 你用力地为自己的这一年许了愿，那颗星在水面上燃尽了。这感觉像是对自己许下的承诺，比你想象的要沉。\n# en: Wish for someone else\nopt.right.label [bc6686] = 为别人许愿\n# en: You don't say who. The star falls slowly, or you imagine it does, all the way down to the lake.\nopt.right.say.1 [7dd3ec] = 你没说是为谁。那颗星落得很慢，或者是你以为它很慢，一直落进了湖里。\n\n@event starfall_caught\n# en: The Star in the Jar\ntitle [3b66bd] = 罐中之星\n# en: The star you caught at Starfall still glows in its jar on your windowsill, a little dimmer each night. A collector in the village has offered good money for it. One of the Stargazers says it would like to go back up, if stars like anything.\ntext [f775be] = 你在星坠节接住的那颗星，还在窗台上的罐子里发着光，一夜比一夜暗一点。村里有位收藏家出了个好价钱。一位观星社的社员说，如果星星也会想要什么的话，它想回到天上去。\n# en: A glass jar on a dormitory windowsill holding a tiny glowing star, a pale light on the curtains, frost on the outside of the glass.\nscene [c5924e] = 宿舍窗台上的一只玻璃罐里装着一颗发光的小星星，窗帘上映着淡淡的光，玻璃外结了霜。\n# en: Keep it by your bed\nopt.keep.label [b48f7a] = 把它放在床边\n# en: You keep it, and buy oil to keep the window frost off it. It fades by spring, slowly, like a coal. The last night it glows, you sit up with it.\nopt.keep.say.1 [fb1cf4] = 你留下了它，还买了灯油来防止窗户结霜。它到春天才慢慢熄灭，像一块炭。它发光的最后一夜，你一直陪着它。\n# en: Sell it to the collector\nopt.sell.label [90e67f] = 卖给收藏家\n# en: The collector holds it up to the light and pays without haggling. The windowsill looks very dark that night.\nopt.sell.say.1 [30c842] = 收藏家把它举到灯下，一分没还价就付了钱。那天晚上，窗台显得格外暗。\n# en: Let it go from the roof\nopt.release.label [6abecc] = 从屋顶放它走\n# en: You climb the observatory at midnight and open the jar. It hangs there a moment, then rises, slow and sure, until you can't tell it from the others. A few people saw. The story goes round by breakfast.\nopt.release.say.1 [233362] = 你半夜爬上观星台，打开了罐子。它在那儿悬停了片刻，然后缓缓地、坚定地升起，直到你再也分不清它和其他星星。有几个人看见了。到早饭时，这个故事已经传开了。\n\n@event starfall_at_home\n# en: Starfall at Home\ntitle [e21d08] = 家中的星坠节\n# en: {Starfall at home. There's no observatory here, only the cottage roof and the whole family on it in quilts, as when you were small.|Starfall at home again. Someone has saved you the warm patch of roof by the chimney.|The roof, the quilts, the chimney. Someone asks what the academy does for Starfall, and you find you'd rather be here.} {The village dogs howl at every star.|Somebody's brought cocoa.|A neighbour is telling the one about the star that fell in the duck pond.}\ntext [0fc251] = {家里的星坠节。这里没有观星台，只有小屋的屋顶，全家人裹着被子坐在上面，就像你小时候那样。|又是在家过星坠节。有人给你留了烟囱旁那块暖和的屋顶。|屋顶、被子、烟囱。有人问学院里怎么过星坠节，你发现自己更愿意待在这儿。} {村里的狗冲着每一颗星星嚎叫。|有人带了热可可。|一位邻居在讲那颗星星掉进鸭塘的老故事。}\n# en: A family on the roof of a cottage at night, wrapped in quilts, a grandmother pointing at a streak of light across the sky.\nscene [bb653e] = 夜里，一家人裹着被子坐在小屋的屋顶上，一位老奶奶指着划过天空的一道光。\n# en: Tell them about the academy\nopt.left.label [e245d0] = 给他们讲学院的事\n# en: {You tell them about the towers and the lake and the staircase that moves, and they ask questions until the stars run out. It's past three when you climb down, cold to the bone and glad.|Everyone wants a story. You give them the best ones, slightly improved, until your feet have gone numb on the slates. Nobody minds.|You tell them about the academy, and then the neighbour tells the one about the duck pond, which is better. You laugh until the slates are cold.}\nopt.left.say.1 [70d425] = {你给他们讲塔楼、湖，还有会移动的楼梯，他们一直问到星星落完。你爬下屋顶时已经过了三点，冻透了，心里却很高兴。|每个人都想听故事。你把最好的那些讲给他们听，稍微润色了一下，一直讲到脚在瓦片上冻麻了。没人介意。|你给他们讲学院的事，然后邻居讲了鸭塘那个故事，讲得比你好。你们笑到瓦片都凉了。}\n# en: Slip off to the hill alone\nopt.right.label [1f9874] = 独自溜到山坡上\n# en: {From the hill behind the cottage you name every star the way they taught you, and a few the way you learned as a child. Both names are right.|Alone on the hill, you watch the sky the way an academy student does now, and miss, a little, the way you used to.|From the hill you can still hear the neighbour telling the one about the duck pond. You find you'd rather be on the roof, and walk back before the end.}\nopt.right.say.1 [d6c0a0] = {在小屋后面的山坡上，你按学院教的方法给每颗星星命名，又按小时候学的叫法叫了几颗。两个名字都对。|你独自在山坡上，用学院学生的眼光看着天空，有一点怀念从前看天的样子。|在山坡上，你还能听见邻居在讲鸭塘那个故事。你发现自己更想待在屋顶上，没等故事讲完就走了回去。}\n\n@event starfall_on_internship\n# en: Starfall Far From Home\ntitle [c56ee2] = 远离家乡的星坠节\n# en: Starfall, and you're far from the observatory roof. {member(intern_expedition): Up at the ridge camp the sky is enormous, and the expedition's astronomers are working through it, counting every streak.|{member(intern_thornwick): Thornwick marks Starfall with illusions, and the lawn is full of false stars falling among the real ones. {met(tamsin): Tamsin finds you and points out which ones are theirs.|}|{member(intern_field_station): At the field station the creatures are restless under the falling stars, and someone has to sit up with them.|{member(intern_royal_library) or member(intern_unseen_office): The city has no lawns, only rooftops, and every one of them has people on it tonight.|The other interns have found a flat roof and a crate of something fizzy, and your supervisor is pretending not to know.}}}}\ntext [daf3a0] = 星坠节，而你离观星台的屋顶很远。{member(intern_expedition): 山脊营地上的天空无比辽阔，考察队的天文学家们正在一颗颗地记录，每一道流光都不放过。|{member(intern_thornwick): 荆木学院用幻术庆祝星坠节，草坪上假的星星和真的星星一起坠落。{met(tamsin): 塔姆辛找到了你，指给你看哪些是 TA 做的。|}|{member(intern_field_station): 在野外观测站，动物们在坠落的星光下躁动不安，总得有人守着它们。|{member(intern_royal_library) or member(intern_unseen_office): 城里没有草坪，只有屋顶，而今晚每一片屋顶上都有人。|其他实习生找到了一片平屋顶和一箱冒泡的饮料，你的导师假装不知道。}}}}\n# en: A group of interns on a flat roof far from the academy, wrapped in coats, watching falling stars over unfamiliar rooftops.\nscene [2a4dd5] = 远离学院的一片平屋顶上，一群实习生裹着外套，看着陌生屋顶上空坠落的星星。\n# en: Join the others on the roof\nopt.left.label [a3d8dd] = 和其他人一起上屋顶\n# en: {~You lie on a strange roof among people you met two months ago and count stars until they feel like old friends. Tomorrow's work goes slowly.|Coats, a borrowed blanket, someone's terrible song. It's not the observatory roof, and it's good anyway, though the morning's work comes out half asleep.}\nopt.left.say.1 [985ac4] = {~你躺在一片陌生的屋顶上，身边是两个月前才认识的人，数星星数到他们仿佛成了老朋友。第二天的工作进展很慢。|外套、借来的毯子、某人糟糕透顶的歌。这不是观星台的屋顶，但也很好，只是第二天早上的活儿干得半梦半醒。}\n# en: Keep working through the night\nopt.right.label [8ae9d3] = 通宵工作\n# en: {~You work through it with the window open, glancing up now and then. Your supervisor finds the finished work on the desk at dawn and raises an eyebrow, which is a great deal from them.|The stars fall outside while you finish the job. You see a few. Your supervisor sees all of it.}\nopt.right.say.1 [88a708] = {~你开着窗工作了一整夜，时不时抬头看一眼。导师在黎明时发现桌上完成的工作，扬了扬眉毛，对他们来说这已经是很高的评价了。|星星在窗外坠落，你在屋里把活干完。你看到了几颗。你的导师看到了全部。}\n\n@event ball_robes\n# en: Something to Wear\ntitle [d096f3] = 穿什么好\n# en: {year == 1: The Midwinter Ball is tonight, and you have nothing to wear that isn't a uniform or a nightshirt.|{final_year: The last ball of your life as a student. The old robes look very tired.|{~Ball night again, and last year's robes have shrunk, somehow, in the trunk.|Ball night again. Your robes have a stain on the cuff you'd forgotten about.|Ball night again. The robes fit, but you're tired of the sight of them.}}} {~The village tailor is open till three.|Your roommate is already dressed and gloating.|The mirror has opinions.}\ntext [f420a9] = {year == 1: 仲冬舞会就在今晚，而你除了校服和睡衣，什么能穿的都没有。|{final_year: 你学生时代的最后一场舞会。旧礼袍看起来疲惫极了。|{~又到舞会之夜，去年的礼袍不知怎么在箱子里缩水了。|又到舞会之夜。你的礼袍袖口上有块你早忘了的污渍。|又到舞会之夜。礼袍倒是合身，可你已经看腻了。}}} {~村里的裁缝铺开到三点。|你的室友已经穿戴整齐，正在得意。|镜子有自己的看法。}\n# en: A dormitory room strewn with robes, a student holding up a moth-eaten formal robe against themselves before a cracked mirror.\nscene [e36b69] = 宿舍里散落着各式礼袍，一个学生站在裂了缝的镜子前，把一件被虫蛀过的礼袍比在身上。\n# en: Buy new robes in the village\nopt.left.label [b6ca12] = 去村里买新礼袍\n# en: {~Deep blue, with a lining like the night sky. In the tailor's mirror you look, for once, like someone in a portrait.|New robes, a sensible colour and an insensible price. They fit as if they were waiting for you.|Something bold, chosen in a hurry. The mirror's opinions, for once, are all good ones.}\nopt.left.say.1 [97f189] = {~深蓝色，衬里像夜空。在裁缝铺的镜子里，你难得一次看起来像画像里的人。|新礼袍，颜色稳重，价格不稳重。合身得像是一直在等你。|匆匆挑了一件大胆的。镜子这回的意见，难得全是好话。}\n# en: Glamour your old ones\nopt.right.label [f20065] = 给旧礼袍施个幻术\n# en: The old robes shimmer into silk. Nobody guesses, and three people ask where you had them made.\nopt.right.say.1 [3d5149] = 旧礼袍闪烁着变成了丝绸。没人看出来，还有三个人问你是在哪儿做的。\n# en: At the stroke of nine your glamour slips, and the mended elbow shows through the silk like a confession. You keep your arm by your side all night.\nopt.right.say.2 [76df3a] = 九点钟一到，你的幻术失效了，补过的手肘透过丝绸露了出来，像一份供词。你整晚都把胳膊贴在身侧。\n\n@event ball_ice_garden\n# en: The Ice Garden\ntitle [ed8110] = 冰之花园\n# en: {The frost-mages have turned the courtyard into an ice garden for the ball. Frozen roses line the paths, and the fountain has stopped mid-splash.|The ice garden again, bigger this year, with a hedge maze of frost.|The ice garden, and you know its paths by heart now.} {Couples are walking it before the music has started.|A first-year has got their tongue stuck to a rose.|The lanterns make the ice glow blue.}\ntext [398f71] = {霜法师们把庭院变成了一座迎接舞会的冰之花园。冰冻的玫瑰排列在小径两旁，喷泉停在了水花溅起的那一刻。|又是冰之花园，今年更大了，还有一座霜做的树篱迷宫。|冰之花园，你现在已经熟悉它的每一条小径了。} {音乐还没响起，情侣们已经在里面漫步了。|一个一年级生的舌头粘在了一朵玫瑰上。|灯笼让冰透出蓝光。}\n# en: A castle courtyard turned into a garden of ice, frozen roses along glittering paths, a fountain stopped mid-splash, students walking under lanterns.\nscene [47b745] = 城堡庭院变成了一座冰的花园，闪光的小径旁是冰冻的玫瑰，喷泉停在水花溅起的一刻，学生们在灯笼下漫步。\n# en: Walk it slowly\nopt.left.label [3e196d] = 慢慢地逛\n# en: {You walk every path twice, until your toes are numb and your head is clear. The fountain's frozen spray catches the light like a held breath.|You find the centre of the maze, where someone has left a frozen bench and a frozen apple. You sit a while, until the cold comes up through your shoes.|You know the paths by heart, so you take the ones nobody else finds. Under the blue lanterns you have the garden to yourself for a while.}\nopt.left.say.1 [db9c17] = {每条小径你都走了两遍，直到脚趾冻麻、脑袋清醒。喷泉冻住的水花映着光，像一口屏住的呼吸。|你找到了迷宫的中心，那里有人留下了一张冰冻的长椅和一个冰冻的苹果。你坐了一会儿，直到寒气从鞋底往上钻。|小径你都烂熟于心，于是专挑别人找不到的走。在蓝色的灯笼下，花园有一阵子只属于你。}\n# en: Help the frost-mages finish it\nopt.right.label [946705] = 帮霜法师们收尾\n# en: {They show you how to hold the cold in a petal until it sets. Your hands ache for an hour. One of the roses on the path is yours.|You shape a whole archway of ice under instruction, fingers stiff with cold, and it holds. The frost-mages nod, which is how they applaud.|This year they let you shape a lantern of ice. It glows blue on the path, and nobody knows it's yours but you.}\nopt.right.say.1 [f206d8] = {他们教你怎么把寒气留在花瓣里直到它定形。你的手疼了一个小时。小径上有一朵玫瑰是你做的。|你在指导下塑出了一整座冰拱门，手指冻得发僵，而它立住了。霜法师们点了点头，他们就是这样鼓掌的。|今年他们让你塑了一盏冰灯笼。它在小径上发着蓝光，除了你，谁也不知道是你做的。}\n\n@event ball_last_dance\n# en: The Last Waltz\ntitle [686d0e] = 最后一支华尔兹\n# en: Near midnight the musicians play the last waltz, slow and a little sad. {npc} holds out a hand without saying anything. Half the hall has gone to bed. The chandelier is dripping.\ntext [ddb155] = 快到午夜时，乐师们奏起了最后一支华尔兹，缓慢，带着一点忧伤。{npc} 一言不发地伸出手。大厅里一半的人已经去睡了。枝形吊灯在滴水。\n# en: The great hall near midnight, candles burning low, a single couple still turning slowly beneath a dripping ice-crystal chandelier.\nscene [cf17c9] = 将近午夜的大礼堂，蜡烛快要燃尽，一盏滴着水的冰晶吊灯下，只剩一对舞伴还在缓缓旋转。\n# en: Dance until the music stops\nopt.left.label [bf83ad] = 一直跳到音乐停止\n# en: You dance it slowly, and then once more after the musicians have packed up, to no music at all. Nobody else is left to see, which is the point.\nopt.left.say.1 [7c3db6] = 你们慢慢地跳完这一支，然后在乐师们收拾好乐器之后，在没有音乐的寂静里又跳了一遍。没有别人留下来看，这正是重点。\n# en: Slip out onto the frozen terrace\nopt.right.label [50e9ea] = 溜到结冰的露台上\n# en: Outside it's bitter, and the stars are out over the lake. You talk until you can't feel your fingers, about nothing and then about everything, and go back in blue-lipped and grinning.\nopt.right.say.1 [dfa865] = 外面冷得刺骨，湖面上空满是星星。你们一直聊到手指失去知觉，先聊无关紧要的事，后来聊了一切，回去时嘴唇发紫，笑得合不拢嘴。\n\n@event ball_snowball_war\n# en: The Snowball War\ntitle [da2e94] = 雪仗\n# en: {After the ball, someone throws the first snowball in the courtyard, and within a minute every house is at war, in ball robes.|The snowball war, as much a tradition as the ball. The forts went up during the last waltz.|The snowball war again. The first-years have built a fort that is frankly impressive.} {A prefect is trying to restore order and failing.|Someone has enchanted their snowballs, which is cheating.|The robes are already ruined.}\ntext [6ef7aa] = {舞会结束后，有人在庭院里扔出了第一个雪球，不到一分钟，各学院就穿着舞会礼服打成了一片。|雪仗，和舞会一样是传统。雪堡是在最后一支华尔兹时堆起来的。|又是雪仗。一年级生们堆的雪堡坦白说相当壮观。} {一位级长想恢复秩序，没成功。|有人给自己的雪球施了魔法，这是作弊。|礼服早就毁了。}\n# en: A snowy courtyard at night full of students in ruined ball robes hurling snowballs from behind snow forts, a prefect ducking.\nscene [733bb8] = 夜晚积雪的庭院里，一群穿着破烂舞会礼服的学生躲在雪堡后面互扔雪球，一位级长正在闪躲。\n# en: Charge the enemy fort\nopt.left.label [f5a047] = 冲向敌方雪堡\n# en: You take the fort with a war cry and a single perfect throw. Your house carries you round the fountain on its shoulders. Your robes will never recover.\nopt.left.say.1 [0b4ca7] = 你一声战吼加一记完美的投掷，攻下了雪堡。你的学院把你扛在肩上绕着喷泉转圈。你的礼服再也恢复不了了。\n# en: A snowball, then fourteen. You go down laughing under a drift, and someone has to dig you out. Worth it.\nopt.left.say.2 [ff0315] = 一个雪球，然后是十四个。你笑着被埋进了雪堆，还得有人把你挖出来。值了。\n# en: Watch from the cloister with cocoa\nopt.right.label [6ac2a1] = 端着可可在回廊里看\n# en: {You watch from the cloister, warm and dry, and cheer for whoever's losing. Nobody throws at you. Mostly.|You hold the cocoa and the coats, and you're the only one who goes to bed warm.|You watch the first-years' fort hold against all comers. Yours are the only robes in the courtyard that survive the night.}\nopt.right.say.1 [bcd394] = {你在回廊里看着，温暖又干燥，为输的那一方加油。没人朝你扔雪球。大多数时候。|你替大家拿着可可和外套，是唯一一个暖暖和和上床睡觉的人。|你看着一年级生的堡垒挡住了所有来犯者。整个庭院里，只有你的礼袍熬过了这一夜。}\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/fixtures.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/fixtures.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event amaranth_intro\n# en: No Excuses\ntitle [8daa28] = 不许找借口\n# en: You came in for a plaster. Sister Amaranth takes your hand, turns it to the light and asks how it happened. You tell her. She waits. You tell her the true version. \"Better,\" she says, and reaches for a bottle that is not a plaster.\ntext [2645db] = 你本来只是来要块膏药。阿玛兰斯修女拉过你的手，对着光转了转，问你怎么弄的。你告诉了她。她等着。你又把真实的经过讲了一遍。“这还差不多，”她说着，伸手去拿一个瓶子——不是膏药。\n# en: A stern healer in a white apron holding a student's wrist up to the window light, a row of tonic bottles behind her.\nscene [fc4802] = 一位系着白围裙的严厉治疗师，把一名学生的手腕举到窗前的光里，身后摆着一排补剂瓶。\n# en: Drink whatever it is\nopt.left.label [6cbe5e] = 不管是什么，喝了\n# en: It tastes of iron and regret. The hand stops throbbing before you reach the door. \"Come back if it swells,\" she calls. \"Don't come back if it doesn't.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [9fbd34] = 一股铁锈和后悔的味道。还没走到门口，手就不疼了。“肿了再来，”她在身后喊，“不肿就别来。”\n# en: Just the plaster, thanks\nopt.right.label [1353de] = 给块膏药就行，谢谢\n# en: She gives you the plaster, and a look you will remember the next time you lie to a healer. \"Three crowns,\" she says. \"The look is free.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [606886] = 她给了你膏药，外加一个眼神——下次你再想对治疗师撒谎时，一定会想起它。“三克朗，”她说，“眼神免费。”\n\n@event amaranth_rounds\n# en: Evening Rounds\ntitle [ee9b30] = 晚间查房\n# en: {Sister Amaranth catches you in the corridor and hands you a tray of tonics without a word of explanation.|Amaranth holds out the tonic tray as you pass. It seems you are on the rota now.|The tray is waiting for you on the ward table, labelled YOURS in Amaranth's square hand.} {Six beds on the ward today.|A first-year with hiccups that light up.|Someone has charmed their own eyebrows off.}\ntext [985a3b] = {阿玛兰斯修女在走廊里拦住你，一言不发地把一盘补剂塞到你手里。|你路过时，阿玛兰斯把补剂托盘递了过来。看来你已经上了值班表。|托盘在病房桌上等着你，上面贴着阿玛兰斯方方正正的字：“你的”。} {今天病房里六张床。|一个一年级生，打嗝会发光。|有人把自己的眉毛给咒没了。}\n# en: A healer and a student walking a long infirmary ward at dusk, the student carrying a tray of small glass bottles, lamps being lit along the beds.\nscene [9f326b] = 黄昏时分，一位治疗师和一名学生走在长长的病房里，学生端着一盘小玻璃瓶，病床边的灯一盏盏点亮。\n# en: Do the rounds with her\nopt.left.label [640527] = 跟她一起查房\n# en: {She tells you what each tonic does and why it tastes like that. You remember all of it, to your surprise.|You learn which patients are brave and which are loud, and that they are rarely the same ones.|At the last bed she lets you measure the dose. \"Again,\" she says. You do it again. \"Fine.\"}\nopt.left.say.1 [24b737] = {她告诉你每种补剂的用处，以及为什么是这个味道。出乎你自己意料，你全记住了。|你弄明白了哪些病人勇敢、哪些病人吵闹，而这两种人很少是同一批。|到最后一张床，她让你量剂量。“再来，”她说。你又量了一遍。“行。”}\n# en: You have an essay due\nopt.right.label [427f10] = 你还有篇论文要交\n# en: {\"So does everyone,\" she says, and takes the tray back. \"Off you go.\"|She nods, which is somehow worse than a lecture.|\"Next time,\" she says, as a fact, not a question.}\nopt.right.say.1 [afe44c] = {“谁不是呢，”她说着把托盘拿了回去，“走吧。”|她点点头，这比训你一顿还难受。|“下次，”她说——是陈述，不是询问。}\n# en: Mend the worst one yourself\nopt.up.label [0b5686] = 亲手治好最重的那个\n# en: {You close a split knuckle with a whisper of light. Amaranth inspects it for a long time. \"Neat,\" she says, the highest word she owns.|The sprain knits under your hands. The patient thanks Amaranth. Amaranth points at you.|The student with no eyebrows is the worst case. You coax them halfway back. Amaranth finishes the job, and writes your name on tomorrow's tray as well.}\nopt.up.say.1 [21db53] = {你轻声一念，一道微光合上了裂开的指节。阿玛兰斯端详了很久。“利落，”她说——这是她嘴里最高的评价。|扭伤在你手下愈合。病人向阿玛兰斯道谢，阿玛兰斯指了指你。|那个没了眉毛的学生最棘手。你哄回来了一半。阿玛兰斯收了尾，把你的名字也写上了明天的托盘。}\n\n@event amaranth_orders\n# en: Doctor's Orders\ntitle [abe905] = 医嘱\n# en: {Sister Amaranth has come to find you, which has never happened to anyone. \"You look like a draught of bad decisions,\" she says. \"Bed.\"|Amaranth again, at your door, arms folded. \"I can hear you coughing from the infirmary.\"|\"Bed,\" says Amaranth, before you've opened the door all the way.}\ntext [321559] = {阿玛兰斯修女竟然主动来找你了，这种事从没发生在任何人身上。“你看起来像一剂馊主意，”她说，“上床。”|又是阿玛兰斯，抱着胳膊站在你门口。“我在医务室都听得见你咳嗽。”|门还没完全打开，阿玛兰斯就说：“上床。”}\n# en: A healer standing in a dormitory doorway with her arms folded, a pale student halfway out of bed with a stack of books.\nscene [a0e5b7] = 一位治疗师抱着胳膊站在宿舍门口，一名脸色苍白的学生正抱着一摞书，半个身子下了床。\n# en: Go to bed as ordered\nopt.left.label [c01d90] = 遵医嘱，上床\n# en: {You sleep fourteen hours. She checks on you twice and leaves a tonic on the sill both times.|Two days of broth and boredom. You come out of it a person again.|You argue for one more chapter. You lose, and sleep.}\nopt.left.say.1 [42f270] = {你睡了十四个小时。她来看了你两次，两次都在窗台上留了一瓶补剂。|两天的肉汤和无聊。熬过去，你又像个人了。|你争取再读一章。你输了，睡觉。}\n# en: \"I'm fine, honestly.\"\nopt.right.label [9142ac] = “我没事，真的。”\n# en: {\"Nobody who says 'honestly' is,\" she says, and leaves. You are not fine by Thursday.|She writes something on a card and walks off. It turns out to be your name, on her list.|\"Bed,\" she says again, as if you hadn't spoken. You don't go. By the weekend you wish you had.}\nopt.right.say.1 [091428] = {“说‘真的’的人都有事，”她说完就走了。到了周四，你果然有事。|她在一张卡片上写了点什么就走了。后来发现那是你的名字，写在她的名单上。|“床，”她又说了一遍，好像你什么也没说。你没去。到了周末，你后悔了。}\n\n@event amaranth_trust\n# en: The Cupboard Key\ntitle [11b16f] = 柜子钥匙\n# en: Sister Amaranth closes the storeroom door behind you both. \"I'm away for the solstice and the junior healer faints at blood,\" she says. \"You don't.\" She holds out the key to the tonic cupboard, and with it a page of her own notes on the mending charm.\ntext [8d3c0e] = 阿玛兰斯修女随手关上储藏室的门。“冬至那几天我不在，见习治疗师一见血就晕，”她说，“你不会。”她递来补剂柜的钥匙，还有一页她自己写的愈合咒笔记。\n# en: An infirmary storeroom at night lit by a single lamp, a healer pressing a small brass key into a student's palm, shelves of labelled bottles all around.\nscene [f064f3] = 夜里的医务室储藏室，只点着一盏灯，一位治疗师把一把小铜钥匙按进学生掌心，四周的架子上全是贴好标签的瓶子。\n# en: Take the key and the notes\nopt.left.label [bdfda9] = 收下钥匙和笔记\n# en: \"Mend what you can, send the rest to me, and lie to nobody,\" she says. The notes are written for someone she expects to be good at this. You read them twice and try very hard to be.\nopt.left.say.1 [f5ce15] = “能治的治，治不了的送来给我，对谁都别撒谎，”她说。那页笔记是写给一个她认定会擅长此道的人的。你读了两遍，拼命想成为那样的人。\n# en: \"I'm not ready for that.\"\nopt.right.label [5088da] = “我还没准备好。”\n# en: She looks at you for a long moment. \"Good answer. Wrong, but good.\" She keeps the key. She gives you the notes anyway.\nopt.right.say.1 [a29e05] = 她看了你好一会儿。“好回答。错了，但是好回答。”钥匙她收回去了，笔记还是给了你。\n\n@event brask_shovel\n# en: Hands Wanted\ntitle [82086c] = 缺人手\n# en: {Professor Brask holds out a shovel as you pass the stables. She doesn't say anything. The hippogriff behind her does, loudly.|Brask hands you the shovel before you're through the door.|\"Ah,\" says Brask, and the shovel is already in your hands.} {The griffon chick is in a mood.|The owls have opinions about the new straw.|A hippogriff has eaten a glove. Possibly a professor's.}\ntext [25a91e] = {你路过马厩时，布拉斯克教授递过来一把铲子。她一句话没说。她身后的鹰头马身有翼兽倒是说了，而且很大声。|你还没进门，布拉斯克就把铲子递了过来。|“啊，”布拉斯克说，铲子已经在你手里了。} {狮鹫幼崽在闹脾气。|猫头鹰们对新铺的稻草颇有意见。|一只鹰头马身有翼兽吃了一只手套，可能是某位教授的。}\n# en: A weathered professor in a leather apron holding out a shovel to a student at a stable door, a hippogriff watching suspiciously from its stall.\nscene [b7d66c] = 一位饱经风霜、系着皮围裙的教授在马厩门口把铲子递给一名学生，一只鹰头马身有翼兽在隔栏里狐疑地看着。\n# en: Muck out the stalls\nopt.left.label [6ef185] = 清理马厩\n# en: {An hour of straw and silence. At the end Brask says, \"The grey one likes you,\" which is more than she has said to anyone this term.|She tells you a story about a manticore, a bucket and a bishop. It ends with a bite and a moral. You miss the moral.|The hippogriff bows to you on your way out. Brask pretends not to see.}\nopt.left.say.1 [e1fc8d] = {一个小时的稻草和沉默。最后布拉斯克说：“那只灰的喜欢你。”这比她这学期对任何人说的都多。|她给你讲了一个关于蝎尾狮、一只水桶和一位主教的故事。结尾是被咬了一口和一个道理。道理你没听清。|你走时，那只鹰头马身有翼兽向你鞠了一躬。布拉斯克假装没看见。}\n# en: Decline, politely\nopt.right.label [96c3eb] = 客气地推辞\n# en: {\"Suit yourself,\" she says, and takes the shovel back. \"The straw won't mind.\"|\"The hippogriff doesn't want to either,\" says Brask. \"It helps anyway.\"|You hand the shovel back. Brask takes it, and the hippogriff looks at your gloves with interest. You leave quickly.}\nopt.right.say.1 [022ce5] = {“随你，”她说，把铲子拿了回去，“稻草不会介意的。”|“那只鹰头马身有翼兽也不想干，”布拉斯克说，“可它照样帮忙。”|你把铲子递了回去。布拉斯克接过来，那只鹰头马身有翼兽饶有兴趣地盯着你的手套。你赶紧走了。}\n# en: Ask the stalls what they need\nopt.up.label [30eec0] = 问问它们需要什么\n# en: {The hippogriffs want the window open and the owls want it shut. You negotiate. Brask watches you with her arms folded and something almost like a smile.|The griffon chick tells you, at length, about a grievance. You pass it on. Brask nods. \"She's right, actually.\"|The hippogriff confesses to the glove, more or less, and you negotiate its return. Brask takes it back, damp, and says nothing, which from her is thanks.}\nopt.up.say.1 [d44e9b] = {鹰头马身有翼兽们要开窗，猫头鹰们要关窗。你从中斡旋。布拉斯克抱着胳膊看着你，脸上几乎有点笑意。|狮鹫幼崽向你絮絮叨叨地诉了一大通委屈。你转告了。布拉斯克点点头：“其实它说得对。”|那只鹰头马身有翼兽差不多承认了手套的事，你谈妥了归还。布拉斯克接过湿漉漉的手套，什么也没说，对她来说这就是道谢。}\n\n@event brask_night_owl\n# en: The Sick Owl\ntitle [a56a50] = 生病的猫头鹰\n# en: Brask stops you in the corridor and falls into step without asking, which is a first. \"The old barn owl won't eat,\" she says. \"Somebody should sit with her tonight. Somebody quiet.\" She is looking at you.\ntext [56bef6] = 布拉斯克在走廊里拦住你，招呼也不打就和你并肩走，这还是头一回。“那只老仓鸮不肯吃东西，”她说，“今晚得有人陪着她。得是个安静的人。”她看着你。\n# en: A dim stable loft at night, a student sitting cross-legged beside a straw nest where a sick barn owl sleeps under a blanket, a lantern turned low.\nscene [939266] = 夜里昏暗的马厩阁楼，一名学生盘腿坐在草窝旁，一只生病的仓鸮盖着毯子睡着，提灯拧得很暗。\n# en: Sit up with the owl\nopt.left.label [2fd221] = 守着猫头鹰熬夜\n# en: You sit in the loft till dawn, dripping water onto the owl's beak. At four she takes a mouse from your fingers. In the morning Brask looks at the empty dish for a long time, then claps you once on the shoulder, hard.\nopt.left.say.1 [bae058] = 你在阁楼里坐到天亮，一滴一滴往猫头鹰喙里喂水。凌晨四点，她从你指间叼走了一只老鼠。早上，布拉斯克盯着空碗看了很久，然后在你肩上狠狠拍了一下。\n# en: Send her to Sister Amaranth\nopt.right.label [53c847] = 让她去找阿玛兰斯修女\n# en: Amaranth looks at the owl, looks at you, and says she doesn't do owls. Then she sits with it all night. Brask is not sure how to feel about this, and neither are you.\nopt.right.say.1 [4f82b4] = 阿玛兰斯看看猫头鹰，又看看你，说她不治猫头鹰。然后她陪了它一整夜。布拉斯克不知该作何感想，你也一样。\n\n@event brask_trust\n# en: Old Courtesies\ntitle [39af84] = 古老的礼节\n# en: Brask takes you to the edge of the Whisperwood one afternoon. \"Most people talk at animals,\" she says. \"A few talk to them. Fewer listen.\" She bows, very low, to something in the trees. \"Do as I do. I'll teach you the rest.\"\ntext [5297d4] = 一天下午，布拉斯克带你到低语森林边。“大多数人对动物说话，”她说，“少数人和它们说话。更少的人肯听。”她朝树林里的什么东西深深鞠了一躬。“照我做。剩下的我来教。”\n# en: A professor and a student at the edge of a forest at dusk, both bowing low to a great antlered stag half-hidden in the trees.\nscene [5eaec1] = 黄昏的森林边缘，一位教授和一名学生向半隐在树间的一头巨大雄鹿深深鞠躬。\n# en: Bow, and learn\nopt.left.label [80ee0e] = 鞠躬，然后学\n# en: It takes until full dark, bent double until your back aches, and something huge breathes on your neck for most of it. Then, faintly, you understand a word. It is \"rude\". Brask laughs once, loudly, the way she does once a term.\nopt.left.say.1 [3382a7] = 一直学到天全黑，你弯着腰弯到背都酸了，大部分时间还有个庞然大物在你脖子后面喘气。然后，隐约地，你听懂了一个词：“没礼貌”。布拉斯克大笑了一声——就是她一学期一次的那种。\n# en: Keep your distance\nopt.right.label [0141a6] = 保持距离\n# en: You stay by the path and watch. She doesn't press you. On the walk back she talks about hoof care for an hour, which you suspect is a kindness.\nopt.right.say.1 [2e5c6a] = 你站在小路边看着。她没有勉强你。回去的路上她讲了一个小时的蹄子护理，你怀疑这是一种体贴。\n\n@event vey_pop_quiz\n# en: Is It There?\ntitle [5bb9f5] = 它在那儿吗？\n# en: {Professor Vey is in the corridor with two cups of tea. She offers you one. \"Only one of them is real,\" she says pleasantly.|Vey stops you on the stairs. \"There's a coin behind your ear. Or there isn't.\"|\"Good morning,\" says Vey, and the door you were about to open is, possibly, a wall.} {She looks delighted already.|A student behind you walks into something.|She is humming.}\ntext [b25450] = {维伊教授端着两杯茶站在走廊里，递给你一杯。“只有一杯是真的，”她和颜悦色地说。|维伊在楼梯上拦住你。“你耳朵后面有枚硬币。也可能没有。”|“早上好，”维伊说，而你正要推开的那扇门，可能是一堵墙。} {她看上去已经很高兴了。|你身后有个学生撞上了什么东西。|她在哼歌。}\n# en: A smiling professor in a violet scarf leaning against a corridor wall, a cup of tea in her hand, a second, slightly too perfect cup on the windowsill beside her.\nscene [ad6db0] = 一位系着紫罗兰色围巾的教授笑着倚在走廊墙边，手里端着一杯茶，旁边窗台上还有一杯，完美得有点过分。\n# en: Look closely\nopt.left.label [66d2dd] = 仔细看\n# en: {You pick the real one. Vey raises an eyebrow. \"Insufferable,\" she says, beaming.|You catch the shimmer at the edge. \"Ah,\" says Vey. \"You've been paying attention. Dreadful habit. Keep it.\"|You lay your hand flat on it first. It's a door. Vey stops humming. \"Pity,\" she says, delighted.}\nopt.left.say.1 [145cd8] = {你挑中了真的那个。维伊挑了挑眉。“讨厌鬼，”她笑逐颜开地说。|你捕捉到了边缘那一丝微光。“啊，”维伊说，“你一直在留心。坏习惯。保持住。”|你先把手掌贴了上去。是门。维伊不哼了。“可惜，”她说，高兴得很。}\n# en: {You reach for the real one and close your fingers on air. Vey enjoys this very much.|Wrong. \"It's usually the other one,\" she says, kindly, which is worse.|You walk confidently into what turns out to be a wall. Vey hums on, very pleased, while you hold your nose.}\nopt.left.say.2 [a8dd9c] = {你伸手去拿真的那个，手指却合在了空气上。维伊对此相当享受。|错了。“通常都是另一个，”她和气地说，这更糟。|你信心十足地走了过去，结果那是一堵墙。你捂着鼻子，维伊继续哼着歌，非常满意。}\n# en: Play along and get it wrong\nopt.right.label [e10069] = 配合她，故意猜错\n# en: {You choose the false one on purpose, with feeling. Vey narrows her eyes. \"Either you're bad at this, or very good,\" she says. \"I'll find out.\"|You let her win. She knows. She lets you know she knows.|You walk into it on purpose, braced for a wall. It's a door, and you fall through it. Vey hums all the way down the corridor.}\nopt.right.say.1 [f007d2] = {你故意、而且声情并茂地选了假的那个。维伊眯起眼睛。“要么你很不擅长这个，要么你非常擅长，”她说，“我会弄清楚的。”|你让她赢了。她知道。她也让你知道她知道。|你故意撞上去，准备好了撞墙。结果是门，你一头栽了进去。维伊哼着歌走完了整条走廊。}\n\n@event vey_revue\n# en: The Faculty Revue\ntitle [22db23] = 教职员汇演\n# en: Professor Vey is producing the faculty revue, in which professors make fools of themselves for charity. \"I need someone to play a king who isn't there,\" she says. \"You'd be wonderful. You'll be terrible. Either is fine.\"\ntext [551685] = 维伊教授在排演教职员汇演——教授们为慈善出丑的那种。“我需要一个人演一位并不在场的国王，”她说，“你会很出色。你会演砸。哪样都行。”\n# en: A small stage in the Great Hall hung with violet curtains, a professor directing a nervous student holding a paper crown, chairs being set out below.\nscene [44137b] = 大礼堂里一座挂着紫罗兰色幕布的小舞台，一位教授在指导一名拿着纸王冠的紧张学生，台下正在摆椅子。\n# en: Take the part\nopt.left.label [e6818e] = 接下这个角色\n# en: You are a king who isn't there, magnificently. Thorne, in the front row, says \"Hm\" twice, which the faculty agree is a standing ovation.\nopt.left.say.1 [d507d2] = 你把一位不在场的国王演得气势恢宏。前排的索恩说了两声“嗯”，教职员们一致认为这相当于起立鼓掌。\n# en: You forget your lines, invent new ones and are, by general agreement, the best thing in it for the wrong reasons. Vey keeps the paper crown on her desk.\nopt.left.say.2 [cd7f8a] = 你忘了台词，现编了新的，结果大家一致认为你是整场最精彩的部分——只是原因不太对。维伊把那顶纸王冠留在了她的书桌上。\n# en: Hide in the wings\nopt.right.label [f0270b] = 躲到侧幕\n# en: You run the curtain. The revue goes ahead without a king, which Vey says was the point all along.\nopt.right.say.1 [d2705a] = 你负责拉幕。汇演在没有国王的情况下照常进行，维伊说这正是重点所在。\n\n@event vey_trust\n# en: The Mirror Trick\ntitle [2b6124] = 镜像戏法\n# en: Vey keeps you after class and tilts a tall mirror towards you. Your reflection steps out of it and sits on a desk. \"The trick isn't making a copy,\" she says. \"It's knowing which of you to send. Want to learn it?\"\ntext [a3e14c] = 维伊课后留下你，把一面高高的镜子朝你倾过来。你的倒影从镜子里走出来，坐到了一张桌子上。“诀窍不在于造一个复制品，”她说，“而在于知道该派哪个你出去。想学吗？”\n# en: An empty classroom at twilight, two identical students standing either side of a tall mirror, a professor seated on a desk between them, clapping softly.\nscene [30e560] = 黄昏时空荡荡的教室，一面高大的镜子两侧站着两个一模一样的学生，一位教授坐在中间的桌子上轻轻鼓掌。\n# en: Learn it\nopt.left.label [267092] = 学\n# en: It takes a week. On the last night your reflection refuses to go back. Vey talks it round over tea, then says, \"Now you know why I don't do this in public.\" You are not sure which of you walked home.\nopt.left.say.1 [9e2416] = 学了一周。最后一晚，你的倒影不肯回去了。维伊边喝茶边把它劝了回去，然后说：“现在你知道我为什么不在人前用这个了。”走回去的是你们中的哪一个，你也说不准。\n# en: Keep your own face, thanks\nopt.right.label [78e875] = 谢了，我还是用自己的脸\n# en: \"Sensible,\" she says, disappointed. She teaches you instead how to tell when someone else is using it. It turns out a lot of people are.\nopt.right.say.1 [28577d] = “明智，”她说，有点失望。她转而教你怎么识破别人在用这招。结果发现，用的人还真不少。\n\n@event marisol_intro\n# en: A Dry Patch of Air\ntitle [7b3a88] = 一小片干燥的空气\n# en: It is raining on the whole lake except for one circle on the boathouse steps, where a woman in a soaked hat is reading poetry, perfectly dry. She looks up. \"It's not hard,\" says Professor Marisol. \"You just ask the water to go round. Sit, if you like.\"\ntext [86e87f] = 整片湖都在下雨，只有船屋台阶上的一个圆圈例外：一个戴着湿透帽子的女人在那里读诗，浑身干爽。她抬起头。“不难，”玛丽索尔教授说，“只要请水绕开就行。想坐的话就坐吧。”\n# en: Heavy rain on a lake shore, a woman reading on the boathouse steps beneath a perfectly dry circle of air, raindrops sliding around it as if around glass.\nscene [765fe2] = 湖岸大雨，一个女人坐在船屋台阶上读书，头顶是一圈完全干燥的空气，雨滴像绕过玻璃一样从四周滑落。\n# en: Sit and ask how\nopt.left.label [9059dd] = 坐下，问她怎么做到的\n# en: She explains it as a conversation. The water, apparently, is willing. By the end of the poem the rain is going round you too, for almost a minute, and then it remembers you. \"There,\" she says, and goes back to the poem.\nopt.left.say.1 [806739] = 她把这解释成一场对话。水显然是愿意的。一首诗读完时，雨也绕着你走了，差不多一分钟，然后它又想起了你。“好了，”她说，接着读她的诗。\n# en: Leave her to her book\nopt.right.label [1f57ac] = 别打扰她读书\n# en: You nod and run for cover. Behind you she reads aloud, to nobody, a poem about a storm that comes back every year to see if you've changed.\nopt.right.say.1 [b3911a] = 你点点头，跑去躲雨。身后，她对着空无一人的湖岸朗读一首诗，写的是一场每年都回来看看你变了没有的暴风雨。\n\n@event marisol_cold_water\n# en: Cold Water Is Good for You\ntitle [c84271] = 冷水对你有好处\n# en: {Professor Marisol is in the lake, waist-deep, and waves at you as though this were normal.|Marisol again, in the lake. \"It's lovely once you're in,\" she calls. It is not lovely.|\"You came!\" calls Marisol from the water. You had been walking past.} {There is ice at the edges.|A heron is watching her with professional respect.|She has a book in one hand, held perfectly dry.}\ntext [c110b4] = {玛丽索尔教授站在齐腰深的湖里，朝你挥手，仿佛这再正常不过。|又是玛丽索尔，又在湖里。“下来就舒服了！”她喊。一点也不舒服。|“你来啦！”玛丽索尔在水里喊。你本来只是路过。} {湖边结了冰。|一只苍鹭正以同行的敬意看着她。|她一只手举着书，书一点没湿。}\n# en: Dawn mist on a still lake, a professor standing waist-deep in the water waving cheerfully to a shivering student on the jetty.\nscene [2bea30] = 黎明的薄雾笼罩着平静的湖面，一位教授站在齐腰深的水里，朝码头上冻得发抖的学生快活地挥手。\n# en: Wade in\nopt.left.label [f7f4b9] = 蹚下水\n# en: {She teaches you to feel the lake breathing. You cannot feel your feet. Both are, she says, normal.|You ask the water to rise and it rises, a little, around your knees. Marisol whoops like a child.|By the time you climb out you've stopped shivering, which she says is a good sign and Amaranth will say is a bad one.}\nopt.left.say.1 [5536c9] = {她教你感受湖的呼吸。你感受不到自己的脚。她说两者都很正常。|你请水升起来，它真的在你膝边升起了一点。玛丽索尔像个孩子一样欢呼。|等你爬上岸，你已经不发抖了。她说这是好兆头，阿玛兰斯会说这是坏兆头。}\n# en: Wave back from the jetty\nopt.right.label [c55e26] = 在码头上朝她挥手\n# en: {She waves again, unoffended, and goes under.|\"Coward,\" she calls cheerfully. You are fine with that.|\"Next time, then,\" she calls, and goes back to her book, held perfectly dry above the water.}\nopt.right.say.1 [154360] = {她毫不介意地又挥了挥手，沉了下去。|“胆小鬼，”她快活地喊。你觉得这样挺好。|“那就下次，”她喊道，又看起了书，书在水面上举得干干的。}\n# en: Raise a wave to say hello\nopt.up.label [4bb1cd] = 掀个浪打声招呼\n# en: {A small, neat wave rolls across the lake and taps her on the shoulder. She laughs so hard she sinks.|Your wave arrives slightly late and very large. Marisol rides it in to shore, delighted.|Your wave rolls in neatly and stops just short of her book. Marisol applauds with her free hand.}\nopt.up.say.1 [9c2c82] = {一道小小的、整齐的浪越过湖面，拍了拍她的肩。她笑得沉了下去。|你的浪来得有点晚，也有点大。玛丽索尔乘着它冲上了岸，高兴极了。|你的浪整整齐齐地涌过去，正好停在她的书前面。玛丽索尔用空着的那只手鼓掌。}\n\n@event marisol_storm\n# en: Weather\ntitle [eab633] = 天气\n# en: There's a knock at your window during the worst storm of the year. Professor Marisol is outside, hair everywhere, grinning. \"Nobody learns tides on a calm day,\" she shouts over the wind. \"Coming?\"\ntext [837719] = 一年里最猛的暴风雨中，有人敲你的窗。玛丽索尔教授站在外面，头发乱飞，满脸笑容。“风平浪静的日子里谁也学不会潮汐，”她在风里大喊，“来不来？”\n# en: A wild storm over a dark lake, a professor and a student standing on a rocky point with their arms raised, lightning splitting the sky behind them.\nscene [69a9a9] = 漆黑湖面上的狂暴风雨，一位教授和一名学生站在岩石岬角上高举双臂，身后闪电劈开天空。\n# en: Go out into it\nopt.left.label [d9d690] = 冲进风雨里\n# en: On the point, in the roar, you hold back one wave for three whole seconds. Marisol screams with joy. You go home soaked to the bone and lit up like a lamp.\nopt.left.say.1 [b3da39] = 在岬角上、在轰鸣声中，你把一道浪整整挡住了三秒。玛丽索尔开心得尖叫。你浑身湿透地回家，却像一盏点亮的灯。\n# en: The lake throws you on your back. Marisol hauls you out by the collar, laughing and apologising in equal parts, and walks you all the way to the infirmary.\nopt.left.say.2 [b1eb0b] = 湖水把你掀翻在地。玛丽索尔揪着你的领子把你拖出来，一半在笑一半在道歉，一路把你送进了医务室。\n# en: Close the window\nopt.right.label [486e14] = 关上窗\n# en: She shrugs, cheerful, and runs off into the rain. You lie awake listening to the lake and wondering what you missed.\nopt.right.say.1 [457fde] = 她耸耸肩，毫不在意地跑进雨里。你躺着听了一夜湖水，想知道自己错过了什么。\n\n@event marisol_trust\n# en: The Last Line\ntitle [66ff3e] = 最后一行\n# en: Marisol presses her old book of water poems into your hands. \"I never remember the last lines,\" she says. \"You will. The tidecalling's in the margins; I wrote it there when I was your age. Read it on the shore. It works better on the shore.\"\ntext [dc1bc8] = 玛丽索尔把她那本旧的咏水诗集塞到你手里。“我总记不住最后一行，”她说，“你会记住的。唤潮术写在页边，是我在你这个年纪时写的。去湖边读。在湖边读效果更好。”\n# en: A quiet lakeside at sunset, a professor handing a student a worn book of poems with a ribbon in it, the water perfectly still before them.\nscene [41e145] = 日落时宁静的湖边，一位教授把一本夹着丝带的旧诗集递给学生，眼前的湖水平静如镜。\n# en: Read it on the shore\nopt.left.label [0950d9] = 去湖边读\n# en: You read until dark, ankle-deep. On the last page the lake answers you, softly, all the way out to the far bank. You remember the last line. You write it in, for her.\nopt.left.say.1 [2dd6f7] = 你站在没过脚踝的水里一直读到天黑。翻到最后一页时，湖水轻轻地回应了你，一直传到对岸。你记住了最后一行。你替她把它写了上去。\n# en: Give it back; it's hers\nopt.right.label [404c00] = 还给她，这是她的\n# en: She looks at the book and then at you. \"Nobody's ever given it back,\" she says, and reads you the poem herself. She forgets the last line. You tell her.\nopt.right.say.1 [fcc45a] = 她看看书，又看看你。“从来没人还回来过，”她说，然后亲自给你读了那首诗。她忘了最后一行。你告诉了她。\n\n@event mabel_intro\n# en: The Way You Open a Door\ntitle [3e82a5] = 你推门的样子\n# en: The bell over the door rings. The woman behind the counter looks up, considers how you came in, and reaches for a tin before you've said a word. \"Homesick,\" says Mabel, \"with a side of exam. Sit by the window, petal.\"\ntext [0c0a87] = 门上的铃铛响了。柜台后的女人抬起头，打量了一下你进门的样子，你还没开口，她已经伸手去拿一罐茶了。“想家，”梅布尔说，“外加一份考试。坐窗边吧，小宝贝。”\n# en: A cosy village tea shop with a bell over the door, a woman behind the counter already reaching for a tin of tea as a student steps in from the cold.\nscene [436b7b] = 温馨的村庄茶馆，门上挂着铃铛，一名学生从寒风中走进来，柜台后的女人已经伸手去拿一罐茶。\n# en: Sit by the window\nopt.left.label [8f510d] = 坐到窗边\n# en: The tea is exactly right and you can't say why. She brings a slice of cake you didn't order and doesn't charge for it. \"First visit,\" she says. \"After that I charge double.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [e7981f] = 茶恰到好处，你说不出为什么。她端来一块你没点的蛋糕，没收钱。“第一次来，”她说，“以后收双倍。”\n# en: Say you'll come back another time\nopt.right.label [64684d] = 说你改天再来\n# en: She looks at you over the counter. \"Come back when you've got time to sit,\" she says. \"You'll know when.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [088ded] = 她隔着柜台看着你。“等你有空坐下了再来，”她说，“到时候你自己会知道。”\n\n@event mabel_parcel\n# en: A Tin From Mabel\ntitle [89fe3c] = 梅布尔寄来的茶罐\n# en: {A small tin arrives on the barge with your name on it, in Mabel's round hand. The label says \"FOR THE COUGH I HEARD THROUGH THE DOOR.\"|Another tin from Mabel. The label says \"FOR WHATEVER THIS WEEK IS.\"|Mabel's tins have a shelf of their own now. Today's label says only \"YOU KNOW.\"} {There's a pressed violet under the string.|It smells of honey and woodsmoke.|There's a biscuit taped to the lid.}\ntext [6c5e1e] = {驳船送来一个小罐子，上面是梅布尔圆圆的字迹写着你的名字。标签上写着“治那声隔着门我都听见了的咳嗽”。|梅布尔又寄来一罐。标签上写着“治这一周不管是什么的那些事”。|梅布尔的茶罐已经占了你一整层架子。今天的标签只写着“你懂的”。} {绳子下面压着一朵干紫罗兰。|闻起来有蜂蜜和柴烟的味道。|盖子上用胶带粘着一块饼干。}\n# en: A small tin of tea wrapped in brown paper and string on a dormitory pillow, a handwritten label on top and a pressed flower tucked under the string.\nscene [7e98e5] = 宿舍枕头上放着一小罐用牛皮纸和细绳包好的茶，上面贴着手写标签，绳子下压着一朵干花。\n# en: Bring her something back\nopt.left.label [1f40bf] = 给她捎点东西回去\n# en: {You bring her a jar of honey from the market. She calls it bribery and puts it on the shelf behind the counter.|You bring her flowers from the village stall. \"Don't,\" she says. \"Drink it.\" You do. It works.|You bring her a tin of your own, labelled \"YOU KNOW\". She laughs until she has to sit down, and keeps it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [5f75b3] = {你从集市给她带了一罐蜂蜜。她说这是贿赂，把它摆上了柜台后面的架子。|你从镇上的花摊给她带了束花。“别谢，”她说，“喝。”你喝了。管用。|你给她带去一罐自己的，标签上写着“你知道”。她笑得不得不坐下来，然后收下了。}\n# en: Send it back with a note\nopt.right.label [7dbd32] = 附张字条寄回去\n# en: {She sends it back again with a longer note. You give up and drink it.|The tin comes back on the next barge. The label now says \"STUBBORN.\"|You send it back with a note that says \"I KNOW\". It comes back with two biscuits taped to the lid.}\nopt.right.say.1 [9369ce] = {她又寄了回来，附上一张更长的字条。你放弃抵抗，喝了。|茶罐搭下一班驳船回来了。标签现在写着“犟”。|你寄了回去，附一张字条写着“我知道”。它又回来了，盖子上粘了两块饼干。}\n\n@event mabel_cup\n# en: Your Cup on the Hook\ntitle [8777bb] = 挂钩上你的杯子\n# en: Behind Mabel's counter is a row of hooks with cups on them, each painted with a letter. You've never asked whose they are. Today, while you watch, Mabel hangs a new one with your initial on it. \"Regulars,\" she says, as if that settled it.\ntext [2202c5] = 梅布尔的柜台后面有一排挂钩，挂着杯子，每只都画着一个字母。你从没问过它们是谁的。今天，你看着梅布尔挂上一只新杯子，上面画着你名字的首字母。“常客，”她说，好像这就说明了一切。\n# en: A row of mismatched cups hanging on hooks behind a tea-shop counter, a woman hanging a new one with a student's initial painted on it.\nscene [ea24f5] = 茶馆柜台后挂钩上一排不成套的杯子，一个女人正挂上一只新杯子，上面画着一名学生名字的首字母。\n# en: Ask whose the others are\nopt.left.label [858fea] = 问问其他杯子是谁的\n# en: She tells you, cup by cup: a fisherman, a dead headmaster, a girl who became a duchess and still writes. \"And you,\" she says. Something in your chest unknots.\nopt.left.say.1 [8a1266] = 她一只一只告诉你：一个渔夫，一位已故的校长，一个后来成了公爵夫人、至今仍写信来的姑娘。“还有你，”她说。你胸口有什么东西松开了。\n# en: Say thank you and sit down\nopt.right.label [d9af96] = 道声谢，坐下\n# en: You sit. She brings your tea in your cup. You don't need to say anything else, and she doesn't either.\nopt.right.say.1 [77da72] = 你坐下。她用你的杯子给你端来茶。你不需要再说什么，她也一样。\n\n@event grimsby_intro\n# en: Not That One\ntitle [40767f] = 不是那本\n# en: You ask for a textbook. An elderly man on a ladder says \"Ah!\" with enormous enthusiasm and hands you a history of lighthouses. \"Not what you asked for,\" says Mr Grimsby. \"What you'll need. You'll see.\"\ntext [cb47fb] = 你要一本教科书。梯子上的老人热情洋溢地“啊！”了一声，递给你一本灯塔史。“不是你要的，”格林斯比先生说，“是你需要的。你会明白的。”\n# en: A cramped bookshop with leaning shelves, an elderly bookseller on a ladder holding out a book to a student below, the student holding a list.\nscene [ee1fec] = 拥挤的书店里书架东倒西歪，一位年迈的书商站在梯子上把一本书递给下面的学生，学生手里捏着一张书单。\n# en: Buy the lighthouse book\nopt.left.label [aed25d] = 买下那本灯塔书\n# en: You read it on the barge home. Chapter four has the exact proof your essay was missing. When you go back to say so, he only nods. \"They always come back,\" he says. \"Lighthouses.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [ae1dbc] = 你在回程的驳船上读了它。第四章恰好有你论文缺的那个证明。你回去告诉他，他只是点点头。“它们总会回来的，”他说，“灯塔。”\n# en: Say you'll think about it\nopt.right.label [b4769e] = 说你考虑一下\n# en: He puts the lighthouses back on the shelf, sadly, like a doctor whose prescription has been refused. \"It'll keep,\" he says. \"So will you.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [9586aa] = 他把那本讲灯塔的书放回书架，满脸遗憾，就像一个医生的处方被拒绝了。“它跑不了，”他说，“你也跑不了。”\n\n@event grimsby_parcel\n# en: The Wrong Book, Posted\ntitle [da6a85] = 寄来的错书\n# en: {A parcel arrives from Grimsby's. Inside is a book you didn't order and a note that says \"Thought of you. Pay when convenient. A. G.\"|Another parcel from Mr Grimsby. This one is about eels.|Grimsby's parcels come monthly now, like weather.} {It smells of rain and pipe smoke.|There's a bus ticket from another century inside the cover.|The note is written on the back of a recipe.}\ntext [79ef26] = {格林斯比书店寄来一个包裹。里面是一本你没订的书，附着一张字条，写着“想到了你。方便时再付。A.G.”|格林斯比先生又寄来一个包裹。这本讲的是鳗鱼。|格林斯比的包裹现在每月都来，像天气一样。} {闻起来有雨水和烟斗的味道。|封面里夹着一张上个世纪的车票。|字条写在一张菜谱的背面。}\n# en: A brown-paper parcel tied with string on a library desk, a student unwrapping an odd old book while a cat watches from the next chair.\nscene [443ef4] = 图书馆书桌上放着一个用牛皮纸和细绳捆着的包裹，一名学生正拆开一本古怪的旧书，邻座的猫在一旁看着。\n# en: Keep it and pay him\nopt.left.label [37a868] = 留下，付钱给他\n# en: {It's the wrong book, exactly as promised, and somewhere in it is the right page. It always is.|You read it out of politeness and finish it out of something else.|You pay him, and try the recipe on the back of the note. The book and the cake are both strange, and both good.}\nopt.left.say.1 [b466d3] = {果然是本错书，正如承诺；而书里某处，正是你要的那一页。向来如此。|你出于礼貌开始读，读完却是出于别的原因。|你付了钱，还试了字条背面的菜谱。书和蛋糕都很怪，也都很好。}\n# en: Send it back with the carrier\nopt.right.label [46b3c6] = 交给邮差退回去\n# en: {The carrier takes it back for nothing, grumbling. Grimsby sends a cheerful note that says \"Next time, then.\"|He sends it straight back with a second, wronger book. You keep both.|You send it back. Next month's parcel arrives anyway, as if nothing had happened, with a new recipe on the back of the note.}\nopt.right.say.1 [4792f3] = {邮差骂骂咧咧地把它免费带了回去。格林斯比寄来一张欢快的字条，写着“那就下次吧”。|他立刻把它寄了回来，外加一本更错的书。两本你都留下了。|你寄了回去。下个月的包裹照样寄到，好像什么都没发生过，字条背面换了一份新菜谱。}\n\n@event grimsby_mind_the_shop\n# en: Mind the Shop\ntitle [2eaf46] = 照看书店\n# en: Mr Grimsby has a ladder accident (a mild one; he lands on the poetry) and needs someone to mind the shop for a week. \"You know where everything is,\" he says, which is untrue. \"Better than me,\" he adds, which is.\ntext [355c82] = 格林斯比先生从梯子上摔了下来（不严重，落在了诗歌区），需要有人替他照看书店一周。“你知道东西都在哪儿，”他说，这不是真的。“比我清楚，”他补了一句，这倒是真的。\n# en: A student alone behind the counter of a crowded old bookshop at dusk, a ring of keys on the counter and a customer peering in through the dusty window.\nscene [5a432f] = 黄昏时一名学生独自站在拥挤的旧书店柜台后，柜台上放着一串钥匙，一位顾客透过满是灰尘的橱窗往里张望。\n# en: Mind the shop\nopt.left.label [d31c26] = 照看书店\n# en: You sell eleven books, all of them the wrong ones, and every customer comes back to thank you. Grimsby, on crutches, says you have the gift. He means it as a warning.\nopt.left.say.1 [60e74c] = 你卖出了十一本书，全是错的，而每位顾客都回来道谢。拄着拐杖的格林斯比说你有天分。他是当作警告说的。\n# en: \"I can't, I'm sorry.\"\nopt.right.label [c71ad9] = “我做不到，抱歉。”\n# en: He closes for the week instead, with a sign that says GONE TO LIGHTHOUSE. Nobody knows which one.\nopt.right.say.1 [c09b7c] = 他干脆歇业一周，门上挂了块牌子：“看灯塔去了”。没人知道是哪一座。\n\n@event pell_intro\n# en: A Thousand Drawers\ntitle [e5d6dc] = 一千个抽屉\n# en: You ask for something for a headache. An old man on a ladder stops, counts the drawers under his breath and comes down with exactly one twist of paper. \"Willow and feverfew,\" says Old Pell. \"Three pinches. Not four. Four is for people who don't listen.\"\ntext [b95382] = 你想买点治头痛的药。梯子上的老人停下来，低声数了数抽屉，下来时手里恰好拿着一小包纸。“柳树皮加小白菊，”老佩尔说，“三撮。不是四撮。四撮是给不听话的人的。”\n# en: An old apothecary on a library ladder in front of a wall of tiny labelled drawers, peering down over his spectacles at a student at the counter.\nscene [7dc69c] = 一位老药剂师站在一面贴满标签的小抽屉墙前的梯子上，从眼镜上方俯视着柜台前的学生。\n# en: Ask why not four\nopt.left.label [3eee3f] = 问他为什么不是四撮\n# en: He tells you, at length, with diagrams drawn in the counter dust. He's so pleased someone asked that he forgets to charge you. You leave with the cure, a headache from the lecture and the dosage for nine other things.\nopt.left.say.1 [38fb49] = 他讲了很久，还在柜台的灰上画了图。有人肯问，他高兴得忘了收钱。你带着药、一场讲座带来的新头痛，以及另外九种东西的剂量离开了。\n# en: Pay and go\nopt.right.label [9ac792] = 付钱走人\n# en: He nods at the door as you leave, as if adding you to a ledger of people who will be back. The headache is gone before the quay, and you are back within a month.\nopt.right.say.1 [b2eaf4] = 你走时他朝门口点点头，仿佛把你记进了一本“会回来的人”的账。还没到码头，头就不疼了；不到一个月，你果然回来了。\n\n@event pell_count_drops\n# en: Count Them Aloud\ntitle [6a0261] = 大声数出来\n# en: {Old Pell sets a dropper in your hand. \"Forty. Aloud.\" He watches your lips, not the vial.|\"Forty,\" says Pell, and stands behind you. You can hear him counting along under his breath.|Pell has started leaving you the hard tinctures. That is how he says he trusts you.} {A daughter rolls her eyes at the counter.|The drawers rattle when a cart goes by.|Something in drawer three hundred is humming.}\ntext [2f8c1a] = {老佩尔把一支滴管放进你手里。“四十滴。数出声。”他盯着的是你的嘴唇，不是药瓶。|“四十，”佩尔说，然后站到你身后。你能听见他在低声跟着数。|佩尔开始把难配的酊剂留给你了。这就是他表达信任的方式。} {一个女儿在柜台那边翻了个白眼。|有马车经过时，抽屉们咔嗒作响。|三百号抽屉里有什么东西在嗡嗡响。}\n# en: An old apothecary at a wooden counter holding a glass dropper over a vial, a student beside him counting on their fingers, a wall of tiny labelled drawers behind them.\nscene [6dff31] = 一位老药剂师站在木柜台前，把一支玻璃滴管悬在药瓶上方，身旁的学生掰着手指数数，身后是一整面贴着标签的小抽屉。\n# en: Count, aloud, every drop\nopt.left.label [f6db0f] = 一滴一滴大声数\n# en: {Forty, exactly. Pell says nothing for a full minute, then \"Again.\" You are fairly sure that was praise.|You lose count at thirty-one and start over without being told. Pell grunts approvingly.|Forty, of a tincture that fights back. Pell holds the vial to the light and puts it in drawer three hundred. The humming stops.}\nopt.left.say.1 [426397] = {正好四十滴。佩尔整整一分钟没说话，然后说“再来”。你相当确定那是在夸你。|数到三十一你乱了，没等人说就从头再来。佩尔赞许地哼了一声。|四十滴，还是一种会反抗的酊剂。佩尔把药瓶举到光下看了看，放进了第三百号抽屉。嗡嗡声停了。}\n# en: Eyeball it; it's close enough\nopt.right.label [e3a574] = 目测一下，差不多就行\n# en: {It's right. Pell checks it three times and is visibly annoyed that it's right.|Close enough is, today, exactly right. \"Luck,\" says Pell. \"Don't.\"|The hard tincture forgives you, today. Pell sniffs it and puts it away without comment, which is worse than comment.}\nopt.right.say.1 [5f639d] = {对了。佩尔查了三遍，对它居然是对的这件事明显很恼火。|差不多，今天恰好就是刚好。“运气，”佩尔说，“别指望。”|今天，这难缠的酊剂放了你一马。佩尔闻了闻，一句评价也没有就收了起来，这比评价更糟。}\n# en: {Forty-three. Pell pours it out without a word and hands you the dropper again. The silence lasts all shift.|Too many. A daughter fixes it before he sees. He sees.|Too many, of the hard one. Drawer three hundred hums louder, and Pell keeps the dropper for the rest of the week.}\nopt.right.say.2 [9adae6] = {四十三滴。佩尔一言不发地倒掉，把滴管又递给你。这份沉默持续了整个班。|多了。一个女儿赶在他看见前改好了。他还是看见了。|多了，偏偏是难缠的那种。第三百号抽屉嗡得更响了，佩尔这一星期剩下的日子都没再把滴管给你。}\n\n@event pell_daughters\n# en: Pell & Daughters\ntitle [e047f1] = 佩尔父女\n# en: Pell's daughters want a new sign, brighter labels and a counter that faces the door. Pell wants it to be fifty years ago. All three of them turn to you, the only person in the shop who isn't family. \"Well?\" says Pell.\ntext [5b6af3] = 佩尔的女儿们想要一块新招牌、更醒目的标签，还想把柜台转过来朝着门。佩尔想要五十年前的样子。三个人一齐转向你——铺子里唯一不是家里人的那个。“怎么说？”佩尔问。\n# en: An old apothecary and his two grown daughters arguing across a counter over a new painted shop sign, a student standing awkwardly between them.\nscene [d34c2c] = 一位老药剂师和两个成年的女儿隔着柜台为一块新漆的招牌争吵，一名学生尴尬地站在中间。\n# en: Side with the daughters\nopt.left.label [3b974c] = 站在女儿们一边\n# en: Pell stamps into the back room. A week later the new sign goes up. Underneath it, in small old letters, it still says PELL. He shows you, gruffly, as though you'd asked.\nopt.left.say.1 [fb675a] = 佩尔跺着脚进了里屋。一周后，新招牌挂了上去。招牌下面，用小小的旧字，依然写着“佩尔”。他板着脸指给你看，好像是你问起似的。\n# en: \"The drawers have never been wrong.\"\nopt.right.label [d09cc9] = “抽屉从来没错过。”\n# en: Pell glows. The daughters glare. Pell gives you a bonus and the daughters give you all the worst stocktakes. Everyone feels this is fair.\nopt.right.say.1 [dc138f] = 佩尔容光焕发。女儿们怒目而视。佩尔给了你一笔奖金，女儿们把最糟的盘点全派给了你。大家都觉得这很公平。\n# en: Pell glows. The daughters glare. Pell presses a bottle of his best tonic on you, and the daughters give you a look that lasts a month.\nopt.right.say.2 [3fae36] = 佩尔容光焕发。女儿们怒目而视。佩尔硬塞给你一瓶他最好的补剂，女儿们给了你一个持续一个月的眼神。\n\n@event grisk_pleasantries\n# en: Pleasantries\ntitle [3f3d10] = 寒暄\n# en: {Grisk raises a small glass from the corner table of the Gilded Newt. \"My friend,\" he says. \"Do sit. I'm not working.\"|Grisk, at the corner table again. \"How is the purse?\" he asks, with what sounds like real concern.|{has(in_debt) or flag(grisk_done): \"Ah, my favourite client,\" purrs Grisk.|\"Ah, my favourite non-client,\" purrs Grisk.}} {His ledger stays closed, which feels deliberate.|He is eating a single olive very slowly.|The landlady gives you a warning look.}\ntext [30a799] = {格里斯克在镀金蝾螈酒馆的角落桌边举起一只小酒杯。“我的朋友，”他说，“请坐。我今天不营业。”|格里斯克又坐在角落那张桌子。“钱袋还好吗？”他问，听起来像是真心关切。|{has(in_debt) or flag(grisk_done): “啊，我最喜欢的客户，”格里斯克轻声说。|“啊，我最喜欢的非客户，”格里斯克轻声说。}} {他的账簿一直合着，显然是故意的。|他正非常缓慢地吃着一颗橄榄。|老板娘朝你投来警告的一瞥。}\n# en: A goblin in a velvet waistcoat sitting at a corner table in a low-beamed tavern, raising a small glass to a student, a fat ledger at his elbow.\nscene [83bdbd] = 低矮横梁的酒馆角落，一个穿天鹅绒马甲的地精向一名学生举起一只小酒杯，手肘边放着一本厚厚的账簿。\n# en: Sit for one drink\nopt.left.label [b539a6] = 坐下喝一杯\n# en: {He talks about money the way other people talk about weather, and you learn more in an hour than in a term of Umbral Theory.|He tells you which of the village shops are in trouble and why. It feels like gossip. It is a lesson.|He pays for the drinks and writes nothing down, which is the most unsettling thing he has ever done.}\nopt.left.say.1 [aecf5e] = {他谈起钱来就像别人谈天气，你在一小时里学到的比一学期暗影理论还多。|他告诉你村里哪些铺子遇到了麻烦，以及原因。听着像闲话，其实是一堂课。|他付了酒钱，什么也没记——这是他做过最让人不安的事。}\n# en: Keep walking\nopt.right.label [6ca967] = 继续走\n# en: {\"Another time,\" he calls after you. \"I'm very patient.\"|He raises the glass anyway. You feel it between your shoulder blades all the way out.|You keep walking. The landlady nods to you on your way out, very slightly.}\nopt.right.say.1 [8ecc7b] = {“改天，”他在你身后喊，“我很有耐心。”|他照样举了杯。直到走出门，你都觉得那目光落在你两肩之间。|你继续往外走。经过时，老板娘冲你微微点了下头。}\n\n@event grisk_tip\n# en: A Tip, Between Friends\ntitle [576ce2] = 朋友之间的消息\n# en: Grisk slides a coin across the table. \"Between friends,\" he murmurs. \"The barge company is about to buy a second barge. The ferryman's cousin will sell his share this week, cheap, because he doesn't know. You could know.\"\ntext [127226] = 格里斯克把一枚硬币推过桌面。“朋友之间，”他低声说，“驳船公司要买第二条驳船了。船夫的表亲这周会贱卖他的股份，因为他不知道。你可以知道。”\n# en: A goblin leaning across a tavern table to murmur into a student's ear, sliding a single gold coin across the wood with one finger.\nscene [95b950] = 一个地精隔着酒馆桌子凑到学生耳边低语，用一根手指把一枚金币推过木桌。\n# en: Buy the share\nopt.left.label [cddc5b] = 买下那份股份\n# en: The share costs a fair few crowns.\nopt.left.hint [af6e64] = 这份股份要花上不少克朗。\n# en: The second barge arrives. The share triples. Grisk lifts his glass across the Newt. You feel rich and slightly unwell.\nopt.left.say.1 [d08cfb] = 第二条驳船到了。股份翻了三倍。格里斯克隔着蝾螈酒馆朝你举杯。你觉得自己很有钱，又有点不舒服。\n# en: The second barge sinks on its first crossing. Grisk is sympathetic. \"Risk,\" he says. \"Delicious, and sometimes poison.\"\nopt.left.say.2 [086af1] = 第二条驳船第一次渡湖就沉了。格里斯克很同情。“风险，”他说，“美味，有时也是毒药。”\n# en: Leave the cousin his share\nopt.right.label [f20d63] = 把股份留给那位表亲\n# en: Grisk pockets the coin. \"Honest,\" he says, as if diagnosing something. \"It'll pass. Or it won't, and you'll be poor and admired.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [89bb79] = 格里斯克收起硬币。“老实，”他说，像在诊断什么病，“会好的。要么好不了，那你就会又穷又受人敬重。”\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/incidents.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/incidents.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event inc_fever_bill\n# en: A Fever and a Bill\ntitle [a86a8d] = 一场高烧，一张账单\n# en: You wake in the infirmary with a fever that makes the ceiling breathe. Sister Amaranth holds up a small blue bottle. \"This clears it by morning. It is also not free,\" she says. \"Or you can sweat it out, like your ancestors.\"\ntext [9f097a] = 你在医务室醒来，烧得连天花板都像在呼吸。阿玛兰斯修女举起一只蓝色小瓶。“这个能让你明早退烧。它也不是免费的，”她说，“要不然你就像你的祖先那样，硬扛过去。”\n# en: A pale student in an infirmary bed, a stern nurse holding out a small blue bottle and a folded bill.\nscene [b357c5] = 医务室病床上一个脸色苍白的学生，一位严厉的修女递来一只蓝色小瓶和一张折好的账单。\n# en: Buy the tonic\nopt.left.label [424c55] = 买下药剂\n# en: {~It tastes of tin and regret.|It tastes like a pond that has given up.} By morning the ceiling is a ceiling again, and your purse is noticeably lighter.\nopt.left.say.1 [b9ca12] = {~它尝起来像锡和悔恨。|它尝起来像一潭放弃了的池水。}到了早上，天花板又只是天花板了，你的钱袋却明显轻了一截。\n# en: Sweat it out\nopt.right.label [cf0e70] = 硬扛过去\n# en: Three days of damp sheets and strange dreams about stairs. You miss a week of notes, and come back feeling like a wrung-out dishcloth.\nopt.right.say.1 [fe076d] = 三天的湿床单，还有一堆关于楼梯的怪梦。你漏了一整周的笔记，回来时整个人像一块拧干的抹布。\n\n@event inc_broken_wand\n# en: Snapped on the Stairs\ntitle [fbb3ae] = 断在楼梯上\n# en: The moving stairs shift without warning. You catch yourself; your wand doesn't. It lies in two pieces on the landing, still fizzing at the break like a sulking firework.\ntext [e32b26] = 移动楼梯毫无预兆地挪了位置。你稳住了自己，你的魔杖却没有。它断成两截躺在平台上，断口还在嘶嘶冒火花，像一支赌气的烟花。\n# en: A student kneeling on a stone staircase, holding the two halves of a wand that still fizzes faintly at the break.\nscene [8c70b7] = 一个学生跪在石阶上，手里捧着断成两截的魔杖，断口仍微微冒着火花。\n# en: Have it properly rebound\nopt.left.label [97789e] = 找人好好修\n# en: The repairer in the village tuts, splints it with silver wire and charges you for every tut. It works better than before, which is somehow worse.\nopt.left.say.1 [d64a94] = 山下小镇的修理匠啧啧连声，用银丝把它绑好，每啧一声都算了钱。它比以前更好用了，这反倒更让人憋屈。\n# en: Bind it with string and hope\nopt.right.label [1dbf63] = 拿绳子绑上，听天由命\n# en: It holds. It spits a little when you cast, like a cat that forgives you but wants it noted.\nopt.right.say.1 [75ab3f] = 它撑住了。施法时会稍微啐一下，像一只原谅了你、但要你记住这笔账的猫。\n# en: It holds for three days, then sneezes a spell backwards. Your eyebrows grow back within the week; your marks take longer.\nopt.right.say.2 [76926c] = 它撑了三天，然后打了个喷嚏，把一道咒语倒着放了出去。你的眉毛一周内就长回来了，你的成绩可没那么快。\n\n@event inc_library_fine\n# en: The Overdue Notice\ntitle [716b08] = 逾期通知\n# en: A prefect in a red sash slides a notice across your desk. The library book you borrowed has come back late, damp and, somehow, with teeth marks. \"Damages,\" she says. \"Or you can argue. People do. It never goes well.\"\ntext [14a576] = 一位系着红绶带的级长把一张通知推到你桌上。你借的那本图书馆的书逾期归还，湿漉漉的，还不知怎么多了一圈牙印。“赔偿，”她说，“或者你也可以争辩。有人这么干过。从来没好结果。”\n# en: A prefect in a red sash holding out a long paper notice, a book with teeth marks on the desk between them.\nscene [618e44] = 一位系红绶带的级长举着一张长长的纸质通知，两人之间的桌上放着一本带牙印的书。\n# en: Pay the fine\nopt.left.label [4e47fa] = 交罚款\n# en: She stamps your receipt with great satisfaction. The book is reshelved in a cage, which feels like the right outcome for everyone.\nopt.left.say.1 [197c1b] = 她心满意足地在你的收据上盖了章。那本书被重新上架——关进了笼子里，这对大家来说似乎都是最好的结局。\n# en: Argue that the book bit first\nopt.right.label [ab548f] = 坚称是书先咬的\n# en: You produce the bite on your thumb as evidence. The prefect inspects it, then the book, and waives the fine. \"Fair,\" she says. \"It has a history.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [efd7a4] = 你亮出拇指上的咬痕作为证据。级长检查了它，又检查了那本书，免了罚款。“说得通，”她说，“它有前科。”\n# en: The prefect writes down every word you say, then doubles the fine for \"contempt of librarian\". You pay it, and lose an evening's revision to the demerit hearing besides.\nopt.right.say.2 [3fbf09] = 级长把你说的每个字都记了下来，然后以“藐视图书管理员”为由把罚款翻倍。你交了钱，还为记过听证搭进去一晚上的复习时间。\n\n@event inc_letter_home\n# en: Bad Weather at Home\ntitle [ae05d8] = 家里遭了风雨\n# en: A letter from home, written in a hurry. A storm took half the roof, and the repairs will cost more than anyone has. Nobody asks you for anything. That's how you know it's bad.\ntext [0fccb4] = 一封家信，字迹潦草。一场暴风雨掀掉了半边屋顶，修缮的花费比谁手头的钱都多。没有人向你开口要任何东西。正因如此，你知道情况很糟。\n# en: A student at a window reading a rain-spotted letter, a small pile of coins on the sill beside them.\nscene [e9924d] = 一个学生站在窗边读一封落了雨点的信，窗台上放着一小堆硬币。\n# en: Send crowns home\nopt.left.label [ca82d0] = 往家里寄克朗\n# en: You send everything you can spare and a little you can't. The reply comes a month later: a drawing of a roof, finished, and a pressed flower.\nopt.left.say.1 [bf78ca] = 你寄出了所有能省下的钱，还多寄了一点省不下的。一个月后回信来了：一张画着修好的屋顶的画，还有一朵压干的花。\n# en: Write back with love, not money\nopt.right.label [f26600] = 回信寄去爱，不寄钱\n# en: You write four pages and keep your purse. The reply is kind. It doesn't mention the roof. That night you lie awake and listen to the rain.\nopt.right.say.1 [75b549] = 你写了四页信，钱袋没动。回信很温柔，只字未提屋顶。那晚你躺着睡不着，听了一夜的雨。\n\n@event inc_stolen_purse\n# en: Market Day Fingers\ntitle [597745] = 集市日的快手\n# en: Somewhere between the cheese stall and the man selling enchanted socks, your purse stops being yours. You catch a grey hood slipping between the stalls, already three stalls ahead.\ntext [2d2565] = 在奶酪摊和那个卖魔法袜子的男人之间的某处，你的钱袋不再属于你了。你瞥见一顶灰色兜帽在摊位间溜走，已经领先你三个摊位。\n# en: A crowded village square on market day, a figure in a grey hood slipping away between the stalls with a small purse.\nscene [50e0d7] = 集市日拥挤的小镇广场上，一个戴灰兜帽的身影拿着一只小钱袋从摊位间溜走。\n# en: Run after the hood\nopt.left.label [3e6b9b] = 追那顶兜帽\n# en: You corner the thief behind the bakery. The purse comes back a little lighter; they'd already spent some on a bun. You let them keep the bun.\nopt.left.say.1 [8a1779] = 你在面包房后面堵住了小偷。钱袋还回来时轻了一点——他们已经拿去买了个面包。你让他们留着那个面包。\n# en: You trip over a goose, then a second goose. When you get up, the hood is gone, and so are your crowns.\nopt.left.say.2 [52dac0] = 你被一只鹅绊倒，接着又是第二只鹅。等你爬起来，兜帽不见了，你的克朗也不见了。\n# en: Let it go\nopt.right.label [7a5d2f] = 算了吧\n# en: You tell yourself it's only money. You tell yourself that all the way back up the hill, and it never quite takes.\nopt.right.say.1 [78bd08] = 你告诉自己，不过是钱而已。你一路走回山上都这么告诉自己，可始终没能真的说服自己。\n\n@event inc_hiccuping_robe\n# en: The Robe That Hiccups\ntitle [2955d6] = 会打嗝的长袍\n# en: A cauldron two benches down boils over, and a whole batch of Draught of Unending Hiccups soaks your best robe. The stain fades by supper. The hiccups don't. Every few minutes, the robe jumps.\ntext [291843] = 隔着两张实验台的一口坩埚沸溢了，一整锅“无尽打嗝药剂”泼透了你最好的长袍。污渍到晚饭时就褪了，打嗝却没停。每隔几分钟，长袍就要跳一下。\n# en: A student in potion-stained robes jolting mid-step as their robe gives a visible hiccup, classmates hiding smiles.\nscene [26b228] = 一个穿着沾满药渍长袍的学生走到一半猛地一颤，长袍明显打了个嗝，同学们憋着笑。\n# en: Buy a new robe\nopt.left.label [ce85a0] = 买件新长袍\n# en: The tailor in the village fits you in an afternoon and charges you for a week. The old robe now hiccups quietly in the bottom of your trunk.\nopt.left.say.1 [0564c8] = 山下小镇的裁缝一个下午就给你量好做好，却收了一周的工钱。旧长袍如今在你箱底悄悄地打着嗝。\n# en: Wear it with dignity\nopt.right.label [1a8893] = 体面地穿着它\n# en: {~It hiccups through a lecture on silence.|It hiccups through the headmistress's announcements.} Someone starts a tally on the common-room wall. You are briefly famous for the wrong reasons.\nopt.right.say.1 [20fdad] = {~它在一堂讲沉默的课上一路打嗝。|它在院长讲话时一路打嗝。}有人在公共休息室的墙上开始记次数。你短暂地出了名，原因却不太对。\n\n@event patron_tournament_purse\n# en: A Patron's Purse\ntitle [21cf49] = 赞助人的奖金袋\n# en: The Spring Tournament committee has a velvet cushion, a very thin prize purse and a list of patrons with a lot of room left on it. Patrons are read out before the first bout, in a loud voice, to the whole academy.\ntext [eebe25] = 春季锦标赛筹委会有一块天鹅绒垫子、一只瘪瘪的奖金袋，还有一份空着大片位置的赞助人名单。第一场比赛前，赞助人的名字会被高声念给全学院听。\n# en: A festival committee table on a lawn, a thin purse on a velvet cushion and a list of patrons with plenty of space left.\nscene [614979] = 草坪上一张节庆筹委会的桌子，天鹅绒垫子上放着一只瘪瘪的钱袋，旁边一份还空着很多位置的赞助人名单。\n# en: Fund the champion's purse\nopt.left.label [151eb5] = 包下冠军奖金\n# en: {~Your name is read out first, and mispronounced only slightly.|Your name rings across the lawn before the first bout. Two first-years bow to you, unsure why.} The champion thanks you from the podium.\nopt.left.say.1 [ce25e1] = {~你的名字第一个被念出来，只念错了一点点。|第一场比赛前，你的名字响彻草坪。两个一年级生朝你鞠躬，也不知为什么。}冠军在领奖台上向你致谢。\n# en: Cheer from the stands instead\nopt.right.label [cf44f8] = 改在看台上加油\n# en: Other names are read out, among them a professor's cat and \"a well-wisher\". You cheer for all of them, and loudest for the champion.\nopt.right.say.1 [083b9f] = 念到了别人的名字，其中有一位教授的猫和“一位好心人”。你为他们每一个欢呼，为冠军喊得最响。\n\n@event patron_ledger_print\n# en: Paper for the Press\ntitle [f32e02] = 印刷机要纸\n# en: Nessa Quill corners you by the library stairs, holding an empty paper tray like evidence. \"Special edition. Biggest story of the term. No paper. You,\" she says, pointing, \"look solvent. Patrons get their name on the masthead.\"\ntext [2bc86d] = 内莎·奎尔在图书馆楼梯边把你堵住，举着一只空纸盘，像举着证物。“特刊。本学期最大新闻。没纸了。你，”她指着你说，“看起来有钱。赞助人能上报头。”\n# en: A cramped office under a staircase, a groaning printing press, and a young editor holding out an empty paper tray.\nscene [a206ca] = 楼梯下一间狭小的办公室，一台呻吟着的印刷机，一位年轻的编辑举着一只空纸盘。\n# en: Pay for the whole print run\nopt.left.label [451de8] = 包下整期印刷\n# en: \"Headline: generous student saves free press,\" Nessa says, already writing it. The masthead lists you in tiny capitals, which she says is the most honour anyone gets.\nopt.left.say.1 [079497] = “头条：慷慨学生拯救自由报业，”内莎说着，已经开始写了。报头上用极小的字印着你的名字，她说这已是任何人能得到的最高荣誉。\n# en: Help her fold the edition instead\nopt.right.label [5a2823] = 改为帮她折报纸\n# en: You'd rather give her your time than your crowns. Nessa scrounges paper from the Ledger's scrap bin, and you fold until midnight, fingers grey with ink. Nessa credits you on the back page as \"the folding department\".\nopt.right.say.1 [177253] = 你宁愿给她时间，也不想给她克朗。内莎从《流明纪事报》的废纸箱里搜罗出纸来，你一直折到午夜，手指被油墨染得灰扑扑的。内莎在报纸背面给你署了名：“折页部”。\n\n@event donate_greenhouse_glass\n# en: The Broken Panes\ntitle [cd7452] = 碎掉的玻璃\n# en: Last night's hail went through the greenhouse glass like a hammer through toffee. A collection tin hangs on the gate with a hand-lettered sign: every pane paid for keeps the mandrakes from catching cold.\ntext [fa105f] = 昨夜的冰雹砸穿温室的玻璃，就像锤子砸进太妃糖。大门上挂着一只募捐罐和一块手写的牌子：每捐一块玻璃，曼德拉草就少一分着凉的危险。\n# en: A collection tin on a garden gate, broken glass glittering in the grass behind it and steam escaping from a damaged greenhouse.\nscene [45c440] = 花园大门上挂着一只募捐罐，后面草地上碎玻璃闪闪发光，一座破损的温室正往外冒着热气。\n# en: Pay for a whole pane\nopt.left.label [14b4c4] = 捐一整块玻璃\n# en: A gardener with mud to the elbows insists on showing you your pane. Inside it is warm, green and loud with opinions. You are welcome, she says, any morning.\nopt.left.say.1 [96b7a3] = 一位泥巴糊到手肘的园丁非要带你去看“你的”那块玻璃。里面温暖、葱绿，满是各种意见。她说，你哪天早上来都欢迎。\n# en: Walk on\nopt.right.label [383259] = 径直走过\n# en: You walk past the tin. Behind the gate, something small and leafy sneezes. You feel, obscurely, that you've been judged.\nopt.right.say.1 [8ff62f] = 你从募捐罐旁走过。大门后，有个小小的、长着叶子的东西打了个喷嚏。你隐约觉得自己被评判了。\n\n@event donate_duel_floor\n# en: The Scorched Floor\ntitle [47b123] = 烧焦的地板\n# en: A duellist with a bandaged hand rattles a box at the foot of the east stair. \"The hall floor,\" he says. \"Someone set it on fire again. Every board you buy, we carve your initials on the underside. Honour, and splinters.\"\ntext [d8070e] = 一个手上缠着绷带的决斗者在东楼梯脚下摇着一只箱子。“大厅的地板，”他说，“又有人把它点着了。你买一块板，我们就把你的姓名缩写刻在背面。荣誉，外加木刺。”\n# en: A sweaty duellist with a collection box at the foot of a stair, scorched floorboards stacked behind them.\nscene [ea8375] = 一个满头大汗的决斗者在楼梯脚下捧着募捐箱，身后堆着烧焦的地板。\n# en: Buy a row of floorboards\nopt.left.label [a3aa26] = 买下一整排地板\n# en: He drags you upstairs to see. The hall smells of smoke and wax, and the practice dummies glare at you. Your initials go on the underside of the board by the door.\nopt.left.say.1 [58bb9f] = 他拽着你上楼去看。大厅里一股烟和蜡的味道，练习假人们瞪着你。你的姓名缩写刻在了门边那块板的背面。\n# en: Wish him luck with the bandage\nopt.right.label [8e4b8e] = 祝他绷带早日拆掉\n# en: He thanks you with exaggerated courtesy and goes back to rattling. Somewhere above, steel rings on steel, and nobody invites you up.\nopt.right.say.1 [c52343] = 他夸张地向你行礼致谢，又回去接着摇箱子。楼上某处传来钢铁相击的声音，没有人邀请你上去。\n\n@event xchg_arrival\n# en: Guests in Green\ntitle [d9ca04] = 身着绿袍的客人\n# en: Four students in Thornwick green arrive for a term, with trunks and very polite smiles. Within an hour every portrait in the entrance hall is winking at the wrong people. A prefect looks around for someone, anyone, to show them the castle.\ntext [5855c8] = 四个穿着荆木绿袍的学生来这里待一个学期，带着箱子和非常客气的笑容。不到一个小时，门厅里每一幅肖像都在冲错的人眨眼。一位级长四处张望，想找个人——随便谁——带他们参观城堡。\n# en: Four students in green Thornwick robes standing in a grand entrance hall, the portraits on the walls all turned to stare at them.\nscene [4447a8] = 四个穿绿色荆木长袍的学生站在宏伟的门厅里，墙上的肖像全都转过头盯着他们。\n# en: Offer to show them round\nopt.left.label [52352b] = 主动带他们参观\n# en: You give the full tour, moving stairs included. By the end the boy with the sketchbook admits Lumenhold's towers are \"not as crooked as reported\".\nopt.left.say.1 [110d38] = 你带他们走完了全程，移动楼梯也算在内。最后那个拿速写本的男生承认，流明堡的塔“没有传闻中那么歪”。\n# en: Halfway round, they convince you a wall is a door. It isn't. They are very sorry, and laughing too hard to say so.\nopt.left.say.2 [5cd71f] = 走到一半，他们让你相信一面墙是一扇门。它不是。他们非常抱歉，只是笑得太厉害，说不出口。\n# en: Watch them from a distance\nopt.right.label [e05dd0] = 远远地观察他们\n# en: You watch them all week. They watch back. You learn that the quiet one counts doorways, and that they know you're watching.\nopt.right.say.1 [175ece] = 你观察了他们整整一周。他们也观察你。你发现那个安静的人会数门洞，也发现他们知道你在看。\n\n@event xchg_ink_cuffs\n# en: The Ink-Stained Cuffs\ntitle [a6af14] = 沾墨的袖口\n# en: Late in the library, you find the Thornwick girl with the ink-stained cuffs copying from a chained book she shouldn't have reached. She sees you see her. \"It's only history,\" she says. \"Unless you want it to be a scandal.\"\ntext [2833d3] = 深夜的图书馆里，你撞见那个袖口沾着墨迹的荆木女生正在抄一本她本不该够得着的锁链书。她看见你看见了她。“只是历史而已，”她说，“除非你想让它变成丑闻。”\n# en: A library at dusk, a student in green robes with ink-stained cuffs copying from a book chained to a desk.\nscene [00e6b0] = 黄昏的图书馆，一个袖口沾墨的绿袍学生正从一本锁在桌上的书里抄写。\n# en: Report her to the librarian\nopt.left.label [c73db6] = 向管理员举报她\n# en: The librarian thanks you, coldly. The Thornwick girl is confined to the guest wing for a week. She nods to you at dinner afterwards, as if you had both played well.\nopt.left.say.1 [66098b] = 图书管理员冷冷地向你道了谢。那个荆木女生被禁足在客房区一周。之后的晚饭上她朝你点点头，好像你们俩都下了一手好棋。\n# en: Keep quiet, for a favour\nopt.right.label [cca079] = 保持沉默，换个人情\n# en: She tears out the page she copied and hands you the other half of her notes instead. \"We're even,\" she says. You're fairly sure you aren't.\nopt.right.say.1 [ee5c42] = 她撕下抄好的那页，转而把自己笔记的另一半递给你。“我们扯平了，”她说。你很确定并没有。\n\n@event xchg_farewell\n# en: A Thornwick Farewell\ntitle [7e24b5] = 荆木的告别\n# en: The Thornwick students leave on today's barge. The boy with the sketchbook finds you on the jetty and holds out his scarf. \"It's a custom,\" he says. \"Probably. I've just decided.\" Behind him his friends pretend not to listen.\ntext [c9e163] = 荆木的学生们今天乘驳船离开。拿速写本的男生在栈桥上找到你，递出他的围巾。“这是个习俗，”他说，“大概吧。我刚决定的。”他身后的朋友们假装没在听。\n# en: Students in green and blue robes on a jetty at dawn, a barge waiting, one of them holding out a striped scarf.\nscene [75f77e] = 黎明时分的栈桥上，穿绿袍和蓝袍的学生们站着，一艘驳船在等，其中一人递出一条条纹围巾。\n# en: Swap scarves\nopt.left.label [1fdbff] = 交换围巾\n# en: You trade scarves on the jetty. Someone from your own house mutters \"traitor\" at breakfast. You wear it anyway, and get a letter from across the hills by the end of the month.\nopt.left.say.1 [5d5b9e] = 你们在栈桥上交换了围巾。早饭时，你自己学院的某人嘀咕了一句“叛徒”。你照样围着它，月底前还收到了一封翻山而来的信。\n# en: Challenge him to one last duel\nopt.right.label [07d107] = 邀他最后决斗一场\n# en: You win, narrowly, on the wet boards. He laughs, bows, and sketches you mid-spell on the barge as it pulls away. The sketch arrives by post, flattering.\nopt.right.say.1 [debf60] = 你在湿漉漉的木板上险胜。他大笑着鞠了一躬，驳船离岸时还在给施法中的你画速写。那张速写后来寄到了，画得相当美化。\n# en: He wins with an illusion of a second you. You both end up in the lake. It is, somehow, the best goodbye you've had.\nopt.right.say.2 [cbf2ca] = 他用一个“第二个你”的幻象赢了。你们俩最后都掉进了湖里。不知怎么，这竟是你经历过最好的一次告别。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/jobs.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/jobs.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event lib_biting_book\n# en: The Book That Bites\ntitle [e0be45] = 会咬人的书\n# en: Reshelving in the zoology section, a fat leather book clamps onto your finger and refuses to let go. The head librarian, without looking up, says: \"Stroke the spine.\"\ntext [68fee7] = 你在动物学区上架时，一本厚厚的皮面书咬住了你的手指，死活不松口。图书馆馆长头也不抬地说：“摸摸它的书脊。”\n# en: A thick leather book with tiny teeth along its pages snapping at a hand reaching for it on a high shelf.\nscene [b648b2] = 一本书页边缘长着细牙的厚皮书，正冲着伸向高处书架的手咬去。\n# en: Muzzle it with a belt\nopt.left.label [c9d229] = 用皮带把它的嘴捆上\n# en: You wrestle it into submission and shelve it. It growls at you every time you pass for the rest of the term.\nopt.left.say.1 [91c33d] = 你把它摁服了，放回书架。接下来整个学期，你每次经过它都会冲你低吼。\n# en: Stroke the spine and read it\nopt.right.label [b279ad] = 摸摸书脊，读一读\n# en: It purrs, opens and turns out to be a fascinating treatise on carnivorous plants. It only bites twice more. The reshelving waits until tomorrow.\nopt.right.say.1 [ad9f03] = 它发出呼噜声，自己翻开了——原来是一部关于食肉植物的精彩论著。它只又咬了你两次。上架的活只好等到明天。\n\n@event lib_overdue_professor\n# en: Overdue\ntitle [40f62b] = 逾期未还\n# en: Professor Thorne returns a book that is eleven years overdue. The fine, by library rules, is forty crowns. He looks at you. You look at the ledger.\ntext [f16fb3] = 索恩教授还了一本逾期十一年的书。按照图书馆规定，罚款是四十克朗。他看着你。你看着罚款簿。\n# en: A severe professor towering over a library desk, a stack of books under one arm, a very small student assistant on the other side holding a fines ledger.\nscene [9af617] = 一位严厉的教授高高站在图书馆柜台前，胳膊下夹着一摞书，柜台另一边一个小小的学生助理捧着罚款簿。\n# en: Waive the fine\nopt.left.label [20e10a] = 免了罚款\n# en: \"Hm,\" says Thorne. You can't tell if you've earned his respect or lost it.\nopt.left.say.1 [30eaf0] = “嗯。”索恩说。你说不清自己是赢得了他的尊重，还是失去了它。\n# en: Enforce the rules\nopt.right.label [32a605] = 照章办事\n# en: A long silence. Then he pays, in exact change. \"Good,\" he says, and you could swear he almost smiles.\nopt.right.say.1 [a7c7e3] = 漫长的沉默。然后他付了钱，分文不差。“很好。”他说，你敢发誓他差点笑了。\n\n@event lib_restricted_cart\n# en: The Restricted Cart\ntitle [1e31b9] = 禁书推车\n# en: You are asked to wheel the Restricted Section returns to the vault. The cart's chain has come loose. One of the books has fallen open on a page that is writing itself.\ntext [e83ef7] = 你被派去把禁书区归还的书推回书库。推车的锁链松了。其中一本书摊开在一页正在自己书写的纸上。\n# en: A wheeled library cart chained shut, piled with black-bound books, parked alone in a candlelit aisle.\nscene [e3b4b3] = 一辆用锁链锁着的图书推车，堆满黑皮书，孤零零地停在烛光摇曳的过道里。\n# en: Close it without looking\nopt.left.label [69f4b5] = 不看，直接合上\n# en: The book snaps shut with a sound like a disappointed sigh. The vault door clangs. The head librarian notes the time, approvingly.\nopt.left.say.1 [2fb0b1] = 书啪地合上，发出一声像是失望的叹息。书库的门哐当关上了。馆长记下了时间，满意地点点头。\n# en: Read a few lines\nopt.right.label [40815b] = 读上几行\n# en: The words crawl off the page and up your sleeve. That night, you can't get warm.\nopt.right.say.1 [166cd2] = 文字从书页上爬出来，钻进了你的袖子。那天夜里，你怎么也暖和不起来。\n# en: A spell for walking through mirrors. You will never try it. Probably.\nopt.right.say.2 [ee6c9d] = 一个穿过镜子的咒语。你永远不会去试的。大概吧。\n\n@event tavern_brawl\n# en: Brawl at the Newt\ntitle [6ed513] = 蝾螈酒馆斗殴\n# en: A dwarf and a troll disagree about the price of turnips, and the Gilded Newt erupts. A chair flies past your head. The owner is hiding behind the bar, gesturing at you to do something.\ntext [f434ad] = 一个矮人和一个巨魔为芜菁的价格吵了起来，镀金蝾螈酒馆瞬间炸了锅。一把椅子从你头顶飞过。老板躲在吧台后面，比划着让你想想办法。\n# en: A low-beamed tavern mid-brawl: a chair in mid-air, a dwarf on a table, cider sloshing everywhere.\nscene [d9c407] = 一间低梁小酒馆正在混战：一把椅子悬在半空，一个矮人站在桌上，苹果酒洒得到处都是。\n# en: Duck behind the bar too\nopt.left.label [56a05c] = 也躲到吧台后面\n# en: You crouch beside the owner and share a bag of crisps while the room destroys itself.\nopt.left.say.1 [87132f] = 你蹲在老板旁边，和他分吃一袋薯片，看着整个屋子自我毁灭。\n# en: Break it up\nopt.right.label [12966a] = 上去劝架\n# en: You slam a tray on the bar and bellow. Silence. The troll apologises. The dwarf tips you fifteen crowns.\nopt.right.say.1 [b417fb] = 你把托盘往吧台上一拍，大吼一声。全场安静。巨魔道了歉。矮人给了你十五克朗小费。\n# en: You step in. Someone's elbow steps into your face.\nopt.right.say.2 [763c5b] = 你上前一步。不知谁的胳膊肘也上前一步，到了你脸上。\n\n@event tavern_star_coin\n# en: A Generous Stranger\ntitle [678324] = 慷慨的陌生人\n# en: A hooded stranger nurses a cider all evening and leaves a tip: a black coin stamped with a hollow star. \"For the student,\" they say. \"Tell Kael Dumont his family sends regards.\"\ntext [b2c4b8] = 一个戴兜帽的陌生人一整晚只喝了一杯苹果酒，留下的小费是一枚印着空心星的黑色硬币。“给这个学生的，”对方说，“转告凯尔·杜蒙，他的家人向他问好。”\n# en: A hooded figure at a corner table sliding a black coin across the wood, its surface stamped with a dark star.\nscene [fe800a] = 角落桌旁一个戴兜帽的身影把一枚黑色硬币推过木桌，币面上印着一颗暗星。\n# en: Refuse the coin\nopt.left.label [999c57] = 拒绝这枚硬币\n# en: The stranger shrugs and pockets it. When you look again, the corner table is empty and the cider is frozen solid.\nopt.left.say.1 [53629a] = 陌生人耸耸肩，把它收回口袋。你再看时，角落的桌子空了，那杯苹果酒冻成了冰。\n# en: Take it\nopt.right.label [18a43f] = 收下它\n# en: The coin is cold as ice and heavier than it should be. You find yourself turning it over in your pocket for days.\nopt.right.say.1 [2b7ed8] = 硬币冷得像冰，比看上去要重。接下来好几天，你发现自己总在口袋里摩挲它。\n\n@event tavern_extra_shifts\n# en: More Shifts\ntitle [bc17d5] = 加班\n# en: The owner is short-handed. \"Double shifts for a month,\" he says. \"Double pay. I know you've got studies, but you've also got rent.\"\ntext [daff5a] = 老板人手不够。“一个月双班，”他说，“双倍工钱。我知道你还要上学，但你也还要交房租。”\n# en: A ruddy tavern owner leaning on the bar, wiping a mug, a rota pinned behind him covered in crossed-out names.\nscene [205014] = 一个红光满面的酒馆老板靠在吧台上擦杯子，身后钉着的排班表上划掉了一个又一个名字。\n# en: Decline\nopt.left.label [eaef39] = 拒绝\n# en: \"Fair enough,\" he says, and asks again next week.\nopt.left.say.1 [2ad5ce] = “行吧。”他说，然后下周又来问。\n# en: Take the extra shifts\nopt.right.label [124a34] = 接下加班\n# en: A month of late nights and early lectures. Your purse is heavy. So are your eyelids.\nopt.right.say.1 [bef75a] = 一个月的深夜打工和清早上课。你的钱袋沉甸甸的。眼皮也是。\n\n@event tavern_broken_glass\n# en: Broken Glass\ntitle [6a7d8c] = 打碎的酒杯\n# en: {A tray of tankards meets a regular's elbow, and the floor is suddenly all glass.|Another tray, another floor of glass. The Newt goes through tankards the way other places go through napkins.|Glass on the floor again, and the same regular's elbow to blame.} {The regular is very sorry, loudly.|Everyone lifts their feet.|The dog is already heading for it.}\ntext [f64d54] = {一托盘酒杯撞上了一位常客的胳膊肘，地上顿时满是碎玻璃。|又一托盘，又是一地玻璃。蝾螈酒馆消耗酒杯的速度，就像别处消耗餐巾。|地上又是玻璃，又是那位常客的胳膊肘惹的祸。} {那位常客大声地连连道歉。|所有人都把脚抬了起来。|那条狗已经朝碎片走过去了。}\n# en: A tavern floor glittering with broken tankards, a server crouching with a cloth, a dog padding closer.\nscene [6d91e1] = 酒馆地板上闪着碎酒杯的光，一名侍应蹲着拿抹布，一条狗正慢慢凑近。\n# en: Clear it by hand, fast\nopt.left.label [b1cbdf] = 徒手快快收拾\n# en: {You have it cleared before the dog gets there, with a cut across your palm to show for it. The landlady binds it with a bar towel.|Fast and thorough, apart from the sliver you find in your thumb at midnight.|You beat the dog to it by a whisker and a nicked finger. The landlady gives the dog a sausage for its trouble.}\nopt.left.say.1 [7d2b36] = {你赶在狗之前收拾干净了，手掌上多了一道口子作证。老板娘用一条吧台毛巾给你包上。|又快又干净，只是半夜你才在拇指里发现一根玻璃碴。|你以一根胡须的差距和一根划破的手指抢在了狗前面。老板娘给了狗一根香肠作为补偿。}\n# en: Fetch the broom and do it properly\nopt.right.label [32e157] = 拿扫帚好好扫\n# en: {By the time you're back with the broom, three customers have left with their cider unfinished. The landlady counts them.|You sweep slowly and safely while the bar goes dry. The landlady taps the clock.|You go for the broom, and the landlady has to hold the dog back by the collar while the customers' cider goes warm.}\nopt.right.say.1 [e43aa3] = {等你拿着扫帚回来，已经有三个客人苹果酒没喝完就走了。老板娘一个个数着。|你慢慢地、稳稳地扫，吧台那边却断了酒。老板娘敲了敲钟。|你去拿扫帚，老板娘只好揪着狗的项圈拦住它，客人们的苹果酒都放温了。}\n\n@event tavern_after_closing\n# en: After Closing\ntitle [c895a2] = 打烊之后\n# en: {Your shift tonight runs until the Newt closes, long after the last barge.|Another late shift. By closing time the lake will be dark.|Late shift again. You've stopped expecting to see the last barge.} {season == \"winter\": These nights the shore path is ice under snow.|{Fog comes off the water most nights now.|The shore path has no lamps.|Somebody always sings, badly, on the way home.}} It's a long walk round the lake to the castle.\ntext [920705] = {你今晚的班要上到蝾螈酒馆打烊，那时末班驳船早就开走了。|又是晚班。到打烊时，湖面会一片漆黑。|又是晚班。你已经不指望赶上末班驳船了。} {season == \"winter\": 这些夜里，湖岸小路上是雪下的冰。|{这阵子湖上夜夜起雾。|湖岸小路上没有灯。|回家路上总有人在唱歌，唱得很难听。}} 绕湖走回城堡，路还很长。\n# en: A lantern-lit shore path at night, a tired server walking alone beside black water, the castle's towers far off.\nscene [f876cc] = 夜里一条提灯照亮的湖岸小路，一名疲惫的侍应独自走在黑水边，城堡的塔楼远在天边。\n# en: Walk the shore path\nopt.left.label [8e64c1] = 走湖岸小路\n# en: {Two hours round the lake, and every noise in the reeds is something. You reach the gates with wet boots and a turned ankle.|You walk home in the dark and slip twice. You fall into bed as the first bell rings.|You walk home with the other late workers, singing badly to keep the dark off. You're hoarse by the gates, and late to bed.}\nopt.left.say.1 [aebb96] = {绕湖走了两个钟头，芦苇里每一声响都像有什么东西。你走到城门时靴子湿透，脚踝也扭了。|你摸黑走回去，滑倒了两次。第一遍钟响时你才倒在床上。|你和其他上晚班的人一起走回去，唱着难听的歌驱散黑暗。到城门时嗓子哑了，睡得也晚了。}\n# en: Sleep by the Newt's fire\nopt.right.label [66046e] = 睡在酒馆炉边\n# en: {The landlady lends you a blanket and the settle by the fire. You wake at dawn smelling of woodsmoke and miss the first lecture.|You sleep on the settle under somebody's coat and catch the morning barge, too late for your first class.|You hear the others singing their way home along the shore and choose the fire. The settle is lumpy, and you miss the first lecture anyway.}\nopt.right.say.1 [cf7c9a] = {老板娘借你一条毯子，让你睡炉边的长椅。你在黎明醒来，一身木烟味，错过了第一节课。|你盖着不知谁的外套睡在长椅上，搭上了早班驳船，却赶不上第一节课了。|你听见别人沿着湖岸唱着歌回去，自己选了炉火。长椅硌得慌，第一节课你照样错过了。}\n\n@event apoth_wrong_label\n# en: A Mislabelled Jar\ntitle [2e5055] = 贴错的标签\n# en: The jar labelled \"Sleeping Draught\" is green. Sleeping Draught is blue. You're fairly sure the green stuff is Draught of Unending Hiccups. Old Pell is very proud of his labelling.\ntext [90816e] = 标着“安眠药剂”的罐子是绿色的。安眠药剂是蓝色的。你很确定这绿色的东西是“无尽打嗝药剂”。老佩尔对自己的标签工作非常自豪。\n# en: Shelves of glass jars in a narrow shop, one jar labelled SLEEPING DRAUGHT glowing an ominous green.\nscene [da4c60] = 狭窄店铺里一排排玻璃罐，其中一只标着“安眠药剂”的罐子发出不祥的绿光。\n# en: Quietly fix the label\nopt.left.label [d3ca76] = 悄悄改掉标签\n# en: Nobody ever knows. Somewhere, a customer sleeps soundly instead of hiccupping for a week.\nopt.left.say.1 [3b7685] = 永远没人知道。某个地方，一位顾客安然入睡，而不是打一星期的嗝。\n# en: Point it out to Pell\nopt.right.label [4225c6] = 告诉佩尔\n# en: Pell goes red, then thanks you gruffly and slips you three crowns \"for your eyes\".\nopt.right.say.1 [cdda86] = 佩尔脸一红，粗声道了谢，塞给你三克朗，“算你眼尖”。\n\n@event apoth_poor_mother\n# en: Can't Afford It\ntitle [f4bd61] = 买不起\n# en: A woman counts out coppers for fever tonic. She's fifteen crowns short. Her son coughs into her coat. Pell is in the back room.\ntext [1d93f2] = 一位妇人一枚枚数着铜板买退烧药。还差十五克朗。她的儿子靠在她外套上咳嗽。佩尔在后屋。\n# en: A tired woman at a shop counter counting copper coins, a small child coughing beside her.\nscene [6ddd5b] = 一位疲惫的妇人在柜台前数着铜币，身边一个小孩在咳嗽。\n# en: Charge the full price\nopt.left.label [9ea316] = 照价收钱\n# en: She leaves without it. The bell on the door rings for a very long time.\nopt.left.say.1 [b36f54] = 她没买成就走了。门上的铃铛响了很久很久。\n# en: Pay the difference yourself\nopt.right.label [28226a] = 自己垫上差价\n# en: You slip the tonic into her basket. Next week a pie appears on the counter with your name on it.\nopt.right.say.1 [8762e7] = 你把药悄悄放进她的篮子。下周，柜台上出现了一个写着你名字的馅饼。\n\n@event apoth_nightshade_order\n# en: The Nightshade Order\ntitle [d8aa3b] = 颠茄订单\n# en: A note arrives with a sealed purse: a large order of nightshade, grave-moss and powdered moonstone. Legal, technically. Ingredients for something very old and very forbidden, also technically.\ntext [7f5f4d] = 一张便条随一只封好的钱袋送来：大量的颠茄、墓苔和月长石粉。严格来说，合法。严格来说，也是某种非常古老、非常禁忌之物的原料。\n# en: An order slip on a counter listing a dozen deadly reagents, signed with an elegant, illegible flourish.\nscene [fe014b] = 柜台上一张订货单，列着十几种致命试剂，签名是一个优雅却无法辨认的花体字。\n# en: Fill the order\nopt.left.label [c3e9db] = 照单配货\n# en: The courier who collects it never shows his face. The purse is very generous.\nopt.left.say.1 [c33bee] = 来取货的信使始终没有露脸。钱袋非常丰厚。\n# en: Refuse and report it\nopt.right.label [ce8f5b] = 拒绝并上报\n# en: The headmistress listens to your report in silence. \"Thank you,\" she says. \"Tell no one else.\" She looks worried.\nopt.right.say.1 [5c685a] = 院长沉默地听完你的报告。“谢谢你，”她说，“别再告诉任何人。”她看起来很担忧。\n\n@event stables_hippogriff\n# en: Bow to the Hippogriff\ntitle [0d01e9] = 向鹰头马身有翼兽鞠躬\n# en: The new hippogriff won't let anyone near the feed trough. The stablemaster says to bow, keep eye contact and not blink. \"Whatever you do, don't blink.\"\ntext [e114f7] = 新来的鹰头马身有翼兽不让任何人靠近食槽。马厩总管说要鞠躬、保持对视、不许眨眼。“无论如何，别眨眼。”\n# en: A proud hippogriff in a straw-strewn stall, head lowered, regarding a stable hand who is bowing very deeply.\nscene [3828d0] = 铺满稻草的马厩里，一头高傲的鹰头马身有翼兽低着头，打量着一个深深鞠躬的马厩工。\n# en: Leave the feed and run\nopt.left.label [4fb2e9] = 扔下饲料就跑\n# en: You toss the feed bucket and flee. The hippogriff eats it, and the bucket.\nopt.left.say.1 [e88a40] = 你扔下饲料桶就逃。它把饲料吃了，连桶一起。\n# en: Bow and hold its gaze\nopt.right.label [d95cb6] = 鞠躬，与它对视\n# en: It bows back. After that it follows you around the stables like a large, feathered dog.\nopt.right.say.1 [9dbab4] = 它回了一个礼。从那以后，它在马厩里跟着你转，像一只长了羽毛的大狗。\n# en: You blinked. It knocks you flat against the trough, and you spend the evening in the infirmary with your arm in a sling.\nopt.right.say.2 [a82f7a] = 你眨眼了。它一下把你撞倒在食槽边，你吊着胳膊在医务室里度过了整个晚上。\n\n@event stables_dragon_egg\n# en: An Egg in the Hay\ntitle [b1a78f] = 草堆里的蛋\n# en: Mucking out the far stall, you find an egg the size of a melon, nestled in the hay and steaming gently. It's very warm. It's very definitely not a chicken's.\ntext [067bf4] = 清扫最里面的隔间时，你在草堆里发现一枚西瓜大的蛋，正轻轻冒着热气。它很烫。它绝对不是鸡蛋。\n# en: A speckled egg the size of a melon nestled in hay, steam rising from it, a stable hand's lantern held over it.\nscene [bb4b8b] = 一枚西瓜大小、带斑点的蛋窝在干草里冒着热气，马厩工的提灯举在它上方。\n# en: Report it to Professor Brask\nopt.left.label [983969] = 报告布拉斯克教授\n# en: Brask nearly faints. \"Smugglers! A firedrake egg!\" It's taken to the menagerie. You get a commendation.\nopt.left.say.1 [95ded5] = 布拉斯克差点晕过去。“走私犯！火龙蛋！”蛋被送进了动物园。你得到了一份嘉奖。\n# en: Keep it\nopt.right.label [87c2f5] = 留下它\n# en: You smuggle it back to your dormitory wrapped in a scarf. It purrs. Eggs shouldn't purr.\nopt.right.say.1 [8852b1] = 你用围巾把它裹好，偷偷带回了宿舍。它在打呼噜。蛋不该会打呼噜的。\n\n@event egg_hatches\n# en: It Hatches\ntitle [8c317e] = 破壳了\n# en: At 3am your pillow catches fire. The egg has hatched: a firedrake the size of a kitten, delighted with itself and setting fire to everything it loves. It loves you very much.\ntext [c42d90] = 凌晨三点，你的枕头着火了。蛋孵出来了：一条小猫大的火龙，对自己满意极了，见到喜欢的东西就点着。它非常非常喜欢你。\n# en: A tiny red dragon perched on a scorched dormitory pillow, a puff of smoke rising from its nostrils, a horrified roommate in the doorway.\nscene [41e73c] = 一条小红龙蹲在烧焦的宿舍枕头上，鼻孔冒出一缕烟，门口的室友吓呆了。\n# en: Hand it over to the menagerie\nopt.left.label [141f8c] = 交给动物园\n# en: Brask takes it gently. \"You'll be able to visit.\" You do, every week. It always tries to set you on fire, affectionately.\nopt.left.say.1 [695354] = 布拉斯克轻轻接过它。“你可以来探望。”你每周都去。它总是试图把你点着，满怀爱意地。\n# en: Hide it as long as you can\nopt.right.label [7752e9] = 能藏多久藏多久\n# en: You keep it secret for a month — buying fireproof sheets, raw meat, an asbestos sock — before releasing it in the mountains. It circles once, roaring.\nopt.right.say.1 [40bff7] = 你把它藏了一个月——买防火床单、生肉、一只石棉袜子——然后在山里放了它。它盘旋一圈，长啸一声。\n# en: The smoke alarm charm goes off in three towers at once. Discovered, singed, and in very deep trouble.\nopt.right.say.2 [25dba6] = 三座塔的烟雾警报咒同时响起。被发现了，被烧焦了，而且麻烦大了。\n\n@event stables_night_shift\n# en: The Sick Pegasus\ntitle [651cda] = 生病的天马\n# en: The oldest pegasus in the stables is sick. Someone has to sit with her overnight, keep her warm, and make sure she drinks. It's unpaid. Old Maud says she'll do it if no one else will. Maud is eighty.\ntext [e3c4b6] = 马厩里最老的天马病了。得有人整夜守着她、给她保暖、确保她喝水。没有报酬。老莫德说没人去的话她就去。莫德八十岁了。\n# en: A white pegasus lying in a moonlit stall, wings folded, a student sitting beside it with a lantern and a blanket.\nscene [e7765d] = 一匹白色天马收着翅膀卧在月光照亮的马厩里，一个学生提着灯、抱着毯子坐在它身旁。\n# en: Let Maud do it\nopt.left.label [e408cf] = 让莫德去\n# en: You sleep well. Maud is a little slower the next day.\nopt.left.say.1 [d77695] = 你睡得很好。第二天，莫德的动作慢了一些。\n# en: Take the night shift\nopt.right.label [ead26b] = 你来值夜班\n# en: She drinks. You talk to her all night about home. At dawn she stands, shakes her wings, and nuzzles your hair.\nopt.right.say.1 [e093f9] = 她喝了水。你整夜跟她聊家乡。黎明时，她站起来抖了抖翅膀，用鼻子蹭了蹭你的头发。\n\n@event tutor_hopeless\n# en: The Hopeless Case\ntitle [323c15] = 没救的学生\n# en: Your tutee has been trying to levitate a feather for three weeks. The feather has not moved. The tutee is starting to cry. You have one more session before their exam.\ntext [04a494] = 你辅导的学生已经尝试让一根羽毛悬浮三个星期了。羽毛一动没动。学生快哭了。考试前你们只剩一次课。\n# en: A tiny first-year in oversized robes pointing a wand at a feather, which is stubbornly not moving, while a tutor watches with infinite patience.\nscene [29897d] = 一个穿着过大长袍的小个子一年级生用魔杖指着一根纹丝不动的羽毛，辅导员以无限的耐心在旁边看着。\n# en: Drill the basics, again\nopt.left.label [652518] = 再练一遍基础\n# en: Wrist, word, will. Wrist, word, will. On the exam, the feather twitches. It's enough to pass. Your own levitation has never been cleaner.\nopt.left.say.1 [1d2c6c] = 手腕、咒语、意志。手腕、咒语、意志。考试时，羽毛抽动了一下。够及格了。你自己的悬浮咒从没这么干净过。\n# en: Try something unorthodox\nopt.right.label [b16c64] = 试点不寻常的办法\n# en: You tell them to imagine the feather is in love with the ceiling. It shoots upward and sticks there. They pass with distinction.\nopt.right.say.1 [e57332] = 你让对方想象羽毛爱上了天花板。羽毛嗖地升上去，粘在了那里。对方以优异成绩通过。\n# en: You tell them to imagine the feather is in love with the ceiling. They are confused. So is the feather.\nopt.right.say.2 [79cec2] = 你让对方想象羽毛爱上了天花板。对方很困惑。羽毛也很困惑。\n\n@event tutor_bribe\n# en: An Indecent Proposal\ntitle [e847fe] = 不体面的提议\n# en: A rich second-year doesn't want tutoring. He wants an essay. \"Thirty crowns,\" he says, dropping a purse on the table. \"Nobody needs to know.\"\ntext [5e864c] = 一个富家二年级生不想要辅导。他想要一篇论文。“三十克朗，”他说着把钱袋扔在桌上，“没人需要知道。”\n# en: A rich student in tailored robes sliding a heavy coin purse across a study table, smiling smugly.\nscene [188c2e] = 一个穿着定制长袍的富家学生把一只沉甸甸的钱袋推过书桌，得意地笑着。\n# en: Refuse\nopt.left.label [cada35] = 拒绝\n# en: He sneers and goes elsewhere. You spend the evening on your own essays. Later you see him in detention, scrubbing a cauldron.\nopt.left.say.1 [1faaba] = 他冷笑一声，另寻他人去了。你把晚上花在了自己的论文上。后来你看见他在禁闭室里刷坩埚。\n# en: Write the essay\nopt.right.label [b2934c] = 替他写\n# en: His professor recognises your style. You both end up in detention. He doesn't speak to you. Fine.\nopt.right.say.1 [955908] = 他的教授认出了你的文风。你们俩都被关了禁闭。他不再理你。正好。\n# en: He gets a decent mark. You get thirty crowns and a slightly sour feeling.\nopt.right.say.2 [b854de] = 他拿到了不错的分数。你拿到了三十克朗，和一丝酸溜溜的感觉。\n\n@event tutor_prodigy\n# en: Surpassed\ntitle [679359] = 青出于蓝\n# en: Your newest tutee, a first-year barely a month in, casts in a week what took you a year. Then she asks you a question you can't answer.\ntext [04f3f6] = 你新带的学生，一个入学才一个月的一年级生，一周就学会了你花一年才学会的咒语。然后她问了你一个你答不上来的问题。\n# en: A small first-year casting a dazzling spell that fills a classroom with light, their tutor watching with complicated feelings.\nscene [9e6498] = 一个小个子一年级生施展出一道耀眼的咒语，满屋光芒，辅导员在一旁神情复杂。\n# en: Bluff an answer\nopt.left.label [86ba79] = 硬编一个答案\n# en: She nods politely. You both know.\nopt.left.say.1 [7e3422] = 她礼貌地点点头。你们俩心里都明白。\n# en: Admit you don't know and find out together\nopt.right.label [b4d010] = 承认不知道，一起去查\n# en: You spend an evening in the library together. She thanks you as if you've taught her something. Maybe you have.\nopt.right.say.1 [92c9bb] = 你们在图书馆待了一晚上。她向你道谢，好像你教了她什么。也许你真的教了。\n\n@event courier_storm\n# en: Delivery in a Storm\ntitle [d6be36] = 风暴中送件\n# en: An urgent parcel needs to cross the lake tonight. There's a storm. The dispatcher offers double pay to whoever's fool enough. Everyone looks at you.\ntext [7b10ad] = 一件急件今晚必须送过湖。外面有风暴。调度员给愿意去的傻瓜开双倍工钱。所有人都看着你。\n# en: A courier on a broom bent low against a lightning-lit sky, a parcel strapped to their back, rain sheeting sideways.\nscene [eccc6d] = 一名信使伏低身子骑着飞帚冲进闪电照亮的夜空，背上绑着包裹，雨水横扫。\n# en: Wait for the storm to pass\nopt.left.label [fd0d0f] = 等风暴过去\n# en: The parcel arrives late. The client deducts it from your wages. Rude, but survivable.\nopt.left.say.1 [c849f6] = 包裹迟到了。客户从你的工钱里扣了钱。无礼，但还活得下去。\n# en: Fly through it\nopt.right.label [d49b80] = 穿过风暴\n# en: You thread the lightning like a needle and land soaked, laughing, with the parcel dry.\nopt.right.say.1 [419505] = 你像穿针一样穿过闪电，浑身湿透地落地，大笑着，包裹却是干的。\n# en: Lightning strikes close enough to stand your hair on end. You lose the broom, the parcel and the pay, and gain a limp.\nopt.right.say.2 [71c971] = 一道闪电劈得太近，你的头发都竖了起来。你丢了飞帚、包裹和报酬，得了一条瘸腿。\n\n@event courier_whispering_parcel\n# en: The Whispering Parcel\ntitle [00dbb7] = 会低语的包裹\n# en: Your parcel is addressed to \"K. Dumont, Stillwater House\". It is ice cold and it whispers when you aren't looking at it.\ntext [a7f392] = 你的包裹寄给“静水院，K. 杜蒙”。它冷得像冰，你不看它的时候，它会低语。\n# en: A brown paper parcel tied with black string on a courier's satchel, frost forming on its corners, faint whispers rising from it.\nscene [cef7c7] = 信使挎包上一个用黑绳捆着的牛皮纸包裹，边角结着霜，隐隐传出低语声。\n# en: Deliver it unopened\nopt.left.label [edbe83] = 原封不动地送达\n# en: Kael takes it without a word. His hands shake. The next day, he looks like he hasn't slept.\nopt.left.say.1 [6ad92e] = 凯尔一言不发地接过包裹。他的手在抖。第二天，他看起来像一夜没睡。\n# en: Peek inside\nopt.right.label [95f6de] = 偷看一眼\n# en: Inside: a letter in a cold, beautiful hand. \"The Star wakes. Come home, or open the door. Your family.\" You reseal it perfectly.\nopt.right.say.1 [49350a] = 里面是一封信，字迹冰冷而优美：“星辰苏醒了。回家，或者打开那扇门。——你的家人。”你把它完美地重新封好。\n# en: The seal hisses as it breaks. Kael is waiting at the door when you arrive. He saw. He says nothing, which is worse.\nopt.right.say.2 [1d94cc] = 封印裂开时发出嘶嘶声。你到的时候，凯尔已经等在门口了。他看见了。他什么也没说，这更糟。\n\n@event courier_sky_pirates\n# en: Sky Pirates\ntitle [933c94] = 空中海盗\n# en: Three figures on patched brooms drop out of a cloud and box you in. \"Toll,\" says their leader, a woman with a parrot that is also on a tiny broom. \"Eight crowns or the parcel.\"\ntext [0892d1] = 三个骑着补丁飞帚的身影从云里冲出来围住了你。“过路费，”领头的女人说，她的鹦鹉也骑着一把小飞帚，“八克朗，或者包裹。”\n# en: Three ragged figures on patched brooms surrounding a courier above the clouds, cutlasses glinting.\nscene [5f6143] = 云层之上，三个衣衫褴褛、骑着补丁飞帚的身影围住一名信使，弯刀闪闪发亮。\n# en: Pay the toll\nopt.left.label [a1a28f] = 交过路费\n# en: They let you pass with a salute. The parrot salutes too.\nopt.left.say.1 [de94eb] = 他们敬了个礼放你过去。鹦鹉也敬了个礼。\n# en: Outfly them\nopt.right.label [a811f6] = 甩掉他们\n# en: You dive through a cloudbank and lose them over the lake. The client tips extra for the excitement.\nopt.right.say.1 [b17031] = 你俯冲穿过云堤，在湖面上空甩掉了他们。客户因为这份刺激多给了小费。\n# en: They catch you, take the toll, the parcel and your hat. The parrot wears it.\nopt.right.say.2 [8905f7] = 他们追上了你，拿走了过路费、包裹，还有你的帽子。鹦鹉戴上了它。\n\n@event broom_courier_bad_landing\n# en: A Bad Landing\ntitle [cc3b0c] = 摔了一跤\n# en: {The courtyard flagstones are wet, and your broom has opinions about wet flagstones.|Another wet landing. Your knees remember the last one.|Wet flagstones again. You've learned to dread this courtyard.} {You come in too fast.|A gust takes the tail as you drop.|A pigeon chooses this moment to exist.} You hit the ground rolling, and your ankle says no.\ntext [afa176] = {庭院的石板是湿的，而你的扫帚对湿石板很有意见。|又是一次湿漉漉的降落。你的膝盖还记得上一次。|又是湿石板。你已经学会害怕这个庭院了。} {你降得太快了。|你往下落时一阵风掀起了扫帚尾。|一只鸽子偏偏挑这个时候出现。} 你滚着落了地，脚踝说：不行。\n# en: A courier sprawled on wet flagstones beside a broom, parcels scattered across a rain-slick courtyard.\nscene [20fcd9] = 一名信使摔倒在湿漉漉的石板上，扫帚在旁，包裹散落在雨水打湿的庭院里。\n# en: Finish the round anyway\nopt.left.label [bde896] = 坚持送完这一趟\n# en: {You limp the last three parcels to their doors. The dispatcher notes your time and, later, your ankle.|You finish the round on one good leg and a great deal of stubbornness. The bruise is spectacular by evening.|You finish the round with a limp and a grudge against pigeons. The dispatcher writes down both.}\nopt.left.say.1 [7abcb4] = {你一瘸一拐地把最后三个包裹送到门口。派件员记下了你的用时，后来又记下了你的脚踝。|你靠一条好腿和满满的倔劲送完了这一趟。到晚上，那块淤青壮观极了。|你一瘸一拐地送完了这一趟，还记恨上了鸽子。派件员把两样都记下了。}\n# en: Hand your sack to another courier\nopt.right.label [54b1a8] = 把邮袋交给别人\n# en: {Another courier takes your sack with a sympathetic wince. Half a shift's pay, and an evening with your foot up.|You sit on the loft steps with a cold cloth on your ankle while someone else flies your round. They keep half the pay.|You hand over your sack and spend the evening on the loft steps, foot up, glaring at pigeons.}\nopt.right.say.1 [0a316e] = {另一位信使同情地皱了皱眉，接过你的邮袋。半个班次的工钱，外加一晚上把脚架高。|你坐在阁楼台阶上，用凉布敷着脚踝，别人替你飞完了这一趟。工钱他们分走一半。|你交出邮袋，一晚上坐在阁楼台阶上，脚架得高高的，狠狠瞪着鸽子。}\n\n@event broom_courier_lost_parcel\n# en: One Parcel Short\ntitle [bffcca] = 少了一个包裹\n# en: {Somewhere between the loft and the lecture tower, a parcel has left your sack.|One parcel short, again. You've started counting at every landing.|Another parcel gone. You count at every landing, and still you lose one.} {It was marked \"Fragile\".|It was marked \"Urgent\", in red, twice.|It was addressed to a professor.}\ntext [4d5e5e] = {从阁楼到讲堂塔之间的某处，一个包裹离开了你的邮袋。|又少了一个包裹。你已经开始每次落地都数一遍了。|又丢了一个包裹。你每次落地都数，可还是丢了一个。} {上面写着“易碎”。|上面用红笔写着“加急”，写了两遍。|收件人是一位教授。}\n# en: A courier on a windy tower top turning an empty mail sack inside out, the lake far below.\nscene [6920d2] = 一名信使站在大风的塔顶上，把空邮袋翻了个底朝天，湖面远在下方。\n# en: Retrace your route\nopt.left.label [0a2eb5] = 沿原路找回去\n# en: {You find it in a hedge behind the greenhouses, whole and slightly damp. You deliver it late, with a story nobody believes.|It's on the boathouse roof. You climb up for it and deliver it with moss on your knees.|You find it in a gutter of the lecture tower and deliver it to the professor, who signs for it without looking up.}\nopt.left.say.1 [e91e55] = {你在温室后面的树篱里找到了它，完好无损，只是有点潮。你迟到着送达，还附上一个没人相信的故事。|它在船屋屋顶上。你爬上去拿，送到时膝盖上沾满了青苔。|你在讲堂塔的排水沟里找到了它，送到那位教授手里，教授头也没抬就签收了。}\n# en: {You search until dark. The loft takes the parcel's worth from your pay, and the dispatcher stops calling you reliable.|Nothing. The client complains twice, in writing, and the loss comes out of your wages.|The professor's parcel never turns up. The professor does, at the loft, and the loss comes out of your wages.}\nopt.left.say.2 [770b4a] = {你一直找到天黑。阁楼从你的工钱里扣了包裹的钱，派件员也不再说你靠得住了。|什么也没找到。客户写信投诉了两次，损失从你的工钱里扣。|教授的包裹始终没找到。教授本人倒是找到阁楼来了，损失从你的工钱里扣。}\n# en: Report it lost at once\nopt.right.label [5e3d08] = 立刻报告丢失\n# en: {The dispatcher writes it in the ledger and the loss in your pay. \"Happens,\" they say, in a voice that means it shouldn't.|You own up straight away. The loft takes it out of your wages, but the dispatcher seems to think better of you for it.|You report it at once. The dispatcher winces at the address, a professor's. It comes out of your pay, with an apology to write.}\nopt.right.say.1 [ead196] = {派件员把它记进账本，把损失记进你的工钱。“常有的事，”派件员说，语气却分明是说不该有。|你马上就承认了。阁楼从你的工钱里扣了钱，但派件员似乎对你更看得起了。|你立刻报了丢失。派件员看到地址是位教授，皱了皱眉。钱从你的工钱里扣，你还得写一封道歉信。}\n\n@event job_off_shift\n# en: Not Your Shift\ntitle [4d3d09] = 不是你的班\n# en: You turn up for work out of habit. The roster by the door says your shifts this term are done, and whoever runs the place says so too. \"Nobody pays you to be here today. Go and have a life.\"\ntext [d6e252] = 你出于习惯来上工。门边的值班表写着你这学期的班都上完了，管事的人也这么说：“今天没人付钱让你待在这儿。去过你自己的日子吧。”\n# en: A duty roster pinned by a staff door, every one of a student's shifts for the term already ticked off.\nscene [dbf031] = 员工门边钉着一张值班表，一个学生这学期的每一班都已打了勾。\n# en: Take the time for yourself\nopt.left.label [52cbbe] = 把时间留给自己\n# en: {~You leave before anyone can change their mind, and spend the hours on nothing in particular. It's wonderful.|You take the advice. The place gets on without you, and so, it turns out, do you.}\nopt.left.say.1 [78a31c] = {~你趁别人改主意之前就走了，把这几个钟头花在无所事事上。美极了。|你听了劝。这地方没有你照样转，而你，原来没有它也照样过得好。}\n# en: Stay and help anyway\nopt.right.label [0f8ed9] = 还是留下帮忙\n# en: {~You stay, unpaid. Someone notices. Nobody thanks you, and you're tired the next morning.|You lend a hand for an hour. It's not a shift and it doesn't pay, but people remember who stayed.}\nopt.right.say.1 [990370] = {~你留了下来，没有工钱。有人注意到了。没人道谢，第二天早上你很累。|你搭了一个钟头的手。这不算一班，也没有钱，但大家记得谁留下了。}\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/last_day.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/last_day.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event last_morning\n# en: The Last Morning\ntitle [1e4ad4] = 最后的早晨\n# en: You wake before the bell, out of habit, and there's no bell. Your trunk is packed and strapped. The robes hang on the wardrobe door. It's Starfall, the last day of the year, and tomorrow you graduate. The castle is very quiet, as if it's listening.\ntext [c12f0c] = 你习惯性地在钟声前醒来，可已经没有钟声了。你的箱子收拾好了，捆紧了。礼袍挂在衣柜门上。今天是星坠节，一年的最后一天，明天你就毕业了。城堡非常安静，像是在倾听。\n# en: A dormitory room in early light, trunks packed and strapped, graduation robes hanging on the wardrobe door, the towers gold through the window.\nscene [b7f47f] = 清晨微光中的宿舍，箱子已经收拾好捆紧，毕业礼袍挂在衣柜门上，窗外的塔楼一片金黄。\n# en: Get up and begin the last day\nopt.ok.label [72f307] = 起床，开始最后一天\n# en: {has(origin_noble): You dress with care, the way you were raised to.|{has(origin_street): You check your pockets out of habit, then laugh at yourself.|You dress slowly, wanting the morning to last.}} The staircase that moves waits for you at the top, for once, as if it knows.\nopt.ok.say.1 [c634ce] = {has(origin_noble): 你按从小被教导的那样，仔细地穿戴整齐。|{has(origin_street): 你习惯性地摸了摸口袋，然后笑自己。|你慢慢地穿衣服，想让这个早晨长一点。}} 那道会移动的楼梯难得一次在顶上等着你，好像它知道。\n\n@event last_results\n# en: The Final Lists\ntitle [8ae58d] = 最终名单\n# en: The final lists are pinned to the great hall door, and the whole year is crowded in front of them. {gpa >= 3.5: Your name is near the top, and someone has drawn a small star beside it.|{gpa >= 2.5: Your name sits in the middle of the list, solid and unremarkable, exactly where you hoped.|{gpa >= 2.0: Your name is near the bottom, but it's there.|Your name is on the list, just. Someone has pencilled \"phew\" next to it, possibly you.}}} {flag(thesis_distinction): In the thesis column, one word in gold ink, Distinction.|{flag(thesis_passed): In the thesis column, a plain pass.|{flag(thesis_resubmit): In the thesis column, in red, To be resubmitted.|}}}\ntext [fc0c76] = 最终名单钉在大礼堂的门上，全年级的人都挤在前面。{gpa >= 3.5: 你的名字靠近最上面，有人在旁边画了一颗小星星。|{gpa >= 2.5: 你的名字在名单中间，稳稳当当，毫不起眼，正是你希望的位置。|{gpa >= 2.0: 你的名字靠近末尾，但它在上面。|你的名字在名单上，勉强。有人在旁边用铅笔写了“呼”，可能是你自己。}}} {flag(thesis_distinction): 论文一栏里，一个金色墨水写的词，优等。|{flag(thesis_passed): 论文一栏里，平平常常的通过。|{flag(thesis_resubmit): 论文一栏里，红字写着，需重交。|}}}\n# en: A crowd of final-year students around a great oak door in the entrance hall, long lists of names pinned to it, one student reaching up to touch a line.\nscene [888bd9] = 门厅里一群毕业生围着一扇巨大的橡木门，门上钉着长长的名单，一个学生伸手去摸其中一行。\n# en: Touch your name, for luck\nopt.ok.label [85aada] = 摸一摸你的名字，讨个好运\n# en: {gpa >= 3.0: People you barely know shake your hand on the way out. You let them.|{gpa >= 2.0: You run a finger along the line. Four years, one line. It's enough.|You stand there a while after the crowd has gone. It's on the list. It counts.}}\nopt.ok.say.1 [a8c0d4] = {gpa >= 3.0: 出去的路上，几乎不认识的人都来和你握手。你由着他们。|{gpa >= 2.0: 你用手指划过那一行。四年，一行字。够了。|人群散去后，你在那儿站了一会儿。名字在名单上。这就算数。}}\n\n@event last_career\n# en: What Comes Next\ntitle [4fbe37] = 接下来\n# en: {flag(career_accepted): A letter about your start date. {flag(career_conclave): The Conclave expects you at the Seven Towers in autumn, in grey.|{flag(career_library): The Royal Library has a desk for you, and a key to a room nobody else may enter.|{flag(career_guild): The Guild of Alchemists sends a list of the protective clothing you must buy yourself.|{flag(career_unseen): The Office of Unseen Affairs sends no letter at all, which is how you know you're expected.|{flag(career_circus): The circus wagons will pass the village in a week, and they'll slow down, but they won't stop.|The faculty barge leaves in autumn, and you'll be on it, on the wrong side of the lectern.}}}}}|{flag(career_offered): The offer is still on your desk, unsigned. Today is the last day it stands.|No letter, no start date. Everyone else seems to know where they're going, or says so.}}\ntext [3e8f77] = {flag(career_accepted): 一封关于入职日期的信。{flag(career_conclave): 七塔议会等你秋天穿着灰袍去七塔报到。|{flag(career_library): 皇家图书馆给你留了一张桌子，还有一把通往一间别人不得进入的房间的钥匙。|{flag(career_guild): 炼金师公会寄来一张清单，列着你得自己购买的防护服。|{flag(career_unseen): 王室隐秘事务署根本没有来信，你就是这样知道他们在等你。|{flag(career_circus): 马戏团的马车一周后会经过村子，它们会放慢速度，但不会停下。|教职员的驳船秋天出发，你会在船上，站在讲台的另一边。}}}}}|{flag(career_offered): 那份录用书还放在你桌上，没签字。今天是它有效的最后一天。|没有信，没有入职日期。其他人似乎都知道自己要去哪儿，或者说自己知道。}}\n# en: A letter with a heavy seal open on a packed trunk, a student sitting on the trunk lid reading it by the window.\nscene [564759] = 一封盖着厚重封蜡的信摊开在收拾好的箱子上，一个学生坐在箱盖上，就着窗边的光读着。\n# en: Sign the offer after all\nopt.sign.label [411b94] = 最后还是签下录用书\n# en: You sign it on the trunk lid and run it to the owl tower before you can change your mind. {iv_score >= 3: The signing purse lapsed with their deadline; the place, it turns out, did not. |} Your handwriting looks like a grown-up's.\nopt.sign.say.1 [04765d] = 你在箱盖上签了字，趁自己还没改主意，一路跑去了猫头鹰塔。{iv_score >= 3: 签约金随着他们的期限一起作废了；位置倒是还在。|}你的笔迹看起来像个大人的。\n# en: Write back that you'll be there\nopt.reply.label [db9603] = 回信说你会去\n# en: You write it in your best hand and seal it with the academy's wax for the last time. Then you sit for a while with your hands in your lap, getting used to being expected somewhere.\nopt.reply.say.1 [dbc595] = 你用最好的字迹写好回信，最后一次用学院的蜡封上。然后你双手放在膝上坐了一会儿，慢慢习惯被某个地方期待着的感觉。\n# en: Leave it all open\nopt.road.label [5cbfd6] = 一切都先不定\n# en: {flag(career_accepted): The letter can wait a day. Today is still yours, the last one that is.|You decide not to decide, not today. The world is very large, and it's been waiting four years. It can wait one more day.}\nopt.road.say.1 [6f0c03] = {flag(career_accepted): 那封信可以等一天。今天还是你的，最后一个属于你的日子。|你决定不做决定，至少今天不做。世界很大，它已经等了四年。再多等一天也无妨。}\n# en: Write home about what's next\nopt.home.label [5c1adf] = 给家里写信说说以后\n# en: You write home, and tell them the truth about what comes next, or what doesn't yet. It's the longest letter you've ever sent, and the easiest.\nopt.home.say.1 [466235] = 你给家里写信，如实说了接下来的打算，或者还没有打算。这是你寄过的最长的一封信，也是最轻松的一封。\n\n@event last_lake\n# en: The Lake, One Last Time\ntitle [3fc408] = 最后一次看湖\n# en: You walk down to the lake before breakfast, the way you did your first morning. {flag(hollow_sealed): It's clearer than you've ever seen it, clear all the way down, and nothing looks back. You did that. Almost nobody knows.|{flag(hollow_freed): It has been different since the spring it froze, warmer somehow, and the candles along the shore lean toward you as you pass. You did that too.|{flag(hollow_bargain): The water is still. The mark on your palm is not. It hums as you come near, like something greeting something.|{flag(hollow_fled): The water looks ordinary. It isn't. Somewhere under it a door stands ajar, because you ran, and the headmistress's hair has been white since that night.|{flag(hollow_failed): It's calm this morning. You know what lies beneath it, and who paid to keep it closed. You stand on the jetty a long time.|{clues(hollowstar) >= 1: There were stories about this lake, and you followed some of them a little way. You never learned what they meant, or whether they meant anything. The lake isn't telling.|It's flat and blue and ordinary, and the towers stand upside down in it. Four years, and it never once did anything strange. That you saw.}}}}}}\ntext [aee919] = 你在早饭前走到湖边，就像你来的第一个早晨那样。{flag(hollow_sealed): 它比你见过的任何时候都清澈，一直清到湖底，没有什么在回望你。这是你做到的。几乎没人知道。|{flag(hollow_freed): 自从那年春天它结冰之后，它就变了，不知怎么暖了些，你经过时，岸边的蜡烛都朝你倾斜。这也是你做到的。|{flag(hollow_bargain): 湖水很静。你掌心的印记却不静。你走近时它嗡嗡作响，像是某样东西在问候另一样东西。|{flag(hollow_fled): 湖水看上去很普通。其实不然。它底下某处有一扇门半开着，因为你跑了，而院长的头发从那一夜起就白了。|{flag(hollow_failed): 今天早上它很平静。你知道它底下是什么，也知道是谁付出代价让它保持关闭。你在码头上站了很久。|{clues(hollowstar) >= 1: 关于这座湖有过一些传说，你顺着其中几个走了一小段。你始终没弄明白它们是什么意思，或者是否有什么意思。湖不会告诉你。|它平坦、湛蓝、普普通通，塔楼倒映在水里。四年了，它从没做过一件奇怪的事。至少你没看见。}}}}}}\n# en: A student standing alone at the end of a wooden jetty at dawn, the Mirror Lake perfectly still, the towers reflected upside down in it.\nscene [0a0e22] = 黎明时分，一个学生独自站在木码头的尽头，镜湖一片静止，塔楼倒映在水中。\n# en: Say goodbye to the lake\nopt.ok.label [822a12] = 向湖道别\n# en: {~You skim one stone. It skips seven times, which is your record, and nobody saw.|You crouch and put your hand in the water. It's cold, the way it always was.} Then you walk back up to the castle without looking round.\nopt.ok.say.1 [3cf7b9] = {~你打了一个水漂。跳了七下，是你的纪录，没人看见。|你蹲下来把手伸进水里。很凉，和以前一样。} 然后你走回城堡，没有回头。\n\n@event last_friends\n# en: The Last Ordinary Morning\ntitle [090df6] = 最后一个寻常的早晨\n# en: {friend} finds you in the courtyard with two mugs of tea and no particular reason. \"Last ordinary morning,\" {they} say{s}. \"I thought it should be spent properly.\" You sit on the rim of the fountain and don't talk about tomorrow.\ntext [f8ed5f] = {friend} 端着两杯茶在庭院里找到你，没什么特别的理由。“最后一个寻常的早晨，”{they} 说。“我觉得应该好好过。”你们坐在喷泉边上，不谈明天。\n# en: Two students sitting on the rim of a courtyard fountain with mugs of tea, packed trunks stacked by the doors behind them.\nscene [d79b2e] = 两个学生端着茶杯坐在庭院的喷泉边上，身后的门边堆着收拾好的箱子。\n# en: Promise to visit, and mean it\nopt.left.label [c31f13] = 约好去看对方，说到做到\n# en: You set a date, a real one, with a place and a time, and write it in both your notebooks. {friend} underlines it twice. You both know which of you will be late.\nopt.left.say.1 [a5332c] = 你们定了一个日子，一个真正的日子，有地点也有时间，写在两个人的笔记本上。{friend} 在下面划了两道线。你们都知道到时候谁会迟到。\n# en: Give {them} something of yours\nopt.right.label [e3fb07] = 把你的一样东西送给 {them}\n# en: You give {friend} the thing on your desk you'd most miss: a cracked mug, a dog-eared book, the stone from the lake. {They} turn{s} it over and over and say{s} nothing, which says enough.\nopt.right.say.1 [b34494] = 你把桌上你最舍不得的东西给了 {friend}：一只裂了的杯子、一本卷了角的书、一块湖边的石头。{They} 把它翻来覆去地看，什么也没说，这已经说明了一切。\n\n@event last_partner\n# en: After Tomorrow\ntitle [2d7d5c] = 明天之后\n# en: {partner} has a map, an apple and the whole afternoon. \"Tomorrow we're not students,\" {they} say{s}. \"So I thought we'd better have a plan. Or decide not to.\" The map is spread on the battlement between you, weighted at one corner with the apple.\ntext [67a061] = {partner} 带着一张地图、一个苹果和一整个下午。“明天我们就不是学生了，”{they} 说。“所以我觉得我们最好有个计划。或者决定不要计划。”地图摊在你们之间的城垛上，一角压着那个苹果。\n# en: Two students sitting on a castle battlement at noon, legs dangling over the edge, a map of the realm spread between them, weighted with an apple.\nscene [c72168] = 正午时分，两个学生坐在城堡的城垛上，双腿悬在外面，王国的地图摊在两人之间，用一个苹果压着。\n# en: Plan the first year together\nopt.left.label [ed42fa] = 一起规划第一年\n# en: You plan it badly and in detail: a town on the map, a room above a shop, whose turn it is to cook. {partner} draws a circle round the town in ink, so it can't be rubbed out.\nopt.left.say.1 [345a32] = 你们规划得很糟又很细：地图上的一座小镇、一间店铺楼上的屋子、轮到谁做饭。{partner} 用墨水把那座小镇圈了起来，这样就擦不掉了。\n# en: Fold the map, and just have today\nopt.right.label [6d2d59] = 收起地图，只过好今天\n# en: You fold the map and put it away. \"Whatever comes,\" you say, and {partner} takes your hand, and for an afternoon there's nothing on the other side of tomorrow at all.\nopt.right.say.1 [fc8f32] = 你把地图折起来收好。“不管以后怎样，”你说，{partner} 握住了你的手，整整一个下午，明天的另一边什么都没有。\n\n@event last_rival\n# en: One Last Word\ntitle [38a94f] = 最后一句话\n# en: {rival} is waiting in the empty duelling hall, as if by appointment. Four years of grudges, and tomorrow there won't be a school to have them in. \"Well,\" {they} say{s}. \"That's that, then.\"\ntext [aa9863] = {rival} 在空荡荡的决斗厅里等着，像是约好了似的。四年的积怨，而明天就没有学校可以让你们较劲了。“好吧，”{they} 说。“就这样了。”\n# en: Two students facing each other in an empty duelling hall, wands holstered, packed trunks visible through the open door behind them.\nscene [fe4904] = 空荡荡的决斗厅里，两个学生面对面站着，魔杖都收在套里，身后敞开的门外能看见收拾好的箱子。\n# en: Offer your hand\nopt.left.label [1f34dd] = 伸出你的手\n# en: {rival} looks at your hand for a long moment, then takes it, hard. \"You were worth beating,\" {they} say{s}, and then, almost too quietly to hear, \"and worth losing to.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [598903] = {rival} 盯着你的手看了很久，然后握住了，握得很用力。“你值得被打败，”{they} 说，然后，轻得几乎听不见，“也值得输给你。”\n# en: \"Rematch, in ten years.\"\nopt.right.label [3736fb] = “十年后再战。”\n# en: \"Ten years,\" says {rival}, \"and I'll still win.\" You shake on it in front of the last of the Circle, who spread it round the year by supper. It's the closest either of you will come to saying anything kind.\nopt.right.say.1 [ae5a6c] = “十年，”{rival} 说，“我还是会赢。”你们当着决斗社最后几个人的面握手为定，到晚饭时全年级都知道了。这是你们俩最接近说句好话的时刻了。\n\n@event last_starfall\n# en: The Last Starfall\ntitle [78d505] = 最后的星坠节\n# en: Tonight is Starfall, and by tradition the final-years have the observatory roof to themselves. The first-years are down on the lawns with their jars. Up here, everyone is pretending not to be sad.\ntext [b059cd] = 今晚是星坠节，按照传统，观星台的屋顶归毕业生们独享。一年级生们拿着罐子在下面的草坪上。在这上面，每个人都在假装不难过。\n# en: Final-year students in a row on the observatory roof under a sky full of falling stars, graduation robes thrown over their shoulders like blankets.\nscene [f698e6] = 毕业生们在观星台屋顶上排成一排，头顶的天空满是坠落的星星，毕业礼袍像毯子一样披在肩上。\n# en: Catch one for the road\nopt.left.label [cc3892] = 接一颗带上路\n# en: It drops into your hands with a hiss, the last thing Lumenhold gives you. The whole roof cheers. You'll keep it in its jar on a windowsill somewhere you haven't seen yet.\nopt.left.say.1 [317c17] = 它嘶的一声落进你手里，这是流明堡给你的最后一样东西。整个屋顶都在欢呼。你会把它装在罐子里，放在某个你还没见过的地方的窗台上。\n# en: You lunge, miss, and sit down hard, and the whole roof laughs, and you laugh too. Some things you're supposed to leave behind.\nopt.left.say.2 [83ff36] = 你扑过去，没接住，一屁股坐下，整个屋顶都笑了，你也笑了。有些东西本来就该留下。\n# en: Lie back with everyone\nopt.right.label [41957d] = 和大家一起躺下\n# en: You lie in a row with your year, shoulder to shoulder, and count the stars aloud until the counting falls apart. Somebody starts to cry, and then everybody is laughing.\nopt.right.say.1 [d8e7fe] = 你和同年级的人肩并肩躺成一排，大声数着星星，直到数乱了。有人开始哭，然后所有人都笑了起来。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/life.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/life.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event life_staircase\n# en: The Staircase Moves\ntitle [1e1113] = 楼梯动了\n# en: Halfway up the west staircase, it decides to be somewhere else. You're now heading toward a floor you've never seen, at speed, and you're late for class.\ntext [7bd8a9] = 西楼梯爬到一半，它决定去别的地方。你正被高速送往一层你从没见过的楼，而你上课要迟到了。\n# en: A grand spiral staircase in mid-rotation, a student clinging to the banister as the steps swing toward a different floor.\nscene [684196] = 一道宏伟的螺旋楼梯正在旋转，一个学生紧抓扶手，台阶正转向另一层楼。\n# en: Hold on and see where it goes\nopt.left.label [441219] = 抓紧，看它去哪儿\n# en: It deposits you in a dusty forgotten classroom. In a desk: eight old crowns and a love letter from 1802.\nopt.left.say.1 [0aecae] = 它把你放在一间落满灰尘、被遗忘的教室里。课桌里有八枚旧克朗和一封 1802 年的情书。\n# en: It takes you to the roof. Then the cellar. Then the roof again. You miss your morning lectures entirely.\nopt.left.say.2 [cf6831] = 它把你送上屋顶。然后是地窖。然后又是屋顶。你完美错过了整个上午的课。\n# en: Jump for the landing\nopt.right.label [fc3ab6] = 跳向平台\n# en: You leap and land neatly. A passing portrait applauds.\nopt.right.say.1 [e697a6] = 你一跃而起，稳稳落地。路过的一幅肖像画鼓起了掌。\n# en: You leap and land, but not neatly. A passing portrait winces.\nopt.right.say.2 [ff31a3] = 你一跃而起，落地了，但不太稳。路过的一幅肖像画皱了皱眉。\n\n@event life_mystery_stew\n# en: Mystery Stew\ntitle [afd265] = 神秘炖菜\n# en: Tonight's dinner is described on the board only as \"Stew\". It is grey-green. The ladle is standing up by itself. The cook is watching you with hope.\ntext [70292b] = 今晚的晚餐在菜单板上只写着“炖菜”。它是灰绿色的。汤勺自己立在里面。厨师满怀希望地看着你。\n# en: A huge cauldron of grey-green stew in the dining hall, a ladle standing upright in it unaided.\nscene [1a7e62] = 餐厅里一大锅灰绿色的炖菜，一把汤勺自己笔直地立在锅中。\n# en: Eat it bravely\nopt.left.label [b372d7] = 勇敢地吃下去\n# en: It's... delicious? Rich, peppery, faintly glowing. You go back for seconds. The cook beams.\nopt.left.say.1 [768d2a] = 它……很好吃？浓郁、带着胡椒味，还微微发光。你又去盛了一碗。厨师笑开了花。\n# en: It's not delicious. It's not anything a stomach should know about. You spend the night regretting it.\nopt.left.say.2 [995531] = 不好吃。它不是任何胃应该认识的东西。你后悔了一整晚。\n# en: Toast and jam again\nopt.right.label [31a8e9] = 还是吐司配果酱\n# en: You eat toast. The cook looks hurt. You feel guilty all evening.\nopt.right.say.1 [e74e29] = 你吃了吐司。厨师一脸受伤。你内疚了一整晚。\n\n@event life_lost_wand\n# en: Where Is Your Wand?\ntitle [e9e5f4] = 你的魔杖呢？\n# en: Your wand is gone. It was on your desk. Now it isn't. Classes start in twenty minutes.\ntext [006487] = 你的魔杖不见了。它刚才还在桌上。现在没了。二十分钟后就要上课了。\n# en: A dormitory turned upside down, clothes everywhere, a student on hands and knees peering under a bed.\nscene [9d31f7] = 一间被翻得底朝天的宿舍，衣服扔得到处都是，一个学生趴在地上往床底下看。\n# en: Tear the room apart\nopt.left.label [a2a2bf] = 把房间翻个底朝天\n# en: It's in your boot. Why is it in your boot? You make it to class with seconds to spare.\nopt.left.say.1 [f6b100] = 它在你的靴子里。它为什么会在你靴子里？你在最后几秒赶到了教室。\n# en: You find it at lunch, inside the pillowcase. You've missed a whole morning.\nopt.left.say.2 [2b5185] = 午饭时你在枕套里找到了它。你错过了整整一上午。\n# en: Try a summoning charm\nopt.right.label [18e9e7] = 试试召唤咒\n# en: It flies into your hand from inside the wardrobe, offended at having been lost.\nopt.right.say.1 [d8a6af] = 它从衣柜里飞进你手里，对自己被弄丢一事颇为不满。\n# en: Every pencil in the dormitory flies at your face. The wand stays hidden out of spite.\nopt.right.say.2 [1fe091] = 宿舍里每一支铅笔都朝你脸上飞来。魔杖出于怨气，继续藏着。\n\n@event life_snoring_roommate\n# en: The Snorer\ntitle [b3c66f] = 打呼噜的人\n# en: Someone in your dormitory snores like a dragon with a head cold, and nobody will admit to it. You haven't slept properly in a week.\ntext [ab60f0] = 宿舍里有人打起呼噜来像一条感冒的龙，而且没人承认。你已经一个星期没睡好了。\n# en: A dark dormitory, one bed shaking with snores so loud the curtains flutter, another occupant glaring with a pillow over their head.\nscene [a2130d] = 漆黑的宿舍里，一张床随着震天的呼噜声摇晃，窗帘都在飘，另一个人用枕头捂着头怒目而视。\n# en: Brew a silencing draught\nopt.left.label [716d57] = 熬一剂静音药剂\n# en: Perfect silence. Too perfect. The culprit, the quiet one by the window, is now silent even when awake. It wears off by Tuesday. You never quite ask their permission.\nopt.left.say.1 [f6a256] = 完美的寂静。太完美了。罪魁祸首——靠窗那个安静的家伙——现在醒着也发不出声音。到周二才失效。你始终没问过人家同不同意。\n# en: The draught finds the wrong bed. Now two people snore, in harmony.\nopt.left.say.2 [80815d] = 药剂找错了床。现在有两个人在打呼噜，还是和声。\n# en: Move to the common room sofa\nopt.right.label [65d659] = 搬到公共休息室的沙发上\n# en: You sleep on the sofa and meet everyone who comes down at 3am. Some of them are very interesting.\nopt.right.say.1 [168a41] = 你睡在沙发上，结识了每一个凌晨三点下楼的人。其中有些人非常有意思。\n\n@event life_portrait_gossip\n# en: The Portraits Talk\ntitle [b767ea] = 肖像画在说话\n# en: Two portraits in the east gallery are gossiping about a student. Then one of them says your name and they both go suspiciously quiet.\ntext [feda64] = 东画廊里两幅肖像画正在八卦某个学生。然后其中一幅说出了你的名字，两幅画都可疑地安静了下来。\n# en: A long gallery of gilded portraits, painted figures leaning out of their frames toward each other, whispering behind painted hands.\nscene [58f7f0] = 长长的画廊里挂满镀金肖像，画中人探出画框凑在一起，用画出来的手捂着嘴窃窃私语。\n# en: Bribe them with flattery\nopt.left.label [729d7c] = 用奉承贿赂它们\n# en: Ten minutes of compliments on their brushwork, and they tell you everything. Gossip about half the faculty, too.\nopt.left.say.1 [b45427] = 夸了十分钟它们的笔触，它们就什么都告诉你了。还附送了半个教职员团队的八卦。\n# en: \"Flattery,\" sniffs the duchess, \"is so common.\" They turn their backs. Literally.\nopt.left.say.2 [20a283] = “奉承，”公爵夫人嗤之以鼻，“太俗气了。”它们背过身去。字面意义上的。\n# en: Pretend you didn't hear\nopt.right.label [e071a6] = 假装没听见\n# en: You walk on. Behind you, the whispering resumes, louder.\nopt.right.say.1 [546c2f] = 你继续往前走。身后，窃窃私语又响了起来，更大声了。\n\n@event life_pixies\n# en: Pixie Infestation\ntitle [12ac66] = 小精灵泛滥\n# en: Someone left a window open and the dormitory is now full of pixies. They're stealing socks, unscrewing bedposts and laughing in very high voices.\ntext [53332f] = 有人没关窗户，宿舍里现在满是小精灵。它们在偷袜子、拧床柱，还用尖细的嗓音大笑。\n# en: A dormitory full of tiny blue winged creatures swinging from curtains, stealing socks and pulling hair.\nscene [9a096c] = 宿舍里到处是蓝色带翅膀的小生物，荡在窗帘上，偷袜子，扯头发。\n# en: Fight them off\nopt.left.label [a5f0b8] = 把它们打跑\n# en: You catch nine with a laundry bag. The dorm cheers. The pixies swear at you, colourfully.\nopt.left.say.1 [db8849] = 你用洗衣袋抓住了九只。宿舍一片欢呼。小精灵们花样百出地骂你。\n# en: You lose. The pixies hang you from the chandelier by your robe. It takes an hour to get down.\nopt.left.say.2 [87d5ba] = 你输了。小精灵把你的长袍挂在吊灯上，连人一起。你花了一个小时才下来。\n# en: Bribe them with sugar\nopt.right.label [8661f5] = 用糖贿赂它们\n# en: You lay a trail of sugar cubes out of the window. The pixies follow it, munching, and leave one sock as a tip.\nopt.right.say.1 [ace496] = 你在窗外铺了一路方糖。小精灵们边吃边跟着走了，还留下一只袜子当小费。\n\n@event life_gargoyle\n# en: A Talkative Gargoyle\ntitle [7093dd] = 话痨石像鬼\n# en: The gargoyle above the east door clears its throat as you pass. \"You,\" it rumbles. \"You look like someone who'd listen. Nobody listens to gargoyles.\"\ntext [4162d1] = 你经过时，东门上方的石像鬼清了清嗓子。“你，”它低沉地说，“你看起来像会听人说话的人。没人愿意听石像鬼说话。”\n# en: A weathered stone gargoyle on a parapet, one eye open, mouth moving, a student sitting beside it with a sandwich.\nscene [2eb36a] = 护墙上一尊饱经风霜的石像鬼睁着一只眼，嘴在动，一个学生拿着三明治坐在它旁边。\n# en: Hurry along\nopt.left.label [1a6798] = 赶紧走开\n# en: \"Typical,\" says the gargoyle. It sulks until spring.\nopt.left.say.1 [852a4c] = “典型。”石像鬼说。它一直闷闷不乐到了春天。\n# en: Sit and listen\nopt.right.label [1a3cf5] = 坐下来听\n# en: It tells you three hundred years of academy gossip, including where the founders hid the good biscuits. You find the biscuits.\nopt.right.say.1 [3415ec] = 它给你讲了三百年的学院八卦，包括创始者把好饼干藏在了哪里。你找到了那些饼干。\n\n@event life_brownies\n# en: Help in the Night\ntitle [fae0e4] = 夜里的帮手\n# en: You wake to find your desk tidied, your socks darned and your essay proofread. Brownies. They expect something in return. Traditionally: cream.\ntext [bfda31] = 你醒来发现书桌被收拾整齐、袜子被补好、论文被校对过了。是棕仙。它们期待回报。按照传统：奶油。\n# en: Tiny bearded figures in aprons tidying a cluttered student desk by moonlight, one darning a sock.\nscene [bb94a0] = 月光下，系着围裙的长胡子小人在整理一张凌乱的书桌，其中一个在补袜子。\n# en: Leave out a bowl of cream\nopt.left.label [a73198] = 放一碗奶油\n# en: Your room stays spotless all term. Your essays have suspiciously good spelling.\nopt.left.say.1 [f0b341] = 整个学期你的房间都一尘不染。你的论文拼写好得可疑。\n# en: Leave them nothing\nopt.right.label [760b5f] = 什么都不留\n# en: The next morning, every one of your socks is tied in knots. Brownies keep score.\nopt.right.say.1 [ac042b] = 第二天早上，你的每一只袜子都被打了结。棕仙是记账的。\n\n@event life_midnight_snack\n# en: Midnight Hunger\ntitle [5673a0] = 午夜饥饿\n# en: It's midnight and you're starving. The kitchens are three floors down, past a prefect's post.\ntext [d97eb5] = 午夜了，你饿得要命。厨房在三层楼下，要经过一个级长的岗哨。\n# en: A student in pyjamas tiptoeing past a sleeping prefect toward a door marked KITCHENS, a candle cupped in their hand.\nscene [6a7e01] = 一个穿睡衣的学生踮着脚经过打盹的级长，走向一扇写着“厨房”的门，双手护着一支蜡烛。\n# en: Go back to bed hungry\nopt.left.label [6a67a5] = 饿着回去睡\n# en: You dream of sandwiches. Long, detailed, beautiful sandwiches.\nopt.left.say.1 [d77265] = 你梦见了三明治。长长的、细节丰富的、美丽的三明治。\n# en: Sneak down\nopt.right.label [a9ae15] = 偷偷溜下去\n# en: Cold pie, a pot of custard, a heel of bread. Feast of kings.\nopt.right.say.1 [2a2136] = 冷馅饼、一罐蛋奶冻、一块面包头。帝王盛宴。\n# en: The prefect is awake. You spend a week washing the pots you were trying to raid.\nopt.right.say.2 [151ad8] = 级长醒着。你洗了一星期你本想去偷吃的那些锅。\n\n@event life_insomnia\n# en: The Ceiling at 3am\ntitle [bbad03] = 凌晨三点的天花板\n# en: You can't sleep. Your brain has decided to replay every embarrassing thing you've said since you were six.\ntext [0cce8a] = 你睡不着。你的大脑决定把你六岁以来说过的每一句丢人的话都重播一遍。\n# en: A student lying awake staring at a ceiling where faint painted stars have started slowly moving.\nscene [eda828] = 一个学生睁眼躺着，盯着天花板，上面画着的淡淡星星开始缓缓移动。\n# en: Get up and study\nopt.left.label [dd9384] = 起床学习\n# en: By dawn you've read three chapters and remembered one of them.\nopt.left.say.1 [d35323] = 到天亮时你读了三章，记住了其中一章。\n# en: Chamomile, and count the stars\nopt.right.label [b39bbc] = 泡杯洋甘菊，数星星\n# en: Halfway through counting, the stars start counting back. Then you're asleep.\nopt.right.say.1 [297bdf] = 数到一半，星星开始反过来数你。然后你就睡着了。\n\n@event life_morning_run\n# en: The Dawn Run\ntitle [97a783] = 黎明晨跑\n# en: A group of students runs the lakeshore every dawn. They've invited you. It's very early. It's very cold.\ntext [b909a7] = 有一群学生每天黎明沿着湖岸跑步。他们邀请了你。很早。很冷。\n# en: The lakeshore path at dawn, mist on the water, a lone runner's breath steaming, the towers catching the first gold light.\nscene [0d10ee] = 黎明的湖岸小路，水面上浮着薄雾，一个孤独的跑者呼着白气，塔楼映着第一缕金光。\n# en: Sleep in\nopt.left.label [de2af7] = 睡懒觉\n# en: You hear them come back, glowing and smug. You are warm and unrepentant.\nopt.left.say.1 [58ff7c] = 你听见他们回来了，容光焕发，一脸得意。你暖暖和和，毫无悔意。\n# en: Run with them\nopt.right.label [8e7ee8] = 和他们一起跑\n# en: You nearly die at the first mile and feel immortal by the third. The lake shines like a coin. You feel every step of it all through your lectures.\nopt.right.say.1 [2e95a0] = 第一英里你差点死掉，到第三英里你觉得自己长生不老。湖面亮得像一枚硬币。整堂课上，你都能感觉到每一步的后劲。\n\n@event life_homework_mountain\n# en: The Pile\ntitle [654764] = 作业山\n# en: Three essays, a potion log, a star chart and a reading list the length of your arm. All due this week. The pile has achieved its own weather.\ntext [68b440] = 三篇论文、一份药剂日志、一张星图，还有一份和你胳膊一样长的阅读书单。全部本周截止。这座作业山已经形成了自己的小气候。\n# en: A desk buried under a precarious tower of scrolls and books, a student peeking hopelessly over the top.\nscene [7c65a4] = 书桌被一座摇摇欲坠的卷轴和书本之塔淹没，一个学生绝望地从塔顶探出头来。\n# en: Grind through it\nopt.left.label [6c90f1] = 埋头苦干\n# en: You finish at 4am on the last day. Every piece is handed in. You sleep through breakfast.\nopt.left.say.1 [2f2b04] = 最后一天凌晨四点你全部完成。每一份都交上去了。你睡过了早饭。\n# en: Pick what matters, skip the rest\nopt.right.label [321412] = 挑重要的做，其余的跳过\n# en: You hand in two of five and go for a walk. The professors are not pleased. You feel great, temporarily.\nopt.right.say.1 [c142df] = 你交了五份中的两份，然后出去散步了。教授们不太高兴。你感觉好极了，暂时。\n\n@event life_stolen_answers\n# en: The Answers\ntitle [48b36f] = 答案\n# en: A third-year is selling copies of next week's exam papers. Eight crowns for the set. \"Guaranteed real,\" he whispers. \"Straight from the examiner's desk.\"\ntext [47adc0] = 一个三年级生在卖下周考试的整套试卷。八克朗一套。“保真，”他低声说，“直接从考官桌上拿的。”\n# en: A folded paper being passed under a library table, the faint glow of an examiner's seal on it.\nscene [fab1a5] = 一张折好的纸从图书馆桌下传过，上面隐隐闪着考官的印章。\n# en: Walk away\nopt.left.label [96da1a] = 走开\n# en: Two weeks later, five students who bought copies are suspended. The answers were fake.\nopt.left.say.1 [882a6f] = 两周后，买了复印件的五个学生被停学了。那些答案是假的。\n# en: Buy a copy\nopt.right.label [cae18d] = 买一份\n# en: They're real. You know every question before you sit down. You also can't quite look the examiner in the eye.\nopt.right.say.1 [775981] = 是真的。你还没坐下就知道了每一道题。你也没法直视考官的眼睛了。\n# en: They're fake, and planted. You're caught with a copy. Detention, and a very cold letter home.\nopt.right.say.2 [aaad15] = 是假的，而且是个圈套。你被抓到持有复印件。禁闭，外加一封非常冰冷的家书。\n\n@event life_backfire\n# en: Spell Backfire\ntitle [195f68] = 咒语反噬\n# en: You mispronounce a minor cheering charm in the corridor. Something goes wrong. Every time you hiccup, a tiny firework comes out.\ntext [4df613] = 你在走廊里把一个小小的振奋咒念错了。出了点问题。现在你每打一个嗝，就会冒出一朵小烟花。\n# en: A student mid-hiccup, a small firework bursting from their mouth, classmates ducking.\nscene [89a5a5] = 一个学生正在打嗝，一朵小烟花从嘴里炸开，同学们纷纷闪躲。\n# en: Go to the infirmary\nopt.left.label [f09b9c] = 去医务室\n# en: Sister Amaranth cures it in a minute and charges you three crowns. \"For the ceiling,\" she says.\nopt.left.say.1 [4e5350] = 阿玛兰斯修女一分钟就治好了，收了你三克朗。“赔天花板的。”她说。\n# en: Live with it\nopt.right.label [a97cce] = 就这么过吧\n# en: You become briefly famous as \"the sparkler\". It's not the fame you wanted.\nopt.right.say.1 [01b18e] = 你作为“烟花人”短暂地出了名。这不是你想要的那种出名。\n\n@event life_lake_swim\n# en: A Swim in the Lake\ntitle [939267] = 湖中游泳\n# en: It's hot and the whole year has gone down to the jetty. Something large is rumoured to live in the deep part. People are swimming anyway.\ntext [9f8141] = 天很热，整个年级都跑到栈桥边去了。传说湖的深处住着什么大东西。大家还是照游不误。\n# en: Students leaping off a wooden jetty into a sparkling lake on a hot afternoon, something large and dark moving far beneath the surface.\nscene [36073b] = 炎热的午后，学生们从木栈桥跳进波光粼粼的湖里，水面下极深处有个巨大的黑影在移动。\n# en: Stay on the jetty\nopt.left.label [a30932] = 待在栈桥上\n# en: You dangle your feet and eat cherries. Something brushes your toe. You keep your feet up after that.\nopt.left.say.1 [5776ea] = 你晃着脚丫吃樱桃。有什么东西擦过你的脚趾。从那以后你一直把脚抬着。\n# en: Dive in\nopt.right.label [eb830e] = 跳下去\n# en: In the deep part, you open your eyes underwater and see a round door, far below, glowing. Then you need air.\nopt.right.say.1 [0ca583] = 在深水区，你在水下睁开眼，看见很深很深的地方有一扇圆门在发光。然后你需要换气了。\n# en: The water's perfect. Something large and friendly swims under you, and you decide not to panic.\nopt.right.say.2 [0e8868] = 水温完美。有个友善的大家伙从你身下游过，你决定不慌。\n\n@event life_stray_cat\n# en: A Cat Moves In\ntitle [9441a9] = 猫搬进来了\n# en: A black cat with one torn ear is sitting on your pillow. It looks at you as though you are the one trespassing.\ntext [f24184] = 一只耳朵缺了一角的黑猫正坐在你的枕头上。它看着你，好像闯进来的人是你。\n# en: A scruffy black cat sitting on a dormitory pillow as if it has always lived there, one torn ear, very green eyes.\nscene [2adf64] = 一只邋遢的黑猫坐在宿舍枕头上，仿佛一直住在这里，一只耳朵破了，眼睛绿得发亮。\n# en: Shoo it out\nopt.left.label [bd0ff5] = 把它赶出去\n# en: It leaves with enormous dignity and moves in with your neighbour, who spoils it rotten.\nopt.left.say.1 [73a8df] = 它极其庄重地离开了，搬去和你的邻居住，邻居把它宠上了天。\n# en: Let it stay\nopt.right.label [b09a3c] = 让它留下\n# en: It's called Professor now. It sleeps on your homework. You've never been happier.\nopt.right.say.1 [7e205d] = 它现在叫“教授”。它睡在你的作业上。你从没这么幸福过。\n\n@event life_storm_night\n# en: The Lights Go Out\ntitle [3af5ad] = 灯灭了\n# en: A storm knocks out every light-crystal in the dormitory. The common room fills with students, blankets and somebody's terrible ghost story.\ntext [cb21f0] = 一场风暴让宿舍里所有的光晶石都熄灭了。公共休息室里挤满了学生、毯子，和某人讲的烂透了的鬼故事。\n# en: A dark common room lit only by lightning, students huddled under blankets, one telling a ghost story.\nscene [09a304] = 漆黑的公共休息室只被闪电照亮，学生们裹着毯子挤在一起，有人在讲鬼故事。\n# en: Tell an even scarier story\nopt.left.label [36f701] = 讲个更吓人的故事\n# en: Your story is so good that three people sleep with the lights on for a week. You're a legend.\nopt.left.say.1 [d941b3] = 你的故事太好了，有三个人开着灯睡了一个星期。你成了传奇。\n# en: Your story is not scary. It is, however, accidentally hilarious.\nopt.left.say.2 [226bee] = 你的故事不吓人。不过，意外地非常好笑。\n# en: Light the room with a spell\nopt.right.label [928a24] = 用咒语照亮房间\n# en: You fill the room with soft floating lights. Someone starts singing. It turns into the best night of term.\nopt.right.say.1 [a9ec71] = 你让屋里飘满柔和的光点。有人唱起了歌。那成了本学期最美好的一夜。\n# en: You light the sofa. Briefly. The storm seems less frightening after that.\nopt.right.say.2 [725129] = 你点着了沙发。短暂地。那之后，风暴似乎也没那么可怕了。\n\n@event life_mirror_talks\n# en: The Honest Mirror\ntitle [11488a] = 诚实的镜子\n# en: The bathroom mirror has started commenting on your appearance. \"Hair,\" it says this morning. Just: \"Hair.\"\ntext [197720] = 浴室的镜子开始点评你的外表了。今天早上它说：“头发。”就两个字：“头发。”\n# en: A bathroom mirror with a stern painted face in its silvering, frowning at a student mid-toothbrush.\nscene [bed5d4] = 一面浴室镜子的镀银里嵌着一张严肃的画中脸，正对着刷牙刷到一半的学生皱眉。\n# en: Ask for its advice\nopt.left.label [97bf15] = 向它请教\n# en: It turns out to be an excellent stylist. You look great. You hate that it's right.\nopt.left.say.1 [88d699] = 原来它是个出色的造型师。你看起来棒极了。你讨厌它说得对。\n# en: Cover it with a towel\nopt.right.label [719e1f] = 用毛巾盖住它\n# en: It sulks under the towel. Muffled: \"Hair.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [2b3d43] = 它在毛巾下面生闷气。闷声闷气地：“头发。”\n\n@event life_prank_war\n# en: Prank War\ntitle [562813] = 恶作剧大战\n# en: The dormitory across the corridor turned your door into a goat. The goat is fine. Honour, however, demands retaliation.\ntext [a19079] = 走廊对面的宿舍把你的门变成了一只山羊。山羊没事。然而荣誉要求你报复。\n# en: A corridor ankle-deep in enchanted soap bubbles, two dormitories' worth of students hurling them at each other.\nscene [9791ed] = 走廊里没过脚踝的魔法肥皂泡，两个宿舍的学生互相扔着泡泡。\n# en: Rise above it\nopt.left.label [fe2376] = 不跟他们计较\n# en: You take the high road. The goat eats your homework. You let it.\nopt.left.say.1 [f7115b] = 你选择了高尚。山羊吃了你的作业。你随它去了。\n# en: Retaliate\nopt.right.label [5cb1e8] = 报复回去\n# en: You fill their dorm with a thousand singing bubbles. They concede by dawn.\nopt.right.say.1 [59c97e] = 你让他们宿舍里塞满了一千个会唱歌的泡泡。天亮前他们就认输了。\n# en: You're caught carrying a crate of bubbles. Detention, but the goat seems proud of you.\nopt.right.say.2 [aee56b] = 你抱着一箱泡泡时被逮住了。禁闭，但那只山羊似乎为你骄傲。\n# en: Glamour their door into a goat\nopt.up.label [9c44fe] = 用魅影把他们的门变成山羊\n# en: You return the compliment with a glamour: their door becomes a goat, an extremely convincing one, which bleats when knocked. It lasts three days. Nobody can prove a thing.\nopt.up.say.1 [d28289] = 你用一道魅影回敬：他们的门变成了一只山羊，逼真极了，一敲门就咩咩叫。这持续了三天。谁也拿不出证据。\n\n@event life_mail_owl\n# en: The Wrong Letter\ntitle [38e547] = 送错的信\n# en: A delivery owl drops a scented pink letter in your porridge. It's addressed to someone else. It's a love letter. It's very, very passionate.\ntext [6296cf] = 一只送信猫头鹰把一封带香味的粉色信扔进了你的粥里。收件人不是你。是封情书。非常、非常热烈的情书。\n# en: A ruffled delivery owl dropping a scented pink envelope onto a breakfast plate, a student looking up in confusion.\nscene [2c78c3] = 一只羽毛凌乱的送信猫头鹰把一只带香味的粉色信封扔到早餐盘上，一个学生困惑地抬起头。\n# en: Deliver it to its owner\nopt.left.label [6dedfd] = 把它送给收信人\n# en: The recipient goes crimson and thanks you. Two weeks later, they're holding hands with the sender in the dining hall.\nopt.left.say.1 [364f07] = 收信人满脸通红地向你道谢。两周后，他们和写信人在餐厅里手牵着手。\n# en: Read it first\nopt.right.label [e728e4] = 先读一读\n# en: You read it. You will never be able to look the potions professor in the eye again.\nopt.right.say.1 [038e70] = 你读了。你再也无法直视魔药教授的眼睛了。\n\n@event life_money_from_home\n# en: A Parcel From Home\ntitle [0fa1f2] = 家里寄来的包裹\n# en: A parcel from home: a knitted scarf, a jar of jam, a note telling you to eat vegetables, and a small purse of coins.\ntext [e2ed27] = 一个家里寄来的包裹：一条织好的围巾、一罐果酱、一张叮嘱你多吃蔬菜的字条，还有一小袋硬币。\n# en: A brown paper parcel tied with string on a dormitory bed, a knitted scarf, a jar of jam and a small coin purse spilling out.\nscene [632059] = 宿舍床上一个用绳子捆着的牛皮纸包裹，露出一条针织围巾、一罐果酱和一只小钱袋。\n# en: Save the money\nopt.left.label [59078b] = 把钱存起来\n# en: You tuck it away. The jam lasts six days.\nopt.left.say.1 [c9f65f] = 你把钱收好。果酱吃了六天。\n# en: Spend it on a treat for your friends\nopt.right.label [c9ff29] = 请朋友们吃点好的\n# en: Cake from the village for everyone. You're popular for an entire afternoon.\nopt.right.say.1 [182e55] = 从镇上买了蛋糕分给大家。你受欢迎了整整一个下午。\n\n@event life_found_purse\n# en: A Purse in the Grass\ntitle [558b87] = 草丛里的钱袋\n# en: A fat velvet purse lies in the grass by the path. Thirty crowns at least. Embroidered initials: A.T.\ntext [bc1f08] = 小路边的草丛里躺着一只鼓鼓的天鹅绒钱袋。至少三十克朗。上面绣着缩写：A.T.\n# en: A velvet purse lying in the long grass beside a path, coins glinting at its open mouth, initials embroidered on it.\nscene [b64283] = 小路旁长草间躺着一只天鹅绒钱袋，袋口露出闪亮的硬币，上面绣着姓名缩写。\n# en: Return it to its owner\nopt.left.label [c3ae44] = 物归原主\n# en: A.T. turns out to be Aldric Thorne. He takes the purse and stares at you a long moment. \"Honest,\" he says. It sounds like a diagnosis.\nopt.left.say.1 [ccc630] = A.T. 原来是奥尔德里克·索恩。他接过钱袋，盯着你看了很久。“诚实。”他说。听起来像是诊断结果。\n# en: Keep it\nopt.right.label [87c2f5] = 据为己有\n# en: Thirty crowns richer. A few days later, Thorne mentions in class that he lost something. He looks at no one in particular.\nopt.right.say.1 [3a88fb] = 你多了三十克朗。几天后，索恩在课上提到他丢了东西。他没有特意看向任何人。\n\n@event life_peddler\n# en: The Travelling Peddler\ntitle [c295f6] = 行脚商人\n# en: A peddler's wagon has parked by the gates. \"Lucky charms! Genuine article! Thirty crowns, and fortune follows you for a season!\"\ntext [985dd5] = 一辆货郎车停在了校门边。“幸运护身符！货真价实！三十克朗，好运伴你一整季！”\n# en: A gaudy painted wagon at the academy gates, hung with lanterns and trinkets, a peddler with a gold tooth grinning.\nscene [aca905] = 学院门口一辆花哨的彩绘货车，挂满灯笼和小玩意儿，一个镶金牙的货郎咧嘴笑着。\n# en: Buy a lucky charm\nopt.left.label [00deab] = 买个幸运护身符\n# en: 30 gold\nopt.left.hint [54caf0] = 30 金币\n# en: It's a real one. Things go right all month. You start talking to it.\nopt.left.say.1 [eb2634] = 是真货。整个月诸事顺遂。你开始跟它说话了。\n# en: It turns your neck green. Fortune does not follow you. The peddler is long gone.\nopt.left.say.2 [a11a74] = 它把你的脖子染绿了。好运并没有跟着你。货郎早就跑没影了。\n# en: Just browse\nopt.right.label [dbbc5f] = 随便看看\n# en: You buy nothing and enjoy a sugared apple and the peddler's outrageous stories.\nopt.right.say.1 [017402] = 你什么都没买，享受了一只糖苹果和货郎天花乱坠的故事。\n\n@event life_bookshop\n# en: Sale at Grimsby's\ntitle [89ece4] = 格林斯比书店大促\n# en: Grimsby's Books is having a sale. An old copy of \"Advanced Arcane Theory\" for fifteen crowns. Normally forty. It's annotated, apparently, by a famous wizard.\ntext [e4e3d1] = 格林斯比书店在打折。一本旧版《高等奥术理论》只要十五克朗。平时要四十。据说上面有一位著名巫师的批注。\n# en: A crooked bookshop with books stacked to the ceiling, a hand-painted sign in the window reading HALF OFF, MOSTLY.\nscene [3daef9] = 一家歪歪扭扭的书店，书一直堆到天花板，橱窗上手绘的招牌写着：半价，大部分。\n# en: Buy it\nopt.left.label [0aa234] = 买下\n# en: 15 gold\nopt.left.hint [05537f] = 15 金币\n# en: The annotations are brilliant, rude and occasionally about the author's divorce. You learn a great deal.\nopt.left.say.1 [0f1da3] = 批注精彩、刻薄，偶尔还聊到作者的离婚。你学到了很多。\n# en: Leave it\nopt.right.label [3e2fed] = 算了\n# en: You walk away. You think about it for weeks.\nopt.right.say.1 [08ccd0] = 你走开了。接下来好几周你都在惦记它。\n\n@event life_new_robes\n# en: Shabby Robes\ntitle [a4fae7] = 破旧的长袍\n# en: Your robes are fraying at every hem. Someone at dinner called them \"vintage\". It was not a compliment.\ntext [afc5fd] = 你的长袍每一处下摆都磨毛了。晚饭时有人说它们很“复古”。那可不是夸奖。\n# en: A student in a hall mirror holding up a fraying sleeve, a tailor's flyer in their other hand.\nscene [c82b72] = 一个学生站在大厅的镜子前，举起磨毛的袖子，另一只手里拿着裁缝铺的传单。\n# en: Patch them yourself\nopt.left.label [475422] = 自己缝补\n# en: Your stitching is crooked but cheerful. Some people start copying it.\nopt.left.say.1 [019c7e] = 你的针脚歪歪扭扭，却很欢快。有些人开始模仿了。\n# en: Buy new robes (30 gold)\nopt.right.label [ed7264] = 买新长袍（30 金币）\n# en: 30 gold\nopt.right.hint [54caf0] = 30 金币\n# en: Midnight-blue wool, silver trim. People actually turn to look. You walk differently all week.\nopt.right.say.1 [2c96cc] = 午夜蓝的羊毛，银色镶边。人们真的会回头看。你一整周走路的姿势都不一样了。\n\n@event life_card_game\n# en: A Game of Wizard's Hand\ntitle [7c9c91] = 一局巫师牌\n# en: A late-night game of Wizard's Hand. The cards change faces when no one's looking. The pot is thirty crowns. The upper-years look hungry.\ntext [0d1104] = 深夜的一局巫师牌。没人看时，牌面会自己变。奖池三十克朗。高年级生们看起来很饿。\n# en: A smoky corner of the common room, students around a low table with cards and coins, one card floating and changing face.\nscene [404f4d] = 公共休息室烟雾缭绕的角落，学生们围着矮桌打牌，桌上堆着硬币，一张牌浮在空中变换牌面。\n# en: Play\nopt.left.label [ab6f83] = 下场\n# en: You read every bluff and keep a straight face. Thirty crowns and a reputation.\nopt.left.say.1 [36d8bf] = 你识破了每一次虚张声势，始终面不改色。三十克朗，外加名声。\n# en: You lose fifteen crowns and the upper-years' respect, in that order.\nopt.left.say.2 [47f34e] = 你输掉了十五克朗和高年级生的尊重，按这个顺序。\n# en: Watch and learn\nopt.right.label [37e8ef] = 看着学\n# en: You learn more watching than you would have playing. Mostly about who cheats.\nopt.right.say.1 [11df36] = 你看到的比下场玩学到的还多。主要是谁在出老千。\n\n@event life_essay_prize\n# en: The Essay Prize\ntitle [f26093] = 论文奖\n# en: The Lumen Essay Prize: thirty crowns for the best essay on \"Magic and Duty\". The deadline is Friday. You have an idea already.\ntext [04145b] = 流明论文奖：五十克朗，奖给以“魔法与责任”为题的最佳论文。周五截止。你已经有了一个想法。\n# en: A brass plaque on a noticeboard announcing THE LUMEN ESSAY PRIZE — 30 CROWNS, a quill pinned beneath it.\nscene [cb3beb] = 布告栏上一块黄铜牌宣布“流明论文奖——30 克朗”，下面钉着一支羽毛笔。\n# en: Enter\nopt.left.label [f863ad] = 参赛\n# en: The essay eats your week, and wins. Your essay is read aloud at dinner. You don't know where to look.\nopt.left.say.1 [3f2c62] = 这篇论文吃掉了你一整个星期，然后赢了。晚饭时你的论文被当众朗读。你不知道眼睛该往哪儿看。\n# en: The essay eats your week, and doesn't win, but the judges' notes are glowing. Next year, maybe.\nopt.left.say.2 [0043f5] = 这篇论文吃掉了你一整个星期，没能获奖，但评委的点评赞不绝口。也许明年吧。\n# en: Skip it\nopt.right.label [9909cb] = 不参加\n# en: The winner reads their essay aloud at dinner. Yours would have been better. Probably. You had a very pleasant week instead.\nopt.right.say.1 [071ce6] = 晚饭时获奖者朗读了自己的论文。你的本来会更好。大概吧。而你度过了非常惬意的一个星期。\n\n@event life_charity\n# en: The Winter Fund\ntitle [04edf0] = 冬季基金\n# en: The village winter fund is collecting for families who can't afford fuel this winter. The jar is half full.\ntext [cf1ae3] = 小镇冬季基金正在为今冬买不起柴火的家庭募捐。募捐罐已经满了一半。\n# en: A collection jar shaped like a lantern on the dining hall table, half full of coins, a hand-lettered sign: FOR THE VILLAGE WINTER FUND.\nscene [cb02b0] = 餐厅桌上一只灯笼形状的募捐罐，半满的硬币，手写的牌子：小镇冬季基金。\n# en: Donate fifteen crowns\nopt.left.label [645481] = 捐十五克朗\n# en: 15 gold\nopt.left.hint [05537f] = 15 金币\n# en: Your name goes on a little list by the jar. You feel warm, which is the point.\nopt.left.say.1 [e202cc] = 你的名字被写进了罐子旁边的小名单。你觉得心里暖暖的，这正是意义所在。\n# en: Walk past\nopt.right.label [d5359c] = 走过去\n# en: {gold >= 15: You walk past. The jar fills anyway. You think about it on cold nights.|You have nothing to give this year. The jar fills anyway. You think about it on cold nights.}\nopt.right.say.1 [9ee81d] = {gold >= 15: 你走了过去。罐子还是被装满了。寒冷的夜里，你会想起这件事。|今年你什么也拿不出来。罐子还是被装满了。寒冷的夜里，你会想起这件事。}\n\n@event life_shady_offer\n# en: A Quiet Job\ntitle [c6d337] = 一份安静的差事\n# en: A hooded figure behind the tavern: \"Deliver this envelope to the boathouse tonight. Don't open it. Thirty crowns.\" The envelope is warm. Under the hood, a glint of pince-nez, and teeth: this is one of Grisk's errands.\ntext [8351fe] = 酒馆后巷里一个戴兜帽的人：“今晚把这封信送到船屋。别拆开。三十克朗。”信封是温的。兜帽底下闪过一副夹鼻眼镜，还有牙齿：这是格里斯克的差事。\n# en: A hooded figure in an alley behind the village tavern holding out a sealed envelope, a purse visible in their other hand.\nscene [7d377f] = 镇上酒馆后的小巷里，一个戴兜帽的身影递出一封封好的信，另一只手里露出钱袋。\n# en: Refuse\nopt.left.label [cada35] = 拒绝\n# en: The figure melts into the dark. The next morning, someone's boat is missing, and you wonder what was in the envelope, and whether you could have stopped it.\nopt.left.say.1 [b7d42e] = 那人融进了黑暗。第二天早上，有人的船不见了，你忍不住想那信封里装的是什么，你本来能不能拦住这件事。\n# en: Do the job\nopt.right.label [573204] = 接下这份差事\n# en: You make the drop. Bram is watching from the boathouse window and writes something down. On the jetty, a note in a neat hand: \"Good work. Midnight, any month. G.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [5ad49e] = 你送到了。布拉姆正从船屋的窗口看着，记下了什么。码头上有一张字迹工整的字条：“干得好。午夜，哪个月都行。G。”\n# en: You make the drop. Nobody sees. Thirty crowns, a cold feeling in your stomach, and a note on the jetty: \"Good work. Midnight, any month. G.\"\nopt.right.say.2 [9f54a8] = 你送到了。没人看见。三十克朗，胃里一点凉飕飕的感觉，还有码头上的一张字条：“干得好。午夜，哪个月都行。G。”\n\n@event life_food_poisoning\n# en: The Fish Was Off\ntitle [e4f066] = 鱼坏了\n# en: The fish pie at dinner was a mistake. Your stomach is staging a revolution.\ntext [cd4a4a] = 晚饭的鱼肉馅饼是个错误。你的胃正在闹革命。\n# en: A student curled on a dormitory bed clutching their stomach, a half-eaten fish pie on the bedside table.\nscene [4844ba] = 一个学生蜷缩在宿舍床上捂着肚子，床头柜上放着吃了一半的鱼肉馅饼。\n# en: Ride it out\nopt.left.label [43478c] = 硬扛过去\n# en: A miserable night. You never look at fish pie again.\nopt.left.say.1 [6ff02f] = 悲惨的一夜。你再也不看鱼肉馅饼一眼了。\n# en: Get a tonic from the infirmary\nopt.right.label [16a4f3] = 去医务室拿药\n# en: The tonic tastes like old pennies, but it works.\nopt.right.say.1 [f9e7fb] = 药尝起来像旧铜板，但是管用。\n\n@event life_self_doubt\n# en: Imposter\ntitle [ee3a4e] = 冒牌货\n# en: Everyone around you seems to belong here more than you do. They cast faster, learn quicker, laugh easier. Maybe the letter was a mistake.\ntext [5f044e] = 你身边的每个人似乎都比你更属于这里。他们施法更快、学得更快、笑得更自在。也许那封录取信是个错误。\n# en: A student sitting alone on the edge of a fountain at night, staring at their reflection broken up by ripples.\nscene [1842d0] = 夜里一个学生独自坐在喷泉边沿，盯着被涟漪打碎的倒影。\n# en: Write down everything you've learned\nopt.left.label [19e441] = 写下你学到的一切\n# en: The list is longer than you expected. Much longer. You pin it above your bed.\nopt.left.say.1 [c065df] = 清单比你想象的长。长得多。你把它钉在了床头。\n# en: Talk to someone about it\nopt.right.label [9fbd4a] = 找人聊聊\n# en: Your friend laughs — not at you. \"You? Everyone thinks you're the one who belongs.\" It helps more than you can say.\nopt.right.say.1 [eac20a] = 你的朋友笑了——不是在笑你。“你？大家都觉得你才是那个真正属于这里的人。”这比你能说出口的还要管用。\n# en: You talk to the night porter. He tells you everyone feels this way. You decide to believe him.\nopt.right.say.2 [bebf73] = 你和守夜门房聊了聊。他告诉你每个人都有这种感觉。你决定相信他。\n\n@event life_sleepwalking\n# en: Sleepwalking\ntitle [8fc4f7] = 梦游\n# en: You wake up on the battlements in your pyjamas, at the very edge, with no memory of how you got there. Your feet are wet with lake water.\ntext [8d1ce1] = 你醒来时穿着睡衣站在城垛上，就在最边缘，完全不记得自己是怎么上来的。你的脚被湖水打湿了。\n# en: A student in pyjamas standing on a moonlit battlement, eyes closed, one foot raised over the edge, an owl watching.\nscene [ee10e3] = 月光下一个穿睡衣的学生站在城垛上，双眼紧闭，一只脚悬在边缘外，一只猫头鹰在旁观。\n# en: Tell Sister Amaranth\nopt.left.label [3f4d80] = 告诉阿玛兰斯修女\n# en: A sleeping draught and a stern warning. It doesn't happen again.\nopt.left.say.1 [c0917f] = 一剂安眠药和一番严厉的警告。再也没发生过。\n# en: Keep it to yourself\nopt.right.label [09de43] = 自己藏着\n# en: It happens twice more. Each time you're facing the lake. Each time, you're a little closer to the water.\nopt.right.say.1 [bd1bd8] = 又发生了两次。每一次你都面朝湖水。每一次，你都离水更近一点。\n# en: It doesn't happen again. You start locking your door from the inside, just in case.\nopt.right.say.2 [3c9aa9] = 再也没发生过。你开始从里面反锁房门，以防万一。\n\n@event life_quiet_afternoon\n# en: A Quiet Afternoon\ntitle [4b76f8] = 安静的午后\n# en: For once, nothing is due, nobody needs you, and the sun is out. A whole afternoon, unclaimed.\ntext [135c6b] = 难得一次，没有作业要交，没有人需要你，太阳也出来了。一整个午后，无人认领。\n# en: A student lying in the long grass under an oak tree, a book open on their chest, clouds drifting above the towers.\nscene [9574c3] = 一个学生躺在橡树下的长草里，一本书摊在胸口，云朵飘过塔楼上空。\n# en: Nap under the oak tree\nopt.left.label [320d04] = 在橡树下打个盹\n# en: You wake to find a bird sitting on your book. It seems content. So are you.\nopt.left.say.1 [6fb673] = 你醒来时发现一只鸟落在你的书上。它看起来很满足。你也是。\n# en: Practise spells in the meadow\nopt.right.label [2c28fa] = 在草地上练习咒语\n# en: You make dandelion seeds dance in spirals for an hour. Nobody sees. It's perfect.\nopt.right.say.1 [2cbd71] = 你让蒲公英的种子跳了一个小时的螺旋舞。没人看见。完美。\n\n@event life_injury_stairs\n# en: The Trick Step\ntitle [cc11b3] = 陷阱台阶\n# en: The ninth step on the north stair is a trick step. Everyone knows. You forgot. Your leg sinks to the knee and the stair holds on.\ntext [9cc7ba] = 北楼梯的第九级是陷阱台阶。人人都知道。你忘了。你的腿陷到了膝盖，台阶紧紧咬住不放。\n# en: A stone staircase with one step glowing faintly, a student mid-fall, books flying everywhere.\nscene [02874d] = 一道石阶上有一级微微发光，一个学生正往下摔，书本飞得到处都是。\n# en: Wait for someone to help\nopt.left.label [8e8e6b] = 等人来帮忙\n# en: Forty minutes later, a first-year pulls you out. You're late for everything.\nopt.left.say.1 [be4901] = 四十分钟后，一个一年级生把你拔了出来。你什么都迟到了。\n# en: Wrench yourself free\nopt.right.label [ec2221] = 硬把腿拔出来\n# en: You pull free with a pop. Your shoe stays in the stair forever.\nopt.right.say.1 [652719] = 你“啵”的一声挣脱了。你的鞋永远留在了台阶里。\n# en: Something in your knee goes click. Not a good click.\nopt.right.say.2 [17703b] = 你膝盖里有什么东西咔哒响了一声。不是好的那种咔哒。\n\n@event life_lake_fever\n# en: Lake Fever Season\ntitle [5e2ca1] = 湖热病季节\n# en: Half your dormitory has Lake Fever. Noses are glowing blue. You feel a tickle in your throat.\ntext [77a4bc] = 你宿舍里一半的人得了湖热病。鼻子都泛着蓝光。你觉得喉咙有点痒。\n# en: A dormitory where half the beds hold sniffling students with faintly blue-glowing noses, tissues everywhere.\nscene [87b40c] = 宿舍里半数床位躺着吸鼻子的学生，鼻头泛着淡淡的蓝光，纸巾扔得到处都是。\n# en: Dose up on Maud's tea\nopt.left.label [f3b875] = 猛灌莫德的茶\n# en: Maud's lemon-and-thunder tea keeps you upright through the whole outbreak.\nopt.left.say.1 [b6b4d0] = 莫德那杯柠檬加雷声的茶让你挺过了整场流行病。\n# en: The tea from the kitchens isn't the same. Your nose starts glowing on Thursday.\nopt.left.say.2 [9b0859] = 厨房的茶不是一回事。周四你的鼻子开始发光了。\n# en: You dodge it. Somehow.\nopt.left.say.3 [61efce] = 你躲过去了。不知怎么做到的。\n# en: Keep going as normal\nopt.right.label [5303bd] = 照常生活\n# en: You keep going as normal for exactly two days.\nopt.right.say.1 [68c5c6] = 你照常生活了整整两天。\n# en: Iron constitution. You sail through. Everyone hates you a little.\nopt.right.say.2 [7e0fc1] = 铁打的体质。你安然度过。大家都有点恨你。\n\n@event life_first_snow\n# en: First Snow\ntitle [16a8b8] = 初雪\n# en: The first snow of winter falls overnight and the whole academy wakes up giddy. Classes are optional, officially. Unofficially, they're cancelled by snowball.\ntext [59861c] = 冬天的第一场雪在夜里落下，整个学院醒来时都兴奋不已。官方说法是课程自愿参加。非官方说法是课程被雪球取消了。\n# en: The academy courtyard blanketed in fresh snow, students mid-snowball fight, a snowman wearing a professor's hat.\nscene [c59afe] = 学院庭院铺满新雪，学生们正在打雪仗，一个雪人戴着教授的帽子。\n# en: Join the snowball war\nopt.left.label [dd3116] = 加入雪仗\n# en: You take a snowball to the face from the headmistress herself. Her aim is terrifying.\nopt.left.say.1 [286cf4] = 你脸上挨了一个雪球——是院长本人扔的。她的准头吓死人。\n# en: Your side wins. You build a snow-fort that lasts until Thawmoon.\nopt.left.say.2 [d4a0aa] = 你们这边赢了。你堆的雪堡一直立到了融雪月。\n# en: Hot chocolate by the fire\nopt.right.label [cba09c] = 在炉边喝热可可\n# en: You watch from the window, warm and smug, while the snowballs fly.\nopt.right.say.1 [da3e9c] = 你在窗边看着雪球横飞，暖和又得意。\n\n@event life_spring_cleaning\n# en: Spring Cleaning\ntitle [757d73] = 春季大扫除\n# en: Spring cleaning. The brooms are enchanted, but the enchantment needs supervising. Your dorm's brooms have formed a union.\ntext [cd3829] = 春季大扫除。扫帚都施了魔法，但魔法需要监督。你们宿舍的扫帚成立了工会。\n# en: A dormitory with every window thrown open, brooms sweeping by themselves, dust swirling in sunbeams.\nscene [d4aefd] = 宿舍里所有窗户都敞开着，扫帚自己扫着地，灰尘在阳光里打转。\n# en: Negotiate with the brooms\nopt.left.label [086b0c] = 和扫帚谈判\n# en: You agree to one day off a week and a polish. The dorm sparkles.\nopt.left.say.1 [627f3a] = 你同意每周给它们放一天假，外加一次抛光。宿舍焕然一新。\n# en: The brooms do not accept your terms. They sweep you into a cupboard.\nopt.left.say.2 [56e3e0] = 扫帚们不接受你的条件。它们把你扫进了柜子里。\n# en: Clean by hand\nopt.right.label [7a78f4] = 亲手打扫\n# en: An afternoon of scrubbing. It's oddly satisfying. You find three lost quills and a sandwich from Thawmoon.\nopt.right.say.1 [ffa004] = 擦洗了一个下午。出奇地让人满足。你找到了三支丢失的羽毛笔和一个融雪月的三明治。\n\n@event life_summer_heat\n# en: The Heat\ntitle [24ddd6] = 酷暑\n# en: The heat is unbearable. Even the gargoyles are sweating. The lake is crowded, the library stifling, the dormitories ovens.\ntext [b86aba] = 热得让人受不了。连石像鬼都在冒汗。湖边挤满了人，图书馆闷得透不过气，宿舍就是烤炉。\n# en: Students lying flat on the cool stone floor of a shaded colloquy, fanning themselves with exam papers.\nscene [ecf9e7] = 学生们平躺在阴凉讲堂冰凉的石地板上，用考卷扇着风。\n# en: Cast a cooling charm on your room\nopt.left.label [71928c] = 给房间施个降温咒\n# en: Your room becomes an icebox. People pay to visit. You let them for free. Mostly.\nopt.left.say.1 [4b7a2e] = 你的房间变成了冰窖。有人付钱来参观。你让他们免费进来。大部分人。\n# en: You make it colder. Briefly. Then hotter. Then it rains inside.\nopt.left.say.2 [fdb685] = 你让它变冷了。短暂地。然后变得更热了。然后屋里下起了雨。\n# en: Sleep on the roof\nopt.right.label [3e5cc0] = 睡在屋顶上\n# en: You sleep under the stars with half the dormitory. It's the best idea anyone's had all year.\nopt.right.say.1 [d71668] = 你和半个宿舍的人一起睡在星空下。这是今年所有人想出来的最好的主意。\n\n@event life_harvest_festival\n# en: The Harvest Feast\ntitle [d8f2a6] = 丰收宴\n# en: The Harvest Feast: pies the size of cartwheels, cider, and {&a pumpkin carved to look like the headmistress, which she pretends not to notice.|a pumpkin carved to look like Professor Thorne this year, which he pretends not to notice, badly.|a turnip carved to look like the headmistress, by popular demand. She is seen smiling at it.}\ntext [e6b42d] = 丰收宴：车轮那么大的馅饼，苹果酒，还有{&一个刻成院长模样的南瓜——她假装没注意到。|今年刻成索恩教授模样的南瓜——他假装没注意到，装得很差。|应大家要求，一个刻成院长模样的芜菁。有人看见她对着它微笑。}\n# en: Long tables groaning under pies and roast vegetables, candles floating above, a giant pumpkin carved like the headmistress.\nscene [749624] = 长桌上堆满了馅饼和烤蔬菜，蜡烛浮在上方，一个巨大的南瓜被刻成了院长的模样。\n# en: Eat until you can't move\nopt.left.label [36bf14] = 吃到动不了为止\n# en: {&You eat three helpings of everything. You're carried back to the dorm by friends. Worth it.|You pace yourself this year, and still lose to the pies. Worth it.|You win the cider-tasting by not falling over. Worth it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [186b8b] = {&每样东西你都吃了三份。朋友们把你抬回了宿舍。值了。|今年你控制了节奏，结果还是败给了馅饼。值了。|你靠着没摔倒赢了苹果酒品鉴。值了。}\n# en: Help in the kitchens for extra pay\nopt.right.label [9e76c8] = 去厨房帮忙赚外快\n# en: {&You serve pie to the entire academy. The cooks slip you coins and a whole pie of your own.|The cooks remember you and put you on gravy, a promotion. Coins, and a pie of your own.|You carve the pumpkin yourself this year. The cooks pay you double in pie and the usual in coins.}\nopt.right.say.1 [b6d05c] = {&你给全院的人端馅饼。厨师们悄悄塞给你一些硬币，还有一整个属于你的馅饼。|厨师们还记得你，让你负责浇肉汁——算是升职了。硬币，外加一个属于你的馅饼。|今年南瓜是你亲手刻的。厨师们付了你双份的馅饼和照常的硬币。}\n\n@event life_rival_academy\n# en: Visitors From Thornwick\ntitle [ac57b9] = 荆木来客\n# en: A delegation from Thornwick Academy is visiting. Their students are smug, polished and very loudly unimpressed with Lumenhold's plumbing. {met(tamsin): One of them is scanning the crowd with a letter in their hand, and you know the handwriting.|}\ntext [061f04] = 荆木学院的代表团来访。他们的学生傲慢、体面，而且非常大声地对流明堡的水管表示不屑。{met(tamsin): 其中一人手里捏着一封信，在人群里张望，你认得那笔迹。|}\n# en: A delegation of students in green robes marching through the gates, noses raised, a Lumenhold crowd watching suspiciously.\nscene [78c6a2] = 一队身穿绿袍的学生昂首阔步地走进校门，流明堡的人群怀疑地注视着他们。\n# en: Be a gracious host\nopt.left.label [071642] = 做个大方的东道主\n# en: Tamsin finds you before you find them, and is exactly like their letters, only faster. You show them the observatory. They admit, grudgingly, that it's better than theirs.\nopt.left.say.1 [d7a689] = 塔姆辛先找到了你，和信里一模一样，只是语速更快。你带 TA 参观了观星台。TA 不情不愿地承认，这比他们的好。\n# en: You show them the observatory. One of them admits, grudgingly, that it's better than theirs.\nopt.left.say.2 [a32053] = 你带他们参观了观星台。其中一人不情不愿地承认，这比他们的好。\n# en: Defend Lumenhold's honour\nopt.right.label [cfa83c] = 捍卫流明堡的荣誉\n# en: You challenge their best to a friendly duel and win. The Thornwick students are quieter at dinner.\nopt.right.say.1 [291905] = 你向他们最强的人发起友谊决斗，并且赢了。晚饭时荆木的学生安静多了。\n# en: You challenge their best to a friendly duel and lose, quite unfriendly. Thornwick will be insufferable.\nopt.right.say.2 [ad3104] = 你向他们最强的人发起友谊决斗，然后输得一点也不友谊。荆木的人会嚣张得让人受不了了。\n\n@event life_lost_firstyear\n# en: A Lost First-Year\ntitle [ed808e] = 迷路的一年级生\n# en: A tiny first-year is standing in the middle of the corridor, clutching an upside-down timetable and trying very hard not to cry.\ntext [a1cbbc] = 一个小个子一年级生站在走廊中间，攥着一张拿倒了的课表，拼命忍着不哭。\n# en: A tiny first-year in enormous robes standing in the middle of a junction of five identical corridors, clutching a timetable upside down.\nscene [6a5ef7] = 一个穿着宽大长袍的小个子一年级生站在五条一模一样的走廊交汇处，倒拿着课表。\n# en: Point the way and hurry on\nopt.left.label [ee0d43] = 指个路就赶紧走\n# en: You point. They go the wrong way anyway. You hope they're all right.\nopt.left.say.1 [f7d5b2] = 你指了路。对方还是走错了。你希望对方没事。\n# en: Walk them to class\nopt.right.label [e53c7b] = 送对方去教室\n# en: You walk them all the way and they talk the entire time. You're late, but they've decided you're their hero.\nopt.right.say.1 [344963] = 你一路把对方送到，对方一路说个不停。你迟到了，但对方已经认定你是自己的英雄。\n\n@event life_frog_escape\n# en: The Frogs Are Out\ntitle [abdd0d] = 青蛙跑出来了\n# en: Someone knocked over the frog crate in Potions. Thirty frogs are loose. One has climbed into the professor's hat. He has not noticed yet.\ntext [0c896d] = 有人在魔药课上打翻了青蛙箱。三十只青蛙跑了出来。一只爬进了教授的帽子里。他还没发现。\n# en: A potions classroom overrun by dozens of hopping green frogs, one sitting smugly on the professor's desk.\nscene [8a2633] = 魔药教室里到处是蹦跳的绿青蛙，一只得意地蹲在教授的讲台上。\n# en: Help round them up\nopt.left.label [d47151] = 帮忙把它们抓回来\n# en: You catch eleven, a personal best. The professor finds the hat frog at dinner. You were not involved.\nopt.left.say.1 [87225e] = 你抓了十一只，个人最佳纪录。教授在晚饭时发现了帽子里的那只。与你无关。\n# en: Quietly let a few escape out the window\nopt.right.label [73e9bc] = 悄悄放几只从窗户逃走\n# en: Five frogs hop to freedom in the lake. You feel like a liberator.\nopt.right.say.1 [076f02] = 五只青蛙跳进湖里重获自由。你觉得自己像个解放者。\n\n@event life_fortune_teller\n# en: The Village Fortune-Teller\ntitle [203728] = 镇上的算命婆\n# en: In the village, an old fortune-teller beckons you into her tent. \"Eight crowns for a good future, dearie. The bad ones I tell for free.\"\ntext [a3e3a4] = 在镇上，一个算命的老婆婆招手让你进她的帐篷。“八克朗算个好未来，孩子。坏的我免费说。”\n# en: A beaded doorway into a tiny dim tent, an old woman with a crystal ball and too many rings beckoning.\nscene [0e06ff] = 一顶昏暗小帐篷的珠帘门口，一个戴着太多戒指、捧着水晶球的老妇人在招手。\n# en: Decline politely\nopt.left.label [c5820d] = 礼貌地谢绝\n# en: \"Suit yourself,\" she calls after you. \"You will meet a tall stranger.\" You already have. Several. It's an academy.\nopt.left.say.1 [97b3f6] = “随你，”她在你身后喊，“你会遇到一个高个子陌生人。”你已经遇到了。好几个。这是所学院。\n# en: \"Beware of water,\" she calls after you, free of charge. She won't say more. You think about the lake a lot.\nopt.left.say.2 [96d8c7] = “小心水，”她在你身后喊，免费的。她不肯再多说。你常常想起那片湖。\n# en: Pay eight crowns\nopt.right.label [196642] = 付八克朗\n# en: Your future is wonderful. She's very specific. You are fairly sure she's making it up, and you don't care.\nopt.right.say.1 [7d6032] = 你的未来美好极了。她说得非常具体。你很确定她是在瞎编，而你不在乎。\n\n@event life_volunteer\n# en: Clean-Up Duty\ntitle [2e9a08] = 清理值日\n# en: After the storm, the grounds are a wreck. Volunteers needed to help clear branches and rebuild the hen-house. No pay. Lots of mud.\ntext [540f81] = 风暴过后，校园一片狼藉。需要志愿者帮忙清理断枝、重建鸡舍。没有报酬。满地泥巴。\n# en: Students in rolled-up sleeves raking leaves on the great lawn, a groundskeeper directing them with a pitchfork.\nscene [5de831] = 卷起袖子的学生们在大草坪上耙落叶，一位园丁拿着草叉指挥。\n# en: Volunteer\nopt.left.label [39a371] = 报名志愿\n# en: A long day of mud and laughter. An old woman in a straw hat brings everyone cocoa and remembers your name.\nopt.left.say.1 [e39352] = 漫长的一天，满身泥巴和笑声。一位戴草帽的老妇人给每个人送来了热可可，还记住了你的名字。\n# en: Stay in and study\nopt.right.label [6d8b57] = 留在屋里学习\n# en: You study through the shouting and hammering outside. The hen-house is very nice when it's done.\nopt.right.say.1 [1e9737] = 你在外面的喧闹和敲打声中学习。鸡舍建好后很漂亮。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/night.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/night.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event night_lead_in\n# en: An Unsigned Note\ntitle [f1e5f3] = 一张没有署名的字条\n# en: A note under your door, unsigned: \"The castle has a second timetable. It starts at midnight. Curfew is a suggestion made by people who go to bed early.\" In the corner, a map no bigger than a thumbnail, showing six doors that are open when they shouldn't be.\ntext [e2406e] = 门下塞着一张没有署名的字条：“城堡有第二张时间表，从午夜开始。宵禁只是早睡的人提的建议。”角落里画着一张不比拇指大的地图，标出六扇本不该开着的门。\n# en: A folded note slipped under a dormitory door at night, unsigned, a tiny silver map drawn in one corner.\nscene [c4d793] = 夜里塞进宿舍门缝的一张折好的字条，没有署名，角落里画着一张小小的银色地图。\n# en: Burn it\nopt.left.label [162285] = 烧掉它\n# en: You hold it to the candle. The map in the corner is the last thing to burn, and you could swear it rearranged itself first.\nopt.left.say.1 [23cef0] = 你把它凑到烛火上。角落的地图最后才烧着，你敢发誓它在那之前先自己重新排列了一番。\n# en: Keep it\nopt.right.label [87c2f5] = 留着它\n# en: You fold it into your book. Some nights you take it out and look at the six doors.\nopt.right.say.1 [6468db] = 你把它折起来夹进书里。有些夜晚，你会拿出来看看那六扇门。\n\n@event night_out\n# en: After Curfew\ntitle [0fdaf7] = 宵禁之后\n# en: {month == 12: Starfall is close, and the sky over the towers is already restless.|{month == 4 or month == 10: Exams soon. Half the windows on your corridor are still lit.|{season == \"winter\": {~Frost on the window, and the corridors are colder than the snow.|Snow on the sills; the tower creaks like a ship.|Ice in the water jug, and the tower silent with snow.}|{season == \"summer\": {~The night is warm and the castle's windows are all open.|Moths at the window, and somebody laughing on the lawn.|The night is short and never quite dark.}|{~The castle settles for the night, beam by beam.|Rain on the leads, and a gargoyle gargling it.|The wind finds every keyhole in the tower.}}}}} {has(watched): Bram does his rounds earlier these nights. You've learned the sound of his boots.|{health < 30: Every bone you own would like to stay in bed.|{mood < 25: You can't sleep anyway.|{gold < 10: Your purse is light, and some doors after midnight pay.|{bonds(partner) > 0: {~A note under your door, in a hand you know: \"Roof?\"|You could be on the observatory roof with someone right now.|Somewhere across the castle, someone you love is also awake.}|{final_year: {~Your last year. The castle at night won't be yours much longer.|The doors you know by heart will open for someone else next year.|Next year someone else will lie awake in this bed.}|{~Somewhere a door that should be locked isn't.|Down in the courtyard, a lantern goes out on purpose.|Someone has left the servants' stair unbolted again.}}}}}}}\ntext [5904cb] = {month == 12: 星坠节快到了，塔楼上方的天空已经躁动起来。|{month == 4 or month == 10: 快考试了。你们走廊上一半的窗户还亮着。|{season == \"winter\": {~窗上结了霜，走廊比雪还冷。|窗台上积着雪；塔楼像一艘船一样嘎吱作响。|水罐里结了冰，塔楼被雪捂得悄无声息。}|{season == \"summer\": {~夜很暖，城堡的窗户全开着。|飞蛾扑着窗户，有人在草坪上笑。|夜很短，始终不曾全黑。}|{~城堡一根梁接一根梁地沉入夜色。|雨打在屋顶的铅皮上，一只石像鬼在咕噜咕噜地漱口。|风钻进了塔楼里的每一个锁眼。}}}}}{has(watched): 这些夜里布拉姆巡夜更早了。你已经听熟了他的靴子声。|{health < 30: 你身上的每一根骨头都想待在床上。|{mood < 25: 反正你也睡不着。|{gold < 10: 你的钱袋很轻，而午夜之后，有些门是给钱的。|{bonds(partner) > 0: {~门缝底下塞进一张字条，字迹你认得：“屋顶？”|此刻你本可以和某个人待在观星台的屋顶上。|城堡另一头，你爱的人也还醒着。}|{final_year: {~这是你的最后一年。夜里的城堡很快就不再属于你了。|你烂熟于心的那些门，明年会为别人打开。|明年，会有别人在这张床上睁着眼睡不着。}|{~某处一扇本该锁着的门没有锁。|庭院里，一盏灯笼被人故意熄灭了。|又有人没闩上仆役楼梯的门。}}}}}}}\n# en: A dormitory window at midnight, the castle dark beyond it, one lantern moving along a far battlement.\nscene [0df004] = 午夜的宿舍窗户，窗外城堡一片漆黑，远处城垛上有一盏提灯在移动。\n# en: Sleep\nopt.left.label [a62ca8] = 睡觉\n# en: {~You blow out the candle and let the castle keep its secrets for one more night.|Sleep. The night can wait. It always does.|You pull the blanket up and listen to the castle until you're asleep.}\nopt.left.say.1 [5e979b] = {~你吹灭蜡烛，让城堡再守住一晚它的秘密。|睡吧。夜晚可以等。它向来会等。|你把毯子拉上来，听着城堡的声音，直到睡着。}\n# en: Go out\nopt.right.label [1d9fab] = 出去\n# en: {~You wait for the last footsteps to fade, then ease the door open.|Boots in hand, you take the servants' stair.|You slip out while the castle is still settling, and nobody hears.}\nopt.right.say.1 [c72d45] = {~你等最后的脚步声消失，然后轻轻推开门。|你拎着靴子，走仆人用的楼梯。|你趁城堡还在安顿下来时溜了出去，谁也没听见。}\n\n@event night_suspicion\n# en: A Word in the Morning\ntitle [42e6c8] = 早上的一句话\n# en: {has(watched): Bram is waiting at the foot of the stair, and this time he has a notebook open. \"Twice,\" he says. \"I did warn you.\"|Bram is waiting at the foot of the stair. \"Someone was out last night,\" he says, looking at nobody in particular, which is to say at you.}\ntext [04ce49] = {has(watched): 布拉姆等在楼梯脚下，这次手里摊着一本笔记本。“两次了，”他说，“我警告过你。”|布拉姆等在楼梯脚下。“昨晚有人出去了。”他说着，没看任何人——也就是说，看着你。}\n# en: A prefect waiting at the foot of a dormitory stair at breakfast time, arms folded, a small notebook open.\nscene [e8d3a9] = 早饭时分，一名级长抱着胳膊等在宿舍楼梯脚下，摊开着一本小笔记本。\n# en: Deny everything\nopt.left.label [b2b240] = 一口否认\n# en: Three months of detention and a door that is checked at midnight. Bram writes it all down, neatly.\nopt.left.say.1 [509dcb] = 三个月的禁闭，外加一扇午夜有人查的门。布拉姆把这一切工工整整地记了下来。\n# en: \"I'll be keeping an eye on you.\" He means it literally.\nopt.left.say.2 [772653] = “我会盯着你的。”他是字面意思。\n# en: Own up\nopt.right.label [3a0128] = 承认\n# en: Three months of detention and a door that is checked at midnight. Bram writes it all down, neatly.\nopt.right.say.1 [509dcb] = 三个月的禁闭，外加一扇午夜有人查的门。布拉姆把这一切工工整整地记了下来。\n# en: \"I'll be keeping an eye on you.\" He means it literally.\nopt.right.say.2 [772653] = “我会盯着你的。”他是字面意思。\n\n@event midnight_newt_cards\n# en: Upstairs at the Newt\ntitle [fe653a] = 镀金蝾螈的楼上\n# en: Upstairs at the Gilded Newt, after hours, the real game starts. The stakes are higher than anything downstairs, and nobody asks your name.\ntext [ef03fa] = 打烊之后，镀金蝾螈酒馆楼上，真正的牌局才开始。赌注比楼下任何一桌都大，也没人问你的名字。\n# en: A smoky upstairs room at a tavern after hours, a card table under one lamp, coins stacked high.\nscene [e9f7a8] = 打烊后酒馆楼上烟雾缭绕的房间，一盏灯下一张牌桌，硬币堆得老高。\n# en: Play a few hands\nopt.left.label [efba54] = 玩几把\n# en: {~You leave with more than you came with and a few new enemies who smile too much.|Luck, nerve and one well-timed sigh. The pot is yours.}\nopt.left.say.1 [6262fc] = {~你走的时候比来的时候多了钱，也多了几个笑得太多的新仇人。|运气、胆量，加上一声恰到好处的叹气。彩池归你了。}\n# en: {~The cards go cold, and so does your purse. On the stair down, a face from the castle turns away a little too fast.|You lose your stake and walk back in the dark, poorer, wiser and not sure you went unseen.}\nopt.left.say.2 [10d4e5] = {~牌运转冷，你的钱袋也跟着凉了。下楼时，一张城堡里的面孔转开得有点太快。|你输掉了赌本，摸黑走回去，钱少了，人精了，也说不准有没有被人看见。}\n# en: Watch, then leave\nopt.right.label [908b14] = 看一会儿就走\n# en: {~You watch one hand, see someone lose a familiar on a bluff, and leave before you're tempted.|You stay long enough to learn the rules, and leave before they learn you.}\nopt.right.say.1 [b1d011] = {~你看了一把，看见有人虚张声势输掉了自己的使魔，趁自己还没心动就走了。|你待到学会了规矩，又在他们认清你之前离开了。}\n\n@event midnight_boathouse_ring\n# en: The Ring Under the Boathouse\ntitle [31b605] = 船屋下的擂台\n# en: Under the boathouse, by lantern light, someone has chalked a ring on the flagstones. Purses go to whoever is still standing. \"Next,\" says the student holding the money, looking at you.\ntext [f5dc25] = 船屋底下，借着提灯的光，有人在石板上用粉笔画了个圈。钱袋归最后还站着的人。“下一个。”管钱的学生看着你说。\n# en: A cellar under a boathouse, lanterns hung from the beams, students in a ring around two duellists, coins changing hands.\nscene [faa793] = 船屋下的地窖，梁上挂着提灯，学生们围成一圈看两个决斗者，硬币在人手间传递。\n# en: Step into the ring\nopt.left.label [bf566a] = 走进圈里\n# en: {~You win the purse and the ring's respect, which is worth less and lasts longer.|Your opponent yields. Somebody whistles. The purse is heavier than you expected.}\nopt.left.say.1 [e69adc] = {~你赢了钱袋和擂台的尊重，后者不值钱，却更持久。|对手认输了。有人吹了声口哨。钱袋比你想的要沉。}\n# en: {~You lose, and limp home past a window where a light is still burning.|A bruising loss, and a lantern somewhere on the path home. Maybe it saw you; maybe not.}\nopt.left.say.2 [77e262] = {~你输了，一瘸一拐地回去，路过一扇还亮着灯的窗。|输得鼻青脸肿，回去的路上远处有一盏提灯。也许它看见了你，也许没有。}\n# en: Bet on someone else\nopt.right.label [c75252] = 押别人\n# en: {~Your fighter wins. You collect, quietly.|A good bet. You leave before anyone remembers your face.}\nopt.right.say.1 [663262] = {~你押的人赢了。你悄悄领了钱。|押得好。你在别人记住你的脸之前就走了。}\n# en: {~Your fighter goes down in the first exchange. So does your stake.|You picked badly. The bookmaker smiles.}\nopt.right.say.2 [fc67b4] = {~你押的人第一回合就倒下了。你的赌本也是。|你押错了。庄家笑了。}\n\n@event midnight_kitchen_raid\n# en: The Kitchens at Midnight\ntitle [174e5a] = 午夜的厨房\n# en: The refectory kitchens at midnight: banked fires, a larder that isn't locked, and the smell of tomorrow's bread already rising.\ntext [14230d] = 午夜的膳堂后厨：封好的炉火，一间没上锁的储藏室，明天的面包已经在发酵，香气飘了出来。\n# en: A dark castle kitchen at midnight, a student with a candle reaching into a larder, a cat watching from the hearth.\nscene [22b604] = 午夜昏暗的城堡厨房，一个学生举着蜡烛把手伸进储藏室，一只猫在炉边看着。\n# en: Fill your pockets\nopt.left.label [4d57c0] = 装满口袋\n# en: {~Bread, cheese and half a pie. Nobody counts the pies, you hope, and the scullery door creaks as you leave.|You eat by the banked fire with the kitchen cat. On your way out, a kitchen boy is up early. Did he see you?}\nopt.left.say.1 [e01feb] = {~面包、奶酪和半个馅饼。没人会数馅饼——但愿如此。你离开时，洗碗间的门吱呀响了一声。|你和厨房的猫一起在封好的炉火边吃东西。出门时，一个厨房小工起得很早。他看见你了吗？}\n# en: Just warm yourself by the fire\nopt.right.label [9fd3db] = 只在火边暖暖身子\n# en: {~You sit by the embers until your hands stop hurting, and take nothing. The walk back is cold.|The kitchen cat sits with you. You leave the larder alone, feel strangely proud of it, and shiver all the way back to bed.}\nopt.right.say.1 [ff8310] = {~你坐在余烬旁直到手不再疼，什么也没拿。回去的路很冷。|厨房的猫陪你坐着。你没碰储藏室，心里有种奇怪的自豪，一路哆嗦着回到床上。}\n\n@event midnight_restricted_stacks\n# en: The Restricted Stacks\ntitle [700d36] = 禁书区的书架\n# en: The Restricted Section at midnight. The chains on the shelves clink when nobody touches them. One book has been left open, as if for you.\ntext [a86ad6] = 午夜的禁书区。书架上的锁链在没人碰的时候叮当作响。有一本书摊开着，仿佛是为你留的。\n# en: A chained library section at midnight, a student reading by a stolen candle, shelves leaning in like listeners.\nscene [9d85ce] = 午夜被锁链锁住的藏书区，一个学生借着偷来的蜡烛读书，书架像听众一样倾身靠近。\n# en: Read the open book\nopt.left.label [5b587f] = 读那本摊开的书\n# en: {~Three pages before the book closes itself. You remember every word, which is worrying.|The book shows you a diagram you're not supposed to understand. You understand it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [133dfa] = {~读了三页，书就自己合上了。你记住了每一个字，这让人不安。|书给你看了一张你本不该看懂的图。你看懂了。}\n# en: {~The book slams shut and a lamp comes on two floors down. You're gone before it reaches the stair.|The pages scream, briefly. You leave quickly, and hope nobody heard.}\nopt.left.say.2 [4c4306] = {~书啪地合上，楼下两层亮起一盏灯。等它照到楼梯，你已经走了。|书页短促地尖叫了一声。你赶紧离开，但愿没人听见。}\n# en: Leave it open and go\nopt.right.label [a40ffc] = 让它开着，离开\n# en: {~You back away from the book. It seems disappointed.|You leave it open for whoever it was waiting for. It wasn't you, you decide.}\nopt.right.say.1 [43824e] = {~你从书前退开。它似乎很失望。|你让它摊着，留给它在等的人。你认定那不是你。}\n\n@event midnight_observatory_stars\n# en: Stars at Midnight\ntitle [aa78c1] = 午夜的星星\n# en: The observatory roof at midnight is the quietest place in the castle. {bonds(partner) > 0: You've brought someone.|The stars are very close tonight.} Nobody comes up here after curfew, mostly.\ntext [ef69f2] = 午夜的观星台屋顶是城堡里最安静的地方。{bonds(partner) > 0: 你带了个人来。|今晚星星离得很近。}宵禁之后，基本上没人上来。\n# en: The observatory roof at midnight, a student lying on the leads, the sky thick with stars.\nscene [1bfcc3] = 午夜的观星台屋顶，一个学生躺在铅皮屋面上，满天繁星。\n# en: Stay until the sky turns\nopt.left.label [6b603a] = 待到天色转亮\n# en: {~You count stars until you lose count, and then until the sky goes grey. You'll be tired. It was worth it.|A meteor, then another. You stay until the first bell.}\nopt.left.say.1 [8ea599] = {~你数星星数到数乱了，又一直数到天色发灰。你会很累。值得。|一颗流星，又一颗。你一直待到第一声钟响。}\n# en: An hour, then bed\nopt.right.label [d38b58] = 待一个钟头就睡\n# en: {~An hour of stars, and you're back in bed before anyone stirs, you think.|You slip back down the stair, the sky still in your eyes. A lantern turns a far corner, and you don't wait to see whose.}\nopt.right.say.1 [a31603] = {~看了一个钟头星星，你回到床上时还没人醒——你是这么想的。|你溜下楼梯，眼里还满是星空。远处拐角转过一盏提灯，你没等着看是谁的。}\n\n@event midnight_passage_mapping\n# en: The Passage After Dark\ntitle [8077de] = 入夜后的密道\n# en: Behind the third gargoyle the passage is busier at midnight than at noon. {met(corvin): Corvin's chalk marks are fresh.|Someone's chalk marks are fresh.} A draught comes from a direction that has no wall.\ntext [77c2e1] = 第三座石像鬼后面的密道，午夜比正午还热闹。{met(corvin): 科尔文的粉笔记号还是新的。|有人的粉笔记号还是新的。}一阵风从一个没有墙的方向吹来。\n# en: A secret passage behind a gargoyle at midnight, chalk marks on the walls, a candle guttering in a draught from nowhere.\nscene [e3c614] = 午夜石像鬼后的密道，墙上有粉笔记号，一支蜡烛在不知从哪来的风里摇曳。\n# en: Follow the draught\nopt.left.label [989dda] = 顺着风走\n# en: {~The draught leads you to a door you've never seen, and then, politely, closes it.|A new turning, a new stair. You chalk it for the map.}\nopt.left.say.1 [8bc3e0] = {~风把你领到一扇你从没见过的门前，然后礼貌地把它关上了。|一个新的拐角，一段新的楼梯。你用粉笔记下来，画进地图。}\n# en: {~After an hour of cold stone, the passage lets you out somewhere unexpected: the prefects' corridor, empty, you hope.|You get lost for an hour and come out in the wrong tower, cold and dusty, and not alone on the stair.}\nopt.left.say.2 [84c0e8] = {~在冰冷的石头间走了一个钟头后，密道把你放到了一个意想不到的地方：级长的走廊，但愿是空的。|你迷路了一个钟头，从错误的塔里钻出来，又冷又满身灰，楼梯上还不止你一个人。}\n# en: Mark it and go back\nopt.right.label [776869] = 做个记号就回去\n# en: {~You chalk an arrow at the turning and leave the rest for another night.|You mark it on your copy of the map and go back to bed, a little sorry.}\nopt.right.say.1 [c06aa7] = {~你在拐角处用粉笔画了个箭头，剩下的留给下一个夜晚。|你在自己那份地图上做了记号，回去睡觉，有点遗憾。}\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/origin_funding.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/origin_funding.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event noble_letter\n# en: A Letter from Home\ntitle [56bb53] = 家书\n# en: {month == 6: Before the spring term|Before the new year} a letter comes with your family's seal and a draft on the family bank. {flag(family_gift): {&Folded inside is a smaller note from your grandmother, and a little more money \"for something nice\".|There's an extra sum this time, marked \"a gift\", which in your family means it will be mentioned at dinner for a year.|Your father has added something on top, with a line about how proud he would be of better marks.}|{&The allowance, to the crown, and three pages about cousins who are doing very well.|The usual sum, and a reminder that the family name goes wherever you go.|The allowance, and a postscript asking whether you have met anyone suitable.}}\ntext [f5f6d8] = {month == 6: 春季学期开学前|新学年开始前}，一封盖着家族印章的信寄到了，里面夹着一张家族银行的汇票。{flag(family_gift): {&信里还折着一张祖母写的小纸条，外加一点钱，“买点好东西”。|这次多了一笔，标着“礼物”——在你们家，这意味着接下来一年的晚饭桌上都会被提起。|父亲在上面又添了一笔，附了一句：要是成绩再好些，他会多么骄傲。}|{&零花钱一个克朗不差，外加三页纸讲表亲们过得多么出色。|还是那个数目，外加一句提醒：家族的姓氏走到哪儿都跟着你。|零花钱，外加一句附言，问你有没有遇到合适的人。}}\n# en: A heavy envelope with a wax family seal on a writing desk, a bank draft half out of it.\nscene [72b255] = 书桌上一个厚重的信封，封着家族的蜡印，一张银行汇票露出一半。\n# en: Fold the letter away\nopt.ok.label [81ff5f] = 把信折好收起\n# en: {&You read it twice: once for the money, once for what it wants from you.|You put the draft in your purse and the letter in a drawer, and the letter is heavier.|You write back the same night: thank you, yes, of course, you'll try.}\nopt.ok.say.1 [704304] = {&你读了两遍：一遍为了钱，一遍为了信里对你的期望。|你把汇票放进钱袋，把信放进抽屉，而信要沉得多。|你当晚就回了信：谢谢，好的，当然，我会努力。}\n\n@event scholarship_award\n# en: The Bursary List\ntitle [a6c6d0] = 助学金名单\n# en: The bursary list {month == 6: for the spring term|for the new year} goes up outside the registrar's office. {term_gpa >= 3.5: {&Your name is near the top with a small star beside it: the full stipend, and a merit bonus.|The clerk hands you the envelope herself. The full stipend, and a little extra \"for distinction\".|Your name, the full sum, and the word \"Merit\" in red ink, which the clerk says she rarely gets to use.}|{term_gpa >= 3.0: {&Your name is there: the stipend is renewed for another term.|\"Renewed,\" says the list, in the clerk's tidy hand. You breathe out.|Your stipend stands for another term. The marks held, if only just.}|{term_gpa > 0: {&Your name is there with a line through the amount. Last term's marks fell below the 3.0 the Crown pays for, and the stipend waits until they come back up.|The clerk is apologetic: the Crown pays for marks of 3.0 and above, and last term yours were not. Next term, perhaps.|No stipend this term. Your marks dipped below what the Crown pays for, and the list says so in plain ink.}|{&Your name isn't on the list. No marks were recorded for you last term, and the Crown pays for marks.|The clerk checks twice. No grades last term means no stipend this one.|An empty line where your amount should be: the Crown pays for marks, and last term you had none.}}}}\ntext [2fe469] = {month == 6: 春季学期|新学年}的助学金名单贴在了教务处门外。{term_gpa >= 3.5: {&你的名字排在前头，旁边画着一颗小星：全额津贴，外加一笔奖学金。|教务员亲手把信封递给你。全额津贴，外加一点“以资嘉奖”。|你的名字、全额数目，还有用红墨水写的“优秀”二字——教务员说她难得用上这个词。}|{term_gpa >= 3.0: {&你的名字在上面：津贴续发一个学期。|名单上，教务员工整地写着“续发”。你松了口气。|你的津贴又保住了一个学期。成绩撑住了，虽然只是勉强。}|{term_gpa > 0: {&你的名字在上面，金额却被划掉了。上学期的成绩没达到王室资助所要求的 3.0，津贴要等成绩回来才会恢复。|教务员一脸歉意：王室只资助 3.0 及以上的成绩，而你上学期没有达到。下学期吧，也许。|这学期没有津贴。你的成绩掉到了王室资助的线下，名单上白纸黑字写着。}|{&名单上没有你的名字。上学期你没有任何成绩记录，而王室资助的是成绩。|教务员查了两遍。上学期没有成绩，这学期就没有津贴。|你的金额那一栏空着：王室资助的是成绩，而上学期你一门也没有。}}}}\n# en: A list of names pinned outside the registrar's office, a clerk's red ink beside some of them.\nscene [fa74fa] = 教务处门外钉着的一张名单，有些名字旁边写着教务员的红墨水字。\n# en: Read your line again\nopt.ok.label [7b4222] = 再读一遍你那一行\n# en: {term_gpa >= 3.0: {&You fold the envelope into your book and walk a little taller to breakfast.|Tuition, board and something left over. You allow yourself one good dinner.|You write home that the stipend held. Your family will read the letter aloud to the neighbours.}|{&You do the sums on the back of a lecture note, and they come out short. Work, then, or thinner meals.|You walk away from the list without looking back at it. Next term the marks will be better; they have to be.|You go straight to the library, which is free, and stay until it closes.}}\nopt.ok.say.1 [075262] = {term_gpa >= 3.0: {&你把信封夹进书里，走去吃早饭时腰板都挺直了些。|学费、膳宿，还能剩一点。你允许自己吃一顿好的。|你写信回家说津贴保住了。家里人会把信念给邻居们听。}|{&你在一张讲义背面算了算，钱不够。那就打工，或者吃得更省些。|你离开名单，没有回头看。下学期成绩会更好的；必须更好。|你径直去了图书馆——那里不要钱——一直待到闭馆。}}\n\n@event trading_report\n# en: The Company's Report\ntitle [79d431] = 商行的报告\n# en: {month == 6: The company's half-year report|The company's year-end report} arrives with a clerk's note on top. {flag(trade_good): {&The spice fleet came in early and whole. Your share is paid in full.|A good season: the northern warehouses sold out by midsummer. Your share comes with your mother's compliments.|Profit on every route but one. Your share is enclosed, and so is a list of what it cost.}|{&A ship lost off the southern coast, and a cargo of silk ruined by rain. There is no share this term.|The markets turned. The report is long and the envelope is empty: no share until trade recovers.|Pirates, tariffs and a warehouse fire, in that order. Your mother writes that the company cannot spare anything this term.}}\ntext [0265ca] = {month == 6: 商行的半年报告|商行的年终报告}寄到了，最上面是一位书记员的便条。{flag(trade_good): {&香料船队提早到港，完好无损。你的分红全额付清。|好年景：北方的货栈仲夏就卖空了。你的分红随信附上，还有母亲的问候。|除了一条航线，条条都赚了钱。你的分红附在里面，还有一张清单，写着这一切的代价。}|{&一艘船在南方海岸外沉没，一批丝绸被雨水泡坏。这学期没有分红。|行情变了。报告写得很长，信封却是空的：要等生意好转才有分红。|海盗、关税、一场货栈大火，依次而来。母亲写信说，商行这学期一分钱也挪不出来。}}\n# en: A merchant company's ledger report on thick paper, columns of figures, a bank draft clipped to the first page or not.\nscene [3a2c29] = 厚纸上的商行账目报告，一栏栏数字，第一页上别着一张银行汇票——或者没有。\n# en: Fold the report away\nopt.ok.label [452c3d] = 把报告折好收起\n# en: {flag(trade_good): {&You count it twice and lock it away. Next term's tuition is safe.|You read the figures like a story with a happy ending, and put the draft somewhere safe.|You think about what the good season cost someone else, and then about tuition, and put the draft away.}|{&You count what's in your purse instead. It will have to do.|You read the figures twice, as if they might change. They don't.|You write back that you understand, and then go and look at the job board.}}\nopt.ok.say.1 [8a9933] = {flag(trade_good): {&你数了两遍，锁了起来。下学期的学费有着落了。|你像读一个圆满结局的故事那样读那些数字，然后把汇票收好。|你想了想这个好年景让别人付出了什么，又想了想学费，然后把汇票收了起来。}|{&你转而数了数钱袋里的钱。只能将就了。|你把数字读了两遍，仿佛它们会变。它们没有。|你回信说你理解，然后去看了看招工告示板。}}\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/people_others.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/people_others.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event kael_snowball\n# en: Snow in Thawmoon\ntitle [1b8dde] = 融雪月里的雪\n# en: It's snowing in the courtyard. Only in a small circle, around Kael, who is holding a snowball and looking at it as though he's never been allowed to throw one.\ntext [540758] = 庭院里在下雪。只在一小圈范围里，围着凯尔下。他手里捧着一个雪球，盯着它看，好像从来没被允许扔过雪球。\n# en: A courtyard where it is snowing in one small circle only, a boy in a fur coat standing at its centre holding a perfect snowball.\nscene [8ebdb4] = 庭院里只有一小圈地方在下雪，一个穿毛皮大衣的少年站在中央，捧着一个完美的雪球。\n# en: Leave him to it\nopt.left.label [b84f2c] = 让他自己待着\n# en: When you pass again, the circle of snow is gone, and there's a small, lopsided snowman in its place.\nopt.left.say.1 [631d77] = 你再经过时，那圈雪已经不见了，原地立着一个歪歪扭扭的小雪人。\n# en: Throw the first one\nopt.right.label [c2945e] = 先扔出第一个\n# en: Your snowball hits him in the ear. He stares. Then he laughs — a real laugh, startled out of him — and the snow triples.\nopt.right.say.1 [5c45fb] = 你的雪球砸中了他的耳朵。他愣愣地看着你。然后他笑了——一声真正的、被吓出来的笑——雪下得大了三倍。\n\n@event kael_cold_hands\n# en: Cold Hands\ntitle [a4a2d1] = 冰冷的手\n# en: Kael's hands are always cold. In the library tonight they're so cold the ink in his pen has frozen. He's trying to thaw it with his breath, embarrassed.\ntext [2a76e5] = 凯尔的手总是冰的。今晚在图书馆里，冷到他笔里的墨水都冻住了。他正难为情地往笔上哈气，想把它化开。\n# en: Two students sitting close in a library alcove, one holding the other's frost-white hand between both of theirs.\nscene [d7ab82] = 图书馆的壁龛里，两个学生挨坐在一起，一人用双手捧着另一人霜白的手。\n# en: Lend him your gloves\nopt.left.label [7bd15d] = 把手套借给他\n# en: He wears them for the rest of the term. They're far too small. He doesn't care. Your own fingers go numb on the walk back.\nopt.left.say.1 [6436b9] = 他戴了一整个学期。手套小得离谱。他不在乎。回去的路上，你自己的手指冻得发麻。\n# en: Warm his hands in yours\nopt.right.label [e4839d] = 用你的手暖他的手\n# en: He goes very still. Then, slowly, the frost on his fingertips melts. \"Nobody's ever been warm enough,\" he says quietly. \"Before.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [79ec72] = 他一动不动。然后，慢慢地，他指尖的霜融化了。“从来没有人足够温暖，”他轻声说，“在你之前。”\n\n@event kael_confession\n# en: Thaw\ntitle [448405] = 解冻\n# en: \"I was taught not to want things,\" Kael says. \"Wanting things is how they control you.\" Frost blooms across the window behind him. \"I want you. I'm sorry. I don't know what to do with it.\"\ntext [6c0866] = “我被教导不要想要任何东西，”凯尔说，“想要东西，他们就能控制你。”他身后的窗户上绽开了霜花。“我想要你。对不起。我不知道该拿它怎么办。”\n# en: Frost-ferns spreading across a dark window behind a pale boy who is, for once, looking directly at someone.\nscene [992624] = 一个苍白的少年身后，黑暗的窗上蔓延着霜蕨；这一次，他直视着某个人。\n# en: Gently say no\nopt.left.label [f46b92] = 温柔地拒绝\n# en: He nods, as if he expected it. \"Thank you for being honest. Nobody is.\" The frost on the window fades slowly.\nopt.left.say.1 [b81eae] = 他点点头，像是早有预料。“谢谢你的诚实。没有人诚实过。”窗上的霜慢慢褪去了。\n# en: Show him what to do with it\nopt.right.label [6ebbe7] = 告诉他该怎么办\n# en: You kiss him. Every window in the corridor frosts over at once, and then, very slowly, melts.\nopt.right.say.1 [775fed] = 你吻了他。走廊里所有的窗户一下子都结满了霜，然后，非常非常缓慢地，融化了。\n\n@event bram_intro\n# en: Running in the Halls\ntitle [b6d158] = 走廊奔跑\n# en: \"Running in the halls.\" A burly prefect with a red sash steps into your path, ledger already open. \"Name?\" You weren't running. You were walking briskly.\ntext [db3db6] = “在走廊里奔跑。”一个系着红绶带的魁梧级长挡住你的去路，记过簿已经翻开了。“名字？”你没在跑。你只是走得比较快。\n# en: A burly prefect with a red sash blocking a corridor, a demerit ledger open in one hand and a quill poised.\nscene [de700d] = 一个系红绶带的魁梧级长堵在走廊里，一手翻开记过簿，羽毛笔蓄势待发。\n# en: Accept the demerit\nopt.left.label [758a3a] = 接受记过\n# en: He writes your name very slowly, enjoying it. Bram Hollis. You will learn to recognise his footsteps.\nopt.left.say.1 [67c2a7] = 他非常缓慢地写下你的名字，乐在其中。布拉姆·霍利斯。你会学会辨认他的脚步声的。\n# en: Argue\nopt.right.label [2f9da9] = 争辩\n# en: \"Walking briskly,\" you insist. Two demerits: one for running, one for \"lip\". Some students nearby clap quietly.\nopt.right.say.1 [4c2d1e] = “是快走。”你坚持道。记两次过：一次因为奔跑，一次因为“顶嘴”。附近几个学生悄悄鼓起了掌。\n\n@event bram_hall_tax\n# en: The Hall Tax\ntitle [86f712] = 走廊税\n# en: Bram has invented a \"hall tax\" for using the east staircase. Eight crowns. \"Maintenance,\" he says. The first-years are paying. He looks at you expectantly.\ntext [7f337c] = 布拉姆发明了一种使用东楼梯的“走廊税”。八克朗。“维护费。”他说。一年级生们都在交。他满怀期待地看着你。\n# en: A prefect leaning against a stone archway, palm out, a line of nervous first-years shuffling past him.\nscene [77c192] = 一名级长靠在石拱门上，摊着手掌，一排紧张的一年级生从他身边挪过。\n# en: Pay it\nopt.left.label [e4d5d6] = 交钱\n# en: He pockets the coins. \"Pleasure.\" You take the west staircase forever after.\nopt.left.say.1 [ed6293] = 他把硬币揣进兜里。“荣幸。”从此以后你永远走西楼梯。\n# en: Refuse loudly\nopt.right.label [c7392c] = 大声拒绝\n# en: The first-years stop paying too. Bram is furious. He'll find a way to make you pay later. He always does.\nopt.right.say.1 [9b89a4] = 一年级生们也不交了。布拉姆气坏了。他迟早会想办法让你付出代价。他总会的。\n# en: Your refusal spreads. Within a week, nobody pays the hall tax. Bram drops it, scowling.\nopt.right.say.2 [7da052] = 你的拒绝传开了。不出一周，再也没人交走廊税。布拉姆黑着脸取消了它。\n\n@event bram_sabotage\n# en: Missing Notes\ntitle [a08f2a] = 消失的笔记\n# en: Two days before exams, your revision notes vanish from your desk. All of them. You saw a red sash in the corridor.\ntext [bb41e4] = 考试前两天，你的复习笔记从桌上消失了。全部。你在走廊里看见了一抹红绶带。\n# en: An empty desk drawer, a few scattered pages on the floor, a red prefect's sash glimpsed disappearing around a corner.\nscene [a3c002] = 一只空荡荡的抽屉，地上散落着几页纸，一条红色的级长绶带消失在拐角。\n# en: Rewrite them from memory\nopt.left.label [429e64] = 凭记忆重写\n# en: You rewrite everything in one furious night. Your new notes are better than the old ones. Take that, Bram.\nopt.left.say.1 [2171a3] = 你愤怒地熬了一夜，把一切重写了一遍。新笔记比旧的还好。吃瘪吧，布拉姆。\n# en: You rewrite what you can. It isn't enough. Exams go worse than they should.\nopt.left.say.2 [0f1b73] = 你重写了能记住的部分。不够。考试考得比该有的差。\n# en: Search his room\nopt.right.label [8fc51c] = 搜他的房间\n# en: You find your notes under his mattress, and also a teddy bear. You take your notes and leave the bear exactly where it was, slightly straightened. He will know.\nopt.right.say.1 [0edcbd] = 你在他床垫下找到了你的笔记，还有一只泰迪熊。你拿回笔记，把熊原样放好，还替它摆正了一点。他会明白的。\n# en: He catches you in his room. That's two weeks of detention, and he gets to supervise.\nopt.right.say.2 [297e67] = 他在自己房间里逮住了你。两周禁闭，由他亲自监督。\n\n@event bram_crying\n# en: Behind the Stables\ntitle [321daa] = 马厩后面\n# en: Behind the stables, you find Bram Hollis sitting on a bucket, crying into a crumpled letter. He sees you and freezes. \"If you tell anyone,\" he begins, but his voice cracks.\ntext [b7fc13] = 在马厩后面，你发现布拉姆·霍利斯坐在一只水桶上，对着一封揉皱的信哭。他看见你，僵住了。“你要是敢告诉别人——”他开口，声音却哑了。\n# en: A big prefect sitting on an upturned bucket behind the stables, a crumpled letter in his fist, shoulders shaking.\nscene [a696df] = 一个高大的级长坐在马厩后倒扣的水桶上，拳头里攥着揉皱的信，肩膀在发抖。\n# en: Walk away and never mention it\nopt.left.label [6b7a64] = 走开，永远不提\n# en: You leave. You never tell anyone. Somehow, he knows you didn't. His demerits become fewer.\nopt.left.say.1 [27fbd6] = 你走开了。你从没告诉任何人。不知怎么的，他知道你没说。他给你记的过变少了。\n# en: Sit down and ask what happened\nopt.right.label [bf652a] = 坐下来，问他出了什么事\n# en: His father. A letter saying he's a disappointment, again. \"He was a prefect too,\" Bram says. \"Head boy. I'm just... loud.\" He wipes his face. \"Don't be nice to me. I don't know what to do with it.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [0fc8cf] = 是他父亲。又一封说他令人失望的信。“他以前也是级长，”布拉姆说，“学生会主席。我只是……嗓门大。”他擦了擦脸。“别对我好。我不知道该拿它怎么办。”\n\n@event bram_turns\n# en: An Unexpected Ally\ntitle [fb9811] = 意外的盟友\n# en: Two fourth-years corner you by the boathouse, looking for trouble. Then a red sash steps between you. \"Problem?\" says Bram, cracking his knuckles. They leave fast.\ntext [8efcfc] = 两个四年级生在船屋边把你堵住，想找茬。这时一条红绶带插到了你们中间。“有问题吗？”布拉姆捏着指关节说。那两人很快就走了。\n# en: A prefect stepping between a cornered student and a pair of older bullies, arms crossed.\nscene [b5e93a] = 一名级长抱着双臂挡在被逼到角落的学生和两个高年级恶霸之间。\n# en: Thank him awkwardly\nopt.left.label [a37131] = 别扭地道谢\n# en: \"Don't make it weird,\" he says, and walks off. He's smiling, slightly.\nopt.left.say.1 [bd632c] = “别搞得这么肉麻。”他说完就走了。他在笑，有一点。\n# en: Offer your hand\nopt.right.label [1f34dd] = 伸出手\n# en: He stares at your hand. Then he shakes it, hard. \"Fine. Friends. But I'm still giving you demerits.\" He does. Fewer.\nopt.right.say.1 [5c04b4] = 他盯着你的手。然后用力握住。“行吧。朋友。但我照样给你记过。”他照样记。少了些。\n\n@event bram_duel\n# en: A Formal Challenge\ntitle [0903a6] = 正式挑战\n# en: Bram throws a glove at your feet in front of half the dormitory. \"Formal challenge. The dueling green, right now. Unless you're a coward.\" Everyone is watching.\ntext [28f81c] = 布拉姆当着半个宿舍的人把一只手套扔在你脚下。“正式挑战。决斗草坪，现在就去。除非你是个懦夫。”所有人都在看。\n# en: A white glove lying on a dormitory floor, a red sash folded beside it, a crowd peering through the doorway.\nscene [c0de53] = 宿舍地板上一只白手套，旁边叠着一条红绶带，门口挤满了张望的人。\n# en: Decline\nopt.left.label [eaef39] = 拒绝\n# en: \"Coward,\" he says, delighted. It sticks for a while. Then people forget. Mostly.\nopt.left.say.1 [821d12] = “懦夫。”他兴高采烈地说。这个外号跟了你一阵子。后来大家就忘了。大体上。\n# en: Accept\nopt.right.label [b26149] = 接受\n# en: You disarm him in three moves. The crowd goes wild. Bram, on the grass, starts laughing. \"Fine,\" he says. \"Fine.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [5700d2] = 你三招就缴了他的械。人群沸腾了。躺在草地上的布拉姆笑了起来。“行，”他说，“行。”\n# en: He wins. But it takes him a full ten minutes, and he's limping too. He nods to you after. Grudging respect.\nopt.right.say.2 [a5c4a7] = 他赢了。但足足花了十分钟，而且他也一瘸一拐的。事后他朝你点了点头。不情不愿的尊重。\n\n@event thorne_intro\n# en: The First Question\ntitle [7b3e5e] = 第一个问题\n# en: First Evocation lecture. Professor Thorne surveys the room like a hawk over a field of mice. His finger lands on you. \"You. What is the first law of the Arcane?\"\ntext [94bd6c] = 第一堂塑能术课。索恩教授扫视教室，像一只盯着田鼠的鹰。他的手指落在你身上。“你。奥术第一定律是什么？”\n# en: A steep lecture theatre, a stern grey-bearded professor pointing a long finger at one student in the middle row.\nscene [662f2a] = 陡峭的阶梯讲堂，一位严厉的灰胡子教授用修长的手指指着中间一排的一个学生。\n# en: Admit you don't know\nopt.left.label [a10b0e] = 承认你不知道\n# en: \"Correct answer,\" he says. \"The first law is: know what you don't know. The rest of you, write that down.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [7cfbd1] = “正确答案，”他说，“第一定律是：知道自己不知道什么。其余的人，记下来。”\n# en: Take a guess\nopt.right.label [b3c370] = 猜一个\n# en: \"Energy follows intention.\" His eyebrows twitch. \"Acceptable.\" The room exhales.\nopt.right.say.1 [a4daea] = “能量追随意念。”他的眉毛抽动了一下。“可以接受。”全班松了口气。\n# en: Your guess is so wrong the student next to you gasps. Thorne simply says, \"No,\" and moves on.\nopt.right.say.2 [0a5012] = 你猜得错得离谱，旁边的同学倒吸一口冷气。索恩只说了一句“不对”，就继续讲下去了。\n\n@event thorne_office_hours\n# en: Office Hours\ntitle [2f554a] = 答疑时间\n# en: Thorne stops you after class. \"Your casting is sloppy and your instincts are good. That's an annoying combination. Thursdays. Seven o'clock. My office. Don't be late.\"\ntext [e4245b] = 下课后索恩叫住了你。“你的施法很马虎，直觉却很好。这是个恼人的组合。周四。七点。我的办公室。别迟到。”\n# en: A cramped office full of scorched books and a single lit candle, a stern professor leaning back in his chair, fingers steepled.\nscene [78fbf0] = 一间堆满烧焦书本、只点着一支蜡烛的狭小办公室，严厉的教授靠在椅背上，十指相抵。\n# en: Politely decline — you're stretched thin\nopt.left.label [d714b1] = 礼貌地拒绝——你已经分身乏术\n# en: \"Hm.\" He seems neither surprised nor pleased. Thorne is rarely either.\nopt.left.say.1 [c8eaad] = “嗯。”他看起来既不意外，也不高兴。索恩很少有这两种情绪。\n# en: Be there at seven\nopt.right.label [5fe202] = 七点准时到\n# en: Two hours of brutal drills. At the end, he hands you a book from his own shelf. \"Read it. Return it unburnt.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [80a8e2] = 两个小时残酷的操练。结束时，他从自己的书架上抽出一本书递给你。“读完。还的时候别烧着。”\n\n@event thorne_storm_story\n# en: The Storm Duel\ntitle [c9a5ed] = 风暴决斗\n# en: During a thunderstorm, Thorne stops mid-lecture and watches the lightning. \"They say I out-dueled a storm once,\" he says, to nobody in particular. \"Would you like to know what actually happened?\"\ntext [1d58d7] = 雷雨中，索恩讲到一半停了下来，望着窗外的闪电。“他们说我曾经在决斗中赢过一场风暴，”他说，不知是对谁说的，“想知道真正发生了什么吗？”\n# en: An old professor standing at a rain-lashed window, lightning illuminating a long scar along his jaw.\nscene [3aa02a] = 一位年迈的教授站在被雨水抽打的窗前，闪电照亮了他下颌上一道长长的伤疤。\n# en: \"Yes, sir.\"\nopt.left.label [ad622a] = “想，教授。”\n# en: He lost, he says. Badly. He spent a year learning why. \"Losing well,\" he says, \"is the only thing worth teaching.\" The class is silent. The lecture never resumes.\nopt.left.say.1 [d85954] = 他说，他输了。输得很惨。他花了一年才弄明白为什么。“输得漂亮，”他说，“是唯一值得教的东西。”全班鸦雀无声。那堂课再也没讲下去。\n# en: Stay quiet\nopt.right.label [9adbd8] = 保持沉默\n# en: He shakes his head and returns to the lecture, which is a good one. You'll always wonder.\nopt.right.say.1 [01c1de] = 他摇摇头，继续讲课，讲得很好。你会一直好奇下去。\n\n@event thorne_recommendation\n# en: A Letter of Recommendation\ntitle [c8fd60] = 推荐信\n# en: Thorne calls you to his office and slides an envelope across the desk. \"The Conclave asked for my recommendations. I wrote one. I don't write many.\" He pauses. \"Don't make me regret it.\"\ntext [126063] = 索恩把你叫到办公室，把一个信封推过桌面。“议会请我写推荐信。我写了一封。我很少写。”他顿了顿，“别让我后悔。”\n# en: A wax-sealed envelope on a professor's desk with a student's name written on it in sharp, angular handwriting.\nscene [96717b] = 教授桌上一个封着火漆的信封，上面用锋利棱角的字迹写着一个学生的名字。\n# en: Thank him sincerely\nopt.left.label [028201] = 真诚地道谢\n# en: \"Go away,\" he says gruffly. \"I have marking.\" You see him smile at the window as you leave.\nopt.left.say.1 [91553f] = “走吧，”他粗声说，“我还要批作业。”你离开时，看见他对着窗户笑了。\n# en: Ask why you\nopt.right.label [ca1833] = 问为什么是你\n# en: \"Because you ask questions like that,\" he says. \"Most students don't. Go.\" You leave unsure whether you were praised or dismissed.\nopt.right.say.1 [52bd7f] = “因为你会问这种问题，”他说，“大多数学生不会。走吧。”你离开时也说不清自己是被夸了还是被打发了。\n\n@event thorne_disappointed\n# en: A Word in Private\ntitle [ff3088] = 私下谈谈\n# en: Thorne holds up your last three Evocation essays. All red. \"You're better than this,\" he says. \"That's not a compliment. It's an accusation.\"\ntext [c9cdc7] = 索恩举起你最近的三篇塑能术论文。全是红的。“你不止这个水平，”他说，“这不是夸奖。是指控。”\n# en: A professor standing by his desk with a sheaf of red-marked papers, a student standing awkwardly before him.\nscene [9d745e] = 一位教授站在桌旁，手持一沓满是红批的论文，一个学生尴尬地站在他面前。\n# en: Make excuses\nopt.left.label [271029] = 找借口\n# en: He listens to them all and says nothing. The silence is the worst grade yet.\nopt.left.say.1 [f08449] = 他听完了你所有的借口，一言不发。这份沉默是你得过最差的分数。\n# en: Ask for help\nopt.right.label [e11804] = 请求帮助\n# en: He sighs and pulls out a chair. \"Sit. We'll start with the first essay, and we'll stay until it's right.\" You stay until midnight.\nopt.right.say.1 [0a15d2] = 他叹了口气，拉出一把椅子。“坐。我们从第一篇开始，改到对为止。”你们一直改到半夜。\n\n@event maud_chickens\n# en: The Great Chicken Escape\ntitle [c0698d] = 鸡群大逃亡\n# en: Forty chickens are loose on the main lawn, pursued by an old woman in a straw hat. \"Don't just stand there, dearie!\" she shouts at you. \"Grab the speckled one, she's the ringleader!\"\ntext [c7632d] = 四十只鸡在大草坪上四处逃窜，一位戴草帽的老妇人在后面追。“别光站着呀，孩子！”她冲你喊，“抓那只带斑点的，她是头儿！”\n# en: Chickens fleeing across a lawn in every direction, an old woman in a straw hat waving a broom, students diving after birds.\nscene [f345a3] = 鸡群在草坪上四散奔逃，戴草帽的老妇人挥着扫帚，学生们扑向鸡群。\n# en: Keep walking\nopt.left.label [6ca967] = 继续走你的路\n# en: You hear a crash, a squawk and a triumphant cackle behind you. The chickens, it seems, are caught.\nopt.left.say.1 [62bfac] = 你听见身后一声巨响、一声鸡叫和一阵得意的笑。看来鸡都被抓住了。\n# en: Grab the speckled one\nopt.right.label [5c1b61] = 抓住那只带斑点的\n# en: You tackle the ringleader. The rest give up. \"Good hands,\" says Old Maud, pressing a warm egg into your palm. \"Come for tea.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [a339b6] = 你扑倒了头儿。其余的都放弃了。“好身手。”老莫德说着把一枚温热的鸡蛋塞进你手心，“来喝茶吧。”\n# en: The ringleader escapes and pecks you, but Maud is impressed by your enthusiasm. \"Come for tea. Bring plasters.\"\nopt.right.say.2 [bfdfbf] = 头儿逃走了还啄了你一口，但莫德很欣赏你的热情。“来喝茶吧。带上创可贴。”\n\n@event maud_honey\n# en: Honey for a Cold\ntitle [b7cd42] = 治感冒的蜂蜜\n# en: Maud spots you sniffling across the lawn and drags you into her cottage. \"Sit,\" she orders, pouring honey into something steaming that smells of lemon and thunder.\ntext [7490d5] = 莫德隔着草坪看见你在吸鼻子，把你拽进了她的小屋。“坐下。”她命令道，把蜂蜜倒进一杯冒着热气、闻起来像柠檬和雷声的东西里。\n# en: A small cottage kitchen with bundles of herbs hanging from the beams, an old woman pouring golden honey into a steaming mug.\nscene [eeaeed] = 小屋厨房的房梁上挂着一捆捆草药，老妇人正把金色的蜂蜜倒进一只冒热气的杯子。\n# en: Drink it all\nopt.left.label [06ea01] = 一饮而尽\n# en: It tastes like summer and lightning. You sneeze sparks for the rest of the day. By morning you're cured and slightly glowing.\nopt.left.say.1 [2d602b] = 它尝起来像夏天和闪电。你一整天都在打喷嚏，喷出小火星。第二天早上你就好了，身上还微微发光。\n# en: Drink it and help with chores\nopt.right.label [cbf3a4] = 边喝边帮她干活\n# en: You sip and sort herbs while she tells you the names of every plant in the room. You remember most of them.\nopt.right.say.1 [d8a82c] = 你一边小口喝着，一边分拣草药，她把屋里每一株植物的名字都告诉了你。你记住了大半。\n\n@event maud_old_student\n# en: Maud Was a Student Once\ntitle [bad37c] = 莫德曾经也是学生\n# en: \"I was a student here, you know,\" Maud says, watching the lake. \"Sixty years ago. The lake froze one spring. Three of my friends went down to the boathouse that night. Only two came back.\" She doesn't look at you. \"Ask me what I know.\"\ntext [eef642] = “你知道吗，我以前也是这里的学生，”莫德望着湖面说，“六十年前。有一年春天，湖结冰了。那天夜里，我的三个朋友下到了船屋。只回来了两个。”她没有看你。“问我知道些什么吧。”\n# en: An old woman sitting on a bench by the lake at dusk, looking at the water as if it might look back.\nscene [89e70b] = 黄昏时分，一位老妇人坐在湖边长椅上，望着湖水，仿佛湖水也会回望她。\n# en: \"You don't have to tell me.\"\nopt.left.label [194cf6] = “您不必告诉我的。”\n# en: She pats your hand. \"Kind. But time's short, dearie.\" She tells you anyway, a little at a time, over many cups of tea.\nopt.left.say.1 [9b1d22] = 她拍了拍你的手。“好孩子。可时间不多了。”她还是告诉了你，一点一点地，就着许多杯茶。\n# en: \"What do you know?\"\nopt.right.label [099f17] = “您知道些什么？”\n# en: \"The founders built this place as a lid. Seven towers, and the key kept in the one nobody climbs.\" She finally looks at you. \"The thing below loves a lonely heart. Don't be lonely.\" It's a long walk back in the dark.\nopt.right.say.1 [bcd702] = “创始者把这地方建成了一个盖子。七座塔，钥匙藏在那座没人爬的塔里。”她终于看向你。“下面那个东西，最爱孤独的心。别孤单。”回去的路在黑暗里显得很长。\n\n@event maud_bees\n# en: The Silver Bees\ntitle [1eab66] = 银蜂\n# en: \"The silver bees need moving to the summer meadow,\" Maud says, handing you a veiled hat. \"They're friendly. Mostly. Don't make any sudden thoughts.\"\ntext [30a86a] = “银蜂要搬去夏季草场了，”莫德说着递给你一顶带面纱的帽子，“它们很友好。大体上。别突然冒出什么念头就行。”\n# en: Wooden hives in a wildflower meadow, an old woman in a veiled hat lifting a frame dripping with silver honey, bees glinting in the sun.\nscene [53eac5] = 野花草场上的木蜂箱，一位戴面纱帽的老妇人举起一框滴着银色蜂蜜的巢脾，蜜蜂在阳光下闪闪发亮。\n# en: Help with the hives\nopt.left.label [05ba5e] = 帮忙搬蜂箱\n# en: The bees hum around you like a blessing. Maud gives you a jar of silver honey that sells for eight crowns. You eat half first.\nopt.left.say.1 [6e9b07] = 蜜蜂围着你嗡嗡作响，像一种祝福。莫德给了你一罐能卖八克朗的银蜜。你先吃了一半。\n# en: You have a sudden thought. The bees know. You spend the evening with vinegar on your stings.\nopt.left.say.2 [8b74be] = 你突然冒出了一个念头。蜜蜂知道了。你整晚都在往蜇伤上抹醋。\n# en: Watch from a safe distance\nopt.right.label [5c0ef7] = 在安全距离外观看\n# en: You watch Maud move hives barehanded, singing to them. It's the calmest you've felt all month.\nopt.right.say.1 [fca1ee] = 你看着莫德赤手搬动蜂箱，一边对它们唱歌。这是你这个月最平静的时刻。\n\n@event maud_tea_tells\n# en: Over Many Cups of Tea\ntitle [dc0f5a] = 一杯又一杯茶\n# en: Maud keeps her word, a little at a time. Over a month of afternoons and more tea than is good for anyone, the story of that spring comes out in pieces: the boathouse, the cold, the friend who didn't come back.\ntext [062301] = 莫德说话算话，一点一点地讲。一个月的下午，加上多得对谁都没好处的茶，那年春天的故事一片片拼了出来：船屋、寒冷，还有那个没能回来的朋友。\n# en: A cluttered cottage kitchen at dusk, two teacups and a pot between an old woman and a student, the lake grey in the window.\nscene [906453] = 黄昏时杂乱的农舍厨房，老妇人和学生之间摆着两只茶杯和一把茶壶，窗外的湖一片灰色。\n# en: Listen to the end\nopt.left.label [ba4aa9] = 听她讲完\n# en: \"The founders built this place as a lid,\" she says on the last afternoon. \"Seven towers, and the key kept in the one nobody climbs. The thing below loves a lonely heart. Don't be lonely.\" She pours another cup.\nopt.left.say.1 [16e283] = “创始者把这地方建成了一个盖子，”最后一个下午她说，“七座塔，钥匙藏在没人爬的那一座里。下面那东西喜欢孤独的心。别孤独。”她又倒了一杯。\n# en: Tell her it can wait\nopt.right.label [ada58d] = 告诉她不急\n# en: \"It can't, dearie,\" she says, \"but I like you for saying so.\" She tells you about her chickens instead. You'll come back for the rest.\nopt.right.say.1 [f1347f] = “急的，亲爱的，”她说，“不过你这么说，我很喜欢。”她改讲起了她的鸡。剩下的，你还会回来听。\n\n@event maud_ill\n# en: Maud Is Unwell\ntitle [1570b9] = 莫德病了\n# en: Maud's chickens are running wild, unfed. You find her in bed, grey-faced and coughing. \"Just a chill,\" she wheezes. \"I'm eighty-one. I'm allowed.\"\ntext [d16f4b] = 莫德的鸡没人喂，满地乱跑。你发现她躺在床上，脸色灰白，咳个不停。“只是着了点凉，”她喘着气说，“我八十一了。有这个资格。”\n# en: A small cottage bedroom with a patchwork quilt, an old woman propped up on pillows, a student reading aloud by candlelight.\nscene [a2af84] = 小屋卧室里铺着拼布被，一位老妇人靠在枕头上，一个学生在烛光下为她读报。\n# en: Fetch Sister Amaranth\nopt.left.label [0ebdc9] = 去叫阿玛兰斯修女\n# en: Amaranth brings tonics and a stern lecture. Maud is back on her feet in a week, grumbling about fuss.\nopt.left.say.1 [79a7ad] = 阿玛兰斯带来了药剂和一通严厉的训话。一周后莫德就下了床，嘟囔着大家小题大做。\n# en: Look after her yourself for a week\nopt.right.label [c0447c] = 亲自照顾她一周\n# en: You feed chickens, brew teas, read her the newspaper. When she's well, she gives you a sprig of rosemary. \"You'll do,\" she says. \"You'll do.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [4fecac] = 你喂鸡、煮茶、给她读报纸。她康复后，给了你一枝迷迭香。“你行，”她说，“你行的。”\n\n@event isolde_intro\n# en: She Knows Your Name\ntitle [9a7a2b] = 她知道你的名字\n# en: At dinner, a cool hand rests on the back of your chair. The headmistress. \"{player},\" she says, though you've never met. \"Your marks in Arcana are interesting. Your marks in punctuality, less so.\" She moves on.\ntext [0b5a32] = 晚餐时，一只微凉的手搭在你的椅背上。是院长。“{player}，”她说，尽管你们从未见过，“你的奥术成绩很有意思。你的守时成绩，就没那么有意思了。”她继续往前走了。\n# en: A tall pale woman in dark robes pausing behind a student's chair in a candlelit dining hall, a hand resting lightly on the chair back.\nscene [b2fa1a] = 烛光摇曳的餐厅里，一位身着深色长袍的高挑苍白女子在一名学生的椅子后驻足，一只手轻轻搭在椅背上。\n# en: Stammer an apology\nopt.left.label [d97a4c] = 结结巴巴地道歉\n# en: She's already three tables away. Every student between you sits a little straighter.\nopt.left.say.1 [e1b364] = 她已经走出三张桌子远了。你们之间的每个学生都坐直了一点。\n# en: \"Interesting how, Headmistress?\"\nopt.right.label [836bca] = “怎么个有意思法，院长？”\n# en: She pauses. Turns. Almost smiles. \"Ask me again at the end of your first year,\" she says. The table stares at you.\nopt.right.say.1 [912689] = 她停下脚步。转身。几乎笑了。“等你一年级结束时再问我一次。”她说。整桌人都盯着你看。\n\n@event isolde_tea\n# en: Tea in the Tower\ntitle [b7137e] = 塔楼下午茶\n# en: An invitation to tea with the headmistress. Students talk about these the way soldiers talk about battles. She pours. \"Tell me, {player}: what do you want to become?\"\ntext [9726d0] = 一份与院长共进下午茶的邀请。学生们谈论这种事，就像士兵谈论战役。她倒着茶。“告诉我，{player}：你想成为什么样的人？”\n# en: A round tower study at twilight, two delicate teacups on a small table, a headmistress pouring tea with a perfectly steady hand.\nscene [71ceab] = 暮色中的圆形塔楼书房，小桌上两只精致的茶杯，院长倒茶的手稳得纹丝不动。\n# en: Give an ambitious answer\nopt.left.label [aa3b70] = 给一个雄心勃勃的回答\n# en: She listens without interrupting, then says, \"Good. Ambition without arrogance. Rarer than you'd think.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [7c5144] = 她一句也没打断地听完，然后说：“很好。有雄心而不傲慢。比你想象的要少见。”\n# en: She nods politely. \"Ambition is a fine servant,\" she says, \"and a poor master.\" You're dismissed shortly after.\nopt.left.say.2 [7dba0b] = 她礼貌地点点头。“雄心是个好仆人，”她说，“却是个坏主人。”没过多久你就被请走了。\n# en: Admit you don't know yet\nopt.right.label [0c2530] = 承认你还不知道\n# en: \"Neither did I, at your age,\" she says. \"I still don't, some days. That's the point of an academy.\" She refills your cup.\nopt.right.say.1 [80f029] = “我在你这个年纪时也不知道，”她说，“有些日子我现在也不知道。这正是学院存在的意义。”她为你续上了茶。\n\n@event isolde_warning\n# en: A Quiet Warning\ntitle [a5c708] = 一句轻声的警告\n# en: The headmistress falls into step beside you in the colonnade. \"I hear your name more often than I'd like,\" she says pleasantly. \"In the wrong rooms. I'd hate to hear it in mine.\"\ntext [01291a] = 院长在柱廊里走到你身边，与你并肩而行。“我听到你名字的次数比我希望的要多，”她和气地说，“在不该听到的房间里。我可不想在我的房间里听到它。”\n# en: A headmistress walking alongside a student in a long colonnade, hands clasped behind her back, speaking without looking at them.\nscene [052fbe] = 长长的柱廊里，院长背着手与一名学生并肩而行，说话时并不看对方。\n# en: Promise to do better\nopt.left.label [cba502] = 保证会改\n# en: \"Good.\" She stops walking. You keep going. When you look back, she's gone.\nopt.left.say.1 [b48ece] = “很好。”她停下脚步。你继续往前走。回头时，她已经不见了。\n# en: Defend yourself\nopt.right.label [b9d2ca] = 为自己辩护\n# en: You lay it out plainly. She listens, then nods once. \"Fair. I'll have a word with certain prefects.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [72ff59] = 你把事情原原本本讲清楚。她听完，点了一下头。“公道。我会找某些级长谈谈。”\n# en: \"Hm,\" she says. \"Let's hope you're right.\" It doesn't feel like hope.\nopt.right.say.2 [d3a208] = “嗯，”她说，“但愿你是对的。”这听起来可不像但愿。\n\n@event vey_intro\n# en: Tea That Isn't There\ntitle [52dadd] = 不存在的茶\n# en: First Illusion lecture. Professor Vey pours you a cup of tea from a pot you would swear is empty, and waits. \"Milk?\" she asks. The whole row turns to see what you'll do with a cup of nothing.\ntext [e330e1] = 第一节幻术课。维伊教授从一只你敢发誓是空的茶壶里给你倒了一杯茶，然后等着。“加奶吗？”她问。整排人都转过头来，看你怎么对付一杯什么都没有的茶。\n# en: A lecture room hung with velvet curtains, a woman in a violet coat pouring from an empty teapot into a student's empty cup.\nscene [7245af] = 挂着天鹅绒帘子的教室里，一个穿紫罗兰色外套的女人从空茶壶往学生的空杯子里倒茶。\n# en: Drink it, and thank her\nopt.left.label [374044] = 喝下去，再道谢\n# en: You sip, sigh and ask where she buys it. Vey's mouth twitches. \"Lapsang. There was never any tea, of course. Sit down; you'll do.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [5466bf] = 你抿一口，叹口气，问她在哪儿买的。维伊的嘴角抽动了一下。“正山小种。当然，从来就没有茶。坐吧，你行。”\n# en: You raise the cup with a flourish and tip it a little too far. Nothing pours out, and the whole row sees nothing pour out. Vey writes something down, and smiles at it.\nopt.left.say.2 [2dbeaf] = 你潇洒地举起杯子，却倾得太过了。什么也没倒出来，而整排人都看见什么也没倒出来。维伊记了点什么，对着它笑了。\n# en: Say the cup is empty\nopt.right.label [9ea330] = 说杯子是空的\n# en: \"It's empty,\" you say. \"So it is,\" says Vey, pleased and disappointed at once. \"Honest eyes. We shall have to do something about those.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [73d780] = “是空的，”你说。“确实，”维伊说，既满意又失望。“诚实的眼睛。我们得想办法治一治。”\n\n@event brask_intro\n# en: Shovel. There. Go.\ntitle [ee049c] = 铲子。那儿。去。\n# en: First Beastcraft lesson, at the stables. Professor Brask is explaining to a hippogriff, in full and courteous sentences, that the new students will be clumsy and she is to be patient with them. Then she turns to you. \"Shovel. There. Go.\"\ntext [dc2351] = 第一节驭兽课，在马厩。布拉斯克教授正用完整而客气的句子向一头鹰头马身有翼兽解释：新生会笨手笨脚，请它多担待。然后她转向你。“铲子。那儿。去。”\n# en: A long stable in morning light, a grey-haired professor talking earnestly to a hippogriff while a student stands holding a shovel.\nscene [98b211] = 晨光中长长的马厩，一位灰发教授正认真地对一头鹰头马身有翼兽说话，一个学生拿着铲子站在一旁。\n# en: Muck out without a word\nopt.left.label [ef11b2] = 一声不吭地铲\n# en: You shovel until your arms shake. Brask watches, then tells the hippogriff, loud enough for you to hear, \"This one might do.\" To you she says nothing, which you later learn is praise.\nopt.left.say.1 [2284b5] = 你铲到胳膊发抖。布拉斯克看着，然后对鹰头马身有翼兽说，声音大得刚好让你听见：“这个兴许行。”她对你什么也没说——后来你才知道，那就是夸奖。\n# en: Ask what today's lesson is\nopt.right.label [068c9f] = 问今天学什么\n# en: Brask tells the hippogriff a story about a student who asked questions before shovelling. It ends with a bite. \"Moral: shovel.\" You shovel, a little behind the others.\nopt.right.say.1 [af14ce] = 布拉斯克给鹰头马身有翼兽讲了个故事：有个学生总在铲粪之前提问。结尾是被咬了一口。“寓意：铲。”你开始铲，比别人慢了一步。\n\n@event pell_first_shift\n# en: Count Aloud\ntitle [39b25b] = 数出声来\n# en: Your first shift at Pell & Daughters. Old Pell climbs down from his ladder, hands you a dropper and a bottle of tincture, and points at a waiting customer. \"Twelve drops,\" he says. \"Aloud. Everyone counts aloud in this shop. The drawers like it.\"\ntext [e01d70] = 你在佩尔父女药铺的第一个班。老佩尔从梯子上爬下来，递给你一支滴管和一瓶酊剂，指了指一位等着的顾客。“十二滴，”他说，“数出声。这店里人人都数出声。抽屉们喜欢听。”\n# en: A narrow apothecary lined with tiny labelled drawers, an old man on a ladder holding out a glass dropper to a nervous new clerk.\nscene [9281b1] = 一间狭长的药铺，墙上满是贴着标签的小抽屉，梯子上的老人向一个紧张的新店员递过一支玻璃滴管。\n# en: Count every drop aloud\nopt.left.label [2046bb] = 每一滴都数出声\n# en: \"One. Two. Three.\" Your voice fills the shop; the customer joins in at nine. Pell nods once. \"Twelve. Not eleven. Not thirteen. You'll count like that until they bury you.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [5a7443] = “一。二。三。”你的声音充满了整间铺子；数到九时顾客也跟着数。佩尔点了一下头。“十二。不是十一，不是十三。你就这么数，一直数到入土。”\n# en: Count in your head, quickly\nopt.right.label [585870] = 在心里快快地数\n# en: Twelve drops, done in a breath; the customer tells the whole queue how quick the new clerk is. Pell counts the level against the light. \"Correct. Next time, aloud. I like to hear a thing being right.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [95b946] = 十二滴，一口气滴完；顾客跟整条队的人夸新店员手快。佩尔把瓶子举到光下看刻度。“对。下次数出声。我喜欢听见事情做对的声音。”\n# en: You lose count at eight and guess. Pell tips the bottle out and starts it again, aloud, without a word to you. The customer leaves with the right dose and a look at you.\nopt.right.say.2 [2e54e1] = 数到八你就数乱了，只好估。佩尔把瓶子倒空，重新数了一遍，出声地数，一句话也没对你说。顾客拿着正确的剂量走了，临走看了你一眼。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/people_students.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/people_students.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event lyra_intro\n# en: The Last Copy\ntitle [370a21] = 最后一本\n# en: You reach for the last copy of \"Foundations of Flame\" and a ringed hand gets there first. \"I need it more,\" says a tall girl with crimson hair, not looking at you. \"You'll only underline the wrong bits.\"\ntext [81419e] = 你伸手去拿最后一本《火焰基础》，一只戴戒指的手抢先了一步。“我更需要它，”一个高挑的红发女孩说，看都没看你，“你只会划错重点。”\n# en: Two hands reaching for the same red-bound book on a library shelf; one hand wears a signet ring with a flame crest.\nscene [396ae6] = 两只手伸向图书馆书架上同一本红皮书；其中一只手上戴着刻有火焰家徽的印章戒指。\n# en: Let her have it\nopt.left.label [6e7c4e] = 让给她\n# en: \"At least you know your place,\" she says, which is somehow worse than rudeness. Lyra Vaneshade. Everyone knows the name.\nopt.left.say.1 [69ebd2] = “至少你有自知之明。”她说，这不知怎么比无礼更伤人。莱拉·凡妮希德。人人都知道这个名字。\n# en: Argue for it\nopt.right.label [503eb7] = 据理力争\n# en: You quote chapter three at her. She raises an eyebrow, then hands you the book. \"Fine. Return it underlined correctly.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [bbdc0c] = 你给她背了一段第三章。她挑起眉毛，然后把书递给了你。“行。还的时候把重点划对。”\n# en: She wins the argument in four sentences and leaves with the book. You think of the perfect comeback that night.\nopt.right.say.2 [b0bbf8] = 她四句话就赢了这场争论，拿着书走了。那天晚上你才想到完美的回嘴。\n\n@event lyra_letter\n# en: A Letter With a Black Seal\ntitle [47420d] = 黑色封印的信\n# en: You find Lyra on a window seat with a crumpled letter. Black seal. Her family's. She's not crying; Vaneshades don't. But she's not quite not crying either.\ntext [4caeea] = 你发现莱拉坐在窗台上，手里攥着一封揉皱的信。黑色封印。她家族的。她没有哭；凡妮希德家的人不哭。但她也不完全是没在哭。\n# en: A girl sitting on a window seat in an empty corridor, crushing a letter with a black wax seal in her fist, face turned away.\nscene [ed4b56] = 空荡荡的走廊里，一个女孩坐在窗台上，把一封黑蜡封口的信攥在拳中，脸转向一边。\n# en: Pretend you didn't see\nopt.left.label [25b345] = 假装没看见\n# en: You walk past. Later, she's flawless again, as if nothing happened. Maybe nothing did.\nopt.left.say.1 [8cb435] = 你走了过去。后来，她又是那个无懈可击的她了，仿佛什么都没发生。也许真的什么都没发生。\n# en: Sit down beside her\nopt.right.label [e6df6e] = 在她身边坐下\n# en: \"Second place,\" she says eventually. \"I came second in the Winter Trials. My mother wants to know who beat me.\" She almost laughs. \"It's always someone.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [2ea3fc] = “第二名，”她终于开口，“我在冬季考核里拿了第二。我母亲想知道是谁赢了我。”她几乎笑了出来。“总会有那么一个人。”\n\n@event lyra_study_rivalry\n# en: Top of the Class\ntitle [76842e] = 全班第一\n# en: The professors post this week's rankings, with an open paper this afternoon for anyone who wants to improve their place. Lyra is first{&. You're close enough to see the back of her head.|, again. You're close enough to read her handwriting.|. Of course she is. You're one name below her, which is somehow worse than three.} She notices you noticing.\ntext [f904e6] = 教授们公布了本周排名，还说今天下午有一场公开考试，想提高名次的人都可以参加。莱拉第一{&。你离她近到能看见她的后脑勺。|，又是第一。你离她近到能看清她的笔迹。|。当然是她。你就排在她下面一名，这不知怎么比差三名还难受。}她注意到了你在注意她。\n# en: Two students at opposite ends of a long library table, surrounded by towers of books, glaring over the tops of them.\nscene [e05ebb] = 长长的图书馆桌两端各坐一个学生，被一摞摞书包围着，隔着书堆互相瞪视。\n# en: Suggest studying together\nopt.left.label [d07df2] = 提议一起学习\n# en: {&\"Together?\" She considers this like a foreign custom. Then she pulls out a chair. She's an excellent, merciless study partner.|She already has two cups of tea and your chair pulled out. \"You're late,\" she says. You aren't.|\"Fine. But I'm quizzing you first.\" She is merciless again, and you're better for it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [a3ad5c] = {&“一起？”她像在考虑一种外国习俗。然后她拉出了一把椅子。她是个出色而无情的学习搭档。|她已经备好了两杯茶，把你的椅子也拉了出来。“你迟到了，”她说。你并没有。|“行。但我先考你。”她又一次毫不留情，而你因此进步了。}\n# en: Beat her outright\nopt.right.label [7983cc] = 正面击败她\n# en: The afternoon paper puts you first. Lyra stares at the list for a long time. Then she finds you and says, \"Good.\" She means it.\nopt.right.say.1 [8e7fb5] = 下午的考试让你排到了第一。莱拉盯着名单看了很久。然后她找到你，说：“很好。”她是认真的。\n# en: {&You cram through lunch and come third. Lyra is still first. She doesn't gloat. That's worse.|Second, by one mark. Lyra leaves a note on your desk: \"Close.\" You keep it.|You come fourth. Lyra looks almost disappointed on your behalf, which is unbearable.}\nopt.right.say.2 [1024cc] = {&你午饭时间都在临阵磨枪，结果是第三。莱拉还是第一。她没有得意。这更糟。|第二，差一分。莱拉在你桌上留了张字条：“很接近。”你把它收了起来。|你排第四。莱拉看上去几乎在替你失望，这简直让人受不了。}\n\n@event lyra_family_gala\n# en: The Vaneshade Winter Gala\ntitle [cc6875] = 凡妮希德冬季晚宴\n# en: \"My family's winter gala,\" Lyra says, too casually. \"I need a guest who won't embarrass me. You'll do. Probably. You'll need proper clothes.\"\ntext [f5575c] = “我家的冬季晚宴，”莱拉说，语气太过随意，“我需要一个不会让我丢脸的客人。你还行。大概。你得穿得像样点。”\n# en: A glittering ballroom in a noble house, candles floating in mid-air, a tall woman in crimson watching the crowd like a hawk.\nscene [bf8dd3] = 贵族宅邸里金碧辉煌的舞厅，蜡烛悬浮在空中，一位身着深红衣裙的高挑女人像鹰一样注视着人群。\n# en: Decline\nopt.left.label [eaef39] = 拒绝\n# en: \"Of course.\" A pause. \"It doesn't matter.\" It clearly does.\nopt.left.say.1 [1fe8ea] = “当然。”停顿。“无所谓。”显然有所谓。\n# en: Go with her (15 gold for clothes)\nopt.right.label [e8a9e8] = 陪她去（置装花费 15 金币）\n# en: Lyra's mother asks who your parents are, and then asks no further questions at all. Lyra squeezes your hand under the table, hard.\nopt.right.say.1 [3ae20f] = 莱拉的母亲问起你父母是谁，然后就再也没有问别的了。莱拉在桌下用力握住了你的手。\n# en: You dance, you charm a duke, you don't spill anything. In the carriage home, Lyra falls asleep on your shoulder.\nopt.right.say.2 [db666b] = 你跳舞，你迷倒了一位公爵，你什么都没洒。回家的马车上，莱拉靠在你肩上睡着了。\n\n@event lyra_confession\n# en: Lyra Doesn't Lose\ntitle [4e22c6] = 莱拉从不认输\n# en: After practice, Lyra doesn't leave. \"I don't lose,\" she says, staring at the scorch marks. \"I don't. But I think — \" She stops. Starts again. \"I think I've lost to you. Say something before I set something on fire.\"\ntext [0b2d38] = 练习结束后，莱拉没有走。“我不会输，”她盯着地上的焦痕说，“我不会。但我觉得——”她停住了。又重新开口。“我觉得我输给你了。快说点什么，不然我要烧东西了。”\n# en: A girl with crimson hair standing very close in a practice ring at dusk, sparks still drifting from her wand, cheeks flushed.\nscene [fa1888] = 黄昏的练习圈里，一个红发女孩站得很近，魔杖上还飘着火星，脸颊泛红。\n# en: Let her down gently\nopt.left.label [c62f10] = 温柔地拒绝她\n# en: She nods, sharp and quick. \"Understood.\" She practises alone for a month. Then one day she nods to you in the corridor, and it's almost all right.\nopt.left.say.1 [d294fb] = 她干脆利落地点了点头。“明白了。”她独自练了一个月。后来有一天她在走廊里朝你点点头，一切差不多都好了。\n# en: Kiss her\nopt.right.label [5fa79c] = 吻她\n# en: Nothing catches fire. Well. Something does, but it's only the practice dummy, and neither of you notices for a while.\nopt.right.say.1 [90be4c] = 什么都没着火。好吧。有东西着了，但只是练习用的假人，而且你们好一阵子都没注意到。\n\n@event lyra_family_ultimatum\n# en: The Ultimatum\ntitle [f0d1f2] = 最后通牒\n# en: Another black-sealed letter. Lyra reads it twice and goes white. \"My mother has found out about us. End it, she says, or she ends my inheritance.\" She looks at you. \"Tell me what to do.\"\ntext [fa5d3c] = 又一封黑色封印的信。莱拉读了两遍，脸色发白。“我母亲知道我们的事了。她说，要么断了，要么她断了我的继承权。”她看着你。“告诉我该怎么办。”\n# en: Lyra holding a letter with a black seal, her other hand clenched around a ring she has just pulled off.\nscene [8c7e83] = 莱拉手持一封黑色封印的信，另一只手紧紧攥着一枚刚摘下的戒指。\n# en: \"Choose yourself, not them.\"\nopt.left.label [1f5be4] = “选择你自己，而不是他们。”\n# en: She tears the letter in half. Then in quarters. Then she sets the quarters on fire and laughs like she hasn't in years.\nopt.left.say.1 [3f7f4b] = 她把信撕成两半。再撕成四片。然后把碎片点着，笑得像好多年没这样笑过。\n# en: \"Easy for you to say,\" she snaps. She doesn't mean it. She also doesn't take it back.\nopt.left.say.2 [66097c] = “说得轻巧。”她冲你吼道。她不是真心的。但她也没有收回这句话。\n# en: She tears the letter in half. Then in quarters. Then she sets the quarters on fire and laughs like she hasn't in years.\nopt.left.say.3 [3f7f4b] = 她把信撕成两半。再撕成四片。然后把碎片点着，笑得像好多年没这样笑过。\n# en: \"Easy for you to say,\" she snaps. She doesn't mean it. She also doesn't take it back.\nopt.left.say.4 [66097c] = “说得轻巧。”她冲你吼道。她不是真心的。但她也没有收回这句话。\n# en: Step back so she doesn't lose everything\nopt.right.label [9c8862] = 退一步，让她不至于失去一切\n# en: You end it before she has to. She doesn't argue. That's how you know how much it costs her.\nopt.right.say.1 [27b1b0] = 在她不得不开口之前，你先结束了这段感情。她没有争辩。正因如此，你才知道这对她代价有多大。\n\n@event lyra_rival_sabotage\n# en: Sabotage in Class\ntitle [1db464] = 课堂上的暗算\n# en: Your perfect potion turns black and starts screaming. Across the room, Lyra is very carefully not looking at you.\ntext [8b133c] = 你那锅完美的药剂突然变黑，开始尖叫。教室另一头，莱拉非常小心地没往你这边看。\n# en: A cauldron spitting black smoke, a crimson-haired student walking away with a tiny vial hidden in her sleeve.\nscene [f64dbb] = 一口坩埚喷出黑烟，一个红发学生走开，袖子里藏着一只小瓶。\n# en: Retaliate\nopt.left.label [5cb1e8] = 报复回去\n# en: Next lesson, her wand produces only bubbles. Pink ones. She knows. You both know. It's war. It's sort of fun.\nopt.left.say.1 [6d8609] = 下节课，她的魔杖只能冒出泡泡。粉红色的。她知道。你们俩都知道。开战了。还挺好玩的。\n# en: You're caught slipping something into her bag. She looks genuinely delighted.\nopt.left.say.2 [7dfe71] = 你往她包里塞东西时被逮了个正着。她看起来真心高兴。\n# en: Confront her directly\nopt.right.label [eed285] = 当面质问她\n# en: \"That was beneath you.\" She flushes. \"...Yes. It was.\" The next day there's a perfect replacement potion on your desk.\nopt.right.say.1 [3c2fd2] = “这有失你的身份。”她脸红了。“……是。是有失身份。”第二天，你桌上放着一份完美的替代药剂。\n\n@event lyra_burnout\n# en: Lyra Falls\ntitle [d0abf9] = 莱拉倒下了\n# en: You find Lyra face-down on a library desk at dawn, not asleep — out cold. She's been studying for three days straight. \"Don't tell anyone,\" she mumbles when she comes round. \"Mother can't know.\"\ntext [794170] = 黎明时分你发现莱拉趴在图书馆的桌上，不是睡着了——是昏过去了。她已经连续学了三天。“别告诉任何人，”她醒过来时含糊地说，“不能让母亲知道。”\n# en: A girl collapsed across a library desk among scattered notes, quill still in hand, candle burned down to nothing.\nscene [91fdd5] = 一个女孩瘫倒在图书馆书桌上，周围散落着笔记，手里还握着羽毛笔，蜡烛已经燃尽。\n# en: Take her to the infirmary anyway\nopt.left.label [26e217] = 还是把她送去医务室\n# en: She's furious. Then she sleeps for nineteen hours. When she wakes up, she says, quietly, \"Thank you.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [0d4dbd] = 她气坏了。然后她睡了十九个小时。醒来时，她轻声说：“谢谢你。”\n# en: Cover for her and make her rest\nopt.right.label [bfbbb0] = 替她打掩护，逼她休息\n# en: You tell her professors she has a cold and sit by her door with your homework. She sleeps. You keep watch.\nopt.right.say.1 [1b1d64] = 你告诉她的教授们她感冒了，然后带着作业守在她门口。她睡了。你守着。\n\n@event tobias_intro\n# en: The Lost Boy\ntitle [3f2dc0] = 迷路的男孩\n# en: A big, broad-shouldered boy is leading a crying first-year through the corridors. \"Don't worry, I know exactly where the infirmary is,\" he says, and walks confidently into a broom cupboard.\ntext [03f851] = 一个高大宽肩的男生正领着一个哭鼻子的一年级生穿过走廊。“别担心，我很清楚医务室在哪儿。”他说着，满怀信心地走进了扫帚间。\n# en: A large, gentle-faced boy leading a crying first-year by the hand down the wrong corridor, both very lost.\nscene [0ae971] = 一个面容温和的大个子男生牵着一个哭泣的一年级生走在错误的走廊里，两人都迷路了。\n# en: Point them the right way\nopt.left.label [f53c4f] = 给他们指路\n# en: \"Ah. Left, you say. Right. I mean, correct.\" He grins, enormous and sheepish. \"Tobias. Turnip scholarship. Thanks.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [74c134] = “啊。你说左边。对。我是说，没错。”他咧嘴一笑，笑容大大的，有点不好意思。“托比亚斯。芜菁奖学金。谢啦。”\n# en: Walk with them\nopt.right.label [799c3a] = 陪他们一起走\n# en: You end up taking the scenic route, and the first-year stops crying because Tobias is telling a very long story about a goat.\nopt.right.say.1 [c5721c] = 你们最后绕了一条风景路线，一年级生不哭了，因为托比亚斯在讲一个关于山羊的超长故事。\n\n@event tobias_homesick\n# en: Homesick\ntitle [a133e0] = 想家\n# en: Tobias has gone quiet. He's planted seedlings in every cup in the common room. \"Harvest at home,\" he says. \"First one I've missed. Ma's doing it alone.\"\ntext [fd42a8] = 托比亚斯变得沉默了。他在公共休息室的每个杯子里都种了幼苗。“家里在收庄稼，”他说，“这是我第一次错过。我妈一个人在干。”\n# en: A dormitory windowsill crowded with clay pots of seedlings, a big boy watering them with a tiny can.\nscene [433f64] = 宿舍窗台上挤满了种着幼苗的陶盆，一个大个子男生正用一只小水壶浇水。\n# en: Cheer him up with a joke\nopt.left.label [c33487] = 讲个笑话逗他开心\n# en: Your joke is terrible. He laughs anyway, the big rumbling laugh, and that seems to help.\nopt.left.say.1 [e60401] = 你的笑话烂透了。他还是笑了，那种轰隆隆的大笑，好像真的管用。\n# en: Help him build a window garden\nopt.right.label [8aab52] = 帮他建一个窗台菜园\n# en: You buy seeds and a stack of pots. By the end of the week the common room smells of tomato leaves. Tobias whistles while he waters them.\nopt.right.say.1 [4f8881] = 你买来种子和一摞花盆。到周末，公共休息室里满是番茄叶的味道。托比亚斯浇水时吹着口哨。\n\n@event tobias_bad_harvest\n# en: The Harvest Failed\ntitle [aab2ca] = 歉收\n# en: Tobias is packing. Blight took the harvest. His family needs money, and hands. \"Thirty crowns would get them through winter,\" he says, not asking. \"But I haven't got thirty crowns. I've got two arms.\"\ntext [b3ac08] = 托比亚斯在收拾行李。枯萎病毁了收成。他家需要钱，也需要人手。“三十克朗就能让他们熬过冬天，”他说，并不是在开口要，“可我没有三十克朗。我只有两条胳膊。”\n# en: A boy packing a battered trunk in an empty dormitory, a letter with smudged handwriting open on the bed.\nscene [7cc0f7] = 空荡荡的宿舍里，一个男生在收拾一只破旧的行李箱，床上摊着一封字迹模糊的信。\n# en: Persuade him to stay and find work here\nopt.left.label [4e1985] = 劝他留下，在这里找工作\n# en: You walk him to the job board. He signs up for double shifts at the stables and sends every crown home. He stays.\nopt.left.say.1 [3cac05] = 你陪他去了招工告示板。他报了马厩的双班，把每一枚克朗都寄回了家。他留下了。\n# en: He listens, nods, and takes the barge in the morning. He's back in two months, thinner and quieter.\nopt.left.say.2 [373ae1] = 他听着，点点头，第二天早上坐上了驳船。两个月后他回来了，瘦了，也更沉默了。\n# en: Give him thirty crowns\nopt.right.label [902ad2] = 给他三十克朗\n# en: He tries to refuse three times. Then he hugs you so hard your spine cracks. \"I'll pay you back. Every crown. Every one.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [2214f7] = 他推辞了三次。然后抱住你，抱得你脊椎咔咔响。“我会还你的。每一枚克朗。每一枚。”\n\n@event tobias_repays\n# en: Every Crown\ntitle [807b67] = 每一枚克朗\n# en: A lumpy, hand-stitched pouch on your pillow: thirty crowns exactly, in small coins. And a jar of honey from home. A note in big, careful letters: \"TOLD YOU. — T.\"\ntext [150790] = 你枕头上放着一只鼓鼓囊囊、手工缝制的小袋子：正好三十克朗，全是零钱。还有一罐家里的蜂蜜。一张字条，字写得又大又认真：“说到做到。——托”\n# en: A clumsy hand-stitched pouch of coins and a jar of honey left on a dormitory pillow.\nscene [1e69a7] = 宿舍枕头上一只笨拙的手缝钱袋和一罐蜂蜜。\n# en: Smile\nopt.ok.label [c2972f] = 笑一笑\n# en: The honey tastes of a farm you have never seen and somehow already miss.\nopt.ok.say.1 [8639ca] = 那罐蜂蜜尝起来像一座你从没见过、却已经开始想念的农场。\n\n@event tobias_godparent\n# en: The Mandrake Baby\ntitle [5adaaf] = 曼德拉草宝宝\n# en: Tobias presents a flowerpot containing a very small mandrake in a knitted hat. \"This is Gerald,\" he says. \"I'd like you to be his godparent.\" Gerald screams softly.\ntext [287127] = 托比亚斯捧出一个花盆，里面是一株戴着针织帽的小小曼德拉草。“这是杰拉尔德，”他说，“我想请你当他的教父母。”杰拉尔德轻声尖叫。\n# en: A tiny mandrake in a pot wearing a knitted hat, a beaming boy presenting it like a newborn.\nscene [39f8e3] = 一株戴着针织帽的小曼德拉草种在盆里，一个笑容灿烂的男生像捧着新生儿一样把它捧出来。\n# en: Politely decline\nopt.left.label [8a601d] = 礼貌地拒绝\n# en: \"No pressure,\" says Tobias, visibly pressured. Gerald glares at you from under his hat.\nopt.left.say.1 [00f5a4] = “没压力的。”托比亚斯说，明显压力很大。杰拉尔德从帽子底下瞪着你。\n# en: Accept with honour\nopt.right.label [395d80] = 荣幸地接受\n# en: You are now Gerald's godparent. Gerald bites you. Tobias says this means Gerald loves you.\nopt.right.say.1 [95091f] = 你现在是杰拉尔德的教父母了。杰拉尔德咬了你一口。托比亚斯说这代表杰拉尔德爱你。\n\n@event tobias_bullied\n# en: Tobias and the Prefect\ntitle [0d0b40] = 托比亚斯与级长\n# en: Bram is doing an impression of Tobias's accent in the corridor. People are laughing. Tobias is pretending not to hear, ears burning red.\ntext [cf84da] = 布拉姆在走廊里模仿托比亚斯的口音。大家都在笑。托比亚斯假装没听见，耳朵红得发烫。\n# en: A burly prefect mocking a big farm boy in a crowded corridor, students sniggering, the boy's ears bright red.\nscene [d6b2fe] = 拥挤的走廊里，一个魁梧的级长在嘲笑一个高大的农家男孩，学生们在窃笑，男孩的耳朵通红。\n# en: Stay out of it\nopt.left.label [03d24a] = 不掺和\n# en: Tobias doesn't mention it. He doesn't sit with you at dinner, either.\nopt.left.say.1 [be0b18] = 托比亚斯没提这事。晚饭时他也没和你坐一起。\n# en: Step in\nopt.right.label [db9576] = 站出来\n# en: \"Funny,\" you say. \"Do your father next.\" The laughter turns on Bram. He stalks off. Tobias stares at you in awe.\nopt.right.say.1 [26c55a] = “真好笑，”你说，“下一个学你爸吧。”笑声转向了布拉姆。他气冲冲地走了。托比亚斯敬畏地看着你。\n# en: Bram turns his attention to you instead, with interest. Tobias brings you a pie later. A whole pie.\nopt.right.say.2 [a956b8] = 布拉姆转而饶有兴趣地盯上了你。后来托比亚斯给你送来一个馅饼。一整个。\n\n@event tobias_confession\n# en: A Confession, With Vegetables\ntitle [a19fc5] = 一场带着蔬菜的告白\n# en: Tobias corners you in the greenhouse holding a very large pumpkin. \"I grew this,\" he says. \"For you. It's the best one. I didn't know how else to — I like you. Like that. I've practised this on the pumpkin. Sorry.\"\ntext [157367] = 托比亚斯抱着一个很大的南瓜在温室里拦住了你。“这是我种的，”他说，“送你的。是最好的那个。我不知道还能怎么——我喜欢你。是那种喜欢。我对着南瓜练习过了。对不起。”\n# en: A big boy holding out a perfect pumpkin like an offering in a greenhouse, cheeks scarlet.\nscene [563b87] = 温室里一个大个子男生像献礼一样捧出一个完美的南瓜，脸颊通红。\n# en: Let him down gently\nopt.left.label [b009f9] = 温柔地拒绝他\n# en: He nods a lot. \"That's all right. That's fine. You can still have the pumpkin.\" You keep it until it rots, out of loyalty.\nopt.left.say.1 [f837ce] = 他拼命点头。“没关系。没事的。南瓜你还是可以留着。”出于情义，你一直留到它烂掉。\n# en: Say yes\nopt.right.label [c73ccb] = 答应他\n# en: He drops the pumpkin. It's fine; pumpkins are sturdy. So, it turns out, are his hugs.\nopt.right.say.1 [3f29ab] = 他手里的南瓜掉了。没事；南瓜很结实。事实证明，他的拥抱也是。\n\n@event tobias_picnic\n# en: A Picnic in the Orchard\ntitle [2dff91] = 果园野餐\n# en: Tobias has packed a picnic: {~bread he baked, cheese he begged from the kitchens, apples from the orchard he has befriended|a pie with your initials in the crust, slightly burnt, and a jar of honey from someone he helped|plums, hard cheese and something he calls a salad, which is mostly flowers}. \"No {~exams|essays|professors} for six hours,\" he announces. \"I checked.\"\ntext [70bc74] = 托比亚斯准备了野餐：{~他自己烤的面包，从厨房软磨硬泡来的奶酪，还有他“交了朋友”的果园里的苹果|一个酥皮上刻着你名字缩写的馅饼，有点烤焦了，还有一罐他帮过的人送的蜂蜜|李子、硬奶酪，还有一样他称为沙拉的东西，基本上全是花}。“六个小时内没有{~考试|论文|教授}，”他宣布，“我查过了。”\n# en: A checked blanket under blossoming apple trees, a basket of bread and cheese, two students lying in the dappled light.\nscene [66c097] = 开花的苹果树下铺着格子毯，一篮面包和奶酪，两个学生躺在斑驳的光影里。\n# en: Bring your books anyway\nopt.left.label [b8ab05] = 还是带上书\n# en: You study on the blanket. He eyes the books, sighs a very small sigh, and falls asleep with his head in your lap. It's still lovely.\nopt.left.say.1 [fcc2e1] = 你在毯子上学习。他瞄了一眼那些书，很轻很轻地叹了口气，然后枕着你的腿睡着了。依然很美好。\n# en: Leave everything behind\nopt.right.label [1ca23f] = 把一切都抛在脑后\n# en: The afternoon goes golden and slow. You can't remember the last time you didn't think about grades.\nopt.right.say.1 [5c67d2] = 午后变得金黄而缓慢。你已经记不起上一次不去想成绩是什么时候了。\n\n@event seraphine_intro\n# en: Your Left Shoe\ntitle [0404ae] = 你的左脚鞋\n# en: A girl drifts past you in the corridor and says, without stopping, \"You're going to lose your left shoe today. Don't worry, it's under the stairs in the east wing.\"\ntext [01fffe] = 一个女孩从你身边飘过，头也不回地说：“你今天会丢一只左脚的鞋。别担心，它在东翼的楼梯底下。”\n# en: A slight girl with silver-grey eyes and a scarf of stars passing in a corridor, pointing absently at someone's feet.\nscene [c9cd41] = 一个纤瘦的女孩围着星星图案的围巾，长着银灰色的眼睛，在走廊里经过时漫不经心地指着某人的脚。\n# en: Ignore her\nopt.left.label [44f360] = 不理她\n# en: That afternoon a staircase moves and eats your left shoe. It is, eventually, under the stairs in the east wing.\nopt.left.say.1 [b13d26] = 那天下午，一道楼梯挪了位置，吃掉了你的左脚鞋。最终，它确实在东翼的楼梯底下。\n# en: Ask how she knows\nopt.right.label [48f36e] = 问她怎么知道的\n# en: \"I don't know things,\" she says. \"Things know me.\" Then: \"Seraphine. You'll want the shoe back by four.\" You get it back at 3:58.\nopt.right.say.1 [ef5d82] = “我不知道事情，”她说，“是事情知道我。”然后又说：“瑟拉芬。你会想在四点前把鞋拿回来的。”你在三点五十八分拿了回来。\n\n@event seraphine_tarot\n# en: The Cards\ntitle [c5101b] = 牌\n# en: Seraphine shuffles a battered tarot deck. \"One card,\" she says. \"For you. Only if you want it. They're quite rude sometimes.\"\ntext [95625a] = 瑟拉芬洗着一副破旧的塔罗牌。“抽一张，”她说，“给你的。只要你想。它们有时候挺没礼貌的。”\n# en: A tarot spread on a velvet cloth in candlelight, a girl's pale fingers turning over a card showing a lightning-struck tower.\nscene [8a2f94] = 烛光下天鹅绒布上摊开的塔罗牌阵，一个女孩苍白的手指翻开一张画着雷击高塔的牌。\n# en: Draw a card\nopt.left.label [a80dc6] = 抽一张\n# en: The Star. \"Hope,\" she says, delighted. \"And someone's going to tell you a secret. Oh — it's me. I like you.\" She blushes.\nopt.left.say.1 [bfb1c6] = 星星。“希望，”她高兴地说，“还有人要告诉你一个秘密。噢——是我。我喜欢你。”她脸红了。\n# en: The Tower. She looks at it a long time. \"It's fine,\" she says. \"Towers fall. That's what they're for.\"\nopt.left.say.2 [ecbdc5] = 高塔。她看了很久。“没关系，”她说，“塔会倒。塔就是用来倒的。”\n# en: Decline\nopt.right.label [eaef39] = 不抽\n# en: \"Good,\" she says, putting the deck away. \"Knowing is overrated.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [1ff9e2] = “很好。”她把牌收起来，“知道太多被高估了。”\n\n@event seraphine_lake_vision\n# en: She Walks Into the Lake\ntitle [7cd453] = 她走进了湖里\n# en: From your window you see Seraphine walking into the lake in her nightgown, eyes shining silver, already up to her waist.\ntext [89cff3] = 你从窗口看见瑟拉芬穿着睡袍走进湖里，眼睛泛着银光，水已经没到了腰。\n# en: A girl in a nightgown wading into a misty lake at night, eyes glowing silver, arms outstretched toward something under the water.\nscene [21af5a] = 夜里一个穿睡袍的女孩涉入雾气笼罩的湖中，双眼泛着银光，双臂伸向水下的某样东西。\n# en: Wake a prefect\nopt.left.label [9162cd] = 叫醒一位级长\n# en: The prefect drags her out, coughing. She doesn't remember anything. She looks at you strangely for days.\nopt.left.say.1 [f25863] = 级长把她拖了出来，她咳个不停。她什么都不记得了。接下来几天，她看你的眼神都怪怪的。\n# en: Run down and pull her out\nopt.right.label [4787f1] = 冲下去把她拉出来\n# en: You reach her just as she goes under. On the shore she wakes and whispers, \"It was calling me. It called you too.\" Then she holds on very tight.\nopt.right.say.1 [d29a81] = 她沉下去的那一刻你抓住了她。在岸上，她醒过来低声说：“它在呼唤我。它也呼唤了你。”然后她紧紧抱住了你。\n# en: You both nearly drown. You both come down with Lake Fever. She brings you soup that tastes faintly of the future.\nopt.right.say.2 [571f07] = 你们俩都差点淹死。你们俩都得了湖热病。她给你送来的汤，隐约有未来的味道。\n\n@event seraphine_future\n# en: I Saw You Old\ntitle [9b881b] = 我看见你老了\n# en: \"I saw you old,\" Seraphine says suddenly. \"Last night. You were happy. Really happy. There was someone with you.\" She pauses. \"Do you want to know who?\"\ntext [c5d67f] = “我看见你老了，”瑟拉芬突然说，“昨晚。你很幸福。真的很幸福。有人陪着你。”她顿了顿，“你想知道是谁吗？”\n# en: Two students sitting on the rim of a dry fountain, one looking up at the stars, the other looking at the first with a soft smile.\nscene [027150] = 两个学生坐在干涸的喷泉边沿，一个仰望星空，另一个温柔地笑着看向前者。\n# en: Yes, tell me\nopt.left.label [f7e607] = 想，告诉我\n# en: She looks at you for a long time. \"I'll tell you when you're old,\" she says, and goes pink.\nopt.left.say.1 [4bb771] = 她看了你很久。“等你老了我再告诉你。”她说，脸红了。\n# en: \"Someone kind,\" she says. \"That's all I could see. Their hands were kind.\"\nopt.left.say.2 [19f673] = “一个温柔的人，”她说，“我只看得到这么多。那双手很温柔。”\n# en: No, let it be a surprise\nopt.right.label [97a4ad] = 不了，留个惊喜吧\n# en: She smiles like you've given her a present. \"That's the right answer. Nobody ever gives the right answer.\" Not knowing nags at you for days.\nopt.right.say.1 [ce9281] = 她笑了，像你送了她一份礼物。“这是正确答案。从来没人给出过正确答案。”不知道答案这件事，让你心痒了好几天。\n\n@event seraphine_overwhelmed\n# en: Too Many Tomorrows\ntitle [409210] = 太多个明天\n# en: Seraphine hasn't come to class in a week. You find her in a dark storeroom with her hands over her eyes. \"It's too loud,\" she whispers. \"Everyone's futures. All at once.\"\ntext [92ab7f] = 瑟拉芬已经一周没来上课了。你在一间黑漆漆的储藏室里找到了她，她用双手捂着眼睛。“太吵了，”她低声说，“每个人的未来。一下子全涌过来。”\n# en: A girl curled in a dark corner of an observatory with her hands over her eyes, a teacup gone cold beside her.\nscene [7f0e93] = 天文台黑暗的角落里蜷缩着一个女孩，双手捂着眼睛，身旁的茶早已凉透。\n# en: Fetch Sister Amaranth\nopt.left.label [0ebdc9] = 去叫阿玛兰斯修女\n# en: Amaranth gives her a sleeping draught and a dark room. She recovers. She doesn't say much about it.\nopt.left.say.1 [42cef1] = 阿玛兰斯给了她一剂安眠药和一间暗室。她恢复了。她对此不怎么提起。\n# en: Sit with her in the dark\nopt.right.label [30debe] = 在黑暗中陪着她\n# en: You don't speak. After an hour she takes her hands from her eyes. \"Yours is quiet,\" she says. \"Your future. It's very quiet. I like it.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [44b951] = 你没有说话。一个小时后，她把手从眼睛上拿开。“你的很安静，”她说，“你的未来。非常安静。我喜欢。”\n\n@event seraphine_confession\n# en: She Already Knew\ntitle [e91972] = 她早就知道\n# en: \"I've seen this conversation forty-one times,\" Seraphine says. \"In twenty-nine of them, you say yes. I've been very nervous about the other twelve.\" She holds out her hand. \"I like you. That part was always the same.\"\ntext [94995b] = “这段对话我已经看过四十一次了，”瑟拉芬说，“其中二十九次你答应了。另外十二次让我很紧张。”她伸出手。“我喜欢你。这一部分每次都一样。”\n# en: A girl with silver eyes holding out a single falling-star seed in her palm on a dark observatory balcony.\nscene [966292] = 漆黑的天文台阳台上，一个银色眼睛的女孩掌心托着一粒流星的种子递过来。\n# en: Gently say no\nopt.left.label [f46b92] = 温柔地拒绝\n# en: She nods. \"Twelve,\" she says softly. \"I knew it might be twelve.\" She's kind to you afterwards. Somehow that hurts more.\nopt.left.say.1 [4e2d00] = 她点点头。“十二，”她轻声说，“我就知道可能是十二。”之后她对你依然很温柔。不知为何，这更让人难过。\n# en: Take her hand\nopt.right.label [4658a2] = 握住她的手\n# en: \"Twenty-nine,\" she whispers, and laughs, and for once she's entirely in the present.\nopt.right.say.1 [cd7951] = “二十九。”她低声说，笑了起来，难得地，她完完全全活在当下。\n\n@event corvin_intro\n# en: Light Fingers\ntitle [fc7fa5] = 手快\n# en: Someone bumps you in the crowd. A moment later a dark-haired boy is bowing and holding up your purse. \"You dropped this,\" he says. \"Into my pocket. Terrible habit of yours. Corvin Ashby.\"\ntext [88b693] = 人群里有人撞了你一下。片刻之后，一个黑发男生朝你鞠躬，手里举着你的钱袋。“你掉了这个，”他说，“掉进了我的口袋。你这习惯可真糟。科尔文·阿什比。”\n# en: A dark-haired student with a crooked grin holding up a purse between two fingers, mid-bow.\nscene [77be60] = 一个嘴角歪歪笑着的黑发学生，两指夹着一只钱袋，正弯腰行礼。\n# en: Snatch it back and report him\nopt.left.label [69c705] = 一把夺回来，举报他\n# en: He's gone before the prefect arrives. Your purse is all there, plus an IOU you don't remember receiving.\nopt.left.say.1 [2b1b37] = 级长来之前他就不见了。你的钱袋一分不少，只是多了一张你不记得收过的欠条。\n# en: Laugh\nopt.right.label [52d15e] = 笑出声\n# en: \"Oh, I like you,\" he says, and teaches you the trick on the spot. You practise it on him. He lets you win. Probably. Later, you are three crowns short. Probably a coincidence.\nopt.right.say.1 [0c1a5b] = “噢，我喜欢你。”他说，当场就把这招教给了你。你拿他练手。他让你赢了。大概吧。后来你发现少了三克朗。大概是巧合。\n\n@event corvin_kitchen_raid\n# en: The Kitchen Raid\ntitle [0e6a0c] = 突袭厨房\n# en: \"Midnight,\" Corvin whispers. \"The cooks have made forty treacle tarts for the faculty dinner. Forty. Nobody needs forty.\"\ntext [ccccc4] = “午夜，”科尔文低声说，“厨师们给教职员晚宴做了四十个糖浆馅饼。四十个。没人需要四十个。”\n# en: Two students creeping through a cavernous castle kitchen at night, a mountain of tarts on a table, a sleeping cook snoring by the hearth.\nscene [c71678] = 夜里两个学生蹑手蹑脚地穿过城堡里洞穴般的大厨房，桌上堆着小山一样的馅饼，炉边的厨师打着呼噜。\n# en: Not tonight\nopt.left.label [4bf72b] = 今晚不行\n# en: He goes alone. The next morning there's a treacle tart on your pillow. You decide not to ask.\nopt.left.say.1 [4aa606] = 他自己去了。第二天早上你的枕头上放着一个糖浆馅饼。你决定不问。\n# en: Lead the raid\nopt.right.label [a419e4] = 带头突袭\n# en: You get out with nine tarts and a bottle of elderflower cordial. You eat them on the roof until sunrise.\nopt.right.say.1 [f86666] = 你们带着九个馅饼和一瓶接骨木花露溜了出来。在屋顶上一直吃到日出。\n# en: The cook wakes up. You both spend a week scrubbing pans, laughing too hard to work.\nopt.right.say.2 [eb0bd4] = 厨师醒了。你们俩刷了一星期的锅，笑得根本干不动活。\n\n@event corvin_debt\n# en: Corvin's Little Problem\ntitle [7d9f5d] = 科尔文的小麻烦\n# en: Corvin has a black eye and a very casual expression. \"Small misunderstanding with some trolls,\" he says. \"Thirty crowns would clear it right up. I'll pay you back. Probably with interest. Possibly with a goat.\"\ntext [6c578d] = 科尔文顶着一只黑眼圈，表情非常随意。“和几个巨魔有点小误会，”他说，“三十克朗就能摆平。我会还你的。大概还带利息。也可能带一只山羊。”\n# en: A boy with a black eye sitting on the dormitory stairs, trying very hard to look casual.\nscene [720349] = 一个顶着黑眼圈的男生坐在宿舍楼梯上，非常努力地装作若无其事。\n# en: Refuse\nopt.left.label [cada35] = 拒绝\n# en: \"Course. No problem.\" He has another black eye the next week. He's still friendly. Mostly.\nopt.left.say.1 [71b32c] = “当然。没问题。”下周他又多了一只黑眼圈。他对你依然友好。大体上。\n# en: Give him thirty crowns\nopt.right.label [902ad2] = 给他三十克朗\n# en: He stares at the coins, then at you. \"No one's ever just... done that.\" He's quiet for a whole minute. A record.\nopt.right.say.1 [9d7807] = 他盯着硬币，又盯着你。“从来没有人就这么……给过。”他安静了整整一分钟。破纪录了。\n\n@event corvin_repays\n# en: Interest\ntitle [8f88f3] = 利息\n# en: There's a fat purse on your desk and a jack of spades tucked under it. It's heavy, and it clinks.\ntext [5a0eec] = 你桌上放着一只鼓鼓的钱袋，下面压着一张黑桃 J。沉甸甸的，叮当作响。\n# en: A small brass key and a fat purse left on a desk with a playing card, the jack of spades, tucked under them.\nscene [476a3b] = 桌上放着一把黄铜小钥匙和一只鼓鼓的钱袋，下面压着一张扑克牌——黑桃 J。\n# en: Count it\nopt.left.label [5fce81] = 数一数\n# en: Sixty crowns. A note: \"Interest. Don't ask. — C.\" You don't ask.\nopt.left.say.1 [f9e2eb] = 六十克朗。一张字条：“利息。别问。——科”你没问。\n# en: Three crowns and a very convincing IOU for the other twenty-seven, with a doodle of a goat.\nopt.left.say.2 [8ec290] = 三克朗，外加一张写着剩下二十七克朗、非常有说服力的欠条，上面还画了只山羊。\n# en: Find him and ask where it came from\nopt.right.label [397662] = 找到他，问钱哪儿来的\n# en: \"I won it,\" he says. \"Fairly. Mostly.\" He pushes the exact thirty into your hand. \"The rest's mine. I've got another debt to pay.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [efb600] = “赢来的，”他说，“公平地。大体上。”他把正好三十克朗塞进你手里。“剩下的是我的。我还有另一笔债要还。”\n\n@event corvin_rooftop\n# en: Up on the Roof\ntitle [6ebee8] = 屋顶上\n# en: On the roof at 2am, Corvin goes quiet. \"I'm on my last warning,\" he says. \"One more detention and they send me home. And home is... not a good place.\" He laughs, badly.\ntext [dc6f28] = 凌晨两点的屋顶上，科尔文沉默了下来。“我已经是最后一次警告了，”他说，“再关一次禁闭，他们就送我回家。而家……不是个好地方。”他笑了，笑得很难看。\n# en: Two students sitting on a steep slate roof under a full moon, legs dangling over a dizzying drop, sharing a bottle of something fizzy.\nscene [802031] = 满月下两个学生坐在陡峭的石板屋顶上，双腿悬在令人眩晕的高处，分享着一瓶冒泡的饮料。\n# en: Tell him to keep his head down\nopt.left.label [b7afec] = 叫他低调点\n# en: \"Head down. Right. I'll try anything once.\" He keeps his head down for almost a month.\nopt.left.say.1 [199c3a] = “低调。好。什么我都愿意试一次。”他低调了将近一个月。\n# en: Promise you'll cover for him\nopt.right.label [f74b9c] = 保证会帮他打掩护\n# en: He looks at you like nobody has ever promised him anything. \"You're an idiot,\" he says. \"Thank you.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [34b1d4] = 他看着你，好像从没有人给过他任何承诺。“你是个傻瓜，”他说，“谢谢你。”\n\n@event corvin_framed\n# en: Corvin Is Framed\ntitle [bd8e3b] = 科尔文被陷害了\n# en: The headmistress's silver goblet has been found in Corvin's trunk. He swears he didn't take it. Bram, who found it, looks very pleased. Corvin is one detention from expulsion.\ntext [7254e4] = 院长的银高脚杯在科尔文的行李箱里被找到了。他发誓不是他拿的。发现它的布拉姆看起来非常得意。科尔文离被开除只差一次禁闭。\n# en: A boy standing before a disciplinary desk, a stolen silver goblet on it, a smug prefect at his shoulder.\nscene [c0253d] = 一个男生站在纪律桌前，桌上放着一只失窃的银高脚杯，一个得意的级长站在他肩旁。\n# en: Stay out of it\nopt.left.label [03d24a] = 不掺和\n# en: Corvin talks his way out, barely. He doesn't look at you for a month.\nopt.left.say.1 [90a1f1] = 科尔文勉强靠嘴皮子脱了身。他一个月没正眼看你。\n# en: Corvin is suspended for a month. When he comes back, he's different. Quieter.\nopt.left.say.2 [506649] = 科尔文被停学一个月。回来时，他变了。更沉默了。\n# en: Find proof he was framed\nopt.right.label [0665b1] = 找出他被陷害的证据\n# en: You find the bill of sale: Bram bought a fake. Bram is stripped of his sash for the rest of the year. Corvin buys you a goat. An actual goat.\nopt.right.say.1 [e722b3] = 你找到了那张收据：是布拉姆买了个赝品。布拉姆被摘掉了绶带，这一年都别想再戴。科尔文送了你一只山羊。真的山羊。\n# en: You can't prove it. But you stood up for him in front of everyone, and he'll never forget it. Neither will Bram.\nopt.right.say.2 [1b083f] = 你证明不了。但你当着所有人的面为他站了出来，他永远不会忘记。布拉姆也不会。\n\n@event corvin_confession\n# en: Corvin, Serious for Once\ntitle [4a31ce] = 难得认真的科尔文\n# en: Corvin holds out a playing card. The queen of hearts. His hand isn't quite steady. \"No trick,\" he says. \"No angle. I just — I like you more than I like anything, and I'm terrified, and I wanted you to have this.\"\ntext [87fdba] = 科尔文递过来一张扑克牌。红桃 Q。他的手有点不稳。“没有把戏，”他说，“没有算计。我只是——我喜欢你，胜过喜欢任何东西，我怕得要命，而且我想把这个给你。”\n# en: A boy holding out a playing card — the queen of hearts — with an unsteady hand, no grin for once.\nscene [e450dd] = 一个男生用不太稳的手递出一张扑克牌——红桃 Q——这一次他没有笑。\n# en: Gently decline\nopt.left.label [975956] = 温柔地拒绝\n# en: He laughs, too quickly. \"Worth a shot.\" He keeps the card. You see it sometimes, tucked in his sleeve.\nopt.left.say.1 [cd0293] = 他笑了，笑得太快。“值得一试。”他留着那张牌。你有时会看见它藏在他的袖子里。\n# en: Take the card\nopt.right.label [e7f785] = 接过那张牌\n# en: For once, Corvin Ashby has nothing to say. He just kisses you, and then grins, and then can't stop grinning.\nopt.right.say.1 [108f24] = 科尔文·阿什比难得地无话可说。他只是吻了你，然后咧嘴笑，然后再也停不下来。\n\n@event corvin_runaway\n# en: Let's Leave Tonight\ntitle [03c686] = 今晚就走吧\n# en: Corvin's at your door at midnight, bag over his shoulder. \"There's a sky-barge at the dock and nobody guarding it. Let's go. Tonight. The world's huge and we're wasting it in classrooms.\"\ntext [c8d466] = 午夜，科尔文背着包站在你门口。“码头上停着一艘天空驳船，没人看守。走吧。今晚。世界那么大，我们却在教室里浪费它。”\n# en: A small sky-barge tied to the academy dock at midnight, lanterns lit, a boy at the tiller holding out his hand.\nscene [6e8955] = 午夜时分，一艘小天空驳船系在学院码头上，灯笼亮着，一个男生站在舵边向你伸出手。\n# en: Stay and finish what you started\nopt.left.label [6b504c] = 留下来，把开始的事做完\n# en: He looks at you for a long time. Then he drops the bag. \"Fine. But after graduation, we steal a boat. A big one.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [d46286] = 他看了你很久。然后放下了包。“行。但毕业以后，我们去偷条船。一条大的。”\n# en: Take his hand\nopt.right.label [0c017b] = 握住他的手\n\n@event mir_intro\n# en: An Explosion in the West Wing\ntitle [dbebf9] = 西翼爆炸\n# en: The west wing explodes. Not badly. A figure in goggles stumbles out of the purple smoke, coughing, and gives you a thumbs-up. \"Don't worry! That was intentional! Mostly! Can you hold this?\" They hand you a ticking teapot.\ntext [61d334] = 西翼炸了。不严重。一个戴护目镜的身影咳嗽着从紫烟里踉跄出来，冲你竖起大拇指。“别担心！是故意的！大体上！能帮我拿一下这个吗？”TA递给你一只滴答作响的茶壶。\n# en: A cloud of purple smoke billowing from a workshop door, a soot-covered student emerging with goggles and a thumbs-up.\nscene [6592e9] = 一团紫烟从工坊门口滚滚涌出，一个满身煤灰、戴着护目镜的学生走出来竖起大拇指。\n# en: Put the teapot down and back away\nopt.left.label [0e8c08] = 放下茶壶，往后退\n# en: The teapot stops ticking and starts humming the academy anthem. \"Mir,\" they say. \"They/them. Thanks for not throwing it.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [e3a008] = 茶壶停止了滴答，开始哼起院歌。“米尔，”TA说，“代词用 TA。谢谢你没把它扔出去。”\n# en: Hold the teapot steady\nopt.right.label [74455a] = 稳稳端住茶壶\n# en: You keep it level while they twist a valve. It stops ticking. \"You've got good hands,\" Mir says. \"I'm going to need those.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [ce47f9] = 你端平茶壶，TA拧了拧一个阀门。滴答声停了。“你的手很稳，”米尔说，“我会需要这双手的。”\n# en: The teapot goes off. Your eyebrows survive, mostly. Mir is apologising and laughing at the same time.\nopt.right.say.2 [3dba78] = 茶壶炸了。你的眉毛基本保住了。米尔一边道歉一边笑。\n\n@event mir_owl_gift\n# en: A Present With Gears\ntitle [940c6d] = 带齿轮的礼物\n# en: Mir presents a brass owl with amber glass eyes. It blinks, hoots the hour and says \"HOMEWORK\" in a tinny voice. \"Made it for you. It remembers deadlines. And birthdays. And grudges, slightly.\"\ntext [1b3dc2] = 米尔拿出一只长着琥珀玻璃眼睛的黄铜猫头鹰。它眨眨眼，报了时，然后用细细的声音说：“作业。”“给你做的。它会记住截止日期。还有生日。还有一点点仇。”\n# en: A small brass owl with glowing amber eyes perched on a student's wrist, its clockwork feathers fluffing.\nscene [0ec99d] = 一只长着发光琥珀眼睛的小黄铜猫头鹰栖在学生的手腕上，发条羽毛蓬松起来。\n# en: It's too much\nopt.left.label [8c1449] = 这太贵重了\n# en: Mir looks crushed, then rallies. \"I'll give it to Thorne. He needs reminding to smile.\" The owl, you hear, refuses to work for him.\nopt.left.say.1 [b9afa1] = 米尔一脸受伤，随即振作起来。“那我送给索恩。他需要有人提醒他笑一笑。”你听说，那只猫头鹰拒绝为他工作。\n# en: Accept it gratefully\nopt.right.label [0fb4bf] = 感激地收下\n# en: The owl settles on your shoulder and says, \"WELCOME.\" Mir pretends to have something in their eye. At six the next morning, the owl says \"HOMEWORK.\" It says it every morning for the rest of the year.\nopt.right.say.1 [4cd7a1] = 猫头鹰落在你肩上，说：“欢迎。”米尔假装眼睛里进了东西。第二天早上六点，猫头鹰说：“作业。”接下来的一整年，它每天早上都这么说。\n\n@event mir_fair\n# en: The Artificers' Fair\ntitle [e5694f] = 奇械博览会\n# en: The Artificers' Fair. Mir's entry is under a sheet and occasionally growls. \"I need an assistant to stand next to it and look confident,\" they say. \"And possibly run.\"\ntext [f589cf] = 奇械博览会。米尔的参赛作品盖在一块布下，时不时发出低吼。“我需要一个助手站在它旁边，看起来很自信，”TA说，“可能还需要跑。”\n# en: A hall crammed with bizarre inventions — walking teapots, singing clocks — and one enormous brass machine draped in a sheet.\nscene [63de4d] = 大厅里挤满了千奇百怪的发明——会走路的茶壶、会唱歌的钟——还有一台盖着布的巨大黄铜机器。\n# en: Watch from a safe distance\nopt.left.label [5c0ef7] = 在安全距离外观看\n# en: The machine turns out to be a mechanical bear that bakes bread. It wins second place. Mir is ecstatic and flour-covered.\nopt.left.say.1 [f14a15] = 那台机器原来是一只会烤面包的机械熊。它拿了二等奖。米尔欣喜若狂，满身面粉。\n# en: Be the confident assistant\nopt.right.label [bfb41b] = 当那个自信的助手\n# en: You present it like a showman. The bear bakes a perfect loaf and bows. First prize. Mir kisses the trophy, then, in a fluster, your cheek.\nopt.right.say.1 [cb3cfc] = 你像个表演家一样介绍它。熊烤出一条完美的面包，还鞠了一躬。一等奖。米尔亲了一下奖杯，然后慌慌张张地，亲了一下你的脸颊。\n# en: The bear eats your sleeve and swats you into the flour bins on its way to the judges. The crowd loves it. Third prize and a very good story.\nopt.right.say.2 [6f8c14] = 熊吃了你的袖子，在走向评委的路上还把你一巴掌拍进了面粉桶。观众爱死了。三等奖，外加一个特别好的故事。\n\n@event mir_workshop_closed\n# en: The Workshop Is Shut\ntitle [61b17b] = 工坊被封了\n# en: After the third fire this term, the faculty has padlocked Mir's workshop. Mir is sitting on a toolbox outside it, looking like someone took their heart and filed it under \"hazards\".\ntext [f5f0a5] = 这学期第三场火灾之后，学院给米尔的工坊上了锁。米尔坐在门外的工具箱上，看起来像有人把TA的心拿走，归档到了“危险品”一栏。\n# en: A workshop door chained shut with a faculty notice nailed to it, a forlorn student sitting on a toolbox outside.\nscene [d57061] = 一扇被铁链锁住的工坊门，上面钉着学院告示，一个失魂落魄的学生坐在门外的工具箱上。\n# en: Plead their case to Professor Thorne\nopt.left.label [fa15a7] = 向索恩教授求情\n# en: Thorne agrees to reopen it — with fire buckets and a weekly inspection. Mir cries on you. Oil and all.\nopt.left.say.1 [f9ab11] = 索恩同意重新开放——条件是配备消防桶并每周检查。米尔抱着你哭了。连油带泪。\n# en: Thorne refuses. But Mir heard you try. \"Nobody's ever argued for me before,\" they say.\nopt.left.say.2 [d56047] = 索恩拒绝了。但米尔听到了你的争取。“从来没有人替我争过。”TA说。\n# en: Help them build a secret workshop\nopt.right.label [4db846] = 帮TA建一个秘密工坊\n# en: An abandoned classroom behind a tapestry. You bring candles; Mir brings everything else. It's perfect. It's almost entirely fireproof.\nopt.right.say.1 [090930] = 挂毯后面一间废弃的教室。你带来了蜡烛；米尔带来了其他所有东西。完美。而且几乎完全防火。\n# en: Bram finds the secret workshop on day two. Worth it, says Mir, scrubbing cauldrons beside you.\nopt.right.say.2 [f9bf9c] = 第二天布拉姆就发现了秘密工坊。值了，米尔一边在你旁边刷坩埚一边说。\n\n@event mir_dance_machine\n# en: The Dancing Machine\ntitle [88d879] = 跳舞机\n# en: Mir has built a machine that teaches dancing. It has six legs and opinions. \"It needs a test subject,\" they say. \"It's mostly safe. It only kicked me twice.\"\ntext [afec3b] = 米尔造了一台教人跳舞的机器。它有六条腿，还有自己的主见。“它需要一个测试对象，”TA说，“基本上是安全的。它只踢了我两次。”\n# en: A spindly brass contraption with too many legs attempting a waltz in an empty classroom, a student dancing with it.\nscene [76ab76] = 空荡荡的教室里，一台腿太多的细长黄铜装置正试图跳华尔兹，一个学生在和它共舞。\n# en: Make Mir test it\nopt.left.label [78abd6] = 让米尔自己测\n# en: You watch Mir and the machine waltz, badly, across the room. It kicks them twice; Mir gives you a look. It's the funniest thing you've ever seen.\nopt.left.say.1 [e8b145] = 你看着米尔和机器笨拙地在教室里跳华尔兹。它踢了米尔两脚；米尔瞪了你一眼。这是你见过最好笑的事。\n# en: Dance with the machine\nopt.right.label [39f27e] = 和机器跳舞\n# en: It kicks you twice too. But by the end you can waltz, and Mir is clapping with oily hands.\nopt.right.say.1 [4fe4d1] = 它也踢了你两次。但最后你学会了华尔兹，米尔用满是油污的手鼓着掌。\n\n@event mir_confession\n# en: Blueprint\ntitle [680200] = 蓝图\n# en: Mir unrolls a blueprint. It's a mechanical heart, labelled in tiny careful handwriting: \"Does not work without the other part.\" They clear their throat. \"I'm bad at saying things. So I drew it. You're the other part. If you want.\"\ntext [f59e74] = 米尔展开一张蓝图。上面是一颗机械心脏，用细小工整的字迹标注着：“缺少另一个部件时无法运转。”TA清了清嗓子。“我不擅长说话。所以我画了出来。你就是那个部件。如果你愿意的话。”\n# en: A rolled-out blueprint on a workbench showing a heart-shaped mechanism labelled with tiny careful notes, a nervous artificer beside it.\nscene [4f0a67] = 工作台上展开一张蓝图，画着心形机械装置并标满细小工整的注释，旁边站着一个紧张的奇械师。\n# en: Kindly say no\nopt.left.label [304162] = 委婉地拒绝\n# en: Mir rolls up the blueprint carefully. \"Good to know. Data is data.\" They're fine. They're mostly fine.\nopt.left.say.1 [457a7a] = 米尔小心地卷起蓝图。“知道了。数据就是数据。”TA没事。TA基本上没事。\n# en: \"I want.\"\nopt.right.label [12191a] = “我愿意。”\n# en: The heart doesn't need to work. But Mir builds it anyway, and it ticks on your windowsill, in time with your own.\nopt.right.say.1 [7bae7b] = 那颗心不需要真的运转。但米尔还是把它造了出来，它在你的窗台上滴答作响，与你的心跳同拍。\n\n@event mir_family_business\n# en: The Family Clockworks\ntitle [a29c6c] = 家族钟表行\n# en: A letter from Mir's parents: the family clockworks needs them home. A ticket is enclosed. \"They think this is a hobby,\" Mir says. \"This. Is. Not. A. Hobby.\"\ntext [51cd75] = 米尔父母来信：家族钟表行需要TA回家。信里附了一张车票。“他们以为这是兴趣爱好，”米尔说，“这。不是。兴趣。爱好。”\n# en: An artificer holding a stiff letter embossed with a gear crest, a train ticket tucked inside, staring at it blankly.\nscene [2991ee] = 一个奇械师拿着一封印有齿轮家徽的硬挺信件，里面夹着一张车票，茫然地盯着它。\n# en: Tell them family comes first\nopt.left.label [170ef7] = 告诉TA家人最重要\n# en: Mir goes home for a month and comes back with a new scar and a clearer jaw. \"They know now,\" they say. \"It's not a hobby. You were right to send me.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [68c5b3] = 米尔回家待了一个月，回来时多了一道新疤，下颌线也更坚定了。“他们现在知道了，”TA说，“这不是兴趣爱好。你让我回去是对的。”\n# en: Help them write back\nopt.right.label [7d4c22] = 帮TA写回信\n# en: Together you spend three evenings, and most of your coursework, on a letter so persuasive it should be framed. Mir's mother replies: \"Fine. Win a prize.\" Mir intends to win all of them.\nopt.right.say.1 [e7acb4] = 你们一起花了三个晚上，搭进去大半功课，写了一封有说服力到该裱起来的信。米尔的母亲回信：“好吧。拿个奖回来。”米尔打算把所有奖都拿回来。\n\n@event nessa_intro\n# en: First Impressions\ntitle [eee0a7] = 第一印象\n# en: A girl with a quill floating at her shoulder plants herself in front of you. \"Nessa Quill, Lumen Ledger. First impressions of Lumenhold, go. Be interesting, please. The last six were boring.\"\ntext [569607] = 一个肩头飘着羽毛笔的女孩往你面前一站。“内莎·奎尔，《流明纪事报》。对流明堡的第一印象，说吧。拜托说点有意思的。前六个都很无聊。”\n# en: A girl with ink-stained fingers and a self-writing quill floating at her shoulder, holding a notebook out expectantly.\nscene [e133f9] = 一个手指沾满墨迹的女孩，肩头飘着一支自动羽毛笔，满怀期待地递出笔记本。\n# en: Give a safe answer\nopt.left.label [6b761a] = 给个稳妥的回答\n# en: The quill writes \"SAFE\" in the margin and underlines it twice. Nessa sighs and moves on.\nopt.left.say.1 [9dd6db] = 羽毛笔在页边写下“稳妥”，还划了两道线。内莎叹了口气，走向下一个人。\n# en: Say something outrageous\nopt.right.label [9ecd42] = 说点惊人之语\n# en: You're quoted on the front page. Half the academy now thinks you're hilarious. The other half thinks you're dangerous. Nessa thinks both.\nopt.right.say.1 [83d022] = 你上了头版。现在半个学院觉得你很搞笑。另一半觉得你很危险。内莎觉得两者都是。\n# en: You are quoted. Out of context. Extremely out of context.\nopt.right.say.2 [8af99f] = 你被引用了。断章取义了。极其断章取义。\n\n@event nessa_gossip_column\n# en: You're in the Gossip Column\ntitle [22e6ad] = 你上了八卦专栏\n# en: This week's Ledger gossip column features you, prominently, under the headline \"WHO IS THE MYSTERY STUDENT ALWAYS IN THE LIBRARY AT MIDNIGHT?\" Nessa swears she didn't write it.\ntext [6890e5] = 本周《纪事报》的八卦专栏以醒目的标题刊登了你：“总在午夜出现在图书馆的神秘学生是谁？”内莎发誓不是她写的。\n# en: A copy of the Lumen Ledger pinned to a noticeboard, a gossip column circled in red ink, students gathered round giggling.\nscene [ab1073] = 布告栏上钉着一份《流明纪事报》，八卦专栏被红墨水圈了出来，学生们围在一起咯咯笑。\n# en: Confront Nessa\nopt.left.label [e29ecc] = 找内莎对质\n# en: She prints a correction. Sort of. It's a much more flattering paragraph about you.\nopt.left.say.1 [4756b3] = 她登了一则更正。算是吧。是一段把你夸得天花乱坠的文字。\n# en: \"Freedom of the press,\" says Nessa, sweetly. You both know she wrote it.\nopt.left.say.2 [8b1a52] = “新闻自由。”内莎甜甜地说。你们都知道是她写的。\n# en: Lean into the mystery\nopt.right.label [0d58af] = 顺水推舟，故弄玄虚\n# en: You start leaving cryptic notes in the library. The column runs for six more weeks. Nessa is delighted.\nopt.right.say.1 [ddffb0] = 你开始在图书馆里留下神秘的字条。这个专栏又连载了六周。内莎乐坏了。\n# en: The column runs for six more weeks. By the fourth it has decided you are haunting the library, and people move tables when you sit down.\nopt.right.say.2 [524d7e] = 专栏又连载了六周。到第四周，它认定你在图书馆闹鬼，你一坐下，别人就换桌子。\n\n@event nessa_source\n# en: The Source\ntitle [86f411] = 线人\n# en: Nessa leans in. \"You've got faculty access. The professors are planning to change the exam format and they're hiding it. I just need a quote. Anonymous. Please?\"\ntext [9e9d3b] = 内莎凑过来。“你能接触到教职员。教授们打算改考试形式，还瞒着大家。我只需要一句引述。匿名的。拜托？”\n# en: Two students in a dark corner of the library, one whispering, the other writing furiously in a notebook.\nscene [7686b3] = 图书馆昏暗的角落里两个学生，一个在低语，另一个在笔记本上奋笔疾书。\n# en: Refuse — that's a breach of trust\nopt.left.label [a7260f] = 拒绝——这是背信\n# en: Nessa sulks for a day. Then she respects you for it, which she hates. You sleep well.\nopt.left.say.1 [06a158] = 内莎生了一天闷气。然后她因此尊重你，这让她很恼火。你睡得很安稳。\n# en: Give her the scoop\nopt.right.label [3204b4] = 把独家消息给她\n# en: The story runs. Thorne knows exactly who talked. His glare could curdle milk.\nopt.right.say.1 [019448] = 报道刊出了。索恩完全清楚是谁说出去的。他的瞪视能让牛奶结块。\n# en: The story runs. Students are grateful. Nobody knows it was you. Nessa buys you cake.\nopt.right.say.2 [baa867] = 报道刊出了。学生们心怀感激。没人知道是你。内莎请你吃了蛋糕。\n\n@event nessa_hollow_story\n# en: Nessa Smells a Story\ntitle [9fa969] = 内莎嗅到了新闻\n# en: Nessa's office wall is covered in red string. In the middle: a sketch of a black star. \"Frozen fountains. Missing pages. A transfer from the North. Something's going on and you know about it. Spill.\"\ntext [f90ecc] = 内莎办公室的墙上缠满了红线。正中间：一幅黑星的速写。“结冰的喷泉。失踪的书页。一个从北方来的转学生。有什么事正在发生，而你知道内情。说吧。”\n# en: A wall in the newspaper office covered in pinned clippings and red string connecting a frozen lake, a black star and a fur-coated boy.\nscene [320fce] = 报社墙上钉满剪报，红线连接着冰封的湖、一颗黑星和一个穿毛皮大衣的少年。\n# en: Warn her off — it's dangerous\nopt.left.label [deabae] = 劝她收手——太危险了\n# en: \"That's what people say right before the best stories.\" But she takes the string down. Mostly.\nopt.left.say.1 [448d70] = “大家都是在最好的新闻出现之前这么说的。”但她还是把红线拆了。大部分。\n# en: Share what you know\nopt.right.label [6ba460] = 告诉她你知道的\n# en: Nessa's article runs with half the facts. The man in grey reads it. So does Bram, who starts following you around.\nopt.right.say.1 [84698a] = 内莎的文章只登出了一半事实。灰衣人读到了。布拉姆也读到了，他开始到处跟着你。\n# en: Nessa's notes are scarily thorough. She's found a record of three students who vanished in 1723. All on Blossom moons.\nopt.right.say.2 [ab1477] = 内莎的笔记详尽得吓人。她找到了一份记录：1723 年有三个学生失踪。全都在花潮之月。\n\n@event nessa_paper_threatened\n# en: Stop the Presses\ntitle [447c01] = 停印\n# en: The headmistress has threatened to shut down the Ledger after an article about faculty salaries. Nessa is organising a protest. \"I need people on the steps. Visible people. You.\"\ntext [2ab439] = 一篇关于教职员薪水的文章刊出后，院长威胁要关停《纪事报》。内莎正在组织抗议。“我需要有人站在台阶上。显眼的人。你。”\n# en: A printing press with a faculty seal stamped across it, a defiant student standing in front of it with arms crossed.\nscene [94267d] = 一台被盖上学院封印的印刷机，一个倔强的学生抱着双臂站在它前面。\n# en: Stay out of it\nopt.left.label [03d24a] = 不掺和\n# en: The protest is small. The Ledger survives, barely. Nessa notices who wasn't there.\nopt.left.say.1 [98928f] = 抗议规模很小。《纪事报》勉强保住了。内莎记住了谁没来。\n# en: Stand on the steps\nopt.right.label [1c328c] = 站上台阶\n# en: Forty students on the steps, chanting. The headmistress comes out and, astonishingly, listens, looking straight at you the whole time, and not warmly. The Ledger stays.\nopt.right.say.1 [adae4f] = 四十个学生站在台阶上高呼口号。院长走了出来，出人意料地，她听了，而且自始至终直直地看着你，目光并不温和。《纪事报》保住了。\n\n@event nessa_confession\n# en: Off the Record\ntitle [a5d3b6] = 不予记录\n# en: Nessa puts down her quill. Then she tells it to stop writing. It's the first time you've seen it still. \"This is off the record,\" she says. \"Completely. I like you. I've drafted this eleven times. None of the drafts were good enough.\"\ntext [b22faa] = 内莎放下了羽毛笔。然后命令它停止书写。这是你第一次看见它静止不动。“这段不予记录，”她说，“完全不记。我喜欢你。这段话我打了十一遍草稿。没有一稿够好。”\n# en: A girl with an inkpot in one hand, her self-writing quill hovering above a blank page that she has commanded to stay blank.\nscene [b44415] = 一个女孩一手拿着墨水瓶，她的自动羽毛笔悬在一张空白的纸上方——她命令那张纸保持空白。\n# en: Gently say no\nopt.left.label [f46b92] = 温柔地拒绝\n# en: \"Noted,\" she says crisply. She keeps her quill still a moment longer. Then it starts writing again, and so does she.\nopt.left.say.1 [3ad266] = “记下了。”她干脆地说。她让羽毛笔多静止了一会儿。然后它重新开始书写，她也是。\n# en: Kiss her\nopt.right.label [5fa79c] = 吻她\n# en: Her quill, unattended, writes a single word in the margin: \"FINALLY.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [995169] = 无人看管的羽毛笔在页边写下了两个字：“终于。”\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/pools/castle_halls.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/pools/castle_halls.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event courtyard_sundial_bench\n# en: The Slow Sundial\ntitle [eef7f9] = 慢吞吞的日晷\n# en: {There's a bench by the old sundial, which runs eleven minutes slow.|Someone has scratched a tiny \"+11\" by the dial's noon. A fellow sufferer.|The sundial bench is yours by custom. A first-year asked if you came with it.} {season == \"winter\": Frost fills the hour lines, and the shadow skates across them.|{Pigeons are holding a meeting on the gnomon.|A first-year checks a watch against it, horrified.|Someone has chalked a face where noon should be.}}\ntext [211578] = {老日晷旁有一张长椅。这日晷慢十一分钟。|有人在晷面正午旁刻了个小小的“+11”。同病相怜的人。|日晷长椅按惯例归你了。有个一年级生问过，你是不是和它配套的。} {season == \"winter\": 霜填满了时刻刻线，影子在上面滑冰。|{一群鸽子正在晷针上开会。|一个一年级生拿怀表对它，吓坏了。|有人在正午的位置用粉笔画了张脸。}}\n# en: A stone bench beside a weathered sundial in a flagstone courtyard, a student reading with their back to the dial.\nscene [549ef0] = 石板庭院里，一张石凳挨着一座饱经风霜的日晷，一个学生背对晷面读书。\n# en: Read until the shadow moves\nopt.left.label [7b2740] = 读到影子挪动为止\n# en: {season == \"winter\": {Your fingers go numb by page two. The chapter is worth it.|You read in gloves, turning pages with your teeth.|You read in your own bench's frost-free corner. A first-year brings you a hot chestnut, as a tribute to the bench.}|{You finish a chapter in the minutes the sundial owes you, squinting into the glare.|A pigeon reads over your shoulder, unimpressed, and your neck has a crick in it by the end.|The shadow crawls; you outpace it by three pages and a stiff back.}}\nopt.left.say.1 [47ae9e] = {season == \"winter\": {读到第二页手指就冻麻了。这一章值得。|你戴着手套读，用牙齿翻页。|你在自己那条长椅上不结霜的角落里读书。一个一年级生给你送来一颗热栗子，算是向长椅致敬。}|{日晷欠你的那几分钟，够你眯着眼迎着日光读完一章。|一只鸽子凑在你肩头一起读，不以为然；读完时你的脖子也僵了。|影子慢慢爬，你领先它三页，背也坐僵了。}}\n# en: Close your eyes a while\nopt.right.label [64f7ca] = 闭眼歇一会儿\n# en: {You wake with a chalk moustache and no suspects.|You wake to a prefect clearing his throat an inch from your ear.|You wake rested and late, eleven minutes late by the dial.}\nopt.right.say.1 [1d321d] = {你醒来时脸上多了粉笔胡子，却找不到嫌疑人。|你醒来时，一位级长正贴着你耳边清嗓子。|你醒来时神清气爽，也迟到了——按日晷算，正好迟到十一分钟。}\n\n@event courtyard_saved_seat\n# en: A Seat on the Rim\ntitle [c87795] = 喷泉边的座位\n# en: {{here} has claimed the dry stretch of the fountain rim and waves you over.|{here} is on your favourite stretch of the fountain rim, the one the sun reaches first, and waves you over.|Your stretch of the fountain rim is known now. {here} is sitting on it and shifts over without being asked.} {Two towers' bells are arguing about the hour.|Pigeons circle like creditors.|{season == \"winter\": Your breath hangs between you.|Someone's lunch smells better than yours.}}\ntext [a68fe1] = {{here}占了喷泉边沿上干燥的一段，朝你招手。|{here}坐在你最喜欢的那段喷泉边沿上——太阳最先照到的那段——朝你招手。|喷泉边沿上你的那一段如今人尽皆知。{here}正坐在上面，不等你开口就挪了挪。} {两座塔的钟正为几点钟争执不休。|鸽子像债主一样盘旋。|{season == \"winter\": 你们之间呵出的白气悬在半空。|别人的午饭闻起来比你的香。}}\n# en: Two students sitting on the dry edge of a courtyard fountain, bags between them, pigeons circling overhead.\nscene [9547e3] = 两个学生坐在庭院喷泉干燥的边沿上，书包放在中间，鸽子在头顶盘旋。\n# en: Sit down and catch up\nopt.left.label [ebe7f0] = 坐下聊聊\n# en: {Corvin tells you three stories, one of them true, and calls you \"saint\" when you don't ask which.|Corvin spots a prefect, palms something off the rim, and goes on talking as if nothing happened.|Corvin shifts over and hands you half of something he shouldn't have. \"Your seat, saint. I kept it warm. Illegally.\"}\nopt.left.say.1 [96bb2a] = {科尔文给你讲了三个故事，只有一个是真的；你没问是哪个，他便叫你“圣人”。|科尔文瞥见一位级长，从边沿上顺走了什么，接着若无其事地往下说。|科尔文挪了挪，递给你半块他本不该有的东西。“你的座，圣人。我替你捂热了。非法地。”}\n# en: {Nessa interviews you about the weather, the soup and your love life, in that order, and writes down all three.|Nessa reads you tomorrow's headline and asks if it's too cruel. It is. She keeps it.|Nessa shifts over without looking up from her notes. \"You're in tomorrow's column,\" she says. \"Nicely, for once.\"}\nopt.left.say.2 [3929a9] = {内莎就天气、汤和你的感情生活依次采访了你，三样都记了下来。|内莎给你念明天的头条，问是不是太刻薄了。是的。她决定照登。|内莎头也不抬地挪了挪位置。“你上了明天的专栏，”她说，“难得是好话。”}\n# en: {Bram lists every rule broken in the courtyard while you sit. Then, gruffly, he asks how your exams went.|Bram writes a demerit for a passing first-year, then tears it up when he thinks you aren't looking.|Bram shifts over with great dignity and informs you the seat is technically unassigned. Then he guards it while you fetch a cider.}\nopt.left.say.3 [f1eb70] = {你坐着的工夫，布拉姆把庭院里违反的校规一条条数了一遍。然后，他粗声粗气地问你考得怎么样。|布拉姆给路过的一年级生记了一次过，以为你没看见时又把单子撕了。|布拉姆郑重其事地挪了挪，通知你这个座位严格来说不属于任何人。然后在你去买苹果酒时替你守着它。}\n# en: {You trade the castle's news for an hour. {here} does most of the trading.|You talk until the shadows cross the fountain. Neither of you can remember about what.|You sit on your stretch of the rim and catch up. {here} already knows half your news and makes you tell it anyway.}\nopt.left.say.4 [eb16ba] = {你们交换了一小时城堡新闻，大多是{here}在说。|你们一直聊到影子爬过喷泉，谁也想不起聊了些什么。|你坐在你那段池沿上叙旧。你的消息{here}已经知道一半了，可还是让你从头讲。}\n# en: The cider from the cart by the gate is on you.\nopt.left.say.5 [e9717b] = 城门边小推车上的苹果酒，算你请的。\n# en: Wave, and keep walking\nopt.right.label [ff1be9] = 挥挥手，继续走\n# en: {You promise next time and take your book to the library instead. {here} looks as if {they} {are} counting.|You spend the hour with a book instead. It is a good book; it is not much company.|You wave and keep walking. {here} stays on your stretch of the rim, which feels like a small theft.}\nopt.right.say.1 [5791d7] = {你说下次一定，拿着书去了图书馆。{here}看上去像是在记账。|你改和一本书待了一小时。书很好，只是不太能陪人。|你挥挥手，继续走。{here}留在了你那段池沿上，感觉像被偷了一小样东西。}\n\n@event courtyard_seasonal_game\n# en: The Courtyard Game\ntitle [bfcc17] = 庭院游戏\n# en: {season == \"winter\": A snowball war between Emberhall and Stillwater has reached the treaty stage.|{season == \"spring\": A charmed ball is loose, dodging everyone who kicks at it.|{season == \"summer\": It's too hot for sport, so it's catch with a charmed apple that bites.|It's conker season. The champion conker is said to be older than the fountain.}}} {Someone shouts for one more player.|They shout for you by name.|Both sides shout for you, and start bidding.}\ntext [d0e0af] = {season == \"winter\": 烬厅和静水之间的雪仗已经打到了签订和约的阶段。|{season == \"spring\": 一只施了魔法的球在庭院里乱窜，谁去踢就躲谁。|{season == \"summer\": 天太热，没法运动，于是改玩抛接一只会咬人的魔法苹果。|七叶树果大战的季节到了。据说那颗冠军果比喷泉还老。}}} {有人喊着还差一个人。|这回他们直接喊你的名字。|两边都在喊你，还开始竞价。}\n# en: Students playing a rowdy game across a flagstone courtyard while others crowd the edges shouting bets.\nscene [9846ca] = 学生们在石板庭院里玩得热火朝天，旁边围着的人大声下注。\n# en: Join in\nopt.left.label [a87be8] = 加入\n# en: {Your side wins, and the result is disputed until supper.|You score the deciding point and are carried three whole steps before they drop you.|You join the side that bid more. They win, and the other side says you were bought, which you were.}\nopt.left.say.1 [d99628] = {你们这边赢了，比分一直争到晚饭。|你拿下了制胜一分，被大家抬着走了整整三步，然后摔了下来。|你加入了出价更高的那边。你们赢了，另一边说你是被收买的，确实如此。}\n# en: {You are flattened, politely, by both sides at once.|You spend the rest of the game as the thing the other side aims at.|Both sides think you're on theirs. Both sides find out, at once, in the middle of the courtyard.}\nopt.left.say.2 [5f9187] = {你被两队人马同时、礼貌地压扁了。|余下的比赛里，你成了对方瞄准的那个东西。|两边都以为你是自己人。两边同时发现了真相，就在庭院正中央。}\n# en: Hold the coats and the bets\nopt.right.label [175f1b] = 帮忙看外套和赌注\n# en: {The favourite loses. You are the only person in the courtyard who is pleased about it.|The underdog wins by a whisker, and the coats you are holding are suddenly lighter on your conscience.|You hold the coats and both sides' bids. The underdog wins. You are, briefly, the richest person in the courtyard.}\nopt.right.say.1 [f80232] = {大热门输了。整个庭院里只有你一个人高兴。|冷门险胜，你手里抱着的外套忽然让你良心轻了不少。|你抱着外套，也替两边保管着赌注。冷门赢了。有那么一会儿，你是庭院里最有钱的人。}\n# en: {The favourite wins, and so does everyone who backed them, out of your pocket.|Everyone bet the same way, and everyone was right. You pay out in a hail of small coins.|The favourite wins, and both sides had bet on it through you. You pay out twice, which shouldn't be possible.}\nopt.right.say.2 [42e100] = {大热门赢了，押它的人也都赢了——钱从你兜里出。|大家都押了同一边，也都押对了。你在一阵小硬币的冰雹里赔了钱。|大热门赢了，而两边都是通过你押的它。你赔了两次，这本不该发生。}\n# en: The favourite wins. With nothing in your pocket to pay out, you're chased twice round the fountain and lose a coat button to the crowd.\nopt.right.say.3 [69835b] = 热门赢了。你口袋里一个子儿也没有，赔不出钱，被人追着绕喷泉跑了两圈，还在人堆里丢了一颗外套扣子。\n\n@event library_wandering_book\n# en: The Book That Moved\ntitle [fe710d] = 会挪窝的书\n# en: {Yesterday your book was on the third floor. Today the third floor has never heard of it.|Your book has moved again, leaving a bookmark in the gap, like a note.|You keep a map of where the shelves hide your books. They've started hiding the map.} {In its place is a shelf of cookery spells.|In its place are bound weather reports.|In its place is one slim volume, face out, waiting.} {final_year: Soon they'll play these games with someone else.|}\ntext [cfc482] = {昨天你要的书还在三楼。今天三楼说从没听说过它。|你的书又挪走了，空位上留了一张书签，像一张字条。|你画了一张书架藏书的地图。书架开始藏那张地图了。} {原位上是一架烹饪咒语。|原位上是一排装订好的天气报告。|原位上只有一本薄薄的书，封面朝外，像在等你。} {final_year: 很快，它们就要和别人玩这种游戏了。|}\n# en: Tall library shelves leaning very slightly, a gap where one book should be, a student on a ladder peering into it.\nscene [d131b1] = 高高的书架微微倾斜，本该放一本书的地方空着，一个学生站在梯子上往缝里瞧。\n# en: Hunt it down\nopt.left.label [e1addf] = 把它找出来\n# en: {You find it on the sixth floor, filed under \"regret\".|You find it in the stairwell, halfway between floors, as if it had been trying to leave.|You find it at once. Either you are learning the shelves, or they are getting lazy.}\nopt.left.say.1 [05a6d5] = {你在六楼找到了它，归在“悔恨”类下。|你在楼梯间找到了它，卡在两层之间，好像它正打算出走。|你一下子就找到了。要么是你摸透了书架，要么是书架变懒了。}\n# en: Read what's in front of you\nopt.right.label [5bdb99] = 读手边这本\n# en: {You read the wrong book with great conviction, and quote it at supper.|You learn a great deal about something nobody will ever ask you about.|The wrong book turns out to be a very good novel. You read all of it, and none of it will be on any exam.}\nopt.right.say.1 [bb68b5] = {你满怀信心地读了一本错的书，晚饭时还引用了它。|你学到了大量永远不会有人问起的知识。|读错的书原来是一本很好的小说。你把它读完了，而里面没有一个字会出现在考试里。}\n\n@event library_shared_table\n# en: The Last Free Chair\ntitle [694ef7] = 最后一把空椅子\n# en: {The library is full. The only free chair is opposite {here}.|The only free chair is opposite {here}. A shelf has leaned over it, as if keeping it for you.|The library knows your habits now. The free chair, opposite {here}, has a book you wanted waiting on it.|The chair opposite {here} is so reliably free that you suspect the library of arranging it.} {Rain ticks on the tall windows.|A shelf nearby is quietly rearranging itself.|Footnote is asleep in the returns basket.|{season == \"winter\": The radiators clank like a ghost with a grudge.|Dust turns gold in the light.}}\ntext [ce55ed] = {图书馆满了。唯一的空椅子在{here}对面。|唯一的空椅子在{here}对面。一个书架斜靠在椅子上方，像在替你占座。|图书馆已经摸清了你的习惯。{here}对面那把空椅子上，放着一本你想看的书在等你。|{here}对面的椅子总是空着，空得太可靠，你怀疑是图书馆安排的。} {雨点敲着高窗。|附近的书架正悄悄自己重新排列。|“脚注”睡在还书筐里。|{season == \"winter\": 暖气片哐哐作响，像个怀恨在心的幽灵。|灰尘在光里变成金色。}}\n# en: A crowded library reading room, every table full but one, a single empty chair facing a student absorbed in a book.\nscene [b58e03] = 拥挤的图书馆阅览室，所有桌子都坐满了，只有一把空椅子，对面是个埋头读书的学生。\n# en: Take the chair and work\nopt.left.label [4d48da] = 坐下来用功\n# en: {Kael doesn't look up. By the end of the hour there is frost on your inkwell and a tiny, careful correction in your margin.|Kael pushes a page of his notes across to you, then looks away as if it had moved on its own.|Kael moves his inkwell to make room for yours. By the end, there's frost on both, and a note in his hand: \"Your third line.\"|Kael says one sentence in the whole hour. It's the answer to the question you were stuck on. He goes back to reading.}\nopt.left.say.1 [fbd64b] = {凯尔没有抬头。一小时后，你的墨水瓶上结了霜，页边多了一处细小工整的订正。|凯尔把一页笔记推到你面前，然后移开视线，仿佛那页纸是自己挪过去的。|凯尔挪开墨水瓶，给你的腾出地方。到最后，两只墨水瓶上都结了霜，还有一张他写的字条：“你的第三行。”|凯尔一个钟头里只说了一句话。那正是你卡住的那道题的答案。他接着看书。}\n# en: {Lyra slides the candle an inch toward your side without looking up. Neither of you mentions it.|Lyra finishes before you, and waits, and says she wasn't waiting.|Lyra corrects your notation without asking and is right. You correct hers and are also right. Neither of you mentions it.|Lyra works at your pace for once, and you finish together. She says it was coincidence, twice.}\nopt.left.say.2 [6bcf1c] = {莱拉头也不抬，把蜡烛往你这边推了一寸。你们谁也没提。|莱拉比你先写完，却等着你，还说她没在等。|莱拉没问就改了你的记法，改得对。你改了她的，也改得对。你们谁都没提。|莱拉难得和你同步，你们一起写完了。她说那是巧合，说了两遍。}\n# en: {Seraphine writes a word in your notes that you won't understand until next week.|Seraphine reads the same page for an hour, smiling at something that isn't on it.|Seraphine slides you a book you hadn't asked for. You'll need it on Thursday, it turns out.|Seraphine works in silence, then says your name once, softly, as if checking it was still there.}\nopt.left.say.3 [06a254] = {瑟拉芬在你的笔记上写了一个词，你要到下周才会明白。|瑟拉芬对着同一页读了一小时，冲着一件不在书上的事微笑。|瑟拉芬把一本你没要过的书推给你。结果你星期四正好用得上。|瑟拉芬默默用功，然后轻轻念了一声你的名字，好像在确认它还在。}\n# en: {Bram informs you that you breathe too loudly. Then he lends you his ruler.|Bram underlines every rule in his book twice. Once, he asks your opinion of one.|Bram shares his lamp and lectures you on its proper use. The lecture is long. The lamp is very good.|Bram reads your essay upside down from across the table and finds the mistake. He's insufferable about it, and right.}\nopt.left.say.4 [3cbff8] = {布拉姆通知你：你呼吸太大声了。然后他把尺子借给了你。|布拉姆把书里每一条规则都划了两道线。有一次，他问你对其中一条怎么看。|布拉姆和你共用他的灯，还就它的正确用法给你上了一课。课很长。灯很好。|布拉姆隔着桌子倒着读你的论文，找出了错处。他为此得意得让人受不了，可他是对的。}\n# en: {You work in silence for an hour, which turns out to be a kind of company.|Neither of you speaks. Both of you get further than you would have alone.|You work opposite {here} as if you'd planned to. By the end you've both got further, and nodded once.|You share the table for the hour and the cat for the last ten minutes. It's a good arrangement.}\nopt.left.say.5 [1c2a28] = {你们默默用功了一小时，原来这也算一种陪伴。|你们谁也没说话，却都比一个人时走得更远。|你在{here}对面用功，就像早就约好了。到最后你们都比原来走得更远，彼此点了一下头。|一个钟头里你们共用一张桌子，最后十分钟还共用了一只猫。这安排不错。}\n# en: Whisper a question\nopt.right.label [1952d6] = 小声问个问题\n# en: {The conversation is worth it. The look from the front desk has a long memory.|You get three sentences in before a shush arrives from two floors up.|You whisper your question to {here} and get a whispered answer. The front desk lets it pass, this once.|Your whisper wakes the cat in the returns basket, who complains loudly. You are shushed for the cat's noise.}\nopt.right.say.1 [ea8730] = {这番交谈很值。只是前台投来的那一眼，记性很长。|你刚说了三句，两层楼上就传来一声“嘘”。|你小声向{here}提了个问题，得到了一个小声的回答。前台这回放过了你们。|你的低语吵醒了还书筐里的猫，它大声抗议。你却因为猫的动静挨了一声“嘘”。}\n\n@event library_overdue\n# en: Overdue\ntitle [40f62b] = 逾期\n# en: {The book you borrowed in a hurry is overdue, and the library knows it. The spine has started to sigh.|Another overdue book. The front desk has begun keeping a little card with your name on it.|Your overdue books sigh in chorus now, and the card at the front desk has a second side.} {Footnote watches you approach the returns basket with professional interest.|The front desk is, for once, unattended.|A queue of first-years is paying fines in small, sad coins.}\ntext [3a56cd] = {你匆忙借的那本书逾期了，图书馆心知肚明。书脊开始叹气。|又一本逾期的书。前台开始为你单独留一张写着你名字的小卡片。|你的逾期书现在齐声叹气，前台那张卡片已经写到了背面。} {“脚注”以专业的兴趣看着你走近还书筐。|前台难得没人。|一队一年级生正用小小的、可怜的硬币交罚款。}\n# en: A student holding a book behind their back near a wicker returns basket, a cat on the front desk watching them.\nscene [9136c2] = 一个学生把书藏在身后，靠近一只柳条还书筐，前台上的猫盯着他们。\n# en: Own up and pay the fine\nopt.left.label [1ffaa0] = 认错交罚款\n# en: {The fine is paid; the desk, mollified, lends you the sequel.|You pay, and are lent another book, which you suspect is a trap.|The desk sighs, takes your coins and hands you a book it says you'll like. It is always right, which is the annoying part.}\nopt.left.say.1 [e1d0b0] = {罚款交了，前台消了气，把续集借给了你。|你交了钱，又被借了一本书，你怀疑这是个圈套。|前台叹口气，收下硬币，递给你一本说你会喜欢的书。它总是对的，这才让人恼火。}\n# en: You own up with empty pockets. The desk lets you work the fine off reshelving, which takes all evening, and lends you the sequel anyway.\nopt.left.say.2 [6ba749] = 你两手空空地去认错。前台让你靠整理书架抵罚款，整整花了一个晚上，还照样把续集借给了你。\n# en: Slip it into the basket\nopt.right.label [aa0b65] = 偷偷塞进还书筐\n# en: {The book goes in without a sound. It stops sighing at once, which feels like forgiveness.|You drop it in under cover of a sneeze. Footnote saw. Footnote will keep it to himself, for a price.|You slip it in while the first-years pay their fines. Nobody notices one more sad sound.}\nopt.right.say.1 [ca2e56] = {书悄无声息地滑了进去，立刻不再叹气，像是原谅了你。|你借着一个喷嚏把书丢了进去。“脚注”看见了。“脚注”会替你保密——有代价的。|你趁一年级生们交罚款时把书塞了进去。多一声难过的声音，没人注意。}\n# en: {The book announces itself with a sigh like a dying organ. Everyone turns. You pay double.|It lands with a groan. The front desk adds a mark to your card, and the fine, doubled.|The book sighs in harmony with your other overdue books. The desk adds a third side to your card.}\nopt.right.say.2 [fe215d] = {那本书发出一声垂死管风琴般的叹息，宣告了自己的到来。所有人都回过头。你交了双倍罚款。|书落进筐里，呻吟了一声。前台在你的卡片上又记了一笔，罚款翻倍。|那本书和你的其他逾期书一起和声叹气。前台给你的卡片添了第三面。}\n# en: The book announces itself with a sigh like a dying organ. Everyone turns. With nothing to pay, you're put on reshelving until the fine is worked off twice over.\nopt.right.say.3 [cb93a1] = 那本书发出一声垂死管风琴般的叹息，宣告了自己的到来。所有人都回过头。你没钱可付，只好去整理书架，直到把双倍罚款抵完。\n\n@event refectory_late_supper\n# en: Late Supper\ntitle [095a3c] = 晚来的晚饭\n# en: {You come in late. The benches are empty and the cooks are wiping the far tables.|The cook leaves a lamp lit at the end of the bench now, where the draught can't find you.|Your late bowl is waiting under a plate, with a spoon laid beside it. Nobody admits to it.} {year == 1: Home feels very far away tonight.|{final_year: You count the suppers you have left, and stop.|{The candles are down to stubs.|Someone has left a half-done puzzle on the next table.|The cook is humming in the kitchen behind the hatch.}}}\ntext [aeeedf] = {你来晚了。长凳都空着，厨工们在擦远处的桌子。|厨娘现在会在长凳尽头、穿堂风找不到的地方给你留一盏灯。|你那碗晚饭扣在盘子下等着你，旁边摆好了勺子。没人承认是谁放的。} {year == 1: 今晚，家显得格外遥远。|{final_year: 你数了数还剩几顿这样的晚饭，然后不数了。|{蜡烛快烧到底了。|有人在隔壁桌上留了一幅拼了一半的拼图。|厨娘在窗口后的厨房里哼着歌。}}}\n# en: The far end of a long refectory table at night, most benches empty, a single student with a bowl and a half-written letter.\nscene [0600ae] = 深夜膳堂长桌的尽头，大部分长凳空着，一个学生面前放着一只碗和一封写了一半的信。\n# en: Write a letter home\nopt.left.label [67bcd4] = 给家里写信\n# en: {You write two pages and post them with the last of your coppers.|You write about the food. You do not write about anything else, but they'll know.|You write a letter that is mostly questions. You post it before you can make it braver.}\nopt.left.say.1 [bbe078] = {你写了两页，用最后几枚铜板寄了出去。|你写了饭菜，别的什么都没写，但他们会懂的。|你写了一封几乎全是问题的信，趁自己还没想把它写得更勇敢之前寄了出去。}\n# en: Revise over the bowl\nopt.right.label [5fbeee] = 边吃边复习\n# en: {You learn three dates and a spill of gravy across all of them.|The cook reads over your shoulder and corrects one of your answers. She is right.|You revise over your bowl. The cook takes the plate away when you've finished and leaves a second helping.}\nopt.right.say.1 [99e02c] = {你记住了三个年份，还有一摊洒在上面的肉汁。|厨娘从你肩后看了一眼，改正了你的一个答案。她是对的。|你对着碗复习。你吃完后，厨娘把盘子收走，又留下了第二份。}\n\n@event refectory_house_tables\n# en: Where to Sit\ntitle [3b38cd] = 坐哪儿\n# en: {Supper, and the question of where to sit.|Your house table has started saving a gap on the bench, roughly your width.|The gap is a named seat now, and the quiet table nods at you too.} Your house table is {loud with a story about a goat|in the middle of a pudding treaty|singing, badly}. At the far end, {season == \"winter\": some second-years from Rootward huddle round a warming charm.|some second-years from Duskgate are arguing quietly about a map.}\ntext [f8a7d4] = {晚饭时间，又是那个问题：坐哪儿。|你们学院那桌开始在长凳上留一个空位，差不多你那么宽。|那个空位现在有了名字，安静的那桌也会朝你点头了。} 你们学院那桌{正在大声讲一个关于山羊的故事|正在谈判一份布丁和约|正在唱歌，唱得很难听}。远处那头，{season == \"winter\": 几个根庐的二年级生围着一个取暖咒挤成一团。|几个暮门的二年级生正低声争论一张地图。}\n# en: A crowded refectory at suppertime, a student standing with a tray between a loud house table and a quieter one of strangers.\nscene [6196b3] = 晚饭时拥挤的膳堂，一个学生端着托盘站在一张喧闹的学院长桌和一张陌生人的安静桌子之间。\n# en: Squeeze in with your house\nopt.left.label [956401] = 挤进自己学院那桌\n# en: {You eat with an elbow in your ribs and laugh the whole way through.|Somebody's story gets better every time you hear it. So does the pudding.|You squeeze into your gap and join the singing, badly, to fit in. It works.}\nopt.left.say.1 [7c3c22] = {你肋下顶着别人的胳膊肘吃完了饭，从头笑到尾。|有人的故事每听一遍就更精彩一点，布丁也是。|你挤进你的空位，跟着唱，唱得很差，以示合群。效果很好。}\n# en: Sit with the strangers\nopt.right.label [88cc69] = 和陌生人坐一起\n# en: {They are polite, then curious, then actually rather good company.|It is awkward for exactly one course. By pudding, they have told you their secret map route.|The quiet table makes room as if you'd always sat there. They ask about your house's singing, with sympathy.}\nopt.right.say.1 [542ca6] = {他们先是客气，然后好奇，然后竟成了相当不错的伙伴。|尴尬只持续了一道菜。到布丁时，他们已经把秘密地图路线告诉了你。|那桌安静的人给你让出位置，像你一直就坐在那儿。他们同情地问起你们学院的歌声。}\n\n@event refectory_pudding_tax\n# en: The Pudding Tax\ntitle [03d210] = 布丁税\n# en: {{friend} sits down opposite you and looks at your pudding the way a cat looks at a fish.|Your pudding has a reputation now. {friend} sits down opposite you with a spoon already in hand.|Word of your pudding tax has got round the refectory. Tonight {friend} comes to collect, with a little receipt.} {season == \"winter\": Tonight it's treacle sponge, steaming.|{Tonight it's apple crumble.|Tonight it's a custard that wobbles with intent.|Tonight it's jam roly-poly, much defended.}}\ntext [277329] = {{friend}在你对面坐下，看你的布丁就像猫看鱼。|你的布丁如今声名在外。{friend}在你对面坐下，勺子已经握在手里。|你的布丁税已经在膳堂传开了。今晚{friend}来收税，还带着一张小收据。} {season == \"winter\": 今晚是热气腾腾的糖浆海绵布丁。|{今晚是苹果酥。|今晚是一份意图不明地颤动着的蛋奶冻。|今晚是果酱卷布丁，戒备森严。}}\n# en: Two friends at a refectory table, one guarding a bowl of treacle pudding, the other holding a spoon at the ready.\nscene [2f960a] = 膳堂桌边的两个朋友，一个护着一碗糖浆布丁，另一个举着勺子随时准备出手。\n# en: Pay the pudding tax\nopt.left.label [7c4562] = 交布丁税\n# en: {Tobias apologises four times, eats it all, then tells you a long story about a goat who did the same. It helps, somehow.|Tobias eats half, then pushes the bowl back with a sprig of mint from his pocket on top, as an apology.|Tobias pays you back with a receipt of his own: half a jar of honey from home. You call it even.}\nopt.left.say.1 [47bf0f] = {托比亚斯道了四次歉，把它吃得一干二净，然后给你讲了个长长的故事，说一只山羊也干过这种事。不知怎么，这让你好受了些。|托比亚斯吃了一半，把碗推回来，上面放着一枝从口袋里掏出的薄荷，算是赔礼。|托比亚斯用他自己的“收据”还了你：半罐家里带来的蜂蜜。你们算两清了。}\n# en: {\"Efficient,\" Lyra says, finishing it. The next day a whole pudding appears on your tray. She doesn't mention it.|Lyra eats it in four exact bites and critiques the custard. She does not give it back.|Lyra presents the receipt, takes exactly the tax and not a crumb more. She is, you have to admit, scrupulous.}\nopt.left.say.2 [71588b] = {“高效。”莱拉吃完说道。第二天，你的托盘上出现了一整份布丁。她只字未提。|莱拉精确地四口吃完，还点评了蛋奶酱。她没还给你。|莱拉出示收据，不多不少正好拿走税额，一点渣也不多拿。你不得不承认，她一丝不苟。}\n# en: {Mir eats it while explaining a pudding-defence device that would probably work. Probably.|Mir eats it with a spoon they built themselves, which hums. They say the humming is probably normal.|Mir hands you a receipt they've engraved on brass, collects, and promises a refund mechanism by spring.}\nopt.left.say.3 [694d5c] = {米尔边吃边讲解一种防布丁被偷的装置，说大概能用。大概。|米尔用自己做的勺子吃，那勺子嗡嗡作响。TA说嗡嗡响大概是正常的。|米尔递给你一张刻在黄铜上的收据，收了税，承诺春天前做出退款装置。}\n# en: {{friend} eats exactly half and leaves you the burnt corner.|{friend} eats it slowly, savouring your defeat.|{friend} collects the tax and writes \"paid\" on the receipt. You're starting to suspect the receipts are the point.}\nopt.left.say.4 [10b26e] = {{friend}正好吃了一半，把烤焦的那角留给了你。|{friend}慢慢地吃，细细品味你的失败。|{friend}收了税，在收据上写上“已付”。你开始怀疑收据才是重点。}\n# en: Defend your pudding\nopt.right.label [c73449] = 捍卫你的布丁\n# en: {You hide it under a napkin and point at the window. It works. {friend} will remember this.|You claim it's been sneezed on. {friend} retreats in disgust, and you eat in peace.|You refuse to recognise the receipt. {friend} appeals to the table, which rules in your favour, narrowly.}\nopt.right.say.1 [b3831f] = {你把它藏在餐巾下，指着窗外。成功了。{friend}会记住这一笔的。|你声称有人对着它打过喷嚏。{friend}嫌恶地退却，你安心吃完。|你拒不承认那张收据。{friend}向全桌申诉，全桌以微弱多数判你胜诉。}\n# en: {{friend} sees straight through you and takes the whole thing as a penalty. It is hard to stay cross.|{friend} distracts you with a story and the bowl is empty before it ends.|You dispute the receipt. {friend} produces a second receipt for the dispute, and takes the pudding as costs.}\nopt.right.say.2 [a94a04] = {{friend}一眼看穿了你，把整份没收作为罚款。很难一直生气。|{friend}用一个故事分散你的注意力，故事还没讲完碗就空了。|你对收据提出异议。{friend}又拿出一张收据，专门针对这次异议，并把布丁收作诉讼费。}\n\n@event lecture_tower_old_board\n# en: Someone Else's Lecture\ntitle [108850] = 别人的课\n# en: {term: {The fourth-floor hall is empty, but someone else's lecture is still on the board.|In the empty hall, a stub of chalk waits on the ledge, as if for you.|The board has a corner fenced off in chalk, labelled \"answers here\".}|In the break the boards are wiped, except one nobody dares touch.} {Today it's a ward diagram, labelled \"wrong, but how?\"|Today it's half a proof, ending in a thumbprint.|Today it's a list headed \"on the exam\", crossed out.}\ntext [91923d] = {term: {四楼的讲堂空着，黑板上却还留着别人的课。|空讲堂里，一截粉笔头在槽里等着，像是留给你的。|黑板角上用粉笔圈出一块，标着“答案写这里”。}|假期里黑板都擦干净了，只有一块没人敢碰。} {今天是一幅护界图，标注着“错了，但错在哪？”|今天是半个证明，结尾是一枚指纹。|今天是一张题为“会考的内容”的清单，被划掉了。}\n# en: An empty spiral lecture hall, a blackboard still dense with chalk diagrams from a class that has long gone.\nscene [b21ec8] = 空荡荡的螺旋讲堂，黑板上还密密麻麻留着一堂早已散场的课的粉笔图解。\n# en: Copy it all down\nopt.left.label [71d09a] = 全部抄下来\n# en: {Your hand cramps. Your head, for once, does not.|You copy it all and understand most of it. The rest you'll grow into.|You copy the board, fenced corner and all. Your answer from last time is still there, with a tick beside it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [337380] = {你的手抽筋了。你的脑子却难得没有。|你全抄了下来，大部分都懂。剩下的，你会慢慢长进去。|你把黑板连同那个围起来的角落一起抄下。你上次写的答案还在，旁边多了一个勾。}\n# en: Add your own answer\nopt.right.label [b05ba7] = 写上你的答案\n# en: {Next time, someone has added \"yes!\" under your line.|Someone has answered your answer. The board has become a correspondence.|By the afternoon someone has drawn a box around your line, to keep it safe.}\nopt.right.say.1 [9a51e5] = {下次再来，有人在你那行下面加了个“对！”|有人回答了你的回答。黑板成了一场书信往来。|到了下午，有人在你那行字周围画了个框，好好护着。}\n# en: {The professor whose board it was finds you holding the chalk. \"Hm,\" says the look.|Your answer is wiped by the time you reach the door, by someone who knew better.|You write in the \"answers here\" corner. The next morning there's a line through it, and \"nearly\", underlined.}\nopt.right.say.2 [92a4ef] = {那块黑板的主人——一位教授——撞见你手里拿着粉笔。那一眼在说：“嗯。”|你还没走到门口，你的答案就被一个更懂行的人擦掉了。|你在“答案写这里”的角落写了。第二天早上，上面被划了一道，旁边写着“差一点”，还加了下划线。}\n\n@event lecture_tower_stair_question\n# en: One Question a Flight\ntitle [d64af4] = 一层楼一个问题\n# en: {You meet Professor Thorne on the spiral stair. He allows one question per flight, and climbs fast.|Professor Thorne on the stair. He has slowed down by about one step in seven, which is how you know he expects you.|Thorne hands you half his stack of papers at the foot of the stair, without a word, and starts climbing.} {term: |The tower is half empty for the break; your footsteps echo twice.}{final_year: There are not many of these climbs left.|}\ntext [fa2b42] = {你在螺旋楼梯上遇见索恩教授。他允许每层楼问一个问题，而且爬得很快。|楼梯上的索恩教授。他每七级台阶慢下一级，你由此知道他在等你。|索恩在楼梯口一言不发地把一半文件塞给你，然后开始往上爬。} {term: |假期里塔楼空了一半，你的脚步声有两重回音。}{final_year: 这样的攀爬已经不剩几次了。|}\n# en: A narrow spiral stair inside a stone tower, a severe professor climbing ahead with a stack of papers, a student a step behind.\nscene [0a9608] = 石塔里狭窄的螺旋楼梯，一位严厉的教授抱着一摞文件走在前面，一个学生落后一级台阶。\n# en: Ask a hard question\nopt.left.label [7531ee] = 问个难题\n# en: {He answers in four words, and the four words are enough.|\"Hm,\" he says, then answers it properly, all the way to the seventh floor.|You carry half his papers and ask your question on the fourth flight. He answers on the seventh, and it was worth the wait.}\nopt.left.say.1 [2020c3] = {他只用四个字回答，而这四个字就够了。|“嗯。”他说，然后认真地回答了，一直讲到七楼。|你抱着他的一半文件，在第四层提了问。他在第七层才回答，值得等。}\n# en: {He answers with a harder question and leaves you on the landing.|\"Wrong question,\" he says. \"Find the right one by Thursday.\"|He takes his papers back on the landing. \"Better question next time,\" he says, which means he'll be expecting one.}\nopt.left.say.2 [8660d6] = {他用一个更难的问题作答，把你留在了楼梯平台上。|“问错了。”他说，“星期四之前找到对的问题。”|他在平台上拿回了文件。“下次问个更好的，”他说，意思是他会等着。}\n# en: Climb in silence beside him\nopt.right.label [f05732] = 默默陪他爬楼\n# en: {He climbs faster than you. At the top he says, \"Adequate,\" and you are fairly sure he means the climbing.|Neither of you says a word for seven flights. It is oddly restful, apart from your legs.|You carry his papers in silence. At the top he takes them back and says, \"Thursday,\" which is an invitation.}\nopt.right.say.1 [03a653] = {他爬得比你快。到了顶上，他说：“尚可。”你相当确定他指的是爬楼。|七层楼里你们一句话也没说。除了腿，这出奇地让人放松。|你默默地替他抱着文件。到顶后他接回去，说了句“星期四”，那是邀请。}\n\n@event lecture_tower_scorched_room\n# en: The Scorched Room\ntitle [b134c8] = 烧焦的练习室\n# en: {The practice room on the Evocation floor is empty. A sign forbids unsupervised casting.|The scorched room is empty. Since your last visit, someone has underlined the sign.|The scorched room. The sign is underlined three times now, and signed by the headmistress.} {A fresh scorch mark is shaped like a surprised face.|Someone has left a bucket of sand by the door.|{season == \"winter\": The walls are the only warm thing here.|The air smells faintly of singed eyebrow.}}\ntext [6ca0fe] = {塑能术那层的练习室空着。告示禁止无人监督施法。|烧焦的练习室空着。自你上次来过，有人在告示下划了线。|烧焦的练习室。告示上现在划了三道线，还有院长的签名。} {一道新焦痕的形状像一张吃惊的脸。|有人在门边放了一桶沙子。|{season == \"winter\": 这里只有墙是暖的。|空气里隐约有烧焦眉毛的味道。}}\n# en: A practice room on a lower floor of a stone tower, walls streaked with scorch marks, a sign by the door forbidding unsupervised casting.\nscene [5b6deb] = 石塔低层的一间练习室，墙上满是焦痕，门边的告示禁止无人监督施法。\n# en: Practise anyway\nopt.left.label [5b8fa1] = 照练不误\n# en: {Your spell lands clean in the centre of the oldest scorch mark. You leave no evidence.|You practise until the flame does what you mean, not what you say.|You practise under the headmistress's signature. Your spell is very good. You leave before it's tempted to be better.}\nopt.left.say.1 [681bca] = {你的咒语正中最古老那道焦痕的中心，没留下任何证据。|你一直练到火焰照你的意思行事，而不是照你的话。|你在院长的签名底下练习。你的咒语很好。在它想变得更好之前，你走了。}\n# en: {A new scorch mark joins the wall, shaped very like you.|The spell comes back at you. You leave with your eyebrows, mostly.|Your scorch mark lands right under the headmistress's signature. You'd swear the signature looks disappointed.}\nopt.left.say.2 [22262c] = {墙上添了一道新焦痕，形状很像你。|咒语反弹到你身上。你离开时眉毛还在——大部分。|你的焦痕正好落在院长的签名下面。你敢发誓那签名看起来很失望。}\n# en: Work through the theory\nopt.right.label [0d1f5b] = 钻研理论\n# en: {You draw the spell on paper fifty times. Paper is very forgiving.|You learn exactly why the walls look like that, and resolve not to add to them.|You work the theory under the thrice-underlined sign. You'll cast it properly, supervised, and it will be perfect.}\nopt.right.say.1 [bc9305] = {你在纸上把咒语画了五十遍。纸很宽容。|你彻底弄懂了墙为什么是这副模样，并决心不再给它添彩。|你在那块划了三道线的告示下面钻研理论。等到有人监督时你会好好施放，那时它会完美无缺。}\n\n@event great_hall_tomorrow_sky\n# en: Tomorrow's Weather\ntitle [fdb53c] = 明天的天气\n# en: {The great hall is empty but for the candles. The ceiling shows tomorrow.|One candle drifts over to you and hovers at reading height.|Your candle meets you at the door now and leads you to your bench.} {season == \"winter\": Tomorrow, it says, it will snow.|{season == \"spring\": Tomorrow, a storm with bad manners.|{season == \"summer\": Tomorrow is cloudless, stars and all.|Tomorrow is fog, rolling across the vaults.}}} {final_year: There are fewer tomorrows here now.|}\ntext [e5b36c] = {大礼堂里空无一人，只有蜡烛。天花板在放映明天。|一支蜡烛飘到你身边，停在适合读书的高度。|你的蜡烛现在会在门口迎你，把你领到你的长凳。} {season == \"winter\": 天花板说，明天要下雪。|{season == \"spring\": 明天，一场不讲礼貌的暴风雨。|{season == \"summer\": 明天万里无云，满天星斗。|明天是雾，在拱顶间缓缓滚过。}}} {final_year: 这里剩下的明天不多了。|}\n# en: An empty great hall in the evening, floating candles drifting under a vaulted ceiling that shows a different sky from the windows.\nscene [85f031] = 傍晚空荡的大礼堂，漂浮的蜡烛在拱顶下飘移，天花板上的天空与窗外不同。\n# en: Lie on a bench and watch\nopt.left.label [e7d231] = 躺在长凳上看\n# en: {You watch tomorrow until your neck stiffens. It is worth it.|A candle drifts down to see if you are all right, and stays.|You fall half asleep and dream tomorrow a little early.}\nopt.left.say.1 [a177d2] = {你看着明天，直到脖子发僵。值得。|一支蜡烛飘下来看你是否安好，然后就留下了。|你半睡半醒，提前梦见了一点明天。}\n# en: Read by the floating candles\nopt.right.label [e66c83] = 借烛光读书\n# en: {A candle drips wax onto the best paragraph. You read it anyway, through the wax.|The candles follow your page like curious moths. It is the best light in the castle.|Your candle sits on the bench beside you like a friend. The light is steady, and you read further than you meant to.}\nopt.right.say.1 [ec7422] = {一支蜡烛把蜡滴在了最好的那段上。你隔着蜡照读不误。|蜡烛像好奇的飞蛾一样跟着你的书页。这是城堡里最好的光。|你的蜡烛像朋友一样待在你身边的长凳上。光很稳，你读得比打算的更远。}\n\n@event great_hall_choir_rehearsal\n# en: The Choir Rehearses\ntitle [2fc949] = 合唱团排练\n# en: {The choir is rehearsing on the stage, a foot off the floor, and one voice short.|Still a voice short. The conductor has left a spare songbook on your bench.|The spare songbook has your name pencilled in it, and the alto line marked in red.} {season == \"winter\": They are learning the Midwinter music.|{Tonight it's a round that nobody can find the end of.|Tonight it's a lament, sung far too cheerfully.|Tonight it's a song for the lake, sung very softly.}}\ntext [9d02cd] = {合唱团在舞台上排练，离地一尺，还差一个声部。|还是差一个声部。指挥在你的长凳上放了一本备用歌本。|那本备用歌本上用铅笔写了你的名字，女低音声部用红笔标了出来。} {season == \"winter\": 他们在学仲冬的曲子。|{今晚是一首没人找得到结尾的轮唱。|今晚是一首挽歌，唱得未免太欢快了。|今晚是一首唱给湖的歌，唱得很轻很轻。}}\n# en: A choir rehearsing on the stage of a candlelit great hall, a few singers floating a foot off the boards, the benches mostly empty.\nscene [f55935] = 烛光摇曳的大礼堂舞台上，合唱团正在排练，几位歌手离地漂浮一尺，长凳上几乎空着。\n# en: Listen from the back\nopt.left.label [73c47c] = 在后排听\n# en: {The collection hat comes round. It is worth every coin.|You listen until the candles gutter. The hat comes round twice.|You listen from the back with your name pencilled into a book you're not singing from. It's the best of both.}\nopt.left.say.1 [8391f5] = {募捐的帽子传了过来。每一枚硬币都值。|你一直听到蜡烛摇摇欲灭。帽子传了两回。|你在后排听着，手里那本没用来唱的歌本里用铅笔写着你的名字。两全其美。}\n# en: Fill the missing voice\nopt.right.label [2ec625] = 补上那个声部\n# en: {You hit every note but one. The conductor says it was the best one.|You float a little off the floor on the last chord. Nobody mentions it, which is how you know.|You sing your marked alto line and the choir is whole. The conductor doesn't say anything, and doesn't need to.}\nopt.right.say.1 [af0466] = {你只错了一个音。指挥说那是最好的一个。|最后一个和弦时你微微离了地。没人提起，你就知道了。|你唱起标好的女低音声部，合唱团完整了。指挥什么也没说，也不需要说。}\n# en: {You come in confidently, in the wrong key, in the wrong song. The choir is very kind about it, which is worse.|You sing the round in a straight line and finish alone. The conductor thanks you for your enthusiasm.|You sing the red line, which is the soprano line, it turns out. The choir carries you, out of kindness.}\nopt.right.say.2 [5f53e1] = {你信心满满地进了拍——调不对，歌也不对。合唱团对此非常宽容，这更糟。|你把轮唱唱成了一条直线，最后一个人唱完。指挥感谢你的热情。|你唱了那条红线，结果那是女高音声部。合唱团出于好心带着你唱完。}\n\n@event great_hall_high_table_dare\n# en: The High Table Dare\ntitle [09ad02] = 高桌之赌\n# en: {Someone dares you to sit in the headmistress's chair at the empty high table.|The dare comes round again, and someone has started keeping a tally in chalk on the doorframe.|The tally on the doorframe has your column and everyone else's. First-years dare each other to dare you.} {The chair is still warm.|A cushion embroidered with an owl sits on it.|Her teacup has been left behind, still steaming, which is worse.}\ntext [3bd135] = {有人赌你不敢坐到空着的高桌上，坐院长的椅子。|这个赌又来了，有人开始在门框上用粉笔记分。|门框上的记分表里有你的一栏和其他所有人的。一年级生们互相怂恿着来赌你。} {椅子还是温的。|椅子上放着一个绣着猫头鹰的坐垫。|她的茶杯落在那儿，还冒着热气，这更糟。}\n# en: An empty high table on a dais at the end of a dark great hall, a carved chair at its centre, students daring each other from the doorway.\nscene [788e3e] = 昏暗大礼堂尽头的高台上，一张空着的高桌，中央是一把雕花椅子，学生们在门口互相起哄。\n# en: Take the dare\nopt.left.label [6cb2fd] = 接下赌约\n# en: {You sit, cross your legs, and sip the air. The doorway erupts.|You sit for a full minute. The chair is surprisingly comfortable, and very slightly warm.|You take the dare, and lift the steaming teacup, and drink. Nobody in the doorway breathes. It's very good tea.}\nopt.left.say.1 [8a9adf] = {你坐下，跷起腿，对着空气啜了一口。门口炸开了锅。|你坐了整整一分钟。椅子出奇地舒服，还微微有点暖。|你接下赌约，端起那杯冒着热气的茶，喝了一口。门口没人敢出气。茶非常好。}\n# en: {As you sit, a quiet, delighted laugh comes from the dark beyond the dais. You leave very fast.|A portrait above the table clears its throat. You have never moved so fast in your life.|You reach for the steaming teacup and a voice behind you says, \"That's mine.\" You don't look round. You simply leave.}\nopt.left.say.2 [ffe96a] = {你刚坐下，高台后的黑暗里传来一声轻轻的、愉快的笑。你飞快地离开了。|高桌上方的一幅肖像清了清嗓子。你这辈子从没跑得这么快过。|你伸手去拿那杯冒着热气的茶，身后一个声音说：“那是我的。”你没回头。你直接走了。}\n# en: Tell them you value your life\nopt.right.label [bbde75] = 说你还想活命\n# en: {They call you a coward, fondly. You keep your dignity, and your place at the academy.|\"Coward,\" they chant. You bow. It seems the safest thing to do in that hall.|You decline, and point out the steaming cup. The first-years go very quiet, and so does the dare.}\nopt.right.say.1 [cd4ef0] = {他们亲切地骂你胆小鬼。你保住了尊严，也保住了学籍。|“胆小鬼！”他们齐声喊。你鞠了一躬。在那间礼堂里，这似乎是最安全的做法。|你拒绝了，还指了指那杯冒着热气的茶。一年级生们一下子安静了，赌约也跟着安静了。}\n\n@event infirmary_spare_bed\n# en: The Spare Bed\ntitle [f2835c] = 空床位\n# en: {You only came in for a plaster.|Your name is on the slate by the end bed, in pencil.|Your name on the slate is in ink now.} {health < 40: Sister Amaranth takes one look at you and points at the spare bed.|Sister Amaranth decides, over her spectacles, that you'll live.} {It smells of lavender and singed wool.|Two beds down, someone hiccups in a minor key.|{season == \"winter\": A warming charm hums under every blanket.|The curtains breathe.}}\ntext [babfb3] = {你只是来要块膏药的。|尽头那张床边的石板上，用铅笔写着你的名字。|石板上你的名字现在是用墨水写的了。} {health < 40: 阿玛兰斯修女看了你一眼，指了指那张空床。|阿玛兰斯修女从眼镜上方打量你，判定你死不了。} {屋里有薰衣草和烧焦羊毛的味道。|隔两张床，有人在用小调打嗝。|{season == \"winter\": 每条毯子底下都嗡嗡响着取暖咒。|窗帘在呼吸。}}\n# en: A row of white infirmary beds under a high arched window, one turned down, a stern healer pointing at it.\nscene [6baaff] = 高拱窗下一排白色病床，其中一张已铺开，一位严厉的医护指着它。\n# en: Lie down for an hour\nopt.left.label [a6694f] = 躺一个钟头\n# en: {You sleep the deep, dreamless sleep of the infirmary. The tonic afterwards costs a few crowns and tastes of regret.|You wake under a blanket you didn't pull up. There is a tonic on the side, and a bill under it.|Your name is on the slate in ink, so you take the end bed without asking. The tonic is waiting, and so is the bill.}\nopt.left.say.1 [e69f26] = {你睡了一场医务室式的、无梦的深眠。醒来后的补药要几个克朗，味道像悔恨。|你醒来时身上盖着一条不是你拉的毯子。床头有一瓶补药，下面压着账单。|石板上你的名字是用墨水写的，所以你没问就躺到了最边上那张床。补药已经备好，账单也是。}\n# en: Help roll bandages\nopt.right.label [a9dc7c] = 帮忙卷绷带\n# en: {Sister Amaranth teaches you the knot she uses. You will never tie your boots any other way.|You roll bandages and listen to her opinion of duelling, which is long and specific.|You roll bandages as if you work here. Sister Amaranth says that if you're going to be here this often you may as well.}\nopt.right.say.1 [b86073] = {阿玛兰斯修女教你她打的那种结。从此你系鞋带再也不用别的结。|你一边卷绷带，一边听她对决斗的看法——又长又具体。|你卷绷带的样子像在这儿上班。阿玛兰斯修女说，既然你来得这么勤，不如干脆在这儿上班。}\n\n@event infirmary_visiting_hour\n# en: Visiting Hour\ntitle [d29dca] = 探视时间\n# en: {Visiting hour. {friend} is in the bed by the window,|Visiting hour. Sister Amaranth waves you to the stool you used last time. Today it's {friend},|Visiting hour. The stool by the window has worn to your shape. Today it's {friend},} {nursing a duelling bruise the colour of plums.|recovering from a draught of hiccups that hiccups back.|wrapped in three blankets against a lake chill.} {They} brighten{s} when you come in.\ntext [f59c0b] = {探视时间。{friend}躺在靠窗的床上，|探视时间。阿玛兰斯修女朝你上回坐的凳子招招手。今天是{friend}，|探视时间。窗边那只凳子已经磨成了你的形状。今天是{friend}，} {正护着一块李子色的决斗淤青。|正从一剂会反过来打嗝的打嗝药里恢复。|正裹着三条毯子抵御湖上的寒气。} 看见你进来，{they}眼睛一亮。\n# en: A friend propped up in an infirmary bed by a window, a visitor on a stool beside them with a paper bag of grapes.\nscene [3e7207] = 一位朋友靠坐在窗边的病床上，探视者坐在旁边的凳子上，手里拿着一纸袋葡萄。\n# en: Sit and keep {them} company\nopt.left.label [036ca8] = 坐下陪陪{them}\n# en: {You bring grapes and eat most of them yourself. {friend} doesn't mind.|You stay until Sister Amaranth sweeps you out. {friend} waves from the pillows.|You sit on your worn stool and tell {friend} everything they've missed. {They} fall{s} asleep halfway, smiling.}\nopt.left.say.1 [37af3a] = {你带了葡萄，自己吃了大半。{friend}并不介意。|你一直待到阿玛兰斯修女把你扫地出门。{friend}在枕头间朝你挥手。|你坐在你那张磨旧的凳子上，把{friend}错过的事都讲给{them}听。{they}听到一半就睡着了，还带着笑。}\n# en: Smuggle in a pastry\nopt.right.label [1dcbe0] = 偷带一块点心\n# en: {The pastry makes it past the desk. {friend} eats it under the blanket like a crime.|You hide the pastry in a hollowed-out book. {friend} is delighted by both.|You smuggle in a hot pastry under your coat. {friend} eats it wrapped in all three blankets and declares you a hero.}\nopt.right.say.1 [93dc84] = {点心顺利混过了前台。{friend}躲在毯子底下吃，像在作案。|你把点心藏在一本挖空的书里。{friend}对两样都很开心。|你把一个热点心藏在外套底下带了进来。{friend}裹着三条毯子吃完，宣布你是英雄。}\n# en: {Sister Amaranth confiscates the pastry and eats it herself, slowly, while looking at you.|Sister Amaranth smells the pastry from across the ward and holds out her hand without a word.|Sister Amaranth takes the pastry, cuts it in three, and gives you, {friend} and herself a piece each. Then she lectures you both.}\nopt.right.say.2 [032f46] = {阿玛兰斯修女没收了点心，然后看着你，慢慢地自己吃掉了。|阿玛兰斯修女隔着整个病房就闻到了点心味，一言不发地伸出手。|阿玛兰斯修女拿走点心，切成三块，你、{friend}和她自己各一块。然后把你们俩都训了一顿。}\n\n@event infirmary_tonic_cupboard\n# en: The Tonic Cupboard\ntitle [f80f9b] = 补药柜\n# en: {The tonic cupboard is open, and Sister Amaranth is in the back having her tea.|The tonic cupboard is open at teatime. A new label on the door reads \"counted daily\".|At teatime the cupboard is open, and the label now reads \"counted daily, by name\". The bright blue bottles have moved to the front.} {mana < 20: Your head aches with the dry ache of spent magic.|{The bottles clink in the draught.|A label reads, in her handwriting, \"absolutely not\".|The bright blue bottles have a new lock.}}\ntext [8d3dac] = {补药柜开着，阿玛兰斯修女在里屋喝茶。|下午茶时间，补药柜开着。门上新贴了一张标签：“每日清点”。|下午茶时间，柜门开着，标签现在写着“每日清点，按人登记”。亮蓝色的瓶子都挪到了前排。} {mana < 20: 你的头隐隐作痛，是法力耗尽后那种干涩的疼。|{瓶子在穿堂风里叮当作响。|一张标签上是她的字迹：“绝对不行”。|那些亮蓝色的瓶子上了一把新锁。}}\n# en: A tall glass-fronted cupboard of coloured tonics in an empty infirmary, its door ajar, a teacup steaming on the healer's desk.\nscene [e6e707] = 空荡荡的医务室里，一个装满彩色补药的高玻璃柜，柜门半开，医护的桌上一杯茶正冒着热气。\n# en: Help yourself to a tonic\nopt.left.label [3899be] = 自己拿一瓶\n# en: {You drink it in the corridor. It tastes of peppermint and getting away with things.|You pocket the bottle, rinse it later, and put it back full of water. You feel slightly worse about that.|You take a blue one from the front, since that's where they've been put. You suspect that was the point.}\nopt.left.say.1 [121e38] = {你在走廊里喝了它。薄荷味，外加侥幸得手的味道。|你把瓶子揣进兜里，后来洗净，灌满清水放了回去。这让你心里更过意不去了一点。|你从最前排拿了一瓶蓝的，既然它们被摆在那儿。你怀疑这正是用意。}\n# en: {\"That one is for goats,\" says Sister Amaranth from the doorway. She makes you drink the right one, which is worse.|Sister Amaranth's teacup appears at your elbow. \"Put it back,\" she says, \"and sit down.\"|The blue bottles were put at the front to see who'd take one. Sister Amaranth writes your name in the \"counted daily\" book.}\nopt.left.say.2 [c812eb] = {“那瓶是给山羊的。”阿玛兰斯修女在门口说。她让你喝下对的那瓶，这更糟。|阿玛兰斯修女的茶杯出现在你手肘边。“放回去，”她说，“然后坐下。”|那些蓝瓶子被摆到前排，就是为了看谁会拿。阿玛兰斯修女把你的名字写进了“每日清点”的本子。}\n# en: Ask for one properly\nopt.right.label [8a2146] = 好好开口要\n# en: {She charges you, lectures you, and gives you the good one.|She charges you, lectures you a little less, and gives you the good one.|\"You again,\" she says, and hands it over before you ask.}\nopt.right.say.1 [381f3d] = {她收了钱，训了你一顿，给了你好的那瓶。|她收了钱，训得少了些，给了你好的那瓶。|“又是你。”她说，没等你开口就递了过来。}\n# en: She looks at your empty purse, gives you the good one anyway, and lectures you twice as long.\nopt.right.say.2 [58433e] = 她看了看你空空的钱袋，照样给了你好的那瓶，训话却长了一倍。\n\n@event alchemy_lab_spare_cauldron\n# en: A Spare Cauldron\ntitle [9b8e1b] = 一口空坩埚\n# en: {There is a spare cauldron on the second bench, unclaimed.|The spare cauldron has a chalk mark on its rim like your initial.|Your cauldron, everyone calls it. Someone has scratched your initial into the handle.} {Someone has left a ladle standing upright in something.|The bench smells of mint and burnt sugar.|{season == \"summer\": The heat makes every brew twice as eager.|The windows have steamed over with other people's experiments.}}\ntext [ae405d] = {第二张工作台上有一口空坩埚，没人认领。|那口空坩埚的边沿上有个粉笔记号，像你名字的首字母。|大家都管它叫“你的坩埚”。有人把你的首字母正经刻在了把手上。} {有人把一把长勺竖着插在某种东西里。|工作台上有薄荷和焦糖的味道。|{season == \"summer\": 炎热让每锅药剂都格外急躁。|窗户被别人的实验蒸得一片模糊。}}\n# en: A long laboratory bench in the afternoon light, a small cauldron free among larger ones, jars of ingredients lined up beside it.\nscene [eac0fa] = 午后阳光里一张长长的实验台，大坩埚之间空着一口小坩埚，旁边排着一列药材罐。\n# en: Brew a practice batch\nopt.left.label [7eec57] = 熬一锅练手\n# en: {The brew turns the right colour at the right moment. It feels like being trusted.|It comes out clear and smells of nothing, which is exactly right.|You brew in your cauldron, by your initial. It knows your hand by now and doesn't argue.}\nopt.left.say.1 [89c735] = {药剂在恰好的时刻变成了恰好的颜色。那感觉像是被信任。|成品清澈、无味，正是该有的样子。|你在刻着你缩写的坩埚里熬药。它已经熟悉你的手法，不再跟你较劲。}\n# en: {The brew turns a colour you have never seen. So do your eyebrows.|It foams over and sets, firmly, around your sleeve.|Even your own cauldron turns on you today. The brew sets, and your initial is the only part of the handle you can see.}\nopt.left.say.2 [7e93ad] = {药剂变成了一种你从没见过的颜色，你的眉毛也是。|它沸溢出来，牢牢地凝固在你的袖子上。|今天连你自己的坩埚都和你作对。药剂凝固了，手柄上只露出你刻的缩写。}\n# en: Scrub the cauldrons for crowns\nopt.right.label [cb89c5] = 刷坩埚挣几个钱\n# en: {You learn more about failed potions from their residue than from any lecture.|You scrub until the bench is clean. Then you scratch your own initials into it, small, under the rim.|You scrub every cauldron and yours last, with care. It shines. Someone else uses it the next morning, and you mind.}\nopt.right.say.1 [874eee] = {从失败药剂的残渣里，你学到的比任何一堂课都多。|你把工作台刷得干干净净，然后在边沿下面小小地刻上了自己的名字缩写。|你把每口坩埚都刷了，最后才仔细刷你自己的。它亮闪闪的。第二天早上别人用了它，你很介意。}\n\n@event alchemy_lab_third_floor\n# en: The Third-Floor Fumes\ntitle [fa28aa] = 三楼的烟雾\n# en: {On the third floor the ventilation charm has given up, and the fumes have taken charge.|Someone has hung a sign by the stairs giving the fumes' mood of the day.|The fumes' sign has a column for forecasts now, in several hands.} {Everyone is speaking in rhyme and trying to hide it.|Everyone is laughing at nothing and cannot stop.|Everyone is being unusually honest about everyone else's hair.} {season == \"winter\": The windows are frozen shut.|}\ntext [aa2f98] = {三楼的通风咒已经放弃了，烟雾接管了这里。|有人在楼梯口挂了块牌子，写着烟雾今天的心情。|烟雾的牌子现在多了一栏预报，好几种笔迹。} {所有人都在用押韵说话，还想掩饰。|所有人都在莫名其妙地笑，停不下来。|所有人都对别人的发型异常坦诚。} {season == \"winter\": 窗户冻死了。|}\n# en: A crowded upper floor of an alchemy laboratory, coloured vapour hanging under the ceiling, students working with handkerchiefs tied over their faces.\nscene [51e9da] = 炼金实验室拥挤的楼上，彩色的蒸气悬在天花板下，学生们脸上蒙着手帕干活。\n# en: Stay and work in the fug\nopt.left.label [d14428] = 留在烟里干活\n# en: {You get a great deal done and agree with everyone about everything.|You leave with good notes and a headache that hums.|You work through the honesty fumes and hear what everyone thinks of your hair. Your notes are excellent. Your hair is fine.}\nopt.left.say.1 [6775d1] = {你干了一大堆活，而且对所有人的所有话都表示赞同。|你带着一份好笔记和一阵嗡嗡响的头疼离开。|你在“说实话”的烟雾里干活，听到了每个人对你发型的看法。你的笔记很出色。你的头发没问题。}\n# en: Force the windows open\nopt.right.label [0d2916] = 把窗户撬开\n# en: {season == \"winter\": {The ice cracks. Half the floor cheers; half complains of the cold.|The frozen latch gives, and so does your thumbnail.|The window cracks open and the cold rushes in. Everyone stops being honest at once, with relief.}|{The windows give. The fumes leave, sulking, and the floor applauds.|You wedge the window with a textbook. The fumes drift out, and nearly the textbook too.|You open the window and the honest fumes leave. The silence that follows is extremely polite.}}\nopt.right.say.1 [015c79] = {season == \"winter\": {冰裂开了。半层楼欢呼，另一半抱怨冷。|冻住的插销开了，你的指甲也裂了。|窗户裂开一道缝，冷风灌了进来。大家立刻都不再说实话了，松了口气。}|{窗户开了。烟雾悻悻离去，整层楼鼓掌。|你用一本课本卡住窗户。烟雾飘了出去，课本也差点跟着出去。|你打开窗，那股让人说实话的烟雾散了。随后的沉默极其礼貌。}}\n\n@event alchemy_lab_unlabelled_jar\n# en: The Unlabelled Jar\ntitle [a3c74d] = 没贴标签的罐子\n# en: {Someone has left an unlabelled jar on the shared shelf. It glows, faintly.|Another unlabelled jar on the shared shelf, beside the cautious label someone wrote for the last one.|The shared shelf now has a row of careful labels, and one new jar at the end of it, waiting its turn.} {It smells of thunder.|Something inside it turns over when you look.|It is warm to the touch.} {gpa >= 3: You could probably identify it, given time.|You have no idea what it is.}\ntext [23e27f] = {有人在公用架上落下一只没贴标签的罐子。它微微发光。|公用架上又一只没贴标签的罐子，旁边是有人给上一只写的谨慎标签。|公用架上现在排着一列仔细写好的标签，末尾一只新罐子在排队等着。} {它闻起来像雷声。|你一看它，里面就有东西翻了个身。|摸上去是温的。} {gpa >= 3: 给你点时间，你大概能认出它。|你完全不知道它是什么。}\n# en: A dusty shelf of ingredient jars in an alchemy laboratory, one jar with no label glowing faintly, a student reaching for it.\nscene [328708] = 炼金实验室里一排积灰的药材罐，其中一只没有标签、微微发光，一个学生正伸手去拿。\n# en: Add a pinch to your brew\nopt.left.label [163e45] = 往药里加一撮\n# en: {The brew turns gold and so do you, briefly. You feel enormous.|The brew sings a single note and leaves you humming with power.|A pinch of the warm stuff and your brew purrs like a cat. You don't know what it did, but it's better.}\nopt.left.say.1 [0900d9] = {药剂变成金色，你也短暂地变成了金色。你觉得自己无比庞大。|药剂唱出一个音，让你浑身嗡嗡作响，充满力量。|加了一撮那温热的东西，你的药剂像猫一样咕噜起来。你不知道它起了什么作用，但确实更好了。}\n# en: {The brew ignites, politely, and then impolitely.|Your cauldron sneezes. So, for the rest of the afternoon, do you.|The warm stuff makes your brew too warm, and then your bench, and then your sleeve.}\nopt.left.say.2 [cd1004] = {药剂先是礼貌地、随后很不礼貌地烧了起来。|你的坩埚打了个喷嚏。整个下午，你也跟着打喷嚏。|那温热的东西让你的药剂太热了，然后是你的工作台，然后是你的袖子。}\n# en: Label it, carefully\nopt.right.label [0222a1] = 小心地贴上标签\n# en: {An hour of tests later, you write a cautious label. Someone adds a skull to it.|You work out what it is. Then you wash your hands three times.|You label it and add it to the row. Yours is the most careful label on the shelf, and the most honest: \"Warm. Unsure.\"}\nopt.right.say.1 [7f65dd] = {一小时的测试之后，你写下一张谨慎的标签。有人在上面加了个骷髅。|你弄清了它是什么。然后把手洗了三遍。|你给它贴上标签，排进那一行。你的标签是架子上最谨慎、也最诚实的一张：“温热。不确定。”}\n\n@event job_board_odd_jobs\n# en: Odd Jobs\ntitle [99e6aa] = 零活\n# en: {Under the proper postings, someone has pinned an odd job.|Under the proper postings is an odd job, and a second pin someone has left beside it, for answers.|The odd-jobs corner has a card addressed to you by name, in a hand you're starting to recognise.} {Someone wants a homesick portrait read to.|Someone wants a ladder held, no questions asked.|Someone wants a reluctant gargoyle walked.} {term: |In the break the board is half bare, and the odd jobs pay a little better.}\ntext [11f8f5] = {正式招工启事底下，有人钉了一张零活。|正式启事底下有一张零活，旁边还多钉了一枚图钉，留着给人回复。|零活角有一张写着你名字的卡片，笔迹你开始认得了。} {有人想找人给一幅想家的肖像画念书。|有人想找人扶梯子，不许问问题。|有人想找人遛一只不情愿的石像鬼。} {term: |假期里告示板空了一半，零活的工钱也高了一点。}\n# en: A cork board in an entrance hall, a small notice pinned at an angle under the official job postings, a student reading it with a frown.\nscene [b55309] = 门厅里的软木告示板，一张小启事歪钉在正式招工启事下面，一个学生皱着眉读它。\n# en: Take the job\nopt.left.label [a7359a] = 接下这活\n# en: {It takes all afternoon and pays exactly what it said, which is rarer than it should be.|It is stranger than the notice suggested, and pays slightly less.|You take the gargoyle for its walk. It doesn't want to go, and then it doesn't want to come back. The pay is fair.}\nopt.left.say.1 [e17840] = {花了一整个下午，报酬和说好的分毫不差——这比应有的要少见。|比启事上写的更古怪，工钱也少了一点。|你带石像鬼去散步。它不想去，后来又不想回来。工钱公道。}\n# en: Haggle first\nopt.right.label [789545] = 先讲讲价\n# en: {You haggle the fee up by a third. Your employer seems to respect you more for it.|You agree a price and a biscuit. The biscuit turns out to be the better half of the deal.|You haggle with the hand you recognise. They pay what you ask, and leave a note: \"Worth it.\"}\nopt.right.say.1 [c1ddf4] = {你把价钱抬高了三分之一。雇主似乎因此更尊重你了。|你们谈定了价钱外加一块饼干。结果饼干才是这笔买卖里更划算的那一半。|你和那个你认得笔迹的人讨价还价。对方照你说的价付了，还留了张字条：“值。”}\n# en: {Your employer takes the notice down and pins up a new one, asking for \"someone less difficult\".|The job goes to a first-year who asked for nothing. They seem very happy with it.|You haggle, and the next card in that hand is addressed to someone else. You've been dropped, politely.}\nopt.right.say.2 [ee0c42] = {雇主把启事撕下来，换上一张新的，要找个“好说话一点的人”。|活儿给了一个什么都没要求的一年级生。那孩子看起来非常高兴。|你讨价还价，结果那个笔迹的下一张卡片写给了别人。你被礼貌地换掉了。}\n\n@event job_board_lost_familiars\n# en: Lost and Found\ntitle [7ffffa] = 失物招领\n# en: {The corner of the board for lost things is busier than the jobs.|An old poster in the lost-things corner now reads \"found, thanks to a student\".|The lost-things corner has thank-you notes now. One draws the rescuer with a halo.} {A toad called \"Sir\" is missing, last seen being brave.|A ransom note demands three biscuits for one sock.|A small owl is lost, answers to nothing, and bites.} {season == \"winter\": Half the familiars have simply gone somewhere warm.|}\ntext [d3e159] = {告示板上的失物角比招工那边还热闹。|失物角里一张旧启事上现在写着“已找到，多亏一位学生”。|失物角如今贴着感谢信。有一张把救星画上了光环。} {一只叫“爵士”的蟾蜍走失了，最后一次被人看见时正在逞英雄。|一封勒索信要求三块饼干，换回一只袜子。|一只小猫头鹰走丢了，叫什么都不应，还咬人。} {season == \"winter\": 一半的魔宠只是自己跑去暖和地方了。|}\n# en: The corner of a cork board crowded with hand-drawn posters of lost familiars and a ransom note for a sock.\nscene [8f714b] = 软木告示板的一角，贴满了手绘的走失魔宠启事，还有一封索要一只袜子赎金的勒索信。\n# en: Go looking for it\nopt.left.label [24390e] = 去找找看\n# en: {You find it behind a radiator in the east tower, deeply unrepentant. The reward is small and grateful.|You find it, and the owner cries, and the reward is mostly biscuits.|You find the owl in the rafters of the owlery, pretending to belong. It bites you. The owner pays double, for the bite.}\nopt.left.say.1 [995f44] = {你在东塔的暖气片后面找到了它，毫无悔意。酬金不多，谢意满满。|你找到了它，失主哭了，酬金大部分是饼干。|你在猫头鹰舍的椽子上找到了那只猫头鹰，它正装作是这儿的。它咬了你。失主付了双倍，因为那一口。}\n# en: {You search for an hour and find only a different, much angrier creature.|You don't find it. The next day the poster says \"Found! Thanks anyway.\"|You find a small owl. It is not the small owl. It also bites.}\nopt.left.say.2 [f9f327] = {你找了一小时，只找到另一只脾气暴躁得多的生物。|你没找到。第二天，启事上写着：“找到了！还是谢谢。”|你找到了一只小猫头鹰。不是那一只。它也咬人。}\n# en: Read every notice twice\nopt.right.label [778bc2] = 每张启事读两遍\n# en: {The board is the castle's diary. You learn who lost what, and who owes whom.|You learn three secrets from the small print, and who has terrible handwriting.|You read every notice, including the thank-you notes. One of them is about you. You read that one three times.}\nopt.right.say.1 [de4eb9] = {告示板是城堡的日记。你知道了谁丢了什么，谁欠谁的。|你从小字里读出了三个秘密，还知道了谁的字写得最难看。|你读了每一张启事，包括那些感谢信。其中一封写的是你。那封你读了三遍。}\n\n@event job_board_yellow_notice\n# en: The Oldest Notice\ntitle [0c2305] = 最老的告示\n# en: In the bottom corner of the board, under years of newer notices, is one gone yellow and soft: \"Wanted, Thursdays: someone to read the newssheet aloud to the portrait on the west stair. He likes the sport and the obituaries. Small pay.\" Someone has renewed the pin.\ntext [b5755a] = 告示板底角，压在多年来的新告示下面，有一张已经发黄变软：“招人，每周四：为西侧楼梯上的肖像朗读新闻报。他爱看体育版和讣告。酬劳微薄。”有人换过了图钉。\n# en: A corner of a cork board where one notice, yellowed and curling, is pinned beneath years of newer ones.\nscene [f55d35] = 软木告示板的一角，一张发黄卷边的告示压在多年来的新告示底下。\n# en: Answer it, this Thursday\nopt.left.label [716d02] = 这周四就去应聘\n# en: The portrait is a bald man in an old-fashioned collar who hasn't had the news read to him in years. He cheers the duelling results and sighs at the obituaries. You lose your Thursday evening, and he asks you to come back.\nopt.left.say.1 [129d7b] = 肖像是个戴老式领子的秃顶男人，好多年没人给他读报了。他为决斗赛果欢呼，对着讣告叹气。你搭进了周四晚上，他请你再来。\n# en: Ask the porter who pins it\nopt.right.label [f1824a] = 问门房是谁钉的\n# en: The porter says the notice was there when he started, and that a few crowns appear on the stair every Thursday whether anyone reads or not. He has never asked who leaves it. He suggests you don't either.\nopt.right.say.1 [506c26] = 门房说，他来的时候这张告示就在了，而且每周四楼梯上都会出现几枚克朗，不管有没有人去读。他从没问过是谁留的。他建议你也别问。\n\n@event job_board_torn_notice\n# en: Half a Notice\ntitle [067821] = 半张告示\n# en: A well-paid notice (\"one evening's portering, generous rates\") has drawn two students, who have both got hold of it. It tears down the middle as you arrive. One holds the half with the pay; the other holds the half with the address. Both look at you.\ntext [cdc4cd] = 一张报酬丰厚的告示（“一晚上的搬运活，酬劳优厚”）引来了两个学生，两人都抓住了它。你走过来时它从中间撕开了。一个拿着写报酬的那半，另一个拿着写地址的那半。两人都看着你。\n# en: Two students tugging at either end of a single job notice in front of a crowded cork board, the paper starting to tear.\nscene [2ca221] = 拥挤的软木告示板前，两个学生各拽着一张招工告示的一头，纸开始撕裂。\n# en: Settle it between them\nopt.left.label [c453e7] = 帮他们调解\n# en: You suggest they share the evening and split the fee. They both hate the idea, then both agree to it, then both thank you. You get nothing but a reputation for being reasonable.\nopt.left.say.1 [9bba47] = 你建议他们一起干那一晚，平分报酬。两人都讨厌这个主意，然后都同意了，然后都谢了你。你什么也没得到，除了一个通情达理的名声。\n# en: Read the address and go yourself\nopt.right.label [ff6244] = 看清地址自己去\n# en: You read the address over a shoulder and get there first. The job pays well. By supper both of them have told everyone what you did.\nopt.right.say.1 [9c63ca] = 你从别人肩后瞥见了地址，抢先赶到。这活儿给钱很多。到晚饭时，他俩已经跟所有人说了你干的事。\n\n@event job_board_monthly_letter\n# en: A Letter a Month\ntitle [22898d] = 每月一封信\n# en: A small notice in a careful, shaky hand: \"Wanted: a student to post a letter for me in the village, first of each month. A few crowns. I can't manage the hill any more.\" The letter is pinned beside it, sealed and addressed to someone in the village.\ntext [184718] = 一张小告示，字迹工整却发抖：“招一名学生，每月初帮我到山下小镇寄一封信。几枚克朗。我已经爬不动那座山了。”信就钉在旁边，封好了，寄给小镇上的某个人。\n# en: A small handwritten notice pinned beside a sealed letter on a cork board, a student reaching to unpin both.\nscene [144090] = 软木告示板上，一张手写的小告示旁钉着一封封好的信，一个学生伸手要把两样都取下来。\n# en: Take the letter down the hill\nopt.left.label [6cf051] = 把信带下山\n# en: You post it, then the next, then the next. In the third month the letter-writer leaves a note: the replies have started coming. You never learn who either of them is. You keep the crowns in a jar.\nopt.left.say.1 [9043ac] = 你寄了这封，又寄下一封，再下一封。第三个月，写信人留了张字条：回信开始来了。你始终不知道他们两个是谁。你把那些克朗存在一个罐子里。\n# en: Leave it for someone else\nopt.right.label [43df3f] = 留给别人\n# en: You leave it on the board. The next morning it's gone, and someone has drawn a small heart beside the notice in pencil.\nopt.right.say.1 [5241d2] = 你把它留在告示板上。第二天早上它不见了，有人用铅笔在告示旁画了一颗小小的心。\n\n@event job_board_copying\n# en: Fair Copies Wanted\ntitle [38430b] = 誊抄启事\n# en: {A notice in a shaky hand. A student down with fever needs a fortnight of lecture notes copied out fair, and will pay for neatness.|Another fever, another fortnight of notes to copy fair. The notice says \"neat hand preferred\", and someone has written your name beside it.|The copying notice again. By now you can copy a page without reading a word of it, if you want to.}\ntext [789ff1] = {一张字迹发抖的告示。一个发烧病倒的学生需要人把两周的课堂笔记誊抄干净，字写得工整就给钱。|又一个发烧的，又是两周的笔记要誊清。告示上写着“字迹工整者优先”，旁边有人写上了你的名字。|又是誊抄的告示。到了现在，只要你愿意，你可以抄完一页而一个字都不读进去。}\n# en: A cork board with a notice in a sick student's shaky hand, a stack of borrowed lecture notes tied with string beneath it.\nscene [ce95da] = 一块软木板上钉着一张病中学生手抖写下的告示，下面是一摞用绳子捆着的借来的课堂笔记。\n# en: Copy them for the pay\nopt.left.label [146f74] = 为了报酬抄完\n# en: {Two evenings of careful letters. The pay arrives with a note that says your sevens are beautiful.|You copy every page, blot nothing and are paid exactly. Your wrist aches until the weekend.|You copy without reading, as fast as the ink allows. The pay is fair and the evening is gone.}\nopt.left.say.1 [652de9] = {两个晚上小心翼翼地写字。报酬送来时附了一张字条，说你的“7”写得真漂亮。|你抄完每一页，一个墨点都没有，报酬分毫不差。你的手腕一直酸到周末。|你一个字不读，墨水干得多快你就抄得多快。报酬公道，晚上也没了。}\n# en: Copy them slowly, and learn them too\nopt.right.label [11f745] = 慢慢抄，顺便学会\n# en: {You read as you copy. Half the pay goes on candles, and you learn a course you aren't even taking.|You copy slowly and read every line. The notes are better than your own, and you keep a few of the ideas.|You read it all, slowly. The student pays a little less for the delay, and you know a little more for it.}\nopt.right.say.1 [efde5d] = {你边抄边读。一半报酬花在了蜡烛上，你却学会了一门你根本没选的课。|你抄得很慢，每一行都读了。这些笔记比你自己的还好，你留下了其中几个想法。|你慢慢地全读了一遍。那个学生因为拖延少付了一点，你也因此多懂了一点。}\n\n@event job_board_message_run\n# en: Messages Across the Castle\ntitle [7ff393] = 穿越城堡送信\n# en: {A professor wants a runner for the afternoon, with notes for every tower before the bell. A small fee, and no reading them.|The runner's notice is back, with a longer list of towers.|The runner's notice, and a list you could walk in your sleep. The staircases have learned your face.}\ntext [4f0fd0] = {一位教授想找个人跑一下午腿，在钟响前把字条送到每座塔。报酬不多，不许偷看。|跑腿的告示又来了，塔楼的名单更长了。|跑腿的告示，和一张你闭着眼都能走完的名单。楼梯们已经认得你的脸了。}\n# en: A student with a satchel of folded notes running across a castle bridge between towers, a staircase swinging away behind them.\nscene [772972] = 一个背着装满折好字条的挎包的学生在塔楼之间的廊桥上奔跑，身后一段楼梯正荡开。\n# en: Run the round yourself\nopt.left.label [8d64a6] = 亲自跑完这一趟\n# en: {You reach the last tower as the bell goes. Your legs are jelly and the fee is honest.|Seven towers, two moving staircases and one argument with a portrait. Paid in full.|You take the shortcuts the staircases allow you now. Paid, and back in time for supper.}\nopt.left.say.1 [2686fa] = {钟响时你正好到了最后一座塔。腿软得像果冻，报酬实打实。|七座塔、两段会动的楼梯，外加和一幅肖像吵了一架。报酬全额付清。|你走那些楼梯现在肯让你走的近路。拿了钱，还赶上了晚饭。}\n# en: Send the owls instead\nopt.right.label [b749ed] = 改派猫头鹰去送\n# en: {You pay the owlery out of the fee and keep the rest, and the afternoon. The professor never knows.|The owls do it faster than you would have. You pocket what's left and feel only slightly guilty.|The owls know the round now as well as you. You keep a little of the fee and all of your afternoon.}\nopt.right.say.1 [a4dbd9] = {你从报酬里付了猫头鹰棚的钱，剩下的归你，下午也归你。教授永远不会知道。|猫头鹰送得比你快。你把剩下的钱揣进兜里，只有一点点内疚。|猫头鹰现在和你一样熟悉这条路线了。你留下一点报酬，还有整个下午。}\n\n@event job_board_market_stall\n# en: Mind the Stall\ntitle [e20a8c] = 帮忙看摊\n# en: {A villager with a bad knee wants someone to mind her market stall on Saturday morning, for a share of the takings.|The stall again: jars, woollens, the bad knee and the share of the takings.|The stall owner pins the notice up with your name already on it, and a question mark, to be polite.}\ntext [31b0c3] = {一个膝盖不好的村民想找人周六上午替她看集市摊位，按营业额分成。|又是那个摊位：罐子、毛织品、那个坏膝盖，还有那份分成。|摊主钉告示时已经写上了你的名字，后面出于礼貌加了个问号。}\n# en: A village market stall heaped with jars and woollens, a student behind it with a cash tin, the owner hobbling away towards a chair in the sun.\nscene [1561ec] = 村里集市上一个堆满罐子和毛织品的摊位，一个学生守在钱箱后面，摊主一瘸一拐地走向阳光下的一把椅子。\n# en: Mind it as she asked\nopt.left.label [2530bc] = 照她说的看好摊\n# en: {You sell three jars and a scarf and count the tin twice. She pays you a fair share and a jar.|A slow morning, honestly counted. She is pleased with the tin and gives you your share.|You know the prices by heart now. She hands you your share without counting it, which is a kind of compliment.}\nopt.left.say.1 [0d8fb2] = {你卖出三个罐子和一条围巾，把钱箱数了两遍。她分给你公道的一份，外加一个罐子。|一个冷清的上午，账目清清楚楚。她对钱箱很满意，把你那份给了你。|价钱你早已烂熟于心。她没数就把你那份递给你，这也算是一种夸奖。}\n# en: Talk up the wares\nopt.right.label [f95d45] = 卖力吆喝\n# en: {You sell like a pedlar and the takings are up. Most of the extra goes back to her, as it should, and you've learned a patter.|You talk a farmer into two scarves he didn't know he needed. Your share is small and your voice is gone.|You could sell her jars to anyone now. She jokes about leaving you the stall.}\nopt.right.say.1 [5f455f] = {你像个货郎一样吆喝，营业额涨了。多出来的大部分理所当然地归了她，你学会了一套说辞。|你说动一个农夫买了两条他原本不知道自己需要的围巾。你的分成不多，嗓子却哑了。|现在你能把她的罐子卖给任何人。她开玩笑说要把摊位留给你。}\n\n@event job_board_lost_property\n# en: The Lost-Property Box\ntitle [312ea9] = 失物箱\n# en: {The porter wants the lost-property box sorted before it starts to smell, and pays by the hour.|The lost-property box is full again, the porter says, mostly of the same gloves.|The porter slides the box across without a word. You know where the odd boot goes now.}\ntext [a8b4a9] = {门房想在失物箱发臭之前找人整理一下，按钟点付钱。|门房说失物箱又满了，大多还是那几只手套。|门房一言不发地把箱子推过来。那只落单的靴子该放哪儿，你现在已经知道了。}\n# en: The porter's lodge, a vast wooden box of odd gloves, scarves, books and one boot, a student sorting them into piles.\nscene [2e859c] = 门房小屋里，一个巨大的木箱装满了不成对的手套、围巾、书和一只靴子，一个学生正把它们分成几堆。\n# en: Sort it quickly for the pay\nopt.left.label [cf84ec] = 为了报酬快快理完\n# en: {Gloves, scarves, books and one boot, in piles by dusk. The porter pays and keeps the boot for himself.|You sort it in an hour. The porter pays for two, out of habit.|You sort it fast. The porter pays, and then tries the odd boot on again, hopefully.}\nopt.left.say.1 [37988e] = {手套、围巾、书和一只靴子，天黑前分成了几堆。门房付了钱，把那只靴子留给了自己。|你一个钟头就理完了。门房出于习惯，按两个钟头付了钱。|你理得飞快。门房付了钱，然后满怀希望地又试穿了一次那只落单的靴子。}\n# en: Read what people lose\nopt.right.label [51b2bd] = 看看大家都丢了什么\n# en: {You learn who carries love letters and who never returns library books. The porter pays you less for taking so long.|You read the names in the books and the notes in the pockets, and put everything back exactly. A slow hour, and a useful one.|You know the castle by what it drops. The porter pays a little less, and you put the gossip away for later.}\nopt.right.say.1 [144709] = {你知道了谁随身带着情书，谁从来不还图书馆的书。门房嫌你磨蹭，少付了一点。|你读了书里的名字和口袋里的字条，又把一切原样放回。一个慢吞吞却很有用的钟头。|你从城堡掉落的东西里认识了这座城堡。门房少付了一点，你把这些八卦收好留着以后用。}\n\n@event job_board_sick_familiar\n# en: A Sick Familiar\ntitle [d87019] = 生病的魔宠\n# en: {A student going home for a funeral needs someone to sit up with their sick familiar for two nights. They will pay.|Another notice for a night sitter, for a sick toad this time, and the pay is better.|The familiars' night-sitter: that's you, apparently. A cat this time, and a worried owner.}\ntext [966bf7] = {一个要回家奔丧的学生需要人帮忙陪护生病的魔宠两个晚上。会付钱。|又一张找夜间看护的告示，这回是一只生病的蟾蜍，报酬更高。|魔宠的夜间看护：看来就是你了。这回是一只猫，和一个忧心忡忡的主人。}\n# en: A student sitting up by lamplight beside a basket where a small sick familiar is curled in a blanket, a bottle of tonic on the table.\nscene [b6ed91] = 灯下，一个学生坐在篮子旁守夜，篮子里一只生病的小魔宠裹在毯子里蜷着，桌上放着一瓶补药。\n# en: Sit up with it, both nights\nopt.left.label [1a5705] = 两个晚上都守着\n# en: {Two nights of tonic every hour and very little sleep. It recovers. So, eventually, do you.|The toad recovers and you nearly don't. The owner pays double and calls you a saint.|The cat is ungrateful and lives. You're paid, and spend the next day asleep in lectures.}\nopt.left.say.1 [57e360] = {两个晚上每小时喂一次补药，几乎没睡。它好了。你最后也缓了过来。|蟾蜍好了，你却差点没撑住。主人付了双倍，说你是圣人。|那只猫毫不领情，但活了下来。你拿到了钱，第二天在课上睡了一整天。}\n# en: Sit up one night, and find a second sitter\nopt.right.label [ca3c8a] = 守一晚，再找个人守第二晚\n# en: {You take the first night and talk a friend into the second. The pay splits; the sleep doesn't.|One night each, you and a sleepy first-year. The familiar is fine. Half the pay is still pay.|You know who will sit up for a little money now. You split the nights and the pay, and sleep.}\nopt.right.say.1 [80e56e] = {你守第一晚，说动一个朋友守第二晚。报酬分了，觉却没少睡。|你和一个睡眼惺忪的一年级生一人守一晚。魔宠没事。一半报酬也是报酬。|你现在知道谁愿意为一点小钱熬夜了。你们分了夜班和报酬，你睡了个好觉。}\n\n@event job_board_porters_crates\n# en: Crates for the Porter\ntitle [63c7e3] = 帮门房搬箱子\n# en: {The porter needs crates carried up from the barge, a great many of them, up a great many stairs.|Crates again, the porter says, pointing at the barge. More of them than last time.|You hear the barge bell and the porter waves before you're even down the stairs. Crates.}\ntext [fd3c49] = {门房需要人把箱子从驳船上搬上来，箱子很多，楼梯也很多。|门房指着驳船说，又是箱子，比上回还多。|你一听见驳船的铃声，还没下楼门房就冲你挥手了。箱子。}\n# en: A cart of wooden crates at the foot of a long castle stair, a student lifting one while the porter points upward.\nscene [f08061] = 一段长长的城堡楼梯脚下停着一车木箱，一个学生正抬起一只，门房指着楼上。\n# en: Carry them all\nopt.left.label [7cb1d5] = 全部搬完\n# en: {Forty crates up seventy stairs. The porter pays you and a little extra for not dropping the one that clinked.|Your back will remember every crate. The pay is good.|You carry the lot and don't count them any more. Paid, and sore.}\nopt.left.say.1 [c46a9e] = {四十只箱子，七十级台阶。门房付了钱，还因为你没摔那只叮当响的箱子多给了一点。|你的背会记住每一只箱子。报酬不错。|你搬完了所有箱子，已经懒得去数了。拿了钱，浑身酸痛。}\n# en: Carry what you can, and train with it\nopt.right.label [ca388f] = 能搬多少搬多少，当作锻炼\n# en: {You take them slowly, two steps at a time, and count it as exercise. The porter pays for the crates you managed.|Half the crates, carried properly. Your legs are stronger and your purse a little lighter than it could be.|You carry what you can and stop before your back does. Paid for half, and stronger for it.}\nopt.right.say.1 [4873fd] = {你慢慢地搬，一次两级台阶，权当锻炼。门房按你搬上去的箱子付钱。|搬了一半，每一只都搬得稳稳当当。你的腿更有力了，钱袋比本可以的轻了一点。|你能搬多少搬多少，在背扛不住之前停了下来。拿到一半的钱，人也更结实了。}\n\n@event job_board_tutor_hour\n# en: An Hour of Tutoring\ntitle [e711ef] = 一小时辅导\n# en: {A first-year's notice, written with great care, asks for an hour's help with an essay. They can pay a little.|The same first-year's careful handwriting: another essay, another hour.|The first-year has stopped writing notices. They just pin up the essay, with a coin taped to the corner.}\ntext [7ff910] = {一个一年级生写得一丝不苟的告示，想请人帮忙改一小时论文。对方能付一点钱。|还是那个一年级生工工整整的字迹：又一篇论文，又一个钟头。|那个一年级生已经不写告示了。对方直接把论文钉上去，角上用胶带粘着一枚硬币。}\n# en: An empty classroom, a nervous first-year with an essay full of crossings-out and an older student pointing at a line.\nscene [31065a] = 一间空教室里，一个紧张的一年级生拿着一篇涂改满篇的论文，一个高年级学生正指着其中一行。\n# en: Take the hour, and the coin\nopt.left.label [6ef63a] = 收下这一小时和那枚硬币\n# en: {An hour of commas and confusion. The essay improves; so does your purse, slightly.|You take them through it line by line. They pay, and thank you twice.|You know their mistakes before they make them now. The hour goes quickly, and they pay.}\nopt.left.say.1 [b044e6] = {一个钟头的逗号和困惑。论文改好了，你的钱袋也稍微鼓了一点。|你带着对方一行一行地过。对方付了钱，道了两次谢。|对方还没犯错你就知道会错在哪儿了。一个钟头过得很快，对方付了钱。}\n# en: Help, and leave the coin pinned\nopt.right.label [2ddff2] = 帮忙，把硬币留在板上\n# en: {You leave the coin taped to the board. The essay comes back with a good mark, and the first-year tells everyone.|You help for nothing. They try to pay you anyway, and you won't let them.|You leave the coin where it is, as always. Somebody has drawn a small sun beside the notice.}\nopt.right.say.1 [53ee57] = {你让那枚硬币继续粘在告示板上。论文拿回了不错的分数，那个一年级生逢人就说。|你分文不取地帮了忙。对方还是想付钱，你没让。|你照旧把硬币留在原处。有人在告示旁边画了一个小小的太阳。}\n\n@event job_board_greenhouse_pots\n# en: Pots for Old Maud\ntitle [fb81b6] = 帮老莫德洗花盆\n# en: {Old Maud's notice asks for clay pots scrubbed before planting, for a small wage and a cup of tea.|Maud's notice again. More pots, the same tea.|Maud has given up pinning notices. The pots are just waiting at the greenhouse door, with your brush on top.}\ntext [60254d] = {老莫德的告示：种植前要把陶盆刷干净，给一点工钱，外加一杯茶。|又是莫德的告示。更多的花盆，同样的茶。|莫德已经懒得钉告示了。花盆就堆在温室门口等着，你的刷子放在最上面。}\n# en: A greenhouse doorway stacked with muddy clay pots, an old gardener on a stool directing a student with a scrubbing brush.\nscene [289e8d] = 温室门口堆着满是泥巴的陶盆，一位老园丁坐在凳子上，指挥着一个拿刷子的学生。\n# en: Scrub them for the wage\nopt.left.label [e405b2] = 为了工钱刷完\n# en: {A hundred pots, scrubbed and stacked. Maud pays you and says you're slower than her and twice as thorough.|You scrub them all and drink the tea. The wage is small and honestly earned.|You scrub in silence, and so does she. She pays you and lets you take a cutting home.}\nopt.left.say.1 [f75392] = {一百只花盆，刷净摞好。莫德付了钱，说你比她慢，却比她仔细一倍。|你刷完了所有花盆，喝了那杯茶。工钱不多，挣得踏实。|你默默地刷，她也默默地看。她付了钱，还让你带一根插条回家。}\n# en: Scrub them and ask about the plants\nopt.right.label [efad4e] = 边刷边问她植物的事\n# en: {Maud talks while you scrub, about which leaf cures what and which leaf kills. She pays a little less, because of the talking.|You learn three remedies and a poison you should never brew. The wage is smaller; the lesson isn't.|Maud tests you on last month's lesson while you scrub. You pass, mostly. The wage is small and the tea is good.}\nopt.right.say.1 [7f0d49] = {你刷的时候莫德一直在讲：哪片叶子治什么，哪片叶子会要命。因为说了话，她少付了一点。|你学会了三种药方，还有一种永远别熬的毒药。工钱少了，学到的东西可不少。|你刷盆的时候，莫德考你上个月学的东西。你大致都答上了。工钱不多，茶很好喝。}\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/pools/castle_towers.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/pools/castle_towers.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event observatory_night_log\n# en: The Night Log\ntitle [9e1784] = 观星日志\n# en: {The observatory keeps a night log, and anyone may write in it.|{flag(wrote_night_log): Since your last entry|Since you were last here}, someone has drawn a moustache on the moon.|You know the log's hands by now: the careful one, the frantic one, the one who only ever writes \"cloudy\".} {season == \"winter\": Frost feathers the inside of the dome.|{The telescope turns, unasked, towards the north.|A draught turns the pages for you.|The lamp is low on oil, as always.}}\ntext [02074c] = {观星台有一本观星日志，谁都可以往里写。|{flag(wrote_night_log): 自你上次写过之后|自你上次来过之后}，有人给月亮画了两撇胡子。|日志里的笔迹你都认得了：工整的那位，潦草的那位，还有永远只写“多云”的那位。} {season == \"winter\": 霜花在穹顶内侧结成羽毛。|{望远镜没人碰，自己转向了北方。|一阵穿堂风替你翻开了书页。|灯油照例快烧干了。}}\n# en: A brass observatory dome at night, one student writing in a huge logbook by lamplight while the great telescope swings round on its own.\nscene [59d5ae] = 夜晚的黄铜穹顶下，一名学生借灯光在一本巨大的日志上书写，大望远镜自顾自地转动。\n# en: Log what the telescope finds\nopt.left.label [8d5562] = 记下望远镜所见\n# en: {You chart a star that wasn't there last month. The log already has it, in a hand forty years older.|Three hours, two pages and one sneeze that smudges a constellation.|The telescope settles on a quiet blue star and waits until you've written it down properly.}\nopt.left.say.1 [b0df4e] = {你标出一颗上个月还不存在的星。日志里早有记载，笔迹比你老四十年。|三个小时，两页纸，外加一个喷嚏糊掉了一整个星座。|望远镜停在一颗安静的蓝星上，一直等到你把它好好写下来。}\n# en: Read the old entries instead\nopt.right.label [c05d01] = 改读旧日的记录\n# en: {A winter entry from long ago says \"Saw nothing. Stayed anyway. Worth it.\"|A love letter written across a page of moon sightings. Very bad. Very sweet.|{final_year and flag(wrote_night_log): You find your own first entry and barely know the hand.|A student from your year logged your star a week before you did, with a small drawn smile.}}\nopt.right.say.1 [6075ac] = {很久以前的一个冬夜，有人写道：“什么也没看到。还是留下了。值得。”|一封情书，横跨一整页的月相记录。写得很烂。也很甜。|{final_year and flag(wrote_night_log): 你找到了自己的第一条记录，几乎认不出那笔迹。|你同届的一个学生比你早一周记下了你那颗星，旁边还画了个小笑脸。}}\n\n@event observatory_one_star_each\n# en: One Star Each\ntitle [0eea9a] = 每人一颗星\n# en: {The rule chalked on the observatory door says one star each, then pass it on. {here} has been at the eyepiece for some time.|Someone has added \"two if they're small\" under the chalk rule. {here} is at the eyepiece.|Someone is always at the telescope when you arrive; tonight it's {here}.} {season == \"summer\": The nights are short and the queue is impatient.|{The dome is cold enough to see your breath.|The telescope creaks round to follow something.|Below, the bridges of light are going out one by one.}}\ntext [4ceaec] = {观星台门上用粉笔写着规矩：每人一颗星，看完就让。{here}已经在目镜前待了好一阵。|有人在粉笔规矩下面添了一句“小的可以看两颗”。{here}正守着目镜。|你每次来，望远镜前总有人；今晚是{here}。} {season == \"summer\": 夏夜短，排队的人都很急。|{穹顶里冷得能看见呼出的白气。|望远镜吱呀一声，转去追什么东西。|下方，光之桥一座接一座熄灭。}}\n# en: Two students at the eyepiece of a great brass telescope, one stepping aside to let the other look.\nscene [7fe37e] = 两名学生在一架巨大的黄铜望远镜目镜旁，一人侧身让另一人观看。\n# en: Share the eyepiece\nopt.left.label [b40888] = 一起用目镜\n# en: {cast_as(here, seraphine): {\"That one,\" Seraphine says, \"is going to matter in, oh.\" She doesn't finish.|\"Left of it,\" Seraphine says. \"Next spring's left.\" You look anyway.|\"There,\" Seraphine says, and moves your hand an inch. \"That's the one you'll want. Don't ask me when.\"}|{cast_as(here, kael): {Kael steps aside and points. When your hands get cold, he stands closer.|Kael says the star's name in a Northern word, once, and doesn't explain.|Kael shares the eyepiece without being asked. Below, the bridges go out one by one, and neither of you counts them.}|You take turns until neither of you remembers whose star it was.}}\nopt.left.say.1 [f1688b] = {cast_as(here, seraphine): {“那一颗，”瑟拉芬说，“将来会很要紧，大概在——噢。”她没说完。|“它左边，”瑟拉芬说，“明年春天的左边。”你还是看了。|“那儿，”瑟拉芬说着把你的手挪了一寸，“那一颗是你会想要的。别问我什么时候。”}|{cast_as(here, kael): {凯尔让开，伸手指给你看。你的手冻僵时，他站得近了些。|凯尔用一个北境的词念出那颗星的名字，只念一次，不作解释。|凯尔没等你开口就让出了目镜。下面，光之桥一座接一座地熄灭，你们谁也没去数。}|你们轮流看，看到谁也记不清那颗星是谁先找到的。}}\n# en: Wait your turn properly\nopt.right.label [da2ba7] = 规规矩矩排队\n# en: {cast_as(here, seraphine): {Seraphine leaves mid-sentence. \"You'll want it,\" she says. \"Later. Now, I mean.\"|\"Yours is the next one,\" Seraphine says, and it is.|Seraphine steps back as you arrive, as if she'd been holding your place. \"Right on time,\" she says, puzzling you.}|{cast_as(here, kael): {Kael yields with a formal nod and goes out onto the roof. Frost follows.|Kael waits behind you. When you turn round, he's gone.|Kael gives up the eyepiece the moment you arrive and stays to watch the bridges go out with you.}|By your turn the star has set. The next one is nearly as good.}}\nopt.right.say.1 [eae430] = {cast_as(here, seraphine): {瑟拉芬话说到一半就走了。“你会用得上的，”她说，“以后。我是说现在。”|“下一颗是你的。”瑟拉芬说。果然如此。|你一到，瑟拉芬就退开了，好像一直在替你占位。“正好，”她说，让你摸不着头脑。}|{cast_as(here, kael): {凯尔郑重地点头让开，走到屋顶上去。霜跟着他。|凯尔在你身后等着。你回头时，他已经不在了。|你一到，凯尔就让出了目镜，留下来陪你看光之桥一座座熄灭。}|轮到你时，那颗星已经落下了。下一颗也差不多好。}}\n\n@event observatory_roof_ledge\n# en: The Roof Ledge\ntitle [9c29ff] = 屋顶的窄檐\n# en: {A window in the dome opens onto the roof, and onto a ledge that everyone swears they have never sat on.|Someone else who never sits there has oiled the latch.|The ledge. You know where the loose tile is now.} {season == \"winter\": The tiles are white with frost.|{The lake below is a second sky.|A warm wind comes up from the village.|Someone's scarf is snagged on the gutter.}}\ntext [95a3fa] = {穹顶上有扇窗通向屋顶，窗外有一道窄檐——人人都发誓自己从没坐过。|另一位“从没坐过”的人给窗闩上了油。|那道窄檐。你已经知道哪块瓦是松的了。} {season == \"winter\": 瓦片上白霜一片。|{下方的湖是第二片天空。|一阵暖风从山下小镇吹上来。|不知谁的围巾挂在了檐沟上。}}\n# en: A student sitting on a narrow ledge outside a brass dome, legs dangling over the castle roofs, the lake far below.\nscene [00d358] = 一名学生坐在黄铜穹顶外的窄檐上，双腿悬在城堡屋顶之上，湖在远远的下方。\n# en: Climb out onto the ledge\nopt.left.label [3e2750] = 爬到窄檐上去\n# en: {You sit with the whole sky to yourself until the stars begin to wheel.|The castle looks small from here, and kind, and very old.|You step over the loose tile without looking. Someone's scarf is still on the gutter; you leave it, for whoever comes next.}\nopt.left.say.1 [c6653f] = {整片天空归你一个人，你一直坐到群星开始旋转。|从这里看，城堡显得很小，很温柔，也很老。|你看都不看就跨过了那块松瓦。不知谁的围巾还挂在檐沟上；你没动它，留给下一个人。}\n# en: {The loose tile is loose in a new place. You slide, grab, and hang on, and climb back in with skinned palms.|A gust takes your balance and most of your dignity. The gutter keeps the rest.|You reach for the snagged scarf and the gutter reaches for you. You get back in with the scarf and a bruise.}\nopt.left.say.2 [416f31] = {那块松瓦这回在别处松了。你一滑，一抓，挂住了，爬回来时手掌都磨破了皮。|一阵风夺走了你的平衡和大半体面。剩下的归檐沟。|你伸手去够挂住的围巾，檐沟也伸手够你。你带着围巾和一块淤青爬了回来。}\n# en: Watch through the glass\nopt.right.label [8e53e6] = 隔着玻璃看\n# en: {You see half the sky and all of the smudges.|Through the glass the stars look like they're behind a shop window. You can look; you can't have.|You watch through the glass. On the ledge outside, someone else is sitting where you'd have sat, and they wave.}\nopt.right.say.1 [bb55c6] = {你看到了半边天空，和玻璃上所有的污渍。|隔着玻璃，星星像摆在橱窗里。能看，不能拿。|你隔着玻璃看。外面的窗台上，有人坐在你本会坐的地方，朝你挥了挥手。}\n\n@event observatory_scraped_word\n# en: The Scraped-Out Word\ntitle [5f3087] = 被刮掉的词\n# en: {In the night log's oldest volume, every entry by a student who had finished Advanced Astromancy uses the same word, and every time it has been scraped out.|You've worked out that the missing word was something you did to a star, not something you saw.|You know these pages now. The word is gone; the shape of the work around it is not.} {In one margin, \"Ask. Be told.\"|A pressed flower falls out.|The lamp gutters as you turn the page.}\ntext [622aaf] = {日志最老的那一卷里，凡是修完高等星象学的学生写下的记录，都用到同一个词，而每一处都被刮掉了。|你已经弄明白，那个缺失的词是你对星星做的事，而不是你看见的东西。|这些书页你已经很熟了。那个词不在了，可它周围那份功课的轮廓还在。} {页边写着：“去问。会有人告诉你。”|一朵压干的花掉了出来。|你翻页时，灯焰摇曳了一下。}\n# en: An old observatory logbook open by lamplight, the same word scraped out of line after line.\nscene [cccc67] = 灯下摊开一本古老的观星日志，同一个词被一行又一行地刮掉。\n# en: Trace the gaps by lamplight\nopt.left.label [5f1275] = 借灯光描摹空缺\n# en: You feed a thread of witchlight into the gaps, and it drinks more than you meant to give. {The words around the gaps say \"called\", \"named\", \"down\". Whoever taught it taught it aloud, to people who had finished Advanced Astromancy.|\"...and the light came down when I said its name...\" Then the gap. You will need someone to tell you the word.|The shape around the gap is a verb. Whatever it was, it was done to a star, and it was taught, not written.}\nopt.left.say.1 [960edb] = 你把一缕巫光送进空缺里，它吸走的比你想给的多。{空缺周围的词是“唤”“名”“降下”。不管是谁教的，都是口头教的，教给已经修完高等星象学的人。|“……当我念出它的名字，光便降了下来……”然后是空缺。你得有人把这个词告诉你。|空缺周围的形状说明那是个动词。不管是什么，都是对一颗星做的，而且是口授的，不是写下来的。}\n# en: Ask the telescope, half joking\nopt.right.label [de5250] = 半开玩笑地问望远镜\n# en: {The telescope swings to one bright star and stays there, patient as a teacher who knows you aren't ready.|It turns to a named star and holds it. You get the feeling you have to finish Advanced Astromancy before it explains.|The telescope turns, very slowly, to the same star as always. You're beginning to think it's a reminder, not an answer.}\nopt.right.say.1 [c7ee59] = {望远镜转向一颗明亮的星，停住不动，耐心得像个知道你还没准备好的老师。|它转向一颗有名字的星，稳稳对准。你觉得，得先修完高等星象学，它才肯解释。|望远镜极慢地转向了那颗老地方的星。你开始觉得这是提醒，不是答案。}\n\n@event workshop_spare_bench\n# en: The Spare Bench\ntitle [b782e9] = 备用工作台\n# en: {Mir keeps a spare bench for visitors, under a sign that says TOUCH THINGS (CAREFULLY).|The sign over the spare bench has grown a second line, (MORE CAREFULLY).|Your bench, as Mir has started calling it, has a jar of springs waiting.} {Mir waves a spanner at you without looking up.|Something on a high shelf is ticking.|{season == \"winter\": The forge-lamp makes it the warmest room in the west wing.|The window is open to let the smoke out.}}\ntext [52eaf0] = {米尔给访客留了一张备用工作台，上方挂着牌子：随便摸（小心点）。|备用工作台上方的牌子多了一行：（再小心点）。|“你的工作台”——米尔开始这么叫它了——上面放着一罐弹簧等你。} {米尔头也不抬，冲你挥了挥扳手。|高处的架子上有什么在滴答作响。|{season == \"winter\": 锻炉灯让这里成了西翼最暖和的房间。|窗开着，好放烟出去。}}\n# en: A cluttered workshop bench under a hand-painted sign, a music box in pieces beside a jar of springs, fire buckets by the door.\nscene [d9570d] = 杂乱的工坊工作台上方挂着手绘的牌子，一只拆散的音乐盒旁放着一罐弹簧，门边摆着灭火水桶。\n# en: Mend a jammed music box\nopt.left.label [84f39e] = 修一只卡住的音乐盒\n# en: A loose spring bites your thumb on the way in. {It plays again, a little too fast, like it's late for something.|You fix the tune and break the lid. Mir says that's probably progress.|It plays a waltz nobody has heard in years. You leave it on the sill to finish.}\nopt.left.say.1 [c84c0f] = 进门时一根松掉的弹簧咬了你的拇指一下。{它又响了，稍微快了点，像是赶着去哪儿。|曲子修好了，盒盖弄坏了。米尔说这大概也算进步。|它奏起一支多年没人听过的华尔兹。你把它放在窗台上，让它自己奏完。}\n# en: Just watch Mir work\nopt.right.label [b61e05] = 就看米尔干活\n# en: {Mir explains a gear train for an hour. You follow about a third, which they say is probably a record.|You sit by the fire buckets and watch the sparks. Your eyes sting. It's a good afternoon.|Mir hands you things to hold and forgets you're holding them. It's oddly restful.}\nopt.right.say.1 [fc41a6] = {米尔讲了一个钟头的齿轮传动。你听懂了三分之一，TA说这大概是纪录。|你坐在灭火水桶旁看火花。眼睛被熏得发酸。是个好下午。|米尔把东西递给你拿着，然后忘了你还拿着。出奇地让人放松。}\n\n@event workshop_volunteer\n# en: A Volunteer With Hands\ntitle [1b4b0c] = 有手的志愿者\n# en: {\"Perfect timing! I need a volunteer and you have hands!\" {maker} holds up {a pair of boots that walk on their own|a hat that remembers where you left things|goggles that see round corners}.|{maker} has a new invention and a familiar look. Today: {boots that walk on their own|a hat with a memory|goggles for corners}.|You don't ask anymore. You just look at what {maker} is holding, then at the fire bucket.}\ntext [28d24b] = {“来得正好！我需要一个志愿者，而你有手！”{maker}举起{一双会自己走路的靴子|一顶记得你把东西放在哪儿的帽子|一副能看见拐角后面的护目镜}。|{maker}有了新发明，还有那熟悉的眼神。今天是：{自己走路的靴子|有记性的帽子|看拐角的护目镜}。|你已经不问了。你只是看看{maker}手里的东西，再看看灭火水桶。}\n# en: An artificer in soot-streaked goggles holding out a strange brass contraption, a fire bucket waiting hopefully on the floor.\nscene [204031] = 一位戴着沾满煤灰护目镜的奇械师举着一件古怪的黄铜装置，地上一只灭火水桶满怀期待地候着。\n# en: Try it on\nopt.left.label [fdeee0] = 试戴一下\n# en: {It works! For eleven glorious minutes, it works, and {maker} writes down every one of them.|It does exactly what it should. {maker} looks almost suspicious and walks round it three times.|It works. You still don't know what it was for, and {maker} is too delighted to tell you.}\nopt.left.say.1 [1f3f1b] = {成功了！整整十一分钟的辉煌，它一直在运转，{maker}把每一分钟都记了下来。|它完全按设计运转。{maker}几乎起了疑心，围着它转了三圈。|成功了。你还是不知道它是干什么用的，{maker}高兴得顾不上告诉你。}\n# en: {It works in the wrong direction. You spend a while upside down. {maker} apologises at length, with diagrams.|There's a bang, a smell of singed eyebrow, and {maker} saying \"Interesting!\" in a voice that means sorry.|It goes off with a pop and a smell of hot brass. {maker} writes \"promising\" and hands you the bucket.}\nopt.left.say.2 [deb94d] = {它朝反方向运转了。你倒立了好一阵。{maker}长篇大论地道歉，还附了图解。|砰的一声，一股烧焦眉毛的味道，{maker}说着“有意思！”——那语气其实是“对不起”。|它“砰”的一声炸了，一股热黄铜的味道。{maker}写下“有前途”，把水桶递给了你。}\n# en: Hold the fire bucket\nopt.right.label [f76215] = 帮忙拎水桶\n# en: {You hold the bucket. You don't need the bucket. {maker} looks faintly disappointed.|You need the bucket. Briefly, splendidly, you need the bucket.|You hold the bucket. You need it twice, which {maker} calls an improvement.}\nopt.right.say.1 [fdbcfe] = {你拎着水桶。水桶没派上用场。{maker}看起来有点失望。|水桶派上用场了。短暂而壮丽地派上了用场。|你拎着水桶。用上了两次，{maker}说这算是进步。}\n\n@event workshop_stirring_cauldron\n# en: The Self-Stirring Cauldron\ntitle [0d87b7] = 自动搅拌坩埚\n# en: {The self-stirring cauldron in the corner wears a note. DO NOT ADJUST. IT KNOWS WHAT IT DID.|Someone has underlined the cauldron's note twice and added the word STILL.|The cauldron's spoon pauses when you come in, as if it remembers you.} {It's going anticlockwise, which it isn't supposed to.|It smells of toffee and lightning.|{season == \"spring\": Someone has put flowers in it. It is stirring them.|It's stirring soup for nobody.}}\ntext [28bf0c] = {角落里那口自动搅拌的坩埚上贴着纸条。禁止调整。它知道自己干了什么。|有人在坩埚的纸条下划了两道线，又添了两个字：仍然。|你一进门，坩埚的勺子就停了一下，好像认得你。} {它在逆时针搅，按理不该这样。|它闻起来像太妃糖和闪电。|{season == \"spring\": 有人往里面放了花。它正在搅花。|它在给谁也不是的人搅汤。}}\n# en: A small cauldron in a workshop corner stirring itself with a wooden spoon, a stern paper note pinned to its rim.\nscene [2055ca] = 工坊角落里一口小坩埚正用木勺自己搅拌，锅沿上别着一张严厉的纸条。\n# en: Adjust the enchantment\nopt.left.label [826eac] = 调整附魔\n# en: {It settles into a slow, contented clockwise. Mir, passing, says \"Oh!\" and then \"Huh,\" and then \"Teach me.\"|The spoon taps the rim twice, like a thank-you, and gets on with it.|You adjust it, gently, the way it seems to want. It stirs the soup for nobody a little more cheerfully.}\nopt.left.say.1 [7a273c] = {它慢下来，心满意足地顺时针转。米尔路过，先说“噢！”，再说“嗯？”，然后说“教我。”|勺子在锅沿上敲了两下，像在道谢，然后继续干活。|你照它似乎想要的样子，轻轻调了一下。它给没人喝的汤搅得更欢快了些。}\n# en: {It stirs your sleeve, then you, then your sleeve again.|It speeds up instead. The ceiling now has toffee on it, and so do you.|It recognises you, and holds a grudge. The spoon flicks soup at you until you back away.}\nopt.left.say.2 [afcde7] = {它搅了你的袖子，然后搅你，然后又搅袖子。|它反而转得更快了。天花板上现在有太妃糖，你身上也有。|它认出了你，而且记仇。勺子一个劲儿朝你弹汤，直到你退开。}\n# en: Take notes from a safe distance\nopt.right.label [cca44e] = 远远地做笔记\n# en: {You write down everything it's doing wrong. The list is long and slightly envious.|You sketch the rune on its base. The cauldron splashes your page, on purpose you think.|You take notes from the doorway. The spoon pauses, as if waiting for you to finish the sentence.}\nopt.right.say.1 [1dc55a] = {你把它做错的地方全记了下来。清单很长，还带着点羡慕。|你描下它底座上的符文。坩埚溅湿了你的纸——你觉得是故意的。|你在门口记笔记。勺子停了停，好像在等你把那句话写完。}\n\n@event workshop_glass_cabinet\n# en: The Glass Cabinet\ntitle [331550] = 玻璃柜\n# en: {At the back of the workshop is a glass cabinet that isn't Mir's, full of faculty machines, very still.|A clockwork mouse in the glass cabinet has moved since your last visit, and now faces the door.|The cabinet. Mir says the machines in it think, probably, and are waiting for the right student.} A card in old ink: \"Artifice. By invitation, to those who have passed Alchemy II and Evocation II.\"\ntext [b26316] = {工坊最里面有一只不属于米尔的玻璃柜，装满了教职员做的机器，一动不动。|玻璃柜里那只发条老鼠比你上次来时挪了位置，现在正对着门。|那只柜子。米尔说里面的机器会思考，大概吧，它们在等合适的学生。} 一张旧墨水写的卡片：“奇械术。仅限受邀，须已通过炼金学 II 与塑能术 II。”\n# en: A tall glass cabinet at the back of a workshop, clockwork animals inside it, a faded card propped against the glass.\nscene [beb857] = 工坊后方一只高高的玻璃柜，里面摆着发条动物，一张褪色的卡片斜靠在玻璃上。\n# en: Study the machines through the glass\nopt.left.label [4744ef] = 隔着玻璃研究机器\n# en: {Nothing moves while you watch. The moment you look away, something ticks.|You count the gears in a brass beetle and lose count. Somebody made this who could make things and make them burn.|The machines don't move, but you're sure they're thinking. One of them, very slightly, nods.}\nopt.left.say.1 [4109c3] = {你盯着看时，什么都不动。你一移开目光，就有东西滴答一声。|你数一只黄铜甲虫身上的齿轮，数乱了。造它的人，既会造东西，也会让东西烧起来。|机器们一动不动，可你确信它们在思考。其中一台极轻微地点了点头。}\n# en: Try the cabinet lock\nopt.right.label [c81d94] = 试试柜子的锁\n# en: {The lock gives. The mouse winds itself up, bows, and goes back to sleep. You close the door very quietly.|The door swings open an inch, and every machine inside turns its head to look at you. You close it again, politely.|The lock opens at a touch, as if it had been waiting. The machines look at you, and then, satisfied, at each other.}\nopt.right.say.1 [b3b98e] = {锁开了。老鼠给自己上紧发条，鞠了一躬，又睡了。你轻轻地关上柜门。|柜门开了一寸，里面每一台机器都转过头来看你。你又礼貌地把门关上了。|锁一碰就开了，好像一直在等着。机器们看看你，然后满意地看看彼此。}\n# en: {The cabinet shrieks like a kettle. Mir is delighted. The corridor is not.|The lock bites your pick and keeps it. Mir says it does that, probably, to people it likes.|The lock doesn't bite this time. It simply refuses, the way a cat refuses, and Mir says that's progress.}\nopt.right.say.2 [5ff644] = {柜子像水壶一样尖叫起来。米尔乐坏了。走廊里的人可没有。|锁咬住了你的撬片，不还了。米尔说，它大概对喜欢的人都这样。|这回锁没咬你。它只是拒绝，像猫那样拒绝，米尔说这就是进步。}\n\n@event ledger_office_unprintable\n# en: Letters We Will Not Print\ntitle [ae0100] = 不予刊登的来信\n# en: {By the Ledger's door sits a basket marked LETTERS WE WILL NOT PRINT. Visitors may read them.|The unprintable basket has overflowed into a second basket, which is also full.|You are a connoisseur of the unprintable basket now.} {On top is a complaint about the soup, in verse.|One is just a drawing of a prefect, unflattering and accurate.|{season == \"winter\": Someone demands that snow be cancelled.|Someone asks who keeps moving the stairs.}}\ntext [9924ff] = {《流明纪事报》编辑部门口放着一只篮子，标着“不予刊登的来信”。访客可以随便读。|不予刊登的来信溢到了第二只篮子里，第二只也满了。|你现在是这只篮子的资深鉴赏家了。} {最上面是一封用韵文写的、投诉膳堂汤的信。|有一封只画了一位级长，不好看，但很像。|{season == \"winter\": 有人要求取消下雪。|有人问到底是谁老在挪楼梯。}}\n# en: A wicker basket overflowing with letters by the door of a cramped newspaper office, front pages papering the walls.\nscene [276fb2] = 狭小的报社编辑部门口，一只柳条篮里塞满了信，墙上贴满了旧头版。\n# en: Read the whole basket\nopt.left.label [7b9421] = 把整篮都读完\n# en: {The best one is an apology to a goat.|One is from a first-year who misses home. You put it back gently.|{final_year and flag(ledger_letter_sent): A letter in your own first-year hand. You don't remember sending it. You were right.|Three letters about the same broken window, all blaming different people.}}\nopt.left.say.1 [0cb17b] = {最好的一封，是写给一只山羊的道歉信。|有一封来自一个想家的一年级生。你轻轻把它放了回去。|{final_year and flag(ledger_letter_sent): 一封你自己一年级时的笔迹。你不记得寄过它。你当时说得没错。|三封信写的是同一扇碎窗户，各怪各的人。}}\n# en: Write one of your own\nopt.right.label [fec258] = 自己也写一封\n# en: {They print it. Two people quote it at you at breakfast; one of them disagrees with it loudly.|It runs on the back page, under your initials. You feel oddly famous for a day.|They print it, with a footnote from the editor disagreeing. It's the most-read thing in the issue.}\nopt.right.say.1 [ed4137] = {他们登了。早饭时有两个人向你引用了它，其中一个大声表示反对。|它登在了末版，署着你的缩写。你莫名其妙地出了一天名。|他们登了，还附了一条编辑表示反对的脚注。那是这一期读的人最多的东西。}\n# en: {It goes in the basket. By next week, someone is reading it aloud.|It's rejected with a note: \"Too sensible.\" You aren't sure that's a criticism.|It goes in the basket. You find it there next week, with someone else's reply pinned to it.}\nopt.right.say.2 [a4de71] = {它进了篮子。下个星期，就有人在大声朗读它。|退稿附了一张字条：“太有道理了。”你不确定这算不算批评。|它进了篮子。下周你在那儿找到它时，上面别着一封别人的回信。}\n\n@event ledger_office_deadline_night\n# en: Deadline Night\ntitle [d06cb7] = 截稿之夜\n# en: {The press is groaning like a dragon on deadline, and {here} eyes you like a captain eyes a spare oar.|{here} hands you a stack before you've said hello, and a rag for your fingers.|You take a stack and start folding. {here} doesn't look up, which is a compliment.} {The ink on the front page is still wet.|A headline has a spelling mistake nobody will admit to.|{final_year and flag(ledger_folded): You've folded this paper for years now.|The press shudders as if it's thinking of stopping.}}\ntext [b3ba57] = {印刷机像截稿夜的龙一样呻吟，{here}打量你的眼神，就像船长打量一支备用的桨。|你还没打招呼，{here}就递来一摞报纸，外加一块擦手的布。|你拿起一摞就开始折。{here}头都不抬——这已经是夸奖了。} {头版的墨迹还没干。|有个标题拼错了，谁也不肯认。|{final_year and flag(ledger_folded): 你已经折了好几年这份报纸。|印刷机一阵哆嗦，像在考虑罢工。}}\n# en: A cramped office lit by one lamp, a groaning printing press, two students folding newspapers into tall stacks.\nscene [241781] = 一盏灯照亮狭小的编辑部，印刷机轰鸣呻吟，两名学生把报纸折成高高的几摞。\n# en: Fold until the press stops\nopt.left.label [b5b377] = 折到印刷机停下\n# en: {cast_as(here, nessa): {\"Good. Fast. Again,\" says Nessa. From her, that's a sonnet.|Nessa folds twice as fast as you and reads every page as she goes.|Nessa doesn't say anything. She just slides the next stack to you, which is the highest honour on deadline night.}|{By dawn your fingers are grey with ink, and you know tomorrow's news before anyone.|You fold until the stacks are taller than you. Then you fold the ones that fell over.|You fold in rhythm with the press. When it stops, you keep going for a moment, out of habit.}}\nopt.left.say.1 [618b63] = {cast_as(here, nessa): {“好。快。再来。”内莎说。从她嘴里出来，这就是一首十四行诗。|内莎折得比你快一倍，还边折边把每一页读完。|内莎什么也没说。她只是把下一摞推给你，这是截稿夜的最高礼遇。}|{天亮时你的手指被墨染成灰色，明天的新闻你比谁都先知道。|你折到报纸堆得比你还高。然后再把倒下来的那些重新折好。|你跟着印刷机的节奏折。它停了，你还习惯性地多折了一会儿。}}\n# en: Make your excuses\nopt.right.label [1694c0] = 找个借口溜走\n# en: {cast_as(here, nessa): {\"Deserter,\" Nessa calls. \"Noted. Quoted. Forgiven by Thursday.\"|Nessa doesn't look up. \"Coward,\" she says, almost fondly, and writes something down.|Nessa looks up for once. \"Fine. Go. You owe me a Thursday.\" You do, and she collects.}|{You slip away before the second stack. The press groans after you, reproachfully.|You plead an essay. Nobody believes you. Tomorrow's paper mentions a certain absent volunteer.|You leave, and the press shudders to a stop as you go. Somebody blames you, and it goes in the paper.}}\nopt.right.say.1 [959246] = {cast_as(here, nessa): {“逃兵，”内莎喊，“已记录。已引用。周四前原谅你。”|内莎头也不抬。“胆小鬼。”她说，语气近乎亲昵，然后记下了点什么。|内莎难得抬了头。“行。走吧。你欠我一个周四。”你确实欠，她也确实来讨了。}|{第二摞还没折，你就溜了。印刷机在你身后埋怨地呻吟。|你说要赶论文。没人信。第二天的报上提到了某位缺席的志愿者。|你走了，印刷机随着你离开哆嗦着停了下来。有人把账算到你头上，还上了报。}}\n\n@event ledger_office_drying_line\n# en: The Drying Line\ntitle [7b0073] = 晾报绳\n# en: {Tomorrow's gossip column is pegged on a line to dry, and nobody is watching it.|The proofs hang on the line, and the peg on the gossip column is a new red one.|The drying line. You know which peg holds the gossip column.} {One item names your house.|One item begins \"Which second-year was seen...\"|{final_year: One item is about the graduating year. Possibly you.|A whole paragraph is about a broken window.}}\ntext [f77302] = {明天的八卦专栏夹在绳子上晾着，没人看着。|校样挂在绳上，夹八卦专栏的换成了一个新的红夹子。|那根晾报绳。你知道哪个夹子夹着八卦专栏。} {有一条提到了你的学院。|有一条开头是“哪位二年级生被人看见……”|{final_year: 有一条写的是毕业班。也许就是你。|整整一段都在说一扇碎窗户。}}\n# en: Freshly printed pages pegged on a line across a cramped office to dry, one page hanging slightly crooked.\nscene [125d66] = 刚印好的报纸夹在横贯狭小编辑部的绳子上晾干，其中一页挂得有点歪。\n# en: Read ahead\nopt.left.label [f50674] = 先睹为快\n# en: {You read it all and hang it back exactly as crooked as it was.|You know tomorrow's scandal tonight. It is much smaller than it will look in print.|You read the item under the red peg. It's about you, and it's true, and it's very short. You leave it exactly as it was.}\nopt.left.say.1 [f01188] = {你全读完了，又把它原样挂回去，歪得一模一样。|今晚你就知道了明天的丑闻。它远没有印出来时看着那么大。|你读了红夹子下面那条。写的是你，是真的，而且很短。你原样挂了回去。}\n# en: {Caught. The next column has an item beginning \"Which nosy visitor...\"|A peg snaps. The page lands face-down in the ink tray, and everyone turns round.|You lift the red peg and the whole line comes down. Tomorrow's column has a new item, and it's about you.}\nopt.left.say.2 [cc8425] = {被抓了。下一期专栏里有一条，开头是“哪位好奇的访客……”|一个夹子崩断了。那页纸正面朝下掉进了墨盘，所有人都转过头来。|你一提那只红夹子，整条绳子都塌了。明天的专栏多了一条，写的是你。}\n# en: Leave the line alone\nopt.right.label [1d8c18] = 别碰那根绳子\n# en: {You spend the night wondering. That's the price of manners.|You find out with everyone else, at breakfast. It was about you anyway.|You leave it. You know which peg it is, and that's almost the same as knowing.}\nopt.right.say.1 [dbd5de] = {你琢磨了一整晚。这就是讲礼貌的代价。|第二天早饭时，你和大家一起知道了。反正写的就是你。|你没碰。你知道是哪一个夹子，这几乎等于知道了。}\n\n@event headmistress_tower_stone_owl\n# en: The Stone Owl\ntitle [6ea1cf] = 石雕猫头鹰\n# en: {The stone owl on the stair opens one eye. \"A question first,\" it says, in a voice like a door closing.|The owl's other eye is half open tonight. It has been waiting all day for someone to ask.|The owl opens both eyes for you now, which may or may not be an honour.} {Today's question is about the founders.|Today's question is a riddle with no right answer.|{season == \"winter\": There is snow on its head, and it is not amused.|A moth has landed on its beak. It is pretending not to notice.}}\ntext [ae3fd2] = {楼梯上的石雕猫头鹰睁开一只眼。“先回答一个问题。”它说，声音像一扇正在合上的门。|今晚猫头鹰的另一只眼也半睁着。它等了一整天，就盼着有人来问。|猫头鹰现在会为你睁开两只眼了，这也许是种荣幸，也许不是。} {今天的问题关于创始者。|今天的问题是个没有正确答案的谜语。|{season == \"winter\": 它头上积了雪，很不高兴。|一只飞蛾停在它的喙上。它假装没注意到。}}\n# en: A stone owl on the newel post of a spiral stair, one eye open, a student standing very still before it.\nscene [770d5b] = 螺旋楼梯的立柱上蹲着一只石雕猫头鹰，睁着一只眼，一名学生在它面前一动不敢动。\n# en: Answer the question\nopt.left.label [65b450] = 回答问题\n# en: {The owl closes its eyes and the stair is yours. Upstairs, someone laughs, short and pleased.|\"Adequate,\" says the owl, which from a stone owl is a standing ovation.|You answer before it finishes asking. The owl looks at you with both eyes for a long time, then closes them.}\nopt.left.say.1 [75b8c5] = {猫头鹰闭上眼，楼梯归你了。楼上有人笑了一声，短促而愉快。|“尚可。”猫头鹰说。从一只石头猫头鹰嘴里说出来，这等于全场起立鼓掌。|它还没问完你就答了。猫头鹰睁着两只眼睛看了你很久，然后闭上了。}\n# en: {\"No,\" says the owl, and closes both eyes. You climb anyway, feeling marked down.|The owl repeats your answer back to you, slowly. It sounds worse.|Your answer is right and the owl says \"No\" anyway, to see what you'll do. You climb on. It seems satisfied.}\nopt.left.say.2 [566636] = {“不对。”猫头鹰说着，闭上了两只眼。你还是爬了上去，感觉被扣了分。|猫头鹰把你的答案慢慢复述了一遍。听起来更糟了。|你答对了，猫头鹰却照样说“不对”，想看看你会怎么做。你继续往上爬。它似乎很满意。}\n# en: Climb past without a word\nopt.right.label [ef7de3] = 一言不发地走过去\n# en: {The owl says nothing. At the top, the door is shut. You have the strong feeling you've been noted.|You get past. The owl's eye follows you all the way up, and all the way down again.|You climb past and nod to it, as you'd nod to a porter. It nods back, very slightly.}\nopt.right.say.1 [a5a9f6] = {猫头鹰什么也没说。到了顶上，门关着。你强烈地感觉自己被记了一笔。|你过去了。猫头鹰的眼睛一路跟着你上楼，又一路跟着你下楼。|你经过时朝它点点头，就像朝门房点头一样。它极轻微地点头回礼。}\n\n@event headmistress_tower_tea\n# en: Two Cups Poured\ntitle [91cc0d] = 两杯已斟好\n# en: {The headmistress pours two cups unasked. \"Sit. Tell me how your term is going, and then how it is really going.\"|Two cups, already poured. \"Well,\" says {host}, \"how is it really going?\"|You sit before she asks. She looks pleased, and doesn't say so.} {The crystal towers on her board have moved.|Rain ticks on the high windows.|{final_year: \"Your last year,\" she says. \"I remember your first.\"|Footnote, the library cat, sleeps on her papers.}}\ntext [0b49e7] = {院长没问你要不要，就斟了两杯茶。“坐。说说你这学期过得怎么样，再说说真正过得怎么样。”|两杯茶，已经斟好。“那么，”{host}说，“真正过得怎么样？”|她还没开口，你就坐下了。她看上去很满意，但没说出来。} {她棋盘上的水晶塔挪了位置。|雨点轻敲着高窗。|{final_year: “你的最后一年了，”她说，“我还记得你的第一年。”|图书馆的猫脚注睡在她的文件上。}}\n# en: A tower study full of books, a tall pale woman pouring two cups of tea beside a board of crystal towers by the window.\nscene [56714a] = 堆满书的塔楼书房里，一位高挑苍白的女士在窗边的水晶塔棋盘旁斟着两杯茶。\n# en: Tell her the truth\nopt.left.label [bfb1ef] = 跟她说实话\n# en: {She listens without interrupting, which is worse than any lecture and better than most comfort.|She hears all of it and says only, \"Yes. I thought so.\" Somehow that helps, later.|You tell her before she asks. She hears it all, then tells you something true about her own first years. It doesn't help, and then it does.}\nopt.left.say.1 [133c55] = {她从头听到尾，一句也不打断。这比任何训话都难熬，又比大多数安慰都管用。|她全听完了，只说：“是的。我也这么想。”不知怎么，过后这句话很有用。|她还没问你就说了。她全听完，然后讲了一件她自己刚入学那几年的真事。起初没用，后来有用了。}\n# en: Give her the polished version\nopt.right.label [02edb8] = 给她一个漂亮的版本\n# en: {She laughs, short and delighted, and lets you get away with it. You aren't sure that's a good sign.|\"Very good,\" she says. \"Next time, the true one.\" She means both halves.|She lets the polished version stand and pours more tea. \"You've got better at it,\" she says. You can't tell if that's praise.}\nopt.right.say.1 [5cf7c6] = {她笑了一声，短促而愉快，放你过了关。你拿不准这是不是好兆头。|“很好，”她说，“下次讲真的那个。”两句她都是认真的。|她任由那个体面的版本过关，又添了茶。“你说得越来越好了，”她说。你听不出这是不是夸奖。}\n# en: {\"Mm,\" she says, and pours you more tea, and waits. You end up telling her anyway.|She lets the silence go on until it says everything you didn't.|She hears the polished version, then repeats the one sentence of it that was true. You talk for an hour after that.}\nopt.right.say.2 [92b23b] = {“嗯。”她说着，又给你添了茶，然后等着。最后你还是全说了。|她任由沉默延续，直到沉默把你没说的都说了。|她听完那个体面的版本，然后把其中唯一一句真话复述了一遍。之后你说了一个钟头。}\n\n@event headmistress_tower_window_seat\n# en: The Window Seat\ntitle [15c249] = 窗边座位\n# en: {The headmistress waves you to the window seat without looking up from her letters. \"Read anything. Break nothing.\"|A book has been left on the window seat, open, as if for you.|Your window seat, the headmistress calls it now, as though it always was.} {Below, the lake is perfectly still.|Her quill scratches; the clock does not tick.|{season == \"autumn\": Leaves blow past at the height of the clouds.|The bridges of light come on one by one below.}}\ntext [e9f89d] = {院长头也不抬地朝窗边座位挥了挥手，继续写信。“随便读。别弄坏东西。”|窗边座位上摊着一本书，像是给你留的。|院长现在管它叫“你的窗边座位”，好像一直如此。} {下方的湖面纹丝不动。|她的笔沙沙作响；钟却不走。|{season == \"autumn\": 落叶在云的高度飘过。|下方的光之桥一座座亮起来。}}\n# en: A deep window seat high in a tower, a student reading with the lake far below, a headmistress writing letters at a desk behind.\nscene [830655] = 高塔上一个深深的窗边座位，一名学生在读书，湖在远远的下方，院长在身后的书桌前写信。\n# en: Read what she left out\nopt.left.label [95fb60] = 读她摊开的书\n# en: {A history of the seven towers, annotated in her hand. One note just says \"No.\"|A book of old law, with a dried rose marking a page about second chances.|A treatise on wards so dull it must be important. You get through it. She notices.}\nopt.left.say.1 [43c36e] = {一本七塔史，页边有她的批注。有一条只写着“不”。|一本古老的律法书，一朵干玫瑰夹在讲第二次机会的那一页。|一本讲护界的论著，枯燥得一定很重要。你读完了。她注意到了。}\n# en: Doze on the window seat\nopt.right.label [c962c8] = 在窗边座位上打个盹\n# en: {You wake to find a blanket over you and no comment at all, which is its own kind of comment.|You sleep through most of the visit. When you wake, she is still writing, and gives you a look over her spectacles that says she had hoped you would read.|You doze in your window seat. You wake to find she's moved her desk lamp so it doesn't shine in your eyes.}\nopt.right.say.1 [74052f] = {你醒来时身上多了条毯子，她一句评论也没有——这本身就是一种评论。|你睡掉了大半次拜访。醒来时她还在写，从眼镜上方看了你一眼，那眼神是说她本来希望你会读点书。|你在你的窗边座位上打盹。醒来时发现她把台灯挪了个位置，不让光照到你眼睛。}\n\n@event restricted_section_breathing_shelves\n# en: The Breathing Shelves\ntitle [e6d5c6] = 会呼吸的书架\n# en: {The black-bound books breathe in the dark, slow as sleepers. You find a chair and light a candle.|You've learned to breathe with the shelves; it helps.|Your chair is where you left it. The books shift, as if making room.} {One spine is warm to the touch.|Somewhere a page turns by itself.|{season == \"winter\": Your breath fogs. Theirs doesn't.|The candle burns low and blue.}}\ntext [50fdb9] = {黑色封皮的书在黑暗里呼吸，缓慢得像熟睡的人。你找了把椅子，点上蜡烛。|你学会了跟着书架一起呼吸，这有帮助。|你的椅子还在原处。书挪了挪，像在给你腾地方。} {有一本书脊摸上去是温的。|某处有一页书自己翻了过去。|{season == \"winter\": 你呼出白气。它们没有。|烛火烧得又低又蓝。}}\n# en: Black-bound books on tall shelves in the dark, a single candle on a reading table, the spines faintly rising and falling.\nscene [4628e1] = 黑暗中高高的书架上摆满黑色封皮的书，阅读桌上只点着一支蜡烛，书脊微微起伏。\n# en: Read until the candle gutters\nopt.left.label [15382b] = 读到蜡烛燃尽\n# en: {You read about wards that were old when the towers were new. Afterwards you can't sleep, and don't quite want to.|You read a whole chapter before you notice the book has been reading along, a line ahead.|You read in your chair by a low blue candle. The shelves breathe with you, and the chapter goes in like a held breath.}\nopt.left.say.1 [1ebbef] = {你读到了一些护界，它们在七塔落成时就已经古老。之后你睡不着，也不太想睡。|读完一整章，你才发现那本书一直在跟着读，比你快一行。|你在你的椅子上，就着低低的蓝色烛光读书。书架跟着你呼吸，那一章像一口屏住的气一样被你吸了进去。}\n# en: Read only the spines\nopt.right.label [5119aa] = 只读书脊\n# en: {Even the titles take something out of you. One of them is just your house's name.|You walk the shelves by candlelight. The spines lean towards you, and then politely away.|You walk the spines, and they make room for you as you pass. One spine you haven't seen before has no title at all.}\nopt.right.say.1 [542757] = {光是书名就让你耗神。其中一本只写着你学院的名字。|你借着烛光走过一排排书架。书脊朝你倾过来，又礼貌地退了回去。|你走过一排排书脊，它们在你经过时给你让路。有一本你从没见过的，书脊上什么字也没有。}\n\n@event restricted_section_lantern\n# en: Holding the Lantern\ntitle [7c19fb] = 提灯的人\n# en: {{friend} came with you, for courage, and is holding the lantern in both hands.|You bring a second candle this time; {friend} takes the lantern without being asked.|You know which aisles creak now. {friend} holds the lantern where you point.} {\"Did that book just sigh?\" {they} whisper{s}.|A shelf creaks behind you both.|{final_year: \"I'll miss this,\" {they} say{s}, not sounding sure.|The lantern makes the shadows walk.}}\ntext [61f06e] = {{friend}陪你来壮胆，双手提着灯。|你这回多带了一支蜡烛；{friend}不等你开口就接过了灯。|哪条过道会吱呀响，你现在都知道了。{friend}把灯举到你指的地方。} {“那本书是不是叹了口气？”{they}小声说。|你们身后有个书架吱呀了一声。|{final_year: “我会想念这个的。”{they}说，听起来没什么把握。|灯光让影子走动起来。}}\n# en: Two students in a dark library aisle, one holding a lantern high, the other reading a black book, shadows stretching behind them.\nscene [403cba] = 黑暗的图书馆过道里，一名学生高举提灯，另一名在读一本黑色的书，影子在两人身后拉长。\n# en: Read while they keep watch\nopt.left.label [9567b8] = 你读书，TA放哨\n# en: {You read; {friend} keeps watch and keeps count of every noise, out loud.|You lose an hour in a book. {friend} loses an hour staring at a shelf that keeps staring back.|You read; {friend} holds the light where you point, and the shadows walk where {they} hold{s} it. Neither of you says much on the way back.}\nopt.left.say.1 [2727b8] = {你读书，{friend}放哨，并且把每一声响动都大声数出来。|你在一本书里丢了一个钟头。{friend}丢了一个钟头，跟一个不停回瞪的书架对视。|你读书；{friend}照你指的地方举灯，影子就随着{them}举的地方走。回去的路上你们都没怎么说话。}\n# en: Take the lantern for a while\nopt.right.label [83afe8] = 换你来提一会儿灯\n# en: {You hold the light so {friend} can read. Something in the dark rustles the whole time, and you don't let it show.|{friend} reads aloud in a whisper. The books seem to lean in to listen.|You take the lantern and {friend} reads to you, a page you'll both remember. The shadows stop walking to listen.}\nopt.right.say.1 [9b56bf] = {你提着灯让{friend}读书。黑暗里一直有什么在窸窣作响，你没露出来。|{friend}小声地朗读。书好像都凑过来听。|你接过灯笼，{friend}给你念了一页，你们俩都会记住的那一页。影子都停下来听。}\n\n@event restricted_section_chained_book\n# en: The Chained Book\ntitle [1aa621] = 锁链之书\n# en: {One book here is chained to its shelf, and tonight it has opened itself to a page.|The chained book lies open at a different page, and the chain is a link longer.|The chained book has been expecting you. That's the worst part.} {The page is in a script you almost know.|The page shows a lock with no keyhole.|The page is blank until you look away.}\ntext [84ae3a] = {这里有一本书被锁链拴在书架上，今晚它自己翻开到了某一页。|锁链之书摊开在另一页，锁链长了一节。|锁链之书一直在等你。这才是最糟的。} {那一页的文字你几乎认得。|那一页画着一把没有锁孔的锁。|那一页是空白的，直到你移开目光。}\n# en: A heavy book chained to a library shelf, lying open on its own under a dim candle, the chain hanging slack.\nscene [04b543] = 一本沉重的书被锁链拴在图书馆书架上，在昏暗的烛光下自己摊开，锁链松垂。\n# en: Read the open page\nopt.left.label [a03931] = 读那翻开的一页\n# en: A thread of stored power runs up your arm from the page. {The words make sense for exactly as long as you're reading them. Afterwards you only remember that they did.|You read it, and the book turns its own page, satisfied, as if you'd passed something.|The page stays blank while you look and fills when you blink. You read it in blinks. It takes all night.}\nopt.left.say.1 [3233d6] = 一缕储存的力量从书页顺着你的手臂流上来。{那些字只在你读着的时候才有意义。读完后，你只记得它们曾经有意义。|你读完了，书满意地自己翻了一页，好像你通过了什么。|你盯着看时那一页是空白的，一眨眼就写满了。你一眨一眨地读。读了一整夜。}\n# en: {The words read you back. For a day afterwards you keep feeling underlined.|The candle goes out mid-line. In the dark, the page keeps going.|You look away and the page fills; you look back and it's blank, and smug. Your eyes ache for a day.}\nopt.left.say.2 [3ce6bf] = {那些字反过来读了你。之后一整天，你总觉得自己被划了线。|蜡烛在读到一半时灭了。黑暗中，那一页还在继续。|你一移开目光那页就写满了；一回头又是空白，还带着得意。你的眼睛疼了一天。}\n# en: Close it and step back\nopt.right.label [fad0c0] = 合上它，退后\n# en: As the cover shuts, something that had been quietly drawing on your magic lets go. {The chain rattles as the cover shuts, like a sigh. You stand with your back to it until your hands stop shaking.|You close it gently, the way you'd close a door on someone sleeping.|You close it before it can finish expecting you. The chain lies still, a link shorter.}\nopt.right.say.1 [eb31e6] = 封面合上时，那个一直悄悄汲取你魔力的东西松开了。{锁链哗啦一响，像一声叹息。你背对着它站着，直到手不再发抖。|你轻轻地合上它，就像给熟睡的人关门。|你在它把期待说完之前合上了它。锁链静静躺着，短了一节。}\n\n@event restricted_section_handleless_door\n# en: The Door Without a Handle\ntitle [8f3217] = 没有把手的门\n# en: {At the very back is a door with no handle and no window. On it, in chalk that never smudges, HOLLOW STUDIES, BY LEAVE OF THE HEADMISTRESS.|Tonight the door without a handle is warm, like the outer door.|You've read its chalk so often you could recite it.} Underneath, smaller: \"Umbral Theory II. Astromancy II. And know what the founders made, and why.\"\ntext [9b1c7d] = {最深处有一扇没有把手也没有窗的门。门上用永不褪色的粉笔写着：空洞研究，须经院长准许。|今晚，那扇没有把手的门是温的，和外面那扇门一样。|门上的粉笔字你读得太多，都能背下来了。} 下面一行小字：“暗影理论 II。星象学 II。并且知道创始者造了什么，又为何而造。”\n# en: A plain door with no handle at the back of a dark library, chalk writing on it that has not smudged in years.\nscene [2de116] = 漆黑图书馆的最深处，一扇没有把手的朴素木门，门上的粉笔字多年未曾模糊。\n# en: Knock\nopt.left.label [60f31f] = 敲门\n# en: {Nobody answers. Then, very far away, a pleasant voice says, \"Not yet.\"|Your knock doesn't echo. It just stops, as if something on the other side caught it. Next day the headmistress nods to you in the corridor, as if she heard.|You knock, as you always do. This time, from far away, someone knocks back, once, as if to say they've noted it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [db6448] = {没人应。然后，在很远很远的地方，一个温和的声音说：“还不是时候。”|你的敲门声没有回音。它就那么停了，像门另一边有什么接住了它。第二天，院长在走廊里朝你点了点头，好像她听见了。|你像往常一样敲了门。这一回，在很远的地方，有人回敲了一下，好像在说已经记下了。}\n# en: Copy the chalk into your notes\nopt.right.label [d25ac8] = 把粉笔字抄进笔记\n# en: Copying the chalk draws on your magic like a cold draught. {The chalk makes your pencil cold. Two courses and a question, and the question is the hard part.|You copy it word for word. Back in your room, your notes have the same words, and one more underline than you made.|You copy it from memory and check it against the door. The chalk agrees with you, which feels like being agreed with.}\nopt.right.say.1 [b95b40] = 抄写粉笔字像一阵冷风似的抽走你的魔力。{粉笔字让你的铅笔变得冰凉。两门课和一个问题，难的是那个问题。|你一字不差地抄了下来。回到房间，笔记上还是那些字，却比你画的多了一道下划线。|你凭记忆抄下来，再对着门核对。粉笔字和你一致，感觉像被认可了。}\n\n@event gargoyle_passage_old_marks\n# en: Marks on the Wall\ntitle [1ff1e7] = 墙上的刻痕\n# en: {The passage walls are covered in initials, dates and tiny arrows, scratched by generations who were never here.|Among the marks on the wall is a new one, still sharp.|You know these walls now: the arrows that lie, the dates that can't be right, the initials that keep coming back.} {The wind sings through the gargoyle's teeth.|Below, a door closes that shouldn't be there.|{season == \"winter\": Frost picks out every letter.|Moths sleep in the deeper scratches.}}\ntext [b1f5d2] = {通道的墙上刻满了缩写、日期和小箭头，出自一代又一代“从没来过这里”的人。|墙上的刻痕里多了一道新的，还很锋利。|这些墙你已经熟了：说谎的箭头，不可能对的日期，反复出现的缩写。} {风穿过石像鬼的牙缝唱着歌。|下方，一扇不该存在的门关上了。|{season == \"winter\": 霜把每个字母都描了出来。|飞蛾睡在较深的刻痕里。}}\n# en: A narrow stone passage behind a gargoyle, its walls covered in scratched initials, dates and tiny arrows, lit by a stub of candle.\nscene [5e6982] = 石像鬼身后一条狭窄的石头通道，墙上刻满缩写、日期和小箭头，一截蜡烛头照着它们。\n# en: Add your own mark\nopt.left.label [5a8fd8] = 刻下你自己的记号\n# en: {You scratch your initials small and low, with a tiny arrow pointing nowhere. It feels like signing something.|{final_year and flag(left_wall_mark): You add the year beside your old mark. It looks so much younger than you.|A knuckle pays for it. The mark is crooked and entirely yours.}|You add your mark beside the initials that keep coming back. Next time, there's a tiny arrow pointing to yours.}\nopt.left.say.1 [c9b8ca] = {你把缩写刻得又小又低，再加一个指向虚空的小箭头。感觉像在签什么契约。|{final_year and flag(left_wall_mark): 你在旧记号旁添上了年份。它看起来比你年轻太多。|赔进去一个指关节。记号歪歪扭扭，完全属于你。}|你在那组反复出现的缩写旁刻下自己的记号。下次来时，有个小箭头指向了你的。}\n# en: Follow an old arrow\nopt.right.label [40d53f] = 跟着旧箭头走\n# en: {The arrow leads to another arrow, which leads to a wall, which has a joke scratched on it.|The arrow leads to a slit window with a view of the lake no plan admits to. Your knees disagree with the route.|You follow the arrow you know lies, to see how it lies. It lies beautifully, all the way to a view of the stars.}\nopt.right.say.1 [d4dff3] = {箭头指向另一个箭头，又指向一堵墙，墙上刻着一个笑话。|箭头通向一道窄窗，窗外的湖景没有哪张地图承认。你的膝盖不同意这条路线。|你顺着那个你知道会撒谎的箭头走，想看看它怎么撒谎。它撒得很漂亮，一路把你带到了能看星星的地方。}\n\n@event gargoyle_passage_offering_dish\n# en: The Gargoyle's Dish\ntitle [695172] = 石像鬼的碟子\n# en: {The third gargoyle has a dish worn into its paw, and in the dish are offerings, a button, a biscuit, a bent key.|Someone has left the gargoyle a single grape, peeled.|The gargoyle looks at you the way a landlord looks at the first of the month.} {Tonight it looks hungry.|Tonight it looks smug.|{season == \"summer\": The stone is still warm from the day.|Rain runs out of its mouth in a steady complaint.}}\ntext [0e642d] = {第三座石像鬼的爪子上磨出了一只碟子，碟里放着供品——一颗纽扣，一块饼干，一把弯钥匙。|有人给石像鬼留了一颗葡萄，还剥了皮。|石像鬼看你的眼神，就像房东在月初看房客。} {今晚它看起来很饿。|今晚它看起来很得意。|{season == \"summer\": 石头还留着白天的余温。|雨水从它嘴里流出来，像一串不停的抱怨。}}\n# en: A weathered gargoyle on a moonlit parapet with a dish worn into its stone paw, holding a button, a biscuit and a bent key.\nscene [1fa1f4] = 月光下的护墙上，一座饱经风霜的石像鬼，石爪上磨出一只碟子，盛着一颗纽扣、一块饼干和一把弯钥匙。\n# en: Leave a few crowns in the dish\nopt.left.label [adb70b] = 往碟里放几枚克朗\n# en: {By morning the crowns are gone, and so is the biscuit. Nobody admits to either.|The crowns clink in. The gargoyle's expression doesn't change, but the wind drops.|You pay the gargoyle its crowns, like rent. The night is calm, and the stair doesn't move once on your way back.}\nopt.left.say.1 [a9c805] = {第二天早上，克朗不见了，饼干也不见了。谁都不承认。|几枚克朗叮当落进碟里。石像鬼的表情没变，但风停了。|你像交房租一样把克朗付给石像鬼。夜很平静，回去的路上楼梯一次也没挪。}\n# en: Borrow the biscuit\nopt.right.label [d81344] = 借走那块饼干\n# en: {It's a very good biscuit. Somebody's mother made it. You feel a little bad, and then less.|Shortbread, still crisp. The gargoyle allows it, this once.|You borrow the peeled grape. The gargoyle's look follows you down the stair, but the grape is excellent.}\nopt.right.say.1 [67d63f] = {是块很好的饼干。准是谁的母亲做的。你有点愧疚，然后愧疚少了些。|黄油酥饼，还是脆的。石像鬼这回准了。|你借走了那颗剥好的葡萄。石像鬼的目光一路跟着你下楼，可葡萄好吃极了。}\n# en: {It's made of stone. The gargoyle's joke, and it's been waiting a long time to tell it.|The wind gets up the moment you take it, and stays up all night, rattling your window.|You take the grape, and the gargoyle collects. Your boots are full of rainwater by the bottom of the stair.}\nopt.right.say.2 [d84807] = {是石头做的。石像鬼的玩笑，它等了很久才等到有人上当。|你一拿起它，风就刮了起来，刮了一整夜，把你的窗户摇得哐哐响。|你拿了葡萄，石像鬼也来收账了。走到楼梯底时，你的靴子里灌满了雨水。}\n\n@event gargoyle_passage_parapet_cards\n# en: Cards on the Parapet\ntitle [d8d199] = 护墙上的牌局\n# en: {On the parapet, by lantern-light, {here} is dealing cards for buttons, and crowns for anyone who dares.|{here} has a new deck, and the pot of buttons has grown.|{here} deals you in before you've sat down.} {The wind keeps trying to take the jack.|A pigeon watches the game with too much interest.|{final_year: \"One more for the road,\" {they} say{s}.|The lake below holds the lantern perfectly still.}}\ntext [e4e500] = {护墙上，借着灯光，{here}正以纽扣为注发牌，谁有胆量也可以押克朗。|{here}换了一副新牌，纽扣堆也变大了。|你还没坐下，{here}就给你发了牌。} {风一直想把那张杰克卷走。|一只鸽子对牌局关心得过了头。|{final_year: “最后再来一局。”{they}说。|下方的湖把灯影托得纹丝不动。}}\n# en: Two students playing cards by lantern-light on a high castle parapet, a pot of buttons between them and the lake far below.\nscene [68ef4e] = 两名学生借着灯光在城堡高处的护墙上打牌，中间一堆纽扣，湖在远远的下方。\n# en: Play a hand\nopt.left.label [fc6de9] = 来一手\n# en: {cast_as(here, corvin): {\"Well, look at you,\" Corvin says, paying out. \"Who taught you that? Don't say me.\"|\"Remind me never to owe you money,\" Corvin tells the pigeon, and pays you in buttons and, grudgingly, crowns.|Corvin deals you in, loses, and laughs. \"You're getting good. I'll have to start cheating properly.\"}|{You win the pot, buttons and a few actual crowns.|You bluff with nothing and the wind turns your card over at the right moment. You take the buttons, and the crowns under them, with dignity.|You're dealt in before you sit and win before you've warmed up. The pot is mostly buttons, and some of it is crowns.}}\nopt.left.say.1 [dcb430] = {cast_as(here, corvin): {“哟，瞧瞧你，”科尔文边付账边说，“谁教你的？别说是我。”|“提醒我永远别欠你钱。”科尔文对鸽子说，然后用纽扣付了账，又不情不愿地添了几枚克朗。|科尔文给你发牌，输了，笑起来。“你越来越厉害了。我得开始认真作弊了。”}|{你赢下了那堆纽扣，外加几枚真克朗。|你手里什么都没有却虚张声势，风恰好在对的时候掀开了你的牌。你体面地收下了纽扣，还有压在下面的克朗。|你还没坐下就被发了牌，还没暖手就赢了。赌注大多是纽扣，也有些克朗。}}\n# en: {cast_as(here, corvin): {Corvin scoops the pot and winces. \"Saint, you're the worst liar I love playing.\" He gives you your buttons back, at least.|\"Never bluff a man who owes three people money,\" Corvin says, kindly.|Corvin deals from the bottom and you know it and can't prove it. You lose with a smile, which annoys him.}|{You lose your buttons, then some crowns, then the will to bluff.|The wind takes your best card over the parapet. It was the only one you had.|You lose the hand to a card the pigeon was standing on. The pigeon will not be questioned.}}\nopt.left.say.2 [dfaef2] = {cast_as(here, corvin): {科尔文把赌注一扫而空，龇了龇牙。“圣人，你是我最爱的最烂骗子。”至少他把你的纽扣还给了你。|“别跟一个欠了三个人钱的人诈牌。”科尔文和气地说。|科尔文从牌底发牌，你知道却证明不了。你笑着输了，这让他很恼火。}|{你输光了纽扣，又输了几枚克朗，最后连虚张声势的心气也输了。|风把你最好的那张牌卷过了护墙。那也是你唯一一张好牌。|你输给了一张鸽子一直踩着的牌。鸽子拒绝接受质询。}}\n# en: Watch the lake instead\nopt.right.label [1e5c6c] = 还是看湖吧\n# en: {cast_as(here, corvin): {\"Saint,\" Corvin says, not looking up, \"you'll never be rich.\" He passes you his scarf.|Corvin deals you out without a word and tosses you his scarf when the wind picks up.|Corvin deals you out without asking and hands you his scarf before the wind does. \"Lake's nice tonight,\" he says. It is.}|{You sit with your back to the stone and watch the dark water while the cards slap behind you. It's cold, and peaceful.|You count the lights in the village until the game's over.|You watch the lake hold the lantern still. Behind you, someone wins, and someone else blames the pigeon.}}\nopt.right.say.1 [5bd2f1] = {cast_as(here, corvin): {“圣人，”科尔文头也不抬地说，“你这辈子发不了财。”他把围巾递给了你。|科尔文一言不发地不再给你发牌，风大起来时，把围巾扔给了你。|科尔文没问就不给你发牌了，风还没起就把围巾递给了你。“今晚湖不错，”他说。确实不错。}|{你背靠石墙，看着漆黑的湖水，身后纸牌啪啪作响。很冷，也很安宁。|你数着山下小镇的灯火，一直数到牌局结束。|你看着湖面把灯光托得稳稳的。身后有人赢了，另一个人怪到鸽子头上。}}\n\n@event seventh_tower_stair_count\n# en: The Stair That Changes\ntitle [cf510f] = 会变的楼梯\n# en: {The seventh tower's stair never has the same number of steps twice. Scholars count it to learn how the castle bends; everyone else says it lets you up faster if you don't look.|Back on the stair that won't keep its count. You've started chalking each total on the wall; no two agree.|You've stopped expecting the stair to agree with you. You count anyway; it's a kind of manners.} {Dust lies thick on every step but yours.|The moon follows you from window to window.|{season == \"winter\": The cold comes up through your shoes.|Your candle leans towards the top, like a dog on a lead.}}\ntext [04d107] = {第七塔的楼梯从来不会两次有同样多的台阶。学者们数它，是为了弄懂城堡是怎么弯折的；其他人则说，你不看，它就让你上得更快。|又来到这道数不准的楼梯。你开始用粉笔把每次的总数写在墙上；没有两个是一样的。|你已经不指望楼梯跟你意见一致了。你照数不误，这算一种礼貌。} {每级台阶都积着厚灰，只有你踩过的除外。|月亮从一扇窗跟到另一扇窗。|{season == \"winter\": 寒气从鞋底往上钻。|你的烛火朝塔顶倾斜，像一条被牵着的狗。}}\n# en: A narrow spiral stair inside a dusty stone tower, a single candle climbing it, moonlight falling through slit windows.\nscene [e0f863] = 落满灰尘的石塔里一道狭窄的螺旋楼梯，一支蜡烛向上移动，月光从细窄的窗缝洒进来。\n# en: Count every step\nopt.left.label [08b067] = 一级一级地数\n# en: {You lose count twice and get two answers, both certain, both wrong.|Up, one number; down, one more. You don't stay to find the extra step.|{final_year and flag(counted_seventh_stair): The count matches your very first climb. You can't say why that frightens you.|You count a step that isn't there, and your foot believes you.}}\nopt.left.say.1 [a74ac1] = {你数乱了两次，得出两个答案，都很笃定，都不对。|上楼一个数，下楼多一级。你没留下来找那多出来的一级。|{final_year and flag(counted_seventh_stair): 这次的数字和你第一次爬时一模一样。你说不出为什么这让你害怕。|你数到了一级并不存在的台阶，而你的脚信了。}}\n# en: Climb without counting\nopt.right.label [c06bdf] = 不数，直接爬\n# en: {You climb by feel, in the dark between windows. The stair seems to like that better.|You stop counting and the climb is suddenly short, as if the tower was in a hurry too.|You climb without counting, candle leaning ahead. You're at the top before you've thought about it, and it feels allowed.}\nopt.right.say.1 [f4397d] = {你在窗与窗之间的黑暗里摸着爬。楼梯似乎更喜欢这样。|你一不数，路就忽然变短了，好像塔也急着赶路。|你不数台阶，烛火在前面倾着。还没来得及想，你已经到了顶上，感觉像是被允许的。}\n\n@event seventh_tower_dust_room\n# en: Dust and Moonlight\ntitle [17a713] = 尘埃与月光\n# en: {At the top is one room of dust and moonlight, with a star-shaped hollow in the wall. The footprints in the dust aren't yours.|More footprints in the dust; some are yours now.|The room keeps your footprints like a guest book.} {The moon sits in the window as if it lives here.|The hollow in the wall is darker than the night.|A moth beats at the glass from outside, trying to get in.}\ntext [4d4f05] = {塔顶只有一间满是尘埃与月光的房间，墙上有一个星形的凹槽。灰尘里的脚印不是你的。|灰尘里的脚印更多了；有些是你的了。|这间房像本访客簿一样收着你的脚印。} {月亮坐在窗里，好像住在这儿。|墙上的凹槽比夜色还黑。|一只飞蛾在窗外扑打玻璃，想要进来。}\n# en: A bare round room at the top of a tower, thick dust on the floor marked by footprints, moonlight slanting through one window onto a star-shaped hollow in the wall.\nscene [e73e97] = 塔顶一间空荡的圆形房间，地上厚厚的灰尘留着脚印，月光从唯一的窗户斜照在墙上一个星形凹槽上。\n# en: Sit in the moonlight\nopt.left.label [dda8de] = 坐在月光里\n# en: {The light is cold and kind. You sit until your shadow forgets which way to fall.|You sit where the moon is brightest. It feels like being looked at by someone very old and very tired.|You sit with the moth tapping at the glass and the moon in the window. Nothing happens, very deeply, for an hour.}\nopt.left.say.1 [4c9aa0] = {月光冷，却温柔。你一直坐到影子忘了该往哪边倒。|你坐在月光最亮的地方。感觉像被一个非常古老、非常疲倦的人注视着。|你坐着，飞蛾在玻璃上轻叩，月亮在窗里。一个钟头里，什么也没发生，深深地什么也没发生。}\n# en: Sweep away your footprints\nopt.right.label [6070ae] = 抹掉你的脚印\n# en: {You sweep your way out backwards with your sleeve. The dust settles behind you, and then, you'd swear, rearranges itself.|You cough your way to the door. The other footprints you leave alone. They seem to want to stay.|You sweep your prints away and leave the others. By the next visit, yours are back, in the same places.}\nopt.right.say.1 [69ac17] = {你用袖子边退边扫出了门。灰尘在你身后落定，然后——你敢发誓——自己重新排列了一遍。|你一路咳着走到门口。别人的脚印你没动。它们似乎想留下。|你扫掉了自己的脚印，留下了别人的。下次来时，你的又回来了，在原来的位置。}\n\n@event seventh_tower_lake_window\n# en: The Window Over the Lake\ntitle [749eec] = 临湖的窗\n# en: {The tower's one window looks down on the Mirror Lake, and the latch isn't locked. They say if you open it, you can hear the lake.|The latch moves more easily than last time, as if someone else has been practising.|The window. You've stood here often enough that your hand finds the latch before you decide anything.} {The lake is so still it looks painted.|There's a light on the water that isn't the moon's.|{season == \"winter\": The lake is grey and hard.|Mist lies on the water.}}\ntext [a83495] = {塔里唯一的窗俯瞰着镜湖，窗闩没有锁。据说打开它，就能听见湖的声音。|窗闩比上次更好拨了，好像另有人在练习。|这扇窗。你在这里站过太多次，还没拿定主意，手就已经找到了窗闩。} {湖面静得像画上去的。|水面上有一点光，不是月亮的。|{season == \"winter\": 湖面灰而坚硬。|雾躺在水面上。}}\n# en: A small arched window high in a tower, its latch half open, the Mirror Lake lying still and silver far below.\nscene [4ee686] = 高塔上一扇小小的拱窗，窗闩半开，镜湖静静地泛着银光，躺在远远的下方。\n# en: Open it and listen\nopt.left.label [846a0a] = 打开窗听一听\n# en: {You hear water, and under it a sound too low to be wind. You decide it was the pipes, and don't quite believe yourself.|Nothing but wind and water. You stay until you're sure, and you're never quite sure.|You open it without deciding to. The lake is quiet, and then not, and then quiet again, as if it changed its mind about you.}\nopt.left.say.1 [5c06d2] = {你听见水声，水声底下还有一种低得不像风的声音。你认定那是水管，自己却不太信。|只有风声和水声。你一直待到确定为止，可你从没真正确定过。|你还没决定，手就把窗打开了。湖很安静，然后不安静，然后又安静了，像是对你改了主意。}\n# en: {The cold comes in and won't leave. For days you hear dripping in dry rooms.|The lake is silent, entirely, the way a room goes silent when you walk in on a conversation.|The window opens on its own before your hand gets there. You shut it fast, and the latch is cold for a week.}\nopt.left.say.2 [aaa95c] = {寒气钻进来就不走了。之后好几天，你在干燥的房间里也听见滴水声。|湖彻底安静了，就像你走进一间屋子时，屋里的谈话戛然而止。|你的手还没碰到，窗就自己开了。你赶紧关上，窗闩冷了一个星期。}\n# en: Leave it latched\nopt.right.label [d532fe] = 让它闩着\n# en: {You leave the latch alone and go down to bed, and lie awake wondering what you didn't hear.|You put your palm flat on the cold glass instead. The lake does nothing. It's very good at that.|You leave it latched and rest your hand on the latch a while. It feels like keeping a promise to someone.}\nopt.right.say.1 [64571c] = {你没碰窗闩，下楼睡觉，然后醒着躺了半夜，想着你没听见的是什么。|你把手掌平贴在冰冷的玻璃上。湖什么也没做。它很擅长这个。|你没打开，只是把手在窗闩上搁了一会儿。感觉像是在对谁守一个诺言。}\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/pools/clubs_jobs.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/pools/clubs_jobs.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event duelling_ward_line\n# en: The Ward Line\ntitle [56f9d8] = 护盾阵线\n# en: {Ward drill. Members hold shields in a line while the seniors lob stinging hexes down the hall.|You know by now which senior aims low, and which one apologises.|You could hold a shield in your sleep, and one night you did.} {A dummy at the end of the line has a shield of its own, out of pity.|Someone has chalked a tally of scorched floorboards. It is long.|A first-year holds the shield upside down and does surprisingly well.}\ntext [86fffa] = {护盾演练。社员排成一列举着护盾，高年级生从大厅那头往这边扔刺痛咒。|你早就知道哪位学长专往低处打，哪位打完还会道歉。|你闭着眼都能举盾，有一晚还真在梦里举了一整夜。} {队尾的假人也被分到一面盾，纯属同情。|有人用粉笔记下了烧焦的地板数，那一串很长。|一个一年级生把盾举反了，居然撑得出奇地好。}\n# en: A row of students holding shimmering shields across a scorched hall while seniors lob sparks at them.\nscene [271b31] = 一排学生在焦黑的大厅里举着微光闪烁的护盾，高年级生朝他们抛掷火花。\n# en: Take the front of the line\nopt.left.label [d28df6] = 站到队伍最前面\n# en: {Your shield takes everything and hums about it afterwards.|Hexes splash off your ward like rain off a good hat.|You hold until the bell. {around(lyra): Lyra|The senior on the whistle} writes something down, which is either praise or a list of your faults.}\nopt.left.say.1 [c1da68] = {你的护盾照单全收，事后还嗡嗡响了好一阵。|咒语从你的护界上溅开，像雨水打在一顶好帽子上。|你一直撑到铃响。{around(lyra): 莱拉|吹哨的高年级生}记了点什么，要么是夸奖，要么是你的毛病清单。}\n# en: {A hex finds the gap by your elbow. Your arm fizzes until supper.|Your ward folds like wet paper. The senior apologises, mostly to the floor.|You hold the front half asleep, as usual, and a hex wakes you. Your arm fizzes until supper, and the first-year beside you holds.}\nopt.left.say.2 [4d8fe9] = {一道咒语钻进你手肘边的空隙，整条胳膊麻到晚饭。|你的护盾像湿纸一样折了。那位学长道了歉，主要是对着地板。|你照常半睡半醒地守在前排，一道恶咒把你惊醒了。你的胳膊一直麻到晚饭，旁边那个一年级生却守住了。}\n# en: Hold the back row tonight\nopt.right.label [a28ef7] = 今晚待在后排\n# en: {From the back you watch how the good shields bend instead of break. Nobody watches you.|You spend the drill studying the front row's mistakes, which feels useful and looks like hiding.|Safe at the back, you learn a great deal about the ceiling.}\nopt.right.say.1 [b86875] = {在后排，你看清了好护盾是弯而不折的。没人看你。|你整场都在研究前排的失误，感觉很有用，看起来很像在躲。|在安全的后排，你对天花板有了深入了解。}\n# en: Hold the line with a real ward\nopt.up.label [c704b4] = 用真正的护界守住阵线\n# en: {You raise a proper ward, the one from Umbral Theory, and the seniors' hexes slide off it like rain off glass. {around(lyra): Lyra stops the drill to look.|The seniors stop the drill to look.}|Your ward holds the whole line's flank. Your arm aches from the mana, not the hexes.|You raise the real ward without thinking, the way you hold a shield in your sleep. The seniors give up hexing your end of the line.}\nopt.up.say.1 [b48b54] = {你立起一道真正的护界，暗影理论课上学的那种，高年级生的恶咒从上面滑开，像雨水滑过玻璃。{around(lyra): 莱拉停下训练来看。|高年级生们停下训练来看。}|你的护界守住了整条阵线的侧翼。你的胳膊酸痛，是因为法力，不是因为恶咒。|你不假思索就立起了真正的护界，就像你睡着都能举盾一样。高年级生们放弃了朝你这头放恶咒。}\n\n@event duelling_dummy_mending\n# en: Mending the Dummies\ntitle [d3be80] = 缝补假人\n# en: {After practice someone has to mend the dummies, and tonight the rota says it's you.|Mending night again, and the rota has your name on it.|You know every dummy here by its scars. They look at you with something like trust.} {The tall dummy is missing an arm. Someone always takes the arm.|One dummy is now mostly scorch mark.|Someone has painted a new name on a dummy's chest, and a grudge beneath it.}\ntext [556795] = {练习结束后总得有人缝补假人，值日表上今晚写的是你。|又是缝补之夜，值日表上又有你的名字。|这里每个假人身上的伤疤你都认得。它们看你的眼神有点像信任了。} {那个高个子假人少了一条胳膊。总有人把胳膊拿走。|有个假人现在基本只剩焦痕。|有人在一个假人胸口画了新名字，下面还写着一笔旧怨。}\n# en: A student alone in a duelling hall at night, stitching straw back into a battered practice dummy.\nscene [29efd2] = 夜里，一个学生独自坐在决斗厅里，把稻草塞回一个破旧的练习假人。\n# en: Stay late and stitch them up\nopt.left.label [84da41] = 留下来把它们缝好\n# en: {By midnight every dummy is whole and you have straw in places straw should not be.|You sew one dummy's arm on slightly backwards. It looks more dangerous for it.|The hall is quiet, the needle is steady, and for an hour nobody is shouting about footwork.}\nopt.left.say.1 [2293d0] = {到午夜，所有假人都完好如初，你身上也多了几处不该有稻草的地方。|你把一个假人的胳膊缝反了。它看起来反倒更危险了。|大厅很安静，针脚很稳，整整一个钟头没人嚷嚷步法。}\n# en: Leave them for the morning\nopt.right.label [3c93be] = 留到明早再说\n# en: {In the morning the dummies are still in pieces, and so is the seniors' patience.|You slip out early. Someone finds the dummies and puts your name on next week's rota, underlined twice.|You leave them. In the morning the dummy with the new name has been mended by someone else, with your name painted under the grudge.}\nopt.right.say.1 [552491] = {第二天早上，假人们还是一地零件，高年级生们的耐心也是。|你提前溜了。有人发现了这些假人，把你的名字写进下周值日表，还画了两道线。|你没管它们。第二天早上，那个写着新名字的假人被别人补好了，怨气下面还被画上了你的名字。}\n\n@event duelling_refereeing_lyra\n# en: Refereeing Lyra\ntitle [9a9369] = 给莱拉当裁判\n# en: {Officers referee, so tonight you hold the whistle while Lyra duels a second-year.|You know the circle's chalk by heart now, and Lyra's footwork better.|{rel(lyra) >= 12: Lyra no longer asks who is refereeing. She looks for you.|The whistle has a dent in it from some earlier, unrecorded argument.}} {Her last spell landed a breath after the bell.|She stepped out of the circle by a boot's width, and knows it.|Her last hex went in after her opponent had called the yield.} {final_year: One of the last bouts you will call for her.|}\ntext [e5a2df] = {干部要当裁判，于是今晚你拿着哨子，看莱拉和一个二年级生对决。|决斗圈的粉笔线你已烂熟于心，莱拉的步法你更熟。|{rel(lyra) >= 12: 莱拉已经不问谁当裁判了。她直接找你。|哨子上有个凹痕，来自某次没记录在案的争执。}} {她最后一道咒语比铃声晚了一口气。|她踏出圈外一只靴子宽，她自己也知道。|她最后一道恶咒是在对手喊了认输之后才打出去的。} {final_year: 这是你最后几次为她判比赛了。|}\n# en: A student with a whistle watching a noble duellist in a chalk circle, her opponent's shield just failing.\nscene [a5fea0] = 一个拿着哨子的学生注视着圈中的贵族决斗者，她对手的护盾正要崩溃。\n# en: Call it against her\nopt.left.label [dff616] = 判她犯规\n# en: {\"Noted,\" Lyra says, in a voice that could chill wine. Next day her feet are perfect.|Lyra stares at you, then shakes her opponent's hand. Later a cup of tea appears on your bench.|The hall goes silent. Lyra bows to the second-year, very correctly, and to you, slightly less.}\nopt.left.say.1 [f1b129] = {“记下了。”莱拉说，那声音能把酒冰镇。第二天，她的步法无懈可击。|莱拉盯着你看了好一会儿，然后和对手握了手。后来你的长凳上出现了一杯茶。|大厅一片寂静。莱拉向那个二年级生行礼，极其标准；向你行礼，稍逊一些。}\n# en: Let it stand\nopt.right.label [d81aa7] = 维持原判\n# en: {Lyra wins. The second-year looks at you, and you find something interesting about your whistle.|Nobody objects. Lyra does not thank you; if anything, she looks a little disappointed in you.|The bout goes in the book as hers. It sits there all week like a stone in your shoe.}\nopt.right.say.1 [f9ce12] = {莱拉赢了。那个二年级生看向你，你忽然觉得自己的哨子很有意思。|没人反对。莱拉没有谢你，甚至显得对你有点失望。|这场比赛作为她的胜场记进了册子，整整一周都像鞋里的一颗石子。}\n\n@event duelling_running_practice\n# en: Running Practice\ntitle [9e7aed] = 主持练习\n# en: A note is pinned to the hall door in Lyra's sharp hand: \"Called home. You are running practice. Footwork first. Do not let anyone set the ceiling on fire again.\" The whole Circle turns to see what you will say.\ntext [189cd9] = 决斗厅门上钉着一张字条，是莱拉凌厉的笔迹：“家里有事。你来主持练习。先练步法。别再让任何人把天花板点着。”整个决斗社都转过头来，看你要说什么。\n# en: A note pinned to a duelling hall door, a crowd of students waiting inside in chalk circles.\nscene [6d7864] = 决斗厅门上钉着一张字条，门内一群学生站在粉笔圈里等着。\n# en: Run it exactly her way\nopt.left.label [b06e03] = 完全照她的方式来\n# en: Footwork, then shields, then footwork again. Nobody sets the ceiling on fire. Lyra reads your practice log on her return and says \"adequate\", then reads it again when she thinks you've gone.\nopt.left.say.1 [a1b74e] = 步法，护盾，再练步法。没人把天花板点着。莱拉回来读了你的练习记录，说了句“还行”，又趁她以为你走了的时候读了一遍。\n# en: Run it your own way\nopt.right.label [68c6ad] = 按你自己的方式来\n# en: You turn the footwork drill into a game of tag with stinging hexes. The ceiling only smoulders. A week later Lyra runs the same game and calls it her idea, which is the highest praise she has.\nopt.right.say.1 [b32c5f] = 你把步法练习改成了带刺痛咒的捉人游戏。天花板只是冒了点烟。一周后莱拉照搬这个游戏，说是她自己的主意——这是她能给出的最高赞美。\n\n@event lumen_ledger_letters_box\n# en: The Letters Box\ntitle [4a9d28] = 读者来信箱\n# en: {The letters box is full. Stringers sort it, Nessa says, having no dignity yet to lose.|Sorting day. A regular correspondent has started numbering their letters.|Under the stairs the box has burst its lid. You know the regulars by their handwriting now.} {Porridge complaints, a poem about the lake and a sock.|A furious letter about the crossword, in a professor's hand.|An anonymous tip in lemon juice, noticed only because it smells nice.}\ntext [547c71] = {读者来信箱满了。内莎说，来信由见习记者来分，因为他们还没有尊严可丢。|分信日。有位常客开始给自己的信编号了。|楼梯下，信箱被撑开了盖。如今你光看笔迹就认得出那些常客。} {抱怨粥的信、一首写湖的诗，还有一只袜子。|一封痛骂填字游戏的信，显然是某位教授的笔迹。|一条用柠檬汁写的匿名线索，你注意到它只因为它闻起来很香。}\n# en: A battered wooden box under a library staircase, overflowing with folded letters and one sock.\nscene [83935d] = 图书馆楼梯下一只破旧的木箱，塞满了折好的信，还有一只袜子。\n# en: Print the angriest letter\nopt.left.label [321002] = 刊登最愤怒的那封\n# en: {It runs on page four. By breakfast several people are angry about the angry letter, which Nessa calls \"engagement\".|The letter runs. Its author glares at you over the porridge, as if you had written it.|You print the lemon-juice tip, warmed over a candle. Half the castle holds the page to the fire. Nessa calls it the best-selling issue of term.}\nopt.left.say.1 [60d770] = {它登在第四版。到早饭时，已经有好几个人对这封愤怒的信感到愤怒，内莎管这叫“读者互动”。|信登出去了。写信人在早餐桌上瞪着你，好像信是你写的。|你把那条柠檬汁写的线报放在蜡烛上烘出字来，登了出去。半个城堡的人都把那一页凑到火边烤。内莎说这是本学期卖得最好的一期。}\n# en: Answer them all by hand\nopt.right.label [931b86] = 逐一亲笔回信\n# en: {You write back to every one of them, even the ones that weren't really letters. Your hand cramps; somewhere in the castle a lonely first-year gets a reply.|A pile of replies, all polite. Nessa calls it \"a waste of a perfectly good grudge\", then borrows your phrasing.|You write back to every regular, by number. The numbering correspondent replies with a number of their own, and a thank-you.}\nopt.right.say.1 [c7409a] = {你给每一封都回了信，连那些算不上信的也回了。手抽筋了，但城堡某处，一个孤单的一年级生收到了回信。|一摞回信，封封客气。内莎说这是“浪费了一份上好的怨气”，然后借用了你的措辞。|你按编号给每位常客回了信。那位编号的通信者回了一封，也编了号，还附上一句谢谢。}\n\n@event lumen_ledger_courtyard_quotes\n# en: A Word from the Courtyard\ntitle [2bef78] = 庭院之声\n# en: {Every issue needs a word from the courtyard. One question, many opinions.|Word has got round the courtyard that the Ledger is asking questions.|Half the courtyard now takes the long way round your notebook.} This week: {is the soup getting thinner?|who will win the Spring Tournament?|is the lake louder lately?} {season == \"winter\": Everyone is hurrying indoors.|{Some second-years pretend not to see you.|A passing professor looks very quotable.|A prefect makes a detour to avoid you, which is a quote of a sort.}}\ntext [8f39dd] = {每期报纸都要一段“庭院之声”。一个问题，众说纷纭。|庭院里已经传开了，说《流明纪事报》在到处提问。|如今半个庭院见了你的本子都绕道走。} 本周的问题是{汤是不是越来越稀了？|谁会赢得春季锦标赛？|湖最近是不是更吵了？} {season == \"winter\": 人人都急着往屋里走。|{几个二年级生假装没看见你。|一位路过的教授看上去很值得引用。|一位级长为了躲你特意绕了路，这也算是一句引语。}}\n# en: A student with a notebook stopping passers-by in a busy courtyard.\nscene [3bd888] = 一个拿着笔记本的学生在热闹的庭院里拦住路人。\n# en: Stop strangers and ask\nopt.left.label [91c2da] = 拦住陌生人提问\n# en: {A gardener, a prefect and a very small first-year give you the best quotes of the term. The first-year's is unprintable, and perfect.|You get a quote so good Nessa moves it to the front page and pretends it was her idea to ask.|You ask the question to the people who take the long way round. They answer, to get rid of you, and the answers are the best yet.}\nopt.left.say.1 [b843d5] = {一位园丁、一位级长和一个特别小的一年级生给了你本学期最好的引语。那个一年级生的没法登，但完美。|你拿到一句绝妙的引语，内莎把它挪上头版，还假装是她想到要去问的。|你专问那些绕远路躲你的人。他们为了打发你才回答，答案却是到目前为止最好的。}\n# en: {Everyone is busy, cold or suddenly deaf. You come back with \"no comment\" in five different tones of voice.|A fourth-year answers at length, entirely about himself.|Everyone takes the long way round you. You get one quote, from the prefect, and it is \"No.\"}\nopt.left.say.2 [9bebc2] = {人人都忙、都冷、或者突然聋了。你带回了五种语气的“无可奉告”。|一个四年级生滔滔不绝地回答，内容全是他自己。|人人都绕开你走。你只拿到一句引语，来自那位级长，内容是“不”。}\n# en: Just quote your friends\nopt.right.label [1f9ee3] = 直接引用朋友的话\n# en: {Every opinion comes from someone you had lunch with. Nessa reads them and says, \"Interesting. Your friends are very consistent.\"|It's quick. It's also the courtyard as represented by one person in several scarves.|You quote your friends again. Nessa runs it under a headline about the same four people, and it's somehow popular.}\nopt.right.say.1 [a242a3] = {每条意见都来自和你一起吃午饭的人。内莎读完说：“有意思。你的朋友们观点很统一。”|又快又省事。只是整个庭院的意见，都由同一个人围着几条不同的围巾代表了。|你又引用了你的朋友。内莎给它配了个关于“还是那四个人”的标题，不知怎么还挺受欢迎。}\n\n@event lumen_ledger_red_ink\n# en: Red Ink\ntitle [025680] = 红墨水\n# en: {Nessa edits your story with a red quill and no mercy.|Nessa has your draft, and this time the red quill hesitates once before it moves.|Edit night. You have learned to bring two copies, one for Nessa to kill.} \"{Kill your darlings. Bury them. Page six.|Lead's buried. Dig it up. Headline it.|Good. Too long. Shorter. Shorter than that.}\" She has cut your favourite paragraph.\ntext [4f9392] = {内莎用一支红羽毛笔毫不留情地改你的稿子。|内莎拿到了你的草稿，这一次，红笔落下前迟疑了一下。|改稿之夜。你学会了带两份稿子，一份留给内莎去杀。} “{杀掉你的心头好。埋了。第六版。|导语埋太深了。挖出来。做标题。|好。太长。再短。比那还短。}”她删掉了你最喜欢的那一段。\n# en: A cramped office papered with old front pages, an editor slashing a manuscript with a red quill.\nscene [f3d694] = 贴满旧头版的狭小办公室里，一位编辑正用红羽毛笔大刀阔斧地删改稿件。\n# en: Fight for the paragraph\nopt.left.label [528d97] = 为那一段据理力争\n# en: {Nessa reads it again, out loud, and puts it back. \"Fine. Stays. Don't get used to it.\"|You win the paragraph. Nessa makes you cut a different one, as a tax.|You fight for it, and it gets shorter, and shorter than that, and it stays. Nessa calls it \"a paragraph now\".}\nopt.left.say.1 [6f10cf] = {内莎把那段大声念了一遍，又放了回去。“行。留着。别习惯。”|你保住了那一段。内莎让你删掉另一段，当作税。|你为它争，它越来越短，短了又短，但留下了。内莎说“这才算一段”。}\n# en: {\"No,\" says Nessa, and that is the whole argument.|She listens to your case with great interest, then cuts the paragraph anyway, plus the one you defended it with.|You fight with your second copy ready. Nessa cuts the paragraph from both.}\nopt.left.say.2 [76aa1d] = {“不行。”内莎说，这就是全部论证。|她兴致勃勃地听完你的辩护，然后照删不误，连你用来辩护的那段也删了。|你备着第二份稿子来争。内莎把两份里的那段都删了。}\n# en: Let her cut it\nopt.right.label [8f19c2] = 让她删\n# en: {It reads better without it. You will never forgive her.|The story runs, tighter and less yours. Nessa scribbles \"good\" in the margin, a word Nessa hands out like gold.|You let her cut it, and slip it into next week's story instead. She notices, and lets it stand.}\nopt.right.say.1 [ede85e] = {删了确实更好。你永远不会原谅她。|稿子登了，更紧凑，也更不像你的。内莎在页边写了个“好”，这个字她发得像金子一样省。|你让她删了，转手塞进了下周的稿子。她发现了，也就让它留着了。}\n\n@event lumen_ledger_front_page\n# en: Front Page\ntitle [c50df3] = 头版\n# en: The press groans like a dragon and spits out the dawn edition. Your story is on the front page, under your own name. Nessa hands you a tack without looking up. \"Wall,\" she says. \"That's where front pages go.\"\ntext [911ced] = 印刷机像龙一样呻吟着，吐出了黎明版。你的报道登上头版，署着你自己的名字。内莎头也不抬地递给你一枚图钉。“墙上，”她说，“头版都钉那儿。”\n# en: A creaking printing press at dawn spitting out newspapers, a student holding the first copy up to the light.\nscene [632373] = 黎明时分，吱嘎作响的印刷机吐出报纸，一个学生把第一份举向光亮处。\n# en: Pin it to the wall yourself\nopt.left.label [ea7224] = 亲手把它钉上墙\n# en: You find a gap between a scandal from before you were born and a recipe someone printed by mistake. It looks small up there, and permanent. Nessa pretends not to watch you step back and admire it.\nopt.left.say.1 [1fa814] = 你在一桩你出生前的丑闻和一份误印的菜谱之间找到一处空隙。它在墙上显得很小，也很长久。内莎假装没看见你退后一步欣赏它。\n# en: Send the first copy home\nopt.right.label [a16d32] = 把第一份寄回家\n# en: You post it home with a note that says nothing about how long it took. The reply comes within the week: they have framed it, slightly crooked, over the kitchen fire.\nopt.right.say.1 [0f1990] = 你把它寄回家，附的字条里只字未提这花了多久。一周之内回信就到了：家里把它装了框，挂在厨房炉火上方，有点歪。\n\n@event verdant_watering_rota\n# en: The Watering Rota\ntitle [c1243f] = 浇水值日表\n# en: {You're on the watering rota, a chart the size of a door.|The ferns have learned your footsteps and lean in.|You no longer need the chart, luckily, since a fern ate it.} {season == \"winter\": Frost outside the glass, drips down your collar inside.|{season == \"summer\": It's so hot the cacti look smug.|{The biting ferns are in a mood.|Something in the far bed grew overnight.|The moonflowers do nothing, beautifully, as they will for years.}}}\ntext [3987d3] = {你上了浇水值日表，那是一张门板大的图表。|蕨类已经认得你的脚步，纷纷凑过来。|你已经不需要那张表了，幸好，因为它被一株蕨吃了。} {season == \"winter\": 玻璃外结着霜，玻璃内的水滴顺着你的领口往下流。|{season == \"summer\": 热得连仙人掌都一脸得意。|{咬人蕨今天心情不好。|远处苗床里有东西一夜之间长高了。|月光花什么也不做，美美地，接下来许多年都会这样。}}}\n# en: A student with a watering can and a huge chart in a steaming greenhouse, ferns leaning in hungrily.\nscene [535c02] = 热气蒸腾的温室里，一个学生拿着水壶和一张巨大的图表，几株蕨饥渴地凑过来。\n# en: Follow the chart exactly\nopt.left.label [577433] = 严格按表来\n# en: {Warm rainwater for the ferns, cold tea for the moss, nothing at all for the cactus that bites. It takes all afternoon.|By the chart, to the drop. {around(tobias): Tobias says the plants look \"right pleased\", and they do, in a leafy way.|The plants look pleased, in a leafy way.}|You follow the chart from memory, since the fern ate it. You're right to the drop, and the fern looks unrepentant.}\nopt.left.say.1 [02d5da] = {给蕨浇温雨水，给苔藓浇冷茶，那株咬人的仙人掌一滴也不给。整整花了一下午。|按表来，一滴不差。{around(tobias): 托比亚斯说植物们看着“可高兴了”，确实，以叶子的方式。|植物们看着很高兴，以叶子的方式。}|你凭记忆照着那张表浇，因为表被蕨吃了。一滴不差，那株蕨毫无悔意。}\n# en: Water by feel\nopt.right.label [36cb6b] = 凭感觉浇\n# en: {You read the leaves instead of the chart. Everything perks up, and one fern makes a sound suspiciously like purring.|You guess right on every bed. {around(tobias): Tobias asks how you knew. You honestly don't.|You couldn't say how you knew.}|You water by feel and talk to the moonflowers. They still do nothing, beautifully, and the rest of the house thrives.}\nopt.right.say.1 [5cac1b] = {你看叶子而不看表。一切都精神起来了，有一株蕨发出的声音很像呼噜。|每一床你都猜对了。{around(tobias): 托比亚斯问你怎么知道的。你真的不知道。|你自己也说不清是怎么知道的。}|你凭感觉浇水，还跟月光花说话。它们依然美丽地什么都不做，温室其余的植物倒是长得很旺。}\n# en: {You drown a pot of sundew, and a biting fern takes a polite nip of your thumb.|The moss wanted tea, not water. It sulks visibly, and the cactus you reach past bites.|You water the moonflowers by feel and drown them by feel. They do nothing, as usual, but wetly.}\nopt.right.say.2 [4dd1ee] = {你淹了一盆茅膏菜，一株咬人蕨还客客气气地啃了你的拇指一口。|苔藓要的是茶，不是水。它明显在生闷气，你伸手越过的那株仙人掌还咬了你一口。|你凭感觉给月光花浇水，也凭感觉把它们淹了。它们照旧什么都不做，只是湿漉漉的。}\n\n@event verdant_repotting_day\n# en: Repotting Day\ntitle [5b9bc1] = 换盆日\n# en: {Repotting day. The mandrakes have outgrown their pots and have opinions about it.|Someone always takes the good earmuffs, so you bring your own now.|You know which mandrakes kick, and which only pretend to.} {Tobias's own mandrake watches from a shelf in a knitted hat, superior.|A seed tray of something blue has started to hum.|{season == \"spring\": Everything is trying to grow at once.|The glass is fogged and the benches crowded.}}\ntext [56f63b] = {换盆日。曼德拉草长得比盆大了，而且对此很有意见。|好耳罩总被人抢走，所以你现在自己带一副。|你知道哪些曼德拉草真会踢人，哪些只是装装样子。} {托比亚斯自己的那株曼德拉草戴着针织帽在架子上旁观，一脸高傲。|一盘蓝色的幼苗开始嗡嗡作响。|{season == \"spring\": 一切都在同时拼命生长。|玻璃上起了雾，苗床挤得满满当当。}}\n# en: Students in earmuffs wrestling squalling mandrakes into bigger pots on a crowded greenhouse bench.\nscene [24e9d7] = 戴耳罩的学生们在拥挤的温室长桌上，把尖叫的曼德拉草塞进更大的花盆。\n# en: Take on the mandrakes\nopt.left.label [4dbb5a] = 去对付曼德拉草\n# en: {One mandrake has rooted into the bench itself. It takes a trowel, a lullaby and both hands to talk it out.|Mandrake after mandrake, each into a bigger pot. Your ears ring all the way to supper.|The biggest one goes in headfirst and comes out, somehow, the right way up. You are not sure who won.}\nopt.left.say.1 [20e693] = {有一株曼德拉草把根扎进了长桌里。你用了一把小铲、一首摇篮曲和两只手，才把它劝出来。|一株接一株，全换进了大盆。你的耳朵一直响到晚饭。|最大的那株头朝下进了盆，不知怎么又正着出来了。你说不清是谁赢了。}\n# en: Take the quiet seed trays\nopt.right.label [62e44f] = 去弄安静的育苗盘\n# en: {Hundreds of seedlings, pricked out one by one. Your back will remember this tomorrow.|The seed trays are peaceful and endless. By dusk you have names for most of them.|You do the seed trays with your own earmuffs on, for the peace. One tray hums, and you hum back.}\nopt.right.say.1 [5af201] = {几百株幼苗，一株一株地分栽。你的背明天会记住这件事。|育苗盘安静又没有尽头。到黄昏，你给大部分幼苗都起了名字。|你戴着自己的耳罩做育苗盘，图个清静。有一盘在哼歌，你也哼回去。}\n\n@event verdant_tobias_cuttings\n# en: The Cuttings Question\ntitle [95ea7f] = 插条之难\n# en: {The benches are full and someone must choose which cuttings stay. Tobias can't. \"It's like choosing between goats,\" he says.|Bench day. Tobias hovers over a tray of cuttings, apologising to them in advance and glancing at you.|Tobias hands you the tray before he can change his mind. He is grateful in a way that looks like grief.} {season == \"autumn\": The frost will take the weakest soon anyway.|}\ntext [964812] = {苗床满了，得有人决定留下哪些插条。托比亚斯做不到。“这就像在山羊里挑一只。”他说。|整理苗床的日子。托比亚斯守着一盘插条，提前向它们道歉，一边偷偷看你。|托比亚斯赶在自己反悔之前把盘子递给你。他的感激看起来很像悲伤。} {season == \"autumn\": 反正霜冻很快就会带走最弱的那些。|}\n# en: A big gentle student cradling a tray of leggy cuttings, eyeing a full compost heap with dread.\nscene [9813c9] = 一个高大温和的学生抱着一盘细长的插条，忧心忡忡地看着堆满的堆肥堆。\n# en: Choose, and compost the rest\nopt.left.label [0d0865] = 挑好，其余堆肥\n# en: {You keep the strongest and compost the rest. Tobias says \"fair's fair\", then goes very quiet over the heap.|The benches breathe again. Tobias thanks you, then tells you a long story about a goat he couldn't sell. You understand it is about the cuttings.|You choose quickly, so Tobias doesn't have to watch. He takes the compost bucket himself, and you let him.}\nopt.left.say.1 [bd94fd] = {你留下最壮的，其余的堆了肥。托比亚斯说“公平就是公平”，然后对着肥堆沉默了很久。|苗床终于松了口气。托比亚斯谢了你，又讲了个关于一只卖不掉的山羊的长故事。你明白，他说的是插条。|你选得很快，好让托比亚斯不用在旁边看。他自己提起了堆肥桶，你由他去。}\n# en: Find room for every one\nopt.right.label [31bf75] = 给每一株都找地方\n# en: {You find room on windowsills, under benches and in a teacup. Nothing grows well, but everything grows. Tobias laughs from the belly.|\"Ma always said a crowded field still feeds you,\" Tobias says, beaming. The benches groan. So does Old Maud, when she sees.|You find room again, on the last windowsill nobody had thought of. Tobias names that one after you.}\nopt.right.say.1 [885501] = {窗台上、长桌下、一只茶杯里，你都找到了地方。哪株都长得不太好，但哪株都在长。托比亚斯笑得肚子直颤。|“俺娘说，挤一挤的地也照样养人。”托比亚斯笑得合不拢嘴。苗床在呻吟。老莫德看见了，也在呻吟。|你又找到了地方，在一个谁都没想到的窗台上。托比亚斯用你的名字给那一株起了名。}\n\n@event verdant_greenhouse_keys\n# en: The Greenhouse Keys\ntitle [5b68d3] = 温室钥匙\n# en: At dusk Tobias holds out the iron key ring, warm from his pocket. \"Ma says you give the keys to whoever shuts the gate without being asked,\" he says. \"You always shut the gate.\" From now on, opening up and locking up are yours.\ntext [f2975e] = 黄昏时，托比亚斯递过来那串铁钥匙，还带着他口袋里的温度。“俺娘说，钥匙要交给不用人提醒就会关门的人，”他说，“你每次都关门。”从今往后，开门和锁门都归你了。\n# en: A heavy ring of old iron keys handed over in a steaming glasshouse at dusk.\nscene [0698f9] = 黄昏时热气蒸腾的玻璃温室里，一串沉重的旧铁钥匙被交到另一个人手中。\n# en: Open up at dawn\nopt.left.label [75086f] = 黎明时开门\n# en: Dawn, the glass silver with dew, every plant turning to the light you let in. You had never noticed they all turn at once. For a moment you are the only person in the world.\nopt.left.say.1 [186b94] = 黎明，玻璃上挂着银色的露水，每株植物都转向你放进来的光。你从没注意过它们是同时转过去的。那一刻，世上仿佛只有你一个人。\n# en: Walk the night round with a lamp\nopt.right.label [4c41e6] = 提灯巡夜\n# en: You walk the benches with a lamp, checking latches and saying goodnight, because Tobias does and it feels rude not to. Something in the far bed says it back. You decide not to mention it.\nopt.right.say.1 [03e898] = 你提着灯沿苗床走一圈，检查门闩，道声晚安，因为托比亚斯就这么做，不说好像不太礼貌。远处苗床里有什么东西也回了一句。你决定不提这事。\n\n@event cartographers_survey_run\n# en: A Survey Run\ntitle [4bf18f] = 测绘之行\n# en: {Initiates do the pacing. Corvin hands you chalk and a passage to measure before the castle changes its mind.|Your chalk is a stub now, so you mark the turns with notches.|Your stride is a unit of measurement on the master map now, and the castle seems to know it.} {The passage smells of oranges, which nobody can explain.|Behind the wall, a clock ticks that no one has ever found.|Your candle leans, very slightly, toward the left-hand turning.}\ntext [85a385] = {步测是新人的活。科尔文递给你粉笔和一条要量的通道，得赶在城堡改主意之前量完。|你的粉笔只剩一小截了，于是你改用刻痕标记拐角。|你的步幅如今已是总图上的计量单位，城堡似乎也知道。} {通道里有股橘子味，谁也解释不了。|墙后有一座钟在走，从来没人找到过它。|你的蜡烛火苗微微偏向左边的岔口。}\n# en: A student pacing a dark stone passage by candlelight, chalking marks on the wall.\nscene [04b918] = 一个学生借着烛光在黑暗的石砌通道里步测，在墙上做粉笔记号。\n# en: Pace it properly\nopt.left.label [6eb50c] = 老老实实步测\n# en: {Every turning chalked and counted. The passage is exactly where it was last month, which on the master map counts as a scandal.|The passage is two strides longer from this end than from the other. Corvin is delighted.|You pace it with the stride the map uses. The left turning, where your candle leans, wasn't there last week. Corvin inks it in.}\nopt.left.say.1 [3a9fc5] = {每个拐角都标好数清。通道和上个月一模一样，这在总图上算得上丑闻。|这条通道从这头量比从那头量长两步。科尔文乐坏了。|你用总图采用的那种步幅去量。你的蜡烛偏向的那个左拐口，上周还不存在。科尔文把它画了上去。}\n# en: {Your chalk marks lead neatly into a wall that wasn't there when you started.|You lose count somewhere in the dark and have to admit it, which in this club is rarer than a map.|You follow the leaning candle into the left-hand turning and come out a floor below, with no stairs in between.}\nopt.left.say.2 [0f3a54] = {你的粉笔记号整整齐齐地通进一堵你开始时还不存在的墙。|你在黑暗里数乱了，只好承认，这在本社比地图还稀罕。|你跟着倾斜的烛火走进左边的岔道，出来时到了下面一层，中间没有楼梯。}\n# en: Copy last month's figures\nopt.right.label [47f515] = 抄上个月的数\n# en: {You copy the old figures and sit in an alcove eating toffee. The castle, as if offended, moves the passage a foot.|The figures match last month's exactly. Corvin looks at them, then at you. \"Forger,\" he says, approvingly.|You copy the figures with your own stride as the unit. The castle, offended, changes your stride.}\nopt.right.say.1 [1ae24e] = {你抄了旧数据，坐在壁龛里吃太妃糖。城堡像是被冒犯了，把通道挪了一尺。|数据和上个月分毫不差。科尔文看看数据，又看看你。“伪造犯。”他说，语气里带着赞许。|你用自己的步幅作单位抄了数据。城堡被冒犯了，改了你的步幅。}\n\n@event cartographers_lantern_patrol\n# en: The Prefect's Lantern\ntitle [709761] = 级长的提灯\n# en: {Every expedition has the moment a lantern comes round the corner. Tonight's has a red sash behind it.|A lantern, a red sash, footsteps. You know this part.|You can tell the prefects on patrol apart by their footsteps now.} {The alcove beside you is just big enough for one.|A loose flagstone ahead clanks if you step on it.|The tapestry here already has feet showing under it, and they aren't yours.}\ntext [b69280] = {每次探险都有那么一刻，一盏提灯从拐角绕过来。今晚的提灯后面是一条红色绶带。|提灯、红绶带、脚步声。这一段你熟。|你现在光听脚步就分得出是哪位级长在巡夜。} {你身边的壁龛刚好只够藏一个人。|前面有块松动的石板，一踩就响。|这里的挂毯底下已经露出了一双脚，不是你的。}\n# en: Students pressed into a stone alcove as a lantern's glow sweeps along a dark corridor.\nscene [0eba4e] = 几个学生紧贴在石砌壁龛里，提灯的光扫过黑暗的走廊。\n# en: Freeze in the shadows\nopt.left.label [df6ae3] = 在阴影里一动不动\n# en: {The light slides over you like water off a duck. The footsteps go on.|You don't breathe for a very long time. The prefect sneezes, apologises to nobody and walks on.|You freeze beside the tapestry with the feet. The prefect sees the feet, not yours, and someone else spends Saturday scrubbing pans.}\nopt.left.say.1 [47e514] = {灯光从你身上滑过，像水从鸭子背上流走。脚步声继续往前。|你很久很久没敢呼吸。那位级长打了个喷嚏，不知向谁道了歉，走了。|你在那块露着脚的挂毯边僵住了。级长看见的是那双脚，不是你的，于是刷锅的星期六归了别人。}\n# en: {The lantern stops on your face. You spend Saturday scrubbing pans and inventing a reason you were inside a wall.|You talk your way out with a story about a lost cat. It costs you the night's mapping and most of your dignity.|The feet under the tapestry turn out to be the prefect's own. You both have some explaining to do.}\nopt.left.say.2 [3c0370] = {灯光停在你脸上。你的星期六用来刷锅，顺便编一个你为什么在墙里的理由。|你编了个找丢失的猫的故事脱了身。代价是一晚的测绘和大半的尊严。|挂毯下那双脚原来是级长自己的。你们俩都有得解释了。}\n# en: Go back the long way\nopt.right.label [457126] = 绕远路回去\n# en: {The long way is four flights of freezing stairs. You learn every one of them by heart.|You double back through the kitchens and arrive late, cold and unseen. Corvin raises a mug of milk he certainly didn't pay for.|You know these footsteps. You take the long way at exactly the prefect's pace, one corridor behind.}\nopt.right.say.1 [299511] = {远路是四段冰冷的楼梯。你把每一级都记在了心里。|你从厨房绕回去，到得又晚又冷，但没人看见。科尔文举起一杯他肯定没付钱的牛奶。|你认得这脚步声。你走远路，步子和那位级长一模一样，始终落后一条走廊。}\n\n@event cartographers_inking_night\n# en: Inking Night\ntitle [e28233] = 上墨之夜\n# en: {Mappers ink the master map. Corvin unrolls it, silver lines shifting like breath, and passes you the pen.|Inking night. The map has redrawn a stair since your last visit, out of spite.|The map has started leaving a blank wherever you hesitate.} {\"Leave the one behind the kitchens off,\" he says. \"Not for long. For me.\"|\"The stair under the clock. Don't draw it. Don't ask why.\"|He taps a corridor. \"That one stays off the map. Humour me.\"}\ntext [7d989b] = {绘图员负责给总图上墨。科尔文展开地图，银色线条如呼吸般起伏，把笔递给你。|上墨之夜。自你上次来过，地图重画了一段楼梯，纯属赌气。|地图开始在你每一处犹豫的地方留下空白。} {“厨房后面那条别画上去，”他说，“不会太久。算帮我。”|“钟底下那段楼梯。别画。别问为什么。”|他点了点一条走廊。“这条不上图。就当迁就我。”}\n# en: Two students bent over a huge map drawn in silver ink that shifts as they watch, in a hidden candlelit room.\nscene [172414] = 隐秘的烛光小屋里，两个学生俯身在一张用银墨绘成、边看边变的巨大地图上。\n# en: Ink it anyway\nopt.left.label [f376f9] = 照样画上去\n# en: {You draw it, line by honest line. Corvin watches, then laughs, a little too late. \"Saint,\" he says, without the usual smile.|It goes on the map. Corvin is quiet for the rest of the night, which on him is very loud.|You ink it, and the map leaves a blank there anyway, as if it agreed with Corvin. He looks at you and says nothing.}\nopt.left.say.1 [b28752] = {你一笔一画老老实实地画了。科尔文看着，然后笑了，笑得晚了一点。“圣人。”他说，没有平常那种笑意。|它上了图。科尔文一整晚都没说话，这在他身上响亮得很。|你画上了，可地图还是在那里留出一块空白，好像它站在科尔文那边。他看着你，什么也没说。}\n# en: Leave it off for him\nopt.right.label [1f8fe9] = 为他留白\n# en: {You leave a careful blank. Corvin squeezes your shoulder and doesn't make a joke, which is how you know it matters.|The map gets a gap and Corvin keeps his secret. You wonder, later, what it's for.|You leave the corridor off. The map closes over the gap so smoothly you'd never know. Neither would anyone else.}\nopt.right.say.1 [9ac197] = {你小心地留出一块空白。科尔文捏了捏你的肩膀，没开玩笑，你就知道这事要紧。|地图多了块空白，科尔文守住了他的秘密。后来你想，那到底是为了什么。|你没画那条走廊。地图把空隙合得严丝合缝，谁也看不出来，别人也不会。}\n\n@event cartographers_silver_pen\n# en: A Pen of Your Own\ntitle [e2f8fb] = 你自己的笔\n# en: Corvin presses a pen into your hand: silver nib, black barrel, warm from his pocket. \"Mappers carry their own,\" he says. \"And the first passage you find, you name. Club rule. I made it up, but it's a rule.\" Tonight you found one.\ntext [4a3c7e] = 科尔文把一支笔按进你手里：银笔尖，黑笔杆，还带着他口袋里的温度。“绘图员都带自己的笔，”他说，“而且你找到的第一条通道，由你命名。社规。我编的，但它是社规。”今晚你找到了一条。\n# en: A silver-nibbed pen laid on a folded map in a candlelit stone alcove.\nscene [7722b8] = 烛光摇曳的石砌壁龛里，一支银尖钢笔放在一张折好的地图上。\n# en: Name it after yourself\nopt.left.label [080f1a] = 以自己的名字命名\n# en: Your name goes on the master map in your own silver hand, at the mouth of a passage that smells faintly of rain. The ink shivers, then settles, as if the castle had agreed.\nopt.left.say.1 [6ddc6c] = 你用自己的银墨把名字写在总图上，就在一条隐隐有雨味的通道口。墨迹颤了颤，又安定下来，好像城堡同意了。\n# en: Name it for a club joke\nopt.right.label [cf8658] = 用社里的笑话命名\n# en: You name it after the night's worst joke. Corvin laughs so hard a prefect two floors down stops to listen. \"Keeper material,\" he whispers, and for once he doesn't laugh after.\nopt.right.say.1 [6bb20d] = 你用当晚最烂的笑话给它命名。科尔文笑得太厉害，两层楼下的级长都停下来听。“守图人的料。”他低声说，这一次他说完没有笑。\n\n@event library_assistant_wandering_shelves\n# en: The Wandering Section\ntitle [ed4770] = 出走的书区\n# en: {The shelves rearranged themselves overnight. History is missing, and a queue is forming for it.|The shelves have wandered, this time the {poetry|herbals|atlases}. You have brought string to mark the way.|The head librarian simply hands you the catalogue now and points upward.} {Footnote sits where the section used to be.|A first-year swears she saw it go upstairs.|There is a faint trail in the dust, as if something heavy had tiptoed.}\ntext [c7c525] = {书架一夜之间自己挪了位置。历史区不见了，找它的人排起了队。|书架又出走了，这回是{诗歌区|草药区|地图集区}。你带了根绳子来标路。|图书馆馆长现在直接把目录递给你，再往上一指。} {脚注坐在那个书区原来的位置上。|一个一年级生发誓说她看见它上楼了。|灰尘里有一道淡淡的痕迹，好像有什么重东西踮着脚走过。}\n# en: A library aisle with an empty gap where a whole bookcase should be, a student holding a catalogue.\nscene [e42286] = 图书馆过道里本该立着一整排书架的地方空空如也，一个学生拿着目录。\n# en: Hunt it down by catalogue\nopt.left.label [8c8f5d] = 按目录把它找回来\n# en: {You find it on the fifth floor, wedged between the cookery books, looking innocent.|You track it by its shelfmarks to a landing that wasn't there yesterday. The queue cheers, quietly, because it's a library.|You follow the tiptoe trail to the atlases, three floors up. They are pretending to be geography.}\nopt.left.say.1 [bc8194] = {你在五楼找到它，夹在烹饪书中间，一脸无辜。|你按索书号追到一个昨天还不存在的楼梯平台。排队的人欢呼了，小声地，毕竟这是图书馆。|你顺着那串踮脚的痕迹找到了地图集，在三层楼上。它们正假装自己是地理书。}\n# en: {You search until closing. In the morning the section is back where it started, faintly smug.|You find the wrong section, twice. The queue disperses with sighs you will hear in your sleep.|You follow the trail in the dust to a blank wall. The trail continues on the other side, presumably.}\nopt.left.say.2 [3c2d26] = {你一直找到闭馆。第二天早上，那个书区回到了原处，隐隐有点得意。|你两次找错了书区。排队的人叹着气散了，那叹气声你在梦里都听得见。|你顺着灰尘里的痕迹走到一堵空墙前。痕迹大概在墙的另一边继续。}\n# en: Put up a sign and wait\nopt.right.label [f19929] = 贴个告示等它回来\n# en: {\"Back shortly,\" says your sign. The section returns in the afternoon. The queue does not.|You sit at the desk and wait. A student asks whether the sign means you or the books.|You put up the sign and the catalogue, open at the right page. The section comes back to see what you wrote about it.}\nopt.right.say.1 [d713c9] = {你的告示写着“马上回来”。书区下午回来了，排队的人没有。|你坐在柜台后等。一个学生问，告示说的是你还是那些书。|你挂上告示，再把目录翻到对的那一页摆着。那个书区回来，想看看你写了它什么。}\n\n@event library_assistant_hush_round\n# en: The Hush Round\ntitle [bd75f0] = 肃静巡视\n# en: {Hush round. One lap of the reading room, a lamp, a finger to your lips.|The reading room has learned the sound of your lamp. Whispers stop a table ahead of you.|The regulars hear your step and straighten up before you reach them.} {A table of second-years is giggling.|An old scholar snores on his notes, in a scholarly key.|Somebody's quill is scratching so loudly it echoes.} {final_year: Soon this will be someone else's lap.|}\ntext [88e9ee] = {肃静巡视。在阅览室走一圈，一盏灯，一根竖在唇边的手指。|阅览室已经认得你提灯的声音，你还隔着一张桌子，低语就停了。|常客们听到你的脚步，不等你走近就坐直了。} {一桌二年级生笑个不停。|一位老学者趴在笔记上打鼾，鼾声都带着学术腔调。|不知谁的羽毛笔沙沙响得都有回声了。} {final_year: 很快这一圈就是别人的了。|}\n# en: A student assistant with a lamp walking between study tables in a dim library, one finger to her lips.\nscene [207931] = 昏暗的图书馆里，一个提灯的学生助理在自习桌之间走过，一根手指竖在唇边。\n# en: Hush them firmly\nopt.left.label [b21b8c] = 严厉地让他们安静\n# en: {One look and the room falls silent. Somewhere a bookcase seems to approve.|You hush them. They glare. In the morning one of them leaves a note on the desk: \"sorry\", in very small writing.|You hush the scratching quill. Its owner hears your step before you arrive and is already writing quietly.}\nopt.left.say.1 [77efeb] = {你一个眼神，整间屋子就安静了。某个书架似乎表示赞许。|你让他们安静。他们瞪你。第二天早上，柜台上有张字条写着“对不起”，字很小很小。|你让那支沙沙作响的羽毛笔安静下来。笔的主人在你走到之前就听见了你的脚步，已经轻轻地写了起来。}\n# en: Let them be\nopt.right.label [29efb1] = 由他们去\n# en: {You let it go. The noise rises, the head librarian appears from nowhere, and looks at you, not at them.|You leave them to it. For a while the murmur is the nicest sound in the building.|You let them be. The regulars straighten up anyway when you pass, out of habit, and the noise sorts itself out.}\nopt.right.say.1 [42969a] = {你没管。声音越来越大，图书馆馆长不知从哪儿冒了出来，看的是你，不是他们。|你随他们去。有那么一会儿，那阵低语是整栋楼里最好听的声音。|你随他们去。你经过时常客们还是习惯性地坐直了，声音自己就小了下去。}\n\n@event library_assistant_reference_desk\n# en: The Reference Desk\ntitle [6eeb1e] = 咨询台\n# en: {Clerks take the reference desk alone. The first question arrives before you sit down.|The desk has a worn patch now, exactly where your elbows go.|You keep a list of the strangest questions now, and it is long.} {A professor wants \"the green book, about this thick, I read it in my youth\".|A first-year wants the book their professor \"definitely mentioned\".|A visiting scholar wants the one book that explains why bees hum in thirds, by tonight.}\ntext [5dc304] = {文员要独自值咨询台。你还没坐下，第一个问题就来了。|柜台上如今磨出了一块亮斑，正好是你搁手肘的地方。|你现在专门记录最古怪的问题，单子已经很长了。} {一位教授要找“那本绿皮书，大概这么厚，我年轻时读过”。|一个一年级生要找他们教授“肯定提过”的那本书。|一位来访学者要在今晚之前找到那本解释蜜蜂为什么用三度音嗡嗡叫的书。}\n# en: A student behind a reference desk facing a line of readers, one of them holding up a green ribbon.\nscene [d1f7a3] = 一个学生坐在咨询台后，面对一队读者，其中一人举着一根绿丝带。\n# en: Dig until you find it\nopt.left.label [f45a7c] = 一直找到为止\n# en: {You find it in the end, in the wrong section, under a cat. The reader looks at you as if you had worked a miracle, and you have.|Three ladders, two catalogues and an educated guess. It is the right book.|There's no such book. You find three that explain it between them, by tonight. The scholar adds your name to the acknowledgements.}\nopt.left.say.1 [31df1b] = {你终于找到了，在错误的书区，压在一只猫底下。读者看你的眼神像你创造了奇迹，你确实创造了。|三架梯子、两本目录，外加一个有根据的猜测。就是这本。|根本没有那样一本书。你找来三本，合起来把它讲清楚了，赶在当晚。那位学者把你的名字加进了致谢。}\n# en: {Hours later you admit defeat. The book, it turns out, was on the desk all along, propping up the inkpot.|You never find it. You dream of card catalogues all night.|The bees remain unexplained. You add the question to your list, at the top.}\nopt.left.say.2 [fcd48c] = {几个小时后你认输了。结果那本书一直在柜台上，垫着墨水瓶。|你始终没找到。你梦了一整夜的卡片目录。|蜜蜂的问题依旧没有答案。你把这个问题加进了清单，放在最上面。}\n# en: Send them off with a good guess\nopt.right.label [1e3693] = 随口指个方向\n# en: {You point confidently at a staircase. They come back an hour later with a different book, which they say they liked better.|\"Try the fourth floor.\" They do. You hope the fourth floor helps.|You send them to the apiary. They come back with a bee and no book, delighted.}\nopt.right.say.1 [a45acf] = {你自信地指向一道楼梯。一小时后他们带着另一本书回来，说更喜欢这本。|“去四楼找找。”他们去了。你希望四楼帮得上忙。|你让他们去养蜂场。他们带着一只蜜蜂回来了，没带书，开心得很。}\n\n@event library_assistant_stack_keys\n# en: The Stack Keys\ntitle [dd3ec0] = 书库钥匙\n# en: At closing the head librarian presses a brass ring of keys into your hand: the upper stacks, the map drawers, the ladder chains. Not the Restricted Section. \"Lock up. The shelves settle after dark. Let them.\"\ntext [3fbf9b] = 闭馆时，图书馆馆长把一串黄铜钥匙按进你手里：上层书库、地图抽屉、梯子的锁链。不包括禁书区。“锁门。天黑后书架会安顿下来。随它们去。”\n# en: An old librarian handing a brass ring of keys to a student among towering shelves at closing time.\nscene [5cab1e] = 闭馆时分，高耸的书架间，一位老馆员把一串黄铜钥匙交给一个学生。\n# en: Do the closing round alone\nopt.left.label [4df85b] = 独自巡视闭馆\n# en: You walk every floor with the keys chiming. The shelves shift and creak and settle, like an old house going to sleep, and this time you are the one who gets to hear it.\nopt.left.say.1 [185d52] = 你带着叮当作响的钥匙走遍每一层。书架挪动、吱呀、安定下来，像一座老房子入睡，而这一次，听见这一切的是你。\n# en: Let Footnote come along\nopt.right.label [7a3ccf] = 让脚注一起来\n# en: Footnote pads ahead of you up every stair, checking each lock before you do. At the top he sits in the window and lets you scratch his ears, once. You suspect that was the real promotion.\nopt.right.say.1 [dd5a65] = 脚注在你前面轻步走上每一级台阶，抢在你之前检查每一把锁。到了顶层，它坐在窗台上，让你挠了一下耳朵，就一下。你怀疑那才是真正的晋升。\n\n@event tavern_cider_rush\n# en: The Cider Rush\ntitle [9095b3] = 苹果酒高峰\n# en: {The rush at the Gilded Newt. A dozen orders shouted at once, and a tray that holds fewer.|The door won't stay shut. You have started remembering faces by their drink, which works until two people want spiced cider.|The regulars just point now, and you know what the points mean.} {A barge crew has come in soaked and thirsty.|The card table is arguing about the rules again.|{season == \"winter\": Snow is melting off every coat in the room.|Half the village is on the step.}}\ntext [926886] = {镀金蝾螈酒馆的高峰。十几份点单同时喊过来，托盘却放不下那么多。|门怎么也关不上。你开始凭各人点的酒记住他们的脸，直到有两个人都要香料苹果酒。|常客们现在只用手指，你也都看得懂。} {一队驳船船员浑身湿透地进来，口渴得很。|牌桌那边又在为规则吵架了。|{season == \"winter\": 满屋大衣上的雪都在化。|半个小镇的人都站在门口台阶上。}}\n# en: A crowded low-beamed tavern, a server balancing a tray of steaming mugs through the press of bodies.\nscene [3aaff4] = 低梁的酒馆里人头攒动，一个侍应托着一盘冒热气的杯子穿过人群。\n# en: Keep every order in your head\nopt.left.label [588aa1] = 全凭脑子记单\n# en: {Every mug to the right hand. The whole room tips like it's payday.|You don't miss once. The landlady, who remembers every tab to the penny, gives you a look of professional respect.|The regulars point, and you deliver. The door won't stay shut, and nobody waits more than a minute.}\nopt.left.say.1 [1f2c5d] = {每一杯都送到了对的人手里。整屋人给小费都像发了工钱。|你一次也没错。那位把每笔账记到分毫的老板娘，向你投来同行间的敬意。|常客们一指，你就送到。门关不上，可谁都没等过一分钟。}\n# en: {The spiced cider goes to the wrong table. The right table has opinions. The wrong table drinks it anyway.|You mix up two orders and three tempers.|Half the village points at once. You misread two points and a wave.}\nopt.left.say.2 [246428] = {香料苹果酒送错了桌。对的那桌很有意见。错的那桌照喝不误。|你弄混了两份点单和三个人的脾气。|半个村子的人同时在指。你看错了两个指向和一个挥手。}\n# en: Write every order down\nopt.right.label [80b465] = 每单都写下来\n# en: {Slow but sure. Nobody gets the wrong drink; nobody tips the slow server either.|Your order pad fills with a shorthand only you can read. By closing, you can't either.|You write every order, even the points. By closing you have a full record of the village's habits.}\nopt.right.say.1 [100d2c] = {慢，但稳。没人拿错酒，也没人给慢吞吞的侍应小费。|你的点单本上写满了只有你看得懂的速记。到打烊时，你也看不懂了。|你把每一份点单都记了下来，连手指的也记了。到打烊时，你有了一份全村习惯的完整记录。}\n\n@event tavern_regulars_corner\n# en: The Regulars' Corner\ntitle [98ab7f] = 常客角落\n# en: {The regulars' corner by the fire wants you to sit for a minute. The fire is warm, and the minute is tempting.|The regulars' corner has saved you a stool now, with a cushion on it.|The regulars wave you over by name. They want to know how your exams went, all of them, in order.} {The old barge-man is telling the eel story again.|The herb-wife from the lane wants her pie warmed \"exactly as before\".|A quiet traveller is listening to everything.}\ntext [d6ae20] = {炉火边的常客角落想让你坐一会儿。炉火很暖，这一会儿很诱人。|常客角落如今给你留了张凳子，上面还垫了个软垫。|常客们喊着你的名字招手。他们想知道你的考试考得怎样，每一门，按顺序。} {老驳船工又在讲那个鳗鱼的故事。|巷子里那位卖草药的大婶要她的馅饼“和上次一样”热一热。|一个安静的旅人什么都在听。}\n# en: An old barge-man, a herb-wife from the lane and a quiet traveller at a fireside table, beckoning a server over.\nscene [017fca] = 炉火边的桌旁，一位老驳船工、一位巷子里卖草药的大婶和一个安静的旅人正招呼侍应过去。\n# en: Sit with them a while\nopt.left.label [c29077] = 陪他们坐一会儿\n# en: {You hear the latest instalment of everyone's story. They tip you for listening, which is a better rate than serving.|You sit for one minute and stay for ten. The landlady clears her throat across the room like a falling tree.|You tell them about your exams, all of them, in order. The quiet traveller listens hardest and tips best.}\nopt.left.say.1 [9dc187] = {你听完了每个人故事的最新一集。他们为你肯听付了小费，比端盘子还划算。|你说坐一会儿，结果坐了好一阵。老板娘在屋子那头清了清嗓子，像一棵树倒下。|你把你的考试一门一门按顺序讲给他们听。那位安静的旅人听得最认真，小费给得也最多。}\n# en: Keep the trays moving\nopt.right.label [579530] = 托盘不停手\n# en: {You wave and keep going. The corner toasts your back. The eel story will keep.|You work the room like weather. The corner calls you \"the brisk one\" now, which is not a compliment.|You keep moving. The quiet traveller watches you all evening, and leaves without a word, and a large tip.}\nopt.right.say.1 [9813b7] = {你挥挥手继续忙。角落里的人朝你背影举杯。鳗鱼的故事跑不了。|你像一阵风一样忙遍全场。角落里的人现在叫你“利索的那个”，这可不是夸奖。|你一直在忙。那位安静的旅人看了你一整晚，一言不发地走了，留下一大笔小费。}\n\n@event tavern_tab_book\n# en: The Tab Book\ntitle [c2811c] = 赊账簿\n# en: {Barkeeps keep the tab book, and the tab book says {regular} owes the Newt rather a lot.|The tab book has grown a second volume, and {regular} has a page in it.|{regular} is at the end of the bar, and so, in the book, is a long column of figures.} {{They} smile{s} at you hopefully.|{They} {are} pretending to be fascinated by the fire.|{They} {are} counting coins that clearly won't be enough.}\ntext [cf577d] = {吧台侍应管着赊账簿，账簿上说{regular}欠了酒馆不少钱。|赊账簿已经续到第二册了，{regular}在里面也有一页。|{regular}坐在吧台尽头，账簿里也排着长长一列数字。} {{They}满怀希望地冲你笑。|{They}假装对炉火很着迷。|{They}在数一把显然不够的硬币。}\n# en: A ledger open on a tavern bar, a long column of figures, a hopeful customer at the far end.\nscene [1a746d] = 酒馆吧台上摊开一本账簿，一长列数字，吧台尽头坐着一位满怀希望的客人。\n# en: Call the tab in\nopt.left.label [6ebe68] = 把账收回来\n# en: {{regular} pays, slowly, coin by coin, as if each one were a friend leaving. The landlady slides you a few crowns for the collecting.|You call it in. {regular} pays half and an apology. The landlady counts both, and gives you a few crowns of it for your trouble.|You call it in. {regular} pays in coins and a promise, and the landlady accepts the coins. You get a share of those.}\nopt.left.say.1 [05b268] = {{regular}一枚一枚慢慢付钱，好像每枚硬币都是一位离去的朋友。老板娘塞给你几枚克朗，算收账的辛苦费。|你开口要账。{regular}付了一半，外加一句道歉。老板娘把两样都点了数，从里头分了你几枚克朗作辛苦费。|你开口要账。{regular}付了些硬币，外加一个承诺，老板娘收了硬币。你分到了一点。}\n# en: Let it ride another week\nopt.right.label [2f306c] = 再宽限一周\n# en: {{regular} looks at you as if you had hung the moon. The landlady looks at you as if you had lost it.|Another week. The column grows, and so, oddly, does {regular}'s loyalty.|You let it ride and add a line to the long column. {regular} buys you a drink with money {they} clearly owe the Newt.}\nopt.right.say.1 [f41919] = {{regular}看你的眼神像是你把月亮挂上了天。老板娘看你的眼神像是你把脑子弄丢了。|又一周。那列数字更长了，{regular}的忠诚也奇怪地跟着长了。|你又宽限了一周，在那长长的一列下又添了一行。{regular}用显然欠着酒馆的钱请你喝了一杯。}\n\n@event tavern_behind_the_bar\n# en: Behind the Bar\ntitle [7962ab] = 吧台之后\n# en: The landlady sets the cellar key on the bar between you. \"You pour from tonight,\" she says. \"Barkeeps feed the fire, too. It hasn't gone out in my lifetime, nor my mother's. Don't be the one.\" The hearth crackles, as if listening.\ntext [8a0483] = 老板娘把地窖钥匙放在你们之间的吧台上。“从今晚起你来倒酒，”她说，“吧台的人也要添火。这火在我这辈子、我娘那辈子都没灭过。别让它灭在你手里。”炉火噼啪作响，像在听着。\n# en: A landlady setting a heavy cellar key on a polished tavern bar, the hearth fire glowing behind her.\nscene [818643] = 炉火在后方闪着光，老板娘把一把沉重的地窖钥匙放在擦得锃亮的吧台上。\n# en: Bank the fire at closing\nopt.left.label [065f76] = 打烊时封好炉火\n# en: You stay after the last song, banking the coals the way she showed you, slow and careful as tucking in a child. In the morning it is still burning. You check twice anyway.\nopt.left.say.1 [b13718] = 最后一支歌唱完，你留下来，照她教的封住炭火，又慢又小心，像给孩子掖被子。第二天早上火还燃着。你还是检查了两遍。\n# en: Pour the staff a round\nopt.right.label [82794d] = 给伙计们倒一轮\n# en: You pour your first round for the cooks and the pot-boy. The landlady takes hers without a word, then taps the bar twice, which apparently means \"well poured\" in a language older than the Newt.\nopt.right.say.1 [64fdee] = 你给厨子和洗杯小工倒了你的第一轮酒。老板娘一言不发地接过她那杯，在吧台上敲了两下——据说在一门比镀金蝾螈酒馆还老的语言里，这意思是“倒得好”。\n\n@event apothecary_clerk_counting_drops\n# en: Counting Drops\ntitle [77984e] = 数滴数\n# en: {Old Pell's first rule is to count every drop aloud. Today the counter is lined with tinctures waiting to be measured.|Drop-counting. Your tongue knows the numbers before your eyes do.|Counting drops. Old Pell says you count like a metronome now, which from him is a sonnet.} {A customer is waiting, tapping a coin on the glass.|One of the daughters is humming, which throws off your count.|Today's tincture is the colour of a bruise and smells of violets.}\ntext [bf51c4] = {老佩尔的第一条规矩是每一滴都要大声数出来。今天柜台上排满了等着量的酊剂。|数滴数。你的舌头比眼睛先知道数字。|数滴数。老佩尔说你现在数得像节拍器，这话从他嘴里说出来就是一首十四行诗。} {一位客人在等，用硬币轻敲玻璃柜台。|老佩尔的一个女儿在哼歌，打乱了你的节奏。|今天的酊剂是淤青的颜色，闻起来却是紫罗兰味。}\n# en: A student squinting at a glass dropper over a tiny vial at an apothecary counter, a wall of labelled drawers behind.\nscene [44e4e3] = 药铺柜台前，一个学生眯眼盯着小药瓶上的玻璃滴管，身后是一整墙贴着标签的抽屉。\n# en: Count aloud, every drop\nopt.left.label [2ee43b] = 一滴一滴大声数\n# en: {Every vial exact. Anyone in the shop has now heard you count higher than anyone should.|Aloud, drop by drop. Old Pell, passing, nods once without breaking stride.|You count the violet one aloud in perfect time. Pell, passing, nods in time too.}\nopt.left.say.1 [9792a6] = {每一瓶都分毫不差。铺子里谁都已经听你数到了一个谁都不该数到的数。|大声地，一滴一滴。老佩尔路过，头也不停地点了一下。|你把那瓶紫罗兰色的大声数完，节拍分毫不差。佩尔路过，点头也合着拍子。}\n# en: Count by eye, faster\nopt.right.label [f2a5b1] = 目测，快一些\n# en: {Quick and true. You clear the counter before the tea goes cold, and a satisfied customer leaves a coin.|By eye, and spot on. One of the daughters raises an eyebrow and says nothing to her father.|You count by eye in your metronome rhythm. Pell checks one vial, finds it perfect, and stops checking.}\nopt.right.say.1 [55f6e2] = {又快又准。茶还没凉，你就清空了柜台，一位满意的客人留下一枚硬币。|目测，正中。佩尔的一个女儿挑了挑眉，什么也没跟她父亲说。|你按自己节拍器般的节奏目测。佩尔查了一瓶，分毫不差，就不再查了。}\n# en: {One drop over. Old Pell can tell from across the shop. He tips the vial out and makes you count the next one aloud, twice.|You overshoot. The tincture turns from violet to a colour with no name, and Pell sighs like a closing door.|You go by eye and the violet one goes the colour of a worse bruise. Pell makes you count the rest aloud, as punishment and as lesson.}\nopt.right.say.2 [975042] = {多了一滴。老佩尔隔着整间铺子都看得出来。他把药瓶倒空，让你把下一瓶大声数两遍。|你倒多了。酊剂从紫罗兰色变成一种没有名字的颜色，佩尔叹了口气，像一扇门关上。|你凭目测，那瓶紫罗兰色的变成了更难看的淤青色。佩尔罚你把剩下的都大声数完，既是惩罚，也是一课。}\n\n@event apothecary_clerk_drawer_check\n# en: The Thousand Drawers\ntitle [76310c] = 千屉之墙\n# en: {Each shift a clerk checks a row of drawers against the stock book. Today's row starts with one that is stuck.|A different row today, and the same feeling that something in it is awake.|Your row for the week. You have learned that the wrong drawer is never just the wrong drawer.} {One is labelled in a hand that isn't Pell's.|One is rattling, very gently, as if politely.|One smells of snow, in {season == \"winter\": a shop this warm|the wrong season entirely}.}\ntext [c43280] = {每个班次，店员要对着库存簿核对一排抽屉。今天这一排的第一个就卡住了。|今天换了一排，还是那种里面有什么醒着的感觉。|这周轮到你这一排。你已经明白，拉错的抽屉从来不只是拉错了抽屉。} {有一个抽屉的标签不是佩尔的笔迹。|有一个抽屉在轻轻地响，几乎是礼貌地。|有一个抽屉闻起来像雪，{season == \"winter\": 在这么暖和的铺子里|完全不合时节}。}\n# en: A wall of tiny labelled wooden drawers, one of them rattling on its own.\nscene [c05965] = 一整墙贴着标签的小木抽屉，其中一个自己在咔哒作响。\n# en: Open the odd drawer\nopt.left.label [7cc2f8] = 打开那个古怪的抽屉\n# en: {Inside, a nest of dried moonpetals that belong on the top row. You move them, and the drawer stops sulking.|You coax it open and find the missing powdered newt. Pell crosses something off a list he has kept for a year.|Inside is a little snow that hasn't melted, and a note in Pell's hand: \"Don't.\" You close it. Pell thanks you, oddly sincerely.}\nopt.left.say.1 [599d4d] = {里面是一窝干月光花瓣，本该放在最上排。你挪好了，抽屉不再赌气。|你哄开抽屉，找到了失踪的蝾螈粉。佩尔在一张他留了一年的清单上划掉了一项。|里面是一点没化的雪，还有一张佩尔笔迹的字条：“别。”你关上了。佩尔向你道谢，出奇地真诚。}\n# en: {The drawer opens with a sneeze of pepperwort. You sneeze until closing.|Something small and grey bolts out of the drawer and into another, and you never find out which.|The snow-smelling drawer is full of cold. It gets out, and the shop is frosted until noon.}\nopt.left.say.2 [f0921c] = {抽屉一开，喷出一股胡椒草粉。你一直打喷嚏到打烊。|一个灰色的小东西从抽屉里窜出来，钻进了另一个抽屉，你始终不知道是哪个。|那个有雪味的抽屉里装满了寒气。寒气跑了出来，铺子到中午都结着霜。}\n# en: Sign it off unchecked\nopt.right.label [49847c] = 没核对就签字\n# en: {You tick the row and move on. Behind you a drawer rattles, as if to say it noticed.|\"Checked,\" you write. Pell reads it, opens one drawer at random, and closes it again with a look.|You sign off the row, cold drawer and all. Pell checks that one himself, as if he'd expected you to.}\nopt.right.say.1 [1abc55] = {你打了个勾，继续往下。身后一个抽屉咔哒一响，像是在说它看见了。|你写下“已核对”。佩尔看了看，随手拉开一个抽屉，又关上，看了你一眼。|你把这一排签了，连那个冷冰冰的抽屉也签了。佩尔亲自查了那一个，好像早料到你会这样。}\n\n@event apothecary_clerk_counter_advice\n# en: Asking the Dispenser\ntitle [23739a] = 请教配药师\n# en: {Dispensers advise, and today a customer asks you instead of Pell.|Pell has moved your stool to the end of the counter, where the customers come.|You know the regular coughs by sound now, before anyone opens the door.} {A farmer has a cough that sounds like a gate.|A nervous student wants something for courage before exams.|A barge-hand has a back that hasn't straightened since autumn.}\ntext [13f53a] = {配药师要给人出主意，今天有位客人没找佩尔，而是来问你。|佩尔把你的凳子挪到了柜台尽头，客人们来的那一头。|如今常客的咳嗽声你一听就认得，门还没开就认得。} {一个农夫咳得像一扇破门。|一个紧张的学生想要点考前壮胆的东西。|一个驳船工的腰从秋天起就没直过。}\n# en: A worried customer leaning over an apothecary counter, a student behind it consulting a heavy recipe book.\nscene [7ad077] = 一位忧心的客人倚在药铺柜台上，柜台后的学生正翻阅一本厚重的配方书。\n# en: Prescribe it yourself\nopt.left.label [34d97a] = 自己开方\n# en: {You mix it, label it and explain the dose twice. A week later they come back just to say it worked.|Your remedy, your writing on the bottle. Pell reads the label, reaches for his pen, and leaves it as it is.|You know the barge-hand's back from the sound of the door. The liniment is ready before they reach the counter.}\nopt.left.say.1 [2154a0] = {你配好、贴上标签，把用量讲了两遍。一周后他们专程回来，只为说一句管用。|你的方子，瓶上是你的字。佩尔读了标签，伸手去拿笔，又原样留下了。|你听开门声就知道是那个船工的腰又来了。人还没走到柜台，药膏已经备好了。}\n# en: {It helps a little, then not at all. They come back, and this time they ask for Pell.|Pell takes the bottle from your hand, adds one thing and gives it back. He doesn't say a word, which is worse.|You recommend the liniment for the barge-hand's back. It's the wrong one, and the barge-hand smells of it for a week.}\nopt.left.say.2 [73fce8] = {起初有点用，后来就没用了。他们又来了，这回点名要佩尔。|佩尔从你手里接过瓶子，加了一样东西，又递还给你。他一句话也没说，这更糟。|你给船工的腰推荐了药膏。推荐错了，船工身上那股味儿留了一个星期。}\n# en: Fetch one of the daughters\nopt.right.label [f428e6] = 去叫佩尔家的女儿\n# en: {The elder daughter takes over and talks the customer down like a nervous horse. You watch and write it all down.|The younger daughter mixes it in half the time you would have. She lets you hold the funnel.|Both daughters come, and argue the case across you. You learn twice as much and get the cure from neither.}\nopt.right.say.1 [fd0320] = {大女儿接过手，像安抚一匹受惊的马一样把客人安抚下来。你在一旁看，全记了下来。|小女儿配药只用了你一半的时间。她让你帮忙扶漏斗。|两个女儿都来了，隔着你争论起来。你学到了双倍的东西，可方子哪个也没给你。}\n\n@event apothecary_clerk_labelling_pen\n# en: The Labelling Pen\ntitle [cbd6f5] = 标签笔\n# en: Old Pell is very proud of his labelling. So it means something when he puts his own pen in your hand, its nib worn to his slant, and slides a stack of blank labels across the counter. \"New stock,\" he says. \"Your hand from now on. Neat, mind.\"\ntext [ea44d4] = 老佩尔对自己写的标签极为自豪。所以当他把自己的笔放进你手里——笔尖已经磨成他的斜度——又把一摞空白标签推过柜台时，这就意味着什么。“新货，”他说，“往后用你的字。写工整点。”\n# en: An old apothecary handing over a well-worn pen beside a stack of fresh blank labels.\nscene [5f9c82] = 一位老药剂师递出一支用旧了的笔，旁边是一摞崭新的空白标签。\n# en: Label the new drawers yourself\nopt.left.label [15781b] = 亲手给新抽屉贴标签\n# en: You write every label slowly, in your best hand. Pell inspects them through his spectacles one by one and changes not a single letter. His daughters notice before you do.\nopt.left.say.1 [a6af78] = 你用最好的字迹，一张一张慢慢写。佩尔隔着眼镜逐张检查，一个字母也没改。他的女儿们比你先察觉到这意味着什么。\n# en: Ask him to watch the first one\nopt.right.label [bd2847] = 请他看你写第一张\n# en: He watches. Your first label wobbles at the end. Pell takes the pen, adds a tiny flourish to your wobble so that it looks deliberate, and returns the pen for good.\nopt.right.say.1 [f27abb] = 他看着。你的第一张标签末尾抖了一下。佩尔拿过笔，在那一抖上添了个小小的花饰，让它看起来像是故意的，然后把笔还给你，从此归你。\n\n@event broom_courier_morning_crossing\n# en: The Lake Crossing\ntitle [6090d5] = 过湖\n# en: {The dispatch bell rings. The village sack is ready, and the quickest way is over the lake.|You have started timing yourself against the gulls.|The lake knows your shadow by now.} {season == \"winter\": Ice fog sits on the water like a lid.|{season == \"summer\": The water is so still you fly over your own reflection.|{A headwind is coming off the hills.|Gulls are holding a meeting on your route.|The far shore is lost in mist.}}}\ntext [03ae44] = {派件铃响了。送往山下小镇的邮袋已经备好，最快的路是飞过湖面。|你开始和海鸥比速度了。|湖早就认得你的影子了。} {season == \"winter\": 冰雾像锅盖一样压在水面上。|{season == \"summer\": 湖水静得你像在自己的倒影上飞。|{山那边吹来一阵逆风。|一群海鸥在你的航线上开会。|对岸隐没在雾里。}}}\n# en: A courier on a broom skimming low over a mirror-still lake at dawn, a mail sack over one shoulder.\nscene [021120] = 黎明时分，一个肩挎邮袋的信使骑着飞帚，贴着镜面般平静的湖水低飞。\n# en: Fly straight across\nopt.left.label [d53a49] = 直线飞过去\n# en: {Straight as a thrown stone. You're early, and the village postmistress tips early couriers.|You skim the water so low your boots leave a wake. Nobody sees, which is a shame.|You fly straight into the mist, by memory. The far shore appears exactly where you expected it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [a90b36] = {直得像扔出去的石子。你到早了，小镇的女邮政局长会给早到的信使小费。|你贴着水面飞得那么低，靴子都划出了水痕。没人看见，真可惜。|你凭记忆一头扎进雾里。对岸恰好出现在你预料的地方。}\n# en: {A gust drops you into the shallows. The sack stays dry; you do not.|The far shore vanishes and you fly a slow circle round a buoy. You arrive late and very damp.|The mist takes the far shore and your sense of direction with it. You arrive at the right village from the wrong side.}\nopt.left.say.2 [e92243] = {一阵风把你吹进了浅水。邮袋没湿，你湿透了。|对岸消失了，你绕着一个浮标慢慢兜圈子。到的时候又晚又湿。|雾把对岸连同你的方向感一起吞了。你从错的一边到了对的村子。}\n# en: Take the long way round\nopt.right.label [d7b80a] = 沿湖岸绕远路\n# en: {Round the shore, safe and slow. The dispatcher checks the clock and sighs.|The long way. You wave to a fisherman, who does not wave back.|The long way, round the mist. The fisherman waves, for the first time. You're so surprised you nearly fall off.}\nopt.right.say.1 [e98d91] = {沿着湖岸，稳稳当当，慢慢悠悠。派件员看了看钟，叹了口气。|绕远路。你朝一个渔夫挥手，他没挥回来。|绕远路，避开了雾。那个渔夫第一次朝你挥了手。你吃惊得差点从扫帚上掉下来。}\n\n@event broom_courier_broom_scramble\n# en: The Broom Scramble\ntitle [90ac74] = 抢飞帚\n# en: {Only one loft broom is in a good state, and every courier on shift wants it.|You have started arriving early for the brooms, and so has everyone else.|You could pick the good broom blindfold, by the smell of its varnish.} {What's left is one that pulls left.|What's left sheds twigs when nervous.|What's left only flies if you're polite to it.} {season == \"winter\": Frost has got into all their bristles.|}\ntext [78fa0f] = {阁楼里只有一把飞帚是好的，当班的信使人人都想要它。|你开始提早到场抢飞帚，别人也一样。|你闭着眼凭清漆味都能挑出那把好飞帚。} {剩下的那把总往左偏。|剩下的那把一紧张就掉枝条。|剩下的那把只有你对它客气才肯飞。} {season == \"winter\": 霜已经钻进了所有飞帚的帚毛里。|}\n# en: A draughty loft rack of battered brooms, couriers jostling to grab the least battered one.\nscene [bf0220] = 透风的阁楼里一排破旧的飞帚，信使们推搡着去抢最不破的那一把。\n# en: Shoulder in for the good broom\nopt.left.label [fe1030] = 挤上去抢好飞帚\n# en: {You get there first, by a whisker and an elbow. The second-year you elbowed will remember.|You shoulder a second-year aside and the good broom is yours. It flies like a dream; theirs flies like a complaint.|You pick out the good broom by its varnish while the others are still arguing. You're over the lake before they notice.}\nopt.left.say.1 [30a9d7] = {你抢先一步，赢在毫厘和一记手肘。被你挤开的那个二年级生会记住的。|你用肩膀把一个二年级生挤到一边，好飞帚归你了。它飞起来像一场梦；他们那把飞起来像一串抱怨。|别人还在争，你已经凭清漆的气味挑出了好飞帚。等他们发现时，你已经飞过了湖。}\n# en: Take the difficult one\nopt.right.label [c8d2e7] = 骑那把难缠的\n# en: {It fights you the whole way across the lake. Your arms will remember this, and so will you.|It takes the whole route to come to an understanding with it. You win, narrowly.|You're polite to the difficult one, very polite. It flies well, and everyone else is too embarrassed to try it.}\nopt.right.say.1 [7f0918] = {它一路和你较劲，直到过了湖。你的胳膊会记住，你也会。|整条路线下来，你们才算达成了谅解。你赢了，险胜。|你对那把难伺候的很客气，非常客气。它飞得不错，别人都不好意思去试。}\n\n@event broom_courier_dead_letters\n# en: The Dead-Letter Shelf\ntitle [c14849] = 死信架\n# en: {Couriers sort the dead letters, post with no proper address, waiting on a dusty shelf.|The dead-letter shelf has grown a second row.|You know which parcels have waited longest now, and which have stopped expecting anyone.} Today, a parcel addressed only to {\"the tall one in the red scarf, third tower\"|\"whoever plays the flute at dawn\"|\"my brother, the one who never writes\"}.\ntext [56186c] = {信使要整理死信，就是那些地址不全、躺在落灰架子上的邮件。|死信架又多了一层。|你现在知道哪些包裹等得最久，哪些已经不再指望有人来取。} 今天有个包裹，收件人只写着{“围红围巾的高个子，第三塔”|“黎明时吹长笛的人”|“我哥哥，从来不写信的那个”}。\n# en: A dusty shelf of undeliverable parcels in a courier loft, one tied with a faded ribbon.\nscene [dcc319] = 信使阁楼里一架落满灰尘的无法投递的包裹，其中一个系着褪色的丝带。\n# en: Track down the recipient\nopt.left.label [6456ac] = 找到收件人\n# en: {It takes three towers and a dozen questions, but the parcel reaches the right hands. They open it at once and cry a little, in a good way.|Found them. The look on their face is worth every stair.|You find the brother. He's a porter in the next village and he does write; the letters just never arrive. Now one has.}\nopt.left.say.1 [040dc3] = {跑了三座塔、问了十几个问题，包裹终于送到了对的人手里。他们当场拆开，哭了一小会儿，是好的那种。|找到了。他们脸上的表情值得爬每一级楼梯。|你找到了那个哥哥。他是邻村的门房，其实他写信的，只是信总寄不到。现在有一封到了。}\n# en: {You knock on every door you can think of. The parcel goes back on the shelf, a little more crumpled, a little more sad.|Wrong door. Wrong tower. Wrong week.|There are a great many brothers who never write. None of them is this one.}\nopt.left.say.2 [7d911b] = {你敲遍了能想到的每一扇门。包裹回到了架子上，更皱了一点，也更难过了一点。|敲错门。找错塔。赶错周。|从不写信的哥哥多得是。没有一个是这一个。}\n# en: Leave it on the shelf\nopt.right.label [52ecc9] = 放回架子上\n# en: {It goes back with the others. Some of them have been waiting longer than you have been alive.|You leave it. That night you dream of a parcel on a high tower, waiting.|You leave it, but you dust the shelf. The parcels that have stopped expecting anyone look a little less forgotten.}\nopt.right.say.1 [39d2ed] = {它回到了其他包裹中间。有些在那儿等的时间比你活的还久。|你没管它。那晚你梦见一个包裹在高塔上，等着。|你没管它，但掸了掸架子。那些已经不再等人的包裹，看上去不那么被遗忘了。}\n\n@event broom_courier_own_pigeonhole\n# en: Your Own Pigeonhole\ntitle [a87c3e] = 你自己的信格\n# en: The dispatcher has painted your name over a pigeonhole, in letters that will outlast the loft's roof. \"Your route from now on,\" the dispatcher says. \"Village, barge landing, the high towers. When the bell rings for you, you answer.\" The dispatch bell rings. It's for you.\ntext [f9338e] = 派件员在一个信格上方漆上了你的名字，那字迹会比阁楼的屋顶活得还久。“从今往后这是你的路线，”派件员说，“山下小镇、驳船码头、高塔区。铃为你响，你就去。”派件铃响了。是找你的。\n# en: A courier loft wall of pigeonholes, one freshly painted with a student's name, a brass bell above.\nscene [517d9b] = 信使阁楼里一整面墙的信格，其中一格刚漆上一个学生的名字，上方挂着一只黄铜铃。\n# en: Fly the route at once\nopt.left.label [9240b6] = 立刻飞一趟\n# en: You fly your own route for the first time, every turning yours. At the barge landing someone calls you by name to hand over a letter. The wind is cold, and you don't mind at all.\nopt.left.say.1 [b37714] = 你第一次飞自己的路线，每个拐弯都属于你。在驳船码头，有人喊着你的名字递来一封信。风很冷，你一点也不在意。\n# en: Stand the runners a round\nopt.right.label [631a00] = 请跑腿的伙计们喝一轮\n# en: The runners toast your pigeonhole with mugs of loft tea. Somebody chalks a little crown above your name. It is still there at the end of the year, smudged, the most permanent thing in the loft.\nopt.right.say.1 [0c186d] = 跑腿的伙计们端着阁楼茶，为你的信格干杯。有人用粉笔在你名字上方画了顶小王冠。到年底它还在，糊了，却是阁楼里最长久的东西。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/pools/courses_a.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/pools/courses_a.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event evo_snuffing_drill\n# en: The Snuffing Drill\ntitle [1894a9] = 灭火操练\n# en: {Evocation I opens with a lesson nobody expected. \"Anyone can start a fire,\" says the lecturer. \"Stopping one is the course.\"|The braziers are lit before you arrive. You know which one smokes.|Your brazier goes out when you walk in now, out of habit. The lecturer relights it, unamused.} {The front row smells of singed eyebrow.|Someone's brazier has gone out from fright.|The lecturer is timing you with an egg-timer.}\ntext [cc5e7b] = {塑能术 I 的第一课出乎所有人意料。“谁都会点火，”讲师说，“学会灭火，才是这门课。”|你到之前火盆就已经点上了。哪一只爱冒烟，你心里有数。|如今你一走进来，你的火盆就习惯性地熄了。讲师把它重新点上，脸色不太好看。}{前排飘着一股烧焦眉毛的味道。|有人的火盆被吓灭了。|讲师正拿着煮蛋计时器给你们计时。}\n# en: A row of iron braziers burning in a scorched lecture hall, students standing before them with their hands raised as if to calm a horse.\nscene [9cf592] = 焦痕斑斑的讲堂里，一排铁火盆燃着火，学生们站在盆前举起双手，像在安抚一匹马。\n# en: Call it back slowly\nopt.left.label [e24679] = 慢慢收回来\n# en: {The flames shrink into embers, then into a warm ache in your palm.|It takes the whole hour. Your brazier goes out like a sigh.|You call it back while the egg-timer runs. It goes out with a second to spare, and the lecturer turns the timer over again.} {season == \"winter\": The hall is colder afterwards, and it's your fault.|The lecturer nods once.}\nopt.left.say.1 [33d908] = {火焰缩成余烬，又缩成你掌心里一阵温热的酸胀。|整整用了一节课。你的火盆像叹了口气似的熄了。|你在沙漏计时下把火召回。它熄灭时还剩一秒，讲师又把计时器翻了过来。}{season == \"winter\": 之后讲堂冷了一截，这得怪你。|讲师点了一下头。}\n# en: Snuff it in one breath\nopt.right.label [f77f0b] = 一口气掐灭\n# en: {The fire folds itself into your hand all at once.|One breath, and the brazier is dark.|You snuff it before the egg-timer has turned.} The lecturer writes something down without looking surprised, which is the compliment.\nopt.right.say.1 [607399] = {火一下子把自己折进了你的手心。|一口气，火盆就暗了。|计时器还没翻过来，你已经把它掐灭了。}讲师头也不抬地记了一笔，一点也不显得惊讶——这就是夸奖了。\n# en: {The fire goes out. It goes out up your sleeve first.|The brazier flares, then sulks, then goes out on its own terms.|You rush it for the timer, and the fire rushes back.} You spend the rest of the hour holding your wrist in a bucket.\nopt.right.say.2 [a8748b] = {火是灭了。不过它先顺着你的袖子灭了一遍。|火盆猛地一窜，接着闹起脾气，最后按它自己的意思熄了。|你为了赶计时匆匆下手，火也匆匆反扑。}这节课剩下的时间，你都把手腕泡在水桶里。\n\n@event evo_bucket_duty\n# en: Bucket Duty\ntitle [af4be1] = 水桶值日\n# en: Every Evocation I lesson needs someone on the buckets: sand for the pyromancers, water for everyone else. {&Today it's your name on the board.|It's your turn again. The rota has a sense of humour.|Your name is on the board. You suspect the rota is personal.} {&The water bucket has a frog in it.|Someone has labelled the sand bucket \"Hope\".|The water bucket is half empty, because the tidecallers borrowed it.}\ntext [ad6c20] = 每节塑能术 I 都得有人守着水桶：沙桶留给火焰术的人，水桶留给其他所有人。{&今天黑板上写的是你的名字。|又轮到你了。这张值日表挺有幽默感。|黑板上又是你的名字。你怀疑这张值日表针对你。}{&水桶里有只青蛙。|有人在沙桶上贴了张标签：“希望”。|水桶只剩半桶，因为唤潮术的人借走了。}\n# en: A student standing at the edge of a busy lecture hall beside a bucket of sand and a bucket of water, watching sparks fly.\nscene [b53c8a] = 一个学生站在热闹讲堂的边上，身旁一桶沙、一桶水，看着火星四溅。\n# en: Mind the buckets, watch closely\nopt.left.label [6fe757] = 守好水桶，看仔细\n# en: {&You put out four sleeves and a textbook, and learn more from their mistakes than from your own.|Watching from the side, you finally see what the lecturer means by \"breathe first\".|You keep the buckets full and borrowed back from the tidecallers, and watch the whole lesson from the best seat in the hall.} {month == 4 or month == 10: It's the best revision you've done all term.|Nobody thanks the bucket monitor.}\nopt.left.say.1 [eb2b4f] = {&你扑灭了四只袖子和一本课本，从别人的失误里学到的比从自己的还多。|站在一旁看着，你终于明白了讲师说的“先呼吸”是什么意思。|你把水桶装满，又从唤潮术的人那儿要了回来，然后坐在大厅里最好的位置看完整节课。}{month == 4 or month == 10: 这是你整个学期做过的最好的复习。|没人会感谢守桶的人。}\n# en: Swap for a turn up front\nopt.right.label [38056d] = 换个机会上前练\n# en: {&You trade your duty for a turn at the practice line. Nobody is minding the buckets when your spark jumps the desk.|A first-year takes your buckets for a promise of help with their essay. Your practice goes well; your thumb goes pink.|You swap, and the tidecallers have the water bucket. Your spark finds the one dry curtain in the hall.}\nopt.right.say.1 [c6bfcf] = {&你用值日换了一次上前练习的机会。你的火星跳过课桌时，没人在守水桶。|一个一年级生答应替你守桶，条件是你帮他们改论文。你练得不错，拇指却红了一块。|你换了班，可水桶在唤潮术的人那儿。你的火星偏偏找到了大厅里唯一一块干窗帘。}\n\n@event evo_steady_cup\n# en: The Steady Cup\ntitle [1aa292] = 稳住杯子\n# en: {Professor Marisol's Tidecalling mornings begin with a full cup held at arm's length. Keep the water level by will alone while she reads poetry aloud.|Marisol has brought a new book of poems, thicker than the last.|You can hold the cup half asleep now, and often are.} {season == \"winter\": The water has a skin of ice, which she says is cheating.|{The poem is very long.|The poem is about the sea, and so are your sleeves.|A gull is watching your cup with intent.}}\ntext [31f8b5] = {玛丽索尔教授的唤潮术早课，从伸直手臂端一满杯水开始。她朗读诗歌时，你得单凭意志让水面保持平稳。|玛丽索尔带了本新诗集，比上一本还厚。|你现在半睡半醒也端得稳杯子，也确实常常半睡半醒。}{season == \"winter\": 水面结了一层薄冰，她说这算作弊。|{这首诗很长。|这首诗写的是大海，你的袖子也成了大海。|一只海鸥正意味深长地盯着你的杯子。}}\n# en: A row of students on a stone terrace holding brimming cups at arm's length while a professor reads aloud from a slim book of poems.\nscene [a1e523] = 石砌露台上站着一排学生，手臂伸直端着满溢的杯子，一位教授在一旁捧着薄薄的诗集朗读。\n# en: Hold it to the last line\nopt.left.label [18e90f] = 撑到最后一行\n# en: {Your arm burns by the third stanza. The water doesn't move.|By the end your arm is shaking and the water isn't.|The gull lands on your wrist in the last verse. The water doesn't notice, and neither, quite, do you.} The poem goes on for ever, and so, it feels, do you. Marisol closes the book at last. \"There. The lake would approve.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [a0a25d] = {读到第三节，你的手臂火辣辣地疼。水纹丝不动。|到最后，你的手臂在抖，水却没有。|最后一节时海鸥落在了你的手腕上。水毫无察觉，你也差不多。}那首诗像是永远念不完，你也觉得自己永远熬不完。玛丽索尔终于合上书：“好了。湖会满意的。”\n# en: Spill, and study her hands\nopt.right.label [9eabd6] = 洒了，转而看她的手\n# en: {Your cup tips over in the second verse. You spend the rest watching how Marisol turns her wrist, and write it down.|You give up on the cup and watch Marisol instead. She holds a whole pond steady while turning pages.|The gull takes your cup the moment you spill it. You watch Marisol hold hers steady through the gull's attempt on hers too.} {final_year: You'll miss her terrible poems.|Your shoes are wet.}\nopt.right.say.1 [4c881d] = {第二段时你的杯子就翻了。剩下的时间你都在看玛丽索尔怎么转手腕，并把它记了下来。|你放弃了杯子，改看玛丽索尔。她一边翻书，一边让整整一池水纹丝不动。|你的杯子一洒，海鸥就把它叼走了。你看着玛丽索尔在海鸥对她下手时，照样稳稳托住了自己那杯。}{final_year: 你会想念她那些糟糕的诗的。|你的鞋湿了。}\n\n@event evo_shaping_hour\n# en: Shaping Hour\ntitle [45641d] = 塑形课\n# en: Evocation II is shaping: flame made into a sphere, a key, a bird. The lecturer keeps a ledger of every scorch mark in the hall and makes you sign for yours. {flag(scorched_ledger): Your page in the ledger has a few signatures on it already.|Your page in the ledger is still blank.} {&Today's shape is a bird.|Today's shape is a key that turns.|Today's shape is your own initial, which is harder than it sounds.}\ntext [909b1c] = 塑能术 II 学的是塑形：把火捏成球、钥匙、鸟。讲师有一本账簿，记着讲堂里每一处焦痕，谁烧的谁签字。{flag(scorched_ledger): 你那一页上已经有几个签名了。|你在账簿上那一页还是空白。}{&今天要捏的是一只鸟。|今天要捏的是一把能转动的钥匙。|今天要捏的是你自己名字的首字母，比听上去难。}\n# en: A lecture hall full of small fires shaped like birds, keys and letters, one of them visibly trying to escape through a window.\nscene [876135] = 讲堂里满是捏成鸟、钥匙和字母形状的小火团，其中一团明显正想从窗户溜走。\n# en: A hundred plain spheres\nopt.left.label [d097ee] = 捏一百个普通火球\n# en: {&Sphere after sphere, until your hands do it without asking you.|It's dull, honest work. Your spheres come out round, every one.|Spheres until your fingers know the curve. Your initial would come easier after this, you suspect.} {season == \"summer\": The hall is an oven by noon.|The lecturer says nothing, which is approval.}\nopt.left.say.1 [c0d2bb] = {&一个接一个，直到你的手不用问你就会做。|枯燥而老实的活儿。你捏出的每个火球都是圆的。|一直捏火球，直到手指记住了那道弧线。你猜，这之后捏你的缩写会容易些。}{season == \"summer\": 不到中午，讲堂就成了烤炉。|讲师什么也没说，这就是认可。}\n# en: Try the hard shape\nopt.right.label [183457] = 挑战难的形状\n# en: {&Your bird flaps twice, circles the lectern and goes out on a perch.|Your key turns in the air, with a click you feel more than hear.|Your initial hangs in the air, crisp at every edge.} The lecturer turns the ledger round to show it hasn't moved.\nopt.right.say.1 [03bae9] = {&你的鸟扑了两下翅膀，绕讲台飞了一圈，落在栖木上熄灭了。|你的钥匙在半空中转动，那一声咔哒与其说听见，不如说感觉到。|你的首字母悬在空中，每一道边都清清楚楚。}讲师把账簿转过来给你看：一笔也没添。\n# en: {&Your bird has opinions, and leaves through the window.|Your key comes out as a fork, and a hot one.|Your initial comes out as somebody else's, and singes your cuff.} You sign the ledger. The lecturer blots it for you, almost kindly.\nopt.right.say.2 [a197f3] = {&你的鸟很有主见，从窗户飞走了。|你的钥匙捏成了一把叉子，还是烫的。|你的首字母捏成了别人的，还燎到了你的袖口。}你在账簿上签了字。讲师替你吸干了墨迹，几乎称得上温柔。\n# en: Shape it at a whisper\nopt.up.label [457949] = 轻声唤火塑形\n# en: {&You call the flame softly and it comes, small and obedient as a candle. The lecturer leans in to watch.|The shape comes out right first time, at a whisper. Your page in the ledger stays clean.|The flame stays the size you asked for, which is the whole lesson.}\nopt.up.say.1 [044f8c] = {&你轻轻唤火，它便来了，小小的，乖得像一支蜡烛。讲师凑近来看。|形状一次就成了，轻声细语之间。你在账簿上那一页依旧干干净净。|火焰保持着你要的大小——这门课教的就是这个。}\n\n@event evo_kettle_pairs\n# en: Fire Meets Water\ntitle [566646] = 水火相逢\n# en: {This is why Pyromancy and Tidecalling are taught together. One of you heats the kettle, one cools it, and it must simmer and never boil.|The kettles come out of the cupboard, every dent a story.|You know every kettle by its dents now. Two dents are yours.} {Your partner is a nervous second-year from Stillwater.|Your partner is very confident, which is worrying.|Your partner yawns hugely.}\ntext [6afbdf] = {火焰术和唤潮术之所以一起教，就是为了这个。你们一人加热水壶，一人给它降温，要它一直微沸，绝不能滚开。|水壶从柜子里搬了出来，每一处凹痕都有故事。|如今每把水壶你都能凭凹痕认出来。其中两处是你的杰作。}{你的搭档是个紧张的静水院二年级生。|你的搭档信心十足，这让人担心。|你的搭档打了个大大的哈欠。}\n# en: Pairs of students on either side of copper kettles, one with a palm of flame and one with a palm of water, steam curling between them.\nscene [a6ec61] = 学生两两站在铜水壶两侧，一人掌心托火，一人掌心托水，蒸汽在两人之间袅袅升起。\n# en: Take the fire side\nopt.left.label [60e2d6] = 负责加热\n# en: {You and your partner find the rhythm together, and the kettle hums all hour.|Push, give, push. The kettle simmers like a contented cat.|Your partner yawns through the whole hour, and the kettle simmers anyway. You've been doing both halves.}\nopt.left.say.1 [3fbb32] = {你和搭档一起找到了节奏，水壶整节课都在轻轻哼唱。|推，让，再推。水壶微微沸着，像只心满意足的猫。|你的搭档打了一节课的哈欠，水壶照样微沸。原来你一个人干了两份。}\n# en: {The kettle boils, then whistles, then lifts its lid at you. You both get a faceful of steam.|You push too hard. Your partner panics and freezes the spout, and the trapped steam bursts out of the lid at you.|Your partner falls asleep mid-lesson and the kettle boils. You both wake up very suddenly.}\nopt.left.say.2 [baf5b3] = {水壶滚了，接着鸣笛，然后冲你掀起了盖子。你们俩都被蒸汽扑了一脸。|你推得太猛。搭档一慌，把壶嘴冻住了，憋住的蒸汽从壶盖冲出来扑了你一脸。|搭档上课上到一半睡着了，水壶滚开了。你们俩都一下子醒了。}\n# en: Take the water side\nopt.right.label [9f0835] = 负责降温\n# en: {Cooling is the patient half. You hold the kettle back all hour and your partner gets the credit.|You lend the kettle your cold for an hour and feel drained by noon.|You hold the water side alone while your partner yawns. The lecturer notices who did the work.} {final_year: The kettle has been here longer than you, and will stay after you've gone.|Your fingertips stay damp until lunch.}\nopt.right.say.1 [b0936b] = {降温是需要耐心的那一半。你整节课都在压着水壶，功劳却归了搭档。|你把自己的凉意借给水壶一整节课，还没到中午就觉得被掏空了。|搭档打着哈欠，你独自撑着水的那一边。讲师注意到了是谁在干活。}{final_year: 这把水壶在这儿的年头比你长，你走后它也还会在。|你的指尖一直湿到午饭时分。}\n\n@event evo_deep_call\n# en: Calling From the Deep\ntitle [758834] = 唤自深处\n# en: {Advanced Evocation calls water from the lake bed, where it is cold and old and in no hurry.|The jetty is slick at this hour.|The jetty boards know your boots by now.} The lecturer paces behind the line. The water comes up carrying things. {Today, a bottle with no message in it.|Today, a single boot, laced.|Today, nothing at all, which is somehow worse.} {season == \"winter\": Ice grinds at the edges of the column.|Mist sits on the water.}\ntext [c7918a] = {高等塑能术要从湖底唤水，那里的水又冷又老，一点也不着急。|这个时辰栈桥很滑。|栈桥的木板已经认得你的靴子了。}讲师在队伍后面来回踱步。水上来时总带着东西。{今天是一只瓶子，里面没有信。|今天是一只靴子，鞋带还系着。|今天什么也没有，不知怎的反而更糟。}{season == \"winter\": 冰在水柱边缘咯吱作响。|水面上浮着一层雾。}\n# en: Advanced students on a jetty with their arms raised, a column of dark, old lake water rising toward them out of the depths.\nscene [285a58] = 高年级学生站在栈桥上高举双臂，一柱幽暗古老的湖水正从深处朝他们升起。\n# en: Reach deeper than the rest\nopt.left.label [3c22bf] = 探得比别人更深\n# en: {You call water up from somewhere it has been still for a century. It rises like a held breath and comes down gently.|The water obeys, then lingers, as if it wanted to talk.|You call from deeper than anyone, and the water brings nothing up at all. It feels like being refused.} The lecturer stops pacing.\nopt.left.say.1 [40261d] = {你从一个静止了百年的地方唤起水来。它像一口屏住的气那样升起，又轻轻落下。|水服从了，随后又流连不去，像是想说点什么。|你从比谁都深的地方唤水，水却什么也没带上来。感觉像是被拒绝了。}讲师停下了脚步。\n# en: {The lake pulls back. You're on your knees on the jetty, cold to the bone.|The water rises, considers you, and drops onto your head.|You reach for the deep water and something reaches back, gently, and lets go. You sit down on the jetty for a while.} The lecturer pulls you up with no comment, which is a comment.\nopt.left.say.2 [96ea88] = {湖往回一拽。你跪倒在栈桥上，冷到骨头里。|水升起来，打量了你一番，然后浇在你头上。|你朝深水伸手，有什么也轻轻伸了回来，然后松开了。你在栈桥上坐了好一会儿。}讲师一言不发地把你拉起来——不说话本身就是评语。\n# en: Keep to the shallows\nopt.right.label [98776f] = 守在浅水\n# en: {You keep to safer water and log what the others bring up. It's useful and it's dull.|You hold your column at a sensible depth and watch someone else go white with the cold.|You keep to the shallows. The others bring up nothing today, and look at the water a long time.} {gpa >= 3: The lecturer says you have nothing to prove. You'd like to.|You'll go deeper next time, perhaps.}\nopt.right.say.1 [1b3c19] = {你守着更安全的水域，记录别人捞上来的东西。有用，也无聊。|你把水柱维持在稳妥的深度，看着别人冻得脸色发白。|你守在浅水处。今天别人什么也没捞上来，都盯着水面看了很久。}{gpa >= 3: 讲师说你没什么需要证明的。你倒想证明一下。|下次也许再探深一点。}\n\n@event evo_signature_flame\n# en: A Fire of Your Own\ntitle [aff5b8] = 属于你的火\n# en: Advanced Evocation asks every student for a signature flame: its own colour, its own heat, its own temper. {Yours is a sulky orange.|Yours has turned a gold that you're quietly proud of.|Yours knows you now. It leans toward you when you come in.} {Somebody's flame across the hall is green and smells of pine.|Somebody's flame is silver and hums.|The lecturer's own flame is lit on the lectern, blue and tiny, never discussed.}\ntext [55236d] = 高等塑能术要求每个学生有一簇自己的标志之火：自己的颜色、自己的温度、自己的脾气。{你的是一团闹别扭的橙色。|你的已经变成一种金色，你暗暗为它骄傲。|你的火认得你了。你一进门，它就朝你倾过来。}{讲堂那头有人的火是绿的，闻起来像松林。|有人的火是银色的，还会嗡嗡响。|讲师自己的火点在讲台上，小小的，蓝色的，从不提起。}\n# en: A student alone at a stone bench in a dim hall, coaxing a flame of an unusual colour above their palm.\nscene [e0b3dd] = 昏暗的讲堂里，一个学生独自坐在石凳旁，掌心上方哄着一簇颜色不寻常的火。\n# en: Push it somewhere new\nopt.left.label [2cb28f] = 推它去新的地方\n# en: {Your flame slides into a colour nobody has a name for. The lecturer asks you to do it again, slowly.|For a moment your flame burns without smoke and without heat, just light.|Your flame leans toward the lecturer's blue one and takes a little of its colour. The lecturer covers hers.}\nopt.left.say.1 [2a3c0a] = {你的火滑进了一种没人叫得出名字的颜色。讲师请你再做一遍，慢一点。|有那么一刻，你的火燃得既无烟也无热，只有光。|你的火朝讲师那簇蓝火倾过去，借走了它一点颜色。讲师把自己的那簇罩住了。}\n# en: {Your flame flares white, takes a bite of your hand and goes back to orange as if nothing happened.|The new colour lasts a heartbeat. The blister lasts a week.|Your flame reaches for something new and finds your sleeve. It knows you, apparently, and still bites.}\nopt.left.say.2 [bd6a2a] = {你的火猛地变白，咬了你的手一口，又若无其事地变回橙色。|新颜色只维持了一次心跳。水泡却维持了一个星期。|你的火想去够点新东西，结果够到了你的袖子。它显然认得你，可照样咬。}\n# en: Refine what it already is\nopt.right.label [83d981] = 打磨它本来的样子\n# en: {You spend the morning making it steadier, not stranger. It's a better flame by noon, and a less exciting one.|You tune the heat by a hair, then another hair.|You refine it until it leans toward you on its own. That's all it does, and it's enough.} {final_year: You'll carry this fire out of Lumenhold with you.|It's yours, at least.}\nopt.right.say.1 [67bbc3] = {你花了一上午让它更稳，而不是更奇。到中午它是一簇更好的火，也是一簇更无趣的火。|你把温度调了一丝，又调了一丝。|你把它调到会自己朝你倾过来。它也就做到这一步，但够了。}{final_year: 你会带着这簇火离开流明堡。|至少，它是你的。}\n\n@event astro_tea_rota\n# en: Another Pot of Tea\ntitle [62fbae] = 又一壶茶\n# en: Astromancy I has a tea-leaf morning every week, and tea leaves require tea. {The class gets through three pots before ten.|The tea rota has your name on it again.|The teapot greets you like an old colleague.} {Your cup shows something that is either a ship or a boot.|Your cup shows a shape the textbook calls the ladder. It looks like a spoon.|Your cup shows rain. Your cup always shows rain.}\ntext [caf8a2] = 星象与占卜 I 每周都有一个读茶叶的早上，而读茶叶得先喝茶。{全班十点前就喝光了三壶。|泡茶值日表上又是你的名字。|茶壶像老同事一样跟你打招呼。}{你的杯底要么是一艘船，要么是一只靴子。|你的杯底有个形状，课本管它叫梯子。看着像把勺子。|你的杯底显示有雨。你的杯底永远显示有雨。}\n# en: A round classroom under a brass dome, every desk crowded with teacups, a huge brown teapot steaming on the lectern.\nscene [990f2d] = 黄铜穹顶下的圆形教室，每张桌上都挤满茶杯，讲台上一把棕色大茶壶冒着热气。\n# en: Drink the pot and read\nopt.left.label [d0f9d4] = 喝完一壶再读\n# en: {By the fourth cup your hands are shaking and the leaves are making more sense.|You read six cups in a row and see the pattern underneath the shapes.|Your cup shows rain again. It rains. You're starting to think that's the reading.} {season == \"winter\": At least the tea is warm.|You will not sleep tonight.}\nopt.left.say.1 [5069bc] = {到第四杯，你的手开始抖，茶叶却越来越说得通了。|你一连读了六杯，看出了形状底下的规律。|你的杯子又显出了雨。下雨了。你开始觉得那就是你的解读。}{season == \"winter\": 好在茶是热的。|你今晚别想睡了。}\n# en: Brew the next pot\nopt.right.label [213708] = 去泡下一壶\n# en: {You take the rota and brew for the room. Watching leaves settle in a whole pot teaches you more than any one cup.|You brew it strong, the way the lecturer likes it, and learn the shapes as they sink.|You brew the pot as the teapot likes it, which is the lecturer's way plus a pinch. The room reads better for it.} {season == \"summer\": The classroom is a steam bath by ten.|Nobody thanks the brewer, but everyone notices weak tea.}\nopt.right.say.1 [6aff56] = {你接下值日，给全屋泡茶。看一整壶茶叶慢慢沉淀，比读哪一只杯子学到的都多。|你按讲师喜欢的那样泡得浓浓的，看着茶叶下沉时记住了它们的形状。|你按茶壶喜欢的方式泡茶，也就是讲师的方法再多加一撮。全屋的人都读得更好了。}{season == \"summer\": 不到十点，教室就成了蒸笼。|没人感谢泡茶的人，可茶一淡，人人都会发现。}\n\n@event astro_star_names\n# en: Naming the Stars\ntitle [fb678b] = 群星之名\n# en: {Astromancy I begins with names. Three hundred stars painted on the dome, to be recited on request.|The long pole comes out. The lecturer points; you name.|You dream the star names some nights now, and wake reciting.} {Today it's the winter constellations.|Today it's the faint stars nobody can see without squinting.|Today the lecturer points at a patch of bare plaster and waits to see who invents a name.}\ntext [c2d99a] = {星象与占卜 I 从名字学起。穹顶上画着三百颗星，点到哪颗就得背出哪颗。|长杆又拿出来了。讲师指，你报名字。|如今有些夜里你会梦见星名，醒来时还在背。}{今天背冬季星座。|今天背那些不眯眼就看不见的暗星。|今天讲师指着一块光秃秃的灰泥，等着看谁会编出个名字。}\n# en: Students craning their necks under a painted dome of constellations while a lecturer points upward with a very long pole.\nscene [2a227c] = 学生们仰着脖子，头顶是画满星座的穹顶，讲师拿一根很长的杆子往上指。\n# en: Recite them until perfect\nopt.left.label [d30e26] = 背到一字不差\n# en: {You chant star names under your breath all morning, like a prayer you don't quite believe in.|By noon you can name the whole southern sky backwards.|You recite them in your sleep, apparently, and again at breakfast. Your roommate can name the southern sky now too.} {month == 4 or month == 10: The exam is close, and the names are finally sticking.|Your neck aches from looking up.}\nopt.left.say.1 [510c73] = {你一上午都在低声念星名，像在念一段自己不太信的祷词。|到中午，你已经能把整片南天倒着背出来。|显然你在梦里背，早饭时又背。你的室友现在也能报出整片南天了。}{month == 4 or month == 10: 考试近了，这些名字总算记牢了。|你的脖子仰得发酸。}\n# en: Volunteer to go first\nopt.right.label [391066] = 自告奋勇先背\n# en: {You name every star the pole touches, including the bare plaster, which you correctly call \"nothing\".|The lecturer runs out of stars before you run out of names.|The lecturer points at bare plaster. You invent a name so plausible that two people write it down.}\nopt.right.say.1 [1dc2c2] = {长杆点到哪颗你就报出哪颗，连那块灰泥也没难倒你——你准确地称它为“什么也没有”。|讲师的星星都点完了，你的名字还没报完。|讲师指向一块光秃秃的灰泥。你编了个名字，像模像样到有两个人记了下来。}\n# en: {You call a bright star by the name of a lesser one. The lesser one's champion in the back row is offended.|You blank on the fourth star, and a whole constellation falls out of your head.|The lecturer points at bare plaster and you name it confidently, with a star that doesn't exist. It's in three people's notes by lunch.}\nopt.right.say.2 [6cd980] = {你把一颗亮星叫成了一颗暗星的名字。后排那颗暗星的拥护者很不高兴。|背到第四颗你脑子一空，一整个星座从你脑袋里掉了出去。|讲师指着一块灰泥，你信心满满地叫出了一颗不存在的星。到午饭时，它已经出现在三个人的笔记里了。}\n\n@event astro_cold_dome\n# en: The Open Dome\ntitle [06fe07] = 敞开的穹顶\n# en: {Astromancy mornings start before the sun, under a dome cranked open to the sky.|The dome creaks open. You've learned which side of the stove is warmest.|The telescope's eyepiece has the shape of your eye by now.} {season == \"winter\": It is bitterly cold, and the stove is the most popular student in the class.|{season == \"summer\": The air is soft and the sky is already going pale.|There's dew on the telescope and on you.}} Somebody has to take the telescope.\ntext [7c7b4e] = {星象学的早课在日出前开始，穹顶摇开，直对天空。|穹顶吱呀呀地打开。你已经摸清了火炉哪一边最暖。|望远镜的目镜如今已经是你眼睛的形状了。}{season == \"winter\": 冷得刺骨，火炉成了班上最受欢迎的同学。|{season == \"summer\": 空气柔和，天色已经开始泛白。|望远镜上有露水，你身上也有。}}总得有人去守望远镜。\n# en: The observatory's brass dome cranked open to a pale dawn sky, one student at the great telescope and the rest huddled round an iron stove.\nscene [66aa5f] = 观星台的黄铜穹顶摇开，露出淡淡的晨空，一个学生守在巨大的望远镜前，其余人围着一只铁炉子取暖。\n# en: Take the telescope turn\nopt.left.label [d7ad20] = 轮班守望远镜\n# en: {You track a planet across the eyepiece until {season == \"winter\": your eyelashes stick to it|your eyes water and your neck gives up}.|The great telescope wanders, as it does. You coax it back to the sky you were assigned.|The eyepiece fits your eye. You find the planet before the lecturer has finished saying where it is.} {final_year: You've looked through it more times than you can count, and you'll still miss it.|{season == \"winter\": Your fingers take an hour to thaw.|You yawn through the whole of breakfast.}}\nopt.left.say.1 [3d1054] = {你在目镜里追着一颗行星，直到{season == \"winter\": 睫毛粘在了上面|眼睛流泪、脖子也撑不住了}。|巨大的望远镜照例四处乱转。你把它哄回分给你的那片天。|目镜正合你的眼。讲师还没说完它在哪儿，你已经找到了那颗行星。}{final_year: 你已经数不清从它里面看过多少次了，可你还是会想念它。|{season == \"winter\": 你的手指过了一个钟头才暖过来。|整顿早饭你都在打哈欠。}}\n# en: Stay by the stove with charts\nopt.right.label [d88b06] = 守着炉子看星图\n# en: {The stove students form a little society of the warm and the guilty. You copy charts and hear every complaint.|You annotate the charts while the others shiver. The lecturer notices who is warm.|You keep the stove and the charts. Someone else takes the telescope and complains it's shaped wrong.}\nopt.right.say.1 [166768] = {守炉子的学生自成一个小团体，又暖和又心虚。你抄着星图，听遍了每一句抱怨。|别人冻得发抖，你在给星图做批注。讲师注意到了谁是暖和的。|你守着炉子和星图。别人接了望远镜，抱怨说它的形状不对。}\n\n@event astro_foresight_drill\n# en: Catch the Chalk\ntitle [f960c8] = 接住粉笔\n# en: {Foresight at Astromancy II is practised on chalk. The lecturer drops a stick without warning; you catch it before it falls, by seeing it a heartbeat early.|The lecturer has opened a new box of chalk, which is a threat.|The lecturer's drops have got sneakier, and so have your hands.} {The floor is white with failures.|A student from Stillwater has caught four in a row and is unbearable about it.|The lecturer is dropping two at once today.}\ntext [c74eb0] = {星象与占卜 II 用粉笔练预见。讲师毫无预兆地松手，你得在粉笔落地前接住它——靠的是提前一个心跳看见它。|讲师拆开了一盒新粉笔，这是一种威胁。|讲师的出手越来越刁钻，你的手也是。}{地上一片白，全是失手的痕迹。|一个静水院的学生连接了四根，得意得让人受不了。|讲师今天一次扔两根。}\n# en: A lecturer letting a stick of chalk fall from an outstretched hand while a student lunges to catch it a moment before it drops.\nscene [9286f6] = 讲师伸手松开一根粉笔，一个学生扑上前，在它落下前的一瞬接住了它。\n# en: Catch it before it falls\nopt.left.label [351adb] = 落地前接住它\n# en: {Your hand is there before the chalk is. The lecturer looks at it, then at you, then drops another.|You catch it a breath before the drop.|Two sticks at once. You catch one in each hand, and the lecturer drops a third out of spite.}\nopt.left.say.1 [69469f] = {粉笔还没到，你的手已经在那儿了。讲师看看粉笔，看看你，又扔了一根。|你在它落下前一口气的工夫接住了。|一次两根。你一手接一根，讲师赌气又扔了第三根。}\n# en: {You see it falling, clearly, half a second after it lands.|You catch a stick of chalk that wasn't dropped. The real one hits your shoe.|Two sticks at once, and you catch the one in the past.}\nopt.left.say.2 [6af02e] = {你清清楚楚地看见它在下落——在它落地半秒之后。|你接住了一根根本没扔的粉笔。真的那根砸在了你鞋上。|一次两根，你接住的那根是过去的。}\n# en: Time the drops instead\nopt.right.label [873a97] = 改为计时观察\n# en: {You keep a tally of every drop and every catch and start to see the lecturer's rhythm.|You work out that the lecturer always blinks first. It's not foresight, but it helps.|Your tally shows the lecturer drops the second stick a beat after the first, always. It's not foresight. It works.} Holding a little seeing-charm open the whole hour to check your tally leaves your head aching. {gpa >= 3: The lecturer asks to see your tally.|You'd rather have caught one.}\nopt.right.say.1 [8b3cef] = {你记下了每一次扔和每一次接，开始摸到讲师的节奏。|你发现讲师每次都先眨眼。这不是预见，但有用。|你的记录显示，讲师总是在第一根之后隔一拍扔第二根。这不是预见。但管用。}为了核对记录，你整整一小时都开着一个小小的视咒，头都疼了。{gpa >= 3: 讲师要看你的记录。|你宁愿接住一根。}\n# en: Let foresight do it\nopt.up.label [b09d2f] = 交给预见术\n# en: {You stop trying and let the spell look ahead for you. Your hand opens, and the chalk drops into it like a tame bird.|It's a two-chalk day. The spell shows you both, and you catch both.|The lecturer stops mid-drop and just looks at you. You catch it anyway.}\nopt.up.say.1 [6f00f3] = {你不再使劲，让咒语替你往前看。你的手一张开，粉笔就像一只驯服的鸟落了进来。|今天是两根粉笔的日子。咒语让你两根都看见了，你两根都接住了。|讲师扔到一半停了手，只是看着你。你照样接住了。}\n\n@event astro_starlight_jars\n# en: Bottling Starlight\ntitle [5ed382] = 装瓶星光\n# en: {Starlight is gathered before dawn, one star at a time, in glass jars on the observatory roof.|The roof is slippery with frost or dew, depending.|The starlight jars. Yours has a chip in the rim you've grown fond of.} {Most of the class is aiming for the brightest stars.|The lecturer says faint light keeps longer.|There's a slip of cloud coming in from the lake.}\ntext [ff47ba] = {星光要在黎明前采集，在观星台屋顶上一颗一颗地装进玻璃罐。|屋顶上不是霜就是露，滑得很。|星光罐。你那只罐口有个缺口，你已经对它有了感情。}{班上大多数人都瞄准最亮的星。|讲师说，暗淡的光存得更久。|一缕云正从湖上飘过来。}\n# en: Students on the observatory roof before dawn, holding glass jars up to the sky, each jar glowing faintly with captured light.\nscene [066f15] = 黎明前的观星台屋顶上，学生们把玻璃罐举向天空，每只罐里都微微亮着捕获的光。\n# en: Wait for the faint stars\nopt.left.label [ccc16b] = 等那些暗淡的星\n# en: {Your jar fills slowly with a cool, pale light that lasts all day in your pocket.|The faint stars come one at a time.|You wait out the cloud with your chipped jar. The faint stars come back for you, one by one.} The lecturer holds your jar up and says, \"That will keep.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [87f22b] = {你的罐子慢慢盛满了一种清凉苍白的光，在口袋里亮了一整天。|暗星一颗一颗地来了。|你抱着那只有缺口的罐子等云散。暗星一颗一颗地为你回来了。}讲师举起你的罐子：“这个存得住。”\n# en: {The cloud comes in before your jar is half full, and you're cold for nothing.|You wait so long the sun rises on you and burns the jar to plain glass.|The cloud from the lake settles in for the morning. Your chipped jar stays empty, and you stay cold.}\nopt.left.say.2 [abb18b] = {罐子还没装到一半，云就来了，你白挨了一场冻。|你等得太久，太阳升起来，把罐子晒回了一只普通的玻璃瓶。|湖上来的云整个早上都赖着不走。你那只有缺口的罐子一直空着，你也一直冷着。}\n# en: Take the bright, easy ones\nopt.right.label [a8446a] = 收亮的、容易的\n# en: {Your jar is full in ten minutes and glows like a lamp. The lecturer calls it a lamp.|Easy light, quickly gathered and gone by noon.|You fill your chipped jar with the bright ones before the cloud arrives. It's gone by lunch, as usual.} {season == \"summer\": The short nights are no help.|You're back in bed before the bell.}\nopt.right.say.1 [435903] = {十分钟你的罐子就满了，亮得像盏灯。讲师管它叫灯。|来得容易的光，收得快，不到中午就散了。|云来之前，你用亮星把那只有缺口的罐子装满了。照例，不到午饭就散了。}{season == \"summer\": 夏夜太短，帮不上忙。|铃响之前你就回到了床上。}\n\n@event astro_moving_charts\n# en: The Charts Move\ntitle [1e1b5e] = 会动的星图\n# en: {Advanced Astromancy uses charts that move. The inked stars drift to where they will be, not where they are.|The charts are livelier this morning, and one has crept off the table.|You've stopped flinching when the stars move. You still check twice.} {Your chart keeps rearranging itself into a shape like a doorway.|Your chart is sulking in one corner.|Your chart is going faster than the others.} Pin it and read it, or read it as it goes.\ntext [ea583c] = {高等星象学用的星图会动。墨画的星星会漂向它们将要在的位置，而不是现在的位置。|今早的星图格外活泼，有一张已经爬下了桌子。|星星移动时你已经不再吓一跳了。不过你还是会核对两遍。}{你的星图总是自己排成一个门洞似的形状。|你的星图缩在一角闹脾气。|你的星图跑得比别人的都快。}是把它钉住再读，还是任它走着读？\n# en: A long table covered in star charts whose inked constellations are visibly sliding across the parchment, students pinning corners down.\nscene [042f72] = 长桌上铺满星图，墨画的星座在羊皮纸上清晰可见地滑动，学生们正按住纸角。\n# en: Pin it and trace the drift\nopt.left.label [89dcbd] = 钉住它，描出轨迹\n# en: {You pin each star and trace its path, and for a moment you can see the whole season ahead laid out in ink.|The drift makes sense all at once.|You pin the fast chart and trace a season so quickly it makes you dizzy. The lecturer asks to keep the tracing.}\nopt.left.say.1 [1a33d5] = {你把每颗星钉住，描出它的轨迹，有那么一刻，整个下一季在墨迹里铺展在你眼前。|漂移的规律一下子全通了。|你钉住那张跑得快的星图，描出一整季的轨迹，快得你头晕。讲师要了你的描图留着。}\n# en: {You pour magic into the pins to hold it, and the chart slides out from under them anyway, leaving ink on your sleeves.|You hold the chart down with a binding until your magic runs thin, then trace the drift for an hour. It goes back to where it started, laughing at you in its own way.|The fast chart drags the pins with it across the table and onto the floor. You chase it on your knees.}\nopt.left.say.2 [42508a] = {你往图钉里灌注魔力想把它按住，星图还是从底下溜走了，在你袖子上留了一片墨。|你用一道束缚把星图按住，直到魔力快要耗尽，然后描了一个钟头的轨迹。它又回到了原处，用它自己的方式嘲笑你。|那张跑得快的星图拖着图钉，一路跑过桌面掉到地上。你跪着追它。}\n# en: Sketch every shape it makes\nopt.right.label [25762b] = 画下它的每个形状\n# en: {You fill four pages with the shapes the chart passes through. Most are nothing. One is almost something.|You draw it as it moves, like a portrait of someone who won't sit still.|You sketch the fast chart at its own pace. Your drawing hand is exhausted. The shapes are beautiful.} {final_year: Soon the stars will move without you watching.|The lecturer collects your pages without a word.}\nopt.right.say.1 [6cf0ae] = {你画满了四页星图经过的形状。大多什么也不是。有一个差点是点什么。|你边看边画，像给一个坐不住的人画肖像。|你按它自己的速度画那张跑得快的星图。你画画的手累坏了。那些形状很美。}{final_year: 很快，星星就会在没有你注视的地方移动了。|讲师一言不发地收走了你的画稿。}\n\n@event astro_far_glimpse\n# en: Looking Too Far\ntitle [2bc51d] = 看得太远\n# en: {At this level Foresight reaches a season ahead. The lecturer allows one glimpse each, timed by an hourglass. People who look longer come back strange.|The dark mirror waits behind its curtain.|The hourglass is turned before you sit down. The lecturer knows your pace.} {final_year: This is your last year of looking ahead from this room, which makes the mirror harder to look at.|{The curtains are drawn against the morning.|Someone before you came out very quiet.|The mirror is very clean today, which is somehow worse.}}\ntext [8e31d1] = {到了这一级，预见术能看到一季之后。讲师只许每人看一眼，用沙漏计时。看得更久的人，回来时都有些不对劲。|暗镜在帘子后面等着。|你还没坐下，沙漏就翻过来了。讲师知道你的节奏。}{final_year: 这是你在这间屋里向前看的最后一年，这让那面镜子更难看下去。|{窗帘拉着，挡住了早晨。|你前面那个人出来时一声不吭。|镜子今天格外干净，不知怎么反而更糟。}}\n# en: A student staring into a dark mirror at the centre of a curtained room, a lecturer standing close with an hourglass.\nscene [3ee78e] = 拉着帘子的房间中央，一个学生凝视着一面暗色的镜子，讲师拿着沙漏站在近旁。\n# en: Look a season ahead\nopt.left.label [39e11f] = 看一季之后\n# en: {You see a lit window and a cup going cold, and you know exactly where and when it will be. You keep it to yourself.|You see something small and true. You'll remember it when it happens.|You see a season ahead before the hourglass is half gone. You stop yourself, which the lecturer notes.}\nopt.left.say.1 [671289] = {你看见一扇亮着的窗和一杯渐凉的茶，清楚地知道那会在何时何地。你没告诉任何人。|你看见一件小而真实的事。等它发生时你会想起来。|沙漏还没漏完一半，你就看见了一季之后。你自己停了下来，讲师记下了这一点。}\n# en: {The mirror shows you too much at once. You come out with a nosebleed and a headache that lasts till dinner.|You see nothing, and then you see everything.|You look until the sand runs out, and a little after. The lecturer pulls you back by the shoulder.}\nopt.left.say.2 [0784b6] = {镜子一下子给你看了太多。你出来时流着鼻血，头疼到晚饭。|你什么也没看见，然后看见了一切。|你一直看到沙子漏完，又多看了一点。讲师抓着你的肩膀把你拉了回来。}\n# en: Look only as far as tomorrow\nopt.right.label [9c5502] = 只看到明天\n# en: {Tomorrow, says the mirror, it will rain at noon. It does. It's not much, but it's right.|You look only one day ahead. Lunch will be soup.|You look only to tomorrow, in your timed turn. It will be cold. It is.} {gpa >= 3: The lecturer says caution is a talent too.|It's safe, and it feels small.}\nopt.right.say.1 [da131a] = {镜子说，明天正午会下雨。真下了。不算什么，但它是对的。|你只往前看了一天。午饭是汤。|在计时的轮次里，你只看到明天。会很冷。果然很冷。}{gpa >= 3: 讲师说谨慎也是一种天赋。|稳妥，却显得渺小。}\n\n@event alch_stir_count\n# en: Clockwise\ntitle [255eb5] = 顺时针\n# en: {Alchemy I is mostly stirring, and the stirring is counted aloud by the whole class, in unison, like a choir with spoons.|The chorus is getting good. Someone has started harmonising.|You count stirs in your sleep now, and sometimes on the stairs.} {Today's draught is a simple tonic.|Today's draught is meant to turn blue at forty.|Today the lecturer keeps changing the count to see who's listening.}\ntext [e3e914] = {炼金与治愈 I 大半是搅拌，全班齐声数着搅拌的次数，像一支拿勺子的合唱团。|合唱越来越齐了，已经有人开始唱和声。|如今你睡着了都在数搅拌，有时走楼梯也在数。}{今天的药剂是一种简单的补剂。|今天的药剂应该在第四十下时变蓝。|今天讲师不停地改拍子，看谁在认真听。}\n# en: A laboratory of identical cauldrons, a whole class stirring in unison while the lecturer counts aloud from the front.\nscene [ddbff8] = 一间摆满同样坩埚的实验室，全班整齐划一地搅拌，讲师在前面大声数数。\n# en: Count every stir aloud\nopt.left.label [363802] = 每一下都数出声\n# en: {By the hundredth stir your voice is gone and your tonic is perfect.|Your count is right and your draught is dull. Both are what was asked.|The lecturer changes the count; you change with it, aloud. Your tonic is the only one that's right.} {month == 4 or month == 10: The exam recipes come back to you in rhythm.|Your wrist aches.}\nopt.left.say.1 [b51928] = {搅到第一百下，你的嗓子哑了，补剂完美无缺。|你数得一下不差，药剂平平无奇。这两样都正是要求的。|讲师改了数，你也跟着改，大声地。全班只有你的补剂是对的。}{month == 4 or month == 10: 考试要背的配方随着节拍回到了你脑子里。|你的手腕酸了。}\n# en: Stir by the colour instead\nopt.right.label [b579ed] = 改看颜色来搅\n# en: {You stop counting and watch the colour. It turns blue exactly when you expect it to.|You lose the count and keep the draught. The lecturer can tell, and allows it.|The count changes and you don't care, because you're watching the colour. The lecturer asks how you knew.}\nopt.right.say.1 [16cf77] = {你不再数数，只盯着颜色。它正好在你预料的那一刻变蓝。|你数乱了，药剂却保住了。讲师看得出来，也由着你。|数改了，你不在乎，因为你在看颜色。讲师问你是怎么知道的。}\n# en: {You watch the colour too closely and miss it changing. The fumes make your eyes water.|Your draught turns a colour the lecturer has never seen. It's not a good sign.|You watch the colour and miss the count changing. Your draught is right for a different recipe.}\nopt.right.say.2 [ad5f71] = {你盯颜色盯得太紧，反倒错过了它变色的那一刻。烟熏得你眼泪直流。|你的药剂变成了讲师从没见过的颜色。这可不是好兆头。|你盯着颜色，错过了改数。你的药剂对的是另一个方子。}\n\n@event alch_chopping_board\n# en: Prep Work\ntitle [41ffcb] = 备料\n# en: {&Half of alchemy, says the lecturer, is cutting things up correctly. The ingredients come from Pell & Daughters in the village, packed in straw, every one labelled in a careful hand.|The crate from the village is heavier today.|Your knife knows the way, and your thumb knows where not to be.} {&Today it's roots, to be sliced paper-thin.|Today it's beetles, to be crushed and not thought about.|Today it's berries, which stain everything.}\ntext [d8fc32] = {&讲师说，炼金有一半功夫在于把东西切对。材料来自村里的佩尔父女药铺，用稻草包着，每一样都贴着字迹工整的标签。|今天从村里运来的箱子更沉。|你的刀认得路，你的拇指也知道该躲在哪儿。}{&今天是根茎，要切得薄如纸。|今天是甲虫，要碾碎，而且别多想。|今天是浆果，什么都会被染色。}\n# en: A long scrubbed bench heaped with roots, berries and dried beetles, students bent over chopping boards with small sharp knives.\nscene [89c924] = 一条擦洗干净的长桌上堆满根茎、浆果和干甲虫，学生们俯身在砧板前，握着锋利的小刀。\n# en: Prep for the whole bench\nopt.left.label [65b98b] = 替整张桌备料\n# en: {&You cut enough roots for six. Your bench-mates bring you tea, which is fair.|You crush beetles for an hour and think firmly about other things.|You prepare enough berries for six, and your hands stay purple till dinner.} The crate is empty when you're done.\nopt.left.say.1 [caae87] = {&你切了六个人份的根茎。同桌给你端来了茶，这很公平。|你碾了一个钟头的甲虫，坚决地想着别的事。|你备好了六个人份的浆果，手一直紫到晚饭。}你忙完时，箱子空了。\n# en: Prep yours, read ahead\nopt.right.label [da51d3] = 切完自己的就预习\n# en: You rush your share and get to the reading, plus a small cut on your thumb you'll be explaining all day.\nopt.right.say.1 [31b264] = 你匆匆备完自己那份赶去预习，外加拇指上一道小伤口，得解释一整天。\n\n@event alch_bandage_round\n# en: Bandage Round\ntitle [f4fd11] = 绷带值班\n# en: {&The healing half of Alchemy I is taught in the infirmary, where Sister Amaranth has the class roll bandages and watch her work.|Sister Amaranth inspects every roll, and unrolls one of yours.|Sister Amaranth hands you a roll without looking up.} {&A third-year is in with a burnt hand from Evocation.|A first-year is in with a scraped knee and a great deal to say about it.|A second-year is in with a sprained wrist from the flight field.}\ntext [f052ed] = {&炼金与治愈 I 的治愈部分在医务室上，阿玛兰斯修女让全班卷绷带，看她做事。|阿玛兰斯修女每一卷都要检查，还拆开了你的一卷。|阿玛兰斯修女头也不抬地递给你一卷绷带。}{&一个三年级生上塑能术烧伤了手，正在这儿。|一个一年级生擦伤了膝盖，而且对此有很多话要说。|一个二年级生在飞行场扭伤了手腕。}\n# en: A sunny infirmary ward where students roll bandages at a long table while a nun in a starched apron checks their work.\nscene [fb0ff4] = 阳光明亮的医务室病房里，学生们在长桌边卷绷带，一位系着浆硬围裙的修女检查他们的活儿。\n# en: Roll bandages and watch\nopt.left.label [216947] = 卷绷带，在旁观察\n# en: {&You roll, and watch Sister Amaranth clean a cut in about three movements.|Your bandages pass inspection.|You roll, and watch Sister Amaranth strap a wrist so it can't move and doesn't hurt. You try it on your own wrist later.} {season == \"winter\": The ward fills with sniffling students all morning.|She says nothing, which is high praise.}\nopt.left.say.1 [ef727f] = {&你一边卷，一边看阿玛兰斯修女三两下就清理好一道伤口。|你卷的绷带通过了检查。|你一边卷，一边看阿玛兰斯修女把一只手腕绑得既动不了又不疼。后来你在自己手腕上试了试。}{season == \"winter\": 一上午，病房里挤满了吸鼻子的学生。|她什么也没说，这已是很高的评价。}\n# en: Offer to treat a patient\nopt.right.label [e0bf94] = 主动接诊一位病人\n# en: {&You treat the burn properly. Sister Amaranth says, \"Hm.\" From her, that is a speech.|You clean and bind the scraped knee neatly. The patient doesn't even wince. Sister Amaranth adjusts one knot.|You strap the wrist firmly and neatly. The patient flexes it and looks impressed.}\nopt.right.say.1 [90c6f1] = {&你把烧伤处理得很妥当。阿玛兰斯修女说了声“嗯”。从她嘴里出来，这就算一篇演讲了。|你把擦伤的膝盖清理包扎得干净利落。病人连眉头都没皱。阿玛兰斯修女只调了一个结。|你把手腕绑得又紧又整齐。病人活动了一下，一脸佩服。}\n# en: {&You bandage the wrong place entirely. The patient is too polite to say so; Sister Amaranth is not.|Your bandage unrolls itself and the patient has to wait while you start again.|You strap the wrist so tightly the patient's fingers go white. Sister Amaranth loosens it, and looks at you.}\nopt.right.say.2 [ffb39c] = {&你完全包错了地方。病人太客气没说，阿玛兰斯修女可不客气。|你的绷带自己散开了，病人只好等你从头再来。|你把手腕绑得太紧，病人的手指都发白了。阿玛兰斯修女把它松开，看了你一眼。}\n\n@event alch_purify_samples\n# en: Lake Water Samples\ntitle [468eaa] = 湖水样本\n# en: {Alchemy II hands out jars of murky lake water, to be made fit to drink. The lecturer has a habit of drinking the results.|The lecturer looks thirsty, which is never a good sign.|The lecturer has brought a favourite cup for this, chipped at the rim.} {Your jar has a small weed in it.|Your jar is suspiciously green.|Your jar has a tiny snail in it, which you set free.}\ntext [8b5060] = {炼金与治愈 II 发下一罐罐浑浊的湖水，要处理到能喝。讲师有个习惯，总要亲口喝下成果。|讲师看上去渴了，这从来不是好兆头。|讲师为此带来了一只心爱的杯子，杯沿有个缺口。}{你的罐子里有一根小水草。|你的罐子绿得可疑。|你的罐子里有只小蜗牛，你把它放生了。}\n# en: A bench lined with jars of murky lake water, a lecturer at the end raising one now-clear jar to drink it.\nscene [3c5465] = 实验桌上摆着一排浑浊湖水的罐子，桌尾的讲师正举起一罐已变清澈的水要喝。\n# en: Purify it thoroughly\nopt.left.label [be5a08] = 彻底净化\n# en: {Your water comes out clear and sweet. The lecturer drinks it, pauses and nods.|Clean as rainwater.|Your water goes into the lecturer's chipped cup, and the lecturer drinks it to the bottom.} {flag(purified_well): The lecturer drinks a second glass, and pretends not to.|The lecturer drinks a second glass, which nobody has seen before.}\nopt.left.say.1 [f9cb5c] = {你的水出来清澈甘甜。讲师喝了，顿了一下，点点头。|干净得像雨水。|你的水倒进了讲师那只有缺口的杯子，讲师一饮而尽。}{flag(purified_well): 讲师喝了第二杯，还假装没有。|讲师又喝了第二杯，这可是谁都没见过的。}\n# en: {You test it first, just a sip, as the lecturer always does. You spend the afternoon close to the washroom.|Your water clears, then clouds again. You taste it anyway, to be sure, and regret it all afternoon.|You purify it so hard the snail you set free comes back to complain. The water tastes of snail anyway.}\nopt.left.say.2 [02a39b] = {你学讲师的样子先抿了一口试试。整个下午你都待在离盥洗室不远的地方。|你的水清了，又浑了。为了确认，你还是尝了一口，后悔了一下午。|你净化得太狠，连你放走的那只小蜗牛都回来抗议了。水还是有股蜗牛味。}\n# en: Filter it the slow way\nopt.right.label [425b53] = 用老办法慢慢滤\n# en: {Cloth, sand and charcoal, the old way. It takes the whole lesson.|You filter it by hand and write every step down. The lecturer drinks it and says, \"Patient.\"|You filter it slowly and the snail goes back to the lake in a cup of its own. The lecturer approves of both.} {final_year: You'll be filtering water like this for the rest of your life, probably.|Your hands are grey with charcoal.}\nopt.right.say.1 [e4ea79] = {布、沙、木炭，老办法。花了整整一节课。|你亲手过滤，把每一步都记了下来。讲师喝了，说：“有耐心。”|你慢慢地过滤，那只蜗牛被装在专属的杯子里送回了湖里。讲师对两件事都表示赞许。}{final_year: 这辈子你大概都会这样滤水了。|你的手被木炭染得灰扑扑的。}\n\n@event alch_long_simmer\n# en: The Long Simmer\ntitle [191077] = 漫长的文火\n# en: {Some potions simmer for hours and must be watched. Whoever is on watch when the colour turns gets the credit, or the blame.|The class takes turns at the cauldron, and the rota has come round.|Your turn at the simmer. You've brought a cushion this time.} {The rest of the class has gone to lunch.|The potion is making a noise like a contented cat.|The lecturer has left a note that says \"Do not leave it.\"}\ntext [c30c9e] = {有些药剂要文火熬上好几个钟头，得有人看着。颜色变的那一刻谁在值守，功劳或黑锅就归谁。|全班轮流守坩埚，值日表又轮回来了。|轮到你守文火了。这回你带了个坐垫。}{其他人都去吃午饭了。|药剂发出的声音像只心满意足的猫。|讲师留了张字条，上面写着“别走开”。}\n# en: A bubbling cauldron in an empty laboratory, a single student slumped on a stool beside it with an hourglass and a book.\nscene [d188f1] = 空荡荡的实验室里，一口坩埚咕嘟冒泡，旁边一个学生瘫坐在凳子上，手边一只沙漏、一本书。\n# en: Sit the whole watch\nopt.left.label [85d1b0] = 守满整班\n# en: {Three hours, one cauldron, one hourglass. You see it turn and catch it at the right moment.|You read the same paragraph forty times and stir at every bubble.|You sit the watch on your cushion, as the note says. The potion turns, and you're there.} {season == \"summer\": The laboratory is stifling.|By the end, you can tell its mood from the bubbles.}\nopt.left.say.1 [fde0ad] = {三个钟头，一口坩埚，一只沙漏。你看着它变色，在恰好的时刻接住了。|同一段话你读了四十遍，每冒一个泡就搅一下。|你照字条说的，坐在你的垫子上守着。药剂变色了，你就在那儿。}{season == \"summer\": 实验室闷得透不过气。|到最后，你能从气泡听出它的心情。}\n# en: Set a charm to watch it\nopt.right.label [5a82a4] = 设个咒语替你看\n# en: {Your charm rings like a bell when the colour turns. You come back from lunch in time to take it off the fire.|The charm works and you get to eat.|You set the charm and leave a note of your own under the lecturer's: \"I didn't, technically.\" It works.}\nopt.right.say.1 [4479a5] = {颜色一变，你的咒语就像铃铛一样响了。你吃完午饭回来，正好把它端下火。|咒语管用，你也吃上了饭。|你设好咒语，在讲师的字条下面留了一张自己的：“严格来说，我没走。”成功了。}\n# en: {Your charm falls asleep. The potion boils over and you breathe in a lot of steam getting it off the fire.|The charm rings for no reason, three times, and the fourth time you ignore it. That was the real one: you get a faceful of steam pulling the cauldron off the fire.|The charm watches it perfectly until the lecturer's note blows onto it. Then it watches the note.}\nopt.right.say.2 [17c1a9] = {你的咒语睡着了。药剂溢了出来，你把它端下火时吸了一大口蒸汽。|咒语无缘无故响了三次，第四次你没理它。偏偏那次是真的：你把坩埚端下火时被蒸汽扑了一脸。|咒语看得好好的，直到讲师的字条被风吹到它上面。然后它就盯着字条看了。}\n\n@event alch_volatile_reagents\n# en: Volatile Reagents\ntitle [2e9938] = 不稳定的试剂\n# en: {Advanced Alchemy handles reagents that react to being looked at the wrong way. The lecturer has one rule. One person speaks at a time, and that person is the one holding the vial.|Nobody is speaking. Somebody is breathing too loudly.|You've stopped holding your breath, mostly.} {Today's vial is pale yellow and fizzing.|Today's vial is dark red and perfectly still, which is worse.|Today's vial is labelled in the lecturer's hand: \"Gently.\"}\ntext [1918d3] = {高等炼金学处理的试剂，被人看错一眼都会起反应。讲师只有一条规矩：一次只许一个人说话，那个人就是拿着药瓶的人。|没人说话。有人喘气声太大了。|你已经不再屏住呼吸了，大体上。}{今天的药瓶是淡黄色的，滋滋冒泡。|今天的药瓶是暗红色的，一动不动，这更糟。|今天的药瓶上是讲师的笔迹：“轻拿。”}\n# en: A hushed laboratory, a student holding a glass vial of glowing liquid at arm's length, everyone else very still behind a screen.\nscene [448114] = 鸦雀无声的实验室里，一个学生伸直手臂捏着一只发光的玻璃药瓶，其余人都一动不动地躲在屏风后。\n# en: Handle it yourself\nopt.left.label [733ca1] = 亲手处理\n# en: {You measure it drop by drop, speaking only the numbers. It settles exactly as it should.|Your hands don't shake.|You handle it gently, as labelled, and it behaves for you. The lecturer takes the label off and keeps it.} The lecturer lets out a breath.\nopt.left.say.1 [38830a] = {你一滴一滴地量，只开口念数字。它不偏不倚地沉静下来。|你的手一点没抖。|你照标签说的轻手轻脚，它也乖乖听话。讲师揭下标签收了起来。}讲师长舒了一口气。\n# en: {The vial pops like a cork. Your eyebrows survive; your gloves do not.|A drop too many. The flask hisses and coughs out blue smoke, and you spend the hour in the infirmary.|You handle it gently, which turns out not to be gently enough. The label lands in your hair.}\nopt.left.say.2 [d5e284] = {药瓶像软木塞一样砰地一响。你的眉毛保住了，手套没保住。|多了一滴。烧瓶嘶嘶作响，咳出一股蓝烟，你在医务室待了一个钟头。|你轻手轻脚了，结果还不够轻。标签落在了你头发上。}\n# en: Pass it to the steadiest hand\nopt.right.label [699cc4] = 交给手最稳的人\n# en: {You hand the vial to someone with steadier hands and take careful notes. It stings a little.|You watch someone else do it right. You'll know how next time.|You pass the \"Gently\" vial on. The steady hand drops it, very gently, into the sink. Everyone breathes again.} {gpa >= 3: The lecturer says you're allowed to be cautious.|You note every step.}\nopt.right.say.1 [7c59ec] = {你把药瓶递给手更稳的人，自己仔细记笔记。心里有点刺痛。|你看着别人做对了。下次你就知道怎么做了。|你把那瓶“轻拿”递了出去。那只稳手非常轻地把它丢进了水槽。大家又能喘气了。}{gpa >= 3: 讲师说你可以谨慎。|你记下了每一步。}\n\n@event alch_original_remedy\n# en: A Remedy of Your Own\ntitle [8095a0] = 你自己的药方\n# en: {Advanced Alchemy asks for an original remedy, invented, written up, and tested under Sister Amaranth's eye.|Another revision. The notebook has more crossings-out than words.|Your remedy is nearly finished. It has been nearly finished for weeks.} {This version is for headaches.|This version is for the common cold, which has resisted remedies for centuries.|This version is for exam nerves, which you need.}\ntext [554fde] = {高等炼金学要求一份原创药方：自己发明、自己写成，在阿玛兰斯修女的注视下试验。|又一次修改。笔记本上划掉的比留下的还多。|你的药方快完成了。它已经“快完成了”好几个星期。}{这一版治头疼。|这一版治普通感冒——几百年来它一直抵抗着各种药方。|这一版治考前紧张，你正需要。}\n# en: A student at a cluttered bench writing a recipe in a notebook, a small flask of their own remedy steaming beside it.\nscene [811658] = 一个学生在杂乱的实验桌前往笔记本上写配方，旁边一小瓶自制药剂冒着热气。\n# en: Test it on yourself\nopt.left.label [2dfc84] = 在自己身上试\n# en: {It works. Your headache lifts and your mood with it.|It works better than you expected.|It works. Your hands stop shaking, and for an afternoon so does your dread of exams.} {flag(remedy_in_book): Sister Amaranth adds a note to your page in her book.|Sister Amaranth writes your recipe in her own book.}\nopt.left.say.1 [4e43d6] = {成了。头疼消了，心情也跟着好了。|效果比你预想的还好。|成了。你的手不抖了，一个下午里，你对考试的恐惧也不抖了。}{flag(remedy_in_book): 阿玛兰斯修女在本子里你那一页添了一条批注。|阿玛兰斯修女把你的配方抄进了她自己的本子。}\n# en: {It does something, but not what you wanted. Your ears are warm for the rest of the day.|It cures the sniffles and replaces them with hiccups.|It calms your nerves so thoroughly that you fall asleep at the bench.}\nopt.left.say.2 [f580ae] = {它确实起了作用，只是不是你想要的。你的耳朵热了一整天。|鼻塞治好了，换来了打嗝。|它把你的神经安抚得太彻底，你在工作台前睡着了。}\n# en: Ask Sister Amaranth to review it\nopt.right.label [1828ec] = 请阿玛兰斯修女审阅\n# en: {She crosses out three ingredients and one adjective.|She reads it twice, then hands it back. \"Sound,\" she says. \"Dull.\"|She reads it and takes one dose herself, before the exam she's invigilating. \"Adequate,\" she says, calmly.} {final_year: You'll keep this recipe long after you leave Lumenhold.|It's better already, and you hate it a little.}\nopt.right.say.1 [4f9f35] = {她划掉了三种材料和一个形容词。|她读了两遍，递还给你。“可靠，”她说，“乏味。”|她读完，在她要监考的那场考试前自己服了一剂。“尚可，”她说，很平静。}{final_year: 离开流明堡很久以后，你还会留着这张药方。|已经好多了，你却有点恨它。}\n# en: Purify the base first\nopt.up.label [568fb8] = 先净化基底\n# en: {You draw every impurity out of the base before you start. The remedy comes out clear and gentle.|The base comes clean in one pass. Sister Amaranth smells it and says nothing, which is approval.|With a pure base, the fault in your recipe finally shows. You fix it.}\nopt.up.say.1 [063068] = {动手之前，你把基底里的每一丝杂质都抽了出来。药剂清澈温和。|基底一遍就净了。阿玛兰斯修女闻了闻，什么也没说——这就是认可。|基底一纯，配方里的毛病终于显了出来。你把它改好了。}\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/pools/courses_b.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/pools/courses_b.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event illu_candle_drill\n# en: The Candle Drill\ntitle [81a84c] = 蜡烛操练\n# en: {Illusion I opens with a row of cold candles. Make them burn, Professor Vey says, without burning.|Vey's drawer of candles is open. Every one is older than you.|The candle drill. Some mornings you could do it asleep.} {Vey passes her hand through each flame and says \"cold\" in the same flat voice.|Someone's flame leans in a draught only it can feel.|The student beside you has made a lovely candle and forgotten the flame.}\ntext [a2d324] = {幻术 I 以一排冷蜡烛开场。让它们燃烧，维伊教授说，但不许真的烧起来。|维伊那一抽屉蜡烛开着，每一根都比你年纪大。|蜡烛操练。有些早晨你闭着眼都能做。} {维伊把手掌从每一朵火焰中穿过，用同样平板的声音说：“冷的。”|有人的火焰朝一侧倾斜，像在躲一阵只有它感觉得到的穿堂风。|你旁边的同学造出了一根漂亮的蜡烛，却忘了火焰。}\n# en: A row of unlit candles on a lecture bench, each crowned with a flame that casts no shadow, a professor holding her palm over one.\nscene [f627aa] = 讲台长桌上一排未点燃的蜡烛，每根都顶着一朵不投影子的火焰，一位教授把手掌悬在其中一朵上方。\n# en: Hold the flame until it's right\nopt.left.label [6ee431] = 守着火焰直到对为止\n# en: {Your flame steadies, then flickers in time with your breathing, which Vey says is the point.|Vey's palm hovers over your flame a moment longer than the others. \"Almost warm,\" she says. From her, that's a sonnet.|By the bell your flame has a proper blue heart. You feel oddly proud of something that isn't there.}\nopt.left.say.1 [11c5cf] = {你的火焰稳住了，随后跟着你的呼吸一起闪烁，维伊说这正是要点。|维伊的手掌在你的火焰上方比别人多停了一会儿。“几乎是暖的。”她说。出自她口，这简直是一首十四行诗。|下课铃响时，你的火焰有了一颗蓝色的芯。你为一样根本不存在的东西感到莫名骄傲。}\n# en: Make it gutter and smoke\nopt.right.label [5f6b6b] = 让它摇曳冒烟\n# en: {You add a thread of smoke and a little gutter. The student behind you reaches to pinch it out.|Your candle drips wax that isn't there onto a desk that is. Vey watches it fall and says nothing, which means yes.|Your candle gutters and smokes so well that the student beside you lends it their flame. Vey writes \"two for one\".}\nopt.right.say.1 [e4aead] = {你添上一缕烟和一点摇曳。后排的同学伸手想把它掐灭。|你的蜡烛把不存在的蜡滴在真实的桌面上。维伊看着它落下，一言不发，意思是行。|你的蜡烛摇曳冒烟得太逼真，旁边的同学把自己的火苗借给了它。维伊写下“一举两得”。}\n# en: {The smoke goes up and the flame goes out, as though embarrassed by its own ambition.|Vey waves a hand through the whole performance. \"A candle doesn't try this hard,\" she says.|You add so much smoke the drill becomes a fog. Vey opens a window that isn't there, and the fog obligingly leaves.}\nopt.right.say.2 [b6c491] = {烟升了起来，火却灭了，像是为自己的野心难为情。|维伊一挥手穿过你的整场表演。“蜡烛不会这么卖力。”她说。|你添的烟太多，练习成了一场雾。维伊打开一扇并不存在的窗，雾也很配合地出去了。}\n\n@event illu_one_small_change\n# en: One Small Change\ntitle [d7183c] = 一处小改动\n# en: {Hand mirrors all round. Glamour your own face, Vey says; change one small thing and hold it.|Out come the mirrors. A glamour you can't hold on yourself, Vey says, won't hold on anyone.|Mirror practice. You know your own face too well by now.} {Vey says people always change their eyes first.|A second-year from Rootward is giving himself a better chin, slowly.|The mirrors fog, as they do {season == \"winter\": in the cold|when the room warms}.}\ntext [2e07ab] = {人手一面小镜子。给自己的脸施魅影，维伊说；改一处小地方，并且维持住。|镜子拿出来了。维伊说，连自己身上都撑不住的魅影，在谁身上也撑不住。|镜子练习。到如今你对自己的脸太熟悉了。} {维伊说，人们总是先改眼睛。|一个根庐的二年级生正在慢慢给自己换个更好看的下巴。|镜子起了雾，{season == \"winter\": 天冷时总是这样。|屋里一暖就会这样。}}\n# en: Students bent over little hand mirrors, one changing the colour of her own eyes, another squinting at a nose that isn't quite his.\nscene [274c55] = 学生们俯身对着小手镜，一个正在改变自己眼睛的颜色，另一个眯眼看着一只不太像自己的鼻子。\n# en: Change something trivial\nopt.left.label [1bc550] = 改点无关紧要的\n# en: {You move a freckle half an inch. It takes the whole hour. Vey initials your page without looking up.|You darken one eyebrow and keep it dark. Dull, careful work, and the glamour holds.|A small scar vanishes and comes back, vanishes and comes back. By the end it stays gone until you let it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [a7b2a4] = {你把一颗雀斑挪了半寸，花了整整一节课。维伊头也不抬地在你的纸上签了字。|你把一边眉毛描深，一直保持着。枯燥又细致的活儿，魅影稳住了。|一道小疤消失又出现，消失又出现。到最后，除非你放手，它一直不见。}\n# en: Change what you'd really change\nopt.right.label [e542b4] = 改你真正想改的\n# en: {It holds beautifully. You look at it longer than the exercise requires, and let it go more slowly than you'd like.|Vey glances over your shoulder. \"Ah,\" she says, kindly enough. \"That one. Everybody has one.\"|You hold it all hour. Afterwards your real face seems slightly unfamiliar, like a coat you've lent out.}\nopt.right.say.1 [bf826e] = {它维持得很美。你看它的时间比练习要求的长，放手时也比你愿意的慢。|维伊从你肩后瞥了一眼。“啊，”她说，语气还算温和，“那一处。每个人都有一处。”|你维持了整节课。之后你真正的脸显得有点陌生，像一件借出去又还回来的外套。}\n\n@event illu_spot_the_coin\n# en: Spot the Coin\ntitle [5eda6f] = 找出那枚硬币\n# en: {Partner work. {partner} lays out three crowns, and one of them is an illusion. Say which.|Three crowns from {partner}. One isn't there, and {they} look{s} pleased about it.|You and {partner} have started keeping score.} {Vey walks the room flicking real coins, to confuse everyone.|The windows are bright and the illusions show it.|The next bench is arguing whether a coin can be \"partly fake\".}\ntext [6cbe59] = {搭档练习。{partner}摆出三枚克朗，其中一枚是幻象。说出是哪一枚。|{partner}摆出三枚克朗。有一枚并不存在，{they}看起来对此很得意。|你和{partner}已经开始记分了。} {维伊在教室里走动，往空中弹真硬币，存心把大家搅糊涂。|窗外很亮，幻象在光里露了馅。|隔壁桌在争论一枚硬币能不能“部分是假的”。}\n# en: Two students at a bench with three coins between them, one coin shimmering very slightly at the edge.\nscene [056f6c] = 两个学生对坐在长桌旁，中间放着三枚硬币，其中一枚边缘微微闪烁。\n# en: Study the coins honestly\nopt.left.label [1297d9] = 老老实实看硬币\n# en: {It's the one with no tarnish. Illusions always come out cleaner than money does.|You stare until your eyes water and pick the middle one. Wrong. {partner} pockets the real two, laughing.|You get it on the third try, by the shine that doesn't move when the light does.}\nopt.left.say.1 [69ff81] = {是没有锈迹的那枚。幻象总比真钱干净。|你盯到眼睛发酸，选了中间那枚。错了。{partner}笑着把两枚真的收进口袋。|第三次你猜对了，凭的是那道不随光线移动的反光。}\n# en: Watch their face, not the coins\nopt.right.label [8b4837] = 看对方的脸，不看硬币\n# en: {{partner} glances at the left coin a fraction too fondly. You point. {They} groan{s}.|You keep smiling until {partner} gives it away by smiling back. Vey notes that this isn't the exercise, and that it worked.|You know {partner}'s face by now. {They} know{s} you know, and pick{s} a coin with a straight face to beat you. It doesn't work.}\nopt.right.say.1 [fda8ff] = {{partner}瞄向左边那枚的眼神稍稍太温柔了。你一指。{they}哀叹一声。|你一直笑，直到{partner}回了你一个笑，露了底。维伊指出这不是练习的内容，也指出它奏效了。|你现在已经熟悉{partner}的表情了。{they}知道你熟悉，于是板着脸挑了一枚来对付你。没用。}\n# en: {{partner} has a better face than you'd thought. Your wrong guess buys {them} a cup of tea.|You watch {partner} so hard you miss the coin blinking out of existence entirely.|{partner} has been practising a blank face in the mirror. It shows. You lose a point and buy the tea.}\nopt.right.say.2 [86633f] = {{partner}的表情管理比你想的好。你猜错的代价是请{them}喝一杯茶。|你盯{partner}盯得太用力，完全没看见那枚硬币凭空消失。|{partner}一直在对着镜子练面无表情。看得出来。你丢了一分，还得请喝茶。}\n\n@event illu_bread_smell\n# en: The Smell of Bread\ntitle [9ea386] = 面包的香味\n# en: {Illusion II adds the other senses. Today, a loaf that looks, smells and sounds fresh when broken.|More bread. Sight is easy, Vey says; smell is where liars are caught.|The bread lesson. The floor smells of loaves that never existed.} {Somebody's loaf smells strongly of wet dog.|One loaf crackles beautifully and smells of nothing.|{gpa >= 3: Vey asks you to go first.|Vey asks you to go last, which feels pointed.}}\ntext [0bd632] = {幻术 II 加入了其他感官。今天的题目是一条看起来、闻起来、掰开时听起来都新鲜的面包。|面包课还没完。视觉容易，维伊说；骗子都栽在气味上。|面包课。整层楼都弥漫着从未存在过的面包的味道。} {有人的面包浓浓一股湿狗味。|有一条面包咔嚓作响，却什么味道也没有。|{gpa >= 3: 维伊让你第一个来。|维伊让你最后一个来，像是意有所指。}}\n# en: A lecture bench with an illusory loaf of bread on a board, steam rising from it, and several students sniffing it doubtfully.\nscene [92b758] = 讲台长桌上，木板上放着一条幻象面包，正冒着热气，几个学生半信半疑地凑上去闻。\n# en: Get the smell right\nopt.left.label [721e15] = 把香味做对\n# en: {You spend an hour on the smell of a crust. By the end it's nearly convincing and you're desperately hungry.|You think of a bakery on a cold street and the smell arrives with it. Vey sniffs, and nods once.|The smell is right. The sound of breaking is wrong. Vey says that's the usual order.}\nopt.left.say.1 [5c9a39] = {你花了一小时琢磨面包皮的味道。到最后几乎以假乱真，而你饿得发慌。|你想起寒冷街角的一家面包房，香味便随之而来。维伊嗅了嗅，点了一下头。|香味对了，掰开的声音不对。维伊说这是常见的顺序。}\n# en: Add the warmth of the oven\nopt.right.label [111260] = 加上烤炉的温度\n# en: {Vey picks your loaf up and puts it down hastily, as if it had burnt her. The class actually applauds.|The bench beside you swears the room got warmer. It didn't. That's the point.|You add the warmth of the oven and the smell of the morning after. Vey takes your loaf home, she claims, by mistake.}\nopt.right.say.1 [7b1411] = {维伊拿起你的面包，又匆忙放下，像被烫着了。全班真的鼓起了掌。|旁边那桌发誓屋里变暖了。并没有。这正是重点。|你添上了烤炉的温度和第二天清晨的气味。维伊把你的面包带回了家，她说是拿错了。}\n# en: {The warmth arrives and the loaf leaves. You're left holding a hot, empty board.|Your bread steams, then smokes, then smells of a cupboard. Vey writes something short.|You add warmth, and the smell goes with it. The loaf is perfect and hot and smells of absolutely nothing.}\nopt.right.say.2 [9771e5] = {温度来了，面包走了。你手里只剩一块发烫的空木板。|你的面包冒气，然后冒烟，然后有股橱柜味。维伊写下了很短的一句评语。|你添了温度，气味却跟着没了。面包完美、滚烫，而且一点味道都没有。}\n\n@event illu_find_vey\n# en: Find the Professor\ntitle [4d9a54] = 找出教授\n# en: {Professor Vey is rarely at the lectern in Illusion II. She is somewhere in the room, wearing someone else, and the first to name her wins.|Vey's chair is empty. Everyone eyes the porter with the mop.|The lectern is empty. You trust nothing in this room.} {There's a pigeon on the sill.|A student nobody remembers has taken the back row.|One of the portraits seems unusually attentive.}\ntext [93c884] = {幻术 II 的课上，维伊教授很少站在讲台后。她就在教室某处，扮成别人，第一个叫出她的人获胜。|维伊的椅子空着。大家都盯着那个拿拖把的杂役。|讲台空着。这间屋里你什么都不信了。} {窗台上有只鸽子。|后排坐着一个谁也不记得的学生。|有幅肖像画看起来格外专注。}\n# en: A lecture hall where the professor's chair is empty and everyone is looking suspiciously at everyone else, including a pigeon on the sill.\nscene [f6ad02] = 一间讲堂，教授的椅子空着，每个人都狐疑地打量着别人，包括窗台上的一只鸽子。\n# en: Watch everyone, all lesson\nopt.left.label [b92af8] = 整节课盯着每个人\n# en: {You don't find her, but you notice who blinks late. Vey turns out to have been the pigeon.|At the end she steps out of the porter's coat. You'd suspected the mop. The mop was real.|You learn three classmates' nervous habits and nothing about Vey, who was the attentive portrait all along.}\nopt.left.say.1 [e45672] = {你没找到她，但留意到了谁眨眼慢半拍。原来维伊是那只鸽子。|最后她从门房的外套里走了出来。你本来怀疑的是拖把。拖把是真的。|你摸清了三个同学的紧张小动作，却对维伊一无所知——她从头到尾都是那幅专注的肖像。}\n# en: Name her early\nopt.right.label [cf9752] = 抢先叫出她\n# en: {You name her before the bell has finished ringing. She says, \"Hm,\" which from Vey means a great deal.|\"Professor,\" you say to the new student in the back row. The glamour falls away like a dropped shawl. Vey looks almost pleased.|You name the attentive portrait. The paint slides off Vey's face like a curtain. \"Hm,\" she says.}\nopt.right.say.1 [80bf8b] = {铃声还没响完，你就叫出了她。她说了声“嗯”，从维伊嘴里说出来，分量很重。|“教授。”你对后排那个新面孔说。魅影像一条滑落的披肩般褪去。维伊看上去几乎是满意的。|你叫出了那幅专注的肖像。颜料像帘子一样从维伊脸上滑落。“嗯，”她说。}\n# en: {You accuse a perfectly ordinary classmate. He will remember it. So will Vey, who was standing behind you.|You accuse the quiet student in the back row, who turns out to be a quiet student in the back row. Vey was the mop.|You point at the portrait. The portrait points back. Vey, at the lectern after all, makes a note.}\nopt.right.say.2 [4d12ea] = {你指认了一个再普通不过的同学。他会记住这件事。维伊也会——她就站在你身后。|你指认了后排那个安静的学生，结果那真的只是个后排的安静学生。维伊是那把拖把。|你指向那幅肖像。肖像也指向你。维伊原来就在讲台后，记了一笔。}\n\n@event illu_courtyard_hour\n# en: Holding the Courtyard\ntitle [9a561c] = 撑住庭院\n# en: {Advanced Illusion leaves the classroom. Hold an illusion over the busy courtyard for an hour, and don't let it fray.|Another courtyard hour. The porters step round you now.|The courtyard. {final_year: One of the last times you'll hold this sky.|Your hands know the weight of it.}} {A flock of swallows today.|Rain that doesn't wet anyone.|{season == \"winter\": Snow that never lands.|{season == \"spring\": Blossom that never lands.|{season == \"autumn\": Leaves that never land.|Thistledown that never lands.}}}}\ntext [3cbd95] = {高等幻术走出了教室。在热闹的庭院上空维持一个幻象一小时，别让它散开。|庭院一小时，照旧。杂役们现在会绕着你走。|庭院。{final_year: 这是你最后几次撑起这片天空了。|你的双手已熟悉它的分量。}} {今天是一群燕子。|淋不湿任何人的雨。|{season == \"winter\": 永远落不了地的雪。|{season == \"spring\": 永远落不了地的花瓣。|{season == \"autumn\": 永远落不了地的落叶。|永远落不了地的蓟花绒。}}}}\n# en: A student standing very still in a busy courtyard, sweat on their brow, while a flock of illusory swallows circles above the crowd.\nscene [84d039] = 一个学生一动不动地站在热闹的庭院里，额上冒汗，一群幻象燕子在人群上方盘旋。\n# en: Hold it the full hour\nopt.left.label [f25e9f] = 撑满一整个小时\n# en: {By the end your head rings like a struck bell. Nobody looked up once, which is how you know it worked.|A first-year holds out a hand to catch something you made. You keep it falling for her until the bell.|You hold it, then sit on the fountain's edge for longer than you'd like anyone to see.}\nopt.left.say.1 [40c796] = {结束时你的脑袋嗡嗡作响，像被敲过的钟。没有一个人抬头看过，这说明你成功了。|一个一年级生伸手去接你造出来的东西。你一直让它为她飘落，直到下课铃响。|你撑住了，然后在喷泉边坐了好一会儿，比你愿意让人看见的更久。}\n# en: Keep it small and steady\nopt.right.label [e4edc7] = 做小一点，求稳\n# en: {You keep only a corner of it, perfect, for an hour. Vey says ambition is cheap and an hour is not.|You shrink it to a patch of weather over one bench. It never frays, and nobody notices, which is the point and the pity.|You keep it small: a few flakes over one bench, falling for the hour. A first-year sits under it and doesn't know why she's happy.}\nopt.right.say.1 [2f1933] = {你只留住它的一角，完美无瑕，整整一小时。维伊说野心廉价，一小时可不廉价。|你把它缩成一张长椅上空的一小片天气。它始终没散，也没人注意到——这既是重点，也是遗憾。|你把它缩得很小：一张长椅上空飘着几片，落了整整一个钟头。一个一年级生坐在下面，不知道自己为什么高兴。}\n\n@event illu_original_work\n# en: An Illusion of Your Own\ntitle [61c895] = 你自己的幻象\n# en: {Original work, an illusion nobody taught you. Vey asks one question of each piece. \"What does it make people feel?\"|More original work. Vey's question hasn't changed; your answers get longer.|Studio morning. {final_year: Next year, Vey says, she'll ask someone else.|Vey goes round the room asking her question.}} {A classmate has made a door that sighs.|Someone has made the smell of rain on hot stone.|Someone's illusion is humming.}\ntext [2e4955] = {原创作业，一个没人教过你的幻象。维伊对每件作品只问一个问题。“它让人感受到什么？”|原创作业继续。维伊的问题没变，你的回答越来越长。|工作室的早晨。{final_year: 明年，维伊说，她就要问别人了。|维伊在屋里挨个问她的问题。}} {有个同学做了一扇会叹气的门。|有人做出了雨落在热石头上的气味。|有人的幻象在哼歌。}\n# en: A darkened studio room, a student alone at the centre, and around them the half-formed image of a lit window at dusk.\nscene [62326f] = 一间昏暗的工作室，一个学生独自站在中央，四周是一扇黄昏亮灯的窗户的半成形影像。\n# en: Make something true\nopt.left.label [dd57e1] = 做一件真实的东西\n# en: {You make the window of a house you grew up near, at dusk. Vey stands in front of it a long time and writes nothing down.|You make a corridor that remembers who walked down it. It's sadder than you meant. Vey says that's usually the good sign.|You make the hum of a kitchen at night, kettle cooling. Vey sits down in front of it, which she never does.}\nopt.left.say.1 [4d3103] = {你做出了童年附近一栋房子黄昏时的窗。维伊在它前面站了很久，什么也没写。|你做了一条记得谁走过的走廊。它比你预想的更悲伤。维伊说，这通常是好兆头。|你做出了夜里厨房的嗡嗡声，水壶慢慢凉下去。维伊在它前面坐了下来，她从不这样。}\n# en: Make something dazzling\nopt.right.label [9f8272] = 做一件耀眼的东西\n# en: {A staircase of light climbs into the ceiling and keeps going. The class goes quiet. \"Yes,\" says Vey. \"But what does it feel like?\"|Your piece fills the room with lanterns, one for each person watching. Vey counts them, and finds one for herself.|Your piece hums and blazes and then, at the end, goes very quiet and small. Vey writes \"that last part\" in her notes.}\nopt.right.say.1 [664883] = {一道光之阶梯升进天花板，还在继续往上。全班安静下来。“是的，”维伊说，“可它感觉起来像什么？”|你的作品让满屋亮起灯笼，每个观看的人一盏。维伊数了数，发现有一盏是给她的。|你的作品嗡鸣、绽放，最后变得非常安静、非常小。维伊在笔记里写下“最后那一段”。}\n# en: {It's dazzling for a breath and then empty, like a fair after the fair has gone.|Vey watches it all, politely. \"Very bright,\" she says. You'd have preferred anything else.|It hums, and blazes, and Vey waits politely for it to stop. \"And when it's over?\" she asks. You don't have an answer.}\nopt.right.say.2 [5c7316] = {它耀眼了一瞬，然后空了，像集市散场后的集市。|维伊礼貌地看完了全程。“很亮。”她说。你宁愿听到任何别的评语。|它嗡鸣、绽放，维伊礼貌地等它结束。“结束以后呢？”她问。你答不上来。}\n\n@event beast_mucking_out\n# en: Mucking Out\ntitle [2efb0b] = 清理马厩\n# en: {Beastcraft I begins in the stables, with a pitchfork. Professor Brask says you don't touch a creature until you've cleaned up after it.|Pitchforks. Brask still hasn't said when that rule ends.|The stables, early. You know which stall to start with.} {The hippogriff in the end stall is watching you.|Owls shift in the rafters, unimpressed.|{season == \"summer\": The heat doubles the smell.|A cold wind finds every gap.}}\ntext [99dfd0] = {驭兽学 I 从马厩和一把干草叉开始。布拉斯克教授说，没替一只生灵收拾过，就别碰它。|干草叉。布拉斯克仍然没说这条规矩什么时候结束。|清早的马厩。你知道该从哪个隔栏开始了。} {尽头隔栏里的鹰头马正盯着你。|猫头鹰在椽子上挪动，一脸不以为然。|{season == \"summer\": 热天让气味翻了一倍。|冷风钻进每一道缝。}}\n# en: A student with a pitchfork in a straw-strewn stable, a hippogriff watching from its stall with a look of deep judgement.\nscene [8f7a38] = 一个学生拿着干草叉站在铺满稻草的马厩里，一头鹰头马从隔栏里投来深深审视的目光。\n# en: Muck out properly\nopt.left.label [795f12] = 认真清理干净\n# en: {Two stalls, a barrow and a blister. Brask inspects your work and calls the straw \"nearly decent\".|You finish before anyone else. The hippogriff treads straight through your clean straw, deliberately.|Brask says nothing, which you've learned is the best review she gives humans.}\nopt.left.say.1 [390e61] = {两个隔栏、一辆推车、一个水泡。布拉斯克检查后说这稻草“勉强像样”。|你第一个干完。鹰头马故意从你刚铺好的干净稻草上踩了过去。|布拉斯克一言不发，你已经明白这是她给人类的最高评价。}\n# en: Bow to the hippogriff first\nopt.right.label [d249bf] = 先向鹰头马鞠躬\n# en: {You bow low and hold it. The hippogriff considers, then bows back. Brask writes your name on the board, without comment.|The hippogriff lets you near enough to see where its feathers turn to fur. You'll remember the exact line.|You bow to the hippogriff in the end stall, as you always do now. It bows back before you've finished.}\nopt.right.say.1 [d4557e] = {你深深鞠躬并保持住。鹰头马考虑了一下，回了一礼。布拉斯克在黑板上写下你的名字，不加评语。|鹰头马让你走近到能看清它的羽毛在哪儿变成皮毛。你会记住那条分界线。|你像现在每次那样，朝最里面隔间的鹰头马鞠躬。你还没鞠完，它就回礼了。}\n# en: {You bow too fast. The hippogriff takes it as a challenge and accepts.|The hippogriff looks at your bow, then at your hat, and shoves you into the barrow to get at it. It eats the hat.|You bow and the hippogriff ignores you entirely, which is worse than the hat. You muck out under its scorn.}\nopt.right.say.2 [03c9ed] = {你鞠躬太快了。鹰头马把它当成挑战，并且接受了。|鹰头马看了看你的鞠躬，又看了看你的帽子，一把把你拱进推车，够着了帽子。它吃掉了帽子。|你鞠了躬，鹰头马完全不理你，这比吃帽子还糟。你在它的鄙夷下清完了马厩。}\n\n@event beast_hover_line\n# en: The Hover Line\ntitle [253240] = 悬停队列\n# en: {Flight is half the course. Brask lines you up on the lawn to hover a foot up and hold still while she counts.|The hover line. Brask counts slowly on purpose, you're sure.|Hover line. You and your broom have an understanding, of sorts.} {Somebody's broom keeps edging toward the lake.|An owl has landed on the end of a broom, and its rider is pretending not to notice.|Brask says, as always, that brooms are harder to reason with than griffons.}\ntext [2eec93] = {飞行占这门课的一半。布拉斯克让你们在草坪上排成一行，离地一尺悬停，她数数时不许动。|悬停队列。你确信布拉斯克是故意数得这么慢。|悬停队列。你和你的扫帚已经算是达成了某种共识。} {有人的扫帚一直往湖那边挪。|一只猫头鹰落在一把扫帚的尾端，骑手假装没注意到。|布拉斯克照例说，扫帚比狮鹫难讲道理多了。}\n# en: A line of students hovering a foot above a lawn on brooms, one broom drifting out of line, a stern professor counting slowly.\nscene [10dd11] = 一排学生骑着扫帚悬停在离草坪一尺高的地方，一把扫帚偏离了队列，一位严厉的教授正慢慢数数。\n# en: Hold still until the count\nopt.left.label [37c0d2] = 一动不动直到数完\n# en: {You hold it until forty. Your legs remember it for a day.|The broom bucks once at thirty-one. You hold on. Brask gets to fifty and nods.|Steady all the way. Brask says it's nice to see a broom that knows who's in charge, and she means the broom.}\nopt.left.say.1 [749e71] = {你撑到了四十。你的腿记了一整天。|数到三十一时扫帚猛地一颠。你抓住了。布拉斯克数到五十，点了点头。|全程稳稳当当。布拉斯克说很高兴看见一把知道谁说了算的扫帚——她指的是扫帚。}\n# en: Study how the others fall off\nopt.right.label [4c91b6] = 研究别人怎么摔下来\n# en: {You stand at the end and watch. You learn a great deal about balance, and nobody lets you forget you didn't fly.|You take notes on every wobble. Brask reads them and says you should consider a career in inquests.|You watch the owl on the broom-end win the hover line. Brask writes down the owl's name.}\nopt.right.say.1 [ae91bb] = {你站在队尾观察。你学到了很多关于平衡的东西，也没人让你忘记你没飞。|你把每一次摇晃都记了下来。布拉斯克读完说你该考虑去当验尸官。|你看着那只落在扫帚尾上的猫头鹰赢下了悬停。布拉斯克记下了猫头鹰的名字。}\n\n@event beast_feeding_round\n# en: Feeding Round\ntitle [d6808f] = 喂食巡回\n# en: {Feeding round, in pairs. You and {partner} have two buckets and a list, and the griffon chick is first on it.|Feeding round with {partner}. The griffon chick has heard the buckets.|You and {partner} could do the round blindfolded now. The chick would still sulk.} {Brask has written \"DO NOT HURRY\" at the top of the list.|The owls lean down from the rafters to watch.|{partner} is humming to the buckets.}\ntext [34e935] = {两人一组喂食。你和{partner}有两只桶和一张清单，狮鹫雏鸟排在第一个。|和{partner}一起喂食。狮鹫雏鸟已经听见了桶的声音。|你和{partner}现在蒙着眼都能走完一圈。雏鸟照样会闹脾气。} {布拉斯克在清单顶上写着“不许着急”。|猫头鹰从椽子上探下身来看。|{partner}在对着桶哼歌。}\n# en: Two students carrying buckets between stalls, a sulky griffon chick in a nest of straw, owls leaning down from the rafters.\nscene [28f709] = 两个学生提着桶在隔栏间穿行，一只闹脾气的狮鹫雏鸟窝在草堆里，猫头鹰从椽子上探下身来。\n# en: Take the griffon chick\nopt.left.label [2348a0] = 负责狮鹫雏鸟\n# en: {The chick eats from the bucket, then from your hand, then tries your sleeve. {partner} applauds quietly.|It sulks, then eats, then sulks about having eaten. Brask says that's progress for a griffon.|You and {partner} feed the chick in turns, humming. It sulks, but eats in rhythm.}\nopt.left.say.1 [1639b7] = {雏鸟先从桶里吃，再从你手里吃，然后尝了尝你的袖子。{partner}轻轻鼓掌。|它闹脾气，然后吃，然后为自己吃了而闹脾气。布拉斯克说对狮鹫来说这就是进步。|你和{partner}轮流喂雏鸟，一边哼歌。它还在闹脾气，却跟着节拍吃完了。}\n# en: {The chick bites the bucket, then the hand holding it. {partner} finishes the round alone, kindly.|It knocks the bucket over, and you with it, and sits in it, glaring. You spend the hour, bruised and soaked, fishing out its breakfast.|{partner}'s humming puts the chick in a mood. It takes the mood out on your hand.}\nopt.left.say.2 [7f9047] = {雏鸟咬了桶，接着咬了提桶的手。{partner}好心地独自喂完了一圈。|它把桶打翻，连你也一起撞倒，然后坐在桶里怒目而视。你浑身淤青、湿透，花了一小时把它的早饭捞出来。|{partner}的哼歌惹得雏鸟心情不好。它把气全撒在了你的手上。}\n# en: Take the owls instead\nopt.right.label [40efde] = 改去喂猫头鹰\n# en: {You learn the owls' names from the tags on their perches, and what a pellet is, in detail.|Up and down the ladder for an hour. {partner} survives the chick and is grateful in a quiet way.|The owls take their mice with great ceremony. One of them seems to be counting yours.}\nopt.right.say.1 [436aaa] = {你从栖木上的名牌认全了猫头鹰的名字，还详细了解了什么是食丸。|在梯子上爬上爬下一小时。{partner}熬过了雏鸟那一关，默默地心怀感激。|猫头鹰们隆重地接过各自的老鼠。其中一只似乎在数你手里的。}\n\n@event beast_hare_paddock\n# en: Running with Hares\ntitle [07bbef] = 与野兔同跑\n# en: {Beastcraft II teaches haste the old way. Run with the hares until their speed rubs off. \"Borrow it,\" Brask says. \"Don't steal it.\"|The hare paddock. The hares remember you.|Hares. You dream in sudden right angles now.} {The grass is wet and the hares don't care.|Brask times you with a crooked hourglass.|{season == \"summer\": It's hot and the hares are lazy.|The paddock smells of rain.}}\ntext [54e3b7] = {驭兽学 II 用老办法教疾速：跟着野兔跑，直到它们的速度沾到你身上。“借，”布拉斯克说，“别偷。”|野兔围场。野兔们记得你。|野兔。你现在连做梦都是突然的直角转弯。} {草是湿的，野兔不在乎。|布拉斯克用一只歪斜的沙漏给你计时。|{season == \"summer\": 天很热，野兔很懒。|围场里有雨的气味。}}\n# en: Students sprinting across a paddock alongside a scattering of brown hares, one student's feet suddenly much faster than the rest of them.\nscene [853b14] = 学生们和一群棕色野兔一起冲过围场，其中一个学生的脚突然比身体其他部分快了许多。\n# en: Run until it catches\nopt.left.label [e48d98] = 跑到速度沾上身为止\n# en: {For three strides you're faster than you've any right to be. Then you're in the hedge.|You catch it on the fourth lap, a lightness behind the knees. You pay for it with a nap you can't refuse.|The hares let you keep up for a moment, out of curiosity. It's the best moment of your week.}\nopt.left.say.1 [5f6f03] = {有三步你快得毫无道理。然后你一头扎进了树篱。|第四圈时你抓住了它，膝盖后面一阵轻盈。代价是一场你拒绝不了的午睡。|野兔们出于好奇，让你跟上了一会儿。这是你这周最好的时刻。}\n# en: Watch the hares and take notes\nopt.right.label [5a946d] = 观察野兔，记笔记\n# en: {You sketch the way a hare turns, everything at once, no warning. Brask says your sketch is better than your running.|You note how the hares rest between bursts. Brask reads it and says, \"Yes. That's the half everyone forgets.\"|You sketch the hares in sudden right angles. Brask pins your page up by the paddock gate.}\nopt.right.say.1 [42dee1] = {你画下野兔转身的样子，一切同时发生，毫无预兆。布拉斯克说你画得比跑得好。|你记下野兔在两次冲刺之间如何休息。布拉斯克读完说：“对。这是大家都忘掉的那一半。”|你画下野兔突然的直角转弯。布拉斯克把你那一页钉在了围场门边。}\n\n@event beast_complaints\n# en: The Complaints Hour\ntitle [225734] = 投诉时间\n# en: {Beast-speech practice. You sit on a bucket in the stables and listen. Every creature has been waiting to complain, and the owls start with the draught.|Another hour on the bucket. The owls open, as always, with the draught.|The bucket, the owls, the draught. Brask calls it fieldwork.} {The hippogriff wants different hay. It is very specific.|The griffon chick has a list.|{gpa >= 3: Brask leaves you alone with them, which feels like trust.|Brask stays near the door, which feels like supervision.}}\ntext [f1a417] = {兽语练习。你坐在马厩里一只倒扣的桶上倾听。每只生灵都早就等着要投诉，猫头鹰先说穿堂风。|还是那只桶，再坐一小时。猫头鹰照例先说穿堂风。|桶，猫头鹰，穿堂风。布拉斯克管这叫田野调查。} {鹰头马想换一种干草，要求非常具体。|狮鹫雏鸟列了一张清单。|{gpa >= 3: 布拉斯克让你单独和它们待着，像是一种信任。|布拉斯克一直守在门边，像是一种监督。}}\n# en: A student sitting on an upturned bucket in a stable, surrounded by animals who all seem to be talking at once.\nscene [1a419d] = 一个学生坐在马厩里一只倒扣的桶上，周围的动物似乎都在同时说话。\n# en: Hear every complaint out\nopt.left.label [770d17] = 把每条投诉听完\n# en: {The owls talk for forty minutes about the draught. You understand every word now, which is the trouble.|The hippogriff explains its views on hay at length. You come away fluent and exhausted.|A mouse you didn't know was there wants to be on the record. You put it on the record.}\nopt.left.say.1 [27f588] = {猫头鹰就穿堂风讲了四十分钟。你现在每个字都听得懂，麻烦就在这儿。|鹰头马详尽阐述了它对干草的看法。你出来时既流利又筋疲力尽。|一只你都不知道在场的老鼠要求记录在案。你把它记录在案了。}\n# en: Fix the draught yourself\nopt.right.label [79b85b] = 亲手把漏风堵上\n# en: {You climb into the rafters with a hammer and a plank. The owls supervise. The draught stops, and so do the complaints, briefly.|You patch the gap with straw and a board. The owls say thank you, then mention the window.|You fix the draught. The owls thank you, then move on to the window, the hay and the mouse, in order.}\nopt.right.say.1 [912b4b] = {你带着锤子和木板爬上椽子。猫头鹰在一旁监工。风停了，投诉也短暂地停了。|你用稻草和一块木板堵上了缝。猫头鹰道了谢，然后提起了窗户。|你修好了漏风处。猫头鹰道了谢，然后依次说起窗户、干草和那只老鼠。}\n\n@event beast_lake_flight\n# en: Over the Lake\ntitle [abc6c7] = 掠过湖面\n# en: {Advanced Beastcraft. A hippogriff each, over the lake, no saddle unless you ask. Brask won't think less of you for asking. She'll just remember.|The lake flight. Your hippogriff knows the way.|Over the lake. {final_year: You catch yourself memorising the view.|Your reflection keeps pace.}} {The water is perfectly still.|A barge below, its passengers waving.|{season == \"summer\": The air is full of midges.|The wind off the water stings.}}\ntext [2da61e] = {高等驭兽学。每人一头鹰头马，飞越湖面，除非你开口要，否则没有鞍。布拉斯克不会因为你要鞍而看轻你。她只会记住。|湖上飞行。你的鹰头马比你认路。|湖面上空。{final_year: 你发现自己在默默记住这片风景。|你的倒影一路跟着你。}} {湖水纹丝不动。|下方有一艘驳船，乘客们在挥手。|{season == \"summer\": 空中满是蠓虫。|湖面吹来的风刺痛脸颊。}}\n# en: A student on a hippogriff's back skimming low over a mirror-still lake, their reflection racing along beneath them.\nscene [20d2f8] = 一个学生骑在鹰头马背上低低掠过镜面般平静的湖，倒影在下方飞驰。\n# en: Ride bareback, low over the water\nopt.left.label [e0de3a] = 无鞍贴着水面飞\n# en: {You skim so low a wingtip draws a line across the lake. For one breath you and your reflection touch hands.|The hippogriff banks and you go with it, as if you'd always known how. Brask says nothing at all, then grins.|You fly low enough for your reflection to keep pace, wingtip to wingtip. You don't want to land.}\nopt.left.say.1 [5a8524] = {你飞得那么低，一侧翼尖在湖面划出一道线。有那么一口气的工夫，你和你的倒影碰了碰手。|鹰头马侧身转弯，你顺势而动，仿佛生来就会。布拉斯克一言不发，然后咧嘴笑了。|你飞得低到倒影能和你齐头并进，翼尖对着翼尖。你不想降落。}\n# en: {The hippogriff dips and you don't. The lake is colder than it looks. It always is.|You slide off on the turn and hang on by a fistful of feathers, all the way back to shore.|You race your reflection and your reflection wins. You surface laughing, and very cold.}\nopt.left.say.2 [d60787] = {鹰头马往下一沉，你没有跟上。湖水比看上去冷。向来如此。|你在转弯时滑了下去，攥着一把羽毛吊了一路，直到岸边。|你和自己的倒影赛跑，倒影赢了。你从水里冒出来，笑着，冷得要命。}\n# en: Take the saddle and the high circuit\nopt.right.label [6b0bf1] = 上鞍，走高处的航线\n# en: {Saddled, high and steady. You see all seven towers at once, and nobody sees you.|The safe circuit, twice. Brask remembers, as promised, and nods at you all the same.|You take the saddle and the high circuit, and look down at the lake as if memorising it. Brask lets you take a second lap.}\nopt.right.say.1 [92de41] = {上鞍，高飞，平稳。你一眼看尽七塔，却没人看见你。|安全航线，飞了两圈。布拉斯克果然记住了，但还是朝你点了点头。|你上了鞍，走高线，低头看着湖，像要把它背下来。布拉斯克让你再飞一圈。}\n\n@event beast_manticore_rounds\n# en: Manticore Rounds\ntitle [f18c88] = 蝎尾狮巡检\n# en: {Advanced students check the manticore once a month. Someone holds the tail; someone hums the lullaby. Brask lets you choose.|Manticore rounds. It knows the lullaby better than you do.|The manticore yawns at you, which is either a welcome or a threat.} {Its tail twitches in its sleep.|It has been off its food, Brask says, and is in a mood.|{final_year: You wonder who'll hold the tail after you.|Brask has brought a bucket of meat and a smaller one of patience.}}\ntext [c5506e] = {高年级学生每月检查一次蝎尾狮。一人握住尾巴，一人哼摇篮曲。布拉斯克让你自己选。|蝎尾狮巡检。那首摇篮曲它比你还熟。|蝎尾狮冲你打了个哈欠，不知是欢迎还是威胁。} {它的尾巴在睡梦中抽动。|布拉斯克说它最近不肯好好吃东西，正闹情绪。|{final_year: 你想知道你走后谁来握这条尾巴。|布拉斯克带来了一桶肉，和一小桶耐心。}}\n# en: A manticore lying in straw with its eyes half-closed, one student gently holding its scorpion tail while another hums nearby.\nscene [a03d57] = 一头蝎尾狮半眯着眼躺在稻草里，一个学生轻轻握着它的蝎尾，另一个在旁边哼歌。\n# en: Hold the tail\nopt.left.label [d27267] = 握住尾巴\n# en: {You hold the tail still while Brask checks the sting. It's warm and heavy and does not, at any point, stab you.|The manticore lets you hold its tail the way a cat lets you hold a paw. Under protest, briefly, as a favour.|You hold the tail while Brask feeds it from the bucket. It eats from her hand and watches you the whole time.}\nopt.left.say.1 [edb591] = {布拉斯克检查毒刺时，你稳稳握着尾巴。它温热、沉重，而且自始至终没有蜇你。|蝎尾狮让你握着它的尾巴，就像猫让你握它的爪子——不情不愿，片刻而已，算是赏脸。|布拉斯克从桶里喂它时，你握着尾巴。它从她手里吃，眼睛却一直盯着你。}\n# en: {The tail flicks. You let go just in time, mostly. Sister Amaranth asks no questions; she's seen this course before.|It rolls over, tail and all, onto you. Nothing breaks, but you're bruised in the exact shape of a manticore.|It's in a mood, and the tail knows. You let go a moment too late and spend an hour with Sister Amaranth.}\nopt.left.say.2 [d4a6ce] = {尾巴一甩。你及时松了手，大体上及时。阿玛兰斯修女什么也没问；这门课她见多了。|它连尾巴带身子翻了过来，压在你身上。没断什么，但你身上的淤青恰好是一头蝎尾狮的形状。|它心情不好，尾巴也知道。你松手晚了一刻，在阿玛兰斯修女那儿待了一个钟头。}\n# en: Hum the lullaby\nopt.right.label [978181] = 哼摇篮曲\n# en: {You hum the whole lullaby twice, off-key on the high bit. The manticore sleeps through its own check-up.|You hum. A classmate holds the tail. Brask says the humming is the harder job, and nobody believes her.|You hum while Brask brings the meat. The manticore eats, slowly, and for once the mood lifts.}\nopt.right.say.1 [58461b] = {你把整首摇篮曲哼了两遍，高音那段跑了调。蝎尾狮在自己的体检中一觉到底。|你哼歌，一个同学握尾巴。布拉斯克说哼歌才是更难的活儿，没人信她。|布拉斯克拿来肉时你在哼歌。蝎尾狮慢慢吃着，难得心情好了起来。}\n\n@event umbral_dim_lamps\n# en: Reading by Shadow\ntitle [1839c4] = 借影识物\n# en: {Umbral Theory I, top of the tower, lamps low on purpose. Name what's under each cloth by its shadow alone.|The cloths are back. The lamps are as low as ever.|Shadow reading. The dark no longer seems strange, which the professor says is the first lesson.} {One shadow is slightly larger than its object.|The professor moves the lamp an inch, and every answer changes.|{season == \"summer\": It's warm outside, never up here.|Rain on the windows makes the shadows shiver.}}\ntext [bfc068] = {暗影理论 I，塔顶，灯故意调得很暗。只凭影子说出每块布下面是什么。|布又盖回桌上了。灯和往常一样暗。|读影。黑暗不再显得陌生——教授说，这就是第一课。} {有一道影子比它的物体略大一点。|教授把灯挪了一寸，每个答案都变了。|{season == \"summer\": 外面很暖，这上面从来不暖。|窗上的雨让影子瑟瑟发抖。}}\n# en: A dim lecture room at the top of a tower, objects hidden under cloths on a table, their shadows thrown long on the wall behind.\nscene [3e8e65] = 塔顶一间昏暗的教室，桌上的物件盖着布，它们的影子长长地投在身后的墙上。\n# en: Read them patiently\nopt.left.label [21c0ff] = 耐心地读影子\n# en: {A teapot, a skull, a folded letter addressed to you. You open it later. It's blank.|You name every shadow but one. Under that cloth there is nothing at all. The professor doesn't explain.|Slowly, you get them all. The shadows told you, the professor says; you only had to stop interrupting.}\nopt.left.say.1 [962f6c] = {一把茶壶，一个头骨，一封写给你的折起来的信。你后来拆开，是空白的。|你认出了所有影子，只有一个例外。那块布下面什么也没有。教授没有解释。|慢慢地，你全认出来了。是影子告诉你的，教授说；你只是不再打断它们。}\n# en: Slip in a false shadow\nopt.right.label [131d22] = 混进一道假影子\n# en: {You add the shadow of a cat to the wall. Three classmates write \"cat\". The professor writes your name, and underlines it.|Your false shadow passes all morning. The professor only catches it when it doesn't move with the lamp.|Your false shadow shivers with the rain like the real ones. Nobody finds it. The professor shakes your hand, which is cold.}\nopt.right.say.1 [8fc52d] = {你在墙上添了一道猫的影子。三个同学写下“猫”。教授写下你的名字，还划了线。|你的假影子撑过了整个上午。直到它没跟着灯动，教授才发现。|你的假影子像真影子一样随雨发抖。没人发现它。教授和你握了手，那只手是冷的。}\n# en: {Your false shadow points the wrong way. The professor turns it round gently, like straightening a picture.|Your shadow-cat sits up and leaves. It wasn't meant to do that. The professor doesn't seem surprised.|The rain makes every shadow shiver except yours. The professor notices the one that keeps still.}\nopt.right.say.2 [b417db] = {你的假影子朝向反了。教授轻轻把它转了回来，像扶正一幅画。|你的影子猫坐起来，走了。它本不该这样。教授似乎并不意外。|雨让每道影子都在发抖，唯独你的没有。教授注意到了那道不动的影子。}\n\n@event umbral_chalk_ward\n# en: Pale Shells\ntitle [003c50] = 苍白的壳\n# en: {Ward drill, in pairs. One of you raises a pale shell over the other, and the professor lets the lamp-shadows loose along the floor to find the gap.|Pairs again. The professor's shadows have learned new tricks since last time.|The paired drill. You know how the shadows like to come: low, and from behind.} {Your partner's shell keeps flickering.|Your partner is a first-year who has never held a ward for more than a minute.|{gpa >= 3: The professor pairs you with the weakest student, on purpose.|The professor pairs you with someone as nervous as you are.}}\ntext [36de71] = {护界操练，两人一组。一人在另一人头上撑起一层苍白的壳，教授放出灯影沿着地面去找缝隙。|又是两人一组。教授的影子比上次多学了几招。|结对操练。你知道影子喜欢怎么来：贴着地，从背后。} {你搭档的壳一直在闪。|你的搭档是个一年级生，从没撑过一分钟以上的护界。|{gpa >= 3: 教授故意把你和最弱的学生分在一组。|教授把你和一个跟你一样紧张的人分在一组。}}\n# en: Students behind faint, pale bubbles of light as a professor flicks pieces of chalk at them from the front of a dim room.\nscene [710e30] = 学生们躲在一层层淡淡的苍白光罩后面，一位教授在昏暗的教室前方朝他们弹粉笔。\n# en: Hold the shell over both of you\nopt.left.label [bd8e9e] = 把护界撑在你们俩头上\n# en: {You stretch your shell over your partner as well. The shadows nose along its edge for an hour and find nothing. You feel every touch in your teeth.|Two people under one ward is twice the weight. Yours holds until the bell, just.|The shadows give up on your shell and go after the next pair. The professor nods at you, once.}\nopt.left.say.1 [6dfac8] = {你把壳也撑到搭档头上。影子沿着边缘嗅探了一整个钟头，什么也没找到。每一下触碰你都震在牙根里。|一个护界罩两个人，就是双倍的分量。你的撑到了下课，勉强。|影子放弃了你的壳，转去找下一组。教授朝你点了一下头。}\n# en: Coach your partner through theirs\nopt.right.label [a6ede4] = 指导搭档撑起自己的\n# en: {You talk your partner through it, breath by breath. Their shell holds for nine minutes, then a shadow slips under it and makes you both jump. \"Teaching,\" says the professor, \"is also a ward.\"|You keep your own shell down and watch for the gaps in theirs. By the end, they've stopped flickering. You've learned where shadows like to get in.|You coach your nervous partner, and you're nervous too. The shadows come low and from behind; you both turn in time.}\nopt.right.say.1 [d45323] = {你一口气一口气地带着搭档撑。他们的壳撑了九分钟，然后一道影子从底下钻了进来，把你们俩都吓了一跳。“教人，”教授说，“也是一种护界。”|你撤下自己的壳，盯着对方壳上的缝隙。到最后，他们的壳不再闪了。你也弄明白了影子喜欢从哪儿钻进来。|你带着紧张的搭档，自己也紧张。影子从低处、从背后来；你们俩都及时转过了身。}\n\n@event umbral_quiet_minutes\n# en: The Quiet Minutes\ntitle [089065] = 静默的十分钟\n# en: {Each lesson opens with ten minutes of silence. \"Listen to what's hidden,\" the professor says, and puts out every lamp but one.|The quiet minutes. You can hear the tower settle, stone by stone.|Ten minutes of silence. You look forward to it, and also not.} {Someone's stomach rumbles at the worst moment.|The candle leans toward the door, though nothing comes in.|{season == \"summer\": A bee is lost in the room somewhere.|The wind hums in the stairwell.}}\ntext [540b0c] = {每节课都以十分钟的静默开始。“去听隐藏之物。”教授说，然后熄灭了所有灯，只留一盏。|静默的十分钟。你能听见塔一块石头一块石头地沉降。|十分钟的静默。你有点期待它，又有点不。} {有人的肚子在最不合适的时候叫了。|烛火朝门口倾斜，尽管什么也没进来。|{season == \"summer\": 屋里不知哪儿有只迷路的蜜蜂。|风在楼梯井里低鸣。}}\n# en: A circle of students sitting perfectly still in near darkness, a single candle in the middle, everyone listening very hard.\nscene [5f3b47] = 一圈学生在近乎全黑中一动不动地坐着，中间只有一支蜡烛，每个人都在用力倾听。\n# en: Listen properly\nopt.left.label [e31a7f] = 认真倾听\n# en: {You hear the tower breathing, which it shouldn't, and a sigh on the stair that nobody makes.|You hear the lake, which is impossible from up here. The professor says that's normal for this room. It is not reassuring.|In the silence you hear someone you miss, very faintly. When the lamps come up, you don't mention it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [0ee9e7] = {你听见塔在呼吸——它不该呼吸——还有楼梯上一声没人发出的叹息。|你听见了湖，从这里本不可能听见。教授说这在这间屋里很正常。这话并不让人安心。|静默里，你隐约听见一个你思念的人。灯亮起来时，你没提这件事。}\n# en: Quietly finish other homework\nopt.right.label [29deb1] = 悄悄写别的作业\n# en: {You finish a whole essay by feel in the dark. The professor never notices, or never says.|Not a scratch of the quill. When the lamps come up, your essay is done and your face is innocent.|You write in the dark with the wind humming the rhythm. The essay is done when the lamps come up.}\nopt.right.say.1 [c2c5c4] = {你在黑暗里摸索着写完了一整篇论文。教授始终没发现，或者始终没说。|羽毛笔一点声音都没出。灯亮时，你的论文写完了，脸上一派无辜。|你在黑暗里写着，风在楼梯间哼着节拍。灯亮时，论文写完了。}\n# en: {When the lamps come up the professor is behind you, reading your essay. \"Your third paragraph is weak,\" she says.|Your quill squeaks. In that silence it sounds like a scream. Everyone turns.|A gust slams the stairwell door and you drop your quill. It rolls, loudly, to the professor's feet.}\nopt.right.say.2 [f43498] = {灯亮时，教授就站在你身后读你的论文。“你的第三段很弱。”她说。|你的羽毛笔吱了一声。在那片寂静里，听起来像一声尖叫。所有人都转过头来。|一阵风把楼梯间的门撞上了，你的羽毛笔掉了。它骨碌碌地、响亮地滚到了教授脚边。}\n\n@event umbral_short_distances\n# en: Short Distances\ntitle [35c701] = 短距离\n# en: {Umbral Theory II brings the shadowstep, desk to desk. \"The shadows in between are not friendly,\" the professor says, \"so don't linger.\"|Shadowstep drill. Three desks this time, then four.|The desks. You know the way between them, and the cold that lives there.} {Someone comes out under the wrong desk.|The lamps are lower than usual.|{gpa >= 3: The professor sets you the long way round.|The professor sets you the short way, and watches.}}\ntext [142afe] = {暗影理论 II 教影步，从一张桌子到另一张。“中间那些影子可不友善，”教授说，“所以别逗留。”|影步操练。这次三张桌子，然后四张。|这些桌子。你认得它们之间的路，也认得住在那里的寒意。} {有人从错误的桌子底下钻了出来。|灯比平时更暗。|{gpa >= 3: 教授让你绕远路。|教授让你走近路，并且盯着。}}\n# en: A student stepping into the shadow under one desk and halfway out of the shadow under another, the room's lamps burning low.\nscene [18d81b] = 一个学生踏进一张桌子下的影子，半个身子从另一张桌子下的影子里出来，屋里的灯低低燃着。\n# en: Step across\nopt.left.label [894fac] = 一步跨过去\n# en: {In, through, out. It takes no time and lasts forever. You come out under the far desk with frost on your sleeves.|Four desks, clean. Something in between brushes your ankle, curious, and lets you pass.|You take your set route and come out exactly where the professor pointed. The cold in between nods at you, almost.}\nopt.left.say.1 [83def2] = {进去，穿过，出来。不花一点时间，又像过了一辈子。你从最远那张桌子下出来，袖子上结了霜。|四张桌子，干净利落。中间有什么东西好奇地擦过你的脚踝，然后放你过去了。|你走指定的路线，正好从教授指的地方出来。中间的寒冷几乎朝你点了点头。}\n# en: {You come out one desk short and somewhat upside down. The cold stays in your fingers until lunch.|You linger, just for a moment. It's enough. You come back pale, and the professor sits you by the one warm lamp.|The professor's route is longer than you'd like. You come out at the end, but so does the cold, with you.}\nopt.left.say.2 [a8397a] = {你差一张桌子就出来了，而且有点头朝下。寒意在你指尖一直留到午饭。|你逗留了，只有一瞬。这就够了。你脸色苍白地回来，教授让你坐到唯一一盏暖灯旁。|教授给的路线比你想的长。你出来了，寒冷也跟着你一起出来了。}\n# en: Map the route on paper first\nopt.right.label [f66070] = 先在纸上画好路线\n# en: {You draw every shadow in the room, and where it goes at each hour. The professor keeps your map.|You work out the route in ink and walk it once, slowly, in the light. It's dull, and correct.|You map the set route desk by desk. The professor checks your map and adds one shadow you'd missed.}\nopt.right.say.1 [95dc62] = {你画下屋里每一道影子，以及它每个钟点会移到哪里。教授留下了你的图。|你用墨水推好路线，然后在光亮里慢慢走了一遍。枯燥，但正确。|你把指定路线一张桌子一张桌子地画了出来。教授检查后添上了一道你漏掉的影子。}\n\n@event umbral_hidden_object\n# en: Present but Unseen\ntitle [a63725] = 在场而不可见\n# en: {Hide a small key in this room, present but unseen. \"Hiding is not removal,\" the professor says. \"Removal is for amateurs.\"|The key, and somewhere to hide it. Your classmates are better at finding things.|The key exercise. Half the class pats their pockets out of habit.} {The key is brass today.|The key is silver and very cold.|Last time a key hid in a shadow for two days.}\ntext [c74599] = {把一把小钥匙藏在这间屋里，让它在场却不可见。“藏不是拿走，”教授说，“拿走是外行干的。”|钥匙，和一个藏它的地方。同学们找东西的本事见长。|钥匙练习。半个班都养成了拍口袋的习惯。} {今天的钥匙是黄铜的。|钥匙是银的，冰凉刺骨。|上次有把钥匙在影子里躲了两天。}\n# en: A dim classroom being searched by several students at once while one of them sits calmly at a desk with a small brass key in plain sight.\nscene [e384e8] = 一间昏暗的教室里，好几个学生同时在翻找，其中一个却平静地坐在桌旁，一把小铜钥匙就摆在眼前。\n# en: Hide it, and stay calm\nopt.left.label [7d28db] = 藏好它，保持镇定\n# en: {You leave the key on the desk in plain sight, in your own shadow. Nobody sees it. The professor smiles, which is rarer than a key.|You hide it in the dark behind your own ear. They search the room twice and never once look at you.|You hide the key in a shadow and leave it there for two days, like the last one. Nobody finds it. The professor does, and approves.}\nopt.left.say.1 [50e23d] = {你把钥匙明晃晃地放在桌上，就在你自己的影子里。没人看见。教授笑了，这比钥匙还难找。|你把它藏在自己耳后的暗处。他们把屋子搜了两遍，一次也没看你。|你像上一把那样，把钥匙藏进一道影子，放了两天。没人找到。教授找到了，表示赞许。}\n# en: {A first-rate hiding place, found in under a minute by someone who simply asked the shadow.|You hide it so well you can't find it yourself. The professor retrieves it from the wall with a sigh.|You hide it in a shadow, and the shadow moves with the afternoon. By the bell the key is in the corridor.}\nopt.left.say.2 [178ee3] = {一个一流的藏处，被人不到一分钟就找到了——对方只是问了问影子。|你藏得太好，连自己都找不到了。教授叹了口气，从墙里把它取了出来。|你把它藏进一道影子，可影子随着午后的光移动了。下课铃响时，钥匙已经到了走廊上。}\n# en: Search for everyone else's\nopt.right.label [69b186] = 去找别人藏的\n# en: {You find four keys, one in a hat, one in a hinge, one in a shadow that didn't want to let go.|You find one key and a pencil nobody has missed for years. The professor counts both.|You find the key that hid for two days. It's still in its shadow, and you have to ask it nicely to come out.}\nopt.right.say.1 [6d225e] = {你找到了四把钥匙：一把在帽子里，一把在合页里，一把在一道不肯松手的影子里。|你找到了一把钥匙，和一支多年没人想起的铅笔。教授两样都算数。|你找到了那把藏了两天的钥匙。它还在那道影子里，你得好言相劝它才肯出来。}\n\n@event umbral_stair_wards\n# en: The Old Stair Wards\ntitle [a4469b] = 古老的阶梯护界\n# en: {Advanced students renew the small wards on the tower's old stair. Some are newer than the stone. Some, the professor says, are much older.|The stair wards. You know which step hums.|The stair. {final_year: Someone else will kneel here next year.|Your knees know every step.}} {One ward is warm, as if someone's just left.|Something beyond a ward leans back when you touch it.|The stair lamps are mostly a suggestion.}\ntext [594d21] = {高年级学生负责加固塔里古老阶梯上的小护界。有些比石头新。有些，教授说，要古老得多。|阶梯护界。你知道哪一级会嗡鸣。|阶梯。{final_year: 明年会是别人跪在这里。|你的膝盖认得每一级台阶。}} {有一道护界是温的，像刚有人离开。|你一碰，护界另一侧有什么东西往后靠了靠。|阶梯上的灯基本只是个意思。}\n# en: A student kneeling on an ancient spiral stair, hands pressed to a faint ring of light set into the stone.\nscene [c60e93] = 一个学生跪在古老的螺旋阶梯上，双手按着嵌进石头里的一圈微光。\n# en: Renew them carefully\nopt.left.label [405067] = 仔细地加固\n# en: {Step by step, ring by ring. Each ward takes a little of you and hums.|You renew eleven and pause at the twelfth. Leave that one, the professor says, and doesn't say whose it is.|At the top you're light-headed and the wards are bright. Something on the other side has gone quiet.}\nopt.left.say.1 [84a4db] = {一级一级，一圈一圈。每道护界都取走你一点东西，然后嗡嗡作响。|你加固了十一道，在第十二道前停了下来。教授说那道别碰，却没说是谁的。|到了顶上你头晕目眩，护界都亮了。另一侧的什么已经安静下来。}\n# en: Study the oldest ward instead\nopt.right.label [6da660] = 转而研究最老的那道\n# en: {You trace the oldest ring with one finger. The pattern folds in on itself like a knot nobody was meant to untie, and you feel watched for noticing even that.|The old ward is cold, careful work, made by someone who was afraid. You can feel the fear still in the stone.|You study the oldest ward by your hands alone, the lamps being no help. It's older than the stair. You're sure of that much.}\nopt.right.say.1 [d84215] = {你用一根手指描着最古老的那一圈。那图案向内层层折叠，像一个本就不该被人解开的结；仅仅察觉到这一点，你就觉得被什么盯上了。|那道古老的护界是冰冷而小心的手艺，出自一个害怕的人。那份恐惧至今还留在石头里。|灯帮不上忙，你只凭双手研究那道最古老的护界。它比楼梯还老。这一点你很确定。}\n\n@event umbral_errand\n# en: The Shadow's Errand\ntitle [febd12] = 影子的差事\n# en: {Original work. Send your own shadow on an errand across the room, the professor says, and have it come back. \"It will want to wander. They all do.\"|Shadow errands. Yours picks its own route now.|Errand day. {final_year: Does your shadow know you're leaving soon?|Your shadow seems eager, which worries you.}} {Someone's shadow is still under a desk from last week.|The professor's own shadow is missing today.|The lamps are low and the shadows long.}\ntext [e2911a] = {原创作业。派你自己的影子到屋子另一头办件差事，教授说，然后让它回来。“它会想乱跑。它们都这样。”|影子差事。你的影子现在会自己挑路线了。|差事日。{final_year: 你的影子知道你快要离开了吗？|你的影子显得跃跃欲试，这让你担心。}} {有人的影子从上周起还待在一张桌子底下。|教授自己的影子今天不见了。|灯很暗，影子很长。}\n# en: A student standing in lamplight with no shadow at all, while across the room their shadow lifts a book from a shelf.\nscene [fe154c] = 一个学生站在灯光下，身上没有一丝影子，而屋子另一头，他的影子正从书架上取下一本书。\n# en: Send it, and trust it\nopt.left.label [80242c] = 派它去，信任它\n# en: {Your shadow crosses the room, lifts a book, and brings it back open at a page you needed. You didn't ask it to.|It comes back with a feather from the rafters, which wasn't the errand. The professor counts it as initiative.|Your shadow goes out long and comes back short, carrying a stub of candle. You don't know where it got it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [f2385c] = {你的影子穿过屋子，取下一本书，带回来时正翻在你需要的那一页。你并没有叫它这么做。|它带回一根椽子上的羽毛，这不是差事内容。教授把它算作主动性。|你的影子拖得长长地出去，短短地回来，还带着一截蜡烛头。你不知道它是从哪儿弄来的。}\n# en: {It comes back an hour late and a little the wrong shape. For a day it lags behind you on stairs, sulking, and you can't get warm.|It doesn't come back until dusk. When it does, it smells of the lake. The professor makes you sit with the lamp until your hands stop shaking with cold.|It stretches out under the low lamps and doesn't want to come back. When it does, it's longer than you for a day.}\nopt.left.say.2 [9d55ca] = {它晚了一小时才回来，形状还有点不对。接下来一整天，它在楼梯上总落在你后面，闹着别扭，而你怎么也暖和不起来。|它直到黄昏才回来。回来时带着一股湖水的气味。教授让你在灯旁坐着，直到你的手不再冷得发抖。|在低矮的灯光下它伸得长长的，不想回来。回来后，一整天它都比你还长。}\n# en: Keep it on a short leash\nopt.right.label [bfc9a2] = 把它拴短一点\n# en: {Your shadow goes as far as the window and no further. It looks out a long time before it comes back.|Two yards and home. Dull, safe and precise. The professor says precision is how shadow-workers get old.|You keep it close under the low lamps. It strains toward the long shadows and comes back to you, reluctantly.}\nopt.right.say.1 [306102] = {你的影子走到窗边就停下了。它朝外望了很久，才回来。|两码远就回家。枯燥、安全、精确。教授说，精确就是影术师能活到老的原因。|在低矮的灯光下，你把它拴得很近。它朝那些长影子使劲，最后还是不情不愿地回到了你身边。}\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/pools/courses_hidden.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/pools/courses_hidden.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event art_escapement_drill\n# en: The Ticking Hour\ntitle [0f189f] = 滴答一课\n# en: {Artifice begins with a heart, a fist of clockwork that must tick evenly before it is allowed to think.|Your clockwork heart is waiting where you left it, sulking at the wrong speed.|You can hear your own heart on the bench from the doorway now. Its tick is almost yours.} {The bench beside you ticks like hail.|One heart near the door is running backwards, proudly.|The lecturer listens to each bench with eyes closed.} {season == \"winter\": The brass is cold enough to bite.|The workshop smells of solder.}\ntext [d91972] = {奇械术从一颗心开始：一团发条，必须先走得均匀，才被允许思考。|你的发条心还在你留下它的地方，用错误的速度生着闷气。|如今你在门口就能听见自己工作台上那颗心。它的滴答声几乎就是你的。} {隔壁工作台滴答得像下冰雹。|门边有一颗心在倒着走，还挺得意。|讲师闭着眼，挨个工作台地听。} {season == \"winter\": 黄铜冷得咬手。|工坊里一股焊锡味。}\n# en: A cluttered workshop bench covered in tiny brass gears, files and loupes, a row of half-built clockwork hearts ticking out of time with one another.\nscene [2c43ab] = 杂乱的工坊长凳上堆满细小的黄铜齿轮、锉刀和放大镜，一排半成品的发条心各走各的节拍。\n# en: File every tooth by hand\nopt.left.label [93a8dc] = 一齿一齿手工锉\n# en: {Tooth by tooth, under a loupe, until your eyes water. The tick evens out by the bell.|You file all morning and burn one thumb on the solder. The heart ticks steady and slightly smug.|It's slow, fiddly work, and your hands learn it faster than your head does.}\nopt.left.say.1 [89f0bd] = {一齿接一齿，伏在放大镜下，直到眼睛发酸。下课铃响前，滴答声匀了。|你锉了一上午，还被焊锡烫了一下拇指。那颗心走得稳稳当当，还有点得意。|又慢又磨人的活儿，你的手比脑子学得快。}\n# en: Charm the spring to keep time\nopt.right.label [93d2cf] = 给发条施咒定时\n# en: {The charm settles into the spring like a cat into a basket. Your heart ticks like a metronome.|You whisper the rhythm into the coil and it keeps it. The lecturer opens one eye, then closes it again.|The charm takes your own pulse as its pattern. The lecturer listens with eyes closed and nods: your heart ticks in time with you.}\nopt.right.say.1 [1d3cfa] = {咒语钻进发条，像猫钻进篮子。你的心走得像节拍器一样准。|你把节奏低声说进发条圈里，它就守住了。讲师睁开一只眼，又闭上了。|咒语把你自己的脉搏当成了节拍。讲师闭着眼听了听，点点头：你的心和你走的是同一个拍子。}\n# en: {The spring takes the charm too literally and unwinds all at once, across the room.|Your heart ticks perfectly for a minute, then very fast, then not at all. Someone hands you a fire bucket just in case.|The charm copies your pulse a little too well, and when you get nervous, so does the heart. The lecturer listens, eyes closed, and winces.}\nopt.right.say.2 [a72bd2] = {发条把咒语理解得太字面，一口气全松开，飞过了整间屋子。|你的心完美地走了一分钟，然后飞快，然后彻底停了。有人递给你一只灭火桶，以防万一。|咒语把你的脉搏学得太像了，你一紧张，那颗心也跟着紧张。讲师闭着眼听了听，皱起了眉。}\n\n@event art_stirring_cauldron\n# en: The Cauldron That Stirs\ntitle [dc6732] = 自己搅拌的坩埚\n# en: {The class project is a cauldron that stirs itself. It does, mostly, in whatever direction it pleases.|The cauldron has been rebuilt again. It stirs with a new brass arm and the same old temper.|The cauldron recognises you, or behaves as if it does.} {Today it stirs clockwise, then argues.|Today it stirs so fast the porridge in it is climbing out.|Today the arm keeps tapping the rim like an impatient guest.} {final_year: You'll miss its moods, a little.|The fire buckets by the door are full, for once.}\ntext [9992ac] = {这门课的项目是一口会自己搅拌的坩埚。它确实会搅，大多数时候，朝它高兴的方向。|坩埚又被重装了一遍。新的黄铜搅臂，还是老脾气。|坩埚认得你，或者至少装得像认得。} {今天它先顺时针搅，然后开始闹别扭。|今天它搅得太快，锅里的粥都往外爬。|今天搅臂一个劲儿地敲锅沿，像个不耐烦的客人。} {final_year: 你会有点想念它的脾气。|门边的灭火桶难得都是满的。}\n# en: A copper cauldron on iron legs stirring itself with a long brass arm while students crowd round with notebooks and a wary distance.\nscene [9242ac] = 一口铁腿铜坩埚用一根长长的黄铜臂自己搅拌，学生们抱着笔记本围在一旁，小心地保持着距离。\n# en: Recut the runes on the arm\nopt.left.label [72bcaa] = 重刻搅臂上的符文\n# en: {You spend the morning with a graver and a magnifier, recutting the stirring runes one stroke at a time. Your fingers are nicked; the arm moves sweetly.|Fine work in brass, the whole lesson long. The arm stirs evenly, and you have solder burns in a neat little row.|The arm taps the rim the whole time you work, as if hurrying you. When the runes are recut it stops tapping and stirs, which may be thanks.}\nopt.left.say.1 [b5b22d] = {你拿着刻刀和放大镜过了一上午，一笔一画重刻搅拌符文。手指划了几道口子，搅臂动得顺滑了。|一整节课都在黄铜上做细活。搅臂搅得均匀了，你手上多了一排整整齐齐的焊锡烫痕。|你干活时，搅臂一直敲着锅沿，好像在催你。符文重刻好了，它不敲了，开始搅，这也许算是道谢。}\n# en: Test it on a live brew\nopt.right.label [4e638a] = 拿真药剂试一试\n# en: {The cauldron stirs your brew exactly as the recipe asks, and you take the credit for both.|The brew turns the right colour at the right minute. The cauldron seems to know it did well.|The arm stops tapping the moment your brew goes in, and stirs it perfectly. It seems to like you.}\nopt.right.say.1 [49a10c] = {坩埚完全按配方搅好了你的药剂，两份功劳你都领了。|药剂在对的那一分钟变成了对的颜色。坩埚似乎知道自己干得不错。|你的药剂一倒进去，搅臂就不敲了，搅得恰到好处。它好像挺喜欢你。}\n# en: {The arm speeds up, the brew climbs the walls, and the whole class steps back as one.|The cauldron stirs your brew into something with the texture of an argument. You scrub it out until the bell.|The arm taps the rim, impatient, then stirs your brew in a temper. You clean the ceiling until the bell.}\nopt.right.say.2 [8e8241] = {搅臂越转越快，药剂爬上锅壁，全班齐刷刷后退一步。|坩埚把你的药剂搅成了一种像吵架一样的质地。你一直刷锅刷到下课。|搅臂不耐烦地敲着锅沿，然后带着脾气搅起了你的药剂。你一直擦天花板擦到下课。}\n\n@event star_names_in_the_dark\n# en: Names in the Dark\ntitle [d9ffd3] = 黑暗中的名字\n# en: {Starcalling is taught by appointment, at night, and this morning's note gives you tonight's hour. It begins with names. \"You cannot call what you cannot name.\"|Another note at breakfast, another night appointment under the dome. The list of names is longer than it was.|Tonight's appointment under the dome again. You know where the draught comes in.} {The note warns that the great telescope has been swinging round on its own.|Meteors are expected tonight.|The note is written on the back of a star chart.} {season == \"winter\": Tonight your breath will freeze on the eyepiece.|Tonight the air will smell of cut grass from the lawns below.}\ntext [3b909c] = {唤星术只在夜里预约授课，今早的字条告诉了你今晚的钟点。它从名字开始。“叫不出名字的，就唤不下来。”|早饭时又来了一张字条，又是一次穹顶下的夜间预约。名单比上次更长了。|今晚又是穹顶下的预约。你知道冷风从哪儿钻进来。} {字条提醒说，大望远镜最近总是自己转方向。|今晚会有流星。|字条写在一张星图的背面。} {season == \"winter\": 今晚你的呼气会冻在目镜上。|今晚的夜风里会有下面草坪刚割过的青草味。}\n# en: A dark brass dome open to a sky full of stars, a student at the eyepiece of a great telescope while another recites from memory, breath steaming.\nscene [383740] = 黄铜穹顶在黑暗中向满天星斗敞开，一名学生凑在大望远镜的目镜前，另一名呼着白气凭记忆背诵。\n# en: Recite the names until perfect\nopt.left.label [5a0fad] = 把名字背到一字不差\n# en: {You say them over and over into the cold until they stop being words and start being places.|Name after name, in order and then backwards. By the end you could find them blindfold, and nearly have to.|You stumble on the same name four times. The fifth time it comes out right, and you feel it land somewhere.}\nopt.left.say.1 [eebfcf] = {你对着寒气一遍遍念，直到它们不再是词，而成了地方。|一个接一个，正着背，再倒着背。到最后你闭着眼也能找到它们，也差点真得闭着眼。|同一个名字你卡了四次。第五次终于念对，你感觉它落在了某个地方。}\n# en: Let the telescope choose a star\nopt.right.label [9ee4f3] = 让望远镜挑一颗星\n# en: {The telescope settles on a star you've never heard of. You find its name in the old charts before the lecturer can tell you.|Wherever it points, you follow, and the charts agree with it. That's a whole lesson learned the other way round.|You let the telescope choose and check it against the chart on the back of the note. It's right, and so are you.}\nopt.right.say.1 [f57d71] = {望远镜停在一颗你从没听说过的星上。讲师还没开口，你已经在旧星图里找到了它的名字。|它指哪儿你跟哪儿，星图也都对得上。这一课是倒着学会的。|你让望远镜挑，再拿字条背面的星图核对。它对了，你也对了。}\n# en: {The telescope picks a star and then changes its mind every ten minutes. You chase it round the dome until midnight.|You spend the night at the eyepiece and come down with a stiff neck and one name you aren't sure of.|The telescope chooses a star that isn't on the chart on the back of the note. You spend the night in the draught, trying to prove one of them wrong.}\nopt.right.say.2 [c4490f] = {望远镜挑了一颗星，然后每十分钟改一次主意。你绕着穹顶追它追到午夜。|你在目镜前熬了一夜，下来时脖子僵了，还有一个拿不准的名字。|望远镜挑了一颗字条背面的星图上没有的星。你在穿堂风里待了一整夜，想证明它们俩之中有一个错了。}\n\n@event star_thread_of_light\n# en: A Thread of Light\ntitle [af6199] = 一缕星光\n# en: {Tonight's lesson, says the morning note, is to call down a single thread of a named star's light, no thicker than a hair.|Tonight's lesson is the same as ever: one thread, one star, held as long as you can bear it.|You've called down threads before. The morning note says tonight the lecturer wants it held longer.} {The morning sky is clear, and the night promises to be.|Thin cloud is forecast to cross the star you've chosen.|At breakfast the others are already practising the hand positions.}\ntext [49defa] = {早上的字条说，今晚这一课要试着唤下一缕某颗有名之星的光，细不过一根头发。|今晚这一课永远一样：一缕光，一颗星，能撑多久撑多久。|你以前唤下过光缕。早上的字条说，今晚讲师要你撑得更久。} {早晨的天空晴朗，夜里看来也会如此。|预报说薄云会掠过你选中的那颗星。|早饭时其他人已经在练手势了。}\n# en: A student in a dark observatory with hands cupped upward, a single silver thread of starlight running down through the open dome into their palms.\nscene [db7c41] = 昏暗的观星台里，一名学生双手向上合拢，一缕银色星光穿过敞开的穹顶落进掌心。\n# en: Call the thread down yourself\nopt.left.label [3c139b] = 亲手把光唤下来\n# en: {The thread comes down cold and bright and pours into your palms. You hold it until your hands shake.|It takes three tries. On the third, the light drops into your hands like water from a great height.|A hair of light, the length of the whole tower. When it fades, your fingers glow for an hour.}\nopt.left.say.1 [63b5e3] = {光缕冰冷而明亮地落下，注满你的掌心。你一直撑到双手发抖。|试了三次。第三次，光像从极高处落下的水一样掉进你手里。|一根头发粗的光，有整座塔那么长。它消散后，你的指尖还亮了一个钟头。}\n# en: Keep the night log instead\nopt.right.label [314549] = 改去记夜间日志\n# en: {You write down every thread the others call, with the star, the minute and how long it held. It's dull, cold work, and you start to see the pattern.|You hold the lamp and the log while the others call. Watching from outside, you see exactly where their hands go wrong.|Tonight the others hold their threads longer, and you note to the second when each one breaks. The pattern is in the breaking.} {season == \"winter\": Your ink keeps freezing.|The lecturer reads your log twice.}\nopt.right.say.1 [71a3b8] = {你记下别人唤下的每一缕光：哪颗星、几点几分、撑了多久。又冷又枯燥，但你开始看出规律了。|别人唤光时你举灯记录。从旁边看，你清清楚楚看到他们的手错在哪儿。|今晚别人的光丝都撑得更久，你把每一缕断掉的时刻记到秒。规律就藏在断开的地方。} {season == \"winter\": 你的墨水一直结冰。|讲师把你的日志读了两遍。}\n# en: Hold your own starlight up to it\nopt.up.label [658616] = 举起自己的星光迎它\n# en: {You light your own starlight and the thread finds it, like calling to like. It holds twice as long.|Your starlight steadies the thread, and the lecturer has you show the others how.|The thread drops straight into the light you're already holding. For a moment the dome is bright as noon.}\nopt.up.say.1 [48b9e8] = {你点亮自己的星光，那缕光循着它而来，同类相召。它撑了两倍长。|你的星光稳住了光缕，讲师让你示范给其他人看。|光缕径直落进你手中已有的光里。那一刻，穹顶亮如正午。}\n\n@event hollow_sealed_boxes\n# en: How It Was Sealed\ntitle [e1eab1] = 它是怎么封上的\n# en: {Each lesson, the headmistress sets a sealed thing in front of you. \"Tell me how it was closed,\" she says. \"Not what is inside. How.\"|Another sealed thing on the table. Today it is {a letter with a wax seal|a small lacquered box|a jar with its lid charmed shut}.|The headmistress places today's sealed thing with a little smile. She has started choosing harder ones.} {The lamp hisses.|The books on the shelves breathe slowly in the dark.|She sits with her hands folded, unhurried.}\ntext [e8bcf8] = {每一课，院长都把一样封住的东西放到你面前。“告诉我它是怎么封上的，”她说，“不是里面有什么。是怎么封的。”|桌上又是一样封住的东西。今天是{一封盖着蜡印的信|一只小漆盒|一个盖子被咒语封死的罐子}。|院长带着一丝微笑放下今天的封物。她已经开始挑更难的了。} {油灯嘶嘶作响。|书架上的书在黑暗里缓缓呼吸。|她双手交叠坐着，不慌不忙。}\n# en: A windowless room lined with black-bound books, a single lamp on a table, a tall calm woman setting a small locked box in front of a student.\nscene [8b6e1a] = 一间没有窗户的房间，四壁摆满黑色封皮的书，桌上一盏孤灯，一位高挑沉静的女士把一只上锁的小盒子放在学生面前。\n# en: Study the seal for the hour\nopt.left.label [b8b4fc] = 用整节课研究封印\n# en: {You trace every line of it without touching. At the end you can name each step of the sealing, in order. She nods once.|It takes the whole hour and a headache, but you work it out backwards, layer by layer.|You get two layers right and one wrong. She tells you which, kindly, and you'll remember.}\nopt.left.say.1 [af3511] = {你不碰它，只把每一道纹路都描了一遍。最后你能按顺序说出封印的每一步。她点了一下头。|花了整整一节课和一场头痛，你一层一层倒推了出来。|你对了两层，错了一层。她温和地告诉你是哪一层，你会记住的。}\n# en: Open it without leaving a trace\nopt.right.label [ffc168] = 打开它，不留痕迹\n# en: {You open it and close it again, and nothing shows. She looks at it, then at you, and laughs, short and delighted.|Open, closed, not a mark. \"Good,\" she says. \"Now never do that to a door.\"|It's a harder one, and it opens anyway, and closes, and shows nothing. She unfolds her hands for the first time all lesson.}\nopt.right.say.1 [d151b6] = {你打开又合上，什么也看不出来。她看看它，又看看你，短促而愉快地笑了一声。|开了，合了，一丝痕迹也没有。“很好，”她说，“以后永远别对一扇门这么做。”|这个更难，可它还是开了，又合上了，什么也看不出来。她整节课第一次松开了交叠的双手。}\n# en: {The seal cracks with a tiny, final sound. The headmistress writes something in her book. She remembers every mistake, and now there is one more.|It opens, and it shows. \"Everyone breaks one,\" she says pleasantly. \"Rarely so loudly.\"|The harder seals are harder for a reason. It opens with a mark that won't go, and she lets you sit with it, unhurried.}\nopt.right.say.2 [d1b681] = {封印发出一声细小而决绝的脆响。院长在本子上写了点什么。她记得每一个错误，现在又多了一个。|开是开了，痕迹也露了。“每个人都会弄坏一个，”她和颜悦色地说，“很少有人弄得这么响。”|更难的封印难是有原因的。它开了，留下一道去不掉的痕迹，她不慌不忙，让你对着它坐着。}\n\n@event hollow_reading_the_blank\n# en: Reading the Blank Page\ntitle [1973a9] = 读空白的一页\n# en: {\"Most things that are hidden are not gone,\" the headmistress says, and gives you a blank page. \"Find what was written. Gently.\"|A new blank page today. It feels warmer than paper should.|You know the weight of hidden writing in a page by now, like a pocket with something in it.} {The books on the nearest shelf seem to lean closer.|The lamp burns low and blue.|Somewhere in the stacks, a book turns its own page.} {final_year: She asks where you'll go after graduation, and listens.|She is reading while you work, and doesn't look up.}\ntext [747881] = {“大多数被藏起来的东西并没有消失，”院长说着，递给你一张白纸，“找出写过的东西。轻一点。”|今天又是一张新的白纸。它比纸该有的温度要暖。|如今你能掂出一页纸里藏着字的分量，就像口袋里装着东西。} {最近那排书架上的书似乎凑近了些。|油灯烧得又低又蓝。|书库深处，有本书自己翻了一页。} {final_year: 她问你毕业后要去哪里，然后认真听着。|她在你干活时看书，头也不抬。}\n# en: A student bent over an apparently blank page by lamplight, faint lines of ink surfacing under their fingertips, black-bound books leaning in from the shelves.\nscene [6dbe72] = 一名学生借着灯光俯身看一张看似空白的纸，淡淡的墨迹在指尖下浮现，黑色封皮的书从书架上倾身过来。\n# en: Coax the ink up slowly\nopt.left.label [a0bd04] = 慢慢把墨迹引出来\n# en: {You warm the page with a thread of power, a breath at a time. The writing surfaces slowly, safe and dull. It's someone's laundry list, very carefully hidden.|Line by line, the ink comes up. It's an old timetable. The lesson was in the finding.|It takes most of your strength. The page gives up a single sentence about the weather, decades ago.}\nopt.left.say.1 [ea3c8e] = {你用一缕法力一口气一口气地温着纸页。字迹慢慢浮上来，安全又无聊：某人的洗衣清单，藏得非常仔细。|墨迹一行一行浮现，是一张旧课表。这一课的意义在于找到它。|这几乎耗尽了你的力气。纸页只吐出一句话，讲的是几十年前的天气。}\n# en: Read it bare-handed\nopt.right.label [de8da5] = 徒手直接读\n# en: {The ink comes up fast under your fingers, and so does a chill that stays in your hand until morning.|You read it quickly and well. Your fingertips ache for an hour, and the headmistress hands you a cup of tea without comment.|You find the writing the way you'd find a coin in a pocket, and read it bare-handed. Somewhere in the stacks a page turns, and your fingers are cold until supper.}\nopt.right.say.1 [592cda] = {墨迹在你指下飞快浮现，一股寒意也一起涌上来，在你手里一直待到早上。|你读得又快又好。指尖疼了一个钟头，院长一言不发地递给你一杯茶。|你像在口袋里摸到一枚硬币那样找到了字迹，空手读了出来。书架深处有一页书翻了过去，你的手指一直凉到晚饭。}\n# en: Let your ward do the reading\nopt.up.label [3adba1] = 让护界替你读\n# en: {Your ward settles over the page like a held breath, and the writing rises into it. The headmistress raises an eyebrow, pleased.|You read through the ward as if through glass. It costs you, but not your hands.|The hidden lines surface inside your shell, one by one, in no hurry.}\nopt.up.say.1 [50deed] = {你的护界像一口屏住的气一样罩在纸页上，字迹浮进其中。院长扬起眉毛，颇为满意。|你隔着护界读，像隔着玻璃。它耗你的力，但不伤你的手。|藏起的字句在你的护壳里一行行浮现，不慌不忙。}\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/pools/courses_upper.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/pools/courses_upper.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event evo_rain_barrel\n# en: Rain on Demand\ntitle [b62937] = 呼雨即来\n# en: {Tidecalling at Evocation II. Pull a cloud off the lake, park it over a barrel, and fill the barrel to the chalk line. Not over, not under.|The barrels are out on the terrace again, each with a chalk line.|You know your barrel now, the one with the iron band that's slipped.} {Professor Marisol is reading on the steps, under a perfectly dry patch of air.|The wind keeps trying to borrow the clouds.|{season == \"winter\": The clouds come off the lake as sleet.|A heron watches the barrels with professional interest.}}\ntext [21af36] = {塑能术 II 的唤潮课。从湖上拽一朵云，停在木桶上方，把桶灌到粉笔线为止。不许多，也不许少。|木桶又摆上了露台，每只都画着粉笔线。|如今你认得自己的桶了，就是铁箍滑下来的那只。} {玛丽索尔教授坐在台阶上看书，头顶那一小块空气干干爽爽。|风一直想把云借走。|{season == \"winter\": 云从湖上过来，落下的是冻雨。|一只苍鹭以专业的兴趣盯着这些木桶。}}\n# en: A row of students on a lakeside terrace, each with a small grey cloud above a wooden barrel, one cloud raining sideways onto a neighbour.\nscene [c50d40] = 湖畔露台上站着一排学生，每人头顶一小朵灰云，下方是一只木桶，其中一朵云正斜着把雨下到邻座身上。\n# en: Wring it out to the line\nopt.left.label [0e4325] = 拧到粉笔线\n# en: {The last drop lands on the chalk. Marisol looks up from her book and back down, which counts.|You stop the rain a breath early and let the drip finish the job. Exactly to the line.|You know how much your barrel leaks at the slipped band, and you rain that much extra. Exactly to the line.}\nopt.left.say.1 [fe435c] = {最后一滴正好落在粉笔线上。玛丽索尔从书上抬眼看了看，又低下头去，这就算数了。|你提前一口气收住雨，让余滴把活干完。不多不少，正到线上。|你知道你那只桶在松脱的铁箍处漏多少，就多下那么多。不多不少，正到线上。}\n# en: {Your cloud overshoots, fills the barrel, and keeps going down your collar.|The cloud drifts left and rains on the next barrel, which is not yours, and on its owner, who is very not yours. The splashback finds your collar anyway.|The heron moves at the wrong moment and so does your cloud. It empties itself over the heron, who leaves offended.}\nopt.left.say.2 [e794c2] = {你的云下过了头，灌满木桶后接着往你衣领里灌。|云往左飘，下在了旁边的桶上，那不是你的桶，也下在了桶的主人身上，那更不是你的人。溅起的水还是找到了你的衣领。|苍鹭在不该动的时候动了，你的云也是。它把雨全倒在了苍鹭身上，苍鹭气呼呼地走了。}\n# en: Drizzle it in, slowly\nopt.right.label [b367cf] = 慢慢地洒进去\n# en: {It takes the whole lesson. Your cloud is the size of a pillow by the end, and very tired.|You fill it a thimble at a time. The others have gone in for lunch. The line is met.|You drizzle it in while your barrel leaks it out at the band. It's a long lesson, but you win in the end.}\nopt.right.say.1 [9eee07] = {这花了整整一节课。到最后你的云只剩枕头那么大，而且累坏了。|你一顶针一顶针地往里灌。别人都去吃午饭了。线终于到了。|你一点一点往里下，桶又从铁箍那儿一点一点往外漏。这节课很漫长，但最后你赢了。}\n\n@event evo_ember_stair\n# en: Carrying the Ember\ntitle [5d68d8] = 掌中余火\n# en: {Pyromancy at Evocation II. Carry a live ember in your bare palm from the ground floor to the top of the Lecture Tower. Keep it alight. Keep your hand.|The ember drill again. The stair hasn't got any shorter.|Ember day. You've counted the stair by now, and you know where the draught is.} {A door on the third floor opens just as you pass.|Someone ahead of you is climbing very fast and swearing quietly.|The lamps on the stair are out, and your ember is the only light.}\ntext [633df9] = {塑能术 II 的火焰术课。赤手托一块燃着的炭，从讲堂塔底层走到塔顶。别让它灭，也别丢了你的手。|又是托炭练习。楼梯并没有变短。|托炭日。你早数清了台阶，也知道穿堂风在哪儿。} {你经过时，三楼的一扇门恰好打开。|你前面有人爬得飞快，嘴里小声骂着。|楼梯上的灯全灭了，你掌心的炭是唯一的光。}\n# en: A student climbing a dim spiral stair, a single glowing ember resting in their bare open palm.\nscene [0a00df] = 一名学生在昏暗的螺旋楼梯上攀登，一块发光的余炭躺在摊开的赤裸掌心里。\n# en: Feed it, and climb fast\nopt.left.label [76bdf4] = 喂着火，快步上\n# en: {You keep the ember bright with small breaths of heat and take the stairs two at a time. It arrives fat and orange.|You reach the top with the ember glowing and your palm only warm. The lecturer tips it into a brazier without a word.|Your ember lights the dark stair all the way up. People wait on the landings to follow you.}\nopt.left.say.1 [ca1e10] = {你用细细的热息让炭保持明亮，一步跨两级台阶。到顶时它又胖又红。|你到达塔顶，炭还亮着，掌心只是温热。讲师一言不发地把它倒进火盆。|你的炭一路照亮了漆黑的楼梯。人们在平台上等着跟你走。}\n# en: {The ember flares on the landing and you learn exactly how hot a palm can get. You arrive with it alight, and with a blister.|You feed it too much at the draughty window. It bites. You carry it up the last flight in your other hand.|You feed it in the dark and misjudge it. It bites, and you finish the climb by its light and your own swearing.}\nopt.left.say.2 [2166ef] = {炭在楼梯平台上猛地一窜，你终于知道掌心能有多烫。到顶时它还燃着，你也多了个水泡。|在透风的窗边你喂得太多，它咬了你一口。最后一段楼梯，你换了只手托着它。|黑暗里你喂错了量。它咬了你一口，你借着它的光，骂骂咧咧地爬完了剩下的路。}\n# en: Shield it, and climb slow\nopt.right.label [d3a230] = 护着火，慢慢上\n# en: {You climb at a crawl with the ember cupped against the draughts. It is alight at the top, just. So are your nerves.|Step by step, a held breath at each window. Last up, but the ember's still glowing.|You shield it from the draught you know is coming. It reaches the top small and steady, and so do you.}\nopt.right.say.1 [216201] = {你一步一挪地往上爬，双手拢着炭挡风。到顶时它还亮着，勉强亮着。你的神经也是。|一步一步，每过一扇窗就屏一口气。你最后一个到，但炭还亮着。|你提前挡住了你知道会来的那股风。它到顶时小小的、稳稳的，你也是。}\n\n@event evo_firebreak\n# en: The Reed Burn\ntitle [b50131] = 烧芦苇\n# en: {Advanced Evocation does real work, the controlled burn of the dead reeds along the lake shore. Pyromancers lead the fire, tidecallers hold the line behind it.|Burn day. The shore smells of smoke for a week afterwards.|The reed burn. You can tell a fire's mood from its smoke now.} {The wind is steady from the lake.|The wind can't decide.|The reeds are drier than anyone would like.} {season == \"summer\": The air is hot already.|Your breath steams.}\ntext [9e6bd2] = {高等塑能术要干真活，湖岸那片枯芦苇的控制烧荒。火焰术的人领着火走，唤潮术的人在后面守线。|烧荒日。之后整整一周，湖岸都是烟味。|烧芦苇。如今你看烟就知道火的脾气。} {风从湖上稳稳地吹来。|风拿不定主意。|芦苇干得让谁都不放心。} {season == \"summer\": 空气已经很热了。|你呼出的气都成了白雾。}\n# en: Advanced students along a lake shore in a line, half of them calling a low wall of fire through dry reeds, half of them holding back a curtain of lake water behind it.\nscene [c4e483] = 高年级学生沿湖岸站成一排，一半人引着一道低矮的火墙穿过枯芦苇，另一半人在火墙后撑起一道湖水的帘幕。\n# en: Walk at the fire's head\nopt.left.label [13e32e] = 走在火头\n# en: {The fire follows your hands down the shore like a dog on a lead. The lecturer lets you take it all the way to the boathouse.|You lead the fire low and even, and it goes out where you tell it. Behind you there's nothing but clean black ground.|You lead the fire through the dry reeds slowly and keep it on a short lead. Not a spark gets away.}\nopt.left.say.1 [807c0c] = {火像牵着绳的狗，跟着你的手沿岸走。讲师让你一路把它带到船屋。|你把火领得又低又匀，它在你说停的地方熄灭。身后只剩干干净净的黑土。|你领着火慢慢穿过干芦苇，绳子收得很短。一颗火星都没逃掉。}\n# en: {A gust lifts the fire over your head. The tidecallers drop it in a wall of lake water, and you with it.|The fire jumps the line and runs. It's caught in a minute, but your eyebrows weren't.|The dry reeds go up faster than you can lead them. The tidecallers save the boathouse; you save some of your sleeve.}\nopt.left.say.2 [1c028e] = {一阵风把火掀过你头顶。唤潮术的人一道湖水墙把它压了下去，连你一起。|火越过了线，跑了起来。一分钟就被逮住了，你的眉毛却没逃掉。|干芦苇烧得比你领得还快。唤潮术的人保住了船屋，你保住了一部分袖子。}\n# en: Hold the water line\nopt.right.label [efca5e] = 守住水线\n# en: {You stand in the shallows holding a curtain of lake water for an hour. Nothing gets past you. Nobody notices, which is the point.|Your arms ache from keeping the water up. It's the least glamorous job on the shore and the one the lecturer checks first.|You hold the water line through the worst of it. When the dry reeds flare, your curtain is the one that holds.}\nopt.right.say.1 [20cdcb] = {你站在浅水里撑着一道湖水帘幕，撑了一个钟头。什么都没漏过去。没人注意到，这正是要点。|手臂撑水撑得发酸。这是岸上最不光彩的差事，却是讲师头一个检查的。|你在最凶险的时候撑住了水线。干芦苇蹿起火来时，撑住的正是你那道水帘。}\n\n@event evo_drowned_flame\n# en: Fire Under Water\ntitle [29fc18] = 水下之火\n# en: {Original work in Advanced Evocation, a fire that burns underwater. Every student gets a tank. Most tanks get a new pane every week.|Your tank is waiting, with a fresh crack along one pane.|The tank again. You talk to the water now, and to the fire, separately.} {Someone's tank has boiled dry and they're staring at it.|A flame at the next bench burns green beneath the water, quite calm.|The lecturer is standing well back today.}\ntext [b9657b] = {高等塑能术的原创作业：一团在水下燃烧的火。每人一只水箱。大多数水箱每周都要换块新玻璃。|你的水箱在等你，一块玻璃上新添了一道裂纹。|又是水箱。如今你会分别跟水和火说话。} {有人的水箱烧干了，他们正盯着它发呆。|邻桌一团火在水底燃着绿光，十分平静。|讲师今天站得远远的。}\n# en: A glass tank of water on a scorched bench, a small blue flame burning steadily at the bottom of it.\nscene [c9026b] = 一张烧焦的长桌上放着一只玻璃水箱，一小团蓝色火焰在箱底稳稳燃烧。\n# en: Light it at the bottom\nopt.left.label [a339ad] = 在箱底点燃它\n# en: {A small blue flame takes hold under the water and stays. The bubbles go up in a thin silver line.|It catches, gutters, and settles. The water around it doesn't even warm. You don't know how you did it.|You light it while the lecturer stands well back. It burns, small and stubborn, and the lecturer comes a step closer.}\nopt.left.say.1 [1987da] = {一小团蓝火在水下点着了，并且留了下来。气泡排成一道细细的银线往上冒。|它点着了，摇晃了一下，安顿下来。周围的水甚至没变暖。你也不知道自己是怎么做到的。|你点火时讲师站得远远的。它烧着了，小小的，很倔，讲师往前走了一步。}\n# en: {The water boils in a heartbeat and the pane splits. You wear the steam on your face for the rest of the day.|The flame goes up instead of in. The tank cracks, and your sleeve is wet and scorched at once.|You learn why the lecturer is standing back. The pane goes, and so does your eyebrow.}\nopt.left.say.2 [b4a824] = {水一瞬间就沸了，玻璃裂开。一整天你脸上都带着那股蒸汽。|火没往里钻，反往上蹿。水箱裂了，你的袖子同时又湿又焦。|你明白了讲师为什么站得那么远。玻璃碎了，你的一边眉毛也没了。}\n# en: Wrap it in a bubble first\nopt.right.label [69bcca] = 先裹一个气泡\n# en: {You hold a skin of air around the flame and sink them both. It's half a cheat, and the lecturer says so. It burns all hour.|Your flame sits in a bubble at the bottom like a moth in a jar. Not the assignment, but not nothing.|You wrap it in a bubble, as you always do now. The lecturer says it's time to try without, and doesn't make you.}\nopt.right.say.1 [495623] = {你用一层空气包住火，把它们一起沉下去。这算半个作弊，讲师也这么说。但它烧了一整节课。|你的火待在箱底的气泡里，像罐子里的飞蛾。不是作业要求的，但也不是什么都没有。|你像现在每次那样，给它裹上气泡。讲师说是时候试试不用了，但没有逼你。}\n\n@event astro_sealed_forecast\n# en: Tomorrow's Weather\ntitle [fdb53c] = 明日天气\n# en: {Foresight at Astromancy II. Write down tomorrow's weather to the hour, seal it, pin it to the board. The lecturer opens the envelopes a day later, in public.|The envelopes come out again.|You've written so many forecasts you dream in drizzle.} {Outside the dome the sky is giving nothing away.|Last week's best forecast just said \"Wet\", and was right.|{season == \"winter\": Frost on the dome, and snow somewhere in the future.|Clouds over the lake, moving with intent.}}\ntext [7b1548] = {星象学 II 的预见课。写下明天每个时辰的天气，封好，钉到板上。讲师第二天会当众拆信。|信封又拿出来了。|你写过太多预报，连做梦都在下毛毛雨。} {穹顶外的天空什么都不肯透露。|上周最好的预报只写了一个“湿”字，而且对了。|{season == \"winter\": 穹顶上结了霜，未来某处有雪。|湖上的云正带着目的移动。}}\n# en: A row of sealed envelopes pinned to a corkboard in the observatory, each labelled with a student's name and tomorrow's date.\nscene [63cf64] = 观星台的软木板上钉着一排封好的信封，每只都写着学生的名字和明天的日期。\n# en: Commit to the hour\nopt.left.label [1bcf44] = 精确到时辰\n# en: {\"Rain at the third bell, stopping at the fourth.\" It does. The lecturer reads it out twice.|You forecast a fog to the hour. When the envelope is opened, the fog is pressing on the dome.|You write \"Sleet at the second bell, from the lake.\" It comes off the lake at the second bell. The lecturer stops reading the others.}\nopt.left.say.1 [39d2c0] = {“三更钟时下雨，四更钟停。”果然如此。讲师念了两遍。|你预报了一场准点的雾。信封拆开时，雾正贴在穹顶上。|你写下“二更钟，湖上来雨夹雪。”二更钟时，雨夹雪果然从湖上来了。讲师不再念别人的了。}\n# en: {\"Clear by noon.\" It rains till evening. Your envelope is read out to great enjoyment.|You predicted wind. Tomorrow turns out so still that a feather on the sill doesn't move.|You forecast with great confidence. Tomorrow is the opposite of everything you wrote, in order.}\nopt.left.say.2 [fda6b5] = {“午前转晴。”雨一直下到傍晚。你的信封被念出来，大家听得很开心。|你预报有风。结果第二天静得连窗台上的羽毛都没动一下。|你信心满满地写下预报。第二天的天气和你写的每一条都相反，而且顺序一致。}\n# en: Hedge, and learn from it\nopt.right.label [53eefc] = 留有余地，边看边学\n# en: {\"Some weather, at some point.\" The lecturer reads it out flatly and moves on. But you spent the hour watching how the good forecasters work.|You write a careful forecast with three possibilities. It's right, technically. The lecturer calls it \"an umbrella of a prophecy\".|You hedge, and read the others' envelopes after. You can tell the good ones by how short they are.}\nopt.right.say.1 [5376c0] = {“某个时候会有某种天气。”讲师面无表情地念完，继续下一个。但这一节课你都在看高手们怎么做。|你写了一份周全的预报，列了三种可能。严格说它是对的。讲师称之为“一把伞似的预言”。|你写得模棱两可，之后读了别人的信封。好的预报一看就知道，因为它们都很短。}\n\n@event astro_stubborn_telescope\n# en: The Stubborn Telescope\ntitle [2959bb] = 倔强的望远镜\n# en: {Astromancy II needs exact positions, and that means a night on the great telescope. At the morning lecture your name goes up on tonight's rota. The great telescope, everyone warns you, points wherever it likes.|Your name is on tonight's telescope rota again. The lecturer warns you it is already pointing at something else.|Your night on the telescope again. The lecturer only raises an eyebrow at you as the rota goes up.} {This week it wants the moon.|This week it's fixed on the lake, which is not in the sky.|This week it keeps drifting east, slowly, like a sigh.}\ntext [278ba4] = {星象学 II 需要精确的星位，这就得在大望远镜前熬一夜。早课上，你的名字被排进了今晚的轮值表。大家都提醒你：大望远镜爱指哪儿就指哪儿。|今晚的望远镜轮值表上又有你。讲师提醒你，它已经指着别处了。|又轮到你守望远镜。轮值表贴出来时，讲师只是冲你挑了挑眉。} {这周它想看月亮。|这周它盯着湖面，可湖不在天上。|这周它一直慢慢往东偏，像一声叹息。}\n# en: A student tugging at a huge brass telescope in a dome, while it points stubbornly at a different patch of sky.\nscene [6f250f] = 穹顶下，一名学生拽着一架巨大的黄铜望远镜，它却固执地指着另一片天空。\n# en: Argue it round\nopt.left.label [dba882] = 把它劝回来\n# en: {You coax it, inch by inch, and it settles on your star with a creak like a shrug.|You find the right thing to say to an old brass instrument. It points where you ask. You write the position fast.|You coax it off its eastward drift with a gentle hand and a sharp word. It sighs back to your star.}\nopt.left.say.1 [1f875f] = {你一寸一寸地哄它，它终于停在你的星上，嘎吱一声，像是耸了耸肩。|你找到了对一件老黄铜仪器该说的话。它指向你要的地方。你飞快地记下星位。|你温柔地推、严厉地说，把它从往东漂的劲头里哄了回来。它叹着气回到了你的星上。}\n# en: {It spends the night pointing where it likes. So do you, in the end, cold and annoyed.|You lean on it. It leans back. You go to bed at the fourth bell with nothing written down.|It drifts east all night, slowly, and takes you with it. You write down a very good position for the wrong star.}\nopt.left.say.2 [47a320] = {它整晚都指着它想指的地方。最后你也只好陪着，又冷又恼。|你靠在它身上使劲，它也靠回来。四更钟你上床时，本子上一个字都没有。|它整晚都在慢慢往东漂，把你也带走了。你为一颗错的星记下了一个非常精确的位置。}\n# en: Work it out from tables\nopt.right.label [0fbd24] = 用星表推算\n# en: {You leave the telescope to its moods and work the positions out by candlelight from the old tables. It takes until dawn.|Pages of sums by candle. The telescope watches over your shoulder, pointedly.|You use the tables and let the telescope drift where it likes. Your positions are good, and you're in bed by midnight.}\nopt.right.say.1 [53ea77] = {你随望远镜闹脾气去，借着烛光用旧星表把星位算出来。一直算到天亮。|一页又一页的烛下演算。望远镜在你肩后盯着，意味深长。|你用星表，任望远镜漂去它想去的地方。星位算得不错，午夜前你就上床了。}\n\n@event astro_long_watch\n# en: Keeping the Light\ntitle [72610b] = 守住星光\n# en: {Advanced Starlight. At the morning lecture you're given tonight's task. Hold one star's light in a lantern through a whole night on the roof. It will want to go home before dawn. Your job is to keep it.|The night watch on the roof comes round again. After the morning lecture you collect a lantern and a blanket.|Your lantern, your star, your corner of the roof, booked for tonight.} {The star you've drawn is pale and blue.|The star you've drawn is small and stubborn.|The star you've drawn is very old and very faint.} {season == \"winter\": There will be frost on the slates.|The night will be warm and full of moths.}\ntext [903ab3] = {高等星光课。早课上，你领到了今晚的任务。把一颗星的光装进灯笼，在屋顶守上一整夜。天亮前它会想回家，你的任务是留住它。|又轮到屋顶守夜了。早课后，你领了一盏灯笼和一条毯子。|你的灯笼，你的星，你在屋顶的那个角落，今晚都订好了。} {你抽到的星又淡又蓝。|你抽到的星又小又倔。|你抽到的星非常古老，非常微弱。} {season == \"winter\": 石板瓦上会结霜。|今夜会很温暖，满是飞蛾。}\n# en: A student on the observatory roof before dawn, a glass lantern in their lap holding a single cold point of starlight.\nscene [efcd22] = 天亮前的观星台屋顶上，一名学生膝上放着一盏玻璃灯笼，里面装着一点冷冷的星光。\n# en: Keep it all night\nopt.left.label [828910] = 守它一整夜\n# en: {At dawn your star is still in the lantern, blinking at the sun it was never meant to meet. You are blinking too.|You keep it till the lecturer comes up at sunrise. Your eyes are full of sand and your lantern is full of light.|Your old faint star almost goes out at the fourth bell. You talk to it until dawn, and it stays.}\nopt.left.say.1 [8f3c73] = {天亮时你的星还在灯笼里，对着它本不该遇见的太阳眨眼。你也在眨眼。|你一直守到讲师日出时上来。你眼里全是沙，灯笼里全是光。|你那颗古老而微弱的星在四更钟几乎熄灭。你跟它说话说到天亮，它留下了。}\n# en: Let it go at midnight\nopt.right.label [d7678c] = 午夜放它走\n# en: {You open the lantern and the light goes up like a kite let loose. You watch it all the way home and then go to bed.|You let it go early. The lecturer's note just says \"Released at midnight.\" You suspect it's not a compliment.|You let the old star go at midnight. It goes slowly, as if it isn't sure of the way. You watch it until you can't.}\nopt.right.say.1 [c4bdd3] = {你打开灯笼，光像脱了线的风筝一样升上去。你一路目送它回家，然后去睡觉。|你早早放了它。讲师的记录上只写着“午夜放归”。你怀疑这不是夸奖。|你在午夜放走了那颗老星。它走得很慢，好像不太认得路。你一直看到看不见为止。}\n# en: Feed it your own starlight\nopt.up.label [07c1bd] = 喂它你的星光\n# en: {You pour a little of your own starlight into the lantern. The star brightens as if it had been lonely.|Your light and the star's together are enough to read by. You read until dawn.|You give the old star some of your light. It burns brighter than it has in a long time, and you feel it go out of you.}\nopt.up.say.1 [89444f] = {你往灯笼里倒了一点自己的星光。那颗星亮了起来，好像它一直很寂寞。|你的光和星的光合在一起，足够看书了。你一直读到天亮。|你把自己的光分了一些给那颗老星。它烧得比很久以来都亮，你感觉到那份光从你身上流走了。}\n\n@event astro_two_futures\n# en: Both Futures\ntitle [c2e1aa] = 两种未来\n# en: {Advanced Foresight. The lecturer balances a coin on its edge. Read both futures at once, the one where it falls heads and the one where it falls tails, and hold them both.|The coin on its edge again. You know how it feels to be two places at once now.|The coin. People come out of this lesson pressing their temples.} {Someone in the front row says \"heads\" in two voices.|The lecturer is drinking tea and looking at nobody.|The candles burn straight up, very still.}\ntext [8cb25c] = {高等预见课。讲师把一枚硬币立在边缘上。同时读出两种未来，正面落地的那个和反面落地的那个，并把两者都握住。|又是立着的硬币。如今你知道同时身处两地是什么感觉了。|硬币。走出这节课的人都按着太阳穴。} {前排有人用两个声音说“正面”。|讲师在喝茶，谁也不看。|烛火笔直向上，一动不动。}\n# en: A coin balanced on edge on a dark table in the observatory, with two faint ghostly outcomes hovering above it.\nscene [137628] = 观星台一张深色桌上，一枚硬币立在边缘，上方悬浮着两个淡淡的、幽灵似的结果。\n# en: Hold both to the end\nopt.left.label [acd6d2] = 两者握到最后\n# en: {For a long moment you live both futures, side by side, and neither is frightening. Then the coin falls, and one of them goes out.|You hold both until the lecturer flicks the coin. One future folds away, neat as a letter.|You hold both in the still candlelight until the lecturer stops the coin with a finger. Both futures are still there. \"Interesting,\" the lecturer says.}\nopt.left.say.1 [7dbfc6] = {有那么长长的一刻，你并肩活在两种未来里，哪一种都不可怕。然后硬币倒下，其中一种熄灭了。|你一直握着两者，直到讲师弹倒硬币。一种未来折了起来，整齐得像一封信。|你在一动不动的烛光里握住两者，直到讲师用一根手指按住硬币。两种未来都还在。“有意思，”讲师说。}\n# en: {The futures tangle and you get a headache that lasts till supper. For an hour you keep reaching for the wrong door handle.|You lose track of which future is which, and of which way is down. The lecturer sits you by an open window.|You hold both too long. For the rest of the day you hear things a moment before they happen, and you hate it.}\nopt.left.say.2 [91f883] = {两种未来缠在一起，你头疼到晚饭。之后一个钟头你总是去拧错的门把手。|你分不清哪个未来是哪个了，也分不清哪边是下。讲师让你坐到开着的窗边。|你握得太久了。这一整天你都比别人早一刻听到事情发生，你讨厌这种感觉。}\n# en: Read one, then the other\nopt.right.label [e61653] = 先读一个，再读另一个\n# en: {You read heads, then tails, carefully, one after the other. It's not the exercise, but you get both right.|One future at a time. Slower, safer, and the lecturer says you'll get there.|One future, then the other, and you know both now before you read them. The lecturer says that's the start of holding them.}\nopt.right.say.1 [95f2f8] = {你先读正面，再读反面，一个接一个，小心翼翼。这不是练习的要求，但两个你都读对了。|一次一个未来。更慢，更稳，讲师说你会做到的。|一个未来，然后是另一个，现在你还没读就知道两个了。讲师说这是握住它们的开始。}\n\n@event alch_unlabelled_vial\n# en: The Unlabelled Vial\ntitle [ec7cd4] = 没有标签的药瓶\n# en: {Alchemy II hands out an unlabelled vial each, and an hour to say what's in it. Paper strips, a candle, a nose and a notebook are allowed.|Another unlabelled vial. The lecturer enjoys these.|The vial is waiting on your bench. You've started recognising the corks.} {Yours is perfectly clear and smells faintly of pears.|Yours is cloudy and keeps settling into layers.|Yours is warm to the touch.} {The ventilation charm is wheezing again.|The lab is quiet except for scratching quills.|The lecturer is humming, which means the vials are harder this week.}\ntext [8be139] = {炼金 II 发给每人一只没有标签的药瓶，一个钟头内说出里面是什么。试纸、蜡烛、鼻子和笔记本都可以用。|又是没标签的药瓶。讲师很享受这个。|药瓶在你桌上等着。你已经开始认得那些软木塞了。} {你的这瓶清澈透明，隐约有股梨香。|你的这瓶浑浊，总是沉淀分层。|你的这瓶摸上去是温的。} {通风咒又在喘了。|实验室里很安静，只有羽毛笔沙沙作响。|讲师在哼歌，这说明这周的瓶子更难了。}\n# en: A vial of clear liquid on a lab bench beside a strip of paper turning slowly purple, a student squinting at both.\nscene [3b7b2e] = 实验台上放着一瓶透明液体，旁边一条试纸正慢慢变紫，一名学生眯着眼看着两者。\n# en: Test it the long way\nopt.left.label [2f891a] = 老老实实逐项测\n# en: {Strip, flame, cold, filter. By the end of the hour you've a name and three reasons for it.|Every test, in order, written up neatly. Your notes are dull and your answer is right.|The warm one takes the whole hour, test by test. It's something you've never met, and you name it anyway, correctly.}\nopt.left.say.1 [b95931] = {试纸、火焰、冷却、过滤。一个钟头结束时，你有了一个名字和三条理由。|每一项测试，按部就班，写得工工整整。你的笔记很无聊，答案是对的。|那瓶温热的花了整整一个钟头，一项一项地测。是你从没遇到过的东西，但你还是叫出了它的名字，而且叫对了。}\n# en: Trust your nose\nopt.right.label [c495ff] = 相信你的鼻子\n# en: {One sniff and you know it. You write the name and use the spare hour to check yourself.|You tip the vial under your nose and the answer arrives before the smell does. The lecturer raises an eyebrow and a tick.|It's warm, and that tells you most of it. You sniff to confirm. The lecturer stops humming.}\nopt.right.say.1 [73827a] = {一闻你就知道了。你写下名字，用多出来的一个钟头检查自己。|你把瓶子凑到鼻子下，答案比气味先到。讲师挑了挑眉，打了个勾。|它是温的，这就说明了大半。你闻了一下确认。讲师不哼歌了。}\n# en: {One sniff and your eyes stream for the rest of the morning. You guessed wrong, too.|It's not what you thought, and it is very much up your nose. You spend the hour at the window.|You sniff the warm one. It sniffs back, in a way, and you spend the hour with a damp cloth on your face.}\nopt.right.say.2 [82aa3d] = {一闻之下，你整个上午都在流眼泪。而且你还猜错了。|它不是你以为的东西，而且结结实实冲进了你的鼻子。这一节课你都待在窗边。|你闻了闻那瓶温热的。某种意义上它也闻了闻你，这一节课你都捂着一块湿布。}\n\n@event alch_cracked_tray\n# en: The Cracked Tray\ntitle [a94d69] = 一盘裂物\n# en: {Mend at Alchemy II. Sister Amaranth sets out a tray of cracked things, cup, mirror and bone. \"Mend those,\" she says, \"and then we'll see about people.\"|The tray again. Sister Amaranth has found new things to crack.|Sister Amaranth puts the tray down and nods at it. You know the drill.} {A second-year is waiting on a cot with a sprained wrist from broom practice.|A third-year with a scalded hand from Evocation is waiting.|A first-year is in with a split lip and a long story about a door.}\ntext [7de022] = {炼金 II 的愈合课。阿玛兰斯修女摆出一盘裂开的东西：杯子、镜子、骨头。“先把这些补好，”她说，“然后再说人。”|又是这盘东西。阿玛兰斯修女找到了新的可以弄裂的东西。|阿玛兰斯修女放下托盘，朝它点点头。你知道该怎么做。} {一个二年级生坐在病床上等着，手腕是练飞帚时扭的。|一个在塑能课上烫伤手的三年级生在等着。|一个一年级生嘴唇裂了，正讲着一个关于门的长故事。}\n# en: A tray in the infirmary laid with a cracked teacup, a cracked hand mirror and a cracked bone from a teaching skeleton, a student's hands hovering over them.\nscene [a85abf] = 医务室里一只托盘，放着一只裂开的茶杯、一面裂开的手镜和一根教学骨架上裂开的骨头，一名学生的双手悬在它们上方。\n# en: Mend the tray, perfectly\nopt.left.label [9eeb5c] = 把托盘补得完美\n# en: {The cracks close like seams being sewn shut. Sister Amaranth runs a thumbnail along the cup and finds nothing.|Cup, mirror, bone, whole. Sister Amaranth says \"Fine,\" which is her highest word.|You mend the tray in half the time and Sister Amaranth sends you to the first-year with the split lip. It mends cleanly.}\nopt.left.say.1 [846031] = {裂缝像被缝上的接缝一样合拢。阿玛兰斯修女用拇指甲沿杯沿划过去，什么也没摸到。|杯子、镜子、骨头，全都完好。阿玛兰斯修女说“还行”，这是她的最高评价。|你只用一半的时间就补好了托盘，阿玛兰斯修女派你去给那个嘴唇裂开的一年级生看。缝得很干净。}\n# en: {The cup mends with a ridge in it, the mirror with a ripple. Sister Amaranth sets them on the shelf of shame.|You mend the bone a little crooked. Sister Amaranth says that's how you learn and puts it back in the skeleton anyway.|The mirror mends with your face in it, slightly wrong. Sister Amaranth turns it to the wall.}\nopt.left.say.2 [0cb5fe] = {杯子补好了，却留了一道棱；镜子补好了，却起了一道波纹。阿玛兰斯修女把它们放上了耻辱架。|你把骨头补得有点歪。阿玛兰斯修女说这就是学习，还是把它装回了骨架。|镜子补好了，里面却留着你的脸，还有点走样。阿玛兰斯修女把它翻过去对着墙。}\n# en: Mend the patient instead\nopt.right.label [c58841] = 改去治病人\n# en: {You skip the tray and take the patient, with Sister Amaranth's hand on your shoulder. It goes slowly. It works.|Sister Amaranth lets you try on the patient while she watches. You're shaky and slow, and you'll never mend a cup the same way again.|You mend the first-year's lip and hear the whole story about the door. It wasn't a door. You keep that to yourself.}\nopt.right.say.1 [c520ad] = {你跳过托盘，接手病人，阿玛兰斯修女的手搭在你肩上。进展很慢，但成了。|阿玛兰斯修女看着你在病人身上试。你手抖，动作慢，而且从此再也不会用原来的方式补杯子了。|你给那个一年级生缝好了嘴唇，听了关于那扇门的全部故事。根本不是门。你没说出去。}\n\n@event alch_lake_fever\n# en: Lake Fever Season\ntitle [5e2ca1] = 湖热季\n# en: {Advanced Alchemy treats real patients. The infirmary has Lake Fever cases, from swimming where they shouldn't. Sister Amaranth wants the fever drawn out, not just cooled.|Lake Fever again. Students never learn, Sister Amaranth says, and neither do lakes.|Fever duty. Sister Amaranth hands you a list without looking up.} {Your patient says they only went in up to the knees.|Your patient is a prefect, very embarrassed.|Your patient insists they were pushed.}\ntext [d87cd6] = {高等炼金要治真正的病人。医务室里有湖热病例，都是在不该游泳的地方游了泳。阿玛兰斯修女要你把热病拔出来，而不只是降温。|又是湖热。修女说，学生从不长记性，湖也不长。|退热值班。阿玛兰斯修女头也不抬地递给你一张名单。} {你的病人说他们只下水到膝盖。|你的病人是一位级长，非常难为情。|你的病人坚称是被人推下去的。}\n# en: A row of infirmary cots, students under blankets looking flushed and sorry for themselves, an advanced student drawing a faint grey haze out of one patient's brow.\nscene [4db536] = 一排医务室病床，学生们裹着毯子，满脸通红，可怜兮兮；一名高年级生正从一位病人额头上抽出一缕淡淡的灰雾。\n# en: Draw the fever out by hand\nopt.left.label [cf9f68] = 亲手把热拔出来\n# en: {The fever comes out like a grey thread pulled from wool. Your patient sleeps. You'd like to.|You draw it out slowly and the flush leaves their face. Sister Amaranth checks your work and doesn't check twice.|You draw out the fever and the story with it. They were pushed. Sister Amaranth writes down the name.}\nopt.left.say.1 [5d5e1c] = {热病像从羊毛里抽出的一根灰线，被你拉了出来。病人睡着了。你也很想睡。|你慢慢把它抽出来，潮红从他们脸上退去。阿玛兰斯修女检查了你的活儿，没有检查第二遍。|你把热病连同那个故事一起拔了出来。对方确实是被推下去的。阿玛兰斯修女记下了那个名字。}\n# en: {You draw out half of it and the half you drew comes with you. You spend the evening shivering in the next cot.|The cure has to come from somewhere. You wake up two hours later on the floor, with a blanket on you.|The fever comes out angry and goes into you. You spend the night in the next cot, insisting you weren't pushed.}\nopt.left.say.2 [520af0] = {你只拔出了一半，而拔出来的那一半跟上了你。整个晚上你都在隔壁病床上发抖。|治愈总得从某处来。两个钟头后你在地板上醒来，身上盖着一条毯子。|热病带着怒气出来，钻进了你身上。你在隔壁病床上过了一夜，一个劲儿地说自己不是被推下去的。}\n# en: Brew the slow tonic\nopt.right.label [af7952] = 熬慢效补剂\n# en: {Three hours of stirring willow bark and lake moss. The tonic works by morning, which is slower and safer.|You brew the old remedy by the book. Sister Amaranth tastes it, grimaces, and approves.|You brew the tonic without the book now. Sister Amaranth tastes it and doesn't grimace, which worries you more.}\nopt.right.say.1 [ed12ab] = {三个钟头搅着柳树皮和湖苔。补剂到早上才见效，慢一些，也稳一些。|你照书熬了老方子。阿玛兰斯修女尝了一口，皱了皱脸，点头认可。|现在你不看书也能熬这补剂了。阿玛兰斯修女尝了一口，没皱脸，这反倒让你更担心了。}\n# en: Purify it in one pass\nopt.up.label [f808f7] = 一次净化干净\n# en: {Your spell pulls the fever clean in a single pass. It leaves you yawning.|One pass of Purify and the patient sits up asking for soup. The tiredness lands on you an hour later.|You purify it in one pass, and your patient sits up and says who pushed them. You're asleep before Sister Amaranth asks.}\nopt.up.say.1 [83434a] = {你的咒语一遍就把热病拔得干干净净。它让你直打哈欠。|一遍净化，病人就坐起来要汤喝。一个钟头后，疲倦落到了你身上。|你一遍就净化干净了，病人坐起来说出了是谁推的。阿玛兰斯修女还没来得及问，你已经睡着了。}\n\n@event alch_third_floor_still\n# en: The Still Cracks\ntitle [7ecae5] = 蒸馏器裂了\n# en: {Advanced Alchemy distils essences that would rather be gas. The ventilation charm gave up on the third floor years ago. Today your still cracks.|Distilling day, and a hiss from your still that you know.|Your still has cracked again. You're getting fast at noticing.} {The vapour is green and smells of mint.|The vapour is pale and smells of nothing, which is worse.|The vapour is curling toward the next bench.}\ntext [fbd2ac] = {高等炼金要蒸馏那些宁愿当气体的精华。三楼的通风咒多年前就放弃了。今天你的蒸馏器裂了。|蒸馏日，你的蒸馏器又发出那种你熟悉的嘶嘶声。|你的蒸馏器又裂了。你发现得越来越快了。} {蒸汽是绿的，有薄荷味。|蒸汽颜色很淡，什么味道都没有，这更糟。|蒸汽正卷向隔壁的实验台。}\n# en: A copper still on a long bench, hissing from a fresh crack, a thin green vapour creeping across the laboratory floor.\nscene [e5be1b] = 长实验台上一只铜蒸馏器从新裂口里嘶嘶作响，一缕淡绿色的蒸汽正爬过实验室的地板。\n# en: Mend the crack, still hot\nopt.left.label [127d5d] = 趁热把裂缝补上\n# en: {You close the copper under your palms while it's still singing. The hiss stops. The class breathes out.|The crack seals with a sound like a sigh, and the batch is saved. The lecturer opens a window, for form's sake.|You seal it before the vapour reaches the next bench. Its owner never knows how close it came.}\nopt.left.say.1 [a9fb6d] = {铜还在嗡鸣，你就用掌心把它合上了。嘶声停了，全班松了口气。|裂缝合拢，发出一声叹息般的轻响，这一批保住了。讲师还是开了扇窗，走个形式。|你在蒸汽飘到隔壁台子之前封好了它。台子的主人永远不知道刚才有多悬。}\n# en: {The crack closes, then reopens with a pop. You breathe in a mouthful and spend the afternoon seeing double.|You burn your fingertips on the copper and the vapour gets out anyway. The lab is cleared for an hour.|The vapour reaches the next bench before you do. Two of you spend the afternoon seeing double.}\nopt.left.say.2 [a9cec7] = {裂缝合上了，又“噗”地一声裂开。你吸进一大口，整个下午看东西都是重影。|你的指尖被铜烫伤，蒸汽还是跑了出来。实验室清场一个钟头。|蒸汽比你先到了隔壁的台子。你们两个整个下午看东西都是重影。}\n# en: Shout, and clear the bench\nopt.right.label [17c853] = 大喊一声，清空台面\n# en: {You call the warning and clear the bench. The batch is lost, nobody is hurt, and you start again next week.|Everyone is out in the corridor in a minute flat. The lecturer goes back in, a scarf over the mouth, and comes out carrying your ruined batch.|You clear the bench and the one next to it. The lecturer notes that you thought of the neighbours, and the batch is still lost.}\nopt.right.say.1 [ac5419] = {你喊出警告，清空了台面。这一批毁了，没人受伤，你下周重来。|一分钟内所有人都到了走廊上。讲师用围巾捂着口鼻回去，把你那批报废的东西端了出来。|你清空了自己的台子，也清空了隔壁的。讲师注意到你考虑到了邻座，可这一批还是毁了。}\n\n@event illu_walking_double\n# en: The Walking Double\ntitle [188b9c] = 会走的分身\n# en: {Mirror at Illusion II. Make a double of yourself and walk it across the room. \"Standing doubles are furniture,\" says Professor Vey. \"Make it walk.\"|Doubles day. Vey watches feet, not faces.|Your double's walk is improving. It has your slouch now.} {The floor has been dusted with chalk, to show footprints.|Someone's double is walking backwards.|Vey is sitting in the front row, which is unsettling.}\ntext [6e95b9] = {幻术 II 的镜像课。给自己造一个分身，让它走过教室。“站着的分身是家具，”维伊教授说，“让它走。”|分身日。维伊看脚，不看脸。|你的分身走得越来越好了。它现在连你的驼背都学会了。} {地上撒了粉笔灰，好显出脚印。|有人的分身在倒着走。|维伊坐在第一排，这让人心里发毛。}\n# en: Two identical students walking side by side across a lecture hall, one of them leaving no footprints in the chalk dust.\nscene [45f81a] = 两个一模一样的学生并肩走过讲堂，其中一个在粉笔灰上没留下脚印。\n# en: Walk it right across\nopt.left.label [8d74d0] = 让它走完全程\n# en: {Your double crosses the room at your pace and even scuffs the chalk. Vey watches its feet the whole way and says nothing, which is high praise.|It walks, it turns, it nearly sits down. Vey says, \"Better.\"|Your double slouches across the room, Vey in the front row watching every step. \"That's you,\" Vey says. It isn't a compliment, quite.}\nopt.left.say.1 [836f34] = {你的分身按你的步子穿过教室，甚至蹭花了粉笔灰。维伊一路盯着它的脚，什么也没说，这是很高的赞扬。|它走，它转身，它差点坐下。维伊说：“好些了。”|你的分身弓着背穿过教室，维伊坐在第一排盯着每一步。“那就是你，”维伊说。这不全是夸奖。}\n# en: {Halfway across, your double's legs forget to move and it glides. Vey says it's a ghost, not a person, and moves on.|Your double walks into the wall and keeps walking. The class watches it vanish into the plaster.|Your double sees Vey in the front row and freezes, exactly as you would. It's very lifelike, and it doesn't walk.}\nopt.left.say.2 [f9609c] = {走到一半，分身的腿忘了动，开始飘。维伊说那是鬼，不是人，然后走开了。|你的分身撞上墙，还在继续走。全班看着它消失在灰泥里。|你的分身看见维伊坐在第一排就僵住了，跟你一模一样。非常逼真，可它就是不走。}\n# en: Keep it still and perfect\nopt.right.label [7cb3fb] = 让它站着，完美无缺\n# en: {Your double stands at your elbow, perfect to the last button. It doesn't move. Vey calls it a very good wardrobe.|You make it flawless and keep it standing. Vey taps its nose, and frowns when it holds.|Your double stands perfectly still in front of Vey, slouch and all. Vey reaches out and adjusts its collar, which holds.}\nopt.right.say.1 [c74e86] = {你的分身站在你手肘边，连最后一颗纽扣都完美。它不动。维伊称它是一件很好的衣橱。|你把它做得天衣无缝，然后让它站着。维伊敲了敲它的鼻子，发现敲得到，皱起了眉。|你的分身一动不动地站在维伊面前，连驼背都一样。维伊伸手理了理它的领子，领子还真在。}\n\n@event illu_good_news\n# en: Good News, Read Aloud\ntitle [0d109c] = 当众念好消息\n# en: {Glamour at Illusion II, and today it's the voice. Vey hands you a dull notice and asks you to read it so the class hears good news.|Another notice from Vey's drawer of very dull notices.|Vey has saved the dullest notice for you.} {Today's is about the drains.|Today's is about a change to the laundry rota.|Today's is a list of overdue library books, in full.}\ntext [f297c7] = {幻术 II 的魅影课，今天练的是声音。维伊递给你一份枯燥的通知，要你念得让全班听成好消息。|又是一份来自维伊那抽屉超级枯燥通知的通知。|维伊把最枯燥的那份留给了你。} {今天这份讲的是排水沟。|今天这份讲的是洗衣轮值表的调整。|今天这份是图书馆逾期书目，全文。}\n# en: A student standing at a lectern reading from a dull notice while the listening class smiles as though hearing wonderful news.\nscene [74b6a2] = 一名学生站在讲台前念着一份枯燥的通知，台下的同学们却笑容满面，像在听天大的好消息。\n# en: Glamour every word\nopt.left.label [3eefc7] = 字字施魅影\n# en: {By the second line the room is beaming. Someone claps. Vey waits for the clapping to stop and asks what the notice said. Nobody knows.|You read it warm and low, and the class leans in as if you were reading their letters from home.|You read out the overdue books like a list of prize-winners. Three students go to the library that afternoon.}\nopt.left.say.1 [af6971] = {念到第二行，满屋子都笑开了。有人鼓掌。维伊等掌声停了，问通知说了什么。没人知道。|你念得温暖而低沉，全班都探过身来，好像你在念他们的家书。|你把逾期的书单念得像获奖名单。那天下午有三个学生去了图书馆。}\n# en: {Your glamour slips on a long word and the room hears exactly what it is, a notice. Someone yawns on purpose.|You overdo it. The class looks alarmed, as if the notice were about to propose.|The glamour holds for twenty titles and drops on the twenty-first. The class hears the rest exactly as they are.}\nopt.left.say.2 [4f5ba7] = {魅影在一个长词上滑脱，全屋听到的正是它本来的样子：一份通知。有人故意打了个哈欠。|你用力过猛。全班一脸惊恐，好像这份通知要求婚了。|魅影撑过了二十个书名，在第二十一个上掉了下来。剩下的全班听到的都是原样。}\n# en: Read it plain, watch others\nopt.right.label [c74a90] = 照念，看别人\n# en: {You read it straight and sit down. Then you watch the others, and notice the best of them barely change their voices at all.|Plain reading, polite applause. You spend the hour writing down where the good ones breathe.|You read the list plain. It takes a long time. You learn more watching Vey's face than you would have by trying.}\nopt.right.say.1 [7cc167] = {你照原样念完坐下。然后你看别人念，发现最好的那几个几乎没怎么改变嗓音。|平平地念，礼貌的掌声。这一节课你都在记高手们在哪里换气。|你平平地念完那张清单。念了很久。看维伊的表情，你学到的比自己试还多。}\n# en: Glamour the notice itself\nopt.up.label [286fbe] = 给告示本身施魅影\n# en: {You don't touch your voice; you glamour the paper. Every student who reads it over your shoulder sees good news in their own hand. Vey applauds, twice, slowly.|You glamour the ink so it shimmers like a wedding invitation. The class fights over the notice afterwards. It is still, underneath, a very dull notice.|You glamour the list into an honours roll. Half the class goes to look for their names, and finds their overdue books.}\nopt.up.say.1 [577a68] = {你不动嗓子，而是给纸施魅影。每个从你肩后看过去的学生，都用自己的笔迹读到了好消息。维伊鼓了两下掌，很慢。|你给墨水施魅影，让它像婚礼请柬一样闪闪发光。课后全班抢那张告示。说到底，它还是一份非常无聊的通知。|你给那张清单施魅影，让它变成一份荣誉榜。半个班都跑去找自己的名字，结果找到了自己的逾期书。}\n\n@event illu_false_room\n# en: The Room That Isn't\ntitle [04316d] = 不存在的房间\n# en: {Advanced Illusion asks for a double of a whole room. Hang it over the real one and walk a classmate through it. Vey's rule is simple. If anyone walks into a wall, it's your fault.|The room exercise. Somebody always walks into something.|You hang your room. You've learned where the real walls are.} {Vey's volunteer today is a porter with a tray of cups.|Vey's volunteer today is Vey.|The real room has been rearranged overnight.}\ntext [307d9f] = {高等幻术要你造出一整个房间的分身，罩在真房间上，再让一个同学从中走过。维伊的规矩很简单。谁撞了墙，都算你的错。|房间练习。总有人会撞上点什么。|你把房间挂上去。你已经学会记住真墙在哪儿。} {维伊今天的志愿者是一位端着一托盘杯子的门房。|维伊今天的志愿者是维伊。|真房间昨夜被重新布置过了。}\n# en: A student walking confidently into a room that is partly painted light, a real stone wall standing where the illusion shows an open doorway.\nscene [487838] = 一名学生自信地走进一个部分由光绘成的房间，幻象里敞开的门洞处，其实立着一堵真正的石墙。\n# en: Hang the whole room\nopt.left.label [f08ac4] = 挂上整个房间\n# en: {Your walker crosses the false room and sits down in a false chair, which you hold up for them. Vey writes for a long time.|The room holds. Nobody touches a real wall. Vey walks it twice to be sure.|You hang your room over the rearranged one and nobody notices which is which, including, briefly, Vey.}\nopt.left.say.1 [3df0e0] = {走的人穿过假房间，坐上一把假椅子，你替他们撑住了它。维伊写了很久。|房间撑住了。没人碰到一堵真墙。维伊走了两遍才确认。|你把你的房间挂在重新布置过的那间上，没人分得清哪个是哪个，有那么一会儿，连维伊也分不清。}\n# en: {Your walker steps through a door you painted onto solid stone. You catch them as they fall, take the edge of the stone on your own elbow, and walk them to Sister Amaranth with an apology.|Halfway across the room flickers, and the walker freezes. So do you, and you walk hip-first into a real chair.|You hang your room over where the walls were yesterday. The walker finds today's walls with their nose.}\nopt.left.say.2 [1e6334] = {走的人迈进了你画在实心石头上的门。你在他们倒下时接住了，自己的手肘磕上了石棱，然后带着歉意把他们送到阿玛兰斯修女那里。|走到一半房间闪了一下，走的人僵住了。你也僵住了，胯骨一头撞上了一把真椅子。|你把房间挂在了昨天墙所在的地方。走的人用鼻子找到了今天的墙。}\n# en: Chalk the real walls first\nopt.right.label [26c22a] = 先用粉笔标出真墙\n# en: {You hang half a room, with the real walls marked out in chalk so nobody could miss them. Vey calls it \"a room with a safety rope\".|Safe and visible, chalk lines everywhere. Vey sighs, and then copies down how you did the window.|You chalk the new walls first, all of them. Vey says that's the only correct answer to a rearranged room.}\nopt.right.say.1 [d4e5f8] = {你只挂了半个房间，真墙都用粉笔标了出来，谁也不会错过。维伊称之为“系了安全绳的房间”。|安全，显眼，到处是粉笔线。维伊叹了口气，然后抄下了你做窗户的方法。|你先把新墙都用粉笔标了出来，一面不落。维伊说，对一间重新布置过的房间，这是唯一正确的答案。}\n\n@event illu_peeling_layers\n# en: Seeing Through\ntitle [014ea1] = 看穿\n# en: {Advanced Illusion. To make illusions you must see through them. Vey layers the hall in false rooms, one on another, and you peel them back one at a time.|Layer day. The hall looks normal. That's never a good sign.|You count the layers when you come in. You're usually wrong.} {The top layer has velvet curtains.|The top layer is a summer garden.|The top layer is the hall itself, just slightly too tidy.}\ntext [fa4567] = {高等幻术。要造幻象，就得先学会看穿幻象。维伊把讲堂层层罩上假房间，一层叠一层，你要一层一层揭开。|分层日。讲堂看起来很正常。这从来不是好兆头。|你一进门就数层数。你通常数错。} {最上面一层挂着天鹅绒帘子。|最上面一层是夏日花园。|最上面一层就是讲堂本身，只是整洁得有点过分。}\n# en: A lecture hall in layers like peeled paper: one layer with velvet curtains, one bare stone, one darker one beneath, and a student standing at its centre.\nscene [ee496c] = 一间像剥开的纸一样分层的讲堂：一层挂着天鹅绒帘子，一层是裸露的石头，下面还有更暗的一层，一名学生站在中央。\n# en: Peel down to the stone\nopt.left.label [12cf64] = 一直揭到石头\n# en: {Seven layers, then bare stone. Vey asks how many. You say seven. She smiles and says eight, and points at herself.|You reach the stone and stay there, sure of it. Vey nods slowly. \"Hold on to that,\" she says.|The too-tidy hall comes off last, and under it is the real hall, untidy and wonderful. Vey says you're the first today.}\nopt.left.say.1 [3ff14c] = {七层，然后是裸石。维伊问几层。你说七层。她笑着说八层，指了指自己。|你揭到石头，并且停在那里，确信无疑。维伊慢慢点头。“抓住这个感觉，”她说。|太整洁的讲堂最后被揭下，底下是真正的讲堂，乱糟糟的，美极了。维伊说你是今天第一个。}\n# en: {You peel one too many and the stone underneath looks false. For the rest of the day you knock on walls, with a headache behind your eyes.|After the fourth layer you stop being sure which way is up. Vey walks you to a chair, which is real.|You peel the tidy hall and find another tidy hall. And another. Vey stops you at the sixth, gently.}\nopt.left.say.2 [a76a84] = {你多揭了一层，底下的石头看起来也像假的。这一整天你都在敲墙，眼睛后面隐隐作痛。|揭到第四层后，你再也不确定哪边是上。维伊把你领到一把椅子前，那把椅子是真的。|你揭开整洁的讲堂，下面还是一个整洁的讲堂。又一个。维伊在第六层时轻轻叫停了你。}\n# en: Stop at the third layer\nopt.right.label [b16cdd] = 揭到第三层就停\n# en: {Three layers and you stop, dizzy. Vey says knowing when to stop is the other half of the skill.|You get through three and sit down. The hall still looks too tidy, and you decide not to ask.|You stop at three, and you're right to: the third was the real one. Vey says so, with some surprise.}\nopt.right.say.1 [9d3b88] = {三层，你停下来，头晕目眩。维伊说，知道何时停下是这门技艺的另一半。|你揭了三层就坐下了。讲堂看起来还是太整洁，你决定不问。|你在第三层停下了，停得没错：第三层就是真的。维伊这么说了，有点意外。}\n\n@event beast_hedge_run\n# en: The Hedge Run\ntitle [d8e2dc] = 越篱飞行\n# en: {Beastcraft II puts haste on a broom, low over the hedges, as fast as you dare. Professor Brask says brooms are harder to reason with than hippogriffs, and harder to apologise to.|The hedge run. Your broom remembers last time.|Hedges. You've learned to read a broom's sulk through the handle.} {Brask is timing with a crooked hourglass.|The hedges have been trimmed, unevenly.|{season == \"autumn\": Leaves come up behind every broom.|{season == \"spring\": The hedges are full of nesting birds who object.|The ground is hard and unforgiving.}}}\ntext [7b76b5] = {驭兽学 II 把疾速用在飞帚上，贴着树篱低飞，能多快就多快。布拉斯克教授说，飞帚比鹰头马身有翼兽更难讲道理，也更难道歉。|越篱飞行。你的飞帚记得上一次。|树篱。你已经学会从帚柄上读出飞帚的闷气。} {布拉斯克拿着一只歪沙漏计时。|树篱修剪过了，修得高低不平。|{season == \"autumn\": 每把飞帚身后都扬起落叶。|{season == \"spring\": 树篱里满是筑巢的鸟，它们很有意见。|地面又硬又不饶人。}}}\n# en: Students on brooms skimming low over a line of hedges in a field, one broom bucking as its rider leans forward.\nscene [a6f163] = 学生们骑着飞帚贴着田里的一排树篱掠过，其中一把飞帚在骑手前倾时尥起了蹶子。\n# en: Haste at the last hedge\nopt.left.label [73bbd3] = 最后一道篱前加速\n# en: {You borrow a hawk's speed for the last hedge and clear it by a hand. Brask stops the hourglass and nods.|The broom flinches, then commits. You come in fastest of the morning, with hedge in your hair.|You feel the broom's sulk through the handle and haste it anyway, kindly. It clears the last hedge and forgives you.}\nopt.left.say.1 [8af82a] = {你借来鹰的速度飞越最后一道树篱，高出一掌。布拉斯克停了沙漏，点了点头。|飞帚一缩，然后豁出去了。你是这个早上最快的，头发里还挂着树篱。|你从帚柄上感觉到它在赌气，还是温和地给它加了疾速。它越过最后一道树篱，原谅了你。}\n# en: {The broom bucks at the haste and you go over the hedge without it.|You're fast. The broom is faster, briefly, and then upside down. You land in a bramble.|The broom has been sulking all morning and saves it for the last hedge. You go over; it stays.}\nopt.left.say.2 [96ca99] = {飞帚被疾速一惊，你独自越过了树篱，它没跟上。|你很快。飞帚短暂地比你更快，然后翻了个底朝天。你落进了荆棘丛。|飞帚赌了一上午的气，全留到了最后一道树篱。你过去了，它留下了。}\n# en: Fly it clean and steady\nopt.right.label [caf423] = 飞得干净平稳\n# en: {No haste, just a clean low line over every hedge. Brask says it's the best boring flying she's seen all week.|You fly it slow and tidy. The broom approves. Brask is less impressed.|You fly it steady and let it sulk. Brask says the broom will remember you were patient, and so will she.}\nopt.right.say.1 [27f320] = {不用疾速，每道树篱都是一条干净的低线。布拉斯克说这是她这周见过最好的无聊飞行。|你飞得又慢又整齐。飞帚很满意。布拉斯克没那么满意。|你飞得稳稳的，由它赌气。布拉斯克说飞帚会记得你有耐心，她也会。}\n\n@event beast_owl_post\n# en: Teaching the Owls\ntitle [ba75dc] = 教猫头鹰送信\n# en: {Beast-speech at Beastcraft II. The young owls must learn the post routes. Brask hands you one and says, \"Explain the castle to it.\"|A new young owl, and the same castle to explain.|Another owl. You've got a speech now, and a map scratched on a beam.} {Your owl wants to know why there are so many towers.|Your owl has strong opinions about the library.|Your owl keeps falling asleep, standing up.}\ntext [fabd5c] = {驭兽学 II 的兽语课。小猫头鹰们得学会送信的路线。布拉斯克递给你一只，说：“把城堡给它讲清楚。”|又一只小猫头鹰，又是同一座城堡要讲。|又一只猫头鹰。你如今有一套讲稿，还在梁上刻了张地图。} {你的猫头鹰想知道为什么塔有这么多座。|你的猫头鹰对图书馆有很强烈的看法。|你的猫头鹰总是站着就睡着了。}\n# en: A student in a stable loft with a young owl on their arm, pointing out of a high window toward a distant tower.\nscene [3d64bf] = 马厩阁楼里，一名学生手臂上停着一只小猫头鹰，正指着高窗外远处的一座塔。\n# en: Explain it in owl\nopt.left.label [27c58e] = 用猫头鹰的话讲\n# en: {You describe the castle in winds, perches and mice. The owl blinks, and goes and comes back with Brask's letter.|It takes a while to get \"left\" across to a bird. When it does, the owl does the route twice to show off.|You give your speech, map and all. The sleepy owl wakes for the important part and does the route without a mistake.}\nopt.left.say.1 [42dfb9] = {你用风、栖木和老鼠来描述城堡。猫头鹰眨眨眼，飞出去，带回了布拉斯克的信。|要让一只鸟明白“左”可得花点工夫。等它明白了，它把路线飞了两遍来炫耀。|你讲完你那套说辞，连地图也用上了。打瞌睡的猫头鹰在关键处醒了过来，一处没错地飞完了路线。}\n# en: {The owl listens closely, then flies to the kitchens. Every time.|The owl makes you explain \"tower\" seven times and then goes to sleep on your head.|Your owl sleeps through the whole speech, then wakes and flies to the kitchens. You're starting to take it personally.}\nopt.left.say.2 [2c3a4c] = {猫头鹰听得很认真，然后飞去了厨房。每一次都是。|猫头鹰让你把“塔”解释了七遍，然后在你头上睡着了。|你的猫头鹰整篇说辞都在睡觉，醒来就飞去了厨房。你开始觉得这是针对你了。}\n# en: Walk the route with it\nopt.right.label [beabeb] = 带它走一遍路线\n# en: {You walk the whole post route with the owl on your arm, up every stair. Your legs ache and the owl has learned it.|Up and down the castle with an owl on your wrist. People stare. The owl stares back.|You walk the route with the owl asleep on your arm. It learns it anyway, somehow. Brask says they do that.}\nopt.right.say.1 [c28df0] = {你让猫头鹰停在手臂上，把整条送信路线走了一遍，每段楼梯都爬了。你腿疼，它学会了。|胳膊上架着一只猫头鹰，在城堡里上上下下。人们盯着看。猫头鹰盯回去。|你走着那条路线，猫头鹰在你胳膊上睡着了。可它还是学会了。布拉斯克说它们就是这样。}\n\n@event beast_hooves\n# en: Hoof Day\ntitle [dcf707] = 修蹄日\n# en: {Advanced Beastcraft does the work nobody else wants. Today it's hooves. A hippogriff's need trimming each month, and the hippogriff disagrees.|Hoof day. The stall is the same; the hippogriff's opinion is too.|Hooves. You go in with a bucket of apples and a plan.} {Today's hippogriff is grey and patient.|Today's is young and very sure of itself.|Today's is old and wants to tell you about it.}\ntext [61631e] = {高等驭兽学干的是没人想干的活。今天是修蹄。鹰头马身有翼兽的蹄子每月要修一次，而它们不同意。|修蹄日。马棚还是那个马棚，它的意见也还是那个意见。|修蹄。你带着一桶苹果和一个计划进去。} {今天这只是灰色的，很有耐心。|今天这只很年轻，非常自以为是。|今天这只很老，想跟你聊聊。}\n# en: A student crouched beside a hippogriff in a stall, a hoof trimming knife in hand, the hippogriff looking down with one sceptical eye.\nscene [e931b9] = 一名学生蹲在马棚里一只鹰头马身有翼兽旁边，手里拿着修蹄刀，那只兽用一只怀疑的眼睛俯视着。\n# en: Talk it into lifting a foot\nopt.left.label [33c422] = 劝它抬起蹄子\n# en: {It listens to your case and lifts a hoof with enormous dignity. Brask watches from the door and says nothing.|You talk it through every hoof. It sighs at you between each one, like a patient uncle.|The old hippogriff tells you about its youth for every hoof. You listen to all of it, and it lifts each foot when the story ends.}\nopt.left.say.1 [067441] = {它听完你的理由，极有尊严地抬起一只蹄子。布拉斯克在门口看着，什么也没说。|你一只一只蹄子地跟它谈。每修完一只它就冲你叹口气，像个耐心的叔叔。|修每只蹄子时，老鹰头马身有翼兽都要讲一段它年轻时的事。你全听完了，每段故事讲完它就抬起一只脚。}\n# en: {It lets you get all the way to the last hoof before stepping on your foot.|It hears you out, then sits on you. Brask lifts it off with one hand and a word.|The old one talks and talks and doesn't lift a foot. You've been had, and Brask is laughing at the door.}\nopt.left.say.2 [54f491] = {它一直等到你修最后一只蹄子，才踩了你的脚。|它听你说完，然后坐在了你身上。布拉斯克一只手加一句话就把它挪开了。|那只老的说个不停，就是不抬脚。你被耍了，布拉斯克在门口笑。}\n# en: Rope, and Brask holding\nopt.right.label [34c6c0] = 上绳子，请布拉斯克按着\n# en: {The old way, then. A rope, a strong arm, Brask at its head. It's sweaty work and nobody enjoys it, including the hippogriff.|With Brask holding the head and you holding on, you finish every hoof. Your shoulders will remember it.|Apples first, then rope, then Brask. The plan works, and the hippogriff eats the plan.}\nopt.right.say.1 [278161] = {老办法：一根绳，一条有力的胳膊，布拉斯克按着它的头。又累又满身汗，谁都不喜欢，包括那只兽。|布拉斯克按着头，你死死抓着，每只蹄子都修完了。你的肩膀会记住这一天。|先给苹果，再上绳子，再叫布拉斯克。计划成功了，鹰头马身有翼兽把计划也吃了。}\n\n@event beast_night_stable\n# en: Sitting Up With the Sick\ntitle [789593] = 守着病兽\n# en: {Advanced students take turns sitting up in the stables when a creature is ill. At the morning lecture Brask hands you a lantern, a blanket and a list for tonight.|Your name is on Brask's night-watch list again, and the list is shorter.|Night watch again tonight. Brask hands over the lantern without a word; the owls will only mutter.} {This time it's the griffon chick, off its food.|This time it's an old owl with a cough.|This time it's a hippogriff who ate something it shouldn't.} {season == \"winter\": The stable will be cold enough to see your breath.|At least it's warm in the straw.}\ntext [bc241e] = {有生物生病时，高年级生轮流在马厩守夜。早课上，布拉斯克交给你一盏灯笼、一条毯子和今晚的清单。|布拉斯克的守夜名单上又有你，清单短了些。|今晚又守夜。布拉斯克一言不发地把灯笼递给你；猫头鹰们只会嘀咕几声。} {这次是不肯吃东西的狮鹫雏鸟。|这次是一只咳嗽的老猫头鹰。|这次是一只吃了不该吃的东西的鹰头马身有翼兽。} {season == \"winter\": 马厩会冷得能看见自己的呼气。|至少干草堆里很暖和。}\n# en: A student sitting in straw by lantern light, a sick creature curled beside them, the rest of the stables asleep.\nscene [88ac60] = 一名学生借着灯笼光坐在干草里，一只生病的生物蜷在身旁，马厩里其余的一切都睡着了。\n# en: Sit up all night\nopt.left.label [464a6d] = 守它一整夜\n# en: {You don't sleep, and by dawn it's breathing easier. Brask finds you asleep in the straw and leaves you there.|You watch it all night, changing the water and the straw. In the morning it eats, and so do you, enormously.|You sit up with the hippogriff while it groans about its mistake. By dawn it's eating again, and you're asleep against its side.}\nopt.left.say.1 [2d3b38] = {你一夜没睡，天亮时它呼吸顺畅了些。布拉斯克发现你睡在干草里，就让你接着睡。|你看了它一整夜，换水，换干草。早上它吃东西了，你也吃了，吃了很多。|你守着那只为自己吃错东西而呻吟的鹰头马身有翼兽。天亮时它又开始吃东西了，你靠着它的身子睡着了。}\n# en: Ask it where it hurts\nopt.right.label [b5e61b] = 问它哪里疼\n# en: {It tells you, in its own way, exactly where it hurts. You write it on Brask's list and both of you sleep.|It points with its whole body. You bring the right tonic and it drops off before the lantern does.|It tells you exactly what it ate, with some pride. You fetch the purge and it forgives you by morning.}\nopt.right.say.1 [0f5834] = {它用自己的方式准确地告诉了你哪里疼。你写在布拉斯克的清单上，你们俩都睡了。|它用整个身子指给你看。你拿来对的补剂，它比灯笼先睡着。|它带着几分得意，一五一十地告诉你吃了什么。你取来了催吐药，早上它就原谅了你。}\n# en: {It tells you a great deal, about its neighbours, the hay and the moon, and nothing about where it hurts.|It won't talk to you. It talks to the lantern. You stay up anyway, which is the point.|It won't say what it ate, out of embarrassment. You guess, and Brask corrects the guess in the morning.}\nopt.right.say.2 [23dbdb] = {它跟你讲了很多，关于邻居、干草和月亮，就是没讲哪里疼。|它不跟你说话。它跟灯笼说话。你还是守着，这才是要点。|出于难为情，它不肯说吃了什么。你猜了一个，第二天早上布拉斯克纠正了你的猜测。}\n# en: Speak with it properly\nopt.up.label [70fe7d] = 好好跟它说说话\n# en: {With the spell, it's easy. It explains the pain, the food, and what it thinks of the night. You fetch the right remedy.|You speak properly, and it answers properly, slowly, like someone who's been waiting. You both feel better.|You ask it properly and it tells you, ashamed, what it ate. The remedy is simple. The lecture you give it is not.}\nopt.up.say.1 [68b3c8] = {有了咒语就容易了：它讲了疼在哪儿，吃了什么，还有它对这一夜的看法。你取来了对的药。|你好好地说，它也好好地答，慢慢的，像等了很久的人。你们俩都好受些了。|你好好地问，它羞愧地说出了吃了什么。药很简单。你给它上的那一课可不简单。}\n\n@event umbral_ward_candle\n# en: Ward the Candle\ntitle [b4ebd5] = 护住蜡烛\n# en: {Ward at Umbral Theory II. Ward a candle while your partner tries to put it out. Today that's {partner}.|Candle wards again, and the lamps are lower. {partner} is across the bench.|The candle exercise. You're paired with {partner}, who rolls up {their} sleeves.} {The professor walks between the benches with a hand over the flames.|The room smells of wax and burnt thread.|Someone's ward has shrunk to the size of a thimble.}\ntext [294dde] = {暗影理论 II 的护界课。给一根蜡烛设下护界，同时你的搭档设法把它弄灭。今天的搭档是{partner}。|又是蜡烛护界，灯压得更低了。{partner}坐在桌子对面。|蜡烛练习。你和{partner}一组，{they}卷起了{their}袖子。} {教授在桌间走动，一只手从火焰上方掠过。|屋里一股蜡和烧焦线头的味道。|有人的护界缩得只有顶针大小。}\n# en: Two students at a dim bench under low lamps, a candle between them inside a faint pale shell, one student trying to blow it out.\nscene [5a828b] = 低垂的灯下，两名学生坐在昏暗的长桌前，中间一根蜡烛罩在一层淡淡的苍白外壳里，其中一人正试图把它吹灭。\n# en: A thick, honest shell\nopt.left.label [53a438] = 一层厚实老实的壳\n# en: {Your ward is plain and solid. {partner} blows and blows and the candle doesn't even lean.|Strong and dull. {partner} gives up and the two of you watch the flame burn down.|Your shell is thick and calm. {partner}'s sleeves are rolled for nothing; the flame doesn't even notice {them}.}\nopt.left.say.1 [d60e26] = {你的护界朴素而结实。{partner}吹了又吹，蜡烛连歪都没歪。|结实而乏味。{partner}放弃了，你们俩一起看着火焰燃尽。|你的护界又厚又沉稳。{partner}白卷了袖子，火苗根本没注意到{them}。}\n# en: A ward that turns them aside\nopt.right.label [6b490b] = 设个把人引开的护界\n# en: {Your ward turns {partner}'s breath round, and it blows {their} own hair into {their} eyes. The professor stops and says, \"Ah.\"|{partner} reaches for your candle and finds {their} hand on the wrong side of the bench. The professor approves.|Your ward turns {partner} aside so gently that {they} try{s} three times before working out what happened.}\nopt.right.say.1 [156f4c] = {你的护界把{partner}的气息拐了个弯，吹得{their}头发糊了一脸。教授停下脚步，说：“啊。”|{partner}伸手去够你的蜡烛，却发现自己的手到了桌子另一边。教授表示赞许。|你的护界把{partner}引开得如此轻柔，{they}试了三次才弄明白是怎么回事。}\n# en: {Your clever ward turns aside the wrong thing, and the candle goes out with a smug little puff. {partner} smirks.|The ward bends {partner}'s breath straight into your face. You sneeze and the candle goes out.|Your ward shrinks to a thimble, like your neighbour's, and {partner} pinches the flame out with two fingers.}\nopt.right.say.2 [1f1f04] = {你那个聪明的护界引开了错的东西，蜡烛得意地“噗”一声灭了。{partner}偷笑。|护界把{partner}的气息直直拐到你脸上。你打了个喷嚏，蜡烛灭了。|你的护界缩成了顶针大小，跟邻座的一样，{partner}两根手指就把火苗捏灭了。}\n\n@event umbral_dark_roll\n# en: Who Moved\ntitle [133ff8] = 谁动了\n# en: {Umbral Theory II. The professor puts out every lamp, and in the dark one student moves. Say who, and where to.|Lamps out. You've learned to hear a sleeve.|The dark. You know the room's sounds, and which floorboard is a liar.} {Rain on the tower roof makes it harder.|It's so quiet you can hear the stair breathing.|Somebody's stomach rumbles, which helps no one.}\ntext [37cfa1] = {暗影理论 II。教授熄掉每一盏灯，黑暗中有一个学生会移动。说出是谁，挪到了哪里。|灯灭了。你已经学会听见一只袖子的声音。|黑暗。你熟悉这间屋子的声音，也知道哪块地板会撒谎。} {雨打在塔顶，让这更难了。|安静得能听见楼梯在呼吸。|有人肚子咕咕叫，这对谁都没帮助。}\n# en: A pitch-dark classroom at the top of a tower, a few vague shapes of students sitting perfectly still in the gloom.\nscene [d2fcaa] = 塔顶一间漆黑的教室，几个模糊的学生身影一动不动地坐在昏暗里。\n# en: Listen, and name them\nopt.left.label [80bc08] = 听，然后说出名字\n# en: {\"Third bench, to the window.\" Right. The professor relights a lamp and moves on.|You get it on the second try, by the creak of a bench and a faint smell of soap.|\"Second bench, toward the door, stepping over the liar.\" The professor laughs in the dark, once.}\nopt.left.say.1 [8cf4b3] = {“第三排，往窗边去了。”对了。教授重新点亮一盏灯，继续下一轮。|第二次你猜中了，靠的是一张长凳的吱呀声和一丝淡淡的肥皂味。|“第二排，往门那边去，跨过了那块说谎的地板。”教授在黑暗中笑了一声。}\n# en: Be the one who moves\nopt.right.label [25e0c1] = 做那个移动的人\n# en: {You cross the whole room in the dark and nobody names you. When the lamps come up you're behind the professor.|Nobody hears you. The professor says, \"Someone moved,\" and seems pleased not to know who.|You step over the floorboard that lies and cross the room in silence. Even the professor guesses wrong.}\nopt.right.say.1 [9ff72e] = {你在黑暗中穿过整个房间，没人叫出你的名字。灯亮起时你站在教授身后。|没人听见你。教授说：“有人动了，”似乎很高兴自己不知道是谁。|你跨过那块会说谎的地板，悄无声息地穿过房间。连教授都猜错了。}\n# en: {You catch a desk with your shin. \"Window, limping,\" says everyone at once.|Your shoe squeaks on the second step, you turn your ankle stopping, and the whole class names you, with feeling.|You avoid the liar and find a new one. The whole room names you before you've reached the wall.}\nopt.right.say.2 [a666e1] = {你的小腿撞上了桌子。“窗边，瘸着腿，”所有人异口同声。|你的鞋在第二级台阶上吱了一声，急停时还崴了脚。全班带着感情叫出了你的名字。|你避开了那块说谎的地板，却踩上了一块新的。还没走到墙边，全屋就叫出了你的名字。}\n\n@event umbral_corridor_step\n# en: Across the Corridor\ntitle [d8d8cf] = 穿过走廊\n# en: {Advanced Umbral Theory. Shadowstep across the corridor, door to door, the farthest the professor allows. \"Short distances only,\" the professor says. \"This is as short as long gets.\"|The corridor. The cold in between is colder than last time.|You stand at the door with your heels on the line. You know the cold that's coming.} {The lamps are out on the landing.|A draught is moving along the floor.|Someone came back from it with frost on one shoulder.}\ntext [09146d] = {高等暗影理论。用影步穿过走廊，从这扇门到那扇门，这是教授允许的最远距离。“只能短距离，”教授说，“这已经是短里面最长的了。”|走廊。中间的寒冷比上次更冷。|你脚跟抵着线站在门口。你知道将要袭来的那股寒意。} {楼梯平台上的灯灭了。|一股穿堂风贴着地面流动。|有人回来时一边肩膀上结着霜。}\n# en: A dim stone corridor at the top of a tower, a student half stepping out of the shadow of one doorway while their own shadow lingers in another across the hall.\nscene [e52a16] = 塔顶一条昏暗的石砌走廊，一名学生半个身子正从一扇门的阴影里跨出，而他们自己的影子还逗留在走廊对面的另一扇门里。\n# en: Step across\nopt.left.label [894fac] = 一步跨过去\n# en: {One shadow, cold and long, and then the other. You come out on the far side shaking and grinning.|You step in by one door and out by the other, with a breath of frost on your collar. The professor writes your name, and a time.|You step into the cold you knew was coming and out the far door with nothing on your shoulder. The professor writes a shorter time.}\nopt.left.say.1 [63de51] = {一片影子，又冷又长，然后是另一片。你从对面出来，浑身发抖，咧着嘴笑。|你从一扇门进，从另一扇门出，衣领上带着一口霜气。教授写下你的名字和一个时间。|你踏进早知会来的寒冷，从远处的门出来，肩上什么也没沾。教授写下了一个更短的时间。}\n# en: {The cold in between is longer than you thought. You come out in the right doorway and can't feel your hands for an hour.|You step and come out in the same doorway you went in by, with the feeling that something looked at you on the way.|You come back with frost on one shoulder, like the others. It takes until supper to melt, and it isn't only cold.}\nopt.left.say.2 [78743d] = {中间的寒冷比你想的更长。你从对的门口出来，一个钟头手都没有知觉。|你跨了一步，却从进去的那扇门出来了，总觉得路上有什么东西看了你一眼。|你像别人一样，回来时一边肩膀上结了霜。一直到晚饭才化，而且不只是冷。}\n# en: Walk it, counting shadows\nopt.right.label [de4a9d] = 走过去，数着影子\n# en: {You walk the corridor and count every shadow and every lamp. The professor calls this \"mapping your road\".|You pace it out and write down each shadow's depth. Next time you'll step it. Probably.|You walk it and count the shadows, again. They're the same as last time, which you now realise is the lesson.}\nopt.right.say.1 [e6a1e7] = {你走过走廊，数清每一片影子和每一盏灯。教授称之为“给你的路画地图”。|你一步步量过去，记下每片影子的深浅。下次你会用影步。大概吧。|你又走了一遍，数着影子。它们和上次一样，你这才明白这正是这一课。}\n# en: Step with a held breath\nopt.up.label [1d6b25] = 屏住呼吸踏影\n# en: {You step without thinking about it, the way the spell knows. The cold barely touches you.|The spell takes you over quick and clean. Your fingers are cold for a minute.|You hold your breath and step. No frost, no cold, no time at all. The professor stops writing.}\nopt.up.say.1 [207e6c] = {你不假思索地一步跨过，就像咒语本身知道路。寒意几乎没碰到你。|咒语带着你又快又干净地过去了。你的手指冷了一分钟。|你屏住呼吸，迈步。没有霜，没有冷，几乎没用时间。教授停下了笔。}\n\n@event umbral_unpick_ward\n# en: Unpicking the Box\ntitle [be7a63] = 拆箱上的护界\n# en: {Advanced Umbral Theory. The professor wards a small box and you unpick the ward without it noticing. \"A ward that notices you,\" the professor says, \"tells someone.\"|Box day. The professor has a new box, and a new ward on it.|The box. You've got a favourite needle for this now.} {This ward is fine as a cobweb.|This ward is thick and patient.|This ward hums when you breathe on it.}\ntext [7eb3d9] = {高等暗影理论。教授给一只小箱子设下护界，你要在它察觉不到的情况下把护界拆掉。“察觉到你的护界，”教授说，“会告诉别人。”|拆箱日。教授拿来一只新箱子，上面是新的护界。|箱子。你现在有一根专门用来干这个的针了。} {这道护界细得像蛛网。|这道护界又厚又有耐心。|你对它呼气时，这道护界会嗡嗡作响。}\n# en: A small iron-bound box on a bench under a dim lamp, faint threads of a pale ward wrapped around it, a student leaning close with a needle.\nscene [b89192] = 昏暗灯下的长桌上放着一只铁箍小箱，淡淡的苍白护界丝线缠绕着它，一名学生拿着针凑近。\n# en: Thread by thread\nopt.left.label [085b9e] = 一根一根地拆\n# en: {An hour with a needle and a steady breath, and the box opens. The professor checks the ward and it has no idea.|You undo it strand by strand. It takes the whole lesson, and you don't look up once.|Your favourite needle and a slow breath. The humming ward goes quiet one strand at a time, and never notices it's gone.}\nopt.left.say.1 [0fc048] = {一根针，一口稳稳的气，一个钟头后箱子开了。教授检查护界，它浑然不觉。|你一缕一缕地解开它。这花了整节课，你一次头都没抬。|你最顺手的那根针，一口慢慢的气。嗡鸣的护界一缕一缕地安静下来，始终没发觉自己已经没了。}\n# en: Cut it in one stroke\nopt.right.label [bb2609] = 一刀切断\n# en: {You find the knot and cut it clean. The ward never knows. The professor raises a brow and opens the box.|One cut and the threads fall away. The professor spends a moment looking at the box and then at you.|You cut it between hums. The ward stops mid-note and never starts again. The professor writes something down.}\nopt.right.say.1 [bd0b78] = {你找到结，干净利落地切断。护界始终没察觉。教授挑了挑眉，打开了箱子。|一刀下去，丝线纷纷散落。教授看了箱子一会儿，又看了看你。|你在两声嗡鸣之间下刀。护界在半个音上停住，再也没响起。教授记了点什么。}\n# en: {The ward notices, snaps across your knuckles and rings like a little bell. The professor says, \"And now someone knows.\" Everyone looks at you.|The ward snaps back at your hand and stings. The box stays shut.|You cut it mid-hum. It shrieks instead, and the professor has to open the window to let the sound out.}\nopt.right.say.2 [ba15c0] = {护界察觉了，啪地抽过你的指节，像一只小铃铛一样响起来。教授说：“现在有人知道了。”所有人都看向你。|护界弹回来蜇了你的手。箱子还是关着。|你在嗡鸣中途下刀。它尖叫起来，教授只好开窗把声音放出去。}\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/pools/dating.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/pools/dating.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event date_market_day\n# en: Market Day\ntitle [0bcdaf] = 集市日\n# en: {Market day in the village, and {partner} has decided you're going together.|Market day again. {partner} knows which stall has the good pies now.|{partner} has a list for the market, and you're on it.} {A stall sells hats nobody should wear.|There's a juggler with one ball too many.|{season == \"winter\": Roasted chestnuts, paper cones, cold fingers.|Strawberries, a whole cart of them.}}\ntext [d9c093] = {村里的集市日，{partner} 已经决定你们要一起去。|又是集市日。{partner} 现在知道哪个摊子的派好吃了。|{partner} 列了一张集市清单，你也在上面。} {有个摊子卖谁都不该戴的帽子。|有个杂耍艺人多抛了一个球。|{season == \"winter\": 烤栗子、纸筒、冻僵的手指。|草莓，整整一车。}}\n# en: Two students wandering a village market arm in arm, stalls of ribbons and pies, one holding up a ridiculous hat.\nscene [08939c] = 两个学生挽着胳膊逛村里的集市，摊位上挂着丝带、摆着馅饼，其中一个举起一顶滑稽的帽子。\n# en: Buy {them} something silly\nopt.left.label [89db94] = 给 {them} 买点傻东西\n# en: {You buy {partner} the worst hat at the market. {They} wear{s} it all day, with enormous dignity.|A tin ring from a pedlar, too big for any finger. {partner} threads it on a string and doesn't take it off.|Halfway down {partner}'s list, between flour and string, you find \"something silly\" in your own handwriting. A paper crown. {They} wear{s} it home.}\nopt.left.say.1 [f4dea1] = {你给 {partner} 买了集市上最丑的帽子。{They} 戴了一整天，极其庄重。|从小贩那儿买来一枚锡戒指，哪根手指戴都太大。{partner} 把它穿在绳子上，再没摘下来。|{partner} 的清单写到一半，在面粉和绳子之间，有一行是你的笔迹：“买点傻东西”。一顶纸王冠。{They} 戴着它回了家。}\n# en: Just walk and talk\nopt.right.label [a304f1] = 就走走聊聊\n# en: {You walk the whole market twice and buy nothing at all. You talk the entire time and remember none of it, only the voice.|An afternoon of nothing in particular, which is exactly what you both needed.|You ignore the list together and just walk. {partner} crosses things off anyway, one by one, as if you'd bought them.}\nopt.right.say.1 [3a1d75] = {你们把整个集市逛了两遍，什么都没买。你们一直在聊，聊了什么一句也不记得，只记得那个声音。|一个无所事事的下午，正是你们俩都需要的。|你们一起把清单抛到脑后，只是走着。{partner} 还是一项一项地划掉，好像你们都买了似的。}\n\n@event date_study_together\n# en: Studying Together\ntitle [1131fc] = 一起学习\n# en: {{partner} suggests studying together. In theory, this is efficient.|Another study evening with {partner}. The theory remains a theory.|{partner} has saved you a seat in the library and stacked your books for you.} {The exam is in a fortnight.|The library is very quiet.|Your knees keep touching under the table.}\ntext [aaefba] = {{partner} 提议一起学习。理论上，这很高效。|又一个和 {partner} 一起学习的晚上。理论依然只是理论。|{partner} 在图书馆给你占了座，还把你的书摞好了。} {考试还有两个星期。|图书馆非常安静。|你们的膝盖在桌子底下一直碰到一起。}\n# en: Two students at a library table, books piled between them, one pretending to read while watching the other.\nscene [10169c] = 图书馆的桌旁，两个学生之间堆着书，一个假装在读，其实在看另一个。\n# en: Actually study\nopt.left.label [c324b5] = 认真学习\n# en: {You study, properly, in companionable silence. {partner} sighs once, theatrically. You both pass the next quiz.|Two hours of real work. {partner} quizzes you on the walk back, a little wistful that it was only studying, and you get them all right.|You keep your knees to yourself and your eyes on the page, mostly. {partner} stacked the books in the right order, and you get through all of them.}\nopt.left.say.1 [713429] = {你们在惬意的沉默中好好学习。{partner} 夸张地叹了一次气。你们俩下次小测都过了。|两个小时实打实的功课。回去的路上 {partner} 考你，有点遗憾这回真的只是学习，而你全答对了。|你管好自己的膝盖，眼睛也大多盯着书页。{partner} 把书按正确的顺序摞好了，你们全都读完了。}\n# en: Don't study at all\nopt.right.label [82fa8a] = 完全不学\n# en: {You pass notes, then drawings, then you're both thrown out of the library for laughing. Worth it.|Not a page is turned. The librarian moves you apart, which only makes it worse.|Your knees keep touching and neither of you moves. The books stay stacked exactly as {partner} left them.}\nopt.right.say.1 [3126aa] = {你们传纸条，然后传画，然后因为笑得太大声被一起赶出了图书馆。值了。|一页书都没翻。图书管理员把你们分开坐，结果更糟了。|你们的膝盖一直碰着，谁也没挪开。书还原样摞着，跟 {partner} 摆好时一模一样。}\n\n@event date_first_quarrel\n# en: A Quarrel\ntitle [719a6c] = 一次争吵\n# en: Your first real quarrel with {partner}. It started about something small, a missed supper, and became about something large before either of you noticed. Now you're on opposite ends of a bench and neither of you will speak first.\ntext [14e59c] = 你和 {partner} 第一次真正的争吵。起因是件小事，一顿错过的晚饭，在两人都没察觉的时候，变成了一件大事。现在你们各坐长椅一头，谁也不肯先开口。\n# en: Two students on opposite ends of a cold courtyard bench, not looking at each other, a dropped scarf between them.\nscene [ebd0c4] = 两个学生坐在冷清庭院里一张长椅的两端，互不相看，中间掉着一条围巾。\n# en: Speak first\nopt.left.label [e3b9df] = 先开口\n# en: \"I'm sorry,\" you say, and it costs you. {partner} looks at you, then shuffles along the bench. \"Me too.\" You talk it through until the bell. It's better after, and you both know how it's done now.\nopt.left.say.1 [9514e8] = “对不起，”你说，说出这句话不容易。{partner} 看着你，然后挪了过来。“我也是。”你们一直谈到钟响。之后好多了，你们俩也都知道该怎么和好了。\n# en: Wait for {them}\nopt.right.label [bbb6ab] = 等 {them} 先开口\n# en: You wait. So does {partner}. You leave separately. The next day you both pretend it didn't happen, and it lies between you like the scarf on the bench.\nopt.right.say.1 [efe5a6] = 你等着。{partner} 也等着。你们各自离开。第二天你们都假装什么也没发生，它横在你们之间，就像长椅上那条围巾。\n\n@event date_meet_friends\n# en: Meeting the Friends\ntitle [92ece0] = 见朋友\n# en: {friend} and {partner} have heard a great deal about each other, all from you, and now they're at the same supper table. {friend} is being very polite. {partner} is being very polite back. You are dying.\ntext [91a7b1] = {friend} 和 {partner} 从你嘴里听说了对方很多事，现在他们坐在同一张晚餐桌旁。{friend} 非常有礼貌。{partner} 也非常有礼貌地回应。你快死了。\n# en: Three students around a refectory table, two of them sizing each other up over the bread basket, one in the middle smiling too hard.\nscene [1fa419] = 膳堂桌旁的三个学生，其中两个隔着面包篮互相打量，夹在中间的那个笑得太用力。\n# en: Steer the talk to something they share\nopt.left.label [6a05ef] = 把话题引向他们的共同点\n# en: You find it on the third try: a shared loathing of Thursday's soup. By pudding they're ganging up on you, which is exactly what you wanted.\nopt.left.say.1 [988245] = 试到第三次你找到了：两人都痛恨星期四的汤。到吃甜点时，他们已经联手对付你了，这正是你想要的。\n# en: Every subject dies. They're kind about it, both of them, and the silence is very long. \"Well,\" says {friend}, afterwards. \"They're nice.\" It's not quite a compliment.\nopt.left.say.2 [055ea9] = 每个话题都冷了场。他们俩对此都很客气，沉默非常漫长。“嗯，”事后 {friend} 说，“人挺好的。”这不完全是夸奖。\n# en: Let them find their own way\nopt.right.label [a54eb8] = 让他们自己找到相处之道\n# en: You eat your supper and let them circle each other. By the end they've agreed on nothing except that you talk in your sleep. You never find out how {partner} knew that and {friend} did.\nopt.right.say.1 [bde158] = 你吃着晚饭，让他们互相试探。到最后，他们除了一致认为你说梦话之外，什么都没达成共识。你始终没弄明白 {partner} 和 {friend} 是怎么知道的。\n\n@event date_rainy_afternoon\n# en: Rained In\ntitle [95b599] = 被雨困住\n# en: {Rain all afternoon, and {partner} has found the window seat in the west tower, a blanket and an old board game with half the pieces.|Rain again, and {partner} has claimed the window seat for the two of you.|Rain, a blanket, and a game whose missing pieces someone has replaced with ones carved from soap.} {The rain is loud on the leads.|The tea is too hot.|The game's rules are mostly invented.}\ntext [4bfc24] = {下了一下午的雨，{partner} 找到了西塔的窗边座位、一条毯子和一副缺了一半棋子的旧棋盘游戏。|又下雨了，{partner} 替你们俩占好了窗边座位。|雨、毯子，还有一副游戏，缺的棋子被人用肥皂刻的补上了。} {雨打在铅皮屋顶上很响。|茶太烫了。|游戏规则大多是编的。}\n# en: Rain streaming down a tall window seat, two students under one blanket with a board game between them, cups of tea steaming.\nscene [ce0ce8] = 雨水顺着高高的窗户淌下，窗边座位上两个学生裹着一条毯子，中间摆着棋盘，茶杯冒着热气。\n# en: Play to win\nopt.left.label [6c2256] = 认真求胜\n# en: {You win, ruthlessly. {partner} demands a rematch and wins that. Neither of you will ever forget the score.|You cheat; {partner} cheats better. The game ends in a pillow fight and a broken teacup.|You invent a rule; {partner} invents a better one. A soap piece wins in the end, and you both agree it earned it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [37cbd3] = {你赢了，毫不留情。{partner} 要求再来一局，然后赢了。你们俩永远都不会忘记这个比分。|你作弊；{partner} 作弊作得更好。游戏以一场枕头大战和一只摔碎的茶杯告终。|你发明了一条规则；{partner} 发明了一条更好的。最后赢的是一枚肥皂棋子，你们俩一致认为它实至名归。}\n# en: Forget the game\nopt.right.label [13db77] = 忘了游戏\n# en: {The game is abandoned by the second move. You talk about your families until the rain stops and the room is dark and you're late for supper.|You fall asleep under the blanket and wake up cold and late and very happy.|You carve another soap piece together while the rain goes on. Nobody can say what it was meant to be, and {partner} keeps it anyway.}\nopt.right.say.1 [6073d4] = {第二步游戏就被抛在了一边。你们聊着各自的家人，直到雨停，屋里黑了，晚饭也迟到了。|你们在毯子底下睡着了，醒来时又冷又晚，非常快乐。|雨一直下，你们一起又刻了一枚肥皂棋子。谁也说不清它本来该是什么，{partner} 还是留下了它。}\n\n@event date_forgotten_day\n# en: The Day You Forgot\ntitle [58f7f4] = 你忘了的日子\n# en: {partner} hands you a small parcel, wrapped carefully. \"It's our day,\" {they} say{s}. Apparently there is a day. You didn't know. You have nothing. {partner} sees you realise it.\ntext [24f954] = {partner} 递给你一个小包裹，包得很仔细。“今天是我们的日子，”{they}说。原来还有这么个日子。你不知道。你什么也没准备。{partner} 看见你反应过来了。\n# en: A student holding a small wrapped present out to another student, whose hands are empty and whose face has just fallen.\nscene [cac280] = 一个学生把一个小小的包好的礼物递向另一个学生，对方两手空空，脸刚刚垮下来。\n# en: Confess and make it up\nopt.left.label [112bdb] = 坦白，然后补救\n# en: \"I forgot,\" you say. {They} laugh{s}, a little hurt. That evening you turn up with flowers from the village and a terrible poem, and the poem does it.\nopt.left.say.1 [d71455] = “我忘了，”你说。{They} 笑了，有一点受伤。那天晚上你带着从村里买来的花和一首糟糕的诗出现，是那首诗打动了 {them}。\n# en: Pretend your gift is \"coming\"\nopt.right.label [d78c81] = 假装你的礼物“在路上”\n# en: You run to the village before the shops shut and spend more than you meant on something you hope looks planned. {partner} believes you, or kindly pretends to.\nopt.right.say.1 [f03c99] = 你赶在商店关门前跑到村里，花了比预想更多的钱，买了一件希望看起来像是早有准备的东西。{partner} 相信了你，或者好心地装作相信。\n# en: The shops are shut. Your gift \"is delayed\" for a week. {partner} doesn't ask about it again, and that is worse than asking.\nopt.right.say.2 [bd2585] = 商店都关门了。你的礼物“延误了”一个星期。{partner} 再也没问起过，这比问起更糟。\n\n@event date_dance_lesson\n# en: Learning to Dance\ntitle [2d93c7] = 学跳舞\n# en: With the {month == 4: Midwinter Ball|Starfall dance} coming, {partner} has decided you're going to learn properly, in an empty classroom, with the chairs pushed back. {~You start on the dance with the spin.|\"Again,\" says {partner}. \"From the turn.\"|Somebody is watching from the door.|Your feet have opinions.|There's no music, only counting.}\ntext [bab42b] = {month == 4: 仲冬舞会|星坠节舞会}快到了，{partner} 决定要你好好学一学，在一间空教室里，椅子都推到了墙边。{~你们从那支有转圈的舞开始。|“再来，”{partner} 说。“从转身开始。”|有人在门口偷看。|你的脚自有主张。|没有音乐，只有数拍子。}\n# en: Two students practising dance steps in an empty classroom, chairs pushed back, one counting aloud and the other looking at their feet.\nscene [ba646d] = 空教室里两个学生在练舞步，椅子推到一边，一个大声数着拍子，另一个盯着自己的脚。\n# en: Practise until it's right\nopt.left.label [efeeed] = 练到对为止\n# en: {~By the end of the hour you can do the turn without looking. {partner} says \"There,\" very softly, and doesn't let go straight away.|Your feet ache, and you can dance. Nearly.|Whoever is watching from the door claps when you finally get the turn. {partner} bows to them, and then to you.|Your feet stop arguing about halfway through the hour. By the end you can do the turn, and {partner} is insufferably pleased.|You count until the counting turns into a tune, and the turn comes out right on the last beat.}\nopt.left.say.1 [16140f] = {~一个小时结束时，你已经能不看脚就转身了。{partner} 很轻地说了声“对了”，没有马上松手。|你的脚很疼，但你会跳舞了。差不多吧。|你终于学会转身时，门口那个看热闹的人鼓起了掌。{partner} 向那人鞠了一躬，然后向你。|练到半个钟头，你的脚不再闹别扭了。结束时你已经会转身了，{partner} 得意得叫人受不了。|你数着拍子，数到拍子变成了曲调，转身正好落在最后一拍上。}\n# en: Make it a joke\nopt.right.label [9667e2] = 当成玩笑\n# en: {~You invent a new dance, mostly flailing. {partner} laughs so hard {they} ha{s} to sit down, then joins in.|You never learn the turn. You learn that {partner} snorts when {they} laugh{s}, which is better.|You play it up for whoever is watching at the door. They laugh, {partner} laughs, and nobody learns the turn.|You let your feet do what they like. {partner} copies them exactly, and it becomes a dance of its own.|You count in the wrong order on purpose. {partner} loses count laughing, and that's the end of the lesson.}\nopt.right.say.1 [8c4cea] = {~你发明了一种新舞，主要是乱扑腾。{partner} 笑得不得不坐下来，然后也加入了。|你始终没学会那个转身。你学到的是 {partner} 笑起来会打鼻响，这更好。|你冲着门口那个看热闹的人卖力表演。那人笑了，{partner} 也笑了，谁也没学会转身。|你由着自己的脚乱来。{partner} 一步不差地跟着学，结果成了一支自成一派的舞。|你故意把拍子数乱。{partner} 笑得数不下去，这堂课就此结束。}\n\n@event date_after_graduation\n# en: After\ntitle [dd3a69] = 以后\n# en: On the castle wall, with the light going long over the valley, {partner} asks the question neither of you has said out loud: \"After graduation. What happens to us?\" The valley below is gold. {They} {are} not looking at you, very deliberately.\ntext [070535] = 城墙上，光线在山谷上拉得长长的，{partner} 问出了你们俩都没说出口的那个问题：“毕业以后。我们怎么办？”下面的山谷一片金黄。{They} 很刻意地没有看你。\n# en: Two students sitting on the castle wall at sunset, legs dangling, the whole valley below them gold.\nscene [b579db] = 日落时分，两个学生坐在城墙上，双腿悬空，下方整个山谷一片金黄。\n# en: \"We go together.\"\nopt.left.label [1a21cd] = “我们一起走。”\n# en: {They} let{s} out a breath {they}'d been holding for months. You plan it there and then, badly: a city, a cottage, a list of rules for the kitchen. It doesn't matter that it's badly. It's yours.\nopt.left.say.1 [0e5dc5] = {They} 长长地呼出一口憋了好几个月的气。你们当场就规划起来，规划得很糟：一座城市，一间小屋，一张厨房规矩清单。规划得糟不要紧。那是你们的。\n# en: \"Let's not decide yet.\"\nopt.right.label [7da709] = “先别决定。”\n# en: \"Not yet,\" {they} agree{s}, too quickly, and takes your hand. You watch the gold go grey. It's honest, and it's lonelier than the other answer.\nopt.right.say.1 [726a88] = “先不急，”{they} 答应得太快，然后握住了你的手。你们看着金色褪成灰色。这很诚实，也比另一个答案更孤单。\n\n@event lyra_date_dawn_duel\n# en: A Duel for Two\ntitle [0910cb] = 两个人的决斗\n# en: {Lyra's idea of a romantic afternoon is a duel in the empty courtyard, \"to keep you sharp\".|Another courtyard duel. Lyra has started calling them \"our afternoons\".|Lyra is already in the courtyard, wand out, sleeves rolled.} {She's brought coffee for after.|She's in a terrible mood, which means she slept well.|\"No holding back,\" she says. \"I'll know.\"}\ntext [a39eb6] = {莱拉心目中浪漫的下午，是在空荡的庭院里来一场决斗，“让你保持敏锐”。|又一场庭院决斗。莱拉开始管它们叫“我们的下午”了。|莱拉已经在庭院里了，魔杖在手，袖子卷起。} {她带了咖啡，留着决斗后喝。|她心情糟透了，这说明她睡得很好。|“不许留手，”她说。“我会知道的。”}\n# en: Two duellists bowing in a misty courtyard at dawn, wands lowered, both trying not to smile.\nscene [faa89f] = 雾气弥漫的黎明庭院里，两名决斗者垂下魔杖互相鞠躬，都在努力憋着不笑。\n# en: Duel her properly\nopt.left.label [356d49] = 认真和她决斗\n# en: {You win. Lyra stares at you as if you'd proposed. \"Again,\" she says, breathless, and then, \"Tomorrow.\" It's the best thing anyone has ever said to you.|A clean win. She shakes your hand, then doesn't let go of it.|No holding back, so you don't, and you win. She grins as if she'd won herself, and demands you show her how.}\nopt.left.say.1 [de4d37] = {你赢了。莱拉盯着你，好像你刚向她求了婚。“再来，”她喘着气说，然后，“明天。”这是有人对你说过的最好的话。|一场干净利落的胜利。她和你握手，然后没有松开。|不许留手，你就没留，然后赢了。她笑得好像赢的是她自己，非要你教她是怎么做到的。}\n# en: {She wins, because she always does, and then sits beside you on the cold steps with the coffee and explains your mistake, very gently, for her.|You lose. She bandages your knuckle herself and pretends it's an inspection.|No holding back on either side. She wins, just, and admits on the steps that it was closer than she'll ever tell anyone else.}\nopt.left.say.2 [32f32b] = {她赢了，因为她总是赢，然后和你一起坐在冰冷的台阶上喝咖啡，讲解你的失误，以她的标准来说非常温柔。|你输了。她亲手给你包扎指关节，假装这是一次检查。|两边都没留手。她险胜，坐在台阶上承认，这一场比她会对任何别人承认的都要接近。}\n# en: Let her win, and tell her so\nopt.right.label [6e593e] = 让她赢，并告诉她\n# en: {She knows the moment you drop your guard. \"Don't you dare,\" she says, and then kisses you, furious, and then makes you do it again properly.|She's outraged. She's also, for the rest of the day, unusually soft.|\"I said I'd know,\" she says, the moment you drop your guard. She makes you start again, and this time you don't dare.}\nopt.right.say.1 [aaeac7] = {你一放下防守，她就知道了。“你敢，”她说，然后吻了你，气冲冲的，然后逼你好好再打一遍。|她气坏了。可她这一整天也格外温柔。|你一放下防守，她就说：“我说过我会知道的。”她让你重新开始，这一次你不敢了。}\n\n@event tobias_date_greenhouse_supper\n# en: Lunch Among the Tomatoes\ntitle [5ce58a] = 番茄丛中的午餐\n# en: {Tobias has cooked lunch in the greenhouse, at a table between the tomato rows, with everything he grew himself.|Lunch in the greenhouse again. The tomatoes have names now.|Tobias has added a second candle and a jar of wildflowers.} {The soup is his grandmother's recipe.|A tomato plant is leaning in to listen.|There is far too much bread.}\ntext [e1963e] = {托比亚斯在温室里做了午饭，桌子摆在番茄垄之间，所有东西都是他亲手种的。|又是温室里的午饭。番茄们现在都有名字了。|托比亚斯多点了一根蜡烛，还摆了一罐野花。} {汤是他祖母的配方。|一株番茄凑过来偷听。|面包多得离谱。}\n# en: A candlelit table set up between rows of tomato plants in a warm greenhouse at night, a big student serving soup from a pot.\nscene [128ebb] = 夜里温暖的温室中，番茄垄之间摆着一张烛光餐桌，一个大个子学生正从锅里盛汤。\n# en: Eat everything, and ask for seconds\nopt.left.label [0c79a4] = 全吃光，再要一份\n# en: {You eat three bowls and he beams like the sun. He tells you about the farm, the goats, the year the river froze. You could listen for ever, and nearly do.|Seconds, thirds, a slice of pie. Tobias watches you eat with an expression you want to keep in a jar.|You eat until the bread is gone, which takes some doing. Tobias lights the second candle off the first and says he'll bake less next time. He won't.}\nopt.left.say.1 [d65a7f] = {你吃了三碗，他笑得像太阳。他给你讲农场、山羊、河水结冰的那一年。你可以一直听下去，也差点就这么做了。|第二份、第三份、一块派。托比亚斯看你吃饭的表情，你想装进罐子里留着。|你一直吃到面包见底，这可不容易。托比亚斯用第一支蜡烛点亮了第二支，说下回少烤点。他不会的。}\n# en: Help him in the beds after\nopt.right.label [b0cee6] = 饭后帮他打理苗床\n# en: {You weed the beds in the afternoon sun, side by side. Your back aches; your hands smell of tomato leaves. He hums the whole time.|An hour of planting. He shows you how to tell a seedling it's doing well. You feel ridiculous, and do it anyway, until your knees ache from the kneeling.|You work off the bread in the beds after. He picks you a wildflower for the jar and pretends it was a weed.}\nopt.right.say.1 [72f734] = {你们在午后的阳光下并肩除草。你的背很酸，手上满是番茄叶的气味。他一直哼着歌。|种了一个小时的苗。他教你怎么告诉一株幼苗它长得很好。你觉得自己很可笑，但还是照做了，一直跪到膝盖发疼。|吃完你们去苗床干活，把面包消化掉。他给你摘了一朵野花插进罐子里，假装那是杂草。}\n\n@event seraphine_date_a_future\n# en: A Future She Saw\ntitle [d092d0] = 她看见的未来\n# en: {Seraphine says she's seen a future with the two of you in it, and wants to tell you about it on the observatory floor, under the shuttered dome.|Another future, another hour on the observatory floor.|\"I saw us again,\" Seraphine says, lying down beside you. \"Older.\"} {She's shuffling the cards without looking.|The dome's painted stars have faded to grey.|She is, for once, entirely in the present.}\ntext [0a231d] = {瑟拉芬说她看见了一个有你们俩的未来，想在观星台合拢的穹顶下、躺在地板上讲给你听。|又一个未来，又一个钟头躺在观星台的地板上。|“我又看见我们了，”瑟拉芬在你身边躺下说。“老了以后。”} {她看都不看地洗着牌。|穹顶上画的星星已经褪成了灰色。|难得一次，她完完全全地活在当下。}\n# en: A seer and a student lying on the observatory floor beneath the open dome, a scatter of tarot cards between them.\nscene [1af1ef] = 一位预言者和一个学生躺在观星台敞开的穹顶下的地板上，两人之间散落着塔罗牌。\n# en: Ask her to tell you\nopt.left.label [fb782a] = 请她讲给你听\n# en: {She tells you. A kitchen, a cat, a terrible argument about curtains, a garden. It's so ordinary you could cry. She says it's her favourite one.|A future with snow in it, and an old friend who comes to stay and never leaves. She stops halfway and holds your hand.|She tells you about the older two of you: slower, quieter, still arguing about curtains. She laughs at her own future and holds your hand all the way through it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [b5d4e3] = {她讲了：一间厨房，一只猫，一场关于窗帘的激烈争吵，一座花园。平凡得让你想哭。她说这是她最喜欢的一个。|一个下着雪的未来，还有一位来做客、再也没走的老朋友。她讲到一半停下，握住了你的手。|她给你讲年老的你们俩：慢一些，安静一些，还在为窗帘吵架。她笑着讲自己的未来，从头到尾握着你的手。}\n# en: Ask her not to\nopt.right.label [5b1e61] = 请她别讲\n# en: {\"Let it surprise us,\" you say. She looks at you as if no one has ever asked her that. Then she puts the cards away, all of them.|She's quiet a while, then laughs. \"You're the only person I can't read,\" she says. \"It's very restful.\"|\"Then don't tell me,\" you say. She doesn't. For a whole hour she's only here, with you, and that's the best future yet.}\nopt.right.say.1 [eb3e1f] = {“让它给我们一个惊喜吧，”你说。她看着你，好像从来没有人这样要求过她。然后她把牌收了起来，全部。|她沉默了一会儿，然后笑了。“你是我唯一读不透的人，”她说。“这很让人放松。”|“那就别告诉我，”你说。她没说。整整一个小时，她只在这里，和你在一起，这是目前为止最好的未来。}\n\n@event corvin_date_rooftop_supper\n# en: Supper on the Roof\ntitle [5ad156] = 屋顶晚餐\n# en: {Corvin leads you through three passages and a window to a rooftop between chimneys, where a tablecloth and supper are waiting.|Another rooftop, another tablecloth. You don't ask where he finds them.|\"New roof,\" says Corvin. \"Better view. Mostly legal.\"} {There are pastries you don't recognise.|There's a candle in a jam jar.|He's brought cushions, which is unlike him.}\ntext [084c33] = {科尔文领你穿过三条通道和一扇窗户，来到烟囱之间的一片屋顶，那儿铺着桌布，晚餐已经备好了。|又一片屋顶，又一块桌布。你没问他从哪儿找来的。|“新屋顶，”科尔文说。“视野更好。基本合法。”} {有些酥皮点心你认不出来路。|果酱罐里点着一根蜡烛。|他带了靠垫，这可不像他。}\n# en: A tablecloth spread on a castle rooftop between chimneys, a candle in a jar, two students eating pastries above the sleeping towers.\nscene [3fb767] = 城堡屋顶的烟囱之间铺着一块桌布，罐子里点着蜡烛，两个学生在沉睡的塔楼上方吃着酥皮点心。\n# en: Ask where the supper came from\nopt.left.label [7accc3] = 问晚餐是哪儿来的\n# en: {\"Borrowed,\" he says. From the headmistress's kitchen. You eat it anyway, and it tastes of getting away with something. He grins all evening.|\"Don't ask, saint.\" You don't. The pastries are perfect.|\"Mostly legal,\" he repeats, when you ask. The supper is from the prefects' pantry. You eat it on his cushions and laugh until a window opens below.}\nopt.left.say.1 [9bb678] = {“借的，”他说。从院长的厨房借的。你还是吃了，吃起来有一种逃过一劫的味道。他咧嘴笑了一整晚。|“别问，圣人。”你没问。点心完美无缺。|你一问，他就重复：“基本合法。”晚饭是从级长们的食品柜里来的。你们坐在他的垫子上吃，笑到楼下有扇窗户打开了。}\n# en: Just enjoy it\nopt.right.label [a859b1] = 尽情享受\n# en: {You eat supper on the roof until the candle gutters and the cold comes up through the slates. He doesn't make a single joke for a whole hour, and when you notice, he kisses you to stop you saying so.|The chimneys are warm, the air is cold, and you climb down at midnight scraped and happy.|You lean back on the cushions and watch the towers go dark one by one. He says nothing clever all evening, and you like him better for it.}\nopt.right.say.1 [c980c1] = {你们在屋顶上吃到蜡烛燃尽，寒气从石板瓦里冒上来。整整一个小时他一个笑话都没讲，你注意到的时候，他吻了你，不让你说出来。|烟囱是暖的，空气是冷的，你们半夜爬下来，蹭破了皮，满心欢喜。|你靠在垫子上，看着塔楼一座座暗下去。他一整晚没说一句俏皮话，你反而更喜欢他了。}\n\n@event mir_date_invention\n# en: They Built You Something\ntitle [bcced4] = TA 给你做了个东西\n# en: {Mir has built you a present, a brass bird that sings your name, mostly.|Another present from Mir's bench, ticking ominously.|Mir presents their newest invention with a flourish and a fire bucket.} {It's warm to the touch.|It has seven moving parts and one they don't explain.|\"It's probably safe,\" they say.}\ntext [4cafd6] = {米尔给你做了一件礼物，一只会唱你名字的黄铜鸟，大致会唱。|米尔工作台上的又一件礼物，嘀嗒作响，不太妙。|米尔挥手一扬，展示 TA 的最新发明，旁边备着一只消防桶。} {摸上去是温的。|它有七个活动部件，还有一个 TA 不肯解释。|“应该是安全的，”TA 说。}\n# en: A cluttered workshop bench, a small brass contraption with wings sitting on it, a grinning inventor and a nervous student leaning away.\nscene [3fca62] = 杂乱的工作台上放着一个带翅膀的小小黄铜装置，一个咧嘴笑的发明家，和一个紧张地往后仰的学生。\n# en: Wind it up at once\nopt.left.label [5a2193] = 马上上发条\n# en: {It sings your name, slightly wrong, all the way round the workshop, and lands in your hand. Mir bursts into happy tears and pretends it's soot.|It works. It works! They kiss you on the cheek and immediately start on version two.|It's probably safe, and for once it is. It hums a tune you both know and settles on your shoulder. Mir puts the fire bucket down, astonished.}\nopt.left.say.1 [9a7283] = {它绕着工坊唱着你的名字，稍微有点走调，然后落在你手里。米尔喜极而泣，假装是被煤灰熏的。|成功了。成功了！TA 亲了一下你的脸颊，立刻开始做第二版。|它大概是安全的，这一次还真是。它哼起一支你们俩都会的曲子，落在你肩上。米尔放下灭火桶，目瞪口呆。}\n# en: {It sings for three seconds and then explodes, gently, into springs. Mir is mortified. You laugh until they do too, and help them pick up every piece.|It sets your sleeve on fire. Mir has the bucket ready. \"Version two,\" they say grimly.|It's probably not safe after all. The fire bucket earns its keep, and Mir chalks \"probably\" on the bench as a warning to themself.}\nopt.left.say.2 [952817] = {它唱了三秒，然后轻轻地炸成了一堆弹簧。米尔羞愧极了。你笑到 TA 也笑了，然后帮 TA 把每一个零件捡起来。|它把你的袖子点着了。米尔的水桶早就备好了。“第二版，”TA 阴沉地说。|结果它大概并不安全。灭火桶派上了用场，米尔在工作台上用粉笔写下“大概”两个字，提醒自己。}\n# en: Ask them how it works first\nopt.right.label [0e03e7] = 先问 TA 它是怎么工作的\n# en: {They explain for an hour, with diagrams. You understand a third of it and love all of it. You wind it up together, very carefully, and it works.|Two hours of explanation and three cups of tea. The bird sings at the end. You've never been so proud of a thing you didn't make.|They explain why it's probably safe, at length, with the fire bucket close by. It is. You wind it up together, and it works.}\nopt.right.say.1 [f66e3f] = {TA 配着图解讲了一个小时。你听懂了三分之一，却爱上了全部。你们一起小心翼翼地上好发条，它成功了。|两个小时的讲解，三杯茶。最后那只鸟唱起了歌。你从没为一件不是自己做的东西这么骄傲过。|TA 把它为什么大概安全讲了很久，灭火桶就放在手边。它确实安全。你们一起上好发条，它成功了。}\n\n@event kael_date_snow\n# en: Snow, Just for You\ntitle [63bfb8] = 只为你下的雪\n# en: {Kael leads you to a corner of the lawn and makes it snow, only there, only on the two of you.|Another private snowfall. He's getting better at the shapes.|Kael has made it snow again. This time the flakes are stars.} {season == \"summer\": It's a hot day; the snow steams as it falls.|{The flakes are as big as coins.|Somewhere a bird is very confused.|It's the most he's said with his hands in weeks.}}\ntext [5b4dfd] = {凯尔领你走到草坪的一角，让雪落下来，只落在那里，只落在你们两个身上。|又一场私人的雪。他造的形状越来越好了。|凯尔又下雪了。这一次雪花是星星的形状。} {season == \"summer\": 天很热；雪一边落一边冒着热气。|{雪片大得像硬币。|某处有只鸟非常困惑。|这是他几个星期以来用双手说过最多的话。}}\n# en: Snow falling over one small patch of a spring lawn, two students standing in it, one holding out his hand to catch the flakes.\nscene [102324] = 雪落在春日草坪的一小块地方，两个学生站在其中，一个伸出手去接雪花。\n# en: Catch the flakes\nopt.left.label [0ecdf3] = 接雪花\n# en: {You catch them on your tongue until your teeth ache with cold, and laugh, and Kael, astonishingly, laughs too, out loud. The snow falls harder when he's happy, it turns out.|You're both soaked and frozen. He warms your hands in his, which have warmed, against all habit.|You catch a star-shaped flake on your glove and it doesn't melt for a whole minute. Kael watches it more than you do, and smiles when it goes.}\nopt.left.say.1 [4ceb1d] = {你用舌头接雪花，接到牙齿都冻疼了，笑了起来，而凯尔，令人惊讶地，也笑出了声。原来他开心的时候，雪会下得更大。|你们俩都湿透了，冻僵了。他用双手焐着你的手，那双手一反常态，是暖的。|你用手套接住一片星形的雪花，它整整一分钟都没化。凯尔看它看得比你还认真，它化掉时他笑了。}\n# en: Ask him what it costs\nopt.right.label [649ee9] = 问他这要付出什么\n# en: {\"Nothing,\" he says. Then, honestly, \"A little cold in the chest, for a day.\" You make him stop. He looks at you as if no one had ever asked him to.|He tells you about Northern frost-craft and what it takes. Then he makes one last snowflake, small, and lets you keep it.|\"Less, now,\" he says, which isn't an answer. You make him stop anyway, and he lets the last star land in your hair.}\nopt.right.say.1 [fec7eb] = {“什么都不用，”他说。然后，老实地：“胸口会冷上一天。”你让他停下。他看着你，好像从来没人这样要求过他。|他给你讲了北方的霜术，以及它需要付出什么。然后他造了最后一片雪花，小小的，让你留着。|“现在少一些了，”他说，这不算回答。你还是让他停了，他让最后一颗星落在你的头发上。}\n\n@event nessa_date_off_record\n# en: Off the Record\ntitle [a5d3b6] = 不留记录\n# en: {Nessa locks the Ledger office, puts her quill in a drawer and says, \"Off the record. For as long as you like.\"|Another hour off the record. The quill sulks in its drawer.|Nessa has started saying \"off the record\" when she means \"I love you\". You've noticed.} {There's a pot of tea and a plate of toast.|The proofs are stacked and, for once, ignored.|It's raining on the skylight.}\ntext [84fbca] = {内莎锁上《流明纪事报》编辑部的门，把羽毛笔放进抽屉，说：“不留记录。想待多久都行。”|又一个不留记录的钟头。羽毛笔在抽屉里生闷气。|内莎开始在想说“我爱你”的时候说“不留记录”了。你注意到了。} {有一壶茶和一盘吐司。|校样摞得整整齐齐，难得一次被冷落在一边。|雨落在天窗上。}\n# en: A newspaper office late at night, an enchanted quill lying still on a desk for once, two students sharing a pot of tea among the proofs.\nscene [c29441] = 深夜的报社编辑部，一支施了魔法的羽毛笔难得安静地躺在桌上，两个学生在校样堆里分享一壶茶。\n# en: Ask her about herself\nopt.left.label [a87cd5] = 问她自己的事\n# en: {She talks about her childhood for an hour, which she never does. A printing shop, a father who read the news aloud. You don't take a single note. She notices.|Nessa, off the record, is quieter and funnier. You stay until the lamp runs out.|You ask about her, and she says \"off the record\" first, and then, quietly, a great deal. The rain on the skylight keeps it between the two of you.}\nopt.left.say.1 [1eb01c] = {她讲了一个小时自己的童年，这是她从来不做的事：一家印刷铺，一个会把新闻大声读出来的父亲。你一个字的笔记都没记。她注意到了。|不留记录的内莎更安静，也更有趣。你一直待到灯油耗尽。|你问起她的事，她先说了句“不留记录”，然后轻声说了很多。天窗上的雨声把这些话留在了你们俩之间。}\n# en: Tell her a secret of your own\nopt.right.label [06d39a] = 告诉她一个你自己的秘密\n# en: {You tell her something nobody knows. She holds it like it's the most valuable story she's ever had, and never writes it down.|She listens, and then tells you one back. Off the record. It feels like a promise.|You tell her your secret. She says \"off the record\" back, and you both know what she means.}\nopt.right.say.1 [feb578] = {你告诉了她一件没人知道的事。她像捧着自己得到过的最珍贵的故事那样捧着它，从没写下来。|她听完，也告诉了你一个。不留记录。这感觉像一个承诺。|你把秘密告诉了她。她回了一句“不留记录”，你们俩都知道她是什么意思。}\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/pools/grounds.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/pools/grounds.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event lakeshore_shore_walk\n# en: The Long Way Round\ntitle [250a85] = 绕远路\n# en: {You came out to walk alone. {date} finds you on the shore path and falls into step without asking.|The shore path is where you go to think. Today {date} has the same idea.|You know every stone of the shore path by now. Today {date} walks it with you.} {season == \"winter\": The lake is frozen at the edges and your breath walks ahead of you.|{season == \"summer\": The water is warm and full of sky.|{Mist lies on the water like a sheet someone forgot to fold.|The towers stand upside down in the lake, lit window by lit window.|The water is so still that a dropped leaf makes rings for a minute.}}}\ntext [8ddf61] = {你本想独自走走。{date}在湖岸小径上遇见你，没问一声就和你并肩走了起来。|湖岸小径是你想事情的地方。今天{date}也是这么想的。|湖岸小径上的每块石头你都认得了。今天{date}陪你走。} {season == \"winter\": 湖边结了冰，你的呼吸走在你前面。|{season == \"summer\": 湖水温暖，盛满了天空。|{雾铺在水面上，像一张忘了叠的床单。|七塔倒映在湖里，一扇亮窗接着一扇亮窗。|湖面静得出奇，一片落叶荡起的涟漪要一分钟才平。}}}\n# en: Two figures walking a lakeside path at dusk, the towers reflected upside down in the still water beside them.\nscene [33361d] = 黄昏时两个人影走在湖边小径上，身旁静水里倒映着颠倒的高塔。\n# en: Walk the long way round\nopt.left.label [793774] = 绕远路走一圈\n# en: {final_year: Halfway round, {date} says \"next year\" and stops, because there won't be one here. You walk the rest closer together.|{season == \"winter\": {You walk until your toes go numb. {date} lends you {their} scarf and pretends not to be cold.|The ice sings under your boots. {date} takes your arm on the slippery stretch and forgets to let go.|You walk the frozen edge in step. {date} counts the herons out loud, and you let {them} lose count.}|{You walk until the far lamps go out. Neither of you mentions the time.|{date} points out a lit tower window and tells you who lives behind it. Half of it is invented. You don't mind which half.|You stand at the water's edge in silence, and the silence is the good kind.}}}\nopt.left.say.1 [8dd7fc] = {final_year: 走到一半，{date}说了句“明年”，又停住了，因为在这里不会再有明年。剩下的路，你们靠得更近。|{season == \"winter\": {你们一直走到你的脚趾冻僵。{date}把围巾借给你，假装自己不冷。|冰在你们靴子下歌唱。走到滑的那段，{date}挽住你的胳膊，忘了松开。|你们沿着结冰的岸边并肩走。{date}大声数着苍鹭，你由着{them}数乱。}|{你们一直走到对岸的灯火一盏盏熄灭。谁也没提时间。|{date}指着一扇亮着的塔窗，告诉你里面住的是谁。一半是编的。你不在乎是哪一半。|你们在水边默默站着，那是很好的那种沉默。}}}\n# en: Talk about anything but us\nopt.right.label [9c9439] = 聊什么都行，别聊我们\n# en: {You talk about exams, the cook and the weather. It's easy. It's also not what either of you came for.|You argue cheerfully about whether the lake has a bottom. It's a good walk. It's only a walk.|You talk about the lake, the cook and everything except the thing. It gets easier to leave it unsaid, which is not a good sign.}\nopt.right.say.1 [b9b89b] = {你们聊考试、聊厨娘、聊天气。很轻松。也不是你们俩来这里想说的。|你们兴致勃勃地争论这湖到底有没有底。散步很愉快。也只是散步。|你们聊湖、聊厨娘，什么都聊，除了那件事。不说出口越来越容易了，这可不是好兆头。}\n\n@event lakeshore_cold_swim\n# en: The Cold Swim\ntitle [242756] = 冷水游泳\n# en: {season == \"winter\": Students in towels are daring each other into the lake through a hole in the ice.|{season == \"summer\": Half the academy is in the lake; the other half pretends not to want to be.|A few hardy students are swimming out to the flat rock, shrieking.}} {The water looks inviting. It always does.|You know how cold the Mirror Lake really is. You're tempted anyway.|You and the lake have an understanding. It doesn't help.}\ntext [be4c03] = {season == \"winter\": 几个裹着毛巾的学生正互相怂恿，要从冰窟窿里跳进湖去。|{season == \"summer\": 半个学院都泡在湖里，另一半在假装自己不想下水。|几个不怕冷的学生正往平顶岩游去，一路尖叫。}} {湖水看上去很诱人。它一向如此。|你知道镜湖实际上有多冷。可你还是心动了。|你和这片湖之间有某种默契。这并没有用。}\n# en: Students in towels on a lakeshore, one wading in up to the knees and shrieking, the towers reflected behind them.\nscene [7cec80] = 湖岸上裹着毛巾的学生们，一个人蹚水到膝盖，正在尖叫，身后倒映着高塔。\n# en: Brave the water\nopt.left.label [5f5f4d] = 下水试试\n# en: {season == \"winter\": You go under, come up roaring, and are hauled out a hero.|{You slap the flat rock, whoop, and swim back to applause from people with dry hair.|You reach the rock first. The cold reaches you second, but you were already winning.|You swim out to the rock and back without thinking about it. That's the trick, it turns out.}}\nopt.left.say.1 [3a2e8d] = {season == \"winter\": 你沉下去，吼着冒出头，被当成英雄拖上了岸。|{你一掌拍上平顶岩，欢呼一声，在一群头发干爽的人的掌声中游了回来。|你第一个碰到岩石。寒冷第二个碰到你，可你已经赢了。|你想都没想就游到岩石那儿又游了回来。原来诀窍就是这个。}}\n# en: {You come back blue to the lips and sneezing. Sister Amaranth sighs before you've even sat down.|Halfway out, the lake reminds you who is in charge. You turn back with what dignity you have left.|The lake wins again. Sister Amaranth has a blanket ready before you've reached the shore.}\nopt.left.say.2 [8c811e] = {你回来时嘴唇发青、喷嚏连天。你还没坐下，阿玛兰斯修女就先叹了口气。|游到一半，湖水提醒你谁才是主人。你带着仅剩的体面掉头回来。|湖又赢了。你还没上岸，阿玛兰斯修女就已经备好了毯子。}\n# en: Hold the towels and heckle\nopt.right.label [a0e0ba] = 帮忙拿毛巾，顺便起哄\n# en: {You announce every dive like a herald. You're dry, popular and a tiny bit jealous.|You keep the towels warm and the commentary warmer. Someone throws a wet sock at you, which is fair.|You keep the towels and the score. The swimmers argue with your score, which means they trust it.}\nopt.right.say.1 [80a183] = {你像传令官一样播报每一次跳水。你很干爽、很受欢迎，也有一点点嫉妒。|你把毛巾捂得暖暖的，解说更热烈。有人朝你扔了一只湿袜子，这很公平。|你管着毛巾，也管着记分。游泳的人跟你争比分，说明他们信得过你。}\n\n@event duel_hall_dummies\n# en: The Practice Dummies\ntitle [01b66d] = 练习假人\n# en: {The near end of the hall belongs to the practice dummies, who watch you come in. The one with the painted moustache looks especially unforgiving.|The dummy with the moustache is still here, with a new scorch mark and, you'd swear, a new grudge.|The moustached dummy greets you with its usual silence. Someone has given it an eyepatch.} {The floorboards are still warm from the last session.|A singed glove lies abandoned on the rack.|Rain drums on the high windows.}\ntext [4bdf3a] = {大厅近端归练习假人所有，它们看着你进门。那个画了小胡子的看起来格外不肯原谅人。|画小胡子的假人还在，多了一道焦痕，你敢发誓，还多了一份怨气。|小胡子假人照例沉默地迎接你。有人给它戴了个眼罩。} {地板还留着上一场练习的余温。|架子上丢着一只烧焦的手套。|雨点敲打着高窗。}\n# en: An empty duelling hall with scorched floorboards and a row of battered straw practice dummies, one with a painted moustache.\nscene [ddc84f] = 空荡的决斗厅，地板满是焦痕，一排破旧的草扎练习假人，其中一个画着小胡子。\n# en: Drill your hexes on it\nopt.left.label [74919a] = 拿它练咒\n# en: {Hex after hex until your wrists ache. The dummy loses a sleeve and none of its contempt.|You land a clean one at last. A wisp of straw floats down, slowly, like applause.|You knock the eyepatch clean off. The dummy looks more menacing without it, which you didn't plan for.}\nopt.left.say.1 [ec81dd] = {一道咒语接一道咒语，直到手腕发酸。假人掉了一只袖子，轻蔑却一分没少。|你终于打出一记干净利落的。一缕稻草慢慢飘落，像是掌声。|你一咒把眼罩打飞了。没了眼罩，那假人看着更凶了，这可不在你的计划里。}\n# en: Restuff the battered ones\nopt.right.label [218ba9] = 给破假人重新填草\n# en: {You haul a bale of straw over from the stables and restuff three dummies. They look almost grateful, which is unsettling.|You restuff the moustached one with special care. It still looks like it's planning something.|You restuff the moustached one and straighten its eyepatch. It's the best-dressed thing in the hall.}\nopt.right.say.1 [a942de] = {你从马厩扛来一捆稻草，给三个假人重新填好。它们看上去几乎心怀感激，这让人不安。|你格外用心地给小胡子假人填了草。它看上去还是在盘算着什么。|你给小胡子假人重新填了草，扶正了它的眼罩。它成了整个大厅里穿得最体面的。}\n\n@event duel_hall_gallery\n# en: From the Gallery\ntitle [f73290] = 看台之上\n# en: {From the gallery above the duelling floor you can watch whoever is practising. Today it's {here}.|You've learned to read duels from up here. Today {here} is drilling.|The gallery rail has a groove where you lean. Below, {here} is drilling.} {Sparks drift up to the gallery rail.|Somebody's familiar is asleep on the scoreboard.|The ceiling has acquired a new scorch mark.}\ntext [d11fa8] = {从决斗场上方的看台，你能看到谁在练习。今天是{here}。|你已经学会从这上面看懂决斗了。今天{here}在练。|看台栏杆上有一道你常倚的凹痕。下面，{here}在练。} {火星飘上看台的栏杆。|不知谁的使魔睡在记分板上。|天花板又添了一道新焦痕。}\n# en: A narrow wooden gallery above a duelling floor, one student watching another drill spells at a target below.\nscene [5292ba] = 决斗场上方狭窄的木制看台，一个学生看着下面另一个对着靶子练咒。\n# en: Offer to spar\nopt.left.label [ed03b2] = 提出陪练\n# en: {You take a round off {here}. {They} stare{s} at you for a long second, then ask{s} for another.|You win with a feint you stole from watching {here}. {They} notice{s}, and look{s} annoyed and pleased at once.|You spar with {here} as if you'd done it for years, because from the gallery, you have. {They} win{s} narrowly and look{s} at you differently.}\nopt.left.say.1 [0d7472] = {你从{here}手里拿下一局。{They}盯着你看了好一会儿，然后要求再来一局。|你用看{here}练习时偷学的虚招赢了。{They}看出来了，又恼又高兴。|你和{here}过招，就像练了好多年，在看台上你确实看了好多年。{They}险胜，看你的眼神不一样了。}\n# en: {{here} puts you on the floorboards, politely. From down there the ceiling's scorch marks make a sort of map.|{here} disarms you so neatly that the dummies seem to wince. Your wand skids under the gallery stairs.|{here} lands a spell that singes the ceiling and you with it. You add your own mark to the map.}\nopt.left.say.2 [96e65d] = {{here}很有礼貌地把你放倒在地板上。从下面看，天花板上的焦痕像是一张地图。|{here}缴械缴得干净利落，连假人都像是缩了一下。你的魔杖滑进了看台楼梯底下。|{here}一道咒语打得天花板焦了，你也一起焦了。你给那张“地图”添上了自己的一笔。}\n# en: Watch and take notes\nopt.right.label [d829ad] = 看着记笔记\n# en: {You fill a page with what {here} does with {their} feet. You'll never get to try it, and you know it.|You sketch {here}'s guard from above. On paper it has a gap. On the floor it doesn't.|You take notes from your groove at the rail. Your notes on {here} could fill a book now, and might.}\nopt.right.say.1 [0327ff] = {你把{here}的步法记了满满一页。你永远不会有机会试，你也知道。|你从上方画下{here}的防守架势。纸上有个破绽。场上没有。|你倚着栏杆上那道属于你的凹槽记笔记。你关于{here}的笔记已经能写成一本书了，说不定真会。}\n\n@event duel_hall_humming_circle\n# en: The Humming Circle\ntitle [1a4455] = 嗡鸣的法阵\n# en: {The evening bouts are over. At the near end of the hall the warding circle is still humming.|The warding circle is humming. Whoever's drilling at the far end isn't looking your way.|The circle hums. You know its tune by now.} {final_year: Soon someone else will be sneaking in here after hours.|{A practice wand lies on the rack, still warm.|The steward's broom leans by the door, watching.|Tonight the ceiling stain looks like a dragon.}}\ntext [52ec0c] = {晚上的比试结束了。大厅近端的护界法阵还在嗡嗡作响。|护界法阵在嗡鸣。远处练习的人没往你这边看。|法阵在嗡鸣。你已经熟悉它的调子了。} {final_year: 很快就会有别人在闭馆后溜进这里了。|{架子上放着一根练习魔杖，还是温的。|管理员的扫帚靠在门边，像在盯着你。|今晚天花板上的污渍看起来像一条龙。}}\n# en: A dark duelling hall lit only by a ring of faintly glowing runes on the floor.\nscene [939412] = 漆黑的决斗厅，只有地上一圈微微发光的符文照明。\n# en: Step in and try a hard one\nopt.left.label [9c5c46] = 走进去试个难的\n# en: {The spell comes out clean and the circle drinks it. Somewhere a dummy loses its head and nobody ever finds out how.|You try the thing you saw a fourth-year do. It works. You tell no one, and walk back grinning.|You cast the hard one and the circle hums the note back to you, a little higher. You think that's approval.}\nopt.left.say.1 [2c0d44] = {咒语干净利落地打出，法阵把它吞了下去。某处一个假人掉了脑袋，没人知道是怎么回事。|你试了一招见四年级生用过的。成功了。你谁也没告诉，一路笑着走回去。|你施出了那道难的，法阵把音调哼回给你，稍高了一点。你想那是赞许。}\n# en: {The circle hands your spell straight back to you. Your eyebrows will regrow.|The circle flares, the dummies rattle, and you leave very quickly before the steward arrives.|The circle swallows half your spell and spits out the other half in your direction. The steward's broom falls over, laughing.}\nopt.left.say.2 [f848f6] = {法阵把你的咒语原样还给了你。你的眉毛会长回来的。|法阵一闪，假人们哗啦作响，你赶在管理员来之前飞快地溜了。|法阵吞了你半道咒语，把另一半朝你吐了回来。管理员的扫帚倒了，像在大笑。}\n# en: Sweep up for the steward\nopt.right.label [b29618] = 替管理员扫地\n# en: {You sweep up a winter's worth of ash. The steward pays you a few crowns and a grunt that might be thanks.|You sweep. The circle hums at you the whole time, like a cat that wants to be let out.|You sweep in time with the hum. The steward says you're the only one who does it properly, and pays you for the compliment.}\nopt.right.say.1 [1224ce] = {你扫掉了一整个冬天的灰烬。管理员给了你几枚克朗，外加一声可能是道谢的哼哼。|你扫着地。法阵一直冲你嗡嗡叫，像一只想出门的猫。|你随着嗡嗡声的节拍扫地。管理员说只有你扫得像样，还为这句夸奖付了你钱。}\n\n@event greenhouses_warm_glass\n# en: The Warm Glass\ntitle [4d5441] = 温暖的玻璃\n# en: {season == \"winter\": Outside it's snowing. Inside, the glass runs with warm water and everything smells of earth.|{season == \"summer\": The greenhouses are sweltering, and everyone has retreated to the far end where the vents are.|The greenhouses steam gently. Someone is singing to the mandrakes at the far end.}} {You find a dry corner by the ferns, out of everyone's way.|Someone has left a cushion in your corner by the ferns.|The ferns lean toward you when you come in, as they do for you now.}\ntext [4ff1a0] = {season == \"winter\": 外面在下雪。里面，玻璃上淌着温热的水珠，到处是泥土的气味。|{season == \"summer\": 温室里闷热难当，大家都躲到了有通风口的那一头。|温室里轻轻蒸腾着水汽。远处有人在给曼德拉草唱歌。}} {你在蕨类旁找到一个干爽的角落，不碍任何人的事。|有人在你蕨类旁的那个角落里留了个垫子。|你一进门，蕨类就朝你探过来，如今它们一向如此。}\n# en: A student reading on an upturned pot among tall ferns inside a steamy glass greenhouse, snow on the panes outside.\nscene [9eaa5e] = 雾气蒸腾的玻璃温室里，一个学生坐在倒扣的花盆上，在高高的蕨类间读书，窗外落着雪。\n# en: Do your reading in the warm\nopt.left.label [2d902a] = 在暖和处读书\n# en: {You get through a whole chapter. The pages curl in the damp and you come out smelling of moss.|You read until the steam makes the ink run. What's left of the chapter sticks.|You read with the ferns leaning in to listen. The chapter goes in easily, and the moss smell stays a day.}\nopt.left.say.1 [45e294] = {你读完了整整一章。书页在潮气里卷了边，你出来时一身青苔味。|你一直读到水汽把墨迹洇开。剩下的那部分章节倒是记住了。|你读书时蕨类都凑过来听。这一章读得很顺，青苔味留了一整天。}\n# en: Nap on the potting bench\nopt.right.label [8ac69b] = 在盆栽台上打个盹\n# en: {You wake with a seed tray for a pillow and a first-year staring at you. By supper it's a story.|You wake to a mandrake humming a lullaby. You choose not to tell anyone. Someone tells everyone.|You nap on your cushion among the ferns. Someone has put a sign by your feet. It says \"Do not water\".}\nopt.right.say.1 [bca74a] = {你醒来时枕着育苗盘，一个一年级生正盯着你。到晚饭时，这已经成了一段故事。|你在曼德拉草哼的摇篮曲里醒来。你决定谁也不告诉。有人告诉了所有人。|你在蕨丛里枕着你的垫子打盹。有人在你脚边放了块牌子，上面写着“请勿浇水”。}\n\n@event greenhouses_repotting\n# en: Repotting Day\ntitle [5b9bc1] = 换盆日\n# en: {here} is up to the elbows in compost, repotting {season == \"spring\": seedlings that have outgrown everything|{season == \"autumn\": bulbs for the winter|something with more teeth than leaves}}. {\"Hands?\" {they} ask{s}.|{They} hand{s} you a trowel before you've said hello.|There's a trowel on the hook that has come to be yours. {here} nods at it.}\ntext [36e480] = {here}正把胳膊埋在堆肥里，给{season == \"spring\": 长得什么都装不下的幼苗|{season == \"autumn\": 准备过冬的球根|一株牙齿比叶子还多的东西}}换盆。{“搭把手？”{they}问。|你还没打招呼，{They}就递给你一把小铲子。|钩子上有一把小铲子，渐渐成了你的。{here}朝它点点头。}\n# en: A student elbow-deep in compost at a greenhouse bench, handing a trowel to someone arriving.\nscene [c4461e] = 温室工作台边，一个学生胳膊深埋在堆肥里，把小铲子递给刚到的人。\n# en: Get your hands dirty\nopt.left.label [f6a380] = 动手干活\n# en: {You repot a whole row. Something nips your thumb in thanks.|You're filthy, nipped and oddly happy. {here} says you have good hands for roots.|You take your trowel off its hook and repot the difficult ones. Nothing bites you, which {here} says is a first.}\nopt.left.say.1 [5df3ab] = {你给一整排换了盆。有什么东西咬了一下你的拇指，算是道谢。|你满身泥污，被咬了几口，却莫名开心。{here}说你天生一双侍弄根的手。|你从钩子上取下你的小铲子，给难伺候的那几株换了盆。没有东西咬你，{here}说这还是头一回。}\n# en: Ask questions, stay clean\nopt.right.label [d36613] = 光提问，不沾手\n# en: {You learn a great deal about soil. {here} learns that you're afraid of it.|You take notes. {here} gives you a look over the trowel that says notes are not hands.|You ask your questions with your trowel in your hand, which {here} counts as progress.}\nopt.right.say.1 [6e48c2] = {你学到了很多关于泥土的知识。{here}则学到了你怕泥土。|你记着笔记。{here}隔着小铲子看了你一眼，意思是笔记不能代替手。|你手里握着小铲子提问，{here}认为这算是进步了。}\n\n@event greenhouses_cuttings\n# en: The Cuttings Bench\ntitle [c5d8ff] = 插条台\n# en: {A row of silver-leaf cuttings is drying on the bench|A tray of night-orchid cuttings sits by the door|A bundle of moonpetal cuttings is wrapped in damp cloth}, and Pell & Daughters pays well for that sort of thing. {Nobody is watching. The snapdragons are.|The snapdragons have seen you here before. They remember.|The snapdragons snap at you on principle now.}\ntext [31a502] = {台子上晾着一排银叶插条|门边放着一盘夜兰插条|一捆月瓣花插条裹在湿布里}，佩尔父女药铺收这种东西出价不低。{没人在看。金鱼草在看。|金鱼草以前见过你。它们记得。|金鱼草现在出于原则见你就咬。}\n# en: A greenhouse bench of labelled cuttings drying in rows, a row of snapdragons with actual teeth watching from their pots.\nscene [4762ee] = 温室工作台上一排排晾着的插条，带标签，一排长着真牙齿的金鱼草在盆里盯着。\n# en: Pocket a cutting\nopt.left.label [44bf20] = 偷偷揣一根插条\n# en: {Pell's counts the leaves twice and pays without a question. You don't look at the snapdragons on your next visit.|It sells for more than you expected, which somehow makes it worse.|Moonpetal sells for twice the others. Pell's doesn't ask where it came from, which you notice.}\nopt.left.say.1 [d43598] = {佩尔家把叶子数了两遍，二话不说付了钱。下次来时你没敢看金鱼草。|它卖的价钱比你想的还高，不知怎么反而更糟了。|月瓣花的价钱是别的两倍。佩尔家没问是从哪儿来的，这一点你注意到了。}\n# en: {A snapdragon shrieks. Three Verdant students turn around at once. You put it back with a cough that convinces no one.|Your pocket rustles at the wrong moment. You spend the rest of the visit apologising to a fern.|The snapdragons have been waiting for this. They raise the alarm before your hand reaches the cloth.}\nopt.left.say.2 [b74995] = {一株金鱼草尖叫起来。三个青翠社的学生同时回头。你干咳一声把它放回去，谁也没被说服。|你的口袋在最不该的时候沙沙作响。这一趟剩下的时间，你都在向一株蕨类道歉。|金鱼草们就等着这一刻。你的手还没碰到湿布，它们就叫了起来。}\n# en: Feed the snapdragons\nopt.right.label [22dfda] = 喂金鱼草\n# en: {They take the food, then a little of your finger. You learn which end to hold the tongs by.|Fed, the snapdragons purr like kettles. One of them still bites you, out of habit.|You feed the snapdragons and they snap anyway, gently, out of principle. You've come to find it friendly.}\nopt.right.say.1 [ff1769] = {它们吃了食物，又顺带吃了一点你的手指。你学会了夹子该拿哪一头。|吃饱的金鱼草像水壶一样咕噜咕噜。还是有一株咬了你，出于习惯。|你喂了金鱼草，它们还是咬了，轻轻地，出于原则。你已经觉得这挺友好的了。}\n\n@event flight_field_cheating_wind\n# en: The Cheating Wind\ntitle [c14839] = 耍赖的风\n# en: {season == \"winter\": The flight field is white, the hoops wearing caps of snow.|{season == \"spring\": The field is soft with new grass and the wind is playful.|{The field is empty but for the wind, practising on its own.|A lone first-year is flying very careful circles.|A pair of fourth-years are racing low over the grass.}}} {The school brooms in the shed are, at best, opinionated.|Your usual broom is in the shed, the one that pulls left.|The broom that pulls left now pulls right, out of spite.}\ntext [e42d53] = {season == \"winter\": 飞行场一片雪白，圈门都戴上了雪帽。|{season == \"spring\": 场地铺满柔软的新草，风很调皮。|{场上空无一人，只有风在自己练习。|一个一年级生在小心翼翼地绕圈飞。|两个四年级生贴着草皮比赛。}}} {棚里的学校扫帚，往好里说，也是很有主见。|你常用的那把扫帚在棚里，就是总往左偏的那把。|那把往左偏的扫帚现在往右偏了，纯属赌气。}\n# en: A long green field with tall hoops at either end, one student wobbling on a school broom against a gusting wind.\nscene [7ab1d7] = 两端立着高高圈门的长条绿地上，一个学生骑着学校扫帚在阵风里摇摇晃晃。\n# en: Practise your landings\nopt.left.label [516481] = 练习降落\n# en: {A dozen landings. A few of them on purpose. The grass keeps a careful record of the rest.|The wind waits until you're nearly down, then shoves. You land in a hoop instead. Technically, a goal.|Your broom pulls right; you lean left. The landings come out straight, for once.}\nopt.left.say.1 [fba672] = {降落了十几次，其中几次是故意的。草地仔细记下了其余的。|风等你快落地时才推了一把。你落进了圈门里。严格来说，算进球。|你的扫帚往右拽，你就往左倾。这一回，降落都是直的。}\n# en: Hire a decent broom\nopt.right.label [cee8bc] = 租一把像样的扫帚\n# en: {The good broom goes where you look, not where it feels like. For an hour, flying is easy and the field is yours.|You fly the length of the field and back with the wind on your side for once. It costs a week's sweets.|You hire the good broom and leave the spiteful one in the shed. It will be waiting next time, and it will remember.}\nopt.right.say.1 [3e2550] = {好扫帚会去你看的地方，而不是它想去的地方。有那么一个钟头，飞行很轻松，整个场地都是你的。|你顺着风飞了个来回，这可是头一回。代价是一周的零食钱。|你租了好扫帚，把那把记仇的留在棚里。下次它还会在那儿等着，而且会记得。}\n\n@event flight_field_pickup_race\n# en: The Pick-Up Race\ntitle [22c25a] = 临时赛\n# en: {A pick-up race is forming at the far hoops. Three brooms, two rules, no referee.|The pick-up racers are back. The rules have grown to four.|The pick-up race is a fixture now. Someone has painted a finish line, crookedly.} {A second-year from Emberhall is taking bets.|A Brasswick student has bolted a lantern to their broom-tail.|A Rootward student is flying barefoot, for grip.}\ntext [9c1159] = {远处的圈门那边正在凑一场临时赛。三把扫帚，两条规则，没有裁判。|临时赛的人又来了。规则涨到了四条。|临时赛已经成了固定节目。有人歪歪扭扭地画了一条终点线。} {一个烬厅的二年级生在坐庄。|一个铜芯的学生在扫帚尾巴上拴了盏灯笼。|一个根庐的学生光着脚飞，说是为了抓得牢。}\n# en: Three students on brooms jostling at a crooked painted start line, a fourth holding up a scarf as a starting flag.\nscene [66b550] = 三个骑扫帚的学生在一条歪斜的起跑线前推搡，第四个人举着围巾当发令旗。\n# en: Borrow a broom and race\nopt.left.label [18a85c] = 借把扫帚去比赛\n# en: {You win by a broom's length and a lot of shouting. The second-year taking bets has to pay out and never forgives you.|You take the last hoop sideways and cross first. Someone adds a new rule, about you.|You cross the crooked finish line first, barefoot Rootward student close behind. Your name goes up on the post in chalk.}\nopt.left.say.1 [0cdf5d] = {你以一扫帚之差、外加一片叫喊声获胜。那个坐庄的二年级生只好赔钱，从此再没原谅你。|你侧着身子穿过最后一个圈门，第一个冲线。有人加了一条新规则，是关于你的。|你第一个冲过那条歪歪扭扭的终点线，光脚的根庐学生紧随其后。你的名字被用粉笔写上了柱子。}\n# en: {You finish last, and partly in a hedge.|The wind takes your side, then changes its mind. You walk the broom back to the start.|You lose to someone flying barefoot. You consider the lesson in this and decide against it.}\nopt.left.say.2 [3d662a] = {你最后一名到达，有一部分是在树篱里到达的。|风先站在你这边，然后又改了主意。你推着扫帚走回起点。|你输给了一个光脚飞的人。你想了想这里面的道理，决定不去想。}\n# en: Bet a few crowns on the outsider\nopt.right.label [abc686] = 押几枚克朗给冷门\n# en: {The outsider wins on a gust nobody else saw coming. You are suddenly very good at betting.|The outsider comes in first by a nose. You toast the nose.|You bet on the barefoot flyer. Grip, it turns out, is everything.}\nopt.right.say.1 [a6209c] = {冷门借着一阵谁也没料到的风赢了。你突然成了押注高手。|冷门以一个鼻尖的优势夺冠。你为那个鼻尖干杯。|你押了光脚的那位。原来抓地力才是一切。}\n# en: {The outsider stops halfway to admire the view. Your crowns go with them.|Your flyer is disqualified for a rule invented on the spot. You don't argue. It's a good rule.|Your outsider's broom throws them at the first hoop. The crowns stay where they landed, which is not with you.}\nopt.right.say.2 [97ab34] = {冷门飞到一半停下来欣赏风景。你的克朗也跟着去了。|你押的人因为一条当场发明的规则被取消资格。你没争辩。那是条好规则。|你押的冷门在第一个圈门就被扫帚甩了下来。克朗留在了它们落下的地方，反正不是你这儿。}\n\n@event flight_field_long_grass\n# en: The Long Grass\ntitle [f6b1e4] = 长草丛\n# en: {The long grass at the edge of the flight field catches everything that falls off a broom.|Someone has planted a little flag in the long grass, to mark where things land.|You've come to think of the long grass as the academy's real lost-and-found.|Somebody has started a ledger of the long grass, pinned to the fence. Today's entry is yours to write.} Today it's {a velvet glove|a letter with no envelope|a single boot, laced|someone's notes for an exam}. {season == \"autumn\": The leaves are trying to bury it.|{season == \"winter\": It's half under frost.|A grasshopper is sitting on it.}}\ntext [ea2f70] = {飞行场边的长草丛接住了所有从扫帚上掉下来的东西。|有人在长草丛里插了面小旗，标出东西落下的地方。|你已经把长草丛当成学院真正的失物招领处了。|有人在篱笆上钉了一本长草丛登记簿。今天这一条该你来写。}今天是{一只天鹅绒手套|一封没有信封的信|一只系好鞋带的靴子|某人的考试笔记}。{season == \"autumn\": 落叶正想把它埋起来。|{season == \"winter\": 它有一半埋在霜里。|一只蚱蜢正坐在上面。}}\n# en: The long grass at the edge of a flight field, scattered with things dropped from brooms, a student crouching to look.\nscene [b64b13] = 飞行场边的长草丛里散落着从扫帚上掉下的东西，一个学生蹲下来查看。\n# en: Keep what you find\nopt.left.label [86edf0] = 捡到就归我\n# en: {The owner lands right beside you while you're still holding it. There's a short, very long silence.|A second-year marches over the grass shouting \"Thief!\" at the sky in general and you in particular.|A boy hops across the field on one foot, shouting. He wants his boot back, and he wants to know why you're holding it.|The owner of the notes finds you reading them. Their exam is tomorrow. It turns out yours is too.}\nopt.left.say.1 [519834] = {你还拿在手里，失主就在你旁边落了地。接下来是一阵短暂而又漫长的沉默。|一个二年级生大步穿过草地，冲着天空泛泛地大喊“小偷！”，具体来说是冲着你。|一个男生单脚跳着穿过场地，一边喊。他要回他的靴子，还想知道你为什么拿着它。|笔记的主人发现你在读它们。对方明天考试。原来你明天也考。}\n# en: {Finders, keepers. The grass doesn't argue, and neither does your conscience, much.|Nobody comes looking. You tell yourself that settles it.|It's a good boot. You'll need another one, but it's a start.|The notes are better than yours. You tell yourself that settles it.}\nopt.left.say.2 [96b460] = {谁捡到归谁。草丛没意见，你的良心也没多大意见。|没人来找。你告诉自己这就算了结了。|是只好靴子。你还需要另一只，不过总算开了个头。|那份笔记比你的好。你告诉自己这就算了结了。}\n# en: Carry it to the porter's lodge\nopt.right.label [e8195a] = 送到门房去\n# en: {It's a long walk round the field. The porter writes it in a book and gives you a look of mild surprise.|The porter thanks you with a peppermint from a jar that has clearly been open since the founders.|The porter puts the boot on a shelf of single boots, all left feet. This one is a right. He looks almost moved.|The porter reads the first page of the notes, says \"Hm,\" and files them under urgent.}\nopt.right.say.1 [e772d8] = {绕场一圈可不近。门房把它记进本子，略带惊讶地看了你一眼。|门房从一个显然自创始者时代就开着的罐子里掏出一颗薄荷糖谢你。|门房把靴子放到一排单只的靴子旁边，那些全是左脚的。这只是右脚。他看上去几乎有点感动。|门房读了笔记的第一页，“嗯”了一声，归进了加急一栏。}\n\n@event stables_owl_loft\n# en: The Owl Loft\ntitle [2be609] = 猫头鹰阁楼\n# en: {Up the ladder, above the hippogriffs, is a hay loft full of owls and quiet.|The owls open one eye each, decide you're furniture, and go back to sleep.|The owls have stopped opening their eyes for you. You've been accepted.} {season == \"winter\": The warmth of the animals rises through the boards.|{Rain ticks on the roof.|A stray letter has fallen into the hay.|The griffon chick is complaining somewhere below.}}\ntext [449aa4] = {爬上梯子，在鹰头马身有翼兽的上方，是一间满是猫头鹰和安静的干草阁楼。|猫头鹰们各睁开一只眼，认定你是件家具，又睡了。|猫头鹰们已经懒得为你睁眼了。你被接纳了。} {season == \"winter\": 动物们的暖意透过木板升上来。|{雨点滴答敲着屋顶。|一封走失的信掉进了干草里。|楼下的狮鹫幼崽在什么地方抱怨。}}\n# en: A hay loft in old stables, rows of sleepy owls on the rafters above a student reading by a lantern.\nscene [d922b6] = 老马厩的干草阁楼里，椽子上一排排睡眼惺忪的猫头鹰，下面一个学生就着灯笼读书。\n# en: Read up among the owls\nopt.left.label [928968] = 在猫头鹰中间读书\n# en: {You read until the light goes. The hay gets in everywhere, and the chapter gets in too.|An owl reads over your shoulder for a whole page, then hoots, pointedly, at a footnote.|You read until the griffon below stops complaining. The owls don't stir. You belong up here now.}\nopt.left.say.1 [521bc5] = {你一直读到天黑。干草钻得到处都是，书里的内容也钻进了脑子。|一只猫头鹰趴在你肩上读了整整一页，然后意味深长地冲一条脚注叫了一声。|你一直读到楼下的狮鹫不再抱怨。猫头鹰们动也不动。现在你属于这里了。}\n# en: Help sort the owl post\nopt.right.label [37a567] = 帮忙分拣猫头鹰邮件\n# en: {You sort a dropped bundle of post by hand. One owl seems to know which letters you're going to misfile.|You deliver three soggy letters to their owners on the way back. One of them hugs you, which you weren't ready for.|You sort the owl post by the light of the hatch. An owl drops a letter in your lap; it's for you.}\nopt.right.say.1 [968d35] = {你亲手整理了一捆掉落的信件。有只猫头鹰似乎早就知道你会放错哪几封。|回去路上你把三封湿漉漉的信交给了收信人。其中一个人抱住了你，你完全没准备好。|你借着天窗的光整理猫头鹰邮件。一只猫头鹰往你膝上丢了一封信，是给你的。}\n\n@event stables_brask_rounds\n# en: Brask's Rounds\ntitle [11370d] = 布拉斯克巡厩\n# en: {Professor Brask is on her rounds, telling each hippogriff she's a very good girl. She tells you to mind the bucket.|Brask is on her rounds. She nods at you, which is more than most get.|Brask greets you by name now, somewhere after the owls.} {season == \"spring\": There are new hippogriff foals, all legs and beak.|{A hippogriff is sulking about its feathers.|The feed is late and everyone is cross.|The stable cat is asleep in the oats.}}\ntext [794b64] = {布拉斯克教授正在巡厩，挨个告诉每只鹰头马身有翼兽它是个好姑娘。她让你当心水桶。|布拉斯克在巡厩。她冲你点了点头，这比大多数人得到的都多。|布拉斯克现在会叫你的名字了，排在猫头鹰后面。} {season == \"spring\": 新生的小兽崽全是腿和喙。|{一只鹰头马身有翼兽在为自己的羽毛闹脾气。|饲料送晚了，大家都很暴躁。|马厩的猫睡在燕麦堆里。}}\n# en: A burly professor stroking a hippogriff's beak in a stable aisle, ignoring a student holding two feed buckets.\nscene [5fb5c2] = 魁梧的教授在马厩过道里抚摸一只鹰头马身有翼兽的喙，无视旁边拎着两桶饲料的学生。\n# en: Ask Brask a question\nopt.left.label [3880c8] = 向布拉斯克请教\n# en: {She answers it at length, to the hippogriff. You take notes anyway.|\"Good question,\" she says, to the owl. You decide that counts.|She answers you directly, which has never happened. The stable cat wakes up to see what's going on.}\nopt.left.say.1 [bc27a8] = {她详详细细地回答了，对着那只鹰头马身有翼兽。你还是记了笔记。|“好问题，”她对猫头鹰说。你决定这也算数。|她直接回答了你，这可是从没有过的事。马厩的猫醒过来，想看看出了什么事。}\n# en: Carry the feed buckets\nopt.right.label [2dfcee] = 帮忙提饲料桶\n# en: {Every stall, two buckets each. Brask says nothing, which is her way of saying thank you.|A hippogriff bows to you over its bucket. Brask looks almost jealous.|You carry the buckets and work around the cat in the oats. Brask hands you the last bucket without being asked.}\nopt.right.say.1 [d25cf5] = {每个隔间两桶。布拉斯克一言不发，这就是她道谢的方式。|一只鹰头马身有翼兽隔着饲料桶向你鞠了一躬。布拉斯克看起来几乎有点嫉妒。|你提着桶，绕开燕麦里的猫干活。布拉斯克没等你开口，就把最后一桶递给了你。}\n\n@event stables_griffon_sulk\n# en: The Sulking Griffon\ntitle [16bcc6] = 闹别扭的狮鹫\n# en: {The griffon chick is sulking in the corner stall, feathers puffed, refusing breakfast.|The griffon chick is sulking again. It's bigger this time, and so is the sulk.|The griffon is hardly a chick any more, and still sulks like one. It watches you come in.} {year == 1: The sign on the stall says not to hand-feed it, underlined twice.|{The sign on the stall now has a drawing of a bitten hand.|The sign on the stall has been underlined until the paper tore.|Someone has added \"I mean it\" to the sign, in Brask's hand.}}\ntext [05bcf4] = {狮鹫幼崽缩在角落的隔间里生闷气，羽毛炸着，不肯吃早饭。|狮鹫幼崽又在生闷气。它长大了些，闷气也大了些。|那只狮鹫已经算不上幼崽了，生起闷气来还和从前一样。它看着你进门。} {year == 1: 隔间上的牌子写着不许手喂，下面画了两道线。|{隔间的牌子上现在画了一只被咬的手。|隔间牌子上的线被画到纸都破了。|有人在牌子上添了句“我是认真的”，是布拉斯克的笔迹。}}\n# en: A griffon chick with puffed-up feathers glaring from the corner of a stall, a student holding out a morsel at arm's length.\nscene [63efe8] = 一只羽毛炸起的狮鹫幼崽在隔间角落里怒视，一个学生伸直胳膊递出一小块食物。\n# en: Offer it breakfast by hand\nopt.left.label [1ec395] = 亲手喂它早饭\n# en: {It considers you for a long time, then takes the morsel with great delicacy. You feel chosen.|It eats from your hand and then, briefly, leans on you. You don't move for ten minutes.|It takes the morsel and then nudges your hand for another. Brask, passing, crosses out \"I mean it\".}\nopt.left.say.1 [b7e2e4] = {它打量了你很久，然后极其斯文地叼走了那块食物。你觉得自己被选中了。|它从你手里吃了东西，然后短暂地靠在你身上。你十分钟没敢动。|它叼走了食物，又拱拱你的手要第二块。布拉斯克路过，划掉了“我是认真的”。}\n# en: {It takes the morsel, a corner of your sleeve and a nip of your thumb. The sign was right.|It turns its back on you so pointedly that a passing owl looks embarrassed, and its tail catches you hard across the shins on the way round.|It takes the morsel and your glove, and then the sign agrees with it. Brask adds a second underline.}\nopt.left.say.2 [aba662] = {它叼走了食物、你袖子的一角，还啄了一下你的拇指。牌子说得没错。|它转过身去背对你，刻意得连路过的猫头鹰都替你尴尬；转身时它的尾巴狠狠扫过你的小腿。|它叼走了食物和你的手套，牌子跟它站在了一边。布拉斯克又加了一道下划线。}\n# en: Leave it to its sulk\nopt.right.label [bcf991] = 随它生闷气\n# en: {You read the notice about griffon moods instead. It is mostly a list of things that will not help.|You leave it alone. It looks, as you go, a little disappointed you gave up.|You leave it to sulk. It follows you along the bars, sulking at a closer range.}\nopt.right.say.1 [a4d095] = {你转而读起了关于狮鹫脾气的告示。上面大部分是一张“这些都没用”的清单。|你没去打扰它。你走的时候，它看上去有点失望你就这么放弃了。|你由它生闷气。它沿着栏杆跟着你，近距离地继续生闷气。}\n\n@event boathouse_jetty_end\n# en: The End of the Jetty\ntitle [d7bf11] = 栈桥尽头\n# en: {{date} is at the end of the jetty, boots over the water. {They} move{s} over without a word.|The end of the jetty has become your place for saying things. Today {date} is already there.|You know which plank creaks. {date} doesn't, and finds it.} {season == \"winter\": The lake has frozen round the posts and creaks like an old house.|{The rowing boats knock gently together.|A heron fishes in the shallows, pretending not to listen.|The village lamps blink on across the water.}}\ntext [c3f932] = {{date}坐在栈桥尽头，靴子悬在水面上。{They}一句话没说，挪出了位置。|栈桥尽头成了你说心里话的地方。今天{date}已经在那儿了。|你知道哪块木板会响。{date}不知道，一脚就踩上了。} {season == \"winter\": 湖水在桩子周围结了冰，像老房子一样吱嘎作响。|{划艇轻轻地互相碰撞。|一只苍鹭在浅水里捕鱼，假装没在听。|对岸小镇的灯一盏盏亮起来。}}\n# en: Two people sitting side by side at the end of a wooden jetty, boots over dark water, a lantern between them.\nscene [738e69] = 两个人并肩坐在木栈桥尽头，靴子悬在黑色的水面上，中间放着一盏灯笼。\n# en: Hire a boat and row out\nopt.left.label [d01173] = 租条船划出去\n# en: {season == \"winter\": You can't row on ice, so you sit in a beached boat and pretend. {date} calls it a fine voyage.|{You row badly, {date} laughs, and then neither of you is rowing at all.|Out in the middle the shore goes quiet. {date} says your name like the end of a sentence.|You row out until the village lamps are behind you. {date} trails a hand in the water and doesn't say anything for a long time.}}\nopt.left.say.1 [726e2f] = {season == \"winter\": 冰上没法划船，你们就坐在搁浅的船里假装。{date}说这是一趟不错的航行。|{你划得很糟，{date}笑你，然后谁也不划了。|划到湖心，岸上的声音都静了。{date}念你的名字，像在说一句话的结尾。|你们一直划到村里的灯火落在身后。{date}把一只手垂进水里，很久都没说话。}}\n# en: Stay on the jetty and talk\nopt.right.label [dedd57] = 留在栈桥上聊天\n# en: {final_year: You talk about where you'll both be next year. Neither of you says \"together\", and both of you hear it.|{You talk until the lantern gutters. The jetty is cold and you don't notice until you stand.|You say one of the things you couldn't say indoors. {date} listens all the way to the end.|You stay on the jetty, on the plank that creaks. {date} says it sounds like you, and won't explain.}}\nopt.right.say.1 [8e2da1] = {final_year: 你们聊起明年各自会在哪里。谁也没说“一起”，可两个人都听见了。|{你们一直聊到灯笼的火苗摇摇欲灭。栈桥很冷，站起来时你才发觉。|你说出了一件在屋里说不出口的事。{date}一直听到了最后。|你们待在栈桥上，就坐在那块会吱嘎响的木板上。{date}说它听起来像你，却不肯解释。}}\n\n@event boathouse_green_boat\n# en: The Leaky Green Boat\ntitle [c4a35d] = 漏水的绿船\n# en: {The boathouse smells of tar and old rope. Its rowing boats are mostly leaks held together by paint, the green one worst of all.|Someone has patched the green boat since your last visit, badly, with what looks like a cake tin.|The boathouse knows you. The green boat still leaks.} {Initials are carved into every beam, some so old they've gone soft.|Rain patters on the roof.|A spider has strung a web across the oars, and seems proud of it.}\ntext [c1e87e] = {船屋里是焦油和旧绳子的气味。这里的划艇大多是用油漆粘在一起的漏洞，绿色那条最糟。|自你上次来过，有人给绿船打了补丁，打得很烂，用的像是个蛋糕模子。|船屋认得你。绿船还在漏水。} {每根房梁上都刻着姓名缩写，有些老得已经模糊了。|雨点轻敲着屋顶。|一只蜘蛛在船桨间结了网，看样子很得意。}\n# en: Inside an old wooden boathouse, an upturned green rowing boat on trestles, initials carved into every beam above.\nscene [4e9c10] = 老旧木船屋里，一条绿色划艇倒扣在支架上，上方每根房梁都刻满了姓名缩写。\n# en: Patch the green boat\nopt.left.label [df86c5] = 给绿船打补丁\n# en: {You spend an hour with tar and a mallet. The green boat floats, briefly, with an air of surprise.|Your patch holds for a whole circuit of the boathouse. You have tar in your eyebrows and a certain pride.|You patch the green boat again. It leaks again. You're starting to think that's what it's for.}\nopt.left.say.1 [84066a] = {你拿着焦油和木槌忙了一个钟头。绿船浮了起来，短暂地，带着一脸惊讶。|你的补丁撑过了绕船屋一整圈。你眉毛上沾着焦油，心里有几分骄傲。|你又给绿船打了补丁。它又漏了。你开始觉得这就是它存在的意义。}\n# en: Carve your initials in a beam\nopt.right.label [383067] = 在房梁上刻名字缩写\n# en: The boatman charges a few crowns a beam.\nopt.right.hint [f69342] = 船夫刻一根梁要收几枚克朗。\n# en: {final_year: You pay the boatman and carve them next to a pair from sixty years ago. Somebody, one day, will run a thumb over yours and wonder.|{You pay the boatman, and your penknife snaps on the last letter. The initials stay, slightly wrong, which feels right.|You pay the boatman, find a carving that could almost be yours from long ago, and add your own beneath it.|You pay the boatman and carve your initials under the spider's web. The spider doesn't object.}}\nopt.right.say.1 [037e6f] = {final_year: 你付了船夫钱，把它刻在一对六十年前的缩写旁边。总有一天，会有人用拇指摸过你的，心生好奇。|{你付了船夫钱，最后一个字母刻到一半，小刀断了。缩写就这样留着，有点歪，感觉却刚好。|你付了船夫钱，找到一处很久以前的刻痕，几乎像是你的，于是把自己的刻在它下面。|你付了船夫钱，在蜘蛛网下面刻下了你的缩写。蜘蛛没有异议。}}\n\n@event boathouse_overheard\n# en: Things Said Outside\ntitle [bff476] = 屋外的话\n# en: {Two voices out on the jetty, low and urgent. People come to the boathouse to say what they can't say indoors.|You've found the gap in the boathouse planks where every word on the jetty comes through.|Another quiet conversation on the jetty. You're starting to know the academy by its secrets.} {It sounds like a break-up.|It sounds like an apology that isn't going well.|It sounds like someone confessing to a pudding.}\ntext [2a1a7e] = {栈桥上有两个声音，低沉而急切。人们来船屋，是为了说屋里说不出口的话。|你发现了船屋木板上的一道缝，栈桥上的每个字都从那里漏进来。|栈桥上又一段悄悄话。你开始从秘密里认识这所学院了。} {听起来像是在分手。|听起来像是一次不太顺利的道歉。|听起来像是有人在向一块布丁告白。}\n# en: Two silhouettes talking quietly on a jetty at night, seen through the slats of a boathouse wall.\nscene [fbc1ce] = 夜里两个身影在栈桥上低声交谈，从船屋墙板的缝隙间望去。\n# en: Listen a little longer\nopt.left.label [9863e8] = 再多听一会儿\n# en: {A plank creaks under you. The voices stop. Two very red faces come round the door, and now they know your face too.|An oar slides off its peg with a clatter. Footsteps; a hiss of \"Who's there?\"; you are, unfortunately.|You lean on the plank you know creaks. The pudding confession stops mid-sentence, and two faces you know appear at the door.}\nopt.left.say.1 [827606] = {你脚下一块木板吱嘎一响。说话声停了。两张通红的脸从门边探过来，现在他们也认得你的脸了。|一支船桨哐当一声从钉子上滑落。脚步声；一声低喝“谁在那儿？”；很不幸，是你。|你靠在了那块你明知会响的木板上。对布丁的忏悔戛然而止，两张你认识的脸出现在门口。}\n# en: {You learn more than you wanted to. You'll carry it carefully, and never tell.|It ends well, as it happens. You slip away before anyone has to know you were there.|It's about a pudding, honestly. You leave knowing more than any academy should about one pudding.}\nopt.left.say.2 [88f8a7] = {你听到的比你想听的多。你会小心收好，永远不说出去。|结果是个好结局。你在有人发现之前悄悄溜走了。|真的是关于一个布丁。你离开时，对一个布丁的了解超过了任何学院应有的程度。}\n# en: Cough loudly and leave\nopt.right.label [eb5a76] = 大声咳嗽，走开\n# en: {They wait for you to pass, hushed and grateful. You'll never know how it ended, and that's the price.|You make a great show of looking for an oar. By the time you've found one, they've gone.|You cough and leave. On the path you meet the pudding's owner, and decide not to say anything.}\nopt.right.say.1 [8398fc] = {他们安静地等你走过，满怀感激。你永远不会知道后来怎样了，这就是代价。|你装模作样地找船桨。等你找到时，他们已经走了。|你咳嗽一声走了。在小路上你遇见了布丁的主人，决定什么也不说。}\n\n@event orchard_under_the_trees\n# en: Under the Old Trees\ntitle [42dae6] = 老树之下\n# en: {season == \"spring\": Blossom is coming down like snow, and {date} has some in {their} hair.|{season == \"autumn\": The trees are heavy with apples and wasps, and {date} is reaching for one.|{season == \"winter\": The orchard is bare and silver, and {date} is walking between the trees.|{date} has claimed the best of the orchard shade.}}} {{They} wave{s} you over.|{They} saw you coming before you saw {them}.|The old trees seem to expect you by now. {date} only half does.}\ntext [f17cc4] = {season == \"spring\": 花瓣像雪一样落下，{date}的头发上沾了几片。|{season == \"autumn\": 树上挂满了苹果和黄蜂，{date}正伸手去够一个。|{season == \"winter\": 果园光秃秃的，一片银白，{date}在树间散步。|{date}占了果园里最好的一片树荫。}}} {{They}招手让你过去。|你还没看见{them}，{They}就先看见了你。|老树们似乎已经习惯等你了。{date}倒没那么笃定。}\n# en: Two people beneath an ancient apple tree, blossom drifting down around them like snow.\nscene [f622bf] = 两个人坐在一棵古老的苹果树下，花瓣像雪一样飘落在他们周围。\n# en: Stay until the light goes\nopt.left.label [953fd7] = 待到天黑\n# en: {season == \"spring\": {You stay until the blossom buries your shoes. You miss supper, and you'd do it again.|{date} picks blossom out of your hair, one petal at a time, and takes far too long about it.|You lie in the blossom until you're both freckled with it. {date} says it suits you, and keeps a petal.}|{season == \"winter\": {Your fingers go white, and {date} takes your hands to warm them, and doesn't give them back.|You lean against the same cold trunk until the stars come out over the bare branches.|You walk the bare rows until the frost comes up. {date} breathes on your hands and says it's for practical reasons.}|{You stay until the shadows outgrow the trees. You miss supper. Neither of you mentions it.|The light goes gold, then blue. {date} says something quiet you'll turn over for days.|You stay until the first star. {date} names it after something you said, and won't tell you which thing.}}}\nopt.left.say.1 [53ad5d] = {season == \"spring\": {你们一直待到花瓣埋住了鞋子。你错过了晚饭，下次还会这样。|{date}把花瓣从你头发里一片片挑出来，挑得太慢了。|你们躺在落花里，直到两人身上都缀满了花瓣。{date}说这很衬你，留下了一片。}|{season == \"winter\": {你的手指冻得发白，{date}握住你的手替你暖着，再没还给你。|你们靠着同一根冰冷的树干，直到星星爬上光秃秃的枝头。|你们在光秃秃的树行间走到霜气升起。{date}往你手上呵气，说是出于实际考虑。}|{你们一直待到影子比树还长。你错过了晚饭。谁也没提。|光先变成金色，又变成蓝色。{date}轻声说了句什么，你会反复回味好几天。|你们待到第一颗星出来。{date}用你说过的某句话给它起了名字，却不告诉你是哪句。}}}\n# en: Help in the orchard together\nopt.right.label [1e4a49] = 一起在果园帮忙\n# en: {season == \"autumn\": {You fill two baskets and earn one wasp sting each. The cook gives you a pie, which you share on the steps.|{date} holds the ladder and says nothing useful, and you don't want {them} to.|You pick from the top branches while {date} sorts the basket below. Every good apple goes to you, which you notice.}|{season == \"winter\": {You prune dead wood side by side until your hands ache. {date} stacks it with great seriousness.|You wrap the young trees against the frost. {date} ties every knot twice.|You mend the orchard fence together, one post each. {date}'s post is straighter, and {they} won't stop mentioning it.}|{season == \"spring\": {You carry hives along the rows for the blossom. One bee follows {date} all afternoon.|You clear the grass under the blossom. {date} keeps stopping to look up.|You graft a branch onto an old tree. {date} says you'll both have to come back to see if it takes.}|{You water the young trees from a bucket chain of two. {date} is soaked by the end, and so are you.|You prop up a branch bowed with early apples. {date} holds it, and holds still, and you notice.|You rake the cut grass into rows. {date} lies down in the first row, and the work goes no further.}}}}\nopt.right.say.1 [062ac3] = {season == \"autumn\": {你们装满了两筐，各挨了一下黄蜂蜇。厨娘给了你们一个馅饼，你们坐在台阶上分着吃了。|{date}扶着梯子，什么有用的话也没说，你也不希望{them}说。|你在高处的枝头摘，{date}在下面理篮子。好苹果都留给了你，你注意到了。}|{season == \"winter\": {你们并肩修剪枯枝，直到手都酸了。{date}郑重其事地把枝条码好。|你们给小树裹上防霜的草帘。{date}每个结都系两遍。|你们一起修果园的篱笆，一人一根桩子。{date}那根更直，{they}一直提个不停。}|{season == \"spring\": {你们沿着树行搬蜂箱去授粉。一只蜜蜂跟了{date}一下午。|你们清理花下的杂草。{date}总是停下来抬头看。|你们往一棵老树上嫁接了一根枝条。{date}说你们俩都得回来看看它活没活。}|{你们俩排成一条水桶接力，给小树浇水。最后{date}浑身湿透，你也是。|你们撑起一根被早熟苹果压弯的枝条。{date}扶着它，一动不动，你注意到了。|你们把割下的草耙成一行行。{date}在第一行躺了下来，活就到此为止了。}}}}\n\n@event orchard_windfalls\n# en: Windfalls\ntitle [03d58f] = 落果\n# en: {The orchard trees are older than the towers, and never tidy.|The oldest tree has dropped its fruit in a ring, like a table laid for guests.|You know which tree drops what by now, and which one throws.} {season == \"autumn\": Pears and apples lie bruised in the grass, attended by wasps.|{season == \"winter\": Last year's apples lie frozen, hard as stones.|{season == \"spring\": It's mostly blossom, and the odd stubborn pear.|Early apples lie in the shade, small and sharp.}}}\ntext [7efe1c] = {果园的树比七塔还老，从来不收拾干净。|最老的那棵树把果子落成一圈，像为客人摆好的餐桌。|你现在知道哪棵树掉什么，哪棵树会砸人。} {season == \"autumn\": 梨和苹果磕伤了躺在草里，黄蜂在一旁看守。|{season == \"winter\": 去年的苹果冻在地上，硬得像石头。|{season == \"spring\": 大多是落花，还有几只倔强的梨。|早熟的苹果躺在树荫里，又小又酸。}}}\n# en: A student gathering fallen fruit into a basket under gnarled orchard trees, older than the towers behind them.\nscene [0d2ab4] = 盘曲的老果树下，一个学生把落果捡进篮子，树比身后的高塔还老。\n# en: Gather windfalls to sell\nopt.left.label [0a0959] = 捡落果去卖\n# en: {The cook buys your basket for the cider vats and finds {season == \"winter\": a frozen beetle|a wasp} in it. You both pretend that's normal.|A full basket, {season == \"winter\": numb fingers|two stings} and a crick in your back. The crowns feel earned.|You go straight to the trees that drop the good ones. The cook pays for a full basket and says you have an eye.}\nopt.left.say.1 [6d5b1b] = {厨娘买下你的篮子拿去酿苹果酒，在里面发现了{season == \"winter\": 一只冻僵的甲虫|一只黄蜂}。你们俩都假装这很正常。|满满一篮，{season == \"winter\": 冻僵的手指|两处蜇伤}，外加腰酸。这些克朗挣得踏实。|你径直走向掉好果子的那几棵树。厨娘为满满一篮付了钱，说你眼光不错。}\n# en: Eat one under the oldest tree\nopt.right.label [bfdcad] = 在最老的树下吃一个\n# en: {{season == \"winter\": It's frozen solid. Your teeth will remember this.|There was a wasp in it. There was, briefly, a wasp in you.}|It's sour enough to turn your face inside out, and your stomach complains about it until supper. A passing first-year laughs, and so, eventually, do you.|The one that throws gets you on the shoulder. You eat its apple out of spite, and it's {season == \"winter\": frozen through|full of wasp}, and so is your mood.}\nopt.right.say.1 [60f84f] = {{season == \"winter\": 冻得像石头。你的牙会记住这一口。|里面有只黄蜂。有那么一会儿，黄蜂在你嘴里。}|酸得你五官都皱到了一起，胃一直闹到晚饭。路过的一年级生笑了，最后你也笑了。|那棵会扔果子的树砸中了你的肩膀。你赌气吃了它的苹果，结果{season == \"winter\": 冻透了|满是黄蜂}，你的心情也一样。}\n# en: {It's the best thing you've eaten all month. The old tree creaks, as if pleased.|You eat it slowly. Above you, the oldest tree drops one more, gently, beside your hand.|You eat it under the oldest tree, as you always do now. It drops a leaf on your knee, and you keep it.}\nopt.right.say.2 [1bec50] = {这是你这个月吃过最好吃的东西。老树吱呀一声，像是很高兴。|你慢慢地吃。头顶上，最老的那棵树又轻轻落下一个，正落在你手边。|你像现在每次那样，在最老的树下吃。它在你膝上落下一片叶子，你留着了。}\n\n@event orchard_cider_shed\n# en: The Cider Shed\ntitle [b0d03f] = 苹果酒棚\n# en: {In the orchard shed, a few students are running an unofficial cider press.|The lookout at the cider shed has started nodding you in.|The cider shed is a fixture now. Even the lookout seems bored.} {season == \"autumn\": The press groans with this year's apples.|The shed smells of last autumn and wet straw.} {A goat is watching from the hedge, very carefully saying nothing.|Someone has carved a menu into the door.|A prefect's scarf hangs on a nail, unexplained.}\ntext [8c11d7] = {果园的棚子里，几个学生在偷偷经营一台苹果酒榨机。|苹果酒棚望风的人开始点头放你进去了。|苹果酒棚已经成了固定去处。连望风的都显得无聊了。} {season == \"autumn\": 榨机被今年的苹果压得吱嘎作响。|棚里一股去年秋天和湿稻草的味道。} {一只山羊在树篱边看着，非常小心地一言不发。|有人在门上刻了一份酒单。|钉子上挂着一条级长的围巾，来历不明。}\n# en: A tumbledown orchard shed with a wooden cider press inside, three students turning the screw and a fourth keeping watch at the door.\nscene [31b8c3] = 破旧的果园棚子里有一台木制苹果酒榨机，三个学生在拧螺杆，第四个在门口望风。\n# en: Buy a cup\nopt.left.label [e3b76c] = 买一杯\n# en: {A prefect turns up as you raise the cup. Everyone is suddenly very interested in apples.|The lookout whistles too late. A prefect takes your name, then, after some thought, your cup.|The prefect whose scarf it is comes back for it. Everyone looks at the scarf, then at you, holding a cup.}\nopt.left.say.1 [261919] = {你刚举起杯子，一个级长就出现了。大家突然都对苹果产生了浓厚兴趣。|望风的口哨吹晚了。一个级长记下你的名字，想了想，又没收了你的杯子。|那条围巾的主人，一位级长，回来取它了。大家先看看围巾，再看看端着杯子的你。}\n# en: {It's sharp, cold and a little bit fizzy. The day goes golden at the edges.|You drink it on an upturned bucket while someone tells a story about a goose. Excellent cider. Better goose.|You drink it under the prefect's scarf. Nobody asks, and the cider is the best yet.}\nopt.left.say.2 [5b388c] = {酒又酸又凉，还有一点点气泡。这一天的边缘都变成了金色。|你坐在倒扣的桶上喝，有人在讲一只鹅的故事。酒很好。鹅更好。|你在级长的围巾底下喝着。没人问起，这一批酒是到目前为止最好的。}\n# en: Help turn the press, for a share\nopt.right.label [297dd0] = 帮忙榨酒，换一份\n# en: {You turn the screw until your shoulders burn. The second-year in charge teaches you why some apples are better bruised.|You're sticky to the elbows and you've learned more about fermentation than in a term of class.|You turn the press with the regulars now. The second-year gives you a jar of your own to take home.}\nopt.right.say.1 [908468] = {你拧螺杆拧到肩膀火辣辣地疼。主事的二年级生教你为什么有些苹果磕伤了反而更好。|你黏到了胳膊肘，学到的发酵知识比一学期的课还多。|现在你和常客们一起拧压榨机了。那个二年级生给了你一罐属于你自己的，让你带回去。}\n\n@event maud_cottage_kettle\n# en: Tea at Maud's\ntitle [14a007] = 莫德家的茶\n# en: {Old Maud's kettle is nearly boiling, as it always is. \"Sit down, dearie,\" she says. \"You look like a puddle with a worry.\"|The kettle is nearly boiling. Maud has already put out your cup, the chipped one.|Your chipped cup is waiting. Maud says it's been asking after you.|Maud pours before you've sat down. \"Same as ever, dearie,\" she says, and it is.} {Bartholomew is eating a tea towel.|Rain drums on the thatch.|A hen is asleep on the windowsill.|Bees drone in the eaves.}\ntext [6fa262] = {老莫德的水壶快要开了，一向如此。“坐吧，亲爱的，”她说，“你看着像个犯愁的水洼。”|水壶快要开了。莫德已经摆好了你的杯子，那只有缺口的。|你那只有缺口的杯子在等你。莫德说它一直在打听你。|你还没坐下，莫德就倒好了茶。“老样子，亲爱的，”她说，确实如此。} {巴塞洛缪在吃一条茶巾。|雨点敲着茅草屋顶。|一只母鸡睡在窗台上。|蜜蜂在屋檐下嗡嗡作响。}\n# en: A low cottage kitchen, a kettle on the hob, an old woman pouring tea into a chipped cup, a goat's head poking through the open door.\nscene [20892a] = 低矮的小屋厨房，炉上坐着水壶，一位老妇人往有缺口的杯子里倒茶，一只山羊从敞开的门外探进头来。\n# en: Stay for a second cup\nopt.left.label [0720c4] = 再喝一杯\n# en: {Maud tells you a sad story the way other people give a recipe. You walk home late and cold, thinking about it.|\"Never trust a dry spring, dearie,\" she says. You won't understand it for a fortnight.|The second cup becomes a third. It's dark when you leave, and you've only half noticed.|You stay for a second cup and the bees in the eaves put Maud to sleep mid-story. You wash the cups and let yourself out.}\nopt.left.say.1 [2fdeaa] = {莫德像别人讲菜谱那样讲了一个伤心的故事。你很晚才冒着寒气走回去，一路想着它。|“永远别信干燥的春天，亲爱的，”她说。两个星期后你才会明白。|第二杯变成了第三杯。你离开时天已经黑了，你几乎没察觉。|你留下喝第二杯，屋檐下的蜜蜂嗡嗡声让莫德讲着讲着就睡着了。你洗好杯子，自己出了门。}\n# en: Ask her for a remedy\nopt.right.label [72f493] = 向她讨个偏方\n# en: {She brews you something that tastes of ditches. \"Down in one, dearie.\" You feel better at once, and resent it.|\"For what ails you,\" she says, and doesn't say what she thinks that is. It tastes of nettles and of being right.|She hands you a remedy before you've said what's wrong. It works, which is the annoying part.|\"Honey and bee-balm, dearie, and stop worrying.\" It tastes of summer. You stop worrying, briefly.}\nopt.right.say.1 [f3e623] = {她给你熬了一碗沟渠味的东西。“一口喝干，亲爱的。”你立刻好多了，并为此愤愤不平。|“治你的毛病，”她说，却没说她觉得你是什么毛病。那味道像荨麻，也像她说对了。|你还没说哪儿不舒服，她就把药递给了你。药管用，烦人的正是这一点。|“蜂蜜加蜂香草，亲爱的，别再发愁了。”尝起来像夏天。你短暂地不再发愁。}\n\n@event maud_cottage_bartholomew\n# en: Bartholomew Is Out\ntitle [e4bd17] = 巴塞洛缪跑了\n# en: {Bartholomew the goat has got out.|Bartholomew is out again, and has learned to lift the gate latch with his teeth.|Bartholomew has escaped so often that the hens no longer scatter.} {slot == \"evening\": Maud doesn't even look up from her knitting.|Maud's note on the door says she's at the greenhouses, and to mind the goat.} {He has eaten half a washing line.|He is standing on the roof of the henhouse, looking pleased.|He is in the cabbages, conducting a slow and thorough war.}\ntext [b77b4f] = {巴塞洛缪跑出来了。|巴塞洛缪又跑出来了，它学会了用牙齿抬门闩。|巴塞洛缪跑得太勤，母鸡们都懒得四散奔逃了。} {slot == \"evening\": 莫德连头都不从针线活上抬。|门上莫德留的字条说她去温室了，让人看好山羊。} {它已经吃掉了半根晾衣绳。|它站在鸡舍顶上，一脸得意。|它在卷心菜地里，打一场缓慢而彻底的战争。}\n# en: A goat standing triumphantly on the roof of a henhouse, chickens scattering, a student approaching with an apple held out.\nscene [d9f8cd] = 一只山羊得意洋洋地站在鸡舍顶上，鸡群四散，一个学生举着苹果靠近。\n# en: Chase him down\nopt.left.label [f61b49] = 把它追回来\n# en: {You corner him by the water butt. He gives up with the air of someone who allowed it. {slot == \"evening\": \"Ta, dearie,\" calls Maud.|You tie him to the gate and leave Maud a note.}|You catch him on the second lap. He chews your sleeve all the way back, as a souvenir.|You chase him out of the cabbages with the ease of long practice. The hens don't even look up.}\nopt.left.say.1 [0d1439] = {你在接雨水的桶边把它堵住了。它投降的样子像是在施舍你。{slot == \"evening\": “谢啦，亲爱的，”莫德喊道。|你把它拴在门上，给莫德留了张字条。}|你在第二圈抓住了它。它一路嚼着你的袖子回去，当作纪念品。|你熟门熟路地把它从卷心菜地里赶了出来。母鸡们连头都没抬。}\n# en: {He leads you twice round the orchard and once through the hen run, then trots home on his own.|You end up in the cabbages. Bartholomew watches you from the roof, chewing, unhurried.|He leads you through the cabbages and leaves you there. The war continues without you.}\nopt.left.say.2 [a0e7cf] = {它领着你绕果园跑了两圈，又穿过鸡圈一回，然后自己溜达回家了。|你最后栽进了卷心菜地。巴塞洛缪站在屋顶上看着你，嚼着东西，不慌不忙。|它领你穿过卷心菜地，把你丢在那儿。战争在没有你的情况下继续。}\n# en: Bribe him with your lunch\nopt.right.label [eb4050] = 拿午饭贿赂它\n# en: {He takes your sandwich and follows you home like a gentleman. You go hungry until supper.|Your lunch disappears in three bites. Bartholomew looks at you as if you might be worth knowing.|He takes your lunch and walks home ahead of you. He knows the way better than you do by now.}\nopt.right.say.1 [c1181b] = {它接过你的三明治，像位绅士一样跟你回家。你一直饿到晚饭。|你的午饭三口就没了。巴塞洛缪看着你，像是觉得你或许值得结交。|它吃了你的午饭，走在你前面回家。如今它比你还熟悉这条路。}\n\n@event maud_cottage_top_shelf\n# en: The Top Shelf\ntitle [fd2f93] = 最高一层架子\n# en: {{slot == \"evening\": Maud has stepped out to the hens.|Maud is up at the greenhouses.} The top shelf of jars is right there, every label in her spidery hand.|Maud has stepped out, and the top shelf has gained a jar since you were last here.|You know the top shelf by heart, except for whichever jar Maud added last.} One label reads {\"not for you, dearie\"|\"only in emergencies\"|\"do not shake\"}, and the jar is {glowing faintly|humming|warm to the touch}.\ntext [523727] = {{slot == \"evening\": 莫德出去喂鸡了。|莫德去温室了。}最高一层的罐子就在眼前，每张标签都是她那蜘蛛似的笔迹。|莫德出去了，最高一层比你上次来时多了个罐子。|最高一层你已经烂熟于心，只有莫德最近添的那个罐子除外。}其中一张标签写着{“不是给你的，亲爱的”|“仅限紧急情况”|“切勿摇晃”}，那罐子{发着微光|在嗡嗡作响|摸上去是温的}。\n# en: A high cottage shelf crowded with stoppered jars and handwritten labels, one jar glowing faintly blue.\nscene [c62b98] = 小屋里高高的架子上挤满了塞着瓶塞的罐子和手写标签，其中一罐发着淡淡的蓝光。\n# en: Open it, just a little\nopt.left.label [73d5dd] = 打开一点点\n# en: {Something sweet and blue drifts out and settles in your chest. You feel, briefly, enormous.|A breath of summer rain escapes. You put the lid back on smiling.|It's warm, and so is the air that comes out. You stand in it like a cat in a sunbeam until you hear Maud's step.}\nopt.left.say.1 [fd566a] = {一缕甜甜的蓝色飘出来，落进你的胸口。有那么一会儿，你觉得自己无比庞大。|一口夏雨的气息逃了出来。你笑着把盖子盖了回去。|罐子是热的，飘出来的空气也是。你站在里面，像晒太阳的猫，直到听见莫德的脚步声。}\n# en: {Your eyebrows turn the colour of the label. When Maud sees them, she says nothing whatsoever.|It sneezes. Then you sneeze, for an hour. \"Told you, dearie,\" says Maud later, not unkindly.|You shook it, a little. The label was right. Your hair stands on end until supper.}\nopt.left.say.2 [298202] = {你的眉毛变成了标签的颜色。莫德看见后，一个字也没说。|它打了个喷嚏。然后你打了一个钟头的喷嚏。“早说过了，亲爱的，”莫德后来说，语气并不凶。|你稍微晃了它一下。标签说得没错。你的头发一直竖到晚饭。}\n# en: Dust the shelf instead\nopt.right.label [cb81d8] = 改为掸掸灰\n# en: {You dust every jar and read every label. Maud's handwriting is a whole education, if a strange one.|Maud finds a clean shelf. \"Somebody's been learning,\" she says, which isn't quite thanks.|You dust the new jar first and read its label twice. Maud finds you there and gives you a look that's almost proud.}\nopt.right.say.1 [a14959] = {你掸了每个罐子，读了每张标签。莫德的笔迹本身就是一门学问，虽然古怪。|莫德发现架子干干净净。“有人在长学问了，”她说，这不太算道谢。|你先掸了那只新罐子，把它的标签读了两遍。莫德发现你在那儿，看你的眼神几乎有点骄傲。}\n\n@event secret_garden_humming_pear\n# en: The Humming Pear\ntitle [041751] = 哼歌的梨树\n# en: {The pear tree in Maud's secret garden is humming to itself.|The pear tree has changed its tune since you were last here, as if it's been practising.|The pear tree hums when you come in now. You'd like to think it's for you.} {season == \"winter\": Its tune is low and slow, like something asleep.|{season == \"spring\": Its tune has gone quick and bright with blossom.|{Its tune is one you almost know.|Its tune has a sad turn in the middle.|Its tune is one you've heard Maud whistle.}}}\ntext [48719c] = {莫德秘密花园里的梨树在自顾自地哼歌。|自你上次来过，梨树换了个调子，像是一直在练习。|现在你一进来，梨树就开始哼歌。你愿意相信那是为你哼的。} {season == \"winter\": 它的调子又低又慢，像某种在沉睡的东西。|{season == \"spring\": 它的调子随着开花变得轻快明亮。|{那是一支你几乎会唱的曲子。|那支曲子中间有个忧伤的转折。|那支曲子你听莫德吹过口哨。}}}\n# en: A student lying in the grass under a pear tree in a hidden walled garden, ferns glowing softly around them.\nscene [69e58c] = 隐蔽的围墙花园里，一个学生躺在梨树下的草地上，周围的蕨类柔柔发光。\n# en: Hum along with it\nopt.left.label [d18c15] = 跟着它一起哼\n# en: {You find the harmony on the third try. The tree corrects you twice, kindly.|The two of you hum until the ferns glow brighter. Afterwards you can't get the tune out of your head for days.|You hum along without thinking now. The pear tree drops a pear in your lap at the end, which feels like applause.}\nopt.left.say.1 [785686] = {第三次你才找到和声。梨树温和地纠正了你两次。|你们一起哼，直到蕨类更亮了。之后好几天，那调子都在你脑子里挥之不去。|现在你不假思索就能跟着哼。末了，梨树往你膝上掉了一只梨，像是在鼓掌。}\n# en: Nap in its shade\nopt.right.label [981938] = 在树荫下打个盹\n# en: {You wake rested, a few crowns lighter, with a fresh root curled over the spot where they fell.|You sleep like a seed. A few crowns slip out of your pocket and the roots close over them. Rent, perhaps.|You sleep in your usual hollow between the roots. A few crowns are gone when you wake, and the tree looks very innocent.}\nopt.right.say.1 [11a9c3] = {你醒来时神清气爽，口袋里少了几枚克朗，掉落的地方盘着一条新长的根。|你睡得像一颗种子。几枚克朗从口袋滑出，树根把它们合拢了。大概是房租吧。|你在树根间那个熟悉的凹处睡着了。醒来时少了几枚克朗，梨树看上去一脸无辜。}\n# en: You sleep like a seed and wake with your empty pocket turned out, as if the roots had checked.\nopt.right.say.2 [85a98a] = 你睡得像一颗种子，醒来时空口袋被翻了出来，仿佛树根检查过了。\n\n@event secret_garden_silver_bees\n# en: The Silver Bees\ntitle [1eab66] = 银蜂\n# en: {The silver bees are working the ferns; their hive in the old stump drips honey the colour of moonlight.|The silver bees have built a second comb, lopsided, as if in a hurry.|The silver bees know you now, more or less.} {season == \"winter\": Even in winter they fly, slowly, like snow going the wrong way.|{The comb is fuller than you've ever seen it.|One of them lands on your sleeve and stays.|They're dancing a direction you can't read.}}\ntext [2aba13] = {银蜂在蕨类间忙碌；老树桩里的蜂巢淌着月光色的蜂蜜。|银蜂又筑了第二块蜂巢，歪歪扭扭，像是赶工。|银蜂现在或多或少认得你了。} {season == \"winter\": 即使在冬天它们也在飞，慢慢的，像飘错了方向的雪。|{蜂巢比你见过的任何时候都满。|一只落在你的袖子上，不走了。|它们跳着一个你读不懂的方向。}}\n# en: Bees made of silver light hovering around a hive in a hollow stump, a student reaching carefully towards a dripping comb.\nscene [db712c] = 银光构成的蜜蜂绕着空心树桩里的蜂巢盘旋，一个学生小心翼翼地伸手去取滴蜜的蜂巢。\n# en: Take a little comb\nopt.left.label [9d8f0c] = 取一小块蜂巢\n# en: {A silver sting feels like a cold spark. Your hand glows for an hour and aches for two.|The bees file a complaint, in the form of your thumb.|They know your hand by now, and sting it anyway, more out of habit than anger.}\nopt.left.say.1 [b98702] = {被银蜂蜇一下，像一颗冰冷的火星。你的手亮了一个钟头，又疼了两个钟头。|蜜蜂提出了抗议——抗议书就是你肿起来的拇指。|它们现在认得你的手了，还是蜇了它，与其说是生气，不如说是习惯。}\n# en: {You take a thumb's width of comb. It tastes like starlight and burns a little going down.|The bees let you take a little, and watch to make sure it's only a little.|They let you take a little and dance something at you afterwards. You'd give a lot to know what it meant.}\nopt.left.say.2 [b10aea] = {你取了拇指宽的一小块。尝起来像星光，咽下去有点烫。|蜜蜂让你取了一点，还盯着确保真的只有一点。|它们让你取了一点，之后朝你跳了一段舞。你很想知道那是什么意思。}\n# en: Follow a bee home\nopt.right.label [3d2092] = 跟着一只蜜蜂回家\n# en: {The bee leads you through brambles to a fern you've never seen, and then leaves you there, scratched and curious.|You follow it all the way round the garden and back to the stump. It seems to find this funny.|You follow a bee that's dancing the strange direction. It leads you to the garden wall, and over it, and you can't follow.}\nopt.right.say.1 [b7ed2a] = {蜜蜂领你穿过荆棘，来到一株你从没见过的蕨类前，然后把你丢在那儿，满身划痕，满心好奇。|你跟着它绕花园一圈，又回到树桩。它似乎觉得这很好笑。|你跟着一只跳着奇怪方向的蜜蜂。它把你领到花园墙边，然后飞了过去，你跟不过去。}\n\n@event secret_garden_glowing_ferns\n# en: The Glowing Ferns\ntitle [725061] = 发光的蕨类\n# en: {The ferns in the secret garden glow when the light goes. One frond would light your dormitory for a week. Maud said not to pick them.|Since your last visit a new fern has unfurled by the gate, already glowing.|The ferns glow. You know exactly what a frond is worth, and what Maud said.} {One has grown over the path, right at your hand.|A broken frond lies on the ground already, still glowing.|They're brighter than you've ever seen them.}\ntext [f2564e] = {天色一暗，秘密花园里的蕨类就亮了。一片叶子就能把你的宿舍照亮一个星期。莫德说过不许摘。|自你上次来过，门边又舒展开一株新蕨，已经亮了。|蕨类在发光。你清楚一片叶子值多少，也清楚莫德说过什么。} {有一株长到了小路上，正在你手边。|地上已经有一片折断的叶子，还在发光。|它们比你见过的任何时候都亮。}\n# en: A hidden garden at dusk, waist-high ferns glowing pale green, one student crouched with fingers near a frond.\nscene [cac6b0] = 黄昏时的隐秘花园，齐腰高的蕨类发着淡绿色的光，一个学生蹲着，手指靠近一片叶子。\n# en: Pick a single frond\nopt.left.label [ed6b96] = 只摘一片\n# en: {The frond goes dark before you reach the door. The garden feels a little colder to you the next time.|The frond curls up in your hand like a woodlouse and goes out. The silver bees follow you to the gate, accusingly.|You pick the brightest one. It goes out in your hand like a sigh, and the rest dim a little as you pass.}\nopt.left.say.1 [af70bb] = {还没走到门口，那片叶子就暗了。下次来时，花园对你冷淡了一些。|叶子在你手里像鼠妇一样蜷起来，熄灭了。银蜂一路跟你到门口，满是责备。|你摘了最亮的那片。它在你手里熄灭了，像一声叹息，你经过时其余的也暗了一点。}\n# en: {It lights your dormitory green for a week. You read by it, and feel very slightly watched.|You keep it in a jar by your bed. It glows softer every night, like something being polite.|It's the brightest frond you've ever seen, and it lights your room for a fortnight. You don't tell Maud.}\nopt.left.say.2 [368109] = {它把你的宿舍照成绿色，亮了一个星期。你借着它读书，总觉得有一点点被注视。|你把它放在床边的罐子里。它一夜比一夜暗，像在客气。|那是你见过最亮的一片叶子，把你的房间照亮了两个星期。你没告诉莫德。}\n# en: Leave them glowing\nopt.right.label [76d055] = 让它们继续发光\n# en: {You sit and watch the glow instead, and learn the way it moves with your breath. Maud would approve, if she knew.|You leave them be. On the way out, the ferns lean after you, like a dog that wanted to come.|You sit among them at their brightest. They glow brighter while you stay, and that's enough.}\nopt.right.say.1 [3a0884] = {你坐下来看那光，发现它随着你的呼吸起伏。莫德要是知道，会赞许的。|你没去碰它们。离开时，蕨类朝你的方向倾着，像一只想跟来的狗。|你在它们最亮的时候坐在它们中间。你在的时候它们更亮了，这就够了。}\n\n@event courier_loft_letter_home\n# en: A Letter Home\ntitle [8bce33] = 一封家书\n# en: {The courier loft will take a letter home for next to nothing. You've meant to write for weeks.|This time the letter has been half-written in your head since breakfast.|Writing home is a habit now, and the loft knows your handwriting.} {final_year: There aren't many letters left to send from here.|{season == \"winter\": Frost on the window; the pigeonholes stuffed with cards.|{The dispatch bell rings twice while you write.|A broken broom leans against your elbow.|A courier is reading over your shoulder, and apologises.}}}\ntext [53c5b7] = {信使阁楼花不了几个钱就能帮你把信送回家。你已经想写好几个星期了。|这一封从早饭起就在你脑子里写了一半。|往家里写信成了习惯，阁楼也认得你的笔迹了。} {final_year: 能从这里寄出的信不多了。|{season == \"winter\": 窗上结着霜，信格里塞满了贺卡。|{你写信时，发件铃响了两次。|一把坏扫帚靠在你胳膊肘边。|一个信使从你肩后看你写信，然后道了歉。}}}\n# en: A draughty loft full of pigeonholes, a student writing a letter on a parcel by the light of a high window.\nscene [953afc] = 满是信格的透风阁楼里，一个学生借着高窗的光，在一个包裹上写信。\n# en: Write it all down and send it\nopt.left.label [2de5e0] = 全写下来寄出去\n# en: {You write three pages and post them before you can cross anything out. You feel lighter, and a little homesick.|You tell them about your classes, and not about the hard parts. Then, in a postscript, you tell them about the hard parts.|You write the letter you've written before, with this month's news in it. The courier says your family must look forward to them.}\nopt.left.say.1 [877bbb] = {你写了三页，在来得及划掉任何一句之前就寄了出去。你觉得轻松了些，也有点想家。|你跟他们讲了你的课，没讲难处。然后，在附言里，你讲了难处。|你写了一封以前写过的那种信，填进这个月的消息。信使说你家人一定很盼着这些信。}\n# en: Write it, then don't send it\nopt.right.label [cf5f6f] = 写好了，但不寄\n# en: {You read it over and fold it into your pocket instead. Some letters are only for writing.|You keep this one. The courier asks if you're sure. You aren't.|You put this one with the others you didn't send. It's getting to be quite a stack.}\nopt.right.say.1 [d52bd1] = {你读了一遍，折好放进口袋。有些信只是用来写的。|这封你留下了。信使问你确定吗。你并不确定。|你把这封和其他没寄的放在一起。那一沓越来越厚了。}\n\n@event courier_loft_dispatch_bell\n# en: The Dispatch Bell\ntitle [e813ff] = 发件铃\n# en: {The dispatch bell clangs and the loft explodes into brooms. A courier shoves a parcel at you. \"You've got legs. Lecture tower!\"|The couriers have started eyeing the ladder whenever someone climbs it.|The bell. A parcel. A look. You know the drill.} {The parcel is ticking.|The parcel is addressed to \"Whoever Is On Duty\".|The parcel is very cold.}\ntext [fcbf9d] = {发件铃一响，阁楼里扫帚乱飞。一个信使把包裹塞给你。“你有腿。讲堂塔！”|现在每次有人爬梯子，信使们都会瞟上一眼。|铃声。包裹。一个眼神。你知道该怎么做。} {包裹在滴答作响。|包裹上写着“寄给值班的随便谁”。|包裹冰凉冰凉的。}\n# en: A chaotic loft as a dispatch bell clangs, couriers grabbing brooms and parcels, one holding a parcel out to a visitor.\nscene [b87d28] = 发件铃大作，乱成一团的阁楼里信使们抓起扫帚和包裹，其中一个把包裹递给来客。\n# en: Run the parcel across campus\nopt.left.label [71bfc4] = 跑一趟送包裹\n# en: {You get it there out of breath and in time. The tip is a few crowns and a very strange look.|You run it up four flights of stairs. The recipient signs for it without looking up, as if you were the post.|You run the cold parcel across campus with your hands going numb. The tip is generous, and given quickly.}\nopt.left.say.1 [b2e672] = {你气喘吁吁但准时送到了。小费是几枚克朗外加一个非常奇怪的眼神。|你跑上四层楼。收件人头也不抬就签了字，好像你本来就是邮差。|你捧着那个冰冷的包裹横穿校园，手都冻麻了。小费给得很大方，也给得很快。}\n# en: Hand it back, politely\nopt.right.label [b4d5ae] = 客气地还回去\n# en: {\"Fine, fine,\" says the courier, already on a broom. You hear about it, loudly, from the rafters.|The courier takes it back with a sigh that follows you down the ladder.|You hand the cold parcel back. The courier holds it at arm's length and doesn't argue.}\nopt.right.say.1 [05070f] = {“行吧行吧，”信使说着已经跨上了扫帚。你从椽子上方听到了响亮的抱怨。|信使叹着气接回包裹，那声叹息一路跟着你下了梯子。|你把冰冷的包裹还了回去。信使伸直胳膊拿着它，没有争辩。}\n\n@event courier_loft_broom_rack\n# en: The Fast Broom\ntitle [0775c7] = 快扫帚\n# en: {At the end of the rack is the courier loft's fast broom, tagged in red. Couriers only. Nobody's watching.|The fast broom is back on the rack, red tag and all.|The red-tagged broom. You've thought about it every time you've come up here.} {season == \"winter\": The loft doors are open on a sky full of snow.|{The loft doors stand open on a blue sky.|Someone has polished it recently.|The red tag has a fresh scorch mark.}}\ntext [e04714] = {架子尽头是信使阁楼的快扫帚，挂着红牌。仅限信使。没人在看。|快扫帚又回到了架子上，红牌也在。|那把挂红牌的扫帚。每次上来你都会想到它。} {season == \"winter\": 阁楼的门敞着，外面是漫天飞雪。|{阁楼的门敞着，外面是蓝天。|最近有人给它上过油。|红牌上有一道新焦痕。}}\n# en: A rack of courier brooms in a loft, one sleek black broom set apart from the others with a red tag on its handle.\nscene [76169f] = 阁楼里一排信使扫帚，其中一把光滑的黑扫帚单独放着，柄上挂着红牌。\n# en: Take it for one lap\nopt.left.label [c03fc1] = 骑它飞一圈\n# en: {One lap of the towers, fast enough to make your ears sing. You put it back with the bristles barely warm.|You fly it under the bridges of light and come back laughing. Nobody saw, except, possibly, everybody.|You take the fast broom for one lap at last. It flies like it's been waiting for you. You put it back and can't stop smiling.}\nopt.left.say.1 [221919] = {绕七塔一圈，快得耳朵嗡嗡响。你放回去时，帚毛几乎没热。|你骑着它从光之桥底下穿过，笑着飞回来。没人看见，除了，也许，所有人。|你终于骑着快扫帚飞了一圈。它飞起来像是一直在等你。你把它放回去，笑得停不下来。}\n# en: {It knows you aren't a courier. It shows you the courtyard, very close up.|It bucks you off into a haystack outside the stables. The stable hand laughs about it for a week.|The broom with the scorched tag has a scorched temper. It puts you through the stable roof.}\nopt.left.say.2 [617e56] = {它知道你不是信使。它让你近距离地看了看庭院的地面。|它把你甩进了马厩外的干草堆。马夫为这事笑了一个星期。|标签烧焦的扫帚，脾气也烧焦了。它把你甩穿了马厩的屋顶。}\n# en: Tighten its bristles instead\nopt.right.label [8b8ed8] = 改为给它紧紧帚毛\n# en: {You spend an hour learning how a good broom is bound. It's more satisfying than you'd admit.|You retie the bristles properly. Some courier will fly a little faster tomorrow and never know why.|You rebind the bristles and the scorched tag. The head courier notices and says nothing, which is promising.}\nopt.right.say.1 [6edfab] = {你花了一个钟头学一把好扫帚是怎么扎成的。这比你愿意承认的更让人满足。|你把帚毛重新扎好。明天会有个信使飞得快一点，却永远不知道为什么。|你重新扎好帚毛，还换了那块烧焦的标签。首席信使注意到了，什么也没说，这是个好兆头。}\n\n@event whisperwood_sleeptalk\n# en: The Forest Talks\ntitle [380652] = 森林在说话\n# en: {The Whisperwood is talking in its sleep. You stand very still to make out the words.|You've started to catch whole words in the forest's mumbling.|You've learned to stand still and let the forest talk.} Today it's {listing trees that were felled|arguing about the rain|saying a name, over and over, that isn't yours}. {season == \"winter\": Snow muffles half of it.|{season == \"autumn\": Leaves fall with every word.|Fog moves between the trunks.}}\ntext [27381d] = {低语森林在说梦话。你一动不动地站着，想听清那些词。|你开始能听清森林嘟囔里的整个词了。|你已经学会静静站着，让森林说下去。}今天它在{列举被砍倒的树|为下雨的事争吵|一遍遍念着一个不是你的名字}。{season == \"winter\": 雪捂住了一半。|{season == \"autumn\": 每说一个字就落下一片叶子。|雾在树干间游移。}}\n# en: A student standing very still in a misty old forest, head tilted, as if listening to the trees.\nscene [9d0c9e] = 雾气弥漫的古老森林里，一个学生一动不动地站着，侧着头，像是在听树说话。\n# en: Listen closely\nopt.left.label [21f0ed] = 仔细听\n# en: {You catch a whole sentence. It's about a winter long ago, and it's sad, and you walk home with it.|It notices you listening and goes quiet, the way a sleeper does. You leave feeling rude.|You listen to the name until you could say it in your sleep. You don't know whose it is. You'll find out, one day.}\nopt.left.say.1 [2b9f0d] = {你听清了完整的一句。那是关于很久以前的一个冬天，很悲伤，你带着它走回了家。|它发现你在听，便像睡着的人那样安静下来。你离开时觉得自己很失礼。|你听那个名字听到能在梦里说出来。你不知道那是谁的名字。总有一天你会知道。}\n# en: Walk on, humming\nopt.right.label [f1a5bc] = 哼着歌继续走\n# en: {You walk the long loop humming loudly. The forest grumbles and turns over. You come back with muddy boots and a clear head.|You hum all the way round. Twice, the forest hums back.|You hum over the name it keeps saying. The forest falls quiet, as if it had been waiting for someone to answer.}\nopt.right.say.1 [ee327a] = {你大声哼着歌走完长长的一圈。森林嘟囔着翻了个身。你带着一靴子泥和清醒的脑袋回来。|你一路哼着歌。有两次，森林也哼了回来。|你用哼唱盖过它反复念叨的名字。森林安静下来，像是一直在等人回答。}\n\n@event whisperwood_foragers\n# en: Foraging Season\ntitle [067bb9] = 采集季\n# en: {season == \"autumn\": The Whisperwood is full of mushrooms and students with baskets.|{season == \"winter\": A few hardy foragers are scraping lichen off the oaks.|{season == \"spring\": The clearings are thick with wild garlic and foragers.|The brambles are heavy with berries, and so are the foragers.}}} {A second-year from Rootward waves you over.|The Rootward forager is back, and saves you a spot.|The foragers treat you as one of their own now.}\ntext [62e283] = {season == \"autumn\": 低语森林里满是蘑菇和提着篮子的学生。|{season == \"winter\": 几个不怕冷的采集者在刮橡树上的地衣。|{season == \"spring\": 林间空地长满了野蒜，也挤满了采集者。|荆棘丛上挂满了浆果，采集者身上也挂满了。}}} {一个根庐的二年级生朝你招手。|那个根庐的采集者又来了，给你留了个位置。|采集者们现在把你当自己人了。}\n# en: A handful of students with baskets crouching in a forest clearing, one holding up a mushroom for the others to inspect.\nscene [6bbb73] = 几个提篮子的学生蹲在林间空地上，其中一个举起一只蘑菇给其他人看。\n# en: Forage for the apothecary\nopt.left.label [c317dd] = 为药铺采集\n# en: {Pell & Daughters buys your basket and throws out a third of it. You've still got nettle rash.|A good basket, a fair price, and brambles in places you'd rather not discuss.|The foragers show you their own patch. Pell & Daughters keeps the whole basket, which has never happened.}\nopt.left.say.1 [78e029] = {佩尔父女药铺收下你的篮子，扔掉了三分之一。你身上的荨麻疹还在。|一篮好货，一个公道价，还有一些你不愿细说的地方扎进了荆棘。|采集的人把他们自己的地盘指给了你。佩尔父女药铺把一整篮都留下了，这是从没有过的事。}\n# en: Learn the plants from the expert\nopt.right.label [5629c5] = 跟行家认植物\n# en: {The second-year names everything, twice, with its uses and its poisons. You come away full of names and short of patience.|You learn which mushroom is supper and which is a funeral. It takes until supper, and you'll need it.|You teach a first-year what the Rootward student taught you, and get two of the names wrong. They're corrected for you, kindly.}\nopt.right.say.1 [4edc47] = {那个二年级生把每样东西都说了两遍，连用途带毒性。你满脑子名字，耐心所剩无几。|你学会了分辨哪种蘑菇是晚餐，哪种是葬礼。一直学到晚饭时分，你会用得上的。|你把根庐学生教你的东西教给一个一年级生，说错了两个名字。别人好心地替你纠正了。}\n\n@event whisperwood_off_path\n# en: The Path That Wasn't\ntitle [916a4a] = 本不存在的小路\n# en: {A path opens to your left that wasn't there last time.|Another path that wasn't there before. The Whisperwood does this.|The wood has offered you a new path again. You're starting to think it likes you.} {It's lined with pale stones.|It smells of woodsmoke where there's no fire.|It goes downhill, which is wrong, because you're at the bottom of the hill.}\ntext [3b11a8] = {你左手边出现了一条上次没有的小路。|又一条以前没有的小路。低语森林就是这样。|森林又给你开了一条新路。你开始觉得它喜欢你。} {路边排着苍白的石头。|没有火，却闻得到柴烟味。|它往下坡走，这不对，因为你已经在山脚了。}\n# en: A narrow path branching off into fog between old trees, a student pausing at the fork with a hand on a marked trunk.\nscene [a1f31c] = 古树间一条窄路分岔伸进雾里，一个学生在岔口停下，手扶着一棵做了记号的树干。\n# en: Follow it a little way\nopt.left.label [03da75] = 跟着走一小段\n# en: {It leads to a clearing you'll never find again, full of moonpetal buds. You come back with a pocketful of quiet.|It loops you round to an old stone with words on it. You copy them down. They mean nothing yet.|You follow it downhill, though there's no down left. It brings you out above the lake, which is impossible, and lovely.}\nopt.left.say.1 [842b36] = {它通向一片你再也找不到的空地，开满了月瓣花的花苞。你带回了满满一口袋的安静。|它绕着你来到一块刻着字的老石头前。你把字抄了下来。它们暂时还没有意义。|你顺着它往下走，虽然已经没有更低的地方了。它把你带到了湖的上方，这不可能，却很美。}\n# en: {You walk in circles until dusk and come out at the lake, with no idea how.|The path ends at a wall of fog. Turning back takes much longer than going in did.|You follow it downhill for an hour and come out exactly where you started. The wood seems pleased with itself.}\nopt.left.say.2 [fe46de] = {你转圈转到黄昏，出来时到了湖边，不知道是怎么走到的。|小路尽头是一堵雾墙。往回走比进来花的时间长得多。|你顺着它往下走了一个钟头，出来时正好在起点。树林看上去很得意。}\n# en: Mark a tree and turn back\nopt.right.label [851506] = 在树上做记号，回头\n# en: {You carve an arrow in the bark. The next time you come, the arrow points somewhere else.|You turn back. The path watches you go, and quietly closes behind you.|You mark a tree. The next morning there's a mark on the tree beside it, in the same hand.}\nopt.right.say.1 [56987c] = {你在树皮上刻了个箭头。下次来时，箭头指向了别处。|你转身回去。小路目送你离开，在你身后悄悄合上了。|你在一棵树上做了记号。第二天早上，旁边那棵树上出现了一个同样笔迹的记号。}\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/pools/internships.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/pools/internships.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event intern_midsummer_review\n# en: The Midsummer Review\ntitle [9f057d] = 仲夏评估\n# en: {Halfway through the placement, your supervisor asks to see you. There's a single sheet of notes on the desk, face down.|The midsummer review again: the desk, the sheet, the tea nobody drinks.|You know the review's shape by now. You still don't know what's on the sheet.} {performance(internship) >= 60: Your supervisor is almost smiling.|{performance(internship) < 40: Your supervisor is not smiling.|Your supervisor's face gives nothing away.}}\ntext [e7c69e] = {实习过半，主管叫你过去。桌上有一张笔记，扣着放。|又是仲夏评估：那张桌子，那张纸，那杯没人喝的茶。|你现在熟悉评估的套路了。你仍然不知道那张纸上写了什么。}{performance(internship) >= 60: 你的主管几乎在微笑。|{performance(internship) < 40: 你的主管没有笑。|你的主管面无表情。}}\n# en: A supervisor's desk with a single sheet of notes face down, a student sitting opposite with hands folded too tightly.\nscene [cda6e8] = 主管的桌上扣着一张笔记，一个学生坐在对面，双手握得太紧。\n# en: Ask for honest criticism\nopt.left.label [7e14a3] = 请对方直言批评\n# en: {You get it, three things you do badly, in order. You fix two of them by the end of the summer. Your supervisor notices both.|\"Most interns ask for praise,\" your supervisor says, and turns the sheet over. It's a long list. You're grateful for every line, eventually.|This time you ask to see the sheet itself. It's shorter than you feared, and every line is fair.}\nopt.left.say.1 [22ee92] = {你得到了：你做得不好的三件事，按顺序排列。到夏天结束时你改掉了两件。你的主管两件都注意到了。|“大多数实习生要的是表扬，”你的主管说着，把纸翻了过来。清单很长。最终你对每一行都心存感激。|这一次你要求看那张纸本身。它比你担心的短，每一行都很公道。}\n# en: Ask what you've done well\nopt.right.label [d826f5] = 问问自己哪里做得好\n# en: {Your supervisor reads the good half aloud. You leave glowing, and never hear the other half.|\"Your handwriting,\" your supervisor says, after a pause, \"is excellent.\" You decide to take it.|Your supervisor reads you the top of the sheet and folds the rest away. You'll wonder what's under the fold.}\nopt.right.say.1 [5452ea] = {你的主管把好的那一半念了出来。你满面春风地离开，再也没听到另一半。|“你的字，”你的主管顿了一下说，“写得非常好。”你决定接受这句话。|你的主管给你念了纸的上半截，把剩下的折了起来。你会一直好奇折痕底下写了什么。}\n\n@event intern_credit_taken\n# en: Someone Else's Name\ntitle [737ae1] = 别人的名字\n# en: {The report you spent a week on is pinned up in the corridor, praised by everyone, with another intern's name at the bottom.|Another piece of your work, another name on it.|It's happened again, and this time the other intern doesn't even look away.} {Your supervisor is reading it with approval.|The other intern is being congratulated.|Your notes are still on your desk, in your hand.}\ntext [eb5b31] = {你花了一个星期写的报告钉在走廊里，人人称赞，署名却是另一个实习生。|又一份你的成果，又一个别人的名字。|又发生了，这一次那个实习生连目光都不躲了。}{你的主管正赞许地读着它。|那个实习生正接受大家的祝贺。|你的笔记还在你桌上，是你的笔迹。}\n# en: A report pinned on a wall, a student staring at the name at the bottom of it, which is not their own.\nscene [27205c] = 一份钉在墙上的报告，一个学生盯着报告底部的名字，那不是他自己的。\n# en: Take it to your supervisor\nopt.left.label [13030f] = 找你的主管\n# en: {You bring your notes, your drafts, your dates. Your supervisor reads them in silence and changes the name in red ink. The corridor goes very quiet around you.|You make your case calmly. The name is changed. The other intern doesn't speak to you again, which is fine.|You put your notes on your supervisor's desk, in your hand and dated. The name is changed, and this time the other intern looks away.}\nopt.left.say.1 [399e9e] = {你带上笔记、草稿和日期。主管默默读完，用红墨水改了名字。你周围的走廊一下子安静了。|你冷静地陈述理由。名字改了。那个实习生再也没和你说过话，这样也好。|你把笔记放在主管桌上，你的笔迹，注明了日期。名字改了，这一次，那个实习生移开了目光。}\n# en: {Your supervisor hears you out and says it's \"a matter for the two of you\". It isn't. You go back to your desk with your face burning.|Without proof enough, you sound petty, and you know it. The name stays.|Your notes are in your hand, but so, it turns out, is a draft in theirs. Your supervisor can't tell which came first, and the name stays.}\nopt.left.say.2 [974ec6] = {主管听你说完，说这是“你们俩之间的事”。不是的。你回到桌前，脸上火辣辣的。|证据不够，你显得斤斤计较，你自己也知道。名字没改。|你的笔记是你的笔迹，可结果对方也有一份自己笔迹的草稿。主管分不清哪份在先，名字没改。}\n# en: Let it go and work faster\nopt.right.label [1f0a6c] = 算了，干得更快些\n# en: {You say nothing and do the next one twice as well, in ink that's hard to copy. It doesn't feel like justice. It feels like work.|You let it go. You write your name on every page from then on, small, in the corner.|You take your notes home. The next piece of work never leaves your sight until it's on the supervisor's desk.}\nopt.right.say.1 [d7ade9] = {你什么也没说，把下一份做得加倍好，用难以仿写的墨水。这感觉不像公道。这感觉像干活。|你算了。从那以后，你在每一页的角落里都写上自己的名字，小小的。|你把笔记带回了住处。下一份活儿，在放上主管的桌子之前，一刻也没离开过你的视线。}\n\n@event intern_city_evening\n# en: An Evening Off\ntitle [6fa683] = 休息的夜晚\n# en: {{member(intern_expedition) or member(intern_field_station): The other interns are going out tonight, three miles to a village dance, with a fiddler and a supper laid on at a few coins a head.|The other interns are going out tonight, to an inn with music and a stall that sells fried bread at midnight.}|Another evening off, and the others are already at the door.|\"Coming?\" says the intern with the biscuits. \"Last time you said next time.\"} {member(intern_expedition) or member(intern_field_station): {The track is dark, but somebody has a lantern.|It's the only dance for thirty miles.|Someone has brought a fiddle of their own.}|{The streets are bright.|There's a fair on the river.|Somebody knows a band.}} Your work for tomorrow isn't done.\ntext [a6fa73] = {{member(intern_expedition) or member(intern_field_station): 其他实习生今晚要出去，走三英里去村里的舞会，有提琴手，还有几个钱一位的晚饭。|其他实习生今晚要出去，去一家有音乐的客栈，还有个半夜卖炸面包的小摊。}|又一个休息的夜晚，其他人已经站在门口了。|“去吗？”带饼干的那个实习生说，“上次你说下次。”}{member(intern_expedition) or member(intern_field_station): {小路很黑，好在有人带了灯笼。|这是方圆三十英里唯一的舞会。|有人自己带了把小提琴。}|{街道灯火通明。|河上有个集市。|有人认识一支乐队。}}你明天的活儿还没干完。\n# en: A lamplit street far from the academy, interns spilling out of a busy inn, one looking back at the lit window of the workplace.\nscene [9f0bec] = 远离学院的一条灯火通明的街道，实习生们从热闹的客栈里涌出来，其中一个回头望着工作地点亮着灯的窗户。\n# en: Go out with them\nopt.left.label [e99515] = 和他们一起去\n# en: {You pay your share of the supper, dance badly, laugh well and walk home at two in the morning, arm in arm with people you met last month. Tomorrow's work is a little worse for it.|You go, and stand your round. You learn more about the place from the other interns in one evening than from a month of briefings, and remember half of it.|You keep last time's promise and go. The intern with the biscuits shares them on the walk home, and tomorrow's work is a little worse for it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [89a4dc] = {你付了自己那份晚饭钱，跳舞跳得很糟，笑得很开心，凌晨两点和上个月才认识的人挽着胳膊走回家。明天的活儿因此差了一点。|你去了，还请了一轮。一个晚上从其他实习生那里了解到的东西，比一个月的晨会还多，虽然只记住了一半。|你兑现了上次的承诺，去了。带饼干的那个实习生在回去的路上分给你吃，明天的活儿因此差了一点。}\n# en: Finish tomorrow's work\nopt.right.label [2c6920] = 干完明天的活\n# en: {You finish the work by lamplight. You hear them come home, singing. In the morning your work is the best on the table.|You stay in. The work is good. The evening is long.|\"Next time,\" you say again, and mean it less. Your work is excellent, and the biscuits are gone by morning.}\nopt.right.say.1 [07633f] = {你在灯下干完了活。你听见他们唱着歌回来。早上，你的成果是桌上最好的。|你留了下来。活儿干得很好。夜晚很长。|“下次吧，”你又说了一遍，这回没那么真心。你的活儿干得很出色，饼干到早上已经没了。}\n\n@event intern_letter_from_campus\n# en: A Letter From the Castle\ntitle [ffb8b9] = 城堡来信\n# en: {A letter from {friend}, forwarded twice, with the castle drawn in the margins.|Another letter from campus, this one from {friend}.|{friend}'s handwriting on the envelope. You open it before your coat is off.} {The letter misses you, in a roundabout way.|{They} {are} bored without you, {they} say{s}.|There's gossip, and a pressed flower.} Answering it properly would take the evening.\ntext [890ed9] = {一封{friend}的信，转寄了两次，页边画着城堡。|又一封来自学院的信，这次是{friend}写的。|信封上是{friend}的字迹。你外套还没脱就拆开了。}{这封信拐弯抹角地说想你。|{They}说没有你很无聊。|里面有八卦，还有一朵压干的花。}好好回信得花一个晚上。\n# en: A letter open on a desk far from the academy, its margins full of little drawings of the castle, a student smiling at it.\nscene [f98846] = 远离学院的一张桌上摊着一封信，页边画满了城堡的小插画，一个学生对着它微笑。\n# en: Write back at length\nopt.left.label [7bb28c] = 长长地回一封\n# en: {Six pages, with drawings of your own. You post it at dawn and are late for the briefing, and don't mind.|You write back everything: the work, the place, the interns. It's like talking to {them}.|You answer the gossip with gossip of your own, and press a flower from here to send back. It's like being in two places.}\nopt.left.say.1 [d15588] = {六页纸，还配了你自己画的插图。你天亮时寄出，晨会迟到了，也不在乎。|你把一切都写了回去：工作、这个地方、实习生们。就像在和{them}说话。|你用自己这边的八卦回应那边的八卦，还压了一朵这里的花寄回去。就像同时身在两处。}\n# en: Send a quick note\nopt.right.label [40b631] = 回一张短笺\n# en: {\"Busy. Miss you. More soon.\" It's true, and it's thin, and you know it.|Three lines and a promise. You mean to write more. The summer is very full.|A quick note, and the pressed flower kept in your notebook. You'll write properly when the summer's less full.}\nopt.right.say.1 [2d0d0f] = {“忙。想你。回头细说。”是真话，很单薄，你自己也知道。|三行字和一个承诺。你本想多写一些。这个夏天太满了。|一张短笺，那朵压花夹进了你的笔记本。等夏天没这么满了，你再好好写。}\n\n@event intern_conclave_your_ward\n# en: A Ward of Your Own\ntitle [dbf01a] = 属于你的护界\n# en: The Conclave is testing a new ward, and the researcher who designed it is ill. \"You've read every note,\" says your supervisor. \"Cast it. If it holds, it's yours to keep, and your name goes in the paper.\" Behind the glass, half the Conclave has come to watch.\ntext [7343f4] = 七塔议会在测试一种新的护界，设计它的研究员病了。“每一条笔记你都读过，”你的主管说，“施放它。如果它撑住了，就归你了，你的名字也会登上论文。”玻璃后面，半个议会的人都来看了。\n# en: A circular test chamber, a student at the centre holding up a shimmering ward against a bolt of light, researchers behind thick glass on their feet.\nscene [9c1957] = 一间圆形的测试室，一个学生站在中央，举起一道闪烁的护界挡住一束光，厚玻璃后面的研究员们都站了起来。\n# en: Cast it\nopt.left.label [e73317] = 施放它\n# en: The bolt hits the ward and stops, humming, an inch from your nose. Behind the glass, someone starts clapping. Your name goes in the paper, third of four, and the ward is yours for good.\nopt.left.say.1 [a78dfe] = 光束击中护界停了下来，嗡嗡作响，离你的鼻尖只有一寸。玻璃后面有人开始鼓掌。你的名字登上了论文，四位作者中的第三位，而这道护界从此永远属于你。\n# en: The ward holds for half a heartbeat and then doesn't. You wake on the floor with your hair standing up and a researcher fanning you with your own notes.\nopt.left.say.2 [aa069c] = 护界撑了半个心跳，然后没撑住。你在地板上醒来，头发竖着，一个研究员正拿你自己的笔记给你扇风。\n# en: Ask them to wait for the designer\nopt.right.label [03931f] = 请他们等设计者回来\n# en: \"Sensible,\" says your supervisor, in a voice that means \"disappointing\". The designer recovers, casts it, and thanks you in the paper's footnotes for the notes you kept.\nopt.right.say.1 [4dc945] = “明智，”你的主管说，语气的意思是“令人失望”。设计者康复后施放了它，并在论文的脚注里感谢你保存的笔记。\n\n@event intern_alchemists_safety\n# en: The Cracked Vat\ntitle [177790] = 裂开的大桶\n# en: The seventh vat at the Guild works has a crack in it. Everyone knows. The foreman says it'll last the season, and the season's quota depends on it. Workers walk past it without looking. You've seen what the steam does to brass.\ntext [7b9994] = 公会工坊的第七口大桶裂了。人人都知道。工头说它能撑过这一季，这一季的定额全指望它。工人们从旁边走过，看都不看。你见过那蒸汽对黄铜做了什么。\n# en: A huge iron vat in an alchemical works, a hairline crack weeping green steam, workers walking past it without looking.\nscene [ddd11a] = 炼金工坊里一口巨大的铁桶，一道细缝正渗出绿色的蒸汽，工人们从旁边走过，看都不看。\n# en: Report it to the Guild masters\nopt.left.label [0fdc16] = 报告给公会长老\n# en: The masters close the seventh vat for a week and the foreman doesn't speak to you again. In the third week the replacement vat arrives, and an old worker shakes your hand without a word.\nopt.left.say.1 [cd1d6c] = 长老们把第七口桶关停了一个星期，工头从此再没和你说过话。第三个星期，替换的桶运到了，一位老工人一言不发地和你握了握手。\n# en: Mend it yourself, quietly\nopt.right.label [b83056] = 自己悄悄修好\n# en: You seal it from the inside in the night, with a sealant of your own. It holds through the season. The foreman never knows. The old worker does, somehow, and leaves an apple on your bench every morning.\nopt.right.say.1 [b14f13] = 你在夜里从内部用自己调的密封剂把它封好。它撑过了这一季。工头始终不知道。那位老工人不知怎么知道了，每天早上在你的工作台上放一个苹果。\n# en: The steam gets you as the seal gives. You wake in the works infirmary with blistered hands, and the vat is closed anyway.\nopt.right.say.2 [e799fa] = 密封失效时蒸汽扑到了你身上。你在工坊的医务室里醒来，双手起了水泡，那口桶还是被关停了。\n\n@event intern_expedition_new_star\n# en: A Star Nobody Charted\ntitle [6ddf06] = 无人记录的星\n# en: The lead gives you tonight's watch at the eyepiece, alone. At three in the morning there's a star in it that isn't on any chart: small, steady, blue. The expedition lead is asleep. By custom, whoever records a new star first may name it.\ntext [bf4e99] = 队长把今晚目镜前的值班交给了你一个人。凌晨三点，目镜里出现了一颗任何星图上都没有的星：小小的，稳定的，蓝色的。考察队长在睡觉。按照惯例，第一个记录新星的人可以为它命名。\n# en: A telescope on a high plateau at night, a student with an eye to the eyepiece and a chart on their knee, a single new star circled in red.\nscene [c44b5d] = 高原上夜色中的一架望远镜，一个学生一只眼贴着目镜，膝上放着星图，一颗新星被红笔圈了出来。\n# en: Wake the lead and share it\nopt.left.label [5148ef] = 叫醒队长一起分享\n# en: The lead comes in a nightshirt and stares until dawn. The star goes in the record under the expedition's name and yours, and the whole camp toasts it at breakfast.\nopt.left.say.1 [9a791d] = 队长穿着睡衣赶来，一直盯到天亮。这颗星以考察队和你的名义记入了记录，全营地在早饭时为它干杯。\n# en: Record it in your own name\nopt.right.label [43c352] = 以你自己的名字记录\n# en: You write it into the log: the time, the place, a name you choose. It's yours for as long as there are charts. At breakfast the lead reads the log twice, and is polite to you all week, which is worse.\nopt.right.say.1 [b9bb49] = 你把它写进日志：时间、位置，还有一个你挑的名字。只要星图还在，它就是你的。早饭时队长把日志读了两遍，接下来一个星期都对你很客气，这更糟。\n\n@event intern_field_station_foal\n# en: The Orphaned Foal\ntitle [5085db] = 失去母亲的幼驹\n# en: On the moor, a hippogriff foal, newborn and alone. The station's rule is plain: you count wild creatures, you don't raise them. The station chief says it won't last the night. It looks at you as if you were the only warm thing on the moor, which you are.\ntext [ed8dba] = 荒原上有一只鹰头马身有翼兽的幼驹，刚出生，孤零零的。站里的规矩很明确：野生动物只数不养。站长说它活不过今晚。它看着你，好像你是荒原上唯一温暖的东西，而你确实是。\n# en: A newborn hippogriff foal shivering in the heather on the moor, a student kneeling with a blanket, the field station lamps far off.\nscene [7b4dbb] = 一只刚出生的鹰头马身有翼兽幼驹在荒原的石楠丛中发抖，一个学生拿着毯子跪在一旁，观察站的灯光远在天边。\n# en: Take it back and raise it\nopt.left.label [f031dc] = 带回去养大\n# en: You feed it every two hours for a month and break the rule every one of them. By summer's end it flies. Back at Lumenhold, Professor Brask hears the story and invites you to supper. \"Rules,\" she says, \"are for people who've never held a foal.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [df3e67] = 你每两个小时喂它一次，喂了一个月，每一次都在违反规矩。到夏末，它能飞了。回到流明堡，布拉斯克教授听说了这件事，请你吃晚饭。“规矩，”她说，“是给从没抱过幼驹的人定的。”\n# en: Count it and leave it\nopt.right.label [16751c] = 记下它，然后离开\n# en: You write it down: one foal, alone, the date. You don't sleep. In the morning the chief tells you its mother came back in the night. You never find out if that's true.\nopt.right.say.1 [fc1a21] = 你把它记了下来：一只幼驹，孤零零的，日期。你没睡着。第二天早上站长告诉你，它的母亲夜里回来了。你永远不知道这是不是真的。\n\n@event intern_thornwick_offer\n# en: An Offer From Thornwick\ntitle [ca717f] = 荆木学院的邀请\n# en: Thornwick's dean asks to see you on your last week. \"Transfer to us,\" she says, sliding a sealed offer across the desk. \"Full scholarship, your own lab bench, a seat in our illusion faculty in time. Lumenhold never valued glamour. We do.\" She's sincere, and that's the danger.\ntext [545e64] = 荆木学院的院长在你最后一个星期请你去见她。“转学到我们这儿来，”她说着把一份封好的录取书推过桌面。“全额奖学金，你自己的实验台，将来幻术系的一个教席。流明堡从不看重魅影。我们看重。”她是真诚的，这正是危险所在。\n# en: A lacquered office at a rival academy, a smiling dean sliding a sealed offer across a desk, a window full of unfamiliar towers.\nscene [6db64f] = 对手学院一间上了漆的办公室，一位微笑的院长把一份封好的录取书推过桌面，窗外满是陌生的塔楼。\n# en: Decline, warmly\nopt.left.label [1a38c7] = 热情地谢绝\n# en: \"Lumenhold is home,\" you say. She smiles as if she expected it. \"The offer stands.\" Back at the castle, Professor Vey hears about it somehow and gives you an approving look that lasts all term.\nopt.left.say.1 [00f1eb] = “流明堡是我的家，”你说。她笑了，好像早料到了。“邀请一直有效。”回到城堡，维伊教授不知怎么听说了，给了你一个赞许的眼神，持续了整整一个学期。\n# en: Keep the offer, unopened\nopt.right.label [a4e05b] = 收下邀请，不拆开\n# en: You keep it, sealed, in the bottom of your trunk. Thornwick sends a stipend \"as a courtesy\". Knowing there's a door out makes some evenings easier, and some harder.\nopt.right.say.1 [25475a] = 你把它封着收在箱底。荆木学院寄来一笔津贴，“聊表心意”。知道有一扇退路的门，让有些夜晚好过一些，也让有些夜晚更难熬。\n\n@event intern_unseen_office_file\n# en: The File With Your Name\ntitle [725538] = 写着你名字的档案\n# en: Sorting files for the Office, you find one with your name on it. It's thin. The clerk has gone to lunch. The last page is about Lumenhold, and the lake, and a tower nobody has opened in a century.\ntext [273c79] = 替署里整理档案时，你发现一份写着你名字的档案。很薄。文书吃午饭去了。最后一页写的是流明堡、那座湖，还有一座一百年没人打开过的塔。\n# en: A windowless office lined with drawers, a student holding open a thin file with their own name on the label, a clerk's empty chair behind them.\nscene [210c9d] = 一间没有窗户、四壁都是抽屉的办公室，一个学生翻开一份标签上写着自己名字的薄档案，身后是文书空着的椅子。\n# en: Read it all\nopt.left.label [f23585] = 全部读完\n# en: They know where you sleep and what you eat, which is unsettling. The last page is mostly blacked out. What's left says the seventh tower \"is to remain closed\", and nothing about why. When the clerk comes back you are filing something else. The clerk looks at you a little too long.\nopt.left.say.1 [9882d7] = 他们知道你睡在哪儿、吃些什么，这让人不安。最后一页大半被涂黑了。剩下的部分只说第七塔“应保持封闭”，没说为什么。文书回来时，你正在整理别的东西。文书看你的时间稍微长了一点。\n# en: Put it back unread\nopt.right.label [154115] = 不看，放回去\n# en: You put it back. The clerk returns, checks the drawer and smiles for the first time all summer. \"The Office notices who doesn't look,\" the clerk says. You didn't look. You'll wonder for years.\nopt.right.say.1 [4506c9] = 你把它放了回去。文书回来，检查了抽屉，这个夏天第一次露出了笑容。“署里会注意到谁不看，”文书说。你没有看。你会为此纳闷好多年。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/pools/letters.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/pools/letters.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event letter_from_a_friend\n# en: A Letter From {friend}\ntitle [eb2348] = {friend}的来信\n# en: {friend} has written. {~Three pages, mostly about nothing, which is the best kind.|Half of it is crossed out and the other half underlined.|There's a drawing in the margin that is probably meant to be you.} {off_campus: The castle, they say, is too quiet without you.|They're away, and they say the quiet there is the wrong kind.}\ntext [28b6ff] = {friend}来信了。{~三页纸，大多在说些没什么要紧的事，这正是最好的那种。|一半划掉了，另一半画了线。|页边有一幅画，大概画的是你。}{off_campus: 信上说，没有你，城堡太安静了。|他们不在学校，信上说那边的安静不对劲。}\n# en: A folded letter on a windowsill, the handwriting crowded to the very edge of the page, a pressed leaf inside.\nscene [26e2f7] = 窗台上一封折好的信，字一直挤到纸边，里面夹着一片压干的叶子。\n# en: Write back at length\nopt.left.label [7bb28c] = 认认真真写封长信\n# en: {~You write until the candle gutters and spend the postage you meant for something else. It's worth it.|Four pages back, and a pressed flower of your own. You pay the extra stamp.|You write back pages and draw them in the margin, just as badly. The postage costs more than you meant it to.}\nopt.left.say.1 [cbff63] = {~你一直写到蜡烛快灭，把原本打算另作他用的钱花在了邮费上。值得。|回了四页，再夹上一朵你自己压的花。你多付了一张邮票。|你回了好几页，还在页边把他们画了进去，画得一样糟。邮费比你打算的贵。}\n# en: Send a short note\nopt.right.label [91c7fe] = 回一张短笺\n# en: {~A few lines, warm enough. They'll know you read it.|\"Missing you. More soon.\" It says enough.|A few lines, and a drawing of them in return. They'll laugh.}\nopt.right.say.1 [755e4a] = {~寥寥几行，温度刚好。他们会知道你读过了。|“想你。回头细说。”这就够了。|寥寥几行，再回赠一幅他们的画像。他们会笑的。}\n# en: {~Your note is so short it reads as cross. You meant it kindly.|Two lines. You can picture their face reading it, and it isn't pleased.|A short note, and no drawing. They'll notice the missing drawing more than the words.}\nopt.right.say.2 [fc7d32] = {~你的短笺短得像在生气。你本意是好的。|两行字。你能想象他们读信时的表情，不太高兴。|一张短笺，没有画。比起字，他们更会注意到少了那幅画。}\n\n@event letter_castle_news\n# en: News From the Castle\ntitle [8eca1f] = 城堡来的消息\n# en: Someone has forwarded the Lumen Ledger to you at home. {~A professor's hat has gone missing.|The lake froze, or thawed, depending on which column you believe.|There's a letter to the editor about the refectory soup, and it is furious.} It's strange to read about the castle from your old bedroom.\ntext [8f0788] = 有人把《流明纪事报》转寄到了你家。{~一位教授的帽子不见了。|湖结冰了，或者化冻了，取决于你信哪个专栏。|有一封写给编辑的读者来信，痛斥膳堂的汤，措辞激烈。}在自己从前的卧室里读城堡的新闻，感觉很奇怪。\n# en: A copy of the academy newssheet, forwarded in an envelope, its corners soft from travelling.\nscene [d5e48b] = 一份装在信封里转寄来的学院报纸，长途颠簸让纸角都软了。\n# en: Read it cover to cover\nopt.left.label [4fc56d] = 从头读到尾\n# en: {~You read every word, even the notices. By the end you're homesick for a place you're not at.|The whole paper, twice. Home feels very far from the castle tonight, and the castle very far from home.|You read every word, down to the furious letter about the soup, and find you miss even the soup.}\nopt.left.say.1 [dd08fe] = {~你读了每一个字，连启事都没放过。读完时，你想念起一个此刻不在的地方。|整份报纸读了两遍。今晚，家离城堡很远，城堡也离家很远。|你读了每一个字，一直读到那封关于汤的愤怒来信，发现自己连那汤都想念。}\n# en: Just the gossip page\nopt.right.label [381870] = 只看八卦版\n# en: {~The gossip is excellent. You laugh out loud and have to explain it to everyone at the table.|Someone you know is in it, for something ridiculous. Perfect.|The gossip is good, but the furious letter about the soup is better. You read it aloud at the table.}\nopt.right.say.1 [97c306] = {~八卦精彩极了。你笑出了声，只好给饭桌上的每个人解释一遍。|上面有个你认识的人，为了件荒唐事。完美。|八卦不错，可那封关于汤的愤怒来信更好。你在饭桌上把它念了出来。}\n# en: {~The gossip is about someone you like, and it's unkind.|There's nothing in it you didn't already know. Somehow that's worse.|The gossip is all about a quarrel you'd rather not hear of. Even the letter about the soup can't save it.}\nopt.right.say.2 [466e09] = {~八卦说的是你喜欢的人，而且不太厚道。|里面没有一件你不知道的事。不知怎么，这更糟。|八卦全是一场你宁愿没听说的争吵。连那封关于汤的来信也救不回来。}\n\n@event letter_writing_home\n# en: Writing Home\ntitle [b3ea96] = 给家里写信\n# en: The placement keeps you busy, and home feels further away than the miles. {~There's ink, paper and a quiet hour.|The lamp in your lodgings is good for writing.|You owe home a letter, and you know it.}\ntext [941b10] = 实习让你忙得团团转，家感觉比实际的路程还要远。{~有墨水，有纸，还有一个安静的钟头。|住处的灯很适合写字。|你欠家里一封信，你自己清楚。}\n# en: A borrowed desk in a strange room, a half-written letter under a lamp, a map of somewhere else pinned on the wall.\nscene [a9923b] = 陌生房间里一张借来的书桌，灯下一封写了一半的信，墙上钉着一张别处的地图。\n# en: Write a proper letter home\nopt.left.label [a92ffc] = 好好给家里写封信\n# en: {~Four pages about the work, the food and the people. The postage is dear from here. You send it anyway.|You tell them everything, and in telling it you find out how much you like it here.|You owe them a letter, so you write a long one, and the debt feels paid with interest. The postage hurts.}\nopt.left.say.1 [6ed788] = {~四页纸，写工作、吃食和这里的人。从这儿寄信邮费很贵。你还是寄了。|你把一切都告诉了他们，说着说着才发现自己有多喜欢这里。|你欠家里一封信，于是写了封长的，感觉连本带利都还上了。邮费让你肉疼。}\n# en: A few lines and back to work\nopt.right.label [b65885] = 写几行就回去干活\n# en: {~Short and cheerful. It'll do, and they'll be glad of it.|\"All well, more soon.\" True, mostly.|A few lines to settle what you owe. They'll be glad of it, and you'll owe them a longer one.}\nopt.right.say.1 [be7409] = {~简短又开心。够了，他们会高兴的。|“一切都好，回头细说。”大体属实。|寥寥几行，算是还了欠的信。他们会高兴的，而你又欠下了一封更长的。}\n# en: {~You write three lines and feel guilty for a week.|The letter is so brief it reads like a receipt.|You pay what you owe with three lines. It doesn't feel paid.}\nopt.right.say.2 [91454d] = {~你写了三行，内疚了一个星期。|信短得像一张收据。|你用三行字还了欠的信。感觉并没有还上。}\n\n@event letter_reading_list\n# en: A Reading List\ntitle [7ffe18] = 书单\n# en: A professor has written over the break, as some of them do, with a reading list for next term: eleven titles, three of them underlined, one with \"(if you dare)\" beside it. {~The handwriting slopes like a staircase.|There's a coffee ring on the corner.}\ntext [11229b] = 有位教授在假期里写信来，有些教授会这样，附上下学期的书单：十一本书，三本画了线，一本旁边写着“（如果你敢的话）”。{~字迹斜得像楼梯。|角落里有个咖啡渍。}\n# en: A long handwritten list of book titles on academy paper, a small inkblot at the bottom where the writer paused.\nscene [9412bb] = 学院信纸上一份长长的手写书单，底部有个小墨点，是写信人停笔的地方。\n# en: Start on the list now\nopt.left.label [df9c89] = 现在就开始读\n# en: {~You read the first two by candlelight while everyone else is resting. The third is the one marked \"if you dare\". You don't, yet.|You make real progress and lose two afternoons of holiday to it. Next term will be easier for it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [b59de3] = {~别人都在休息时，你借着烛光读完了头两本。第三本就是标着“如果你敢的话”的那本。你还不敢。|你进展不小，为此搭进了两个假期的下午。下学期会因此轻松些。}\n# en: Put it under the bed until term\nopt.right.label [4a4931] = 塞到床底下等开学\n# en: {~The list goes under the bed with your winter boots. The holiday stays a holiday.|You don't read a word, and you don't feel a bit sorry.}\nopt.right.say.1 [90ed0a] = {~书单和你的冬靴一起进了床底。假期还是假期。|你一个字也没读，也一点都不后悔。}\n\n@event letter_parcel_from_home\n# en: A Parcel From Home\ntitle [0fa1f2] = 家里寄来的包裹\n# en: A parcel from home, squashed by the post: {~a cake that has become a different shape of cake|a tin of biscuits and a jar of jam|knitted socks and a fruit loaf}, and a letter that says, in four different hands, that everyone is well and the cat is not.\ntext [51b9c8] = 家里寄来的包裹，被邮路压扁了：{~一块已经变成另一种形状的蛋糕|一罐饼干和一罐果酱|织好的袜子和一条水果面包}，还有一封信，四种笔迹轮流写着：大家都好，猫不太好。\n# en: A brown paper parcel tied with string, opened to show a squashed cake, a pair of knitted socks and a short letter.\nscene [324a7a] = 一个用绳子捆着的牛皮纸包裹，打开后露出一块压扁的蛋糕、一双织好的袜子和一封短信。\n# en: Share it with everyone around you\nopt.left.label [c39a82] = 分给身边的每个人\n# en: {~You pass it round and it vanishes in minutes. People you barely know thank you by name the next day.|You share it out to the last crumb. Someone gives you a biscuit back, out of pity.|You cut the fruit loaf into as many slices as there are hands. The socks you keep.}\nopt.left.say.1 [33c1ad] = {~你传了一圈，几分钟就没了。第二天，几个你几乎不认识的人叫着你的名字道谢。|你分到最后一点渣。有人出于同情还了你一块饼干。|你把果干面包切成和伸过来的手一样多的片。袜子你留下了。}\n# en: Keep it for yourself\nopt.right.label [676f93] = 留给自己\n# en: {~You eat it slowly, over a week, a slice a night. It tastes like your kitchen table.|You hide it under your bed and ration it. It lasts, and so does the feeling of home.|You keep the loaf for the cold nights and wear the socks to bed. Both last till the end of the month.}\nopt.right.say.1 [d1c823] = {~你慢慢吃，吃了一周，一晚一片。吃起来像你家的餐桌。|你把它藏在床底下，省着吃。它撑了很久，家的感觉也是。|你把面包留到冷夜里吃，穿着袜子睡觉。两样都撑到了月底。}\n\n@event letter_wrong_address\n# en: Not For You\ntitle [2eac86] = 不是给你的\n# en: A letter arrives that isn't yours: the right house, the wrong name, a perfumed envelope sealed with green wax. It's addressed to someone you've never heard of. The seal is very thin.\ntext [b48d1a] = 来了一封不是你的信：门牌对，名字错，一只喷了香水的信封，用绿色火漆封着。收信人是个你从没听说过的人。封口非常薄。\n# en: A sealed envelope addressed in an unfamiliar hand to a name that isn't the reader's, lying on a windowsill beside a stamp.\nscene [2f479a] = 窗台上一个陌生笔迹的封好的信封，收信人不是读信的人，旁边放着一张邮票。\n# en: Forward it unopened\nopt.left.label [30b9df] = 原封不动转寄\n# en: You write \"not known here, please try again\" on the back, add a stamp of your own and send it on its way. You'll never know what it said, which is how it should be.\nopt.left.say.1 [9a3fdb] = 你在背面写上“查无此人，请重新投递”，贴上自己的邮票寄走了。你永远不会知道信里写了什么，这本来就该如此。\n# en: Open it, just to see\nopt.right.label [d16d56] = 拆开看看\n# en: It's a love letter, and a bad one, full of rhymes on \"moon\". You reseal it carefully and send it on, feeling worse than you expected.\nopt.right.say.1 [b2636c] = 是一封情书，还是一封很糟的情书，满篇都押着“月亮”的韵。你小心地重新封好寄走，心里比想象的更不是滋味。\n\n@event letter_bookshop_bill\n# en: A Bill You Don't Remember\ntitle [d2015b] = 一张你不记得的账单\n# en: A bill from the village bookshop for a book called \"On Small Explosions\", which you have never seen, never borrowed and never wanted. The bookseller writes that you signed for it. The signature looks a little like yours.\ntext [674d82] = 山下小镇书店寄来的账单，买的是一本叫《论小型爆炸》的书，你从没见过，从没借过，也从没想要过。书商说你签收了。那个签名看上去有点像你的。\n# en: A crisp bill from a bookshop, an itemised list with one title circled, held up beside a student's puzzled face.\nscene [ffa128] = 一张书店的账单，列着清单，其中一本书名被圈了起来，举在一个困惑的学生脸旁。\n# en: Pay it and be done\nopt.left.label [9a6124] = 付了了事\n# en: You pay it. A week later the book arrives, and it's actually quite good. You still don't know who signed for it.\nopt.left.say.1 [4adf54] = 你付了钱。一周后书到了，其实还挺好看的。你仍然不知道是谁签收的。\n# en: Write back and dispute it\nopt.right.label [00c863] = 回信提出异议\n# en: You write a polite, firm letter. The bookseller writes back: wrong student, same name, profuse apologies, and a bookmark by way of sorry.\nopt.right.say.1 [2d6831] = 你写了一封礼貌而坚决的信。书商回信说：搞错了学生，同名，连连道歉，还附上一枚书签聊表歉意。\n# en: The bookseller replies with a copy of the signature and a note that she has \"dealt with your sort before\". You pay, and add a stiff note of your own.\nopt.right.say.2 [cabe9d] = 书商回信附了一份签名副本，还说她“以前就对付过你这种人”。你付了钱，也附上一张措辞生硬的字条。\n# en: The bookseller replies with a copy of the signature and a note that she has \"dealt with your sort before\". Your purse is empty, so you work the bill off, shelving her stock for a long, dusty afternoon.\nopt.right.say.3 [1d253f] = 书商回信附上了签名的抄件，还写着她“以前就对付过你这种人”。你的钱袋空空，只好去帮她上架整整一个尘土飞扬的下午，把账抵掉。\n\n@event letter_old_friend\n# en: From Before\ntitle [5c8cd8] = 从前\n# en: A letter from someone you grew up with, from before Lumenhold. {~They're apprenticed to a cooper now.|They're getting married in the spring.|They've moved to the city and hate it.} The letter asks what magic is like. It's a good question, and you don't know how to answer it.\ntext [606db9] = 一个从小一起长大的人写来的信，那是流明堡之前的事了。{~TA 现在给一个箍桶匠当学徒。|TA 春天要结婚了。|TA 搬进城里，讨厌那儿。}信里问魔法是什么样的。这是个好问题，你不知道怎么回答。\n# en: A letter in a familiar childhood hand, with a small drawing of a village street in the margin.\nscene [050c3d] = 一封熟悉的童年笔迹写的信，页边画着一条小镇街道。\n# en: Write back pages and pages\nopt.left.label [a415ae] = 写上一页又一页\n# en: You try to explain magic, and the castle, and what it's like to fail an exam in front of a ghost. It takes four pages and an extra stamp. It's the best letter you've written.\nopt.left.say.1 [0d9849] = 你试着解释魔法，解释城堡，解释当着一个幽灵考砸是什么感觉。写了四页，多贴了一张邮票。这是你写过最好的信。\n# en: Send a postcard of the towers\nopt.right.label [5c4590] = 寄一张七塔明信片\n# en: A postcard of the seven towers, and \"It's hard to explain. Come and see?\" You both know they won't. It's a kind postcard all the same.\nopt.right.say.1 [5c3765] = 一张七塔明信片，写着：“很难解释。来看看？”你们都知道对方不会来。但这仍是一张温暖的明信片。\n\n@event letter_ledger_competition\n# en: The Holiday Essay Prize\ntitle [413b7f] = 假期征文奖\n# en: The Lumen Ledger's holiday issue has found you: an essay prize, \"What I Did Not Do This Holiday\", five hundred words, the winner printed on the front page and paid in crowns.\ntext [5510d3] = 《流明纪事报》的假期特刊找到了你：一个征文奖，题目是“我这个假期没做的事”，五百字，获奖作品登头版，奖金以克朗支付。\n# en: A folded copy of a student newspaper with a competition notice circled in red ink.\nscene [ca3ca9] = 一份折起来的学生报纸，一则征文启事被红墨水圈了出来。\n# en: Write an entry\nopt.left.label [e2f007] = 写一篇投稿\n# en: Your essay about the lake you didn't swim in wins. It's printed on the front page, a little cut, under your name spelled right for once.\nopt.left.say.1 [162172] = 你那篇关于没去游泳的那片湖的文章获奖了。它登上了头版，删了一点，署名难得拼对了。\n# en: You write a good essay that doesn't win. The winner wrote about a goat. Everyone agrees the goat was the right choice.\nopt.left.say.2 [4a0cae] = 你写了篇好文章，但没获奖。获奖的写的是一只山羊。大家一致认为选山羊是对的。\n# en: Let someone else win\nopt.right.label [23e569] = 让别人去赢\n# en: You don't write a word of it, and spend the afternoon doing one of the things you didn't do.\nopt.right.say.1 [85c954] = 你一个字也没写，把下午花在了做一件你没做过的事上。\n\n@event letter_from_a_firstyear\n# en: Advice, Please\ntitle [82c643] = 求教\n# en: A first-year you once showed the way to the library has written to you over the break. They're thinking of leaving. The letter is short, crossed out in places, and ends: \"Did you ever feel like this?\"\ntext [9ac181] = 一个你曾给指过去图书馆的路的一年级生，在假期里给你写信。TA 在考虑退学。信很短，有些地方划掉了，结尾是：“你有过这种感觉吗？”\n# en: A short anxious letter on cheap paper, several words crossed out, a small blot where the writer pressed too hard.\nscene [ed0b56] = 一封写在廉价纸上的焦虑短信，几个词被划掉，一处墨迹是写信人用力过猛留下的。\n# en: Write back honestly, at length\nopt.left.label [e52764] = 认真地写一封长信\n# en: You tell them about your own worst month, which isn't easy to write. Next term they find you at breakfast and say they stayed because of it.\nopt.left.say.1 [f41b21] = 你跟 TA 讲了你自己最糟的那个月，这并不好写。下学期，TA 在早餐时找到你，说就是因为那封信才留下来的。\n# en: Send a few kind lines\nopt.right.label [095653] = 写几句暖心话\n# en: \"Yes. It passes. Come back.\" It's short, and it's true. You hope it's enough. At the start of term you see them in the queue for the barge.\nopt.right.say.1 [0f9557] = “有过。会过去的。回来吧。”很短，但是真话。你希望这就够了。开学时，你在登驳船的队伍里看见了 TA。\n\n@event letter_registrar_forms\n# en: Forms in Triplicate\ntitle [13e7ce] = 一式三份的表格\n# en: The registrar's office has sent forms, in triplicate, about next term: your courses, your board, your \"intentions regarding extracurricular activities\". There is a box marked \"Other (explain)\". It is very small.\ntext [f55f6a] = 教务处寄来了下学期的表格，一式三份：你的课程、你的食宿、你的“课外活动意向”。有一栏写着“其他（请说明）”。格子非常小。\n# en: A thick envelope of forms with the academy's seal, three identical copies of everything fanned out on a table.\nscene [808b43] = 一个盖着学院火漆的厚信封装满表格，所有东西都有三份一模一样的，在桌上摊成扇形。\n# en: Fill them in properly now\nopt.left.label [3b10e6] = 现在就认真填好\n# en: An afternoon of copying your own name three times. At the start of term, your enrolment takes four minutes while everyone else queues. Smug is a feeling too.\nopt.left.say.1 [ab1a4f] = 一个下午都在把自己的名字抄三遍。开学时，别人都在排队，你四分钟就选完了课。得意也是一种感觉。\n# en: Leave them until the barge\nopt.right.label [2dd2ac] = 留到驳船上再说\n# en: You fill them in on the barge, on your knee, in the wind. One copy goes over the side. The registrar sighs at you all the way through enrolment.\nopt.right.say.1 [8ebeff] = 你在驳船上，垫着膝盖，顶着风填表。有一份被吹进了湖里。整个选课过程中，教务员都冲着你叹气。\n\n@event letter_lyra_drills\n# en: A List of Drills\ntitle [04f721] = 训练清单\n# en: Lyra has written. It's a list of drills, numbered, with a note at the bottom: \"Do these. You're too slow. Everyone is.\" There's a postscript under that, scratched out and rewritten three times and then scratched out for good.\ntext [d1f640] = 莱拉来信了。是一份训练清单，编了号，底下一行：“照做。你太慢了。大家都太慢。”再下面有一句附言，划掉重写了三次，最后彻底划掉了。\n# en: A letter of numbered duelling drills in crisp handwriting, a scratched-out postscript at the bottom.\nscene [302edd] = 一封字迹利落、列着编号决斗训练的信，底部一句附言被划掉了。\n# en: Do every drill, and send your times\nopt.left.label [15f2f8] = 全部练完，寄去成绩\n# en: You do them in the cold every morning and post her your times. Her reply is one word: \"Adequate.\" It's underlined, which from Lyra is a bouquet.\nopt.left.say.1 [03a70a] = 你每天早上顶着寒气练完，把成绩寄给她。她的回信只有一个词：“尚可。”还画了线，这在莱拉那里等于一束花。\n# en: Ask what the postscript said\nopt.right.label [435145] = 问附言写了什么\n# en: Two weeks later, one line: \"It said: write back.\" Then, smaller: \"You did. Good.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [2bbcc6] = 两周后，一行字：“写的是：回信。”下面小一点：“你回了。很好。”\n# en: She ignores the question entirely and sends a second list, longer, with no postscript at all.\nopt.right.say.2 [cab3bf] = 她对这个问题完全不理，又寄来第二份清单，更长，一句附言也没有。\n\n@event letter_lyra_book\n# en: Foundations of Flame\ntitle [19e259] = 火焰基础\n# en: A parcel from Lyra: her own copy of \"Foundations of Flame\", every margin crowded with notes in red ink. On the flyleaf: \"You'll only underline the wrong bits. So I've done it for you.\" The handwriting goes a little uneven at the end.\ntext [5b0778] = 莱拉寄来的包裹：她自己的那本《火焰基础》，每一处页边都挤满了红墨水的批注。扉页上写着：“你只会画错重点。所以我替你画好了。”最后几个字有点歪。\n# en: A worn copy of a spellbook unwrapped from brown paper, every margin full of small, fierce annotations in red ink.\nscene [652cfa] = 一本翻旧了的咒语书从牛皮纸里拆出来，每处页边都是红墨水写的小而凶的批注。\n# en: Read every note in the margins\nopt.left.label [4a9afd] = 读完页边每一条批注\n# en: Her notes are sharp, funny and occasionally rude about the author. On page ninety there's a small flame drawn beside a paragraph, and your initials.\nopt.left.say.1 [891eea] = 她的批注犀利、好笑，偶尔还对作者出言不逊。第九十页的一段旁边画着一朵小火苗，还有你名字的缩写。\n# en: Annotate it back and return it\nopt.right.label [4d3ceb] = 批注回去再寄还\n# en: You add your own notes in green and post it back at your own expense. Her reply says you're wrong about chapter four. She keeps the book, and your notes, and doesn't cross them out.\nopt.right.say.1 [0b0409] = 你用绿墨水添上自己的批注，自掏邮费寄了回去。她回信说你对第四章的理解是错的。她留下了书，也留下了你的批注，一条都没划掉。\n\n@event letter_tobias_goats\n# en: Four Pages of Goats\ntitle [1d32a6] = 四页山羊\n# en: Tobias has written four pages, mostly about goats: one has escaped, one has eaten a hat, and one is, he writes, \"going through something\". Tucked in the fold is a recipe for his gran's barley soup, \"for when the castle food gets you down\".\ntext [38e597] = 托比亚斯写了四页，大部分是关于山羊的：一只跑了，一只吃了顶帽子，还有一只，他写道，“正在经历一些事”。信纸折缝里夹着他奶奶的大麦汤菜谱，“城堡的饭让你难受时喝”。\n# en: A long letter in a big, careful hand with a drawing of a goat in the corner and a recipe card tucked into the fold.\nscene [cdd1b2] = 一封长信，字又大又认真，角落里画着一只山羊，折缝里夹着一张菜谱卡。\n# en: Make the soup\nopt.left.label [ae568b] = 做那道汤\n# en: You buy the barley and borrow a pot. The soup is thick and plain and exactly what you needed. You write and tell him so; he writes back that his gran says you're welcome.\nopt.left.say.1 [524420] = 你买了大麦，借了口锅。汤又稠又朴素，正是你需要的。你写信告诉他；他回信说奶奶说不客气。\n# en: Write back with a story of your own\nopt.right.label [fdb4e3] = 回他一个你的故事\n# en: You send him the story of the ghost in the bathtub, at length. His reply says he read it to the goats, and that the one going through something seemed to feel better.\nopt.right.say.1 [920678] = 你把浴缸里那个幽灵的故事详详细细地寄给他。他回信说念给山羊听了，那只正在经历一些事的好像好些了。\n\n@event letter_tobias_seeds\n# en: Seeds for a Windowsill\ntitle [404277] = 窗台上的种子\n# en: A packet of seeds from Tobias, tied with garden twine. \"For your windowsill. They'll keep you company. Water them Tuesdays.\" Then, squashed in at the bottom: \"Sorry if that's soppy.\"\ntext [bee9b5] = 托比亚斯寄来一包种子，用园艺麻绳系着。“放你窗台上。它们会陪着你。周二浇水。”底下挤着一行：“要是太肉麻了，抱歉。”\n# en: A small paper packet of seeds tied with garden twine, a note in big careful handwriting beside it.\nscene [288954] = 一小包用园艺麻绳系着的纸袋种子，旁边一张字又大又认真的字条。\n# en: Plant them straight away\nopt.left.label [31f418] = 马上种下\n# en: Three shoots by the end of the month, leaning toward the light. You water them on Tuesdays and think of him on the other days too.\nopt.left.say.1 [1f93d2] = 到月底冒出了三棵芽，朝着光倾斜。你每周二浇水，其他日子也会想起他。\n# en: Write back that it isn't soppy\nopt.right.label [0e8c61] = 回信说一点也不肉麻\n# en: You write that it isn't soppy, and then a whole page about why. His reply is two lines long and you can tell he went red writing it.\nopt.right.say.1 [4e2c28] = 你写道一点也不肉麻，然后用整整一页解释为什么。他的回信只有两行，你看得出他写的时候脸红了。\n\n@event letter_seraphine_card\n# en: Half a Sentence\ntitle [604144] = 半句话\n# en: An envelope from Seraphine with a single tarot card inside, the Wheel, and half a sentence on the back in silver ink: \"Not this week but the one after, don't take the\"\ntext [e989bc] = 瑟拉芬寄来的信封里只有一张塔罗牌，命运之轮，背面用银墨水写着半句话：“不是这周而是下周，别走那条”\n# en: A single tarot card in an envelope, with half a sentence written across the back in silver ink.\nscene [ca331d] = 信封里一张塔罗牌，背面用银墨水写着半句话。\n# en: Try to work out the rest\nopt.left.label [4089a7] = 试着猜出后半句\n# en: You spend an evening with the card and every book on tarot you can find, and spend the following week avoiding stairs, carts and soup. Nothing happens. Probably.\nopt.left.say.1 [e71146] = 你对着那张牌和能找到的每本塔罗书耗了一个晚上，接下来一周躲着楼梯、马车和汤。什么也没发生。大概吧。\n# en: Write and ask her to finish it\nopt.right.label [b3478c] = 写信请她写完\n# en: Her reply comes ten days later, and finishes the sentence: \"…long way round. Or do. It's lovely either way.\" It is, when you take it.\nopt.right.say.1 [172042] = 十天后她的回信到了，补完了那句话：“……远路。或者走也行。怎样都很好。”等你真走的时候，确实很好。\n\n@event letter_seraphine_painting\n# en: A Painting of You\ntitle [ef73c2] = 你的画像\n# en: Seraphine has sent a small watercolour: you, reading under a tree in blossom, lit from one side. It is very good. You have never sat under that tree, and it isn't spring.\ntext [e00bbd] = 瑟拉芬寄来一幅小小的水彩画：你在一棵开花的树下读书，光从一侧照来。画得非常好。你从没在那棵树下坐过，而且现在不是春天。\n# en: A small watercolour of a student reading under a tree in blossom, unwrapped from tissue paper on a desk.\nscene [62b63e] = 书桌上，从薄纸里拆出一幅小水彩，画着一个学生在开花的树下读书。\n# en: Hang it by your bed\nopt.left.label [fe8501] = 挂在床边\n# en: It's the first thing you see each morning. It makes you look kinder than you feel, and you start, a little, to feel it.\nopt.left.say.1 [f624b8] = 这是你每天早上看见的第一样东西。画里的你比你感觉中的自己更温柔，而你开始，一点点地，真的有那种感觉了。\n# en: Write and ask when she saw this\nopt.right.label [32b429] = 写信问她什么时候看到的\n# en: She writes back, \"Next spring,\" and nothing else, and then a second letter the same day: \"I hope that's all right.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [7d6c58] = 她回信写道：“明年春天。”别的什么也没有。同一天又来了第二封：“希望这样没关系。”\n\n@event letter_corvin_bakery\n# en: The Best Bakery in the Kingdom\ntitle [adfc9f] = 王国最好的面包房\n# en: A letter from Corvin, with a map of a town you've never heard of and a cross on one corner. \"Found the best bakery in the kingdom. Can't afford it. Thought you should know where it is, saint, in case you're ever richer than me. Low bar.\"\ntext [a47484] = 科尔文来信，附了一张你从没听说过的小镇的地图，一角打了个叉。“找到了全王国最好的面包房。买不起。觉得你该知道它在哪儿，圣人，万一哪天你比我有钱呢。门槛很低。”\n# en: A hand-drawn map of an unfamiliar town with a cross in ink over one corner, a postscript crammed into the margin.\nscene [b16f79] = 一张手绘的陌生小镇地图，一角用墨水打了个叉，页边挤着一句附言。\n# en: Send him a few crowns for a pastry\nopt.left.label [e868ed] = 寄几枚克朗让他买点心\n# en: You fold a few crowns into the reply. His next letter is mostly about the pastry, in loving detail, and then, at the very end and without a joke: \"Thank you.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [868bcb] = 你在回信里夹了几枚克朗。他下一封信几乎全在讲那块点心，细节满怀深情，然后在最末尾，没开玩笑：“谢谢。”\n# en: Write back a map of your own\nopt.right.label [06c636] = 回他一张你的地图\n# en: You draw him a map of every place you've found that he hasn't, with crosses and a key. He writes back that two of them are wrong and one of them is brilliant.\nopt.right.say.1 [15af53] = 你给他画了一张地图，标出你找到而他没找到的每个地方，打上叉，配了图例。他回信说其中两个是错的，有一个绝了。\n\n@event letter_corvin_no_jokes\n# en: A Letter With No Jokes\ntitle [69148b] = 一封不开玩笑的信\n# en: Corvin's letter has no jokes in it. You read it twice to be sure. It says the place is too quiet, that he keeps turning to say something to you, and that he isn't used to that. It isn't signed \"your favourite thief\". It's just signed.\ntext [04e616] = 科尔文的信里没有一句玩笑。你读了两遍确认。信上说那地方太安静了，他总忍不住转身想跟你说点什么，还不习惯这样。落款不是“你最喜欢的小偷”。只是落了款。\n# en: A single short page in quick handwriting, with no doodles and no crossings-out, folded very small.\nscene [12b61e] = 一页短短的信，字迹飞快，没有涂鸦，没有涂改，折得很小。\n# en: Write back just as plainly\nopt.left.label [82f63a] = 同样坦白地回信\n# en: You write back with no jokes either, and it's harder than you thought. His reply comes by return of post, and the first line is a joke, and the second line isn't.\nopt.left.say.1 [19f9f9] = 你回信时也没开玩笑，这比你想的难。他的回信很快就到了，第一行是个玩笑，第二行不是。\n# en: Send a joke to make it easier\nopt.right.label [e1a744] = 寄个笑话缓和一下\n# en: You send him the worst joke you know. He replies with a worse one, and you're both on safer ground. You wonder, later, if you missed something.\nopt.right.say.1 [ca8efc] = 你把你知道的最烂的笑话寄给他。他回了个更烂的，你们都回到了安全地带。后来你想，自己是不是错过了什么。\n\n@event letter_mir_blueprint\n# en: A Letter That Folds Itself\ntitle [059cc4] = 会自己折起来的信\n# en: Mir's letter starts folding itself back up while you're still reading it. Inside is a blueprint for a self-folding letter (\"PROBABLY works!!\") and a page of excited tangents about hinges.\ntext [08a336] = 你还没读完，米尔的信就开始自己折回去了。里面是一张自折信的设计图（“大概能用！！”），外加一页关于铰链的兴奋跑题。\n# en: A letter folding itself back up on a desk like a paper bird, a blueprint with arrows and exclamation marks spread out underneath.\nscene [30a2dd] = 一封信在桌上像纸鸟一样自己折起来，下面摊着一张画满箭头和感叹号的设计图。\n# en: Build one yourself\nopt.left.label [ce7e08] = 自己做一个\n# en: Wire, wax paper and one small enchantment. Your letter folds itself into a perfect swan and then, unprompted, into a slightly worse frog. Mir's reply is all exclamation marks.\nopt.left.say.1 [faa571] = 铁丝、蜡纸和一个小小的附魔。你的信把自己折成一只完美的天鹅，然后自作主张地又折成一只稍差的青蛙。米尔的回信全是感叹号。\n# en: Write back with improvements\nopt.right.label [d39ffe] = 回信提改进意见\n# en: You suggest a stiffer hinge and a slower fold. Mir writes back that you are a genius and that they've already tried it and it exploded, but in a promising way.\nopt.right.say.1 [e737ed] = 你建议铰链硬一点、折得慢一点。米尔回信说你是天才，还说 TA 已经试过了，炸了，但炸得很有前途。\n\n@event letter_mir_ticking\n# en: Something That Ticks\ntitle [d34a62] = 会滴答响的东西\n# en: A small brass thing the size of a walnut, ticking softly, with a note in Mir's hurried hand: \"It's a metronome. Probably. It's set to a resting heart rate. Mine, actually, it took ages. Anyway!!\"\ntext [d5b61e] = 一个核桃大小的黄铜小东西，轻轻滴答作响，附着米尔潦草的字条：“这是个节拍器。大概吧。调成了静息心率。其实是我的，调了好久。总之！！”\n# en: A tiny brass mechanism the size of a walnut ticking softly on a pillow, a note in hurried handwriting beside it.\nscene [94b0cf] = 枕头上一个核桃大小的黄铜小机关轻轻滴答作响，旁边一张字迹潦草的字条。\n# en: Keep it on your pillow\nopt.left.label [4d185e] = 放在枕头上\n# en: It ticks you to sleep every night, slightly faster than a resting heart rate. You don't mention that. Some nights it keeps you awake.\nopt.left.say.1 [aab89f] = 它每晚滴答着送你入睡，比静息心率稍微快一点。你没提这事。有些晚上它让你睡不着。\n# en: Write back how much you like it\nopt.right.label [e76305] = 回信说你有多喜欢\n# en: You write that you like it very much. Mir's reply is three pages about escapements, and then at the very end, very small: \"good. i'm glad. good.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [5b29e2] = 你写道你非常喜欢。米尔回了三页关于擒纵机构的话，然后在最末尾，很小的字：“好。我很高兴。好。”\n\n@event letter_kael_three_lines\n# en: Three Lines\ntitle [3b7ab0] = 三行字\n# en: A letter from Kael. Three lines, in careful handwriting, with a great deal of white paper below them: the weather, a star he saw, and \"I hope you are well. That is the whole letter.\"\ntext [e2a01b] = 凯尔的来信。三行字，字迹工整，下面是大片的空白：天气，他看见的一颗星，还有“希望你一切都好。信就这些。”\n# en: A sheet of good paper with only three short lines on it in careful handwriting, a great deal of white space below.\nscene [0c0d59] = 一张上好的信纸，只有三行工整的短句，下面大片空白。\n# en: Write back three lines\nopt.left.label [b6a6e8] = 回他三行\n# en: You write back three lines: your weather, a star of your own, and that you're well. His reply comes promptly. It has four lines. You count them twice.\nopt.left.say.1 [98ce2c] = 你回了三行：你那边的天气，你自己的一颗星，还有你很好。他的回信很快就到了。有四行。你数了两遍。\n# en: Write back three pages\nopt.right.label [74ab93] = 回他三页\n# en: You write three pages and pay the extra postage. His reply takes a long time and says only: \"Thank you. I read it four times. I do not know how to answer so much.\" He keeps it, though.\nopt.right.say.1 [a1028b] = 你写了三页，多付了邮费。他的回信隔了很久，只写着：“谢谢。我读了四遍。我不知道怎么回答这么多。”不过他把信留着了。\n\n@event letter_kael_star_chart\n# en: The Sky on Your Birthday\ntitle [480746] = 你出生那晚的星空\n# en: Kael has sent a star chart, inked by hand, with one cluster circled in pencil. The note says: \"The sky on the night you were born, from here. I checked the tables twice. It is a good sky.\"\ntext [b5c1fa] = 凯尔寄来一张手绘的星图，用铅笔圈出了一小簇星星。字条上写：“你出生那晚的天空，从这里看。我查了两遍星表。是片好天空。”\n# en: A hand-inked star chart unfolded on a bed, one small cluster of stars circled in pencil, a short note pinned to the corner.\nscene [6c2418] = 一张手绘墨线星图摊在床上，一小簇星星被铅笔圈起，角上别着一张短字条。\n# en: Find those stars yourself tonight\nopt.left.label [e14a16] = 今晚亲自去找那些星星\n# en: You stand outside in the cold until you find them, low over the roofs. Your feet go numb. It is a good sky; he was right.\nopt.left.say.1 [e70d0d] = 你在寒风里站着，直到在屋顶上方低低的地方找到了它们。你的脚冻麻了。是片好天空；他说得对。\n# en: Thank him plainly\nopt.right.label [790b3e] = 坦白地谢谢他\n# en: You write that it's the kindest thing anyone has sent you. His reply says \"You are welcome.\" Below that, smaller, as if added later: \"Very.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [3fed19] = 你写道，这是别人送过你最体贴的东西。他回信写：“不客气。”下面小一点，像是后来加上的：“非常。”\n\n@event letter_nessa_deadline\n# en: URGENT\ntitle [44fe30] = 急件\n# en: A letter from Nessa, URGENT underlined three times: the Ledger needs three hundred words on holidays for the first issue of term, \"interesting ones please, the last six were about weather\". Deadline circled. Twice.\ntext [1b7daf] = 内莎来信，“急件”两个字下面画了三道线：《纪事报》开学第一期需要三百字的假期见闻，“要有意思的，上六篇都在写天气”。截稿日期圈了出来。圈了两次。\n# en: A letter with URGENT written across the top in capitals and underlined three times, a deadline circled at the bottom.\nscene [cadd6e] = 一封信顶上用大写写着“急件”，画了三道线，底部的截稿日期被圈了起来。\n# en: Write the piece\nopt.left.label [01f165] = 写这篇稿子\n# en: You spend an evening on three hundred words and post them. Nessa cuts eighty, prints the rest on page four and writes back \"Good. Rewrite the ending next time.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [4915d3] = 你花一个晚上写了三百字寄去。内莎删了八十字，把剩下的登在第四版，回信说：“不错。下次把结尾重写。”\n# en: Write back off the record\nopt.right.label [6db94e] = 回一封不公开的信\n# en: You write back with no piece at all, just how she is. Her reply has no capitals in it. It's the longest thing she's ever written that she isn't going to print.\nopt.right.say.1 [9ae8c1] = 你一篇稿子也没写，只问她过得怎么样。她的回信里没有一个大写词。这是她写过的最长的一篇不打算刊登的东西。\n\n@event letter_nessa_off_record\n# en: Off the Record\ntitle [a5d3b6] = 不公开\n# en: Nessa's letter has OFF THE RECORD at the top, and then, for once, short sentences with nothing to sell. She's tired. The holidays are long. She keeps writing headlines about you in her head and crossing them out.\ntext [ec4fcf] = 内莎的信顶上写着“不公开”，下面难得是一些没什么可推销的短句。她累了。假期很长。她总在脑子里给你写标题，又一个个划掉。\n# en: A letter with OFF THE RECORD written at the top and then, for once, a page of short, unhurried sentences.\nscene [2fa8be] = 一封信顶上写着“不公开”，下面难得是一整页简短而从容的句子。\n# en: Reply off the record too\nopt.left.label [f9cfa8] = 也回一封不公开的\n# en: You write back slowly and honestly, which costs you more than you expected. Her answer is posted the day your letter must have reached her, which means she wrote it the moment she read yours.\nopt.left.say.1 [179402] = 你慢慢地、诚实地回了信，代价比你想的大。她的回信恰好在你的信寄到那天寄出，说明她一读完你的信就动笔了。\n# en: Ask to see the headlines\nopt.right.label [cb2616] = 要看看那些标题\n# en: She sends three. One is unprintable, one is very sweet and one is \"LOCAL STUDENT STILL NOT REPLYING\", which is fair. You reply at once.\nopt.right.say.1 [8d60fc] = 她寄来三个。一个没法登，一个很甜，还有一个是“本地学生仍未回信”，这倒公平。你立刻回了信。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/pools/money.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/pools/money.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event aid_notice\n# en: The Hardship Notice\ntitle [a50a45] = 困难补助告示\n# en: {A small notice by the bursar's door reads \"Hardship fund. Applications in writing. All circumstances considered.\"|The hardship notice again, under a new one about lost owls.|Someone has underlined \"all circumstances\" on the hardship notice.} {Three students are pretending not to queue.|The ink on the forms is still wet.|The bursar's door is ajar.}\ntext [071496] = {财务官门边贴着一张小告示：“困难基金。书面申请。各种情况均予考虑。”|又是那张困难补助告示，上面盖了一张寻找走失猫头鹰的新告示。|有人在困难补助告示的“各种情况”下面画了线。}{三个学生假装没在排队。|表格上的墨迹还没干。|财务官的门半开着。}\n# en: A notice pinned beside the bursar's office door, half covered by a newer notice about lost owls, a queue of three students pretending not to queue.\nscene [b50ea8] = 财务官办公室门边钉着一张告示，被一张关于走失猫头鹰的新告示盖住了一半，三个学生假装没在排队。\n# en: Fill in the forms\nopt.left.label [396803] = 填写表格\n# en: {Four pages. One asks you to list everything you own. It doesn't take long, and that is the hard part.|You fill in the forms at a library table with your arm around them so nobody can see.|The bursar's door is open, so you hand the forms in yourself. \"All circumstances,\" the bursar says, reading. \"Yes.\"}\nopt.left.say.1 [5ae318] = {四页纸。其中一页要你列出你拥有的所有东西。没花多少时间，这才是难受的地方。|你在图书馆的桌子上填表，用胳膊挡着，不让别人看见。|财务室的门开着，你就亲手把表交了进去。“一切情况，”财务主管边看边说，“是的。”}\n# en: Not yet\nopt.right.label [8dd27c] = 先不了\n# en: {You walk past. Your pride is intact, and so is your empty purse.|Another month, you tell yourself. You have a button left.|You read \"all circumstances\" twice and walk past the open door. Not yet.}\nopt.right.say.1 [6ca79a] = {你走了过去。你的自尊完好无损，你的空钱袋也是。|再等一个月，你对自己说。你还剩一颗纽扣。|你把“一切情况”读了两遍，从敞开的门前走了过去。还不到时候。}\n\n@event aid_interview\n# en: The Bursar Will See You\ntitle [7f82b3] = 财务官要见你\n# en: The bursar has your forms open under her hand and a ledger the size of a tombstone at her elbow. \"The fund is small,\" she says, \"and the hands are many. Tell me why it should be yours. Or, if you'd rather not beg, I can lend you sixty crowns against your future, at no interest.\"\ntext [17eea6] = 财务官一只手按着你的申请表，手肘边是一本墓碑那么大的账簿。“基金很少，”她说，“伸手的人很多。告诉我为什么该给你。或者，如果你不想求人，我可以以你的前途作抵押借给你六十克朗，不收利息。”\n# en: A small cluttered office, a grey-haired bursar behind a ledger as large as a tombstone, a student sitting very upright on a hard chair.\nscene [9486c8] = 一间狭小杂乱的办公室，一位灰发的财务官坐在一本墓碑大小的账簿后面，一个学生笔直地坐在一张硬椅子上。\n# en: Make your case\nopt.left.label [d1740a] = 陈述你的理由\n# en: You tell her plainly, and she listens without pity, which helps. \"One term,\" she says, and stamps it. \"Come back if it's still hard.\" Walking out, you feel both lighter and smaller.\nopt.left.say.1 [5528de] = 你直截了当地说了，她听着，不带怜悯，这反倒有帮助。“一个学期，”她说着盖了章。“如果还是困难，再来。”走出去时，你觉得轻松了，也觉得自己变小了。\n# en: She hears you out, and then reads you the list of people ahead of you. Some of their circumstances are worse. \"Next year,\" she says, kindly. You almost wish she hadn't been kind.\nopt.left.say.2 [fc9f6d] = 她听你说完，然后把排在你前面的人的名单念给你听。有些人的情况更糟。“明年吧，”她和气地说。你几乎希望她没那么和气。\n# en: Take the loan\nopt.right.label [e30491] = 接受贷款\n# en: Sixty crowns, counted out in old coins, and your name in the tombstone ledger. \"Eight a month, when you can, and a little less at the end,\" she says. \"I'm a patient woman. The ledger is more patient still.\" It sits on your shoulder from then on.\nopt.right.say.1 [4e2ca5] = 六十克朗，用旧硬币数给你，你的名字记进了墓碑账簿。“每月八克朗，付得起的时候再付，最后一次少一点，”她说，“我是个有耐心的女人。账簿比我更有耐心。”从那以后，它一直压在你肩上。\n\n@event favour_vey_letter\n# en: A Discreet Errand\ntitle [14d2e3] = 一件隐秘的差事\n# en: Professor Vey stops you in an empty corridor with a sealed letter and a silver coin. \"A letter for the village. The house with the blue shutters. Into the hand of whoever opens the door, and not a word.\" Her smile is magnificent and gives nothing away.\ntext [adde6b] = 维伊教授在一条空走廊里拦住你，手里拿着一封封好的信和一枚银币。“一封送到村里的信。蓝色百叶窗的那户人家。交到开门的人手里，一个字也别说。”她的笑容光彩夺目，却什么也没透露。\n# en: A professor in a violet coat holding out a sealed letter and a silver coin between two gloved fingers, in an empty theatre corridor.\nscene [6d6c0c] = 一位穿紫罗兰色外套的教授在空荡荡的剧场走廊里，用戴着手套的两根手指递出一封封好的信和一枚银币。\n# en: Deliver it, unread\nopt.left.label [edeb48] = 送去，不看\n# en: The house with the blue shutters is at the far end of the village, in the rain. An old man opens the door, reads the name on the letter and starts to cry. You walk back soaked and never ask.\nopt.left.say.1 [1431e1] = 蓝色百叶窗的那户人家在村子最远的一头，下着雨。一位老人开了门，看了看信上的名字，哭了起来。你浑身湿透地走回去，从来没有问过。\n# en: Hold it up to the light first\nopt.right.label [eda50a] = 先对着光看一眼\n# en: You make out one line: \"...I have never stopped...\" Then you deliver it. The next day Vey says, without looking at you, \"The light in that corridor is very good, isn't it?\"\nopt.right.say.1 [8662e4] = 你辨认出一行字：“……我从未停止……”然后你把信送到了。第二天，维伊教授看都没看你一眼就说：“那条走廊的光线真好，不是吗？”\n\n@event favour_thorne_marking\n# en: Forty Essays\ntitle [fcfc15] = 四十篇论文\n# en: Professor Thorne puts forty first-year essays on your desk. \"Mark them. A crown for every five. Be honest. Honesty is a kindness they will not thank you for.\" He leaves. The first essay begins, \"Fire is hot.\"\ntext [0ac70d] = 索恩教授把四十篇一年级生的论文放在你桌上。“批改。每五篇一克朗。要诚实。诚实是一种他们不会感激的仁慈。”他走了。第一篇的开头是：“火是热的。”\n# en: A stack of forty first-year essays on a desk under a lamp, a red pen, a student with an expression of growing despair.\nscene [e38153] = 灯下桌上一摞四十篇一年级生的论文，一支红笔，一个越来越绝望的学生。\n# en: Mark them honestly\nopt.left.label [5bc996] = 诚实地批改\n# en: Forty essays, eleven of them good, one brilliant and the rest about how fire is hot. Thorne reads your marks and says \"Hm.\" You've learned that \"Hm\" is pay too.\nopt.left.say.1 [a64129] = 四十篇论文，十一篇不错，一篇出色，其余都在讲火是热的。索恩教授看了你的批改，说了声“嗯”。你已经明白，“嗯”也算一种报酬。\n# en: Mark them kindly\nopt.right.label [f8616f] = 宽厚地批改\n# en: You give every first-year a word of encouragement. Thorne raises your marks on three and lowers them on twenty. \"Kindness,\" he says, \"is not the same as a pass.\" He pays you anyway.\nopt.right.say.1 [475d7f] = 你给每个一年级生都写了一句鼓励的话。索恩教授把其中三篇的分数提高了，二十篇压低了。“仁慈，”他说，“和及格不是一回事。”他还是付了你钱。\n\n@event favour_amaranth_shift\n# en: The Night Shift\ntitle [99806e] = 夜班\n# en: Sister Amaranth needs a pair of hands for the infirmary's night shift. \"Basins, bandages, and sitting with whoever can't sleep. Eight crowns. Don't faint. If you faint, you're a patient, and patients don't get paid.\"\ntext [bcef7e] = 阿玛兰斯修女需要一双手帮医务室值夜班。“端盆、换绷带，陪着睡不着的人。八克朗。别晕倒。晕倒了你就是病人，病人可拿不到钱。”\n# en: A dim infirmary ward at night, a student in an apron carrying a basin between rows of beds, a stern healer writing by a single lamp.\nscene [6cb985] = 夜里昏暗的医务室病房，一个系着围裙的学生端着水盆走在一排排病床之间，一位严厉的治疗师在一盏灯下写字。\n# en: Take the shift\nopt.left.label [384351] = 值这个夜班\n# en: A first-year with a fever, a prefect with a broken wrist, a boy who just wants someone awake. By dawn you're grey with tiredness. Amaranth says, \"Keep doing that,\" and pays you.\nopt.left.say.1 [f40aef] = 一个发烧的一年级生，一个手腕骨折的级长，一个只是想有人醒着陪他的男孩。到了天亮，你累得脸色发灰。阿玛兰斯修女说“继续这样做”，然后付了你钱。\n# en: Offer to roll bandages by day instead\nopt.right.label [a37891] = 提出白天来卷绷带\n# en: She looks at you over her spectacles and hands you a mountain of linen. \"Bandages it is. Less pay, all of the tedium.\" By the end you could roll them in your sleep.\nopt.right.say.1 [a6e389] = 她从眼镜上方看了你一眼，递给你一大堆亚麻布。“那就卷绷带。报酬少些，枯燥一点不少。”到最后你闭着眼都能卷。\n\n@event upper_year_errands\n# en: Errands for Upper-Years\ntitle [a1842a] = 替高年级跑腿\n# en: {Upper-years pay for errands, a book fetched or a queue stood in or a letter carried to the owlery.|An upper-year waves a list and a coin at you. Word has got round.|\"You again,\" says the upper-year, pleased. \"Good. The usual, plus my laundry.\"} {The list is long.|The coin is small.|One item on the list just says \"find my cat\".}\ntext [f10c64] = {高年级生会付钱让人跑腿，取本书，排个队，或者把信送到猫头鹰塔。|一个高年级生冲你挥着一张清单和一枚硬币。消息已经传开了。|“又是你，”高年级生高兴地说，“好。老样子，再加上我的脏衣服。”}{清单很长。|硬币很小。|清单上有一项只写着“找到我的猫”。}\n# en: A crowded common room, a lounging upper-year holding out a coin and a list, a younger student taking the list with a sigh.\nscene [6dbb95] = 一间拥挤的公共休息室，一个懒洋洋的高年级生递出一枚硬币和一张清单，一个低年级生叹着气接过清单。\n# en: Run the errands\nopt.left.label [54caa0] = 去跑腿\n# en: {You run up and down the towers all evening and are paid in coins and condescension.|You fetch, carry and queue. Your legs are tired; your purse is less light.|The usual, plus the laundry, plus the cat, which is on the owlery roof. You're paid for two of the three.}\nopt.left.say.1 [8ec74e] = {你一整晚在塔楼间上上下下，拿到的报酬是硬币加居高临下。|你取东西、搬东西、排队。腿累了，钱袋没那么轻了。|老一套，加上脏衣服，再加上那只猫，它在猫头鹰棚的屋顶上。三样里你拿到了两样的钱。}\n# en: Name your price\nopt.right.label [979a40] = 开个价\n# en: {\"More, or find your own cat.\" They pay. You find the cat anyway; it was in the laundry.|You haggle like a merchant's child. The upper-year pays with grudging respect.|\"The laundry's extra,\" you say. They pay it, and add the cat for free. You find it in the library.}\nopt.right.say.1 [289112] = {“加钱，不然自己找猫去。”他们付了。你还是把猫找到了，就在脏衣服堆里。|你像商人家的孩子一样讨价还价。高年级生不情不愿地带着几分佩服付了钱。|“洗衣服另算，”你说。对方付了，还白搭了找猫的活儿。你在图书馆里找到了它。}\n# en: {\"Never mind,\" says the upper-year, and gives the list to a first-year behind you. Word gets round of that too.|They laugh, and find someone cheaper. You keep your dignity and nothing else.|\"The laundry's extra,\" you say. The upper-year gives the list to someone who doesn't think so.}\nopt.right.say.2 [e96058] = {“算了，”高年级生说着，把清单给了你身后的一个一年级生。这件事也传开了。|他们笑了，找了个更便宜的人。你保住了尊严，别的什么也没留下。|“洗衣服另算，”你说。那个高年级生把清单给了一个不这么想的人。}\n\n@event pawnbroker_window\n# en: The Pawnbroker's Window\ntitle [8d463b] = 当铺的橱窗\n# en: {The pawnbroker's window is full of other people's treasures, wands and rings and a stuffed owl. The sign says \"Fair prices. Redemption within the year.\"|{flag(pawned_something): The pawnbroker's window again. Something of yours is in it now.|The pawnbroker's window again, the same treasures in new places.}|The pawnbroker nods at you through the glass like an acquaintance.} {A first-year's silver locket is in the front row.|There's a familiar's collar, empty.|The stuffed owl seems to judge you.}\ntext [29c61e] = {当铺的橱窗里满是别人的宝贝，魔杖、戒指，还有一只猫头鹰标本。招牌上写着“价格公道，一年内可赎回”。|{flag(pawned_something): 又是当铺的橱窗。现在里面有一件是你的东西。|又是当铺的橱窗，还是那些宝贝，换了位置。}|当铺老板隔着玻璃冲你点点头，像个熟人。}{一个一年级生的银挂坠摆在第一排。|有一个魔宠项圈，空的。|那只猫头鹰标本好像在评判你。}\n# en: A dusty village shop window crowded with pawned treasures, wands, rings and a stuffed owl, a student looking in with their hands in empty pockets.\nscene [4f25be] = 村里一扇积满灰尘的店铺橱窗，挤满了典当来的宝贝，魔杖、戒指和一只猫头鹰标本，一个学生双手插在空口袋里往里看。\n# en: Pawn something you'd miss\nopt.left.label [5697a9] = 当掉一件你会想念的东西\n# en: {Your good boots, or your grandmother's ring, or the wand-case with your name burned in it. The money is fair; the ticket says \"within the year\". You put the ticket somewhere safe and try not to look at it.|You hand over something you love, and take the money, and walk home faster than usual.|The pawnbroker greets you by name and writes the ticket without asking what it is. The owl watches the whole transaction.}\nopt.left.say.1 [6794d2] = {你的好靴子，或者祖母的戒指，或者烙着你名字的魔杖盒。钱给得公道；当票上写着“一年内”。你把当票收在稳妥的地方，尽量不去看它。|你交出一件心爱的东西，接过钱，走回家的步子比平常快。|当铺老板叫着你的名字打招呼，没问是什么就开了当票。那只猫头鹰全程看着这笔交易。}\n# en: Walk on\nopt.right.label [383259] = 走过去\n# en: {You walk on with your hands in your pockets. They're empty, but they're yours.|Not today. The owl watches you go.|The pawnbroker nods through the glass; you nod back, and walk on. The owl looks unconvinced.}\nopt.right.say.1 [40060d] = {你双手插在口袋里走了过去。口袋是空的，但它们是你的。|今天不了。那只猫头鹰目送你离开。|当铺老板隔着玻璃点头；你也点点头，走了过去。那只猫头鹰看起来并不信服。}\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/pools/night.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/pools/night.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event newt_company\n# en: Company for the Evening\ntitle [36541c] = 陪客的夜晚\n# en: {The woman who runs the upstairs room looks you over. \"The guests like good company. Laugh at the jokes, keep the glasses full, lose gracefully at cards. Nothing more. Guests who want more are shown the stair.\"|The upstairs room again: velvet, lamplight, guests from the capital who tip in silver.|You know the room now, and which guests to sit beside.} {Tonight it's a merchant's birthday.|Tonight, a card party for a visiting magistrate.|Tonight the guests are masked, for no reason anyone gives.}\ntext [875f43] = {经营楼上房间的女人上下打量着你。“客人们喜欢好伙伴。笑他们的笑话，把酒杯添满，打牌时输得体面些。仅此而已。想要更多的客人，会被请下楼。”|又是楼上那个房间：天鹅绒、灯光，还有从都城来、用银币打赏的客人。|你现在熟悉这个房间了，也知道该坐在哪些客人旁边。}{今晚是一位商人的生日。|今晚是为一位来访的地方官办的牌局。|今晚客人们都戴着面具，没人说为什么。}\n# en: An upstairs room at a tavern after hours, card tables under low lamps, well-dressed guests laughing, a student in borrowed finery refilling a glass.\nscene [538ab9] = 打烊后酒馆楼上的房间，低垂的灯下摆着牌桌，衣着体面的客人在笑，一个穿着借来华服的学生在添酒。\n# en: Work the evening\nopt.left.label [754d13] = 做这一晚的活\n# en: {You laugh in all the right places and lose at cards exactly as much as you're meant to. The tips are heavy, and so is your head on the way home.|A guest tells you the story of their life. You listen as if it mattered. Perhaps it did. The tip says it did.|Behind the masks the guests are easier, and so are you. You sit beside the right ones and come home with heavy pockets and no names.}\nopt.left.say.1 [84f1ea] = {你在该笑的地方笑，打牌时不多不少正好输掉该输的。小费沉甸甸的，回家路上你的脑袋也沉甸甸的。|一位客人给你讲了他的一生。你听得好像这很要紧。也许确实要紧。小费说确实要紧。|戴着面具的客人们放松些，你也是。你坐在该坐的人旁边，回家时口袋沉甸甸的，一个名字也没记住。}\n# en: {A guest's hand wanders and doesn't take the hint. You leave by the back stair with a few coins and a cold feeling that lasts the week.|You say the wrong thing to the wrong magistrate, and are paid off early and shown the stair. Someone from the castle is drinking at the bar below.|A masked guest won't take no for an answer, and nobody can see who it is. You leave by the back stair with a few coins and a very bad feeling.}\nopt.left.say.2 [3eb80a] = {一位客人的手不规矩，暗示也不听。你从后楼梯离开，带着几枚硬币和一种冷冰冰的感觉，那感觉持续了一整个星期。|你对错的地方官说了错的话，被提前打发了钱、请下了楼。楼下吧台边，有个城堡里的人正在喝酒。|一个戴面具的客人不肯接受拒绝，谁也看不出那是谁。你从后楼梯离开，带着几枚硬币和一种非常糟糕的感觉。}\n# en: Not tonight\nopt.right.label [4bf72b] = 今晚不了\n# en: {You turn the work down and walk home past the lit windows, a little poorer and quite sure of yourself.|\"Another night, then,\" says the woman, writing something in a small book. You wonder what.|You turn it down. The woman nods as if she expected it, and puts a small mark by your name in her book.}\nopt.right.say.1 [e2630c] = {你推掉了这份活，从亮着灯的窗下走回家，穷了一点，心里却很踏实。|“那就改天，”那女人说着，在一个小本子上写了些什么。你很想知道她写了什么。|你推掉了。那女人点点头，好像早料到了，在本子上你的名字旁做了个小记号。}\n\n@event newt_high_table\n# en: The High Table\ntitle [06018c] = 大赌桌\n# en: {Behind the upstairs room there's a back room, and in it the high table. Grisk keeps the bank. Nobody here plays for crowns alone.|The high table again. Grisk nods to you as if you were a regular. You are becoming one.|Grisk has kept your seat warm, he says. It is not a comforting thought.} {A first-year's familiar sits in a cage beside the pot.|Someone has staked a silver wand and is sweating about it.|Somebody's signed promise is in the pot, folded small.}\ntext [4a665f] = {楼上房间后面还有一间后屋，里面就是大赌桌。格里斯克坐庄。这里没人只为克朗下注。|又是大赌桌。格里斯克冲你点点头，就像你是熟客。你正在变成熟客。|格里斯克说他一直给你留着座位。这话可一点也不让人安心。}{一个一年级生的魔宠被关在笼子里，就放在赌注旁边。|有人押上了一根银魔杖，正在冒汗。|赌注里有一张签了名的承诺书，折得小小的。}\n# en: A round table in a back room under a single green lamp, piles of coins, a goblin in a velvet waistcoat keeping the bank.\nscene [f589cb] = 后屋里一张圆桌，头顶一盏绿灯，桌上堆着硬币，一个穿天鹅绒马甲的地精在坐庄。\n# en: Stake your purse\nopt.purse.label [6dad53] = 押上你的钱袋\n# en: Guile against the table.\nopt.purse.hint [8deb0b] = 诡计，对抗整张桌子。\n# en: {You read the table like a book you've already finished. The pot slides over, and Grisk applauds politely with two fingers.|Your nerve holds and theirs doesn't. You walk home heavy with silver and hollow with nerves.|Somebody's folded promise is in the pot. You win it, and give it back to the owner unread. Grisk thinks that's hilarious.}\nopt.purse.say.1 [65ecad] = {你把这张牌桌读得像一本早就读完的书。赌注推到了你面前，格里斯克用两根手指礼貌地鼓掌。|你的胆量撑住了，他们的没撑住。你揣着沉甸甸的银币走回家，心里却空落落的。|赌注里有一张折得小小的承诺书。你赢了，没打开就还给了它的主人。格里斯克觉得这太好笑了。}\n# en: {The cards turn on you, then the room does. You leave lighter, and a prefect's lamp is going round the courtyard.|You lose the pot and your temper with it. Grisk sympathises, with interest.|You lose, and Grisk offers to take a signed promise instead of the coin you're short. You pay in coin, all of it.}\nopt.purse.say.2 [6e767c] = {牌先跟你作对，接着整个房间都跟你作对。你走的时候钱袋轻了，一位级长的灯正绕着庭院转。|你输了赌注，脾气也跟着输掉了。格里斯克表示同情，连本带利。|你输了，格里斯克提出可以用一张签了名的承诺书抵你欠的钱。你付了现钱，一分不少。}\n# en: Stake a favour to Grisk\nopt.favour.label [7e2bea] = 押上欠格里斯克的一个人情\n# en: Win big, or owe him a job.\nopt.favour.hint [7556bd] = 赢一大笔，或者欠他一份差事。\n# en: You stake \"one favour, to be named\" and win. Grisk smiles, tears up the promise, and keeps the pieces. \"Next time,\" he says.\nopt.favour.say.1 [985418] = 你押上“一个人情，日后再说”，赢了。格里斯克笑着把承诺书撕了，却把碎片收了起来。“下次吧，”他说。\n# en: You lose, and Grisk folds your promise into his waistcoat pocket like a love letter. \"I'll be in touch,\" he says. \"Soon.\"\nopt.favour.say.2 [d7a232] = 你输了，格里斯克把你的承诺书折好，像收情书一样放进马甲口袋。“我会跟你联系的，”他说，“很快。”\n# en: Play the bone-dice\nopt.health.label [b0d402] = 玩骨骰\n# en: The old northern game. Win or bleed.\nopt.health.hint [2b4c16] = 古老的北方游戏。要么赢，要么流血。\n# en: The bone-dice game is played with a knife between the fingers. You keep all ten and win the pot. Grisk calls you \"dear heart\" for the rest of the night.\nopt.health.say.1 [5a985b] = 骨骰的玩法是把刀子插在指缝之间。你十根手指一根不少，还赢了赌注。整晚格里斯克都管你叫“心肝儿”。\n# en: You win the pot and lose a fair amount of skin from your left hand. Amaranth, in the morning, asks no questions, very loudly.\nopt.health.say.2 [6659bd] = 你赢了赌注，左手却掉了不少皮。第二天早上，阿玛兰斯修女什么也没问，问得非常大声。\n# en: Watch one hand, then go\nopt.leave.label [1c61ac] = 看一局就走\n# en: {You watch a student lose a familiar on a bluff, and go home and hug yours, or your pillow.|You watch, learn and leave. Grisk raises his glass to your back.|You watch a signed promise change hands across the table, and decide never to sign anything in this room.}\nopt.leave.say.1 [d0582e] = {你看着一个学生在一次诈唬里输掉了自己的魔宠，回家后抱紧了你的魔宠，或者你的枕头。|你看了，学了，走了。格里斯克冲着你的背影举了举杯。|你看着一张签了名的承诺书在桌上易手，决定在这个房间里永远什么都不签。}\n\n@event night_partner_asks\n# en: Where Were You?\ntitle [725fa7] = 你去哪儿了？\n# en: {partner} is waiting on the stair with a silver token from the Gilded Newt in {their} palm. It fell out of your coat. \"The upstairs room,\" {they} say{s}. \"People talk. I'd rather hear it from you.\"\ntext [5d738c] = {partner} 在楼梯上等你，掌心里放着一枚镀金蝾螈酒馆的银筹码。它是从你外套里掉出来的。“楼上那个房间，”{they}说，“大家都在议论。我宁愿听你自己说。”\n# en: Two students on a cold stone stair in the morning, one holding a tavern's silver token found in the other's coat.\nscene [67e6d6] = 清晨冰冷的石阶上的两个学生，一个手里拿着从另一个外套里掉出来的酒馆银筹码。\n# en: Tell {them} everything\nopt.left.label [002271] = 把一切都告诉{them}\n# en: You tell the truth: the glasses, the cards, the guests, the stair. {partner} is quiet a long time. \"Thank you for telling me,\" {they} say{s} at last, and means it, and it still costs you both.\nopt.left.say.1 [132cfe] = 你说了实话：酒杯、牌局、客人、楼梯。{partner} 沉默了很久。“谢谢你告诉我，”{they}终于说，是真心的，可你们俩还是都付出了代价。\n# en: \"It's nothing. A card game.\"\nopt.right.label [129d58] = “没什么。就是打牌。”\n# en: {partner} looks at the token, then at you, and puts it in your hand. \"All right,\" {they} say{s}. It isn't. For a week {they} sit{s} a little further away at supper.\nopt.right.say.1 [1b1621] = {partner} 看了看筹码，又看了看你，把它放回你手里。“好吧，”{they}说。并不好。接下来一个星期，晚饭时{they}都坐得离你远了一点。\n\n@event grisk_names_favour\n# en: The Favour, Named\ntitle [44924a] = 人情，到期了\n# en: Grisk is waiting in the library alcove with your promise between two fingers. \"The favour,\" he says. \"A small one. A crate goes from the boathouse to the village. Tonight. Or, if you prefer, the price of the crate.\" He smiles. \"I always offer a choice.\"\ntext [f47834] = 格里斯克在图书馆的壁龛里等你，两根手指夹着你的承诺书。“那个人情，”他说，“很小的一个。有个箱子要从船屋运到村里。今晚。或者，你也可以付清这箱货的价钱。”他笑了。“我总是给人选择的。”\n# en: A goblin in a velvet waistcoat waiting in a library alcove, a folded promise held up between two fingers.\nscene [91dfbe] = 一个穿天鹅绒马甲的地精在图书馆壁龛里等着，两根手指举着一张折好的承诺书。\n# en: Move the crate\nopt.left.label [f1cc88] = 运箱子\n# en: The crate clinks and, faintly, sings. You don't look inside. Grisk tears up your promise. \"You're good at this,\" he says, which is the worst thing he could say.\nopt.left.say.1 [eb8ddb] = 箱子叮当作响，还隐约在唱歌。你没往里看。格里斯克撕了你的承诺书。“你干这行很在行，”他说，这是他能说出的最糟的一句话。\n# en: Bram stops you on the jetty and lifts the tarp. Charm-wine. The crate is gone, the favour counts as paid, and Bram has your name.\nopt.left.say.2 [818fe5] = 布拉姆在码头上拦住了你，掀开了油布。是魔法酒。箱子没了，人情算是还了，布拉姆也记下了你的名字。\n# en: Pay him off\nopt.right.label [e88f0a] = 花钱了事\n# en: You count out the crowns and Grisk gives you back the promise with a small bow. It feels like buying your own name back. It is, more or less.\nopt.right.say.1 [2b02e3] = 你数出克朗，格里斯克微微鞠躬，把承诺书还给了你。感觉就像花钱买回了自己的名字。差不多就是这么回事。\n\n@event ring_on_yourself\n# en: Bet on Yourself\ntitle [c79e66] = 押自己赢\n# en: {The ring under the boathouse has a bookmaker now, with a slate. \"Fancy yourself?\" he says. \"Put your money where your fists are.\"|The bookmaker knows your name and your odds, and they've shortened.|Your name is on the slate tonight before you've said you'll fight.} {The water slaps under the floor.|Somebody is collecting teeth in a jar, as a joke, probably.|The crowd is bigger than last time.}\ntext [da9126] = {船屋底下的擂台现在有了个庄家，拿着一块石板。“对自己有信心？”他说，“那就拿钱来证明你的拳头。”|庄家知道你的名字，也知道你的赔率，而且赔率变低了。|你还没说要打，石板上已经写上了你的名字。}{地板下的水拍打着。|有人在拿罐子收集牙齿，大概是开玩笑。|观众比上次多。}\n# en: A lantern-lit cellar under a boathouse, a chalk ring on wet flagstones, a bookmaker holding up a slate of odds, a crowd in scarves.\nscene [63e7b8] = 船屋底下一间灯笼照亮的地窖，湿漉漉的石板上画着粉笔圈，一个庄家举着写满赔率的石板，周围是一群围着围巾的人。\n# en: Bet your purse on yourself\nopt.left.label [5b980a] = 把钱袋押在自己身上\n# en: {You win, bloodied and grinning, and the bookmaker pays out through his teeth.|The crowd roars. You collect your bet and your opponent's grudging handshake, and limp home rich.|Your name was on the slate before you agreed, and the crowd is bigger for it. You win, and the bookmaker pays you in front of all of them.}\nopt.left.say.1 [f343f7] = {你赢了，满身是血地咧嘴笑着，庄家咬着牙付了钱。|人群欢呼起来。你收下赌注和对手勉强的握手，一瘸一拐地揣着钱回家。|你还没答应，名字就上了黑板，人群也因此更多了。你赢了，庄家当着所有人的面付了钱。}\n# en: {You go down in the third exchange, and your purse goes with you. On the way home a light is on in the prefects' corridor.|A bad loss: the bet, a tooth's worth of pride and most of your skin.|The bigger crowd came to see you lose, it turns out. You oblige, and your purse goes with your pride.}\nopt.left.say.2 [99a473] = {第三回合你就倒下了，钱袋也跟着没了。回家路上，级长那条走廊里亮着一盏灯。|输得很惨：赌注、一颗牙那么多的骄傲，还有大半层皮。|原来人多是来看你输的。你如了他们的愿，钱袋跟着骄傲一起没了。}\n# en: Fight for the purse only\nopt.right.label [0acb58] = 只为奖金而战\n# en: {A clean win for a small purse. Nobody's rich, nobody's hurt much.|You win, bow to your opponent and leave before the bookmaker can talk you into more.|You fight for the purse alone and win it in front of the biggest crowd yet. The bookmaker sighs at the money he didn't make on you.}\nopt.right.say.1 [2a3d70] = {干净利落地赢了一小笔奖金。没人发财，也没人伤得太重。|你赢了，向对手鞠了一躬，在庄家劝你押更多之前离开了。|你只为奖金而战，在最多的人面前赢了。庄家为没能从你身上赚到的钱叹了口气。}\n# en: {You lose, bow, and spend the walk home finding new bruises.|A loss, and the loser's purse, which is nothing at all.|You lose in front of the biggest crowd yet. At least it was only the purse.}\nopt.right.say.2 [cc0e51] = {你输了，鞠了一躬，回家路上一路发现新的淤青。|输了，拿到的是输家的奖金，也就是什么都没有。|你在最多的人面前输了。至少输的只是奖金。}\n\n@event lyra_ring_word\n# en: Lyra Has Heard\ntitle [d959ef] = 莱拉听说了\n# en: Lyra blocks the corridor. \"The ring under the boathouse.\" She says it as if it were a smell. \"Brawling for coins in a cellar, in front of drunks. You can actually duel. I've seen you. This is beneath you.\" A pause. \"I'd hate to lose to someone who does it.\"\ntext [739e81] = 莱拉堵住了走廊。“船屋底下的擂台。”她说这话的语气像在说一股臭味。“在地窖里当着醉鬼的面为几个钱打架。你明明会决斗。我见过。这有失你的身份。”她顿了一下。“我可不想输给一个干这种事的人。”\n# en: A proud red-haired student blocking a corridor with folded arms, a duelling glove tucked in her belt.\nscene [437476] = 一个骄傲的红发学生双臂交叉堵在走廊上，腰带里别着一只决斗手套。\n# en: \"Then spar with me instead.\"\nopt.left.label [b55172] = “那就跟我对练吧。”\n# en: She stares, then almost smiles. \"Tomorrow, the Duelling Hall, six sharp.\" She beats you, and then shows you why, which from Lyra is close to an apology for the corridor.\nopt.left.say.1 [9f2ac9] = 她盯着你，然后差点笑了出来。“明天，决斗厅，六点整。”她打败了你，然后把原因指给你看，对莱拉来说，这差不多就是为走廊上的事道歉了。\n# en: \"It pays. You wouldn't know.\"\nopt.right.label [140619] = “它挣钱。你不会懂的。”\n# en: Her face shuts like a door. \"No,\" she says. \"I wouldn't.\" She walks off. Later the Newt's landlady hands back the deposit you left on your bar tab: Lyra has paid it off. That is somehow worse.\nopt.right.say.1 [71f78c] = 她的脸像一扇门一样关上了。“对，”她说，“我不会懂。”她走了。后来蝾螈酒馆的老板娘把你押在酒账上的钱退给了你：莱拉替你付清了。这不知怎么更糟。\n\n@event grisk_pickup\n# en: A Parcel at the Boathouse\ntitle [e699bb] = 船屋的包裹\n# en: {Grisk is waiting on the jetty, a parcel in oilcloth under his arm. \"Into the village by tomorrow afternoon. A cobbler's back door. Don't open it, don't drop it, don't be seen.\"|The jetty, the oilcloth, Grisk's teeth in the dark. \"Same arrangement,\" he says.|Grisk again, and a heavier parcel than last time.} {It ticks faintly.|It's cold enough to frost your gloves.|It smells of oranges and something under the oranges.}\ntext [4f4a69] = {格里斯克在码头上等着，胳膊底下夹着一个油布包。“明天下午前送到村里。鞋匠铺的后门。别打开，别掉了，别被人看见。”|码头，油布包，黑暗里格里斯克的牙齿。“老规矩，”他说。|又是格里斯克，这次的包裹比上次重。}{它微微地滴答作响。|它冷得能让你的手套结霜。|它闻起来有橙子味，橙子味底下还有别的什么。}\n# en: A dark boathouse jetty at midnight, a small goblin holding out a parcel wrapped in oilcloth, the lake black behind him.\nscene [df2f86] = 午夜一座漆黑的船屋码头，一个小个子地精递出一个油布包裹，身后是黑沉沉的湖。\n# en: Take the parcel\nopt.left.label [8ea6ee] = 接下包裹\n# en: {You take it. It is heavier than it looks, and the walk back to your room is very long.|You hide it under your bed. You don't sleep well with it there.|You take the heavier parcel. It smells of oranges, and under the oranges of something you decide not to name.}\nopt.left.say.1 [8e3d40] = {你接了。它比看上去重，回房间的路显得特别长。|你把它藏在床底下。有它在那儿，你睡不好。|你接过了那个更沉的包裹。它有橙子的气味，橙子底下还有一种你决定不去叫出名字的气味。}\n# en: Not this month\nopt.right.label [9e6244] = 这个月不了\n# en: {\"Next month, then,\" says Grisk, as if it were already arranged. He tucks the parcel away. You feel watched all the way back up the lawn.|Grisk shrugs. \"There's always someone.\" You don't like how true it sounds.|You turn down the heavier parcel. Grisk weighs it in his hand, then in his mind. \"Your loss,\" he says. \"Or not.\"}\nopt.right.say.1 [73971d] = {“那就下个月，”格里斯克说，好像早就说定了似的。他把包裹收了起来。回草坪那头的一路上，你都觉得有人在看着你。|格里斯克耸耸肩。“总有别人的。”你不喜欢这话有多真。|你拒绝了那个更沉的包裹。格里斯克在手里掂了掂，又在心里掂了掂。“你的损失，”他说，“也许不是。”}\n\n@event grisk_delivery\n# en: The Cobbler's Back Door\ntitle [136b0a] = 鞋匠铺的后门\n# en: {~You carry Grisk's parcel down to the village in broad daylight.|Grisk's parcel, the village lane, and too many people about.|The parcel is in your satchel and the satchel is suddenly the heaviest thing you own.} Bram is at the apple stall. The cobbler's back door is past him.\ntext [6201ff] = {~你光天化日之下把格里斯克的包裹带进了村子。|格里斯克的包裹，村里的小巷，还有太多的人。|包裹在你的挎包里，挎包突然成了你拥有的最重的东西。}布拉姆在苹果摊前。鞋匠铺的后门就在他身后。\n# en: A narrow village lane in daylight, a student with an oilcloth parcel under one arm walking past a prefect who is buying apples.\nscene [8d39ee] = 白天一条狭窄的村巷，一个学生胳膊底下夹着油布包裹，正从一个买苹果的级长身边走过。\n# en: Walk past as if you belong\nopt.left.label [a61db7] = 若无其事地走过去\n# en: {~You nod to Bram, buy an apple, and knock twice at the cobbler's door. A hand takes the parcel; another gives you a purse. Your heart takes an hour to slow down.|The door opens before you knock. Coins, a nod, done. You eat the apple on the way home and taste none of it.|The satchel feels heavier with every step past Bram. He waves. You wave. The cobbler pays, and your hands shake until supper.}\nopt.left.say.1 [3b834c] = {~你冲布拉姆点点头，买了个苹果，在鞋匠铺的门上敲了两下。一只手接过包裹，另一只手递给你一个钱袋。你的心跳过了一个钟头才慢下来。|你还没敲门，门就开了。硬币，点头，完事。你在回家路上吃了那个苹果，一点味道也没尝出来。|从布拉姆身边走过，挎包每一步都更沉。他挥手，你也挥手。鞋匠付了钱，你的手一直抖到晚饭。}\n# en: {~Bram calls your name. You drop the parcel in a horse trough and walk on, very calmly. Bram follows you halfway home.|You panic at the corner and leave the parcel behind a water butt. When you go back, it's gone, and Bram is writing in his notebook.|Bram asks to see inside your satchel, kindly. You show him your books, and he never looks in the other pocket. You walk home sick.}\nopt.left.say.2 [82c22c] = {~布拉姆叫了你的名字。你把包裹扔进饮马槽，非常镇定地继续走。布拉姆跟了你半路。|你在拐角处慌了神，把包裹丢在了一个水桶后面。等你回去找，它已经不见了，布拉姆正在本子上写着什么。|布拉姆客气地要看看你的挎包。你给他看了书，他始终没看另一个口袋。你走回家时一阵恶心。}\n# en: Leave it at the door and run\nopt.right.label [44439b] = 丢在门口就跑\n# en: {~You go the long way round, drop it on the cobbler's step and run. The cobbler pays you, short, for the lack of manners.|You don't knock, and you don't wait. Short pay, slipped under your door that night.|You drop the satchel's weight on the cobbler's step and run. He pays you short, and you're glad to be rid of it.}\nopt.right.say.1 [e7e8e0] = {~你绕远路，把它丢在鞋匠铺门口的台阶上就跑。鞋匠因为你没礼貌，少付了你一些钱。|你不敲门，也不等。那天夜里，一份打了折扣的报酬从你门缝底下塞了进来。|你把挎包里的东西丢在鞋匠的台阶上就跑。他少付了你钱，你却庆幸终于甩掉了它。}\n\n@event grisk_cross\n# en: Grisk Is Not Pleased\ntitle [2bad70] = 格里斯克不高兴了\n# en: Grisk is sitting on your bed. You didn't leave the window open. \"A parcel was lost,\" he says pleasantly, turning a page in his ledger. \"Lost things have a price. I've written it down. Would you like to see?\"\ntext [ecab8b] = 格里斯克坐在你的床上。你没有开窗。“一个包裹丢了，”他愉快地说，翻着账簿。“丢了的东西是有价钱的。我已经记下来了。要看看吗？”\n# en: A goblin in a velvet waistcoat sitting on a student's bed uninvited, a ledger open on his knee, the dormitory window open behind him.\nscene [e93020] = 一个穿天鹅绒马甲的地精不请自来地坐在学生床上，膝上摊着账簿，身后宿舍的窗户开着。\n# en: Put it on your account\nopt.left.label [e01cc0] = 记在你的账上\n# en: \"Two more months,\" he says, and writes it neatly. \"Such a good customer.\" He leaves by the window. You don't sleep with it open again.\nopt.left.say.1 [aa63e2] = “再加两个月，”他说着，把它写得整整齐齐。“真是个好主顾。”他从窗户走了。从那以后你再也没开着窗睡过觉。\n# en: He writes your name in the ledger under a new line: one parcel, six payments. \"Twelve a month,\" he says, \"as usual.\" He leaves by the window. You don't sleep with it open again.\nopt.left.say.2 [0b39d1] = 他在账簿上你的名字下面新添了一行：一个包裹，分六期。“每月十二克朗，”他说，“老规矩。”他从窗户走了。从那以后你再也没开着窗睡过觉。\n# en: Tell him you're done\nopt.right.label [1b5f57] = 告诉他你不干了\n# en: \"Everyone is, eventually,\" Grisk says. On your way to breakfast, the stair you always take collapses three steps under you. Nobody saw. Everybody heard. You never see him again.\nopt.right.say.1 [1693f0] = “每个人最后都会这么说，”格里斯克说。去吃早饭的路上，你常走的那段楼梯在你脚下塌了三级。没人看见。所有人都听见了。你再也没见过他。\n\n@event kitchen_stores\n# en: The Stores\ntitle [de5442] = 储藏室\n# en: {Behind the kitchens is the storeroom, full of sugar, spices and oranges from the south. The village pays well for oranges in winter, and nobody counts the crates.|The storeroom again. You know which crate is at the back and never counted.|The stores have a new padlock, which is mostly decorative.} {A mouse watches you without judgement.|The kitchen cat does judge you.|Somewhere a pot is still dripping.}\ntext [855855] = {厨房后面是储藏室，堆满了糖、香料和南方运来的橙子。冬天村里橙子卖得好，而且没人清点这些箱子。|又是储藏室。你知道哪一箱在最里面，从来没人数过。|储藏室换了一把新挂锁，基本上是个摆设。}{一只老鼠看着你，毫无评判之意。|厨房的猫倒是在评判你。|某处一口锅还在滴水。}\n# en: A dark storeroom behind a castle kitchen, sacks of sugar and crates of oranges stacked high, a student with an empty satchel.\nscene [6082df] = 城堡厨房后面一间漆黑的储藏室，糖袋和橙子箱堆得老高，一个学生拿着空挎包。\n# en: Fill a satchel to sell\nopt.left.label [d756fa] = 装满一挎包去卖\n# en: {Oranges, a cone of sugar, a jar of saffron. The village grocer asks no questions and pays in coppers. Somebody in the castle will be short of saffron.|A full satchel and an empty conscience, or nearly. On the way out, the scullery door bangs behind you.|The new padlock opens with a hairpin. You fill the satchel, and the grocer pays, and you wonder who'll be blamed for the lock.}\nopt.left.say.1 [43fa37] = {橙子、一块圆锥形的糖、一罐藏红花。村里的杂货商什么也不问，付的是铜板。城堡里会有人缺藏红花用了。|挎包装满了，良心几乎清空了。出门时，洗碗间的门在你身后砰的一响。|新挂锁用一根发夹就开了。你装满了挎包，杂货商付了钱，你想知道这锁的事会怪到谁头上。}\n# en: Take one orange and go\nopt.right.label [1f5959] = 拿一个橙子就走\n# en: {You eat it in the dark by the banked fire. It's the best orange you have ever eaten, and you know exactly why.|One orange. You leave the peel in the fire so nobody finds it.|You take one orange past the new padlock and feel worse about it than about all the crates.}\nopt.right.say.1 [4f7fd6] = {你在黑暗中靠着封住的炉火把它吃了。这是你吃过最好吃的橙子，你也很清楚为什么。|一个橙子。你把皮丢进火里，这样没人会发现。|你绕过新挂锁拿了一个橙子，感觉比偷了所有箱子还糟。}\n\n@event kitchen_night_baker\n# en: The Night Baker\ntitle [97e00b] = 守夜的面包师\n# en: {The bakehouse behind the kitchens is lit. The night baker, flour to the elbows, looks up. \"Hungry or helpful?\" she says. \"Both is allowed.\"|The night baker waves you in without looking up. \"Dough's waiting.\"|The bakehouse smells of tomorrow's bread. The baker has saved you a heel of yesterday's.} {The oven sighs.|A tray of rolls is cooling by the door.|She is humming something old.}\ntext [1b7271] = {厨房后面的烤房亮着灯。守夜的面包师手臂上沾满面粉，抬起头来。“饿了还是来帮忙？”她说，“两样都行。”|守夜的面包师头也不抬地招手让你进来。“面团在等你。”|烤房里飘着明天的面包香。面包师给你留了一块昨天的面包头。}{炉子叹了口气。|一盘小面包正在门边晾着。|她在哼一支古老的曲子。}\n# en: A warm castle bakehouse at midnight, a flour-dusted old baker kneading dough by the light of an open oven, a student leaning in the doorway.\nscene [5fcd67] = 午夜里温暖的城堡烤房，一位满身面粉的老面包师借着敞开的炉火揉面，一个学生倚在门口。\n# en: Knead until the oven's hot\nopt.left.label [f28ed8] = 揉面直到炉子烧热\n# en: {You knead until your arms shake. She gives you the first roll out of the oven and no lecture at all.|An hour of flour and warmth, and a hot roll for your trouble. You go back to bed with your hands smelling of bread, and sleep like a stone.|You knead while she hums something old. When the loaves go in, she toasts yesterday's heel for you, and you eat it on the step.}\nopt.left.say.1 [1c4c8b] = {你揉面揉到胳膊发抖。她把第一个出炉的小面包给了你，一句说教也没有。|一个钟头的面粉和温暖，还有一个热乎乎的小面包作为酬劳。你回去睡觉时手上还带着面包味，睡得像块石头。|她哼着一支老歌，你揉着面。面包进炉后，她把昨天的面包头烤了给你，你坐在台阶上吃了。}\n# en: Just sit by the oven\nopt.right.label [363883] = 就坐在炉边\n# en: {You sit by the open oven with your knees pulled up. She works, talks to the dough, and lets you be.|You sit by the oven with a roll from the cooling tray while she works. She doesn't ask why you're up. You don't ask why she is.|You eat the heel of bread and warm your feet. Upstairs, the castle is cold; down here, it never is.}\nopt.right.say.1 [efc83a] = {你抱着膝盖坐在敞开的炉边。她干她的活，跟面团说话，由你待着。|她干活，你拿着晾盘上的一个小面包坐在炉边。她没问你为什么还醒着。你也没问她。|你吃了那块面包头，暖了暖脚。楼上的城堡很冷；这下面，从来不冷。}\n\n@event restricted_page_buyer\n# en: A Buyer for Old Pages\ntitle [8c6109] = 古书页的买主\n# en: {Grisk mentioned, once, that a collector pays for illuminated pages \"of a certain age\". The Restricted Section is full of them.|{flag(sold_page): There's a gap on the shelf where the last page you sold came from.|The illuminated book you read last time is back on its shelf, untouched.}|The illuminated books lean away from you as you pass.} {A gold-leaf dragon glints on one page.|One margin is full of tiny stars, all black.|A page here is worth a month's board.}\ntext [bb1bed] = {格里斯克曾提过一次，有个收藏家肯为“有些年头的”彩绘书页出钱。禁书区里满是这种书页。|{flag(sold_page): 书架上有个空缺，你上次卖的那一页就是从那儿来的。|你上次读过的那本彩绘书回到了书架上，原封未动。}|你经过时，那些彩绘书都往旁边躲。}{一页上有条金箔画的龙在闪光。|一处页边画满了小星星，全是黑的。|这里的一页抵得上一个月的食宿费。}\n# en: A dim Restricted Section aisle at midnight, a student holding a razor over an illuminated page, a black book watching from the shelf above.\nscene [84c409] = 午夜禁书区一条昏暗的过道，一个学生拿着刀片悬在一页彩绘书页上方，书架上一本黑书在看着。\n# en: Cut a page\nopt.left.label [5f6c23] = 割下一页\n# en: {One clean cut. The book doesn't cry out, which is somehow worse. The collector pays well, and never asks where.|You sell the page and keep the guilt, which no one will buy.|The books lean away, and you cut a page anyway. A month's board, in one stroke. You don't come back to this aisle for a long time.}\nopt.left.say.1 [c1c51f] = {干净利落的一刀。书没有叫出声，这不知怎么更糟。收藏家出价很高，也从不问来路。|你卖掉了书页，留下了愧疚，这东西没人肯买。|书都朝远离你的方向倾，你还是割了一页。一刀下去，一个月的食宿费。很长一段时间你都没再来这排书架。}\n# en: Read it instead\nopt.right.label [19ccdc] = 改为读它\n# en: {You read the illuminated page by moonlight. It's a recipe for honey cakes, in exquisite gold leaf, and the gilding draws at your mana the whole time you read. You leave it where it is.|The page is a list of stars, by name, and each name pulls a little magic out of you as you read it. You learn three, and put the book back.|The books lean away from you, then back, as you read instead of cut. The page is a map of the castle as it was. It costs you a little magic to look at it.}\nopt.right.say.1 [9d1c63] = {你借着月光读那页彩绘书页。是一份蜂蜜蛋糕的食谱，用精美的金箔写成，你读的时候那层金箔一直在吸你的魔力。你把它留在原处。|那一页是一份星星的名单，每个名字在你读出时都会从你身上抽走一点魔力。你记住了三颗，把书放了回去。|你读而不割，书先是朝外倾，后来又倾了回来。那一页是城堡从前的地图。看它要耗掉你一点魔力。}\n\n@event midnight_roof_partner\n# en: The Roof, Together\ntitle [ad8b7c] = 屋顶上的两个人\n# en: {You've brought {partner} up to the observatory roof, with a stolen blanket and two cups of cocoa going cold.|{partner} knows the way up now, and goes first.|{partner} has brought the blanket this time.} {The stars are very close.|A meteor, then another.|The castle below is all asleep.}\ntext [6b40c7] = {你把{partner}带上了观星台的屋顶，带着一条偷来的毯子和两杯渐渐变凉的可可。|{partner}现在认得上来的路了，走在前面。|这次是{partner}带了毯子。}{星星离得很近。|一颗流星，接着又是一颗。|下面的城堡全都睡着了。}\n# en: Two students lying side by side on an observatory roof at midnight under a sky full of stars, one blanket between them.\nscene [2af17c] = 午夜里两个学生并排躺在观星台屋顶上，头顶是满天星斗，两人盖着同一条毯子。\n# en: Stay until the sky turns\nopt.left.label [6b603a] = 待到天色变亮\n# en: {You name the stars between you, wrong, and argue about it, and stay until the sky goes grey. The stair down is lit by a prefect's lamp you just avoid.|You fall asleep up there and wake to the first bell, frozen and happy.|{partner} wraps you both in the blanket {they} brought and talks you to sleep. The castle wakes up around you.}\nopt.left.say.1 [bd9e3a] = {你们俩一起给星星起名字，起错了，争了起来，一直待到天色发灰。下楼时一位级长的灯光照亮了楼梯，你们刚好躲开。|你们在上面睡着了，醒来时第一遍钟已经响了，冻得要命，却很快乐。|{partner}用{them}带来的毯子把你们俩裹起来，说着话把你哄睡了。城堡在你们周围醒来。}\n# en: An hour, then back\nopt.right.label [3dda16] = 待一个钟头就回去\n# en: {One hour, one blanket, back in bed before anyone stirs. You can still feel where {partner} was leaning on you.|You go down early. {partner} squeezes your hand on the stair, which is better than any star.|One hour under {partner}'s blanket. {They} fold{s} it and give{s} it to you at the bottom of the stair: \"For next time.\"}\nopt.right.say.1 [57c547] = {一个钟头，一条毯子，在任何人醒来前回到床上。你还能感觉到{partner}靠在你身上的地方。|你们早早下了楼。{partner}在楼梯上捏了捏你的手，比哪颗星星都好。|在{partner}的毯子底下待了一个钟头。在楼梯底下，{they}把毯子叠好递给你：“下次用。”}\n\n@event kael_midnight_roof\n# en: Kael at Midnight\ntitle [c04a03] = 午夜的凯尔\n# en: {Kael is on the observatory parapet, which he shouldn't be, with frost spreading from his fingers into the stone. He moves over to make room.|Kael again, on the parapet, the night's cold not touching him.|Kael has left room on the parapet before you arrive.} {\"I can't sleep south of the mountains,\" he says. \"Too warm.\"|He is watching the lake, not the stars.|\"Northern stars,\" he says, pointing at nothing you can see.}\ntext [45cc53] = {凯尔坐在观星台的护墙上，这本来是不允许的，霜从他的指尖蔓延进石头里。他挪了挪，给你让出位置。|又是凯尔，坐在护墙上，夜里的寒气碰不到他。|你还没到，凯尔就已经在护墙上给你留好了位置。}{“在山脉以南我睡不着，”他说，“太暖和了。”|他在看湖，不在看星星。|“北方的星星，”他说着，指向一处你什么也看不见的地方。}\n# en: A northern student sitting on the observatory parapet at midnight with frost spreading from his hands, the lake far below, the sky thick with stars.\nscene [330d76] = 午夜里一个北方学生坐在观星台护墙上，霜从他手上蔓延开来，湖在远远的下方，天上星斗密布。\n# en: Sit with him\nopt.left.label [6fadbf] = 陪他坐一会儿\n# en: {You sit until your fingers ache from the cold. He names northern constellations in a language that sounds like snow falling. It's the most he's ever said.|He doesn't talk. Neither do you. When you go, he says \"Goodnight,\" as if it were difficult, and it was.|He's left room, so you take it. He points out a northern star you can't see, and you pretend you can. He knows, and is pleased.}\nopt.left.say.1 [c3f2b3] = {你一直坐到手指冻得发疼。他用一种听起来像落雪的语言说出北方星座的名字。这是他说话最多的一次。|他不说话。你也不说。你走的时候，他说“晚安”，好像这很难说出口，确实如此。|他留了位置，你就坐了。他指着一颗你看不见的北方的星，你假装看见了。他知道，还挺高兴。}\n# en: Ask what he's watching for\nopt.right.label [442add] = 问他在等什么\n# en: {\"For it to wake,\" he says, and then shuts up like a book. You've pressed too hard. But you know which way he faces now.|\"Nothing,\" he says, too fast. He's watching the middle of the lake. You leave him to it, colder than when you came.|\"Home,\" he says, about the northern stars, and nothing more. It's the most honest thing he's told you, and he looks away after.}\nopt.right.say.1 [085545] = {“等它醒来，”他说完，就像一本书一样合上了。你逼得太紧了。但现在你知道他面朝哪个方向了。|“没什么，”他说得太快。他在盯着湖中央。你留他一个人在那儿，走时比来时更冷。|“家，”他指着北方的星星说，再没多说。这是他对你说过最坦白的话，说完他移开了目光。}\n\n@event corvin_note_was_mine\n# en: Second Timetable\ntitle [4a5d2a] = 第二张作息表\n# en: In the gargoyle passage, Corvin holds up a map no bigger than a thumbnail. \"Recognise it? The castle has a second timetable. It starts at midnight.\" He grins. \"Who did you think writes notes like that? Prefects?\"\ntext [2e7f05] = 在石像鬼通道里，科尔文举起一张不比拇指大的地图。“认得吗？城堡有第二张作息表。从午夜开始。”他咧嘴一笑。“你以为是谁写那种字条的？级长吗？”\n# en: A secret passage behind a gargoyle at midnight, a grinning dark-haired student holding up a thumbnail-sized silver map, chalk marks on the wall behind him.\nscene [35de3d] = 午夜一条石像鬼后面的秘密通道，一个咧嘴笑着的黑发学生举着一张拇指大小的银色地图，身后墙上画着粉笔记号。\n# en: \"Why me?\"\nopt.left.label [e79264] = “为什么是我？”\n# en: \"Because you looked at that door at the back of the library the way I do.\" He shrugs, and doesn't joke. \"And because it's lonely, knowing all the doors.\" You don't sleep much, after.\nopt.left.say.1 [b6a5ed] = “因为你看图书馆后面那扇门的眼神，跟我一样。”他耸耸肩，没有开玩笑。“还因为，知道所有的门，挺孤单的。”那之后你没怎么睡着。\n# en: \"You could have signed it.\"\nopt.right.label [e5208c] = “你本可以署名的。”\n# en: \"And spoil it?\" He tucks the map into your pocket. \"Keep it. The doors move. The map knows.\" You check it in the morning, and one of the six doors has moved.\nopt.right.say.1 [0b70cd] = “那多没意思？”他把地图塞进你口袋。“留着吧。门会挪地方。地图知道。”第二天早上你一看，六扇门里有一扇挪了位置。\n\n@event corvin_midnight_watch\n# en: Corvin Keeps Watch\ntitle [aa7961] = 科尔文放风\n# en: {Corvin leans out of a niche as you pass. \"Bram's on the east stair. Take the west. You owe me nothing, I'm a saint.\"|Corvin's in his niche again with an apple and a list of where every prefect is.|\"West stair,\" says Corvin, before you ask.} {He looks tired.|He has a new scratch on his cheek.|He's whistling, which he shouldn't.}\ntext [45e683] = {你经过时，科尔文从一个壁龛里探出身来。“布拉姆在东边楼梯。走西边。你什么都不欠我，我是圣人。”|科尔文又待在他的壁龛里，拿着一个苹果和一张每个级长的位置清单。|“西边楼梯，”你还没开口，科尔文就说了。}{他看起来很累。|他脸颊上多了一道新划痕。|他在吹口哨，这可不应该。}\n# en: A dark corridor at midnight, a dark-haired student leaning out of a niche to wave someone past, a prefect's lantern turning a far corner.\nscene [151b9a] = 午夜一条漆黑的走廊，一个黑发学生从壁龛里探出身来挥手让人通过，远处拐角有一盏级长的灯笼在转。\n# en: Keep him company\nopt.left.label [dd3cdd] = 陪他待一会儿\n# en: {You share his apple and his niche. He tells you which tapestry hides a cupboard full of confiscated wands. You're up till the bells.|You keep watch with him for an hour. He's funnier at midnight, and sadder, and pretends to be neither.|He whistles until you shush him, then whistles quieter. You keep watch till the bell and learn three new routes.}\nopt.left.say.1 [c9e2c9] = {你分享了他的苹果和他的壁龛。他告诉你哪块挂毯后面藏着一柜子没收来的魔杖。你一直待到钟声响起。|你陪他放了一个钟头的风。午夜的他更风趣，也更伤感，却假装两样都不是。|他一直吹口哨，你嘘了他，他就吹得小声些。你陪他守到钟响，学会了三条新路线。}\n# en: Take the west stair\nopt.right.label [19b545] = 走西边楼梯\n# en: {The west stair is dark and empty, as promised. Corvin's map is never wrong. You wonder who taught him to be so careful.|You slip past Bram's lamp with a corridor to spare.|West stair, before you've asked. Corvin's whistling follows you down it, far too loud, and nobody catches either of you.}\nopt.right.say.1 [f44f09] = {西边楼梯漆黑而空荡，正如他所说。科尔文的地图从来不会错。你很好奇是谁教会他这么小心的。|你隔着一条走廊的距离从布拉姆的灯下溜了过去。|你还没问他就说了西边楼梯。科尔文的口哨声一路跟着你下去，响得过分，可谁也没抓到你们俩。}\n\n@event midnight_swim\n# en: The Lake at Midnight\ntitle [d7eddc] = 午夜的湖\n# en: {season == \"winter\": The lake at midnight has a skin of ice at the edges and stars in the middle.|{~The lake at midnight is black and silver and perfectly still.|Nobody swims after curfew, which is why you do.|The water is warm from the day and the air is not.}} {clock(hollowstar) >= 5: Far out, a ring of frost forms and fades.|{~An owl crosses the moon.|Somewhere on the far shore, a lantern.|A fish jumps, very loudly.}}\ntext [aaf6ce] = {season == \"winter\": 午夜的湖边缘结了一层冰，中央倒映着星星。|{~午夜的湖黑中带银，纹丝不动。|宵禁后没人游泳，所以你才去。|水还留着白天的温度，空气却没有。}}{clock(hollowstar) >= 5: 远处，一圈霜冻成形，又消散了。|{~一只猫头鹰飞过月亮。|对岸某处有一盏灯笼。|一条鱼跳了起来，声音很响。}}\n# en: A moonlit lakeshore at midnight, clothes folded on a rock, a swimmer's head and a ring of ripples in the silver water.\nscene [7c16dd] = 午夜月光下的湖岸，衣服叠放在一块石头上，银色的水面上露出一个游泳者的头和一圈圈涟漪。\n# en: Swim out to the middle\nopt.left.label [1bcb3e] = 游到湖中央\n# en: {~You swim until the castle is a toy and the stars are all around you. The cold gets into your bones and stays till breakfast.|You float on your back in the middle of the lake. Something far below hums, once. You swim back faster than you swam out.|You swim out and turn on your back. The stars and the water are the same colour, and you lose track of which is which. You swim back slowly.}\nopt.left.say.1 [b1622d] = {~你一直游到城堡变得像个玩具，四面八方都是星星。寒气钻进骨头里，一直待到早饭时。|你仰面漂在湖中央。下面很深的地方嗡地响了一声。你游回去比游出来快多了。|你游出去，翻身仰躺。星星和湖水是同一种颜色，你分不清哪个是哪个。你慢慢地游了回去。}\n# en: Paddle, then get dry\nopt.right.label [cf2997] = 蹚蹚水就上岸擦干\n# en: {~Knee-deep, then waist-deep, then out, gasping and laughing at yourself. Your teeth chatter all the way up the lawn.|A quick, cold paddle, and back to bed glowing.|You paddle until the fish jumps right beside you. You're out of the water before you've decided to be.}\nopt.right.say.1 [04f629] = {~齐膝，齐腰，然后上岸，喘着气笑话自己。回草坪那头的一路上你的牙齿都在打颤。|又快又冷地蹚了一会儿水，回到床上浑身发热。|你蹚着水，直到那条鱼就在你身边跃起。你还没决定上岸，人已经在岸上了。}\n\n@event grey_on_the_shore\n# en: Someone Else Who Can't Sleep\ntitle [cc0062] = 另一个睡不着的人\n# en: The man in grey is sitting on an upturned boat at the water's edge, as if he'd been waiting for a ferry. \"You can't sleep either,\" he says pleasantly. \"Nobody sleeps well near this lake. Sit. I won't keep you long. I only want to tell you what the headmistress won't.\"\ntext [b8c4b5] = 灰衣人坐在水边一条倒扣的船上，像是在等渡船。“你也睡不着，”他愉快地说，“在这湖边没人睡得好。坐吧。我不会耽搁你太久。我只是想告诉你校长不肯说的事。”\n# en: A moonlit lakeshore at midnight, a tall man in a grey coat sitting on an upturned boat, frost spreading from his boots across the pebbles.\nscene [a47a73] = 午夜月光下的湖岸，一个穿灰大衣的高个男人坐在一条倒扣的船上，霜从他的靴子底下蔓延到卵石上。\n# en: Sit and listen\nopt.left.label [1a3cf5] = 坐下听他说\n# en: He tells you, kindly, that the founders were frightened people who buried what they couldn't understand, and that it has been lonely down there for three hundred years. It sounds true. You walk back with it, and can't put it down.\nopt.left.say.1 [7f0733] = 他和气地告诉你，创始者们是一群受了惊吓的人，把自己不理解的东西埋了起来，而那东西在下面已经孤单了三百年。听起来像是真的。你带着这些话走回去，怎么也放不下。\n# en: Walk away\nopt.right.label [96da1a] = 走开\n# en: You turn your back and walk, and the cold follows you all the way up the lawn like a dog. At the door you look back. The boat is empty, and frosted white.\nopt.right.say.1 [a9b029] = 你转身就走，寒意像条狗一样一路跟着你走过草坪。到了门口你回头一看，那条船空了，结满了白霜。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/pools/placeholders.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/pools/placeholders.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event courier_loft_quiet_hour\n# en: A Quiet Hour at the Loft\ntitle [d389f3] = 阁楼里安静的一小时\n# en: The Courier Loft is quiet today. The dispatch bell hangs still, the pigeonholes are full and nobody needs anything flown anywhere. It's a nice place when it isn't in a hurry.\ntext [9f81f9] = 今天信使阁楼很安静。派件铃一动不动地挂着，信格都塞满了，也没人需要把什么东西飞送到哪里去。不赶时间的时候，这里是个不错的地方。\n# en: An empty courier loft in slanting light, parcels stacked neatly, a single broom asleep in its rack.\nscene [3ebd34] = 斜阳照进空荡荡的信使阁楼，包裹码得整整齐齐，一把飞帚在架子上打盹。\n# en: Sit by the window a while\nopt.left.label [d7f0cb] = 在窗边坐一会儿\n# en: {~You watch the brooms doze in their rack and the dust turn in the light. Nothing happens, pleasantly.|An hour among the parcels with nobody shouting your name. You leave in a better mood.}\nopt.left.say.1 [1d96c5] = {~你看着飞帚在架子上打盹，灰尘在光里打转。什么也没发生，挺好。|在包裹堆里待了一个钟头，没人喊你的名字。你离开时心情好了些。}\n# en: Head off\nopt.right.label [3153de] = 走吧\n# en: {~You leave the loft to its quiet.|Nothing to fly today. You go and find something else to do.}\nopt.right.say.1 [89c506] = {~你把阁楼留给它的安静。|今天没什么要送的。你去找别的事做。}\n\n@event headmistress_tower_quiet_hour\n# en: An Empty Study\ntitle [46743e] = 空无一人的书房\n# en: The study at the top of the tower is empty. The headmistress is elsewhere, the tea has gone cold and the crystal board by the window throws little rainbows on the books. It's a nice room when nobody is deciding your future in it.\ntext [550ff3] = 塔顶的书房空着。院长去了别处，茶已经凉了，窗边的水晶棋盘把小小的彩虹投在书上。没人在这里决定你前途的时候，这是个不错的房间。\n# en: A tower study with nobody in it, tea going cold on a tray, a board of crystal towers catching the afternoon sun.\nscene [1d8fbb] = 空无一人的塔顶书房，托盘上的茶渐渐变凉，一盘水晶塔棋映着午后的阳光。\n# en: Enjoy the view a moment\nopt.left.label [b26df9] = 欣赏一会儿风景\n# en: {~You stand at the window and look down at the whole castle, small and busy. Then you go, before anyone asks why you're here.|The rainbows move slowly across the shelves. It's very peaceful, and a little like trespassing.}\nopt.left.say.1 [b8cd9d] = {~你站在窗前俯瞰整座城堡，小小的，忙忙碌碌。然后在有人问你为什么在这儿之前离开。|彩虹慢慢爬过书架。很安宁，也有点像擅闯。}\n# en: Leave before anyone comes\nopt.right.label [8e0dfa] = 趁没人来先走\n# en: {~You close the door softly behind you.|Nobody here today. You take the long stair down.}\nopt.right.say.1 [555f7d] = {~你轻轻带上身后的门。|今天这里没人。你沿着长长的楼梯走下去。}\n\n@event ledger_office_quiet_hour\n# en: A Slow News Day\ntitle [a6734a] = 没什么新闻的一天\n# en: It's a slow news day at the Lumen Ledger. The press is cold, the editor's chair is empty and the only sound is the library above, creaking. It's a nice room when nobody is on deadline.\ntext [6b16f1] = 今天《流明纪事报》没什么新闻。印刷机冷着，主编的椅子空着，唯一的声音是楼上图书馆的吱呀声。没人赶截稿的时候，这是个不错的房间。\n# en: A cramped newspaper office under a staircase, the press silent, old front pages curling on the walls.\nscene [a80603] = 楼梯下狭小的报社编辑部，印刷机静悄悄的，墙上的旧头版卷着边。\n# en: Read the old front pages\nopt.left.label [ad86e4] = 读读旧头版\n# en: {~You read headlines from before you were born. Half of them are about the refectory soup.|An hour with the old front pages. The castle has always been like this, it turns out.}\nopt.left.say.1 [8c60cf] = {~你读着你出生前的头条。一半都在说膳堂的汤。|和旧头版待了一个钟头。原来这座城堡一直都是这样。}\n# en: Come back on deadline night\nopt.right.label [37993d] = 截稿那晚再来\n# en: {~Nothing to write today. You leave the press to its nap.|You tidy a stack of proofs and go.}\nopt.right.say.1 [b77242] = {~今天没什么可写的。你让印刷机继续打盹。|你理好一叠校样，走了。}\n\n@event flight_field_quiet_hour\n# en: An Empty Field\ntitle [93dd37] = 空荡荡的飞行场\n# en: Nobody is flying today. The Flight Field is all grass and wind, the hoops at either end swinging a little on their own. It's a nice place when nobody is crashing into it.\ntext [430c2b] = 今天没人飞。飞行场上只有草和风，两头的圈环自己轻轻晃着。没人往地上栽的时候，这里是个不错的地方。\n# en: A long green flying field with nobody on it, hoops at either end, grass flattened by the wind.\nscene [e2279e] = 长长的绿色飞行场上空无一人，两头立着圈环，草被风压平了。\n# en: Lie in the grass a while\nopt.left.label [8fb767] = 在草地上躺一会儿\n# en: {~You watch the clouds race the hoops. The clouds win.|The wind does the flying today. You just watch it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [5a09ac] = {~你看着云和圈环赛跑。云赢了。|今天是风在飞。你只是看着。}\n# en: Walk on\nopt.right.label [383259] = 继续走\n# en: {~You cut across the empty field and go on your way.|No lessons, no races. You leave the field to the wind.}\nopt.right.say.1 [1f07bf] = {~你穿过空荡荡的飞行场，继续赶路。|没有课，没有比赛。你把飞行场留给了风。}\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/pools/plans_gigs.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/pools/plans_gigs.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event holiday_gigs\n# en: Holiday Work\ntitle [ec8a5c] = 假期打工\n# en: Two notices are still pinned by the gates. {season == \"winter\": The snow has got under one of them.|The sun has curled the corners of both.} Old Maud wants hands for the grounds. The Refectory kitchens want someone who can peel fast and not complain about it. {rank(library_assistant) >= 3 or rank(tavern) >= 3 or rank(apothecary_clerk) >= 3 or rank(broom_courier) >= 3: A third notice has your name on it.|}\ntext [9dfc2d] = 城门边还钉着两张告示。{season == \"winter\": 雪已经钻到其中一张底下了。|太阳把两张的边角都晒卷了。}老莫德要人手打理庭园。膳堂后厨要一个削皮快、还不抱怨的人。{rank(library_assistant) >= 3 or rank(tavern) >= 3 or rank(apothecary_clerk) >= 3 or rank(broom_courier) >= 3: 第三张告示上写着你的名字。|}\n# en: A noticeboard by the castle gates in the emptying holidays, two handwritten notices pinned side by side.\nscene [e17fee] = 假期里渐渐空下来的城堡大门边，一块告示板上并排钉着两张手写告示。\n# en: Work the grounds for Old Maud\nopt.grounds.label [304060] = 跟老莫德打理庭园\n# en: Outdoor work, heavy and honest. Good pay; hard on the body.\nopt.grounds.hint [1918f6] = 户外活，累但实在。工钱不错；伤身体。\n# en: Maud looks you up and down, hands you a pair of gloves two sizes too big, and says, \"You'll grow into them.\"\nopt.grounds.say.1 [d53e24] = 莫德把你从头打量到脚，递给你一副大了两号的手套，说：“你会长大的。”\n# en: Work the Refectory kitchens\nopt.kitchens.label [fb1ad3] = 去膳堂后厨帮工\n# en: Long hours by the ovens. Good pay; hard on the temper.\nopt.kitchens.hint [305589] = 在炉灶边站一整天。工钱不错；磨脾气。\n# en: The head cook gives you an apron, a knife and a sack of onions, and doesn't ask your name until the sack is empty.\nopt.kitchens.say.1 [e58aab] = 大厨递给你一条围裙、一把刀和一麻袋洋葱，直到麻袋空了才问你叫什么。\n# en: Run the library's holiday stocktake\nopt.library.label [b69455] = 负责图书馆的假期盘点\n# en: For senior clerks. Better pay; long days in the stacks.\nopt.library.hint [8ad639] = 给资深馆员的活。报酬更高；整天泡在书库里。\n# en: The head librarian hands you the stocktake ledger, a lamp and a pair of gauntlets. \"Count everything,\" she says. \"Twice, if it moves.\"\nopt.library.say.1 [0880b9] = 总馆员把盘点账簿、一盏灯和一副护手套交给你。“什么都要数，”她说，“会动的数两遍。”\n# en: Run the Newt through the holiday rush\nopt.newt.label [3759b3] = 带蝾螈酒馆撑过节日高峰\n# en: For the Newt's managers. The best tips of the year; the longest nights.\nopt.newt.hint [b33ad6] = 给蝾螈酒馆经理的活。一年里最好的小费；最长的夜。\n# en: The landlady gives you the keys and goes to visit her sister. \"Don't let the fire go out,\" she says, \"or the village.\"\nopt.newt.say.1 [33e627] = 老板娘把钥匙交给你，去看她妹妹了。“别让炉火灭了，”她说，“也别让村子乱了。”\n# en: Mix the season's remedies at Pell's\nopt.pell.label [beefb9] = 在佩尔的铺子调配应季药剂\n# en: For assistant apothecaries. Good pay; the village depends on you.\nopt.pell.hint [48142a] = 给助理药师的活。报酬不错；全村都指望着你。\n# en: Old Pell ties an apron on you himself. \"Half the valley will be coughing by next week,\" he says, delighted. \"We'll be rich.\"\nopt.pell.say.1 [3c5cdc] = 老佩尔亲手给你系上围裙。“下个星期半个山谷都要咳嗽，”他高兴地说，“我们要发财了。”\n# en: Fly the holiday post\nopt.post.label [339ed9] = 飞送节日邮件\n# en: For chief couriers. Every parcel in the valley; the weather against you.\nopt.post.hint [0579c6] = 给首席信使的活。山谷里的每一个包裹；还有跟你作对的天气。\n# en: The loft is stacked to the rafters with parcels in brown paper. Your name is chalked on the board above the busiest route.\nopt.post.say.1 [50dcda] = 阁楼里牛皮纸包裹堆到了房梁。最繁忙的那条路线上方的板子上，用粉笔写着你的名字。\n\n@event holiday_notices\n# en: Notices by the Gate\ntitle [92fc07] = 城门边的告示\n# en: {Holiday work notices are going up by the gates, for the grounds and the kitchens, both paying better than any term job.|The holiday notices again, the same two, in fresher ink.|The notices by the gate. You know them by heart.} {rank(library_assistant) >= 3 or rank(tavern) >= 3 or rank(apothecary_clerk) >= 3 or rank(broom_courier) >= 3: One is addressed to you by name, from your own job.|{Someone has written \"and board!\" under one.|A first-year is copying them out.|The wind is trying to take them.}}\ntext [466e79] = {城门边贴出了假期招工告示，庭园和后厨，报酬都比任何学期里的工作高。|又是那两张假期告示，墨迹更新了。|城门边的告示。你已经背下来了。}{rank(library_assistant) >= 3 or rank(tavern) >= 3 or rank(apothecary_clerk) >= 3 or rank(broom_courier) >= 3: 其中一张是你自己的工作写给你的，指名道姓。|{有人在一张下面写了“还包食宿！”|一个一年级生正在抄写。|风正想把它们刮走。}}\n# en: A noticeboard by the castle gates near the end of term, fresh notices for holiday work pinned over the old ones, students stopping to read.\nscene [ff22d4] = 学期快结束时城门边的告示板，新的假期招工告示钉在旧告示上面，学生们停下来看。\n# en: Pin them up for the cook\nopt.left.label [bead3d] = 替厨师把告示钉好\n# en: {The head cook pays you a coin to pin the new notices straight. Working the break would mean no lie-ins, and real money.|You pin them up, get a coin for it, and read the hours. It's tempting, and tiring just to read.|You pin them up against the wind for the head cook, who pays you a coin and asks, again, whether you'll be staying.}\nopt.left.say.1 [ac56f1] = {后厨大厨给你一枚硬币，让你把新告示钉正。假期打工意味着没有懒觉，但有真金白银。|你把告示钉好，得了一枚硬币，顺便看了看工时。很诱人，光是读一读就累了。|你顶着风替大厨把告示钉好，他给了你一枚硬币，又一次问你假期留不留下。}\n# en: Walk past\nopt.right.label [d5359c] = 走过去\n# en: {The break is for sleeping, you decide, and walk on. The notices flap behind you.|Not this time. You'll choose when the break comes.|You walk past. You know them by heart, and the break is still for sleeping.}\nopt.right.say.1 [bec8c3] = {假期是用来睡觉的，你决定，然后走开了。告示在你身后哗啦作响。|这次就算了。放假的时候再选吧。|你走了过去。告示你早就背熟了，假期还是用来睡觉的。}\n\n@event gig_grounds_hard_ground\n# en: Hard Ground\ntitle [fbbd07] = 硬土\n# en: {season == \"winter\": The ground is frozen two fingers deep and Maud wants the beds turned anyway.|The ground is baked hard and Maud wants the beds turned anyway.} \"The soil doesn't take holidays,\" she says. {~She hands you the heavier spade.|The bees watch you from their hives with professional interest.|She is already on her third bed.}\ntext [3eb87e] = {season == \"winter\": 地冻了两指深，莫德照样要你把苗床翻一遍。|地被晒得梆硬，莫德照样要你把苗床翻一遍。}“土可不放假。”她说。{~她把重的那把铁锹递给你。|蜂箱里的蜜蜂以一种专业的兴趣看着你。|她已经在翻第三块了。}\n# en: A student and an old woman working a frozen garden bed at dawn, breath steaming, a wheelbarrow between them.\nscene [2f53ea] = 黎明时分，一个学生和一位老妇人在结冻的苗床上干活，呼出白气，两人之间放着一辆手推车。\n# en: Dig until it's done\nopt.left.label [9aec2a] = 一直挖到干完\n# en: {~By noon your hands have blisters on their blisters. Maud counts your wages into them anyway.|You turn every bed. Maud inspects them, says \"Hm,\" and pays you a little over.|You dig until Maud's third bed is matched by your third. She pays you, and gives you the lighter spade next time.}\nopt.left.say.1 [a1924d] = {~到中午，你手上的水泡又起了水泡。莫德照样把工钱数进你手里。|你翻完了每一块苗床。莫德检查一遍，说了声“嗯”，多给了你一点。|你一直挖到你翻完的苗床追上了莫德的第三块。她付了钱，还说下回给你轻些的铁锹。}\n# en: Work at Maud's pace\nopt.right.label [1e7055] = 跟着莫德的节奏\n# en: {~Maud works slowly and never stops, and by evening you understand why. Less done, a smaller purse, an easier back.|You keep to her pace and her stories. She pays for the beds you turned, not the ones she did.|You work at her pace, and she still finishes three beds to your one. She pays for yours, and laughs about it.}\nopt.right.say.1 [abb3c1] = {~莫德干得慢，却从不停手，到傍晚你才明白为什么。活干得少，钱袋轻些，腰也轻松些。|你跟着她的节奏，听着她的故事。她按你翻的苗床付钱，不算她翻的。|你照她的节奏干，她还是翻了三块，你才翻一块。她按你翻的付钱，笑个不停。}\n\n@event gig_grounds_storm_damage\n# en: After the Storm\ntitle [683893] = 风暴过后\n# en: {season == \"winter\": Last night's storm brought down half a pear tree.|A summer storm in the night has stripped the orchard.} Maud wants it cleared before the stay-behinds go walking. A cracked limb still hangs over the path.\ntext [506bdf] = {season == \"winter\": 昨夜的风暴吹倒了半棵梨树。|夜里一场夏日风暴把果园刮得七零八落。}莫德要在留校的人出来散步之前把路清出来。一根裂开的树枝还悬在小路上方。\n# en: A fallen branch across an orchard path after a storm, a student with a saw, an old woman pointing at something higher up.\nscene [a80029] = 风暴过后，一根断枝横在果园小路上，一个学生拿着锯子，一位老妇人指着更高处的什么东西。\n# en: Climb up and cut it down\nopt.left.label [d35b25] = 爬上去把它锯下来\n# en: {~The limb comes down where you meant it to. Maud pays you extra for not falling.|You lower it on a rope like a professional. Maud pretends not to be impressed.}\nopt.left.say.1 [378de5] = {~树枝正落在你想让它落的地方。莫德因为你没摔下来，多给了你钱。|你像个行家一样用绳子把它放了下来。莫德假装没被打动。}\n# en: {~The limb comes down where it wanted to. So do you, more or less. Maud pays you and sends you to the infirmary.|You get the limb and the limb gets you.}\nopt.left.say.2 [10307d] = {~树枝落在了它想落的地方。你也差不多。莫德付了钱，把你送去了医务室。|你拿下了树枝，树枝也拿下了你。}\n# en: Rope it off and clear the rest\nopt.right.label [27673a] = 拉绳隔开，清理其余\n# en: {~You clear the path and rope off the danger. Maud nods: she'll ask someone with a ladder. The pay is for the path.|Safe and sensible, and a smaller purse for it.}\nopt.right.say.1 [8ff26f] = {~你清好了小路，把危险的地方用绳子隔开。莫德点点头：她会找个有梯子的人来。工钱只算小路的。|稳妥又明智，钱袋也因此轻一些。}\n\n@event gig_grounds_hive_check\n# en: Checking the Hives\ntitle [5a5a35] = 查看蜂箱\n# en: Maud's hives sit at the end of the orchard and she checks them {season == \"winter\": by ear in winter, knocking and listening|by hand in summer, slow and bare-armed}. Today she wants you to do it. \"Tell them who you are,\" she says. \"They like manners.\"\ntext [169e49] = 莫德的蜂箱放在果园尽头，她{season == \"winter\": 冬天靠耳朵检查，敲一敲，听一听|夏天亲手检查，慢慢来，光着胳膊}。今天她要你来做。“告诉它们你是谁，”她说，“它们讲礼貌。”\n# en: A student in a veiled hat leaning over an open beehive beside an old woman, who isn't wearing one.\nscene [d6c9fc] = 一个戴着面纱帽的学生俯身在打开的蜂箱前，旁边的老妇人什么也没戴。\n# en: Introduce yourself properly\nopt.left.label [182928] = 规规矩矩地自我介绍\n# en: {~You tell the bees your name and your house. Only one stings you, on principle. Maud pays you and calls you a natural.|You feel foolish for about a minute, and then you don't. The hive hums back, and one bee makes a point of it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [5a9176] = {~你把名字和学院告诉了蜜蜂。只有一只蜇了你，出于原则。莫德付了钱，说你是天生的料。|你觉得自己傻了大约一分钟，然后就不觉得了。蜂箱嗡嗡地回应你，有一只蜜蜂特意强调了一下。}\n# en: Get it done quickly\nopt.right.label [b40e28] = 快点干完\n# en: {~Quick and quiet. The bees let it pass this once.|You're done before the bees notice. Maud notices.}\nopt.right.say.1 [0c0b37] = {~又快又安静。蜜蜂这次放过了你。|你干完了，蜜蜂还没察觉。莫德察觉了。}\n# en: {~The bees take your hurry personally. Maud pays you and pulls out the stings with no sympathy at all.|Three stings and a lesson about manners.}\nopt.right.say.2 [f4f88a] = {~蜜蜂把你的匆忙当成了冒犯。莫德付了钱，毫无同情地替你拔出蜂刺。|三根蜂刺，外加一堂礼貌课。}\n\n@event gig_kitchens_onions\n# en: The Onion Sack\ntitle [c188ed] = 洋葱麻袋\n# en: {The holiday kitchens feed fewer mouths and work no fewer hours.|Back at the long table, back to the sack.|The head cook has started saving the worst sack for you, as a compliment.} {season == \"winter\": The Midwinter stock needs a hundred onions.|The summer stew needs a hundred onions.} Nobody has ever needed fewer.\ntext [dc295a] = {假期里的后厨要喂的嘴少了，活一点没少。|回到长桌，回到麻袋。|大厨开始把最糟的那袋留给你，算是一种夸奖。}{season == \"winter\": 仲冬的高汤要一百个洋葱。|夏天的炖菜要一百个洋葱。}从来没人需要少一点。\n# en: A student at a scrubbed kitchen table behind a mountain of onions, eyes streaming, a huge cook stirring a cauldron behind them.\nscene [a0922b] = 擦洗干净的厨房长桌后，一个学生埋在洋葱堆里泪流满面，身后一个高大的厨子在搅一口大锅。\n# en: Peel the whole sack\nopt.left.label [211f4f] = 把整袋都削完\n# en: {A hundred onions and not one tear left in you. The head cook pays you and adds a heel of fresh bread.|You finish the sack. The head cook counts the onions, then your wages, both correctly.|You peel the worst sack in the kitchen and don't cry once. The head cook calls it a personal best, and pays it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [e277dd] = {一百个洋葱，你一滴眼泪都不剩了。大厨付了钱，又塞给你一块新鲜面包。|你削完了整袋。大厨数了洋葱，又数了你的工钱，都没数错。|你削完了厨房里最难对付的那袋，一滴眼泪都没掉。大厨说这是你的个人最好成绩，照价付钱。}\n# en: Peel, then help at the stove\nopt.right.label [f73412] = 削一半，再去灶台帮忙\n# en: {Half a sack and an hour at the stove. Less pay, but now you know why the soup tastes like home.|The head cook lets you stir. Somehow the stew tastes of your grandmother's. You get paid for the onions.|You peel half the worst sack and stir the rest of the morning. The head cook pays for the onions and teaches you a stew.}\nopt.right.say.1 [f6b173] = {半袋洋葱，加上灶台边的一个钟头。钱少些，但现在你知道这汤为什么有家的味道了。|大厨让你搅锅。不知怎么，炖菜有了你外婆的味道。你拿到了洋葱的工钱。|你削了那袋最难对付的一半，剩下的上午都在搅锅。大厨按洋葱付了钱，还教了你一道炖菜。}\n\n@event gig_kitchens_burnt_pan\n# en: The Burnt Pan\ntitle [ce0174] = 烧焦的锅\n# en: You turn away for a moment and the porridge for the stay-behinds catches. The smell is already halfway to the dining hall. The head cook hasn't noticed. Yet.\ntext [3467e5] = 你一转身，给留校的人煮的粥就糊了。焦味已经飘到半路的饭堂。大厨还没发现。暂时。\n# en: A smoking pan on a range, a student holding it at arm's length, a cook turning round with a ladle raised.\nscene [078326] = 灶台上一口冒烟的锅，一个学生伸直胳膊举着它，一个厨子举着汤勺转过身来。\n# en: Own up and start again\nopt.left.label [df7779] = 坦白，重新煮\n# en: {~The head cook sighs the sigh of thirty years in kitchens and docks you for the oats. You make it again, properly.|\"Everyone burns one,\" says the head cook. \"Once.\" You are paid, minus the oats.}\nopt.left.say.1 [d4b5ef] = {~大厨叹了一口三十年后厨的气，扣了你的燕麦钱。你重新煮了一锅，这次好好煮。|“谁都烧糊过一锅，”大厨说，“一次。”你拿到了工钱，扣掉燕麦。}\n# en: Scrape it and serve it\nopt.right.label [1bae79] = 刮一刮端出去\n# en: {~Nobody notices. The stay-behinds eat it and ask for more. You don't feel great about it.|A little smoky. \"Rustic,\" says someone. You get the full day's pay.}\nopt.right.say.1 [15e03b] = {~没人发现。留校的人吃了还要添。你心里不太舒服。|有点烟熏味。“很有乡土风味。”有人说。你拿到了全天的工钱。}\n# en: {~A professor sends it back with a note. The head cook reads it aloud, slowly, to the whole kitchen.|The smell gives you away. You're paid in full and in disapproval.}\nopt.right.say.2 [8635a7] = {~一位教授把粥退了回来，附了张字条。大厨当着整个后厨，一字一字慢慢念了出来。|焦味出卖了你。工钱一分不少，白眼也一个不少。}\n\n@event gig_kitchens_feast_night\n# en: Feast Night\ntitle [17ed66] = 宴会之夜\n# en: {season == \"winter\": Tonight the castle keeps a small Midwinter table for those who stayed, and the kitchens will cook as if for the whole school.|Tonight the stay-behinds hold a summer supper in the courtyard, and the kitchens will cook as if for the whole school.} At breakfast the head cook chalks up the rota: somebody carries trays, somebody plates.\ntext [2e15f4] = {season == \"winter\": 今晚城堡要为留下来的人摆一小桌仲冬宴，后厨会按全校的分量来做。|今晚留校的人要在庭院里办夏日晚餐，后厨会按全校的分量来做。}早饭时大厨在黑板上排了班：有人端托盘，有人装盘。\n# en: A steaming kitchen on a feast night, trays going out in a stream, a student in an apron grinning through the chaos.\nscene [53b738] = 宴会之夜热气腾腾的后厨，托盘一个接一个端出去，一个系着围裙的学生在混乱中咧嘴笑着。\n# en: Volunteer to carry trays\nopt.left.label [cb7924] = 主动去端托盘\n# en: {~You carry until your arms forget being arms. The head cook pays you double for the night, in coins and leftovers.|Up and down the stairs until midnight. Somebody toasts the kitchens and you hear it through the door.}\nopt.left.say.1 [aecfbc] = {~你端到胳膊都忘了自己是胳膊。大厨付了你这一晚的双份工钱，一份是钱，一份是剩菜。|楼上楼下一直跑到半夜。有人为后厨举杯，你隔着门都听见了。}\n# en: Ask to plate in the back\nopt.right.label [c0f09c] = 申请在后面装盘\n# en: {~You plate by the ovens and listen to the feast through the wall. Smaller pay, a scorched wrist, and the best seat in the house.|Quiet hands in a hot room. The head cook gives you the last slice of pie, which is its own wage.}\nopt.right.say.1 [cae555] = {~你在烤炉边装盘，隔着墙听宴会的热闹。钱少些，手腕烫了一下，却是全场最好的位置。|热屋子里一双安静的手。大厨把最后一块派给了你，这本身就是一份工钱。}\n\n@event gig_grounds_goat\n# en: Bartholomew Escapes\ntitle [ecc00d] = 巴塞洛缪越狱了\n# en: {Bartholomew the goat has got out again and is standing on the shed roof, eating the thatch.|Bartholomew is on the shed roof. He has made this a habit.|The goat is on the roof. You don't even ask how any more.} Maud is laughing too hard to help. {The thatch is going fast.|He has a cabbage in his mouth.|He looks at you like a duke.}\ntext [b83cf2] = {山羊巴塞洛缪又跑出来了，站在棚屋顶上啃茅草。|巴塞洛缪在棚屋顶上。他已经养成习惯了。|山羊在屋顶上。你都懒得问它是怎么上去的了。}莫德笑得太厉害，帮不上忙。{茅草少得飞快。|他嘴里叼着一颗卷心菜。|他看你的样子像个公爵。}\n# en: A goat standing triumphantly on the roof of a garden shed, a student with a rope below, an old woman laughing behind them.\nscene [10fe10] = 一只山羊得意洋洋地站在园中棚屋的屋顶上，下面一个学生拿着绳子，后面一位老妇人在笑。\n# en: Climb up after him\nopt.left.label [7a4523] = 爬上去追他\n# en: {You get him down after an hour and a fall. Maud pays you double for the entertainment, she says, and a little for the bruises.|Up the ladder, along the ridge, one hand on his horn. He comes down when he's ready, which is later than you are.|You climb up and bow to him. He considers it, then lets himself be led down like a duke leaving a ball. Maud pays you for the bow.}\nopt.left.say.1 [7362ea] = {你摔了一跤，花了一个钟头才把他弄下来。莫德说为了这场好戏付你双倍工钱，再加一点淤青的钱。|爬上梯子，沿着屋脊，一只手抓住他的角。他想下来的时候才下来，比你想的晚。|你爬上去，向他鞠了一躬。他考虑了一下，然后像离开舞会的公爵一样让你牵了下来。莫德为那一躬付了钱。}\n# en: Bribe him down with an apple\nopt.right.label [bc5cca] = 用苹果哄他下来\n# en: {One apple, held very still. He considers it for a long time, like a merchant, and comes down. Maud pays for the hour you saved.|He takes the apple and stays where he is. Then, at his own pace, he comes down. Maud pays you for patience.|He takes the apple like tribute and descends. Maud pays you, and says he'll expect two next time.}\nopt.right.say.1 [6324d9] = {一个苹果，一动不动地举着。他像个商人一样考虑了很久，然后下来了。莫德为你省下的那个钟头付了钱。|他接过苹果，站着不动。然后，按他自己的节奏，下来了。莫德为你的耐心付了钱。|他像收贡品一样接过苹果，屈尊下来了。莫德付了钱，说他下回会要两个。}\n\n@event gig_kitchens_staff_supper\n# en: The Staff Supper\ntitle [b2c3f9] = 员工晚餐\n# en: {At the morning briefing the head cook announces a staff supper tonight, after the last service. The whole kitchen eats at the scrubbed table, on leftovers, bread and the head cook's opinions.|Staff supper again tonight. They're saving you a place now.|The head cook bangs a ladle at breakfast. \"Staff supper tonight. Sit, or serve.\"} {Somebody is already promising a story about a swan.|The pot-boy is asleep on his feet.|There's a cake cooling that nobody will admit to making.}\ntext [cebb18] = {晨会上大厨宣布，今晚最后一轮供餐结束后有员工晚餐。整个后厨围着擦洗干净的桌子一起吃，吃剩菜、面包，还有大厨的高见。|今晚又是员工晚餐。他们现在给你留了位置。|大厨在早饭时敲了敲长柄勺。“今晚员工晚餐。要么坐，要么端。”}{已经有人许诺要讲一个关于天鹅的故事。|洗锅的男孩站着就睡着了。|有个蛋糕正在晾着，没人承认是自己做的。}\n# en: Cooks and kitchen hands crowded round a scrubbed table late at night, eating the day's leftovers, one student serving.\nscene [7186d1] = 深夜里厨师和帮厨们挤在一张擦洗干净的桌子旁吃当天的剩菜，一个学生在上菜。\n# en: Serve, then wash up\nopt.left.label [c4f447] = 先上菜，再洗碗\n# en: {You finish the washing-up at midnight. The head cook pays you extra and calls you \"the useful one\".|You serve, wash up, and wake the pot-boy at midnight to send him to bed. The head cook pays you for both jobs.|You serve forty bowls, wash forty bowls, and are paid for the extra hour in coins and a slice of the mystery cake.}\nopt.left.say.1 [e47162] = {你午夜才洗完碗。大厨多付了你钱，叫你“有用的那个”。|你端菜、洗碗，半夜还叫醒了洗锅的男孩让他去睡觉。大厨两份活都付了钱。|你端了四十碗，洗了四十碗，多干的那个钟头的报酬是硬币和一块神秘蛋糕。}\n# en: Sit and eat with them\nopt.right.label [22c3ea] = 坐下和他们一起吃\n# en: {You eat, and laugh at the swan story, and leave earlier with a smaller purse and a full stomach.|You sit. The cooks tell you which professors send back their soup. It's worth more than the wages.|You sit. Nobody admits to the cake, so everyone eats it. You leave full and a little poorer.}\nopt.right.say.1 [ca6576] = {你吃着饭，听天鹅的故事笑出声，早早离开，钱袋小了些，肚子饱了。|你坐了下来。厨师们告诉你哪些教授会把汤退回来。这比工钱值钱。|你坐了下来。没人承认那个蛋糕是谁做的，于是大家一起吃了。你离开时肚子饱了，钱少了一点。}\n\n@event gig_library_stocktake\n# en: The Stocktake\ntitle [987da1] = 盘点\n# en: {The library in the holidays is all yours and all to be counted.|The stocktake ledger is half full; the shelves are not half counted.|You've stopped counting books and started counting floors.} {A shelf of atlases has migrated to the poetry section.|One book is not on any list and seems pleased about it.|The lamp in the east wing keeps going out, politely.}\ntext [9fdd33] = {假期里的图书馆全归你，也全等着你数。|盘点账簿填了一半；书架连一半都还没数完。|你已经不数书了，开始数楼层。}{一架地图集迁移到了诗歌区。|有本书不在任何清单上，似乎还挺得意。|东翼的灯老是礼貌地熄灭。}\n# en: A deserted library in the holidays, a student on a tall ladder with a ledger, books stacked on every table, dust in the lamplight.\nscene [d520da] = 假期里空无一人的图书馆，一个学生拿着账簿站在高梯上，每张桌子上都堆满了书，灯光里浮着灰尘。\n# en: Count the restricted shelves too\nopt.left.label [0c6c3e] = 连禁书架也一起数\n# en: {The restricted shelves count back. You finish with the right number and a bite on the thumb. The librarian dresses the bite and writes it in the ledger, under \"damages\".|You count every chained volume. One of them counts you. The pay is better than term-time's by a good margin.|You count the restricted floor by the lamp that keeps going out. The books count you in the dark. The pay is the best of the break.}\nopt.left.say.1 [f84570] = {禁书架会回数你。你数对了数目，拇指上挨了一口。总馆员给你包扎好伤口，把它记在账本的“损耗”一栏。|你数遍了每一本锁链书。其中一本也数了你。报酬比学期里高出一大截。|你借着那盏老是熄灭的灯数禁书那层。书在黑暗里数你。报酬是整个假期里最高的。}\n# en: Keep to the open stacks\nopt.right.label [fa9957] = 只数开放书库\n# en: {Open stacks only, every book counted, the ledger perfect. The librarian pays you in full and says nothing about the shelves you left.|A long, dusty, honest day. You go to bed counting.|You count the open floors and leave the east wing to its lamp. The librarian pays in full and asks no questions.}\nopt.right.say.1 [0c8ec2] = {只数开放书库，每本书都数了，账簿完美。总馆员全额付了钱，对你没数的书架只字不提。|漫长、满是灰尘、老老实实的一天。你躺在床上还在数。|你数完开放的楼层，把东翼留给那盏灯。总馆员全额付钱，什么也没问。}\n\n@event gig_library_lost_shelf\n# en: The Shelf That Wasn't There\ntitle [b74ee7] = 不存在的书架\n# en: {The stocktake finds a shelf that isn't in any ledger.|The extra shelf again. It has grown a book since yesterday.|The shelf is longer than the room it's in, now.} {The books on it have no titles.|One is warm.|One is labelled with the date of tomorrow.}\ntext [ad6982] = {盘点时发现了一个任何账簿上都没有的书架。|又是那个多出来的书架。从昨天起它又长出了一本书。|现在这个书架比它所在的房间还长。}{上面的书都没有书名。|有一本是温的。|有一本标着明天的日期。}\n# en: A narrow library aisle that seems to go on further than the building, a student holding a lamp up to an unfamiliar shelf.\nscene [6b7883] = 一条窄窄的图书馆过道，似乎比建筑本身还长，一个学生把灯举向一个陌生的书架。\n# en: Catalogue it\nopt.left.label [b5435f] = 把它编目\n# en: {You list every book. The librarian reads your list, goes pale and pays you to forget it. You don't.|You catalogue the shelf by lamplight. In the morning, it's gone, and your list is the only proof.|You catalogue the book labelled with tomorrow's date. Tomorrow, the label has changed to the day after. The librarian pays you and takes your list.}\nopt.left.say.1 [fd0152] = {你列出了每一本书。总馆员读了你的清单，脸色发白，付钱让你忘掉它。你没忘。|你借着灯光给书架编了目。第二天早上它不见了，你的清单是唯一的证据。|你把那本标着明天日期的书编了目。到了明天，标签变成了后天。总馆员付了钱，收走了你的清单。}\n# en: Leave it off the ledger\nopt.right.label [bee004] = 不记进账簿\n# en: {You don't write it down. Some shelves don't want to be counted. The librarian, passing, nods as if you'd passed a test.|You skip it. The ledger balances. The shelf, you'd swear, looks relieved.|You don't write down the book with tomorrow's date. Tomorrow, it isn't there. The librarian thanks you, oddly sincerely.}\nopt.right.say.1 [b9760e] = {你没有把它写下来。有些书架不想被数。总馆员经过时点了点头，好像你通过了一场考验。|你跳过了它。账簿平了。那个书架，你敢发誓，看起来松了口气。|你没记那本标着明天日期的书。到了明天，它不见了。总馆员向你道谢，出奇地真诚。}\n\n@event gig_library_last_day\n# en: The Ledger Balances\ntitle [0c78c2] = 账目平了\n# en: {The last page of the stocktake. The librarian reads it slowly, all of it.|Another stocktake done, another ledger signed.|The librarian signs, and blots, and signs again for luck.} {\"Not one missing,\" she says. \"That has never happened.\"|\"Three missing, and one extra. Normal.\"|She closes the ledger with both hands.}\ntext [462f2e] = {盘点的最后一页。总馆员慢慢地读，一字不漏。|又一次盘点结束，又一本账簿签了字。|总馆员签了字，吸干墨迹，为了好运又签了一次。}{“一本没少，”她说，“从来没有过。”|“少了三本，多了一本。正常。”|她用双手合上账簿。}\n# en: A head librarian signing a thick stocktake ledger at a desk under a window, a tired student waiting with ink on every finger.\nscene [41e847] = 窗下书桌前一位总馆员在厚厚的盘点账簿上签字，一个疲惫的学生在等着，每根手指上都沾着墨水。\n# en: Ask for a reference\nopt.left.label [d577d3] = 请她写封推荐信\n# en: {She writes one on the spot, three lines, and the third makes you blush. The pay is good and the reference is better.|She writes you a reference in a hand like engraving. It's worth more than the wages, someone tells you later.|She signs the ledger and then, without being asked, a reference. \"You'll want it,\" she says. You will.}\nopt.left.say.1 [63ffa9] = {她当场写了一封：三行字，第三行让你脸红。报酬不错，推荐信更好。|她用镌刻般的字迹给你写了推荐信。后来有人告诉你，它比工钱值钱。|她签完账本，没等你开口又签了一封推荐信。“你会用得上的，”她说。你会的。}\n# en: Ask for the afternoon off\nopt.right.label [25c4ff] = 请她放你一个下午的假\n# en: {She gives you the afternoon and a little less pay. You spend it asleep in the reading room, which is the best use of it.|An afternoon off, and a library to yourself. You read for pleasure for the first time in months.|She closes the ledger and gives you the afternoon before you ask. You spend it in the window seat, doing nothing whatsoever.}\nopt.right.say.1 [04e177] = {她给了你一个下午，工钱少了一点。你在阅览室里睡了一下午，这是最好的用法。|一个下午的假，一整座图书馆归你。几个月来你第一次为了乐趣读书。|你还没开口，她就合上账本，给了你一个下午。你在窗边座位上度过了它，什么也没干。}\n\n@event gig_newt_rush\n# en: The Holiday Rush\ntitle [c1bbea] = 节日高峰\n# en: {The landlady hands you the keys over breakfast. Tonight, she warns, the Newt will be full to the rafters with farmers, barge crews and families home for the season.|Another holiday night ahead, and the Newt is already promised to a full house.|By morning, half the valley has said it's coming to the Newt tonight.} {A wedding party has sent word.|The fiddler has promised a song with forty verses.|The firewood is stacked to the ceiling.}\ntext [26936d] = {早饭时老板娘把钥匙交给你。她提醒你，今晚蝾螈酒馆会挤满农夫、驳船船员和回家过节的一家家人。|又一个节日之夜要来了，蝾螈酒馆早已订满。|天一亮，半个山谷的人都说今晚要来蝾螈酒馆。}{一支婚礼队伍已经捎了话来。|提琴手答应要拉一首四十段的歌。|木柴一直堆到了天花板。}\n# en: A packed village tavern at the holidays, garlands over the bar, every table full, a student manager behind the bar with three jugs in hand.\nscene [c18223] = 节日里挤满人的村酒馆，吧台上挂着花环，每张桌子都坐满了，一个学生经理站在吧台后，手里拿着三个酒壶。\n# en: Keep the bar open till the last song\nopt.left.label [687db7] = 营业到最后一首歌\n# en: {You pour till the song ends, then pour for the encore. The till is the heaviest you've ever counted, and your feet will never forgive you.|Closing at three, the till heavy, the floor sticky. The landlady's note says \"Well done\" in two places.|Half the valley comes, and you keep pouring until the firewood runs low. The till is heavy, and so is your sleep.}\nopt.left.say.1 [fef2c7] = {你一直倒酒倒到歌唱完，又为返场接着倒。这是你数过最沉的钱柜，你的脚永远不会原谅你。|凌晨三点打烊，钱柜沉甸甸的，地板黏糊糊的。老板娘的字条上有两处写着“干得好”。|半个山谷的人都来了，你一直倒酒倒到柴火见底。钱柜很沉，你睡得也很沉。}\n# en: Close at the proper hour\nopt.right.label [d3eedf] = 按时打烊\n# en: {You call time at midnight, firmly and politely. Some grumble. The till is lighter, and you sleep.|Last orders on the stroke. The till loses some takings; you keep your temper.|You close on the stroke, half the valley or not. They go home singing, which counts as a good night.}\nopt.right.say.1 [c910f0] = {你在午夜坚定而礼貌地宣布打烊。有人抱怨。钱柜轻了一些，你睡了个好觉。|准点最后点单。钱柜少了些进账；你保住了好脾气。|不管半个山谷的人在不在，你准点打烊。他们唱着歌回了家，这也算是个好夜晚。}\n\n@event gig_newt_brawl\n# en: A Fight Over a Song\ntitle [08605c] = 为一首歌打起来\n# en: {Two barge crews disagree about the last verse of a song, loudly.|The barge crews again, and the song again, and the same verse.|This time it's a farmer and a fisherman, and the song is a different one.} {A chair is already in the air.|Somebody has taken their coat off.|The fiddler has stopped playing.}\ntext [f3af46] = {两拨驳船船员为一首歌的最后一段争得不可开交。|又是那两拨船员，又是那首歌，又是那一段。|这次是一个农夫和一个渔夫，歌也换了一首。}{已经有一把椅子飞在空中了。|有人脱了外套。|提琴手停了下来。}\n# en: Two barge crews squaring up in the middle of a crowded tavern, a student manager stepping between them with a tray.\nscene [768c1a] = 两拨驳船船员在拥挤的酒馆中央对峙，一个学生经理端着托盘挡在他们中间。\n# en: Step between them\nopt.left.label [f242e3] = 站到他们中间\n# en: {You stop the first punch with a tray and the second with a look. Both crews buy a round to make up. You take the tips.|You walk between them and stay there. Eventually somebody laughs, and the song goes on.|The fiddler stops; you start the next verse yourself, badly. Both sides correct you, together, and forget to fight.}\nopt.left.say.1 [39c3a7] = {你用托盘挡住了第一拳，用眼神挡住了第二拳。两拨人各请了一轮酒赔罪。小费归你。|你走到他们中间，站着不动。最后有人笑了，歌又唱了下去。|提琴手停了；你自己唱起了下一段，唱得很糟。两边一起纠正你，忘了打架。}\n# en: {The tray takes one punch and your jaw takes the other. They pay for the chair, at least.|You get a black eye and a free drink, which you can't drink, being on duty.|The fiddler stops, and so does the fight, just as you step in. Everyone laughs at your timing. You take the bruise for nothing.}\nopt.left.say.2 [00905e] = {托盘挨了一拳，你的下巴挨了另一拳。至少他们赔了椅子。|你得了一只黑眼圈和一杯免费的酒，可你在当班，不能喝。|你刚走进去，提琴手停了，架也停了。大家都笑你来得真是时候。你白挨了一下。}\n# en: Bribe them with a free round\nopt.right.label [63b266] = 请他们喝一轮\n# en: {One round on the house, and they're singing together by the second verse. The house is a little poorer.|A free round fixes most things. The landlady's ledger will notice.|A free round, and the fiddler starts again. The farmer and the fisherman sing a duet, and the landlady's ledger winces.}\nopt.right.say.1 [912a4e] = {店里请了一轮，到第二段他们就一起唱起来了。店里穷了一点。|一轮免费的酒能解决大多数问题。老板娘的账簿会注意到的。|一轮免费的酒，提琴手又拉了起来。农夫和渔夫唱起了二重唱，老板娘的账簿疼得一缩。}\n\n@event gig_newt_fire\n# en: The Fire Must Not Go Out\ntitle [bda4da] = 炉火不能灭\n# en: {The landlady's fire has never gone out in her lifetime or her mother's. She's away tonight, and this morning the wind is already in the chimney.|The landlady is away again tonight, and the fire is already low. The wind is from the north.|The fire flickered last night as you locked up, as if to test you. Tonight it's yours again.} {The wood is damp.|There's frost on the inside of the windows.|The cat is sitting on the warmest stone, unhelpfully.}\ntext [d3a8ff] = {老板娘的炉火在她这辈子和她母亲那辈子都没灭过。她今晚不在，而今早风已经灌进了烟囱。|老板娘今晚又不在，火已经弱了。刮的是北风。|昨晚你锁门时火苗闪了闪，像是在考验你。今晚它又归你管了。}{木柴是潮的。|窗户里面结了霜。|猫坐在最暖的石头上，一点也不帮忙。}\n# en: The great hearth of a village tavern late at night, embers banked low, a student kneeling with tongs and a bucket of ash.\nscene [95b956] = 深夜里村酒馆的大壁炉，余烬低低地封着，一个学生拿着火钳和一桶炉灰跪在旁边。\n# en: Sit up with it all night\nopt.left.label [18ab48] = 守它一整夜\n# en: {You feed it one stick at a time until dawn. When the landlady comes back, she puts a hand on the stones and nods at you without a word.|You sleep in the chair with the tongs in your lap. The fire lives. So, nearly, do you.|You sit up with it, cat on the warmest stone. At dawn it's still burning, and the cat has not moved once.}\nopt.left.say.1 [00b64c] = {你一根一根地添柴，一直到天亮。老板娘回来后，把手放在炉石上，一言不发地朝你点了点头。|你抱着火钳在椅子上睡着了。火活了下来。你也差不多。|你守着火，猫占着最暖的那块石头。天亮时火还烧着，那只猫一次都没挪窝。}\n# en: Bank it and trust it\nopt.right.label [fc6608] = 封好火，相信它\n# en: {You bank it the way she showed you and go to bed. You dream of ash. In the morning, it's alive, and you're shaking.|You trust it. It holds. You don't sleep a wink anyway.|You bank it and move the cat. The cat moves back. In the morning the fire's alive, and the cat is very warm.}\nopt.right.say.1 [df4e6b] = {你按她教的方法封好火，上床睡觉。你梦见了灰烬。早上它还活着，你却在发抖。|你相信了它。它撑住了。你还是一眼都没合上。|你封好火，把猫挪开。猫又挪了回来。早上火还活着，猫暖和极了。}\n\n@event gig_pell_remedy_rush\n# en: Cough Season\ntitle [efc796] = 咳嗽季\n# en: {The whole valley is coughing, and Pell's queue is out of the door.|Day three of the cough. Pell is humming. You are not.|The queue at Pell's has its own gossip by now.} {A carter wants the cherry one.|A farmer wants something for his cow.|A professor in disguise wants something \"for a friend\".}\ntext [e7e4fe] = {整个山谷都在咳嗽，佩尔铺子的队伍排出了门外。|咳嗽的第三天。佩尔在哼歌。你没有。|佩尔铺子前的队伍已经有了自己的八卦。}{一个赶车人要樱桃味的。|一个农夫想给他的牛要点药。|一位乔装的教授要点药“给一个朋友”。}\n# en: A crowded village apothecary in winter, a queue of coughing villagers, a student and an old man filling bottles as fast as they can.\nscene [390033] = 冬天里拥挤的村药铺，一队咳嗽的村民，一个学生和一位老人飞快地灌着药瓶。\n# en: Mix to order, carefully\nopt.left.label [101dc9] = 按需调配，仔细些\n# en: {Every bottle mixed for the person in front of you. It's slow, and Pell pays you for every one of them.|You learn sixty coughs by ear. Pell says you're wasted on students.|You mix the disguised professor's remedy \"for a friend\" with great discretion. Pell charges double for the discretion and gives you half.}\nopt.left.say.1 [bb839b] = {每一瓶都为眼前的人单独调配。很慢，佩尔为每一瓶都付了钱。|你听声音就能分辨六十种咳嗽了。佩尔说你当学生真是可惜了。|你极其谨慎地为那位乔装的教授调配了“给朋友”的药。佩尔为这份谨慎收了双倍，分给你一半。}\n# en: Mix in big batches\nopt.right.label [14ba6e] = 大批量调配\n# en: {One cauldron, one recipe, three hundred bottles. It works for nearly everyone. Pell pays you and looks at the cauldron for a long time.|Fast and efficient. A farmer comes back the next day about the cow.|The professor in disguise takes a bottle from the batch and looks offended to be treated like everyone else.}\nopt.right.say.1 [0901fc] = {一口大锅，一个配方，三百瓶。对几乎所有人都管用。佩尔付了钱，盯着那口锅看了很久。|又快又省事。第二天，那个农夫为了牛的事又来了。|那位乔装的教授从大批货里拿了一瓶，被当作普通人对待，一脸不悦。}\n\n@event gig_pell_night_knock\n# en: A Knock After Closing\ntitle [55f4f5] = 打烊后的敲门声\n# en: {Pell asks you over breakfast to sleep above the shop tonight. There's a fever about, and the knocks come after closing.|Another night above the shop, Pell asks. The knocks keep coming after dark.|You're sleeping above the shop again tonight. You've stopped bothering to unlace your boots.} {Pell sleeps like a stone.|The fire goes out at ten.|The ledger says not to open after dark.}\ntext [40913e] = {早饭时佩尔请你今晚睡在铺子楼上。外面在闹热病，打烊后总有人来敲门。|佩尔又请你在铺子楼上过一夜。天黑后敲门声一直没断过。|今晚你又睡在铺子楼上。你已经懒得解鞋带了。}{佩尔睡得像块石头。|炉火十点就灭了。|账簿上写着天黑后不开门。}\n# en: An apothecary's door at night, a lantern held up to a frightened villager in the doorway, a student in a nightcap behind the counter.\nscene [742c58] = 夜里药铺的门口，一盏灯照着门口一个惊慌的村民，柜台后面一个戴睡帽的学生。\n# en: Mix any remedy yourself\nopt.left.label [a8590a] = 有人来就自己调药\n# en: {The knock comes at midnight. A shepherd, his wife burning with fever. You mix the remedy by candlelight from memory. She's better by morning; he pays in eggs and coins, and Pell pays you for the night.|The remedy is right. Pell, in the morning, checks your work and says nothing, which from him is a medal.|You sleep in your boots and mix the remedy before you're properly awake. It's right. Pell finds the mortar warm in the morning, and nods.}\nopt.left.say.1 [656516] = {半夜敲门声响了：一个牧羊人，他妻子烧得滚烫。你凭记忆在烛光下调好了药。到早上她就好多了；他用鸡蛋和硬币付了账，佩尔为这一夜付了你钱。|药是对的。佩尔早上检查了你的成果，什么也没说，这对他来说就是一枚奖章。|你穿着靴子睡，还没完全醒就把药调好了。是对的。佩尔早上发现研钵还是热的，点了点头。}\n# en: {The first batch is wrong. You wake Pell, who fixes it in a minute and pays you anyway, for trying.|You get it right on the third try, at dawn, and you shake all the next day.|You mix it half asleep and half right. The knock comes back an hour later, and Pell comes down.}\nopt.left.say.2 [1e47f9] = {第一批调错了。你叫醒佩尔，他一分钟就改好了，还是为你的尝试付了钱。|你试到第三次，天亮时才调对，第二天一整天都在发抖。|你半睡半醒地调了药，对了一半。一个钟头后敲门声又来了，佩尔下了楼。}\n# en: Wake Pell for anything\nopt.right.label [e9f587] = 有人来就叫醒佩尔\n# en: {When the knock comes, Pell comes down grumbling, mixes it in two minutes and goes back to bed. You learn more watching than mixing.|You wake him. He's not cross. \"That's what I'm for,\" he says.|You wake Pell, who's already pulling his boots on. He's started sleeping in them too.}\nopt.right.say.1 [3e8f55] = {敲门声一响，佩尔就嘟囔着下楼，两分钟调好药，又回去睡了。你看着学到的比自己调学到的还多。|你叫醒了他。他没生气。“这就是我该干的，”他说。|你叫醒佩尔，他已经在穿靴子了。他也开始穿着靴子睡了。}\n\n@event gig_pell_ledger\n# en: Pell's Books\ntitle [b8833a] = 佩尔的账\n# en: {Pell's account books have not been balanced since his daughters left home.|The account books again. Pell has found another drawer of receipts.|You're nearly at the bottom of the receipts. Pell is nearly at the bottom of his patience.} {Half the debts are owed to him.|Half the debts are owed by him.|Some debts are in a currency that no longer exists.}\ntext [9e9f3d] = {自从女儿们离家后，佩尔的账就再没平过。|又是那些账本。佩尔又找到了一抽屉收据。|收据快理到底了。佩尔的耐心也快到底了。}{一半的账是别人欠他的。|一半的账是他欠别人的。|有些账用的是早已不存在的货币。}\n# en: A cluttered apothecary back room, an old man and a student bent over a ledger full of crossings-out, jars all around them.\nscene [982dfe] = 杂乱的药铺后屋，一位老人和一个学生埋头看着一本涂改满页的账簿，四周都是药罐。\n# en: Balance them properly\nopt.left.label [39ae80] = 把账理清\n# en: {Three days of receipts. At the end, Pell is owed more than he thought. He pays you out of it, generously.|You balance every page. Pell signs it as if it were a treaty.|You convert the vanished currency at an old rate you find in a drawer. Pell is owed a goat, a few crowns and an apology. He pays you from the crowns.}\nopt.left.say.1 [abf9c5] = {三天的收据。理完后，别人欠佩尔的比他以为的多。他从中拿出一笔，大方地付给你。|你把每一页都理平了。佩尔签字的样子像在签条约。|你在抽屉里找到一个旧汇率，把那种早已不存在的货币换算了出来。有人欠佩尔一只山羊、几个克朗和一句道歉。他从那几个克朗里付了你。}\n# en: Forgive the small debts\nopt.right.label [48c657] = 免掉小额欠款\n# en: {You strike out every small debt owed to Pell, everything under a crown. Pell reads the list and nods. \"They'd never have paid,\" he says. \"Now they'll come back.\"|Small debts forgiven, the book is simpler and the village is kinder to Pell. The pay is a little less.|You forgive the debts in the vanished currency, since nobody could pay them anyway. Pell laughs for the first time in a week.}\nopt.right.say.1 [38451e] = {你把别人欠佩尔的小额欠款，凡是不到一克朗的，全划掉了。佩尔读了清单，点点头。“反正他们也不会还，”他说，“现在他们会回来了。”|小额欠款免了，账簿简单了，村里人对佩尔也更亲切了。报酬少了一点。|你把那些用已不存在的货币欠下的账都免了，反正也没人还得了。佩尔一星期来头一次笑了。}\n\n@event gig_post_storm_run\n# en: The Storm Run\ntitle [a96ce0] = 风暴航线\n# en: {The holiday post doesn't wait for weather. Tonight's route crosses the ridge in a storm.|The ridge again, and the wind again.|You've flown this route so often the farm dogs know your broom.} {The sack is heavier than the broom likes.|One parcel is labelled \"fragile\" in four languages.|The light at the last farm is always on for you.}\ntext [c327a9] = {节日邮件不等天气。今晚的路线要在风暴中翻过山脊。|又是山脊，又是大风。|这条路线你飞了太多遍，连农场的狗都认得你的飞帚。}{邮袋比飞帚喜欢的要重。|有个包裹用四种语言写着“易碎”。|最后一座农场的灯总是为你亮着。}\n# en: A courier on a broom flying low over snowy fields in a storm, a sack of parcels strapped behind them, a farmhouse light ahead.\nscene [6d3bf8] = 一个信使骑着飞帚在风暴中低低掠过雪地，身后绑着一袋包裹，前方是农舍的灯光。\n# en: Fly straight over the ridge\nopt.left.label [12b64e] = 直接飞越山脊\n# en: {Over the ridge and down to the farms in half the time. The bonus for early delivery is very good.|The wind does its worst and you do better. Every parcel delivered, dry.|You fly the ridge with the farm dogs barking you home. The light at the last farm is on, and the bonus is too.}\nopt.left.say.1 [1a587e] = {翻过山脊，用一半的时间飞到农场。提前送达的奖金非常丰厚。|风使出了浑身解数，你做得更好。每个包裹都送到了，干干的。|你翻过山脊，农场的狗一路叫着送你回家。最后那座农场的灯亮着，奖金也到手了。}\n# en: {The wind takes you into a hedge. The parcels survive. You are less sure about yourself.|You deliver everything, late and frozen, with a sprained wrist.|The storm hides the light at the last farm. You find it by the dogs, an hour late and very wet.}\nopt.left.say.2 [ed2797] = {风把你卷进了树篱。包裹们幸存了下来。你对自己就没那么有把握了。|你把所有东西都送到了，晚了，冻僵了，还扭伤了手腕。|暴风雨遮住了最后那座农场的灯。你循着狗叫声找到了它，晚了一个钟头，浑身湿透。}\n# en: Take the valley road\nopt.right.label [e98ab2] = 走山谷那条路\n# en: {The long way round, low and slow. Late, but every parcel's in one piece, and so are you.|The valley road adds two hours and no danger. The pay is the pay.|You take the valley road. The light at the last farm is still on when you get there, late. Somebody waited.}\nopt.right.say.1 [697a3e] = {绕远路，飞得又低又慢。晚了，但每个包裹都完好无损，你也一样。|山谷那条路多花两个钟头，没有危险。报酬就是报酬。|你走山谷那条路。你迟到时，最后那座农场的灯还亮着。有人一直在等。}\n\n@event gig_post_wrong_parcel\n# en: The Wrong Parcel\ntitle [3a22ae] = 送错的包裹\n# en: {A parcel you delivered yesterday has come back. The old woman says it isn't hers, and it's ticking.|Another wrong address. The holiday post is chaos.|The labels are getting worse. One just says \"Aunt\".} {The ticking is getting faster.|It smells of oranges.|It's addressed to someone who died years ago.}\ntext [b6a56d] = {你昨天送的一个包裹被退回来了。那位老太太说不是她的，而且它在滴答作响。|又一个错地址。节日邮件一团糟。|标签越来越离谱。有一张只写着“姨妈”。}{滴答声越来越快。|它闻起来有橙子味。|收件人好几年前就去世了。}\n# en: A farmhouse doorway in the snow, an old woman holding up a parcel that is clearly ticking, a courier on the step looking worried.\nscene [3247cb] = 雪中农舍的门口，一位老太太举着一个明显在滴答作响的包裹，台阶上的信使一脸担忧。\n# en: Find the right house tonight\nopt.left.label [f52daa] = 今晚就找到对的人家\n# en: {You fly the valley till midnight and find the right house. The ticking was a clock, a present for a grandfather. He gives you a slice of cake and a coin.|You find the owner three farms over. The bonus for \"lost and found\" is surprisingly good.|The person it's addressed to died years ago. You find their granddaughter instead, who opens it in the doorway and laughs and cries at once. The bonus is good.}\nopt.left.say.1 [64cc35] = {你在山谷里飞到半夜，找到了对的人家。滴答声原来是一只钟，送给一位祖父的礼物。他给了你一块蛋糕和一枚硬币。|你在三座农场外找到了主人。“失而复得”的奖金出奇地丰厚。|收件人好多年前就去世了。你转而找到了他们的孙女，她在门口拆开包裹，又笑又哭。奖金很丰厚。}\n# en: Return it to the loft\nopt.right.label [f4d50d] = 退回阁楼\n# en: {You take it back to the loft. Someone else will fly it tomorrow, and get the bonus.|The loft-master sighs and puts it on the shelf of the lost. It ticks there all week.|You take it back. The loft-master reads the name, goes quiet, and says he'll deliver this one himself.}\nopt.right.say.1 [6386e8] = {你把它带回阁楼。明天会有别人送，奖金也归别人。|阁楼主管叹了口气，把它放上失物架。它在那儿滴答了一整个星期。|你把它带了回去。阁楼主管读了那个名字，沉默了，说这一个他亲自去送。}\n\n@event gig_post_last_parcel\n# en: The Last Parcel\ntitle [364d35] = 最后一个包裹\n# en: {The last parcel of the day is small, and addressed in a shaky hand to a cottage on the far moor.|One parcel left again. Always one.|The last parcel, and the last daylight.} {It's light as a feather.|It's warm.|It rattles like a box of buttons.}\ntext [897117] = {今天的最后一个包裹很小，寄往远处荒原上的一座小屋，字迹颤抖。|又只剩一个包裹了。总是剩一个。|最后一个包裹，最后一缕天光。}{它轻得像一根羽毛。|它是温的。|它摇起来像一盒纽扣。}\n# en: An empty courier loft at the end of a holiday shift, one small parcel left on the shelf, addressed in shaky handwriting.\nscene [f9a7c2] = 节日班次结束时空荡荡的信使阁楼，架子上只剩一个小包裹，地址写得歪歪扭扭。\n# en: Fly it out now\nopt.left.label [9bacc3] = 现在就送出去\n# en: {The cottage is an hour into the dark. An old man opens the door and holds the parcel as if it were alive. \"From my son,\" he says. The pay feels like the least of it.|You deliver it at dusk to a woman who cries. You don't ask why. You fly home slowly.|It rattles all the way across the moor. Buttons, the woman says at the door, from her mother's coat. She gives you one.}\nopt.left.say.1 [df55a8] = {那座小屋在一个钟头的夜路之外。一位老人开了门，捧着包裹，好像它是活的。“我儿子寄的，”他说。报酬倒显得最不重要了。|黄昏时你把它交给一个女人，她哭了。你没问为什么。你慢慢地飞回家。|它一路叮当响着穿过荒原。门口的女人说那是纽扣，是她母亲大衣上的。她送了你一颗。}\n# en: Leave it for the morning\nopt.right.label [f26d71] = 留到明天早上\n# en: {It waits on the shelf all night and you think about it all night. You fly it at dawn.|In the morning you deliver it, a day late. Nobody minds but you.|You leave it till morning, last light being gone. It rattles on the shelf all night, as if impatient.}\nopt.right.say.1 [2ad8c9] = {它在架子上等了一整夜，你也想了它一整夜。天一亮你就送去了。|早上你把它送到了，晚了一天。除了你，没人在意。|最后一点天光已经没了，你把它留到了早上。它在架子上叮当响了一整夜，像是等不及了。}\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/pools/plans_more.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/pools/plans_more.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event break_castle_holiday_repairs\n# en: Holiday Repairs\ntitle [d8f001] = 假期修缮\n# en: {The caretaker has a list of jobs that can only be done when the castle is empty, and an eye for idle students.|The caretaker's list is out again. Your name is pencilled in at the bottom, provisionally.|The caretaker hands you the list and a mug of tea, in that order.} {season == \"winter\": Today it's snow on the battlements, heavy enough to worry the roof leads.|Today it's the dormitory mattresses, which must be carried out and beaten in the sun.}\ntext [74f49c] = {看门人有一张只能趁城堡空着时才能干的活儿清单，还有一双专盯闲人的眼睛。|看门人的清单又拿出来了。你的名字用铅笔写在最底下，暂定。|看门人递给你清单和一杯茶，顺序如上。} {season == \"winter\": 今天是城垛上的积雪，重得叫屋顶的铅皮都发愁。|今天是宿舍的床垫，得一张张搬出去，在太阳底下拍打。}\n# en: A caretaker with a long list on a clipboard in an empty castle corridor, a student trying to look busy nearby.\nscene [b4f7a1] = 空荡荡的城堡走廊里，看门人拿着夹板上的一长串清单，旁边一个学生努力装出很忙的样子。\n# en: Lend a hand\nopt.left.label [d5e12c] = 搭把手\n# en: {season == \"winter\": {You shovel snow off the battlements until you can't feel your ears. The view is worth one of them.|You and the caretaker clear a whole parapet in silence. At the end you're handed a biscuit, which is how the caretaker says thank you.|You drink the tea, then shovel the battlements till noon. The caretaker ticks you off the list, which feels like a medal.}|{You beat forty mattresses. Two of them contained socks, one a love letter, and one a very surprised mouse.|You carry mattresses down six flights of moving stairs. One flight moves halfway through, with you and the mattress on it.|Tea first, then mattresses until the sun goes down. The caretaker ticks you off the list with a flourish.}}\nopt.left.say.1 [b3529e] = {season == \"winter\": {你在城垛上铲雪，铲到耳朵没了知觉。这景色值其中一只耳朵。|你和看门人一声不吭地清完了一整段城墙。最后你得到一块饼干，这是看门人说谢谢的方式。|你喝完茶，在城垛上铲雪铲到中午。看门人在清单上把你勾掉了，感觉像得了一枚勋章。}|{你拍了四十张床垫。其中两张里有袜子，一张里有情书，还有一张里有一只非常吃惊的老鼠。|你扛着床垫下了六段会动的楼梯。有一段走到一半动了，连你带床垫一起。|先喝茶，然后拍床垫拍到太阳下山。看门人郑重其事地在清单上把你勾掉了。}}\n# en: Make myself scarce\nopt.right.label [8a06da] = 我先溜了\n# en: {You remember an urgent appointment with a book and vanish round a corner. The caretaker's sigh follows you a long way.|You hide in a window seat until the list has moved on to someone else.|You drink the tea, hand back the list and disappear before the mug is cold.}\nopt.right.say.1 [20a132] = {你想起一本书有急事等你，拐个弯就不见了。看门人的叹气声追了你好远。|你躲在窗台座里，直到清单换了别人。|你喝完茶，把清单还回去，杯子还没凉人就没影了。}\n# en: {The caretaker finds you behind a tapestry and hands you a shovel without comment.|You get as far as the stairs, which choose that moment to move you straight back to the caretaker.|You finish the tea and try to slip off. The caretaker taps the list, and you work down it until supper.}\nopt.right.say.2 [4a8e69] = {看门人在挂毯后面找到了你，一言不发地递来一把铲子。|你刚跑到楼梯口，楼梯就挑了这个时候，把你直接送回看门人跟前。|你喝完茶想溜。看门人敲了敲清单，你照着它一直干到晚饭。}\n\n@event break_castle_flight_field\n# en: The Field to Yourself\ntitle [4a919a] = 独享飞行场\n# en: {With the flying lessons over for the break, the flight field is empty and nobody has locked the broom shed.|The broom shed is unlocked again. You suspect it's on purpose now.|The flight field in the break. You know which broom in the shed pulls left.} {season == \"winter\": The hoops are rimed with frost and the wind has teeth.|The air shimmers with heat, and the wind is lazy for once.} {A crow is using the far hoop as a perch.|Somebody's lost scarf is snagged on the near hoop.|The grass is printed with a fox's tracks.}\ntext [f1561b] = {假期飞行课停了，飞行场空无一人，也没人锁扫帚棚。|扫帚棚又没锁。你现在怀疑是故意的。|假期的飞行场。你知道棚里哪把扫帚往左偏。} {season == \"winter\": 圆环上结着白霜，风像长了牙。|空气热得发颤，风难得懒洋洋的。} {一只乌鸦把远处的圆环当成了栖木。|近处的圆环上挂着不知谁丢的围巾。|草地上印着一串狐狸的脚印。}\n# en: An empty flight field in the holidays, hoops at either end, a single student on a broom high above the grass.\nscene [2e2a95] = 假期里空荡荡的飞行场，两端立着圆环，一个学生骑着扫帚高高掠过草地。\n# en: Fly the hoops, fast\nopt.left.label [91ec70] = 高速穿环\n# en: {You thread every hoop and pull up so late that the grass brushes your boots. Nobody saw. That's almost a shame.|You lap the field until the hoops blur. Landing, you find you're grinning like a first-year.|You take the broom that pulls left and fly the hoops leaning right. Below you, the fox's tracks blur into one long line.}\nopt.left.say.1 [27c099] = {你穿过每一个圆环，拉起得那么晚，靴子都擦到了草尖。没人看见。这几乎有点可惜。|你绕场飞到圆环都模糊了。落地时，你发现自己笑得像个一年级生。|你骑上那把往左偏的扫帚，身子往右压着穿圆环。在你身下，狐狸的脚印模糊成了一条长线。}\n# en: {The wind cheats, as it always does, and you meet the far hoop with your shoulder.|You land in the long grass, which has caught a great many students before you. It does not catch gently.|You forget which broom pulls left until the far hoop reminds you. You land in the fox's tracks, hard.}\nopt.left.say.2 [439eb0] = {风照例耍赖，你的肩膀撞上了远处的圆环。|你落进了长草丛，它接住过许多学生，但从不温柔。|你忘了哪把扫帚往左偏，直到远处的圆环提醒了你。你重重地落在狐狸的脚印上。}\n# en: Practise slow, careful turns\nopt.right.label [5ede57] = 慢慢练转弯\n# en: {Left, right, left, at walking pace, for an hour. It is deeply dull and, annoyingly, it works.|You practise the turn that always throws you until it doesn't. Your arms ache all evening.|You turn against the broom that pulls left until it gives up and flies straight. You land beside the fox's tracks, pleased with you both.}\nopt.right.say.1 [80c865] = {左、右、左，步行的速度，整整一个钟头。无聊透顶，而且，可气的是，真有用。|你一遍遍练那个总把你甩下去的转弯，直到它不再甩你。整个傍晚胳膊都在酸。|你一直跟那把往左偏的扫帚较劲，直到它认输，飞直了。你在狐狸的脚印旁落地，对你们俩都很满意。}\n\n@event break_castle_bored_stairs\n# en: The Bored Staircases\ntitle [1e7930] = 无聊的楼梯\n# en: {With nobody to carry up and down, the castle's staircases have grown restless.|The staircases are bored again, and they've noticed you.|The stairs know you're one of the few left. One swings over the moment you reach the landing.} {season == \"winter\": The stairwell is cold enough to see your breath.|Warm air drifts up the stairwell with the smell of cut grass.} {A flight is drifting past with a lost shoe on its top step.|Two staircases are sulking against the wall.|One flight keeps nudging the landing, like a dog with a stick.}\ntext [0534b0] = {没人可载上载下，城堡的楼梯开始坐不住了。|楼梯又无聊了，而且它们注意到了你。|楼梯知道你是少数留下的人之一。你一走到平台，就有一段荡了过来。} {season == \"winter\": 楼梯井冷得能看见自己的呼吸。|暖风带着刚割过的青草味飘上楼梯井。} {一段楼梯飘过，最上面一级放着一只丢了的鞋。|两段楼梯靠着墙在赌气。|有一段不停地拱着平台，像叼着木棍的狗。}\n# en: A grand castle stairwell with several staircases swinging slowly through the air, a lone student waiting on a landing.\nscene [491257] = 宏伟的城堡楼梯井里，几段楼梯在半空中缓缓摇荡，一个学生独自在平台上等着。\n# en: Ride whichever one comes\nopt.left.label [b32bfb] = 来哪段坐哪段\n# en: {It carries you to a corridor you've never seen, full of portraits who are thrilled to have a visitor.|It takes you the long way, through three towers, and sets you down exactly where you wanted to be.|The eager flight carries you off like a dog with a stick, the long way round, and brings you back very pleased with itself. It was a good ride.}\nopt.left.say.1 [fb796f] = {它把你送到一条你从没见过的走廊，满墙的肖像见有人来，高兴坏了。|它绕了远路，穿过三座塔，最后恰好把你放在你想去的地方。|那段热心的楼梯像叼着树枝的狗一样把你带走，绕了一大圈，又得意扬扬地把你送了回来。这一趟坐得不错。}\n# en: {It drops you on a landing in the east wing and refuses to come back. You walk for an hour.|It swings out over the stairwell and stops. You wait there, cramped and cold, with nothing to read, until it gets bored again.|The eager flight carries you off like a dog with a stick and drops you somewhere it thinks you'll like. You don't. It takes an hour to walk back.}\nopt.left.say.2 [757ffc] = {它把你丢在东翼的一个平台上，死活不肯回来。你走了一个钟头。|它荡到楼梯井半空就停住了。你在那儿干等，又冷又憋屈，手边什么书也没有，直到它又觉得无聊。|那段热心的楼梯像叼着树枝的狗一样把你带走，丢在一个它以为你会喜欢的地方。你并不喜欢。走回来花了一个钟头。}\n# en: Take the long way, on foot\nopt.right.label [a78eb5] = 绕远路，走过去\n# en: {You take the servants' stair, all two hundred steps of it. The staircases watch you go, disappointed.|You walk round by the bridges. It takes all morning, and your legs will remember it.|You walk past the eager flight. It follows you the length of the stairwell, nudging the banister, and you don't give in.}\nopt.right.say.1 [17addc] = {你走仆人楼梯，整整两百级。楼梯们目送你离开，一脸失望。|你从廊桥绕过去，花了一整个上午，腿会记住这一趟的。|你从那段热心的楼梯旁走过。它跟着你走完整个楼梯井，一下下碰着扶手，你没有让步。}\n\n@event break_castle_night_rounds\n# en: The Lone Prefect\ntitle [5e697b] = 孤独的级长\n# en: {Only one prefect has stayed for the break, and at breakfast they sigh that seven towers are a lot to patrol tonight.|The lone prefect is pinning tonight's rounds to the noticeboard again.|You know the lone prefect's rounds by now, to the quarter-hour, and tonight's are on the noticeboard.} {season == \"winter\": The corridors are cold enough to frost the portraits.|The nights are warm, and every window in the castle stands open after dark.} {A staircase shifts somewhere below with a long stone sigh.|An owl has got into the east wing.|Somewhere a door bangs in a draught.}\ntext [f2b041] = {假期只有一位级长留了下来，早饭时级长叹气说，今晚七座塔可不好巡。|那位级长又在布告栏上钉今晚的巡夜路线了。|你现在对那位级长的巡夜路线了如指掌，精确到一刻钟，今晚的路线就贴在布告栏上。} {season == \"winter\": 走廊冷得连肖像都结了霜。|夜里很暖，天黑后城堡的每扇窗都开着。} {楼下某处，一段楼梯长长地叹了口石头气，挪了位置。|一只猫头鹰钻进了东翼。|某处一扇门被穿堂风吹得砰砰响。}\n# en: A single lantern bobbing along a bridge between dark towers at night, a student watching it from a window.\nscene [1d3f26] = 夜里，一盏灯笼沿着两座黑塔之间的廊桥晃动，一个学生在窗边看着它。\n# en: Wander the towers tonight\nopt.left.label [bb3a36] = 今晚在塔里闲逛\n# en: {You have the castle to yourself for an hour. Moonlit bridges, sleeping portraits, the whole lake laid out below.|You slip past the lantern twice and watch the lake from a window nobody uses. It feels like owning the place.|You know the rounds to the quarter-hour, and you're never where the lantern is. The banging door covers your footsteps all the way to the roof.}\nopt.left.say.1 [168eae] = {整整一个钟头，城堡归你一个人：月光下的廊桥，熟睡的肖像，脚下铺开的整片湖。|你两次溜过灯笼，在一扇没人用的窗前看湖。感觉像这地方归你所有。|你对巡夜路线熟到一刻钟不差，灯笼在哪儿你就不在哪儿。那扇砰砰响的门一路替你盖住脚步声，直到屋顶。}\n# en: {The lantern swings round and finds you. The prefect writes your name in the demerit ledger with visible relief at having something to write.|You walk straight into the prefect on a dark landing. You both scream, and then you get a demerit.|The prefect changed the rounds after pinning them up. The lantern is exactly where you are, and a moment later so is your name, in the ledger.}\nopt.left.say.2 [bc0149] = {灯笼一转，照住了你。级长把你的名字记进记过簿，看得出总算有东西可记，松了口气。|你在漆黑的平台上一头撞上级长。你们俩都尖叫了，然后你被记了一过。|级长贴出路线后又改了。灯笼正好照在你身上，片刻之后，你的名字也进了记过簿。}\n# en: Offer to walk the rounds\nopt.right.label [37694d] = 主动陪着巡夜\n# en: {The prefect is glad of the company and tells you which portraits snore. You go to bed very late.|You carry the lantern while the prefect checks doors. Somewhere in the fourth tower you become friends, a little.|You walk the rounds you already know by heart. The prefect is impressed, then suspicious, and you shut the banging door together.}\nopt.right.say.1 [e4999f] = {级长很高兴有人作伴，告诉你哪些肖像会打呼。你很晚才睡。|级长查门，你提着灯笼。走到第四座塔的时候，你们成了朋友，一点点。|你陪着走那条早已烂熟于心的路线。级长先是佩服，然后起了疑心，你们一起关上了那扇砰砰响的门。}\n\n@event break_home_family_recipe\n# en: The Family Recipe\ntitle [3ea562] = 家传菜谱\n# en: {Someone at home has decided you're old enough for the family recipe, and ties an apron on you before breakfast.|The recipe lessons resume. You're trusted with the knife now, if not the salt.|You know the first half of the recipe by heart. The second half is still guarded like a treaty.} {season == \"winter\": It's the stew for the cold months, which takes all day.|It's the summer preserve, and every fruit in the house is involved.} {Nobody measures anything.|A pinch turns out to mean a handful.|The pot is older than you are.}\ntext [a7b002] = {家里有人认定你够大了，可以学家传菜谱了，早饭前就给你系上了围裙。|菜谱课继续。如今刀可以交给你了，盐还不行。|菜谱的前半你已经烂熟于心。后半还像条约一样严防死守。} {season == \"winter\": 是冬天的炖菜，要炖一整天。|是夏天的果酱，家里每一颗果子都得出力。} {谁也不称分量。|“一小撮”原来是一大把。|这口锅比你年纪还大。}\n# en: A warm family kitchen, an older relative guiding a student's hands over a steaming pot, jars and bowls everywhere.\nscene [7fcbc3] = 温暖的家中厨房，一位长辈握着学生的手在冒热气的锅上方指点，四处摆满罐子和碗。\n# en: Write it all down\nopt.left.label [c13862] = 全都记下来\n# en: {You take notes like it's a potions practical. You're corrected on every line, and then on the corrections.|Your notes are thorough, exact and, you're told, missing the point.|You write down the first half. When the second half begins, your notebook is gently confiscated.}\nopt.left.say.1 [9e0334] = {你像上魔药实践课一样做笔记。每一行都被纠正，然后纠正又被纠正。|你的笔记详尽、精确，而且据说完全没抓住要点。|你记下了前一半。后一半刚开始，你的本子就被客客气气地没收了。}\n# en: Learn it by hand, the old way\nopt.right.label [990459] = 用手学，老办法\n# en: {You burn a finger and get a nod. In this kitchen, that's a diploma.|You get it wrong in a way that turns out to be how your grandmother made it. Everyone goes quiet, and then they eat it all.|Your hands learn a step of the second half before anyone means to teach it. Nobody says a word, but you're the one left stirring the old pot.}\nopt.right.say.1 [c60525] = {你烫了一根手指，换来一个点头。在这间厨房里，这就是毕业证书。|你做错了，错法恰好跟你祖母当年一样。大家沉默了一会儿，然后把它吃了个精光。|还没人打算教你，你的手就学会了后一半里的一步。谁也没说什么，可搅那口老锅的人换成了你。}\n\n@event break_home_own_bed\n# en: Your Own Bed\ntitle [cf9821] = 自己的床\n# en: {Your own bed, after a term in the dormitory. You wake at the academy's hour anyway.|The house wakes at dawn. It always has; you'd forgotten.|You know which floorboard creaks on the way down, and that everyone else learned it first.} {season == \"winter\": There's frost on the window and the blankets weigh a ton.|Sunlight is coming through the curtains, and someone is already whistling in the yard.} Downstairs, {the kettle is going.|someone is calling your name.|the door bangs for the third time.}\ntext [290942] = {在宿舍住了一学期，终于睡回自己的床。你还是在学院的钟点醒了。|家里天一亮就醒。一直如此，你只是忘了。|你知道下楼时哪块地板会响，也知道全家早就比你先知道。} {season == \"winter\": 窗上结着霜，被子重得像一吨。|阳光透过窗帘，院子里已经有人在吹口哨。} 楼下，{水壶响了。|有人在喊你的名字。|门第三次砰地关上。}\n# en: A narrow bed under a cottage window in early light, a student buried in blankets while the house bustles below.\nscene [12a0dd] = 清晨微光中，小屋窗下一张窄床，一个学生埋在被子里，楼下一片忙碌。\n# en: Sleep until noon\nopt.left.label [7405ea] = 睡到中午\n# en: {You sleep like a stone. At lunch you're called \"the scholar\" with a new and pointed emphasis.|You wake at noon, rested and ashamed. The whole house has done a morning's work, and mentions it.|You sleep through the door banging a third time. When you finally come down, you miss the creaky floorboard, and nobody hears how late you are.}\nopt.left.say.1 [29956b] = {你睡得像块石头。午饭时，大家喊你“学者”，带着一种新鲜而意味深长的重音。|你中午醒来，神清气爽又满心惭愧。全家已经干完一上午的活，还特意提了提。|门第三次砰地响，你还在睡。等你终于下楼，你躲开了那块会响的地板，谁也没听出你起得有多晚。}\n# en: Get up with everyone\nopt.right.label [b1c969] = 跟大家一起起床\n# en: {season == \"winter\": {You break the ice on the water butt and carry wood in before breakfast. Your hands sting all morning.|You're out in the dark with a lantern before the cockerel. The cockerel resents the competition.|You step over the creaking floorboard and are at the woodpile before anyone knows you're up. Your fingers sting till noon.}|{You're in the yard by sunrise, hauling and fetching. Breakfast, when it comes, is the best of the year.|You work the cool hours with everyone else and see why they swear by them.|You step over the creaking floorboard and are in the yard before the door bangs again. By breakfast the work is half done.}}\nopt.right.say.1 [f756ab] = {season == \"winter\": {早饭前你敲开水桶上的冰，把柴搬进屋。整个上午手都在刺痛。|公鸡还没叫，你就提着灯笼出门了。公鸡对这个竞争者很不满。|你跨过那块会响的地板，还没人知道你起了，你已经在柴堆边了。手指一直刺痛到中午。}|{日出时你已经在院子里搬这搬那。早饭端上来时，是一年里最香的一顿。|你跟大家一起趁凉干活，总算明白他们为什么对这几个钟头赞不绝口。|你跨过那块会响的地板，门还没再响，你已经在院子里了。到早饭时，活已经干完一半。}}\n\n@event break_home_mending_chair\n# en: The Mending Chair\ntitle [56e7dd] = 待修的椅子\n# en: {Word goes round the house that the scholar can fix things.|The things that need fixing have been waiting for you on a chair by the door.|The mending chair fills up between breaks, like a well.} {season == \"winter\": Today it's a stove door that won't stay shut.|Today it's a window that sticks in the heat.} {Also a clock that strikes thirteen.|Also a doll with a missing eye.|Also a teapot nobody admits to breaking.}\ntext [1709e9] = {家里传开了：学者会修东西。|要修的东西已经在门边的椅子上等着你了。|这把待修的椅子在两次假期之间就会堆满，像一口井。} {season == \"winter\": 今天是一扇关不严的炉门。|今天是一扇热天卡住的窗户。} {还有一只会敲十三下的钟。|还有一个少了一只眼睛的布娃娃。|还有一把没人承认打碎的茶壶。}\n# en: A chair by a cottage door piled with broken household things, a student in academy robes eyeing them with a wand.\nscene [691b03] = 小屋门边一把椅子上堆满了坏掉的家什，一个穿学院长袍的学生拿着魔杖打量它们。\n# en: Mend it with a spell\nopt.left.label [16d363] = 用咒语修好\n# en: {It mends with a click and a little puff of light. The family applauds, then brings out three more things.|You fix it so well that it works better than new. Now everyone wants their things to work better than new.|The teapot mends without a crack showing. Somebody looks very relieved, and the chair fills up again by supper.}\nopt.left.say.1 [f638e0] = {咔哒一声，冒出一小团光，修好了。全家鼓掌，然后又搬出三件来。|你修得比新的还好用。现在人人都想让自己的东西比新的还好用。|茶壶修好了，一道裂纹也看不出。有人明显松了一口气，到晚饭时椅子上又堆满了。}\n# en: {It's mended, technically. It also hums now, and nobody can make it stop.|The spell fixes it and then, for good measure, everything else on the chair. Most of it did not need fixing that way.|The teapot mends, and now pours only for whoever broke it. Nobody will pick it up.}\nopt.left.say.2 [760c25] = {严格来说，修好了。只是它现在会嗡嗡响，谁也停不下来。|咒语修好了它，顺带把椅子上别的东西也一并修了。大部分并不需要那样修。|茶壶修好了，可现在它只给打碎它的人倒茶。谁也不肯去拿它。}\n# en: Mend it by hand\nopt.right.label [2c35c0] = 动手修\n# en: {Glue, string, a borrowed hammer and the whole morning. It holds. You don't trust it, but it holds.|You fix it the way your family always has: slowly, with a lot of opinions from the doorway.|You glue the teapot together crack by crack. Nobody admits to anything, but someone brings you tea in it.}\nopt.right.say.1 [0c31d9] = {胶水、绳子、借来的锤子，外加一整个上午。它撑住了。你不太信它，可它撑住了。|你照家里一贯的法子修：慢慢来，门口一堆人发表意见。|你一道裂纹一道裂纹地把茶壶粘好。谁也没承认什么，可有人用它给你端来了茶。}\n\n@event break_home_the_trunk\n# en: The Trunk in the Corner\ntitle [bc6453] = 角落里的箱子\n# en: {Your trunk stands open in the corner, and the family keeps putting things in it.|The trunk is open again, and it's filling up again.|The trunk is a family project now.} {season == \"winter\": Woollen socks, a jar of something pickled, a cake wrapped in three cloths.|Dried fruit, a straw hat nobody asked for, a cake wrapped in three cloths.} {final_year: Someone says \"for next term\", then stops.|{The lid won't close.|Someone sits on the lid.|A note is tucked into a sock, not to be read till later.}}\ntext [82fec8] = {你的箱子敞在角落里，家里人不停地往里塞东西。|箱子又敞开了，又满起来了。|这只箱子如今是全家的工程。} {season == \"winter\": 羊毛袜、一罐不知腌了什么的东西、一块裹了三层布的蛋糕。|果干、一顶没人要的草帽、一块裹了三层布的蛋糕。} {final_year: 有人说“下学期带着”，然后停住了。|{盖子合不上。|有人坐到了盖子上。|一只袜子里塞着一张纸条，说好以后再看。}}\n# en: An open travelling trunk in the corner of a small bedroom, overflowing with jars, socks and parcels in cloth.\nscene [cc7e9b] = 小卧室角落里一只敞开的旅行箱，罐子、袜子和布包的东西满得溢了出来。\n# en: Take everything\nopt.left.label [054bdb] = 全都带上\n# en: {The bargeman charges you double for the weight, but you eat well for a month.|You pay the barge extra for the weight and haul it all back across the lake. Half the corridor smells of pickles until spring.|You take everything. The bargeman charges for the weight, and somewhere in the third cloth you find a note from home.}\nopt.left.say.1 [fdcb36] = {箱子太沉，船夫收了你双倍船钱，可你一个月都吃得很好。|你为超重多付了船钱，把这些全拖过了湖。半条走廊一直到春天都是腌菜味。|你全都带上了。船夫按重量收了钱，在第三层布里，你找到了一张家里写的字条。}\n# en: Sneak half of it back\nopt.right.label [4040fd] = 偷偷放回一半\n# en: {You smuggle the jars back into the pantry at night. In the morning they're in the trunk again, wrapped tighter.|You leave the cake on a shelf by the door. You feel worse about it than you expected.|You manage to leave one jar behind. In its place, someone has tucked a note.}\nopt.right.say.1 [1c5798] = {你夜里把罐子偷偷送回储藏室。第二天早上，它们又回到了箱子里，裹得更紧了。|你把蛋糕留在门边的架子上。你比自己预想的更过意不去。|你总算留下了一个罐子。在它原来的位置上，有人塞了一张字条。}\n\n@event break_work_stocktake\n# en: Stocktaking\ntitle [8bb9d1] = 盘点库存\n# en: {The quiet days of the break are, says whoever is in charge, the time to count everything.|Stocktaking again. You have opinions about how to count things now.|Stocktaking. You count faster than your boss, and slower than you'd like.} {has(gig_newt): At the Gilded Newt that means every tankard, keg and spoon.|{has(gig_pell): At Pell & Daughters that means a thousand drawers.|{has(gig_post): At the loft that means every parcel, pigeonhole and broom.|{has(gig_library): In the library that means books that won't hold still to be counted.|{has(gig_kitchens): In the kitchens that means every ladle, pot and sack of flour.|{has(gig_grounds): In the sheds that means every trowel, rake and seed packet.|That means everything, twice.}}}}}} The tally comes out one short.\ntext [a3cf2a] = {管事的人说，假期的清闲日子正适合把东西全数一遍。|又盘点了。如今你对怎么数东西都有自己的见解了。|盘点。你数得比上头快，比你自己希望的慢。} {has(gig_newt): 在镀金蝾螈酒馆，这意味着每一只酒杯、每一桶酒、每一把勺子。|{has(gig_pell): 在佩尔父女药铺，这意味着一千个抽屉。|{has(gig_post): 在阁楼，这意味着每一个包裹、每一个信格、每一把扫帚。|{has(gig_library): 在图书馆，这意味着那些不肯老老实实让人数的书。|{has(gig_kitchens): 在后厨，这意味着每一把长柄勺、每一口锅、每一袋面粉。|{has(gig_grounds): 在工具棚，这意味着每一把小铲、每一把耙子、每一包种子。|这意味着所有东西，数两遍。}}}}}} 总数差了一个。\n# en: A student with a ledger and pencil counting rows of stock in a quiet workplace, the owner reading over their shoulder.\nscene [199b94] = 安静的工作场所里，一个学生拿着账本和铅笔清点一排排货物，老板从肩后看着。\n# en: Track down the missing one\nopt.left.label [855fc3] = 找出少的那一个\n# en: {You find it wedged behind the counter, where it has been since the last stocktake. You're tipped for your thoroughness.|You find the mistake in the ledger itself: someone counted a box as a crate. You're paid a little extra, and nothing else is said.|You count faster than your boss and find it first, under a sack nobody has moved in years. You're tipped, a little grudgingly.}\nopt.left.say.1 [a484db] = {你在柜台后面找到了它，它从上次盘点起就卡在那儿了。你因为细心得了点赏钱。|你发现错在账本本身：有人把一箱记成了一桶。你多拿了一点工钱，别的谁也没提。|你数得比上头快，先找到了它，就在一个好几年没人挪过的麻袋底下。你得了点赏钱，给得有点不情愿。}\n# en: {You search until dusk, on your knees in the dust. It stays missing, and your boss looks at you as if you might have eaten it.|You count everything again and get a different number. Then a third.|You count faster than your boss, and wrong. The missing one stays missing, and now so does another.}\nopt.left.say.2 [876a80] = {你跪在灰里找到天黑。它还是不见踪影，上头看你的眼神，好像是你把它吃了。|你又全数了一遍，得出另一个数。然后是第三个。|你数得比上头快，也数错了。少的那件还是没找到，现在又少了一件。}\n# en: Write it off and move on\nopt.right.label [71a437] = 记作损耗了事\n# en: {\"Breakage,\" you write. A coin is docked from your pay, on principle.|You write it off, and your boss sighs and takes it from your wages.|You write it off before your boss has finished counting. It comes out of your wages all the same.}\nopt.right.say.1 [042fa5] = {你写下“损耗”。你的工钱照规矩被扣了一枚。|你把它记作损耗，上头叹口气，从你工钱里扣了。|上头还没数完，你就把它记成了损耗。照样从你工钱里扣。}\n\n@event break_work_complaint\n# en: The Complaint\ntitle [dd0cf5] = 投诉\n# en: {Someone has come back with a complaint and a voice built for it.|Another complaint, another raised voice. You've learned to breathe out first.|This one has been saving the complaint all week. You can tell.} {has(gig_newt): The cider was too warm, or too cold, or too cider.|{has(gig_pell): The tonic did nothing, which is worse, they say, than doing something.|{has(gig_post): The parcel arrived wet, late and somehow smug.|{has(gig_library): The book they want is out, and they want it in.|{has(gig_kitchens): The stew had a button in it, and they want to know whose.|{has(gig_grounds): A visitor's boots are ruined, and the mud on the path is apparently your mud.|The complaint has several parts, all of them loud.}}}}}} Whoever's in charge is out.\ntext [b04d1c] = {有人带着投诉回来了，嗓门天生就是为投诉准备的。|又一桩投诉，又一个大嗓门。你已经学会先呼一口气。|这位攒了一整个星期的投诉。你看得出来。} {has(gig_newt): 苹果酒太热，或者太冷，或者太苹果酒了。|{has(gig_pell): 补药毫无效果，他们说，这比有点效果还糟。|{has(gig_post): 包裹送到时又湿又迟，还不知怎么透着一股得意。|{has(gig_library): 他们要的书借出去了，他们要它现在就回来。|{has(gig_kitchens): 炖菜里有颗纽扣，他们想知道是谁的。|{has(gig_grounds): 一位访客的靴子毁了，小路上的泥据说是你的泥。|投诉分好几部分，每一部分都很响。}}}}}} 管事的人不在。\n# en: An irate customer leaning over a counter, finger raised, while a young worker stands very still behind it.\nscene [eec2a5] = 一位气冲冲的客人俯身在柜台上竖着手指，柜台后的年轻伙计一动不动。\n# en: Talk them down\nopt.left.label [dfea51] = 好言安抚\n# en: {You agree with everything until there's nothing left to agree with. They leave calm, and they leave a tip.|You listen, nod and offer them a chair. By the end they're telling you about their sister.|You let them say a week's worth of complaint without interrupting once. By the end they're almost apologetic, and they tip.}\nopt.left.say.1 [f6fb1a] = {你什么都附和，直到再没什么可附和。对方心平气和地走了，还留了小费。|你倾听、点头，请对方坐下。说到最后，对方在跟你讲他们的姐妹。|你让对方把攒了一星期的抱怨说完，一次都没打断。说到最后对方几乎有点过意不去，还给了小费。}\n# en: {It gets louder, and then it leaves, still loud, to tell everyone in the valley about you.|They demand your boss, then you, then your boss again, and you spend the rest of the day hearing about it.|A week's worth of complaint, and you interrupt it once. That's once too many.}\nopt.left.say.2 [59f1d5] = {声音越来越大，然后对方走了，一路还在嚷，要让整个山谷都知道你。|对方要找你上头，然后找你，然后又找你上头，你剩下的一整天都在听这件事。|攒了一星期的抱怨，你打断了一次。就这一次，也多了。}\n# en: Give them what they want\nopt.right.label [876ac8] = 给他们想要的\n# en: {You hand over a refund out of your wages. They leave. The quiet afterwards is worth a coin.|A free one, on you. They take it without thanks, and the next one is lovely.|You give in before they're halfway through a week's worth of complaint. They seem almost disappointed.}\nopt.right.say.1 [a83120] = {你从工钱里退了款。对方走了。之后的清静值一枚钱。|这份算你请。对方连谢都没说就拿走了，下一位倒是可爱极了。|攒了一星期的抱怨，对方还没说到一半你就让步了。对方看上去几乎有点失望。}\n\n@event break_work_half_hour\n# en: Half an Hour Off\ntitle [647a16] = 半个钟头的休息\n# en: {Half an hour off in the middle of a long holiday shift.|Your half hour again. You know exactly how far it goes now.|Half an hour. You spend the first minute deciding.} {season == \"winter\": Outside, the snow is coming down sideways.|Outside, the sun is bright enough to hurt.} {The back room has one chair and one draught.|A colleague has left half a pie, possibly for sharing.|A clock somewhere strikes the wrong hour.}\ntext [8b3489] = {漫长的假期班次中间，有半个钟头休息。|又是你的半个钟头。如今你清楚它能走多远。|半个钟头。头一分钟用来做决定。} {season == \"winter\": 外面，雪横着飘。|外面，太阳亮得刺眼。} {后屋有一把椅子和一股穿堂风。|同事留下半个馅饼，也许是给大家分的。|某处的钟敲错了钟点。}\n# en: A tired student in a work apron standing in a back doorway, looking out at the weather with a cup in hand.\nscene [defd5a] = 一个系着围裙的疲惫学生站在后门口，手里捧着杯子，望着外面的天气。\n# en: Walk out into the weather\nopt.left.label [e0bf5d] = 出门走走\n# en: {season == \"winter\": {You buy roast chestnuts and eat them walking, snow in your collar. You come back soaked and much happier.|You buy a hot pie for the walk to the lake's edge and back. Your nose will not forgive you.|You spend a minute deciding and the rest in the snow, to the end of the lane and back. A clock strikes the wrong hour as you come in.}|{You buy a cold drink and sit in the sun until your neck burns. Worth it.|You buy a lemonade and walk to the water and stand there. Half an hour is longer outside.|You spend a minute deciding and the rest in the sun. A clock strikes the wrong hour, and you nearly believe it.}}\nopt.left.say.1 [d8c737] = {season == \"winter\": {你买了烤栗子边走边吃，雪灌进领口。回来时湿透了，心情却好多了。|你买了个热馅饼，边吃边走到湖边又走回来。你的鼻子不会原谅你。|你花一分钟拿主意，剩下的时间都在雪里，走到巷子尽头又走回来。你进门时，一座钟敲错了点。}|{你买了杯冷饮，坐在太阳底下晒到脖子发红。值。|你买了杯柠檬水，走到水边站着。半个钟头在外面显得更长。|你花一分钟拿主意，剩下的时间都在太阳底下。一座钟敲错了点，你差点就信了。}}\n# en: Nap in the back room\nopt.right.label [a2e9fa] = 在后屋打个盹\n# en: {You sleep sitting up and wake with the shape of a crate on your cheek.|You doze off and dream of work, which seems unfair. The bell wakes you mid-shift in the dream.|You spend a minute deciding, and wake to a clock striking the wrong hour. You're late, or early. Nobody can say.}\nopt.right.say.1 [3b3011] = {你坐着睡着了，醒来脸上印着木箱的形状。|你打了个盹，梦见在干活，这似乎不太公平。梦里干到一半，铃声把你叫醒了。|你花一分钟拿主意，然后被一座敲错点的钟吵醒。你迟到了，或者早到了。谁也说不准。}\n\n@event break_work_short_wages\n# en: A Coin Short\ntitle [996363] = 少了一枚\n# en: {Wages, counted out into your hand. You count them again.|Wage day. You count your coins at the counter, in front of everyone, on principle.|Wage day. Your boss counts slowly, and so do you.} {It's short by one.|It's short, and there's a button where a coin should be.|It's short by just enough to notice.} {has(gig_newt): The landlady remembers every tab to the penny, and has somehow forgotten this one.|{has(gig_pell): Old Pell counted it himself, drop by drop.|{has(gig_post): The dispatch ledger says otherwise, in smudged ink.|{has(gig_library): The pay envelope says otherwise, in very small writing.|{has(gig_kitchens): The head cook's tally is written in gravy.|{has(gig_grounds): Maud's pay book says otherwise, in pencil.|Someone's sums say otherwise.}}}}}}\ntext [755880] = {工钱数到你手里。你又数了一遍。|发工钱的日子。你现在当众在柜台上数钱，这是原则。|发工钱的日子。上头数得慢，你也数得慢。} {少了一枚。|少了，本该是钱的地方放着一颗纽扣。|少的刚好够你察觉。} {has(gig_newt): 老板娘记得每一笔赊账，分毫不差，偏偏把这一笔忘了。|{has(gig_pell): 是老佩尔亲手数的，一滴一滴地数。|{has(gig_post): 派送账簿上可不是这么写的，墨迹还糊了。|{has(gig_library): 工资袋上可不是这么写的，字小得很。|{has(gig_kitchens): 大厨的账是用肉汁写的。|{has(gig_grounds): 莫德的工钱簿上可不是这么写的，用的是铅笔。|有人的算术可不是这么算的。}}}}}}\n# en: A student counting a small pile of coins on a counter for the second time, frowning, an employer pretending not to watch.\nscene [5dacbb] = 一个学生在柜台上第二次清点一小堆硬币，皱着眉，雇主假装没在看。\n# en: Point it out\nopt.left.label [d2676e] = 指出来\n# en: {You show your sums. Your boss checks them, coughs, and pays you the difference with a little extra for the embarrassment.|You point it out politely and firmly. The missing coin appears, with an apology and a pastry.|You count as slowly as your boss did, aloud, coin by coin. The difference is paid before you reach the end.}\nopt.left.say.1 [9644e9] = {你把算式摆出来。上头核对一遍，咳了一声，补上差额，还为这份尴尬多给了一点。|你礼貌而坚定地指出来。少的那枚出现了，附带一句道歉和一块点心。|你像上头一样慢，一枚一枚大声数。还没数完，差额就补上了。}\n# en: {Your boss recounts, gets the same wrong answer, and looks hurt that you asked.|You're told it's for the breakage. You don't remember any breakage. Nobody else does either.|You count aloud, slowly. Your boss counts back, slower, and reaches a different number. You give up before supper.}\nopt.left.say.2 [49fbf8] = {上头重数一遍，得出同样的错数，还一脸受伤，怪你多问。|你被告知这是赔损耗的。你不记得有什么损耗。别人也不记得。|你慢慢地大声数。上头更慢地数回来，得出另一个数。晚饭前你放弃了。}\n# en: Let it go this once\nopt.right.label [b87d7e] = 这次就算了\n# en: {You pocket it without a word. Your boss sees you notice, and you're given the easy shift next week.|One coin. You decide you can afford to be the bigger person, just about.|Short by just enough to notice, and you notice, and say nothing. Your boss counts faster next time.}\nopt.right.say.1 [e3a0a0] = {你一声不吭地收下了。上头看见你注意到了，下周给你排了轻松的班。|一枚而已。你决定自己大度得起，勉强吧。|少得刚好能察觉，你察觉了，什么也没说。下一回，上头数得快多了。}\n\n@event refectory_cooks_taster\n# en: The Cook's Taster\ntitle [64d79d] = 厨娘的试吃员\n# en: The cook stops you before you reach the bench and holds out a spoon. \"You're in here more than the cats,\" she says. \"So you can taste the new pot first. Regulars do.\" Behind her a dozen steaming pots wait their turn, and the other students in the queue look on with naked envy.\ntext [6626d7] = 你还没走到长凳，厨娘就拦住你，递过来一把勺子。“你来得比猫还勤，”她说，“所以新的一锅你先尝。常客都这样。”她身后十几口锅冒着热气等着轮到，排队的学生们毫不掩饰地眼红。\n# en: A refectory cook holding out a small spoon over the counter to a student, a new pot steaming behind her.\nscene [2e4b9e] = 膳堂厨娘隔着柜台递给学生一把小勺，身后一口新锅冒着热气。\n# en: Give an honest verdict\nopt.left.label [5b70db] = 说实话\n# en: You say it wants salt and less of the grey thing. The cook frowns, tastes, and nods. From then on your bowl comes from the bottom of the pot, where the good bits are.\nopt.left.say.1 [e40fb3] = 你说得多放盐，少放那灰乎乎的东西。厨娘皱眉尝了尝，点了点头。从此你的那碗都从锅底盛，好料都在那儿。\n# en: Praise it to the rafters\nopt.right.label [7c4191] = 夸上天去\n# en: You declare it the best soup in the kingdom. The cook doesn't believe a word of it, and gives you a second spoonful anyway.\nopt.right.say.1 [c29b20] = 你宣布这是王国里最好喝的汤。厨娘一个字也不信，还是又给了你一勺。\n\n@event library_footnote_bag\n# en: Footnote's Approval\ntitle [c453a2] = 脚注的认可\n# en: You come back from the shelves to find Footnote asleep on your bag, curled tight, one paw over the strap. Footnote sleeps on the reference desk, as a rule, and almost never on anyone's belongings; everybody knows that. A passing librarian stops, looks from the cat to you, and nods, as if something official has happened.\ntext [c26442] = 你从书架间回来，发现脚注睡在你的书包上，蜷得紧紧的，一只爪子搭着背带。人人都知道，脚注照例只睡参考台，几乎从不睡在别人的东西上。一位路过的图书管理员停下来，看看猫又看看你，点了点头，仿佛刚发生了什么正式的事。\n# en: A huge old library cat curled asleep on a student's satchel under a reading desk, a student standing over it, uncertain.\nscene [515a63] = 阅览桌下，一只硕大的老图书馆猫蜷在学生的书包上睡觉，学生站在一旁不知所措。\n# en: Wait for him to wake\nopt.left.label [03f336] = 等他睡醒\n# en: You read standing up until the lamps are lit. Footnote wakes, stretches, and walks off without a glance. Your bag smells of warm cat for a week, and you find you don't mind.\nopt.left.say.1 [5ee19a] = 你站着看书，一直看到点灯。脚注醒了，伸个懒腰，看都不看你就走了。你的书包一个星期都带着暖烘烘的猫味，而你发现自己并不介意。\n# en: Ease the bag out, inch by inch\nopt.right.label [e23815] = 一寸寸抽出书包\n# en: You slide it free a fraction at a time. Footnote opens one eye, judges you, and allows it. The librarian says she's never seen him allow anything.\nopt.right.say.1 [2fedfc] = 你一点一点把书包抽出来。脚注睁开一只眼，审视了你一番，准了。图书管理员说她从没见他准过任何事。\n\n@event mabels_your_usual\n# en: Your Usual\ntitle [c3563c] = 老样子\n# en: You haven't even reached the counter. Mabel already has your cup out, the right tea steeping and the cake you always end up choosing on a plate. \"Your usual,\" she says, and it isn't a question. On the slate behind the till, among the regulars, there's a new line in chalk.\ntext [05c137] = 你还没走到柜台，梅布尔已经摆好了你的杯子，对的茶正在泡，盘子里放着你每次最后都会挑的那块蛋糕。“老样子，”她说，不是在问。收银台后的石板上，常客名单里多了一行新粉笔字。\n# en: A small tea shop counter with a cup already poured and a slice of cake on a plate, the shopkeeper smiling at a student in the doorway.\nscene [a81742] = 小茶馆的柜台上已经倒好一杯茶、放着一块蛋糕，店主朝门口的学生微笑。\n# en: Have the usual\nopt.left.label [de70ee] = 就要老样子\n# en: It's exactly right, as it always is. Mabel watches you take the first sip and turns back to the shelves, satisfied.\nopt.left.say.1 [ba0c49] = 恰到好处，一如既往。梅布尔看着你喝下第一口，心满意足地转回货架。\n# en: Ask for something new\nopt.right.label [35d8bb] = 来点新的\n# en: Mabel's eyebrows rise. Then she smiles like someone handed a puzzle and reaches for a tin from the very top shelf. It's odd and smoky, and you don't like it, and you're glad you asked.\nopt.right.say.1 [09aadb] = 梅布尔挑起眉毛。接着她笑得像拿到了一道谜题，伸手去够最顶层架子上的一个茶罐。那茶古怪又带烟味，你不喜欢，但你很高兴自己问了。\n\n@event bakery_bun_set_aside\n# en: The Bun Set Aside\ntitle [795ce5] = 留给你的面包\n# en: The baker's son reaches under the counter before you've said a word and brings out a bun wrapped in paper, with your initial on it in flour. \"Saved from the first batch,\" he says. \"Regulars get the corner ones.\" Behind him, by the ovens, the oldest baker nods as if she arranged it all herself.\ntext [09ad6a] = 你还没开口，面包师的儿子就从柜台下拿出一个纸包的面包，纸上用面粉写着你名字的首字母。“头一炉留的，”他说，“常客拿边角的。”他身后的炉边，最年长的面包师点点头，好像这一切都是她安排的。\n# en: A baker's son handing a paper-wrapped bun across a flour-dusted counter, a letter written on the paper in flour.\nscene [e72281] = 面包师的儿子隔着撒满面粉的柜台递过一个纸包面包，纸上用面粉写着一个字母。\n# en: Insist on paying for it\nopt.left.label [5b7a00] = 坚持付钱\n# en: He refuses twice and accepts the third time. The coin goes in a jar by the till marked, in the oldest baker's hand, \"for the post\".\nopt.left.say.1 [d333cd] = 他推辞了两次，第三次收下了。那枚钱进了收银台边的一个罐子，罐上是最年长的面包师的笔迹：“寄信用”。\n# en: Thank him and eat it warm\nopt.right.label [8942f0] = 道谢，趁热吃\n# en: It's still warm and far too big, and you eat it on the step in four bites and burn your mouth. You'd do it again tomorrow.\nopt.right.say.1 [aea84b] = 面包还热着，大得离谱，你坐在台阶上四口吃完，烫了嘴。明天你还会这么干。\n\n@event grimsbys_right_book\n# en: The Right Book, First Time\ntitle [38138c] = 一次就拿对的书\n# en: You step into Grimsby's and open your mouth, and Mr Grimsby is already holding out a book. It is exactly the one you came for. He looks as astonished as you are. \"Must be getting to know you,\" he says, and then, worried, \"Don't tell anyone.\"\ntext [c259e5] = 你走进格林斯比书店，刚张开嘴，格林斯比先生已经把一本书递了过来。正是你要找的那本。他和你一样惊讶。“大概是开始了解你了，”他说，又担心地补了一句，“别告诉别人。”\n# en: A cramped bookshop of leaning shelves, an elderly bookseller holding out a book to a student who has only just stepped inside.\nscene [83e433] = 书架东倒西歪的狭小书店里，一位年迈的书商把一本书递给刚进门的学生。\n# en: Buy it before it changes\nopt.left.label [8c5f21] = 趁它没变赶紧买\n# en: You pay at once, in case it turns into a book on beekeeping. It doesn't. You carry it all the way back to the castle, checking.\nopt.left.say.1 [e0afe4] = 你立刻付钱，以防它变成一本养蜂的书。它没变。你一路把它抱回城堡，边走边检查。\n# en: Ask what else he'd pick for me\nopt.right.label [4293db] = 问他还会给我挑啥\n# en: Restored to form, Mr Grimsby brings you a history of eels and a book of riddles, and lets you read the riddles in the window seat until closing. They're very good, and no help at all.\nopt.right.say.1 [89473a] = 格林斯比先生恢复了常态，给你拿来一本鳗鱼史和一本谜语书，还让你坐在窗边的座位上读谜语，一直读到打烊。谜语很好，一点用也没有。\n\n@event courtyard_in_the_know\n# en: In the Know\ntitle [143948] = 消息灵通\n# en: The knot of students by the fountain goes quiet as you pass, and then one of them waves you over. \"You're out here every day,\" says a second-year from Duskgate. \"So. What have you heard?\" It seems you've been counted among the courtyard's regulars, and regulars are expected to bring news.\ntext [f4ab24] = 你经过喷泉时，那一圈学生安静下来，接着其中一个朝你招手。“你天天在这儿，”一个暮门的二年级生说，“那么，你听到什么了？”看来你已经被算作庭院的常客了，而常客是要带消息来的。\n# en: A knot of students gathered on the rim of a courtyard fountain, leaning in, one waving a newcomer over.\nscene [ed9aa8] = 一群学生聚在庭院喷泉边沿，凑头说话，其中一人朝新来的人招手。\n# en: Share something harmless\nopt.left.label [743b67] = 说点无伤大雅的\n# en: You offer up a rumour about the refectory pudding. It's received with grave interest and is all over the castle by supper, much improved.\nopt.left.say.1 [04ca03] = 你贡献了一条关于膳堂布丁的传闻。大家郑重其事地听了，到晚饭时它已传遍城堡，还被添油加醋了不少。\n# en: Say I've heard nothing\nopt.right.label [4cfbdc] = 说我啥也没听说\n# en: They take your silence as proof that you know everything. By the end of the week, people are asking you for the news.\nopt.right.say.1 [60a72f] = 他们把你的沉默当成你什么都知道的证据。不到一个星期，就有人来找你打听消息了。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/pools/plans_thesis.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/pools/plans_thesis.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event break_castle_one_fire\n# en: The One Lit Fire\ntitle [c9e9bf] = 唯一一炉火\n# en: {With the houses empty, the stewards light one common room and let the rest go dark.|Someone has brought a kettle, and now there's a rota for it.|The break common room. You know everyone's blanket by now.} {season == \"winter\": The best armchair is right by the fire.|The best armchair is by the only breeze.} {A first-year from Stillwater is asleep in it.|Footnote is in it. Footnote moves for no one.|The steward's dog is in it, and the steward is on the dog's side.}\ntext [a49c22] = {宿舍楼都空了，管事们只点亮一间公共休息室，其余的任它暗着。|有人带来了水壶，现在还排了轮值表。|假期的公共休息室。现在每个人的毯子你都认得了。} {season == \"winter\": 最好的扶手椅就在炉火旁。|最好的扶手椅就在唯一有风的窗边。} {一个静水院的一年级生在椅子上睡着了。|脚注占着那把椅子。脚注不给任何人让座。|管事的狗占着它，而管事站在狗那边。}\n# en: A cavernous common room in the holidays, a handful of students in blankets crowded around {season == \"winter\": one roaring hearth|one open window}.\nscene [24c42d] = 假期里一间空旷的公共休息室，几个裹着毯子的学生挤在{season == \"winter\": 一炉旺火|一扇敞开的窗}旁。\n# en: Talk my way into the armchair\nopt.left.label [483910] = 把扶手椅骗到手\n# en: {You mention that the kitchens are giving out free toffee. The chair empties. You feel only slightly bad.|You announce, with great authority, that the armchair is due to be re-stuffed. It is, now, by you.|You offer the dog your toast. It's a fair trade, and the chair is still warm.}\nopt.left.say.1 [dd333e] = {你随口提到厨房在免费发太妃糖。椅子空了。你只觉得有一点点愧疚。|你郑重宣布这把扶手椅要重新填絮了。现在它确实被填上了——用你。|你把吐司让给了那条狗。很公平的交易，椅子还是热的。}\n# en: {Nobody moves. Someone says, \"Nice try,\" without looking up from their book.|The whole room watches you fail to dislodge the armchair's occupant. It's the most entertainment anyone has had all break.|The dog growls, the steward sides with the dog, and you take the floor.}\nopt.left.say.2 [6998ca] = {没人动。有人头也不抬地说：“想得美。”|全屋的人看着你没能把扶手椅上的那位挪开。这是整个假期里大家看过最好看的节目。|狗冲你低吼，管事站在狗那边，你只好坐地板。}\n# en: Take the draughty corner\nopt.right.label [590fe4] = 去漏风的角落\n# en: {season == \"winter\": {The corner is cold enough to see your breath, but you read three chapters.|You read in gloves. Turning pages becomes a skill.|You find a blanket nobody claims and read until your nose goes numb.}|{It smells of old ink and warm dust. You read three chapters.|A wasp shares your corner. You reach an understanding.|The breeze turns your pages for you, mostly the right way. You read three chapters anyway.}}\nopt.right.say.1 [7c5c66] = {season == \"winter\": {角落冷得能看见自己的呼气，但你读完了三章。|你戴着手套读书。翻页成了一门手艺。|你找到一条没人认领的毯子，一直读到鼻子冻僵。}|{那里有旧墨水和晒暖的灰尘的气味。你读完了三章。|一只黄蜂和你共享角落。你们达成了谅解。|风替你翻页，大多翻对了方向。你还是读完了三章。}}\n\n@event break_castle_kitchen_rota\n# en: The Holiday Kitchen\ntitle [cef060] = 假期的厨房\n# en: {Half the kitchen staff have gone home for the break, and the cook has started drafting whoever wanders past.|The cook spots you in the corridor and holds up an apron.|The cook holds out an apron without a word. It has your name stitched on it now.} {season == \"winter\": There are puddings to steam for the few who stayed, and a goose that refuses to fit the oven.|The orchard has dropped everything at once, and every pan in the kitchen is full of jam.}\ntext [21605b] = {一半厨工回家过假期了，厨娘开始拉路过的人来帮忙。|厨娘在走廊里看见你，举起一条围裙。|厨娘一言不发地递过一条围裙。上面现在绣着你的名字。} {season == \"winter\": 要给留下的几个人蒸布丁，还有一只死活塞不进烤炉的鹅。|果园里的果子一下子全落了，厨房里每口锅都在熬果酱。}\n# en: A steaming castle kitchen with half its ovens cold, the cook handing aprons to two reluctant students.\nscene [d1a5e4] = 冒着热气的城堡厨房，一半烤炉是冷的，厨娘把围裙递给两个不情愿的学生。\n# en: Roll up your sleeves\nopt.left.label [52915f] = 挽起袖子\n# en: {season == \"winter\": {You stir puddings till your arm aches. \"Just like potion work,\" says the cook. It isn't.|You baste the goose. The goose wins, narrowly.|The goose fits at last. The cook says it was her idea.}|{A morning of jam teaches you more about setting points than a term of alchemy.|You label forty jars. The cook re-spells \"gooseberry\" on all of them.|You stone plums all morning. Your fingers are purple until Tuesday.}}\nopt.left.say.1 [85e3c1] = {season == \"winter\": {你搅布丁搅到胳膊发酸。“跟熬魔药一样。”厨娘说。并不一样。|你给鹅刷油。鹅以微弱优势获胜。|鹅终于塞进去了。厨娘说这是她的主意。}|{一上午的果酱让你对凝固点的了解超过一学期炼金课。|你给四十个罐子贴了标签。厨娘把每张上的“醋栗”都重新拼了一遍。|你一上午都在给李子去核。手指一直紫到星期二。}}\n# en: Discover urgent business elsewhere\nopt.right.label [14e943] = 想起别处有急事\n# en: {You escape to the village and buy a pie you could have had for free. The cook sees you come back with the crumbs.|You hide in the village bakery until lunch is over and buy what you could have eaten for free. The cook gives you a meaningful look all evening.|You claim a library emergency. The cook, who knows the library is shut, says nothing and saves you nothing.}\nopt.right.say.1 [73f1e1] = {你溜去山下小镇，买了一个本来可以白吃的馅饼。厨娘看见你带着饼渣回来。|你躲在镇上的面包房里，一直躲到午饭结束，还花钱买了本来可以白吃的东西。厨娘一整晚都意味深长地看着你。|你说图书馆有急事。厨娘知道图书馆关着门，什么也没说，也什么都没给你留。}\n\n@event break_castle_empty_library\n# en: The Library on Holiday\ntitle [4ce56c] = 放假的图书馆\n# en: {The librarians have gone home, and the library has been left to you and the cat.|Footnote has moved from the reference desk to your chair, and opens one eye.|Your break-morning library. One window seat is yours now.} {season == \"winter\": Frost has drawn ferns on the tall windows.|Dust turns slowly in the long summer light.} {Every desk is free.|Someone has left half an essay on the theory of hiccups.|The reshelving cart is full, and nobody is coming for it.}\ntext [8f56d2] = {图书管理员们都回家了，图书馆留给了你和那只猫。|脚注从咨询台挪到了你的椅子上，睁开一只眼。|你的假期早晨图书馆。有一个靠窗的座位现在归你了。} {season == \"winter\": 霜在高窗上画满了蕨叶。|灰尘在长长的夏日光线里慢慢打转。} {每张书桌都空着。|有人留下半篇论打嗝理论的论文。|还书车堆满了，没人会来推。}\n# en: A great library with no one at the desks, a cat asleep on the reference desk and a single student choosing a book.\nscene [e16f43] = 一座书桌空无一人的大图书馆，一只猫睡在咨询台上，一个学生在挑书。\n# en: Read whatever I like\nopt.left.label [0ceb4d] = 想读什么读什么\n# en: {You read a book about the history of spoons and forget to eat lunch. It was a very good book about spoons.|You read until the light goes, then a little after. Your eyes will mention it tomorrow.|You finish the book you started last break. The bookmark was still where you left it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [1afd3a] = {你读了一本讲勺子历史的书，忘了吃午饭。那是一本非常好的讲勺子的书。|你一直读到天光散尽，又多读了一会儿。明天你的眼睛会提起这件事。|你读完了上个假期开始读的那本书。书签还夹在你留下的地方。}\n# en: Get ahead on next term\nopt.right.label [d47dc6] = 提前预习下学期\n# en: {final_year: You make notes for a term you'll only half get to use. It still feels like the right thing to do.|{You make a neat set of notes for next term. Future you will be grateful. Present you is bored stiff.|You read ahead three chapters and find the professor's favourite mistake waiting in the fourth.|You copy out next term's reading list and get through half of it. The other half looks smug.}}\nopt.right.say.1 [425249] = {final_year: 你为一个只能用上一半的学期做笔记。可还是觉得该这么做。|{你为下学期整理了一套整齐的笔记。将来的你会感激。现在的你闷得要命。|你往前读了三章，在第四章撞见了教授最爱犯的那个错误。|你抄下下学期的书单，读完了一半。另一半看起来很得意。}}\n\n@event break_castle_post_day\n# en: Post for the Stay-Behinds\ntitle [38476c] = 留守者的信件\n# en: {Post day. The porter lays the letters out on one table in the Great Hall.|The porter now sorts the stay-behinds' letters into a pile of their own.|The porter nods to you. He keeps your letters on top now.} {season == \"winter\": {Most of it is Midwinter cards for people who went home.|One parcel is exactly the shape of a scarf.|One envelope has your name on it, in handwriting you know.}|{Most of it is postcards from people's travels.|A postcard says \"Wish you were here\", and nobody knows whose it is.|One envelope has your name on it and smells of the sea.}}\ntext [5fca27] = {邮件日。门房把信摊在大礼堂的一张桌子上。|门房现在把留守者的信单独分成一小叠。|门房朝你点点头。他现在把你的信放在最上面。} {season == \"winter\": {大多是寄给回家的人的仲冬贺卡。|有个包裹的形状和一条围巾一模一样。|有一个信封上写着你的名字，字迹你认得。}|{大多是旅行中的人寄来的明信片。|一张明信片上只写着“真希望你也在”，没人知道是给谁的。|有一个信封写着你的名字，闻起来有海的味道。}}\n# en: A porter sorting a thin pile of letters on a long empty table, a few students waiting hopefully.\nscene [b2c195] = 门房在空荡的长桌上分拣一小叠信，几个学生满怀希望地等着。\n# en: Write a long letter home\nopt.left.label [b376a0] = 给家里写封长信\n# en: {final_year: You write that this is your last break at the castle, and then look at the sentence for a long time.|{You write four pages and pay for the heavy stamp. It's the closest thing to a conversation you've had all week.|You describe the empty castle so well that you feel lonelier writing it than living it.|You write about the porter, the biscuit and the empty hall. It reads like a good life, which it is.}}\nopt.left.say.1 [bc5632] = {final_year: 你写道这是你在城堡度过的最后一个假期，然后盯着这句话看了很久。|{你写了四页，付了厚信的邮费。这是你这一周里最像聊天的一次。|你把空荡荡的城堡写得太好，写的时候比住在里面还寂寞。|你写了门房、饼干和空荡荡的大厅。读起来像一种不错的生活，事实也是。}}\n# en: Help the porter sort it all\nopt.right.label [329389] = 帮门房分拣\n# en: {The porter tells you who writes to whom, in strict confidence. You know far too much about the second-years now.|You sort the post into houses. There's nothing for you this time. The porter gives you a biscuit, which is nearly as good.|You sort the pile in half the time it took you once. The porter calls you his deputy, and nobody argues.}\nopt.right.say.1 [fa9973] = {门房严格保密地告诉你谁给谁写信。现在你对二年级生知道得太多了。|你把信按学院分好。这次没有你的。门房给了你一块饼干，差不多一样好。|你分完这堆信，只用了当初一半的时间。门房管你叫他的副手，没人反对。}\n\n@event break_home_kitchen_table\n# en: Homework at the Kitchen Table\ntitle [081adf] = 厨房桌上的功课\n# en: {You spread your books across the kitchen table, where you did your sums as a child.|Your books have been moved for the bread, and one is being used as a trivet.|The family works around your books now. Mostly.} {season == \"winter\": The stove is the only warm place, so everyone is in the kitchen.|The door is open, and everyone passing asks what the book is about.} {A cousin has drawn a dragon in your margin.|The kettle boils every quarter-hour, on principle.|Someone has put a book of yours under a wobbly table leg, and it works.}\ntext [266433] = {你把书摊在厨房桌上，小时候你就在这儿算算术。|你的书被挪开给面包腾地方，有一本被拿去垫了锅。|家里人现在会绕着你的书干活。大多数时候。} {season == \"winter\": 炉子是屋里唯一暖和的地方，所以大家都在厨房。|门朝院子开着，每个路过的人都要问这书讲什么。} {一个表亲在你的页边画了条龙。|水壶每一刻钟烧开一次，原则问题。|有人把你的一本书垫在了一条晃动的桌腿下面，还真管用。}\n# en: A family kitchen in the morning, a student's books spread among the breakfast plates, someone reaching across them for the butter.\nscene [6a8ca6] = 早晨的家中厨房，学生的书摊在早餐盘子之间，有人越过书去够黄油。\n# en: Study through the noise\nopt.left.label [9218fa] = 顶着吵闹学习\n# en: {You read the same paragraph nine times and learn it perfectly, along with the price of eggs.|You get through a chapter. Your notes now have a jam stain, a crumb and the shopping list on them.|You learn to read with one hand and pass the salt with the other. A whole chapter goes in.}\nopt.left.say.1 [bc5a95] = {同一段你读了九遍，背得滚瓜烂熟，连带记住了鸡蛋的价钱。|你读完了一章。你的笔记上现在有一块果酱渍、一粒面包屑和一张购物清单。|你学会了一手看书一手递盐。读进去了整整一章。}\n# en: Give up and help with lunch\nopt.right.label [91e189] = 放弃，帮忙做午饭\n# en: {You're sent to the shop for the one thing everyone forgot, and you buy sweets with the change.|You peel, chop and get told about everyone in the village. Your book stays shut, and it's the best morning of the break.|You're on potatoes again. You're told you peel like a scholar, which isn't a compliment.}\nopt.right.say.1 [7b491a] = {你被派去店里买大家都忘了的那样东西，用找零买了糖。|你削皮、切菜，听了村里每个人的近况。书一直没打开，这是假期里最好的一个上午。|你又被派去削土豆。家里人说你削皮的样子像个学者，这不是夸奖。}\n\n@event break_home_the_box\n# en: The Box Under the Bed\ntitle [dfd52f] = 床底下的盒子\n# en: {Your old room is smaller than you remember. Under the bed is the box where you kept everything that mattered when you were nine.|Someone has dusted the box since last time and pretended not to look inside.|You've almost reached the bottom of the box.} {This time it's a wand you made from a stick and a lot of string.|This time it's the letter you wrote to the academy at age ten, asking to be let in early.|This time it's a drawing of seven towers, each with a little flag.}\ntext [00a18c] = {你的旧房间比记忆里小。床底下是你九岁时装一切重要东西的盒子。|上次之后有人掸过盒子上的灰，还假装没往里看。|你快翻到盒底了。} {这次是一根你用树枝和一大堆绳子做的魔杖。|这次是你十岁时写给学院的信，请求提前入学。|这次是一幅七座塔的画，每座塔上都插着小旗。}\n# en: A narrow childhood bedroom with a bed far too short for its occupant, an old box of keepsakes pulled out onto the floor.\nscene [2fff89] = 一间窄小的儿时卧室，床对现在的主人来说短得多，一盒旧纪念品被拖到地板上。\n# en: Pack it in your trunk\nopt.left.label [60f445] = 装进行李箱\n# en: {It comes back to Lumenhold with you. The trunk is heavier, and so, somehow, are you.|You pack it between your robes. Every time you open the trunk this term, it will be the first thing you see.|You pack it carefully. There's not much left in the box now, and that feels like something ending.}\nopt.left.say.1 [c4ea52] = {它跟你一起回了流明堡。箱子更沉了，不知怎么，你也是。|你把它夹在袍子中间。这学期每次打开箱子，它都会是你第一眼看到的东西。|你小心地把它收好。盒子里剩的不多了，感觉像什么东西要结束了。}\n# en: Give it to your cousin\nopt.right.label [a1cb8e] = 送给你的表亲\n# en: {Your cousin, who is applying to the academy next autumn, takes it with enormous seriousness. You feel very old.|Your cousin promises to keep it safe. By supper it has been traded to a neighbour for a jar of honey.|Your cousin says thank you and puts it in a box of their own, under their own bed. You feel very old and quite happy.}\nopt.right.say.1 [155820] = {你的表亲明年秋天要报考学院，无比郑重地接了过去。你觉得自己老了。|表亲保证会好好收着。到晚饭时，它已经被换给邻居，换来一罐蜂蜜。|表亲道了谢，把它放进了自己床底下的盒子里。你觉得自己老了，也挺高兴。}\n\n@event break_home_family_debate\n# en: The Family Debate\ntitle [673f25] = 家庭辩论\n# en: {At breakfast the family is arguing, and someone says, \"Ask the scholar.\"|The argument has started without you. Everyone turns as you come down the stairs.|\"The scholar is up,\" someone announces. They've saved today's argument for you.} {season == \"winter\": {Is it colder than the year of the big snow?|Can you freeze a pond by thinking at it?|Can a wizard make the hens lay in winter?}|{Do swallows sleep at the bottom of rivers?|Will it rain on the fair, and can you stop it?|Is the old well really haunted, and should it be?}}\ntext [86f635] = {早饭时一家人在争论，有人说：“问问读书人。”|争论没等你就开始了。你下楼时，所有人都转过头来。|“读书人起来了。”有人宣布。他们把今天的争论留给了你。} {season == \"winter\": {今年是不是比大雪那年还冷？|光靠想就能把池塘冻住吗？|巫师能让母鸡冬天下蛋吗？}|{燕子是不是睡在河底？|集市那天会下雨吗，你能不能拦住？|那口老井真的闹鬼吗？该不该闹？}}\n# en: A breakfast table mid-argument, several relatives pointing at a student as if they were the deciding vote.\nscene [0af97b] = 争论正酣的早餐桌，几个亲戚指着一个学生，好像那是决定性的一票。\n# en: Settle it with what I've learned\nopt.left.label [650629] = 用学到的东西解决\n# en: {You explain it so clearly that the argument ends. There is a short, respectful silence, and then a new argument starts.|You settle it. An uncle demands to see it in writing. You write it on a napkin, and he keeps the napkin.|You settle it with a diagram on the back of the almanac. It's still pinned by the stove the next time you're home.}\nopt.left.say.1 [98ec0a] = {你讲得清清楚楚，争论结束了。一阵短暂而尊敬的沉默，然后新的争论开始了。|你解决了。一位叔叔要你写下来。你写在餐巾上，他把餐巾收了起来。|你在历书背面画了张图解决了。下次你回家时，它还钉在炉子边上。}\n# en: {You explain it perfectly, and they all decide the academy has taught you nonsense.|Halfway through you realise you're wrong. So does everyone else.|You're right, and you prove it, and nobody believes you, because the almanac says otherwise.}\nopt.left.say.2 [364c86] = {你讲得完美无缺，结果他们一致认定学院教了你一堆胡话。|讲到一半你发现自己错了。其他人也发现了。|你是对的，也证明了，可没人信你，因为历书上写的不一样。}\n# en: Refuse to take sides\nopt.right.label [146465] = 拒绝站队\n# en: {You butter your toast with great care and say, \"Hm.\" It does not stop anyone, but it does save you.|You change the subject to the neighbour's fence. It works, but now there are two arguments.|You say both sides have a point. Both sides turn on you. It's the most united the family has been all year.}\nopt.right.say.1 [97d4a0] = {你非常仔细地给吐司抹黄油，说了声“嗯”。没能阻止任何人，但救了你自己。|你把话题转到邻居家的篱笆上。奏效了，但现在有两场争论了。|你说双方都有道理。双方一起转向了你。这是全家一年里最团结的时候。}\n\n@event break_home_village_children\n# en: The Wizard Is Home\ntitle [167820] = 巫师回来了\n# en: {Word has spread that you're home from the wizard school. Half the village has found a reason to pass the gate.|The neighbours are back at the gate, with a cousin from the next village to show you off to.|The gate committee is in session. They've drawn up a list.} {season == \"winter\": They want more snow for the sledging.|They want it to stop being so hot.} {The baker has brought a jar, in case you make something worth keeping.|The miller's wife asks if you've ever met a dragon.|The blacksmith has a stick that might be a wand.}\ntext [ccbdc0] = {消息传开了，说你从巫师学校回来了。半个村子的人都找了个理由从门口经过。|邻居们又来到门口，还带了个邻村的表亲来看你。|门口委员会又开会了。他们列了一张清单。} {season == \"winter\": 他们想要更多的雪来滑雪橇。|他们想让天别这么热。} {面包师带了个罐子，万一你变出什么值得留着的东西。|磨坊主的妻子问你见没见过龙。|铁匠拿着一根棍子，说不定真是魔杖。}\n# en: A gaggle of villagers at a cottage gate in the morning, staring hopefully at a student in academy robes.\nscene [3660a2] = 早晨一群村民聚在小屋门口，满怀期待地盯着一个穿学院长袍的学生。\n# en: Show them some sparks\nopt.left.label [b1cdc8] = 给他们放点火花\n# en: {You make a little burst of light over the gate. The neighbours cheer and demand it again, and again.|You make a spark dance across the gatepost. The baker tries to catch it in her apron, or thinks she does, which is the same thing.|You work through the committee's list of sparks, in order. They tick each one off and ask for the list again tomorrow.}\nopt.left.say.1 [44bf13] = {你在门上方变出一小团光。邻居们欢呼起来，要你再来，再来，再来。|你让一颗火花在门柱上跳舞。面包师想用围裙兜住它，或者以为兜住了，那也一样。|你照着委员会的单子，一个一个放火花。他们看完一个就打个勾，还要你明天再来一遍。}\n# en: Teach them something real\nopt.right.label [f0ec48] = 教他们点真东西\n# en: {You explain how the light gets into a spell. They listen politely and then ask for sparks.|You show them how to sit still and listen to a candle. The old miller really hears it. You can tell.|You make the blacksmith's stick glow for a minute. It isn't a wand, but he'll never believe that now.}\nopt.right.say.1 [93d4e1] = {你讲光是怎么进到咒语里的。他们礼貌地听完，然后要火花。|你教他们静静坐着听一支蜡烛。老磨坊主真的听见了。你看得出来。|你让铁匠那根棍子亮了一分钟。它不是魔杖，可他现在再也不会信了。}\n\n@event break_work_holiday_rush\n# en: The Holiday Rush\ntitle [c1bbea] = 假日高峰\n# en: {The holidays bring the crowds.|This time you've learned to eat breakfast before the rush, not during it.|You can feel the rush coming an hour before it arrives.} {has(gig_newt): The Gilded Newt is packed to the beams.|{has(gig_pell): The queue at Pell & Daughters is out of the door.|{has(gig_post): The loft's shelves are stacked to the rafters with parcels.|{has(gig_library): The library is full of last-minute readers.|{has(gig_kitchens): The Refectory is feeding everyone who stayed, and all their visiting families.|{has(gig_grounds): The grounds are full of visitors walking off their holiday dinners, and every one of them has a question.|Everyone wants something, and now.}}}}}} {season == \"winter\": {Everyone wants it before Midwinter.|Wet boots, short tempers.|Everyone is in a hurry to be somewhere warm.}|{Everyone is sunburnt.|Travellers keep asking the way.|The heat slows everyone down except the queue.}}\ntext [acfd1b] = {假期带来了人潮。|这次你学会了在高峰前吃早饭，而不是在高峰中。|高峰还有一个钟头才到，你已经感觉到了。} {has(gig_newt): 镀金蝾螈酒馆挤得水泄不通。|{has(gig_pell): 佩尔父女药铺的队排到了门外。|{has(gig_post): 阁楼的架子上包裹一直堆到房梁。|{has(gig_library): 图书馆里全是临时抱佛脚的读者。|{has(gig_kitchens): 食堂要喂饱所有留校的人，还有他们来探望的家人。|{has(gig_grounds): 场地上全是吃完节日大餐出来散步的访客，每个人都有问题要问。|人人都想要点什么，而且现在就要。}}}}}} {season == \"winter\": {人人都想在仲冬前拿到。|湿靴子，坏脾气。|人人都急着去暖和的地方。}|{人人都晒得通红。|旅人不停地问路。|炎热让每个人都慢下来，除了队伍。}}\n# en: A holiday rush at a busy workplace, a student in the thick of it wearing the fixed smile of someone on their fourth hour of the same question.\nscene [0eb180] = 假期里忙碌的工作场所，一个学生身处人潮中央，挂着第四个钟头回答同一个问题的僵硬笑容。\n# en: Smile through it\nopt.left.label [35213b] = 笑着撑过去\n# en: {You smile until your face aches. Somebody tips you a coin and a peppermint.|You smile through the fourth complaint about the weather, as if it were your fault. Somehow the tips are better than ever.|You have a smile for every complaint now, sorted by type. The tips follow.}\nopt.left.say.1 [6aa1dc] = {你笑到脸发酸。有人给了你一枚小费和一颗薄荷糖。|第四个人来抱怨天气，好像是你的错，你照样笑着。不知怎么，小费比哪次都多。|现在你每种抱怨都备好了一种笑容，分门别类。小费随之而来。}\n# en: Take the heavy jobs instead\nopt.right.label [7fdbc4] = 改干重活\n# en: {You haul the heaviest loads all morning and talk to no one. Your arms complain, and nobody else does.|You take the dull, heavy work nobody wants. It's tiring and blissfully quiet.|You know where every heavy thing goes by now. You're done by noon, and nobody has spoken to you. Perfect.}\nopt.right.say.1 [020c37] = {你搬了一上午最沉的东西，一句话没说。胳膊在抱怨，别的谁也没抱怨。|你揽下了没人想干的又闷又重的活。累，但安静得幸福。|现在你知道每样重东西该放哪儿。不到中午就干完了，没人跟你说过一句话。完美。}\n\n@event break_work_opening_up\n# en: Opening Up\ntitle [307370] = 开门\n# en: {In the holidays you're trusted with the keys. You open up at dawn, alone.|You've learned to lift the door as you turn the key.|You could open up in your sleep, and once you nearly did.} {season == \"winter\": It's still dark, and the stove has gone out.|The sun is up and the street smells of warm stone.} {There's a note under the door from someone who wanted to come in at midnight.|A cat waits on the step as if it has an appointment.|The place is yours for half an hour.}\ntext [b1d7dc] = {假期里你被托付了钥匙。天一亮你独自开门。|你学会了转钥匙时顺手把门往上一抬。|你闭着眼都能开门，有一次差点真这么干了。} {season == \"winter\": 天还黑着，炉子夜里灭了。|太阳已经升起，街上有暖石头的气味。} {门缝下塞着一张字条，来自半夜想进来的人。|一只猫在台阶上等着，好像有预约。|这地方有半个钟头归你一个人。}\n# en: A student unlocking a shop door in the half-light of early morning, breath steaming, the street empty behind them.\nscene [7b5489] = 清晨微光中一个学生在开店门，呼出白气，身后的街道空无一人。\n# en: Do it all by the book\nopt.left.label [433ff5] = 一切按规矩来\n# en: {Swept, stocked and opened to the minute. The owner notices and slips you a little extra.|Everything is ready when the first customer arrives. Nobody notices but you. Still, the coin is good.|You open up to the minute, as always. The owner has stopped checking.}\nopt.left.say.1 [4593ee] = {扫好、摆好、准点开门。老板注意到了，悄悄多塞给你一点。|第一位客人来时一切就绪。除了你没人注意。不过钱是实在的。|你一如既往地准点开门。老板已经不再来查了。}\n# en: Steal a quiet half hour\nopt.right.label [48ac16] = 偷半个钟头清闲\n# en: {You drink tea by the window and watch the street wake up. It's the best part of the job.|You read a chapter with your feet up. Nobody ever knows.|There's a chair by the window that's yours now. Half an hour of nothing, and nobody the wiser.}\nopt.right.say.1 [73930f] = {你在窗边喝茶，看街道醒来。这是这份工作最好的部分。|你把脚翘起来读了一章。没人知道。|现在窗边有一把你专属的椅子。半个钟头什么也不干，谁也不知道。}\n# en: {The owner arrives early and finds you with your feet up. The half hour comes out of your wages.|The first customer arrives early and finds the door locked. They leave a note. So does the owner.|The owner has taken to arriving early just to check, and today it pays off. The half hour comes out of your wages.}\nopt.right.say.2 [36d6dc] = {老板来早了，看见你翘着脚。那半个钟头从工钱里扣了。|第一位客人来早了，发现门锁着。他留了张字条。老板也留了一张。|老板开始故意早到来查岗，今天被逮个正着。那半个钟头从工钱里扣了。}\n\n@event break_work_regular\n# en: The Holiday Regular\ntitle [4ae6e4] = 假期常客\n# en: {Every holiday has its regular. This one comes every morning and wants to talk.|The regular is back. They wave to you as if you were old friends.|The regular has started calling you by your first name.} {Today it's about their late husband's beard.|Today it's about a dragon they swear they saw as a girl.|Today it's about the year the lake froze, which may or may not have happened.} The queue behind them is getting longer.\ntext [e2a150] = {每个假期都有个常客。这一位每天早上都来，而且想聊天。|常客又来了。冲你挥手，像是老朋友。|常客开始直接叫你的名字了。} {今天聊的是过世丈夫的胡子。|今天聊的是一条龙，她发誓小时候见过。|今天聊的是湖结冰的那一年，那年也许有，也许没有。} 后面的队越排越长。\n# en: An elderly customer settling in for a long chat at a counter, a student behind it glancing at the queue.\nscene [4825ff] = 一位年长的客人在柜台前坐定准备长谈，柜台后的学生瞥着排队的人。\n# en: Let them finish the story\nopt.left.label [884a81] = 让他们把故事讲完\n# en: {You hear the whole story, standing, while the queue thins out to somewhere else. Your feet ache by the end. The regular leaves happy, and so, oddly, are you.|The story has an ending you didn't expect, and you'll think about it all day. You stood through all of it, and your back says so.|You know how their stories end by now, and you let them tell it anyway. The queue waits. Your feet don't forgive you.}\nopt.left.say.1 [ca67b9] = {你站着听完了整个故事，排队的人散去别家了。到最后你的脚都站疼了。常客高高兴兴地走了，奇怪的是，你也是。|故事的结局出乎意料，你会想一整天。你从头站到尾，腰都在抗议。|现在你已经知道对方的故事怎么结尾了，还是让对方讲完。队伍在等。你的脚可不原谅你。}\n# en: Hurry them gently along\nopt.right.label [7b4db3] = 温和地催一催\n# en: {You serve the queue and the regular leaves, mid-sentence, with a little wave. It shouldn't bother you as much as it does.|The queue is grateful. The regular says, \"Another time, then,\" and you both know they mean it.|You keep the queue moving and promise the regular the rest tomorrow. They hold you to it, every time.}\nopt.right.say.1 [890677] = {你招呼了排队的人，常客话说到一半，挥挥手走了。这事不该让你这么在意。|排队的人很感激。常客说：“那下回吧。”你们都知道这话是真心的。|你让队伍动起来，答应常客明天听完剩下的。每一次，对方都会记着要你兑现。}\n\n@event break_work_new_hire\n# en: The Holiday Hire\ntitle [ff5b6a] = 假期新人\n# en: {The boss has taken on a holiday hire and put them next to you.|Another holiday hire, another nervous face. You're the old hand now.|This break's new hire looks at you the way you once looked at everyone.} {They've already dropped something.|They ask whether the job is always like this. You don't answer.|They're very keen and very fast, and very nearly right.}\ntext [86103e] = {老板招了个假期临时工，安排在你旁边。|又一个临时工，又一张紧张的脸。现在你是老手了。|这个假期的新人看你的眼神，就像你当初看所有人。} {他们已经摔了个东西。|他们问这活儿是不是一直这样。你没回答。|他们很热心、很快，而且差一点就对了。}\n# en: A nervous new worker holding a tray at the wrong angle, a student leaning over to correct them.\nscene [c6ec03] = 一个紧张的新人用错误的角度端着托盘，一个学生探身过去纠正。\n# en: Show them the ropes\nopt.left.label [f00110] = 带带新人\n# en: {By noon they know where everything is and that the boss is all bark. You've lost a morning and gained a friend.|You teach them the trick with the till. They teach you a song. You both get told off for the song.|You show them everything you wish someone had shown you. By the end of the day they're showing the next one.}\nopt.left.say.1 [be1b0b] = {到中午他们知道了每样东西在哪，也知道老板只是嘴凶。你搭进一个上午，多了个朋友。|你教他们收银台的窍门。他们教你一首歌。你们俩因为那首歌一起挨了骂。|你把当初希望有人教你的全教给了他们。到下班时，他们已经在教下一个了。}\n# en: Let them learn the hard way\nopt.right.label [c3bb81] = 让他们吃点苦头\n# en: {They learn. Unfortunately, they learn by breaking a jar that the boss puts on your account.|You let them flounder. It takes them all day to find the stockroom, and you end up covering their shift anyway. The boss takes the stock they spoiled out of your pay.|They learn fast, mostly from your mistakes, which they found in the ledger. The boss bills you for the latest one.}\nopt.right.say.1 [bf6511] = {他们学会了。可惜是靠打碎一个罐子学会的，老板记在了你账上。|你让他们自己摸索。他们找了一整天库房，最后还是你替他们顶了班。他们糟蹋的货，老板从你工钱里扣了。|他们学得很快，主要是从账本上你犯过的错里学的。老板把最新的一笔记在了你头上。}\n\n@event break_internship_lodgings\n# en: Breakfast at the Lodgings\ntitle [ae293d] = 寄宿处的早餐\n# en: {{member(intern_expedition): Your lodging for the placement is a tent that flaps all night.|{member(intern_field_station): Your lodging is a bunk in the station cabin, under a shelf of jars.|Your lodging is one small room and a breakfast you pay for.}}|By now you can make any lodging home in an evening, a book on the sill and your boots by the door.|You've slept in enough borrowed beds to know breakfast matters more than the mattress.} {season == \"winter\": {Your breath steams.|The water jug has frozen.|Ice on the inside of the window.}|{It's warm before sunrise.|A bird is shouting outside.|The window is open, and someone's breakfast is frying.}} {Breakfast is porridge.|Breakfast is porridge with opinions.|Breakfast is unnamed.}\ntext [dd9184] = {{member(intern_expedition): 这次实习的住处是一顶整夜扑扑作响的帐篷。|{member(intern_field_station): 你的住处是野外站小木屋里的一张铺位，头顶一排罐子。|你的住处是一个小房间，外加一顿要付钱的早餐。}}|如今不管什么住处，你一个晚上就能住成家：窗台上一本书，门边一双靴子。|你睡过的借来的床够多了，知道早餐比床垫要紧。} {season == \"winter\": {呼出的气冒着白烟。|水壶冻住了。|窗户里面结了冰。}|{太阳没出来就暖了。|外面有只鸟在大喊。|窗开着，不知谁的早饭在煎。}} {早餐是粥。|早餐是有主见的粥。|早餐是一样没人叫得出名的东西。}\n# en: A borrowed bed at dawn, boots by the door, a bowl of breakfast waiting and a notebook open on the blanket.\nscene [7aadaf] = 黎明时一张借来的床，靴子放在门边，一碗早餐等着，毯子上摊着一本笔记。\n# en: Eat a proper breakfast\nopt.left.label [f0ec45] = 好好吃顿早饭\n# en: {You eat slowly and listen to everyone's gossip. You arrive late enough to be noticed.|Fed, warm and five minutes late. Your supervisor looks at the clock and says nothing at all.|You eat everything and ask for the recipe. You're late, and your supervisor has started to expect it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [dfef2d] = {你慢慢吃，听着各种闲话。到得晚了，晚到会被注意。|吃饱了，暖和了，晚了五分钟。导师看了看钟，什么也没说。|你吃得一干二净，还要了做法。你迟到了，导师已经开始预料到这一点。}\n# en: Skip it and go in early\nopt.right.label [cfe210] = 不吃了，早点去\n# en: {You're the first one in. You get through a pile of work before anyone else arrives, and your stomach growls through every meeting after.|You read the day's notes on the way. By lunchtime you're faint and well informed.|You go in early with an apple in your pocket. It's gone by the first meeting, and the hunger stays until supper.}\nopt.right.say.1 [5cc63c] = {你第一个到。别人来之前你已经处理掉一堆活，之后的每场会上你的肚子都在叫。|你在路上读当天的笔记。到午饭时你头晕眼花，消息灵通。|你揣着一个苹果提早去了。第一场会还没开完它就没了，饿意一直撑到晚饭。}\n\n@event break_internship_briefing\n# en: The Morning Briefing\ntitle [7aa6d4] = 晨间布置\n# en: {Your supervisor reads the day's tasks aloud, once, very fast.|You bring two pens now, and a spare for the intern beside you.|You've learned to read the briefing from the way your supervisor holds the list.} {One task makes no sense at all.|One task seems to mean the opposite of what it says.|The last task is mumbled into a cup of tea.} {final_year: It's your last placement, and you want it to count.|The others all write as if they understood.}\ntext [672368] = {导师把当天的任务大声念一遍，只念一次，念得飞快。|你现在带两支笔，还给旁边的实习生备一支。|你已经能从导师拿清单的姿势读出任务了。} {有一项任务完全说不通。|有一项任务似乎和字面意思正相反。|最后一项任务是对着茶杯嘟囔出来的。} {final_year: 这是最后一次实习，你想让它有分量。|其他人都写得好像全听懂了。}\n# en: A supervisor reading a list of tasks aloud to a row of interns, one of whom has stopped writing.\nscene [ba7fb6] = 导师对着一排实习生大声念任务清单，其中一个停下了笔。\n# en: Ask what it means\nopt.left.label [5dfe5f] = 问清楚是什么意思\n# en: {Your supervisor explains it again, slowly, while the other interns smirk. Two of them come to you at lunch to ask what it meant.|\"Good question,\" says your supervisor, in a tone that suggests it wasn't. You get the task right, anyway.|You ask before anyone else can pretend. Your supervisor answers, and three pens around you start writing.}\nopt.left.say.1 [1d092e] = {导师又慢慢解释了一遍，其他实习生偷笑。午饭时有两个人跑来问你那是什么意思。|“好问题。”导师说，语气表明并不是。不管怎样，你把任务做对了。|你抢在别人装懂之前就问了。导师作了答，你周围三支笔开始动起来。}\n# en: Guess and get on with it\nopt.right.label [f42afd] = 猜一猜，先干起来\n# en: {You guess right. Your supervisor nods as if they never doubted you.|It turns out the odd task was \"take the rest of the morning off\". Lucky. You spend it reading in the archive.|You guess, and you've guessed this one before. The task is done before anyone else has finished asking.}\nopt.right.say.1 [a8aac1] = {你猜对了。导师点点头，好像从没怀疑过你。|原来那项古怪的任务是“上午剩下的时间休息”。运气不错。你在档案室里读了一上午。|你猜了，而这种你以前就猜过。别人还没问完，你已经做完了。}\n# en: {You guess wrong and spend the morning carefully doing the opposite of what was wanted.|You did the right task to the wrong thing. The wrong thing will never be the same again.|Your guess is confident, thorough and wrong. Your supervisor reads the result twice and asks you, kindly, to ask next time.}\nopt.right.say.2 [ba5ae5] = {你猜错了，一上午都在一丝不苟地做和要求相反的事。|你对错的东西做了对的任务。那样东西再也回不去了。|你猜得自信、周全，而且错了。导师把结果读了两遍，好心地让你下次先问。}\n\n@event break_internship_other_interns\n# en: The Other Interns\ntitle [ebc4c3] = 其他实习生\n# en: {There are other interns here, and they've already formed a supper table.|You've learned to find the supper table on the first day, and which seat isn't anyone's.|By now you can tell within a morning which intern is the one to sit beside.} {One has a cousin on the Conclave and mentions it hourly.|One is better than all of you, and very nice about it.|One has been here before and knows where the biscuits are.} {season == \"winter\": Supper is eaten in coats.|Supper is eaten in the last of the sun.}\ntext [d1c192] = {这里还有别的实习生，他们已经凑成了一张晚饭桌。|你学会了第一天就找到晚饭桌，还有哪个座位不属于任何人。|如今一上午你就能看出该坐在哪个实习生旁边。} {有一个的表亲在七塔议会，每小时提一次。|有一个比你们都强，而且人很好。|有一个以前来过，知道好饼干藏在哪。} {season == \"winter\": 晚饭是穿着外套吃的。|晚饭是晒着最后一点太阳吃的。}\n# en: A small group of interns eating supper together, one sitting slightly apart with a notebook.\nscene [719a03] = 几个实习生一起吃晚饭，一个拿着笔记本的人坐得稍远。\n# en: Join them for supper\nopt.left.label [a8d96d] = 和他们一起吃晚饭\n# en: {You chip in for the shared food and find out which of the supervisors are kind. Useful, and fun.|You talk shop until your supper is cold. It turns out everyone else is as lost as you are, which helps.|You sit beside the one who knows where the biscuits are. By pudding you know where everything else is too.}\nopt.left.say.1 [b0f35d] = {你凑钱买了大家分的吃食，打听到哪些导师心肠好。有用，也开心。|你们聊工作聊到饭都凉了。原来大家和你一样摸不着头脑，这让人宽心。|你坐到了那个知道饼干放哪儿的人旁边。到吃甜点时，你连别的东西在哪儿也都知道了。}\n# en: Eat alone with your notes\nopt.right.label [5262d8] = 独自看笔记吃饭\n# en: {You finish the reading for tomorrow. You hear laughter from the table, and you don't look up.|You get ahead of everyone. Nobody saves you a biscuit.|You eat with your notes again. Someone leaves a biscuit by your elbow without a word.}\nopt.right.say.1 [966ee9] = {你读完了明天的材料。桌子那边传来笑声，你没抬头。|你领先了所有人。没人给你留饼干。|你又一边吃饭一边看笔记。有人一言不发地在你手肘边放了块饼干。}\n\n@event break_internship_stipend\n# en: Stipend Day\ntitle [75f50a] = 发津贴的日子\n# en: {Stipend day. A small envelope, your name spelled almost right.|You've learned to budget the envelope before you open it.|Stipend day. You know what it's for before it arrives, and it still feels like a gift.} {member(intern_expedition) or member(intern_field_station): {The supply cart has come, with a pedlar riding on top.|A pedlar has walked a very long way to sell you socks.|The pedlar is back, and has heard about you.}|{season == \"winter\": {There's a warm scarf in a shop window you pass.|A bookshop nearby has a sale on almanacs.|The baker on the corner has started putting one aside on stipend day.}|{A cart is selling lemon ices on the corner.|A bookshop nearby has put its sale out in the sun.|The baker on the corner sells the last of the cherry buns at noon.}}}\ntext [b62368] = {发津贴了。一个小信封，你的名字几乎拼对了。|你学会了在拆信封之前就把钱安排好。|发津贴的日子。钱还没到你就知道它的用处，可它仍然像一份礼物。} {member(intern_expedition) or member(intern_field_station): {补给车来了，车顶上坐着一个货郎。|一个货郎走了很远的路来卖袜子给你。|那个货郎又来了，而且听说过你。}|{season == \"winter\": {你路过的橱窗里有一条暖和的围巾。|附近的书店在打折卖年历。|街角的面包师开始在发津贴的日子给你留一个。}|{街角有辆小车在卖柠檬冰。|附近的书店把打折书摆到了太阳底下。|街角的面包师中午卖最后一批樱桃面包。}}}\n# en: A small envelope of coins on a desk, a student looking at it with the expression of someone doing sums.\nscene [8956ab] = 书桌上一个装着硬币的小信封，一个学生看着它，一脸在算账的表情。\n# en: Treat yourself\nopt.left.label [ebab6c] = 犒劳一下自己\n# en: {member(intern_expedition) or member(intern_field_station): {You buy the pedlar's thickest socks. Your feet have never been so grateful.|You buy a jar of honey from the pedlar and eat it with a spoon, in triumph.|You buy the pedlar's whole stock of liquorice. He says you're his best customer, and you probably are.}|{season == \"winter\": {You buy it. It's quite unnecessary, and the warmest you've felt all week.|You spend the lot before lunch and regret none of it.|You spend it on something warm and something sweet, and keep a single coin back for luck.}|{You spend it before the corner and don't regret a crown.|You take your purchase to a bench in the sun and stay until your nose burns.|You buy the last cherry bun and a book you don't need, and eat one while reading the other.}}}\nopt.left.say.1 [655edc] = {member(intern_expedition) or member(intern_field_station): {你买了货郎最厚的袜子。你的脚从没这么感激过。|你从货郎那儿买了一罐蜂蜜，用勺子吃，吃得扬眉吐气。|你把货郎的甘草糖全买了。他说你是他最好的主顾，大概真是。}|{season == \"winter\": {你买下了它。完全没必要，却是你这一周最暖和的时刻。|午饭前你就花光了，一点也不后悔。|你买了点暖和的、点甜的，留下一枚硬币图个吉利。}|{没走到街角你就花完了，一个克朗都不后悔。|你带着买来的东西坐到阳光下的长椅上，一直坐到鼻子晒伤。|你买了最后一个樱桃面包和一本用不着的书，一边吃一边读。}}}\n# en: Save it and borrow the book\nopt.right.label [034d5f] = 省下钱，借那本书\n# en: {You put the envelope away and borrow the book instead. It's the dullest thing you've ever read, and the most useful. You copy out half of it the first night.|The envelope goes under your mattress. You read the borrowed book, and the next time your supervisor mentions it you nod in the right places.|The envelope joins the others under the mattress. The pile is getting interesting, and so is the book.}\nopt.right.say.1 [374872] = {你把信封收好，改去借了那本书。这是你读过最枯燥的书，也最有用。第一晚你就抄了一半。|信封塞到了床垫下面。你读了借来的书，下次导师提到它时，你在该点头的地方点了头。|信封和其他的一起塞到了床垫下面。那一沓越来越可观，书也越来越有意思。}\n\n@event thesis_margin_notes\n# en: Notes in the Margin\ntitle [6ecc14] = 页边批注\n# en: {Professor Thorne returns your chapter. In the margin of page one he has written \"No.\"|Another chapter back. The margins say \"Why?\", \"Source?\" and, once, \"Hm.\"|Thorne's notes are getting shorter. You can't decide whether that's good.} {Page six has a single word underlined three times.|One note just says \"See me.\"|There's a tick on page nine. You stare at it for a while.} {progress(thesis) >= 60: The argument is nearly there, and he knows it.|There's a long way to go.}\ntext [f474bd] = {索恩教授把你的章节退了回来。第一页的页边上他写着：“不。”|又一章退回来了。页边写着“为什么？”“出处？”，还有一处：“嗯。”|索恩的批注越来越短。你拿不准这是不是好事。} {第六页有一个词被画了三道线。|有一条批注只写着“来见我”。|第九页有一个勾。你盯着它看了好一会儿。} {progress(thesis) >= 60: 论证就快成了，他也知道。|还有很长的路要走。}\n# en: A thesis draft returned with a professor's spiky handwriting crawling up every margin.\nscene [981dda] = 一份退回的论文草稿，教授尖利的字迹爬满了每一处页边。\n# en: Rewrite to his notes\nopt.left.label [cbc3e9] = 照他的批注改\n# en: {You rewrite every page he marked. The chapter is shorter and better, and you hate that he was right.|You take every note. When he reads it again he crosses out only one thing, and you could cry.|You rewrite to his notes without arguing now. It saves a week, and he's usually right, which still annoys you.}\nopt.left.say.1 [c5eee1] = {他标过的每一页你都重写了。这一章更短、更好，你恨他是对的。|你采纳了每一条批注。他再读时只划掉了一处，你差点哭出来。|现在你照他的批注改，不再争了。省了一个星期，而他通常是对的，这依旧让你恼火。}\n# en: Defend my version\nopt.right.label [6497f5] = 为我的版本辩护\n# en: {You make your case at his office door. He listens, says \"Hm,\" and crosses out his own note.|You argue for your paragraph and he lets you keep it. \"Then make it say so,\" he says, which is almost praise.|You defend the paragraph with a source he hasn't read. He reads it overnight and gives you the paragraph.}\nopt.right.say.1 [feebbe] = {你在他办公室门口陈述理由。他听完，说了声“嗯”，划掉了自己的批注。|你为自己那一段据理力争，他让你留下了。“那就让它把话说清楚。”他说，这几乎算是夸奖。|你拿一份他没读过的资料为那段辩护。他连夜读完，把那段留给了你。}\n# en: {You argue. Thorne waits until you've finished, then points at the sentence that proves him right.|He hears you out, then writes a new note in the margin. It says \"No.\" again, in smaller letters.|You argue. He agrees with every point and still writes \"No.\" You've begun to find this comforting.}\nopt.right.say.2 [e68279] = {你争了。索恩等你说完，然后指着证明他是对的那一句。|他听你说完，在页边写了一条新批注。还是“不。”，字小了一号。|你争了。他每一条都同意，可还是写下“不。”你开始觉得这有点让人安心。}\n\n@event thesis_carrel_neighbour\n# en: The Next Carrel\ntitle [1fe6fe] = 隔壁隔间\n# en: {Your carrel neighbour, a final-year from Rootward, reads aloud under their breath.|Your carrel neighbour now reads your chapter titles aloud too.|You and your carrel neighbour now speak a language of sighs.} {They're stuck on chapter three.|They've found a source that disagrees with you both.|They pass you half a sandwich over the partition.} {season == \"winter\": The thesis lab is cold enough for gloves.|Someone's notes are escaping through the window.}\ntext [3b1774] = {你的隔间邻居是一位根庐的毕业年级生，总在小声念书。|你的隔间邻居现在连你的章节标题也念出声来。|你和隔间邻居现在用叹气交流。} {他们卡在第三章了。|他们找到一份和你俩都唱反调的资料。|他们从隔板上方递给你半个三明治。} {season == \"winter\": 论文实验室冷得要戴手套。|有人的笔记正从窗口往外逃。}\n# en: Two thesis carrels side by side, the partition between them covered in pinned notes and a shared candle.\nscene [e1d83d] = 两个并排的论文隔间，中间的隔板上钉满了笔记，还有一支共用的蜡烛。\n# en: Swap drafts for an hour\nopt.left.label [d31846] = 交换草稿看一小时\n# en: {They find the hole in your argument in ten minutes. You find three in theirs. Everyone feels better and worse.|They read your chapter and say, \"Oh, I see.\" You're not sure you did, until now.|You swap drafts over the partition, sandwich crumbs and all. Both chapters come back better.}\nopt.left.say.1 [e140b0] = {他们十分钟就找到了你论证里的漏洞。你在他们的里面找到三个。大家都觉得好受了些，也难受了些。|他们读完你的章节，说：“哦，我明白了。”在这之前你都不确定自己明白。|你们隔着隔板交换草稿，连带着三明治屑。两章都改得更好了。}\n# en: {They love your chapter, which helps nobody. You spend the hour fixing their commas.|They ask so many questions that you start doubting your title.|Their notes on your chapter are longer than your chapter. You lose the afternoon reading them.}\nopt.left.say.2 [792da9] = {他们很喜欢你的章节，这对谁都没帮助。你花了一小时改他们的逗号。|他们问了太多问题，你开始怀疑起自己的标题。|他们给你那一章写的批注比那一章还长。你搭进一下午读这些批注。}\n# en: Hunt a source in the stacks\nopt.right.label [714cad] = 去书库找资料\n# en: {You find it on the bottom shelf, under a dictionary and a sleeping cat. It says exactly what you hoped, in a footnote.|Three hours of climbing ladders for one paragraph. It's a very good paragraph.|You find the source and a better one beside it. You bring both back and share the second.}\nopt.right.say.1 [1030e2] = {你在最底层书架上找到了它，压在一本词典和一只睡着的猫下面。它说的正是你希望的，在一条脚注里。|爬了三个钟头梯子换来一段话。是非常好的一段话。|你找到了那份资料，旁边还有一份更好的。你把两份都带了回来，把第二份分给了对方。}\n\n@event thesis_is_it_good\n# en: Is It Any Good?\ntitle [cfa126] = 写得好吗？\n# en: {Halfway through rereading your thesis, you can't tell if it's brilliant or obvious.|The doubt has learned your timetable. It comes the morning after good work.|You know this doubt now. It reaches your carrel first.} {progress(thesis) >= 60: Most of it is written, which almost makes it worse.|So much of it is still unwritten.} {final_year and season == \"spring\": After this term there are no more terms.|{First-years laugh in the courtyard below.|Last night's candle is a puddle.|Someone two carrels down sighs the same sigh.}}\ntext [c8d24e] = {重读论文到一半，你分不清它是精彩还是平庸。|怀疑摸清了你的作息。它总在干得不错的一夜之后的早晨出现。|你认得这种怀疑了。它比你先到隔间。} {progress(thesis) >= 60: 大部分已经写完，这几乎让事情更糟。|还有太多没写。} {final_year and season == \"spring\": 这学期过后，就再也没有学期了。|{楼下庭院里一年级生在笑。|昨晚的蜡烛化成了一摊。|隔两个隔间的人叹出了一模一样的气。}}\n# en: A student at a carrel in early light, holding a thick draft at arm's length as if it might bite.\nscene [43c23c] = 清晨的隔间里，一个学生把厚厚的草稿举得远远的，好像它会咬人。\n# en: Push on regardless\nopt.left.label [b83a2c] = 不管了，继续写\n# en: {You write a page you don't believe in. By lunch you believe in about half of it, which is a start.|You keep going. The doubt keeps you company, and gets bored by evening.|You write through it, as you have before. By evening the doubt has gone quiet, and the page is good.}\nopt.left.say.1 [bf5167] = {你写了一页自己都不信的东西。到午饭时你信了大约一半，算是个开始。|你接着写。怀疑一直陪着你，到傍晚它自己也腻了。|你像以前一样写过去。到傍晚怀疑安静了，那一页也写得不错。}\n# en: Walk by the lake instead\nopt.right.label [ff9e77] = 改去湖边散步\n# en: {You walk around the lake and come back with no new pages and one good idea. You'll find out tomorrow if it's good.|The lake is mirror-still and has no opinion on your thesis. It's a relief.|You walk the long way round the lake. The doubt can't keep up, and you come back with a sentence that works.}\nopt.right.say.1 [fcedbe] = {你绕湖走了一圈，回来时没有新页，只有一个好主意。明天就知道它好不好了。|湖面如镜，对你的论文毫无意见。真是解脱。|你绕湖走了远路。怀疑跟不上你，你带着一句好用的句子回来了。}\n\n@event thesis_lab_spare_carrel\n# en: The Spare Carrel\ntitle [59d7e8] = 空着的隔间\n# en: {There's an empty carrel at the end of the row, with a candle stub and nobody's name on it.|The spare carrel is still unclaimed. You've started thinking of its candle stub as yours.|You know the carrels by their scratches now. The spare one has a new scratch, and you suspect it's yours.} {season == \"winter\": Everyone writes in fingerless gloves.|The windows are open, and a footnote blows off someone's desk.} {Somewhere a quill scratches without stopping.|Someone is whispering \"however\" over and over.|A candle gutters out three carrels down, and someone groans.}\ntext [434cc4] = {这排尽头有个空隔间，只有一截蜡烛头，没写任何人的名字。|那个空隔间还是没人认领。你开始把那截蜡烛头当成自己的了。|你现在能凭划痕认出每个隔间。空着的那个多了一道新划痕，你怀疑是你划的。} {season == \"winter\": 人人都戴着露指手套写字。|窗户开着，一条脚注从别人桌上飞走了。} {某处一支羽毛笔沙沙写个不停。|有人在一遍遍低声念着“然而”。|隔三个隔间的蜡烛灭了，有人哀叹了一声。}\n# en: A long room of wooden carrels, one at the end empty but for a candle stub and a stray page of footnotes.\nscene [9557dc] = 一长排木制研读隔间，尽头那个空着，只有一截蜡烛头和一页散落的脚注。\n# en: Work until the candle dies\nopt.left.label [6a43fb] = 写到蜡烛燃尽\n# en: {You write until the stub drowns in its own wax. Your notes are good, and your neck will remember them.|You read one book and half of another. The room's silence does half the work for you.|You work until the stub gives out and finish the paragraph by feel. It's better than the ones you could see.}\nopt.left.say.1 [244afc] = {你一直写到烛头淹没在自己的蜡油里。笔记很好，脖子会记住它们。|你读完一本书，又读了半本。屋里的安静替你干了一半的活。|你一直干到烛头熄灭，摸着黑写完了那一段。比看得见时写的那些还好。}\n# en: Just sit in the quiet\nopt.right.label [fbb3b9] = 只是在安静里坐坐\n# en: {You sit with your hands in your lap and listen to everyone else think. It's oddly restful.|You doze off with your cheek on someone's old footnotes. Nobody minds.|You sit in the quiet and the argument rearranges itself while you're not looking. You write it down before it changes its mind.}\nopt.right.say.1 [9e69a4] = {你双手放在膝上，听着别人思考。出奇地让人放松。|你脸贴着别人的旧脚注睡着了。没人介意。|你坐在安静里，论证趁你不注意自己理顺了。你赶在它改主意之前把它记了下来。}\n# en: {An advisor looks into the carrel and asks what you're working on. \"Thinking,\" you say. He writes that down.|A final-year asks if you'd mind the corner. Their own candle has given out.|An advisor finds you sitting still and lectures you on idleness for exactly as long as you'd have sat.}\nopt.right.say.2 [a0d748] = {一位导师探头问你在研究什么。“思考。”你说。他把这记了下来。|一位毕业年级生问你介不介意让出角落。他们自己的蜡烛燃尽了。|一位导师发现你坐着不动，就“游手好闲”给你训了一通话，时长正好等于你本打算坐的时间。}\n\n@event thesis_lab_footnote_war\n# en: The Footnote War\ntitle [e57e26] = 脚注之战\n# en: {Two advisors are arguing about a footnote across three carrels, and every final-year in the room has stopped pretending to work.|The footnote war has broken out again. Someone has drawn up sides on a scrap of paper.|By now the footnote war has rules, a referee and a running score chalked on the door.} {Today it's about whether an old founders' letter counts as a source.|Today it's about a comma.|Today it's about a footnote to a footnote.}\ntext [985478] = {两位导师隔着三个隔间为一条脚注争吵，屋里每个毕业年级生都不再假装干活了。|脚注之战又打响了。有人在纸片上分好了阵营。|如今脚注之战有了规则、裁判，门上还用粉笔记着比分。} {今天争的是创始者的一封旧信算不算出处。|今天争的是一个逗号。|今天争的是一条脚注的脚注。}\n# en: Two advisors arguing across a row of carrels, final-year students peering over the partitions to watch.\nscene [0da858] = 两位导师隔着一排隔间争吵，毕业年级生们从隔板上方探头观战。\n# en: Weigh in with a source\nopt.left.label [52a73e] = 拿出处加入战局\n# en: {You produce the source, open at the right page. Both advisors fall silent, and one of them asks for your name.|Your citation settles it. The loser shakes your hand and then rereads the page for an hour.|Your source wins the round. The referee chalks a point on the door under a new column, which has your name on it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [131400] = {你拿出那份资料，正翻在对的那一页。两位导师都安静了，其中一位问了你的名字。|你的引文终结了争论。输的一方和你握了手，然后把那一页重读了一个钟头。|你的资料赢下了这一回合。裁判在门上新开了一栏记上一分，栏头写着你的名字。}\n# en: {Your source turns out to be the one they were both arguing against. They unite, briefly, to explain why.|You cite the wrong edition. The room winces as one.|Your source is ruled out of order by the referee, on a technicality you'll be studying for weeks.}\nopt.left.say.2 [69773d] = {你的资料恰好是他们俩都在反对的那份。他们短暂地联手，向你解释为什么。|你引错了版本。全屋人齐齐一缩。|裁判以一条技术理由判你的资料无效，那条理由够你研究好几个星期。}\n# en: Keep my head down and listen\nopt.right.label [a9d2c8] = 低头听着\n# en: {You learn more about sources from ten minutes of the war than from a term of lectures, and you never have to say a word.|You take notes on both sides. Neither of them would like how fair your notes are.|You keep your head down and your notebook open. The war is the best seminar in the building, and it's free.}\nopt.right.say.1 [fa65d3] = {十分钟的战事教你的出处知识比一学期的课还多，而你一句话都不用说。|你把双方的论点都记了下来。他们谁都不会喜欢你记得这么公允。|你埋着头，开着笔记本。这场战争是整栋楼里最好的研讨课，还不收钱。}\n\n@event thesis_lab_borrowed_candle\n# en: Borrowing a Candle\ntitle [7f13b4] = 借一支蜡烛\n# en: {The candles here give out at the worst moment. Yours just has, and the carrel next door has a full box and nobody in it.|Your candle gives out again. Next door, the usual box of spares and nobody in the chair.|The lab's candles have outlasted no one's patience tonight, least of all yours. There's a box next door.} {A draft lies open beside the box, face up.|A note on the box says \"Help yourself\" in one hand and \"Don't\" in another.|Someone has drawn a small eye on the box.}\ntext [e34b72] = {这里的蜡烛总在最要命的时候燃尽。你的刚灭，隔壁隔间有一整盒，人却不在。|你的蜡烛又灭了。隔壁照例放着一盒备用的，椅子上没人。|今晚实验室的蜡烛没能比任何人的耐心撑得更久，尤其是你的。隔壁有一盒。} {盒子旁边摊着一份草稿，正面朝上。|盒子上一张字条写着“请自取”，另一种笔迹写着“别”。|有人在盒子上画了一只小眼睛。}\n# en: A dark carrel lit by one dying candle, a full box of candles on the empty desk next door.\nscene [a34fc6] = 一支将熄的蜡烛照着昏暗的隔间，隔壁空桌上放着满满一盒蜡烛。\n# en: Borrow one and keep working\nopt.left.label [178881] = 借一支接着干\n# en: {You light it and work until the bell. You leave a note that says \"IOU one candle\", unsigned.|One candle, two good hours. You put the stub back, which is either honest or worse.|You borrow one and replace it the next morning with two. The box owner has started leaving the lid open.}\nopt.left.say.1 [e699bf] = {你点上它一直干到钟响。你留了张字条：“欠蜡烛一支”，没署名。|一支蜡烛，两个好钟头。你把烛头放了回去，这要么算诚实，要么更糟。|你借了一支，第二天早上还了两支。盒子的主人开始把盖子开着了。}\n# en: {The owner comes back for a forgotten scarf and finds you lit by their candle. They say nothing, which is worse.|By morning the whole lab knows who borrowed the candle. Someone leaves a box on your desk, with a ribbon.|The box is counted now, and the count is short. A note appears asking the borrower to come forward. You don't.}\nopt.left.say.2 [fad0d1] = {主人回来取忘下的围巾，发现你正借着他们的烛光。他们什么也没说，这更糟。|到早上全实验室都知道是谁借了蜡烛。有人在你桌上放了一盒，还系了丝带。|那盒蜡烛现在有人数了，数目不对。一张字条出现了，请借蜡烛的人站出来。你没有。}\n# en: Go back in the dark\nopt.right.label [48d592] = 摸黑回去\n# en: {You walk back across the bridges in the dark, your argument going round and round. It is better by the time you reach your bed.|You stub your toe twice on the way out. The idea you had on the stairs was worth both.|You know the way back in the dark by now. The argument walks with you and arrives a step ahead.}\nopt.right.say.1 [330b06] = {你摸黑走过光之桥，论证在脑子里转个不停。走到床边时它已经好多了。|出门路上你踢到两次脚趾。楼梯上冒出的那个主意值这两下。|现在你摸黑也认得回去的路。论证陪你一路走着，比你早一步到。}\n\n@event intern_royal_library_slips\n# en: Request Slips\ntitle [75d659] = 借阅单\n# en: {Scholars upstairs send their requests down a brass chute. Your job is to fetch them from the stacks, which go down for miles.|You've worn a path in the dust of the third landing.|You can read the chute's rattle now. A long rattle means someone wants something from the very bottom.} {One slip is in a hand so old the ink has gone brown.|One slip asks for \"the blue one\".|One slip is a love letter, sent down by mistake.}\ntext [4e976f] = {楼上的学者把借阅单从黄铜滑槽送下来。你的工作是去书库里取书，而书库往下延伸好几里。|你在第三层平台的灰尘里踩出了一条路。|你现在听得懂滑槽的响声了。长长的一声，意味着有人要最底层的东西。} {有张单子的笔迹古老得墨水都变褐了。|有张单子写着“蓝色那本”。|有张单子是封情书，寄错了地方。}\n# en: A brass chute dropping paper slips into a basket at the top of a deep stairwell lined with shelves.\nscene [7b85db] = 一道黄铜滑槽把纸条投进篮子，篮子放在一口排满书架的深井楼梯顶上。\n# en: Take the deep requests\nopt.left.label [879821] = 接最深处的单子\n# en: {Four hundred steps down, the air is cold enough to keep books for ever. You come back up with the book and a lot of opinions about stairs.|You find the volume wedged behind a pipe. The scholar who asked for it nearly weeps.|You take the bottom slip without looking at it. Down there, you know the shelves better than the catalogue does.}\nopt.left.say.1 [b77c1b] = {往下四百级台阶，空气冷得书能永远保存。你带着那本书和一肚子对楼梯的意见回到上面。|你在一根管子后面找到了那本书。要它的学者差点哭了。|你看都不看就接了最底层的单子。在下面，你比目录更熟悉那些书架。}\n# en: Take the near shelves\nopt.right.label [ee4e7d] = 只取近处书架的\n# en: {You fetch the easy ones and leave the deep slips for someone else. Someone else notices.|You're fast and pleasant, and everyone knows you've never been below the third landing.|You clear the near shelves twice over. The long rattles go to someone else, who looks at you as they pass.}\nopt.right.say.1 [f0ab93] = {你取了容易的，把深处的单子留给别人。别人注意到了。|你又快又和气，可大家都知道你从没下过第三层平台。|你把近处的书架清了两遍。长响的单子归了别人，对方经过时看了你一眼。}\n\n@event intern_royal_library_mending\n# en: The Mending Table\ntitle [1b5cc4] = 修补台\n# en: {Today you're at the mending table, where crumbling records are put back together one flake at a time.|The binder hands you the tweezers without a word, which counts as a greeting.|The binder trusts you with the older pages now, which are the worst.} {The page is a royal decree on the correct length of goose necks.|The page is a recipe, half eaten by something.|The page is a list of names, one crossed out very hard.}\ntext [fe093e] = {今天你在修补台，把碎裂的档案一片一片拼回去。|装订师一言不发地把镊子递给你，这就算打招呼了。|装订师现在把更老的书页交给你了，那些最难。} {这页是一道王室法令，规定鹅脖子的正确长度。|这页是一份食谱，被什么东西啃掉了一半。|这页是一份名单，有一个名字被狠狠划掉了。}\n# en: A bright worktable under a lamp, a crumbling manuscript laid out in pieces and a student holding fine tweezers.\nscene [cb79c7] = 灯下一张明亮的工作台，一份碎裂的手稿摊成好几块，一个学生拿着细镊子。\n# en: Mend it myself\nopt.left.label [67b19a] = 我自己来修\n# en: {Every flake goes back where it belongs. The binder holds it up to the lamp and says, \"Acceptable,\" which here is a medal.|You save the page and read it while the glue dries. It's better than you expected.|You put the list back together, crossed-out name and all. The binder reads the name and says nothing for a while.}\nopt.left.say.1 [f4c658] = {每一片都回到了原处。装订师把它举到灯下说：“还行。”在这里，这就是勋章。|你救下了这一页，趁胶水干的时候读了它。比你想的有意思。|你把名单拼了回去，连那个被划掉的名字也拼好了。装订师读了那个名字，好一会儿没说话。}\n# en: {A sneeze. The binder looks at the scattered flakes and then at you. You both get the tweezers.|One flake ends up in the wrong century. Nobody can prove it was you, but everybody knows.|The oldest flake crumbles at your touch. The binder writes \"lost in repair\" in the ledger, in your hand.}\nopt.left.say.2 [a2111d] = {一个喷嚏。装订师看看散落的碎片，又看看你。你们俩一起拿起了镊子。|有一片被放进了错误的世纪。没人能证明是你，但人人都知道。|最老的一片一碰就碎了。装订师让你亲手在账簿上写下“修复中遗失”。}\n# en: Watch the binder work\nopt.right.label [5b7d5a] = 看装订师干活\n# en: {You watch for an hour and learn more than a book could tell you. The binder says you're slow. You were.|The binder works without speaking and you learn by watching. It's very peaceful and very little gets done.|You watch the binder mend the page you'd have ruined. Next time, you'll know how. That's the idea.}\nopt.right.say.1 [45e807] = {你看了一个钟头，学到的比书上能教的多。装订师说你慢。你确实慢。|装订师一言不发地干活，你在一旁看着学。非常宁静，也几乎没干成什么。|你看着装订师修好了那页你本会弄坏的纸。下次你就会了。这正是用意所在。}\n\n@event intern_conclave_test_chamber\n# en: The Test Chamber\ntitle [cb5a52] = 测试室\n# en: {The Conclave's researchers need someone to cast their newest spell for the first time. The walls are thick for a reason.|Someone has chalked a new circle on the floor. The old one is scorched.|You know every scorch mark in the chamber by now, and who made each one.} {Today's spell is meant to make a candle burn backwards.|Today's spell is meant to tidy a room. It has opinions about what tidy means.|Today's spell is unnamed. Nobody will say why.}\ntext [d61ad5] = {议会的研究员们需要有人第一次施放他们最新的咒语。墙那么厚是有原因的。|有人在地上用粉笔画了个新圈。旧的那个已经焦了。|如今测试室里每道焦痕你都认得，也知道是谁留下的。} {今天的咒语据说能让蜡烛倒着烧。|今天的咒语据说能整理房间。它对“整理”有自己的看法。|今天的咒语没有名字。没人肯说为什么。}\n# en: A circular chamber with scorch-marked walls, a student at the centre with a wand raised and researchers watching through thick glass.\nscene [033a72] = 一间墙上满是焦痕的圆形测试室，一个学生站在中央举着魔杖，研究员们隔着厚玻璃观看。\n# en: Step into the chamber\nopt.left.label [2de272] = 走进测试室\n# en: {The spell works on the first try. Behind the glass, someone writes your name in the margin of their notes.|It works, more or less, for one glorious minute before the spell remembers itself.|The spell works, and nobody behind the glass will say what it was for. They thank you very politely.}\nopt.left.say.1 [f386b1] = {咒语一次成功。玻璃后面有人在笔记页边写下了你的名字。|基本成功了，光辉的一分钟之后，咒语才想起自己是谁。|咒语成功了，玻璃后面没人肯说它是做什么用的。他们非常客气地向你道谢。}\n# en: {The spell goes off sideways. Your eyebrows are fine. Your fringe has views of its own now.|The spell goes off sideways, and your robes will never fold the same way again.|The spell goes off sideways and adds a new scorch mark to the wall. Someone labels it with your name.}\nopt.left.say.2 [218469] = {咒语往旁边炸开了。你的眉毛没事。你的刘海从此有了自己的主见。|咒语往旁边炸开了，你的袍子再也叠不回原来的样子。|咒语往旁边炸开了，在墙上添了一道新焦痕。有人在旁边标上了你的名字。}\n# en: Take readings behind the glass\nopt.right.label [531746] = 在玻璃后记读数\n# en: {You write down numbers for three hours while someone else gets to be brave. They're very good numbers.|You note every flicker. A senior researcher reads your page and asks for your notes next time.|You take the readings for a spell nobody will name. The numbers are strange, and you're asked to forget them.}\nopt.right.say.1 [b1070e] = {别人去当勇士，你记了三个钟头数字。都是非常好的数字。|你记下了每一次闪烁。一位资深研究员读了你的记录，要你下次把笔记给他。|你为一个没人肯说出名字的咒语记录读数。数字很奇怪，有人请你把它们忘掉。}\n\n@event intern_conclave_minutes\n# en: Taking the Minutes\ntitle [3271ff] = 做会议记录\n# en: {You've been asked to take the minutes of a Conclave research committee. There are nine members, and none of them ever agrees.|The committee is still arguing about last time's question, and now about the minutes too.|You could take these minutes in your sleep, and some of the committee do attend them that way.} {Two of them are not speaking to each other, and want it recorded.|One of them asks what you think.|One of them falls asleep, and wakes up to vote.}\ntext [eece1c] = {你被派去给议会的一个研究委员会做记录。委员有九位，从没有意见一致过。|委员会还在吵上次的问题，现在连上次的记录也一起吵。|这记录你闭着眼都能做，有几位委员也确实是闭着眼出席的。} {其中两位互不说话，并要求记录在案。|其中一位问你怎么看。|其中一位睡着了，醒来正好投票。}\n# en: A long table of elderly mages in heavy robes, all talking at once, a young intern writing furiously at the end.\nscene [998689] = 一张长桌旁坐满穿厚袍的年长法师，全都在同时说话，桌尾一个年轻实习生在拼命记录。\n# en: Offer an opinion when asked\nopt.left.label [48ef1a] = 被问到时说出看法\n# en: {The table goes silent. Then the oldest mage says, \"The intern is right,\" and the rest pretend they agreed all along.|Your opinion settles the argument. It also starts two new ones.|You give your opinion and the sleeper wakes up to agree with it. It carries, seven to two.}\nopt.left.say.1 [299c8d] = {桌边鸦雀无声。然后最年长的法师说：“实习生说得对。”其余人假装自己早就同意。|你的看法平息了争论。也引发了两场新的。|你说出了看法，睡着的那位醒来表示同意。七比二通过。}\n# en: {Nine heads turn towards you, slowly. You say something about wands. It isn't minuted.|You give your opinion and it is recorded in full, with the note \"(intern)\".|Your opinion is noted, discussed, voted down and then adopted next meeting without credit.}\nopt.left.say.2 [0d59e7] = {九颗脑袋慢慢转向你。你说了点关于魔杖的话。没被记录。|你的看法被完整记录在案，后面注着“（实习生）”。|你的看法被记录、讨论、否决，然后在下次会上被采纳，没提你的名字。}\n# en: Keep my head down and write\nopt.right.label [1678b2] = 低头只管记录\n# en: {Your minutes are perfect and so dull that nobody reads them, which in a committee is the highest praise.|You record every word of the argument. Your hand aches, and you understand the Conclave much better than you wanted to.|You write it all down, including the vote of the member who slept through it. The minutes are accurate, which nobody wanted.}\nopt.right.say.1 [bf8a20] = {你的记录完美无缺，枯燥到没人读，在委员会里这是最高的赞誉。|你记下了争论的每一个字。手腕发酸，对议会的了解远超你的本意。|你把一切都记了下来，包括那位睡了全程的委员的投票。记录很准确，可这正是谁都不想要的。}\n\n@event intern_alchemists_vat_watch\n# en: Vat Watch\ntitle [6ef11f] = 看守大缸\n# en: {Your task at the works is to watch one great vat and call the foreman if it changes colour.|You've brought your own colour chart now, with notes in the margins.|You and the vat understand each other now. It's an unsettling feeling.} {The amber is drifting, very slowly.|There's a bubble that keeps coming back, like a thought.|The vat smells faintly of cinnamon, which it never has before.} The foreman is all the way at the other end of the hall.\ntext [065445] = {你在工坊的任务是看守一口大缸，一旦变色就去叫工头。|你现在自带色卡，页边写满了笔记。|你和大缸现在心意相通。这感觉让人不安。} {琥珀色正在极慢地漂移。|有个气泡一直冒回来，像一个念头。|大缸有一丝肉桂味，以前从没有过。} 工头在大厅的另一头。\n# en: A huge copper vat in a steaming hall, a student on a gantry staring into it with a colour chart in hand.\nscene [e8908d] = 蒸汽弥漫的大厅里一口巨大的铜缸，一个学生站在吊台上拿着色卡盯着缸里。\n# en: Adjust it myself\nopt.left.label [76449d] = 我自己调\n# en: {A pinch of salt and a slow stir, and the vat settles back to its proper self. The foreman never knew, but the vat did.|You correct it before it goes wrong. When the foreman passes, he gives the vat a satisfied look as if he did it.|You trace the cinnamon to a cracked spice valve and close it. The vat settles, and the foreman finds the valve next week.}\nopt.left.say.1 [a41b7a] = {一撮盐，慢慢搅动，大缸恢复了本来的样子。工头永远不会知道，但大缸知道。|你在出事之前就纠正了。工头路过时满意地看了大缸一眼，好像是他干的。|你顺着肉桂味找到了一个裂开的香料阀，把它关上了。大缸平静下来，工头下个星期才发现那个阀门。}\n# en: {The vat turns a brilliant violet and hisses. The foreman arrives at a run, and you'll be hearing about it for weeks.|You add too much salt. The vat sulks a muddy brown until the foreman coaxes it back, at length, with you watching.|You make it worse. A lot worse. The whole hall smells of cinnamon until the end of the week.}\nopt.left.say.2 [816115] = {大缸变成鲜亮的紫罗兰色，嘶嘶作响。工头一路跑来，这事你要被念叨好几个星期。|你盐放多了。大缸赌气似的变成了泥褐色，工头哄了半天才把它哄回来，你在旁边看着。|你把它弄得更糟。糟得多。整座大厅到周末都是肉桂味。}\n# en: Run for the foreman\nopt.right.label [4b3ef4] = 跑去叫工头\n# en: {You run the length of the hall. The foreman fixes it in a second and explains how, at length, as you catch your breath.|By the time you're back with the foreman the colour has corrected itself. He stares at you, then at the vat.|You run for the foreman, who sniffs the air, says \"Cinnamon,\" and goes very pale. He fixes it, and doesn't explain.}\nopt.right.say.1 [ca9c22] = {你跑完整个大厅。工头一秒钟就修好了，趁你喘气时详详细细地讲了怎么修。|等你带着工头回来，颜色已经自己恢复了。他盯着你，又盯着大缸。|你跑去叫工头。他嗅了嗅空气，说了句“肉桂”，脸色发白。他修好了，没解释。}\n\n@event intern_alchemists_no_flame\n# en: The Rule About Flames\ntitle [a32af9] = 关于明火的规定\n# en: {There's a strict rule at the works against naked flames, and against any heating reaction the foreman hasn't approved. The break room has spent forty years looking for one he will.|Break time. The kettle sits on its warm stone, and the workers look at you hopefully.|By now the break room asks the academy intern first, whatever the problem.} {Today the warm stone has gone cold.|Today someone wants toast, which is a much bigger question.|Today a new inspector is touring the works.}\ntext [37d043] = {工坊有严格规定：不许明火，也不许用工头没批准的加热反应。休息室花了四十年，想找出一个他肯批的。|休息时间。水壶坐在温热的石头上，工人们满怀希望地看着你。|如今休息室不管遇到什么问题，都先问学院来的实习生。} {今天那块温石凉了。|今天有人想吃烤面包，这可是大得多的问题。|今天新来的巡查员在视察工坊。}\n# en: A break room in an alchemical works, workers gathered around a kettle sitting on a glowing stone that is not, technically, on fire.\nscene [03a5ac] = 炼金工坊的休息室，工人们围着一把放在发光石头上的水壶，严格说来那并不算着火。\n# en: Help brew the flameless tea\nopt.left.label [823819] = 帮忙泡无火茶\n# en: {You heat the kettle with a reaction you learned in your first year. The break room calls it \"the academy method\" and adopts it on the spot.|It works, though everything boiled on it comes out a little purple.|You brew the tea with the new inspector in the room, and explain the method so confidently that he takes notes.}\nopt.left.say.1 [b5e9da] = {你用一年级学的一个反应把水壶烧热了。休息室管它叫“学院法”，当场采用。|成功了，不过用它煮出来的东西都有点发紫。|新来的督察就在屋里，你照样煮茶，而且把方法讲得头头是道，他还记了笔记。}\n# en: {Your method works so well that someone in charge notices the steam from a reaction nobody approved. There's a meeting about it, with you in it.|The kettle boils, and so, briefly, does the table.|The inspector walks in mid-reaction. There's a form for it, and you fill it in.}\nopt.left.say.2 [634dfa] = {你的方法太好用了，有位管事的注意到了一个没人批准过的反应冒出的蒸汽。为此开了个会，你也在场。|水壶开了，桌子也短暂地开了。|反应进行到一半，督察走了进来。这种事有专门的表格，你填了。}\n# en: Stick to the rules\nopt.right.label [b9e488] = 遵守规定\n# en: {You drink cold water while everyone else drinks tea brewed on an unapproved reaction. The foreman notes your conduct, and the break room notes it too.|You remind everyone of the rule. You are absolutely right and nobody offers you a biscuit for the rest of the week.|You keep to the rules with the inspector watching. He commends you by name, and the break room never forgives it.}\nopt.right.say.1 [91b8e6] = {别人喝着用未经批准的反应煮的茶，你喝凉水。工头记下了你的表现，休息室也记下了。|你提醒大家注意规定。你完全正确，这一整周没人再分你饼干。|你当着督察的面守规矩。他点名表扬了你，休息室从此再没原谅你。}\n\n@event intern_expedition_thin_air\n# en: Up the Ridge\ntitle [23ba3c] = 登上山脊\n# en: {The instruments have to go up to the ridge before dark, and the air on the plateau is very thin.|Your legs know the path now. Your lungs are still negotiating.|You've carried things up this ridge so often that the goats have stopped looking at you.} {season == \"winter\": {Snow has drifted against the path.|The wind cuts through every layer you own.|Ice has glazed the steps cut into the rock.}|{The rocks are still hot from the afternoon.|The sky is clear all the way to the top of the world.|The evening wind smells of thyme and stone.}}\ntext [77ea0c] = {天黑前仪器必须运上山脊，而高原上的空气非常稀薄。|腿已经认得路了。肺还在讨价还价。|你往这道山脊上搬过太多次东西，山羊都不再看你了。} {season == \"winter\": {雪堆在了路边。|风穿透你身上的每一层衣服。|凿在岩石上的台阶结了一层冰。}|{石头还留着下午的热。|天空晴朗，一直晴到世界的顶端。|傍晚的风里有百里香和石头的气味。}}\n# en: A line of expedition members climbing a rocky ridge at dusk, carrying brass instruments wrapped in blankets.\nscene [00b5b6] = 黄昏时一队远征队员攀上岩石山脊，抱着裹在毯子里的黄铜仪器。\n# en: Carry the great lens\nopt.left.label [e7e4f2] = 扛那块大透镜\n# en: {You carry the great lens to the top without dropping it, then sit very still for a long time. The leader nods at you, once.|Your arms are numb by the top, and the lens is perfect. It's worth it when the first star shows in it.|You carry the lens up without thinking about it. Nobody watches you any more, which is the compliment.}\nopt.left.say.1 [a354cc] = {你把大透镜扛到山顶，一点没摔，然后一动不动地坐了很久。领队朝你点了一下头。|到山顶时你的胳膊已经麻了，透镜完好无损。当第一颗星出现在镜中，一切都值了。|你想都没想就把透镜扛了上去。已经没人再盯着你了，这就是夸奖。}\n# en: Do the sums in the tent\nopt.right.label [d9b374] = 在帐篷里算数\n# en: {You work the night's sums by lamplight, warm and quiet. The lamp gutters twice. Your sums don't.|You have the charts ready when they come down. Nobody thanks you, because the charts are always ready.|You do the sums in the tent, as usual. The numbers are good, and so is the stove.}\nopt.right.say.1 [624053] = {你借着灯光算当晚的数，温暖又安静。灯芯跳了两下，你的算式一下也没跳。|他们下来时星图已经准备好了。没人道谢，因为星图总是准备好的。|你照常在帐篷里算数。数字不错，炉子也不错。}\n\n@event intern_expedition_chart_dispute\n# en: A Star Out of Place\ntitle [dbb002] = 错位的星\n# en: {Your chart and the senior astronomer's disagree about where one faint star is.|The senior astronomer's charts and yours have a running disagreement now, kept in red ink.|The senior astronomer now checks your charts first. A compliment, possibly.} {Probably your hand shook.|Probably their eyesight.|Probably the thin air.} {season == \"winter\": The nights are long, with time to recheck everything.|The nights are short, and every hour counts.}\ntext [2abc0a] = {你的星图和资深天文学家的星图，在一颗暗星的位置上不一致。|资深天文学家的星图和你的现在有一场持续的分歧，用红墨水记着。|资深天文学家现在先查你的星图。也许算是夸奖。} {大概是你的手抖了。|大概是他们的眼神。|大概是稀薄的空气。} {season == \"winter\": 夜很长，有时间把一切都复核一遍。|夜很短，每个钟头都要紧。}\n# en: Two expedition members bent over a star chart by lamplight, one pointing firmly at a dot.\nscene [576570] = 两名远征队员借着灯光俯身看一张星图，其中一人坚定地指着一个点。\n# en: Recheck it all night\nopt.left.label [19c9f0] = 通宵复核\n# en: {You recheck it until dawn. You were right by a hair, and nobody will ever care but you.|You were wrong by a hair. You fix it, and know the sky better than you did.|You recheck by lamplight and find the air was bending the star. You note it, and the astronomer copies your note.}\nopt.left.say.1 [f7e30b] = {你一直核对到天亮。你对了，差之毫厘，除了你没人会在乎。|你错了，差之毫厘。你改了过来，对星空的了解又多了一点。|你借着灯光复核，发现是空气让那颗星偏了位。你记了下来，天文学家抄走了你的笔记。}\n# en: Raise it with the astronomer\nopt.right.label [8f17a5] = 向天文学家提出来\n# en: {The astronomer checks, grumbles and corrects their chart. Then they buy you a cup of the camp's terrible coffee.|\"Well spotted,\" says the astronomer, through their teeth.|You raise it, and the astronomer has already checked it. \"You first, this time,\" they say.}\nopt.right.say.1 [e54689] = {天文学家查了一遍，嘟囔着改了自己的星图。然后请你喝了一杯营地里难喝的咖啡。|“眼尖。”天文学家咬着牙说。|你提了出来，天文学家早已核对过。“这次你先来。”对方说。}\n# en: {The astronomer checks and shows you, in front of everyone, exactly where you went wrong.|They don't check. They just look at you over their spectacles until you go away.|The astronomer blames the thin air and refuses to discuss it further. The star stays where they put it.}\nopt.right.say.2 [1426da] = {天文学家查了，当着所有人的面给你指出哪里错了。|他们没查。只是从眼镜上方看着你，直到你走开。|天文学家把错推给稀薄的空气，拒绝再谈。那颗星就待在对方画的位置。}\n\n@event intern_field_station_count\n# en: The Dawn Count\ntitle [c272a9] = 黎明清点\n# en: {Every morning someone has to lie in the heather and count what's living on the moor. Today it's you.|You've learned which tussock is the least wet, and you guard it.|You're the station's best counter now, mostly because you've stopped minding the damp.} {season == \"winter\": {Snow hares are out, white on white.|Frost has made the heather crackle under you.|A fox is counting the same hares.}|{The skylarks are going up one after another.|The midges have found you.|The heather is warm, and so is your tussock.}}\ntext [2ab961] = {每天早上都得有人趴在石楠丛里，清点荒原上活着的东西。今天轮到你。|你摸清了哪个草丛最不湿，并且守着它。|你现在是野外站最好的清点员，主要是因为你不再在乎潮湿了。} {season == \"winter\": {雪兔出来了，白上加白。|霜让石楠在你身下咔咔作响。|一只狐狸在数同一群野兔。}|{云雀一只接一只飞上天。|蠓虫找到你了。|石楠是暖的，你那块草墩也是。}}\n# en: A student lying flat in the heather at dawn, a tally board in hand, watching nesting towers on the moor.\nscene [94a0ee] = 黎明时一个学生平趴在石楠丛中，手拿计数板，望着荒原上的筑巢塔。\n# en: Count every last one\nopt.left.label [1a8fb1] = 一只不落地数\n# en: {You count until your fingers are numb and the tally is perfect. The warden reads it twice and pins it on the wall.|You stay out until the very last nest is counted. You come back soaked and very proud of the number.|You count them all from your tussock. The warden copies your tally into the station book without checking it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [6946d4] = {你数到手指发麻，记录完美无缺。站长读了两遍，钉在了墙上。|你一直待到最后一个巢数完。回来时浑身湿透，为这个数字非常骄傲。|你在你的草墩上把它们全数完了。站长没核对就把你的记录抄进了站志。}\n# en: Make a sensible estimate\nopt.right.label [db92d7] = 合理估一个数\n# en: {You mark it \"estimated\" in the margin, the way the station counts the far nests anyway. The warden accepts it, and you spend the extra hour by the stove with a cup of tea.|The warden checks your estimate against his own count and nods. He does the same on wet mornings.|Your estimate is close enough that the warden uses it for the far nests too. You get the stove first.}\nopt.right.say.1 [994847] = {你在页边注明“估算”，反正站里数远处的巢也是这么数的。站长认了，省下的那个钟头你在炉边喝了杯茶。|站长拿你的估算和他自己的数对了对，点点头。下雨的早上，他也是这么干的。|你的估算很接近，站长连远处的巢也用了它。你先占到了炉子。}\n# en: {The warden counted too. His number is not your number. He makes a note.|Your estimate includes a flock that left last week. The warden is very nice about it, which is worse.|Your estimate misses a new colony on the far slope. The warden found it yesterday and was waiting to see if you would.}\nopt.right.say.2 [60dffb] = {站长也数了。他的数不是你的数。他记了一笔。|你的估计里包括了一群上周就飞走的鸟。站长对此非常客气，这更糟。|你的估算漏掉了远坡上一个新的鸟群。站长昨天就发现了，一直在等着看你会不会发现。}\n\n@event intern_field_station_patient\n# en: The Patient in Paddock Three\ntitle [813ec2] = 三号围场的病号\n# en: {The station's latest patient is a moor-drake with a splinted wing and no gratitude at all.|Feeding time in paddock three. The patient sees you coming and lowers its head.|The drake in paddock three recognises you now. This hasn't made it any nicer.} {It has eaten a glove.|It is pretending to sleep, which it does very badly.|It has learned to open the bucket by itself.} {season == \"winter\": Its breath steams in the frosty air.|It is lying in the one patch of shade.}\ntext [a5113d] = {野外站最新的病号是一头荒原幼龙，翅膀上着夹板，毫无感恩之心。|三号围场喂食时间。病号看见你来，低下了头。|三号围场的幼龙现在认得你了。这并没让它变得友善。} {它吃了一只手套。|它在装睡，装得很差。|它学会了自己打开桶。} {season == \"winter\": 它的呼吸在霜冷的空气里冒着白气。|它躺在唯一一块阴凉里。}\n# en: A muddy paddock with a splinted creature glaring from a straw nest, a student holding out a bucket at arm's length.\nscene [ddabad] = 泥泞的围场里一头上着夹板的生物在草窝里瞪着眼，一个学生伸长胳膊递出一只桶。\n# en: Feed it by hand\nopt.left.label [cc2f18] = 亲手喂它\n# en: {It takes the food from your hand, very gently. Then it bites the bucket, to make a point.|It lets you check the splint, grumbling the whole time like an old man at the barber's.|It eats from your hand and then opens the bucket to check you haven't kept any back.}\nopt.left.say.1 [c27074] = {它从你手里非常轻地叼走食物。然后咬了一口桶，以示立场。|它让你检查了夹板，全程嘟嘟囔囔，像理发店里的老头。|它从你手里吃完，又自己打开桶，确认你没私藏。}\n# en: {It takes the food, a bit of your sleeve and a bruise's worth of your arm.|It butts you into the mud and then looks pleased with itself.|It opens the bucket itself and eats the lot, then bites you for being slow.}\nopt.left.say.2 [69facb] = {它叼走了食物、一截你的袖子，还在你胳膊上留了一块淤青。|它把你拱进泥里，然后一脸得意。|它自己打开桶吃了个精光，然后因为你太慢咬了你一口。}\n# en: Use the long tongs\nopt.right.label [cd0b4c] = 用长夹子喂\n# en: {You feed it from a safe distance. It eats, and looks at you with deep contempt.|The tongs work perfectly. You feel you've let the patient down, somehow.|It takes the tongs off you and drops them in the mud. You feed it by hand after all.}\nopt.right.say.1 [06f014] = {你隔着安全距离喂它。它吃了，用深深的鄙夷看着你。|夹子好用极了。可你总觉得自己辜负了这位病号。|它把夹子从你手里夺走，丢进了泥里。你最后还是用手喂了它。}\n\n@event intern_thornwick_pastries\n# en: Thornwick Hospitality\ntitle [5e9c3e] = 荆木的待客之道\n# en: {Each morning your Thornwick hosts leave pastries on your desk, with a polite note.|The notes have grown longer and politer, which is worrying.|The pastries are a game now, and both sides know it.} {They look perfect, which in an illusion lab is a warning.|One is steaming slightly, which it shouldn't be.|Today there's one plain biscuit, which is somehow worse.} {season == \"winter\": The pastries look very warm.|The icing hasn't melted in the heat.}\ntext [5adad2] = {每天早上，荆木的东道主都在你桌上放一盘点心，附一张客气的字条。|字条越写越长、越写越客气，这让人担心。|点心现在成了一场游戏，双方都心知肚明。} {它们看起来完美无缺，在幻术实验室这就是警告。|有一块在微微冒热气，它不该冒的。|今天只有一块普通饼干，不知怎么更可疑了。} {season == \"winter\": 点心看起来很暖。|天这么热，糖霜却没化。}\n# en: A polished laboratory table at a rival academy, a plate of perfect pastries set out for a visiting student.\nscene [7a642b] = 对手学院一张光洁的实验桌上，一盘完美的点心摆在来访学生面前。\n# en: Eat one and smile\nopt.left.label [bb9a84] = 吃一块，再笑一笑\n# en: {It's real, and very good. The Thornwick interns look almost disappointed.|It's real. You eat it slowly, in front of everyone, and thank them warmly.|You eat the plain biscuit with every sign of delight. Nobody in the lab can tell if it was real, including you.}\nopt.left.say.1 [95343d] = {是真的，而且非常好吃。荆木的实习生看起来几乎有点失望。|是真的。你当着所有人的面慢慢吃完，热情地道谢。|你满脸欣喜地吃下了那块素饼干。实验室里没人知道它是不是真的，包括你。}\n# en: {It's a glamour over something dry and joyless. You eat the whole thing with a smile, and the lab respects that.|It tastes of nothing at all. You compliment the baker, and someone in the corner laughs.|The biscuit turns to air in your mouth. You keep chewing anyway, and the lab gives you a small round of applause.}\nopt.left.say.2 [452496] = {是套在某种又干又无趣的东西上的魅影。你笑着吃完了整块，实验室对此肃然起敬。|它什么味道都没有。你夸了夸烘焙师，角落里有人笑出了声。|饼干在你嘴里化成了空气。你还是照嚼不误，实验室为你鼓了一小阵掌。}\n# en: Bring my own breakfast\nopt.right.label [34615e] = 自带早饭\n# en: {You eat your own bread, very pointedly. The Thornwick students write something down.|You leave the plate untouched. Your supervisor here calls it \"a very Lumenhold choice\", and doesn't mean it nicely.|You eat your own bread and offer them some. They check it for glamours, which is the friendliest thing they've done.}\nopt.right.say.1 [d95cb8] = {你意味深长地吃着自己的面包。荆木的学生记了点什么。|你没碰那盘点心。这里的导师说这是“非常流明堡的选择”，不是好话。|你吃着自己的面包，还分给他们一些。他们查了查有没有魅影，这是他们做过最友好的事。}\n\n@event intern_thornwick_rivalry\n# en: The Old Grudge\ntitle [7f876b] = 老恩怨\n# en: {The Thornwick students have questions about Lumenhold. Most of them are about the last time the two schools met on the duelling field.|The Thornwick students have found a new angle on the old grudge.|You and the Thornwick students have a routine now. It's almost friendly.} {Today they want to know if Lumenhold's towers still lean.|Today they ask, sweetly, if Lumenhold teaches illusion yet.|Today one of them pretends to be you, very well.}\ntext [ffc4d0] = {荆木的学生对流明堡有些问题。大多是关于两校上一次在决斗场上交手的事。|荆木的学生为这桩老恩怨找到了新角度。|你和荆木的学生现在有了固定节目。几乎算友好。} {今天他们想知道流明堡的塔是不是还歪着。|今天他们甜甜地问，流明堡是不是终于开幻术课了。|今天他们中有人扮成了你，扮得非常像。}\n# en: A group of rival students around a lab bench, one of them leaning in with a pointed smile towards a visitor in different robes.\nscene [07d8c1] = 一群对手学院的学生围在实验台边，其中一个朝穿着不同长袍的访客探过身去，笑得意味深长。\n# en: Out-charm them\nopt.left.label [a1eb25] = 用魅力压过他们\n# en: {You answer every barb with a better one. By lunch they're laughing with you rather than at you.|You make the room laugh at their joke, and then at them. Their supervisor smiles behind her notes.|You do a better impression of them than they did of you. The lab agrees, and so, grudgingly, do they.}\nopt.left.say.1 [476851] = {每句挖苦你都回以更妙的一句。到午饭时，他们是在跟你一起笑，而不是笑你。|你让满屋人先笑他们的笑话，再笑他们。他们的导师在笔记后面露出微笑。|你学他们学得比他们学你还像。全实验室都同意，他们也不情不愿地同意了。}\n# en: {You try to be witty. It comes out as a speech about the founders. They let you finish, kindly.|You lose the exchange so badly that one of them takes a bow on your behalf.|Their impression of you is so good that you forget your answer. They finish it for you, in your voice.}\nopt.left.say.2 [8aa82a] = {你想说点俏皮话，结果说成了一篇关于创始者的演讲。他们好心地让你讲完了。|你输得太惨，他们中有人替你鞠了一躬。|他们学你学得太像了，你把要说的话都忘了。他们用你的声音替你说完了。}\n# en: Let it slide\nopt.right.label [344baa] = 随它去\n# en: {You smile and go back to work. Your supervisor here notices, and approves, and you feel like you lost anyway.|You say nothing. It takes them all morning to realise you've won.|You let it go, as usual. They seem almost disappointed, and one of them saves you a seat at lunch.}\nopt.right.say.1 [bf66ed] = {你笑笑，回去干活。这里的导师注意到了，也赞许，可你还是觉得自己输了。|你什么也没说。他们花了一上午才意识到你赢了。|你照常没接茬。他们看起来几乎有点失望，其中一个午饭时给你留了座。}\n\n@event intern_unseen_office_coffee\n# en: The Coffee Machine\ntitle [9f1fbd] = 咖啡机\n# en: {Nobody at the Office talks about anything, except the coffee machine. It's very good.|A queue forms around the machine each evening, though nobody seems to join it.|The coffee queue, you've learned, is where the Office's real work gets done.} {Two people beside you discuss the weather in a way that isn't about the weather.|A cup has a name on it that was there yesterday, under another name.|The machine hisses, and everyone falls silent until it stops.}\ntext [82c4dc] = {署里没人谈论任何事，除了咖啡机。它非常好用。|每晚咖啡机周围都会排起队，却没人看起来是去排队的。|你已经明白，咖啡队伍才是署里真正干活的地方。} {你旁边两个人在聊天气，聊法显然不是在聊天气。|一只杯子上写着一个名字，昨天它也在，写的是另一个名字。|机器一嘶响，所有人都停下话头，直到它响完。}\n# en: A plain room with no windows and a gleaming coffee machine, quiet people in plain coats standing near it, not quite talking.\nscene [2a796e] = 一间没有窗户的朴素房间，一台锃亮的咖啡机，几个穿着普通外套的安静的人站在旁边，不太算是在交谈。\n# en: Linger at the machine\nopt.left.label [a4aa91] = 在咖啡机边多待会儿\n# en: {You learn three things nobody told you, and one thing nobody knows you know.|You drink your coffee slowly and listen. By the second cup you understand what nobody is saying.|You stand in the queue long enough to be part of it. Someone tells you something, and you understand it was meant for you.}\nopt.left.say.1 [f0d88c] = {你知道了三件没人告诉你的事，还有一件没人知道你知道的事。|你慢慢喝着咖啡听着。到第二杯，你听懂了没人说出口的东西。|你在队伍里站得够久，成了它的一部分。有人跟你说了件事，你明白那是说给你听的。}\n# en: {Someone notices you listening and asks, very kindly, how your day is going. You don't sleep that night.|You overhear something you very much wish you hadn't. It can't be unheard.|The machine hisses mid-sentence and the room goes silent. When it stops, everyone is looking at you.}\nopt.left.say.2 [006b70] = {有人发现你在听，非常和气地问你今天过得怎样。那晚你没睡着。|你无意中听到一件真希望没听到的事。听到了就忘不掉了。|机器在话说到一半时嘶嘶作响，屋里一片寂静。等它停下，所有人都在看着你。}\n# en: Take my cup back to my desk\nopt.right.label [f05b97] = 端回自己桌上\n# en: {You work through the evening with your coffee. The work is dull and your supervisor approves, which is a little chilling.|Your desk is very quiet. It's the most anyone at the Office will say about you, and here that's high praise.|You take your cup to your desk. When you come back for the next one, the queue has saved your place.}\nopt.right.say.1 [d9c64f] = {你就着咖啡工作了一晚。活很枯燥，导师很满意，这有点让人发冷。|你的桌子非常安静。这是署里任何人对你说过的最多的话，在这里这是高度赞扬。|你把杯子端回了桌上。等你回来续杯时，队伍给你留着位置。}\n\n@event intern_unseen_office_wrong_file\n# en: The Wrong File\ntitle [a461ed] = 错放的卷宗\n# en: {A file has been left on your desk. It isn't yours, and it isn't labelled.|A second unlabelled file. You're starting to think it's a test.|The unlabelled files keep arriving. You've stopped asking where they come from.} This one is tied shut with a red ribbon. {It's very thin.|It's very thick, with a coffee ring on the cover.|The knot is one you don't recognise.}\ntext [d1b2bf] = {有份卷宗被留在你桌上。不是你的，也没有标签。|第二份没有标签的卷宗。你开始觉得这是一场考验。|没有标签的卷宗不停地送来。你已经不再问它们从哪来。} 这份用红丝带系着。{它非常薄。|它非常厚，封面上有个咖啡杯印。|那个结你从没见过。}\n# en: A plain folder with no label lying on an empty desk under a single lamp.\nscene [395e41] = 一盏孤灯下的空桌上，放着一个没有标签的朴素文件夹。\n# en: Read it before returning it\nopt.left.label [6c5a88] = 先读再归还\n# en: {You read it, retie the ribbon perfectly and hand it in. Your supervisor checks the knot and smiles, very slightly.|You read every page. It's about a man who sells hats. Nothing has ever made you more nervous.|You read it, retie the unfamiliar knot from memory and hand it in. Your supervisor checks it twice and then teaches you a new one.}\nopt.left.say.1 [b5d200] = {你读完了，把丝带系得一模一样，交了回去。导师检查了那个结，非常轻微地笑了笑。|你读了每一页。讲的是一个卖帽子的人。从没有什么让你这么紧张过。|你读完了，凭记忆系回那个陌生的结，交了回去。导师检查了两遍，然后教了你一个新的。}\n# en: {Your supervisor takes the file back and glances at the ribbon. \"Interesting knot,\" she says.|You put the pages back in the wrong order. Somehow, everyone knows by morning.|You can't retie the knot. You hand it in with an ordinary bow, and your supervisor looks at it for a long time.}\nopt.left.say.2 [bfe2f7] = {导师拿回卷宗，瞥了一眼丝带。“有意思的结。”她说。|你把书页放回去时顺序错了。不知怎么，第二天早上人人都知道了。|你系不回那个结。你系了个普通的蝴蝶结交上去，导师盯着它看了很久。}\n# en: Return it unopened\nopt.right.label [ab3e46] = 原封不动地归还\n# en: {You hand it back without looking. Your supervisor says nothing, which here is approval. You'll wonder about it for weeks.|You return it with the ribbon untouched. The next day there's a slightly better coffee waiting on your desk.|You return it unopened, as always. The files stop coming for a week, and then there are two.}\nopt.right.say.1 [dc565a] = {你看都没看就交了回去。导师什么也没说，在这里这就是认可。你会为此琢磨好几个星期。|你把丝带原样未动地还了回去。第二天你桌上多了一杯稍好一点的咖啡。|你照旧原封不动地还了回去。卷宗停了一个星期，然后一下来了两份。}\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/pools/renown.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/pools/renown.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event renown_chance\n# en: A Chance to Be Noticed\ntitle [41fb8e] = 被注意到的机会\n# en: {A gold-bordered notice in the entrance hall says the academy wants volunteers.|Another gold notice, and the crowd around it is bigger this time.|The gold notice again. People glance at you as they read it now.} {Whoever stands up will be seen by the whole school.|The village is invited.|There's a prize, and there's the shame of losing in public.}\ntext [4b3bb1] = {门厅里一张镶金边的告示说，学院在征集志愿者。|又一张金边告示，这次围观的人更多了。|又是金边告示。现在人们读它时会瞟你一眼。} {站出来的人会被全校看到。|村里人也受邀了。|有奖品，也有当众失败的羞耻。}\n# en: A noticeboard in the entrance hall crowded with announcements, one gold-bordered notice pinned dead centre, students jostling to read it.\nscene [947222] = 门厅的告示板上挤满了通知，正中央钉着一张镶金边的告示，学生们挤着去看。\n# en: Cast a spell before the school\nopt.demonstrate.label [0e28c7] = 在全校面前施一道咒语\n# en: Arcana, hard. Fame or embarrassment.\nopt.demonstrate.hint [b4213a] = 奥术，困难。出名或出丑。\n# en: You write your name on the list before you can think better of it.\nopt.demonstrate.say.1 [d4ad55] = 你没来得及多想，就把名字写在了名单上。\n# en: Enter the great debate\nopt.debate.label [c11160] = 参加大辩论\n# en: Lore, hard. The faculty will be listening.\nopt.debate.hint [c454bd] = 学识，困难。教职员会在台下听着。\n# en: You sign up for the debate. Your hands are shaking already.\nopt.debate.say.1 [43d367] = 你报名参加了辩论。手已经在抖了。\n# en: Perform at the village fair\nopt.fair.label [54524e] = 在村里的集市上表演\n# en: Charm and a spell. The village pays and remembers.\nopt.fair.hint [e9a0c5] = 魅力加一道咒语。村里人会付钱，也会记住你。\n# en: You put your name down for the village fair. Somebody will have to teach you to bow.\nopt.fair.say.1 [86c4a5] = 你报名参加了村里的集市。得有人教你怎么鞠躬。\n# en: Keep your head down\nopt.pass.label [b59315] = 低调行事\n# en: {You read the notice and walk on. Fame can find someone else this term.|Not this time. You've got enough to do without an audience.|People glance at you as you walk past the notice. Let them. You'd rather not lose in public this term.}\nopt.pass.say.1 [e991f8] = {你读了告示，走开了。这学期，让名声去找别人吧。|这次算了。不需要观众，你要做的事已经够多了。|你从告示前走过时，有人朝你瞥了一眼。随他们去。这学期你可不想当众出丑。}\n\n@event renown_demonstration\n# en: Before the Whole School\ntitle [edcef6] = 在全校面前\n# en: The whole school is in the Great Hall, and so is the faculty, and so, at the end, is the headmistress. You're in the middle of the floor. Your spell is the most ambitious thing you know. There's a long silence while everyone waits for you to begin.\ntext [91116c] = 全校都在大礼堂里，教职员也在，最后连院长也来了。你站在场地正中。你的咒语是你会的最有野心的东西。所有人都在等你开始，一片长长的寂静。\n# en: The Great Hall packed to the rafters, every house at its table, a single student standing in the middle of the floor with a wand raised.\nscene [923d71] = 大礼堂挤得水泄不通，各学院坐在各自的长桌旁，一个学生独自站在场地正中，举着魔杖。\n# en: Cast the hardest thing you know\nopt.left.label [4a80f8] = 施展你会的最难的咒语\n# en: It works, beautifully, better than ever before. The hall is silent and then very loud. For a month, first-years point at you in corridors.\nopt.left.say.1 [649754] = 它成功了，美得惊人，比以往任何一次都好。礼堂先是一片寂静，然后震耳欲聋。整整一个月，一年级生在走廊里对你指指点点。\n# en: It fizzles in front of everyone, with a noise like a sad goose. The hall is very kind about it, which is unbearable. You'll be \"the goose\" until someone else fails.\nopt.left.say.2 [1eb78c] = 它当着所有人的面熄了火，发出一声像伤心的鹅一样的声音。礼堂里的人对此非常体贴，这才叫人受不了。在别人出丑之前，你都会是“那只鹅”。\n# en: Cast something safe, perfectly\nopt.right.label [09f177] = 施一道稳妥的咒语，完美无缺\n# en: Something simple, done flawlessly. Polite applause. Nobody will remember it, and nobody will laugh at you either.\nopt.right.say.1 [492d38] = 简单的咒语，施得毫无瑕疵。礼貌的掌声。没人会记得，也没人会笑话你。\n\n@event renown_debate\n# en: The Great Debate\ntitle [b747aa] = 大辩论\n# en: The great debate: \"That magic should be taught to anyone who asks.\" You've drawn the side you disagree with, against a fourth-year who has never lost. The faculty are in the front row. Thorne is taking notes.\ntext [055fb1] = 大辩论的辩题：“魔法应当传授给任何求学的人。”你抽到了自己不赞同的那一方，对手是一个从没输过的四年级生。教职员坐在第一排。索恩教授在做笔记。\n# en: Two lecterns on a stage under banners, a student at one pointing emphatically, an older student at the other frowning, a packed hall of faces in the dark.\nscene [deb5fb] = 旗帜下的舞台上有两个讲台，一个学生在一边激动地指点着，另一边一个年长的学生皱着眉，台下黑暗中坐满了人。\n# en: Argue your side brilliantly anyway\nopt.left.label [b2d090] = 照样把你那方辩得精彩\n# en: You argue the side you don't believe so well that you half-believe it. The fourth-year concedes. Thorne, afterwards, says \"Hm,\" twice, which has never happened.\nopt.left.say.1 [93af97] = 你把自己不信的一方辩得太好，好到自己都信了一半。四年级生认输了。事后，索恩教授说了两次“嗯”，这从来没发生过。\n# en: The fourth-year takes you apart, politely and completely. The hall applauds you for trying, which is the cruellest kind of applause.\nopt.left.say.2 [709e30] = 四年级生客客气气、彻彻底底地把你驳倒了。全场为你的努力鼓掌，这是最残忍的一种掌声。\n# en: Argue what you actually believe\nopt.right.label [fa097f] = 说出你真正相信的\n# en: You throw away the brief and argue your own side, which is against the rules. You lose on points and win the hall. Thorne writes something down, and you'll never know what.\nopt.right.say.1 [76c0d1] = 你扔掉了辩稿，为自己的立场辩护，这是违反规则的。你按积分输了，却赢得了全场。索恩教授写下了什么，你永远不会知道写的是什么。\n\n@event renown_village_fair\n# en: Magic at the Fair\ntitle [ba2f28] = 集市上的魔法\n# en: The village fair has a stage, bunting and a crowd of farmers who have seen every trick there is. The village apprentices sit at the front, cross-legged, unimpressed in advance.\ntext [7d86f8] = 村里的集市有一个舞台、彩旗，还有一群见过所有把戏的农夫。村里的学徒们盘腿坐在最前面，还没开始就一脸不以为然。\n# en: A small stage at a village fair under bunting, a student conjuring coloured lights over a crowd of apprentices and farmers.\nscene [ce56eb] = 村集市彩旗下的一个小舞台，一个学生在一群学徒和农夫头顶变出彩色的光。\n# en: Put on a real show\nopt.left.label [a47725] = 来一场真正的表演\n# en: Lights, a dancing hat, a dove that turns into a shower of petals. The apprentices whoop. The farmers pay for the hat trick twice. The village talks about it until harvest.\nopt.left.say.1 [90cf71] = 彩光、一顶会跳舞的帽子、一只变成花瓣雨的鸽子。学徒们欢呼起来。农夫们为帽子戏法付了两次钱。全村一直谈论到收获季。\n# en: The dove refuses to turn into anything and sits on your head. The apprentices love it; the farmers don't pay.\nopt.left.say.2 [5b1927] = 鸽子拒绝变成任何东西，就蹲在你头上。学徒们很喜欢；农夫们没付钱。\n# en: Do card tricks for the front row\nopt.right.label [7a00f1] = 给前排变纸牌戏法\n# en: You sit on the edge of the stage and do card tricks for the apprentices until your voice gives out. No fame to speak of. Half of them walk you to the gate.\nopt.right.say.1 [45b3b7] = 你坐在舞台边上给学徒们变纸牌戏法，一直变到嗓子哑了。谈不上什么名声。有一半人一路送你到门口。\n\n@event renown_ice_rescue\n# en: A First-Year on the Ice\ntitle [36cb5d] = 冰上的一年级生\n# en: A first-year has gone through the ice on the lake. A crowd is shouting on the shore, and nobody is moving. You're nearest.\ntext [89d72c] = 一个一年级生掉进了湖里的冰窟窿。岸上一群人在喊，没人动。你离得最近。\n# en: A small figure clinging to the edge of a broken patch of ice in the middle of a frozen lake, students shouting on the shore, a student running out across the ice.\nscene [cb70d0] = 结冰的湖中央，一个小小的身影扒着一块碎冰的边缘，岸上的学生在喊，一个学生正冲上冰面。\n# en: Go out on the ice\nopt.left.label [4c1b80] = 到冰上去\n# en: You crawl out flat on the ice and pull them free. By supper the whole school knows your name, and the first-year's mother writes you a letter you keep for ever.\nopt.left.say.1 [e4cca1] = 你平趴在冰面上爬过去，把 TA 拉了出来。到晚饭时全校都知道了你的名字，那个一年级生的母亲给你写了一封信，你一直留着。\n# en: The ice gives under you too. The prefects pull you both out with a ladder. You spend a week in the infirmary, a little famous, mostly frozen.\nopt.left.say.2 [8de0f6] = 冰在你身下也裂了。级长们用梯子把你们俩拖了上来。你在医务室躺了一个星期，小有名气，主要是冻坏了。\n# en: Run for the prefects\nopt.right.label [770692] = 跑去找级长\n# en: You run for help, and the prefects get there with ladders in time. Someone else is the hero. You were right, and you feel wrong about it all winter.\nopt.right.say.1 [2c0c95] = 你跑去求救，级长们及时带着梯子赶到。英雄是别人。你做得对，可你一整个冬天都觉得不对劲。\n\n@event renown_portrait\n# en: A Portrait for the Hall\ntitle [316b46] = 礼堂里的肖像\n# en: The academy hangs a portrait of its most talked-about students in the long gallery each year, and someone has suggested you. The painter's fee is yours to pay, as is the custom. The other portraits are already watching you.\ntext [1c2fbd] = 学院每年会把最受热议的学生的肖像挂在长廊里，有人提名了你。按惯例，画师的酬金由你来付。其他肖像已经在看着你了。\n# en: A painter's easel in a sunlit corridor, a half-finished portrait of a student in formal robes, the sitter trying very hard not to scratch their nose.\nscene [136a0d] = 阳光明媚的走廊里立着一个画架，上面是一幅画了一半的穿礼袍的学生肖像，坐着的人正拼命忍住不去挠鼻子。\n# en: Sit for it\nopt.left.label [e61125] = 去当模特\n# en: Six sittings, an itchy collar and a painter who hums. The portrait is good, if a little more heroic than you are. Passing it, first-years straighten their backs.\nopt.left.say.1 [41686f] = 画了六次，领子扎人，画师一直在哼歌。肖像画得很好，只是比你本人英勇了一点。一年级生从它面前经过时，都会挺直腰板。\n# en: Decline politely\nopt.right.label [c5820d] = 礼貌地谢绝\n# en: You decline. Your purse is grateful. The portrait that goes up instead is of someone who looks very pleased with themselves, and you find you don't mind at all.\nopt.right.say.1 [1dcbbd] = 你谢绝了。你的钱袋很感激。挂上去的是另一个人的肖像，看起来对自己非常满意，而你发现自己一点也不介意。\n\n@event renown_rumour\n# en: A Rumour About You\ntitle [e03f40] = 关于你的流言\n# en: Being known has a price: there's a rumour about you going round the castle, untrue and a little bit funny, and it's in Nessa's gossip column by Friday. People stop talking when you walk in.\ntext [d8f51f] = 出名是有代价的：一则关于你的流言在城堡里传开了，不是真的，还有点好笑，星期五之前就上了内莎的八卦专栏。你一走进来，大家就不说话了。\n# en: Two students whispering behind their hands in a corridor, a third walking past them with a stiff back, a crumpled gossip sheet on the floor.\nscene [c72b51] = 走廊里两个学生捂着嘴窃窃私语，第三个学生挺直脊背从他们身边走过，地上丢着一张揉皱的八卦小报。\n# en: Answer it in public\nopt.left.label [2a7890] = 公开回应\n# en: You laugh it off at supper, loudly and well, with a better story of your own. By Monday everyone's repeating yours instead.\nopt.left.say.1 [6fad96] = 晚饭时你一笑置之，笑得响亮又漂亮，还讲了个更好的故事。到星期一，大家传的都是你那个了。\n# en: You deny it too hard, and it sounds true. The rumour grows a second chapter. It'll pass. It takes a month.\nopt.left.say.2 [de5cf2] = 你否认得太用力，听起来倒像真的。流言又长出了第二章。它会过去的。过了一个月。\n# en: Ignore it\nopt.right.label [e2ae0c] = 不理它\n# en: You say nothing. It burns for a fortnight and then dies of boredom. You learn who repeated it, and remember.\nopt.right.say.1 [e7e80e] = 你什么也没说。它热闹了两个星期，然后无聊死了。你知道了是谁在传，并记住了。\n\n@event renown_patron_dinner\n# en: Dinner With a Patron\ntitle [939524] = 与资助人共进晚餐\n# en: A wealthy patron of the academy has asked to meet \"the students everyone's talking about\", and you're one of them. Dinner is nine courses. The patron likes clever talk and has been known to fund a promising student's whole final year.\ntext [773d2a] = 学院的一位富有的资助人想见见“大家都在谈论的学生”，你就是其中之一。晚餐有九道菜。这位资助人喜欢机智的谈话，据说曾资助过一个有前途的学生整个最后一年的学业。\n# en: A long candlelit dining table in a grand townhouse, a student in borrowed formal robes seated between a wealthy patron and a portrait of the patron.\nscene [de7bac] = 一座豪华宅邸里长长的烛光餐桌，一个穿着借来的礼袍的学生坐在一位富有的资助人和资助人的画像之间。\n# en: Be dazzling\nopt.left.label [166f5f] = 光彩照人\n# en: You're witty through eight courses and wise on the ninth. The patron sends you home with a purse \"for books\" and an invitation to come back next year.\nopt.left.say.1 [00008d] = 前八道菜你妙语连珠，第九道时又显得睿智。资助人送你回去时塞给你一袋钱“买书用”，还邀请你明年再来。\n# en: You use the wrong fork, then the wrong joke. The patron is kind and bored. You're not asked back.\nopt.left.say.2 [0f38a6] = 你用错了叉子，然后讲错了笑话。资助人很和善，也很无聊。你没有再被邀请。\n# en: Be honest about your struggles\nopt.right.label [8dbca1] = 坦诚说出你的难处\n# en: You talk plainly about money and work and exams. The patron listens, the only one at the table who does. A small purse arrives the next week with no letter, just a crest.\nopt.right.say.1 [11822a] = 你坦白地谈起钱、工作和考试。资助人在听，是整张桌上唯一在听的人。下个星期寄来了一小袋钱，没有信，只有一枚纹章。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/pools/spell_lessons.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/pools/spell_lessons.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event lesson_flame\n# en: Putting Fire Out\ntitle [dc5132] = 把火灭掉\n# en: Flame is yours now. Professor Thorne gathers the year's new Flame casters in the stone practice room and lights a brazier. \"Any fool can start a fire,\" he says. \"You are here to learn to stop one.\" There is a bucket of water at his feet, and he forbids you to touch it.\ntext [490b3a] = 火焰咒如今归你了。索恩教授把这一年新学会火焰咒的人召集到石头练习室，点燃一只火盆。“任何蠢货都能生火，”他说，“你们来这儿，是学怎么把火停下。”他脚边有一桶水，他不许你碰。\n# en: A stone practice room with scorched walls, a stern grey-bearded professor beside a roaring brazier, a bucket of water at his feet.\nscene [dd2300] = 四壁焦黑的石头练习室，一位严厉的灰胡子教授站在熊熊燃烧的火盆旁，脚边一桶水。\n# en: Pull the fire back into your hand\nopt.left.label [4451c4] = 把火收回手里\n# en: You call the flame back like a sleeve pulled over a wrist. It stings, and the brazier flares and sinks. \"Again,\" says Thorne. He says it eleven times.\nopt.left.say.1 [c20bd6] = 你把火焰召回，就像把袖子拉回手腕。手被灼得生疼，火盆窜起又落下。“再来，”索恩说。他说了十一遍。\n# en: Ask what the bucket is for\nopt.right.label [f6e8aa] = 问那桶水是干什么的\n# en: \"For the student who doesn't ask,\" says Thorne, and empties it over the brazier of the boy beside you. Then he explains, for an hour, how every great fire he has known began.\nopt.right.say.1 [933d3a] = “给不提问的学生用的，”索恩说着，把整桶水浇在你旁边那个男生的火盆上。然后他花了一个钟头，讲他见过的每一场大火是怎么烧起来的。\n\n@event lesson_tide\n# en: Waist-Deep at Dawn\ntitle [82cbf9] = 黎明时齐腰深\n# en: Professor Marisol teaches Tide where it lives: waist-deep in the lake at dawn. \"Cold water,\" she says happily, \"is good for everyone.\" She recites a poem about waves and stops before the last line, frowning. The lake waits for you.\ntext [7c2f23] = 玛丽索尔教授在潮涌咒住的地方教它：黎明时，湖里齐腰深的水中。“冷水，”她高兴地说，“对谁都有好处。”她背了一首关于海浪的诗，在最后一行前停住，皱起眉头。湖水在等你。\n# en: A misty lake at dawn, a professor standing waist-deep in the water with arms raised, students hesitating on the pebbled shore.\nscene [93a9d4] = 黎明时雾气蒙蒙的湖，一位教授站在齐腰深的水里高举双臂，学生们在卵石岸边犹豫不前。\n# en: Wade in and call a wave\nopt.left.label [103d24] = 蹚进水里唤一道浪\n# en: The water is so cold it feels like an opinion. Your wave rises knee-high and slaps Marisol full in the chest, and she whoops. \"The last line!\" she shouts. \"It was about this!\"\nopt.left.say.1 [10fb6d] = 水冷得像一种观点。你唤起的浪只有膝盖高，正正拍在玛丽索尔胸口，她欢呼起来。“最后一行！”她喊，“写的就是这个！”\n# en: Call it from the shore\nopt.right.label [a49270] = 在岸上施咒\n# en: You work the spell from the dry pebbles and raise a neat little swell. Marisol looks at your dry boots with real sorrow, as if at a poem with no ending.\nopt.right.say.1 [0ca97b] = 你站在干燥的卵石上施咒，掀起一道整整齐齐的小浪。玛丽索尔看着你干爽的靴子，真心实意地难过，像在看一首没有结尾的诗。\n\n@event lesson_foresight\n# en: The Bottom of the Cup\ntitle [d6ff5b] = 杯底\n# en: The astromancy lecturer, who wears three scarves in every season, sits the class at the observatory with cups of tea and no instructions. Foresight, it turns out, begins at the bottom of the cup: what you think you see, and what you want to.\ntext [4e84c1] = 那位一年四季围三条围巾的星象讲师，让全班坐在观星台里，每人一杯茶，什么也不讲。原来，预见始于杯底：你以为自己看见了什么，和你想看见什么。\n# en: An observatory room with tea cups on every desk, a lecturer in three scarves peering into a student's cup with a magnifying glass.\nscene [abb57c] = 观星台的房间里每张桌上都放着茶杯，一位围着三条围巾的讲师拿放大镜窥看学生的杯子。\n# en: Read your own cup\nopt.left.label [130c28] = 读你自己的杯子\n# en: The leaves say a hard week is coming. It does, from Tuesday. The lecturer says that's the trouble with reading your own: you start making it true.\nopt.left.say.1 [fdc255] = 茶叶说，艰难的一周就要来了。从周二开始，果然来了。讲师说，读自己的杯子麻烦就在这儿：你会开始让它成真。\n# en: Read your neighbour's\nopt.right.label [661301] = 读邻座的杯子\n# en: You tell your neighbour a letter is on its way. A letter arrives at supper. Now half the dormitory wants their leaves read, and you have to learn to say \"unclear\" kindly.\nopt.right.say.1 [15b70b] = 你告诉邻座，有封信在路上。晚饭时信就到了。现在半个宿舍都想让你读茶叶，你只好学会温和地说“看不清”。\n\n@event lesson_starlight\n# en: A Jar on the Roof\ntitle [346e45] = 屋顶上的罐子\n# en: Starlight is taught after midnight on the observatory roof, where the astromancy lecturer hands you a glass jar and tells you to hold it up \"until something falls in\". The stars are very far away. The roof is very cold.\ntext [ee2fcf] = 星光咒是午夜后在观星台屋顶上教的。星象讲师递给你一只玻璃罐，让你举着，“直到有东西掉进去”。星星非常远。屋顶非常冷。\n# en: A flat observatory roof at night, a student holding up a glass jar toward a sky crowded with stars, breath steaming.\nscene [64cb32] = 夜里平坦的观星台屋顶，一个学生朝满天繁星举起玻璃罐，呼出的气冒着白雾。\n# en: Hold the jar until it fills\nopt.left.label [72681d] = 举着罐子，直到装满\n# en: At three in the morning a thread of light settles in the jar like milk in tea. You carry it down the stairs in both hands. You can't feel your fingers until breakfast.\nopt.left.say.1 [12b784] = 凌晨三点，一缕光沉进罐子里，像牛奶落进茶里。你双手捧着它下楼。直到早饭时，你的手指都没有知觉。\n# en: Lie back and watch the sky\nopt.right.label [3f4130] = 躺下看星空\n# en: You lie on the roof and name the constellations with whoever is beside you. Your jar stays empty. The lecturer, passing, says that's the first lesson too, and the one most people skip.\nopt.right.say.1 [ad51ae] = 你躺在屋顶上，和身边的人一起数星座。你的罐子一直空着。讲师路过时说，这也是第一课，而且是大多数人跳过的那一课。\n\n@event lesson_mend\n# en: Teacups First\ntitle [606e4e] = 先修茶杯\n# en: Sister Amaranth teaches Mend with teacups. She drops one on the infirmary floor without looking. \"Mend it.\" When it's whole she drops another. \"Bones are harder,\" she says, rolling bandages. \"Cups first.\"\ntext [bd4c39] = 阿玛兰斯修女用茶杯教愈合咒。她看都不看就把一只茶杯摔在医务室地上。“修好它。”修好了，她又摔一只。“骨头更难，”她一边卷绷带一边说，“先修杯子。”\n# en: An infirmary storeroom, a stern healer dropping a teacup to the stone floor while a student kneels over the pieces with a wand.\nscene [32aa56] = 医务室的储藏间，一位严厉的医者把茶杯摔在石地上，一个学生拿着魔杖跪在碎片旁。\n# en: Mend cup after cup\nopt.left.label [0314eb] = 一只接一只地修\n# en: By the twentieth cup you can hear the crack close before you see it. Amaranth says \"Keep doing that,\" and drops a teapot.\nopt.left.say.1 [0628fd] = 修到第二十只，你还没看见裂缝合上，就先听见了。阿玛兰斯说“继续这样做”，然后摔了一只茶壶。\n# en: Ask her about the bones\nopt.right.label [4f800c] = 问她骨头的事\n# en: She tells you, still rolling bandages, about the worst break she ever set. It was a student's; that student is a professor now. You go a little pale, and listen to every word.\nopt.right.say.1 [37dcc5] = 她一边卷绷带，一边给你讲她接过的最糟的一次骨折。那是个学生的；那个学生现在当了教授。你脸色有点发白，但一字不落地听完了。\n\n@event lesson_purify\n# en: Make It Water\ntitle [1817e7] = 把它变成水\n# en: Purify is taught over a cauldron of something green that Sister Amaranth will not name. \"Make it water,\" she says, and goes back to her own work. That is the whole of the instruction. The smell is a kind of instruction too.\ntext [cf22c3] = 净化咒是在一锅绿色东西上教的，阿玛兰斯修女不肯说那是什么。“把它变成水，”她说完就回去忙自己的了。这就是全部的指导。那股气味也算一种指导。\n# en: An alchemy lab bench with a cauldron of thick green liquid, a healer standing over a student who holds a wand above it.\nscene [77f705] = 炼金实验室的工作台上一锅浓稠的绿色液体，一位医者站在举着魔杖的学生身边。\n# en: Purify it layer by layer\nopt.left.label [9b8dfa] = 一层一层地净化\n# en: Slowly, layer by layer, the green turns grey, then cloudy, then clear. You breathe the fumes for two hours and get a headache for one. Amaranth tastes the result, nods and pours it into the kettle.\nopt.left.say.1 [e3259c] = 慢慢地，一层一层，绿色变成灰色，再变浑，再变清。你吸了两个钟头的烟气，头疼了一个钟头。阿玛兰斯尝了尝成果，点点头，倒进了水壶。\n# en: Ask what it was\nopt.right.label [210c34] = 问那是什么\n# en: \"Last term's soup,\" she says. You decide not to believe her, then decide you do. She shows you the trick of it while you laugh, and you purify half a cup, badly.\nopt.right.say.1 [08c946] = “上学期的汤，”她说。你决定不信，然后又决定信。你笑的时候她把诀窍教给了你，你净化了半杯，净化得很糟。\n\n@event lesson_glamour\n# en: Pick a Card\ntitle [760613] = 抽一张牌\n# en: Professor Vey's glamour lesson is a card game. Each student glamours a plain card to look like the queen of stars, and Vey picks out the true queen from the pile. Nobody, she says, has ever made her pick wrong. \"Fool me, and you may skip the essay.\"\ntext [952f53] = 维伊教授的魅影课是一场纸牌游戏。每个学生把一张普通的牌伪装成星辰皇后，维伊从一堆牌里挑出真正的皇后。她说，从来没人让她挑错过。“骗过我，就可以免交论文。”\n# en: A velvet-curtained classroom, a professor in a violet coat fanning out a hand of playing cards toward a row of students.\nscene [dce887] = 挂着天鹅绒帘子的教室，一位穿紫罗兰色外套的教授把一手扑克牌扇形展开，朝向一排学生。\n# en: Glamour your card\nopt.left.label [0c0fb9] = 给你的牌施魅影\n# en: Vey picks your card, turns it over and finds a plain two. She laughs out loud, a real laugh, and the class goes silent. \"Well,\" she says. \"Well, well. No essay for you.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [d0cb32] = 维伊挑中你的牌，翻过来，是一张普通的二。她放声大笑，真心的笑，全班鸦雀无声。“好，”她说，“好，好啊。你不用交论文了。”\n# en: Vey passes over your card without a glance. \"The queen of stars,\" she says kindly, \"does not blush.\"\nopt.left.say.2 [e75a0c] = 维伊看都没看就略过了你的牌。“星辰皇后，”她和气地说，“是不会脸红的。”\n# en: Watch how she catches them\nopt.right.label [be78bf] = 看她怎么识破\n# en: You watch her eyes instead of the cards. She never looks at the faces, only at the hands holding them. You write that down, and she sees you write it, and smiles.\nopt.right.say.1 [1511f9] = 你不看牌，看她的眼睛。她从不看牌面，只看拿牌的手。你把这点记下来，她看见你在记，笑了。\n\n@event lesson_mirror\n# en: Gently\ntitle [99b2bc] = 温柔些\n# en: Professor Vey teaches Mirror in an empty classroom with the curtains drawn, which is not like her. \"A mirror charm shows people themselves,\" she says. \"Do it gently. Most people have not asked to see.\" She stands to one side of every glass in the room.\ntext [0cc1d8] = 维伊教授在一间拉上窗帘的空教室里教镜像咒，这不像她。“镜像咒让人看见自己，”她说，“要温柔些。大多数人没要求看。”教室里每一面镜子，她都站在旁边避开。\n# en: A dim classroom with the curtains drawn, a hand mirror on every desk, a professor standing well to one side of the glass.\nscene [927d45] = 拉上窗帘的昏暗教室，每张桌上一面手镜，一位教授远远地站在镜子侧面。\n# en: Practise on yourself first\nopt.left.label [8a2034] = 先在自己身上练\n# en: The charm shows you yourself a moment too honestly. You practise until you can look at it without flinching. Mostly.\nopt.left.say.1 [f32110] = 咒语让你看见的自己，诚实得过了一点。你一直练到能看着它不躲闪。差不多吧。\n# en: Ask why she avoids the glass\nopt.right.label [42aa1b] = 问她为什么躲开镜子\n# en: \"Vanity,\" says Vey, with a magnificent smile that answers nothing at all. But she lets you stay after the others leave, and shows you how to soften the charm's edges.\nopt.right.say.1 [ffe355] = “虚荣，”维伊说，露出一个华丽的笑容，什么也没回答。不过别人走后她让你留下，教你怎么把咒语的锋芒磨软一些。\n\n@event lesson_haste\n# en: Race the Hippogriff\ntitle [8af651] = 和鹰头马身有翼兽赛跑\n# en: Professor Brask teaches Haste on the flight field, with a stopwatch and a hippogriff that is faster than the spell. \"Beat her,\" she says to you. To the hippogriff she explains, at length and politely, that she is not to bite you if you win.\ntext [165778] = 布拉斯克教授在飞行场教疾速咒，拿着一块秒表，还有一头比咒语还快的鹰头马身有翼兽。“赢过她，”她对你说。然后她又对那头兽客客气气、详详细细地解释：你要是赢了，不许咬你。\n# en: A windy flight field marked with flags, a grey-haired professor holding a stopwatch, a student sprinting beside a hippogriff.\nscene [57e5ef] = 插着旗子的飞行场上风很大，一位灰发教授拿着秒表，一个学生在鹰头马身有翼兽旁边飞奔。\n# en: Race the hippogriff\nopt.left.label [691071] = 和它赛跑\n# en: You cross the flag a beak's length ahead. The hippogriff looks at Brask; Brask looks at the stopwatch, then at you. \"Hm,\" she says. From her, it's a medal.\nopt.left.say.1 [d51c8e] = 你领先一个喙的长度冲过旗子。那头兽看着布拉斯克；布拉斯克看着秒表，又看看你。“嗯，”她说。从她嘴里出来，这就是一枚奖章。\n# en: The hippogriff wins by a length and takes your hat as her prize. Brask tells a story about a student who lost to a goose. It ends with a bite. \"Moral: faster.\"\nopt.left.say.2 [4beef4] = 那头兽领先一个身位赢了，还把你的帽子叼走当奖品。布拉斯克讲了个学生输给一只鹅的故事。结尾是被咬了一口。“寓意：快点。”\n# en: Time the others first\nopt.right.label [f5b3cc] = 先给别人计时\n# en: You hold the stopwatch and watch the fast ones. They aren't running harder; they start the spell a step before they need it. Brask sees you notice, and hands you the next race.\nopt.right.say.1 [38d0ad] = 你拿着秒表看跑得快的人。他们并没有跑得更用力；他们在需要之前一步就起咒了。布拉斯克看出你注意到了，把下一场比赛交给了你。\n\n@event lesson_beast_speech\n# en: The Mule Has Views\ntitle [ec4453] = 骡子有看法\n# en: Beast-speech, says Professor Brask, is mostly listening. She sits you in a stall beside an old mule who has, she says, \"views\". You are to hear them. The mule looks at you as if you're the one being taught.\ntext [460ee4] = 布拉斯克教授说，兽语大部分是倾听。她让你坐在一个畜栏里，旁边是一头老骡子，她说它“有看法”。你得听出来。骡子看着你，好像你才是来受教的那个。\n# en: A warm stall with straw on the floor, a student sitting cross-legged opposite a grey old mule who stares back without blinking.\nscene [13f579] = 铺着稻草的温暖畜栏，一个学生盘腿坐着，对面一头灰色老骡子一眨不眨地回瞪。\n# en: Sit with him all afternoon\nopt.left.label [32ae78] = 陪它坐一下午\n# en: In the fourth hour the mule's thoughts come through: mostly apples, and one long grievance about a farrier. Brask says \"Good,\" to the mule, not to you.\nopt.left.say.1 [b8cda1] = 第四个钟头，骡子的想法传了过来：大多是苹果，还有一大段对某个钉马掌的人的抱怨。布拉斯克说“好”，是对骡子说的，不是对你。\n# en: Try the spell straight away\nopt.right.label [59b22b] = 直接施咒\n# en: You cast before you've listened and get one word back, very clearly: \"Rude.\" Brask laughs once, loudly. It's the laugh of the term, and everyone in the stables heard it.\nopt.right.say.1 [9b7054] = 你还没听就施了咒，清清楚楚地收到一个词：“无礼。”布拉斯克大笑了一声。这是本学期的那一声笑，整个马厩都听见了。\n\n@event lesson_ward\n# en: Chalk\ntitle [e38b98] = 粉笔\n# en: At the top of the lecture tower, where the lamps burn dim on purpose, the umbral lecturer teaches Ward by throwing chalk. Hard, and without warning. \"A ward that only works when you're ready,\" says the lecturer, \"is a decoration.\"\ntext [dbcf81] = 讲堂塔顶上，灯故意点得很暗，暗影讲师教护界的方法是扔粉笔。扔得很狠，毫无预兆。“只在你准备好时才管用的护界，”讲师说，“是装饰品。”\n# en: A dim room at the top of a tower, low lamps, a lecturer mid-throw with a piece of chalk while students hold up shimmering wards.\nscene [066bb6] = 塔顶昏暗的房间，灯光低垂，一位讲师正扔出一截粉笔，学生们举着微微发光的护界。\n# en: Hold the ward all hour\nopt.left.label [2d1be2] = 撑住护界一整节课\n# en: The first chalk hits your forehead. The fifth bounces off the ward with a sound like a bell. By the end of the hour your ward is up before you know you've raised it.\nopt.left.say.1 [c53b86] = 第一截粉笔砸中你的额头。第五截从护界上弹开，响得像一声钟。到下课时，你的护界在你意识到之前就已经立起来了。\n# en: Watch the lecturer's wrist\nopt.right.label [965178] = 盯着讲师的手腕\n# en: You let the ward drop and watch how the throws begin. Three pieces of chalk find your forehead while you do. But you see it now: the shoulder moves first.\nopt.right.say.1 [2c86ff] = 你放下护界，观察每一次投掷是怎么开始的。这期间有三截粉笔砸中了你的额头。但你看出来了：先动的是肩膀。\n\n@event lesson_shadowstep\n# en: Where the Voice Was\ntitle [9b35f0] = 声音在的地方\n# en: Shadowstep is taught in the dark. The umbral lecturer puts out the last dim lamp and calls your name from somewhere else in the room. You have to arrive where the voice was before it moves. Somewhere, a chair is waiting for your shins.\ntext [05e14f] = 影步是在黑暗里教的。暗影讲师熄灭最后一盏暗灯，从房间别处叫你的名字。你得在声音移开之前，到达它刚才所在的地方。某处有一把椅子，正等着你的小腿。\n# en: A pitch-dark tower room with one dim lamp guttering out, the outline of a student stepping between deep shadows.\nscene [877abd] = 漆黑的塔中房间，一盏暗灯即将熄灭，一个学生的轮廓正踏入深深的阴影之间。\n# en: Step into the dark\nopt.left.label [2680ac] = 踏进黑暗\n# en: You step, and the dark folds, and you're across the room with a bruise from the chair. The voice is right beside you. \"Better,\" it says, and moves away again.\nopt.left.say.1 [25eb2c] = 你迈出一步，黑暗折叠起来，你已到了房间另一头，腿上被椅子撞出一块青。声音就在你身边。“好一点了，”它说，又移开了。\n# en: Wait for your eyes to adjust\nopt.right.label [565cb0] = 等眼睛适应\n# en: You stand still until the dark has edges. The lecturer, somewhere, says that's cheating, and that cheating is half of umbral magic. You step only once, but you step exactly.\nopt.right.say.1 [98d714] = 你一动不动，直到黑暗有了边缘。讲师在某处说这是作弊，而作弊是暗影魔法的一半。你只迈了一步，但这一步分毫不差。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/pools/statuses.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/pools/statuses.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event hungry_leftovers\n# en: The Refectory's Leftovers\ntitle [dbc5bf] = 膳堂的剩饭\n# en: {The refectory is closing, and the kitchens are scraping the pots. A cook sees you hovering in the doorway and holds up a ladle, eyebrows raised.|The same cook, the same ladle. She doesn't ask any more; she just raises her eyebrows.|You know the kitchens' closing time to the minute now.} {A prefect is still at the end table, finishing an essay.|Two first-years are sweeping, and watching.|The cook is humming, which means there's plenty.}\ntext [8bcb19] = {膳堂快关门了，厨房在刮锅底。一个厨娘看见你在门口徘徊，举起汤勺，扬了扬眉毛。|还是那个厨娘，还是那把汤勺。她不再问了，只扬扬眉毛。|如今你对厨房关门的时间精确到分钟。}{尽头的桌子边还坐着一个级长，在赶论文。|两个一年级生在扫地，一边扫一边看。|厨娘在哼歌，这说明还剩不少。}\n# en: A refectory kitchen door at closing time, a cook scraping the last of a stew into a bowl for a student waiting in the doorway.\nscene [8b6728] = 关门时分膳堂的厨房门口，厨娘把最后一点炖菜刮进碗里，递给等在门边的学生。\n# en: Take the bowl\nopt.left.label [629c3f] = 接过那碗\n# en: {It's the burnt bit at the bottom of the stew and it is the best thing you've ever eaten. The prefect looks up from her essay and remembers your face.|The cook fills the bowl to the brim. The first-years see, and by breakfast half your year knows why.|You know the closing time to the minute, and so does the cook. She fills the bowl before you're through the door, humming.}\nopt.left.say.1 [043db7] = {是炖菜锅底烧焦的那层，却是你吃过最好吃的东西。级长从论文上抬起头，记住了你的脸。|厨娘把碗盛得满满的。一年级生们看见了，到早饭时，你们年级一半人都知道为什么。|关门时间你算得分秒不差，厨娘也是。你还没进门，她就哼着歌把碗盛满了。}\n# en: Shake your head and leave\nopt.right.label [d3d37c] = 摇摇头走开\n# en: {\"Not hungry,\" you say, to nobody in particular, and go upstairs on an empty stomach that disagrees.|You walk out with your pride and nothing else. Your stomach complains all night.|You know the closing time, and leave a minute before it. The humming follows you up the stairs, and so does the smell.}\nopt.right.say.1 [013365] = {“不饿，”你对着空气说，空着肚子上了楼，肚子不同意。|你带着自尊离开，别的什么也没带。你的肚子抱怨了一整夜。|你知道关门时间，提前一分钟走了。哼歌声跟着你上了楼梯，香味也是。}\n\n@event hungry_pocketed_rolls\n# en: Rolls for Later\ntitle [e2a96a] = 留着以后吃的面包\n# en: {The refectory's bread rolls are piled high. Board-payers only, says the sign; it doesn't say how many.|The rolls again, and the sign again, and your empty pockets.|Another meal. You've got good at looking as if you've paid.} {The prefect on duty is reading a letter.|The prefect on duty is looking straight at the bread.|The prefect on duty has gone for more tea.}\ntext [12b52e] = {食堂的面包卷堆得高高的。告示写着只供交了食宿费的人；没写能拿几个。|又是面包卷，又是那块告示，又是你空空的口袋。|又一顿饭。你已经很会装作交过钱的样子了。}{值班的级长在读信。|值班的级长正盯着面包。|值班的级长去续茶了。}\n# en: A breakfast table heaped with bread rolls, a student's hand hovering near a pocket while a prefect patrols the aisle.\nscene [5e16e9] = 堆满面包卷的早餐桌，一个学生的手在口袋边犹豫，一个级长在过道里巡视。\n# en: Pocket a few for later\nopt.left.label [daa943] = 揣几个留着\n# en: {Three rolls in your robe, still warm. They last a day, and so does the guilt.|You fill your pockets and walk out at an even pace, crumbs and all.|Two rolls and an apple. Nobody stops you. You eat them on the stairs, a little ashamed.}\nopt.left.say.1 [b89f46] = {三个面包卷揣进袍子里，还是热的。它们撑了一整天，内疚也是。|你塞满口袋，不紧不慢地走出去，连面包屑一起。|两个面包卷加一个苹果。没人拦你。你坐在楼梯上吃完，有点羞愧。}\n# en: Eat what's on your plate\nopt.right.label [7782d7] = 只吃盘里的\n# en: {One roll, eaten slowly, and nothing taken that isn't yours. By the next meal you're light-headed and honest.|You eat what you're given, and the hunger keeps you company for hours.|One roll, slowly, with the look of someone who has paid. The hunger knows better.}\nopt.right.say.1 [4f010d] = {一个面包卷，慢慢吃，不拿不属于你的东西。到下一顿饭时你头晕眼花，但问心无愧。|给什么吃什么，饥饿陪了你好几个钟头。|一个面包卷，慢慢吃，一副付过钱的样子。饥饿可不这么想。}\n\n@event hungry_maud_soup\n# en: Soup at the Cottage\ntitle [b46ed5] = 小屋里的汤\n# en: {Old Maud catches you on the path by her cottage. \"You've a hungry look, dearie. Come and eat.\" She has a pot on already, as if she knew.|Maud's pot is on again. She doesn't ask, and you don't explain.} {The soup is leek and something she won't name.|A bee sits on the windowsill, waiting for its share.}\ntext [019fd3] = {老莫德在她小屋旁的小路上拦住你。“你一脸饿相，亲爱的。进来吃点。”她的锅已经炖上了，好像早就知道。|莫德的锅又炖上了。她不问，你也不解释。}{汤里有韭葱，还有一样她不肯说的东西。|窗台上落着一只蜜蜂，等着分一份。}\n# en: A crooked cottage kitchen full of hanging herbs, an old woman ladling soup from a black pot into a chipped bowl.\nscene [65b863] = 歪歪扭扭的小屋厨房里挂满草药，一位老妇人从黑锅里舀汤倒进一只缺口的碗。\n# en: Stay for soup and a story\nopt.left.label [16b691] = 留下喝汤听故事\n# en: {Two bowls, bread, and a sad story about her sister told like a recipe. Your reading waits until morning, and it'll be late.|She talks about the winter the lake froze twice while you eat. The essay you meant to write tonight stays unwritten.}\nopt.left.say.1 [309b82] = {两碗汤、面包，还有一个关于她姐姐的伤心故事，讲得像菜谱一样。你的阅读只好等到明早，肯定要迟交了。|你吃饭时，她讲起湖结了两次冰的那个冬天。你本打算今晚写的论文，一个字也没写。}\n# en: Take a jar back to your room\nopt.right.label [6ec9c2] = 带一罐回房间\n# en: {She fills a jar and wraps it in a cloth. \"Bring the jar back,\" she says. \"That's the price.\" You eat at your desk, studying.|You carry the warm jar up the hill and eat it over your books. Maud waves from the door, a little disappointed.}\nopt.right.say.1 [f489b9] = {她装了一罐，用布包好。“罐子要还回来，”她说，“这就是价钱。”你坐在书桌前边吃边学。|你捧着温热的罐子走上山，对着书本吃完。莫德在门口挥手，有点失望。}\n\n@event detention_cauldrons\n# en: Cauldron Duty\ntitle [f28745] = 刷坩埚\n# en: {Detention, and a row of crusted cauldrons and a prefect by the door with a book.|Detention again. The cauldrons are the same; the crust is worse.|You know these cauldrons by name now. One of them, you suspect, knows you.} {Something in the third one is still bubbling.|The prefect is pretending not to be bored.|The prefect has brought a second book this time.}\ntext [fb7d05] = {禁闭，一排结满污垢的坩埚，门口一个拿着书的级长。|又是禁闭。坩埚还是那些，污垢更厚了。|这些坩埚你都叫得出名字了。你怀疑其中一只也认得你。}{第三只里还有东西在冒泡。|级长在假装不无聊。|这回级长带了第二本书。}\n# en: A dim potions storeroom, a student up to the elbows in a crusted cauldron while a prefect reads by a lamp at the door.\nscene [942c95] = 昏暗的魔药储藏室，一个学生胳膊伸进结垢的坩埚里直到手肘，门边一个级长借着灯光看书。\n# en: Scrub them properly\nopt.left.label [1ea45b] = 好好刷干净\n# en: {You scrub until your knuckles are raw, and learn which residues bite back and which only sulk.|By the last cauldron you can tell a week-old draught from a month-old one by the smell alone. Your hands sting for days.|You scrub your old acquaintances one by one. The one that knows you gives up its crust almost gently.}\nopt.left.say.1 [0cda46] = {你刷到指节发红，学会了哪些残渣会反咬一口，哪些只会闹脾气。|刷到最后一只时，你光凭气味就能分辨放了一周和放了一个月的药剂。你的手刺痛了好几天。|你把这些老相识一只只刷过去。认得你的那只几乎是温顺地交出了锅垢。}\n# en: Talk your way to an early night\nopt.right.label [358ac0] = 说服级长早点放人\n# en: {You talk about the prefect's book until the prefect is talking about it too, and at nine they wave you off. \"Same time next week. Possibly.\"|You find out you're both from the same county. The cauldrons can wait for someone less charming.|The prefect is on the second book and glad of the interruption. You talk until nine, and the cauldrons stay crusted another week.}\nopt.right.say.1 [a81dd2] = {你跟级长聊起他手里那本书，聊到他自己也聊开了，九点就挥手放你走。“下周同一时间。大概吧。”|你们发现彼此是同乡。坩埚可以留给不那么讨人喜欢的人。|级长看到了第二本书，很乐意被打断。你们聊到九点，坩埚又结了一星期的垢。}\n# en: {\"Nice try,\" says the prefect, and hands you the worst cauldron in the row.|The prefect hears you out, then points at the third cauldron, the bubbling one. \"For the speech.\"|The prefect doesn't look up from the second book. \"The one that knows you,\" they say. \"Start there.\"}\nopt.right.say.2 [c6834e] = {“想得美，”级长说，把那一排里最糟的坩埚递给了你。|级长听你说完，指了指第三口坩埚，还在冒泡的那口。“奖励你的演讲。”|级长头也没从第二本书上抬起来。“认得你的那只，”级长说，“从它开始。”}\n\n@event detention_lines\n# en: Lines, With Company\ntitle [e410cf] = 有人作伴的罚抄\n# en: Detention means lines, a hundred of them, and {other} is at the next desk doing the same. {~The sentence is \"I will not bring livestock into the library.\"|The sentence is \"Curfew is not a suggestion.\"|The sentence is \"Stairs are for walking.\"} An enchanted quill at the front of the room writes down everything anyone says.\ntext [10752a] = 禁闭就是罚抄，一百遍，{other}就在隔壁桌抄同样的东西。{~句子是“我不会把牲口带进图书馆。”|句子是“宵禁不是建议。”|句子是“楼梯是用来走的。”}教室前面有一支施了魔法的羽毛笔，会把任何人说的每句话都记下来。\n# en: An empty classroom at dusk, two students at separate desks writing the same sentence over and over, a quill scratching on its own at the front.\nscene [e7f96f] = 黄昏时空荡荡的教室，两个学生坐在不同的桌前一遍遍抄同一句话，前面一支羽毛笔自己沙沙地写着。\n# en: Do your homework under the desk\nopt.left.label [9b4823] = 在桌子底下写作业\n# en: {~A hundred lines and an evening's revision for every course. The quill at the front records nothing but scratching.|You alternate a line and a page of notes. Nobody checks. Your whole week's reading is the better for it.|You write \"Stairs are for walking\" with one hand and a week's notes with the other. The quill at the front records only the stairs.}\nopt.left.say.1 [c03b4d] = {~一百遍罚抄，外加把每门课都复习了一晚上。前面的羽毛笔除了沙沙声什么也没记下。|你抄一行，看一页笔记，交替进行。没人检查。你这一整周的功课都因此更扎实了。|你一只手抄“楼梯是用来走的”，另一只手写了一星期的笔记。前面的羽毛笔只记下了楼梯。}\n# en: {~The prefect lifts your lines, finds your notes underneath and confiscates them until the end of term.|Your notes fall on the floor at exactly the wrong moment. The prefect keeps them, and makes you start the lines again.|Between the stairs and the notes you lose track. The prefect finds forty lines of notes among the stairs, and you start again.}\nopt.left.say.2 [8df37d] = {~级长掀起你的罚抄纸，发现底下的笔记，一直没收到学期结束。|你的笔记在最不该掉的时候掉到了地上。级长收走了它们，还让你从头再抄。|楼梯和笔记一交替，你就乱了。级长在楼梯中间发现了四十行笔记，你只好从头再抄。}\n# en: Talk to {other}\nopt.right.label [730ff0] = 和{other}聊天\n# en: {~By the fortieth line you know why {other} is here, and it's a better story than yours. The quill at the front writes it all down, word for word.|You compare crimes. {other} laughs so hard the quill at the front writes \"LAUGHTER\" in capitals.|{other} tells you which staircase is to blame. You laugh until the quill at the front writes \"STAIRS?\" in the margin.}\nopt.right.say.1 [339d75] = {~抄到第四十遍时，你已经知道{other}为什么在这儿了，比你的故事精彩。前面的羽毛笔一字不差地全记了下来。|你们比较各自的罪行。{other}笑得太厉害，前面的羽毛笔用大写写下了“笑声”。|{other}告诉你该怪哪段楼梯。你笑到前面的羽毛笔在页边写下了“楼梯？”。}\n\n@event probation_advisor\n# en: A Word With Your Advisor\ntitle [98682b] = 和导师谈谈\n# en: {A summons to your advisor's office. Your marks lie on the desk between you, upside down for you, right way up for them.|Your advisor again, and the same marks, a little better or a little worse.} \"Two terms under the line and the Disciplinary Board sits,\" they say. \"Shall we make sure it doesn't?\"\ntext [00903e] = {你被叫到导师办公室。你的成绩单摆在你们中间的桌上，对你是倒着的，对他们是正着的。|又是导师，又是那份成绩，好了一点或者差了一点。}“连续两个学期低于及格线，纪律委员会就要开会了，”他们说，“我们一起确保这不会发生，好吗？”\n# en: A cluttered faculty office with a ticking clock, an advisor and a student either side of a desk with a marks list between them.\nscene [edee64] = 杂乱的教职员办公室里时钟滴答作响，导师和学生隔着书桌坐着，中间放着成绩单。\n# en: Make a study plan together\nopt.left.label [c5684e] = 一起订个学习计划\n# en: {An hour later you have a timetable that colours every evening grey, and you start on the first evening of it that night. It is a very good timetable. You hate it.|The plan has a column for sleep, and it is narrow. You work through the first week of it with your advisor checking in.}\nopt.left.say.1 [e6ba58] = {一小时后，你得到一张把每个晚上都涂成灰色的时间表，当晚就照着它开始了第一晚。这是一张非常好的时间表。你恨它。|计划里有一栏是睡觉，那一栏很窄。你按它熬过了第一周，导师时不时过问一下。}\n# en: Talk about what's going wrong\nopt.right.label [d074c1] = 聊聊出了什么问题\n# en: {You say aloud the thing you hadn't said to anyone. Your advisor listens and makes no plan at all. You leave lighter, and no better prepared.|It turns out your advisor failed a year, once. You talk about that instead of your marks.}\nopt.right.say.1 [0f21a6] = {你把从没对人说过的话说出了口。导师听着，一点计划也没订。你离开时轻松了些，准备却一点没多。|原来你的导师也曾留过一级。你们聊的是这个，而不是你的成绩。}\n\n@event probation_letter\n# en: The Warning Letter\ntitle [a7ab28] = 警告信\n# en: A letter from the registrar, in the stiff envelope everyone dreads: Academic Probation, your name spelled correctly for once, and a line about \"consequences\" underlined twice. A copy, it says, has gone to your family.\ntext [29a24a] = 教务处来信，装在人人害怕的那种硬邦邦的信封里：学业察看，你的名字难得拼对了，“后果”一词下面画了两道线。信上说，副本已寄给你的家人。\n# en: A stiff envelope with the academy's seal lying open on a desk, a student holding the letter at arm's length.\nscene [f197c9] = 书桌上一个盖着学院火漆的硬信封已经拆开，一个学生把信拿得远远的。\n# en: Pin it above your desk\nopt.left.label [94c02c] = 钉在书桌上方\n# en: The letter glares at you every time you look up. It works, in the way a headache works: you study, and you don't enjoy a minute of it.\nopt.left.say.1 [2e8283] = 你每次抬头，那封信都瞪着你。它管用，就像头疼管用一样：你学了，但没有一分钟是享受的。\n# en: Put it in a drawer\nopt.right.label [73100a] = 放进抽屉\n# en: Out of sight, mostly out of mind. Mostly. Some nights you open the drawer, look at it and close the drawer again.\nopt.right.say.1 [9a09b4] = 眼不见，心大致不烦。大致。有些晚上你拉开抽屉，看它一眼，又关上。\n\n@event sick_in_the_infirmary\n# en: A Bed by the Window\ntitle [d71099] = 窗边的病床\n# en: {Lake fever. Your nose glows faintly blue, and Sister Amaranth says \"Bed,\" in the voice that means it.|The fever again, and the same bed by the window.} {Lectures are going on without you.|Someone has left a stack of notes on the chair.}\ntext [3abe07] = {湖热病。你的鼻尖泛着淡蓝的光，阿玛兰斯修女说了声“躺下”，用的是不容商量的语气。|又发烧了，还是窗边那张床。}{课在没有你的情况下照常进行。|有人在椅子上留了一沓笔记。}\n# en: A row of white infirmary beds, a student wrapped in blankets with a faintly glowing blue nose and a steaming mug beside them.\nscene [0cb8c2] = 一排白色的医务室病床，一个学生裹着毯子，鼻尖发着淡淡的蓝光，身边放着一杯冒热气的饮品。\n# en: Stay in bed all week\nopt.left.label [1adbad] = 卧床一整周\n# en: {You sleep, drink something bitter and sleep again. When you go back, the class is two chapters ahead.|A week of blankets and broth. You feel much better and know much less.}\nopt.left.say.1 [7b6e03] = {你睡觉，喝点苦东西，再睡。等你回去，班上已经讲到两章以后了。|一周的毯子和肉汤。你感觉好多了，知道的却少多了。}\n# en: Go to lectures in a scarf\nopt.right.label [f539a4] = 围着围巾去上课\n# en: {You sniffle through Arcana in three scarves. The notes are good; the coughing fit in the middle is not.|You make every lecture, and every lecture makes you worse. Sister Amaranth sees you on the stairs and says nothing, very loudly.}\nopt.right.say.1 [fa9f22] = {你围着三条围巾吸着鼻子上完了奥术课。笔记记得很好，中途那阵咳嗽就不好了。|每节课你都去了，每节课都让你病得更重。阿玛兰斯修女在楼梯上看见你，什么也没说，说得很大声。}\n\n@event injured_on_the_stairs\n# en: The Moving Stairs, Limping\ntitle [442cc2] = 瘸着腿走会动的楼梯\n# en: {Your ankle is bandaged and the stairs are moving, as stairs here do. The lecture is three landings up and starts in five minutes.|The stairs again, the ankle again. The lecture, as ever, is at the top.}\ntext [c197eb] = {你的脚踝缠着绷带，而楼梯在动，这里的楼梯就是这样。课在三层楼梯之上，五分钟后开始。|又是楼梯，又是脚踝。课照旧在最上面。}\n# en: A grand staircase swinging slowly between landings, a student with a bandaged ankle clinging to the banister as it moves.\nscene [6c9dff] = 一道宏伟的楼梯在两个平台之间缓缓摆动，一个脚踝缠着绷带的学生紧抓着移动的扶手。\n# en: Take the long way, slowly\nopt.left.label [c434dc] = 绕远路，慢慢走\n# en: {You limp round by the back corridor and arrive as everyone is packing up. The ankle thanks you; your notes don't.|Slow, careful and very late. The ankle mends a little faster for it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [27a6aa] = {你一瘸一拐地绕过后走廊，到的时候大家正在收拾东西。脚踝谢谢你；你的笔记不谢。|慢、小心，迟到很久。脚踝因此好得快了一点。}\n# en: Hop the gap as it swings\nopt.right.label [eee215] = 趁它摆过来时跳过去\n# en: {You time it perfectly and land on one foot. Somebody on the landing applauds.|One hop, one wobble, one bow. You're on time and briefly famous.}\nopt.right.say.1 [25eaae] = {你把握好时机，单脚落地。平台上有人鼓掌。|一跳，一晃，一鞠躬。你准时到了，还短暂地出了名。}\n# en: {You land on the wrong foot, the bad one. The stairs swing on without you, and you sit on the step for a while.|The gap is wider than it looked. You arrive on time and limping twice as badly.}\nopt.right.say.2 [87c7cb] = {你落在了错的那只脚上，伤的那只。楼梯丢下你摆走了，你在台阶上坐了好一会儿。|缝隙比看上去宽。你准时到了，瘸得比原来厉害一倍。}\n\n@event purse_and_the_board\n# en: A Light Purse\ntitle [a59033] = 钱袋空空\n# en: {You tip out your purse on the bed. Three coins and a button.|The purse again. The button is still there; the coins are fewer.|The purse, the button, and fewer coins than ever. You've stopped counting the button.} Board is due at the start of every month. {On the way to breakfast you passed the Job Board in the entrance hall, and someone was taking down a notice.|Someone in your year says the Newt is hiring again.|There's a hardship notice by the bursar's door, someone says, and professors who pay for errands.}\ntext [c10029] = {你把钱袋倒在床上。三枚硬币，一颗纽扣。|又是这个钱袋。纽扣还在，硬币更少了。|钱袋，纽扣，硬币比哪次都少。你已经不再把纽扣算进去了。}每个月初都要交食宿费。{去吃早饭的路上，你经过门厅的招工告示板，有人正在揭下一张告示。|你们年级有人说，蝾螈酒馆又在招人了。|有人说，财务官门边贴着困难补助的告示，还有教授会花钱请人跑腿。}\n# en: A student emptying a coin purse on a dormitory bed, three coins and a button rolling out across the blanket.\nscene [edb439] = 一个学生把钱袋倒在宿舍床上，三枚硬币和一颗纽扣滚过毯子。\n# en: Sell your second-best cloak\nopt.left.label [da0dc9] = 卖掉你第二好的斗篷\n# en: {A second-hand stall in the village gives you a fair price and an unfair look. The castle is draughty without it.|You sell the cloak. It was your grandmother's. You're nearly sure she'd understand.|The second-hand stall knows you by now. The price is fair, and the look is kinder than last time.}\nopt.left.say.1 [6ad26e] = {山下小镇的二手摊给了你一个公道的价钱，和一个不太公道的眼神。没了它，城堡里冷飕飕的。|你卖掉了斗篷。那是你祖母的。你几乎可以肯定她会理解。|二手摊已经认得你了。价钱公道，眼神也比上次和善。}\n# en: Look at the Job Board later\nopt.right.label [e5acc5] = 回头去看看招工告示板\n# en: {You make a note. The Job Board, entrance hall, morning to evening. One job at a time, paid by the shift.|You'll go and read the notices. Probably. Board is due either way.|You'll look at the Job Board, and at the hardship notice by the bursar's door. Board is due either way.}\nopt.right.say.1 [f564c8] = {你记下：招工告示板，门厅，早上到傍晚。一次一份工，按班次付钱。|你会去看看那些告示的。大概吧。反正食宿费总是要交的。|你会去看招工告示板，还有财务室门口的困难补助告示。反正食宿费总是要交的。}\n\n@event ember_on_the_essay\n# en: A Fox on the Essay\ntitle [342c5e] = 论文上的狐狸\n# en: {Ember has chosen your week's coursework to sleep on, and her tail is smouldering, as it does when she dreams.|Ember is asleep on your notes again. The corner of every page is already brown.|Ember has a new favourite sleeping place, and all of it is due on Monday.}\ntext [365726] = {余烬选中你这一周的功课当床，尾巴冒着烟，她做梦时总是这样。|余烬又在你的笔记上睡着了。每一页的纸角都已经焦黄。|余烬有了新的心头好睡觉地点，而且这些全都周一要交。}\n# en: A fox-spirit curled asleep on a student's half-written essay, her tail smouldering gently at one corner of the page.\nscene [0a42c9] = 一只狐灵蜷在学生写了一半的论文上睡觉，尾巴在纸角轻轻冒烟。\n# en: Rescue the essay\nopt.left.label [e5bb3c] = 抢救论文\n# en: {You slide the pile out from under her. She opens one eye, says something rude in fox and sulks under the bed until supper.|You rescue the pages and lose a fox: she spends the evening on your pillow with her back to you.|You lift her off Monday's work in one piece. She lets you, which is worse: she's planning something.}\nopt.left.say.1 [cda348] = {你把那摞纸从她身下抽出来。她睁开一只眼，用狐狸语说了句难听的话，然后躲到床底下生闷气，一直到晚饭。|你救下了那几页纸，却失去了一只狐狸：她整晚背对着你趴在你的枕头上。|你把她从周一要交的作业上整个抱了下来。她由着你，这更糟：她在打什么主意。}\n# en: Let her sleep and write around her\nopt.right.label [775316] = 让她睡，绕着她写\n# en: {You write the conclusion in the margin, round a sleeping fox. Your essays come back scorched at one corner and marked down for it.|She's warm, she's purring in her own way, and your notes have a hole in them now. It's worth it.|You write Monday's work round her, a paragraph at a time, while her tail smoulders in the margin. It smells of smoke, and it's marked down for it, and it's in.}\nopt.right.say.1 [5217d0] = {你绕着一只熟睡的狐狸，把结论写在了页边。论文发回来时一角烧焦了，还因此被扣了分。|她暖暖的，以她自己的方式打着呼噜，你的笔记现在多了个洞。值得。|你绕着她写周一要交的作业，一次一段，她的尾巴在页边冒着烟。作业有股烟味，因此被扣了分，但总算交上了。}\n\n@event ember_night_hunt\n# en: Ember Goes Hunting\ntitle [093af9] = 余烬去打猎\n# en: {Ember tugs at your blanket. Then at your sleeve. Then at your ear. She wants you to come.|Ember is at the door again, tail glowing, waiting.} {Somewhere below, something is scratching.|The corridors are cold and very quiet.}\ntext [184d85] = {余烬拽你的毯子。然后拽你的袖子。然后拽你的耳朵。她想让你跟着来。|余烬又守在门口，尾巴发着光，等着。}{楼下某处，有什么东西在抓挠。|走廊又冷又静。}\n# en: A dark castle corridor lit only by the glow of a fox's tail, a student in a nightgown following at a distance.\nscene [050b20] = 只有狐狸尾巴的微光照亮的昏暗城堡走廊，一个穿睡袍的学生远远跟在后面。\n# en: Follow her through the castle\nopt.left.label [8c04fb] = 跟着她穿过城堡\n# en: {She leads you down three staircases to a loose stone, and sits by it, very pleased. Behind it, nothing but a draught. You're back at dawn, frozen.|A mouse, two corridors and a door you'd never noticed. You get back to bed cold and much better at walking quietly.}\nopt.left.say.1 [cc981e] = {她领你走下三段楼梯，来到一块松动的石头前，坐在旁边，得意极了。石头后面：除了一股穿堂风什么也没有。你天亮才回去，冻得透透的。|一只老鼠，两条走廊，一扇你从没注意过的门。你回到床上时浑身冰凉，走路却轻了许多。}\n# en: Pull the blanket over your head\nopt.right.label [352adf] = 把毯子蒙过头\n# en: {You sleep. Ember goes alone and comes back at dawn smelling of the kitchens, enormously smug.|She sighs at you, a fox's sigh, and goes without you. You sleep like a stone.}\nopt.right.say.1 [55c90a] = {你睡了。余烬独自出门，天亮回来时一身厨房的味道，得意得不得了。|她冲你叹了口气，狐狸式的叹气，自己去了。你睡得像块石头。}\n\n@event ember_and_the_sandwich\n# en: The Prefect's Sandwich\ntitle [4bfec6] = 级长的三明治\n# en: Ember trots through the refectory with a prefect's sandwich in her jaws, past forty witnesses. The prefect looks at the empty plate, then at the fox, then at the room. \"Whose,\" the prefect says, \"is that?\"\ntext [3b22cb] = 余烬叼着一个级长的三明治，从四十个目击者面前小跑着穿过膳堂。级长看看空盘子，看看狐狸，又看看满屋子的人。“这是谁的，”级长说，“那只？”\n# en: A crowded refectory, a small fire-tailed fox trotting off with a sandwich while a prefect stares at an empty plate.\nscene [928f92] = 拥挤的膳堂里，一只火尾小狐狸叼着三明治小跑而去，一个级长瞪着空盘子。\n# en: Own up and buy a new one\nopt.left.label [66e428] = 认了，再买一个\n# en: You raise your hand. The room laughs; you pay for a sandwich at the counter and hand it over. The prefect accepts it with dignity. Ember eats the first one under your chair.\nopt.left.say.1 [27502f] = 你举起手。满屋子的人都笑了；你在柜台买了个三明治递过去。级长庄重地收下了。余烬在你椅子底下吃掉了第一个。\n# en: Look as puzzled as everyone else\nopt.right.label [c5a60c] = 跟大家一样一脸茫然\n# en: You look around the room, as baffled as anybody. Ember sits on your foot under the table, chewing. The prefect never finds out, and Ember never lets you forget it.\nopt.right.say.1 [cbaa51] = 你环顾四周，和所有人一样困惑。余烬坐在桌下你的脚上嚼着。级长始终没查出来，而余烬始终不让你忘了这件事。\n\n@event owl_six_oclock\n# en: \"HOMEWORK\"\ntitle [a40fe3] = “作业”\n# en: {Bedtime. \"HOMEWORK,\" says the clockwork owl, and then, because you haven't moved, \"HOMEWORK. DUE. TOMORROW.\"|\"HOMEWORK,\" says the owl, at bedtime, as ever. This time it's right. A week's exercises are due tomorrow, in every course, and you'd forgotten.|The owl's bedtime hoot has a new word in it: \"LATE.\"}\ntext [3d755a] = {睡觉时间。“作业，”发条猫头鹰说，见你没动，又说：“作业。明天。要交。”|“作业，”猫头鹰照例在睡前说。这次它说对了。每门课都有一周的练习明天要交，你给忘了。|猫头鹰睡前的报时里多了一个新词：“迟了。”}\n# en: A brass clockwork owl on a bedpost at dawn, its amber eyes glowing, a student groaning under a pillow.\nscene [d3576f] = 黎明时床柱上站着一只黄铜发条猫头鹰，琥珀色眼睛发着光，一个学生在枕头底下呻吟。\n# en: Stay up and write it now\nopt.left.label [e04890] = 熬夜马上写\n# en: {You write it all by candlelight with your eyes half shut. It's not your best work, but it's in. The owl says \"GOOD,\" which you didn't know it could.|Two hours of frantic quill-work by the last of the candle. You hand it in on time and nod off in the next lecture.|You write until the owl stops saying \"LATE\" and says nothing at all. It goes in on time, just.}\nopt.left.say.1 [8a7dcf] = {你半睁着眼在烛光下全部写完。不是你最好的作品，但交上了。猫头鹰说“好”，你都不知道它会说这个。|就着最后一截蜡烛奋笔疾书了两个钟头。你按时交了，下一节课却打起了瞌睡。|你一直写到猫头鹰不再说“迟了”，什么也不说了。作业勉强按时交上。}\n# en: Hand it in late\nopt.right.label [6db6d8] = 迟交\n# en: {You go to sleep. The owl says \"DISAPPOINTED\" twice and goes quiet. Your work goes in a day late and is marked accordingly.|You sleep until eight. The owl doesn't speak to you until lunch, and then only to say \"LATE.\"|\"LATE,\" says the owl, and it is. You hand the work in a day after, and the owl says it again at breakfast.}\nopt.right.say.1 [8b8cee] = {你去睡了。猫头鹰说了两遍“失望”，然后不吭声了。你的作业晚交了一天，分数也相应打了折。|你一觉睡到八点。猫头鹰直到午饭都不理你，开口也只说“迟了”。|“迟了，”猫头鹰说，确实迟了。你晚一天交了作业，猫头鹰早饭时又说了一遍。}\n\n@event owl_birthday\n# en: \"BIRTHDAY\"\ntitle [8d676a] = “生日”\n# en: \"BIRTHDAY,\" says the owl, flapping in front of your face, and then, louder, \"BIRTHDAY.\" It turns its head toward the window, toward the dormitory where {friend} lives. Mir built it to remember birthdays. You had not.\ntext [2c9379] = “生日，”猫头鹰扑棱着翅膀在你面前说，然后更大声：“生日。”它把头转向窗外，转向{friend}住的那栋宿舍。米尔造它时就让它记生日。你可没记。\n# en: A clockwork owl fluttering in front of a student, its beak open mid-hoot, a calendar on the wall behind.\nscene [65c33f] = 一只发条猫头鹰在学生面前扑腾，喙张着正在叫，身后墙上挂着日历。\n# en: Buy a proper present\nopt.left.label [c20c31] = 买份像样的礼物\n# en: You run down to the village before the shops close and come back with something {friend} actually wanted. {They} look{s} at you as if you've always remembered.\nopt.left.say.1 [6c6795] = 你赶在商店关门前跑下山，带回一样{friend}真正想要的东西。{They}看着你，好像你一直都记得。\n# en: Write a card, quickly\nopt.right.label [74c735] = 赶紧写张卡片\n# en: A card, a drawing of the owl and \"Happy birthday\" in your best hand. {friend} pins it up anyway, and asks how you knew.\nopt.right.say.1 [5972f0] = 一张卡片，画了那只猫头鹰，用你最好的字写着“生日快乐”。{friend}还是把它钉了起来，问你是怎么知道的。\n\n@event owl_wound_down\n# en: The Owl Winds Down\ntitle [cca46b] = 猫头鹰没电了\n# en: The clockwork owl has started saying \"HOME… WORK…\" very slowly, like a music box running down. One wing droops. There is a tiny panel in its chest, and a tinier key, and absolutely no instructions.\ntext [20f376] = 发条猫头鹰开始很慢很慢地说“作……业……”，像一只快走完的八音盒。一边翅膀耷拉着。它胸口有个小小的盖板，一把更小的钥匙，没有任何说明。\n# en: A brass owl slumped on a desk with one wing drooping, a student peering into a small open panel in its chest with a magnifying glass.\nscene [c0c42f] = 一只黄铜猫头鹰瘫在桌上，一边翅膀下垂，一个学生拿着放大镜往它胸口打开的小盖板里看。\n# en: Take it back to Mir\nopt.left.label [e10bd7] = 带回去给米尔\n# en: Mir lights up and spends the whole evening inside the owl with tweezers, explaining every spring. You miss your reading entirely. The owl comes back saying \"HOMEWORK\" faster than ever.\nopt.left.say.1 [7214a4] = 米尔眼睛一亮，整个晚上都拿着镊子钻在猫头鹰肚子里，一根根发条地讲解。你的阅读完全泡汤了。猫头鹰回来后说“作业”说得比以前还快。\n# en: Fix it yourself\nopt.right.label [bf3e8e] = 自己修\n# en: A wound spring, a drop of oil and a charm to keep it turning. The owl shakes itself and says \"THANK YOU,\" which it has never said before.\nopt.right.say.1 [1c89db] = 上紧一根发条，滴一滴油，再加一道让它转下去的咒语。猫头鹰抖了抖身子，说了声“谢谢”，它以前从没说过。\n# en: You fix it. Now it hoots on the half hour instead of the hour, all night, and says \"HOMEWORK\" in a voice like a kettle.\nopt.right.say.2 [ca3ce4] = 你修好了。现在它不是整点报时，而是每半点报一次，整夜都报，说“作业”的声音像烧开的水壶。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/pools/upper_ranks.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/pools/upper_ranks.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event duelling_captain_challenge\n# en: The Captain's Challenge\ntitle [3e0fbf] = 队长的挑战\n# en: {A visiting champion has challenged the Circle's captain, by name, in front of everyone. The captain is you.|Another challenger at the door, asking for the captain.|They come for the captain now, from three valleys away.} {The hall is packed to the rafters.|{around(lyra): Lyra is in the front row with her arms folded.|The old captains are in the front row with their arms folded.}|Somebody is taking bets in the corridor.}\ntext [616d0c] = {一位来访的冠军当着所有人的面，点名挑战决斗社的队长。队长就是你。|门口又来了一个挑战者，指名要找队长。|如今有人从三个山谷之外赶来挑战队长。}{大厅里挤得水泄不通。|{around(lyra): 莱拉抱着胳膊坐在第一排。|几位前任队长抱着胳膊坐在第一排。}|有人在走廊里坐庄下注。}\n# en: A captain of a duelling circle in a chalk ring facing a visiting champion, the whole hall packed to the rafters.\nscene [f26cae] = 决斗社的队长站在粉笔圈里，对面是来访的冠军，整个大厅挤得水泄不通。\n# en: Accept in front of everyone\nopt.left.label [c550e1] = 当着所有人的面应战\n# en: {You win the third exchange and the hall comes apart. The champion bows, properly. The Circle will talk about it for a year.|A long, hard bout, and yours at the end. Your name goes on the board in gold paint.|You accept, with bets being taken in the corridor. You win, and somebody in the corridor wins a great deal more.}\nopt.left.say.1 [a1e423] = {你在第三回合取胜，整个大厅炸开了锅。冠军郑重地鞠了一躬。决斗社会把这件事谈论一整年。|一场漫长而艰苦的较量，最后是你赢了。你的名字被用金漆写上了荣誉榜。|走廊里有人在下注，你接受了挑战。你赢了，走廊里有人赢得比你多得多。}\n# en: {You lose, in front of everyone, by a breath. The Circle carries you to the infirmary anyway, which is its own verdict.|Beaten, cleanly. The champion is gracious. That's the worst part.|You lose to someone from three valleys away, and the corridor pays out. The Circle buys you a drink with the winnings it didn't make.}\nopt.left.say.2 [fb3105] = {你当着所有人的面输了，只差一口气。决斗社还是把你抬去了医务室，这本身就是一种评价。|干脆利落地输了。冠军很有风度。这才是最难受的。|你输给了一个从三个山谷外来的人，走廊里的赌局开始赔钱。决斗社用它没赢到的钱请你喝了一杯。}\n# en: Send your best officer instead\nopt.right.label [50ddb4] = 派你最好的干事上场\n# en: {Your officer wins, narrowly, and the hall cheers for the Circle. A few people look at you and wonder why you didn't step up.|You coach from the edge. The officer loses but fights well. It was the right call, you think, mostly.|You send your officer. They win, and the corridor's bookmaker grumbles that the captain was the better bet.}\nopt.right.say.1 [fbcd3e] = {你的干事险胜，大厅为决斗社欢呼。有几个人看着你，纳闷你为什么没有亲自上场。|你在场边指导。干事输了，但打得很好。这是正确的决定，你想，大体上是。|你派出了干事。干事赢了，走廊里坐庄的人抱怨说押队长才稳。}\n\n@event duelling_captain_injury\n# en: The Captain's Call\ntitle [65921a] = 队长的决定\n# en: {A first-year in the Circle has twisted a wrist in the final of the house cup, and insists on finishing.|Another final, another stubborn first-year with an injury.|The house cup final again, and again someone hurt who won't stop.} {The house's honour is riding on it.|Their parents are in the gallery.|The referee looks at you: the captain decides.}\ntext [d60d62] = {决斗社的一个一年级生在院际杯决赛中扭伤了手腕，却坚持要打完。|又一场决赛，又一个受了伤还不肯下场的一年级生。|又是院际杯决赛，又有人受了伤却不肯停。}{学院的荣誉全押在这一场上。|他们的父母就在看台上。|裁判看向你：由队长决定。}\n# en: A duellist kneeling in a chalk circle clutching a wrist, a captain crouching beside them, a referee waiting with a whistle.\nscene [89c135] = 一个决斗者跪在粉笔圈里捂着手腕，队长蹲在一旁，裁判拿着哨子等待。\n# en: Let them finish\nopt.left.label [726d27] = 让他们打完\n# en: {They finish, and win, with one hand. The gallery stands. You stood beside them, and you'll remember the fear more than the win.|They finish and win. Amaranth bandages them afterwards and gives you a look that could strip paint.|The referee looks at you, and you nod. They finish, and win, and you hold your breath through every exchange.}\nopt.left.say.1 [54b4bf] = {他们用一只手打完了，还赢了。看台上的人全站了起来。你一直站在他们身旁，你记住的会是恐惧，而不是胜利。|他们打完了，赢了。阿玛兰斯修女事后给他们包扎，看你的眼神能刮掉一层漆。|裁判看向你，你点了头。他们打完了，赢了，每一回合你都屏着气。}\n# en: {They finish and lose, and the wrist is worse. Sister Amaranth sends for you specifically, to explain.|The bout ends with a second injury. You let it happen. You don't sleep that night.|The referee looks at you, you nod, and you're wrong. The wrist goes. You walk them to the infirmary yourself.}\nopt.left.say.2 [2b62e7] = {他们打完了，输了，手腕伤得更重了。阿玛兰斯修女特地派人找你去解释。|这场比赛以第二次受伤告终。是你让它发生的。那天夜里你没有睡着。|裁判看向你，你点了头，可你错了。手腕彻底伤了。你亲自把他们送去了医务室。}\n# en: Stop the bout\nopt.right.label [484d08] = 叫停比赛\n# en: {You call it. The house loses the cup and the first-year cries and calls you a coward. A week later, in the corridor, they thank you.|You stop it. The gallery boos. {around(lyra): Lyra, in the front row, nods once.|The referee, pocketing the whistle, nods once.}|The referee looks at you, and you stop it. The first-year doesn't speak to you for a week, then asks you to coach them.}\nopt.right.say.1 [43f5f4] = {你叫停了。学院输掉了奖杯，那个一年级生哭着骂你是懦夫。一个星期后，在走廊里，他们向你道了谢。|你叫停了。看台上一片嘘声。{around(lyra): 第一排的莱拉点了一下头。|裁判把哨子揣回兜里，点了一下头。}|裁判看向你，你叫停了。那个一年级生一个星期没理你，然后来请你当他们的教练。}\n\n@event lumen_ledger_editor_scoop\n# en: The Story That Could Close Us\ntitle [202765] = 可能让报社关门的报道\n# en: {A reporter brings you a story about a governor of the academy, apparently true and well sourced, and dangerous.|Another story with teeth, on your desk with a dawn deadline.|Nessa calls it \"the kind of story that closes papers\". She says it admiringly.} {The evidence is a stack of letters.|The source wants to stay unnamed.|The printer needs the pages by dawn.}\ntext [b5834d] = {一个记者给你带来一篇关于学院一位董事的报道，看上去属实，来源可靠，也很危险。|又一篇带刺的报道摆在你的桌上，天亮前就得付印。|内莎说这是“能让报社关门的那种报道”。她说这话时带着钦佩。}{证据是一叠信件。|消息来源要求匿名。|印刷工天亮前就要版面。}\n# en: A cramped newspaper office at night, a student editor reading a draft under a lamp, a stack of evidence beside it and the clock showing late.\nscene [4d4f9d] = 夜里一间狭小的报社办公室，一个学生主编在灯下读着稿子，旁边摞着证据，钟表显示已经很晚了。\n# en: Run it on the front page\nopt.left.label [b4bc4b] = 登上头版\n# en: {Every fact holds. The Ledger sells out by breakfast; the governor resigns by supper. Nessa frames the front page.|It runs, it holds, and the castle talks of nothing else for a week.|You run it with the printer waiting, and every fact holds. Nessa says it's the story that closes papers, and it closes someone else's career instead.}\nopt.left.say.1 [71ac0c] = {每一个事实都站得住。《流明纪事报》早饭前就卖光了；那位董事晚饭前就辞了职。内莎把那期头版裱了起来。|报道刊出，经得住推敲，整个城堡一个星期都在谈论这件事。|你在印刷工的等待中把它发了出去，每个事实都站得住。内莎说这是能让报纸关门的稿子，结果关门的是别人的前程。}\n# en: {One fact was wrong, and it's the one they sue over. The Ledger prints a retraction; you write it. Detention, too, for good measure.|It runs, with a mistake in it. The governor's lawyers are very polite, and very thorough.|You run it by dawn, and the dawn is too soon. One fact needed another day. The retraction is yours to write.}\nopt.left.say.2 [acb720] = {有一个事实错了，偏偏就是他们起诉的那一条。《流明纪事报》刊登了更正声明，由你执笔。顺便还有一次禁闭。|报道刊出了，里面有个错误。那位董事的律师们非常有礼貌，也非常彻底。|你赶在黎明前发了稿，可黎明来得太早了。有一个事实还需要多核实一天。更正由你来写。}\n# en: Hold it until it's airtight\nopt.right.label [145ca0] = 压到万无一失再说\n# en: {You hold it. Another paper in the village runs a thinner version first. Nessa doesn't speak to you for two days, then admits you were right.|You hold it for a month and check every line. When it finally runs, it's bulletproof, and old news.|You hold the presses until it's airtight. The printer charges you for the dawn, and Nessa pays it, grumbling.}\nopt.right.say.1 [af6116] = {你压下了稿子。村里另一家报纸先登了一个单薄的版本。内莎两天没跟你说话，然后承认你是对的。|你压了一个月，每一行都核实过。等它终于刊出，已经无懈可击，也已经是旧闻了。|你压住印刷机，直到稿子无懈可击。印刷工为这个黎明收了你的钱，内莎嘟囔着付了。}\n\n@event lumen_ledger_editor_threat\n# en: A Letter to the Editor\ntitle [2a57e7] = 致编辑的信\n# en: {A letter to the editor, unsigned, about a story you ran. It knows where you sleep.|Another unsigned letter, the same careful hand.|The letters have stopped being polite.} {It smells of expensive ink.|The seal has been scraped off.|It's been slipped under the office door, not posted.}\ntext [e93e25] = {一封致编辑的匿名信，针对你刊出的一篇报道。它知道你睡在哪儿。|又一封匿名信，同样工整的笔迹。|这些信已经不再客气了。}{它闻起来是昂贵墨水的味道。|火漆印被刮掉了。|它是从编辑部门缝底下塞进来的，不是寄来的。}\n# en: A black-edged letter open on a newspaper desk, a student editor reading it with a cold face, the window behind them dark.\nscene [34ca74] = 一封黑边信摊在报社的桌上，一个学生主编面色冰冷地读着，身后的窗户一片漆黑。\n# en: Print it, with a reply\nopt.left.label [0cd1a2] = 登出来，附上回应\n# en: {You print it in full on page two, with three lines of your own beneath. The castle loves it. You check your door twice every night for a month.|The letter goes in the paper. The next one takes a month to come. The waiting for it is worse.|You print the impolite one, impolitely answered. The letters stop. You don't know whether that's good.}\nopt.left.say.1 [185ef7] = {你把它全文登在第二版，下面附上你自己的三行字。全城堡都爱看。接下来一个月，你每晚都要检查两遍门。|信登在了报纸上。下一封隔了一个月才来。等它的滋味更难受。|你把那封不客气的信登了出来，也不客气地回了。信不再来了。你不知道这是好是坏。}\n# en: Take it to the headmistress\nopt.right.label [069089] = 交给校长\n# en: {She reads it once and puts it in a drawer. \"Leave it with me.\" For a long while, you don't get another. Nessa says that's censorship. It's also sleep.|The headmistress handles it. You don't ask how. The Ledger is quieter for a while.|The headmistress reads the one slipped under the door and asks who has a key to the office. The locks are changed by evening.}\nopt.right.say.1 [24b851] = {她读了一遍就把信放进了抽屉。“交给我吧。”很长一段时间里，你没再收到过。内莎说这是审查。这也是能睡个好觉。|校长处理了这件事。你没问她怎么处理的。《流明纪事报》安静了一阵子。|院长读了那封塞在门下的信，问谁有编辑部的钥匙。傍晚之前锁就换了。}\n\n@event verdant_head_gardener_blight\n# en: The Blight\ntitle [b7ffa6] = 枯萎病\n# en: {Blight in the greenhouses, black spots spreading by the hour. The Head Gardener decides what to burn.|The blight is back, in a different house.|Black spots again. You know the smell of it now.} {{around(tobias): Tobias is close to tears.|The Circle's youngest member is close to tears.}|The rarest plant in the collection is at the edge of it.|Maud has come up from the cottage to look.}\ntext [a2f41a] = {温室里闹起了枯萎病，黑斑一小时一小时地蔓延。首席园丁决定烧掉什么。|枯萎病又来了，在另一间温室。|又是黑斑。你现在已经熟悉它的气味了。}{{around(tobias): 托比亚斯快哭了。|社里年纪最小的成员快哭了。}|收藏里最稀有的那株植物就在病区边上。|莫德从小屋上来看了。}\n# en: A greenhouse full of wilting plants, black spots on every leaf, a head gardener holding a sickly seedling up to the light.\nscene [5e45ab] = 一间满是枯萎植物的温室，每片叶子上都有黑斑，一个首席园丁把一株病恹恹的幼苗举到光下。\n# en: Try an untested cure on everything\nopt.left.label [ee10e0] = 在所有植物上试一种未经检验的疗法\n# en: {The cure works, overnight. Every plant lives, even the rare one. Maud says she's never seen it done, and asks for the recipe.|By morning the spots are fading. The Circle sings, badly, in the greenhouse.|Maud watches you try the cure and says nothing until it works. Then she asks for the recipe, and you give it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [3b609d] = {疗法一夜见效。每一株都活了下来，连那株稀有的也是。莫德说她从没见过有人做到，还向你要了配方。|到了早上，黑斑渐渐褪去。青翠社的人在温室里唱起了歌，唱得很难听。|莫德看着你试那个疗法，一直没说话，直到它见效。然后她向你要配方，你给了。}\n# en: {The cure kills what the blight hadn't. You burn the whole house. {around(tobias): Tobias helps|The Circle helps}, and nobody blames you, which is worse.|It fails. You lose half the collection, and all of your confidence for a week.|Maud watches the cure fail and helps you burn what's left. \"Everyone loses a house once,\" she says. \"Once.\"}\nopt.left.say.2 [f0aeea] = {疗法杀死了枯萎病没杀死的东西。你烧掉了整间温室。{around(tobias): 托比亚斯来帮忙|社员们来帮忙}，谁也没有怪你，这更难受。|失败了。你失去了一半的收藏，还有整整一个星期的自信。|莫德看着疗法失败，帮你把剩下的烧了。“谁都会丢一间温室，”她说，“一次。”}\n# en: Burn the sick plants now\nopt.right.label [90fc07] = 现在就烧掉病株\n# en: {You burn a third of the house and save the rest. It's the right call and it smells of grief.|You burn the sick ones before it spreads. The rare plant is among them.|Maud nods at what you burn and points at one plant you missed. You burn that too. She was right.}\nopt.right.say.1 [051d3a] = {你烧掉了三分之一的温室，保住了其余的。这是正确的决定，闻起来却像悲伤。|你在它蔓延之前烧掉了病株。那株稀有的也在其中。|莫德对你烧掉的那些点点头，又指了指一株你漏掉的。你把那株也烧了。她是对的。}\n\n@event verdant_head_gardener_show\n# en: The Valley Fair\ntitle [a34a57] = 山谷集市\n# en: {The valley's flower show, and the Verdant Circle's entry is yours to choose.|The flower show again; the judges remember you.|The show tent smells of crushed grass and rivalry.} {A farmer's pumpkin is the size of a cart.|Thornwick has sent a team.|The judges are three old women who have seen everything.}\ntext [6fe4b1] = {山谷里的花展到了，青翠社的参展作品由你来选。|又是花展；评委们还记得你。|展棚里弥漫着压碎的青草味和较劲的味道。}{一个农夫的南瓜有一辆车那么大。|荆木学院派了一支队伍来。|评委是三位见多识广的老太太。}\n# en: A crowded show tent in a village field, prize vegetables on trestle tables, a student head gardener carrying an enormous potted plant toward the judges.\nscene [1ae86c] = 村里田野上一座拥挤的展棚，搁板桌上摆着获奖的蔬菜，一个学生首席园丁捧着一盆巨大的植物走向评委。\n# en: Enter the night-blooming lily\nopt.left.label [0b838e] = 送夜开百合参展\n# en: {It opens at dusk in front of the judges, as if on cue. First prize, and a purse, and a drawing in the village paper.|The lily wins, narrowly, over a cabbage. Nobody will forget the cabbage's grower's face.|The three old judges watch the lily open and say nothing. Then all three write \"first\" at once.}\nopt.left.say.1 [3e17d0] = {黄昏时它在评委面前绽放，仿佛算准了时机。头奖，一袋奖金，还有村报上的一幅插画。|百合以微弱优势胜过了一颗卷心菜。没人会忘记那位卷心菜种植者的表情。|三位老评委看着百合绽放，一言不发。然后三个人同时写下了“第一”。}\n# en: {It refuses to open. The cabbage wins. The lily blooms at midnight, for nobody, in your arms.|Second place, to Thornwick. {around(tobias): Tobias says|The Circle says} second is good. It isn't.|The three old judges have seen night-blooming lilies before. They give it a polite second and the cabbage a ribbon.}\nopt.left.say.2 [add267] = {它不肯开。卷心菜赢了。百合在午夜绽放，只为你一个人，在你怀里。|第二名，输给了荆木学院。{around(tobias): 托比亚斯说|社员们说}第二名也很好。并不好。|那三位老评委以前就见过夜开的百合。她们礼貌地给了它第二名，给了卷心菜一条绶带。}\n# en: Enter the reliable roses\nopt.right.label [3ae698] = 送稳妥的玫瑰参展\n# en: {Third prize for the roses. Solid, respectable, forgettable.|The roses place. The Circle is pleased; the Circle is also bored.|The three old judges approve of roses. Second prize, and a long talk about pruning you'll actually use.}\nopt.right.say.1 [3995c0] = {玫瑰拿了第三名。扎实，体面，转眼就忘。|玫瑰得了名次。青翠社很满意；青翠社也很无聊。|那三位老评委喜欢玫瑰。第二名，外加一席关于修剪的长谈，你还真用得上。}\n\n@event library_senior_banned_book\n# en: A Request for a Banned Book\ntitle [5026f0] = 借阅禁书的请求\n# en: {A first-year brings you a request slip for a chained book. Senior clerks hold the keys, and the rules say no.|Another request for a chained book, from a student who looks desperate.|They've heard you hold the keys. The slips keep coming.} {The first-year's essay depends on it.|The slip is signed by a professor, perhaps.|The book in question bites.}\ntext [775ce5] = {一个一年级生递来一张借书单，要借一本上了锁链的书。钥匙在资深馆员手里，规矩说不行。|又一张借锁链书的单子，借书的学生看起来走投无路。|他们听说钥匙在你手里。借书单一张接一张地来。}{那个一年级生的作业全靠这本书了。|借书单上有一位教授的签名，大概吧。|那本书会咬人。}\n# en: A library desk at closing time, a nervous first-year holding a request slip for a chained book, a senior clerk with the keys looking down at it.\nscene [c4f362] = 闭馆时分的图书馆服务台，一个紧张的一年级生拿着借锁链书的单子，一个拿着钥匙的资深馆员低头看着它。\n# en: Fetch it, against the rules\nopt.left.label [b72eb7] = 违规去取\n# en: {You fetch it, watch them read it, and chain it back before closing. Nobody knows. The first-year passes with honours and leaves you a cake with crowns folded under it.|The book goes out and comes back, with a grateful envelope. The rules bent, and didn't break.|You fetch it in gloves. It bites the glove and not the reader. The essay is excellent, and the glove is a loss.}\nopt.left.say.1 [527293] = {你取来书，看着他们读完，闭馆前再锁回去。没人知道。那个一年级生以优异成绩通过，给你留了一块蛋糕，底下压着几枚克朗。|书借出去又还回来，附带一个表示感谢的信封。规矩弯了，没断。|你戴着手套去取。它咬了手套，没咬读者。论文写得很出色，手套报废了。}\n# en: {The head librarian finds the chain unlocked. Half your pay docked and a long, quiet talk.|Caught, with the key in your hand, and docked for it. You're still Senior Clerk; nobody quite knows how.|It bites the reader, and the head librarian sees the bandage. Your pay is docked, and the reader's thanks are muffled.}\nopt.left.say.2 [289a6c] = {总馆员发现锁链开着。扣了一半的工钱，外加一场长长的、安静的谈话。|被抓了个正着，钥匙还在你手里，工钱也因此被扣。你仍然是资深馆员；没人说得清这是怎么回事。|它咬了读者，总馆员看见了绷带。你的工钱被扣了，读者的道谢也说得含含糊糊。}\n# en: Refuse\nopt.right.label [cada35] = 拒绝\n# en: {You say no. The first-year leaves in tears. The head librarian, who heard, nods.|The rules hold. So does your conscience, more or less.|You say no, and show them the book from a safe distance. They see it snap at the air and stop asking.}\nopt.right.say.1 [d33e92] = {你说了不。那个一年级生哭着走了。听到这番话的总馆员点了点头。|规矩守住了。你的良心也大致守住了。|你说了不，然后隔着安全距离让他们看了看那本书。他们看见它对着空气咬了一口，就不再求了。}\n\n@event library_senior_night_flood\n# en: Water in the Stacks\ntitle [ccea75] = 书库进水\n# en: {A pipe bursts in the lower stacks at the end of your shift, and the senior clerk on duty is you.|Water in the stacks again. The pipes are older than the books.|The lower level floods every spring now.} {The rarest books are on the bottom shelf.|The water is rising.|Someone is shouting for help two floors up.}\ntext [1c6040] = {你的班快结束时，下层书库的一根水管爆了，当值的资深馆员就是你。|书库又进水了。这些水管比书还老。|如今下层每年春天都要淹。}{最稀有的书就在最底层的书架上。|水在上涨。|楼上两层有人在喊救命。}\n# en: A flooded lower library level at night, water lapping at the bottom shelves, a senior clerk wading with an armful of books.\nscene [58c52c] = 夜里被淹的图书馆下层，水拍打着最底层的书架，一个资深馆员抱着一摞书蹚水而行。\n# en: Save the rare books first\nopt.left.label [e4b589] = 先抢救稀有的书\n# en: {You carry armful after armful up the stair in freezing water until long after closing. Every rare book saved. Your boots are not.|You save the rarest and lose the common ones, and the head librarian, arriving at last, hugs you, which nobody has ever seen.|You save the rare books and then go up for the shouting. It's a first-year stuck on a ladder. You save them too.}\nopt.left.say.1 [02e465] = {你在冰冷的水里一趟又一趟地把书抱上楼梯，一直忙到闭馆后很久。每一本稀有的书都保住了。你的靴子没保住。|你救下了最稀有的，丢掉了普通的，总馆员终于赶到，抱了抱你，这是谁都没见过的事。|你救下了稀有的书，又上楼去找那个喊叫的人。是个困在梯子上的一年级生。你把对方也救了下来。}\n# en: Run for the head librarian\nopt.right.label [ffaf08] = 跑去找总馆员\n# en: {By the time she comes, a shelf is lost. \"You did right to fetch me,\" she says, and looks at the shelf.|She takes charge. You carry what she points at.|You run for the head librarian, past the shouting two floors up. She sends you back for the shouter first. She was right.}\nopt.right.say.1 [100588] = {等她赶到时，一整架书已经毁了。“你去找我是对的，”她说，看着那架书。|她接手指挥。她指什么，你就搬什么。|你跑去找总馆员，路过两层楼上的呼喊声。她让你先回去救喊叫的人。她是对的。}\n\n@event tavern_manager_inspector\n# en: The Inspector Calls\ntitle [663f74] = 检查员来访\n# en: {The licensing inspector is here, unannounced, and the landlady is away. The manager is you.|The inspector again, with the same long coat and longer list.|The inspector nods at you like an old enemy.} {The cellar has a leak.|The ledger has a page you'd rather they didn't read.|There's a goblin asleep in the back booth.}\ntext [69f885] = {执照检查员不打招呼就来了，老板娘不在。经理就是你。|又是那位检查员，同样的长大衣，更长的清单。|检查员像对老冤家一样冲你点点头。}{地窖漏水。|账簿里有一页你宁愿他们别看。|后面的卡座里睡着一个地精。}\n# en: A village tavern in the morning, a stern inspector in a long coat running a finger along a bar, a student manager following with a ledger.\nscene [3c5a55] = 早上的村酒馆，一个穿长大衣的严厉检查员用手指抹过吧台，一个学生经理拿着账簿跟在后面。\n# en: Show everything, honestly\nopt.left.label [cf598d] = 老老实实地全部展示\n# en: {The inspector finds the leak, notes it and passes the Newt with a warning. The landlady, back, gives you a bonus and her late mother's tankard.|A clean bill, almost. The inspector shakes your hand.|The inspector finds the goblin in the back booth, who turns out to be licensed. A clean bill, and a very long afternoon.}\nopt.left.say.1 [c08744] = {检查员发现了漏水，记了下来，给了蝾螈酒馆一个警告就放行了。老板娘回来后给了你一笔奖金，还有她已故母亲的那只大酒杯。|几乎干干净净。检查员和你握了握手。|检查员在后面的卡座里发现了那个地精，结果对方有执照。检查通过，下午也过得很漫长。}\n# en: {The fine is steep, the landlady takes a share out of your wages, and her face is steeper.|Three infractions. The till covers most of the fine. Your own wages cover the rest.|The inspector wakes the goblin in the back booth, who has opinions about inspectors. The fine is for the opinions.}\nopt.left.say.2 [7b7778] = {罚款很重，老板娘从你的工钱里扣了一份，她的脸色更重。|三项违规。钱柜付了大部分罚款，剩下的由你的工钱来付。|检查员吵醒了后面卡座里的地精，对方对检查员很有意见。罚款罚的就是这些意见。}\n# en: Steer them away from the cellar\nopt.right.label [666330] = 引他们避开地窖\n# en: {The inspector never goes downstairs. You fix the leak that night with your own hands, guiltily.|A clean bill, by omission. You don't tell the landlady how.|You steer the inspector past the back booth and the goblin sleeps on. A clean bill, by the narrowest of margins.}\nopt.right.say.1 [aae7bf] = {检查员始终没下楼。那天夜里你亲手修好了漏水，心里有愧。|靠隐瞒换来的干净。你没告诉老板娘是怎么做到的。|你引着检查员绕过后面的卡座，地精继续睡。检查通过，险之又险。}\n# en: {The inspector goes straight to the cellar. The fine doubles for \"obstruction\". Bram hears of it somehow.|Caught steering. The inspector writes your name down in a separate book.|The goblin in the back booth wakes and gives the inspector a tour. Of the cellar.}\nopt.right.say.2 [68e7ad] = {检查员径直走向地窖。罚款因为“妨碍检查”翻了一倍。布拉姆不知怎么听说了。|你引路被识破了。检查员把你的名字记在另一个本子上。|后面卡座里的地精醒了，热情地带检查员参观。参观的是地窖。}\n\n@event tavern_manager_grisk_offer\n# en: An Investor\ntitle [49ae9a] = 一位投资人\n# en: {Grisk would like to invest in the Newt, with new chairs and a second fire, \"and a little room upstairs for my associates\".|Grisk again, with a bigger bag of coin.|\"My offer stands,\" says Grisk, \"and grows.\"} {The chairs really are falling apart.|The landlady is away.|The bag of coin is very heavy.}\ntext [329733] = {格里斯克想投资蝾螈酒馆，新椅子，再加一个壁炉，“还有楼上给我的合伙人留个小房间”。|又是格里斯克，钱袋更大了。|“我的提议依然有效，”格里斯克说，“而且还在加码。”}{那些椅子确实快散架了。|老板娘不在。|那袋钱非常沉。}\n# en: A goblin in a velvet waistcoat sitting at a tavern's best table, a bag of coin and a contract in front of him, a student manager standing opposite.\nscene [dbb834] = 一个穿天鹅绒马甲的地精坐在酒馆最好的桌子旁，面前放着一袋钱和一份合同，一个学生经理站在对面。\n# en: Take his money for the chairs\nopt.left.label [e60668] = 收下他的钱换椅子\n# en: {New chairs, a second fire, a fat \"manager's commission\" in your pocket, and Grisk's associates upstairs on Thursdays. Trade is up. So is the noise, at midnight.|Grisk pays you a finder's cut in a sealed envelope. The Newt has never looked better. The upstairs room has never been busier, and you don't go up there.|You take the heavy bag. Grisk's offer grows into the upstairs, then the cellar, then your evenings. The commission is excellent.}\nopt.left.say.1 [797c9a] = {新椅子，第二个壁炉，你口袋里一笔丰厚的“经理佣金”，每周四楼上坐着格里斯克的合伙人。生意好了。午夜的吵闹也多了。|格里斯克用一个封好的信封付了你一笔介绍费。蝾螈酒馆从没这么气派过。楼上的房间从没这么热闹过，而你从不上去。|你收下了那只沉甸甸的钱袋。格里斯克的投资从楼上扩展到地窖，再到你的晚上。佣金非常可观。}\n# en: Turn him down\nopt.right.label [b56858] = 拒绝他\n# en: {\"Your loss,\" says Grisk, and means it as a threat. The chairs stay wobbly.|You say no. Grisk tips his hat and drinks somewhere else for a month, which hurts trade.|You turn the heavier bag down too. Grisk laughs, and says he likes a manager with standards. He'll be back.}\nopt.right.say.1 [d2cba7] = {“你的损失，”格里斯克说，这话是威胁。椅子还是摇摇晃晃。|你说了不。格里斯克抬了抬帽子，去别处喝了一个月，生意受了影响。|你连更沉的那只钱袋也拒绝了。格里斯克笑了，说他喜欢有原则的经理。他还会再来。}\n\n@event apothecary_assistant_poison\n# en: A Dangerous Prescription\ntitle [08b2dc] = 危险的处方\n# en: {A prescription comes in from a village healer with a dose ten times too strong. Pell is out. Assistant apothecaries decide.|Another slip with a strange dose. You've learned to read the healer's handwriting, which doesn't help.|A dose that would fell a horse, for an old shepherd.} {The patient's grown son is waiting.|The healer is a day's ride away.|It might be a slip of the pen. It might not.}\ntext [89141c] = {村里一位治疗师送来一张处方，剂量重了十倍。佩尔不在。由助理药师决定。|又一张剂量古怪的单子。你已经认得那位治疗师的字迹了，可这没用。|能放倒一匹马的剂量，开给一个老牧羊人。}{病人已成年的儿子在等着。|治疗师在一天的路程之外。|可能是笔误。也可能不是。}\n# en: An apothecary counter, a prescription slip with a dose underlined in red, a young assistant alone behind the counter frowning at it.\nscene [ac1da0] = 药铺柜台上一张处方，剂量下面画着红线，一位年轻的助理独自站在柜台后，皱眉看着它。\n# en: Mix it at a safe dose\nopt.left.label [bc845e] = 按安全剂量调配\n# en: {You work out what the healer meant and mix that. The patient recovers. Pell, back, reads the slip and goes white, then pats your shoulder for a long time.|Right dose, right remedy. The healer writes later to thank you, embarrassed.|You mix the shepherd a dose that wouldn't fell a sparrow. It works. The healer, told, sends Pell a very apologetic letter.}\nopt.left.say.1 [98b4d0] = {你推断出治疗师的本意，按那个剂量调了药。病人好了。佩尔回来看了那张单子，脸都白了，然后拍了你的肩膀很久。|剂量对，药也对。治疗师后来写信道谢，很是难为情。|你给那位牧羊人调了一剂连麻雀都撂不倒的药。管用了。治疗师听说后，给佩尔写了一封非常抱歉的信。}\n# en: {Your safe dose is too weak. The patient recovers slowly, and the son tells the whole village.|You get it nearly right. Nearly is a word you stop using for a while.|You cut the dose so far that it does nothing. The shepherd comes back, unwell, and Pell has to sort it out.}\nopt.left.say.2 [2abb4d] = {你的安全剂量太弱了。病人好得很慢，他儿子告诉了全村。|你差不多调对了。有一阵子你不再用“差不多”这个词。|你把剂量减得太多，毫无作用。牧羊人又回来了，还是不舒服，佩尔只好亲自处理。}\n# en: Refuse until the healer confirms\nopt.right.label [14e4f0] = 等治疗师确认后再说\n# en: {A day's wait. The patient's son shouts at you. The healer confirms the slip was wrong. You were right, and it was a long day.|You send a rider. The answer comes at midnight. You stay up for it.|It was a slip of the pen. The healer confirms by midnight, and you mix the right dose by one.}\nopt.right.say.1 [b2d046] = {等了一天。病人的儿子冲你大喊大叫。治疗师确认单子写错了。你是对的，那是漫长的一天。|你派人骑马去问。答复半夜才到。你一直等着。|那是笔误。治疗师午夜前确认了，你一点前调好了正确的剂量。}\n\n@event apothecary_assistant_rival_shop\n# en: A Shop Across the Square\ntitle [3596c9] = 广场对面的铺子\n# en: {A new apothecary has opened across the square, all brass and bright bottles and half Pell's prices.|The shop across the square has a queue now.|Pell has started calling the new shop \"that brass insult\".} {Their remedies are mostly coloured water.|They've hired a singer for the doorway.|Pell's daughters have written to ask what's wrong.}\ntext [e6dd15] = {广场对面新开了一家药铺，满眼铜器和亮闪闪的瓶子，价钱只有佩尔的一半。|广场对面的铺子现在排起了队。|佩尔开始管那家新铺子叫“那个铜皮侮辱”。}{他们的药大多是染了色的水。|他们雇了个歌手站在门口。|佩尔的女儿们写信来问出了什么事。}\n# en: A gleaming new apothecary shop across a village square, its window full of bright bottles, an old apothecary peering at it from his own doorway.\nscene [1729f3] = 村广场对面一家闪亮的新药铺，橱窗里满是亮闪闪的瓶子，一位老药师从自家门口朝那边张望。\n# en: Expose their coloured water\nopt.left.label [4016d1] = 揭穿他们的染色水\n# en: {You buy a bottle of everything and test them in Pell's back room. The results go on a board in the window. The brass shop closes by the season's end.|Your tests are fair and public. The village comes back to Pell, a little sheepish.|You write to Pell's daughters with your test results. They come home for a week and help you nail the board to the window.}\nopt.left.say.1 [a4b64a] = {你把他们的每样药都买了一瓶，在佩尔的后屋里检验。结果贴在橱窗的告示板上。那家铜器铺子没到季末就关门了。|你的检验公平又公开。村里人回到了佩尔这儿，有点不好意思。|你把检验结果写信告诉了佩尔的女儿们。她们回来住了一个星期，帮你把告示板钉到了橱窗上。}\n# en: {Their remedies turn out to work, annoyingly. You look petty. Pell says, \"Well, now we know.\"|Inconclusive, and everyone saw you try.|Pell's daughters write back that the brass shop's remedies are fine and that you should worry about Pell's. You do.}\nopt.left.say.2 [a8d769] = {偏偏他们的药是管用的。你显得小家子气。佩尔说：“好吧，现在我们知道了。”|没有定论，而且所有人都看见你试过了。|佩尔的女儿们回信说铜器铺子的药没问题，你该担心的是佩尔的药。你开始担心了。}\n# en: Lower Pell's prices\nopt.right.label [8b354b] = 降低佩尔的价钱\n# en: {Cheaper remedies, longer hours, a full shop. Pell grumbles and is secretly delighted.|You match them on price and beat them on care. It's slow, and it works.|You lower the prices and write to the daughters that all is well. It is, nearly. Pell reads your letter over your shoulder and doesn't object.}\nopt.right.say.1 [fd517c] = {更便宜的药，更长的营业时间，满满一铺子人。佩尔嘴上抱怨，心里暗暗高兴。|你在价钱上追平他们，在用心上胜过他们。很慢，但管用。|你降了价，写信告诉女儿们一切都好。差不多算好。佩尔在你肩后读了信，没有反对。}\n\n@event courier_chief_urgent_crossing\n# en: The Urgent Crossing\ntitle [50b65f] = 紧急穿越\n# en: {A red-sealed package for the far side of the mountains, by tomorrow, and only the chief courier flies red seals.|Another red seal, another gale over the pass.|The red seals come to you by name now.} {The pass is in cloud.|A storm is coming in from the west.|The package is humming.}\ntext [d958a7] = {一个盖着红色火漆的包裹，明天要送到山那边，只有首席信使能送红漆件。|又一个红漆件，山口上又是一阵大风。|如今红漆件都指名给你。}{山口被云遮住了。|一场风暴正从西边过来。|包裹在嗡嗡作响。}\n# en: A courier on a broom flying through a mountain pass in a gale, a red-sealed package clutched to their chest, lightning on the peaks.\nscene [75ad0b] = 一个骑着飞帚的信使在大风中穿过山口，怀里紧抱着一个红漆包裹，山峰上电闪雷鸣。\n# en: Fly the pass tonight\nopt.left.label [8a3c7b] = 今晚就飞过山口\n# en: {Through the pass in the dark, lightning on both sides. You deliver at dawn to a pale woman who breaks the seal and weeps with relief. The bonus is the biggest of your life.|You make it, frozen, at dawn. The recipient signs with shaking hands.|The humming package hums louder in the pass and the wind stays off you. You deliver at dawn and don't ask what it was.}\nopt.left.say.1 [15256d] = {你在黑暗中穿过山口，两侧都是闪电。天亮时你把包裹交给一个脸色苍白的女人，她拆开火漆，如释重负地哭了。这是你一生中最大的一笔奖金。|你冻僵了，天亮时赶到。收件人签字时双手发抖。|那个嗡嗡作响的包裹在山口里响得更大声，风一直没扑到你身上。你天亮时送到了，没问那是什么。}\n# en: {The gale takes you into a snowfield. You walk the last mile with the package and a broken wrist, and arrive late.|You crash short of the pass and are found by a goatherd. The package arrives a day late, via goat.|The humming package hums a note the lightning likes. You land hard and walk it in, late, with the hair on your arms standing up.}\nopt.left.say.2 [c99d6d] = {大风把你卷进了雪原。你带着包裹和一只断了的手腕走完最后一英里，到得晚了。|你在山口前坠落，被一个牧羊人发现。包裹晚到了一天，是羊驮去的。|那个嗡嗡作响的包裹哼出了一个闪电喜欢的音。你重重落地，走着把它送到，晚了，胳膊上的汗毛全竖着。}\n# en: Wait for the storm to pass\nopt.right.label [fd0d0f] = 等风暴过去\n# en: {You wait out the storm and fly at dawn. A day late. The recipient is gracious, and the loft-master isn't.|Late but whole. You'll always wonder what was in it.|You wait out the storm with the humming package on the table. It hums all night. You don't sleep.}\nopt.right.say.1 [676ccb] = {你等风暴过去，天亮才飞。晚了一天。收件人很宽厚，阁楼主管可不。|晚了，但人是完整的。你会一直好奇里面装的是什么。|你等着暴风雨过去，那个嗡嗡作响的包裹就放在桌上。它哼了一整夜。你没睡着。}\n\n@event courier_chief_new_runner\n# en: The New Runner\ntitle [c54f90] = 新来的跑腿\n# en: {The loft has a new runner, younger than you were, who holds the broom like a mop.|Another new runner, another bad grip.|The new runners all ask for you now.} {Their first route is across the lake.|They've never flown in rain.|Their parents are watching from the gate.}\ntext [fcaa4e] = {阁楼来了个新跑腿，比你当年还小，握飞帚像握拖把。|又一个新跑腿，又一个错误的握法。|如今新跑腿们都点名要你带。}{他们的第一条路线要飞过湖。|他们从没在雨里飞过。|他们的父母在门口看着。}\n# en: A courier loft at dawn, a nervous young runner holding a broom the wrong way round, a chief courier showing them the grip.\nscene [7eddf7] = 清晨的信使阁楼，一个紧张的年轻跑腿把飞帚拿反了，一位首席信使在教他们怎么握。\n# en: Fly their first route with them\nopt.left.label [03c8ff] = 陪他们飞第一条路线\n# en: {You fly beside them across the lake, calling the wind. They land shaking and grinning. They'll name a broom after you, they say.|A slow route, flown twice. They'll be good, one day.|You fly their first route with their parents watching from the gate. They land perfectly and pretend it was nothing.}\nopt.left.say.1 [6979fa] = {你在他们身边飞过湖面，替他们喊出风向。他们落地时发着抖，咧着嘴笑。他们说要用你的名字给飞帚命名。|一条慢悠悠的路线，飞了两遍。他们总有一天会飞得很好。|他们的父母在门口看着，你陪他们飞了第一条路线。他们落得完美无缺，还装作若无其事。}\n# en: Let them learn on their own\nopt.right.label [4c5eb3] = 让他们自己学\n# en: {They come back soaked and late, with every parcel. You were the same.|They learn the hard way. So did you.|You let them go alone with their parents watching. They wobble, recover, and wave to the gate. You breathe again.}\nopt.right.say.1 [8df8f9] = {他们回来时浑身湿透，晚了，但每个包裹都送到了。你当年也一样。|他们吃了苦头才学会。你也是。|你让他们在父母的注视下独自出发。他们晃了晃，稳住了，朝门口挥手。你这才又喘过气来。}\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/pools/village_home.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/pools/village_home.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event village_barge_crossing\n# en: The Barge Across\ntitle [bac81a] = 渡湖驳船\n# en: {The village barge is slower than walking, if you could walk on water.|The bargeman has started nodding at you like a regular.|The bargeman saves you the dry end of the bench.} {season == \"winter\": Ice ticks against the hull.|{season == \"summer\": Dragonflies ride the bow for free.|{A first-year is being sick, very politely.|Two prefects are pretending not to hold hands.|Someone is reading aloud to a crate of chickens.}}}\ntext [26d4cd] = {去小镇的驳船慢得很——如果你能在水上走路，走过去都比它快。|船夫开始像对待熟客那样冲你点头了。|船夫开始给你留长凳上干的那一头了。} {season == \"winter\": 薄冰一下下轻敲着船身。|{season == \"summer\": 蜻蜓停在船头，免费搭船。|{一个一年级生正趴在船边呕吐，吐得非常有礼貌。|两个级长正假装没在牵手。|有人在给一笼鸡大声朗读。}}}\n# en: A flat-bottomed barge crossing a mirror-still lake, the seven towers shrinking behind it.\nscene [456c6d] = 一艘平底驳船驶过镜面般平静的湖，七座塔在身后渐渐变小。\n# en: Read on the crossing\nopt.left.label [467133] = 渡湖时看书\n# en: {You finish a chapter and miss the towers turning gold behind you.|You read the same paragraph four times; the lake keeps interrupting.|You look up only when the hull bumps the quay, and feel you've skipped something.}\nopt.left.say.1 [7d78ba] = {你读完一章，却错过了身后的塔楼被夕阳染成金色。|同一段你读了四遍；湖光总来打岔。|直到船身撞上码头你才抬头，总觉得错过了什么。}\n# en: Chat with the bargeman\nopt.right.label [89b231] = 跟船夫聊天\n# en: {He tells you the lake was rougher in his father's day, and expects a tip for the history.|He has opinions about every tower and charges for none of them, except in tips.|He asks after your exams as if they were a sick relative. You tip him for the sympathy.}\nopt.right.say.1 [886987] = {他说他父亲那时候湖上风浪大得多，并且指望你为这段历史付点小费。|他对每座塔都有看法，一概免费——小费除外。|他问起你的考试，口气像在问候一位生病的亲戚。你为这份同情付了小费。}\n\n@event village_square_errand\n# en: One Thing in the Square\ntitle [e25a84] = 广场上的“就一件事”\n# en: {friend} has come down to the village with you \"for one thing\", which turns out to be several. {First, a hat.|You've learned that \"one thing\" means at least three.|You've stopped asking what the one thing is.} {A stallholder is selling candied chestnuts in paper twists.|A tinker is mending a cauldron with a great deal of swearing.|The market cat has chosen {friend} and will not be moved.}\ntext [f59a25] = {friend}陪你下山到小镇，说是“就办一件事”，结果是好几件。{先是一顶帽子。|你已经明白，“一件事”至少意味着三件。|你已经不再问那“一件事”是什么了。}{一个摊贩在卖纸包的糖炒栗子。|一个补锅匠正骂骂咧咧地修一口坩埚。|集市上的猫选中了{friend}，怎么也赶不走。}\n# en: A crooked village market square, two students weaving between stalls, one already carrying too many parcels.\nscene [52254a] = 歪歪扭扭的小镇集市广场，两个学生在摊位间穿行，其中一个已经抱了太多包裹。\n# en: Carry the parcels\nopt.left.label [faeb8d] = 帮忙拎包裹\n# en: {You come back up the hill with an armful of string-tied parcels and sore wrists.|{friend} buys you a chestnut as payment. It is not enough payment.|One parcel clinks suspiciously. You decide not to ask.}\nopt.left.say.1 [c7a573] = {你抱着一摞系着绳子的包裹爬回山上，手腕酸痛。|{friend}买了颗栗子当报酬。这报酬远远不够。|有个包裹叮当作响，十分可疑。你决定不问。}\n# en: Talk {them} out of it\nopt.right.label [2a8f41] = 劝{them}别买了\n# en: {You talk {friend} out of the third thing. {They} sulk{s} the whole way back to the barge.|You win. {friend} says you'd make a terrible shopkeeper and a worse friend, then takes your arm anyway.|You talk {friend} out of the third thing. The market cat follows {them} home anyway, which is a sort of purchase.}\nopt.right.say.1 [41937f] = {你劝{friend}放弃了第三样东西。{They}一路生着闷气回到驳船上。|你赢了。{friend}说你会是个糟糕的店主、更糟糕的朋友，然后照样挽起你的胳膊。|你劝{friend}放弃了第三样东西。可市场的猫还是跟着{them}回了家，这也算买了点什么。}\n# en: Your argument is excellent. {friend} buys the thing anyway, and now you've been wrong in public.\nopt.right.say.2 [c1ae81] = 你的论证无懈可击。{friend}还是买了，于是你当众输了一回。\n\n@event village_last_barge\n# en: The Last Barge\ntitle [d7085d] = 末班驳船\n# en: {The last barge back leaves at the bell, and the bell is ringing.|The last-barge bell, and you're nowhere near the quay. The bargeman knows your running by now.|The bell. Of course the bell.} {season == \"winter\": Snow is coming down thick over the square.|{The inn has started a song with too many verses.|The lamps are only half lit.|The lake has gone the colour of pewter.}} {final_year: Not many evenings like this are left.|You could run. You could stay.}\ntext [52b86c] = {回城堡的末班驳船随钟声开船，而钟声正在响。|末班船的钟声响了，你离码头还远着呢。船夫早就熟悉你奔跑的样子了。|钟声。当然是钟声。} {season == \"winter\": 大雪正纷纷扬扬落在广场上。|{酒馆里有人唱起一首段落多得没完的歌。|街灯才点亮一半。|湖水变成了锡镴般的灰色。}} {final_year: 这样的夜晚剩下的不多了。|你可以跑。你也可以留下。}\n# en: A lamplit village quay at dusk, the last barge pulling at its rope, a student hesitating at the edge of the square.\nscene [aa10dd] = 黄昏时灯火点点的小镇码头，末班驳船拽着缆绳，一个学生在广场边犹豫。\n# en: Run for it\nopt.left.label [38d292] = 跑去赶船\n# en: {You make it with a leap and one wet boot.|You make it; your scarf does not.|The bargeman holds the rope a heartbeat longer than he should. You owe him.}\nopt.left.say.1 [e72148] = {你纵身一跃赶上了，湿了一只靴子。|你赶上了；你的围巾没赶上。|船夫多攥了一会儿缆绳，比他该攥的久一点。你欠他个人情。}\n# en: Stay a little longer\nopt.right.label [4a025a] = 再待一会儿\n# en: {A fisherman rows you back for a ruinous fare and a story you didn't ask for.|You pay the night ferryman far too much. He rows like he has somewhere better to be.|The fisherman who rows you back knows you by now and charges you the regular's rate, which is still a great deal.}\nopt.right.say.1 [238628] = {一个渔夫划船送你回去，要价高得离谱，还附赠一个你没想听的故事。|你付给夜间摆渡人太多钱了。他划船的样子，像是有更要紧的地方要去。|划船送你回去的渔夫现在认得你了，给你算的是熟客价，那也还是很贵。}\n# en: You have no fare for the night ferryman, so you sleep on a bench under the Newt's eaves and wake with a cat on your feet.\nopt.right.say.2 [2c3fa2] = 你付不起夜里船夫的船钱，只好在蝾螈酒馆的屋檐下睡了一夜长凳，醒来时脚上趴着一只猫。\n# en: {You sleep on a bench under the Newt's eaves and wake up with a cat on your feet.|The first barge of morning finds you yawning on the quay.|You sleep under the Newt's eaves again. The cat has started saving you the warm end of the bench.}\nopt.right.say.3 [dab239] = {你在蝾螈酒馆的屋檐下睡了一夜长凳，醒来时脚上趴着一只猫。|清晨的头班驳船发现你正在码头上打哈欠。|你又在蝾螈酒馆的屋檐下睡了一夜。那只猫开始给你留长凳暖和的那一头了。}\n\n@event gilded_newt_fireside\n# en: By the Newt's Fire\ntitle [5b6147] = 蝾螈的炉火边\n# en: {The Gilded Newt's fire has never gone out, and the landlady says it never will while she lives.|The landlady chalks your name on her slate before you've ordered.|Your corner by the fire is free. The regulars leave it for you now.} {season == \"winter\": Snow hisses off the boots of everyone who comes in.|{A dog is asleep with its paws in the hearth.|Someone has hung wet socks on the fire-irons.|A farmer is explaining turnips to nobody.}}\ntext [988636] = {镀金蝾螈酒馆的炉火从没熄过，老板娘说只要她还活着，就永远不会熄。|你还没点单，老板娘就已经在石板上写好了你的名字。|炉边你的那个角落空着。常客们现在都把它留给你。} {season == \"winter\": 每个进门的人靴子上的雪都在嘶嘶作响。|{一条狗把爪子搭在炉边睡着了。|有人把湿袜子挂在了火钳上。|一个农夫正对着空气讲解芜菁。}}\n# en: A low-beamed tavern with a roaring hearth, a student tucked into the corner seat beside it with a steaming cup.\nscene [886f83] = 低矮房梁的酒馆里炉火熊熊，一个学生窝在炉边的角落座位，捧着一杯冒热气的饮料。\n# en: Order a cider and stay\nopt.left.label [fc74cf] = 点杯苹果酒坐着\n# en: {The cider is warm and spiced and costs exactly what the landlady says it does.|You stay until your cheeks are red and your worries are smaller.|The landlady brings the cider without being asked, and charges for it all the same.}\nopt.left.say.1 [d21078] = {苹果酒温热、带着香料味，价钱分毫不差，正如老板娘所说。|你一直坐到脸颊发红，烦恼也变小了。|老板娘没等你开口就端来了苹果酒，钱照收不误。}\n# en: Work by the firelight\nopt.right.label [01152e] = 借着炉光干活\n# en: {You get two pages done between three toasts and a fiddle.|The noise is terrible, and somehow you concentrate better than in the library.|A regular reads over your shoulder and corrects your spelling of \"wyvern\".}\nopt.right.say.1 [ba7f3e] = {在三轮祝酒和一段提琴之间，你写完了两页。|吵得要命，可不知为何你比在图书馆里还专心。|一位常客从你肩后看过来，纠正了你“双足飞龙”的拼写。}\n\n@event gilded_newt_card_game\n# en: Cards at the Newt\ntitle [cae7f0] = 蝾螈的牌局\n# en: {There's a card game in the back room of the Gilded Newt, and an empty chair.|The empty chair in the back room has a cushion on it now, as if it's been waiting.|{flag(newt_cards_played): The card players greet you by the name of your worst hand.|The card players nod you towards the chair as if you were a regular.}} {Tonight it's a tinker, a widow and a man who shuffles like a conjuror.|Tonight the stakes are copper and pride.|Tonight a second-year from Duskgate is losing very cheerfully.}\ntext [9a54d0] = {镀金蝾螈的后屋有一局牌，还有一把空椅子。|后屋那把空椅子上如今多了个坐垫，像是一直在等你。|{flag(newt_cards_played): 牌友们用你最烂的那手牌给你起了外号来打招呼。|牌友们朝那把椅子努努嘴，好像你是常客。}} {今晚是一个补锅匠、一个寡妇，还有一个洗牌像变戏法的男人。|今晚的赌注是铜板和面子。|今晚一个暮门的二年级生正输得兴高采烈。}\n# en: A smoky back room in a tavern, a card game around a scarred table and one empty chair.\nscene [939cdf] = 烟雾缭绕的酒馆后屋，一张满是划痕的桌上正在打牌，还有一把空椅子。\n# en: Take the empty chair\nopt.left.label [2c28bd] = 坐上那把空椅子\n# en: The stake is a few crowns a hand.\nopt.left.hint [dc8887] = 每手的赌注是几枚克朗。\n# en: {You win a handful of crowns and a reputation for an honest face.|You win, and the widow asks where you learned to bluff like that.|The Duskgate second-year loses to you as cheerfully as to everyone. You win a few crowns and an invitation to lose them back.}\nopt.left.say.1 [6273ff] = {你赢了一把克朗，还落了个“老实面相”的名声。|你赢了，寡妇问你从哪儿学的这手虚张声势。|那个暮门的二年级生输给你时和输给别人一样开心。你赢了几枚克朗，外加一份把它们输回去的邀请。}\n# en: {You lose your crowns slowly, and then all at once.|The tinker takes your last coin and buys you a drink with it, which somehow makes it worse.|The Duskgate second-year wins for once, delighted. You lose your stake and keep your good humour, mostly.}\nopt.left.say.2 [498b22] = {你的克朗先是慢慢地输，然后一下子全没了。|补锅匠赢走你最后一枚硬币，又用它请你喝了一杯，不知怎的更难受了。|那个暮门的二年级生难得赢了一回，乐坏了。你输了赌注，好心情大体保住了。}\n# en: Watch for the tells\nopt.right.label [f1d424] = 观察他们的破绽\n# en: {You spend the evening watching. The man who shuffles like a conjuror scratches his ear when he lies.|You learn a great deal about bluffing and nothing about having fun.|Someone asks if you're playing or haunting. You leave before you have to answer.}\nopt.right.say.1 [879773] = {你看了一整晚。那个洗牌像变戏法的男人，撒谎时会挠耳朵。|你学到了很多虚张声势的门道，却一点乐子也没有。|有人问你是来打牌的还是来闹鬼的。你没等回答就走了。}\n\n@event gilded_newt_corner_table\n# en: The Corner Table\ntitle [30043d] = 角落那张桌\n# en: {here} is at the corner table with {a jug of cider and no plans to leave.|the house jug, which the landlady refills for whoever sits there.|the table's jug. The landlady nods at you as you come in, as if you're expected.} \"{Sit down, you're blocking the fire.|You look like you need a drink. I look like I need company.|Don't tell anyone you saw me here.}\" {season == \"winter\": Snow is piling up against the window.|The window is open to the night.}\ntext [3523fb] = {here}坐在角落那张桌旁，{面前一壶苹果酒，完全没有走的意思。|面前是店里那只专属酒壶，谁坐在那儿，老板娘就给谁续满。|面前是那张桌的酒壶。你进门时老板娘冲你点点头，好像早料到你会来。}“{坐下吧，你挡着火了。|你看上去需要喝一杯。我看上去需要个伴。|别告诉别人你在这儿见过我。}”{season == \"winter\": 雪正堆在窗台上。|窗户朝着夜色敞开。}\n# en: A tavern corner table with a half-empty jug of cider, someone familiar waving a student over.\nscene [5c8294] = 酒馆角落的桌上放着半壶苹果酒，一个熟面孔正招手叫学生过去。\n# en: Sit down for one drink\nopt.left.label [e16ef4] = 坐下喝一杯\n# en: {One drink becomes three stories and a long walk to the barge.|{here} tells you something true halfway down the jug, then pretends not to have.|You stay later than you meant to. It's worth the morning.}\nopt.left.say.1 [2314b6] = {一杯变成了三个故事，外加一段走回驳船的长路。|喝到半壶时{here}跟你说了句真心话，然后装作没说过。|你待得比打算的晚。明早难熬，但值得。}\n# en: Wave and keep walking\nopt.right.label [11b59d] = 挥挥手继续走\n# en: {You wave and get back to your book. {here} toasts your back, a little hurt.|{term: You plead an early lecture. It's even true, and you read for it before bed.|You plead an early start, and read in your room instead.}|You wave. {here} raises the jug at you as if you'll be back. You will.}\nopt.right.say.1 [807665] = {你挥挥手，回去看你的书。{here}冲你的背影举杯，有点受伤。|{term: 你推说明早有课。这居然是真的，睡前你还为它预习了。|你推说明天要早起，回房间看书去了。}|你挥挥手。{here}朝你举起酒壶，好像笃定你会回来。你会的。}\n\n@event mabels_the_door\n# en: Mabel Reads the Door\ntitle [d9674c] = 梅布尔看门识人\n# en: {Mabel looks up as you open the door and is already reaching for a tin.|Mabel watches you open the door the way a doctor watches a cough.|You've tried opening Mabel's door every way you can think of. She still knows.} {The bell over the door rings a little flat today.|There's a queue of two, which for Mabel's is a crowd.|A cake is cooling on the counter, out of reach and smug about it.}\ntext [3862f4] = {你一推门，梅布尔抬起头，手已经伸向了一个茶罐。|梅布尔看你推门的样子，就像医生听人咳嗽。|你试过用各种方式推梅布尔的门。她照样看得出来。} {今天门上的铃铛响得有点走调。|店里排着两个人的队，对梅布尔茶馆来说这已经算拥挤了。|柜台上晾着一块蛋糕，够不着，还一脸得意。}\n# en: A tiny tea shop crammed with tins, the shop-bell over the door still swinging, a shrewd woman behind the counter already reaching for a tin.\nscene [8b72a6] = 塞满茶罐的小茶馆，门上的铃铛还在摇晃，柜台后一位精明的妇人已经伸手去拿茶罐。\n# en: Let Mabel choose your tea\nopt.left.label [d2a63d] = 让梅布尔替你选\n# en: {She brings you something smoky and says, \"For the week you've had.\" You hadn't told her about the week.|She brings you something with rose in it and won't explain why. It helps.|It's mint. It's always mint when you've been lying to yourself, apparently.}\nopt.left.say.1 [f17eac] = {她端来一杯带烟熏味的茶，说：“给你这一周喝的。”你可没跟她提过这一周。|她端来一杯有玫瑰味的茶，也不解释为什么。挺管用。|是薄荷茶。据说你自欺欺人的时候，永远是薄荷茶。}\n# en: Fake a cheerful entrance\nopt.right.label [35b845] = 装出高兴的样子进门\n# en: {She brings you the cheerful tea and waves your coins away, \"for the performance\". It's so good you actually cheer up, which may have been her plan all along.|Mabel looks fooled, and doesn't charge you. Then she adds a biscuit, which means she wasn't.|You've practised the door. Mabel gives you the cheerful tea and a long look, and charges you for neither.}\nopt.right.say.1 [bf7468] = {她端来了“开心的茶”，摆摆手不收你的钱，说是“看在表演的分上”。好喝得你真的开心起来——说不定这本来就是她的打算。|梅布尔一副被骗过去的样子，没收你的钱。然后她多给了你一块饼干，说明她根本没被骗。|你练过怎么开门了。梅布尔给了你开心的茶，又久久地看了你一眼，两样都没收钱。}\n# en: {She brings you a pot labelled, in her own hand, \"for people pretending\". She doesn't charge for it, which is somehow worse.|\"Nice try, dear,\" says Mabel, and brings the strong one, on the house.|Mabel watches your cheerful entrance the way a doctor watches a cough, and brings the strong one, free.}\nopt.right.say.2 [ec9730] = {她端来一壶茶，壶上是她亲手写的标签：“给装样子的人”。她没收钱，这反而更糟。|“想得美，亲爱的。”梅布尔说完，端来了浓的那种，算店里请的。|梅布尔像医生看咳嗽一样看着你开心地进门，然后端来了浓的那种，不收钱。}\n\n@event mabels_window_seat\n# en: The Window Seat\ntitle [15c249] = 靠窗的座位\n# en: {Mabel's window seat looks out over the lake to the castle, and it's free.|Mabel has stopped asking if you're waiting for someone at the window seat.|Your window seat. Mabel puts a cushion on it when she sees you on the quay.} {season == \"winter\": Frost has drawn ferns on the glass.|{season == \"spring\": Blossom keeps landing on the sill.|{The towers look small from here, almost friendly.|Rain streaks the glass and blurs the towers.|Below, the barges come and go, slow as thoughts.}}}\ntext [1bd9bd] = {梅布尔茶馆的靠窗座位能望见湖对岸的城堡，而且正空着。|你坐在靠窗座位时，梅布尔已经不再问你是不是在等人了。|你的靠窗座位。梅布尔一看见你在码头，就往上放个软垫。} {season == \"winter\": 霜在玻璃上画出了蕨叶。|{season == \"spring\": 花瓣不停落在窗台上。|{从这儿看，塔楼显得小小的，几乎有点亲切。|雨水顺着玻璃流下，把塔楼晕成一片。|下面，驳船来来往往，慢得像思绪。}}}\n# en: A window seat in a tea shop looking across a still lake to seven distant towers, a teapot and open notes on the sill.\nscene [36406e] = 茶馆的靠窗座位，隔着平静的湖望见远处七座塔，窗台上放着茶壶和摊开的笔记。\n# en: Make one pot last all day\nopt.left.label [abe18d] = 一壶茶坐一整天\n# en: {You get through a whole chapter on the one free pot Mabel gives window-seat readers, under her increasingly pointed looks.|By the third hour the tea is cold and Mabel is dusting very close to your table.|You finish your notes. Mabel says nothing, which is worse.}\nopt.left.say.1 [aed9c1] = {梅布尔给坐窗边看书的人免费续一壶茶，你靠这一壶读完了整整一章，她的眼神越来越意味深长。|到第三个钟头，茶凉了，梅布尔在离你桌子很近的地方掸灰。|你写完了笔记。梅布尔一句话也没说，这更可怕。}\n# en: Order cake with it\nopt.right.label [8d317c] = 再配块蛋糕\n# en: {flag(mabel_lemon): {Mabel has started cutting the lemon cake before you sit down.|Mabel brings the lemon without asking. You eat it slowly while the barges come and go.|The lemon cake, the window, the lake. The work can wait.}|Mabel has three cakes. You pick the lemon, and it's worth every crown.}\nopt.right.say.1 [9d5597] = {flag(mabel_lemon): {梅布尔开始在你坐下之前就切好柠檬蛋糕了。|梅布尔没等你开口就端来了柠檬蛋糕。你慢慢吃着，看驳船来来去去。|柠檬蛋糕、窗户、湖。功课可以等一等。}|梅布尔有三种蛋糕。你挑了柠檬味的，每个克朗都花得值。}\n\n@event mabels_forty_teas\n# en: Forty Kinds of Tea\ntitle [e3533b] = 四十种茶\n# en: {friend} wants to try one of Mabel's forty teas, and Mabel insists on choosing. {Today she starts a chart of who has tried what.|Mabel's chart of your friends' teas is pinned behind the counter now; {friend}'s column gets a tick.|Mabel's chart runs to a second sheet. {friend}'s column is the shortest, and Mabel looks at it pointedly.} {Mabel pretends not to be interested.|Mabel has opinions about the order.|Mabel insists on the order, and on the chart.}\ntext [beab52] = {friend}想尝尝梅布尔的四十种茶之一，而梅布尔坚持由她来选。{今天她开始画一张表，记下谁喝过什么。|梅布尔那张记录你朋友们喝茶的表，如今钉在了柜台后面；{friend}那一栏添了一个勾。|梅布尔的表已经画到第二页了。{friend}那一栏最短，梅布尔意味深长地盯着它。}{梅布尔假装不感兴趣。|梅布尔对喝的顺序很有意见。|梅布尔坚持顺序，也坚持那张表。}\n# en: Two students at a small tea-shop table crowded with mismatched teapots, one writing in a notebook.\nscene [b50dbf] = 两个学生坐在茶馆的小桌旁，桌上挤满了不成套的茶壶，其中一人在本子上写着什么。\n# en: Pay for both pots\nopt.left.label [76b503] = 两壶都我请\n# en: {{friend} pronounces today's tea \"bracing\", which means awful. You drink yours anyway.|You pay. Mabel marks you on the chart as a patron of the arts.|{friend} declares it the best tea in the village, then sniffs yours to be sure.}\nopt.left.say.1 [77f68a] = {{friend}宣布今天的茶“提神”，意思是难喝。你还是把自己那杯喝完了。|你付了钱。梅布尔在表上把你记作“艺术赞助人”。|{friend}宣布这是全镇最好的茶，然后凑过来闻了闻你那杯，以防万一。}\n# en: Let {friend} pay this time\nopt.right.label [586795] = 这次让{friend}请客\n# en: {{friend} pays, and calls you unadventurous for ordering your usual. Your tea is perfect, thank you.|\"Coward,\" says {friend} fondly, paying for your usual and their something purple.|{friend} pays, grumbling, and drinks something that smells of bonfires and looks betrayed by it.}\nopt.right.say.1 [2a1c2b] = {{friend}付了钱，还说你点老样子太不爱冒险。你的茶好得很，谢谢。|“胆小鬼，”{friend}亲昵地说，一边替你的老样子和自己那杯紫色的东西付了钱。|{friend}嘟囔着付了钱，喝了一杯有篝火味的茶，一脸被背叛的表情。}\n\n@event grimsbys_wrong_book\n# en: Not the Book You Wanted\ntitle [480464] = 不是你要的那本\n# en: {You ask Mr Grimsby for a textbook. He vanishes into the shelves and comes back with something else entirely.|You ask Mr Grimsby for one thing. He brings you another, with great confidence.|Mr Grimsby sees you coming and is already halfway up a ladder, looking for the wrong book.} {Today's is a cookery book with a whole chapter on eels.|Today's is a history of a town that may not exist.|Today's is a guide to the moods of weather.}\ntext [9e8037] = {你向格林斯比先生要一本教科书。他消失在书架间，拿回来的完全是另一本。|你向格林斯比先生要一样东西。他信心十足地拿来另一样。|格林斯比先生一看见你进门，就已经爬上半截梯子，去找那本错的书了。} {今天这本是菜谱，有整整一章讲鳗鱼。|今天这本是一座可能并不存在的小镇的历史。|今天这本是一本讲天气脾性的指南。}\n# en: A bookshop so crowded the shelves lean together, an elderly bookseller on a ladder triumphantly holding up the wrong book.\nscene [ea591e] = 挤得书架互相倚靠的书店里，一位老书商站在梯子上，得意地举着一本错的书。\n# en: Buy what he found\nopt.left.label [8212ca] = 买下他找到的书\n# en: {It's useless and wonderful. You read half of it on the barge.|You learn more than you meant to about something you never meant to learn.|There's a pressed flower in it, and a name you don't know on the flyleaf.}\nopt.left.say.1 [012e04] = {它毫无用处，又妙不可言。你在驳船上就读了一半。|你学到了比预想更多的东西，关于一件你从没打算学的事。|书里夹着一朵压干的花，扉页上写着一个你不认识的名字。}\n# en: Search the shelves yourself\nopt.right.label [68f103] = 自己去书架上找\n# en: {You find it behind a stack of almanacs, exactly where he said it wasn't, and read the chapter you need at the counter.|You find it. Mr Grimsby looks wounded and lets you read it at the counter for free, since you found it yourself.|You find it yourself, above the weather guides. Mr Grimsby insists it's a weather guide too, in a way.}\nopt.right.say.1 [81b56d] = {你在一摞年鉴后面找到了它，正是他说不在的地方，就在柜台边读完了你要的那一章。|你找到了。格林斯比先生一脸受伤，因为是你自己找到的，就让你在柜台边免费读了。|你在天气指南上面一层自己找到了它。格林斯比先生坚持说，某种意义上它也算天气指南。}\n# en: {A shelf leans on you for support. You hold it up for a long time.|An afternoon, a sneezing fit, and no book.|You search and find only more guides to the moods of weather. The weather today is a sulk.}\nopt.right.say.2 [8c1e75] = {一个书架靠在你身上寻求支撑。你扶了它很久。|一个下午，一阵喷嚏，没有书。|你找了半天，只找到更多关于天气脾性的指南。今天的天气是在赌气。}\n\n@event grimsbys_damp_barrow\n# en: The Damp Barrow\ntitle [b887f1] = 受潮的书车\n# en: {Outside Grimsby's there's a barrow of secondhand books, a coin apiece, slightly damp.|The barrow has a new sign: \"NO READING WITHOUT BUYING\". Nobody obeys it.|The barrow. You know its smell now: rain, glue and other people's thoughts.} {season == \"winter\": Someone has thrown a sack over it against the sleet.|{The top book is about knots.|A cat is asleep on the poetry.|A card in the corner says \"NOT RUDE, JUST OLD\".}}\ntext [491f09] = {格林斯比书店门外有一车二手书，一枚硬币一本，略微受潮。|书车上多了块新牌子：“不买不许看。”没人理会。|那辆书车。你现在认得它的味道了：雨水、胶水，还有别人的思绪。} {season == \"winter\": 有人往上面盖了只麻袋挡雨雪。|{最上面那本讲的是打绳结。|一只猫睡在诗集上。|角落里一张卡片写着：“不是没礼貌，只是旧了。”}}\n# en: A wooden barrow of damp secondhand books outside a crooked bookshop, a student reading one in the drizzle.\nscene [908d11] = 歪斜的书店外，一辆木推车装满受潮的二手书，一个学生在细雨中读着其中一本。\n# en: Buy a damp book\nopt.left.label [428d93] = 买本受潮的书\n# en: {It dries out wavy and full of someone else's underlinings.|The margins are better than the book. Someone argued with every page.|The last page is a shopping list in faded ink. You find it oddly moving.}\nopt.left.say.1 [94105c] = {书晾干后皱巴巴的，满是别人画的线。|页边批注比正文还精彩。有人跟每一页都吵过架。|最后一页是一张褪色的购物清单。你莫名有些感动。}\n# en: Read it standing in the rain\nopt.right.label [e908fc] = 站在雨里读\n# en: {You read until your feet are cold and Mr Grimsby taps on the window.|You read a whole chapter in the drizzle and don't buy it. You feel like a thief and a scholar at once.|A previous owner has written \"Don't believe this bit\" in the margin. You don't.}\nopt.right.say.1 [5daa57] = {你一直读到脚冻僵，格林斯比先生敲了敲窗户。|你在细雨里读完一整章，却没买。你觉得自己既像小偷又像学者。|一位前主人在页边写着“这段别信”。你没信。}\n\n@event grimsbys_last_copy\n# en: The Last Copy\ntitle [370a21] = 最后一本\n# en: There's one copy left of the book you need, and {friend} has {their} hand on it too. {You both laugh. Neither of you lets go.|This keeps happening to you at Grimsby's. You're starting to suspect the shop.|Mr Grimsby swears he ordered two. It never arrives as two.} {\"I saw it first,\" {they} say{s}.|\"We could share,\" {they} offer{s}, unconvincingly.|Mr Grimsby says there might be another in the cellar. There won't.}\ntext [8d6c4c] = 你要的那本书只剩一本了，而{friend}的手也搭在上面。{你们都笑了。谁也没松手。|在格林斯比书店，这种事总落到你头上。你开始怀疑是这家店在搞鬼。|格林斯比先生发誓他订了两本。到货时从来都只有一本。}{“我先看到的。”{they}说。|“我们可以合着看。”{they}提议，毫无说服力。|格林斯比先生说地窖里也许还有一本。不会有的。}\n# en: Two students in a cramped bookshop, both holding the same book, neither letting go.\nscene [ec8868] = 拥挤的书店里，两个学生握着同一本书，谁也不肯放手。\n# en: Let {them} have it\nopt.left.label [e68f07] = 让给{them}\n# en: {{friend} promises to lend it to you \"very soon\". You know what that means.|You let go. {friend} buys you a bun on the way back, which nearly makes up for it.|{friend} reads it on the barge and tells you all the best parts, in the wrong order.}\nopt.left.say.1 [f54922] = {{friend}答应“很快”就借给你。你知道那是什么意思。|你松了手。{friend}回去路上请你吃了个面包，差不多算扯平了。|{friend}在驳船上就读了，把最精彩的部分讲给你听，顺序全是乱的。}\n# en: Haggle for it\nopt.right.label [3e6001] = 讨价还价抢下它\n# en: {Mr Grimsby takes your side for reasons of his own. {friend} won't speak to you until the quay.|You win it fair and square, and {friend} says so in a voice that means the opposite.|You haggle, and win. Mr Grimsby promises {friend} the second copy he ordered. You both know it won't come.}\nopt.right.say.1 [5e588d] = {格林斯比先生出于自己的理由站在你这边。{friend}一直到码头都没理你。|你光明正大地赢了，{friend}也这么说，只是语气意思正相反。|你讨价还价，赢了。格林斯比先生答应把他订的第二本给{friend}。你们俩都知道那本不会来。}\n# en: {{friend} outbids you, and Mr Grimsby hands your crowns back with a sympathetic look.|You argue so long that a third-year buys it while you're both talking. Mr Grimsby gives your crowns back.|{friend} wins, and Mr Grimsby promises you the second copy. It never arrives as two.}\nopt.right.say.2 [05bcc7] = {{friend}出价比你高，格林斯比先生带着同情的眼神把你的克朗退了回来。|你们争了太久，一个三年级生趁你们说话时把书买走了。格林斯比先生把你的克朗退了回来。|{friend}赢了，格林斯比先生答应把第二本给你。它从来不会成双到货。}\n\n@event pell_apothecary_drawers\n# en: A Thousand Drawers\ntitle [e5d6dc] = 一千个抽屉\n# en: {Pell & Daughters has a thousand drawers, each labelled in a different hand.|You find a drawer you'd swear wasn't there last time.|You've read half the labels in the shop. The other half seem to be reading you.} Today it's {\"Moonwort. Do not hum near.\"|\"Dried courage, low quality.\"|\"Owl pellets (sorted).\"} {season == \"winter\": The shop smells of camphor and wet wool.|The shop smells of mint and something burnt.}\ntext [c1af29] = {佩尔父女药铺有一千个抽屉，每个标签的笔迹都不一样。|你找到一个抽屉，敢发誓上次还不在。|店里的标签你读了一半。另一半好像在读你。}今天是{“月草。附近勿哼歌。”|“晒干的勇气，品质较次。”|“猫头鹰食丸（已分拣）。”}{season == \"winter\": 店里一股樟脑和湿羊毛的味道。|店里一股薄荷和什么东西烧焦的味道。}\n# en: An apothecary wall of a thousand tiny labelled drawers, brass handles gleaming, a student reading the labels on tiptoe.\nscene [2f6345] = 药铺的一整面墙是上千个贴着标签的小抽屉，黄铜把手闪闪发亮，一个学生踮着脚读标签。\n# en: Buy a pinch of it\nopt.left.label [a72b3a] = 买一小撮\n# en: {One of the daughters weighs it on scales finer than an eyelash and tells you what it's really for.|It smells of the shop all the way back across the lake.|Old Pell counts the grains himself and warns you about three things you hadn't planned to do.}\nopt.left.say.1 [673e61] = {一个女儿用比睫毛还精细的秤给你称好，告诉你它真正的用途。|回去渡湖的一路上，它都散发着药铺的气味。|老佩尔亲自数了颗粒，还提醒你三件你本来没打算做的事。}\n# en: Just read the labels\nopt.right.label [82b51d] = 只看看标签\n# en: {Old Pell watches you over his spectacles the whole time, as if labels could be stolen.|You copy a dozen labels into your notebook. A daughter asks if you're planning to open a rival shop.|You learn a great deal. Old Pell sighs every time you touch a handle.}\nopt.right.say.1 [73de50] = {老佩尔一直从眼镜上方盯着你，好像标签也能被偷走。|你把十几个标签抄进本子。一个女儿问你是不是打算开家对手店。|你学到了很多。你每碰一下把手，老佩尔就叹一口气。}\n\n@event pell_apothecary_queue\n# en: Symptoms in the Queue\ntitle [ab0a8c] = 排队的病症\n# en: {The queue at Pell & Daughters notices your academy robes. A woman shows you her rash.|Word has got round the queue at Pell's that you're \"the young doctor\".|The queue parts for you as if you were a physician. You are not a physician.} {A farmer has a cough like a door hinge.|A boy has a frog stuck to his hand, and the frog seems content.|An old man wants something for \"the usual\", and won't say what the usual is.}\ntext [12e73c] = {佩尔药铺排队的人注意到了你的学院袍。一位妇人给你看她的疹子。|佩尔药铺的队伍里传开了，大家都叫你“小大夫”。|队伍为你让开一条路，好像你是医师。你不是医师。} {一个农夫咳得像吱呀作响的门轴。|一个男孩手上粘着一只青蛙，青蛙看起来挺满足。|一位老人要买治“老毛病”的药，死活不说是什么毛病。}\n# en: A queue of villagers at an apothecary counter, all turning to a student in academy robes.\nscene [87f566] = 药铺柜台前排着一队镇民，全都转向一个身穿学院袍的学生。\n# en: Offer some advice\nopt.left.label [55dc45] = 给点建议\n# en: {Your advice is sound. One of the daughters nods, which in this shop is a medal.|The farmer tries your remedy and comes back to thank you. Old Pell grumbles about lost trade.|You guess the old man's \"usual\" from the way he walks. Old Pell grunts, which is agreement.}\nopt.left.say.1 [fe74ee] = {你的建议很靠谱。一个女儿点了点头，在这家店里这就是勋章。|农夫试了你的方子，回来向你道谢。老佩尔抱怨生意被抢了。|你从老人走路的样子猜出了他的“老毛病”。老佩尔哼了一声，那是同意。}\n# en: {Old Pell corrects you in front of the whole queue, kindly, which is worse.|Your advice would have worked on a horse. A daughter says so.|You guess the old man's \"usual\" wrong, out loud. He tells the queue what it actually is. Everyone wishes he hadn't.}\nopt.left.say.2 [74e07a] = {老佩尔当着全队人的面纠正了你，语气很和善，这更糟。|你的建议拿去治马倒是管用。一个女儿这么说。|你大声猜错了老人的“老毛病”。他把真正的毛病告诉了整个队伍。大家都希望他没说。}\n# en: Study a drawer intently\nopt.right.label [37493b] = 专心研究抽屉\n# en: {You pretend to be fascinated by a drawer of buttons until the queue moves on.|You say \"Hm\" like Professor Thorne and leave quickly.|You become very interested in the floor. The woman with the rash tells her friend you were rude.}\nopt.right.say.1 [4ab101] = {你假装对一抽屉纽扣着了迷，直到队伍往前挪走。|你学索恩教授说了声“嗯。”，然后迅速离开。|你突然对地板产生了浓厚兴趣。长疹子的妇人跟朋友说你没礼貌。}\n\n@event pell_apothecary_tonic\n# en: The Daughters' Tonic\ntitle [7c5d2b] = 女儿们的补药\n# en: {One of Pell's daughters has a new tonic and needs someone to try it. Her father must not know.|The daughters have another tonic, and a notebook with your name at the top of a column.|The daughters see you and reach under the counter before you've said hello.} {This one is green and fizzes.|This one smells of pears and thunder.|This one is clear, which they say is the worrying kind.}\ntext [9724e2] = {佩尔的一个女儿调了一种新补药，需要有人试喝。绝不能让她父亲知道。|女儿们又调了一种补药，还有个本子，其中一栏最上面写着你的名字。|女儿们一看见你，没等你打招呼就把手伸到了柜台底下。} {这一瓶是绿色的，还冒泡。|这一瓶闻起来像梨和雷雨。|这一瓶是透明的，她们说这是最让人担心的那种。}\n# en: Two apothecary's daughters leaning over a counter, offering a student a small bottle of something that fizzes.\nscene [ecaffe] = 药铺的两个女儿趴在柜台上，递给学生一小瓶冒着泡的东西。\n# en: Drink it down\nopt.left.label [65d992] = 一口喝下\n# en: {You feel wonderful, and briefly taller. The daughters take notes.|Your cold vanishes. So, for an hour, does your sense of smell.|The clear one does nothing at all, then everything at once. You feel marvellous. The daughters look nervous.}\nopt.left.say.1 [c0f9ae] = {你感觉棒极了，还短暂地长高了一点。女儿们记着笔记。|你的感冒消失了。你的嗅觉也消失了一个钟头。|那瓶透明的先是毫无作用，然后一下子全来了。你感觉好极了。女儿们看上去很紧张。}\n# en: {You hiccup bubbles all the way back to the barge.|Your ears go warm and stay warm. The daughters take very interested notes.|The clear one turns your tongue blue. The daughters write \"clear: not clear\" in the notebook.}\nopt.left.say.2 [1be2f5] = {你一路打着冒泡的嗝回到驳船。|你的耳朵发热，而且一直热着。女儿们兴致勃勃地记着笔记。|那瓶透明的把你的舌头染成了蓝色。女儿们在本子上写下“透明：并不透明”。}\n# en: Buy a proper remedy\nopt.right.label [8493b6] = 买副正经药\n# en: {Old Pell sells you something brown and reliable. The daughters look disappointed in you.|You buy the dull, tested thing. It works, dully.|A daughter wraps it and murmurs that you'll be sorry you missed the green one.}\nopt.right.say.1 [c1eb98] = {老佩尔卖给你一种褐色的、靠得住的药。女儿们对你很失望。|你买了那种无聊又经过检验的药。它管用，无聊地管用。|一个女儿一边包药一边嘀咕，说你错过那瓶绿的会后悔的。}\n\n@event bakery_bun_queue\n# en: The Bun Queue\ntitle [b02932] = 买面包的队伍\n# en: {There's a queue outside the bakery for the afternoon batch, and it smells like a promise.|The baker's son counts heads in the bun queue and knows you by your scarf.|The bun queue. You know everyone in it by their order.} {season == \"winter\": Breath steams in the cold.|{season == \"autumn\": Leaves skitter round everyone's ankles.|{The baker's son is singing off-key.|A goat has joined the queue and nobody has the heart to send it away.|The queue is swapping recipes it will never use.}}}\ntext [207118] = {面包房门外为下午那一炉排起了队，空气里的香味像一个承诺。|面包师的儿子数着排队的人头，认得你的围巾。|买面包的队伍。队里每个人你都能按他们点的东西认出来。} {season == \"winter\": 呼出的气在冷空气里冒着白雾。|{season == \"autumn\": 落叶在每个人的脚踝边打转。|{面包师的儿子在跑调地唱歌。|一只山羊也排进了队，谁都不忍心把它赶走。|队伍里的人在交换一些永远不会用的菜谱。}}}\n# en: A queue of villagers and students outside a little bakery at dawn, steam and the smell of cinnamon pouring out of the door.\nscene [09ed66] = 黎明时一家小面包房外排着镇民和学生，热气和肉桂香从门里涌出来。\n# en: Buy a bag for your corridor\nopt.left.label [fed2ca] = 给走廊的人买一袋\n# en: {You come back with a warm bag and are, for one evening, the most popular person in your house.|The buns are gone before you've taken your coat off. Someone leaves you a thank-you note on a napkin.|A prefect takes one \"to check it for contraband\". It passes.}\nopt.left.say.1 [49a9cb] = {你带回一袋热面包，整整一个晚上成了学院里最受欢迎的人。|你还没脱外套，面包就被抢光了。有人在餐巾上给你留了张感谢条。|一个级长拿走一个，说要“检查有没有违禁品”。检查通过。}\n# en: Take a broken bun from the free tray\nopt.right.label [355ae4] = 从免费盘里拿个碎面包\n# en: {The baker's son gives you one that came out lopsided. You eat it on the quay while it's still too hot. Worth every burnt finger.|A broken bun, free: butter, cinnamon and the lake in the afternoon. Nothing has ever been better.|The free tray has one left. You eat it slowly and think about nothing at all.}\nopt.right.say.1 [2aad9a] = {面包师的儿子给了你一个烤歪了的。你在码头上趁烫就吃了。烫了手指也值。|一个碎掉的面包，免费的：黄油、肉桂，还有午后的湖。再没有比这更好的了。|免费盘里还剩最后一个。你慢慢吃着，什么也不想。}\n\n@event bakery_oldest_baker\n# en: The Oldest Baker\ntitle [efae3a] = 最老的面包师\n# en: The oldest of the bakers sits by the ovens, too old to knead and too stubborn to leave. {She asks, as she asks every student, whether the post at the castle is reliable these days.|She asks about the castle post. Her daughter gives you a look that says: just answer.|\"Is the post reliable?\" she asks. You've learned the answer she likes.} {The ovens tick as they heat.|Flour hangs in the light.|The bell over the door rings, and she looks up, every time.}\ntext [a4c10c] = 面包师里最年长的那位坐在烤炉边，老得揉不动面，又倔得不肯走。{她问你——就像她问每个学生那样——城堡如今的邮递是否靠得住。|她问起城堡的邮递。她女儿给了你一个眼神：回答就是了。|“邮递靠得住吗？”她问。你已经学会了她爱听的答案。}{烤炉升温时发出轻轻的嗒嗒声。|面粉悬在光里。|门上的铃铛一响，她就抬头，每次都是。}\n# en: A very old woman in a flour-dusted apron sitting by warm bread ovens, looking up hopefully as the shop door opens.\nscene [3f911a] = 一位系着沾满面粉围裙的老妇人坐在温热的烤炉边，门一开，她便满怀期待地抬起头。\n# en: Sit with her a while\nopt.left.label [4b2f48] = 陪她坐一会儿\n# en: {She tells you about her grandmother's grandmother, who waited for a letter. She doesn't say how it ended.|She tells you the family has baked here for two hundred years. She makes it sound like a promise kept.|She falls asleep mid-story. Her daughter tucks a bun into your pocket and says thank you.}\nopt.left.say.1 [bafc08] = {她讲起她祖母的祖母，那人一直在等一封信。她没说结局。|她说她家在这里烤了两百年面包。她说得像一个守住了的承诺。|她讲着讲着睡着了。她女儿往你口袋里塞了个面包，说了声谢谢。}\n# en: Help knead the dough\nopt.right.label [d60b9f] = 帮忙揉面\n# en: {The dough fights back. Your arms ache by the time the trays go in.|The baker shows you the turn of the wrist. You don't have it yet.|You knead until your shoulders burn. The first loaf out has your thumbprint on it.}\nopt.right.say.1 [b8cff0] = {面团会反抗。等烤盘送进炉子时，你的胳膊已经酸了。|面包师教你手腕那一转。你还没学会。|你揉到肩膀发烫。出炉的第一个面包上留着你的拇指印。}\n\n@event bakery_cooling_rack\n# en: The Cooling Rack\ntitle [5733b6] = 晾面包的窗台\n# en: {Behind the bakery a window stands open, and on the sill a tray of buns is cooling, unwatched.|The cooling tray is back on the sill, nearer the edge this time. The bakers either trust everyone or are testing you.|The tray, the sill, the open window. It's practically an invitation.} {A crow is eyeing it too.|Two first-years are pretending not to look at it.|Steam curls off the tray into the cold air.}\ntext [10f14f] = {面包房后面有扇窗开着，窗台上晾着一盘面包，没人看着。|那盘面包又晾在窗台上了，这回离边缘更近。面包师们要么谁都信任，要么是在考验你。|托盘、窗台、开着的窗。简直就是邀请。} {一只乌鸦也盯着它。|两个一年级生正假装没在看它。|热气从托盘上袅袅升进冷空气里。}\n# en: A tray of steaming buns cooling on the sill of an open bakery window, a crow and a student both eyeing it.\nscene [22dd92] = 面包房敞开的窗台上晾着一盘冒热气的面包，一只乌鸦和一个学生都盯着它。\n# en: Lift one off the sill\nopt.left.label [9b35c2] = 从窗台顺一个\n# en: {You get away with it. It tastes slightly of guilt and mostly of cinnamon.|Clean away. The crow watches you with professional respect.|You lift one through the steam. Nobody sees except the crow, who takes the next.}\nopt.left.say.1 [38fb17] = {你得手了。吃起来有一点点内疚味，主要是肉桂味。|干净利落。乌鸦用同行的敬意看着你。|你隔着热气拿了一个。除了乌鸦没人看见，乌鸦拿走了下一个。}\n# en: {A floury hand appears from the window, palm up. You pay double.|Caught by the baker's son, who makes you pay double and tells everyone in the queue.|The steam gives you away. A floury hand takes your coins, twice over.}\nopt.left.say.2 [84e187] = {一只沾满面粉的手从窗里伸出来，掌心朝上。你付了双倍。|被面包师的儿子逮个正着，他让你付了双倍，还告诉了队伍里的每一个人。|热气出卖了你。一只沾满面粉的手收了你的钱，收了双份。}\n# en: A floury hand appears from the window, palm up. Your pockets are empty, so you spend an hour scrubbing trays instead.\nopt.left.say.3 [af2de5] = 一只沾满面粉的手从窗里伸出来，掌心朝上。你口袋空空，只好去刷了一个钟头的烤盘。\n# en: Ask for yesterday's\nopt.right.label [eb962f] = 讨点昨天的\n# en: {The baker gives you a bag of yesterday's for nothing. They're as hard as roof slates.|You get the broken ones free. You eat one and lose a little faith in bread.|\"For the student who asks,\" says the baker, and hands you a stale one with a wink.}\nopt.right.say.1 [694f85] = {面包师白送你一袋昨天的面包。硬得像屋顶的石板。|你免费拿到了碎掉的那些。吃了一个，你对面包的信仰动摇了一点。|“给肯开口问的学生。”面包师说着，眨眨眼递给你一个干硬的。}\n\n@event home_cottage_old_room\n# en: Your Old Room\ntitle [8a919e] = 你的旧房间\n# en: {Your old room is exactly as you left it, except smaller.|Someone has been storing apples in your old room.|Your old room has started to feel like a guest room, and you like it less for that.} {season == \"winter\": There's frost on the inside of the window, as always.|The window won't shut properly in the heat, as always.} Downstairs, the house is busy without you.\ntext [ad7a94] = {你的旧房间和你离开时一模一样，只是变小了。|有人在你的旧房间里存了苹果。|你的旧房间开始像一间客房了，你因此不那么喜欢它了。} {season == \"winter\": 窗户内侧结着霜，一如往常。|天热时窗户总关不严，一如往常。}楼下，一家人没等你就忙开了。\n# en: A small childhood bedroom under the eaves, a too-short bed, a window looking out over fields.\nscene [0981a6] = 屋檐下一间小小的儿时卧室，一张太短的床，窗外是田野。\n# en: Nap the afternoon away\nopt.left.label [f2dad7] = 睡掉一个下午\n# en: {You sleep like a stone. Downstairs, someone has done all your chores, loudly.|You wake to the smell of supper and a pointed silence.|You sleep through the afternoon. You feel human, and slightly ashamed.}\nopt.left.say.1 [00f0c9] = {你睡得像块石头。楼下有人把你的家务全干了，动静很大。|你在晚饭的香味和意味深长的沉默中醒来。|你睡了整整一下午。感觉活过来了，也有点惭愧。}\n# en: Go down and help\nopt.right.label [5819cf] = 下楼帮忙\n# en: {final_year: {You go down and realise there won't be many more afternoons like this.|You memorise the kitchen without meaning to.|You go down and help, and try to learn how every sound in the house goes, for later.}|{You help with the fire. Nobody says much. Nobody needs to.|The creaky stair announces you. Someone hands you a cup before you've asked.|You go down and are handed the job you always had. Your hands know it before you do.}}\nopt.right.say.1 [bb5985] = {final_year: {你下楼时忽然意识到，这样的下午不会再有很多了。|你不知不觉把厨房的样子记在了心里。|你下楼帮忙，努力记住屋里每一种声音，留着以后想。}|{你帮着生火。谁都没多说话。也不需要。|吱呀的楼梯报告了你的到来。你还没开口，就有人递给你一杯茶。|你下楼，被分到了你一直干的那份活。你的手比你先想起来。}}\n\n@event home_cottage_list\n# en: The List on the Table\ntitle [e8c27a] = 桌上的清单\n# en: {&Home has remembered that you know where everything is.|A day home, and already a list.|The list. Your name, underlined.} {has(origin_noble): {&The steward wants the ledgers checked.|The steward wants the tenants' letters answered.|The steward wants you to sit in on a dispute about a fence.}|{has(origin_merchant): {&The shop's stock needs counting.|The shop sign needs repainting.|The shop's ledger has a column nobody can explain.}|{has(origin_hedge): {&Your grandmother wants herbs hung to dry.|Your grandmother wants every jar labelled.|Your grandmother wants the bees told the news.}|{&The woodpile needs splitting.|The hen house door argues.|The well rope has frayed.}}}}\ntext [3e4b7b] = {&家里没忘，东西放在哪儿你最清楚。|才回家一天，就有了一张清单。|那张清单。你的名字，下面画着线。} {has(origin_noble): {&管家想让你核一核账本。|管家想让你给佃户们回信。|管家想让你旁听一桩关于篱笆的纠纷。}|{has(origin_merchant): {&店里的存货要清点。|店门口的招牌要重新上漆。|店里的账本上有一栏谁也说不清。}|{has(origin_hedge): {&你外婆要你把草药挂起来晾干。|你外婆要你给每个罐子都贴好标签。|你外婆要你去把近来的消息告诉蜜蜂。}|{&柴堆要劈。|鸡舍的门总在跟人较劲。|井绳磨毛了。}}}}\n# en: A kitchen table with a long handwritten list of chores weighted down by a teapot, a student in academy robes reading it with dismay.\nscene [a8ef94] = 厨房桌上压着茶壶的一张长长的家务清单，一个穿学院袍的学生沮丧地读着它。\n# en: Work through the whole list\nopt.left.label [6b16eb] = 把清单全干完\n# en: {&By supper the list is done, and someone you love says \"Well.\" It's the highest praise in the house.|It takes all day. You sleep better than you have all term.|You finish, and the list grows a second page in a different hand.}\nopt.left.say.1 [32257d] = {&到晚饭时清单全做完了，一位你爱的人说了声“嗯，行。”这是家里的最高赞誉。|干了一整天。你睡得比整个学期都好。|你干完了，清单上又多出了第二页，笔迹不一样。}\n# en: Do it with magic\nopt.right.label [155e10] = 用魔法干\n# en: {&The work does itself while you supervise from a chair. The family is impressed and slightly unnerved.|It works. Your mother says it's cheating, then asks if you can do the gutters.|It works so well that a neighbour asks to borrow you.}\nopt.right.say.1 [403d61] = {&活儿自己干完了，你坐在椅子上监工。家人既佩服又有点发毛。|成功了。你母亲说这是作弊，然后问你能不能把檐沟也弄了。|效果太好了，一个邻居问能不能把你借去用用。}\n# en: {&The spell goes slightly wrong. Something is now upside down that wasn't before.|Soot. Everywhere. Someone laughs until they cry, then hands you a broom.|The spell does the list twice, including the things that were already done.}\nopt.right.say.2 [cce0ce] = {&咒语出了点偏差。有样东西现在是倒着的，原来不是。|煤灰。到处都是。有人笑到流泪，然后递给你一把扫帚。|咒语把清单做了两遍，连已经做完的也重做了一遍。}\n\n@event home_cottage_after_supper\n# en: The Same Question\ntitle [2bfa76] = 同一个问题\n# en: After supper, when the dishes are done, someone always asks it. {\"So what will you do, after?\"|\"Still don't know what you'll do after?\" An aunt, this time.|By now you could ask it yourself, in any of their voices, and someone does.} {final_year: This time the answer is close enough to touch.|{The fire pops.|Nobody pretends not to be listening.|Someone pours more tea to give you time.}}\ntext [78cdad] = 晚饭后，碗碟洗完，总有人会问那个问题。{“那你以后打算做什么？”|“还不知道以后做什么吗？”这回是一位姑妈。|如今你自己都能用他们每个人的腔调问出这句话了——而总有人真的问了。}{final_year: 这一次，答案已经近在眼前。|{炉火噼啪一响。|没人假装不在听。|有人又倒了些茶，给你留点时间。}}\n# en: A family kitchen after supper by candlelight, dishes drying, everyone turned towards one student.\nscene [ff9628] = 晚饭后烛光下的家庭厨房，碗碟正在晾干，所有人都转向一个学生。\n# en: Tell them the truth\nopt.left.label [f3042a] = 说实话\n# en: {You say you don't know. It's quiet for a moment, then someone says that's allowed.|You tell them what you're afraid of. It sounds smaller said aloud at this table.|The truth makes your mother go quiet, and then hug you, which you weren't ready for.}\nopt.left.say.1 [f3962b] = {你说你不知道。安静了一会儿，然后有人说，这也没关系。|你把你害怕的事告诉了他们。在这张桌边说出来，它显得小了些。|实话让你母亲沉默了一下，然后抱住了你，你完全没准备好。}\n# en: Give the polished answer\nopt.right.label [fcba81] = 给个体面的答案\n# en: {You give the answer you'd give a professor. They're proud. It sits oddly in your chest all night.|You make it sound like a plan. Everyone relaxes except you.|\"Very sensible,\" says an aunt. You feel as if you've told her a story.}\nopt.right.say.1 [b251a7] = {你给出了对教授说的那种答案。他们很骄傲。整晚它都在你心里硌着。|你说得像个计划。所有人都松了口气，除了你。|“很稳妥。”一位姑妈说。你觉得自己像给她讲了个故事。}\n\n@event home_fields_long_rows\n# en: The Long Rows\ntitle [e5b4eb] = 长长的田垄\n# en: {You walk the long rows to the field's end, where you used to imagine towers.|You've seen the towers now. The horizon at the end of the rows still pulls.|The walk to the field's end is a habit now. Your feet know it by heart.} {season == \"winter\": The furrows crunch with frost.|The wheat is high and loud with insects.} {A hare bolts.|A crow follows you at a respectful distance.|Smoke rises from a chimney you can't see.}\ntext [15efc7] = {你沿着长长的田垄走到田地尽头，从前你就在那里想象高塔。|你如今见过那些塔了。田垄尽头的地平线依然在召唤。|走到田地尽头已成了习惯。你的脚比你的脑子更熟悉这条路。} {season == \"winter\": 垄沟结着霜，踩上去嘎吱作响。|麦子长得老高，虫鸣一片。} {一只野兔窜了出去。|一只乌鸦隔着得体的距离跟着你。|远处看不见的烟囱里升起一缕烟。}\n# en: A student walking alone between long furrowed rows towards a low wall at the edge of the fields, the horizon wide and empty.\nscene [edb3f6] = 一个学生独自走在长长的田垄间，走向田地边缘的矮墙，地平线辽阔而空旷。\n# en: Walk the whole boundary\nopt.left.label [259a1e] = 沿田界走一整圈\n# en: {You come back muddy to the knees and hungry as a first-year.|You walk until your legs ache and your head is finally quiet.|You sit on the low wall where you always sat, and the wind does the thinking.}\nopt.left.say.1 [622c11] = {你回来时泥到膝盖，饿得像个一年级生。|你一直走到腿酸，脑子终于安静了。|你坐在一直坐的那段矮墙上，让风替你思考。}\n# en: Practise where no one watches\nopt.right.label [b2a1d1] = 没人时练练法术\n# en: {You set a scarecrow's hat spinning. Nobody sees. It's glorious.|You practise until your hands tingle and the crows give you a wide berth.|You try the spell you keep failing at school. Out here, with nobody watching, it nearly works.}\nopt.right.say.1 [741772] = {你让稻草人的帽子转了起来。没人看见。太棒了。|你练到双手发麻，乌鸦都绕着你飞。|你试了那个在学校总失败的咒语。在这儿，没人看着，它差点就成了。}\n\n@event home_fields_neighbours\n# en: Neighbours' Work\ntitle [332e5b] = 邻居们的活计\n# en: {season == \"winter\": The neighbours are hauling wood before the next snow.|The neighbours are bringing in the hay before the weather turns.} {They wave you over without asking if you're busy.|This time somebody has saved you a pair of gloves.|They've stopped waving. They just hand you the gloves.} {The neighbour's boy wants to be turned into a frog.|A farmer calls you \"the scholar\", mostly kindly.|Someone has brought a jug of mostly cider.}\ntext [dd78c9] = {season == \"winter\": 邻居们赶在下一场雪前运柴。|邻居们赶在变天前收干草。} {他们招手叫你过去，也不问你忙不忙。|这回有人给你留了一副手套。|他们不再招手了，直接把手套递给你。} {邻居家的男孩想知道你能不能把他变成青蛙。|一个农夫叫你“读书人”，大体是善意的。|有人带来一壶基本算是苹果酒的东西。}\n# en: Neighbours working together at the edge of a field, one waving a student in academy robes over to help.\nscene [962599] = 邻居们在田边一起干活，其中一人招手叫一个穿学院袍的学生过来帮忙。\n# en: Lend a hand\nopt.left.label [d5e12c] = 搭把手\n# en: {Blisters by noon, and a supper invitation from three households.|You work till dusk. Nobody mentions the academy once, which is restful.|Your back reminds you that books are lighter than anything out here.}\nopt.left.say.1 [d60208] = {中午就起了水泡，还收到三户人家的晚饭邀请。|你一直干到黄昏。没人提起学院一个字，这让人很放松。|你的后背提醒你，书比这儿的任何东西都轻。}\n# en: Bring them all a drink\nopt.right.label [a87c41] = 给大家带点喝的\n# en: {You come back from the market with a basket and become the most popular person in the field.|They drink to the academy, then to you, then to the weather.|The neighbour's boy asks for a frog again. You give him a biscuit; it's a compromise.}\nopt.right.say.1 [19cd62] = {你从集市提回一篮子东西，成了田里最受欢迎的人。|他们为学院干杯，再为你干杯，再为天气干杯。|邻居家的男孩又要变青蛙。你给了他一块饼干，算是折中。}\n\n@event home_fields_pond\n# en: The Old Pond\ntitle [ed9a38] = 老池塘\n# en: {season == \"winter\": The pond at the bottom of the fields has frozen over, and the young farmhands are daring each other onto the ice.|The pond at the bottom of the fields is warm and green, and the young farmhands are daring each other off the willow.} {You used to be one of them.|This time they dare you too.|They've started daring you first.} {A dog barks at the whole business.|The blacksmith's apprentice is keeping score.|Your old initials are still carved in the willow.}\ntext [567520] = {season == \"winter\": 田地尽头的池塘结了冰，年轻的农工们正互相激将着上冰。|田地尽头的池塘温暖碧绿，年轻的农工们正互相激将着从柳树上跳下去。} {你从前也是他们中的一个。|这回他们也激你了。|他们现在先激你。} {一条狗冲着这一切汪汪叫。|铁匠的学徒在记分。|你小时候刻的名字缩写还在柳树上。}\n# en: A pond at the bottom of a field under an old willow, local youngsters daring each other at its edge.\nscene [6031f0] = 田边一棵老柳树下的池塘，镇上的年轻人在岸边互相激将。\n# en: Take the dare\nopt.left.label [6cb2fd] = 接受挑战\n# en: {season == \"winter\": {You skate across and back to roaring applause from three farmhands and a dog.|You slide the whole length on your back. It counts.|You skate past your old initials frozen into the willow's roots. The apprentice gives you full marks for nostalgia.}|{You drop off the willow into the green. It's colder than memory, and better.|A perfect splash. The scorekeeper gives it top marks.|You drop off the branch beside your old initials. The score is one you'll be proud of for a week.}}\nopt.left.say.1 [e24a97] = {season == \"winter\": {你滑过去又滑回来，赢得三个农工和一条狗的欢呼。|你仰面滑完了全程。这也算数。|你滑过冻在柳树根上的旧缩写。学徒为你的怀旧给了满分。}|{你从柳树上跳进碧水里。比记忆中更冷，也更好。|完美的水花。记分的人给了最高分。|你从刻着你旧缩写的那根枝上跳了下去。那个分数够你得意一星期。}}\n# en: {season == \"winter\": {You go through the ice up to your knees and limp home to a lecture and a fire.|The ice cracks under you and you scramble back, soaked and laughed at.|You stop to look at your initials, and the ice stops holding you.}|{You belly-flop. The sound carries across three fields. Everyone heard.|You slip off the willow early and land in the reeds, muddy to the ears.|You climb to your old initials and slip on them. The splash is not dignified.}}\nopt.left.say.2 [dbdae5] = {season == \"winter\": {你踩破冰面，水没到膝盖，一瘸一拐地回家，迎接你的是一顿数落和一炉火。|冰在你脚下裂开，你手脚并用爬回岸上，浑身湿透，被笑了个够。|你停下来看你的缩写，冰就不再托着你了。}|{你肚皮拍水。那声响传遍了三块田。所有人都听见了。|你还没站稳就从柳树上滑了下去，一头栽进芦苇丛，泥糊到耳朵。|你爬到你的旧缩写那儿，在上面滑了一跤。水花一点也不体面。}}\n# en: Hold the coats\nopt.right.label [aecc6f] = 帮大家看外套\n# en: {You hold the coats and referee the arguments. Someone has to be the grown-up now.|You keep score and are accused of bias by every side.|Holding the coats, you feel very old and quite content.}\nopt.right.say.1 [e71ba6] = {你看着外套，调解争吵。现在总得有人当大人了。|你负责记分，结果每一方都说你偏心。|抱着一堆外套，你觉得自己老了，也挺知足。}\n\n@event home_market_inn_crowd\n# en: Outside the Inn\ntitle [d68f78] = 客栈门外\n# en: {The old crowd is outside the inn, exactly where you left them. \"Look who it is,\" someone says. \"The wizard.\"|The old crowd have been rehearsing the wizard joke. It has a second part now.|The old crowd outside the inn. Fewer of them every break: somebody married, somebody moved away.} {season == \"winter\": They're stamping their feet against the cold.|They're sitting on the inn steps in the long light.}\ntext [9c5c3d] = {老伙计们聚在客栈门外，跟你离开时一样。“看看这是谁，”有人说，“大巫师回来啦。”|老伙计们排练过“巫师”那个笑话了。现在还多了第二段。|客栈门外的老伙计们。每个假期都少几个人：有人成了家，有人搬走了。} {season == \"winter\": 他们在寒风里跺着脚。|他们坐在客栈台阶上，沐着长长的日光。}\n# en: A knot of young locals outside a country inn, raising their cups as a student in academy robes walks up.\nscene [a67c59] = 乡间客栈外聚着一群年轻的本地人，看见一个穿学院袍的学生走来，纷纷举起杯子。\n# en: Buy a round\nopt.left.label [ac2c88] = 请大家喝一轮\n# en: {They drink your health and ask if you can make the beer stronger. You can't. You're asked again.|For an hour it's as if you never left.|Someone tells the story of the time you fell in the duck pond. It has grown since you left.}\nopt.left.say.1 [10bb4f] = {他们为你的健康干杯，问你能不能把啤酒变得更烈。你不能。他们又问了一遍。|有那么一个钟头，仿佛你从未离开。|有人讲起你掉进鸭塘的那件事。你走后，这故事又长了不少。}\n# en: Stay for the gossip only\nopt.right.label [b278e5] = 只听八卦\n# en: {You learn who's courting, who's feuding and whose goat ate whose washing. Somebody mutters that academy folk are tight with a coin.|You keep your crowns in your pocket and get very well informed.|They notice you didn't buy a round. They notice everything.}\nopt.right.say.1 [ed0ec0] = {你知道了谁在恋爱、谁在结仇、谁家的山羊吃了谁家晾的衣服。有人嘀咕学院的人真抠门。|你把克朗留在兜里，消息却灵通得很。|他们注意到你没请客。他们什么都注意得到。}\n\n@event home_market_charm_seller\n# en: The Charm Seller\ntitle [dc4b46] = 卖护身符的人\n# en: {A stall in the market is selling \"genuine Lumenhold charms, blessed in the seven towers\".|The charm seller has new charms and a bigger sign. The claims have grown to match.|The charm seller sees your robes and starts packing up, then thinks better of it.} {has(origin_merchant): You know that markup. You were raised on it.|{A grandmother is buying one for luck.|The charms are bent nails tied with ribbon.|One of them is, worryingly, glowing.}}\ntext [5cfb18] = {集市上有个摊子在卖“正宗流明堡护身符，七塔加持”。|卖护身符的人换了新货，招牌也更大了。说辞跟着一起膨胀。|卖护身符的人看见你的学院袍，开始收摊，又改了主意。} {has(origin_merchant): 你认得那种加价。你就是在这种加价里长大的。|{一位老奶奶正买一个求好运。|那些护身符是系着丝带的弯钉子。|其中一个居然在发光，叫人不安。}}\n# en: A market stall hung with ribbons and trinkets under a hand-painted sign about academy blessings, a grandmother reaching for her purse.\nscene [2e47e6] = 挂满丝带和小饰物的集市摊位，手绘招牌上吹嘘着学院加持，一位老奶奶正伸手去掏钱袋。\n# en: Call out the fakes\nopt.left.label [2cfc0c] = 揭穿假货\n# en: {The crowd turns on him. A grandmother gets her coin back and gives you a cake. The seller gives you a look.|He folds, and offers you a share of the trade. You decline. He seems genuinely surprised.|You point out the glowing one. The seller goes pale and hands it to you to take away, free. The crowd cheers you anyway.}\nopt.left.say.1 [cbbdbd] = {人群倒向了你这边。一位老奶奶拿回了钱，送你一块蛋糕。摊主瞪了你一眼。|他认输了，提出分你一份生意。你拒绝了。他似乎真心感到意外。|你指出那个发光的。摊主脸色发白，把它白送给你让你拿走。人群还是为你欢呼。}\n# en: {The crowd takes his side. \"Academy snob,\" someone says, buying two.|He out-talks you in front of everyone. You'd admire it if it were happening to someone else.|You call out the glowing one, and it glows brighter, as if insulted. The crowd decides it's genuine and buys the lot.}\nopt.left.say.2 [e5327e] = {人群站在他那边。“学院来的势利眼。”有人说着，买了两个。|他当着所有人的面把你说得哑口无言。要是发生在别人身上，你会佩服的。|你点出那个发光的，它亮得更厉害了，像是被冒犯了。人群认定它是真货，全买光了。}\n# en: Buy one for an aunt\nopt.right.label [7ab465] = 给姑妈买一个\n# en: {Your aunt will love it. It's a bent nail. She will love it anyway.|You buy one. It's useless, and the old woman beside you beams at you with approval.|You buy one that glows a little. You decide not to think about why.}\nopt.right.say.1 [56b1f8] = {你姑妈会喜欢的。那是根弯钉子。她照样会喜欢。|你买了一个。毫无用处，但旁边的老妇人对你赞许地笑了。|你买了一个微微发光的。你决定不去想它为什么发光。}\n\n@event home_market_post_office\n# en: Letters to the Castle\ntitle [b5b8cc] = 寄往城堡的信\n# en: {The town's post office is the size of a cupboard, and its clerk has never posted anything to a magic academy.|The clerk has started weighing your letters with ceremony.|The clerk reaches for the castle stamp before you speak.} {season == \"winter\": The window is fogged; it smells of sealing wax.|Flies wander in to read the notices.} {final_year: Soon you'll write to the castle from elsewhere.|You think about who at the castle you miss.}\ntext [69c4a9] = {镇上的邮局只有柜子那么大，邮务员从没往魔法学院寄过东西。|邮务员开始郑重其事地称你的信了，像举行仪式。|你还没开口，邮务员就去拿城堡邮戳了。} {season == \"winter\": 窗户上蒙着雾气，有股封蜡的味道。|苍蝇飞进来读告示。} {final_year: 很快，你就要从别处给城堡写信了。|你想着城堡里那些你想念的人。}\n# en: A post office the size of a cupboard, a clerk ceremoniously weighing a stack of letters addressed to a castle on a lake.\nscene [8ee65f] = 柜子大小的邮局里，邮务员郑重地称着一摞寄往湖上城堡的信。\n# en: Write to everyone\nopt.left.label [0c3aa2] = 给每个人写信\n# en: {You write until your hand cramps. The clerk sighs at the stack.|You draw the cottage for someone who has never seen one.|You start one letter with \"I miss\" and decide to leave it that way.}\nopt.left.say.1 [236fa8] = {你写到手抽筋。邮务员对着那一摞叹气。|你给一个从没见过乡下小屋的人画了一幅你家的小屋。|你一封信开头写了“我想念”，决定就这样留着。}\n# en: Write the essay you owe\nopt.right.label [4e919a] = 写欠下的论文\n# en: {You write the essay on the clerk's counter, between parcels. It's not bad.|You post an essay to a professor. The clerk asks if it'll get you into trouble. Probably.|You finish it and post it. It's the dullest thing the clerk has ever weighed.}\nopt.right.say.1 [1080f8] = {你在邮局柜台上、包裹之间写完了论文。还不赖。|你把论文寄给一位教授。邮务员问这会不会给你惹麻烦。大概会。|你写完寄出。这是邮务员称过的最无聊的东西。}\n\n@event village_errand_parcel\n# en: The Parcel That Clinked\ntitle [e7dea6] = 叮当响的包裹\n# en: {npc} turns up at your door with one of the parcels you carried up the hill: the one that clinked. \"This one was for you,\" {they} say{s}. \"I needed someone to carry it without guessing.\" Inside is a jar of village honey and a note: For the porter.\ntext [995baa] = {npc}拿着一个你帮忙扛上山的包裹出现在你门口：就是那个叮当响的。“这个是给你的，”{they}说，“我需要一个不会乱猜的人来扛。”里面是一罐小镇的蜂蜜，附一张字条：给门房。\n# en: A student unwrapping a string-tied parcel on a dormitory bed, a small glass jar of honey and a note inside.\nscene [f44ded] = 一个学生在宿舍床上拆开一个系着绳子的包裹，里面是一小罐蜂蜜和一张字条。\n# en: Open the honey now and share it\nopt.left.label [5fc1c8] = 现在就打开一起吃\n# en: You eat half the jar with {npc} on the dormitory floor, with spoons borrowed from the kitchens, and talk until your reading is a lost cause.\nopt.left.say.1 [55abe3] = 你和{npc}坐在宿舍地板上，用从厨房借来的勺子吃掉了半罐，一直聊到你的阅读彻底没救。\n# en: Save it for exam month\nopt.right.label [484c1f] = 留到考试月\n# en: You put it on the shelf, labelled. In exam month, a spoonful at midnight gets you through two chapters. {npc} asks, later, if you liked it. You did.\nopt.right.say.1 [6b66a3] = 你把它放上架子，贴好标签。到了考试月，半夜一勺，撑你读完两章。后来{npc}问你喜不喜欢。你喜欢。\n\n@event village_errand_third_thing\n# en: The Third Thing, After All\ntitle [625bb2] = 第三样东西，终究还是买了\n# en: {npc} has gone back down to the village for the third thing, the one you talked {them} out of, and has come back with it. \"You were right,\" {they} say{s}. \"I bought it anyway. Now I need you to say it suits me.\"\ntext [e9d06f] = {npc}又下山去买了第三样东西，就是你劝{them}别买的那样，还带了回来。“你说得对，”{they}说，“可我还是买了。现在我需要你说它适合我。”\n# en: A village stall with a single extravagant hat on a stand, two students looking at it, one with a purse already open.\nscene [c42650] = 山下小镇的一个摊位上，架子上摆着一顶夸张的帽子，两个学生看着它，其中一个已经打开了钱袋。\n# en: Say it suits {them}\nopt.left.label [a89beb] = 说它很适合{them}\n# en: \"It suits you,\" you say. It doesn't, at all. {npc} wears it to breakfast every day for a week, beaming.\nopt.left.say.1 [57cf74] = “很适合你，”你说。其实一点也不适合。{npc}连续一周每天戴着它去吃早饭，笑容满面。\n# en: Tell {them} the truth, kindly\nopt.right.label [c2201f] = 委婉地说实话\n# en: You say it's a brave choice. {npc} hears what you mean, laughs and gives it to the porter, who wears it all winter with great dignity.\nopt.right.say.1 [bfd2a8] = 你说这是个大胆的选择。{npc}听出了你的意思，笑了，把帽子送给了门房，门房整个冬天都庄重地戴着它。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/slice_2a.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/slice_2a.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event slice_ward_lesson\n# en: The First Ward\ntitle [bb9ea0] = 第一道护界\n# en: Professor Thorne holds up a hand and the lamplight bends around it into a pale shell. \"A ward,\" he says. \"{~It stops one blow.|One blow. That's all it ever stops.} Learn it properly or not at all.\"\ntext [df5c56] = 索恩教授抬起一只手，灯光绕着它弯成一层苍白的护壳。“护界，”他说，“{~它能挡一击。|一击。它永远只挡得住一击。}要么好好学，要么别学。”\n# en: An office lit by one lamp, an old professor holding a pale shell of light between his palms like a soap bubble.\nscene [86947c] = 一间只点着一盏灯的办公室，一位老教授在两掌之间托着一层苍白的光壳，像一个肥皂泡。\n# en: Practise until it holds\nopt.left.label [ca9f8f] = 练到它撑得住为止\n# en: By midnight your shell holds for a full breath. Thorne says \"Hm,\" which from him is a standing ovation.\nopt.left.say.1 [1c452f] = 到午夜，你的护壳能撑满一整口气。索恩说了声“嗯”——从他嘴里出来，这相当于全场起立鼓掌。\n# en: Ask about the theory first\nopt.right.label [e6ac23] = 先问问原理\n# en: He talks for an hour about lattices and intent. You understand perhaps a third of it. \"Come back when the third is a half,\" he says.\nopt.right.say.1 [75d35a] = 他讲了一个小时的晶格与意图。你大概听懂了三分之一。“等这三分之一变成一半再来，”他说。\n\n@event slice_candle_flame\n# en: Borrowed Fire\ntitle [f667df] = 借来的火\n# en: Lyra watches you fail to light a candle for the third time. \"You're asking it,\" she says. \"Fire doesn't want to be asked. {rel(lyra) >= 12: Here — watch my hand.|Watch, if you can keep up.}\"\ntext [8ed13c] = 莱拉看着你第三次没能点燃蜡烛。“你在请求它，”她说，“火不喜欢被请求。{rel(lyra) >= 12: 来——看我的手。|看好了，如果你跟得上的话。}”\n# en: A crimson-haired student balancing a flame on one fingertip while another student squints at a sputtering candle.\nscene [7237b8] = 一个绯红头发的学生指尖托着一簇火苗，另一个学生眯眼盯着一根噼啪作响的蜡烛。\n# en: Watch her hand\nopt.left.label [8f7d80] = 看她的手\n# en: She doesn't explain. She just does it, slowly, twice. On the third try your candle catches, and so, very briefly, does your sleeve.\nopt.left.say.1 [59ceef] = 她不解释，只是慢慢地做了两遍。第三次，你的蜡烛点着了——你的袖子也短暂地着了一下。\n# en: Challenge her to teach you properly\nopt.right.label [02abfd] = 激她好好教你\n# en: You hold your own long enough that she stops and actually explains. \"Fine,\" she says. \"You'll do.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [24afbf] = 你撑得够久，久到她停下来真的开始讲解。“行吧，”她说，“你还凑合。”\n# en: She wins, and you hit the floor hard enough to feel it tomorrow, but she shows you the trick on the way out, as if it were nothing.\nopt.right.say.2 [cfe391] = 她赢了，你重重摔在地上，明天还会疼，但离开时她顺手把诀窍演示给你看，仿佛那不值一提。\n# en: You end up on the floor with singed eyebrows. Lyra steps over you. \"Ask better,\" she says.\nopt.right.say.3 [a982db] = 你最后躺在地上，眉毛被燎焦了。莱拉从你身上跨过去。“下次问得好一点，”她说。\n\n@event slice_stray_cold\n# en: The Drift\ntitle [b9d28f] = 寒流\n# en: Cold drifts down the corridor like a tide coming in, and the torches lean away from it. {spell_level(ward) > 0: Your ward prickles at your fingertips.|You have nothing to hold it back.}\ntext [74bc38] = 寒意像涨潮一样漫过走廊，火把纷纷向一旁倾斜。{spell_level(ward) > 0: 你的护界在指尖隐隐刺痛。|你没有任何东西能挡住它。}\n# en: A corridor at night where frost spreads across the flagstones toward a student holding a candle.\nscene [e9d25c] = 夜里的一条走廊，霜正沿着石板向一个举着蜡烛的学生蔓延。\n# en: Run for the stairs\nopt.left.label [3e7200] = 往楼梯跑\n# en: You make the stairs with frost in your hair. Behind you, the corridor sighs and is just a corridor again.\nopt.left.say.1 [498ddb] = 你冲上楼梯时，头发里结满了霜。身后的走廊叹了口气，又变回了普通的走廊。\n# en: Stand and study it\nopt.right.label [0a0a0c] = 站住，研究它\n# en: It isn't weather. It feels like being looked at, from somewhere far below. You write down everything before your hands warm up, and none of it makes sense yet.\nopt.right.say.1 [791d24] = 这不是天气。更像是被注视着，从很深很深的下面。你趁双手还没回暖，把一切都记了下来，可眼下还一点也看不明白。\n# en: You learn exactly one thing, which is how cold cold can be. Sister Amaranth wraps you in four blankets.\nopt.right.say.2 [46b7a7] = 你只学到了一件事：冷能冷到什么地步。阿玛兰斯修女给你裹了四层毯子。\n# en: Raise a ward\nopt.up.label [d5a702] = 撑起护界\n# en: Your ward flares, pale and steady. The cold breaks against it and slides away, the way a wave gives up on a rock.\nopt.up.say.1 [e39cb8] = 你的护界亮起，苍白而稳定。寒流撞上它，滑开了，像浪头对一块礁石放弃了。\n\n@event slice_late_lamps\n# en: The Last Lamp\ntitle [5ba6ee] = 最后一盏灯\n# en: {~The library is emptying.|Rain on the high windows, and the library is emptying early.|Exam fever has filled the library all day; now, finally, it is emptying.} {~Footnote, the library cat, is asleep on the book you need|The book you need is on the top shelf, and the ladder squeaks|Someone has left a half-finished essay on your table, and a biscuit}, and the last lamp is yours if you want it.\ntext [d83f80] = {~图书馆渐渐空了。|雨打在高窗上，图书馆早早就空了。|考试热潮挤满了图书馆一整天；现在，终于，人渐渐散了。}{~图书馆的猫“脚注”趴在你要的那本书上睡着了|你要的那本书在最高一层，梯子吱呀作响|有人在你桌上留了一篇写了一半的论文，还有一块饼干}，最后一盏灯，你想要就是你的。\n# en: The library at closing time, one desk still lit, a cat asleep on an open book.\nscene [6a380e] = 闭馆时的图书馆，只剩一张书桌还亮着，一只猫睡在摊开的书上。\n# en: Keep the lamp\nopt.left.label [b4ddff] = 守着这盏灯\n# en: {~You read until Footnote leaves in protest.|You read until the words swim.|You read until the night porter coughs pointedly.}\nopt.left.say.1 [9dbe5e] = {~你读到“脚注”愤然离席。|你读到字都开始游动。|你读到守夜人意味深长地咳嗽。}\n# en: Go to bed\nopt.right.label [2fa817] = 回去睡觉\n# en: {~You sleep like a stone. The book will still be there. So, probably, will Footnote.|You fall asleep before your head finds the pillow. You wake up knowing one thing you didn't, somehow.|You sleep well and dream of shelves. The lamp goes to someone who needed it more.}\nopt.right.say.1 [edb97a] = {~你睡得像块石头。书还会在那里。“脚注”大概也是。|你头还没沾到枕头就睡着了。醒来时，不知怎么，你多懂了一件事。|你睡得很好，梦里全是书架。那盏灯留给了更需要它的人。}\n\n@event slice_quarrel\n# en: A Quarrel in the Courtyard\ntitle [cdc8fb] = 庭院里的争执\n# en: Kael and Lyra are facing each other in the courtyard, and the {season == \"winter\": snow|grass} between them is half {season == \"winter\": melted|scorched}, half frozen. \"Say it again,\" Lyra says. Kael doesn't. He just looks at you.\ntext [d5c1cc] = 凯尔和莱拉在庭院里对峙，两人之间的{season == \"winter\": 雪|草地}一半{season == \"winter\": 融化|焦枯}、一半冻结。“再说一遍，”莱拉说。凯尔没有说。他只是看向你。\n# en: Two students facing each other in a snowy courtyard, one breathing frost, the other with sparks at her fingertips.\nscene [38c0d1] = 积雪的庭院里，两个学生面对面站着，一个呼出寒霜，另一个指尖迸着火星。\n# en: Step between them\nopt.left.label [f242e3] = 站到他们中间\n# en: Lyra's sparks catch your sleeve, Kael's frost puts it out. They both stare at you. The fight, somehow, is over.\nopt.left.say.1 [5ee694] = 莱拉的火星燎到你的袖子，凯尔的霜又把它扑灭。两人都盯着你。不知怎么，这场架就这么结束了。\n# en: Walk past\nopt.right.label [d5359c] = 径直走过\n# en: Behind you, something cracks: ice, or temper. At dinner they sit at opposite ends of the hall.\nopt.right.say.1 [d025e8] = 身后有什么裂开了：是冰，还是脾气。晚饭时，他们坐在大厅的两头。\n\n@event slice_burned_letter\n# en: Ashes in the Grate\ntitle [4b34a6] = 壁炉里的灰\n# en: Lyra is feeding a letter into the common-room fire, a corner at a time. Black seal. She doesn't look round. \"It says 'well done',\" she says. \"It doesn't. I'm checking whether it burns better if I pretend.\"\ntext [847047] = 莱拉正把一封信一角一角地喂进公共休息室的壁炉。黑色封蜡。她没有回头。“信上写着‘做得好’，”她说，“其实没有。我在试试，假装有的话它会不会烧得更旺。”\n# en: A fireplace where the corner of a letter with a black seal is curling into flame.\nscene [e9282e] = 一座壁炉，一封盖着黑色封蜡的信的一角正卷进火焰里。\n# en: Sit with her\nopt.left.label [654e0a] = 陪她坐着\n# en: You watch it burn together. \"She's never said it,\" Lyra says, to the fire. \"Not once.\" You don't say anything, and that seems to be right.\nopt.left.say.1 [437064] = 你们一起看着它烧完。“她从没说过，”莱拉对着火说，“一次都没有。”你什么也没说，而这似乎正是对的。\n# en: Make a joke\nopt.right.label [5f1ec3] = 开个玩笑\n# en: She almost smiles. Then she puts the rest of the letter in at once and leaves you with the smoke.\nopt.right.say.1 [282f1d] = 她差点笑了。然后她把剩下的信一股脑儿丢进火里，留你和烟待在一起。\n\n@event slice_hollow_whisper\n# en: A Voice in the Water\ntitle [b31d19] = 水中的声音\n# en: At the lakeshore your reflection speaks before you do. \"You're running low,\" it says, kindly. \"{mana < 15: I could fill you up.|I could make you stronger.} Just say yes. Saying yes costs nothing.\"\ntext [f94671] = 在湖岸边，你的倒影先于你开口。“你快见底了，”它温和地说，“{mana < 15: 我可以把你填满。|我可以让你更强。}只要说‘好’。说‘好’什么都不花。”\n# en: A student kneeling at the edge of a still lake at night, their reflection smiling when they are not.\nscene [4bb1f8] = 夜里，一个学生跪在平静的湖边，水中的倒影在笑，而他自己并没有笑。\n# en: Say yes\nopt.left.label [c73ccb] = 说“好”\n# en: Cold pours into you, generous and endless. When you stand, your reflection is a little slower to stand with you.\nopt.left.say.1 [3ad748] = 寒意涌进你的身体，慷慨而无穷无尽。你站起来时，倒影慢了一拍才跟着站起来。\n# en: Walk away\nopt.right.label [96da1a] = 转身离开\n# en: The reflection's smile stays on the water after you've gone. You don't look back. You can feel it not minding.\nopt.right.say.1 [8bbf02] = 你走后，倒影的笑容还留在水面上。你没有回头。你能感觉到它并不在意。\n\n@event slice_market_haggle\n# en: The Spice Stall\ntitle [8682c8] = 香料摊\n# en: The stall-holder in the village market wants thirty crowns for {~a jar of starpepper|a twist of dragon-chilli|a pot of cliff-bee honey}. Mabel swears by it for colds. The stall-holder swears by his prices.\ntext [ed6115] = 小镇集市上的摊主要三十克朗卖{~一罐星辰胡椒|一束龙椒|一罐崖蜂蜜}。梅布尔发誓它治感冒有奇效。摊主则发誓他的价钱童叟无欺。\n# en: A crowded village market stall heaped with jars of glowing spices and a stall-holder with an enormous moustache.\nscene [ba55f9] = 拥挤的小镇集市摊位，堆满发光的香料罐，摊主留着一大把胡子。\n# en: Haggle\nopt.left.label [90048b] = 讨价还价\n# en: Eight crowns and a compliment on your persistence. Mabel says you've been robbed, but only slightly.\nopt.left.say.1 [ec3b10] = 八克朗，外加一句夸你有毅力。梅布尔说你被坑了，不过只坑了一点点。\n# en: Fifteen crowns. He throws in a free sneeze's worth of pepper, which you did not want.\nopt.left.say.2 [93bde1] = 十五克朗。他还白送你一个喷嚏的胡椒量——你并不想要。\n# en: You pay full price and somehow promise to come back for more. He waves you off like a nephew.\nopt.left.say.3 [3758d8] = 你付了全价，还不知怎么答应了下次再来买。他像送别侄子一样挥手送你走。\n# en: Leave it\nopt.right.label [3e2fed] = 算了\n# en: You walk on. You catch a cold the next week, out of spite.\nopt.right.say.1 [128e73] = 你走开了。下周你就感冒了，纯属赌气。\n\n@event slice_wager\n# en: The Tower Wager\ntitle [013b2f] = 钟楼赌约\n# en: Half the common room is betting on whether you can climb the clock tower before the bell. Corvin is holding the purse, which is either reassuring or not.\ntext [3371c6] = 半个公共休息室的人在赌你能不能在钟响前爬上钟楼。钱袋在科尔文手里——这让人放心，也可能不。\n# en: A knot of students around a table where coins and a pocket watch are being stacked as bets.\nscene [217f9f] = 一群学生围着一张桌子，桌上叠着当赌注的硬币和一块怀表。\n# en: Climb\nopt.left.label [2c7b2a] = 爬\n# en: {~You make it with a heartbeat to spare.|The bell rings as your hand touches the top.} They carry you round the common room on a chair.\nopt.left.say.1 [91cad2] = {~你提前一个心跳的工夫登了顶。|你的手碰到塔顶的那一刻，钟声响了。}他们把你放在椅子上，抬着绕公共休息室转了一圈。\n# en: The bell rings when you're two-thirds up, and the caretaker has to talk you down with a ladder. They'll tell it for years.\nopt.left.say.2 [e4a38c] = 你才爬了三分之二，钟就响了，最后是管理员架着梯子把你劝下来的。他们会把这事讲上好几年。\n# en: Decline politely\nopt.right.label [c5820d] = 礼貌地拒绝\n# en: The crowd groans. Corvin pockets the purse thoughtfully and you decide not to ask.\nopt.right.say.1 [3f87ed] = 人群一片哀叹。科尔文若有所思地把钱袋揣进兜里，你决定不问。\n\n@event slice_frost_duel\n# en: Frost Against Fire\ntitle [63f7ba] = 霜与火\n# en: Kael asks for a practice duel, which for him is an astonishing number of words. \"Not to win,\" he adds. \"I want to see what you do when it's cold.\"\ntext [4a4fd1] = 凯尔请你和他练一场决斗——对他来说，这已经是惊人的字数。“不是为了赢，”他补充道，“我想看看天冷的时候你会怎么做。”\n# en: A duelling ring at dawn, frost spreading from one side and a faint warmth glowing from the other.\nscene [8d420a] = 黎明时分的决斗场，一边蔓延着寒霜，另一边泛着微弱的暖光。\n# en: Duel him straight\nopt.left.label [a7dfd1] = 正面和他决斗\n# en: You win by a whisker, breathing steam. Kael bows, and his hands, for once, are warm when he shakes yours. Half the duelling club, it turns out, was watching from the doorway.\nopt.left.say.1 [07b3d4] = 你以毫厘之差获胜，呼出白汽。凯尔鞠了一躬，和你握手时，他的手难得是暖的。原来半个决斗社都在门口看着。\n# en: A draw, of sorts: you're both on the ground. He laughs, a short surprised sound, and helps you up.\nopt.left.say.2 [a850c0] = 算是平局：你们俩都倒在地上。他笑了，短促而意外的一声，然后把你拉了起来。\n# en: He wins, gently. \"You stopped when it got cold,\" he says. \"Don't.\"\nopt.left.say.3 [9548e4] = 他赢了，赢得很温和。“天一冷，你就停下了，”他说，“别停。”\n# en: Decline; offer him tea instead\nopt.right.label [3c2e56] = 婉拒，改请他喝茶\n# en: He thinks about this for a long time. \"Tea,\" he says. It freezes in his cup. You make him another.\nopt.right.say.1 [c0affc] = 他想了很久。“茶，”他说。茶在他杯子里冻住了。你又给他泡了一杯。\n# en: Answer with fire\nopt.up.label [589d8f] = 以火回应\n# en: Your flame meets his frost in a column of steam that fills the ring and brings half the corridor running. When it clears, Kael is smiling at you, properly, for the first time.\nopt.up.say.1 [f78aa4] = 你的火焰迎上他的寒霜，一柱蒸汽填满了整个决斗场，引得半条走廊的人都跑了过来。散去时，凯尔正对你微笑——第一次真正地微笑。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/slice_2b.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/slice_2b.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event evo_first_spark\n# en: The First Spark\ntitle [5b0523] = 第一缕火花\n# en: Evocation I begins with a single instruction from the lectern: \"Make heat. Not fire. Heat.\" Half the class makes nothing. One student makes a small, apologetic fire.\ntext [db1b7b] = 塑能术 I 以讲台上的一句指令开场：“造热。不是火。是热。”半个班什么都没造出来。有一个学生造出了一小团满怀歉意的火。\n# en: A lecture hall with scorch marks on every desk, students holding their palms up as if testing for rain.\nscene [47f33f] = 每张课桌上都有焦痕的讲堂，学生们摊开手掌，像在试探下没下雨。\n# en: Aim for heat, carefully\nopt.left.label [ac4d80] = 小心地只求热\n# en: Your palm warms like a stone left in the sun. The lecturer nods, which is better than most get.\nopt.left.say.1 [fe739e] = 你的掌心暖得像晒过太阳的石头。讲师点了点头，这已经胜过大多数人了。\n# en: Aim for fire, boldly\nopt.right.label [639beb] = 大胆地求火\n# en: A neat little flame, exactly where you meant it. The lecturer writes your name down. Approvingly, you hope.\nopt.right.say.1 [013ce4] = 一小簇利落的火苗，正好出现在你想要的位置。讲师记下了你的名字。你希望是带着赞许的。\n# en: Your sleeve learns more about fire than you do. The lecture continues around the smell.\nopt.right.say.2 [090858] = 你的袖子比你更懂火了。课在这股焦味里继续。\n\n@event evo_puddle_drill\n# en: Puddle Drill\ntitle [3a9384] = 水洼操练\n# en: Tidecalling half of the course: a puddle each, and the task of making it stand up and stay up. Yours trembles, considers it, and lies back down.\ntext [4ca6c9] = 课程的唤潮术部分：每人一个水洼，任务是让它站起来并保持站立。你的那个颤了颤，考虑了一下，又躺了回去。\n# en: Students crouched over puddles in a stone courtyard, trying to make the water stand up.\nscene [83fa0c] = 学生们蹲在石砌庭院的水洼前，试图让水站起来。\n# en: Practise until it stands\nopt.left.label [1dfa4e] = 练到它站起来\n# en: By the end of the hour your puddle stands like a sulky child. It counts.\nopt.left.say.1 [106f8b] = 一节课快结束时，你的水洼像个赌气的孩子一样站着。算数。\n# en: Watch the student next to you\nopt.right.label [b884e9] = 观察旁边同学的做法\n# en: You learn their trick, and also that they hum while they cast. You hum too. It works, inexplicably.\nopt.right.say.1 [c0e062] = 你学到了对方的诀窍，还发现他们施法时会哼歌。你也哼了。莫名其妙地，管用。\n\n@event evo_misfire\n# en: Misfire\ntitle [3350b4] = 失手\n# en: Someone's tidecalling meets someone else's flame in the middle of the room. The hall fills with scalding steam and twenty students who all have opinions.\ntext [e661e8] = 某人的唤潮术在房间正中撞上了另一个人的火焰。讲堂里灌满了滚烫的蒸汽，还有二十个各有意见的学生。\n# en: A lecture hall filling with steam after a spell meets a bucket of water at the wrong moment.\nscene [ffbe1f] = 一道咒语在错误的时刻撞上一桶水，讲堂里蒸汽弥漫。\n# en: Shield the front row\nopt.left.label [1e4bc0] = 护住前排\n# en: You take the worst of it on your sleeves. The front row looks at you the way people look at lifeboats.\nopt.left.say.1 [d4298c] = 最烫的那一下你用袖子挡了。前排的人看你的眼神，就像看救生艇。\n# en: Get out and open the windows\nopt.right.label [eb7ddc] = 出去开窗\n# en: The steam pours out into the courtyard and becomes, briefly, a very small cloud with a grudge.\nopt.right.say.1 [3f970d] = 蒸汽涌进庭院，短暂地变成了一朵很小、很记仇的云。\n\n@event evo_tide_wall\n# en: The Tide Wall\ntitle [f1844c] = 潮墙\n# en: Evocation II takes the class to the lakeshore. The exercise: raise a wall of water and hold it while a classmate walks past behind it. Your classmate looks nervous. So do you.\ntext [5eb5bd] = 塑能术 II 把全班带到湖岸。练习：升起一道水墙并撑住，让一位同学从墙后走过。那位同学看起来很紧张。你也是。\n# en: A student holding back a wall of lake water on the shore while classmates watch from behind them.\nscene [23ccc0] = 一名学生在岸边挡住一道湖水筑成的墙，同学们在后面看着。\n# en: Hold it steady\nopt.left.label [d6f0e9] = 稳稳撑住\n# en: The wall holds. Your classmate walks past dry and amazed. You let it down gently, which is the hard part.\nopt.left.say.1 [240cd4] = 水墙撑住了。同学干干爽爽地走过去，满脸惊叹。你把墙轻轻放下——这才是最难的部分。\n# en: The wall holds until it doesn't, and comes down on both of you. Your classmate is very wet and very gracious about it. You shiver until dinner.\nopt.left.say.2 [51a46b] = 水墙撑住了，直到撑不住，哗地砸在你们俩身上。同学浑身湿透，却对此十分大度。你一直哆嗦到晚饭。\n# en: Ask to go second\nopt.right.label [208e6d] = 请求第二个上\n# en: You watch three people do it first and learn from all of their mistakes.\nopt.right.say.1 [2b89b8] = 你看着三个人先做，把他们的错误统统学了个遍。\n\n@event evo_lattice\n# en: The Flame Lattice\ntitle [7d30cd] = 火焰格网\n# en: Fire woven into a lattice, each strand holding the others up. The lecturer says the founders built wards like this. The lecturer also says you have twenty minutes.\ntext [954a4b] = 火被编成格网，每一股都撑着其他几股。讲师说创始者当年就是这样筑护界的。讲师还说你有二十分钟。\n# en: Interlocking threads of fire hanging in the air of a lecture hall like a glowing net.\nscene [827db1] = 交错的火线悬在讲堂半空，像一张发光的网。\n# en: Build it slowly\nopt.left.label [03433a] = 慢慢搭\n# en: Your lattice is small and ugly and holds perfectly. The lecturer says \"Founder-ish,\" which you choose to take as praise.\nopt.left.say.1 [068b5a] = 你的格网又小又丑，却撑得完美无缺。讲师说“有点创始者的味道”，你决定把它当成表扬。\n# en: Build it big\nopt.right.label [33df52] = 搭个大的\n# en: {~It's magnificent for nine seconds.|It holds just long enough for everyone to see it.} Then the strands find each other and the whole thing sighs into sparks, and you understand exactly why.\nopt.right.say.1 [f9a733] = {~它壮丽了整整九秒。|它撑得刚好够每个人看清。}然后火线彼此找到对方，整个东西叹息一声，化作火星，而你完全明白了原因。\n# en: The strands find each other in the first second, and the whole thing goes up in your face. You spend the rest of the lesson picking sparks out of your hair.\nopt.right.say.2 [aa5b3b] = 第一秒火线就缠到了一起，整个东西在你脸前炸开。那节课剩下的时间，你都在从头发里挑火星。\n\n@event evo_storm_pairing\n# en: Storm Pairing\ntitle [6e518e] = 风暴搭档\n# en: Advanced Evocation pairs students for a storm: one calls the rain, the other the lightning, and neither is allowed to let go first. You're paired with {partner}.\ntext [bcffa7] = 高等塑能术让学生两两搭档召唤风暴：一人唤雨，一人引雷，谁都不许先松手。你的搭档是{partner}。\n# en: Two students on a windswept tower top, one calling rain, the other calling lightning, the sky between them churning.\nscene [0f5fd7] = 狂风中的塔顶上，两名学生一个唤雨一个引雷，两人之间的天空翻涌不息。\n# en: Call the lightning\nopt.left.label [26bb28] = 引雷\n# en: The storm answers both of you at once. {partner} laughs into the wind, soaked and astonished.\nopt.left.say.1 [2fb5cc] = 风暴同时回应了你们俩。{partner}迎着风大笑，浑身湿透，满脸惊愕。\n# en: The storm answers both of you at once. Lyra looks at you as though you've finally said something worth hearing.\nopt.left.say.1@lyra [e5dc7b] = 风暴同时回应了你们俩。莱拉看着你，仿佛你终于说了句值得一听的话。\n# en: The lightning goes where it likes. You both spend an afternoon in the infirmary, comparing burns.\nopt.left.say.2 [f7819a] = 闪电想去哪儿就去哪儿。你们俩在医务室待了一下午，互相比较烧伤。\n# en: Call the rain, and let them lead\nopt.right.label [f3854c] = 唤雨，让对方主导\n# en: You hold the rain steady while {partner} draws the lightning down. Afterwards {they} thank{s} you for not trying to steal it.\nopt.right.say.1 [be557c] = 你稳住雨幕，{partner}把闪电引了下来。之后{they}谢谢你没有抢风头。\n\n@event evo_final_practical\n# en: The Final Practical\ntitle [035f37] = 期末实践考\n# en: The last practical of Advanced Evocation: melt an ice dummy without touching the floor around it. Professor Thorne is on the panel, and he is not blinking.\ntext [9b1d08] = 高等塑能术的最后一场实践考：融化一个冰人偶，不许碰到周围的地面。索恩教授在考官席上，眼都不眨。\n# en: An examination hall cleared of desks, a single student facing three examiners and a target dummy made of ice.\nscene [49ac8b] = 清空了课桌的考场，一名学生面对三位考官和一个冰做的靶子人偶。\n# en: Fire, precisely\nopt.left.label [c25e11] = 用火，精准地\n# en: The ice runs off the dummy and not a drop touches the floor. Thorne says \"Hm,\" and writes something underlined.\nopt.left.say.1 [0fdd37] = 冰从人偶上流下，没有一滴碰到地面。索恩说了声“嗯”，写下一行带下划线的字。\n# en: The dummy melts, and so does a fair part of the floor. Thorne writes something with a question mark.\nopt.left.say.2 [20fe15] = 人偶融了，地板也融了不少。索恩写下一行带问号的字。\n# en: Water, patiently\nopt.right.label [21c7ce] = 用水，耐心地\n# en: You draw the cold out of the ice with water instead of fire. It takes twice as long. The panel watches every minute.\nopt.right.say.1 [8c406a] = 你用水而不是火把冰里的寒意抽了出来。花了两倍的时间。考官们一分钟都没错过。\n\n@event duel_first_practice\n# en: Footwork\ntitle [86c7d4] = 步法\n# en: Lyra runs practice like a general. \"Footwork,\" she says. \"If your feet are wrong, your spell is a rumour.\" She pairs you with a dummy called Gerald the Second.\ntext [ef78a6] = 莱拉像将军一样主持练习。“步法，”她说，“步法错了，你的咒语就只是个传闻。”她给你配了一个叫“杰拉尔德二世”的假人。\n# en: A duelling hall with chalk circles on the floor and practice dummies with names painted on their chests.\nscene [f28b25] = 地上画着粉笔圈的决斗厅，练习假人胸口上漆着名字。\n# en: Drill footwork\nopt.left.label [7dee9f] = 练步法\n# en: Two hours of stepping in circles. Gerald the Second wins every round. Your feet, however, are now correct.\nopt.left.say.1 [9d28ca] = 绕圈走了两个小时。杰拉尔德二世赢了每一回合。不过你的脚，现在是对的了。\n# en: Spar for real\nopt.right.label [9e070e] = 真刀真枪对练\n# en: You land a clean disarm on your partner. Across the hall, Lyra raises an eyebrow, which is her version of a standing ovation.\nopt.right.say.1 [71d335] = 你干净利落地缴了对手的械。大厅另一头，莱拉挑了挑眉——这是她版本的起立鼓掌。\n# en: You spend the evening relearning which way is up. Lyra says that's the first lesson anyway.\nopt.right.say.2 [99acac] = 你一晚上都在重新学习哪边是上。莱拉说反正这本来就是第一课。\n\n@event duel_footwork_feud\n# en: The Footwork Feud\ntitle [cf293b] = 步法之争\n# en: Bram says the Circle's footwork is \"Emberhall nonsense\" and Lyra says Bram's footwork is \"a hazard to shipping\". Both of them look at you to settle it.\ntext [8b7ae0] = 布拉姆说决斗社的步法是“烬厅的胡闹”，莱拉说布拉姆的步法是“航运公害”。两人都看着你，等你裁决。\n# en: Two students arguing in the middle of a duelling circle while the rest of the club pretends not to listen.\nscene [a9dbb3] = 两名学生在决斗圈中央争吵，社里其他人假装没在听。\n# en: Side with Lyra\nopt.left.label [451590] = 站莱拉这边\n# en: Lyra nods as if this were obvious. Bram takes a demerit book out of his pocket, looks at it, and puts it back. Progress.\nopt.left.say.1 [dfa265] = 莱拉点点头，好像这是理所当然。布拉姆从口袋里掏出记过本，看了看，又放了回去。有进步。\n# en: Side with Bram\nopt.right.label [1bec38] = 站布拉姆这边\n# en: Bram looks startled to be agreed with. Lyra looks at you like a puzzle she will solve later, with fire.\nopt.right.say.1 [4720c9] = 有人赞同自己，布拉姆显得很吃惊。莱拉看你的样子，像在看一道她以后会用火来解的谜题。\n\n@event duel_fixture\n# en: Fixture Against Thornwick\ntitle [d8c992] = 对阵荆木\n# en: Thornwick Academy's duelling team has crossed the hills for a fixture. The Circle needs one more duellist in the line-up, and the captain is looking at you.\ntext [7ff7a2] = 荆木学院的决斗队翻山过来比赛。决斗社的阵容还差一名决斗者，社长正看着你。\n# en: A duelling green with rival banners, students from both academies packed onto benches.\nscene [0b0deb] = 挂着两校旗帜的决斗场，两所学院的学生挤满长凳。\n# en: Duel for the Circle\nopt.left.label [4b23b1] = 为决斗社出战\n# en: You win your bout, and the Circle wins the fixture by a whisker. Someone carries you a few steps before remembering they don't like you.\nopt.left.say.1 [8c53d7] = 你赢了自己那场，决斗社以毫厘之差赢下比赛。有人把你扛起来走了几步，才想起他们并不喜欢你。\n# en: A draw. The Circle loses by one bout, but nobody blames you, and {around(lyra): Lyra|the captain} says \"acceptable\" twice.\nopt.left.say.2 [ec8511] = 平局。决斗社输了一场，但没人怪你，{around(lyra): 莱拉|队长}说了两遍“还行”。\n# en: The Thornwick duellist is very good and very polite about it, and every bruise you collect is a polite one. You hate both.\nopt.left.say.3 [1736bb] = 荆木的决斗者非常厉害，而且对此非常有礼貌，你身上的每一处淤青都挨得很有礼貌。两样你都讨厌。\n# en: Offer to be the reserve\nopt.right.label [44cb08] = 主动当替补\n# en: You hold coats and bring water. It turns out reserves see everything, including how Thornwick cheats.\nopt.right.say.1 [eac3f5] = 你帮人拿外套、递水。原来替补什么都看得见，包括荆木是怎么作弊的。\n\n@event duel_captain_call\n# en: The Captain's Question\ntitle [dfe526] = 社长的问题\n# en: After practice, the captain sits beside you. \"When I graduate, someone has to run this place,\" she says. \"Someone who turns up. You turn up.\"\ntext [3fd3c8] = 练习结束后，社长在你身边坐下。“我毕业以后，总得有人管这地方，”她说，“得是个会准时出现的人。你会。”\n# en: An empty duelling hall at night, two students sitting on the edge of a chalk circle.\nscene [0d20d7] = 夜里空荡荡的决斗厅，两名学生坐在粉笔圈边上。\n# en: Say you'll think about it\nopt.left.label [b4769e] = 说你会考虑\n# en: \"Think fast,\" she says. \"Feet first, then spells.\" You're fairly sure that's a yes.\nopt.left.say.1 [a70c37] = “考虑快点，”她说，“先动脚，再施法。”你很确定这算是答应了。\n# en: Say you're not the type\nopt.right.label [9fe8f9] = 说你不是那块料\n# en: \"Nobody's the type,\" she says. \"That's why it has to be someone who turns up.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [cd23ec] = “没人是那块料，”她说，“所以才得是个会准时出现的人。”\n\n@event lib_shelving_rush\n# en: The Shelving Rush\ntitle [5625e2] = 上架高峰\n# en: Exam week, and every book in the library has been borrowed, read and returned at once. The cart is taller than you are. Footnote is sitting on top of it.\ntext [6fa1df] = 考试周，图书馆里的每本书都同时被借走、读完又还回来。书车比你还高。脚注正坐在最顶上。\n# en: A library cart overflowing with returned books, a student pushing it at a run between towering shelves.\nscene [7fe4c3] = 一辆堆满还书的图书车，一名学生推着它在高耸的书架间小跑。\n# en: Shelve properly\nopt.left.label [bac2cb] = 规规矩矩地上架\n# en: Every book in its place by closing. Footnote has moved to the top shelf to supervise.\nopt.left.say.1 [3b9e2e] = 闭馆前每本书都归了位。脚注已经挪到最高一层书架上监工了。\n# en: Shelve quickly\nopt.right.label [9d5012] = 快速上架\n# en: The books are on shelves. Most of them are even on the right shelves. Someone will find out in a decade.\nopt.right.say.1 [79d70e] = 书都在架子上了。大部分甚至在对的架子上。十年后会有人发现的。\n\n@event lib_overdue_mage\n# en: The Overdue Mage\ntitle [99bf32] = 逾期的法师\n# en: An old mage returns a book sixty years overdue and asks, with great dignity, what the fine is. The ledger says the fine is more than the library.\ntext [6a40d1] = 一位老法师归还一本逾期六十年的书，并十分庄重地询问罚金是多少。账本上的罚金比图书馆还值钱。\n# en: An elderly mage at the library desk clutching a book that is sixty years overdue.\nscene [b70648] = 一位年迈的法师站在图书馆柜台前，紧攥着一本逾期六十年的书。\n# en: Waive it\nopt.left.label [899866] = 免了吧\n# en: The old mage thanks you and leaves a sweet on the desk. Later you find out he taught Professor Thorne.\nopt.left.say.1 [ef4397] = 老法师谢过你，在柜台上留下一颗糖。后来你才知道，他教过索恩教授。\n# en: Charge it, by the book\nopt.right.label [396965] = 照章收费\n# en: The mage pays in coins so old the bank has to look them up. The head librarian is delighted, and gives you the collector's share of the fine, as is tradition.\nopt.right.say.1 [9de00f] = 法师付的硬币老到银行都得去查。馆长高兴坏了，还按惯例分给你一份收罚款的提成。\n\n@event lib_footnote_strike\n# en: Footnote Goes on Strike\ntitle [ec48a4] = 脚注罢工\n# en: Footnote has lain down across the reference desk and will not move. Students are queueing. The cat has made its demands clear: none.\ntext [8627c8] = 脚注横躺在参考咨询台上，一动不动。学生们在排队。这只猫已经把它的诉求表达得很清楚了：没有诉求。\n# en: A large library cat lying across a reference desk, refusing to move, while students queue in front of it.\nscene [bcfc48] = 一只大个儿的图书馆猫横躺在参考咨询台上不肯挪窝，学生们在它面前排起了队。\n# en: Bribe him with fish\nopt.left.label [ca7565] = 用鱼贿赂它\n# en: Footnote accepts the fish, the apology and the chair you bring him. The queue resumes.\nopt.left.say.1 [d9ebb3] = 脚注接受了鱼、道歉，还有你搬来的椅子。队伍恢复了流动。\n# en: Work around him\nopt.right.label [ea0c37] = 绕开它干活\n# en: You stamp books on the far end of the desk for an hour. Footnote watches with the calm of a cat who has won.\nopt.right.say.1 [40e4a9] = 你在柜台另一头盖了一小时的章。脚注以胜利者的平静注视着你。\n\n@event court_fountain_freeze\n# en: The Frozen Fountain\ntitle [f87a8e] = 冻住的喷泉\n# en: The courtyard fountain has frozen in the middle of a splash. The water hangs in the air like glass. A first-year is trying to lick it.\ntext [abc188] = 庭院的喷泉在水花溅起的一瞬冻住了。水像玻璃一样悬在半空。一个一年级生正想去舔它。\n# en: A courtyard fountain frozen mid-splash, the water hanging in the air like glass.\nscene [8a0a42] = 庭院喷泉冻在了水花飞溅的瞬间，水像玻璃一样悬在空中。\n# en: Stop the first-year\nopt.left.label [d24736] = 拦住那个一年级生\n# en: You pull the first-year away just as the ice cracks. For a moment you'd swear you saw a black star reflected in it.\nopt.left.say.1 [267623] = 冰裂开的那一刻，你正好把一年级生拉开。有一瞬间，你发誓看见冰里映着一颗黑色的星。\n# en: Study the ice\nopt.right.label [e6dcae] = 研究这块冰\n# en: The ice is colder than ice should be. Your notes say \"colder than ice should be\" three times.\nopt.right.say.1 [3744c0] = 这冰比冰该有的还冷。你的笔记里把“比冰该有的还冷”写了三遍。\n\n@event court_rumour_mill\n# en: The Rumour Mill\ntitle [b2db14] = 流言工坊\n# en: {gossip} has news, which is to say gossip, which is to say three different stories about the same broken window. \"You'll want to hear this,\" {they} say{s}.\ntext [ba7e30] = {gossip}有新闻——也就是八卦——也就是关于同一扇破窗户的三个不同版本。“你会想听的，”{they}说。\n# en: Students huddled around the courtyard fountain trading whispers, one of them clearly enjoying it most.\nscene [bc5c84] = 学生们围在庭院喷泉边交换耳语，其中一个显然最乐在其中。\n# en: Listen to all of it\nopt.left.label [71c08a] = 全都听一遍\n# en: You hear all three stories. Two are true. One is about you.\nopt.left.say.1 [1839fa] = 三个版本你都听了。两个是真的。一个是关于你的。\n# en: You hear all three stories. Nessa is already writing the fourth.\nopt.left.say.1@nessa [198fa7] = 三个版本你都听了。内莎已经在写第四个了。\n# en: Make up a better one\nopt.right.label [ed234b] = 编个更好的\n# en: Your story is better than all three. By dinner it's the official version.\nopt.right.say.1 [2aa741] = 你的版本比那三个都好。到晚饭时，它已经成了官方说法。\n\n@event lake_skimming\n# en: Skimming Stones\ntitle [a14e0e] = 打水漂\n# en: {friend} is skimming stones on the Mirror Lake. The water is so still each stone leaves rings that go on forever. \"Seven,\" {they} say{s}. \"Beat that.\"\ntext [90f788] = {friend}在镜湖上打水漂。湖面静得出奇，每块石头留下的涟漪都仿佛永远不会散。“七下，”{they}说，“来比比。”\n# en: Two students skimming stones across a perfectly still lake at dusk.\nscene [d7fdb5] = 黄昏时分，两名学生在一片静止如镜的湖面上打水漂。\n# en: Try to beat it\nopt.left.label [1284ba] = 试着赢过去\n# en: Nine skips. {friend} demands a rematch and gets one. The afternoon disappears into stones.\nopt.left.say.1 [1f9fa1] = 九下。{friend}要求再来一局，也如愿了。整个下午都消失在石子里。\n# en: Your stone sinks with great dignity. {friend} laughs until {they} have to sit down.\nopt.left.say.2 [533d65] = 你的石头带着极大的尊严沉了下去。{friend}笑得只好坐下来。\n# en: Just watch the rings\nopt.right.label [39fffc] = 就看涟漪\n# en: You sit and watch the rings spread until the light goes. Neither of you says much. It's one of the good afternoons.\nopt.right.say.1 [2ebf1f] = 你坐着看涟漪扩散，直到天光散尽。你们俩都没说什么。这是那种美好的下午之一。\n\n@event lake_reflection_bet\n# en: The Reflection Bet\ntitle [7b2fea] = 倒影的赌约\n# en: Your reflection in the Mirror Lake is holding a coin you don't have. It flips it. It catches it. It offers it to you.\ntext [ad0e65] = 你在镜湖里的倒影拿着一枚你并没有的硬币。它抛起，接住，然后递向你。\n# en: A student staring into a still lake where their reflection appears to be holding a coin.\nscene [407b4a] = 一名学生凝视着平静的湖面，水中的倒影似乎握着一枚硬币。\n# en: Take the coin\nopt.left.label [4cb026] = 接过硬币\n# en: The coin is real, and cold, and you can't remember putting it in your pocket.\nopt.left.say.1 [67628e] = 硬币是真的，冰凉，而你想不起自己是什么时候把它放进口袋的。\n# en: Shake your head\nopt.right.label [5e0346] = 摇头\n# en: Your reflection shrugs, pockets the coin and walks out of the frame before you do.\nopt.right.say.1 [3c806f] = 你的倒影耸耸肩，把硬币揣进兜里，先你一步走出了画面。\n\n@event ref_food_fight\n# en: Pudding Diplomacy\ntitle [f97acc] = 布丁外交\n# en: A pudding sails across the refectory and lands in Bram's lap. The hall goes silent. Every eye turns to the table it came from, which is yours.\ntext [e5a752] = 一块布丁飞过膳堂，落在布拉姆腿上。大厅一片死寂。所有目光都转向它飞来的那张桌子——你的那张。\n# en: A refectory mid-food-fight, puddings in the air, a prefect rising slowly from behind a table.\nscene [b7e5a2] = 膳堂里一场食物大战正酣，布丁在空中飞，一位级长从桌子后面缓缓站起。\n# en: Own up\nopt.left.label [3a0128] = 承认是我\n# en: Bram writes you a demerit, then pauses. \"Good throw,\" he says, almost without wanting to.\nopt.left.say.1 [d32724] = 布拉姆给你记了一次过，然后顿了顿。“扔得好，”他说，几乎是不情愿的。\n# en: Throw another\nopt.right.label [7417cc] = 再扔一块\n# en: The second pudding starts a war that the refectory will talk about for years. Historians will argue about who won. You know.\nopt.right.say.1 [2e4f89] = 第二块布丁引发了一场膳堂会议论好几年的战争。史学家会争论谁赢了。你知道。\n\n@event ref_cook_secret\n# en: The Cook's Secret\ntitle [c0a5b6] = 厨娘的秘密\n# en: The cook catches you lingering by the kitchen door. \"You look like someone who misses proper soup,\" she says, and hands you a bowl before you can deny it.\ntext [1cf0fd] = 厨娘逮到你在厨房门口徘徊。“你看着就像个想念正经汤的人，”她说，不等你否认就递来一碗。\n# en: A huge kitchen behind the refectory, an enormous cook stirring a pot with a spoon the size of an oar.\nscene [ea98ca] = 膳堂后面的大厨房，一位魁梧的厨娘用船桨那么大的勺子搅着锅。\n# en: Eat it slowly\nopt.left.label [3da080] = 慢慢地喝\n# en: It tastes like the kitchen at home. You don't say so. She can tell anyway.\nopt.left.say.1 [d6f79e] = 这味道像家里的厨房。你没说出口。她反正看得出来。\n# en: Ask for the recipe\nopt.right.label [604f64] = 讨要配方\n# en: \"Ha,\" says the cook, which means no, which means you'll have to keep coming back.\nopt.right.say.1 [fdb0a2] = “哈，”厨娘说。意思是不行，也就是说你得一直回来。\n\n@event obs_comet_watch\n# en: The Comet Watch\ntitle [d479ab] = 守望彗星\n# en: A comet is due tonight and the observatory is full of students pretending they came for the science. The telescope has a queue. The roof has a view.\ntext [20d293] = 今晚有彗星经过，观星台里挤满了假装是为科学而来的学生。望远镜前排着队。屋顶上有好风景。\n# en: The brass dome of an observatory open to the night, a student at the eyepiece, another beside them with a notebook.\nscene [3227ec] = 向夜空敞开的黄铜穹顶观星台，一名学生凑在目镜前，另一人拿着笔记本站在旁边。\n# en: Queue for the telescope\nopt.left.label [1a7ef0] = 排队用望远镜\n# en: Your turn at the eyepiece lasts thirty seconds. The comet's tail is green. You'll remember it longer than most lectures.\nopt.left.say.1 [54f31c] = 轮到你看目镜只有三十秒。彗尾是绿色的。你会记得它比大多数课都久。\n# en: Watch from the roof\nopt.right.label [5e0cfb] = 去屋顶看\n# en: The comet crosses the sky slowly, like it's looking for something. Kael is already on the roof. He moves over to make room.\nopt.right.say.1 [344326] = 彗星慢慢划过天空，像在找什么东西。凯尔已经在屋顶上了。他挪了挪，给你让出位置。\n\n@event obs_star_chart\n# en: The Missing Star\ntitle [f95144] = 缺失的星\n# en: An old star chart in the observatory archive has a circle drawn where a star should be, with a note in faded ink: \"It eats the light. Do not chart it.\"\ntext [f9ab12] = 观星台档案里一张旧星图上，本该是一颗星的地方画着一个圈，旁边用褪色的墨水写着：“它吞噬光。勿绘。”\n# en: An old star chart spread on a desk, one constellation with a careful empty circle drawn where a star should be.\nscene [8cbcfa] = 桌上摊开一张旧星图，某个星座里本该有星的位置被仔细地画了一个空圈。\n# en: Copy the chart\nopt.left.label [ddb5f3] = 抄下星图\n# en: You copy it carefully. That night you dream of a circle with nothing inside it, waiting.\nopt.left.say.1 [c900a1] = 你仔细地抄了下来。那晚你梦见一个空无一物的圈，在等待。\n# en: Put it back\nopt.right.label [862ed2] = 放回原处\n# en: You return it to the drawer. The drawer feels colder when you close it.\nopt.right.say.1 [ca8bc6] = 你把它放回抽屉。合上时，抽屉似乎冷了些。\n\n@event green_mandrake_choir\n# en: The Mandrake Choir\ntitle [a16982] = 曼德拉草合唱团\n# en: Tobias has taught the greenhouse mandrakes to scream in harmony. He would like your opinion. The mandrakes would like an audience.\ntext [26476f] = 托比亚斯教会了温室里的曼德拉草用和声尖叫。他想听听你的意见。曼德拉草想要观众。\n# en: A greenhouse bench of potted mandrakes wearing tiny earmuffs, a student conducting them with a pencil.\nscene [556372] = 温室长凳上一排戴着小耳罩的盆栽曼德拉草，一名学生拿铅笔指挥它们。\n# en: Conduct a verse\nopt.left.label [8d7d71] = 指挥一段\n# en: You conduct one verse. Your ears ring for a day. Tobias says it's the best performance they've given.\nopt.left.say.1 [fe14aa] = 你指挥了一段。耳朵嗡嗡响了一整天。托比亚斯说这是它们最好的一场演出。\n# en: Wear earmuffs and listen\nopt.right.label [fa6dc3] = 戴上耳罩听\n# en: Through the earmuffs it sounds almost beautiful. Tobias is so proud he cries a little.\nopt.right.say.1 [07ed14] = 隔着耳罩，听起来几乎很美。托比亚斯骄傲得哭了一小会儿。\n\n@event green_moonflower_sprout\n# en: A Moonflower Seedling\ntitle [e51a03] = 月光花幼苗\n# en: In a forgotten corner of the greenhouse, a moonflower seed has sprouted on its own, eleven years early. It glows faintly when you lean over it.\ntext [b895be] = 温室一个被遗忘的角落里，一粒月光花种子自己发了芽，早了整整十一年。你俯身靠近时，它发出微弱的光。\n# en: A tiny silver-leaved seedling in a clay pot, glowing faintly in a dark greenhouse.\nscene [1fd66d] = 陶盆里一株银叶小苗，在黑暗的温室中幽幽发光。\n# en: Pot it and look after it\nopt.left.label [aa4dfe] = 移进盆里好好照料\n# en: You give it a pot, a label and a name. It glows a little brighter when you visit.\nopt.left.say.1 [3976bb] = 你给了它一个盆、一张标签和一个名字。你来看它时，它会亮一点。\n# en: Tell Tobias\nopt.right.label [fe6832] = 告诉托比亚斯\n# en: Tobias is speechless, then very loud. The seedling gets a sign, a fence and a rota.\nopt.right.say.1 [69881e] = 托比亚斯先是说不出话，然后非常大声。幼苗得到了一块牌子、一圈篱笆和一张值班表。\n\n@event castle_empty_halls\n# en: Empty Halls\ntitle [468042] = 空荡的走廊\n# en: The castle in the holidays is enormous and quiet. Your footsteps echo down corridors you've never been alone in. A door you've never noticed is standing open.\ntext [30fae9] = 假期里的城堡巨大而安静。你的脚步声在从没独自走过的走廊里回荡。一扇你从未注意过的门敞开着。\n# en: A long castle corridor in the holidays, dust in sunbeams, one student walking alone.\nscene [c48035] = 假期里一条长长的城堡走廊，阳光里浮着灰尘，一名学生独自走着。\n# en: Go through the door\nopt.left.label [974ffb] = 穿过那扇门\n# en: It leads to a stair that leads to a locked door that leads, you're almost sure, to the seventh tower.\nopt.left.say.1 [6544d0] = 门后是一段楼梯，楼梯通向一扇锁着的门，而那扇门——你几乎可以肯定——通向第七塔。\n# en: Read in the window seat\nopt.right.label [a83ff6] = 在窗边座位上读书\n# en: You read all day in a window seat with the whole lake below you. The best kind of nothing.\nopt.right.say.1 [19769a] = 你在窗边座位上读了一整天书，整片湖就在脚下。最好的那种无所事事。\n\n@event castle_skeleton_crew\n# en: The Skeleton Crew\ntitle [6ec6d4] = 留守的人们\n# en: Supper in the holidays is one table in the Great Hall: you, three other students, the cook, two prefects and Old Maud, who has brought her goat.\ntext [a9be7b] = 假期的晚饭只在大礼堂摆一张桌子：你、另外三个学生、厨娘、两位级长，还有带着山羊来的老莫德。\n# en: A handful of staff and students sharing a long table in a nearly empty great hall.\nscene [4c6b8f] = 几乎空荡的大礼堂里，寥寥几名职员和学生共用一张长桌。\n# en: Sit next to Maud\nopt.left.label [d82b60] = 坐在莫德旁边\n# en: Maud tells a story about the frozen spring that she's never told the term-time students. Bartholomew eats your napkin.\nopt.left.say.1 [2c57bf] = 莫德讲了一个关于冰封之春的故事，那是她从没对学期里的学生讲过的。巴塞洛缪吃掉了你的餐巾。\n# en: Play cards with the prefects\nopt.right.label [a22285] = 和级长们打牌\n# en: The prefects play cards like they enforce rules: badly and with great confidence. You win dessert and most of their pocket money.\nopt.right.say.1 [a7aad7] = 级长们打牌就像执行校规：水平很烂，信心十足。你赢了甜点，还有他们大半的零花钱。\n\n@event home_family_supper\n# en: Supper at Home\ntitle [1f26c6] = 家里的晚饭\n# en: Supper at home. Everyone wants to know what you've learned. You try to explain evocation to an aunt who thinks it's a kind of cheese.\ntext [8ab73f] = 家里的晚饭。每个人都想知道你学了什么。你试着向一位以为塑能术是某种奶酪的姑妈解释它。\n# en: A crowded kitchen table, a family passing dishes, a student back from the academy being asked too many questions.\nscene [ec2a5f] = 挤满人的餐桌，一家人传着菜，一个从学院回来的学生被问了太多问题。\n# en: Show them a spell\nopt.left.label [803b4f] = 给他们露一手\n# en: You light every candle in the room at once. Your aunt applauds and asks if you can do it with cheese.\nopt.left.say.1 [5d4fe6] = 你一下点亮了屋里所有的蜡烛。姑妈鼓掌，问你能不能对奶酪也来一下。\n# en: Just listen\nopt.right.label [6a9f3e] = 就听着\n# en: You let them talk. You'd forgotten how loud and how good it is.\nopt.right.say.1 [738cc0] = 你让他们说。你都忘了这有多吵，又有多好。\n\n@event home_old_friend\n# en: An Old Friend\ntitle [8809c5] = 老朋友\n# en: Your oldest friend is sitting on the wall at the edge of the fields, exactly where you left them. \"So,\" they say. \"Are you a wizard now, or what?\"\ntext [e85e1a] = 你最老的朋友坐在田边的矮墙上，就在你离开时的那个位置。“所以，”对方说，“你现在算巫师了，还是怎样？”\n# en: Two old friends sitting on a field wall at sunset, one in academy robes, the other in work clothes.\nscene [82fd45] = 夕阳下两位老朋友坐在田边矮墙上，一个穿着学院长袍，另一个穿着干活的衣服。\n# en: Tell them everything\nopt.left.label [09f8b1] = 把一切都告诉对方\n# en: You talk until the stars come out. They want to hear about the lake most of all.\nopt.left.say.1 [5d2429] = 你们一直聊到星星出来。对方最想听的是那片湖。\n# en: Ask about their life instead\nopt.right.label [24a3a1] = 反过来问问对方的生活\n# en: They've been busy too: a farm, a fiancé, a goat that bites. Your life isn't the only one that grew.\nopt.right.say.1 [91a591] = 对方也很忙：一座农场、一位未婚夫、一只咬人的山羊。长大的不只是你的生活。\n\n@event home_market_day\n# en: Market Day\ntitle [0bcdaf] = 赶集日\n# en: Market day in town. A stallholder recognises your academy robes and asks if you can do anything about his leaky roof. For a price.\ntext [ebfea1] = 镇上的赶集日。一个摊主认出了你的学院长袍，问你能不能帮他修修漏雨的屋顶。有偿的。\n# en: A small country market, a student in academy robes being stared at by stallholders.\nscene [a7a658] = 一个乡间小集市，一名穿着学院长袍的学生被摊主们盯着看。\n# en: Fix it with magic\nopt.left.label [77d22c] = 用魔法修好它\n# en: The roof is dry, the stallholder is thrilled and you are slightly scorched. He pays in apples and coins.\nopt.left.say.1 [f09cd8] = 屋顶干了，摊主乐坏了，你被轻微烤焦了。他用苹果和硬币付账。\n# en: Just buy sweets\nopt.right.label [bbf438] = 只买糖\n# en: The sweet shop still sells your favourites. You eat them on the bridge like you used to.\nopt.right.say.1 [ea94aa] = 糖果店还卖你最爱的那几样。你像从前一样坐在桥上吃。\n\n@event work_double_shift\n# en: The Double Shift\ntitle [6df679] = 连轴转\n# en: Holiday shifts pay double and feel triple. It's your second shift today and the evening rush hasn't started.\ntext [60fdf7] = 假期的班工钱翻倍，感觉翻三倍。这是你今天的第二个班，晚高峰还没开始。\n# en: A student asleep on their feet behind a counter, a clock on the wall showing a very late hour.\nscene [6bbbbe] = 一名学生站在柜台后面睡着了，墙上的钟指着一个很晚的时间。\n# en: Push through\nopt.left.label [f6ed7c] = 咬牙撑过去\n# en: You push through. The coins are heavy in your pocket and your feet are heavy everywhere else.\nopt.left.say.1 [900c8d] = 你撑过去了。口袋里的硬币沉甸甸的，身上其他地方也沉甸甸的。\n# en: Swap the shift\nopt.right.label [dcf3f9] = 找人换班\n# en: A colleague takes it for a favour you'll owe later. You sleep for eleven hours.\nopt.right.say.1 [6106e9] = 一位同事替了你，换你以后欠一个人情。你睡了十一个小时。\n\n@event intern_first_assignment\n# en: The First Assignment\ntitle [12864b] = 第一项任务\n# en: Your supervisor at the Royal Library hands you a list of forty uncatalogued boxes. \"Find out what's in them,\" she says. \"Carefully. One of them is probably alive.\"\ntext [106ac0] = 你在皇家图书馆的导师递给你一张清单，上面是四十个未编目的箱子。“查清里面有什么，”她说，“小心点。其中有一个大概是活的。”\n# en: A vast underground archive, a supervisor handing a student a list longer than their arm.\nscene [cf395e] = 一座巨大的地下档案馆，导师递给学生一张比胳膊还长的清单。\n# en: Work through the boxes\nopt.left.label [af7cdf] = 逐个整理箱子\n# en: Box thirty-one is alive. It's a very small, very old dictionary that bites. You catalogue it.\nopt.left.say.1 [f9f573] = 第三十一号箱是活的。里面是一本很小、很老、会咬人的词典。你把它编了目。\n# en: Ask the other interns\nopt.right.label [d7f73e] = 问问其他实习生\n# en: The other interns have a system, a rota and a song. You learn all three.\nopt.right.say.1 [b61185] = 其他实习生有一套系统、一张值班表和一首歌。三样你都学会了。\n\n@event intern_index_maze\n# en: The Index Maze\ntitle [708be7] = 索引迷宫\n# en: A cross-reference in the archive leads to another, and another, and another, deeper under the city. The last card is about the founders of Lumenhold.\ntext [6ca7e0] = 档案里的一条交叉引用指向另一条，又一条，再一条，越来越深地钻进城市地下。最后一张卡片讲的是流明堡的创始者。\n# en: A student lost between endless shelves underground, following an index card trail by lamplight.\nscene [37ed2d] = 一名学生迷失在地下无尽的书架间，借着灯光追寻一串索引卡的线索。\n# en: Follow it to the end\nopt.left.label [b0b834] = 追到底\n# en: At the end is a letter from a founder about \"the seventh lock\". You copy it with shaking hands.\nopt.left.say.1 [89da61] = 尽头是一位创始者写的信，谈到“第七把锁”。你手发着抖把它抄了下来。\n# en: The trail ends at a missing card. Somebody took it. Recently.\nopt.left.say.2 [a4b290] = 线索断在一张缺失的卡片上。有人拿走了它。就在最近。\n# en: Report it to your supervisor\nopt.right.label [10c4be] = 报告给导师\n# en: Your supervisor reads the card, goes pale, thanks you for your care, and writes a letter to the headmistress of Lumenhold.\nopt.right.say.1 [ecfad6] = 导师读了卡片，脸色发白，谢过你的细心，提笔给流明堡的院长写了封信。\n\n@event thesis_proposal\n# en: The Proposal\ntitle [0d98d2] = 开题\n# en: Your thesis proposal is due. Professor Thorne wants one sentence that says what you'll find out. You have eleven sentences and none of them is it.\ntext [6735b0] = 论文开题报告该交了。索恩教授要一句话，说清你要查明什么。你有十一句话，没有一句是。\n# en: A desk covered in drafts, one page with a single sentence circled many times.\nscene [19addc] = 一张铺满草稿的桌子，有一页上只有一句话，被圈了很多遍。\n# en: Rewrite it all night\nopt.left.label [2fa9ed] = 通宵重写\n# en: At dawn you have one sentence. Thorne reads it twice. \"Yes,\" he says. \"That.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [348567] = 黎明时你有了一句话。索恩读了两遍。“对，”他说，“就是这个。”\n# en: Hand in what you have\nopt.right.label [648c9a] = 交上手头的\n# en: Thorne reads all eleven sentences and circles the fourth. \"That one,\" he says. \"Next time, only that one.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [8b0a3c] = 索恩读完十一句话，圈出第四句。“这一句，”他说，“下次，只要这一句。”\n\n@event thesis_lab_night\n# en: Late in the Lab\ntitle [d2c7a0] = 实验室深夜\n# en: The thesis lab, long after everyone else has gone home: and your argument has a hole in it the size of the seventh tower.\ntext [676a0d] = 论文实验室，其他人早都回去了：而你的论证里有一个第七塔那么大的漏洞。\n# en: A thesis laboratory at midnight, one carrel lit, a student surrounded by open books.\nscene [9ec7f2] = 午夜的论文实验室，只有一个隔间亮着灯，一名学生被摊开的书围着。\n# en: Find the hole\nopt.left.label [35b489] = 找到漏洞\n# en: You find it, fill it and find two more. That's progress, apparently.\nopt.left.say.1 [e36cbd] = 你找到了，补上了，又发现了两个。据说这就叫进展。\n# en: Go to bed\nopt.right.label [2fa817] = 去睡觉\n# en: You sleep. In the morning the hole is still there, but you can see the edges of it.\nopt.right.say.1 [0eb578] = 你睡了。早上漏洞还在，但你能看清它的边了。\n\n@event bell_first_ring\n# en: A Bell Under the Water\ntitle [1289ef] = 水下的钟声\n# en: In the middle of the night you hear a bell, low and slow, from the middle of the lake. Nobody else in your dormitory wakes up.\ntext [cfa605] = 半夜里你听见钟声，低沉而缓慢，从湖心传来。宿舍里没有别人醒来。\n# en: A dark lakeshore at night, ripples spreading from the middle of the lake though nothing has fallen in.\nscene [38d9e1] = 夜里漆黑的湖岸，涟漪从湖心扩散开来，尽管什么都没有落进水里。\n# en: Go down to the shore\nopt.left.label [c0c878] = 下到湖边去\n# en: The water is perfectly still. Then, once, a ring spreads out from the centre, as if the lake had been struck from below.\nopt.left.say.1 [baf151] = 湖面纹丝不动。然后，只一次，一圈涟漪从中心荡开，仿佛湖被人从下面敲了一下。\n# en: Pull the blanket over your head\nopt.right.label [352adf] = 把毯子蒙过头\n# en: It rings seven times. You count every one. In the morning you are the only one who remembers.\nopt.right.say.1 [e7bc35] = 钟响了七下。你每一下都数了。到了早上，只有你一个人还记得。\n\n@event bell_maud_rope\n# en: Maud's Rope\ntitle [852d20] = 莫德的绳子\n# en: {keeper} shows you a rope, frayed and black with lake water, that she's kept in her shed for sixty years. \"From the bell,\" she says. \"It came up the night the lake froze. Someone cut it.\"\ntext [090813] = {keeper}给你看一根绳子，磨损了，被湖水浸得发黑，她在棚子里留了六十年。“钟上的，”她说，“湖冻上那晚它浮了上来。有人割断了它。”\n# en: An old woman in a garden shed holding up a frayed, waterlogged bell-rope.\nscene [609899] = 花园棚屋里，一位老妇人举着一根磨损、浸透了水的钟绳。\n# en: Ask who cut it\nopt.left.label [a6108f] = 问是谁割断的\n# en: \"That,\" says {keeper}, \"is the question I've been asking for sixty years.\" She promises to tell you what she knows, when she's ready.\nopt.left.say.1 [4bd3a1] = “这个嘛，”{keeper}说，“我问了六十年了。”她答应等她准备好，就把知道的告诉你。\n# en: Leave her to her memories\nopt.right.label [9f94d2] = 让她独自回忆\n# en: You leave her with the rope in her lap. She doesn't notice you go.\nopt.right.say.1 [10a7b7] = 你留她抱着膝上的绳子。她没注意到你离开。\n\n@event bell_answer\n# en: What Maud Remembers\ntitle [7f91ce] = 莫德记得的事\n# en: {npc} finds you by the boathouse. \"The rope,\" she says. \"Edmund cut it. To stop the bell ringing the thing awake. It went on ringing anyway.\"\ntext [b0e638] = {npc}在船屋边找到你。“那根绳子，”她说，“是埃德蒙割的。为了不让钟声把那东西敲醒。可钟还是一直在响。”\n# en: An old woman and a student sitting by a cottage fire, a waterlogged rope coiled between them.\nscene [6145b3] = 小屋炉火边坐着一位老妇人和一名学生，两人之间盘着一根浸透水的绳子。\n# en: Listen\nopt.ok.label [03cec6] = 听着\n# en: She talks until the light goes. When she's finished, she seems lighter, and you seem to be carrying something.\nopt.ok.say.1 [de8d6c] = 她一直讲到天光散尽。讲完时她似乎轻松了，而你似乎背上了什么。\n\n@event bell_drowned\n# en: The Diving Bell\ntitle [980982] = 潜水钟\n# en: The bell has rung every night for a week. The boathouse has an old diving helmet and a rope. The lake is very dark and very still.\ntext [62e2e6] = 钟已经连着响了一个星期。船屋里有一个旧潜水头盔和一根绳子。湖水非常黑，非常静。\n# en: A student in a borrowed diving helmet standing at the end of a jetty over black water.\nscene [f56404] = 一名学生戴着借来的潜水头盔，站在黑水之上的栈桥尽头。\n# en: Dive\nopt.left.label [ad77f7] = 潜下去\n# en: Far down, the bell lies on its side, and around its rim, letters: a name you don't know. You surface gasping, and you keep the name.\nopt.left.say.1 [f03244] = 在很深的地方，钟侧躺着，钟沿上刻着字：一个你不认识的名字。你喘着气浮出水面，把那个名字记在了心里。\n# en: The cold drives you back up before you see anything. Old Maud is waiting on the jetty with a blanket and a look.\nopt.left.say.2 [32863f] = 你还没看见什么，寒冷就把你逼了回去。老莫德拿着毯子和一个眼神等在栈桥上。\n# en: Wait for the bell to stop\nopt.right.label [d2920e] = 等钟声停下\n# en: You wait for a week, listening from the shore, until you know its rhythm by heart. It doesn't stop. Something in you decides it will have to be you, next time.\nopt.right.say.1 [211b61] = 你在岸边听了一个星期，直到把它的节奏背得滚瓜烂熟。它没有停。你心里有什么东西决定了：下一次，得是你去。\n\n@event bell_silence\n# en: The Bell Falls Silent\ntitle [6d5cb4] = 钟声沉寂\n# en: {clue(bell_name): You say the name from the bell's rim out loud, over the water.|You stand at the shore with Maud's rope in your hands.} The bell rings one last time, and then the lake is just a lake.\ntext [721f49] = {clue(bell_name): 你对着湖面，大声念出钟沿上的那个名字。|你站在岸边，手里握着莫德的绳子。}钟最后响了一次，然后湖就只是一片湖了。\n# en: The Mirror Lake at dawn, perfectly still, a student standing at the water's edge with a lantern.\nscene [82908a] = 黎明的镜湖，平静如镜，一名学生提着灯站在水边。\n# en: Let it rest\nopt.ok.label [657b8b] = 让它安息\n# en: For the first time in sixty years, the lake is quiet. Somewhere in her cottage, you think, Maud sleeps through the night.\nopt.ok.say.1 [e9634e] = 六十年来第一次，湖安静了下来。你想，此刻在小屋里，莫德一定一觉睡到了天亮。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/social.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/social.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event soc_study_session\n# en: Study Session\ntitle [c13d4e] = 一起自习\n# en: {~{npc} drops into the seat across from you with an armful of books. \"Exams are coming for us all. Misery loves company. Study with me?\"|{npc} slides a stack of notes onto your table. \"I've read this chapter four times and it's still in a foreign language. Help?\"|{npc} finds you in the library and sits down without asking. \"If I study alone I'll just reorganise my quills. Keep me honest?\"}\ntext [4f74e2] = {~{npc}抱着一摞书一屁股坐到你对面。“考试要来收拾我们所有人了。同病相怜嘛。一起学？”|{npc}把一叠笔记推到你桌上。“这一章我读了四遍，还是像外语。帮帮我？”|{npc}在图书馆找到你，不问一声就坐下了。“我一个人学只会去整理羽毛笔。你帮我盯着点？”}\n# en: Two students at a candlelit table piled with books, one explaining something with wild hand gestures.\nscene [cb85c7] = 两个学生坐在堆满书的烛光桌前，其中一个正手舞足蹈地讲解着什么。\n# en: You work better alone\nopt.left.label [1f18da] = 你一个人效率更高\n# en: You get a lot done. {~{npc} sits at the next table and sighs loudly every few minutes.|{npc} finds someone else, and their laughter carries across the library all evening.|{npc} leaves you a biscuit on the way out, which somehow makes it worse.}\nopt.left.say.1 [fb167d] = 你完成了很多。{~{npc}坐在隔壁桌，每隔几分钟就大声叹一口气。|{npc}找了别人一起学，他们的笑声整晚都在图书馆里回荡。|{npc}走的时候给你留了一块饼干，不知怎么反而更难受。}\n# en: Pull up a chair\nopt.right.label [d0a229] = 拉过一把椅子\n# en: You get half as much done and remember twice as much of it. {~{npc} explains the Third Law with a biscuit diagram.|You quiz each other until the candle gutters.|{npc} makes up a rude rhyme for every date, and you will never forget a single one.}\nopt.right.say.1 [2f03ab] = 你只完成了一半，却记住了两倍的内容。{~{npc}用饼干摆图讲解了第三定律。|你们互相提问，直到蜡烛快燃尽。|{npc}给每个日期都编了一句粗俗的顺口溜，你一个也忘不掉了。}\n\n@event soc_borrow_money\n# en: A Small Loan\ntitle [cd870f] = 一笔小借款\n# en: {npc} turns out an empty purse. \"I'm fifteen crowns short {~for rent|for the term's ink and parchment|for the wand-mender, and it's my only wand}. I'll pay you back. I swear on my wand.\"\ntext [ddf34a] = {npc}把空空的钱袋翻了过来。“我还差十五克朗{~交房租|买这学期的墨水和羊皮纸|付修魔杖的钱，我就这一根魔杖}。我会还你的。我以我的魔杖发誓。”\n# en: A student holding out an empty purse upside down with a sheepish grin.\nscene [d2da5b] = 一个学生把空钱袋倒过来递给你看，脸上挂着不好意思的笑。\n# en: Sorry, you're tight too\nopt.left.label [d94071] = 抱歉，你也手头紧\n# en: \"No, of course, I understand.\" {They} {are} quiet at dinner.\nopt.left.say.1 [e86862] = “不不，当然，我理解。”晚饭时{they}一直没怎么说话。\n# en: Lend fifteen crowns\nopt.right.label [0326cf] = 借十五克朗\n# en: {npc} hugs you with the empty purse still in {their} hand.\nopt.right.say.1 [995967] = {npc}抱住了你，手里还攥着那只空钱袋。\n# en: {npc} hugs you. You suspect you will never see those fifteen crowns again.\nopt.right.say.2 [506678] = {npc}抱住了你。你怀疑那十五克朗再也回不来了。\n\n@event soc_loan_repaid\n# en: Paid Back\ntitle [ed4502] = 还钱\n# en: {~{npc} finds you after class and presses a little stack of coins into your hand, plus a sticky bun. \"Fifteen crowns. And interest.\" The interest is the bun.|{npc} turns up at breakfast with a small purse and a very straight face. \"Fifteen crowns. I said I would.\"|{npc} slides fifteen crowns across the library table, one coin at a time. The last one has a note under it: \"Thank you. Really.\"}\ntext [d1878a] = {~下课后{npc}找到你，往你手里塞了一小摞硬币，外加一个黏糊糊的小面包。“十五克朗。还有利息。”利息就是那个面包。|{npc}在早餐时出现，拿着一个小钱袋，一脸严肃。“十五克朗。我说过会还的。”|{npc}在图书馆的桌子上把十五克朗一枚一枚推到你面前。最后一枚下面压着一张字条：“谢谢你。真的。”}\n# en: A small stack of coins and a sweet bun left on a desk with a thank-you note.\nscene [a79734] = 桌上放着一小摞硬币和一个甜面包，旁边还有一张感谢字条。\n# en: Count it\nopt.left.label [5fce81] = 数一数\n# en: You count it, because {they}'d be offended if you didn't. Fifteen exactly. {npc} nods, satisfied, as if a debt of honour has been settled.\nopt.left.say.1 [3ec7dd] = 你数了一遍，因为不数的话{they}会觉得被冒犯。正好十五。{npc}满意地点点头，好像一笔关乎荣誉的债终于了结了。\n# en: Pocket it without counting\nopt.right.label [4b0d5d] = 不数就收下\n# en: You put it away uncounted. {npc} looks quietly pleased to be trusted, which was worth more than the crowns.\nopt.right.say.1 [997bc1] = 你没数就收了起来。{npc}看起来暗自高兴被你信任——这比那些克朗值钱多了。\n\n@event soc_become_friends\n# en: Something Like Friendship\ntitle [68b938] = 类似友谊的东西\n# en: {npc} saves you a seat without asking now, and you save {them} one. You trade notes, complaints and the last biscuit without keeping score. Somewhere along the way, this became a friendship.\ntext [013159] = 现在{npc}会不声不响地给你占座，你也给{them}占。你们交换笔记、牢骚和最后一块饼干，从不记账。不知从何时起，这已经成了一段友谊。\n# en: Two students walking across the lawn at sunset, laughing at the same joke, shadows long behind them.\nscene [ea4144] = 夕阳下两个学生穿过草坪，为同一个笑话大笑，身后拖着长长的影子。\n# en: Keep a little distance\nopt.left.label [37e0f8] = 保持一点距离\n# en: You stay friendly. Just not too close. Some walls are load-bearing.\nopt.left.say.1 [4470d5] = 你们保持友好。只是不太亲近。有些墙是承重墙。\n# en: Call it what it is\nopt.right.label [9f3cf3] = 挑明了说\n# en: \"So we're friends now,\" {npc} says. \"Officially.\" You shake on it, solemnly, then ruin it by laughing.\nopt.right.say.1 [eee457] = “那我们现在是朋友了，”{npc}说，“正式的。”你们郑重地握手，然后笑场毁掉了这份庄重。\n\n@event soc_become_close\n# en: The Kind of Friend You Call at 3am\ntitle [e891f8] = 凌晨三点可以去敲门的那种朋友\n# en: It's 3am. You can't sleep, and you've walked to {npc}'s door before you've even decided to. {They} open{s} it before you knock.\ntext [216ec9] = 凌晨三点。你睡不着，还没想好就已经走到了{npc}的门前。你还没敲门，{they}就把门打开了。\n# en: Two friends sitting on a dormitory floor at 3am wrapped in one blanket, empty mugs between them.\nscene [9f5741] = 凌晨三点，两个朋友裹着同一条毯子坐在宿舍地板上，中间摆着空杯子。\n# en: Apologise and go back to bed\nopt.left.label [f9def6] = 道个歉，回去睡觉\n# en: \"Don't be daft,\" {npc} says, but lets you go. In the morning there's tea outside your door.\nopt.left.say.1 [5078b4] = “别傻了。”{npc}说，但还是让你走了。早上，你门外放着一杯茶。\n# en: Talk until sunrise\nopt.right.label [f5afa4] = 聊到日出\n# en: You tell {them} things you haven't told anyone. {They} tell you things back. The sun comes up and neither of you notices.\nopt.right.say.1 [c821d1] = 你告诉了{them}从没告诉过任何人的事。{they}也告诉了你{their}心事。太阳升起来了，你们谁都没注意到。\n\n@event soc_secret\n# en: A Secret\ntitle [880a88] = 一个秘密\n# en: {~{npc} pulls you under the stairs. \"I need to tell someone or I'll burst. Swear you won't tell anyone. Not even the portraits.\"|{npc} closes the dormitory door, checks it, and checks it again. \"I have to tell someone. Swear you'll keep it.\"|{npc} walks you to the far end of the lake path, where nobody goes. \"Can you keep something? Really keep it?\"}\ntext [620902] = {~{npc}把你拉到楼梯底下。“我得告诉一个人，不然我会憋炸的。发誓你谁也不说。连肖像画都不说。”|{npc}关上宿舍门，检查了一遍，又检查了一遍。“我得告诉一个人。发誓你会保密。”|{npc}把你带到湖边小路没人去的尽头。“你能守住一件事吗？真的守住？”}\n# en: Two students huddled under a staircase, one whispering into the other's ear, a finger to their lips.\nscene [e33a93] = 两个学生挤在楼梯下，一个凑在另一个耳边低语，手指竖在唇边。\n# en: \"I'd rather not carry it.\"\nopt.left.label [a4f5da] = “我宁愿不背这个包袱。”\n# en: {npc} looks hurt, then relieved. \"Maybe that's better.\" You'll never know.\nopt.left.say.1 [8b5a46] = {npc}先是一脸受伤，然后松了口气。“也许这样更好。”你永远不会知道了。\n# en: \"I swear.\"\nopt.right.label [381a0e] = “我发誓。”\n# en: {~It's not a big secret. It's the kind that's only big to the person holding it. You hold it with {them}.|It's about a letter from home, and it's heavier than {they} let on. You carry half of it now.|It's ridiculous, and {they} know it's ridiculous, and {they} needed someone else to know anyway. You do.}\nopt.right.say.1 [5f42d1] = {~不是什么大秘密。是那种只对守着它的人来说很大的秘密。你陪{them}一起守着。|是关于一封家书的事，比{they}表现出来的沉重得多。现在你替{them}分担了一半。|很荒唐，{they}自己也知道很荒唐，但{they}还是需要另一个人知道。现在你知道了。}\n\n@event soc_argument\n# en: Words Exchanged\ntitle [260001] = 口角\n# en: It starts with something small — {~a borrowed book, a joke that landed wrong|whose turn it was to fetch the tea|a seat someone saved and someone else took} — and suddenly you and {npc} are arguing {~in the corridor|on the stairs|in the middle of the refectory}, louder than either of you intended.\ntext [838628] = 起因是件小事——{~一本借走的书，一个说错时机的玩笑|该谁去打茶|有人占的座位被别人坐了}——突然间你和{npc}就{~在走廊里|在楼梯上|在食堂正中间}吵了起来，嗓门比你们俩想的都大。\n# en: Two students facing off in an empty corridor, one with arms crossed, the other mid-gesture, both flushed.\nscene [935b50] = 两个学生在空荡的走廊里对峙，一个抱着双臂，另一个正比划着，两人都涨红了脸。\n# en: Apologise first\nopt.left.label [9f3966] = 先道歉\n# en: {~\"I'm sorry.\" {npc} deflates. \"...Me too.\" It's over. It feels better than winning would have.|You say sorry first. {npc} looks almost disappointed not to get to say it. You share a bun about it later.|\"I'm sorry.\" {npc} blinks, then laughs. \"We were both being absurd.\" You were.}\nopt.left.say.1 [d0711f] = {~“对不起。”{npc}一下子泄了气。“……我也是。”结束了。这比吵赢了感觉更好。|你先道了歉。{npc}看上去几乎有点失望，没轮到自己说。后来你们为这事分吃了一个面包。|“对不起。”{npc}眨眨眼，然后笑了。“我们俩都太荒唐了。”确实。}\n# en: Stand your ground\nopt.right.label [3881f5] = 坚持立场\n# en: {npc} storms off. You were right. It doesn't feel as good as you'd hoped.\nopt.right.say.1 [23d1ff] = {npc}气冲冲地走了。你是对的。可感觉并没有你希望的那么好。\n# en: You argue it out until you're both laughing at how ridiculous it got. Somehow you're closer after.\nopt.right.say.2 [16b2d4] = 你们一直吵到两个人都笑了出来，笑这事有多荒唐。不知怎么的，吵完反而更亲近了。\n\n@event soc_village_trip\n# en: A Day in the Village\ntitle [1afb52] = 镇上一日\n# en: \"Barge to the village? Tea at Mabel's, a wander round the bookshops, back before curfew?\" {npc} is already wearing {their} coat.\ntext [07e032] = “坐驳船去镇上吗？去梅布尔茶馆喝茶，逛逛书店，宵禁前回来？”{npc}已经穿好外套了。\n# en: A cobbled village street with crooked shopfronts, two students sharing a paper cone of roasted nuts.\nscene [743ea4] = 鹅卵石铺就的小镇街道，歪歪扭扭的店面，两个学生分享着一纸筒烤坚果。\n# en: Not this week\nopt.left.label [0fe3ce] = 这周不行\n# en: {~{They} bring{s} you back a cinnamon bun anyway.|{They} bring{s} you back a postcard of the village, which is three streets long.|{They} come{s} back with a story about a goat and tell{s} it badly.}\nopt.left.say.1 [b6d43c] = {~{they}还是给你带回来一个肉桂卷。|{they}给你带回来一张小镇明信片，那小镇总共只有三条街。|{they}回来时带着一个关于山羊的故事，讲得很烂。}\n# en: Grab your coat\nopt.right.label [b14325] = 抓起外套\n# en: {~Tea, buns, a bookshop cat that sits on {npc}'s feet. You almost miss the last barge, laughing.|Mabel's, then the market, then a long argument about which bun is best. You get the last barge home with sticky fingers.|Rain, so you spend the whole afternoon in the bookshop. {npc} buys a book about knots and explains every one on the barge home.}\nopt.right.say.1 [64f247] = {~喝茶，吃面包，书店的猫趴在{npc}脚上。你们笑着差点错过最后一班驳船。|先去梅布尔茶馆，再逛集市，然后为哪种面包最好吵了半天。你们带着黏糊糊的手指赶上了最后一班驳船。|下雨了，你们一下午都待在书店里。{npc}买了一本讲绳结的书，回程的驳船上把每个结都讲了一遍。}\n\n@event soc_friend_sick\n# en: Sick Friend\ntitle [ac406d] = 生病的朋友\n# en: {npc} has Lake Fever and is miserable in the infirmary. Sister Amaranth says visitors are allowed, but she won't be responsible.\ntext [0a1413] = {npc}得了湖热病，在医务室里难受得要命。阿玛兰斯修女说可以探视，但出了事她不负责。\n# en: A student tucked into an infirmary bed under a mountain of blankets, a friend sitting beside them with a bowl of soup.\nscene [43529f] = 一个学生被埋在医务室病床的一堆毯子底下，一位朋友端着一碗汤坐在床边。\n# en: Send a card\nopt.left.label [72cab2] = 送张卡片\n# en: You send a card with a drawing of Sister Amaranth as a dragon. {npc} keeps it on {their} bedside table.\nopt.left.say.1 [eae564] = 你送了一张卡片，上面画着变成了龙的阿玛兰斯修女。{npc}把它摆在床头柜上。\n# en: Sit with them every evening\nopt.right.label [09fda2] = 每晚都去陪着\n# en: You keep {them} company for a week. Then you're in the next bed. You both find this hilarious.\nopt.right.say.1 [8c2cc5] = 你陪了{them}一个星期。然后你住进了隔壁床。你们俩都觉得这太好笑了。\n# en: {~You read aloud, badly, doing voices. {npc} recovers and claims it was the voices.|You teach {them} every card game you know and lose all of them. {npc} recovers, insufferably.|You bring soup from the kitchens every evening and tell {them} all the gossip. {npc} recovers mostly out of curiosity.}\nopt.right.say.2 [0b98ac] = {~你给{them}念书，念得很烂，还学各种腔调。{npc}康复后坚称是那些腔调治好了{them}。|你把会的纸牌游戏全教给了{them}，结果每一局都输了。{npc}康复了，得意得叫人受不了。|你每晚从厨房端汤过来，把所有八卦都讲给{them}听。{npc}康复，主要是出于好奇。}\n\n@event soc_gift_given\n# en: A Small Gift\ntitle [9dafb3] = 一件小礼物\n# en: There's a small parcel on your pillow. A note in {npc}'s handwriting: \"Saw this and thought of you. Don't make it weird.\"\ntext [1d6ca6] = 你的枕头上放着一个小包裹。附着一张{npc}字迹的字条：“看见这个就想到了你。别想太多。”\n# en: A small wrapped parcel tied with ribbon left on a dormitory pillow, a pressed flower tucked into the bow.\nscene [5a37d3] = 宿舍枕头上一个系着丝带的小包裹，蝴蝶结里夹着一朵压花。\n# en: Thank {them} later\nopt.left.label [a0fa69] = 回头再谢{them}\n# en: {~It's a tiny brass compass that always points to the nearest teapot. You use it constantly.|It's a bookmark that hums when you stop reading. Annoying. You love it.|It's a pair of gloves that stay warm all winter. You wear them until they're threadbare.}\nopt.left.say.1 [e85db0] = {~是一个小小的黄铜罗盘，永远指向最近的茶壶。你天天都在用。|是一枚书签，你一停下不读它就哼哼。烦人。你爱死它了。|是一副整个冬天都暖和的手套。你一直戴到磨破。}\n# en: Get {them} something back (8 gold)\nopt.right.label [42a27b] = 回赠一份礼物（8 金币）\n# en: You find {them} {~a book {they}'d wanted for months|a scarf in exactly {their} colour|a sketchbook with good paper}. {npc}'s face when {they} open{s} it is worth twice the price.\nopt.right.say.1 [f29a77] = 你给{them}找到了{~一本{they}想要了好几个月的书|一条正是{their}颜色的围巾|一本纸张很好的速写本}。{npc}打开时的表情，值两倍的价钱。\n\n@event soc_drift\n# en: Drifting Apart\ntitle [73c51a] = 渐行渐远\n# en: You realise you haven't really talked to {npc} in weeks. You see {them} {~across the courtyard|at the far end of the refectory|on the library steps}, laughing with people you don't know.\ntext [27eced] = 你发现自己已经好几周没好好跟{npc}说过话了。你看见{them}{~在庭院那头|在食堂另一端|在图书馆台阶上}，和一群你不认识的人说笑。\n# en: A student glancing back across a busy courtyard at another who hasn't seen them, the space between them full of strangers.\nscene [e8800d] = 一个学生回头望向熙攘庭院另一端没看见自己的人，两人之间隔满了陌生人。\n# en: Let it be\nopt.left.label [71cc4c] = 顺其自然\n# en: People drift. It's nobody's fault. You still smile when you pass in the halls.\nopt.left.say.1 [8a48fa] = 人总会渐行渐远。不是谁的错。在走廊里擦肩而过时，你们仍会微笑。\n# en: Go over and say hello\nopt.right.label [cdae69] = 走过去打个招呼\n# en: {~\"There you are!\" {npc} says, as if you'd been the one missing. Maybe you had.|{npc} introduces you to everyone as \"the one I told you about\". You wonder what {they} told them. It seems to have been good.|{npc} shifts over to make room on the bench without a word, as if your seat had been waiting.}\nopt.right.say.1 [edea7c] = {~“你在这儿呀！”{npc}说，好像失踪的人是你。也许确实是。|{npc}向所有人介绍你：“就是我跟你们说过的那个。”你很好奇{they}说了什么。听起来是好话。|{npc}一言不发地挪了挪，在长凳上给你腾出位置，好像你的座位一直在等你。}\n# en: You go over. It is friendly, and short, and you both say \"we should catch up\" in a way that means you won't.\nopt.right.say.2 [46fd68] = 你走过去。气氛很友好，也很短，你们都说了句“改天聚聚”，那语气意味着不会有改天。\n\n@event soc_rumour\n# en: Rumours About {npc}\ntitle [e3d846] = 关于{npc}的流言\n# en: A nasty rumour about {npc} is going round {~the common room|the refectory|the library steps}: {~that {they} cheated on an exam|that {they} {are} leaving at the end of term in disgrace|that {they} said something cruel about a friend}. It's not true. You know it's not true. People are laughing.\ntext [fdfb88] = {~公共休息室里|食堂里|图书馆台阶上}流传着一条关于{npc}的恶毒流言：{~说{they}考试作弊|说{they}学期末要灰溜溜地退学|说{they}对朋友说了很刻薄的话}。不是真的。你知道不是真的。大家在笑。\n# en: A cluster of students whispering behind their hands in a corridor, glancing toward someone just out of view.\nscene [78a3d1] = 一群学生在走廊里捂着嘴窃窃私语，朝视线外的某个人瞟去。\n# en: Let it blow over\nopt.left.label [962dcd] = 等它自己平息\n# en: It blows over. {npc} never finds out you heard it. You find out you don't like yourself for it, a little.\nopt.left.say.1 [95d2f0] = 它平息了。{npc}永远不会知道你听到过。可你发现，你有点不喜欢这样的自己。\n# en: Shut it down\nopt.right.label [e3e364] = 当场制止\n# en: You make the rumour-monger look so foolish that the story dies on the spot. {npc} hears about it later and just squeezes your arm.\nopt.right.say.1 [78c724] = 你让造谣的人出尽洋相，流言当场就死了。{npc}后来听说了，只是轻轻捏了捏你的胳膊。\n# en: You make a scene and now there are rumours about you too. {npc} thinks you're an idiot. A loyal idiot.\nopt.right.say.2 [ea7565] = 你大闹了一场，现在也有关于你的流言了。{npc}觉得你是个傻瓜。一个忠诚的傻瓜。\n\n@event soc_homework_help\n# en: Desperate Homework\ntitle [b925b8] = 作业告急\n# en: {npc} waves a half-finished {~essay|lab report|star chart} at you. \"It's due in an hour and I've written the title twice. Help.\"\ntext [eb5473] = {npc}朝你挥着一份写了一半的{~论文|实验报告|星图}。“一小时后就要交了，我只把标题写了两遍。救命。”\n# en: A student with ink on their face clutching a half-finished essay, another student leaning over to read it.\nscene [d955d2] = 一个脸上沾着墨水的学生攥着一篇半成品论文，另一个学生凑过来看。\n# en: Explain the concept\nopt.left.label [d8e8f3] = 讲解知识点\n# en: You explain it so clearly that {npc} finishes the essay with time to spare. {They} insist{s} you're a genius.\nopt.left.say.1 [cf80f6] = 你讲得清清楚楚，{npc}提前写完了论文。{they}坚称你是个天才。\n# en: You explain it so confusingly that you both end up more lost. You hand it in together, two confused people against the world.\nopt.left.say.2 [a0a001] = 你讲得一团乱麻，两个人都更糊涂了。你们一起交了上去——两个糊涂人对抗全世界。\n# en: \"Copy mine.\"\nopt.right.label [b2d7d3] = “抄我的吧。”\n# en: The professor notices two identical pieces of work. You both get detention, a lecture, and a note on your record that every one of your professors reads.\nopt.right.say.1 [d3d733] = 教授发现了两份一模一样的作业。你们俩都被关了禁闭，挨了一顿训，档案上还记了一笔，你的每位教授都会看到。\n# en: {npc} hands it in and gets a better mark than you did. Unbelievable.\nopt.right.say.2 [c22ac9] = {npc}交了上去，分数比你还高。难以置信。\n\n@event soc_faculty_advice\n# en: A Word of Advice\ntitle [594b8b] = 一句忠告\n# en: {npc} catches you after a lecture. \"You've been working hard. Or hardly working. I can never tell with your year. Some advice, if you'll take it?\"\ntext [890d50] = 讲座结束后{npc}叫住了你。“你一直很用功。或者说一直很用‘空’。你们这一届我总是分不清。给你点建议，听不听？”\n# en: A professor walking with a student along a covered walkway, pointing at something in an open book.\nscene [41385c] = 一位教授和一名学生沿着有顶棚的步道走着，指着一本摊开的书里的某处。\n# en: Politely decline\nopt.left.label [8a601d] = 礼貌地拒绝\n# en: \"Suit yourself.\" {They} walk{s} off. Faculty have long memories.\nopt.left.say.1 [19801b] = “随你。”{they}走开了。教职员的记性可都很好。\n# en: Listen carefully\nopt.right.label [1b6b4d] = 认真听\n# en: It's good advice about how to study, {~annoyingly good|delivered at great length|illustrated with a story from {their} own disastrous first year}. Every piece of work you hand in that month is better for it.\nopt.right.say.1 [3db8c9] = 是关于怎么学习的好建议，{~好得让人恼火|讲得极其冗长|还配了一个{their}自己一年级时惨痛经历的故事}。那个月你交的每一份作业都因此进步了。\n\n@event soc_spark\n# en: A Spark\ntitle [21e629] = 火花\n# en: You and {npc} reach for {&the same quill|the last biscuit on the plate|the same library book} at the same moment. Your hands touch. Neither of you lets go immediately. {&It's a perfectly ordinary quill.|It's a perfectly ordinary biscuit.|It's a perfectly ordinary book.}\ntext [8c5ce5] = 你和{npc}同时伸手去拿{&同一支羽毛笔|盘子里最后一块饼干|同一本图书馆的书}。你们的手碰到了。谁也没有马上松开。{&那只是一支再普通不过的羽毛笔。|那只是一块再普通不过的饼干。|那只是一本再普通不过的书。}\n# en: Two hands brushing as they reach for the same quill, both students freezing for a heartbeat.\nscene [d87696] = 两只手在伸向同一支羽毛笔时轻轻相触，两个学生都愣了一拍。\n# en: Laugh it off\nopt.left.label [29c02a] = 一笑了之\n# en: \"All yours,\" you say. {npc} laughs. The moment passes. Mostly.\nopt.left.say.1 [134287] = “你用吧。”你说。{npc}笑了。那一刻过去了。大体上。\n# en: Hold on a moment longer\nopt.right.label [04d88b] = 多握一会儿\n# en: {npc} looks up. Something in {their} face shifts. Then {they} let{s} go, a little pink, and {&the quill rolls under the desk|the biscuit breaks neatly in half|the book falls open on the floor between you}.\nopt.right.say.1 [c7d64d] = {npc}抬起头。{their}脸上有什么东西变了。然后{they}松开了手，脸有点红，{&羽毛笔滚到了桌子底下|饼干整整齐齐地断成了两半|书摊开掉在你们中间的地上}。\n# en: {npc} pulls back as if burned, and the rest of the {&lesson|meal|afternoon} is very, very awkward.\nopt.right.say.2 [671934] = {npc}像被烫到一样缩回了手，那{&节课|顿饭|个下午}剩下的时间尴尬得要命。\n\n@event soc_date\n# en: An Invitation\ntitle [aba1c1] = 一次邀约\n# en: You could ask {npc} to {&the lantern show in the village|the travelling players at the Newt|the music on the quay} this weekend. As friends. Or, you know. Not as friends.\ntext [c7f340] = 你可以邀请{npc}这个周末去{&镇上看灯笼表演|镀金蝾螈酒馆看巡演剧团|码头听音乐}。以朋友的身份。或者，你懂的。不只是朋友。\n# en: A student nervously holding out a hand-drawn ticket to the village's floating lantern show.\nscene [57c131] = 一个学生紧张地递出一张手绘的小镇浮灯表演门票。\n# en: Ask as friends\nopt.left.label [bd7c43] = 以朋友身份邀请\n# en: You pay for both tickets and the hot cider. It's a lovely evening. You're both very careful about elbows.\nopt.left.say.1 [0135a9] = 两张票和热苹果酒都是你付的。那是个美好的夜晚。你们俩都非常小心地不碰到对方的胳膊肘。\n# en: Ask as more than friends\nopt.right.label [e73a1b] = 以不只是朋友的身份邀请\n# en: {npc} says yes before you finish the sentence. You hold hands through the whole {&show and miss every lantern|play and miss every line|evening and miss every song}.\nopt.right.say.1 [fb9a21] = 你话还没说完{npc}就答应了。{&整场表演你们都牵着手，一盏灯笼也没看见|整场戏你们都牵着手，一句台词也没听见|一整晚你们都牵着手，一首歌也没听进去}。\n# en: {npc} hesitates, then says {they}'d rather go as friends. It's a nice evening. It's a long evening.\nopt.right.say.2 [097e94] = {npc}犹豫了一下，说还是以朋友的身份去吧。那是个不错的夜晚。也是个漫长的夜晚。\n# en: {npc} says yes before you finish the sentence. You hold hands through the whole {&show and miss every lantern|play and miss every line|evening and miss every song}.\nopt.right.say.3 [fb9a21] = 你话还没说完{npc}就答应了。{&整场表演你们都牵着手，一盏灯笼也没看见|整场戏你们都牵着手，一句台词也没听见|一整晚你们都牵着手，一首歌也没听进去}。\n# en: {npc} hesitates, then says {they}'d rather go as friends. It's a nice evening. It's a long evening.\nopt.right.say.4 [097e94] = {npc}犹豫了一下，说还是以朋友的身份去吧。那是个不错的夜晚。也是个漫长的夜晚。\n\n@event soc_confess_generic\n# en: Feelings, Spoken\ntitle [0fdaae] = 说出口的心意\n# en: On the bridge between the towers, the wind snatching at your robes, you realise you can't keep it in anymore. {npc} is looking at you like {they} know what you're about to say.\ntext [0fb936] = 在塔楼之间的桥上，风扯着你的长袍，你意识到自己再也藏不住了。{npc}看着你，好像已经知道你要说什么。\n# en: Two students standing very close on a windswept bridge between towers, one with a hand over their heart.\nscene [80f257] = 两个学生紧挨着站在塔楼之间风大的桥上，其中一人手按在心口。\n# en: Say nothing\nopt.left.label [d1151d] = 什么也不说\n# en: The moment passes. You walk back together, a careful inch apart.\nopt.left.say.1 [8f2183] = 那一刻过去了。你们一起往回走，小心地隔着一寸距离。\n# en: Tell {them}\nopt.right.label [3c60a0] = 告诉{them}\n# en: \"Finally,\" {npc} says, and kisses you, and the wind steals your breath anyway.\nopt.right.say.1 [bdde21] = “终于说了。”{npc}说着吻了你，而风依旧夺走了你的呼吸。\n# en: {npc} takes a long breath. \"I care about you,\" {they} say{s}. \"Just not like that. I'm sorry.\" The wind is very cold.\nopt.right.say.2 [fe43d9] = {npc}深吸了一口气。“我在乎你，”{they}说，“只是不是那种在乎。对不起。”风很冷。\n\n@event soc_partner_date\n# en: Date Night\ntitle [b6d8e3] = 约会之夜\n# en: {npc} has booked the window table at Mabel's. Candles. {season == \"winter\": Snow outside.|The lake going gold outside.} \"We're splitting the bill,\" {they} say{s}. \"Don't argue.\"\ntext [ae9174] = {npc}订了梅布尔茶馆靠窗的位子。蜡烛。{season == \"winter\": 窗外飘着雪。|窗外的湖正染成金色。}“这顿我们平摊，”{they}说，“别跟我争。”\n# en: Two people at a tiny candlelit table in a window alcove of a village tea shop, snow falling outside.\nscene [7b9157] = 小镇茶馆窗边凹室里一张小小的烛光桌，两个人对坐，窗外飘着雪。\n# en: Talk about the future\nopt.left.label [4d8f04] = 聊聊未来\n# en: {&You talk about where you'll be in ten years. In every version, you're both there.|You talk about after graduation, carefully, then not carefully at all.|You make a list of places you'll go together. It is already too long.}\nopt.left.say.1 [c9de6d] = {&你们聊起十年后会在哪里。每一个版本里，你们俩都在。|你们聊起毕业以后的事，起初小心翼翼，后来一点也不小心了。|你们列了一张以后要一起去的地方的清单。已经太长了。}\n# en: Talk about absolutely nothing\nopt.right.label [75d2d9] = 聊些完全没用的事\n# en: You argue for an hour about {&whether soup is a drink|whether owls are smart or just look it|which professor would win in a duel}, and {npc} pays the bill while you're making your closing argument. {&It is the best date of your life.|It might be better than the last one.|You lose the argument and don't mind.}\nopt.right.say.1 [cc5078] = 你们争论了一个小时{&汤到底算不算饮料|猫头鹰是真聪明还是只是看起来聪明|哪位教授决斗会赢}，你还在做总结陈词时，{npc}已经把账结了。{&这是你这辈子最棒的一次约会。|也许比上一次还要好。|你辩输了，却一点也不在意。}\n\n@event soc_partner_neglect\n# en: \"You're Always Busy\"\ntitle [46e97c] = “你总是很忙”\n# en: {npc} is waiting outside your door. \"I feel like I only see you in corridors,\" {they} say{s}. \"Do you even want this?\"\ntext [0e3f61] = {npc}等在你门外。“我觉得我只能在走廊里见到你，”{they}说，“你到底还想不想要这段感情？”\n# en: A person waiting alone at a table set for two, a candle burned low, the other chair empty.\nscene [b603a7] = 一个人独自坐在摆好两人份餐具的桌前，蜡烛快燃尽了，另一把椅子空着。\n# en: \"I'm sorry. I'll make time.\"\nopt.left.label [b59fcc] = “对不起。我会挤出时间的。”\n# en: You skip a study session for a long walk by the lake. It's the right call.\nopt.left.say.1 [8a05e5] = 你翘掉一次自习，陪{them}在湖边走了很久。这个决定是对的。\n# en: \"You know how busy I am.\"\nopt.right.label [a60981] = “你知道我有多忙。”\n# en: {npc} nods slowly. Something between you cools a degree.\nopt.right.say.1 [2bc62a] = {npc}缓缓点了点头。你们之间的什么东西，冷了一度。\n\n@event soc_breakup\n# en: The End of Something\ntitle [179272] = 某件事的结束\n# en: {npc} sits down at the far end of the bench. \"I think we both know,\" {they} say{s} quietly. \"This isn't working anymore.\"\ntext [c059c0] = {npc}在长椅的另一头坐下。“我想我们都心知肚明，”{they}轻声说，“这已经行不通了。”\n# en: Two people sitting on opposite ends of a bench in a grey garden, not looking at each other, rain beginning.\nscene [4b79cd] = 灰蒙蒙的花园里，两个人坐在长椅两端，谁也不看谁，开始下雨了。\n# en: Fight for it\nopt.left.label [7ed077] = 争取一下\n# en: You talk for hours. It's raw and honest and it hurts. And at the end, {npc} takes your hand again.\nopt.left.say.1 [207908] = 你们谈了几个小时。赤裸、坦诚，而且很痛。最后，{npc}又握住了你的手。\n# en: You try. It only makes it harder. In the end you're both crying, and it's over anyway.\nopt.left.say.2 [2de21e] = 你努力了。却只让一切更难。最后你们都哭了，而它终究还是结束了。\n# en: Let {them} go\nopt.right.label [185a66] = 放{them}走\n# en: You agree. It's terrible and gentle and right. {npc} kisses your forehead once before {they} leave{s}.\nopt.right.say.1 [e0897b] = 你同意了。这很糟糕，很温柔，也很对。{npc}离开前在你额头上吻了一下。\n\n@event soc_two_timing\n# en: Found Out\ntitle [f1af67] = 东窗事发\n# en: {npc} is holding two letters. One is to {them}. The other isn't. \"I found it in your bag,\" {they} say{s}, very quietly. Half the corridor has stopped to watch.\ntext [b63ccd] = {npc}手里拿着两封信。一封是写给{them}的。另一封不是。“我在你包里找到的，”{they}说，声音很轻。半条走廊的人都停下来看着。\n# en: A person holding up two crumpled love letters in a crowded corridor, the crowd parting around them.\nscene [28e97e] = 拥挤的走廊里，一个人举着两封揉皱的情书，人群在他们周围分开。\n# en: Beg forgiveness\nopt.left.label [b42d94] = 乞求原谅\n# en: {npc} doesn't forgive you. But {they} don't leave. Not yet. You're on borrowed time.\nopt.left.say.1 [27d6d1] = {npc}没有原谅你。但{they}也没有离开。暂时没有。你在借来的时间里过日子。\n# en: {npc} tears both letters in half and walks away. Everyone knows by dinner.\nopt.left.say.2 [3f568e] = {npc}把两封信都撕成两半，转身离开。晚饭时所有人都知道了。\n# en: Say nothing\nopt.right.label [d1151d] = 一言不发\n# en: Your silence is an answer. {npc} drops the letters at your feet and leaves. The story is all over the academy.\nopt.right.say.1 [855b96] = 你的沉默就是答案。{npc}把信扔在你脚边，走了。这件事传遍了整个学院。\n\n@event soc_ex_encounter\n# en: Awkward\ntitle [8ea9c0] = 尴尬\n# en: {~You turn into the library aisle and there's {npc}. Your ex. Holding the same book you came for.|The only free seat in the refectory is opposite {npc}. Your ex. {They} look{s} up at the same moment you do.|You open the door to the owlery and there's {npc}. Your ex. Holding a letter, halfway to an owl.} Neither of you moves.\ntext [d14bf3] = {~你拐进图书馆的一条过道，{npc}就在那儿。你的前任。手里拿着你正要找的那本书。|食堂里唯一的空位在{npc}对面。你的前任。{they}和你同时抬起了头。|你推开猫头鹰塔的门，{npc}就在那儿。你的前任。手里拿着一封信，正要交给猫头鹰。}你们谁也没动。\n# en: Two people coming face to face in a narrow library aisle, both holding books, neither moving.\nscene [0292af] = 两个人在图书馆狭窄的过道里迎面相遇，手里都拿着书，谁也没动。\n# en: Turn around and leave\nopt.left.label [a746c5] = 转身离开\n# en: You find a different book in a different aisle and read the same paragraph six times.\nopt.left.say.1 [a75b45] = 你在另一条过道找了另一本书，把同一段读了六遍。\n# en: Say hello like a person\nopt.right.label [06a265] = 像个正常人一样打招呼\n# en: It's awkward for one minute, then it isn't. You end up laughing about the worst of the dates you went on. Friends, maybe. Really.\nopt.right.say.1 [38140b] = 尴尬了一分钟，然后就不尴尬了。你们最后笑着聊起约会时最糟的那一次。也许能做朋友。真的。\n# en: \"Hello.\" \"Hello.\" You both stand there. Then you both leave. It's a start.\nopt.right.say.2 [f4910b] = “你好。”“你好。”你们俩站在那儿。然后各自离开。算是个开始。\n\n@event soc_rival_taunt\n# en: A Public Taunt\ntitle [0b1cd5] = 当众嘲讽\n# en: In the middle of a crowded {~meal|common room|library queue}, {npc} loudly recounts {~your most embarrassing moment of the term|the time you walked into a door that was a painting|your worst spell in front of the whole class}. People laugh. {They} look{s} very pleased.\ntext [86e910] = 在挤满人的{~饭桌上|公共休息室里|图书馆排队时}，{npc}大声讲起{~你这学期最丢人的一幕|你一头撞上一扇其实是画的门那次|你当着全班的面施出的最烂的咒语}。大家都笑了。{they}看起来非常得意。\n# en: A smirking student surrounded by a small audience, pointing at someone across a crowded dining hall.\nscene [fc4e76] = 一个得意洋洋的学生被一小群观众围着，隔着拥挤的餐厅指向某人。\n# en: Ignore {them}\nopt.left.label [c17487] = 不理{them}\n# en: The laughter dies down. {~You carry on with as much dignity as you can find.|You wait it out, and stare at a fixed point on the wall.|Someone nearby passes you a biscuit in silent solidarity.}\nopt.left.say.1 [b738e5] = 笑声渐渐平息。{~你尽可能体面地继续做自己的事。|你熬过去，眼睛一直盯着墙上的某一点。|旁边有人默默递给你一块饼干，以示声援。}\n# en: Fire back\nopt.right.label [edc214] = 反唇相讥\n# en: {~Your comeback is so good that someone at the far table applauds.|Your comeback lands so well that people repeat it for a week.|Your comeback is quiet, exact and devastating.} {npc} goes scarlet.\nopt.right.say.1 [6c86a5] = {~你的回击太漂亮了，远处那桌有人鼓起掌来。|你的回击一语中的，大家足足学了一个星期。|你的回击轻声、精准、致命。}{npc}满脸通红。\n# en: Your comeback comes out as a squeak. The laughter doubles.\nopt.right.say.2 [992f30] = 你的回击变成了一声尖叫。笑声翻了一倍。\n\n@event soc_make_peace\n# en: Burying the Hatchet\ntitle [91164f] = 握手言和\n# en: {npc} finds you after class, scuffing {their} shoes. \"This whole rivalry thing. It's getting tiring. Truce?\"\ntext [d5620e] = 下课后{npc}找到你，鞋尖蹭着地面。“这整个当对手的事儿。有点累了。休战？”\n# en: Two former rivals shaking hands on a dueling green at sunset, wands tucked away.\nscene [f9c463] = 夕阳下的决斗草坪，两个昔日对手收起魔杖握手。\n# en: Keep the edge\nopt.left.label [a15600] = 保持锋芒\n# en: \"Thought so,\" {npc} says, half-smiling. The game goes on.\nopt.left.say.1 [4e5e1c] = “我就知道。”{npc}半笑着说。较量继续。\n# en: Shake on it\nopt.right.label [982713] = 握手为定\n# en: You shake. It's strange — you know {them} better than most of your friends. Maybe that's the point.\nopt.right.say.1 [e1ff36] = 你们握了手。说来奇怪——你对{them}的了解，比对大多数朋友都深。也许这正是意义所在。\n\n@event soc_rival_bet\n# en: The Wager\ntitle [5a804e] = 赌约\n# en: \"Eight crowns says I {~outscore you in the next practical|light more candles in one breath than you|hold a ward longer than you},\" {npc} says, slapping coins on the table. A crowd forms.\ntext [ff384f] = “八克朗，赌我{~下次实践课分数比你高|一口气点的蜡烛比你多|护界撑得比你久}。”{npc}把硬币拍在桌上。一群人围了过来。\n# en: Two students slapping coins onto a table between them, a crowd gathering, a chalked tally on the wall.\nscene [8dd838] = 两个学生把硬币拍在中间的桌上，人群聚拢，墙上用粉笔记着比分。\n# en: Decline the bet\nopt.left.label [99b979] = 不赌\n# en: \"Thought so.\" {npc} pockets the coins. You decide not to care. You care.\nopt.left.say.1 [556d0b] = “我就知道。”{npc}收起了硬币。你决定不在乎。你很在乎。\n# en: Take the bet\nopt.right.label [8a4d52] = 接下赌约\n# en: You win by a hair. {npc} pays up with a theatrical bow. \"Next time.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [e1aac3] = 你以毫厘之差赢了。{npc}夸张地鞠了一躬，付了钱。“下次再说。”\n# en: You lose by a hair. \"Next time,\" you say. {npc} grins. \"Next time.\"\nopt.right.say.2 [8023a9] = 你以毫厘之差输了。“下次再说。”你说。{npc}咧嘴一笑。“下次再说。”\n\n@event spark_cooldown\n# en: Something Between You\ntitle [e1d3d8] = 你们之间的什么\n# en: Something has shifted between you and {npc}. {~They hold your gaze a moment too long.|They've started saving you a seat, and blushing about it.|Your names keep turning up next to each other in other people's gossip.} You could let it grow, or gently let it cool.\ntext [042740] = 你和{npc}之间有什么变了。{~对方看着你的时间长了那么一瞬。|对方开始给你留座位，还为此脸红。|你们的名字总在别人的闲话里挨在一起。}你可以任它生长，也可以轻轻让它冷下来。\n# en: Two students on a bench a little closer than friends usually sit, both pretending to read the same page.\nscene [dd0df1] = 两个学生坐在长椅上，比朋友通常坐得近一点，都假装在读同一页书。\n# en: Let it grow\nopt.left.label [8ba126] = 任它生长\n# en: {~You don't say anything. You don't move away either.|You hold their gaze back, for exactly as long.|Let them gossip. You sit beside them anyway, in front of everyone.}\nopt.left.say.1 [82adf3] = {~你什么也没说。也没有挪开。|你回望对方，时间不多不少，正好一样长。|让他们去说吧。你还是当着所有人的面坐到了对方身边。}\n# en: Let it cool, kindly\nopt.right.label [6bd6f5] = 温和地让它冷却\n# en: {~You find a gentle way to say you value them as a friend. They look relieved, or almost.|You keep it light and friendly, and the moment passes. The friendship stays.|You give the gossip nothing to work with, gently, and in a week it moves on. The friendship doesn't.}\nopt.right.say.1 [028f08] = {~你找了个温和的方式，说你珍惜这份友情。对方看起来松了口气，或者差不多。|你把气氛保持得轻松友好，那一刻过去了。友情还在。|你温和地不给流言任何把柄，一个星期后它就转向了别处。友情没有。}\n\n@event jealousy_two_invitations\n# en: Two Invitations\ntitle [b97cfd] = 两份邀请\n# en: Two notes in one morning. {suitor} asks if you're free on Friday evening, in neat handwriting, crossed out once. {other} asks the same, for the same evening, in a hurry. They both know the other asked. The whole corridor seems to know.\ntext [cbc824] = 一个早上收到两张字条。{suitor}问你周五晚上有没有空，字迹工整，划掉过一处。{other}问了同样的事，同一个晚上，写得很匆忙。他们都知道对方也问了。整条走廊似乎都知道。\n# en: A student holding two folded notes, one in each hand, in a corridor where two people wait at opposite ends.\nscene [657e49] = 一个学生一手拿着一张折好的字条，站在走廊里，走廊两头各等着一个人。\n# en: Say yes to {suitor}\nopt.left.label [686714] = 答应{suitor}\n# en: Friday with {suitor} is everything the note promised. On Saturday {other} passes you on the stairs with a smile that stops a little short of the eyes.\nopt.left.say.1 [396855] = 和{suitor}的周五，和字条上许诺的一样好。周六，{other}在楼梯上与你擦肩而过，笑容还没到眼睛就停住了。\n# en: Say yes to {other}\nopt.right.label [88b7a4] = 答应{other}\n# en: Friday with {other} runs late and warm. On Saturday {suitor} is very polite to you at breakfast, which is worse than if they'd been rude.\nopt.right.say.1 [4809fb] = 和{other}的周五很晚才散，暖洋洋的。周六早餐时，{suitor}对你客客气气，比翻脸还难受。\n\n@event jealousy_seen_together\n# en: \"Who Was That?\"\ntitle [8e9dfe] = “那是谁？”\n# en: {suitor} saw you laughing with {other} in the courtyard yesterday, and is now asking about it too casually, in the voice people use when they've rehearsed. \"You two looked… close. Not that it's any of my business.\"\ntext [f83a3f] = {suitor}昨天看见你在庭院里和{other}说说笑笑，现在正装作随口问起，用的是那种事先排练过的语气。“你们俩看起来……挺亲近的。当然，这不关我的事。”\n# en: Two students talking by a window in a crowded common room, one of them asking a question too casually while looking at someone across the room.\nscene [3f099c] = 拥挤的公共休息室里，两个学生在窗边说话，其中一个装作随口一问，眼睛却瞟着屋子另一头的人。\n# en: Tell the truth: you like {other}\nopt.left.label [093300] = 说实话：你喜欢{other}\n# en: It lands like a dropped plate. {suitor} nods, and swallows, and thanks you for not pretending. It's the kindest thing you've done all week, and it doesn't feel kind at all.\nopt.left.say.1 [9b3e8a] = 这话像一只摔碎的盘子。{suitor}点点头，咽了一下，谢谢你没有装傻。这是你这周做过最善良的事，却一点也不觉得善良。\n# en: \"Oh, we're just friends\"\nopt.right.label [8fedc9] = “哦，我们只是朋友”\n# en: {suitor}'s shoulders come down an inch. You spend the rest of the evening together, and nobody mentions the courtyard again.\nopt.right.say.1 [b90ca0] = {suitor}的肩膀放松了一寸。那天晚上剩下的时间你们一直在一起，谁也没再提庭院的事。\n# en: It goes well, until word gets back to {other}, who hears \"just friends\" and decides to believe it.\nopt.right.say.2 [0917cb] = 一切都好，直到话传到了{other}耳朵里。TA听到了“只是朋友”，决定当真。\n\n@event jealousy_cold_supper\n# en: A Quiet Supper\ntitle [c2a4e7] = 沉默的晚饭\n# en: {suitor} has gone quiet at supper, and answers in single words. From the far end of the hall, {other} is waving you over to an empty seat. You know exactly why {suitor} is quiet. So, by the look of it, does {other}.\ntext [c7f87d] = {suitor}晚饭时一言不发，问什么都只答一两个字。大厅另一头，{other}正招手让你过去，旁边有个空位。你很清楚{suitor}为什么沉默。看样子，{other}也很清楚。\n# en: A long refectory table at supper, one student eating in pointed silence beside another, a third person waving from the far end of the hall.\nscene [6a1477] = 晚饭时长长的膳堂餐桌，一个学生在另一个人身边刻意沉默地吃饭，大厅另一头有人在招手。\n# en: Stay and talk it through\nopt.left.label [5621eb] = 留下来把话说开\n# en: A long walk after supper and a careful, clumsy conversation. {suitor} laughs at the end of it, which is something. At the far end of the hall, the empty seat stays empty.\nopt.left.say.1 [16900d] = 晚饭后长长的散步，一场小心又笨拙的谈话。最后{suitor}笑了，总算有点什么。大厅那头的空位，一直空着。\n# en: Take the seat {other} saved\nopt.right.label [9c1575] = 坐到{other}留的位子上\n# en: The seat is warm and so is {other}. Behind you, {suitor} finishes supper alone and leaves without the pudding, which everyone notices.\nopt.right.say.1 [58b5f7] = 位子是暖的，{other}也是。你身后，{suitor}一个人吃完晚饭，连甜点都没吃就走了，所有人都看见了。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/states/states.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/states/states.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@state origin_noble\n# en: Minor Nobility\nname [ccd408] = 小贵族出身\n# en: A family name that opens doors, and letters from home that bring money and expectations in the same envelope.\ndesc [1593e8] = 一个能敲开大门的姓氏，还有家书：钱和期望装在同一个信封里。\n# en: Comfortable: 90 crowns to start, and 38 from the family before each later term, now and then with a gift. Every letter weighs on you a little. Tuition is yours to pay.\nmoney [372df7] = 宽裕：起步 90 克朗，此后每学期开学前家里寄来 38 克朗，偶尔还有一份礼物。每封信都让你心里沉一沉。学费自己付。\n\n@state origin_scholar\n# en: Scholarship Student\nname [036234] = 奖学金学生\n# en: The Crown pays for clever students, for as long as they stay clever.\ndesc [a89deb] = 王室资助聪明的学生——只要他们一直聪明。\n# en: Tight: 60 crowns to start. Before each later term, 38 crowns if last term's GPA was 3.0 or better, 46 at 3.5 or better, and nothing below. Tuition is yours to pay.\nmoney [1857b7] = 拮据：起步 60 克朗。此后每学期开学前，若上学期 GPA 达到 3.0，发 38 克朗；达到 3.5，发 46 克朗；低于 3.0 则分文没有。学费自己付。\n\n@state origin_hedge\n# en: Hedge-Witch's Grandchild\nname [b36bba] = 乡野女巫的孙辈\n# en: Raised among drying herbs and muttered charms: a practical hand with potions, and not much patience for theory.\ndesc [e66cc4] = 在晾晒的草药和念叨的咒语间长大：调药是一把好手，对理论却没什么耐心。\n# en: Poor: 45 crowns and no money from home. You will need a job from the first term.\nmoney [4fe1b9] = 贫穷：45 克朗，家里不会寄钱来。你从第一学期起就需要打工。\n\n@state origin_street\n# en: Street-Raised\nname [9b8f09] = 街头长大\n# en: You learned to read crowds before you learned to read runes.\ndesc [8551b3] = 你学会读懂人群，比学会读懂符文还早。\n# en: Poor: 38 crowns and no one to send more. You will need a job from the first term.\nmoney [2467f4] = 贫穷：38 克朗，也没人会再寄钱来。你从第一学期起就需要打工。\n\n@state origin_merchant\n# en: Merchant's Child\nname [a051a5] = 商人之子\n# en: Everything has a price, and you were taught to haggle it down.\ndesc [c5b5c4] = 万物皆有价，而你从小就学会了砍价。\n# en: Rich but unsteady: 150 crowns to start, and 78 from the company before each later term when trade goes well, about two terms in three; nothing when it doesn't. Tuition is yours to pay.\nmoney [286e39] = 富有但不稳定：起步 150 克朗；此后每学期开学前，生意好时商行寄来 78 克朗，大约三个学期有两个；生意不好就分文没有。学费自己付。\n\n@state class_pyromancy\n# en: Elective: Pyromancy\nname [bc2cb8] = 选修：火焰术\n# en: Fire answers to will, not wishes. Singed eyebrows are considered a rite of passage.\ndesc [f7cdfd] = 火焰回应意志，而非愿望。烧焦眉毛被视为一种成人礼。\n\n@state class_tidecalling\n# en: Elective: Tidecalling\nname [25e58c] = 选修：唤潮术\n# en: Lessons on the lake shore, bending water and patience in equal measure.\ndesc [550653] = 在湖岸上课，操纵湖水与磨练耐心，二者同等重要。\n\n@state class_illusion\n# en: Elective: Illusion & Glamour\nname [b30964] = 选修：幻术与魅影\n# en: The art of making people see what you want. Professor Vey insists it is not lying.\ndesc [7aa865] = 让人看见你想让他们看见的东西的艺术。维伊教授坚称这不算撒谎。\n\n@state class_alchemy\n# en: Elective: Practical Alchemy\nname [8a43a8] = 选修：实用炼金\n# en: Brewing, distilling and the occasional controlled explosion.\ndesc [4cfafb] = 熬煮、蒸馏，以及偶尔一场受控的爆炸。\n\n@state class_divination\n# en: Elective: Divination\nname [14ec75] = 选修：占卜学\n# en: Tea leaves, star charts and the Mirror of Next Tuesday.\ndesc [531e06] = 茶叶、星图，还有“下周二之镜”。\n\n@state class_artifice\n# en: Elective: Artifice\nname [20fedd] = 选修：奇械术\n# en: Enchanting objects to do what they were never meant to do.\ndesc [1d11f0] = 给物品施加魔法，让它们做本不该做的事。\n\n@state class_beastcraft\n# en: Elective: Beastcraft\nname [664aeb] = 选修：驭兽学\n# en: Familiars, griffon husbandry and dodging the manticore pen.\ndesc [d23187] = 魔宠、狮鹫饲养，以及绕开蝎尾狮的围栏。\n\n@state class_astromancy\n# en: Elective: Astromancy\nname [58b65d] = 选修：星象学\n# en: Midnight lectures on the observatory roof. You will learn the stars; you will lose sleep.\ndesc [9aeff9] = 天文台屋顶上的午夜讲座。你会认识星星，也会失去睡眠。\n\n@state class_healing\n# en: Elective: Healing Arts\nname [9056e4] = 选修：治愈术\n# en: Mending flesh with light and needle. Sister Amaranth expects perfection.\ndesc [1f8e64] = 以光与针缝合血肉。阿玛兰斯修女要求完美。\n\n@state class_umbral\n# en: Elective: Umbral Theory\nname [44e968] = 选修：暗影理论\n# en: Restricted. The study of shadow, death and what lies behind both. Strictly theoretical. Mostly.\ndesc [ddfba6] = 受限课程。研究阴影、死亡，以及二者背后的东西。纯属理论。大体上是。\n\n@state club_dueling\n# en: Club: Dueling Circle\nname [ba1685] = 社团：决斗社\n# en: Bruises, glory and the Spring Tournament.\ndesc [284ecb] = 淤青、荣耀，还有春季锦标赛。\n\n@state club_choir\n# en: Club: Choir of the Spheres\nname [7a5e02] = 社团：天球合唱团\n# en: Harmonies that make the candles flicker in time.\ndesc [f7c729] = 和声能让烛火随着节拍摇曳。\n\n@state club_skyrace\n# en: Club: Skyrace Squad\nname [ffb40f] = 社团：天空竞速队\n# en: Brooms, hoops and screaming crowds.\ndesc [727b30] = 飞帚、光环和尖叫的人群。\n\n@state club_ledger\n# en: Club: The Lumen Ledger\nname [c5d754] = 社团：《流明纪事报》社\n# en: The student paper. Deadlines, scoops and enemies.\ndesc [0bbdce] = 学生报纸。截稿日、独家新闻和仇家。\n\n@state club_garden\n# en: Club: Verdant Circle\nname [b7b687] = 社团：青翠社\n# en: Greenhouse afternoons with mandrakes that sing off-key.\ndesc [443e64] = 温室里的午后时光，还有唱歌跑调的曼德拉草。\n\n@state club_stargazers\n# en: Club: Stargazers\nname [82b69d] = 社团：观星社\n# en: Telescopes, cocoa and constellations nobody else can name.\ndesc [7d729f] = 望远镜、热可可，以及谁也叫不出名字的星座。\n\n@state club_cartographers\n# en: Club: Midnight Cartographers\nname [6737ac] = 社团：午夜制图社\n# en: A club that officially does not exist, mapping corridors that officially do not either.\ndesc [e99289] = 一个官方上并不存在的社团，绘制着官方上同样不存在的走廊。\n\n@state job_library\n# en: Job: Library Assistant\nname [79ff99] = 工作：图书馆助理\n# en: Reshelving books that bite. Quiet hours, modest pay.\ndesc [c1eafc] = 把会咬人的书放回书架。工时安静，薪水微薄。\n\n@state job_tavern\n# en: Job: The Gilded Newt\nname [73eefc] = 工作：镀金蝾螈酒馆\n# en: Serving cider in the village tavern. Good tips, sore feet.\ndesc [598a90] = 在镇上的酒馆端苹果酒。小费不错，脚很酸。\n\n@state job_apothecary\n# en: Job: Apothecary Clerk\nname [1e38cf] = 工作：药铺店员\n# en: Labelling jars at Pell & Daughters. Do not lick anything.\ndesc [ddcc95] = 在佩尔父女药铺给瓶瓶罐罐贴标签。什么都别舔。\n\n@state job_stables\n# en: Job: Familiar Stables\nname [ecb6b5] = 工作：魔宠马厩\n# en: Feeding hippogriffs and mucking out things that breathe smoke.\ndesc [1c9431] = 喂鹰头马身有翼兽，清扫会喷烟的东西。\n\n@state job_tutor\n# en: Job: First-Year Tutor\nname [c57f7a] = 工作：一年级辅导员\n# en: Explaining the difference between a hex and a jinx, again.\ndesc [0f630b] = 再一次解释“恶咒”和“小咒”的区别。\n\n@state job_courier\n# en: Job: Broom Courier\nname [c82174] = 工作：飞帚信使\n# en: Parcels across the lake at full speed. Pays well. Hurts often.\ndesc [216f36] = 全速飞越湖面送包裹。报酬丰厚。经常受伤。\n\n@state hungry\n# en: Hungry\nname [0ae59c] = 饥饿\n# en: The board went unpaid this month. Bread rolls pocketed from the refectory only go so far.\ndesc [67e035] = 这个月的伙食费没交上。从膳堂顺来的面包卷也只能撑这么久。\n# en: {~Your stomach growls through every lecture.|Bread rolls in your pockets, and not enough of them.|You learn exactly how long a bowl of porridge lasts.}\ntick.say.1 [169a81] = {~每节课上你的肚子都在叫。|兜里揣着面包，可惜不够多。|你精确地知道了一碗粥能撑多久。}\n\n@state sick\n# en: Lake Fever\nname [977d44] = 湖热病\n# en: Shivers, sniffles and a nose that glows faintly blue.\ndesc [ca2430] = 发抖、流鼻涕，鼻头还泛着淡淡的蓝光。\n# en: {~You sniffle through the month, nose glowing faintly blue.|Shivers, tea and far too many handkerchiefs.}\ntick.say.1 [f0b7d2] = {~你吸着鼻子过了一整个月，鼻尖泛着淡淡的蓝光。|发抖、喝茶，还有多得离谱的手帕。}\n\n@state injured\n# en: Injured\nname [624ada] = 受伤\n# en: Something is bruised, sprained or bent the wrong way.\ndesc [316db4] = 身上有什么地方青了、扭了，或者弯错了方向。\n# en: {~Every staircase reminds you where it hurts.|You limp, and the moving stairs show no sympathy.}\ntick.say.1 [c69fb6] = {~每一段楼梯都在提醒你哪里疼。|你一瘸一拐，会动的楼梯毫不同情。}\n\n@state exhausted\n# en: Exhausted\nname [34cdec] = 精疲力竭\n# en: You could sleep standing up. You might have.\ndesc [6c252f] = 你站着都能睡着。说不定已经睡过了。\n# en: {~You yawn through a whole lecture without ever closing your mouth.|You could sleep standing up, and once you do.}\ntick.say.1 [1a54ed] = {~你打了一整节课的哈欠，嘴就没合上过。|你站着都能睡着，而且真睡着过一次。}\n\n@state inspired\n# en: Inspired\nname [b91753] = 灵感迸发\n# en: Ideas arrive faster than you can write them down.\ndesc [ba8e50] = 想法涌来的速度比你记下来还快。\n# en: {~Ideas arrive faster than you can write them down.|You fill a notebook and start another.}\ntick.say.1 [d826d8] = {~灵感来得比你记下的还快。|你写满一本笔记，又开了一本。}\n\n@state heartbroken\n# en: Heartbroken\nname [a560a2] = 心碎\n# en: Songs are suddenly about you. All of them.\ndesc [dd2311] = 突然之间，所有的歌唱的都是你。每一首。\n# en: {~Every song in the common room is about you.|You walk the long way round, to miss a certain corridor.}\ntick.say.1 [f73fc5] = {~公共休息室里的每首歌都在唱你。|你绕远路走，只为避开某条走廊。}\n\n@state slacking\n# en: Skipping Classes\nname [169826] = 逃课中\n# en: Freedom! Also: falling behind.\ndesc [e0a116] = 自由！以及：进度落后。\n# en: {~A free morning, a lie-in, a twinge of guilt.|You skip a lecture, and nobody seems to mind yet.}\ntick.say.1 [2416b1] = {~一个自由的早晨，一场懒觉，一丝内疚。|你逃了一节课，暂时好像没人在意。}\n\n@state detention\n# en: Detention\nname [49189b] = 禁闭\n# en: Scrubbing cauldrons under the eye of a prefect.\ndesc [e387b0] = 在级长的监视下刷坩埚。\n# en: {~Cauldrons scrubbed under a prefect's eye.|Evenings in detention, smelling of old potion.}\ntick.say.1 [20fe13] = {~在级长的注视下刷坩埚。|一个个禁闭的夜晚，满身陈年药水味。}\n\n@state probation\n# en: Academic Probation\nname [e41d9e] = 留校察看\n# en: Your marks are below the line. Two terms under a 1.0 and the Disciplinary Board sits. A GPA of 2.0 clears it.\ndesc [9f9702] = 你的成绩跌破了底线。连续两个学期GPA 低于 1.0，纪律委员会就要开会了。GPA 回到 2.0 即可解除。\n# en: {~Probation sits on your shoulder through every lecture.|Your name is on a list you never wanted to be on.}\ntick.say.1 [95cc4f] = {~每节课上，察看处分都压在你肩头。|你的名字上了一张你从不想上的名单。}\n\n@state hardship_grant\n# en: Hardship Grant\nname [db03a0] = 困难补助\n# en: The bursar's hardship fund pays 8 crowns a month for a term. The paperwork asked how poor you were, in detail.\ndesc [b1f685] = 财务官的困难基金每月发 8 克朗，为期一个学期。申请表把你有多穷问得清清楚楚。\n# en: {~The hardship grant arrives in a brown envelope with your name spelled wrong.|Eight crowns from the fund, and a form to say you received them.}\ntick.say.1 [6386b4] = {~困难补助装在一个牛皮纸信封里送来，你的名字拼错了。|基金寄来八克朗，外加一张让你签收的表格。}\n\n@state bursar_loan\n# en: Bursar's Loan\nname [9b1ce0] = 财务官的贷款\n# en: The academy lent you sixty crowns. It takes 8 a month when you can pay, seven times, then a last 4, with no interest and no collectors: only a very patient ledger.\ndesc [2599b5] = 学院借给你六十克朗。等你付得起时每月还 8 克朗，共七次，最后一次还 4 克朗，不收利息，也没有催债人：只有一本非常有耐心的账簿。\n# en: {~No loan payment this month. The bursar notes it without comment, which is its own comment.|The loan waits. The ledger is patient; you are less so.}\ntick.say.1 [863378] = {~这个月没还贷款。财务官记下了，一言不发，这本身就是一种表态。|贷款在等着。账簿很有耐心，你就没那么有耐心了。}\n# en: The bursar's loan is paid off. She stamps the ledger with something like a smile.\ntick.say.2 [f6df86] = 财务官的贷款还清了。她在账簿上盖了个章，脸上的表情近乎微笑。\n\n@state in_debt\n# en: Debt to Grisk\nname [d38823] = 欠格里斯克的债\n# en: The goblin moneylender takes 12 crowns a month until the debt is cleared. Ignore a missed payment and he adds another.\ndesc [37f244] = 地精放贷人每月拿走 12 克朗，直到债务还清。漏还一笔却置之不理，他就再加一个月。\n\n@state famous\n# en: Talk of the Academy\nname [a60529] = 学院热议人物\n# en: Strangers know your name. Some of them even say it nicely.\ndesc [e6822e] = 陌生人都知道你的名字。其中一些人提起时甚至还挺客气。\n\n@state hiccup_hex\n# en: Hiccup Hex\nname [c18908] = 打嗝恶咒\n# en: Every hiccup releases a small, harmless, extremely embarrassing firework.\ndesc [14430d] = 每打一个嗝，就会冒出一朵小小的、无害的、极其丢人的烟花。\n\n@state blessed\n# en: Maud's Blessing\nname [d2410d] = 莫德的祝福\n# en: A sprig of rosemary in your pocket that never wilts.\ndesc [7af4e4] = 口袋里一枝永不枯萎的迷迭香。\n\n@state familiar_fox\n# en: Familiar: Ember\nname [7343af] = 魔宠：余烬\n# en: A fox-spirit with a smouldering tail who has decided you are hers.\ndesc [8b3cfa] = 一只尾巴冒着余火的狐灵，她已经认定你是她的人了。\n\n@state familiar_owl\n# en: Familiar: Clockwork Owl\nname [e993d5] = 魔宠：发条猫头鹰\n# en: Mir's gift. It hoots the hour, mostly correctly, and remembers your homework.\ndesc [863cd5] = 米尔的礼物。它会报时（大多准确），还会记得你的作业。\n\n@state training\n# en: Tournament Training\nname [5aecec] = 锦标赛特训\n# en: Dawn drills on the dueling green.\ndesc [ba8ab4] = 在决斗草坪上的黎明操练。\n\n@state wakeful\n# en: Draught of Wakefulness\nname [e9272f] = 清醒药剂\n# en: You haven't blinked in eleven hours. Your notes are immaculate.\ndesc [8764b7] = 你已经十一个小时没眨眼了。笔记无可挑剔。\n\n@state haunted\n# en: Haunted Dormitory\nname [42efc1] = 闹鬼的宿舍\n# en: Something rearranges your socks at night.\ndesc [88b052] = 夜里有什么东西在重新整理你的袜子。\n\n@state watched\n# en: Under Bram's Eye\nname [53b10a] = 被布拉姆盯上\n# en: The prefect is watching you. Specifically, and with relish.\ndesc [03458f] = 那位级长正盯着你。专门盯着，而且乐在其中。\n\n@state umbral_curse\n# en: Umbral Curse\nname [11a418] = 暗影诅咒\n# en: A cold that lives under your skin. Something in the restricted section followed you home.\ndesc [3ffb2e] = 一股寒意住在你的皮肤下。禁书区里有什么东西跟着你回来了。\n\n@state hollow_dreams\n# en: Hollow Dreams\nname [b977da] = 空洞之梦\n# en: Every night: a black star, a door beneath the lake, a voice that knows your name.\ndesc [b0df12] = 每一夜：一颗黑色的星，一扇湖底之门，一个知道你名字的声音。\n\n@state star_mark\n# en: The Star-Mark\nname [b7b256] = 星痕\n# en: A pale, star-shaped mark on your palm that hums near old magic.\ndesc [c1678d] = 掌心一枚苍白的星形印记，靠近古老魔法时会嗡嗡作响。\n\n@state summer_away\n# en: Summer Away\nname [77bf0e] = 离校度暑\n# en: Away from the academy for the summer recess. No rent, no classes.\ndesc [abac1f] = 暑假期间离开学院。不交房租，不用上课。\n\n@state summer_work\n# en: Summer Work\nname [2d8ae9] = 暑期打工\n# en: A summer of honest (and occasionally dishonest) labour.\ndesc [4dd36c] = 一个诚实（偶尔不太诚实）劳动的夏天。\n\n@state frog_voice\n# en: Croaking\nname [5daa73] = 呱呱叫\n# en: A botched transfiguration left you with the voice of a bullfrog.\ndesc [02c975] = 一次失败的变形术，让你的嗓音变成了牛蛙。\n\n@state garden_plot\n# en: Garden Plot\nname [7ba473] = 一方菜园\n# en: A little square of earth behind the greenhouses that is yours. Things grow; so do you.\ndesc [3a1dd5] = 温室后面一小块属于你的泥土。东西在长，你也在长。\n\n@state pen_pal\n# en: Pen Pal in Thornwick\nname [7e85f7] = 荆木学院的笔友\n# en: A student at the rival academy who writes you long, funny letters full of terrible gossip.\ndesc [b0b239] = 对手学院的一名学生，常给你写又长又好笑、满是糟糕八卦的信。\n\n@state frost_ward\n# en: Ilse's Frost-Ward\nname [302a95] = 伊尔泽的霜之护符\n# en: A sliver of Northern winter that Ilse sewed into your collar. Cold things think twice about you.\ndesc [ca24da] = 伊尔泽缝进你衣领里的一缕北境寒冬。冰冷的东西靠近你之前都会犹豫一下。\n\n@state plan_castle\n# en: Break: at the castle\nname [961ce0] = 假期：留在城堡\n# en: A skeleton crew, empty corridors and secrets. Board costs 6 crowns a month.\ndesc [c6616d] = 只剩少数留守的职员、空荡的走廊和秘密。每月食宿 6 克朗。\n\n@state plan_home\n# en: Break: at home\nname [5a492e] = 假期：回家\n# en: Family, old friends and free board. The days are slower here.\ndesc [8e2284] = 家人、老朋友和免费食宿。这里的日子过得慢些。\n\n@state gig_grounds\n# en: Holiday gig: the grounds\nname [1908bb] = 假期打工：庭园\n# en: Working the grounds for Old Maud this break. Good pay, sore hands.\ndesc [c0cf6b] = 这个假期跟老莫德打理庭园。工钱不错，手会酸。\n\n@state gig_kitchens\n# en: Holiday gig: the kitchens\nname [78fcf0] = 假期打工：后厨\n# en: Working the Refectory kitchens this break. Good pay, short temper.\ndesc [b6d78e] = 这个假期在膳堂后厨帮工。工钱不错，脾气会变短。\n\n@state gig_library\n# en: Holiday gig: the stocktake\nname [4d1632] = 假期零工：图书盘点\n# en: The library's holiday stocktake, for its senior clerks. Every book counted, every bite noted. Better pay than the baseline gigs.\ndesc [ffcda9] = 图书馆的假期盘点，给资深馆员的活。每本书都要数，每次被咬都要记。比基础零工报酬更高。\n\n@state gig_newt\n# en: Holiday gig: the Newt's holiday rush\nname [d06f0d] = 假期零工：蝾螈酒馆的节日高峰\n# en: Running the Gilded Newt through the holiday rush, for its managers. The best tips of the year, and the longest nights.\ndesc [1bfad0] = 替镀金蝾螈酒馆撑过节日高峰，给经理的活。一年里最好的小费，也是最长的夜。\n\n@state gig_pell\n# en: Holiday gig: the remedy rush\nname [cbd921] = 假期零工：药剂旺季\n# en: Mixing the season's remedies at Pell & Daughters, for its assistant apothecaries. Good pay, and the village depends on it.\ndesc [13afb3] = 在佩尔父女药铺调配应季药剂，给助理药师的活。报酬不错，全村都指望着你。\n\n@state gig_post\n# en: Holiday gig: the holiday post\nname [715a4f] = 假期零工：节日邮件\n# en: Flying the holiday post for the courier loft, for its chief couriers. Every parcel in the valley, and the weather against you.\ndesc [efbb90] = 替信使阁楼飞送节日邮件，给首席信使的活。山谷里的每一个包裹，还有跟你作对的天气。\n\n@state plan_work\n# en: Break: working full-time\nname [3237e0] = 假期：全职打工\n# en: Working on campus through the break: a holiday gig, and board as usual. Tiring.\ndesc [91b8eb] = 整个假期在校内打工：一份假期零工，食宿照常付。很累。\n\n@state plan_internship\n# en: Break: internship\nname [4871d8] = 假期：实习\n# en: A research placement away from the castle, with a stipend and a supervisor.\ndesc [f66cf3] = 离开城堡的研究实习，有津贴，也有导师。\n\n@state locked_out\n# en: Confined After Dark\nname [3f39dd] = 入夜禁足\n# en: Caught once too often. Your door is checked at midnight, and the night is closed to you.\ndesc [a2bb11] = 被抓得太多次了。午夜有人查你的门，夜晚对你关上了。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/ui.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) interface strings.\n# Keys come from UI_EN in src/i18n.js. Keep {placeholders}; \"_html\" keys may contain <b> tags.\n# pronoun.* keys fill {they} {them} {their} {theirs} {are} {s} in content text (empty is allowed).\n\n@ui\n# en: English\nlang.name = 简体中文\n# en: Language\nlang.label = 语言\n# en: Your name\ntitle.yourName = 你的名字\n# en: Seed (optional)\ntitle.seed = 随机种子（可选）\n# en: random\ntitle.seedPlaceholder = 随机\n# en: Begin your first year\ntitle.begin = 开始你的第一学年\n# en: Who were you before?\norigin.heading = 来这里之前，你是谁？\n# en: Where you come from decides what you start with, and how hard money will be.\norigin.intro = 你的出身决定了你起步时有什么，以及钱会有多难挣。\n# en: Hard mode for money: plan on working a job most terms.\norigin.poorWarning = 手头最紧：做好大多数学期都要打工的准备。\n# en: Back\norigin.back = 返回\n# en: Continue · {info}\ntitle.continue = 继续 · {info}\n# en: {name} — {month}, Year {year}\ntitle.saveInfo = {name} — 第 {year} 年{month}\n# en: <b>Swipe</b> each card left or right (or press ← / →) to choose. Some moments open a <b>menu</b> with more options. Keep your <b>Health, Mood, Gold and Standing</b> above zero for three years — 36 months — and see who you become. Glowing dots above a meter show what a choice will touch.\ntitle.howto_html = 左右<b>滑动</b>卡牌（或按 ← / →）做出选择。某些时刻会弹出<b>菜单</b>，提供更多选项。在三年——共 36 个月——里，让你的<b>健康、心情、金币和学业</b>都保持在零以上，看看你会成为怎样的人。属性上方亮起的光点，提示这个选择会影响什么。\n# en: Wren, Ash, Rowan, Elowen, Fenn, Isla, Corin, Sable, Theo, Marlo, Juniper, Ezra\ntitle.names = 雷恩, 艾什, 罗文, 埃洛温, 芬恩, 艾拉, 科林, 塞布尔, 西奥, 马洛, 朱妮珀, 以斯拉\n# en: Wren\ntitle.defaultName = 雷恩\n# en: Character & people\ntop.people = 角色与人物\n# en: Save & return to title\ntop.quit = 保存并返回标题\n# en: Language\ntop.lang = 语言\n# en: YEAR {year} · MONTH {turn} OF {max}\ndate.sub = 第 {year} 年 · 第 {turn} / {max} 月\n# en: You\ntab.you = 你\n# en: People\ntab.people = 人物\n# en: Clues\ntab.clues = 线索\n# en: Nothing to follow yet. Odd things you notice, and where they point, gather here.\nside.noClues = 还没有可追查的事。你注意到的怪事，以及它们指向何处，都会汇集在这里。\n# en: Followed up\njournal.finished = 已追查\n# en: Journal\ntab.journal = 日志\n# en: Conditions & commitments\nside.conditions = 状态与承诺\n# en: Nothing yet.\nside.nothing = 暂时还没有。\n# en: {n} mo\nside.monthsLeft = 剩 {n} 月\n# en: You haven't really met anyone yet.\nside.noPeople = 你还没真正认识谁。\n# en: Relationship {n}\nside.relationship = 关系 {n}\n# en: Spark {n}\nside.romance = 心动 {n}\n# en: Spells\nside.spells = 咒语\n# en: {n} mana\nside.spellCost = {n} 法力\n# en: Your story is yet unwritten.\nside.emptyJournal = 你的故事尚未落笔。\n# en: {month} · Y{year}\njournal.when = 第 {year} 年 · {month}\n# en: Act {n} of {of} · {name}\njournal.act = 第 {n} 幕（共 {of} 幕）· {name}\n# en: Clues\njournal.clues = 线索\n# en: Leads\njournal.leads = 头绪\n# en: followed\njournal.followed = 已追查\n# en: a key clue: needed beneath the lake\njournal.keyClue = 关键线索：湖底用得上\n# en: What happened\njournal.log = 发生过的事\n# en: friend\nbond.friend = 朋友\n# en: close\nbond.close = 挚友\n# en: rival\nbond.rival = 对手\n# en: nemesis\nbond.nemesis = 宿敌\n# en: partner\nbond.partner = 恋人\n# en: ex\nbond.ex = 前任\n# en: Class\ntag.class = 课程\n# en: Club\ntag.club = 社团\n# en: Work\ntag.job = 打工\n# en: Social\ntag.social = 社交\n# en: Romance\ntag.romance = 恋爱\n# en: Study\ntag.study = 学业\n# en: Mystery\ntag.mystery = 谜团\n# en: Danger\ntag.danger = 危险\n# en: Crisis\ntag.crisis = 危机\n# en: Festival\ntag.festival = 节庆\n# en: Coin\ntag.money = 钱财\n# en: Grounds\ntag.nature = 校园\n# en: Home\ntag.home = 日常\n# en: Rivalry\ntag.rival = 较量\n# en: Exam\ntag.exam = 考试\n# en: Chronicle\ntag.story = 编年\n# en: Magic\ntag.magic = 魔法\n# en: Academy life\ntag.default = 学院生活\n# en: consequence\ncard.consequence = 后果\n# en: Illustration: {text}\ncard.illustration = 插图：{text}\n# en: Not available\ncard.notAvailable = 无法选择\n# en: Not enough crowns ({cost} needed)\ncard.cantAfford = 克朗不够（需要 {cost}）\n# en: {skill} check · {p}%\ncheck.button = {skill}检定 · {p}%\n# en: {skill} check ({p}%) · {result}\ncheck.chip = {skill}检定（{p}%）· {result}\n# en: success\ncheck.success = 成功\n# en: failed\ncheck.failed = 失败\n# en: max\nchip.max = 已满\n# en: {name} {delta}\nchip.rel = {name} {delta}\n# en: {track} performance {delta}\nchip.perf = {track}表现 {delta}\n# en: {track} progress {delta}\nchip.progress = {track}进度 {delta}\n# en: {track} study {delta}\nchip.study = {track}备考 {delta}\n# en: Exam preparation, {n} courses\nchip.studyAll = 备考（{n}门课）\n# en: {name} (spark) {delta}\nchip.love = {name}（心动）{delta}\n# en: You met {name}.\nnote.meet = 你认识了{name}。\n# en: {name}: {status}\nnote.bond = {name}：{status}\n# en: {name} now sees you as a rival: respect with an edge, which can sit alongside friendship\nnote.bondRival = {name} 把你视为对手：是尊重，也带着锋芒，可以与友谊并存\n# en: + {state}\nnote.gain = + {state}\n# en: − {state}\nnote.lose = − {state}\n# en: New spell: {spell} {level}\nnote.spell = 新咒语：{spell} {level}\n# en: New spell: {spell} {level} ({bonus})\nnote.spellBonus = 新咒语：{spell} {level}（{bonus}）\n# en: You learned something about {name}.\nnote.fact = 你对{name}多了一分了解。\n# en: {kind} ({p}%) · {result}\ncheck.challengeChip = {kind}（{p}%）· {result}\n# en: {kind} · {p}%\ncheck.challengeButton = {kind} · {p}%\n# en: Combat · {p}% · −{cost} mana\ncheck.combatButton = 战斗 · {p}% · 法力 −{cost}\n# en: Exam: {skill} · pass {p}% · A {a}%\ncheck.examButton = 考试：{skill} · 及格 {p}% · A {a}%\n# en: wild\nswing.wild = 起伏大\n# en: steady\nswing.steady = 稳定\n# en: luck\nswing.luck = 看运气\n# en: Combat\nchallenge.combat = 战斗\n# en: Tests {skill}\ncourse.tests = 考查{skill}\n# en: {tier} exam, {p}% pass today\ncourse.exam = {tier}考试，按现在算及格率 {p}%\n# en: teaches {spells}\ncourse.teaches = 教授{spells}\n# en: About {gold} crowns a shift · {n} shifts a term\njob.outlook = 每班约 {gold} 克朗 · 每学期 {n} 班\n# en: Leaves {from}: your {rank} rank and {perf} performance; you start here as new\ntrack.switch = 将离开{from}：你的{rank}职位和 {perf} 表现；在这里从新人做起\n# en: Easy\ntier.easy = 简单\n# en: Standard\ntier.standard = 标准\n# en: Medium\ntier.medium = 中等\n# en: Hard\ntier.hard = 困难\n# en: Heroic\ntier.heroic = 英雄级\n# en: Duel\nchallenge.duel = 决斗\n# en: Negotiation\nchallenge.negotiate = 交涉\n# en: Deception\nchallenge.deceive = 欺瞒\n# en: Exam\nchallenge.exam = 考试\n# en: Research\nchallenge.research = 研究\n# en: Brewing\nchallenge.brew = 调制\n# en: Enchanting\nchallenge.enchant = 附魔\n# en: Feat\nchallenge.feat = 体能\n# en: Spellwork\nchallenge.spell = 施法\n# en: win\nchallenge.win = 胜\n# en: partial\nchallenge.partial = 小胜\n# en: lose\nchallenge.lose = 负\n# en: {spell} {level} · {cost} mana\nspell.button = {spell} {level} · {cost} 法力\n# en: Not enough mana\nspell.short = 法力不足\n# en: Something follows from this.\npreview.follows = 这会引出后续。\n# en: Your saved game was from an older version of the game and can no longer be loaded. Its run log was kept.\ntitle.oldSave = 你的存档来自旧版本游戏，已无法读取。它的游戏记录已被保留。\n# en: From an older version; can’t be loaded\nslots.tooOld = 旧版本存档，无法读取\n# en: tap or swipe to continue\noutcome.tap = 轻点或滑动继续\n# en: Continue\noutcome.continue = 继续\n# en: Year {year} · Month {turn} of {max}\nmonth.header = 第 {year} 年 · 第 {turn} / {max} 月\n# en: This month\nmonth.thisMonth = 本月\n# en: Begin {month}\nmonth.begin = 开始{month}\n# en: Continue\nmonth.continue = 继续\n# en: The leaves turn. A new term begins.\nseason.1 = 落叶纷飞，新学期开始了。\n# en: Lanterns over the lake.\nseason.2 = 灯笼飘过湖面。\n# en: Fog creeps up the stairwells.\nseason.3 = 雾气爬上了楼梯间。\n# en: Frost on the windows; exams in the air.\nseason.4 = 窗上结霜，考试的气息弥漫开来。\n# en: Deep winter. The Midwinter Ball approaches.\nseason.5 = 隆冬时节。仲冬舞会近了。\n# en: The thaw, and a new term.\nseason.6 = 冰雪消融，新学期又至。\n# en: Storms roll in off the mountains.\nseason.7 = 风暴从山那边滚滚而来。\n# en: The lawns are green again. Tournament season.\nseason.8 = 草坪又绿了。锦标赛的季节到了。\n# en: Blossom on every bough.\nseason.9 = 枝头开满了花。\n# en: High summer heat — and the Grand Examinations.\nseason.10 = 盛夏酷暑——还有年度大考。\n# en: Summer recess. The towers empty.\nseason.11 = 暑假到了。塔楼空了下来。\n# en: The last weeks of summer. Stars begin to fall.\nseason.12 = 夏天的最后几周。星星开始坠落。\n# en: Your story ends early · {month}, Year {year}\nending.early = 你的故事提前落幕 · 第 {year} 年{month}\n# en: Graduation · Ending\nending.graduation = 毕业 · 结局\n# en: The people who mattered\nending.people = 那些重要的人\n# en: Begin anew\nending.again = 重新开始\n# en: Read your journal\nending.journal = 翻阅你的日志\n# en: Back to the ending\nending.back = 回到结局\n# en: fond\nending.fond = 亲近\n# en: cold\nending.cold = 冷淡\n# en: Export this run\nending.export = 导出本局记录\n# en: The Hollow Star\nending.slot.mystery = 空洞之星\n# en: Bonds\nending.slot.relationships = 羁绊\n# en: Remembered as\nending.slot.personal = 人们记得你是\n# en: This card\nfb.this = 本卡\n# en: Last card\nfb.last = 上一张\n# en: Previous\nfb.prev = 前一张\n# en: Ending\nfb.ending = 结局\n# en: Good\nfb.good = 好\n# en: Bad\nfb.bad = 不好\n# en: Rate & comment (F)\nfb.open = 评分与评论（F）\n# en: Feedback\nfb.heading = 反馈\n# en: Rate cards and leave notes for the writers. Everything is saved with this run’s log.\nfb.intro = 为卡牌评分并给作者留言。所有内容都会随本局记录一起保存。\n# en: You chose: {choice}\nfb.chose = 你的选择：{choice}\n# en: What worked, what didn’t? Tone, typos, balance, a confusing choice…\nfb.placeholder = 哪里好，哪里不好？语气、错字、数值平衡、令人困惑的选项……\n# en: Save\nfb.save = 保存\n# en: Cancel\nfb.cancel = 取消\n# en: Nothing to rate yet.\nfb.none = 还没有可以评分的内容。\n# en: {month} · Y{year}\nfb.when = 第 {year} 年 · {month}\n# en: Playtest log: {runs} runs · {notes} notes\nfb.log = 试玩记录：{runs} 局 · {notes} 条反馈\n# en: Export\nfb.export = 导出\n# en: Morning\nslot.morning = 清晨\n# en: Noon\nslot.noon = 正午\n# en: Afternoon\nslot.afternoon = 午后\n# en: Evening\nslot.evening = 傍晚\n# en: Night\nslot.night = 夜晚\n# en: Midnight\nslot.midnight = 午夜\n# en: Term\nphase.term = 学期中\n# en: Break\nphase.break = 假期\n# en: Wrap-up\nphase.wrapup = 收尾\n# en: {month} · Year {year} · {phase}\nmap.title = 第 {year} 年 · {month} · {phase}\n# en: Where will you spend the afternoon?\nmap.question1 = 这个下午你要去哪儿？\n# en: Where will you spend the afternoon and the evening?\nmap.question2 = 这个下午和傍晚你要去哪儿？\n# en: Where will you spend the afternoon?\nmap.q.afternoon = 这个下午你要去哪儿？\n# en: Where will you spend the evening?\nmap.q.evening = 这个傍晚你要去哪儿？\n# en: Where will you go tonight?\nmap.q.midnight = 今晚你要去哪儿？\n# en: visit {n} of {of}\nmap.visitOf = 第 {n} / {of} 次拜访\n# en: Stay in\nmap.stayIn = 待在住处\n# en: {event}: at {place}, this {slot}\nmap.festivalHere = {event}：这个{slot}在{place}\n# en: choose…\nmap.choose = 选择……\n# en: Begin the day\nmap.go = 开始这一天\n# en: List view\nmap.showList = 列表视图\n# en: Map view\nmap.showMap = 地图视图\n# en: Somewhere you haven’t found yet.\nmap.undiscovered = 一个你还没发现的地方。\n# en: Both visits are picked; tap one to change it.\nmap.full = 两次拜访都选好了；点其中一个可以更换。\n# en: {name} isn’t open then (open: {hours}).\nmap.closed = {name}那时不开放（开放时间：{hours}）。\n# en: closed now\nmap.closedNow = 现在关闭\n# en: open\nmap.open = 开放\n# en: not yet found\nmap.hinted = 尚未发现\n# en: festival this {slot}\nmap.festival = 这个{slot}有节庆\n# en: here: {names}\nmap.here = 在这里：{names}\n# en: something new\nmap.new = 有新鲜事\n# en: Practice · {n} left\nmap.practiceLeft = 练习 · 还剩 {n} 次\n# en: Shift · {n} left\nmap.shiftsLeft = 班次 · 还剩 {n} 次\n# en: Shifts · all done this term\nmap.shiftsDone = 班次 · 本学期已全部完成\n# en: visited today\nmap.visitedNow = 今天去过了\n# en: You’ve already been to {name} today.\nmap.visitedToday = 你今天已经去过{name}了。\n# en: getting ready for the festival this {slot}\nmap.festivalSoon = 正在为{slot}的节庆做准备\n# en: {name} is closed while it gets ready for the festival this {slot}.\nmap.festivalLater = {name}正在为{slot}的节庆做准备，暂不开放。\n# en: Session · {n} left\nmap.sessionsLeft = 课时 · 还剩 {n} 次\n# en: Confirm ({n}/{max})\nmenu.confirm = 确认（{n}/{max}）\n# en: {delta} next month\ndue.next = 下月 {delta}\n# en: Next month:\ndue.heading = 下个月：\n# en: Other\ndue.other = 其他\n# en: A choice\nmenu.tagline = 抉择\n# en: Make your choice\nmenu.lead = 做出选择\n# en: Choose up to {n}\nmenu.leadMany = 最多选 {n} 项\n# en: Choose one.\nmenu.prompt = 选一项。\n# en: Choose up to {n}, then confirm.\nmenu.promptMany = 最多选 {n} 项，然后确认。\n# en: Signs (+ ++ +++)\namounts.marks = 符号（+ ++ +++）\n# en: Numbers\namounts.numbers = 数字\n# en: Icons only\namounts.icons = 仅图标\n# en: Settings\nsettings.heading = 设置\n# en: Amounts in previews\nsettings.amounts = 预览中的数值\n# en: How a choice's effects show before you make it. Afterwards the outcome always shows the numbers; Crowns and Mana are always exact.\nsettings.amountsHint = 选择之前，效果如何显示。选择之后，结果总会显示具体数字；克朗和魔力始终显示确切数值。\n# en: Illustrative text\nsettings.notes = 插图说明\n# en: The line under each card that describes its picture.\nsettings.notesHint = 每张卡片下方描述其画面的那一行文字。\n# en: Show\nsettings.on = 显示\n# en: Hide\nsettings.off = 隐藏\n# en: tiny\nsize.tiny = 极小\n# en: small\nsize.small = 小\n# en: medium\nsize.medium = 中\n# en: large\nsize.large = 大\n# en: huge\nsize.huge = 极大\n# en: {track}: {grade}\nnote.grade = {track}：{grade}\n# en: {track}: {from} → {grade} on appeal\nnote.regrade = {track}：申诉后 {from} → {grade}\n# en: {track}: {grade} ({skill} {value} against an exam bar of {bar}; pass chance {p}%)\nnote.gradeWhy = {track}：{grade}（{skill} {value}，考试门槛 {bar}；及格几率 {p}%）\n# en: Enrolled: {track}\nnote.enrol = 已选课：{track}\n# en: Joined: {track}\nnote.join = 已加入：{track}\n# en: Left: {track}\nnote.leave = 已退出：{track}\n# en: {track}: done\nnote.thesisDone = {track}：已完成\n# en: Promoted in {track}\nnote.promote = 在{track}获得晋升\n# en: Dropped from {track}\nnote.dropped = 被{track}除名\n# en: {track}: missed {n}\nnote.missed = {track}：缺席 {n} 次\n# en: {track}: {n} left this term\nnote.attend = {track}：本学期还剩 {n} 次\n# en: {track}: your wages, {gold} crowns\nnote.wage = {track}：本次工钱 {gold} 克朗\n# en: {track}: an extra visit (breaks don't count toward the term)\nnote.attendBreak = {track}：额外的一次（假期不计入学期要求）\n# en: New place: {place}\nnote.discover = 新地点：{place}\n# en: A clue found\nnote.clue = 发现了一条线索\n# en: Studies & commitments\nside.studies = 学业与职责\n# en: GPA {gpa}\nside.gpa = GPA {gpa}\n# en: Enrolled this term\nside.enrolled = 本学期所选课程\n# en: Preparation {n}/{cap}\nside.prep = 备考 {n}/{cap}\n# en: exam in {n} months\nside.examIn = {n} 个月后考试\n# en: exam next month\nside.examNext = 下个月考试\n# en: exam this month\nside.examNow = 本月考试\n# en: Pass {p}%\nside.passToday = 及格 {p}%\n# en: If the exam were today: pass {pass}%, A or B {top}%. Lectures and study choices add preparation (up to the cap); your skill keeps growing until the exam.\nside.oddsTip = 如果今天考试：及格 {pass}%，A 或 B {top}%。上课和学习选择会增加备考（有上限）；考试前你的技能还会继续成长。\n# en: Grades: {grades}\nside.grades = 成绩：{grades}\n# en: {rank} · {due} due this term\nside.rankLine = {rank} · 本学期需出席 {due} 次\n# en: {rank} · all shifts done this term\nside.shiftsDone = {rank} · 本学期的班都上完了\n# en: {rank} · no shifts over the break\nside.noShifts = {rank} · 假期没有班\n# en: {n}%\nside.perf = {n}%\n# en: Progress {n}%\nside.progress = 进度 {n}%\n# en: a society: no dues, no ranks\nside.society = 秘密社团：无出勤要求，无职级\n# en: Applied\nside.applied = 已申请\n# en: Saved games\nslots.heading = 存档\n# en: Autosave\nslots.auto = 自动存档\n# en: Slot {n}\nslots.slot = 存档位 {n}\n# en: Empty\nslots.empty = 空\n# en: {name} — {month}, Year {year}\nslots.info = {name} — 第 {year} 年 · {month}\n# en: Save here\nslots.saveHere = 存到这里\n# en: Load\nslots.load = 读取\n# en: Saved\nslots.saved = 已保存\n# en: Close\nslots.close = 关闭\n# en: Stored on the server. Loading a slot continues it as a new run.\nslots.whereServer = 存档保存在服务器上。读取存档位后，会作为一局新的游戏继续。\n# en: Stored in this browser. Loading a slot continues it as a new run.\nslots.whereLocal = 存档保存在本浏览器中。读取存档位后，会作为一局新的游戏继续。\n# en: Connected: runs and feedback are saved on the server.\nnet.connected = 已连接：游戏记录与反馈会保存到服务器。\n# en: Admin\nnet.admin = 管理\n# en: she\npronoun.she.they = 她\n# en: her\npronoun.she.them = 她\n# en: her\npronoun.she.their = 她的\n# en: hers\npronoun.she.theirs = 她的\n# en: is\npronoun.she.are = \n# en: s\npronoun.she.s = \n# en: he\npronoun.he.they = 他\n# en: him\npronoun.he.them = 他\n# en: his\npronoun.he.their = 他的\n# en: his\npronoun.he.theirs = 他的\n# en: is\npronoun.he.are = \n# en: s\npronoun.he.s = \n# en: they\npronoun.they.they = TA\n# en: them\npronoun.they.them = TA\n# en: their\npronoun.they.their = TA的\n# en: theirs\npronoun.they.theirs = TA的\n# en: are\npronoun.they.are = \n# en: \npronoun.they.s = \n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/arcs.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/arcs.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@arc hollowstar\n# en: The Hollow Star\nname [e1bf91] = 空洞之星\n# en: Omens, Investigation, Escalation, Convergence\nact_names [aff64b] = 预兆、调查、升级、汇聚\n# en: Something that eats light sleeps beneath the Mirror Lake, behind a door the founders sealed with seven locks. Someone wants it open.\ndesc [30af88] = 有一种吞噬光的东西沉睡在镜湖底下，被创始人用七道锁封在一扇门后。有人想把门打开。\n\n@arc lyra_and_her_mother\n# en: Second Place\nname [604ed6] = 第二名\n# en: Rivals, Cracks, Choice\nact_names [254274] = 对手、裂痕、抉择\n# en: Lyra Vaneshade has never once heard \"well done\". Her rivalry with you can become respect, friendship or more, and her mother will have something to say about all three.\ndesc [227832] = 莱拉·凡妮希德从未听过一句“做得好”。她与你的竞争可以变成尊重、友谊，甚至更多——而她的母亲对这三者都会有话要说。\n\n@arc kael_thaw\n# en: Thaw\nname [448405] = 解冻\n# en: Frost, Trust, Thaw\nact_names [0abe5d] = 冰封、信任、解冻\n# en: Kael Dumont was sent to open a door and came to stop it. Whether he manages depends on whether anyone is warm enough.\ndesc [4e1d95] = 凯尔·杜蒙被派来打开一扇门，却来阻止它。他能否做到，取决于有没有人足够温暖。\n\n@arc ilse_dumont\n# en: The Girl in White Furs\nname [a99aba] = 白裘少女\n# en: Arrival, The Man in Grey, North\nact_names [b9bace] = 到来、灰衣人、北方\n# en: Kael's sister was raised by the Hollow cult to do what he wouldn't. The man in grey wants her home. She might let him.\ndesc [7d861b] = 凯尔的妹妹被空洞教团养大，去做他不肯做的事。灰衣人想带她回家。她也许会跟他走。\n\n@arc the_bell\n# en: The Bell Under the Water\nname [f2ca3f] = 水下的钟\n# en: Echo, Rope\nact_names [ee3094] = 回声、绳索\n# en: The drowned bell under the Mirror Lake rings when no one is near it. Someone wants it found.\ndesc [e24896] = 镜湖底那口沉钟会在无人靠近时鸣响。有人想让它被找到。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/cast.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/cast.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@npc lyra\n# en: Lyra Vaneshade\nname [7ccd7a] = 莱拉·凡妮希德\n# en: Heir of House Vaneshade, pyromancy prodigy\ntitle [a4c6ad] = 凡妮希德家族继承人，火焰术天才\n# en: Emberhall\nhouse [6adebf] = 烬厅\n# en: Brilliant, impatient and used to winning. She treats everyone as either a rival or furniture — and is quietly lonely about it.\ndesc [b8c380] = 才华横溢，缺乏耐心，习惯了赢。在她眼里，每个人要么是对手，要么是家具——她为此默默地孤独着。\n\n@npc tobias\n# en: Tobias Fennick\nname [b5c657] = 托比亚斯·芬尼克\n# en: Farmer's son on a turnip scholarship\ntitle [ae6bc4] = 拿着芜菁奖学金的农家子弟\n# en: Rootward\nhouse [dd14d3] = 根庐\n# en: Big hands, bigger heart. Talks to plants and, worryingly, the plants seem to answer.\ndesc [325d88] = 手掌大，心更大。他会跟植物说话，而令人担忧的是，植物似乎会回答。\n\n@npc seraphine\n# en: Seraphine Hale\nname [b9b6ae] = 瑟拉芬·海尔\n# en: Seer who never quite looks at the present\ntitle [a72b9b] = 从不正眼看“现在”的预言者\n# en: Stillwater\nhouse [67e7fc] = 静水\n# en: Speaks in half-sentences, as if finishing the other half in a different week. Often right. Unsettlingly often.\ndesc [abbc16] = 说话总是只说半句，仿佛另一半要留到下个星期再说完。她常常是对的。准得让人发毛。\n\n@npc corvin\n# en: Corvin Ashby\nname [5f13bd] = 科尔文·阿什比\n# en: Charming liability\ntitle [c06ba3] = 迷人的麻烦精\n# en: Duskgate\nhouse [a1e05f] = 暮门\n# en: Knows every locked door in the academy and has opinions about all of them. Owes money to at least three people.\ndesc [3fb5db] = 认识学院里每一扇锁着的门，并对每一扇都有意见。至少欠着三个人的钱。\n\n@npc mir\n# en: Mir Ten\nname [31baa8] = 米尔·滕\n# en: Artificer of questionable inventions\ntitle [dcc640] = 发明可疑的奇械师\n# en: Brasswick\nhouse [6604c0] = 铜芯\n# en: Smells permanently of solder and ozone. Has built a clockwork owl, a self-stirring cauldron and at least one small fire.\ndesc [7b73bc] = 身上永远带着焊锡和臭氧的味道。造过一只发条猫头鹰、一口自动搅拌坩埚，以及至少一场小火灾。\n\n@npc kael\n# en: Kael Dumont\nname [18aead] = 凯尔·杜蒙\n# en: Transfer student from the frozen North\ntitle [80355d] = 来自冰封北境的转学生\n# en: Stillwater\nhouse [67e7fc] = 静水\n# en: Arrived mid-term with no luggage and frost in his eyelashes. Doesn't talk about where he came from. Watches the sky at night.\ndesc [058f2e] = 学期中途到来，没带行李，睫毛上挂着霜。从不谈起自己从哪里来。夜里总在看天。\n\n@npc nessa\n# en: Nessa Quill\nname [3ad688] = 内莎·奎尔\n# en: Editor of the Lumen Ledger\ntitle [c35a40] = 《流明纪事报》主编\n# en: Duskgate\nhouse [a1e05f] = 暮门\n# en: Runs the student paper with a quill that writes on its own. Everyone is a story; some are just not finished yet.\ndesc [2179de] = 用一支会自己写字的羽毛笔经营着学生报纸。每个人都是一个故事；有些只是还没写完。\n\n@npc bram\n# en: Bram Hollis\nname [182554] = 布拉姆·霍利斯\n# en: Prefect, enforcer, menace\ntitle [2cee1b] = 级长、执法者、祸害\n# en: Emberhall\nhouse [6adebf] = 烬厅\n# en: A prefect of your own year, with a ledger of demerits and a fondness for writing your name in it.\ndesc [44da9b] = 和你同年级的级长，揣着一本记过簿，特别喜欢在上面写你的名字。\n\n@npc thorne\n# en: Professor Aldric Thorne\nname [734bbb] = 奥尔德里克·索恩教授\n# en: Professor Thorne\nshort [fff982] = 索恩教授\n# en: Master of Arcana\ntitle [c13eee] = 奥术大师\n# en: Faculty\nhouse [078d5f] = 教职员\n# en: Severe, exacting, allergic to flattery. Rumoured to have once out-dueled a storm.\ndesc [a1a584] = 严厉、苛刻，对奉承过敏。传说他曾在决斗中赢过一场风暴。\n\n@npc maud\n# en: Old Maud\nname [9c77f5] = 老莫德\n# en: Old Maud\nshort [9c77f5] = 老莫德\n# en: Groundskeeper and hedge-witch\ntitle [e5de9f] = 园丁兼乡野女巫\n# en: Faculty\nhouse [078d5f] = 教职员\n# en: Keeps bees, chickens and a rather large secret garden. Gives advice you only understand two weeks later.\ndesc [8d1e7c] = 养蜂、养鸡，还有一座相当大的秘密花园。她的建议，你往往要过两周才听得懂。\n\n@npc isolde\n# en: Headmistress Isolde Marrow\nname [9e3659] = 伊索尔德·马罗院长\n# en: Headmistress Marrow\nshort [feeebf] = 马罗院长\n# en: Headmistress of Lumenhold\ntitle [b622da] = 流明堡院长\n# en: Faculty\nhouse [078d5f] = 教职员\n# en: Tall, pale and impossibly calm. She remembers every student's name — and every student's mistakes.\ndesc [264ae0] = 高挑、苍白，冷静得不可思议。她记得每个学生的名字——也记得每个学生犯过的错。\n\n@npc ilse\n# en: Ilse Dumont\nname [7e9501] = 伊尔泽·杜蒙\n# en: Kael's younger sister, a storm in white furs\ntitle [8c137b] = 凯尔的妹妹，一场裹着白色毛皮的风暴\n# en: none\nhouse [a7afdb] = 无\n# en: Raised by the Hollow cult to open a door she has never seen. Sharp as an icicle, lonely as a frozen lake, and much younger than she pretends to be.\ndesc [908293] = 被空洞教团养大，只为打开一扇她从未见过的门。锋利如冰锥，孤独如冰封的湖——而且比她装出来的样子年轻得多。\n\n@npc tamsin\n# en: Tamsin Reed\nname [cc4cab] = 塔姆辛·里德\n# en: Tamsin\nshort [4365c9] = 塔姆辛\n# en: Illusionist at Thornwick, your pen pal\ntitle [7ad721] = 荆木学院的幻术学生，你的笔友\n# en: none\nhouse [a7afdb] = 无\n# en: A Thornwick illusionist who answered a letter to \"a Lumenhold student chosen at random\" and kept writing. Insufferable, by their own account, and the best correspondent you have.\ndesc [54270b] = 荆木学院的一名幻术师，回了一封寄给“随机抽中的流明堡学生”的信，然后一直写了下去。按 TA 自己的说法，让人受不了；也是你最好的通信对象。\n\n@npc amaranth\n# en: Sister Amaranth\nname [f35ea5] = 阿玛兰斯修女\n# en: Sister Amaranth\nshort [f35ea5] = 阿玛兰斯修女\n# en: Healer of the infirmary\ntitle [6b950d] = 医务室的治疗师\n# en: Faculty\nhouse [078d5f] = 教职员\n# en: Runs the infirmary with warming charms, tonics and no patience for excuses. Teaches the healing half of Alchemy & Healing.\ndesc [8363a5] = 掌管医务室：暖身咒、补剂，以及对借口零容忍。也教炼金与治愈里治愈的那一半。\n\n@npc brask\n# en: Professor Hedda Brask\nname [dafdd1] = 赫达·布拉斯克教授\n# en: Professor Brask\nshort [a513af] = 布拉斯克教授\n# en: Professor of Beastcraft & Flight\ntitle [af9122] = 驭兽与飞行教授\n# en: Faculty\nhouse [078d5f] = 教职员\n# en: Teaches Beastcraft & Flight and is kinder to animals than to students, though the gap closes for anyone who mucks out without being asked.\ndesc [34945d] = 教驭兽与飞行，对动物比对学生和气；不过谁要是不等吩咐就去铲马厩，这差距就会缩小。\n\n@npc vey\n# en: Professor Ottilie Vey\nname [0ed5bc] = 奥蒂莉·维伊教授\n# en: Professor Vey\nshort [a16fca] = 维伊教授\n# en: Professor of Illusion & Glamour\ntitle [3b7ca9] = 幻术与魅影教授\n# en: Faculty\nhouse [078d5f] = 教职员\n# en: Teaches Illusion & Glamour and grades on whether you fooled her. She is very hard to fool, and very generous to the few who manage it.\ndesc [e5e975] = 教幻术与魅影，按你骗没骗过她来打分。她极难被骗，对少数骗过她的人却极其慷慨。\n\n@npc marisol\n# en: Professor Ines Marisol\nname [36ca1e] = 伊内丝·玛丽索尔教授\n# en: Professor Marisol\nshort [40ede1] = 玛丽索尔教授\n# en: Professor of Tidecalling\ntitle [867c83] = 唤潮术教授\n# en: Faculty\nhouse [078d5f] = 教职员\n# en: Teaches the tidecalling half of Evocation, waist-deep in the lake at dawn. Keeps the lake calm on exam days and reads poetry under a dry patch of air when it rains.\ndesc [9fa4f2] = 教塑能术里唤潮的那一半，黎明时分站在齐腰深的湖水里上课。考试那几天由她让湖面平静；下雨时，她在一小片干燥的空气下读诗。\n\n@npc mabel\n# en: Mabel Thistlewood\nname [472ddc] = 梅布尔·蓟木\n# en: Mabel\nshort [60ac42] = 梅布尔\n# en: Keeper of the tea shop\ntitle [389e2b] = 茶馆老板娘\n# en: none\nhouse [a7afdb] = 无\n# en: Forty kinds of tea, three kinds of cake, and a talent for reading your mood from the way you open her door.\ndesc [5c8604] = 四十种茶，三种蛋糕，还有一样本事：看你怎么推门，就知道你今天心情如何。\n\n@npc grimsby\n# en: Mr Alder Grimsby\nname [be7a57] = 奥尔德·格林斯比先生\n# en: Mr Grimsby\nshort [aa7b23] = 格林斯比先生\n# en: Bookseller\ntitle [c29f43] = 书商\n# en: none\nhouse [a7afdb] = 无\n# en: Keeps a bookshop so crowded the shelves lean on each other. He will find anything except what you came in for, which is often what you needed.\ndesc [bf3215] = 他的书店挤得书架互相倚靠。你要什么他都找得到，唯独找不到你进门要的那本——而那往往正是你需要的。\n\n@npc pell\n# en: Old Pell\nname [345189] = 老佩尔\n# en: Old Pell\nshort [345189] = 老佩尔\n# en: Apothecary of Pell & Daughters\ntitle [c6c952] = 佩尔父女药铺的药剂师\n# en: none\nhouse [a7afdb] = 无\n# en: Taught three generations of the village to count drops. His daughters run the counter now; he runs the drawers, and the drawers are never wrong.\ndesc [5f1bca] = 教会了村里三代人数药滴。如今柜台归他的女儿们管，他管抽屉，而抽屉从不出错。\n\n@npc grisk\n# en: Grisk\nname [9443cd] = 格里斯克\n# en: Grisk\nshort [9443cd] = 格里斯克\n# en: Moneylender, friend to the temporarily embarrassed\ntitle [ad4b4c] = 放贷人，一时手头拮据者的朋友\n# en: none\nhouse [a7afdb] = 无\n# en: A goblin in a velvet waistcoat who lends to students: sixty crowns today, twelve a month for six. He keeps a ledger, and sometimes asks for favours instead of coin.\ndesc [cc2c49] = 穿天鹅绒马甲的地精，专借钱给学生：今天六十克朗，之后六个月每月十二。他有一本账簿，偶尔要人情而不要钱。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/clues.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/clues.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@clue lead_lake_breathed\n# en: The lake held its breath\nname [9bf32e] = 湖屏住了呼吸\n# en: In Mistveil the lake froze for one breath and the drowned bell tolled. Whoever keeps the boathouse has heard that shore for years.\ntext [488b1c] = 雾纱月里，湖面冻结了一口气的工夫，沉钟响了一声。守船屋的人在那片岸边听了许多年。\n\n@clue lead_founders_archive\n# en: What the founders wrote\nname [7110fd] = 创始者写下了什么\n# en: The headmistress told the hall it was nothing. The founders' records in the library archive may disagree, and the Restricted Section keeps what the archive won't.\ntext [6e77dc] = 校长对全场说那不算什么。图书馆档案库里创始者的记录也许另有说法，而档案库不肯留的东西，禁书区会留着。\n\n@clue lead_maud_friend\n# en: Maud lost a friend\nname [13ea1d] = 老莫德失去过一个朋友\n# en: Old Maud was a student the last time the lake froze, and lost a friend to it. She might tell you more, if she trusts you.\ntext [9085dd] = 上一次湖面封冻时，老莫德还是这里的学生，她因此失去了一个朋友。如果她信任你，也许会告诉你更多。\n\n@clue lead_man_in_grey\n# en: A man in grey\nname [44c1ae] = 灰衣人\n# en: A man in a grey coat told Kael to open something, or his sister would. Kael knows what. He'll tell someone he trusts.\ntext [6568f1] = 一个穿灰大衣的男人要凯尔打开某样东西，否则就由他妹妹来。凯尔知道是什么。他会告诉他信任的人。\n\n@clue lead_black_star_dream\n# en: A dream of a black star\nname [077898] = 黑星之梦\n# en: You dreamt of the lake from underneath. Seraphine dreams too, and the observatory sees further than any dream.\ntext [310e35] = 你梦见了从下方看到的湖。瑟拉芬也做这样的梦，而观星台看得比任何梦都远。\n\n@clue lead_seventh_tower\n# en: The seventh tower\nname [0db95c] = 第七塔\n# en: Seven towers, seven locks. One tower has been locked for a century, and something is kept at the top of it.\ntext [4f08ee] = 七座塔，七把锁。其中一座塔已锁了一百年，塔顶藏着什么东西。\n\n@clue lead_white_furs\n# en: A girl in white furs\nname [f6670a] = 白裘少女\n# en: A Northern girl has taken a room above the village bakery. Kael went pale when he heard.\ntext [5dc951] = 一个北方姑娘在村里面包房楼上租了间屋子。凯尔听说后脸都白了。\n\n@clue heard_of_starcalling\n# en: A scraped-out word\nname [9bfbf7] = 被刮掉的词\n# en: One word has been scraped out of the observatory's oldest logs. Students who finished Advanced Astromancy were taught it aloud.\ntext [90a830] = 观星台最老的日志里，有一个词被刮掉了。修完高等星象学的学生，都是被人当面口授这个词的。\n\n@clue founders_ledger\n# en: The razored page\nname [68d0a8] = 被割去的一页\n# en: The founding year was cut out of the Founders' Ledger with a razor, but the pen's impression survived on the page beneath.\ntext [dfbf3b] = 《创始者账簿》里建校那一年被人用刀片割掉了，但笔尖的压痕留在了下面那一页上。\n\n@clue knows_founders_seal\n# en: The founders' seal\nname [44bb6a] = 创始者的封印\n# en: Lumenhold was built as a seal: seven towers, seven locks, and a key shaped like a star. Something sleeps beneath the water.\ntext [934b3b] = 流明堡是作为一道封印建起来的：七座塔，七把锁，还有一把星形的钥匙。有什么东西在水底沉睡。\n\n@clue lake_bell\n# en: The bell under the water\nname [e5ecdf] = 水下的钟\n# en: A drowned bell lies under the lake and tolls when the thing below stirs. A tunnel runs down from the boathouse toward it.\ntext [8955ef] = 一口沉钟躺在湖底，下面的东西一动，它就会响。一条隧道从船屋一路通向它。\n\n@clue second_shadow\n# en: A second shadow\nname [ac1374] = 第二道影子\n# en: Something small and dark crossed the sky the wrong way and stopped directly above the lake.\ntext [49214c] = 一个又小又暗的东西逆着方向划过天空，正好停在湖的上方。\n\n@clue seven_locks\n# en: Seven locks\nname [bb2192] = 七把锁\n# en: One lock in each tower; when a tower goes dark, so does its lock. Locks can be mended. They need hands, and a key.\ntext [8ec40d] = 每座塔里一把锁；一座塔熄了灯，它的锁也就沉寂了。锁可以修补，需要人手，也需要一把钥匙。\n\n@clue star_key\n# en: The star key\nname [213cd3] = 星钥\n# en: A key shaped like a star that holds no light, kept at the top of the seventh tower. The seal can't be renewed without it, or without the founders' words.\ntext [82437c] = 一把星形的钥匙，不含一丝光，藏在第七塔塔顶。没有它，或者没有创始者的咒文，封印就无法重铸。\n\n@clue kael_family\n# en: The Dumonts\nname [6f83b6] = 杜蒙家族\n# en: Kael's family serve the thing under the lake. They sent him to open its door. He came to stop them.\ntext [245d59] = 凯尔的家族侍奉着湖底的那个东西。他们派他来打开那扇门。他却是来阻止他们的。\n\n@clue ilse_truth\n# en: What Ilse was raised for\nname [48407c] = 伊尔泽被养大的目的\n# en: The Dumonts raised Kael's sister to open the door if he wouldn't. Nobody ever asked her if she wanted to.\ntext [6eb048] = 杜蒙家把凯尔的妹妹养大，就是为了在凯尔不肯时由她去开门。从来没人问过她愿不愿意。\n\n@clue codex_names\n# en: The Codex of Hollow Names\nname [b71797] = 《空洞名录》\n# en: A book in the Restricted Section lists everyone the Star has noticed. The last name is still being written, and it is yours.\ntext [3e42ac] = 禁书区里有一本书，列着所有被那颗星注意到的人。最后一个名字还在写，那是你的名字。\n\n@clue bell_heard\n# en: A bell where none is\nname [d030c0] = 无人之处的钟声\n# en: The drowned bell rang in the night with no one near it, and nobody else woke.\ntext [233601] = 夜里沉钟响了，附近一个人也没有，而别人都没有醒。\n\n@clue bell_rope\n# en: The cut rope\nname [581c10] = 被割断的绳\n# en: Maud has kept the bell's rope for sixty years. It came up the night the lake froze, cut clean.\ntext [6af71a] = 老莫德把那口钟的绳子留了六十年。湖面封冻的那一夜它浮了上来，断口齐整。\n\n@clue bell_name\n# en: The name on the rim\nname [3ef04f] = 钟沿上的名字\n# en: Letters run around the bell's rim on the lakebed: a name you don't know, and now keep.\ntext [05aeb6] = 湖底那口钟的边沿上刻着一圈字母：一个你不认识的名字，如今你记住了。\n\n@clue black_seal_letters\n# en: Letters under a black seal\nname [cd05c9] = 黑色火漆的信\n# en: House Vaneshade writes to Lyra under its black seal. Her mother asks who beat her, never how she is.\ntext [9609b0] = 凡妮希德家用黑色火漆给莱拉写信。她母亲只问是谁赢了她，从不问她过得好不好。\n\n@clue lead_moonpetal\n# en: The cold that won't leave\nthread [fcb8af] = 挥之不去的寒意\n# en: Moonpetal\nname [676f04] = 月瓣花\n# en: Old Maud says the cure is a moonpetal from the Whisperwood, which blooms only after dark, and that you have to fetch it yourself.\ntext [7cd738] = 老莫德说，解药是低语森林里的一朵月瓣花，只在天黑后开放，而且必须由你亲手去采。\n\n@clue lead_second_timetable\n# en: The unsigned note\nthread [41bfa1] = 没有署名的字条\n# en: A second timetable\nname [efd783] = 第二张时间表\n# en: Someone slipped a note under your door: the castle has a second timetable, and it starts at midnight. Whoever wrote it knows the passages.\ntext [631b45] = 有人从你门缝塞进一张字条：城堡还有第二张时间表，从午夜开始。写字条的人熟悉那些暗道。\n\n@clue lead_grisk_jetty\n# en: Grisk's parcels\nthread [c5fea9] = 格里斯克的包裹\n# en: The jetty at midnight\nname [8b40af] = 午夜的栈桥\n# en: Grisk's arrangement: a parcel collected at the boathouse jetty at midnight, carried to a cobbler's back door in the village by day.\ntext [6eb586] = 格里斯克的安排：午夜在船屋栈桥取一个包裹，白天送到村里一家鞋匠铺的后门。\n\n@clue lead_sky_championship\n# en: The Skyrace team\nthread [dc4a0d] = 天空竞速队\n# en: The championship\nname [b9ac19] = 锦标赛\n# en: You made the team. The championship against Thornwick is flown at the flight field later in the year.\ntext [bc0a04] = 你进了校队。对荆木学院的锦标赛，会在今年晚些时候于飞行场举行。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/factions.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/factions.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@faction wardens\n# en: The Wardens\nname [2a89ea] = 守望者\n# en: The headmistress's quiet circle, sworn to keep the founders' seal. They recruit students only when the seal grows thin.\ndesc [f1e201] = 院长身边一个安静的小圈子，誓守创始人的封印。只有在封印变薄时，他们才会招募学生。\n\n@faction hollow_cult\n# en: The Hollow Cult\nname [b781ad] = 空洞教团\n# en: Northern families, the Dumonts first among them, who serve the star beneath the lake. Their messenger is the man in grey.\ndesc [25011f] = 侍奉湖底之星的北境家族，杜蒙家首当其冲。他们的信使就是灰衣人。\n\n@faction midnight_cartographers\n# en: The Midnight Cartographers\nname [40c563] = 午夜制图社\n# en: The club that doesn't exist: students who map the castle's secret passages and share them with almost no one.\ndesc [14391b] = 那个不存在的社团：一群绘制城堡秘密通道、却几乎从不与人分享的学生。\n\n@faction faculty\n# en: The Faculty\nname [5b97ac] = 教职员\n# en: The professors and staff of Lumenhold: strict, eccentric, and mostly on your side whether you like it or not.\ndesc [645f6f] = 流明堡的教授与职员：严格、古怪，而且不管你乐不乐意，大多站在你这一边。\n\n@faction conclave\n# en: The Conclave of the Seven Towers\nname [c832a4] = 七塔议会\n# en: The governing body of magic in the realm, headquartered in the capital. Hands out research posts, licences and very long letters.\ndesc [76fd4f] = 王国魔法界的管理机构，总部设在王都。负责分配研究职位、颁发执照，以及寄来非常长的信。\n\n@faction thornwick\n# en: Thornwick Academy\nname [74b242] = 荆木学院\n# en: The rival school across the hills. Famous for illusionists, tournament grudges and spies who think they're subtle.\ndesc [28e5ca] = 山那边的对手学校。以幻术师、锦标赛积怨，以及自以为很隐蔽的间谍闻名。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/facts/amaranth.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/facts/amaranth.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@fact amaranth.healer\n# en: Runs the infirmary and teaches the healing half of Alchemy & Healing. Believes no excuse.\ntext [63b5e6] = 掌管医务室，教炼金与治愈里治愈的那一半。任何借口她都不信。\n\n@fact amaranth.truth\n# en: She treats the true version of an injury faster than the story you tell her, and she always knows which one you told.\ntext [76020a] = 你说出伤的真实来历，她治得比听你编的故事更快；而你说的是哪一种，她总是知道。\n\n@fact amaranth.rounds\n# en: She does evening rounds of every bed herself, and has never once missed one.\ntext [8c9554] = 每晚的查房她都亲自走遍每张床，从未落下过一次。\n\n@fact amaranth.key\n# en: Only a handful of students have ever been trusted with the key to her tonic cupboard.\ntext [5f5b57] = 被托付过她补剂柜钥匙的学生，屈指可数。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/facts/bram.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/facts/bram.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@fact bram.prefect\n# en: A prefect of your own year, with a red sash, a ledger of demerits and a fondness for your name.\ntext [6bfca0] = 和你同年级的级长，系着红绶带，揣着一本记过簿，特别喜欢往上写你的名字。\n\n@fact bram.father\n# en: His father was head boy, and has told Bram all his life that he will never amount to anything.\ntext [414950] = 他父亲当年是男生学生会主席，一辈子都在告诉布拉姆他不会有出息。\n\n@fact bram.hall_tax\n# en: He once invented a \"hall tax\" for the east staircase.\ntext [67e9e9] = 他曾为东侧楼梯发明过一项“走廊税”。\n\n@fact bram.cheating\n# en: You saw him cheat in an exam. He knows you saw.\ntext [2e1460] = 你看见他考试作弊。他知道你看见了。\n\n@fact bram.ally\n# en: Under the sash, he will step between you and trouble. Then he tells you not to make it weird.\ntext [e7ac5a] = 在那条绶带底下，他会挡在你和麻烦之间。然后叫你别把气氛搞得奇怪。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/facts/brask.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/facts/brask.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@fact brask.beasts\n# en: Teaches Beastcraft & Flight from the stables, and talks to every creature in them more kindly than to students.\ntext [267a74] = 在马厩里教驭兽与飞行，对里面每一只生灵说话都比对学生和气。\n\n@fact brask.stories\n# en: Every one of her stories ends with a bite and a moral. She laughs out loud about once a term.\ntext [cbc9ea] = 她讲的每个故事都以被咬一口和一个道理收尾。她大约一学期大笑一次。\n\n@fact brask.owl\n# en: The old barn owl in the stable loft has outlived three of her colleagues. She would not say which loss hurt most.\ntext [88c4c3] = 马厩阁楼上那只老仓鸮，比她的三位同事都活得长。哪一次失去最让她难过，她不肯说。\n\n@fact brask.courtesies\n# en: She bows to the things that live in the Whisperwood, and believes it's why they have never bitten her. Much.\ntext [ce36dc] = 她会向住在低语森林里的东西鞠躬，并相信正因如此它们从没咬过她。基本没有。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/facts/corvin.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/facts/corvin.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@fact corvin.locks\n# en: Knows every locked door in the academy and owes money to at least three people.\ntext [8de015] = 学院里每一扇锁着的门他都知道，而且至少欠着三个人的钱。\n\n@fact corvin.last_warning\n# en: He is on his last warning. One more detention and they send him home, and home is not a good place.\ntext [b33ba6] = 他已经是最后一次警告了。再被罚一次禁闭，就会被送回家——而家，不是个好地方。\n\n@fact corvin.brother\n# en: His brother turns up for money and threatens to tell the headmistress where Corvin learned to pick locks.\ntext [bb32a8] = 他哥哥会找上门来要钱，还威胁要告诉院长科尔文是在哪儿学会撬锁的。\n\n@fact corvin.grandfather\n# en: His grandfather's lucky deck sat in the confiscation vault for decades. It really is lucky.\ntext [26a338] = 他祖父的幸运牌在没收品库里躺了几十年。那副牌是真的幸运。\n\n@fact corvin.cartographers\n# en: He runs the Midnight Cartographers, the club that doesn't exist.\ntext [d80fd1] = 他是午夜制图社的头儿——那个不存在的社团。\n\n@fact corvin.trolls\n# en: He once owed thirty crowns to trolls and offered to pay you back with a goat.\ntext [976180] = 他曾欠巨魔三十克朗，还提出用一只山羊来还你钱。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/facts/grimsby.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/facts/grimsby.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@fact grimsby.books\n# en: Keeps the village bookshop. He will find anything except what you came in for.\ntext [034041] = 开着村里的书店。你要什么他都找得到，唯独找不到你进门要的那本。\n\n@fact grimsby.wrong\n# en: The book he gives you instead is, more often than not, the one you needed.\ntext [2be71b] = 他塞给你的那本“错书”，多半正是你需要的。\n\n@fact grimsby.parcels\n# en: He posts books to students he thinks need them, on credit, and has never once chased a debt.\ntext [5362f8] = 他会把他觉得学生需要的书赊账寄过去，从没催过一次债。\n\n@fact grimsby.lighthouses\n# en: He has a weakness for lighthouses, and when he closes the shop, it is usually to go and look at one.\ntext [f1d0ca] = 他对灯塔情有独钟；他关店的时候，通常就是去看某座灯塔了。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/facts/grisk.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/facts/grisk.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@fact grisk.lender\n# en: A goblin moneylender in a velvet waistcoat: sixty crowns today, twelve a month for six months.\ntext [023206] = 穿天鹅绒马甲的地精放贷人：今天六十克朗，之后六个月每月十二。\n\n@fact grisk.ledger\n# en: He calls debts \"friendships\", and keeps all of them in one ledger he never lets out of reach.\ntext [fa5464] = 他把债叫作“交情”，全都记在一本从不离手的账簿里。\n\n@fact grisk.favours\n# en: He sometimes asks for favours instead of coin, and the favours are for clients he never names.\ntext [a745d9] = 他有时要人情而不要钱，而那些人情是替他从不透露姓名的客户讨的。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/facts/ilse.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/facts/ilse.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@fact ilse.furs\n# en: Kael's younger sister: white furs, a private snowstorm and coins that freeze to counters.\ntext [e7eab1] = 凯尔的妹妹：一身白裘，一场随身的私人小暴风雪，还有会冻在柜台上的硬币。\n\n@fact ilse.raised\n# en: The Hollow cult raised her to open a door she has never seen.\ntext [a006d7] = 空洞教团把她养大，要她去打开一扇她从没见过的门。\n\n@fact ilse.frost_craft\n# en: She knows Northern frost-craft better than anything the professors teach.\ntext [ac2fad] = 她对北境霜术的了解，胜过教授们教的任何东西。\n\n@fact ilse.grey\n# en: The man in grey came to take her home. She didn't call for help; she only looked.\ntext [f8b96d] = 灰衣人来带她回家。她没有呼救，只是看着。\n\n@fact ilse.scared\n# en: She admits, to her brother, that she was scared. So was he.\ntext [fe62fb] = 她向哥哥承认，她当时很害怕。他也是。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/facts/isolde.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/facts/isolde.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@fact isolde.names\n# en: She remembers every student's name, and every student's mistakes.\ntext [00ea88] = 她记得每个学生的名字，也记得每个学生犯过的错。\n\n@fact isolde.portrait\n# en: As a student she set the observatory on fire twice. Her portrait from those days is hidden in a forgotten corridor.\ntext [389493] = 她当学生时，两次把观星台点着了。那时的肖像被藏在一条被遗忘的走廊里。\n\n@fact isolde.chess\n# en: She plays tower chess by her window and laughs, short and delighted, when she loses.\ntext [29f019] = 她在窗边下水晶塔棋，输了的时候会短促而愉快地笑出声。\n\n@fact isolde.thread\n# en: She has been following the same thread you have: the seal beneath the lake is weakening.\ntext [db9906] = 她一直在追查和你同一条线索：湖底的封印正在变弱。\n\n@fact isolde.mercy\n# en: She asks students what they think is just, and sometimes she listens.\ntext [d24034] = 她会问学生，他们认为怎样才算公正——有时，她会听。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/facts/kael.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/facts/kael.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@fact kael.north\n# en: A transfer from the frozen North who arrived mid-term with frost in his eyelashes.\ntext [c365b9] = 来自冰封北境的转学生，学期中途抵达，睫毛上挂着霜。\n\n@fact kael.cold_hands\n# en: His hands are always cold. Nobody has ever been warm enough before.\ntext [4b3405] = 他的手总是冰凉。以前从没有人暖到足以让它们回温。\n\n@fact kael.sky\n# en: He watches a patch of sky with no stars in it: a star that eats light.\ntext [1206d5] = 他凝望着一片没有星星的夜空：一颗吞噬光的星。\n\n@fact kael.family\n# en: His family serve something under the lake. They sent him to open its door; he came to stop them.\ntext [cfe636] = 他的家族侍奉着湖底的某样东西。他们派他来打开那扇门；他来，是为了阻止他们。\n\n@fact kael.sister\n# en: He has a younger sister, Ilse, raised to do what he wouldn't.\ntext [8def5c] = 他有一个妹妹，伊尔泽，被培养去做他不肯做的事。\n\n@fact kael.mother\n# en: His mother never once said she was proud of him.\ntext [a4117c] = 他的母亲从没说过一次为他骄傲。\n\n@fact kael.wanting\n# en: He was taught not to want things, because wanting is how they control you.\ntext [34ad8d] = 他被教导不要想要任何东西，因为想要，就是他们控制你的方式。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/facts/lyra.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/facts/lyra.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@fact lyra.prodigy\n# en: The pyromancy prodigy of House Vaneshade. Everyone knows the name, and she makes sure of it.\ntext [7f3f8a] = 凡妮希德家族的火焰术天才。人人都知道这个名字，她也确保人人都知道。\n\n@fact lyra.toast\n# en: She can incinerate a target at forty paces. She cannot make toast.\ntext [8eef62] = 她能在四十步外把靶子烧成灰。她不会烤面包片。\n\n@fact lyra.mother\n# en: Her mother has never once told her \"well done\".\ntext [a56886] = 她的母亲从没对她说过一句“做得好”。\n\n@fact lyra.second_place\n# en: She came second in the Winter Trials once. Her mother wrote to ask who beat her.\ntext [c586c0] = 她在冬季试炼中得过一次第二名。她母亲写信来问是谁赢了她。\n\n@fact lyra.burnout\n# en: She studies until she collapses, and begs you not to let her mother find out.\ntext [8e25bd] = 她会一直学到昏过去，还求你别让她母亲知道。\n\n@fact lyra.rival\n# en: You are the only person here who makes her better. She hates it. She doesn't want it to stop.\ntext [049900] = 你是这里唯一能让她变得更好的人。她恨这一点。她不想让它停下。\n\n@fact lyra.inheritance\n# en: Her mother threatened her inheritance over you. She set the letter on fire.\ntext [b8b62f] = 她母亲为了你，拿她的继承权来威胁她。她把那封信烧了。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/facts/mabel.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/facts/mabel.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@fact mabel.teashop\n# en: Keeps the tea shop in the village: forty kinds of tea, three kinds of cake.\ntext [c5dd16] = 在村里开茶馆：四十种茶，三种蛋糕。\n\n@fact mabel.door\n# en: She reads your mood from the way you open her door, and is rarely wrong.\ntext [33ce2b] = 看你怎么推她的门，就知道你的心情，而且很少看错。\n\n@fact mabel.tins\n# en: She sends tins of tea up to the castle on the barge for students she's worried about, and refuses payment for them.\ntext [390792] = 她会用驳船把一罐罐茶送上城堡，给她放心不下的学生，而且坚决不收钱。\n\n@fact mabel.cups\n# en: The cups on the hooks behind her counter belong to her regulars, living and dead. She remembers every one of them.\ntext [3e39fe] = 她柜台后挂钩上的杯子属于她的常客，有活着的，也有已故的。每一位她都记得。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/facts/marisol.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/facts/marisol.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@fact marisol.tides\n# en: Teaches the tidecalling half of Evocation, waist-deep in the lake at dawn, and keeps the lake calm on exam days.\ntext [5356ef] = 教塑能术里唤潮的那一半，黎明时站在齐腰深的湖水里上课；考试那几天由她让湖面平静。\n\n@fact marisol.poems\n# en: She reads poems about water in the rain, under a dry patch of air, and never remembers their last lines.\ntext [936059] = 她在雨里、在一小片干燥的空气下读写水的诗，却从来记不住最后一行。\n\n@fact marisol.storms\n# en: She goes out into the worst storms of the year on purpose. She says nobody learns tides on a calm day.\ntext [2d8f05] = 她专挑一年里最猛的暴风雨出门。她说风平浪静的日子里，谁也学不会潮汐。\n\n@fact marisol.margins\n# en: She wrote her own tidecalling into the margins of a book of poems when she was a student, and still teaches from it.\ntext [2b7267] = 她还是学生时，把自己的唤潮术写在一本诗集的页边，至今仍照着它教。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/facts/maud.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/facts/maud.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@fact maud.groundskeeper\n# en: Groundskeeper and hedge-witch, keeper of bees, chickens and a goat called Bartholomew.\ntext [54cb93] = 园丁兼乡野女巫，养蜂、养鸡，还有一只叫巴塞洛缪的山羊。\n\n@fact maud.student\n# en: She was a student here sixty years ago, the spring the lake froze.\ntext [5b3f12] = 六十年前她也是这里的学生——就在湖面结冰的那个春天。\n\n@fact maud.edmund\n# en: Edmund went down beneath the lake that night and didn't come back. She kept his silver wand.\ntext [624c6b] = 那天夜里埃德蒙下到湖底，再没回来。她一直留着他的银魔杖。\n\n@fact maud.soup\n# en: Her soup cures colds, broken hearts and most curses. You're not allowed to write it down.\ntext [395a7c] = 她的汤能治感冒、心碎和大多数诅咒。不许写下来。\n\n@fact maud.garden\n# en: Behind the potting shed there is a door to a garden that only exists for people she shows it to.\ntext [408faa] = 盆栽棚后面有一扇门，通往一座只为她领进去的人而存在的花园。\n\n@fact maud.age\n# en: She is eighty-one, and says she's allowed the occasional chill.\ntext [601a9c] = 她八十一岁了，说自己偶尔着个凉是被允许的。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/facts/mir.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/facts/mir.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@fact mir.artificer\n# en: An artificer who smells of solder and ozone and has started at least one small fire.\ntext [6f21ba] = 一个浑身焊锡和臭氧味的奇械师，至少引发过一场小火灾。\n\n@fact mir.owl\n# en: Built a clockwork owl that remembers deadlines, birthdays and, slightly, grudges.\ntext [841167] = 造过一只发条猫头鹰，能记住截止日期、生日，还有一点点的仇。\n\n@fact mir.notebooks\n# en: Three years of notebooks are the most precious things they own.\ntext [9a3403] = 三年的笔记本是 TA 最珍贵的东西。\n\n@fact mir.family\n# en: Their family runs a clockworks and thinks invention is a hobby. It is not a hobby.\ntext [91fa32] = TA 家开着一间钟表工坊，觉得发明只是个爱好。这不是爱好。\n\n@fact mir.name_day\n# en: Nobody ever remembered their name day before.\ntext [40158a] = 以前从没有人记得 TA 的命名日。\n\n@fact mir.workshop\n# en: After the third fire in a term, the faculty padlocked their workshop.\ntext [14af34] = 一个学期里第三次失火之后，教职员给 TA 的工坊上了挂锁。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/facts/nessa.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/facts/nessa.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@fact nessa.editor\n# en: Editor of the Lumen Ledger, with a quill that writes on its own.\ntext [e5df5e] = 《流明纪事报》的主编，有一支会自己写字的羽毛笔。\n\n@fact nessa.quill\n# en: Left unsupervised, her quill starts inventing the news.\ntext [e4c012] = 一旦没人看着，她的羽毛笔就会开始编新闻。\n\n@fact nessa.interview\n# en: Nobody has interviewed the headmistress in twenty years. She means to be the first.\ntext [0599a6] = 二十年来没有人采访过院长。她打算做第一个。\n\n@fact nessa.string\n# en: Her office wall is covered in red string around a sketch of a black star.\ntext [98be58] = 她办公室的墙上缠满了红线，正中间是一颗黑色星星的草图。\n\n@fact nessa.press\n# en: She would chain herself to the printing press before letting anyone shut the Ledger.\ntext [c72444] = 谁要是想关掉《流明纪事报》，她宁可把自己锁在印刷机上。\n\n@fact nessa.off_record\n# en: The only time her quill stops is when she tells it the conversation is off the record.\ntext [9ac8f2] = 她的羽毛笔唯一停下的时候，是她告诉它这段对话不作记录。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/facts/pell.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/facts/pell.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@fact pell.apothecary\n# en: Runs the drawers at Pell & Daughters. He taught three generations of the village to count drops.\ntext [0b856c] = 管着佩尔父女药铺的抽屉。他教会了村里三代人数药滴。\n\n@fact pell.counting\n# en: He makes every new clerk count aloud. He trusts people who measure twice.\ntext [4c5e9c] = 每个新店员都得被他逼着大声数数。他信得过量两遍的人。\n\n@fact pell.daughters\n# en: His daughters run the counter and want the shop to change; he wants it to be fifty years ago. They're all secretly proud of each other.\ntext [4a27be] = 女儿们管柜台，想让铺子变一变；他却希望一切停在五十年前。其实三个人都暗暗以彼此为傲。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/facts/seraphine.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/facts/seraphine.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@fact seraphine.seer\n# en: A seer who speaks in half-sentences and is unsettlingly often right.\ntext [8fee9c] = 一个说话总说半句的先知，准得叫人心里发毛。\n\n@fact seraphine.slips\n# en: Sometimes she slips a day ahead and needs someone to tell her what today is.\ntext [6bdae8] = 有时她会滑到明天去，需要有人告诉她今天是哪一天。\n\n@fact seraphine.oracle\n# en: Her mother is the Oracle of Vell, who wants her home to tell kings what they want to hear.\ntext [e701e0] = 她的母亲是维尔神谕，想让她回家，去对国王们说他们爱听的话。\n\n@fact seraphine.self\n# en: Seers can't see themselves, unless someone is standing next to them.\ntext [90e477] = 先知看不见自己——除非有人站在他们身边。\n\n@fact seraphine.lake\n# en: The lake calls to her at night. She says it calls to you too.\ntext [37ecd3] = 夜里，湖在呼唤她。她说，它也在呼唤你。\n\n@fact seraphine.too_loud\n# en: When everyone's futures get too loud she hides in the dark. Yours, she says, is quiet.\ntext [9cdeb7] = 当所有人的未来吵得太响时，她会躲进黑暗里。她说，你的未来很安静。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/facts/tamsin.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/facts/tamsin.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@fact tamsin.thornwick\n# en: An illusion student at Thornwick, in your year, who writes to you.\ntext [c2878b] = 荆木学院幻术专业的学生，与你同年级，一直给你写信。\n\n@fact tamsin.theatre\n# en: Their grandmother runs a travelling theatre; they learned illusion painting her backdrops.\ntext [93eb25] = TA 的祖母经营一个巡回剧团；TA 是在给她画布景时学会幻术的。\n\n@fact tamsin.assigned\n# en: Thornwick told them to write to a Lumenhold student and report back. They stopped reporting by the third letter.\ntext [3ef61a] = 荆木学院让 TA 写信给一名流明堡学生，并回去汇报。到第三封信时，TA 就不再汇报了。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/facts/thorne.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/facts/thorne.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@fact thorne.storm\n# en: Rumoured to have once out-duelled a storm.\ntext [fa9226] = 据说他曾在决斗中打败过一场风暴。\n\n@fact thorne.lost\n# en: He lost to the storm, badly. Losing well, he says, is the only thing worth teaching.\ntext [f094e4] = 他输给了那场风暴，输得很惨。他说，好好地输，是唯一值得教的东西。\n\n@fact thorne.roses\n# en: He tends a tiny walled rose garden behind the observatory at dawn and talks to the roses.\ntext [ec7ccc] = 黎明时分，他在观星台后面一座小小的围墙玫瑰园里打理花草，还对玫瑰说话。\n\n@fact thorne.hands\n# en: His hands have started to shake. Age comes for every storm eventually.\ntext [95c9a9] = 他的手开始发抖了。岁月终究会找上每一场风暴。\n\n@fact thorne.rival\n# en: A flamboyant old wizard in purple has wanted a rematch with him for forty years.\ntext [054b17] = 一个穿紫袍、作派浮夸的老巫师，四十年来一直想跟他再比一场。\n\n@fact thorne.letters\n# en: He writes very few letters of recommendation, and the Conclave reads every one.\ntext [067d68] = 他很少写推荐信，而七塔议会每一封都会读。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/facts/tobias.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/facts/tobias.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@fact tobias.scholarship\n# en: A farmer's son on a turnip scholarship, built like a barn door and gentle as a lamb.\ntext [2b6d69] = 一个拿芜菁奖学金的农家子弟，块头像谷仓门，性子像小羊羔。\n\n@fact tobias.plants\n# en: He talks to plants. Worryingly, they seem to answer.\ntext [e42ce7] = 他会跟植物说话。令人担心的是，它们似乎会回答。\n\n@fact tobias.ma\n# en: His mother runs the farm alone at harvest. It's the first harvest he has ever missed.\ntext [b4dd4f] = 收获季节，他母亲一个人打理农场。这是他第一次错过收获。\n\n@fact tobias.gerald\n# en: He is raising a mandrake called Gerald, who wears a knitted hat and screams softly.\ntext [f5ffc4] = 他在养一株叫杰拉尔德的曼德拉草，戴着一顶针织小帽，会轻声尖叫。\n\n@fact tobias.moonflowers\n# en: The greenhouse moonflowers bloom once every eleven years. He would stand in a storm for them.\ntext [047bca] = 温室里的月光花每十一年才开一次。为了它们，他愿意站在暴风雨里。\n\n@fact tobias.blight\n# en: Blight took his family's harvest. Thirty crowns stood between them and a hungry winter.\ntext [15f8ba] = 枯萎病毁了他家的收成。他们和一个挨饿的冬天之间，只隔着三十克朗。\n\n@fact tobias.dancing\n# en: He practised waltzing with a broom before the Midwinter Ball. The broom complained.\ntext [3db4ab] = 冬至舞会前，他拿一把扫帚练华尔兹。扫帚提出了抗议。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/facts/vey.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/facts/vey.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@fact vey.illusion\n# en: Teaches Illusion & Glamour and grades on whether you fooled her.\ntext [c1fb18] = 教幻术与魅影，按你骗没骗过她来打分。\n\n@fact vey.tests\n# en: She tests students in corridors, unannounced, with tea that isn't there. She keeps score.\ntext [60f4e6] = 她会在走廊里突击考学生，用并不存在的茶。她一直在记分。\n\n@fact vey.revue\n# en: She produces the faculty revue every year, and has never once let Professor Thorne refuse a part.\ntext [d5edf4] = 她每年都负责排演教职员汇演，从没让索恩教授推掉过一个角色。\n\n@fact vey.mirror\n# en: She doesn't use the mirror charm in public. Once, her reflection didn't come back for a week.\ntext [950435] = 她从不在人前用镜像咒。有一次，她的倒影整整一周没回来。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/places.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/places.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@place courtyard\n# en: The Courtyard\nname [1b8037] = 庭院\n# en: Flagstones worn smooth by three centuries of students, a fountain that freezes when it shouldn't, and the best gossip in the castle.\ndesc [d60535] = 被三百年学生踩得光滑的石板，一座总在不该结冰时结冰的喷泉，以及全城堡最好的八卦。\n\n@place library\n# en: The Library\nname [64e91f] = 图书馆\n# en: Seven floors of shelves that rearrange themselves when nobody is looking. Footnote the cat keeps order.\ndesc [9e9a17] = 七层书架，趁没人注意时自己重新排列。图书馆猫“脚注”负责维持秩序。\n\n@place refectory\n# en: The Refectory\nname [0b96c1] = 膳堂\n# en: Long tables, longer benches, and soup that tastes faintly of whatever you're homesick for.\ndesc [289d61] = 长桌，更长的长凳，还有一种汤——它尝起来隐约像你正在想念的那个地方。\n\n@place lakeshore\n# en: The Lakeshore\nname [6c0614] = 湖岸\n# en: The Mirror Lake lies so still it shows the towers upside down. On some nights it shows other things.\ndesc [733f61] = 镜湖静得能倒映出倒立的塔楼。有些夜晚，它倒映出的是别的东西。\n\n@place village\n# en: The Village\nname [973da4] = 山下小镇\n# en: A barge ride across the lake: crooked roofs, a market square and shopkeepers who have seen every kind of student.\ndesc [419536] = 乘驳船渡过湖面：歪斜的屋顶、集市广场，还有见识过各种学生的店主们。\n\n@place lecture_tower\n# en: The Lecture Tower\nname [d37b7f] = 讲堂塔\n# en: Spiral lecture halls stacked one above another. Evocation on the lower floors, where scorch marks are expected; Umbral Theory at the top, where the lamps burn dim.\ndesc [3bff12] = 一层叠一层的螺旋讲堂。塑能术在底层——那里的焦痕是意料之中的事；暗影理论在顶层，灯光昏暗。\n\n@place observatory\n# en: The Observatory\nname [202edc] = 观星台\n# en: A brass dome at the top of the east tower. The great telescope points wherever it likes; usually that is the right place.\ndesc [4813c0] = 东塔顶上的黄铜穹顶。大望远镜想指向哪儿就指向哪儿——通常，那正是该看的地方。\n\n@place great_hall\n# en: The Great Hall\nname [e97d10] = 大礼堂\n# en: Floating candles, a vaulted ceiling that shows tomorrow's weather, and the stage where the choir rehearses and the Midwinter Ball is held.\ndesc [eb7d92] = 漂浮的蜡烛，能显示明天天气的拱顶，还有合唱团排练、冬至舞会举办的舞台。\n\n@place infirmary\n# en: The Infirmary\nname [b3b383] = 医务室\n# en: Clean white beds, a cupboard of tonics and Sister Amaranth, who has seen every kind of injury and believes none of the excuses.\ndesc [80a36a] = 洁白的床铺，一柜子补药，还有阿玛兰斯修女——她见过各种伤，却一个借口都不信。\n\n@place alchemy_lab\n# en: The Alchemy Laboratory\nname [333e04] = 炼金实验室\n# en: Long benches, cauldrons in every size, and a ventilation charm that has given up on the third floor.\ndesc [9767f5] = 长条工作台，大大小小的坩埚，还有一道在三楼早已放弃抵抗的通风咒。\n\n@place workshop\n# en: Mir's Workshop\nname [4b161e] = 米尔的工坊\n# en: A former classroom full of springs, gears, half-finished machines and the smell of solder. Fire buckets by the door, on the faculty's orders.\ndesc [fed5e7] = 一间旧教室，堆满弹簧、齿轮、半成品机器和焊锡味。门边按教职员的要求摆着几只灭火桶。\n# en: Something clanks and hums behind the west-wing tapestry.\nhint [507a91] = 西翼挂毯后面，有什么东西在叮当作响、嗡嗡低鸣。\n\n@place job_board\n# en: The Job Board\nname [377c1b] = 招工告示板\n# en: A cork board in the entrance hall layered with notices: jobs, lost familiars, club recruitment and the occasional ransom note for a sock.\ndesc [4e9473] = 门厅里的一块软木板，层层叠叠贴满告示：招工、走失的使魔、社团招新，偶尔还有一封某只袜子的赎金信。\n\n@place ledger_office\n# en: The Lumen Ledger Office\nname [fdd2aa] = 《流明纪事报》编辑部\n# en: A cramped room under the library stairs, walls papered with old front pages. The printing press groans like a dragon on deadline night.\ndesc [40d726] = 图书馆楼梯下一间逼仄的小屋，墙上贴满旧头版。截稿之夜，印刷机呻吟得像一条赶工的龙。\n\n@place headmistress_tower\n# en: The Headmistress's Tower\nname [735fdf] = 院长塔\n# en: Books, tea and a board of crystal towers by the window. You are only here because she wants you here.\ndesc [a5fc77] = 书、茶，还有窗边一盘水晶塔棋。你会在这里，只因为她想让你在这里。\n# en: A spiral stair guarded by a stone owl that only opens its eyes for some students.\nhint [49a3ae] = 一道由石头猫头鹰把守的螺旋楼梯——它只为某些学生睁开眼睛。\n\n@place restricted_section\n# en: The Restricted Section\nname [b1cf8a] = 禁书区\n# en: Black-bound books that breathe in the dark. Some of them read you back.\ndesc [9d32a6] = 在黑暗中呼吸的黑皮书。有些书会反过来读你。\n# en: A door at the back of the library that is always locked, and always slightly warm.\nhint [16cc43] = 图书馆最里面有一扇门，总是锁着，而且总是微微发烫。\n\n@place gargoyle_passage\n# en: The Third Gargoyle\nname [e05e0b] = 第三座石像鬼\n# en: Behind the third gargoyle on the north parapet, a passage that isn't on any map. Except theirs.\ndesc [e1c46f] = 北侧护墙上第三座石像鬼背后，有一条任何地图上都没有的通道。除了他们的地图。\n# en: Corvin keeps glancing at a particular gargoyle.\nhint [b7dd0a] = 科尔文总忍不住瞟向某一座石像鬼。\n\n@place seventh_tower\n# en: The Seventh Tower\nname [44b8dc] = 第七塔\n# en: Locked for a century. At the very top, one room of dust and moonlight, and a star-shaped hollow in the wall.\ndesc [f3b2a5] = 已经锁了一个世纪。塔顶只有一间屋子：灰尘、月光，以及墙上一个星形的凹槽。\n# en: A locked stair that leads up into a tower nobody admits is there.\nhint [5d54eb] = 一道上锁的楼梯，通往一座没人承认存在的塔。\n\n@place thesis_lab\n# en: The Thesis Laboratory\nname [92f1a5] = 论文实验室\n# en: Rows of carrels where final-year students and their advisors argue about footnotes until the candles give out.\ndesc [136592] = 一排排研读隔间，毕业年级的学生和导师在这里为脚注争论，直到蜡烛燃尽。\n\n@place duel_hall\n# en: The Duelling Hall\nname [481ae1] = 决斗厅\n# en: Scorched floorboards and a ceiling that has seen things. The practice dummies have names, and grudges.\ndesc [5bea7b] = 烧焦的地板，和一块见过太多场面的天花板。练习用的假人都有名字，也都记仇。\n# en: Steel rings somewhere above the east stair.\nhint [96a1b0] = 东侧楼梯上方，某处传来金铁交鸣。\n\n@place greenhouses\n# en: The Greenhouses\nname [27cc8e] = 温室\n# en: Glass houses that steam even in winter. The moonflowers bloom once every eleven years; the mandrakes have opinions every day.\ndesc [b1e233] = 冬天也冒着热气的玻璃房。月光花每十一年才开一次；曼德拉草每天都有意见。\n\n@place flight_field\n# en: The Flight Field\nname [ee78ba] = 飞行场\n# en: A long green field with hoops at either end and a wind that cheats. Broom lessons, races and spectacular landings.\ndesc [1c581a] = 一片狭长的草场，两端各立着圆环，风总爱作弊。飞帚课、竞速，以及各种壮观的着陆。\n\n@place stables\n# en: The Familiar Stables\nname [151887] = 使魔马厩\n# en: Hippogriffs, a sulky griffon chick, owls in the rafters and Professor Brask, who talks to all of them more kindly than to students.\ndesc [b3d42f] = 鹰头马身兽、一只闹脾气的狮鹫幼崽、椽梁上的猫头鹰，还有布拉斯克教授——他对它们说话，比对学生温柔得多。\n\n@place boathouse\n# en: The Boathouse\nname [ba1cb2] = 船屋\n# en: An old wooden boathouse and a jetty out over the water. People come here to say the things they can't say indoors.\ndesc [cf59e4] = 一座旧木船屋，一道伸向水面的栈桥。人们来这里说那些在室内说不出口的话。\n\n@place orchard\n# en: The Orchard\nname [f7870f] = 果园\n# en: Apple and pear trees older than the towers. In spring the blossom falls like snow; once, it was snow.\ndesc [73fd80] = 比塔楼还老的苹果树和梨树。春天花落如雪；有一次，落下的真是雪。\n\n@place maud_cottage\n# en: Old Maud's Cottage\nname [37a3a6] = 老莫德的小屋\n# en: A thatched cottage with chickens on the doorstep, bees in the eaves, a goat called Bartholomew and a kettle that is always nearly boiling.\ndesc [77de96] = 一间茅草小屋：门口有鸡，屋檐有蜂，还有一只叫巴塞洛缪的山羊，以及一只永远快要烧开的水壶。\n\n@place secret_garden\n# en: The Secret Garden\nname [2193f1] = 秘密花园\n# en: Glowing ferns, silver bees and a pear tree that hums. It only exists for people Maud has shown it to.\ndesc [7a21e5] = 发光的蕨类、银色的蜜蜂，还有一棵会哼歌的梨树。它只为莫德带进来过的人存在。\n# en: A door in the wall behind the potting shed that you keep walking past.\nhint [17ccd0] = 盆栽棚后面的墙上有一扇门，你总是从它旁边走过去。\n\n@place courier_loft\n# en: The Courier Loft\nname [ef540e] = 信使阁楼\n# en: A draughty loft full of parcels, pigeonholes and brooms in various states of repair. The dispatch bell rings at all hours.\ndesc [6cb721] = 一间漏风的阁楼，堆满包裹、信格和各种状态的扫帚。派送铃不分昼夜地响。\n\n@place gilded_newt\n# en: The Gilded Newt\nname [0d7995] = 镀金蝾螈酒馆\n# en: The village tavern: low beams, a fire that never goes out, and a landlady who remembers every student's tab to the penny.\ndesc [5e1794] = 小镇的酒馆：低矮的房梁，一炉从不熄灭的火，还有一位把每个学生的欠账都记到最后一个铜子儿的老板娘。\n\n@place mabels\n# en: Mabel's Tea Shop\nname [d2018a] = 梅布尔茶馆\n# en: Forty kinds of tea, three kinds of cake and Mabel, who reads your mood from the way you open the door.\ndesc [a4c364] = 四十种茶，三种蛋糕，还有梅布尔——她能从你推门的样子读出你的心情。\n\n@place grimsbys_books\n# en: Grimsby's Books\nname [5f12ee] = 格林斯比书店\n# en: A bookshop so crowded the shelves lean on each other for support. Mr Grimsby will find anything except what you came in for.\ndesc [555651] = 一家挤得书架都要互相倚靠的书店。格林斯比先生什么都找得到，除了你进门要找的那本。\n\n@place pell_apothecary\n# en: Pell & Daughters\nname [e047f1] = 佩尔父女药铺\n# en: An apothecary of a thousand labelled drawers. Old Pell taught three generations to count drops; his daughters taught him to smile at customers.\ndesc [4bb1ef] = 一家有上千个贴着标签的抽屉的药铺。老佩尔教会了三代人数药滴；他的女儿们教会了他对客人微笑。\n\n@place bakery\n# en: The Bakery\nname [ddad08] = 面包房\n# en: The same family has baked here for two hundred years. The buns are famous; so, in certain circles, is a letter that arrived very late.\ndesc [7aadb6] = 同一家人在这里烤了两百年面包。小圆面包远近闻名；在某些圈子里，一封迟到了很久的信也是。\n\n@place whisperwood\n# en: The Whisperwood\nname [0cc2ff] = 低语森林\n# en: An old forest that talks in its sleep. Moonpetal grows in its clearings; so do things that are better left there.\ndesc [c682e5] = 一片会说梦话的古老森林。月瓣花长在林间空地上；那里也长着一些最好留在原地的东西。\n# en: Fog sits on the western woods even at noon.\nhint [a946d3] = 西边的树林里，即使正午也笼着雾。\n\n@place royal_library_archives\n# en: The Royal Library Archives\nname [c0d567] = 皇家图书馆档案馆\n# en: Miles of stacks beneath the capital, where the oldest records of the realm are kept in temperature-controlled silence.\ndesc [1c545c] = 王都地下绵延数里的书库，王国最古老的记录在恒温的寂静中保存。\n\n@place conclave_laboratory\n# en: The Conclave Laboratory\nname [858e84] = 七塔议会实验室\n# en: The research laboratory of the Conclave of the Seven Towers, where new spells are tested behind very thick walls.\ndesc [d888bf] = 七塔议会的研究实验室，新咒语在非常非常厚的墙后接受测试。\n\n@place alchemists_works\n# en: The Guild Works\nname [f658d1] = 公会工坊\n# en: The Guild of Alchemists' great works by the river: vats, chimneys and a strict rule about naked flames.\ndesc [041a92] = 炼金师公会在河边的大工坊：大缸、烟囱，以及一条关于明火的严格规定。\n\n@place observatory_camp\n# en: The Expedition Camp\nname [bdc258] = 远征营地\n# en: Tents on a high plateau where the air is thin and the stars are close enough to argue with.\ndesc [aa6f2e] = 高原上的帐篷。空气稀薄，星星近得可以和它们争论。\n\n@place field_station\n# en: The Beastcraft Field Station\nname [93e08b] = 驭兽术野外站\n# en: A cabin on the moors, ringed with paddocks and nesting towers, where injured creatures are mended and wild ones are counted.\ndesc [0090c1] = 荒原上的一座小木屋，四周是围场和筑巢塔。受伤的生灵在这里被治好，野生的则在这里被清点。\n\n@place thornwick_lab\n# en: The Thornwick Exchange Lab\nname [efa297] = 荆木交换实验室\n# en: A guest laboratory at Thornwick Academy, the rival school, where the illusionists are excellent and the hospitality is suspicious.\ndesc [8e0893] = 对手学校荆木学院的一间客座实验室：幻术师们一流，待客之道可疑。\n\n@place unseen_office\n# en: The Office of Unseen Affairs\nname [dcf6f5] = 隐秘事务署\n# en: The Crown's Office of Unseen Affairs. It has no sign, no address, and a very good coffee machine.\ndesc [fb76d0] = 王室隐秘事务署。没有招牌，没有地址，只有一台非常好用的咖啡机。\n\n@place home_cottage\n# en: The Family Cottage\nname [be9171] = 老家小屋\n# en: The kitchen table, the creaky stair, the smell of whatever is in the oven. Everyone asks how school is.\ndesc [e5a1df] = 餐桌、吱呀的楼梯、烤箱里不知什么的香味。每个人都问你学校怎么样。\n\n@place home_fields\n# en: The Fields\nname [50cb01] = 田野\n# en: Long rows, a low wall to sit on, and the same view you used to stare at when you dreamed of towers.\ndesc [5dc6c3] = 长长的田垄，一堵可以坐的矮墙，还有你从前梦想高塔时一直盯着看的那片风景。\n\n@place home_market\n# en: The Market Town\nname [28baa8] = 集镇\n# en: Market stalls, the old crowd outside the inn, and the one shop that still sells your favourite sweets.\ndesc [0cdab5] = 集市摊位、旅店门口的老面孔，还有那家仍在卖你最爱的糖果的小店。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/spells.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/spells.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@spell flame\n# en: Flame\nname [1551a0] = 火焰\n# en: Fire from the fingertips: a candle's worth at first, a bonfire's later. Burns through wards that only expect cleverness.\ndesc [500494] = 指尖生火：起初只有一根蜡烛的分量，后来是一堆篝火。能烧穿那些只防备聪明人的结界。\n\n@spell tide\n# en: Tide\nname [f63371] = 潮涌\n# en: Water called up and moved: a wave, a wall, a lake politely asked to step aside.\ndesc [dfeef2] = 召来并驱使水：一道浪，一堵水墙，或请一整片湖礼貌地让开。\n\n@spell foresight\n# en: Foresight\nname [1834e4] = 预见\n# en: A glimpse of the next few moments. Enough to know which way the dice will fall, sometimes.\ndesc [d1ae0e] = 瞥见接下来的片刻。有时，足以知道骰子会落在哪一面。\n\n@spell starlight\n# en: Starlight\nname [461e3b] = 星光\n# en: The light of a named star, held in the hand. Darkness that eats light finds it hard to swallow.\ndesc [c90510] = 一颗有名字的星星的光，握在手里。吞噬光的黑暗很难把它咽下去。\n\n@spell mend\n# en: Mend\nname [c5efbd] = 愈合\n# en: Knits cuts, cracks and broken teacups. Hearts take longer.\ndesc [fff564] = 缝合伤口、裂缝和摔碎的茶杯。心要慢一些。\n\n@spell purify\n# en: Purify\nname [3d9ade] = 净化\n# en: Draws out poison, curse-cold and bad ingredients. The cure has to come from somewhere; it leaves you tired.\ndesc [3c860c] = 拔除毒素、诅咒之寒和坏掉的药材。解药总得从某处来；它会让你很累。\n\n@spell glamour\n# en: Glamour\nname [ca8c7e] = 魅影\n# en: A little more charm than you really have. People remember liking you and not quite why.\ndesc [844244] = 比你实际拥有的多一点点魅力。人们记得喜欢你，却说不清为什么。\n\n@spell mirror\n# en: Mirror\nname [247ee2] = 镜像\n# en: A double of yourself, or of anything you can see. It doesn't talk, which is its only flaw.\ndesc [664e97] = 一个你自己的替身，或任何你看得见的东西的替身。它不会说话——这是它唯一的缺点。\n\n@spell haste\n# en: Haste\nname [2f6010] = 疾速\n# en: Speed borrowed from hares and hawks. You act first; you pay for it with a long nap.\ndesc [1f52a3] = 从野兔和鹰那里借来的速度。你先出手；代价是之后睡上很久。\n\n@spell beast_speech\n# en: Beast-speech\nname [ba402d] = 兽语\n# en: Talk with animals, briefly. Most of them have been waiting to complain.\ndesc [9298ce] = 短暂地与动物交谈。它们大多一直在等着抱怨。\n\n@spell ward\n# en: Ward\nname [a11053] = 护界\n# en: A pale shell that turns aside the next harm.\ndesc [349fd9] = 一层苍白的护壳，能挡开下一次伤害。\n\n@spell shadowstep\n# en: Shadowstep\nname [d5b5ab] = 影步\n# en: Step into one shadow and out of another. Short distances only; the shadows in between are not friendly.\ndesc [c7cfde] = 踏入一片影子，从另一片影子里走出。只限短距离；中间的那些影子并不友善。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/tracks.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/tracks.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@track evocation\n# en: Evocation\nname [ade249] = 塑能术\n# en: Evocation I, Evocation II, Advanced Evocation\nlevel_names [bc7426] = 塑能术 I、塑能术 II、高等塑能术\n# en: Fire and water, called up and held. Pyromancy and Tidecalling, taught together because they keep putting each other out.\ndesc [17db46] = 召唤并驾驭火与水。火焰术和唤潮术合在一起教，因为它们总是互相浇灭。\n# en: You sign up for Evocation. The reading list comes with a note about singed eyebrows.\npicked [99ef0b] = 你报了塑能术。书单后面附了一张关于眉毛烧焦的提醒。\n\n@track astromancy\n# en: Astromancy & Divination\nname [f9e4fb] = 星象与占卜\n# en: Astromancy I, Astromancy II, Advanced Astromancy\nlevel_names [9c3a9b] = 星象学 I、星象学 II、高等星象学\n# en: Reading the sky and the time that hasn't happened yet. Tea leaves in the first year; star charts that move in the third.\ndesc [74bd21] = 解读星空，以及尚未发生的时间。一年级看茶叶；三年级的星图会自己动。\n# en: You sign up for Astromancy. Someone hands you a teacup and tells you not to drink from it.\npicked [a1269f] = 你报了星象与占卜。有人递给你一只茶杯，叮嘱你别喝里面的东西。\n\n@track alchemy\n# en: Alchemy & Healing\nname [dd40d4] = 炼金与治愈\n# en: Alchemy I, Alchemy II, Advanced Alchemy\nlevel_names [5ac82d] = 炼金学 I、炼金学 II、高等炼金学\n# en: Potions, remedies and the patience to stir clockwise. Half of it happens in the laboratory and half in the infirmary.\ndesc [88b971] = 药剂、疗方，以及顺时针搅拌的耐心。一半在实验室里学，一半在医务室里学。\n# en: You sign up for Alchemy. Your first lesson is how to stir clockwise, and why.\npicked [f50cd5] = 你报了炼金与治愈。第一课是怎么顺时针搅拌，以及为什么。\n\n@track illusion\n# en: Illusion & Glamour\nname [5cdf61] = 幻术与魅影\n# en: Illusion I, Illusion II, Advanced Illusion\nlevel_names [b3f52d] = 幻术 I、幻术 II、高等幻术\n# en: Making people see what you choose. Professor Vey grades on whether you fooled her, and she is very hard to fool.\ndesc [fcaca3] = 让别人看见你选择的东西。维伊教授的评分标准是你有没有骗过她——而她很难被骗。\n# en: You sign up for Illusion. Professor Vey looks at you as if you might already be one.\npicked [0ae3d9] = 你报了幻术与魅影。维伊教授看你的眼神，好像你本人就可能是个幻象。\n\n@track beastcraft\n# en: Beastcraft & Flight\nname [d8160a] = 驭兽与飞行\n# en: Beastcraft I, Beastcraft II, Advanced Beastcraft\nlevel_names [ff1301] = 驭兽学 I、驭兽学 II、高等驭兽学\n# en: Creatures, familiars and brooms. Professor Brask believes the first two are easier to reason with than the third.\ndesc [4a5da1] = 生灵、使魔与扫帚。布拉斯克教授认为前两者比第三者讲道理。\n# en: You sign up for Beastcraft. Your name goes on the list beside a broom and a hippogriff.\npicked [39f700] = 你报了驭兽与飞行。你的名字写在名单上，旁边是一把扫帚和一只鹰头马身有翼兽。\n\n@track umbral\n# en: Umbral Theory\nname [97bda7] = 暗影理论\n# en: Umbral Theory I, Umbral Theory II, Advanced Umbral Theory\nlevel_names [3a8538] = 暗影理论 I、暗影理论 II、高等暗影理论\n# en: Shadow, wards and the magic of what is hidden. Taught at the top of the tower, where the lamps burn dim on purpose.\ndesc [418538] = 影、结界，以及隐藏之物的魔法。在塔顶讲授，那里的灯故意点得很暗。\n# en: You sign up for Umbral Theory. The lamps at the top of the tower are dim on purpose.\npicked [fe0dba] = 你报了暗影理论。塔顶的灯光是故意调暗的。\n\n@track artifice\n# en: Artifice\nname [de3328] = 奇械术\n# en: Artifice\nlevel_names [de3328] = 奇械术\n# en: Enchanted machinery: clockwork that thinks, cauldrons that stir themselves. Offered to students who already know how to make things and how to make them burn.\ndesc [7cb472] = 附魔机械：会思考的发条装置，会自己搅拌的坩埚。只开放给已经懂得如何造东西、也懂得东西如何着火的学生。\n# en: You take the place in Artifice. Someone shows you where the fire buckets are before anything else.\npicked [8435e9] = 你接下了奇械术的名额。在一切开始之前，有人先告诉你灭火桶放在哪儿。\n\n@track starcalling\n# en: Starcalling\nname [7a102b] = 唤星术\n# en: Starcalling\nlevel_names [7a102b] = 唤星术\n# en: Calling down the light of a named star. Not on any syllabus; taught by appointment, at night.\ndesc [73276b] = 召唤一颗有名字的星星的光。不在任何课表上；需预约，夜间授课。\n# en: You accept the appointment for Starcalling. The first one is tonight.\npicked [d972c5] = 你接受了唤星术的约见。第一次就在今晚。\n\n@track hollow_studies\n# en: Hollow Studies\nname [1138df] = 空洞研究\n# en: Hollow Studies\nlevel_names [1138df] = 空洞研究\n# en: What the founders sealed, and how. The headmistress teaches it herself, to very few students, in a room without windows.\ndesc [e6cd54] = 创始人封印了什么，又是如何封印的。院长亲自讲授，学生极少，在一间没有窗的屋子里。\n# en: You accept a seat in Hollow Studies. The headmistress writes your name in a book with a black cover.\npicked [10801c] = 你接受了空洞研究的席位。院长把你的名字写进一本黑色封皮的书里。\n\n@track duelling\n# en: Duelling Circle\nname [d0fcd5] = 决斗社\n# en: Member, Officer, Captain\nranks [b9c401] = 社员、干事、队长\n# en: Practice bouts, the Spring Tournament and a great deal of shouting about footwork.\ndesc [cd83f9] = 练习对决、春季锦标赛，还有关于步法的大量吼叫。\n# en: You join the Duelling Circle. Someone is already explaining footwork to you, loudly.\npicked [5318b4] = 你加入了决斗社。已经有人在大声给你讲步法了。\n\n@track lumen_ledger\n# en: The Lumen Ledger\nname [2606ae] = 《流明纪事报》社\n# en: Stringer, Reporter, Editor\nranks [82cebb] = 通讯员、记者、主编\n# en: The student newspaper. Scoops, deadlines, gossip columns and the occasional duty to the truth.\ndesc [ab972d] = 学生报纸。独家新闻、截稿日、八卦专栏，偶尔还有对真相的责任。\n# en: You join the Lumen Ledger. Nessa hands you a notebook and your first deadline.\npicked [2d283c] = 你加入了《流明纪事报》社。内莎递给你一个笔记本和你的第一个截稿日。\n\n@track verdant\n# en: Verdant Circle\nname [7ac3fd] = 青翠社\n# en: Member, Gardener, Head Gardener\nranks [cddc83] = 社员、园丁、首席园丁\n# en: The greenhouse club: moonflowers, mandrakes and long talks with plants that occasionally answer.\ndesc [263288] = 温室社团：月光花、曼德拉草，还有和偶尔会回话的植物之间的长谈。\n# en: You join the Verdant Circle. Tobias introduces you to the plants, one by one.\npicked [dec315] = 你加入了青翠社。托比亚斯把植物一株一株介绍给你。\n\n@track cartographers\n# en: Midnight Cartographers\nname [771e36] = 午夜制图社\n# en: The club that doesn't exist, mapping the passages the castle would prefer to keep. Never offered at the Club Fair; you are invited, or you aren't.\ndesc [f48173] = 那个不存在的社团，专门绘制城堡宁愿藏起来的通道。社团招新会上从不出现；你要么被邀请，要么没有。\n\n@track library_assistant\n# en: Library Assistant\nname [111c30] = 图书馆助理\n# en: Apprentice, Clerk, Senior Clerk\nranks [e4d902] = 学徒、馆员、资深馆员\n# en: Shelving books that don't want to be shelved, and keeping Footnote off the reference desk.\ndesc [dd4493] = 把不想被上架的书上架，并且别让“脚注”坐上参考咨询台。\n# en: You sign on at the library. Your first shift is next week, and Footnote is already watching you.\npicked [a968ac] = 你当上了图书馆助理。第一个班在下周，脚注已经在盯着你了。\n\n@track tavern\n# en: Server at the Gilded Newt\nname [e530c9] = 镀金蝾螈酒馆侍应\n# en: Server, Barkeep, Manager\nranks [a43a61] = 侍应、吧台手、经理\n# en: Evening shifts behind the bar. Tips depend on charm; survival depends on remembering who ordered the spiced cider.\ndesc [173a67] = 吧台后的晚班。小费取决于魅力；能否活下来，取决于你记不记得是谁点了香料苹果酒。\n# en: You sign on at the Gilded Newt. The landlady shows you the cellar and the back door.\npicked [7077f7] = 你在镀金蝾螈酒馆当上了侍应。老板娘带你看了酒窖和后门。\n\n@track apothecary_clerk\n# en: Apothecary Clerk\nname [7d9f6c] = 药铺店员\n# en: Clerk, Dispenser, Assistant Apothecary\nranks [53ed21] = 店员、配药员、助理药师\n# en: Counting drops and reading labels at Pell & Daughters. The wrong drawer is never just the wrong drawer.\ndesc [b1db1f] = 在佩尔父女药铺数药滴、认标签。拿错的抽屉，从来不只是拿错了抽屉。\n# en: You sign on at Pell & Daughters. Pell shows you which drawers never to open.\npicked [23e86f] = 你在佩尔父女药铺当上了店员。佩尔告诉你哪些抽屉永远别打开。\n\n@track broom_courier\n# en: Broom Courier\nname [8250e5] = 飞帚信使\n# en: Runner, Courier, Chief Courier\nranks [1d2734] = 跑腿、信使、首席信使\n# en: Parcels, letters and the occasional live delivery, flown across the lake in all weathers.\ndesc [2e4f08] = 包裹、信件，偶尔还有活物，风雨无阻地飞越湖面送达。\n# en: You sign on at the courier loft. They give you a satchel and a broom that pulls left.\npicked [48f0a6] = 你当上了飞帚信使。他们给了你一个挎包和一把往左偏的扫帚。\n\n@track intern_royal_library\n# en: Royal Library Archives\nname [df141e] = 皇家图书馆档案馆\n# en: Cataloguing and research in the oldest archives of the realm.\ndesc [fa724c] = 在王国最古老的档案馆里编目、做研究。\n\n@track intern_conclave\n# en: Conclave Laboratory\nname [132d7b] = 七塔议会实验室\n# en: Spell research with the Conclave of the Seven Towers. Prestigious; a faculty recommendation is expected.\ndesc [256192] = 在七塔议会从事咒语研究。名额珍贵；通常需要教职员的推荐信。\n\n@track intern_alchemists\n# en: Guild of Alchemists' Works\nname [6d98e1] = 炼金师公会工坊\n# en: Industrial alchemy at the Guild's great works.\ndesc [56dec3] = 在公会的大工坊里做工业炼金。\n\n@track intern_expedition\n# en: Observatory Expedition\nname [fe57ea] = 观星远征\n# en: A summer of sky charts on a high plateau.\ndesc [03b2f6] = 在高原上与星图共度一个夏天。\n\n@track intern_field_station\n# en: Beastcraft Field Station\nname [939e0e] = 驭兽术野外站\n# en: Tracking, tending and counting creatures on the moors.\ndesc [7564b3] = 在荒原上追踪、照料、清点各种生灵。\n\n@track intern_thornwick\n# en: Thornwick Exchange Lab\nname [28923e] = 荆木交换实验室\n# en: An exchange placement at the rival academy's illusion laboratory.\ndesc [51032c] = 前往对手学院的幻术实验室交换实习。\n\n@track intern_unseen_office\n# en: Office of Unseen Affairs\nname [c20130] = 隐秘事务署\n# en: By invitation only. Nobody applies to the Office of Unseen Affairs.\ndesc [55df65] = 只凭邀请。没有人会申请隐秘事务署。\n\n@track thesis\n# en: Thesis\nname [187afd] = 毕业论文\n# en: A year-long research project with a faculty advisor, ending in a defence before a committee of three.\ndesc [a30062] = 与一位教职员导师合作、为期一年的研究课题，最后在三人委员会面前答辩。\n"}]};
